if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-27681"]="# THE FLIGHT ACROSS THE LAKE 214\n\nAs soon as this slight salutation had passed, Montcalm moved towards them with a quick but graceful step, baring his head to the veteran, and dropping his spotless plume nearly to the earth in courtesy\nThe scout having ascertained that the Mohicans were sufficient of themselves to maintain the requisite distance, deliberately laid aside his paddle, and raised the fatal rifle\n\n# THE TERMAGANT 250\n\nThrowing back her light vestment, she stretched forth her long skinny arm, in derision\nThe grim head fell on one side, and in its place appeared the honest, sturdy countenance of the scout\n\nHeyward and Alice took their way together towards the distant village of the Delawares\nCora had cast herself to her knees; and, with hands clenched in each other and pressed upon her bosom, she remained like a beauteous and breathing model of her sex\n\n\"Mine ear is open, and my heart prepared: The worst is worldly loss thou canst unfold: Say, is my kingdom lost?\"\nIt was a feature peculiar to the colonial wars of North America, that the toils and dangers of the wilderness were to be encountered before the adverse hosts could meet. A wide and apparently an impervious boundary of forests severed the possessions of the hostile provinces of France and England. The hardy colonist, and the trained European who fought at his side, frequently expended months in struggling against the rapids of the streams, or in effecting the rugged passes of the mountains, in quest of an opportunity to exhibit their courage in a more martial conflict. But, emulating the patience and self-denial of the practised native warriors, they learned to overcome every difficulty; and it would seem that, in time, there was no recess of the woods so dark, nor any secret place so lovely, that it might claim exemption from the inroads of those who had pledged their blood to satiate their vengeance, or to uphold the cold and selfish policy of the distant monarchs of Europe.\n\nPerhaps no district throughout the wide extent of the intermediate frontiers can furnish a livelier picture of the cruelty and fierceness of the savage warfare of those periods than the country which lies between the head waters of the Hudson and the adjacent lakes.\nThe facilities which nature had there offered to the march of the combatants were too obvious to be neglected. The lengthened sheet of the Champlain stretched from the frontiers of Canada, deep within the borders of the neighboring province of New York, forming a natural passage across half the distance that the French were compelled to master in order to strike their enemies. Near its southern termination, it received the contributions of another lake, whose waters were so limpid as to have been exclusively selected by the Jesuit missionaries to perform the typical purification of baptism, and to obtain for it the title of lake \"du Saint Sacrement.\" The less zealous English thought they conferred a sufficient honor on its unsullied fountains, when they bestowed the name of their reigning prince, the second of the house of Hanover. The two united to rob the untutored possessors of its wooded scenery of their native right to perpetuate its original appellation of \"Horican.\"[1]\n\nWinding its way among countless islands, and imbedded in mountains, the \"holy lake\" extended a dozen leagues still farther to the south. With the high plain that there interposed itself to the further passage of the water, commenced a portage of as many miles, which conducted the adventurer to the banks of the Hudson, at a point where, with the usual obstructions of the rapids, or rifts, as they were then termed in the language of the country, the river became navigable to the tide.\nWhile, in the pursuit of their daring plans of annoyance, the restless enterprise of the French even attempted the distant and difficult gorges of the Alleghany, it may easily be imagined that their proverbial acuteness would not overlook the natural advantages of the district we have just described. It became, emphatically, the bloody arena, in which most of the battles for the mastery of the colonies were contested. Forts were erected at the different points that commanded the facilities of the route, and were taken and retaken, razed and rebuilt, as victory alighted on the hostile banners. While the husbandman shrank back from the dangerous passes, within the safer boundaries of the more ancient settlements, armies larger than those that had often disposed of the sceptres of the mother countries, were seen to bury themselves in these forests, whence they rarely returned but in skeleton bands, that were haggard with care, or dejected by defeat. Though the arts of peace were unknown to this fatal region, its forests were alive with men; its shades and glens rang with the sounds of martial music, and the echoes of its mountains threw back the laugh, or repeated the wanton cry, of many a gallant and reckless youth, as he hurried by them, in the noontide of his spirits, to slumber in a long night of forgetfulness.\n\nIt was in this scene of strife and bloodshed that the incidents we shall attempt to relate occurred, during the third year of the war which England and France last waged for the possession of a country that neither was destined to retain.\nThe imbecility of her military leaders abroad, and the fatal want of energy in her councils at home, had lowered the character of Great Britain from the proud elevation on which it had been placed, by the talents and enterprise of her former warriors and statesmen. No longer dreaded by her enemies, her servants were fast losing the confidence of self-respect. In this mortifying abasement, the colonists, though innocent of her imbecility, and too humble to be the agents of her blunders, were but the natural participators.\n\nThey had recently seen a chosen army from that country, which, reverencing as a mother, they had blindly believed invincible--an army led by a chief who had been selected from a crowd of trained warriors, for his rare military endowments, disgracefully routed by a handful of French and Indians, and only saved from annihilation by the coolness and spirit of a Virginian boy, whose riper fame has since diffused itself, with the steady influence of moral truth, to the uttermost confines of Christendom.[2] A wide frontier had been laid naked by this unexpected disaster, and more substantial evils were preceded by a thousand fanciful and imaginary dangers. The alarmed colonists believed that the yells of the savages mingled with every fitful gust of wind that issued from the interminable forests of the west. The terrific character of their merciless enemies increased immeasurably the natural horrors of warfare. Numberless recent massacres were still vivid in their recollections; nor was there any ear in the provinces so deaf as not to have drunk in with avidity the narrative of some fearful tale of midnight murder, in which the natives of the forests were the principal and barbarous actors. As the credulous and excited traveller related the hazardous chances of the wilderness, the blood of the timid curdled with terror, and mothers cast anxious glances even at those children which slumbered within the security of the largest towns. In short, the magnifying influence of fear began to set at naught the calculations of reason, and to render those who should have remembered their manhood, the slaves of the basest of passions. Even the most confident and the stoutest hearts began to think the issue of the contest was becoming doubtful; and that abject class was hourly increasing in numbers, who thought they foresaw all the possessions of the English crown in America subdued by their Christian foes, or laid waste by the inroads of their relentless allies.\n\nWhen, therefore, intelligence was received at the fort, which covered the southern termination of the portage between the Hudson and the lakes, that Montcalm had been seen moving up the Champlain, with an army \"numerous as the leaves on the trees,\" its truth was admitted with more of the craven reluctance of fear than with the stern joy that a warrior should feel, in finding an enemy within reach of his blow. The news had been brought, towards the decline of a day in midsummer, by an Indian runner, who also bore an urgent request from Munro, the commander of a work on the shore of the \"holy lake,\" for a speedy and powerful reinforcement. It has already been mentioned that the distance between these two posts was less than five leagues. The rude path, which originally formed their line of communication, had been widened for the passage of wagons; so that the distance which had been travelled by the son of the forest in two hours, might easily be effected by a detachment of troops, with their necessary baggage, between the rising and setting of a summer sun. The loyal servants of the British crown had given to one of these forest fastnesses the name of William Henry, and to the other that of Fort Edward; calling each after a favorite prince of the reigning family. The veteran Scotchman just named held the first, with a regiment of regulars and a few provincials; a force really by far too small to make head against the formidable power that Montcalm was leading to the foot of his earthen mounds. At the latter, however, lay General Webb, who commanded the armies of the king in the northern provinces, with a body of more than five thousand men. By uniting the several detachments of his command, this officer might have arrayed nearly double that number of combatants against the enterprising Frenchman, who had ventured so far from his reinforcements, with an army but little superior in numbers.\n\nBut under the influence of their degraded fortunes, both officers and men appeared better disposed to await the approach of their formidable antagonists, within their works, than to resist the progress of their march, by emulating the successful example of the French at Fort du Quesne, and striking a blow on their advance.\n\nAfter the first surprise of the intelligence had a little abated, a rumor was spread through the entrenched camp, which stretched along the margin of the Hudson, forming a chain of outworks to the body of the fort itself, that a chosen detachment of fifteen hundred men was to depart, with the dawn, for William Henry, the post at the northern extremity of the portage. That which at first was only rumor, soon became certainty, as orders passed from the quarters of the commander-in-chief to the several corps he had selected for this service, to prepare for their speedy departure. All doubt as to the intention of Webb now vanished, and an hour or two of hurried footsteps and anxious faces succeeded. The novice in the military art flew from point to point, retarding his own preparations by the excess of his violent and somewhat distempered zeal; while the more practised veteran made his arrangements with a deliberation that scorned every appearance of haste; though his sober lineaments and anxious eye sufficiently betrayed that he had no very strong professional relish for the as yet untried and dreaded warfare of the wilderness. At length the sun set in a flood of glory, behind the distant western hills, and as darkness drew its veil around the secluded spot the sounds of preparation diminished; the last light finally disappeared from the log cabin of some officer; the trees cast their deeper shadows over the mounds and the rippling stream, and a silence soon pervaded the camp, as deep as that which reigned in the vast forest by which it was environed.\n\nAccording to the orders of the preceding night, the heavy sleep of the army was broken by the rolling of the warning drums, whose rattling echoes were heard issuing, on the damp morning air, out of every vista of the woods, just as day began to draw the shaggy outlines of some tall pines of the vicinity, on the opening brightness of a soft and cloudless eastern sky. In an instant the whole camp was in motion; the meanest soldier arousing from his lair to witness the departure of his comrades, and to share in the excitement and incidents of the hour. The simple array of the chosen band was soon completed. While the regular and trained hirelings of the king marched with haughtiness to the right of the line, the less pretending colonists took their humbler position on its left, with a docility that long practice had rendered easy. The scouts departed; strong guards preceded and followed the lumbering vehicles that bore the baggage; and before the gray light of the morning was mellowed by the rays of the sun, the main body of the combatants wheeled into column, and left the encampment with a show of high military bearing, that served to drown the slumbering apprehensions of many a novice, who was now about to make his first essay in arms. While in view of their admiring comrades, the same proud front and ordered array was observed, until the notes of their fifes growing fainter in distance, the forest at length appeared to swallow up the living mass which had slowly entered its bosom.\n\nThe deepest sounds of the retiring and invisible column had ceased to be borne on the breeze to the listeners, and the latest straggler had already disappeared in pursuit; but there still remained the signs of another departure, before a log cabin of unusual size and accommodations, in front of which those sentinels paced their rounds, who were known to guard the person of the English general. At this spot were gathered some half dozen horses, caparisoned in a manner which showed that two, at least, were destined to bear the persons of females, of a rank that it was not usual to meet so far in the wilds of the country. A third wore the trappings and arms of an officer of the staff; while the rest, from the plainness of the housings, and the travelling mails with which they were encumbered, were evidently fitted for the reception of as many menials, who were, seemingly, already awaiting the pleasure of those they served. At a respectful distance from this unusual show were gathered divers groups of curious idlers; some admiring the blood and bone of the high-mettled military charger, and others gazing at the preparations, with dull wonder of vulgar curiosity. There was one man, however, who, by his countenance and actions, formed a marked exception to those who composed the latter class of spectators, being neither idle, nor seemingly very ignorant.\n\nThe person of this individual was to the last degree ungainly, without being in any particular manner deformed. He had all the bones and joints of other men, without any of their proportions. Erect, his stature surpassed that of his fellows; seated, he appeared reduced within the ordinary limits of the race. The same contrariety in his members seemed to exist throughout the whole man. His head was large; his shoulders narrow; his arms long and dangling; while his hands were small, if not delicate. His legs and thighs were thin, nearly to emaciation, but of extraordinary length; and his knees would have been considered tremendous, had they not been outdone by the broader foundations on which this false superstructure of the blended human orders was so profanely reared. The ill-assorted and injudicious attire of the individual only served to render his awkwardness more conspicuous. A sky-blue coat, with short and broad skirts and low cape, exposed a long thin neck, and longer and thinner legs, to the worst animadversions of the evil disposed. His nether garment was of yellow nankeen, closely fitted to the shape, and tied at his bunches of knees by large knots of white ribbon, a good deal sullied by use. Clouded cotton stockings, and shoes, on one of the latter of which was a plated spur, completed the costume of the lower extremity of this figure, no curve or angle of which was concealed, but, on the other hand, studiously exhibited, through the vanity or simplicity of its owner. From beneath the flap of an enormous pocket of a soiled vest of embossed silk, heavily ornamented with tarnished silver lace, projected an instrument, which, from being seen in such martial company, might have been easily mistaken for some mischievous and unknown implement of war. Small as it was, this uncommon engine had excited the curiosity of most of the Europeans in the camp, though several of the provincials were seen to handle it, not only without fear, but with the utmost familiarity. A large, civil cocked hat, like those worn by clergymen within the last thirty years, surmounted the whole, furnishing dignity to a good-natured and somewhat vacant countenance, that apparently needed such artificial aid, to support the gravity of some high and extraordinary trust.\n\nWhile the common herd stood aloof, in deference to the quarters of Webb, the figure we have described stalked in the centre of the domestics, freely expressing his censures or commendations on the merits of the horses, as by chance they displeased or satisfied his judgment.\n\n\"This beast, I rather conclude, friend, is not of home raising, but is from foreign lands, or perhaps from the little island itself over the blue water?\" he said, in a voice as remarkable for the softness and sweetness of its tones, as was his person for its rare proportions: \"I may speak of these things, and be no braggart; for I have been down at both havens; that which is situate at the mouth of Thames, and is named after the capital of Old England, and that which is called 'Haven,' with the addition of the word 'New'; and have seen the snows and brigantines collecting their droves, like the gathering to the ark, being outward bound to the Island of Jamaica, for the purpose of barter and traffic in four-footed animals; but never before have I beheld a beast which verified the true Scripture war-horse like this: 'He paweth in the valley, and rejoiceth in his strength: he goeth on to meet the armed men. He saith among the trumpets, Ha, ha; and he smelleth the battle afar off, the thunder of the captains, and the shouting.' It would seem that the stock of the horse of Israel has descended to our own time; would it not, friend?\"\n\nReceiving no reply to this extraordinary appeal, which in truth, as it was delivered with the vigor of full and sonorous tones, merited some sort of notice, he who had thus sung forth the language of the Holy Book turned to the silent figure to whom he had unwittingly addressed himself, and found a new and more powerful subject of admiration in the object that encountered his gaze. His eyes fell on the still, upright, and rigid form of the \"Indian runner,\" who had borne to the camp the unwelcome tidings of the preceding evening. Although in a state of perfect repose, and apparently disregarding, with characteristic stoicism, the excitement and bustle around him, there was a sullen fierceness mingled with the quiet of the savage, that was likely to arrest the attention of much more experienced eyes than those which now scanned him, in unconcealed amazement. The native bore both the tomahawk and knife of his tribe; and yet his appearance was not altogether that of a warrior. On the contrary, there was an air of neglect about his person, like that which might have proceeded from great and recent exertion, which he had not yet found leisure to repair. The colors of the war-paint had blended in dark confusion about his fierce countenance, and rendered his swarthy lineaments still more savage and repulsive than if art had attempted an effect which had been thus produced by chance. His eye, alone, which glistened like a fiery star amid lowering clouds, was to be seen in its state of native wildness. For a single instant, his searching and yet wary glance met the wondering look of the other, and then changing its direction, partly in cunning, and partly in disdain, it remained fixed, as if penetrating the distant air.\n\nIt is impossible to say what unlooked-for remark this short and silent communication, between two such singular men, might have elicited from the white man, had not his active curiosity been again drawn to other objects. A general movement among the domestics, and a low sound of gentle voices, announced the approach of those whose presence alone was wanted to enable the cavalcade to move. The simple admirer of the war-horse instantly fell back to a low, gaunt, switch-tailed mare, that was unconsciously gleaning the faded herbage of the camp nigh by; where, leaning with one elbow on the blanket that concealed an apology for a saddle, he became a spectator of the departure, while a foal was quietly making its morning repast, on the opposite side of the same animal.\n\nA young man, in the dress of an officer, conducted to their steeds two females, who, as it was apparent by their dresses, were prepared to encounter the fatigues of a journey in the woods. One, and she was the most juvenile in her appearance, though both were young, permitted glimpses of her dazzling complexion, fair golden hair, and bright blue eyes, to be caught, as she artlessly suffered the morning air to blow aside the green veil which descended low from her beaver. The flush which still lingered above the pines in the western sky was not more bright nor delicate than the bloom on her cheek; nor was the opening day more cheering than the animated smile which she bestowed on the youth, as he assisted her into the saddle. The other, who appeared to share equally in the attentions of the young officer, concealed her charms from the gaze of the soldiery, with a care that seemed better fitted to the experience of four or five additional years. It could be seen, however, that her person, though moulded with the same exquisite proportions, of which none of the graces were lost by the travelling dress she wore, was rather fuller and more mature than that of her companion.\n\nNo sooner were these females seated, than their attendant sprang lightly into the saddle of the war-horse, when the whole three bowed to Webb, who, in courtesy, awaited their parting on the threshold of his cabin, and turning their horses' heads, they proceeded at a slow amble, followed by their train, towards the northern entrance of the encampment. As they traversed that short distance, not a voice was heard amongst them; but a slight exclamation proceeded from the younger of the females, as the Indian runner glided by her, unexpectedly, and led the way along the military road in her front. Though this sudden and startling movement of the Indian produced no sound from the other, in the surprise her veil also was allowed to open its folds, and betrayed an indescribable look of pity, admiration, and horror, as her dark eye followed the easy motions of the savage. The tresses of this lady were shining and black, like the plumage of the raven. Her complexion was not brown, but it rather appeared charged with the color of the rich blood, that seemed ready to burst its bounds. And yet there was neither coarseness nor want of shadowing in a countenance that was exquisitely regular and dignified, and surpassingly beautiful. She smiled, as if in pity at her own momentary forgetfulness, discovering by the act a row of teeth that would have shamed the purest ivory; when, replacing the veil, she bowed her face, and rode in silence, like one whose thoughts were abstracted from the scene around her.\n\n\"Sola, sola, wo, ha, ho, sola!\"\n\nWhile one of the lovely beings we have so cursorily presented to the reader was thus lost in thought, the other quickly recovered from the alarm which induced the exclamation, and, laughing at her own weakness, she inquired of the youth who rode by her side,--\n\n\"Are such spectres frequent in the woods, Heyward; or is this sight an especial entertainment on our behalf? If the latter, gratitude must close our mouths; but if the former, both Cora and I shall have need to draw largely on that stock of hereditary courage which we boast, even before we are made to encounter the redoubtable Montcalm.\"\n\n\"Yon Indian is a 'runner' of the army; and, after the fashion of his people, he may be accounted a hero,\" returned the officer. \"He has volunteered to guide us to the lake, by a path but little known, sooner than if we followed the tardy movements of the column: and, by consequence, more agreeably.\"\n\n\"I like him not,\" said the lady, shuddering, partly in assumed, yet more in real terror. \"You know him, Duncan, or you would not trust yourself so freely to his keeping?\"\n\n\"Say, rather, Alice, that I would not trust you. I do know him, or he would not have my confidence, and least of all at this moment. He is said to be a Canadian, too; and yet he served with our friends the Mohawks, who, as you know, are one of the six allied nations.[3] He was brought among us, as I have heard, by some strange accident in which your father was interested, and in which the savage was rigidly dealt by--but I forget the idle tale; it is enough, that he is now our friend.\"\n\n\"If he has been my father's enemy, I like him still less!\" exclaimed the now really anxious girl. \"Will you not speak to him, Major Heyward, that I may hear his tones? Foolish though it may be, you have often heard me avow my faith in the tones of the human voice!\"\n\n\"It would be in vain; and answered, most probably, by an ejaculation. Though he may understand it, he affects, like most of his people, to be ignorant of the English; and least of all will he condescend to speak it, now that war demands the utmost exercise of his dignity. But he stops; the private path by which we are to journey is, doubtless, at hand.\"\n\nThe conjecture of Major Heyward was true. When they reached the spot where the Indian stood, pointing into the thicket that fringed the military road, a narrow and blind path, which might, with some little inconvenience, receive one person at a time, became visible.\n\n\"Here, then, lies our way,\" said the young man, in a low voice. \"Manifest no distrust, or you may invite the danger you appear to apprehend.\"\n\n\"Cora, what think you?\" asked the reluctant fair one. \"If we journey with the troops, though we may find their presence irksome, shall we not feel better assurance of our safety?\"\n\n\"Being little accustomed to the practices of the savages, Alice, you mistake the place of real danger,\" said Heyward. \"If enemies have reached the portage at all, a thing by no means probable, as our scouts are abroad, they will surely be found skirting the column where scalps abound the most. The route of the detachment is known, while ours, having been determined within the hour, must still be secret.\"\n\n\"Should we distrust the man because his manners are not our manners, and that his skin is dark?\" coldly asked Cora.\n\nAlice hesitated no longer; but giving her Narragansett[4] a smart cut of the whip, she was the first to dash aside the slight branches of the bushes, and to follow the runner along the dark and tangled pathway. The young man regarded the last speaker in open admiration, and even permitted her fairer though certainly not more beautiful companion to proceed unattended, while he sedulously opened the way himself for the passage of her who has been called Cora. It would seem that the domestics had been previously instructed; for, instead of penetrating the thicket, they followed the route of the column; a measure which Heyward stated had been dictated by the sagacity of their guide, in order to diminish the marks of their trail, if, haply, the Canadian savages should be lurking so far in advance of their army. For many minutes the intricacy of the route admitted of no further dialogue; after which they emerged from the broad border of underbrush which grew along the line of the highway, and entered under the high but dark arches of the forest. Here their progress was less interrupted, and the instant the guide perceived that the females could command their steeds, he moved on, at a pace between a trot and a walk, and at a rate which kept the sure-footed and peculiar animals they rode, at a fast yet easy amble. The youth had turned to speak to the dark-eyed Cora, when the distant sound of horses' hoofs, clattering over the roots of the broken way in his rear, caused him to check his charger; and, as his companions drew their reins at the same instant, the whole party came to a halt, in order to obtain an explanation of the unlooked-for interruption.\n\nIn a few moments a colt was seen gliding, like a fallow-deer, among the straight trunks of the pines; and, in another instant, the person of the ungainly man described in the preceding chapter, came into view, with as much rapidity as he could excite his meagre beast to endure without coming to an open rupture. Until now this personage had escaped the observation of the travellers. If he possessed the power to arrest any wandering eye when exhibiting the glories of his altitude on foot, his equestrian graces were still more likely to attract attention. Notwithstanding a constant application of his one armed heel to the flanks of the mare, the most confirmed gait that he could establish was a Canterbury gallop with the hind legs, in which those more forward assisted for doubtful moments, though generally content to maintain a loping trot. Perhaps the rapidity of the changes from one of these paces to the other created an optical illusion, which might thus magnify the powers of the beast; for it is certain that Heyward, who possessed a true eye for the merits of a horse, was unable, with his utmost ingenuity, to decide by what sort of movement his pursuer worked his sinuous way on his footsteps with such persevering hardihood.\n\nThe industry and movements of the rider were not less remarkable than those of the ridden. At each change in the evolutions of the latter, the former raised his tall person in the stirrups; producing, in this manner, by the undue elongation of his legs, such sudden growths and diminishings of the stature, as baffled every conjecture that might be made as to his dimensions. If to this be added the fact that, in consequence of the ex parte application of the spur, one side of the mare appeared to journey faster than the other; and that the aggrieved flank was resolutely indicated by unremitted flourishes of a bushy tail, we finish the picture of both horse and man.\n\nThe frown which had gathered around the handsome, open, and manly brow of Heyward, gradually relaxed, and his lips curled into a slight smile, as he regarded the stranger. Alice made no very powerful effort to control her merriment; and even the dark, thoughtful eye of Cora lighted with a humor that, it would seem, the habit, rather than the nature of its mistress repressed.\n\n\"Seek you any here?\" demanded Heyward, when the other had arrived sufficiently nigh to abate his speed; \"I trust you are no messenger of evil tidings?\"\n\n\"Even so,\" replied the stranger, making diligent use of his triangular castor, to produce a circulation in the close air of the woods, and leaving his hearers in doubt to which of the young man's questions he responded; when, however, he had cooled his face, and recovered his breath, he continued, \"I hear you are riding to William Henry; as I am journeying thitherward myself, I concluded good company would seem consistent to the wishes of both parties.\"\n\n\"You appear to possess the privilege of a casting vote,\" returned Heyward; \"we are three, whilst you have consulted no one but yourself.\"\n\n\"Even so. The first point to be obtained is to know one's own mind. Once sure of that, and where women are concerned, it is not easy, the next is, to act up to the decision. I have endeavored to do both, and here I am.\"\n\n\"If you journey to the lake, you have mistaken your route,\" said Heyward, haughtily; \"the highway thither is at least half a mile behind you.\"\n\n\"Even so,\" returned the stranger, nothing daunted by this cold reception; \"I have tarried at 'Edward' a week, and I should be dumb not to have inquired the road I was to journey; and if dumb there would be an end to my calling.\" After simpering in a small way, like one whose modesty prohibited a more open expression of his admiration of a witticism that was perfectly unintelligible to his hearers, he continued: \"It is not prudent for any one of my profession to be too familiar with those he is to instruct; for which reason I follow not the line of the army; besides which, I conclude that a gentleman of your character has the best judgment in matters of wayfaring; I have therefore decided to join company, in order that the ride may be made agreeable, and partake of social communion.\"\n\n\"A most arbitrary, if not a hasty decision!\" exclaimed Heyward, undecided whether to give vent to his growing anger, or to laugh in the other's face. \"But you speak of instruction, and of a profession; are you an adjunct to the provincial corps, as a master of the noble science of defence and offence; or, perhaps, you are one who draws lines and angles, under the pretence of expounding the mathematics?\"\n\nThe stranger regarded his interrogator a moment, in wonder; and then, losing every mark of self-satisfaction in an expression of solemn humility, he answered:--\n\n\"Of offence, I hope there is none, to either party: of defence, I make none--by God's good mercy, having committed no palpable sin since last entreating his pardoning grace. I understand not your allusions about lines and angles; and I leave expounding to those who have been called and set apart for that holy office. I lay claim to no higher gift than a small insight into the glorious art of petitioning and thanksgiving, as practised in psalmody.\"\n\n\"The man is, most manifestly, a disciple of Apollo,\" cried the amused Alice, \"and I take him under my own especial protection. Nay, throw aside that frown, Heyward, and in pity to my longing ears, suffer him to journey in our train. Besides,\" she added, in a low and hurried voice, casting a glance at the distant Cora, who slowly followed the footsteps of their silent but sullen guide, \"it may be a friend added to our strength, in time of need.\"\n\n\"Think you, Alice, that I would trust those I love by this secret path, did I imagine such need could happen?\"\n\n\"Nay, nay, I think not of it now; but this strange man amuses me; and if he 'hath music in his soul,' let us not churlishly reject his company.\" She pointed persuasively along the path with her riding-whip, while their eyes met in a look which the young man lingered a moment to prolong; then yielding to her gentle influence, he clapped his spurs into his charger, and in a few bounds was again at the side of Cora.\n\n\"I am glad to encounter thee, friend,\" continued the maiden, waving her hand to the stranger to proceed, as she urged her Narragansett to renew its amble. \"Partial relatives have almost persuaded me that I am not entirely worthless in a duet myself; and we may enliven our wayfaring by indulging in our favorite pursuit. It might be of signal advantage to one, ignorant as I, to hear the opinions and experience of a master in the art.\"\n\n\"It is refreshing both to the spirits and to the body to indulge in psalmody, in befitting seasons,\" returned the master of song, unhesitatingly complying with her intimation to follow; \"and nothing would relieve the mind more than such a consoling communion. But four parts are altogether necessary to the perfection of melody. You have all the manifestations of a soft and rich treble; I can, by especial aid, carry a full tenor to the highest letter; but we lack counter and bass! Yon officer of the king, who hesitated to admit me to his company, might fill the latter, if one may judge from the intonations of his voice in common dialogue.\"\n\n\"Judge not too rashly from hasty and deceptive appearances,\" said the lady, smiling; \"though Major Heyward can assume such deep notes on occasion, believe me, his natural tones are better fitted for a mellow tenor than the bass you heard.\"\n\n\"Is he, then, much practised in the art of psalmody?\" demanded her simple companion.\n\nAlice felt disposed to laugh, though she succeeded in suppressing her merriment, ere she answered,--\n\n\"I apprehend that he is rather addicted to profane song. The chances of a soldier's life are but little fitted for the encouragement of more sober inclinations.\"\n\n\"Man's voice is given to him, like his other talents, to be used, and not to be abused. None can say they have ever known me neglect my gifts! I am thankful that, though my boyhood may be said to have been set apart, like the youth of the royal David, for the purposes of music, no syllable of rude verse has ever profaned my lips.\"\n\n\"You have, then, limited your efforts to sacred song?\"\n\n\"Even so. As the psalms of David exceed all other language, so does the psalmody that has been fitted to them by the divines and sages of the land, surpass all vain poetry. Happily, I may say that I utter nothing but the thoughts and the wishes of the King of Israel himself; for though the times may call for some slight changes, yet does this version which we use in the colonies of New England, so much exceed all other versions, that, by its richness, its exactness, and its spiritual simplicity, it approacheth, as near as may be, to the great work of the inspired writer. I never abide in any place, sleeping or waking, without an example of this gifted work. 'Tis the six-and-twentieth edition, promulgated at Boston, Anno Domini 1744; and is entitled, _The Psalms, Hymns, and Spiritual Songs of the Old and New Testaments; faithfully translated into English Metre, for the Use, Edification, and Comfort of the Saints, in Public and Private, especially in New England_.\"\n\nDuring this eulogium on the rare production of his native poets, the stranger had drawn the book from his pocket, and, fitting a pair of iron-rimmed spectacles to his nose, opened the volume with a care and veneration suited to its sacred purposes. Then, without circumlocution or apology, first pronouncing the word \"Standish,\" and placing the unknown engine, already described, to his mouth, from which he drew a high, shrill sound, that was followed by an octave below, from his own voice, he commenced singing the following words, in full, sweet, and melodious tones, that set the music, the poetry, and even the uneasy motion of his ill-trained beast at defiance:--\n\n\"How good it is, O see, And how it pleaseth well, Together, e'en in unity, For brethren so to dwell. It's like the choice ointment, From the head to the beard did go: Down Aaron's beard, that downward went, His garment's skirts unto.\"\n\nThe delivery of these skilful rhymes was accompanied, on the part of the stranger, by a regular rise and fall of his right hand, which terminated at the descent, by suffering the fingers to dwell a moment on the leaves of the little volume; and on the ascent, by such a flourish of the member as none but the initiated may ever hope to imitate. It would seem that long practice had rendered this manual accompaniment necessary; for it did not cease until the preposition which the poet had selected for the close of his verse, had been duly delivered like a word of two syllables.\n\nSuch an innovation on the silence and retirement of the forest could not fail to enlist the ears of those who journeyed at so short a distance in advance. The Indian muttered a few words in broken English to Heyward, who, in his turn, spoke to the stranger; at once interrupting, and, for the time, closing his musical efforts.\n\n\"Though we are not in danger, common prudence would teach us to journey through this wilderness in as quiet a manner as possible. You will, then, pardon me, Alice, should I diminish your enjoyments, by requesting this gentleman to postpone his chant until a safer opportunity.\"\n\n\"You will diminish them, indeed,\" returned the arch girl, \"for never did I hear a more unworthy conjunction of execution and language, than that to which I have been listening; and I was far gone in a learned inquiry into the causes of such an unfitness between sound and sense, when you broke the charm of my musings by that bass of yours, Duncan!\"\n\n\"I know not what you call my bass,\" said Heyward, piqued at her remark, \"but I know that your safety, and that of Cora, is far dearer to me than could be any orchestra of Handel's music.\" He paused and turned his head quickly towards a thicket, and then bent his eyes suspiciously on their guide, who continued his steady pace, in undisturbed gravity. The young man smiled to himself, for he believed he had mistaken some shining berry of the woods for the glistening eyeballs of a prowling savage, and he rode forward, continuing the conversation which had been interrupted by the passing thought.\n\nMajor Heyward was mistaken only in suffering his youthful and generous pride to suppress his active watchfulness. The cavalcade had not long passed, before the branches of the bushes that formed the thicket were cautiously moved asunder, and a human visage, as fiercely wild as savage art and unbridled passions could make it, peered out on the retiring footsteps of the travellers. A gleam of exultation shot across the darkly painted lineaments of the inhabitant of the forest, as he traced the route of his intended victims, who rode unconsciously onward; the light and graceful forms of the females waving among the trees, in the curvatures of their path, followed at each bend by the manly figure of Heyward, until, finally, the shapeless person of the singing-master was concealed behind the numberless trunks of trees, that rose, in dark lines, in the intermediate space.\n\n\"Before these fields were shorn and tilled, Full to the brim our rivers flowed; The melody of waters filled The fresh and boundless wood; And torrents dashed, and rivulets played, And fountains spouted in the shade.\"\n\nLeaving the unsuspecting Heyward and his confiding companions to penetrate still deeper into a forest that contained such treacherous inmates, we must use an author's privilege, and shift the scene a few miles to the westward of the place where we have last seen them.\n\nOn that day, two men were lingering on the banks of a small but rapid stream, within an hour's journey of the encampment of Webb, like those who awaited the appearance of an absent person, or the approach of some expected event. The vast canopy of woods spread itself to the margin of the river overhanging the water, and shadowing its dark current with a deeper hue. The rays of the sun were beginning to grow less fierce, and the intense heat of the day was lessened, as the cooler vapors of the springs and fountains rose above their leafy beds, and rested in the atmosphere. Still that breathing silence, which marks the drowsy sultriness of an American landscape in July, pervaded the secluded spot, interrupted only by the low voices of the men, the occasional and lazy tap of a woodpecker, the discordant cry of some gaudy jay, or a swelling on the ear, from the dull roar of a distant waterfall.\n\nThese feeble and broken sounds were, however, too familiar to the foresters, to draw their attention from the more interesting matter of their dialogue. While one of these loiterers showed the red skin and wild accoutrements of a native of the woods, the other exhibited, through the mask of his rude and nearly savage equipments, the brighter, though sunburnt and long-faded complexion of one who might claim descent from a European parentage. The former was seated on the end of a mossy log, in a posture that permitted him to heighten the effect of his earnest language, by the calm but expressive gestures of an Indian engaged in debate. His body, which was nearly naked, presented a terrific emblem of death, drawn in intermingled colors of white and black. His closely shaved head, on which no other hair than the well known and chivalrous scalping tuft[5] was preserved, was without ornament of any kind, with the exception of a solitary eagle's plume, that crossed his crown, and depended over the left shoulder. A tomahawk and scalping-knife, of English manufacture, were in his girdle; while a short military rifle, of that sort with which the policy of the whites armed their savage allies, lay carelessly across his bare and sinewy knee. The expanded chest, full formed limbs, and grave countenance of this warrior, would denote that he had reached the vigor of his days, though no symptoms of decay appeared to have yet weakened his manhood.\n\nThe frame of the white man, judging by such parts as were not concealed by his clothes, was like that of one who had known hardships and exertion from his earliest youth. His person, though muscular, was rather attenuated than full; but every nerve and muscle appeared strung and indurated by unremitted exposure and toil. He wore a hunting-shirt of forest green, fringed with faded yellow[6], and a summer cap of skins which had been shorn of their fur. He also bore a knife in a girdle of wampum, like that which confined the scanty garments of the Indian, but no tomahawk. His moccasins were ornamented after the gay fashion of the natives, while the only part of his under-dress which appeared below the hunting-frock, was a pair of buckskin leggings, that laced at the sides, and which were gartered above the knees with the sinews of a deer. A pouch and horn completed his personal accoutrements, though a rifle of great length[7], which the theory of the more ingenious whites had taught them was the most dangerous of all fire-arms, leaned against a neighboring sapling. The eye of the hunter, or scout, whichever he might be, was small, quick, keen, and restless, roving while he spoke, on every side of him, as if in quest of game, or distrusting the sudden approach of some lurking enemy. Notwithstanding the symptoms of habitual suspicion, his countenance was not only without guile, but at the moment at which he is introduced, it was charged with an expression of sturdy honesty.\n\n\"Even your traditions make the case in my favor, Chingachgook,\" he said, speaking in the tongue which was known to all the natives who formerly inhabited the country between the Hudson and the Potomac, and of which we shall give a free translation for the benefit of the reader; endeavoring, at the same time, to preserve some of the peculiarities, both of the individual and of the language. \"Your fathers came from the setting sun, crossed the big river,[8] fought the people of the country, and took the land; and mine came from the red sky of the morning, over the salt lake, and did their work much after the fashion that had been set them by yours; then let God judge the matter between us, and friends spare their words!\"\n\n\"My fathers fought with the naked redmen!\" returned the Indian sternly, in the same language. \"Is there no difference, Hawkeye, between the stone-headed arrow of the warrior, and the leaden bullet with which you kill?\"\n\n\"There is reason in an Indian, though nature has made him with a red skin!\" said the white man, shaking his head like one on whom such an appeal to his justice was not thrown away. For a moment he appeared to be conscious of having the worst of the argument, then, rallying again, he answered the objection of his antagonist in the best manner his limited information would allow: \"I am no scholar, and I care not who knows it; but judging from what I have seen, at deer chases and squirrel hunts, of the sparks below, I should think a rifle in the hands of their grandfathers was not so dangerous as a hickory bow and a good flint-head might be, if drawn with Indian judgment, and sent by an Indian eye.\"\n\n\"You have the story told by your fathers,\" returned the other, coldly waving his hand. \"What say your old men? do they tell the young warriors, that the pale-faces met the redmen, painted for war and armed with the stone hatchet and wooden gun?\"\n\n\"I am not a prejudiced man, nor one who vaunts himself on his natural privileges, though the worst enemy I have on earth, and he is an Iroquois, daren't deny that I am genuine white,\" the scout replied, surveying, with secret satisfaction, the faded color of his bony and sinewy hand; \"and I am willing to own that my people have many ways, of which, as an honest man, I can't approve. It is one of their customs to write in books what they have done and seen, instead of telling them in their villages, where the lie can be given to the face of a cowardly boaster, and the brave soldier can call on his comrades to witness for the truth of his words. In consequence of this bad fashion, a man who is too conscientious to misspend his days among the women, in learning the names of black marks, may never hear of the deeds of his fathers, nor feel a pride in striving to outdo them. For myself, I conclude the Bumppos could shoot, for I have a natural turn with a rifle, which must have been handed down from generation to generation, as, our holy commandments tell us, all good and evil gifts are bestowed; though I should be loth to answer for other people in such a matter. But every story has its two sides; so I ask you, Chingachgook, what passed, according to the traditions of the redmen, when our fathers first met?\"\n\nA silence of a minute succeeded, during which the Indian sat mute; then, full of the dignity of his office, he commenced his brief tale, with a solemnity that served to heighten its appearance of truth.\n\n\"Listen, Hawkeye, and your ear shall drink no lie. 'Tis what my fathers have said, and what the Mohicans have done.\" He hesitated a single instant, and bending a cautious glance toward his companion, he continued, in a manner that was divided between interrogation and assertion, \"Does not this stream at our feet run towards the summer, until its waters grow salt, and the current flows upward?\"\n\n\"It can't be denied that your traditions tell you true in both these matters,\" said the white man; \"for I have been there, and have seen them; though, why water, which is so sweet in the shade, should become bitter in the sun, is an alteration for which I have never been able to account.\"\n\n\"And the current!\" demanded the Indian, who expected his reply with that sort of interest that a man feels in the confirmation of testimony, at which he marvels even while he respects it; \"the fathers of Chingachgook have not lied!\"\n\n\"The Holy Bible is not more true, and that is the truest thing in nature. They call this up-stream current the tide, which is a thing soon explained, and clear enough. Six hours the waters run in, and six hours they run out, and the reason is this: when there is higher water in the sea than in the river, they run in, until the river gets to be highest, and then it runs out again.\"\n\n\"The waters in the woods, and on the great lakes, run downward until they lie like my hand,\" said the Indian, stretching the limb horizontally before him, \"and then they run no more.\"\n\n\"No honest man will deny it,\" said the scout, a little nettled at the implied distrust of his explanation of the mystery of the tides; \"and I grant that it is true on the small scale, and where the land is level. But everything depends on what scale you look at things. Now, on the small scale, the 'arth is level; but on the large scale it is round. In this manner, pools and ponds, and even the great fresh-water lake, may be stagnant, as you and I both know they are, having seen them; but when you come to spread water over a great tract, like the sea, where the earth is round, how in reason can the water be quiet? You might as well expect the river to lie still on the brink of those black rocks a mile above us, though your own ears tell you that it is tumbling over them at this very moment!\"\n\nIf unsatisfied by the philosophy of his companion, the Indian was far too dignified to betray his unbelief. He listened like one who was convinced, and resumed his narrative in his former solemn manner.\n\n\"We came from the place where the sun is hid at night, over great plains where the buffaloes live, until we reached the big river. There we fought the Alligewi, till the ground was red with their blood. From the banks of the big river to the shores of the salt lake, there was none to meet us. The Maquas followed at a distance. We said the country should be ours from the place where the water runs up no longer on this stream, to a river twenty suns' journey toward the summer. The land we had taken like warriors, we kept like men. We drove the Maquas into the woods with the bears. They only tasted salt at the licks; they drew no fish from the great lake; we threw them the bones.\"\n\n\"All this I have heard and believe,\" said the white man, observing that the Indian paused: \"but it was long before the English came into the country.\"\n\n\"A pine grew then where this chestnut now stands. The first pale-faces who came among us spoke no English. They came in a large canoe, when my fathers had buried the tomahawk with the redmen around them. Then, Hawkeye,\" he continued, betraying his deep emotion only by permitting his voice to fall to those low, guttural tones, which rendered his language, as spoken at times, so very musical; \"then, Hawkeye, we were one people, and we were happy. The salt lake gave us its fish, the wood its deer, and the air its birds. We took wives who bore us children; we worshipped the Great Spirit; and we kept the Maquas beyond the sound of our songs of triumph!\"\n\n\"Know you anything of your own family at that time?\" demanded the white. \"But you are a just man, for an Indian! and, as I suppose you hold their gifts, your fathers must have been brave warriors, and wise men at the council fire.\"\n\n\"My tribe is the grandfather of nations, but I am an unmixed man. The blood of chiefs is in my veins, where it must stay forever. The Dutch landed, and gave my people the fire-water; they drank until the heavens and the earth seemed to meet, and they foolishly thought they had found the Great Spirit. Then they parted with their land. Foot by foot, they were driven back from the shores, until I, that am a chief and a sagamore, have never seen the sun shine but through the trees, and have never visited the graves of, my fathers!\"\n\n\"Graves bring solemn feelings over the mind,\" returned the scout, a good deal touched at the calm suffering of his companion; \"and they often aid a man in his good intentions; though, for myself, I expect to leave my own bones unburied, to bleach in the woods, or to be torn asunder by the wolves. But where are to be found those of your race who came to their kin in the Delaware country, so many summers since?\"\n\n\"Where are the blossoms of those summers!--fallen, one by one: so all of my family departed, each in his turn, to the land of spirits. I am on the hill-top, and must go down into the valley; and when Uncas follows in my footsteps, there will no longer be any of the blood of the sagamores, for my boy is the last of the Mohicans.\"\n\n\"Uncas is here!\" said another voice, in the same soft, guttural tones, near his elbow; \"who speaks to Uncas?\"\n\nThe white man loosened his knife in his leathern sheath, and made an involuntary movement of the hand towards his rifle, at this sudden interruption; but the Indian sat composed, and without turning his head at the unexpected sounds.\n\nAt the next instant, a youthful warrior passed between them, with a noiseless step, and seated himself on the bank of the rapid stream. No exclamation of surprise escaped the father, nor was any question asked, or reply given, for several minutes; each appearing to await the moment when he might speak, without betraying womanish curiosity or childish impatience. The white man seemed to take counsel from their customs, and, relinquishing his grasp of the rifle, he also remained silent and reserved. At length Chingachgook turned his eyes slowly towards his son, and demanded,--\n\n\"Do the Maquas dare to leave the print of their moccasins in these woods?\"\n\n\"I have been on their trail,\" replied the young Indian, \"and know that they number as many as the fingers of my two hands; but they lie hid, like cowards.\"\n\n\"The thieves are outlying for scalps and plunder!\" said the white man, whom we shall call Hawkeye, after the manner of his companions. \"That bushy Frenchman, Montcalm, will send his spies into our very camp, but he will know what road we travel!\"\n\n\"Tis enough!\" returned the father, glancing his eye towards the setting sun; \"they shall be driven like deer from their bushes. Hawkeye, let us eat to-night, and show the Maquas that we are men to-morrow.\"\n\n\"I am as ready to do the one as the other; but to fight the Iroquois 'tis necessary to find the skulkers; and to eat, 'tis necessary to get the game--talk of the devil and he will come; there is a pair of the biggest antlers I have seen this season, moving the bushes below the hill! Now, Uncas,\" he continued in a half whisper, and laughing with a kind of inward sound, like one who had learnt to be watchful, \"I will bet my charger three times full of powder, against a foot of wampum, that I take him atwixt the eyes, and nearer to the right than to the left.\"\n\"It cannot be!\" said the young Indian, springing to his feet with youthful eagerness; \"all but the tips of his horns are hid!\"\n\n\"He's a boy!\" said the white man, shaking his head while he spoke, and addressing the father. \"Does he think when a hunter sees a part of the creatur', he can't tell where the rest of him should be!\"\n\n[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n_Avoiding the horns of the infuriated animal, Uncas darted to his side, and passed his knife across the throat_]\n\nAdjusting his rifle, he was about to make an exhibition of that skill, on which he so much valued himself, when the warrior struck up the piece with his hand, saying--\n\n\"Hawkeye! will you fight the Maquas?\"\n\n\"These Indians know the nature of the woods, as it might be by instinct!\" returned the scout, dropping his rifle, and turning away like a man who was convinced of his error. \"I must leave the buck to your arrow, Uncas, or we may kill a deer for them thieves, the Iroquois, to eat.\"\n\nThe instant the father seconded this intimation by an expressive gesture of the hand, Uncas threw himself on the ground, and approached the animal with wary movements. When within a few yards of the cover, he fitted an arrow to his bow with the utmost care, while the antlers moved, as if their owner snuffed an enemy in the tainted air. In another moment the twang of the cord was heard, a white streak was seen glancing into the bushes, and the wounded buck plunged from the cover, to the very feet of his hidden enemy. Avoiding the horns of the infuriated animal, Uncas darted to his side, and passed his knife across the throat, when bounding to the edge of the river it fell, dyeing the waters with its blood.\n\n\"'Twas done with Indian skill,\" said the scout, laughing inwardly, but with vast satisfaction; \"and 'twas a pretty sight to behold! Though an arrow is a near shot, and needs a knife to finish the work.\"\n\n\"Hugh!\" ejaculated his companion, turning quickly, like a hound who scented game.\n\n\"By the Lord, there is a drove of them!\" exclaimed the scout, whose eyes began to glisten with the ardor of his usual occupation; \"if they come within range of a bullet I will drop one, though the whole Six Nations should be lurking within sound! What do you hear, Chingachgook? for to my ears the woods are dumb.\"\n\n\"There is but one deer, and he is dead,\" said the Indian, bending his body till his ear nearly touched the earth. \"I hear the sounds of feet!\"\n\n\"Perhaps the wolves have driven the buck to shelter, and are following on his trail.\"\n\n\"No. The horses of white men are coming!\" returned the other, raising himself with dignity, and resuming his seat on the log with his former composure. \"Hawkeye, they are your brothers; speak to them.\"\n\n\"That will I, and in English that the king needn't be ashamed to answer,\" returned the hunter, speaking in the language of which he boasted; \"but I see nothing, nor do I hear the sounds of man or beast; 'tis strange that an Indian should understand white sounds better than a man who, his very enemies will own, has no cross in his blood, although he may have lived with the redskins long enough to be suspected! Ha! there goes something like the cracking of a dry stick, too--now I hear the bushes move--yes, yes, there is a trampling that I mistook for the falls--and--but here they come themselves; God keep them from the Iroquois!\"\n\n\"Well, go thy way: thou shalt not from this grove Till I torment thee for this injury.\"\n\n_Midsummer Night's Dream._\n\nThe words were still in the mouth of the scout, when the leader of the party, whose approaching footsteps had caught the vigilant ear of the Indian, came openly into view. A beaten path, such as those made by the periodical passage of the deer, wound through a little glen at no great distance, and struck the river at the point where the white man and his red companions had posted themselves. Along this track the travellers, who had produced a surprise so unusual in the depths of the forest, advanced slowly towards the hunter, who was in front of his associates, in readiness to receive them.\n\n\"Who comes?\" demanded the scout, throwing his rifle carelessly across his left arm, and keeping the forefinger of his right hand on the trigger, though he avoided all appearance of menace in the act, \"Who comes hither, among the beasts and dangers of the wilderness?\"\n\n\"Believers in religion, and friends to the law and to the king,\" returned he who rode foremost. \"Men who have journeyed since the rising sun, in the shades of this forest, without nourishment, and are sadly tired of their wayfaring.\"\n\n\"You are, then, lost,\" interrupted the hunter, \"and have found how helpless 'tis not to know whether to take the right hand or the left?\"\n\n\"Even so; sucking babes are not more dependent on those who guide them than we who are of larger growth, and who may now be said to possess the stature without the knowledge of men. Know you the distance to a post of the crown called William Henry?\"\n\n\"Hoot!\" shouted the scout, who did not spare his open laughter, though, instantly checking the dangerous sounds, he indulged his merriment at less risk of being overheard by any lurking enemies. \"You are as much off the scent as a hound would be, with Horican atwixt him and the deer! William Henry, man! if you are friends to the king, and have business with the army, your better way would be to follow the river down to Edward, and lay the matter before Webb; who tarries there, instead of pushing into the defiles, and driving this saucy Frenchman back across Champlain, into his den again.\"\n\nBefore the stranger could make any reply to this unexpected proposition, another horseman dashed the bushes aside, and leaped his charger into the pathway, in front of his companion.\n\n\"What, then, may be our distance from Fort Edward?\" demanded a new speaker; \"the place you advise us to seek we left this morning, and our destination is the head of the lake.\"\n\n\"Then you must have lost your eyesight afore losing your way, for the road across the portage is cut to a good two rods, and is as grand a path, I calculate, as any that runs into London, or even before the palace of the king himself.\"\n\n\"We will not dispute concerning the excellence of the passage,\" returned Heyward, smiling; for, as the reader has anticipated, it was he. \"It is enough, for the present, that we trusted to an Indian guide to take us by a nearer, though blinder path, and that we are deceived in his knowledge. In plain words, we know not where we are.\"\n\n\"An Indian lost in the woods!\" said the scout, shaking his head doubtingly; \"when the sun is scorching the tree-tops, and the water-courses are full; when the moss on every beech he sees, will tell him in which quarter the north star will shine at night! The woods are full of deer paths which run to the streams and licks, places well known to everybody; nor have the geese done their flight to the Canada waters altogether! 'Tis strange that an Indian should be lost atwixt Horican and the bend in the river. Is he a Mohawk?\"\n\n\"Not by birth, though adopted in that tribe; I think his birthplace was farther north, and he is one of those you call a Huron.\"\n\n\"Hugh!\" exclaimed the two companions of the scout, who had continued, until this part of the dialogue, seated immovable, and apparently indifferent to what passed, but who now sprang to their feet with an activity and interest that had evidently got the better of their reserve, by surprise.\n\n\"A Huron!\" repeated the sturdy scout, once more shaking his head in open distrust; \"they are a thievish race, nor do I care by whom they are adopted; you can never make anything of them but skulks and vagabonds. Since you trusted yourself to the care of one of that nation, I only wonder that you have not fallen in with more.\"\n\n\"Of that there is little danger, since William Henry is so many miles in our front. You forget that I have told you our guide is now a Mohawk, and that he serves with our forces as a friend.\"\n\n\"And I tell you that he who is born a Mingo will die a Mingo,\" returned the other, positively. \"A Mohawk! No, give me a Delaware or a Mohican for honesty; and when they will fight, which they won't all do, having suffered their cunning enemies, the Maquas, to make them women--but when they will fight at all, look to a Delaware, or a Mohican, for a warrior!\"\n\n\"Enough of this,\" said Heyward, impatiently; \"I wish not to inquire into the character of a man that I know, and to whom you must be a stranger. You have not yet answered my question: what is our distance from the main army at Edward?\"\n\n\"It seems that may depend on who is your guide. One would think such a horse as that might get over a good deal of ground atwixt sun-up and sun-down.\"\n\n\"I wish no contention of idle words with you, friend,\" said Heyward, curbing his dissatisfied manner, and speaking in a more gentle voice; \"if you will tell me the distance to Fort Edward, and conduct me thither, your labor shall not go without its reward.\"\n\n\"And in so doing, how know I that I don't guide an enemy, and a spy of Montcalm, to the works of the army? It is not every man who can speak the English tongue that is an honest subject.\"\n\n\"If you serve with the troops, of whom I judge you to be a scout, you should know of such a regiment of the king as the 60th.\"\n\n\"The 60th! you can tell me little of the Royal Americans that I don't know, though I do wear a hunting-shirt instead of a scarlet jacket.\"\n\n\"Well, then, among the other things, you may know the name of its major?\"\n\n\"Its major!\" interrupted the hunter, elevating his body like one who was proud of his trust. \"If there is a man in the country who knows Major Effingham, he stands before you.\"\n\n\"It is a corps which has many majors; the gentleman you name is the senior, but I speak of the junior of them all; he who commands the companies in garrison at William Henry.\"\n\n\"Yes, yes, I have heard that a young gentleman of vast riches, from one of the provinces far south, has got the place. He is over young, too, to hold such rank, and to be put above men whose heads are beginning to bleach; and yet they say he is a soldier in his knowledge, and a gallant gentleman!\"\n\n\"Whatever he may be, or however he may be qualified for his rank, he now speaks to you, and of course can be no enemy to dread.\"\n\nThe scout regarded Heyward in surprise, and then lifting his cap, he answered, in a tone less confident than before, though still expressing doubt,--\n\n\"I have heard a party was to leave the encampment this morning, for the lake shore.\"\n\n\"You have heard the truth; but I preferred a nearer route, trusting to the knowledge of the Indian I mentioned.\"\n\n\"And he deceived you, and then deserted?\"\n\n\"Neither, as I believe; certainly not the latter, for he is to be found in the rear.\"\n\n\"I should like to look at the creatur'; if it is a true Iroquois I can tell him by his knavish look, and by his paint,\" said the scout, stepping past the charger of Heyward, and entering the path behind the mare of the singing-master, whose foal had taken advantage of the halt to exact the maternal contribution. After shoving aside the bushes, and proceeding a few paces, he encountered the females, who awaited the result of the conference with anxiety, and not entirely without apprehension. Behind these, the runner leaned against a tree, where he stood the close examination of the scout with an air unmoved, though with a look so dark and savage, that it might in itself excite fear. Satisfied with his scrutiny, the hunter soon left him. As he repassed the females, he paused a moment to gaze upon their beauty, answering to the smile and nod of Alice with a look of open pleasure. Thence he went to the side of the motherly animal, and spending a minute in a fruitless inquiry into the character of her rider, he shook his head and returned to Heyward.\n\n\"A Mingo is a Mingo, and God having made him so, neither the Mohawks nor any other tribe can alter him,\" he said, when he had regained his former position. \"If we were alone, and you would leave that noble horse at the mercy of the wolves to-night, I could show you the way to Edward, myself, within an hour, for it lies only about an hour's journey hence; but with such ladies in your company 'tis impossible!\"\n\n\"And why? they are fatigued, but they are quite equal to a ride of a few more miles.\"\n\n\"'Tis a natural impossibility!\" repeated the scout; \"I wouldn't walk a mile in these woods after night gets into them, in company with that runner, for the best rifle in the colonies. They are full of outlying Iroquois, and your mongrel Mohawk knows where to find them too well, to be my companion.\"\n\n\"Think you so?\" said Heyward, leaning forward in the saddle, and dropping his voice nearly to a whisper; \"I confess I have not been without my own suspicions, though I have endeavored to conceal them, and affected a confidence I have not always felt, on account of my companions. It was because I suspected him that I would follow no longer; making him, as you see, follow me.\"\n\n\"I knew he was one of the cheats as soon as I laid eyes on him!\" returned the scout, placing a finger on his nose, in sign of caution. \"The thief is leaning against the foot of the sugar sapling, that you can see over them bushes; his right leg is in a line with the bark of the tree, and,\" tapping his rifle, \"I can take him from where I stand, between the ankle and the knee, with a single shot, putting an end to his tramping through the woods, for at least a month to come. If I should go back to him, the cunning varmint would suspect something, and be dodging through the trees like a frightened deer.\"\n\n\"It will not do. He may be innocent, and I dislike the act. Though, if I felt confident of his treachery--\"\n\n\"'Tis a safe thing to calculate on the knavery of an Iroquois,\" said the scout, throwing his rifle forward, by a sort of instinctive movement.\n\n\"Hold!\" interrupted Heyward, \"it will not do--we must think of some other scheme; and yet, I have much reason to believe the rascal has deceived me.\"\n\nThe hunter, who had already abandoned his intention of maiming the runner, mused a moment, and then made a gesture, which instantly brought his two red companions to his side. They spoke together earnestly in the Delaware language, though in an undertone; and by the gestures of the white man, which were frequently directed towards the top of the sapling, it was evident he pointed out the situation of their hidden enemy. His companions were not long in comprehending his wishes, and laying aside their fire-arms, they parted, taking opposite sides of the path, and burying themselves in the thicket, with such cautious movements, that their steps were inaudible.\n\n\"Now, go you back,\" said the hunter, speaking again to Heyward, \"and hold the imp in talk; these Mohicans here will take him without breaking his paint.\"\n\n\"Nay,\" said Heyward, proudly, \"I will seize him myself.\"\n\n\"Hist! what could you do, mounted, against an Indian in the bushes?\"\n\n\"I will dismount.\"\n\n\"And, think you, when he saw one of your feet out of the stirrup, he would wait for the other to be free? Whoever comes into the woods to deal with the natives, must use Indian fashions, if he would wish to prosper in his undertakings. Go, then, talk openly to the miscreant, and seem to believe him the truest friend you have on 'arth.\"\n\nHeyward prepared to comply, though with strong disgust at the nature of the office he was compelled to execute. Each moment, however, pressed upon him a conviction of the critical situation in which he had suffered his invaluable trust to be involved through his own confidence. The sun had already disappeared, and the woods, suddenly deprived of his light,[9] were assuming a dusky hue, which keenly reminded him that the hour the savage usually chose for his most barbarous and remorseless acts of vengeance or hostility, was speedily drawing near. Stimulated by apprehension, he left the scout, who immediately entered into a loud conversation with the stranger that had so unceremoniously enlisted himself in the party of travellers that morning. In passing his gentler companions Heyward uttered a few words of encouragement, and was pleased to find that, though fatigued with the exercise of the day, they appeared to entertain no suspicion that their present embarrassment was other than the result of accident. Giving them reason to believe he was merely employed in a consultation concerning the future route, he spurred his charger, and drew the reins again, when the animal had carried him within a few yards of the place where the sullen runner still stood, leaning against the tree.\n\n\"You may see, Magua,\" he said, endeavoring to assume an air of freedom and confidence, \"that the night is closing around us, and yet we are no nearer to William Henry than when we left the encampment of Webb with the rising sun. You have missed the way, nor have I been more fortunate. But, happily we have fallen in with a hunter, he whom you hear talking to the singer, that is acquainted with the deer-paths and by-ways of the woods, and who promises to lead us to a place where we may rest securely till the morning.\"\n\nThe Indian riveted his glowing eyes on Heyward as he asked, in his imperfect English, \"Is he alone?\"\n\n\"Alone!\" hesitatingly answered Heyward to whom deception was too new to be assumed without embarrassment. \"O! not alone, surely, Magua, for you know that we are with him.\"\n\n\"Then Le Renard Subtil will go,\" returned the runner, coolly raising his little wallet from the place where it had lain at his feet; \"and the pale-faces will see none but their own color.\"\n\n\"Go! Whom call you Le Renard?\"\n\n\"'Tis the name his Canada fathers have given to Magua,\" returned the runner, with an air that manifested his pride at the distinction. \"Night is the same as day to Le Subtil, when Munro waits for him.\"\n\n\"And what account will Le Renard give the chief of William Henry concerning his daughters? Will he dare to tell the hot-blooded Scotsman that his children are left without a guide, though Magua promised to be one?\"\n\n\"Though the gray head has a loud voice, and a long arm, Le Renard will not hear him, or feel him, in the woods.\"\n\n\"But what will the Mohawks say? They will make him petticoats, and bid him stay in the wigwam with the women, for he is no longer to be trusted with the business of a man.\"\n\n\"Le Subtil knows the path to the great lakes, and he can find the bones of his fathers,\" was the answer of the unmoved runner.\n\n\"Enough, Magua,\" said Heyward; \"are we not friends? Why should there be bitter words between us? Munro has promised you a gift for your services when performed, and I shall be your debtor for another. Rest your weary limbs, then, and open your wallet to eat. We have a few moments to spare; let us not waste them in talk like wrangling women. When the ladies are refreshed we will proceed.\"\n\n\"The pale-faces make themselves dogs to their women,\" muttered the Indian, in his native language, \"and when they want to eat, their warriors must lay aside the tomahawk to feed their laziness.\"\n\n\"What say you, Renard?\"\n\n\"Le Subtil says it is good.\"\n\nThe Indian then fastened his eyes keenly on the open countenance of Heyward, but meeting his glance, he turned them quickly away, and seating himself deliberately on the ground, he drew forth the remnant of some former repast, and began to eat, though not without first bending his looks slowly and cautiously around him.\n\n\"This is well,\" continued Heyward; \"and Le Renard will have strength and sight to find the path in the morning;\" he paused, for sounds like the snapping of a dried stick, and the rustling of leaves, rose from the adjacent bushes, but recollecting himself instantly, he continued,--\"we must be moving before the sun is seen, or Montcalm may lie in our path, and shut us out from the fortress.\"\n\nThe hand of Magua dropped from his mouth to his side, and though his eyes were fastened on the ground, his head was turned aside, his nostrils expanded, and his ears seemed even to stand more erect than usual, giving to him the appearance of a statue that was made to represent intense attention.\n\nHeyward, who watched his movements with a vigilant eye, carelessly extricated one of his feet from the stirrup, while he passed a hand towards the bear-skin covering of his holsters. Every effort to detect the point most regarded by the runner was completely frustrated by the tremulous glances of his organs, which seemed not to rest a single instant on any particular object, and which, at the same time, could be hardly said to move. While he hesitated how to proceed, Le Subtil cautiously raised himself to his feet, though with a motion so slow and guarded, that not the slightest noise was produced by the change. Heyward felt it had now become incumbent on him to act. Throwing his leg over the saddle, he dismounted, with a determination to advance and seize his treacherous companion, trusting the result to his own manhood. In order, however, to prevent unnecessary alarm, he still preserved an air of calmness and friendship.\n\n\"Le Renard Subtil does not eat,\" he said, using the appellation he had found most flattering to the vanity of the Indian. \"His corn is not well parched, and it seems dry. Let me examine; perhaps something may be found among my own provisions that will help his appetite.\"\n\nMagua held out the wallet to the proffer of the other. He even suffered their hands to meet, without betraying the least emotion, or varying his riveted attitude of attention. But when he felt the fingers of Heyward moving gently along his own naked arm, he struck up the limb of the young man, and uttering a piercing cry as he darted beneath it, plunged, at a single bound, into the opposite thicket. At the next instant the form of Chingachgook appeared from the bushes, looking like a spectre in its paint, and glided across the path in swift pursuit. Next followed the shout of Uncas, when the woods were lighted by a sudden flash, that was accompanied by the sharp report of the hunter's rifle.\n\n\"In such a night Did Thisbe fearfully o'ertrip the dew; And saw the lion's shadow ere himself.\"\n\n_Merchant of Venice._\n\nThe suddenness of the flight of his guide, and the wild cries of the pursuers, caused Heyward to remain fixed, for a few moments, in inactive surprise. Then recollecting the importance of securing the fugitive, he dashed aside the surrounding bushes, and pressed eagerly forward to lend his aid in the chase. Before he had, however, proceeded a hundred yards, he met the three foresters already returning from their unsuccessful pursuit.\n\n\"Why so soon disheartened!\" he exclaimed; \"the scoundrel must be concealed behind some of these trees, and may yet be secured. We are not safe while he goes at large.\"\n\n\"Would you set a cloud to chase the wind?\" returned the disappointed scout; \"I heard the imp, brushing over the dry leaves, like a black snake, and blinking a glimpse of him, just over ag'in yon big pine, I pulled as it might be on the scent; but 'twouldn't do! and yet for a reasoning aim, if anybody but myself had touched the trigger, I should call it a quick sight; and I may be accounted to have experience in these matters, and one who ought to know. Look at this sumach; its leaves are red, though everybody knows the fruit is in the yellow blossom, in the month of July!\"\n\n\"'Tis the blood of Le Subtil! he is hurt, and may yet fall!\"\n\n\"No, no,\" returned the scout, in decided disapprobation of this opinion, \"I rubbed the bark off a limb, perhaps, but the creature leaped the longer for it. A rifle-bullet acts on a running animal, when it barks him, much the same as one of your spurs on a horse; that is, it quickens motion, and puts life into the flesh, instead of taking it away. But when it cuts the ragged hole, after a bound or two, there is, commonly, a stagnation of further leaping, be it Indian or be it deer!\"\n\n\"We are four able bodies, to one wounded man!\"\n\n\"Is life grievous to you?\" interrupted the scout. \"Yonder red devils would draw you within swing of the tomahawks of his comrades, before you were heated in the chase. It was an unthoughtful act in a man who has so often slept with the war-whoop ringing in the air, to let off his piece within sound of an ambushment! But then it was a natural temptation! 'twas very natural! Come, friends, let us move our station, and in such a fashion, too, as will throw the cunning of a Mingo on a wrong scent, or our scalps will be drying in the wind in front of Montcalm's marquee, ag'in this hour to-morrow.\"\n\nThis appalling declaration, which the scout uttered with the cool assurance of a man who fully comprehended, while he did not fear to face the danger, served to remind Heyward of the importance of the charge with which he himself had been intrusted. Glancing his eyes around, with a vain effort to pierce the gloom that was thickening beneath the leafy arches of the forest, he felt as if, cut off from human aid, his unresisting companions would soon lie at the entire mercy of those barbarous enemies, who, like beasts of prey, only waited till the gathering darkness might render their blows more fatally certain. His awakened imagination, deluded by the deceptive light, converted each waving bush, or the fragment of some fallen tree, into human forms, and twenty times he fancied he could distinguish the horrid visages of his lurking foes, peering from their hiding-places, in never-ceasing watchfulness of the movements of his party. Looking upward, he found that the thin fleecy clouds, which evening had painted on the blue sky, were already losing their faintest tints of rose-color, while the imbedded stream, which glided past the spot where he stood, was to be traced only by the dark boundary of its wooded banks.\n\n\"What is to be done?\" he said, feeling the utter helplessness of doubt in such a pressing strait; \"desert me not, for God's sake! remain to defend those I escort, and freely name your own reward!\"\n\nHis companions, who conversed apart in the language of their tribe, heeded not this sudden and earnest appeal. Though their dialogue was maintained in low and cautious sounds, but little above a whisper, Heyward, who now approached, could easily distinguish the earnest tones of the younger warrior from the more deliberate speeches of his seniors. It was evident that they debated on the propriety of some measure that nearly concerned the welfare of the travellers. Yielding to his powerful interest in the subject, and impatient of a delay that seemed fraught with so much additional danger, Heyward drew still nigher to the dusky group, with an intention of making his offers of compensation more definite, when the white man, motioning, with his hand, as if he conceded the disputed point, turned away, saying in a sort of soliloquy, and in the English tongue,--\n\n\"Uncas is right! it would not be the act of men to leave such harmless things to their fate, even though it breaks up the harboring place forever. If you would save these tender blossoms from the fangs of the worst of serpents, gentleman, you have neither time to lose nor resolution to throw away!\"\n\n\"How can such a wish be doubted! have I not already offered--\"\n\n\"Offer your prayers to Him who can give us wisdom to circumvent the cunning of the devils who fill these woods,\" calmly interrupted the scout, \"but spare your offers of money, which neither you may live to realize, nor I to profit by. These Mohicans and I will do what man's thoughts can invent, to keep such flowers, which, though so sweet, were never made for the wilderness, from harm, and that without hope of any other recompense but such as God always gives to upright dealings. First, you must promise two things, both in your own name and for your friends, or without serving you, we shall only injure ourselves!\"\n\n\"Name them.\"\n\n\"The one is, to be still as these sleeping woods, let what will happen; and the other is, to keep the place where we shall take you, forever a secret from all mortal men.\"\n\n\"I will do my utmost to see both these conditions fulfilled.\"\n\n\"Then follow, for we are losing moments that are as precious as the heart's blood to a stricken deer!\"\n\nHeyward could distinguish the impatient gesture of the scout, through the increasing shadows of the evening, and he moved in his footsteps, swiftly, towards the place where he had left the remainder of his party. When they rejoined the expecting and anxious females, he briefly acquainted them with the conditions of their new guide, and with the necessity that existed for their hushing every apprehension, in instant and serious exertions. Although his alarming communication was not received without much secret terror by the listeners, his earnest and impressive manner, aided perhaps by the nature of the danger, succeeded in bracing their nerves to undergo some unlooked-for and unusual trial. Silently, and without a moment's delay, they permitted him to assist them from their saddles, when they descended quickly to the water's edge, where the scout had collected the rest of the party, more by the agency of expressive gestures than by any use of words.\n\n\"What to do with these dumb creatures!\" muttered the white man, on whom the sole control of their future movements appeared to devolve; \"it would be time lost to cut their throats, and cast them into the river; and to leave them here, would be to tell the Mingos that they have not far to seek to find their owners!\"\n\n\"Then give them their bridles, and let them range the woods,\" Heyward ventured to suggest.\n\n\"No; it would be better to mislead the imps, and make them believe they must equal a horse's speed to run down their chase. Ay, ay, that will blind their fire-balls of eyes! Chingach--Hist? what stirs the bush?\"\n\n\"The colt.\"\n\n\"That colt, at least, must die,\" muttered the scout, grasping the mane of the nimble beast, which easily eluded his hand; \"Uncas, your arrows!\"\n\n\"Hold!\" exclaimed the proprietor of the condemned animal, aloud, without regard to the whispering tones used by the others; \"spare the foal of Miriam! it is the comely offspring of a faithful dam, and would willingly injure naught.\"\n\n\"When men struggle for the single life God has given them,\" said the scout sternly, \"even their own kind seem no more than the beasts of the wood. If you speak again, I shall leave you to the mercy of the Maquas! Draw to your arrow's head, Uncas; we have no time for second blows.\"\n\nThe low, muttering sounds of his threatening voice were still audible, when the wounded foal, first rearing on its hinder legs, plunged forward to its knees. It was met by Chingachgook, whose knife passed across its throat quicker than thought, and then precipitating the motions of the struggling victim, he dashed it into the river, down whose stream it glided away, gasping audibly for breath with its ebbing life. This deed of apparent cruelty, but of real necessity, fell upon the spirits of the travellers like a terrific warning of the peril in which they stood, heightened as it was by the calm though steady resolution of the actors in the scene. The sisters shuddered and clung closer to each other, while Heyward instinctively laid his hand on one of the pistols he had just drawn from their holsters, as he placed himself between his charge and those dense shadows that seemed to draw an impenetrable veil before the bosom of the forest.\n\nThe Indians, however, hesitated not a moment, but taking the bridles, they led the frightened and reluctant horses into the bed of the river.\n\nAt a short distance from the shore they turned, and were soon concealed by the projection of the bank, under the brow of which they moved, in a direction opposite to the course of the waters. In the meantime, the scout drew a canoe of bark from its place of concealment beneath some low bushes, whose branches were waving with the eddies of the current, into which he silently motioned for the females to enter. They complied without hesitation, though many a fearful and anxious glance was thrown behind them towards the thickening gloom which now lay like a dark barrier along the margin of the stream.\n\nSo soon as Cora and Alice were seated, the scout, without regarding the element, directed Heyward to support one side of the frail vessel, and posting himself at the other, they bore it up against the stream, followed by the dejected owner of the dead foal. In this manner they proceeded, for many rods, in a silence that was only interrupted by the rippling of the water, as its eddies played around them, or the low dash made by their own cautious footsteps. Heyward yielded the guidance of the canoe implicitly to the scout, who approached or receded from the shore, to avoid the fragments of rocks, or deeper parts of the river, with a readiness that showed his knowledge of the route they held. Occasionally he would stop; and in the midst of a breathing stillness, that the dull but increasing roar of the waterfall only served to render more impressive, he would listen with painful intenseness, to catch any sounds that might arise from the slumbering forest. When assured that all was still, and unable to detect, even by the aid of his practised senses, any sign of his approaching foes, he would deliberately resume his slow and unguarded progress. At length they reached a point in the river, where the roving eye of Heyward became riveted on a cluster of black objects, collected at a spot where the high bank threw a deeper shadow than usual on the dark waters. Hesitating to advance, he pointed out the place to the attention of his companion.\n\n\"Ay,\" returned the composed scout, \"the Indians have hid the beasts with the judgment of natives! Water leaves no trail, and an owl's eyes would be blinded by the darkness of such a hole.\"\n\nThe whole party was soon reunited, and another consultation was held between the scout and his new comrades, during which, they whose fates depended on the faith and ingenuity of these unknown foresters, had a little leisure to observe their situation more minutely.\n\nThe river was confined between high and cragged rocks, one of which impended above the spot where the canoe rested. As these, again, were surmounted by tall trees, which appeared to totter on the brows of the precipice, it gave the stream the appearance of running through a deep and narrow dell. All beneath the fantastic limbs and ragged tree-tops, which were, here and there, dimly painted against the starry zenith, lay alike in shadowed obscurity. Behind them, the curvature of the banks soon bounded the view, by the same dark and wooded outline; but in front, and apparently at no great distance, the water seemed piled against the heavens, whence it tumbled into caverns, out of which issued those sullen sounds that had loaded the evening atmosphere. It seemed, in truth, to be a spot devoted to seclusion, and the sisters imbibed a soothing impression of security, as they gazed upon its romantic, though not unappalling beauties. A general movement among their conductors, however, soon recalled them from a contemplation of the wild charms that night had assisted to lend the place, to a painful sense of their real peril.\n\nThe horses had been secured to some scattered shrubs that grew in the fissures of the rocks, where, standing in the water, they were left to pass the night. The scout directed Heyward and his disconsolate fellow-travellers to seat themselves in the forward end of the canoe, and took possession of the other himself, as erect and steady as if he floated in a vessel of much firmer materials. The Indians warily retraced their steps towards the place they had left, when the scout, placing his pole against a rock, by a powerful shove, sent his frail bark directly into the centre of the turbulent stream. For many minutes the struggle between the light bubble in which they floated, and the swift current, was severe and doubtful. Forbidden to stir even a hand, and almost afraid to breathe, lest they should expose the frail fabric to the fury of the stream, the passengers watched the glancing waters in feverish suspense. Twenty times they thought the whirling eddies were sweeping them to destruction, when the master-hand of their pilot would bring the bows of the canoe to stem the rapid. A long, a vigorous, and, as it appeared to the females, a desperate effort, closed the struggle. Just as Alice veiled her eyes in horror, under the impression that they were about to be swept within the vortex at the foot of the cataract, the canoe floated, stationary, at the side of a flat rock, that lay on a level with the water.\n\n\"Where are we? and what is next to be done?\" demanded Heyward, perceiving that the exertions of the scout had ceased.\n\n\"You are at the foot of Glenn's,\" returned the other, speaking aloud, without fear of consequences, within the roar of the cataract; \"and the next thing is to make a steady landing, lest the canoe upset, and you should go down again the hard road we have travelled, faster than you came up; 'tis a hard rift to stem, when the river is a little swelled; and five is an unnatural number to keep dry, in the hurry-skurry, with a little birchen bark and gum. There, go you all on the rock, and I will bring up the Mohicans with the venison. A man had better sleep without his scalp, than famish in the midst of plenty.\"\n\nHis passengers gladly complied with these directions. As the last foot touched the rock, the canoe whirled from its station, when the tall form of the scout was seen, for an instant, gliding above the waters, before it disappeared in the impenetrable darkness that rested on the bed of the river. Left by their guide, the travellers remained a few minutes in helpless ignorance, afraid even to move along the broken rocks, lest a false step should precipitate them down some one of the many deep and roaring caverns, into which the water seemed to tumble, on every side of them. Their suspense, however, was soon relieved; for aided by the skill of the natives, the canoe shot back into the eddy, and floated again at the side of the low rock before they thought the scout had even time to rejoin his companions.\n\n\"We are now fortified, garrisoned, and provisioned,\" cried Heyward, cheerfully, \"and may set Montcalm and his allies at defiance. How, now, my vigilant sentinel, can you see anything of those you call the Iroquois, on the mainland?\"\n\n\"I call them Iroquois, because to me every native, who speaks a foreign tongue, is accounted an enemy, though he may pretend to serve the king! If Webb wants faith and honesty in an Indian, let him bring out the tribes of the Delawares, and send these greedy and lying Mohawks and Oneidas, with their six nations of varlets, where in nature they belong, among the French!\"\n\n\"We should then exchange a warlike for a useless friend! I have heard that the Delawares have laid aside the hatchet, and are content to be called women!\"\n\n\"Ay, shame on the Hollanders[10] and Iroquois, who circumvented them by their deviltries, into such a treaty! But I have known them for twenty years, and I call him liar, that says cowardly blood runs in the veins of a Delaware. You have driven their tribes from the sea-shore, and would now believe what their enemies say, that you may sleep at night upon an easy pillow. No, no; to me, every Indian who speaks a foreign tongue is an Iroquois, whether the castle[11] of his tribe be in Canada, or be in New York.\"\n\nHeyward, perceiving that the stubborn adherence of the scout to the cause of his friends the Delawares or Mohicans, for they were branches of the same numerous people, was likely to prolong a useless discussion, changed the subject.\n\n\"Treaty or no treaty, I know full well, that your two companions are brave and cautious warriors! have they heard or seen anything of our enemies?\"\n\n\"An Indian is a mortal to be felt afore he is seen,\" returned the scout, ascending the rock, and throwing the deer carelessly down. \"I trust to other signs than such as come in at the eye, when I am outlying on the trail of the Mingos.\"\n\n\"Do your ears tell you that they have traced our retreat?\"\n\n\"I should be sorry to think they had, though this is a spot that stout courage might hold for a smart skrimmage. I will not deny, however, but the horses cowered when I passed them, as though they scented the wolves; and a wolf is a beast that is apt to hover about an Indian ambushment, craving the offals of the deer the savages kill.\"\n\n\"You forget the buck at your feet! or, may we not owe their visit to the dead colt? Ha! what noise is that?\"\n\n\"Poor Miriam!\" murmured the stranger; \"thy foal was foreordained to become a prey to ravenous beasts!\" Then, suddenly lifting up his voice, amid the eternal din of the waters, he sang aloud,--\n\n\"First born of Egypt, smite did He, Of mankind, and of beast also; O, Egypt! wonders sent 'midst thee, On Pharaoh and his servants too!\"\n\n\"The death of the colt sits heavy on the heart of its owner,\" said the scout; \"but it's a good sign to see a man account upon his dumb friends. He has the religion of the matter, in believing what is to happen will happen; and with such a consolation, it won't be long afore he submits to the rationality of killing a four-footed beast, to save the lives of human men. It may be as you say,\" he continued, reverting to the purport of Heyward's last remark; \"and the greater the reason why we should cut our steaks, and let the carcase drive down the stream, or we shall have the pack howling along the cliffs, begrudging every mouthful we swallow. Besides, though the Delaware tongue is the same as a book to the Iroquois, the cunning varlets are quick enough at understanding the reason of a wolf's howl.\"\n\nThe scout, whilst making his remarks, was busied in collecting certain necessary implements; as he concluded, he moved silently by the group of travellers, accompanied by the Mohicans, who seemed to comprehend his intentions with instinctive readiness, when the whole three disappeared in succession, seeming to vanish against the dark face of a perpendicular rock, that rose to the height of a few yards within as many feet of the water's edge.\n\n\"Those strains that once did sweet in Zion glide; He wales a portion with judicious care; And 'Let us worship God,' he says, with solemn air.\"\n\nHeyward, and his female companions, witnessed this mysterious movement with secret uneasiness; for, though the conduct of the white man had hitherto been above reproach, his rude equipments, blunt address, and strong antipathies, together with the character of his silent associates, were all causes for exciting distrust in minds that had been so recently alarmed by Indian treachery.\n\nThe stranger alone disregarded the passing incidents. He seated himself on a projection of the rocks, whence he gave no other signs of consciousness than by the struggles of his spirit, as manifested in frequent and heavy sighs. Smothered voices were next heard, as though men called to each other in the bowels of the earth, when a sudden light flashed upon those without, and laid bare the much-prized secret of the place.\n\nAt the farther extremity of a narrow, deep cavern in the rock, whose length appeared much extended by the perspective and the nature of the light by which it was seen, was seated the scout, holding a blazing knot of pine. The strong glare of the fire fell full upon his sturdy, weather-beaten countenance and forest attire, lending an air of romantic wildness to the aspect of an individual, who, seen by the sober light of day, would have exhibited the peculiarities of a man remarkable for the strangeness of his dress, the iron-like inflexibility of his frame, and the singular compound of quick, vigilant sagacity, and of exquisite simplicity, that by turns usurped the possession of his muscular features. At a little distance in advance stood Uncas, his whole person thrown powerfully into view. The travellers anxiously regarded the upright, flexible figure of the young Mohican, graceful and unrestrained in the attitudes and movements of nature. Though his person was more than usually screened by a green and fringed hunting-shirt, like that of the white man, there was no concealment to his dark, glancing, fearless eye, alike terrible and calm; the bold outline of his high, haughty features, pure in their native red; or to the dignified elevation of his receding forehead, together with all the finest proportions of a noble head, bared to the generous scalping tuft. It was the first opportunity possessed by Duncan and his companions, to view the marked lineaments of either of their Indian attendants, and each individual of the party felt relieved from a burden of doubt, as the proud and determined, though wild expression of the features of the young warrior forced itself on their notice. They felt it might be a being partially benighted in the vale of ignorance, but it could not be one who would willingly devote his rich natural gifts to the purposes of wanton treachery. The ingenuous Alice gazed at his free air and proud carriage, as she would have looked upon some precious relic of the Grecian chisel, to which life had been imparted by the intervention of a miracle; while Heyward, though accustomed to see the perfection of form which abounds among the uncorrupted natives, openly expressed his admiration at such an unblemished specimen of the noblest proportions of man.\n\n\"I could sleep in peace,\" whispered Alice, in reply, \"with such a fearless and generous looking youth for my sentinel. Surely, Duncan, those cruel murders, those terrific scenes of torture, of which we read and hear so much, are never acted in the presence of such as he!\"\n\n\"This, certainly, is a rare and brilliant instance of those natural qualities, in which these peculiar people are said to excel,\" he answered. \"I agree with you, Alice, in thinking that such a front and eye were formed rather to intimidate than to deceive; but let us not practise a deception upon ourselves, by expecting any other exhibition of what we esteem virtue than according to the fashion of a savage. As bright examples of great qualities are but too uncommon among Christians, so are they singular and solitary with the Indians; though, for the honor of our common nature, neither are incapable of producing them. Let us then hope that this Mohican may not disappoint our wishes, but prove, what his looks assert him to be, a brave and constant friend.\"\n\n\"Now Major Heyward speaks as Major Heyward should,\" said Cora; \"who, that looks at this creature of nature, remembers the shade of his skin!\"\n\nA short, and apparently an embarrassed silence succeeded this remark, which was interrupted by the scout calling to them, aloud, to enter.\n\n\"This fire begins to show too bright a flame,\" he continued, as they complied, \"and might light the Mingos to our undoing. Uncas, drop the blanket, and show the knaves its dark side. This is not such a supper as a major of the Royal Americans has a right to expect, but I've known stout detachments of the corps glad to eat their venison raw, and without a relish too.[12] Here, you see, we have plenty of salt, and can make a quick broil. There's fresh sassafras boughs for the ladies to sit on, which may not be as proud as their my-hog-guinea chairs, but which sends up a sweeter flavor than the skin of any hog can do, be it of Guinea, or be it of any other land. Come, friend, don't be mournful for the colt; 'twas an innocent thing, and had not seen much hardship. Its death will save the creature many a sore back and weary foot!\"\n\nUncas did as the other had directed, and when the voice of Hawkeye ceased, the roar of the cataract sounded like the rumbling of distant thunder.\n\n\"Are we quite safe in this cavern?\" demanded Heyward. \"Is there no danger of surprise? A single armed man, at its entrance, would hold us at his mercy.\"\n\nA spectral-looking figure stalked from out the darkness behind the scout, and seizing a blazing brand, held it towards the farther extremity of their place of retreat. Alice uttered a faint shriek, and even Cora rose to her feet, as this appalling object moved into the light; but a single word from Heyward calmed them, with the assurance it was only their attendant, Chingachgook, who, lifting another blanket, discovered that the cavern had two outlets. Then, holding the brand, he crossed a deep, narrow chasm in the rocks, which ran at right angles with the passage they were in, but which, unlike that, was open to the heavens, and entered another cave, answering to the description of the first, in every essential particular.\n\n\"Such old foxes as Chingachgook and myself are not often caught in a burrow with one hole,\" said Hawkeye, laughing; \"you can easily see the cunning of the place--the rock is black limestone, which everybody knows is soft; it makes no uncomfortable pillow, where brush and pine wood is scarce; well, the fall was once a few yards below us, and I dare to say was, in its time, as regular and as handsome a sheet of water as any along the Hudson. But old age is a great injury to good looks, as these sweet young ladies have yet to l'arn! The place is sadly changed! These rocks are full of cracks, and in some places they are softer than at othersome, and the water has worked out deep hollows for itself, until it has fallen back, ay, some hundred feet, breaking here and wearing there, until the falls have neither shape nor consistency.\"\n\n\"In what part of them are we?\" asked Heyward.\n\n\"Why, we are nigh the spot that Providence first placed them at, but where, it seems, they were too rebellious to stay. The rock proved softer on each side of us, and so they left the centre of the river bare and dry, first working out these two little holes for us to hide in.\"\n\n\"We are then on an island?\"\n\n\"Ay! there are the falls on two sides of us, and the river above and below. If you had daylight, it would be worth the trouble to step up on the height of this rock, and look at the perversity of the water. It falls by no rule at all; sometimes it leaps, sometimes it tumbles; there, it skips; here, it shoots; in one place 'tis white as snow, and in another 'tis green as grass; hereabouts, it pitches into deep hollows, that rumble and quake the 'arth; and hereaway, it ripples and sings like a brook, fashioning whirlpools and gulleys in the old stone, as it 'twas no harder than trodden clay. The whole design of the river seems disconcerted. First it runs smoothly, as if meaning to go down the descent as things were ordered; then it angles about and faces the shores; nor are there places wanting where it looks backward, as if unwilling to leave the wilderness, to mingle with the salt! Ay, lady, the fine cobweb-looking cloth you wear at your throat, is coarse, and like a fish-net, to little spots I can show you, where the river fabricates all sorts of images, as if, having broke loose from order, it would try its hand at everything. And yet what does it amount to! After the water has been suffered to have its will, for a time, like a headstrong man, it is gathered together by the hand that made it, and a few rods below you may see it all, flowing on steadily towards the sea, as was foreordained from the first foundation of the 'arth!\"\n\nWhile his auditors received a cheering assurance of the security of their place of concealment, from this untutored description of Glenn's,[13] they were much inclined to judge differently from Hawkeye, of its wild beauties. But they were not in a situation to suffer their thoughts to dwell on the charms of natural objects; and, as the scout had not found it necessary to cease his culinary labors while he spoke, unless to point out, with a broken fork, the direction of some particularly obnoxious point in the rebellious stream, they now suffered their attention to be drawn to the necessary, though more vulgar consideration of their supper.\n\nThe repast, which was greatly aided by the addition of a few delicacies that Heyward had the precaution to bring with him when they left their horses, was exceedingly refreshing to the wearied party. Uncas acted as attendant to the females, performing all the little offices within his power, with a mixture of dignity and anxious grace, that served to amuse Heyward, who well knew that it was an utter innovation on the Indian customs, which forbid their warriors to descend to any menial employment, especially in favor of their women. As the rites of hospitality were, however, considered sacred among them, this little departure from the dignity of manhood excited no audible comment. Had there been one there sufficiently disengaged to become a close observer, he might have fancied that the services of the young chief were not entirely impartial. That while he tendered to Alice the gourd of sweet water and the venison in a trencher, neatly carved from the knot of the pepperidge, with sufficient courtesy, in performing the same offices to her sister, his dark eye lingered on her rich, speaking countenance. Once or twice he was compelled to speak, to command the attention of those he served. In such cases, he made use of English, broken and imperfect, but sufficiently intelligible, and which he rendered so mild and musical, by his deep,[14] guttural voice, that it never failed to cause both ladies to look up in admiration and astonishment. In the course of these civilities, a few sentences were exchanged, that served to establish the appearance of an amicable intercourse between the parties.\n\nIn the meanwhile, the gravity of Chingachgook remained immovable. He had seated himself more within the circle of light, where the frequent uneasy glances of his guests were better enabled to separate the natural expression of his face from the artificial terrors of the war-paint. They found a strong resemblance between father and son, with the difference that might be expected from age and hardships. The fierceness of his countenance now seemed to slumber, and in its place was to be seen the quiet, vacant composure, which distinguishes an Indian warrior, when his faculties are not required for any of the greater purposes of his existence. It was, however, easy to be seen, by the occasional gleams that shot across his swarthy visage, that it was only necessary to arouse his passions, in order to give full effect to the terrific device which he had adopted to intimidate his enemies. On the other hand, the quick, roving eye of the scout seldom rested. He ate and drank with an appetite that no sense of danger could disturb, but his vigilance seemed never to desert him. Twenty times the gourd or the venison was suspended before his lips, while his head was turned aside, as though he listened to some distant and distrusted sounds--a movement that never failed to recall his guests from regarding the novelties of their situation, to a recollection of the alarming reasons that had driven them to seek it. As these frequent pauses were never followed by any remark, the momentary uneasiness they created quickly passed away, and for a time was forgotten.\n\n\"Come, friend,\" said Hawkeye, drawing out a keg from beneath a cover of leaves, towards the close of the repast, and addressing the stranger who sat at his elbow, doing great justice to his culinary skill, \"try a little spruce; 'twill wash away all thoughts of the colt, and quicken the life in your bosom. I drink to our better friendship, hoping that a little horse-flesh may leave no heartburnings atween us. How do you name yourself?\"\n\n\"Gamut--David Gamut,\" returned the singing-master, preparing to wash down his sorrows in a powerful draught of the woodman's high-flavored and well-laced compound.\n\n\"A very good name, and, I dare say, handed down from honest forefathers. I'm an admirator of names, though the Christian fashions fall far below savage customs in this particular. The biggest coward I ever knew was called Lyon; and his wife, Patience, would scold you out of hearing in less time than a hunted deer would run a rod. With an Indian 'tis a matter of conscience; what he calls himself, he generally is--not that Chingachgook, which signifies Big Sarpent, is really a snake, big or little; but that he understands the windings and turnings of human natur', and is silent, and strikes his enemies when they least expect him. What may be your calling?\"\n\n\"I am an unworthy instructor in the art of psalmody.\"\n\n\"Anan!\"\n\n\"I teach singing to the youths, of the Connecticut levy.\"\n\n\"You might be better employed. The young hounds go laughing and singing too much already through the woods, when they ought not to breathe louder than a fox in his cover. Can you use the smooth bore, or handle the rifle?\"\n\n\"Praised be God, I have never had occasion to meddle with murderous implements!\"\n\n\"Perhaps you understand the compass, and lay down the water-courses and mountains of the wilderness on paper, in order that they who follow may find places by their given names?\"\n\n\"I practise no such employment.\"\n\n\"You have a pair of legs that might make a long path seem short! you journey sometimes, I fancy, with tidings for the general.\"\n\n\"Never; I follow no other than my own high vocation, which is instruction in sacred music!\"\n\n\"'Tis a strange calling!\" muttered Hawkeye, with an inward laugh, \"to go through life, like a catbird, mocking all the ups and downs that may happen to come out of other men's throats. Well, friend, I suppose it is your gift, and mustn't be denied any more than if 'twas shooting, or some other better inclination. Let us hear what you can do in that way; 'twill be a friendly manner of saying good-night, for 'tis time that these ladies should be getting strength for a hard and a long push, in the pride of the morning, afore the Maquas are stirring!\"\n\n\"With joyful pleasure do I consent,\" said David, adjusting his iron-rimmed spectacles, and producing his beloved little volume, which he immediately tendered to Alice. \"What can be more fitting and consolatory, than to offer up evening praise, after a day of such exceeding jeopardy!\"\n\nAlice smiled; but regarding Heyward, she blushed and hesitated.\n\n\"Indulge yourself,\" he whispered: \"ought not the suggestion of the worthy namesake of the Psalmist to have its weight at such a moment?\"\n\nEncouraged by his opinion, Alice did what her pious inclinations and her keen relish for gentle sounds, had before so strongly urged. The book was open at a hymn not ill adapted to their situation, and in which the poet, no longer goaded by his desire to excel the inspired king of Israel, had discovered some chastened and respectable powers. Cora betrayed a disposition to support her sister, and the sacred song proceeded, after the indispensable preliminaries of the pitch-pipe and the tune had been duly attended to by the methodical David.\n\nThe air was solemn and slow. At times it rose to the fullest compass of the rich voices of the females, who hung over their little book in holy excitement, and again it sank so low, that the rushing of the waters ran through their melody, like a hollow accompaniment. The natural taste and true ear of David governed and modified the sounds to suit the confined cavern, every crevice, and cranny of which was filled with the thrilling notes of their flexible voices. The Indians riveted their eyes on the rocks, and listened with an attention that seemed to turn them into stone. But the scout, who had placed his chin in his hand, with an expression of cold indifference, gradually suffered his rigid features to relax, until, as verse succeeded verse, he felt his iron nature subdued, while his recollection was carried back to boyhood, when his ears had been accustomed to listen to similar sounds of praise, in the settlements of the colony. His roving eyes began to moisten, and before the hymn was ended, scalding tears rolled out of fountains that had long seemed dry, and followed each other down those cheeks, that had oftener felt the storms of heaven than any testimonials of weakness. The singers were dwelling on one of those low, dying chords, which the ear devours with such greedy rapture, as if conscious that it is about to lose them, when a cry, that seemed neither human nor earthly, rose in the outward air, penetrating not only the recesses of the cavern, but to the inmost hearts of all who heard it. It was followed by a stillness apparently as deep as if the waters had been checked in their furious progress, at such a horrid and unusual interruption.\n\n\"What is it?\" murmured Alice, after a few moments of terrible suspense.\n\n\"What is it?\" repeated Heyward aloud.\n\nNeither Hawkeye nor the Indians made any reply. They listened, as if expecting the sound would be repeated, with a manner that expressed their own astonishment. At length they spoke together earnestly, in the Delaware language, when Uncas, passing by the inner and most concealed aperture, cautiously left the cavern. When he had gone, the scout first spoke in English.\n\n\"What it is, or what it is not, none here can tell; though two of us have ranged the woods for more than thirty years! I did believe there was no cry that Indians or beast could make, that my ears had not heard; but this has proved that I was only a vain and conceited mortal!\"\n\n\"Was it not, then, the shout the warriors make when they wish to intimidate their enemies?\" asked Cora, who stood drawing her veil about her person, with a calmness to which her agitated sister was a stranger.\n\n\"No, no; this was bad, and shocking, and had a sort of unhuman sound; but when you once hear the war-whoop, you will never mistake it for anything else! Well, Uncas!\" speaking in Delaware to the young chief as he re-entered, \"what see you? do our lights shine through the blankets?\"\n\nThe answer was short, and apparently decided, being given in the same tongue.\n\n\"There is nothing to be seen without,\" continued Hawkeye, shaking his head in discontent; \"and our hiding-place is still in darkness! Pass into the other cave, you that need it, and seek for sleep; we must be afoot long before the sun, and make the most of our time to get to Edward, while the Mingos are taking their morning nap.\"\n\nCora set the example of compliance, with a steadiness that taught the more timid Alice the necessity of obedience. Before leaving the place, however, she whispered a request to Duncan that he would follow. Uncas raised the blanket for their passage, and as the sisters turned to thank him for this act of attention, they saw the scout seated again before the dying embers, with his face resting on his hands, in a manner which showed how deeply he brooded on the unaccountable interruption which had broken up their evening devotions.\n\nHeyward took with him a blazing knot, which threw a dim light through the narrow vista of their new apartment. Placing it in a favorable position, he joined the females, who now found themselves alone with him for the first time since they had left the friendly ramparts of Fort Edward.\n\n\"Leave us not, Duncan,\" said Alice; \"we cannot sleep in such a place as this, with that horrid cry still ringing in our ears!\"\n\n\"First let us examine into the security of your fortress,\" he answered, \"and then we will speak of rest.\"\n\nHe approached the farther end of the cavern, to an outlet, which, like the others, was concealed by blankets, and removing the thick screen, breathed the fresh and reviving air from the cataract. One arm of the river flowed through a deep, narrow ravine, which its current had worn in the soft rock, directly beneath his feet, forming an effectual defence, as he believed, against any danger from that quarter; the water, a few rods above them, plunging, glancing, and sweeping along, in its most violent and broken manner.\n\n\"Nature has made an impenetrable barrier on this side,\" he continued, pointing down the perpendicular declivity into the dark current, before he dropped the blanket; \"and as you know that good men and true are on guard in front, I see no reason why the advice of our honest host should be disregarded. I am certain Cora will join me in saying that sleep is necessary to you both.\"\n\n\"Cora may submit to the justice of your opinion, though she cannot put it in practise,\" returned the elder sister, who had placed herself by the side of Alice, on a couch of sassafras; \"there would be other causes to chase away sleep, though we had been spared the shock of this mysterious noise. Ask yourself, Heyward, can daughters forget the anxiety a father must endure, whose children lodge, he knows not where or how, in such a wilderness, and in the midst of so many perils?\"\n\n\"He is a soldier, and knows how to estimate the chances of the woods.\"\n\n\"He is a father, and cannot deny his nature.\"\n\n\"How kind has he ever been to all my follies! how tender and indulgent to all my wishes!\" sobbed Alice. \"We have been selfish, sister, in urging our visit at such hazard!\"\n\n\"I may have been rash in pressing his consent in a moment of much embarrassment, but I would have proved to him, that however others might neglect him in his strait, his children at least were faithful!\"\n\n\"When he heard of your arrival at Edward,\" said Heyward, kindly, \"there was a powerful struggle in his bosom between fear and love; though the latter, heightened, if possible, by so long a separation, quickly prevailed. 'It is the spirit of my noble-minded Cora that leads them, Duncan,' he said, 'and I will not balk it. Would to God, that he who holds the honor of our royal master in his guardianship, would show but half her firmness!'\"\n\n\"And did he not speak of me, Heyward?\" demanded Alice, with jealous affection. \"Surely, he forgot not altogether his little Elsie?\"\n\n\"That was impossible,\" returned the young man; \"he called you by a thousand endearing epithets, that I may not presume to use, but to the justice of which I can warmly testify. Once, indeed, he said--\"\n\nDuncan ceased speaking; for while his eyes were riveted on those of Alice, who had turned towards him with the eagerness of filial affection, to catch his words, the same strong horrid cry, as before, filled the air, and rendered him mute. A long, breathless silence succeeded, during which each looked at the others in fearful expectation of hearing the sound repeated. At length the blanket was slowly raised, and the scout stood in the aperture with a countenance whose firmness evidently began to give way, before a mystery that seemed to threaten some danger, against which all his cunning and experience might prove of no avail.\n\n\"They do not sleep. On yonder cliffs, a grisly band, I see them sit.\"\n\n\"'Twould be neglecting a warning that is given for our good, to lie hid any longer,\" said Hawkeye, \"when such sounds are raised in the forest! The gentle ones may keep close, but the Mohicans and I will watch upon the rock, where I suppose a major of the 60th would wish to keep us company.\"\n\n\"Is then our danger so pressing?\" asked Cora.\n\n\"He who makes strange sounds, and gives them out for man's information, alone knows our danger. I should think myself wicked, unto rebellion against his will, was I to burrow with such warnings in the air! Even the weak soul who passes his days in singing, is stirred by the cry, and, as he says, is 'ready to go forth to the battle.' If 'twere only a battle, it would be a thing understood by us all, and easily managed; but I have heard that when such shrieks are atween heaven and 'arth, it betokens another sort of warfare!\"\n\n\"If all our reasons for fear, my friend, are confined to such as proceed from supernatural causes, we have but little occasion to be alarmed,\" continued the undisturbed Cora; \"are you certain that our enemies have not invented some new and ingenious method to strike us with terror, that their conquest may become more easy?\"\n\n\"Lady,\" returned the scout, solemnly, \"I have listened to all the sounds of the woods for thirty years, as a man will listen, whose life and death depend on the quickness of his ears. There is no whine of the panther, no whistle of the catbird, nor any invention of the devilish Mingos, that can cheat me! I have heard the forest moan like mortal men in their affliction; often, and again, have I listened to the wind playing its music in the branches of the girdled trees; and I have heard the lightning cracking in the air, like the snapping of blazing brush, as it spitted forth sparks and forked flames; but never have I thought that I heard more than the pleasure of Him who sported with the things of his hand. But neither the Mohicans, nor I, who am a white man without a cross, can explain the cry just heard. We, therefore, believe it a sign given for our good.\"\n\n\"It is extraordinary!\" said Heyward, taking his pistols from the place where he had laid them on entering; \"be it a sign of peace or a signal of war, it must be looked to. Lead the way, my friend; I follow.\"\n\nOn issuing from their place of confinement, the whole party instantly experienced a grateful renovation of spirits, by exchanging the pent air of the hiding-place for the cool and invigorating atmosphere, which played around the whirlpools and pitches of the cataract. A heavy evening breeze swept along the surface of the river, and seemed to drive the roar of the falls into the recesses of their own caverns, whence it issued heavily and constant, like thunder rumbling beyond the distant hills. The moon had risen, and its light was already glancing here and there on the waters above them; but the extremity of the rock where they stood still lay in shadow. With the exception of the sounds produced by the rushing waters, and an occasional breathing of the air, as it murmured past them in fitful currents, the scene was as still as night and solitude could make it. In vain were the eyes of each individual bent along the opposite shores, in quest of some signs of life, that might explain the nature of the interruption they had heard. Their anxious and eager looks were baffled by the deceptive light, or rested only on naked rocks, and straight and immovable trees.\n\n\"There is nothing to be seen but the gloom and quiet of a lovely evening,\" whispered Duncan: \"how much should we prize such a scene, and all this breathing solitude, at any other moment, Cora! Fancy yourselves in security and what now, perhaps, increases your terror, may be made conducive to enjoyment--\"\n\n\"Listen!\" interrupted Alice.\n\nThe caution was unnecessary. Once more the same sound arose, as if from the bed of the river, and having broken out of the narrow bounds of the cliffs, was heard undulating through the forest, in distant and dying cadences.\n\n\"Can any here give a name to such a cry?\" demanded Hawkeye, when the last echo was lost in the woods; \"if so, let him speak; for myself, I judge it not to belong to 'arth!\"\n\n\"Here, then, is one who can undeceive you,\" said Duncan; \"I know the sound full well, for often have I heard it on the field of battle, and in situations which are frequent in a soldier's life. 'Tis the horrid shriek that a horse will give in his agony; oftener drawn from him in pain, though sometimes in terror. My charger is either a prey to the beasts of the forest, or he sees his danger, without the power to avoid it. The sound might deceive me in the cavern, but in the open air I know it too well to be wrong.\"\n\nThe scout and his companions listened to this simple explanation with the interest of men who imbibe new ideas, at the same time that they get rid of old ones, which had proved disagreeable inmates. The two latter uttered their usual and expressive exclamation, \"Hugh!\" as the truth first glanced upon their minds, while the former, after a short musing pause, took upon himself to reply.\n\n\"I cannot deny your words,\" he said; \"for I am little skilled in horses, though born where they abound. The wolves must be hovering above their heads on the bank, and the timorsome creatures are calling on man for help, in the best manner they are able. Uncas,\"--he spoke in Delaware--\"Uncas, drop down in the canoe, and whirl a brand among the pack; or fear may do what the wolves can't get at to perform, and leave us without horses in the morning, when we shall have so much need to journey swiftly!\"\n\nThe young native had already descended to the water, to comply, when a long howl was raised on the edge of the river, and was borne swiftly off into the depths of the forest, as though the beasts, of their own accord, were abandoning their prey in sudden terror. Uncas, with instinctive quickness, receded, and the three foresters held another of their low, earnest conferences.\n\n\"We have been like hunters who have lost the points of the heavens, and from whom the sun has been hid for days,\" said Hawkeye, turning away from his companions; \"now we begin again to know the signs of our course, and the paths are cleared from briers! Seat yourselves in the shade which the moon throws from yonder beech--'tis thicker than that of the pines--and let us wait for that which the Lord may choose to send next. Let all your conversation be in whispers; though it would be better, and perhaps, in the end, wiser, if each one held discourse with his own thoughts, for a time.\"\n\nThe manner of the scout was seriously impressive, though no longer distinguished by any signs of unmanly apprehension. It was evident that his momentary weakness had vanished with the explanation of a mystery which his own experience had not served to fathom; and though he now felt all the realities of their actual condition, that he was prepared to meet them with the energy of his hardy nature. This feeling seemed also common to the natives, who placed themselves in positions which commanded a full view of both shores, while their own persons were effectually concealed from observation. In such circumstances, common prudence dictated that Heyward and his companions should imitate a caution that proceeded from so intelligent a source. The young man drew a pile of the sassafras from the cave, and placing it in the chasm which separated the two caverns, it was occupied by the sisters, who were thus protected by the rocks from any missiles, while their anxiety was relieved by the assurance that no danger could approach without a warning. Heyward himself was posted at hand, so near that he might communicate with his companions without raising his voice to a dangerous elevation, while David, in imitation of the woodsmen, bestowed his person in such a manner among the fissures of the rocks, that his ungainly limbs were no longer offensive to the eye.\n\nIn this manner, hours passed by without further interruption. The moon reached the zenith, and shed its mild light perpendicularly on the lovely sight of the sisters slumbering peacefully in each other's arms. Duncan cast the wide shawl of Cora before a spectacle he so much loved to contemplate, and then suffered his own head to seek a pillow on the rock. David began to utter sounds that would have shocked his delicate organs in more wakeful moments; in short, all but Hawkeye and the Mohicans lost every idea of consciousness, in uncontrollable drowsiness. But the watchfulness of these vigilant protectors neither tired nor slumbered. Immovable as that rock, of which each appeared to form a part, they lay, with their eyes roving, without intermission, along the dark margin of trees that bounded the adjacent shores of the narrow stream. Not a sound escaped them; the most subtle examination could not have told they breathed. It was evident that this excess of caution proceeded from an experience that no subtlety on the part of their enemies could deceive. It was, however, continued without any apparent consequences, until the moon had set, and a pale streak above the tree-tops, at the bend of the river a little below, announced the approach of day.\n\nThen, for the first time, Hawkeye was seen to stir. He crawled along the rock, and shook Duncan from his heavy slumbers.\n\n\"Now is the time to journey,\" he whispered; \"awake the gentle ones, and be ready to get into the canoe when I bring it to the landing-place.\"\n\n\"Have you had a quiet night?\" said Heyward; \"for myself, I believe sleep has got the better of my vigilance.\"\n\n\"All is yet still as midnight. Be silent, but be quick.\"\n\nBy this time Duncan was thoroughly awake, and he immediately lifted the shawl from the sleeping females. The motion caused Cora to raise her hand as if to repulse him, while Alice murmured, in her soft, gentle voice, \"No, no, dear father, we were not deserted; Duncan was with us!\"\n\n\"Yes, sweet innocence,\" whispered the youth; \"Duncan is here, and while life continues or danger remains, he will never quit thee. Cora! Alice! awake! The hour has come to move!\"\n\nA loud shriek from the younger of the sisters, and the form of the other standing upright before him, in bewildered horror, was the unexpected answer he received. While the words were still on the lips of Heyward, there had arisen such a tumult of yells and cries as served to drive the swift currents of his own blood back from its bounding course into the fountains of his heart. It seemed, for near a minute, as if demons of hell had possessed themselves of the air about them, and were venting their savage humors in barbarous sounds. The cries came from no particular direction, though it was evident they filled the woods, and as the appalled listeners easily imagined, the caverns of the falls, the rocks, the bed of the river, and the upper air. David raised his tall person in the midst of the infernal din, with a hand on either ear, exclaiming--\n\n\"Whence comes this discord! Has hell broke loose, that man should utter sounds like these!\"\n\nThe bright flashes and the quick reports of a dozen rifles, from the opposite banks of the stream, followed this incautious exposure of his person, and left the unfortunate singing-master senseless on that rock where he had been so long slumbering. The Mohicans boldly sent back the intimidating yell of their enemies, who raised a shout of savage triumph at the fall of Gamut. The flash of rifles was then quick and close between them, but either party was too well skilled to leave even a limb exposed to the hostile aim. Duncan listened with intense anxiety for the strokes of the paddle, believing that flight was now their only refuge. The river glanced by with its ordinary velocity, but the canoe was nowhere to be seen on its dark waters. He had just fancied they were cruelly deserted by the scout, as a stream of flame issued from the rock beneath him, and a fierce yell, blended with a shriek of agony, announced that the messenger of death, sent from the fatal weapon of Hawkeye, had found a victim. At this slight repulse the assailants instantly withdrew, and gradually the place became as still as before the sudden tumult.\n\nDuncan seized the favorable moment to spring to the body of Gamut, which he bore within the shelter of the narrow chasm that protected the sisters. In another minute the whole party was collected in this spot of comparative safety.\n\n\"The poor fellow has saved his scalp,\" said Hawkeye, coolly passing his hand over the head of David; \"but he is a proof that a man may be born with too long a tongue! 'Twas downright madness to show six feet of flesh and blood, on a naked rock, to the raging savages. I only wonder he has escaped with life.\"\n\n\"Is he not dead!\" demanded Cora, in a voice whose husky tones showed how powerfully natural horror struggled with her assumed firmness. \"Can we do aught to assist the wretched man?\"\n\n\"No, no! the life is in his heart yet, and after he has slept awhile he will come to himself, and be a wiser man for it, till the hour of his real time shall come,\" returned Hawkeye, casting another oblique glance at the insensible body, while he filled his charger with admirable nicety. \"Carry him in, Uncas, and lay him on the sassafras. The longer his nap lasts the better it will be for him, as I doubt whether he can find a proper cover for such a shape on these rocks; and singing won't do any good with the Iroquois.\"\n\n\"You believe, then, the attack will be renewed?\" asked Heyward.\n\n\"Do I expect a hungry wolf will satisfy his craving with a mouthful! They have lost a man, and 'tis their fashion, when they meet a loss, and fail in the surprise, to fall back; but we shall have them on again, with new expedients to circumvent us, and master our scalps. Our main hope,\" he continued, raising his rugged countenance, across which a shade of anxiety just then passed like a darkening cloud, \"will be to keep the rock until Munro can send a party to our help! God send it may be soon, and under a leader that knows the Indian customs!\"\n\n\"You hear our probable fortunes, Cora,\" said Duncan, \"and you know we have everything to hope from the anxiety and experience of your father. Come, then, with Alice, into this cavern, where you, at least, will be safe from the murderous rifles of our enemies and where you may bestow a care suited to your gentle natures on our unfortunate comrade.\"\n\nThe sisters followed him into the outer cave, where David was beginning, by his sighs, to give symptoms of returning consciousness; and then commending the wounded man to their attention, he immediately prepared to leave them.\n\n\"Duncan!\" said the tremulous voice of Cora, when he had reached the mouth of the cavern. He turned, and beheld the speaker, whose color had changed to a deadly paleness, and whose lip quivered, gazing after him, with an expression of interest which immediately recalled him to her side. \"Remember, Duncan, how necessary your safety is to our own--how you bear a father's sacred trust--how much depends on your discretion and care--in short,\" she added, while the tell-tale blood stole over her features, crimsoning her very temples, \"how very deservedly dear you are to all of the name of Munro.\"\n\n\"If anything could add to my own base love of life,\" said Heyward, suffering his unconscious eyes to wander to the youthful form of the silent Alice, \"it would be so kind an assurance. As major of the 60th, our honest host will tell you I must take my share of the fray; but our task will be easy; it is merely to keep these blood-hounds at bay for a few hours.\"\n\nWithout waiting for reply, he tore himself from the presence of the sisters, and joined the scout and his companions, who still lay within the protection of the little chasm between the two caves.\n\n\"I tell you, Uncas,\" said the former, as Heyward joined them, \"you are wasteful of your powder, and the kick of the rifle disconcerts your aim! Little powder, light lead, and a long arm, seldom fail of bringing the death screech from a Mingo! At least, such has been my experience with the creatur's. Come, friends; let us to our covers, for no man can tell when or where a Maqua[15] will strike his blow.\"\n\nThe Indians silently repaired to their appointed stations, which were fissures in the rocks, whence they could command the approaches to the foot of the falls. In the centre of the little island, a few short and stunted pines had found root, forming a thicket, into which Hawkeye darted with the swiftness of a deer, followed by the active Duncan. Here they secured themselves, as well as circumstances would permit, among the shrubs and fragments of stone that were scattered about the place. Above them was a bare, rounded rock, on each side of which the water played its gambols, and plunged into the abysses beneath, in the manner already described. As the day had now dawned, the opposite shores no longer presented a confused outline, but they were able to look into the woods, and distinguish objects beneath the canopy of gloomy pines.\n\nA long and anxious watch succeeded, but without any further evidences of a renewed attack; and Duncan began to hope that their fire had proved more fatal than was supposed, and that their enemies had been effectually repulsed. When he ventured to utter this impression to his companion, it was met by Hawkeye with an incredulous shake of the head.\n\n\"You know not the nature of a Maqua, if you think he is so easily beaten back without a scalp!\" he answered. \"If there was one of the imps yelling this morning, there were forty! and they know our number and quality too well to give up the chase so soon. Hist! look into the water above, just where it breaks over the rocks. I am no mortal, if the risky devils haven't swam down upon the very pitch, and, as bad luck would have it, they have hit the head of the island. Hist! man, keep close! or the hair will be off your crown in the turning of a knife!\"\n\nHeyward lifted his head from the cover, and beheld what he justly considered a prodigy of rashness and skill. The river had worn away the edge of the soft rock in such a manner, as to render its first pitch less abrupt and perpendicular than is usual at waterfalls. With no other guide than the ripple of the stream where it met the head of the island, a party of their insatiable foes had ventured into the current, and swam down upon this point, knowing the ready access it would give, if successful, to their intended victims. As Hawkeye ceased speaking, four human heads could be seen peering above a few logs of drift-wood that had lodged on these naked rocks, and which had probably suggested the idea of the practicability of the hazardous undertaking. At the next moment, a fifth form was seen floating over the green edge of the fall, a little from the line of the island. The savage struggled powerfully to gain the point of safety, and, favored by the glancing water, he was already stretching forth an arm to meet the grasp of his companions, when he shot away again with the whirling current, appeared to rise into the air, with uplifted arms and starting eyeballs, and fell, with a sullen plunge, into that deep and yawning abyss over which he hovered. A single, wild, despairing shriek rose from the cavern, and all was hushed again, as the grave.\n\nThe first generous impulse of Duncan was to rush to the rescue of the hapless wretch; but he felt himself bound to the spot by the iron grasp of the immovable scout.\n\n\"Would ye bring certain death upon us, by telling the Mingos where we lie?\" demanded Hawkeye, sternly; \"'tis a charge of powder saved, and ammunition is as precious now as breath to a worried deer! Freshen the priming of your pistols--the mist of the falls is apt to dampen the brimstone--and stand firm for a close struggle, while I fire on their rush.\"\n\nHe placed his finger in his mouth, and drew a long, shrill whistle, which was answered from the rocks that were guarded by the Mohicans. Duncan caught glimpses of heads above the scattered drift-wood, as this signal rose on the air, but they disappeared again as suddenly as they had glanced upon his sight. A low, rustling sound next drew his attention behind him, and turning his head, he beheld Uncas within a few feet, creeping to his side. Hawkeye spoke to him in Delaware, when the young chief took his position with singular caution and undisturbed coolness. To Heyward this was a moment of feverish and impatient suspense; though the scout saw fit to select it as a fit occasion to read a lecture to his more youthful associates on the art of using fire-arms with discretion.\n\n\"Of all we'pons,\" he commenced, \"the long-barrelled, true-grooved, soft-metalled rifle is the most dangerous in skilful hands, though it wants a strong arm, a quick eye, and great judgment in charging, to put forth all its beauties. The gunsmiths can have but little insight into their trade, when they make their fowling-pieces and short horsemen's--\"\n\nHe was interrupted by the low but expressive \"Hugh!\" of Uncas.\n\n_Each of the combatants threw all his energies into that effort, and the result was, that both tottered on the brink of the precipice_]\n\"I see them, boy, I see them!\" continued Hawkeye; \"they are gathering for the rush, or they would keep their dingy backs below the logs. Well, let them,\" he added, examining his flint; \"the leading man certainly comes on to his death, though it should be Montcalm himself!\"\n\nAt that moment the woods were filled with another burst of cries, and at the signal four savages sprang from the cover of the drift-wood. Heyward felt a burning desire to rush forward to meet them, so intense was the delirious anxiety of the moment; but he was restrained by the deliberate examples of the scout and Uncas. When their foes who leaped over the black rock that divided them, with long bounds, uttering the wildest yells, were within a few rods, the rifle of Hawkeye slowly rose among the shrubs, and poured out its fatal contents. The foremost Indian bounded like a stricken deer, and fell headlong among the clefts of the island.\n\n\"Now, Uncas!\" cried the scout, drawing his long knife, while his quick eyes began to flash with ardor, \"take the last of the screeching imps; of the other two we are sartain!\"\n\nHe was obeyed; and but two enemies remained to be overcome. Heyward had given one of his pistols to Hawkeye, and together they rushed down a little declivity towards their foes; they discharged their weapons at the same instant, and equally without success.\n\n\"I know'd it! and I said it!\" muttered the scout, whirling the despised little implement over the falls with bitter disdain. \"Come on, ye bloody minded hell-hounds! ye meet a man without a cross!\"\n\nThe words were barely uttered, when he encountered a savage of gigantic stature, and of the fiercest mien. At the same moment, Duncan found himself engaged with the other, in a similar contest of hand to hand. With ready skill, Hawkeye and his antagonist each grasped that uplifted arm of the other which held the dangerous knife. For near a minute they stood looking one another in the eye, and gradually exerting the power of their muscles for the mastery. At length, the toughened sinews of the white man prevailed over the less practised limbs of the native. The arm of the latter slowly gave way before the increasing force of the scout, who suddenly wresting his armed hand from the grasp of the foe, drove the sharp weapon through his naked bosom to the heart. In the meantime Heyward had been pressed in a more deadly struggle. His slight sword was snapped in the first encounter. As he was destitute of any other means of defence, his safety now depended entirely on bodily strength and resolution. Though deficient in neither of these qualities, he had met an enemy every way his equal. Happily, he soon succeeded in disarming his adversary, whose knife fell on the rock at their feet; and from this moment it became a fierce struggle, who should cast the other over the dizzy height into a neighboring cavern of the falls. Every successive struggle brought them nearer to the verge, where Duncan perceived the final and conquering effort must be made. Each of the combatants threw all his energies into that effort, and the result was, that both tottered on the brink of the precipice. Heyward felt the grasp of the other at his throat, and saw the grim smile the savage gave, under the revengeful hope that he hurried his enemy to a fate similar to his own, as he felt his body slowly yielding to a resistless power, and the young man experienced the passing agony of such a moment in all its horrors. At that instant of extreme danger, a dark hand and glancing knife appeared before him; the Indian released his hold, as the blood flowed freely from around the several tendons of the wrist; and while Duncan was drawn backward by the saving arm of Uncas, his charmed eyes were still riveted on the fierce and disappointed countenance of his foe, who fell sullenly and disappointed down the irrecoverable precipice.\n\n\"To cover! to cover!\" cried Hawkeye, who just then had despatched the enemy; \"to cover, for your lives! the work is but half ended!\"\n\nThe young Mohican gave a shout of triumph, and, followed by Duncan, he glided up the acclivity they had descended to the combat, and sought the friendly shelter of the rocks and shrubs.\n\n\"They linger yet, Avengers of their native land.\"\n\nThe warning call of the scout was not uttered without occasion. During the occurrence of the deadly encounter just related, the roar of the falls was unbroken by any human sound whatever. It would seem that interest in the result had kept the natives on the opposite shores in breathless suspense, while the quick evolutions and swift changes in the position of the combatants, effectually prevented a fire that might prove dangerous alike to friend and enemy. But the moment the struggle was decided, a yell arose as fierce and savage as wild and revengful passions could throw into the air. It was followed by the swift flashes of the rifles, which sent their leaden messengers across the rock in volleys, as though the assailants would pour out their impotent fury on the insensible scene of the fatal contest.\n\nA steady, though deliberate return was made from the rifle of Chingachgook, who had maintained his post throughout the fray with unmoved resolution. When the triumphant shout of Uncas was borne to his ears, the gratified father raised his voice in a single responsive cry, after which his busy piece alone proved that he still guarded his pass with unwearied diligence. In this manner many minutes flew by with the swiftness of thought: the rifles of the assailants speaking, at times, in rattling volleys, and at others, in occasional, scattering shots. Though the rock, the trees, and the shrubs, were cut and torn in a hundred places around the besieged, their cover was so close, and so rigidly maintained, that, as yet, David had been the only sufferer in their little band.\n\n\"Let them burn their powder,\" said the deliberate scout, while bullet after bullet whizzed by the place where he securely lay; \"there will be a fine gathering of lead when it is over, and I fancy the imps will tire of the sport, afore these old stones cry out for mercy! Uncas, boy, you waste the kernels by overcharging: and a kicking rifle never carries a true bullet. I told you to take that loping miscreant under the line of white paint; now, if your bullet went a hair's breadth, it went two inches above it. The life lies low in a Mingo, and humanity teaches us to make a quick end of the sarpents.\"\n\nA quiet smile lighted the haughty features of the young Mohican, betraying his knowledge of the English language, as well as of the other's meaning; but he suffered it to pass away without vindication or reply.\n\n\"I cannot permit you to accuse Uncas of want of judgment or of skill,\" said Duncan; \"he saved my life in the coolest and readiest manner, and he has made a friend who never will require to be reminded of the debt he owes.\"\n\nUncas partly raised his body, and offered his hand to the grasp of Heyward. During this act of friendship, the two young men exchanged looks of intelligence which caused Duncan to forget the character and condition of his wild associate. In the meanwhile, Hawkeye, who looked on this burst of youthful feeling with a cool but kind regard, made the following reply:--\n\n\"Life is an obligation which friends often owe each other in the wilderness. I dare say I may have served Uncas some such turn myself before now; and I very well remember that he has stood between me and death five different times: three times from the Mingos, once in crossing Horican, and--\"\n\n\"That bullet was better aimed than common!\" exclaimed Duncan, involuntarily shrinking from a shot which struck the rock at his side with a smart rebound.\n\nHawkeye laid his hand on the shapeless metal, and shook his head, as he examined it, saying, \"Falling lead is never flattened! had it come from the clouds this might have happened!\"\n\nBut the rifle of Uncas was deliberately raised towards the heavens, directing his companions to a point, where the mystery was immediately explained. A ragged oak grew on the right bank of the river, nearly opposite to their position, which, seeking the freedom of the open space, had inclined so far forward, that its upper branches overhung that arm of the stream which flowed nearest to its own shore. Among the topmost leaves, which scantily concealed the gnarled and stunted limbs, a savage was nestled, partly concealed by the trunk of the tree, and partly exposed, as though looking down upon them to ascertain the effect produced by his treacherous aim.\n\n\"These devils will scale heaven to circumvent us to our ruin,\" said Hawkeye; \"keep him in play, boy, until I can bring 'Killdeer' to bear, when we will try his metal on each side of the tree at once.\"\n\nUncas delayed his fire until the scout uttered the word. The rifles flashed, the leaves and the bark of the oak flew into the air, and were scattered by the wind, but the Indian answered their assault by a taunting laugh, sending down upon them another bullet in return, that struck the cap of Hawkeye from his head. Once more the savage yells burst out of the woods, and the leaden hail whistled above the heads of the besieged, as if to confine them to a place where they might become easy victims to the enterprise of the warrior who had mounted the tree.\n\n\"This must be looked to!\" said the scout, glancing about him with an anxious eye. \"Uncas, call up your father; we have need of all our we'pons to bring the cunning varmint from his roost.\"\n\nThe signal was instantly given; and, before Hawkeye had reloaded his rifle, they were joined by Chingachgook. When his son pointed out to the experienced warrior the situation of their dangerous enemy, the usual exclamatory \"Hugh!\" burst from his lips; after which, no further expression of surprise or alarm was suffered to escape him. Hawkeye and the Mohicans conversed earnestly together in Delaware for a few moments, when each quietly took his post, in order to execute the plan they had speedily devised.\n\nThe warrior in the oak had maintained a quick, though ineffectual fire, from the moment of his discovery. But his aim was interrupted by the vigilance of his enemies, whose rifles instantaneously bore on any part of his person that was left exposed. Still his bullets fell in the centre of the crouching party. The clothes of Heyward, which rendered him peculiarly conspicuous, were repeatedly cut, and once blood was drawn from a slight wound in his arm.\n\nAt length, emboldened by the long and patient watchfulness of his enemies, the Huron attempted a better and more fatal aim. The quick eye of the Mohicans caught the dark line of his lower limbs incautiously exposed through the thin foliage, a few inches from the trunk of the tree. Their rifles made a common report, when, sinking on his wounded limb, part of the body of the savage came into view. Swift as thought, Hawkeye seized the advantage and discharged his fatal weapon into the top of the oak. The leaves were unusually agitated; the dangerous rifle fell from its commanding elevation, and after a few moments of vain struggling, the form of the savage was seen swinging in the wind, while he still grasped a ragged and naked branch of the tree, with hands clenched in desperation.\n\n\"Give him, in pity give him--the contents of another rifle!\" cried Duncan, turning away his eyes in horror from the spectacle of a fellow-creature in such awful jeopardy.\n\n\"Not a karnel!\" exclaimed the obdurate Hawkeye; \"his death is certain, and we have no powder to spare, for Indian fights sometimes last for days; 'tis their scalps or ours!--and God, who made us, has put into our natures the craving to keep the skin on the head!\"\n\nAgainst this stern and unyielding morality, supported as it was by such visible policy, there was no appeal. From that moment the yells in the forest once more ceased, the fire was suffered to decline, and all eyes, those of friends as well as enemies, became fixed on the hopeless condition of the wretch who was dangling between heaven and earth. The body yielded to the currents of air, and though no murmur or groan escaped the victim, there were instants when he grimly faced his foes, and the anguish of cold despair might be traced, through the intervening distance, in possession of his swarthy lineaments. Three several times the scout raised his piece in mercy, and as often prudence getting the better of his intention, it was again silently lowered. At length one hand of the Huron lost its hold, and dropped exhausted to his side. A desperate and fruitless struggle to recover the branch succeeded, and then the savage was seen for a fleeting instant, grasping wildly at the empty air. The lightning is not quicker than was the flame from the rifle of Hawkeye; the limbs of the victim trembled and contracted, the head fell to the bosom, and the body parted the foaming waters like lead, when the element closed above it, in its ceaseless velocity, and every vestige of the unhappy Huron was lost forever.\n\nNo shout of triumph succeeded this important advantage, but even the Mohicans gazed at each other in silent horror. A single yell burst from the woods, and all was again still. Hawkeye, who alone appeared to reason on the occasion, shook his head at his own momentary weakness, even uttering his self-disapprobation aloud.\n\n\"'Twas the last charge in my horn, and the last bullet in my pouch, and 'twas the act of a boy!\" he said; \"what mattered it whether he struck the rock living or dead: feeling would soon be over. Uncas, lad, go down to the canoe, and bring up the big horn; it is all the powder we have left, and we shall need it to the last grain, or I am ignorant of the Mingo nature.\"\n\nThe young Mohican complied, leaving the scout turning over the useless contents of his pouch, and shaking the empty horn with renewed discontent. From this unsatisfactory examination, however, he was soon called by a loud and piercing exclamation from Uncas, that sounded, even to the unpractised ears of Duncan, as the signal of some new and unexpected calamity. Every thought filled with apprehension for the precious treasure he had concealed in the cavern, the young man started to his feet, totally regardless of the hazard he incurred by such an exposure. As if actuated by a common impulse, his movement was imitated by his companions, and, together, they rushed down the pass to the friendly chasm, with a rapidity that rendered the scattering fire of their enemies perfectly harmless. The unwonted cry had brought the sisters, together with the wounded David, from their place of refuge; and the whole party, at a single glance, was made acquainted with the nature of the disaster that had disturbed even the practised stoicism of their youthful Indian protector.\n\nAt a short distance from the rock, their little bark was to be seen floating across the eddy, towards the swift current of the river, in a manner which proved that its course was directed by some hidden agent. The instant this unwelcome sight caught the eye of the scout, his rifle was levelled as by instinct, but the barrel gave no answer to the bright sparks of the flint.\n\n\"'Tis too late, 'tis too late!\" Hawkeye exclaimed, dropping the useless piece in bitter disappointment; \"the miscreant has struck the rapid; and had we powder, it could hardly send the lead swifter than he now goes!\"\n\nThe adventurous Huron raised his head above the shelter of the canoe, and while it glided swiftly down the stream, he waved his hand, and gave forth the shout, which was the known signal of success. His cry was answered by a yell and a laugh from the woods, as tauntingly exulting as if fifty demons were uttering their blasphemies at the fall of some Christian soul.\n\n\"Well may you laugh, ye children of the devil!\" said the scout, seating himself on a projection of the rock, and suffering his gun to fall neglected at his feet, \"for the three quickest and surest rifles in these woods are no better than so many stalks of mullein, or the last year's horns of a buck!\"\n\n\"What is to be done?\" demanded Duncan, losing the first feeling of disappointment in a more manly desire for exertion; \"what will become of us?\"\n\nHawkeye made no other reply than by passing his finger around the crown of his head, in a manner so significant, that none who witnessed the action could mistake its meaning.\n\n\"Surely, surely, our case is not so desperate!\" exclaimed the youth; \"the Hurons are not here; we may make good the caverns; we may oppose their landing.\"\n\n\"With what?\" coolly demanded the scout. \"The arrows of Uncas, or such tears as women shed! No, no; you are young, and rich, and have friends, and at such an age I know it is hard to die! But,\" glancing his eyes at the Mohicans, \"let us remember we are men without a cross, and let us teach these natives of the forest that white blood can run as freely as red, when the appointed hour is come.\"\n\nDuncan turned quickly in the direction indicated by the other's eyes, and read a confirmation of his worst apprehensions in the conduct of the Indians. Chingachgook, placing himself in a dignified posture on another fragment of the rock, had already laid aside his knife and tomahawk, and was in the act of taking the eagle's plume from his head, and smoothing the solitary tuft of hair in readiness to perform its last and revolting office. His countenance was composed, though thoughtful, while his dark gleaming eyes were gradually losing the fierceness of the combat in an expression better suited to the change he expected momentarily to undergo.\n\n\"Our case is not, cannot be so hopeless!\" said Duncan; \"even at this moment succor may be at hand. I see no enemies! they have sickened of a struggle in which they risk so much with so little prospect of gain!\"\n\n\"It may be a minute, or it may be an hour, afore the wily sarpents steal upon us, and it is quite in natur' for them to be lying within hearing at this very moment,\" said Hawkeye; \"but come they will, and in such a fashion as will leave us nothing to hope! Chingachgook\"--he spoke in Delaware--\"my brother, we have fought our last battle together, and the Maquas will triumph in the death of the sage man of the Mohicans, and of the pale-face, whose eyes can make night as day, and level the clouds to the mists of the springs!\"\n\n\"Let the Mingo women go weep over their slain!\" returned the Indian, with characteristic pride and unmoved firmness; \"the Great Snake of the Mohicans has coiled himself in their wigwams, and has poisoned their triumph with the wailings of children whose fathers have not returned! Eleven warriors lie hid from the graves of their tribes since the snows have melted, and none will tell where to find them when the tongue of Chingachgook shall be silent! Let them draw the sharpest knife, and whirl the swiftest tomahawk, for their bitterest enemy is in their hands. Uncas, topmost branch of a noble trunk, call on the cowards to hasten or their hearts will soften, and they will change to women!\"\n\n\"They look among the fishes for their dead!\" returned the low, soft voice of the youthful chieftain; \"the Hurons float with the slimy eels! They drop from the oaks like fruit that is ready to be eaten! and the Delawares laugh!\"\n\n\"Ay, ay,\" muttered the scout, who had listened to this peculiar burst of the natives with deep attention; \"they have warmed their Indian feelings, and they'll soon provoke the Maquas to give them a speedy end. As for me, who am of the whole blood of the whites, it is befitting that I should die as becomes my color, with no words of scoffing in my mouth, and without bitterness at the heart!\"\n\n\"Why die at all!\" said Cora, advancing from the place where natural horror had, until this moment, held her riveted to the rock; \"the path is open on every side; fly, then, to the woods, and call on God for succor. Go, brave men, we owe you too much already; let us no longer involve you in our hapless fortunes!\"\n\n\"You but little know the craft of the Iroquois, lady, if you judge they have left the path open to the woods!\" returned Hawkeye, who, however, immediately added in his simplicity, \"the down stream current, it is certain, might soon sweep us beyond the reach of their rifles or the sounds of their voices.\"\n\n\"Then try the river. Why linger to add to the number of the victims of our merciless enemies?\"\n\n\"Why,\" repeated the scout, looking about him proudly, \"because it is better for a man to die at peace with himself than to live haunted by an evil conscience! What answer could we give Munro, when he asked us where and how we left his children?\"\n\n\"Go to him, and say, that you left them with a message to hasten to their aid,\" returned Cora, advancing nigher to the scout, in her generous ardor; \"that the Hurons bear them into the northern wilds, but that by vigilance and speed they may yet be rescued; and if, after all, it should please heaven that his assistance come too late, bear to him,\" she continued, her voice gradually lowering, until it seemed nearly choked, \"the love, the blessings, the final prayers of his daughters, and bid him not mourn their early fate, but to look forward with humble confidence to the Christian's goal to meet his children.\"\n\nThe hard, weather-beaten features of the scout began to work, and when she had ended, he dropped his chin to his hand, like a man musing profoundly on the nature of the proposal.\n\n\"There is reason in her words!\" at length broke from his compressed and trembling lips; \"ay, and they bear the spirit of Christianity; what might be right and proper in a redskin, may be sinful in a man who has not even a cross in blood to plead for his ignorance. Chingachgook! Uncas! hear you the talk of the dark-eyed woman!\"\n\nHe now spoke in Delaware to his companions, and his address, though calm and deliberate, seemed very decided. The elder Mohican heard him with deep gravity, and appeared to ponder on his words, as though he felt the importance of their import. After a moment of hesitation, he waved his hand in assent, and uttered the English word \"Good!\" with the peculiar emphasis of his people. Then, replacing his knife and tomahawk in his girdle, the warrior moved silently to the edge of the rock which was most concealed from the banks of the river. Here he paused a moment, pointed significantly to the woods below, and saying a few words in his own language, as if indicating his intended route, he dropped into the water, and sank from before the eyes of the witnesses of his movements.\n\nThe scout delayed his departure to speak to the generous girl, whose breathing became lighter as she saw the success of her remonstrance.\n\n\"Wisdom is sometimes given to the young, as well as to the old,\" he said; \"and what you have spoken is wise, not to call it by a better word. If you are led into the woods, that is such of you as may be spared for a while, break the twigs on the bushes as you pass, and make the marks of your trail as broad as you can, when, if mortal eyes can see them, depend on having a friend who will follow to the ends of 'arth afore he desarts you.\"\n\nHe gave Cora an affectionate shake of the hand, lifted his rifle, and after regarding it a moment with melancholy solicitude, laid it carefully aside, and descended to the place where Chingachgook had just disappeared. For an instant he hung suspended by the rock; and looking about him, with a countenance of peculiar care, he added, bitterly, \"Had the powder held out, this disgrace could never have befallen!\" then, loosening his hold, the water closed above his head, and he also became lost to view.\n\nAll eyes were now turned on Uncas, who stood leaning against the ragged rock, in immovable composure. After waiting a short time, Cora pointed down the river, and said:--\n\n\"Your friends have not been seen, and are now, most probably, in safety; is it not time for you to follow?\"\n\n\"Uncas will stay,\" the young Mohican calmly answered in English.\n\n\"To increase the horror of our capture, and to diminish the chances of our release! Go, generous young man,\" Cora continued, lowering her eyes under the gaze of the Mohican, and, perhaps, with an intuitive consciousness of her power; \"go to my father, as I have said, and be the most confidential of my messengers. Tell him to trust you with the means to buy the freedom of his daughters. Go! 'tis my wish, 'tis my prayer, that you will go!\"\n\nThe settled, calm look of the young chief changed to an expression of gloom, but he no longer hesitated. With a noiseless step he crossed the rock, and dropped into the troubled stream. Hardly a breath was drawn by those he left behind, until they caught a glimpse of his head emerging for air, far down the current, when he again sank, and was seen no more.\n\nThese sudden and apparently successful experiments had all taken place in a few minutes of that time which had now become so precious. After the last look at Uncas, Cora turned, and, with a quivering lip, addressed herself to Heyward:--\n\n\"I have heard of your boasted skill in the water, too, Duncan,\" she said; \"follow, then, the wise example set you by these simple and faithful beings.\"\n\n\"Is such the faith that Cora Munro would exact from her protector?\" said the young man, smiling mournfully, but with bitterness.\n\n\"This is not a time for idle subtleties and false opinions,\" she answered; \"but a moment when every duty should be equally considered. To us you can be of no further service here, but your precious life may be saved for other and nearer friends.\"\n\nHe made no reply, though his eyes fell wistfully on the beautiful form of Alice, who was clinging to his arm with the dependency of an infant.\n\n\"Consider,\" continued Cora, after a pause, during which she seemed to struggle with a pang even more acute than any that her fears had excited, \"that the worst to us can be but death; a tribute that all must pay at the good time of God's appointment.\"\n\n\"There are evils worse than death,\" said Duncan, speaking hoarsely, and as if fretful at her importunity, \"but which the presence of one who would die in your behalf may avert.\"\n\nCora ceased her entreaties; and, veiling her face in her shawl, drew the nearly insensible Alice after her into the deepest recess of the inner cavern.\n\n\"Be gay securely; Dispel, my fair, with smiles, the tim'rous clouds, That hang on thy clear brow.\"\n\n_Death of Agrippina._\n\nThe sudden and almost magical change, from the stirring incidents of the combat to the stillness that now reigned around him, acted on the heated imagination of Heyward like some exciting dream. While all the images and events he had witnessed remained deeply impressed on his memory, he felt a difficulty in persuading himself of their truth. Still ignorant of the fate of those who had trusted to the aid of the swift current, he at first listened intently to any signal, or sounds of alarm, which might announce the good or evil fortune of their hazardous undertaking. His attention was, however, bestowed in vain; for, with the disappearance of Uncas, every sign of the adventurers had been lost, leaving him in total uncertainty of their fate.\n\nIn a moment of such painful doubt, Duncan did not hesitate to look about him, without consulting that protection from the rocks which just before had been so necessary to his safety. Every effort, however, to detect the least evidence of the approach of their hidden enemies, was as fruitless as the inquiry after his late companions. The wooded banks of the rivers seemed again deserted by everything possessing animal life. The uproar which had so lately echoed through the vaults of the forest was gone, leaving the rush of the waters to swell and sink on the currents of the air, in the unmingled sweetness of nature. A fish-hawk, which, secure on the topmost branches of a dead pine, had been a distant spectator of the fray, now stooped from his high and ragged perch, and soared, in wide sweeps, above his prey; while a jay, whose noisy voice had been stilled by the hoarser cries of the savages, ventured again to open his discordant throat, as though once more in undisturbed possession of his wild domains. Duncan caught from these natural accompaniments of the solitary scene a glimmering of hope; and he began to rally his faculties to renewed exertions, with something like a reviving confidence of success.\n\n\"The Hurons are not to be seen,\" he said, addressing David, who had by no means recovered from the effects of the stunning blow he had received; \"let us conceal ourselves in the cavern, and trust the rest to Providence.\"\n\n\"I remember to have united with two comely maidens, in lifting up our voices in praise and thanksgiving,\" returned the bewildered singing-master; \"since which time I have been visited by a heavy judgment for my sins. I have been mocked with the likeness of sleep, while sounds of discord have rent my ears, such as might manifest the fulness of time, and that nature had forgotten her harmony.\"\n\n\"Poor fellow! thine own period was, in truth, near its accomplishment! But arouse, and come with me; I will lead you where all other sounds but those of your own psalmody shall be excluded.\"\n\n\"There is melody in the fall of the cataract, and the rushing of many waters is sweet to the senses!\" said David, pressing his hand confusedly on his brow. \"Is not the air yet filled with shrieks and cries, as though the departed spirits of the damned--\"\n\n\"Not now, not now,\" interrupted the impatient Heyward, \"they have ceased, and they who raised them, I trust in God, they are gone too! everything but the water is still and at peace; in, then, where you may create those sounds you love so well to hear.\"\n\nDavid smiled sadly, though not without a momentary gleam of pleasure, at this allusion to his beloved vocation. He no longer hesitated to be led to a spot which promised such unalloyed gratification to his wearied senses; and, leaning on the arm of his companion, he entered the narrow mouth of the cave. Duncan seized a pile of the sassafras, which he drew before the passage, studiously concealing every appearance of an aperture. Within this fragile barrier he arranged the blankets abandoned by the foresters, darkening the inner extremity of the cavern, while its outer received a chastened light from the narrow ravine, through which one arm of the river rushed, to form the junction with its sister branch, a few rods below.\n\n\"I like not that principle of the natives, which teaches them to submit without a struggle, in emergencies that appear desperate,\" he said, while busied in this employment; \"our own maxim, which says, 'while life remains there is hope,' is more consoling, and better suited to a soldier's temperament. To you, Cora, I will urge no words of idle encouragement; your own fortitude and undisturbed reason will teach you all that may become your sex; but cannot we dry the tears of that trembling weeper on your bosom?\"\n\n\"I am calmer, Duncan,\" said Alice, raising herself from the arms of her sister, and forcing an appearance of composure through her tears; \"much calmer, now. Surely, in this hidden spot we are safe, we are secret, free from injury; we will hope everything from those generous men who have risked so much already in our behalf.\"\n\n\"Now does our gentle Alice speak like a daughter of Munro!\" said Heyward, pausing to press her hand as he passed towards the outer entrance of the cavern. \"With two such examples of courage before him, a man would be ashamed to prove other than a hero.\" He then seated himself in the centre of the cavern, grasping his remaining pistol with a hand convulsively clenched, while his contracted and frowning eye announced the sullen desperation of his purpose. \"The Hurons, if they come, may not gain our position so easily as they think,\" he lowly muttered; and dropping his head back against the rock, he seemed to await the result in patience, though his gaze was unceasingly bent on the open avenue to their place of retreat.\n\nWith the last sound of his voice, a deep, a long, and almost breathless silence succeeded. The fresh air of the morning had penetrated the recess, and its influence was gradually felt on the spirits of its inmates. As minute after minute passed by, leaving them in undisturbed security, the insinuating feeling of hope was gradually gaining possession of every bosom, though each one felt reluctant to give utterance to expectations that the next moment might so fearfully destroy.\n\nDavid alone formed an exception to these varying emotions. A gleam of light from the opening crossed his wan countenance, and fell upon the pages of the little volume, whose leaves he was again occupied in turning, as if searching for some song more fitted to their condition than any that had yet met his eye. He was, most probably, acting all this time under a confused recollection of the promised consolation of Duncan. At length, it would seem, his patient industry found its reward; for, without explanation or apology, he pronounced aloud the words \"Isle of Wight,\" drew a long, sweet sound from his pitch-pipe, and then ran through the preliminary modulations of the air, whose name he had just mentioned with the sweeter tones of his own musical voice.\n\n\"May not this prove dangerous?\" asked Cora, glancing her dark eye at Major Heyward.\n\n\"Poor fellow! his voice is too feeble to be heard amid the din of the falls,\" was the answer; \"besides, the cavern will prove his friend. Let him indulge his passion, since it may be done without hazard.\"\n\n\"Isle of Wight!\" repeated David, looking about him with that dignity with which he had long been wont to silence the whispering echoes of his school; \"'tis a brave tune, and set to solemn words; let it be sung with meet respect!\"\n\nAfter allowing a moment of stillness, to enforce his discipline, the voice of the singer was heard, in low, murmuring syllables, gradually stealing on the ear, until it filled the narrow vault with sounds rendered trebly thrilling by the feeble and tremulous utterance produced by his debility. The melody, which no weakness could destroy, gradually wrought its sweet influence on the senses of those who heard it. It even prevailed over the miserable travesty of the song of David which the singer had selected from a volume of similar effusions, and caused the sense to be forgotten in the insinuating harmony of the sounds. Alice unconsciously dried her tears, and bent her melting eyes on the pallid features of Gamut with an expression of chastened delight that she neither affected nor wished to conceal. Cora bestowed an approving smile on the pious efforts of the namesake of the Jewish prince, and Heyward soon turned his steady, stern look from the outlet of the cavern, to fasten it, with a milder character, on the face of David, or to meet the wandering beams which at moments strayed from the humid eyes of Alice. The open sympathy of the listeners stirred the spirit of the votary of music, whose voice regained its richness and volume, without losing that touching softness which proved its secret charm. Exerting his renovated powers to their utmost, he was yet filling the arches of the cave with long and full tones, when a yell burst into the air without, that instantly stilled his pious strains, choking his voice suddenly, as though his heart had literally bounded into the passage of his throat.\n\n\"We are lost!\" exclaimed Alice, throwing herself into the arms of Cora.\n\n\"Not yet, not yet,\" returned the agitated but undaunted Heyward; \"the sound came from the centre of the island, and it has been produced by the sight of their dead companions. We are not yet discovered, and there is still hope.\"\n\nFaint and almost despairing as was the prospect of escape, the words of Duncan were not thrown away, for it awakened the powers of the sisters in such a manner that they awaited the result in silence. A second yell soon followed the first, when a rush of voices was heard pouring down the island, from its upper to its lower extremity, until they reached the naked rock above the caverns, where, after a shout of savage triumph, the air continued full of horrible cries and screams, such as man alone can utter, and he only when in a state of the fiercest barbarity.\n\nThe sounds quickly spread around them in every direction. Some called to their fellows from the water's edge, and were answered from the heights above. Cries were heard in the startling vicinity of the chasm between the two caves, which mingled with hoarser yells that arose out of the abyss of the deep ravine. In short, so rapidly had the savage sounds diffused themselves over the barren rock, that it was not difficult for the anxious listeners to imagine they could be heard beneath, as in truth they were above and on every side of them.\n\nIn the midst of this tumult, a triumphant yell was raised within a few yards of the hidden entrance to the cave. Heyward abandoned every hope, with the belief it was the signal that they were discovered. Again the impression passed away, as he heard the voices collect near the spot where the white man had so reluctantly abandoned his rifle. Amid the jargon of the Indian dialects that he now plainly heard, it was easy to distinguish not only words, but sentences, in the _patois_ of the Canadas. A burst of voices had shouted simultaneously, \"La Longue Carabine!\" causing the opposite woods to re-echo with a name which, Heyward well remembered, had been given by his enemies to a celebrated hunter and scout of the English camp, and who, he now learnt for the first time, had been his late companion.\n\n\"La Longue Carabine! La Longue Carabine!\" passed from mouth to mouth, until the whole band appeared to be collected around a trophy which would seem to announce the death of its formidable owner. After a vociferous consultation, which was, at times, deafened by bursts of savage joy, they again separated, filling the air with the name of a foe, whose body, Heyward could collect from their expressions, they hoped to find concealed in some crevice of the island.\n\n\"Now,\" he whispered to the trembling sisters, \"now is the moment of uncertainty! if our place of retreat escape this scrutiny, we are still safe! In every event, we are assured, by what has fallen from our enemies, that our friends have escaped, and in two short hours we may look for succor from Webb.\"\n\nThere were now a few minutes of fearful stillness, during which Heyward well knew that the savages conducted their search with greater vigilance and method. More than once he could distinguish their footsteps, as they brushed the sassafras, causing the faded leaves to rustle, and the branches to snap. At length, the pile yielded a little, a corner of the blanket fell, and a faint ray of light gleamed into the inner part of the cave. Cora folded Alice to her bosom in agony, and Duncan sprang to his feet. A shout was at that moment heard, as if issuing from the centre of the rock, announcing that the neighboring cavern had at length been entered. In a minute, the number and loudness of the voices indicated that the whole party was collected in and around that secret place.\n\nAs the inner passages to the two caves were so close to each other, Duncan, believing that escape was no longer possible, passed David and the sisters, to place himself between the latter and the first onset of the terrible meeting. Grown desperate by his situation, he drew nigh the slight barrier which separated him only by a few feet from his relentless pursuers, and placing his face to the casual opening, he even looked out, with a sort of desperate indifference, on their movements.\n\nWithin reach of his arm was the brawny shoulder of a gigantic Indian, whose deep and authoritative voice appeared to give directions to the proceedings of his fellows. Beyond him again, Duncan could look into the vault opposite, which was filled with savages, upturning and rifling the humble furniture of the scout. The wound of David had dyed the leaves of sassafras with a color that the natives well knew was anticipating the season. Over this sign of their success, they set up a howl, like an opening from so many hounds who had recovered a lost trail. After this yell of victory, they tore up the fragrant bed of the cavern, and bore the branches into the chasm, scattering the boughs, as if they suspected them of concealing the person of the man they had so long hated and feared. One fierce and wild-looking warrior approached the chief bearing a load of the brush, and pointing, exultingly, to the deep red stains with which it was sprinkled, uttered his joy in Indian yells, whose meaning Heyward was only enabled to comprehend by the frequent repetition of the name of \"La Longue Carabine!\" When his triumph had ceased, he cast the brush on the slight heap that Duncan had made before the entrance of the second cavern, and closed the view. His example was followed by others, who, as they drew the branches from the cave of the scout, threw them into one pile, adding, unconsciously, to the security of those they sought. The very slightness of the defence was its chief merit, for no one thought of disturbing a mass of brush, which all of them believed, in that moment of hurry and confusion, had been accidentally raised by the hands of their own party.\n\nAs the blankets yielded before the outward pressure, and the branches settled in the fissure of the rock by their own weight, forming a compact body, Duncan once more breathed freely. With a light step, and lighter heart, he returned to the centre of the cave, and took the place he had left, where he could command a view of the opening next the river. While he was in the act of making this movement, the Indians, as if changing their purpose by a common impulse, broke away from the cavern in a body, and were heard rushing up the island again, towards the point whence they had originally descended. Here another wailing cry betrayed that they were again collected around the bodies of their dead comrades.\n\nDuncan now ventured to look at his companions; for, during the most critical moments of their danger, he had been apprehensive that the anxiety of his countenance might communicate some additional alarm to those who were so little able to sustain it.\n\n\"They are gone, Cora!\" he whispered; \"Alice, they are returned whence they came, and we are saved! To Heaven, that has alone delivered us from the grasp of so merciless an enemy, be all the praise!\"\n\n\"Then to Heaven will I return my thanks!\" exclaimed the younger sister, rising from the encircling arms of Cora, and casting herself with enthusiastic gratitude on the naked rock; \"to that Heaven who has spared the tears of a gray-headed father; has saved the lives of those I so much love--\"\n\nBoth Heyward, and the more tempered Cora, witnessed the act of involuntary emotion with powerful sympathy, the former secretly believing that piety had never worn a form so lovely as it had now assumed in the youthful person of Alice. Her eyes radiant with the glow of grateful feelings; the flush of her beauty was again seated on her cheeks, and her whole soul seemed ready and anxious to pour out its thanksgivings, through the medium of her eloquent features. But when her lips moved, the words they should have uttered appeared frozen by some new and sudden chill. Her bloom gave place to the paleness of death; her soft and melting eyes grew hard, and seemed contracting with horror; while those hands which she had raised, clasped in each other, towards heaven, dropped in horizontal lines before her, the fingers pointed forward in convulsed motion. Heyward turned, the instant she gave a direction to his suspicions, and, peering just above the ledge which formed the threshold of the open outlet of the cavern, he beheld the malignant, fierce, and savage features of Le Renard Subtil.\n\nIn that moment of surprise, the self-possession of Heyward did not desert him. He observed by the vacant expression of the Indian's countenance, that his eye, accustomed to the open air, had not yet been able to penetrate the dusky light which pervaded the depth of the cavern. He had even thought of retreating beyond a curvature in the natural wall, which might still conceal him and his companions, when, by the sudden gleam of intelligence that shot across the features of the savage, he saw it was too late, and that they were betrayed.\n\nThe look of exultation and brutal triumph which announced this terrible truth was irresistibly irritating. Forgetful of everything but the impulses of his hot blood, Duncan levelled his pistol and fired. The report of the weapon made the cavern bellow like an eruption from a volcano; and when the smoke it vomited had been driven away before the current of air which issued from the ravine, the place so lately occupied by the features of his treacherous guide was vacant. Rushing to the outlet, Heyward caught a glimpse of his dark figure, stealing around a low and narrow ledge, which soon hid him entirely from sight.\n\nAmong the savages, a frightful stillness succeeded the explosion, which had just been heard bursting from the bowels of the rock. But when Le Renard raised his voice in a long and intelligible whoop, it was answered by a spontaneous yell from the mouth of every Indian within hearing of the sound. The clamorous noises again rushed down the island; and before Duncan had time to recover from the shock, his feeble barrier of brush was scattered to the winds, the cavern was entered at both its extremities, and he and his companions were dragged from their shelter and borne into the day, where they stood surrounded by the whole band of the triumphant Hurons.\n\n\"I fear we shall outsleep the coming morn As much as we this night have overwatched!\"\n\n_Midsummer Night's Dream._\n\nThe instant the shock of this sudden misfortune had abated, Duncan began to make his observations on the appearance and proceedings of their captors. Contrary to the usages of the natives in the wantonness of their success, they had respected, not only the persons of the trembling sisters, but his own. The rich ornaments of his military attire had indeed been repeatedly handled by different individuals of the tribe with eyes expressing a savage longing to possess the baubles; but before the customary violence could be resorted to, a mandate in the authoritative voice of the large warrior already mentioned, stayed the uplifted hand, and convinced Heyward that they were to be reserved for some object of particular moment.\n\nWhile, however, these manifestations of weakness were exhibited by the young and vain of the party, the more experienced warriors continued their search throughout both caverns, with an activity that denoted they were far from being satisfied with those fruits of their conquest which had already been brought to light. Unable to discover any new victim, these diligent workers of vengeance soon approached their male prisoners, pronouncing the name of \"La Longue Carabine,\" with a fierceness that could not easily be mistaken. Duncan affected not to comprehend the meaning of their repeated and violent interrogatories, while his companion was spared the effort of a similar deception by his ignorance of French. Wearied, at length, by their importunities, and apprehensive of irritating his captors by too stubborn a silence, the former looked about him in quest of Magua; who might interpret his answers to questions which were at each moment becoming more earnest and threatening.\n\nThe conduct of this savage had formed a solitary exception to that of all his fellows. While the others were busily occupied in seeking to gratify their childish passion for finery, by plundering even the miserable effects of the scout, or had been searching, with such bloodthirsty vengeance in their looks, for their absent owner, Le Renard had stood at a little distance from the prisoners, with a demeanor so quiet and satisfied, as to betray that he had already effected the grand purpose of this treachery. When the eyes of Heyward first met those of his recent guide, he turned them away in horror at the sinister though calm look he encountered. Conquering his disgust, however, he was able, with an averted face, to address his successful enemy.\n\n\"Le Renard Subtil is too much of a warrior,\" said the reluctant Heyward, \"to refuse telling an unarmed man what his conquerors say.\"\n\n\"They ask for the hunter who knows the paths through the woods,\" returned Magua, in his broken English, laying his hand, at the same time, with a ferocious smile, on the bundle of leaves with which a wound on his own shoulder was bandaged. \"La Longue Carabine! his rifle is good, and his eye never shut; but, like the short gun of the white chief, it is nothing against the life of Le Subtil!\"\n\n\"Le Renard is too brave to remember the hurts received in war, or the hands that gave them!\"\n\n\"Was it war, when the tired Indian rested at the sugar-tree to taste his corn! who filled the bushes with creeping enemies! who drew the knife! whose tongue was peace, while his heart was colored with blood! Did Magua say that the hatchet was out of the ground, and that his hand had dug it up?\"\n\nAs Duncan dared not retort upon his accuser by reminding him of his own premeditated treachery, and disdained to deprecate his resentment by any words of apology, he remained silent. Magua seemed also content to rest the controversy as well as all further communication there, for he resumed the leaning attitude against the rock, from which, in momentary energy, he had arisen. But the cry of \"La Longue Carabine\" was renewed the instant the impatient savages perceived that the short dialogue was ended.\n\n\"You hear,\" said Magua, with stubborn indifference; \"the red Hurons call for the life of 'The Long Rifle,' or they will have the blood of them that keep him hid!\"\n\n\"He is gone--escaped; he is far beyond their reach.\"\n\nRenard smiled with cold contempt, as he answered,--\n\n\"When the white man dies, he thinks he is at peace; but the redmen know how to torture even the ghosts of their enemies. Where is his body? Let the Hurons see his scalp!\"\n\n\"He is not dead, but escaped.\"\n\nMagua shook his head incredulously.\n\n\"Is he a bird, to spread his wings; or is he a fish, to swim without air! The white chief reads in his books, and he believes the Hurons are fools!\"\n\n\"Though no fish, The Long Rifle can swim. He floated down the stream when the powder was all burnt, and when the eyes of the Hurons were behind a cloud.\"\n\n\"And why did the white chief stay?\" demanded the still incredulous Indian. \"Is he a stone that goes to the bottom, or does the scalp burn his head?\"\n\n\"That I am not a stone, your dead comrade, who fell into the falls, might answer, were the life still in him,\" said the provoked young man, using, in his anger, that boastful language which was most likely to excite the admiration of an Indian. \"The white man thinks none but cowards desert their women.\"\n\nMagua muttered a few words, inaudibly, between his teeth, before he continued, aloud,--\n\n\"Can the Delawares swim, too, as well as crawl in the bushes? Where is Le Gros Serpent?\"\n\nDuncan, who perceived by the use of these Canadian appellations, that his late companions were much better known to his enemies than to himself, answered, reluctantly, \"He also is gone down with the water.\"\n\n\"Le Cerf Agile is not here?\"\n\n\"I know not whom you call 'The Nimble Deer,'\" said Duncan, gladly profiting by any excuse to create delay.\n\n\"Uncas,\" returned Magua, pronouncing the Delaware name with even greater difficulty than he spoke his English words. \"'Bounding Elk' is what the white man says, when he calls to the young Mohican.\"\n\n\"Here is some confusion in names between us, Le Renard,\" said Duncan, hoping to provoke a discussion. \"_Daim_ is the French for deer, and _cerf_ for stag; _elan_ is the true term, when one would speak of an elk.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" muttered the Indian, in his native tongue; \"the pale-faces are prattling women! they have two words for each thing, while a redskin will make the sound of his voice speak for him.\" Then changing his language, he continued, adhering to the imperfect nomenclature of his provincial instructors, \"The deer is swift, but weak; the elk is swift, but strong; and the son of Le Serpent is Le Cerf Agile. Has he leaped the river to the woods?\"\n\n\"If you mean the younger Delaware, he too is gone down with the water.\"\n\nAs there was nothing improbable to an Indian in the manner of the escape, Magua admitted the truth of what he had heard, with a readiness that afforded additional evidence how little he would prize such worthless captives. With his companions, however, the feeling was manifestly different.\n\nThe Hurons had awaited the result of this short dialogue with characteristic patience, and with a silence that increased until there was a general stillness in the band. When Heyward ceased to speak, they turned their eyes, as one man, on Magua, demanding, in this expressive manner, an explanation of what had been said. Their interpreter pointed to the river, and made them acquainted with the result, as much by the action as by the few words he uttered. When the fact was generally understood, the savages raised a frightful yell, which declared the extent of their disappointment. Some ran furiously to the water's edge, beating the air with frantic gestures, while others spat upon the element, to resent the supposed treason it had committed against their acknowledged rights as conquerors. A few, and they not the least powerful and terrific of the band, threw lowering looks, in which the fiercest passion was only tempered by habitual self-command, at those captives who still remained in their power; while one or two even gave vent to their malignant feelings by the most menacing gestures, against which neither the sex nor the beauty of the sisters was any protection. The young soldier made a desperate, but fruitless effort, to spring to the side of Alice, when he saw the dark hand of a savage twisted in the rich tresses which were flowing in volumes over her shoulders, while a knife was passed around the head from which they fell, as if to denote the horrid manner in which it was about to be robbed of its beautiful ornament. But his hands were bound; and at the first movement he made, he felt the grasp of the powerful Indian who directed the band, pressing his shoulder like a vise. Immediately conscious how unavailing any struggle against such an overwhelming force must prove, he submitted to his fate, encouraging his gentle companions by a few low and tender assurances that the natives seldom failed to threaten more than they performed.\n\nBut, while Duncan resorted to these words of consolation to quiet the apprehensions of the sisters, he was not so weak as to deceive himself. He well knew that the authority of an Indian chief was so little conventional, that it was oftener maintained by physical superiority than by any moral supremacy he might possess. The danger was, therefore, magnified exactly in proportion to the number of the savage spirits by which they were surrounded. The most positive mandate from him who seemed the acknowledged leader, was liable to be violated at each moment, by any rash hand that might choose to sacrifice a victim to the _manes_ of some dead friend or relative. While, therefore, he sustained an outward appearance of calmness and fortitude, his heart leaped into his throat, whenever any of their fierce captors drew nearer than common to the helpless sisters, or fastened one of their sullen wandering looks on those fragile forms which were so little able to resist the slightest assault.\n\nHis apprehensions were, however, greatly relieved, when he saw that the leader had summoned his warriors to himself in council. Their deliberations were short, and it would seem, by the silence of most of the party, the decision unanimous. By the frequency with which the few speakers pointed in the direction of the encampment of Webb, it was apparent they dreaded the approach of danger from that quarter. This consideration probably hastened their determination, and quickened the subsequent movements.\n\nDuring this short conference, Heyward, finding a respite from his greatest fears, had leisure to admire the cautious manner in which the Hurons had made their approaches, even after hostilities had ceased.\n\nIt has already been stated, that the upper half of the island was a naked rock, and destitute of any other defences than a few scattered logs of drift-wood. They had selected this point to make their descent, having borne the canoe through the wood around the cataract for that purpose. Placing their arms in the little vessel, a dozen men clinging to its sides had trusted themselves to the direction of the canoe, which was controlled by two of the most skilful warriors, in attitudes that enabled them to command a view of the dangerous passage. Favored by this arrangement, they touched the head of the island at that point which had proved so fatal to their first adventures, but with the advantages of superior numbers, and the possession of fire-arms. That such had been the manner of their descent was rendered quite apparent to Duncan; for they now bore the light bark from the upper end of the rock, and placed it in the water, near the mouth of the outer cavern. As soon as this change was made, the leader made signs to the prisoners to descend and enter.\n\nAs resistance was impossible, and remonstrance useless, Heyward set the example of submission, by leading the way into the canoe, where he was soon seated with the sisters, and the still wondering David. Notwithstanding the Hurons were necessarily ignorant of the little channels among the eddies and rapids of the stream, they knew the common signs of such a navigation too well to commit any material blunder. When the pilot chosen for the task of guiding the canoe had taken his station, the whole band plunged again into the river, the vessel glided down the current, and in a few moments the captives found themselves on the south bank of the stream, nearly opposite to the point where they had struck it the preceding evening.\n\nHere was held another short but earnest consultation, during which the horses, to whose panic their owners ascribed their heaviest misfortune, were led from the cover of the woods, and brought to the sheltered spot. The band now divided. The great chief so often mentioned, mounting the charger of Heyward, led the way directly across the river, followed by most of his people, and disappeared in the woods, leaving the prisoners in charge of six savages, at whose head was Le Renard Subtil. Duncan witnessed all their movements with renewed uneasiness.\n\nHe had been fond of believing, from the uncommon forbearance of the savages, that he was reserved as a prisoner to be delivered to Montcalm. As the thoughts of those who are in misery seldom slumber, and the invention is never more lively than when it is stimulated by hope, however feeble and remote, he had even imagined that the parental feelings of Munro were to be made instrumental in seducing him from his duty to the king. For though the French commander bore a high character for courage and enterprise, he was also thought to be expert in those political practices, which do not always respect the nicer obligations of morality, and which so generally disgraced the European diplomacy of that period.\n\nAll those busy and ingenious speculations were now annihilated by the conduct of his captors. That portion of the band who had followed the huge warrior took the route towards the foot of the Horican, and no other expectation was left for himself and companions, than that they were to be retained as hopeless captives by their savage conquerors. Anxious to know the worst, and willing, in such an emergency, to try the potency of gold, he overcame his reluctance to speak to Magua. Addressing himself to his former guide, who had now assumed the authority and manner of one who was to direct the future movements of the party, he said, in tones as friendly and confiding as he could assume,--\n\n\"I would speak to Magua, what is fit only for so great a chief to hear.\"\n\nThe Indian turned his eyes on the young soldier scornfully, as he answered,--\n\n\"Speak; trees have no ears!\"\n\n\"But the red Hurons are not deaf; and counsel that is fit for the great men of a nation would make the young warriors drunk. If Magua will not listen, the officer of the king knows how to be silent.\"\n\nThe savage spoke carelessly to his comrades, who were busied, after their awkward manner, in preparing the horses for the reception of the sisters, and moved a little to one side, whither, by a cautious gesture, he induced Heyward to follow.\n\n\"Now speak,\" he said; \"if the words are such as Magua should hear.\"\n\n\"Le Renard Subtil has proved himself worthy of the honorable name given to him by his Canada fathers,\" commenced Heyward; \"I see his wisdom, and all that he has done for us, and shall remember it, when the hour to reward him arrives. Yes! Renard has proved that he is not only a great chief in council, but one who knows how to deceive his enemies!\"\n\n\"What has Renard done?\" coldly demanded the Indian.\n\n\"What! has he not seen that the woods were filled with outlying parties of the enemies, and that the Serpent could not steal through them without being seen? Then, did he not lose his path to blind the eyes of the Hurons? Did he not pretend to go back to his tribe, who had treated him ill, and driven him from their wigwams like a dog? And, when we saw what he wished to do, did we not aid him, by making a false face, that the Hurons might think the white man believed that his friend was his enemy? Is not all this true? And when Le Subtil had shut the eyes and stopped the ears of his nation by his wisdom, did they not forget that they had once done him wrong, and forced him to flee to the Mohawks? And did they not leave him on the south side of the river, with their prisoners, while they have gone foolishly on the north? Does not Renard mean to turn like a fox on his footsteps, and to carry to the rich and gray-headed Scotchman his daughters? Yes, Magua, I see it all, and I have already been thinking how so much wisdom and honesty should be repaid. First, the chief of William Henry will give as a great chief should for such a service. The medal[16] of Magua will no longer be of tin, but of beaten gold; his horn will run over with powder; dollars will be as plenty in his pouch as pebbles on the shore of Horican; and the deer will lick his hand, for they will know it to be vain to fly from the rifle he will carry! As for myself, I know not how to exceed the gratitude of the Scotchman, but I--yes, I will--\"\n\n\"What will the young chief who comes from towards the sun, give?\" demanded the Huron, observing that Heyward hesitated in his desire to end the enumeration of benefits with that which might form the climax of an Indian's wishes.\n\n\"He will make the fire-water from the Islands in the salt lake flow before the wigwam of Magua, until the heart of the Indian shall be lighter than the feathers of the humming-bird, and his breath sweeter than the wild honeysuckle.\"\n\nLe Renard had listened gravely as Heyward slowly proceeded in his subtle speech. When the young man mentioned the artifice he supposed the Indian to have practised on his own nation, the countenance of the listener was veiled in an expression of cautious gravity. At the allusion to the injury which Duncan affected to believe had driven the Huron from his native tribe, a gleam of such ungovernable ferocity flashed from the other's eyes, as induced the adventurous speaker to believe he had struck the proper chord. And by the time he reached the part where he so artfully blended the thirst of vengeance with the desire of gain, he had, at least, obtained a command of the deepest attention of the savage. The question put by Le Renard had been calm, and with all the dignity of an Indian; but it was quite apparent, by the thoughtful expression of the listener's countenance, that the answer was most cunningly devised. The Huron mused a few moments, and then laying his hand on the rude bandages of his wounded shoulder, he said, with some energy,--\n\n\"Do friends make such remarks?\"\n\n\"Would La Longue Carabine cut one so light on an enemy?\"\n\n\"Do the Delawares crawl upon those they love, like snakes, twisting themselves to strike?\"\n\n\"Would Le Gros Serpent have been heard by the ears of one he wished to be deaf?\"\n\n\"Does the white chief burn his powder in the faces of his brothers?\"\n\n\"Does he ever miss his aim, when seriously bent to kill?\" returned Duncan, smiling with well acted sincerity.\n\nAnother long and deliberate pause succeeded these sententious questions and ready replies. Duncan saw that the Indian hesitated. In order to complete his victory, he was in the act of recommencing the enumeration of the rewards, when Magua made an expressive gesture and said--\n\n\"Enough; Le Renard is a wise chief, and what he does will be seen. Go, and keep the mouth shut. When Magua speaks, it will be the time to answer.\"\n\nHeyward, perceiving that the eyes of his companion were warily fastened on the rest of the band, fell back immediately, in order to avoid the appearance of any suspicious confederacy with their leader. Magua approached the horses, and affected to be well pleased with the diligence and ingenuity of his comrades. He then signed to Heyward to assist the sisters into the saddles, for he seldom deigned to use the English tongue, unless urged by some motive of more than usual moment.\n\nThere was no longer any plausible pretext for delay; and Duncan was obliged, however reluctantly, to comply. As he performed this office, he whispered his reviving hopes in the ears of the trembling females, who, through dread of encountering the savage countenances of their captors, seldom raised their eyes from the ground. The mare of David had been taken with the followers of the large chief; in consequence, its owner, as well as Duncan, was compelled to journey on foot. The latter did not, however, so much regret this circumstance, as it might enable him to retard the speed of the party; for he still turned his longing looks in the direction of Fort Edward, in the vain expectation of catching some sound from that quarter of the forest, which might denote the approach of succor.\n\nWhen all were prepared, Magua made the signal to proceed, advancing in front to lead the party in person. Next followed David, who was gradually coming to a true sense of his condition, as the effects of the wound became less and less apparent. The sisters rode in his rear, with Heyward at their side, while the Indians flanked the party, and brought up the close of the march, with a caution that seemed never to tire.\n\nIn this manner they proceeded in uninterrupted silence, except when Heyward addressed some solitary word of comfort to the females, or David gave vent to the moanings of his spirit in piteous exclamations, which he intended should express the humility of resignation. Their direction lay towards the south, and in a course nearly opposite to the road to William Henry. Notwithstanding this apparent adherence in Magua to the original determination of his conquerors, Heyward could not believe his tempting bait was so soon forgotten; and he knew the windings of an Indian path too well, to suppose that its apparent course led directly to its object, when artifice was at all necessary. Mile after mile was, however, passed through the boundless woods, in this painful manner, without any prospect of a termination to their journey. Heyward watched the sun, as he darted his meridian rays through the branches of the trees, and pined for the moment when the policy of Magua should change their route to one more favorable to his hopes. Sometimes he fancied the wary savage, despairing of passing the arm of Montcalm in safety, was holding his way towards a well-known border settlement, where a distinguished officer of the crown, and a favored friend of the Six Nations, held his large possessions, as well as his usual residence. To be delivered into the hands of Sir William Johnson was far preferable to being led into the wilds of Canada; but in order to effect even the former, it would be necessary to traverse the forest for many weary leagues, each step of which was carrying him farther from the scene of the war, and, consequently, from the post, not only of honor, but of duty.\n\nCora alone remembered the parting injunctions of the scout, and whenever an opportunity offered, she stretched forth her arm to bend aside the twigs that met her hands. But the vigilance of the Indians rendered this act of precaution both difficult and dangerous. She was often defeated in her purpose, by encountering their watchful eyes, when it became necessary to feign an alarm she did not feel, and occupy the limb by some gesture of feminine apprehension. Once, and once only, was she completely successful; when she broke down the bough of a large sumach, and, by a sudden thought, let her glove fall at the same instant. This sign, intended for those that might follow, was observed by one of her conductors, who restored the glove, broke the remaining branches of the bush in such a manner that it appeared to proceed from the struggling of some beast in its branches, and then laid his hand on his tomahawk, with a look so significant, that it put an effectual end to these stolen memorials of their passage.\n\nAs there were horses, to leave the prints of their footsteps, in both bands of the Indians, this interruption cut off any probable hopes of assistance being conveyed through the means of their trail.\n\nHeyward would have ventured a remonstrance, had there been anything encouraging in the gloomy reserve of Magua. But the savage, during all this time, seldom turned to look at his followers, and never spoke. With the sun for his only guide, or aided by such blind marks as are only known to the sagacity of a native, he held his way along the barrens of pine, through occasional little fertile vales, across brooks and rivulets, and over undulating hills, with the accuracy of instinct, and nearly with the directness of a bird. He never seemed to hesitate. Whether the path was hardly distinguishable, whether it disappeared, or whether it lay beaten and plain before him, made no sensible difference in his speed or certainty. It seemed as if fatigue could not affect him. Whenever the eyes of the wearied travellers rose from the decayed leaves over which they trod, his dark form was to be seen glancing among the stems of the trees in front, his head immovably fastened in a forward position, with the light plume on his crest fluttering in a current of air, made solely by the swiftness of his own motion.\n\nBut all this diligence and speed were not without an object. After crossing a low vale, through which a gushing brook meandered, he suddenly ascended a hill, so steep and difficult of ascent, that the sisters were compelled to alight, in order to follow. When the summit was gained, they found themselves on a level spot, but thinly covered with trees, under one of which Magua had thrown his dark form, as if willing and ready to seek that rest which was so much needed by the whole party.\n\n\"Cursed by my tribe If I forgive him.\"\n\n_Shylock._\n\nThe Indian had selected, for this desirable purpose, one of those steep, pyramidal hills, which bear a strong resemblance to artificial mounds, and which so frequently occur in the valleys of America. The one in question was high and precipitous; its top flattened, as usual; but with one of its sides more than ordinarily irregular. It possessed no other apparent advantage for a resting-place than in its elevation and form, which might render defence easy, and surprise nearly impossible. As Heyward, however, no longer expected that rescue which time and distance now rendered so improbable, he regarded these little peculiarities with an eye devoid of interest, devoting himself entirely to the comfort and condolence of his feebler companions. The Narragansetts were suffered to browse on the branches of the trees and shrubs that were thinly scattered over the summit of the hill, while the remains of their provisions were spread under the shade of a beech, that stretched its horizontal limbs like a canopy above them.\n\nNotwithstanding the swiftness of their flight, one of the Indians had found an opportunity to strike a straggling fawn with an arrow, and had borne the more preferable fragments of the victim patiently on his shoulders, to the stopping-place. Without any aid from the science of cookery, he was immediately employed, in common with his fellows, in gorging himself with this digestible sustenance. Magua alone sat apart, without participation in the revolting meal, and apparently buried in the deepest thought.\n\nThis abstinence, so remarkable in an Indian, when he possessed the means of satisfying hunger, at length attracted the notice of Heyward. The young man willingly believed that the Huron deliberated on the most eligible manner of eluding the vigilance of his associates. With a view to assist his plans, by any suggestion of his own, and to strengthen the temptation, he left the beech, and straggled as if without an object, to the spot where Le Renard was seated.\n\n\"Has not Magua kept the sun in his face long enough to escape all danger from the Canadians?\" he asked, as though no longer doubtful of the good intelligence established between them; \"and will not the chief of William Henry be better pleased to see his daughters before another night may have hardened his heart to their loss, to make him less liberal in his reward?\"\n\n\"Do the pale-faces love their children less in the morning than at night?\" asked the Indian, coldly.\n\n\"By no means,\" returned Heyward, anxious to recall his error, if he had made one; \"the white man may, and does often, forget the burial-place of his fathers; he sometimes ceases to remember those he should love and has promised to cherish; but the affection of a parent for his child is never permitted to die.\"\n\n\"And is the heart of the white-headed chief soft, and will he think of the babes that his squaws have given him? He is hard to his warriors, and his eyes are made of stone!\"\n\n\"He is severe to the idle and wicked, but to the sober and deserving he is a leader, but just and humane. I have known many fond and tender parents, but never have I seen a man whose heart was softer towards his child. You have seen the gray-head in front of his warriors, Magua; but I have seen his eyes swimming in water, when he spoke of those children who are now in your power!\"\n\nHeyward paused, for he knew not how to construe the remarkable expression that gleamed across the swarthy features of the attentive Indian. At first it seemed as if the remembrance of the promised reward grew vivid in his mind, while he listened to the sources of parental feeling which were to assure its possession; but as Duncan proceeded, the expression of joy became so fiercely malignant, that it was impossible not to apprehend it proceeded from some passion more sinister than avarice.\n\n\"Go,\" said the Huron, suppressing the alarming exhibition in an instant, in a death-like calmness of countenance; \"go to the dark-haired daughter, and say, Magua waits to speak. The father will remember what the child promises.\"\n\nDuncan, who interpreted this speech to express a wish for some additional pledge that the promised gifts should not be withheld, slowly and reluctantly repaired to the place where the sisters were now resting from their fatigue, to communicate its purport to Cora.\n\n\"You understand the nature of an Indian's wishes,\" he concluded, as he led her towards the place where she was expected, \"and must be prodigal of your offers of powder and blankets. Ardent spirits are, however, the most prized by such as he; nor would it be amiss to add some boon from your own hand, with that grace you so well know how to practise. Remember, Cora, that on your presence of mind and ingenuity even your life, as well as that of Alice, may in some measure depend.\"\n\n\"Heyward, and yours!\"\n\n\"Mine is of little moment; it is already sold to my king, and is a prize to be seized by any enemy who may possess the power. I have no father to expect me, and but few friends to lament a fate which I have courted with the insatiable longings of youth after distinction. But hush! we approach the Indian. Magua, the lady with whom you wish to speak is here.\"\n\nThe Indian rose slowly from his seat, and stood for near a minute silent and motionless. He then signed with his hand for Heyward to retire, saying coldly,--\n\n\"When the Huron talks to the women, his tribe shut their ears.\"\n\nDuncan, still lingering, as if refusing to comply, Cora said, with a calm smile--\n\n\"You hear, Heyward, and delicacy at least should urge you to retire. Go to Alice, and comfort her with our reviving prospects.\"\n\nShe waited until he had departed, and then turning to the native, with the dignity of her sex in her voice and manner, she added, \"What would Le Renard say to the daughter of Munro?\"\n\n\"Listen,\" said the Indian, laying his hand firmly upon her arm, as if willing to draw her utmost attention to his words; a movement that Cora as firmly but quietly repulsed, by extricating the limb from his grasp: \"Magua was born a chief and a warrior among the red Hurons of the lakes; he saw the suns of twenty summers make the snows of twenty winters run off in the streams, before he saw a pale-face; and he was happy! Then his Canada fathers came into the woods, and taught him to drink the fire-water, and he became a rascal. The Hurons drove him from the graves of his fathers, as they would chase the hunted buffalo. He ran down the shores of the lakes, and followed their outlet to the 'city of cannon.' There he hunted and fished, till the people chased him again through the woods into the arms of his enemies. The chief, who was born a Huron, was at last a warrior among the Mohawks!\"\n\n\"Something like this I had heard before,\" said Cora, observing that he paused to suppress those passions which began to burn with too bright a flame, as he recalled the recollection of his supposed injuries.\n\n\"Was it the fault of Le Renard that his head was not made of rock? Who gave him the fire-water? who made him a villain? 'Twas the pale-faces, the people of your own color.\"\n\n\"And am I answerable that thoughtless and unprincipled men exist, whose shades of countenance may resemble mine?\" Cora calmly demanded of the excited savage.\n\n\"No; Magua is a man, and not a fool; such as you never open their lips to the burning stream: the Great Spirit has given you wisdom!\"\n\n\"What then have I to do, or say, in the matter of your misfortunes, not to say of your errors?\"\n\n\"Listen,\" repeated the Indian, resuming his earnest attitude; \"when his English and French fathers dug up the hatchet, Le Renard struck the war-post of the Mohawks, and went out against his own nation. The pale-faces have driven the redskins from their hunting-grounds, and now when they fight, a white man leads the way. The old chief at Horican, your father, was the great captain of our war-party. He said to the Mohawks do this, and do that, and he was minded. He made a law, that if an Indian swallowed the fire-water, and came into the cloth wigwams of his warriors, it should not be forgotten. Magua foolishly opened his mouth, and the hot liquor led him into the cabin of Munro. What did the gray-head? let his daughter say.\"\n\n\"He forgot not his words, and did justice by punishing the offender,\" said the undaunted daughter.\n\n\"Justice!\" repeated the Indian, casting an oblique glance of the most ferocious expression at her unyielding countenance; \"is it justice to make evil, and then punish for it? Magua was not himself; it was the fire-water that spoke and acted for him! but Munro did not believe it. The Huron chief was tied up before all the pale-faced warriors, and whipped like a dog.\"\n\nCora remained silent, for she knew not how to palliate this imprudent severity on the part of her father, in a manner to suit the comprehension of an Indian.\n\n\"See!\" continued Magua, tearing aside the slight calico that very imperfectly concealed his painted breast; \"here are scars given by knives and bullets--of these a warrior may boast before his nation; but the gray-head has left marks on the back of the Huron chief, that he must hide, like a squaw, under this painted cloth of the whites.\"\n\n\"I had thought,\" resumed Cora, \"that an Indian warrior was patient, and that his spirit felt not, and knew not, the pain his body suffered.\"\n\n\"When the Chippewas tied Magua to the stake, and cut this gash,\" said the other, laying his finger on a deep scar, \"the Huron laughed in their faces, and told them, Women struck so light! His spirit was then in the clouds! But when he felt the blows of Munro, his spirit lay under the birch. The spirit of a Huron is never drunk; it remembers forever!\"\n\n\"But it may be appeased. If my father has done you this injustice, show him how an Indian can forgive an injury, and take back his daughters. You have heard from Major Heyward--\"\n\nMagua shook his head, forbidding the repetition of offers he so much despised.\n\n\"What would you have?\" continued Cora, after a most painful pause, while the conviction forced itself on her mind that the too sanguine and generous Duncan had been cruelly deceived by the cunning of the savage.\n\n\"What a Huron loves--good for good; bad for bad!\"\n\n\"You would then revenge the injury inflicted by Munro on his helpless daughters. Would it not be more like a man to go before his face, and take the satisfaction of a warrior?\"\n\n\"The arms of the pale-faces are long, and their knives sharp!\" returned the savage, with a malignant laugh: \"why should Le Renard go among the muskets of his warriors, when he holds the spirit of the gray-head in his hand?\"\n\n\"Name your intention, Magua,\" said Cora, struggling with herself to speak with steady calmness. \"Is it to lead us prisoners to the woods, or do you contemplate even some greater evil? Is there no reward, no means of palliating the injury, and of softening your heart? At least, release my gentle sister, and pour out all your malice on me. Purchase wealth by her safety, and satisfy your revenge with a single victim. The loss of both of his daughters might bring the aged man to his grave, and where would then be the satisfaction of Le Renard?\"\n\n\"Listen,\" said the Indian again. \"The light eyes can go back to the Horican, and tell the old chief what has been done, if the dark-haired woman will swear by the Great Spirit of her fathers to tell no lie.\"\n\n\"What must I promise?\" demanded Cora, still maintaining a secret ascendency over the fierce native, by the collected and feminine dignity of her presence.\n\n\"When Magua left his people, his wife was given to another chief; he has now made friends with the Hurons, and will go back to the graves of his tribe, on the shores of the great lake. Let the daughter of the English chief follow, and live in his wigwam forever.\"\n\nHowever revolting a proposal of such a character might prove to Cora, she retained, notwithstanding her powerful disgust, sufficient self-command to reply, without betraying the weakness.\n\n\"And what pleasure would Magua find in sharing his cabin with a wife he did not love; one who would be of a nation and color different from his own? It would be better to take the gold of Munro, and buy the heart of some Huron maid with his gifts.\"\n\nThe Indian made no reply for near a minute, but bent his fierce looks on the countenance of Cora, in such wavering glances, that her eyes sank with shame, under an impression that, for the first time, they had encountered an expression that no chaste female might endure. While she was shrinking within herself, in dread of having her ears wounded by some proposal still more shocking than the last, the voice of Magua answered, in its tones of deepest malignancy--\n\n\"When the blows scorched the back of the Huron, he would know where to find a woman to feel the smart. The daughter of Munro would draw his water, hoe his corn, and cook his venison. The body of the gray-head would sleep among his cannon, but his heart would lie within reach of the knife of Le Subtil.\"\n\n\"Monster! well dost thou deserve thy treacherous name!\" cried Cora, in an ungovernable burst of filial indignation. \"None but a fiend could meditate such a vengeance! But thou overratest thy power! You shall find it is, in truth, the heart of Munro you hold, and that it will defy your utmost malice!\"\n\nThe Indian answered this bold defiance by a ghastly smile, that showed an unaltered purpose, while he motioned her away, as if to close the conference forever. Cora, already regretting her precipitation, was obliged to comply, for Magua instantly left the spot, and approached his gluttonous comrades. Heyward flew to the side of the agitated female, and demanded the result of a dialogue that he had watched at a distance with so much interest. But unwilling to alarm the fears of Alice, she evaded a direct reply, betraying only by her countenance her utter want of success, and keeping her anxious looks fastened on the slightest movements of their captors. To the reiterated and earnest questions of her sister, concerning their probable destination, she made no other answer than by pointing towards the dark group, with an agitation she could not control, and murmuring, as she folded Alice to her bosom--\n\n\"There, there; read our fortunes in their faces; we shall see; we shall see!\"\n\nThe action, and the choked utterance of Cora, spoke more impressively than any words, and quickly drew the attention of her companions on that spot where her own was riveted with an intenseness that nothing but the importance of the stake could create.\n\nWhen Magua reached the cluster of lolling savages, who, gorged with their disgusting meal, lay stretched on the earth in brutal indulgence, he commenced speaking with the dignity of an Indian chief. The first syllables he uttered had the effect to cause his listeners to raise themselves in attitudes of respectful attention. As the Huron used his native language, the prisoners, notwithstanding the caution of the natives had kept them within the swing of their tomahawks, could only conjecture the substance of his harrangue, from the nature of those significant gestures with which an Indian always illustrates his eloquence.\n\nAt first, the language, as well as the action of Magua, appeared calm and deliberate. When he had succeeded in sufficiently awakening the attention of his comrades, Heyward fancied, by his pointing so frequently towards the direction of the great lakes, that he spoke of the land of their fathers, and of their distant tribe. Frequent indications of applause escaped the listeners, who, as they uttered the expressive \"Hugh!\" looked at each other in commendation of the speaker. Le Renard was too skilful to neglect his advantage. He now spoke of the long and painful route by which they had left those spacious grounds and happy villages, to come and battle against the enemies of their Canadian fathers. He enumerated the warriors of the party; their several merits; their frequent services to the nation; their wounds, and the number of the scalps they had taken. Whenever he alluded to any present (and the subtle Indian neglected none), the dark countenance of the flattered individual gleamed with exultation, nor did he even hesitate to assert the truth of the words, by gestures of applause and confirmation. Then the voice of the speaker fell, and lost the loud, animated tones of triumph with which he had enumerated their deeds of success and victory. He described the cataract of Glenn's; the impregnable position of its rocky island, with its caverns, and its numerous rapids and whirlpools; he named the name of La Longue Carabine, and paused until the forest beneath them had sent up the last echo of a loud and long yell, with which the hated appellation was received. He pointed towards the youthful military captive, and described the death of a favorite warrior, who had been precipitated into the deep ravine by his hand. He not only mentioned the fate of him who, hanging between heaven and earth, had presented such a spectacle of horror to the whole band, but he acted anew the terrors of his situation, his resolution and his death, on the branches of a sapling; and, finally, he rapidly recounted the manner in which each of their friends had fallen, never failing to touch upon their courage, and their most acknowledged virtues. When this recital of events was ended, his voice once more changed, and became plaintive, and even musical, in its low guttural sounds. He now spoke of the wives and children of the slain; their destitution; their misery, both physical and moral; their distance; and, at last, of their unavenged wrongs. Then suddenly lifting his voice to a pitch of terrific energy, he concluded, by demanding,--\n\n\"Are the Hurons dogs to bear this? Who shall say to the wife of Menowgua that the fishes have his scalp, and that his nation have not taken revenge! Who will dare meet the mother of Wassawattimie, that scornful woman, with his hands clean! What shall be said to the old men when they ask us for scalps, and we have not a hair from a white head to give them! The women will point their fingers at us. There is a dark spot on the names of the Hurons, and it must be hid in blood!\"\n\nHis voice was no longer audible in the burst of rage which now broke into the air, as if the wood, instead of containing so small a band, was filled with the nation. During the foregoing address the progress of the speaker was too plainly read by those most interested in his success, through the medium of the countenances of the men he addressed. They had answered his melancholy and mourning by sympathy and sorrow; his assertions, by gestures of confirmation; and his boastings, with the exultation of savages. When he spoke of courage, their looks were firm and responsive; when he alluded to their injuries, their eyes kindled with fury; when he mentioned the taunts of the women, they dropped their heads in shame; but when he pointed out their means of vengeance, he struck a chord which never failed to thrill in the breast of an Indian. With the first intimation that it was within their reach, the whole band sprang upon their feet as one man; giving utterance to their rage in the most frantic cries, they rushed upon their prisoners in a body with drawn knives and uplifted tomahawks. Heyward threw himself between the sisters and the foremost, whom he grappled with a desperate strength that for a moment checked his violence. This unexpected resistance gave Magua time to interpose, and with rapid enunciation and animated gesture, he drew the attention of the band again to himself. In that language he knew so well how to assume, he diverted his comrades from their instant purpose, and invited them to prolong the misery of their victims. His proposal was received with acclamations, and executed with the swiftness of thought.\n\nTwo powerful warriors cast themselves on Heyward, while another was occupied in securing the less active singing-master. Neither of the captives, however, submitted without a desperate though fruitless struggle. Even David hurled his assailant to the earth; nor was Heyward secured until the victory over his companion enabled the Indians to direct their united force to that object. He was then bound and fastened to the body of the sapling, on whose branches Magua had acted the pantomime of the falling Huron. When the young soldier regained his recollection, he had the painful certainty before his eyes that a common fate was intended for the whole party. On his right was Cora, in a durance similar to his own, pale and agitated, but with an eye, whose steady look still read the proceedings of their enemies. On his left, the withes which bound her to a pine, performed that office for Alice which her trembling limbs refused, and alone kept her fragile form from sinking. Her hands were clasped before her in prayer, but instead of looking upwards towards that power which alone could rescue them, her unconscious looks wandered to the countenance of Duncan with infantile dependency. David had contended, and the novelty of the circumstance held him silent, in deliberation on the propriety of the unusual occurrence.\n\nThe vengeance of the Hurons had now taken a new direction, and they prepared to execute it with that barbarous ingenuity with which they were familiarized by the practice of centuries. Some sought knots, to raise the blazing pile; one was riving the splinters of pine, in order to pierce the flesh of their captives with the burning fragments; and others bent the tops of two saplings to the earth, in order to suspend Heyward by the arms between the recoiling branches. But the vengeance of Magua sought a deeper and a more malignant enjoyment.\n\nWhile the less refined monsters of the band prepared, before the eyes of those who were to suffer, these well known and vulgar means of torture, he approached Cora, and pointed out, with the most malign expression of countenance, the speedy fate that awaited her:--\n\n\"Ha!\" he added, \"what says the daughter of Munro? Her head is too good to find a pillow in the wigwam of Le Renard; will she like it better when it rolls about this hill a plaything for the wolves? Her bosom cannot nurse the children of a Huron; she will see it spit upon by Indians!\"\n\n\"What means the monster!\" demanded the astonished Heyward.\n\n\"Nothing!\" was the firm reply. \"He is a savage, a barbarous and ignorant savage, and knows not what he does. Let us find leisure, with our dying breath, to ask for him penitence and pardon.\"\n\n\"Pardon!\" echoed the fierce Huron, mistaking, in his anger, the meaning of her words; \"the memory of an Indian is longer than the arm of the pale-faces; his mercy shorter than their justice! Say; shall I send the yellow hair to her father, and will you follow Magua to the great lakes, to carry his water, and feed him with corn?\"\n\nCora beckoned him away, with an emotion of disgust she could not control.\n\n\"Leave me,\" she said, with a solemnity that for a moment checked the barbarity of the Indian; \"you mingle bitterness in my prayers; you stand between me and my God!\"\n\nThe slight impression produced on the savage was, however, soon forgotten, and he continued pointing, with taunting irony, towards Alice.\n\n\"Look! the child weeps! She is young to die! Send her to Munro, to comb his gray hairs, and keep life in the heart of the old man.\"\n\nCora could not resist the desire to look upon her youthful sister, in whose eyes she met an imploring glance, that betrayed the longings of nature.\n\n\"What says he, dearest Cora?\" asked the trembling voice of Alice. \"Did he speak of sending me to our father?\"\n\nFor many moments the elder sister looked upon the younger, with a countenance that wavered with powerful and contending emotions. At length she spoke, though her tones had lost their rich and calm fulness, in an expression of tenderness that seemed maternal.\n\n\"Alice,\" she said, \"the Huron offers us both life, nay, more than both; he offers to restore Duncan, our invaluable Duncan, as well as you, to our friends--to our father--to our heart-stricken, childless father, if I will bow down this rebellious, stubborn pride of mine, and consent--\"\n\nHer voice became choked, and clasping her hands, she looked upward, as if seeking, in her agony, intelligence from a wisdom that was infinite.\n\n\"Say on,\" cried Alice; \"to what, dearest Cora? O, that the proffer were made to me! to save you, to cheer our aged father, to restore Duncan, how cheerfully could I die!\"\n\n\"Die!\" repeated Cora, with a calmer and a firmer voice, \"that were easy! Perhaps the alternative may not be less so. He would have me,\" she continued, her accents sinking under a deep consciousness of the degradation of the proposal, \"follow him to the wilderness; go to the habitations of the Hurons; to remain there: in short to become his wife! Speak, then, Alice; child of my affections! sister of my love! And you, too, Major Heyward, aid my weak reason with your counsel. Is life to be purchased by such a sacrifice? Will you, Alice, receive it at my hands at such a price? And _you_, Duncan, guide me; control me between you; for I am wholly yours.\"\n\n\"Would I!\" echoed the indignant and astonished youth. \"Cora! Cora! you jest with our misery! Name not the horrid alternative again; the thought itself is worse than a thousand deaths.\"\n\n\"That such would be _your_ answer, I well knew!\" exclaimed Cora, her cheeks flushing, and her dark eyes once more sparkling with the lingering emotions of a woman. \"What says my Alice? for her will I submit without another murmur.\"\n\nAlthough both Heyward and Cora listened with painful suspense and the deepest attention, no sounds were heard in reply. It appeared as if the delicate and sensitive form of Alice would shrink into itself, as she listened to this proposal. Her arms had fallen lengthwise before her, the fingers moving in slight convulsions; her head dropped upon her bosom, and her whole person seemed suspended against the tree, looking like some beautiful emblem of the wounded delicacy of her sex, devoid of animation, and yet keenly conscious. In a few moments, however, her head began to move slowly, in a sign of deep, unconquerable disapprobation.\n\n\"No, no, no; better that we die as we have lived, together!\"\n\n\"Then die!\" shouted Magua, hurling his tomahawk with violence at the unresisting speaker, and gnashing his teeth with a rage that could no longer be bridled, at this sudden exhibition of firmness in the one he believed the weakest of the party. The axe cleaved the air in front of Heyward, and cutting some of the flowing ringlets of Alice, quivered in the tree above her head. The sight maddened Duncan to desperation. Collecting all his energies in one effort, he snapped the twigs which bound him and rushed upon another savage who was preparing with loud yells, and a more deliberate aim, to repeat the blow. They encountered, grappled, and fell to the earth together. The naked body of his antagonist afforded Heyward no means of holding his adversary, who glided from his grasp, and rose again with one knee on his chest, pressing him down with the weight of a giant. Duncan already saw the knife gleaming in the air, when a whistling sound swept past him, and was rather accompanied, than followed, by the sharp crack of a rifle. He felt his breast relieved from the load it had endured; he saw the savage expression of his adversary's countenance change to a look of vacant wildness, when the Indian fell dead on the faded leaves by his side.\n\n\"_Clo._--I am gone, sir, And anon, sir, I'll be with you again.\"\n\n_Twelfth Night._\n\nThe Hurons stood aghast at this sudden visitation of death on one of their band. But, as they regarded the fatal accuracy of an aim which had dared to immolate an enemy at so much hazard to a friend, the name of \"La Longue Carabine\" burst simultaneously from every lip, and was succeeded by a wild and a sort of plaintive howl. The cry was answered by a loud shout from a little thicket, where the incautious party had piled their arms; and at the next moment, Hawkeye, too eager to load the rifle he had regained, was seen advancing upon them, brandishing the clubbed weapon, and cutting the air with wide and powerful sweeps. Bold and rapid as was the progress of the scout, it was exceeded by that of a light and vigorous form which, bounding past him, leaped, with incredible activity and daring, into the very centre of the Hurons, where it stood, whirling a tomahawk, and flourishing a glittering knife, with fearful menaces, in front of Cora. Quicker than the thoughts could follow these unexpected and audacious movements, an image, armed in the emblematic panoply of death, glided before their eyes, and assumed a threatening attitude at the other's side. The savage tormentors recoiled before these warlike intruders, and uttered as they appeared in such quick succession, the often repeated and peculiar exclamation of surprise, followed by the well known and dreaded appellations of--\n\n\"Le Cerf Agile! Le Gros Serpent!\"\n\nBut the wary and vigilant leader of the Hurons was not so easily disconcerted. Casting his keen eyes around the little plain, he comprehended the nature of the assault at a glance, and encouraging his followers by his voice as well as by his example, he unsheathed his long and dangerous knife, and rushed with a loud whoop upon the expecting Chingachgook. It was the signal for a general combat. Neither party had fire-arms, and the contest was to be decided in the deadliest manner; hand to hand, with weapons of offence, and none of defence.\n\nUncas answered the whoop, and leaping on an enemy, with a single, well directed blow of his tomahawk, cleft him to the brain. Heyward tore the weapon of Magua from the sapling, and rushed eagerly towards the fray. As the combatants were now equal in number, each singled an opponent from the adverse band. The rush and blows passed with the fury of a whirlwind, and the swiftness of lightning. Hawkeye soon got another enemy within reach of his arm, and with one sweep of his formidable weapon he beat down the slight and inartificial defences of his antagonist, crushing him to the earth with the blow. Heyward ventured to hurl the tomahawk he had seized, too ardent to await the moment of closing. It struck the Indian he had selected on the forehead, and checked for an instant his onward rush. Encouraged by this slight advantage, the impetuous young man continued his onset, and sprang upon his enemy with naked hands. A single instant was enough to assure him of the rashness of the measure, for he immediately found himself fully engaged, with all his activity and courage, in endeavoring to ward the desperate thrusts made with the knife of the Huron. Unable longer to foil an enemy so alert and vigilant, he threw his arms about him, and succeeded in pinning the limbs of the other to his side, with an iron grasp, but one that was far too exhausting to himself to continue long. In this extremity he heard a voice near him, shouting--\n\n\"Extarminate the varlets! no quarter to an accursed Mingo!\"\n\nAt the next moment, the breech of Hawkeye's rifle fell on the naked head of his adversary, whose muscles appeared to wither under the shock, as he sank from the arms of Duncan, flexible and motionless.\n\nWhen Uncas had brained his first antagonist, he turned, like a hungry lion, to seek another. The fifth and only Huron disengaged at the first onset had paused a moment, and then seeing that all around him were employed in the deadly strife, he sought, with hellish vengeance, to complete the baffled work of revenge. Raising a shout of triumph, he sprang towards the defenceless Cora, sending his keen axe, as the dreadful precursor of his approach. The tomahawk grazed her shoulder, and cutting the withes which bound her to the tree, left the maiden at liberty to fly. She eluded the grasp of the savage, and reckless of her own safety, threw herself on the bosom of Alice, striving with convulsed and ill-directed fingers, to tear asunder the twigs which confined the person of her sister. Any other than a monster would have relented at such an act of generous devotion to the best and purest affection; but the breast of the Huron was a stranger to sympathy. Seizing Cora by the rich tresses which fell in confusion about her form, he tore her from her frantic hold, and bowed her down with brutal violence to her knees. The savage drew the flowing curls through his hand, and raising them on high with an outstretched arm, he passed the knife around the exquisitely moulded head of his victim, with a taunting and exulting laugh. But he purchased this moment of fierce gratification with the loss of the fatal opportunity. It was just then the sight caught the eye of Uncas. Bounding from his footsteps he appeared for an instant darting through the air, and descending in a ball he fell on the chest of his enemy, driving him many yards from the spot, headlong and prostrate. The violence of the exertion cast the young Mohican at his side. They arose together, fought, and bled, each in his turn. But the conflict was soon decided; the tomahawk of Heyward and the rifle of Hawkeye descended on the skull of the Huron, at the same moment that the knife of Uncas reached his heart.\n\nThe battle was now entirely terminated, with the exception of the protracted struggle between Le Renard Subtil and Le Gros Serpent. Well did these barbarous warriors prove that they deserved those significant names which had been bestowed for deeds in former wars. When they engaged, some little time was lost in eluding the quick and vigorous thrusts which had been aimed at their lives. Suddenly darting on each other, they closed, and came to the earth, twisted together like twining serpents, in pliant and subtle folds. At the moment when the victors found themselves unoccupied, the spot where these experienced and desperate combatants lay, could only be distinguished by a cloud of dust and leaves which moved from the centre of the little plain towards its boundary, as if raised by the passage of a whirlwind. Urged by the different motives of filial affection, friendship, and gratitude, Heyward and his companions rushed with one accord to the place, encircling the little canopy of dust which hung above the warriors. In vain did Uncas dart around the cloud, with a wish to strike his knife into the heart of his father's foe; the threatening rifle of Hawkeye was raised and suspended in vain, while Duncan endeavored to seize the limbs of the Huron with hands that appeared to have lost their power. Covered, as they were, with dust and blood, the swift evolutions of the combatants seemed to incorporate their bodies into one. The death-like looking figure of the Mohican, and the dark form of the Huron, gleamed before their eyes in such quick and confused succession, that the friends of the former knew not where nor when to plant the succoring blow. It is true there were short and fleeting moments, when the fiery eyes of Magua were seen glittering, like the fabled organs of the basilisk, through the dusty wreath by which he was enveloped, and he read by those short and deadly glances the fate of the combat in the presence of his enemies; ere, however, any hostile hand could descend on his devoted head, its place was filled by the scowling visage of Chingachgook. In this manner the scene of the combat was removed from the centre of the little plain to its verge. The Mohican now found an opportunity to make a powerful thrust with his knife; Magua suddenly relinquished his grasp, and fell backward without motion, and seemingly without life. His adversary leaped on his feet, making the arches of the forest ring with the sounds of triumph.\n\n\"Well done for the Delawares! victory to the Mohican!\" cried Hawkeye, once more elevating the butt of the long and fatal rifle; \"a finishing blow from a man without a cross will never tell against his honor, nor rob him of his right to the scalp.\"\n\nBut, at the very moment when the dangerous weapon was in the act of descending, the subtle Huron rolled swiftly from beneath the danger, over the edge of the precipice, and falling on his feet, was seen leaping, with a single bound, into the centre of a thicket of low bushes, which clung along its sides. The Delawares, who had believed their enemy dead, uttered their exclamation of surprise, and were following with speed and clamor, like hounds in open view of the deer, when a shrill and peculiar cry from the scout instantly changed their purpose, and recalled them to the summit of the hill.\n\n\"'Twas like himself,\" cried the inveterate forester, whose prejudices contributed so largely to veil his natural sense of justice in all matters which concerned the Mingos; \"a lying and deceitful varlet as he is. An honest Delaware now, being fairly vanquished, would have lain still, and been knocked on the head, but these knavish Maquas cling to life like so many cats-o'-the-mountain. Let him go--let him go; 'tis but one man, and he without rifle or bow, many a long mile from his French commerades; and, like a rattler that has lost his fangs, he can do no further mischief, until such time as he, and we too, may leave the prints of our moccasins over a long reach of sandy plain. See, Uncas,\" he added, in Delaware, \"your father is flaying the scalps already. It may be well to go round and feel the vagabonds that are left, or we may have another of them loping through the woods, and screeching like a jay that has been winged.\"\n\n[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n_The battle was now entirely terminated, with the exception of the protracted struggle between Le Renard Subtil and Le Gros Serpent_]\n\nSo saying, the honest, but implacable scout, made the circuit of the dead, into whose senseless bosoms he thrust his long knife, with as much coolness as though they had been so many brute carcasses. He had, however, been anticipated by the elder Mohican, who had already torn the emblems of victory from the unresisting heads of the slain.\nBut Uncas, denying his habits, we had almost said his nature, flew with instinctive delicacy, accompanied by Heyward, to the assistance of the females, and quickly releasing Alice, placed her in the arms of Cora. We shall not attempt to describe the gratitude to the Almighty Disposer of events which glowed in the bosoms of the sisters, who were thus unexpectedly restored to life and to each other. Their thanksgivings were deep and silent; the offerings of their gentle spirits, burning brightest and purest on the secret altars of their hearts; and their renovated and more earthly feelings exhibiting themselves in long and fervent, though speechless caresses. As Alice rose from her knees, where she had sunk by the side of Cora, she threw herself on the bosom of the latter; and sobbed aloud the name of their aged father, while her soft, dove-like eyes sparkled with the rays of hope.\n\n\"We are saved! we are saved!\" she murmured; \"to return to the arms of our dear, dear father, and his heart will not be broken with grief. And you too, Cora, my sister; my more than sister, my mother; you too are spared. And Duncan,\" she added, looking round upon the youth with a smile of ineffable innocence, \"even our own brave and noble Duncan has escaped without a hurt.\"\n\nTo these ardent and nearly incoherent words Cora made no other answer than by straining the youthful speaker to her heart, as she bent over her, in melting tenderness. The manhood of Heyward felt no shame in dropping tears over this spectacle of affectionate rapture; and Uncas stood, fresh and blood-stained from the combat, a calm, and, apparently, an unmoved looker-on, it is true, but with eyes that had already lost their fierceness, and were beaming with a sympathy that elevated him far above the intelligence, and advanced him probably centuries before the practices of his nation.\n\nDuring this display of emotions so natural in their situation, Hawkeye, whose vigilant distrust had satisfied itself that the Hurons, who disfigured the heavenly scene, no longer possessed the power to interrupt its harmony, approached David, and liberated him from the bonds he had, until that moment, endured with the most exemplary patience.\n\n\"There,\" exclaimed the scout, casting the last withe behind him, \"you are once more master of your own limbs, though you seem not to use them with greater judgment than that in which they were first fashioned. If advice from one who is not older than yourself, but who having lived most of his time in the wilderness, may be said to have experience beyond his years, will give no offence, you are welcome to my thoughts; and these are, to part with the little tooting instrument in your jacket to the first fool you meet with, and buy some useful we'pon with the money, if it be only the barrel of a horseman's pistol. By industry and care, you might thus come to some prefarment; for by this time, I should think, your eyes would plainly tell you that a carrion crow is a better bird than a mocking thresher. The one will, at least, remove foul sights from before the face of man, while the other is only good to brew disturbances in the woods, by cheating the ears of all that hear them.\"\n\n\"Arms and the clarion for the battle, but the song of thanksgiving to the victory!\" answered the liberated David. \"Friend,\" he added, thrusting forth his lean, delicate hand towards Hawkeye, in kindness, while his eyes twinkled and grew moist, \"I thank thee that the hairs of my head still grow where they were first rooted by Providence; for, though those of other men may be more glossy and curling, I have ever found mine own well suited to the brain they shelter. That I did not join myself to the battle, was less owing to disinclination, than to the bonds of the heathen. Valiant and skilful hast thou proved thyself in the conflict, and I hereby thank thee, before proceeding to discharge other and more important duties, because thou hast proved thyself well worthy of a Christian's praise.\"\n\n\"The thing is but a trifle, and what you may often see, if you tarry long among us,\" returned the scout, a good deal softened towards the man of song, by this unequivocal expression of gratitude. \"I have got back my old companion, 'Killdeer,'\" he added, striking his hand on the breech of his rifle; \"and that in itself is a victory. These Iroquois are cunning, but they outwitted themselves when they placed their fire-arms out of reach; and had Uncas or his father been gifted with only their common Indian patience, we should have come in upon the knaves with three bullets instead of one, and that would have made a finish of the whole pack; yon loping varlet, as well as his commerades. But 'twas all foreordered, and for the best.\"\n\n\"Thou sayest well,\" returned David, \"and hast caught the true spirit of Christianity. He that is to be saved will be saved, and he that is predestined to be damned will be damned. This is the doctrine of truth, and most consoling and refreshing it is to the true believer.\"\n\nThe scout, who by this time was seated, examining into the state of his rifle with a species of parental assiduity, now looked up at the other in a displeasure that he did not affect to conceal, roughly interrupting further speech.\n\n\"Doctrine, or no doctrine,\" said the sturdy woodsman, \"'tis the belief of knaves, and the curse of an honest man. I can credit that yonder Huron was to fall by my hand, for with my own eyes I have seen it; but nothing short of being a witness will cause me to think he had met with any reward, or that Chingachgook, there, will be condemned at the final day.\"\n\n\"You have no warranty for such an audacious doctrine, nor any covenant to support it,\" cried David, who was deeply tinctured with the subtle distinctions which, in his time, and more especially in his province, had been drawn around the beautiful simplicity of revelation, by endeavoring to penetrate the awful mystery of the divine nature, supplying faith by self-sufficiency, and by consequence, involving those who reasoned from such human dogmas in absurdities and doubt; \"your temple is reared on the sands, and the first tempest will wash away its foundation. I demand your authorities for such an uncharitable assertion (like other advocates of a system, David was not always accurate in his use of terms). Name chapter and verse; in which of the holy books do you find language to support you?\"\n\n\"Book!\" repeated Hawkeye, with singular and ill-concealed disdain; \"do you take me for a whimpering boy at the apron-string of one of your old gals; and this good rifle on my knee for the feather of a goose's wing, my ox's horn for a bottle of ink, and my leathern pouch for a cross-barred handkercher to carry my dinner? Book! what have such as I, who am a warrior of the wilderness, though a man without a cross, to do with books? I never read but in one, and the words that are written there are too simple and too plain to need much schooling; though I may boast that of forty long and hard-working years.\"\n\n\"What call you the volume?\" said David, misconceiving the other's meaning.\n\n\"Tis open before your eyes,\" returned the scout; \"and he who owns it is not a niggard of its use. I have heard it said that there are men who read in books to convince themselves there is a God. I know not but man may so deform his works in the settlement, as to leave that which is so clear in the wilderness a matter of doubt among traders and priests. If any such there be, and he will follow me from sun to sun, through the windings of the forest, he shall see enough to teach him that he is a fool, and that the greatest of his folly lies in striving to rise to the level of One he can never equal, be it in goodness, or be it in power.\"\n\nThe instant David discovered that he battled with a disputant who imbibed his faith from the lights of nature, eschewing all subtleties of doctrine, he willingly abandoned a controversy from which he believed neither profit nor credit was to be derived. While the scout was speaking, he had also seated himself, and producing the ready little volume and the iron-rimmed spectacles, he prepared to discharge a duty, which nothing but the unexpected assault he had received in his orthodoxy could have so long suspended. He was, in truth, a minstrel of the western continent--of a much later day, certainly, than those gifted bards, who formerly sang the profane renown of baron and prince, but after the spirit of his own age and country; and he was now prepared to exercise the cunning of his craft, in celebration of, or rather in thanksgiving for, the recent victory. He waited patiently for Hawkeye to cease, then lifting his eyes, together with his voice, he said, aloud,--\n\n\"I invite you, friends, to join in praise for this signal deliverance from the hands of barbarians and infidels, to the comfortable and solemn tones of the tune, called 'Northampton.'\"\n\nHe next named the page and verse where the rhymes selected were to be found, and applied the pitch-pipe to his lips, with the decent gravity that he had been wont to use in the temple. This time he was, however, without any accompaniment, for the sisters were just then pouring out those tender effusions of affection which have been already alluded to. Nothing deterred by the smallness of his audience, which, in truth, consisted only of the discontented scout, he raised his voice, commencing and ending the sacred song without accident or interruption of any kind.\n\nHawkeye listened, while he coolly adjusted his flint and reloaded his rifle; but the sounds, wanting the extraneous assistance of scene and sympathy, failed to awaken his slumbering emotions. Never minstrel, or by whatever more suitable name David should be known, drew upon his talents in the presence of more insensible auditors; though considering the singleness and sincerity of his motive, it is probable that no bard of profane song ever uttered notes that ascended so near to that throne where all homage and praise is due. The scout shook his head, and muttering some unintelligible words, among which \"throat\" and \"Iroquois,\" were alone audible, he walked away, to collect, and to examine into, the state of the captured arsenal of the Hurons. In this office he was now joined by Chingachgook, who found his own, as well as the rifle of his son, among the arms. Even Heyward and David were furnished with weapons; nor was ammunition wanting to render them all effectual.\n\nWhen the foresters had made their selection, and distributed their prizes, the scout announced that the hour had arrived when it was necessary to move. By this time the song of Gamut had ceased, and the sisters had learned to still the exhibition of their emotions. Aided by Duncan and the younger Mohican, the two latter descended the precipitous sides of that hill which they had so lately ascended under so very different auspices, and whose summit had so nearly proved the scene of their massacre. At the foot, they found the Narragansetts browsing the herbage of the bushes; and having mounted, they followed the movements of a guide, who, in the most deadly straits, had so often proved himself their friend. The journey was, however, short. Hawkeye, leaving the blind path that the Hurons had followed, turned short to his right, and entering the thicket, he crossed a babbling brook, and halted in a narrow dell, under the shade of a few water elms. Their distance from the base of the fatal hill was but a few rods, and the steeds had been serviceable only in crossing the shallow stream.\n\nThe scout and the Indians appeared to be familiar with the sequestered place where they now were; for, leaning their rifles against the trees, they commenced throwing aside the dried leaves, and opening the blue clay, out of which a clear and sparkling spring of bright, glancing water, quickly bubbled. The white man then looked about him, as though seeking for some object, which was not to be found as readily as he expected:--\n\n\"Them careless imps, the Mohawks, with their Tuscarora and Onondaga brethren, have been here slaking their thirst,\" he muttered, \"and the vagabonds have thrown away the gourd! This is the way with benefits, when they are bestowed on such disremembering hounds! Here has the Lord laid his hand, in the midst of the howling wilderness, for their good, and raised a fountain of water from the bowels of the 'arth, that might laugh at the richest shop of apothecary's ware in all the colonies; and see! the knaves have trodden in the clay, and deformed the cleanliness of the place, as though they were brute beasts, instead of human men.\"\n\nUncas silently extended towards him the desired gourd, which the spleen of Hawkeye had hitherto prevented him from observing, on a branch of an elm. Filling it with water, he retired a short distance, to a place where the ground was more firm and dry; here he coolly seated himself, and after taking a long, and, apparently, a grateful draught, he commenced a very strict examination of the fragments of food left by the Hurons, which had hung in a wallet on his arm.\n\n\"Thank you, lad!\" he continued, returning the empty gourd to Uncas; \"now we will see how these rampaging Hurons lived, when outlying in ambushments. Look at this! The varlets know the better pieces of the deer; and one would think they might carve and roast a saddle, equal to the best cook in the land! But everything is raw, for the Iroquois are thorough savages. Uncas, take my steel, and kindle a fire; a mouthful of a tender broil will give natur' a helping hand, after so long a trail.\"\n\nHeyward, perceiving that their guides now set about their repast in sober earnest, assisted the ladies to alight, and placed himself at their side, not unwilling to enjoy a few moments of grateful rest, after the bloody scene he had just gone through. While the culinary process was in hand, curiosity induced him to inquire into the circumstances which had led to their timely and unexpected rescue:--\n\n\"How is it that we see you so soon, my generous friend,\" he asked, \"and without aid from the garrison of Edward?\"\n\n\"Had we gone to the bend in the river, we might have been in time to rake the leaves over your bodies, but too late to have saved your scalps,\" coolly answered the scout. \"No, no; instead of throwing away strength and opportunity by crossing to the fort, we lay by, under the bank of the Hudson, waiting to watch the movements of the Hurons.\"\n\n\"You were, then, witnesses of all that passed?\"\n\n\"Not of all; for Indian sight is too keen to be easily cheated, and we kept close. A difficult matter it was, too, to keep this Mohican boy snug in the ambushment. Ah! Uncas, Uncas, your behavior was more like that of a curious woman than of a warrior on his scent.\"\n\nUncas permitted his eyes to turn for an instant on the sturdy countenance of the speaker, but he neither spoke nor gave any indication of repentance. On the contrary, Heyward thought the manner of the young Mohican was disdainful, if not a little fierce, and that he suppressed passions that were ready to explode, as much in compliment to the listeners, as from the deference he usually paid to his white associate.\n\n\"You saw our capture?\" Heyward next demanded.\n\n\"We heard it,\" was the significant answer. \"An Indian yell is plain language to men who have passed their days in the woods. But when you landed, we were driven to crawl, like sarpents, beneath the leaves; and then we lost sight of you entirely, until we placed eyes on you again, trussed to the trees, and ready bound for an Indian massacre.\"\n\n\"Our rescue was the deed of Providence. It was nearly a miracle that you did not mistake the path, for the Hurons divided, and each band had its horses.\"\n\n\"Ay! there we were thrown off the scent, and might, indeed, have lost the trail, had it not been for Uncas; we took the path, however, that led into the wilderness; for we judged, and judged rightly, that the savages would hold that course with their prisoners. But when we had followed it for many miles, without finding a single twig broken, as I had advised, my mind misgave me; especially as all the footsteps had the prints of moccasins.\"\n\n\"Our captors had the precaution to see us shod like themselves,\" said Duncan, raising a foot, and exhibiting the buckskin he wore.\n\n\"Ay, 'twas judgmatical, and like themselves; though we were too expart to be thrown from a trail by so common an invention.\"\n\n\"To what, then, are we indebted for our safety?\"\n\n\"To what, as a white man who has no taint of Indian blood, I should be ashamed to own; to the judgment of the young Mohican, in matters which I should know better than he, but which I can now hardly believe to be true, though my own eyes tell me it is so.\"\n\n\"'Tis extraordinary! will you not name the reason?\"\n\n\"Uncas was bold enough to say, that the beasts ridden by the gentle ones,\" continued Hawkeye, glancing his eyes, not without curious interest, on the fillies of the ladies, \"planted the legs of one side on the ground at the same time, which is contrary to the movements of all trotting four-footed animals of my knowledge, except the bear. And yet here are horses that always journey in this manner, as my own eyes have seen, and as their trail has shown for twenty long miles.\"\n\n\"'Tis the merit of the animal! They come from the shores of Narragansett Bay, in the small province of Providence Plantations, and are celebrated for their hardihood, and the ease of this peculiar movement; though other horses are not unfrequently trained to the same.\"\n\n\"It may be--it may be,\" said Hawkeye, who had listened with singular attention to this explanation; \"though I am a man who has the full blood of the whites, my judgment in deer and beaver is greater than in beasts of burden. Major Effingham has many noble chargers, but I have never seen one travel after such a sideling gait.\"\n\n\"True; for he would value the animals for very different properties. Still is this a breed highly esteemed, and as you witness, much honored with the burdens it is often destined to bear.\"\n\nThe Mohicans had suspended their operations about the glimmering fire, to listen; and when Duncan had done, they looked at each other significantly, the father uttering the never-failing exclamation of surprise. The scout ruminated, like a man digesting his newly acquired knowledge, and once more stole a curious glance at the horses.\n\n\"I dare to say there are even stranger sights to be seen in the settlements!\" he said, at length; \"natur' is sadly abused by man, when he once gets the mastery. But, go sideling or go straight, Uncas had seen the movement, and their trail led us on to the broken bush. The outer branch, near the prints of one of the horses, was bent upward, as a lady breaks a flower from its stem, but all the rest were ragged and broken down, as if the strong hand of a man had been tearing them! So I concluded that the cunning varmints had seen the twig bent, and had torn the rest, to make us believe a buck had been feeling the boughs with his antlers.\"\n\n\"I do believe your sagacity did not deceive you; for some such thing occurred!\"\n\n\"That was easy to see,\" added the scout, in no degree conscious of having exhibited any extraordinary sagacity; \"and a very different matter it was from a waddling horse! It then struck me the Mingos would push for this spring, for the knaves well know the vartue of its waters!\"\n\n\"Is it, then, so famous?\" demanded Heyward, examining, with a more curious eye, the secluded dell, with its bubbling fountain, surrounded, as it was, by earth of a deep dingy brown.\n\n\"Few redskins, who travel south and east of the great lakes, but have heard of its qualities. Will you taste for yourself?\"\n\nHeyward took the gourd, and after swallowing a little of the water, threw it aside with grimaces of discontent. The scout laughed in his silent, but heartfelt manner, and shook his head with vast satisfaction.\n\n\"Ah! you want the flavor that one gets by habit; the time was when I liked it as little as yourself; but I have come to my taste, and I now crave it, as a deer does the licks.[17] Your high spiced wines are not better liked than a redskin relishes this water; especially when his natur' is ailing. But Uncas has made his fire, and it is time we think of eating, for our journey is long, and all before us.\"\n\nInterrupting the dialogue by this abrupt transition, the scout had instant recourse to the fragments of food which had escaped the voracity of the Hurons. A very summary process completed the simple cookery, when he and the Mohicans commenced their humble meal, with the silence and characteristic diligence of men who ate in order to enable themselves to endure great and unremitting toil.\n\nWhen this necessary, and, happily, grateful duty had been performed, each of the foresters stooped and took a long and parting draught at that solitary and silent spring,[18] around which and its sister fountains, within fifty years, the wealth, beauty, and talents of a hemisphere were to assemble in throngs, in pursuit of health and pleasure. Then Hawkeye announced his determination to proceed. The sisters resumed their saddles; Duncan and David grasped their rifles, and followed on their footsteps; the scout leading the advance, and the Mohicans bringing up the rear. The whole party moved swiftly through the narrow path, towards the north, leaving the healing waters to mingle unheeded with the adjacent brook, and the bodies of the dead to fester on the neighboring mount, without the rites of sepulture; a fate but too common to the warriors of the woods to excite either commiseration or comment.\n\n\"I'll seek a readier path.\"\n\nThe route taken by Hawkeye lay across those sandy plains, relieved by occasional valleys and swells of land, which had been traversed by their party on the morning of the same day, with the baffled Magua for their guide. The sun had now fallen low towards the distant mountains; and as their journey lay through the interminable forest, the heat was no longer oppressive. Their progress, in consequence, was proportionate; and long before the twilight gathered about them, they had made good many toilsome miles on their return.\n\nThe hunter, like the savage whose place he filled, seemed to select among the blind signs of their wild route, with a species of instinct, seldom abating his speed, and never pausing to deliberate. A rapid and oblique glance at the moss on the trees, with an occasional upward gaze towards the setting sun, or a steady but passing look at the direction of the numerous water-courses, through which he waded, were sufficient to determine his path, and remove his greatest difficulties. In the meantime, the forest began to change its hues, losing that lively green which had embellished its arches, in the graver light which is the usual precursor of the close of day.\n\nWhile the eyes of the sisters were endeavoring to catch glimpses through the trees, of the flood of golden glory which formed a glittering halo around the sun, tinging here and there with ruby streaks, or bordering with narrow edgings of shining yellow, a mass of clouds that lay piled at no great distance above the western hills, Hawkeye turned suddenly, and, pointing upwards towards the gorgeous heavens, he spoke:--\n\n\"Yonder is the signal given to a man to seek his food and natural rest,\" he said: \"better and wiser would it be, if he could understand the signs of nature, and take a lesson from the fowls of the air and the beasts of the fields! Our night, however, will soon be over; for, with the moon, we must be up and moving again. I remember to have fou't the Maquas, hereaways, in the first war in which I ever drew blood from man; and we threw up a work of blocks, to keep the ravenous varmints from handling our scalps. If my marks do not fail me, we shall find the place a few rods farther to our left.\"\n\nWithout waiting for an assent, or, indeed, for any reply, the sturdy hunter moved boldly into a dense thicket of young chestnuts, shoving aside the branches of the exuberant shoots which nearly covered the ground, like a man who expected, at each step, to discover some object he had formerly known. The recollection of the scout did not deceive him. After penetrating through the brush, matted as it was with briers, for a few hundred feet he entered an open space, that surrounded a low, green hillock, which was crowned by the decayed block-house in question. This rude and neglected building was one of those deserted works, which, having been thrown up on an emergency, had been abandoned with the disappearance of danger, and was now quietly crumbling in the solitude of the forest, neglected, and nearly forgotten, like the circumstances which had caused it to be reared. Such memorials of the passage and struggles of man are yet frequent throughout the broad barrier of wilderness which once separated the hostile provinces, and form a species of ruins that are intimately associated with the recollections of colonial history, and which are in appropriate keeping with the gloomy character of the surrounding scenery.[19] The roof of bark had long since fallen, and mingled with the soil; but the huge logs of pine, which had been hastily thrown together, still preserved their relative positions, though one angle of the work had given way under the pressure, and threatened a speedy downfall to the remainder of the rustic edifice. While Heyward and his companions hesitated to approach a building so decayed, Hawkeye and the Indians entered within the low walls, not only without fear, but with obvious interest. While the former surveyed the ruins, both internally and externally, with the curiosity of one whose recollections were reviving at each moment, Chingachgook related to his son, in the language of the Delawares, and with the pride of a conqueror, the brief history of the skirmish which had been fought, in his youth, in that secluded spot. A strain of melancholy, however, blended with his triumph, rendering his voice, as usual, soft and musical.\n\nIn the meantime, the sisters gladly dismounted, and prepared to enjoy their halt in the coolness of the evening, and in a security which they believed nothing but the beasts of the forest could invade.\n\n\"Would not our resting-place have been more retired, my worthy friend,\" demanded the more vigilant Duncan, perceiving that the scout had already finished his short survey, \"had we chosen a spot less known, and one more rarely visited than this?\"\n\n\"Few live who know the block-house was ever raised,\" was the slow and musing answer; \"'tis not often that books are made, and narratives written, of such a scrimmage as was here fou't atween the Mohicans and the Mohawks, in a war of their own waging. I was then a younker, and went out with the Delawares, because I know'd they were a scandalized and wronged race. Forty days and forty nights did the imps crave our blood around this pile of logs, which I designed and partly reared, being, as you'll remember, no Indian myself, but a man without a cross. The Delawares lent themselves to the work, and we made it good, ten to twenty, until our numbers were nearly equal, and then we sallied out upon the hounds, and not a man of them ever got back to tell the fate of his party. Yes, yes; I was then young, and new to the sight of blood; and not relishing the thought that creatures who had spirits like myself should lay on the naked ground, to be torn asunder by beasts, or to bleach in the rains, I buried the dead with my own hands, under that very little hillock where you have placed yourselves; and no bad seat does it make neither, though it be raised by the bones of mortal men.\"\n\nHeyward and the sisters arose, on the instant, from the grassy sepulchre; nor could the two latter, notwithstanding the terrific scenes they had so recently passed through, entirely suppress an emotion of natural horror, when they found themselves in such familiar contact with the grave of the dead Mohawks. The gray light, the gloomy little area of dark grass, surrounded by its border of brush, beyond which the pines rose, in breathing silence, apparently, into the very clouds, and the death-like stillness of the vast forest, were all in unison to deepen such a sensation.\n\n\"They are gone, and they are harmless,\" continued Hawkeye, waving his hand, with a melancholy smile, at their manifest alarm: \"they'll never shout the war-whoop nor strike a blow with the tomahawk again! And of all those who aided in placing them where they lie, Chingachgook and I only are living! The brothers and family of the Mohican formed our war-party; and you see before you all that are now left of his race.\"\n\nThe eyes of the listeners involuntarily sought the forms of the Indians, with a compassionate interest in their desolate fortune. The dark persons were still to be seen within the shadows of the block-house, the son listening to the relation of his father with that sort of intenseness which would be created by a narrative that redounded so much to the honor of those whose names he had long revered for their courage and savage virtues.\n\n\"I had thought the Delawares a pacific people,\" said Duncan, \"and that they never waged war in person; trusting the defence of their lands to those very Mohawks that you slew!\"\n\n\"'Tis true in part,\" returned the scout, \"and yet, at the bottom, 'tis a wicked lie. Such a treaty was made in ages gone by, through the deviltries of the Dutchers, who wished to disarm the natives that had the best right to the country where they had settled themselves. The Mohicans, though a part of the same nation, having to deal with the English, never entered into the silly bargain, but kept to their manhood; as in truth did the Delawares, when their eyes were opened to their folly. You see before you a chief of the great Mohican Sagamores! Once his family could chase their deer over tracts of country wider than that which belongs to the Albany Patteroon, without crossing brook or hill that was not their own; but what is left to their descendant! He may find his six feet of earth when God chooses, and keep it in peace, perhaps, if he has a friend who will take the pains to sink his head so low that the ploughshares cannot reach it!\"\n\n\"Enough!\" said Heyward, apprehensive that the subject might lead to a discussion that would interrupt the harmony so necessary to the preservation of his fair companions: \"we have journeyed far, and few among us are blessed with forms like that of yours, which seems to know neither fatigue nor weakness.\"\n\n\"The sinews and bones of a man carry me through it all,\" said the hunter, surveying his muscular limbs with a simplicity that betrayed the honest pleasure the compliment afforded him: \"there are larger and heavier men to be found in the settlements, but you might travel many days in a city before you could meet one able to walk fifty miles without stopping to take breath, or who has kept the hounds within hearing during a chase of hours. However, as flesh and blood are not always the same, it is quite reasonable to suppose that the gentle ones are willing to rest, after all they have seen and done this day. Uncas, clear out the spring, while your father and I make a cover for their tender heads of these chestnut shoots, and a bed of grass and leaves.\"\n\nThe dialogue ceased, while the hunter and his companions busied themselves in preparations for the comfort and protection of those they guided. A spring, which many long years before had induced the natives to select the place for their temporary fortification, was soon cleared of leaves, and a fountain of crystal gushed from the bed, diffusing its waters over the verdant hillock. A corner of the building was then roofed in such a manner as to exclude the heavy dew of the climate, and piles of sweet shrubs and dried leaves were laid beneath it for the sisters to repose on.\n\nWhile the diligent woodsmen were employed in this manner, Cora and Alice partook of that refreshment which duty required much more than inclination prompted them to accept. They then retired within the walls, and first offering up their thanksgivings for past mercies, and petitioning for a continuance of the divine favor throughout the coming night, they laid their tender forms on the fragrant couch, and in spite of recollections and forebodings, soon sank into those slumbers which nature so imperiously demanded, and which were sweetened by hopes for the morrow. Duncan had prepared himself to pass the night in watchfulness near them, just without the ruin, but the scout, perceiving his intention, pointed towards Chingachgook, as he coolly disposed his own person on the grass, and said--\n\n\"The eyes of a white man are too heavy and too blind for such a watch as this! The Mohican will be our sentinel, therefore let us sleep.\"\n\n\"I proved myself a sluggard on my post during the past night,\" said Heyward, \"and have less need of repose than you, who did more credit to the character of a soldier. Let all the party seek their rest, then, while I hold guard.\"\n\n\"If we lay among the white tents of the 60th, and in front of an enemy like the French, I could not ask for a better watchman,\" returned the scout; \"but in the darkness and among the signs of the wilderness your judgment would be like the folly of a child, and your vigilance thrown away. Do then, like Uncas and myself, sleep, and sleep in safety.\"\n\nHeyward perceived, in truth, that the younger Indian had thrown his form on the side of the hillock while they were talking, like one who sought to make the most of the time allotted to rest, and that his example had been followed by David, whose voice literally \"clove to his jaws,\" with the fever of his wound, heightened, as it was, by their toilsome march. Unwilling to prolong a useless discussion, the young man affected to comply, by posting his back against the logs of the block-house, in a half-recumbent posture, though resolutely determined, in his own mind, not to close an eye until he had delivered his precious charge into the arms of Munro himself. Hawkeye, believing he had prevailed, soon fell asleep, and a silence as deep as the solitude in which they had found it, pervaded the retired spot.\n\nFor many minutes Duncan succeeded in keeping his senses on the alert, and alive to every moaning sound that arose from the forest. His vision became more acute as the shades of evening settled on the place; and even after the stars were glimmering above his head, he was able to distinguish the recumbent forms of his companions, as they lay stretched on the grass, and to note the person of Chingachgook, who sat upright and motionless as one of the trees which formed the dark barrier on every side. He still heard the gentle breathings of the sisters, who lay within a few feet of him, and not a leaf was ruffled by the passing air, of which his ear did not detect the whispering sound. At length, however, the mournful notes of a whippoorwill became blended with the moanings of an owl; his heavy eyes occasionally sought the bright rays of the stars, and then he fancied he saw them through the fallen lids. At instants of momentary wakefulness he mistook a bush for his associate sentinel; his head next sank upon his shoulder, which, in its turn, sought the support of the ground; and, finally, his whole person become relaxed and pliant, and the young man sank into a deep sleep, dreaming that he was a knight of ancient chivalry, holding his midnight vigils before the tent of a recaptured princess, whose favor he did not despair of gaining, by such a proof of devotion and watchfulness.\n\nHow long the tired Duncan lay in this insensible state he never knew himself, but his slumbering visions had been long lost in total forgetfulness, when he was awakened by a light tap on the shoulder. Aroused by this signal, slight as it was, he sprang upon his feet with a confused recollection of the self-imposed duty he had assumed with the commencement of the night.\n\n\"Who comes?\" he demanded, feeling for his sword at the place where it was usually suspended, \"Speak! friend or enemy?\"\n\n\"Friend,\" replied the low voice of Chingachgook; who, pointing upwards at the luminary which was shedding its mild light through the opening in the trees, directly in their bivouac, immediately added, in his rude English, \"moon comes, and white man's fort far--far off; time to move, when sleep shuts both eyes of the Frenchman!\"\n\n\"You say true! call up your friends, and bridle the horses, while I prepare my own companions for the march!\"\n\n\"We are awake, Duncan,\" said the soft, silvery tones of Alice within the building, \"and ready to travel very fast after so refreshing a sleep; but you have watched through the tedious night in our behalf, after having endured so much fatigue the live-long day!\"\n\n\"Say, rather, I would have watched, but my treacherous eyes betrayed me; twice have I proved myself unfit for the trust I bear.\"\n\n\"Nay, Duncan, deny it not,\" interrupted the smiling Alice, issuing from the shadows of the building into the light of the moon, in all the loveliness of her freshened beauty; \"I know you to be a heedless one, when self is the object of your care, and but too vigilant in favor of others. Can we not tarry here a little longer, while you find the rest you need? Cheerfully, most cheerfully, will Cora and I keep the vigils, while you, and all these brave men, endeavor to snatch a little sleep!\"\n\n\"If shame could cure me of my drowsiness, I should never close an eye again,\" said the uneasy youth, gazing at the ingenuous countenance of Alice, where, however, in its sweet solicitude, he read nothing to confirm his half awakened suspicion. \"It is but too true, that after leading you into danger by my heedlessness, I have not even the merit of guarding your pillows as should become a soldier.\"\n\n\"No one but Duncan himself should accuse Duncan of such a weakness. Go, then, and sleep; believe me, neither of us, weak girls as we are, will betray our watch.\"\n\nThe young man was relieved from the awkwardness of making any further protestations of his own demerits, by an exclamation from Chingachgook, and the attitude of riveted attention assumed by his son.\n\n\"The Mohicans hear an enemy!\" whispered Hawkeye, who, by this time, in common with the whole party, was awake and stirring. \"They scent danger in the wind!\"\n\n\"God forbid!\" exclaimed Heyward. \"Surely we have had enough of bloodshed!\"\n\nWhile he spoke, however, the young soldier seized his rifle, and advancing towards the front, prepared to atone for his venial remissness, by freely exposing his life in defence of those he attended.\n\n\"'Tis some creature of the forest prowling around us in quest of food,\" he said, in a whisper, as soon as the low, and apparently distant sounds, which had startled the Mohicans, reached his own ears.\n\n\"Hist!\" returned the attentive scout; \"'tis man; even I can now tell his tread, poor as my senses are when compared to an Indian's! That scampering Huron has fallen in with one of Montcalm's outlying parties, and they have struck upon our trail. I shouldn't like, myself, to spill more human blood in this spot,\" he added, looking around with anxiety in his features, at the dim objects by which he was surrounded; \"but what must be, must! Lead the horses into the block-house, Uncas; and, friends, do you follow to the same shelter. Poor and old as it is, it offers a cover, and has rung with the crack of a rifle afore to-night!\"\n\nHe was instantly obeyed, the Mohicans leading the Narragansetts within the ruin, whither the whole party repaired with the most guarded silence.\n\nThe sounds of approaching footsteps were now too distinctly audible to leave any doubts as to the nature of the interruption. They were soon mingled with voices calling to each other in an Indian dialect, which the hunter, in a whisper, affirmed to Heyward was the language of the Hurons. When the party reached the point where the horses had entered the thicket which surrounded the block-house, they were evidently at fault, having lost those marks which, until that moment, had directed their pursuit.\n\nIt would seem by the voices that twenty men were soon collected at that one spot, mingling their different opinions and advice in noisy clamor.\n\n\"The knaves know our weakness,\" whispered Hawkeye, who stood by the side of Heyward, in deep shade, looking through an opening in the logs, \"or they wouldn't indulge their idleness in such a squaw's march. Listen to the reptiles! each man among them seems to have two tongues, and but a single leg.\"\n\nDuncan, brave as he was in the combat, could not, in such a moment of painful suspense, make any reply to the cool and characteristic remark of the scout. He only grasped his rifle more firmly, and fastened his eyes upon the narrow opening, through which he gazed upon the moonlight view with increasing anxiety. The deeper tones of one who spoke as having authority were next heard, amid a silence that denoted the respect with which his orders, or rather advice, was received. After which, by the rustling of leaves, and cracking of dried twigs, it was apparent the savages were separating in pursuit of the lost trail. Fortunately for the pursued, the light of the moon, while it shed a flood of mild lustre upon the little area around the ruin, was not sufficiently strong to penetrate the deep arches of the forest, where the objects still lay in deceptive shadow. The search proved fruitless; for so short and sudden had been the passage from the faint path the travellers had journeyed into the thicket, that every trace of their footsteps was lost in the obscurity of the woods.\n\nIt was not long, however, before the restless savages were heard beating the brush, and gradually approaching the inner edge of that dense border of young chestnuts which encircled the little area.\n\n\"They are coming,\" muttered Heyward, endeavoring to thrust his rifle through the chink in the logs; \"let us fire on their approach.\"\n\n\"Keep everything in the shade,\" returned the scout; \"the snapping of a flint, or even the smell of a single karnel of the brimstone, would bring the hungry varlets upon us in a body. Should it please God that we must give battle for the scalps, trust to the experience of men who know the ways of the savages, and who are not often backward when the war-whoop is howled.\"\n\nDuncan cast his eyes behind him, and saw that the trembling sisters were cowering in the far corner of the building, while the Mohicans stood in the shadow, like two upright posts, ready, and apparently willing, to strike when the blow should be needed. Curbing his impatience, he again looked out upon the area, and awaited the result in silence. At that instant the thicket opened, and a tall and armed Huron advanced a few paces into the open space. As he gazed upon the silent block-house, the moon fell upon his swarthy countenance, and betrayed its surprise and curiosity. He made the exclamation which usually accompanies the former emotion in an Indian, and, calling in a low voice, soon drew a companion to his side.\n\nThese children of the woods stood together for several moments pointing at the crumbling edifice, and conversing in the unintelligible language of their tribe. They then approached, though with slow and cautious steps, pausing every instant to look at the building, like startled deer, whose curiosity struggled powerfully with their awakened apprehensions for the mastery. The foot of one of them suddenly rested on the mound, and he stooped to examine its nature. At this moment, Heyward observed that the scout loosened his knife in his sheath, and lowered the muzzle of his rifle. Imitating these movements, the young man prepared himself for the struggle, which now seemed inevitable.\n\nThe savages were so near, that the least motion in one of the horses, or even a breath louder than common, would have betrayed the fugitives. But, in discovering the character of the mound, the attention of the Hurons appeared directed to a different object. They spoke together, and the sounds of their voices were low and solemn, as if influenced by a reverence that was deeply blended with awe. Then they drew warily back, keeping their eyes riveted on the ruin, as if they expected to see the apparitions of the dead issue from its silent walls, until having reached the boundary of the area, they moved slowly into the thicket, and disappeared.\n\nHawkeye dropped the breech of his rifle to the earth, and drawing a long, free breath, exclaimed, in an audible whisper,--\n\n\"Ay! they respect the dead, and it has this time saved their own lives, and, it may be, the lives of better men too.\"\n\nHeyward lent his attention for a single moment to his companion, but without replying, he again turned towards those who just then interested him more. He heard the two Hurons leave the bushes, and it was soon plain that all the pursuers were gathered about them, in deep attention to their report. After a few minutes of earnest and solemn dialogue, altogether different from the noisy clamor with which they had first collected about the spot, the sounds grew fainter and more distant, and finally were lost in the depths of the forest.\n\nHawkeye waited until a signal from the listening Chingachgook assured him that every sound from the retiring party was completely swallowed by the distance, when he motioned to Heyward to lead forth the horses, and to assist the sisters into their saddles. The instant this was done, they issued through the broken gateway, and stealing out by a direction opposite to the one by which they had entered, they quitted the spot, the sisters casting furtive glances at the silent grave and crumbling ruin, as they left the soft light of the moon, to bury themselves in the gloom of the woods.\n\n\"_Guard._--Qui est la?\n\n_Puc._--Paisans, pauvres gens de France.\"\n\n_King Henry VI._\n\nDuring the rapid movement from the block-house, and until the party was deeply buried in the forest, each individual was too much interested in the escape to hazard a word even in whispers. The scout resumed his post in the advance, though his steps, after he had thrown a safe distance between himself and his enemies, were more deliberate than in their previous march, in consequence of his utter ignorance of the localities of the surrounding woods. More than once he halted to consult with his confederates, the Mohicans, pointing upwards at the moon, and examining the barks of the trees with care. In these brief pauses, Heyward and the sisters listened, with senses rendered doubly acute by the danger, to detect any symptoms which might announce the proximity of their foes. At such moments, it seemed as if a vast range of country lay buried in eternal sleep; not the least sound arising from the forest, unless it was the distant and scarcely audible rippling of a water-course. Birds, beasts, and man, appeared to slumber alike, if, indeed, any of the latter were to be found in that wide tract of wilderness. But the sounds of the rivulet, feeble and murmuring as they were, relieved the guides at once from no trifling embarrassment, and towards it they immediately held their way.\n\nWhen the banks of the little stream were gained, Hawkeye made another halt; and, taking the moccasins from his feet, he invited Heyward and Gamut to follow his example. He then entered the water, and for near an hour they travelled in the bed of the brook, leaving no trail. The moon had already sunk into an immense pile of black clouds, which lay impending above the western horizon, when they issued from the low and devious water-course to rise again to the light and level of the sandy but wooded plain. Here the scout seemed to be once more at home, for he held on his way with the certainty and diligence of a man who moved in the security of his own knowledge. The path soon became more uneven, and the travellers could plainly perceive that the mountains drew nigher to them on each hand, and that they were, in truth, about entering one of their gorges. Suddenly, Hawkeye made a pause, and waiting until he was joined by the whole party, he spoke, though in tones so low and cautious, that they added to the solemnity of his words, in the quiet and darkness of the place.\n\n\"It is easy to know the pathways, and to find the licks and water-courses of the wilderness,\" he said; \"but who that saw this spot could venture to say, that a mighty army was at rest among yonder silent trees and barren mountains?\"\n\n\"We are then at no great distance from William Henry?\" said Heyward, advancing nigher to the scout.\n\n\"It is yet a long and weary path, and when and where to strike it, is now our greatest difficulty. See,\" he said, pointing through the trees towards a spot where a little basin of water reflected the stars from its placid bosom, \"here is the 'bloody pond'; and I am on the ground that I have not only often travelled, but over which I have fou't the enemy, from the rising to the setting sun.\"\n\n\"Ha! that sheet of dull and dreary water, then, is the sepulchre of the brave men who fell in the contest. I have heard it named, but never have I stood on its banks before.\"\n\n\"Three battles did we make with the Dutch-Frenchman[20] in a day,\" continued Hawkeye, pursuing the train of his own thoughts, rather than replying to the remark of Duncan. \"He met us hard by, in our outward march to ambush his advance, and scattered us, like driven deer, through the defile, to the shores of Horican. Then we rallied behind our fallen trees, and made head against him, under Sir William--who was made Sir William for that very deed; and well did we pay him for the disgrace of the morning! Hundreds of Frenchmen saw the sun that day for the last time; and even the leader, Dieskau himself, fell into our hands so cut and torn with the lead, that he has gone back to his own country, unfit for further acts in war.\"\n\n\"'Twas a noble repulse!\" exclaimed Heyward, in the heat of his youthful ardor; \"the fame of it reached us early, in our southern army.\"\n\n\"Ay! but it did not end there. I was sent by Major Effingham, at Sir William's own bidding, to outflank the French, and carry the tidings of their disaster across the portage, to the fort on the Hudson. Just hereaway, where you see the trees rise into a mountain swell, I met a party coming down to our aid, and I led them where the enemy were taking their meal, little dreaming that they had not finished the bloody work of the day.\"\n\n\"And you surprised them?\"\n\n\"If death can be a surprise to men who are thinking only of the cravings of their appetites. We gave them but little breathing time, for they had borne hard upon us in the fight of the morning, and there were few in our party who had not lost friend or relative by their hands. When all was over, the dead, and some say the dying, were cast into that little pond. These eyes have seen its waters colored with blood, as natural water never yet flowed from the bowels of the 'arth.\"\n\n\"It was a convenient, and, I trust, will prove a peaceful grave for a soldier. You have, then, seen much service on this frontier?\"\n\n\"I!\" said the scout, erecting his tall person with an air of military pride; \"there are not many echoes among these hills that haven't rung with the crack of my rifle, nor is there the space of a square mile atwixt Horican and the river, that 'Killdeer' hasn't dropped a living body on, be it an enemy or be it a brute beast. As for the grave, there, being as quiet as you mention, it is another matter. There are them in the camp who say and think, man, to lie still, should not be buried while the breath is in the body; and certain it is that in the hurry of that evening, the doctors had but little time to say who was living and who was dead. Hist! see you nothing walking on the shore of the pond?\"\n\n\"'Tis not probable that any are as houseless as ourselves, in this dreary forest.\"\n\n\"Such as he may care but little for house or shelter, and night dew can never wet a body that passes its days in the water,\" returned the scout, grasping the shoulder of Heyward with such convulsive strength as to make the young soldier painfully sensible how much superstitious terror had got the mastery of a man usually so dauntless.\n\n\"By heaven! there is a human form, and it approaches! Stand to your arms, my friends; for we know not whom we encounter.\"\n\n\"Qui vive?\" demanded a stern, quick voice, which sounded like a challenge from another world, issuing out of that solitary and solemn place.\n\n\"What says it?\" whispered the scout; \"it speaks neither Indian nor English!\"\n\n\"Qui vive?\" repeated the same voice, which was quickly followed by the rattling of arms, and a menacing attitude.\n\n\"France!\" cried Heyward, advancing from the shadow of the trees to the shore of the pond, within a few yards of the sentinel.\n\n\"D'ou venez-vous--ou allez-vous, d'aussi bonne heure?\" demanded the grenadier, in the language and with the accent of a man from old France.\n\n\"Je viens de la decouverte, et je vais me coucher.\"\n\n\"Etes-vous officier du roi?\"\n\n\"Sans doute, mon camarade; me prends-tu pour un provincial! Je suis capitaine de chasseurs (Heyward well knew that the other was of a regiment in the line); j'ai ici, avec moi, les filles du commandant de la fortification. Aha! tu en as entendu parler! je les ai fait prisonnieres pres de l'autre fort, et je les conduis au general.\"\n\n\"Ma foi! mesdames; j'en suis fache pour vous,\" exclaimed the young soldier, touching his cap with grace; \"mais--fortune de guerre! vous trouverez notre general un brave homme, et bien poli avec les dames.\"\n\n\"C'est le caractere des gens de guerre,\" said Cora, with admirable self-possession. \"Adieu, mon ami; je vous souhaiterais un devoir plus agreable a remplir.\"\n\nThe soldier made a low and humble acknowledgment for her civility; and Heyward adding a \"Bonne nuit, mon camarade,\" they moved deliberately forward, leaving the sentinel pacing the banks of the silent pond, little suspecting an enemy of so much effrontery, and humming to himself those words, which were recalled to his mind by the sight of women, and perhaps by recollections of his own distant and beautiful France--\n\n\"Vive le vin, l'amour,\" etc., etc.\n\n\"'Tis well you understood the knave!\" whispered the scout, when they had gained a little distance from the place, and letting his rifle fall into the hollow of his arm again; \"I soon saw that he was one of them uneasy Frenchers; and well for him it was that his speech was friendly and his wishes kind, or a place might have been found for his bones among those of his countrymen.\"\n\nHe was interrupted by a long and heavy groan which arose from the little basin, as though, in truth, the spirits of the departed lingered about their watery sepulchre.\n\n\"Surely it was of flesh!\" continued the scout; \"no spirit could handle its arms so steadily!\"\n\n\"It _was_ of flesh; but whether the poor fellow still belongs to this world may well be doubted,\" said Heyward, glancing his eyes around him, and missing Chingachgook from their little band. Another groan more faint than the former, was succeeded by a heavy and sullen plunge into the water, and all was as still again as if the borders of the dreary pool had never been awakened from the silence of creation. While they yet hesitated in uncertainty, the form of the Indian was seen gliding out of the thicket. As the chief rejoined them, with one hand he attached the reeking scalp of the unfortunate young Frenchman to his girdle, and with the other he replaced the knife and tomahawk that had drunk his blood. He then took his wonted station, with the air of a man who believed he had done a deed of merit.\n\nThe scout dropped one end of his rifle to the earth, and leaning his hands on the other, he stood musing in profound silence. Then shaking his head in a mournful manner, he muttered,--\n\n\"'T would have been a cruel and an unhuman act for a white-skin; but 'tis the gift and natur' of an Indian, and I suppose it should not be denied. I could wish, though, it had befallen an accursed Mingo, rather than that gay young boy from the old countries.\"\n\n\"Enough!\" said Heyward, apprehensive the unconscious sisters might comprehend the nature of the detention, and conquering his disgust by a train of reflections very much like that of the hunter; \"'tis done; and though better it were left undone, cannot be amended. You see we are, too obviously, within the sentinels of the enemy; what course do you propose to follow?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Hawkeye, rousing himself again, \"'tis as you say, too late to harbor further thoughts about it. Ay, the French have gathered around the fort in good earnest, and we have a delicate needle to thread in passing them.\"\n\n\"And but little time to do it in,\" added Heyward, glancing his eyes upward, toward the bank of vapor that concealed the setting moon.\n\n\"And little time to do it in!\" repeated the scout. \"The thing may be done in two fashions, by the help of Providence, without which it may not be done at all.\"\n\n\"Name them quickly, for time presses.\"\n\n\"One would be to dismount the gentle ones, and let their beasts range the plain; by sending the Mohicans in front, we might then cut a lane through their sentries, and enter the fort over the dead bodies.\"\n\n\"It will not do--it will not do!\" interrupted the generous Heyward; \"a soldier might force his way in this manner, but never with such a convoy.\"\n\n\"'Twould be, indeed, a bloody path for tender feet to wade in,\" returned the equally reluctant scout; \"but I thought it befitting my manhood to name it. We must then turn on our trail and get without the line of their look-outs, when we will bend short to the west, and enter the mountains; where I can hide you, so that all the devil's hounds in Montcalm's pay would be thrown off the scent, for months to come.\"\n\n\"Let it be done, and that instantly.\"\n\nFurther words were unnecessary; for Hawkeye, merely uttering the mandate to \"follow,\" moved along the route by which they had just entered their present critical and even dangerous situation. Their progress, like their late dialogue, was guarded, and without noise; for none knew at what moment a passing patrol, or a crouching picket of the enemy, might rise upon their path. As they held their silent way along the margin of the pond, again Heyward and the scout stole furtive glances at its appalling dreariness. They looked in vain for the form they had so recently seen stalking along its silent shores, while a low and regular wash of the little waves, by announcing that the waters were not yet subsided, furnished a frightful memorial of the deed of blood they had just witnessed. Like all that passing and gloomy scene, the low basin, however, quickly melted in the darkness, and became blended with the mass of black objects in the rear of the travellers.\n\nHawkeye soon deviated from the line of their retreat, and striking off towards the mountains which form the western boundary of the narrow plain, he led his followers, with swift steps, deep within the shadows that were cast from their high and broken summits. The route was now painful; lying over ground ragged with rocks, and intersected with ravines, and their progress proportionately slow. Bleak and black hills lay on every side of them, compensating in some degree for the additional toil of the march, by the sense of security they imparted. At length the party began slowly to climb a steep and rugged ascent by a path that curiously wound among rocks and trees, avoiding the one, and supported by the other, in a manner that showed it had been devised by men long practised in the arts of the wilderness. As they gradually rose from the level of the valleys, the thick darkness which usually precedes the approach of day began to disperse, and objects were seen in the plain and palpable colors with which they had been gifted by nature. When they issued from the stunted woods which clung to the barren sides of the mountain, upon a flat and mossy rock that formed its summit, they met the morning, as it came blushing above the green pines of a hill that lay on the opposite side of the valley of the Horican.\n\nThe scout now told the sisters to dismount; and taking the bridles from the mouths, and the saddles off the backs of the jaded beasts, he turned them loose, to glean a scanty subsistence among the shrubs and meagre herbage of that elevated region.\n\n\"Go,\" he said, \"and seek your food where natur' gives it you; and beware that you become not food to ravenous wolves yourselves, among these hills.\"\n\n\"Have we no further need of them?\" demanded Heyward.\n\n\"See, and judge with your own eyes,\" said the scout, advancing towards the eastern brow of the mountain, whither he beckoned for the whole party to follow; \"if it was as easy to look into the heart of man as it is to spy out the nakedness of Montcalm's camp from this spot, hypocrites would grow scarce, and the cunning of a Mingo might prove a losing game, compared to the honesty of a Delaware.\"\n\nWhen the travellers reached the verge of the precipice, they saw, at a glance, the truth of the scout's declaration, and the admirable foresight with which he had led them to their commanding station.\n\nThe mountain on which they stood, elevated, perhaps, a thousand feet in the air, was a high cone that rose a little in advance of that range which stretches for miles along the western shores of the lake, until meeting its sister piles, beyond the water, it ran off towards the Canadas, in confused and broken masses of rock, thinly sprinkled with evergreens. Immediately at the feet of the party, the southern shore of the Horican swept in a broad semicircle, from mountain to mountain, marking a wide strand, that soon rose into an uneven and somewhat elevated plain. To the north stretched the limpid, and, as it appeared from that dizzy height, the narrow sheet of the \"holy lake,\" indented with numberless bays, embellished by fantastic headlands, and dotted with countless islands. At the distance of a few leagues, the bed of the waters became lost among mountains, or was wrapped in the masses of vapor that came slowly rolling along their bosom, before a light morning air. But a narrow opening between the crests of the hills pointed out the passage by which they found their way still farther north, to spread their pure and ample sheets again, before pouring out their tribute into the distant Champlain. To the south stretched the defile, or rather broken plain, so often mentioned. For several miles in this direction, the mountains appeared reluctant to yield their dominion, but within reach of the eye they diverged, and finally melted into the level and sandy lands, across which we have accompanied our adventurers in their double journey. Along both ranges of hills, which bounded the opposite sides of the lake and valley, clouds of light vapor were rising in spiral wreaths from the uninhabited woods, looking like the smokes of hidden cottages; or rolled lazily down the declivities, to mingle with the fogs of the lower land. A single, solitary, snow-white cloud floated above the valley, and marked the spot beneath which lay the silent pool of the \"bloody pond.\"\n\nDirectly on the shore of the lake, and nearer to its western than to its eastern margin, lay the extensive earthen ramparts and low buildings of William Henry. Two of the sweeping bastions appeared to rest on the water which washed their bases, while a deep ditch and extensive morasses guarded its other sides and angles. The land had been cleared of wood for a reasonable distance around the work, but every other part of the scene lay in the green livery of nature, except where the limpid water mellowed the view, or the bold rocks thrust their black and naked heads above the undulating outline of the mountain ranges. In its front might be seen the scattered sentinels, who held a weary watch against their numerous foes; and within the walls themselves, the travellers looked down upon men still drowsy with a night of vigilance. Towards the southeast, but in immediate contact with the fort, was an entrenched camp, posted on a rocky eminence, that would have been far more eligible for the work itself, in which Hawkeye pointed out the presence of those auxiliary regiments that had so recently left the Hudson in their company. From the woods, a little farther to the south, rose numerous dark and lurid smokes, that were easily to be distinguished from the purer exhalations of the springs, and which the scout also showed to Heyward, as evidences that the enemy lay in force in that direction.\n\nBut the spectacle which most concerned the young soldier was on the western bank of the lake, though quite near to its southern termination. On a strip of land, which appeared, from his stand, too narrow to contain such an army, but which, in truth, extended many hundreds of yards from the shores of the Horican to the base of the mountain, were to be seen the white tents and military engines of an encampment of ten thousand men. Batteries were already thrown up in their front, and even while the spectators above them were looking down, with such different emotions, on a scene which lay like a map beneath their feet, the roar of artillery rose from the valley, and passed off in thundering echoes, along the eastern hills.\n\n\"Morning is just touching them below,\" said the deliberate and musing scout, \"and the watchers have a mind to wake up the sleepers by the sound of cannon. We are a few hours too late? Montcalm has already filled the woods with his accursed Iroquois.\"\n\n\"The place is, indeed, invested,\" returned Duncan, \"but is there no expedient by which we may enter? capture in the works would be far preferable to falling again into the hands of roving Indians.\"\n\n\"See!\" exclaimed the scout, unconsciously directing the attention of Cora to the quarters of her own father, \"how that shot has made the stones fly from the side of the commandant's house! Ay! these Frenchers will pull it to pieces faster than it was put together, solid and thick though it be.\"\n\n\"Heyward, I sicken at the sight of danger that I cannot share,\" said the undaunted, but anxious daughter. \"Let us go to Montcalm, and demand admission: he dare not deny a child the boon.\"\n\n\"You would scarce find the tent of the Frenchman with the hair on your head,\" said the blunt scout. \"If I had but one of the thousand boats which lie empty along that shore, it might be done. Ha! here will soon be an end of the firing, for yonder comes a fog that will turn day to night, and make an Indian arrow more dangerous than a moulded cannon. Now, if you are equal to the work, and will follow, I will make a push; for I long to get down into that camp, if it be only to scatter some Mingo dogs that I see lurking in the skirts of yonder thicket of birch.\"\n\n\"We are equal,\" said Cora, firmly: \"on such an errand we will follow to any danger.\"\n\nThe scout turned to her with a smile of honest and cordial approbation as he answered,--\n\n\"I would I had a thousand men, of brawny limbs and quick eyes, that feared death as little as you! I'd send them jabbering Frenchers back into their den again, afore the week was ended, howling like so many fettered hounds or hungry wolves. But stir,\" he added, turning from her to the rest of the party, \"the fog comes rolling down so fast, we shall have but just the time to meet it on the plain, and use it as a cover. Remember, if any accident should befall me, to keep the air blowing on your left cheeks--or rather, follow the Mohicans; they'd scent their way, be it in day or be it at night.\"\n\nHe then waved his hand for them to follow, and threw himself down the steep declivity, with free, but careful footsteps. Heyward assisted the sisters to descend, and in a few minutes they were all far down a mountain whose sides they had climbed with so much toil and pain.\n\nThe direction taken by Hawkeye soon brought the travellers to the level of the plain, nearly opposite to a sally-port in the western curtain of the fort, which lay, itself, at the distance of about half a mile from the point where he halted to allow Duncan to come up with his charge. In their eagerness, and favored by the nature of the ground, they had anticipated the fog, which was rolling heavily down the lake, and it became necessary to pause, until the mists had wrapped the camp of the enemy in their fleecy mantle. The Mohicans profited by the delay, to steal out of the woods, and to make a survey of surrounding objects. They were followed at a little distance by the scout, with a view to profit early by their report, and to obtain some faint knowledge for himself of the more immediate localities.\n\nIn a very few moments he returned, his face reddened with vexation, while he muttered his disappointment in words of no very gentle import.\n\n\"Here has the cunning Frenchman been posting a picket directly in our path,\" he said; \"redskins and whites; and we shall be as likely to fall into their midst as to pass them in the fog!\"\n\n\"Cannot we make a circuit to avoid the danger,\" asked Heyward, \"and come into our path again when it is passed?\"\n\n\"Who that once bends from the line of his march in a fog can tell when or how to turn to find it again! The mists of Horican are not like the curls from a peace-pipe, or the smoke which settles above a mosquito fire.\"\n\nHe was yet speaking, when a crashing sound was heard, and a cannon-ball entered the thicket, striking the body of a sapling, and rebounding to the earth, its force being much expended by previous resistance. The Indians followed instantly like busy attendants on the terrible messenger, and Uncas commenced speaking earnestly and with much action, in the Delaware tongue.\n\n\"It may be so, lad,\" muttered the scout, when he had ended; \"for desperate fevers are not to be treated like a toothache. Come, then, the fog is shutting in.\"\n\n\"Stop!\" cried Heyward; \"first explain your expectations.\"\n\n\"'Tis soon done, and a small hope it is; but it is better than nothing. This shot that you see,\" added the scout, kicking the harmless iron with his foot, \"has ploughed the 'arth in its road from the fort, and we shall hunt for the furrow it has made, when all other signs may fail. No more words, but follow, or the fog may leave us in the middle of our path, a mark for both armies to shoot at.\"\n\nHeyward perceiving that, in fact, a crisis had arrived when acts were more required than words, placed himself between the sisters, and drew them swiftly forward, keeping the dim figure of their leader in his eye. It was soon apparent that Hawkeye had not magnified the power of the fog, for before they had proceeded twenty yards, it was difficult for the different individuals of the party to distinguish each other, in the vapor.\n\nThey had made their little circuit to the left, and were already inclining again towards the right, having, as Heyward thought, got over nearly half the distance to the friendly works, when his ears were saluted with the fierce summons, apparently within twenty feet of them, of--\n\n\"Qui va la?\"\n\n\"Push on!\" whispered the scout, once more bending to the left.\n\n\"Push on!\" repeated Heyward; when the summons was renewed by a dozen voices, each of which seemed charged with menace.\n\n\"C'est moi,\" cried Duncan, dragging, rather than leading those he supported, swiftly onward.\n\n\"Bete!--qui?--moi!\"\n\n\"Ami de la France.\"\n\n\"Tu m'as plus l'air d'un _ennemi_ de la France; arrete! ou pardieu je te ferai ami du diable. Non! feu, camarades, feu!\"\n\nThe order was instantly obeyed, and the fog was stirred by the explosion of fifty muskets. Happily, the aim was bad, and the bullets cut the air in a direction a little different from that taken by the fugitives; though still so nigh them, that to the unpractised ears of David and the two females, it appeared as if they whistled within a few inches of the organs. The outcry was renewed, and the order, not only to fire again, but to pursue, was too plainly audible. When Heyward briefly explained the meaning of the words they heard, Hawkeye halted, and spoke with quick decision and great firmness.\n\n\"Let us deliver our fire,\" he said; \"they will believe it a sortie, and give way, or they will wait for reinforcements.\"\n\nThe scheme was well conceived, but failed in its effect. The instant the French heard the pieces, it seemed as if the plain was alive with men, muskets rattling along its whole extent, from the shores of the lake to the farthest boundary of the woods.\n\n\"We shall draw their entire army upon us, and bring on a general assault,\" said Duncan: \"lead on, my friend, for your own life, and ours.\"\n\nThe scout seemed willing to comply; but, in the hurry of the moment, and in the change of position, he had lost the direction. In vain he turned either cheek towards the light air; they felt equally cool. In this dilemma, Uncas lighted on the furrow of the cannon-ball, where it had cut the ground in three adjacent ant-hills.\n\n\"Give me the range!\" said Hawkeye, bending to catch a glimpse of the direction, and then instantly moving onward.\n\nCries, oaths, voices calling to each other, and the reports of muskets, were now quick and incessant, and, apparently, on every side of them. Suddenly, a strong glare of light flashed across the scene, the fog rolled upwards in thick wreaths, and several cannon belched across the plain, and the roar was thrown heavily back from the bellowing echoes of the mountain.\n\n\"'Tis from the fort!\" exclaimed Hawkeye, turning short on his tracks; \"and we, like stricken fools, were rushing to the woods, under the very knives of the Maquas.\"\n\nThe instant their mistake was rectified, the whole party retraced the error with the utmost diligence. Duncan willingly relinquished the support of Cora to the arm of Uncas, and Cora as readily accepted the welcome assistance. Men, hot and angry in pursuit, were evidently on their footsteps, and each instant threatened their capture, if not their destruction.\n\n\"Point de quartier aux coquins!\" cried an eager pursuer, who seemed to direct the operations of the enemy.\n\n\"Stand firm, and be ready, my gallant 60ths!\" suddenly exclaimed a voice above them; \"wait to see the enemy,--fire low, and sweep the glacis.\"\n\n\"Father! father!\" exclaimed a piercing cry from out the mist; \"it is I! Alice! thy own Elsie! spare, O! save your daughters!\"\n\n\"Hold!\" shouted the former speaker, in the awful tones of parental agony, the sound reaching even to the woods, and rolling back in solemn echo. \"'Tis she! God has restored me my children! Throw open the sally-port; to the field, 60ths, to the field; pull not a trigger, lest ye kill my lambs! Drive off these dogs of France with your steel.\"\n\nDuncan heard the grating of the rusty hinges, and darting to the spot, directed by the sound, he met a long line of dark red warriors, passing swiftly towards the glacis. He knew them for his own battalion of the royal Americans, and flying to their head, soon swept every trace of his pursuers from before the works.\n\nFor an instant, Cora and Alice had stood trembling and bewildered by this unexpected desertion; but, before either had leisure for speech, or even thought, an officer of gigantic frame whose locks were bleached with years and service, but whose air of military grandeur had been rather softened than destroyed by time, rushed out of the body of the mist, and folded them to his bosom, while large scalding tears rolled down his pale and wrinkled cheeks, and he exclaimed, in the peculiar accent of Scotland,--\n\n\"For this I thank thee, Lord! Let danger come as it will, thy servant is now prepared!\"\n\n\"Then go we in, to know his embassy; Which I could, with ready guess, declare, Before the Frenchman speak a word of it.\"\n\n_King Henry V._\n\nA few succeeding days were passed amid the privations, the uproar, and the dangers of the siege, which was vigorously pressed by a power against whose approaches Munro possessed no competent means of resistance. It appeared as if Webb, with his army, which lay slumbering on the banks of the Hudson, had utterly forgotten the strait to which his countrymen were reduced. Montcalm had filled the woods of the portage with his savages, every yell and whoop from whom rang through the British encampment, chilling the hearts of men who were already but too much disposed to magnify the danger.\n\nNot so, however, with the besieged. Animated by the words, and stimulated by the examples, of their leaders, they had found their courage, and maintained their ancient reputation, with zeal that did justice to the stern character of their commander. As if satisfied with the toil of marching through the wilderness to encounter his enemy, the French general, though of approved skill, had neglected to seize the adjacent mountains; whence the besieged might have been exterminated with impunity, and which, in the more modern warfare of the country, would not have been neglected for a single hour. This sort of contempt for eminences, or rather dread of the labor of ascending them, might have been termed the besetting weakness of the warfare of the period. It originated in the simplicity of the Indian contests, in which, from the nature of the combats, and the density of the forests, fortresses were rare, and artillery next to useless. The carelessness engendered by these usages descended even to the war of the Revolution, and lost the States the important fortress of Ticonderoga, opening a way for the army of Burgoyne into what was then the bosom of the country. We look back at this ignorance, or infatuation, whichever it may be called, with wonder, knowing that the neglect of an eminence, whose difficulties, like those of Mount Defiance, have been so greatly exaggerated, would, at the present time, prove fatal to the reputation of the engineer who had planned the works at their base, or to that of the general whose lot it was to defend them.\n\nThe tourist, the valetudinarian, or the amateur of the beauties of nature, who, in the train of his four-in-hand, now rolls through the scenes we have attempted to describe, in quest of information, health, or pleasure, or floats steadily towards his object on those artificial waters which have sprung up under the administration of a statesman[21] who has dared to stake his political character on the hazardous issue, is not to suppose that his ancestors traversed those hills, or struggled with the same currents with equal facility. The transportation of a single heavy gun was often considered equal to a victory gained; if, happily, the difficulties of the passage had not so far separated it from its necessary concomitant, the ammunition, as to render it no more than an useless tube of unwieldy iron.\n\nThe evils of this state of things pressed heavily on the fortunes of the resolute Scotsman who now defended William Henry. Though his adversary neglected the hills, he had planted his batteries with judgment on the plain, and caused them to be served with vigor and skill. Against this assault, the besieged could only oppose the imperfect and hasty preparations of a fortress in the wilderness.\n\nIt was in the afternoon of the fifth day of the siege, and the fourth of his own service in it, that Major Heyward profited by a parley that had just been beaten, by repairing to the ramparts of one of the water bastions, to breathe the cool air from the lake, and to take a survey of the progress of the siege. He was alone, if the solitary sentinel who paced the mound be excepted; for the artillerists had hastened also to profit by the temporary suspension of their arduous duties. The evening was delightfully calm, and the light air from the limpid water fresh and soothing. It seemed as if, with the termination to the roar of artillery and the plunging of shot, nature had also seized the moment to assume her mildest and most captivating form. The sun poured down his parting glory on the scene, without the oppression of those fierce rays that belong to the climate and the season. The mountains looked green and fresh and lovely; tempered with the milder light, or softened in shadow, as thin vapors floated between them and the sun. The numerous islands rested on the bosom of the Horican, some low and sunken, as if imbedded in the waters, and others appearing to hover above the element, in little hillocks of green velvet; among which the fishermen of the beleaguering army peacefully rowed their skiffs, or floated at rest on the glassy mirror, in quiet pursuit of their employment.\n\nThe scene was at once animated and still. All that pertained to nature was sweet, or simply grand; while those parts which depended on the temper and movements of man were lively and playful.\n\nTwo little spotless flags were abroad, the one on a salient angle of the fort, and the other on the advanced battery of the besiegers; emblems of the truce which existed, not only to the acts, but it would seem, also, to the enmity of the combatants.\n\nBehind these, again, swung, heavily opening and closing in silken folds, the rival standards of England and France.\n\nA hundred gay and thoughtless young Frenchmen were drawing a net to the pebbly beach, within dangerous proximity to the sullen but silent cannon of the fort, while the eastern mountain was sending back the loud shouts and gay merriment that attended their sport. Some were rushing eagerly to enjoy the aquatic games of the lake, and others were already toiling their way up the neighboring hills, with the restless curiosity of their nation. To all these sports and pursuits, those of the enemy who watched the besieged, and the besieged themselves, were, however, merely the idle, though sympathizing spectators. Here and there a picket had, indeed, raised a song, or mingled in a dance, which had drawn the dusky savages around them, from their lairs in the forest. In short, everything wore rather the appearance of a day of pleasure, than of an hour stolen from the dangers and toil of a bloody and vindictive warfare.\n\nDuncan had stood in a musing attitude, contemplating this scene a few minutes, when his eyes were directed to the glacis in front of the sally-port already mentioned, by the sounds of approaching footsteps. He walked to an angle of the bastion, and beheld the scout advancing, under the custody of a French officer, to the body of the fort. The countenance of Hawkeye was haggard and careworn, and his air dejected, as though he felt the deepest degradation at having fallen into the power of his enemies. He was without his favorite weapon, and his arms were even bound behind him with thongs, made of the skin of a deer. The arrival of flags, to cover the messengers of summons, had occurred so often of late, that when Heyward first threw his careless glance on this group, he expected to see another of the officers of the enemy, charged with a similar office; but the instant he recognized the tall person, and still sturdy, though downcast features of his friend the woodsman, he started with surprise, and turned to descend from the bastion into the bosom of the work.\n\nThe sounds of other voices, however, caught his attention, and for a moment caused him to forget his purpose. At the inner angle of the mound he met the sisters, walking along the parapet in search, like himself, of air and relief from confinement. They had not met from that painful moment when he deserted them on the plain, only to assure their safety. He had parted from them worn with care, and jaded with fatigue; he now saw them refreshed and blooming, though timid and anxious. Under such an inducement, it will cause no surprise that the young man lost sight, for a time, of other objects in order to address them. He was, however, anticipated by the voice of the ingenuous and youthful Alice.\n\n\"Ah! thou truant! thou recreant knight! he who abandons his damsels in the very lists!\" she cried; \"here have we been days, nay, ages, expecting you at our feet, imploring mercy and forgetfulness of your craven backsliding, or, I should rather say, back-running--for verily you fled in a manner that no stricken deer, as our worthy friend the scout would say, could equal!\"\n\n\"You know that Alice means our thanks and our blessings,\" added the graver and more thoughtful Cora. \"In truth, we have a little wondered why you should so rigidly absent yourself from a place where the gratitude of the daughters might receive the support of a parent's thanks.\"\n\n\"Your father himself could tell you, that though absent from your presence, I have not been altogether forgetful of your safety,\" returned the young man; \"the mastery of yonder village of huts,\" pointing to the neighboring entrenched camp, \"has been keenly disputed; and he who holds it is sure to be possessed of this fort, and that which it contains. My days and my nights have all been passed there since we separated, because I thought that duty called me thither. But,\" he added with an air of chagrin, which he endeavored, though unsuccessfully, to conceal, \"had I been aware that what I then believed a soldier's conduct could so be construed, shame would have been added to the list of reasons.\"\n\n\"Heyward!--Duncan!\" exclaimed Alice, bending forward to read his half-averted countenance, until a lock of her golden hair rested on her flushed cheek, and nearly concealed the tear that had started to her eye; \"did I think this idle tongue of mine had pained you, I would silence it forever, Cora can say, if Cora would, how justly we have prized your services, and how deep--I had almost said, how fervent--is our gratitude.\"\n\n\"And will Cora attest the truth of this?\" cried Duncan, suffering the cloud to be chased from his countenance by a smile of open pleasure. \"What says our graver sister? Will she find an excuse for the neglect of the knight in the duty of a soldier?\"\n\nCora made no immediate answer, but turned her face towards the water, as if looking on the sheet of the Horican. When she did bend her dark eyes on the young man, they were yet filled with an expression of anguish that at once drove every thought but that of kind solicitude from his mind.\n\n\"You are not well, dearest Miss Munro!\" he exclaimed; \"we have trifled while you are in suffering.\"\n\n\"'Tis nothing,\" she answered, refusing his offered support with feminine reserve. \"That I cannot see the sunny side of the picture of life, like this artless but ardent enthusiast,\" she added, laying her hand lightly, but affectionately, on the arm of her sister, \"is the penalty of experience, and, perhaps, the misfortune of my nature. See,\" she continued, as if determined to shake off infirmity, in a sense of duty; \"look around you, Major Heyward, and tell me what a prospect is this for the daughter of a soldier whose greatest happiness is his honor and his military renown.\"\n\n\"Neither ought nor shall be tarnished by circumstances over which he has had no control,\" Duncan warmly replied. \"But your words recall me to my own duty. I go now to your gallant father, to hear his determination in matters of the last moment to the defence. God bless you in every fortune, noble--Cora--I may and must call you.\" She frankly gave him her hand, though her lip quivered, and her cheeks gradually became of an ashy paleness. \"In every fortune, I know you will be an ornament and honor to your sex. Alice, adieu\"--his tone changed from admiration to tenderness--\"adieu, Alice; we shall soon meet again; as conquerors, I trust, and amid rejoicings!\"\n\nWithout waiting for an answer from either, the young man threw himself down the grassy steps of the bastion, and moving rapidly across the parade, he was quickly in the presence of their father. Munro was pacing his narrow apartment with a disturbed air and gigantic strides as Duncan entered.\n\n\"You have anticipated my wishes, Major Heyward,\" he said; \"I was about to request this favor.\"\n\n\"I am sorry to see, sir, that the messenger I so warmly recommended has returned in custody of the French! I hope there is no reason to distrust his fidelity?\"\n\n\"The fidelity of 'The Long Rifle' is well known to me,\" returned Munro, \"and is above suspicion; though his usual good fortune seems, at last, to have failed. Montcalm has got him, and with the accursed politeness of his nation, he has sent him in with a doleful tale, of 'knowing how I valued the fellow, he could not think of retaining him.' A Jesuitical way, that, Major Duncan Heyward, of telling a man of his misfortunes!\"\n\n\"But the general and his succor?\"\n\n\"Did ye look to the south as ye entered, and could ye not see them?\" said the old soldier, laughing bitterly. \"Hoot! hoot! you're an impatient boy, sir, and cannot give the gentlemen leisure for their march!\"\n\n\"They are coming, then? The scout has said as much?\"\n\n\"When? and by what path? for the dunce has omitted to tell me this. There is a letter, it would seem, too; and that is the only agreeable part of the matter. For the customary attentions of your Marquis of Montcalm--I warrant me, Duncan, that he of Lothian would buy a dozen such marquisates--but, if the news of the letter were bad, the gentility of this French monsieur would certainly compel him to let us know it.\"\n\n\"He keeps the letter, then, while he releases the messenger!\"\n\n\"Ay, that does he, and all for the sake of what you call your '_bonhommie_,' I would venture, if the truth was known, the fellow's grandfather taught the noble science of dancing.\"\n\n\"But what says the scout? he has eyes and ears, and a tongue: what verbal report does he make?\"\n\n\"O! sir, he is not wanting in natural organs, and he is free to tell all that he has seen and heard. The whole amount is this: there is a fort of his majesty's on the banks of the Hudson, called Edward, in honor of his gracious highness of York, you'll know; and it is well filled with armed men, as such a work should be.\"\n\n\"But was there no movement, no signs of any intention to advance to our relief?\"\n\n\"There were the morning and evening parades; and when one of the provincial loons--you'll know, Duncan, you're half a Scotsman yourself--when one of them dropped his powder over his porretch, if it touched the coals, it just burnt!\" Then suddenly changing his bitter, ironical manner, to one more grave and thoughtful, he continued; \"and yet there might, and must be, something in that letter which it would be well to know!\"\n\n\"Our decision should be speedy,\" said Duncan, gladly availing himself of this change of humor, to press the more important objects of their interview; \"I cannot conceal from you, sir, that the camp will not be much longer tenable; and I am sorry to add, that things appear no better in the fort; more than half the guns are bursted.\"\n\n\"And how should it be otherwise? Some were fished from the bottom of the lake; some have been rusting in the woods since the discovery of the country; and some were never guns at all--mere privateersmen's playthings! Do you think, sir, you can have Woolwich Warren in the midst of a wilderness, three thousand miles from Great Britain!\"\n\n\"The walls are crumbling about our ears, and provisions begin to fail us,\" continued Heyward, without regarding this new burst of indignation; \"even the men show signs of discontent and alarm.\"\n\n\"Major Heyward,\" said Munro, turning to his youthful associate with the dignity of his years and superior rank; \"I should have served his majesty for half a century, and earned these gray hairs, in vain, were I ignorant of all you say, and of the pressing nature of our circumstances; still, there is everything due to the honor of the king's arms and something to ourselves. While there is hope of succor, this fortress will I defend, though it be to be done with pebbles gathered on the lake shore. It is a sight of the letter, therefore, that we want, that we may know the intentions of the man the Earl of Loudon has left among us as his substitute.\"\n\n\"And can I be of service in the matter?\"\n\n\"Sir, you can; the Marquis of Montcalm has, in addition to his other civilities, invited me to a personal interview between the works and his own camp; in order, as he says, to impart some additional information. Now, I think it would not be wise to show any undue solicitude to meet him, and I would employ you, an officer of rank, as my substitute; for it would but ill comport with the honor of Scotland to let it be said one of her gentlemen was outdone in civility by a native of any other country on earth.\"\n\nWithout assuming the supererogatory task of entering into a discussion of the comparative merits of national courtesy, Duncan cheerfully assented to supply the place of the veteran in the approaching interview. A long and confidential communication now succeeded, during which the young man received some additional insight into his duty, from the experience and native acuteness of his commander, and then the former took his leave.\n\nAs Duncan could only act as the representative of the commandant of the fort, the ceremonies which should have accompanied a meeting between the heads of the adverse forces were of course dispensed with. The truce still existed, and with a roll and beat of the drum, and covered by a little white flag, Duncan left the sally-port, within ten minutes after his instructions were ended. He was received by the French officer in advance with the usual formalities, and immediately accompanied to a distant marquee of the renowned soldier who led the forces of France.\n\nThe general of the enemy received the youthful messenger, surrounded by his principal officers, and by a swarthy band of the native chiefs, who had followed him to the field, with the warriors of their several tribes. Heyward paused short, when, in glancing his eyes rapidly over the dark group of the latter, he beheld the malignant countenance of Magua, regarding him with the calm but sullen attention which marked the expression of that subtle savage. A slight exclamation of surprise even burst from the lips of the young man; but instantly recollecting his errand, and the presence in which he stood, he suppressed every appearance of emotion, and turned to the hostile leader, who had already advanced a step to receive him.\n\nThe Marquis of Montcalm was, at the period of which we write, in the flower of his age, and, it may be added, in the zenith of his fortunes. But, even in that enviable situation, he was affable, and distinguished as much for his attention to the forms of courtesy, as for that chivalrous courage which, only two short years afterwards, induced him to throw away his life on the plains of Abraham. Duncan, in turning his eyes from the malign expression of Magua, suffered them to rest with pleasure on the smiling and polished features, and the noble military air, of the French general.\n\n\"Monsieur,\" said the latter, \"j'ai beaucoup de plaisir a--bah!--ou est cet interprete?\"\n\n\"Je crois, monsieur, qu'il ne sera pas necessaire,\" Heyward modestly replied; \"je parle un peu Francais.\"\n\n\"Ah! j'en suis bien aise,\" said Montcalm, taking Duncan familiarly by the arm, and leading him deep into the marquee, a little out of ear-shot; \"je deteste ces fripons-la; on ne sait jamais sur quel pie on est avec eux. Eh, bien! monsieur,\" he continued, still speaking in French; \"though I should have been proud of receiving your commandant, I am very happy that he has seen proper to employ an officer so distinguished, and who, I am sure, is so amiable, as yourself.\"\n\nDuncan bowed low, pleased with the compliment, in spite of a most heroic determination to suffer no artifice to allure him into forgetfulness of the interest of his prince; and Montcalm, after a pause of a moment, as if to recollect his thoughts, proceeded,--\n\n\"Your commandant is a brave man, and well qualified to repel my assault. Mais, monsieur, is it not time to begin to take more counsel of humanity, and less of your courage? The one as strongly characterizes the hero as the other.\"\n\n\"We consider the qualities as inseparable,\" returned Duncan, smiling; \"but while we find in the vigor of your excellency every motive to stimulate the one, we can, as yet, see no particular call for the exercise of the other.\"\n\nMontcalm, in his turn, slightly bowed, but it was with the air of a man too practised to remember the language of flattery. After musing a moment, he added,--\n\n\"It is possible my glasses have deceived me, and that your works resist our cannon better than I had supposed. You know our force?\"\n\n\"Our accounts vary,\" said Duncan, carelessly; \"the highest, however, has not exceeded twenty thousand men.\"\n\nThe Frenchman bit his lip, and fastened his eyes keenly on the other as if to read his thoughts; then, with a readiness peculiar to himself, he continued, as if assenting to the truth of an enumeration which quite doubled his army,--\n\n\"It is a poor compliment to the vigilance of us soldiers, monsieur, that, do what we will, we never can conceal our numbers. If it were to be done at all, one would believe it might succeed in these woods. Though you think it too soon to listen to the calls of humanity,\" he added, smiling archly, \"I may be permitted to believe that gallantry is not forgotten by one so young as yourself. The daughters of the commandant, I learn, have passed into the fort since it was invested?\"\n\n\"It is true, monsieur; but, so far from weakening our efforts, they set us an example of courage in their own fortitude. Were nothing but resolution necessary to repel so accomplished a soldier as M. de Montcalm, I would gladly trust the defence of William Henry to the elder of those ladies.\"\n\n\"We have a wise ordinance in our Salique laws, which says, 'The crown of France shall never degrade the lance to the distaff,'\" said Montcalm, dryly, and with a little hauteur; but instantly adding, with his former frank and easy air, \"as all the nobler qualities are hereditary, I can easily credit you; though, as I said before, courage has its limits, and humanity must not be forgotten. I trust, monsieur, you come authorized to treat for the surrender of the place?\"\n\n\"Has your excellency found our defence so feeble as to believe the measure necessary?\"\n\n\"I should be sorry to have the defence protracted in such a manner as to irritate my red friends there,\" continued Montcalm, glancing his eyes at the group of grave and attentive Indians, without attending to the other's question; \"I find it difficult, even now, to limit them to the usages of war.\"\n\nHeyward was silent; for a painful recollection of the dangers he had so recently escaped came over his mind, and recalled the images of those defenceless beings who had shared in all his sufferings.\n\n\"Ces messieurs-la,\" said Montcalm, following up the advantage which he conceived he had gained, \"are most formidable when baffled: and it is unnecessary to tell you with what difficulty they are restrained in their anger. Eh bien, monsieur! shall we speak of the terms?\"\n\n\"I fear your excellency has been deceived as to the strength of William Henry, and the resources of its garrison!\"\n\n\"I have not sat down before Quebec, but an earthen work, that is defended by twenty-three hundred gallant men,\" was the laconic reply.\n\n\"Our mounds are earthen, certainly--nor are they seated on the rocks of Cape Diamond; but they stand on that shore which proved so destructive to Dieskau and his army. There is also a powerful force within a few hours' march of us, which we account upon as part of our means.\"\n\n\"Some six or eight thousand men,\" returned Montcalm, with much apparent indifference, \"whom their leader wisely judges to be safer in their works than in the field.\"\n\nIt was now Heyward's turn to bite his lip with vexation, as the other so coolly alluded to a force which the young man knew to be overrated. Both mused a little while in silence, when Montcalm renewed the conversation, in a way that showed he believed the visit of his guest was solely to propose terms of capitulation. On the other hand, Heyward began to throw sundry inducements in the way of the French general, to betray the discoveries he had made through the intercepted letter. The artifice of neither, however, succeeded; and after a protracted and fruitless interview, Duncan took his leave, favorably impressed with an opinion of the courtesy and talents of the enemy's captain, but as ignorant of what he came to learn as when he arrived. Montcalm followed him as far as the entrance of the marquee, renewing his invitations to the commandant of the fort to give him an immediate meeting in the open ground, between the two armies.\n\nThere they separated, and Duncan returned to the advanced post of the French, accompanied as before; whence he instantly proceeded to the fort, and to the quarters of his own commander.\n\n\"_Edg._--Before you fight the battle, ope this letter.\"\n\n_King Lear._\n\nMajor Heyward found Munro attended only by his daughters. Alice sat upon his knee, parting the gray hairs on the forehead of the old man with her delicate fingers; and, whenever he affected to frown on her trifling, appeasing his assumed anger by pressing her ruby lips fondly on his wrinkled brow. Cora was seated nigh them, a calm and amused looker-on; regarding the wayward movements of her more youthful sister, with that species of maternal fondness which characterized her love for Alice. Not only the dangers through which they had passed, but those which still impended above them, appeared to be momentarily forgotten, in the soothing indulgence of such a family meeting. It seemed as if they had profited by the short truce, to devote an instant to the purest and best affections: the daughters forgetting their fears, and the veteran his cares, in the security of the moment. Of this scene, Duncan, who in his eagerness to report his arrival had entered unannounced, stood many moments an unobserved and a delighted spectator. But the quick and dancing eyes of Alice soon caught a glimpse of his figure reflected from a glass, and she sprang blushing from her father's knee, exclaiming aloud,--\n\n\"Major Heyward!\"\n\n\"What of the lad?\" demanded the father; \"I have sent him to crack a little with the Frenchman. Ha! sir, you are young, and you're nimble! Away with you, ye baggage; as if there were not troubles enough for a soldier, without having his camp filled with such prattling hussies as yourself!\"\n\nAlice laughingly followed her sister, who instantly led the way from an apartment where she perceived their presence was no longer desirable. Munro, instead of demanding the result of the young man's mission, paced the room for a few moments, with his hands behind his back, and his head inclined towards the floor, like a man lost in thought. At length he raised his eyes, glistening with a father's fondness, and exclaimed,--\n\n\"They are a pair of excellent girls, Heyward, and such as any one may boast of.\"\n\n\"You are not now to learn my opinion of your daughters, Colonel Munro.\"\n\n\"True, lad, true,\" interrupted the impatient old man; \"you were about opening your mind more fully on that matter the day you got in; but I did not think it becoming in an old soldier to be talking of nuptial blessings and wedding jokes when the enemies of his king were likely to be unbidden guests at the feast! But I was wrong, Duncan, boy, I was wrong there; and I am now ready to hear what you have to say.\"\n\n\"Notwithstanding the pleasure your assurance gives me, dear sir, I have just now a message from Montcalm--\"\n\n\"Let the Frenchman and all his host go to the devil, sir?\" exclaimed the hasty veteran. \"He is not yet master of William Henry, nor shall he ever be, provided Webb proves himself the man he should. No, sir! thank Heaven, we are not yet in such a strait that it can be said Munro is too much pressed to discharge the little domestic duties of his own family. Your mother was the only child of my bosom friend, Duncan; and I'll just give you a hearing, though all the knights of St. Louis were in a body at the sally-port, with the French saint at their head, craving to speak a word under favor. A pretty degree of knighthood, sir, is that which can be bought with sugar-hogsheads! and then your two-penny marquisates! The thistle is the order for dignity and antiquity; the veritable _nemo me impune lacessit_ of chivalry! Ye had ancestors in that degree, Duncan, and they were an ornament to the nobles of Scotland.\"\n\nHeyward, who perceived that his superior took a malicious pleasure in exhibiting his contempt for the message of the French general, was fain to humor a spleen that he knew would be short-lived; he therefore replied with as much indifference as he could assume on such a subject,--\n\n\"My request, as you know, sir, went so far as to presume to the honor of being your son.\"\n\n\"Ay, boy, you found words to make yourself very plainly comprehended. But, let me ask ye, sir, have you been as intelligible to the girl?\"\n\n\"On my honor, no,\" exclaimed Duncan, warmly; \"there would have been an abuse of a confided trust, had I taken advantage of my situation for such a purpose.\"\n\n\"Your notions are those of a gentleman, Major Heyward, and well enough in their place. But Cora Munro is a maiden too discreet, and of a mind too elevated and improved, to need the guardianship even of a father.\"\n\n\"Cora!\"\n\"Ay--Cora! we are talking of your pretensions to Miss Munro, are we not, sir?\"\n\n\"I--I--I was not conscious of having mentioned her name,\" said Duncan, stammering.\n\n\"And to marry whom, then, did you wish my consent, Major Heyward?\" demanded the old soldier, erecting himself in the dignity of offended feeling.\n\n\"You have another, and not less lovely child.\"\n\n\"Alice!\" exclaimed the father, in an astonishment equal to that with which Duncan had just repeated the name of her sister.\n\n\"Such was the direction of my wishes, sir.\"\n\nThe young man awaited in silence the result of the extraordinary effect produced by a communication which, as it now appeared, was so unexpected. For several minutes Munro paced the chamber with long and rapid strides, his rigid features working convulsively, and every faculty seemingly absorbed in the musings of his own mind. At length, he paused directly in front of Heyward, and riveting his eyes upon those of the other, he said, with a lip that quivered violently,--\n\n\"Duncan Heyward, I have loved you for the sake of him whose blood is in your veins; I have loved you for your own good qualities; and I have loved you, because I thought you would contribute to the happiness of my child. But all this love would turn to hatred, were I assured that what I so much apprehend is true.\"\n\n\"God forbid that any act or thought of mine should lead to such a change!\" exclaimed the young man, whose eye never quailed under the penetrating look it encountered. Without adverting the impossibility of the other's comprehending those feelings which were hid in his own bosom, Munro suffered himself to be appeased by the unaltered countenance he met, and with a voice sensibly softened, he continued,--\n\n\"You would be my son, Duncan, and you're ignorant of the history of the man you wish to call your father. Sit ye down, young man, and I will open to you the wounds of a seared heart, in as few words as may be suitable.\"\n\nBy this time, the message of Montcalm was as much forgotten by him who bore it as by the man for whose ears it was intended. Each drew a chair, and while the veteran communed a few moments with his own thoughts, apparently in sadness, the youth suppressed his impatience in a look and attitude of respectful attention. At length the former spoke:--\n\n\"You'll know, already, Major Heyward, that my family was both ancient and honorable,\" commenced the Scotsman; \"though it might not altogether be endowed with that amount of wealth that should correspond with its degree. I was, may be, such an one as yourself when I plighted my faith to Alice Graham, the only child of a neighboring laird of some estate. But the connection was disagreeable to her father, on more accounts than my poverty. I did therefore what an honest man should--restored the maiden her troth, and departed the country in the service of my king. I had seen many regions, and had shed much blood in different lands, before duty called me to the islands of the West Indies. There it was my lot to form a connection with one who in time became my wife, and the mother of Cora. She was the daughter of a gentleman of those isles, by a lady whose misfortune it was, if you will,\" said the old man, proudly, \"to be descended, remotely, from that unfortunate class who are so basely enslaved to administer to the wants of a luxurious people. Ay, sir, that is a curse entailed on Scotland by her unnatural union with a foreign and trading people. But could I find a man among them who would dare to reflect on my child, he should feel the weight of a father's anger! Ha! Major Heyward, you are yourself born at the south, where these unfortunate beings are considered of a race inferior to your own.\"\n\n\"'Tis most unfortunately true, sir,\" said Duncan, unable any longer to prevent his eyes from sinking to the floor in embarrassment.\n\n\"And you cast it on my child as a reproach! You scorn to mingle the blood of the Heywards with one so degraded--lovely and virtuous though she be?\" fiercely demanded the jealous parent.\n\n\"Heaven protect me from a prejudice so unworthy of my reason!\" returned Duncan, at the same time conscious of such a feeling, and that as deeply rooted as if it had been ingrafted in his nature. \"The sweetness, the beauty, the witchery of your younger daughter, Colonel Munro, might explain my motives, without imputing to me this injustice.\"\n\n\"Ye are right, sir,\" returned the old man, again changing his tones to those of gentleness, or rather softness; \"the girl is the image of what her mother was at her years, and before she had become acquainted with grief. When death deprived me of my wife I returned to Scotland, enriched by the marriage; and would you think it, Duncan! The suffering angel had remained in the heartless state of celibacy twenty long years, and that for the sake of a man who could forget her! She did more, sir; she over-looked my want of faith, and all difficulties being now removed, she took me for her husband.\"\n\n\"And became the mother of Alice?\" exclaimed Duncan, with an eagerness that might have proved dangerous at a moment when the thoughts of Munro were less occupied than at present.\n\n\"She did, indeed,\" said the old man, \"and dearly did she pay for the blessing she bestowed. But she is a saint in heaven, sir; and it ill becomes one whose foot rests on the grave to mourn a lot so blessed. I had her but a single year, though; a short term of happiness for one who had seen her youth fade in hopeless pining.\"\n\nThere was something so commanding in the distress of the old man, that Heyward did not dare to venture a syllable of consolation. Munro sat utterly unconscious of the other's presence, his features exposed and working with the anguish of his regrets, while heavy tears fell from his eyes, and rolled unheeded from his cheeks to the floor. At length he moved, as if suddenly recovering his recollection; when he arose, and taking a single turn across the room, he approached his companion with an air of military grandeur, and demanded,--\n\n\"Have you not, Major Heyward, some communication that I should hear from the Marquis de Montcalm?\"\n\nDuncan started, in his turn, and immediately commenced, in an embarrassed voice, the half-forgotten message. It is unnecessary to dwell upon the evasive, though polite manner, with which the French general had eluded every attempt of Heyward to worm from him the purport of the communication he had proposed making, or on the decided, though still polished message, by which he now gave his enemy to understand, that unless he chose to receive it in person, he should not receive it at all. As Munro listened to the detail of Duncan, the excited feelings of the father gradually gave way before the obligations of his station, and when the other was done, he saw before him nothing but the veteran, swelling with the wounded feelings of a soldier.\n\n\"You have said enough, Major Heyward!\" exclaimed the angry old man: \"enough to make a volume of commentary on French civility. Here has this gentleman invited me to a conference, and when I send him a capable substitute, for ye're all that, Duncan, though your years are but few, he answers me with a riddle.\"\n\n\"He may have thought less favorably of the substitute, my dear sir; and you will remember that the invitation, which he now repeats, was to the commandant of the works, and not to his second.\"\n\n\"Well, sir, is not a substitute clothed with all the power and dignity of him who grants the commission? He wishes to confer with Munro! Faith, sir, I have much inclination to indulge the man, if it should only be to let him behold the firm countenance we maintain in spite of his numbers and his summons. There might be no bad policy in such a stroke, young man.\"\n\nDuncan, who believed it of the last importance that they should speedily come at the contents of the letter borne by the scout, gladly encouraged this idea.\n\n\"Without doubt, he could gather no confidence by witnessing our indifference,\" he said.\n\n\"You never said truer word. I could wish, sir, that he would visit the works in open day, and in the form of a storming party: that is the least failing method of proving the countenance of an enemy, and would be far preferable to the battering system he has chosen. The beauty and manliness of warfare has been much deformed, Major Heyward, by the arts of your Monsieur Vauban. Our ancestors were far above such scientific cowardice!\"\n\n\"It may be very true, sir; but we are now obliged to repel art by art. What is your pleasure in the matter of the interview?\"\n\n\"I will meet the Frenchman, and that without fear or delay; promptly; sir, as becomes a servant of my royal master. Go, Major Heyward, and give them a flourish of the music; and send out a messenger to let them know who is coming. We will follow with a small guard, for such respect is due to one who holds the honor of his king in keeping; and harkee, Duncan,\" he added, in a half whisper, though they were alone, \"it may be prudent to have some aid at hand, in case there should be treachery at the bottom of it all.\"\n\nThe young man availed himself of this order to quit the apartment; and, as the day was fast coming to a close, he hastened, without delay, to make the necessary arrangements. A very few minutes only were necessary to parade a few files, and to despatch an orderly with a flag to announce the approach of the commandant of the fort. When Duncan had done both these, he led the guard to the sally-port, near which he found his superior ready, waiting his appearance. As soon as the usual ceremonials of a military departure were observed, the veteran and his more youthful companion left the fortress, attended by the escort.\n\nThey had proceeded only a hundred yards from the works, when the little array which attended the French general to the conference, was seen issuing from the hollow way, which formed the bed of a brook that ran between the batteries of the besiegers and the fort. From the moment that Munro left his own works to appear in front of his enemies, his air had been grand, and his step and countenance highly military. The instant he caught a glimpse of the white plume that waved in the hat of Montcalm, his eye lighted, and age no longer appeared to possess any influence over his vast and still muscular person.\n\n\"Speak to the boys to be watchful, sir,\" he said, in an undertone, to Duncan; \"and to look well to their flints and steel, for one is never safe with a servant of these Louises; at the same time, we will show them the front of men in deep security. Ye'll understand me, Major Heyward!\"\n\nHe was interrupted by the clamor of a drum from the approaching Frenchmen, which was immediately answered, when each party pushed an orderly in advance, bearing a white flag, and the wary Scotsman halted, with his guard close at his back. As soon as this slight salutation had passed, Montcalm moved towards them with a quick but graceful step, baring his head to the veteran, and dropping his spotless plume nearly to the earth in courtesy. If the air of Munro was more commanding and manly, it wanted both the ease and insinuating polish of that of the Frenchman. Neither spoke for a few moments, each regarding the other with curious and interested eyes. Then, as became his superior rank and the nature of the interview, Montcalm broke the silence. After uttering the usual words of greeting, he turned to Duncan, and continued with a smile of recognition, speaking always in French,--\n[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n\n_As soon as this slight salutation had passed, Montcalm moved towards them with a quick but graceful step, baring his head to the veteran, and dropping his spotless plume nearly to the earth in courtesy_]\n\n\"I am rejoiced, monsieur, that you have given us the pleasure of your company on this occasion. There will be no necessity to employ an ordinary interpreter; for, in your hands, I feel the same security as if I spoke your language myself.\"\nDuncan acknowledged the compliment, when Montcalm, turning to his guard, which, in imitation of that of their enemies, pressed close upon him, continued,--\n\n\"En arriere, mes enfans--il fait chaud; retirez-vous un peu.\"\n\nBefore Major Heyward would imitate this proof of confidence, he glanced his eyes around the plain, and beheld with uneasiness the numerous dusky groups of savages, who looked out from the margin of the surrounding woods, curious spectators of the interview.\n\n\"Monsieur de Montcalm will readily acknowledge the difference in our situation,\" he said, with some embarrassment, pointing at the same time towards those dangerous foes, who were to be seen in almost every direction. \"Were we to dismiss our guard, we should stand here at the mercy of our enemies.\"\n\n\"Monsieur, you have the plighted faith of _un gentilhomme Francais_; for your safety,\" returned Montcalm, laying his hand impressively on his heart; \"it should suffice.\"\n\n\"It shall. Fall back,\" Duncan added to the officer who led the escort; \"fall back, sir, beyond hearing, and wait for orders.\"\n\nMunro witnessed this movement with manifest uneasiness; nor did he fail to demand an instant explanation.\n\n\"Is it not our interest, sir, to betray no distrust?\" retorted Duncan. \"Monsieur de Montcalm pledges his word for our safety, and I have ordered the men to withdraw a little, in order to prove how much we depend on his assurance.\"\n\n\"It may be all right, sir, but I have no overweening reliance on the faith of these marquesses, or marquis, as they call themselves. Their patents of nobility are too common to be certain that they bear the seal of true honor.\"\n\n\"You forget, dear sir, that we confer with an officer distinguished alike in Europe and America for his deeds. From a soldier of his reputation we can have nothing to apprehend.\"\n\nThe old man made a gesture of resignation, though his rigid features still betrayed his obstinate adherence to a distrust, which he derived from a sort of hereditary contempt of his enemy, rather than from any present signs which might warrant so uncharitable a feeling. Montcalm waited patiently until this little dialogue in demi-voice was ended, when he drew nigher, and opened the subject of their conference.\n\n\"I have solicited this interview from your superior, monsieur,\" he said, \"because I believe he will allow himself to be persuaded that he has already done everything which is necessary for the honor of his prince, and will not listen to the admonitions of humanity. I will forever bear testimony that his resistance has been gallant, and was continued as long as there was hope.\"\n\nWhen this opening was translated to Munro, he answered with dignity, but with sufficient courtesy,--\n\n\"However I may prize such testimony from Monsieur Montcalm, it will be more valuable when it shall be better merited.\"\n\nThe French general smiled, as Duncan gave him the purport of this reply, and observed,--\n\n\"What is now so freely accorded to approved courage, may be refused to useless obstinacy. Monsieur would wish to see my camp, and witness, for himself, our numbers, and the impossibility of his resisting them, with success?\"\n\n\"I know that the king of France is well served,\" returned the unmoved Scotsman, as soon as Duncan ended his translation; \"but my own royal master has as many and as faithful troops.\"\n\n\"Though not at hand, fortunately for us,\" said Montcalm, without waiting, in his ardor, for the interpreter. \"There is a destiny in war, to which a brave man knows how to submit, with the same courage that he faces his foes.\"\n\n\"Had I been conscious that Monsieur Montcalm was master of the English, I should have spared myself the trouble of so awkward a translation,\" said the vexed Duncan, dryly; remembering instantly his recent by-play with Munro.\n\n\"Your pardon, monsieur,\" rejoined the Frenchman, suffering a slight color to appear on his dark cheek. \"There is a vast difference between understanding and speaking a foreign tongue; you will, therefore, please to assist me still.\" Then after a short pause, he added, \"These hills afford us every opportunity of reconnoitring your works, messieurs, and I am possibly as well acquainted with their weak condition as you can be yourselves.\"\n\n\"Ask the French general if his glasses can reach to the Hudson,\" said Munro, proudly; \"and if he knows when and where to expect the army of Webb.\"\n\n\"Let General Webb be his own interpreter,\" returned the politic Montcalm, suddenly extending an open letter towards Munro, as he spoke; \"you will there learn, monsieur, that his movements are not likely to prove embarrassing to my army.\"\n\nThe veteran seized the offered paper, without waiting for Duncan to translate the speech, and with an eagerness that betrayed how important he deemed its contents. As his eye passed hastily over the words, his countenance changed from its look of military pride to one of deep chagrin: his lip began to quiver; and, suffering the paper to fall from his hand, his head dropped upon his chest, like that of a man whose hopes were withered at a single blow. Duncan caught the letter from the ground, and without apology for the liberty he took, he read at a glance its cruel purport. Their common superior, so far from encouraging them to resist, advised a speedy surrender, urging in the plainest language as a reason, the utter impossibility of his sending a single man to their rescue.\n\n\"Here is no deception!\" exclaimed Duncan, examining the billet both inside and out; \"this is the signature of Webb, and must be the captured letter.\"\n\n\"The man has betrayed me!\" Munro at length bitterly exclaimed: \"he has brought dishonor to the door of one where disgrace was never before known to dwell, and shame has he heaped heavily on my gray hairs.\"\n\n\"Say not so,\" cried Duncan; \"we are yet masters of the fort, and of our honor. Let us then sell our lives at such a rate as shall make our enemies believe the purchase too dear.\"\n\n\"Boy, I thank thee,\" exclaimed the old man, rousing himself from his stupor; \"you have, for once, reminded Munro of his duty. We will go back, and dig our graves behind those ramparts.\"\n\n\"Messieurs,\" said Montcalm, advancing towards them a step, in generous interest, \"you little know Louis de St. Veran, if you believe him capable of profiting by this letter to humble brave men, or to build up a dishonest reputation for himself. Listen to my terms before you leave me.\"\n\n\"What says the Frenchman?\" demanded the veteran, sternly; \"does he make a merit of having captured a scout, with a note from headquarters? Sir, he had better raise this siege, to go and sit down before Edward if he wishes to frighten his enemy with words.\"\n\nDuncan explained the other's meaning.\n\n\"Monsieur de Montcalm, we will hear you,\" the veteran added, more calmly, as Duncan ended.\n\n\"To retain the fort is now impossible,\" said his liberal enemy; \"it is necessary to the interests of my master that it should be destroyed; but, as for yourselves, and your brave comrades, there is no privilege dear to a soldier that shall be denied.\"\n\n\"Our colors?\" demanded Heyward.\n\n\"Carry them to England, and show them to your king.\"\n\n\"Our arms?\"\n\n\"Keep them; none can use them better.\"\n\n\"Our march; the surrender of the place?\"\n\n\"Shall all be done in a way most honorable to yourselves.\"\n\nDuncan now turned to explain these proposals to his commander, who heard him with amazement, and a sensibility that was deeply touched by such unusual and unexpected generosity.\n\n\"Go you, Duncan,\" he said; \"go with this marquess, as indeed marquess he should be; go to his marquee and arrange it all. I have lived to see two things in my old age, that never did I expect to behold. An Englishman afraid to support a friend, and a Frenchman too honest to profit by his advantage.\"\n\nSo saying, the veteran again dropped his head to his chest, and returned slowly towards the fort, exhibiting, by the dejection of his air, to the anxious garrison, a harbinger of evil tidings.\n\nFrom the shock of this unexpected blow the haughty feelings of Munro never recovered; but from that moment there commenced a change in his determined character, which accompanied him to a speedy grave. Duncan remained to settle the terms of the capitulation. He was seen to re-enter the works during the first watches of the night, and immediately after a private conference with the commandant, to leave them again, It was then openly announced, that hostilities must cease--Munro having signed a treaty, by which the place was to be yielded to the enemy, with the morning; the garrison to retain their arms, their colors, and their baggage, and consequently, according to military opinion, their honor.\n\n\"Weave we the woof. The thread is spun. The web is wove. The work is done.\"\n\nThe hostile armies, which lay in the wilds of the Horican, passed the night of the 9th of August, 1757, much in the manner they would had they encountered on the fairest fields of Europe. While the conquered were still, sullen, and dejected, the victors triumphed. But there are limits alike to grief and joy; and long before the watches of the morning came, the stillness of those boundless woods was only broken by a gay call from some exulting young Frenchman of the advanced pickets, or a menacing challenge from the fort, which sternly forbade the approach of any hostile footsteps before the stipulated moment. Even these occasional threatening sounds ceased to be heard in that dull hour which precedes the day, at which period a listener might have sought in vain any evidence of the presence of those armed powers that then slumbered on the shores of the \"holy lake.\"\n\nIt was during these moments of deep silence, that the canvas which concealed the entrance to a spacious marquee in the French encampment was shoved aside, and a man issued from beneath the drapery into the open air. He was enveloped in a cloak that might have been intended as a protection from the chilling damps of the woods, but which served equally well as a mantle, to conceal his person. He was permitted to pass the grenadier, who watched over the slumbers of the French commander, without interruption, the man making the usual salute which betokens military deference, as the other passed swiftly through the little city of tents, in the direction of William Henry. Whenever this unknown individual encountered one of the numberless sentinels who crossed his path, his answer was prompt, and as it appeared satisfactory; for he was uniformly allowed to proceed, without further interrogation.\n\nWith the exception of such repeated, but brief interruptions, he had moved, silently, from the centre of the camp, to its most advanced outposts, when he drew nigh the soldier who held his watch nearest to the works of the enemy. As he approached he was received with the usual challenge,--\n\n\"Qui vive?\"\n\n\"France,\" was the reply.\n\n\"Le mot d'ordre?\"\n\n\"La victoire,\" said the other, drawing so nigh as to be heard in a loud whisper.\n\n\"C'est bien,\" returned the sentinel, throwing his musket from the charge to his shoulder; \"vous vous promenez bien matin, monsieur!\"\n\n\"Il est necessaire d'etre vigilant, mon enfant,\" the other observed, dropping a fold of his cloak, and looking the soldier close in the face, as he passed him, still continuing his way towards the British fortification. The man started; his arms rattled heavily, as he threw them forward, in the lowest and most respectful salute; and when he had again recovered his piece, he turned to walk his post, muttering between his teeth,--\n\n\"Il faut etre vigilant, en verite! je crois que nous avons la, un caporal qui ne dort jamais!\"\n\nThe officer proceeded, without affecting to hear the words which escaped the sentinel in his surprise; nor did he again pause until he had reached the low strand, and in a somewhat dangerous vicinity to the western water bastion of the fort. The light of an obscure moon was just sufficient to render objects, though dim, perceptible in their outlines. He, therefore, took the precaution to place himself against the trunk of a tree, where he leaned for many minutes, and seemed to contemplate the dark and silent mounds of the English works in profound attention. His gaze at the ramparts was not that of a curious or idle spectator; but his looks wandered from point to point, denoting his knowledge of military usages, and betraying that his search was not unaccompanied by distrust. At length he appeared satisfied; and having cast his eyes impatiently upwards towards the summit of the eastern mountain, as if anticipating the approach of the morning, he was in the act of turning on his footsteps, when a light sound on the nearest angle of the bastion caught his ear, and induced him to remain.\n\nJust then a figure was seen to approach the edge of the rampart, where it stood, apparently contemplating in its turn the distant tents of the French encampment. Its head was then turned towards the east, as though equally anxious for the appearance of light, when the form leaned against the mound, and seemed to gaze upon the glassy expanse of the waters, which, like a submarine firmament, glittered with its thousand mimic stars. The melancholy air, the hour, together with the vast frame of the man who thus leaned, in musing, against the English ramparts, left no doubt as to his person, in the mind of his observant spectator. Delicacy, no less than prudence, now urged him to retire; and he had moved cautiously round the body of the tree for that purpose, when another sound drew his attention, and once more arrested his footsteps. It was a low and almost inaudible movement of the water, and was succeeded by a grating of pebbles one against the other. In a moment he saw a dark form rise, as it were out of the lake, and steal without further noise to the land, within a few feet of the place where he himself stood. A rifle next slowly rose between his eyes and the watery mirror; but before it could be discharged his own hand was on the lock.\n\n\"Hugh!\" exclaimed the savage, whose treacherous aim was so singularly and so unexpectedly interrupted.\n\nWithout making any reply, the French officer laid his hand on the shoulder of the Indian, and led him in profound silence to a distance from the spot, where their subsequent dialogue might have proved dangerous, and where it seemed that one of them, at least, sought a victim. Then, throwing open his cloak, so as to expose his uniform and the cross of St. Louis which was suspended at his breast, Montcalm sternly demanded,--\n\n\"What means this! Does not my son know that the hatchet is buried between the English and his Canadian Father?\"\n\n\"What can the Hurons do?\" returned the savage, speaking also, though imperfectly, in the French language. \"Not a warrior has a scalp, and the pale-faces make friends!\"\n\n\"Ha! Le Renard Subtil! Methinks this is an excess of zeal for a friend who was so late an enemy! How many suns have set since Le Renard struck the war-post of the English?\"\n\n\"Where is that sun!\" demanded the sullen savage. \"Behind the hill; and it is dark and cold. But when he comes again, it will be bright and warm. Le Subtil is the sun of his tribe. There have been clouds, and many mountains between him and his nation; but now he shines, and it is a clear sky!\"\n\n\"That Le Renard has power with his people, I well know,\" said Montcalm; \"for yesterday he hunted for their scalps, and to-day they hear him at the council-fire.\"\n\n\"Magua is a great chief.\"\n\n\"Let him prove it, by teaching his nation how to conduct itself towards our new friends.\"\n\n\"Why did the chief of the Canadas bring his young men into the woods, and fire his cannon at the earthen house?\" demanded the subtle Indian.\n\n\"To subdue it. My master owns the land, and your father has been ordered to drive off these English squatters. They have consented to go, and now he calls them enemies no longer.\"\n\n\"'Tis well. Magua took the hatchet to color it with blood. It is now bright; when it is red, it shall be buried.\"\n\n\"But Magua is pledged not to sully the lilies of France. The enemies of the great king across the salt lake are his enemies; his friends, the friends of the Hurons.\"\n\n\"Friends!\" repeated the Indian, in scorn. \"Let his father give Magua a hand.\"\n\nMontcalm, who felt that his influence over the warlike tribes he had gathered was to be maintained by concession rather than by power, complied reluctantly with the other's request. The savage placed the finger of the French commander on a deep scar in his bosom, and then exultingly demanded,--\n\n\"Does my father know that?\"\n\n\"What warrior does not? 'tis where a leaden bullet has cut.\"\n\n\"And this?\" continued the Indian, who had turned his naked back to the other, his body being without its usual calico mantle.\n\n\"This!--my son has been sadly injured, here; who has done this?\"\n\n\"Magua slept hard in the English wigwams, and the sticks have left their mark,\" returned the savage, with a hollow laugh, which did not conceal the fierce temper that nearly choked him. Then recollecting himself, with sudden and native dignity, he added, \"Go; teach your young men, it is peace. Le Renard Subtil knows how to speak to a Huron warrior.\"\n\nWithout deigning to bestow further words, or to wait for any answer, the savage cast his rifle into the hollow of his arm, and moved silently through the encampment towards the woods where his own tribe was known to lie. Every few yards as he proceeded he was challenged by the sentinels; but he stalked sullenly onward, utterly disregarding the summons of the soldiers, who only spared his life because they knew the air and tread no less than the obstinate daring of an Indian.\n\nMontcalm lingered long and melancholy on the strand, where he had been left by his companion, brooding deeply on the temper which his ungovernable ally had just discovered. Already had his fair fame been tarnished by one horrid scene, and in circumstances fearfully resembling those under which he now found himself. As he mused he became keenly sensible of the deep responsibility they assume who disregard the means to attain their end, and of all the danger of setting in motion an engine which it exceeds human power to control. Then shaking off a train of reflections that he accounted a weakness in such a moment of triumph, he retraced his steps towards his tent, giving the order as he passed, to make the signal that should arouse the army from its slumbers.\n\nThe first tap of the French drums was echoed from the bosom of the fort, and presently the valley was filled with the strains of martial music, rising long, thrilling, and lively above the rattling accompaniment. The horns of the victors sounded merry and cheerful flourishes, until the last laggard of the camp was at his post; but the instant the British fifes had blown their shrill signal, they became mute. In the meantime the day had dawned, and when the line of the French army was ready to receive its general, the rays of a brilliant sun were glancing along the glittering array. Then that success, which was already so well known, was officially announced; the favored band who were selected to guard the gates of the fort were detailed, and defiled before their chief; the signal of their approach was given, and all the usual preparations for a change of masters were ordered and executed directly under the guns of the contested works.\n\nA very different scene presented itself within the lines of the Anglo-American army. As soon as the warning signal was given, it exhibited all the signs of a hurried and forced departure. The sullen soldiers shouldered their empty tubes and fell into their places, like men whose blood had been heated by the past contest, and who only desired the opportunity to revenge an indignity which was still wounding to their pride, concealed as it was under all the observances of military etiquette. Women and children ran from place to place, some bearing the scanty remnants of their baggage, and others searching in the ranks for those countenances they looked up to for protection.\n\nMunro appeared among his silent troops firm but dejected. It was evident that the unexpected blow had struck deep into his heart, though he struggled to sustain his misfortune with the port of man.\n\nDuncan was touched at the quiet and impressive exhibition of his grief. He had discharged his own duty, and he now pressed to the side of the old man, to know in what particular he might serve him.\n\n\"My daughters,\" was the brief but expressive reply.\n\n\"Good heavens! are not arrangements already made for their convenience?\"\n\n\"To-day I am only a soldier, Major Heyward,\" said the veteran. \"All that you see here, claim alike to be my children.\"\n\nDuncan had heard enough. Without losing one of those moments which had now become so precious, he flew towards the quarters of Munro, in quest of the sisters. He found them on the threshold of the low edifice, already prepared to depart, and surrounded by a clamorous and weeping assemblage of their own sex, that had gathered about the place, with a sort of instinctive consciousness that it was the point most likely to be protected. Though the cheeks of Cora were pale, and her countenance anxious, she had lost none of her firmness; but the eyes of Alice were inflamed, and betrayed how long and bitterly she had wept. They both, however, received the young man with undisguised pleasure; the former, for a novelty, being the first to speak.\n\n\"The fort is lost,\" she said, with a melancholy smile; \"though our good name, I trust, remains.\"\n\n\"'Tis brighter than ever. But, dearest Miss Munro, it is time to think less of others, and to make some provision for yourself. Military usage,--pride,--that pride on which you so much value yourself, demands that your father and I should for a little while continue with the troops. Then where to seek a proper protector for you against the confusion and chances of such a scene?\"\n\n\"None is necessary,\" returned Cora; \"who will dare to injure or insult the daughter of such a father, at a time like this?\"\n\n\"I would not leave you alone,\" continued the youth, looking about him in a hurried manner, \"for the command of the best regiment in the pay of the king. Remember, our Alice is not gifted with all your firmness, and God only knows the terror she might endure.\"\n\n\"You may be right,\" Cora replied, smiling again, but far more sadly than before. \"Listen! chance has already sent us a friend when he is most needed.\"\n\nDuncan did listen, and on the instant comprehended her meaning. The low and serious sounds of the sacred music, so well known to the eastern provinces, caught his ear, and instantly drew him to an apartment in an adjacent building, which had already been deserted by its customary tenants. There he found David, pouring out his pious feelings, through the only medium in which he ever indulged. Duncan waited, until, by the cessation of the movement of the hand, he believed the strain was ended, when, by touching his shoulder, he drew the attention of the other to himself, and in a few words explained his wishes.\n\n\"Even so,\" replied the single-minded disciple of the King of Israel, when the young man had ended; \"I have found much that is comely and melodious in the maidens, and it is fitting that we who have consorted in so much peril, should abide together in peace. I will attend them, when I have completed my morning praise, to which nothing is now wanting but the doxology. Wilt thou bear a part, friend? The metre is common, and the tune, 'Southwell.'\"\n\nThen, extending the little volume, and giving the pitch of the air anew with considerate attention, David recommenced and finished his strains, with a fixedness of manner that it was not easy to interrupt. Heyward was fain to wait until the verse was ended; when, seeing David relieving himself from the spectacles, and replacing the book, he continued,--\n\n\"It will be your duty to see that none dare to approach the ladies with any rude intention, or to offer insult or taunt at the misfortune of their brave father. In this task you will be seconded by the domestics of their household.\"\n\n\"Even so.\"\n\n\"It is possible that the Indians and stragglers of the enemy may intrude, in which case you will remind them of the terms of the capitulation, and threaten to report their conduct to Montcalm. A word will suffice.\"\n\n\"If not, I have that here which shall,\" returned David, exhibiting his book, with an air in which meekness and confidence were singularly blended. \"Here are words which, uttered, or rather thundered, with proper emphasis, and in measured time, shall quiet the most unruly temper:--\n\n\"'Why rage the heathen furiously!'\"--\n\n\"Enough,\" said Heyward, interrupting the burst of his musical invocation: \"we understand each other; it is time that we should now assume our respective duties.\"\n\nGamut cheerfully assented, and together they sought the females. Cora received her new, and somewhat extraordinary protector, courteously at least; and even the pallid features of Alice lighted again with some of their native archness as she thanked Heyward for his care. Duncan took occasion to assure them he had done the best that circumstances permitted, and, as he believed, quite enough for the security of their feelings; of danger there was none. He then spoke gladly of his intention to rejoin them the moment he had led the advance a few miles towards the Hudson, and immediately took his leave.\n\nBy this time the signal of departure had been given, and the head of the English column was in motion. The sisters started at the sound, and glancing their eyes around, they saw the white uniforms of the French grenadiers, who had already taken possession of the gates of the fort. At that moment, an enormous cloud seemed to pass suddenly above their heads, and looking upward, they discovered that they stood beneath the wide folds of the standard of France.\n\n\"Let us go,\" said Cora; \"this is no longer a fit place for the children of an English officer.\"\n\nAlice clung to the arm of her sister, and together they left the parade, accompanied by the moving throng that surrounded them.\n\nAs they passed the gates, the French officers, who had learned their rank, bowed often and low, forbearing, however, to intrude those attentions which they saw, with peculiar tact, might not be agreeable. As every vehicle and each beast of burden was occupied by the sick and wounded, Cora had decided to endure the fatigues of a foot march, rather than interfere with their comforts. Indeed, many a maimed and feeble soldier was compelled to drag his exhausted limbs in the rear of the columns, for the want of the necessary means of conveyance, in that wilderness. The whole, however, was in motion; the weak and wounded, groaning, and in suffering; their comrades, silent and sullen; and the women and children in terror, they knew not of what.\n\nAs the confused and timid throng left the protecting mounds of the fort, and issued on the open plain, the whole scene was at once presented to their eyes. At a little distance on the right, and somewhat in the rear, the French army stood to their arms, Montcalm having collected his parties, so soon as his guards had possession of the works. They were attentive but silent observers of the proceedings of the vanquished, failing in none of the stipulated military honors, and offering no taunt or insult, in their success, to their less fortunate foes. Living masses of the English, to the amount in the whole of near three thousand, were moving slowly across the plain, towards the common centre, and gradually approached each other, as they converged to the point of their march, a vista cut through the lofty trees, where the road to the Hudson entered the forest. Along the sweeping borders of the woods, hung a dark cloud of savages, eying the passage of their enemies, and hovering, at a distance, like vultures, who were only kept from swooping on their prey, by the presence and restraint of a superior army. A few had straggled among the conquered columns, where they stalked in sullen discontent; attentive, though, as yet, passive observers of the moving multitude.\n\nThe advance, with Heyward at its head, had already reached the defile, and was slowly disappearing, when the attention of Cora was drawn to a collection of stragglers, by the sounds of contention. A truant provincial was paying the forfeit of his disobedience, by being plundered of those very effects which had caused him to desert his place in the ranks. The man was of powerful frame, and too avaricious to part with his goods without a struggle. Individuals from either party interfered; the one side to prevent, and the other to aid in the robbery. Voices grew loud and angry, and a hundred savages appeared, as it were by magic, where a dozen only had been seen a minute before. It was then that Cora saw the form of Magua gliding among his countrymen, and speaking with his fatal and artful eloquence. The mass of women and children stopped, and hovered together like alarmed and fluttering birds. But the cupidity of the Indian was soon gratified, and the different bodies again moved slowly onward.\n\nThe savages now fell back, and seemed content to let their enemies advance without further molestation. But as the female crowd approached them, the gaudy colors of a shawl attracted the eyes of a wild and untutored Huron. He advanced to seize it, without the least hesitation. The woman, more in terror than through love of the ornament, wrapped her child in the coveted article, and folded both more closely to her bosom. Cora was in the act of speaking, with an intent to advise the woman to abandon the trifle, when the savage relinquished his hold of the shawl, and tore the screaming infant from her arms. Abandoning everything to the greedy grasp of those around her, the mother darted, with distraction in her mien, to reclaim her child. The Indian smiled grimly, and extended one hand, in sign of a willingness to exchange, while with the other, he flourished the babe over his head, holding it by the feet as if to enhance the value of the ransom.\n\n\"Here--here--there--all--any--everything!\" exclaimed the breathless woman; tearing the lighter articles of dress from her person, with ill-directed and trembling fingers; \"take all, but give me my babe!\"\n\nThe savage spurned the worthless rags, and perceiving that the shawl had already become a prize to another, his bantering but sullen smile changing to a gleam of ferocity, he dashed the head of the infant against a rock, and cast its quivering remains to her very feet. For an instant, the mother stood, like a statue of despair, looking wildly down at the unseemly object, which had so lately nestled in her bosom and smiled in her face; and then she raised her eyes and countenance towards heaven, as if calling on God to curse the perpetrator of the foul deed. She was spared the sin of such a prayer; for, maddened at his disappointment, and excited at the sight of blood, the Huron mercifully drove his tomahawk into her own brain. The mother sank under the blow, and fell, grasping at her child, in death, with the same engrossing love that had caused her to cherish it when living.\n\nAt that dangerous moment Magua placed his hands to his mouth, and raised the fatal and appalling whoop. The scattered Indians started at the well-known cry, as coursers bound at the signal to quit the goal; and, directly, there arose such a yell along the plain, and through the arches of the wood, as seldom burst from human lips before. They who heard it listened with a curdling horror at the heart, little inferior to that dread which may be expected to attend the blasts of the final summons.\n\nMore than two thousand raving savages broke from the forest at the signal, and threw themselves across the fatal plain with instinctive alacrity. We shall not dwell on the revolting horrors that succeeded. Death was everywhere, and in his most terrific and disgusting aspects. Resistance only served to inflame the murderers, who inflicted their furious blows long after their victims were beyond the power of their resentment. The flow of blood might be likened to the outbreaking of a torrent; and, as the natives became heated and maddened by the sight, many among them even kneeled to the earth, and drank freely, exultingly, hellishly, of the crimson tide.\n\nThe trained bodies of the troops threw themselves quickly into solid masses, endeavoring to awe their assailants by the imposing appearance of a military front. The experiment in some measure succeeded, though far too many suffered their unloaded muskets to be torn from their hands, in the vain hope of appeasing the savages.\n\nIn such a scene none had leisure to note the fleeting moments. It might have been ten minutes (it seemed an age), that the sisters had stood riveted to one spot, horror-stricken, and nearly helpless. When the first blow was struck, their screaming companions had pressed upon them in a body, rendering flight impossible; and now that fear or death had scattered most, if not all, from around them, they saw no avenue open, but such as conducted to the tomahawks of their foes. On every side arose shrieks, groans, exhortations, and curses. At this moment Alice caught a glimpse of the vast form of her father, moving rapidly across the plain, in the direction of the French army. He was, in truth, proceeding to Montcalm, fearless of every danger, to claim the tardy escort for which he had before conditioned. Fifty glittering axes and barbed spears were offered unheeded at his life, but the savages respected his rank and calmness, even in their fury. The dangerous weapons were brushed aside by the still nervous arm of the veteran, or fell of themselves, after menacing an act that it would seem no one had courage to perform. Fortunately, the vindictive Magua was searching for his victim in the very band the veteran had just quitted.\n\n\"Father--father--we are here!\" shrieked Alice, as he passed, at no great distance, without appearing to heed them. \"Come to us, father, or we die!\"\n\nThe cry was repeated, and in terms and tones that might have melted a heart of stone, but it was unanswered. Once, indeed, the old man appeared to catch the sounds, for he paused and listened; but Alice had dropped senseless on the earth, and Cora had sunk at her side, hovering in untiring tenderness over her lifeless form. Munro shook his head in disappointment, and proceeded, bent on the high duty of his station.\n\n\"Lady,\" said Gamut, who, helpless and useless as he was, had not yet dreamed of deserting his trust, \"it is the jubilee of the devils, and this is not a meet place for Christians to tarry in. Let us up and fly.\"\n\n\"Go,\" said Cora, still gazing at her unconscious sister; \"save thyself. To me thou canst not be of further use.\"\n\nDavid comprehended the unyielding character of her resolution, by the simple but expressive gesture that accompanied her words. He gazed, for a moment, at the dusky forms that were acting their hellish rites on every side of him, and his tall person grew more erect, while his chest heaved, and every feature swelled, and seemed to speak with the power of the feelings by which he was governed.\n\n\"If the Jewish boy might tame the evil spirit of Saul by the sound of his harp, and the words of sacred song, it may not be amiss,\" he said, \"to try the potency of music here.\"\n\nThen raising his voice to its highest tones, he poured out a strain so powerful as to be heard even amid the din of that bloody field. More than one savage rushed towards them, thinking to rifle the unprotected sisters of their attire, and bear away their scalps; but when they found this strange and unmoved figure riveted to his post, they paused to listen. Astonishment soon changed to admiration, and they passed on to other and less courageous victims, openly expressing their satisfaction at the firmness with which the white warrior sang his death song. Encouraged and deluded by his success, David exerted all his powers to extend what he believed so holy an influence. The unwonted sounds caught the ears of a distant savage, who flew raging from group to group, like one who, scorning to touch the vulgar herd, hunted for some victim more worthy of his renown. It was Magua, who uttered a yell of pleasure when he beheld his ancient prisoners again at his mercy.\n\n\"Come,\" he said, laying his soiled hands on the dress of Cora, \"the wigwam of the Huron is still open. Is it not better than this place?\"\n\n\"Away!\" cried Cora, veiling her eyes from his revolting aspect.\n\nThe Indian laughed tauntingly, as he held up his reeking hand, and answered,--\"It is red, but it comes from white veins!\"\n\n\"Monster! there is blood, oceans of blood, upon thy soul; thy spirit has moved this scene.\"\n\n\"Magua is a great chief!\" returned the exulting savage; \"will the dark hair go to his tribe?\"\n\n\"Never! strike, if thou wilt, and complete thy revenge.\"\n\nHe hesitated a moment; and then catching the light and senseless form of Alice in his arms, the subtle Indian moved swiftly across the plain towards the woods.\n\n\"Hold!\" shrieked Cora, following wildly on his footsteps; \"release the child! wretch! what is't you do?\"\n\nBut Magua was deaf to her voice; or rather he knew his power, and was determined to maintain it.\n\n\"Stay--lady--stay,\" called Gamut, after the unconscious Cora. \"The holy charm is beginning to be felt, and soon shalt thou see this horrid tumult stilled.\"\n\nPerceiving that, in his turn, he was unheeded, the faithful David followed the distracted sister, raising his voice again in sacred song, and sweeping the air to the measure, with his long arm, in diligent accompaniment. In this manner they traversed the plain, through the flying, the wounded, and the dead. The fierce Huron was, at any time, sufficient for himself and the victim that he bore; though Cora would have fallen, more than once, under the blows of her savage enemies, but for the extraordinary being who stalked in her rear, and who now appeared to the astonished natives gifted with the protecting spirit of madness.\n\nMagua, who knew how to avoid the more pressing dangers, and also to elude pursuit, entered the woods through a low ravine, where he quickly found the Narragansetts, which the travellers had abandoned so shortly before, awaiting his appearance, in custody of a savage as fierce and as malign in his expression as himself. Laying Alice on one of the horses, he made a sign to Cora to mount the other.\n\nNotwithstanding the horror excited by the presence of her captor, there was a present relief in escaping from the bloody scene enacting on the plain, to which Cora could not be altogether insensible. She took her seat, and held forth her arms for her sister, with an air of entreaty and love that even the Huron could not deny. Placing Alice, then, on the same animal with Cora, he seized the bridle, and commenced his route by plunging deeper into the forest. David, perceiving that he was left alone, utterly disregarded, as a subject too worthless even to destroy, threw his long limb across the saddle of the beast they had deserted, and made such progress in the pursuit as the difficulties of the path permitted.\n\nThey soon began to ascend, but as the motion had a tendency to revive the dormant faculties of her sister, the attention of Cora was too much divided between the tenderest solicitude in her behalf, and in listening to the cries which were still too audible on the plain, to note the direction in which they journeyed. When, however, they gained the flattened surface of the mountain-top, and approached the eastern precipice, she recognized the spot to which she had once before been led under the more friendly auspices of the scout. Here Magua suffered them to dismount; and, notwithstanding their own captivity, the curiosity which seems inseparable from horror, induced them to gaze at the sickening sight below.\n\nThe cruel work was still unchecked. On every side the captured were flying before their relentless persecutors, while the armed columns of the Christian king stood fast in an apathy which has never been explained, and which has left an unmovable blot on the otherwise fair escutcheon of their leader. Nor was the sword of death stayed until cupidity got the mastery of revenge. Then, indeed, the shrieks of the wounded and the yells of their murderers grew less frequent, until, finally, the cries of horror were lost to their ear, or were drowned in the loud, long, and piercing whoops of the triumphant savages.\n\n\"Why, anything: An honorable murderer, if you will; For naught I did in hate, but all in honor.\"\n\n_Othello._\n\nThe bloody and inhuman scene rather incidentally mentioned than described in the preceding chapter, is conspicuous in the pages of colonial history, by the merited title of \"The Massacre of William Henry.\" It so far deepened the stain which a previous and very similar event had left upon the reputation of the French commander, that it was not entirely erased by his early and glorious death. It is now becoming obscured by time; and thousands, who know that Montcalm died like a hero on the plains of Abraham, have yet to learn how much he was deficient in that moral courage without which no man can be truly great. Pages might be written to prove, from this illustrious example, the defects of human excellence; to show how easy it is for generous sentiments, high courtesy, and chivalrous courage, to lose their influence beneath the chilling blight of selfishness, and to exhibit to the world a man who was great in all the minor attributes of character, but who was found wanting when it became necessary to prove how much principle is superior to policy. But the task would exceed our prerogatives; and, as history, like love, is so apt to surround her heroes with an atmosphere of imaginary brightness, it is probable that Louis de Saint Veran will be viewed by posterity only as the gallant defender of his country, while his cruel apathy on the shores of the Oswego and of the Horican will be forgotten. Deeply regretting this weakness on the part of a sister muse, we shall at once retire from her sacred precincts, within the proper limits of our own humble vocation.\n\nThe third day from the capture of the fort was drawing to a close, but the business of the narrative must still detain the reader on the shores of the \"holy lake.\" When last seen, the environs of the works were filled with violence and uproar. They were now possessed by stillness and death. The blood-stained conquerors had departed; and their camp, which had so lately rung with the merry rejoicings of a victorious army, lay a silent and deserted city of huts. The fortress was a smouldering ruin; charred rafters, fragments of exploded artillery, and rent mason-work, covering its earthen mounds in confused disorder.\n\nA frightful change had also occurred in the season. The sun had hid its warmth behind an impenetrable mass of vapor, and hundreds of human forms, which had blackened beneath the fierce heats of August, were stiffening in their deformity, before the blasts of a premature November. The curling and spotless mists, which had been seen sailing above the hills towards the north, were now returning in an interminable dusky sheet, that was urged along by the fury of a tempest. The crowded mirror of the Horican was gone; and, in its place, the green and angry waters lashed the shores, as if indignantly casting back its impurities to the polluted strand. Still the clear fountain retained a portion of its charmed influence, but it reflected only the sombre gloom that fell from the impending heavens. That humid and congenial atmosphere which commonly adorned the view, veiling its harshness, and softening its asperities, had disappeared, and the northern air poured across the waste of water so harsh and unmingled, that nothing was left to be conjectured by the eye, or fashioned by the fancy.\n\nThe fiercer element had cropped the verdure of the plain, which looked as though it were scathed by the consuming lightning. But, here and there, a dark green tuft rose in the midst of the desolation; the earliest fruits of a soil that had been fattened with human blood. The whole landscape, which, seen by a favoring light, and in a genial temperature, had been found so lovely, appeared now like some pictured allegory of life, in which objects were arrayed in their harshest but truest colors, and without the relief of any shadowing.\n\nThe solitary and arid blades of grass arose from the passing gusts fearfully perceptible; the bold and rocky mountains were too distinct in their barrenness, and the eye even sought relief, in vain, by attempting to pierce the illimitable void of heaven, which was shut to its gaze by the dusky sheet of ragged and driving vapor.\n\nThe wind blew unequally; sometimes sweeping heavily along the ground, seeming to whisper its moanings in the cold ears of the dead, then rising in a shrill and mournful whistling, it entered the forest with a rush that filled the air with the leaves and branches it scattered in its path. Amid the unnatural shower, a few hungry ravens struggled with the gale; but no sooner was the green ocean of woods, which stretched beneath them, passed, than they gladly stopped, at random, to their hideous banquet.\n\nIn short, it was the scene of wildness and desolation; and it appeared as if all who had profanely entered it had been stricken, at a blow, by the relentless arm of death. But the prohibition had ceased; and for the first time since the perpetrators of those foul deeds which had assisted to disfigure the scene were gone, living human beings had now presumed to approach the place.\n\nAbout an hour before the setting of the sun, on the day already mentioned, the forms of five men might have been seen issuing from the narrow vista of trees, where the path to the Hudson entered the forest, and advancing in the direction of the ruined works. At first their progress was slow and guarded, as though they entered with reluctance amid the horrors of the spot, or dreaded the renewal of its frightful incidents. A light figure preceded the rest of the party, with the caution and activity of a native; ascending every hillock to reconnoitre, and indicating, by gestures, to his companions, the route he deemed it most prudent to pursue. Nor were those in the rear wanting in every caution and foresight known to forest warfare. One among them, he also was an Indian, moved a little on one flank, and watched the margin of the woods, with eyes long accustomed to read the smallest sign of danger. The remaining three were white, though clad in vestments adapted, both in quality and color, to their present hazardous pursuit,--that of hanging on the skirts of a retiring army in the wilderness.\n\nThe effects produced by the appalling sights that constantly arose in their path to the lake shore, were as different as the characters of the respective individuals who composed the party. The youth in front threw serious but furtive glances at the mangled victims, as he stepped lightly across the plain, afraid to exhibit his feelings, and yet too inexperienced to quell entirely their sudden and powerful influence. His red associate, however, was superior to such a weakness. He passed the groups of dead with a steadiness of purpose, and an eye so calm, that nothing but long and inveterate practice could enable him to maintain. The sensations produced in the minds of even the white men were different, though uniformly sorrowful. One, whose gray locks and furrowed lineaments, blending with a martial air and tread, betrayed, in spite of the disguise of a woodsman's dress, a man long experienced in scenes of war, was not ashamed to groan aloud, whenever a spectacle of more than usual horror came under his view. The young man at his elbow shuddered, but seemed to suppress his feelings in tenderness to his companion. Of them all, the straggler who brought up the rear appeared alone to betray his real thoughts, without fear of observation or dread of consequences. He gazed at the most appalling sight with eyes and muscles that knew not how to waver, but with execrations so bitter and deep as to denote how much he denounced the crime of his enemies.\n\nThe reader will perceive at once, in these respective characters, the Mohicans, and their white friend, the scout; together with Munro and Heyward. It was, in truth, the father in quest of his children, attended by the youth who felt so deep a stake in their happiness, and those brave and trusty foresters, who had already proved their skill and fidelity through the trying scenes related.\n\nWhen Uncas, who moved in front, had reached the centre of the plain, he raised a cry that drew his companions in a body to the spot. The young warrior had halted over a group of females who lay in a cluster, a confused mass of dead. Notwithstanding the revolting horror of the exhibition, Munro and Heyward flew towards the festering heap, endeavoring, with a love that no unseemliness could extinguish, to discover whether any vestiges of those they sought were to be seen among the tattered and many-colored garments. The father and lover found instant relief in the search; though each was condemned again to experience the misery of an uncertainty that was hardly less insupportable than the most revolting truth. They were standing, silent and thoughtful, around the melancholy pile, when the scout approached. Eying the sad spectacle with an angry countenance, the sturdy woodsman, for the first time since his entering the plain, spoke intelligibly and aloud:--\n\n\"I have been on many a shocking field, and have followed a trail of blood for many miles,\" he said, \"but never have I found the hand of the devil so plain as it is here to be seen! Revenge is an Indian feeling, and all who know me know that there is no cross in my veins; but this much will I say--here, in the face of heaven, and with the power of the Lord so manifest in this howling wilderness,--that should these Frenchers ever trust themselves again within the range of a ragged bullet, there is one rifle shall play its part, so long as flint will fire or powder burn! I leave the tomahawk and knife to such as have a natural gift to use them. What say you, Chingachgook,\" he added in Delaware; \"shall the Hurons boast of this to their women when the deep snows come?\"\n\nA gleam of resentment flashed across the dark lineaments of the Mohican chief: he loosened his knife in its sheath; and then turning calmly from the sight, his countenance settled into a repose as deep as if he never knew the instigation of passion.\n\n\"Montcalm! Montcalm!\" continued the deeply resentful and less self-restrained scout; \"they say a time must come, when all the deeds done in the flesh will be seen at a single look; and that by eyes cleared from mortal infirmities. Woe betide the wretch who is born to behold this plain, with the judgment hanging about his soul! Ha--as I am a man of white blood, yonder lies a redskin, without the hair of his head where nature rooted it! Look to him, Delaware; it may be one of your missing people; and he should have burial like a stout warrior. I see it in your eye, Sagamore: a Huron pays for this, afore the fall winds have blown away the scent of the blood!\"\n\nChingachgook approached the mutilated form, and turning it over, he found the distinguishing marks of one of those six allied tribes, or nations, as they were called, who, while they fought in the English ranks, were so deadly hostile to his own people. Spurning the loathsome object with his foot, he turned from it with the same indifference he would have quitted a brute carcass. The scout comprehended the action, and very deliberately pursued his own way, continuing, however, his denunciations against the French commander in the same resentful strain.\n\n\"Nothing but vast wisdom and unlimited power should dare to sweep off men in multitudes,\" he added; \"for it is only the one that can know the necessity of the judgment; and what is there, short of the other, that can replace the creatures of the Lord? I hold it a sin to kill the second buck afore the first is eaten, unless a march in the front, or an ambushment, be contemplated. It is a different matter with a few warriors in open and rugged fight, for 'tis their gift to die with the rifle or the tomahawk in hand; according as their natures may happen to be, white or red. Uncas, come this way, lad, and let the ravens settle upon the Mingo. I know, from often seeing it, that they have a craving for the flesh of an Oneida; and it is as well to let the bird follow the gift of its natural appetite.\"\n\n\"Hugh!\" exclaimed the young Mohican, rising on the extremities of his feet, and gazing intently in his front, frightening the raven to some other prey, by the sound and the action.\n\n\"What is it, boy?\" whispered the scout, lowering his tall form into a crouching attitude, like a panther about to take his leap; \"God send it be a tardy Frencher, skulking for plunder. I do believe 'Killdeer' would take an oncommon range to-day!\"\n\nUncas, without making any reply, bounded away from the spot, and in the next instant he was seen tearing from a bush, and waving in triumph a fragment of the green riding-veil of Cora. The movement, the exhibition, and the cry, which again burst from the lips of the young Mohican, instantly drew the whole party about him.\n\n\"My child!\" said Munro, speaking quick and wildly \"give me my child!\"\n\n\"Uncas will try,\" was the short and touching answer.\n\nThe simple but meaning assurance was lost on the father, who seized the piece of gauze, and crushed it in his hand, while his eyes roamed fearfully among the bushes, as if he equally dreaded and hoped for the secrets they might reveal.\n\n\"Here are no dead,\" said Heyward; \"the storm seems not to have passed this way.\"\n\n\"That's manifest; and clearer than the heavens above our heads,\" returned the undisturbed scout; \"but either she, or they that have robbed her, have passed the bush; for I remember the rag she wore to hide a face that all did love to look upon. Uncas, you are right; the dark-hair has been here, and she has fled like a frightened fawn, to the wood; none who could fly would remain to be murdered. Let us search for the marks she left; for to Indian eyes, I sometimes think even a humming-bird leaves his trail in the air.\"\n\nThe young Mohican darted away at the suggestion, and the scout had hardly done speaking, before the former raised a cry of success from the margin of the forest. On reaching the spot, the anxious party perceived another portion of the veil fluttering on the lower branch of a beech.\n\n\"Softly, softly,\" said the scout, extending his long rifle in front of the eager Heyward; \"we now know our work, but the beauty of the trail must not be deformed. A step too soon may give us hours of trouble. We have them, though; that much is beyond denial.\"\n\n\"Bless ye, bless ye, worthy man!\" exclaimed Munro; \"whither, then, have they fled, and where are my babes?\"\n\n\"The path they have taken depends on many chances. If they have gone alone, they are quite as likely to move in a circle as straight, and they may be within a dozen miles of us; but if the Hurons, or any of the French Indians, have laid hands on them, 'tis probable they are now near the borders of the Canadas. But what matters that?\" continued the deliberate scout, observing the powerful anxiety and disappointment the listeners exhibited; \"here are the Mohicans and I on one end of the trail, and, rely on it, we find the other, though they should be a hundred leagues asunder! Gently, gently, Uncas, you are as impatient as a man in the settlements; you forget that light feet leave but faint marks!\"\n\n\"Hugh!\" exclaimed Chingachgook, who had been occupied in examining an opening that had been evidently made through the low underbrush, which skirted the forest; and who now stood erect, as he pointed downwards, in the attitude and with the air of a man who beheld a disgusting serpent.\n\n\"Here is the palpable impression of the footstep of a man,\" cried Heyward, bending over the indicated spot; \"he has trod in the margin of this pool, and the mark cannot be mistaken. They are captives.\"\n\n\"Better so than left to starve in the wilderness,\" returned the scout; \"and they will leave a wider trail. I would wager fifty beaver skins against as many flints, that the Mohicans and I enter their wigwams within the month! Stoop to it, Uncas, and try what you can make of the moccasin; for moccasin it plainly is, and no shoe.\"\n\nThe young Mohican bent over the track, and removing the scattered leaves from around the place, he examined it with much of that sort of scrutiny that a money-dealer, in these days of pecuniary doubts, would bestow on a suspected due-bill. At length he arose from his knees, satisfied with the result of the examination.\n\n\"Well, boy,\" demanded the attentive scout, \"what does it say? can you make anything of the tell-tale?\"\n\n\"Le Renard Subtil!\"\n\n\"Ha! that rampaging devil again! there never will be an end of his loping, till 'Killdeer' has said a friendly word to him.\"\n\nHeyward reluctantly admitted the truth of this intelligence, and now expressed rather his hopes than his doubts by saying,--\n\n\"One moccasin is so much like another, it is probable there is some mistake.\"\n\n\"One moccasin like another! you may as well say that one foot is like another; though we all know that some are long, and others short; some broad, and others narrow; some with high, and some with low insteps; some in-toed, and some out. One moccasin is no more like another than one book is like another; though they who can read in one are seldom able to tell the marks of the other. Which is all ordered for the best, giving to every man his natural advantages. Let me get down to it, Uncas; neither book nor moccasin is the worse for having two opinions, instead of one.\" The scout stooped to the task, and instantly added, \"You are right, boy; here is the patch we saw so often in the other chase. And the fellow will drink when he can get an opportunity: your drinking Indian always learns to walk with a wider toe than the natural savage, it being the gift of a drunkard to straddle, whether of white or red skin. 'Tis just the length and breadth too! look at it, Sagamore: you measured the prints more than once, when we hunted the varmints from Glenn's to the health-springs.\"\n\nChingachgook complied; and after finishing his short examination, he arose, and with a quiet demeanor, he merely pronounced the word--\n\n\"Magua!\"\n\n\"Ay, 'tis a settled thing; here then have passed the dark-hair and Magua.\"\n\n\"And not Alice?\" demanded Heyward.\n\n\"Of her we have not yet seen the signs,\" returned the scout, looking closely around at the trees, the bushes, and the ground. \"What have we there? Uncas, bring hither the thing you see dangling from yonder thorn-bush.\"\n\nWhen the Indian had complied, the scout received the prize, and holding it on high, he laughed in his silent but heartfelt manner.\n\n\"'Tis the tooting we'pon of the singer! now we shall have a trail a priest might travel,\" he said. \"Uncas, look for the marks of a shoe that is long enough to uphold six feet two of tottering human flesh. I begin to have some hopes of the fellow, since he has given up squalling to follow some better trade.\"\n\n\"At least, he has been faithful to his trust,\" said Heyward; \"and Cora and Alice are not without a friend.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Hawkeye, dropping his rifle, and leaning on it with an air of visible contempt, \"he will do their singing. Can he slay a buck for their dinner; journey by the moss on the beeches, or cut the throat of a Huron? If not, the first catbird[22] he meets is the cleverest of the two. Well, boy, any signs of such a foundation?\"\n\n\"Here is something like the footstep of one who has worn a shoe; can it be that of our friend?\"\n\n\"Touch the leaves lightly, or you'll disconsart the formation. That! that is the print of a foot, but 'tis the dark-hair's; and small it is, too, for one of such a noble height and grand appearance. The singer would cover it with his heel.\"\n\n\"Where! let me look on the footsteps of my child,\" said Munro, shoving the bushes aside, and bending fondly over the nearly obliterated impression. Though the tread, which had left the mark, had been light and rapid, it was still plainly visible. The aged soldier examined it with eyes that grew dim as he gazed; nor did he rise from his stooping posture until Heyward saw that he had watered the trace of his daughter's passage with a scalding tear. Willing to divert a distress which threatened each moment to break through the restraint of appearances, by giving the veteran something to do, the young man said to the scout,--\n\n\"As we now possess these infallible signs, let us commence our march. A moment, at such a time, will appear an age to the captives.\"\n\n\"It is not the swiftest leaping deer that gives the longest chase,\" returned Hawkeye, without moving his eyes from the different marks that had come under his view; \"we know that the rampaging Huron has passed,--and the dark hair,--and the singer,--but where is she of the yellow locks and blue eyes? Though little, and far from being as bold as her sister, she is fair to the view, and pleasant in discourse. Has she no friend, that none care for her?\"\n\n\"God forbid she should ever want hundreds! Are we not now in her pursuit? for one, I will never cease the search till she be found.\"\n\n\"In that case we may have to journey by different paths; for here she has not passed, light and little as her footstep would be.\"\n\nHeyward drew back, all his ardor to proceed seeming to vanish on the instant. Without attending to this sudden change in the other's humor, the scout, after musing a moment, continued,--\n\n\"There is no woman in this wilderness could leave such a print as that, but the dark-hair or her sister. We know that the first has been here, but where are the signs of the other? Let us push deeper on the trail, and if nothing offers, we must go back to the plain and strike another scent. Move on, Uncas, and keep your eyes on the dried leaves. I will watch the bushes, while your father shall run with a low nose to the ground. Move on, friends; the sun is getting behind the hills.\"\n\n\"Is there nothing that I can do?\" demanded the anxious Heyward.\n\n\"You!\" repeated the scout, who, with his red friends, was already advancing in the order he had prescribed; \"yes, you can keep in our rear, and be careful not to cross the trail.\"\n\nBefore they had proceeded many rods, the Indians stopped, and appeared to gaze at some signs on the earth, with more than their usual keenness. Both father and son spoke quick and loud, now looking at the object of their mutual admiration, and now regarding each other with the most unequivocal pleasure.\n\n\"They have found the little foot!\" exclaimed the scout, moving forward, without attending further to his own portion of the duty. \"What have we here? An ambushment has been planted in the spot? No, by the truest rifle on the frontiers, here have been them one-sided horses again! Now the whole secret is out, and all is plain as the north star at midnight. Yes, here they have mounted. There the beasts have been bound to a sapling, in waiting; and yonder runs the broad path away to the north, in full sweep for the Canadas.\"\n\n\"But still there are no signs of Alice--of the younger Miss Munro,\"--said Duncan.\n\n\"Unless the shining bauble Uncas has just lifted from the ground should prove one. Pass it this way, lad, that we may look at it.\"\n\nHeyward instantly knew it for a trinket that Alice was fond of wearing, and which he recollected, with the tenacious memory of a lover, to have seen, on the fatal morning of the massacre, dangling from the fair neck of his mistress. He seized the highly prized jewel; and as he proclaimed the fact, it vanished from the eyes of the wondering scout, who in vain looked for it on the ground, long after it was warmly pressed against the beating heart of Duncan.\n\n\"Pshaw!\" said the disappointed Hawkeye, ceasing to rake the leaves with the breech of his rifle; \"'tis a certain sign of age, when the sight begins to weaken. Such a glittering gewgaw, and not to be seen! Well, well, I can squint along a clouded barrel yet, and that is enough to settle all disputes between me and the Mingos. I should like to find the thing too, if it were only to carry it to the right owner, and that would be bringing the two ends of what I call a long trail together,--for by this time the broad St. Lawrence, or, perhaps, the Great Lakes themselves, are atwixt us.\"\n\n\"So much the more reason why we should not delay our march,\" returned Heyward; \"let us proceed.\"\n\n\"Young blood and hot blood, they say, are much the same thing. We are not about to start on a squirrel hunt, or to drive a deer into the Horican, but to outlie for days and nights, and to stretch across a wilderness where the feet of men seldom go, and where no bookish knowledge would carry you through harmless. An Indian never starts on such an expedition without smoking over his council-fire; and though a man of white blood, I honor their customs in this particular, seeing that they are deliberate and wise. We will, therefore, go back, and light our fire to-night in the ruins of the old fort, and in the morning we shall be fresh, and ready to undertake our work like men, and not like babbling women or eager boys.\"\n\nHeyward saw, by the manner of the scout, that altercation would be useless. Munro had again sunk into that sort of apathy which had beset him since his late overwhelming misfortunes, and from which he was apparently to be roused only by some new and powerful excitement. Making a merit of necessity, the young man took the veteran by the arm, and followed in the footsteps of the Indians and the scout, who had already begun to retrace the path which conducted them to the plain.\n\n\"_Salar._--Why, I am sure, if he forfeit, thou wilt not take his flesh; what's that good for?\"\n\n\"_Shy._--To bait fish withal: if it will feed nothing else, it will feed my revenge.\"\n\n_Merchant of Venice._\n\nThe shades of evening had come to increase the dreariness of the place, when the party entered the ruins of William Henry. The scout and his companions immediately made their preparations to pass the night there; but with an earnestness and sobriety of demeanor, that betrayed how much the unusual horrors they had just witnessed worked on even their practised feelings. A few fragments of rafters were reared against a blackened wall; and when Uncas had covered them slightly with brush, the temporary accommodations were deemed sufficient. The young Indian pointed towards his rude hut, when his labor was ended; and Heyward, who understood the meaning of the silent gesture, gently urged Munro to enter. Leaving the bereaved old man alone with his sorrows, Duncan immediately returned to the open air, too much excited himself to seek the repose he had recommended to his veteran friend.\n\nWhile Hawkeye and the Indians lighted their fire, and took their evening's repast, a frugal meal of dried bear's meat, the young man paid a visit to that curtain of the dilapidated fort which looked out on the sheet of the Horican. The wind had fallen, and the waves were already rolling on the sandy beach beneath him, in a more regular and tempered succession. The clouds, as if tired of their furious chase, were breaking asunder; the heavier volumes, gathering in black masses about the horizon, while the lighter scud still hurried above the water, or eddied among the tops of the mountains, like broken flights of birds, hovering around their roosts. Here and there, a red and fiery star struggled through the drifting vapor, furnishing a lurid gleam of brightness to the dull aspect of the heavens. Within the bosom of the encircling hills, an impenetrable darkness had already settled; and the plain lay like a vast and deserted charnel-house, without omen or whisper to disturb the slumbers of its numerous and hapless tenants.\n\nOf this scene, so chillingly in accordance with the past, Duncan stood for many minutes a rapt observer. His eyes wandered from the bosom of the mound, where the foresters were seated around their glimmering fire, to the fainter light which still lingered in the skies, and then rested long and anxiously on the embodied gloom, which lay like a dreary void on that side of him where the dead reposed. He soon fancied that inexplicable sounds arose from the place, though so indistinct and stolen, as to render not only their nature but even their existence uncertain. Ashamed of his apprehensions, the young man turned towards the water, and strove to divert his attentions to the mimic stars that dimly glimmered on its moving surface. Still, his too conscious ears performed their ungrateful duty, as if to warn him of some lurking danger. At length a swift trampling seemed quite audibly to rush athwart the darkness. Unable any longer to quiet his uneasiness, Duncan spoke in a low voice to the scout, requesting him to ascend the mound to the place where he stood. Hawkeye threw his rifle across an arm, and complied, but with an air so unmoved and calm, as to prove how much he counted on the security of their position.\n\n\"Listen!\" said Duncan, when the other placed himself deliberately at his elbow: \"there are suppressed noises on the plain which may show that Montcalm has not yet entirely deserted his conquest.\"\n\n\"Then ears are better than eyes,\" said the undisturbed scout, who, having just deposited a portion of bear between his grinders, spoke thick and slow, like one whose mouth was doubly occupied. \"I, myself, saw him caged in Ty, with all his host; for your Frenchers, when they have done a clever thing, like to get back, and have a dance, or a merry-making, with the women over their success.\"\n\n\"I know not. An Indian seldom sleeps in war, and plunder may keep a Huron here after his tribe has departed. It would be well to extinguish the fire, and have a watch--listen! you hear the noise I mean!\"\n\n\"An Indian more rarely lurks about the graves. Though ready to slay, and not over-regardful of the means, he is commonly content with the scalp, unless when blood is hot, and temper up; but after the spirit is once fairly gone, he forgets his enmity, and is willing to let the dead find their natural rest. Speaking of spirits, Major, are you of opinion that the heaven of a redskin and of us whites will be one and the same?\"\n\n\"No doubt--no doubt. I thought I heard it again! or was it the rustling of the leaves in the top of the beech?\"\n\n\"For my own part,\" continued Hawkeye, turning his face, for a moment, in the direction indicated by Heyward, but with a vacant and careless manner, \"I believe that paradise is ordained for happiness; and that men will be indulged in it according to their dispositions and gifts. I therefore judge that a redskin is not far from the truth when he believes he is to find them glorious hunting-grounds of which his traditions tell; nor, for that matter, do I think it would be any disparagement to a man without a cross to pass his time--\"\n\n\"You hear it again?\" interrupted Duncan.\n\n\"Ay, ay; when food is scarce, and when food is plenty, a wolf grows bold,\" said the unmoved scout. \"There would be picking, too, among the skins of the devils, if there was light and time for the sport. But, concerning the life that is to come, major: I have heard preachers say, in the settlements, that heaven was a place of rest. Now men's minds differ as to their ideas of enjoyment. For myself, and I say it with reverence to the ordering of Providence, it would be no great indulgence to be kept shut up in those mansions of which they preach, having a natural longing for motion and the chase.\"\n\nDuncan, who was now made to understand the nature of the noises he had heard, answered with more attention to the subject which the humor of the scout had chosen for discussion, by saying,--\n\n\"It is difficult to account for the feelings that may attend the last great change.\"\n\n\"It would be a change, indeed, for a man who has passed his days in the open air,\" returned the single-minded scout; \"and who has so often broken his fast on the head-waters of the Hudson, to sleep within sound of the roaring Mohawk. But it is a comfort to know we serve a merciful Master, though we do it each after his fashion, and with great tracts of wilderness atween us--what goes there?\"\n\n\"Is it not the rushing of the wolves you have mentioned?\"\n\nHawkeye slowly shook his head, and beckoned for Duncan to follow him to a spot, to which the glare from the fire did not extend. When he had taken this precaution, the scout placed himself in an attitude of intense attention, and listened long and keenly for a repetition of the low sound that had so unexpectedly startled him. His vigilance, however, seemed exercised in vain; for, after a fruitless pause, he whispered to Duncan,--\n\n\"We must give a call to Uncas. The boy has Indian senses, and may hear what is hid from us; for being a white-skin, I will not deny my nature.\"\n\nThe young Mohican, who was conversing in a low voice with his father, started as he heard the moaning of an owl, and springing on his feet he looked towards the black mounds, as if seeking the place whence the sounds proceeded. The scout repeated the call, and in a few moments, Duncan saw the figure of Uncas stealing cautiously along the rampart, to the spot where they stood.\n\nHawkeye explained his wishes in a very few words, which were spoken in the Delaware tongue. So soon as Uncas was in possession of the reason why he was summoned, he threw himself flat on the turf; where, to the eyes of Duncan, he appeared to lie quiet and motionless. Surprised at the immovable attitude of the young warrior, and curious to observe the manner in which he employed his faculties to obtain the desired information, Heyward advanced a few steps, and bent over the dark object, on which he had kept his eyes riveted. Then it was he discovered that the form of Uncas had vanished, and that he beheld only the dark outline of an inequality in the embankment.\n\n\"What has become of the Mohican?\" he demanded of the scout, stepping back in amazement; \"it was here that I saw him fall, and I could have sworn that here he yet remained.\"\n\n\"Hist! speak lower; for we know not what ears are open, and the Mingos are a quick-witted breed. As for Uncas, he is out on the plain, and the Maquas, if any such are about us, will find their equal.\"\n\n\"You think that Montcalm has not called off all his Indians? Let us give the alarm to our companions, that we may stand to our arms. Here are five of us, who are not unused to meet an enemy.\"\n\n\"Not a word to either, as you value life. Look at the Sagamore, how like a grand Indian chief he sits by the fire. If there are any skulkers out in the darkness, they will never discover by his countenance that we suspect danger at hand.\"\n\n\"But they may discover him, and it will prove his death. His person can be too plainly seen by the light of that fire, and he will become the first and most certain victim.\"\n\n\"It is undeniable that now you speak the truth,\" returned the scout, betraying more anxiety than was usual; \"yet what can be done? A single suspicious look might bring on an attack before we are ready to receive it. He knows, by the call I gave to Uncas, that we have struck a scent: I will tell him that we are on the trail of the Mingos; his Indian nature will teach him how to act.\"\n\nThe scout applied his fingers to his mouth, and raised a low hissing sound, that caused Duncan, at first, to start aside, believing that he heard a serpent. The head of Chingachgook was resting on a hand, as he sat musing by himself; but the moment he heard the warning of the animal whose name he bore, it arose to an upright position and his dark eyes glanced swiftly and keenly on every side of him. With this sudden and perhaps involuntary movement, every appearance of surprise or alarm ended. His rifle lay untouched, and apparently unnoticed, within reach of his hand. The tomahawk that he had loosened in his belt for the sake of ease, was even suffered to fall from its usual situation to the ground, and his form seemed to sink, like that of a man whose nerves and sinews were suffered to relax for the purpose of rest. Cunningly resuming his former position, though with a change of hands, as if the movement had been made merely to relieve the limb, the native awaited the result with a calmness and fortitude that none but an Indian warrior would have known how to exercise.\n\nBut Heyward saw that while to a less instructed eye the Mohican chief appeared to slumber, his nostrils were expanded, his head was turned a little to one side, as if to assist the organs of hearing, and that his quick and rapid glances ran incessantly over every object, within the power of his vision.\n\n\"See the noble fellow!\" whispered Hawkeye, pressing the arm of Heyward; \"he knows that a look or a motion might discansart our schemes, and put us at the mercy of them imps--\"\n\nHe was interrupted by the flash and report of a rifle. The air was filled with sparks of fire around that spot where the eyes of Heyward were still fastened with admiration and wonder. A second look told him that Chingachgook had disappeared in the confusion. In the meantime the scout had thrown forward his rifle, like one prepared for service, and awaited impatiently the moment when an enemy might rise to view. But with the solitary and fruitless attempt made on the life of Chingachgook, the attack appeared to have terminated. Once or twice the listeners thought they could distinguish the distant rustling of bushes, as bodies of some unknown description rushed through them; nor was it long before Hawkeye pointed out the \"scampering of the wolves,\" as they fled precipitately before the passage of some intruder on their proper domains. After an impatient and breathless pause, a plunge was heard in the water, and it was immediately followed by the report of another rifle.\n\n\"There goes Uncas!\" said the scout; \"the boy bears a smart piece! I know its crack, as well as a father knows the language of his child, for I carried the gun myself until a better offered.\"\n\n\"What can this mean?\" demanded Duncan; \"we are watched, and, as it would seem, marked for destruction.\"\n\n\"Yonder scattered brand can witness that no good was intended, and this Indian will testify that no harm has been done,\" returned the scout, dropping his rifle across his arm again, and following Chingachgook, who just then reappeared within the circle of light, into the bosom of the works. \"How is it, Sagamore? Are the Mingos upon us in earnest, or is it only one of those rept_y_les who hang upon the skirts of a war party, to scalp the dead, go in, and make their boast among the squaws of the valiant deeds done on the pale-faces?\"\n\nChingachgook very quietly resumed his seat; nor did he make any reply, until after he had examined the firebrand which had been struck by the bullet that had nearly proved fatal to himself. After which, he was content to reply, holding a single finger up to view, with the English monosyllable,--\n\n\"One.\"\n\n\"I thought as much,\" returned Hawkeye, seating himself; \"and as he had got the cover of the lake afore Uncas pulled upon him, it is more than probable the knave will sing his lies about some great ambushment, in which he was outlying on the trail of two Mohicans and a white hunter--for the officers can be considered as little better than idlers in such a scrimmage. Well, let him--let him. There are always some honest men in every nation, though heaven knows, too, that they are scarce among the Maquas, to look down an upstart when he brags ag'in the face of reason. The varlet sent his lead within whistle of your ears, Sagamore.\"\n\nChingachgook turned a calm and incurious eye towards the place where the ball had struck, and then resumed his former attitude, with a composure that could not be disturbed by so trifling an incident. Just then Uncas glided into the circle, and seated himself at the fire, with the same appearance of indifference as was maintained by his father.\n\nOf these several movements Heyward was a deeply interested and wondering observer. It appeared to him as though the foresters had some secret means of intelligence, which had escaped the vigilance of his own faculties. In place of that eager and garrulous narration with which a white youth would have endeavored to communicate, and perhaps exaggerate, that which had passed out in the darkness of the plain, the young warrior was seemingly content to let his deeds speak for themselves. It was, in fact, neither the moment nor the occasion for an Indian to boast of his exploits; and it is probable, that had Heyward neglected to inquire, not another syllable would, just then, have been uttered on the subject.\n\n\"What has become of our enemy, Uncas?\" demanded Duncan: \"we heard your rifle, and hoped you had not fired in vain.\"\n\nThe young chief removed a fold of his hunting-shirt, and quietly exposed the fatal tuft of hair, which he bore as the symbol of victory. Chingachgook laid his hand on the scalp, and considered it for a moment with deep attention. Then dropping it, with disgust depicted in his strong features, he ejaculated,--\n\n\"Oneida!\"\n\n\"Oneida!\" repeated the scout, who was fast losing his interest in the scene, in an apathy nearly assimilated to that of his red associates, but who now advanced with uncommon earnestness to regard the bloody badge. \"By the Lord, if the Oneidas are outlying upon the trail, we shall be flanked by devils on every side of us! Now, to white eyes there is no difference between this bit of skin and that of any other Indian, and yet the Sagamore declares it came from the poll of a Mingo; nay, he even names the tribe of the poor devil with as much ease as if the scalp was the leaf of a book, and each hair a letter. What right have Christian whites to boast of their learning, when a savage can read a language that would prove too much for the wisest of them all! What say _you_, lad; of what people was the knave?\"\n\nUncas raised his eyes to the face of the scout, and answered, in his soft voice,--\n\n\"Oneida.\"\n\n\"Oneida, again! when one Indian makes a declaration it is commonly true; but when he is supported by his people, set it down as gospel!\"\n\n\"The poor fellow has mistaken us for French,\" said Heyward; \"or he would not have attempted the life of a friend.\"\n\n\"He mistake a Mohican in his paint for a Huron! You would be as likely to mistake the white-coated grenadiers of Montcalm for the scarlet jackets of the 'Royal Americans',\" returned the scout. \"No, no, the sarpent knew his errand; nor was there any great mistake in the matter, for there is but little love atween a Delaware and a Mingo, let their tribes go out to fight for whom they may, in a white quarrel. For that matter, though the Oneidas do serve his sacred majesty, who is my own sovereign lord and master, I should not have deliberated long about letting off 'Killdeer' at the imp myself, had luck thrown him in my way.\"\n\n\"That would have been an abuse of our treaties, and unworthy of your character.\"\n\n\"When a man consorts much with a people,\" continued Hawkeye, \"if they are honest and he no knave, love will grow up atwixt them. It is true that white cunning has managed to throw the tribes into great confusion as respects friends and enemies; so that the Hurons and the Oneidas, who speak the same tongue, or what may be called the same, take each other's scalps, and the Delawares are divided among themselves; a few hanging about their great council-fire on their own river, and fighting on the same side with the Mingos, while the greater part are in the Canadas, out of natural enmity to the Maquas--thus throwing everything into disorder, and destroying all the harmony of warfare. Yet a red natur' is not likely to alter with every shift of policy; so that the love atwixt a Mohican and a Mingo is much like the regard between a white man and a sarpent.\"\n\n\"I regret to hear it; for I had believed those natives who dwelt within our boundaries had found us too just and liberal, not to identify themselves fully with our quarrels.\"\n\n\"Why, I believe it is natur' to give a preference to one's own quarrels before those of strangers. Now, for myself, I do love justice; and therefore I will not say I hate a Mingo, for that may be unsuitable to my color and my religion, though I will just repeat, it may have been owing to the night that 'Killdeer' had no hand in the death of this skulking Oneida.\"\n\nThen, as if satisfied with the force of his own reasons, whatever might be their effect on the opinions of the other disputant, the honest but implacable woodsman turned from the fire, content to let the controversy slumber. Heyward withdrew to the rampart, too uneasy and too little accustomed to the warfare of the woods to remain at ease under the possibility of such insidious attacks. Not so, however, with the scout and the Mohicans. Those acute and long practised senses, whose powers so often exceed the limits of all ordinary credulity, after having detected the danger, had enabled them to ascertain its magnitude and duration. Not one of the three appeared in the least to doubt their perfect security, as was indicated by the preparations that were soon made to sit in council over their future proceedings.\n\nThe confusion of nations, and even of tribes, to which Hawkeye alluded, existed at that period in the fullest force. The great tie of language, and, of course, of a common origin, was severed in many places; and it was one of its consequences, that the Delaware and the Mingo (as the people of the Six Nations were called) were found fighting in the same ranks, while the latter sought the scalp of the Huron, though believed to be the root of his own stock. The Delawares were even divided among themselves. Though love for the soil which had belonged to his ancestors kept the Sagamore of the Mohicans with a small band of followers who were serving at Edward, under the banners of the English king, by far the largest portion of his nation were known to be in the field as allies of Montcalm. The reader probably knows, if enough has not already been gleaned from this narrative, that the Delaware, or Lenape, claimed to be the progenitors of that numerous people, who once were masters of most of the Eastern and Northern States of America, of whom the community of the Mohicans was an ancient and highly honored member.\n\nIt was, of course, with a perfect understanding of the minute and intricate interest which had armed friend against friend, and brought natural enemies to combat by each other's side, that the scout and his companions now disposed themselves to deliberate on the measures that were to govern their future movements, amid so many jarring and savage races of men. Duncan knew enough of Indian customs to understand the reason that the fire was replenished, and why the warriors, not excepting Hawkeye, took their seats within the curl of its smoke with so much gravity and decorum. Placing himself at an angle of the works, where he might be a spectator of the scene within, while he kept a watchful eye against any danger from without, he awaited the result with as much patience as he could summon.\n\nAfter a short and impressive pause, Chingachgook lighted a pipe whose bowl was curiously carved in one of the soft stones of the country, and whose stem was a tube of wood, and commenced smoking. When he had inhaled enough of the fragrance of the soothing weed, he passed the instrument into the hands of the scout. In this manner the pipe had made its rounds three several times, amid the most profound silence, before either of the party opened his lips. Then the Sagamore, as the oldest and highest in rank, in a few calm and dignified words, proposed the subject for deliberation. He was answered by the scout; and Chingachgook rejoined, when the other objected to his opinions. But the youthful Uncas continued a silent and respectful listener, until Hawkeye, in complaisance, demanded his opinion. Heyward gathered from the manners of the different speakers, that the father and son espoused one side of a disputed question, while the white man maintained the other. The contest gradually grew warmer, until it was quite evident the feelings of the speakers began to be somewhat enlisted in the debate.\n\nNotwithstanding the increasing warmth of the amicable contest, the most decorous Christian assembly, not even excepting those in which its reverend ministers are collected, might have learned a wholesome lesson of moderation from the forbearance and courtesy of the disputants. The words of Uncas were received with the same deep attention as those which fell from the maturer wisdom of his father; and so far from manifesting any impatience, neither spoke in reply, until a few moments of silent meditation were, seemingly, bestowed in deliberating on what had already been said.\n\nThe language of the Mohicans was accompanied by gestures so direct and natural, that Heyward had but little difficulty in following the thread of their argument. On the other hand, the scout was obscure; because, from the lingering pride of color, he rather affected the cold and artificial manner which characterizes all classes of Anglo-Americans, when unexcited. By the frequency with which the Indians described the marks of a forest trail, it was evident they urged a pursuit by land, while the repeated sweep of Hawkeye's arm towards the Horican denoted that he was for a passage across its waters.\n\nThe latter was, to every appearance, fast losing ground, and the point was about to be decided against him, when he arose to his feet, and shaking off his apathy, he suddenly assumed the manner of an Indian, and adopted all the arts of native eloquence. Elevating an arm, he pointed out the track of the sun, repeating the gesture for every day that was necessary to accomplish their object. Then he delineated a long and painful path, amid rocks and water-courses. The age and weakness of the slumbering and unconscious Munro were indicated by signs too palpable to be mistaken. Duncan perceived that even his own powers were spoken lightly of, as the scout extended his palm, and mentioned him by appellation of the \"Open Hand,\"--a name his liberality had purchased of all the friendly tribes. Then came a representation of the light and graceful movements of a canoe, set in forcible contrast to the tottering steps of one enfeebled and tired. He concluded by pointing to the scalp of the Oneida, and apparently urging the necessity of their departing speedily, and in a manner that should leave no trail.\n\nThe Mohicans listened gravely, and with countenances that reflected the sentiments of the speaker. Conviction gradually wrought its influence, and towards the close of Hawkeye's speech, his sentences were accompanied by the customary exclamation of commendation. In short, Uncas and his father became converts to his way of thinking, abandoning their own previously expressed opinions with a liberality and candor that, had they been the representatives of some great and civilized people, would have infallibly worked their political ruin, by destroying, forever, their reputation for consistency.\n\nThe instant the matter in discussion was decided, the debate, and everything connected with it, except the results, appeared to be forgotten. Hawkeye, without looking round to read his triumph in applauding eyes, very composedly stretched his tall frame before the dying embers, and closed his own organs in sleep.\n\nLeft now in a measure to themselves, the Mohicans, whose time had been so much devoted to the interests of others, seized the moment to devote some attention to themselves. Casting off, at once, the grave and austere demeanor of an Indian chief, Chingachgook commenced speaking to his son in the soft and playful tones of affection. Uncas gladly met the familiar air of his father; and before the hard breathing of the scout announced that he slept, a complete change was effected in the manner of his two associates.\n\nIt is impossible to describe the music of their language, while thus engaged in laughter and endearments, in such a way as to render it intelligible to those whose ears have never listened to its melody. The compass of their voices, particularly that of the youth, was wonderful,--extending from the deepest bass to tones that were even feminine in softness. The eyes of the father followed the plastic and ingenious movements of the son with open delight, and he never failed to smile in reply to the other's contagious, but low laughter. While under the influence of these gentle and natural feelings, no trace of ferocity was to be seen in the softened features of the Sagamore. His figured panoply of death looked more like a disguise assumed in mockery, than a fierce annunciation of a desire to carry destruction in his footsteps.\n\nAfter an hour passed in the indulgence of their better feelings, Chingachgook abruptly announced his desire to sleep, by wrapping his head in his blanket, and stretching his form on the naked earth. The merriment of Uncas instantly ceased; and carefully raking the coals in such a manner that they should impart their warmth to his father's feet, the youth sought his own pillow among the ruins of the place.\n\nImbibing renewed confidence from the security of these experienced foresters, Heyward soon imitated their example; and long before the night had turned, they who lay in the bosom of the ruined work, seemed to slumber as heavily as the unconscious multitude whose bones were already beginning to bleach on the surrounding plain.\n\n\"Land of Albania! let me bend mine eyes On thee, thou rugged nurse of savage men!\"\n\n_Childe Harold._\n\nThe heavens were still studded with stars, when Hawkeye came to arouse the sleepers. Casting aside their cloaks Munro and Heyward were on their feet while the woodsman was still making his low calls, at the entrance of the rude shelter where they had passed the night. When they issued from beneath its concealment, they found the scout awaiting their appearance nigh by, and the only salutation between them was the significant gesture for silence, made by their sagacious leader.\n\n\"Think over your prayers,\" he whispered, as they approached him; \"for He to whom you make them knows all tongues; that of the heart as well as those of the mouth. But speak not a syllable; it is rare for a white voice to pitch itself properly in the woods, as we have seen by the example of that miserable devil, the singer. Come,\" he continued, turning towards a curtain of the works; \"let us get into the ditch on this side, and be regardful to step on the stones and fragments of wood as you go.\"\n\nHis companions complied, though to two of them the reasons of this extraordinary precaution were yet a mystery. When they were in the low cavity that surrounded the earthen fort on three sides, they found the passage nearly choked by the ruins. With care and patience, however, they succeeded in clambering after the scout, until they reached the sandy shore of the Horican.\n\n\"That's a trail that nothing but a nose can follow,\" said the satisfied scout, looking back along their difficult way; \"grass is a treacherous carpet for a flying party to tread on, but wood and stone take no print from a moccasin. Had you worn your armed boots, there might, indeed, have been something to fear; but with the deer-skin suitably prepared, a man may trust himself, generally, on rocks with safety. Shove in the canoe nigher to the land, Uncas; this sand will take a stamp as easily as the butter of the Jarmans on the Mohawk. Softly, lad, softly; it must not touch the beach, or the knaves will know by what road we have left the place.\"\n\nThe young man observed the precaution; and the scout, laying a board from the ruins to the canoe, made a sign for the two officers to enter. When this was done, everything was studiously restored to its former disorder; and then Hawkeye succeeded in reaching his little birchen vessel, without leaving behind him any of those marks which he appeared so much to dread. Heyward was silent, until the Indians had cautiously paddled the canoe some distance from the fort, and within the broad and dark shadow that fell from the eastern mountain on the glassy surface of the lake; then he demanded,--\n\n\"What need have we for this stolen and hurried departure?\"\n\n\"If the blood of an Oneida could stain such a sheet of pure water as this we float on,\" returned the scout, \"your two eyes would answer your own question. Have you forgotten the skulking rept_y_le that Uncas slew?\"\n\n\"By no means. But he was said to be alone, and dead men give no cause for fear.\"\n\n\"Ay, he was alone in his deviltry! but an Indian whose tribe counts so many warriors, need seldom fear his blood will run, without the death-shriek coming speedily from some of his enemies.\"\n\n\"But our presence--the authority of Colonel Munro--would prove a sufficient protection against the anger of our allies, especially in a case where a wretch so well merited his fate. I trust in Heaven you have not deviated a single foot from the direct line of our course, with so slight a reason!\"\n\n\"Do you think the bullet of that varlet's rifle would have turned aside, though his majesty the king had stood in its path?\" returned the stubborn scout. \"Why did not the grand Frencher, he who is captain-general of the Canadas, bury the tomahawks of the Hurons, if a word from a white can work so strongly on the natur' of an Indian?\"\n\nThe reply of Heyward was interrupted by a groan from Munro; but after he had paused a moment, in deference to the sorrow of his aged friend, he resumed the subject.\n\n\"The Marquis of Montcalm can only settle that error with his God,\" said the young man solemnly.\n\n\"Ay, ay; now there is reason in your words, for they are bottomed on religion and honesty. There is a vast difference between throwing a regiment of white coats atwixt the tribes and the prisoners, and coaxing an angry savage to forget he carries a knife and a rifle, with words that must begin with calling him your son. No, no,\" continued the scout, looking back at the dim shore of William Henry, which was now fast receding, and laughing in his own silent but heartfelt manner; \"I have put a trail of water atween us; and unless the imps can make friends with the fishes, and hear who has paddled across their basin, this fine morning, we shall throw the length of the Horican behind us, before they have made up their minds which path to take.\"\n\n\"With foes in front, and foes in our rear, our journey is like to be one of danger.\"\n\n\"Danger!\" repeated Hawkeye, calmly; \"no, not absolutely of danger; for, with vigilant ears and quick eyes, we can manage to keep a few hours ahead of the knaves; or, if we must try the rifle, there are three of us who understand its gifts as well as any you can name on the borders. No, not of danger; but that we shall have what you may call a brisk push of it is probable; and it may happen, a brush, a skrimmage, or some such divarsion, but always where covers are good, and ammunition abundant.\"\n\nIt is possible that Heyward's estimate of danger differed in some degree from that of the scout, for, instead of replying, he now sat in silence, while the canoe glided over several miles of water. Just as the day dawned, they entered the narrows of the lake,[23] and stole swiftly and cautiously among their numberless little islands. It was by this road that Montcalm had retired with his army; and the adventurers knew not but he had left some of his Indians in ambush, to protect the rear of his forces, and collect the stragglers. They, therefore, approached the passage with the customary silence of their guarded habits.\n\nChingachgook laid aside his paddle; while Uncas and the scout urged the light vessel through crooked and intricate channels, where every foot that they advanced exposed them to the danger of some sudden rising on their progress. The eyes of the Sagamore moved warily from islet to islet, and copse to copse, as the canoe proceeded; and when a clearer sheet of water permitted, his keen vision was bent along the bald rocks and impending forests, that frowned upon the narrow strait.\n\nHeyward, who was a doubly interested spectator, as well from the beauties of the place as from the apprehension natural to his situation, was just believing that he had permitted the latter to be excited without sufficient reason, when the paddle ceased moving, in obedience to a signal from Chingachgook.\n\n\"Hugh!\" exclaimed Uncas, nearly at the moment that the light tap his father had made on the side of the canoe notified them of the vicinity of danger.\n\n\"What now?\" asked the scout; \"the lake is as smooth as if the winds had never blown, and I can see along its sheet for miles; there is not so much as the black head of a loon dotting the water.\"\n\nThe Indian gravely raised his paddle, and pointed in the direction in which his own steady look was riveted. Duncan's eyes followed the motion. A few rods in their front lay another of the low wooded islets, but it appeared as calm and peaceful as if its solitude had never been disturbed by the foot of man.\n\n\"I see nothing,\" he said, \"but land and water; and a lovely scene it is.\"\n\n\"Hist!\" interrupted the scout. \"Ay, Sagamore, there is always a reason for what you do. 'Tis but a shade, and yet it is not natural. You see the mist, major, that is rising above the island; you can't call it a fog, for it is more like a streak of thin cloud--\"\n\n\"It is vapor from the water.\"\n\n\"That a child could tell. But what is the edging of blacker smoke that hangs along its lower side, and which you may trace down into the thicket of hazel? 'Tis from a fire; but one that, in my judgment, has been suffered to burn low.\"\n\n\"Let us then push for the place, and relieve our doubts,\" said the impatient Duncan; \"the party must be small that can lie on such a bit of land.\"\n\n\"If you judge of Indian cunning by the rules you find in books, or by white sagacity, they will lead you astray, if not to your death,\" returned Hawkeye, examining the signs of the place with that acuteness which distinguished him. \"If I may be permitted to speak in this matter, it will be to say, that we have but two things to choose between: the one is, to return, and give up all thoughts of following the Hurons--\"\n\n\"Never!\" exclaimed Heyward, in a voice far too loud for their circumstances.\n\n\"Well, well,\" continued Hawkeye, making a hasty sign to repress his impatience; \"I am much of your mind myself; though I thought it becoming my experience to tell the whole. We must then make a push, and if the Indians or Frenchers are in the narrows, run the gauntlet through these toppling mountains. Is there reason in my words, Sagamore?\"\n\nThe Indian made no other answer than by dropping his paddle into the water, and urging forward the canoe. As he held the office of directing its course, his resolution was sufficiently indicated by the movement. The whole party now plied their paddles vigorously, and in a very few moments they had reached a point whence they might command an entire view of the northern shore of the island, the side that had hitherto been concealed.\n\n\"There they are, by all the truth of signs,\" whispered the scout; \"two canoes and a smoke. The knaves haven't yet got their eyes out of the mist, or we should hear the accursed whoop. Together, friend! we are leaving them, and are already nearly out of whistle of a bullet.\"\n\nThe well known crack of a rifle, whose ball came skipping along the placid surface of the strait, and a shrill yell from the island, interrupted his speech, and announced that their passage was discovered. In another instant several savages were seen rushing into the canoes, which were soon dancing over the water, in pursuit. These fearful precursors of a coming struggle produced no change in the countenances and movements of his three guides, so far as Duncan could discover, except that the strokes of their paddles were longer and more in unison, and caused the little bark to spring forward like a creature possessing life and volition.\n\n\"Hold them there, Sagamore,\" said Hawkeye, looking coolly backward over his left shoulder, while he still plied his paddle; \"keep them just there. Them Hurons have never a piece in their nation that will execute at this distance; but 'Killdeer' has a barrel on which a man may calculate.\"\n\nThe scout having ascertained that the Mohicans were sufficient of themselves to maintain the requisite distance, deliberately laid aside his paddle, and raised the fatal rifle. Three several times he brought the piece to his shoulder, and when his companions were expecting its report, he as often lowered it to request the Indians would permit their enemies to approach a little nigher. At length his accurate and fastidious eye seemed satisfied, and throwing out his left arm on the barrel, he was slowly elevating the muzzle, when an exclamation from Uncas, who sat in the bow, once more caused him to suspend the shot.\n\n\"What now, lad?\" demanded Hawkeye; \"you saved a Huron from the death-shriek by that word; have you reason for what you do?\"\n\nUncas pointed towards the rocky shore a little in their front, whence another war canoe was darting directly across their course. It was too obvious now that their situation was imminently perilous to need the aid of language to confirm it. The scout laid aside his rifle, and resumed the paddle, while Chingachgook inclined the bows of the canoe a little towards the western shore, in order to increase the distance between them and this new enemy. In the meantime they were reminded of the presence of those who pressed upon their rear, by wild and exulting shouts. The stirring scene awakened even Munro from his apathy.\n\n\"Let us make for the rocks on the main,\" he said, with the mien of a tired soldier, \"and give battle to the savages. God forbid that I, or those attached to me and mine, should ever trust again to the faith of any servant of the Louis's!\"\n\n\"He who wishes to prosper in Indian warfare,\" returned the scout, \"must not be too proud to learn from the wit of a native. Lay her more along the land, Sagamore; we are doubling on the varlets, and perhaps they may try to strike our trail on the long calculation.\"\n\nHawkeye was not mistaken; for when the Hurons found their course was likely to throw them behind their chase, they rendered it less direct, until, by gradually bearing more and more obliquely, the two canoes were ere long, gliding on parallel lines, within two hundred yards of each other. It now became entirely a trial of speed. So rapid was the progress of the light vessels, that the lake curled in their front, in miniature waves, and their motion became undulating by its own velocity. It was, perhaps, owing to this circumstance, in addition to the necessity of keeping every hand employed at the paddles, that the Hurons had not immediate recourse to their fire-arms. The exertions of the fugitives were too severe to continue long, and the pursuers had the advantage of numbers. Duncan observed, with uneasiness, that the scout began to look anxiously about him, as if searching for some further means of assisting their flight.\n\n\"Edge her a little more from the sun, Sagamore,\" said the stubborn woodsman; \"I see the knaves are sparing a man to the rifle. A single broken bone might lose us our scalps. Edge more from the sun and we will put the island between us.\"\n\nThe expedient was not without its use. A long, low island lay at a little distance before them, and as they closed with it, the chasing canoe was compelled to take a side opposite to that on which the pursued passed. The scout and his companions did not neglect this advantage, but the instant they were hid from observation by the bushes, they redoubled efforts that before had seemed prodigious. The two canoes came round the last low point, like two coursers at the top of their speed, the fugitives taking the lead. This change had brought them nigher to each other, however, while it altered their relative positions.\n\n\"You showed knowledge in the shaping of birchen bark, Uncas, when you chose this from among the Huron canoes,\" said the scout, smiling, apparently more in satisfaction at their superiority in the race, than from that prospect of final escape which now began to open a little upon them. \"The imps have put all their strength again at the paddles, and we are to struggle for our scalps with bits of flattened wood, instead of clouded barrels and true eyes. A long stroke, and together, friends.\"\n\n\"They are preparing for a shot,\" said Heyward; \"and as we are in a line with them, it can scarcely fail.\"\n\n\"Get you then into the bottom of the canoe,\" returned the scout; \"you and the colonel; it will be so much taken from the size of the mark.\"\n\nHeyward smiled, as he answered,--\n\n\"It would be but an ill example for the highest in rank to dodge, while the warriors were under fire!\"\n\n\"Lord! Lord! That is now a white man's courage!\" exclaimed the scout; \"and like too many of his notions, not to be maintained by reason. Do you think the Sagamore, or Uncas, or even I, who am a man without a cross, would deliberate about finding a cover in the skrimmage, when an open body would do no good? For what have the Frenchers reared up their Quebec, if fighting is always to be done in the clearings?\"\n[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n\n_The scout having ascertained that the Mohicans were sufficient of themselves to maintain the requisite distance, deliberately laid aside his paddle, and raised the fatal rifle_]\n\n\"All that you say is very true, my friend,\" replied Heyward; \"still, our customs must prevent us from doing as you wish.\"\nA volley from the Hurons interrupted the discourse, and as the bullets whistled about them, Duncan saw the head of Uncas turned, looking back at himself and Munro. Notwithstanding the nearness of the enemy, and his own great personal danger, the countenance of the young warrior expressed no other emotion, as the former was compelled to think, than amazement at finding men willing to encounter so useless an exposure. Chingachgook was probably better acquainted with the notions of white men, for he did not even cast a glance aside from the riveted look his eye maintained on the object by which he governed their course. A ball soon struck the light and polished paddle from the hands of the chief, and drove it through the air, far in the advance. A shout arose from the Hurons, who seized the opportunity to fire another volley. Uncas described an arc in the water with his own blade, and as the canoe passed swiftly on, Chingachgook recovered his paddle, and flourishing it on high, he gave the war-whoop of the Mohicans, and then lent his strength and skill again to the important task.\n\nThe clamorous sounds of \"Le Gros Serpent!\" \"La Longue Carabine!\" \"Le Cerf Agile!\" burst at once from the canoes behind, and seemed to give new zeal to the pursuers. The scout seized \"Killdeer\" in his left hand, and elevating it above his head, he shook it in triumph at his enemies. The savages answered the insult with a yell, and immediately another volley succeeded. The bullets pattered along the lake, and one even pierced the bark of their little vessel. No perceptible emotion could be discovered in the Mohicans during this critical moment, their rigid features expressing neither hope nor alarm; but the scout again turned his head, and laughing in his own silent manner, he said to Heyward,--\n\n\"The knaves love to hear the sounds of their pieces; but the eye is not to be found among the Mingos that can calculate a true range in a dancing canoe! You see the dumb devils have taken off a man to charge, and by the smallest measurement that can be allowed, we move three feet to their two!\"\n\nDuncan, who was not altogether as easy under this nice estimate of distances as his companions, was glad to find, however, that owing to their superior dexterity, and the diversion among their enemies, they were very sensibly obtaining the advantage. The Hurons soon fired again, and a bullet struck the blade of Hawkeye's paddle without injury.\n\n\"That will do,\" said the scout, examining the slight indentation with a curious eye; \"it would not have cut the skin of an infant, much less of men, who, like us, have been blown upon by the heavens in their anger. Now, major, if you will try to use this piece of flattened wood, I'll let 'Killdeer' take a part in the conversation.\"\n\nHeyward seized the paddle, and applied himself to the work with an eagerness that supplied the place of skill, while Hawkeye was engaged in inspecting the priming of his rifle. The latter then took a swift aim, and fired. The Huron in the bows of the leading canoe had risen with a similar object, and he now fell backward, suffering his gun to escape from his hands into the water. In an instant, however, he recovered his feet, though his gestures were wild and bewildered. At the same moment his companions suspended their efforts, and the chasing canoes clustered together, and became stationary. Chingachgook and Uncas profited by the interval to regain their wind, though Duncan continued to work with the most persevering industry. The father and son now cast calm but inquiring glances at each other, to learn if either had sustained any injury by the fire; for both well knew that no cry or exclamation would, in such a moment of necessity, have been permitted to betray the accident. A few large drops of blood were trickling down the shoulder of the Sagamore, who, when he perceived that the eyes of Uncas dwelt too long on the sight, raised some water in the hollow of his hand, and washing off the stain, was content to manifest, in this simple manner, the slightness of the injury.\n\n\"Softly, softly, major,\" said the scout, who by this time had reloaded his rifle; \"we are a little too far already for a rifle to put forth its beauties, and you see yonder imps are holding a council. Let them come up within striking distance--my eye may well be trusted in such a matter--and I will trail the varlets the length of the Horican, guaranteeing that not a shot of theirs shall, at the worst, more than break the skin, while 'Killdeer' shall touch the life twice in three times.\"\n\n\"We forget our errand,\" returned the diligent Duncan. \"For God's sake let us profit by this advantage, and increase our distance from the enemy.\"\n\n\"Give me my children,\" said Munro hoarsely; \"trifle no longer with a father's agony, but restore me my babes.\"\n\nLong and habitual deference to the mandates of his superiors had taught the scout the virtue of obedience. Throwing a last and lingering glance at the distant canoes, he laid aside his rifle, and relieving the wearied Duncan, resumed the paddle, which he wielded with sinews that never tired. His efforts were seconded by those of the Mohicans, and a very few minutes served to place such a sheet of water between them and their enemies, that Heyward once more breathed freely.\n\nThe lake now began to expand, and their route lay along a wide reach, that was lined, as before, by high and ragged mountains. But the islands were few, and easily avoided. The strokes of the paddles grew more measured and regular, while they who plied them continued their labor, after the close and deadly chase from which they had just relieved themselves, with as much coolness as though their speed had been tried in sport, rather than under such pressing, nay, almost desperate circumstances.\n\nInstead of following the western shore, whither their errand led them, the wary Mohican inclined his course more towards those hills behind which Montcalm was known to have led his army into the formidable fortress of Ticonderoga. As the Hurons, to every appearance, had abandoned the pursuit, there was no apparent reason for this excess of caution. It was, however, maintained for hours, until they had reached a bay, nigh the northern termination of the lake. Here the canoe was driven upon the beach, and the whole party landed. Hawkeye and Heyward ascended an adjacent bluff, where the former, after considering the expanse of water beneath him, pointed out to the latter a small black object, hovering under a headland, at the distance of several miles.\n\n\"Do you see it?\" demanded the scout. \"Now, what would you account that spot, were you left alone to white experience to find your way through this wilderness?\"\n\n\"But for its distance and its magnitude, I should suppose it a bird. Can it be a living object?\"\n\n\"'Tis a canoe of good birchen bark, and paddled by fierce and crafty Mingos. Though Providence has lent to those who inhabit the woods eyes that would be needless to men in the settlements, where there are inventions to assist the sight, yet no human organs can see all the dangers which at this moment circumvent us. These varlets pretend to be bent chiefly on their sun-down meal, but the moment it is dark they will be on our trail, as true as hounds on the scent. We must throw them off, or our pursuit of Le Renard Subtil may be given up. These lakes are useful at times, especially when the game takes the water,\" continued the scout, gazing about him with a countenance of concern; \"but they give no cover, except it be to the fishes. God knows what the country would be, if the settlements should ever spread far from the two rivers. Both hunting and war would lose their beauty.\"\n\n\"Let us not delay a moment, without some good and obvious cause.\"\n\n\"I little like that smoke, which you may see worming up along the rock above the canoe,\" interrupted the abstracted scout. \"My life on it, other eyes than ours see it, and know its meaning. Well, words will not mend the matter, and it is time that we were doing.\"\n\nHawkeye moved away from the look-out, and descended, musing profoundly, to the shore. He communicated the result of his observations to his companions, in Delaware, and a short and earnest consultation succeeded. When it terminated, the three instantly set about executing their new resolutions.\n\nThe canoe was lifted from the water, and borne on the shoulders of the party. They proceeded into the wood, making as broad and obvious a trail as possible. They soon reached a water-course, which they crossed, and continued onward, until they came to an extensive and naked rock. At this point, where their footsteps might be expected to be no longer visible, they retraced their route to the brook, walking backwards, with the utmost care. They now followed the bed of the little stream to the lake, into which they immediately launched their canoe again. A low point concealed them from the headland, and the margin of the lake was fringed for some distance with dense and overhanging bushes. Under the cover of these natural advantages, they toiled their way, with patient industry, until the scout pronounced that he believed it would be safe once more to land.\n\nThe halt continued until evening rendered objects indistinct and uncertain to the eye. Then they resumed their route, and, favored by the darkness, pushed silently and vigorously towards the western shore. Although the rugged outline of mountain, to which they were steering, presented no distinctive marks to the eyes of Duncan, the Mohican entered the little haven he had selected with the confidence and accuracy of an experienced pilot.\n\nThe boat was again lifted and borne into the woods where it was carefully concealed under a pile of brush. The adventurers assumed their arms and packs, and the scout announced to Munro and Heyward that he and the Indians were at last in readiness to proceed.\n\"If you find a man there, he shall die a flea's death.\"\n\n_Merry Wives of Windsor._\nThe party had landed on the border of a region that is, even to this day, less known to the inhabitants of the States, than the deserts of Arabia, or the steppes of Tartary. It was the sterile and rugged district which separates the tributaries of Champlain from those of the Hudson, the Mohawk, and the St. Lawrence. Since the period of our tale, the active spirit of the country has surrounded it with a belt of rich and thriving settlements, though none but the hunter or the savage is ever known, even now, to penetrate its wild recesses.\n\nAs Hawkeye and the Mohicans had, however, often traversed the mountains and valleys of this vast wilderness, they did not hesitate to plunge into its depths, with the freedom of men accustomed to its privations and difficulties. For many hours the travellers toiled on their laborious way, guided by a star, or following the direction of some water-course, until the scout called a halt, and holding a short consultation with the Indians, they lighted their fire, and made the usual preparations to pass the remainder of the night where they then were.\n\nImitating the example, and emulating the confidence, of their more experienced associates, Munro and Duncan slept without fear, if not without uneasiness. The dews were suffered to exhale, and the sun dispersed the mists, and was shedding a strong and clear light in the forest, when the travellers resumed their journey.\n\nAfter proceeding a few miles, the progress of Hawkeye, who led the advance, became more deliberate and watchful. He often stopped to examine the trees; nor did he cross a rivulet, without attentively considering the quantity, the velocity, and the color of its waters. Distrusting his own judgment his appeals to the opinion of Chingachgook were frequent and earnest. During one of these conferences, Heyward observed that Uncas stood a patient and silent, though, as he imagined, an interested listener. He was strongly tempted to address the young chief, and demand his opinion of their progress; but the calm and dignified demeanor of the native induced him to believe that, like himself, the other was wholly dependent on the sagacity and intelligence of the seniors of the party. At last, the scout spoke in English, and at once explained the embarrassment of their situation.\n\n\"When I found that the home path of the Hurons run north,\" he said, \"it did not need the judgment of many long years to tell that they would follow the valleys, and keep atween the waters of the Hudson and the Horican, until they might strike the springs of the Canada streams, which would lead them into the heart of the country of the Frenchers. Yet here are we, within a short range of the Scaroon, and not a sign of a trail have we crossed! Human natur' is weak, and it is possible we may not have taken the proper scent.\"\n\n\"Heaven protect us from such an error!\" exclaimed Duncan. \"Let us retrace our steps, and examine as we go, with keener eyes. Has Uncas no counsel to offer in such a strait?\"\n\nThe young Mohican cast a glance at his father, but maintaining his quiet and reserved mien, he continued silent. Chingachgook had caught the look, and motioning with his hand, he bade him speak. The moment this permission was accorded, the countenance of Uncas changed from its grave composure to a gleam of intelligence and joy. Bounding forward like a deer, he sprang up the side of a little acclivity, a few rods in advance, and stood exultingly over a spot of fresh earth that looked as though it had been recently upturned by the passage of some heavy animal. The eyes of the whole party followed the unexpected movement, and read their success in the air of triumph that the youth assumed.\n\n\"'Tis the trail!\" exclaimed the scout, advancing to the spot: \"the lad is quick of sight and keen of wit for his years.\"\n\n\"'Tis extraordinary that he should have withheld his knowledge so long,\" muttered Duncan, at his elbow.\n\n\"It would have been more wonderful had he spoken without a bidding. No, no; your young white, who gathers his learning from books and can measure what he knows by the page, may conceit that his knowledge, like his legs, outruns that of his father; but where experience is the master, the scholar is made to know the value of years, and respects them accordingly.\"\n\n\"See!\" said Uncas, pointing north and south, at the evident marks of the broad trail on either side of him: \"the dark-hair has gone towards the frost.\"\n\n\"Hound never ran on a more beautiful scent,\" responded the scout, dashing forward, at once, on the indicated route; \"we are favored, greatly favored, and can follow with high noses. Ay, here are both your waddling beasts: this Huron travels like a white general. The fellow is stricken with a judgment, and is mad! Look sharp for wheels, Sagamore,\" he continued, looking back, and laughing in his newly awakened satisfaction; \"we shall soon have the fool journeying in a coach, and that with three of the best pair of eyes on the borders, in his rear.\"\n\nThe spirits of the scout, and the astonishing success of the chase, in which a circuitous distance of more than forty miles had been passed, did not fail to impart a portion of hope to the whole party. Their advance was rapid; and made with as much confidence as a traveller would proceed along a wide highway. If a rock, or a rivulet, or a bit of earth harder than common, severed the links of the clue they followed, the true eye of the scout recovered them at a distance, and seldom rendered the delay of a single moment necessary. Their progress was much facilitated by the certainty that Magua had found it necessary to journey through the valleys; a circumstance which rendered the general direction of the route sure. Nor had the Huron entirely neglected the arts uniformly practised by the natives when retiring in front of any enemy. False trails, and sudden turnings, were frequent, wherever a brook, or the formation of the ground, rendered them feasible; but his pursuers were rarely deceived, and never failed to detect their error, before they had lost either time or distance on the deceptive track.\n\nBy the middle of the afternoon they had passed the Scaroon, and were following the route of the declining sun. After descending an eminence to a low bottom, through which a stream glided, they suddenly came to a place where the party of Le Renard had made a halt. Extinguished brands were lying around a spring, the offals of a deer were scattered about the place, and the trees bore evident marks of having been browsed by the horses. At a little distance, Heyward discovered, and contemplated with tender emotion, the small bower under which he was fain to believe that Cora and Alice had reposed. But while the earth was trodden, and the footsteps of both men and beasts were so plainly visible around the place, the trail appeared to have suddenly ended.\n\nIt was easy to follow the track of the Narragansetts, but they seemed only to have wandered without guides, or any other object than the pursuit of food. At length Uncas, who, with his father, had endeavored to trace the route of the horses, came upon a sign of their presence that was quite recent. Before following the clue, he communicated his success to his companions; and while the latter were consulting on the circumstance, the youth reappeared, leading the two fillies, with their saddles broken, and the housings soiled, as though they had been permitted to run at will for several days.\n\n\"What should this mean?\" said Duncan, turning pale, and glancing his eyes around him, as if he feared the brush and leaves were about to give up some horrid secret.\n\n\"That our march is come to a quick end, and that we are in an enemy's country,\" returned the scout. \"Had the knaves been pressed, and the gentle ones wanted horses to keep up with the party, he might have taken their scalps; but without an enemy at his heels, and with such rugged beasts as these, he would not hurt a hair of their heads. I know your thoughts, and shame be it to our color that you have reason for them; but he who thinks that even a Mingo would ill-treat a woman, unless it be to tomahawk her, knows nothing of Indian natur', or the laws of the woods. No, no; I have heard that the French Indians had come into these hills, to hunt the moose, and we are getting within scent of their camp. Why should they not? the morning and evening guns of Ty may be heard any day among these mountains; for the Frenchers are running a new line atween the provinces of the king and the Canadas. It is true that the horses are here, but the Hurons are gone; let us then hunt for the path by which they departed.\"\n\nHawkeye and the Mohicans now applied themselves to their task in good earnest. A circle of a few hundred feet in circumference was drawn, and each of the party took a segment for his portion. The examination, however, resulted in no discovery. The impressions of footsteps were numerous, but they all appeared like those of men who had wandered about the spot, without any design to quit it. Again the scout and his companions made the circuit of the halting-place, each slowly following the other, until they assembled in the centre once more, no wiser than when they started.\n\n\"Such cunning is not without its deviltry,\" exclaimed Hawkeye, when he met the disappointed looks of his assistants.\n\n\"We must get down to it, Sagamore, beginning at the spring, and going over the ground by inches. The Huron shall never brag in his tribe that he has a foot which leaves no print.\"\n\nSetting the example himself, the scout engaged in the scrutiny with renewed zeal. Not a leaf was left unturned. The sticks were removed, and the stones lifted; for Indian cunning was known frequently to adopt these objects as covers, laboring with the utmost patience and industry, to conceal each footstep as they proceeded. Still no discovery was made. At length Uncas, whose activity had enabled him to achieve his portion of the task the soonest, raked the earth across the turbid little rill which ran from the spring, and diverted its course into another channel. So soon as its narrow bed below the dam was dry, he stooped over it with keen and curious eyes. A cry of exultation immediately announced the success of the young warrior. The whole party crowded to the spot where Uncas pointed out the impression of a moccasin in the moist alluvion.\n\n\"The lad will be an honor to his people,\" said Hawkeye, regarding the trail with as much admiration as a naturalist would expend on the tusk of a mammoth or the rib of a mastodon; \"ay, and a thorn in the sides of the Hurons. Yet that is not the footstep of an Indian! the weight is too much on the heel, and the toes are squared, as though one of the French dancers had been in, pigeon-winging his tribe! Run back, Uncas, and bring me the size of the singer's foot. You will find a beautiful print of it just opposite yon rock, agin the hillside.\"\n\nWhile the youth was engaged in this commission, the scout and Chingachgook were attentively considering the impressions. The measurements agreed, and the former unhesitatingly pronounced that the footstep was that of David, who had once more, been made to exchange his shoes for moccasins.\n\n\"I can now read the whole of it, as plainly as if I had seen the arts of Le Subtil,\" he added; \"the singer, being a man whose gifts lay chiefly in his throat and feet, was made to go first, and the others have trod in his steps, imitating their formation.\"\n\n\"But,\" cried Duncan, \"I see no signs of--\"\n\n\"The gentle ones,\" interrupted the scout; \"the varlet has found a way to carry them, until he supposed he had thrown any followers off the scent. My life on it, we see their pretty little feet again, before many rods go by.\"\n\nThe whole party now proceeded, following the course of the rill, keeping anxious eyes on the regular impressions. The water soon flowed into its bed again, but watching the ground on either side, the foresters pursued their way content with knowing that the trail lay beneath. More than half a mile was passed, before the rill rippled close around the base of an extensive and dry rock. Here they paused to make sure that the Hurons had not quitted the water.\n\nIt was fortunate they did so. For the quick and active Uncas soon found the impression of a foot on a bunch of moss, where it would seem an Indian had inadvertently trodden. Pursuing the direction given by this discovery, he entered the neighboring thicket, and struck the trail, as fresh and obvious as it had been before they reached the spring. Another shout announced the good fortune of the youth to his companions, and at once terminated the search.\n\n\"Ay, it has been planned with Indian judgment,\" said the scout, when the party was assembled around the place; \"and would have blinded white eyes.\"\n\n\"Shall we proceed?\" demanded Heyward.\n\n\"Softly, softly: we know our path; but it is good to examine the formation of things. This is my schooling, major; and if one neglects the book, there is little chance of learning from the open hand of Providence. All is plain but one thing, which is the manner that the knave contrived to get the gentle ones along the blind trail. Even a Huron would be too proud to let their tender feet touch the water.\"\n\n\"Will this assist in explaining the difficulty?\" said Heyward, pointing towards the fragments of a sort of handbarrow, that had been rudely constructed of boughs, and bound together with withes, and which now seemed carelessly cast aside as useless.\n\n\"'Tis explained!\" cried the delighted Hawkeye. \"If them varlets have passed a minute, they have spent hours in striving to fabricate a lying end to their trail! Well, I've known them to waste a day in the same manner, to as little purpose. Here we have three pair of moccasins, and two of little feet. It is amazing that any mortal beings can journey on limbs so small! Pass me the thong of buckskin, Uncas, and let me take the length of this foot. By the Lord, it is no longer than a child's and yet the maidens are tall and comely. That Providence is partial in its gifts, for its own wise reasons, the best and most contented of us must allow.\"\n\n\"The tender limbs of my daughters are unequal to these hardships,\" said Munro, looking at the light footsteps of his children, with a parent's love: \"we shall find their fainting forms in this desert.\"\n\n\"Of that there is little cause of fear,\" returned the scout, slowly shaking his head; \"this is a firm and straight, though a light step, and not over long. See, the heel has hardly touched the ground; and there the dark-hair has made a little jump, from root to root. No, no; my knowledge for it, neither of them was nigh fainting, hereaway. Now, the singer was beginning to be foot-sore and leg-weary as is plain by his trail. There, you see, he slipped; here he has travelled wide, and tottered; and there, again, it looks as though he journeyed on snow-shoes. Ay, ay, a man who uses his throat altogether, can hardly give his legs a proper training.\"\n\nFrom such undeniable testimony did the practised woodsman arrive at the truth, with nearly as much certainty and precision as if he had been a witness of all those events which his ingenuity so easily elucidated. Cheered by these assurances, and satisfied by a reasoning that was so obvious, while it was so simple, the party resumed its course, after making a short halt to take a hurried repast.\n\nWhen the meal was ended, the scout cast a glance upwards at the setting sun, and pushed forward with a rapidity which compelled Heyward and the still vigorous Munro to exert all their muscles to equal. Their route now lay along the bottom which had already been mentioned. As the Hurons had made no further efforts to conceal their footsteps, the progress of the pursuers was no longer delayed by uncertainty. Before an hour had elapsed, however, the speed of Hawkeye sensibly abated, and his head, instead of maintaining its former direct and forward look, began to turn suspiciously from side to side, as if he were conscious of approaching danger. He soon stopped again, and waited for the whole party to come up.\n\n\"I scent the Hurons,\" he said, speaking to the Mohicans; \"yonder is open sky, through the tree-tops, and we are getting too nigh their encampment. Sagamore, you will take the hillside, to the right; Uncas will bend along the brook to the left, while I will try the trail. If anything should happen, the call will be three croaks of a crow. I saw one of the birds fanning himself in the air, just beyond the dead oak--another sign that we are touching an encampment.\"\n\nThe Indians departed their several ways without reply, while Hawkeye cautiously proceeded with the two gentlemen. Heyward soon pressed to the side of their guide, eager to catch an early glimpse of those enemies he had pursued with so much toil and anxiety. His companion told him to steal to the edge of the wood, which, as usual, was fringed with a thicket, and wait his coming, for he wished to examine certain suspicious signs a little on one side. Duncan obeyed, and soon found himself in a situation to command a view which he found as extraordinary as it was novel.\n\nThe trees of many acres had been felled, and the glow of a mild summer's evening had fallen on the clearing, in beautiful contrast to the gray light of the forest. A short distance from the place where Duncan stood, the stream had seemingly expanded into a little lake, covering most of the low land, from mountain to mountain. The water fell out of this wide basin, in a cataract so regular and gentle, that it appeared rather to be the work of human hands, than fashioned by nature. A hundred earthen dwellings stood on the margin of the lake, and even in its water, as though the latter had overflowed its usual banks. Their rounded roofs, admirably moulded for defence against the weather, denoted more of industry and foresight than the natives were wont to bestow on their regular habitations, much less on those they occupied for the temporary purposes of hunting and war. In short, the whole village or town, whichever it might be termed, possessed more of method and neatness of execution, than the white men had been accustomed to believe belonged, ordinarily, to the Indian habits. It appeared, however, to be deserted. At least, so thought Duncan for many minutes; but, at length, he fancied he discovered several human forms advancing towards him on all fours, and apparently dragging in their train some heavy, and as he was quick to apprehend, some formidable engine. Just then a few dark looking heads gleamed out of the dwellings, and the place seemed suddenly alive with beings, which, however, glided from cover to cover so swiftly, as to allow no opportunity of examining their humors or pursuits. Alarmed at these suspicious and inexplicable movements, he was about to attempt the signal of the crows, when the rustling of leaves at hand drew his eyes in another direction.\n\nThe young man started, and recoiled a few paces instinctively, when he found himself within a hundred yards of a stranger Indian. Recovering his recollection on the instant, instead of sounding an alarm, which might prove fatal to himself, he remained stationary, an attentive observer of the other's motions.\n\nAn instant of calm observation served to assure Duncan that he was undiscovered. The native, like himself, seemed occupied in considering the low dwellings of the village, and the stolen movements of its inhabitants. It was impossible to discover the expression of his features, through the grotesque mask of paint under which they were concealed; though Duncan fancied it was rather melancholy than savage. His head was shaved, as usual, with the exception of the crown, from whose tuft three or four faded feathers from a hawk's wing were loosely dangling. A ragged calico mantle half-encircled his body, while his nether garment was composed of an ordinary shirt, the sleeves of which were made to perform the office that is usually executed by a much more commodious arrangement. His legs were bare, and sadly cut and torn by briers. The feet were, however, covered with a pair of good deer-skin moccasins. Altogether, the appearance of the individual was forlorn and miserable.\n\nDuncan was still curiously observing the person of his neighbor, when the scout stole silently and cautiously to his side.\n\n\"You see we have reached their settlement or encampment,\" whispered the young man; \"and here is one of the savages himself, in a very embarrassing position for our further movements.\"\n\nHawkeye started, and dropped his rifle, directed by the finger of his companion, the stranger came under his view. Then lowering the dangerous muzzle, he stretched forward his long neck, as if to assist a scrutiny that was already intensely keen.\n\n\"The imp is not a Huron,\" he said, \"nor of any of the Canada tribes; and yet you see, by his clothes, the knave has been plundering a white. Ay, Montcalm has raked the woods for his inroad, and a whooping, murdering set of varlets has he gathered together. Can you see where he has put his rifle or his bow?\"\n\n\"He appears to have no arms; nor does he seem to be viciously inclined. Unless he communicate the alarm to his fellows, who as you see are dodging about the water, we have but little to fear from him.\"\n\nThe scout turned to Heyward, and regarded him a moment with unconcealed amazement. Then opening wide his mouth, he indulged in unrestrained and heartfelt laughter, though in that silent and peculiar manner which danger had so long taught him to practise.\n\nRepeating the words, \"fellows who are dodging about the water!\" he added, \"so much for schooling and passing a boyhood in the settlements! The knave has long legs, though, and shall not be trusted. Do you keep him under your rifle while I creep in behind, through the bush, and take him alive. Fire on no account.\"\n\nHeyward had already permitted his companion to bury part of his person in the thicket, when, stretching forth an arm, he arrested him, in order to ask,--\n\n\"If I see you in danger, may I not risk a shot?\"\n\nHawkeye regarded him a moment, like one who knew not how to take the question; then nodding his head, he answered, still laughing, though inaudibly,--\n\n\"Fire a whole platoon, major.\"\n\nIn the next moment he was concealed by the leaves. Duncan waited several minutes in feverish impatience, before he caught another glimpse of the scout. Then he reappeared, creeping along the earth, from which his dress was hardly distinguishable, directly in the rear of his intended captive. Having reached within a few yards of the latter, he arose to his feet, silently and slowly. At that instant, several loud blows were struck on the water, and Duncan turned his eyes just in time to perceive that a hundred dark forms were plunging, in a body, into the troubled little sheet. Grasping his rifle, his looks were again bent on the Indian near him. Instead of taking the alarm, the unconscious savage stretched forward his neck, as if he also watched the movements about the gloomy lake, with a sort of silly curiosity. In the meantime, the uplifted hand of Hawkeye was above him. But, without any apparent reason, it was withdrawn, and its owner indulged in another long, though still silent, fit of merriment. When the peculiar and hearty laughter of Hawkeye was ended, instead of grasping his victim by the throat, he tapped him lightly on the shoulder, and exclaimed aloud,--\n\n\"How now, friend! have you a mind to teach the beavers to sing?\"\n\n\"Even so,\" was the ready answer. \"It would seem that the Being that gave them power to improve his gifts so well, would not deny them voices to proclaim his praise.\"\n\n_\"Bot._--Are we all met?\"\n\n_\"Qui._--Pat--pat; and here's a marvellous Convenient place for our rehearsal.\"\n\n_Midsummer Night's Dream._\n\nThe reader may better imagine, than we describe, the surprise of Heyward. His lurking Indians were suddenly converted into four-footed beasts; his lake into a beaver pond; his cataract into a dam, constructed by those industrious and ingenious quadrupeds; and a suspected enemy into his tried friend, David Gamut, the master of psalmody. The presence of the latter created so many unexpected hopes relative to the sisters that, without a moment's hesitation, the young man broke out of his ambush, and sprang forward to join the two principal actors in the scene.\n\nThe merriment of Hawkeye was not easily appeased. Without ceremony, and with a rough hand, he twirled the supple Gamut around on his heel, and more than once affirmed that the Hurons had done themselves great credit in the fashion of his costume. Then seizing the hand of the other, he squeezed it with a gripe that brought the tears into the eyes of the placid David, and wished him joy of his new condition.\n\n\"You were about opening your throat-practysings among the beavers, were ye?\" he said. \"The cunning devils know half the trade already, for they beat the time with their tails, as you heard just now; and in good time it was too, or 'Killdeer' might have sounded the first note among them. I have known greater fools, who could read and write, than an experienced old beaver; but as for squalling, the animals are born dumb! What think you of such a song as this?\"\n\nDavid shut his sensitive ears, and even Heyward, apprised as he was of the nature of the cry, looked upwards in quest of the bird, as the cawing of a crow rang in the air about them.\n\n\"See!\" continued the laughing scout, as he pointed towards the remainder of the party, who, in obedience to the signal, were already approaching: \"this is music which has its natural virtues; it brings two good rifles to my elbow, to say nothing of the knives and tomahawks. But we see that you are safe; now tell us what has become of the maidens.\"\n\n\"They are captives to the heathen,\" said David; \"and though greatly troubled in spirit, enjoying comfort and safety in the body.\"\n\n\"Both?\" demanded the breathless Heyward.\n\n\"Even so. Though our wayfaring has been sore and our sustenance scanty, we have had little other cause for complaint, except the violence done our feelings, by being thus led in captivity into a far land.\"\n\n\"Bless ye for these very words!\" exclaimed the trembling Munro; \"I shall then receive my babes spotless and angel-like, as I lost them!\"\n\n\"I know not that their delivery is at hand,\" returned the doubting David; \"the leader of these savages is possessed of an evil spirit that no power short of Omnipotence can tame. I have tried him sleeping and waking, but neither sounds nor language seem to touch his soul.\"\n\n\"Where is the knave?\" bluntly interrupted the scout.\n\n\"He hunts the moose to-day, with his young men; and to-morrow, as I hear, they pass farther into these forests, and nigher to the borders of Canada. The elder maiden is conveyed to a neighboring people, whose lodges are situate beyond yonder black pinnacle of rock; while the younger is detained among the women of the Hurons, whose dwellings are but two short miles hence, on a table-land, where the fire has done the office of the axe, and prepared the place for their reception.\"\n\n\"Alice, my gentle Alice!\" murmured Heyward; \"she has lost the consolation of her sister's presence!\"\n\n\"Even so. But so far as praise and thanksgiving in psalmody can temper the spirit in affliction, she has not suffered.\"\n\n\"Has she then a heart for music?\"\n\n\"Of the graver and more solemn character; though it must be acknowledged that, in spite of all my endeavors, the maiden weeps oftener than she smiles. At such moments I forbear to press the holy songs; but there are many sweet and comfortable periods of satisfactory communication, when the ears of the savages are astounded with the upliftings of our voices.\"\n\n\"And why are you permitted to go at large, unwatched?\"\n\nDavid composed his features into what he intended should express an air of modest humility, before he meekly replied--\n\n\"Little be the praise to such a worm as I. But, though the power of psalmody was suspended in the terrible business of that field of blood through which we passed, it has recovered its influence even over the souls of the heathen, and I am suffered to go and come at will.\"\n\nThe scout laughed, and tapping his own forehead significantly, he perhaps explained the singular indulgence more satisfactorily when he said--\n\n\"The Indians never harm a non-composser. But why, when the path lay open before your eyes, did you not strike back on your own trail (it is not so blind as that which a squirrel would make), and bring in the tidings to Edward?\"\n\nThe scout, remembering only his own sturdy and iron nature, had probably exacted a task that David, under no circumstances, could have performed. But, without entirely losing the meekness of his air, the latter was content to answer--\n\n\"Though my soul would rejoice to visit the habitations of Christendom once more, my feet would rather follow the tender spirits intrusted to my keeping, even into the idolatrous province of the Jesuits, than take one step backward, while they pined in captivity and sorrow.\"\n\nThough the figurative language of David was not very intelligible, the sincere and steady expression of his eye, and the glow on his honest countenance, were not easily mistaken. Uncas pressed closer to his side, and regarded the speaker with a look of commendation, while his father expressed his satisfaction by the ordinary pithy exclamation of approbation. The scout shook his head as he rejoined--\n\n\"The Lord never intended that the man should place all his endeavors in his throat, to the neglect of other and better gifts! But he has fallen into the hands of some silly woman, when he should have been gathering his education under a blue sky, among the beauties of the forest. Here, friend; I did intend to kindle a fire with this tooting whistle of thine; but as you value the thing, take it, and blow your best on it!\"\n\nGamut received his pitch-pipe with as strong an expression of pleasure as he believed compatible with the grave functions he exercised. After essaying its virtues repeatedly, in contrast with his own voice, and satisfying himself that none of its melody was lost, he made a very serious demonstration towards achieving a few stanzas of one of the longest effusions in the little volume so often mentioned.\n\nHeyward, however, hastily interrupted his pious purpose, by continuing questions concerning the past and present condition of his fellow-captives, and in a manner more methodical than had been permitted by his feelings in the opening of their interview. David, though he regarded his treasure with longing eyes, was constrained to answer: especially as the venerable father took a part in the interrogatories, with an interest too imposing to be denied. Nor did the scout fail to throw in a pertinent inquiry, whenever a fitting occasion presented. In this manner, though with frequent interruptions, which were filled with certain threatening sounds from the recovered instrument, the pursuers were put in possession of such leading circumstances as were likely to prove useful in accomplishing their great and engrossing object--the recovery of the sisters. The narrative of David was simple, and the facts but few.\n\nMagua had waited on the mountain until a safe moment to retire presented itself, when he had descended, and taken the route along the western side of the Horican, in the direction of the Canadas. As the subtle Huron was familiar with the paths, and well knew there was no immediate danger of pursuit, their progress had been moderate, and far from fatiguing. It appeared from the unembellished statement of David, that his own presence had been rather endured than desired; though even Magua had not been entirely exempt from that veneration with which the Indians regard those whom the Great Spirit has visited in their intellects. At night, the utmost care had been taken of the captives, both to prevent injury from the damps of the woods, and to guard against an escape. At the spring, the horses were turned loose, as has been seen; and notwithstanding the remoteness and length of their trail, the artifices already named were resorted to, in order to cut off every clue to their place of retreat. On their arrival at the encampment of his people, Magua, in obedience to a policy seldom departed from, separated his prisoners. Cora had been sent to a tribe that temporarily occupied an adjacent valley, though David was too ignorant of the customs and history of the natives to be able to declare anything satisfactory concerning their name or character. He only knew that they had not engaged in the late expedition against William Henry; that, like the Hurons themselves, they were allies of Montcalm; and that they maintained an amicable, though a watchful intercourse with the warlike and savage people, whom chance had, for a time, brought in such close and disagreeable contact with themselves.\n\nThe Mohicans and the scout listened to his interrupted and imperfect narrative, with an interest that obviously increased as he proceeded; and it was while attempting to explain the pursuits of the community in which Cora was detained, that the latter abruptly demanded--\n\n\"Did you see the fashion of their knives? Were they of English or French formation?\"\n\n\"My thoughts were bent on no such vanities, but rather mingled in consolation with those of the maidens.\"\n\n\"The time may come when you will not consider the knife of a savage such a despisable vanity,\" returned the scout, with a strong expression of contempt for the other's dulness. \"Had they held their corn-feast--or can you say anything of the totems of the tribe?\"\n\n\"Of corn, we had many and plentiful feasts; for the grain, being in the milk, is both sweet to the mouth and comfortable to the stomach. Of totem, I know not the meaning; but if it appertaineth in any wise to the art of Indian music, it need not be inquired after at their hands. They never join their voices in praise, and it would seem that they are among the profanest of the idolatrous.\"\n\n\"Therein you belie the nature of an Indian. Even the Mingo adores but the true and living God. 'Tis a wicked fabrication of the whites, and I say it to the shame of my color, that would make the warrior bow down before images of his own creation. It is true, they endeavor to make truces with the wicked one--as who would not with an enemy he cannot conquer!--but they look up for favor and assistance to the Great and Good Spirit only.\"\n\n\"It may be so,\" said David; \"but I have seen strange and fantastic images drawn in their paint, of which their admiration and care savored of spiritual pride; especially one, and that, too, a foul and loathsome object.\"\n\n\"Was it a sarpent?\" quickly demanded the scout.\n\n\"Much the same. It was in the likeness of an abject and creeping tortoise.\"\n\n\"Hugh!\" exclaimed both the attentive Mohicans in a breath; while the scout shook his head with an air of one who had made an important, but by no means a pleasing discovery. Then the father spoke, in the language of the Delawares, and with a calmness and dignity that instantly arrested the attention even of those to whom his words were unintelligible. His gestures were impressive, and at times energetic. Once he lifted his arm on high; and as it descended, the action threw aside the folds of his light mantle, a finger resting on his breast, as if he would enforce his meaning by the attitude. Duncan's eyes followed the movement, and he perceived that the animal just mentioned was beautifully, though faintly, worked in a blue tint, on the swarthy breast of the chief. All that he had ever heard of the violent separation of the vast tribes of the Delawares rushed across his mind, and he awaited the proper moment to speak, with a suspense that was rendered nearly intolerable, by his interest in the stake. His wish, however, was anticipated by the scout, who turned from his red friend, saying--\n\n\"We have found that which may be good or evil to us, as Heaven disposes. The Sagamore is of the high blood of the Delawares, and is the great chief of their Tortoises! That some of this stock are among the people of whom the singer tells us, is plain, by his words; and had he but spent half the breath in prudent questions, that he has blown away in making a trumpet of his throat, we might have known how many warriors they numbered. It is, altogether, a dangerous path we move in; for a friend whose face is turned from you often bears a bloodier mind than the enemy who seeks your scalp.\"\n\n\"Explain,\" said Duncan.\n\n\"'Tis a long and melancholy tradition, and one I little like to think of; for it is not to be denied, that the evil has been mainly done by men with white skins. But it has ended in turning the tomahawk of brother against brother, and brought the Mingo and the Delaware to travel in the same path.\"\n\n\"You then suspect it is a portion of that people among whom Cora resides?\"\n\nThe scout nodded his head in assent, though he seemed anxious to waive the further discussion of a subject that appeared painful. The impatient Duncan now made several hasty and desperate propositions to attempt the release of the sisters. Munro seemed to shake off his apathy, and listened to the wild schemes of the young man with a deference that his gray hairs and reverend years should have denied. But the scout, after suffering the ardor of the lover to expend itself a little, found means to convince him of the folly of precipitation, in a matter that would require their coolest judgment and utmost fortitude.\n\n\"It would be well,\" he added, \"to let this man go in again, as usual, and for him to tarry in the lodges, giving notice to the gentle ones of our approach, until we call him out, by signal, to consult. You know the cry of a crow, friend, from the whistle of the whippoorwill?\"\n\n\"'Tis a pleasing bird,\" returned David, \"and has a soft and melancholy note! though the time is rather quick and ill-measured.\"\n\n\"He speaks of the wish-ton-wish,\" said the scout; \"well, since you like his whistle, it shall be your signal. Remember, then, when you hear the whippoorwill's call three times repeated, you are to come into the bushes where the bird might be supposed----\"\n\n\"Stop,\" interrupted Heyward; \"I will accompany him.\"\n\n\"You!\" exclaimed the astonished Hawkeye; \"are you tired of seeing the sun rise and set?\"\n\n\"David is a living proof that the Hurons can be merciful.\"\n\n\"Ay, but David can use his throat, as no man in his senses would pervert the gift.\"\n\n\"I, too, can play the madman, the fool, the hero; in short, any or everything to rescue her I love. Name your objections no longer; I am resolved.\"\n\nHawkeye regarded the young man a moment in speechless amazement. But Duncan, who, in deference to the other's skill and services, had hitherto submitted somewhat implicitly to his dictation, now assumed the superior, with a manner that was not easily resisted. He waved his hand, in sign of his dislike to all remonstrance, and then, in more tempered language, he continued--\n\n\"You have the means of disguise; change me; paint me, too, if you will; in short, alter me to anything--a fool.\"\n\n\"It is not for one like me to say that he who is already formed by so powerful a hand as Providence, stands in need of a change,\" muttered the discontented scout. \"When you send your parties abroad in war, you find it prudent, at least, to arrange the marks and places of encampment, in order that they who fight on your side may know when and where to expect a friend.\"\n\n\"Listen,\" interrupted Duncan; \"you have heard from this faithful follower of the captives, that the Indians are of two tribes, if not of different nations. With one, whom you think to be a branch of the Delawares, is she you call the 'dark-hair'; the other, and younger of the ladies, is undeniably with our declared enemies, the Hurons. It becomes my youth and rank to attempt the latter adventure. While you, therefore, are negotiating with your friends for the release of one of the sisters, I will effect that of the other, or die.\"\n\nThe awakened spirit of the young soldier gleamed in his eyes, and his form became imposing under its influence. Hawkeye, though too much accustomed to Indian artifices not to foresee the danger of the experiment, knew not well how to combat this sudden resolution.\n\nPerhaps there was something in the proposal that suited his own hardy nature, and that secret love of desperate adventure, which had increased with his experience, until hazard and danger had become, in some measure, necessary to the enjoyment of his existence. Instead of continuing to oppose the scheme of Duncan, his humor suddenly altered, and he lent himself to its execution.\n\n\"Come,\" he said, with a good-humored smile; \"the buck that will take to the water must be headed, and not followed. Chingachgook has as many different paints as the engineer officer's wife, who takes down natur' on scraps of paper, making the mountains look like cocks of rusty hay, and placing the blue sky in reach of your hand. The Sagamore can use them, too. Seat yourself on the log; and my life on it, he can soon make a natural fool of you, and that well to your liking.\"\n\nDuncan complied; and the Mohican, who had been an attentive listener to the discourse, readily undertook the office. Long practised in all the subtle arts of his race, he drew, with great dexterity and quickness, the fantastic shadow that the natives were accustomed to consider as the evidence of a friendly and jocular disposition. Every line that could possibly be interpreted into a secret inclination for war, was carefully avoided; while, on the other hand, he studied those conceits that might be construed into amity.\n\nIn short, he entirely sacrificed every appearance of the warrior to the masquerade of a buffoon. Such exhibitions were not uncommon among the Indians; and as Duncan was already sufficiently disguised in his dress, there certainly did exist some reason for believing that, with his knowledge of French, he might pass for a juggler from Ticonderoga, straggling among the allied and friendly tribes.\n\nWhen he was thought to be sufficiently painted, the scout gave him much friendly advice; concerted signals, and appointed the place where they should meet, in the event of mutual success. The parting between Munro and his young friend was more melancholy; still, the former submitted to the separation with an indifference that his warm and honest nature would never have permitted in a more healthful state of mind. The scout led Heyward aside, and acquainted him with his intention to leave the veteran in some safe encampment, in charge of Chingachgook, while he and Uncas pursued their inquiries among the people they had reason to believe were Delawares. Then renewing his cautions and advice, he concluded by saying, with a solemnity and warmth of feeling, with which Duncan was deeply touched:\n\n\"And now God bless you! You have shown a spirit that I like; for it is the gift of youth, more especially one of warm blood and a stout heart. But believe the warning of a man who has reason to know all he says to be true. You will have occasion for your best manhood, and for a sharper wit than what is to be gathered in books, afore you outdo the cunning, or get the better of the courage of a Mingo. God bless you! if the Hurons master your scalp, rely on the promise of one who has two stout warriors to back him. They shall pay for their victory, with a life for every hair it holds. I say, young gentleman, may Providence bless your undertaking, which is altogether for good; and remember, that to outwit the knaves it is lawful to practise things that may not be naturally the gift of a white skin.\"\n\nDuncan shook his worthy and reluctant associate warmly by the hand, once more recommended his aged friend to his care, and returning his good wishes, he motioned to David to proceed. Hawkeye gazed after the high-spirited and adventurous young man for several moments, in open admiration; then shaking his head doubtingly, he turned, and led his own division of the party into the concealment of the forest.\n\nThe route taken by Duncan and David lay directly across the clearing of the beavers, and along the margin of their pond.\n\nWhen the former found himself alone with one so simple, and so little qualified to render any assistance in desperate emergencies, he first began to be sensible of the difficulties of the task he had undertaken. The fading light increased the gloominess of the bleak and savage wilderness that stretched so far on every side of him; and there was even a fearful character in the stillness of those little huts, that he knew were so abundantly peopled. It struck him, as he gazed at the admirable structures and the wonderful precautions of their sagacious inmates, that even the brutes of these vast wilds were possessed of an instinct nearly commensurate with his own reason; and he could not reflect, without anxiety, on the unequal contest that he had so rashly courted. Then came the glowing image of Alice; her distress; her actual danger; and all the peril of his situation was forgotten. Cheering David, he moved on with the light and vigorous step of youth and enterprise.\n\nAfter making nearly a semicircle around the pond, they diverged from the water-course, and began to ascend to the level of a slight elevation in that bottom land, over which they journeyed. Within half an hour they gained the margin of another opening that bore all the signs of having been also made by the beavers, and which those sagacious animals had probably been induced, by some accident, to abandon, for the more eligible position they now occupied. A very natural sensation caused Duncan to hesitate a moment, unwilling to leave the cover of their bushy path, as a man pauses to collect his energies before he essays any hazardous experiment, in which he is secretly conscious they will all be needed. He profited by the halt, to gather such information as might be obtained from his short and hasty glances.\n\nOn the opposite side of the clearing, and near the point where the brook tumbled over some rocks, from a still higher level, some fifty or sixty lodges, rudely fabricated of logs, brush, and earth intermingled, were to be discovered. They were arranged without any order, and seemed to be constructed with very little attention to neatness or beauty. Indeed, so very inferior were they in the two latter particulars to the village Duncan had just seen, that he began to expect a second surprise, no less astonishing than the former. This expectation was in no degree diminished, when, by the doubtful twilight, he beheld twenty or thirty forms rising alternately from the cover of the tall, coarse grass, in front of the lodges, and then sinking again from the sight, as it were to burrow in the earth. By the sudden and hasty glimpses that he caught of these figures, they seemed more like dark glancing spectres, or some other unearthly beings, than creatures fashioned with the ordinary and vulgar materials of flesh and blood. A gaunt, naked form was seen, for a single instant, tossing its arms wildly in the air, and then the spot it had filled was vacant; the figure appearing suddenly in some other and distant place, or being succeeded by another, possessing the same mysterious character. David, observing that his companion lingered, pursued the direction of his gaze, and in some measure recalled the recollection of Heyward, by speaking.\n\n\"There is much fruitful soil uncultivated here,\" he said; \"and I may add, without the sinful leaven of self-commendation, that since my short sojourn in these heathenish abodes, much good seed has been scattered by the wayside.\"\n\n\"The tribes are fonder of the chase than of the arts of men of labor,\" returned the unconscious Duncan, still gazing at the objects of his wonder.\n\n\"It is rather joy than labor to the spirit, to lift up the voice in praise; but sadly do these boys abuse their gifts. Rarely have I found any of their age, on whom nature has so freely bestowed the elements of psalmody; and surely, surely, there are none who neglect them more. Three nights have I now tarried here, and three several times have I assembled the urchins to join in sacred song; and as often have they responded to my efforts with whoopings and howlings that have chilled my soul!\"\n\n\"Of whom speak you?\"\n\n\"Of those children of the devil, who waste the precious moments in yonder idle antics. Ah! the wholesome restraint of discipline is but little known among this self-abandoned people. In a country of birches, a rod is never seen; and it ought not to appear a marvel in my eyes, that the choicest blessings of Providence are wasted in such cries as these.\"\n\nDavid closed his ears against the juvenile pack, whose yell just then rang shrilly through the forest; and Duncan, suffering his lip to curl, as in mockery of his own superstition, said firmly:\n\n\"We will proceed.\"\n\nWithout removing the safeguards from his ears, the master of song complied, and together they pursued their way towards what David was sometimes wont to call \"the tents of the Philistines.\"\n\n\"But though the beast of game The privilege of chase may claim; Though space and law the stag we lend Ere hound we slip, or bow we bend; Who ever recked, where, how, or when The prowling fox was trapped or slain?\"\n\n_Lady of the Lake._\nIt is unusual to find an encampment of the natives, like those of the more instructed whites, guarded by the presence of armed men. Well informed of the approach of every danger, while it is yet at a distance, the Indian generally rests secure under his knowledge of the signs of the forest, and the long and difficult paths that separate him from those he has most reason to dread. But the enemy who, by any lucky concurrence of accidents, has found means to elude the vigilance of the scouts, will seldom meet with sentinels nearer home to sound the alarm. In addition to this general usage, the tribes friendly to the French knew too well the weight of the blow that had just been struck, to apprehend any immediate danger from the hostile nations that were tributary to the crown of Britain.\n\nWhen Duncan and David, therefore, found themselves in the centre of the children, who played the antics already mentioned, it was with the least previous intimation of their approach. But so soon as they were observed, the whole of the juvenile pack raised, by common consent, a shrill and warning whoop; and then sank, as it were, by magic, from before the sight of their visitors. The naked, tawny bodies of the crouching urchins blended so nicely, at that hour, with the withered herbage, that at first it seemed as if the earth had, in truth, swallowed up their forms; though when surprise permitted Duncan to bend his look more curiously about the spot, he found it everywhere met by dark, quick, and rolling eyeballs.\n\nGathering no encouragement from this startling presage of the nature of the scrutiny he was likely to undergo from the more mature judgments of the men, there was an instant when the young soldier would have retreated. It was, however, too late to appear to hesitate. The cry of the children had drawn a dozen warriors to the door of the nearest lodge, where they stood clustered in a dark and savage group, gravely awaiting the nearer approach of those who had unexpectedly come among them.\n\nDavid, in some measure familiarized to the scene, led the way with a steadiness that no slight obstacle was likely to disconcert, into this very building. It was the principal edifice of the village, though roughly constructed of the bark and branches of trees; being the lodge in which the tribe held its councils and public meetings during their temporary residence on the borders of the English province. Duncan found it difficult to assume the necessary appearance of unconcern, as he brushed the dark and powerful frames of the savages who thronged its threshold; but, conscious that his existence depended on his presence of mind, he trusted to the discretion of his companion, whose footsteps he closely followed, endeavoring, as he proceeded, to rally his thoughts for the occasion. His blood curdled when he found himself in absolute contact with such fierce and implacable enemies; but he so far mastered his feelings as to pursue his way into the centre of the lodge, with an exterior that did not betray the weakness. Imitating the example of the deliberate Gamut, he drew a bundle of fragrant brush from beneath a pile that filled a corner of the hut, and seated himself in silence.\n\nSo soon as their visitor had passed, the observant warriors fell back from the entrance, and arranging themselves about him, they seemed patiently to await the moment when it might comport with the dignity of the stranger to speak. By far the greater number stood leaning, in lazy, lounging attitudes, against the upright posts that supported the crazy building, while three or four of the oldest and most distinguished of the chiefs placed themselves on the earth a little more in advance.\n\nA flaring torch was burning in the place, and sent its red glare from face to face and figure to figure, as it waved in the currents of air. Duncan profited by its light to read the probable character of his reception, in the countenances of his hosts. But his ingenuity availed him little, against the cold artifices of the people he had encountered. The chiefs in front scarce cast a glance at his person, keeping their eyes on the ground, with an air that might have been intended for respect, but which it was quite easy to construe into distrust. The men in shadow were less reserved. Duncan soon detected their searching, but stolen looks, which, in truth, scanned his person and attire inch by inch; leaving no emotion of the countenance, no gesture, no line of the paint, nor even the fashion of a garment, unheeded, and without comment.\n\nAt length one whose hair was beginning to be sprinkled with gray, but whose sinewy limbs and firm tread announced that he was still equal to the duties of manhood, advanced out of the gloom of a corner, whither he had probably posted himself to make his observations unseen, and spoke. He used the language of the Wyandots, or Hurons; his words were, consequently, unintelligible to Heyward, though they seemed, by the gestures that accompanied them, to be uttered more in courtesy than anger. The latter shook his head, and made a gesture indicative of his inability to reply.\n\n\"Do none of my brothers speak the French or the English?\" he said, in the former language, looking about him from countenance to countenance, in hopes of finding a nod of assent.\n\nThough more than one had turned, as if to catch the meaning of his words, they remained unanswered.\n\n\"I should be grieved to think,\" continued Duncan, speaking slowly, and using the simplest French of which he was the master, \"to believe, that none of this wise and brave nation understand the language that the 'Grand Monarque' uses when he talks to his children. His heart would be heavy did he believe his red warriors paid him so little respect!\"\n\nA long and grave pause succeeded, during which no movement of a limb, nor any expression of an eye, betrayed the impression produced by his remark. Duncan, who knew that silence was a virtue among his hosts, gladly had recourse to the custom, in order to arrange his ideas. At length the same warrior who had before addressed him replied, by dryly demanding, in the language of the Canadas--\n\n\"When our Great Father speaks to his people, is it with the tongue of a Huron?\"\n\n\"He knows no difference in his children, whether the color of the skin be red, or black, or white,\" returned Duncan, evasively; \"though chiefly is he satisfied with the brave Hurons.\"\n\n\"In what manner will he speak,\" demanded the wary chief, \"when the runners count to him the scalps which five nights ago grew on the heads of the Yengeese?\"\n\n\"They were his enemies,\" said Duncan, shuddering involuntarily; \"and, doubtless, he will say, It is good; my Hurons are very gallant.\"\n\n\"Our Canada father does not think it. Instead of looking forward to reward his Indians, his eyes are turned backward. He sees the dead Yengeese, but no Huron. What can this mean?\"\n\n\"A great chief, like him, has more thoughts than tongues. He looks to see that no enemies are on his trail.\"\n\n\"The canoe of a dead warrior will not float on the Horican,\" returned the savage, gloomily. \"His ears are open to the Delawares, who are not our friends, and they fill them with lies.\"\n\n\"It cannot be. See; he has bid me, who am a man that knows the art of healing, to go to his children, the red Hurons of the great lakes, and ask if any are sick!\"\n\nAnother silence succeeded this annunciation of the character Duncan had assumed. Every eye was simultaneously bent on his person, as if to inquire into the truth or falsehood of the declaration, with an intelligence and keenness that caused the subject of their scrutiny to tremble for the result. He was, however, relieved again by the former speaker.\n\n\"Do the cunning men of the Canadas paint their skins?\" the Huron coldly continued; \"we have heard them boast that their faces were pale.\"\n\n\"When an Indian chief comes among his white fathers,\" returned Duncan, with great steadiness, \"he lays aside his buffalo robe, to carry the shirt that is offered him. My brothers have given me paint, and I wear it.\"\n\nA low murmur of applause announced that the compliment to the tribe was favorably received. The elderly chief made a gesture of commendation, which was answered by most of his companions, who each threw forth a hand, and uttered a brief exclamation of pleasure. Duncan began to breathe more freely, believing that the weight of his examination was past; and as he had already prepared a simple and probable tale to support his pretended occupation, his hopes of ultimate success grew brighter.\n\nAfter a silence of a few moments, as if adjusting his thoughts, in order to make a suitable answer to the declaration their guest had just given, another warrior arose, and placed himself in an attitude to speak. While his lips were yet in the act of parting, a low but fearful sound arose from the forest, and was immediately succeeded by a high, shrill yell, that was drawn out, until it equalled the longest and most plaintive howl of the wolf. The sudden and terrible interruption caused Duncan to start from his seat, unconscious of everything but the effect produced by so frightful a cry. At the same moment, the warriors glided in a body from the lodge, and the outer air was filled with loud shouts, that nearly drowned those awful sounds, which were still ringing beneath the arches of the woods. Unable to command himself any longer, the youth broke from the place, and presently stood in the centre of a disorderly throng, that included nearly everything having life, within the limits of the encampment. Men, women, and children; the aged, the infirm, the active, and the strong, were alike abroad; some exclaiming aloud, others clapping their hands with a joy that seemed frantic, and all expressing their savage pleasure in some unexpected event. Though astounded, at first, by the uproar, Heyward was soon enabled to find its solution by the scene that followed.\n\nThere yet lingered sufficient light in the heavens to exhibit those bright openings among the tree-tops, where different paths left the clearing to enter the depths of the wilderness. Beneath one of them, a line of warriors issued from the woods, and advanced slowly towards the dwellings. One in front bore a short pole, on which, as it afterwards appeared, were suspended several human scalps. The startling sounds that Duncan had heard were what the whites have not inappropriately called the \"death-halloo;\" and each repetition of the cry was intended to announce to the tribe the fate of an enemy. Thus far the knowledge of Heyward assisted him in the explanation; and as he now knew that the interruption was caused by the unlooked-for return of a successful war-party, every disagreeable sensation was quieted in inward congratulation, for the opportune relief and insignificance it conferred on himself.\n\nWhen at the distance of a few hundred feet from the lodges, the newly arrived warriors halted. Their plaintive and terrific cry, which was intended to represent equally the wailings of the dead and the triumph of the victors, had entirely ceased. One of their number now called aloud, in words that were far from appalling, though not more intelligible to those for whose ears they were intended, than their expressive yells. It would be difficult to convey a suitable idea of the savage ecstasy with which the news thus imparted was received. The whole encampment, in a moment, became a scene of the most violent bustle and commotion. The warriors drew their knives, and flourishing them, they arranged themselves in two lines, forming a lane that extended from the war-party to the lodges. The squaws seized clubs, axes, or whatever weapon of offence first offered itself to their hands, and rushed eagerly to act their part in the cruel game that was at hand. Even the children would not be excluded; but boys, little able to wield the instruments, tore the tomahawks from the belts of their fathers, and stole into the ranks, apt imitators of the savage traits exhibited by their parents.\n\nLarge piles of brush lay scattered about the clearing, and a wary and aged squaw was occupied in firing as many as might serve to light the coming exhibition. As the flame arose, its power exceeded that of the parting day, and assisted to render objects at the same time more distinct and more hideous. The whole scene formed a striking picture, whose frame was composed of the dark and tall border of pines. The warriors just arrived were the most distant figures. A little in advance stood two men, who were apparently selected from the rest, as the principal actors in what was to follow. The light was not strong enough to render their features distinct, though it was quite evident that they were governed by very different emotions. While one stood erect and firm, prepared to meet his fate like a hero, the other bowed his head, as if palsied by terror or stricken with shame. The high-spirited Duncan felt a powerful impulse of admiration and pity towards the former, though no opportunity could offer to exhibit his generous emotions. He watched his slightest movement, however, with eager eyes; and as he traced the fine outline of his admirably proportioned and active frame, he endeavored to persuade himself, that if the powers of man, seconded by such noble resolution, could bear one harmless through so severe a trial, the youthful captive before him might hope for success in the hazardous race he was about to run. Insensibly the young man drew nigher to the swarthy lines of the Hurons, and scarcely breathed, so intense became his interest in the spectacle. Just then the signal yell was given, and the momentary quiet which had preceded it was broken by a burst of cries, that far exceeded any before heard. The most abject of the two victims continued motionless; but the other bounded from the place at the cry, with the activity and swiftness of a deer. Instead of rushing through the hostile lines, as had been expected, he just entered the dangerous defile, and before time was given for a single blow, turned short, and leaping the heads of a row of children, he gained at once the exterior and safer side of the formidable array. The artifice was answered by a hundred voices raised in imprecations; and the whole of the excited multitude broke from their order, and spread themselves about the place in wild confusion.\n\nA dozen blazing piles now shed their lurid brightness on the place, which resembled some unhallowed and supernatural arena, in which malicious demons had assembled to act their bloody and lawless rites. The forms in the background looked like unearthly beings, gliding before the eye, and cleaving the air with frantic and unmeaning gestures; while the savage passions of such as passed the flames, were rendered fearfully distinct by the gleams that shot athwart their inflamed visages.\n\nIt will easily be understood, that amid such a concourse of vindictive enemies, no breathing time was allowed the fugitive. There was a single moment when it seemed as if he would have reached the forest, but the whole body of his captors threw themselves before him, and drove him back into the centre of his relentless persecutors. Turning like a headed deer, he shot, with the swiftness of an arrow, through a pillar of forked flame, and passing the whole multitude harmless, he appeared on the opposite side of the clearing. Here too he was met and turned by a few of the older and more subtle of the Hurons. Once more he tried the throng, as if seeking safety in its blindness, and then several moments succeeded, during which Duncan believed the active and courageous young stranger was lost.\n\nNothing could be distinguished but a dark mass of human forms tossed and involved in inexplicable confusion. Arms, gleaming knives, and formidable clubs, appeared above them, but the blows were evidently given at random. The awful effect was heightened by the piercing shrieks of the women and the fierce yells of the warriors. Now and then Duncan caught a glimpse of a light form cleaving the air in some desperate bound, and he rather hoped than believed that the captive yet retained the command of his astonishing powers of activity. Suddenly the multitude rolled backward, and approached the spot where he himself stood. The heavy body in the rear pressed upon the women and children in front, and bore them to the earth. The stranger reappeared in the confusion. Human power could not, however, much longer endure so severe a trial. Of this the captive seemed conscious. Profiting by the momentary opening, he darted from among the warriors, and made a desperate, and, what seemed to Duncan, a final effort to gain the wood. As if aware that no danger was to be apprehended from the young soldier, the fugitive nearly brushed his person in his flight. A tall and powerful Huron, who had husbanded his forces, pressed close upon his heels, and with an uplifted arm menaced a fatal blow. Duncan thrust forth a foot, and the shock precipitated the eager savage headlong, many feet in advance of his intended victim. Thought itself is not quicker than was the motion with which the latter profited by the advantage; he turned, gleamed like a meteor again before the eyes of Duncan, and at the next moment, when the latter recovered his recollection, and gazed around in quest of the captive, he saw him quietly leaning against a small painted post, which stood before the door of the principal lodge.\n\nApprehensive that the part he had taken in the escape might prove fatal to himself, Duncan left the place without delay. He followed the crowd, which drew nigh the lodges, gloomy and sullen, like any other multitude that had been disappointed in an execution. Curiosity, or perhaps a better feeling, induced him to approach the stranger. He found him, standing with one arm cast about the protecting post, and breathing thick and hard, after his exertions, but disdaining to permit a single sign of suffering to escape. His person was now protected by immemorial and sacred usage, until the tribe in council had deliberated and determined on his fate. It was not difficult, however, to foretell the result, if any presage could be drawn from the feelings of those who crowded the place.\n\nThere was no term of abuse known to the Huron vocabulary that the disappointed women did not lavishly expend on the successful stranger. They flouted at his efforts, and told him, with bitter scoffs, that his feet were better than his hands; and that he merited wings, while he knew not the use of an arrow or a knife. To all this the captive made no reply; but was content to preserve an attitude in which dignity was singularly blended with disdain. Exasperated as much by his composure as by his good-fortune, their words became unintelligible, and were succeeded by shrill, piercing yells. Just then the crafty squaw, who had taken the necessary precaution to fire the piles, made her way through the throng, and cleared a place for herself in front of the captive. The squalid and withered person of this hag might well have obtained for her the character of possessing more than human cunning. Throwing back her light vestment, she stretched forth her long skinny arm, in derision, and using the language of the Lenape, as more intelligible to the subject of her gibes, she commenced aloud--\n\n\"Look you, Delaware!\" she said, snapping her fingers in his face; \"your nation is a race of women, and the hoe is better fitted to your hands than the gun. Your squaws are the mothers of deer; but if a bear, or a wild cat, or a serpent were born among you, ye would flee. The Huron girls shall make you petticoats, and we will find you a husband.\"\n\nA burst of savage laughter succeeded this attack, during which the soft and musical merriment of the younger females strangely chimed with the cracked voice of their older and more malignant companion. But the stranger was superior to all their efforts. His head was immovable; nor did he betray the slightest consciousness that any were present, except when his haughty eye rolled towards the dusky forms of the warriors, who stalked in the background, silent and sullen observers of the scene.\n\nInfuriated at the self-command of the captive, the woman placed her arms akimbo; and throwing herself into a posture of defiance, she broke out anew, in a torrent of words that no art of ours could commit successfully to paper. Her breath was, however, expended in vain; for, although distinguished in her own nation as a proficient in the art of abuse, she was permitted to work herself into such a fury as actually to foam at the mouth, without causing a muscle to vibrate in the motionless figure of the stranger. The effect of his indifference began to extend itself to the other spectators; and a youngster, who was just quitting the condition of a boy, to enter the state of manhood, attempted to assist the termagant, by flourishing his tomahawk before their victim, and adding his empty boasts to the taunts of the woman. Then, indeed, the captive turned his face towards the light, and looked down on the stripling with an expression that was superior to contempt. At the next moment he resumed his quiet and reclining attitude against the post. But the change of posture had permitted Duncan to exchange glances with the firm and piercing eyes of Uncas.\n[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n\n_Throwing back her light vestment, she stretched forth her long skinny arm, in derision_]\nBreathless with amazement, and heavily oppressed with the critical situation of his friend, Heyward recoiled before the look, trembling lest its meaning might, in some unknown manner, hasten the prisoner's fate. There was not, however, any instant cause for such an apprehension. Just then a warrior forced his way into the exasperated crowd. Motioning the women and children aside with a stern gesture, he took Uncas by the arm, and led him towards the door of the council lodge. Thither all the chiefs, and most of the distinguished warriors, followed; among whom the anxious Heyward found means to enter without attracting any dangerous attention to himself.\n\nA few minutes were consumed in disposing of those present in a manner suitable to their rank and influence in the tribe. An order very similar to that adopted in the preceding interview was observed; the aged and superior chiefs occupying the area of the spacious apartment, within the powerful light of a glaring torch, while their juniors and inferiors were arranged in the background, presenting a dark outline of swarthy and marked visages. In the very centre of the lodge, immediately under an opening that admitted the twinkling light of one or two stars, stood Uncas, calm, elevated, and collected. His high and haughty carriage was not lost on his captors, who often bent their looks on his person, with eyes which, while they lost none of their inflexibility of purpose, plainly betrayed their admiration of the stranger's daring.\n\nThe case was different with the individual whom Duncan had observed to stand forth with his friend, previously to the desperate trial of speed; and who, instead of joining in the chase, had remained, throughout its turbulent uproar, like a cringing statue, expressive of shame and disgrace. Though not a hand had been extended to greet him, nor yet an eye had condescended to watch his movements, he had also entered the lodge, as though impelled by a fate to whose decrees he submitted, seemingly, without a struggle. Heyward profited by the first opportunity to gaze in his face, secretly apprehensive he might find the features of another acquaintance; but they proved to be those of a stranger, and, what was still more inexplicable, of one who bore all the distinctive marks of a Huron warrior. Instead of mingling with his tribe, however, he sat apart, a solitary being in a multitude, his form shrinking into a crouching and abject attitude, as if anxious to fill as little space as possible. When each individual had taken his proper station, and silence reigned in the place, the gray-haired chief already introduced to the reader, spoke aloud, in the language of the Lenni Lenape.\n\n\"Delaware,\" he said, \"though one of a nation of women, you have proved yourself a man. I would give you food; but he who eats with a Huron should become his friend. Rest in peace till the morning sun, when our last words shall be spoken.\"\n\n\"Seven nights, and as many summer days, have I fasted on the trail of the Hurons,\" Uncas coldly replied; \"the children of the Lenape know how to travel the path of the just without lingering to eat.\"\n\n\"Two of my young men are in pursuit of your companion,\" resumed the other, without appearing to regard the boast of his captive; \"when they get back, then will our wise men say to you 'live' or 'die.'\"\n\n\"Has a Huron no ears?\" scornfully exclaimed Uncas; \"twice, since he has been your prisoner, has the Delaware heard a gun that he knows. Your young men will never come back!\"\n\nA short and sullen pause succeeded this bold assertion. Duncan, who understood the Mohican to allude to the fatal rifle of the scout, bent forward in earnest observation of the effect it might produce on the conquerors; but the chief was content with simply retorting,--\n\n\"If the Lenape are so skilful, why is one of their bravest warriors here?\"\n\n\"He followed in the steps of a flying coward, and fell into a snare. The cunning beaver may be caught.\"\n\nAs Uncas thus replied, he pointed with his finger toward the solitary Huron, but without deigning to bestow any other notice on so unworthy an object. The words of the answer and the air of the speaker produced a strong sensation among his auditors. Every eye rolled sullenly towards the individual indicated by the simple gesture, and a low, threatening murmur passed through the crowd. The ominous sounds reached the outer door, and the women and children pressing into the throng, no gap had been left, between shoulder and shoulder, that was not now filled with the dark lineaments of some eager and curious human countenance.\n\nIn the meantime, the more aged chiefs, in the centre, communed with each other in short and broken sentences. Not a word was uttered that did not convey the meaning of the speaker, in the simplest and most energetic form. Again, a long and deeply solemn pause took place. It was known, by all present, to be the grave precursor of a weighty and important judgment. They who composed the outer circle of faces were on tiptoe to gaze; and even the culprit for an instant forgot his shame in a deeper emotion, and exposed his abject features, in order to cast an anxious and troubled glance at the dark assemblage of chiefs. The silence was finally broken by the aged warrior so often named. He arose from the earth, and moving past the immovable form of Uncas, placed himself in a dignified attitude before the offender. At that moment, the withered squaw already mentioned moved into the circle, in a slow, sideling sort of a dance, holding the torch, and muttering the indistinct words of what might have been a species of incantation. Though her presence was altogether an intrusion, it was unheeded.\n\nApproaching Uncas, she held the blazing brand in such a manner as to cast its red glare on his person, and to expose the slightest emotion of his countenance. The Mohican maintained his firm and haughty attitude; and his eye, so far from deigning to meet her inquisitive look, dwelt steadily on the distance, as though it penetrated the obstacles which impeded the view, and looked into futurity. Satisfied with her examination, she left him, with a slight expression of pleasure, and proceeded to practise the same trying experiment on her delinquent countryman.\n\nThe young Huron was in his war paint, and very little of a finely moulded form was concealed by his attire. The light rendered every limb and joint discernible, and Duncan turned away in horror when he saw they were writhing in irrepressible agony. The woman was commencing a low and plaintive howl at the sad and shameful spectacle, when the chief put forth his hand and gently pushed her aside.\n\n\"Reed-that-bends,\" he said, addressing the young culprit by name, and in his proper language, \"though the Great Spirit has made you pleasant to the eyes, it would have been better that you had not been born. Your tongue is loud in the village, but in battle it is still. None of my young men strike the tomahawk deeper into the war-post--none of them so lightly on the Yengeese. The enemy know the shape of your back, but they have never seen the color of your eyes. Three times have they called on you to come, and as often did you forget to answer. Your name will never be mentioned again in your tribe--it is already forgotten.\"\n\nAs the chief slowly uttered these words, pausing impressively between each sentence, the culprit raised his face, in deference to the other's rank and years. Shame, horror, and pride struggled in its lineaments. His eye, which was contracted with inward anguish, gleamed on the persons of those whose breath was his fame; and the latter emotion for an instant predominated. He arose to his feet, and baring his bosom, looked steadily on the keen, glittering knife, that was already upheld by his inexorable judge. As the weapon passed slowly into his heart he even smiled, as if in joy at having found death less dreadful than he had anticipated, and fell heavily on his face, at the feet of the rigid and unyielding form of Uncas.\n\nThe squaw gave a loud and plaintive yell, dashed the torch to the earth, and buried everything in darkness. The whole shuddering group of spectators glided from the lodge, like troubled sprites; and Duncan thought that he and the yet throbbing body of the victim of an Indian judgment had now become its only tenants.\n\n\"Thus spoke the sage: the kings without delay Dissolve the council, and their chief obey.\"\n\nPOPE'S _Iliad._\n\nA single moment served to convince the youth that he was mistaken. A hand was laid, with a powerful pressure, on his arm, and the low voice of Uncas muttered in his ears,--\n\n\"The Hurons are dogs. The sight of a coward's blood can never make a warrior tremble. The 'Gray Head' and the Sagamore are safe, and the rifle of Hawkeye is not asleep. Go,--Uncas and the 'Open Hand' are now strangers. It is enough.\"\n\nHeyward would gladly have heard more, but a gentle push from his friend urged him towards the door, and admonished him of the danger that might attend the discovery of their intercourse. Slowly and reluctantly yielding to the necessity, he quitted the place, and mingled with the throng that hovered nigh. The dying fires in the clearing cast a dim and uncertain light on the dusky figures that were silently stalking to and fro; and occasionally a brighter gleam than common glanced into the lodge, and exhibited the figure of Uncas still maintaining its upright attitude near the dead body of the Huron.\n\nA knot of warriors soon entered the place again, and reissuing, they bore the senseless remains into the adjacent woods. After this termination of the scene, Duncan wandered among the lodges, unquestioned and unnoticed, endeavoring to find some trace of her in whose behalf he incurred the risk he ran. In the present temper of the tribe, it would have been easy to have fled and rejoined his companions, had such a wish crossed his mind. But, in addition to the never-ceasing anxiety on account of Alice, a fresher, though feebler interest in the fate of Uncas assisted to chain him to the spot. He continued, therefore, to stray from hut to hut, looking into each only to encounter additional disappointment, until he had made the entire circuit of the village. Abandoning a species of inquiry that proved so fruitless, he retraced his steps to the council lodge, resolved to seek and question David, in order to put an end to his doubts.\n\nOn reaching the building which had proved alike the seat of judgment and the place of execution, the young man found that the excitement had already subsided. The warriors had reassembled, and were now calmly smoking, while they conversed gravely on the chief incidents of their recent expedition to the head of the Horican. Though the return of Duncan was likely to remind them of his character, and the suspicious circumstances of his visit, it produced no visible sensation. So far, the terrible scene that had just occurred proved favorable to his views, and he required no other prompter than his own feelings to convince him of the expediency of profiting by so unexpected an advantage.\n\nWithout seeming to hesitate, he walked into the lodge, and took his seat with a gravity that accorded admirably with the deportment of his hosts. A hasty but searching glance sufficed to tell him that, though Uncas still remained where he had left him, David had not reappeared. No other restraint was imposed on the former than the watchful looks of a young Huron, who had placed himself at hand; though an armed warrior leaned against the post that formed one side of the narrow door-way. In every other respect, the captive seemed at liberty; still he was excluded from all participation in the discourse, and possessed much more of the air of some finely moulded statue than a man having life and volition.\n\nHeyward had too recently witnessed a frightful instance of the prompt punishments of the people into whose hands he had fallen, to hazard an exposure by any officious boldness. He would greatly have preferred silence and meditation to speech, when a discovery of his real condition might prove so instantly fatal. Unfortunately for this prudent resolution, his entertainers appeared otherwise disposed. He had not long occupied the seat wisely taken a little in the shade, when another of the elder warriors, who spoke the French language, addressed him:--\n\n\"My Canada father does not forget his children,\" said the chief; \"I thank him. An evil spirit lives in the wife of one of my young men. Can the cunning stranger frighten him away?\"\n\nHeyward possessed some knowledge of the mummery practised among the Indians, in the cases of such supposed visitations. He saw, at a glance, that the circumstance might possibly be improved to further his own end. It would, therefore, have been difficult, just then, to have uttered a proposal that would have given him more satisfaction. Aware of the necessity of preserving the dignity of his imaginary character, however, he repressed his feelings, and answered with suitable mystery,--\n\n\"Spirits differ; some yield to the power of wisdom, while others are too strong.\"\n\n\"My brother is a great medicine,\" said the cunning savage; \"he will try?\"\n\nA gesture of assent was the answer. The Huron was content with the assurance, and resuming his pipe, he awaited the proper moment to move. The impatient Heyward, inwardly execrating the cold customs of the savages, which required such sacrifices to appearance, was fain to assume an air of indifference, equal to that maintained by the chief, who was, in truth, a near relative of the afflicted woman. The minutes lingered, and the delay had seemed an hour to the adventurer in empiricism, when the Huron laid aside his pipe, and drew his robe across his breast, as if about to lead the way to the lodge of the invalid. Just then, a warrior of powerful frame darkened the door, and stalking silently among the attentive group, he seated himself on one end of the low pile of brush which sustained Duncan. The latter cast an impatient look at his neighbor, and felt his flesh creep with uncontrollable horror when he found himself in actual contact with Magua.\n\nThe sudden return of this artful and dreaded chief caused a delay in the departure of the Huron. Several pipes, that had been extinguished, were lighted again; while the newcomer, without speaking a word, drew his tomahawk from his girdle, and filling the bowl on its head, began to inhale the vapors of the weed through the hollow handle, with as much indifference as if he had not been absent two weary days on a long and toilsome hunt. Ten minutes, which appeared so many ages to Duncan, might have passed in this manner; and the warriors were fairly enveloped in a cloud of white smoke before any of them spoke.\n\n\"Welcome!\" one at length uttered; \"has my friend found the moose?\"\n\n\"The young men stagger under their burdens,\" returned Magua. \"Let 'Reed-that-bends' go on the hunting-path; he will meet them.\"\n\nA deep and awful silence succeeded the utterance of the forbidden name. Each pipe dropped from the lips of its owner as though all had inhaled an impurity at the same instant. The smoke wreathed above their heads in little eddies, and curling in a spiral form, it ascended swiftly through the opening in the roof of the lodge, leaving the place beneath clear of its fumes, and each dark visage distinctly visible. The looks of most of the warriors were riveted on the earth; though a few of the younger and less gifted of the party suffered their wild and glaring eyeballs to roll in the direction of a white-headed savage, who sat between two of the most venerated chiefs of the tribe. There was nothing in the air or attire of this Indian that would seem to entitle him to such a distinction. The former was rather depressed, than remarkable for the bearing of the natives; and the latter was such as was commonly worn by the ordinary men of the nation. Like most around him, for more than a minute his look too was on the ground; but, trusting his eyes at length to steal a glance aside, he perceived that he was becoming an object of general attention. Then he arose and lifted his voice in the general silence.\n\n\"It was a lie,\" he said; \"I had no son. He who was called by that name is forgotten; his blood was pale; and it came not from the veins of a Huron; the wicked Chippewas cheated my squaw. The Great Spirit has said, that the family of Wiss-entush should end; he is happy who knows that the evil of his race dies with himself. I have done.\"\n\nThe speaker, who was the father of the recreant young Indian, looked round and about him, as if seeking commendation of his stoicism in the eyes of his auditors. But the stern customs of his people had made too severe an exaction of the feeble old man. The expression of his eye contradicted his figurative and boastful language, while every muscle in his wrinkled visage was working with anguish. Standing a single minute to enjoy his bitter triumph, he turned away, as if sickening at the gaze of men, and veiling his face in his blanket, he walked from the lodge with the noiseless step of an Indian, seeking, in the privacy of his own abode, the sympathy of one like himself, aged, forlorn, and childless.\n\nThe Indians, who believe in the hereditary transmission of virtues and defects in character, suffered him to depart in silence. Then, with an elevation of breeding that many in a more cultivated state of society might profitably emulate, one of the chiefs drew the attention of the young men from the weakness they had just witnessed, by saying, in a cheerful voice, addressing himself in courtesy to Magua, as the newest comer,--\n\n\"The Delawares have been like bears after the honey-pots, prowling around my village. But who has ever found a Huron asleep?\"\n\nThe darkness of the impending cloud which precedes a burst of thunder was not blacker than the brow of Magua as he exclaimed,--\n\n\"The Delawares of the Lakes!\"\n\n\"Not so. They who wear the petticoats of squaws, on their own river. One of them has been passing the tribe.\"\n\n\"Did my young men take his scalp?\"\n\n\"His legs were good, though his arm is better for the hoe than the tomahawk,\" returned the other, pointing to the immovable form of Uncas.\n\nInstead of manifesting any womanish curiosity to feast his eyes with the sight of a captive from a people he was known to have so much reason to hate, Magua continued to smoke, with the meditative air that he usually maintained, when there was no immediate call on his cunning or his eloquence. Although secretly amazed at the facts communicated by the speech of the aged father, he permitted himself to ask no questions, reserving his inquiries for a more suitable moment. It was only after a sufficient interval that he shook the ashes from his pipe, replaced the tomahawk, tightened his girdle, and arose, casting for the first time a glance in the direction of the prisoner, who stood a little behind him. The wary, though seemingly abstracted Uncas, caught a glimpse of the movement, and turning suddenly to the light, their looks met. Near a minute these two bold and untamed spirits stood regarding one another steadily in the eye, neither quailing in the least before the fierce gaze he encountered. The form of Uncas dilated, and his nostrils opened like those of a tiger at bay; but so rigid and unyielding was his posture, that he might easily have been converted by the imagination into an exquisite and faultless representation of the warlike deity of his tribe. The lineaments of the quivering features of Magua proved more ductile; his countenance gradually lost its character of defiance in an expression of ferocious joy, and heaving a breath from the very bottom of his chest, he pronounced aloud the very formidable name of--\n\n\"Le Cerf Agile!\"\n\nEach warrior sprang upon his feet at the utterance of the well known appellation, and there was a short period during which the stoical constancy of the natives was completely conquered by surprise. The hated and yet respected name was repeated as by one voice, carrying the sound even beyond the limits of the lodge. The women and children, who lingered around the entrance, took up the words in an echo, which was succeeded by another shrill and plaintive howl. The latter was not yet ended, when the sensation among the men had entirely abated. Each one in presence seated himself, as though ashamed of his precipitation; but it was many minutes before their meaning eyes ceased to roll towards their captive, in curious examination of a warrior who had so often proved his prowess on the best and proudest of their nation. Uncas enjoyed his victory, but was content with merely exhibiting his triumph by a quiet smile--an emblem of scorn which belongs to all time and every nation.\n\nMagua caught the expression, and raising his arm, he shook it at the captive, the light silver ornaments attached to his bracelet rattling with the trembling agitation of the limb, as, in a tone of vengeance, he exclaimed, in English,--\n\n\"Mohican, you die!\"\n\n\"The healing waters will never bring the dead Hurons to life,\" returned Uncas, in the music of the Delawares; \"the tumbling river washes their bones; their men are squaws; their women owls. Go! call together the Huron dogs, that they may look upon a warrior. My nostrils are offended; they scent the blood of a coward.\"\n\nThe latter allusion struck deep, and the injury rankled. Many of the Hurons understood the strange tongue in which the captive spoke, among which number was Magua. This cunning savage beheld, and instantly profited by his advantage. Dropping the light robe of skin from his shoulder, he stretched forth his arm, and commenced a burst of his dangerous and artful eloquence. However much his influence among his people had been impaired by his occasional and besetting weakness, as well as by his desertion of the tribe, his courage and his fame as an orator were undeniable. He never spoke without auditors, and rarely without making converts to his opinions. On the present occasion, his native powers were stimulated by the thirst of revenge.\n\nHe again recounted the events of the attack on the island at Glenn's, the death of his associates, and the escape of their most formidable enemies. Then he described the nature and position of the mount whither he had led such captives as had fallen into their hands. Of his own bloody intentions towards the maidens, and of his baffled malice he made no mention, but passed rapidly on to the surprise of the party by La Longue Carabine, and its fatal termination. Here he paused, and looked about him, in affected veneration for the departed, but, in truth, to note the effect of his opening narrative. As usual, every eye was riveted on his face. Each dusky figure seemed a breathing statue, so motionless was the posture, so intense the attention of the individual.\n\nThen Magua dropped his voice, which had hitherto been clear, strong, and elevated, and touched upon the merits of the dead. No quality that was likely to command the sympathy of an Indian escaped his notice. One had never been known to follow the chase in vain; another had been indefatigable on the trail of their enemies. This was brave, that generous. In short, he so managed his allusions, that in a nation, which was composed of so few families, he contrived to strike every chord that might find, in its turn, some breast in which to vibrate.\n\n\"Are the bones of my young men,\" he concluded, \"in the burial-place of the Hurons? You know they are not. Their spirits are gone towards the setting sun, and are already crossing the great waters, to the happy hunting-grounds. But they departed without food, without guns or knives, without moccasins, naked and poor as they were born. Shall this be? Are their souls to enter the land of the just like hungry Iroquois or unmanly Delawares; or shall they meet their friends with arms in their hands and robes on their backs? What will our fathers think the tribes of the Wyandots have become? They will look on their children with a dark eye, and say, Go! a Chippewa has come hither with the name of a Huron. Brothers, we must not forget the dead; a redskin never ceases to remember. We will load the back of this Mohican until he staggers under our bounty, and despatch him after my young men. They call to us for aid, though our ears are not open; they say, Forget us not. When they see the spirit of this Mohican toiling after them with his burden, they will know we are of that mind. Then will they go on happy; and our children will say, 'So did our fathers to their friends, so must we do to them.' What is a Yengee? we have slain many, but the earth is still pale. A stain on the name of a Huron can only be hid by blood that comes from the veins of an Indian. Let this Delaware die.\"\n\nThe effect of such an harangue, delivered in the nervous language and with the emphatic manner of a Huron orator, could scarcely be mistaken. Magua had so artfully blended the natural sympathies with the religious superstition of his auditors, that their minds, already prepared by custom to sacrifice a victim to the _manes_ of their countrymen, lost every vestige of humanity in a wish for revenge. One warrior in particular, a man of wild and ferocious mien, had been conspicuous for the attention he had given to the words of the speaker. His countenance had changed with each passing emotion, until it settled into a look of deadly malice. As Magua ended he arose, and uttering the yell of a demon, his polished little axe was seen glancing in the torch-light as he whirled it above his head. The motion and the cry were too sudden for words to interrupt his bloody intention. It appeared as if a bright gleam shot from his hand, which was crossed at the same moment by a dark and powerful line. The former was the tomahawk in its passage; the latter the arm that Magua darted forward to divert its aim. The quick and ready motion of the chief was not entirely too late. The keen weapon cut the war-plume from the scalping-tuft of Uncas, and passed through the frail wall of the lodge, as though it were hurled from some formidable engine.\n\nDuncan had seen the threatening action, and sprang upon his feet, with a heart which while it leaped into his throat, swelled with the most generous resolution in behalf of his friend. A glance told him that the blow had failed, and terror changed to admiration. Uncas stood still, looking his enemy in the eye with features that seemed superior to emotion. Marble could not be colder, calmer, or steadier than the countenance he put upon this sudden and vindictive attack. Then, as if pitying a want of skill which had proved so fortunate to himself, he smiled, and muttered a few words of contempt in his own tongue.\n\n\"No!\" said Magua, after satisfying himself of the safety of the captive; \"the sun must shine on his shame; the squaws must see his flesh tremble, or our revenge will be like the play of boys. Go! take him where there is silence; let us see if a Delaware can sleep at night, and in the morning die.\"\n\nThe young men whose duty it was to guard the prisoner instantly passed their ligaments of bark across his arms, and led him from the lodge, amid a profound and ominous silence. It was only as the figure of Uncas stood in the opening of the door that his firm step hesitated. There he turned, and, in the sweeping and haughty glance that he threw around the circle of his enemies, Duncan caught a look which he was glad to construe into an expression that he was not entirely deserted by hope.\n\nMagua was content with his success, or too much occupied with his secret purposes to push his inquiries any further. Shaking his mantle, and folding it on his bosom, he also quitted the place, without pursuing a subject which might have proved so fatal to the individual at his elbow. Notwithstanding his rising resentment, his natural firmness, and his anxiety in behalf of Uncas, Heyward felt sensibly relieved by the absence of so dangerous and so subtle a foe. The excitement produced by the speech gradually subsided. The warriors resumed their seats, and clouds of smoke once more filled the lodge. For near half an hour, not a syllable was uttered, or scarcely a look cast aside; a grave and meditative silence being the ordinary succession to every scene of violence and commotion among those beings, who were alike so impetuous and yet so self-restrained.\n\nWhen the chief who had solicited the aid of Duncan finished his pipe, he made a final and successful movement towards departing. A motion of a finger was the intimation he gave the supposed physician to follow; and passing through the clouds of smoke, Duncan was glad, on more accounts than one, to be able, at last, to breathe the pure air of a cool and refreshing summer evening.\n\nInstead of pursuing his way among those lodges where Heyward had already made his unsuccessful search, his companion turned aside, and proceeded directly towards the base of an adjacent mountain, which overhung the temporary village. A thicket of brush skirted its foot, and it became necessary to proceed through a crooked and narrow path. The boys had resumed their sports in the clearing, and were enacting a mimic chase to the post among themselves. In order to render their games as like the reality as possible, one of the boldest of their number had conveyed a few brands into some piles of tree-tops that had hitherto escaped the burning. The blaze of one of these fires lighted the way of the chief and Duncan, and gave a character of additional wildness to the rude scenery. At a little distance from a bald rock, and directly in its front, they entered a grassy opening, which they prepared to cross. Just then fresh fuel was added to the fire, and a powerful light penetrated even to that distant spot. It fell upon the white surface of the mountain, and was reflected downwards upon a dark and mysterious-looking being that arose, unexpectedly, in their path.\n\nThe Indian paused, as if doubtful whether to proceed, and permitted his companion to approach his side. A large black ball, which at first seemed stationary, now began to move in a manner that to the latter was inexplicable. Again the fire brightened, and its glare fell more distinctly on the object. Then even Duncan knew it, by its restless and sideling attitudes, which kept the upper part of its form in constant motion, while the animal itself appeared seated, to be a bear. Though it growled loudly and fiercely, and there were instants when its glistening eyeballs might be seen, it gave no other indications of hostility. The Huron, at least, seemed assured that the intentions of this singular intruder were peaceable, for after giving it an attentive examination, he quietly pursued his course.\n\nDuncan, who knew that the animal was often domesticated among the Indians, followed the example of his companion, believing that some favorite of the tribe had found its way into the thicket, in search of food. They passed it unmolested. Though obliged to come nearly in contact with the monster, the Huron, who had at first so warily determined the character of his strange visitor, was now content with proceeding without wasting a moment in further examination; but Heyward was unable to prevent his eyes from looking backward, in salutary watchfulness against attacks in the rear. His uneasiness was in no degree diminished when he perceived the beast rolling along their path, and following their footsteps. He would have spoken, but the Indian at that moment shoved aside a door of bark, and entered a cavern in the bosom of the mountain.\n\nProfiting by so easy a method of retreat, Duncan stepped after him, and was gladly closing the slight cover to the opening, when he felt it drawn from his hand by the beast, whose shaggy form immediately darkened the passage. They were now in a straight and long gallery, in a chasm of the rocks, where retreat without encountering the animal was impossible. Making the best of the circumstances, the young man pressed forward, keeping as close as possible to his conductor. The bear growled frequently at his heels, and once or twice its enormous paws were laid on his person, as if disposed to prevent his further passage into the den.\n\nHow long the nerves of Heyward would have sustained him in this extraordinary situation, it might be difficult to decide; for, happily, he soon found relief. A glimmer of light had constantly been in their front, and they now arrived at the place whence it proceeded.\n\nA large cavity in the rock had been rudely fitted to answer the purposes of many apartments. The subdivisions were simple but ingenious, being composed of stone, sticks, and bark, intermingled. Openings above admitted the light by day, and at night fires and torches supplied the place of the sun. Hither the Hurons had brought most of their valuables, especially those which more particularly pertained to the nation; and hither, as it now appeared, the sick woman, who was believed to be the victim of supernatural power, had been transported also, under an impression that her tormentor would find more difficulty in making his assaults through walls of stone than through the leafy coverings of the lodges. The apartment into which Duncan and his guide first entered, had been exclusively devoted to her accommodation. The latter approached her bedside, which was surrounded by females, in the centre of whom Heyward was surprised to find his missing friend David.\n\nA single look was sufficient to apprise the pretended leech that the invalid was far beyond his powers of healing. She lay in a sort of paralysis, indifferent to the objects which crowded before her sight, and happily unconscious of suffering. Heyward was far from regretting that his mummeries were to be performed on one who was much too ill to take an interest in their failure or success. The slight qualm of conscience which had been excited by the intended deception was instantly appeased, and he began to collect his thoughts, in order to enact his part with suitable spirit, when he found he was about to be anticipated in his skill by an attempt to prove the power of music.\n\nGamut, who had stood prepared to pour forth his spirit in song when the visitors entered, after delaying a moment, drew a strain from his pipe, and commenced a hymn that might have worked a miracle, had faith in its efficacy been of much avail. He was allowed to proceed to the close, the Indians respecting his imaginary infirmity, and Duncan too glad of the delay to hazard the slightest interruption. As the dying cadence of his strains was falling on the ears of the latter, he started aside at hearing them repeated behind him in a voice half human, half sepulchral. Looking around, he beheld the shaggy monster seated on end in a shadow of the cavern, where, while his restless body swung in the uneasy manner of the animal, it repeated, in a sort of low growl, sound, if not words, which bore some slight resemblance to the melody of the singer.\n\nThe effect of so strange an echo on David may better be imagined than described. His eyes opened as if he doubted their truth; and his voice became instantly mute in excess of wonder. A deep-laid scheme, of communicating some important intelligence to Heyward, was driven from his recollection by an emotion which very nearly resembled fear, but which he was fain to believe was admiration. Under its influence, he exclaimed aloud--\"She expects you, and is at hand;\" and precipitately left the cavern.\n\n_\"Snug._--Have you the lion's part written? Pray you, if it be, give it me, for I am slow of study.\"\n\n_\"Quince_.--You may do it extempore, for it is nothing but roaring.\"\n\n_Midsummer Night's Dream._\n\nThere was a strange blending of the ridiculous with that which was solemn in this scene. The beast still continued its rolling, and apparently untiring movements, though its ludicrous attempt to imitate the melody of David ceased the instant the latter abandoned the field. The words of Gamut were, as has been seen, in his native tongue; and to Duncan they seemed pregnant with some hidden meaning, though nothing present assisted him in discovering the object of their illusion. A speedy end was, however, put to every conjecture on the subject, by the manner of the chief, who advanced to the bedside of the invalid, and beckoned away the whole group of female attendants that had clustered there to witness the skill of the stranger. He was implicitly, though reluctantly, obeyed; and when the low echo which rang along the hollow natural gallery from the distant closing door had ceased, pointing towards his insensible daughter, he said,--\n\n\"Now let my brother show his power.\"\n\nThus unequivocally called on to exercise the functions of his assumed character, Heyward was apprehensive that the smallest delay might prove dangerous. Endeavoring then to collect his ideas, he prepared to perform that species of incantation, and those uncouth rites, under which the Indian conjurers are accustomed to conceal their ignorance and impotency. It is more than probable that, in the disordered state of his thoughts, he would soon have fallen into some suspicious, if not fatal error, had not his incipient attempts been interrupted by a fierce growl from the quadruped. Three several times did he renew his efforts to proceed, and as often was he met by the same unaccountable opposition, each interruption seeming more savage and threatening than the preceding.\n\n\"The cunning ones are jealous,\" said the Huron; \"I go. Brother, the woman is the wife of one of my bravest young men; deal justly by her. Peace!\" he added, beckoning to the discontented beast to be quiet; \"I go.\"\n\nThe chief was as good as his word, and Duncan now found himself alone in that wild and desolate abode, with the helpless invalid, and the fierce and dangerous brute. The latter listened to the movements of the Indian with that air of sagacity that a bear is known to possess, until another echo announced that he had also left the cavern, when it turned and came waddling up to Duncan, before whom it seated itself, in its natural attitude, erect like a man. The youth looked anxiously about him for some weapon, with which he might make a resistance against the attack he now seriously expected.\n\nIt seemed, however, as if the humor of the animal had suddenly changed. Instead of continuing its discontented growls, or manifesting any further signs of anger, the whole of its shaggy body shook violently, as if agitated by some strange internal convulsion. The huge and unwieldy talons pawed stupidly about the grinning muzzle, and while Heyward kept his eyes riveted on its movements with jealous watchfulness, the grim head fell on one side, and in its place appeared the honest, sturdy countenance of the scout, who was indulging from the bottom of his soul, in his own peculiar expression of merriment.\n\n\"Hist!\" said the wary woodsman, interrupting Heyward's exclamation of surprise; \"the varlets are about the place, and any sounds that are not natural to witchcraft would bring them back upon us in a body.\"\n\n\"Tell me the meaning of this masquerade; and why you have attempted so desperate an adventure.\"\n\n\"Ah! reason and calculation are often outdone by accident,\" returned the scout. \"But as a story should always commence at the beginning, I will tell you the whole in order. After we parted I placed the commandant and the Sagamore in an old beaver lodge, where they are safer from the Hurons than they would be in the garrison of Edward, for your high northwest Indians, not having as yet got the traders among them, continue to venerate the beaver. After which Uncas and I pushed for the other encampment, as was agreed; have you seen the lad?\"\n\n\"To my great grief! he is captive, and condemned to die at the rising of the sun.\"\n\n# THE MASQUERADER 268\n\n_The grim head fell on one side, and in its place appeared the honest, sturdy countenance of the scout_]\n\"I had misgivings that such would be his fate,\" resumed the scout, in a less confident and joyous tone. But soon regaining his naturally firm voice, he continued: \"His bad fortune is the true reason of my being here, for it would never do to abandon such a boy to the Hurons. A rare time the knaves would have of it, could they tie The Bounding Elk and The Long Carabine, as they call me, to the same stake! Though why they have given me such a name I never knew, there being as little likeness between the gifts of 'Killdeer,' and the performance of one of your real Canada carabynes, as there is between the natur' of a pipe-stone and a flint!\"\n\n\"Keep to your tale,\" said the impatient Heyward; \"we know not at what moment the Hurons may return.\"\n\n\"No fear of them. A conjurer must have his time, like a straggling priest in the settlements. We are as safe from interruption as a missionary would be at the beginning of a two hours' discourse. Well, Uncas and I fell in with a return party of the varlets; the lad was much too forward for a scout; nay, for that matter, being of hot blood, he was not so much to blame; and, after all, one of the Hurons proved a coward, and in fleeing led him into an ambushment.\"\n\n\"And dearly has he paid for the weakness!\"\n\nThe scout significantly passed his hand across his own throat, and nodded, as if he said, \"I comprehend your meaning.\" After which he continued, in a more audible though scarcely more intelligible language,--\n\n\"After the loss of the boy I turned upon the Hurons, as you may judge. There have been scrimmages atween one or two of their outlyers and myself; but that is neither here nor there. So, after I had shot the imps, I got in pretty nigh to the lodges without further commotion. Then what should luck do in my favor, but lead me to the very spot where one of the most famous conjurers of the tribe was dressing himself, as I well knew, for some great battle with Satan--though why should I call that luck, which it now seems was an especial ordering of Providence! So a judgmatical rap over the head stiffened the lying impostor for a time, and leaving him a bit of walnut for his supper, to prevent an uproar, and stringing him up atween two sapplings, I made free with his finery, and took the part of the bear on myself, in order that the operations might proceed.\"\n\n\"And admirably did you enact the character; the animal itself might have been shamed by the representation.\"\n\n\"Lord, major,\" returned the flattered woodsman, \"I should be but a poor scholar for one who has studied so long in the wilderness, did I not know how to set forth the movements and natur' of such a beast. Had it been now a catamount, or even a full-sized panther, I would have embellished a performance for you worth regarding. But it is no such marvellous feat to exhibit the feats of so dull a beast; though, for that matter too, a bear may be overacted. Yes, yes; it is not every imitator that knows natur' may be outdone easier than she is equalled. But all our work is yet before us: where is the gentle one?\"\n\n\"Heaven knows; I have examined every lodge in the village, without discovering the slightest trace of her presence in the tribe.\"\n\n\"You heard what the singer said, as he left us,--'She is at hand, and expects you'?\"\n\n\"I have been compelled to believe he alluded to this unhappy woman.\"\n\n\"The simpleton was frightened, and blundered through his message; but he had a deeper meaning. Here are walls enough to separate the whole settlement. A bear ought to climb; therefore will I take a look above them. There may be honey-pots hid in these rocks, and I am a beast you know, that has a hankering for the sweets.\"\n\nThe scout looked behind him, laughing at his own conceit, while he clambered up the partition, imitating, as he went, the clumsy motions of the beast he represented; but the instant the summit was gained he made a gesture for silence, and slid down with the utmost precipitation.\n\n\"She is here,\" he whispered, \"and by that door you will find her. I would have spoken a word of comfort to the afflicted soul; but the sight of such a monster might upset her reason. Though for that matter, major, you are none of the most inviting yourself in your paint.\"\n\nDuncan, who had already sprung eagerly forward, drew instantly back on hearing these discouraging words.\n\n\"Am I, then, so very revolting?\" he demanded, with an air of chagrin.\n\n\"You might not startle a wolf, or turn the Royal Americans from a charge; but I have seen the time when you had a better-favored look; your streaked countenances are not ill-judged of by the squaws, but young women of white blood give the preference to their own color. See,\" he added, pointing to a place where the water trickled from a rock, forming a little crystal spring before it found an issue through the adjacent crevices; \"you may easily get rid of the Sagamore's daub, and when you come back I will try my hand at a new embellishment. It's as common for a conjurer to alter his paint as for a buck in the settlements to change his finery.\"\n\nThe deliberate woodsman had little occasion to hunt for arguments to enforce his advice. He was yet speaking when Duncan availed himself of the water. In a moment every frightful or offensive mark was obliterated, and the youth appeared again in the lineaments with which he had been gifted by nature. Thus prepared for an interview with his mistress, he took a hasty leave of his companion, and disappeared through the indicated passage. The scout witnessed his departure with complacency, nodding his head after him, and muttering his good wishes; after which he very coolly set about an examination of the state of the larder, among the Hurons--the cavern, among other purposes, being used as a receptacle for the fruits of their hunts.\n\nDuncan had no other guide than a distant glimmering light, which served, however, the office of a polar star to the lover. By its aid he was enabled to enter the haven of his hopes, which was merely another apartment of the cavern, that had been solely appropriated to the safe-keeping of so important a prisoner as a daughter of the commandant of William Henry. It was profusely strewed with the plunder of that unlucky fortress. In the midst of this confusion he found her he sought, pale, anxious, and terrified, but lovely. David had prepared her for such a visit.\n\n\"Duncan!\" she exclaimed, in a voice that seemed to tremble at the sounds created by itself.\n\n\"Alice\" he answered, leaping carelessly among trunks, boxes, arms, and furniture, until he stood at her side.\n\n\"I knew that you would never desert me,\" she said, looking up with a momentary glow on her otherwise dejected countenance. \"But you are alone! grateful as it is to be thus remembered, I could wish to think you are not entirely alone.\"\n\nDuncan, observing that she trembled in a manner which betrayed her inability to stand, gently induced her to be seated, while he recounted those leading incidents which it has been our task to record. Alice listened with breathless interest; and though the young man touched lightly on the sorrows of the stricken father, taking care, however, not to wound the self-love of his auditor, the tears ran as freely down the cheeks of the daughter as though she had never wept before. The soothing tenderness of Duncan, however, soon quieted the first burst of her emotions, and she then heard him to the close with undivided attention, if not with composure.\n\n\"And now, Alice,\" he added, \"you will see how much is still expected of you. By the assistance of our experienced and invaluable friend, the scout, we may find our way from this savage people, but you will have to exert your utmost fortitude. Remember that you fly to the arms of your venerable parent, and how much his happiness, as well as your own, depends on those exertions.\"\n\n\"Can I do otherwise for a father who has done so much for me?\"\n\n\"And for me too,\" continued the youth, gently pressing the hand he held in both his own.\n\nThe look of innocence and surprise which he received in return convinced Duncan of the necessity of being more explicit.\n\n\"This is neither the place nor the occasion to detain you with selfish wishes,\" he added; \"but what heart loaded like mine would not wish to cast its burden? They say misery is the closest of all ties; our common suffering in your behalf left but little to be explained between your father and myself.\"\n\n\"And dearest Cora, Duncan; surely Cora was not forgotten?\"\n\n\"Not forgotten! no; regretted, as woman was seldom mourned before. Your venerable father knew no difference between his children; but I--Alice, you will not be offended when I say, that to me her worth was in a degree obscured--\"\n\n\"Then you knew not the merit of my sister,\" said Alice, withdrawing her hand; \"of you she ever speaks as of one who is her nearest friend.\"\n\n\"I would gladly believe her such,\" returned Duncan, hastily; \"I could wish her to be even more; but with you, Alice, I have the permission of your father to aspire to a still nearer and dearer tie.\"\n\nAlice trembled violently, and there was an instant during which she bent her face aside, yielding to the emotions common to her sex; but they quickly passed away, leaving her mistress of her deportment, if not of her affections.\n\n\"Heyward,\" she said, looking him full in the face with a touching expression of innocence and dependency, \"give me the sacred presence and the holy sanction of that parent before you urge me further.\"\n\n\"Though more I should not, less I could not say,\" the youth was about to answer, when he was interrupted by a light tap on his shoulder. Starting to his feet, he turned, and, confronting the intruder, his looks fell on the dark form and malignant visage of Magua. The deep guttural laugh of the savage sounded, at such a moment, to Duncan like the hellish taunt of a demon. Had he pursued the sudden and fierce impulse of the instant, he would have cast himself on the Huron, and committed their fortunes to the issue of a deadly struggle. But, without arms of any description, ignorant of what succor his subtle enemy could command, and charged with the safety of one who was just then dearer than ever to his heart, he no sooner entertained than he abandoned the desperate intention.\n\n\"What is your purpose?\" said Alice, meekly folding her arms on her bosom, and struggling to conceal an agony of apprehension in behalf of Heyward, in the usual cold and distant manner with which she received the visits of her captor.\n\nThe exulting Indian had resumed his austere countenance, though he drew warily back before the menacing glance of the young man's fiery eye. He regarded both his captives for a moment with a steady look, and then stepping aside, he dropped a log of wood across a door different from that by which Duncan had entered. The latter now comprehended the manner of his surprise, and believing himself irretrievably lost, he drew Alice to his bosom, and stood prepared to meet a fate which he hardly regretted, since it was to be suffered in such company. But Magua meditated no immediate violence. His first measures were very evidently taken to secure his new captive; nor did he even bestow a second glance at the motionless forms in the centre of the cavern, until he had completely cut off every hope of retreat through the private outlet he had himself used. He was watched in all his movements by Heyward, who, however, remained firm, still folding the fragile form of Alice to his heart, at once too proud and too hopeless to ask favor of an enemy so often foiled. When Magua had effected his object he approached his prisoners, and said in English,--\n\n\"The pale-faces trap the cunning beavers; but the redskins know how to take the Yengeese.\"\n\n\"Huron, do your worst!\" exclaimed the excited Heyward, forgetful that a double stake was involved in his life; \"you and your vengeance are alike despised.\"\n\n\"Will the white man speak these words at the stake?\" asked Magua; manifesting, at the same time, how little faith he had in the other's resolution by the sneer that accompanied his words.\n\n\"Here; singly to your face, or in the presence of your nation.\"\n\n\"Le Renard Subtil is a great chief!\" returned the Indian; \"he will go and bring his young men to see how bravely a pale-face can laugh at the tortures.\"\n\nHe turned away while speaking, and was about to leave the place through the avenue by which Duncan had approached, when a growl caught his ear, and caused him to hesitate. The figure of the bear appeared in the door, where it sat, rolling from side to side in its customary restlessness. Magua, like the father of the sick woman, eyed it keenly for a moment, as if to ascertain its character. He was far above the more vulgar superstitions of his tribe, and so soon as he recognized the well-known attire of the conjurer, he prepared to pass it in cool contempt. But a louder and more threatening growl caused him again to pause. Then he seemed as if suddenly resolved to trifle no longer, and moved resolutely forward. The mimic animal, which had advanced a little, retired slowly in his front, until it arrived again at the pass, when rearing on its hinder legs it beat the air with its paws, in the manner practised by its brutal prototype.\n\n\"Fool!\" exclaimed the chief, in Huron, \"go play with the children and squaws; leave men to their wisdom.\"\n\nHe once more endeavored to pass the supposed empiric, scorning even the parade of threatening to use the knife, or tomahawk, that was pendent from his belt. Suddenly the beast extended its arms, or rather legs, and inclosed him in a grasp that might have vied with the far-famed power of the \"bear's hug\" itself. Heyward had watched the whole procedure, on the part of Hawkeye, with breathless interest. At first he relinquished his hold of Alice; then he caught up a thong of buckskin, which had been used around some bundle, and when he beheld his enemy with his two arms pinned to his side by the iron muscles of the scout, he rushed upon him, and effectually secured them there. Arms, legs, and feet were encircled in twenty folds of the thong, in less time than we have taken to record the circumstance. When the formidable Huron was completely pinioned, the scout released his hold, and Duncan laid his enemy on his back, utterly helpless.\n\nThroughout the whole of this sudden and extraordinary operation, Magua, though he had struggled violently, until assured he was in the hands of one whose nerves were far better strung than his own, had not uttered the slightest exclamation. But when Hawkeye, by way of making a summary explanation of his conduct, removed the shaggy jaws of the beast, and exposed his own rugged and earnest countenance to the gaze of the Huron, the philosophy of the latter was so far mastered as to permit him to utter the never-failing,--\n\n\"Hugh!\"\n\n\"Ay! you've found your tongue,\" said his undisturbed conqueror; \"now, in order that you shall not use it to our ruin, I must make free to stop your mouth.\"\n\nAs there was no time to be lost, the scout immediately set about effecting so necessary a precaution; and when he had gagged the Indian, his enemy might safely have been considered as _hors de combat_.\n\n\"By what place did the imp enter?\" asked the industrious scout, when his work was ended. \"Not a soul has passed my way since you left me.\"\n\nDuncan pointed out the door by which Magua had come, and which now presented too many obstacles to a quick retreat.\n\n\"Bring on the gentle one, then,\" continued his friend; \"we must make a push for the woods by the other outlet.\"\n\n\"'Tis impossible!\" said Duncan; \"fear has overcome her, and she is helpless. Alice! my sweet, my own Alice, arouse yourself; now is the moment to fly. 'Tis in vain! she hears, but is unable to follow. Go, noble and worthy friend; save yourself, and leave me to my fate!\"\n\n\"Every trail has its end, and every calamity brings its lesson!\" returned the scout. \"There, wrap her in them Indian cloths. Conceal all of her little form. Nay, that foot has no fellow in the wilderness; it will betray her. All, every part. Now take her in your arms, and follow. Leave the rest to me.\"\n\nDuncan, as may be gathered from the words of his companion, was eagerly obeying; and as the other finished speaking, he took the light person of Alice in his arms, and followed on the footsteps of the scout. They found the sick woman as they had left her, still alone, and passed swiftly on, by the natural gallery, to the place of entrance. As they approached the little door of bark, a murmur of voices without announced that the friends and relatives of the invalid were gathered about the place, patiently awaiting a summons to re-enter.\n\n\"If I open my lips to speak,\" Hawkeye whispered, \"my English, which is the genuine tongue of a white-skin, will tell the varlets that an enemy is among them. You must give 'em your jargon, major; and say that we have shut the evil spirit in the cave, and are taking the woman to the woods in order to find strengthening roots. Practyse all your cunning, for it is a lawful undertaking.\"\n\nThe door opened a little, as if one without was listening to the proceedings within, and compelled the scout to cease his directions. A fierce growl repelled the eavesdropper, and then the scout boldly threw open the covering of bark, and left the place, enacting the character of the bear as he proceeded. Duncan kept close at his heels, and so found himself in the centre of a cluster of twenty anxious relatives and friends.\n\nThe crowd fell back a little, and permitted the father, and one who appeared to be the husband of the woman, to approach.\n\n\"Has my brother driven away the evil spirit?\" demanded the former. \"What has he in his arms?\"\n\n\"Thy child,\" returned Duncan, gravely; \"the disease has gone out of her; it is shut up in the rocks. I take the woman to a distance, where I will strengthen her against any further attacks. She shall be in the wigwam of the young man when the sun comes again.\"\n\nWhen the father had translated the meaning of the stranger's words into the Huron language, a suppressed murmur announced the satisfaction with which the intelligence was received. The chief himself waved his hand for Duncan to proceed, saying aloud, in a firm voice, and with a lofty manner,--\n\n\"Go; I am a man, and I will enter the rock and fight the wicked one.\"\n\nHeyward had gladly obeyed, and was already past the little group, when these startling words arrested him.\n\n\"Is my brother mad?\" he exclaimed; \"is he cruel! He will meet the disease, and it will enter him; or he will drive out the disease, and it will chase his daughter into the woods. No; let my children wait without, and if the spirit appears beat him down with clubs. He is cunning, and will bury himself in the mountain, when he sees how many are ready to fight him.\"\n\nThis singular warning had the desired effect. Instead of entering the cavern, the father and husband drew their tomahawks, and posted themselves in readiness to deal their vengeance on the imaginary tormentor of their sick relative, while the women and children broke branches from the bushes, or seized fragments of the rock, with a similar intention. At this favorable moment the counterfeit conjurers disappeared.\n\nHawkeye, at the same time that he had presumed so far on the nature of the Indian superstitions, was not ignorant that they were rather tolerated than relied on by the wisest of the chiefs. He well knew the value of time in the present emergency. Whatever might be the extent of the self-delusion of his enemies, and however it had tended to assist his schemes, the slightest cause of suspicion, acting on the subtle nature of an Indian, would be likely to prove fatal. Taking the path, therefore, that was most likely to avoid observation, he rather skirted than entered the village. The warriors were still to be seen in the distance, by the fading light of the fires, stalking from lodge to lodge. But the children had abandoned their sports for their beds of skins, and the quiet of night was already beginning to prevail over the turbulence and excitement of so busy and important an evening.\n\nAlice revived under the renovating influence of the open air, and as her physical rather than her mental powers had been the subject of weakness, she stood in no need of any explanation of that which had occurred.\n\n\"Now let me make an effort to walk,\" she said, when they had entered the forest, blushing, though unseen, that she had not been sooner able to quit the arms of Duncan; \"I am indeed restored.\"\n\n\"Nay, Alice, you are yet too weak.\"\n\nThe maiden struggled gently to release herself, and Heyward was compelled to part with his precious burden. The representative of the bear had certainly been an entire stranger to the delicious emotions of the lover while his arms encircled his mistress; and he was, perhaps, a stranger also to the nature of that feeling of ingenuous shame that oppressed the trembling Alice. But when he found himself at a suitable distance from the lodges he made a halt, and spoke on a subject of which he was thoroughly the master.\n\n\"This path will lead you to the brook,\" he said; \"follow its northern bank until you come to a fall; and mount the hill on your right, and you will see the fires of the other people. There you must go and demand protection; if they are true Delawares, you will be safe. A distant flight with that gentle one, just now, is impossible. The Hurons would follow up our trail, and master our scalps, before we had got a dozen miles. Go, and Providence be with you.\"\n\n\"And you!\" demanded Heyward, in surprise; \"surely we part not here?\"\n\n\"The Hurons hold the pride of the Delawares; the last of the high blood of the Mohicans is in their power,\" returned the scout; \"I go to see what can be done in his favor. Had they mastered your scalp, major, a knave should have fallen for every hair it held, as I promised; but if the young Sagamore is to be led to the stake, the Indians shall see also how a man without a cross can die.\"\n\nNot in the least offended with the decided preference that the sturdy woodsman gave to one who might, in some degree, be called the child of his adoption, Duncan still continued to urge such reasons against so desperate an effort as presented themselves. He was aided by Alice, who mingled her entreaties with those of Heyward that he would abandon a resolution that promised so much danger, with so little hope of success. Their eloquence and ingenuity were expended in vain. The scout heard them attentively, but impatiently, and finally closed the discussion, by answering, in a tone that instantly silenced Alice, while it told Heyward how fruitless any further remonstrances would be,--\n\n\"I have heard,\" he said, \"that there is a feeling in youth which binds man to woman closer than the father is tied to the son. It may be so. I have seldom been where women of my color dwell; but such may be the gifts of nature in the settlements. You have risked life, and all that is dear to you, to bring off this gentle one, and I suppose that some such disposition is at the bottom of it all. As for me, I taught the lad the real character of a rifle; and well has he paid me for it. I have fou't at his side in many a bloody scrimmage; and so long as I could hear the crack of his piece in one ear, and that of the Sagamore in the other, I knew no enemy was on my back. Winters and summers, nights and days, have we roved the wilderness in company, eating of the same dish, one sleeping while the other watched; and afore it shall be said that Uncas was taken to the torment, and I at hand--There is but a single Ruler of us all, whatever may be the color of the skin; and Him I call to witness, that before the Mohican boy shall perish for the want of a friend, good faith shall depart the 'arth, and 'Killdeer' become as harmless as the tooting we'pon of the singer!\"\n[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n\n# THE LOVERS 278\n\n_Heyward and Alice took their way together towards the distant village of the Delawares_]\nDuncan released his hold on the arm of the scout, who turned, and steadily retraced his steps towards the lodges. After pausing a moment to gaze at his retiring form, the successful and yet sorrowful Heyward, and Alice, took their way together towards the distant village of the Delawares.\n\n\"_Bot._--Let me play the lion too.\"\n\n_Midsummer Night's Dream._\n\nNotwithstanding the high resolution of Hawkeye, he fully comprehended all the difficulties and dangers he was about to incur. In his return to the camp, his acute and practised intellects were intently engaged in devising means to counteract a watchfulness and suspicion on the part of his enemies, that he knew were, in no degree, inferior to his own. Nothing but the color of his skin had saved the lives of Magua and the conjurer, who would have been the first victims sacrificed to his own security, had not the scout believed such an act, however congenial it might be to the nature of an Indian, utterly unworthy of one who boasted a descent from men that knew no cross of blood. Accordingly, he trusted to the withes and ligaments with which he had bound his captives, and pursued his way directly towards the centre of the lodges.\n\nAs he approached the buildings, his steps became more deliberate, and his vigilant eye suffered no sign, whether friendly or hostile, to escape him. A neglected hut was a little in advance of the others, and appeared as if it had been deserted when half completed--most probably on account of failing in some of the more important requisites; such as food or water. A faint light glimmered through its cracks, however, and announced that, notwithstanding its imperfect structure, it was not without a tenant. Thither, then, the scout proceeded, like a prudent general, who was about to feel the advanced positions of his enemy, before he hazarded the main attack.\n\nThrowing himself into a suitable posture for the beast he represented, Hawkeye crawled to a little opening, where he might command a view of the interior. It proved to be the abiding-place of David Gamut. Hither the faithful singing-master had now brought himself, together with all his sorrows, his apprehensions, and his meek dependence on the protection of Providence. At the precise moment when his ungainly person came under the observation of the scout, in the manner just mentioned, the woodsman himself, though in his assumed character, was the subject of the solitary being's profoundest reflections.\n\nHowever implicit the faith of David was in the performance of ancient miracles, he eschewed the belief of any direct supernatural agency in the management of modern morality. In other words, while he had implicit faith in the ability of Balsam's ass to speak, he was somewhat skeptical on the subject of a bear's singing; and yet he had been assured of the latter, on the testimony of his own exquisite organs. There was something in his air and manner that betrayed to the scout the utter confusion of the state of his mind. He was seated on a pile of brush, a few twigs from which occasionally fed his low fire, with his head leaning on his arm, in a posture of melancholy musing. The costume of the votary of music had undergone no other alteration from that so lately described, except that he had covered his bald head with the triangular beaver, which had not proved sufficiently alluring to excite the cupidity of any of his captors.\n\nThe ingenious Hawkeye, who recalled the hasty manner in which the other had abandoned his post at the bedside of the sick woman, was not without his suspicions concerning the subject of so much solemn deliberation. First making the circuit of the hut, and ascertaining that it stood quite alone, and that the character of its inmate was likely to protect it from visitors, he ventured through its low door, into the very presence of Gamut. The position of the latter brought the fire between them; and when Hawkeye had seated himself on end, near a minute elapsed, during which the two remained regarding each other without speaking. The suddenness and the nature of the surprise had nearly proved too much for--we will not say the philosophy--but for the faith and resolution of David. He fumbled for his pitch-pipe, and arose with a confused intention of attempting a musical exorcism.\n\n\"Dark and mysterious monster!\" he exclaimed, while with trembling hands he disposed of his auxiliary eyes, and sought his never-failing resource in trouble, the gifted version of the Psalms: \"I know not your nature nor intents; but if aught you meditate against the person and rights of one of the humblest servants of the temple, listen to the inspired language of the youth of Israel, and repent.\"\n\nThe bear shook his shaggy sides, and then a well-known voice replied,--\n\n\"Put up the tooting we'pon, and teach your throat modesty. Five words of plain and comprehensible English are worth, just now, an hour of squalling.\"\n\n\"What art thou!\" demanded David, utterly disqualified to pursue his original intention, and nearly gasping for breath.\n\n\"A man like yourself; and one whose blood is as little tainted by the cross of a bear, or an Indian, as your own. Have you so soon forgotten from whom you received the foolish instrument you hold in your hand?\"\n\n\"Can these things be?\" returned David, breathing more freely, as the truth began to dawn upon him. \"I have found many marvels during my sojourn with the heathen, but surely nothing to excel this!\"\n\n\"Come, come,\" returned Hawkeye, uncasing his honest countenance, the better to assure the wavering confidence of his companion; \"you may see a skin, which, if it be not as white as one of the gentle ones, has no tinge of red to it that the winds of heaven and the sun have not bestowed. Now let us to business.\"\n\n\"First tell me of the maiden, and of the youth who so bravely sought her,\" interrupted David.\n\n\"Ay, they are happily freed from the tomahawks of these varlets. But can you put me on the scent of Uncas?\"\n\n\"The young man is in bondage, and much I fear his death is decreed. I greatly mourn that one so well disposed should die in his ignorance, and I have sought a goodly hymn--\"\n\n\"Can you lead me to him?\"\n\n\"The task will not be difficult,\" returned David, hesitating; \"though I greatly fear your presence would rather increase than mitigate his unhappy fortunes.\"\n\n\"No more words, but lead on,\" returned Hawkeye, concealing his face again, and setting the example in his own person, by instantly quitting the lodge.\n\nAs they proceeded, the scout ascertained that his companion found access to Uncas, under privilege of his imaginary infirmity, aided by the favor he had acquired with one of the guards, who, in consequence of speaking a little English, had been selected by David as the subject of a religious conversation. How far the Huron comprehended the intentions of his new friend, may well be doubted; but as exclusive attention is as flattering to a savage as to a more civilized individual, it had produced the effect we have mentioned. It is unnecessary to repeat the shrewd manner with which the scout extracted these particulars from the simple David; neither shall we dwell in this place on the nature of the instructions he delivered, when completely master of all the necessary facts; as the whole will be sufficiently explained to the reader in the course of the narrative.\n\nThe lodge in which Uncas was confined was in the very centre of the village, and in a situation, perhaps, more difficult than any other to approach, or leave, without observation. But it was not the policy of Hawkeye to affect the least concealment. Presuming on his disguise, and his ability to sustain the character he had assumed, he took the most plain and direct route to the place. The hour, however, afforded him some little of that protection which he appeared so much to despise. The boys were already buried in sleep, and all the women, and most of the warriors, had retired to their lodges for the night. Four or five of the latter only lingered about the door of the prison of Uncas, wary but close observers of the manner of their captive.\n\nAt the sight of Gamut, accompanied by one in the well known masquerade of their most distinguished conjurer, they readily made way for them both. Still they betrayed no intention to depart. On the other hand, they were evidently disposed to remain bound to the place by an additional interest in the mysterious mummeries that they of course expected from such a visit.\n\nFrom the total inability of the scout to address the Hurons in their own language, he was compelled to trust the conversation entirely to David. Notwithstanding the simplicity of the latter, he did ample justice to the instructions he had received, more than fulfilling the strongest hopes of his teacher.\n\n\"The Delawares are women!\" he exclaimed, addressing himself to the savage who had a slight understanding of the language in which he spoke; \"the Yengeese, my foolish countrymen, have told them to take up the tomahawk, and strike their fathers in the Canadas, and they have forgotten their sex. Does my brother wish to hear Le Cerf Agile ask for his petticoats, and see him weep before the Hurons, at the stake?\"\n\nThe exclamation \"Hugh!\" delivered in a strong tone of assent, announced the gratification the savage would receive in witnessing such an exhibition of weakness in an enemy so long hated and so much feared.\n\n\"Then let him step aside, and the cunning man will blow upon the dog! Tell it to my brothers.\"\n\nThe Huron explained the meaning of David to his fellows, who, in their turn, listened to the project with that sort of satisfaction that their untamed spirits might be expected to find in such a refinement in cruelty. They drew back a little from the entrance, and motioned to the supposed conjurer to enter. But the bear, instead of obeying, maintained the seat it had taken, and growled.\n\n\"The cunning man is afraid that his breath will blow upon his brothers, and take away their courage too,\" continued David, improving the hint he received; \"they must stand farther off.\"\n\nThe Hurons, who would have deemed such a misfortune the heaviest calamity that could befall them, fell back in a body, taking a position where they were out of ear-shot, though at the same time they could command a view of the entrance to the lodge. Then, as if satisfied of their safety, the scout left his position, and slowly entered the place. It was silent and gloomy, being tenanted solely by the captive, and lighted by the dying embers of a fire, which had been used for the purposes of cookery.\n\nUncas occupied a distant corner, in a reclining attitude, being rigidly bound, both hands and feet, by strong and painful withes. When the frightful object first presented itself to the young Mohican, he did not deign to bestow a single glance on the animal. The scout, who had left David at the door, to ascertain they were not observed, thought it prudent to preserve his disguise until assured of their privacy. Instead of speaking, therefore, he exerted himself to enact one of the antics of the animal he represented. The young Mohican, who at first believed his enemies had sent in a real beast to torment him, and try his nerves, detected, in those performances that to Heyward had appeared so accurate, certain blemishes, that at once betrayed the counterfeit. Had Hawkeye been aware of the low estimation in which the more skilful Uncas held his representations, he would probably have prolonged the entertainment a little in pique. But the scornful expression of the young man's eye admitted of so many constructions, that the worthy scout was spared the mortification of such a discovery. As soon, therefore, as David gave the pre-concerted signal, a low hissing sound was heard in the lodge, in place of the fierce growlings of the bear.\n\nUncas had cast his body back against the wall of the hut, and closed his eyes, as if willing to exclude so contemptible and disagreeable an object from his sight. But the moment the noise of the serpent was heard, he arose, and cast his looks on each side of him, bending his head low, and turning it inquiringly in every direction, until his keen eye rested on the shaggy monster, where it remained riveted, as though fixed by the power of a charm. Again the same sounds were repeated, evidently proceeding from the mouth of the beast. Once more the eyes of the youth roamed over the interior of the lodge, and returning to their former resting place, he uttered, in a deep, suppressed voice,--\n\n\"Hawkeye!\"\n\n\"Cut his bands,\" said Hawkeye to David, who just then approached them.\n\nThe singer did as he was ordered, and Uncas found his limbs released. At the same moment the dried skin of the animal rattled, and presently the scout arose to his feet, in proper person. The Mohican appeared to comprehend the nature of the attempt his friend had made, intuitively; neither tongue nor feature betraying another symptom of surprise. When Hawkeye had cast his shaggy vestment, which was done by simply loosing certain thongs of skin, he drew a long glittering knife, and put it in the hands of Uncas.\n\n\"The red Hurons are without,\" he said; \"let us be ready.\"\n\nAt the same time he laid his finger significantly on another similar weapon, both being the fruits of his prowess among their enemies during the evening.\n\n\"We will go,\" said Uncas.\n\n\"Whither?\"\n\n\"To the Tortoises; they are the children of my grandfathers.\"\n\n\"Ay, lad,\" said the scout in English--a language he was apt to use when a little abstracted in mind; \"the same blood runs in your veins, I believe; but time and distance have a little changed its color. What shall we do with the Mingos at the door? They count six, and this singer is as good as nothing.\"\n\n\"The Hurons are boasters,\" said Uncas scornfully; \"their 'totem' is a moose, and they run like snails. The Delawares are children of the tortoise, and they outstrip the deer.\"\n\n\"Ay, lad, there is truth in what you say; and I doubt not, on a rush, you would pass the whole nation; and, in a straight race of two miles, would be in, and get your breath again, afore a knave of them all was within hearing of the other village. But the gift of a white man lies more in his arms than in his legs. As for myself, I can brain a Huron as well as a better man; but when it comes to a race, the knaves would prove too much for me.\"\n\nUncas, who had already approached the door, in readiness to lead the way, now recoiled; and placed himself, once more, in the bottom of the lodge. But Hawkeye, who was too much occupied with his own thoughts to note the movement, continued speaking more to himself than to his companion.\n\n\"After all,\" he said, \"it is unreasonable to keep one man in bondage to the gifts of another. So, Uncas, you had better take the leap, while I put on the skin again, and trust to cunning for want of speed.\"\n\nThe young Mohican made no reply, but quietly folded his arms, and leaned his body against one of the upright posts that supported the wall of the hut.\n\n\"Well,\" said the scout, looking up at him, \"why do you tarry? There will be time enough for me, as the knaves will give chase to you at first.\"\n\n\"Uncas will stay,\" was the calm reply.\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"To fight with his father's brother, and die with the friend of the Delawares.\"\n\n\"Ay, lad,\" returned Hawkeye, squeezing the hand of Uncas between his own iron fingers; \"'twould have been more like a Mingo than a Mohican had you left me. But I thought I would make the offer, seeing that youth commonly loves life. Well, what can't be done by main courage, in war, must be done by circumvention. Put on the skin; I doubt not you can play the bear nearly as well as myself.\"\n\nWhatever might have been the private opinion of Uncas of their respective abilities in this particular, his grave countenance manifested no opinion of his own superiority. He silently and expeditiously encased himself in the covering of the beast, and then awaited such other movements as his more aged companion saw fit to dictate.\n\n\"Now, friend,\" said Hawkeye, addressing David, \"an exchange of garments will be a great convenience to you, inasmuch as you are but little accustomed to the make-shifts of the wilderness. Here, take my hunting shirt and cap, and give me your blanket and hat. You must trust me with the book and spectacles, as well as the tooter, too; if we ever meet again, in better times, you shall have all back again, with many thanks into the bargain.\"\n\nDavid parted with the several articles named with a readiness that would have done great credit to his liberality, had he not certainly profited, in many particulars, by the exchange. Hawkeye was not long in assuming his borrowed garments; and when his restless eyes were hid behind the glasses, and his head was surmounted by the triangular beaver, as their statures were not dissimilar, he might readily have passed for the singer by star-light. As soon as these dispositions were made, the scout turned to David, and gave him his parting instructions.\n\n\"Are you much given to cowardice?\" he bluntly asked, by way of obtaining a suitable understanding of the whole case before he ventured a prescription.\n\n\"My pursuits are peaceful, and my temper, I humbly trust, is greatly given to mercy and love,\" returned David, a little nettled at so direct an attack on his manhood; \"but there are none who can say that I have ever forgotten my faith in the Lord, even in the greatest straits.\"\n\n\"Your chiefest danger will be at the moment when the savages find out that they have been deceived. If you are not then knocked in the head, your being a non-composser will protect you; and you'll then have good reason to expect to die in your bed. If you stay, it must be to sit down here in the shadow, and take the part of Uncas, until such times as the cunning of the Indians discover the cheat, when, as I have already said, your time of trial will come. So choose for yourself,--to make a rush or tarry here.\"\n\n\"Even so,\" said David, firmly; \"I will abide in the place of the Delaware. Bravely and generously has he battled in my behalf; and this, and more, will I dare in his service.\"\n\n\"You have spoken as a man, and like one who, under wiser schooling, would have been brought to better things. Hold your head down, and draw in your legs; their formation might tell the truth too early. Keep silent as long as may be; and it would be wise, when you do speak, to break out suddenly in one of your shoutings, which will serve to remind the Indians that you are not altogether as responsible as men should be. If, however, they take your scalp, as I trust and believe they will not, depend on it, Uncas and I will not forget the deed, but revenge it as becomes true warriors and trusty friends.\"\n\n\"Hold!\" said David, perceiving that with this assurance they were about to leave him; \"I am an unworthy and humble follower of One who taught not the damnable principle of revenge. Should I fall, therefore, seek no victims to my manes, but rather forgive my destroyers; and if you remember them at all, let it be in prayers for the enlightening of their minds, and for their eternal welfare.\"\n\nThe scout hesitated, and appeared to muse.\n\n\"There is a principle in that,\" he said, \"different from the law of the woods; and yet it is fair and noble to reflect upon.\" Then, heaving a heavy sigh, probably among the last he ever drew in pining for a condition he had so long abandoned, he added, \"It is what I would wish to practise, myself, as one without a cross of blood, though it is not always easy to deal with an Indian as you would with a fellow Christian. God bless you, friend; I do believe your scent is not greatly wrong, when the matter is duly considered, and keeping eternity before the eyes, though much depends on the natural gifts, and the force of temptation.\"\n\nSo saying, the scout returned and shook David cordially by the hand; after which act of friendship he immediately left the lodge, attended by the new representative of the beast.\n\nThe instant Hawkeye found himself under the observation of the Hurons, he drew up his tall form in the rigid manner of David, threw out his arm in the act of keeping time, and commenced what he intended for an imitation of his psalmody. Happily for the success of this delicate adventure, he had to deal with ears but little practised in the concord of sweet sounds, or the miserable effort would infallibly have been detected. It was necessary to pass within a dangerous proximity of the dark group of the savages, and the voice of the scout grew louder as they drew nigher. When at the nearest point, the Huron who spoke the English thrust out an arm, and stopped the supposed singing-master.\n\n\"The Delaware dog!\" he said, leaning forward, and peering through the dim light to catch the expression of the other's features; \"is he afraid? will the Hurons hear his groans?\"\n\nA growl so exceedingly fierce and natural proceeded from the beast, that the young Indian released his hold and started aside, as if to assure himself that it was not a veritable bear, and no counterfeit, that was rolling before him. Hawkeye, who feared his voice would betray him to his subtle enemies, gladly profited by the interruption, to break out anew in such a burst of musical expression as would, probably, in a more refined state of society have been termed \"a grand crash.\" Among his actual auditors, however, it merely gave him an additional claim to that respect which they never withhold from such as are believed to be the subjects of mental alienation. The little knot of Indians drew back in a body, and suffered, as they thought, the conjurer and his inspired assistant to proceed.\n\nIt required no common exercise of fortitude in Uncas and the scout, to continue the dignified and deliberate pace they had assumed in passing the lodges; especially as they immediately perceived that curiosity had so far mastered fear, as to induce the watchers to approach the hut, in order to witness the effect of the incantations. The least injudicious or impatient movement on the part of David might betray them, and time was absolutely necessary to insure the safety of the scout. The loud noise the latter conceived it politic to continue, drew many curious gazers to the doors of the different huts as they passed; and once or twice a dark-looking warrior stepped across their path, led to the act by superstition or watchfulness. They were not, however, interrupted; the darkness of the hour, and the coldness of the attempt, proving their principal friends.\n\nThe adventurers had got clear of the village, and were now swiftly approaching the shelter of the woods, when a loud and long cry arose from the lodge where Uncas had been confined. The Mohican started on his feet, and shook his shaggy covering, as though the animal he counterfeited was about to make some desperate effort.\n\n\"Hold!\" said the scout, grasping his friend by the shoulder, \"let them yell again! 'Twas nothing but wonderment.\"\n\nHe had no occasion to delay, for the next instant a burst of cries filled the outer air, and ran along the whole extent of the village. Uncas cast his skin, and stepped forth in his own beautiful proportions. Hawkeye tapped him lightly on the shoulder, and glided ahead.\n\n\"Now let the devils strike our scent!\" said the scout, tearing two rifles, with all their attendant accoutrements, from beneath a bush, and flourishing \"Killdeer\" as he handed Uncas his weapon; \"two, at least, will find it to their deaths.\"\n\nThen throwing their pieces to a low trail, like sportsmen in readiness for their game, they dashed forward, and were soon buried in the sombre darkness of the forest.\n\n\"_Ant._ I shall remember: When Caesar says _Do this_, it is performed.\"\n\n_Julius Caesar._\n\nThe impatience of the savages who lingered about the prison of Uncas, as has been seen, had overcome their dread of the conjurer's breath. They stole cautiously, and with beating hearts, to a crevice, through which the faint light of the fire was glimmering. For several minutes they mistook the form of David for that of their prisoner; but the very accident which Hawkeye had foreseen occurred. Tired of keeping the extremities of his long person so near together, the singer gradually suffered the lower limbs to extend themselves, until one of his misshapen feet actually came in contact with and shoved aside the embers of the fire. At first the Hurons believed the Delaware had been thus deformed by witchcraft. But when David, unconscious of being observed, turned his head, and exposed his simple, mild countenance, in place of the haughty lineaments of their prisoner, it would have exceeded the credulity of even a native to have doubted any longer. They rushed together into the lodge, and laying their hands, with but little ceremony, on their captive, immediately detected the imposition. Then arose the cry first heard by the fugitives. It was succeeded by the most frantic and angry demonstrations of vengeance. David, however firm in his determination to cover the retreat of his friends, was compelled to believe that his own final hour had come. Deprived of his book and his pipe, he was fain to trust to a memory that rarely failed him on such subjects; and breaking forth in a loud and impassioned strain, he endeavored to soothe his passage into the other world, by singing the opening verse of a funeral anthem. The Indians were seasonably reminded of his infirmity, and rushing into the open air, they aroused the village in the manner described.\n\nA native warrior fights as he sleeps, without the protection of anything defensive. The sounds of the alarm were, therefore, hardly uttered, before two hundred men were afoot, and ready for the battle or the chase, as either might be required. The escape was soon known; and the whole tribe crowded, in a body, around the council-lodge, impatiently awaiting the instruction of their chiefs. In such a sudden demand on their wisdom, the presence of the cunning Magua could scarcely fail of being needed. His name was mentioned, and all looked round in wonder that he did not appear. Messengers were then despatched to his lodge, requiring his presence.\n\nIn the meantime, some of the swiftest and most discreet of the young men were ordered to make the circuit of the clearing, under cover of the woods, in order to ascertain that their suspected neighbors, the Delawares, designed no mischief. Women and children ran to and fro; and in short, the whole encampment exhibited another scene of wild and savage confusion. Gradually, however, these symptoms of disorder diminished; and in a few minutes the oldest and most distinguished chiefs were assembled in the lodge, in grave consultation.\n\nThe clamor of many voices soon announced that a party approached, who might be expected to communicate some intelligence that would explain the mystery of the novel surprise. The crowd without gave way, and several warriors entered the place, bringing with them the hapless conjurer, who had been left so long by the scout in duress.\n\nNotwithstanding this man was held in very unequal estimation among the Hurons, some believing implicitly in his power, and others deeming him an impostor, he was now listened to by all with the deepest attention. When his brief story was ended, the father of the sick woman stepped forth, and, in a few pithy expressions, related, in his turn, what he knew. These two narratives gave a proper direction to the subsequent inquiries, which were now made with the characteristic cunning of savages.\n\nInstead of rushing in a confused and disorderly throng to the cavern, ten of the wisest and firmest among the chiefs were selected to prosecute the investigation. As no time was to be lost, the instant the choice was made the individuals appointed rose in a body, and left the place without speaking. On reaching the entrance, the younger men in advance made way for their seniors; and the whole proceeded along the low, dark gallery, with the firmness of warriors ready to devote themselves to the public good, though, at the same time, secretly doubting the nature of the power with which they were about to contend.\n\nThe outer apartment of the cavern was silent and gloomy. The woman lay in her usual place and posture, though there were those present who affirmed they had seen her borne to the woods, by the supposed \"medicine of the white men.\" Such a direct and palpable contradiction of the tale related by the father, caused all eyes to be turned on him. Chafed by the silent imputation, and inwardly troubled by so unaccountable a circumstance, the chief advanced to the side of the bed, and stooping, cast an incredulous look at the features, as if distrusting their reality. His daughter was dead.\n\nThe unerring feeling of nature, for a moment prevailed, and the old warrior hid his eyes in sorrow. Then recovering his self-possession, he faced his companions, and pointing towards the corpse, he said, in the language of his people,--\n\n\"The wife of my young man has left us! the Great Spirit is angry with his children.\"\n\nThe mournful intelligence was received in solemn silence. After a short pause, one of the elder Indians was about to speak, when a dark-looking object was seen rolling out of an adjoining apartment, into the very centre of the room where they stood. Ignorant of the nature of the beings they had to deal with, the whole party drew back a little, and gazed in admiration, until the object fronted the light, and rising on end, exhibited the distorted, but still fierce and sullen features of Magua. The discovery was succeeded by a general exclamation of amazement.\n\nAs soon, however, as the true situation of the chief was understood, several ready knives appeared, and his limbs and tongue were quickly released. The Huron arose, and shook himself like a lion quitting his lair. Not a word escaped him, though his hand played convulsively with the handle of his knife, while his lowering eyes scanned the whole party, as if they sought an object suited to the first burst of his vengeance.\n\nIt was happy for Uncas and the scout, and even David, that they were all beyond the reach of his arm at such a moment; for, assuredly, no refinement in cruelty would then have deferred their deaths, in opposition to the promptings of the fierce temper that nearly choked him. Meeting everywhere faces that he knew as friends, the savage grated his teeth together like rasps of iron, and swallowed his passion for want of a victim on whom to vent it. This exhibition of anger was noted by all present; and, from an apprehension of exasperating a temper that was already chafed nearly to madness, several minutes were suffered to pass before another word was uttered. When, however, suitable time had elapsed, the oldest of the party spoke.\n\n\"My friend has found an enemy,\" he said. \"Is he nigh, that the Hurons may take revenge?\"\n\n\"Let the Delaware die!\" exclaimed Magua, in a voice of thunder.\n\nAnother long and expressive silence was observed, and was broken, as before, with due precaution, by the same individual.\n\n\"The Mohican is swift of foot, and leaps far,\" he said; \"but my young men are on his trail.\"\n\n\"Is he gone?\" demanded Magua, in tones so deep and guttural, that they seemed to proceed from his inmost chest.\n\n\"An evil spirit has been among us, and the Delaware has blinded our eyes.\"\n\n\"An evil spirit!\" repeated the other, mockingly; \"'tis the spirit that has taken the lives of so many Hurons; the spirit that slew my young men at 'the tumbling river'; that took their scalps at the 'healing spring'; and who has now bound the arms of Le Renard Subtil!\"\n\n\"Of whom does my friend speak?\"\n\n\"Of the dog who carries the heart and cunning of a Huron under a pale skin--La Longue Carabine.\"\n\nThe pronunciation of so terrible a name produced the usual effect among his auditors. But when time was given for reflection, and the warriors remembered that their formidable and daring enemy had even been in the bosom of their encampment, working injury, fearful rage took the place of wonder, and all those fierce passions with which the bosom of Magua had just been struggling were suddenly transferred to his companions. Some among them gnashed their teeth in anger, others vented their feelings in yells, and some, again beat the air as frantically as if the object of their resentment were suffering under their blows. But this sudden outbreaking of temper as quickly subsided in the still and sullen restraint they most affected, in their moments of inaction.\n\nMagua who had in his turn found leisure for reflection, now changed his manner, and assumed the air of one who knew how to think and act with a dignity worthy of so grave a subject.\n\n\"Let us go to my people,\" he said; \"they wait for us.\"\n\nHis companions consented in silence, and the whole of the savage party left the cavern and returned to the council-lodge. When they were seated, all eyes turned on Magua, who understood, from such an indication, that, by common consent, they had devolved the duty of relating what had passed on him. He arose, and told his tale without duplicity or reservation. The whole deception practised by both Duncan and Hawkeye was, of course, laid naked; and no room was found, even for the most superstitious of the tribe, any longer to affix a doubt on the character of the occurrences. It was but too apparent that they had been insultingly, shamefully, disgracefully deceived. When he had ended, and resumed his seat, the collected tribe--for his auditors, in substance, included all the fighting men of the party--sat regarding each other like men astonished equally at the audacity and the success of their enemies. The next consideration, however, was the means and opportunities for revenge.\n\nAdditional pursuers were sent on the trail of the fugitives; and then the chiefs applied themselves, in earnest, to the business of consultation. Many different expedients were proposed by the elder warriors, in succession, to all of which Magua was a silent and respectful listener. That subtle savage had recovered his artifice and self-command, and now proceeded towards his object with his customary caution and skill. It was only when each one disposed to speak had uttered his sentiments, that he prepared to advance his own opinions. They were given with additional weight from the circumstance that some of the runners had already returned, and reported that their enemies had been traced so far as to leave no doubt of their having sought safety in the neighboring camp of their suspected allies, the Delawares. With the advantage of possessing this important intelligence, the chief warily laid his plans before his fellows, and, as might have been anticipated from his eloquence and cunning, they were adopted without a dissenting voice. They were, briefly, as follows, both in opinions and in motives.\n\nIt has been already stated that, in obedience to a policy rarely departed from, the sisters were separated so soon as they reached the Huron village. Magua had early discovered that in retaining the person of Alice, he possessed the most effectual check on Cora. When they parted, therefore, he kept the former within reach of his hand, consigning the one he most valued to the keeping of their allies. The arrangement was understood to be merely temporary, and was made as much with a view to flatter his neighbors as in obedience to the invariable rule of Indian policy.\n\nWhile goaded incessantly by those revengeful impulses that in a savage seldom slumber, the chief was still attentive to his more permanent personal interests. The follies and disloyalty committed in his youth were to be expiated by a long and painful penance, ere he could be restored to the full enjoyment of the confidence of his ancient people; and without confidence, there could be no authority in an Indian tribe. In this delicate and arduous situation, the crafty native had neglected no means of increasing his influence; and one of the happiest of his expedients had been the success with which he had cultivated the favor of their powerful and dangerous neighbors. The result of his experiment had answered all the expectations of his policy; for the Hurons were in no degree exempt from that governing principle of nature, which induces man to value his gifts precisely in the degree that they are appreciated by others.\n\nBut, while he was making this ostensible sacrifice to general considerations, Magua never lost sight of his individual motives. The latter had been frustrated by the unlooked-for events which had placed all his prisoners beyond his control; and he now found himself reduced to the necessity of suing for favors to those whom it had so lately been his policy to oblige.\n\nSeveral of the chiefs had proposed deep and treacherous schemes to surprise the Delawares, and, by gaining possession of their camp, to recover their prisoners by the same blow; for all agreed that their honor, their interests, and the peace and happiness of their dead countrymen, imperiously required them speedily to immolate some victims to their revenge. But plans so dangerous to attempt, and of such doubtful issue, Magua found little difficulty in defeating. He exposed their risk and fallacy with his usual skill; and it was only after he had removed every impediment, in the shape of opposing advice, that he ventured to propose his own projects.\n\nHe commenced by flattering the self-love of his auditors; a never-failing method of commanding attention. When he had enumerated the many different occasions on which the Hurons had exhibited their courage and prowess, in the punishment of insults, he digressed in a high encomium on the virtue of wisdom. He painted the quality, as forming the great point of difference between the beaver and other brutes; between brutes and men; and, finally, between the Hurons, in particular, and the rest of the human race. After he had sufficiently extolled the property of discretion, he undertook to exhibit in what manner its use was applicable to the present situation of their tribe. On the one hand, he said, was their great pale father, the governor of the Canadas, who had looked upon his children with a hard eye since their tomahawks had been so red; on the other, a people as numerous as themselves, who spoke a different language, possessed different interests, and loved them not, and who would be glad of any pretence to bring them in disgrace with the great white chief. Then he spoke of their necessities; of the gifts they had a right to expect for their past services; of their distance from their proper hunting-grounds and native villages; and of the necessity of consulting prudence more, and inclination less, in so critical circumstances. When he perceived that, while the old men applauded his moderation, many of the fiercest and most distinguished of the warriors listened to these politic plans with lowering looks, he cunningly led them back to the subject which they most loved. He spoke openly of the fruits of their wisdom, which he boldly pronounced would be a complete and final triumph over their enemies. He even darkly hinted that their success might be extended, with proper caution, in such a manner as to include the destruction of all whom they had reason to hate. In short, he so blended the warlike with the artful, the obvious with the obscure, as to flatter the propensities of both parties, and to leave to each subject of hope, while neither could say it clearly comprehended his intentions.\n\nThe orator, or the politician, who can produce such a state of things, is commonly popular with his contemporaries, however he may be treated by posterity. All perceived that more was meant than was uttered, and each one believed that the hidden meaning was precisely such as his own faculties enabled him to understand, or his own wishes led him to anticipate.\n\nIn this happy state of things, it is not surprising that the management of Magua prevailed. The tribe consented to act with deliberation, and with one voice they committed the direction of the whole affair to the government of the chief who had suggested such wise and intelligible expedients.\n\nMagua had now attained one great object of all his cunning and enterprise. The ground he had lost in the favor of his people was completely regained, and he found himself even placed at the head of affairs. He was, in truth, their ruler; and, so long as he could maintain his popularity, no monarch could be more despotic, especially while the tribe continued in a hostile country. Throwing off, therefore, the appearance of consultation, he assumed the grave air of authority necessary to support the dignity of his office.\n\nRunners were despatched for intelligence in different directions; spies were ordered to approach and feel the encampment of the Delawares; the warriors were dismissed to their lodges, with an intimation that their services would soon be needed; and the women and children were ordered to retire, with a warning that it was their province to be silent. When these several arrangements were made, Magua passed through the village, stopping here and there to pay a visit where he thought his presence might be flattering to the individual. He confirmed his friends in their confidence, fixed the wavering, and gratified all. Then he sought his own lodge. The wife the Huron chief had abandoned, when he was chased from among his people, was dead. Children he had none; and he now occupied a hut, without companion of any sort. It was, in fact, the dilapidated and solitary structure in which David had been discovered, and whom he had tolerated in his presence, on those few occasions when they met, with the contemptuous indifference of a haughty superiority.\n\nHither, then, Magua retired, when his labors of policy were ended. While others slept, however, he neither knew nor sought repose. Had there been one sufficiently curious to have watched the movements of the newly elected chief, he would have seen him seated in a corner of his lodge, musing on the subject of his future plans, from the hour of his retirement to the time he had appointed for the warriors to assemble again. Occasionally the air breathed through the crevices of the hut, and the low flames that fluttered about the embers of the fire threw their wavering light on the person of the sullen recluse. At such moments it would not have been difficult to have fancied the dusky savage the Prince of Darkness, brooding on his own fancied wrongs, and plotting evil.\n\nLong before the day dawned, however, warrior after warrior entered the solitary hut of Magua, until they had collected to the number of twenty. Each bore his rifle, and all the other accoutrements of war, though the paint was uniformly peaceful. The entrance of these fierce-looking beings was unnoticed; some seating themselves in the shadows of the place, and others standing like motionless statues, until the whole of the designated band was collected.\n\nThen Magua arose and gave the signal to proceed, marching himself in advance. They followed their leader singly, and in that well-known order which has obtained the distinguishing appellation of \"Indian file.\" Unlike other men engaged in the spirit-stirring business of war, they stole from their camp unostentatiously and unobserved, resembling a band of gliding spectres, more than warriors seeking the bubble reputation by deeds of desperate daring.\n\nInstead of taking the path which led directly towards the camp of the Delawares, Magua led his party for some distance down the windings of the stream, and along the little artificial lake of the beavers. The day began to dawn as they entered the clearing which had been formed by those sagacious and industrious animals. Though Magua, who had resumed his ancient garb, bore the outline of a fox on the dressed skin which formed his robe, there was one chief of his party who carried the beaver as his peculiar symbol, or \"totem.\" There would have been a species of profanity in the omission, had this man passed so powerful a community of his fancied kindred, without bestowing some evidence of his regard. Accordingly, he paused, and spoke in words as kind and friendly as if he were addressing more intelligent beings. He called the animals his cousins, and reminded them that his protecting influence was the reason they remained unharmed, while so many avaricious traders were prompting the Indians to take their lives. He promised a continuance of his favors, and admonished them to be grateful. After which, he spoke of the expedition in which he was himself engaged, and intimated, though with sufficient delicacy and circumlocution, the expediency of bestowing on their relative a portion of that wisdom for which they were so renowned.[24]\n\nDuring the utterance of this extraordinary address, the companions of the speaker were as grave and as attentive to his language as though they were all equally impressed with its propriety. Once or twice black objects were seen rising to the surface of the water, and the Huron expressed pleasure, conceiving that his words were not bestowed in vain. Just as he had ended his address, the head of a large beaver was thrust from the door of a lodge, whose earthen walls had been much injured, and which the party had believed, from its situation, to be uninhabited. Such an extraordinary sign of confidence was received by the orator as a highly favorable omen; and though the animal retreated a little precipitately, he was lavish of his thanks and commendations.\n\nWhen Magua thought sufficient time had been lost in gratifying the family affection of the warrior, he again made the signal to proceed. As the Indians moved away in a body, and with a step that would have been inaudible to the ears of any common man, the same venerable-looking beaver once more ventured his head from its cover. Had any of the Hurons turned to look behind them, they would have seen the animal watching their movements with an interest and sagacity that might easily have been mistaken for reason. Indeed, so very distinct and intelligible were the devices of the quadruped, that even the most experienced observer would have been at a loss to account for its actions, until the moment when the party entered the forest, when the whole would have been explained, by seeing the entire animal issue from the lodge, uncasing, by the act, the grave features of Chingachgook from his mask of fur.\n\n\"Brief, I pray you; for you see, 'tis a busy time with me.\"\n\n_Much Ado About Nothing._\n\nThe tribe, or rather half tribe, of Delawares, which has been so often mentioned, and whose present place of encampment was so nigh the temporary village of the Hurons, could assemble about an equal number of warriors with the latter people. Like their neighbors, they had followed Montcalm into the territories of the English crown, and were making heavy and serious inroads on the hunting-grounds of the Mohawks; though they had seen fit, with the mysterious reserve so common among the natives, to withhold their assistance at the moment when it was most required. The French had accounted for this unexpected defection on the part of their ally in various ways. It was the prevalent opinion, however, that they had been influenced by veneration for the ancient treaty, that had once made them dependent on the Six Nations for military protection, and now rendered them reluctant to encounter their former masters. As for the tribe itself, it had been content to announce to Montcalm, through his emissaries, with Indian brevity, that their hatchets were dull, and time was necessary to sharpen them. The politic captain of the Canadas had deemed it wiser to submit to entertain a passive friend, than by any acts of ill-judged severity to convert him into an open enemy.\n\nOn that morning when Magua led his silent party from the settlement of the beavers into the forest, in the manner described, the sun rose upon the Delaware encampment as if it had suddenly burst upon a busy people, actively employed in all the customary avocations of high noon. The women ran from lodge to lodge, some engaged in preparing their morning's meal, a few earnestly bent on seeking the comforts necessary to their habits, but more pausing to exchange hasty and whispered sentences with their friends. The warriors were lounging in groups, musing more than they conversed; and when a few words were uttered, speaking like men who deeply weighed their opinions. The instruments of the chase were to be seen in abundance among the lodges; but none departed. Here and there a warrior was examining his arms, with an attention that is rarely bestowed on the implements, when no other enemy than the beasts of the forest is expected to be encountered. And, occasionally, the eyes of a whole group were turned simultaneously towards a large and silent lodge in the centre of the village, as if it contained the subject of their common thoughts.\n\nDuring the existence of this scene, a man suddenly appeared at the farthest extremity of a platform of rock which formed the level of the village. He was without arms, and his paint tended rather to soften than increase the natural sternness of his austere countenance. When in full view of the Delawares he stopped, and made a gesture of amity, by throwing his arm upward towards heaven, and then letting it fall impressively on his breast. The inhabitants of the village answered his salute by a low murmur of welcome, and encouraged him to advance by similar indications of friendship. Fortified by these assurances, the dark figure left the brow of the natural rocky terrace, where it had stood a moment, drawn in a strong outline against the blushing morning sky, and moved with dignity into the very centre of the huts. As he approached, nothing was audible but the rattling of the light silver ornaments that loaded his arms and neck, and the tinkling of the little bells that fringed his deer-skin moccasins. He made, as he advanced, many courteous signs of greeting to the men he passed, neglecting to notice the women, however, like one who deemed their favor, in the present enterprise, of no importance. When he had reached the group in which it was evident, by the haughtiness of their common mien, that the principal chiefs were collected, the stranger paused, and then the Delawares saw that the active and erect form that stood before them was that of the well-known Huron chief, Le Renard Subtil.\n\nHis reception was grave, silent, and wary. The warriors in front stepped aside, opening the way to their most approved orator by the action; one who spoke all those languages that were cultivated among the northern aborigines.\n\n\"The wise Huron is welcome,\" said the Delaware, in the language of the Maquas; \"he is come to eat his 'succotash,'[25] with his brothers of the lakes.\"\n\n\"He is come,\" repeated Magua, bending his head with the dignity of an Eastern prince.\n\nThe chief extended his arm, and taking the other by the wrist, they once more exchanged friendly salutations. Then the Delaware invited his guest to enter his own lodge, and share his morning meal. The invitation was accepted; and the two warriors, attended by three or four of the old men, walked calmly away, leaving the rest of the tribe devoured by a desire to understand the reasons of so unusual a visit, and yet not betraying the least impatience by sign or word.\n\nDuring the short and frugal repast that followed, the conversation was extremely circumspect, and related entirely to the events of the hunt in which Magua had so lately been engaged. It would have been impossible for the most finished breeding to wear more of the appearance of considering the visit as a matter of course, than did his hosts, notwithstanding every individual present was perfectly aware that it must be connected with some secret object, and that probably of importance to themselves. When the appetites of the whole were appeased, the squaws removed the trenchers and gourd, and the two parties began to prepare themselves for a subtle trial of their wits.\n\n\"Is the face of my great Canada father turned again towards his Huron children?\" demanded the orator of the Delawares.\n\n\"When was it ever otherwise?\" returned Magua. \"He calls my people 'most beloved.'\"\n\nThe Delaware gravely bowed his acquiescence to what he knew to be false, and continued,--\n\n\"The tomahawks of your young men have been very red.\"\n\n\"It is so; but they are now bright and dull; for the Yengeese are dead, and the Delawares are our neighbors.\"\n\nThe other acknowledged the pacific compliment by a gesture of the hand, and remained silent. Then Magua, as if recalled to such a recollection, by the allusion to the massacre, demanded,--\n\n\"Does my prisoner give trouble to my brothers?\"\n\n\"She is welcome.\"\n\n\"The path between the Hurons and the Delawares is short, and it is open; let her be sent to my squaws, if she gives trouble to my brother.\"\n\n\"She is welcome,\" returned the chief of the latter nation, still more emphatically.\n\nThe baffled Magua continued silent several minutes, apparently indifferent, however, to the repulse he had received in this his open effort to gain possession of Cora.\n\n\"Do my young men leave the Delawares room on the mountains for their hunts?\" he at length continued.\n\n\"The Lenape are rulers of their own hills,\" returned the other, a little haughtily.\n\n\"It is well. Justice is the master of a redskin! Why should they brighten their tomahawks, and sharpen their knives against each other? Are not the pale-faces thicker than the swallows in the season of flowers?\"\n\n\"Good!\" exclaimed two or three of his auditors at the same time.\n\nMagua waited a little, to permit his words to soften the feelings of the Delawares, before he added,--\n\n\"Have there not been strange moccasins in the woods? Have not my brothers scented the feet of white men?\"\n\n\"Let my Canada father come,\" returned the other evasively; \"his children are ready to see him.\"\n\n\"When the great chief comes, it is to smoke with the Indians in their wigwams. The Hurons say, too, he is welcome. But the Yengeese have long arms, and legs that never tire! My young men dreamed they had seen the trail of the Yengeese nigh the village of the Delawares?\"\n\n\"They will not find the Lenape asleep.\"\n\n\"It is well. The warrior whose eye is open can see his enemy,\" said Magua, once more shifting his ground, when he found himself unable to penetrate the caution of his companion. \"I have brought gifts to my brother. His nation would not go on the war-path because they did not think it well; but their friends have remembered where they lived.\"\n\nWhen he had thus announced his liberal intention, the crafty chief arose, and gravely spread his presents before the dazzled eyes of his hosts. They consisted principally of trinkets of little value, plundered from the slaughtered females of William Henry. In the division of the baubles the cunning Huron discovered no less art than in their selection. While he bestowed those of greater value on the two most distinguished warriors, one of whom was his host, he seasoned his offerings to their inferiors with such well-timed and apposite compliments, as left them no grounds of complaint. In short, the whole ceremony contained such a happy blending of the profitable with the flattering, that it was not difficult for the donor immediately to read the effect of a generosity so aptly mingled with praise, in the eyes of those he addressed.\n\nThis well-judged and politic stroke on the part of Magua was not without instantaneous results. The Delawares lost their gravity in a much more cordial expression; and the host, in particular, after contemplating his own liberal share of the spoil for some moments with peculiar gratification, repeated with strong emphasis, the words,--\n\n\"My brother is a wise chief. He is welcome!\"\n\n\"The Hurons love their friends the Delawares,\" returned Magua. \"Why should they not? they are colored by the same sun, and their just men will hunt in the same grounds after death. The redskins should be friends, and look with open eyes on the white men. Has not my brother scented spies in the woods?\"\n\nThe Delaware, whose name in English signified \"Hard Heart,\" an appellation that the French had translated into \"Le Coeur-dur,\" forgot the obduracy of purpose, which had probably obtained him so significant a title. His countenance grew very sensibly less stern, and now deigned to answer more directly.\n\n\"There have been strange moccasins about my camp. They have been tracked into my lodges.\"\n\n\"Did my brother beat out the dogs?\" asked Magua, without adverting in any manner to the former equivocation of the chief.\n\n\"It would not do. The stranger is always welcome to the children of the Lenape.\"\n\n\"The stranger, but not the spy.\"\n\n\"Would the Yengeese send their women as spies? Did not the Huron chief say he took women in the battle?\"\n\n\"He told no lie. The Yengeese have sent out their scouts. They have been in my wigwams, but they found there no one to say welcome. Then they fled to the Delawares--for, say they, the Delawares are our friends; their minds are turned from their Canada father!\"\n\nThis insinuation was a home thrust, and one that in a more advanced state of society, would have entitled Magua to the reputation of a skilful diplomatist. The recent defection of the tribe had, as they well knew themselves, subjected the Delawares to much reproach among their French allies; and they were now made to feel that their future actions were to be regarded with jealousy and distrust. There was no deep insight into causes and effects necessary to foresee that such a situation of things was likely to prove highly prejudicial to their future movements. Their distant villages, their hunting-grounds, and hundreds of their women and children, together with a material part of their physical force, were actually within the limits of the French territory. Accordingly, this alarming annunciation was received, as Magua intended, with manifest disapprobation, if not with alarm.\n\n\"Let my father look in my face,\" said Le Coeur-dur; \"he will see no change. It is true, my young men did not go out on the war-path; they had dreams for not doing so. But they love and venerate the great white chief.\"\n\n\"Will he think so when he hears that his greatest enemy is fed in the camp of his children? When he is told a bloody Yengee smokes at your fire? That the pale-face who has slain so many of his friends goes in and out among the Delawares? Go! my great Canada father is not a fool!\"\n\n\"Where is the Yengee that the Delawares fear?\" returned the other; \"who has slain my young men? who is the mortal enemy of my Great Father!\"\n\n\"La Longue Carabine.\"\n\nThe Delaware warriors started at the well-known name, betraying, by their amazement, that they now learnt, for the first time, one so famous among the Indian allies of France was within their power.\n\n\"What does my brother mean?\" demanded Le Coeur-dur, in a tone that, by its wonder, far exceeded the usual apathy of his race.\n\n\"A Huron never lies!\" returned Magua coldly, leaning his head against the side of the lodge, and drawing his slight robe across his tawny breast. \"Let the Delawares count their prisoners; they will find one whose skin is neither red nor pale.\"\n\nA long and musing pause succeeded. The chief consulted apart with his companions, and messengers were despatched to collect certain others of the most distinguished men of the tribe.\n\nAs warrior after warrior dropped in, they were each made acquainted, in turn, with the important intelligence that Magua had just communicated. The air of surprise, and the usual low, deep, guttural exclamation, were common to them all. The news spread from mouth to mouth, until the whole encampment became powerfully agitated. The women suspended their labors, to catch such syllables as unguardedly fell from the lips of the consulting warriors. The boys deserted their sports, and walking fearlessly among their fathers, looked up in curious admiration, as they heard the brief exclamations of wonder they so freely expressed at the temerity of their hated foe. In short, every occupation was abandoned for the time, and all other pursuits seemed discarded, in order that the tribe might freely indulge, after their own peculiar manner, in an open expression of feeling.\n\nWhen the excitement had a little abated, the old men disposed themselves seriously to consider that which it became the honor and safety of their tribe to perform, under circumstances of so much delicacy and embarrassment. During all these movements, and in the midst of the general commotion, Magua had not only maintained his seat, but the very attitude he had originally taken, against the side of the lodge, where he continued as immovable, and, apparently, as unconcerned, as if he had no interest in the result. Not a single indication of the future intentions of his hosts, however, escaped his vigilant eyes. With his consummate knowledge of the nature of the people with whom he had to deal, he anticipated every measure on which they decided; and it might almost be said, that, in many instances, he knew their intentions, even before they became known to themselves.\n\nThe council of the Delawares was short. When it was ended, a general bustle announced that it was to be immediately succeeded by a solemn and formal assemblage of the nation. As such meetings were rare, and only called on occasions of the last importance, the subtle Huron, who still sat apart, a wily and dark observer of the proceedings, now knew that all his projects must be brought to their final issue. He therefore left the lodge, and walked silently forth to the place in front of the encampment whither the warriors were already beginning to collect.\n\nIt might have been half an hour before each individual, including even the women and children, was in his place. The delay had been created by the grave preparations that were deemed necessary to so solemn and unusual a conference. But when the sun was seen climbing above the tops of that mountain against whose bosom the Delawares had constructed their encampment, most were seated; and as his bright rays darted from behind the outline of trees that fringed the eminence, they fell upon as grave, as attentive, and as deeply interested a multitude, as was probably ever before lighted by his morning beams. Its number somewhat exceeded a thousand souls.\n\nIn a collection of such serious savages, there is never to be found any impatient aspirant after premature distinction, standing ready to move his auditors to some hasty, and, perhaps, injudicious discussion, in order that his own reputation may be the gainer. An act of so much precipitancy and presumption would seal the downfall of precocious intellect forever. It rested solely with the oldest and most experienced of the men to lay the subject of the conference before the people. Until such a one chose to make some movement, no deeds in arms, no natural gifts, nor any renown as an orator, would have justified the slightest interruption. On the present occasion, the aged warrior whose privilege it was to speak, was silent, seemingly oppressed with the magnitude of his subject. The delay had already continued long beyond the usual deliberative pause that always precedes a conference; but no sign of impatience or surprise escaped even the youngest boy. Occasionally, an eye was raised from the earth, where the looks of most were riveted, and strayed towards a particular lodge, that was, however, in no manner distinguished from those around it, except in the peculiar care that had been taken to protect it against the assaults of the weather.\n\nAt length, one of those low murmurs that are so apt to disturb a multitude, was heard, and the whole nation arose to their feet by a common impulse. At that the door of the lodge in question opened, and three men, issuing from it, slowly approached the place of consultation. They were all aged, even beyond that period to which the oldest present had reached; but one in the centre, who leaned on his companions for support, had numbered an amount of years to which the human race is seldom permitted to attain. His frame, which had once been tall and erect, like the cedar, was now bending under the pressure of more than a century. The elastic, light step of an Indian was gone, and in its place he was compelled to toil his tardy way over the ground, inch by inch. His dark, wrinkled countenance was in singular and wild contrast with the long white locks which floated on his shoulders in such thickness as to announce that generations had probably passed away since they had last been shorn.\n\nThe dress of this patriarch--for such, considering his vast age, in conjunction with his affinity and influence with his people, he might very properly be termed--was rich and imposing, though strictly after the simple fashions of the tribe. His robe was of the finest skins, which had been deprived of their fur, in order to admit of a hieroglyphical representation of various deeds in arms, done in former ages. His bosom was loaded with medals, some in massive silver, and one or two even in gold, the gifts of various Christian potentates during the long period of his life. He also wore armlets, and cinctures above the ankles, of the latter precious metal. His head, on the whole of which the hair had been permitted to grow, the pursuits of war having so long been abandoned, was encircled by a sort of plated diadem, which, in its turn, bore lesser and more glittering ornaments, that sparkled amid the glossy hues of three drooping ostrich feathers, dyed a deep black, in touching contrast to the color of his snow-white locks. His tomahawk was nearly hid in silver, and the handle of his knife shone like a horn of solid gold.\n\nSo soon as the first hum of emotion and pleasure, which the sudden appearance of this venerated individual created, had a little subsided, the name of \"Tamenund\" was whispered from mouth to mouth. Magua had often heard the fame of this wise and just Delaware; a reputation that even proceeded so far as to bestow on him the rare gift of holding secret communion with the Great Spirit, and which has since transmitted his name, with some slight alteration, to the white usurpers of his ancient territory, as the imaginary tutelar saint of a vast empire. The Huron chief, therefore, stepped eagerly out a little from the throng, to a spot whence he might catch a nearer glimpse of the features of the man, whose decision was likely to produce so deep an influence on his own fortunes.\n\nThe eyes of the old man were closed, as though the organs were wearied with having so long witnessed the selfish workings of the human passions. The color of his skin differed from that of most around him, being richer and darker, the latter hue having been produced by certain delicate and mazy lines of complicated and yet beautiful figures, which had been traced over most of his person by the operation of tattooing. Notwithstanding the position of the Huron, he passed the observant and silent Magua without notice, and leaning on his two venerable supporters proceeded to the high place of the multitude, where he seated himself in the centre of his nation, with the dignity of a monarch and the air of a father.\n\nNothing could surpass the reverence and affection with which this unexpected visit from one who belonged rather to another world than to this, was received by his people. After a suitable and decent pause, the principal chiefs arose; and approaching the patriarch, they placed his hands reverently on their heads, seeming to entreat a blessing. The younger men were content with touching his robe, or even drawing nigh his person, in order to breathe in the atmosphere of one so aged, so just, and so valiant. None but the most distinguished among the youthful warriors even presumed so far as to perform the latter ceremony; the great mass of the multitude deeming it a sufficient happiness to look upon a form so deeply venerated, and so well beloved. When these acts of affection and respect were performed, the chiefs drew back again to their several places, and silence reigned in the whole encampment.\n\nAfter a short delay, a few of the young men, to whom instructions had been whispered by one of the aged attendants of Tamenund, arose, left the crowd, and entered the lodge which has already been noted as the object of so much attention throughout that morning. In a few minutes they reappeared, escorting the individuals who had caused all these solemn preparations towards the seat of judgment. The crowd opened in a lane; and when the party had re-entered, it closed in again, forming a large and dense belt of human bodies, arranged in an open circle.\n\n\"The assembly seated, rising o'er the rest, Achilles thus the king of men addressed.\"\n\nPOPE'S _Iliad._\n\nCora stood foremost among the prisoners, entwining her arms in those of Alice, in the tenderness of sisterly love. Notwithstanding the fearful and menacing array of savages on every side of her, no apprehension on her own account could prevent the noble-minded maiden from keeping her eyes fastened on the pale and anxious features of the trembling Alice. Close at their side stood Heyward, with an interest in both, that, at such a moment of intense uncertainty, scarcely knew a preponderance in favor of her whom he most loved. Hawkeye had placed himself a little in the rear, with a deference to the superior rank of his companions, that no similarity in the state of their present fortunes could induce him to forget. Uncas was not there.\n\nWhen perfect silence was again restored, and after the usual long, impressive pause, one of the two aged chiefs who sat at the side of the patriarch arose, and demanded aloud, in very intelligible English,--\n\n\"Which of my prisoners is La Longue Carabine?\"\n\nNeither Duncan nor the scout answered. The former, however, glanced his eyes around the dark and silent assembly, and recoiled a pace, when they fell on the malignant visage of Magua. He saw, at once, that this wily savage had some secret agency in their present arraignment before the nation, and determined to throw every possible impediment in the way of the execution of his sinister plans. He had witnessed one instance of the summary punishments of the Indians, and now dreaded that his companion was to be selected for a second. In this dilemma, with little or no time for reflection, he suddenly determined to cloak his invaluable friend, at any or every hazard to himself. Before he had time, however, to speak, the question was repeated in a louder voice, and with a clearer utterance.\n\n\"Give us arms,\" the young man haughtily replied, \"and place us in yonder woods. Our deeds shall speak for us!\"\n\n\"This is the warrior whose name has filled our ears!\" returned the chief, regarding Heyward with that sort of curious interest which seems inseparable from man, when first beholding one of his fellows to whom merit or accident, virtue or crime, has given notoriety. \"What has brought the white man into the camp of the Delawares?\"\n\n\"My necessities. I come for food, shelter and friends.\"\n\n\"It cannot be. The woods are full of game. The head of a warrior needs no other shelter than a sky without clouds; and the Delawares are the enemies, and not the friends, of the Yengeese. Go! the mouth has spoken, while the heart said nothing.\"\n\nDuncan, a little at a loss in what manner to proceed, remained silent; but the scout, who had listened attentively to all that passed, now advanced steadily to the front.\n\n\"That I did not answer to the call for La Longue Carabine, was not owing either to shame or fear,\" he said; \"for neither one nor the other is the gift of an honest man. But I do not admit the right of the Mingos to bestow a name on one whose friends have been mindful of his gifts, in this particular; especially as their title is a lie, 'Killdeer' being a grooved barrel and no carabyne. I am the man, however, that got the name of Nathaniel from my kin; the compliment of Hawkeye from the Delawares, who live on their own river; and whom the Iroquois have presumed to style the 'Long Rifle,' without any warranty from him who is most concerned in the matter.\"\n\nThe eyes of all present, which had hitherto been gravely scanning the person of Duncan, were now turned, on the instant, towards the upright iron frame of this new pretender to the distinguished appellation. It was in no degree remarkable that there should be found two who were willing to claim so great an honor, for impostors, though rare, were not unknown amongst the natives; but it was altogether material to the just and severe intentions of the Delawares, that there should be no mistake in the matter. Some of their old men consulted together in private, and then, as it would seem, they determined to interrogate their visitor on the subject.\n\n\"My brother has said that a snake crept into my camp,\" said the chief to Magua; \"which is he?\"\n\nThe Huron pointed to the scout.\n\n\"Will a wise Delaware believe the barking of a wolf?\" exclaimed Duncan, still more confirmed in the evil intentions of his ancient enemy: \"a dog never lies, but when was a wolf known to speak the truth?\"\n\nThe eyes of Magua flashed fire; but, suddenly recollecting the necessity of maintaining his presence of mind, he turned away in silent disdain, well assured that the sagacity of the Indians would not fail to extract the real merits of the point in controversy. He was not deceived; for, after another short consultation, the wary Delaware turned to him again, and expressed the determination of the chiefs, though in the most considerate language.\n\n\"My brother has been called a liar,\" he said, \"and his friends are angry. They will show that he has spoken the truth. Give my prisoners guns, and let them prove which is the man.\"\n\nMagua affected to consider the expedient, which he well knew proceeded from distrust of himself, as a compliment, and made a gesture of acquiescence, well content that his veracity should be supported by so skilful a marksman as the scout. The weapons were instantly placed in the hands of the friendly opponents, and they were bid to fire over the heads of the seated multitude at an earthen vessel, which lay, by accident, on a stump some fifty yards from the place where they stood.\n\nHeyward smiled to himself at the idea of a competition with the scout, though he determined to persevere in the deception, until apprised of the real designs of Magua. Raising his rifle with the utmost care, and renewing his aim three several times, he fired. The bullet cut the wood within a few inches of the vessel; and a general exclamation of satisfaction announced that the shot was considered a proof of great skill in the use of the weapon. Even Hawkeye nodded his head, as if he would say, it was better than he had expected. But, instead of manifesting an intention to contend with the successful marksman, he stood leaning on his rifle for more than a minute, like a man who was completely buried in thought. From this reverie he was, however, awakened by one of the young Indians who had furnished the arms, and who now touched his shoulder, saying, in exceedingly broken English,--\n\n\"Can the pale-face beat it?\"\n\n\"Yes, Huron!\" exclaimed the scout, raising the short rifle in his right hand, and shaking it at Magua, with as much apparent ease as if it were a reed; \"yes, Huron, I could strike you now, and no power of earth could prevent the deed! The soaring hawk is not more certain of the dove than I am this moment of you, did I choose to send a bullet to your heart! Why should I not? Why!--because the gifts of my color forbid it, and I might draw down evil on tender and innocent heads. If you know such a being as God, thank Him, therefore, in your inward soul; for you have reason.\"\n\nThe flushed countenance, angry eye, and swelling figure of the scout, produced a sensation of secret awe in all that heard him. The Delawares held their breath in expectation; but Magua himself, even while he distrusted the forbearance of his enemy, remained immovable and calm, where he stood wedged in by the crowd, as one who grew to the spot.\n\n\"Beat it,\" replied the young Delaware at the elbow of the scout.\n\n\"Beat what, fool!--what!\" exclaimed Hawkeye, still flourishing the weapon angrily above his head, though his eye no longer sought the person of Magua.\n\n\"If the white man is the warrior he pretends,\" said the aged chief, \"let him strike nigher to the mark.\"\n\nThe scout laughed aloud--a noise that produced the startling effect of an unnatural sound on Heyward; then dropping the piece heavily into his extended left hand, it was discharged, apparently by the shock, driving the fragments of the vessel into the air, and scattering them on every side. Almost at the same instant, the rattling sound of the rifle was heard, as he suffered it to fall, contemptuously, to the earth.\n\nThe first impression of so strange a scene was engrossing admiration. Then a low, but increasing murmur, ran through the multitude, and finally swelled into sounds that denoted a lively opposition in the sentiments of the spectators. While some openly testified their satisfaction at so unexampled dexterity, by far the larger portion of the tribe were inclined to believe the success of the shot was the result of accident. Heyward was not slow to confirm an opinion that was so favorable to his own pretentions.\n\n\"It was chance!\" he exclaimed; \"none can shoot without an aim!\"\n\n\"Chance!\" echoed the excited woodsman, who was now stubbornly bent on maintaining his identity at every hazard, and on whom the secret hints of Heyward to acquiesce in the deception were entirely lost. \"Does yonder lying Huron, too, think it chance? Give him another gun, and place us face to face, without cover or dodge, and let Providence, and our own eyes, decide the matter atween us! I do not make the offer to you, major; for our blood is of a color, and we serve the same master.\"\n\n\"That the Huron is a liar, is very evident,\" returned Heyward, coolly; \"you have yourself heard him assert you to be La Longue Carabine.\"\n\nIt were impossible to say what violent assertion the stubborn Hawkeye would have next made, in his headlong wish to vindicate his identity, had not the aged Delaware once more interposed.\n\n\"The hawk which comes from the clouds can return when he will,\" he said; \"give them the guns.\"\n\nThis time the scout seized the rifle with avidity; nor had Magua, though he watched the movement of the marksman with jealous eyes, any further cause for apprehension.\n\n\"Now let it be proved, in the face of this tribe of Delawares, which is the better man,\" cried the scout, tapping the butt of his piece with that finger which had pulled so many fatal triggers. \"You see the gourd hanging against yonder tree, major; if you are a marksman fit for the borders, let me see you break its shell!\"\n\nDuncan noted the object, and prepared himself to renew the trial. The gourd was one of the usual little vessels used by the Indians, and it was suspended from a dead branch of a small pine, by a thong of deer-skin, at the full distance of a hundred yards. So strangely compounded is the feeling of self-love, that the young soldier, while he knew the utter worthlessness of the suffrages of his savage umpires, forgot the sudden motives of the contest in a wish to excel. It has been seen, already, that his skill was far from being contemptible, and he now resolved to put forth its nicest qualities. Had his life depended on the issue, the aim of Duncan could not have been more deliberate or guarded. He fired; and three or four young Indians, who sprang forward at the report, announced with a shout, that the ball was in the tree, a very little on one side of the proper object. The warriors uttered a common ejaculation of pleasure, and then turned their eyes inquiringly on the movements of his rival.\n\n\"It may do for the Royal Americans!\" said Hawkeye, laughing once more in his own silent, heartfelt manner; \"but had my gun often turned so much from the true line, many a marten, whose skin is now in a lady's muff, would still be in the woods; ay, and many a bloody Mingo, who has departed to his final account, would be acting his deviltries at this very day, atween the provinces. I hope the squaw who owns the gourd has more of them in her wigwam, for this will never hold water again!\"\n\nThe scout had shook his priming, and cocked his piece, while speaking; and, as he ended, he threw back a foot, and slowly raised the muzzle from the earth: the motion was steady, uniform, and in one direction. When on a perfect level, it remained for a single moment, without tremor or variation, as though both man and rifle were carved in stone. During that stationary instant, it poured forth its contents, in a bright, glancing sheet of flame. Again the young Indians bounded forward; but their hurried search and disappointed looks announced that no traces of the bullet were to be seen.\n\n\"Go!\" said the old chief to the scout, in a tone of strong disgust; \"thou art a wolf in the skin of a dog. I will talk to the 'Long Rifle' of the Yengeese.\"\n\n\"Ah! had I that piece which furnished the name you use, I would obligate myself to cut the thong, and drop the gourd without breaking it!\" returned Hawkeye, perfectly undisturbed by the other's manner, \"Fools, if you would find the bullet of a sharpshooter of these woods, you must look in the object and not around it!\"\n\nThe Indian youths instantly comprehended his meaning--for this time he spoke in the Delaware tongue--and tearing the gourd from the tree, they held it on high with an exulting shout, displaying a hole in its bottom, which had been cut by the bullet, after passing through the usual orifice in the centre of its upper side. At this unexpected exhibition, a loud and vehement expression of pleasure burst from the mouth of every warrior present. It decided the question, and effectually established Hawkeye in the possession of his dangerous reputation. Those curious and admiring eyes which had been turned again on Heyward, were finally directed to the weather-beaten form of the scout, who immediately became the principal object of attention to the simple and unsophisticated beings by whom he was surrounded. When the sudden and noisy commotion had a little subsided, the aged chief resumed his examination.\n\n\"Why did you wish to stop my ears?\" he said, addressing Duncan; \"are the Delawares fools, that they could not know the young panther from the cat?\"\n\n\"They will yet find the Huron a singing-bird,\" said Duncan, endeavoring to adopt the figurative language of the natives.\n\n\"It is good. We will know who can shut the ears of men. Brother,\" added the chief, turning his eyes on Magua, \"the Delawares listen.\"\n\nThus singled, and directly called on to declare his object, the Huron arose; and advancing with great deliberation and dignity into the very centre of the circle, where he stood confronted to the prisoners, he placed himself in an attitude to speak. Before opening his mouth, however, he bent his eyes slowly along the whole living boundary of earnest faces as if to temper his expressions to the capacities of his audience. On Hawkeye he cast a glance of respectful enmity; on Duncan, a look of inextinguishable hatred; the shrinking figure of Alice he scarcely deigned to notice; but when his glance met the firm, commanding, and yet lovely form of Cora, his eye lingered a moment, with an expression that it might have been difficult to define. Then, filled with his own dark intentions, he spoke in the language of the Canadas, a tongue that he well knew was comprehended by most of his auditors.\n\n\"The Spirit that made men colored them differently,\" commenced the subtle Huron. \"Some are blacker than the sluggish bear. These He said would be slaves; and He ordered them to work forever, like the beaver. You may hear them groan, when the south wind blows, louder than the lowing buffaloes, along the shores of the great salt lake, where the big canoes come and go with them in droves. Some He made with faces paler than the ermine of the forests: and these He ordered to be traders; dogs to their women, and wolves to their slaves. He gave this people the nature of the pigeon: wings that never tire; young, more plentiful than the leaves on the trees, and appetites to devour the earth. He gave them tongues like the false call of the wild-cat; hearts like rabbits; the cunning of the hog (but none of the fox), and arms longer than the legs of the moose. With his tongue, he stops the ears of the Indians; his heart teaches him to pay warriors to fight his battles; his cunning tells him how to get together the goods of the earth; and his arms inclose the land from the shores of the salt-water to the islands of the great lake. His gluttony makes him sick. God gave him enough, and yet he wants all. Such are the pale-faces.\n\n\"Some the Great Spirit made with skins brighter and redder than yonder sun,\" continued Magua, pointing impressively upwards to the lurid luminary, which was struggling through the misty atmosphere of the horizon; \"and these did He fashion to His own mind. He gave them this island as He had made it, covered with trees, and filled with game. The wind made their clearings; the sun and rains ripened their fruits; and the snows came to tell them to be thankful. What need had they of roads to journey by! They saw through the hills. When the beavers worked, they lay in the shade, and looked on. The winds cooled them in summer; in winter, skins kept them warm. If they fought among themselves, it was to prove that they were men. They were brave; they were just; they were happy.\"\n\nHere the speaker paused, and again looked around him, to discover if his legend had touched the sympathies of his listeners. He met everywhere with eyes riveted on his own, heads erect, and nostrils expanded, as if each individual present felt himself able and willing, singly, to redress the wrongs of his race.\n\n\"If the Great Spirit gave different tongues to his red children,\" he continued, in a low, still, melancholy voice, \"it was that all animals might understand them. Some He placed among the snows, with their cousin the bear. Some he placed near the setting sun, on the road to the happy hunting-grounds. Some on the lands around the great fresh waters; but to his greatest, and most beloved, He gave the sands of the salt lake. Do my brothers know the name of this favored people?\"\n\n\"It was the Lenape!\" exclaimed twenty eager voices, in a breath.\n\n\"It was the Lenni Lenape,\" returned Magua, affecting to bend his head in reverence to their former greatness. \"It was the tribes of the Lenape! The sun rose from water that was salt, and set in water that was sweet, and never hid himself from their eyes. But why should I, a Huron of the woods, tell a wise people their own traditions? Why remind them of their injuries; their ancient greatness; their deeds; their glory; their happiness,--their losses; their defeats; their misery? Is there not one among them who has seen it all, and who knows it to be true? I have done. My tongue is still, for my heart is of lead. I listen.\"\n\nAs the voice of the speaker suddenly ceased, every face and all eyes turned, by a common movement, towards the venerable Tamenund. From the moment that he took his seat, until the present instant, the lips of the patriarch had not severed, and scarcely a sign of life had escaped him. He sat bent in feebleness, and apparently unconscious of the presence he was in, during the whole of that opening scene, in which the skill of the scout had been so clearly established. At the nicely graduated sound of Magua's voice, however, he betrayed some evidence of consciousness, and once or twice he even raised his head, as if to listen. But when the crafty Huron spoke of his nation by name, the eyelids of the old man raised themselves, and he looked out upon the multitude with that sort of dull unmeaning expression which might be supposed to belong to the countenance of a spectre. Then he made an effort to rise, and being upheld by his supporters, he gained his feet, in a posture commanding by its dignity, while he tottered with weakness.\n\n\"Who calls upon the children of the Lenape!\" he said, in a deep, guttural voice, that was rendered awfully audible by the breathless silence of the multitude: \"who speaks of things gone! Does not the egg become a worm--the worm a fly, and perish? Why tell the Delawares of good that is past? Better thank the Manitou for that which remains.\"\n\n\"It is a Wyandot,\" said Magua, stepping nigher to the rude platform on which the other stood; \"a friend of Tamenund.\"\n\n\"A friend!\" repeated the sage, on whose brow a dark frown settled, imparting a portion of that severity which had rendered his eye so terrible in middle age. \"Are the Mingos rulers of the earth? What brings a Huron here?\"\n\n\"Justice. His prisoners are with his brothers, and he comes for his own.\"\n\nTamenund turned his head towards one of his supporters, and listened to the short explanation the man gave. Then facing the applicant, he regarded him a moment with deep attention; after which he said, in a low and reluctant voice,--\n\n\"Justice is the law of the great Manitou. My children, give the stranger food. Then, Huron, take thine own and depart.\"\n\nOn the delivery of this solemn judgment, the patriarch seated himself, and closed his eyes again, as if better pleased with the images of his own ripened experience than with the visible objects of the world. Against such a decree there was no Delaware sufficiently hardy to murmur, much less oppose himself. The words were barely uttered when four or five of the younger warriors, stepping behind Heyward and the scout, passed thongs so dexterously and rapidly around their arms, as to hold them both in instant bondage. The former was too much engrossed with his precious and nearly insensible burden, to be aware of their intentions before they were executed; and the latter, who considered even the hostile tribes of the Delawares a superior race of beings, submitted without resistance. Perhaps, however, the manner of the scout would not have been so passive, had he fully comprehended the language in which the preceding dialogue had been conducted.\n\nMagua cast a look of triumph around the whole assembly before he proceeded to the execution of his purpose. Perceiving that the men were unable to offer any resistance, he turned his looks on her he valued most. Cora met his gaze with an eye so calm and firm, that his resolution wavered. Then recollecting his former artifice, he raised Alice from the arms of the warrior against whom she leaned, and beckoning Heyward to follow, he motioned for the encircling crowd to open. But Cora, instead of obeying the impulse he had expected, rushed to the feet of the patriarch, and raising her voice, exclaimed aloud,--\n\n\"Just and venerable Delaware, on thy wisdom and power we lean for mercy! Be deaf to yonder artful and remorseless monster, who poisons thy ears with falsehoods to feed his thirst for blood. Thou that hast lived long, and that hast seen the evil of the world, should know how to temper its calamities to the miserable.\"\n\n# THE SUPPLICANT 320\n\nThe eyes of the old man opened heavily, and he once more looked upwards at the multitude. As the piercing tones of the supplicant swelled on his ears, they moved slowly in the direction of her person, and finally settled there in a steady gaze. Cora had cast herself to her knees; and, with hands clenched in each other and pressed upon her bosom, she remained like a beauteous and breathing model of her sex, looking up in his faded, but majestic countenance, with a species of holy reverence. Gradually the expression of Tamenund's features changed, and losing their vacancy in admiration, they lighted with a portion of that intelligence which a century before had been wont to communicate his youthful fire to the extensive bands of the Delawares. Rising without assistance, and seemingly without an effort, he demanded, in a voice that startled its auditors by its firmness,--\n\n\"What art thou?\"\n\n\"A woman. One of a hated race, if thou wilt--a Yengee. But one who has never harmed thee, and who cannot harm thy people, if she would; who asks for succor.\"\n\n\"Tell me, my children,\" continued the patriarch, hoarsely, motioning to those around him, though his eyes still dwelt upon the kneeling form of Cora, \"where have the Delawares camped?\"\n\n\"In the mountains of the Iroquois, beyond the clear springs of the Horican.\"\n\n\"Many parching summers are come and gone,\" continued the sage, \"since I drank of the water of my own rivers. The children of Minquon[26] are the justest white men; but they were thirsty, and they took it to themselves. Do they follow us so far?\"\n\n\"We follow none; we covet nothing,\" answered Cora. \"Captives against our wills, have we been brought among you; and we ask but permission to depart to our own in peace. Art thou not Tamenund--the father, the judge, I had almost said, the prophet--of this people?\"\n\n\"I am Tamenund of many days.\"\n\n\"'Tis now some seven years that one of thy people was at the mercy of a white chief on the borders of this province. He claimed to be of the blood of the good and just Tamenund. 'Go,' said the white man, 'for thy parent's sake thou art free.' Dost thou remember the name of that English warrior?\"\n\n\"I remember, that when a laughing boy,\" returned the patriarch, with the peculiar recollection of vast age, \"I stood upon the sands of the sea-shore, and saw a big canoe, with wings whiter than the swan's, and wider than many eagles, come from the rising sun.\"\n\n\"Nay, nay; I speak not of a time so very distant, but of favor shown to thy kindred by one of mine, within the memory of thy youngest warrior.\"\n\n\"Was it when the Yengeese and the Dutchmanne fought for the hunting-grounds of the Delawares? Then Tamenund was a chief, and first laid aside the bow for the lightning of the pale faces--\"\n\n# THE SUPPLICANT\n\n_Cora had cast herself to her knees; and, with hands clenched in each other and pressed upon her bosom, she remained like a beauteous and breathing model of her sex_]\n\n\"Nor yet then,\" interrupted Cora, \"by many ages; I speak of a thing of yesterday. Surely, surely, you forget it not.\"\n\"It was but yesterday,\" rejoined the aged man, with touching pathos, \"that the children of the Lenape were masters of the world. The fishes of the salt lake, the birds, the beasts, and the Mengwe of the woods, owned them for sagamores.\"\n\nCora bowed her head in disappointment, and, for a bitter moment, struggled with her chagrin. Then elevating her rich features and beaming eyes, she continued, in tones scarcely less penetrating than the unearthly voice of the patriarch himself,--\n\n\"Tell me, is Tamenund a father?\"\n\nThe old man looked down upon her from his elevated stand, with a benignant smile on his wasted countenance, and then casting his eyes slowly over the whole assemblage, he answered,--\n\n\"Of a nation.\"\n\n\"For myself I ask nothing. Like thee and thine, venerable chief,\" she continued, pressing her hands convulsively on her heart, and suffering her head to droop until her burning cheeks were nearly concealed in the maze of dark glossy tresses that fell in disorder upon her shoulders, \"the curse of my ancestors has fallen heavily on their child. But yonder is one who has never known the weight of Heaven's displeasure until now. She is the daughter of an old and failing man, whose days are near their close. She has many, very many, to love her, and delight in her; and she is too good, much too precious, to become the victim of that villain.\"\n\n\"I know that the pale-faces are a proud and hungry race. I know that they claim not only to have the earth, but that the meanest of their color is better than the sachems of the redman. The dogs and crows of their tribes,\" continued the earnest old chieftain, without heeding the wounded spirit of his listener, whose head was nearly crushed to the earth in shame, as he proceeded, \"would bark and caw before they would take a woman to their wigwams whose blood was not of the color of snow. But let them not boast before the face of the Manitou too loud. They entered the land at the rising, and may yet go off at the setting sun. I have often seen the locusts strip the leaves from the trees, but the season of blossoms has always come again.\"\n\n\"It is so,\" said Cora, drawing a long breath, as if reviving from a trance, raising her face, and shaking back her shining veil, with a kindling eye, that contradicted the death-like paleness of her countenance; \"but why--it is not permitted us to inquire. There is yet one of thine own people who has not been brought before thee; before thou lettest the Huron depart in triumph, hear him speak.\"\n\nObserving Tamenund to look about him doubtingly, one of his companions said,--\n\n\"It is a snake--a redskin in the pay of the Yengeese. We keep him for the torture.\"\n\n\"Let him come,\" returned the sage.\n\nThen Tamenund once more sank into his seat, and a silence so deep prevailed, while the young men prepared to obey his simple mandate, that the leaves, which fluttered in the draught of the light morning air, were distinctly heard rustling in the surrounding forest.\n\n# CHAPTER XXX\n\n\"If you deny me, fie upon your law! There is no force in the decrees of Venice: I stand for judgment; answer, shall I have it?\"\n\n_Merchant of Venice._\n\nThe silence continued unbroken by human sounds for many anxious minutes. Then the waving multitude opened and shut again, and Uncas stood in the living circle. All those eyes, which had been curiously studying the lineaments of the sage, as the source of their own intelligence, turned on the instant, and were now bent in secret admiration on the erect, agile, and faultless person of the captive. But neither the presence in which he found himself, nor the exclusive attention that he attracted, in any manner disturbed the self-possession of the young Mohican. He cast a deliberate and observing look on every side of him, meeting the settled expression of hostility that lowered in the visages of the chiefs, with the same calmness as the curious gaze of the attentive children. But when, last in his haughty scrutiny, the person of Tamenund came under his glance, his eye became fixed, as though all other objects were already forgotten. Then advancing with a slow and noiseless step up the area, he placed himself immediately before the footstool of the sage. Here he stood unnoted, though keenly observant himself, until one of the chiefs apprised the latter of his presence.\n\n\"With what tongue does the prisoner speak to the Manitou?\" demanded the patriarch, without unclosing his eyes.\n\n\"Like his fathers,\" Uncas replied; \"with the tongue of a Delaware.\"\n\nAt this sudden and unexpected annunciation, a low, fierce yell ran through the multitude, that might not inaptly be compared to the growl of the lion, as his choler is first awakened--a fearful omen of the weight of his future anger. The effect was equally strong on the sage, though differently exhibited. He passed a hand before his eyes, as if to exclude the least evidence of so shameful a spectacle, while he repeated, in his low, guttural tones, the words he had just heard.\n\n\"A Delaware! I have lived to see the tribes of the Lenape driven from their council-fires, and scattered, like broken herds of deer, among the hills of the Iroquois! I have seen the hatchets of a strange people sweep woods from the valleys, that the winds of heaven had spared! The beasts that run on the mountains, and the birds that fly above the trees, have I seen living in the wigwams of men; but never before have I found a Delaware so base as to creep, like a poisonous serpent, into the camps of his nation.\"\n\n\"The singing-birds have opened their bills,\" returned Uncas, in the softest notes of his own musical voice; \"and Tamenund has heard their song.\"\n\nThe sage started, and bent his head aside, as if to catch the fleeting sounds of some passing melody.\n\n\"Does Tamenund dream!\" he exclaimed. \"What voice is at his ear! Have the winters gone backward! Will summer come again to the children of the Lenape!\"\n\nA solemn and respectful silence succeeded this incoherent burst from the lips of the Delaware prophet. His people steadily construed his unintelligible language into one of those mysterious conferences he was believed to hold so frequently with a superior intelligence, and they awaited the issue of the revelation in awe. After a patient pause, however, one of the aged men, perceiving that the sage had lost the recollection of the subject before them, ventured to remind him again of the presence of the prisoner.\n\n\"The false Delaware trembles lest he should hear the words of Tamenund,\" he said. \"'Tis a hound that howls, when the Yengeese show him a trail.\"\n\n\"And ye,\" returned Uncas, looking sternly around him, \"are dogs that whine, when the Frenchman casts ye the offals of his deer!\"\n\nTwenty knives gleamed in the air, and as many warriors sprang to their feet, at this biting, and perhaps merited retort; but a motion from one of the chiefs suppressed the outbreaking of their tempers, and restored the appearance of quiet. The task might probably have been more difficult, had not a movement made by Tamenund indicated that he was again about to speak.\n\n\"Delaware!\" resumed the sage, \"little art thou worthy of thy name. My people have not seen a bright sun in many winters; and the warrior who deserts his tribe when hid in clouds is doubly a traitor. The law of the Manitou is just. It is so; while the rivers run and the mountains stand, while the blossoms come and go on the trees, it must be so. He is thine, my children; deal justly by him.\"\n\nNot a limb was moved, nor was a breath drawn louder and longer than common, until the closing syllable of this final decree had passed the lips of Tamenund. Then a cry of vengeance burst at once, as it might be, from the united lips of the nation; a frightful augury of their ruthless intentions. In the midst of these prolonged and savage yells, a chief proclaimed, in a high voice, that the captive was condemned to endure the dreadful trial of torture by fire. The circle broke its order, and screams of delight mingled with the bustle and tumult of preparation. Heyward struggled madly with his captors; the anxious eyes of Hawkeye began to look around him, with an expression of peculiar earnestness; and Cora again threw herself at the feet of the patriarch, once more a suppliant for mercy.\n\nThroughout the whole of these trying moments, Uncas had alone preserved his serenity. He looked on the preparations with a steady eye, and when the tormentors came to seize him, he met them with a firm and upright attitude. One among them, if possible, more fierce and savage than his fellows, seized the hunting-shirt of the young warrior, and at a single effort tore it from his body. Then, with a yell of frantic pleasure, he leaped towards his unresisting victim, and prepared to lead him to the stake. But, at that moment, when he appeared most a stranger to the feelings of humanity, the purpose of the savage was arrested as suddenly as if a supernatural agency had interposed in the behalf of Uncas. The eyeballs of the Delaware seemed to start from their sockets; his mouth opened, and his whole form became frozen in an attitude of amazement. Raising his hand with a slow and regulated motion, he pointed with a finger to the bosom of the captive. His companions crowded about him in wonder, and every eye was, like his own, fastened intently on the figure of a small tortoise, beautifully tattooed on the breast of the prisoner, in a bright blue tint.\n\nFor a single instant Uncas enjoyed his triumph, smiling calmly on the scene. Then motioning the crowd away with a high and haughty sweep of his arm, he advanced in front of the nation with the air of a king, and spoke in a voice louder than the murmur of admiration that ran through the multitude.\n\n\"Men of the Lenni Lenape!\" he said, \"my race upholds the earth! Your feeble tribe stands on my shell![27] What fire that a Delaware can light would burn the child of my fathers,\" he added, pointing proudly to the simple blazonry on his skin; \"the blood that came from such a stock would smother your flames! My race is the grandfather of nations!\"\n\n\"Who art thou?\" demanded Tamenund, rising at the startling tones he heard, more than at any meaning conveyed by the language of the prisoner.\n\n\"Uncas, the son of Chingachgook,\" answered the captive modestly, turning from the nation, and bending his head in reverence to the other's character and years; \"a son of the great Unamis.\"\n\n\"The hour of Tamenund is nigh!\" exclaimed the sage; \"the day is come, at last, to the night! I thank the Manitou, that one is here to fill my place at the council-fire. Uncas, the child of Uncas, is found! Let the eyes of a dying eagle gaze on the rising sun.\"\n\nThe youth stepped lightly, but proudly, on the platform, where he became visible to the whole agitated and wondering multitude. Tamenund held him long at the length of his arm, and read every turn in the fine lineaments of his countenance, with the untiring gaze of one who recalled days of happiness.\n\n\"Is Tamenund a boy?\" at length the bewildered prophet exclaimed. \"Have I dreamt of so many snows--that my people were scattered like floating sands--of Yengeese, more plenty than the leaves on the trees! The arrow of Tamenund would not frighten the fawn; his arm is withered like the branch of a dead oak; the snail would be swifter in the race; yet is Uncas before him as they went to battle against the pale-faces! Uncas, the panther of his tribe, the eldest son of the Lenape, the wisest Sagamore of the Mohicans! Tell me, ye Delawares, has Tamenund been a sleeper for a hundred winters?\"\n\nThe calm and deep silence which succeeded these words, sufficiently announced the awful reverence with which his people received the communication of the patriarch. None dared to answer, though all listened in breathless expectation of what might follow. Uncas, however, looking in his face with the fondness and veneration of a favored child, presumed on his own high and acknowledged rank, to reply.\n\n\"Four warriors of his race have lived, and died,\" he said, \"since the friend of Tamenund led his people in battle. The blood of the turtle has been in many chiefs, but all have gone back into the earth from whence they came except Chingachgook and his son.\"\n\n\"It is true--it is true,\" returned the sage; a flash of recollection destroying all his pleasing fancies, and restoring him at once to a consciousness of the true history of his nation. \"Our wise men have often said that two warriors of the unchanged race were in the hills of the Yengeese; why have their seats at the council-fires of the Delawares been so long empty?\"\n\nAt these words the young man raised his head, which he had still kept bowed a little, in reverence; and lifting his voice so as to be heard by the multitude, as if to explain at once and forever the policy of his family, he said aloud,--\n\n\"Once we slept where we could hear the salt lake speak in its anger. Then we were rulers and sagamores over the land. But when a pale-face was seen on every brook, we followed the deer back to the river of our nation. The Delawares were gone. Few warriors of them all stayed to drink of the stream they loved. Then said my fathers, 'Here will we hunt. The waters of the river go into the salt lake. If we go towards the setting sun, we shall find streams that run into the great lakes of sweet water; there would a Mohican die, like fishes of the sea, in the clear springs. When the Manitou is ready, and shall say \"Come,\" we will follow the river to the sea, and take our own again.' Such, Delawares, is the belief of the children of the Turtle. Our eyes are on the rising, and not towards the setting sun. We know whence he comes, but we know not whither he goes. It is enough.\"\n\nThe men of the Lenape listened to his words with all the respect that superstition could lend, finding a secret charm even in the figurative language with which the young Sagamore imparted his ideas. Uncas himself watched the effect of his brief explanation with intelligent eyes, and gradually dropped the air of authority he had assumed, as he perceived that his auditors were content. Then permitting his looks to wander over the silent throng that crowded around the elevated seat of Tamenund, he first perceived Hawkeye in his bonds. Stepping eagerly from his stand, he made way for himself to the side of his friend; and cutting his thongs with a quick and angry stroke of his own knife, he motioned to the crowd to divide. The Indians silently obeyed, and once more they stood ranged in their circle, as before his appearance among them. Uncas took the scout by the hand, and led him to the feet of the patriarch.\n\n\"Father,\" he said, \"look at this pale-face; a just man, and the friend of the Delawares.\"\n\n\"Is he a son of Minquon?\"\n\n\"Not so; a warrior known to the Yengeese, and feared by the Maquas.\"\n\n\"What name has he gained by his deeds?\"\n\n\"We call him Hawkeye,\" Uncas replied, using the Delaware phrase; \"for his sight never fails. The Mingos know him better by the death he gives their warriors; with them he is 'The Long Rifle.'\"\n\n\"La Longue Carabine!\" exclaimed Tamenund, opening his eyes, and regarding the scout sternly. \"My son has not done well to call him friend.\"\n\n\"I call him so who proves himself such,\" returned the young chief, with great calmness, but with a steady mien. \"If Uncas is welcome among the Delawares, then is Hawkeye with his friends.\"\n\n\"The pale-face has slain my young men; his name is great for the blows he has struck the Lenape.\"\n\n\"If a Mingo has whispered that much in the ear of the Delaware, he has only shown that he is a singing-bird,\" said the scout, who now believed that it was time to vindicate himself from such offensive charges, and who spoke in the tongue of the man he addressed, modifying his Indian figures, however, with his own peculiar notions. \"That I have slain the Maquas I am not the man to deny, even at their own council-fires; but that, knowingly, my hand has ever harmed a Delaware, is opposed to the reason of my gifts, which is friendly to them, and all that belongs to their nation.\"\n\nA low exclamation of applause passed among the warriors, who exchanged looks with each other like men that first began to perceive their error.\n\n\"Where is the Huron?\" demanded Tamenund. \"Has he stopped my ears?\"\n\nMagua, whose feelings during that scene in which Uncas had triumphed may be much better imagined than described, answered to the call by stepping boldly in front of the patriarch.\n\n\"The just Tamenund,\" he said, \"will not keep what a Huron has lent.\"\n\n\"Tell me, son of my brother,\" returned the sage, avoiding the dark countenance of Le Subtil, and turning gladly to the more ingenuous features of Uncas, \"has the stranger a conqueror's right over you?\"\n\n\"He has none. The panther may get into snares set by the women; but he is strong, and knows how to leap through them.\"\n\n\"La Longue Carabine?\"\n\n\"Laughs at the Mingoes. Go, Huron, ask your squaws the color of a bear.\"\n\n\"The stranger and the white maiden that came into my camp together?\"\n\n\"Should journey on an open path.\"\n\n\"And the woman that Huron left with my warriors?\"\n\nUncas made no reply.\n\n\"And the woman that the Mingo has brought into my camp,\" repeated Tamenund, gravely.\n\n\"She is mine,\" cried Magua, shaking his hand in triumph at Uncas. \"Mohican, you know that she is mine.\"\n\n\"My son is silent,\" said Tamenund, endeavoring to read the expression of the face that the youth turned from him in sorrow.\n\n\"It is so,\" was the low answer.\n\nA short and impressive pause succeeded, during which it was very apparent with what reluctance the multitude admitted the justice of the Mingo's claim. At length the sage, in whom alone the decision depended, said, in a firm voice,--\n\n\"Huron, depart.\"\n\n\"As he came, just Tamenund,\" demanded the wily Magua; \"or with hands filled with the faith of the Delawares? The wigwam of Le Renard Subtil is empty. Make him strong with his own.\"\n\nThe aged man mused with himself for a time; and then bending his head towards one of his venerable companions, he asked,--\n\n\"Are my ears open?\"\n\n\"It is true.\"\n\n\"Is this Mingo a chief?\"\n\n\"The first in his nation.\"\n\n\"Girl, what wouldst thou? A great warrior takes thee to wife. Go! thy race will not end.\"\n\n\"Better, a thousand times, it should,\" exclaimed the horror-struck Cora, \"than meet with such a degradation!\"\n\n\"Huron, her mind is in the tents of her fathers. An unwilling maiden makes an unhappy wigwam.\"\n\n\"She speaks with the tongue of her people,\" returned Magua, regarding his victim with a look of bitter irony. \"She is of a race of traders, and will bargain for a bright look. Let Tamenund speak the words.\"\n\n\"Take you the wampum, and our love.\"\n\n\"Nothing hence but what Magua brought hither.\"\n\n\"Then depart with thine own. The great Manitou forbids that a Delaware should be unjust.\"\n\nMagua advanced, and seized his captive strongly by the arm; the Delawares fell back, in silence; and Cora, as if conscious that remonstrance would be useless, prepared to submit to her fate without resistance.\n\n\"Hold, hold!\" cried Duncan, springing forward; \"Huron, have mercy! her ransom shall make thee richer than any of thy people were ever yet known to be.\"\n\n\"Magua is a redskin; he wants not the beads of the pale-faces.\"\n\n\"Gold, silver, powder, lead--all that a warrior needs shall be in thy wigwam; all that becomes the greatest chief.\"\n\n\"Le Subtil is very strong,\" cried Magua, violently shaking the hand which grasped the unresisting arm of Cora; \"he has his revenge!\"\n\n\"Mighty ruler of providence!\" exclaimed Heyward, clasping his hands together in agony, \"can this be suffered! To you, just Tamenund, I appeal for mercy.\"\n\n\"The words of the Delaware are said,\" returned the sage, closing his eyes, and dropping back into his seat, alike wearied with his mental and his bodily exertion. \"Men speak not twice.\"\n\n\"That a chief should not misspend his time in unsaying what had once been spoken, is wise and reasonable,\" said Hawkeye, motioning to Duncan to be silent; \"but it is also prudent in every warrior to consider well before he strikes his tomahawk into the head of his prisoner. Huron, I love you not; nor can I say that any Mingo has ever received much favor at my hands. It is fair to conclude that, if this war does not soon end, many more of your warriors will meet me in the woods. Put it to your judgment, then, whether you would prefer taking such a prisoner as that into your encampment, or one like myself, who am a man that it would greatly rejoice your nation to see with naked hands.\"\n\n\"Will 'The Long Rifle' give his life for the woman?\" demanded Magua, hesitatingly; for he had already made a motion towards quitting the place with his victim.\n\n\"No, no; I have not said so much as that,\" returned Hawkeye, drawing back with suitable discretion, when he noted the eagerness with which Magua listened to his proposal. \"It would be an unequal exchange, to give a warrior, in the prime of his age and usefulness, for the best woman on the frontiers. I might consent to go into winter-quarters, now--at least six weeks afore the leaves will turn--on condition you will release the maiden.\"\n\nMagua shook his head, and made an impatient sign for the crowd to open.\n\n\"Well, then,\" added the scout, with the musing air of a man who had not half made up his mind, \"I will throw 'Killdeer' into the bargain. Take the word of an experienced hunter, the piece has not its equal atween the provinces.\"\n\nMagua still disdained to reply, continuing his efforts to disperse the crowd.\n\n\"Perhaps,\" added the scout, losing his dissembled coolness, exactly in proportion as the other manifested an indifference to the exchange, \"if I should condition to teach your young men the real virtue of the we'pon, it would smooth the little differences in our judgments.\"\n\nLe Renard fiercely ordered the Delawares, who still lingered in an impenetrable belt around him, in hopes he would listen to the amicable proposal, to open his path, threatening, by the glance of his eye, another appeal to the infallible justice of their \"prophet.\"\n\n\"What is ordered must sooner or later arrive,\" continued Hawkeye, turning with a sad and humbled look to Uncas. \"The varlet knows his advantage, and will keep it! God bless you, boy; you have found friends among your natural kin and I hope they will prove as true as some you have met who had no Indian cross. As for me, sooner or later, I must die; it is therefore fortunate there are but few to make my death-howl. After all, it is likely the imps would have managed to master my scalp, so a day or two will make no great difference in the everlasting reckoning of time. God bless you,\" added the rugged woodsman, bending his head aside, and then instantly changing its direction again, with a wistful look towards the youth; \"I loved both you and your father, Uncas, though our skins are not altogether of a color, and our gifts are somewhat different. Tell the Sagamore I never lost sight of him in my greatest trouble; and, as for you, think of me sometimes when on a lucky trail; and depend on it, boy, whether there be one heaven or two, there is a path in the other world by which honest men may come together again. You'll find the rifle in the place we hid it; take it, and keep it for my sake; and harkee, lad, as your natural gifts don't deny you the use of vengeance, use it a little freely on the Mingos; it may unburden grief at my loss, and ease your mind. Huron, I accept your offer; release the woman. I am your prisoner!\"\n\nA suppressed, but still distinct murmur of approbation, ran through the crowd at this generous proposition; even the fiercest among the Delaware warriors manifesting pleasure at the manliness of the intended sacrifice. Magua paused, and for an anxious moment, it might be said, he doubted; then casting his eyes on Cora, with an expression in which ferocity and admiration were strangely mingled, his purpose became fixed forever.\n\nHe intimated his contempt of the offer with a backward motion of his head, and said, in a steady and settled voice,--\n\n\"Le Renard Subtil is a great chief; he has but one mind. Come,\" he added, laying his hand too familiarly on the shoulder of his captive to urge her onward; \"a Huron is no tattler; we will go.\"\n\nThe maiden drew back in lofty womanly reserve, and her dark eye kindled, while the rich blood shot, like the passing brightness of the sun, into her very temples, at the indignity.\n\n\"I am your prisoner, and at a fitting time shall be ready to follow, even to my death. But violence is unnecessary,\" she coldly said; and immediately turning to Hawkeye, added, \"Generous hunter! from my soul I thank you. Your offer is in vain, neither could it be accepted; but still you may serve me, even more than in your own noble intention. Look at that drooping, humbled child! Abandon her not until you leave her in the habitation of civilized men. I will not say,\" wringing the hard hand of the scout, \"that her father will reward you--for such as you are above the rewards of men--but he will thank you, and bless you. And, believe me, the blessing of a just and aged man has virtue in the sight of Heaven. Would to God, I could hear one from his lips at this awful moment!\" Her voice became choked, and, for an instant, she was silent; then advancing a step nigher to Duncan, who was supporting her unconscious sister, she continued, in more subdued tones, but in which feeling and the habits of her sex maintained a fearful struggle,--\"I need not tell you to cherish the treasure you will possess. You love her, Heyward; that would conceal a thousand faults, though she had them. She is kind, gentle, sweet, good, as mortal may be. There is not a blemish in mind or person at which the proudest of you all would sicken. She is fair--O! how surpassingly fair!\" laying her own beautiful, but less brilliant hand, in melancholy affection on the alabaster forehead of Alice, and parting the golden hair which clustered about her brows; \"and yet her soul is pure and spotless as her skin! I could say much--more, perhaps, than cooler reason would approve; but I will spare you and myself\"--Her voice became inaudible, and her face was bent over the form of her sister. After a long and burning kiss, she arose, and with features of the hue of death, but without even a tear in her feverish eye, she turned away, and added, to the savage, with all her former elevation of manner,--\"Now, sir, if it be your pleasure, I will follow.\"\n\n\"Ay, go,\" cried Duncan, placing Alice in the arms of an Indian girl; \"go, Magua, go. These Delawares have their laws, which forbid them to detain you; but I--I have no such obligation. Go, malignant monster--why do you delay?\"\n\nIt would be difficult to describe the expression with which Magua listened to this threat to follow. There was at first a fierce and manifest display of joy, and then it was instantly subdued in a look of cunning coldness.\n\n\"The woods are open,\" he was content with answering. \"'The Open Hand' can come.\"\n\n\"Hold,\" cried Hawkeye, seizing Duncan by the arm, and detaining him by violence; \"you know not the craft of the imp. He would lead you to an ambushment, and your death--\"\n\n\"Huron,\" interrupted Uncas, who, submissive to the stern customs of his people, had been an attentive and grave listener to all that passed; \"Huron, the justice of the Delawares comes from the Manitou. Look at the sun. He is now in the upper branches of the hemlock. Your path is short and open. When he is seen above the trees, there will be men on your trail.\"\n\n\"I hear a crow!\" exclaimed Magua, with a taunting laugh. \"Go!\" he added, shaking his hand at the crowd, which had slowly opened to admit his passage,--\"Where are the petticoats of the Delawares! Let them send their arrows and their guns to the Wyandots; they shall have venison to eat, and corn to hoe. Dogs, rabbits, thieves--I spit on you!\"\n\nHis parting gibes were listened to in a dead, boding silence, and, with these biting words in his mouth, the triumphant Magua passed unmolested into the forest, followed by his passive captive, and protected by the inviolable laws of Indian hospitality.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI\n\n_\"Flue._--Kill the poys and the luggage! 'Tis expressly against the law of arms; 'tis as arrant a piece of knavery, mark you now, as can be offered in the world.\"\n\n_King Henry V._\n\nSo long as their enemy and his victim continued in sight, the multitude remained motionless as beings charmed to the place by some power that was friendly to the Huron; but the instant he disappeared, it became tossed and agitated by fierce and powerful passion. Uncas maintained his elevated stand, keeping his eyes on the form of Cora, until the colors of her dress were blended with the foliage of the forest; when he descended, and moving silently through the throng, he disappeared in that lodge from which he had so recently issued. A few of the graver and more attentive warriors, who caught the gleams of anger that shot from the eyes of the young chief in passing, followed him to the place he had selected for his meditations. After which, Tamenund and Alice were removed, and the women and children were ordered to disperse. During the momentous hour that succeeded, the encampment resembled a hive of troubled bees, who only awaited the appearance and example of their leader to take some distant and momentous flight.\n\nA young warrior at length issued from the lodge of Uncas; and moving deliberately, with a sort of grave march, towards a dwarf pine that grew in the crevices of the rocky terrace, he tore the bark from its body, and then returned whence he came without speaking. He was soon followed by another, who stripped the sapling of its branches, leaving it a naked and blazed[28] trunk. A third colored the posts with stripes of a dark red paint; all which indications of a hostile design in the leaders of the nation were received by the men without in a gloomy and ominous silence. Finally, the Mohican himself reappeared, divested of all his attire except his girdle and leggings, and with one-half of his fine features hid under a cloud of threatening black.\n\nUncas moved with a slow and dignified tread towards the post, which he immediately commenced encircling with a measured step, not unlike an ancient dance, raising his voice, at the same time, in the wild and irregular chant of his war-song. The notes were in the extremes of human sounds; being sometimes melancholy and exquisitely plaintive, even rivalling the melody of birds--and then, by sudden and startling transitions, causing the auditors to tremble by their depth and energy. The words were few and often repeated, proceeding gradually from a sort of invocation, or hymn to the Deity, to an intimation of the warrior's object, and terminating as they commenced with an acknowledgment of his own dependence on the Great Spirit. If it were possible to translate the comprehensive and melodious language in which he spoke, the ode might read something like the following:\n\n\"Manitou! Manitou! Manitou! Thou art great, thou art good, thou art wise: Manitou! Manitou! Thou art just.\n\n\"In the heavens, in the clouds, O, I see Many spots--many dark, many red: In the heavens, O, I see Many clouds.\n\n\"In the woods, in the air, O, I hear The whoop, the long yell, and the cry: In the woods, O, I hear The loud whoop!\n\n\"Manitou! Manitou! Manitou! Thou art weak--thou art strong; I am slow: Manitou! Manitou! Give me aid.\"\n\nAt the end of what might be called each verse he made a pause, by raising a note louder and longer than common, that was peculiarly suited to the sentiment just expressed. The first close was solemn, and intended to convey the idea of veneration; the second descriptive, bordering on the alarming; and the third was the well known and terrific war-whoop, which burst from the lips of the young warrior, like a combination of all the frightful sounds of battle. The last was like the first, humble and imploring. Three times did he repeat this song, and as often did he encircle the post in his dance.\n\nAt the close of the first turn, a grave and highly esteemed chief of the Lenape followed his example, singing words of his own, however, to music of a similar character. Warrior after warrior enlisted in the dance, until all of any renown and authority were numbered in its mazes. The spectacle now became wildly terrific; the fierce-looking and menacing visages of the chiefs receiving additional power from the appalling strains in which they mingled their guttural tones. Just then Uncas struck his tomahawk deep into the post, and raised his voice in a shout, which might be termed his own battle-cry. The act announced that he had assumed the chief authority in the intended expedition.\n\nIt was a signal that awakened all the slumbering passions of a nation. A hundred youths, who had hitherto been restrained by the diffidence of their years, rushed in a frantic body on the fancied emblem of their enemy, and severed it asunder, splinter by splinter, until nothing remained of the trunk but its roots in the earth. During this moment of tumult, the most ruthless deeds of war were performed on the fragments of the tree, with as much apparent ferocity as if they were the living victims of their cruelty. Some were scalped; some received the keen and trembling axe; and others suffered by thrusts from the fatal knife. In short, the manifestations of zeal and fierce delight were so great and unequivocal, that the expedition was declared to be a war of the nation.\n\nThe instant Uncas had struck the blow, he moved out of the circle, and cast his eyes up to the sun, which was just gaining the point, when the truce with Magua was to end. The fact was soon announced by a significant gesture, accompanied by a corresponding cry; and the whole of the excited multitude abandoned their mimic warfare, with shrill yells of pleasure, to prepare for the more hazardous experiment of the reality.\n\nThe whole face of the encampment was instantly changed. The warriors, who were already armed and painted, became as still as if they were incapable of any uncommon burst of emotion. On the other hand, the women broke out of the lodges, with the songs of joy and those of lamentation, so strangely mingled, that it might have been difficult to have said which passion preponderated. None, however, were idle. Some bore their choicest articles, others their young, and some their aged and infirm, into the forest, which spread itself like a verdant carpet of bright green against the side of the mountain. Thither Tamenund also retired, with calm composure, after a short and touching interview with Uncas; from whom the sage separated with the reluctance that a parent would quit a long lost and just recovered child. In the meantime, Duncan saw Alice to a place of safety, and then sought the scout, with a countenance that denoted how eagerly he also panted for the approaching contest.\n\nBut Hawkeye was too much accustomed to the war-song and the enlistments of the natives, to betray any interest in the passing scene. He merely cast an occasional look at the number and quality of the warriors, who, from time to time, signified their readiness to accompany Uncas to the field. In this particular he was soon satisfied; for, as has been already seen, the power of the young chief quickly embraced every fighting man in the nation. After this material point was so satisfactorily decided, he despatched an Indian boy in quest of \"Killdeer\" and the rifle of Uncas, to the place where they had deposited the weapons on approaching the camp of the Delawares; a measure of double policy, inasmuch as it protected the arms from their own fate, if detained as prisoners, and gave them the advantage of appearing among the strangers rather as sufferers than as men provided with the means of defence and subsistence. In selecting another to perform the office of reclaiming his highly prized rifle, the scout had lost sight of none of his habitual caution. He knew that Magua had not come unattended, and he also knew that Huron spies watched the movements of their new enemies, along the whole boundary of the woods. It would, therefore, have been fatal to himself to have attempted the experiment; a warrior would have fared no better; but the danger of a boy would not be likely to commence until after his object was discovered. When Heyward joined him, the scout was coolly awaiting the result of this experiment.\n\nThe boy, who had been well instructed, and was sufficiently crafty, proceeded, with a bosom that was swelling with the pride of such a confidence, and all the hopes of young ambition, carelessly across the clearing to the wood, which he entered at a point at some little distance from the place where the guns were secreted. The instant, however, he was concealed by the foliage of the bushes, his dusky form was to be seen gliding, like that of a serpent, towards the desired treasure. He was successful; and in another moment he appeared flying across the narrow opening that skirted the base of the terrace on which the village stood, with the velocity of an arrow, and bearing a prize in each hand. He had actually gained the crags, and was leaping up their sides with incredible activity, when a shot from the woods showed how accurate had been the judgment of the scout. The boy answered it with a feeble but contemptuous shout; and immediately a second bullet was sent after him from another part of the cover. At the next instant he appeared on the level above, elevating his guns in triumph, while he moved with the air of a conqueror towards the renowned hunter who had honored him by so glorious a commission.\n\nNotwithstanding the lively interest Hawkeye had taken in the fate of his messenger, he received \"Killdeer\" with a satisfaction that, momentarily, drove all other recollections from his mind. After examining the piece with an intelligent eye, and opening and shutting the pan some ten or fifteen times, and trying sundry other equally important experiments on the lock, he turned to the boy, and demanded with great manifestations of kindness, if he was hurt. The urchin looked proudly up in his face, but made no reply.\n\n\"Ah! I see, lad, the knaves have barked your arm!\" added the scout, taking up the limb of the patient sufferer, across which a deep flesh wound had been made by one of the bullets; \"but a little bruised alder will act like a charm. In the meantime I will wrap it in a badge of wampum! You have commenced the business of a warrior early, my brave boy, and are likely to bear a plenty of honorable scars to your grave. I know many young men that have taken scalps who cannot show such a mark as this. Go!\" having bound up the arm; \"you will be a chief!\"\n\nThe lad departed, prouder of his flowing blood than the vainest courtier could be of his blushing ribbon; and stalked among the fellows of his age, an object of general admiration and envy.\n\nBut in a moment of so many serious and important duties, this single act of juvenile fortitude did not attract the general notice and commendation it would have received under milder auspices. It had, however, served to apprise the Delawares of the position and the intentions of their enemies. Accordingly a party of adventurers, better suited to the task than the weak though spirited boy, was ordered to dislodge the skulkers. The duty was soon performed; for most of the Hurons retired of themselves when they found they had been discovered. The Delawares followed to a sufficient distance from their own encampment, and then halted for orders, apprehensive of being led into an ambush. As both parties secreted themselves, the woods were again as still and quiet as a mild summer morning and deep solitude could render them.\n\nThe calm but still impatient Uncas now collected his chiefs, and divided his power. He presented Hawkeye as a warrior, often tried, and always found deserving of confidence. When he found his friend met with a favorable reception, he bestowed on him the command of twenty men, like himself, active, skilful, and resolute. He gave the Delawares to understand the rank of Heyward among the troops of the Yengeese, and then tendered to him a trust of equal authority. But Duncan declined the charge, professing his readiness to serve as a volunteer by the side of the scout. After this disposition, the young Mohican appointed various native chiefs to fill the different situations of responsibility, and the time pressing, he gave forth the word to march. He was cheerfully, but silently, obeyed by more than two hundred men.\n\nTheir entrance into the forest was perfectly unmolested; nor did they encounter any living objects, that could either give the alarm, or furnish the intelligence they needed, until they came upon the lairs of their own scouts. Here a halt was ordered, and the chiefs were assembled to hold a \"whispering council.\"\n\nAt this meeting divers plans of operation were suggested, though none of a character to meet the wishes of their ardent leader. Had Uncas followed the promptings of his own inclinations, he would have led his followers to the charge without a moment's delay, and put the conflict to the hazard of an instant issue; but such a course would have been in opposition to all the received practices and opinions of his countrymen. He was, therefore, fain to adopt a caution that in the present temper of his mind he execrated, and to listen to advice at which his fiery spirit chafed, under the vivid recollection of Cora's danger and Magua's insolence.\n\nAfter an unsatisfactory conference of many minutes, a solitary individual was seen advancing from the side of the enemy, with such apparent haste, as to induce the belief he might be a messenger charged with pacific overtures. When within a hundred yards, however, of the cover behind which the Delaware council had assembled, the stranger hesitated, appeared uncertain what course to take, and finally halted. All eyes were now turned on Uncas, as if seeking directions how to proceed.\n\n\"Hawkeye,\" said the young chief, in a low voice, \"he must never speak to the Hurons again.\"\n\n\"His time has come,\" said the laconic scout, thrusting the long barrel of his rifle through the leaves, and taking his deliberate and fatal aim. But, instead of pulling the trigger he lowered the muzzle again, and indulged himself in a fit of his peculiar mirth. \"I took the imp for a Mingo, as I'm a miserable sinner!\" he said; \"but when my eye ranged along his ribs for a place to get the bullet in--would you think it, Uncas--I saw the musicianer's blower; and so, after all, it is the man they call Gamut, whose death can profit no one, and whose life, if his tongue can do anything but sing, may be made serviceable to our own ends. If sounds have not lost their virtue, I'll soon have a discourse with the honest fellow, and that in a voice he'll find more agreeable than the speech of 'Killdeer.'\"\n\nSo saying, Hawkeye laid aside his rifle; and crawling through the bushes until within hearing of David, he attempted to repeat the musical effort, which had conducted himself, with so much safety and _eclat_, through the Huron encampment. The exquisite organs of Gamut could not readily be deceived (and, to say the truth, it would have been difficult for any other than Hawkeye to produce a similar noise), and consequently, having once before heard the sounds, he now knew whence they proceeded. The poor fellow appeared relieved from a state of great embarrassment; for pursuing the direction of the voice--a task that to him was not much less arduous than it would have been to have gone up in the face of a battery--he soon discovered the hidden songster.\n\n\"I wonder what the Hurons will think of that!\" said the scout, laughing, as he took his companion by the arm, and urged him towards the rear. \"If the knaves lie within ear-shot, they will say there are two non-compossers instead of one! But here we are safe,\" he added, pointing to Uncas and his associates. \"Now give us the history of the Mingo inventions in natural English, and without any ups and downs of voice.\"\n\nDavid gazed about him, at the fierce and wild-looking chiefs, in mute wonder; but assured by the presence of faces that he knew, he soon rallied his faculties so far as to make an intelligent reply.\n\n\"The heathen are abroad in goodly numbers,\" said David, \"and, I fear, with evil intent. There has been much howling and ungodly revelry, together with such sounds as it is profanity to utter, in their habitations within the past hour; so much so, in truth, that I have fled to the Delawares in search of peace.\"\n\n\"Your ears might not have profited much by the exchange, had you been quicker of foot,\" returned the scout, a little dryly. \"But let that be as it may; where are the Hurons?\"\n\n\"They lie hid in the forest, between this spot and their village, in such force, that prudence would teach you instantly to return.\"\n\nUncas cast a glance along the range of trees which concealed his own band and mentioned the name of--\n\n\"Magua?\"\n\n\"Is among them. He brought in the maiden that had sojourned with the Delawares, and leaving her in the cave, has put himself, like a raging wolf, at the head of his savages. I know not what has troubled his spirit so greatly!\"\n\n\"He has left her, you say, in the cave!\" interrupted Heyward; \"'tis well that we know its situation! May not something be done for her instant relief?\"\n\nUncas looked earnestly at the scout, before he asked,--\n\n\"What says Hawkeye?\"\n\n\"Give me twenty rifles, and I will turn to the right, along the stream; and passing by the huts of the beaver, will join the Sagamore and the colonel. You shall then hear the whoop from that quarter; with this wind one may easily send it a mile. Then, Uncas, do you drive in their front; when they come within range of our pieces, we will give them a blow that, I pledge the good name of an old frontiersman, shall make their line bend like an ashen bow. After which, we will carry their village, and take the woman from the cave; when the affair may be finished with the tribe, according to a white man's battle, by a blow and a victory; or, in the Indian fashion, with dodge and cover. There may be no great learning, major, in this plan, but with courage and patience it can all be done.\"\n\n\"I like it much,\" cried Duncan, who saw the release of Cora was the primary object in the mind of the scout; \"I like it much. Let it be instantly attempted.\"\n\nAfter a short conference, the plan was matured, and rendered more intelligible to the several parties; the different signals were appointed, and the chiefs separated, each to his allotted station.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII\n\n\"But plagues shall spread, and funeral fires increase, Till the great king, without a ransom paid, To her own Chrysa send the black-eyed maid.\"\n\nDuring the time Uncas was making this disposition of his forces, the woods were still, and, with the exception of those who had met in council, apparently as much untenanted, as when they came fresh from the hands of their Almighty Creator. The eye could range, in every direction, through the long and shadowed vistas of the trees; but nowhere was any object to be seen that did not properly belong to the peaceful and slumbering scenery. Here and there a bird was heard fluttering among the branches of the beeches, and occasionally a squirrel dropped a nut, drawing the startled looks of the party, for a moment, to the place; but the instant the casual interruption ceased, the passing air was heard murmuring above their heads, along that verdant and undulating surface of forest, which spread itself unbroken, unless by stream or lake, over such a vast region of country. Across the tract of wilderness, which lay between the Delawares and the village of their enemies, it seemed as if the foot of man had never trodden, so breathing and deep was the silence in which it lay. But Hawkeye, whose duty led him foremost in the adventure, knew the character of those with whom he was about to contend too well to trust the treacherous quiet.\n\nWhen he saw his little band collected, the scout threw \"Killdeer\" into the hollow of his arm, and making a silent signal that he would be followed, he led them many rods towards the rear, into the bed of a little brook which they had crossed in advancing. Here he halted; and after waiting for the whole of his grave and attentive warriors to close about him, he spoke in Delaware, demanding--\n\n\"Do any of my young men know whither this run will lead us?\"\n\nA Delaware stretched forth a hand, with the two fingers separated, and indicating the manner in which they were joined at the root, he answered,--\n\n\"Before the sun could go his own length, the little water will be in the big.\" Then he added, pointing in the direction of the place he mentioned, \"the two make enough for the beavers.\"\n\n\"I thought as much,\" returned the scout, glancing his eye upwards at the opening in the tree-tops, \"from the course it takes, and the bearings of the mountains. Men, we will keep within the cover of its banks till we scent the Hurons.\"\n\nHis companions gave the usual brief exclamation of assent, but perceiving that their leader was about to lead the way in person, one or two made signs that all was not as it should be. Hawkeye, who comprehended their meaning glances, turned, and perceived that his party had been followed thus far by the singing-master.\n\n\"Do you know, friend,\" asked the scout gravely, and perhaps with a little of the pride of conscious deserving in his manner, \"that this is a band of rangers chosen for the most desperate service, and put under the command of one who, though another might say it with a better face, will not be apt to leave them idle. It may not be five, it cannot be thirty minutes before we tread on the body of a Huron, living or dead.\"\n\n\"Though not admonished of your intentions in words,\" returned David, whose face was a little flushed, and whose ordinarily quiet and unmeaning eyes glimmered with an expression of unusual fire, \"your men have reminded me of the children of Jacob going out to battle against the Shechemites, for wickedly aspiring to wedlock with a woman of a race that was favored of the Lord. Now, I have journeyed far, and sojourned much in good and evil with the maiden ye seek; and though not a man of war, with my loins girded and my sword sharpened, yet would I gladly strike a blow in her behalf.\"\n\nThe scout hesitated, as if weighing the chances of such a strange enlistment in his mind before he answered,--\n\n\"You know not the use of any we'pon. You carry no rifle; and believe me, what the Mingos take they will freely give again.\"\n\n\"Though not a vaunting and bloodily disposed Goliath,\" returned David, drawing a sling from beneath his parti-colored and uncouth attire, \"I have not forgotten the example of the Jewish boy. With this ancient instrument of war have I practised much in my youth, and peradventure the skill has not entirely departed from me.\"\n\n\"Ay!\" said Hawkeye, considering the deer-skin thong and apron, with a cold and discouraging eye; \"the thing might do its work among arrows, or even knives; but these Mengwe have been furnished by the Frenchers with a good grooved barrel a man. However, it seems to be your gift to go unharmed amid fire; and as you have hitherto been favored--major, you have left your rifle at a cock; a single shot before the time would be just twenty scalps lost to no purpose--singer, you can follow; we may find use for you in the shoutings.\"\n\n\"I thank you, friend,\" returned David, supplying himself, like his royal namesake, from among the pebbles of the brook; \"though not given to the desire to kill, had you sent me away my spirit would have been troubled.\"\n\n\"Remember,\" added the scout, tapping his own head significantly on that spot where Gamut was yet sore, \"we come to fight, and not to musickate. Until the general whoop is given, nothing speaks but the rifle.\"\n\nDavid nodded, as much as to signify his acquiescence with the terms; and then Hawkeye, casting another observant glance over his followers, made the signal to proceed.\n\nTheir route lay, for the distance of a mile, along the bed of the water-course. Though protected from any great danger of observation by the precipitous banks, and the thick shrubbery which skirted the stream, no precaution known to an Indian attack was neglected. A warrior rather crawled than walked on each flank, so as to catch occasional glimpses into the forest; and every few minutes the band came to a halt, and listened for hostile sounds, with an acuteness of organs that would be scarcely conceivable to a man in a less natural state. Their march was, however, unmolested, and they reached the point where the lesser stream was lost in the greater, without the smallest evidence that their progress had been noted. Here the scout again halted, to consult the signs of the forest.\n\n\"We are likely to have a good day for a fight,\" he said, in English, addressing Heyward, and glancing his eye upwards at the clouds, which began to move in broad sheets across the firmament; \"a bright sun and a glittering barrel are no friends to true sight. Everything is favorable; they have the wind, which will bring down their noises and their smoke too, no little matter in itself; whereas, with us it will be first a shot, and then a clear view. But here is an end of our cover; the beavers have had the range of this stream for hundreds of years, and what atween their food and their dams, there is, as you see, many a girdled stub, but few living trees.\"\n\nHawkeye had, in truth, in these few words, given no bad description of the prospect that now lay in their front. The brook was irregular in its width, sometimes shooting through narrow fissures in the rocks, and at others spreading over acres of bottom land, forming little areas that might be termed ponds. Everywhere along its banks were the mouldering relics of dead trees, in all the stages of decay, from those that groaned on their tottering trunks to such as had recently been robbed of those rugged coats that so mysteriously contain their principle of life. A few long, low, and moss-covered piles were scattered among them, like the memorials of a former and long-departed generation.\n\nAll these minute particulars were noted by the scout, with a gravity and interest that they probably had never before attracted. He knew that the Huron encampment lay a short half mile up the brook; and, with the characteristic anxiety of one who dreaded a hidden danger, he was greatly troubled at not finding the smallest trace of the presence of his enemy. Once or twice he felt induced to give the order for a rush, and to attempt the village by surprise; but his experience quickly admonished him of the danger of so useless an experiment. Then he listened intently, and with painful uncertainty, for the sounds of hostility in the quarter where Uncas was left; but nothing was audible except the sighing of the wind, that began to sweep over the bosom of the forest in gusts which threatened a tempest. At length, yielding rather to his unusual impatience than taking counsel from his knowledge, he determined to bring matters to an issue, by unmasking his force, and proceeding cautiously, but steadily, up the stream.\n\nThe scout had stood, while making his observations, sheltered by a brake, and his companions still lay in the bed of the ravine, through which the smaller stream debouched; but on hearing his low, though intelligible signal, the whole party stole up the bank, like so many dark spectres, and silently arranged themselves around him. Pointing in the direction he wished to proceed, Hawkeye advanced, the band breaking off in single files, and following so accurately in his footsteps, as if to leave it, if we except Heyward and David, the trail of but a single man.\n\nThe party was, however, scarcely uncovered before a volley from a dozen rifles was heard in their rear; and a Delaware leaping high into the air, like a wounded deer, fell at his whole length, perfectly dead.\n\n\"Ah! I feared some deviltry like this!\" exclaimed the scout, in English; adding, with the quickness of thought, in his adopted tongue, \"To cover, men, and charge!\"\n\nThe band dispersed at the word, and before Heyward had well recovered from his surprise, he found himself standing alone with David. Luckily, the Hurons had already fallen back, and he was safe from their fire. But this state of things was evidently to be of short continuance; for the scout set the example of pressing on their retreat, by discharging his rifle, and darting from tree to tree, as his enemy slowly yielded ground.\n\nIt would seem that the assault had been made by a very small party of the Hurons, which, however, continued to increase in numbers, as it retired on its friends, until the return fire was very nearly, if not quite, equal to that maintained by the advancing Delawares. Heyward threw himself among the combatants, and imitating the necessary caution of his companions, he made quick discharges with his own rifle. The contest now grew warm and stationary. Few were injured, as both parties kept their bodies as much protected as possible by the trees; never, indeed, exposing any part of their persons except in the act of taking aim. But the chances were gradually growing unfavorable to Hawkeye and his band. The quick-sighted scout perceived his danger, without knowing how to remedy it. He saw it was more dangerous to retreat than to maintain his ground; while he found his enemy throwing out men on his flank, which rendered the task of keeping themselves covered so very difficult to the Delawares, as nearly to silence their fire. At this embarrassing moment, when they began to think the whole of the hostile tribe was gradually encircling them, they heard the yell of combatants, and the rattling of arms, echoing under the arches of the wood, at the place where Uncas was posted; a bottom which, in a manner, lay beneath the ground on which Hawkeye and his party were contending.\n\nThe effects of this attack were instantaneous, and to the scout and his friends greatly relieving. It would seem that, while his own surprise had been anticipated, and had consequently failed, the enemy, in their turn, having been deceived in its object and in his numbers, had left too small a force to resist the impetuous onset of the young Mohican. This fact was doubly apparent, by the rapid manner in which the battle in the forest rolled upwards towards the village, and by an instant falling off in the number of their assailants, who rushed to assist in maintaining the front, and, as it now proved to be, the principal point of defence.\n\nAnimating his followers by his voice, and his own example, Hawkeye then gave the word to bear down upon their foes. The charge, in that rude species of warfare, consisted merely in pushing from cover to cover, nigher to the enemy; and in this manoeuvre he was instantly and successfully obeyed. The Hurons were compelled to withdraw, and the scene of the contest rapidly changed from the more open ground on which it had commenced, to a spot where the assailed found a thicket to rest upon. Here the struggle was protracted, arduous, and seemingly of doubtful issue; the Delawares, though none of them fell, beginning to bleed freely, in consequence of the disadvantage at which they were held.\n\nIn this crisis, Hawkeye found means to get behind the same tree as that which served for a cover to Heyward; most of his own combatants being within call, a little on his right, where they maintained rapid, though fruitless, discharges on their sheltered enemies.\n\n\"You are a young man, major,\" said the scout, dropping the butt of \"Killdeer\" to the earth, and leaning on the barrel, a little fatigued with his previous industry; \"and it may be your gift to lead armies at some future day ag'in these imps the Mingos, You may here see the philosophy of an Indian fight. It consists mainly in a ready hand, a quick eye, and a good cover. Now, if you had a company of the Royal Americans here, in what manner would you set them to work in this business?\"\n\n\"The bayonet would make a road.\"\n\n\"Ay, there is white reason in what you say; but a man must ask himself, in this wilderness, how many lives he can spare. No--horse,\"[29] continued the scout, shaking his head, like one who mused; \"horse, I am ashamed to say, must, sooner or later, decide these scrimmages. The brutes are better than men, and to horse must we come at last. Put a shodden hoof on the moccasin of a redskin; and if his rifle be once emptied, he will never stop to load it again.\"\n\n\"This is a subject that might better be discussed at another time,\" returned Heyward; \"shall we charge?\"\n\n\"I see no contradiction to the gifts of any man, in passing his breathing spells in useful reflections,\" the scout replied. \"As to a rush, I little relish such a measure; for a scalp or two must be thrown away in the attempt. And yet,\" he added, bending his head aside, to catch the sounds of the distant combat, \"if we are to be of use to Uncas, these knaves in our front must be got rid of!\"\n\nThen turning, with a prompt and decided air, he called aloud to his Indians, in their own language. His words were answered by a shout; and, at a given signal, each warrior made a swift movement around his particular tree. The sight of so many dark bodies, glancing before their eyes at the same instant, drew a hasty, and consequently an ineffectual fire from the Hurons. Without stopping to breathe, the Delawares leaped, in long bounds, towards the wood, like so many panthers springing upon their prey. Hawkeye was in front, brandishing his terrible rifle, and animating his followers by his example. A few of the older and more cunning Hurons, who had not been deceived by the artifice which had been practised to draw their fire, now made a close and deadly discharge of their pieces, and justified the apprehensions of the scout, by felling three of his foremost warriors. But the shock was insufficient to repel the impetus of the charge. The Delawares broke into the cover with the ferocity of their natures, and swept away every trace of resistance by the fury of the onset.\n\nThe combat endured only for an instant, hand to hand, and then the assailed yielded ground rapidly, until they reached the opposite margin of the thicket, where they clung to the cover, with the sort of obstinacy that is so often witnessed in hunted brutes. At this critical moment, when the success of the struggle was again becoming doubtful, the crack of the rifle was heard behind the Hurons, and a bullet came whizzing from among some beaver lodges, which were situated in the clearing, in their rear, and was followed by the fierce and appalling yell of the war-whoop.\n\n\"There speaks the Sagamore!\" shouted Hawkeye, answering the cry with his own stentorian voice; \"we have them now in face and back!\"\n\nThe effect on the Hurons was instantaneous. Discouraged by an assault from a quarter that left them no opportunity for cover, their warriors uttered a common yell of disappointment, and breaking off in a body, they spread themselves across the opening, heedless of every consideration but flight. Many fell, in making the experiment, under the bullets and the blows of the pursuing Delawares.\n\nWe shall not pause to detail the meeting between the scout and Chingachgook, or the more touching interview that Duncan held with Munro. A few brief and hurried words served to explain the state of things to both parties; and then Hawkeye pointing out the Sagamore to his band, resigned the chief authority into the hands of the Mohican chief. Chingachgook assumed the station to which his birth and experience gave him so distinguished a claim, with the grave dignity that always gives force to the mandates of a native warrior. Following the footsteps of the scout, he led the party back through the thicket, his men scalping the fallen Hurons, and secreting the bodies of their own dead as they proceeded, until they gained a point where the former was content to make a halt.\n\nThe warriors, who had breathed themselves freely in the preceding struggle, were now posted on a bit of level ground, sprinkled with trees in sufficient numbers to conceal them. The land fell away rather precipitately in front, and beneath their eyes stretched, for several miles, a narrow, dark, and wooded vale. It was through this dense and dark forest that Uncas was still contending with the main body of the Hurons.\n\nThe Mohican and his friends advanced to the brow of the hill, and listened, with practised ears, to the sounds of the combat. A few birds hovered over the leafy bosom of the valley, frightened from their secluded nests; and here and there a light vapory cloud, which seemed already blending with the atmosphere, arose above the trees, and indicated some spot where the struggle had been fierce and stationary.\n\n\"The fight is coming up the ascent,\" said Duncan, pointing in the direction of a new explosion of fire-arms; \"we are too much in the centre of their line to be effective.\"\n\n\"They will incline into the hollow, where the cover is thicker,\" said the scout, \"and that will leave us well on their flank. Go, Sagamore; you will hardly be in time to give the whoop, and lead on the young men. I will fight this scrimmage with warriors of my own color. You know me, Mohican; not a Huron of them all shall cross the swell, into your rear, without the notice of 'Killdeer.'\"\n\nThe Indian chief paused another moment to consider the signs of the contest, which was now rolling rapidly up the ascent, a certain evidence that the Delawares triumphed; nor did he actually quit the place until admonished of the proximity of his friends, as well as enemies, by the bullets of the former, which began to patter among the dried leaves on the ground, like the bits of falling hail which precede the bursting of the tempest. Hawkeye and his three companions withdrew a few paces to a shelter, and awaited the issue with calmness, that nothing but great practice could impart in such a scene.\n\nIt was not long before the reports of the rifles began to lose the echoes of the woods, and to sound like weapons discharged in the open air. Then a warrior appeared, here and there, driven to the skirts of the forest, and rallying as he entered the clearing, as at the place where the final stand was to be made. These were soon joined by the others, until a long line of swarthy figures was to be seen clinging to the cover with the obstinacy of desperation. Heyward began to grow impatient, and turned his eyes anxiously in the direction of Chingachgook. The chief was seated on a rock, with nothing visible but his calm visage, considering the spectacle with an eye as deliberate as if he were posted there merely to view the struggle.\n\n\"The time is come for the Delawares to strike!\" said Duncan.\n\n\"Not so, not so,\" returned the scout; \"when he scents his friends, he will let them know that he is here. See, see; the knaves are getting in that clump of pines, like bees settling after their flight. By the Lord, a squaw might put a bullet into the centre of such a knot of dark skins!\"\n\nAt that instant the whoop was given, and a dozen Hurons fell by a discharge from Chingachgook and his band. The shout that followed was answered by a single war-cry from the forest, and a yell passed through the air that sounded as if a thousand throats were united in a common effort. The Hurons staggered, deserting the centre of their line, and Uncas issued from the forest through the opening they left, at the head of a hundred warriors.\n\nWaving his hands right and left, the young chief pointed out the enemy to his followers, who separated in pursuit. The war now divided, both wings of the broken Hurons seeking protection in the woods again, hotly pressed by the victorious warriors of the Lenape. A minute might have passed, but the sounds were already receding in different directions, and gradually losing their distinctness beneath the echoing arches of the woods. One little knot of Hurons, however, had disdained to seek a cover, and were retiring, like lions at bay, slowly and sullenly up the acclivity which Chingachgook and his band had just deserted, to mingle more closely in the fray. Magua was conspicuous in this party, both by his fierce and savage mien, and by the air of haughty authority he yet maintained.\n\nIn his eagerness to expedite the pursuit, Uncas had left himself nearly alone; but the moment his eyes caught the figure of Le Subtil, every other consideration was forgotten. Raising his cry of battle, which recalled some six or seven warriors, and reckless of the disparity of their numbers, he rushed upon his enemy. Le Renard, who watched the movement, paused to receive him with secret joy. But at the moment when he thought the rashness of his impetuous young assailant had left him at his mercy, another shout was given, and La Longue Carabine was seen rushing to the rescue, attended by all his white associates. The Huron instantly turned, and commenced a rapid retreat up the ascent.\n\nThere was no time for greetings or congratulations; for Uncas, though unconscious of the presence of his friends, continued the pursuit with the velocity of the wind. In vain Hawkeye called to him to respect the covers; the young Mohican braved the dangerous fire of his enemies, and soon compelled them to a flight as swift as his own headlong speed. It was fortunate that the race was of short continuance, and that the white men were much favored by their position, or the Delaware would soon have outstripped all his companions, and fallen a victim to his own temerity. But ere such a calamity could happen, the pursuers and pursued entered the Wyandot village, within striking distance of each other.\n\nExcited by the presence of their dwellings, and tired of the chase, the Hurons now made a stand, and fought around their council-lodge with the fury of despair. The onset and the issue were like the passage and destruction of a whirlwind. The tomahawk of Uncas, the blows of Hawkeye, and even the still nervous arm of Munro, were all busy for that passing moment, and the ground was quickly strewed with their enemies. Still Magua, though daring and much exposed, escaped from every effort against his life, with that sort of fabled protection that was made to overlook the fortunes of favored heroes in the legends of ancient poetry. Raising a yell that spoke volumes of anger and disappointment, the subtle chief, when he saw his comrades fallen, darted away from the place, attended by his two only surviving friends, leaving the Delawares engaged in stripping the dead of the bloody trophies of their victory.\n\nBut Uncas, who had vainly sought him in the _melee_ bounded forward in pursuit; Hawkeye, Heyward, and David still pressing on his footsteps. The utmost that the scout could effect, was to keep the muzzle of his rifle a little in advance of his friend, to whom, however, it answered every purpose of a charmed shield. Once Magua appeared disposed to make another and a final effort to revenge his losses; but, abandoning his intention as soon as demonstrated, he leaped into a thicket of bushes, through which he was followed by his enemies, and suddenly entered the mouth of the cave already known to the reader. Hawkeye, who had only forborne to fire in tenderness to Uncas, raised a shout of success, and proclaimed aloud, that now they were certain of their game. The pursuers dashed into the long and narrow entrance, in time to catch a glimpse of the retreating forms of the Hurons. Their passage through the natural galleries and subterraneous apartments of the cavern was preceded by the shrieks and cries of hundreds of women and children. The place, seen by its dim and uncertain light, appeared like the shades of the infernal regions, across which unhappy ghosts and savage demons were flitting in multitudes.\n\nStill Uncas kept his eye on Magua, as if life to him possessed but a single object. Heyward and the scout still pressed on his rear, actuated, though possibly in a less degree, by a common feeling. But their way was becoming intricate, in those dark and gloomy passages, and the glimpses of the retiring warriors less distinct and frequent; and for a moment the trace was believed to be lost, when a white robe was seen fluttering in the farther extremity of a passage that seemed to lead up the mountain.\n\n\"'Tis Cora!\" exclaimed Heyward, in a voice in which horror and delight were wildly mingled.\n\n\"Cora! Cora!\" echoed Uncas, bending forward like a deer.\n\n\"'Tis the maiden!\" shouted the scout, \"Courage, lady; we come!--we come!\"\n\nThe chase was renewed with a diligence rendered tenfold encouraging by this glimpse of the captive. But the way was rugged, broken, and in spots nearly impassable. Uncas abandoned his rifle, and leaped forward with headlong precipitation. Heyward rashly imitated his example, though both were, a moment afterwards, admonished of its madness, by hearing the bellowing of a piece, that the Hurons found time to discharge down the passage in the rocks, the bullet from which even gave the young Mohican a slight wound.\n\n\"We must close!\" said the scout, passing his friends by a desperate leap; \"the knaves will pick us all off at this distance; and see, they hold the maiden so as to shield themselves!\"\n\nThough his words were unheeded, or rather unheard, his example was followed by his companions, who, by incredible exertions, got near enough to the fugitives to perceive that Cora was borne along between the two warriors, while Magua prescribed the direction and manner of their flight. At this moment the forms of all four were strongly drawn against an opening in the sky, and they disappeared. Nearly frantic with disappointment, Uncas and Heyward increased efforts that already seemed super-human, and they issued from the cavern on the side of the mountain, in time to note the route of the pursued. The course lay up the ascent, and still continued hazardous and laborious.\n\nEncumbered by his rifle, and, perhaps, not sustained by so deep an interest in the captive as his companions, the scout suffered the latter to precede him a little, Uncas, in his turn, taking the lead of Heyward. In this manner, rocks, precipices, and difficulties were surmounted in an incredibly short space, that at another time, and under other circumstances, would have been deemed almost insuperable. But the impetuous young men were rewarded, by finding that, encumbered with Cora, the Hurons were losing ground in the race.\n\n\"Stay, dog of the Wyandots!\" exclaimed Uncas, shaking his bright tomahawk at Magua; \"a Delaware girl calls stay!\"\n\n\"I will go no farther,\" cried Cora, stepping unexpectedly on a ledge of rocks, that overhung a deep precipice, at no great distance from the summit of the mountain. \"Kill me if thou wilt, detestable Huron; I will go no farther.\"\n\nThe supporters of the maiden raised their ready tomahawks with the impious joy that fiends are thought to take in mischief, but Magua stayed the uplifted arms. The Huron chief, after casting the weapons he had wrested from his companions over the rock, drew his knife, and turned to his captive, with a look in which conflicting passions fiercely contended.\n\n\"Woman,\" he said, \"choose; the wigwam or the knife of Le Subtil!\"\n\nCora regarded him not, but dropping on her knees, she raised her eyes and stretched her arms towards heaven, saying, in a meek and yet confiding voice,--\n\n\"I am thine! do with me as thou seest best!\"\n\n\"Woman,\" repeated Magua, hoarsely, and endeavoring in vain to catch a glance from her serene and beaming eye, \"choose!\"\n\nBut Cora neither heard nor heeded his demand. The form of the Huron trembled in every fibre, and he raised his arm on high, but dropped it again with a bewildered air, like one who doubted. Once more he struggled with himself and lifted the keen weapon again; but just then a piercing cry was heard above them, and Uncas appeared, leaping frantically, from a fearful height, upon the ledge. Magua recoiled a step; and one of his assistants, profiting by the chance, sheathed his own knife in the bosom of Cora.\n\nThe Huron sprang like a tiger on his offending and already retreating countryman, but the falling form of Uncas separated the unnatural combatants. Diverted from his object by this interruption, and maddened by the murder he had just witnessed, Magua buried his weapon in the back of the prostrate Delaware, uttering an unearthly shout as he committed the dastardly deed. But Uncas arose from the blow, as the wounded panther turns upon his foe, and struck the murderer of Cora to his feet, by an effort in which the last of his failing strength was expended. Then, with a stern and steady look, he turned to Le Subtil, and indicated by the expression of his eye, all that he would do, had not the power deserted him. The latter seized the nerveless arm of the unresisting Delaware, and passed his knife into his bosom three several times, before his victim, still keeping his gaze riveted on his enemy with a look of inextinguishable scorn, fell dead at his feet.\n\n\"Mercy! mercy! Huron,\" cried Heyward, from above, in tones nearly choked by horror; \"give mercy, and thou shalt receive it!\"\n\nWhirling the bloody knife up at the imploring youth, the victorious Magua uttered a cry so fierce, so wild, and yet so joyous, that it conveyed the sounds of savage triumph to the ears of those who fought in the valley, a thousand feet below. He was answered by a burst from the lips of the scout, whose tall person was just then seen moving swiftly towards him, along those dangerous crags, with steps as bold and reckless as if he possessed the power to move in air. But when the hunter reached the scene of the ruthless massacre, the ledge was tenanted only by the dead.\n\nHis keen eye took a single look at the victims, and then shot its glances over the difficulties of the ascent in his front. A form stood at the brow of the mountain, on the very edge of the giddy height, with uplifted arms, in an awful attitude of menace. Without stopping to consider his person, the rifle of Hawkeye was raised; but a rock, which fell on the head of one of the fugitives below exposed the indignant and glowing countenance of the honest Gamut. Then Magua issued from a crevice, and stepping with calm indifference over the body of the last of his associates, he leaped a wide fissure, and ascended the rocks at a point where the arm of David could not reach him. A single bound would carry him to the brow of the precipice, and assure his safety. Before taking the leap, however, the Huron paused, and shaking his hand at the scout, he shouted,--\n\n\"The pale-faces are dogs! the Delawares women! Magua leaves them on the rocks, for the crows!\"\n\nLaughing hoarsely, he made a desperate leap, and fell short of his mark; though his hand grasped a shrub on the verge of the height. The form of Hawkeye had crouched like a beast about to take its spring, and his frame trembled so violently with eagerness, that the muzzle of the half-raised rifle played like a leaf fluttering in the wind. Without exhausting himself with fruitless efforts, the cunning Magua suffered his body to drop to the length of his arms, and found a fragment for his feet to rest on. Then summoning all his powers, he renewed the attempt, and so far succeeded, as to draw his knees on the edge of the mountain. It was now, when the body of his enemy was most collected together, that the agitated weapon of the scout was drawn to his shoulder. The surrounding rocks themselves were not steadier than the piece became, for the single instant that it poured out its contents. The arms of the Huron relaxed, and his body fell back a little, while his knees still kept their position. Turning a relentless look on his enemy, he shook a hand in grim defiance. But his hold loosened, and his dark person was seen cutting the air with its head downwards, for a fleeting instant, until it glided past the fringe of shrubbery which clung to the mountain, in its rapid flight to destruction.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII\n\n\"They fought, like brave men, long and well, They piled that ground with Moslem slain, They conquered--but Bozzaris fell, Bleeding at every vein. His few surviving comrades saw His smile when rang their proud hurrah, And the red field was won; Then saw in death his eyelids close Calmly, as to a night's repose, Like flowers at set of sun.\"\n\nThe sun found the Lenape, on the succeeding day, a nation of mourners. The sounds of the battle were over, and they had fed fat their ancient grudge, and had avenged their recent quarrel with the Mengwe, by the destruction of a whole community. The black and murky atmosphere that floated around the spot where the Hurons had encamped, sufficiently announced, of itself, the fate of that wandering tribe; while hundreds of ravens, that struggled above the bleak summits of the mountains, or swept, in noisy flocks, across the wide ranges of the woods, furnished a frightful direction to the scene of the combat. In short, any eye, at all practised in the signs of a frontier warfare, might easily have traced all those unerring evidences of the ruthless results which attend an Indian vengeance.\n\nStill, the sun rose on the Lenape a nation of mourners. No shouts of success, no songs of triumph, were heard, in rejoicings for their victory. The latest straggler had returned from his fell employment, only to strip himself of the terrific emblems of his bloody calling, and to join in the lamentations of his countrymen, as a stricken people. Pride and exultation were supplanted by humility, and the fiercest of human passions was already succeeded by the most profound and unequivocal demonstrations of grief.\n\nThe lodges were deserted; but a broad belt of earnest faces encircled a spot in their vicinity, whither everything possessing life had repaired, and where all were now collected, in deep and awful silence. Though beings of every rank and age, of both sexes, and of all pursuits, had united to form this breathing wall of bodies, they were influenced by a single emotion. Each eye was riveted on the centre of that ring, which contained the objects of so much, and of so common, an interest.\n\nSix Delaware girls, with their long, dark, flowing tresses falling loosely across their bosoms, stood apart, and only gave proofs of their existence as they occasionally strewed sweet-scented herbs and forest flowers on a litter of fragrant plants, that, under a pall of Indian robes, supported all that now remained of the ardent, high-souled, and generous Cora. Her form was concealed in many wrappers of the same simple manufacture, and her face was shut forever from the gaze of men. At her feet was seated the desolate Munro. His aged head was bowed nearly to the earth, in compelled submission to the stroke of Providence; but a hidden anguish struggled about his furrowed brow, that was only partially concealed by the careless locks of gray that had fallen, neglected, on his temples. Gamut stood at his side, his meek head bared to the rays of the sun, while his eyes, wandering and concerned, seemed to be equally divided between that little volume, which contained so many quaint but holy maxims, and the being in whose behalf his soul yearned to administer consolation. Heyward was also nigh, supporting himself against a tree, and endeavoring to keep down those sudden risings of sorrow that it required his utmost manhood to subdue.\n\nBut sad and melancholy as this group may easily be imagined, it was far less touching than another, that occupied the opposite space of the same area. Seated, as in life, with his form and limbs arranged in grave and decent composure, Uncas appeared, arrayed in the most gorgeous ornaments that the wealth of the tribe could furnish. Rich plumes nodded above his head; wampum, gorgets, bracelets, and medals, adorned his person in profusion; though his dull eye and vacant lineaments too strongly contradicted the idle tale of pride they would convey.\n\nDirectly in front of the corpse Chingachgook was placed, without arms, paint, or adornment of any sort, except the bright blue blazonry of his race, that was indelibly impressed on his naked bosom. During the long period that the tribe had been thus collected, the Mohican warrior had kept a steady, anxious look on the cold and senseless countenance of his son. So riveted and intense had been that gaze, and so changeless his attitude, that a stranger might not have told the living from the dead, but for the occasional gleamings of a troubled spirit that shot athwart the dark visage of one, and the death-like calm that had forever settled on the lineaments of the other.\n\nThe scout was hard by, leaning in a pensive posture on his own fatal and avenging weapon; while Tamenund, supported by the elders of his nation, occupied a high place at hand, whence he might look down on the mute and sorrowful assemblage of his people.\nJust within the inner edge of the circle stood a soldier, in the military attire of a strange nation; and without it was his war-horse, in the centre of a collection of mounted domestics, seemingly in readiness to undertake some distant journey. The vestments of the stranger announced him to be one who held a responsible situation near the person of the captain of the Canadas; and who, as it would now seem, finding his errand of peace frustrated by the fierce impetuosity of his allies, was content to become a silent and sad spectator of the fruits of a contest that he had arrived too late to anticipate.\n\nThe day was drawing to the close of its first quarter, and yet had the multitude maintained its breathing stillness since its dawn. No sound louder than a stifled sob had been heard among them, nor had even a limb been moved throughout that long and painful period, except to perform the simple and touching offerings that were made, from time to time, in commemoration of the dead. The patience and forbearance of Indian fortitude could alone support such an appearance of abstraction, as seemed now to have turned each dark and motionless figure into stone.\n\nAt length, the sage of the Delawares stretched forth an arm, and leaning on the shoulders of his attendants, he arose with an air as feeble as if another age had already intervened between the man who had met his nation the preceding day, and him who now tottered on his elevated stand.\n\n\"Men of the Lenape!\" he said, in hollow tones that sounded like a voice charged with some prophetic mission; \"the face of the Manitou is behind a cloud! His eye is turned from you; His ears are shut; His tongue gives no answer. You see Him not; yet His judgments are before you. Let your hearts be open and your spirits tell no lie. Men of the Lenape! the face of the Manitou is behind a cloud.\"\n\nAs this simple and yet terrible annunciation stole on the ears of the multitude, a stillness as deep and awful succeeded as if the venerated spirit they worshipped had uttered the words without the aid of human organs; and even the inanimate Uncas appeared a being of life, compared with the humbled and submissive throng by whom he was surrounded. As the immediate effect, however, gradually passed away, a low murmur of voices commenced a sort of chant in honor of the dead. The sounds were those of females, and were thrillingly soft and wailing. The words were connected by no regular continuation, but as one ceased another took up the eulogy, or lamentation, whichever it might be called, and gave vent to her emotions in such language as was suggested by her feelings and the occasion. At intervals the speaker was interrupted by general and loud bursts of sorrow, during which the girls around the bier of Cora plucked the plants and flowers blindly from her body, as if bewildered with grief. But, in the milder moments of their plaint, these emblems of purity and sweetness were cast back to their places, with every sign of tenderness and regret. Though rendered less connected by many and general interruptions and outbreakings, a translation of their language would have contained a regular descant, which, in substance, might have proved to possess a train of consecutive ideas.\n\nA girl, selected for the task by her rank and qualifications, commenced by modest allusions to the qualities of the deceased warrior, embellishing her expressions with those oriental images that the Indians have probably brought with them from the extremes of the other continent, and which form of themselves a link to connect the ancient histories of the two worlds. She called him the \"panther of his tribe\"; and described him as one whose moccasin left no trail on the dews; whose bound was like the leap of the young fawn; whose eye was brighter than a star in the dark night; and whose voice, in battle, was loud as the thunder of the Manitou. She reminded him of the mother who bore him, and dwelt forcibly on the happiness she must feel in possessing such a son. She bade him tell her, when they met in the world of spirits, that the Delaware girls had shed tears above the grave of her child, and had called her blessed.\n\nThen, they who succeeded, changing their tones to a milder and still more tender strain, alluded, with the delicacy and sensitiveness of woman, to the stranger maiden, who had left the upper earth at a time so near his own departure, as to render the will of the Great Spirit too manifest to be disregarded. They admonished him to be kind to her, and to have consideration for her ignorance of those arts which were so necessary to the comfort of a warrior like himself. They dwelt upon her matchless beauty, and on her noble resolution, without the taint of envy, and as angels may be thought to delight in a superior excellence; adding, that these endowments should prove more than equivalent for any little imperfections in her education.\n\nAfter which, others again, in due succession, spoke to the maiden herself, in the low, soft language of tenderness and love. They exhorted her to be of cheerful mind, and to fear nothing for future welfare. A hunter would be her companion, who knew how to provide for her smallest wants; and a warrior was at her side who was able to protect her against every danger. They promised that her path should be pleasant, and her burden light. They cautioned her against unavailing regrets for the friends of her youth, and the scenes where her fathers had dwelt; assuring her that the \"blessed hunting-grounds of the Lenape\" contained vales as pleasant, streams as pure, and flowers as sweet, as the \"heaven of the pale-faces.\" They advised her to be attentive to the wants of her companion, and never to forget the distinction which the Manitou had so wisely established between them. Then, in a wild burst of their chant, they sang with united voices the temper of the Mohican's mind. They pronounced him noble, manly and generous; all that became a warrior, and all that a maid might love. Clothing their ideas in the most remote and subtle images, they betrayed, that, in the short period of their intercourse, they had discovered, with the intuitive perception of their sex, the truant disposition of his inclinations. The Delaware girls had found no favor in his eyes! He was of a race that had once been lords on the shores of the salt lake, and his wishes had led him back to a people who dwelt about the graves of his fathers. Why should not such a predilection be encouraged! That she was of a blood purer and richer than the rest of her nation, any eye might have seen; that she was equal to the dangers and daring of a life in the woods, her conduct had proved; and now, they added, the \"wise one of the earth\" had transplanted her to a place where she would find congenial spirits, and might be forever happy.\n\nThen, with another transition in voice and subject, allusions were made to the virgin who wept in the adjacent lodge. They compared her to flakes of snow; as pure, as white, as brilliant, and as liable to melt in the fierce heats of summer, or congeal in the frosts of winter. They doubted not that she was lovely in the eyes of the young chief, whose skin and whose sorrow seemed so like her own; but, though far from expressing such a preference, it was evident they deemed her less excellent than the maid they mourned. Still they denied her no meed her rare charms might properly claim. Her ringlets were compared to the exuberant tendrils of the vine, her eye to the blue vault of the heavens, and the most spotless cloud, with its glowing flush of the sun, was admitted to be less attractive than her bloom.\n\nDuring these and similar songs nothing was audible but the murmurs of the music; relieved, as it was, or rather rendered terrible, by those occasional bursts of grief which might be called its choruses. The Delawares themselves listened like charmed men; and it was very apparent, by the variations of their speaking countenances, how deep and true was their sympathy. Even David was not reluctant to lend his ears to tones of voices so sweet; and long ere the chant was ended, his gaze announced that his soul was enthralled.\n\nThe scout, to whom alone, of all the white men, the words were intelligible, suffered himself to be a little aroused from his meditative posture, and bent his face aside, to catch their meaning, as the girls proceeded. But when they spoke of the future prospects of Cora and Uncas, he shook his head, like one who knew the error of their simple creed, and resuming his reclining attitude, he maintained it until the ceremony--if that might be called a ceremony, in which feeling was so deeply imbued--was finished. Happily for the self-command of both Heyward and Munro, they knew not the meaning of the wild sounds they heard.\n\nChingachgook was a solitary exception to the interest manifested by the native part of the audience. His look never changed throughout the whole of the scene, nor did a muscle move in his rigid countenance, even at the wildest or the most pathetic parts of the lamentation. The cold and senseless remains of his son was all to him, and every other sense but that of sight seemed frozen, in order that his eyes might take their final gaze at those lineaments he had so long loved, and which were now about to be closed forever from his view.\n\nIn this stage of the funeral obsequies, a warrior much renowned for deeds in arms, and more especially for services in the recent combat, a man of stern and grave demeanor, advanced slowly from the crowd, and placed himself nigh the person of the dead.\n\n\"Why hast thou left us, pride of the Wapanachki?\" he said, addressing himself to the dull ears of Uncas, as if the empty clay retained the faculties of the animated man; \"thy time has been like that of the sun when in the trees; thy glory brighter than his light at noonday. Thou art gone, youthful warrior, but a hundred Wyandots are clearing the briers from thy path to the world of spirits. Who that saw thee in battle would believe that thou couldst die? Who before thee has ever shown Uttawa the way into the fight? Thy feet were like the wings of eagles; thine arm heavier than falling branches from the pine; and thy voice like the Manitou when he speaks in the clouds. The tongue of Uttawa is weak,\" he added, looking about him with a melancholy gaze, \"and his heart exceeding heavy. Pride of the Wapanachki, why hast thou left us?\"\n\nHe was succeeded by others, in due order, until most of the high and gifted men of the nation had sung or spoken their tribute of praise over the _manes_ of the deceased chief. When each had ended, another deep and breathing silence reigned in all the place.\n\nThen a low, deep sound was heard, like the suppressed accompaniment of distant music, rising just high enough on the air to be audible, and yet so indistinctly, as to leave its character, and the place whence it proceeded, alike matters of conjecture. It was, however, succeeded by another and another strain, each in a higher key, until they grew on the ear, first in long drawn and often repeated interjections, and finally in words. The lips of Chingachgook had so far parted, as to announce that it was the monody of the father. Though not an eye was turned towards him, nor the smallest sign of impatience exhibited, it was apparent, by the manner in which the multitude elevated their heads to listen, that they drank in the sounds with an intenseness of attention, that none but Tamenund himself had ever before commanded. But they listened in vain. The strains rose just so loud as to become intelligible, and then grew fainter and more trembling, until they finally sank on the ear, as if borne away by a passing breath of wind. The lips of the Sagamore closed, and he remained silent in his seat, looking, with his riveted eye and motionless form, like some creature that had been turned from the Almighty hand with the form but without the spirit of a man. The Delawares, who knew by these symptoms that the mind of their friend was not prepared for so mighty an effort of fortitude, relaxed in their attention; and, with an innate delicacy, seemed to bestow all their thoughts on the obsequies of the stranger maiden.\n\nA signal was given, by one of the elder chiefs, to the women who crowded that part of the circle near which the body of Cora lay. Obedient to the sign, the girls raised the bier to the elevation of their heads, and advanced with slow and regulated steps, chanting, as they proceeded, another wailing song in praise of the deceased. Gamut, who had been a close observer of rites he deemed so heathenish, now bent his head over the shoulder of the unconscious father, whispering,--\n\n\"They move with the remains of thy child; shall we not follow, and see them interred with Christian burial?\"\n\nMunro started, as if the last trumpet had sounded in his ear, and bestowing one anxious and hurried glance around him, he arose and followed in the simple train, with the mien of a soldier, but bearing the full burden of a parent's suffering. His friends pressed around him with a sorrow that was too strong to be termed sympathy--even the young Frenchman joining in the procession, with the air of a man who was sensibly touched at the early and melancholy fate of one so lovely. But when the last and humblest female of the tribe had joined in the wild, and yet ordered array, the men of the Lenape contracted their circle, and formed again around the person of Uncas, as silent, as grave, and as motionless as before.\n\nThe place which had been chosen for the grave of Cora was a little knoll, where a cluster of young and healthful pines had taken root, forming of themselves a melancholy and appropriate shade over the spot. On reaching it the girls deposited their burden, and continued for many minutes waiting, with characteristic patience, and native timidity, for some evidence that they whose feelings were most concerned were content with the arrangement. At length the scout, who alone understood their habits, said, in their own language,--\n\n\"My daughters have done well; the white men thank them.\"\n\nSatisfied with this testimony in their favor, the girls proceeded to deposit the body in a shell, ingeniously, and not inelegantly, fabricated of the bark of the birch; after which they lowered it into its dark and final abode. The ceremony of covering the remains, and concealing the marks of the fresh earth, by leaves and other natural and customary objects, was conducted with the same simple and silent forms. But when the labors of the kind beings who had performed these sad and friendly offices were so far completed, they hesitated, in a way to show that they knew not how much further they might proceed. It was in this stage of the rites that the scout again addressed them:--\n\n\"My young women have done enough,\" he said; \"the spirit of a pale-face has no need of food or raiment, their gifts being according to the heaven of their color. I see,\" he added, glancing an eye at David, who was preparing his book in a manner that indicated an intention to lead the way in sacred song, \"that one who better knows the Christian fashions is about to speak.\"\n\nThe females stood modestly aside, and, from having been the principal actors in the scene, they now became the meek and attentive observers of that which followed. During the time David was occupied in pouring out the pious feelings of his spirit in this manner, not a sign of surprise, nor a look of impatience, escaped them. They listened like those who knew the meaning of the strange words, and appeared as if they felt the mingled emotions of sorrow, hope, and resignation, they were intended to convey.\n\nExcited by the scene he had just witnessed, and perhaps influenced by his own secret emotions, the master of song exceeded his usual efforts. His full, rich voice was not found to suffer by a comparison with the soft tones of the girls; and his more modulated strains possessed, at least for the ears of those to whom they were peculiarly addressed, the additional power of intelligence. He ended the anthem, as he had commenced it, in the midst of a grave and solemn stillness.\n\nWhen, however, the closing cadence had fallen on the ears of his auditors, the secret, timorous glances of the eyes, and the general, and yet subdued movement of the assemblage, betrayed that something was expected from the father of the deceased. Munro seemed sensible that the time was come for him to exert what is, perhaps, the greatest effort of which human nature is capable. He bared his gray locks, and looked around the timid and quiet throng by which he was encircled with a firm and collected countenance. Then motioning with his hand for the scout to listen, he said,--\n\n\"Say to these kind and gentle females, that a heartbroken and failing man returns them his thanks. Tell them, that the Being we all worship, under different names, will be mindful of their charity; and that the time shall not be distant when we may assemble around his throne without distinction of sex, or rank, or color.\"\n\nThe scout listened to the tremulous voice in which the veteran delivered these words, and shook his head slowly when they were ended, as one who doubted their efficacy.\n\n\"To tell them this,\" he said, \"would be to tell them that the snows come not in the winter, or that the sun shines fiercest when the trees are stripped of their leaves.\"\n\nThen turning to the women, he made such a communication of the other's gratitude as he deemed most suited to the capacities of his listeners. The head of Munro had already sunk upon his chest, and he was again fast relapsing into melancholy, when the young Frenchman before named ventured to touch him lightly on the elbow. As soon as he had gained the attention of the mourning old man, he pointed towards a group of young Indians, who approached with a light but closely covered litter, and then pointed upward towards the sun.\n\n\"I understand you, sir,\" returned Munro, with a voice of forced firmness; \"I understand you. It is the will of Heaven, and I submit. Cora, my child! if the prayers of a heartbroken father could avail thee now, how blessed shouldst thou be! Come, gentlemen,\" he added, looking about him with an air of lofty composure, though the anguish that quivered in his faded countenance was far too powerful to be concealed, \"our duty here is ended; let us depart.\"\n\nHeyward gladly obeyed a summons that took them from a spot where, each instant, he felt his self-control was about to desert him. While his companions were mounting, however, he found time to press the hand of the scout, and to repeat the terms of an engagement they had made, to meet again within the posts of the British army. Then gladly throwing himself into the saddle, he spurred his charger to the side of the litter, whence low and stifled sobs alone announced the presence of Alice. In this manner, the head of Munro again dropping on his bosom, with Heyward and David following in sorrowing silence, and attended by the aide of Montcalm with his guard, all the white men, with the exception of Hawkeye, passed from before the eyes of the Delawares, and were soon buried in the vast forests of that region.\n\nBut the tie which, through their common calamity, had united the feelings of these simple dwellers in the woods with the strangers who had thus transiently visited them, was not so easily broken. Years passed away before the traditionary tale of the white maiden, and of the young warrior of the Mohicans, ceased to beguile the long nights and tedious marches, or to animate their youthful and brave with a desire for vengeance. Neither were the secondary actors in these momentous incidents forgotten. Through the medium of the scout, who served for years afterwards as a link between them and civilized life, they learned, in answer to their inquiries, that the \"Gray Head\" was speedily gathered to his fathers--borne down, as was erroneously believed, by his military misfortunes; and that the \"Open Hand\" had conveyed his surviving daughter far into the settlements of the \"pale-faces,\" where her tears had at last ceased to flow, and had been succeeded by the bright smiles which were better suited to her joyous nature.\n\nBut these were events of a time later than that which concerns our tale. Deserted by all of his color, Hawkeye returned to the spot where his own sympathies led him, with a force that no ideal bond of union could bestow. He was just in time to catch a parting look of the features of Uncas, whom the Delawares were already inclosing in his last vestments of skins. They paused to permit the longing and lingering gaze of the sturdy woodsman, and when it was ended, the body was enveloped, never to be unclosed again. Then came a procession like the other, and the whole nation was collected about the temporary grave of the chief--temporary, because it was proper that, at some future day, his bones should rest among those of his own people.\n\nThe movement, like the feeling, had been simultaneous and general. The same grave expression of grief, the same rigid silence, and the same deference to the principal mourner, were observed around the place of interment as have been already described. The body was deposited in an attitude of repose, facing the rising sun, with the implements of war and of the chase at hand, in readiness for the final journey. An opening was left in the shell, by which it was protected from the soil, for the spirit to communicate with its earthly tenement, when necessary; and the whole was concealed from the instinct, and protected from the ravages of the beasts of prey, with an ingenuity peculiar to the natives. The manual rites then ceased, and all present reverted to the more spiritual part of the ceremonies.\n\nChingachgook became once more the object of the common attention. He had not yet spoken, and something consolatory and instructive was expected from so renowned a chief on an occasion of such interest. Conscious of the wishes of the people, the stern and self-restrained warrior raised his face, which had latterly been buried in his robe, and looked about him with a steady eye. His firmly compressed and expressive lips then severed, and for the first time during the long ceremonies his voice was distinctly audible.\n\n\"Why do my brothers mourn!\" he said, regarding the dark race of dejected warriors by whom he was environed; \"why do my daughters weep! that a young man has gone to the happy hunting-grounds; that a chief has filled his time with honor! He was good; he was dutiful; he was brave. Who can deny it? The Manitou had need of such a warrior, and He has called him away. As for me, the son and the father of Uncas, I am a blazed pine, in a clearing of the pale-faces. My race has gone from the shores of the salt lake, and the hills of the Delawares. But who can say that the Serpent of his tribe has forgotten his wisdom? I am alone--\"\n\n\"No, no,\" cried Hawkeye, who had been gazing with a yearning look at the rigid features of his friend, with something like his own self-command, but whose philosophy could endure no longer; \"no, Sagamore, not alone. The gifts of our colors may be different, but God has so placed us as to journey in the same path. I have no kin, and I may also say, like you, no people. He was your son, and a redskin by nature; and it may be that your blood was nearer--but if ever I forget the lad who has so often fou't at my side in war, and slept at my side in peace, may He who made us all, whatever may be our color or our gifts, forget me! The boy has left us for a time; but, Sagamore, you are not alone.\"\n\nChingachgook grasped the hand that, in the warmth of feeling, the scout had stretched across the fresh earth, and in that attitude of friendship these two sturdy and intrepid woodsmen bowed their heads together, while scalding tears fell to their feet, watering the grave of Uncas like drops of falling rain.\n\nIn the midst of the awful stillness with which such a burst of feeling, coming, as it did, from the two most renowned warriors of that region, was received, Tamenund lifted his voice to disperse the multitude.\n\n\"It is enough,\" he said. \"Go, children of the Lenape, the anger of the Manitou is not done. Why should Tamenund stay? The pale-faces are masters of the earth, and the time of the redmen has not yet come again. My day has been too long. In the morning I saw the sons of Unamis happy and strong; and yet, before the night has come, have I lived to see the last warrior of the wise race of the Mohicans.\"\n\n[1] As each nation of the Indians had either its language or its dialect, they usually gave different names to the same places, though nearly all of their appellations were descriptive of the object. Thus, a literal translation of the name of this beautiful sheet of water, used by the tribe that dwelt on its banks would be \"The Tail of the Lake.\" Lake George, as it is vulgarly, and now indeed legally called, forms a sort of tail to Lake Champlain, when viewed on the map. Hence the name.\n\n[2] Washington: who, after uselessly admonishing the European general of the danger into which he was heedlessly running, saved the remnants of the British army, on this occasion, by his decision and courage. The reputation earned by Washington in this battle was the principal cause of his being selected to command the American armies at a later day. It is a circumstance worthy of observation, that, while all America rang with his well-merited reputation, his name does not occur in any European account of the battle; at least, the author has searched for it without success. In this manner does the mother country absorb even the fame, under that system of rule.\n\n[3] There existed for a long time a confederation among the Indian tribes which occupied the northwestern part of the colony of New York, which was at first known as the \"Five Nations.\" At a later day it admitted another tribe, when the appellation was changed to that of the \"Six Nations.\" The original confederation consisted of the Mohawks, the Oneidas, the Senecas, the Cayugas, and the Onondagas. The sixth tribe was the Tuscaroras. There are remnants of all these people still living on lands secured to them by the State; but they are daily disappearing, either by deaths or by removals to scenes more congenial to their habits. In a short time there will be no remains of these extraordinary people, in those regions in which they dwelt for centuries, but their names. The State of New York has counties named after all of them but the Mohawks and the Tuscaroras. The second river of that State is called the Mohawk.\n\n[4] In the State of Rhode Island there is a bay called Narragansett, so named after a powerful tribe of Indians, which formerly dwelt on its banks. Accident, or one of those unaccountable freaks which nature sometimes plays in the animal world, gave rise to a breed of horses which were once well known in America by the name of the Narragansetts. They were small, commonly of the color called sorrel in America, and distinguished by their habit of pacing. Horses of this race were, and are still, in much request as saddle-horses, on account of their hardiness and the ease of their movements. As they were also sure of foot, the Narragansetts were greatly sought for by females who were obliged to travel over the roots and holes in the \"new countries.\"\n\n[5] The North American warrior caused the hair to be plucked from his whole body; a small tuft, only, was left on the crown of his head in order that his enemy might avail himself of it, in wrenching off the scalp in the event of his fall. The scalp was the only admissible trophy of victory. Thus, it was deemed more important to obtain the scalp than to kill the man. Some tribes lay great stress on the honor of striking a dead body. These practices have nearly disappeared among the Indians of the Atlantic States.\n\n[6] The hunting-shirt is a picturesque smock frock, being shorter, and ornamented with fringes and tassels. The colors are intended to imitate the hues of the wood with a view to concealment. Many corps of American riflemen have been thus attired; and the dress is one of the most striking of modern times. The hunting-shirt is frequently white.\n\n[7] The rifle of the army is short; that of the hunter is always long.\n\n[8] The Mississippi. The scout alludes to a tradition which is very popular among the tribes of the Atlantic States. Evidence of their Asiatic origin is deduced from the circumstances, though great uncertainty hangs over the whole history of the Indians.\n\n[9] The scene of this tale was in the 42d degree of latitude, where the twilight is never of long continuance.\n\n[10] The reader will remember that New York was originally a colony of the Dutch.\n\n[11] The principal villages of the Indians are still called \"castles\" by the whites of New York. \"Oneida castle\" is no more than a scattered hamlet; but the name is in general use.\n\n[12] In vulgar parlance the condiments of a repast are called by the American \"a relish,\" substituting the thing for its effect. These provincial terms are frequently put in the mouths of the speakers, according to their several conditions in life. Most of them are of local use, and others quite peculiar to the particular class of men to which the character belongs. In the present instance, the scout uses the word with immediate reference to the salt, with which his own party was so fortunate as to be provided.\n\n[13] Glenn's Falls are on the Hudson, some forty or fifty miles above the head of tide, or the place where that river becomes navigable for sloops. The description of this picturesque and remarkable little cataract, as given by the scout, is sufficiently correct, though the application of the water to the uses of civilized life has materially injured its beauties. The rocky island and the two caverns are well known to every traveller, since the former sustains a pier of a bridge, which is now thrown across the river, immediately above the fall. In explanation of the taste of Hawkeye, it should be remembered that men always prize that most which is least enjoyed. Thus, in a new country, the woods and other objects, which in an old country would be maintained at great cost, are got rid of, simply with a view of \"improving,\" as it is called.\n\n[14] The meaning of Indian words is much governed by the emphasis and tones.\n\n[15] Mingo was the Delaware term for the Five Nations. Maquas was the name given them by the Dutch. The French, from their first intercourse with them, called them Iroquois.\n\n[16] It has long been a practice with the whites to conciliate the important men of the Indians, by presenting medals, which are worn in the place of their own rude ornaments. Those given by the English generally bear the impression of the reigning king, and those given by the Americans that of the president.\n\n[17] Many of the animals of the American forests resort to those spots where salt springs are found. These are called \"licks\" or \"salt licks,\" in the language of the country, from the circumstance that the quadruped is often obliged to lick the earth, in order to obtain the saline particles. These licks are great places of resort with the hunters, who waylay their game near the paths that lead to them.\n\n[18] The scene of the foregoing incidents is on the spot where the village of Ballston now stands; one of the two principal watering-places of America.\n\n[19] Some years since, the writer was shooting in the vicinity of the ruins of Fort Oswego, which stands on the shores of Lake Ontario. His game was deer, and his chase a forest that stretched with little interruption, fifty miles inland. Unexpectedly he came upon six or eight ladders lying in the woods within a short distance of each other. They were rudely made, and much decayed. Wondering what could have assembled so many of these instruments in such a place, he sought an old man who resided near for the explanation.\n\nDuring the war of 1776 Fort Oswego was held by the British. An expedition had been sent two hundred miles through the wilderness to surprise the fort. It appears that the Americans, on reaching the spot named, which was within a mile or two of the fort, first learned that they were expected, and in great danger of being cut off. They threw away their scaling-ladders, and made a rapid retreat. These ladders had lain unmolested thirty years, in the spot where they had thus been cast.\n\n[20] Baron Dieskau, a German, in the service of France. A few years previous to the period of the tale, this officer was defeated by Sir William Johnson of Johnstown, New York, on the shores of Lake George. See Appendix, Note H.\n\n[21] Evidently the late De Witt Clinton, who died governor of New York in 1828.\n\n[22] The powers of the American mocking-bird are generally known. But the true mocking-bird is not found so far north as the State of New York, where it has, however, two substitutes of inferior excellence; the catbird, so often named by the scout, and the bird vulgarly called ground-thresher. Either of these two last birds is superior to the nightingale, or the lark, though, in general, the American birds are less musical than those of Europe.\n\n[23] The beauties of Lake George are well known to every American tourist. In the height of the mountains which surround it, and in artificial accessories, it is inferior to the finest of the Swiss and Italian lakes, while in outline and purity of water it is fully their equal; and in the number and disposition of its isles and islets much superior to them all together. There are said to be some hundreds of islands in a sheet of water less than thirty miles long. The narrows which connect what may be called, in truth, two lakes, are crowded with islands to such a degree as to leave passages between them frequently of only a few feet in width. The lake itself varies in breadth from one to three miles.\n\nThe State of New York is remarkable for the number and beauty of its lakes. One of its frontiers lies on the vast sheet of Ontario, while Champlain stretches nearly a hundred miles along another. Oneida, Cayuga, Canandaigua, Seneca, and George, are all lakes of thirty miles in length, while those of a size smaller are without number. On most of these lakes there are now beautiful villages, and on many of them steamboats.\n\n[24] These harangues of the beasts are frequent among the Indians. They often address their victims in this way, reproaching them for cowardice, or commending their resolution, as they may happen to exhibit fortitude, or the reverse in suffering.\n\n[25] A dish composed of cracked corn and beans. It is much used also by the whites. By corn is meant maize.\n\n[26] William Penn was termed Minquon by the Delawares, and, as he never used violence or injustice in his dealings with them, his reputation for probity passed into a proverb. The American is justly proud of the origin of his nation, which is perhaps unequalled in the history of the world; but the Pennsylvanian and Jerseyman have more reason to value themselves in their ancestors than the natives of any other State, since no wrong was done the original owners of the soil.\n\n[27] Turtle.\n\n[28] A tree which has been partially or entirely stripped of its bark is said, in the language of the country, to be \"blazed.\" The term is strictly English; for a horse is said to be blazed when it has a white mark.\n\n[29] The American forest admits of the passage of horse, there being little underbush, and few tangled brakes. The plan of Hawkeye is the one which has always proved the most successful in the battles between the whites and the Indians. Wayne, in his celebrated campaign on the Miami, received the fire of his enemies in line; and then causing his dragoons to wheel round his flanks, the Indians were driven from their covers before they had time to load. One of the most conspicuous of the chiefs who fought in the battle of Miami assured the writer, that the redmen could not fight the warriors with \"long knives and leather-stockings\"; meaning the dragoons with their sabres and boots.\nEnd of Project Gutenberg's The Last of the Mohicans, by James Fenimore Cooper";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-27681"]="# THE FLIGHT ACROSS THE LAKE 214\n\n적대하는 양쪽 군대가 서로 맞붙기까지 황야의 노고와 위험을 먼저 견뎌야 한다는 것은 북아메리카 식민지 전쟁만의 독특한 특징이었다. 적대하는 프랑스령과 영국령의 영토는 넓고도 도무지 뚫을 수 없을 듯한 삼림의 경계로 갈라져 있었다. 강인한 개척민과 그 곁에서 싸우는 훈련된 유럽인은, 보다 무인다운 충돌에서 용기를 떨칠 기회를 찾고자, 급류와 싸우거나 험준한 산길을 넘느라 흔히 몇 달을 허비하곤 했다. 그러나 숙련된 원주민 전사들의 인내와 극기를 본받아, 그들은 온갖 난관을 이겨 내는 법을 익혔다. 그리하여 마침내, 복수의 갈증을 채우려 피로써 맹세한 자들이나, 멀리 유럽의 군주들이 펴는 냉혹하고 이기적인 정책을 떠받치려는 자들의 침입을 면할 만큼, 그토록 어두운 숲의 후미진 곳도, 그토록 사랑스러운 은밀한 장소도 없었던 듯하다.\n\n# THE TERMAGANT 250\n\n저 시대 미개한 전쟁의 잔혹함과 흉포함을 〈허드슨〉강의 상류와 인근 호수들 사이에 놓인 고장만큼 생생하게 그려 낼 수 있는 지역은, 광막하게 펼쳐진 중간 변경지대 어디에도 아마 없을 것이다.\n\n그곳에서 자연이 교전자들의 행군에 제공한 편의는 너무도 분명하여 그냥 지나칠 수 없는 것이었다. 길게 뻗은 〈섐플레인〉 호수는 〈캐나다〉의 변경에서부터 이웃한 〈뉴욕〉 영토 깊숙한 곳까지 이어져, 프랑스군이 적을 치기 위해 정복해야 했던 거리의 절반을 가로지르는 천연의 통로를 이루었다. 그 남단 가까이에서, 이 호수는 또 다른 호수의 물을 받아들였는데, 그 물이 어찌나 맑았던지 예수회 선교사들이 세례의 상징적 정화 의식을 행할 곳으로 따로 골라, 그곳에 〈성체호(湖)〉라는 이름을 붙였을 정도였다. 그보다 덜 열성적이던 영국인들은, 통치 중인 자국 군주, 곧 〈하노버〉 왕가의 둘째 왕의 이름을 그 티 없는 샘에 부여하는 것으로 충분한 영예를 주었다고 여겼다. 이 두 무리는 한데 어우러져, 그 숲의 풍경을 지녔던 무지한 소유자들에게서 본래의 명칭 〈호리컨〉을 영원히 이어 갈 토착의 권리를 빼앗았다.\n\n이 \"거룩한 호수\"는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 섬들 사이를 굽이쳐, 산들 속에 안긴 채, 남쪽으로 다시 십여 리그를 더 뻗어 나갔다. 그곳에서 물이 더 나아가는 것을 가로막은 높은 평원과 함께, 그만큼의 마일에 걸친 육로 운반로가 시작되어, 모험가를 〈허드슨〉강 기슭으로 인도했다. 바로 그 지점에서, 당시 그 고장 말로 급류 혹은 여울이라 불리던 흔한 장애물을 지나면, 강은 조수가 닿는 데까지 배가 다닐 수 있게 되었다.\n\n프랑스군의 쉴 줄 모르는 진취성이 적을 괴롭히려는 대담한 계획을 좇아 멀고도 험난한 〈앨러게니〉의 협곡까지 넘보았던 만큼, 그들의 정평 난 예리함이 우리가 방금 묘사한 이 지역의 천연의 이점을 놓쳤을 리 없다는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다. 이곳은 단연코, 식민지의 지배권을 두고 벌어진 대부분의 전투가 다투어진 피의 무대가 되었다. 길의 요충을 제압하는 여러 지점마다 요새가 세워졌고, 승리가 어느 쪽 깃발에 내려앉느냐에 따라 빼앗기고 되찾기를, 허물어지고 다시 쌓이기를 거듭했다. 농부가 위험한 길목에서 물러나 더 오래된 정착지의 안전한 경계 안으로 움츠러드는 동안, 흔히 본국의 왕권을 좌우하곤 했던 군대보다 더 큰 군대가 이 숲속에 파묻히는 광경이 보였으니, 그곳에서 그들은 좀처럼 돌아오지 못했고, 돌아온대야 근심으로 초췌해지거나 패배에 낙담한 해골 같은 무리뿐이었다. 비록 이 숙명의 땅에 평화의 기예는 알려지지 않았으나, 그 숲은 사람들로 들끓었고, 그 그늘과 골짜기는 군악 소리로 울렸으며, 산의 메아리는 혈기 왕성한 무수한 젊은이가 의기 충천한 한낮에 그곳을 서둘러 지나며 터뜨린 웃음을 되돌려 보내거나 거리낌 없는 외침을 되풀이했으니, 그들은 곧 망각의 기나긴 밤 속에 잠들고자 가는 길이었다.\n\n영국과 프랑스가 어느 쪽도 끝내 지킬 운명이 아니었던 한 땅의 소유권을 두고 마지막으로 벌인 전쟁, 그 셋째 해에, 우리가 이제 이야기하려는 사건들이 일어난 곳은 바로 이 다툼과 유혈의 무대였다.\n\n해외에 나간 군 지휘관들의 무능함과 본국 회의에서의 치명적인 활력 부족이, 한때 옛 전사들과 정치가들의 재능과 진취성으로 〈영국〉이 올라섰던 자랑스러운 높이에서 그 위상을 끌어내렸다. 더는 적들에게 두려움을 주지 못하게 되자, 그 신하들은 자존감의 확신을 빠르게 잃어 가고 있었다. 이 굴욕적인 추락 속에서, 식민지 사람들은 본국의 무능에 죄가 없고 그 실책의 앞잡이가 되기에는 너무 미천했음에도, 그저 자연히 그 처지를 함께 나누는 자들일 뿐이었다.\n\n그들은 얼마 전, 어머니로 우러르며 무적이라 맹목적으로 믿어 온 그 나라에서 보낸 정예 군대가, 곧 훈련된 전사들 가운데서 그 보기 드문 군사적 자질로 선발된 한 지휘관이 이끌던 군대가, 한 줌의 프랑스군과 인디언에게 치욕스럽게 궤멸당하고, 어느 〈버지니아〉 청년의 침착함과 기개 덕에 가까스로 전멸을 면하는 것을 목격했다. 그 청년의 무르익은 명성은 그 후 도덕적 진리의 꾸준한 영향력을 띠고 기독교 세계의 가장 먼 변경에까지 퍼져 나갔다. 이 뜻밖의 재앙으로 광대한 변경이 벌거벗겨졌고, 더 실질적인 재난에 앞서 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 공상적이고 허황한 위험들이 먼저 닥쳤다. 겁에 질린 식민지 사람들은, 서쪽의 끝없는 숲에서 불어오는 변덕스러운 바람 한 줄기마다 미개인들의 비명이 섞여 있다고 믿었다. 무자비한 적들의 끔찍한 성격은 전쟁이 본래 지닌 공포를 헤아릴 수 없이 키웠다. 최근에 벌어진 수많은 학살이 아직도 그들의 기억 속에 생생했고, 숲의 원주민들이 주된 야만의 배역을 맡은 어느 한밤중 살인의 무시무시한 이야기를, 게걸스레 빨아들이지 않을 만큼 무딘 귀는 이 영토 안에 하나도 없었다. 잘 속고 흥분한 여행자가 황야의 위험천만한 일들을 늘어놓을 때면, 겁 많은 자들의 피가 공포로 얼어붙었고, 어머니들은 가장 큰 도회의 안전 속에 잠든 아이들에게조차 불안한 눈길을 던졌다. 요컨대, 공포가 부풀려 놓는 영향력이 이성의 계산을 무로 돌리기 시작했고, 사내다움을 기억해야 마땅할 자들을 가장 비천한 격정의 노예로 만들었다. 가장 자신만만하고 가장 굳센 마음들조차 이 싸움의 결말이 의심스러워지고 있다고 생각하기 시작했고, 〈영국〉 왕실이 아메리카에 가진 모든 영토가 기독교도인 적들에게 정복되거나 무자비한 동맹군의 침략에 황폐해지는 것을 미리 내다본다고 여기는 비굴한 무리가 시시각각 그 수를 늘려 갔다.\n\n그러므로, 〈몽칼름〉이 \"나무의 잎사귀처럼 수많은\" 군대를 이끌고 〈섐플레인〉을 거슬러 올라가는 것이 목격되었다는 소식이, 〈허드슨〉강과 호수들 사이 육로 운반로의 남단을 지키던 요새에 전해졌을 때, 그 진위는, 자신의 일격이 닿을 거리에 적이 들어왔음을 알게 된 전사가 느껴야 할 준엄한 기쁨보다는, 오히려 공포에서 비롯된 비겁한 망설임 속에 받아들여졌다. 이 소식은 한여름 어느 날 해 질 무렵, 한 인디언 전령이 가져왔는데, 그는 또한 \"거룩한 호수\" 기슭에 자리한 진지의 지휘관 〈먼로〉가 보낸, 신속하고 강력한 증원을 바라는 긴박한 요청도 지니고 있었다. 이 두 진지 사이의 거리가 다섯 리그가 채 못 된다는 것은 이미 언급한 바 있다. 본래 그 둘을 잇는 통로를 이루던 거친 오솔길은 마차가 지날 수 있도록 넓혀져 있었으니, 숲의 아들이 두 시간 만에 주파한 거리는, 필요한 짐을 거느린 한 부대 병력이라도 여름 해가 뜨고 지는 사이에 어렵잖게 갈 수 있었다. 〈영국〉 왕실의 충직한 신하들은 이 숲의 요새 가운데 하나에 〈윌리엄 헨리〉라는 이름을, 다른 하나에 〈에드워드〉 요새라는 이름을 붙여, 저마다 통치 가문이 아끼는 왕자의 이름을 땄다. 방금 말한 노련한 스코틀랜드인은 첫째 요새를 정규군 한 연대와 약간의 식민지 민병으로 지키고 있었으니, 〈몽칼름〉이 그의 흙벽 발치로 이끌고 오는 가공할 병력에 맞서기에는 사실 턱없이 적은 군세였다. 그러나 후자에는, 북부 영토의 왕의 군대를 지휘하는 〈웨브〉 장군이 오천이 넘는 병력과 함께 머물고 있었다. 휘하의 여러 분견대를 한데 합쳤다면, 이 장교는 거의 그 갑절에 달하는 전투원을 거느리고, 증원 부대에서 그토록 멀리, 병력에서 거의 우위에 있지도 못한 군대로 나선 진취적인 프랑스인에게 맞설 수도 있었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 추락한 운세의 영향 아래, 장교도 병사도, 〈뒤케스느〉 요새에서 거둔 프랑스군의 성공한 본보기를 본받아 적의 행군을 저지하고 진격하는 그들에게 일격을 가하기보다는, 자신들의 진지 안에서 가공할 적이 다가오기를 기다리는 쪽에 더 마음이 기운 듯했다.\n\n소식이 안긴 첫 놀라움이 조금 가라앉자, 〈허드슨〉강 기슭을 따라 길게 뻗어 요새 본체에 외곽 방어선의 사슬을 이루던 보루 진지 안에 한 가지 소문이 퍼졌으니, 정예 분견대 천오백 명이 새벽과 함께 〈윌리엄 헨리〉로, 곧 육로 운반로 북단의 진지로 떠난다는 것이었다. 처음엔 소문에 지나지 않던 것이, 총사령관의 막사에서 이 임무를 위해 선발된 여러 부대로 속히 출발 준비를 하라는 명령이 내려오면서, 이내 확실한 사실이 되었다. 이제 〈웨브〉의 의도에 관한 의심은 모두 사라졌고, 한두 시간에 걸쳐 분주한 발걸음과 근심 어린 얼굴들이 뒤따랐다. 군사에 풋내기인 자는 이 지점에서 저 지점으로 뛰어다니며, 격렬하고 다소 어수선한 열의가 지나친 나머지 도리어 제 준비를 더디게 했다. 반면에 보다 노련한 고참은 서두르는 기색이라곤 일절 경멸하는 듯한 차분함으로 채비를 갖췄으나, 그 침착한 표정과 근심 어린 눈은 아직 겪어 보지 못한, 두려운 황야의 전쟁에 직업적으로 그다지 강한 흥미를 느끼지 못한다는 것을 충분히 드러냈다. 마침내 해가 먼 서쪽 언덕 너머로 영광의 홍수 속에 저물고, 어둠이 이 외딴곳에 장막을 드리우자 준비의 소리는 잦아들었다. 마지막 불빛이 어느 장교의 통나무 오두막에서 끝내 사라졌고, 나무들은 둔덕과 잔물결 이는 시냇물 위로 한층 짙은 그림자를 드리웠으며, 이내 진지에는 그것을 에워싼 광막한 숲을 지배하는 것만큼이나 깊은 정적이 깔렸다.\n\n전날 밤의 명령에 따라, 군대의 깊은 잠은 경고를 알리는 북소리의 울림에 깨졌으니, 그 우르릉대는 메아리가 축축한 아침 공기를 타고 숲의 모든 트인 길마다에서 새어 나오는 것이 들렸다. 바로 그때 동이 트며, 부드럽고 구름 한 점 없는 동쪽 하늘의 밝아 오는 빛 위로 인근 키 큰 소나무 몇 그루의 텁수룩한 윤곽이 그려지기 시작했다. 순식간에 진지 전체가 움직였다. 가장 미천한 병사조차 제 잠자리에서 일어나, 전우들의 출발을 지켜보고 이 시각의 흥분과 사건에 한몫 끼려 했다. 선발된 부대의 단출한 정렬은 곧 마무리되었다. 왕의 정규 훈련된 용병들이 거만하게 대열의 오른편으로 행진하는 동안, 덜 잘난 체하는 식민지 민병들은 오랜 단련으로 몸에 밴 순순함으로 그 왼편의 보다 겸손한 자리를 차지했다. 척후가 떠났고, 강력한 호위대가 짐을 실은 덜컹대는 수레들을 앞뒤로 호위했으며, 아침의 잿빛 빛이 햇살로 부드러워지기도 전에, 전투원의 본대가 종대로 선회하여, 드높은 군인다운 위풍을 떨치며 진지를 떠났으니, 그 위풍은 이제 막 첫 출진을 앞둔 무수한 풋내기의 잠들어 있던 불안을 잠재우기에 족했다. 감탄하는 전우들의 시야 안에 있는 동안에는, 똑같이 당당한 정면과 정연한 대열이 유지되었으나, 이윽고 그들의 피리 소리가 멀어지며 희미해지자, 마침내 숲은 그 품으로 천천히 들어선 살아 있는 무리를 삼켜 버리는 듯했다.\n\n물러가는 보이지 않는 종대의 가장 깊은 소리마저 더는 산들바람에 실려 듣는 이들에게 닿지 않게 되었고, 가장 늦은 낙오자마저 이미 그 뒤를 좇아 사라졌다. 그러나 평소와 달리 크고 안락하게 지은 한 통나무 오두막 앞에는, 또 다른 출발의 기색이 아직 남아 있었으니, 그 앞에서는 〈영국〉 장군의 신변을 지키는 것으로 알려진 보초들이 순찰을 돌고 있었다. 이곳에는 예닐곱 마리의 말이 모여 있었는데, 그 차림새로 보아 적어도 두 마리는, 이 고장 황야 깊숙한 데서는 좀처럼 만나기 힘든 신분의 여인들을 태우도록 마련된 것임을 알 수 있었다. 셋째 마리는 참모 장교의 장구와 무장을 갖추고 있었고, 나머지는 그 수수한 안장과 그것들이 짊어진 여행용 행낭으로 보아, 명백히 그만큼의 하인들을 태우도록 채비된 것이었으니, 그들은 이미 섬기는 이들의 분부를 기다리고 있는 듯했다. 이 예사롭지 않은 광경에서 조심스레 떨어진 거리에는, 호기심 어린 한가한 구경꾼들이 여러 무리로 모여 있었다. 더러는 혈기 넘치는 군마의 혈통과 골격에 감탄했고, 더러는 천박한 호기심의 멍한 경이로 그 채비를 바라보았다. 그러나 그 가운데 한 사내는, 그 표정과 행동으로 보아 후자에 속하는 구경꾼들과는 뚜렷이 구별되었으니, 한가하지도, 그다지 무지해 보이지도 않았다.\n\n이 사람의 몸은 어느 한구석 특별히 기형이랄 데는 없으면서도, 더없이 볼품이 없었다. 그는 다른 이들이 지닌 뼈와 관절을 다 갖추었으되, 그 비례라곤 조금도 갖추지 못했다. 똑바로 서면 그 키가 동료들을 능가했고, 앉으면 보통 사람의 한계 안으로 줄어든 듯 보였다. 그 사지에 깃든 똑같은 어긋남이 온몸에 두루 존재하는 듯했다. 머리는 컸고, 어깨는 좁았으며, 팔은 길어 축 늘어졌으나, 손은 작다 못해 섬세하달 정도였다. 다리와 허벅지는 여위어 거의 수척할 지경이었으나 유달리 길었고, 무릎은 어마어마하다 여겨졌을 법했으나, 인간의 여러 부류가 뒤섞인 이 거짓된 상부 구조가 그토록 함부로 세워진 더 널찍한 토대에 그만 압도당하고 말았다. 어울리지 않고 분별없는 이 사내의 옷차림은 그의 어색함을 한층 두드러지게 할 뿐이었다. 짧고 넓은 자락에 낮은 깃을 단 하늘색 상의는, 길고 가는 목과 그보다 더 길고 가는 다리를, 심술궂은 자들의 더없이 짓궂은 혹평에 고스란히 드러냈다. 그 아래옷은 노란색 난징목면으로, 몸에 꼭 맞게 지어, 울퉁불퉁한 무릎께를 써서 꽤나 더러워진 흰 리본의 큰 매듭으로 묶었다. 얼룩덜룩한 무명 양말과 신발 — 그중 한 짝에는 도금한 박차가 달려 있었다 — 이 이 형상의 하반신 차림을 완성했으니, 그 어떤 곡선이나 각도도 가려지기는커녕, 도리어 임자의 허영심인지 단순함인지로 인해 일부러 드러나 있었다. 빛바랜 은빛 레이스로 잔뜩 장식한 양각 비단 조끼의, 더러워진 커다란 주머니 덮개 아래로는 한 도구가 삐죽 나와 있었는데, 이런 무인다운 무리 속에서 보이는 까닭에, 자칫 어떤 고약하고 알 수 없는 전쟁 도구로 오인되기 십상이었다. 작기는 했어도, 이 흔치 않은 기물은 진지 안 유럽인 대다수의 호기심을 자아냈으나, 몇몇 식민지 민병은 그것을 두려움 없이 다루는 것은 물론, 더없이 친숙하게 다루는 모습도 보였다. 지난 삼십 년 사이 성직자들이 쓰던 것 같은, 크고 점잖은 삼각모가 그 모든 것 위에 얹혀, 그 인위적인 도움이 있어야 어떤 높고 비범한 임무의 무게를 떠받칠 수 있는 듯한, 사람 좋고 다소 멍한 얼굴에 위엄을 더해 주었다.\n\n여느 무리가 〈웨브〉의 막사를 어려워하여 멀찍이 떨어져 선 동안, 우리가 묘사한 형상은 하인들 한가운데를 활보하며, 말들이 우연히 제 판단에 거슬리거나 흡족하거나 함에 따라 그 우열에 관한 비난이나 칭찬을 거리낌 없이 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"이 짐승은 말이오, 친구, 내 생각엔 토종이 아니라 외국 땅에서, 어쩌면 저 푸른 바다 건너 그 작은 섬 자체에서 온 것 같소만?\" 그가 말했으니, 그 목소리는 부드럽고 감미로운 음색으로, 그 몸이 보기 드문 비례로 유별난 것에 못지않게 유별났다. \"내가 이런 것들을 말한다고 허풍쟁이는 아니오. 내 두 항구에 다 내려가 보았으니까. 〈템스〉강 어귀에 자리해 옛 〈영국〉의 수도를 따 이름 지은 곳과, '뉴'라는 말을 덧붙여 '헤이븐'이라 불리는 곳 말이오. 또 거기서 외돛배와 쌍돛배들이, 마치 방주로 모여드는 무리처럼, 네발짐승을 사고팔러 〈자메이카〉섬으로 떠나며 제 떼를 끌어모으는 것도 보았소. 허나 그전엔 결코, 이 짐승처럼 성경 속 참된 군마를 그대로 입증하는 짐승을 본 적이 없소. '그것이 골짜기에서 발굽을 구르며 제 힘을 기뻐하고, 나아가 무장한 자들을 맞이하는도다. 나팔 소리 가운데서 히히 울며, 멀리서 싸움 냄새를 맡고, 장수들의 우렛소리와 함성을 듣는도다.' 〈이스라엘〉 말의 혈통이 우리 시대에까지 내려온 듯하지 않소, 친구?\"\n\n이 유별난 호소에 아무 대답도 받지 못하자 — 사실 그것은 충만하고 낭랑한 음색의 힘으로 전해진 만큼 어떤 식으로든 주의를 받을 만했다 — 이렇게 성서의 언어를 읊어 댄 자는, 자신이 무심코 말을 건넨 말없는 형상 쪽으로 돌아서, 제 시선에 마주친 대상에서 새롭고 한층 강력한 감탄의 소재를 발견했다. 그의 눈길은, 전날 저녁 진지에 반갑잖은 소식을 전한 \"인디언 전령\"의 고요하고 곧고 뻣뻣한 형상에 가닿았다. 완전한 정지의 상태에 있고, 특유의 금욕으로 주위의 흥분과 소란을 무시하는 듯했으나, 미개인의 그 고요함에는 침울한 흉포함이 뒤섞여 있어, 지금 감추지 않은 경탄으로 그를 훑어보는 눈보다 훨씬 더 노련한 눈이라도 사로잡았음 직했다. 이 원주민은 제 부족의 토마호크와 칼을 둘 다 지니고 있었으나, 그 모습이 온전히 전사의 것만은 아니었다. 도리어 그의 몸에는 무언가 방치된 기색이 감돌았으니, 마치 크고도 최근의 분투에서 비롯되었으되 아직 손볼 겨를을 찾지 못한 것 같았다. 전투용 칠의 색채는 그 흉포한 얼굴에 어둡게 뒤섞여 엉클어져, 그 거무스름한 이목구비를, 차라리 기교로써 이런 효과를 노렸을 때보다도 한층 더 야만스럽고 혐오스럽게 만들었으니, 마치 잔뜩 찌푸린 구름 사이로 솟은 것이 그 본래의 야성 그대로 드러나 보이는 듯했다. 한순간, 그의 살피면서도 경계하는 눈길이 상대의 의아해하는 눈빛과 마주쳤다가, 이내 방향을 돌려, 반쯤은 교활함에서, 반쯤은 경멸에서, 마치 먼 허공을 꿰뚫는 듯 한 곳에 고정되었다.\n\n이토록 유별난 두 사내 사이의 짧고 말 없는 이 교감이, 백인에게서 어떤 뜻밖의 말을 끌어냈을지는, 만약 그의 활발한 호기심이 다시 다른 대상으로 쏠리지 않았더라면, 알 도리가 없다. 하인들 사이의 전반적인 움직임과 부드러운 목소리들의 나직한 소리가, 그들이 있어야만 비로소 이 일행이 출발할 수 있는 이들의 다가옴을 알렸다. 군마를 소박하게 흠모하던 자는 즉각, 근처에서 무심코 진지의 시든 풀을 뜯고 있던, 키 작고 깡마른 데다 꼬리가 회초리 같은 암말 쪽으로 물러났다. 그곳에서 그는, 안장이랍시고 둘러친 것을 가린 담요에 한쪽 팔꿈치를 괴고는, 출발을 지켜보는 구경꾼이 되었으니, 그동안 망아지 한 마리가 같은 짐승의 반대편에서 조용히 아침 식사를 하고 있었다.\n\n장교 복장을 한 젊은이가 두 여인을 그들의 말로 인도했는데, 그 옷차림으로 보아 그들이 숲속 여정의 고초를 겪을 채비를 한 것이 분명했다. 둘 다 젊었으나, 그중 가장 앳되어 보이는 한 여인은, 천진하게도 아침 공기가 비버 모자에서 낮게 드리운 초록 베일을 옆으로 날리게 두는 동안, 눈부신 살결과 고운 금빛 머리칼, 밝은 푸른 눈을 언뜻언뜻 내비쳤다. 서쪽 하늘 소나무 위에 아직 머물러 있던 노을빛도 그녀의 뺨에 핀 홍조보다 더 밝거나 곱지 않았고, 밝아 오는 날도, 젊은이가 그녀를 안장에 오르도록 거들 때 그녀가 그에게 보낸 생기 넘치는 미소보다 더 상쾌하지 않았다. 젊은 장교의 보살핌을 똑같이 나누어 받는 듯 보이던 다른 여인은, 네댓 살은 더 든 경험에 더 어울릴 법한 조심성으로, 병사들의 시선에서 제 아리따움을 감추었다. 그러나 그녀의 몸은, 똑같이 빼어난 비례로 빚어져, 입은 여행복으로도 그 우아함을 조금도 잃지 않았으되, 동행보다는 다소 더 풍만하고 무르익었음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n이 여인들이 자리에 앉기가 무섭게, 그들의 시중을 드는 이가 군마의 안장 위로 가뿐히 뛰어올랐고, 그러자 세 사람 모두 〈웨브〉에게 절을 했다. 그는 예의를 갖추어 제 오두막 문턱에서 그들의 작별을 기다리고 있었다. 그들은 말 머리를 돌려, 수행원들을 거느린 채 천천히 느린 걸음으로 진지의 북쪽 입구를 향해 나아갔다. 그 짧은 거리를 가로지르는 동안 그들 사이에서는 한마디도 들리지 않았으나, 인디언 전령이 뜻밖에 더 젊은 여인 곁을 미끄러지듯 스쳐 지나 그녀 앞 군사 도로를 따라 앞장서자, 그녀의 입에서 가벼운 탄성이 새어 나왔다. 이 인디언의 돌연하고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 움직임은 다른 여인에게서는 아무 소리도 자아내지 못했으나, 그 놀라움 속에 그녀의 베일도 주름을 펼치며 열렸으니, 그녀의 검은 눈이 미개인의 유연한 동작을 좇는 동안, 형언할 수 없는 연민과 감탄과 공포의 표정이 드러났다. 이 여인의 머릿단은 갈까마귀의 깃털처럼 윤기 흐르는 검은빛이었다. 그녀의 살결은 갈색이라기보다, 차라리 금방이라도 제 한계를 터뜨릴 듯한 진한 피의 빛깔로 가득 찬 듯 보였다. 그러면서도 더없이 단정하고 기품 있으며 빼어나게 아름다운 그 얼굴에는, 거칠음도, 음영의 부족함도 없었다. 그녀는 마치 한순간의 제 망각을 가엾이 여기는 듯 미소 지었는데, 그 동작으로 가장 순결한 상아조차 무색하게 할 가지런한 잇바디를 드러냈다. 그러고는 베일을 다시 매만져 얼굴을 숙이고, 마치 주위의 광경에서 마음이 멀리 떠난 사람처럼 말없이 말을 몰았다.\n\n\"솔라, 솔라, 워, 하, 호, 솔라!\"\n\n셰익스피어\n우리가 독자에게 그토록 황급히 소개한 사랑스러운 두 사람 가운데 하나가 이렇듯 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안, 다른 하나는 방금 그 외침을 자아낸 놀라움에서 빠르게 헤어나, 제 나약함을 스스로 비웃으며 곁에서 말을 탄 청년에게 물었다.\n\n\"이런 유령들이 숲에서 자주 나타나나요, 〈헤이워드〉? 아니면 이 광경이 우리를 위해 특별히 마련된 볼거리인가요? 만일 후자라면 고마운 마음에 입을 다물어야겠지만, 전자라면 〈코라〉와 나 둘 다, 저 가공할 〈몽칼름〉과 맞서기도 전에, 우리가 자랑으로 삼는 그 물려받은 용기의 밑천을 잔뜩 끌어다 써야겠는걸요.\"\n\n\"저기 저 인디언은 군의 '전령'이오. 제 부족의 방식대로 보자면 그는 영웅으로 여겨질 만하지.\" 장교가 대답했다. \"그는 거의 알려지지 않은 길을 통해 우리를 호수까지 안내하겠다고 자청했소. 종대의 굼뜬 움직임을 따르는 것보다 더 빨리, 따라서 더 기분 좋게 갈 수 있도록 말이오.\"\n\n\"저 사람이 마음에 들지 않아요.\" 여인이 몸을 떨며 말했으니, 얼마쯤은 꾸민 것이었으나 그보다 더 많이는 진짜 두려움에서였다. \"당신은 저 사람을 아시지요, 〈덩컨〉? 그러지 않고서야 이토록 선뜻 그의 손에 자신을 맡기지는 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"차라리, 〈앨리스〉, 당신을 맡기지는 않으리라고 말해 주오. 나는 정말로 그를 잘 알고 있소. 그러지 않았다면 그를 신임하지 않았을 테고, 더구나 이런 때에는 더더욱 그랬을 거요. 그는 〈캐나다〉 사람이라고도 하오. 그러면서도 우리의 벗인 〈모호크〉족과 함께 복무했지. 당신도 알다시피 그들은 여섯 동맹 부족 가운데 하나요. 내가 듣기로 그는 어떤 기이한 사건을 계기로 우리 사이에 들어왔는데, 그 일에는 당신 부친도 관여하셨고, 그 미개인은 엄혹한 처분을 받았다 하오. 허나 그 시시한 이야기는 잊어버리겠소. 그가 지금 우리의 벗이라는 사실로 족하니까.\"\n\n\"그가 내 아버지의 적이었다면, 더욱더 마음에 들지 않아요!\" 이제 정말로 불안해진 처녀가 외쳤다. \"〈헤이워드〉 소령, 그에게 말을 좀 걸어 주시겠어요? 그의 목소리를 들어 보고 싶으니까요. 어리석은 일일지 모르나, 내가 사람 목소리의 음색을 믿는다고 늘 말하는 것을 당신도 여러 번 들으셨잖아요!\"\n\n\"소용없을 거요. 십중팔구 외마디 소리로나 답할 테니까. 그는 영어를 알아들으면서도, 제 부족 대다수가 그러하듯 모르는 체하오. 더구나 전쟁이 그 위엄을 최대한 발휘하기를 요구하는 지금은, 영어로 말하는 데 응하려 들지 않을 거요. 한데 그가 멈추는군. 우리가 가야 할 그 은밀한 오솔길이 분명 가까운가 보오.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉 소령의 짐작은 맞았다. 그 인디언이 서서 군용 도로 가장자리를 두른 덤불 속을 가리키고 있는 곳에 다다르니, 좁고 분간하기 어려운 오솔길이, 다소 불편을 감수하면 한 번에 한 사람씩은 지날 만한 길이 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n\"자, 그러면 우리 길은 이쪽으로 나 있소.\" 청년이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"불신하는 기색을 조금도 내비치지 마시오. 그러지 않으면 그대들이 두려워하는 듯한 그 위험을 도리어 불러들일지 모르니.\"\n\n\"〈코라〉, 너는 어떻게 생각하니?\" 내키지 않아 하는 가녀린 처녀가 물었다. \"군대와 함께 가면, 그들이 곁에 있는 것이 거추장스럽기야 하겠지만, 그래도 우리 안전에 대해 더 큰 확신을 느낄 수 있지 않을까?\"\n\n\"미개인들의 관행에 익숙지 않아서 그렇소, 〈앨리스〉. 그대는 진짜 위험이 도사린 곳을 잘못 짚고 있소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"만일 적이 그 육로 운반로까지 닿았다면 — 우리 척후들이 나가 있으니 결코 있음 직한 일은 아니지만 — 그들은 분명 머릿가죽이 가장 많이 모인 종대 언저리를 어슬렁거리고 있을 거요. 그 분견대의 행로는 알려져 있으나, 우리 길은 한 시간 안에 정해진 것이니 아직 비밀로 남아 있을 게 틀림없소.\"\n\n\"저 사람의 태도가 우리 태도와 다르다고, 또 그 피부가 검다고 해서 그를 불신해야 할까요?\" 〈코라〉가 차갑게 물었다.\n\n〈앨리스〉는 더는 머뭇거리지 않았다. 도리어 자기 〈내러갠셋〉에게 채찍을 매섭게 한 차례 가하고는, 맨 먼저 덤불의 가는 가지들을 옆으로 헤치며 어둡고 얽힌 오솔길을 따라 그 전령의 뒤를 좇았다. 청년은 마지막으로 말한 처녀를 노골적인 감탄으로 바라보았고, 더 희되 결코 더 아름답지는 않은 그 동행이 아무도 거들지 않은 채 나아가도록 내버려 두면서까지, 〈코라〉라 불린 처녀가 지날 길은 손수 부지런히 터 주었다. 하인들은 미리 지시를 받은 모양이었다. 덤불 속으로 파고드는 대신 종대의 행로를 따랐으니, 〈헤이워드〉의 말로는, 혹여 〈캐나다〉의 미개인들이 제 군대보다 그토록 멀리 앞서 숨어 있을 경우에 대비해 일행의 자취를 줄이려는, 안내인의 지혜에서 나온 방책이라 했다. 길이 워낙 뒤얽혀 있어 여러 분 동안은 더 이상 대화를 나눌 수 없었다. 그러고 나서 그들은 큰길을 따라 자란 넓은 잡목 띠에서 빠져나와, 높되 어두운 숲의 아치 아래로 들어섰다. 이곳에서는 행보가 한결 덜 막혔고, 안내인은 여인들이 제 말을 능히 다룬다는 것을 알아채자마자, 빠른 걸음과 보통 걸음 사이의 속도로 나아갔으니, 그들이 탄 발 디딤이 든든하고 유별난 짐승들이 빠르되 편안한 측대보로 가도록 하는 속도였다. 청년이 검은 눈의 〈코라〉에게 말을 걸려고 몸을 돌리는 순간, 뒤편 부서진 길의 뿌리 위를 덜그럭거리며 달려오는 말발굽 소리가 멀리서 들려와 그는 군마를 멈춰 세웠다. 그의 동행들도 같은 순간 고삐를 당기니, 일행 모두가 멈춰 서서 이 뜻밖의 방해가 어찌 된 영문인지 알아보려 했다.\n\n이내 망아지 한 마리가 노루처럼 곧게 뻗은 소나무 줄기들 사이를 미끄러지듯 달려오는 것이 보였다. 또 한순간 뒤에는, 앞 장에서 묘사한 볼품없는 사내의 모습이 시야에 들어왔으니, 그는 비쩍 마른 짐승을 노골적인 파탄에까지 몰지 않으려 애쓰면서도, 그 짐승이 견딜 만한 한도껏 다그쳐 달려오고 있었다. 이때까지 이 인물은 여행자들의 눈길을 피해 있었다. 두 발로 서서 제 키의 영광을 펼칠 때조차 떠도는 눈길을 붙들어 두는 힘이 있었다면, 그가 말 위에서 부리는 우아함은 더더욱 시선을 끌 만했다. 외짝 박차 달린 발뒤꿈치를 끊임없이 암말의 옆구리에 들이대도, 그가 끌어낼 수 있는 가장 한결같은 걸음새라곤 뒷다리로 내딛는 캔터베리식 구보였고, 그 사이 앞다리는 미덥지 못한 순간순간 거들기는 했으나 대개는 껑충대는 속보를 유지하는 데 만족했다. 어쩌면 이 걸음새에서 저 걸음새로 바뀌는 그 빠르기가 착시를 일으켜 짐승의 능력을 부풀려 보이게 했는지도 모른다. 말의 됨됨이를 보는 눈이 정확한 〈헤이워드〉조차, 제 온갖 재간을 다 써도, 자기를 뒤쫓는 자가 어떤 종류의 움직임으로 그토록 끈질긴 담력으로 제 발자취를 따라 구불구불 길을 헤쳐 오는지 분간할 수 없었던 것이 분명하니까.\n\n탄 사람의 부지런함과 동작도 탄 짐승의 그것 못지않게 볼만했다. 짐승의 걸음새가 바뀔 때마다, 사내는 등자를 디뎌 제 큰 몸을 일으켜 세웠다. 이런 식으로 다리를 지나치게 늘여, 키가 별안간 자라났다 줄어들었다 하니, 그 치수가 도대체 어떠한지 헤아릴 만한 온갖 짐작을 무색하게 했다. 여기에, 박차가 한쪽에만 가해진 탓에 암말의 한 옆구리가 다른 옆구리보다 더 빨리 가는 듯 보였다는 것, 그리고 그 억울한 옆구리가 텁수룩한 꼬리의 끊임없는 휘두름으로 단호히 표시되었다는 사실을 보태면, 말과 사람 양쪽의 그림이 마저 완성된다.\n\n〈헤이워드〉의 잘생기고 트이고 사내다운 이마에 잡혔던 찌푸림이 차차 풀렸고, 그가 이 낯선 사내를 바라보는 동안 입술은 옅은 미소로 휘었다. 〈앨리스〉는 제 웃음을 억누르려 그리 애쓰지도 않았다. 〈코라〉의 검고 사려 깊은 눈조차도, 그 주인의 천성이라기보다는 몸에 밴 습관이 억눌러 온 듯한 익살로 환해졌다.\n\n\"여기서 누구를 찾으시오?\" 상대가 속도를 늦출 만큼 가까이 다가오자 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다. \"흉한 소식을 전하는 사자는 아니시기를 바라오만?\"\n\n\"바로 그러하오.\" 낯선 사내가 대답했으니, 삼각모를 부지런히 부쳐 숲의 텁텁한 공기에 바람을 일으키면서, 청년의 두 물음 가운데 어느 쪽에 답하는지 듣는 이들이 알 수 없게 두었다. 그러나 얼굴을 식히고 숨을 고른 뒤에 그는 말을 이었다. \"여러분이 〈윌리엄 헨리〉로 가신다고 들었소. 나도 그쪽으로 가는 길이니, 좋은 동행이라면 양쪽의 바람에 두루 맞으리라 결론지었소이다.\"\n\n\"보아하니 댁은 결정권을 쥔 한 표의 특권을 가진 모양이오.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 받아쳤다. \"우리는 셋인데, 댁은 자기 자신 말고는 아무와도 의논하지 않았으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 가장 먼저 얻어야 할 것은 제 마음을 아는 일이오. 일단 그것이 분명해지면 — 여인이 얽힌 일에서는 쉽지 않은 노릇이오만 — 그다음은 그 결정대로 행하는 것이지요. 나는 그 둘을 다 해 보려 애썼고, 그리하여 여기 와 있소이다.\"\n\n\"호수로 가신다면, 길을 잘못 드셨소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 거만하게 말했다. \"그리로 가는 큰길은 적어도 반 마일은 댁 뒤에 있소.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 이 쌀쌀한 대접에도 조금도 기죽지 않고 낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"나는 〈에드워드〉에 일주일을 머물렀소. 그러니 가야 할 길을 묻지 않았다면 벙어리나 다름없을 게요. 또 벙어리라면 내 소명도 끝장이지요.\" 듣는 이들로서는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는, 그러나 본인은 멋들어졌다 여기는 농담에 대한 감탄을 더 드러내 놓고 표하기를 겸양이 막기라도 한 듯, 그가 조그맣게 히죽이고 나서 말을 이었다. \"내 직업을 가진 자가 제 가르칠 사람들과 너무 허물없이 지내는 것은 현명치 못하오. 그런 까닭에 나는 군대의 행렬을 따르지 않소이다. 게다가 댁 같은 분이라면 길 떠나는 일에 가장 밝은 판단을 지녔으리라 결론지었으니, 그래서 일행에 합류하기로 마음먹었소. 그러면 길 가는 일도 즐거워질 테고, 사람 사이의 사귐도 나눌 수 있을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n\"성급하다고는 못 해도, 더없이 제멋대로인 결정이로군!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤으니, 차오르는 노여움을 터뜨릴지 아니면 상대의 면전에 대고 웃어 줄지 정하지 못한 채였다. \"한데 댁은 가르침과 직업을 말씀하시는데, 댁은 식민지 군단에 딸린, 공방의 고귀한 학문을 가르치는 사범이시오? 아니면 어쩌면, 수학을 풀이한다는 구실로 선과 각을 긋는 사람이시오?\"\n\n낯선 사내는 묻는 이를 한순간 의아하게 바라보더니, 자기만족의 모든 기색을 엄숙한 겸손의 표정 속에 지우며 대답했다.\n\n\"공방이라 하면, 어느 쪽에도 그런 것은 없기를 바라오. 방어로 말하자면 나는 아무것도 짓지 않소. 하느님의 너그러운 자비로, 마지막으로 그 용서의 은총을 빈 이래로는 뚜렷한 죄를 짓지 않았으니 말이오. 선이며 각이며 하는 댁의 비유는 알아듣지 못하겠소. 풀이하는 일은 그 거룩한 직분에 부름받아 따로 세워진 이들에게 맡기겠소. 나는 시편 영창에서 행하는바, 간구와 감사라는 영광스러운 기예에 대한 작은 통찰 말고는 더 높은 재능을 내세우지 않소이다.\"\n* 공방(攻防): 〈헤이워드〉가 'offence'와 'defence'를 군사 용어로 썼으나, 낯선 사내는 이를 '공격(죄)'과 '변명(방어)'의 도덕적 의미로 받아 엇갈리게 답한다.\n\n\"이 사람은, 누가 봐도 〈아폴로〉의 제자로군요.\" 흥겨워진 〈앨리스〉가 외쳤다. \"내가 직접 특별히 보호해 드려야겠어요. 자, 그 찌푸린 얼굴은 거두세요, 〈헤이워드〉. 그리고 갈망하는 내 귀를 가엾이 여겨, 이분이 우리 일행에 끼어 가도록 해 주세요. 게다가,\" 그녀는 낮고 다급한 목소리로, 말없으나 뚱한 안내인의 발자취를 천천히 좇는 저만치의 〈코라〉를 흘긋 보며 덧붙였다, \"어려운 때에 우리 힘에 보태질 한 사람의 벗이 될지도 모르잖아요.\"\n\n\"〈앨리스〉, 그런 일이 일어날 수 있다고 여겼다면 내가 사랑하는 이들을 이 은밀한 길로 데려왔겠소?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요, 지금 그런 걸 두고 하는 말이 아니에요. 다만 이 별난 사람이 나를 즐겁게 하니까요. 그리고 그가 '영혼에 음악을 지녔다'면, 우리가 매정하게 그 동행을 물리치지는 말기로 해요.\" 그녀는 말채찍으로 오솔길 쪽을 가리키며 권하듯 했고, 그러는 동안 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳐 청년은 그 눈맞춤을 한순간 길게 끌었다. 이윽고 그녀의 부드러운 권유에 따라, 그는 군마에 박차를 박아 몇 번 도약 끝에 다시 〈코라〉의 곁에 섰다.\n\n\"이렇게 만나게 되어 기뻐요, 벗이여.\" 처녀는 〈내러갠셋〉을 다시 측대보로 몰면서, 낯선 사내에게 앞장서라고 손을 저으며 말을 이었다. \"팔이 안으로 굽는 친척들이, 나 자신도 이중창에서 아주 쓸모없지는 않다고 거의 믿게 만들었답니다. 그러니 우리가 좋아하는 일에 마음껏 빠져 길 가는 흥을 돋울 수 있겠어요. 나처럼 무지한 사람에게는, 이 기예의 대가가 들려주는 견해와 경험을 듣는 것이 큰 도움이 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"마땅한 철에 시편 영창에 빠지는 것은 정신에도 몸에도 새 기운을 주지요.\" 노래의 대가는 그녀가 따라오라 넌지시 이른 데에 망설임 없이 응하며 대답했다. \"그처럼 위안을 주는 사귐만큼 마음을 풀어 주는 것도 없소이다. 허나 가락이 온전해지려면 네 성부가 다 갖추어져야 하오. 아가씨에게는 부드럽고 풍부한 트레블의 온갖 자질이 다 보이오. 나는 특별한 도움을 받으면 충만한 테너를 가장 높은 음까지 끌어올릴 수 있고. 헌데 우리에겐 카운터와 베이스가 없구려! 나를 일행에 끼워 주기를 망설이던 저기 저 왕의 장교라면, 예사로운 대화에서 들리는 그 음색으로 미루어 보건대, 베이스를 채울 만하겠소만.\"\n\n\"성급하고 그릇되기 쉬운 겉모습으로 너무 섣불리 판단하지는 마세요.\" 여인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"〈헤이워드〉 소령이 때로 그토록 낮은 음을 낼 수는 있어도, 정말이지 그의 본래 음색은 댁이 들으신 그 베이스보다는 부드러운 테너에 더 어울린답니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그분은 시편 영창의 기예에 꽤 단련되었소이까?\" 그녀의 순박한 동행이 물었다.\n\n〈앨리스〉는 웃고 싶은 마음이 들었으나, 대답하기 전에 가까스로 그 웃음을 억눌렀다.\n\n\"내 짐작에 그분은 차라리 속된 노래 쪽에 더 빠져 있는 편이에요. 군인의 삶이 겪는 일들이란 더 경건한 취향을 북돋우기엔 그리 알맞지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"사람의 목소리는 그 밖의 재능과 마찬가지로, 쓰라고 주어진 것이지 함부로 굴리라고 주어진 것이 아니오. 내가 내 재능을 소홀히 하는 것을 보았다고 말할 수 있는 이는 아무도 없소이다! 내 비록 소년 시절이 저 어린 다윗 왕처럼 음악의 목적에 바쳐졌다 할 수 있으나, 거친 시구의 한 음절도 내 입술을 더럽힌 적이 없으니, 그 점이 감사할 따름이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 댁은 거룩한 노래에만 애써 오셨군요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 다윗의 시편이 다른 모든 말을 능가하듯, 이 땅의 신학자와 현인들이 그 시편에 맞춘 영창 또한 온갖 헛된 시가를 능가하지요. 다행히도 나는 〈이스라엘〉 왕 그분 자신의 생각과 소망 말고는 아무것도 읊지 않는다 할 수 있소. 시류가 더러 약간의 변경을 요구할지는 몰라도, 우리가 〈뉴잉글랜드〉 식민지에서 쓰는 이 판본은 다른 모든 판본을 어찌나 능가하는지, 그 풍부함과 정확함과 영적인 단순함으로써, 영감받은 저자의 위대한 작품에 더없이 가까이 다가가 있소이다. 나는 자나 깨나 어디에 머물든, 이 재능 어린 작품 한 부를 곁에 두지 않은 적이 없소. 이것이 그 스물여섯 번째 판이오. 주후 1744년 〈보스턴〉에서 공포되었고, 제목은 『구약과 신약의 시편, 찬송, 그리고 영가 — 성도들의 공적·사적 쓰임과 교화와 위안을 위하여, 특히 〈뉴잉글랜드〉에서, 영어 운율로 충실히 옮김』이라 하오.\"\n\n제 고향 시인들의 이 진귀한 산물을 이렇게 기리는 동안, 낯선 사내는 주머니에서 책을 꺼내, 쇠테 안경을 코에 걸치고는, 그 거룩한 목적에 걸맞은 조심과 경의로 책장을 펼쳤다. 그러고는 에두르거나 양해를 구하지도 않고, 먼저 \"스탠디시\"라는 말을 발음한 뒤, 앞서 말한 그 알 수 없는 도구를 입에 대어 높고 날카로운 소리를 한 차례 끌어냈으며, 거기에 자기 목소리로 한 옥타브 아래 음을 이어 붙이고는, 다음의 가사를 충만하고 감미롭고 가락진 음색으로 부르기 시작했으니, 그 음색은 음악도, 시도, 게다가 변변히 길들지 않은 짐승의 거북한 움직임마저도 능히 무색하게 만들었다.\n\n오, 보라, 이 얼마나 좋은가, 또 이 얼마나 흐뭇한가, 형제가 한데 어우러져, 그토록 한마음으로 사는 것이. 그것은 머리에서 수염으로 흘러내린 귀한 향유와 같으니, 아래로 흐른 아론의 수염을 타고, 그 옷자락에까지 이르렀도다.\n\n이 능란한 운문을 토해 내는 동안, 낯선 사내는 오른손을 규칙적으로 올렸다 내렸다 했으니, 내릴 때는 손가락이 그 작은 책의 책장 위에 한순간 머무는 것으로 끝맺었고, 올릴 때는 그 손을 휘저어 보였는데, 그 솜씨란 입문한 자가 아니고서는 결코 흉내 낼 엄두조차 못 낼 것이었다. 오랜 단련이 이 손짓 반주를 필수로 만든 모양이었다. 시인이 제 시구의 끝맺음으로 고른 그 전치사가 두 음절짜리 낱말처럼 마땅히 다 전달되고 나서야 그 손짓이 멈추었으니까.\n\n숲의 정적과 외짐을 이렇듯 깨뜨리는 일이, 그토록 가까운 거리 앞에서 길을 가는 이들의 귀를 끌지 않을 리 없었다. 그 인디언이 서툰 영어로 〈헤이워드〉에게 몇 마디 중얼거렸고, 〈헤이워드〉는 다시 낯선 사내에게 말을 건네어, 그의 음악적 노고를 단번에 끊고 한동안 닫아 버렸다.\n\n\"우리가 위험에 처한 것은 아니나, 평범한 신중함을 따르더라도 이 황야를 지날 때는 되도록 조용히 가야 할 것이오. 그러니 〈앨리스〉, 이분께 더 안전한 기회가 올 때까지 그 영창을 미뤄 달라 청하여 그대의 즐거움을 줄이게 되더라도 용서해 주오.\"\n\n\"정말이지 줄이는 셈이 되겠어요.\" 짓궂은 처녀가 받았다. \"내가 지금껏 들어 온 것만큼 솜씨와 가사가 어울리지 않게 짝지어진 것은 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없으니까요. 그래서 나는 소리와 뜻 사이의 그런 부조화가 어디서 비롯되는지 학구적으로 깊이 파고들던 참이었는데, 〈덩컨〉, 당신의 그 베이스가 내 사색의 마법을 깨뜨려 버렸지 뭐예요!\"\n\n\"그대가 내 베이스라 부르는 게 무엇인지는 모르겠소만,\" 그녀의 말에 발끈한 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"그대와 〈코라〉의 안전이, 〈헨델〉의 어떤 관현악단보다도 나에겐 훨씬 더 소중하다는 것만은 아오.\" 그는 말을 멈추고 재빨리 어느 덤불 쪽으로 고개를 돌리더니, 이윽고 의심스러운 눈길을 안내인에게 보냈으나, 안내인은 흐트러짐 없는 엄숙함으로 한결같은 걸음을 이어 가고 있었다. 청년은 속으로 미소 지었으니, 숲의 어떤 빛나는 열매를 어슬렁대는 미개인의 번뜩이는 눈알로 잘못 본 것이라 믿었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 앞으로 말을 몰아, 스쳐 간 그 생각으로 끊겼던 대화를 이어 갔다.\n\n〈헤이워드〉 소령의 잘못이라면, 그저 젊고 너그러운 자존심이 그의 민첩한 경계심을 억누르도록 내버려 둔 것뿐이었다. 일행이 지나간 지 얼마 되지 않아, 덤불을 이룬 수풀의 가지들이 조심스레 양옆으로 갈라졌고, 미개의 기예와 고삐 풀린 격정이 만들어 낼 수 있는 한 사납고 거칠게 일그러진 사람의 얼굴 하나가, 멀어져 가는 여행자들의 발자취를 내다보았다. 이 숲의 거주자의 검게 칠해진 이목구비 위로 의기양양한 빛이 번득였으니, 그는 아무것도 모른 채 앞으로 말을 몰아 가는 제 표적들의 행로를 좇고 있었다. 여인들의 가볍고 우아한 자태가 굽이진 길을 따라 나무들 사이에서 너울거렸고, 그 굽이마다 〈헤이워드〉의 사내다운 모습이 뒤따랐으며, 마침내 노래 사범의 볼품없는 몸뚱이가, 그 사이 공간에 어두운 줄을 이루며 솟은 무수한 나무줄기 뒤로 가려졌다.\n\n이 들판이 베이고 갈리기 전에는, 우리네 강물이 가장자리까지 가득 흘렀고, 물의 가락이 싱그럽고 끝없는 숲을 가득 채웠으며, 급류가 부서지고 시냇물이 노닐었고, 샘물이 그늘 속에서 솟구쳤도다.\n브라이언트\n\n아무것도 의심하지 않는 〈헤이워드〉와 그를 굳게 믿는 일행이, 그토록 음흉한 거주자를 품은 숲속으로 더욱 깊이 파고들도록 두고, 우리는 작가의 특권을 빌려, 마지막으로 그들을 보았던 곳에서 서쪽으로 몇 마일 떨어진 데로 장면을 옮겨야겠다.\n\n그날, 두 사내가 작지만 물살 빠른 시냇가에서, 〈웨브〉의 진지에서 한 시간 거리쯤 되는 곳에서 서성이고 있었으니, 마치 자리를 비운 사람이 나타나기를, 혹은 어떤 예정된 일이 다가오기를 기다리는 자들 같았다. 광막한 숲의 천막이 강기슭까지 펼쳐져 물 위로 드리워졌고, 그 검은 물줄기를 한층 짙은 빛으로 그늘 지웠다. 햇살은 그 사나움을 누그러뜨리기 시작했고, 샘과 옹달샘에서 더 서늘한 김이 그 잎새 이불 위로 피어올라 대기 속에 머무르면서, 한낮의 강렬한 더위가 한풀 꺾였다. 그래도, 칠월 아메리카 풍경의 나른한 무더위를 일러 주는 그 숨 막힐 듯한 정적이 이 외딴 자리에 두루 깔려, 다만 사내들의 낮은 목소리와, 이따금 게으르게 두드리는 딱따구리의 소리, 어느 화려한 어치의 거슬리는 울음, 혹은 멀리 폭포의 둔탁한 굉음이 귓전에 부풀어 오는 소리에만 깨어질 뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 이 희미하고 끊어진 소리들은 숲의 사람들에게 너무도 익숙하여, 그들의 한층 흥미로운 대화에서 주의를 끌어내지는 못했다. 이 서성이는 두 사람 가운데 하나는 숲에서 나고 자란 토박이의 붉은 살갗과 거친 차림새를 드러냈고, 다른 하나는 그 투박하고 거의 야만에 가까운 차림새의 가면 너머로, 햇볕에 그을리고 오랜 세월 빛바랜 것이긴 해도 한층 밝은 안색을 보여 주었으니, 유럽 혈통의 후손임을 내세울 만한 자였다. 앞의 사내는 이끼 낀 통나무 끝에 걸터앉아, 논쟁에 임한 인디언의 차분하면서도 뜻을 담은 몸짓으로 제 진지한 말의 효과를 한층 돋울 수 있는 자세를 취하고 있었다. 거의 헐벗은 그 몸은 흰빛과 검은빛이 뒤섞인 색으로 그려진 죽음의 끔찍한 상징을 드러냈다. 바싹 깎은 머리에는, 익히 알려진 호기로운 머리 가죽 상투 외에 다른 머리카락이라곤 남아 있지 않았으며, 정수리를 가로질러 왼쪽 어깨 위로 늘어진 한 가닥 외로운 독수리 깃털 말고는 어떤 장식도 없었다. 〈영국〉제 토마호크와 머리 가죽 벗기는 칼이 허리띠에 꽂혀 있었고, 백인들이 정략으로 그 야만의 동맹군을 무장시킨 그런 종류의 짧은 군용 소총 한 자루가, 그 헐벗고 힘줄 돋은 무릎 위에 무심히 가로놓여 있었다. 떡 벌어진 가슴, 충실히 여문 사지, 그리고 이 전사의 근엄한 표정은 그가 한창때의 기운에 다다랐음을 일러 주었으나, 아직 그 사내다움을 약하게 만든 쇠락의 기미는 보이지 않았다.\n\n옷에 가려지지 않은 부분으로 미루어, 백인의 골격은 가장 어린 시절부터 고난과 노역을 겪어 온 자의 그것 같았다. 그 몸은 근육질이긴 했어도 충실하기보다는 차라리 여위었으나, 모든 신경과 근육이 쉴 새 없는 풍찬노숙과 노고로 팽팽히 당겨지고 단단히 굳어진 듯했다. 그는 숲의 초록빛 사냥 셔츠를 입었는데, 빛바랜 노란색으로 술을 달았으며, 털을 깎아 낸 가죽으로 만든 여름 모자를 썼다. 그도 인디언의 단출한 옷을 여미던 것과 같은 조가비 구슬 허리띠에 칼을 차고 있었으나, 토마호크는 없었다. 그의 모카신은 토박이들의 화려한 양식을 따라 장식되어 있었고, 사냥 셔츠 아래로 드러난 속옷의 유일한 부분은 사슴 가죽 각반 한 쌍이었으니, 옆구리에서 끈으로 매고 무릎 위에서 사슴 힘줄로 동인 것이었다. 주머니 하나와 화약통 하나가 그의 차림새를 마무리했으며, 다만 더 머리 좋은 백인들의 이론이 모든 화기 가운데 가장 위험한 것이라 가르친 매우 긴 소총 한 자루는 곁의 어린 나무에 기대어 있었다. 사냥꾼인지 척후병인지 알 수 없는 이 사내의 눈은 작고 재빠르며 날카롭고 잠시도 가만있지 않아, 그가 말하는 동안에도 사방을 두루 훑었으니, 마치 사냥감을 찾는 듯, 혹은 숨어 있는 적이 느닷없이 다가올까 의심하는 듯했다. 몸에 밴 경계의 기색에도, 그의 표정에는 거짓이 없었을 뿐 아니라, 그가 처음 등장하는 이 순간에는 한결같은 정직함의 기색마저 가득했다.\n\n\"자네네 전설을 봐도 이 일은 내 편을 들어주는걸세, 〈칭가치국〉.\" 그가 말했으니, 일찍이 〈허드슨〉강과 〈포토맥〉강 사이의 땅에 살던 모든 토박이가 알아듣던 말로 이야기한 것이라, 우리는 독자의 편의를 위해 이를 자유로이 옮기되, 동시에 그 개인의, 또 그 언어의 몇몇 특색을 살려 보고자 한다. \"자네 조상은 지는 해 쪽에서 와서, 큰 강을 건너고, 그 땅의 사람들과 싸워 땅을 차지했지. 또 내 조상은 아침의 붉은 하늘 쪽에서, 짠 호수를 건너와, 자네 조상이 남긴 본보기를 거의 그대로 따라 제 할 일을 했고. 그러니 우리 사이의 일은 하느님께서 가리시게 두고, 친구끼리는 말을 아끼세!\"\n\n\"내 조상은 헐벗은 붉은 사람들과 싸웠다!\" 인디언이 같은 말로 준엄하게 받았다. \"전사의 돌촉 화살과, 자네가 사람을 죽이는 데 쓰는 납 탄알 사이에 차이가 없단 말인가, 〈호크아이〉?\"\n\n\"붉은 살갗을 타고났어도 인디언에겐 이치가 있단 말이야!\" 백인이 말했으니, 그렇게 정의에 호소하는 말이 헛되이 흘려보내지지 않은 자처럼 고개를 가로저었다. 잠시 그는 논쟁에서 밀린다는 것을 의식한 듯하더니, 이내 다시 기운을 차려, 제 한정된 식견이 허락하는 한 최선을 다해 상대의 반박에 답했다. \"나야 학자도 아니고, 그걸 누가 알든 상관없네. 허나 사슴 사냥이며 다람쥐 사냥에서 저 아래 불꽃들을 두고 내가 본 바로 미루어 보건대, 그 조상들 손에 들린 소총은, 인디언의 분별로 당기고 인디언의 눈으로 쏜 히커리 활과 좋은 부싯돌 촉만큼 위험하진 않았을 거라 생각하네.\"\n\n\"자넨 자네 조상이 전한 이야기를 가졌지.\" 상대가 차갑게 손을 내저으며 받았다. \"자네네 노인들은 뭐라 하던가? 창백한 얼굴들이, 전쟁 채비로 칠을 하고 돌도끼와 나무총으로 무장한 붉은 사람들을 만났다고, 젊은 전사들에게 일러 주던가?\"\n\n\"나는 편견에 사로잡힌 사람도, 타고난 특권을 떠벌리는 사람도 아닐세. 허나 이 세상에서 내 가장 고약한 원수가 — 그놈은 〈이로쿼이〉인데 — 내가 순수한 백인이라는 걸 감히 부정하진 못하지.\" 척후병이, 뼈 굵고 힘줄 돋은 제 손의 빛바랜 살갗을 은근한 만족 속에 살피며 답했다. \"또 나는 내 동족에게, 정직한 사람으로서 도무지 받아들일 수 없는 구석이 많다는 걸 기꺼이 인정하네. 마을에서 이야기로 전하는 대신, 제가 한 일과 본 일을 책에다 적어 두는 게 그들의 풍습 가운데 하나지. 마을에서 이야기로 전한다면야, 비겁한 허풍쟁이의 면전에 거짓말이라 들이댈 수도 있고, 용감한 병사가 제 말이 참됨을 증언해 달라 전우들을 부를 수도 있건만. 이 못된 풍습 탓에, 검은 부호의 이름이나 익히며 아낙들 틈에서 세월을 허비하기엔 너무 양심 바른 사람은, 제 조상의 공적을 영영 듣지도 못하고, 그들을 능가하려 애쓰는 자부심도 느끼지 못할 수가 있네. 나로 말하자면, 〈범포〉 집안이 사격엔 능했으리라 결론짓는데, 내게 소총을 다루는 타고난 재간이 있는 까닭이라, 우리 거룩한 계명이 일러 주듯 온갖 선하고 악한 재능이 그러하듯, 그 재간도 대를 이어 전해 내려온 게 틀림없거든. 허나 이런 일에서 남을 두고 장담하긴 꺼려지는군. 하지만 어떤 이야기에든 양면이 있는 법. 그러니 자네에게 묻겠네, 〈칭가치국〉. 붉은 사람들의 전설에 따르면, 우리 조상들이 처음 만났을 때 무슨 일이 있었는가?\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그동안 인디언은 말없이 앉아 있었다. 이윽고 제 소임의 위엄을 가득 담아, 그는 짤막한 이야기를 시작했으니, 그 엄숙함이 이야기의 참된 기색을 한층 돋우는 데 보탬이 되었다.\n\n\"들어 보게, 〈호크아이〉. 자네 귀가 거짓을 들이켜는 일은 없을 걸세. 이건 내 조상들이 말한 바요, 〈모히칸〉족이 행한 바라네.\" 그는 한순간 머뭇거리더니, 동행에게 조심스러운 눈길을 던지며, 묻는 것과 단언하는 것 사이를 오가는 투로 말을 이었다. \"우리 발치의 이 시내는 여름 쪽으로 흐르지 않는가, 그 물이 짜질 때까지, 그러다 물줄기가 거꾸로 흐를 때까지 말일세?\"\n\n\"그 두 가지 다 자네 전설이 참된 걸 일러 준다는 건 부정할 수 없지.\" 백인이 말했다. \"나도 거기 가 봤고, 두 눈으로 봤으니까. 허나 그늘에선 그토록 단 물이 어찌하여 햇볕 아래선 쓰게 변하는지, 그건 내가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 변화일세.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 물줄기는!\" 인디언이 캐물었으니, 경탄하면서도 경의를 표하는 증언을 확인할 때 느끼는 그런 흥미로 그의 답을 기다렸다. \"〈칭가치국〉의 조상들이 거짓을 말하진 않았어!\"\n\n\"거룩한 성경도 그보다 더 참되진 않네, 그건 자연에서 가장 참된 것이지. 그 거슬러 오르는 물줄기를 조수라 부르는데, 그건 금세 설명되는, 충분히 분명한 것일세. 여섯 시간은 물이 들어오고, 여섯 시간은 물이 나가지. 그 까닭은 이렇다네. 바다가 강보다 물이 높으면, 강이 가장 높아질 때까지 물이 들어오고, 그러다 다시 나가는 게야.\"\n\n\"숲과 큰 호수들의 물은, 내 손처럼 평평히 누울 때까지 아래로 흐르지.\" 인디언이 팔을 제 앞으로 수평으로 뻗으며 말했다. \"그러고는 더 흐르지 않아.\"\n\n\"정직한 사람이라면 그건 부정 못 하지.\" 척후병이, 조수의 신비를 풀어낸 제 설명에 은근히 깃든 불신에 다소 부아가 나 말했다. \"작은 규모에서, 땅이 평평한 곳에선 그게 참이란 걸 나도 인정하네. 허나 모든 건 무슨 규모로 보느냐에 달려 있어. 자, 작은 규모에선 땅이 평평하지. 허나 큰 규모에선 둥글거든. 이런 까닭에, 웅덩이며 못이며, 심지어 큰 민물 호수까지도 고여 있을 수 있는 게야, 자네나 나나 둘 다 보아서 알듯이 말일세. 허나 바다처럼 드넓은 땅에 물을 펼쳐 놓을 때, 거기선 땅이 둥근데, 어찌 이치상 물이 잠잠할 수 있겠나? 우리 위로 일 마일쯤 떨어진 저 검은 바위 언저리에서 강이 가만히 누워 있길 바라는 거나 마찬가지지, 바로 이 순간에도 그 위로 강물이 굴러떨어지고 있다고 자네 귀가 일러 주는데도 말일세!\"\n\n설령 동행의 이치에 흡족하지 못했더라도, 인디언은 너무도 위엄 있는지라 제 불신을 드러내지 않았다. 그는 납득한 자처럼 귀 기울이고는, 앞서의 엄숙한 투로 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"우리는 밤이면 해가 숨는 곳에서 와서, 들소가 사는 큰 평원을 지나, 큰 강에 다다랐다. 거기서 우리는 〈앨리거위〉족과 싸웠으니, 땅이 그들의 피로 붉어질 때까지였지. 큰 강 기슭에서 짠 호수의 물가에 이르기까지, 우리를 맞설 자는 아무도 없었다. 〈머콰〉족은 멀찍이서 뒤따랐고. 우리는 이 시냇물에서 물이 더는 거슬러 오르지 않는 곳에서부터, 여름 쪽으로 스무 날 거리에 있는 강까지 그 땅이 우리 것이라 선언했다. 전사답게 차지한 땅을, 우리는 사내답게 지켰지. 우리는 〈머콰〉족을 곰들과 함께 숲속으로 몰아냈다. 그들은 소금밭에서나 소금 맛을 보았고, 큰 호수에서 물고기 한 마리 건지지 못했으며, 우리는 그들에게 뼈다귀나 던져 주었지.\"\n\n\"이 모든 걸 나도 들었고, 또 믿네.\" 인디언이 말을 멈추는 것을 보고 백인이 말했다. \"허나 그건 〈영국〉인이 이 땅에 들어오기 한참 전의 일이지.\"\n\n\"지금 이 밤나무가 선 자리에 그때는 소나무가 자랐지. 우리 가운데 처음 온 창백한 얼굴들은 〈영국〉말을 쓰지 않았어. 그들은 큰 카누를 타고 왔는데, 그때는 내 조상들이 둘레의 붉은 사람들과 토마호크를 땅에 묻은 뒤였지. 그때, 〈호크아이〉.\" 그가 말을 이었으니, 그 깊은 감회를 다만 목소리를 저 낮고 그르렁대는 가락으로 떨어뜨리는 것으로만 드러내는 터라, 이따금 그렇게 발음할 때면 그의 언어가 그토록 음악적으로 들렸다. \"그때, 〈호크아이〉, 우리는 한 백성이었고, 우리는 행복했다. 짠 호수는 우리에게 물고기를 주었고, 숲은 사슴을, 하늘은 새를 주었지. 우리는 아내를 맞아 자식을 낳았고, 위대한 정령을 섬겼으며, 〈머콰〉족을 우리 승리의 노랫소리가 닿지 않는 데까지 몰아냈다!\"\n\n\"그 시절 자네 일가에 대해 아는 게 있나?\" 백인이 캐물었다. \"허나 자넨 인디언치고는 공정한 사람이야! 또 자네가 그들의 자질을 물려받았다 치면, 자네 조상은 용감한 전사들이요, 평의회 불가에선 지혜로운 사람들이었겠군.\"\n\n\"내 부족은 여러 민족의 할아버지지만, 나는 피가 섞이지 않은 사람일세. 추장의 피가 내 핏줄에 흐르고, 그건 영영 거기 머물러야 하지. 〈네덜란드〉인이 상륙해 우리 백성에게 불의 물을 주었어. 그들은 하늘과 땅이 맞닿는 듯 보일 때까지 마셔 댔고, 어리석게도 위대한 정령을 찾았다 여겼지. 그러고는 제 땅을 내주었어. 한 발짝 한 발짝, 그들은 물가에서 밀려났으니, 추장이요 〈사가모어〉인 이 몸이, 나무 사이로 비치는 것 말고는 햇빛을 본 적이 없고, 내 조상들의 무덤을 한 번도 찾지 못할 지경에 이른 게야!\"\n\n\"무덤은 마음에 엄숙한 정을 불러오지.\" 동행의 차분한 고통에 적잖이 마음이 움직인 척후병이 받았다. \"또 그건 흔히 사람이 좋은 뜻을 품도록 돕는 법이고. 허나 나로 말하면, 내 뼈는 묻히지 못한 채 숲에서 바래거나, 늑대들에게 찢기리라 여기네. 그건 그렇고, 그 옛날, 그토록 많은 여름 전에 〈델라웨어〉 땅에서 제 일족을 찾아왔다는 자네 종족 사람들은 지금 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"그 여름들의 꽃은 어디 있는가! 하나하나 떨어져 버렸지. 내 일가도 다 그렇게, 저마다 차례로 정령들의 땅으로 떠났다네. 나는 언덕 꼭대기에 있고, 이제 골짜기로 내려가야 하지. 〈언커스〉가 내 발자취를 따라온 다음엔, 〈사가모어〉의 피를 이은 자는 더 이상 없을 게야, 내 아들이 〈모히칸〉족의 최후이니.\"\n\n\"〈언커스〉가 여기 있습니다!\" 또 다른 목소리가, 그 곁에서 똑같이 부드럽고 그르렁대는 가락으로 말했다. \"누가 〈언커스〉를 부르십니까?\"\n\n이 느닷없는 끼어듦에, 백인은 가죽집의 칼을 느슨히 풀고 저도 모르게 손을 소총 쪽으로 가져갔다. 그러나 인디언은 침착히 앉은 채, 뜻밖의 소리에도 고개조차 돌리지 않았다.\n\n다음 순간, 한 젊은 전사가 소리 없는 발걸음으로 둘 사이를 지나, 물살 빠른 시냇가에 자리를 잡았다. 아버지에게선 놀라움의 탄성 하나 새어 나오지 않았고, 몇 분 동안 묻는 말도, 답하는 말도 없었으니, 저마다 아낙네 같은 호기심이나 아이 같은 조급함을 드러내지 않고 말할 수 있는 때를 기다리는 듯했다. 백인은 그들의 풍습에서 본을 받은 듯, 소총 쥐었던 손을 놓고 그 역시 말없이 삼간 채 있었다. 마침내 〈칭가치국〉이 천천히 아들에게로 눈길을 돌리며 물었다.\n\n\"〈머콰〉족이 감히 이 숲에 제 모카신 자국을 남긴단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"그들의 자취를 좇아 봤습니다.\" 젊은 인디언이 답했다. \"그 수가 제 두 손 손가락만큼 된다는 걸 알았지요. 허나 비겁한 자들답게 숨어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그 도둑놈들이 머리 가죽과 노략질을 노리고 잠복해 있는 게로군!\" 백인이 말했으니, 우리는 그 동행들이 부르던 대로 그를 〈호크아이〉라 부르기로 한다. \"그 텁수룩한 프랑스 놈, 〈몽칼름〉이 제 첩자들을 우리 진지 한복판에까지 들여보낼 테지. 허나 우리가 어느 길로 가는지는 알게 될 게야!\"\n\n\"그만하면 됐다!\" 아버지가 지는 해 쪽으로 눈길을 던지며 받았다. \"그들을 덤불에서 사슴처럼 몰아내리라. 〈호크아이〉, 오늘 밤은 먹고, 내일은 〈머콰〉족에게 우리가 사내임을 보여 주세.\"\n\n\"한쪽이든 다른 쪽이든 나야 기꺼이 할 준비가 됐네. 허나 〈이로쿼이〉와 싸우려면 그 숨은 놈들을 찾아내야 하고, 먹으려면 사냥감을 잡아야지. 호랑이도 제 말 하면 온다더니, 이 철에 내가 본 것 중 가장 큰 뿔 한 쌍이 언덕 아래 덤불을 흔들고 있군! 자, 〈언커스〉.\" 그가 반쯤 속삭이며, 경계하는 법을 익힌 자처럼 안으로 삼키는 듯한 소리로 웃으며 말을 이었다. \"내 화약통을 세 번 가득 채운 화약을, 한 자 길이의 조가비 구슬과 걸고 내기하지, 내가 저놈을 두 눈 사이에, 왼쪽보다 오른쪽에 더 가깝게 맞히겠다고 말일세.\"\n\n\"애송이로군!\" 백인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. 그러면서 아버지에게 말을 건넸다. \"사냥꾼이 짐승의 일부만 봐도 나머지가 어디 있는지 알 수 없다고 생각하는 모양이지!\"\n\n\"애로구먼!\" 백인이 그렇게 말하며 고개를 가로젓고는, 아버지를 향해 말했다. \"사냥꾼이 짐승의 한 부분만 봐도 나머지가 어디 있을지 모를 줄 아는 게야!\"\n\n소총을 매만지며, 그가 그토록 스스로 자랑하는 그 솜씨를 한번 보여 주려던 참에, 전사가 손으로 총을 쳐올리며 말했다.\n\n\"〈호크아이〉! 〈머콰〉족과 싸우겠나?\"\n\n\"이 인디언들은 마치 본능으로 그러하듯 숲의 본성을 안단 말이야!\" 척후병이 소총을 내리고, 제 잘못을 깨달은 자처럼 돌아서며 받았다. \"저 수사슴은 자네 화살에 맡겨야겠군, 〈언커스〉. 안 그러면 우리가 저 도둑놈들, 〈이로쿼이〉가 먹으라고 사슴을 잡아 주는 꼴이 될지도 모르니.\"\n\n아버지가 뜻을 담은 손짓으로 이 말에 맞장구를 치자, 〈언커스〉는 곧장 땅에 몸을 던지고 조심스러운 동작으로 짐승에게 다가갔다. 은신처에서 몇 야드 안에 이르렀을 때, 그는 더없이 신중하게 활시위에 화살을 메겼으니, 그동안 뿔은, 그 임자가 더럽혀진 공기에서 적의 냄새라도 맡은 듯 움직였다. 다음 순간 시위 튕기는 소리가 들리고, 흰 줄기 하나가 덤불 속으로 번뜩이며 들어가는 것이 보이더니, 상처 입은 수사슴이 은신처에서 솟구쳐 나와, 바로 그 숨은 적의 발치로 뛰어들었다. 성난 짐승의 뿔을 피해, 〈언커스〉는 그 옆구리로 쏜살같이 달려들어 칼로 목을 그었으니, 짐승은 강가로 펄쩍 뛰어가 쓰러지며 제 피로 물을 물들였다.\n\n\"인디언 솜씨로 해냈군.\" 척후병이 속으로 웃으며, 그러나 그지없는 만족 속에 말했다. \"그리고 보기에도 멋진 광경이었어! 비록 화살은 가까이서 쏘는 거라, 일을 끝내려면 칼이 필요하지만 말일세.\"\n\n\"휴!\" 그의 동행이, 사냥감 냄새를 맡은 사냥개처럼 휙 돌아서며 외마디 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"이런, 저놈들 떼거리로구먼!\" 척후병이 외쳤으니, 그 눈은 늘 하던 일의 열기로 빛나기 시작했다. \"저놈들이 탄알 사정거리 안에 들면, 〈여섯 부족 연합〉 전체가 그 소리 안에 숨어 있다 한들 내 한 놈 거꾸러뜨리겠네! 무슨 소리가 들리나, 〈칭가치국〉? 내 귀엔 숲이 벙어리 같은데.\"\n\n\"사슴은 한 마리뿐이고, 그놈은 죽었다.\" 인디언이 귀가 거의 땅에 닿도록 몸을 굽히며 말했다. \"발소리가 들린다!\"\n\n\"늑대들이 수사슴을 숨을 데로 몰면서, 그 자취를 따라오는 건지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"아니야. 백인들의 말이 오고 있어!\" 상대가 위엄 있게 몸을 일으키며 받고는, 앞서의 침착함으로 다시 통나무에 자리를 잡았다. \"〈호크아이〉, 자네 형제들일세. 자네가 말하게.\"\n\n\"그러지, 그것도 임금님이 답하기에 부끄럽지 않을 〈영국〉말로 말일세.\" 사냥꾼이 제가 자랑하던 그 언어로 말하며 받았다. \"허나 아무것도 안 보이고, 사람이건 짐승이건 그 소리도 안 들리는걸. 인디언이 백인 소리를, 그 피에 한 방울 섞임도 없다는 걸 적들조차 인정하는 사람보다 더 잘 알아듣는다니 이상한 노릇이지, 비록 붉은 사람들과 어울려 산 세월이 의심받을 만큼 길긴 해도 말이야! 하! 저기 마른 나뭇가지 꺾이는 것 같은 소리도 나는군. 이제 덤불이 움직이는 게 들리네. 그래, 그래, 내가 폭포로 잘못 들었던 발 구르는 소리가 있어. 그리고, 아니, 저기 그들이 직접 오는군. 부디 하느님께서 그들을 〈이로쿼이〉에게서 지켜 주시기를!\"\n* 〈여섯 부족 연합〉: 〈이로쿼이〉 연맹을 이루던 여섯 부족을 가리킨다.\n\n\"좋다, 네 갈 길을 가라. 허나 이 작은 숲에서\n내 너를 이 상처의 빚으로 괴롭히기 전에는 떠나지 못하리라.\"\n\n한여름 밤의 꿈\n\n이 말이 아직 척후의 입가에 머물러 있을 때, 그 다가오는 발소리가 인디언의 예민한 귀를 사로잡았던 일행의 우두머리가 대놓고 모습을 드러냈다. 사슴이 철 따라 오가며 낸 것 같은 다져진 오솔길 하나가 멀지 않은 곳에서 작은 골짜기를 가로질러 굽이지다가, 백인과 그 붉은 동료들이 자리 잡은 바로 그 지점에서 강에 가닿았다. 이 자취를 따라, 숲속 깊은 곳에서는 그토록 보기 드문 놀라움을 자아낸 그 여행자들이, 동료들 앞에 서서 그들을 맞을 채비를 갖춘 사냥꾼을 향해 천천히 다가왔다.\n\n\"누가 오는가?\" 척후가 왼팔에 무심히 소총을 걸치고 오른손 검지를 방아쇠에 댄 채, 그러나 그 동작에서 위협의 기색이라곤 일절 피하면서 물었다. \"이 황야의 짐승들과 위험들 한가운데로, 누가 이리로 오는가?\"\n\n\"신앙을 믿는 자들이며, 법과 왕의 벗들이오.\" 맨 앞에서 말을 탄 자가 대답했다. \"해가 뜬 뒤로 이 숲의 그늘 속에서 먹을 것도 없이 길을 걸어온 자들이며, 그 여정에 몹시 지친 자들이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신들은 길을 잃은 게로군.\" 사냥꾼이 말을 끊었다. \"오른쪽으로 가야 할지 왼쪽으로 가야 할지 모른다는 게 얼마나 막막한 일인지 알게 된 게요?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 젖먹이 아기들이 저를 이끄는 이들에게 매달리는 것보다, 다 자란 우리가, 이제는 사람의 키만 갖추었을 뿐 사람의 지혜는 갖추지 못했다 할 우리가 더 매달리고 있구려. 〈윌리엄 헨리〉라 불리는 왕실의 진지까지 거리가 얼마나 되는지 아시오?\"\n\n\"허!\" 척후가 거리낌 없는 웃음을 아끼지 않고 외쳤으나, 곧 그 위험한 소리를 억누르고는, 어디 숨어 있을 적들에게 들킬 위험이 덜한 채로 그 흥겨움을 마음껏 누렸다. \"당신은 사냥개가 저와 사슴 사이에 〈호리컨〉을 둔 꼴만큼이나 냄새를 놓쳤구려! 이보시오, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉라니! 당신들이 왕의 벗이고 군대와 볼일이 있다면, 차라리 강을 따라 〈에드워드〉로 내려가 그 일을 〈웨브〉 앞에 아뢰는 편이 나을 거요. 그 양반은 협곡으로 밀고 들어가 이 건방진 프랑스 놈을 〈섐플레인〉 너머 제 굴속으로 도로 몰아내는 대신, 거기 머물러 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n이 뜻밖의 제안에 낯선 이가 미처 답하기도 전에, 또 다른 기수가 덤불을 헤치며 군마를 몰아 동료 앞으로 오솔길에 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 〈에드워드〉 요새까지 우리 거리가 얼마나 되겠소?\" 새로운 화자가 물었다. \"당신이 우리더러 찾아가라는 그곳은 우리가 오늘 아침 떠나온 곳이고, 우리의 목적지는 호수의 머리요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신들은 길을 잃기 전에 먼저 시력을 잃었던 게로군. 육로 운반로를 가로지르는 길은 좋이 두 로드 너비로 닦여 있어, 내 셈으로는 〈런던〉으로 뻗은 어느 길에도, 아니 왕 자신의 궁전 앞에 난 길에도 못지않은 훌륭한 길이오.\"\n\n\"그 길이 얼마나 좋은지를 두고 다투지는 맙시다.\" 헤이워드가 미소 지으며 대답했으니, 독자가 짐작했듯, 바로 그였다. \"지금으로선, 우리가 한 인디언 안내인을 믿고 더 가깝되 더 어두운 길로 가려 했다가, 그자의 지식에 속고 말았다는 것으로 족하오. 쉽게 말해, 우리는 우리가 어디 있는지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"인디언이 숲에서 길을 잃다니!\" 척후가 의심스레 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"해가 나무 우듬지를 태우고 물길이 가득 차 있을 때, 눈에 들어오는 너도밤나무마다 그 이끼가 밤에 북극성이 어느 방위에 빛날지 일러 줄 텐데! 숲에는 시내와 소금 핥는 자리로 이어지는 사슴 길이 가득하고, 그건 누구나 잘 아는 데란 말이오. 게다가 기러기들이 아직 〈캐나다〉의 물로 다 날아가 버린 것도 아니고! 인디언이 〈호리컨〉과 강의 굽이 사이에서 길을 잃는다는 건 괴이쩍은 일이오. 그자가 〈모호크〉요?\"\n\n\"태생으로는 아니나, 그 부족에 받아들여졌소. 내 생각엔 그자의 태생지는 더 북쪽이고, 당신들이 〈휴런〉이라 부르는 자들 가운데 하나요.\"\n\n\"휴!\" 척후의 두 동료가 외쳤으니, 그들은 이 대목까지 꼼짝 않고 앉아 오가는 말에 무심한 듯하다가, 이제 그 절제를 놀라움에 압도당한 듯한 활기와 관심으로 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉이라!\" 다부진 척후가 다시 한번 드러내 놓고 못 미더운 듯 고개를 저으며 되뇌었다. \"그놈들은 도둑의 족속이라, 누가 받아들였든 나는 상관 안 하오. 그자들로는 숨어 다니는 자나 떠돌이밖에 만들 수가 없소. 당신이 그 부족의 하나에게 몸을 맡겼다 하니, 더 많은 놈들과 마주치지 않은 게 그저 신기할 따름이오.\"\n\n\"그럴 위험은 거의 없소. 〈윌리엄 헨리〉가 우리 앞으로 그토록 여러 마일 떨어져 있으니 말이오. 그 안내인이 이제는 〈모호크〉이며, 우리 군대와 함께 벗으로서 복무한다고 내가 말한 것을 잊으셨군.\"\n\n\"그리고 〈밍고〉로 태어난 자는 〈밍고〉로 죽는다고 내가 말하지 않았소.\" 상대가 단호히 대꾸했다. \"〈모호크〉라! 아니지, 정직함을 말하자면 내게는 〈델라웨어〉나 〈모히칸〉을 주시오. 그리고 그들이 싸우려 들 때면 — 다는 그러지 않소만, 교활한 적인 〈마콰〉가 자기들을 계집으로 만들어 버리는 걸 그냥 두었으니 — 하나 그들이 정말 싸우려 들 때면, 전사를 보고 싶거든 〈델라웨어〉나 〈모히칸〉을 보시오!\"\n\n\"그 이야기는 그만하시오.\" 헤이워드가 참다못해 말했다. \"나는 내가 아는 사람, 그리고 당신에게는 낯선 자임이 분명한 사람의 됨됨이를 캐물을 마음이 없소. 당신은 아직 내 물음에 답하지 않았소. 〈에드워드〉의 본대까지 우리 거리가 얼마나 되오?\"\n\n\"그건 당신의 안내인이 누구냐에 달린 모양이오. 저런 말이라면 해 뜰 녘과 해 질 녘 사이에 꽤나 먼 길을 넘을 수 있다고들 여길 테니.\"\n\n\"당신과 부질없는 말다툼을 벌일 마음은 없소, 친구.\" 헤이워드가 불만스러운 기색을 누르고 한결 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"〈에드워드〉 요새까지 거리를 일러 주고 나를 그리로 인도해 준다면, 당신의 수고가 보답 없이 끝나지는 않을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그리한들, 내가 적을, 〈몽칼름〉의 첩자를 군대의 진지로 안내하는 게 아니라는 걸 어찌 알겠소? 영어를 할 줄 안다고 해서 다 정직한 신하는 아니오.\"\n\n\"당신이 군대와 함께 복무한다면, 내가 보기에 당신은 척후인 듯한데, 왕의 연대 가운데 60연대쯤은 알 거요.\"\n\n\"60연대라! 내가 다홍색 군복 대신 사냥용 셔츠를 걸치고 있긴 하나, 〈로열 아메리칸〉에 관해서라면 내가 모르는 걸 당신이 일러 줄 게 별로 없소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 다른 것들 중에서도, 그 연대 소령의 이름쯤은 알겠구려?\"\n\n\"그 소령이라!\" 사냥꾼이 제 직임을 자랑스러워하는 자처럼 몸을 곧추세우며 말을 끊었다. \"이 고장에 〈에핑엄〉 소령을 아는 사람이 있다면, 그가 바로 당신 앞에 서 있소.\"\n\n\"그것은 소령이 여럿인 부대요. 당신이 댄 분은 선임이지만, 나는 그 가운데 가장 후임을 말하는 것이오. 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 수비대 중대들을 지휘하는 자 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소, 남쪽 먼 어느 식민지 출신의, 막대한 재산을 가진 젊은 양반이 그 자리를 맡았다고 들었소. 그런 계급을 지니기에는, 머리가 희끗희끗해지기 시작한 사람들 위에 놓이기에는 너무 젊긴 하지만, 그래도 사람들 말로는 식견으로는 군인이요, 늠름한 신사라 합디다!\"\n\n\"그가 무엇이든, 또 그 계급에 얼마나 걸맞든, 그가 지금 당신에게 말하고 있으니, 두려워할 적일 리는 당연히 없겠소.\"\n\n척후가 놀라 헤이워드를 바라보더니, 모자를 들어 올리고는, 전보다 덜 자신만만한, 그러나 여전히 의심을 드러내는 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 한 부대가 호숫가로 가려고 진지를 떠난다는 말은 들었소.\"\n\n\"당신이 들은 것이 사실이오. 다만 나는 더 가까운 길을 택했으니, 내가 말한 그 인디언의 지식을 믿고서 말이오.\"\n\n\"그래서 그자가 당신을 속이고는, 그러고 나서 달아났소?\"\n\n\"내가 믿기로는 둘 다 아니오. 적어도 후자는 분명 아니오. 그자는 뒤편에 있을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n\"내 그 짐승을 한번 봐야겠소. 만일 그자가 진짜 〈이로쿼이〉라면, 그 악당 같은 낯짝과 칠한 무늬로 알아볼 수 있을 거요.\" 척후가 헤이워드의 군마 곁을 지나, 그 망아지가 멈춘 틈을 타 어미의 젖을 받아 먹고 있던 노래 선생의 암말 뒤로 난 길로 들어서며 말했다. 덤불을 옆으로 밀치고 몇 걸음 나아가자, 그는 회담의 결과를 근심 어린, 그러면서도 불안이 아주 없지는 않은 마음으로 기다리던 여인들과 마주쳤다. 그 뒤에는 전령이 나무에 기대 서 있었으니, 그는 척후의 면밀한 검사를 흔들림 없는 태도로 견뎌 냈으되, 그 표정이 어찌나 어둡고 사나웠던지, 그 자체로 두려움을 자아낼 만했다. 살핌에 만족한 사냥꾼은 곧 그를 떠났다. 여인들 곁을 다시 지나며 그는 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 그들의 아름다움을 바라보았으니, 〈앨리스〉의 미소와 끄덕임에는 거리낌 없는 기쁨의 눈길로 답했다. 거기서 그는 그 어미다운 짐승 곁으로 가, 그 임자의 됨됨이를 살피느라 일 분쯤 부질없이 보내고는, 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 헤이워드에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"〈밍고〉는 〈밍고〉요, 신이 그를 그렇게 지으셨으니, 〈모호크〉도, 그 어떤 부족도 그를 바꿔 놓을 수 없소.\" 그가 본래 자리로 돌아와서 말했다. \"우리뿐이고, 당신이 오늘 밤 저 고귀한 말을 늑대들의 처분에 맡겨 둔다면, 내가 한 시간 안에 손수 〈에드워드〉로 가는 길을 보여 드릴 수 있소. 여기서 한 시간 남짓한 거리밖에 안 되니 말이오. 허나 당신 일행에 저런 숙녀들이 있으니 그건 불가능하오!\"\n\n\"어째서요? 저들은 지쳤으나, 몇 마일쯤 더 말을 타기에는 넉넉히 견딜 수 있소.\"\n\n\"그건 자연의 이치로 불가능하오!\" 척후가 거듭 말했다. \"나는 식민지에서 제일 좋은 소총을 준대도, 밤이 든 뒤에 저 전령과 함께라면 이 숲에서 한 마일도 걷지 않겠소. 숲에는 밖에 나와 있는 〈이로쿼이〉가 가득하고, 당신의 잡종 〈모호크〉는 그들을 어디서 찾을지 너무 잘 아니, 내 길벗으로는 안 되오.\"\n\n\"그리 생각하시오?\" 헤이워드가 안장에서 몸을 앞으로 기울이고, 목소리를 거의 속삭임에 가깝게 낮추며 말했다. \"고백하건대 나도 나름의 의심이 없지 않았소. 다만 동행들을 생각해, 그 의심을 감추려 애썼고, 늘 느끼지는 못한 믿음을 꾸며 냈을 뿐이오. 그자를 의심했기에 더는 따르지 않으려 했고, 보다시피 그자를 내 뒤를 따르게 한 것이오.\"\n\n\"내 저놈을 보자마자 사기꾼 중 하나란 걸 알았소!\" 척후가 조심하라는 표시로 손가락을 코에 대며 대꾸했다. \"저 도둑은, 당신이 저 덤불 너머로 볼 수 있는 단풍 묘목 밑동에 기대 있소. 놈의 오른쪽 다리가 나무껍질과 일직선을 이루고 있으니,\" 그가 제 소총을 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"내 선 자리에서 발목과 무릎 사이를 단 한 발로 맞혀, 적어도 앞으로 한 달은 놈이 숲을 헤집고 다니는 걸 끝장낼 수 있소. 만일 내가 놈에게로 도로 가면, 그 교활한 짐승은 무언가를 눈치채고 겁먹은 사슴처럼 나무 사이로 요리조리 달아날 거요.\"\n\n\"그건 안 되오. 그자가 죄 없을 수도 있고, 나는 그런 짓이 싫소. 다만, 그자의 배신을 확신한다면야ㅡ\"\n\n\"〈이로쿼이〉의 간계를 셈에 넣는 건 안전한 일이오.\" 척후가 일종의 본능적인 동작으로 제 소총을 앞으로 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"멈추시오!\" 헤이워드가 가로막았다. \"그건 안 되오ㅡ다른 방책을 궁리해야 하오. 그렇긴 해도, 그 악당이 나를 속였다고 믿을 만한 까닭은 충분하오.\"\n\n이미 전령에게 상해를 입히려던 뜻을 접은 사냥꾼은 잠시 골똘히 생각하더니, 손짓을 했고, 그 손짓에 그의 두 붉은 동료가 즉시 그의 곁으로 다가왔다. 그들은 낮은 목소리로나마 〈델라웨어〉 말로 진지하게 함께 이야기를 나누었으니, 묘목 우듬지 쪽을 자주 가리키는 백인의 몸짓으로 보아, 그가 숨은 적의 위치를 일러 주고 있음이 분명했다. 그의 동료들은 그 뜻을 알아채는 데 오래 걸리지 않았고, 총을 내려놓고는 길의 양편으로 갈라져, 발소리 하나 들리지 않을 만큼 조심스러운 움직임으로 덤불 속에 몸을 묻었다.\n\n\"자, 당신은 도로 돌아가시오.\" 사냥꾼이 다시 헤이워드에게 말했다. \"그러고는 저 악마 같은 놈을 말로 붙들어 두시오. 여기 이 〈모히칸〉들이 그놈의 칠한 무늬 하나 흩뜨리지 않고 사로잡을 테니.\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 헤이워드가 자랑스레 말했다. \"내 손수 그를 붙잡겠소.\"\n\n\"쉿! 말을 탄 채로, 덤불 속의 인디언을 상대로 당신이 무얼 할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"내려서겠소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 당신의 한쪽 발이 등자에서 빠지는 걸 보고도, 그놈이 나머지 발이 풀려나기를 기다려 줄 성싶소? 원주민들을 상대하러 숲에 드는 자라면 누구든, 제 일에서 잘되기를 바란다면 인디언의 방식을 써야 하오. 그러니 가서, 그 악당에게 거리낌 없이 말을 걸고, 그자가 이 땅에서 당신이 가진 가장 진실한 벗이라고 믿는 척하시오.\"\n\n헤이워드는 따를 채비를 했으나, 어쩔 수 없이 떠맡게 된 그 일의 성질에 강한 거부감을 느꼈다. 그러나 매 순간이, 제 믿음 탓에 그 더없이 소중한 책임을 위태로운 처지에 빠뜨렸다는 깨달음을 그에게 들이밀었다. 해는 이미 사라졌고, 그 빛을 갑자기 잃은 숲은 어둑한 빛깔을 띠어 가고 있었으니, 미개인이 으레 가장 야만스럽고 가차 없는 복수나 적의의 행위를 저지를 시각으로 고르는 그 시간이 빠르게 다가오고 있음을 그에게 날카롭게 일깨웠다. 불안에 떠밀린 그는 척후를 떠났고, 척후는 곧장 그날 아침 그토록 격식 없이 여행자 일행에 끼어든 낯선 이와 큰 소리로 대화를 나누기 시작했다. 더 연약한 동행들 곁을 지나며 헤이워드는 격려의 말 몇 마디를 건넸고, 그들이 비록 하루의 행로에 지쳤으나, 지금의 곤경이 우연의 결과 이외의 것이라는 의심은 품지 않은 듯하여 마음이 놓였다. 그저 앞으로의 길에 관해 상의하고 있을 뿐이라고 그들이 믿게끔 만든 뒤, 그는 군마에 박차를 가했다가, 그 짐승이 그를 태우고 침울한 전령이 여전히 나무에 기대선 자리에서 몇 야드 안으로 들어섰을 때 다시 고삐를 당겼다.\n\n\"〈머과〉, 자네도 보다시피,\" 그가 자유롭고 자신만만한 기색을 꾸며 보이려 애쓰며 말했다. \"밤이 우리를 에워싸며 다가드는데, 우리는 해 뜰 녘에 〈웨브〉의 진지를 떠났을 때보다 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에 조금도 더 가까워지지 못했네. 자네가 길을 잘못 들었고, 나 또한 더 나을 것이 없었네. 허나 다행히도 우리는 한 사냥꾼을 만났네. 자네가 저 노래꾼과 이야기하는 걸 듣고 있는 저자가, 숲의 사슴 길과 샛길에 밝아, 우리를 아침까지 안전히 쉴 수 있는 곳으로 인도해 주겠다고 약속했네.\"\n\n인디언이 그 이글거리는 눈을 헤이워드에게 못 박고는, 어설픈 영어로 물었다. \"그자 혼자요?\"\n\n\"혼자라!\" 속임수가 너무도 낯설어 거북함 없이는 꾸며 낼 수 없는 헤이워드가 머뭇대며 대답했다. \"오! 〈머과〉, 분명 혼자는 아니지, 우리가 그와 함께 있다는 걸 자네도 알지 않나.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 〈교활한 여우〉는 가겠소.\" 전령이 발치에 놓여 있던 작은 자루를 차갑게 집어 들며 대꾸했다. \"그러면 창백한 얼굴들은 저들과 같은 빛깔밖에 보지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"가겠다니! 자네가 〈여우〉라 부르는 게 누구인가?\"\n\n\"그건 〈캐나다〉의 아버지들이 〈머과〉에게 준 이름이오.\" 전령이 그 명예를 자랑하는 듯한 기색으로 대꾸했다. \"〈먼로〉가 기다릴 때면, 〈교활한 자〉에게는 밤도 낮과 다름없소.\"\n\n\"그러면 〈여우〉는 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 지휘관에게 그 따님들에 관해 무어라 고할 텐가? 〈머과〉가 안내인이 되겠다 약속해 놓고도 그 아이들을 안내인 없이 남겨 두었노라고, 그 다혈질의 스코틀랜드인에게 감히 말할 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"비록 백발의 노인이 큰 목소리와 긴 팔을 가졌어도, 〈여우〉는 숲에서 그를 듣지도, 느끼지도 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"허나 〈모호크〉들은 무어라 하겠나? 그들은 그에게 치마를 입혀, 더는 사내의 일을 맡길 수 없는 자이니 천막 안에서 계집들과 함께 머물러 있으라 명할 걸세.\"\n\n\"〈교활한 자〉는 큰 호수들로 가는 길을 알며, 제 아버지들의 뼈를 찾을 수 있소.\" 흔들림 없는 전령의 대답이었다.\n\n\"그만하게, 〈머과〉.\" 헤이워드가 말했다. \"우리는 벗이 아닌가? 어찌하여 우리 사이에 모진 말이 오가야 하나? 〈먼로〉가 자네의 봉사가 마쳐지면 그 대가로 선물을 주겠다 약속했고, 나 또한 또 다른 선물의 빚을 지게 될 걸세. 그러니 지친 사지를 쉬게 하고, 자루를 열어 먹게나. 우리에게 잠시 여유가 있으니, 다투는 계집들처럼 말로 그 시간을 허비하지는 마세. 숙녀들이 기운을 차리면 우리는 길을 나설 걸세.\"\n\n\"창백한 얼굴들은 제 계집들에게 개처럼 굴어,\" 인디언이 제 모국어로 중얼거렸다. \"그들이 먹고자 할 때면, 그 전사들은 그 게으름을 먹이느라 도끼를 내려놓아야 하는구나.\"\n\n\"무어라 했나, 〈여우〉?\"\n\n\"〈교활한 자〉는 좋다고 했소.\"\n\n그러고 나서 인디언은 헤이워드의 트인 얼굴에 날카롭게 눈을 박았으나, 그 눈길과 마주치자 얼른 눈을 돌리고는, 신중하게 땅바닥에 앉아, 앞서 끼니의 남은 것을 꺼내 먹기 시작했으니, 다만 먼저 천천히, 조심스레 둘레로 시선을 굽혀 살피는 일을 빼놓지 않았다.\n\n\"이거 좋군.\" 헤이워드가 말을 이었다. \"〈여우〉는 아침에 길을 찾을 기운과 눈을 갖추게 될 걸세.\" 그가 말을 멈추었으니, 마른 나뭇가지가 딱 꺾이는 소리, 나뭇잎이 바스락대는 소리 같은 것이 곁의 덤불에서 일었기 때문이다. 그러나 곧 정신을 가다듬고 말을 이었다. \"우리는 해가 보이기 전에 움직여야 하네. 안 그러면 〈몽칼름〉이 우리 길목에 도사려, 우리를 요새에서 가로막아 버릴지도 모르네.\"\n\n〈머과〉의 손이 입에서 옆구리로 떨어졌고, 그 눈은 땅에 못 박혀 있었으나, 머리는 옆으로 돌아갔고, 콧구멍은 벌어졌으며, 두 귀마저 평소보다 더 곧추선 듯하여, 그에게 마치 강렬한 주의를 나타내려 빚어진 조각상 같은 형색을 더했다.\n\n빈틈없는 눈으로 그의 움직임을 지켜보던 헤이워드는, 무심한 척 한쪽 발을 등자에서 빼내며, 한 손을 권총집을 덮은 곰 가죽 쪽으로 옮겼다. 전령이 가장 주시하는 지점을 알아내려는 온갖 노력은, 어느 한순간도 특정한 대상에 머무는 법이 없으면서도 동시에 거의 움직인다고도 할 수 없는, 그 떨리는 눈길에 의해 완전히 좌절되고 말았다. 그가 어찌할지 망설이는 사이, 〈교활한 자〉가 조심스레 몸을 일으켜 발을 디뎠으니, 그 동작이 어찌나 더디고 조심스러웠던지 그 변화로 인한 자그마한 소리조차 나지 않았다. 헤이워드는 이제 자신이 행동에 나서야 할 때라고 느꼈다. 그는 다리를 안장 너머로 넘겨 말에서 내렸으니, 나아가 그 배신자 동행을 붙잡으리라 다짐하고는, 그 결과를 제 사내다움에 맡겼다. 그러나 불필요한 경각을 막고자, 그는 여전히 차분하고 다정한 기색을 지켰다.\n\n\"〈교활한 여우〉가 먹지를 않는군.\" 그가 그 인디언의 허영심에 가장 흡족하리라 여긴 호칭을 쓰며 말했다. \"옥수수가 제대로 볶이지 않아 메마른 듯하네. 어디 내가 살펴보지. 어쩌면 내 식량 가운데 그 입맛을 돋울 무언가가 있을지도 모르니.\"\n\n〈머과〉가 상대의 권유에 자루를 내밀었다. 그는 두 사람의 손이 맞닿는 것까지도, 조금의 동요도 내비치지 않고, 못 박힌 듯한 주의의 자세를 흩뜨리지도 않은 채 내버려 두었다. 그러나 헤이워드의 손가락이 제 맨팔을 따라 부드럽게 움직이는 것을 느끼자, 그는 젊은이의 팔을 쳐올리고는, 그 아래로 몸을 날리며 날카로운 비명을 내지르더니, 단 한 번의 도약으로 맞은편 덤불 속에 뛰어들었다. 다음 순간 〈칭가치국〉의 형상이 덤불에서 나타났으니, 칠한 무늬 속에서 유령처럼 보이는 그가 잽싸게 뒤쫓으며 길을 가로질러 미끄러져 갔다. 뒤이어 〈언카스〉의 외침이 따랐고, 그때 숲은 별안간 번뜩이는 섬광으로 환해졌으니, 거기에는 사냥꾼의 소총이 내는 날카로운 총성이 함께했다.\n* 로드: 길이의 단위로, 한 로드는 약 5미터에 해당한다.\n\n그러한 밤에\n티스베는 두려움에 떨며 이슬을 밟고 건너갔으니,\n\n사자를 보기도 전에 그 그림자를 먼저 보았더라.\n\n베니스의 상인\n길잡이가 느닷없이 달아나고 추격자들의 거친 함성이 터져 나온 탓에, 〈헤이워드〉는 어쩔 줄 모르는 놀라움 속에서 잠시 그 자리에 꼼짝 못 하고 서 있었다. 그러다 도망자를 붙드는 것이 중요함을 떠올린 그는, 사방의 덤불을 헤치고 추격에 힘을 보태려 다급히 앞으로 내달았다. 그러나 채 백 야드도 나아가기 전에, 그는 헛된 추격을 마치고 이미 돌아오던 세 사냥꾼과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"어찌 이리 빨리 낙심하시오!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"그 악당은 분명 이 나무들 어딘가에 숨어 있을 테니, 아직 붙잡을 수 있소. 그자가 풀려나 돌아다니는 한 우리는 안전하지 못하오.\"\n\n\"구름더러 바람을 쫓으라 하시겠소?\" 실망한 정찰꾼이 대꾸했다. \"그 악마 같은 놈이 검은 뱀처럼 마른 잎을 스치며 지나가는 소리를 들었고, 저기 저 큰 소나무 바로 너머로 언뜻 그 모습을 비춰 보고는, 냄새를 좇듯이 한 방 당겼소만, 안 됐소이다! 허나 따져 보면, 나 아닌 다른 누가 방아쇠를 당겼더라면 빠른 솜씨라 일컬었을 것이오. 또 나는 이런 일에 경험이 있다 할 만하고, 마땅히 알 만한 사람이라 여겨지오. 이 옻나무를 보시오. 잎이 붉지 않소, 누구나 그 열매가 칠월에 노란 꽃으로 핀다는 걸 알 텐데 말이오!\"\n\n\"저건 〈르 쉬브틸〉의 피요! 그자가 다쳤으니, 아직 쓰러질지도 모르오!\"\n\n\"아니오, 아니오.\" 정찰꾼이 그 의견을 단호히 부정하며 대꾸했다. \"어쩌면 내가 가지의 껍질을 벗겨 냈는지는 모르나, 그 짐승은 그 때문에 도리어 더 멀리 뛰었소. 소총 탄알이란 달리는 짐승의 살갗을 스칠 때면, 마치 당신의 박차가 말에게 하는 것과 흡사하게 작용하오. 다시 말해, 그 움직임을 빠르게 하고 살에 생기를 불어넣지, 생기를 앗아 가지는 않는다는 거요. 허나 너덜너덜한 구멍을 뚫어 놓으면, 한두 번 뛴 뒤엔 대개 더는 뛰지 못하고 멎고 마니, 인디언이든 사슴이든 매한가지요!\"\n\n\"우리는 멀쩡한 넷이고, 저쪽은 부상당한 하나뿐이오!\"\n\n\"목숨이 그리 짐스럽소?\" 정찰꾼이 말을 가로막았다. \"저 붉은 악마들은 추격으로 몸이 달아오르기도 전에 당신을 제 동료들의 손도끼가 닿는 거리 안으로 끌어들일 거요. 전쟁 함성이 허공에 울리는 가운데서 그토록 자주 잠들곤 한 사람이, 매복이 들리는 거리 안에서 총을 쏘아 버리다니 분별없는 짓이었소! 하긴 그건 자연스러운 유혹이긴 했소만! 참으로 자연스러웠소이다! 자, 친구들, 우리 자리를 옮깁시다. 그것도 〈밍고〉의 교활함을 엉뚱한 냄새 자취로 끌어들일 만한 방식으로 말이오. 안 그러면 내일 이맘때면 우리 머리가죽이 〈몽칼름〉의 천막 앞에서 바람에 말려지고 있을 테니.\"\n\n이 섬뜩한 선언은, 위험을 똑똑히 헤아리면서도 그것을 마주하기를 두려워하지 않는 사람의 냉철한 확신으로 정찰꾼이 내뱉은 것이었으니, 〈헤이워드〉에게 제 자신이 떠맡은 책무의 중대함을 일깨우는 구실을 했다. 숲의 잎 우거진 아치 아래로 짙어 가는 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 헛되이 애쓰며 사방으로 눈길을 던지자, 그는 인간의 도움에서 끊긴 채, 저항조차 못 하는 동행들이 머지않아 저 야만적인 적들의 처분에 온전히 내맡겨지리라는 느낌이 들었으니, 그 적들은 맹수처럼, 짙어 가는 어둠이 제 일격을 더 치명적으로 확실하게 해 줄 때까지 그저 기다리고 있을 따름이었다. 깨어난 그의 상상력은 속임수 같은 빛에 홀려, 흔들리는 덤불이며 쓰러진 나무토막마다를 사람의 형상으로 바꾸어 놓았고, 그는 스무 번이고, 숨어 있는 적들의 끔찍한 얼굴이 은신처에서 내다보며 끊임없는 경계로 제 일행의 움직임을 살피는 것을 분간할 수 있다고 여겼다. 위를 올려다보니, 저녁이 푸른 하늘에 그려 놓았던 얇고 양털 같은 구름은 이미 그 희미하던 장밋빛마저 잃어 가고 있었고, 그가 선 자리를 스쳐 흐르는 깊숙이 박힌 시냇물은 오직 그 숲 우거진 기슭의 어두운 경계로만 더듬을 수 있을 따름이었다.\n\n\"어찌하면 좋겠소?\" 이토록 절박한 곤경에서 망설임이 얼마나 무력한지 절감하며 그가 말했다. \"제발, 나를 저버리지 마시오! 남아서 내가 호위하는 이들을 지켜 주오. 그리고 보답이라면 무엇이든 마음대로 말하시오!\"\n\n제 부족의 말로 따로 떨어져 이야기를 나누던 동행들은 이 갑작스럽고 간절한 호소에 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그들의 대화는 속삭임보다 그리 높지 않은, 나직하고 조심스러운 소리로 이어졌으나, 이제 다가선 〈헤이워드〉는 젊은 전사의 간절한 어조를 손위 사람들의 보다 신중한 말투와 어렵잖게 분간할 수 있었다. 그들이 여행자들의 안위에 깊이 관계되는 어떤 방책의 타당성을 두고 논하고 있음은 분명했다. 그 사안에 품은 강한 관심에 못 이기고, 또 그토록 큰 위험을 더 보태는 듯한 지체에 조바심이 난 〈헤이워드〉는, 보상의 제안을 더 분명히 하려는 뜻으로 그 어둑한 무리에게 한층 가까이 다가섰는데, 마침 그때 백인이, 다투던 쟁점을 양보하기라도 하듯 손짓을 하며 몸을 돌리더니, 혼잣말처럼, 그것도 영어로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"〈언카스〉의 말이 옳소! 이 은신처를 영영 못 쓰게 만드는 한이 있더라도, 이토록 무해한 것들을 운명에 내맡기는 것은 사내가 할 짓이 아니오. 이 여린 꽃들을 더없이 고약한 뱀의 송곳니에서 구하시려거든, 나리, 허비할 시간도, 내던질 결단도 없소이다!\"\n\n\"어찌 그런 바람을 의심할 수 있겠소! 내가 이미 약속하지 않았소—\"\n\n\"이 숲을 가득 메운 악마들의 교활함을 따돌릴 지혜를 주실 수 있는 분께 기도나 드리시오.\" 정찰꾼이 차분히 말을 가로막았다. \"허나 돈을 주겠다는 제안일랑 거두시오. 당신이 살아서 그걸 내줄 수 있을지도, 내가 살아서 그 덕을 볼지도 모르는 일이니. 이 〈모히칸〉들과 나는, 비록 향기로우나 결코 황야를 위해 빚어진 적이 없는 이런 꽃들을 화에서 지키고자 사람의 머리로 생각해 낼 수 있는 일은 다 할 것이오. 그것도, 올바른 처사에 늘 내리시는 하느님의 보답 말고는 다른 어떤 대가도 바라지 않고서 말이오. 우선, 당신은 두 가지를 약속해야 하오. 당신 자신의 이름으로, 또 당신의 벗들을 위하여. 안 그러면 당신을 돕기는커녕 우리 자신만 해치는 꼴이 될 테니!\"\n\n\"무엇인지 말하시오.\"\n\n\"하나는, 무슨 일이 일어나든 이 잠든 숲처럼 고요히 있는 것이오. 다른 하나는, 우리가 당신을 데려갈 곳을 모든 사람에게 영원히 비밀로 지키는 것이오.\"\n\n\"그 두 조건이 다 지켜지도록 내 힘껏 애쓰리다.\"\n\n\"그럼 따라오시오. 화살 맞은 사슴의 심장 피만큼이나 귀한 순간들을 우리가 흘려보내고 있으니!\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 짙어 가는 저녁 그림자 사이로 정찰꾼의 조바심 어린 몸짓을 분간할 수 있었고, 그의 발자취를 좇아, 제 일행의 나머지를 두고 온 자리로 잰걸음을 옮겼다. 기다리며 애태우던 여인들과 다시 합류하자, 그는 새 길잡이가 내건 조건과, 더불어 모든 두려움을 가라앉히고 곧장 진지하게 힘을 모아야 할 필요를 그녀들에게 간략히 알렸다. 그의 놀라운 전갈이 듣는 이들에게 적잖은 은밀한 공포 없이 받아들여지지는 않았으나, 그의 간절하고 절절한 태도는, 어쩌면 위험의 성격에 힘입어, 마침내 그녀들의 신경을 다잡아 예기치 못한 보기 드문 시련을 견디게 하는 데 성공했다. 그녀들은 잠자코, 한순간도 지체 없이, 그가 안장에서 내려 주도록 몸을 맡겼고, 이내 물가로 재빨리 내려갔으니, 그곳에는 정찰꾼이, 말보다는 표정 풍부한 몸짓의 힘으로 나머지 일행을 모아 둔 터였다.\n\n\"이 말 못 하는 짐승들을 어찌한다?\" 앞으로의 움직임에 대한 단독 지휘권이 떠맡겨진 듯한 백인이 중얼거렸다. \"그 목을 베어 강에 던지자니 시간만 허비할 노릇이요, 여기 그냥 두자니 〈밍고〉들에게 그 임자를 찾으려면 멀리 갈 것 없다고 일러 주는 꼴이 될 테고!\"\n\n\"그럼 고삐를 풀어 숲을 마음껏 돌아다니게 둡시다.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 조심스레 제안해 보았다.\n\n\"아니오. 그 악마들을 헷갈리게 해서, 제 사냥감을 따라잡으려면 말의 속도에 견주어야 한다고 믿게 만드는 편이 낫소. 옳지, 옳지, 그러면 그 불덩이 같은 눈깔을 멀게 만들 거요! 〈칭가츠〉— 쉿! 무엇이 덤불을 흔드는 거지?\"\n\n\"망아지요.\"\n\n\"적어도 저 망아지만은 죽여야겠소.\" 정찰꾼이, 제 손길을 가뿐히 피하는 날랜 짐승의 갈기를 움켜쥐며 중얼거렸다. \"〈언카스〉, 자네 화살을!\"\n\n\"멈추시오!\" 사형 선고를 받은 짐승의 임자가, 다른 이들이 쓰던 속삭이는 어조 따위는 아랑곳없이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"〈미리암〉의 새끼를 살려 주오! 충직한 어미가 낳은 잘생긴 새끼로, 무엇 하나 기꺼이 해치려 들지 않는 놈이오.\"\n\n\"사람이 하느님께서 주신 단 하나의 목숨을 두고 발버둥 칠 때에는,\" 정찰꾼이 준엄하게 말했다. \"제 동족조차도 숲의 짐승과 다를 바 없어 보이는 법이오. 한 번 더 입을 열면, 당신을 〈마콰〉의 처분에 맡기고 떠나겠소! 화살을 끝까지 당기게, 〈언카스〉. 두 번째 일격을 가할 틈이 없으니.\"\n\n위협 어린 그의 목소리가 나직이 웅얼대는 소리로 아직 귀에 들리는 가운데, 부상당한 망아지가 먼저 뒷다리로 곧추서더니 앞으로 고꾸라지며 무릎을 꿇었다. 그 짐승을 맞이한 것은 〈칭가츠국〉이었으니, 그의 칼이 생각보다 빠르게 그 목을 가로 그었고, 이어 발버둥 치는 제물의 움직임을 재촉하며, 그는 그것을 강물 속으로 처박았다. 망아지는 그 물줄기를 따라 떠내려가며, 꺼져 가는 목숨과 함께 가쁜 숨을 또렷이 헐떡였다. 겉으로는 잔혹하나 실은 부득이한 이 행위는, 그 장면 속 행위자들의 차분하면서도 흔들림 없는 결연함에 한층 더해져, 여행자들의 마음에 그들이 처한 위험을 알리는 무시무시한 경고처럼 내려앉았다. 자매는 몸서리치며 서로에게 더욱 바싹 매달렸고, 〈헤이워드〉는 본능적으로, 방금 권총집에서 뽑아 든 권총 한 자루에 손을 얹은 채, 제가 지키는 이들과, 숲의 가슴 앞에 뚫을 수 없는 장막을 드리운 듯한 저 짙은 그림자들 사이에 몸을 세웠다.\n\n그러나 인디언들은 한순간도 머뭇거리지 않고, 고삐를 잡아 겁먹고 내키지 않아 하는 말들을 강바닥으로 이끌었다.\n\n물가에서 얼마 떨어지지 않은 곳에서 그들은 방향을 틀었고, 이내 기슭의 돌출부에 가려 보이지 않게 되었으니, 그 벼랑 끝 아래로 물길의 흐름과는 반대 방향으로 나아갔다. 그사이 정찰꾼은, 가지가 물살의 소용돌이에 일렁이는 낮은 덤불 밑 은신처에서 자작나무 껍질로 만든 카누 한 척을 끌어내었고, 거기에 타라고 잠자코 여인들에게 손짓했다. 그녀들은 망설임 없이 그 말을 따랐으나, 이제 시냇가를 따라 어두운 장벽처럼 누운 짙어 가는 어둠을 향해, 두려움과 근심에 찬 눈길을 여러 번 뒤로 던졌다.\n\n〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉가 자리에 앉기가 무섭게, 정찰꾼은 물 따위는 아랑곳하지 않고 〈헤이워드〉에게 그 연약한 배의 한쪽을 받치라 이르고는, 제 자신은 다른 쪽에 자리를 잡아, 둘이서 물살을 거슬러 배를 떠받쳤으니, 죽은 망아지의 낙담한 임자가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 이런 식으로 그들은 여러 로드를, 소용돌이가 둘레에서 노닐며 내는 물결 소리나, 그들 자신의 조심스러운 발걸음이 내는 나직한 물장구 소리 말고는 아무것도 깨뜨리지 못하는 고요 속에서 나아갔다. 〈헤이워드〉는 카누의 길잡이 노릇을 정찰꾼에게 고스란히 내맡겼으니, 그는 바위 조각이나 강의 더 깊은 곳을 피하려 기슭에 다가서거나 물러서며, 자신들이 가는 길에 훤한 사람임을 드러내는 능숙함을 보였다. 이따금 그는 멈추어 섰고, 폭포의 둔중하나 점점 커져 가는 굉음이 도리어 한층 사무치게 만드는 숨죽인 정적 한가운데서, 잠든 숲에서 새어 나올 법한 어떤 소리라도 잡아내려 사무치도록 긴장한 채 귀를 기울였다. 모든 것이 고요하다고, 또 그 단련된 감각의 힘으로도 다가오는 적의 어떤 기척도 알아챌 수 없다고 확신하면, 그는 신중하게 그 느리고 무방비한 전진을 다시 이어 갔다. 마침내 그들은 강의 한 지점에 이르렀는데, 그곳에서 〈헤이워드〉의 두리번거리던 눈은 한 무리의 검은 물체에 못 박혔으니, 높은 기슭이 어두운 물 위로 여느 때보다 더 짙은 그림자를 드리운 자리에 한데 모여 있었다. 나아가기를 망설이며, 그는 그곳을 동행의 주의로 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"옳지,\" 침착한 정찰꾼이 대꾸했다. \"인디언들이 원주민다운 분별로 짐승들을 숨겨 놓았소! 물은 자취를 남기지 않으니, 부엉이의 눈깔이라도 저런 굴의 어둠에는 멀어 버릴 거요.\"\n\n일행 전체가 곧 다시 한자리에 모였고, 정찰꾼과 그의 새 동료들 사이에 또 한 차례 의논이 오갔으니, 그동안, 이 알지 못하는 사냥꾼들의 신의와 기지에 운명이 달린 이들은, 제 처지를 좀 더 자세히 살필 짧은 여유를 얻었다.\n\n강은 높고 험준한 바위들 사이에 갇혀 있었는데, 그중 하나는 카누가 멎은 자리 위로 우뚝 드리워져 있었다. 이 바위들은 또다시, 벼랑 끝에서 흔들거리는 듯한 키 큰 나무들에 덮여 있었으니, 강물이 깊고 좁은 골짜기를 가로질러 흐르는 듯 보였다. 별 총총한 천정을 배경으로 여기저기 어슴푸레 그려진 그 기괴한 가지들과 너덜너덜한 우듬지 아래로는, 죄다 그림자에 잠긴 어둠 속에 똑같이 누워 있었다. 그들 뒤로는, 기슭의 굽이가 이내 똑같이 어둡고 숲 우거진 윤곽으로 시야를 가로막았으나, 앞으로는, 보아하니 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서, 물이 하늘을 향해 쌓여 올라간 듯하더니 거기서 동굴 속으로 곤두박질쳤고, 그 동굴에서 저녁 공기를 짓누르던 그 음울한 소리가 새어 나왔다. 그곳은 참으로 은둔에 바쳐진 자리인 듯했고, 자매는 비록 섬뜩하지 않은 것은 아니나 낭만적인 그 아름다움을 바라보며 안도의 마음을 들이마셨다. 그러나 길잡이들 사이에 일어난 한 차례의 움직임이, 밤이 그곳에 더해 준 거친 매혹에 대한 사색에서 그녀들을 이내 불러내어, 제 실제 위험에 대한 쓰라린 자각으로 되돌려 놓았다.\n\n말들은 바위 틈새에 자란 흩어진 떨기나무 몇 그루에 매여, 물속에 선 채로 밤을 나도록 남겨졌다. 정찰꾼은 〈헤이워드〉와 그 시름에 잠긴 길동무들에게 카누의 앞쪽 끝에 앉으라 이른 다음, 제 자신은 다른 쪽 끝을 차지했으니, 마치 훨씬 단단한 재료로 지은 배에 떠 있기라도 한 듯 꼿꼿하고 흔들림이 없었다. 인디언들이 떠나왔던 자리로 조심스레 발걸음을 되짚어 갈 무렵, 정찰꾼은 삿대를 바위에 대고 힘껏 밀어, 그 연약한 배를 사나운 물살 한복판으로 곧장 내보냈다. 여러 분 동안, 그들이 떠 있는 가벼운 거품 같은 배와 빠른 물살 사이의 다툼은 격렬했고 또 승부를 가늠하기 어려웠다. 손 하나 까딱하는 것조차 금지당하고, 행여 그 연약한 배를 물살의 노여움에 드러낼까 두려워 숨조차 거의 쉬지 못한 채, 승객들은 열병 같은 조마조마함 속에서 번득이는 물살을 지켜보았다. 그들은 스무 번이나 휘도는 소용돌이가 자신들을 파멸로 휩쓸어 가는 줄로만 여겼는데, 그때마다 사공의 능란한 솜씨가 카누의 뱃머리를 급류 쪽으로 돌려세워 거슬러 나아가게 했다. 길고, 힘차며, 여인들이 보기에는 필사적이었던 한 차례의 분투가 그 다툼에 종지부를 찍었다. 〈앨리스〉가 폭포 발치의 소용돌이 속으로 자신들이 휩쓸려 들어가려는 줄로 여겨 공포에 눈을 가리던 바로 그 순간, 카누는 물과 같은 높이로 놓인 평평한 바위 곁에 멎은 채 떠 있었다.\n\n\"우리가 어디에 있는 거요? 또 이제 무엇을 해야 하오?\" 정찰꾼의 분투가 멎은 것을 알아챈 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"〈글렌〉폭포 발치에 와 있소.\" 폭포의 굉음 속이라 결과를 두려워하는 기색 없이 큰 소리로 상대가 대꾸했다. \"이제 할 일은 흔들림 없이 뭍에 오르는 것이오. 안 그러면 카누가 뒤집혀, 올라온 것보다 더 빠르게 우리가 거슬러 온 그 험한 길을 도로 떠내려가게 될 테니. 강물이 조금 불었을 때는 거슬러 오르기 고약한 여울이고, 자작나무 껍질과 송진 약간으로 만든 이런 배 위에서, 그 허둥지둥하는 와중에 다섯이나 되는 머릿수가 젖지 않고 버티기란 부자연스러운 노릇이오. 자, 다들 바위로 오르시오. 나는 〈모히칸〉들을 사슴 고기와 함께 데려오리다. 사람이란 풍족함 한가운데서 굶어 죽느니, 차라리 머리가죽 없이 잠드는 편이 나으니까.\"\n\n승객들은 기꺼이 이 지시를 따랐다. 마지막 발이 바위에 닿자, 카누는 그 자리에서 빙그르르 돌아 떠나갔고, 그 순간 정찰꾼의 큰 형체가 강바닥에 내려앉은 뚫을 수 없는 어둠 속으로 사라지기 직전, 물 위를 미끄러져 가는 것이 잠시 보였다. 길잡이에게 남겨진 여행자들은 무력한 막막함 속에 몇 분을 머물렀으니, 그들 사방으로 물이 곤두박질치는 듯한 그 깊고 굉음 치는 숱한 동굴 가운데 하나로 한 발만 잘못 디뎌도 곤두박질칠까 두려워, 부서진 바위 위를 따라 움직이는 것조차 겁이 났다. 그러나 그들의 조마조마함은 이내 가셨으니, 원주민들의 솜씨에 힘입어 카누가 다시 소용돌이 속으로 쏜살같이 돌아와, 정찰꾼이 제 동료들과 다시 합류할 틈조차 있었을까 싶게, 낮은 바위 곁에 다시 떠 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"이제 우리는 요새화되었고, 수비대를 갖추었으며, 군량까지 마련했으니,\" 〈헤이워드〉가 명랑하게 외쳤다. \"〈몽칼름〉과 그 동맹군쯤이야 거뜬히 맞설 수 있겠소. 자, 그래, 빈틈없는 우리 보초 양반, 당신이 〈이로쿼이〉라 부르는 자들이 뭍에 있는 기색이라도 보이오?\"\n\n\"내가 그들을 〈이로쿼이〉라 부르는 건, 내게는 낯선 말을 쓰는 원주민이라면 누구나, 비록 왕을 섬기는 척한다 해도, 적으로 여겨지기 때문이오! 〈웨브〉가 인디언에게서 신의와 정직을 바란다면, 〈델라웨어〉 부족들을 데려오고, 이 탐욕스럽고 거짓말 잘하는 〈모호크〉와 〈오나이다〉들을, 그 여섯 부족의 악당 떼와 함께, 마땅히 있어야 할 자리로, 곧 프랑스인들 틈으로 보내 버리라 하시오!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우리는 호전적인 벗을 쓸모없는 벗과 맞바꾸는 셈이 되지 않겠소! 듣자 하니 〈델라웨어〉들은 도끼를 내려놓고, 여자라 불리는 것에 만족한다던데!\"\n\n\"옳거니, 제 악랄한 술수로 그들을 그런 조약에 옭아 넣은 〈네덜란드인〉들과 〈이로쿼이〉들에게 부끄러운 줄 알라지! 허나 나는 그들을 이십 년이나 알아 왔으니, 〈델라웨어〉의 핏줄에 비겁한 피가 흐른다고 말하는 자는 거짓말쟁이라 부르겠소. 당신네가 그들의 부족을 바닷가에서 몰아내 놓고, 이제는 밤마다 편한 베개를 베고 자려고, 그 적들이 하는 말을 믿으려 드는 거요. 아니, 아니오. 내게는, 그 부족의 본거지가 〈캐나다〉에 있든 〈뉴욕〉에 있든, 낯선 말을 쓰는 인디언이라면 누구나 〈이로쿼이〉요.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 정찰꾼이 제 벗인 〈델라웨어〉, 곧 〈모히칸〉들 — 그들은 같은 큰 부족의 갈래였으니 — 의 편을 완강히 고집하는 것이 쓸데없는 논쟁을 길게 끌 듯하다 여기고, 화제를 바꾸었다.\n* 본거지: 원문은 부족이 거점으로 삼은 요새 같은 마을을 뜻한다.\n\n\"조약이 있든 없든, 나는 당신의 두 동행이 용맹하고 신중한 전사임을 잘 알고 있소! 그들이 우리 적들에 관해 무언가 듣거나 본 것이 있소?\"\n\n\"인디언이란 보이기에 앞서 느껴지는 인간이오.\" 정찰꾼이 바위로 오르며 사슴을 아무렇게나 내던지며 대꾸했다. \"내가 〈밍고〉들의 자취를 좇아 바깥에 매복해 있을 때면, 눈에 들어오는 것 말고 다른 기척을 믿는 법이오.\"\n\n\"당신 귀가, 그들이 우리의 퇴로를 알아챘다고 일러 주오?\"\n\n\"그렇다고 생각하긴 싫소만, 비록 여기가 굳센 용기라면 한바탕 솜씨 좋은 접전을 벌여 볼 만한 자리이긴 하오. 그래도 부인하지는 않겠소. 내가 곁을 지날 때 말들이 마치 늑대 냄새라도 맡은 듯 움츠러들었으니. 늑대란 인디언 매복 언저리를 맴돌며, 야만인들이 잡은 사슴의 내장 찌꺼기를 탐내는 짐승이오.\"\n\n\"발치의 사슴은 잊으셨소! 아니면, 그들이 찾아든 까닭을 죽은 망아지 탓으로 돌려야 하지 않겠소? 하! 저 소리는 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"가엾은 〈미리암〉!\" 나그네가 중얼거렸다. \"네 새끼는 굶주린 짐승들의 먹이가 되도록 미리 정해져 있었구나!\" 그러더니 별안간 끝없는 물의 굉음 한가운데서 목청을 높여 큰 소리로 노래했다.\n이집트의 맏이를 그분께서 치셨으니,\n\n사람이며 짐승까지도 그러하셨도다.\n오, 이집트여! 네 한가운데로 보내신 이적들이여,\n\n파라오와 그 종들에게도 내리셨도다!\n\"망아지의 죽음이 그 임자의 마음에 무겁게 얹혀 있소.\" 정찰꾼이 말했다. \"허나 사람이 제 말 못 하는 벗들을 소중히 여기는 것을 보니 좋은 징조요. 그는 일어날 일은 일어나리라 믿으니, 그 일에 신앙을 지닌 셈이오. 또 그런 위안을 지녔으니, 사람의 목숨을 구하고자 네발짐승을 죽이는 것이 이치에 맞음을 받아들이기까지 그리 오래 걸리지 않을 거요. 당신 말대로일지도 모르오.\" 그가 〈헤이워드〉의 마지막 말의 취지로 되돌아가며 이어 갔다. \"그러니 더더욱, 우리는 살점을 베어 내고 시체는 물줄기를 따라 떠내려가게 두어야 하오. 안 그러면 늑대 떼가 벼랑을 따라 울부짖으며, 우리가 삼키는 한 입 한 입을 죄다 시샘할 테니. 게다가, 〈델라웨어〉의 말이 〈이로쿼이〉에게는 책 한 권과 다름없다 한들, 그 교활한 악당들은 늑대 울음소리의 까닭쯤은 알아챌 만큼 눈치가 빠르니까.\"\n\n정찰꾼은 그렇게 말하는 동안 필요한 도구 몇 가지를 챙기느라 분주했고, 말을 마치자 〈모히칸〉들을 거느린 채 여행자들의 무리 곁을 잠자코 지나쳤으니, 그들은 본능적인 민첩함으로 그의 뜻을 알아챈 듯 보였다. 이윽고 셋은 차례차례, 물가에서 몇 피트 안쪽에 몇 야드 높이로 솟아오른 깎아지른 바위의 어두운 면을 배경으로, 마치 사라져 버리는 듯 잇따라 자취를 감추었다.\n시온에서 한때 감미로이 흐르던 그 가락들을,\n\n그는 분별 있는 정성으로 한 대목을 가려 뽑아,\n엄숙한 기색으로 말하노라, \"우리 하느님을 경배하세.\"\n\n번스\n〈헤이워드〉와 그의 여인 일행은 이 알 수 없는 움직임을 남모를 불안 속에 지켜보았다. 비록 그 백인의 처신이 지금껏 나무랄 데 없었으나, 그의 거친 차림새와 무뚝뚝한 말투와 강한 반감, 거기에 말없는 동료들의 인상까지 더해져, 인디언의 배신에 얼마 전 놀라 떨었던 마음에 불신을 자아낼 만한 빌미가 되었던 것이다.\n\n오직 그 낯선 사내만이 눈앞에서 벌어지는 일들에 무심했다. 그는 바위의 튀어나온 곳에 자리를 잡았는데, 잦고 무거운 한숨으로 드러나는 영혼의 몸부림 말고는 의식이 깨어 있다는 다른 기색을 보이지 않았다. 이어 무언가 짓눌린 목소리들이 들렸으니, 마치 사람들이 땅속 깊은 데서 서로를 부르는 듯했다. 그러다 별안간 한 줄기 빛이 바깥에 있는 이들 위로 번뜩이며, 그토록 소중히 감춰져 있던 이곳의 비밀을 환히 드러냈다.\n\n바위 속에 난 좁고 깊은 동굴의 가장 안쪽 끝에는, 원근과 그것을 비추는 빛의 성질 때문에 그 길이가 한껏 늘어나 보였는데, 척후가 활활 타는 솔가지 매듭을 든 채 앉아 있었다. 불의 강렬한 빛이 그의 단단하고 풍상에 시달린 얼굴과 숲의 옷차림 위로 가득 쏟아져, 어떤 낭만적 야성을 더해 주었다. 그 사람은 차분한 대낮의 빛 아래서 보았다면, 옷차림의 기이함과 무쇠처럼 굽힐 줄 모르는 체격, 그리고 기민하고 빈틈없는 명민함과 더없이 순박한 단순함이 번갈아 가며 그 근육진 이목구비를 차지하는 그 묘한 결합으로 두드러질 인물이었으리라. 그보다 조금 앞쪽에는 〈언카스〉가 온몸을 또렷이 드러낸 채 서 있었다. 여행자들은 천성의 자세와 동작에 우아하고 거침이 없는, 그 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 곧고도 유연한 자태를 근심스레 바라보았다. 그의 몸은 백인의 것과 같은 초록빛에 술이 달린 사냥용 윗옷으로 평소보다 더 많이 가려져 있었으나, 무섭기도 하고 차분하기도 한 그 검고 번뜩이는 두려움 없는 눈은 감출 길이 없었으며, 본래의 붉은빛으로 순수한, 높고 거만한 이목구비의 대담한 윤곽도, 위엄 있게 우뚝한 뒤로 물러난 이마와, 시원스레 머리 가죽 한 줌까지 드러낸 고귀한 머리의 더없이 빼어난 균형도 가릴 길이 없었다. 〈던컨〉과 그 일행이 두 인디언 안내자 가운데 어느 쪽이든 그 뚜렷한 이목구비를 살펴볼 수 있었던 것은 이번이 처음이었으니, 그 젊은 전사의 야성적이면서도 자랑스럽고 결연한 표정이 절로 눈에 들어오자, 일행은 저마다 의혹의 짐에서 풀려나는 듯한 안도를 느꼈다. 그들은 이 사람이 무지의 골짜기에 어느 정도 어둠에 잠긴 존재일지언정, 그 풍부한 천부의 자질을 거리낌 없는 배신의 목적에 기꺼이 바칠 자는 결코 아니리라 여겼다. 천진한 〈앨리스〉는 그의 자유로운 기품과 자랑스러운 자세를 바라보았으니, 그것은 마치 기적의 손길로 생명을 받은, 그리스 조각가의 끌에서 나온 어떤 귀한 유물을 바라보는 듯했다. 한편 〈헤이워드〉는 타락하지 않은 원주민들 사이에 흔한 완벽한 체형을 익히 보아 왔으면서도, 인간의 가장 고귀한 비례를 이토록 흠 없이 보여 주는 표본 앞에서는 거리낌 없이 감탄을 드러냈다.\n\n\"저토록 두려움 없고 시원스러운 젊은이가 내 보초가 되어 준다면,\" 〈앨리스〉가 속삭이듯 대답했다. \"나는 마음 놓고 잠들 수 있겠어요. 분명, 〈던컨〉, 우리가 그토록 많이 읽고 들었던 저 잔혹한 살육이며, 끔찍한 고문의 광경이며 하는 것들이, 저 사람 같은 이가 있는 데서야 결코 벌어지지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n\"이건 분명, 이 특이한 족속이 빼어나다고 일컬어지는 그 천성의 자질을 보여 주는 드물고도 눈부신 본보기요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"나도 당신과 같은 생각이오, 〈앨리스〉. 저런 이마와 눈은 속이기보다는 위압하도록 빚어진 거라고. 하지만 우리가 미덕이라 여기는 것을, 미개인의 방식이 아닌 다른 식으로 드러내 보이기를 기대함으로써, 스스로를 속이는 일은 하지 맙시다. 큰 자질의 빛나는 본보기가 기독교도 사이에서도 너무나 드문 것처럼, 인디언들 사이에서도 그것은 유별나고 외로운 것이오. 비록 우리 공통된 인간 본성의 영예를 위해 말하자면, 어느 쪽도 그런 본보기를 낳지 못할 만큼 무능하지는 않지만 말이오. 그러니 이 〈모히칸〉이 우리의 바람을 저버리지 않고, 그 생김새가 말해 주는 그대로, 용감하고 한결같은 벗임을 입증해 주기를 바랍시다.\"\n\n\"이제야 〈헤이워드〉 소령께서 〈헤이워드〉 소령답게 말씀하시는군요.\" 〈코라〉가 말했다. \"이 천성의 피조물을 보면서, 그 누가 피부색의 짙고 옅음을 떠올리겠어요!\"\n\n이 말에 짧고도 다소 어색한 침묵이 뒤따랐는데, 척후가 그들에게 큰 소리로 들어오라고 부르는 바람에 그것이 끊겼다.\n\n\"이 불이 너무 밝은 불꽃을 내기 시작했소.\" 그들이 따르는 동안 그가 말을 이었다. \"잘못하면 〈밍고〉들을 우리 파멸로 인도할 빛이 될 게요. 〈언카스〉, 담요를 내려서 그 악당들에게 어두운 쪽을 보여 주거라. 이게 왕립 아메리카 연대의 소령께서 누릴 권리가 있는 그런 만찬은 아니오만, 나는 이 연대의 든든한 분견대들이 사슴 고기를 날로, 그것도 양념 하나 없이 기꺼이 먹는 것을 보아 온 사람이오. 여기 보다시피, 우리한텐 소금이 넉넉하니 후딱 구워 낼 수 있소. 숙녀분들께는 앉으실 싱싱한 사사프라스 가지가 있는데, 그게 그분들의 마-호그-기니 의자만큼 으리으리하진 않아도, 어떤 돼지 가죽이 풍기는 것보다 더 향긋한 냄새를 낸다오, 그게 기니의 돼지든 다른 어느 땅의 돼지든 말이오. 자, 친구, 망아지 때문에 너무 상심 마시오. 그건 죄 없는 짐승이었고, 모진 고생이라곤 별로 겪어 보지도 못했소. 그 죽음 덕에 그 짐승은 등이 까지고 발이 부르트는 숱한 고초를 면하게 될 게요!\"\n* 마-호그-기니: 호카니가 마호가니(mahogany)를 잘못 발음한 것이다.\n\n〈언카스〉는 척후가 시킨 대로 했고, 〈호크아이〉의 목소리가 그치자, 폭포의 굉음이 멀리서 울리는 천둥의 우르릉거림처럼 들려왔다.\n\n\"이 동굴 안이면 우리가 정말 안전한 거요?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다. \"기습당할 위험은 없소? 무장한 사내 하나가 입구에 있으면, 우린 그자의 처분에 맡겨진 신세가 될 텐데.\"\n\n유령 같은 형상 하나가 척후의 등 뒤 어둠 속에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나오더니, 활활 타는 횃불을 움켜쥐고 그들의 은신처 가장 안쪽 끝을 향해 들이댔다. 이 섬뜩한 물체가 불빛 속으로 들어서자 〈앨리스〉가 가냘픈 비명을 질렀고, 심지어 〈코라〉마저 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다. 그러나 〈헤이워드〉의 한마디가 그것이 그들의 안내자 〈칭가치국〉에 지나지 않는다는 확신을 주어 그들을 진정시켰다. 그는 또 다른 담요를 들어 올려, 이 동굴에 출구가 둘임을 드러내 보였다. 그러고는 횃불을 든 채, 그들이 있는 통로와 직각으로 난, 바위 속의 깊고 좁은 틈을 건넜는데, 그 틈은 통로와 달리 하늘을 향해 트여 있었으며, 그는 그것을 지나 또 다른 동굴로 들어갔으니, 그곳은 모든 중요한 점에서 첫째 동굴과 똑같은 모양이었다.\n\n\"〈칭가치국〉이나 나 같은 늙은 여우들은 구멍 하나뿐인 굴에서는 좀처럼 붙잡히지 않는 법이오.\" 〈호크아이〉가 웃으며 말했다. \"이곳의 교묘함은 쉽게 알아볼 수 있을 게요. 이 바위는 검은 석회암인데, 다들 알다시피 무른 돌이오. 덤불이며 솔가지가 귀한 데서는 그리 불편치 않은 베개가 되어 주지. 그건 그렇고, 이 폭포는 한때 우리보다 몇 야드 아래에 있었는데, 단언컨대 그 시절엔 〈허드슨〉강 어디에 못지않게 반듯하고 보기 좋은 물줄기였소. 허나 늙음은 고운 외모에 큰 해를 끼치는 법이니, 이 어여쁜 젊은 숙녀분들이야 아직 배워야 할 노릇이지! 이곳은 슬프게도 변하고 말았소! 이 바위들은 온통 금이 가 있고, 어떤 데는 다른 데보다 더 물러서, 물이 제 갈 길을 깊은 웅덩이로 파내고 또 파내며, 그렇게 뒤로 물러나, 그래, 몇백 피트나 물러나며, 여기서 부수고 저기서 갉아 내어, 마침내 폭포는 모양도 한결같음도 다 잃고 만 게요.\"\n\n\"우리가 지금 그 폭포의 어느 어름에 있는 거요?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"음, 우린 섭리가 폭포를 처음 두었던 자리 가까이에 있소만, 보아하니 폭포는 너무 거역하는 성미라 거기 머물지 못했던 모양이오. 우리 양쪽 바위가 더 무르다는 게 드러나니, 폭포가 강 한가운데를 텅 비고 메마르게 남겨 둔 채, 우리가 숨을 이 작은 구멍 둘을 먼저 파낸 게지.\"\n\n\"그럼 우린 섬 위에 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소! 우리 양쪽으로 폭포가 있고, 위로도 아래로도 강이 있소. 만일 한낮의 빛이 있다면, 이 바위 꼭대기로 올라가 물의 심술궂음을 바라볼 만한 수고를 할 가치가 있을 게요. 물이 도무지 아무 규칙도 없이 떨어지거든. 어떤 데선 뛰어오르고, 어떤 데선 곤두박질치고, 저기선 깡충 튀고, 여기선 쏜살같이 쏘아 대고, 한 군데선 눈처럼 희고, 또 한 군데선 풀처럼 푸르오. 이 어름에선 땅이 우르릉 떨릴 만큼 깊은 웅덩이로 처박히고, 저 어름에선 마치 밟아 다진 진흙보다 더 단단할 것도 없다는 듯, 그 오래된 돌에 소용돌이며 도랑을 빚어내며, 시냇물처럼 잔물결 일으키고 노래하오. 강의 설계 전체가 흐트러진 듯하단 말이오. 처음엔 만물이 정해진 대로 비탈을 내려가려는 듯 매끄럽게 흐르다가, 이내 비스듬히 꺾여 기슭을 마주하고, 또 어떤 데선 마치 황야를 떠나 소금기와 섞이기 싫다는 듯 뒤를 돌아보는 데도 없지 않소! 그래요, 아가씨, 아가씨가 목에 두른 그 거미줄 같은 고운 천도, 강이 온갖 형상을 빚어내는, 내가 보여 줄 수 있는 작은 자리들에 비하면, 거칠고 투박해서 마치 어망 같다오. 마치 질서에서 풀려난 김에 무엇이든 한번 해 보려는 듯이 말이오. 그런데 그게 다 무슨 소용이오! 물이 한동안, 고집 센 사람처럼 제멋대로 굴도록 내버려 둔 뒤엔, 그것을 빚은 손에 의해 한데 모이고, 몇 로드 아래에선 그 모든 물이, 땅이 처음 세워지던 때부터 미리 정해진 대로, 바다를 향해 한결같이 흘러가는 것을 볼 수 있으니!\"\n\n듣는 이들은 〈글렌〉의 이 투박한 묘사에서 자기들 은신처의 안전함에 대한 든든한 확신을 얻으면서도, 그 야성의 아름다움에 관해서는 〈호크아이〉와는 사뭇 다르게 판단하고 싶어졌다. 그러나 그들은 천연의 사물이 지닌 매력에 마음을 둘 처지가 아니었다. 그리고 척후가 말하는 동안에도 부서진 포크로 저 거역하는 물줄기의 유난히 고약한 어느 지점의 방향을 가리킬 때 말고는 굳이 요리 일손을 멈출 필요를 느끼지 못했으므로, 그들은 이제 더 속되긴 해도 꼭 필요한 일, 곧 저녁 식사에 주의를 빼앗기고 말았다.\n\n이 식사는 〈헤이워드〉가 말에서 내릴 때 미리 챙겨 온 몇 가지 진미가 더해져 한결 푸짐해졌으니, 지친 일행에게는 더없이 기운을 북돋아 주었다. 〈언카스〉는 여인들의 시중을 들며, 제 능력이 닿는 온갖 자잘한 수발을 위엄과 근심 어린 우아함이 뒤섞인 태도로 들었는데, 이것이 〈헤이워드〉를 자못 흥겹게 했으니, 그는 이것이 인디언의 관습에 대한 전적인 파격임을 잘 알고 있었던 것이다. 인디언의 관습은 그들의 전사가 어떤 하인 노릇으로든, 특히 여인들을 위해 몸을 낮추는 것을 금하고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 손님 접대의 예법은 그들 사이에서 신성하게 여겨졌으므로, 사내다움의 위엄에서 벗어난 이 작은 일탈은 입 밖에 낼 만한 어떤 평도 불러일으키지 않았다. 만약 그 자리에 면밀한 관찰자가 될 만큼 한가로운 이가 하나라도 있었다면, 그는 이 젊은 추장의 시중이 결코 한결같이 공평하지만은 않다고 여겼을지도 모른다. 그가 〈앨리스〉에게는 충분한 예를 갖춰 단물이 든 표주박과 후추나무 옹이를 깔끔하게 깎아 만든 나무 접시에 담긴 사슴 고기를 건넸으나, 그 언니에게 똑같은 수발을 들 때면 그의 검은 눈이 그녀의 풍부하고 표정이 살아 있는 얼굴 위에 머물렀으니 말이다. 한두 번은 그가 시중드는 이들의 주의를 끌고자 입을 열어야 했다. 그럴 때면 그는 영어를 썼는데, 띄엄띄엄 서툴렀으나 충분히 알아들을 만했고, 그 깊고 후음 섞인 목소리로 어찌나 부드럽고 가락 있게 들려주었던지, 두 숙녀가 감탄과 놀라움에 절로 고개를 들어 올려다보게 만들지 않은 적이 없었다. 이런 정중한 수발이 오가는 사이, 몇 마디 말이 주고받아졌으니, 그것은 양편 사이에 우호적인 교제의 외양을 세우는 데 보탬이 되었다.\n\n그러는 동안에도 〈칭가치국〉의 근엄함은 미동도 없이 그대로였다. 그는 좀 더 불빛이 닿는 안쪽으로 자리를 잡고 앉았는데, 그곳에서는 손님들이 자주 보내는 불안한 곁눈질로도 그의 얼굴의 천성 그대로의 표정을, 전쟁용 칠로 꾸며진 인위적인 공포에서 한결 수월하게 가려낼 수 있었다. 그들은 부자 사이에 짙은 닮음이 있음을 발견했으니, 다만 나이와 모진 고생에서 비롯되었으리라 짐작할 만한 차이가 있을 뿐이었다. 그의 얼굴의 사나움은 이제 잠든 듯했고, 그 자리에는, 인디언 전사가 그 능력을 자기 존재의 더 큰 어떤 목적에도 쓰지 않을 때 두드러지는, 그 조용하고 텅 빈 평정이 자리해 있었다. 그러나 그 검은 얼굴 위로 이따금 스치는 번뜩임으로 미루어, 그의 격정을 일깨우기만 하면 그가 적을 위압하려고 골라 쓴 그 무시무시한 분장이 곧장 온전한 효력을 발휘하리라는 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 반면에 척후의 기민하고 두리번거리는 눈은 좀처럼 한곳에 머물지 않았다. 그는 어떤 위험에 대한 감각도 흩뜨릴 수 없는 식욕으로 먹고 마셨으나, 그 경계심만은 결코 그를 떠나지 않는 듯했다. 스무 번이나 표주박이며 사슴 고기가 그의 입술 앞에서 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 그의 머리는 한쪽으로 돌아가, 마치 멀리서 들려오는 미덥지 못한 어떤 소리에 귀 기울이는 듯했으니, 이 동작은 손님들을 제 처지의 신기함을 들여다보던 데서 끌어내어, 그곳을 찾아 들게 만든 그 놀라운 까닭을 다시금 떠올리게 하지 않은 적이 없었다. 이 잦은 멈춤 뒤에 어떤 말도 뒤따르지 않았으므로, 그것이 자아낸 순간의 불안은 이내 사라졌고, 한동안은 잊히고 말았다.\n\n\"자, 친구.\" 식사가 끝나 갈 무렵, 〈호크아이〉가 나뭇잎으로 덮인 작은 통을 끄집어내며, 자기 옆에 앉아 그 요리 솜씨에 한껏 정성을 다해 주던 그 낯선 사내에게 말했다. \"가문비주를 좀 들어 보시오. 망아지 생각일랑 죄 씻어 내고, 가슴속 생기를 북돋워 줄 게요. 우리 더 깊은 우정을 위해 한잔하리다. 부디 말고기 한 점쯤이 우리 사이에 아무 앙금도 남기지 않기를 바라며. 자, 그래, 그쪽 이름은 어찌 되시오?\"\n\n\"〈개멋〉, 〈데이비드 개멋〉이라 하오.\" 그 성가 교사가 대답하며, 슬픔을 그 숲 사내의 향이 짙고 독한 술 한 모금에 흠뻑 씻어 내릴 채비를 했다.\n\n\"아주 좋은 이름이오, 단언컨대 정직한 조상들로부터 물려받은 게지. 나는 이름을 좋아하는 사람이오, 비록 이 점에서는 기독교도의 방식이 미개인의 관습에 한참 못 미치지만 말이오. 내가 아는 가장 큰 겁쟁이는 이름이 〈라이언〉이었소. 그리고 그 마누라 〈페이션스〉라는 여자는, 쫓기는 사슴이 한 로드 달려갈 짬도 안 되어, 사람을 귀가 멀도록 닦아세우곤 했지. 인디언한테는 이게 양심의 문제요. 자기를 일컫는 이름이, 대개 자기 자신과 같단 말이오. 〈칭가치국〉이라는 게 '큰 뱀'을 뜻한다 해서, 그가 정말로 크든 작든 뱀이라는 건 아니오. 다만 그가 인간 본성의 굽이굽이를 꿰뚫고 있으며, 입이 무겁고, 적이 가장 방심한 틈에 친다는 뜻이지. 그래, 그쪽 직업은 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"나는 시편창의 기예를 가르치는 변변찮은 교사요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\"\n\n\"나는 〈코네티컷〉 징모병의 젊은이들에게 노래를 가르치오.\"\n\n\"더 나은 일에 쓰일 수도 있었을 텐데. 그 젊은 사냥개들은 이미 숲을 지나며 웃고 노래하는 게 지나치다오, 굴속의 여우보다 더 크게 숨을 쉬어서도 안 될 판에 말이오. 그쪽은 활강총을 다룰 줄 아시오, 아니면 소총은 다룰 줄 아시오?\"\n\n\"하느님께 감사하게도, 나는 살인의 도구에 손댈 일이 한 번도 없었소!\"\n\n\"그럼 혹 나침반을 알아서, 황야의 물길과 산을 종이에 옮겨 그릴 줄 아시오? 뒤따라오는 이들이 정해진 이름으로 길을 찾을 수 있도록 말이오?\"\n\n\"나는 그런 일은 하지 않소.\"\n\n\"긴 길도 짧아 보이게 만들 다리 한 쌍은 갖췄구려! 그쪽은 이따금 장군께 전할 소식을 들고 다니기도 하는 모양이지?\"\n\n\"천만에. 나는 내 고귀한 천직 말고는 다른 무엇도 따르지 않소. 그건 성스러운 음악을 가르치는 일이오!\"\n\n\"참 이상한 직업이로군!\" 〈호크아이〉가 속으로 웃으며 중얼거렸다. \"다른 사람들 목구멍에서 우연히 나오는 온갖 오르내림을 흉내 내면서, 잿빛 개똥지빠귀처럼 한평생을 산다니. 글쎄, 친구, 내 짐작엔 그게 그쪽의 천부의 재능인 모양이니, 사격이든 무슨 더 나은 성향이든 그런 것이나 마찬가지로, 부정해서야 안 될 노릇이겠지. 그쪽이 그 방면에서 무엇을 할 수 있는지 한번 들어 봅시다. 그건 정겹게 밤 인사를 건네는 방편이 될 게요, 〈마쿠아〉들이 채 깨어나기도 전, 이른 아침의 자랑스러운 빛 속에 이 숙녀분들이 모질고 긴 행군을 위해 기운을 차려야 할 때가 되었으니 말이오!\"\n\n\"기쁘고 즐거운 마음으로 응하리다.\" 〈데이비드〉가 무쇠 테 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 그 아끼는 작은 책을 꺼내며 말했으니, 그는 그것을 곧장 〈앨리스〉에게 내밀었다. \"이토록 더없는 위태로움의 하루를 보낸 뒤에, 저녁 찬미를 올려 드리는 것보다 더 어울리고 더 위안이 되는 일이 어디 있겠소!\"\n\n〈앨리스〉가 미소 지었으나, 〈헤이워드〉를 바라보고는 얼굴을 붉히며 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"마음껏 하시오.\" 그가 속삭였다. \"이 시편 기자와 이름이 같은 이 가상한 분의 제안이, 이런 순간에 응당 무게를 지녀야 하지 않겠소?\"\n\n그의 의견에 용기를 얻어, 〈앨리스〉는 그녀의 경건한 성향과 부드러운 음률에 대한 예민한 애호가 진작부터 그토록 강하게 권해 온 일을 했다. 책은 그들 처지에 그리 어울리지 않지 않은 어느 찬송가에 펼쳐져 있었는데, 거기서 그 시인은 더 이상 영감을 받은 〈이스라엘〉의 왕을 능가하려는 욕망에 떠밀리지 않은 채, 다소 절제되고 점잖은 솜씨를 보여 주고 있었다. 〈코라〉도 동생을 거들 뜻을 내비쳤고, 꼼꼼한 〈데이비드〉가 음을 잡는 피리와 가락의 빼놓을 수 없는 사전 준비를 정성껏 마치자, 성스러운 노래가 시작되었다.\n\n가락은 엄숙하고 느렸다. 때로는 그것이 두 여인의 풍부한 목소리가 다다를 수 있는 가장 넓은 음역까지 솟구쳤으니, 그들은 거룩한 흥분 속에 그 작은 책 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었으며, 또 어떤 때는 너무도 낮게 가라앉아, 물의 내달리는 소리가 그들의 선율 사이로, 마치 텅 빈 반주처럼 흘러들었다. 〈데이비드〉의 타고난 안목과 정확한 귀가 그 소리를 다스리고 다듬어 이 좁다란 동굴에 맞추니, 그 동굴의 갈라진 틈과 구석마다 그들의 유연한 목소리에서 나온 떨리는 음들로 가득 찼다. 인디언들은 두 눈을 바위에 못 박은 채, 자신들을 돌로 바꿔 놓은 듯한 골똘함으로 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 차가운 무심함의 표정으로 턱을 손에 괴고 있던 척후는, 그 굳은 이목구비가 차츰 풀려, 한 절 한 절 이어지는 사이에, 자신의 무쇠 같은 천성이 누그러지는 것을 느꼈고, 그 기억은 어린 시절로 거슬러 올라갔으니, 그 무렵 그의 귀는 식민지의 정착촌에서 이와 비슷한 찬미의 소리에 귀 기울이는 데 익숙했던 것이다. 두리번거리던 그의 눈이 젖어 들기 시작했고, 찬송이 채 끝나기도 전에, 오랫동안 말라 있는 듯했던 샘에서 뜨거운 눈물이 솟아나, 어떤 나약함의 증표보다도 하늘의 폭풍을 더 자주 견뎌 온 그 두 뺨을 따라 줄지어 굴러내렸다. 노래하는 이들이, 마치 곧 그것을 잃으리란 것을 의식한 듯 귀가 그토록 탐욕스러운 황홀로 삼키는, 그 낮게 잦아드는 화음들 가운데 하나에 머물러 있을 때였다. 인간의 것도 이 세상의 것도 아닌 듯한 외침 하나가 바깥 공기 속에 솟아올라, 동굴 깊은 곳뿐 아니라 그것을 들은 모든 이의 가장 깊은 가슴속까지 꿰뚫었다. 그 뒤로는, 마치 그토록 끔찍하고 예사롭지 않은 방해에 물조차 그 사나운 내달림을 멈춘 듯, 깊디깊은 정적이 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"무슨 소리죠?\" 끔찍한 긴장의 몇 순간이 지난 뒤, 〈앨리스〉가 나직이 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 소리요?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 큰 소리로 되물었다.\n\n〈호크아이〉도 인디언들도 아무 대답이 없었다. 그들은 그 소리가 되풀이되기를 기다리는 듯, 그들 자신의 놀라움을 드러내는 태도로 귀를 기울였다. 마침내 그들은 〈델라웨어〉 말로 진지하게 함께 이야기를 나누었고, 그러자 〈언카스〉가 안쪽의 가장 깊이 감춰진 입구를 지나 조심스레 동굴을 빠져나갔다. 그가 떠나자, 척후가 먼저 영어로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이게 무엇인지, 무엇이 아닌지, 여기 있는 누구도 말할 수 없소. 우리 둘이 삼십 년 넘게 이 숲을 누비고 다녔는데도 말이오! 나는 인디언이나 짐승이 낼 수 있는 외침 가운데 내 귀가 들어 보지 못한 것은 없다고 믿어 왔소만, 이건 내가 한낱 헛되고 우쭐한 인간에 지나지 않았음을 증명해 보인 게요!\"\n\n\"그럼 그건, 전사들이 적을 위압하려 할 때 내지르는 그 함성이 아니었나요?\" 〈코라〉가 물었으니, 그녀는 베일을 제 몸에 둘러 모으며, 동요하는 동생으로서는 알지 못할 침착함으로 서 있었다.\n\n\"아니, 아니오. 이건 고약하고 소름 끼치는, 어딘가 사람의 것이 아닌 소리였소. 허나 일단 전쟁의 함성을 한번 들으면, 그건 결코 다른 무엇과도 혼동하지 않을 게요! 그래, 〈언카스〉!\" 그가 다시 들어온 젊은 추장에게 〈델라웨어〉 말로 말했다. \"무엇이 보이더냐? 우리 불빛이 담요 사이로 새어 나가더냐?\"\n\n대답은 짧았고, 같은 말로 주어졌는데, 보아하니 단호했다.\n\n\"바깥엔 아무것도 보이지 않는다는군.\" 〈호크아이〉가 못마땅한 듯 고개를 저으며 말을 이었다. \"우리 은신처는 아직 어둠 속에 있고! 자, 그래야 할 분들은 다른 동굴로 가서 잠을 청하시오. 우리는 해가 뜨기 한참 전에 일어나, 〈밍고〉들이 아침잠을 자는 동안 시간을 한껏 아껴 〈에드워드〉로 가야 하오.\"\n\n〈코라〉가 한결같은 태도로 본을 보여 따랐으니, 그것은 더 겁 많은 〈앨리스〉에게 순순히 따라야 할 필요를 일러 주었다. 그러나 그곳을 떠나기에 앞서, 그녀는 〈던컨〉에게 따라와 달라고 속삭여 청했다. 〈언카스〉가 그들이 지날 수 있도록 담요를 들어 올렸고, 자매가 이 살뜰한 배려에 고마움을 전하려 몸을 돌렸을 때, 그들은 척후가 다시 꺼져 가는 잉걸불 앞에 앉아, 얼굴을 두 손에 묻은 모습을 보았으니, 그것은 그가 저녁 기도를 깨뜨린 그 알 수 없는 방해를 얼마나 깊이 곱씹고 있는지를 드러내 보이는 모습이었다.\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 활활 타는 솔가지 매듭 하나를 들고 갔는데, 그것은 그들의 새로운 거처의 좁은 통로 사이로 어슴푸레한 빛을 던졌다. 그것을 알맞은 자리에 두고서, 그는 여인들에게로 갔으니, 그들은 〈에드워드〉 요새의 정다운 성벽을 떠난 이래 처음으로 그와 단둘이 있게 된 셈이었다.\n\n\"우리를 떠나지 마세요, 〈던컨〉.\" 〈앨리스〉가 말했다. \"그 끔찍한 외침이 아직도 귓가에 쟁쟁한데, 이런 데서야 우리가 어떻게 잠들 수 있겠어요!\"\n\n\"먼저 당신들의 요새가 얼마나 안전한지 살펴봅시다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그런 다음에 쉬는 얘기를 하지요.\"\n\n그는 동굴의 가장 안쪽 끝, 다른 출구들과 마찬가지로 담요로 가려진 한 출구로 다가가, 그 두꺼운 가리개를 걷어 내고는 폭포에서 불어오는 싱그럽고 생기를 되살려 주는 공기를 들이마셨다. 강의 한 줄기가, 그 물살이 무른 바위에 깎아 낸 깊고 좁은 협곡 사이로, 바로 그의 발밑을 흘러, 그가 믿기로는 그 방면에서 닥칠 어떤 위험에 대해서도 든든한 방어가 되어 주었으니, 그들로부터 몇 로드 위쪽에서 그 물은 더없이 사납고 어지럽게 곤두박질치고 번뜩이며 휩쓸려 내려갔다.\n\n\"이쪽엔 자연이 뚫을 수 없는 방벽을 세워 놓았소.\" 그는 담요를 내려놓기 전에, 깎아지른 비탈 아래 어두운 물살을 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"그리고 앞쪽에선 선량하고 미더운 사람들이 경계를 서고 있음을 당신들도 알 테니, 우리의 정직한 주인장의 충고를 거스를 까닭이 없을 것 같소. 두 분 다 잠이 필요하다는 말에는 〈코라〉도 분명 나와 뜻을 같이할 거요.\"\n\n\"〈코라〉는 당신 의견의 옳음에 따를 수는 있어도, 그것을 실행에 옮기지는 못할 것 같군요.\" 사사프라스로 만든 자리 위에 〈앨리스〉의 곁에 앉아 있던 언니가 대답했다. \"이 알 수 없는 소리의 충격을 면했다 한들, 잠을 쫓아낼 다른 까닭들이 있었을 거예요. 〈헤이워드〉, 스스로에게 물어보세요. 자기 자식들이 이런 황야 속, 그토록 많은 위험 한가운데서, 어디에 어떻게 머무는지조차 알지 못하는 아버지가 견뎌야 할 그 근심을, 딸들이 잊을 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"그분은 군인이오, 숲의 위험을 가늠할 줄 아시지.\"\n\n\"그분은 아버지예요, 그 본성을 부정하실 수는 없어요.\"\n\n\"내 온갖 철없는 짓에도 그분은 늘 얼마나 다정하셨던지! 내 모든 바람에 얼마나 자상하고 너그러우셨던지!\" 〈앨리스〉가 흐느꼈다. \"언니, 우린 이기적이었어, 이런 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 찾아뵙겠다고 졸랐으니!\"\n\n\"내가 그분이 무척 곤혹스러우신 순간에 허락을 졸라 댄 게 경솔했는지도 모르지. 하지만 나는 그분께 증명해 보이고 싶었어, 남들이 그 곤경에 처하신 그분을 저버린다 해도, 적어도 그분의 자식들만은 변치 않음을 말이야!\"\n\n\"그분께서 당신들이 〈에드워드〉에 닿았다는 소식을 들으셨을 때,\" 〈헤이워드〉가 다정하게 말했다. \"그 가슴속에선 두려움과 사랑 사이에 거센 싸움이 일었소. 비록 그토록 오랜 헤어짐으로 어쩌면 한층 더 깊어졌을 그 사랑이 이내 이겼지만 말이오. '저 아이들을 이끄는 건 내 고결한 〈코라〉의 기개일세, 〈던컨〉,' 그분께서 말씀하셨소. '나는 그걸 막지 않겠네. 우리 왕가의 군주의 명예를 그 손에 지키고 있는 자가 그 아이 굳셈의 절반만이라도 보여 준다면 오죽 좋으련만!'\"\n\n\"그래서 그분께서는 제 얘긴 안 하셨나요, 〈헤이워드〉?\" 〈앨리스〉가 시샘 어린 애정으로 물었다. \"분명, 그분께서 당신의 어린 〈엘시〉를 아주 잊지는 않으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"그건 있을 수 없는 일이오.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"그분께서는 당신을 천 가지 다정한 별명으로 부르셨는데, 내가 감히 입에 올릴 수는 없으나, 그 마땅함이야 내가 진심으로 증언할 수 있소. 정말이지 한번은 이렇게 말씀하셨소——\"\n\n〈던컨〉이 말을 멈췄다. 그의 두 눈이 〈앨리스〉의 눈에 못 박혀 있었는데, 그녀가 자식 된 애정의 간절함으로 그의 말을 받아들이려 그를 향해 몸을 돌리던 그때, 앞서와 똑같은 그 거세고 끔찍한 외침이 공기를 채워, 그를 벙어리로 만들어 버렸기 때문이다. 길고 숨 막히는 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그동안 저마다 그 소리가 되풀이되어 들릴까 두려운 기다림 속에 서로를 바라보았다. 마침내 담요가 천천히 들어 올려지더니, 입구에 척후가 서 있었으니, 그 얼굴의 굳셈은, 그의 온갖 꾀와 경험으로도 아무 소용이 없을 듯한 어떤 위험을 들이대는 그 수수께끼 앞에서, 분명 무너지기 시작하고 있었다.\n그들은 잠들지 않는다.\n\n저 너머 벼랑 위에, 소름 끼치는 무리,\n나는 그들이 앉아 있는 것을 본다.\n\n그레이\n\"이런 소리가 숲에 울려 퍼질 때 더 이상 숨어 있는다면, 우리를 위해 주어진 경고를 저버리는 일이 될 거요.\" 〈호크아이〉가 말했다. \"여린 분들은 안에 계셔도 되나, 〈모히칸〉들과 나는 저 바위 위에서 망을 보겠소. 60연대의 소령쯤이면 우리와 함께하고자 하시리라 짐작하오만.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우리가 처한 위험이 그토록 임박한 겁니까?\" 〈코라〉가 물었다.\n\n\"기이한 소리를 내어 그것으로 사람에게 알리는 그분만이 우리의 위험을 아실 따름이오. 이런 경고가 공중에 가득한데도 굴을 파고 들어앉는다면, 나는 그분의 뜻을 거역하는 사악한 자라 스스로를 여길 것이오! 날마다 노래로 세월을 보내는 저 연약한 영혼조차 그 외침에 마음이 흔들려, 제 말마따나 '싸움터로 나아갈 채비'가 된 듯하오. 그저 한 차례 싸움이라면야 우리 모두가 익히 아는 일이라 손쉽게 감당할 터이나, 이런 비명이 하늘과 땅 사이에 울릴 적엔 또 다른 종류의 전쟁을 알리는 거라고 들었소!\"\n\n\"우리가 두려워할 이유가 죄다 초자연적인 원인에서 비롯된 것뿐이라면, 친구여, 놀랄 까닭이 거의 없겠지요.\" 〈코라〉가 흐트러짐 없이 말을 이었다. \"우리의 적이 정복을 한결 수월하게 하려고, 우리에게 공포를 안기려 새롭고 교묘한 수법을 꾸며 낸 것은 아니라고 확신하시나요?\"\n\n\"아가씨.\" 척후가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"나는 삼십 년 동안 숲의 온갖 소리에 귀를 기울여 왔소. 제 목숨이 귀의 빠르기에 달린 사내가 그러하듯 말이오. 표범의 울부짖음도, 캣버드의 휘파람도, 사악한 〈밍고〉들의 그 어떤 술책도 나를 속일 수는 없소! 숲이 고통에 빠진 인간처럼 신음하는 것도 들어 보았고, 둘레의 껍질을 벗긴 나무 가지에서 바람이 제 음악을 연주하는 것도 몇 번이고 다시 들어 보았으며, 번개가 공중에서 불붙은 덤불이 탁탁 터지듯 갈라지며 불꽃과 갈라진 화염을 토해 내는 것도 들어 보았소. 허나 그 어느 것도 제 손으로 빚은 것들을 가지고 노시는 그분의 즐거움 이상의 무엇이라 여긴 적은 없었소. 허나 〈모히칸〉들도, 혼혈 한 점 섞이지 않은 백인인 나도, 방금 들은 그 외침만은 설명할 길이 없소. 그러므로 우리는 그것을 우리를 위해 주어진 징조라 믿는 거요.\"\n\n\"기이한 일이군요!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 안으로 들어오며 내려놓았던 자리에서 권총을 집어 들며 말했다. \"평화의 징조든 전쟁의 신호든, 살펴야 합니다. 앞장서시오, 친구. 내 따르리다.\"\n\n갇혀 있던 자리에서 나오자, 일행 모두는 답답한 은신처의 공기를 폭포의 소용돌이와 낙수 둘레에 감도는 서늘하고 생기를 돋우는 대기와 맞바꿈으로써, 단번에 기운을 되찾는 고마움을 느꼈다. 묵직한 저녁 산들바람이 강물의 표면을 따라 휩쓸며, 폭포의 포효를 제 동굴의 후미진 데로 몰아넣는 듯했으니, 그 소리는 멀리 언덕 너머에서 우르릉대는 천둥처럼 묵직하고 끊임없이 새어 나왔다. 달이 떠올라, 그 빛이 이미 그들 위쪽 물 위로 여기저기 어른거렸으나, 그들이 선 바위 끝자락은 아직 그늘 속에 잠겨 있었다. 쏟아지는 물이 내는 소리와, 변덕스러운 흐름을 타고 곁을 속삭이며 지나는 이따금의 숨결 같은 바람을 제외하면, 그 풍경은 밤과 적막이 빚어낼 수 있는 만큼 고요했다. 그들이 들은 그 방해의 정체를 밝혀 줄 어떤 생명의 기척이라도 찾으려, 저마다의 눈길이 맞은편 기슭을 따라 뻗었으나 헛수고였다. 근심에 차고 간절한 그들의 눈길은 속이는 빛에 농락당하거나, 헐벗은 바위와 곧고 미동도 없는 나무들에만 머물 뿐이었다.\n\n\"사랑스러운 저녁의 어둠과 고요 말고는 보이는 게 아무것도 없군요.\" 〈덩컨〉이 속삭였다. \"다른 때였다면 우리가 이런 풍경을, 숨 쉬는 듯한 이 모든 적막을 얼마나 값지게 여겼겠습니까, 〈코라〉! 안전하다고 상상해 보십시오. 그러면 지금은 당신의 공포를 키우는 것이, 어쩌면 즐거움으로 바뀔 수도 있을 텐데——\"\n\n\"들어 보세요!\" 〈앨리스〉가 말을 가로막았다.\n\n그 주의는 필요치 않았다. 또 한 번 같은 소리가 강바닥에서 솟아나는 듯 일어나, 벼랑의 좁은 경계를 뚫고 나와, 멀리 잦아드는 가락으로 숲을 가로질러 굽이치며 들려왔다.\n\n\"여기 이런 외침에 이름을 붙일 수 있는 이가 있소?\" 마지막 메아리가 숲속으로 사라지자 〈호크아이〉가 물었다. \"있다면, 말해 보시오. 나로서는 그것이 이 땅에 속한 것이라 보지 않소!\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 여기 당신의 오해를 풀어 줄 사람이 있소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"나는 그 소리를 아주 잘 압니다. 전쟁터에서, 그리고 군인의 삶에 흔한 여러 상황에서 자주 들어 보았으니까. 그것은 말이 단말마의 고통 속에서 내지르는 끔찍한 비명이오. 흔히는 고통에서 비롯되나, 때로는 공포에서 나오기도 하지요. 내 군마가 숲의 짐승들에게 먹잇감이 되었거나, 아니면 제 위험을 보면서도 피할 힘이 없는 거요. 동굴 안에서라면 그 소리에 속았을지 몰라도, 트인 바깥에서야 너무도 잘 알기에 틀릴 리 없소.\"\n\n척후와 그의 동료들은 이 단순한 설명을, 새로운 생각을 받아들이는 동시에, 거슬리는 식객임이 드러난 묵은 생각을 떨쳐 내는 자들의 흥미로 들었다. 진실이 처음 그들의 마음을 스치자, 뒤의 두 사람은 늘 하던 의미심장한 외마디 \"흥!\"을 내뱉었고, 앞의 사내는 잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤 스스로 대답을 맡았다.\n\n\"그대의 말을 부인할 수는 없소.\" 그가 말했다. \"말이 흔한 곳에서 태어났으되, 나는 말에 관해서는 별로 아는 게 없으니. 늑대들이 기슭에서 그것들의 머리 위를 맴돌고 있는 게 분명하고, 그 겁 많은 짐승들은 제가 할 수 있는 최선의 방식으로 사람에게 도움을 청하고 있는 거요. 〈웅카스〉,\"——그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 이야기했다——\"〈웅카스〉, 카누를 타고 내려가, 그 무리 한가운데로 횃불을 던져라. 안 그러면 늑대들이 못 해낼 짓을 공포가 해치워, 아침에 우리가 빠르게 길을 가야 할 마당에 말 없이 남겨 둘지도 모르니!\"\n\n젊은 원주민은 명을 따르려 이미 물가로 내려가 있었는데, 그때 길고 긴 울부짖음이 강가에서 일어나, 마치 짐승들이 제 발로, 갑작스러운 공포에 사로잡혀 먹잇감을 버리는 듯, 숲 깊은 데로 빠르게 실려 갔다. 〈웅카스〉는 본능적인 민첩함으로 물러섰고, 세 삼림의 사내들은 또 한 번 나직하고 진지한 논의를 나누었다.\n\n\"우리는 하늘의 방위를 잃고, 며칠 동안 해가 가려진 사냥꾼들 같았소.\" 〈호크아이〉가 동료들에게서 돌아서며 말했다. \"이제야 다시 우리가 갈 길의 징표를 알아보기 시작했고, 길에서 가시덤불이 걷혔구려! 저 너머 너도밤나무가 달빛에 드리운 그늘에 자리를 잡으시오——소나무 그늘보다 더 짙소——그리고 주께서 다음으로 보내고자 하시는 것을 기다립시다. 모든 말은 속삭임으로 하시오. 허나 한동안은 저마다 제 생각과 이야기를 나누는 편이 더 낫고, 어쩌면 끝내 더 현명한 일일 거요.\"\n\n척후의 태도는 진지하게 사람의 마음을 울렸으나, 더는 사내답지 못한 두려움의 기색이라곤 보이지 않았다. 제 경험으로도 헤아릴 수 없었던 수수께끼가 풀리자 그 한순간의 약함은 사라졌음이 분명했고, 이제 그들이 처한 실제 상황의 온갖 현실을 절감하면서도, 그 강인한 천성의 기개로 그것에 맞설 채비가 되어 있음이 또한 분명했다. 이 마음가짐은 원주민들에게도 공통된 듯했으니, 그들은 양쪽 기슭을 두루 굽어볼 수 있으면서도 제 몸은 남의 눈에 효과적으로 가려지는 자리에 들어앉았다. 이런 처지에서, 그토록 명민한 데서 비롯된 신중함을 〈헤이워드〉와 그 일행이 본받는 것은 마땅한 분별의 명령이었다. 청년은 동굴에서 사사프라스 한 무더기를 끌어내, 두 동굴을 가르는 틈에 깔아 두니, 자매가 그 자리를 차지하여, 그리하여 어떤 날아오는 물체로부터도 바위의 보호를 받게 되었고, 한편 어떤 위험도 경고 없이는 다가올 수 없다는 확신으로 그들의 근심도 덜어졌다. 〈헤이워드〉 자신은 가까이 자리를 잡았으니, 위험할 만큼 목소리를 높이지 않고도 동료들과 연락할 수 있을 만큼 바투였고, 한편 〈데이비드〉는 삼림의 사내들을 본떠, 그 볼품없는 사지가 더는 눈에 거슬리지 않도록 바위 틈새에 제 몸을 끼워 넣었다.\n\n이런 식으로 더 이상의 방해 없이 여러 시간이 흘렀다. 달이 천정에 이르러, 서로의 품에 안겨 평화로이 잠든 자매의 사랑스러운 모습 위로 그 부드러운 빛을 수직으로 쏟아 내렸다. 〈덩컨〉은 그토록 바라보기 좋아하던 그 광경 앞에 〈코라〉의 넓은 숄을 드리우고는, 자신의 머리도 바위에 베개를 찾게 했다. 〈데이비드〉는 좀 더 깨어 있는 순간이었다면 제 섬세한 감각에 충격을 주었을 법한 소리를 내기 시작했으니, 요컨대 〈호크아이〉와 〈모히칸〉들을 제외한 모두가 걷잡을 수 없는 졸음 속에 의식이라는 관념을 죄다 잃고 말았다. 그러나 이 빈틈없는 보호자들의 경계심은 지치지도 졸지도 않았다. 저마다 그 일부를 이루는 듯한 바위처럼 미동도 없이, 그들은 좁은 물줄기의 인접한 양 기슭을 두른 나무들의 어두운 가장자리를 따라, 쉼 없이 눈길을 굴리며 엎드려 있었다. 그들에게서는 소리 하나 새어 나오지 않았으니, 아무리 면밀히 살펴도 그들이 숨을 쉬는지조차 알 수 없었을 것이다. 이 지나친 신중함이, 적이 어떤 술책을 부려도 속을 수 없다는 경험에서 비롯되었음이 분명했다. 그러나 그것은 별다른 결과 없이 이어지다가, 마침내 달이 지고, 조금 아래 강이 굽이도는 데 나무 우듬지 위로 옅은 줄무늬가 떠올라 동이 가까워졌음을 알렸다.\n\n그제야 비로소 〈호크아이〉가 움직이는 것이 보였다. 그는 바위를 따라 기어가, 깊은 잠에 빠진 〈덩컨〉을 흔들어 깨웠다.\n\n\"이제 길을 떠날 때요.\" 그가 속삭였다. \"여린 분들을 깨우고, 내가 카누를 나루로 가져오거든 곧장 탈 채비를 하시오.\"\n\n\"조용한 밤을 보내셨소?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"나로 말하면, 잠이 내 경계심을 이긴 듯하오.\"\n\n\"아직 한밤중처럼 모든 게 고요하오. 잠자코, 허나 서둘러야 하오.\"\n\n이 무렵 〈덩컨〉은 완전히 잠에서 깨어, 즉시 잠든 여인들에게서 숄을 들어 올렸다. 그 움직임에 〈코라〉는 그를 밀어내려는 듯 손을 들었고, 한편 〈앨리스〉는 부드럽고 온화한 목소리로 중얼거렸다. \"아니에요, 아니에요, 아버지, 우리는 버림받지 않았어요. 〈덩컨〉이 우리와 함께 있었어요!\"\n\n\"그렇소, 사랑스러운 순결함이여.\" 청년이 속삭였다. \"〈덩컨〉이 여기 있고, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한, 위험이 남아 있는 한, 결코 그대 곁을 떠나지 않으리다. 〈코라〉! 〈앨리스〉! 일어나시오! 움직여야 할 때가 왔소!\"\n\n자매 가운데 동생이 내지른 날카로운 비명과, 언니가 어리둥절한 공포 속에 그의 앞에 꼿꼿이 일어선 모습이, 그가 받은 뜻밖의 대답이었다. 〈헤이워드〉의 입술에 아직 그 말이 남아 있을 때, 그토록 거센 함성과 외침의 소동이 일어나, 제 핏줄을 내달리던 빠른 핏물을 도로 심장의 샘으로 돌려보낼 지경이었다. 거의 한 순간 동안, 지옥의 악귀들이 그들 둘레의 공기를 사로잡고, 야만스러운 소리로 제 흉포한 기분을 토해 내는 듯했다. 그 외침은 어느 한 방향에서 오는 것이 아니었으나, 숲을 가득 메우고, 또 겁에 질린 청자들이 쉬이 상상했듯, 폭포의 동굴과 바위와 강바닥과 위쪽 공중까지 가득 채운 것은 분명했다. 〈데이비드〉가 그 지옥 같은 아우성 한가운데서 제 큰 키를 일으켜, 양손으로 두 귀를 막으며 외쳤다——\n\n\"이 불협화음은 어디서 오는 건가! 지옥이 풀려나기라도 했단 말인가, 사람이 이런 소리를 내다니!\"\n\n맞은편 기슭에서 십여 자루 소총의 환한 섬광과 빠른 발사음이, 그가 경솔하게 제 몸을 드러낸 데 뒤따라, 그 오래도록 잠들어 있던 바위 위에 가엾은 성가 선생을 정신없이 쓰러뜨려 놓았다. 〈모히칸〉들은 적들의 위협하는 함성을 대담하게 되받아쳤고, 적들은 〈거뮤트〉가 쓰러지자 야만스러운 승리의 함성을 질렀다. 그러고는 양편 사이에 소총의 섬광이 빠르고 가깝게 오갔으나, 어느 쪽도 적의 조준에 팔다리 하나조차 드러낼 만큼 미숙하지 않았다. 〈덩컨〉은 이제 도망만이 유일한 피난처라 믿으며, 노 젓는 소리를 간절히 마음 졸이며 들으려 했다. 강물은 여느 때의 속도로 미끄러져 갔으나, 그 어두운 물 위 어디에도 카누는 보이지 않았다. 척후가 무정하게도 자신들을 버렸다고 막 생각하던 차에, 그의 발밑 바위에서 한 줄기 화염이 솟아나고, 격렬한 함성에 단말마의 비명이 뒤섞여, 〈호크아이〉의 치명적인 무기에서 보내진 죽음의 사자가 제물을 찾았음을 알렸다. 이 가벼운 격퇴에 습격자들은 곧장 물러갔고, 차츰 그 자리는 갑작스러운 소동 이전처럼 고요해졌다.\n\n〈덩컨〉은 이 좋은 순간을 붙잡아 〈거뮤트〉의 몸으로 달려가, 그를 자매를 보호하던 좁은 틈의 은신처 안으로 옮겼다. 다시 한 순간 만에 일행 전부가 이 비교적 안전한 자리에 모여들었다.\n\n\"이 가엾은 친구가 머리 가죽은 지켰구려.\" 〈호크아이〉가 〈데이비드〉의 머리 위로 손을 차분히 쓸어 보며 말했다. \"허나 그는 혀가 너무 긴 채로 태어날 수 있다는 산 증거요! 헐벗은 바위 위에서 날뛰는 야만인들에게 6피트나 되는 살과 피를 내보이다니, 순전한 광기였소. 그저 목숨을 부지한 게 놀라울 따름이오.\"\n\n\"죽은 건 아니지요!\" 〈코라〉가 물었으니, 그 쉰 목소리는 타고난 공포가 짐짓 꾸민 의연함과 얼마나 거세게 다투는지를 드러냈다. \"이 가엾은 사람을 도울 길이 있을까요?\"\n\n\"아니오, 아니오! 아직 심장에 목숨이 붙어 있소. 한숨 자고 나면 제정신이 들 것이고, 진짜 때가 올 그 시각까지는 도리어 이로써 더 현명한 사내가 될 거요.\" 〈호크아이〉가 정신을 잃은 몸에 또 한 번 비스듬한 눈길을 던지며 대답했으니, 그러면서도 감탄스러운 정성으로 제 총에 화약을 채웠다. \"안으로 옮기게, 〈웅카스〉, 사사프라스 위에 눕히게. 잠이 길수록 그에게 좋을 거요. 이런 바위에서 저런 체격에 알맞은 가림막을 찾을 수 있을지 의심스럽고, 또 〈이로쿼이〉를 상대로는 노래도 아무 소용이 없으니.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 공격이 다시 시작되리라 보시오?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"굶주린 늑대가 한입으로 제 갈망을 채우리라 기대하겠소! 저들은 한 사람을 잃었고, 손실을 입거나 기습에 실패하면 물러서는 게 저들의 방식이오. 허나 우리를 속여 넘기고 우리 머리 가죽을 차지하려는 새로운 꾀를 가지고 다시 덤벼들 거요. 우리의 주된 희망은,\" 그가 그 거친 얼굴을 들며 말을 이었으니, 그 순간 어두워지는 구름처럼 근심의 그늘이 그 위를 스쳤다. \"〈먼로〉가 우리를 도울 부대를 보낼 때까지 이 바위를 지키는 것이오! 부디 그것이 곧, 인디언의 관습을 아는 지휘관 아래 이루어지기를 신께 비오!\"\n\n\"우리에게 닥칠 법한 운명을 들으셨겠지요, 〈코라〉.\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"그러니 당신 부친의 노심초사와 경험에 우리가 모든 것을 걸 수 있음을 아실 겁니다. 자, 그러면 〈앨리스〉와 함께 이 동굴로 드시오. 거기서 적어도 당신만은 우리 적들의 살기 어린 소총을 피해 안전할 것이고, 우리의 불행한 동료에게 당신들의 다정한 천성에 어울리는 보살핌을 베풀 수 있을 테니.\"\n\n자매는 그를 따라 바깥 동굴로 들어갔으니, 그곳에서는 〈데이비드〉가 한숨으로 의식이 돌아오는 징후를 보이기 시작하고 있었다. 그러고 나서 부상한 사내를 그들의 보살핌에 맡긴 뒤, 그는 즉시 그곳을 떠날 채비를 했다.\n\n\"〈덩컨〉!\" 그가 동굴 입구에 이르렀을 때 〈코라〉의 떨리는 목소리가 불렀다. 그가 돌아서서, 말을 건 이를 바라보니, 그 얼굴빛은 죽은 듯 창백하게 변했고, 입술은 떨리며, 그의 뒤를 좇는 그 눈길에는 곧장 그를 곁으로 다시 불러들이는 간절함이 어려 있었다. \"기억하세요, 〈덩컨〉, 당신의 안전이 우리 자신에게 얼마나 절실한지를——당신이 한 아버지의 신성한 부탁을 짊어지고 있음을——당신의 분별과 보살핌에 얼마나 많은 것이 달려 있는지를——요컨대,\" 그가 덧붙였으니, 속내를 드러내는 핏기가 그 이목구비 위로 번져 관자놀이까지 붉게 물들였다. \"당신이 〈먼로〉라는 이름을 지닌 모든 이에게 얼마나 마땅히 소중한 사람인지를요.\"\n\n\"내 비천한 목숨에 대한 애착에 무엇이든 더해질 수 있다면,\" 〈헤이워드〉가 무심결에 제 눈길이 말없는 〈앨리스〉의 앳된 모습으로 흘러가게 두며 말했다. \"그토록 다정한 다짐이야말로 바로 그것이겠지요. 60연대의 소령으로서, 우리의 정직한 주인장이 말해 주겠지만 나는 이 싸움에서 내 몫을 맡아야 합니다. 허나 우리의 임무는 수월할 거요. 그저 이 핏빛 사냥개들을 몇 시간 동안 막아 두기만 하면 되니까.\"\n\n대답을 기다리지 않고, 그는 자매의 곁에서 몸을 떼어 내, 두 동굴 사이의 작은 틈의 보호 안에 아직 엎드려 있던 척후와 그 동료들에게로 합류했다.\n\n\"내가 말하잖나, 〈웅카스〉.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 합류하자 앞의 사내가 말했다. \"자네는 화약을 낭비하고 있고, 소총의 반동이 자네 조준을 흐트러뜨리고 있어! 화약은 적게, 납탄은 가볍게, 총신은 길게, 그러면 〈밍고〉에게서 죽음의 비명을 끌어내는 데 좀처럼 실패하는 법이 없네! 적어도 그놈들에 관한 내 경험은 그러했지. 자, 친구들, 각자의 엄폐물로 갑시다. 〈마콰〉가 언제 어디서 일격을 날릴지는 아무도 모르니.\"\n* 마콰: 〈이로쿼이〉를 가리키는 다른 이름.\n\n인디언들은 말없이 제게 정해진 자리로 갔으니, 그것은 폭포 발치로 다가오는 길목을 굽어볼 수 있는 바위 틈새였다. 작은 섬 한가운데에는 키 작고 발육이 시원찮은 소나무 몇 그루가 뿌리를 내려 덤불을 이루고 있었는데, 〈호크아이〉가 사슴 같은 날렵함으로 그 안으로 뛰어들었고, 민첩한 〈덩컨〉이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 이곳에서 그들은, 그 자리에 흩어진 관목과 돌 부스러기 사이에, 형편이 허락하는 한 몸을 숨겼다. 그들 위로는 헐벗은 둥근 바위가 있었고, 그 양옆으로 물이 장난치며 놀다가 이미 묘사한 대로 아래쪽 심연으로 곤두박질쳤다. 이제 날이 밝았으므로, 맞은편 기슭은 더 이상 어수선한 윤곽으로 보이지 않았고, 그들은 숲속을 들여다보며 음울한 소나무의 차양 아래 사물들을 분간할 수 있었다.\n\n길고 근심 어린 망보기가 이어졌으나, 공격이 다시 시작되리라는 어떤 기색도 더는 없었다. 그리하여 〈덩컨〉은 자신들의 사격이 짐작보다 더 치명적이었고, 적들이 효과적으로 격퇴되었기를 바라기 시작했다. 그가 이 인상을 동료에게 감히 입에 올리자, 〈호크아이〉는 못 믿겠다는 듯 고개를 가로저으며 그것을 받았다.\n\n\"〈마콰〉가 머리 가죽도 없이 그토록 쉬이 쫓겨났다고 생각한다면, 그대는 그놈의 본성을 모르는 거요!\" 그가 대답했다. \"오늘 아침에 함성을 지른 놈이 하나라면, 마흔은 됐을 거요! 게다가 놈들은 우리의 수와 됨됨이를 너무도 잘 알아, 추격을 그리 빨리 포기하지 않소. 쉿! 위쪽 물을 보시오, 바로 물이 바위를 넘어 부서지는 데를. 저 무모한 악귀들이 바로 그 낙수 위로 헤엄쳐 내려와, 운수 사납게도 섬의 머리에 닿지 않았다면 내가 사람이 아니오. 쉿! 이보시오, 바싹 엎드리시오! 안 그러면 칼 한 번 돌리는 사이에 그대 정수리에서 머리털이 벗겨질 테니!\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉가 엄폐물에서 고개를 들어, 그가 마땅히 무모함과 솜씨의 경이라 여긴 것을 바라보았다. 강물이 무른 바위의 가장자리를 깎아 내어, 그 첫 낙수가 폭포에서 흔히 보이는 것보다 덜 가파르고 덜 수직이 되어 있었다. 물줄기가 섬의 머리와 만나는 데서 일렁이는 잔물결 말고는 다른 길잡이도 없이, 그 만족할 줄 모르는 적들의 한 무리가 물살 속으로 뛰어들어, 성공하기만 하면 노리는 제물에게로 손쉽게 다가갈 수 있음을 알고서, 이 지점으로 헤엄쳐 내려왔던 것이다. 〈호크아이〉가 말을 마치자, 이 헐벗은 바위에 걸린 떠내려온 통나무 몇 개 위로 네 개의 사람 머리가 빠끔히 내다보는 것이 보였으니, 그 통나무들이 아마도 그 위험천만한 거사가 해 볼 만하다는 생각을 일으켰을 것이다. 다음 순간, 섬의 선에서 조금 벗어나, 다섯 번째 형체가 폭포의 푸른 가장자리 위로 떠내려오는 것이 보였다. 그 야만인은 안전한 지점에 닿으려 힘껏 몸부림쳤고, 비스듬히 흐르는 물의 도움으로 이미 한 팔을 뻗어 동료들의 손을 잡으려던 차에, 소용돌이치는 물살에 다시 휩쓸려, 두 팔을 치켜들고 눈알이 튀어나온 채 공중으로 솟구치는 듯하더니, 둔탁한 첨벙 소리와 함께, 그 위를 맴돌던 깊고 입을 벌린 심연 속으로 떨어졌다. 동굴에서 외마디의 거칠고 절망적인 비명이 솟구쳤고, 모든 것은 다시 무덤처럼 잠잠해졌다.\n\n〈덩컨〉의 첫 의로운 충동은 그 가엾은 자를 구하러 달려가는 것이었으나, 미동도 없는 척후의 쇠 같은 손아귀에 그 자리에 붙들린 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"우리가 어디 엎드려 있는지 〈밍고〉들에게 알려, 우리에게 확실한 죽음을 가져오려는 거요?\" 〈호크아이〉가 준엄하게 따져 물었다. \"그건 화약 한 발 아낀 셈이고, 지금 탄약은 쫓기는 사슴에게 숨결만큼이나 귀하오! 권총의 점화약을 갈아 두시오——폭포의 물안개가 화약을 적시기 십상이니——그리고 내가 놈들의 돌격에 사격하는 동안, 백병전에 대비해 굳게 버티시오.\"\n\n그는 손가락을 입에 넣어, 길고 날카로운 휘파람을 불었고, 그것은 〈모히칸〉들이 지키던 바위에서 화답이 왔다. 이 신호가 공중에 울려 퍼지자 〈덩컨〉은 흩어진 떠내려온 통나무 위로 머리들이 어른거리는 것을 얼핏 보았으나, 그것들은 그의 눈에 비친 것만큼이나 갑작스레 다시 사라졌다. 다음으로 나직이 바스락거리는 소리가 등 뒤에서 그의 주의를 끌어, 고개를 돌리니, 몇 피트 안에서 〈웅카스〉가 제 곁으로 기어오는 것이 보였다. 〈호크아이〉가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 그에게 이야기하자, 젊은 추장은 유별난 신중함과 흐트러짐 없는 침착함으로 자리를 잡았다. 〈헤이워드〉에게 이것은 열에 들뜬 안달의 순간이었으나, 척후는 이 순간을 골라 제 손아래 동료들에게 화기를 분별 있게 다루는 기술을 일러 주는 적절한 기회로 삼는 것이 마땅하다고 보았다.\n\n\"모든 무기 가운데,\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"총신이 길고, 강선이 제대로 파였으며, 무른 금속으로 된 소총이 솜씨 좋은 손에서는 가장 무서운 것이오. 허나 그 온갖 아름다움을 죄다 발휘하려면 강한 팔과 빠른 눈, 그리고 장전에 큰 판단력이 필요하지. 총공이 사냥총이나 짧은 기병총 따위를 만들 때면, 제 일에 대한 안목이 거의 없는 게——\"\n\n그는 〈웅카스〉의 나직하나 의미심장한 \"흥!\" 소리에 말이 끊겼다.\n\n\"보인다, 얘야, 보여!\" 〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다. \"놈들이 돌격하려 모이고 있어, 안 그러면 그 거무튀튀한 등을 통나무 아래 감춰 두었을 테니. 좋다, 덤비라지.\" 그가 제 부싯돌을 살피며 덧붙였다. \"선두에 선 놈은, 설령 그게 〈몽칼름〉 본인이라 해도, 분명 제 죽음으로 다가오는 거야!\"\n\n그 순간 숲은 또 한 번의 함성으로 가득 찼고, 그 신호에 네 야만인이 떠내려온 통나무의 엄폐물에서 뛰쳐나왔다. 〈헤이워드〉는 그들을 맞으러 앞으로 달려 나가고픈 불타는 욕망을 느꼈으니, 그 순간의 광란 같은 조바심이 그토록 강렬했다. 그러나 척후와 〈웅카스〉의 침착한 본보기에 제지되었다. 자신들을 가르는 검은 바위를 길게 도약해 뛰어넘으며 더없이 사나운 함성을 내지르던 적들이 몇 로드 안에 들어왔을 때, 〈호크아이〉의 소총이 관목 사이에서 천천히 올라가, 그 치명적인 내용물을 쏟아 냈다. 맨 앞의 인디언이 화살 맞은 사슴처럼 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니, 섬의 갈라진 틈 사이로 곤두박질쳐 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"자, 〈웅카스〉!\" 척후가 제 긴 칼을 뽑으며 외쳤으니, 그 빠른 두 눈은 열의로 번득이기 시작했다. \"저 비명 지르는 악귀들의 마지막 놈을 맡아라. 나머지 둘은 우리가 확실히 처리하마!\"\n\n명대로 되었으니, 이제 물리쳐야 할 적은 둘뿐이었다. 〈헤이워드〉는 제 권총 한 자루를 〈호크아이〉에게 건넸고, 둘은 함께 적들을 향해 자그마한 비탈을 달려 내려가, 같은 순간에 무기를 발사했으나, 둘 다 똑같이 빗나갔다.\n\n\"내 알고 있었지! 그렇게 말했고!\" 척후가 그 멸시하던 작은 물건을 쓰디쓴 경멸로 폭포 너머로 내던지며 중얼거렸다. \"덤벼라, 이 피에 굶주린 지옥의 사냥개들아! 너희는 혼혈 한 점 없는 사내를 만났다!\"\n\n그 말이 채 떨어지기 무섭게, 그는 거대한 체구에 더없이 사나운 인상을 한 야만인과 맞붙었다. 같은 순간, 〈덩컨〉은 자신이 다른 한 놈과 비슷한 백병전에 휘말려 있음을 알아챘다. 〈호크아이〉와 그 적수는 능숙한 솜씨로 저마다 위험한 칼을 쥔 상대의 치켜든 팔을 움켜잡았다. 거의 한 순간 동안 그들은 서로의 눈을 들여다보며 서 있다가, 차츰 우위를 차지하려 제 근육의 힘을 끌어냈다. 마침내 백인의 단련된 힘줄이 덜 단련된 원주민의 팔다리를 이겨 냈다. 후자의 팔이 척후의 점점 커지는 힘 앞에 천천히 굴복했고, 척후는 별안간 무기를 쥔 손을 적의 손아귀에서 비틀어 빼내, 그 날카로운 무기를 헐벗은 가슴팍을 꿰뚫어 심장에 박았다. 그동안 〈헤이워드〉는 한층 죽음에 가까운 싸움에 몰려 있었다. 그의 가느다란 검은 첫 충돌에서 부러졌다. 다른 방어 수단이 전혀 없었으므로, 이제 그의 안전은 오로지 육체의 힘과 결의에 달려 있었다. 그 두 자질 어느 하나도 모자라지 않았으나, 그는 모든 면에서 자신과 대등한 적을 만났다. 다행히, 그는 곧 적수를 무장 해제하는 데 성공하여, 그 칼이 둘의 발치 바위 위로 떨어졌다. 그리고 이 순간부터 그것은 누가 상대를 그 아찔한 높이 너머 폭포의 이웃한 동굴 속으로 내던질 것인가를 두고 벌이는 사나운 다툼이 되었다. 거듭되는 몸싸움마다 둘은 그 가장자리로 점점 가까워졌으니, 〈덩컨〉은 거기서 최후의 결판이 나는 힘겨루기가 벌어지리라는 것을 알아챘다. 두 싸움꾼은 저마다 제 온 기력을 그 안간힘에 쏟아부었고, 그 결과 둘 다 벼랑 끝에서 비틀거렸다. 〈헤이워드〉는 제 목을 죄어 오는 상대의 손아귀를 느꼈고, 야만인이 짓는 험상궂은 미소를 보았으니, 그것은 제 몸이 거역할 수 없는 힘에 천천히 굴복하는 것을 느끼며, 제 적을 제 운명과 같은 곳으로 몰아넣으리라는 복수에 찬 희망에서 나온 것이었고, 청년은 그런 순간의 스쳐 가는 고뇌를 그 온갖 끔찍함 속에서 겪었다. 그 극도의 위험의 순간에, 검은 손 하나와 번득이는 칼 한 자루가 그의 앞에 나타났다. 손목의 여러 힘줄 둘레에서 피가 콸콸 쏟아지자 인디언은 잡았던 손을 놓았다. 그리고 〈웅카스〉의 구해 주는 팔에 〈덩컨〉이 뒤로 끌려가는 동안, 그의 홀린 눈은 사납고 낭패한 적의 얼굴에 여전히 못 박혀 있었으니, 그 적은 침울하게, 낭패한 채로, 돌이킬 수 없는 벼랑 아래로 떨어졌다.\n\n\"엄폐물로! 엄폐물로!\" 마침 그때 제 적을 처치한 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"엄폐물로, 목숨이 아깝거든! 일은 이제 절반밖에 안 끝났소!\"\n\n젊은 〈모히칸〉이 승리의 함성을 질렀고, 〈덩컨〉을 뒤따르게 하여, 싸우러 내려갔던 비탈을 미끄러지듯 올라가, 바위와 관목의 정다운 은신처를 찾아들었다.\n\n그들은 아직 머뭇거리고 있다,\n제 고향 땅의 복수자들이.\n\n그레이\n척후의 경고하는 외침은 까닭 없이 나온 것이 아니었다. 방금 이야기한 치명적인 격투가 벌어지는 동안, 폭포의 포효는 그 어떤 인간의 소리에도 끊기지 않았다. 강 건너편 기슭의 원주민들은 그 결말에 쏠린 관심으로 숨죽인 채 마음을 졸였던 듯하고, 한편 싸우는 자들의 위치가 재빠르게 바뀌고 휙휙 옮겨 다니는 통에 자칫 아군에게도 적군에게도 위험할 수 있는 사격은 사실상 막혀 버렸다. 그러나 승부가 결판난 순간, 사납고 흉포하기가, 광포하고 복수에 찬 격정이 허공으로 내지를 수 있는 한껏의 함성이 솟구쳤다. 뒤이어 소총들이 잽싸게 번쩍이며, 마치 공격자들이 이 치명적인 싸움이 벌어진 무감각한 현장에 부질없는 분노를 쏟아붓기라도 하려는 듯, 일제히 납빛 사자(使者)들을 바위 너머로 날려 보냈다.\n\n이에 대해, 싸움 내내 흔들림 없는 결의로 제 자리를 지켜 온 〈칭가치국〉의 소총에서 꾸준하면서도 신중한 응사가 이루어졌다. 〈언카스〉의 승리에 찬 환호가 그의 귀에 실려 오자, 흡족해진 아비는 한 번의 호응하는 외침으로 목소리를 높였고, 그 뒤로는 분주한 그의 총만이, 그가 여전히 지칠 줄 모르는 부지런함으로 제 길목을 지키고 있음을 증명했다. 이런 식으로 여러 분이 생각만큼이나 빠르게 흘러갔으니, 공격자들의 소총은 때로 우두둑 일제히 울리고, 때로는 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 총성으로 말했다. 바위와 나무와 덤불이 포위당한 자들 주위 백 군데에서 찢기고 갈라졌으나, 그들의 엄폐는 어찌나 빈틈없고 또 어찌나 철저히 유지되었던지, 아직까지는 이 작은 무리 가운데 〈데이비드〉만이 유일한 피해자였다.\n\n\"녀석들이 화약을 실컷 태우게 두시오.\" 신중한 척후가, 그가 안전히 엎드린 자리 옆으로 총알이 잇따라 윙윙 스쳐 가는 동안 말했다. \"다 끝나고 나면 납을 한바탕 그러모으게 될 게요. 그리고 내 짐작엔, 이 오래된 돌들이 자비를 구하짖기도 전에 저 도깨비 같은 것들이 이 놀음에 먼저 지칠 거요! 〈언카스〉, 얘야, 넌 약을 너무 많이 재서 알맹이를 낭비하는구나. 그리고 반동이 큰 소총은 결코 총알을 곧게 보내지 못한다. 저 껑충대는 악당을 흰 칠한 선 아래로 겨누라 일렀거늘, 네 총알이 머리카락 한 올만큼 빗나갔다면, 그 선보다 두 치는 위로 갔을 게다. 〈밍고〉는 목숨이 아래쪽에 깃들어 있으니, 인정(人情)이 우리에게 가르치는 바, 그 뱀들은 단숨에 끝장을 내야 하는 법이다.\"\n\n이 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 오만한 이목구비에 잔잔한 미소가 어렸으니, 그가 영어를 알아들었을 뿐 아니라 상대의 말뜻도 알아챘음을 드러냈다. 그러나 그는 변명도 대꾸도 없이 그 미소를 흘려보냈다.\n\n\"나는 당신이 〈언카스〉를 두고 판단력이 모자라다거나 솜씨가 없다고 탓하는 것을 그냥 둘 수 없소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"그는 더없이 침착하고 더없이 기민하게 내 목숨을 구해 주었고, 자신이 진 빚을 결코 다시 일깨워 줄 필요가 없을 벗을 하나 얻은 것이오.\"\n\n〈언카스〉가 몸을 반쯤 일으켜, 〈헤이워드〉가 마주 쥐도록 손을 내밀었다. 이 우정의 행위가 오가는 동안, 두 젊은이는 서로 뜻이 통하는 눈빛을 주고받았으니, 그로 인해 〈덩컨〉은 이 야생의 동행이 지닌 본성과 처지를 잊고 말았다. 그사이, 이 젊은이다운 감정의 분출을 차분하면서도 다정한 눈길로 지켜보던 〈호크아이〉가 다음과 같이 응답했다.\n\n\"황야에서는 목숨이라는 것이 벗들끼리 흔히 서로 지는 빚이지. 내 감히 말하건대, 나도 전에 〈언카스〉에게 그런 신세를 한두 번쯤 갚아 준 적이 있을 거요. 또 저 친구가 다섯 번이나 나와 죽음 사이를 막아서 주었던 것을 똑똑히 기억하고 있소. 세 번은 〈밍고〉의 손에서, 한 번은 〈호리컨〉을 건너다가, 그리고—\"\n\n\"저 총알은 여느 때보다 잘 겨눈 것이군!\" 〈덩컨〉이, 그의 곁 바위에 날카로운 반동으로 부딪친 한 발에 저도 모르게 움츠리며 외쳤다.\n\n〈호크아이〉가 그 형체 잃은 쇳덩이에 손을 얹고, 그것을 살피며 고개를 저었다. \"떨어지는 납은 결코 납작해지지 않는 법이오! 구름에서 내려왔다면 이렇게 됐을 수도 있겠지만!\"\n\n그러나 〈언카스〉의 소총이 신중하게 하늘을 향해 들렸으니, 동료들을 한 지점으로 이끌었고, 거기서 그 수수께끼가 곧장 풀렸다. 그들의 자리와 거의 마주 보는 강 오른쪽 기슭에 우람한 떡갈나무 한 그루가 자라 있었는데, 트인 공간의 자유를 좇아 너무 앞으로 기울어진 나머지, 그 위쪽 가지들이 제 기슭에 가장 가까이 흐르는 물줄기 위로 드리워져 있었다. 옹이지고 발육이 멈춘 가지들을 듬성듬성 가린 가장 높은 잎새들 사이에, 한 야만인이 둥지를 틀듯 엎드려 있었으니, 일부는 나무줄기에 가려지고 일부는 드러난 채로, 마치 제 사격의 효과를 확인하려 그들을 내려다보는 듯했다.\n\n\"이 악마들이 우리를 옴짝달싹 못 하게 파멸로 몰아넣으려고 하늘까지 기어오르겠군.\" 〈호크아이〉가 말했다. \"얘야, 내가 '킬디어'를 겨눌 수 있을 때까지 저놈을 붙들어 두어라. 그러면 나무 양옆에서 동시에 저놈의 배짱을 시험해 보자꾸나.\"\n\n〈언카스〉는 척후가 말을 뱉을 때까지 사격을 늦추었다. 두 소총이 번쩍였고, 떡갈나무의 잎새와 껍질이 허공으로 튀어 올라 바람에 흩어졌으나, 그 인디언은 그들의 공격에 비웃는 웃음으로 답하며, 답례로 또 한 발을 내리꽂아 〈호크아이〉의 머리에서 모자를 쳐냈다. 또 한 번 야만의 함성이 숲에서 터져 나왔고, 납빛 우박이 포위당한 자들의 머리 위로 휘파람 소리를 내며 날았으니, 마치 나무에 오른 전사의 모험에 손쉬운 제물이 될 만한 곳에 그들을 가두어 두려는 듯했다.\n\n\"이건 어떻게든 손을 써야겠소!\" 척후가 근심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"〈언카스〉, 네 아버지를 불러라. 저 교활한 짐승을 횃대에서 끌어내리자면 우리 무기를 죄다 동원해야겠다.\"\n\n신호는 즉시 전해졌고, 〈호크아이〉가 소총을 미처 다시 장전하기도 전에 그들은 〈칭가치국〉과 합류했다. 아들이 노련한 전사에게 위험한 적의 위치를 가리켜 보이자, 노련한 전사의 입술에서 여느 때처럼 감탄사 \"휴!\"가 터져 나왔다. 그 뒤로는 놀람이나 경악의 기색을 그 어떤 것도 새어 나오게 하지 않았다. 〈호크아이〉와 〈모히칸〉 부자는 잠시 〈델라웨어〉 말로 진지하게 이야기를 나누었고, 이윽고 저마다 황급히 짜낸 계획을 실행하고자 조용히 제 자리를 잡았다.\n\n떡갈나무에 오른 전사는 발각된 순간부터, 효과는 없을지언정 잰 사격을 이어 갔다. 그러나 그의 조준은 적들의 경계심에 가로막혔으니, 적들의 소총은 그의 몸 어느 부분이든 드러나는 즉시 그리로 겨누어졌다. 그래도 그의 총알은 웅크린 무리의 한복판에 떨어졌다. 〈헤이워드〉를 유난히 눈에 띄게 만드는 그 옷은 거듭 찢겼고, 한 번은 팔에 입은 가벼운 상처에서 피가 흘렀다.\n\n마침내, 적들의 길고 끈질긴 경계에 대담해진 〈휴런〉은 더 정확하고 더 치명적인 조준을 시도했다. 〈모히칸〉 부자의 잰 눈이, 나무줄기에서 몇 치 떨어진 곳에서 성긴 잎새 사이로 부주의하게 드러난 그의 하반신의 검은 윤곽을 포착했다. 그들의 소총이 한 발의 총성으로 울렸을 때, 그 야만인이 다친 다리에 무게가 실려 주저앉으며 몸의 일부가 시야에 들어왔다. 생각만큼이나 빠르게, 〈호크아이〉가 그 기회를 붙잡아 떡갈나무 꼭대기에 치명적인 무기를 발사했다. 잎새들이 유난히 요동쳤고, 위험한 소총이 그 위압적인 높이에서 떨어졌으며, 잠시 동안의 헛된 발버둥 끝에, 그 야만인의 몸이 바람에 흔들리는 것이 보였으니, 그는 여전히 절망 속에 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 나무의 앙상하게 벗겨진 가지를 붙들고 있었다.\n\n\"저자에게, 제발 저자에게—다른 소총의 한 발을 먹이시오!\" 〈덩컨〉이, 동족 하나가 그토록 끔찍한 위기에 처한 광경에서 공포에 질려 눈을 돌리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"한 알도 못 쓰오!\" 완강한 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"저자의 죽음은 정해진 일이고, 우리에겐 낭비할 화약이 없소. 인디언과의 싸움은 때로 며칠씩 가는 법이니까. 저들의 머리 가죽이냐, 우리 것이냐의 일이오!—그리고 우리를 지으신 하느님께서 우리 본성 속에, 제 머리에 가죽을 지켜 두려는 갈망을 심어 놓으셨소!\"\n\n이토록 눈에 보이는 타산(打算)이 뒷받침하는, 이 준엄하고 굽힐 줄 모르는 도덕률에는 항변할 여지가 없었다. 그 순간부터 숲의 함성이 다시 한번 그쳤고, 사격은 잦아들도록 내버려졌으며, 아군이든 적군이든 모든 눈이 하늘과 땅 사이에 매달린 그 가련한 자의 가망 없는 처지에 못 박혔다. 몸은 바람의 흐름에 내맡겨졌고, 그 희생자에게서 어떤 신음이나 끙끙대는 소리도 새어 나오지 않았으나, 어떤 순간에는 그가 침울하게 적들을 마주 보았으니, 사이에 가로놓인 거리 너머로도, 차디찬 절망의 고뇌가 그 거무스레한 이목구비를 사로잡고 있음을 알아볼 수 있었다. 척후는 자비심에서 세 차례나 총을 들었고, 그때마다 신중함이 그 의도를 이겨, 총은 다시 말없이 내려졌다. 마침내 〈휴런〉의 한 손이 잡은 것을 놓치고, 기진하여 옆구리로 떨어졌다. 가지를 다시 붙들려는 필사적이면서도 헛된 몸부림이 뒤따랐고, 이윽고 그 야만인이, 허공을 미친 듯이 움켜쥐며 한순간 덧없이 보였다. 〈호크아이〉의 소총에서 인 불꽃은 번개보다도 빨랐다. 희생자의 사지가 떨리며 오그라들었고, 머리가 가슴께로 떨어졌으며, 몸은 거품 이는 물살을 납덩이처럼 가르고 들었으니, 물은 그 끊임없는 빠르기로 그 위에서 다시 닫혔고, 그 불행한 〈휴런〉의 자취는 모조리 영영 사라지고 말았다.\n\n이 중대한 우세에 어떤 승리의 함성도 뒤따르지 않았으니, 〈모히칸〉 부자조차 말없는 공포 속에 서로를 응시했다. 외마디 함성이 숲에서 터져 나왔고, 모든 것이 다시 고요해졌다. 이 일에 홀로 따져 보는 듯하던 〈호크아이〉는 제 한순간의 나약함에 고개를 저었으며, 제 자책의 말을 소리 내어 뱉기까지 했다.\n\n\"내 화약통에 남은 마지막 분량이었고, 내 주머니에 남은 마지막 총알이었는데, 그게 풋내기나 할 짓이었어!\" 그가 말했다. \"저자가 산 채로 바위에 부딪치든 죽어서 부딪치든 그게 무슨 대수였겠나. 어차피 곧 감각도 사라질 텐데. 〈언카스〉, 얘야, 카누로 내려가서 큰 화약뿔을 가져오너라. 우리에게 남은 화약이라곤 그게 전부고, 마지막 한 알까지 다 써야 할 게다. 안 그러면 내가 〈밍고〉의 본성을 모르는 셈이지.\"\n\n젊은 〈모히칸〉은 그 말을 따랐고, 척후는 제 주머니의 쓸모없는 내용물을 뒤적이며 빈 화약뿔을 새삼스레 못마땅한 듯 흔들고 있었다. 그러나 그는 이 만족스럽지 못한 점검에서 곧, 〈언카스〉가 지르는 크고 날카로운 외침에 불려 나왔으니, 그 소리는 군사에 서툰 〈덩컨〉의 귀에마저, 어떤 새롭고 뜻밖의 재앙을 알리는 신호로 들렸다. 동굴 속에 감춰 둔 소중한 보물에 대한 염려로 온통 가득 찬 채, 젊은이는 그렇게 몸을 드러냄으로써 무릅쓰는 위험은 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고 벌떡 일어났다. 마치 한 가지 충동에 떠밀린 듯, 그의 동작은 동료들에게도 그대로 따라졌고, 그들은 함께, 적들의 흩어진 사격이 완전히 무용지물이 될 만큼 빠르게, 그 다정한 협곡을 향해 길목을 달려 내려갔다. 이 예사롭지 않은 외침이 자매를, 부상당한 〈데이비드〉와 함께 그들의 피난처에서 불러냈다. 그리고 그 무리 전체가, 단 한 번의 눈길로, 자기네 젊은 인디언 보호자의 단련된 극기심마저 흔들어 놓은 그 참사의 정체를 알게 되었다.\n\n바위에서 얼마 떨어지지 않은 곳에, 그들의 작은 배가 소용돌이를 가로질러 강의 빠른 물살을 향해 떠가는 것이 보였으니, 그 진로가 어떤 보이지 않는 손에 이끌리고 있음을 증명하는 모양새였다. 이 달갑잖은 광경이 척후의 눈에 들어온 순간, 그의 소총이 본능에 이끌리듯 겨누어졌으나, 총신은 부싯돌의 밝은 불꽃에 아무런 응답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"너무 늦었어, 너무 늦었어!\" 〈호크아이〉가 쓰라린 실망 속에 쓸모없어진 총을 떨구며 외쳤다. \"저 악당이 급류를 탔으니, 우리에게 화약이 있다 한들 지금 저자가 가는 것보다 빠르게 납을 보내기도 어렵겠소!\"\n\n그 대담한 〈휴런〉이 카누의 가림막 위로 고개를 들었고, 배가 물줄기를 따라 쏜살같이 미끄러져 내려가는 동안, 그는 손을 흔들며, 알려진 성공의 신호인 함성을 내질렀다. 그의 외침에는 숲에서 일어난 함성과 웃음이 응답했으니, 마치 쉰 마리의 악마가 어느 기독교도 영혼의 추락에 신성모독의 말을 내뱉기라도 하듯, 약 올리며 의기양양했다.\n\n\"실컷 웃으려무나, 이 악마의 자식들아!\" 척후가 바위의 튀어나온 곳에 주저앉으며, 제 총이 발치에 내버려진 채 떨어지도록 두고 말했다. \"이 숲에서 가장 빠르고 가장 정확한 소총 세 자루가, 그저 우단담배풀 줄기 몇 가닥이나 작년에 떨어진 수사슴 뿔 나부랭이만도 못하게 됐으니!\"\n\n\"어떻게 해야 하오?\" 〈덩컨〉이, 처음의 실망감을 보다 사내다운 분발의 욕구로 떨쳐 내며 물었다. \"우리는 어찌 되는 거요?\"\n\n〈호크아이〉는 다른 대답 대신, 제 머리 정수리 둘레로 손가락을 빙 둘렀으니, 그 동작이 어찌나 의미심장했던지 그것을 본 사람이라면 누구도 그 뜻을 오해할 수 없었다.\n\n\"설마, 설마 우리 처지가 그토록 절망적이지는 않겠지!\" 젊은이가 외쳤다. \"〈휴런〉들은 여기 없잖소. 우리가 동굴을 굳게 지킬 수도, 그들의 상륙을 막을 수도 있지 않소.\"\n\n\"무엇으로?\" 척후가 차갑게 물었다. \"〈언카스〉의 화살이나, 여인들이 흘리는 그런 눈물로? 아니, 아니지. 당신은 젊고, 부유하고, 벗들도 있으니, 그런 나이에 죽기란 힘든 일임을 나도 잘 아오! 하지만,\" 〈모히칸〉 부자에게 눈길을 던지며 그가 말했다. \"우리가 한 점 섞임 없는 사내들임을 기억하고, 정해진 시각이 오면 흰 피도 붉은 피만큼이나 거침없이 흐를 수 있음을 이 숲의 토박이들에게 가르쳐 줍시다.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉이 상대가 눈으로 가리킨 방향으로 재빨리 돌아섰다가, 그 인디언들의 거동에서 자신의 가장 나쁜 우려가 사실임을 읽었다. 〈칭가치국〉은 바위의 또 다른 조각 위에 위엄 있는 자세로 자리 잡고 앉아, 이미 칼과 토마호크를 내려놓은 채, 제 머리에서 독수리 깃을 빼고 그 마지막의 끔찍한 소임을 치를 채비로 외따로 남은 한 줌의 머리채를 매만지고 있었다. 그의 표정은 생각에 잠긴 듯하면서도 차분했으며, 그 검고 번득이는 두 눈은 전투의 사나움을 차츰 잃고, 그가 머지않아 겪으리라 예상하는 변화에 한결 어울리는 표정으로 옮아 가고 있었다.\n\n\"우리 처지는 그렇지 않소, 그토록 가망 없을 리 없소!\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"바로 이 순간에도 구원이 가까이 있을지 모르오. 적이라곤 하나도 보이지 않잖소! 그토록 얻을 것 적은 일에 그토록 많은 것을 걸어야 하는 싸움에 저들도 진력이 난 게요!\"\n\n\"저 교활한 뱀들이 우리에게 살그머니 다가오기까지는 일 분이 걸릴 수도, 한 시간이 걸릴 수도 있소. 그리고 바로 이 순간에도 우리 말소리가 들리는 거리에 엎드려 있는 것이 저들에겐 지극히 당연한 일이지.\" 〈호크아이〉가 말했다. \"허나 저들은 올 게요, 그것도 우리에게 바랄 것이라곤 아무것도 남기지 않을 그런 식으로! 〈칭가치국〉\"—그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 말했다—\"형제여, 우리는 마지막 싸움을 함께 치렀고, 〈마쿠아〉들은 〈모히칸〉의 현명한 사내와, 밤을 낮처럼 만들고 구름을 샘의 안개만큼 낮게 끌어내릴 수 있는 눈을 가진 창백한 얼굴의 죽음에 의기양양하겠지!\"\n\n\"〈밍고〉의 여인들더러 제 죽은 자들이나 곡하게 두라지!\" 인디언이 특유의 긍지와 흔들림 없는 굳셈으로 응답했다. \"〈모히칸〉의 위대한 뱀이 그들의 천막 속에 똬리를 틀어, 아비가 돌아오지 않은 아이들의 통곡으로 그들의 승리에 독을 풀어 놓았다! 눈이 녹은 이래로 열한 명의 전사가 제 부족의 무덤에서 멀리 숨겨졌으니, 〈칭가치국〉의 혀가 잠잠해지면 그들을 어디서 찾을지 아무도 일러 주지 못하리라! 그들더러 가장 날카로운 칼을 뽑고, 가장 빠른 토마호크를 휘두르게 하라. 그들의 가장 지독한 원수가 그들 손안에 있으니. 〈언카스〉야, 고귀한 줄기의 가장 높은 가지야, 저 겁쟁이들에게 서두르라 외쳐라. 안 그러면 그들의 마음이 약해져, 여인으로 변해 버릴 테니!\"\n\n\"그들은 제 죽은 자들을 물고기들 틈에서 찾고 있다!\" 젊은 족장의 낮고 부드러운 목소리가 응답했다. \"〈휴런〉들은 미끈거리는 뱀장어들과 함께 떠다닌다! 그들은 먹기 알맞게 익은 열매처럼 떡갈나무에서 떨어진다! 그리고 〈델라웨어〉들은 웃는다!\"\n\n\"옳지, 옳아.\" 이 원주민들의 유별난 토로를 깊은 주의를 기울여 듣던 척후가 중얼거렸다. \"저들이 인디언다운 감정을 달구어 놓았으니, 곧 〈마쿠아〉들을 들쑤셔 자기들에게 잽싼 최후를 안기게 하겠군. 나로 말하자면, 순전히 백인의 피를 받은 사람이니, 내 빛깔에 어울리게, 입에 비웃는 말 한마디 없이, 또 마음에 쓰라림 없이 죽는 것이 마땅하지!\"\n\n\"어찌하여 죽기까지 한단 말인가요!\" 〈코라〉가, 이 순간까지 본능적인 공포로 자신을 바위에 못 박아 두었던 자리에서 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"사방으로 길이 열려 있어요. 그러니 숲으로 달아나 하느님께 구원을 청하세요. 가세요, 용감한 분들이여, 우리는 이미 당신들에게 너무 많은 빚을 졌어요. 더는 우리의 불운한 운명에 당신들을 끌어들이지 말게 해 주세요!\"\n\n\"〈이로쿼이〉의 간계를 너무 모르는 말씀이오, 아가씨, 저들이 숲으로 가는 길을 열어 두었으리라 여기신다면!\" 〈호크아이〉가 응답했으나, 이내 그 단순한 성품대로 덧붙였다. \"물길을 따라 내려가는 물살이라면, 우리를 저들의 소총이나 목소리가 닿지 않는 데까지 곧 쓸어 가 줄 게 분명하지만.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 강을 시도해 보세요. 어찌하여 머뭇거리며 우리의 무자비한 적들의 제물 수를 늘리려 하시나요?\"\n\n\"어찌하여라니,\" 척후가 자랑스레 주위를 둘러보며 되뇌었다. \"사람이란 사악한 양심에 시달리며 사느니 제 마음과 평화로이 죽는 편이 낫기 때문이오! 우리가 〈먼로〉에게, 그분이 어디서 또 어떻게 제 자식들을 두고 왔느냐 물으신다면, 무어라 답할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그분께 가서, 그들을 구하러 서둘러 와 달라는 전갈과 함께 두고 왔다고 전하세요.\" 〈코라〉가 너그러운 열의로 척후에게 더 가까이 다가서며 응답했다. \"〈휴런〉들이 그들을 북쪽 황야로 데려가지만, 빈틈없이 서두른다면 아직 구해 낼 수 있다고요. 그리고 만에 하나, 끝내 하늘의 뜻으로 그분의 도움이 너무 늦게 닿는다면, 그분께 전해 주세요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었으니, 그 목소리는 차츰 낮아져 거의 목이 메는 듯했다. \"딸들의 사랑과, 축복과, 마지막 기도를요. 그리고 그들의 이른 운명을 슬퍼하지 마시고, 겸손한 믿음으로 자식들을 만날 기독교도의 종착지를 바라보시라 일러 주세요.\"\n\n척후의 모질고 풍상에 시달린 이목구비가 씰룩이기 시작했고, 그녀가 말을 마치자 그는 그 제안의 본질을 깊이 곱씹는 사람처럼 턱을 손에 떨구었다.\n\n\"저 여인의 말에는 일리가 있어!\" 마침내 그의 앙다물려 떨리는 입술에서 말이 터져 나왔다. \"그래, 그리고 그 말에는 기독교의 정신이 담겨 있어. 붉은 피부에게는 옳고 마땅한 일일지라도, 제 무지를 변명할 핏줄의 한 점 섞임조차 없는 사내에게는 죄가 될 수 있지. 〈칭가치국〉! 〈언카스〉! 검은 눈의 여인이 하는 말을 들었나!\"\n\n이제 그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 동료들에게 이야기했으니, 그의 말투는 차분하고 신중했으나 매우 단호한 듯했다. 나이 든 〈모히칸〉은 깊은 진지함으로 그 말을 들었고, 그 의미의 중대함을 느끼는 듯 그의 말을 곰곰이 따져 보는 듯했다. 잠시 망설인 끝에, 그는 동의의 표시로 손을 흔들며 제 종족 특유의 강세로 영어 단어 \"좋다!\"를 내뱉었다. 그러고는 칼과 토마호크를 다시 허리띠에 꽂고, 그 전사는 강기슭에서 가장 잘 가려진 바위 가장자리로 말없이 옮겨 갔다. 여기서 그는 잠시 멈춰, 아래쪽 숲을 의미심장하게 가리키고, 마치 자신이 가려는 길을 일러 주듯 제 말로 몇 마디 한 다음, 물속으로 떨어져 들어가, 자기 움직임을 지켜보던 이들의 눈앞에서 가라앉아 사라졌다.\n\n척후는 떠남을 미루고 그 너그러운 처녀에게 말을 건넸으니, 처녀는 자신의 간언이 먹혀든 것을 보고 숨결이 한결 가벼워졌다.\n\n\"지혜라는 것은 때로 늙은이만이 아니라 젊은이에게도 주어지는 법이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 당신이 한 말은 현명하오, 그보다 더 나은 말로 부르지 않더라도. 만일 당신들이 숲으로 끌려간다면—그러니까 당신들 가운데 한동안 목숨을 부지하게 될 자가 말이오—지나가면서 덤불의 잔가지를 꺾어, 당신들 자취의 흔적을 되도록 넓게 남겨 두시오. 그러면, 사람의 눈으로 그것을 볼 수만 있다면, 당신들을 버리느니 차라리 땅끝까지라도 뒤따라갈 벗이 하나 있음을 믿어도 좋소.\"\n\n그는 〈코라〉의 손을 다정하게 한 번 쥐고 흔든 다음, 소총을 들어 잠시 침울한 근심으로 그것을 바라보다가, 조심스레 한쪽에 내려놓고, 〈칭가치국〉이 방금 사라진 자리로 내려갔다. 한순간 그는 바위에 매달려 있었고, 유난히 근심 어린 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 쓰라리게 덧붙였다. \"화약만 떨어지지 않았더라면, 이런 치욕은 결코 닥치지 않았을 텐데!\" 그러고는 잡은 것을 놓자, 물이 그의 머리 위에서 닫혔고, 그 또한 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n이제 모든 눈이 〈언카스〉에게로 향했으니, 그는 꿈쩍 않는 차분함으로 울퉁불퉁한 바위에 기대어 서 있었다. 잠시 기다린 끝에, 〈코라〉가 강 아래쪽을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"당신의 벗들은 보이지 않으니, 이제는 십중팔구 안전하겠지요. 당신도 뒤따라갈 때가 되지 않았나요?\"\n\n\"〈언카스〉는 남겠소.\" 젊은 〈모히칸〉이 영어로 차분히 답했다.\n\n\"우리가 사로잡히는 끔찍함을 더하고, 우리가 풀려날 가망을 줄이려고요! 가세요, 너그러운 젊은이여,\" 〈코라〉가 그 〈모히칸〉의 응시 아래 눈길을 내리뜨며, 어쩌면 제가 지닌 힘에 대한 직감적인 자각과 함께 말을 이었다. \"내가 말한 대로, 내 아버지께 가서, 내 가장 믿음직한 전령이 되어 주세요. 딸들의 자유를 사들일 방도를 당신께 맡기시라 일러 주세요. 가세요! 당신이 가는 것, 그것이 내 바람이고, 내 기도예요!\"\n\n젊은 족장의 차분히 자리 잡은 표정이 침울한 빛으로 바뀌었으나, 그는 더는 망설이지 않았다. 소리 없는 걸음으로 그는 바위를 가로질러, 일렁이는 물줄기 속으로 떨어져 들어갔다. 뒤에 남은 이들은, 그의 머리가 숨을 쉬려 물길 저 아래로 솟아오르는 것을 언뜻 보기까지 거의 숨도 쉬지 못했으니, 그는 다시 가라앉아 더는 보이지 않았다.\n\n이 갑작스럽고 겉보기엔 성공적인 시도들은 모두, 이제 그토록 소중해진 그 시간의 단 몇 분 사이에 이루어졌다. 〈언카스〉를 마지막으로 한 번 바라본 뒤, 〈코라〉가 돌아서서, 떨리는 입술로 〈헤이워드〉에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"나는 당신이 물에서도 솜씨를 자랑한다고 들었어요, 〈덩컨〉.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그러니, 이 순박하고 충직한 이들이 당신께 보여 준 현명한 본보기를 따르세요.\"\n\n\"〈코라 먼로〉가 제 보호자에게 요구하는 신의가 그런 것이오?\" 젊은이가 슬프게, 그러나 쓰라림을 담아 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"지금은 부질없는 궤변이나 그릇된 견해를 늘어놓을 때가 아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"모든 의무를 똑같이 헤아려야 할 순간이지요. 당신은 이곳에서 우리에게 더는 도움이 될 수 없지만, 당신의 소중한 목숨은 다른, 더 가까운 벗들을 위해 구할 수 있어요.\"\n\n그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 그의 눈은 〈앨리스〉의 아름다운 자태에 애틋하게 머물렀으니, 그녀는 어린아이가 매달리듯 의지하며 그의 팔에 매달려 있었다.\n\n\"생각해 보세요,\" 〈코라〉가 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤 말을 이었으니, 그동안 그녀는 제 두려움이 불러일으킨 그 어떤 것보다도 더 날카로운 고통과 씨름하는 듯했다. \"우리에게 닥칠 최악이라 해야 죽음뿐, 그것은 하느님께서 정하신 좋은 때에 누구나 치러야 할 대가일 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"죽음보다 더한 화(禍)도 있소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 쉰 목소리로, 그녀의 집요함에 짜증이 난 듯 말했다. \"허나 당신을 위해 기꺼이 죽을 한 사람이 곁에 있으면 막아 낼 수 있는 화 말이오.\"\n\n〈코라〉는 애원을 그쳤다. 그러고는 숄로 얼굴을 가리고, 거의 정신을 잃은 〈앨리스〉를 끌고 안쪽 동굴의 가장 깊은 후미진 곳으로 들어갔다.\n\"마음껏 즐거워하라.\n\n나의 어여쁜 이여, 미소로 흩어 버려라, 그대의 맑은 이마에\n드리운 그 겁먹은 구름들을.\"\n\n〈아그리피나의 죽음〉\n\n전투의 격동하는 사건들에서 이제 그를 둘러싼 정적으로의, 그 갑작스럽고 거의 마법 같은 전환은, 〈헤이워드〉의 달아오른 상상에 무슨 흥분된 꿈처럼 작용했다. 그가 목격한 온갖 형상과 사건들이 그의 기억 속에 깊이 새겨진 채 남아 있었으나, 그는 그것들이 사실임을 스스로 납득하기가 어렵게 느껴졌다. 빠른 물살의 도움에 몸을 맡긴 자들의 운명을 아직 모르는 채, 그는 처음에 그들의 위험천만한 시도가 다행한 결말인지 불행한 결말인지를 알릴 만한 어떤 신호나 경고의 소리에 귀를 곤두세웠다. 그러나 그 주의는 헛되이 기울여졌으니, 〈언커스〉가 사라짐과 함께 그 모험가들의 흔적이라곤 죄다 사라져, 그를 그들의 운명에 관한 온전한 불확실 속에 남겨 둔 까닭이었다.\n\n그토록 고통스러운 의심의 순간에, 〈덩컨〉은 바로 조금 전까지 제 안전에 그토록 긴요했던 바위의 보호를 더는 구하지 않고 망설임 없이 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 숨은 적들이 다가오는 기미를 조금이라도 알아채려는 온갖 노력은 방금 전 동행들의 안부를 물었던 것과 마찬가지로 결실이 없었다. 강의 우거진 기슭은 동물적 생명을 지닌 모든 것에게서 다시금 버림받은 듯했다. 바로 조금 전 숲의 둥근 천장을 울리던 소란은 사라지고, 자연의 섞임 없는 감미로움 속에서 물살이 대기의 흐름을 따라 부풀었다 잦아들었다 하게 남겨 두었다. 죽은 소나무의 가장 높은 가지에 안전히 앉아 그 싸움을 멀리서 지켜보던 물수리 한 마리가, 이제 그 높고 거친 횃대에서 몸을 굽혀 내려와 제 먹잇감 위로 넓게 선회하며 솟구쳤다. 한편 미개인들의 더 거친 외침에 그 시끄러운 목소리를 잠재웠던 어치 한 마리는, 마치 한 번 더 제 야생의 영역을 평온히 차지한 듯, 다시금 그 거슬리는 목청을 열 엄두를 냈다. 〈덩컨〉은 이 외딴 풍경에 깃든 자연의 이런 화답들에서 희미한 희망의 빛을 붙잡았고, 다시금 성공을 확신하는 듯한 마음을 되살려, 새로운 분발을 향해 제 기력을 추스르기 시작했다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉족은 보이지 않소.\" 그가 〈데이비드〉에게 말을 건네며 이르렀으니, 그는 자신이 받은 정신이 아득해지는 일격의 여파에서 조금도 회복하지 못한 터였다. \"동굴 속에 몸을 숨깁시다. 그리고 나머지는 섭리에 맡깁시다.\"\n\n\"내 기억하건대 두 어여쁜 처녀와 더불어 찬미와 감사로 목소리를 드높였소만.\" 어리둥절한 성가 선생이 대답했다. \"그 후로 내 죄에 대한 무거운 심판이 내게 임하였소. 나는 잠의 형상으로 조롱당하였고, 그러는 동안 불협화의 소리들이 내 귀를 찢었으니, 마치 때가 다 찼음을, 그리고 자연이 제 조화를 잊었음을 드러내려는 듯하였소.\"\n\n\"가엾은 사람! 참으로 그대 자신의 시간이 거의 다 차 갔던 게요! 허나 정신을 차리고 나와 함께 갑시다. 그대 자신의 성가 외에 다른 모든 소리가 차단될 곳으로 내 그대를 데려가리다.\"\n\n\"폭포가 떨어지는 데에 선율이 있고, 큰 물들이 쏟아지는 소리는 감각에 감미롭소!\" 〈데이비드〉가 어수선하게 제 이마를 손으로 누르며 말했다. \"저주받은 자들의 떠난 혼령들이 그러하듯, 공중은 아직도 비명과 외침으로 가득하지 않소—\"\n\n\"이젠 아니오, 이젠 아니오.\" 참다못한 〈헤이워드〉가 가로막았다. \"그것들은 그쳤고, 그것들을 내질렀던 자들도, 하느님께 바라건대, 그들 역시 떠났소! 물 말고는 모든 것이 고요하고 평온하오. 그러니 들어가시오, 그대가 그토록 즐겨 듣는 그 소리들을 만들어 낼 수 있는 곳으로.\"\n\n이 사랑하는 천직에 대한 언급에, 〈데이비드〉는 한순간의 기쁨의 빛이 없지는 않았으나, 슬프게 미소 지었다. 그는 제 지친 감각에 그토록 순수한 만족을 약속하는 곳으로 이끌려 가기를 더는 망설이지 않았다. 그리고 동행의 팔에 기대어 동굴의 좁은 입구로 들어섰다. 〈덩컨〉은 사사프라스 더미를 움켜잡아 통로 앞으로 끌어다 놓아, 구멍이 있다는 기색을 일절 보이지 않도록 세심히 가렸다. 이 허술한 차단막 안쪽에 그는 숲사람들이 버리고 간 담요를 펼쳐, 동굴 안쪽 끝을 어둡게 했다. 한편 그 바깥쪽은 좁은 협곡에서 누그러진 빛을 받아들였으니, 그 협곡으로는 강의 한 줄기가 쏟아져 내려, 몇 로드 아래에서 제 자매 줄기와 합류를 이루었다.\n\n\"절망적으로 보이는 위급한 상황에서 싸움 없이 굴복하라고 가르치는 원주민들의 그 원칙이 나는 마음에 들지 않소.\" 그가 이 일에 분주한 동안 말했다. \"'목숨이 붙어 있는 한 희망은 있다'고 하는 우리 자신의 격언이 더 위로가 되고, 군인의 기질에 더 잘 맞소. 〈코라〉, 당신에게는 부질없는 격려의 말을 늘어놓지 않으리다. 당신 자신의 굳센 마음과 흐트러지지 않은 이성이, 당신의 성에 어울릴 모든 것을 당신에게 가르쳐 줄 테니. 허나 당신 가슴에 안긴 저 떨고 있는 가엾은 이의 눈물은 우리가 닦아 줄 수 없겠소?\"\n\n\"이젠 더 차분해요, 〈덩컨〉.\" 〈앨리스〉가 언니의 품에서 몸을 일으키며, 눈물을 머금은 채 애써 침착한 모습을 지어 보이며 말했다. \"이젠 훨씬 차분해요. 분명, 이 숨은 곳에서 우리는 안전하고, 우리는 들키지 않았으며, 해를 입을 일도 없어요. 우리는 이미 우리를 위해 그토록 많은 위험을 무릅쓴 저 너그러운 분들에게서 모든 것을 바랄 거예요.\"\n\n\"이제 우리의 다정한 〈앨리스〉가 〈먼로〉의 딸답게 말하는군요!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 동굴의 바깥쪽 입구를 향해 지나가다 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐려 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"그토록 용감한 본보기 둘을 눈앞에 두고서, 사내라면 영웅이 아닌 다른 무엇으로 드러나기를 부끄러워할 거요.\" 그러고 나서 그는 동굴 한가운데에 자리 잡고 앉아, 발작하듯 움켜쥔 손으로 남은 권총 한 자루를 쥐었으니, 한편 그 찌푸려 모은 눈은 제 결심의 침울한 결사를 알리고 있었다. \"〈휴런〉족이 온다 해도, 제 생각만큼 우리 위치를 그리 쉽게 얻지는 못할 거요.\" 그가 낮게 중얼거렸다. 그리고 머리를 뒤로 젖혀 바위에 기댄 채, 그는 인내로 결과를 기다리는 듯했으나, 그 시선은 그들의 은신처로 트인 통로를 향해 쉼 없이 쏠려 있었다.\n\n그의 목소리의 마지막 울림과 함께, 깊고 긴, 거의 숨조차 멎은 듯한 정적이 뒤따랐다. 아침의 신선한 공기가 그 후미진 곳으로 스며들었고, 그 영향이 그 안에 있는 이들의 마음에 차츰 느껴졌다. 한 순간 한 순간이 지나가며 그들을 흔들림 없는 안전 속에 남겨 두자, 슬며시 파고드는 희망의 감정이 차츰 모든 가슴을 사로잡았으나, 저마다 다음 순간 그토록 무섭게 깨질 수도 있는 기대를 입 밖에 내기를 꺼렸다.\n\n이 변덕스러운 감정에 예외를 이룬 것은 〈데이비드〉뿐이었다. 입구에서 들어온 한 줄기 빛이 그의 핏기 없는 얼굴을 가로질러, 그 책장이 다시 넘어가고 있던 작은 책의 면들 위에 떨어졌으니, 그는 마치 지금껏 눈에 든 어떤 노래보다도 그들의 처지에 더 어울리는 노래를 찾기라도 하듯 책장을 넘기고 있었다. 그는 십중팔구 이 내내 〈덩컨〉이 약속한 위안에 대한 어렴풋한 기억을 좇아 이 모든 일을 하고 있었으리라. 마침내, 보아하니 그의 끈질긴 노고가 보답을 찾은 듯했으니, 아무런 설명이나 사과도 없이 그는 \"〈와이트섬〉\"이라는 말을 소리 내어 발음하고, 음정 피리에서 길고 감미로운 소리를 끌어낸 다음, 방금 그 이름을 말한 곡조의 도입 변조를, 제 자신의 음악적 목소리의 더 감미로운 음색으로 훑어 나갔다.\n\n\"이게 위험하지 않을까요?\" 〈코라〉가 〈헤이워드〉 소령에게 검은 눈길을 던지며 물었다.\n\n\"가엾은 사람! 그의 목소리는 폭포의 소란 속에서 들리기엔 너무 약하오.\" 대답이 돌아왔다. \"게다가 동굴이 그의 편이 되어 줄 거요. 위험 없이 할 수 있는 일이니, 그의 열정을 마음껏 누리게 둡시다.\"\n\n\"〈와이트섬〉!\" 〈데이비드〉가 오랫동안 제 학교의 소곤대는 메아리를 잠재울 때 익혀 온 그 위엄으로 주위를 둘러보며 되풀이했다. \"이것은 장한 곡조요, 엄숙한 가사에 붙은 것이니, 마땅한 경의로써 부를지어다!\"\n\n제 규율을 다지고자 한순간의 정적을 허락한 뒤, 그 가수의 목소리가 낮고 웅얼대는 음절로 들려와, 차츰 귓가에 스며들더니, 이윽고 그 좁은 둥근 천장을 가득 채웠으니, 그의 쇠약함이 빚어낸 미약하고 떨리는 발성으로 인해 세 곱으로 짜릿하게 울리는 소리였다. 그 어떤 허약함으로도 무너뜨릴 수 없는 그 선율은, 듣는 이들의 감각에 차츰 그 감미로운 영향을 미쳐 갔다. 그것은 그 가수가 비슷한 토로들을 모은 한 권에서 골라낸 〈데이비드〉의 노래의 그 가련한 졸렬한 모방마저 압도하여, 그 슬며시 파고드는 소리의 조화 속에 가사의 의미를 잊게 만들었다. 〈앨리스〉는 저도 모르게 눈물을 닦고, 녹아내리는 듯한 눈을 〈가뮤트〉의 핏기 없는 얼굴에 쏟았으니, 그 표정은 그녀가 꾸미지도 감추려 하지도 않은 정화된 기쁨이었다. 〈코라〉는 그 유대 왕자와 이름이 같은 이의 경건한 노력에 인정하는 미소를 보냈고, 〈헤이워드〉는 곧 그 한결같고 준엄한 눈길을 동굴의 출구에서 거두어, 더 부드러운 빛깔로 〈데이비드〉의 얼굴에 못박거나, 때때로 〈앨리스〉의 촉촉한 눈에서 새어 나오는 흩어진 빛줄기와 마주했다. 듣는 이들의 숨김없는 공감은 그 음악의 신봉자의 마음을 움직였으니, 그의 목소리는 그 은밀한 매력을 입증하던 가슴 저미는 부드러움을 잃지 않으면서도 그 풍부함과 성량을 되찾았다. 새로워진 기력을 한껏 쥐어짜며, 그가 여전히 동굴의 둥근 천장을 길고 충만한 음으로 채우고 있을 때, 바깥에서 한 비명이 공중으로 터져 나와, 그 즉시 그의 경건한 가락을 멎게 했으니, 마치 그의 심장이 정말로 목구멍의 통로로 뛰어든 듯, 그의 목소리를 별안간 틀어막아 버렸다.\n\n\"우린 끝났어요!\" 〈앨리스〉가 〈코라〉의 품에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아직 아니오, 아직 아니오.\" 동요했으나 굴하지 않은 〈헤이워드〉가 대답했다. \"그 소리는 섬 한가운데서 났고, 죽은 동료들을 본 것이 빚어낸 것이오. 우린 아직 들키지 않았고, 아직 희망이 있소.\"\n\n탈출의 가망은 희미하고 거의 절망적이었으나, 〈덩컨〉의 말은 헛되이 흘러가지 않았으니, 그것이 자매들의 기력을 일깨워 그들로 하여금 침묵 속에 결과를 기다리게 한 까닭이었다. 곧 첫 비명에 이어 둘째 비명이 뒤따랐고, 이윽고 한 무리의 목소리가 섬의 위쪽 끝에서 아래쪽 끝까지 쏟아져 내리는 것이 들렸으니, 그들이 동굴 위의 헐벗은 바위에 다다르자, 미개한 승리의 함성이 터진 뒤로, 공중은 오직 인간만이 내지를 수 있는, 그것도 더없이 사나운 야만의 상태에 있을 때라야 내지를 수 있는 그런 끔찍한 외침과 비명으로 가득 찼다.\n\n그 소리는 사방 모든 방향으로 그들 주위에 빠르게 퍼졌다. 더러는 물가에서 제 동료들을 불렀고, 위쪽 높은 데서 화답이 돌아왔다. 두 동굴 사이의 틈, 그 깜짝 놀랄 만치 가까운 데서 외침이 들려왔으니, 깊은 협곡의 심연에서 솟아오르는 더 거친 비명과 뒤섞였다. 요컨대, 그 미개한 소리가 헐벗은 바위 위로 어찌나 빠르게 퍼졌던지, 근심에 잠긴 청자들이 그것이 자기들 아래에서도 들린다고 상상하기란 어렵지 않았으니, 사실인즉 그것은 그들의 위에도 사방에도 있었다.\n\n이 소란의 한가운데서, 동굴의 숨은 입구로부터 몇 야드 안쪽에서 한 의기양양한 비명이 일어났다. 〈헤이워드〉는 그것이 자기들이 발각되었다는 신호라고 믿고 온갖 희망을 버렸다. 그러나 그 백인이 그토록 마지못해 자신의 소총을 버린 그 지점 가까이로 목소리들이 모여드는 것을 듣자, 그 인상은 다시 사라졌다. 이제 또렷이 들리는 인디언 방언들의 알아들을 수 없는 말 가운데, 단어뿐 아니라 문장까지도 〈캐나다〉 사투리로 구별해 내기란 쉬웠다. 한 무리의 목소리가 일제히 \"라 롱그 카라빈!\" 하고 외쳐, 맞은편 숲이 한 이름을 메아리치게 했으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 잘 기억하건대 그것은 적들이 〈영국〉 진영의 어느 이름난 사냥꾼이자 척후에게 붙인 이름이었고, 그가 이제 처음으로 알게 된 바, 그자가 바로 그의 방금 전 동행이었던 것이다.\n\n\"라 롱그 카라빈! 라 롱그 카라빈!\" 그 말이 입에서 입으로 옮겨 다녔으니, 이윽고 그 무리 전체가, 그 가공할 임자의 죽음을 알리는 듯한 전리품 하나를 둘러싸고 모여든 것처럼 보였다. 떠들썩한 의논 끝에 — 그것은 때때로 미개한 환희의 폭발에 묻혀 들리지 않았다 — 그들은 다시 흩어지며, 그 적의 이름으로 공중을 채웠으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 그들의 말에서 알아낼 수 있었던바, 그들은 그 적의 시신이 섬의 어느 갈라진 틈에 숨겨져 있기를 바라며 찾고 있었다.\n\n\"이제.\" 그가 떠는 자매들에게 속삭였다. \"이제가 불확실한 순간이오! 우리 은신처가 이 수색을 면한다면, 우린 아직 안전하오! 어떤 경우든, 우리 적들의 입에서 새어 나온 말로 미루어, 우리 친구들이 무사히 빠져나갔음은 분명하니, 두어 시간 안에 〈웨브〉에게서 구원을 기대할 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n이제 무서운 정적이 몇 분간 흘렀으니, 그동안 미개인들이 한층 더 빈틈없고 체계적으로 수색을 벌인다는 것을 〈헤이워드〉는 잘 알고 있었다. 그는 한 번 이상, 그들이 사사프라스를 스쳐 시든 잎이 바스락거리고 가지가 우지끈하는 소리에서 그들의 발걸음을 구별해 낼 수 있었다. 마침내 그 더미가 조금 무너지며, 담요 한 귀퉁이가 떨어졌고, 희미한 빛 한 줄기가 동굴 안쪽으로 새어 들었다. 〈코라〉는 비통함 속에 〈앨리스〉를 제 가슴에 끌어안았고, 〈덩컨〉은 벌떡 일어섰다. 바로 그 순간 한 함성이 들려왔으니, 마치 바위 한가운데서 솟아나오는 듯, 이웃한 동굴에 마침내 발을 들였음을 알리는 것이었다. 잠깐 사이에, 목소리의 수와 우렁참은 그 무리 전체가 그 비밀스러운 곳 안과 그 주위에 모였음을 일러 주었다.\n\n두 동굴로 통하는 안쪽 통로가 서로 그토록 가까운 까닭에, 〈덩컨〉은 이제 탈출이 더는 가능하지 않다고 믿고, 〈데이비드〉와 자매들을 지나, 그 자매들과 그 끔찍한 마주침의 첫 들이닥침 사이에 제 몸을 놓으려 했다. 제 처지에 절망한 나머지, 그는 자신을 그 무자비한 추격자들과 겨우 몇 피트로 갈라놓을 뿐인 그 허술한 차단막에 다가가, 우연히 난 틈에 얼굴을 대고, 일종의 절망적인 무심함으로 그들의 움직임을 내다보기까지 했다.\n\n그의 팔이 닿을 거리에는 한 거대한 인디언의 우람한 어깨가 있었으니, 그 깊고 권위 있는 목소리는 동료들의 일 처리에 지시를 내리는 듯했다. 그 너머로 다시, 〈덩컨〉은 맞은편 동굴 안을 들여다볼 수 있었는데, 그곳은 미개인들로 가득 차, 그 척후의 초라한 세간을 뒤집고 샅샅이 뒤지고 있었다. 〈데이비드〉의 상처가 사사프라스 잎을 한 빛깔로 물들여 놓았으니, 원주민들은 그것이 계절을 앞지른 것임을 잘 알았다. 자신들의 성공을 드러내는 이 표시를 두고, 그들은 잃었던 자취를 되찾은 그 많은 사냥개들이 짖어 대듯 울부짖음을 일으켰다. 이 승리의 비명 뒤에, 그들은 동굴의 그 향기로운 잠자리를 헤집어 그 가지들을 협곡으로 날라, 마치 그것이 그토록 오래 미워하고 두려워한 그 사람을 숨기고 있기라도 한 듯, 그 가지들을 흩뿌렸다. 사납고 야만스러운 행색의 한 전사가 덤불을 한 짐 짊어지고 그 우두머리에게 다가가, 거기 뿌려진 짙은 핏자국을 의기양양하게 가리키며, 인디언 비명으로 제 환희를 내질렀으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 그 뜻을 헤아릴 수 있었던 것은 오직 \"라 롱그 카라빈!\"이라는 이름의 잦은 되풀이 덕분이었다. 그의 의기양양함이 그치자, 그는 그 덤불을 〈덩컨〉이 둘째 동굴의 입구 앞에 쌓아 둔 그 허술한 더미 위로 던져, 시야를 막아 버렸다. 그의 본보기를 다른 이들이 따랐으니, 그들은 척후의 동굴에서 가지를 끌어내며 그것을 한 더미에 던져, 저도 모르게 자기들이 찾는 자들의 안전을 더해 주었다. 그 방어막의 바로 그 허술함이 그 으뜸가는 장점이었으니, 아무도 그 덤불 더미를 들쑤셔 볼 생각을 하지 않은 까닭이었다. 그들 모두가 그 서두름과 혼란의 순간에, 그것이 자기네 무리의 손에 우연히 쌓인 것이라고 믿었기 때문이다.\n\n담요가 바깥의 압력에 밀려나고, 가지들이 제 무게로 바위 틈에 내려앉아 빽빽한 한 덩이를 이루자, 〈덩컨〉은 다시 한번 자유로이 숨 쉬었다. 가벼운 걸음으로, 그리고 더 가벼운 마음으로, 그는 동굴 한가운데로 돌아가 자신이 떠났던 자리, 강 쪽의 입구를 내다볼 수 있는 그 자리를 차지했다. 그가 이 움직임을 행하고 있던 바로 그때, 인디언들이 마치 공통의 충동으로 그 목적을 바꾸기라도 한 듯, 한 무리로 동굴에서 떨어져 나와, 다시 섬을 거슬러 올라, 그들이 본래 내려왔던 지점을 향해 달려가는 소리가 들렸다. 이곳에서 또 한 차례의 통곡하는 외침이, 그들이 다시 죽은 동료들의 시신을 둘러싸고 모였음을 일러 주었다.\n\n이제 〈덩컨〉은 제 동행들을 바라볼 엄두를 냈으니, 위험의 가장 위태로운 순간들 동안에는, 제 얼굴의 근심이 그것을 견뎌 낼 힘이 그토록 없는 이들에게 어떤 더한 불안을 전할까 두려워했던 까닭이었다.\n\n\"그들은 갔소, 〈코라〉!\" 그가 속삭였다. \"〈앨리스〉, 그들은 왔던 곳으로 돌아갔고, 우린 살았소! 그토록 무자비한 적의 손아귀에서 우리를 홀로 건져 주신 하늘에, 온 찬미가 있기를!\"\n\n\"그럼 하늘에 제 감사를 돌려 드리겠어요!\" 동생이 〈코라〉의 감싸 안은 팔에서 일어나, 벅찬 감사로 헐벗은 바위 위에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. \"백발의 아버지의 눈물을 아껴 주신 그 하늘에, 제가 그토록 사랑하는 이들의 목숨을 구해 주신—\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉도, 그리고 더 차분한 〈코라〉도, 그 무심결의 격정의 행위를 강렬한 공감으로 지켜보았으니, 전자는 신앙이 지금 〈앨리스〉의 젊은 모습으로 띤 만큼 사랑스러운 형상을 띤 적이 일찍이 없었다고 남몰래 믿었다. 그녀의 눈은 감사의 감정의 빛으로 환히 빛났고, 그 아름다움의 홍조가 다시 그녀의 뺨에 자리 잡았으며, 그녀의 온 영혼은 그 말 잘하는 이목구비를 통해 감사의 기도를 쏟아 낼 준비가 되어 간절한 듯했다. 그러나 그녀의 입술이 움직였을 때, 그것이 발해야 했을 말들은 어떤 새롭고 갑작스러운 한기에 얼어붙은 듯했다. 그녀의 혈색은 죽음의 창백함에 자리를 내주었고, 부드럽게 녹아내리던 눈은 굳어져, 공포로 오그라드는 듯했다. 한편 그녀가 맞잡아 하늘을 향해 들어 올렸던 그 두 손은, 손가락을 발작하듯 앞으로 가리킨 채 그녀 앞에 수평으로 떨어졌다. 그녀가 그의 의심에 방향을 일러 주는 그 즉시 〈헤이워드〉는 몸을 돌렸고, 동굴의 트인 출구의 문지방을 이루는 그 바위턱 바로 위로 엿보니, 그는 〈교활한 여우〉의 그 악의에 차고 사납고 야만스러운 얼굴을 보았다.\n\n그 놀라움의 순간에도, 〈헤이워드〉의 침착함은 그를 저버리지 않았다. 그는 그 인디언의 얼굴에 떠오른 멍한 표정으로 보아, 탁 트인 바깥 공기에 익은 그자의 눈이 동굴 깊은 데를 채운 그 어스레한 빛을 아직 꿰뚫어 보지 못했음을 알아차렸다. 그는 자연이 빚은 벽의 한 굽이 너머로 물러설까 하는 생각마저 했으니, 그 굽이가 아직 그와 그의 동행들을 가려 줄지도 모를 일이었다. 그러나 그 미개인의 얼굴을 가로질러 별안간 번뜩인 깨달음의 빛으로, 그는 이미 너무 늦었음을, 그리고 그들이 발각되었음을 알았다.\n\n이 끔찍한 진실을 알리는 그 의기양양함과 잔인한 승리의 표정은 어찌할 수 없이 사람을 격분케 하는 것이었다. 제 뜨거운 피의 충동 말고는 모든 것을 잊은 채, 〈덩컨〉은 권총을 겨누어 쏘았다. 그 무기의 발사음이 동굴을 화산이 분출하듯 우르릉 울리게 했다. 그리고 그것이 토해 낸 연기가 협곡에서 흘러나온 공기의 흐름에 밀려 흩어졌을 때, 바로 조금 전 그 배신한 길잡이의 얼굴이 차지했던 그 자리는 비어 있었다. 출구로 달려간 〈헤이워드〉는 그의 검은 형체가 낮고 좁은 바위턱을 돌아 슬며시 사라지는 모습을 언뜻 붙잡았으니, 그것은 이내 그를 시야에서 온전히 가려 버렸다.\n\n미개인들 사이에는, 바위의 깊은 속에서 터져 나오는 것으로 막 들렸던 그 폭음 뒤로 무시무시한 정적이 뒤따랐다. 그러나 〈교활한 여우〉가 길고 알아들을 만한 함성으로 목소리를 높이자, 그 소리가 들리는 데 있던 모든 인디언의 입에서 일제히 터진 비명이 그것에 화답했다. 그 떠들썩한 소음이 다시 섬을 따라 쏟아져 내렸다. 그리고 〈덩컨〉이 그 충격에서 미처 회복할 틈도 없이, 그의 허술한 덤불 차단막은 바람에 흩어졌고, 동굴은 그 양쪽 끝에서 침입당했으며, 그와 그의 동행들은 그 은신처에서 끌려 나와 환한 대낮으로 떠밀려 나가, 그곳에서 의기양양한 〈휴런〉족 무리 전체에게 에워싸인 채 섰다.\n\n\"두렵구나, 우리가 다가오는 아침을 너무 늦도록 잠들어 보낼까\n지난밤 너무 오래 깨어 지켜본 것만큼이나!\"\n\n〈한여름 밤의 꿈〉\n\n이 갑작스러운 불행의 충격이 가시는 순간, 〈던컨〉은 자신들을 사로잡은 자들의 모습과 행동을 살피기 시작했다. 그들은 승리에 도취해 방종을 부리는 원주민들의 관습과는 달리, 떨고 있는 자매들의 몸뿐 아니라 그 자신의 몸에도 손대지 않고 존중했다. 그의 화려한 군복 장식은 과연 그 종족의 여러 자가 거듭 손으로 만지작거렸으니, 그 눈에는 그 노리개를 갖고 싶어 하는 야만적인 갈망이 어려 있었다. 그러나 으레 따르던 폭력이 가해지기도 전에, 앞서 언급한 덩치 큰 전사의 위엄 있는 목소리로 내려진 명령이 치켜든 손을 멈춰 세웠고, 〈헤이워드〉로 하여금 자신들이 무언가 특별히 중요한 목적을 위해 남겨질 것임을 깨닫게 했다.\n\n그러나 일행 가운데 젊고 허영심 많은 자들이 이런 나약함의 기색을 드러내는 동안, 더 노련한 전사들은 두 동굴을 샅샅이 뒤지기를 멈추지 않았으니, 그 부지런함은 이미 드러난 정복의 열매에 결코 만족하지 못하고 있음을 말해 주었다. 새로운 희생자를 찾아내지 못하자, 이 부지런한 복수의 일꾼들은 이내 남자 포로들에게 다가가, 〈긴 총〉이라는 이름을, 좀처럼 오해할 수 없을 만큼 사나운 어조로 외쳐 댔다. 〈던컨〉은 거듭되는 폭력적인 심문의 뜻을 알아듣지 못하는 척했고, 그의 동행은 프랑스어를 모르는 덕분에 그와 같은 가장의 수고를 면했다. 마침내 그 끈질긴 추궁에 지치고, 너무 완강한 침묵으로 자신을 사로잡은 자들을 자극할까 두려워, 전자는 〈마구아〉를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았으니, 그라면 시시각각 더 다급하고 위협적으로 변해 가는 물음에 자신의 대답을 통역해 줄 수 있을 터였다.\n\n이 야만인의 행동은 그 모든 동료들과 홀로 동떨어진 예외를 이루었다. 다른 자들이 척후의 보잘것없는 소지품마저 약탈하며 장식품에 대한 어린아이 같은 욕심을 채우기에 분주하거나, 눈에 피에 굶주린 복수심을 띤 채 그 자리에 없는 임자를 찾아 헤매는 동안, 〈르나르〉는 포로들에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에, 그토록 차분하고 흡족한 태도로 서 있었으니, 그것은 그가 이미 이 배신의 큰 목적을 이루었음을 드러내고 있었다. 〈헤이워드〉의 눈이 얼마 전 자신의 안내자였던 자의 눈과 처음 마주쳤을 때, 그는 자신이 맞닥뜨린 그 음험하면서도 차분한 눈길에 진저리치며 시선을 돌렸다. 그러나 혐오를 억누르고, 그는 얼굴을 외면한 채 자신의 의기양양한 적에게 말을 건넬 수 있었다.\n\n\"교활한 〈르나르〉는 무인다운 사내인지라,\" 마지못해 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다, \"무장하지 않은 사람에게 정복자들이 무어라 하는지 말해 주기를 거절하지는 않으리라.\"\n\n\"저들은 숲을 가로지르는 길을 아는 사냥꾼을 찾고 있다.\" 〈마구아〉가 서툰 영어로 대꾸하며, 동시에 사나운 미소를 머금고, 제 어깨의 상처를 동여맨 잎사귀 뭉치 위에 손을 얹었다. \"〈긴 총〉! 그자의 총은 훌륭하고, 그 눈은 결코 감기지 않는다. 허나 흰 추장의 짧은 총처럼, 〈교활한 자〉의 목숨 앞에서는 아무것도 아니다!\"\n\n\"〈르나르〉는 너무 용맹하여 전쟁에서 입은 상처나, 그것을 입힌 손을 기억하지는 않으리라!\"\n\n\"지친 인디언이 단풍나무 아래 쉬며 제 옥수수를 맛보았을 때, 그것이 전쟁이었던가! 누가 덤불을 기어드는 적들로 가득 채웠는가! 누가 칼을 빼 들었는가! 누구의 혀는 평화를 말하면서, 그 마음은 피로 물들어 있었는가! 〈마구아〉가 도끼를 땅에서 뽑았다고, 제 손으로 그것을 파냈다고 말했던가?\"\n\n〈던컨〉은 자신을 비난하는 자에게 그가 미리 꾸민 배신을 일깨워 받아칠 엄두가 나지 않았고, 어떤 변명의 말로 그의 원한을 누그러뜨리기를 떳떳지 않게 여겨, 잠자코 있었다. 〈마구아〉도 이 논쟁을, 그리고 그 밖의 모든 이야기를 거기서 그치는 데 만족한 듯, 순간의 격정에 일어섰던 바위에 다시 몸을 기대었다. 그러나 짧은 대화가 끝났음을 참을성 없는 야만인들이 알아채는 순간, \"〈긴 총〉\"을 외치는 소리가 다시금 일었다.\n\n\"들리는가,\" 〈마구아〉가 완강한 무관심으로 말했다. \"붉은 〈휴런〉들은 '〈긴 총〉'의 목숨을 원하거나, 아니면 그를 숨긴 자들의 피를 원한다!\"\n\n\"그는 가버렸다 — 달아났다. 그는 너희 손이 미치지 못할 멀리 있다.\"\n\n〈르나르〉는 차가운 경멸의 미소를 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"백인은 죽으면 평화에 들었다고 여긴다. 허나 붉은 사람들은 적의 혼령마저 괴롭히는 법을 안다. 그자의 몸은 어디 있는가? 〈휴런〉들에게 그자의 머릿가죽을 보여라!\"\n\n\"그는 죽지 않았고, 달아났다.\"\n\n〈마구아〉는 믿지 못하겠다는 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그자가 날개를 펴는 새인가, 아니면 공기 없이 헤엄치는 물고기인가! 흰 추장은 제 책 속에서 글을 읽고, 〈휴런〉들을 어리석은 자들이라 여기는구나!\"\n\n\"물고기는 아니나, 〈긴 총〉은 헤엄칠 줄 안다. 화약이 다 타 버리고, 〈휴런〉들의 눈이 구름 뒤에 가려졌을 때, 그는 강물을 따라 떠내려갔다.\"\n\n\"그러면 흰 추장은 어찌하여 남았는가?\" 여전히 믿지 못하는 인디언이 따져 물었다. \"그자는 바닥으로 가라앉는 돌인가, 아니면 머릿가죽이 그 머리를 태우기라도 하는가?\"\n\n\"내가 돌이 아니라는 것은, 폭포에 떨어진 너의 죽은 동료가, 아직 그 안에 목숨이 남아 있었다면 대답해 줄 수 있었을 것이다.\" 격분한 젊은이가, 분노에 차서 인디언의 감탄을 가장 잘 자아낼 법한 그 호기로운 말투를 쓰며 말했다. \"백인은 겁쟁이만이 제 여인들을 버린다고 여긴다.\"\n\n〈마구아〉는 이 사이로 몇 마디를 알아들을 수 없게 중얼거리고는, 소리 높여 말을 이었다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉들도 덤불 속을 기는 것만큼이나 헤엄도 잘 치는가? 〈르 그로 세르팡〉은 어디 있는가?\"\n\n이 〈캐나다〉식 호칭을 쓰는 것으로 미루어, 얼마 전까지 함께했던 동행들이 자기보다 적들에게 훨씬 더 잘 알려져 있음을 깨달은 〈던컨〉은 마지못해 대답했다. \"그도 물길을 따라 내려갔다.\"\n\n\"〈르 세르 아질〉은 여기 없는가?\"\n\n\"누구를 '〈날쌘 사슴〉'이라 부르는지 모르겠다.\" 〈던컨〉이 시간을 끌 어떤 핑계라도 반가워하며 말했다.\n\n\"〈웅카스〉다.\" 〈마구아〉가 그 〈델라웨어〉식 이름을, 영어보다도 한층 더 힘겹게 발음하며 대꾸했다. \"'〈뛰노는 큰사슴〉'이 흰 사람이 젊은 〈모히칸〉을 부를 때 쓰는 말이다.\"\n\n\"우리 사이에 이름에 약간 혼동이 있구나, 〈르나르〉.\" 〈던컨〉이 논쟁을 부추기려는 마음으로 말했다. \"프랑스어로 사슴은 〈daim〉, 수사슴은 〈cerf〉다. 큰사슴을 말하려면 〈elan〉이 옳은 낱말이다.\"\n\n\"그렇다.\" 인디언이 제 모어로 중얼거렸다. \"창백한 얼굴들은 재잘대는 여인네들이다! 그들은 무엇 하나에 두 낱말을 가지지만, 붉은 살갗은 제 목소리로 제 뜻을 말하게 한다.\" 그러고는 말을 바꾸어, 그를 가르친 변경 사람들의 불완전한 어법을 좇아 말을 이었다. \"사슴은 빠르나 약하다. 큰사슴은 빠르고도 강하다. 그리고 〈르 세르팡〉의 아들은 〈르 세르 아질〉이다. 그가 강을 뛰어넘어 숲으로 갔는가?\"\n\n\"네가 더 젊은 〈델라웨어〉를 말하는 것이라면, 그 역시 물길을 따라 내려갔다.\"\n\n인디언으로서는 그런 탈출 방식에 도무지 있을 법하지 않은 점이 없었으므로, 〈마구아〉는 들은 바의 진실을 선뜻 받아들였으니, 그 선선함은 그가 이런 하잘것없는 포로들을 얼마나 대수롭지 않게 여기는지를 한층 더 잘 드러내는 증거였다. 그러나 그의 동료들에게는 그 감정이 분명 달랐다.\n\n〈휴런〉들은 이 짧은 대화의 결과를 그들 특유의 인내로, 그리고 마침내 무리 전체가 고요해질 때까지 짙어 가는 침묵 속에서 기다려 왔다. 〈헤이워드〉가 말을 그치자, 그들은 한 사람처럼 〈마구아〉에게로 눈을 돌려, 이 뜻있는 몸짓으로, 무슨 이야기가 오갔는지 설명을 요구했다. 통역을 맡은 자는 강을 가리키며, 몇 마디 말로보다는 그 동작으로 그 결과를 알려 주었다. 그 사실이 두루 이해되자, 야만인들은 무시무시한 함성을 질렀으니, 그것은 그들의 실망이 얼마나 큰지를 드러내는 것이었다. 더러는 물가로 사납게 달려가 미친 듯한 몸짓으로 허공을 후려쳤고, 더러는 정복자로서 인정받은 자신들의 권리에 그 강물이 저질렀다고 여기는 배신을 분풀이하듯, 물 위에 침을 뱉었다. 몇몇은, 그리고 그들은 무리 가운데 가장 힘세고 가장 끔찍한 자들에 못지않았는데, 아직 제 손아귀에 남아 있는 포로들에게 음험한 눈길을 던졌으니, 그 안의 가장 사나운 격정은 오직 몸에 밴 자제력으로만 억눌려 있을 뿐이었다. 한둘은 더 나아가 더없이 위협적인 몸짓으로 그 악의를 터뜨렸으니, 그 앞에서는 자매의 여린 성별도 그 아름다움도 아무런 보호가 되지 못했다. 젊은 군인은 어느 야만인의 거무튀튀한 손이 〈앨리스〉의 어깨 위로 물결치며 흘러내린 풍성한 머릿단을 휘감고, 그 머리카락이 자라난 머리둘레로 칼이 둘러지는 것을 보았을 때 — 마치 그 아름다운 장식을 빼앗으려는 끔찍한 방식을 알리려는 듯 — 필사적이었으나 헛된 몸부림으로 그녀의 곁으로 뛰어들려 했다. 그러나 그의 두 손은 묶여 있었고, 그가 처음 몸을 움직이자, 무리를 지휘하던 그 힘센 인디언의 손아귀가 만력처럼 그의 어깨를 짓누르는 것을 느꼈다. 이토록 압도적인 힘에 맞선 어떤 발버둥도 부질없으리라는 것을 단박에 깨닫고, 그는 제 운명에 순응하며, 원주민들이 행하는 것보다 더 많이 위협하는 일이 좀처럼 드물지 않다는 몇 마디 낮고 다정한 다짐으로 그 온순한 동행들을 다독였다.\n\n그러나 〈던컨〉은 자매들의 두려움을 가라앉히려 이런 위안의 말을 건넸으되, 스스로를 속일 만큼 어리석지는 않았다. 그는 인디언 추장의 권위라는 것이 관례에 거의 기대지 않아, 그가 지녔을 어떤 도덕적 우위보다는 육체적 우월함으로 유지되는 경우가 더 많다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그러므로 위험은 자신들을 에워싼 야만의 혼들의 수에 정확히 비례하여 커지는 것이었다. 인정받은 우두머리로 보이는 자가 내린 더없이 단호한 명령조차, 죽은 벗이나 친지의 혼령에게 희생을 바치려는 어떤 무모한 손에 의해 시시각각 어겨질 수 있었다. 그러므로 그는 겉으로는 차분함과 의연함을 유지하면서도, 그 사나운 포로 잡은 자들 가운데 누구라도 무력한 자매들에게 평소보다 가까이 다가서거나, 가장 작은 공격에도 거의 버티지 못할 그 연약한 몸들에 음울하게 떠도는 눈길 하나라도 박을 때면, 그의 심장은 목구멍까지 치솟았다.\n\n그러나 우두머리가 평의를 위해 제 전사들을 불러 모으는 것을 보고는, 그의 불안은 크게 누그러졌다. 그들의 논의는 짧았고, 대다수가 침묵한 것으로 미루어 그 결정은 만장일치인 듯했다. 몇 안 되는 발언자들이 〈웨브〉의 진영 쪽을 거듭 가리키는 것으로 보아, 그들이 그쪽에서 다가올 위험을 두려워하고 있음이 분명했다. 이러한 고려가 아마도 그들의 결정을 재촉했고, 뒤이은 움직임을 빠르게 했을 것이다.\n\n이 짧은 협의가 이어지는 동안, 가장 큰 두려움에서 잠시 벗어난 〈헤이워드〉는, 적대 행위가 그친 뒤에도 〈휴런〉들이 접근해 온 그 신중한 방식을 감탄하며 살필 여유가 있었다.\n\n섬의 위쪽 절반이 헐벗은 바위로, 흩어진 떠내려온 통나무 몇 개 말고는 어떤 방어물도 없다는 것은 이미 말한 바 있다. 그들은 그곳을 상륙 지점으로 골라, 그러기 위해 폭포를 빙 둘러 숲을 지나 카누를 메고 왔던 것이다. 무기를 그 작은 배에 실어 놓고, 열두 명의 사내가 그 옆구리에 매달려, 위험한 길목을 굽어볼 수 있는 자세를 취한 가장 솜씨 좋은 전사 둘이 다루는 카누의 인도에 제 몸을 맡겼다. 이런 배치 덕분에, 그들은 처음 모험에 그토록 치명적이었던 바로 그 지점에서 섬의 머리에 닿았으되, 이번엔 우월한 수와 총기를 갖춘 이점이 있었다. 그들의 상륙이 그러한 방식으로 이루어졌음은 〈던컨〉에게 자못 분명히 드러났으니, 그들이 이제 그 가벼운 자작나무 배를 바위의 위쪽 끝에서 들어다 바깥쪽 동굴 어귀 가까운 물 위에 내려놓았기 때문이다. 이 옮김이 끝나자마자, 우두머리는 포로들에게 내려와 배에 타라고 손짓했다.\n\n저항은 불가능하고 항의는 무익했으므로, 〈헤이워드〉가 앞장서 카누에 오르며 순응의 본을 보였으니, 그곳에서 그는 곧 자매들과, 여전히 어리둥절해하는 〈데이비드〉와 함께 자리를 잡았다. 〈휴런〉들은 그 강의 소용돌이와 급류 사이로 난 작은 물길을 으레 알지 못했으나, 그런 항행에 흔한 신호들은 너무도 잘 알고 있어 큰 실수라곤 저지르지 않았다. 카누를 인도하는 임무로 뽑힌 키잡이가 제자리를 잡자, 무리 전체가 다시 강물 속으로 뛰어들었고, 배는 물살을 따라 미끄러져 내려갔으며, 몇 순간 만에 포로들은 전날 저녁 강에 닿았던 지점과 거의 마주한, 강의 남쪽 기슭에 자신들이 와 있음을 알게 되었다.\n\n이곳에서 또 한 차례 짧되 진지한 협의가 열렸으니, 그동안 그 임자들이 자신들의 가장 큰 불행의 탓으로 돌렸던, 공포에 질린 말들이 숲의 가림막에서 끌려 나와 이 으슥한 곳으로 옮겨졌다. 이제 무리가 둘로 나뉘었다. 그토록 자주 언급된 그 큰 추장은 〈헤이워드〉의 군마에 올라타, 곧장 강을 가로질러 앞장섰고, 부하 대부분이 그 뒤를 따라 숲속으로 사라지며, 포로들을 여섯 야만인의 손에 맡겼으니, 그 우두머리는 교활한 〈르나르〉였다. 〈던컨〉은 그들의 모든 움직임을 새삼스러운 불안 속에 지켜보았다.\n\n그는 야만인들의 보기 드문 자제로 미루어, 자신이 〈몽칼름〉에게 넘겨질 포로로 남겨진 것이라 즐겨 믿어 왔다. 불행에 빠진 자들의 생각은 좀처럼 잠들지 않고, 아무리 희미하고 먼 것일지언정 희망에 자극받을 때만큼 그 상상이 활발한 적도 없는 법인지라, 그는 〈먼로〉의 부정(父情)이 자신을 왕에 대한 의무에서 꾀어내는 데 쓰일 도구가 되리라고까지 그려 보았다. 그 프랑스 지휘관은 용기와 진취성으로 높은 평판을 지녔으되, 도덕의 더 미묘한 의무를 늘 존중하지는 않으며 그 시대 유럽 외교를 그토록 두루 욕되게 했던 그런 정치적 술책에도 능하다고 여겨졌기 때문이다.\n\n이 모든 분주하고 기발한 추측은 이제 자신을 사로잡은 자들의 행동으로 산산이 부서졌다. 그 덩치 큰 전사를 따라간 무리는 〈호리컨〉의 기슭 쪽으로 길을 잡았고, 그 자신과 동행들에게는 야만의 정복자들에게 가망 없는 포로로 붙들려 있으리라는 것 말고는 어떤 다른 기대도 남지 않았다. 최악을 알고자 안달하고, 이런 위급한 상황에서 황금의 위력을 시험해 볼 마음에서, 그는 〈마구아〉에게 말 거는 데 대한 꺼림을 이겨 냈다. 이제 일행의 앞으로의 움직임을 지휘할 자로서의 권위와 태도를 띤, 얼마 전까지의 안내자에게, 그는 가능한 한 다정하고 미덥게 들리는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"〈마구아〉에게, 그토록 위대한 추장만이 들을 만한 것을 말하고 싶다.\"\n\n인디언은 경멸하듯 젊은 군인에게 눈길을 돌리며 대답했다.\n\n\"말하라. 나무에는 귀가 없으니!\"\n\n\"허나 붉은 〈휴런〉들은 귀머거리가 아니다. 한 민족의 위대한 자들에게 어울리는 의논은 젊은 전사들을 취하게 만들 것이다. 〈마구아〉가 듣지 않겠다면, 왕의 장교는 침묵하는 법을 안다.\"\n\n야만인은 동료들에게 무심히 몇 마디 건넸으니, 그들은 그 어설픈 솜씨로 자매들을 태울 채비로 말들을 손보기에 분주했다. 그러고는 한쪽으로 조금 비켜서며, 조심스러운 몸짓으로 〈헤이워드〉를 그리로 따라오게 했다.\n\n\"이제 말하라.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 말이 〈마구아〉가 들을 만한 것이라면.\"\n\n\"교활한 〈르나르〉는 자신의 〈캐나다〉 아버지들이 붙여 준 그 명예로운 이름에 걸맞은 자임을 입증했다.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 운을 뗐다. \"나는 그의 지혜를, 그리고 그가 우리를 위해 한 모든 일을 보았고, 그에게 보답할 때가 오면 그것을 기억할 것이다. 그렇다! 〈르나르〉는 자신이 평의에서 위대한 추장일 뿐 아니라, 적을 속이는 법을 아는 자임을 입증했다!\"\n\n\"〈르나르〉가 무엇을 했단 말인가?\" 인디언이 차갑게 물었다.\n\n\"무엇을 했느냐고! 숲이 적들의 매복한 무리로 가득 찼고, 〈세르팡〉이 들키지 않고는 그 사이로 빠져나갈 수 없다는 것을 그가 보지 못했단 말인가? 그러고는 〈휴런〉들의 눈을 가리려 길을 잃은 척하지 않았던가? 자신을 박대하고 개처럼 천막에서 내쫓은 제 종족에게 돌아가는 척하지 않았던가? 그리고 그가 무엇을 바라는지 우리가 알아챘을 때, 〈휴런〉들이 백인이 제 친구를 적이라 믿는다고 여기도록, 거짓 얼굴을 지어 우리가 그를 돕지 않았던가? 이 모든 것이 사실이 아닌가? 그리고 〈교활한 자〉가 제 지혜로 제 민족의 눈을 가리고 귀를 막았을 때, 그들이 한때 그에게 부당하게 굴어 그를 〈모호크〉족에게로 달아나게 만들었던 일을 잊지 않았던가? 그리고 그들이 강의 남쪽에 그를 포로들과 함께 남겨 두고, 어리석게도 북쪽으로 가버리지 않았던가? 〈르나르〉는 여우처럼 제 발자취를 되짚어 돌아서서, 부유하고 백발인 그 스코틀랜드인에게 그 딸들을 데려갈 작정이 아닌가? 그렇다, 〈마구아〉여, 나는 그 모두를 알며, 그토록 큰 지혜와 정직에 어떻게 보답해야 할지 이미 헤아려 보았다. 먼저, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 추장은 그런 봉사에 대해 위대한 추장이 마땅히 줄 만큼 줄 것이다. 〈마구아〉의 메달은 더 이상 양철이 아니라 두드린 금이 될 것이며, 그의 화약통은 화약으로 넘쳐흐를 것이고, 달러가 그의 주머니 속에 〈호리컨〉 기슭의 자갈만큼 흔할 것이며, 사슴들은 그의 손을 핥을 터이니, 그가 지닐 총에서 달아나 봤자 부질없음을 알 것이기 때문이다! 나로 말하면, 그 스코틀랜드인의 고마움을 어떻게 넘어설 수 있을지 모르겠으나, 나는 — 그래, 나는 —\"\n\n\"해 뜨는 쪽에서 온 젊은 추장은 무엇을 주겠는가?\" 〈휴런〉이, 인디언의 소망 가운데 그 절정을 이룰 만한 것으로 그 은혜의 나열을 끝맺으려다 〈헤이워드〉가 머뭇거리는 것을 알아채고 물었다.\n\n\"그는 짠 호수의 섬들에서 나는 불의 물을, 인디언의 마음이 벌새의 깃털보다 가벼워지고 그 숨결이 야생 인동덩굴보다 달콤해질 때까지, 〈마구아〉의 천막 앞에 흐르게 할 것이다.\"\n\n〈르나르〉는 〈헤이워드〉가 그 교묘한 말을 천천히 이어 가는 동안 진지하게 귀 기울였다. 젊은이가, 인디언이 제 민족에게 부렸으리라 짐작한 술수를 언급했을 때, 듣는 자의 얼굴은 조심스러운 진지함의 표정으로 가려졌다. 〈휴런〉을 그 본래 종족에게서 달아나게 했다고 〈던컨〉이 믿는 척한 그 상처를 넌지시 비추자, 다른 자의 눈에서 걷잡을 수 없는 흉포함의 섬광이 번뜩였으니, 이 대담한 화자로 하여금 자신이 알맞은 가락을 짚었다고 믿게 했다. 그리고 그가 복수의 갈증을 이득의 욕망과 그토록 교묘히 뒤섞은 대목에 이르렀을 무렵엔, 적어도 그 야만인의 가장 깊은 주의를 사로잡는 데는 성공해 있었다. 〈르나르〉가 던진 물음은 차분했고, 인디언다운 온갖 위엄을 갖춘 것이었으나, 듣는 자의 생각에 잠긴 얼굴빛으로 미루어, 그 대답이 더없이 교활하게 궁리되었음은 자못 분명했다. 〈휴런〉은 몇 순간 골똘히 생각하더니, 부상당한 어깨의 거친 붕대 위에 손을 얹고, 다소 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"벗들이 그런 자국을 남긴단 말인가?\"\n\n\"〈긴 총〉이라면 적에게 그토록 가벼이 한 칼 베었겠는가?\"\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉들은 제가 사랑하는 자들에게, 뱀처럼 몸을 비틀어 치려고 기어든단 말인가?\"\n\n\"〈르 그로 세르팡〉이라면, 귀머거리이기를 바라는 자의 귀에 들렸겠는가?\"\n\n\"흰 추장은 제 형제들의 얼굴에 대고 화약을 태운단 말인가?\"\n\n\"진정 죽일 작정으로 겨눌 때, 그가 과녁을 빗맞히기라도 한단 말인가?\" 〈던컨〉이 그럴듯하게 꾸민 진정성으로 미소 지으며 대꾸했다.\n\n이 함축적인 물음과 막힘없는 대답에 또 한 차례의 길고 신중한 침묵이 이어졌다. 〈던컨〉은 인디언이 망설이고 있음을 보았다. 자신의 승리를 완결 짓고자, 그가 막 그 보답의 나열을 다시 시작하려는 참에, 〈마구아〉가 뜻있는 몸짓을 하며 말했다.\n\n\"되었다. 〈르나르〉는 지혜로운 추장이고, 그가 하는 일은 곧 보게 될 것이다. 가서 입을 다물고 있으라. 〈마구아〉가 말할 때가, 대답할 때이리라.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 자신의 동행자의 눈이 무리의 나머지에게 경계하듯 박혀 있음을 알아채고는, 그 우두머리와 의심스러운 결탁을 한 듯한 기색을 피하고자 곧장 물러났다. 〈마구아〉는 말들에게 다가가, 동료들의 부지런함과 솜씨에 흡족한 척했다. 그러고는 〈헤이워드〉에게 자매들을 안장에 태우는 것을 거들라고 손짓했으니, 그는 평소보다 더 중대한 어떤 동기에 떠밀리지 않는 한 영어를 입에 올리는 일이 좀처럼 없었기 때문이다.\n\n이제는 미룰 그럴듯한 핑계가 더는 없었고, 〈던컨〉은 마지못해서나마 따를 수밖에 없었다. 이 일을 하는 동안, 그는 되살아나는 희망을, 떨고 있는 여인들의 귀에 속삭였으니, 그들은 자신들을 사로잡은 자들의 야만스러운 얼굴과 마주치는 것이 두려워, 좀처럼 눈을 땅에서 들지 못했다. 〈데이비드〉의 암말은 그 큰 추장의 부하들과 함께 끌려갔으므로, 그 결과 그 임자도 〈던컨〉처럼 걸어서 가야만 했다. 그러나 후자는 이 일을 그다지 한탄하지 않았으니, 그것이 일행의 속도를 늦출 수 있게 해 줄지도 몰랐기 때문이다. 그는 여전히 〈에드워드〉 요새 쪽으로 간절한 눈길을 돌리며, 구원의 접근을 알릴 만한 어떤 소리를 그 숲의 방향에서 붙잡으려는 헛된 기대를 품고 있었다.\n\n모두 채비를 마치자, 〈마구아〉가 출발하라는 신호를 하며, 앞으로 나아가 몸소 일행을 이끌었다. 그다음에는 〈데이비드〉가 따랐으니, 상처의 영향이 점점 옅어져 감에 따라 자신의 처지에 대한 참된 분별이 차츰 돌아오고 있었다. 자매들은 그 뒤에서 〈헤이워드〉를 곁에 두고 말을 탔으며, 인디언들은 일행의 양옆을 에워싸고, 결코 지칠 줄 모르는 듯한 경계로 행렬의 끝을 맡았다.\n\n이런 식으로 그들은, 〈헤이워드〉가 여인들에게 이따금 위안의 말 한마디를 건네거나, 〈데이비드〉가 체념의 겸허함을 나타내려는 양 가련한 탄성으로 제 영혼의 신음을 토할 때를 빼고는, 끊임없는 침묵 속에 나아갔다. 그들이 잡은 방향은 남쪽으로, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉로 가는 길과는 거의 정반대인 진로였다. 〈마구아〉가 겉으로는 이렇게 정복자들의 본래 결정을 따르고 있었음에도, 〈헤이워드〉는 자신이 던진 유혹의 미끼가 그토록 빨리 잊혔으리라고는 믿을 수 없었다. 게다가 그는 인디언이 다니는 길의 굽이를 너무도 잘 알았기에, 술수가 조금이라도 필요한 마당에 그 겉보기 진로가 곧장 목적지로 이어지리라고는 여기지 않았다. 그러나 끝없는 숲을 지나, 여정의 끝이 보일 기미라곤 없이, 이 고통스러운 방식으로 몇 마일이고 또 몇 마일이 지나갔다. 〈헤이워드〉는 나뭇가지 사이로 한낮의 햇살을 쏘아 보내는 해를 지켜보며, 〈마구아〉의 계략이 그들의 진로를 자신의 희망에 더 유리한 쪽으로 바꿀 그 순간을 애타게 기다렸다. 때로는, 그 빈틈없는 야만인이 〈몽칼름〉의 군세를 무사히 지나치기를 단념하고, 잘 알려진 변경 정착지로 향하는 길을 잡고 있는 것이리라 상상하기도 했으니, 그곳에는 왕실의 한 이름난 장교이자 〈여섯 부족〉의 총애받는 벗이 그 너른 영지와 더불어 평소의 거처를 두고 있었다. 〈윌리엄 존슨〉 경의 손에 넘겨지는 편이 〈캐나다〉의 황야로 끌려가는 것보다는 훨씬 나았으나, 전자나마 이루려 해도, 그 한 걸음 한 걸음이 그를 전쟁의 무대에서, 따라서 명예의 자리에서뿐 아니라 의무의 자리에서 점점 더 멀리 데려가는, 숲을 가로지르는 지치도록 먼 여러 리그를 횡단해야만 할 터였다.\n\n오직 〈코라〉만이 척후가 헤어질 때 남긴 당부를 기억하고 있었으니, 기회가 있을 때마다 그녀는 손에 닿는 잔가지들을 꺾어 비틀려고 팔을 뻗었다. 그러나 인디언들의 경계는 이 조심스러운 행동을 어렵고도 위험하게 만들었다. 그녀는 그들의 빈틈없는 눈과 마주칠 때면 자주 그 뜻을 이루지 못했고, 그럴 때면 느끼지도 않은 놀람을 가장하여, 여인다운 두려움의 어떤 몸짓으로 그 손을 채워야 했다. 단 한 번, 오직 한 번 그녀는 완전히 성공했으니, 큰 옻나무 가지 하나를 꺾어 내며, 불현듯 떠오른 생각으로 같은 순간에 제 장갑을 떨어뜨렸을 때였다. 뒤따라올 자들을 위한 이 표시는 그녀를 데려가던 자 중 하나에게 들켰으니, 그는 장갑을 되돌려주고, 그 덤불의 남은 가지들을, 마치 어떤 짐승이 그 가지 사이에서 발버둥 친 것처럼 보이게끔 꺾어 놓고는, 제 손도끼에 손을 얹으며 그토록 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌기에, 이 몰래 남기던 자취의 표시는 영영 끝나고 말았다.\n\n두 인디언 무리 모두에 발자국을 남길 말들이 있었으므로, 이 방해로 그들의 자취를 통해 도움이 전해질 만한 어떤 그럴듯한 희망마저 끊기고 말았다.\n\n〈마구아〉의 음울한 과묵함에 무언가 고무될 만한 구석이라도 있었다면, 〈헤이워드〉는 항의를 무릅썼을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 야만인은 이 모든 시간 동안 좀처럼 제 추종자들을 돌아보지 않았고, 결코 입을 열지 않았다. 오직 해를 길잡이로 삼거나, 원주민의 명민함만이 아는 그런 어렴풋한 표지의 도움을 받아, 그는 소나무 황무지를 따라, 이따금 나타나는 작고 비옥한 골짜기를 지나, 시내와 개울을 건너, 굽이치는 언덕을 넘어, 본능의 정확함으로, 그리고 거의 새의 곧음으로 제 길을 나아갔다. 그는 결코 망설이는 법이 없는 듯했다. 길이 거의 알아볼 수 없든, 사라져 버렸든, 아니면 그의 앞에 또렷이 다져진 채 놓여 있든, 그의 속도나 확신에는 이렇다 할 차이가 없었다. 마치 피로라는 것이 그에게는 미치지 못하는 듯했다. 지친 길손들의 눈이 자신들이 밟고 가는 썩은 잎사귀에서 들릴 때마다, 그의 거무튀튀한 형상이 앞쪽 나무줄기들 사이로 어른거리는 것이 보였으니, 그 머리는 미동도 없이 앞을 향해 고정된 채였고, 그 볏 위의 가벼운 깃털은 오직 제 움직임의 날램만으로 일어난 바람결에 나부끼고 있었다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 부지런함과 속도에는 까닭이 없지 않았다. 콸콸 흐르는 시내가 굽이치는 낮은 골짜기 하나를 건넌 뒤, 그는 자매들이 따라오려면 말에서 내릴 수밖에 없을 만큼 가파르고 오르기 힘든 언덕 하나를 별안간 올랐다. 꼭대기에 이르자, 그들은 나무가 듬성듬성 덮인 평평한 자리에 와 있음을 알게 되었으니, 그 한 그루 아래에 〈마구아〉가 자신의 거무튀튀한 몸을 내던지고 있었다. 마치 일행 모두가 그토록 절실히 바라던 그 휴식을, 기꺼이, 마다 않고 구하려는 듯이.\n* 메달: 인디언 추장에게 동맹의 표시로 수여하던 표장.\n\n第11장\n\"내 부족에게 저주받으리라\n\n내가 그를 용서한다면.\"\n\n샤일록\n인디언은 이 바람직한 목적을 위해, 인공으로 쌓아 올린 둔덕과 흡사하여 아메리카의 골짜기마다 흔히 마주치게 되는, 그 가파르고 피라미드처럼 솟은 언덕 가운데 하나를 골랐다. 지금 그 언덕은 높고 험준했으며, 꼭대기는 여느 것들처럼 평평했으나, 한쪽 면이 유달리 들쭉날쭉했다. 그것이 쉴 곳으로서 지닌 유일하게 뚜렷한 이점이라곤, 방어를 수월하게 하고 기습을 거의 불가능하게 만들 만한 그 높이와 형세뿐이었다. 그러나 〈헤이워드〉는 이제 시간과 거리가 그토록 가망 없게 만들어 버린 구조를 더는 기대하지 않았던 까닭에, 이 사소한 특징들을 아무 관심도 없는 눈으로 바라보며, 자신은 오로지 한층 연약한 동행들을 위로하고 편안하게 하는 일에만 몰두했다. 〈내러갠셋〉종 말들은 언덕 꼭대기에 듬성듬성 흩어진 나무와 떨기나무 가지를 뜯도록 내버려 두었고, 그동안 남은 식량은, 마치 천막처럼 수평으로 가지를 뻗은 너도밤나무 그늘 아래 펼쳐졌다.\n\n도주가 그토록 황급했음에도, 인디언 가운데 하나는 무리에서 떨어진 새끼 사슴을 화살로 쏘아 맞힐 틈을 찾아냈고, 그 제물에서 가장 먹음직한 부위를 어깨에 인내심 있게 짊어지고 이 쉴 곳까지 옮겨 왔다. 요리법의 도움이라곤 일절 없이, 그는 곧장 동료들과 더불어 이 소화 잘되는 양식을 게걸스레 먹어 치우는 일에 매달렸다. 오직 〈마구아〉만이 떨어져 앉아, 이 역겨운 식사에 끼지 않은 채, 보기에 더없이 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n배고픔을 채울 수단을 가졌을 때조차 인디언에게는 그토록 보기 드문 이 절식이, 마침내 〈헤이워드〉의 눈길을 끌었다. 청년은 기꺼이, 그 〈휴런〉족이 동료들의 경계를 따돌릴 가장 적합한 방도를 궁리하는 중이라고 믿었다. 자신의 어떤 제안으로든 그 계획을 돕고, 유혹을 한층 강화할 요량으로, 그는 너도밤나무를 떠나 아무 목적도 없는 듯 어슬렁거리며 〈르나르〉가 앉은 자리로 다가갔다.\n\n\"〈마구아〉가 〈캐나다인〉들의 위험에서 모두 벗어날 만큼, 충분히 오래 해를 등지고 걸어오지 않았소?\" 그는 그들 사이에 좋은 양해가 이미 이루어졌음을 더는 의심치 않는 듯이 물었다. \"또〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 지휘관은, 그가 딸들을 잃은 슬픔에 마음이 굳어져 보답에 인색해지기 전, 또 하룻밤이 지나기 전에 딸들을 보는 편을 더 기뻐하지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"창백한 얼굴들은 제 자식을 밤보다 아침에 덜 사랑하오?\" 인디언이 차갑게 물었다.\n\n\"결코 그렇지 않소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 혹여 자신이 실언을 했다면 그것을 되돌리고자 애쓰며 대답했다. \"백인은 제 조상의 무덤 자리를 잊을 수도 있고, 또 흔히 잊소. 때로는 마땅히 사랑해야 하고 아끼겠노라 약속한 이들을 기억하지 못하기도 하오. 그러나 자식을 향한 부모의 정만큼은 결코 죽도록 내버려 두지 않는 법이오.\"\n\n\"그래, 흰머리 지휘관의 마음은 부드러우며, 제 여인들이 낳아 준 어린것들을 생각하겠소? 그는 제 전사들에게는 모질고, 그 눈은 돌로 되어 있던데!\"\n\n\"그는 게으르고 사악한 자들에게는 엄하나, 진중하고 떳떳한 자들에게는 지휘관이로되, 공정하고 인자하오. 나는 자식을 끔찍이 아끼고 다정한 부모를 숱하게 보아 왔지만, 그토록 자식을 향한 마음이 부드러운 사내는 본 적이 없소. 〈마구아〉, 그대는 그 흰머리가 전사들 앞에 선 모습을 보았겠지만, 나는 지금 그대의 손아귀에 든 그 자식들을 이야기할 때 그의 눈이 눈물에 젖는 것을 보았소!\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 말을 멈추었으니, 귀 기울이던 그 인디언의 가무잡잡한 얼굴을 스쳐 번뜩인 기이한 표정을 어찌 풀이해야 할지 알 수 없었던 까닭이다. 처음엔, 약속된 보답에 대한 기억이 그의 마음속에서 또렷해지는 듯했으니, 그 보답을 손에 넣게 해 줄 부정(父情)의 근원에 대한 이야기에 귀를 기울이는 동안이 그러했다. 그러나 〈덩컨〉이 말을 이어 갈수록, 그 기쁨의 표정은 어찌나 사납고 표독스러워졌던지, 그것이 탐욕보다 한층 음험한 어떤 격정에서 비롯된 것임을 헤아리지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"가라.\" 그 〈휴런〉족은 그 섬뜩한 기색을 순식간에 억누르고, 죽은 듯이 잔잔해진 낯빛으로 말했다. \"검은 머리 딸에게 가서, 〈마구아〉가 할 말이 있어 기다린다고 전하라. 아비는 그 딸이 약속한 바를 기억할 것이다.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉은 이 말을, 약속된 선물이 보류되지 않으리라는 추가의 다짐을 바라는 뜻으로 풀이하고, 느릿하고 마지못한 걸음으로 두 자매가 피로를 풀며 쉬고 있는 곳으로 가, 그 취지를 〈코라〉에게 전했다.\n\n\"당신은 인디언이 무엇을 원하는지 그 본질을 아실 테니,\" 그는 그녀가 기다려지고 있는 자리로 그녀를 데려가며 말을 맺었다. \"화약이며 담요를 아낌없이 제안하셔야 합니다. 그러나 저런 자가 가장 귀히 여기는 것은 독한 술이지요. 또 당신이 그토록 능란하게 베풀 줄 아는 그 우아함으로, 손수 무언가 호의를 더하는 것도 나쁘지 않을 겁니다. 명심하십시오, 〈코라〉, 당신의 침착함과 기지에 〈앨리스〉의 목숨은 물론, 어느 정도는 당신 자신의 목숨까지도 달려 있을지 모른다는 것을.\"\n\n\"〈헤이워드〉, 당신의 목숨도요!\"\n\n\"내 목숨은 별것 아닙니다. 이미 내 왕에게 팔아넘긴 것이요, 그만한 힘을 지닌 적이라면 누구든 거머쥘 수 있는 전리품일 뿐이지요. 나를 기다릴 아비도 없고, 명성을 향한 젊음의 채울 길 없는 갈망으로 자초한 운명을 애도해 줄 벗도 몇 안 됩니다. 하지만 쉿! 인디언이 가까워졌습니다. 〈마구아〉, 그대가 이야기하고자 한 부인이 여기 계시오.\"\n\n인디언은 천천히 자리에서 일어나, 일 분 가까이 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 서 있었다. 그러다 손짓으로 〈헤이워드〉에게 물러가라 이르며 차갑게 말했다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉족이 여인에게 말할 때면, 그 부족은 귀를 닫는 법이오.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉이 응하기를 거부하듯 여전히 머뭇거리자, 〈코라〉가 잔잔한 미소를 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"들으셨지요, 〈헤이워드〉. 적어도 예의를 생각해서라도 물러가셔야 합니다. 〈앨리스〉에게 가서, 되살아난 우리의 희망으로 그 애를 위로해 주세요.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 떠날 때까지 기다렸다가, 그 원주민을 향해 돌아서서, 여인의 위엄을 목소리와 몸가짐에 실어 덧붙였다. \"〈르나르〉가 〈먼로〉의 딸에게 무슨 말을 하려는 거지요?\"\n\n\"들으시오.\" 인디언이 자신의 말에 그녀의 온 주의를 끌어모으려는 듯, 그녀의 팔을 단단히 붙잡으며 말했다. 〈코라〉는 그 팔을 그의 손아귀에서 빼내어, 단호하되 조용히 그 동작을 물리쳤다. \"〈마구아〉는 호수의 붉은 〈휴런〉족 가운데서 추장이자 전사로 태어났소. 그는 창백한 얼굴을 처음 보기 전, 스무 번의 여름 해가 스무 번의 겨울 눈을 시냇물로 녹여 흘려보내는 것을 보았소. 그리고 행복했소! 그런데 그의 〈캐나다〉 아비들이 숲으로 들어와, 그에게 불물을 마시는 법을 가르쳤고, 그는 건달이 되고 말았소. 〈휴런〉족은 쫓기는 들소를 몰아내듯, 그를 조상의 무덤에서 내쫓았소. 그는 호수의 기슭을 따라 달려 내려가, 그 물줄기가 흘러 나가는 곳을 좇아 '대포의 도시'에 이르렀소. 그곳에서 그는 사냥하고 고기를 잡았으나, 사람들이 또다시 숲을 지나 그를 적들의 품으로 내몰았소. 〈휴런〉족으로 태어난 그 추장이, 끝내 〈모호크〉족 가운데서 전사가 된 것이오!\"\n\n\"그 비슷한 이야기를 전에 들은 적이 있어요.\" 〈코라〉가 말했으니, 그가 자신이 입었다 여기는 상처의 기억을 떠올리며 너무도 환히 불타오르기 시작하는 격정을 억누르느라 말을 멈추는 것을 본 까닭이었다.\n\n\"〈르나르〉의 머리가 바위로 되어 있지 않은 것이 그의 잘못이오? 누가 그에게 불물을 주었소? 누가 그를 악당으로 만들었소? 바로 창백한 얼굴들, 당신과 같은 빛깔의 족속이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 나와 살빛이 비슷할지 모르는, 생각 없고 줏대 없는 자들이 존재하는 것에 대해 내가 책임이라도 져야 하나요?\" 〈코라〉가 흥분한 그 미개인에게 차분히 따져 물었다.\n\n\"아니오. 〈마구아〉는 사내요, 바보가 아니오. 당신 같은 이는 결코 그 타오르는 물줄기에 입을 대지 않지. 〈위대한 영(靈)〉이 당신에게 지혜를 주었으니까!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그대의 불운에 대해, 그대의 잘못은 말할 것도 없거니와, 내가 무엇을 하고, 무엇을 말해야 한다는 거지요?\"\n\n\"들으시오.\" 인디언이 다시 진지한 태도로 돌아가며 거듭 말했다. \"그의 〈영국〉 아비와 〈프랑스〉 아비가 도끼를 파냈을 때, 〈르나르〉는 〈모호크〉족의 전쟁 기둥을 치고, 제 동족을 향해 나아갔소. 창백한 얼굴들은 붉은 살갗을 사냥터에서 몰아냈고, 이제 그들이 싸울 때는 백인이 앞장을 서지. 〈호리컨〉의 그 늙은 추장, 곧 당신의 아비가 우리 전투대의 대장이었소. 그는 〈모호크〉족에게 이것을 하라, 저것을 하라 일렀고, 그대로 따랐소. 그는 한 가지 법을 만들었으니, 인디언이 불물을 삼키고 제 전사들의 천막으로 들어오면, 그것을 잊지 않겠다는 것이었소. 〈마구아〉는 어리석게 입을 열었고, 그 뜨거운 술이 그를 〈먼로〉의 막사로 이끌었소. 그 흰머리가 어찌했소? 그의 딸이 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"그분은 자신의 말씀을 잊지 않으셨고, 죄지은 자를 벌하여 정의를 행하셨어요.\" 의연한 딸이 말했다.\n\n\"정의라!\" 인디언이 그녀의 굽힘 없는 얼굴에 더없이 흉포한 표정의 곁눈질을 던지며 되뇌었다. \"악을 만들어 놓고, 그것을 빌미로 벌하는 것이 정의요? 〈마구아〉는 제정신이 아니었소. 그를 대신해 말하고 행한 것은 불물이었소! 그런데도 〈먼로〉는 그것을 믿지 않았소. 〈휴런〉족 추장은 창백한 얼굴의 전사들이 모두 보는 앞에서 묶인 채, 개처럼 채찍질을 당했소.\"\n\n〈코라〉는 잠자코 있었으니, 아비 쪽의 이 경솔한 가혹함을, 인디언이 알아들을 만한 방식으로 어떻게 누그러뜨려 변명해야 할지 알 수 없었던 까닭이다.\n\n\"보시오!\" 〈마구아〉가 칠을 한 가슴을 형편없이 가리고 있던 얇은 옥양목을 잡아 헤치며 말을 이었다. \"여기 칼과 총알이 남긴 상처들이 있소. 이런 것이라면 전사가 제 부족 앞에서 자랑할 만하지. 허나 그 흰머리는 〈휴런〉족 추장의 등에, 여인네처럼 백인들의 이 칠한 천 아래 숨겨야만 하는 자국을 남겨 놓았소.\"\n\n\"나는 인디언 전사란 참을성이 있고,\" 〈코라〉가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그 혼은 제 몸이 겪는 고통을 느끼지도 알지도 못하는 줄로 알았어요.\"\n\n\"〈치페와〉족이 〈마구아〉를 말뚝에 묶고 이 상처를 냈을 때,\" 상대가 깊은 흉터에 손가락을 얹으며 말했다. \"그 〈휴런〉족은 그들의 면전에서 웃으며, 여인네들이 이보다 세게 친다고 했소. 그때 그의 혼은 구름 속에 있었소! 그러나 〈먼로〉의 매질을 느꼈을 때, 그의 혼은 자작나무 회초리 아래 깔려 있었소. 〈휴런〉족의 혼은 결코 취하는 법이 없으며, 영원히 기억하오!\"\n\n\"하지만 그 혼도 달랠 수 있어요. 만약 내 아버지가 그대에게 이런 부당한 짓을 했다면, 인디언이 어떻게 상처를 용서하는지 그분께 보여 주고, 그 딸들을 도로 데려가게 해 주세요. 〈헤이워드〉 소령에게서 이미 들으셨다시피——\"\n\n〈마구아〉는 고개를 저어, 그토록 경멸해 마지않는 제안을 되풀이하지 못하게 했다.\n\n\"그럼 그대가 바라는 게 무엇인가요?\" 〈코라〉가 더없이 괴로운 침묵 끝에 말을 이었으니, 그동안 너무도 낙관적이고 너그러운 〈덩컨〉이 그 미개인의 간계에 잔혹하게 속아 넘어갔다는 확신이 그녀의 마음을 짓눌렀다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉족이 사랑하는 것——선에는 선으로, 악에는 악으로!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그대는 〈먼로〉가 끼친 상처를 그의 가엾은 딸들에게 되갚으려는 거군요. 그분의 얼굴 앞으로 나아가, 전사로서 응징을 취하는 편이 더 사내답지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"창백한 얼굴들의 팔은 길고, 그 칼은 날카롭소!\" 미개인이 표독스레 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"이미 그 흰머리의 혼을 손아귀에 쥐고 있는데, 어찌 〈르나르〉가 그 전사들의 머스킷 총 가운데로 들어가겠소?\"\n\n\"그대의 속셈을 말하세요, 〈마구아〉.\" 〈코라〉가 흔들림 없이 침착하게 말하려 스스로와 싸우며 말했다. \"우리를 포로로 숲으로 끌고 가려는 건가요, 아니면 그보다 더 끔찍한 악행을 꾀하는 건가요? 그 상처를 누그러뜨리고 그대의 마음을 풀어 줄 보상도, 방도도 전혀 없나요? 적어도 내 가여운 동생만은 풀어 주고, 그대의 모든 원한은 내게 쏟으세요. 동생의 안전으로 재물을 사들이고, 단 한 사람의 제물로 복수를 채우세요. 두 딸을 다 잃으면 그 노인은 무덤에 들지도 모르니, 그러면 〈르나르〉의 만족은 어디에 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"들으시오.\" 인디언이 다시 말했다. \"검은 머리 여인이 제 조상의 〈위대한 영〉께 맹세코 거짓을 말하지 않겠다고 한다면, 밝은 눈은 〈호리컨〉으로 돌아가 늙은 추장에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 전할 수 있소.\"\n\n\"내가 무엇을 약속해야 하나요?\" 〈코라〉가, 차분하고 여성스러운 위엄으로 그 사나운 원주민을 은밀히 압도하며 물었다.\n\n\"〈마구아〉가 제 부족을 떠났을 때, 그의 아내는 다른 추장에게 주어졌소. 이제 그는 〈휴런〉족과 화해했으니, 그 큰 호수의 기슭, 제 부족의 무덤으로 돌아갈 것이오. 〈영국〉 추장의 딸이 그를 따라가, 그의 천막에서 영원히 살게 하시오.\"\n\n이런 부류의 제안이 〈코라〉에게 아무리 역겨웠을지언정, 그녀는 그 격렬한 혐오감에도 불구하고, 그 약함을 드러내지 않고 대답할 만큼의 자제력을 잃지 않았다.\n\n\"사랑하지도 않는 아내, 게다가 제 것과는 다른 종족, 다른 살빛의 아내와 천막을 함께 쓰며 〈마구아〉가 무슨 기쁨을 얻겠어요? 차라리 〈먼로〉의 황금을 받아, 그 선물로 어느 〈휴런〉족 처녀의 마음을 사는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?\"\n\n인디언은 일 분 가까이 아무런 대꾸도 없이, 그 사나운 눈길을 〈코라〉의 얼굴에 박아 넣었는데, 그 흔들리는 눈초리가 어찌나 음험했던지, 정숙한 여인이라면 도저히 견딜 수 없는 어떤 표정과 처음으로 마주쳤다는 느낌에, 그녀의 눈이 부끄러움으로 내리깔렸다. 그녀가 지난번보다 더 충격적인 어떤 제안에 귀를 다칠까 두려워 제 안으로 움츠러드는 동안, 〈마구아〉의 목소리가 더없이 음험한 음색으로 대답했다.\n\n\"매질이 그 〈휴런〉족의 등을 지져 댈 때, 그는 그 쓰라림을 함께 느낄 여인을 어디서 찾아야 할지 알게 될 것이오. 〈먼로〉의 딸이 그의 물을 긷고, 그의 옥수수밭을 매고, 그의 사슴고기를 지을 것이오. 그 흰머리의 몸뚱이는 제 대포들 가운데서 잠들겠지만, 그의 심장은 〈르 쉬블〉의 칼이 닿는 곳에 놓여 있게 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"악마! 그 음흉한 이름이 네게 참으로 어울리는구나!\" 〈코라〉가 걷잡을 수 없는 효심의 분노로 부르짖었다. \"오직 마귀만이 그런 복수를 꾸밀 수 있을 것이다! 그러나 너는 네 힘을 과대평가하고 있어! 네가 손아귀에 쥔 것은 참으로 〈먼로〉의 심장이며, 그것이 네 극악한 악의를 비웃으리라는 것을 알게 되리라!\"\n\n인디언은 이 대담한 항거에, 변치 않는 결심을 드러내는 섬뜩한 미소로 답하면서, 마치 그 회담을 영영 끝맺으려는 듯 그녀에게 물러가라 손짓했다. 이미 자신의 성급함을 후회하던 〈코라〉는 따를 수밖에 없었으니, 〈마구아〉가 곧장 그 자리를 떠나 게걸스러운 동료들에게로 다가갔던 까닭이다. 〈헤이워드〉가 동요한 여인의 곁으로 달려와, 그토록 깊은 관심으로 멀리서 지켜보던 대화의 결말을 물었다. 그러나 그녀는 〈앨리스〉의 불안을 자극하지 않으려, 직접적인 대답을 피한 채, 오직 표정으로만 자신이 완전히 실패했음을 드러내며, 근심 어린 눈길을 그들을 사로잡은 자들의 사소한 움직임 하나하나에 붙박아 두었다. 그들이 어디로 가게 될지 거듭 간곡히 묻는 동생에게, 그녀는 다스릴 수 없는 동요로 그 검은 무리를 가리키는 것 말고는 다른 답을 하지 못했고, 〈앨리스〉를 제 가슴에 끌어안으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"저기, 저기. 저들의 얼굴에서 우리의 운명을 읽으렴. 곧 알게 될 거야. 곧 알게 될 거야!\"\n\n〈코라〉의 그 동작과 목멘 말은 어떤 말보다도 더 절절하게 와닿았고, 동행들의 시선을 재빨리, 그녀 자신의 눈길이 박혀 있던 그곳으로 끌어당겼으니, 걸린 것의 막중함만이 자아낼 수 있는 그런 골똘함으로 응시한 것이었다.\n\n〈마구아〉가, 그 역겨운 식사로 배를 잔뜩 채우고 짐승 같은 방종 속에 땅바닥에 길게 드러누운 미개인 무리에게 다다랐을 때, 그는 인디언 추장의 위엄으로 말하기 시작했다. 그가 내뱉은 첫 음절들은 듣는 이들로 하여금 공손히 귀 기울이는 자세로 몸을 일으키게 하는 효과를 냈다. 그 〈휴런〉족은 제 모국어를 썼던 까닭에, 포로들은, 원주민들의 경계로 인해 그 토마호크가 닿는 거리 안에 붙들려 있긴 했어도, 인디언이 늘 제 웅변에 곁들이는 그 의미심장한 몸짓의 성격으로 그 일장연설의 요지를 그저 짐작할 수 있을 따름이었다.\n\n처음엔 〈마구아〉의 말도, 몸짓도 차분하고 신중해 보였다. 그가 동료들의 주의를 충분히 일깨우는 데 성공하자, 〈헤이워드〉는 그가 큰 호수들 쪽을 자주 가리키는 것으로 미루어, 그들 조상의 땅과 멀리 있는 부족을 이야기하는 것이라 짐작했다. 듣는 이들에게서 갈채의 기색이 잦게 새어 나왔으니, 그들은 그 표현적인 \"허!\" 소리를 내뱉으며, 연사를 칭찬하듯 서로를 마주 보았다. 〈르나르〉는 이 이점을 소홀히 하기에는 너무도 능란했다. 그는 이제, 그 광활한 땅과 행복한 마을을 떠나 〈캐나다〉 아비들의 적과 싸우러 오기까지 거쳐 온 길고 고통스러운 여정을 이야기했다. 그는 일행의 전사들을 하나하나 꼽으며, 저마다의 공적, 부족에 바친 잦은 봉사, 그들의 상처, 그리고 그들이 거둔 머릿가죽의 수효를 열거했다. 그가 그 자리의 누군가를 언급할 때마다(그리고 그 교활한 인디언은 한 사람도 빠뜨리지 않았다), 우쭐해진 자의 가무잡잡한 얼굴이 의기양양하게 번뜩였고, 그는 갈채와 동의의 몸짓으로 그 말의 진실함을 단언하기를 조금도 망설이지 않았다. 그러다 연사의 목소리가 낮아지며, 그들의 성공과 승리의 무용담을 늘어놓을 때의 그 크고 활기찬 승리의 어조를 잃었다. 그는 〈글렌〉 폭포를, 동굴이며 무수한 급류와 소용돌이를 거느린 그 바위섬의 난공불락의 형세를 그려 냈다. 그는 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉의 이름을 입에 올렸고, 그 미운 이름을 맞이하는 길고 우렁찬 함성의 마지막 메아리를 발아래 숲이 되돌려 보낼 때까지 말을 멈추었다. 그는 그 젊은 군인 포로를 가리키며, 제 손에 의해 깊은 협곡으로 떨어진 한 아끼던 전사의 죽음을 묘사했다. 그는 하늘과 땅 사이에 매달려 무리 전체에게 그토록 끔찍한 광경을 보여 준 그 전사의 운명을 언급했을 뿐 아니라, 어린나무 가지 위에서 그 처지의 공포와, 그의 결의와, 그의 죽음을 새로이 연기해 보였다. 그러고는 마침내, 자신들의 벗 하나하나가 어떻게 쓰러졌는지를 빠르게 되짚으며, 그들의 용기와 가장 인정받은 미덕에 빠짐없이 손을 댔다. 이 사건들의 서술이 끝나자, 그의 목소리는 다시 한번 바뀌어, 그 낮고 목쉰 소리 속에서 애처롭고, 심지어 가락마저 띠었다. 그는 이제 죽은 자들의 아내와 자식들을, 그들의 궁핍을, 육체와 정신 양면의 비참함을, 그들의 멀리 떨어진 처지를, 그리고 마침내 아직 복수하지 못한 그들의 원한을 이야기했다. 그러다 별안간 목소리를 무시무시한 기세의 높이로 끌어올려, 다그쳐 물으며 끝을 맺었다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉족이 이것을 견디는 개들이란 말인가? 누가 〈메노과〉의 아내에게, 물고기들이 그의 머릿가죽을 가져갔으며, 그의 부족은 복수하지 못했다고 말할 텐가! 누가 감히 〈와사와티미〉의 어미, 그 도도한 여인을 빈손으로 마주할 텐가! 노인들이 우리에게 머릿가죽을 내놓으라 물을 때, 그들에게 줄 백인의 머리털 한 올 없다면, 우리는 무어라 말할 텐가! 여인들이 우리에게 손가락질을 할 것이다. 〈휴런〉족의 이름에 검은 얼룩이 졌으니, 그것은 피로 씻어 가려야만 하리라!\"\n\n이제 공중으로 터져 나온 분노의 폭발 속에 그의 목소리는 더는 들리지 않았으니, 마치 숲이 그토록 작은 무리가 아니라 부족 전체로 가득 찬 듯했다. 앞선 연설 동안, 연사의 진행은 그의 성공에 가장 깊은 관심을 둔 이들에게, 그가 향해 말하는 자들의 얼굴을 매개로 너무도 환히 읽혔다. 그들은 그의 비탄과 애도에 공감과 슬픔으로 답했고, 그의 단언에는 동의의 몸짓으로, 그의 자랑에는 미개인의 의기양양함으로 답했다. 그가 용기를 이야기할 때 그들의 눈길은 굳건하고 호응하는 듯했고, 그가 그들의 상처를 들먹일 때 그들의 눈은 분노로 타올랐으며, 그가 여인들의 조롱을 언급할 때 그들은 부끄러움에 고개를 떨구었다. 그러나 그가 복수의 수단을 가리켜 보이자, 그는 인디언의 가슴속에서 결코 떨려 울리지 않는 법이 없는 한 가닥 현(絃)을 건드렸다. 그것이 제 손이 닿는 곳에 있다는 첫 암시에, 무리 전체가 한 사람처럼 벌떡 일어섰다. 더없이 광란에 찬 함성으로 분노를 토해 내며, 그들은 칼을 빼 들고 토마호크를 치켜든 채 한 덩어리가 되어 포로들에게 달려들었다. 〈헤이워드〉는 두 자매와 맨 앞의 자 사이로 몸을 던져, 한순간 그 난폭함을 막아 낸 필사적인 힘으로 그자와 맞붙었다. 이 뜻밖의 저항이 〈마구아〉에게 끼어들 틈을 주었고, 그는 빠른 발성과 활기찬 몸짓으로 무리의 주의를 다시 자신에게로 끌었다. 그토록 능란하게 구사할 줄 아는 그 언어로, 그는 동료들을 그 즉각적인 목적에서 돌려세우고, 그 제물들의 고통을 길게 늘이도록 청했다. 그의 제안은 갈채로 받아들여졌고, 생각의 속도로 실행에 옮겨졌다.\n\n힘센 전사 둘이 〈헤이워드〉에게 달려들었고, 그동안 또 하나는 덜 날쌘 그 성가대 선창자를 결박하는 일에 매달렸다. 그러나 두 포로 어느 쪽도, 비록 헛될지언정 필사적인 몸부림 없이 굴복하지는 않았다. 〈데이비드〉조차 자신을 덮친 자를 땅바닥에 메다꽂았고, 〈헤이워드〉도 동료를 제압한 승리 덕에 인디언들이 그 합쳐진 힘을 그를 향해 돌릴 수 있게 되기까지는 붙들리지 않았다. 이윽고 그는 묶여, 〈마구아〉가 떨어지는 〈휴런〉족의 무언극을 펼쳤던 그 어린나무 몸통에 단단히 매였다. 젊은 군인이 정신을 되찾았을 때, 그는 무리 전체에게 한가지 운명이 예정되어 있다는 고통스러운 확신을 눈앞에 두게 되었다. 그의 오른편에는 〈코라〉가, 자신과 비슷하게 결박된 채, 창백하고 동요한 모습이었으나, 그 눈만큼은 흔들림 없는 시선으로 여전히 적들의 거동을 읽고 있었다. 그의 왼편에서는, 〈앨리스〉를 소나무에 묶은 가지 끈이, 그녀의 떨리는 사지가 거부한 그 노릇을 대신해, 홀로 그 연약한 몸이 주저앉지 않도록 지탱하고 있었다. 그녀의 두 손은 기도하듯 앞으로 모아져 있었으나, 자신들을 구할 수 있는 유일한 힘을 향해 위를 우러르는 대신, 그 무심한 눈길은 어린아이 같은 의지심으로 〈덩컨〉의 얼굴을 향해 헤매었다. 〈데이비드〉는 항거했으나, 그 상황의 생경함이 그를 잠자코 있게 하여, 이 예사롭지 않은 사태가 과연 온당한지를 곰곰이 따져 보게 했다.\n\n이제 〈휴런〉족의 복수는 새로운 방향을 잡았고, 그들은 수백 년의 관행으로 몸에 밴 그 야만적인 재간으로 그것을 실행할 채비를 했다. 더러는 활활 타는 장작더미를 쌓을 옹이를 찾았고, 한 사람은 포로들의 살을 불붙은 조각으로 꿰찌르려 소나무 가시를 쪼개고 있었으며, 또 다른 자들은 〈헤이워드〉를 튕겨 오르는 두 가지 사이에 두 팔로 매달려 하고자 두 어린나무의 우듬지를 땅으로 휘어 당겼다. 그러나 〈마구아〉의 복수는 한층 깊고 한층 음험한 쾌락을 좇고 있었다.\n\n무리 가운데 덜 세련된 흉물들이, 고통받을 자들의 눈앞에서, 이 익히 알려진 천박한 고문 수단들을 준비하는 동안, 그는 〈코라〉에게 다가가, 더없이 음험한 낯빛으로, 그녀를 기다리는 임박한 운명을 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"하!\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"〈먼로〉의 딸이 무어라 하는가? 그 머리가 〈르나르〉의 천막에서 베개를 베기에는 너무 고결하다지. 그것이 늑대들의 놀잇감으로 이 언덕을 굴러다닐 때면 더 마음에 들겠는가? 그 가슴이 〈휴런〉족의 아이들에게 젖을 물릴 수 없다지. 그것이 인디언들에게 침 뱉음을 당하는 꼴을 보게 되리라!\"\n\n\"저 악마가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말입니까!\" 깜짝 놀란 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요!\" 단호한 대답이 돌아왔다. \"그는 미개인이에요, 야만스럽고 무지한 미개인이라, 제가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 몰라요. 죽어 가는 숨결로나마, 그를 위해 참회와 용서를 구할 여유를 가지도록 해요.\"\n\n\"용서라!\" 사나운 〈휴런〉족이, 분노에 차 그녀의 말뜻을 오해하고 되받았다. \"인디언의 기억은 창백한 얼굴들의 팔보다 길고, 그의 자비는 그들의 정의보다 짧다! 말하라, 노란 머리를 그 아비에게 보내 줄 테니, 너는 〈마구아〉를 따라 큰 호수로 가, 그의 물을 긷고, 그에게 옥수수를 먹이겠는가?\"\n\n〈코라〉는 다스릴 수 없는 혐오의 감정으로 그에게 물러가라 손짓했다.\n\n\"날 내버려 둬.\" 그녀가, 한순간 인디언의 야만을 멈칫하게 한 엄숙함으로 말했다. \"넌 내 기도에 쓰라림을 뒤섞고 있어. 넌 나와 내 하느님 사이를 가로막고 있어!\"\n\n그러나 미개인에게 새겨진 그 옅은 인상은 이내 잊혔고, 그는 조롱 섞인 비아냥으로 〈앨리스〉를 가리키기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"보아라! 저 아이가 우는구나! 죽기엔 어리지! 저 애를 〈먼로〉에게 보내, 그의 백발을 빗기고, 노인의 가슴에 목숨을 붙들어 두게 하라.\"\n\n〈코라〉는 어린 동생을 바라보고픈 욕망을 떨칠 수 없었으니, 그 눈에서 그녀는, 본성의 갈망을 드러내는 애원의 눈빛과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"저자가 뭐라고 해, 사랑하는 언니?\" 〈앨리스〉의 떨리는 목소리가 물었다. \"날 아버지께 보내 준다고 했어?\"\n\n한참 동안 언니는, 강렬하고 상반된 감정으로 흔들리는 얼굴로 동생을 바라보았다. 마침내 그녀가 입을 열었으니, 비록 그 어조는 그 풍요롭고 잔잔한 충만함을 잃었으되, 어머니의 것인 듯한 다정함의 표현을 띠고 있었다.\n\n\"〈앨리스〉,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 〈휴런〉족은 우리 둘 다에게 목숨을, 아니, 둘 이상을 주겠다는구나. 〈덩컨〉을, 우리의 더없이 소중한 〈덩컨〉을, 그리고 너를 우리 벗들에게, 우리 아버지에게, 가슴 찢긴 채 자식 잃은 우리 아버지에게 돌려보내 주겠다는구나, 내가 이 반항적이고 고집스러운 자존심을 굽히고, 응한다면——\"\n\n그녀의 목소리가 목메었고, 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 그녀는 그 고뇌 속에서 무한한 지혜로부터 깨달음을 구하려는 듯 위를 우러렀다.\n\n\"말해 줘.\" 〈앨리스〉가 부르짖었다. \"무엇에 응한다는 거야, 사랑하는 언니? 오, 그 제안이 내게 주어진 것이라면! 언니를 구하고, 늙은 아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리고, 〈덩컨〉을 돌려보낼 수 있다면, 내 얼마나 기꺼이 죽겠어!\"\n\n\"죽는 거라!\" 〈코라〉가 한결 차분하고 굳건한 목소리로 되뇌었다. \"그거라면 쉬울 텐데! 어쩌면 그 다른 길도 그만큼 쉬울지 모르지. 저자는 나더러,\" 그녀가, 그 제안의 굴욕에 대한 깊은 자각으로 어조를 가라앉히며 말을 이었다. \"저자를 따라 황야로, 〈휴런〉족의 거처로 가서, 거기 머물라는 거야. 한마디로, 저자의 아내가 되라는 거야! 그러니 말해 줘, 〈앨리스〉, 내 사랑하는 아이야! 내 사랑하는 동생아! 그리고 당신도, 〈헤이워드〉 소령, 당신의 조언으로 내 약한 분별을 도와주세요. 그런 희생으로 목숨을 사야 하나요? 〈앨리스〉, 너는 그런 대가로 내 손에서 목숨을 받겠니? 그리고 당신, 〈덩컨〉, 나를 이끌어 주세요. 두 분이서 나를 다잡아 주세요. 나는 온전히 두 분의 것이니까요.\"\n\n\"내가요!\" 분개하고 깜짝 놀란 청년이 받아쳤다. \"〈코라〉! 〈코라〉! 우리의 비참함을 두고 농을 하시는 겁니까! 그 끔찍한 길은 다시 입에 올리지 마십시오. 그 생각만으로도 천 번 죽는 것보다 더합니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그것이 당신의 대답이리라는 것을, 나는 잘 알고 있었어요!\" 〈코라〉가, 두 뺨이 붉어지고 검은 눈이 다시 한번 여인의 남은 감정으로 반짝이며 외쳤다. \"내 〈앨리스〉는 무어라 하니? 그 애를 위해서라면, 나는 더는 한마디 불평 없이 따르겠어.\"\n\n비록 〈헤이워드〉와 〈코라〉 둘 다 고통스러운 마음 졸임과 더없이 깊은 주의로 귀 기울였으나, 답으로 들려오는 소리는 없었다. 〈앨리스〉의 가녀리고 예민한 몸은, 이 제안에 귀 기울이는 동안 제 안으로 움츠러드는 듯했다. 그녀의 두 팔은 앞으로 길게 늘어뜨려져, 손가락이 가벼운 경련으로 움직였고, 머리는 가슴 위로 떨구어졌으며, 온몸은 나무에 매달린 채, 생기를 잃었으되 날카롭게 의식하고 있는, 상처 입은 여성다움의 어떤 아름다운 표상 같았다. 그러나 잠시 후, 그녀의 머리가 깊고 굽힐 수 없는 거부의 표시로 천천히 움직이기 시작했다.\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 우리가 살아온 그대로, 함께 죽는 편이 차라리 나아!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 죽어라!\" 〈마구아〉가, 무리 가운데 가장 약하다 믿었던 자가 별안간 드러낸 이 굳셈에, 더는 억누를 수 없는 분노로 이를 갈며, 항거하지 않는 그 화자를 향해 토마호크를 사납게 내던지며 외쳤다. 도끼가 〈헤이워드〉의 앞 공중을 가르며, 〈앨리스〉의 흘러내린 곱슬머리 몇 가닥을 베고는, 그녀의 머리 위 나무에 박혀 부르르 떨었다. 그 광경에 〈덩컨〉은 미친 듯 절박해졌다. 온 힘을 한 번에 그러모아, 그는 자신을 묶은 잔가지들을 끊어 버리고, 우렁찬 함성을 지르며 한층 신중한 겨냥으로 그 일격을 되풀이하려 채비하던 또 다른 미개인에게 달려들었다. 그들은 맞붙어, 엉겨, 함께 땅바닥으로 쓰러졌다. 상대의 벗은 몸은 〈헤이워드〉가 적수를 붙들 만한 단서를 주지 않았으니, 그자는 그의 손아귀에서 미끄러져 나가, 한쪽 무릎으로 그의 가슴을 짚고 다시 일어서서는, 거인 같은 무게로 그를 짓눌렀다. 〈덩컨〉은 이미 칼이 공중에서 번뜩이는 것을 보았는데, 그때 휘파람 같은 소리가 그를 스쳐 지나갔고, 그에 뒤이었다기보다는 함께한 것이, 라이플의 날카로운 총성이었다. 그는 자신의 가슴이 견디던 짐에서 풀려나는 것을 느꼈고, 그 인디언이 곁의 시든 낙엽 위로 죽어 쓰러질 때, 적수의 얼굴에 깃든 그 미개한 표정이 멍하니 사나운 빛으로 바뀌는 것을 보았다.\n\n클로. — 갑니다, 나리,\n이내, 나리,\n\n다시 모시러 오리다.\n\n「십이야」\n〈휴런〉족은 제 무리 가운데 하나에게 이토록 느닷없이 죽음이 들이닥치자 아연실색하여 우뚝 섰다. 그러나 동지를 그토록 큰 위험에 빠뜨리면서까지 적을 거꾸러뜨릴 만큼 대담했던 조준의 치명적인 정확성에 눈을 돌리는 순간, \"라 롱그 카라빈\"이라는 이름이 모든 입에서 동시에 터져 나왔고, 뒤이어 거칠고도 어딘가 구슬픈 울부짖음이 따랐다. 그 외침에, 경솔한 무리가 무기를 쌓아 두었던 작은 덤불에서 우렁찬 함성이 화답했다. 그리고 다음 순간, 되찾은 소총을 미처 장전할 겨를도 없을 만큼 다급했던 〈매부리눈〉이 곤봉처럼 그 무기를 휘두르며 넓고 힘찬 호를 그려 허공을 가르며 그들에게로 다가오는 것이 보였다. 척후의 전진이 대담하고 날래기는 했으되, 그를 앞질러 뛰어든, 가볍고도 기운찬 한 형상의 그것에는 미치지 못했다. 그 형상은 믿기 어려운 민첩함과 담대함으로 〈휴런〉족의 한복판으로 뛰어들어, 도끼를 빙빙 돌리고 번쩍이는 칼을 휘두르며, 〈코라〉 앞에 서서 무시무시하게 위협했다. 이 뜻밖의 대담한 동작들을 생각이 미처 좇기도 전에, 죽음의 상징적 갑주로 무장한 또 다른 형상이 그들의 눈앞을 미끄러지듯 지나, 앞선 자의 곁에서 위협적인 자세를 취했다. 미개한 고문자들은 이 호전적인 침입자들 앞에서 뒷걸음질했고, 그토록 빠른 잇따름으로 그들이 나타나자, 자주 되풀이되던 그 독특한 놀라움의 외침을 토했으니, 곧 익히 알려져 두려움을 사던 이름들이 그 뒤를 이었다.\n\n\"르 세르 아질! 르 그로 세르팡!\"\n\n그러나 〈휴런〉족의 빈틈없고 경계심 깊은 우두머리는 그리 쉽사리 당황하지 않았다. 작은 평지를 날카로운 눈으로 휘둘러보며 단번에 이 습격의 본질을 꿰뚫은 그는, 목소리로도 몸소 본을 보임으로써도 부하들을 북돋우며, 길고 위험한 칼을 뽑아 들고 우렁찬 함성과 함께 기다리던 〈칭가치국〉에게 달려들었다. 그것은 전면적 격투의 신호였다. 어느 쪽도 총기를 지니지 못했으니, 이 싸움은 가장 살벌한 방식으로 결판날 참이었다. 맨손으로 맞붙어, 공격용 무기만 있을 뿐 방어용 무기라곤 하나 없이.\n\n〈운카스〉가 그 함성에 응답하여 한 적에게 뛰어들더니, 도끼의 단 한 번 정확한 일격으로 그를 골수까지 쪼개 버렸다. 〈헤이워드〉는 〈마구아〉의 무기를 어린나무에서 뽑아 들고, 격렬히 난투 속으로 달려들었다. 이제 양편의 수가 같아졌으니, 저마다 적의 무리에서 상대 하나를 골랐다. 돌진과 타격이 회오리바람의 광포함과 번개의 신속함으로 오갔다. 〈매부리눈〉은 곧 또 다른 적을 제 팔이 닿는 거리 안에 두었고, 그 가공할 무기의 단 한 번 휘두름으로 적의 빈약하고 어설픈 방어를 쳐 내려, 그 일격으로 땅바닥에 짓이겨 버렸다. 〈헤이워드〉는 맞붙는 순간을 기다리기에는 너무 혈기가 앞선 나머지, 손에 쥔 도끼를 던지는 모험을 감행했다. 그것은 그가 고른 인디언의 이마를 맞혀, 한순간 그 전진을 멈추게 했다. 이 사소한 우위에 고무된 성급한 젊은이는 공격을 이어 가, 맨손으로 적에게 달려들었다. 단 한순간으로도 이 처사의 무모함을 충분히 깨달았으니, 그는 곧 〈휴런〉의 칼이 가하는 필사적인 찌르기를 막아 내려 제 온 민첩함과 용기를 다해 완전히 매달려야 하는 처지에 놓였다. 그토록 기민하고 빈틈없는 적을 더는 당해 낼 수 없게 되자, 그는 두 팔로 적을 끌어안아 무쇠 같은 움켜쥠으로 상대의 사지를 제 몸에 붙들어 매는 데 성공했으나, 그것은 그 자신을 너무도 지치게 하여 오래 버티기 어려운 것이었다. 이 절박한 처지에서 그는 가까이서 한 목소리가 외치는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"악당들을 몰살하라! 저주받은 〈밍고〉에게는 자비란 없다!\"\n\n다음 순간, 〈매부리눈〉의 소총 개머리판이 상대의 맨머리에 떨어졌고, 그 충격에 근육이 시들어 버리는 듯하더니, 그는 〈덩컨〉의 두 팔에서 축 늘어진 채 꿈쩍도 않고 미끄러져 내렸다.\n\n〈운카스〉는 첫 상대의 골을 부순 뒤, 굶주린 사자처럼 몸을 돌려 또 다른 적을 찾았다. 첫 돌진에서 빠져나온 다섯째이자 마지막 〈휴런〉은 한순간 멈칫했으나, 이내 주위가 온통 살벌한 격투에 매여 있는 것을 보고는, 지옥 같은 앙심으로 좌절된 복수의 과업을 마무리하려 했다. 승리의 함성을 지르며, 그는 무방비의 〈코라〉에게로 달려들어, 그 접근의 끔찍한 전조로서 날카로운 도끼를 날렸다. 그 도끼는 그녀의 어깨를 스쳐, 그녀를 나무에 묶었던 가지 끈을 끊어, 처녀가 자유로이 달아날 수 있게 했다. 그녀는 미개인의 손아귀를 피하고, 제 안위는 아랑곳없이 〈앨리스〉의 가슴에 몸을 던져, 경련하는 갈팡질팡한 손가락으로 동생을 옭아맨 잔가지들을 갈라 떼어 내려 안간힘을 썼다. 괴물이 아닌 그 누구라도 가장 선하고 가장 순결한 애정을 향한 이토록 갸륵한 헌신 앞에서는 마음이 누그러졌으련만, 〈휴런〉의 가슴은 연민이란 것을 알지 못했다. 그녀의 형체를 따라 흐트러져 내린 풍성한 머리채를 움켜잡고, 그는 그녀를 그 미친 듯한 매달림에서 떼어 내, 잔인한 폭력으로 무릎 꿇렸다. 미개인은 흘러내린 곱슬머리를 손으로 훑어, 팔을 쭉 뻗어 높이 치켜들고는, 조롱하며 의기양양한 웃음과 함께 제물의 그 빼어나게 빚어진 머리 둘레로 칼을 둘렀다. 그러나 그는 이 사나운 만족의 한순간을, 그 치명적인 기회를 잃는 것으로 사들였다. 바로 그때 그 광경이 〈운카스〉의 눈에 들어왔다. 그는 제자리에서 솟구쳐, 한순간 허공을 가르며 날아오르는 듯하더니, 한 덩어리가 되어 내리꽂혀 적의 가슴팍에 떨어졌고, 그를 그 자리에서 여러 야드나 곤두박질쳐 엎어지게 했다. 그 격렬한 힘은 젊은 〈모히칸〉을 그 곁으로 내동댕이쳤다. 두 사람은 함께 일어나, 싸우고, 번갈아 피를 흘렸다. 그러나 싸움은 곧 결판났다. 〈헤이워드〉의 도끼와 〈매부리눈〉의 소총이 〈휴런〉의 두개골에 떨어지는 바로 그 순간, 〈운카스〉의 칼이 그의 심장에 닿았다.\n\n이제 르 르나르 쉬브틸과 르 그로 세르팡 사이의 길게 끄는 격투를 제외하고는, 전투가 완전히 끝났다. 이 야만의 전사들은 지난 전쟁들의 무공으로 얻은 그 뜻깊은 이름에 걸맞은 자들임을 톡톡히 증명했다. 맞붙고 나서, 서로의 목숨을 노린 빠르고 힘찬 찌르기를 피하느라 잠시 시간이 흘렀다. 별안간 서로에게 덤벼들어, 둘은 한데 엉겨 땅바닥에 쓰러졌으니, 휘감기는 뱀들처럼 유연하고 교묘한 사림으로 서로 뒤틀려 있었다. 승자들이 제 손이 비었음을 깨달은 순간, 이 노련하고 필사적인 격투자들이 누운 자리는, 작은 평지의 한복판에서 그 가장자리로 회오리바람이 지나간 듯 일어나 움직이는 흙먼지와 나뭇잎의 구름으로밖에는 분간할 수 없었다. 효심과 우정과 감사라는 저마다 다른 동기에 떠밀려, 〈헤이워드〉와 그 일행은 한마음으로 그곳으로 달려가, 두 전사 위에 드리운 작은 흙먼지 차양을 에워쌌다. 〈운카스〉가 제 아비의 적의 심장에 칼을 박으려는 마음으로 구름 주위를 헛되이 맴돌았고, 〈매부리눈〉의 위협적인 소총이 헛되이 들려 멈춰 있었으며, 그동안 〈덩컨〉은 그 힘을 잃어버린 듯한 두 손으로 〈휴런〉의 사지를 붙들려 애썼다. 흙먼지와 피로 뒤덮인 채, 격투자들의 날랜 동작은 그 두 몸을 하나로 합쳐 놓는 듯했다. 〈모히칸〉의 죽은 듯한 형상과 〈휴런〉의 거무스름한 형체가 그들의 눈앞에서 그토록 빠르고 어지러운 잇따름으로 번뜩이는지라, 앞선 자의 벗들은 구원의 일격을 어디에 또 언제 꽂아야 할지 알 수 없었다. 사실, 짧고 덧없는 순간들이 있어, 〈마구아〉의 불타는 눈이, 그를 감싼 흙먼지의 화관 사이로, 전설 속 바실리스크의 기관처럼 번득이는 것이 보였고, 그는 그 짧고도 치명적인 눈길로 적들 앞에서 펼쳐지는 싸움의 운명을 읽었다. 그러나 그 어떤 적의 손이 그 운명 지어진 머리 위로 내리치기도 전에, 그 자리는 〈칭가치국〉의 험상궂은 얼굴로 채워졌다. 이런 식으로 격투의 장면은 작은 평지의 한복판에서 그 가장자리로 옮겨 갔다. 이제 〈모히칸〉은 칼로 힘찬 일격을 가할 기회를 잡았다. 〈마구아〉는 별안간 움켜쥠을 풀고, 꿈쩍도 않고, 보아하니 숨도 없이, 뒤로 넘어졌다. 그의 적수는 벌떡 일어서며, 승리의 함성으로 숲의 둥근 천장을 울렸다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉족, 잘했다! 〈모히칸〉에게 승리를!\" 〈매부리눈〉이 길고 치명적인 소총의 개머리판을 또다시 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"혈통이 섞이지 않은 사내가 가하는 마지막 일격은 결코 그 명예에 누가 되지 않을 것이며, 머리가죽에 대한 그의 권리를 앗아 가지도 않으리라.\"\n\n그러나 바로 그 위험한 무기가 내리꽂히려는 순간, 교활한 〈휴런〉은 그 위험 아래에서 잽싸게 굴러 빠져나와, 벼랑 가장자리를 넘어 두 발로 떨어지더니, 단 한 번의 도약으로 그 비탈을 따라 매달려 자란 키 낮은 덤불 숲 한복판으로 뛰어드는 것이 보였다. 적이 죽은 줄로만 믿었던 〈델라웨어〉족은 놀라움의 외침을 토하며, 사슴을 빤히 본 사냥개들처럼 빠른 걸음과 소란으로 뒤쫓으려 했으나, 척후의 날카롭고 독특한 외침이 단번에 그 마음을 돌려, 그들을 언덕 꼭대기로 다시 불러들였다.\n\n\"그자다운 짓이지.\" 〈밍고〉족에 관한 모든 일에서 천성의 정의감을 가리는 데 그토록 크게 한몫하던 편견을 지닌, 이 골수 산림꾼이 외쳤다. \"거짓되고 속임수 능한 악당이니까. 정직한 〈델라웨어〉라면 정정당당히 패한 마당에 가만히 누워 머리를 얻어맞았을 테지만, 이 교활한 〈매콰〉 놈들은 산고양이처럼 목숨에 매달리거든. 가게 둬, 가게 둬. 사내 하나일 뿐이고, 그것도 소총도 활도 없이, 제 프랑스 동무들에게서 까마득히 먼 곳에 있으니까. 게다가 독니를 잃은 방울뱀처럼, 그자가 — 그리고 우리도 — 모래벌판을 한참 가로질러 모카신 자국을 남길 그런 때가 오기 전까지는 더 못된 짓이라곤 할 수가 없지. 보아라, 〈운카스〉.\" 그가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 덧붙였다. \"네 아비가 벌써 머리가죽을 벗기고 있구나. 한 바퀴 돌며 남은 부랑자들을 만져 보는 게 좋겠다. 안 그러면 또 한 놈이 숲을 껑충껑충 가로질러, 날개 다친 어치처럼 꽥꽥대며 달아날지도 모르니까.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며, 이 정직하나 가차 없는 척후는 죽은 자들 사이를 한 바퀴 돌며, 마치 그들이 짐승의 시체이기라도 한 양 태연히 그 감각 없는 가슴팍에 긴 칼을 박았다. 그러나 손위 〈모히칸〉이 이미 그를 앞질러, 저항 없는 시체들의 머리에서 승리의 표지를 떼어 낸 뒤였다.\n\n그러나 〈운카스〉는 제 습성을, 거의 천성이라 해도 좋을 것을 거슬러, 본능적인 섬세함으로 〈헤이워드〉와 함께 여인들을 도우러 날듯이 달려가, 〈앨리스〉를 재빨리 풀어 〈코라〉의 품에 안겼다. 이토록 뜻밖에 목숨을 되찾고 서로를 되찾은 두 자매의 가슴속에 타오른, 만사를 주관하시는 전능자를 향한 감사를 우리는 굳이 묘사하려 들지 않겠다. 그들의 감사는 깊고도 말이 없었다. 그 온유한 영혼의 봉헌은 마음의 은밀한 제단 위에서 가장 밝고 순결하게 타올랐고, 새로워진 그들의 한결 인간적인 감정은 길고 뜨겁되 말 없는 어루만짐으로 드러났다. 〈앨리스〉는 〈코라〉의 곁에 주저앉았던 무릎에서 일어서며, 언니의 가슴에 몸을 던지고 늙은 아버지의 이름을 소리 내어 흐느꼈으니, 그 부드럽고 비둘기 같은 두 눈은 희망의 빛으로 반짝였다.\n\n\"우린 살았어요! 우린 살았어요!\" 그녀가 속삭였다. \"우리의 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아버지의 품으로 돌아가는 거예요. 아버지의 마음은 슬픔으로 찢기지 않을 거예요. 그리고 언니도, 〈코라〉 언니, 언니보다 더한 언니, 내 어머니 같은 언니, 언니도 화를 면했어요. 그리고 〈덩컨〉도.\" 그녀는 형언할 수 없는 순진함의 미소를 띠고 청년을 둘러보며 덧붙였다. \"우리의 용감하고 고귀한 〈덩컨〉마저 상처 하나 없이 벗어났어요.\"\n\n이 뜨겁고도 거의 두서없는 말들에, 〈코라〉는 어린 화자를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며, 녹아드는 다정함으로 그 위에 몸을 숙이는 것 외에 다른 대답을 하지 않았다. 〈헤이워드〉의 사내다움은 이 애정 어린 환희의 광경에 눈물 흘리는 것을 부끄러워하지 않았다. 그리고 〈운카스〉는 격투의 흔적이 채 가시지 않은 핏자국 묻은 모습으로, 차분하게, 또 보아하니 마음의 동요 없는 구경꾼으로 서 있었으니, 그것은 사실이었으되, 그 두 눈은 이미 사나움을 잃고, 그를 제 부족의 지성보다 한참 위로 끌어올리고, 어쩌면 제 부족의 관습보다 수백 년은 앞서게 한 어떤 연민으로 빛나고 있었다.\n\n이 처지에서 그토록 자연스러운 감정의 표출이 펼쳐지는 동안, 빈틈없는 의심으로 저 천국 같은 광경을 흉하게 망쳐 놓던 〈휴런〉족이 더는 그 조화를 깨뜨릴 힘이 없음을 확인하고 안심한 〈매부리눈〉은, 〈데이비드〉에게 다가가, 그가 그 순간까지 더없이 본보기다운 인내로 견뎌 온 결박을 풀어 주었다.\n\n\"자.\" 척후가 마지막 가지 끈을 뒤로 내던지며 외쳤다. \"당신은 다시 한번 제 사지의 주인이 되었소만, 그것이 처음 빚어졌을 때보다 더 분별 있게 그것을 쓰는 것 같지는 않구려. 당신보다 나이가 많지도 않으나 평생 거의를 황야에서 살아 그 나이를 넘는 경험을 쌓았다 할 만한 자의 조언이 거슬리지 않는다면, 내 생각을 기꺼이 들려주리다. 그건 이렇소. 당신 윗옷에 든 그 자그마한 피리 도구를 처음 만나는 바보에게 넘겨주고, 그 돈으로 쓸 만한 무기를, 그것이 한낱 기병 권총의 총신뿐일지라도, 사는 게 좋겠소. 부지런함과 정성으로, 당신은 그렇게 어느 정도 출세할 수도 있을 거요. 이맘때쯤이면, 내 생각엔, 시체를 먹는 까마귀가 흉내쟁이 개똥지빠귀보다 나은 새라는 걸 당신 두 눈이 똑똑히 일러 줄 테니까. 적어도 까마귀는 사람의 눈앞에서 더러운 광경을 치워 주지만, 다른 놈은 그 소리를 듣는 모든 것의 귀를 속여 숲속에 소동만 일으키는 데나 좋을 뿐이니 말이오.\"\n\n\"전투에는 무기와 나팔이나, 승리에는 감사의 노래를!\" 풀려난 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"벗이여.\" 그는 야위고 섬세한 손을 정답게 〈매부리눈〉에게 내밀며 덧붙였으니, 그 두 눈은 반짝이며 촉촉이 젖어 들었다. \"내 머리카락이 섭리께서 처음 뿌리내려 주신 그 자리에 여태 자라고 있음을, 그대에게 감사하오. 다른 이들의 머리칼이 더 윤기 나고 곱슬거릴지언정, 나는 늘 내 것이 그것이 감싼 두뇌에 잘 어울린다고 여겨 왔으니 말이오. 내가 전투에 합세하지 않은 것은 내키지 않아서가 아니라 이교도들의 결박 탓이었소. 그대는 이 싸움에서 용맹하고 능란함을 입증했으니, 더 중하고 다른 본분을 다하러 가기에 앞서, 이로써 그대에게 감사하오. 그대가 기독교인의 칭송을 받아 마땅함을 스스로 증명했기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"그까짓 건 사소한 일이고, 우리와 오래 머물다 보면 자주 보게 될 거요.\" 척후가 이 분명한 감사의 표현에 노래하는 이를 향해 한결 누그러져 대답했다. \"내 옛 동무 '킬디어'를 되찾았으니.\" 그는 소총 개머리판을 손으로 치며 덧붙였다. \"그것만으로도 하나의 승리지. 이 〈이로쿼이〉 놈들은 교활하지만, 제 총기를 손 닿지 않는 데 둔 건 제 꾀에 제가 넘어간 셈이오. 만일 〈운카스〉나 그 아비가 인디언의 흔한 인내심만이라도 타고났던들, 우린 총알 하나가 아니라 셋으로 그 악당들을 덮쳤을 테고, 그러면 저 껑충껑충 달아난 놈까지 그 동무들과 함께 무리 전체를 끝장냈을 거요. 허나 다 미리 정해진 일이고, 그게 최선이었던 게지.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이오.\" 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"그대는 기독교의 참된 정신을 깨우쳤구려. 구원받을 자는 구원받을 것이요, 저주받도록 예정된 자는 저주받을 것이오. 이것이 진리의 교리요, 참된 신자에게는 더없이 위안이 되고 마음을 새롭게 하는 것이오.\"\n\n이때쯤 자리에 앉아 어버이 같은 정성으로 소총의 상태를 살피던 척후는, 이제 감추는 척도 하지 않은 못마땅함으로 상대를 올려다보며, 거칠게 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"교리든 뭐든.\" 이 다부진 산림꾼이 말했다. \"그건 악당들의 믿음이요, 정직한 사람에게는 저주요. 저기 저 〈휴런〉이 내 손에 쓰러질 운명이었다는 건 믿을 수 있소, 내 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보았으니까. 허나 직접 목격하지 않고서는, 그자가 무슨 보상을 받았다거나, 저기 저 〈칭가치국〉이 최후의 심판 날에 정죄당하리라는 건 결코 믿을 수가 없소.\"\n\n\"그대는 그토록 당돌한 교리를 뒷받침할 보증도, 그것을 떠받칠 어떤 언약도 갖지 못했소.\" 〈데이비드〉가 외쳤으니, 그는 제 시대에, 더욱이 제 식민지에서, 신성한 본성의 무서운 신비를 꿰뚫으려는 시도로, 자족으로써 믿음을 메우고, 그 결과 그런 인간의 교의로 추론하는 자들을 부조리와 의심 속에 빠뜨리며, 계시의 아름다운 단순함 둘레에 그어진 그 미묘한 구별들에 깊이 물들어 있었다. \"그대의 신전은 모래 위에 세워졌으니, 첫 폭풍우가 그 기초를 쓸어 가 버릴 거요. 나는 그토록 박정한 주장에 대한 그대의 전거를 요구하는 바요(다른 어떤 교리 옹호자들과 마찬가지로, 〈데이비드〉도 용어를 늘 정확히 쓰지는 못했다). 장과 절을 대 보시오. 어느 성서에서 그대를 뒷받침할 말씀을 찾았소?\"\n\n\"성서라!\" 〈매부리눈〉이 유별나면서도 잘 감추지 못한 경멸로 되뇌었다. \"당신은 나를 늙은 할멈의 앞치마 끈에 매달려 칭얼대는 사내아이쯤으로 보는 거요? 그리고 내 무릎 위의 이 좋은 소총을 거위 깃펜으로, 내 황소 뿔을 잉크병으로, 내 가죽 주머니를 점심을 싸 다니는 격자무늬 손수건쯤으로 보는 거요? 성서라! 비록 혈통이 섞이지 않은 사내이긴 하나 황야의 전사인 나 같은 자가 성서와 무슨 상관이 있단 말이오? 나는 단 하나의 책에서밖에 읽지 않았는데, 거기 쓰인 말씀들은 너무 단순하고 너무 명백해서 많은 가르침이 필요 없소. 비록 그것이 마흔 해의 길고 고된 세월의 가르침이었다고 자랑할 수는 있겠지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"그 책을 무어라 부르시오?\" 〈데이비드〉가 상대의 뜻을 잘못 알아듣고 물었다.\n\n\"당신 두 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있소.\" 척후가 대답했다. \"그리고 그 임자는 그것을 쓰는 데 인색하지 않소. 듣자 하니, 신이 있다는 걸 제 스스로 납득하려고 책에서 읽는 사람들이 있다더군. 사람이 한번 정착지에서 제 일들을 그토록 망쳐 놓아서, 황야에서는 그토록 명백한 것을 장사꾼과 사제들 사이에서는 의심거리로 남겨 두는 건지도 모르지. 그런 자가 있어 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 숲의 굽이굽이를 따라 나를 좇는다면, 자기가 바보라는 것과, 그 어리석음 중 가장 큰 것이 선함에서든 권능에서든 결코 견줄 수 없는 한 분의 자리로 오르려 애쓰는 데 있다는 것을 깨우치기에 족할 만큼은 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n자기가 자연의 빛에서 믿음을 길어 올리고 교리의 온갖 정교함을 멀리하는 논객과 다투고 있음을 깨닫는 순간, 〈데이비드〉는 이득도 명예도 끌어낼 수 없으리라 여긴 그 논쟁을 기꺼이 단념했다. 척후가 말하는 동안, 그 역시 자리에 앉아, 늘 지니던 작은 책과 쇠테 안경을 꺼내, 정통 교리를 향해 받은 뜻밖의 공격이 아니었던들 그토록 오래 미루어졌을 리 없는 본분을 다할 채비를 했다. 사실 그는 서반구의 음유시인이었으니, 일찍이 남작과 군주의 세속적 명성을 노래하던 그 재능 있는 음유시인들보다는 분명 훨씬 후대의 사람이었으되, 제 시대와 제 나라의 정신에 따른 자였다. 그는 이제 최근의 승리를 기리고자, 아니 오히려 그에 감사하고자, 제 기예의 솜씨를 발휘할 채비를 갖추었다. 그는 〈매부리눈〉이 말을 그칠 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다가, 두 눈과 목소리를 함께 들어 올려, 소리 높여 말했다.\n\n\"벗들이여, 야만인과 이교도의 손에서 벗어난 이 놀라운 구원을 두고, '노샘프턴'이라 불리는 곡조의 편안하고도 장엄한 가락으로, 함께 찬미할 것을 권하오.\"\n\n이어 그는 골라 둔 운율이 실린 쪽과 절을 일러 주고, 신전에서 늘 쓰던 그 점잖은 엄숙함으로 음높이 피리를 입에 댔다. 그러나 이번에는 어떤 동반 가창도 없었으니, 두 자매가 바로 그때 앞서 언급한 그 다정한 애정의 토로를 쏟아 내고 있었기 때문이다. 청중이 적은 것에, 사실 못마땅해하는 척후 하나뿐인 청중에 조금도 굴하지 않고, 그는 목소리를 높여, 그 어떤 사고도 방해도 없이 성가를 시작하고 끝맺었다.\n\n〈매부리눈〉은 부싯돌을 태연히 손보고 소총을 재장전하면서 귀를 기울였으나, 그 소리는 정경과 공감이라는 외적인 도움을 결한 까닭에, 잠들어 있는 그의 감정을 일깨우지 못했다. 음유시인이든, 〈데이비드〉를 무어라 더 알맞게 부를 이름이 있든, 이보다 더 무감각한 청중 앞에서 제 재능을 펼친 자는 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그 동기의 한결같음과 진실함을 헤아린다면, 세속의 노래를 부르는 그 어떤 음유시인도, 모든 경배와 찬미가 마땅히 바쳐질 그 보좌에 이토록 가까이 올라간 가락을 토한 적은 없었으리라. 척후는 고개를 가로젓고, 알아들을 수 없는 몇 마디를 중얼거렸는데, 그중 \"목구멍\"과 \"〈이로쿼이〉\"만이 들렸을 뿐이며, 노획한 〈휴런〉족의 무기고 상태를 그러모아 살펴보러 걸어갔다. 이 일에 이제 〈칭가치국〉이 합류했으니, 그는 그 무기들 사이에서 제 것과 아들의 소총을 찾아냈다. 〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉마저 무기를 갖추게 되었고, 그것들을 모두 쓸모 있게 만들 탄약도 모자라지 않았다.\n\n산림꾼들이 고를 것을 다 고르고 노획품을 나누자, 척후는 떠나야 할 시각이 왔음을 알렸다. 이때쯤 〈개멋〉의 노래도 그쳤고, 두 자매도 감정의 표출을 가라앉히는 법을 익힌 터였다. 〈덩컨〉과 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 도움을 받아, 두 자매는 얼마 전 그토록 다른 처지에서 올랐던, 그리고 그 꼭대기가 하마터면 자신들의 학살의 무대가 될 뻔했던 그 가파른 언덕 비탈을 내려왔다. 기슭에 이르러, 그들은 〈내러갠셋〉 말들이 덤불의 풀을 뜯고 있는 것을 보았고, 말에 올라, 더없이 살벌한 곤경에서 그토록 자주 제 벗임을 증명해 온 안내자의 움직임을 따랐다. 그러나 여정은 짧았다. 〈매부리눈〉은 〈휴런〉족이 따라온 어렴풋한 오솔길을 벗어나, 오른편으로 바투 꺾어, 덤불 속으로 들어가, 졸졸대는 시내를 건너, 몇 그루 물느릅나무 그늘 아래 좁은 골짜기에서 멈춰 섰다. 그 운명의 언덕 기슭에서의 거리는 고작 몇 로드에 지나지 않았으니, 말들은 그저 얕은 개울을 건너는 데에나 쓸모가 있었을 뿐이었다.\n\n척후와 인디언들은 지금 이른 이 외딴곳에 익숙한 듯했다. 소총을 나무에 기대 세워 두고, 그들은 마른 잎을 옆으로 헤치며 푸른 진흙을 파헤치기 시작했고, 거기서 맑고 반짝이는 밝은 샘물이 일렁이며 이내 보글보글 솟아났다. 이윽고 백인은, 기대만큼 쉽사리 찾아지지 않는 어떤 것을 찾는 양 둘레를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"저 부주의한 도깨비 같은 〈모호크〉 놈들이, 제 〈터스커로라〉, 〈오논다가〉 형제들과 함께 여기 와서 목을 축였구먼.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"그러고는 부랑자 놈들이 바가지를 내던져 버렸어! 이런 잘 잊어먹는 개 같은 것들한테 은혜를 베풀면 다 이 꼴이지! 여기, 주께서 울부짖는 황야 한복판에서, 그들의 이익을 위해 손을 펴시어, 땅속에서 물의 샘을 솟구치게 하셨으니, 식민지 전역의 그 어떤 으리으리한 약방 가게도 비웃을 만한 것이건만. 그런데 보라지! 악당들이 진흙을 짓밟아, 이곳의 정결함을 망쳐 놓았어. 마치 사람이 아니라 짐승이기라도 한 것처럼.\"\n\n〈운카스〉가 말없이 그를 향해 바라던 바가지를 내밀었으니, 그것은 느릅나무 가지에 걸려 있었으나 〈매부리눈〉의 언짢음 때문에 여태 그의 눈에 띄지 않았던 것이었다. 거기 물을 채워, 그는 땅이 한결 단단하고 마른 데로 잠시 물러갔다. 거기서 그는 태연히 자리를 잡고 앉아, 길게, 또 보아하니 흡족하게 한 모금 들이켠 뒤, 제 팔에 멘 자루에 걸려 있던, 〈휴런〉족이 남긴 음식 조각들을 매우 꼼꼼히 살피기 시작했다.\n\n\"고맙다, 얘야!\" 그가 빈 바가지를 〈운카스〉에게 돌려주며 말을 이었다. \"이제 이 날뛰는 〈휴런〉 놈들이 매복하러 나가 있을 때 어떻게 살았는지 좀 보자꾸나. 이걸 봐! 악당들이 사슴의 좋은 부위는 알아본단 말이야. 이 등심 토막을 보면 이 땅 최고의 요리사 못지않게 발라내고 구울 줄 안다 싶을 정도구먼! 허나 죄다 날것이야, 〈이로쿼이〉는 철저한 미개인들이니까. 〈운카스〉, 내 부싯쇠를 받아 불을 지피거라. 이토록 긴 행군 끝에는, 연한 구이 한 입이 천성에 보탬이 될 테니.\"\n\n안내자들이 이제 진지하게 식사 채비에 나서는 것을 본 〈헤이워드〉는, 숙녀들이 말에서 내리는 것을 도와 그들 곁에 자리를 잡았으니, 방금 겪은 피의 장면을 지나온 뒤 잠시나마 고마운 휴식을 누리는 것이 싫지 않았다. 요리가 진행되는 동안, 호기심에 끌려 그는 자신들의 때맞은 뜻밖의 구출로 이어진 정황을 캐물었다.\n\n\"이토록 빨리 그대를 보게 되다니 어찌 된 일이오, 너그러운 벗이여.\" 그가 물었다. \"그것도 〈에드워드〉 요새의 수비대 도움도 없이?\"\n\n\"우리가 강굽이로 갔던들, 당신들 시신 위에 나뭇잎을 그러모아 덮어 줄 만큼은 제때 닿았을지 모르나, 머리가죽을 구하기엔 너무 늦었을 거요.\" 척후가 태연히 대답했다. \"아니, 아니지. 요새로 건너가느라 힘과 기회를 허비하느니, 우린 〈허드슨〉 강둑 아래 숨어, 〈휴런〉족의 움직임을 지켜보려 엎드려 있었던 거요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그대들은 일어난 모든 일의 목격자였구려?\"\n\n\"전부는 아니오. 인디언의 시력은 너무 예민해서 쉽사리 속아 넘어가지 않으니, 우린 바짝 숨어 있었소. 게다가 이 〈모히칸〉 애송이를 매복 속에 얌전히 붙들어 두는 것도 어려운 일이었지. 아! 〈운카스〉, 〈운카스〉, 네 행동거지는 냄새를 좇는 전사라기보다 호기심 많은 아낙에 가까웠다.\"\n\n〈운카스〉는 한순간 화자의 다부진 얼굴로 눈을 돌렸으나, 입을 떼지도, 뉘우치는 어떤 기색도 보이지 않았다. 도리어 〈헤이워드〉는 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 태도가 사뭇 사납지는 않더라도 경멸 어리며, 또 그가 터질 듯한 격정을, 평소 백인 동료에게 바치던 경의에서만큼이나 듣는 이들에 대한 예우에서, 억누르고 있다고 여겼다.\n\n\"우리가 사로잡히는 걸 보았소?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 다시 물었다.\n\n\"들었소.\" 의미심장한 대답이었다. \"인디언의 함성은 숲에서 평생을 보낸 자들에겐 명백한 언어요. 허나 당신들이 뭍에 오르자, 우린 뱀처럼 나뭇잎 아래로 기어들어야 했고, 그러고는 당신들을 완전히 시야에서 놓쳤다가, 나무에 묶여 인디언의 학살을 위해 단단히 결박된 당신들을 다시 두 눈으로 보게 된 거요.\"\n\n\"우리의 구출은 섭리의 역사였소. 그대가 길을 잘못 들지 않은 게 거의 기적이었으니, 〈휴런〉족이 갈라졌고 무리마다 제 말들을 거느렸으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇소! 거기서 우린 냄새를 놓쳤고, 〈운카스〉가 아니었던들 정말로 자취를 잃었을 거요. 허나 우린 황야로 이어지는 길을 택했소. 미개인들이 포로를 데리고 그 길을 갈 거라고 판단했고, 그 판단이 옳았으니까. 그러나 내가 일러둔 대로 부러진 잔가지 하나 찾지 못한 채 여러 마일을 따라가다 보니, 마음에 미심쩍은 데가 생겼소. 더욱이 모든 발자국이 모카신 자국이었으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"우리를 사로잡은 자들이 우리에게도 자기들처럼 신을 신기는 조심성을 부렸소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 한 발을 들어, 자기가 신은 사슴가죽 신을 내보이며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇군, 그건 분별 있고, 그들다운 짓이었소. 허나 우린 그런 흔한 술책에 자취를 놓칠 만큼 풋내기는 아니었지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우리는 무엇에 우리의 안전을 빚진 거요?\"\n\n\"인디언의 피가 한 방울도 섞이지 않은 백인으로서 인정하기 부끄러운 것에 빚졌소. 내가 그 애보다 더 잘 알았어야 마땅한 일에서, 그 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 판단에 말이오. 비록 내 두 눈이 그렇다 일러 주는데도 지금도 도무지 참이라고 믿기 어려운 것이지만.\"\n\n\"참으로 기이하구려! 그 까닭을 말해 주지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"〈운카스〉는 대담하게도, 저 숙녀분들이 탄 짐승들이,\" 〈매부리눈〉이 호기심 어린 흥미가 없지 않게 숙녀들의 어린 암말들로 눈길을 돌리며 말을 이었다. \"한쪽의 두 다리를 동시에 땅에 디딘다고 했는데, 그건 내가 아는 한 곰을 빼고는 모든 빠른 걸음의 네발짐승의 움직임에 어긋나는 것이오. 그런데 여기, 내 두 눈으로 보았고, 그 자취가 스무 마일 내내 보여 준 대로, 늘 이런 식으로 걷는 말들이 있단 말이지.\"\n\n\"그건 그 짐승의 장점이라오! 그것들은 〈프로비던스 플랜테이션스〉라는 작은 식민지의 〈내러갠셋〉 만 기슭에서 왔는데, 그 강인함과 이 독특한 걸음걸이의 편안함으로 이름이 높소. 비록 다른 말들도 같은 걸음을 익히는 일이 드물지는 않지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지, 그럴 수도 있겠어.\" 이 설명에 유달리 귀를 기울이던 〈매부리눈〉이 말했다. \"비록 내가 백인의 피를 온전히 지닌 사람이긴 하나, 사슴과 비버에 관한 내 판단이 짐 나르는 짐승에 관한 것보다는 나으니까. 〈에핑엄〉 소령께 고귀한 군마가 여럿 있으나, 나는 그런 옆으로 디디는 걸음으로 가는 놈은 한 번도 본 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"옳소. 그분이라면 사뭇 다른 자질로 그 짐승을 높이 살 테니까. 그래도 이건 높이 평가받는 품종이며, 그대가 보다시피, 자주 짊어지도록 운명 지어진 짐으로 큰 영예를 누리는 짐승이오.\"\n\n〈모히칸〉 부자는 가물거리는 불 곁에서 하던 일을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다. 〈덩컨〉의 말이 끝나자, 그들은 의미심장하게 서로를 바라보았고, 아비가 결코 빠지는 법 없는 놀라움의 외침을 토했다. 척후는 새로이 얻은 지식을 곱씹는 사람처럼 되새기더니, 또 한 번 호기심 어린 눈길을 슬그머니 말들에게 던졌다.\n\n\"감히 말하건대 정착지에는 이보다 더 기이한 광경도 볼 수 있겠지!\" 그가 마침내 말했다. \"사람이 일단 주도권을 쥐면, 천성이란 게 딱하게도 짓밟히고 마는 법이야. 허나 옆으로 디디든 곧장 디디든, 〈운카스〉가 그 움직임을 보았고, 그 자취가 우리를 부러진 덤불로 이끌었소. 한 말의 발자국 가까이, 바깥쪽 가지가 위로 휘어 있었는데, 마치 숙녀가 줄기에서 꽃 한 송이를 꺾듯 한 것이었으나, 나머지는 죄다 사내의 억센 손이 잡아 뜯기라도 한 양 너덜너덜 부러져 있었소! 그래서 나는 교활한 놈들이 그 휜 잔가지를 보고, 수사슴이 뿔로 가지를 비벼 댔다고 우리가 믿게끔 나머지를 잡아 뜯었다고 결론지었소.\"\n\n\"그대의 명민함이 그대를 속이지 않은 게 분명하오. 정말 그런 일이 있었으니!\"\n\n\"그건 알아보기 쉬운 일이었소.\" 척후가 어떤 비범한 명민함을 드러냈다는 의식이라곤 조금도 없이 덧붙였다. \"그건 뒤뚱대는 말과는 사뭇 다른 문제였지! 그때 나는 〈밍고〉 놈들이 이 샘으로 밀어붙이리라는 데 생각이 미쳤소. 악당들이 그 물의 효험을 잘 아니까!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그토록 이름난 곳이오?\" 〈헤이워드〉가, 그 깊고 거무칙칙한 갈색 흙으로 둘러싸인, 보글대는 샘이 솟는 이 외딴 골짜기를 한층 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피며 물었다.\n\n\"오대호의 남쪽과 동쪽을 다니는 홍인종치고, 그 효능을 들어 보지 못한 자는 드물 거요. 직접 한번 맛보겠소?\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 바가지를 받아, 물을 조금 삼키더니, 못마땅한 얼굴을 찡그리며 옆으로 던져 버렸다. 척후는 그 소리 없으나 진정에서 우러난 투로 웃으며, 더없는 흡족함으로 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"아! 당신에겐 습관으로 얻어지는 그 맛이 없는 거요. 나도 한때는 당신만큼이나 이 맛을 싫어했지. 허나 차차 내 입에 맞게 되어, 이젠 사슴이 소금기 있는 곳을 찾듯 이걸 갈망하게 됐소. 당신네 진하게 양념한 포도주도 홍인종이 이 물을 즐기는 것만큼은 못한다오. 특히 그 천성이 병들었을 때는 말이지. 허나 〈운카스〉가 불을 지폈으니, 이제 먹을 것을 생각할 때요. 우리 여정은 길고, 죄다 우리 앞에 놓여 있으니.\"\n\n* 핥는 곳: 짐승들이 소금기를 핥으러 모이는 곳.\n이 느닷없는 화제 전환으로 대화를 끊으며, 척후는 곧장 〈휴런〉족의 게걸스러운 식욕을 면한 음식 조각들에 손을 댔다. 매우 간략한 과정으로 그 소박한 요리가 마무리되자, 그와 〈모히칸〉 부자는, 큰 쉼 없는 노고를 견뎌 내기 위해 먹는 사람들 특유의 침묵과 부지런함으로, 그 보잘것없는 식사를 시작했다.\n\n이 필요한, 그리고 다행히도 고마운 본분을 다하고 나서, 산림꾼들은 저마다 몸을 굽혀 그 외롭고 고요한 샘에서 길고도 작별의 한 모금을 들이켰으니, 그 샘과 그 자매 샘들 둘레로 오십 년 안에 반구의 부와 미와 재능이, 건강과 즐거움을 좇아 무리 지어 모여들 참이었다. 이윽고 〈매부리눈〉은 떠나기로 마음먹었음을 알렸다. 자매는 다시 안장에 올랐고, 〈덩컨〉과 〈데이비드〉는 소총을 거머쥐고 그들의 뒤를 따랐다. 척후가 앞장을 서고, 〈모히칸〉 부자가 후미를 맡았다. 일행 전부는 좁은 오솔길을 따라 북쪽으로 빠르게 나아가, 그 치유의 물은 곁의 시내와 아무도 모르게 뒤섞이도록, 또 죽은 자들의 시신은 매장의 예도 없이 이웃한 언덕 위에서 썩어 가도록 남겨 두었으니, 그것은 연민도 논평도 자아내기엔 숲의 전사들에게 너무도 흔한 운명이었다.\n\n「나는 더 빠른 길을 찾으리라.」\n\n파넬\n〈호크아이〉가 잡은 경로는, 바로 그날 아침 좌절한 〈마구아〉를 길잡이로 삼아 일행이 지나왔던, 군데군데 골짜기와 둔덕이 들어선 그 모래 평원을 가로질러 뻗어 있었다. 해는 이제 먼 산들을 향해 낮게 기울었고, 그들의 여정이 끝없는 숲을 통해 이어진 까닭에 더위는 더 이상 견디기 힘들지 않았다. 그리하여 그들의 걸음도 그만큼 빨라졌고, 황혼이 그들을 에워싸기 한참 전에 그들은 돌아오는 길에서 힘겨운 여러 마일을 부지런히 답파했다.\n\n그 자리를 대신한 미개인과 마찬가지로, 사냥꾼은 그들의 거친 경로에 흩어진 희미한 표지들 사이에서 일종의 본능으로 길을 가려내는 듯했으니, 좀처럼 속도를 늦추지 않았고, 망설이느라 멈추는 일은 결코 없었다. 나무에 낀 이끼를 향한 빠르고 비스듬한 눈길, 이따금 지는 해를 향해 위로 던지는 시선, 또는 그가 건너 다니던 무수한 물길의 방향을 향해 차분하나 스쳐 지나가는 듯한 응시만으로도, 그의 길을 정하고 그가 맞닥뜨린 가장 큰 난관을 떨쳐 내기에 족했다. 그러는 동안, 숲은 빛깔을 바꾸기 시작하여, 그 아치를 수놓던 생기 띤 초록을 잃고, 흔히 하루의 끝을 앞서 알리는 한층 무거운 빛으로 잠겨 들었다.\n\n자매의 눈이 나무들 사이로, 해를 에워싸 반짝이는 후광을 이루는 황금빛 영광의 홍수를 — 그것은 서쪽 언덕 위 멀지 않은 곳에 쌓여 있던 한 무리의 구름을, 군데군데 루비빛 줄무늬로 물들이거나 빛나는 노란빛의 가느다란 가장자리로 둘러 가며 — 어렴풋이라도 붙잡으려 애쓰는 동안, 〈호크아이〉가 갑자기 몸을 돌려, 화려한 하늘을 향해 위쪽을 가리키며 입을 열었다.\n\n\"저것이 사람더러 양식과 마땅한 휴식을 찾으라고 주어진 신호요.\" 그가 말했다. \"사람이 자연의 표지를 읽을 줄 알고, 공중의 새와 들의 짐승에게서 한 수 배울 수만 있다면 더 낫고 더 지혜로울 것이오! 허나 우리의 밤은 이내 끝날 거요. 달이 뜨면 우리는 다시 일어나 움직여야 하니. 내가 사람의 피를 처음 본 그 첫 전쟁에서, 이 근방에서 〈마쿠아〉족과 싸웠던 게 기억나오. 그때 우리는 그 게걸스러운 짐승 같은 놈들이 우리 머리 가죽에 손대지 못하게 통나무로 방벽을 쌓았소. 내 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 우리 왼쪽으로 몇 로드만 더 가면 그곳을 찾을 거요.\"\n\n동의를 기다리지도, 사실 그 어떤 대답도 기다리지 않고, 다부진 사냥꾼은 어린 밤나무가 빽빽이 우거진 덤불 속으로 거침없이 들어섰으니, 땅을 거의 다 뒤덮은 무성한 어린 가지들을 헤쳐 가는 그 모습은, 걸음을 옮길 때마다 예전에 알던 어떤 것을 발견하리라 기대하는 사람 같았다. 척후의 기억은 그를 속이지 않았다. 가시덤불로 얽힌 그 잡목 숲을 몇백 피트쯤 뚫고 들어가자, 그는 나지막한 푸른 둔덕을 에워싼 트인 공간으로 들어섰고, 그 둔덕 꼭대기에는 바로 그 허물어진 방어용 통나무 진지가 얹혀 있었다. 거칠고 버려진 이 건물은, 위급할 때 급히 쌓아 올렸다가 위험이 사라지자 버려진 그런 진지들 가운데 하나로, 이제는 숲의 고독 속에서 조용히 무너져 가며, 그것을 세우게 한 사정과 마찬가지로 잊혀 가고 거의 망각되어 있었다. 인간의 통과와 투쟁을 일러 주는 이런 자취는, 한때 적대하는 두 영토를 갈라놓던 광막한 황야의 경계 곳곳에 아직도 흔하여, 식민지 역사의 기억과 긴밀히 얽힌 일종의 폐허를 이루었으니, 그것은 주위 풍경의 음울한 성격과 어울리게 들어맞았다.[19] 나무껍질로 인 지붕은 오래전에 무너져 흙에 섞여 들었으나, 황급히 쌓아 올린 거대한 소나무 통나무들은 여전히 서로의 자리를 지키고 있었다. 다만 그 진지의 한쪽 모서리가 압력에 못 이겨 무너져, 이 투박한 건물의 나머지 부분도 머잖아 폭삭 주저앉을 듯했다. 〈헤이워드〉와 그 일행이 그토록 썩어 가는 건물에 다가가기를 망설이는 동안, 〈호크아이〉와 인디언들은 두려움 없이, 오히려 뚜렷한 흥미를 띠고 그 낮은 벽 안으로 들어섰다. 전자가 순간마다 되살아나는 기억을 지닌 자의 호기심으로 그 폐허의 안팎을 두루 살피는 동안, 〈칭가치국〉은 정복자의 긍지를 담아, 〈델라웨어〉족의 언어로 그의 아들에게, 젊은 시절 이 외딴 자리에서 벌어졌던 전투의 짧은 내력을 들려주었다. 그러나 그 승리에는 한 가닥 우수가 섞여 들어, 여느 때처럼 그의 목소리를 부드럽고 가락 있게 만들었다.\n\n그러는 동안, 자매는 기꺼이 말에서 내려, 저녁의 서늘함 속에서, 그리고 숲의 짐승 말고는 아무것도 침범할 수 없으리라 믿는 안전 속에서 이 휴식을 누릴 채비를 했다.\n\n\"우리의 쉴 자리가 좀 더 외진 곳이었더라면 좋지 않았겠소, 나의 훌륭한 벗이여,\" 보다 경계심 깊은 〈덩컨〉이, 척후가 이미 그 짧은 답사를 마친 것을 알아채고 물었다. \"이곳보다 덜 알려지고, 사람의 발길이 더 드문 자리를 골랐더라면 말이오.\"\n\n\"이 진지가 세워진 적이 있다는 걸 아는 사람은 이제 몇 남지 않았소.\" 느릿하고 사색에 잠긴 대답이 돌아왔다. \"여기서 〈모히칸〉족과 〈모호크〉족 사이에, 그들끼리 벌인 전쟁에서 치러진 그런 작은 싸움을 두고 책이 만들어지고 이야기가 쓰이는 일은 흔치 않소. 그때 나는 풋내기였고, 〈델라웨어〉족과 함께 출진했는데, 그들이 모욕당하고 억울한 일을 당한 종족인 줄 알았기 때문이오. 사십 일 낮 사십 일 밤을, 그 악귀 같은 놈들이 내가 설계하고 일부를 직접 쌓은 이 통나무 더미를 에워싸고 우리 피를 갈구했소. 당신도 기억하다시피, 나 자신은 인디언이 아니라, 피가 섞이지 않은 사람이었지만 말이오. 〈델라웨어〉족이 그 작업에 힘을 보탰고, 우리는 열 대 스물의 형세에서도 그것을 굳건히 지켜, 마침내 양쪽의 수가 거의 같아지자, 우리는 그 사냥개 같은 놈들에게 쳐 나갔고, 그놈들 가운데 단 한 명도 제 패거리의 운명을 전하러 돌아가지 못했소. 그렇소, 그렇소. 그때 나는 젊었고, 피를 보는 데 익숙지 않았소. 그래서 나와 같은 영혼을 지닌 피조물들이 벌거벗은 땅에 누워 짐승들에게 갈가리 찢기거나 빗속에 바래도록 두는 게 영 마뜩잖아, 내 손으로 그 죽은 자들을, 바로 당신들이 앉아 있는 저 작은 둔덕 아래 묻었소. 비록 죽은 인간들의 뼈로 돋워진 것이라 해도, 영 나쁜 자리는 아니지 않소.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉와 자매는 그 순간 풀로 덮인 무덤에서 벌떡 일어났다. 그리고 후자의 두 사람은, 그토록 끔찍한 장면들을 바로 얼마 전에 겪고도, 죽은 〈모호크〉족의 무덤과 그토록 가까이 맞닿아 있음을 깨닫자, 본능적인 공포의 감정을 온전히 억누르지 못했다. 잿빛 빛, 잡목 숲으로 테두리 진 음울하고 자그마한 어두운 풀밭, 그 너머로 숨 쉬는 듯한 정적 속에 솟아 보일 만큼 구름에까지 닿은 소나무들, 그리고 광막한 숲의 죽음 같은 고요 — 이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 그런 감정을 깊게 했다.\n\n\"그들은 갔고, 이제 해를 끼치지 못하오.\" 그들의 뚜렷한 놀라움을 향해 우수 어린 미소로 손을 내저으며 〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다. \"그들은 다시는 전투의 함성을 지르지도, 토마호크로 일격을 가하지도 못할 거요! 그리고 그들을 지금 누워 있는 그 자리에 눕히는 데 거든 모든 이들 가운데, 〈칭가치국〉과 나만이 살아 있소! 이 〈모히칸〉의 형제와 가족들이 우리 전투대를 이루었으니, 지금 당신들 앞에 있는 것이 그 종족의 남은 전부요.\"\n\n듣는 이들의 눈은 저도 모르게 인디언들의 모습을 좇았으니, 그 황량한 운명에 대한 연민 어린 관심에서였다. 그 검은 형상들은 여전히 통나무 진지의 그늘 안에 보였고, 아들은 아버지의 이야기에 귀 기울이고 있었으니, 오래도록 그 용기와 미개의 미덕으로 우러러 온 이들의 이름에 그토록 큰 영예를 더해 주는 이야기가 자아낼 법한 그런 골똘함이었다.\n\n\"나는 〈델라웨어〉족이 평화로운 종족이라, 결코 몸소 전쟁을 벌이지 않고, 자기 땅의 방어를 바로 당신이 죽였다는 그 〈모호크〉족에게 맡긴다고 생각했소만!\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다.\n\n\"일부는 사실이오.\" 척후가 답했다. \"허나 근본을 따지면 그건 사악한 거짓말이오. 그런 협정은 오래전, 자기들이 자리 잡은 땅에 가장 정당한 권리를 가진 원주민들의 무장을 해제시키려 한 네덜란드인들의 농간으로 맺어진 것이오. 〈모히칸〉족은, 같은 민족의 한 갈래였으되, 〈영국〉인을 상대해야 했던 까닭에, 그 어리석은 거래에 결코 발을 들이지 않고 자신들의 사내다움을 지켰소. 사실, 〈델라웨어〉족도 제 어리석음에 눈을 뜨고 나서는 그러했지. 당신들 앞에 있는 이가 바로 위대한 〈모히칸〉의 추장 가문 출신의 한 우두머리요! 한때 그의 가족은 〈올버니〉 영주의 영지보다 더 넓은 땅을 가로질러, 제 것이 아닌 시내나 언덕을 건너는 일 없이 사슴을 몰 수 있었소. 헌데 그 후손에게 남은 것이 무엇이오! 그는 하느님이 정하실 때 여섯 자의 흙을 얻을 수 있을 것이고, 어쩌면 평화로이 그것을 지킬 수도 있겠지. 보습이 닿지 못할 만큼 그의 머리를 깊이 묻어 줄 수고를 마다하지 않는 벗이 그에게 있다면 말이오!\"\n\n\"그만하시오!\" 이 화제가 어여쁜 동행들의 안녕을 지키는 데 그토록 긴요한 화목을 깨뜨릴 논쟁으로 이어질까 염려한 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"우리는 멀리 왔고, 우리 가운데 피로도 약함도 모르는 듯한 당신 같은 체격을 타고난 이는 몇 안 되오.\"\n\n\"사람의 힘줄과 뼈가 나를 이 모든 것을 헤쳐 나가게 해 주오.\" 그 칭찬이 안긴 정직한 기쁨을 드러내는 소박함으로 제 근육진 사지를 살피며 사냥꾼이 말했다. \"정착지에 가면 더 크고 더 무거운 사내들도 있겠소만, 한 도시를 여러 날 돌아다녀도 숨 한 번 고르려 멈추지 않고 오십 마일을 걸을 수 있는 자나, 몇 시간에 걸친 사냥에서 사냥개를 들리는 거리 안에 잡아 둔 자는 좀처럼 만나지 못할 거요. 허나 피와 살이 늘 한결같지는 않으니, 저 점잖은 분들이 오늘 하루 보고 겪은 그 모든 일 끝에 쉬고 싶어 하는 것도 지극히 당연하오. 〈언커스〉야, 샘을 치우거라. 네 아비와 내가 이 밤나무 어린 가지로 저 연약한 머리들을 가릴 지붕을 만들고, 풀과 잎으로 잠자리를 마련하마.\"\n\n사냥꾼과 그 일행이 자신들이 인도하는 이들의 안락과 보호를 위한 채비에 분주한 동안, 대화는 그쳤다. 오래전 원주민들이 임시 요새 자리로 이곳을 고르게 한 한 샘이 이내 잎사귀가 치워지자, 수정 같은 샘물이 바닥에서 솟아 나와, 그 물을 푸른 둔덕 위로 퍼뜨렸다. 이어 건물의 한 모퉁이가, 이 고장의 무거운 이슬을 막아 낼 수 있도록 지붕이 덮였고, 그 아래에는 자매가 쉴 수 있도록 향기로운 관목과 마른 잎이 수북이 깔렸다.\n\n부지런한 숲의 사내들이 이처럼 일하는 동안, 〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉는 마음이 내켜서라기보다 의무가 한층 더 요구하는 그 음식을 들었다. 그러고 나서 그들은 벽 안으로 물러나, 먼저 지난 자비에 대한 감사를 올리고 다가오는 밤 내내 신의 은총이 이어지기를 청한 다음, 그 연약한 몸을 향기로운 잠자리에 뉘었으니, 기억과 불길한 예감에도 불구하고, 자연이 그토록 준엄하게 요구하던, 그리고 내일에 대한 희망으로 달콤해진 그 잠 속으로 이내 빠져들었다. 〈덩컨〉은 폐허 바로 바깥, 그들 가까이에서 밤을 새워 망을 볼 채비를 했으나, 그 뜻을 알아챈 척후는 풀밭에 제 몸을 태연히 누이며 〈칭가치국〉을 가리켰고, 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"백인의 눈은 이런 망을 보기엔 너무 무겁고 너무 멀었소! 〈모히칸〉이 우리의 보초가 될 테니, 그러니 우리는 잡시다.\"\n\n\"나는 지난밤 내 초소에서 게으름뱅이임을 스스로 입증했소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"그리고 병사의 본분에 더 어울리게 처신한 당신보다 내가 쉴 필요가 덜하오. 그러니 일행 모두가 쉬도록 하고, 내가 망을 보겠소.\"\n\n\"우리가 제60연대의 흰 천막들 사이, 그리고 〈프랑스〉군 같은 적 앞에 누워 있다면, 나도 더 나은 파수꾼을 바라지 않겠소.\" 척후가 답했다. \"허나 어둠 속, 황야의 표지들 사이에서 당신의 판단은 어린아이의 어리석음과 같을 것이고, 당신의 경계는 헛수고가 될 거요. 그러니 〈언커스〉와 나처럼, 자시오. 그리고 안심하고 자시오.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는, 그들이 이야기하는 동안 젊은 인디언이 마치 쉬도록 주어진 시간을 최대한 누리려는 사람처럼 둔덕 비탈에 제 몸을 던져 누운 것을, 그리고 그 본보기를 〈데이비드〉가 따른 것을 사실로 알아챘으니, 〈데이비드〉의 목소리는 그 고된 행군으로 한층 심해진 상처의 열로 인해 말 그대로 \"그의 턱에 들러붙어\" 있었다. 쓸데없는 논쟁을 끌고 싶지 않아, 젊은이는 통나무 진지의 통나무에 등을 기대고 반쯤 기댄 자세를 취하며 따르는 시늉을 했으나, 그 소중한 짐을 〈먼로〉 본인의 품에 넘겨주기 전에는 한쪽 눈도 감지 않으리라 마음속으로 단단히 결심하고 있었다. 자신이 뜻을 관철했다고 믿은 〈호크아이〉는 이내 잠이 들었고, 그들이 처음 그것을 발견했을 때의 고독만큼이나 깊은 정적이 그 외딴 자리를 뒤덮었다.\n\n여러 분 동안 〈덩컨〉은 제 감각을 곤두세운 채로, 숲에서 일어나는 신음 같은 온갖 소리에 깨어 있도록 용케 버텼다. 저녁 그늘이 그곳에 내려앉자 그의 시야는 한층 예민해졌고, 별들이 그의 머리 위에서 반짝이기 시작한 뒤에도, 그는 풀밭에 길게 누운 동행들의 모습을 분간할 수 있었고, 사방의 어두운 장벽을 이룬 나무들 가운데 하나처럼 꼿꼿이 미동도 없이 앉은 〈칭가치국〉의 모습도 알아볼 수 있었다. 그는 여전히 몇 피트 떨어진 곳에 누운 자매의 잔잔한 숨소리를 들었고, 스치는 바람에 잎사귀 하나가 흔들려도 그의 귀가 그 속삭이는 소리를 놓치는 법이 없었다. 그러나 마침내, 쏙독새의 구슬픈 가락이 올빼미의 신음과 뒤섞였다. 무거운 그의 눈은 이따금 별들의 밝은 빛줄기를 좇았고, 그러다 그는 감긴 눈꺼풀 너머로 그것들이 보이는 듯한 착각에 빠졌다. 잠깐씩 깨어나는 순간이면 그는 덤불을 동료 보초로 착각했다. 이어 그의 머리는 어깨 위로 떨어졌고, 그 어깨는 또 땅의 받침을 찾았으며, 마침내 그의 온몸이 노곤하게 풀려, 젊은이는 깊은 잠 속으로 가라앉아, 자신이 고대 기사도의 한 기사가 되어, 되찾은 어느 공주의 천막 앞에서 한밤의 망을 보는 꿈을 꾸었으니, 그 헌신과 경계의 증표로써 그녀의 총애를 얻기를 단념하지 않은 기사였다.\n\n지친 〈덩컨〉이 이 무감각한 상태로 얼마나 오래 누워 있었는지 그 자신도 결코 알지 못했으나, 그의 잠든 환상이 완전한 망각 속으로 오래 사라졌을 즈음, 그는 어깨를 가볍게 두드리는 손길에 깨어났다. 아무리 미약했어도 이 신호에 깨어난 그는, 밤이 시작될 때 스스로 떠맡은 임무에 대한 어렴풋한 기억과 함께 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"누구냐?\" 칼이 늘 매여 있던 자리를 더듬으며 그가 다그쳤다. \"말하라! 벗이냐, 적이냐?\"\n\n\"벗이오.\" 〈칭가치국〉의 나직한 목소리가 답했다. 그는 나무 사이의 트인 틈으로 그들의 야영지에 바로 부드러운 빛을 쏟아붓는 천체를 위로 가리키며, 곧바로 그의 투박한 〈영어〉로 덧붙였다. \"달이 떴고, 백인의 요새는 멀리—멀리 있소. 〈프랑스〉인의 두 눈이 잠으로 닫혔을 때가 움직일 때요!\"\n\n\"옳은 말이오! 당신의 벗들을 깨우고 말들에 굴레를 채우시오. 그동안 나는 내 동행들에게 행군 채비를 시키겠소!\"\n\n\"우리는 깨어 있어요, 〈덩컨〉.\" 건물 안에서 〈앨리스〉의 부드럽고 은빛 같은 목소리가 말했다. \"그토록 상쾌한 잠을 잤으니 아주 빨리 갈 채비가 되었답니다. 하지만 당신은 온종일 그렇게 큰 피로를 견디고도, 우리를 위해 그 지루한 밤을 새워 망을 보았군요!\"\n\n\"차라리 망을 보려 했다고 해 두시오. 헌데 내 못 믿을 눈이 나를 배신했소. 두 번이나 나는 내가 맡은 소임에 부적합한 자임을 입증하고 말았소.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 〈덩컨〉, 그것을 부인하지 마세요.\" 미소 띤 〈앨리스〉가, 새로워진 아름다움의 온갖 사랑스러움을 띠고 건물의 그늘에서 달빛 속으로 나서며 가로막았다. \"나는 당신이 자신을 돌보아야 할 때면 부주의한 사람이지만, 남을 위할 때면 지나치리만치 경계하는 사람임을 알아요. 당신이 필요한 휴식을 취하는 동안 우리가 여기 좀 더 머물 수는 없을까요? 〈코라〉와 내가 기꺼이, 더없이 기꺼이 망을 볼게요. 그동안 당신과 이 모든 용감한 분들이 잠깐이라도 잠을 청하시도록 말이에요!\"\n\n\"수치심이 내 졸음을 고쳐 줄 수 있다면, 나는 다시는 한쪽 눈도 감지 않을 거요.\" 불안한 젊은이가 〈앨리스〉의 진실한 얼굴을 바라보며 말했으나, 그 다정한 염려 속에서 그는 반쯤 깨어난 제 의심을 굳혀 줄 그 무엇도 읽어 내지 못했다. \"내 부주의로 당신들을 위험에 끌어들이고도, 병사답게 당신들의 베갯머리를 지키는 공조차 세우지 못했다는 것은 너무도 분명한 사실이오.\"\n\n\"〈덩컨〉을 그런 약함으로 탓할 수 있는 사람은 〈덩컨〉 자신밖에 없어요. 그러니 가서 주무세요. 믿어 주세요, 연약한 처녀들인 우리지만, 둘 중 누구도 우리의 망을 저버리지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n자기 결함을 더 늘어놓아야 하는 어색함에서 젊은이가 벗어난 것은, 〈칭가치국〉이 내뱉은 외침과, 그의 아들이 취한, 한 점에 못 박힌 듯한 주의 깊은 자세 덕분이었다.\n\n\"〈모히칸〉족이 적의 기척을 듣소!\" 〈호크아이〉가 속삭였으니, 이때쯤이면 그는 일행 전체와 마찬가지로 깨어나 움직이고 있었다. \"그들이 바람결에 위험을 맡소!\"\n\n\"제발 그러지 않기를!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤다. \"우리는 분명 유혈을 충분히 겪었소!\"\n\n그러나 그가 말하는 동안에도, 젊은 군인은 제 소총을 움켜쥐고 앞쪽으로 나아가며, 자신이 보살피는 이들을 지키고자 거리낌 없이 목숨을 내놓음으로써, 그 용서받을 만한 태만을 속죄할 채비를 했다.\n\n\"먹이를 찾아 우리 주위를 어슬렁대는 숲의 어떤 짐승이오.\" 〈모히칸〉족을 놀라게 한 그 낮고, 얼핏 멀어 보이는 소리가 그 자신의 귀에 닿자마자 그가 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 주의 깊은 척후가 답했다. \"사람이오. 인디언에 비하면 내 감각이 보잘것없다 해도, 이제 나조차 그 발걸음을 알아차릴 수 있소! 그 달아난 〈휴런〉족 놈이 〈몽칼름〉의 정찰대 하나와 마주쳐, 그들이 우리 자취를 짚어 낸 거요. 나 자신도 이 자리에서 더 이상 사람의 피를 흘리고 싶지는 않소만,\" 그가 제 주위를 에워싼 흐릿한 형체들을 근심 어린 표정으로 둘러보며 덧붙였다. \"허나 일어날 일은 일어나야 하는 법! 말들을 진지 안으로 끌어들여라, 〈언커스〉. 그리고 벗들이여, 당신들도 같은 은신처로 따라 들어오시오. 비록 낡고 오래되었어도, 그것은 몸을 가릴 데가 되고, 오늘 밤 전에도 소총 소리에 울린 적이 있는 곳이오!\"\n\n그 명은 곧바로 받들어졌으니, 〈모히칸〉족이 〈내러갠셋〉종 말들을 폐허 안으로 이끌었고, 일행 전체가 더없이 조심스러운 침묵 속에 그곳으로 들었다.\n\n다가오는 발소리가 이제는 너무도 또렷이 들려, 이 방해가 어떤 성격의 것인지 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그 소리는 이내, 어떤 인디언 방언으로 서로를 부르는 목소리들과 뒤섞였으니, 사냥꾼이 〈헤이워드〉에게 속삭여 일러 준 바로는 그것이 〈휴런〉족의 언어였다. 그 무리가, 진지를 에워싼 덤불 속으로 말들이 들어선 지점에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 분명 갈피를 잃은 듯했으니, 그 순간까지 그들의 추적을 이끌어 온 표지들을 놓쳐 버린 것이었다.\n\n목소리로 미루어 이내 스무 명쯤 되는 사내들이 그 한 자리에 모여, 저마다 다른 의견과 조언을 시끄럽게 떠들어 대며 뒤섞는 듯했다.\n\n\"이 악당들은 우리가 약한 줄 아는 거요.\" 깊은 그늘 속 〈헤이워드〉의 곁에 서서, 통나무 사이의 트인 틈으로 내다보며 〈호크아이〉가 속삭였다. \"그렇지 않고서야 저런 아낙네 같은 굼뜬 행군에 게으름을 부릴 리가 없지. 저 파충류 같은 놈들 소리를 들어 보시오! 놈들은 저마다 혀는 둘이고 다리는 하나뿐인 모양이오.\"\n\n전투에서는 그토록 용감하던 〈덩컨〉도, 그토록 괴로운 긴장의 순간에는, 척후의 그 태연하고 특유한 평에 아무 대꾸도 할 수 없었다. 그는 그저 제 소총을 더 단단히 움켜쥐고, 점점 커지는 불안 속에 달빛 어린 광경을 내다보던 그 좁은 틈에 눈을 박았다. 이어, 권위를 지닌 자처럼 말하는 누군가의 한층 묵직한 목소리가, 그의 명령이, 아니 차라리 조언이 받아들여지는 경의를 드러내는 침묵 가운데 들려왔다. 그런 다음, 잎사귀가 바스락대고 마른 잔가지가 부러지는 소리로 보아, 그 미개인들이 잃어버린 자취를 좇아 흩어지고 있음이 분명했다. 쫓기는 이들에게 다행스럽게도, 달빛은 폐허 둘레의 자그마한 공터에 부드러운 광채의 홍수를 쏟아부으면서도, 형체들이 여전히 사람을 속이는 그림자 속에 누워 있는 숲의 깊은 아치까지 뚫고 들 만큼 강하지는 않았다. 수색은 헛수고로 드러났으니, 여행자들이 지나온 희미한 오솔길에서 덤불 속으로 들어선 그 통과가 어찌나 짧고 갑작스러웠던지, 그들 발자취의 모든 흔적이 숲의 어둠 속에 사라져 버린 까닭이었다.\n\n그러나 그 쉴 줄 모르는 미개인들이 덤불을 두드리며, 그 자그마한 공터를 에워싼 빽빽한 어린 밤나무 테두리의 안쪽 가장자리로 차츰 다가오는 소리가 들리기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다.\n\n\"놈들이 오고 있소.\" 통나무 틈으로 제 소총을 들이밀려 애쓰며 〈헤이워드〉가 중얼거렸다. \"다가오면 쏩시다.\"\n\n\"모든 것을 그늘 속에 두시오.\" 척후가 답했다. \"부싯돌 튀는 소리, 아니 유황 알갱이 하나의 냄새만으로도, 굶주린 그 악당들이 떼로 우리에게 덮쳐들 거요. 우리가 머리 가죽을 걸고 싸워야 하는 것이 하느님의 뜻이라면, 미개인의 방식을 아는, 그리고 전투의 함성이 울릴 때 좀처럼 뒤로 물러서지 않는 사내들의 경험에 맡기시오.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉이 등 뒤를 돌아보니, 떨고 있는 자매가 건물의 저 먼 구석에 웅크려 있는 것이 보였고, 〈모히칸〉족은 그늘 속에 마치 곧추선 두 개의 기둥처럼 서서, 일격이 필요할 때 칠 채비를 갖춘 채, 또 그러기를 분명 마다하지 않는 모습이었다. 조바심을 억누르며 그는 다시 공터를 내다보았고, 침묵 속에 결과를 기다렸다. 그 찰나, 덤불이 갈라지며 키 크고 무장한 〈휴런〉족 하나가 트인 공간으로 몇 걸음 나섰다. 그가 고요한 진지를 바라볼 때, 달빛이 그의 거무스레한 얼굴 위로 떨어져, 그 놀라움과 호기심을 고스란히 드러냈다. 그는 인디언에게서 전자의 감정에 으레 따르는 외침을 내뱉고는, 나직한 목소리로 불러 이내 한 동료를 제 곁으로 끌어들였다.\n\n이 숲의 자식들은 여러 순간 함께 서서, 허물어져 가는 건물을 가리키며 그들 부족의 알 수 없는 언어로 이야기를 나누었다. 그런 다음, 느리고 조심스러운 걸음으로나마 다가오며, 순간마다 멈춰 서서 그 건물을 바라보았으니, 그 모습은 호기심이 깨어난 두려움과 거세게 우열을 다투는, 놀란 사슴 같았다. 그중 하나의 발이 문득 둔덕 위에 닿자, 그는 그것이 무엇인지 살피려 몸을 굽혔다. 이 순간, 〈헤이워드〉는 척후가 칼집 속의 칼을 헐겁게 하고 제 소총 총구를 낮추는 것을 보았다. 이 동작들을 흉내 내며, 젊은이도 이제 피할 수 없어 보이는 그 싸움에 대비했다.\n\n미개인들이 어찌나 가까웠던지, 말 한 마리의 조그만 움직임이나, 평소보다 약간 큰 숨소리 하나만으로도 도망자들의 정체가 드러났을 것이다. 그러나 그 둔덕의 정체를 알아채자, 〈휴런〉족의 주의는 다른 대상으로 쏠리는 듯했다. 그들은 함께 이야기했고, 그 목소리는 깊은 외경이 짙게 어린 경의에 사로잡힌 듯 나직하고 엄숙했다. 그런 다음 그들은, 마치 그 고요한 벽에서 죽은 자들의 망령이 나오기라도 할 듯 두 눈을 폐허에 박은 채, 조심스레 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 공터의 경계에 이르자 천천히 덤불 속으로 들어가 사라졌다.\n\n〈호크아이〉가 소총의 개머리판을 땅에 떨구고, 길고 자유로운 숨을 내쉬며, 들릴 만한 속삭임으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"옳거니! 놈들이 죽은 자들을 경외하는구먼. 그게 이번엔 제 목숨을 구했고, 어쩌면 더 나은 사내들의 목숨까지 구한 셈이오.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 한순간 동행에게 주의를 기울였으나, 대꾸하지 않고 다시, 바로 그때 그의 관심을 더 끄는 이들에게로 몸을 돌렸다. 그는 두 〈휴런〉족이 덤불을 떠나는 소리를 들었고, 이내 추격자들 모두가 그들 둘레에 모여, 그 보고에 깊이 주의를 기울이고 있음이 분명해졌다. 처음 그 자리에 모여들 때의 시끄러운 떠들썩함과는 전혀 다른, 몇 분간의 진지하고 엄숙한 대화 끝에, 그 소리는 점점 희미해지고 멀어지더니, 마침내 숲의 깊은 곳에서 사라졌다.\n\n〈호크아이〉는, 귀 기울여 듣던 〈칭가치국〉이 보낸 신호가, 물러가는 무리에게서 나는 모든 소리가 거리 속에 완전히 삼켜졌음을 그에게 확인해 줄 때까지 기다렸다가, 〈헤이워드〉에게 말들을 끌어내고 자매가 안장에 오르도록 거들라고 손짓했다. 그 일이 끝나는 즉시, 그들은 부서진 출입구를 통해 나가, 들어왔던 방향과 반대 방향으로 몰래 빠져나가며 그 자리를 떠났으니, 자매는 부드러운 달빛을 뒤로하고 숲의 음울함 속에 몸을 묻으며, 그 고요한 무덤과 허물어져 가는 폐허를 향해 슬그머니 흘끔거렸다.\n\n\"보초.—누구냐?\n\n퓌셀.—농부요, 프랑스의 가난한 백성이라오.\"\n\n「헨리 6세」\n\n요새 건물에서 황급히 빠져나와 일행이 숲 깊숙이 묻힐 때까지, 모두가 탈출에 너무 골몰한 나머지 속삭임으로조차 말 한마디 함부로 내뱉지 못했다. 정찰병은 다시 선두의 자리로 돌아갔으나, 적들과 자신 사이에 안전한 거리를 벌려 놓은 뒤로는, 주위 숲의 지형에 전혀 깜깜한 탓에 그 걸음이 앞서의 행군 때보다 한결 신중했다. 그는 한 번 이상 멈춰 서서 동료들인 모히칸족과 의논하며 달을 가리키기도 하고 나무껍질을 조심스레 살피기도 했다. 이 짧은 멈춤들 속에서, 〈헤이워드〉와 자매는 위험으로 두 배나 예민해진 감각으로 귀를 기울여, 적들이 가까이 있음을 알릴 만한 기척이라도 잡아내려 했다. 그런 순간이면, 광막한 들판이 영원한 잠 속에 파묻힌 듯했으니, 멀고도 거의 들리지 않는 시냇물의 잔물결 소리를 빼고는 숲에서 아무런 소리도 일지 않았다. 새도 짐승도 사람도 한결같이 잠든 듯했으니, 설령 이 드넓은 황야의 자락 어딘가에 사람이 있었다 한들 그러했다. 그러나 가냘프게 속삭일 뿐인 그 시냇물 소리가, 안내자들을 적잖은 곤혹에서 단숨에 풀어 주었고, 그들은 곧장 그쪽으로 길을 잡았다.\n\n자그마한 시내의 둑에 다다르자, 〈호크아이〉는 다시 한 번 걸음을 멈추었다. 그러고는 발에서 모카신을 벗어 들며, 〈헤이워드〉와 〈거뭇〉에게도 자기를 따라 하라고 권했다. 그런 다음 물속으로 들어갔고, 한 시간 가까이 그들은 흔적을 남기지 않으려 개울 바닥을 걸었다. 달은 이미 서쪽 지평선 위에 드리운 시커먼 구름 더미 속으로 가라앉아 있었으니, 그들이 낮고 구불구불한 물길에서 벗어나 모래밭이되 나무가 우거진 평지의 빛과 높이로 다시 올라설 무렵이었다. 이곳에서 정찰병은 다시 제 집 안에 든 듯이 보였으니, 제 앎의 든든함 속에서 움직이는 사람의 확신과 부지런함으로 길을 헤쳐 나갔다. 길은 이내 더 험해졌고, 여행자들은 양편으로 산들이 점점 가까이 다가서고 있음을, 그리하여 자신들이 실로 그 협곡 가운데 하나로 막 들어서려 함을 또렷이 알아챌 수 있었다. 갑자기 〈호크아이〉가 걸음을 멈추었고, 일행 모두가 합류하기를 기다린 뒤, 입을 열었으니, 그 목소리는 너무나 낮고 조심스러워서, 그 장소의 고요와 어둠 속에서 그의 말에 한층 엄숙함을 더했다.\n\n\"황야의 길을 알고, 짐승이 소금을 핥는 곳이나 물길을 찾아내기란 쉬운 일이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"허나 이곳을 본 누가, 저 말 없는 나무들과 헐벗은 산들 가운데 거대한 군대가 쉬고 있다고 감히 말할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우리가 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에서 그리 멀지 않다는 말이오?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 정찰병에게 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"아직은 길고 고된 길이오. 더구나 언제, 어디서 그 길로 들어서느냐가 지금 우리의 가장 큰 난제요. 보시오,\" 그가 나무들 사이로, 잔잔한 품에 별빛을 비추는 작은 웅덩이가 있는 곳을 가리키며 말했다. \"여기가 '피의 못'이오. 그리고 나는, 여러 번 지나다녔을 뿐 아니라, 해 뜰 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 적과 싸웠던 바로 그 땅 위에 서 있는 거요.\"\n\n\"하! 그렇다면 저 흐릿하고 음산한 물판이, 그 싸움에서 쓰러진 용사들의 무덤이란 말이로군. 이름은 들어 보았으나, 그 둑 위에 서 본 적은 한 번도 없었소.\"\n\n\"우리는 하루에 그 네덜란드계 프랑스인과 세 차례 싸움을 벌였소.\" 〈호크아이〉는 〈덩컨〉의 말에 대답하기보다는 제 생각의 줄기를 좇아 말을 이었다. \"그자가 우리를 바로 이 가까이에서, 우리가 그자의 진격을 매복하려 나아가던 행군 길에서 맞닥뜨려, 쫓기는 사슴처럼 우리를 협곡을 가로질러 〈호리컨〉의 기슭까지 흩어 놓았소. 그러자 우리는 쓰러진 나무들 뒤로 다시 모여, 〈윌리엄〉 경 — 바로 그 공으로 〈윌리엄〉 경이 되신 분 — 의 지휘 아래 그자에게 맞섰소. 그리고 우리는 아침의 치욕을 톡톡히 갚아 주었소! 그날 수백 명의 프랑스인이 마지막으로 해를 보았고, 우두머리인 〈디스카우〉 자신마저 납탄에 베이고 찢겨 우리 손에 떨어졌으니, 그자는 더는 전쟁에 나설 수 없게 되어 제 나라로 돌아가고 말았소.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 격퇴였소!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 젊은 혈기에 달아올라 외쳤다. \"그 명성은 우리 남부의 군대에까지 일찌감치 닿았소.\"\n\n\"그렇소! 허나 거기서 끝나지 않았소. 나는 〈윌리엄〉 경 친히 내리신 명에 따라 〈에핑엄〉 소령의 지시로, 프랑스군의 측면을 돌아 그들의 참패 소식을 육로 운반로 너머 〈허드슨〉강의 요새로 전하러 보내졌소. 바로 이 근처, 나무들이 산등성이로 솟아오른 저기에서, 나는 우리를 도우러 내려오는 한 무리를 만났고, 그들을 적이 끼니를 들고 있던 곳으로 이끌었소. 그자들은 그날의 피비린 일을 아직 다 끝내지 못했음을 꿈에도 몰랐던 거요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그들을 기습했소?\"\n\n\"오로지 제 식욕의 갈망만 생각하던 자들에게 죽음이 기습이 될 수 있다면 그렇소. 우리는 그자들에게 숨 돌릴 틈조차 거의 주지 않았으니, 그들이 아침의 싸움에서 우리를 모질게 몰아붙였던 데다, 우리 무리에 그들의 손에 벗이나 혈육을 잃지 않은 자가 거의 없었던 까닭이오. 다 끝났을 때, 죽은 자들은, 그리고 더러는 죽어 가는 자들마저, 저 작은 못 속에 던져졌소. 이 두 눈은 그 물이 피로 물든 것을 보았으니, 그런 자연의 물이 일찍이 땅의 속에서 흘러나온 적은 없었소.\"\n\n\"군인에게는 알맞은, 그리고 부디 평온한 무덤이 되기를 바라오. 그렇다면 이 변경에서 많은 전투를 겪었군요?\"\n\n\"내가!\" 정찰병이 군인다운 자긍심으로 그 큰 키를 곧추세우며 말했다. \"이 산들 가운데 내 소총의 총성으로 울리지 않은 메아리가 별로 없고, 〈호리컨〉과 강 사이 한 평방마일의 땅치고 '킬디어'가 산 몸뚱이 하나 떨어뜨리지 않은 데가 없소. 적이든 짐승이든 말이오. 무덤으로 말하자면, 그것이 당신 말처럼 그토록 고요한가는 또 다른 문제요. 진지에는, 사람이 가만히 누우려면 몸에 숨이 붙어 있는 동안에는 묻혀서는 안 된다고 말하고 또 그리 믿는 자들이 있소. 그리고 그날 저녁의 황망함 속에서, 군의관들이 누가 살아 있고 누가 죽었는지 가릴 틈이 거의 없었던 것만은 분명하오. 쉿! 못의 기슭에 무언가 걷고 있는 것이 보이지 않소?\"\n\n\"이 음산한 숲속에서, 우리만큼이나 거처 없는 자가 또 있을 성싶지는 않소.\"\n\n\"그런 자라면 집이나 거처 따위는 거의 개의치 않을 것이고, 밤이슬은 낮을 물속에서 보내는 몸뚱이를 결코 적시지 못하는 법이오.\" 정찰병이 그렇게 받으며, 〈헤이워드〉의 어깨를 어찌나 발작적인 힘으로 움켜쥐던지, 젊은 군인은 평소 그토록 겁 없던 사내가 미신적인 공포에 얼마나 사로잡혀 있는지를 아프도록 절감했다.\n\n\"맙소사! 사람의 형상이오, 그것이 다가오고 있소! 무기를 잡으시오, 동지들. 우리가 누구를 맞닥뜨리는지 알 수 없으니.\"\n\n\"누구냐?\" 준엄하고 날카로운 목소리가 다그쳤으니, 그 외롭고 엄숙한 곳에서 흘러나온 그것은 마치 다른 세상에서 던지는 도전처럼 들렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고 한 거요?\" 정찰병이 속삭였다. \"인디언 말도, 영어도 아니잖소!\"\n\n\"누구냐?\" 같은 목소리가 되풀이했고, 곧이어 무기를 절거덕거리는 소리와 위협하는 자세가 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"프랑스다!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 외치며 나무 그늘에서 못의 기슭으로 나아가, 보초로부터 몇 야드 안쪽까지 다가섰다.\n\n\"어디서 오는가—이런 이른 시각에 어디로 가는가?\" 척탄병이 다그쳤으니, 그 말과 억양은 본토 프랑스에서 온 사람의 것이었다.\n\n\"정찰에서 돌아와, 잠자리에 들러 가는 길이오.\"\n\n\"국왕의 장교인가?\"\n\n\"물론이지, 전우. 나를 식민지 사람으로 보는가! 나는 추격병 대위요.\" (〈헤이워드〉는 상대가 정규 보병 연대 소속임을 잘 알고 있었다.) \"여기 나와 함께 요새 사령관의 따님들을 데리고 있소. 아하! 그 이야기를 들었던 모양이군! 다른 요새 근처에서 이들을 포로로 잡아, 장군께 데려가는 길이오.\"\n\n\"이런! 부인들, 참으로 안되셨습니다.\" 젊은 병사가 모자에 손을 우아하게 갖다 대며 외쳤다. \"허나—전쟁의 운명이지요! 우리 장군께서는 용감한 분이시고, 부인들께 무척 정중하시다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그것이 군인의 본성이지요.\" 〈코라〉가 감탄스러운 침착함으로 말했다. \"잘 가요, 친구여. 그대에게 좀 더 즐거운 임무가 주어지기를 바라겠어요.\"\n\n병사는 그녀의 정중함에 낮고 겸손하게 답례했고, 〈헤이워드〉가 \"잘 자게, 전우\"라고 덧붙이자, 그들은 신중하게 앞으로 나아갔다. 보초는 그토록 뻔뻔한 적을 거의 의심하지 않은 채 말 없는 못의 둑을 거닐며, 여인들을 본 까닭에, 그리고 어쩌면 멀고도 아름다운 제 조국 프랑스에 대한 추억으로 떠오른 그 노랫말을 홀로 흥얼거렸다.\n\n\"포도주여, 사랑이여, 만세.\" 운운.\n\n\"저 악당의 말을 알아들으니 다행이구려!\" 정찰병이, 그곳에서 조금 거리를 두고 나서, 소총을 다시 팔 안쪽에 끼며 속삭였다. \"나는 곧 그자가 저 불안해하는 프랑스인 중 하나임을 알아챘소. 그자에겐 다행이었지, 그 말이 다정하고 그 속내가 친절했기에 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 그자의 뼈가 제 동포들 뼈 틈에 자리를 얻었을 거요.\"\n\n작은 웅덩이에서 길고 무거운 신음이 일어나며 그의 말을 끊었으니, 마치 떠난 자들의 혼령이 정말로 그 물의 무덤가에 머물러 있기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n\"분명 살아 있는 것이었소!\" 정찰병이 말을 이었다. \"어떤 혼령도 그토록 흔들림 없이 무기를 다룰 수는 없으니!\"\n\n\"살아 있는 것이었소. 허나 그 가엾은 친구가 아직도 이 세상에 속해 있는지는 의심스럽소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 주위를 둘러보며, 일행 가운데 〈칭가치국〉이 보이지 않음을 알아채고 말했다. 앞의 것보다 더 희미한 또 한 차례의 신음이 일더니, 이어 물속으로 무겁고 둔하게 풍덩 잠기는 소리가 났고, 그러자 모든 것이 다시 고요해져, 마치 이 음산한 못의 가장자리가 천지창조 이래의 정적에서 한 번도 깨어난 적이 없었던 듯했다. 그들이 아직 불안 속에 머뭇거리는 동안, 인디언의 형상이 덤불에서 미끄러져 나오는 것이 보였다. 우두머리가 그들에게 합류할 때, 한 손으로는 불운한 젊은 프랑스인의 김이 나는 머릿가죽을 제 허리띠에 매달았고, 다른 손으로는 그의 피를 마신 칼과 토마호크를 도로 꽂아 넣었다. 그러고는 공을 세웠다고 믿는 사람의 표정으로 늘 서던 자리에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n정찰병은 소총의 한쪽 끝을 땅에 내려놓고, 다른 쪽 끝에 두 손을 얹은 채, 깊은 침묵 속에 생각에 잠겨 섰다. 그러고는 슬픈 듯이 고개를 가로저으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"백인에게라면 잔인하고 비인간적인 짓이었을 거요. 허나 그것은 인디언의 천성이자 본성이니, 부정해서는 안 되겠지 싶소. 그래도, 저 본국에서 온 명랑한 젊은이가 아니라 저주받은 〈밍고〉족 하나에게 그 일이 닥쳤더라면 좋았을 것을.\"\n\n\"그만하면 됐소!\" 〈헤이워드〉가, 영문 모르는 자매가 이 지체의 까닭을 헤아릴까 염려하여, 그리고 사냥꾼의 것과 자못 비슷한 일련의 생각으로 제 혐오를 억누르며 말했다. \"이미 끝난 일이오. 안 하느니만 못한 일이었다 한들, 돌이킬 수는 없소. 보다시피 우리는 너무도 빤히 적의 보초 안쪽에 들어와 있소. 어떤 길을 따르자고 제안하겠소?\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 〈호크아이〉가 다시 정신을 차리며 말했다. \"당신 말대로요, 그 일을 두고 더 골몰하기엔 너무 늦었소. 그렇소, 프랑스군이 요새 둘레에 단단히 모여들었으니, 그들을 지나려면 우리는 섬세한 바늘귀에 실을 꿰어야 하오.\"\n\n\"그것도 짧은 시간 안에 해야 하오.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 지는 달을 가린 안개의 둑을 향해 위를 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그것도 짧은 시간 안에!\" 정찰병이 되풀이했다. \"그 일은 두 가지 방식으로 해낼 수 있소, 섭리의 도움으로 말이오. 그 도움이 없다면 아예 해낼 수 없소.\"\n\n\"어서 말해 보시오, 시간이 다그치니.\"\n\n\"하나는, 그 고운 분들을 말에서 내리게 하고 짐승들은 들판을 떠돌게 두는 거요. 모히칸족을 앞세워, 그들의 보초들 사이로 길을 베어 내고, 시체들을 밟고 요새로 들어가는 거요.\"\n\n\"안 되오—안 되오!\" 인심 후한 〈헤이워드〉가 가로막았다. \"군인이라면 이런 식으로 길을 뚫을 수 있겠지만, 이런 일행을 거느리고는 결코 안 되오.\"\n\n\"실로, 여린 발이 헤쳐 가기엔 피로 물든 길이 될 거요.\" 마찬가지로 내키지 않아 하던 정찰병이 받았다. \"허나 그것을 말하는 것이 사내다운 도리라 여겼소. 그렇다면 우리는 왔던 자취로 되돌아가 그들의 감시선 밖으로 나가야 하오. 그런 다음 짧게 서쪽으로 꺾어 산으로 들어가는 거요. 거기서 내가 당신들을 숨겨 주리다. 〈몽칼름〉의 돈을 받는 악마의 사냥개를 다 풀어 놓아도, 몇 달이 가도록 냄새를 못 맡게 말이오.\"\n\n\"그리하시오, 그것도 당장에.\"\n\n더는 말이 필요 없었으니, 〈호크아이〉는 그저 \"따라오시오\"라는 명만을 내뱉고, 그들이 방금 이 위태롭고 위험하기까지 한 처지로 들어선 그 길을 따라 움직였다. 그들의 전진은 방금 전의 대화처럼 조심스럽고 소리가 없었으니, 어느 순간에 지나가는 순찰대나 웅크린 적의 보초가 그들의 길 위로 솟아오를지 아무도 몰랐던 까닭이다. 못의 가장자리를 따라 말없이 길을 잡는 동안, 〈헤이워드〉와 정찰병은 다시금 그 소름 끼치는 음산함에 은밀한 시선을 던졌다. 그들은 방금 전 그 고요한 기슭을 거닐던 형상을 찾았으나 헛수고였고, 작은 물결이 낮고 규칙적으로 찰싹이며, 물이 아직 가라앉지 않았음을 알리는 것이, 그들이 방금 목격한 피의 행위를 끔찍하게 떠올리게 했다. 그러나 그 음울하게 지나가던 모든 광경과 마찬가지로, 낮은 웅덩이도 이내 어둠 속에 녹아들어, 여행자들의 뒤편에 늘어선 검은 형상들의 덩어리에 섞여 들었다.\n\n〈호크아이〉는 곧 퇴각의 길에서 벗어나, 좁은 평지의 서쪽 경계를 이루는 산들을 향해 방향을 틀었고, 일행을 빠른 걸음으로 그 높고 험한 봉우리들이 드리운 그림자 깊숙이 이끌었다. 이제 길은 고되었으니, 바위로 울퉁불퉁하고 골짜기로 끊긴 땅 위에 놓여 있어, 그들의 전진도 그만큼 더뎠다. 황량하고 시커먼 언덕들이 사방으로 그들을 에워쌌으니, 행군의 가중된 노고를, 그것들이 안겨 주는 안도감으로 어느 정도 보상해 주었다. 마침내 일행은 바위와 나무 사이를 묘하게 굽이도는 길을 따라, 가파르고 험한 비탈을 천천히 오르기 시작했으니, 그 길은 바위는 피하고 나무에는 기대는 식으로, 황야의 기예에 오래 단련된 사람들이 고안했음을 보여 주었다. 골짜기의 높이에서 차츰 솟아오르자, 날이 다가오기 직전에 으레 깔리던 짙은 어둠이 흩어지기 시작했고, 사물들은 자연이 부여한 그 또렷하고 손에 잡힐 듯한 빛깔로 보이게 되었다. 산의 헐벗은 비탈에 매달린 우거진 숲에서 벗어나, 그 정상을 이루는 평평하고 이끼 낀 바위 위로 나섰을 때, 그들은 아침을 맞았으니, 그 아침은 〈호리컨〉 골짜기 건너편에 놓인 언덕의 푸른 소나무들 위로 붉게 물들며 떠오르고 있었다.\n\n이제 정찰병은 자매에게 말에서 내리라 일렀고, 지친 짐승들의 입에서 재갈을, 등에서 안장을 벗긴 뒤, 그것들을 풀어 놓아, 그 높은 고장의 관목과 빈약한 풀 사이에서 변변찮은 끼니나마 뜯게 했다.\n\n\"가거라,\" 그가 말했다. \"자연이 너희에게 먹을 것을 주는 곳에서 그것을 찾아라. 그리고 이 언덕들 사이에서 너희 자신이 굶주린 늑대의 먹이가 되지 않도록 조심하거라.\"\n\n\"이제 저것들이 더는 필요 없소?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"보시오, 그리고 당신 두 눈으로 판단하시오.\" 정찰병이 산의 동쪽 마루를 향해 나아가며, 일행 모두에게 따라오라 손짓하며 말했다. \"이 자리에서 〈몽칼름〉의 진지가 벌거벗고 드러난 것을 엿보기가 쉬운 만큼, 사람의 마음속을 들여다보기도 쉽다면, 위선자가 드물어질 것이고, 〈밍고〉족의 교활함은 〈델라웨어〉족의 정직함에 견주면 지는 노름이 될 거요.\"\n\n여행자들이 벼랑 끝에 다다랐을 때, 그들은 정찰병의 말이 진실임을, 그리고 그가 자신들을 이 내려다보기 좋은 자리로 이끈 그 빼어난 선견지명을 한눈에 알아보았다.\n\n그들이 서 있는 산은, 어쩌면 천 피트쯤 공중으로 솟았을 높은 원뿔로, 호수의 서쪽 기슭을 따라 여러 마일에 걸쳐 뻗은 산맥보다 조금 앞으로 나와 솟아 있었으니, 그 산맥은 물 건너 자매 봉우리들과 만난 뒤, 어지럽고 깨진 바위 덩어리가 되어 상록수가 듬성듬성 뿌려진 채 〈캐나다〉 쪽으로 달려 나갔다. 일행의 바로 발치에서, 〈호리컨〉의 남쪽 기슭이 산에서 산으로 넓은 반원을 그리며 휘돌아, 너른 모래톱을 이루었으니, 그것이 이내 울퉁불퉁하고 다소 솟아오른 평지로 올라섰다. 북쪽으로는, 맑고, 그 아찔한 높이에서 보기로는 좁다란 \"거룩한 호수\"의 물판이 뻗어 있었으니, 헤아릴 수 없는 만(灣)으로 들쭉날쭉하고, 기이한 곶들로 꾸며졌으며, 수없는 섬들이 점점이 박혀 있었다. 몇 리그쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 물의 바닥은 산들 사이로 자취를 감추거나, 가벼운 아침 바람결에 그 품을 따라 천천히 굴러오는 안개 덩어리에 휩싸였다. 그러나 언덕 마루들 사이의 좁은 틈이, 그 물이 더 북쪽으로 길을 찾아 나가, 맑고 너른 물판을 다시 펼친 뒤 먼 〈섐플레인〉으로 그 물을 부어 넣는 그 통로를 가리켜 보였다. 남쪽으로는, 그토록 자주 언급된 협곡, 아니 끊긴 평지가 뻗어 있었다. 이 방향으로 여러 마일에 걸쳐, 산들은 그 지배권을 내어 주기를 꺼리는 듯했으나, 눈이 닿는 데에서 그것들은 갈라지더니, 마침내 우리가 모험가들을 따라 두 차례의 여정을 함께한 그 평평하고 모래 깔린 땅으로 녹아들었다. 호수와 골짜기의 마주한 양편을 가른 두 산맥을 따라, 가벼운 안개구름이 사람 없는 숲에서 나선의 화환을 그리며 피어올랐으니, 마치 숨은 오두막의 연기처럼 보였고, 더러는 비탈을 따라 나른하게 굴러 내려가 낮은 땅의 안개에 섞여 들었다. 외로이 홀로 떠 있는 눈처럼 흰 구름 하나가 골짜기 위에 떠서, 그 아래에 \"피의 못\"의 말 없는 웅덩이가 놓인 자리를 일러 주었다.\n\n호수의 기슭 바로 위, 동쪽 가장자리보다 서쪽 가장자리에 더 가까이, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 드넓은 흙벽과 낮은 건물들이 놓여 있었다. 휘돌아 나간 두 능보(稜堡)는 그 밑동을 씻는 물 위에 기댄 듯했고, 깊은 해자와 넓은 늪지가 다른 쪽 면과 모서리들을 지켰다. 요새 둘레로 적당한 거리만큼 나무가 베여 있었으나, 그 광경의 다른 모든 부분은 자연의 푸른 제복에 싸여 있었으니, 다만 맑은 물이 풍경을 부드럽게 한 곳이나, 험한 바위들이 산맥의 굽이치는 윤곽선 위로 검고 헐벗은 머리를 내민 곳만은 예외였다. 그 정면에는, 수많은 적에 맞서 고단한 경계를 서던 흩어진 보초들이 보였고, 벽 안쪽으로는 밤샘의 경계로 아직 졸음에 겨운 병사들을 여행자들이 내려다보았다. 남동쪽으로, 그러나 요새와 바로 맞닿은 곳에는, 차라리 요새 그 자체를 짓기에 훨씬 알맞았을 바위 언덕 위에 참호로 둘러친 진지가 자리해 있었으니, 〈호크아이〉는 그곳에, 얼마 전 그들과 함께 〈허드슨〉강을 떠났던 그 지원 연대들이 있음을 가리켜 보였다. 조금 더 남쪽의 숲에서는, 샘의 더 맑은 김과는 쉬이 구별되는 어둡고 음산한 연기가 무수히 피어올랐으니, 정찰병은 그것 또한 〈헤이워드〉에게, 적이 그 방향에 대군으로 진치고 있다는 증거로 보여 주었다.\n\n그러나 젊은 군인의 마음을 가장 사로잡은 광경은 호수의 서쪽 기슭에 있었으니, 다만 그 남쪽 끝에 꽤 가까운 곳이었다. 그가 선 자리에서 보기에는 그런 군대를 담기엔 너무 좁아 보였으나, 실은 〈호리컨〉의 기슭에서 산의 밑동까지 수백 야드나 뻗은 한 줄기 땅 위에, 만 명에 이르는 진영의 흰 천막들과 군용 기계들이 보였다. 그들의 정면에는 이미 포대가 쌓여 있었고, 그 위쪽에서 구경꾼들이 발치에 지도처럼 펼쳐진 광경을 그토록 서로 다른 감정으로 내려다보는 바로 그 순간에도, 포성이 골짜기에서 솟아올라, 우레 같은 메아리가 되어 동쪽 언덕들을 따라 사라져 갔다.\n\n\"아침이 저 아래의 그들을 이제 막 어루만지고 있구려.\" 신중하고 생각에 잠긴 정찰병이 말했다. \"그리고 보초들은 대포 소리로 잠든 자들을 깨우려는 모양이오. 우리가 몇 시간 늦었군. 〈몽칼름〉은 이미 저 저주받은 〈이로쿼이〉족으로 숲을 가득 채웠소.\"\n\n\"이곳은 실로 포위되었군요.\" 〈덩컨〉이 받았다. \"허나 우리가 들어갈 방도가 없겠소? 요새 안에서 사로잡히는 편이, 떠도는 인디언들의 손에 다시 떨어지는 것보다 훨씬 나으니.\"\n\n\"보시오!\" 정찰병이 무심코 〈코라〉의 주의를 그녀의 친아버지의 거처로 돌리며 외쳤다. \"저 포탄이 사령관 집 벽에서 돌을 튀겨 날린 꼴을 좀 보시오! 그렇소! 이 프랑스인들은, 그것이 아무리 단단하고 두껍게 쌓였다 한들, 쌓인 것보다 더 빨리 그것을 산산조각 낼 거요.\"\n\n\"〈헤이워드〉, 나는 함께 나눌 수 없는 위험을 보는 것이 메스껍군요.\" 겁 없으나 근심에 찬 딸이 말했다. \"〈몽칼름〉에게로 가서 들여보내 달라 청합시다. 그는 한 자식의 청을 감히 거절하지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"머리에 머리카락을 붙인 채로는 그 프랑스인의 천막을 찾기 어려울 거요.\" 무뚝뚝한 정찰병이 말했다. \"저 기슭을 따라 텅 빈 채 놓인 그 천 척의 배 가운데 한 척만 내게 있다면, 해 볼 수도 있겠소만. 하! 저기 낮을 밤으로 바꿀, 그리고 인디언의 화살을 주조된 대포보다 더 위험하게 만들 안개가 오고 있으니, 곧 포격도 끝이 나겠소. 자, 만일 그 일을 감당할 수 있고 따라오겠다면, 내가 한번 밀고 나가 보겠소. 나는 저 자작나무 덤불 가장자리에 숨어 도사린 게 보이는 〈밍고〉족 개들 몇 마리를 흩어 놓기 위해서라도, 저 진영으로 내려가고 싶어 좀이 쑤시니까.\"\n\n\"우리는 감당할 수 있어요.\" 〈코라〉가 단호히 말했다. \"그런 임무라면 어떤 위험에라도 따르겠어요.\"\n\n정찰병은 정직하고 따뜻한 칭찬의 미소를 지으며 그녀를 돌아보고는 대답했다.\n\n\"당신만큼이나 죽음을 가벼이 여기는, 억센 팔다리와 빠른 눈을 지닌 사내가 내게 천 명만 있다면! 한 주가 가기 전에, 저 지껄여 대는 프랑스인들을, 사슬에 묶인 사냥개나 굶주린 늑대 떼처럼 울부짖게 하여 제 굴 속으로 도로 쫓아 보낼 텐데. 허나 어서 움직이시오.\" 그가 그녀에게서 나머지 일행 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 덧붙였다. \"안개가 어찌나 빠르게 굴러 내려오는지, 평지에서 그것을 맞아 엄폐물로 쓸 시간이 겨우 있을 뿐이오. 명심하시오, 만일 내게 무슨 변고가 닥치거든, 바람이 당신들의 왼뺨에 불도록 하시오—아니, 차라리 모히칸족을 따르시오. 그들은 낮이든 밤이든 냄새로 제 길을 찾아낼 테니.\"\n\n그러고는 따라오라 손을 저으며, 자유로우나 조심스러운 걸음으로 가파른 비탈 아래로 몸을 던졌다. 〈헤이워드〉가 자매가 내려가는 것을 도왔고, 몇 분 만에 그들은 모두, 그토록 큰 노고와 고통으로 그 비탈을 올랐던 산 아래로 멀찍이 내려가 있었다.\n\n〈호크아이〉가 잡은 방향은 곧 여행자들을 평지의 높이로 데려왔으니, 요새 서쪽 성벽의 출격문과 거의 마주한 자리였다. 그 출격문 자체는, 그가 〈덩컨〉이 일행을 데리고 따라오기를 기다리려 멈춰 선 지점에서 반 마일쯤 떨어져 있었다. 그들은 조급함에, 그리고 지형의 도움을 받아, 호수를 따라 묵직하게 굴러 내려오던 안개보다 먼저 와 버렸고, 그래서 안개가 적의 진영을 그 솜털 같은 외투로 감쌀 때까지 멈추어 있어야 했다. 모히칸족은 이 지체를 틈타 숲에서 살그머니 나가 주위의 사물들을 정찰했다. 정찰병은, 그들의 보고를 일찌감치 활용하고, 자신도 더 가까운 지형을 어렴풋이나마 알아 두려는 뜻으로, 조금 떨어져 그들의 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n이내 그가 돌아왔으니, 얼굴이 분함으로 붉어진 채, 그다지 점잖지 못한 말로 제 낙담을 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"저 교활한 프랑스인이 우리 길목 바로 위에 보초를 세워 놓았소.\" 그가 말했다. \"붉은 피부든 흰 피부든 말이오. 우리는 안개 속에서 그들을 지나치기는커녕, 그들 한가운데로 떨어질 공산이 크겠소!\"\n\n\"그 위험을 피하도록 돌아갔다가,\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다. \"지나친 뒤 다시 우리 길로 들어설 수는 없소?\"\n\n\"안개 속에서 일단 제 행군의 선에서 벗어나면, 언제 어떻게 돌아서야 그것을 다시 찾는지 누가 알겠소! 〈호리컨〉의 안개는 화친 담뱃대에서 피어오르는 연기 가닥이나, 모기 쫓는 불 위에 내려앉는 연기 같은 것이 아니오.\"\n\n그가 아직 말하고 있을 때, 무언가 부서지는 소리가 들리더니, 포탄 하나가 덤불 속으로 날아들어 어린나무의 몸통을 때리고 땅으로 튕겨 나왔으니, 그 힘이 앞선 저항에 거의 다 소진된 뒤였다. 인디언들이 그 무시무시한 전령을 분주히 시중드는 종복들처럼 곧장 뒤따랐고, 〈언카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 열렬히, 그리고 많은 몸짓을 곁들여 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠다, 얘야.\" 그가 말을 마치자 정찰병이 중얼거렸다. \"절박한 열병은 치통 다루듯 다룰 게 아니니. 자, 그러면, 안개가 닥쳐오고 있소.\"\n\n\"멈추시오!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤다. \"먼저 당신 속셈을 설명하시오.\"\n\n\"금세 끝날 이야기요, 그리고 작은 희망이긴 하나, 없는 것보다는 낫소. 당신이 보는 이 포탄이,\" 정찰병이 그 무해한 쇳덩이를 발로 차며 덧붙였다. \"요새에서 날아오는 길에 땅을 갈아 놓았으니, 다른 모든 표지가 다 사라져도, 우리는 그것이 낸 고랑을 좇으면 되오. 더는 말 말고, 따라오시오. 안 그러면 안개가 우리를 길 한복판에, 양쪽 군대가 다 쏘아 대는 과녁으로 버려둘지도 모르오.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는, 실로 말보다 행동이 더 요구되는 위기가 닥쳤음을 깨닫고, 자매 사이에 자리를 잡고 그들을 재빨리 앞으로 이끌며, 안내자의 흐릿한 모습을 눈에서 놓치지 않았다. 〈호크아이〉가 안개의 위력을 부풀려 말한 것이 아님은 곧 분명해졌으니, 그들이 스무 야드도 채 나아가기 전에, 일행의 서로 다른 사람들이 그 안개 속에서 서로를 분간하기조차 어려워졌다.\n\n그들은 왼쪽으로 작게 돌았다가, 이미 다시 오른쪽으로 기울고 있었으니, 〈헤이워드〉의 생각으로는 아군의 진지까지 거리의 거의 절반을 지나왔을 무렵, 그의 귀에 사나운 호령이 와 닿았으니, 그것은 그들에게서 스무 피트도 안 되는 곳에서 들려오는 듯한—\n\n\"누가 가느냐?\"\n\n\"밀고 나가시오!\" 정찰병이 다시 한 번 왼쪽으로 꺾으며 속삭였다.\n\n\"밀고 나가시오!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 되풀이했다. 그러자 그 호령이 열두 명의 목소리로 새로워졌으니, 저마다 위협이 가득 실린 듯했다.\n\n\"나다.\" 〈덩컨〉이 외치며, 자신이 부축하던 이들을 이끈다기보다 끌다시피 재빨리 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n\"멍청한 놈!—누구냐?—나라니!\"\n\n\"프랑스의 벗이다.\"\n\n\"넌 내게 영락없이 프랑스의 적으로 보인다. 멈춰라! 안 그러면 맹세코 네놈을 악마의 벗으로 만들어 주마. 안 돼! 쏴라, 전우들, 쏴!\"\n\n명령은 즉각 받들어졌고, 안개는 쉰 자루의 머스킷이 터지는 소리에 흔들렸다. 다행히 조준이 빗나가, 총알들은 도망자들이 잡은 방향과 조금 다른 쪽으로 공기를 가르고 지나갔으나, 그래도 그들에게 너무 가까워, 〈데이비드〉와 두 여인의 익숙지 못한 귀에는 마치 총알이 귀에서 몇 인치 안쪽을 휘파람 소리를 내며 지나가는 듯했다. 함성이 새로워졌고, 다시 쏘라는 것뿐 아니라 추격하라는 명령마저 너무도 또렷이 들렸다. 〈헤이워드〉가 그들이 들은 말의 뜻을 짤막하게 설명하자, 〈호크아이〉가 멈춰 서서, 빠른 결단과 큰 단호함으로 말했다.\n\n\"우리도 한 차례 쏩시다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그들은 그것을 출격으로 여겨 길을 비키거나, 아니면 증원을 기다릴 거요.\"\n\n그 책략은 잘 구상되었으나, 그 효과는 빗나갔다. 프랑스군이 총소리를 들은 순간, 마치 평지가 사람들로 살아 움직이는 듯하더니, 머스킷 소리가 호수의 기슭에서 숲의 가장 먼 경계까지 그 너른 전역을 따라 절거덕거렸다.\n\n\"우리가 그들의 군대 전체를 끌어들여, 전면적인 공격을 불러오겠소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"앞장서시오, 친구여, 당신 목숨과 우리 목숨을 위해서.\"\n\n정찰병은 따를 마음이 있어 보였으나, 그 황망한 순간에, 그리고 자리를 옮기는 통에, 방향을 잃어버렸다. 그가 어느 쪽 뺨이든 가벼운 바람을 향해 돌려 보았으나 헛수고였으니, 양쪽 다 똑같이 서늘하게 느껴졌다. 이 곤경 속에서, 〈언카스〉가 포탄이 인접한 세 개의 개미집을 가르며 땅을 파 놓은 고랑을 찾아냈다.\n\n\"방향을 일러 다오!\" 〈호크아이〉가 그 방향을 흘끗 잡으려 몸을 굽히며 말하더니, 곧장 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n외침과 욕설, 서로를 부르는 목소리, 그리고 머스킷의 총성이 이제 빠르고 끊임없이, 그리고 보아하니 그들의 사방에서 일었다. 갑자기 강한 빛이 광경을 번쩍 가로지르더니, 안개가 두꺼운 화환을 그리며 위로 솟구쳤고, 여러 문의 대포가 평지를 가로질러 토해 냈으며, 그 굉음이 산의 울부짖는 메아리로부터 묵직하게 되돌아왔다.\n\n\"요새에서 쏘는 것이오!\" 〈호크아이〉가 제 자취 위에서 홱 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"그런데 우리는, 얻어맞은 바보들처럼, 〈마콰〉족의 칼날 바로 밑으로 숲을 향해 돌진하고 있었구려.\"\n\n그들의 실수가 바로잡히는 순간, 일행 모두가 더없는 부지런함으로 그 잘못을 되짚어 갔다. 〈덩컨〉은 기꺼이 〈코라〉의 부축을 〈언카스〉의 팔에 넘겼고, 〈코라〉 또한 기꺼이 그 반가운 도움을 받아들였다. 추격에 달아오르고 성난 자들이 분명 그들의 발자취에 바짝 붙어 있었으니, 매 순간 그들의 파멸은 아닐지라도 사로잡힘이 위협이 되었다.\n\n\"저 악당들에게 자비는 없다!\" 적의 작전을 지휘하는 듯한 한 열렬한 추격자가 외쳤다.\n\n\"굳게 서서, 준비하라, 나의 용감한 60연대여!\" 갑자기 그들 위쪽에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"적이 보일 때까지 기다려라—낮게 쏘아, 비탈을 쓸어 버려라.\"\n\n\"아버지! 아버지!\" 안개 속에서 가슴을 찌르는 외침이 터져 나왔다. \"저예요! 〈앨리스〉! 아버지의 〈엘시〉예요! 살려 주세요, 오! 당신의 딸들을 구해 주세요!\"\n\n\"멈춰라!\" 앞서의 그 목소리가 외쳤으니, 부모의 고뇌가 담긴 그 무시무시한 음성이 숲에까지 닿아 엄숙한 메아리로 되울려 왔다. \"그 애로구나! 신께서 내 아이들을 돌려주셨다! 출격문을 열어라. 들판으로, 60연대여, 들판으로. 방아쇠를 당기지 마라, 내 어린양들을 죽일라! 저 프랑스의 개들을 너희 강철로 몰아내라.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉은 녹슨 돌쩌귀가 삐걱대는 소리를 들었고, 그 소리에 이끌려 그 자리로 내달려, 비탈을 향해 재빠르게 나아가는 검붉은 전사들의 긴 행렬을 만났다. 그는 그들이 자신이 속한 왕립 아메리카 대대임을 알아보았고, 그 선두로 날아가, 곧 요새 앞에서 추격자들의 자취를 남김없이 쓸어 냈다.\n\n한순간, 〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉는 이 뜻밖의 버려짐에 떨며 어쩔 줄 모른 채 서 있었다. 그러나 둘 중 누구도 말할, 아니 생각할 겨를조차 갖기 전에, 거구의 한 장교가—세월과 복무로 머리는 세었으나, 군인다운 위엄의 기풍은 시간에 의해 무너졌다기보다 오히려 부드러워진—안개의 덩어리에서 뛰쳐나와 그들을 제 품에 끌어안았으니, 굵고 뜨거운 눈물이 그의 창백하고 주름진 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 그는 스코틀랜드 특유의 억양으로 외쳤다.\n\"이를 위해 당신께 감사드립니다, 주여! 위험이 어떻게 닥쳐온다 한들, 당신의 종은 이제 준비가 되었나이다!\"\n\n* 60연대: 본문의 \"60ths\"는 영국군 제60보병연대, 곧 왕립 아메리카 연대를 가리킨다.\n\"그럼 우리 들어가, 그가 전할 말을 들어 보세.\n\n프랑스인이 그 일을 한마디라도 꺼내기 전에,\n나는 망설임 없이 알아맞혀 말할 수 있으리니.\"\n\n『헨리 5세』\n\n이어지는 며칠은 포위 공격의 궁핍과 소란, 위험 속에 지나갔으니, 그 공격은 〈먼로〉가 막아 낼 변변한 수단을 갖지 못한 한 세력에 의해 맹렬히 밀어붙여졌다. 마치 〈웨브〉가, 〈허드슨〉강 기슭에 잠든 듯 머무는 그의 군대와 더불어, 동포들이 처한 궁지를 까맣게 잊은 듯했다. 〈몽칼름〉은 육로 운반로의 숲을 미개인들로 가득 채웠고, 그들이 내지르는 비명과 함성 하나하나가 〈영국〉군 진지에 울려 퍼져, 이미 위험을 지나치게 부풀려 헤아리는 데 너무도 기울어 있던 자들의 마음을 얼어붙게 했다.\n\n그러나 포위된 자들은 그렇지 않았다. 지휘관들의 말에 고무되고 그 본보기에 자극받아, 그들은 용기를 되찾았고, 자기네 지휘관의 준엄한 성품에 부끄럽지 않은 열의로 옛 명성을 지켜 냈다. 적을 맞으러 황야를 행군하는 노고로 만족했다는 듯이, 프랑스 장군은 정평 난 솜씨를 지녔으면서도 인접한 산들을 점령하기를 게을리했으니, 그곳에서라면 포위된 자들을 무사히 몰살할 수 있었을 것이요, 이 고장의 보다 근대적인 전쟁이었다면 단 한 시간도 그냥 두지 않았을 곳이었다. 고지에 대한 이런 종류의 경멸, 아니 오히려 그곳을 오르는 노고에 대한 두려움은, 그 시대 전쟁의 고질적인 약점이라 할 만했다. 그것은 인디언 전투의 단순함에서 비롯되었으니, 그 싸움의 성격과 삼림의 빽빽함 탓에 요새는 드물었고 대포는 거의 쓸모가 없었다. 이런 관습이 빚어낸 부주의는 독립 전쟁에까지 이어져, 〈합중국〉이 〈타이콘더로가〉라는 중요한 요새를 잃고, 〈버고인〉의 군대가 당시 이 나라의 한복판으로 들어설 길을 열어 주는 결과를 낳았다. 우리는 이 무지를, 혹은 그것을 어떻게 부르든 그 맹목을 놀라움으로 돌아보거니와, 〈디파이언스산〉의 험준함이 그토록 크게 과장된 것처럼 어느 고지를 소홀히 한다면 오늘날에는 그 발치에 진지를 설계한 공병의 명성에든, 그것을 지킬 운명을 진 장군의 명성에든 치명적임을 우리는 알고 있다.\n\n이제 사두마차 행렬에 실려, 식견이나 건강이나 즐거움을 좇아, 우리가 묘사하려 애쓴 이 풍경 사이를 굴러가는 여행객이나 요양객이나 자연미의 애호가, 혹은 위태로운 결말에 제 정치 생명을 걸 만큼 대담했던 어느 정치가의 치세 아래 솟아난 그 인공의 수로를 따라 목적지로 유유히 떠가는 이는, 자기 선조들이 그 언덕들을 그만한 수월함으로 가로지르거나 같은 물살과 그만큼 쉽게 씨름했으리라고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 무거운 대포 한 문을 옮기는 일은 흔히 거둔 승리 하나와 맞먹는 것으로 여겨졌으니, 다만 길의 험난함이 그것을 필수의 동반물인 탄약과 너무 멀리 갈라놓아, 결국 거추장스러운 무쇠 관 한 개로 만들어 버리지 않았을 경우에 한해서였다.\n\n이런 형편의 폐해는 이제 〈윌리엄 헨리〉를 지키는 단호한 스코틀랜드인의 운명을 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 그의 적수는 산들을 소홀히 했으되, 평지에는 분별 있게 포대를 배치했고, 그것을 활기차고 능란하게 부리도록 했다. 이 공격에 맞서, 포위된 자들이 내놓을 수 있는 것이라곤 황야 속 한 요새의 불완전하고 서두른 채비뿐이었다.\n\n포위 닷새째, 그가 직접 그 일에 가담한 지 나흘째 되던 오후, 〈헤이워드〉 소령은 방금 울린 휴전을 틈타, 호수에서 불어오는 서늘한 바람을 쐬고 포위전의 진척을 둘러보려고 물가 보루 가운데 하나의 흉벽으로 올라갔다. 둔덕을 거니는 외로운 보초 하나를 빼면 그는 혼자였으니, 포병들 역시 힘든 임무가 잠시 멈춘 틈을 서둘러 누리고 있었기 때문이다. 저녁은 더없이 고요했고, 맑은 물에서 불어오는 산들바람은 신선하고 마음을 달래 주었다. 마치 포성과 포탄이 떨어지는 소리가 그친 것과 더불어, 자연도 그 가장 온화하고 매혹적인 모습을 띠기 위해 그 순간을 붙잡은 듯했다. 해는 이 풍경 위로 작별의 영광을 쏟아 부었으니, 이 기후와 계절 특유의 사나운 햇살이 안기는 압박은 없었다. 산들은 푸르고 싱그럽고 사랑스러워 보였으니, 더 부드러운 빛으로 누그러지거나, 산과 해 사이를 떠도는 엷은 안개에 그늘로 부드러워졌다. 무수한 섬들이 〈호리컨〉의 품에 쉬고 있었으니, 더러는 낮게 가라앉아 마치 물속에 묻힌 듯했고, 더러는 푸른 벨벳의 작은 둔덕이 되어 수면 위에 떠 있는 듯 보였다. 그 사이로 포위군의 어부들은 한가로이 작은 배를 저었고, 혹은 유리 같은 거울 위에 멈춰 떠서 조용히 제 일을 좇았다.\n\n이 풍경은 생기에 차 있으면서도 고요했다. 자연에 속한 모든 것은 감미롭거나 단순히 장엄했고, 인간의 기분과 움직임에 달린 부분은 활기차고 흥겨웠다.\n\n티 없이 흰 작은 깃발 두 개가 펄럭이고 있었으니, 하나는 요새의 돌출한 모서리에, 다른 하나는 포위군의 전진 포대에 꽂혀, 교전자들의 행위뿐 아니라, 보아하니 그 적의까지도 멈추게 한 휴전의 표상이었다.\n\n그 뒤로는 다시, 〈영국〉과 〈프랑스〉의 맞선 군기가 비단 자락으로 묵직하게 펴졌다 접혔다 하며 나부꼈다.\n\n밝고 분별없는 젊은 프랑스인 백여 명이 요새의 시무룩하나 잠잠한 대포에 위험천만하게 가까운 자갈밭 해변으로 그물을 끌어 올리는 동안, 동쪽 산이 그들의 놀이에 따른 우렁찬 함성과 흥겨운 떠들썩함을 되돌려 보냈다. 더러는 호수의 물놀이를 즐기려 앞다투어 달려갔고, 더러는 자기 민족 특유의 가만있지 못하는 호기심으로 이웃한 언덕들을 힘겹게 오르고 있었다. 그러나 이 모든 놀이와 일에 대해, 포위된 자들을 지켜보던 적군이나 포위된 자들 자신이나, 그저 한가로이 공감하며 바라보는 구경꾼일 따름이었다. 여기저기서 보초 하나가 노래를 부르거나 춤판에 끼어들기도 했으니, 그것이 숲속 굴에서 거뭇한 미개인들을 그들 주위로 끌어내었다. 요컨대, 모든 것이 피비린내 나고 보복에 찬 전쟁의 위험과 노고에서 훔쳐 낸 한때라기보다는, 오히려 어느 즐거운 하루의 모습을 띠고 있었다.\n\n〈던컨〉은 생각에 잠긴 자세로 잠시 이 풍경을 바라보며 서 있었으니, 다가오는 발소리에 그의 눈이 앞서 말한 출격문 앞의 비탈로 향했다. 그는 보루의 한 모서리로 걸어가, 한 프랑스 장교의 감시 아래 척후가 요새 본체로 다가오는 것을 보았다. 〈호크아이〉의 얼굴은 초췌하고 근심에 절었으며, 그 태도는 풀이 죽어 있었으니, 마치 적의 손아귀에 떨어진 것에 더없이 깊은 치욕을 느끼는 듯했다. 그는 그가 아끼는 무기도 없이, 두 팔마저 사슴 가죽으로 만든 가죽끈에 등 뒤로 묶여 있었다. 항복 권고의 사자를 호위하는 깃발의 도착이 근래 너무도 잦았던 터라, 〈헤이워드〉가 이 무리에 처음 무심한 눈길을 던졌을 때는 비슷한 임무를 띤 또 다른 적군 장교를 보리라 여겼다. 그러나 키 큰 풍채와, 풀 죽은 와중에도 여전히 다부진 그 숲사람 친구의 이목구비를 알아본 순간, 그는 놀라 움찔하더니, 보루에서 진지 안으로 내려가려고 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n그러나 다른 목소리들이 그의 주의를 끌어, 잠시 그 의도를 잊게 했다. 둔덕 안쪽 모서리에서 그는 자매를 만났으니, 그들 또한 그처럼 바람을 쐬고 갇힌 데서 벗어나려 흉벽을 따라 거닐던 참이었다. 그가 그들의 안전을 확인하려고만 평원에서 그들을 버리고 떠났던 그 고통스러운 순간 이래로 그들은 만난 적이 없었다. 그는 근심에 닳고 피로에 지친 채 그들과 헤어졌으나, 이제 그들은 새로워지고 활짝 피었음을 보았으니, 다만 겁먹고 불안해하는 기색이었다. 이런 유인이 있는 터라, 젊은이가 그들에게 말을 건네려고 한동안 다른 일들을 잊었다 한들 놀랄 일은 아니다. 그러나 천진하고 앳된 〈앨리스〉의 목소리가 그를 앞질렀다.\n\n\"아! 이 게으름뱅이! 이 비겁한 기사님! 바로 결투장에서 제 아가씨들을 버리고 가신 분!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"우리는 여기서 며칠을, 아니 몇 세월을, 당신이 우리 발치에 와서 그 겁쟁이 같은 배신을, 아니 차라리 줄행랑이라 해야겠네요 — 정말이지 당신은, 우리의 훌륭한 친구 척후 양반 말마따나, 그 어떤 화살 맞은 사슴도 따라잡지 못할 모양으로 달아나셨으니까요! — 그 줄행랑의 용서와 망각을 빌어 주기를 기다려 왔답니다!\"\n\n\"〈앨리스〉가 말하려는 건 우리의 감사와 축복이라는 걸 아시지요.\" 더 진중하고 사려 깊은 〈코라〉가 덧붙였다. \"사실, 우리는 좀 의아했어요. 딸들의 감사에 아버지의 사례까지 보탤 수 있는 곳에서 어찌 그리도 한사코 모습을 보이지 않으셨는지 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아버님께서 직접 말씀해 주실 수 있을 겁니다. 비록 두 분 곁을 떠나 있었으되, 제가 두 분의 안전을 아주 잊었던 것은 아니라는 걸요.\" 젊은이가 대답했다. \"저쪽 오두막 마을을,\" 그가 이웃한 참호 진지를 가리키며 말했다. \"차지하려고 치열하게 다투어 왔으니, 그곳을 쥔 자가 이 요새와 그 안의 것을 손에 넣게 마련입니다. 우리가 헤어진 뒤로 제 낮과 밤은 모두 그곳에서 보냈지요. 의무가 저를 그리로 부른다고 여겼으니까요. 하지만,\" 그가 숨기려 했으나 끝내 숨기지 못한 분한 기색으로 덧붙였다. \"그때 군인의 도리라 믿었던 것이 그렇게 해석될 수 있음을 알았더라면, 그 이유들의 목록에 부끄러움까지 더해졌을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"〈헤이워드〉! — 〈던컨〉!\" 〈앨리스〉가 외치며, 반쯤 돌린 그의 얼굴을 읽으려고 몸을 앞으로 기울이니, 황금빛 머리카락 한 가닥이 발그레한 뺨에 드리워, 눈에 솟은 눈물을 거의 가려 버렸다. \"이 부질없는 제 혀가 당신을 아프게 했다고 여겼다면, 영영 입을 다물었을 거예요. 〈코라〉가, 〈코라〉만 말하려 든다면, 우리가 당신의 노고를 얼마나 정당하게 귀히 여겼는지, 우리의 감사가 얼마나 깊은지 — 아니, 얼마나 뜨거운지라고 할 뻔했네요 — 말해 줄 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"〈코라〉가 이 말의 진실을 증언해 주겠소?\" 〈던컨〉이, 거리낌 없는 기쁨의 미소가 그 얼굴에서 먹구름을 몰아내도록 두며 외쳤다. \"우리의 진중한 누님은 뭐라 하시려는지? 군인의 의무에서 기사의 태만을 변명해 줄 핑계를 찾아 주시려나?\"\n\n〈코라〉는 곧장 대답하지 않고, 마치 〈호리컨〉의 수면을 바라보듯 얼굴을 물 쪽으로 돌렸다. 그녀가 마침내 그 검은 눈을 젊은이에게 돌렸을 때, 그 눈은 여전히 고뇌의 빛으로 가득 차, 그의 마음에서 다정한 염려 말고는 모든 생각을 단번에 몰아내었다.\n\n\"몸이 편치 않으시군요, 친애하는 〈먼로〉 양!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"우리가 농담을 주고받는 동안 당신은 괴로워하고 있었군요.\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 그녀가 여인다운 절제로 그가 내민 부축을 사양하며 대답했다. \"이 꾸밈없으나 열정에 찬 애호가처럼,\" 그녀가 누이의 팔에 가볍게, 그러나 다정하게 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다. \"제가 인생이라는 그림의 햇살 비치는 면을 보지 못하는 것은, 경험이 치르는 대가요, 어쩌면 제 천성의 불행이겠지요. 보세요,\" 그녀가 의무감으로 약함을 떨쳐 내기로 작정한 듯 말을 이었다. \"둘러보세요, 〈헤이워드〉 소령님, 그리고 말해 주세요. 명예와 무공을 더없는 행복으로 여기는 군인의 딸에게, 이것이 어떤 전망인지를요.\"\n\n\"그 무엇도 그분이 어쩔 수 없었던 상황으로 인해 더럽혀져서는 안 되며, 그러지도 않을 겁니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 따뜻이 대답했다. \"하지만 당신 말씀이 제 임무를 일깨우는군요. 저는 이제 당신의 용맹한 아버님께 가서, 방어에 마지막으로 중대한 문제들에 관한 그분의 결단을 들으렵니다. 어떤 운명 속에서도 신의 가호가 있기를, 고귀한 — 〈코라〉 — 그렇게 불러도 되고, 또 불러야겠지요.\" 그녀가 선뜻 손을 내밀었으나, 입술은 떨렸고 뺨은 차츰 잿빛으로 창백해졌다. \"어떤 운명 속에서도, 당신은 당신 성(性)의 자랑이요 명예가 되리라는 걸 압니다. 〈앨리스〉, 안녕히\" — 그의 어조가 찬미에서 다정함으로 바뀌었다 — \"안녕히, 〈앨리스〉. 우리 곧 다시 만납시다. 그것도 정복자로서, 기쁨 속에서, 그러리라 믿어요!\"\n\n어느 쪽의 대답도 기다리지 않고, 젊은이는 보루의 풀 덮인 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 연병장을 가로질러 재빨리 움직이더니, 이내 그들의 아버지 앞에 섰다. 〈던컨〉이 들어섰을 때 〈먼로〉는 어지러운 기색으로 거대한 걸음을 내디디며 그 비좁은 방을 거닐고 있었다.\n\n\"내 바람을 미리 헤아렸군, 〈헤이워드〉 소령.\" 그가 말했다. \"마침 자네에게 이 부탁을 청하려던 참이었네.\"\n\n\"각하, 제가 그토록 따뜻이 추천한 사자가 프랑스군의 감시 아래 돌아온 것을 보니 안타깝습니다! 그자의 충성을 의심할 까닭은 없겠지요?\"\n\n\"〈긴 소총〉의 충성은 내가 익히 아는 바일세.\" 〈먼로〉가 대답했다. \"의심할 여지가 없지. 다만 그자의 평소 행운이 끝내 떨어진 모양이야. 〈몽칼름〉이 그를 붙잡았고, 제 나라 특유의 저 빌어먹을 정중함으로, '내가 그자를 얼마나 아끼는지 알기에, 차마 붙들어 둘 생각을 할 수 없었노라'는 구슬픈 사연과 함께 그를 들여보냈네. 한 사내에게 제 불행을 일러 주는, 참으로 예수회다운 방식이지, 〈던컨 헤이워드〉 소령!\"\n\n\"하지만 장군과 그 원군은요?\"\n\n\"들어올 때 남쪽을 보았는가, 그들이 보이지 않던가?\" 노병이 쓰디쓰게 웃으며 말했다. \"허! 허! 자네는 성급한 애송이야, 소령, 그 양반들에게 행군할 여유도 못 주는군!\"\n\n\"그럼 오고 있다는 말씀입니까? 척후가 그렇게 말했습니까?\"\n\n\"언제? 또 어느 길로? 그 멍청이가 그건 말해 주지 않았으니. 편지가 한 통 있는 모양인데, 그게 이 일에서 유일하게 흡족한 부분이야. 자네가 말하는 〈몽칼름〉 후작의 그 으레 갖추는 예의로 말하면 — 내 장담하건대, 〈던컨〉, 〈로디언〉의 사내라면 그런 후작 자리 열두 개는 사고도 남을 걸세 — 하지만 편지의 소식이 나쁜 것이었다면, 이 프랑스 양반의 그 점잖음이 분명 그걸 우리에게 알리도록 했을 게야.\"\n\n\"그럼 사자는 풀어 주면서 편지는 쥐고 있다는 말씀이군요!\"\n\n\"그렇다네, 그가 바로 그러는데, 죄다 자네가 말하는 그 '봉오미' 때문이지. 사실이 밝혀진다면 내 감히 말하겠네만, 그자의 할아비가 그 고상한 춤의 기예를 가르쳤을 게야.\"\n\n\"하지만 척후는 뭐라고 합니까? 그에겐 눈도 귀도 혀도 있으니, 입으로는 무슨 보고를 합니까?\"\n\n\"오! 소령, 그자는 타고난 기관이 부족하진 않으니, 보고 들은 바를 죄다 거리낌 없이 말하지. 그 전부가 이렇다네. 〈허드슨〉강 기슭에 폐하의 요새가 하나 있는데, 〈요크〉의 자애로운 전하를 기려 〈에드워드〉라 불리지, 알겠나. 그리고 그런 진지가 응당 그래야 하듯, 무장한 자들로 잘 채워져 있다네.\"\n\n\"하지만 아무 움직임도, 우리를 구하러 진격할 어떤 기색도 없었답니까?\"\n\n\"아침 사열과 저녁 사열은 있었지. 그리고 그 식민지 멍청이 하나가 — 알겠나, 〈던컨〉, 자네도 반은 스코틀랜드인 아닌가 — 그자들 중 하나가 죽 위에다 화약을 흘렸을 때, 그게 숯불에 닿으면 그냥 타 버리더라네!\" 그러더니 그 쓰디쓰고 빈정대는 태도를 별안간 더 진중하고 사려 깊은 것으로 바꾸며 그가 말을 이었다. \"그래도 그 편지 안엔 알아 두면 좋을 무언가가 있을 법하고, 또 분명 있을 게야!\"\n\n\"우리의 결정은 신속해야 합니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 이 기분의 변화를 반갑게 틈타, 이 만남의 더 중요한 목적을 밀어붙이며 말했다. \"각하, 진지가 더 오래 버티지 못하리라는 것을 숨길 수 없습니다. 그리고 안타깝게도 덧붙이건대, 요새 안의 사정도 나을 게 없어, 대포의 절반 이상이 터져 버렸습니다.\"\n\n\"어찌 안 그렇겠나? 더러는 호수 바닥에서 건져 올린 것이고, 더러는 이 고장이 발견된 이래로 숲속에서 녹슬어 온 것이며, 더러는 애당초 대포도 아니었지 — 그저 사략선원의 장난감이었어! 자네는 생각하나, 〈영국〉에서 삼천 마일이나 떨어진 황야 한복판에 〈울위치〉 병기창을 둘 수 있다고!\"\n\n\"성벽은 우리 귀 언저리에서 허물어지고, 식량은 동나기 시작했습니다.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 이 새로운 분노의 폭발에 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다. \"병사들마저 불만과 동요의 기색을 보입니다.\"\n\n\"〈헤이워드〉 소령,\" 〈먼로〉가 제 나이와 더 높은 지위의 위엄을 갖추고 젊은 동료에게 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 반세기를 폐하께 봉직하며 이 백발을 얻고서도, 자네가 하는 말과 우리 처지의 절박함을 모른다면, 그 모두가 헛수고였을 게야. 그래도 폐하의 군기의 명예에 다해야 할 도리가 있고, 우리 자신에게도 얼마간 다해야 할 바가 있지. 원군의 희망이 있는 한, 이 요새를 나는 지킬 테니, 비록 그 일을 호숫가에서 주워 모은 조약돌로 해야 한다 해도 말일세. 그러니 우리가 바라는 것은 바로 그 편지를 보는 것이야, 〈라우든〉 백작이 자기 대리로 우리 가운데 남겨 둔 그 사내의 의중을 알 수 있도록 말이지.\"\n\n\"그 일에 제가 도움이 될 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"있고말고, 소령. 〈몽칼름〉 후작이 다른 호의에 더하여, 진지와 자기 진영 사이에서 나와 직접 회견하자고 청해 왔네. 그가 말하기로는, 얼마간 추가 정보를 전하기 위해서라더군. 한데 그를 만나려고 지나친 열의를 보이는 것은 현명치 못하다 싶으니, 자네를, 지위 있는 장교를 내 대리로 쓰려 하네. 〈스코틀랜드〉의 신사 하나가 이 세상 그 어느 나라 태생에게 예의에서 뒤졌다는 말을 듣게 하는 것은, 〈스코틀랜드〉의 명예에 어울리지 않을 테니 말일세.\"\n\n각 나라의 예절을 견주어 따지는 군더더기 같은 일에 끼어들지 않은 채, 〈던컨〉은 다가오는 회견에서 노병의 자리를 대신하기로 흔쾌히 응낙했다. 이제 길고 은밀한 대화가 이어졌으니, 그동안 젊은이는 그의 지휘관의 경험과 타고난 예리함에서 제 임무에 대한 얼마간의 통찰을 더 얻었고, 그런 뒤 그는 작별을 고했다.\n\n〈던컨〉은 요새 사령관의 대리로서만 행할 수 있었으므로, 맞선 두 군의 수장 사이의 회견에 따랐을 의례는 물론 생략되었다. 휴전은 아직 이어지고 있었고, 북소리의 울림과 두드림과 함께, 작은 흰 깃발의 호위를 받으며, 〈던컨〉은 지시가 끝난 지 십 분 만에 출격문을 나섰다. 그는 앞선 프랑스 장교에게 으레 갖추는 격식으로 맞아들여졌고, 곧장 〈프랑스〉 군을 이끄는 그 이름난 군인의 먼 군막으로 인도되었다.\n\n적의 장군은 휘하의 주요 장교들과, 그를 따라 전장에 온 거뭇한 토착 추장들의 무리에 둘러싸여 그 앳된 사자를 맞았으니, 추장들은 저마다 제 부족의 전사들을 거느리고 있었다. 〈헤이워드〉는 그 거뭇한 무리에 재빨리 눈길을 던지다가, 그 교활한 미개인의 표정을 특징짓는 차분하나 시무룩한 주의로 자기를 바라보는 〈마구아〉의 악의에 찬 얼굴을 보고는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 놀라움의 작은 외마디가 젊은이의 입술에서 새어 나오기까지 했으나, 그는 곧 제 임무와 자기가 서 있는 자리를 떠올리고는, 온갖 감정의 기색을 억누른 채, 이미 그를 맞으려 한 걸음 나선 적장 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n우리가 글로 쓰는 이 시기에, 〈몽칼름〉 후작은 인생의 한창때에, 덧붙이자면 운세의 절정에 있었다. 그러나 그 부러운 처지에서도 그는 상냥했으며, 불과 두 해 뒤 〈에이브러햄〉 평원에서 제 목숨을 던지게 한 그 기사도적 용기에 못지않게, 예법에 기울이는 정성으로도 두드러졌다. 〈던컨〉은 〈마구아〉의 악의에 찬 표정에서 눈을 돌려, 프랑스 장군의 미소 짓는 세련된 이목구비와 고귀한 무인의 풍모에 흐뭇이 눈길을 머물게 했다.\n\n\"무슈,\" 후작이 말했다. \"참으로 기쁩니다, 아 — 흠! — 그 통역은 어디 있는가?\"\n\n\"무슈, 그것은 필요 없을 듯합니다.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 겸손히 대답했다. \"제가 프랑스어를 조금 합니다.\"\n\n\"아! 그거 참 다행이오.\" 〈몽칼름〉이 〈던컨〉의 팔을 친근하게 붙들고 군막 깊숙이, 귀가 닿지 않을 만큼 떨어진 곳으로 이끌며 말했다. \"나는 저 사기꾼 같은 자들이 질색이오. 그들과 함께 있으면 도무지 제 발 디딘 데가 어딘지 알 수가 없으니. 자, 그럼! 무슈,\" 그가 여전히 프랑스어로 말을 이었다. \"비록 당신네 사령관을 맞았다면 영광이었겠으나, 그가 당신처럼 이토록 출중하고, 또 분명 이토록 호감 가는 장교를 쓰기로 한 것이 나는 무척 기쁘오.\"\n\n〈던컨〉은 그 어떤 술책에도 홀려 제 군주의 이익을 잊지 않으리라는 더없이 영웅적인 결심에도 불구하고, 그 찬사에 흐뭇해하며 깊이 머리를 숙였다. 그리고 〈몽칼름〉은, 마치 생각을 가다듬는 듯 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말을 이었다.\n\n\"당신네 사령관은 용감한 사내요, 내 공격을 물리치기에 능히 알맞은 인물이지. 하지만 무슈, 이제는 용기보다 인도(人道)에 더 귀를 기울이기 시작할 때가 아니겠소? 어느 쪽이든 다른 쪽 못지않게 영웅의 증표가 되는 법이니.\"\n\n\"우리는 그 두 자질을 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것으로 여깁니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"다만, 각하의 기세에서 그 하나를 자극할 온갖 동기를 보긴 합니다만, 아직은 다른 하나를 발휘할 특별한 이유를 보지 못하겠습니다.\"\n\n이번에는 〈몽칼름〉이 살짝 머리를 숙였으나, 그것은 아첨의 말을 기억하기에는 너무 노련한 사내의 태도였다. 잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"내 망원경이 나를 속였을지도 모르고, 당신네 진지가 내 짐작보다 우리 대포를 더 잘 견뎌 내는 것일지도 모르오. 우리 병력을 아시오?\"\n\n\"전해 듣는 바가 제각각입니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 무심히 말했다. \"하지만 가장 많이 잡아도 이만을 넘지는 않더군요.\"\n\n프랑스인은 입술을 깨물고, 마치 그의 속을 읽으려는 듯 날카로운 눈길을 상대에게 고정했다. 그러더니 제 특유의 민첩함으로, 자기 군대를 곱절로 부풀린 그 셈을 사실로 인정하는 듯이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"우리 군인들의 경계심에 대한 어줍은 찬사로군요, 무슈. 우리가 무슨 짓을 해도 도무지 그 수를 감출 수 없다니 말이오. 만일 어디서든 그게 가능하다면, 이 숲속에서라면 성공하리라 믿었을 텐데. 비록 당신은 인도의 부름에 귀 기울이기엔 너무 이르다 여기지만,\" 그가 짓궂게 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"당신처럼 젊은 이라면 정중함만은 잊지 않았으리라 믿어도 되겠지요. 듣자 하니, 사령관의 따님들이 포위가 시작된 뒤 요새로 들어왔다지요?\"\n\n\"사실입니다, 무슈. 하지만 그분들은 우리의 노력을 약화시키기는커녕, 그 꿋꿋함으로 우리에게 용기의 본보기를 보여 주십니다. 〈몽칼름〉 씨처럼 노련한 군인을 물리치는 데 결의만 있으면 된다면, 저는 기꺼이 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 방어를 그 두 숙녀 중 손위 분께 맡기겠습니다.\"\n\n\"우리에게는 〈살리카〉 법에 현명한 율법이 하나 있소. '〈프랑스〉의 왕관은 결코 창을 실패로 떨어뜨리지 않으리라.'\" 〈몽칼름〉이 무뚝뚝하게, 그리고 약간의 오만을 띠고 말했으나, 곧 예전의 솔직하고 편안한 태도로 덧붙였다. \"모든 고귀한 자질이 핏줄로 이어지는 만큼, 당신 말을 쉽게 믿겠소. 다만 앞서 말했듯, 용기에도 한계가 있고, 인도를 잊어서는 안 되오. 무슈, 당신이 이곳의 항복을 교섭할 권한을 받고 왔으리라 믿소만?\"\n\n\"각하께서는 우리의 방어가 그 조치가 필요하다고 여기실 만큼 그토록 허약하다 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"방어가 저기 내 붉은 벗들을 노하게 할 만큼 질질 끄는 것을 나는 보고 싶지 않소.\" 〈몽칼름〉이 상대의 물음에는 답하지 않고, 진중하고 주의 깊은 인디언 무리에게 눈길을 던지며 말을 이었다. \"지금도 그들을 전쟁의 관례 안에 묶어 두기가 어렵소.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 잠자코 있었으니, 자기가 그토록 최근에 벗어난 위험들에 대한 고통스러운 기억이 마음에 밀려와, 자기의 온갖 고난을 함께 겪었던 그 무력한 이들의 모습을 떠올렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"저자들은,\" 〈몽칼름〉이 자기가 얻었다고 여긴 우위를 좇으며 말했다. \"뜻이 꺾이면 더없이 무서워지지. 그들의 분노를 억누르기가 얼마나 어려운지는 굳이 말할 필요도 없소. 자, 그럼! 무슈, 우리 조건을 이야기해 볼까요?\"\n\n\"각하께서는 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 힘과 그 수비대의 자원에 대해 잘못 알고 계신 듯합니다!\"\n\n\"나는 〈퀘벡〉 앞에 진을 친 게 아니라, 용맹한 사내 이천삼백이 지키는 흙벽 진지 앞에 진을 친 것이오.\" 간결한 대답이었다.\n\n\"우리 둔덕이 흙으로 된 것은 분명하며, 〈케이프 다이아몬드〉의 바위 위에 앉아 있지도 않습니다. 그러나 그것은 〈디스코〉와 그의 군대에게 그토록 파멸적이었던 그 기슭 위에 서 있습니다. 또한 우리에게서 몇 시간 행군 거리 안에 강력한 병력이 있어, 우리는 그것을 우리 수단의 일부로 셈하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"육칠천 명쯤 되는 군대지요.\" 〈몽칼름〉이 자못 무심한 듯 대답했다. \"그 지휘관이 야전보다는 제 진지 안에 있는 편이 더 안전하다고 현명하게 판단한 자들 말이오.\"\n\n이번에는 〈헤이워드〉가 분해서 입술을 깨물 차례였으니, 상대가 젊은이도 부풀려진 줄 아는 그 병력을 그토록 태연히 언급한 까닭이었다. 둘은 잠시 말없이 생각에 잠겼다가, 〈몽칼름〉이 대화를 다시 시작했으니, 자기 손님의 방문이 오로지 항복 조건을 제안하기 위함이라 믿는 기색이었다. 한편 〈헤이워드〉는 가로챈 편지를 통해 알아낸 사실을 그가 누설하도록, 프랑스 장군에게 갖가지 유인을 던지기 시작했다. 그러나 어느 쪽의 술책도 통하지 않았고, 길고 헛된 회견 끝에 〈던컨〉은 적장의 정중함과 재능에 호의적인 인상을 받은 채 작별을 고했으나, 자기가 알아내러 온 것에 대해서는 도착했을 때와 다름없이 깜깜했다. 〈몽칼름〉은 군막 입구까지 그를 배웅하며, 두 군 사이의 트인 땅에서 즉시 회견하자는 청을 요새 사령관에게 거듭 전했다.\n\n그곳에서 그들은 헤어졌고, 〈던컨〉은 전과 같은 호위를 받으며 프랑스군의 전진 초소로 돌아왔다. 그곳에서 그는 곧장 요새로, 그리고 자기 지휘관의 처소로 나아갔다.\n* 봉오미: 프랑스어 'bonhomie', 곧 사람 좋음, 호인다운 너그러움을 뜻한다.\n\n\"에드거. 싸움을 시작하기 전에, 이 편지를 펼쳐 보십시오.\"\n\n리어 왕\n\n〈헤이워드〉 소령이 〈먼로〉를 찾아갔을 때, 그의 곁에는 두 딸만이 있었다. 〈앨리스〉가 그의 무릎에 앉아 가녀린 손가락으로 노인의 이마에 드리운 백발을 가르고 있었고, 그가 그 장난을 짐짓 못마땅한 듯 찌푸려 보일 때마다, 그 꾸며 낸 노여움을, 주름진 이마에 앵두 같은 입술을 다정히 눌러 달래곤 했다. 〈코라〉는 그들 가까이에 앉아, 차분하고 흐뭇한 구경꾼으로서, 더 어린 동생의 변덕스러운 몸짓을, 〈앨리스〉를 향한 그녀의 사랑을 특징짓는 그 모성 어린 다정함으로 바라보았다. 지나온 위험뿐 아니라 아직도 그들 머리 위에 드리운 위험마저, 이러한 가족의 만남이 주는 위안의 탐닉 속에서 한순간 잊힌 듯했다. 마치 그들은 이 짧은 휴전을 틈타, 가장 순수하고 가장 좋은 애정에 한순간을 바치기로 한 듯했으니, 딸들은 그 두려움을, 노전사는 그 근심을, 그 순간의 안온함 속에 잊고 있었다. 이 광경을, 자신의 도착을 알리려는 조급함에 기별도 없이 들어선 〈던컨〉은, 여러 순간을, 눈에 띄지 않은 채 흐뭇한 구경꾼으로 서서 지켜보았다. 그러나 〈앨리스〉의 재빠르고 춤추는 듯한 눈이 이내 거울에 비친 그의 모습을 흘끗 잡아냈으니, 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 아버지의 무릎에서 벌떡 일어나, 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"〈헤이워드〉 소령님!\"\n\n\"그 친구가 어쨌다는 거냐?\" 아버지가 물었다. \"프랑스인과 이야기를 좀 나누라고 내가 보냈다. 하! 이보게, 자네는 젊고 또 날렵하지! 저리 가거라, 이 말괄량이야. 군인에게는 근심거리가 이미 충분하거늘, 진지를 너 같은 재잘대는 말괄량이로 가득 채우기까지 해야 한단 말이냐!\"\n\n〈앨리스〉는 웃으며 언니를 뒤따랐으니, 언니는 자신들의 존재가 더는 환영받지 못함을 알아채고 곧장 방을 나서는 길을 앞장섰다. 〈먼로〉는 젊은이의 임무 결과를 묻는 대신, 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌리고 머리를 바닥으로 숙인 채, 생각에 잠긴 사람처럼 몇 순간 방 안을 거닐었다. 마침내 그는, 아비의 다정함으로 반짝이는 눈을 들어, 외쳤다.\n\n\"저 애들은 더없이 훌륭한 한 쌍의 딸이지, 〈헤이워드〉, 누구라도 자랑삼을 만한 그런 딸들이야.\"\n\n\"제가 따님들에 대해 어찌 생각하는지는 이제 와 새삼 아실 일이 아니지요, 〈먼로〉 대령님.\"\n\n\"옳아, 이 사람아, 옳은 말일세.\" 조급한 노인이 말을 끊었다. \"자네가 도착하던 날 그 일을 더 속 시원히 털어놓으려 하지 않았던가. 허나 임금의 적들이 잔칫상에 청하지도 않은 손님으로 들이닥칠 판국에, 늙은 군인이 혼례의 축복이며 결혼 농담이나 늘어놓는 것이 온당치 못하다 여겼던 게야! 허나 내가 틀렸네, 〈던컨〉, 이 사람아, 그 점에선 내가 틀렸어. 이제 자네가 하고 싶은 말을 들을 준비가 되었네.\"\n\n\"그리 말씀해 주시니 기쁘기 그지없습니다만, 대령님, 지금 막 〈몽칼름〉에게서 전갈이 왔습니다—\"\n\n\"그 프랑스인과 그 무리는 죄다 악마에게나 가라지, 이 사람아!\" 성마른 노전사가 외쳤다. \"그자는 아직 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 주인이 아니며, 〈웨브〉가 마땅히 그래야 할 사내임을 입증하기만 한다면, 영영 그리되지도 못할 걸세. 아니지, 이 사람아! 하늘에 감사하게도, 우리는 아직 〈먼로〉가 제 집안의 자그마한 대소사를 챙길 겨를도 없을 만큼 쫓기는 처지에 놓이지는 않았네. 자네 어머니는 내 가슴속 벗의 외동딸이었지, 〈던컨〉. 그러니 자네 말을 들어 주겠네, 비록 성 루이의 기사들이 죄다 한 몸이 되어 그 프랑스 성인을 앞세우고 출격문에 몰려와, 황송하옵게도 한마디 아뢰겠다고 간청한다 한들 말일세. 설탕통으로 살 수 있는 기사 작위라니, 참으로 그럴듯한 기사 신분이지! 게다가 그 푼돈짜리 후작 작위들이라니! 위엄과 유서로 따지면 엉겅퀴 훈장이야말로 진정한 기사도의 '네모 메 임푸네 라케시트'이지! 자네에게도 그 작위에 오른 선조들이 있었네, 〈던컨〉, 그들은 스코틀랜드 귀족들의 자랑이었지.\"\n* 네모 메 임푸네 라케시트: \"누구도 나를 건드리고 무사하지 못하리라\"라는 뜻의 스코틀랜드 왕가 좌우명.\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 자신의 상관이 프랑스 장군의 전갈을 향한 경멸을 드러내며 짓궂은 즐거움을 누리고 있음을 알아챘으니, 곧 사그라들 줄 아는 그 노여움을 맞춰 주는 편이 낫겠다 싶었다. 그리하여 그는 그런 주제에 대해 짐짓 꾸며 낼 수 있는 한껏의 무심함으로 대답했다.\n\n\"제 청은, 아시다시피 대령님, 외람되게도 대령님의 아들이 되는 영광에까지 미친 것이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 이 사람아, 자네는 제 뜻을 아주 명확히 전할 만한 말을 찾아냈었지. 허나 한 가지 묻겠네, 이 사람아, 그 애에게도 그만큼 분명히 했는가?\"\n\n\"맹세코, 아닙니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 뜨겁게 외쳤다. \"그런 목적으로 제 처지를 이용했다면, 위탁받은 신뢰를 저버린 셈이 되었겠지요.\"\n\n\"자네 생각은 신사다운 것일세, 〈헤이워드〉 소령, 그 자리에선 더없이 마땅한 것이지. 허나 〈코라 먼로〉는 너무도 분별 있고, 너무도 고매하고 닦인 마음을 지닌 처녀라, 아비의 보호조차 필요로 하지 않는 아이일세.\"\n\"〈코라〉라니요!\"\n\n\"그래—〈코라〉 말일세! 우리는 〈먼로〉 양에게 자네가 품은 뜻을 이야기하는 것 아니던가, 이 사람아?\"\n\n\"저는—저는—제가 그분 이름을 언급한 적이 있다고는 미처 생각지 못했습니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 말을 더듬으며 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 대체 누구와 혼인하고자 내 승낙을 바란 것인가, 〈헤이워드〉 소령?\" 노전사가, 상한 감정의 위엄에 몸을 곧추세우며 물었다.\n\n\"대령님께는 또 한 분, 그에 못지않게 사랑스러운 따님이 계십니다.\"\n\n\"〈앨리스〉라고!\" 아버지가, 방금 〈던컨〉이 언니의 이름을 되뇌었을 때와 똑같은 놀라움에 휩싸여 외쳤다.\n\n\"제 바람은 그쪽을 향해 있었습니다, 대령님.\"\n\n젊은이는 자신이 자아낸 그 뜻밖의 비범한 반응의 결말을 말없이 기다렸다. 여러 순간 〈먼로〉는 길고 빠른 보폭으로 방 안을 거닐었으니, 그 굳은 얼굴이 경련하듯 일그러졌고, 모든 정신이 제 마음속 상념에 온통 사로잡힌 듯했다. 마침내 그는 〈헤이워드〉의 정면에 멈추어 서서, 자신의 두 눈을 상대의 두 눈에 못 박은 채, 격하게 떨리는 입술로 말했다.\n\n\"〈던컨 헤이워드〉, 나는 자네 핏줄에 그 피가 흐르는 사람을 생각하여 자네를 사랑해 왔네. 나는 자네 자신의 훌륭한 자질로 인해 자네를 사랑해 왔고, 또 자네가 내 자식의 행복에 보탬이 되리라 여겼기에 자네를 사랑해 왔네. 허나 내가 그토록 우려하는 바가 사실임을 알게 된다면, 이 모든 사랑은 증오로 변하고 말 걸세.\"\n\n\"제 어떤 행동이나 생각도 그런 변화로 이어지지 않게 하소서, 하느님!\" 젊은이가 외쳤으니, 그의 눈은 마주친 꿰뚫는 시선 아래서도 결코 움츠러들지 않았다. 자신의 가슴속에 감추어진 그 감정을 상대가 이해할 수 없다는 점에는 마음 쓰지 않은 채, 〈먼로〉는 자신이 마주한 변함없는 얼굴에 마음이 누그러지도록 내버려 두었으니, 눈에 띄게 부드러워진 목소리로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자네는 내 아들이 되겠다 하지만, 자네가 아버지라 부르고자 하는 사내의 내력은 알지 못하네. 앉게, 젊은이, 내 화상 입은 가슴의 상처를, 알맞을 만큼 짧은 말로 자네에게 펼쳐 보이겠네.\"\n\n이때쯤이면 〈몽칼름〉의 전갈은, 그것을 받아야 할 귀의 임자에게나 그것을 가져온 자에게나 똑같이 잊혀 있었다. 두 사람은 저마다 의자를 끌어다 앉았고, 노전사가 몇 순간 제 상념과, 보아하니 슬픔에 잠긴 채 마주하는 동안, 젊은이는 공손한 경청의 표정과 자세로 제 조바심을 억눌렀다. 마침내 노전사가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"〈헤이워드〉 소령, 자네는 이미 내 가문이 유서 깊고도 명예로운 집안임을 알고 있을 걸세.\" 스코틀랜드인이 말을 시작했다. \"비록 그 신분에 걸맞을 만큼의 재산을 두루 갖추지는 못했을지라도 말이지. 내가 〈앨리스 그레이엄〉에게 언약을 맹세했을 때, 나는 아마 지금의 자네와 같은 처지였을 걸세. 그녀는 얼마간 재산을 지닌 이웃 영주의 외동딸이었네. 허나 이 혼사는 그녀 아비의 마음에 들지 않았으니, 내 가난뿐 아니라 여러 까닭에서였지. 그리하여 나는 정직한 사내가 마땅히 할 바를 했네—처녀에게 그 언약을 돌려주고, 임금을 섬기고자 그 고장을 떠난 게야. 나는 여러 고장을 보았고, 갖가지 땅에서 많은 피를 흘렸으니, 마침내 임무가 나를 서인도제도로 불렀을 때였지. 그곳에서 나는 한 여인과 인연을 맺을 운명이었고, 그 여인은 때가 되어 내 아내가, 그리고 〈코라〉의 어미가 되었네. 그녀는 그 섬들의 한 신사가, 한 부인에게서 얻은 딸이었으니, 그 부인의 불행이라면, 굳이 그리 말하자면,\" 노인이 자랑스레 말했다. \"사치를 누리는 자들의 필요에 부응하고자 그토록 비천하게 노예로 부려지는 저 불운한 부류에서, 먼 핏줄로 비롯되었다는 것이었네. 그렇다네, 이 사람아, 그것은 스코틀랜드가 외래의 장사치 민족과 어울리지 않는 결합을 맺어 짊어진 저주일세. 허나 그자들 가운데 내 자식을 비방할 만큼 대담한 사내를 내가 찾아낸다면, 그자는 아비의 노여움의 무게를 느끼게 될 걸세! 하! 〈헤이워드〉 소령, 자네 자신이 남부에서 태어나지 않았는가, 그곳에선 이 불운한 사람들을 자네들보다 열등한 인종으로 여기지.\"\n\n\"참으로 불운하게도 사실입니다, 대령님.\" 〈던컨〉이, 더는 두 눈이 당혹감에 바닥으로 가라앉는 것을 막지 못한 채 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 자네는 그것을 내 자식에게 흠으로 씌우는 게로군! 자네는 〈헤이워드〉 가문의 피를, 그토록 천한 핏줄과—제아무리 사랑스럽고 정숙한들—섞기를 멸시하는 게야?\" 시샘에 찬 아비가 사납게 물었다.\n\n\"제 이성에 그토록 어울리지 않는 편견으로부터 저를 지켜 주소서, 하늘이여!\" 〈던컨〉이 대답했으니, 동시에 그러한 감정이, 마치 제 천성에 접붙여진 듯 깊이 뿌리내려 있음을 의식하고 있었다. \"대령님의 작은따님이 지닌 그 다정함과 아름다움, 그 마력이라면, 제게 이런 부당함을 씌우지 않고도 제 동기를 설명해 줄 수 있을 것입니다, 〈먼로〉 대령님.\"\n\n\"옳은 말일세, 이 사람아.\" 노인이 다시 그 어조를 부드러움으로, 아니 차라리 온화함으로 바꾸며 대답했다. \"그 애는 제 어미가 그 나이 때, 그러니까 슬픔을 알기 전의 모습 그대로일세. 죽음이 내게서 아내를 앗아 갔을 때, 나는 그 혼인으로 부유해진 채 스코틀랜드로 돌아갔네. 그런데 자네, 이걸 믿겠는가, 〈던컨〉! 그 고통받던 천사는 매정한 독신의 처지로 스무 해를 그대로 머물러 있었으니, 그것도 자신을 잊을 수 있었던 사내를 위해서였네! 그녀는 그보다 더한 일도 했네, 이 사람아. 그녀는 내 신의 없음을 눈감아 주었고, 이제 모든 장애가 사라지자 나를 제 남편으로 맞아들였네.\"\n\n\"그리하여 〈앨리스〉의 어머니가 되셨군요?\" 〈던컨〉이, 〈먼로〉의 생각이 지금만큼 사로잡혀 있지 않은 순간이었다면 위태로웠을 법한 간절함으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"참으로 그랬네.\" 노인이 말했다. \"그리고 그녀는 자신이 베푼 그 축복의 값을 비싸게도 치렀지. 허나 그녀는 하늘에 있는 성인일세, 이 사람아. 무덤에 발을 디딘 자가 그토록 복된 운명을 애도하는 것은 온당치 못한 일이지. 그래도 나는 그녀를 단 한 해밖에 곁에 두지 못했네. 부질없는 그리움 속에 제 젊음이 시드는 것을 지켜본 이에게는 너무도 짧은 행복의 기간이었지.\"\n\n노인의 비탄에는 무언가 압도하는 데가 있어서, 〈헤이워드〉는 위로의 말 한마디조차 감히 건네지 못했다. 〈먼로〉는 상대의 존재마저 까맣게 잊은 채 앉아 있었으니, 그 얼굴은 회한의 고뇌로 드러난 채 일그러졌고, 무거운 눈물이 두 눈에서 흘러내려, 뺨을 타고 바닥으로 떨어져도 아랑곳하지 않았다. 마침내 그는 불현듯 정신을 차린 듯 몸을 움직였으니, 일어나 방을 한 차례 가로질러 거닌 뒤, 군인다운 위엄을 띠고 동행자에게 다가가 물었다.\n\n\"〈헤이워드〉 소령, 자네가 내게 전할, 〈몽칼름〉 후작이 보낸 무슨 전언이 있지 않은가?\"\n\n이번에는 〈던컨〉이 흠칫 놀라, 곧장 당황한 목소리로 반쯤 잊혔던 전갈을 전하기 시작했다. 프랑스 장군이, 자신이 전하겠다고 제안한 그 전언의 요지를 캐내려는 〈헤이워드〉의 온갖 시도를 정중하면서도 교묘히 피해 간 그 회피적인 태도에 대해서나, 또 직접 받지 않겠다면 아예 받지 못하리라는 뜻을, 단호하면서도 여전히 세련되게 적에게 알린 그 결연한 전갈에 대해 굳이 늘어놓을 필요는 없으리라. 〈먼로〉가 〈던컨〉의 소상한 이야기에 귀 기울이는 동안, 아비로서 격앙되었던 감정은 차츰 제 직분의 의무 앞에 자리를 내주었으니, 〈던컨〉이 이야기를 마쳤을 때 그가 마주한 것은, 군인의 상한 감정으로 부풀어 오른 노전사뿐이었다.\n\n\"말은 그만하면 충분하네, 〈헤이워드〉 소령!\" 노여운 노인이 외쳤다. \"프랑스식 예절에 관한 주석을 한 권 엮어 낼 만큼 충분해. 이 신사가 내게 회담을 청해 놓고, 내가 유능한 대리인을 보냈건만—자네야말로 그런 사람이지, 〈던컨〉, 비록 나이는 어릴지언정—그자는 내게 수수께끼로 답하다니.\"\n\n\"그가 대리인을 그리 탐탁잖게 여겼는지도 모릅니다, 대령님. 게다가 그가 지금 거듭하는 그 초청은, 요새의 사령관에게 한 것이지 그 부관에게 한 것이 아님을 기억하셔야겠지요.\"\n\n\"흠, 이 사람아, 대리인이란 권한을 위임한 자의 모든 권력과 위엄을 두른 자가 아니던가? 그가 〈먼로〉와 회담하길 바란다고! 정말이지, 이 사람아, 그자의 청을 들어주고픈 마음이 굴뚝같네, 설령 그것이 그자의 병력과 그 통첩에도 아랑곳없이 우리가 지키는 굳건한 얼굴을 그자에게 보여 주기 위해서일 뿐이라 해도 말이야. 그런 한 수에 나쁜 책략이랄 것도 없겠지, 젊은이.\"\n\n척후가 지닌 편지의 내용을 신속히 알아내는 일이야말로 더없이 중대하다고 여기던 〈던컨〉은, 이 생각을 기꺼이 부추겼다.\n\n\"의심할 바 없이, 우리의 무심함을 목격한들 그가 자신감을 얻지는 못할 것입니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그보다 더 옳은 말은 없을 걸세. 나로서는, 이 사람아, 그가 환한 대낮에, 그것도 돌격대의 형세로 이 요새를 찾아 주었으면 싶네. 그것이야말로 적의 얼굴을 가늠하는, 가장 어긋남이 적은 방법이며, 그가 택한 포격 방식보다는 훨씬 나으니까. 자네의 그 무슈 보방의 기술 탓에, 전쟁의 아름다움과 사내다움이 몹시도 일그러지고 말았네, 〈헤이워드〉 소령. 우리 선조들은 그런 학술적 비겁함 따위는 한참 위에 있었지!\"\n\n\"지당하신 말씀일지도 모릅니다, 대령님. 허나 우리는 이제 기술을 기술로 물리쳐야 하는 형편입니다. 회견 문제에 관해서는 어찌하기를 바라십니까?\"\n\n\"그 프랑스인을 만나겠네, 그것도 두려움이나 지체 없이, 신속하게 말일세. 이 사람아, 내 임금을 섬기는 신하답게 말이야. 가게, 〈헤이워드〉 소령, 그들에게 음악을 한 가락 울려 주고, 누가 가는지 알리도록 전령을 내보내게. 우리는 소수의 호위와 함께 뒤따르겠네, 임금의 명예를 맡아 지키는 자에게는 그만한 예우가 마땅하니. 그리고 잘 듣게, 〈던컨〉.\" 그는, 둘뿐이었음에도 반쯤 속삭이며 덧붙였다. \"이 모든 일의 바닥에 배신이 깔려 있을 경우를 대비해, 가까이에 약간의 지원을 두는 편이 신중하겠네.\"\n\n젊은이는 이 명령을 핑계 삼아 방을 나섰으니, 해가 빠르게 저물고 있던 터라, 지체 없이 서둘러 필요한 채비를 갖추었다. 몇 줄의 병사를 정렬시키고, 깃발을 든 전령을 보내어 요새 사령관의 행차를 알리는 데에는 단 몇 분이면 족했다. 〈던컨〉은 이 두 가지를 마치자, 호위대를 이끌고 출격문으로 갔으니, 그 가까이에서 채비를 갖추고 그가 나타나기를 기다리는 상관을 발견했다. 군의 출진에 따르는 통상의 의식이 치러지자마자, 노전사와 그 젊은 동행자는 호위대를 거느리고 요새를 떠났다.\n\n요새에서 백 야드밖에 나아가지 않았을 때, 프랑스 장군을 회담으로 수행하는 작은 행렬이, 포위군의 포대와 요새 사이를 흐르는 시내의 바닥을 이룬 움푹한 길에서 나오는 것이 보였다. 〈먼로〉가 제 진지를 떠나 적들 앞에 나서는 순간부터, 그의 거동은 당당했고, 그 걸음과 얼굴은 더없이 군인다웠다. 〈몽칼름〉의 모자에 나부끼는 흰 깃을 흘끗 잡아챈 순간, 그의 눈은 빛났고, 노쇠함은 더 이상 그 거대하고 여전히 다부진 몸에 어떤 영향도 미치지 못하는 듯했다.\n\n\"부하들에게 경계하라 일러 두게, 이 사람아.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 〈던컨〉에게 말했다. \"부싯돌과 격철을 잘 살피라고 말이야, 이 루이의 신하들과는 결코 안심할 수 없으니. 그러는 한편, 우리는 그들에게 깊은 안심에 든 사내들의 얼굴을 보여 주세. 내 말 알아듣겠지, 〈헤이워드〉 소령!\"\n\n다가오는 프랑스인들 쪽에서 울린 요란한 북소리에 그의 말이 끊겼으니, 그것에 곧장 응답이 이어졌고, 그러자 양편은 저마다 흰 깃발을 든 전령을 앞세웠으며, 신중한 스코틀랜드인은 호위대를 바짝 등 뒤에 둔 채 걸음을 멈추었다. 이 가벼운 인사가 오가자마자, 〈몽칼름〉은 빠르면서도 우아한 걸음으로 그들에게 다가왔으니, 노전사에게 모자를 벗고, 그 티 없는 깃을 예의로 거의 땅에까지 드리웠다. 〈먼로〉의 거동이 더 위엄 있고 사내다웠다면, 그것에는 저 프랑스인의 거동이 지닌 편안함도 마음을 파고드는 세련됨도 결여되어 있었다. 두 사람은 몇 순간 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 저마다 호기심과 흥미에 찬 눈으로 상대를 바라보았다. 이윽고, 그의 더 높은 지위와 회견의 성격에 걸맞게, 〈몽칼름〉이 침묵을 깼다. 통상의 인사말을 건넨 뒤, 그는 〈던컨〉을 향해 돌아서서, 알아본다는 미소를 지으며, 시종 프랑스어로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"무슈, 이번 자리에 귀공이 함께해 주시는 즐거움을 우리에게 베풀어 주시니 기쁩니다. 평범한 통역을 쓸 필요가 없겠군요. 귀공의 손에 맡기면, 내가 직접 귀공의 언어로 말하는 것과 다름없는 안심을 느낄 테니 말입니다.\"\n\n〈던컨〉이 그 찬사에 답례하자, 〈몽칼름〉은, 적의 호위대를 본떠 자신에게 바짝 다가선 제 호위대를 향해 돌아서며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"앙 아리에르, 메 장팡—일 페 쇼, 르티레부 욍 푸.\"\n\n* 앙 아리에르…: \"물러서거라, 얘들아—덥구나. 조금 떨어지거라\"라는 뜻의 프랑스어.\n〈헤이워드〉 소령은 이 신뢰의 증표를 본받기에 앞서, 두 눈으로 들판을 두루 살폈으니, 에워싼 숲의 가장자리에서 내다보며 회견을 호기심으로 구경하는 미개인들의 거뭇한 무리가 무수함을 불안한 마음으로 보았다.\n\n\"〈몽칼름〉 후작께서는 우리 처지의 차이를 흔쾌히 인정하실 줄 압니다.\" 그가 다소 당혹스러운 기색으로, 동시에 거의 사방에서 보이는 저 위험한 적들을 가리키며 말했다. \"우리가 호위대를 물린다면, 우리는 이 자리에서 적들의 처분에 내맡겨지는 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"무슈, 귀공에게는 한 프랑스 신사가 맹세한 신의가 있습니다, 귀공의 안전을 위해서 말이지요.\" 〈몽칼름〉이 한 손을 인상적으로 제 가슴에 얹으며 대답했다. \"그것으로 족할 것입니다.\"\n\n\"족하겠지요. 물러서게.\" 〈던컨〉이 호위대를 이끄는 장교에게 덧붙였다. \"물러서게, 소리가 들리지 않는 곳까지. 그리고 명령을 기다리게.\"\n\n〈먼로〉는 이 움직임을 역력한 불안과 함께 지켜보았으니, 즉각 해명을 요구하기를 잊지 않았다.\n\n\"불신을 조금도 내비치지 않는 것이 우리에게 이롭지 않겠습니까, 대령님?\" 〈던컨〉이 응수했다. \"〈몽칼름〉 후작이 우리의 안전을 두고 그 말을 걸었으니, 저는 우리가 그의 보증에 얼마나 의지하는지를 보이고자 병사들에게 조금 물러서라 명한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"다 옳은 일일지도 모르지, 이 사람아, 허나 나는 이 후작들, 아니 제놈들이 일컫는 대로 후작이라는 자들의 신의에 그리 지나친 믿음을 두지 않네. 그들의 귀족 작위장은 너무도 흔해 빠져, 그것이 참된 명예의 인장을 지녔다고 장담할 수 없으니까.\"\n\n\"잊지 마십시오, 대령님, 우리는 그 공적으로 유럽과 아메리카에서 똑같이 이름난 장교와 회담하고 있습니다. 그만한 명성을 지닌 군인에게서 우리가 두려워할 것은 아무것도 없습니다.\"\n\n노인은 체념하는 몸짓을 했으나, 그 굳은 얼굴은 여전히 그 불신을 완강히 고수함을 드러냈으니, 그것은 그토록 박정한 감정을 정당화할 만한 어떤 당장의 징후에서라기보다, 적을 향한 일종의 세습된 경멸에서 비롯된 것이었다. 〈몽칼름〉은 반쯤 죽인 목소리로 오간 이 짤막한 대화가 끝나기를 참을성 있게 기다렸으니, 그것이 끝나자 더 가까이 다가서서, 회담의 본론을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"내가 귀공의 상관에게 이 회견을 청한 것은, 무슈,\" 그가 말했다. \"그가 이미 자기 군주의 명예를 위해 필요한 모든 일을 다했음을 스스로 납득하게 되리라 믿었기 때문이며, 또 인도(人道)의 권고에 귀 기울이지 않을까 해서였습니다. 나는 그의 저항이 용맹했고, 희망이 있는 한 이어졌음을 영원히 증언하겠습니다.\"\n\n이 서두가 〈먼로〉에게 옮겨지자, 그는 위엄을 갖추되 충분한 예의로 답했다.\n\n\"〈몽칼름〉 후작에게서 그런 증언을 받음을 내 아무리 귀히 여긴다 한들, 그것이 더 마땅히 받을 만한 것이 될 때 더욱 값지겠지요.\"\n\n프랑스 장군은, 〈던컨〉이 이 대답의 요지를 전하자 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"입증된 용기에 지금 그토록 선뜻 베풀어지는 것이, 부질없는 고집에는 거절될 수도 있습니다. 무슈께서는 내 진영을 보시고, 우리의 병력과, 그것에 성공적으로 맞서기란 불가능하다는 점을 친히 목격하고 싶으신지요?\"\n\n\"나는 프랑스 임금이 잘 섬김을 받고 있음을 압니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 통역을 마치자마자 동요 없는 스코틀랜드인이 대답했다. \"허나 내 임금께도 그만큼 많고 그만큼 충직한 군대가 있소이다.\"\n\n\"다만, 우리에게는 다행스럽게도, 가까이에 있지 않지요.\" 〈몽칼름〉이, 그 열의에 차 통역을 기다리지도 않고 말했다. \"전쟁에는 운명이라는 것이 있어, 용감한 사내라면 제 적을 마주하는 것과 똑같은 용기로 그것에 따를 줄 안답니다.\"\n\n\"〈몽칼름〉 후작께서 영어에 능하심을 진작 알았더라면, 이토록 서툰 통역의 수고를 덜었을 것을.\" 분이 치민 〈던컨〉이 무뚝뚝하게 말했으니, 〈먼로〉와 막 주고받았던 짤막한 연기가 곧장 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"용서하시오, 무슈.\" 프랑스인이, 거뭇한 뺨에 옅은 홍조가 어리도록 내버려 둔 채 응수했다. \"외국 말을 알아듣는 것과 말하는 것 사이에는 어마어마한 차이가 있지요. 그러니 부디 계속 나를 도와주시오.\" 그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"이 언덕들은 우리에게 귀공들의 요새를 정찰할 온갖 기회를 주니, 무슈들, 나는 아마 귀공들 자신만큼이나 그 허약한 형편을 잘 알고 있을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"프랑스 장군에게 묻게, 그의 망원경이 〈허드슨〉강에까지 미치는지 말일세.\" 〈먼로〉가 자랑스레 말했다. \"그리고 〈웨브〉의 군대가 언제 어디서 올지를 그가 아는지도.\"\n\n\"〈웨브〉 장군이 직접 통역하게 하시지요.\" 책략에 능한 〈몽칼름〉이, 말하면서 별안간 펼쳐진 편지 한 장을 〈먼로〉를 향해 내밀며 대답했다. \"거기서 알게 되실 겁니다, 무슈, 그의 움직임이 내 군대에 곤란을 끼칠 성싶지 않다는 것을 말입니다.\"\n\n노전사는 〈던컨〉이 그 말을 통역하기를 기다리지도 않고, 그 내용을 얼마나 중대히 여기는지 드러내는 간절함으로 내밀어진 종이를 낚아챘다. 그의 눈이 그 글자들 위를 황급히 훑어 가는 동안, 그의 얼굴은 군인다운 자긍의 표정에서 깊은 낭패의 표정으로 변해 갔다. 입술이 떨리기 시작했고, 손에서 종이가 떨어지도록 내버려 둔 채, 단 한 번의 타격에 희망이 시들어 버린 사람처럼 고개가 가슴께로 떨구어졌다. 〈던컨〉이 바닥에서 편지를 집어 들었으니, 제멋대로 그리한 무례를 사과하지도 않은 채, 그 잔혹한 내용을 한눈에 읽어 냈다. 그들 공동의 상관은, 그들에게 저항을 북돋기는커녕, 신속한 항복을 권하면서, 단 한 명의 병사도 그들의 구원에 보낼 수 없다는 절대적인 불가능을 가장 평이한 말로 그 이유랍시고 역설하고 있었다.\n\n\"여기엔 속임수가 없습니다!\" 〈던컨〉이 그 짤막한 글을 안팎으로 살피며 외쳤다. \"이것은 〈웨브〉의 서명이며, 노획된 편지가 틀림없습니다.\"\n\n\"그자가 나를 배신했네!\" 마침내 〈먼로〉가 비통하게 외쳤다. \"그자는, 일찍이 치욕이 깃든 적 없던 집의 문간에 불명예를 끌어들였고, 내 백발 위에 부끄러움을 무겁게도 쌓아 올렸네.\"\n\n\"그리 말씀 마십시오.\" 〈던컨〉이 외쳤다. \"우리는 아직 요새의, 그리고 우리 명예의 주인입니다. 그러니 우리 목숨을, 적들이 그 값을 너무 비싸다 여길 만한 값에 팝시다.\"\n\n\"이 사람아, 고맙네.\" 노인이 그 망연함에서 정신을 추스르며 외쳤다. \"자네가 이번 한 번, 〈먼로〉에게 제 의무를 일깨워 주었군. 우리는 돌아가, 저 성벽 뒤에 우리의 무덤을 파세.\"\n\n\"무슈들,\" 〈몽칼름〉이 관대한 관심에서 그들에게 한 걸음 다가서며 말했다. \"귀공들이 〈루이 드 생베랑〉을, 이 편지를 빌미로 용감한 사내들을 욕보이거나 자신을 위해 떳떳지 못한 명성을 쌓을 자라 여긴다면, 그를 너무도 모르는 것입니다. 나를 떠나기 전에 내 조건을 들어 보시오.\"\n\n\"프랑스인이 뭐라 하는가?\" 노전사가 준엄하게 물었다. \"그자는 본부의 쪽지를 지닌 척후를 사로잡은 것을 공으로 내세우는 게냐? 이 사람아, 그자가 적을 말로 겁주고 싶거든, 차라리 이 포위를 풀고 〈에드워드〉 요새 앞에나 가서 진을 치는 편이 낫겠네.\"\n\n〈던컨〉이 상대의 뜻을 설명했다.\n\n\"〈몽칼름〉 후작, 우리가 귀공의 말을 듣겠소이다.\" 〈던컨〉이 말을 마치자 노전사가 한층 차분히 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이제 요새를 지키기란 불가능합니다.\" 그 너그러운 적이 말했다. \"이곳이 파괴되는 것이 내 군주의 이익에 필요하지요. 허나 귀공들과 귀공들의 용감한 동지들에 관해서라면, 군인에게 소중한 어떤 특권도 거절되지 않을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"우리 군기는요?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"〈영국〉으로 가져가, 귀공의 임금에게 보여 주시오.\"\n\n\"우리 무기는요?\"\n\n\"지니시오. 누구도 그것을 더 잘 쓰지 못할 테니.\"\n\n\"우리의 행군은, 그리고 이곳의 양도는요?\"\n\n\"모두 귀공들에게 더없이 명예로운 방식으로 이루어질 것입니다.\"\n\n이제 〈던컨〉이 이 제안들을 자신의 지휘관에게 설명하고자 돌아서니, 지휘관은 그것을 경이로움과 함께, 그토록 예사롭지 않고 뜻밖의 관대함에 깊이 동하는 감수성으로 들었다.\n\n\"가게, 〈던컨〉.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 후작과 함께 가게, 정녕 후작이라 할 만한 사람이니. 그의 막사로 가서 모든 것을 매듭짓게. 나는 이 늘그막에, 결코 보리라 기대치 않았던 두 가지를 살아서 보았네. 벗을 돕기를 두려워하는 〈영국〉인과, 제 유리한 처지를 이용하기에는 너무도 정직한 프랑스인을 말일세.\"\n\n그리 말하고서, 노전사는 다시 고개를 가슴께로 떨군 채, 천천히 요새를 향해 돌아갔으니, 그 풀 죽은 거동으로, 근심에 찬 수비대에게 흉한 소식의 전조를 내비쳤다.\n\n이 뜻밖의 타격이 안긴 충격으로부터 〈먼로〉의 도도한 감정은 결코 회복되지 못했다. 다만 그 순간부터, 그의 결연한 성품에 한 변화가 일기 시작했으니, 그것은 그를 머지않은 무덤에까지 따라갔다. 〈던컨〉은 항복의 조건을 마무리하고자 남았다. 그가 밤의 초경(初更)에 요새로 다시 들어가는 것이 보였고, 사령관과 은밀한 회담을 가진 직후, 다시 그곳을 떠나는 것이 보였다. 그제야 적대 행위를 중지해야 한다는 것이 공공연히 알려졌으니—〈먼로〉가 한 조약에 서명했던바, 그에 따라 이곳은 아침과 더불어 적에게 양도되며, 수비대는 그 무기와 군기와 군장을, 따라서 군인의 통념에 따르자면 그 명예를 보전하기로 되었다.\n\n우리는 씨실을 짠다. 날실은 다 자아졌다.\n그물은 짜였고, 일은 끝났다.\n\n그레이\n〈호리컨〉의 황야에 진을 친 적대하는 양쪽 군대는, 1757년 8월 9일 밤을, 유럽의 가장 빼어난 전장에서 맞붙었더라도 그러했을 모양과 별반 다르지 않게 보냈다. 패한 자들은 여전히 말이 없고, 침울하고, 풀이 죽어 있었으나, 승자들은 의기양양했다. 그러나 슬픔에도 기쁨에도 한계가 있는 법이어서, 새벽의 야경이 돌아오기 한참 전에, 그 가없는 숲의 정적을 깨뜨린 것이라곤 전초선에 나선 어느 들뜬 젊은 프랑스 병사의 흥겨운 외침이나, 정해진 시각이 오기 전에는 어떤 적의 발걸음도 다가오는 것을 준엄하게 금하는 요새의 위협적인 수하(誰何)뿐이었다. 이 이따금 들리던 위협의 소리마저, 동트기 직전의 그 음울한 시각에는 더 들리지 않게 되었으니, 그 무렵에는 귀 기울이는 자라도 \"거룩한 호수\"의 기슭에 잠들어 있는 그 무장한 세력의 존재를 입증할 무엇 하나 찾으려 해도 헛수고였을 것이다.\n\n이렇게 깊은 정적이 깔린 순간에, 프랑스 진영의 어느 널찍한 천막 입구를 가리고 있던 천이 옆으로 젖혀지더니, 한 사내가 휘장 아래에서 트인 바깥 공기 속으로 나왔다. 그는 숲의 으슬으슬한 습기를 막으려는 것이었을 외투를 두르고 있었으나, 그것은 또한 그 몸을 감추는 망토 노릇도 똑같이 잘 해 주었다. 그는 프랑스군 사령관의 잠을 지키는 척탄병을 거리낌 없이 지나치도록 허락받았으니, 그가 천막의 작은 도성 사이를 〈윌리엄 헨리〉 방면으로 빠르게 지나갈 때, 그 병사는 군인다운 경의를 나타내는 여느 거수경례를 올렸다. 이 알 수 없는 인물이 제 앞길을 가로지르는 무수한 보초 가운데 하나와 마주칠 때마다, 그의 대답은 신속했고 또 흡족한 듯했으니, 그는 한결같이 더 이상의 심문 없이 나아가도록 허락받았다.\n\n이런 거듭된, 그러나 짧은 제지를 빼고는, 그는 진영의 중심에서 가장 앞으로 나선 초소까지 소리 없이 움직였으며, 이윽고 적의 진지에 가장 가까이 보초를 서는 병사에게 다가갔다. 그가 다가가자 여느 수하로 맞이되었다.\n\n\"누구냐?\"\n\n\"프랑스.\" 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"암호는?\"\n\n\"승리.\" 다른 이가 큰 속삭임으로 들릴 만큼 가까이 다가서며 말했다.\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 보초가 견착 자세에서 총을 어깨로 돌려 메며 대꾸했다. \"꽤나 이른 아침에 산책을 하시는군요, 나리!\"\n\n\"방심하지 않는 것이 필요하지, 이 사람아.\" 다른 이가 외투 자락 한 겹을 떨어뜨리며, 여전히 영국군 요새 쪽으로 길을 이어 가는 채로, 병사의 얼굴을 바싹 들여다보며 일렀다. 사내는 흠칫했고, 가장 낮고 가장 공손한 경례로 무기를 앞으로 내밀자 그 쇠붙이가 묵직하게 절그럭거렸다. 그리고 다시 제 총을 추스른 뒤, 초소를 거닐려고 돌아서며 이 사이로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"참말이지 방심하지 말아야겠어! 우리한테 결코 잠들지 않는 상관이 한 분 계신가 봐!\"\n\n장교는 놀란 보초의 입에서 새어 나온 그 말을 듣는 척도 않고 나아갔으며, 낮은 모래톱에 이르러 요새의 서쪽 수상 보루와 다소 위험할 만큼 가까운 데에 다다를 때까지 다시 걸음을 멈추지 않았다. 어슴푸레한 달빛은 사물의 윤곽을, 비록 흐릿할망정, 알아볼 수 있게 할 만큼만 비추었다. 그리하여 그는 한 그루 나무의 둥치에 몸을 기대는 조심성을 부려, 거기에 여러 분 동안 기대선 채로, 영국군 진지의 어둡고 고요한 흙더미를 깊은 주의를 기울여 응시하는 듯했다. 보루를 향한 그의 눈길은 호기심 많거나 한가한 구경꾼의 것이 아니었으니, 그 시선이 이 지점에서 저 지점으로 옮겨 다니며 군사적 관행에 밝다는 것을 드러내었고, 그 살핌이 의심을 동반하지 않은 것이 아님을 누설했다. 마침내 그는 흡족해진 듯했고, 새벽의 다가옴을 기다리기라도 하는 양 동쪽 산의 마루를 향해 초조하게 눈을 치떴다가, 막 발길을 되돌리려던 참에, 보루의 가장 가까운 모서리에서 가벼운 소리가 그 귀를 사로잡아 그를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n바로 그때 한 형체가 흉벽의 가장자리로 다가오는 것이 보였으니, 그것은 거기에 서서, 이번에는 멀리 프랑스 진영의 천막들을 응시하는 듯했다. 이윽고 그 머리가 동쪽으로 돌려졌으니, 마치 그 역시 빛이 나타나기를 똑같이 애타게 기다리는 듯했고, 그러다 그 형체는 흙더미에 기대어, 마치 해저의 창공처럼 수천 개의 흉내 별로 반짝이는 물의 거울 같은 너른 면을 응시하는 듯했다. 그 우수에 잠긴 분위기와, 시각과, 영국군 흉벽에 그렇게 골똘히 기대선 사내의 거대한 골격은, 이를 지켜보던 자의 마음속에 그가 누구인지에 관한 의심을 남기지 않았다. 사려 못지않게 분별심이 이제 그에게 물러가기를 재촉했고, 그가 그러려고 나무 둥치를 돌아 조심스레 움직였을 때, 또 다른 소리가 그의 주의를 끌어 다시 한번 그 발걸음을 붙들어 세웠다. 그것은 물의 나지막한, 거의 들리지 않는 움직임이었으며, 그 뒤를 조약돌이 서로 갈리는 소리가 이었다. 순식간에 그는 어두운 형체 하나가 마치 호수에서 솟아오르듯 일어나, 더는 소리도 없이, 그가 서 있는 자리에서 불과 몇 자 안 되는 곳으로 뭍에 살그머니 올라서는 것을 보았다. 이어 한 자루 소총이 그의 두 눈과 물의 거울 사이로 천천히 들렸으나, 그것이 발사되기 전에 그 자신의 손이 격발 장치를 누르고 있었다.\n\n\"휴!\" 그토록 유별나고 그토록 뜻밖에 그 음험한 겨냥이 가로막힌 야만인이 외쳤다.\n\n아무 대답도 하지 않고, 프랑스 장교는 그 인디언의 어깨에 손을 얹어, 뒤따를 그들의 대화가 위험을 초래할 수도 있었던, 그리고 적어도 둘 중 하나는 제물을 노리는 듯했던 그 자리에서 멀리 떨어진 데로, 깊은 침묵 속에 그를 이끌었다. 그런 다음, 제 군복과 가슴에 매달린 〈성루이〉 십자 훈장이 드러나도록 외투를 활짝 열어젖히며, 〈몽칼름〉이 준엄하게 캐물었다.\n\n\"이것이 무슨 짓이냐! 내 아들은 영국군과 그의 캐나다인 아버지 사이에 손도끼가 묻혔다는 것을 모르는가?\"\n\n\"휴런족이 무엇을 할 수 있겠소?\" 야만인이, 비록 서툴기는 했으나 역시 프랑스 말로 응수했다. \"전사 가운데 머리 가죽 하나 얻은 자가 없는데, 창백한 얼굴들은 친구가 되었구려!\"\n\n\"하! 교활한 〈여우〉여! 이건 얼마 전까지 적이던 벗에 대한 도가 지나친 열성이로구나! 〈여우〉가 영국군의 전쟁 기둥을 친 뒤로 해가 몇 번이나 졌느냐?\"\n\n\"그 해가 어디 있소!\" 침울한 야만인이 따져 물었다. \"언덕 너머에 있으니, 어둡고 차갑소. 허나 그것이 다시 떠오르면, 밝고 따뜻할 것이오. 〈교활한 자〉는 제 부족의 해요. 그와 그의 민족 사이에 구름이 끼고, 많은 산이 가로놓였으나, 이제 그가 빛나니 하늘이 맑소!\"\n\n\"〈여우〉가 제 부족에게 힘이 있다는 것은 내 잘 안다.\" 〈몽칼름〉이 말했다. \"어제는 그들의 머리 가죽을 노려 사냥하더니, 오늘은 평의회 불가에서 그의 말에 귀 기울이니 말이다.\"\n\n\"〈마구아〉는 위대한 추장이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그것을 증명해 보여라, 제 민족에게 우리의 새 벗들을 어떻게 대해야 하는지 가르침으로써.\"\n\n\"캐나다의 추장은 어찌하여 제 젊은이들을 숲으로 데려와, 흙집을 향해 대포를 쏘았소?\" 교활한 인디언이 따져 물었다.\n\n\"그것을 굴복시키기 위함이다. 내 주군께서 그 땅을 소유하시니, 너의 아버지는 이 영국인 무단 점거자들을 몰아내라는 명을 받았다. 그들이 떠나기로 동의했으니, 이제 그분은 그들을 더 이상 적이라 부르지 않으신다.\"\n\n\"좋소. 〈마구아〉는 손도끼를 들어 피로 물들이려 했소. 지금 그것이 번쩍이오. 그것이 붉어지면, 그때 묻힐 것이오.\"\n\n\"그러나 〈마구아〉는 프랑스의 백합을 더럽히지 않겠다고 맹세하였다. 소금 호수 건너 위대한 왕의 적은 그의 적이요, 그의 벗은 휴런족의 벗이다.\"\n\n\"벗이라!\" 인디언이 비웃으며 되뇌었다. \"그의 아버지더러 〈마구아〉에게 손을 한번 내밀어 보라 하시오.\"\n\n자기가 끌어모은 호전적인 부족들에 대한 영향력이 권력보다는 양보로써 유지되어야 함을 느낀 〈몽칼름〉은, 마지못해 상대의 청에 응했다. 야만인은 프랑스군 사령관의 손가락을 제 가슴의 깊은 흉터에 갖다 댄 다음, 의기양양하게 물었다.\n\n\"내 아버지는 저것을 아시오?\"\n\n\"어느 전사인들 모르겠느냐? 납탄이 베어 낸 자국이로다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이것은?\" 인디언이 이으며, 평소의 무명 망토를 걸치지 않은 맨등을 상대에게 돌렸다.\n\n\"이것은! 내 아들이 여기에 몹시 상하였구나. 누가 이런 짓을 하였느냐?\"\n\n\"〈마구아〉가 영국인의 천막에서 모진 잠을 잤더니, 막대기들이 그 자국을 남겼소.\" 야만인이, 그를 거의 질식시킬 듯한 사나운 성정을 감추지 못하는 공허한 웃음과 함께 응수했다. 그러더니 정신을 차리고는, 돌연한, 타고난 위엄으로 덧붙였다. \"가시오. 당신의 젊은이들에게 이제 평화라고 가르치시오. 교활한 〈여우〉는 휴런족 전사에게 말하는 법을 알고 있소.\"\n\n더는 말을 건넬 것도, 어떤 대답을 기다릴 것도 없이, 야만인은 제 소총을 팔 안쪽에 걸치고, 제 부족이 자리 잡고 있다고 알려진 숲을 향해 진영을 가로질러 소리 없이 움직였다. 나아가는 동안 몇 걸음마다 보초들에게 수하를 받았으나, 그는 병사들의 호령을 깡그리 무시한 채 침울하게 성큼성큼 나아갔으니, 병사들이 그의 목숨을 살려 둔 것은 오로지 그의 분위기와 걸음걸이가, 인디언 특유의 고집스러운 대담함 못지않게 분명했기 때문이었다.\n\n〈몽칼름〉은 제 동행이 떠난 모래톱에 오래도록 우울하게 머물러, 다스릴 길 없는 그 동맹자가 방금 드러낸 성정을 깊이 곱씹었다. 이미 그의 빛나던 명성은 한 차례의 끔찍한 장면으로 더럽혀진 바 있었으니, 그것도 그가 지금 처한 것과 무섭도록 닮은 정황 속에서였다. 그는 곰곰이 생각할수록, 목적을 이루는 수단을 도외시하는 자들이 떠맡는 깊은 책임과, 인간의 힘으로 다스릴 수 없는 기계를 가동시키는 온갖 위험을 예민하게 절감하게 되었다. 그러다 이런 승리의 순간에 나약함이라 여겨질 그 일련의 상념을 떨쳐 내고, 그는 천막을 향해 발길을 되돌렸으니, 지나는 길에 군대를 잠에서 깨울 신호를 보내라는 명을 내렸다.\n\n프랑스군 북의 첫 두드림은 요새의 품에서 메아리쳐 돌아왔고, 이내 골짜기는 길고, 짜릿하고, 경쾌하게, 우르릉대는 반주 위로 솟아오르는 군악의 가락으로 가득 찼다. 승자들의 나팔은 진영의 마지막 낙오자까지 제자리에 설 때까지 흥겹고 명랑한 곡을 울렸으나, 영국군 피리가 그 날카로운 신호를 불어 댄 그 순간, 그것들은 잠잠해졌다. 그러는 사이 날이 밝았고, 프랑스군의 대열이 그 장군을 맞이할 채비가 되었을 때, 찬란한 태양의 광선이 번쩍이는 행렬을 따라 비스듬히 비쳤다. 그러자 이미 그토록 잘 알려진 그 승리가 공식적으로 선포되었고, 요새의 성문을 지키도록 선발된 영예로운 부대가 차출되어 그 사령관 앞을 행진해 지나갔으며, 그들의 접근을 알리는 신호가 떨어지고, 주인이 바뀌는 데 따르는 온갖 여느 채비가, 다투던 그 진지의 대포들 바로 아래에서 명해지고 또 이행되었다.\n\n영미 연합군의 전선 안에는 사뭇 다른 광경이 펼쳐졌다. 경고 신호가 떨어지자마자, 그곳은 황급하고 마지못한 출발의 온갖 기색을 드러냈다. 침울한 병사들은 빈 총을 어깨에 메고 제자리에 들어섰으니, 지난 싸움에 피가 끓어오른 채로, 군대의 예법이라는 온갖 격식 아래 감추어졌을지언정 여전히 그들의 자존심을 찌르는 그 모욕을 갚을 기회만을 바라는 자들 같았다. 여인들과 아이들은 이리저리 뛰어다녔으니, 더러는 얼마 안 되는 짐의 잔해를 이고, 더러는 자기들이 보호를 우러러 의지하던 그 얼굴들을 대열 속에서 찾아 헤맸다.\n\n〈먼로〉는 말 없는 그의 군대 가운데 굳건하나 풀이 죽은 모습으로 나타났다. 뜻밖의 일격이 그의 가슴에 깊이 박혔음이 분명했으니, 비록 그가 사내다운 풍모로 그 불운을 견디려 안간힘을 썼으나 그러했다.\n\n〈덩컨〉은 그 조용하고 가슴을 울리는 슬픔의 표출에 마음이 움직였다. 그는 제 임무를 다한 터라, 이제 그 노인의 곁으로 다가가, 어떤 점에서 그를 도울 수 있을지 물었다.\n\n\"내 딸들일세.\" 짧으나 절절한 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"맙소사! 그들의 편의를 위한 채비가 아직 마련되지 않았단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"오늘 나는 한낱 군인일 뿐이라네, 〈헤이워드〉 소령.\" 노병이 말했다. \"여기 자네 눈에 보이는 모든 이가 다 똑같이 내 자식임을 주장하고 있네.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉은 더 들을 것도 없었다. 이제 그토록 소중해진 그 순간들을 한순간도 잃지 않고, 그는 자매를 찾아 〈먼로〉의 숙소를 향해 내달렸다. 그는 그들을 그 나지막한 건물의 문턱에서, 이미 떠날 채비를 갖춘 채로, 자기와 같은 성(性)의 떠들썩하고 흐느끼는 무리에 둘러싸인 모습으로 찾아냈으니, 그 무리는 그곳이 가장 보호받기 쉬운 지점이라는 어떤 본능적 자각에서 그 자리로 모여든 터였다. 〈코라〉의 두 뺨은 창백하고 그 얼굴은 근심에 잠겼으나, 그녀는 굳건함을 조금도 잃지 않았다. 그러나 〈앨리스〉의 두 눈은 충혈되어, 그녀가 얼마나 오래도록 비통하게 울었는지를 누설하고 있었다. 그러나 두 사람 모두 그 청년을 숨김없는 기쁨으로 맞이했으니, 평소와 달리 먼저 입을 연 것은 언니 쪽이었다.\n\n\"요새는 잃었어요.\" 그녀가 우수 어린 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"허나 우리의 좋은 이름만은 남아 있으리라 믿어요.\"\n\n\"그 어느 때보다 빛나고 있습니다. 그러나, 더없이 소중한 〈먼로〉 양, 이제는 남을 덜 생각하고, 당신 자신을 위해 무언가 대비할 때입니다. 군대의 관례가, 자존심이, 당신이 그토록 소중히 여기는 그 자존심이, 당신의 아버님과 제가 잠시 군대와 함께 남아 있기를 요구합니다. 그러니 이런 장면의 혼란과 우연으로부터 당신을 지킬 마땅한 보호자를 어디서 구한단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"보호자는 필요 없어요.\" 〈코라〉가 응수했다. \"이런 때에 누가 감히 그런 아버지의 딸을 해치거나 모욕하겠어요?\"\n\n\"왕의 봉급을 받는 가장 훌륭한 연대의 지휘권을 준대도 당신들을 혼자 두지는 않겠습니다.\" 청년이 황급히 주위를 둘러보며 이었다. \"기억하십시오, 우리 〈앨리스〉는 당신의 모든 굳건함을 타고나지 못했으니, 그녀가 어떤 공포를 견뎌야 할지는 오직 하느님만이 아십니다.\"\n\n\"당신 말이 옳을지도 몰라요.\" 〈코라〉가 다시 미소 지었으나, 아까보다 훨씬 슬프게 대답했다. \"들어 보세요! 우연이 가장 필요한 때에 벌써 우리에게 한 벗을 보내 주었군요.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉은 과연 귀를 기울였고, 그 순간 그녀의 뜻을 알아챘다. 동부의 여러 영토에 익히 알려진 그 성가(聖歌)의 나지막하고 엄숙한 소리가 그의 귀를 사로잡아, 그를 곧장 이웃한 건물의 한 방으로 이끌었으니, 그곳은 본래 머물던 이들이 이미 떠나 버린 곳이었다. 그곳에서 그는 〈데이비드〉가, 그가 평생 빠져들곤 했던 단 하나의 통로를 통해 제 경건한 심정을 쏟아 내고 있는 것을 보았다. 〈덩컨〉은 그 손의 움직임이 멎는 것으로 가락이 끝났다고 여겨질 때까지 기다렸다가, 그의 어깨를 건드려 상대의 주의를 제게로 끌고, 몇 마디로 제 바람을 설명했다.\n\n\"그렇게 하지요.\" 청년이 말을 마치자, 〈이스라엘〉 왕의 한결같은 제자가 대답했다. \"그 처녀들에게서 단정하고 가락 고운 점을 많이 보았으니, 그토록 큰 위험 속에 함께한 우리가 평화 속에 함께 머무는 것이 마땅합니다. 내 아침 찬미를 마치는 대로 그들을 모시지요. 이제 송영(頌詠)밖에 남지 않았소이다. 한 소절 맡아 주시겠소, 친구? 운율은 평이하고, 곡조는 〈사우스웰〉이오.\"\n\n그러고 나서, 그 작은 책을 내밀고 사려 깊은 정성으로 곡의 음을 다시 잡으며, 〈데이비드〉는 좀처럼 가로막기 어려운 한결같은 태도로 그 가락을 다시 시작하여 끝맺었다. 〈헤이워드〉는 그 절이 끝날 때까지 어쩔 수 없이 기다려야 했으니, 〈데이비드〉가 안경을 벗어 내려놓고 책을 다시 집어넣는 것을 보고 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"누구도 무례한 의도로 감히 그 부인들에게 다가가거나, 그분들의 용감한 아버님의 불운에 모욕이나 조롱을 던지지 못하도록 지키는 것이 당신의 임무가 될 것입니다. 이 일에서 당신은 그 댁의 하인들의 도움을 받게 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하지요.\"\n\n\"적의 인디언들과 낙오자들이 끼어들지도 모르는데, 그런 경우에는 그들에게 항복 협정의 조항을 일깨우고, 그들의 행실을 〈몽칼름〉에게 보고하겠다고 위협하시오. 한마디면 족할 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그것으로 안 되면, 여기 그것을 해낼 것이 있소이다.\" 〈데이비드〉가 제 책을 내보이며 응수했으니, 그 태도에는 온순함과 자신감이 유별나게 뒤섞여 있었다. \"여기에, 적절한 강세로, 가락에 맞추어 입에 올리면, 아니 우레처럼 터뜨리면, 더없이 다스리기 힘든 성정도 잠재울 말씀이 있소이다.\n\n이방인들이 어찌하여 사납게 분노하는가!\"\n\n\"됐습니다.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 그 가락 어린 기원의 분출을 가로막으며 말했다. \"우리는 서로 통했습니다. 이제 우리가 저마다의 임무를 맡을 때입니다.\"\n\n〈거뮤트〉가 흔쾌히 동의했고, 그들은 함께 그 여인들을 찾아갔다. 〈코라〉는 자기의 새롭고 다소 별난 보호자를 적어도 정중하게 맞이했으며, 〈앨리스〉의 창백한 이목구비조차 〈헤이워드〉에게 그 보살핌을 감사하면서 본래의 장난기 어린 빛을 얼마간 되찾았다. 〈덩컨〉은 이 기회에, 정황이 허락하는 한 최선을 다했으며, 자기가 믿기로 그들의 마음을 안심시키기에 충분할 만큼은 했다고 그들에게 확언했다. 위험은 없었다. 그러고는 선두를 이끌고 〈허드슨〉강 쪽으로 몇 마일 나아가는 대로 곧장 그들에게 다시 합류하겠다는 뜻을 기쁘게 밝히고, 곧바로 작별했다.\n\n이 무렵 출발 신호가 떨어졌고, 영국군 종대의 선두가 움직이고 있었다. 자매는 그 소리에 흠칫하며 주위로 눈길을 던지다가, 이미 요새의 성문을 차지한 프랑스 척탄병들의 흰 군복을 보았다. 그 순간, 거대한 구름 한 점이 그들의 머리 위를 돌연 지나가는 듯하여 위를 올려다보니, 자기들이 프랑스 군기의 너른 자락 아래에 서 있음을 알게 되었다.\n\n\"가요.\" 〈코라〉가 말했다. \"여기는 더 이상 영국 장교의 딸들에게 어울리는 곳이 아니에요.\"\n\n〈앨리스〉가 언니의 팔에 매달렸고, 둘은 함께 연병장을 떠났으니, 그들을 에워싼 움직이는 무리가 동행했다.\n\n그들이 성문을 지날 때, 그들의 신분을 알게 된 프랑스 장교들은 자주 깊이 허리를 숙였으나, 유별난 눈치로, 그것이 달갑지 않을 수도 있음을 알아채고는 그런 관심을 강요하기를 삼갔다. 모든 수레와 모든 짐승이 병자와 부상자로 차 있었던 까닭에, 〈코라〉는 그들의 편의를 방해하느니 차라리 도보 행군의 고단함을 견디기로 작정한 터였다. 실로, 적잖은 불구의, 쇠약한 병사들이, 그 황야에서 운반할 마땅한 수단이 없어, 그 지친 사지를 끌며 종대의 후미를 따라야 했다. 그러나 전체가 움직이고 있었으니, 쇠약한 자와 부상자는 신음하며 고통받았고, 그들의 전우는 말없이 침울했으며, 여인들과 아이들은 무엇 때문인지도 모르는 공포에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n혼란스럽고 겁에 질린 무리가 요새의 보호하는 흙더미를 떠나 트인 벌판으로 나서자, 그 광경 전체가 단번에 그들의 눈앞에 펼쳐졌다. 오른쪽으로 조금 떨어진, 그리고 다소 뒤편에는 프랑스군이 무장을 갖추고 서 있었으니, 〈몽칼름〉이 그의 호위병이 진지를 차지하자마자 자기 부대들을 한데 모은 터였다. 그들은 패한 자들의 행렬을 주의 깊으나 말없이 지켜보는 자들로서, 약속된 군사적 예우를 하나도 빠뜨리지 않았고, 승리에 도취해 자기들보다 운 없는 적에게 어떤 조롱이나 모욕도 던지지 않았다. 통틀어 거의 삼천에 이르는 영국군의 살아 있는 무리가 벌판을 가로질러 공동의 중심을 향해 천천히 움직였으며, 행군의 목표 지점, 곧 키 큰 나무들 사이로 베어 낸 통로, 〈허드슨〉강으로 가는 길이 숲으로 들어서는 그곳으로 수렴해 가면서 차츰 서로 가까워졌다. 숲의 굽이진 가장자리를 따라서는 야만인들의 어두운 구름이 드리워, 적들의 통행을 노려보며, 멀찍이서 독수리처럼 맴돌았으니, 그들은 오직 더 강한 군대의 존재와 제지에 막혀 제 먹이로 내리꽂지 못할 뿐이었다. 몇몇은 정복된 종대들 사이로 떨어져 나와, 침울한 불만 속에 성큼성큼 거닐었으니, 움직이는 군중을 주의 깊게, 그러나 아직은 수동적으로 지켜보는 자들이었다.\n\n〈헤이워드〉를 선두로 한 선발대는 이미 협로에 다다라 천천히 사라져 가던 차에, 〈코라〉의 주의가 다툼의 소리에 이끌려 한 무리의 낙오자들에게로 쏠렸다. 한 무단이탈한 식민지 병사가 제 불복종의 대가를 치르고 있었으니, 다름 아니라 자기더러 대열에서 제자리를 버리게 만든 바로 그 물건들을 빼앗기는 중이었다. 그 사내는 힘센 골격을 지녔고, 너무 탐욕스러워 싸움 없이는 제 물건을 내놓으려 하지 않았다. 양쪽에서 사람들이 끼어들었으니, 한쪽은 그 약탈을 막으려, 다른 쪽은 거들려 했다. 목소리가 크고 성나게 높아졌고, 일 분 전만 해도 열두엇밖에 보이지 않던 곳에 마치 마법처럼 백여 명의 야만인이 나타났다. 바로 그때 〈코라〉는 〈마구아〉의 형체가 제 동족들 사이로 미끄러져 다니며, 그 치명적이고 교활한 능변으로 말하는 것을 보았다. 여인들과 아이들의 무리가 멈추어, 놀라 푸드덕거리는 새들처럼 한데 옹송그렸다. 그러나 그 인디언의 탐욕은 이내 채워졌고, 갈라졌던 무리는 다시 천천히 앞으로 움직였다.\n\n이제 야만인들은 물러서서, 더 이상의 방해 없이 적들이 나아가도록 내버려 두는 데 만족하는 듯했다. 그러나 여인들의 무리가 그들에게 다가가자, 한 숄의 화려한 빛깔이 거칠고 배운 데 없는 어느 휴런족의 눈을 끌었다. 그는 조금의 망설임도 없이 그것을 채려 다가갔다. 그 장식품을 아껴서라기보다 공포에 사로잡힌 그 여인은, 탐나는 그 물건으로 제 아이를 감싸, 둘 다 제 가슴에 더 꼭 끌어안았다. 〈코라〉가 그 여인에게 그 하찮은 것을 버리라고 일러 주려는 마음에서 막 입을 떼려던 차에, 야만인이 숄에 대한 손아귀를 놓고는, 비명을 지르는 그 갓난아이를 여인의 품에서 잡아챘다. 주위에 몰린 자들의 탐욕스러운 손아귀에 모든 것을 내맡긴 채, 그 어미는 광란이 어린 얼굴로 제 아이를 되찾으려 달려들었다. 인디언은 음산하게 웃으며, 기꺼이 맞바꾸겠다는 표시로 한 손을 내밀었고, 다른 손으로는 그 갓난것을 두 발을 잡아 머리 위로 흔들어, 마치 그 몸값을 부풀리려는 듯했다.\n\n\"여기, 여기, 거기, 전부, 무엇이든, 모조리!\" 숨이 턱에 찬 여인이, 잘못 겨눈 떨리는 손가락으로 제 몸에서 가벼운 옷가지들을 잡아 뜯으며 외쳤다. \"다 가져요, 허나 내 아기를 돌려줘요!\"\n\n야만인은 그 쓸모없는 헝겊들을 걷어찼고, 숄이 이미 다른 자의 전리품이 되어 버린 것을 알아채자, 그의 빈정대지만 침울하던 미소가 흉포함의 번뜩임으로 바뀌더니, 그 갓난아이의 머리를 바위에 내리쳐, 그 부들부들 떠는 잔해를 바로 그녀의 발치에 내던졌다. 한순간, 어미는, 절망의 석상처럼 서서, 그토록 얼마 전까지 제 가슴에 안겨 제 얼굴을 보며 미소 짓던 그 흉측해진 것을 미친 듯이 내려다보았다. 그러고는 두 눈과 얼굴을 하늘로 들어 올려, 마치 그 더러운 짓을 저지른 자를 저주해 달라고 하느님께 부르짖는 듯했다. 그녀는 그런 기도의 죄를 짓는 일만은 면했으니, 제 뜻이 어긋난 데 미쳐 날뛰고 피를 본 데 흥분한 그 휴런족이, 자비롭게도 제 토마호크를 그녀 자신의 머릿골에 박아 넣었기 때문이다. 어미는 그 일격에 쓰러져, 살아 있을 때 제 아이를 아끼게 만든 그 똑같은 골똘한 사랑으로, 죽음 속에서도 제 아이를 움켜쥐며 넘어갔다.\n\n그 위태로운 순간에 〈마구아〉가 두 손을 입에 갖다 대고, 그 치명적이고 소름 끼치는 함성을 올렸다. 흩어져 있던 인디언들이 그 익히 아는 외침에 흠칫했으니, 마치 출발 신호에 군마가 도약하듯 했고, 곧장 벌판을 따라, 숲의 궁륭을 가로질러, 일찍이 인간의 입에서 좀처럼 터져 나온 적 없는 함성이 솟았다. 그것을 들은 자들은 가슴이 얼어붙는 공포로 귀 기울였으니, 그것은 최후의 부름을 알리는 나팔 소리에 따르리라 예상되는 그 두려움에 못지않은 것이었다.\n\n이천이 넘는 광란하는 야만인들이 그 신호에 숲에서 쏟아져 나와, 본능적인 날쌤으로 그 숙명의 벌판을 가로질러 몸을 던졌다. 우리는 그 뒤를 이은 역겨운 참상을 길게 늘어놓지 않으련다. 죽음이 어디에나, 그것도 가장 끔찍하고 가장 혐오스러운 모습으로 있었다. 저항은 다만 그 살인자들을 더욱 격분시킬 뿐이었으니, 그들은 제 희생자들이 그 원한이 미칠 범위를 한참 벗어난 뒤에도 오래도록 사나운 일격을 퍼부었다. 피의 흐름은 급류의 터짐에 비길 만했으니, 원주민들이 그 광경에 달아오르고 미쳐 가면서, 그 가운데 많은 자가 심지어 땅에 무릎을 꿇고, 그 진홍의 물결을 마음껏, 의기양양하게, 지옥처럼 들이켰다.\n\n훈련된 군대의 본대는 재빨리 단단한 무리로 뭉쳐, 군사적 정면의 위압적인 외양으로 제 습격자들을 위협하려 애썼다. 그 시도는 어느 정도 성공했으나, 너무도 많은 자가 야만인들을 달래 보려는 헛된 희망에서 제 장전 안 된 머스킷총을 손에서 빼앗기고 말았다.\n\n이런 장면 속에서 그 덧없는 순간들을 헤아릴 겨를이 있는 자는 아무도 없었다. 자매가 한자리에 못 박힌 채, 공포에 질려, 거의 어쩔 도리 없이 서 있던 것이 십 분쯤이었을지도 모른다(한 세월처럼 느껴졌으나). 첫 일격이 가해졌을 때, 비명을 지르던 그들의 동행이 한 덩어리로 그들을 밀쳐 와 달아날 길을 막아 버렸고, 이제 공포나 죽음이 그들 주위에서 거의 모두를, 아니 전부를 흩어 놓은 지금, 적들의 토마호크로 이어지는 길 말고는 트인 통로라곤 보이지 않았다. 사방에서 비명과 신음, 호소와 저주가 솟았다. 이 순간 〈앨리스〉는 제 아버지의 거대한 형체가 프랑스군 방향으로 벌판을 빠르게 가로질러 가는 것을 얼핏 보았다. 그는 사실, 온갖 위험에도 아랑곳없이, 전에 자기가 조건으로 내걸었던 그 더딘 호위대를 요구하러 〈몽칼름〉에게 나아가는 중이었다. 번쩍이는 도끼와 미늘 달린 창 쉰 자루가 그의 목숨을 노려 들이밀어졌으나 거들떠보아지지 않았으니, 야만인들은 그 격분 속에서도 그의 신분과 침착함을 존중했다. 그 위험한 무기들은 노병의 여전히 힘찬 팔에 옆으로 쳐내어지거나, 아무도 감히 저지를 용기가 없는 듯한 행위를 위협하다가 저절로 떨어졌다. 다행히도, 복수에 불타는 〈마구아〉는 노병이 막 떠나온 바로 그 무리 속에서 제 제물을 찾고 있었다.\n\n\"아버지, 아버지, 우리 여기 있어요!\" 그가 그리 멀지 않은 거리를 그들을 알아채는 기색도 없이 지나갈 때, 〈앨리스〉가 비명을 질렀다. \"우리에게로 오세요, 아버지, 안 그러면 우리는 죽어요!\"\n\n그 외침은 돌의 심장도 녹였을 말과 어조로 거듭되었으나, 응답이 없었다. 과연 한 번, 노인이 그 소리를 알아챈 듯도 했으니, 그가 멈추어 귀 기울였다. 그러나 〈앨리스〉는 이미 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌고, 〈코라〉는 그 곁에 주저앉아, 지칠 줄 모르는 다정함으로 그 생기 없는 몸 위로 몸을 기울이고 있었다. 〈먼로〉는 실망에 고개를 가로젓고는, 제 직분의 드높은 임무를 향한 일념으로 나아갔다.\n\n\"부인.\" 무력하고 쓸모없는 처지였으나 아직 제 소임을 저버릴 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않은 〈거뮤트〉가 말했다. \"지금은 악마들의 환희의 잔치요, 여기는 기독교도가 머무를 마땅한 곳이 아니외다. 일어나 달아납시다.\"\n\n\"가세요.\" 여전히 정신 잃은 동생을 응시하며 〈코라〉가 말했다. \"당신 자신을 구하세요. 내게는 당신이 더 이상 쓸모가 없어요.\"\n\n〈데이비드〉는 그녀의 말에 곁들여진, 소박하나 절절한 몸짓으로, 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 결심의 성격을 알아챘다. 그는 한순간, 제 사방에서 지옥의 의식을 펼치고 있는 거무스름한 형체들을 응시했고, 그 큰 키가 한층 곧추섰으며, 가슴이 부풀어 오르고, 온갖 이목구비가 솟구쳐, 자기를 지배하는 그 감정의 힘으로 말을 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"〈유대〉 소년이 제 하프 소리와 성스러운 노래의 말씀으로 〈사울〉의 악령을 길들일 수 있었다면, 여기서 음악의 힘을 시험해 보는 것도 나쁘지 않으리라.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러고는 제 목소리를 가장 높은 음으로 끌어올려, 그 피비린내 나는 들판의 아우성 한가운데서도 들릴 만큼 힘찬 가락을 쏟아 냈다. 한 사람 이상의 야만인이 보호받지 못하는 자매에게서 그 옷을 약탈하고 머리 가죽을 벗겨 가려는 생각으로 그들에게 달려들었으나, 이 기이하고 미동 없는 형체가 제자리에 못 박혀 있는 것을 보고는 멈추어 귀를 기울였다. 놀라움은 이내 감탄으로 바뀌었고, 그들은 이 백인 전사가 제 죽음의 노래를 부르는 그 굳건함에 흡족함을 드러내 놓고 표하며, 더 만만하고 덜 용감한 다른 제물들에게로 지나갔다. 제 성공에 고무되고 또 현혹된 〈데이비드〉는, 자기가 그토록 성스럽다고 믿는 그 영향력을 넓히려 온 힘을 쏟았다. 그 흔치 않은 소리가 멀리 있던 한 야만인의 귀를 사로잡았으니, 그는 마치 천한 무리를 건드리기를 경멸하여 제 명성에 더 걸맞은 제물을 찾는 자처럼, 이 무리 저 무리로 격노하여 날아다녔다. 그것은 〈마구아〉였으니, 제 오랜 포로들이 다시 제 손아귀에 들어온 것을 보고 기쁨의 함성을 질렀다.\n\n\"이리 와.\" 그가 〈코라〉의 옷에 제 더러운 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"휴런족의 천막은 아직 열려 있다. 이곳보다 낫지 않으냐?\"\n\n\"저리 가!\" 〈코라〉가 그 역겨운 몰골에서 눈을 가리며 외쳤다.\n\n인디언은 김이 오르는 제 손을 들어 보이며 조롱하듯 웃고는 대답했다. \"붉구나, 허나 이건 흰 핏줄에서 나온 것이지!\"\n\n\"괴물! 너의 영혼에는 피가, 피의 바다가 있구나. 너의 혼령이 이 장면을 일으켰어.\"\n\n\"〈마구아〉는 위대한 추장이다!\" 의기양양한 야만인이 응수했다. \"검은 머리는 그의 부족에게 가겠느냐?\"\n\n\"결코! 칠 테면 쳐서, 너의 복수를 끝마쳐라.\"\n\n그는 한순간 머뭇거렸다. 그러더니 가볍고 정신 잃은 〈앨리스〉의 몸을 두 팔에 안아 들고, 교활한 인디언은 숲을 향해 벌판을 가로질러 빠르게 움직였다.\n\n\"멈춰!\" 〈코라〉가 미친 듯이 그 발자취를 좇으며 비명을 질렀다. \"그 아이를 놓아줘! 비열한 놈! 무슨 짓을 하는 거냐?\"\n\n그러나 〈마구아〉는 그녀의 목소리에 귀가 멀었다. 아니 그보다는, 제 힘을 알았기에, 그것을 지키기로 작정한 것이었다.\n\n\"멈춰요, 부인, 멈춰요.\" 〈거뮤트〉가 정신 나간 〈코라〉의 뒤를 향해 외쳤다. \"성스러운 주문이 차츰 통하기 시작하니, 이내 이 끔찍한 소동이 잠잠해지는 것을 보게 되리다.\"\n\n이번에는 제가 거들떠보아지지 않음을 알아챈 충직한 〈데이비드〉는, 다시 한번 성스러운 노래로 목소리를 높이고, 그 긴 팔로 가락에 맞추어 공기를 가르며 부지런히 반주하면서, 그 정신 나간 언니를 뒤따랐다. 이런 식으로 그들은 달아나는 자, 다친 자, 죽은 자들 사이를 지나 벌판을 가로질렀다. 그 사나운 휴런족은, 어느 때든 제 한 몸과 제가 떠멘 제물을 건사하기에 족했다. 다만 〈코라〉는 제 뒤를 성큼성큼 따르는 그 비범한 존재가 아니었다면 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 야만의 적들의 일격에 쓰러졌을 것이니, 그 존재는 이제 놀란 원주민들에게 광기의 수호령을 타고난 자처럼 보였다.\n\n더 급박한 위험을 피할 줄도, 또 추격을 따돌릴 줄도 알았던 〈마구아〉는, 낮은 협곡을 지나 숲으로 들어섰으니, 그곳에서 그는 얼마 전 그 여행자들이 버리고 간 〈나라간세트〉 말들이, 제 모습만큼이나 사납고 악의에 찬 표정의 한 야만인의 손에 맡겨진 채 제가 나타나기를 기다리고 있는 것을 이내 발견했다. 〈앨리스〉를 말 한 마리에 누인 다음, 그는 〈코라〉에게 다른 한 마리에 오르라는 표시를 했다.\n\n자기를 사로잡은 자의 존재가 자아낸 공포에도 불구하고, 벌판에서 벌어지는 그 피비린내 나는 장면에서 벗어나는 데에는 당장의 안도가 있었으니, 〈코라〉도 그것에 아주 무감할 수는 없었다. 그녀는 제 자리에 앉아, 휴런족조차 거절할 수 없을 만큼 간청과 사랑이 어린 태도로, 제 동생을 향해 두 팔을 내밀었다. 그리하여 〈앨리스〉를 〈코라〉와 같은 짐승에 태운 뒤, 그는 고삐를 잡고, 숲 속으로 더욱 깊이 뛰어들며 그 길을 나섰다. 〈데이비드〉는, 죽일 가치조차 없는 대상으로 철저히 무시당한 채 홀로 남겨진 것을 알아채고, 그들이 버려둔 그 짐승의 안장에 제 긴 다리를 걸치고는, 그 길의 어려움이 허락하는 만큼 추격에 나아갔다.\n\n그들은 곧 오르막을 오르기 시작했으나, 그 움직임이 동생의 잠든 의식을 되살리는 경향이 있었던 까닭에, 〈코라〉의 주의는 동생을 위한 더없이 애틋한 염려와, 벌판에서 아직도 너무나 또렷이 들려오는 비명에 귀 기울이는 일 사이로 너무 갈라져, 자기들이 어느 방향으로 가는지를 살필 겨를이 없었다. 그러나 산마루의 평평한 면에 다다라 동쪽 벼랑에 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 일찍이 그 정찰병의 더 다정한 안내 아래 한 차례 인도되었던 그 장소를 알아보았다. 여기서 〈마구아〉는 그들을 말에서 내리게 했고, 자기들이 사로잡힌 처지였음에도, 공포와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 듯한 그 호기심이 그들을 부추겨, 아래편의 그 메스꺼운 광경을 응시하게 했다.\n\n그 잔혹한 짓은 여전히 멎지 않았다. 사방에서 사로잡힌 자들이 그 무자비한 박해자들 앞에서 달아나고 있었으나, 기독교도 왕의 무장한 종대들은 일찍이 설명된 적 없는, 그리고 그렇지 않았더라면 흠 없었을 그 지휘관의 문장(紋章)에 지울 수 없는 오점을 남긴 그 무관심 속에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 죽음의 검은 탐욕이 복수를 압도하기 전까지는 멎지 않았다. 그제야 비로소 부상자들의 비명과 그 살인자들의 함성이 차츰 뜸해지더니, 마침내 그 공포의 외침이 그들의 귀에서 사라지거나, 의기양양한 야만인들의 크고, 길고, 째지는 함성에 묻혀 버렸다.\n\"이런들 어떠하리.\n\n원한다면 명예로운 살인자라 불러도 좋소.\n증오에서 한 일은 하나도 없고, 모두 명예에서 한 일이니.\"\n\n〈오셀로〉\n\n앞 장에서 묘사라기보다 그저 스치듯 언급된 그 피비린내 나고 비인간적인 장면은, \"〈윌리엄 헨리〉 학살\"이라는 마땅한 이름으로 식민지 역사의 페이지에 또렷이 남아 있다. 그 일은, 앞서 일어난 매우 흡사한 사건이 프랑스 지휘관의 명성에 남긴 오점을 한층 짙게 했으니, 그의 이르고도 영광스러운 죽음으로도 그 얼룩은 온전히 지워지지 못했다. 이제 그것은 세월에 가려져 가고 있으며, 〈몽칼름〉이 〈에이브러햄〉 평원에서 영웅처럼 죽었음을 아는 수천의 사람들도, 그가 그것 없이는 어느 누구도 진정 위대해질 수 없는 그 도덕적 용기를 얼마나 결여했는지는 아직 배워야 할 처지다. 이 빛나는 본보기로부터, 인간의 탁월함이 지닌 결함을 입증하기 위해, 그리하여 너그러운 정서와 높은 예의와 기사다운 용기가 이기심이라는 차디찬 마름병 아래서 그 영향력을 잃기가 얼마나 쉬운지를 보이기 위해, 또한 성품의 온갖 사소한 자질에서는 위대하였으나, 신념이 정략보다 얼마나 우월한지를 입증해야 할 때에 이르러서는 모자란 자로 드러난 한 인물을 세상에 내보이기 위해, 여러 페이지를 쓸 수도 있으리라. 그러나 그 일은 우리의 권한을 넘어서는 것이며, 또한 역사란, 사랑과 마찬가지로, 제 영웅들을 상상의 광휘로 에워싸기를 그토록 즐기니, 아마도 〈루이 드 생베랑〉은 후세에 의해 그저 제 조국의 용맹한 수호자로만 비칠 것이요, 〈오스위고〉와 〈호리컨〉의 기슭에서 보인 그 잔혹한 무관심은 잊히고 말 것이다. 자매 격인 한 뮤즈의 이러한 약점을 깊이 애석해하면서, 우리는 곧 그 신성한 경내에서 물러나, 우리 자신의 미천한 소임이 지닌 본래의 한계 안으로 돌아가려 한다.\n\n요새가 함락된 지 사흘째 되는 날이 저물어 가고 있었으나, 이야기의 진행상 독자를 아직 \"거룩한 호수\"의 기슭에 붙들어 두어야겠다. 마지막으로 보았을 때, 진지의 주변은 폭력과 소란으로 가득했다. 이제 그곳을 차지한 것은 정적과 죽음이었다. 피로 물든 정복자들은 떠났고, 얼마 전까지만 해도 승리한 군대의 흥겨운 환호로 울리던 그들의 진영은, 말없이 버려진 오두막들의 도시로 누워 있었다. 요새는 연기 자욱한 폐허였으니, 새까맣게 탄 서까래와 터져 나간 대포의 파편과 갈라진 석조물이 그 흙무더기를 어수선한 무질서 속에 뒤덮고 있었다.\n\n계절에도 무서운 변화가 일어나 있었다. 해는 그 온기를 도무지 뚫을 수 없는 수증기 더미 뒤로 감추었고, 8월의 사나운 더위 아래 검게 그을렸던 수백의 사람 형상이, 때 이른 11월의 돌풍 앞에 그 일그러진 모습 그대로 뻣뻣이 굳어 가고 있었다. 한때 북녘 언덕 위로 떠가던, 둥글게 말려 오르며 한 점 티 없던 안개는, 이제 폭풍의 광포함에 떠밀려 끝없이 이어지는 어두운 휘장이 되어 되돌아오고 있었다. 〈호리컨〉의 빽빽이 들어찼던 거울 면은 사라졌고, 그 자리에서 푸르고 성난 물결이, 마치 제 더러움을 더럽혀진 기슭에 분연히 되던지기라도 하듯, 강기슭을 후려치고 있었다. 그래도 그 맑은 샘은 마법 같은 영향력을 조금은 간직하고 있었으나, 이제 그것이 비추는 것이라곤 머리 위로 짓눌러 오는 하늘에서 내려앉은 음울한 어둠뿐이었다. 흔히 그 풍경을 꾸며, 그 거친 모습을 가리고 그 험한 윤곽을 부드럽게 해 주던 그 축축하고 정겨운 대기는 사라졌고, 북녘 바람이 물의 황무지를 가로질러 너무도 매섭고 거르지 않은 채 쏟아지니, 눈으로 헤아릴 것도, 상상으로 빚어낼 것도 하나 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n더 사나운 기운이 평원의 푸르름을 베어 버려, 그곳은 마치 모든 것을 태우는 번개에 그을린 듯이 보였다. 그러나 군데군데, 그 황량함 한가운데서 짙푸른 풀 더미가 솟아 있었으니, 사람의 피로 기름지게 된 토양이 맺은 그 첫 열매였다. 너그러운 빛 아래, 정겨운 기온 속에서 보면 그토록 사랑스럽던 그 온 풍경이, 이제는 어떤 그림 같은 삶의 우화처럼 보였으니, 그 안에서 사물들은 가장 거칠지만 가장 참된 색채로, 그 어떤 그늘의 완화도 없이 늘어서 있었다.\n\n외롭고 메마른 풀잎들은 스쳐 지나는 돌풍에 무섭도록 또렷이 일렁였고, 우뚝 솟은 바위투성이 산들은 그 헐벗음 속에 지나치게 뚜렷했으며, 눈은 헛되이나마 위안을 구하여 하늘의 끝없는 허공을 꿰뚫어 보려 했으나, 그 시선은 누덕누덕 휘몰아치는 안개의 어두운 휘장에 가로막혔다.\n\n바람은 고르지 않게 불었으니, 어떤 때는 땅을 따라 무겁게 휩쓸어 죽은 자들의 차가운 귀에 제 신음을 속삭이는 듯하다가, 또 어떤 때는 날카롭고 구슬픈 휘파람 소리로 높아지며, 그 길에 흩뿌린 잎과 가지로 허공을 가득 채우는 돌풍과 함께 숲으로 밀려들었다. 그 부자연스러운 소나기 속에서, 굶주린 까마귀 몇 마리가 강풍과 싸우고 있었으나, 발아래 펼쳐진 숲의 푸른 바다를 지나자마자 곧 흡족하게 아무 데나 내려앉아 그 끔찍한 잔치를 벌였다.\n\n요컨대, 그것은 황량함과 폐허의 풍경이었으니, 그곳에 불경하게 발을 들인 모든 자가 죽음의 가차 없는 팔에 단번에 베인 듯 보였다. 그러나 그 금제는 이제 풀렸다. 그리하여 그 풍경을 흉하게 만드는 데 한몫한 그 추악한 짓들을 저지른 자들이 떠난 이래 처음으로, 살아 있는 사람들이 이제 감히 그곳에 다가서고 있었다.\n\n이미 말한 그날, 해가 지기 한 시간쯤 전에, 다섯 사내의 모습이, 〈허드슨〉강으로 가는 길이 숲으로 들어서는 그 좁은 나무들의 통로에서 빠져나와, 허물어진 진지 쪽으로 나아가는 것이 보였을 법하다. 처음에 그들의 발걸음은 더디고 조심스러웠으니, 마치 그곳의 참상 속으로 들어서기를 꺼리거나, 그 무시무시한 사건들이 되풀이될까 두려워하는 듯했다. 한 날렵한 형상이 일행의 앞장을 섰으니, 그 신중함과 민첩함이 토박이의 것이었다. 그는 정찰하려 둔덕마다 오르고, 가장 분별 있다 여겨지는 길을 손짓으로 동료들에게 일러 주었다. 뒤따르는 자들 또한 숲의 전투에서 알려진 온갖 조심성과 선견을 갖추고 있었다. 그들 가운데 한 사람, 그 역시 인디언이었는데, 한쪽 측면으로 조금 떨어져 움직이며, 아주 작은 위험의 기미라도 읽어 내는 데 오래 익숙해진 눈으로 숲의 가장자리를 살폈다. 나머지 셋은 백인이었으나, 그 옷차림은 빛깔과 재질 모두에서 지금의 위험한 추격에 — 황야에서 물러가는 군대의 꽁무니에 매달리는 일에 — 알맞은 것이었다.\n\n호수 기슭으로 가는 그들의 길에 끊임없이 떠오르는 소름 끼치는 광경이 자아내는 영향은, 일행을 이룬 각 개인의 성품만큼이나 제각각이었다. 앞장선 젊은이는 평원을 가볍게 건너며, 처참하게 난도질당한 희생자들에게 진지하면서도 은밀한 눈길을 던졌으니, 제 감정을 드러내기를 두려워하면서도, 그 갑작스럽고 강력한 영향을 온전히 억누르기에는 아직 너무 미숙했다. 그러나 그의 붉은 살갗 동료는 그러한 약점을 넘어서 있었다. 그는 죽은 자들의 무리를 흔들림 없는 결의로, 그리고 오래고 뿌리 깊은 단련이 아니고서는 도무지 지킬 수 없을 만큼 고요한 눈으로 지나쳐 갔다. 백인들의 마음에 일어난 감정마저 제각각이었으되, 한결같이 비통했다. 그중 하나는, 그 희끗한 머리칼과 주름진 이목구비가 군인다운 기품과 걸음걸이와 어우러져, 사냥꾼 차림으로 변장했음에도 전쟁의 장면들에 오래 단련된 사람임을 드러냈는데, 유달리 끔찍한 광경이 그의 눈에 들어올 때마다 큰 소리로 신음하기를 부끄러워하지 않았다. 그 곁의 젊은이는 몸서리쳤으나, 동료를 배려하는 마음에서 제 감정을 억누르는 듯했다. 그들 모두 가운데, 맨 뒤에서 따라오는 낙오자만이 홀로, 남의 눈도, 뒤탈도 두려워하지 않고 제 속내를 고스란히 드러내는 듯했다. 그는 더없이 소름 끼치는 광경을 흔들릴 줄 모르는 눈과 근육으로 응시하면서도, 너무나 쓰라리고 깊은 저주를 내뱉어, 적들의 죄악을 얼마나 규탄하는지를 여실히 드러냈다.\n\n독자는 이 저마다의 인물들 속에서, 곧 〈모히칸〉 부자(父子)와 그들의 백인 친구인 정찰꾼, 그리고 〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉를 단번에 알아볼 것이다. 그것은 실로, 제 아이들을 찾아 나선 아비와, 그 아이들의 안위에 그토록 깊은 이해가 걸린 젊은이, 그리고 이미 그 험난한 장면들을 통해 제 솜씨와 충직함을 증명해 보인 용감하고 믿음직한 숲 사람들이었다.\n\n앞서 움직이던 〈언카스〉가 평원의 한가운데에 이르렀을 때, 그는 동료들을 한데 끌어모으는 외침을 질렀다. 젊은 전사는 한 무더기로 누운 여인들의 무리, 곧 죽은 자들이 뒤엉킨 더미 위에 멈춰 서 있었다. 그 광경의 역겨운 참혹함에도 아랑곳없이, 〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉는 그 썩어 가는 더미를 향해 달려가, 그 어떤 흉한 모습으로도 꺼뜨릴 수 없는 사랑으로, 그들이 찾는 이들의 흔적이 그 갈가리 찢긴 갖가지 빛깔의 옷가지 사이에 보이지 않는지 살피려 애썼다. 아비와 연인은 그 수색에서 곧바로 위안을 얻었으나, 둘 모두 다시금, 더없이 역겨운 진실 못지않게 견디기 힘든 불확실함의 고통을 겪을 운명에 처해 있었다. 그들이 그 서글픈 더미를 둘러싸고 말없이 생각에 잠겨 서 있을 때, 정찰꾼이 다가왔다. 그 우람한 숲 사람은 성난 얼굴로 그 슬픈 광경을 노려보더니, 평원에 들어선 이래 처음으로, 또렷이 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"나는 끔찍한 싸움터를 숱하게 겪었고, 피의 자취를 여러 마일 따라가 보기도 했소.\" 그가 말했다. \"허나 여기서 보이는 것처럼 악마의 손길이 그토록 또렷한 데는 본 적이 없소! 복수는 인디언의 정서요, 나를 아는 이라면 누구나 내 핏줄엔 잡것이 섞이지 않았음을 아오. 허나 이것만은 말하겠소 — 여기, 하늘을 마주하고, 이 울부짖는 황야에 주님의 권능이 이토록 뚜렷이 드러난 가운데서, 만일 저 프랑스 놈들이 다시 한번 누더기 같은 총알 사정거리 안에 제 몸을 들이밀기만 한다면, 부싯돌이 불을 일으키고 화약이 타는 한 제 몫을 다할 소총이 한 자루 있다는 것을! 도끼와 칼은 그것을 쓸 타고난 재주를 지닌 자들에게 맡기겠소. 어찌 생각하오, 〈칭가치국〉?\" 그가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 덧붙였다. \"깊은 눈이 내릴 적에, 〈휴런〉 놈들이 제 여인들에게 이 일을 떠벌리게 두겠소?\"\n\n원한의 빛이 〈모히칸〉 추장의 검은 이목구비를 스쳤다. 그는 칼집에 든 칼을 느슨하게 풀었다. 그러더니 그 광경에서 차분히 몸을 돌려, 격정의 충동이라곤 알지도 못하는 사람처럼 그 얼굴은 깊은 평정 속에 가라앉았다.\n\n\"〈몽칼름〉! 〈몽칼름〉!\" 깊은 원한에 찼으되 절제가 덜한 정찰꾼이 말을 이었다. \"사람들이 말하기를, 육신으로 행한 모든 짓이 단 한 번의 눈길에 드러날 때가 반드시 오고, 그것도 인간의 허약함에서 벗어난 눈으로 보게 된다고 하오. 영혼에 심판이 매달린 채 이 평원을 바라보도록 태어난 가엾은 자에게 화가 있으리라! 하 — 내가 백인의 피를 지닌 사람인 것이 분명하듯, 저기 한 붉은 살갗이 누워 있구려, 자연이 머리털을 박아 둔 자리에 그 한 올도 없이! 저자를 살펴보오, 〈델라웨어〉. 어쩌면 그대의 잃어버린 사람들 중 하나일지도 모르니, 의젓한 전사답게 묻어 주어야 마땅하오. 그대 눈에서 보이는구려, 〈사가모어〉. 가을바람이 피 냄새를 날려 버리기 전에, 〈휴런〉 놈 하나가 이 값을 치를 것이오!\"\n\n〈칭가치국〉이 그 난도질당한 시신에 다가가 뒤집어 보니, 거기서 그 여섯 동맹 부족, 곧 그들이 불리던 이름으로는 여섯 민족 가운데 하나임을 알리는 표지가 나왔는데, 그들은 〈영국〉 대열에서 싸우면서도 그 자신의 겨레에게는 그토록 지독히 적대적인 자들이었다. 그는 그 역겨운 물체를 발로 걷어차더니, 짐승의 주검을 떠나기라도 하듯 똑같은 무관심으로 거기서 돌아섰다. 정찰꾼은 그 행동의 뜻을 알아챘으나, 그는 자못 신중하게 제 갈 길을 가면서도, 같은 원한 어린 어조로 프랑스 지휘관을 향한 규탄을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"오로지 무한한 지혜와 무한한 권능만이 감히 사람들을 무더기로 쓸어버릴 수 있는 법이오.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"심판의 필요를 알 수 있는 것은 오직 그 하나뿐이며, 그 다른 하나가 아니고서야 주님의 피조물을 무엇으로 되돌려 놓을 수 있겠소? 나는 행군이 앞에 있거나 매복이 예정된 게 아니라면, 첫째 사슴을 다 먹기도 전에 둘째 사슴을 잡는 것을 죄로 여기오. 트인 곳에서 거친 싸움을 벌이는 몇몇 전사들 사이의 일은 또 다른 문제니, 그들이 백인이든 붉은 살갗이든 제 천성이 어떠하냐에 따라 소총이나 도끼를 손에 쥔 채 죽는 것이 그들의 타고난 몫인 까닭이오. 〈언카스〉, 이리 오너라, 얘야, 까마귀가 저 〈밍고〉 위에 내려앉게 두어라. 나는 자주 본 터라, 그놈들이 〈오나이다〉의 살을 탐낸다는 것을 알고 있으니, 그 새가 제 타고난 식욕이 시키는 대로 따르게 두는 것도 괜찮은 일이지.\"\n\n\"흐!\" 젊은 〈모히칸〉이 발끝으로 곧추서며 외쳤으니, 제 앞을 뚫어지게 바라보면서, 그 소리와 동작으로 까마귀를 쫓아 다른 먹잇감으로 보내 버렸다.\n\n\"무엇이냐, 얘야?\" 정찰꾼이 막 도약하려는 표범처럼 그 큰 키를 웅크리며 속삭였다. \"노략질하러 어슬렁대는 굼뜬 프랑스 놈이기를 신이여 보내 주소서. 오늘은 '킬디어'가 흔치 않게 멀리까지 날아갈 듯하구나!\"\n\n〈언카스〉는 아무 대꾸도 않고 그 자리에서 뛰쳐나갔으니, 다음 순간 그는 한 덤불에서 무언가를 뜯어내, 〈코라〉의 푸른 승마용 베일 조각을 의기양양하게 흔드는 모습으로 보였다. 그 동작과, 그 내보임과, 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 입에서 또다시 터져 나온 그 외침이, 곧 일행 전부를 그의 주위로 끌어모았다.\n\n\"내 아이!\" 〈먼로〉가 빠르고 격하게 말했다. \"내 아이를 다오!\"\n\n\"〈언카스〉가 애써 보겠습니다.\" 그 짧고도 가슴 저미는 대답이었다.\n\n그 소박하나 뜻 깊은 다짐은 아비에게 가닿지 못했으니, 그는 그 망사 조각을 움켜쥐어 손안에 구겨 넣으면서, 마치 덤불이 드러낼지 모를 비밀을 두려워하면서도 동시에 바라기라도 하듯, 그 눈은 겁먹은 채 덤불 사이를 헤맸다.\n\n\"여기엔 죽은 자가 없소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"폭풍은 이쪽으로는 지나지 않은 듯하오.\"\n\n\"그건 분명하오, 우리 머리 위 하늘보다도 더 분명하지.\" 동요 없는 정찰꾼이 대답했다. \"허나 그 아가씨든, 아니면 그녀를 빼앗아 간 자들이든, 이 덤불을 지난 거요. 모두가 바라보길 좋아하던 그 얼굴을 가리려고 그녀가 둘렀던 천이 기억나니까. 〈언카스〉, 네 말이 옳다. 검은 머리 아가씨가 여기 있었고, 겁먹은 새끼 사슴처럼 숲으로 달아난 것이다. 달아날 수 있는 자라면 누구도 죽임을 당하려고 남아 있진 않았을 테니까. 그녀가 남긴 자취를 찾아보자. 인디언의 눈에는, 벌새조차 허공에 제 자취를 남긴다고 나는 이따금 생각하니 말이다.\"\n\n젊은 〈모히칸〉은 그 말에 쏜살같이 달려 나갔고, 정찰꾼이 미처 말을 끝내기도 전에, 그가 숲 가장자리에서 성공을 알리는 외침을 질렀다. 그 자리에 이르자, 애타는 일행은 너도밤나무의 낮은 가지에서 펄럭이는 또 다른 베일 조각을 알아보았다.\n\n\"가만, 가만.\" 정찰꾼이 다급한 〈헤이워드〉 앞으로 제 긴 소총을 뻗으며 말했다. \"이제 우리가 할 일은 알았소만, 이 자취의 아름다움을 망쳐서는 안 되오. 한 걸음만 너무 일러도 우리에게 몇 시간의 수고를 안길 수 있소. 그래도 우린 놈들을 잡은 거요. 그것만은 부인할 수 없소.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 고맙소, 훌륭한 양반!\" 〈먼로〉가 외쳤다. \"그럼, 그 애들이 어디로 달아났소, 내 자식들은 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"그들이 택한 길은 여러 우연에 달려 있소. 만일 그들끼리만 갔다면, 똑바로 가기보다 빙 둘러 돌았을 공산도 그만큼 크니, 우리에게서 십여 마일 안에 있을 수도 있소. 허나 〈휴런〉 놈들이나 프랑스 편 인디언들이 손을 댔다면, 지금쯤 〈캐나다〉 변경 가까이에 있을 게요. 허나 그게 무슨 상관이겠소?\" 신중한 정찰꾼은 듣는 이들이 드러내는 강렬한 불안과 실망을 살피며 말을 이었다. \"여기 〈모히칸〉 부자와 내가 이 자취의 한쪽 끝에 있으니, 믿으시오, 비록 그들이 백 리그쯤 떨어져 있다 한들 우리는 그 다른 끝을 찾아낼 것이오! 천천히, 천천히, 〈언카스〉, 너는 정착지 사람처럼 성마르구나. 가벼운 발은 희미한 자국밖에 남기지 않는다는 걸 잊었느냐!\"\n\n\"흐!\" 〈칭가치국〉이 외쳤으니, 그는 숲 가장자리를 두른 나지막한 덤불을 분명히 헤집고 난 틈을 살피는 데 골몰해 있다가, 이제 똑바로 일어서서, 역겨운 뱀이라도 본 사람의 자세와 기색으로 아래를 가리켰다.\n\n\"여기 사람 발자국이 또렷하게 찍혀 있소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 그 가리킨 자리 위로 몸을 굽히며 외쳤다. \"이 웅덩이 가장자리를 밟았으니, 자국을 잘못 볼 리 없소. 그들은 포로가 된 거요.\"\n\n\"황야에서 굶어 죽도록 버려진 것보다야 낫소.\" 정찰꾼이 대답했다. \"게다가 더 넓은 자취를 남길 테니. 한 달 안에 〈모히칸〉 부자와 내가 그들의 천막에 들어선다는 데, 비버 가죽 쉰 장을 부싯돌 쉰 개에 걸어도 좋소! 거기에 몸을 굽혀라, 〈언카스〉, 그 모카신으로 무엇을 알아낼 수 있는지 보아라. 그건 분명 모카신이지 신발이 아니니 말이다.\"\n\n젊은 〈모히칸〉이 그 자국 위로 몸을 굽혀, 그 둘레에 흩어진 잎들을 치우고는, 요즘처럼 금전이 의심스러운 시절에 환전상이 미심쩍은 어음을 들여다볼 법한 그 면밀한 눈길로 그것을 살폈다. 마침내 그는 검사 결과에 흡족해하며 무릎을 펴고 일어섰다.\n\n\"자, 얘야,\" 주의 깊은 정찰꾼이 물었다. \"뭐라고 하느냐? 그 일러바치는 자국에서 무얼 알아냈느냐?\"\n\n\"〈르 르나르 스뷔틸〉!\"\n\n\"하! 그 날뛰는 악마가 또로구나! '킬디어'가 그놈에게 다정한 한마디를 건네기 전에는, 그놈의 어슬렁거림은 결코 끝나지 않을 게다.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 마지못해 그 소식이 사실임을 인정했으나, 이제 제 의심보다는 차라리 희망을 드러내며 말했다.\n\n\"모카신이란 게 어느 것이나 너무 비슷하니, 아마 무슨 착오가 있는 게요.\"\n\n\"모카신이 어느 것이나 비슷하다고! 그럼 발이란 게 어느 것이나 비슷하다고 해도 되겠구려. 허나 우리 모두 알다시피, 어떤 것은 길고 어떤 것은 짧으며, 어떤 것은 넓고 어떤 것은 좁고, 어떤 것은 발등이 높고 어떤 것은 낮으며, 어떤 것은 안짱발이고 어떤 것은 밭장발이오. 모카신이 다른 모카신과 닮은 정도는 책 한 권이 다른 책과 닮은 정도와 다를 바 없소. 허나 한 권을 읽을 줄 아는 자라도 다른 한 권의 자국은 좀처럼 읽어 내지 못하는 법이지. 그게 다 가장 좋도록 마련된 것이니, 사람마다 제 타고난 이점을 주는 게요. 내가 거기에 몸을 굽혀 보겠다, 〈언카스〉. 책이든 모카신이든, 의견이 하나가 아니라 둘이라고 해서 나빠질 게 없으니.\" 정찰꾼은 그 일에 몸을 굽혔다가, 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"네 말이 옳다, 얘야. 지난 추격에서 그토록 자주 보았던 그 덧댄 자국이 여기 있구나. 그리고 이놈은 기회만 있으면 술을 마실 게다. 술 마시는 인디언은 으레 타고난 야만인보다 발끝을 더 벌리고 걷는 법을 익히는데, 백인이든 붉은 살갗이든 가랑이를 벌리고 걷는 것은 술꾼의 천성이니까. 길이며 너비도 딱 그대로다! 보시오, 〈사가모어〉. 우리가 〈글렌〉의 폭포에서 약수터까지 그 해충들을 쫓을 때, 그대가 이 자국을 한두 번 잰 게 아니지 않소.\"\n\n〈칭가치국〉은 그대로 따랐다. 그리고 짧은 검사를 마치자 일어서서, 차분한 태도로 그저 한마디를 내뱉었다.\n\n\"〈마구아〉!\"\n\n\"그렇소, 이제 확실해졌소. 그렇다면 여기를 검은 머리 아가씨와 〈마구아〉가 지난 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 〈앨리스〉는 아니란 말이오?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"그 아가씨의 자취는 아직 보지 못했소.\" 정찰꾼이 나무며 덤불이며 땅을 면밀히 둘러보며 대답했다. \"저긴 또 뭐냐? 〈언카스〉, 저쪽 가시덤불에 매달려 흔들리는 저것을 이리 가져오너라.\"\n\n인디언이 그대로 따르자, 정찰꾼은 그 노획물을 받아 들어 높이 쳐들고는, 소리는 없으나 진심에서 우러난 그만의 방식으로 웃었다.\n\n\"그 노래꾼의 나팔 부는 무기로구나! 이제 사제라도 따라갈 만한 자취를 얻겠소.\" 그가 말했다. \"〈언카스〉, 비틀거리는 6피트 2인치 인간의 몸뚱이를 떠받칠 만큼 긴 신발 자국을 찾아보아라. 그자가 꽥꽥대기를 그만두고 무언가 더 나은 일을 좇았으니, 이제 그놈에게 좀 기대를 걸어 볼 만하구나.\"\n\n\"적어도 제 소임에는 충실했던 게요.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉에게 벗이 없지는 않은 셈이오.\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 〈호크아이〉가 소총을 내려놓고, 눈에 띄는 경멸의 기색으로 거기에 몸을 기대며 말했다. \"그자가 그들을 위해 노래는 불러 줄 거요. 헌데 그들의 끼니로 사슴을 잡을 수 있겠소? 너도밤나무의 이끼를 보고 길을 짚거나, 〈휴런〉 놈의 목을 벨 수 있겠소? 그러지 못한다면, 그자가 처음 만나는 개똥지빠귀가 둘 중 더 영리한 셈이오. 자, 얘야, 그런 발판의 흔적이라도 좀 보이느냐?\"\n\n\"여기 신발을 신은 자의 발자국 같은 게 있는데, 우리 벗의 것일 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"잎을 가만히 건드려라, 안 그러면 형태를 망친다. 저거! 저건 발자국이긴 하다만, 검은 머리 아가씨의 것이다. 게다가 그토록 고귀한 키와 당당한 풍모를 지닌 이치고는 작기도 하구나. 그 노래꾼이라면 제 발뒤꿈치 하나로 다 덮어 버릴 게다.\"\n\n\"어디! 내 아이의 발자국을 보게 해 주오.\" 〈먼로〉가 덤불을 옆으로 밀치며, 거의 지워진 그 자국 위로 다정히 몸을 굽히며 말했다. 자국을 남긴 그 걸음이 가볍고 빨랐음에도, 그것은 아직 또렷이 보였다. 늙은 군인은 바라볼수록 흐려지는 눈으로 그것을 살폈으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 보기에 그가 딸이 지나간 자취를 뜨거운 눈물로 적실 때까지도 그 굽힌 자세에서 일어서지 않았다. 매 순간 겉치레의 자제를 뚫고 터져 나올 듯한 그 비통을 달래고자, 노병에게 무언가 할 일을 주려고, 젊은이는 정찰꾼에게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 이 틀림없는 자취를 손에 넣었으니, 행군을 시작합시다. 이런 때엔 한순간이 포로들에게 영겁처럼 느껴질 게요.\"\n\n\"가장 빨리 뛰는 사슴이 가장 긴 추격을 안기는 건 아니오.\" 〈호크아이〉가 제 눈에 들어온 갖가지 자국에서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 대답했다. \"우리는 그 날뛰는 〈휴런〉이 지났음을 알고, 검은 머리 아가씨도, 노래꾼도 그러함을 아오. 허나 노란 머리에 푸른 눈을 한 그 아가씨는 어디 있소? 비록 자그마하고, 제 언니만큼 담대하지는 못해도, 보기에 곱고 말씨도 정겨운 아가씨인데. 아무도 돌보아 주지 않다니, 그 아가씨에겐 벗이 없단 말이오?\"\n\n\"하늘이 막아 주소서, 그 애에게 어찌 수백의 벗이 없겠소! 우리가 지금 그 애를 찾아 나선 게 아니오? 나로 말하면, 그 애를 찾을 때까지 결코 수색을 그치지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우리는 서로 다른 길로 가야 할지도 모르겠소. 그 발걸음이 아무리 가볍고 작다 한들, 여기를 그 아가씨가 지나지는 않았으니.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 물러섰으니, 나아가려던 그 모든 열의가 그 순간 사라지는 듯했다. 다른 이의 기분에 일어난 그 갑작스러운 변화에는 아랑곳없이, 정찰꾼은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이 황야에서 그런 자국을 남길 만한 여인은, 검은 머리 아가씨나 그 동생밖에 없소. 첫째가 여기 있었음은 알겠는데, 다른 하나의 자취는 어디 있단 말이오? 이 자취를 더 깊이 좇아 보다가, 아무것도 나오지 않으면, 우리는 평원으로 되돌아가 다른 냄새를 짚어야 하오. 나아가라, 〈언카스〉, 마른 잎에서 눈을 떼지 마라. 나는 덤불을 살필 테고, 네 아비는 코를 땅에 낮추고 달릴 것이다. 나아갑시다, 벗들이여. 해가 언덕 뒤로 넘어가고 있소.\"\n\n\"내가 할 수 있는 일은 없소?\" 애타는 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"당신 말이오!\" 정찰꾼이 되받았으니, 그는 이미 제 붉은 살갗 친구들과 함께 일러 둔 순서대로 나아가는 중이었다. \"그렇소, 당신은 우리 뒤에 바싹 붙어, 자취를 가로지르지 않도록 조심하면 되오.\"\n\n그들이 몇 로드도 채 나아가기 전에, 인디언들이 멈춰 서더니, 땅 위의 어떤 자국을 평소보다 더한 예리함으로 들여다보는 듯했다. 아비와 아들 둘 다 빠르고 큰 소리로 말하며, 함께 감탄하는 그 대상을 보다가, 또 더없이 분명한 기쁨으로 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그 작은 발을 찾아냈소!\" 정찰꾼이, 제 몫의 임무는 더 살피지도 않고 앞으로 나아가며 외쳤다. \"여기 또 뭐가 있나? 이 자리에 매복이 펼쳐졌었나? 아니, 변경에서 가장 정직한 소총을 두고 맹세컨대, 여기 또 그 한쪽으로 기운 말들이 있었구나! 이제 비밀이 죄다 드러났으니, 한밤중의 북극성처럼 환하게 밝혀졌소. 그렇소, 여기서 그들이 말에 올랐소. 저기엔 짐승들이 어린나무에 매여 기다리고 있었고, 저쪽으로는 넓은 길이 북쪽으로, 〈캐나다〉를 향해 곧장 내달리고 있소.\"\n\n\"허나 여전히 〈앨리스〉의, 〈먼로〉 양 가운데 동생의 자취는 없소.\" 던컨이 말했다.\n\n\"〈언카스〉가 방금 땅에서 집어 든 저 반짝이는 패물이 그 자취가 아니라면 말이오. 이리 넘겨라, 얘야, 우리가 그걸 좀 보게.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 그것이 〈앨리스〉가 즐겨 차던 장신구임을 단번에 알아보았으니, 그는 연인 특유의 끈질긴 기억으로, 그 학살의 운명적인 아침에 그것이 사랑하는 이의 고운 목에 매달려 있던 것을 보았음을 떠올렸다. 그가 그 더없이 소중한 보석을 움켜쥐었다. 그리고 그가 그 사실을 알릴 무렵, 그것은 어리둥절한 정찰꾼의 눈앞에서 사라져 버렸으니, 정찰꾼은 그것이 던컨의 두근거리는 가슴에 따뜻이 눌린 지 한참이 지나도록 헛되이 땅에서 그것을 찾았다.\n\n\"쳇!\" 실망한 〈호크아이〉가 소총 개머리판으로 잎을 헤집기를 그만두며 말했다. \"시력이 약해지기 시작하면 그게 다 나이 든 증거지. 그토록 반짝이는 노리개를 못 보다니! 자, 그래도 나는 흐린 총신을 따라 곁눈질할 수는 있으니, 그것만으로도 나와 〈밍고〉 놈들 사이의 다툼은 죄다 가릴 수 있소. 나도 그 물건을 찾았으면 했는데, 그건 그저 임자에게 돌려주려는 것뿐이오. 그렇게 하면 내가 긴 자취라 부르는 것의 양 끝을 한데 잇는 셈이 되니까. 헌데 이쯤이면 넓은 〈세인트로렌스〉강이, 어쩌면 큰 호수들 자체가 우리 사이를 가로막고 있을 게요.\"\n\n\"그러니 더더욱 우리가 행군을 미뤄선 안 될 까닭이오.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 대답했다. \"어서 갑시다.\"\n\n\"젊은 피와 뜨거운 피는 매한가지라고들 하지. 우리가 다람쥐 사냥을 떠나거나 사슴 한 마리를 〈호리컨〉으로 몰려는 게 아니라, 여러 날 밤낮을 한데서 지새우고, 사람의 발이 좀처럼 닿지 않는, 책에서 얻은 지식 따위로는 무사히 헤쳐 갈 수 없는 황야를 가로질러야 하는 거요. 인디언은 결코 제 평의회의 불가에서 담배를 피우지 않고는 그런 원정에 나서지 않소. 비록 나는 백인의 피를 지녔지만, 이 점에서만은 그들의 관습을 존중하오. 그것이 신중하고 지혜로운 것임을 알기 때문이오. 그러니 우리는 되돌아가, 오늘 밤은 옛 요새의 폐허에서 불을 지피고, 아침이 되면 기운을 차려, 사내답게 우리 일을 맡읍시다, 재잘대는 아낙네나 안달하는 사내아이들처럼이 아니라.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 정찰꾼의 태도로 보아, 옥신각신해 봐야 소용없음을 알았다. 〈먼로〉는 다시금, 근래의 압도적인 불행 이래 그를 사로잡아 온 그 일종의 무기력에 빠져들었으니, 거기서 그를 일으킬 수 있는 것은 오직 어떤 새롭고 강력한 자극뿐인 듯했다. 젊은이는 부득이한 일을 도리어 미덕으로 삼아, 노병의 팔을 붙들고는, 이미 그들을 평원으로 이끌었던 길을 되짚어 가기 시작한 인디언들과 정찰꾼의 뒤를 따랐다.\n* 개똥지빠귀: 원문의 catbird. 북아메리카에 서식하는, 고양이 울음 비슷한 소리를 내는 새.\n\n\"샐라리노. — 글쎄, 그가 약속을 어긴들 자네가 설마 그 살점을 받아 내겠는가? 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있다고?\"\n\n\"샤일록. — 물고기 미끼로나 쓰지. 달리 아무것도 먹이지 못한대도, 내 복수만큼은 먹여 살릴 테니.\"\n\n「베니스의 상인」\n\n저녁 어스름이 내려앉아 이곳의 황량함을 한층 짙게 만들 무렵, 일행은 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 폐허로 들어섰다. 척후와 그 동료들은 곧 그곳에서 밤을 지낼 채비를 시작했으나, 그 진지하고 가라앉은 태도는, 방금 목격한 그 예사롭지 않은 참상이 단련된 그들의 감정에까지 얼마나 깊이 작용했는지를 드러냈다. 서까래의 부서진 조각 몇 개가 검게 그을린 벽에 기대어 세워졌고, 〈웅카스〉가 그 위를 나뭇가지로 엉성하게 덮자, 임시 거처로는 충분하다고 여겨졌다. 일을 마친 젊은 인디언은 자신이 지은 거친 오두막을 가리켰고, 그 말 없는 몸짓의 뜻을 헤아린 〈헤이워드〉는 〈먼로〉에게 안으로 들어가기를 부드럽게 권했다. 슬픔에 잠긴 노인을 그 비탄과 함께 홀로 남겨 둔 채, 〈덩컨〉은 곧 바깥바람 속으로 돌아왔으니, 그 자신이 너무도 격앙되어, 노병인 벗에게 권한 그 휴식을 정작 스스로는 구할 수 없었다.\n\n〈호크아이〉와 인디언들이 불을 피우고, 말린 곰고기로 차린 검소한 저녁 끼니를 드는 동안, 젊은이는 〈호리컨〉의 수면을 굽어보는 허물어진 요새의 그 성벽 쪽을 찾아갔다. 바람은 잦아들었고, 그의 발치 모래 해변으로 파도가 이미 한결 고르고 누그러진 가락으로 밀려오고 있었다. 구름은 사납던 쫓김에 지치기라도 한 듯 갈라지고 있었으니, 무거운 덩어리들은 지평선께에 검은 무리를 이루어 모여들고, 가벼운 조각구름은 여전히 물 위로 서둘러 흘러가거나, 보금자리를 맴도는 흩어진 새 떼처럼 산봉우리 사이를 휘돌았다. 여기저기, 붉고 불타는 별 하나가 떠도는 안개를 뚫고 안간힘을 쓰며, 침침한 하늘의 둔한 낯빛에 음산한 한 줄기 광채를 보탰다. 에워싼 언덕들의 품속에는 이미 뚫을 수 없는 어둠이 내려앉았고, 평원은 광막하고 버려진 납골당처럼 누워 있었으니, 그 무수하고 가엾은 입주자들의 잠을 어지럽힐 그 어떤 조짐도, 속삭임도 없었다.\n\n지나간 일과 이토록 으스스하게 어우러진 이 광경을, 〈덩컨〉은 여러 분 동안 넋을 잃고 바라보며 서 있었다. 그의 두 눈은 삼림의 사내들이 가물거리는 불 둘레에 앉아 있는 둔덕의 품에서, 아직 하늘에 머문 한층 희미한 빛으로 옮겨 갔다가, 이내 죽은 자들이 잠든 그쪽에 음울한 공허처럼 깔린 그 짙은 어둠에, 길고도 불안하게 머물렀다. 그는 곧 그곳에서 설명할 수 없는 소리들이 일어난다고 여겼으나, 그것은 너무도 어렴풋하고 은밀하여, 그 성질은커녕 그 존재 자체마저 분간할 수 없었다. 자신의 불안이 부끄러워, 젊은이는 물 쪽으로 돌아서서, 일렁이는 수면 위로 어슴푸레 반짝이는 그 흉내 낸 별들로 주의를 돌리려 애썼다. 그래도, 너무 예민한 그의 귀는, 어떤 숨어든 위험을 일러 주기라도 하려는 듯, 달갑잖은 제 소임을 다했다. 마침내 무언가가 잰걸음으로 짓밟으며 어둠을 가로질러 내닫는 소리가 또렷이 들리는 듯했다. 더는 불안을 가라앉힐 수 없게 된 〈덩컨〉은 낮은 목소리로 척후에게 말을 건네, 자신이 선 자리로 둔덕을 올라와 달라고 청했다. 〈호크아이〉는 소총을 한쪽 팔에 걸치고 응했으나, 그 태도가 너무도 흔들림 없고 고요하여, 그가 그들의 위치가 안전하다는 데 얼마나 기대고 있는지를 잘 드러냈다.\n\n\"들어 보시오!\" 상대가 천천히 제 팔꿈치 곁에 자리를 잡자 〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"평원에 죽인 소리들이 있소. 〈몽칼름〉이 아직 제가 정복한 땅을 완전히 떠나지 않았다는 표일지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 귀가 눈보다 낫구려.\" 흔들림 없는 척후가 말했으니, 그는 막 곰고기 한 점을 어금니 사이에 밀어 넣은 터라, 입이 두 가지로 차 있는 자처럼 둔하고 느릿하게 말했다. \"나는 그자가 제 군세를 몽땅 거느린 채 〈타이〉에 갇힌 걸 이 두 눈으로 봤소. 당신네 프랑스 친구들은 영리한 한 수를 두고 나면, 돌아가서 여자들과 더불어 제 성공을 두고 춤을 추거나 잔치를 벌이기를 좋아하니까.\"\n\n\"나는 모르겠소. 인디언은 전시에 좀처럼 잠들지 않고, 약탈거리 때문에 〈휴런〉 하나쯤은 제 부족이 떠난 뒤에도 여기 남아 있을 수 있소. 불을 끄고 보초를 세우는 게 좋겠소 — 들어 보시오! 내가 말하는 그 소리가 들리지 않소!\"\n\n\"인디언은 무덤가에 도사리는 일이 더욱 드물지요. 죽일 채비야 늘 되어 있고, 그 수단을 그리 가리지도 않지만, 피가 끓어오르고 성미가 곤두서 있을 때가 아니라면 대개 머리가죽 하나로 만족하오. 허나 일단 혼이 온전히 떠나고 나면, 그는 제 원한을 잊고 죽은 자들이 제 자연스러운 안식을 찾도록 기꺼이 내버려 두지요. 혼 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 소령, 붉은 살갗을 가진 자의 천국과 우리 백인의 천국이 하나요 같은 것이라 보시오?\"\n\n\"의심할 바 없소 — 의심할 바 없소. 또 들린 것 같은데! 아니면 너도밤나무 꼭대기 잎새가 바스락거린 소리였소?\"\n\n\"내 생각으로는 말이오,\" 〈호크아이〉가 잠시 〈헤이워드〉가 가리킨 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸으나, 멍하고 무심한 태도로 말을 이었다. \"낙원은 행복을 위해 마련된 곳이고, 사람은 제 기질과 타고난 재간에 따라 그 안에서 누림을 허락받으리라 믿소. 그러니 붉은 살갗을 가진 자가, 제 전설이 일러 주는 그 영광스러운 사냥터를 거기서 찾으리라 믿을 때, 그가 진실에서 그리 멀지 않다고 나는 판단하오. 또 그 점으로 말하자면, 혈통에 섞임 없는 자가 그 시간을 보내는 게 그에게 조금도 흠이 되리라곤 생각지 않소 —\"\n\n\"또 들리지 않소?\" 〈덩컨〉이 가로막았다.\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소. 먹이가 귀할 때도, 먹이가 넘칠 때도, 늑대는 대담해지는 법이오.\" 흔들림 없는 척후가 말했다. \"빛과 그럴 짬만 있다면, 저 악마들 가죽 가운데서도 골라잡을 게 좀 있을 거요. 허나 앞으로 올 삶에 대해서 말이오, 소령. 나는 정착지에서 설교자들이, 천국은 안식의 자리라 말하는 걸 들었소. 한데 누림에 관한 생각은 사람마다 다르오. 나로 말하자면, 섭리의 정하심에 대한 외경을 담아 말하건대, 움직임과 사냥을 타고난 그리움으로 품은 자에게, 그들이 설교하는 그 저택에 갇혀 지내는 건 그리 큰 누림이 못 될 거요.\"\n\n이제 자신이 들은 소리들의 정체를 알게 된 〈덩컨〉은, 척후의 기분이 골라잡은 그 화제에 한층 주의를 기울여 대답하며 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"마지막 큰 변화에 따를 그 느낌이란 헤아리기 어려운 일이오.\"\n\n\"바깥바람 속에서 평생을 보낸 사람에겐 정말이지 큰 변화일 거요.\" 외곬인 척후가 대꾸했다. \"〈허드슨〉의 발원지에서 그토록 자주 아침 끼니를 들고, 우렁찬 〈모호크〉강의 물소리가 닿는 데서 잠들어 온 자에게는 말이오. 허나 우리가 자비로우신 주인을 섬긴다는 걸 아는 건 위안이 되오. 비록 저마다 제 방식대로, 우리 사이에 광막한 황야를 끼고 그분을 섬길지라도 — 저기 무엇이 지나가오?\"\n\n\"당신이 말한 그 내달리는 늑대들 아니오?\"\n\n〈호크아이〉는 천천히 고개를 저으며, 불빛이 닿지 않는 자리로 따라오라고 〈덩컨〉에게 손짓했다. 이 조심을 다한 뒤, 척후는 온 정신을 곤두세운 자세를 취하고, 그토록 뜻밖에 그를 놀라게 한 그 낮은 소리가 되풀이되기를 길고도 날카롭게 귀 기울였다. 그러나 그 경계는 헛수고로 돌아간 듯했으니, 보람 없는 한참의 멈춤 끝에 그는 〈덩컨〉에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"〈웅카스〉를 불러야겠소. 그 애는 인디언의 감각을 지녔으니, 우리에게 가려진 걸 들을지도 모르오. 나는 흰 살갗을 지닌 몸이라, 내 본성을 부인하진 않겠소.\"\n\n낮은 목소리로 아비와 이야기를 나누던 젊은 〈모히칸〉은, 올빼미가 우는 소리를 듣자 흠칫하더니, 벌떡 일어나 그 소리가 흘러나온 곳을 찾으려는 듯 검은 둔덕들 쪽을 바라보았다. 척후가 그 부름을 되풀이하자, 곧 〈덩컨〉은 〈웅카스〉의 형상이 성벽을 따라 조심스레 그들이 선 자리로 다가오는 것을 보았다.\n\n〈호크아이〉는 자신의 뜻을 〈델라웨어〉 말로, 몇 마디 안 되는 말로 일러 주었다. 〈웅카스〉는 자신이 불려 온 까닭을 알아차리자마자, 잔디 위에 납작 엎드렸고, 〈덩컨〉의 눈에는 그가 고요히 꼼짝 않고 누운 듯 보였다. 젊은 전사의 미동도 없는 자세에 놀라고, 그가 바라는 정보를 얻고자 제 능력을 어떻게 쓰는지 궁금해진 〈헤이워드〉는, 몇 걸음 나아가, 눈을 박아 두었던 그 어두운 물체 위로 몸을 굽혔다. 바로 그때 그는 〈웅카스〉의 형상이 사라지고, 자신이 보고 있는 것이 둑의 울퉁불퉁한 굴곡의 검은 윤곽뿐임을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"그 〈모히칸〉이 어찌 된 거요?\" 그가 놀라 뒷걸음치며 척후에게 물었다. \"바로 여기서 그가 엎드리는 걸 봤고, 여기 아직 그대로 있다고 맹세라도 할 수 있었는데.\"\n\n\"쉿! 더 낮게 말하시오. 어떤 귀가 열려 있을지 우리는 모르고, 〈밍고〉는 머리 회전이 빠른 족속이니까. 〈웅카스〉로 말하자면, 그 애는 평원에 나가 있고, 우리 둘레에 〈매콰〉가 있다면 제 맞수를 만나게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"〈몽칼름〉이 제 인디언들을 다 거두지 않았다고 보는 거요? 우리 동료들에게 경보를 알려, 무기를 들고 맞설 채비를 합시다. 우리는 다섯이고, 적을 맞는 데 낯설지 않은 자들이오.\"\n\n\"목숨이 아깝거든 누구에게도 한마디 마시오. 저 〈사가모어〉를 보시오, 얼마나 위풍당당한 인디언 추장처럼 불가에 앉아 있는지. 어둠 속에 도사린 자가 있다 한들, 우리가 곁에 닥친 위험을 눈치챘다는 걸 그 낯빛으로는 결코 알아내지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저들이 그를 발견할 수 있고, 그러면 그건 그의 죽음이 될 거요. 저 불빛에 그의 몸이 너무 또렷이 보이니, 가장 먼저, 가장 확실한 희생자가 될 거요.\"\n\n\"이제 당신이 옳은 말을 한다는 건 부인할 수 없구려.\" 평소보다 더한 불안을 내비치며 척후가 대꾸했다. \"허나 어쩌겠소? 의심스러운 눈길 한 번이 우리가 받을 채비를 갖추기도 전에 공격을 불러올지 모르오. 그는 내가 〈웅카스〉에게 보낸 부름으로, 우리가 냄새를 맡았다는 걸 알고 있소. 〈밍고〉의 자취를 좇고 있다고 그에게 일러 주리다. 그의 인디언 본성이 어떻게 처신할지를 가르쳐 줄 거요.\"\n\n척후는 손가락을 입에 대고, 낮게 쉭쉭거리는 소리를 냈으니, 〈덩컨〉은 처음엔 뱀 소리를 들은 줄 알고 흠칫 비켜섰다. 〈칭가치국〉은 홀로 생각에 잠긴 채 손에 머리를 괴고 앉아 있었으나, 제가 이름을 따온 그 짐승의 경고를 듣는 순간, 똑바로 일어나 앉았고, 그의 검은 두 눈이 사방을 잽싸고 날카롭게 훑었다. 이 갑작스럽고, 어쩌면 저도 모르게 나온 움직임과 더불어, 놀람이나 경계의 기색은 죄다 사라졌다. 그의 소총은 손이 닿는 데에 손대지도, 눈길도 주지 않은 듯 놓여 있었다. 편하라고 허리띠에서 느슨히 풀어 두었던 손도끼마저 제자리에서 땅으로 떨어지도록 내버려 두었고, 그의 몸은 휴식을 위해 신경과 힘줄을 다 풀어 놓은 자의 그것처럼 가라앉는 듯했다. 마치 그저 팔다리를 편히 하려 한 동작인 양 손만 바꿔, 영리하게 본래 자세로 돌아간 채, 그 원주민은 인디언 전사가 아니고는 누구도 부릴 줄 모를 그런 고요함과 의연함으로 결과를 기다렸다.\n\n그러나 〈헤이워드〉는, 덜 숙련된 눈에는 〈모히칸〉 추장이 졸고 있는 듯 보일지언정, 그의 콧구멍이 벌어져 있고, 청각의 기관을 돕기라도 하려는 듯 머리가 한쪽으로 살짝 기울어 있으며, 그 빠르고 잰 눈길이 제 시야가 미치는 온갖 사물 위로 쉴 새 없이 달리고 있음을 보았다.\n\n\"저 고귀한 친구를 보시오!\" 〈호크아이〉가 〈헤이워드〉의 팔을 누르며 속삭였다. \"눈짓 한 번, 몸짓 하나가 우리 계책을 어그러뜨리고 우리를 저 악귀들의 처분에 맡길 수 있다는 걸 그는 알고 있소 —\"\n\n소총의 섬광과 발사음이 그의 말을 가로막았다. 〈헤이워드〉의 두 눈이 감탄과 경이로 여전히 박혀 있던 그 자리 둘레로, 공기가 불꽃으로 가득 찼다. 다시 한번 보니 〈칭가치국〉은 그 혼란 속에 사라지고 없었다. 그동안 척후는 임무에 나설 채비를 갖춘 자처럼 소총을 앞으로 내밀고, 적이 모습을 드러낼 그 순간을 안달하며 기다렸다. 그러나 〈칭가치국〉의 목숨을 노린 그 외롭고 보람 없는 한 차례 시도와 함께, 공격은 끝난 듯했다. 한두 번, 정체 모를 무언가가 덤불을 헤치고 내달리며 멀리서 바스락거리는 소리를 듣는 이들은 분간할 수 있다고 여겼다. 오래지 않아 〈호크아이〉는, 제 영역을 침범한 누군가가 지나가는 앞에서 황급히 달아나는 \"늑대들의 내뺌\"을 가리켜 보였다. 안달과 숨죽임의 멈춤 끝에, 물에 무언가가 뛰어드는 소리가 들렸고, 곧이어 또 다른 소총의 발사음이 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"저기 〈웅카스〉가 가는군!\" 척후가 말했다. \"그 애는 야무진 총을 지녔지! 아비가 제 자식의 말투를 알듯, 나는 그 총소리를 알아듣소. 더 나은 게 생기기 전까지 그 총은 내가 직접 지니고 다녔으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"이게 무슨 뜻이오?\" 〈덩컨〉이 물었다. \"우리는 감시받고 있고, 보아하니 죽임을 당하도록 점찍힌 듯하오.\"\n\n\"저기 흩어진 불씨가, 선의 따윈 없었다는 걸 증언할 테고, 이 인디언이 아무 해도 입지 않았다는 걸 증언할 거요.\" 척후가 소총을 다시 팔에 걸치며 대꾸하더니, 마침 빛의 둘레 안에 다시 모습을 드러낸 〈칭가치국〉을 따라 진지의 품으로 들어섰다. \"어찌 된 거요, 〈사가모어〉? 〈밍고〉가 진심으로 우리를 노린 거요, 아니면 그저 전투대의 꽁무니에 매달려 죽은 자의 머리가죽을 벗기고 돌아가, 창백한 얼굴들에게 떨친 용맹한 무용담을 여인네들 사이에서 떠벌리는 그런 굼벵이 하나였소?\"\n\n〈칭가치국〉은 아주 조용히 제자리로 돌아가 앉았으며, 하마터면 제 목숨을 앗을 뻔한 그 총알에 맞은 불붙은 장작을 살핀 뒤에야 비로소 대답했다. 그러고 나서, 그는 손가락 하나를 들어 보이며, 영어 한 음절로 대답하는 것으로 족했다.\n\n\"하나.\"\n\n\"나도 그쯤으로 짐작했소.\" 〈호크아이〉가 자리에 앉으며 대꾸했다. \"그리고 〈웅카스〉가 그자에게 방아쇠를 당기기 전에 호수의 엄폐물에 들었으니, 그 악당이 어떤 대단한 매복에 관해 거짓 노래를 불러 댈 게 거의 틀림없소. 〈모히칸〉 둘과 백인 사냥꾼 하나의 자취를 밟아 매복해 있었노라고 말이오 — 이런 옥신각신에서 장교들이야 노는 자들이나 다름없다고 칠 테니까. 뭐, 떠벌리든 말든 — 떠벌리든 말든. 어느 민족에나 정직한 자가 늘 몇은 있는 법이오. 하늘은 알겠지만 〈매콰〉 사이엔 그런 자가 드물긴 하나, 이치에 맞서 허풍을 떠는 풋내기를 한번 눌러 줄 자 말이오. 그 망나니가 당신 귓전을 스치도록 납탄을 보냈소, 〈사가모어〉.\"\n\n〈칭가치국〉은 총알이 박힌 자리로 고요하고 호기심 없는 눈길을 돌렸다가, 이런 하찮은 일 따위로는 흔들릴 수 없는 평정으로 본래 자세로 돌아갔다. 바로 그때 〈웅카스〉가 빛의 둘레 안으로 미끄러져 들어와, 제 아비가 지켜 온 것과 똑같은 무심한 기색으로 불가에 앉았다.\n\n이 여러 움직임을, 〈헤이워드〉는 깊이 흥미를 느끼며 경탄하는 관찰자로서 지켜보았다. 그에게는 삼림의 사내들이, 제 능력의 경계를 벗어난 어떤 은밀한 소식의 수단을 지닌 듯이 여겨졌다. 백인 청년이라면 평원의 어둠 속에서 벌어진 일을 알리려, 어쩌면 부풀리려 들었을 그 열렬하고 수다스러운 늘어놓음 대신, 젊은 전사는 제 행적이 스스로 말하게 두는 것으로 만족하는 듯했다. 사실 인디언이 제 공적을 자랑할 만한 때도, 자리도 아니었으니, 만일 〈헤이워드〉가 묻기를 게을리했다면, 그 일에 관해서는 당장에 더는 한 음절도 새어 나오지 않았으리라 짐작된다.\n\n\"우리 적은 어찌 됐소, 〈웅카스〉?\" 〈덩컨〉이 물었다. \"당신 소총 소리를 들었고, 헛되이 쏘지 않았기를 바랐소.\"\n\n젊은 추장은 사냥 셔츠의 한 자락을 젖혀, 승리의 표징으로 지닌 그 죽음의 머리채를 조용히 드러내 보였다. 〈칭가치국〉은 그 머리가죽에 손을 얹고, 한순간 깊은 주의를 기울여 그것을 살폈다. 그러더니 강인한 이목구비에 혐오를 띤 채 그것을 떨어뜨리며 내뱉었다.\n\n\"〈오나이다〉!\"\n\n\"〈오나이다〉라!\" 척후가 되뇌었으니, 그는 붉은 동료들의 그것에 거의 맞먹는 무심함에 빠져 이 장면에 대한 흥미를 빠르게 잃어 가던 참이었으나, 이제 보기 드문 진지함으로 다가와 그 피 묻은 표지를 들여다보았다. \"맙소사, 〈오나이다〉가 자취 위에 매복해 있다면, 우리는 사방에서 악마들에게 측면을 에워싸이는 거요! 자, 백인의 눈에는 이 살가죽 한 조각과 다른 어느 인디언의 것 사이에 아무 차이도 없건만, 〈사가모어〉는 이게 〈밍고〉의 정수리에서 나온 거라 단언하고, 아니, 그 머리가죽이 책의 한 장이고 털 한 올 한 올이 글자이기라도 한 듯, 그 가엾은 악당의 부족까지 이름 대어 말하지 않소. 미개인이 그들 가운데 가장 지혜로운 자도 당해 내지 못할 언어를 읽어 내는데, 기독교를 믿는 백인이 무슨 권리로 제 학식을 자랑하겠소! 자네는 뭐라 하겠나, 이 사람아. 그 망나니가 어느 종족이었나?\"\n\n〈웅카스〉는 척후의 얼굴로 눈을 들어, 부드러운 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"〈오나이다〉.\"\n\n\"또 〈오나이다〉라! 인디언 하나가 단언하면 대개 참인데, 그가 제 동족의 뒷받침을 받으면, 복음으로 받아들여도 좋소!\"\n\n\"그 가엾은 자가 우리를 프랑스 사람으로 잘못 본 거요.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"아니라면 벗의 목숨을 노렸을 리 없소.\"\n\n\"단장을 한 〈모히칸〉을 〈휴런〉으로 잘못 봤다고! 그건 〈몽칼름〉의 흰 외투 입은 척탄병을 〈로열 아메리칸〉의 진홍색 상의로 잘못 보는 거나 진배없소.\" 척후가 대꾸했다. \"아니, 아니오. 그 뱀은 제가 무슨 볼일로 왔는지 알고 있었소. 또 그 일에 큰 착오도 없었던 게, 백인의 다툼에서 제 부족이 누구를 위해 싸우러 나가든, 〈델라웨어〉와 〈밍고〉 사이엔 정이라곤 거의 없으니까. 그 점으로 말하자면, 〈오나이다〉가 그 거룩하신 폐하, 곧 내 군주요 주인이신 분을 섬긴다 한들, 운이 그 악귀를 내 앞에 던져 줬다면, 나 자신도 '〈킬디어〉'를 그자에게 쏘아 대는 걸 그리 오래 망설이지 않았을 거요.\"\n\n\"그건 우리 조약을 저버리는 짓이고, 당신 인품에 어울리지 않는 일이오.\"\n\n\"사람이 어떤 족속과 오래 어울리다 보면,\" 〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다. \"그들이 정직하고 그 자신이 악당이 아닐 경우엔, 그들 사이에 정이 자라나는 법이오. 백인의 교활함이 벗과 적을 두고 부족들을 큰 혼란에 빠뜨려 놓은 건 사실이오. 그래서 같은 말을, 같다고 할 만한 말을 쓰는 〈휴런〉과 〈오나이다〉가 서로 머리가죽을 벗기고, 〈델라웨어〉는 저희끼리 갈라져, 더러는 제 강가의 큰 평의회 불 둘레에 머물며 〈밍고〉와 한편이 되어 싸우고, 더 많은 수는 〈매콰〉에 대한 타고난 원한으로 〈캐나다〉에 가 있으니 — 이렇게 모든 걸 어지럽혀, 전쟁의 온갖 조화를 깨뜨리는 거요. 그래도 붉은 본성이 정략이 바뀔 때마다 변하지는 않을 터, 〈모히칸〉과 〈밍고〉 사이의 정이란 백인과 뱀 사이의 정 같은 것이오.\"\n\n\"그런 말을 들으니 안타깝소. 나는 우리 경계 안에 사는 그 원주민들이, 우리가 너무도 공정하고 너그럽다 여겨 우리의 다툼에 온전히 한 몸이 되었으리라 믿었기에 하는 말이오.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 남의 다툼보다 제 다툼을 더 치는 게 본성 아니겠소. 자, 나로 말하자면, 나는 정의를 사랑하오. 그러니 〈밍고〉를 미워한다고는 말하지 않겠소. 그건 내 피부색과 내 신앙에 어울리지 않을지 모르니까. 다만 한 번 더 되풀이하건대, '〈킬디어〉'가 이 숨어든 〈오나이다〉의 죽음에 손대지 않은 건 밤 덕분이었는지도 모르오.\"\n\n그러고는, 제 논리가 상대편 논객의 견해에 어떤 영향을 미쳤든 간에, 제 논거의 힘에 흡족했다는 듯, 그 정직하나 누그러질 줄 모르는 숲의 사내는 논쟁을 잠재워 두기로 하고 불에서 돌아앉았다. 〈헤이워드〉는 성벽으로 물러났으니, 너무 마음이 불편하고 숲의 전쟁에 너무 서툴러, 그런 음험한 공격이 있을 수 있다는 상황에서 도무지 마음을 놓을 수 없었다. 그러나 척후와 〈모히칸〉들은 그렇지 않았다. 그 예리하고 오래 단련된 감각은, 그 힘이 흔히 보통의 믿음으로는 가늠할 수 없는 한계를 넘어서는 것이라, 위험을 알아챈 뒤에는 그 규모와 지속을 헤아릴 수 있게 해 주었다. 셋 중 누구도 제 완벽한 안전을 조금도 의심하는 기색이 없었으니, 곧 앞으로의 행보를 두고 평의하려 갖춰진 채비가 그것을 일러 주었다.\n\n〈호크아이〉가 넌지시 말한 그 민족들, 아니 부족들의 혼란은, 그 시절 가장 극심한 정도로 존재하고 있었다. 언어라는, 따라서 공통의 기원이라는 그 위대한 끈이 곳곳에서 끊겼고, 그 결과 가운데 하나로, 〈델라웨어〉와 〈밍고〉(〈6부족 연맹〉의 사람들을 그렇게 불렀다)가 같은 대열에서 싸우는 한편, 〈밍고〉는 제 혈통의 뿌리로 여겨지는 〈휴런〉의 머리가죽을 노리는 광경이 벌어졌다. 〈델라웨어〉마저 저희끼리 갈라져 있었다. 조상에게 속했던 그 땅에 대한 사랑이 〈모히칸〉의 〈사가모어〉를, 영국 왕의 깃발 아래 〈에드워드〉에서 복무하는 한 줌의 추종자들과 함께 붙들어 두었으나, 그 민족의 단연 가장 큰 무리는 〈몽칼름〉의 동맹군으로서 싸움터에 나가 있는 것으로 알려져 있었다. 독자는 아마 알고 있을 터이나 — 이 이야기에서 이미 충분히 헤아리지 못했다면 — 〈델라웨어〉, 곧 〈레나페〉는, 한때 아메리카의 동부와 북부 주(州) 대부분의 주인이었던 그 수많은 민족의 조상임을 자처했으며, 〈모히칸〉의 공동체는 그 오래되고 더없이 존경받는 일원이었다.\n\n벗을 벗과 맞서 무장하게 하고, 타고난 적들을 나란히 곁에서 싸우게 만든 그 미세하고 뒤얽힌 이해관계를 더없이 환히 꿰뚫은 채, 척후와 그 동료들은 이제 이토록 많은 어긋나고 미개한 종족들 가운데서 앞으로의 거동을 다스릴 방책을 의논하고자 자리를 잡았다. 〈덩컨〉은 인디언 풍습을 충분히 알고 있어, 어찌하여 불을 다시 돋우는지, 그리고 어찌하여 〈호크아이〉마저 포함한 전사들이 그토록 엄숙하고 예의 바르게 그 연기의 굽이 안에 자리를 잡는지를 헤아렸다. 안에서 벌어지는 장면을 지켜보는 동시에 바깥으로부터의 어떤 위험에도 경계의 눈길을 늦추지 않을 수 있는 진지의 한 모퉁이에 자리를 잡고서, 그는 그러모을 수 있는 한껏의 인내로 결과를 기다렸다.\n\n짧고도 인상 깊은 멈춤 끝에, 〈칭가치국〉은 이 고장의 무른 돌 하나에 정교히 새긴 대통에, 나무관으로 된 설대를 단 담뱃대에 불을 붙여 피우기 시작했다. 그 달래는 풀의 향을 넉넉히 들이마신 뒤, 그는 그 도구를 척후의 손에 건넸다. 이렇게 담뱃대는 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에 세 차례를 따로따로 돌고 나서야, 비로소 일행 중 누군가가 입을 열었다. 이윽고 〈사가모어〉가, 가장 연장이요 지위가 가장 높은 자로서, 몇 마디 차분하고 위엄 있는 말로 평의할 안건을 내놓았다. 척후가 그에게 대답했고, 상대가 자신의 견해에 이의를 제기하자 〈칭가치국〉이 다시 응했다. 그러나 젊은 〈웅카스〉는, 〈호크아이〉가 정중하게 그 의견을 물을 때까지, 말 없고 공손한 경청자로 머물렀다. 〈헤이워드〉는 저마다 말하는 자들의 태도에서, 아비와 아들이 다투는 문제의 한쪽 편을 들고, 백인이 다른 쪽을 견지하고 있음을 헤아렸다. 다툼은 차츰 뜨거워져, 마침내 말하는 자들의 감정이 그 논의에 다소 실리기 시작한 것이 자못 분명해졌다.\n\n이 우호적인 다툼의 열기가 점점 더해 갔음에도, 더없이 예법을 갖춘 기독교도의 회합이, 그 존경할 만한 목사들이 모인 회합조차도, 이 논객들의 인내와 정중함에서 절제의 유익한 교훈을 배울 수 있었으리라. 〈웅카스〉의 말은 그 아비의 무르익은 지혜에서 나온 말과 똑같이 깊은 주의로 받아들여졌고, 어느 쪽도 조바심을 내기는커녕, 이미 한 말을 두고 몇 순간의 말 없는 사색을 곰곰이 들이는 듯한 시간을 보낸 뒤에야 비로소 대답했다.\n\n〈모히칸〉의 말은 너무도 직접적이고 자연스러운 몸짓이 곁들여져, 〈헤이워드〉는 그 논의의 갈피를 좇는 데 큰 어려움이 없었다. 반면에 척후는 알아듣기 어려웠으니, 피부색에 대한 미련 어린 자긍심에서, 격앙되지 않았을 때의 영국계 아메리카인 모든 계층의 특징인 그 차고 꾸민 듯한 태도를 짐짓 부린 까닭이었다. 인디언들이 숲속 자취의 표를 묘사하는 빈도로 보아, 그들이 육로로의 추적을 주장하고 있음이 분명했고, 〈호리컨〉 쪽으로 거듭 내젓는 〈호크아이〉의 팔은 그가 그 물길을 가로지르는 편을 지지함을 드러냈다.\n\n후자는, 누가 보아도 빠르게 처지고 있어, 자칫 그에게 불리하게 결판이 날 참이었으나, 그가 벌떡 일어서더니 제 무심함을 떨치고 불현듯 인디언의 태도를 취하며, 원주민 웅변의 온갖 기교를 다 펼쳐 보였다. 한쪽 팔을 들어, 그는 해의 자취를 가리키며, 그들의 목적을 이루는 데 필요한 날수만큼 그 몸짓을 거듭했다. 그런 다음 바위와 물길 사이로 난 길고도 고된 길을 그려 보였다. 잠들어 의식 없는 〈먼로〉의 노쇠함과 약함은, 잘못 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 또렷한 손짓으로 일러졌다. 〈덩컨〉은 자신의 능력마저 가벼이 일컬어짐을 알아챘으니, 척후가 손바닥을 펼치며 그를 \"펼친 손\"이라는 칭호로 불렀던 것이다 — 그의 너그러움이 모든 우호적인 부족에게서 사들인 이름이었다. 이어, 카누의 가볍고 우아한 움직임을, 쇠약하고 지친 자의 비틀거리는 걸음과 강한 대조로 놓아 그려 보였다. 그는 〈오나이다〉의 머리가죽을 가리키며, 자취를 남기지 않을 방식으로 속히 떠나야 할 필요를 역설하는 듯하더니 말을 맺었다.\n\n〈모히칸〉들은 엄숙하게, 그리고 말하는 자의 심정을 비추는 낯빛으로 귀 기울였다. 차츰 확신이 그 영향을 미쳤고, 〈호크아이〉의 연설이 끝나갈 무렵엔 그의 문장마다 으레 따르는 찬동의 감탄사가 곁들여졌다. 요컨대, 〈웅카스〉와 그 아비는 그의 사고방식에 귀의하여, 앞서 밝혔던 제 견해를 너그러움과 솔직함으로 버렸으니, 만일 그들이 어느 위대하고 문명한 민족의 대표였더라면, 일관성에 대한 명성을 영영 무너뜨려 그 정치적 파멸을 어김없이 불러왔을 그런 너그러움과 솔직함이었다.\n\n논의되던 문제가 결판나는 순간, 그 논쟁도, 그것에 얽힌 모든 것도, 결과만 빼고는 잊힌 듯했다. 〈호크아이〉는 칭찬하는 눈길 속에서 제 승리를 읽으려 둘러보지도 않은 채, 아주 침착하게 꺼져 가는 잉걸불 앞에 제 큰 키를 뻗고, 두 눈을 감아 잠을 청했다.\n\n이제 어느 정도 저희들끼리 남겨지자, 그토록 많은 시간을 남들의 이익에 바쳤던 〈모히칸〉들은, 그 짬을 타 자신들에게 얼마간 마음을 쓰기로 했다. 인디언 추장의 그 엄숙하고 준엄한 태도를 단번에 벗어 던지고, 〈칭가치국〉은 부드럽고 장난스러운 애정의 어조로 아들에게 말을 건네기 시작했다. 〈웅카스〉는 아비의 그 다정한 기색을 기껍게 맞았다. 척후의 거친 숨소리가 그가 잠들었음을 알리기도 전에, 그 두 동무의 태도에는 완연한 변화가 일었다.\n\n이리하여 웃음과 다정한 말에 빠져든 그들의 언어가 지닌 가락을, 그 선율에 한 번도 귀 기울여 본 적 없는 이들에게 알아듣도록 옮긴다는 것은 도무지 그려 낼 길이 없다. 그들 목소리의 음역은, 특히 그 젊은이의 것은 놀라워, 가장 깊은 저음에서 여인의 것이라 할 만큼 부드러운 음조에까지 뻗쳤다. 아비의 두 눈은 아들의 유연하고 재간 있는 움직임을 숨김없는 기쁨으로 좇았고, 상대의 옮아 오는, 그러나 낮은 웃음에 그는 어김없이 미소로 화답했다. 이 온화하고 자연스러운 감정의 영향 아래 있는 동안, 누그러진 〈사가모어〉의 이목구비에는 흉포함의 자취라곤 조금도 보이지 않았다. 그가 몸에 두른 그 무늬 새긴 죽음의 갑주는, 제 발자국마다 파멸을 실어 나르겠다는 사나운 선포라기보다는, 차라리 조롱으로 걸친 변장처럼 보였다.\n\n한 시간을 이 한결 나은 감정에 빠져 보낸 뒤, 〈칭가치국〉은 담요로 머리를 감싸고 맨땅에 몸을 뻗으며, 잠들고 싶다는 뜻을 불쑥 알렸다. 〈웅카스〉의 즐거움은 그 즉시 멎었고, 잉걸불을 그 온기가 아비의 발에 닿도록 정성껏 그러모은 뒤, 젊은이는 그곳의 폐허 사이에서 제가 벨 베개를 찾았다.\n\n이 노련한 삼림의 사내들이 안전하다는 데서 새로운 자신을 들이마신 〈헤이워드〉도, 곧 그들을 본받았다. 그리하여 밤이 깊어 가기 한참 전에, 허물어진 진지의 품에 누운 자들은, 둘레의 평원 위에서 벌써 그 뼈가 하얗게 바래기 시작한 그 의식 없는 무리만큼이나 깊이 잠든 듯했다.\n\n앨버니아의 땅이여! 그대, 야만의 사내들을 길러 낸 거친 유모여, 그대 위에 내 두 눈을 머물게 하라!\n\n차일드 해럴드\n\n〈호크아이〉가 잠든 이들을 깨우러 왔을 때, 하늘은 여전히 별들로 촘촘했다. 〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉는 망토를 걷어 내며 자리에서 일어섰으니, 그들이 밤을 지낸 거친 은신처 입구에서 숲의 사내가 아직도 나직한 부름을 보내고 있을 때였다. 가려진 그늘 아래에서 빠져나오자, 그들은 척후가 바로 곁에서 모습을 드러내기를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았고, 그들 사이에 오간 유일한 인사라곤 그 슬기로운 인도자가 조용히 하라며 보낸 의미심장한 손짓뿐이었다.\n\n\"기도문을 마음속으로 새겨 두시오.\" 그들이 다가가자 그가 속삭였다. \"그대들이 기도를 올리는 그분은 모든 말을 아시니, 입의 말은 물론 마음의 말까지 다 아시오. 허나 한 마디도 소리 내지 마시오. 백인의 목소리가 숲속에서 제대로 가락을 잡는 일은 드무니, 우리는 저 가련한 가엾은 자, 그 노래꾼을 본보기 삼아 이미 겪지 않았소. 자,\" 그가 방어 시설의 한 막벽 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"이쪽으로 해서 해자 안으로 내려갑시다. 가면서 돌이나 나무 조각만 밟도록 조심하시오.\"\n\n그의 동행들은 따랐으나, 그중 두 사람에게는 이 유별난 조심성의 까닭이 아직 수수께끼였다. 흙으로 쌓은 요새를 세 면으로 에워싼 그 낮은 구덩이 안에 들어서자, 그들은 통로가 잔해로 거의 막혀 있는 것을 발견했다. 그러나 신중함과 인내로 척후의 뒤를 기어오르는 데 성공하여, 마침내 〈호리컨〉의 모래 기슭에 다다랐다.\n\n\"저건 코가 아니고서는 따라올 수 없는 자취요.\" 만족한 척후가 그들이 지나온 험한 길을 돌아보며 말했다. \"풀밭은 달아나는 무리가 밟기에는 믿지 못할 양탄자지만, 나무와 돌은 모카신 자국을 남기지 않으니까. 그대들이 만약 쇠 박은 군화를 신었더라면, 정말이지 두려워할 게 있었을 거요. 허나 사슴 가죽을 알맞게 손질해 신으면, 사람은 대체로 바위 위에서 제 몸을 안심하고 맡길 수 있소. 〈웅카스〉, 카누를 뭍에 좀 더 가까이 밀어라. 이 모래는 〈모호크〉강의 〈독일〉인들 버터만큼이나 쉽게 자국이 박히니까. 살살, 얘야, 살살. 카누가 모래톱에 닿아선 안 돼. 안 그러면 그 악당들이 우리가 어느 길로 떠났는지 알아챌 테니까.\"\n\n젊은이는 그 주의를 지켰다. 그러자 척후는 잔해에서 널빤지 하나를 카누에 걸쳐 놓고, 두 장교에게 들어오라는 손짓을 보냈다. 그 일이 끝나자, 모든 것이 본래의 어수선함 그대로 세심하게 되돌려졌고, 그런 다음 〈호크아이〉는 자신이 그토록 꺼리던 자국을 하나도 남기지 않은 채 제 작은 자작나무 배에 다다르는 데 성공했다. 인디언들이 카누를 조심스레 저어 요새에서 어느 정도 멀어져, 동쪽 산에서 호수의 거울 같은 수면 위로 드리운 넓고 어두운 그림자 안에 들 때까지, 〈헤이워드〉는 입을 다물고 있었다. 그러다 이윽고 그가 물었다.\n\n\"이렇게 몰래, 서둘러 떠날 필요가 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"우리가 떠 있는 이런 맑디맑은 수면을 〈오나이다〉 사람의 피로 물들일 수 있다면,\" 척후가 대답했다. \"그대의 두 눈이 그대 물음에 스스로 답해 줄 거요. 〈웅카스〉가 죽인 그 살금살금 기던 파충류를 잊었소?\"\n\n\"천만에. 허나 그자는 혼자였다고 했고, 죽은 자는 두려움의 빌미가 되지 않잖소.\"\n\n\"맞소, 그자는 제 악행에 있어선 혼자였지! 허나 그 부족에 전사가 그토록 많은 인디언이라면, 제 피가 흐른다 해서 그 적들 가운데 누구의 죽음의 비명이 곧 뒤따르지 않을까 두려워할 일은 좀처럼 없는 법이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 우리가 그 자리에 있었다는 사실은, 〈먼로〉 대령의 권위는, 우리 동맹의 분노에 맞설 충분한 보호가 되어 줄 거요. 더구나 그토록 마땅히 제 운명을 받을 만한 비열한 자의 경우라면 말이오. 부디 하늘에 비건대, 이토록 사소한 이유로 우리 노정의 곧은 길에서 단 한 발짝도 벗어나진 않았겠지요!\"\n\n\"그 악당의 소총알이, 설령 폐하이신 국왕께서 그 길목에 서 계셨던들 비켜 갔으리라 생각하시오?\" 고집스러운 척후가 되받았다. \"백인의 말 한마디가 인디언의 본성에 그토록 강하게 작용할 수 있다면, 어찌하여 저 거룩한 프랑스인, 〈캐나다〉의 총사령관인 그자는 〈휴런〉족의 토마호크를 묻어 두지 않았겠소?\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉의 대답은 〈먼로〉의 신음 소리에 끊겼으나, 늙은 벗의 슬픔을 헤아려 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤, 그는 다시 그 화제를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"〈몽칼름〉 후작은 그 잘못을 오직 자신의 신과 더불어 매듭지을 수 있을 뿐이오.\" 젊은이가 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"옳소, 옳소. 이제야 그대 말에 이치가 있구려. 그 말은 신앙과 정직함에 뿌리를 두고 있으니 말이오. 흰 군복을 입은 한 연대를 부족들과 포로들 사이에 던져 넣는 것과, 성난 미개인더러 칼과 소총을 지녔다는 사실을 잊으라고, 그것도 그를 그대의 아들이라 부르는 말로 시작해야 하는 그런 말로 구슬리는 것 사이엔 엄청난 차이가 있소. 아니, 아니지.\" 척후가 이제 빠르게 멀어져 가는 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 희미한 기슭을 돌아보며, 제 나름의 소리 없되 진심 어린 방식으로 웃으며 말을 이었다. \"내가 우리 사이에 물의 자취를 깔아 놓았소. 그러니 그 악마 같은 놈들이 물고기와 친구가 되어, 오늘 이 멋진 아침에 누가 그 못을 가로질러 노 저어 갔는지 들을 수 있는 게 아닌 한, 우리는 그놈들이 어느 길을 택할지 마음을 정하기도 전에 〈호리컨〉의 길이만큼을 등 뒤에 따돌리게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"앞에도 적, 뒤에도 적이니, 우리 여정은 위험으로 가득할 듯하구려.\"\n\n\"위험이라!\" 〈호크아이〉가 차분히 되뇌었다. \"아니오, 딱히 위험은 아니오. 귀를 곤두세우고 눈을 재빨리 놀리면, 우리는 그 악당들보다 몇 시간쯤 앞서 갈 수 있소. 혹여 소총을 써야 한다면, 우리 셋은 변경에서 그대가 댈 수 있는 누구 못지않게 그 솜씨를 아는 사람들이고. 아니, 위험은 아니오. 다만 그대가 이를테면 한바탕 부산한 몰아치기라 부를 만한 일을 겪게 되긴 할 거요. 그리고 어쩌면 한 차례 충돌이나 작은 접전, 뭐 그런 여흥 같은 게 벌어질 수도 있겠지. 허나 늘 엄폐할 데가 좋고 탄약이 넉넉한 곳에서 말이오.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉가 위험을 헤아리는 잣대는 척후의 그것과 어느 정도 달랐던 모양이니, 그는 대답하는 대신 이제 말없이 앉아 있었고, 그동안 카누는 몇 마일의 물 위를 미끄러져 갔다. 막 동이 트자, 그들은 호수의 좁은 길목으로 들어서, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 작은 섬들 사이를 재빠르고 조심스럽게 빠져나갔다. 〈몽칼름〉이 군대를 이끌고 물러간 길도 바로 이 길이었다. 모험가들은 그가 제 군대의 후미를 지키고 낙오자들을 거두려 인디언 몇을 매복시켜 두었을지도 모른다고 여겼다. 그리하여 그들은 평소의 경계하는 습성다운 침묵 속에 그 길목으로 다가갔다.\n\n〈칭가치국〉은 노를 내려놓았고, 그동안 〈웅카스〉와 척후는 굽이지고 뒤얽힌 물길로 가벼운 배를 몰았으니, 그곳에서는 한 발짝 나아갈 때마다 진로 위로 무언가가 불쑥 솟구칠 위험에 그들을 내맡기게 되었다. 카누가 나아가는 동안 〈사가모어〉의 두 눈은 섬에서 섬으로, 덤불에서 덤불로 빈틈없이 움직였다. 더 트인 수면이 허락할 때면, 그 날카로운 눈길은 그 좁은 해협을 험상궂게 내려다보는 헐벗은 바위와 우뚝 매달린 숲을 따라 뻗어 갔다.\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 그곳의 아름다움 때문이기도 하고 제 처지에서 비롯된 자연스러운 우려 때문이기도 하여 갑절로 마음이 쓰이는 구경꾼이었던 터라, 자신이 충분한 까닭도 없이 후자의 감정을 일게 했던 것이라고 막 믿어 갈 무렵, 〈칭가치국〉의 신호에 따라 노가 움직임을 멈췄다.\n\n\"우후!\" 〈웅카스〉가 외쳤으니, 그 아비가 카누 옆구리를 가볍게 두드려 위험이 가까웠음을 알린 바로 그 순간이었다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\" 척후가 물었다. \"호수는 바람이 한 번도 분 적 없는 듯 잔잔하고, 나는 그 수면을 따라 몇 마일이나 내다볼 수 있는데. 수면을 점찍을 검은 아비새 머리 하나조차 없잖느냐.\"\n\n인디언은 엄숙하게 노를 들어, 제 한결같은 눈길이 못 박혀 있는 방향을 가리켰다. 〈던컨〉의 눈이 그 움직임을 좇았다. 그들 앞으로 몇 로드 떨어진 곳에 또 하나의 낮고 숲 우거진 섬이 놓여 있었으나, 그것은 마치 그 적막함이 인간의 발길에 한 번도 흐트러진 적 없는 듯 고요하고 평화로워 보였다.\n\n\"아무것도 안 보이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"뭍과 물뿐이고, 사랑스러운 경치로구려.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 척후가 가로막았다. \"그렇지, 〈사가모어〉, 자네가 하는 일엔 늘 까닭이 있는 법이지. 그저 그늘에 지나지 않지만, 그래도 자연스럽지가 않아. 소령, 저 섬 위로 피어오르는 안개가 보이오? 그걸 안개라고는 부를 수 없소, 가느다란 구름 한 줄기에 더 가까우니까.\"\n\n\"물에서 올라온 수증기겠지요.\"\n\n\"그건 어린아이라도 알 수 있소. 허나 그 아래쪽에 걸려 있는, 개암나무 덤불까지 죽 따라 내려가 볼 수 있는 저 더 검은 연기의 가장자리는 뭐겠소? 저건 불에서 난 거요. 허나 내 판단으로는 일부러 약하게 사위어 가도록 둔 불이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 저곳으로 밀고 가서 의심을 떨쳐 버립시다.\" 참을성 없는 〈던컨〉이 말했다. \"저만한 땅뙈기에 숨을 수 있는 무리라면 분명 적을 거요.\"\n\n\"인디언의 교활함을 책에서 찾은 규칙이나 백인의 분별로 헤아린다면, 그것은 그대를 그릇된 길로, 죽음으로까지는 아니더라도 어긋난 데로 이끌 거요.\" 그를 남다르게 하던 그 예리함으로 그곳의 징후를 살피며 〈호크아이〉가 대답했다. \"이 일에 대해 내가 말하는 것이 허락된다면, 우리에게 선택할 길이 둘뿐이라는 거요. 하나는, 되돌아가서 〈휴런〉족을 쫓을 생각을 모두 단념하는 것.\"\n\n\"결코 안 되오!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 그들의 처지에 비추어 너무도 큰 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"좋소, 좋아.\" 그의 조급함을 누르려 황급히 손짓을 보내며 〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다. \"나 자신도 그대 마음과 크게 다르지 않소. 다만 다 말해 두는 것이 내 경험에 어울린다고 여겼을 뿐이오. 그러니 우리는 밀고 나아가야 하고, 만약 인디언이나 프랑스인이 그 길목에 있다면, 저 우뚝 솟은 산들 사이를 뚫고 빗발치는 매질을 견뎌 내야 하오. 내 말에 이치가 있는가, 〈사가모어〉?\"\n\n인디언은 노를 물속에 떨구어 카누를 앞으로 밀어내는 것 말고는 다른 어떤 대답도 하지 않았다. 진로를 정하는 소임을 그가 맡고 있었던 만큼, 그 움직임만으로 그의 결의는 충분히 드러났다. 이제 온 일행이 힘차게 노를 저었고, 몇 순간이 채 못 되어 그들은 그때껏 가려져 있던 면, 곧 섬의 북쪽 기슭 전체를 한눈에 볼 수 있는 지점에 다다랐다.\n\n\"저기 있군, 온갖 징후가 다 들어맞았어.\" 척후가 속삭였다. \"카누 두 척에 연기 한 줄기. 그 악당들이 아직 안개에서 눈을 떼지 못했군, 안 그랬으면 그 저주받을 함성이 들렸을 텐데. 함께, 여보게들! 우리는 놈들을 따돌리고 있고, 벌써 총알 휘파람 소리가 닿을 거리에서 거의 벗어났소.\"\n\n소총의 그 익숙한 발사음과 함께, 그 총알이 잔잔한 해협 수면을 따라 튀듯 날아왔고, 섬에서 날카로운 비명이 터져 나와 그의 말을 끊으며, 그들의 통과가 발각되었음을 알렸다. 다음 순간, 여러 미개인들이 카누로 달려드는 것이 보였고, 카누들은 곧 추격에 나서 물 위에서 너울너울 춤추었다. 다가오는 싸움을 알리는 이 무시무시한 전조들도, 〈던컨〉이 알아챌 수 있는 한, 그의 세 인도자의 표정이나 움직임에는 아무런 변화도 일으키지 못했으니, 다만 그들의 노질이 더 길어지고 더 한 호흡으로 맞아 들어, 그 작은 배가 마치 생명과 의지를 지닌 생물처럼 앞으로 솟구치게 했을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"저놈들을 거기 잡아 두게, 〈사가모어〉.\" 여전히 노를 저으며 왼쪽 어깨 너머로 침착하게 뒤를 돌아보며 〈호크아이〉가 말했다. \"딱 거기 붙들어 두라고. 저 〈휴런〉족에겐 이 거리에서 명중시킬 만한 총이 그 부족 전체에 한 자루도 없네. 허나 '킬디어'에겐 사람이 믿고 셈할 수 있는 총신이 있지.\"\n\n척후는 〈모히칸〉족만으로도 필요한 거리를 유지하기에 충분하다는 것을 확인하고는, 차분히 노를 내려놓고 그 치명적인 소총을 들어 올렸다. 세 차례나 그는 그 총을 어깨에 갖다 댔고, 동행들이 그 발사음을 기다릴 적마다, 그는 그만큼 다시 총을 내려, 인디언들에게 적이 좀 더 가까이 다가오도록 두라고 청했다. 마침내 그의 정확하고 까다로운 눈이 만족한 듯했고, 왼팔을 총신 위에 쭉 뻗고 총구를 천천히 들어 올리던 차에, 뱃머리에 앉아 있던 〈웅카스〉의 외침이 다시 한번 그 발사를 멈추게 했다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐냐, 얘야?\" 〈호크아이〉가 다그쳤다. \"그 한마디로 너는 〈휴런〉 한 놈을 죽음의 비명에서 구해 줬다. 네가 하는 일에 까닭이 있느냐?\"\n\n〈웅카스〉는 그들 앞으로 조금 떨어진 바위 기슭을 가리켰으니, 그곳에서 또 다른 전투용 카누가 그들의 진로를 곧장 가로질러 쏜살같이 달려오고 있었다. 그들의 처지가 절박하게 위태롭다는 것은 이제 너무도 분명하여, 말의 도움을 빌려 확인할 필요조차 없었다. 척후는 소총을 내려놓고 다시 노를 잡았고, 그동안 〈칭가치국〉은 이 새로운 적과의 거리를 벌리려 카누의 뱃머리를 서쪽 기슭 쪽으로 조금 기울였다. 그러는 사이 그들의 후미를 바짝 좇는 자들이 거칠고 의기양양한 함성으로 제 존재를 일깨웠다. 이 들끓는 장면은 〈먼로〉마저 무기력에서 깨어나게 했다.\n\n\"본토의 바위로 향합시다.\" 지친 군인의 표정으로 그가 말했다. \"그리고 저 미개인들과 한판 싸웁시다. 나든, 나와 내 핏줄에 매인 자들이든, 다시는 〈루이〉의 그 어떤 신하의 신의에도 의지하는 일이 없게 하소서, 신이여 막아 주소서!\"\n\n\"인디언과의 전쟁에서 잘 헤쳐 나가고자 하는 자는,\" 척후가 대답했다. \"원주민의 슬기에서 배우기를 마다할 만큼 거만해선 안 되오. 배를 뭍에 더 붙이게, 〈사가모어〉. 우리는 그 악당들을 따돌리려 휘돌고 있으니, 어쩌면 놈들이 멀리 셈을 내고 우리 자취를 끊으려 들지도 모르네.\"\n\n〈호크아이〉의 짐작은 틀리지 않았으니, 〈휴런〉족은 자신들의 항로가 추격 대상의 뒤로 처질 듯하다는 것을 알아채자, 차츰 더더욱 비스듬히 방향을 틀어 항로를 덜 곧게 만들었고, 이내 두 카누는 서로 이백 야드 안쪽에서 나란히 미끄러져 갔다. 이제 그것은 전적으로 속도의 겨룸이 되었다. 가벼운 배들이 어찌나 빠르게 나아갔던지, 그 앞에서 호수가 자잘한 물결을 일으키며 곱슬거렸고, 그 움직임 자체의 속도로 인해 배가 너울너울 출렁였다. 〈휴런〉족이 곧장 총기에 의지하지 않은 것은, 모든 손이 노에 매달려 있어야 했던 절박함에 더해, 아마도 이 사정 때문이었을 것이다. 달아나는 자들의 분투는 너무 가혹하여 오래 이어 갈 수 없었고, 추격자들은 수의 우위를 지니고 있었다. 〈던컨〉은 척후가 마치 그들의 도주를 도울 또 다른 방도를 찾기라도 하듯 근심스레 주위를 둘러보기 시작하는 것을 불안하게 지켜보았다.\n\n\"배를 해를 향한 데서 좀 더 비키게, 〈사가모어〉.\" 고집스러운 숲의 사내가 말했다. \"그 악당들이 한 놈을 소총에 떼어 놓는 게 보이네. 뼈 하나만 부러져도 우리 머릿가죽을 잃을 수 있어. 해에서 더 비키게, 그러면 우리가 섬을 사이에 두게 될 테니.\"\n\n그 방책은 쓸모가 없지 않았다. 길고 낮은 섬 하나가 그들 앞으로 조금 떨어진 곳에 놓여 있었고, 그들이 그 섬에 바짝 다가가자, 추격하는 카누는 쫓기는 자들이 지나간 쪽과 반대편으로 돌 수밖에 없었다. 척후와 그의 동행들은 이 이점을 놓치지 않았으니, 덤불에 가려 적의 눈에서 벗어난 순간, 앞서도 어마어마해 보이던 분투를 곱절로 쏟아부었다. 두 카누는 그 마지막 낮은 끝머리를 돌아 나왔으니, 마치 전속력으로 내달리는 두 마리 준마와도 같았고, 달아나는 쪽이 앞장서 있었다. 그러나 이 변화로 인해 그들은 서로 더 가까워졌고, 그러는 동안 그 상대적 위치는 뒤바뀌었다.\n\n\"〈웅카스〉, 자작나무 껍질 배의 됨됨이를 알아보는 식견이 있었구나, 그 〈휴런〉 카누들 가운데서 이것을 골랐으니 말이다.\" 척후가 미소 지으며 말했으니, 이제 막 조금씩 열리기 시작한 그 끝내 벗어날 가망보다는, 이 겨룸에서의 우위에 대한 흡족함에서 비롯된 미소인 듯했다. \"그 악마 같은 놈들이 다시 온 힘을 노에 쏟아붓고 있군, 그러니 우리는 흐릿한 총신과 정확한 눈 대신, 납작하게 깎은 나뭇조각으로 우리 머릿가죽을 위해 싸워야 하는 셈이야. 길게, 그리고 함께 젓게, 여보게들.\"\n\n\"저들이 사격을 준비하고 있소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"그리고 우리가 저들과 일직선상에 있으니, 빗나가기 어려울 거요.\"\n\n\"그러면 그대는 카누 바닥으로 들어가시오.\" 척후가 대답했다. \"그대와 대령 말이오. 그만큼 표적의 크기가 줄어들 테니.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"전사들이 총격을 받고 있는데 최고 계급에 있는 자가 몸을 피한다면, 그건 못된 본보기가 될 뿐이오!\"\n\n\"맙소사! 맙소사! 그게 바로 백인의 용기란 거지!\" 척후가 외쳤다. \"허나 그자의 다른 숱한 생각들이 그렇듯, 이치로 떠받칠 수 없는 거요. 〈사가모어〉나 〈웅카스〉, 아니 혼혈이 섞이지 않은 사내인 나조차도, 몸을 드러내 봐야 아무 득도 없는 접전에서 엄폐할 데를 찾기를 망설이리라 생각하시오? 싸움이 늘 트인 데서만 벌어질 거라면, 프랑스인들이 무엇하러 그 〈퀘벡〉을 쌓아 올렸겠소?\"\n\n척후는 〈모히칸〉들만으로도 필요한 거리를 유지할 수 있음을 확인하고는, 천천히 노를 옆에 내려놓고 그 치명적인 소총을 들어 올렸다\n\n〈휴런〉족의 일제 사격이 그 말을 끊었고, 총알들이 그들 주위로 휘파람 소리를 내며 날아드는 가운데, 〈던컨〉은 〈웅카스〉의 머리가 돌아가 자기와 〈먼로〉를 뒤돌아보는 것을 보았다. 적이 가까이 있고 제 몸 또한 크나큰 위험에 처했음에도, 그 젊은 전사의 얼굴에는, 〈던컨〉이 그리 생각할 수밖에 없었듯, 그토록 쓸데없이 몸을 드러내려는 자들을 보고 느끼는 놀라움 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 떠올라 있지 않았다. 〈칭가치국〉은 아마도 백인의 사고방식에 더 밝았던 모양인지, 진로를 다스리는 그 대상에 못 박힌 눈길을 곁으로 한 번 돌리지조차 않았다. 곧 총알 한 발이 그 추장의 손에서 가볍고 매끄러운 노를 쳐 내, 멀찍이 앞쪽 허공으로 날려 버렸다. 〈휴런〉족에게서 함성이 일었으니, 그들은 그 틈을 놓치지 않고 또 한 차례 일제 사격을 퍼부었다. 〈웅카스〉가 제 노로 물에 호를 그렸고, 카누가 잽싸게 지나가는 사이, 〈칭가치국〉은 노를 되찾아 그것을 높이 휘두르며 〈모히칸〉족의 전투 함성을 내질렀고, 그런 다음 다시 그 중대한 일에 제 힘과 솜씨를 쏟아부었다.\n\n\"르 그로 세르팡!\" \"라 롱그 카라빈!\" \"르 세르 아질!\" 하는 떠들썩한 외침이 뒤따르는 카누들에서 한꺼번에 터져 나와, 추격자들에게 새로운 열의를 불어넣는 듯했다. 척후는 왼손으로 '킬디어'를 거머쥐고, 머리 위로 높이 들어 올려, 적들을 향해 그것을 의기양양하게 흔들었다. 미개인들은 함성으로 그 모욕에 응답했고, 곧장 또 한 차례 일제 사격이 뒤따랐다. 총알들이 호수를 따라 후두두 떨어졌고, 그중 하나는 그들의 작은 배 껍질을 꿰뚫기까지 했다. 이 위태로운 순간에도 〈모히칸〉족에게서는 알아챌 만한 어떤 동요도 찾아볼 수 없었으니, 그 굳은 표정에는 희망도 경악도 떠올라 있지 않았다. 그러나 척후는 다시 고개를 돌려, 제 나름의 소리 없는 방식으로 웃으며 〈헤이워드〉에게 말했다.\n* 르 그로 세르팡(큰 뱀)은 〈칭가치국〉, 라 롱그 카라빈(긴 소총)은 〈호크아이〉, 르 세르 아질(날쌘 사슴)은 〈웅카스〉를 프랑스어로 일컫는 별칭이다.\n\n\"그 악당들은 제 총소리 듣는 걸 좋아하지. 허나 〈밍고〉족 가운데 춤추는 카누에서 참된 사거리를 셈할 수 있는 눈은 찾아볼 수 없소! 보시오, 그 멍청한 악마들이 한 놈을 장전하느라 떼어 놓았으니, 가장 인색하게 잡아도 우리는 놈들이 둘 갈 때 셋을 나아가는 셈이오!\"\n\n이 정교한 거리 셈을 동행들만큼 마음 편하게 받아들이지는 못한 〈던컨〉이었으나, 그래도 그들의 빼어난 솜씨와 적들 사이에 생긴 차질 덕에 그들이 눈에 띄게 우위를 얻어 가고 있다는 것을 알게 되어 기뻤다. 〈휴런〉족은 곧 다시 사격했고, 총알 한 발이 〈호크아이〉의 노 깃을 맞혔으나 상처를 입히지는 못했다.\n\n\"이 정도면 됐군.\" 그 얕은 자국을 신기한 눈으로 살피며 척후가 말했다. \"이건 갓난아이의 살갗도 베지 못했을 거요, 하물며 우리처럼 노한 하늘의 입김을 맞아 온 사내들의 살갗이야. 자, 소령, 그대가 이 납작하게 깎은 나뭇조각을 써 보겠다면, 나는 '킬디어'를 이 대화에 한몫 끼게 하겠소.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 노를 거머쥐고, 솜씨를 메우고도 남을 열의로 그 일에 매달렸고, 그동안 〈호크아이〉는 제 소총의 점화약을 살피는 데 몰두했다. 그런 다음 그는 잽싸게 겨누어 발사했다. 앞장선 카누 뱃머리의 〈휴런〉이 같은 목적으로 몸을 일으켰던 차에, 이제 그는 뒤로 나동그라지며 총이 손에서 물속으로 미끄러져 떨어지게 두었다. 그러나 순식간에 그는 다시 발을 딛고 일어섰으니, 그 몸짓은 거칠고 어지러웠다. 같은 순간 그의 동료들은 분투를 멈췄고, 추격하던 카누들은 한데 모여 멈춰 섰다. 〈칭가치국〉과 〈웅카스〉는 그 짬을 타 숨을 골랐으나, 〈던컨〉은 더없이 끈질긴 부지런함으로 계속 노를 저었다. 아비와 아들은 이제 사격으로 인해 둘 중 누구든 상처를 입지는 않았는지 알아보려 차분하되 캐묻는 눈길을 서로에게 던졌다. 그토록 절박한 순간에는 어떤 외침이나 비명도 그 사고를 드러내도록 허락되지 않으리라는 것을 둘 다 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 굵직한 핏방울 몇이 〈사가모어〉의 어깨를 타고 흘러내리고 있었으니, 그는 〈웅카스〉의 눈이 그 광경에 너무 오래 머무는 것을 알아채자, 오목한 손바닥에 물을 떠 그 얼룩을 씻어 내고는, 이 단출한 방식으로 상처가 가볍다는 것을 드러내는 데 만족했다.\n\n\"살살, 살살하시오, 소령.\" 이즈음 소총을 다시 장전한 척후가 말했다. \"우리는 이미 소총이 제 빼어남을 펼치기엔 조금 너무 멀리 와 있고, 보시다시피 저쪽 악마 같은 놈들이 회의를 열고 있소. 놈들을 명중시킬 거리 안으로 들어오게 둡시다. 이런 일이라면 내 눈은 믿어도 좋소. 그러면 내가 〈호리컨〉의 길이만큼 그 악당들을 따라가며 약속하리다, 놈들의 총알은 잘해야 살갗을 찢는 데 그칠 테고, '킬디어'는 세 번에 두 번은 목숨을 건드릴 거라고.\"\n\n\"우리는 우리 볼일을 잊고 있소.\" 부지런한 〈던컨〉이 대꾸했다. \"제발 이 우위를 이용해, 적과의 거리를 벌립시다.\"\n\n\"내 아이들을 다오.\" 〈먼로〉가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"아비의 고뇌를 더는 희롱하지 말고, 내 어린것들을 돌려다오.\"\n\n상관의 명령에 오래도록 몸에 밴 복종은 척후에게 따름의 덕을 가르쳐 두었다. 멀리 있는 카누들에 마지막으로 미련 어린 눈길을 던지고 나서, 그는 소총을 내려놓고, 지친 〈던컨〉과 자리를 바꿔, 결코 지치지 않는 근력으로 휘두르던 그 노를 다시 잡았다. 그의 분투에 〈모히칸〉족의 분투가 보태졌고, 몇 분이 채 못 되어 그들과 적들 사이에 그만한 수면이 펼쳐져, 〈헤이워드〉는 다시 한번 마음 놓고 숨을 쉬었다.\n\n이제 호수가 넓어지기 시작했고, 그들의 항로는 앞서처럼 높고 험준한 산들이 늘어선 너른 물길을 따라 뻗어 있었다. 그러나 섬은 드물어 쉽게 피할 수 있었다. 노질은 한결 가락 맞고 고르게 되었고, 노를 젓는 자들은 방금 벗어난 그 바짝 좇기던 죽음의 추격이 끝난 뒤에도, 마치 그들의 속도가 그토록 절박한, 아니 거의 필사적인 상황에서가 아니라 놀이로 겨루어진 것이기라도 한 듯한 침착함으로 제 노고를 이어 갔다.\n\n볼일이 그들을 이끄는 서쪽 기슭을 따르는 대신, 신중한 〈모히칸〉은 〈몽칼름〉이 군대를 이끌고 가공할 〈타이콘데로가〉 요새로 들어갔다고 알려진 저 언덕들 쪽으로 항로를 더 기울였다. 〈휴런〉족이 겉보기로는 추격을 단념한 듯했으니, 이토록 지나친 조심성에는 뚜렷한 까닭이 없었다. 그러나 그것은 호수의 북쪽 끝머리 가까이 한 만에 다다를 때까지 몇 시간이나 이어졌다. 여기서 카누를 모래톱에 끌어 올렸고, 온 일행이 뭍에 내렸다. 〈호크아이〉와 〈헤이워드〉는 인근의 벼랑에 올랐으니, 그곳에서 전자는 발아래 펼쳐진 물의 너비를 살펴본 뒤, 후자에게 몇 마일 떨어진 곶 아래에 어른거리는 작고 검은 물체 하나를 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"저게 보이오?\" 척후가 물었다. \"자, 그대가 이 황야를 헤쳐 갈 길을 백인의 경험에만 맡겨진 채 혼자 찾아야 한다면, 저 점을 무엇이라 여기겠소?\"\n\n\"그 거리와 크기만 아니라면, 새라고 여겼을 거요. 살아 있는 것일 수 있소?\"\n\n\"저건 질 좋은 자작나무 껍질 카누요, 사납고 교활한 〈밍고〉족이 노 젓는. 섭리가 숲에 사는 자들에게는, 시력을 돕는 발명품들이 있는 정착지의 사람들에게라면 필요 없을 눈을 빌려주셨으나, 그래도 어떤 인간의 기관도 이 순간 우리를 에워싼 온갖 위험을 다 볼 수는 없소. 저 악당들은 주로 해 질 녘 끼니에만 마음을 쏟는 척하지만, 날이 어두워지는 순간 우리 자취를 좇을 거요, 냄새 맡은 사냥개만큼이나 어김없이. 우리는 놈들을 따돌려야 하오, 안 그러면 르 르나르 쉬브틸을 쫓는 일은 단념해야 할지도 모르오. 이런 호수들은 때로는 쓸모가 있소, 특히 사냥감이 물로 들 때는.\" 근심 어린 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보며 척후가 말을 이었다. \"허나 물고기에게가 아니라면, 엄폐할 데를 내주지 않지. 정착지가 저 두 강에서 멀리까지 뻗어 나가게 된다면, 이 고장이 어찌 될지 신만이 아실 거요. 사냥도 전쟁도 그 아름다움을 잃겠지.\"\n\n\"마땅하고 분명한 까닭이 없는 한, 한 순간도 지체하지 맙시다.\"\n\n\"카누 위로 바위를 따라 꿈틀거리며 올라가는 저 연기가 영 마음에 들지 않는단 말이오.\" 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 척후가 가로막았다. \"내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 우리 말고 다른 눈들도 저걸 보고 그 뜻을 알고 있소. 그래, 말로는 이 일이 나아지지 않으니, 이제 우리가 움직일 때요.\"\n\n〈호크아이〉는 망보던 자리에서 물러나, 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 기슭으로 내려갔다. 그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 동행들에게 제 관찰의 결론을 전했고, 짧고 진지한 의논이 뒤따랐다. 그것이 끝나자, 셋은 즉시 그들의 새로운 결의를 실행하는 데 나섰다.\n\n카누를 물에서 들어 올려, 일행의 어깨에 짊어졌다. 그들은 숲속으로 나아가며, 되도록 넓고 또렷한 자취를 남겼다. 그들은 곧 물줄기 하나에 다다라 그것을 건넜고, 너르고 헐벗은 바위에 이를 때까지 계속 나아갔다. 발자국이 더는 보이지 않으리라 여겨질 만한 이 지점에서, 그들은 더없이 조심스럽게 뒷걸음질하며 시내까지 제 길을 되짚어 갔다. 이제 그들은 그 작은 시내의 바닥을 따라 호수까지 갔고, 그곳에서 곧장 카누를 다시 물에 띄웠다. 낮은 끝머리 하나가 곶에서 그들을 가려 주었고, 호숫가는 어느 정도 거리에 걸쳐 빽빽하게 우거져 드리운 덤불로 술처럼 둘러쳐져 있었다. 이 자연의 이점들에 몸을 가린 채, 그들은 끈기 있는 부지런함으로 길을 헤쳐 나아갔으니, 마침내 척후가 다시 한번 뭍에 올라도 안전할 듯하다고 판가름했다.\n\n저녁이 사물을 눈에 흐릿하고 어슴푸레하게 만들 때까지 멈춤은 이어졌다. 그러고 나서 그들은 다시 길에 올랐고, 어둠의 도움을 받아 서쪽 기슭을 향해 소리 없이 힘차게 밀고 나아갔다. 그들이 향하던 산의 험준한 윤곽이 〈던컨〉의 눈에는 아무런 뚜렷한 표지도 내보이지 않았으나, 〈모히칸〉은 노련한 키잡이의 확신과 정확함으로 자신이 골라 둔 작은 항구로 들어섰다.\n\n배를 다시 들어 올려 숲으로 옮겨, 덤불 더미 아래 조심스레 감추었다. 모험가들은 무기와 짐을 챙겼고, 척후는 〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉에게 자신과 인디언들이 마침내 나아갈 채비가 되었음을 알렸다. \"거기서 누군가를 발견하거든, 벼룩처럼 죽게 될 거요.\"\n\n그 일행은, 오늘날까지도 합중국 주민들에게는 〈아라비아〉의 사막이나 〈타타르〉의 초원만큼이나 알려지지 않은 한 지역의 경계에 발을 디뎠다. 그곳은 〈섐플레인〉의 지류들을 〈허드슨〉, 〈모호크〉, 〈세인트로렌스〉의 지류들로부터 갈라놓는, 메마르고 험준한 고장이었다. 우리 이야기가 펼쳐진 시기 이후로, 이 나라의 진취적인 기상이 그 둘레를 풍요롭고 번성하는 정착지의 띠로 에워쌌으나, 지금까지도 그 거친 후미진 곳을 뚫고 들어가는 자는 사냥꾼이나 미개인뿐이라 알려져 있다.\n\n그러나 〈호크아이〉와 두 〈모히칸〉은 이 광막한 황야의 산과 골짜기를 여러 차례 가로질러 본 터라, 그 궁핍과 곤란에 익숙한 자들 특유의 거리낌 없는 태도로, 망설임 없이 그 깊은 속으로 뛰어들었다. 여행자들은 여러 시간 동안 별 하나를 길잡이 삼거나 어느 물줄기의 방향을 좇으며 고된 길을 헤쳐 나아갔고, 이윽고 척후가 발걸음을 멈추라 외쳤으며, 인디언들과 짤막한 의논을 나눈 뒤, 불을 피우고 그날 밤의 남은 시간을 그 자리에서 보내려는 여느 준비를 갖추었다.\n\n보다 노련한 동료들의 본을 따르고 그 자신감을 본받아, 〈먼로〉와 〈던컨〉은 불안이 없지는 않았으나 두려움 없이 잠들었다. 이슬이 다 마르고, 해가 안개를 흩어 버려 숲에 강하고 맑은 빛을 드리울 무렵에야 여행자들은 다시 길을 떠났다.\n\n몇 마일을 나아간 뒤, 앞장서 이끌던 〈호크아이〉의 걸음이 한층 신중하고 조심스러워졌다. 그는 자주 멈춰 서서 나무들을 살폈고, 시내를 건널 때마다 그 물의 양과 빠르기와 빛깔을 유심히 헤아리지 않고서는 건너지 않았다. 제 판단을 미덥잖게 여기는 그는 〈칭가치국〉의 의견을 구하는 일이 잦고도 간곡했다. 이런 협의가 한창일 때, 〈헤이워드〉는 〈언카스〉가 끈기 있고 말없이 서 있되, 그가 짐작하기로는 관심을 가지고 귀 기울이는 자임을 알아차렸다. 그는 그 젊은 추장에게 말을 걸어 진행에 관한 의견을 물어보고 싶은 마음이 간절했으나, 그 원주민의 침착하고 위엄 있는 태도를 보고는, 그도 자신과 마찬가지로 일행 중 손위 어른들의 명민함과 지혜에 전적으로 기대고 있으리라 여기게 되었다. 마침내 척후가 영어로 입을 열어, 그들이 처한 곤경을 단번에 설명했다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉들이 돌아가는 길이 북쪽으로 났다는 걸 알았을 때,\" 그가 말했다, \"그자들이 골짜기를 따라가며 〈허드슨〉과 〈호리컨〉의 물줄기 사이를 지키다가, 마침내 〈캐나다〉 개울들의 샘에 닿아 그걸 타고 프랑스 놈들 땅 한복판으로 들어가리라는 건, 오랜 세월의 판단이 없어도 알 만한 일이었소. 헌데 우리가 지금 〈스카룬〉에서 멀지 않은 데까지 왔는데도, 흔적 하나 가로질러 본 적이 없단 말이오! 인간의 본성이란 약한 법이라, 어쩌면 우리가 제 냄새를 못 짚었는지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 그런 잘못으로부터 우릴 지켜 주시기를!\" 〈던컨〉이 외쳤다. \"온 길을 되짚으며, 더 날카로운 눈으로 가면서 살펴봅시다. 〈언카스〉는 이런 궁지에 내놓을 조언이 없소?\"\n\n젊은 〈모히칸〉은 제 아비에게 눈길을 던졌으나, 조용하고 삼가는 태도를 지킨 채 잠자코 있었다. 〈칭가치국〉이 그 눈길을 알아채고는, 손짓으로 말하라 일렀다. 이 허락이 떨어진 순간, 〈언카스〉의 표정은 엄숙한 평정에서 명민함과 기쁨이 번득이는 얼굴로 바뀌었다. 사슴처럼 펄쩍 뛰어 앞으로 나아가, 그는 몇 로드 앞 작은 비탈을 단숨에 올라, 어떤 무거운 짐승이 지나가며 갓 파헤친 듯한 신선한 흙더미 위에 의기양양하게 섰다. 일행 모두의 눈이 이 뜻밖의 움직임을 좇았고, 그 젊은이가 짓는 의기양양한 표정에서 그들의 성공을 읽었다.\n\n\"흔적이다!\" 척후가 그 자리로 다가서며 외쳤다. \"이 녀석은 나이에 비해 눈이 빠르고 머리가 예리하군.\"\n\n\"이렇게 오래도록 제가 아는 걸 감춰 두다니 별나구려.\" 〈던컨〉이 그의 곁에서 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"분부도 없이 입을 열었다면 그게 더 놀라운 일이었을 게요. 아니지, 아니야. 책에서 배움을 얻고 제가 아는 것을 책장 수로나 헤아리는 그대들 젊은 백인이야, 제 지식이 제 다리처럼 아비의 것을 앞지른다고 자만할 수도 있겠지. 허나 경험이 스승인 곳에서는, 배우는 자가 세월의 값어치를 알게 되고, 그에 걸맞게 그걸 존중하는 법이라오.\"\n\n\"보십시오!\" 〈언카스〉가 제 양쪽으로 또렷이 난 넓은 흔적의 자국을 가리키며 북쪽과 남쪽을 짚어 말했다. \"검은 머리는 서리 쪽으로 갔습니다.\"\n\n\"사냥개도 이보다 고운 냄새를 좇아 달린 적은 없을 게요.\" 척후가 곧장 그 가리킨 길로 내달으며 응답했다. \"우린 은혜를 입었소, 큰 은혜를 입었으니, 코를 높이 들고 좇을 수 있겠소. 그래, 여기 그대들의 뒤뚱대는 짐승 두 마리가 다 있군. 이 〈휴런〉은 꼭 백인 장군처럼 다니는구려. 이놈은 천벌이 내려 미친 게요! 바퀴 자국을 잘 살피게, 〈사가모어〉.\" 그가 뒤를 돌아보며, 새로 깨어난 만족감에 웃음을 터뜨리며 이었다. \"조만간 저 멍청이가 마차를 타고 다니는 꼴을 보겠소, 그것도 변경에서 가장 뛰어난 눈 세 쌍을 등 뒤에 달고서 말이오.\"\n\n척후의 들뜬 기분과, 사십 마일이 넘는 우회의 거리를 지나온 이 추적의 놀라운 성공은, 일행 전체에게도 어느 정도 희망을 나누어 주지 않을 수 없었다. 그들의 전진은 빨랐고, 여행자가 넓은 한길을 따라 나아가듯 그만한 자신감으로 이루어졌다. 바위나 시내, 혹은 여느 것보다 단단한 흙뙈기가 그들이 좇는 단서의 고리를 끊어 놓으면, 척후의 정확한 눈이 멀찍이서 그것을 되찾았고, 단 한순간의 지체조차 좀처럼 필요로 하지 않았다. 〈마구아〉가 골짜기를 통해 여행하기를 어쩔 수 없이 택했다는 확신은 그들의 진행을 크게 도왔으니, 이 사정이 노정의 전반적 방향을 확실하게 해 주었기 때문이다. 그렇다고 그 〈휴런〉이 어떤 적 앞에서 물러설 때 원주민들이 한결같이 쓰는 술수를 아주 게을리한 것도 아니었다. 개울이나 지형이 그럴 만한 곳이면 어디든 거짓 흔적과 느닷없는 방향 전환이 잦았으나, 추격자들은 좀처럼 속지 않았고, 그 기만의 자취에서 시간이나 거리를 잃기 전에 어김없이 잘못을 가려냈다.\n\n오후가 한가운데에 이를 무렵 그들은 〈스카룬〉을 지났고, 기우는 해의 방향을 좇고 있었다. 어느 높은 언덕을 내려와 시내가 미끄러져 흐르는 나지막한 골짜기에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 느닷없이 〈르나르〉의 일행이 멈춰 쉬었던 자리에 닿았다. 꺼진 장작이 샘 둘레에 흩어져 있었고, 사슴의 내장이 그 자리 여기저기에 널려 있었으며, 나무들은 말들이 뜯어 먹은 흔적을 또렷이 지니고 있었다. 조금 떨어진 곳에서 〈헤이워드〉는 〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉가 쉬었으리라 믿고 싶은 작은 나뭇가지 그늘막을 발견하고는 애틋한 감정으로 바라보았다. 그러나 땅은 밟혀 있었고 사람과 짐승의 발자국이 그 자리 둘레에 그토록 또렷이 보였음에도, 흔적은 느닷없이 끊긴 듯했다.\n\n〈내러갠셋〉 말들의 자취는 좇기 쉬웠으나, 그것들은 길잡이도 없이, 먹이를 찾는 것 말고는 다른 목적도 없이 그저 헤매 다닌 듯할 뿐이었다. 마침내 제 아비와 더불어 말들의 노정을 더듬으려 애쓰던 〈언카스〉가, 자못 최근의 것인 그것들의 흔적을 찾아냈다. 그 단서를 좇기에 앞서, 그는 동료들에게 제 성과를 알렸고, 그들이 그 정황을 두고 의논하는 동안, 그 젊은이가 안장이 부서지고 안장깔개가 더럽혀진 두 어린 암말을 끌고 다시 나타났으니, 말들은 며칠 동안 멋대로 돌아다니도록 내버려진 듯한 꼴이었다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 뜻일까요?\" 〈던컨〉이 창백해진 얼굴로, 마치 덤불과 나뭇잎이 무슨 끔찍한 비밀을 토해 낼까 두려운 듯 사방으로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"우리 행군이 곧 끝났다는 것, 그리고 우리가 적의 땅에 들어와 있다는 뜻이오.\" 척후가 대답했다. \"그 악당들이 쫓기는 처지였고 그 가녀린 분들이 일행을 따라가는 데 말이 필요했다면, 그자는 머리 가죽을 벗겨 버렸을지도 모르오. 허나 뒤를 쫓는 적도 없는 데다 저런 거친 짐승들을 데리고서라면, 그자는 두 분 머리카락 한 올도 다치게 하지 않을 게요. 그대 속마음을 내가 아오, 그리고 그대에게 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다는 게 우리 피부빛에 부끄러운 일이긴 하오. 허나 〈밍고〉조차 도끼로 찍어 죽이려는 게 아닌 다음에야 여인을 함부로 대하리라 여기는 자는, 인디언의 본성도, 숲의 법도도 도무지 모르는 자요. 아니지, 아니야. 내 듣자 하니 프랑스 편 인디언들이 사슴을 사냥하러 이 산중에 들어왔다 하더군, 그리고 우린 그자들 진영의 냄새가 닿는 데까지 다가가고 있소. 그러지 말란 법이 있겠소? 〈타이〉의 아침저녁 대포 소리가 이 산중에서 날마다 들리니 말이오. 프랑스 놈들이 왕의 영토와 〈캐나다〉 사이에 새 경계선을 긋고 있으니까. 말들이 여기 있는 건 사실이나, 〈휴런〉들은 떠나 버렸소. 그러니 그자들이 떠난 길을 찾아봅시다.\"\n\n이제 〈호크아이〉와 두 〈모히칸〉은 본격적으로 그 일에 매달렸다. 둘레가 수백 피트쯤 되는 원이 그려졌고, 일행은 저마다 그 한 자락씩을 제 몫으로 맡았다. 그러나 살핀 결과 아무것도 찾아내지 못했다. 발자국의 자취는 수없이 많았으나, 모두 그 자리를 떠날 뜻 없이 그 주위를 헤매 다닌 자들의 것처럼 보였다. 다시 척후와 그의 동료들은 멈춰 쉰 자리를 한 바퀴 돌았으니, 저마다 천천히 앞사람을 좇다가 다시 한가운데에 모였으나, 출발할 때보다 조금도 더 알아낸 바가 없었다.\n\n\"이런 교활함에는 악마의 솜씨가 깃들지 않았을 리 없지.\" 〈호크아이〉가 조력자들의 실망한 낯빛과 마주치자 외쳤다.\n\n\"우린 이걸 끝까지 파고들어야 하오, 〈사가모어〉, 샘에서 시작해서 한 치 한 치 땅을 훑어 갑시다. 그 〈휴런〉이 제 부족에게 자국을 남기지 않는 발을 가졌노라 떠벌리게 두진 않을 거요.\"\n\n스스로 본을 보이며, 척후는 새로워진 열의로 정밀 조사에 나섰다. 뒤집히지 않은 잎 하나 없었다. 막대기를 치우고 돌을 들어 올렸으니, 인디언의 교활함이 흔히 이런 것들을 가림막으로 삼아, 더없는 인내와 부지런함으로 나아가며 발자국 하나하나를 감추곤 한다고 알려져 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 아무것도 찾아내지 못했다. 마침내 그 민첩함 덕에 제 몫의 일을 가장 먼저 끝낸 〈언카스〉가, 샘에서 흘러나오는 흐릿한 작은 도랑을 가로질러 흙을 긁어모아, 그 물길을 다른 골로 돌려놓았다. 둑 아래 좁은 바닥이 마르자마자, 그는 날카롭고 호기심 어린 눈으로 그 위로 몸을 숙였다. 곧이어 터진 환호성이 그 젊은 전사의 성공을 알렸다. 일행 모두가 〈언카스〉가 가리킨, 축축한 충적토에 찍힌 모카신 자국이 있는 자리로 몰려들었다.\n\n\"이 녀석은 제 겨레의 영예가 될 게요.\" 〈호크아이〉가, 박물학자가 매머드의 어금니나 마스토돈의 갈비뼈에나 쏟을 법한 감탄으로 그 흔적을 바라보며 말했다. \"그래, 그리고 〈휴런〉들의 옆구리에 박힌 가시가 될 게요. 헌데 이건 인디언의 발자국이 아니로군! 무게가 발뒤꿈치에 지나치게 실렸고, 발끝이 네모졌으니, 마치 프랑스 무용수 하나가 끼어들어 제 부족 앞에서 비둘기 날갯짓 춤이라도 춘 것 같소! 〈언카스〉, 도로 뛰어가서 그 노래꾼 발 크기를 재 오너라. 저기 바위 맞은편, 산비탈에 그 고운 자국이 찍혀 있을 게다.\"\n\n그 젊은이가 이 심부름에 나선 동안, 척후와 〈칭가치국〉은 그 자국들을 유심히 살폈다. 치수가 맞아떨어졌고, 척후는 망설임 없이 그것이 〈데이비드〉의 발자국이라 단언했으니, 그가 또 한 번 신을 모카신으로 바꿔 신게 된 것이었다.\n\n\"이제 마치 〈르 쉬빌〉의 술수를 직접 본 듯 그 전모를 똑똑히 읽을 수 있소.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그 노래꾼은 그 재주가 주로 목구멍과 두 발에 있는 자라, 앞장세워졌고, 나머지는 그 발자국 모양을 흉내 내며 그의 자취를 밟은 것이오.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 〈던컨〉이 외쳤다, \"두 분의 흔적은 도무지 보이지 않는데——\"\n\n\"그 가녀린 분들 말이오.\" 척후가 가로막았다. \"그 악당이 두 분을 나르는 수를 찾아낸 거요, 어떤 추격자든 냄새를 떨쳐 냈다 여겨질 때까지 말이오. 내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 몇 로드 가기 전에 우린 두 분의 곱고 작은 발자국을 다시 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n이제 일행 모두가 그 도랑의 흐름을 좇아, 규칙적인 자국들에 근심 어린 눈을 둔 채 나아갔다. 물은 곧 다시 제 바닥으로 흘러들었으나, 양쪽 땅을 살피며 그 숲의 사내들은, 흔적이 그 아래에 있음을 아는 것으로 만족하며 길을 갔다. 반 마일이 넘게 지나서야, 그 도랑이 넓고 메마른 바위의 밑동을 바싹 끼고 잔물결을 일으켰다. 여기서 그들은 〈휴런〉들이 물에서 벗어나지 않았음을 확실히 하려고 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n그리한 것은 다행이었다. 재빠르고 민첩한 〈언카스〉가 곧, 인디언 하나가 무심코 밟은 듯한 이끼 덤불 위의 발자국을 찾아냈기 때문이다. 이 발견이 일러 준 방향을 좇아, 그는 이웃한 덤불로 들어가, 샘에 닿기 전과 다름없이 신선하고 또렷한 흔적을 짚어 냈다. 또 한 번의 외침이 그 젊은이의 행운을 동료들에게 알리며, 단번에 수색을 끝냈다.\n\n\"그래, 인디언의 판단으로 짜낸 계책이었소.\" 일행이 그 자리에 모여들자 척후가 말했다. \"백인의 눈쯤은 멀게 했을 거요.\"\n\n\"이제 가도 되겠소?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다.\n\n\"천천히, 천천히. 우리 길은 알았소만, 사물의 짜임새를 살펴보는 게 좋소. 이게 내 배움이오, 소령. 그리고 그 책을 게을리하면, 섭리의 펼쳐진 손에서 배울 기회는 거의 없는 법이오. 다 분명한데 한 가지만 그렇지 않으니, 그 악당이 이 보이지 않는 흔적을 따라 그 가녀린 분들을 어떻게 데려갔는가 하는 거요. 〈휴런〉이라도 그 여린 발을 물에 닿게 하기엔 자존심이 너무 셀 텐데 말이오.\"\n\n\"이게 그 의문을 푸는 데 도움이 되겠소?\" 〈헤이워드〉가, 나뭇가지로 거칠게 엮고 가는 버들로 동여매어 만든 일종의 들것 조각을 가리키며 말했으니, 그것은 이제 쓸모없어진 듯 아무렇게나 내버려진 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"풀렸소!\" 기뻐하며 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"그 악당들이 일 분이라도 묵었다면, 제 흔적에 거짓 끝을 지어내려 몇 시간은 들였을 게요! 그래, 나도 그자들이 이런 짓으로 하루를 허비하고도 별 소득 없는 꼴을 본 적이 있소. 여기 모카신 자국이 세 쌍, 그중 둘은 작은 발이오. 인간이 이토록 작은 사지로 여행할 수 있다니 놀랍구려! 〈언카스〉, 사슴 가죽 끈을 다오, 이 발 길이를 좀 재 보게. 맙소사, 아이의 것보다 길지도 않은데, 그 처녀들은 키도 크고 곱지 않은가. 섭리가 그 나름의 현명한 까닭으로 베푸는 데 편파적이라는 것은, 우리 가운데 가장 선하고 가장 자족하는 자라도 인정할 수밖에 없는 일이오.\"\n\n\"내 딸들의 여린 사지가 이런 고초를 견딜 만하지 못하오.\" 〈먼로〉가 제 아이들의 가벼운 발자국을 아비의 사랑으로 바라보며 말했다. \"우린 이 황야에서 기진맥진한 그 애들의 모습을 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"그건 두려워할 까닭이 거의 없소.\" 척후가 천천히 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"이건 가볍긴 해도 단단하고 곧은 걸음이고, 보폭이 그리 길지도 않소. 보시오, 발뒤꿈치가 땅에 거의 닿지도 않았소. 그리고 저기서 검은 머리가 뿌리에서 뿌리로 작게 폴짝 뛰었소. 아니지, 아니야. 내 지식을 걸고 말하건대, 이 근처에서 둘 중 누구도 기절할 지경은 아니었소. 헌데 그 노래꾼은 발이 부르트고 다리가 지쳐 가고 있었으니, 그 자취를 보면 분명하오. 저기, 보시오, 그가 미끄러졌소. 여기선 갈팡질팡 비틀거렸고, 또 저기선 마치 눈신을 신고 걸은 것 같소. 그래, 그래, 온통 제 목구멍만 쓰는 사내는 제 다리를 제대로 단련하기 어려운 법이라오.\"\n\n이렇듯 부인할 수 없는 증거로부터, 그 노련한 숲의 사내는, 마치 제 재간으로 그토록 쉽게 풀어낸 그 모든 사건을 직접 목격한 증인이기라도 한 양, 거의 그만한 확실성과 정확성으로 진상에 다다랐다. 이 단언들에 힘을 얻고, 그토록 단순하면서도 그토록 명백한 추론에 흡족해진 일행은, 서둘러 끼니를 들고자 잠시 멈춰 쉰 뒤 다시 길을 이었다.\n\n식사가 끝나자, 척후는 기우는 해를 흘긋 올려다보고는, 〈헤이워드〉와 여전히 정정한 〈먼로〉가 따라잡으려 온 근육을 다 써야 할 만큼 빠른 속도로 밀고 나아갔다. 이제 그들의 노정은 앞서 말한 골짜기를 따라 났다. 〈휴런〉들이 더는 제 발자국을 감추려 애쓰지 않았으므로, 추격자들의 진행은 더 이상 불확실함으로 지체되지 않았다. 그러나 한 시간이 채 지나기도 전에, 〈호크아이〉의 속도가 눈에 띄게 누그러졌고, 그의 머리는 전처럼 곧장 앞을 향하는 대신, 마치 다가오는 위험을 의식한 듯 이쪽저쪽으로 의심스레 돌기 시작했다. 그는 곧 다시 멈춰 서서, 일행 전부가 따라붙기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉들의 냄새가 나오.\" 그가 두 〈모히칸〉에게 말했다. \"저기 나무 우듬지 너머로 트인 하늘이 있고, 우린 그자들 진영에 너무 가까이 다가가고 있소. 〈사가모어〉, 그대는 오른편 산비탈을 맡으시오. 〈언카스〉는 왼편 개울을 따라 굽이쳐 가고, 나는 흔적을 따라가 보겠소. 무슨 일이 생기면, 신호는 까마귀 울음 세 번이오. 방금 저 죽은 떡갈나무 너머 공중에서 그 새 한 마리가 제 깃을 부치는 걸 봤소——그것도 우리가 진영에 닿아 가고 있다는 또 하나의 징표요.\"\n\n인디언들은 대꾸 없이 저마다 제 길로 떠났고, 〈호크아이〉는 두 신사와 함께 조심스레 나아갔다. 〈헤이워드〉는 그토록 큰 노고와 근심으로 좇아온 그 적들을 한시바삐 엿보고 싶은 마음에, 곧 길잡이의 곁으로 바싹 다가섰다. 그의 동행은, 여느 때처럼 덤불로 가장자리가 둘린 숲의 끄트머리로 살금살금 가서 자신이 올 때까지 기다리라 일렀으니, 한쪽 옆에서 미심쩍은 어떤 징표들을 좀 살펴보고 싶었기 때문이다. 〈던컨〉은 시키는 대로 했고, 곧 새롭기도 하거니와 예사롭지 않기도 한 광경을 한눈에 굽어볼 수 있는 자리에 서게 되었다.\n\n수 에이커에 걸쳐 나무가 베어 넘겨져 있었고, 온화한 여름 저녁의 노을빛이 그 개간지 위로 내려앉아, 숲의 잿빛과 아름다운 대조를 이루었다. 〈던컨〉이 선 자리에서 얼마 떨어지지 않은 곳에서, 시내는 작은 호수로 넓어진 듯, 산에서 산까지 그 낮은 땅 대부분을 뒤덮고 있었다. 물은 이 너른 웅덩이에서 흘러나가 폭포를 이루었는데, 어찌나 고르고 부드럽던지, 자연이 빚었다기보다 차라리 사람의 손이 만든 것처럼 보였다. 흙으로 지은 백여 채의 집이 호숫가에, 심지어 그 물속에까지 서 있었으니, 마치 물이 여느 둑을 넘어 불어난 것 같았다. 비바람을 막기에 절묘하게 빚어진 그 둥근 지붕들은, 원주민들이 늘 머무는 거처에, 더구나 사냥과 전쟁이라는 일시적인 목적으로 차지하는 거처에 좀처럼 들이지 않던 부지런함과 선견을 드러냈다. 요컨대, 마을이라 부르든 읍이라 부르든, 그 전체는 백인들이 으레 인디언의 습속에 속한다고 믿어 온 것보다 더 많은 짜임새와 솜씨의 단정함을 지니고 있었다. 그러나 그곳은 버려진 듯 보였다. 적어도 〈던컨〉은 여러 분 동안 그렇게 여겼다. 그러나 마침내, 여러 사람의 형체가 네 발로 그를 향해 다가오며, 보아하니 무언가 무거운——그리고 그가 재빨리 알아챈 바로는 무언가 가공할——기계를 끌고 오는 것을 발견한 듯했다. 바로 그때 거뭇한 머리 몇몇이 집들에서 번뜩였고, 그곳은 갑자기 어떤 존재들로 살아 움직이는 듯했으니, 다만 그것들이 너무도 잽싸게 이 가림막에서 저 가림막으로 미끄러져 다니는 탓에, 그 기질이나 행위를 살필 틈이 도무지 없었다. 이 미심쩍고 알 수 없는 움직임에 놀란 그는, 막 까마귀 신호를 보내려던 참이었는데, 가까이서 잎이 바스락거리는 소리에 그의 눈길이 다른 쪽으로 끌렸다.\n\n그 젊은이는 흠칫하며 본능적으로 몇 걸음 뒷걸음쳤으니, 자신이 낯선 인디언 하나와 백 야드도 안 되는 거리에 있음을 알아차렸기 때문이다. 그는 곧 정신을 가다듬고, 자칫 제 목숨에 치명적일 수도 있는 경보를 울리는 대신, 그 자리에 멈춰 선 채 상대의 움직임을 주의 깊게 지켜보는 관찰자가 되었다.\n\n잠시 차분히 살핀 끝에 〈던컨〉은 자신이 들키지 않았음을 확신했다. 그 원주민도 그처럼 마을의 나지막한 집들과 주민들의 은밀한 움직임을 살피는 데 골몰한 듯했다. 그 얼굴을 가린 기괴한 칠 가면 너머로 그 표정을 읽어 내기란 불가능했으나, 〈던컨〉은 그것이 사납다기보다 차라리 우울하다고 여겼다. 그의 머리는 여느 때처럼 박박 밀려 있었고, 다만 정수리만 남아, 그 다발에서 매의 날개에서 뽑은 빛바랜 깃털 서너 개가 느슨히 흔들렸다. 너덜너덜한 옥양목 망토가 그 몸을 절반쯤 둘렀고, 아랫도리는 평범한 셔츠로 되어 있었으니, 그 소매는 으레 훨씬 더 편리한 차림이 맡는 구실을 하도록 쓰였다. 다리는 맨살이었고, 가시덤불에 베이고 찢겨 보기 딱했다. 그러나 발에는 좋은 사슴 가죽 모카신 한 켤레가 신겨 있었다. 통틀어, 그 사람의 행색은 처량하고 비참했다.\n\n〈던컨〉이 그 이웃한 자의 풍채를 여전히 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피고 있을 때, 척후가 소리 없이 조심스레 그의 곁으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"보다시피 우리가 그자들의 거처, 아니 진영에 닿았소.\" 젊은이가 속삭였다. \"그리고 여기 미개인 하나가, 우리가 더 움직이기에 자못 거북한 자리에 있구려.\"\n\n〈호크아이〉가 흠칫하며 소총을 떨어뜨렸으나, 동행의 손가락이 가리키는 대로 그 낯선 자가 그의 시야에 들어왔다. 그러자 그 위험한 총구를 내리고, 그는 마치 이미 더없이 날카로운 관찰을 거들려는 듯, 긴 목을 앞으로 뺐다.\n\n\"이 마귀 같은 놈은 〈휴런〉이 아니오.\" 그가 말했다, \"〈캐나다〉 부족 중 어느 것도 아니지. 헌데 보다시피, 옷차림으로 보아 이 악당이 백인 하나를 약탈한 게요. 그래, 〈몽칼름〉이 제 침공을 위해 숲을 샅샅이 훑어, 함성을 질러 대며 살육하는 악당들을 한데 그러모은 게요. 그자가 제 소총이나 활을 어디 두었는지 보이오?\"\n\n\"무기는 없는 듯하고, 사납게 굴 기색도 없어 보이오. 보다시피 물가를 요리조리 피해 다니는 제 동료들에게 경보를 알리지만 않는다면, 우리가 저자에게서 두려워할 건 거의 없소.\"\n\n척후가 〈헤이워드〉를 돌아보며, 한순간 감추지 못한 놀라움으로 그를 바라보았다. 그러더니 입을 크게 벌리고, 거리낌 없는 진심 어린 웃음에 빠져들었으니, 다만 위험이 오래도록 그에게 가르쳐 온 그 소리 없고 독특한 방식이었다.\n\n\"물가를 요리조리 피해 다니는 동료들이라!\" 그 말을 되뇌며 그가 덧붙였다. \"정착지에서 학교 다니고 어린 시절을 보낸 게 고작 이 꼴이구려! 허나 그 악당은 다리가 길어서, 믿을 수 없소. 그대는 그자를 소총 겨냥 아래 두고 있으시오, 내가 덤불을 헤치고 뒤로 기어들어 산 채로 잡을 테니. 무슨 일이 있어도 쏘지는 마시오.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 이미 동행이 제 몸 일부를 덤불에 묻도록 내버려 두었다가, 한 팔을 뻗어 그를 붙잡아, 이렇게 물었다——\n\n\"그대가 위험에 처한 게 보이면, 한 발쯤 쏴도 되지 않겠소?\"\n\n〈호크아이〉가 한순간, 그 물음을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 모르는 자처럼 그를 바라보았다. 그러더니 고개를 끄덕이며, 여전히 소리 없이 웃으면서 대답했다——\n\n\"한 소대를 통째로 쏘시오, 소령.\"\n\n다음 순간 그는 잎새에 가려 보이지 않았다. 〈던컨〉은 척후를 다시 한 번 엿보기까지 몇 분을 애타는 초조함 속에 기다렸다. 이윽고 그가 다시 나타났으니, 옷이 거의 분간되지 않을 만큼 땅에 바싹 붙어, 잡으려는 그 포로의 바로 뒤로 기어가고 있었다. 그 뒤로 몇 야드 안에 이르자, 그는 소리 없이 천천히 몸을 일으켜 섰다. 바로 그 순간, 물 위로 요란한 타격음이 여러 번 울렸고, 〈던컨〉이 눈을 돌리니 마침맞게, 백여 개의 거뭇한 형체가 한 덩어리로 그 일렁이는 작은 수면 속으로 뛰어드는 것이 보였다. 소총을 움켜쥐고, 그의 눈길은 다시 가까이 있는 인디언에게로 향했다. 그 영문 모르는 미개인은 놀라기는커녕, 그도 일종의 어리석은 호기심으로 그 음울한 호수 주위의 움직임을 지켜보기라도 하듯 목을 앞으로 뺐다. 그동안 〈호크아이〉의 치켜든 손이 그 위에 있었다. 그러나 이렇다 할 까닭도 없이 그 손이 거두어졌고, 그 임자는 또 한 번, 여전히 소리 없는 긴 웃음에 빠져들었다. 〈호크아이〉의 그 독특하고 진심 어린 웃음이 끝나자, 그는 제 먹잇감의 목을 움켜쥐는 대신, 그 어깨를 가볍게 톡톡 치며 큰 소리로 외쳤다——\n\n\"어떤가, 친구! 비버들에게 노래를 가르칠 셈인가?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 거침없는 대답이 돌아왔다. \"그들에게 그분의 선물을 그토록 잘 갈고닦을 힘을 주신 분이, 그분을 찬양할 목소리야 마다하시지 않을 듯하오만.\"\n\n보텀: 우리 다 모였소?\n\n퀸스: 그래, 그래, 그래. 그리고 여기야말로 우리 연습을 하기에 기막히게 알맞은 자리로구먼.\n\n「한여름 밤의 꿈」\n\n〈헤이워드〉의 놀라움은 우리가 묘사하기보다 독자가 상상하는 편이 나으리라. 숨어 있던 인디언은 별안간 네발짐승으로 바뀌었고, 그가 본 호수는 비버 못으로, 폭포는 그 부지런하고 영리한 네발짐승들이 쌓아 올린 둑으로, 의심하던 적은 시험을 거친 벗인 성가의 명수 〈데이비드 거뮤트〉로 변했다. 후자가 모습을 드러내자 자매들에 관한 뜻밖의 희망이 수없이 솟아올라, 젊은이는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 매복처를 박차고 나와, 그 자리의 두 주역에게 합류하려 앞으로 내달렸다.\n\n〈호크아이〉의 흥겨움은 좀처럼 가라앉지 않았다. 그는 거리낌 없이, 거친 손으로 유연한 〈거뮤트〉를 한 발로 빙그르르 돌려 세우고는, 〈휴런〉족이 그의 차림새를 꾸민 솜씨로 보아 제 명성을 한껏 드높였다고 거듭 단언했다. 그러고는 상대의 손을 거머쥐어, 차분한 〈데이비드〉의 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌 만큼 꽉 쥐고 흔들며, 새로운 처지를 축하해 주었다.\n\n\"비버들 사이에서 막 목청을 가다듬을 참이었던 게요, 안 그렇소?\" 그가 말했다. \"저 교활한 녀석들은 벌써 그 기술의 절반은 알고 있다오. 방금 들었다시피 꼬리로 박자를 맞추니까. 게다가 마침맞은 때였소. 안 그랬으면 〈킬디어〉가 녀석들 사이에서 첫 음을 울렸을지도 모르니. 나는 글을 읽고 쓸 줄 알면서도 경험 많은 늙은 비버보다 더한 바보들을 본 적이 있소. 허나 깩깩대는 짓이라면, 저 짐승들은 벙어리로 태어났단 말이오! 이런 노래는 어떻게 생각하시오?\"\n\n〈데이비드〉는 예민한 귀를 틀어막았고, 그 울음소리의 정체를 익히 알고 있던 〈헤이워드〉조차, 까마귀의 까악 소리가 주위 공기를 갈랐을 때 그 새를 찾아 위를 올려다보았다.\n\n\"보시오!\" 웃음 짓던 척후가 신호에 따라 이미 다가오고 있던 나머지 일행을 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"이건 본디 제 쓸모를 지닌 음악이오. 칼과 도끼는 말할 것도 없거니와, 좋은 소총 두 자루를 내 곁으로 불러들이니 말이오. 허나 그대가 무사하다는 건 알겠소. 자, 이제 그 처녀들이 어찌 되었는지 말해 주시오.\"\n\n\"그분들은 이교도들에게 사로잡힌 몸입니다.\" 〈데이비드〉가 말했다. \"허나 정신은 몹시 괴로울지언정, 몸은 평안과 안전을 누리고 계십니다.\"\n\n\"두 사람 다요?\" 〈헤이워드〉가 숨죽이며 물었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 비록 우리 여정이 고되고 양식이 변변치 못했으나, 이렇게 사로잡힌 채 머나먼 땅으로 끌려가며 감정에 입은 상처를 빼고는 달리 탄식할 까닭이 거의 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 말 한마디로 그대에게 축복이 있기를!\" 떨리는 목소리로 〈먼로〉가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 내 어린것들을, 잃었을 때와 똑같이 티 없고 천사 같은 모습으로 돌려받겠구나!\"\n\n\"그분들의 구출이 가까웠는지는 모르겠습니다.\" 미덥지 못한 듯 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"이 야만인들의 우두머리는 전능하신 분이 아니고서는 그 무엇으로도 길들일 수 없는 사악한 영을 지녔습니다. 잠들었을 때나 깨어 있을 때나 그를 시험해 보았으나, 음률도 말도 그의 영혼에 닿는 듯하지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"그 악당은 어디 있소?\" 척후가 무뚝뚝하게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"오늘은 젊은이들과 함께 큰사슴을 사냥하러 갔습니다. 또 듣자 하니 내일은 이 숲을 더 깊이, 〈캐나다〉 변경에 더 가까이 옮겨 간다고 합니다. 손위 처녀는 이웃한 부족에게로 보내졌는데, 그들의 오두막은 저 검은 바위 봉우리 너머에 자리해 있습니다. 손아래 분은 〈휴런〉족 여인들 가운데 붙들려 있는데, 그들의 거처는 여기서 짧게 두 마일가량 떨어진 평평한 고지에 있으니, 그곳은 불이 도끼의 노릇을 대신해 그들을 맞이할 자리를 마련해 둔 곳입니다.\"\n\n\"〈앨리스〉, 내 가여운 〈앨리스〉!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 중얼거렸다. \"언니가 곁에 있어 주던 위안마저 잃었구나!\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 허나 성가로 찬미하고 감사드리는 것이 고난 속의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 수 있는 한에서는, 그분도 괴로움만 겪지는 않으셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 처녀가 음악을 좋아하는 마음이 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"좀 더 진중하고 엄숙한 성질의 음악을 말입니다. 허나 내 온갖 애씀에도 불구하고, 그 처녀가 웃는 일보다 우는 일이 더 잦다는 것은 인정해야겠지요. 그런 때에는 거룩한 노래를 강권하기를 삼갑니다. 그래도 만족스러운 교감의 감미롭고 위안이 되는 시간이 여러 차례 있어, 우리 목소리가 드높이 울리면 야만인들의 귀가 깜짝 놀라곤 합니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 어찌하여 그대는 감시도 받지 않고 자유로이 돌아다니도록 허락받았소?\"\n\n〈데이비드〉는 겸손하고 점잖은 기색을 드러내려는 양 얼굴 표정을 가다듬고 나서, 온순하게 대답했다.\n\n\"나 같은 미천한 벌레에게 무슨 칭찬이 있겠습니까. 허나 우리가 지나온 그 피의 들판에서 벌어진 끔찍한 일 가운데 성가의 힘이 잠시 멎기는 했어도, 이제는 이교도들의 영혼에까지 그 영향력을 되찾았으니, 나는 마음대로 오가도록 허락받은 것입니다.\"\n\n척후는 웃으며, 의미심장하게 제 이마를 톡톡 두드렸으니, 다음과 같이 말하면서 그 유별난 관용을 어쩌면 더 흡족하게 설명한 셈이었다.\n\n\"인디언은 결코 머리가 성치 않은 자를 해치지 않소. 그런데 어찌하여, 눈앞에 길이 훤히 열려 있었거늘, 그대는 제가 온 자취를 되짚어 돌아가(다람쥐가 남길 자국만큼 흐릿하지도 않은데) 〈에드워드〉에 그 소식을 전하지 않았소?\"\n\n척후는 제 자신의 강건하고 무쇠 같은 천성만을 떠올리며, 〈데이비드〉가 어떤 사정에서도 해낼 수 없을 과업을 요구한 셈이었으리라. 그러나 후자는 점잖은 기색을 온전히 잃지는 않은 채, 이렇게 대답하는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n\"내 영혼은 다시 한번 기독교 세계의 거처를 찾아가기를 기뻐하겠지만, 내 두 발은, 사로잡힌 채 슬픔에 잠겨 시들어 가는 그분들을 두고 한 발짝이라도 물러서기보다는, 차라리 내게 맡겨진 그 가녀린 영혼들을, 저 예수회 우상 숭배자들의 영토까지라도 따라가는 편을 택하겠습니다.\"\n\n〈데이비드〉의 비유적인 말은 그리 알아듣기 쉽지 않았으나, 그 눈빛의 진실하고 한결같은 표정과 정직한 얼굴에 어린 홍조는 좀처럼 오해할 수 없는 것이었다. 〈언카스〉가 그의 곁으로 바짝 다가서서 그 말하는 이를 칭찬하는 눈빛으로 바라보았고, 그의 아버지는 여느 때처럼 짤막한 동의의 감탄으로 흡족함을 드러냈다. 척후는 고개를 가로저으며 다시 말했다.\n\n\"주님께서는 결코, 사람이 다른 더 나은 재능을 저버린 채 제 온 힘을 목구멍에만 쏟으라 하신 적이 없소! 허나 이 사람은, 푸른 하늘 아래 숲의 아름다움 속에서 가르침을 받았어야 마땅하거늘, 어느 어리석은 아낙의 손에 떨어지고 만 게요. 자, 친구. 나는 이 삑삑대는 그대의 피리로 불을 지필 작정이었소만, 그대가 그토록 아끼니, 받아서 한껏 불어 보시오!\"\n\n〈거뮤트〉는 자신이 수행하는 진중한 소임에 어울린다고 여기는 만큼의 강한 기쁨을 드러내며 음정 피리를 받아 들었다. 그것의 효능을 제 목소리와 견주어 거듭 시험해 보고, 그 가락이 조금도 상하지 않았음에 만족하고 나서, 그는 앞서 그토록 자주 언급된 작은 책 속의 가장 긴 시가 가운데 하나에서 몇 연을 읊어 보려는 자못 진지한 채비에 들어갔다.\n\n그러나 〈헤이워드〉가 그의 경건한 의도를 황급히 가로막고, 동료로 사로잡힌 이들의 과거와 현재 처지에 관한 물음을 이어 갔으니, 두 사람이 처음 만났을 때 그의 감정이 허락하던 것보다는 한결 조리 있는 방식이었다. 〈데이비드〉는 제 보물을 애틋한 눈길로 바라보면서도 어쩔 수 없이 대답해야 했으니, 특히 그 노존경할 아버지가, 차마 거절할 수 없을 만큼 압도적인 관심을 품고 물음에 가세한 까닭이었다. 척후 역시 적절한 기회가 닿을 때마다 핵심을 찌르는 질문을 던지기를 잊지 않았다. 이런 식으로, 비록 되찾은 악기에서 흘러나오는 어떤 위협적인 소리들로 메워진 잦은 중단이 있기는 했으되, 추적자들은 그들의 크고도 마음을 온통 사로잡은 목적—자매들의 구출—을 이루는 데 쓸모가 있을 법한 주요한 정황들을 알아내게 되었다. 〈데이비드〉의 이야기는 단순했고, 그 사실이라곤 몇 가지에 지나지 않았다.\n\n〈마구아〉는 안전하게 물러날 순간이 올 때까지 산 위에서 기다리다가, 마침내 내려와 〈호리컨〉 서쪽 기슭을 따라 〈캐나다〉 쪽으로 길을 잡았다. 교활한 〈휴런〉족은 길에 훤했고, 당장은 추격당할 위험이 없음을 잘 알았으므로, 그들의 행보는 더디지도 고되지도 않았다. 〈데이비드〉의 꾸밈없는 진술로 미루어 보건대, 그 자신의 존재는 환영받았다기보다 그저 용인된 셈이었다. 비록 〈마구아〉조차도, 위대한 정령이 그 지성을 찾아왔다고 여기는 자들을 인디언이 우러르는 그 경외심에서 전적으로 자유롭지는 않았으나 말이다. 밤이면 사로잡힌 이들에게 더없는 보살핌이 베풀어졌으니, 숲의 습기로 해를 입지 않게 하는 동시에 도망을 막기 위함이었다. 앞서 본 대로, 샘가에서 말들은 풀려났고, 그들의 자취가 멀고 길었음에도, 그들의 은신처로 이르는 모든 실마리를 끊고자 이미 말한 술책들이 동원되었다. 제 부족의 야영지에 다다르자, 〈마구아〉는 좀처럼 어기지 않는 방침에 따라 포로들을 갈라놓았다. 〈코라〉는 인접한 골짜기를 한동안 차지하고 있던 한 부족에게로 보내졌으나, 〈데이비드〉는 원주민의 관습과 내력에 너무도 어두워 그들의 이름이나 성격에 관해 흡족한 무언가를 단언할 수 없었다. 그는 다만 그들이 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에 맞선 지난 원정에 가담하지 않았다는 것, 〈휴런〉족 자신처럼 그들도 〈몽칼름〉의 동맹이라는 것, 그리고 우연히 한동안 그토록 가깝고 거북한 접촉으로 얽히게 된 그 호전적이고 야만적인 무리와, 비록 경계는 늦추지 않으면서도 우호적인 교류를 이어 가고 있다는 것만을 알 따름이었다.\n\n〈모히칸〉족과 척후는 그의 끊기고 불완전한 이야기에, 그가 말을 이어 갈수록 눈에 띄게 짙어지는 관심을 품고 귀를 기울였다. 그리고 〈코라〉가 붙들려 있는 그 부족의 생업을 설명해 보려 할 때, 후자가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"그들 칼의 생김새를 보았소? 〈영국〉식이었소, 〈프랑스〉식이었소?\"\n\n\"내 생각은 그런 헛된 것에 쏠려 있지 않았고, 오히려 처녀들의 마음을 위로하는 데 함께 어우러져 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"야만인의 칼을 그토록 하찮은 헛것이라 여기지 못할 날이 올지도 모르오.\" 척후가 상대의 둔함을 향한 짙은 경멸을 드러내며 받아쳤다. \"그들이 옥수수 잔치를 벌였소? 아니면 그 부족의 토템에 관해 무어라도 말할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"옥수수라면, 우리는 풍성한 잔치를 여러 차례 누렸습니다. 그 곡식이 알이 차 즙이 들 무렵이면 입에도 달고 위장에도 편안하니까요. 토템이라면, 그 뜻을 모르겠습니다만, 혹 그것이 인디언 음악의 기예에 어떤 식으로든 닿아 있는 것이라면, 그들에게 물어볼 것까지도 없습니다. 그들은 결코 목소리를 모아 찬미하는 법이 없으니, 우상 숭배자들 가운데서도 가장 불경한 축에 드는 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"그 점에서 그대는 인디언의 천성을 그릇 말하는 게요. 〈밍고〉족조차도 오직 참되고 살아 계신 하느님만을 경배하오. 전사로 하여금 제 손으로 빚은 형상 앞에 엎드리게 한다는 것은, 백인들의 사악한 날조요. 내 살빛의 부끄러움을 무릅쓰고 말하건대, 그렇소. 그들이 사악한 자와 휴전을 맺으려 애쓰는 것은 사실이오—제가 이길 수 없는 적과라면 누군들 그러지 않겠소!—허나 그들이 은혜와 도움을 바라 우러르는 것은 오직 위대하고 선한 정령뿐이오.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요.\" 〈데이비드〉가 말했다. \"허나 나는 그들의 그림 물감으로 그려진 기이하고 환상적인 형상들을 보았는데, 그것을 향한 그들의 흠모와 정성은 영적인 교만을 풍기더이다. 특히 하나가 그러했으니, 그것도 더럽고 역겨운 물건이었습니다.\"\n\n\"뱀이었소?\" 척후가 재빨리 물었다.\n\n\"그와 비슷했습니다. 비천하게 기어 다니는 거북의 모습을 하고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"흐읏!\" 귀 기울여 듣던 두 〈모히칸〉족이 한숨에 내뱉었다. 한편 척후는 중대하기는 하나 결코 반갑지는 않은 발견을 한 사람의 기색으로 고개를 가로저었다. 그러자 아버지가 〈델라웨어〉족의 언어로, 그 말을 알아듣지 못하는 이들의 주의마저 단번에 사로잡는 침착함과 위엄을 띠고 입을 열었다. 그의 몸짓은 인상적이었고, 때로 격렬했다. 한번은 팔을 높이 치켜들었는데, 그것을 내리는 동작에 가벼운 망토 자락이 젖혀지며, 마치 그 자세로 제 뜻을 강조하려는 듯 손가락 하나를 가슴에 얹었다. 〈덩컨〉의 눈이 그 움직임을 좇았고, 그는 방금 말한 그 짐승이 추장의 거무스름한 가슴 위에 푸른빛으로, 희미하기는 하나 아름답게 새겨져 있음을 알아보았다. 〈델라웨어〉족의 광대한 부족들이 거센 분열을 겪었다고 일찍이 들어온 모든 이야기가 그의 마음속을 스쳤고, 그는 걸린 이해관계에 대한 관심으로 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼 졸인 마음으로 말할 적절한 순간을 기다렸다. 그러나 그의 바람은 척후가 앞질러, 붉은 피부의 벗에게서 몸을 돌리며 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 하늘이 정하기에 따라 우리에게 길흉이 될 수 있는 무언가를 알아냈소. 〈사가모어〉는 〈델라웨어〉족의 고귀한 혈통으로, 그들 거북 일족의 위대한 추장이오! 이 일족 가운데 일부가, 저 노래꾼이 우리에게 말한 그 무리 가운데 끼어 있다는 것은 그의 말로 보아 분명하오. 그가 제 목구멍을 나팔 삼아 헛되이 날려 보낸 숨의 절반만이라도 신중한 물음에 썼더라면, 그들이 전사를 몇이나 헤아리는지 알 수 있었을 텐데. 우리가 나아가는 길은 도무지 위험한 길이오. 그대에게서 얼굴을 돌린 벗이, 그대의 머릿가죽을 노리는 적보다 더 피에 굶주린 마음을 품는 일이 흔하니 말이오.\"\n\n\"설명해 주시오.\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다.\n\n\"길고도 우울한 전승이오. 나로서는 떠올리기조차 마뜩잖은 이야기요. 그 악행이 주로 흰 피부의 사람들 손으로 저질러졌음을 부인할 수 없으니 말이오. 허나 그것은 끝내 형제의 도끼를 형제에게 겨누게 했고, 〈밍고〉족과 〈델라웨어〉족이 같은 길을 걷게 만들었소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그대는 〈코라〉가 머무는 그 무리가 바로 그 부족의 일부라고 의심하는 게요?\"\n\n척후는 고개를 끄덕여 동의를 표했으나, 괴로워 보이는 그 주제를 더 논하기를 피하고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 조바심 난 〈덩컨〉은 이제 자매들을 구해 내려는 성급하고 무모한 제안을 여럿 내놓았다. 〈먼로〉는 무기력을 떨쳐 낸 듯, 그 백발과 노존경할 연치로 보아 마땅히 거절했어야 할 만큼의 정중함으로 젊은이의 거친 계략에 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 척후는 연인의 열정이 조금 분출되도록 내버려 둔 뒤, 더없이 냉철한 판단과 극도의 인내가 요구될 이런 일에서 성급함이 얼마나 어리석은지를 그에게 깨우칠 방도를 찾아냈다.\n\n\"이렇게 하는 편이 좋겠소.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"이 사람을 여느 때처럼 다시 들여보내, 오두막에 머물면서 그 가녀린 처녀들에게 우리가 다가왔음을 알리게 하고, 우리가 신호를 보내 불러내어 의논할 때까지 기다리게 하는 거요. 친구, 그대는 쏙독새의 휘파람 소리와 까마귀의 울음을 구별할 줄 아시오?\"\n\n\"기분 좋은 새지요.\" 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"부드럽고 구슬픈 음을 내니까요! 다만 박자가 다소 빠르고 가락이 고르지 못합니다만.\"\n\n\"이 사람은 쏙독새를 말하는 게요.\" 척후가 말했다. \"좋소, 그대가 그 휘파람을 좋아하니, 그것을 그대의 신호로 삼읍시다. 그러면 기억하시오. 쏙독새의 울음이 세 번 거듭되는 것을 들으면, 그 새가 있음 직한 덤불로 나오는 거요—\"\n\n\"멈추시오.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 끼어들었다. \"내가 함께 가겠소.\"\n\n\"그대가!\" 깜짝 놀란 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"해가 뜨고 지는 것을 보는 데 싫증이라도 났소?\"\n\n\"〈데이비드〉가 살아 있다는 것이, 〈휴런〉족도 자비를 베풀 수 있다는 증거 아니오.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 허나 〈데이비드〉는 제 목청을 쓸 줄 알지. 제정신인 사람이라면 누구도 그런 재능을 그리 함부로 쓰지는 않을 게요.\"\n\n\"나도 미친 사람, 바보, 영웅 노릇을 할 수 있소. 요컨대, 내가 사랑하는 그녀를 구하기 위해서라면 무엇이든, 그 어떤 것이든 다 할 수 있소. 더는 반대를 입에 올리지 마시오. 나는 결심했소.\"\n\n〈호크아이〉는 한순간 말문이 막힌 경악 속에 젊은이를 바라보았다. 그러나 상대의 솜씨와 공로를 존중하여 그때껏 다소 고분고분 그의 지시에 따르던 〈덩컨〉이, 이제는 좀처럼 거스를 수 없는 태도로 윗사람의 위엄을 내세웠다. 그는 어떤 반박도 싫다는 표시로 손을 내저은 다음, 한결 누그러진 말투로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그대에게는 변장의 수단이 있잖소. 나를 바꿔 주시오. 원한다면 물감도 칠해 주시오. 요컨대, 무엇이든—바보로든—나를 변모시켜 주시오.\"\n\n\"섭리처럼 강력한 손으로 이미 빚어진 자가 변모할 필요가 있다고 말하는 것은, 나 같은 자가 할 노릇이 아니오.\" 못마땅해하던 척후가 중얼거렸다. \"그대들이 전쟁에 부대를 내보낼 때면, 같은 편에서 싸우는 자들이 언제 어디서 벗을 만날지 알 수 있도록, 적어도 표지와 야영지를 정해 두는 것이 현명하다고 여기지 않소.\"\n\n\"들어 보시오.\" 〈덩컨〉이 끼어들었다. \"그대도 사로잡힌 이들의 이 충직한 종자에게 들었듯, 인디언은 두 부족, 어쩌면 서로 다른 두 민족에 속해 있소. 그대가 〈델라웨어〉족의 한 갈래라 여기는 쪽에는 그대가 '검은 머리'라 부르는 분이 있고, 다른 한 분, 더 어린 숙녀는 틀림없이 우리의 공공연한 적인 〈휴런〉족에게 있소. 후자의 모험을 시도하는 것은 내 젊음과 신분에 마땅한 일이오. 그러니 그대가 자매 가운데 한 분의 구출을 위해 벗들과 교섭하는 동안, 나는 다른 분의 구출을 이루거나, 아니면 죽겠소.\"\n\n깨어난 젊은 군인의 기개가 그 눈에 번득였고, 그 몸은 그 영향 아래 위풍을 띠었다. 〈호크아이〉는 인디언의 술책에 너무도 익숙한지라 이 시도의 위험을 내다보지 못할 리 없었으나, 이 돌연한 결심을 어찌 꺾어야 할지 좀처럼 가늠이 서지 않았다.\n\n어쩌면 그 제안에는 그 자신의 강인한 천성과, 경험과 더불어 자라나 마침내 위험과 모험이 어느 정도 그의 삶을 즐기는 데 없어서는 안 될 것이 되어 버린 그 은밀한 무모한 모험에의 애착에 들어맞는 무언가가 있었으리라. 그는 〈덩컨〉의 계획에 계속 맞서기는커녕, 별안간 기분이 바뀌어 그 실행에 손을 보탰다.\n\n\"자.\" 그가 너그러운 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"물에 뛰어들려는 수사슴은 뒤따를 게 아니라 앞을 막아야 하는 법이오. 〈칭가치국〉에게는, 산을 녹슨 건초 더미처럼 보이게 하고 푸른 하늘을 손이 닿을 데까지 끌어다 놓으며 자연을 종잇조각에 옮겨 그리는 그 공병 장교의 아내만큼이나 갖가지 물감이 있다오. 〈사가모어〉도 그것을 쓸 줄 알지. 통나무에 앉으시오. 내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 그가 곧 그대를 천연덕스러운 바보로 만들어 줄 거요. 그것도 그대 마음에 쏙 들도록 말이오.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉이 응했다. 그리고 그 이야기를 귀 기울여 듣던 〈모히칸〉족이 선뜻 그 소임을 맡았다. 제 종족의 온갖 정교한 기예에 오래 단련된 그는, 인디언이 우호적이고 익살스러운 성정의 증거로 여기곤 하는 그 환상적인 그림자를, 더없는 솜씨와 빠르기로 그려 냈다. 자칫 전쟁을 향한 은밀한 마음으로 풀이될 만한 선이라곤 모두 조심스레 피하면서, 다른 한편으로는 우정으로 해석될 만한 도안들을 궁리해 넣었다.\n\n요컨대 그는 전사의 모든 외양을 어릿광대의 가장극에 온전히 내주었다. 그런 차림새는 인디언들 사이에서 드물지 않았다. 〈덩컨〉은 이미 옷차림으로 충분히 변장한 데다, 〈프랑스〉어에 밝았으므로, 동맹을 맺은 우호적인 부족들 사이를 떠도는 〈타이컨더로가〉 출신의 요술쟁이로 통할 만하다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 분명 있었다.\n\n물감을 넉넉히 칠했다고 여겨지자, 척후는 그에게 우정 어린 조언을 많이 건네고, 신호를 짜 맞추고, 서로 성공을 거둘 경우 만날 자리를 정해 주었다. 〈먼로〉와 그 젊은 벗의 작별은 한결 우울했다. 그래도 노인은, 좀 더 건강한 정신 상태였다면 그 따스하고 정직한 천성이 결코 용납하지 않았을 무심함으로 그 이별을 받아들였다. 척후는 〈헤이워드〉를 한쪽으로 데려가, 그 노병을 〈칭가치국〉에게 맡겨 어딘가 안전한 야영지에 머물게 하고, 그동안 자신과 〈언카스〉는 〈델라웨어〉족이라 믿을 만한 까닭이 있는 그 무리 사이에서 탐문을 이어 갈 작정임을 알렸다. 그러고는 다시 한번 주의와 조언을 거듭하며, 〈덩컨〉이 깊이 감동할 만한 엄숙함과 따뜻한 정으로 이렇게 끝맺었다.\n\n\"자, 그러니 신의 가호가 있기를! 그대는 내 마음에 드는 기개를 보여 주었소. 그것은 젊음의 선물이니, 더욱이 뜨거운 피와 굳센 심장을 지닌 자의 선물이오. 허나 제가 하는 말이 모두 참임을 알 만한 까닭이 있는 사내의 경고를 믿으시오. 〈밍고〉족의 교활함을 능가하거나 그 용기를 꺾으려면, 그대는 한껏 사내다워야 하고, 책에서 주워 모을 수 있는 것보다 더 날카로운 기지를 부려야 할 게요. 신의 가호가 있기를! 만일 〈휴런〉족이 그대의 머릿가죽을 차지하거든, 든든한 전사 둘을 거느린 사내의 약속을 믿으시오. 녀석들은 제 승리의 대가를, 머리카락 한 올마다 목숨 하나씩으로 치르게 될 게요. 내 말하건대, 젊은 신사여, 섭리께서 그대의 거사를 축복하시기를. 그것은 오롯이 선한 일이니 말이오. 그리고 기억하시오. 그 악당들의 허를 찌르기 위해서라면, 본디 흰 피부에게 타고난 재능이 아닐지라도 그것을 부리는 것이 정당하다는 것을.\"\n\n〈덩컨〉은 그 갸륵하고 마지못해하던 동료의 손을 따뜻하게 흔들어 잡고, 다시 한번 늙은 벗을 그에게 부탁하고, 그 축원에 답한 다음, 〈데이비드〉에게 앞장서라고 손짓했다. 〈호크아이〉는 기개 넘치고 모험심 강한 젊은이의 뒷모습을 잠시 드러내 놓고 감탄하며 바라보았다. 그러고는 미심쩍은 듯 고개를 가로저으며 돌아서서, 제 쪽 일행을 숲의 은밀한 품속으로 이끌고 들어갔다.\n\n〈덩컨〉과 〈데이비드〉가 잡은 길은 비버들이 닦아 놓은 공터를 곧장 가로질러, 그 못의 가장자리를 따라 뻗어 있었다.\n\n전자는, 절박한 위급 상황에서 도움이라곤 거의 줄 수 없을 만큼 그토록 단순하고 그토록 자격이 모자란 이와 단둘이 남게 되자, 비로소 제가 떠맡은 과업의 어려움을 절감하기 시작했다. 스러져 가는 빛은 사방으로 그토록 멀리 뻗은, 황량하고 거친 황야의 음울함을 더했고, 그가 사람들로 그득하다는 것을 익히 아는 그 작은 오두막들의 정적에마저 무시무시한 기운이 어려 있었다. 그 감탄스러운 구조물들과 영리한 거주자들의 놀라운 대비책을 바라보고 있자니, 이 광막한 야생의 짐승들조차 제 이성에 거의 맞먹는 본능을 지녔다는 생각이 들었다. 그리고 그는 제가 그토록 무모하게 자초한 그 불공평한 다툼을 불안 없이는 곱씹을 수 없었다. 그때 〈앨리스〉의 환한 모습이, 그녀의 비탄이, 그녀가 실제로 처한 위험이 떠올랐고, 제 처지의 온갖 위험은 까맣게 잊혔다. 〈데이비드〉를 다독이며, 그는 젊음과 거사 특유의 가볍고 힘찬 걸음으로 나아갔다.\n\n못 둘레로 거의 반원을 그리고 나서, 그들은 물길에서 갈라져 나와, 그들이 지나온 그 낮은 땅에서 살짝 솟은 둔덕의 마루로 올라가기 시작했다. 반 시간이 채 못 되어 그들은 또 다른 공터의 가장자리에 이르렀는데, 그곳도 비버들이 만든 온갖 흔적을 지니고 있었다. 그 영리한 짐승들은 아마 어떤 사고로 인해 그곳을 버리고, 지금 차지하고 있는 더 알맞은 자리로 옮기게 된 듯했다. 〈덩컨〉은 더없이 자연스러운 감정에 사로잡혀 한순간 머뭇거렸으니, 어떤 위험한 시도를 앞두고 거기에 제 온 힘이 다 필요하리라는 것을 은밀히 의식하는 사람이 기운을 그러모으려 잠시 멈추듯, 덤불 우거진 길의 엄폐물을 떠나기가 꺼려졌던 것이다. 그는 멈춘 김에, 짧고 황급한 눈길로 얻어 낼 수 있는 정보를 그러모았다.\n\n공터 맞은편, 그리고 시냇물이 한층 높은 데서 몇몇 바위를 넘어 쏟아져 내리는 지점 가까이에는, 통나무와 잡목과 흙을 뒤섞어 거칠게 지은 쉰에서 예순 채가량의 오두막이 보였다. 그것들은 아무 질서 없이 늘어서 있었고, 정연함이나 아름다움에는 거의 마음을 쓰지 않은 채 지어진 듯했다. 사실, 그 두 가지 점에서 〈덩컨〉이 방금 본 마을보다 너무도 못한지라, 그는 앞선 것에 못지않게 놀라운 두 번째 경악을 또 한 차례 겪게 되리라 예상하기 시작했다. 이 예상은, 어스름한 황혼 속에서, 오두막들 앞의 키 크고 거친 풀숲에서 스무 명에서 서른 명가량의 형상이 번갈아 솟아올랐다가 다시 시야에서 가라앉으며, 마치 땅속으로 파고드는 듯한 광경을 보았을 때 조금도 수그러들지 않았다. 그 형상들을 별안간 황급히 흘끗거린 모양으로 보아, 그들은 살과 피라는 예사롭고 흔한 재료로 빚어진 피조물이라기보다는, 차라리 어둑하게 번득이는 망령이나 그 밖의 어떤 이승의 것이 아닌 존재처럼 보였다. 여위고 헐벗은 형상 하나가 한순간 두 팔을 허공에 미친 듯이 휘젓는 것이 보이는가 싶더니, 그것이 차지하고 있던 자리가 텅 비었고, 그 형상은 별안간 어딘가 멀리 떨어진 다른 곳에 나타나거나, 똑같이 알 수 없는 성질을 지닌 또 다른 형상으로 바뀌어 있었다. 동료가 머뭇거리는 것을 알아챈 〈데이비드〉가 그 시선의 방향을 좇다가, 입을 열어 어느 정도 〈헤이워드〉의 정신을 되돌려 놓았다.\n\n\"여기엔 갈지 않은 비옥한 땅이 많구려.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 자화자찬이라는 죄스러운 누룩 없이 덧붙이건대, 이 이교의 거처에 잠시 머무는 동안 나는 길가에 좋은 씨앗을 적잖이 흩뿌렸소이다.\"\n\n\"부족들은 일하는 자들의 기예보다 사냥을 더 좋아하지요.\" 여전히 제 경이의 대상을 응시한 채, 무심한 〈덩컨〉이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"목소리를 드높여 찬미하는 것은 영혼에게 노역이라기보다 차라리 기쁨이건만, 슬프게도 이 아이들은 제 재능을 함부로 허비하는구려. 그 또래로서 자연이 이토록 후하게 성가의 자질을 베푼 자들을 나는 좀처럼 본 적이 없소. 그러면서도 분명코, 분명코 그것을 이토록 소홀히 하는 자들 또한 없소이다. 이제 사흘 밤을 여기 머무는 동안 세 차례나 저 개구쟁이들을 모아 거룩한 노래에 함께하게 하려 했으나, 그때마다 녀석들은 내 영혼을 얼어붙게 하는 고함과 울부짖음으로 내 애씀에 답하더이다!\"\n\n\"누구를 두고 하는 말이오?\"\n\n\"저기서 부질없는 익살로 귀한 순간을 허비하는 저 악마의 자식들 말이오. 아! 이 자포자기한 무리 사이에서는 규율의 유익한 단속이 거의 알려져 있지 않소. 자작나무가 지천인 고장에서 회초리란 도무지 구경할 수 없으니, 섭리의 가장 빼어난 축복이 저런 고함 속에 허비되는 것이 내 눈에 놀라울 것도 없는 일이외다.\"\n〈데이비드〉가 그 어린 무리를 향해 귀를 틀어막았으니, 바로 그때 녀석들의 고함이 숲을 가르며 날카롭게 울려 퍼졌다. 그러자 〈덩컨〉은 마치 제 미신을 비웃듯 입술을 비죽이면서 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"갑시다.\"\n귀를 막은 손은 떼지 않은 채, 노래의 명수가 따랐고, 두 사람은 함께 〈데이비드〉가 이따금 \"블레셋 사람들의 천막\"이라 부르곤 하던 곳을 향해 길을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"하나 들짐승이 사냥의\n\n특권을 주장할 수 있을지라도,\n사슴에게는 공간과 법도를 베풀어\n\n사냥개를 풀거나 활을 당기기 전에 여유를 주거니와,\n어슬렁대는 여우가 어디서, 어떻게, 언제\n\n덫에 걸리거나 죽임을 당하는지를 누가 헤아린 적이 있던가?\"\n\n「호수의 여인」\n배운 백인들의 야영지와 달리, 원주민들의 야영지가 무장한 사람들의 존재로 지켜지는 것은 드문 일이다. 온갖 위험이 다가오는 것을 아직 멀리 떨어져 있을 때 미리 잘 알아채는 까닭에, 인디언은 대개 숲의 징후를 읽는 자신의 지식과, 가장 두려워할 까닭이 있는 자들과 자신을 갈라놓는 길고도 험난한 길들을 믿고 안심한다. 그러나 어떤 운 좋은 우연의 겹침으로 척후의 경계를 따돌릴 방도를 찾아낸 적은, 더 가까운 곳에서 경보를 울려 줄 보초와 마주치는 일이 좀처럼 없다. 이 일반적인 관습에 더하여, 프랑스에 우호적인 부족들은 방금 가해진 일격의 무게를 너무도 잘 알고 있었으므로, 〈영국〉 왕실에 조공하는 적대 부족들로부터 어떤 임박한 위험이 닥치리라고는 염려하지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 〈덩컨〉과 〈데이비드〉가, 앞서 말한 익살을 부리며 놀던 아이들 한가운데에 들어섰을 때, 그들이 다가오는 기척은 거의 미리 알려지지 않았다. 그러나 두 사람이 눈에 띄자마자, 어린 무리 전체가 약속이라도 한 듯 날카롭고 경고하는 함성을 질렀고, 이어 마치 마법에 걸린 듯 방문객들의 시야에서 사라져 버렸다. 웅크린 개구쟁이들의 벌거벗은 황갈색 몸은, 그 시각에 시든 풀과 어찌나 곱게 어우러졌던지, 처음엔 정말로 땅이 그 형체들을 삼켜 버린 듯했다. 다만 놀라움이 가신 뒤 〈덩컨〉이 그 자리 여기저기로 좀 더 호기심 어린 눈길을 돌려 보니, 사방에서 검고 재빠르게 굴러가는 눈동자들과 마주칠 뿐이었다.\n\n더 성숙한 어른들의 판단으로부터 그가 겪게 될 듯한 그 검열의 성격을 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 미리 일러 주는 이 조짐에서 아무런 격려도 얻지 못한 까닭에, 젊은 군인은 한순간 물러서고 싶었다. 그러나 망설이는 기색을 보이기에는 이미 늦었다. 아이들의 외침이 가장 가까운 오두막 문간으로 열두엇의 전사를 끌어냈으니, 그들은 거기에 어둡고 사나운 무리를 이루어 모여 서서, 뜻밖에 자기들 가운데로 찾아든 자들이 더 가까이 다가오기를 근엄하게 기다렸다.\n어느 정도 이 광경에 익숙해진 〈데이비드〉가, 웬만한 장애물로는 흔들리지 않을 듯한 침착함으로 앞장서서 바로 그 건물 안으로 이끌었다. 그것은 마을의 으뜸가는 건물이었으니, 비록 나무껍질과 가지로 거칠게 지어졌으나, 그 부족이 〈영국〉 영토의 변경에 임시로 머무는 동안 평의회와 공적인 모임을 여는 오두막이었다. 〈덩컨〉은 문턱에 빽빽이 들어찬 미개인들의 검고 강인한 몸을 스치며 지날 때, 필요한 무관심의 외양을 갖추기가 어려웠다. 그러나 자신의 목숨이 침착함에 달려 있음을 의식한 그는, 발걸음을 바짝 뒤쫓는 동행의 분별에 의지하며, 나아가는 동안 이 자리에 맞게 생각을 추스르려 애썼다. 그토록 사납고 무자비한 적들과 그야말로 맞닿아 있음을 깨달았을 때 그의 피가 얼어붙었다. 그러나 그는 약점을 드러내지 않는 외양으로 오두막 한가운데로 길을 잡아 나아갈 만큼 자신의 감정을 다스렸다. 신중한 〈거뭇〉의 본을 따라, 그는 오두막 한구석을 채운 더미 아래에서 향기로운 잡목 한 묶음을 끌어내어, 말없이 자리에 앉았다.\n\n방문객이 지나가자마자, 유심히 살피던 전사들은 입구에서 물러나 그를 둘러싸고 자리를 잡았으니, 이방인의 위엄에 걸맞게 입을 열 만한 순간이 오기를 참을성 있게 기다리는 듯했다. 단연 더 많은 수가, 그 흔들거리는 건물을 떠받치는 곧추선 기둥에 게으르고 나른한 자세로 기댄 채 서 있었고, 그동안 가장 나이 많고 가장 이름난 추장 서너 명이 조금 더 앞으로 나와 땅바닥에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n타오르는 횃불 하나가 그곳에서 타고 있었으니, 공기의 흐름에 일렁이며 얼굴에서 얼굴로, 형체에서 형체로 그 붉은 빛을 던졌다. 〈덩컨〉은 그 빛을 이용해 주인들의 표정에서 자신이 받을 대접의 짐작되는 성격을 읽어 내려 했다. 그러나 그의 재간은, 그가 마주한 사람들의 차디찬 술책 앞에서 거의 소용이 없었다. 앞쪽의 추장들은 그의 몸에 거의 눈길도 주지 않은 채, 존경의 뜻이었을 법도 하나 불신으로 풀이하기도 아주 쉬운 태도로, 눈을 땅에 두고 있었다. 그늘 속의 사내들은 덜 조심스러웠다. 〈덩컨〉은 곧 그들의 탐색하면서도 몰래 던지는 눈길을 알아챘으니, 그것은 사실 그의 몸과 옷차림을 한 치 한 치 훑었고, 표정의 어떤 동요도, 어떤 몸짓도, 칠해진 무늬 한 줄도, 심지어 옷의 모양새 하나조차 놓치지 않고 평하지 않는 법이 없었다.\n\n마침내, 머리에 희끗희끗 흰빛이 섞이기 시작했으나 그 강건한 사지와 굳건한 걸음걸이로 보아 아직 사내로서의 임무를 감당할 만함을 드러내는 한 사내가, 아마도 들키지 않고 살펴보려 자리를 잡았을 어느 구석의 어둠에서 걸어 나와 입을 열었다. 그는 〈와이언도트〉, 곧 〈휴런〉의 말을 썼으므로, 그의 말은 〈헤이워드〉에게는 알아들을 수 없는 것이었으나, 그에 곁든 몸짓으로 미루어 분노보다는 예의로 발해진 듯했다. 〈헤이워드〉는 고개를 가로저으며, 대답할 수 없음을 나타내는 몸짓을 했다.\n\n\"내 형제들 가운데 프랑스 말이나 영국 말을 하는 이는 없소?\" 그가 앞엣것으로 말하며, 동의하는 끄덕임을 찾기를 바라며 이 얼굴 저 얼굴로 둘러보았다.\n\n한 사람 이상이, 그 말뜻을 알아채려는 듯 돌아보기는 했으나, 대답은 없었다.\n\n\"이토록 지혜롭고 용맹한 부족 가운데 단 한 사람도, '위대한 군주'께서 그 자녀들에게 말씀하실 때 쓰는 언어를 알아듣지 못한다고 생각하면,\" 〈덩컨〉이 천천히, 자신이 다룰 수 있는 가장 단순한 프랑스 말을 써서 이었다. \"나는 마음이 아플 것이오. 자신의 붉은 전사들이 자신을 그토록 우습게 여긴다고 믿으신다면, 그분의 마음이 무거우실 것이오!\"\n\n길고 근엄한 침묵이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 사지의 어떤 움직임도, 눈의 어떤 표정도, 그의 말이 자아낸 인상을 드러내지 않았다. 침묵이 주인들 사이의 미덕임을 알던 〈덩컨〉은, 생각을 정리하고자 기꺼이 그 관습에 의지했다. 마침내 앞서 그에게 말을 건넨 바로 그 전사가, 〈캐나다〉의 언어로 무뚝뚝하게 물으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"우리의 위대한 아버지께서 그 백성에게 말씀하실 때, 〈휴런〉의 혀로 하시오?\"\n\n\"그분은 자녀들을 구별하지 않으시오, 피부 빛깔이 붉든, 검든, 희든 말이오.\" 〈덩컨〉이 둘러대며 대답했다. \"허나 무엇보다 그분은 용맹한 〈휴런〉을 흡족히 여기시오.\"\n\n\"전령들이, 닷새 전 〈잉기즈〉의 머리에서 자라던 머릿가죽을 그분께 헤아려 바칠 때,\" 빈틈없는 추장이 물었다. \"그분은 어떤 식으로 말씀하시겠소?\"\n\n\"그들은 그분의 적이었소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 저도 모르게 몸서리치며 말했다. \"하여, 틀림없이 그분은 이렇게 말씀하실 것이오. 좋도다, 나의 〈휴런〉은 매우 용맹하도다.\"\n\n\"우리의 〈캐나다〉 아버지는 그리 생각지 않으시오. 자신의 인디언에게 상을 내릴 것을 내다보기는커녕, 그분의 눈은 뒤를 향해 있소. 죽은 〈잉기즈〉는 보면서도 〈휴런〉은 보지 못하시오. 이게 무슨 뜻이겠소?\"\n\n\"그분 같은 위대한 추장은 혀보다 생각이 많은 법이오. 그분은 자신의 자취에 적이 없는지를 살피시는 것이오.\"\n\n\"죽은 전사의 카누는 〈호리컨〉 위를 떠다니지 못하오.\" 미개인이 음울하게 대꾸했다. \"그분의 귀는 우리의 벗이 아닌 〈델라웨어〉를 향해 열려 있고, 그들이 그 귀를 거짓으로 채우고 있소.\"\n\n\"그럴 리 없소. 보시오, 그분은, 치유의 술법을 아는 사람인 내게, 그분의 자녀들, 곧 대호수의 붉은 〈휴런〉에게 가서, 병든 이가 있는지 물으라 명하셨소!\"\n\n〈덩컨〉이 떠맡은 신분에 대한 이 선포에 또 한 번의 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 모든 눈이 일제히 그의 몸으로 쏠렸으니, 마치 그 선언의 참됨이나 거짓됨을 캐묻기라도 하듯, 그 명민함과 예리함은 그들 검열의 대상으로 하여금 그 결과가 두려워 떨게 했다. 그러나 그는 앞서의 그 말하던 자에 의해 다시 안도하게 되었다.\n\n\"〈캐나다〉의 술법 부리는 자들이 제 살갗에 칠을 하오?\" 〈휴런〉이 차갑게 이었다. \"우리는 그들이 제 얼굴이 창백하다고 자랑하는 걸 들었소.\"\n\n\"인디언 추장이 백인 아버지들 가운데로 갈 때면,\" 〈덩컨〉이 더없이 침착하게 대답했다. \"그에게 내어 준 셔츠를 걸치고자 자신의 들소 가죽옷을 벗어 두는 법이오. 내 형제들이 내게 칠감을 주었기에, 나는 그것을 칠한 것이오.\"\n\n낮은 갈채의 웅성거림이, 부족을 향한 이 찬사가 호의로 받아들여졌음을 알렸다. 나이 든 추장이 칭찬의 몸짓을 했고, 동료 대부분이 이에 답하여 저마다 한 손을 내뻗으며 짧은 기쁨의 탄성을 내질렀다. 〈덩컨〉은 한결 자유로이 숨 쉬기 시작했으니, 검열의 무게가 지나갔다고 믿었기 때문이다. 게다가 자신이 꾸며 댄 직무를 뒷받침할 단순하고 그럴듯한 이야기를 이미 준비해 둔 터라, 마침내 성공하리라는 그의 희망은 한층 밝아졌다.\n\n손님이 방금 내놓은 말에 알맞은 대답을 하고자 생각을 가다듬기라도 하듯 잠깐의 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 또 다른 전사가 일어서서 말할 자세를 취했다. 그의 입술이 막 벌어지려는 찰나, 낮으나 무시무시한 소리가 숲에서 솟아올랐고, 곧이어 높고 날카로운 비명이 뒤따라, 그것이 늑대의 가장 길고 가장 애처로운 울부짖음에 맞먹을 만큼 길게 끌렸다. 갑작스럽고 무서운 그 방해에 〈덩컨〉은 자리에서 벌떡 일어났으니, 그토록 끔찍한 외침이 자아낸 효과 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못했다. 바로 그 순간, 전사들이 한 무리가 되어 오두막에서 미끄러져 나갔고, 바깥 공기는 우렁찬 함성으로 가득 차, 아직도 숲의 둥근 천장 아래 울리던 그 무서운 소리들을 거의 삼켜 버렸다. 더는 자신을 다스릴 수 없게 된 젊은이는 그곳을 박차고 나가, 이내 야영지의 경계 안에서 살아 있는 거의 모든 것을 망라한 무질서한 무리 한가운데에 섰다. 남자도, 여자도, 아이도, 늙은이도, 병약한 이도, 활동적인 이도, 강건한 이도 다 같이 밖에 나와 있었으니, 더러는 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 더러는 광적인 듯한 기쁨으로 손뼉을 쳤으며, 모두가 어떤 뜻밖의 사건에 대한 미개한 즐거움을 드러내고 있었다. 처음엔 그 소란에 어리둥절했던 〈헤이워드〉도, 뒤이은 광경으로 이내 그 까닭을 알아낼 수 있었다.\n\n하늘에는 아직, 갈래갈래 다른 길들이 빈터를 떠나 황야의 깊은 곳으로 들어가는 저 나무 우듬지 사이의 밝게 트인 곳들을 드러낼 만한 빛이 남아 있었다. 그 가운데 한 길 아래로, 한 줄로 늘어선 전사들이 숲에서 나와 천천히 거처들 쪽으로 다가왔다. 맨 앞의 한 사람이 짧은 장대를 들었는데, 나중에 드러났듯이, 거기에는 사람의 머릿가죽 여럿이 매달려 있었다. 〈덩컨〉이 들었던 그 소름 끼치는 소리는, 백인들이 그리 부적절하지 않게 \"죽음의 함성\"이라 일컫는 것이었으니, 외침이 거듭될 때마다 적 하나의 운명을 부족에게 알리려는 뜻이었다. 여기까지는 〈헤이워드〉의 지식이 그 설명을 도왔다. 그리고 그 방해가 성공을 거둔 전쟁 부대의 뜻밖의 귀환에서 비롯된 것임을 이제 알게 되자, 온갖 언짢은 느낌이, 그것이 자신에게 가져다준 때맞춘 위안과 무의미함에 대한 속으로의 자축 속에 가라앉았다.\n\n새로 도착한 전사들은 오두막에서 몇백 피트 떨어진 곳에 이르자 멈춰 섰다. 죽은 자들의 애도와 승리자들의 개선을 다 같이 나타내려던 그들의 애처롭고 끔찍한 외침이 완전히 그쳤다. 이제 그들 가운데 한 사람이, 그 표현 가득한 비명에 못지않게 듣는 이들에게는 알아들을 수 없으나 결코 소름 끼치지는 않는 말로 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 그리하여 전해진 소식이 받아들여진 그 미개한 황홀을 적절히 그려 내기란 어려운 일일 것이다. 야영지 전체가 한순간에 더없이 격렬한 부산스러움과 소동의 무대가 되었다. 전사들은 칼을 빼어 휘두르며 두 줄로 늘어서서, 전쟁 부대로부터 오두막까지 이어지는 통로를 이루었다. 여자들은 곤봉이며 도끼며, 손에 먼저 잡히는 공격 무기는 무엇이든 거머쥐고, 곧 닥칠 잔혹한 놀이에서 제 몫을 다하려 다투어 달려들었다. 아이들조차 끼지 않으려 하지 않았다. 그 도구를 다루기엔 너무 어린 사내아이들이 제 아비의 허리띠에서 토마호크를 잡아 빼어 대열 속으로 슬며시 끼어들었으니, 부모가 보여 준 미개한 천성을 곧잘 흉내 내는 자들이었다.\n\n빈터 곳곳에는 잡목 더미가 큼직하게 흩어져 있었고, 빈틈없고 나이 든 여자 하나가 곧 펼쳐질 구경거리를 밝힐 만큼 그것들에 불을 붙이느라 분주했다. 불길이 솟아오르자, 그 기세는 저물어 가는 한낮의 빛을 능가하여, 사물을 한층 또렷하면서도 한층 흉측하게 드러내는 데 한몫했다. 그 모든 광경이 한 폭의 강렬한 그림을 이루었으니, 그 액자는 어둡고 우뚝한 소나무 가장자리로 짜여 있었다. 막 도착한 전사들이 가장 멀리 놓인 형상들이었다. 조금 앞쪽에는 두 사내가 서 있었는데, 앞으로 벌어질 일의 주역으로 나머지 가운데서 뽑힌 듯했다. 빛이 그들의 이목구비를 또렷이 드러낼 만큼 강하지는 않았으나, 그들이 사뭇 다른 감정에 사로잡혀 있다는 것만은 아주 분명했다. 한 사람이 영웅처럼 제 운명을 맞을 채비를 한 듯 꼿꼿하고 굳건히 선 동안, 다른 한 사람은 마치 공포에 마비되거나 수치에 짓눌린 듯 고개를 떨구었다. 혈기 왕성한 〈덩컨〉은 앞엣사람을 향해 감탄과 연민의 강한 충동을 느꼈으나, 그 너그러운 감정을 드러낼 기회는 도무지 있을 수 없었다. 그래도 그는 그자의 가장 작은 움직임까지 간절한 눈으로 지켜보았다. 그리고 그자의 감탄스러우리만큼 균형 잡히고 날렵한 몸의 고운 윤곽을 더듬어 가며, 만약 그토록 고결한 결의에 떠받쳐진 사람의 힘이 그토록 혹독한 시련을 무사히 견뎌 낼 수 있다면, 눈앞의 이 젊은 포로도 이제 막 달려야 할 그 위태로운 경주에서 성공을 바랄 수 있으리라고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼다. 어느새 젊은이는 〈휴런〉의 거뭇한 대열에 더 가까이 다가가 있었고, 그 구경거리에 대한 그의 관심이 너무도 깊어진 나머지 거의 숨도 쉬지 못했다. 바로 그때 신호의 함성이 울렸고, 그에 앞섰던 잠깐의 정적이 한바탕 터져 나온 외침으로 깨졌으니, 그것은 앞서 들었던 그 어떤 소리도 한참 넘어서는 것이었다. 두 제물 가운데 더없이 비굴한 자는 움직임 없이 그대로 있었으나, 다른 자는 그 외침에 사슴의 날쌤과 빠르기로 그 자리에서 뛰쳐나갔다. 예상대로 적대하는 대열을 가로질러 돌진하는 대신, 그는 위험한 그 좁은 길에 막 들어서더니, 단 한 번의 일격을 가할 틈도 주지 않고 홱 방향을 틀어, 한 줄로 늘어선 아이들의 머리를 뛰어넘어, 단숨에 그 가공할 대열의 바깥쪽, 더 안전한 편에 이르렀다. 그 술책에 백 개의 목소리가 저주를 퍼부으며 응했고, 흥분한 무리 전체가 대열을 흩뜨려 사납게 어지러이 그 자리에 퍼져 나갔다.\n\n이제 십여 개의 활활 타는 더미가 그곳에 음산한 밝음을 던졌으니, 그곳은 악의에 찬 마귀들이 피비린내 나고 무도한 의식을 행하려 모여든 어떤 저주받고 초자연적인 투기장을 닮아 있었다. 뒤편의 형상들은, 눈앞을 스치듯 미끄러지며 광적이고 영문 모를 몸짓으로 공기를 가르는, 이 세상 것이 아닌 존재들처럼 보였다. 한편 불길을 지나는 자들의 미개한 격정은, 그 달아오른 얼굴을 가로질러 쏘아 대는 번득임으로 무섭도록 또렷이 드러났다.\n\n복수에 불타는 그토록 많은 적이 모인 가운데서, 달아나는 자에게 숨 돌릴 틈이 허락되지 않았으리라는 것은 쉽게 헤아릴 수 있다. 그가 막 숲에 다다른 듯 보이던 한순간이 있었으나, 그를 사로잡은 자들의 무리 전체가 그 앞을 가로막아, 그를 다시 그 무자비한 박해자들 한가운데로 몰아넣었다. 길이 막힌 사슴처럼 방향을 튼 그는, 화살의 빠르기로 갈래진 불기둥 하나를 꿰뚫고, 무리 전체를 무사히 지나, 빈터의 반대편에 모습을 드러냈다. 여기서도 그는 더 나이 들고 더 교활한 몇몇 〈휴런〉과 마주쳐 다시 방향을 돌려야 했다. 그는 한 번 더, 그 눈먼 혼란 속에서 안전을 찾으려는 듯 무리에 부딪쳐 보았고, 그러고 나서 여러 순간이 이어졌으니, 그동안 〈덩컨〉은 날쌔고 용맹한 이 젊은 이방인이 끝장났다고 믿었다.\n\n뒤엉켜 알 수 없는 혼란 속에 출렁이는 검은 사람 형상의 덩어리 말고는 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다. 그들 위로 팔이며 번득이는 칼이며 가공할 곤봉이 보였으나, 그 타격은 분명 닥치는 대로 가해지는 것이었다. 그 무서운 효과는 여자들의 찢어지는 비명과 전사들의 사나운 함성으로 한층 고조되었다. 이따금 〈덩컨〉은 어떤 필사적인 도약 속에 공기를 가르는 가벼운 형체를 언뜻언뜻 보았고, 그 포로가 아직도 그 놀라운 활동력을 마음껏 부리고 있기를, 믿었다기보다 차라리 바랐다. 갑자기 무리가 뒤로 굴러, 그 자신이 서 있던 자리께로 다가왔다. 뒤편의 묵직한 무리가 앞쪽의 여자와 아이들을 짓눌러 그들을 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다. 그 혼란 속에 이방인이 다시 나타났다. 그러나 사람의 힘이 그토록 혹독한 시련을 그리 오래 견뎌 낼 수는 없었다. 포로도 이를 의식하는 듯했다. 한순간 트인 틈을 이용해, 그는 전사들 사이에서 뛰쳐나가, 〈덩컨〉이 보기엔 마지막인 듯한 필사적인 안간힘으로 숲에 이르려 했다. 젊은 군인에게서는 아무런 위험도 염려할 게 없음을 아는 듯, 달아나는 자는 달려가는 길에 그의 몸을 거의 스칠 듯이 지났다. 힘을 아껴 두었던 키 크고 강건한 〈휴런〉 하나가 그 뒤꿈치에 바짝 붙어, 치켜든 팔로 치명적인 일격을 겨누었다. 〈덩컨〉이 발을 쑥 내밀자, 그 충격에 다급하던 미개인은 노리던 제물보다 여러 피트 앞으로 곤두박질쳐 나동그라졌다. 그 이점을 이용한 포로의 동작은 생각 그 자체보다도 빨랐으니, 그는 방향을 틀어 다시 〈덩컨〉의 눈앞에서 유성처럼 번득였고, 다음 순간, 〈덩컨〉이 정신을 되찾아 포로를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 그는 으뜸가는 오두막 문 앞에 서 있는 작고 칠해진 기둥에 조용히 기대 서 있었다.\n\n그 탈주에서 자신이 한 몫이 도리어 제 목숨을 위태롭게 할까 염려하여, 〈덩컨〉은 지체 없이 그 자리를 떴다. 그는, 처형에 어그러진 여느 무리가 그러하듯, 음울하고 시무룩하게 오두막께로 다가가는 군중을 뒤따랐다. 호기심이, 어쩌면 그보다 나은 어떤 감정이, 그로 하여금 그 이방인에게 다가가게 했다. 그는 그자가, 한 팔을 지켜 주는 기둥에 두른 채, 안간힘을 쓴 뒤라 거칠고 가쁘게 숨 쉬면서도, 고통의 기색 하나 새어 나오게 하기를 경멸하며 서 있는 것을 보았다. 이제 그의 몸은, 부족이 평의회에서 그 운명을 숙고하여 결정하기까지, 아득한 옛날부터 내려온 신성한 관습으로 보호받았다. 그러나 그 자리를 메운 자들의 심정에서 어떤 조짐이라도 끌어낼 수 있다면, 그 결과를 미리 점치기란 어렵지 않았다.\n\n뜻을 이루지 못한 여자들이 성공을 거둔 이방인에게 아낌없이 퍼붓지 않은 욕설이라곤, 〈휴런〉의 어휘에 알려진 것 가운데 하나도 없었다. 그들은 그의 분투를 비웃으며, 쓰라린 조롱으로, 그의 발이 손보다 낫더라, 화살이나 칼 쓸 줄도 모르는 주제에 날개가 어울리겠다, 하고 일러 댔다. 이 모든 것에 포로는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 다만 위엄과 경멸이 유달리 뒤섞인 자세를 지키는 것으로 만족했다. 그의 행운에 못지않게 그의 침착함에 약이 오른 그들의 말은 알아들을 수 없게 되었고, 이어 날카롭게 찢어지는 비명으로 바뀌었다. 바로 그때, 더미에 불을 붙이는 필요한 채비를 해 두었던 그 교활한 여자가 무리를 헤치고 나와, 포로 앞에 제 자리를 텄다. 이 노파의 추레하고 시든 몸은, 그가 사람의 것 이상의 교활함을 지녔다는 평판을 얻기에 족했으리라. 가벼운 옷자락을 뒤로 젖히고, 길고 앙상한 팔을 조롱하듯 내뻗으며, 자기 비웃음의 대상에게 더 잘 통하는 〈레나페〉의 말을 써서, 그가 큰 소리로 시작했다.\n\n\"보거라, 〈델라웨어〉여!\" 그가 그의 얼굴에 대고 손가락을 딱 튀기며 말했다. \"네 부족은 계집들의 족속이라, 총보다는 괭이가 네 손에 더 어울리느니라. 네 계집들은 사슴의 어미들이거니와, 너희 가운데 곰이나 들고양이나 뱀이 태어난다면, 너희는 달아나고 말리라. 〈휴런〉 처녀들이 네게 치마를 지어 줄 것이요, 우리가 네게 남편을 찾아 주마.\"\n\n이 공격에 한바탕 미개한 웃음이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 젊은 여자들의 부드럽고 가락 고운 웃음소리가, 더 나이 들고 더 악의에 찬 그 동료의 갈라진 목소리와 야릇하게 어우러졌다. 그러나 이방인은 그들의 온갖 짓에 흔들리지 않았다. 그의 머리는 미동도 없었다. 다만 그의 오만한 눈이 뒤편에서 말없이 시무룩하게 그 광경을 지켜보며 어슬렁대던 전사들의 거뭇한 형상 쪽으로 굴러갈 때를 빼고는, 누구든 그 자리에 있음을 의식하는 기색조차 조금도 내비치지 않았다.\n\n포로의 자제력에 격분한 여자는 두 손을 허리에 짚고, 도전하는 자세를 취하더니, 우리의 어떤 솜씨로도 종이에 옮겨 담아 낼 수 없는 말의 격류 속에 다시 터져 나왔다. 그러나 그의 숨결은 헛되이 소진되었으니, 비록 제 부족에서 욕설의 명수로 손꼽혔음에도, 그는 실로 입에 거품을 물 만큼 스스로를 분노로 몰아붙이도록 내버려 두었으되, 이방인의 미동 없는 형상에서는 근육 하나 떨리게 하지 못했다. 그의 무관심이 자아내는 영향이 다른 구경꾼들에게로 번지기 시작했다. 그리하여 막 소년의 처지를 벗어나 사내의 상태로 들어서려는 한 젊은이가, 그들의 제물 앞에서 토마호크를 휘두르고 여자의 조롱에 제 허튼 허풍을 보태며, 그 표독한 노파를 거들려 했다. 그러자 과연 포로가 빛 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려, 경멸을 넘어선 표정으로 그 풋내기를 내려다보았다. 다음 순간 그는 다시 기둥에 기댄 조용한 자세로 돌아갔다. 그러나 그 자세의 변화로 〈덩컨〉은 〈엉커스〉의 굳건하고 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈과 눈빛을 주고받을 수 있었다.\n\n놀라움에 숨이 막히고, 벗이 처한 위태로운 처지에 무겁게 짓눌린 〈헤이워드〉는, 그 눈빛이 자아낸 뜻이 어떤 알 수 없는 방식으로 포로의 운명을 재촉할까 두려워 떨며, 그 눈빛 앞에서 움찔 물러섰다. 그러나 그런 염려를 할 당장의 까닭은 없었다. 바로 그때 한 전사가 격분한 무리를 헤치고 들어왔다. 준엄한 몸짓으로 여자와 아이들을 옆으로 물러서게 한 그는, 〈엉커스〉의 팔을 잡아 평의회 오두막의 문 쪽으로 데려갔다. 그리로 모든 추장과 이름난 전사 대부분이 뒤따랐으니, 그 가운데로 근심에 잠긴 〈헤이워드〉도, 위험하게 자신에게 이목을 끌지 않고 들어갈 방도를 찾아냈다.\n\n그 자리에 있는 자들을, 부족 내의 신분과 영향력에 걸맞게 배치하는 데 몇 분이 들었다. 앞선 대면에서 따랐던 것과 매우 비슷한 차례가 지켜졌으니, 나이 들고 지위 높은 추장들이 타오르는 횃불의 강한 빛 안에서 그 널찍한 방의 한가운데를 차지했고, 그동안 더 젊고 더 낮은 자들은 뒤편에 늘어서서, 거뭇하고 윤곽 뚜렷한 얼굴들의 어두운 윤곽을 이루었다. 오두막의 바로 한복판, 별 한두 개의 반짝이는 빛이 들어오는 트인 구멍 바로 아래에, 〈엉커스〉가 차분하고 의연하고 침착하게 서 있었다. 그의 드높고 오만한 거동은 그를 사로잡은 자들에게도 헛되지 않았으니, 그들은 자주 그 몸으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 그 눈은 목적의 단호함을 조금도 잃지 않으면서도, 이방인의 대담함에 대한 감탄을 또렷이 드러냈다.\n\n필사적인 속도의 시련에 앞서 자신의 벗과 함께 앞으로 나섰던, 그리고 그 추격에 끼는 대신, 그 떠들썩한 소동 내내, 수치와 치욕을 나타내는 움츠러든 조각상처럼 남아 있던 것을 〈덩컨〉이 눈여겨본 그 인물의 경우는 사정이 달랐다. 그를 맞이하려 내민 손도 하나 없었고, 그의 움직임을 지켜볼 만큼 굽어보아 준 눈도 하나 없었음에도, 그 또한, 다투는 기색 없이 그 명령에 따른 듯한 운명에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯, 오두막으로 들어와 있었다. 〈헤이워드〉는, 또 다른 아는 이의 얼굴을 발견하게 될까 남몰래 염려하며, 첫 기회를 틈타 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그러나 그것은 모르는 자의 얼굴로 드러났고, 더욱이 영문 모를 일은, 〈휴런〉 전사의 온갖 뚜렷한 표지를 다 지닌 자의 얼굴이라는 것이었다. 그러나 그는 제 부족과 어울리는 대신, 한쪽에 따로 떨어져 앉았으니, 무리 가운데 외로운 존재로, 되도록 적은 자리를 차지하려 애쓰기라도 하듯, 그 몸을 웅크리고 비굴한 자세로 움츠리고 있었다. 저마다 제 자리를 잡고, 그곳에 침묵이 깃들자, 앞서 독자에게 소개한 그 백발의 추장이, 〈레니 레나페〉의 말로 큰 소리로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉여,\" 그가 말했다. \"비록 계집들의 부족 가운데 하나일지라도, 너는 네가 사내임을 입증하였다. 내 너에게 먹을 것을 주고 싶으나, 〈휴런〉과 함께 먹는 자는 그의 벗이 되어야 하느니라. 아침 해가 뜰 때까지 편히 쉬어라, 그때 우리의 마지막 말이 내려질 것이다.\"\n\n\"이레 밤과 그만큼의 여름 낮을, 나는 〈휴런〉의 자취를 좇으며 굶었다.\" 〈엉커스〉가 차갑게 대답했다. \"〈레나페〉의 자손들은 먹으려 머뭇대지 않고도 의로운 자의 길을 가는 법을 안다.\"\n\n\"내 젊은이 둘이 네 동행을 뒤쫓고 있다.\" 상대가, 제 포로의 호언을 아랑곳하지 않는 듯이 이었다. \"그들이 돌아오면, 그때 우리의 지혜로운 자들이 네게 '살라' 혹은 '죽으라' 할 것이다.\"\n\n\"〈휴런〉에게는 귀가 없는가?\" 〈엉커스〉가 경멸하며 외쳤다. \"네 포로가 된 뒤로 두 번, 〈델라웨어〉는 자기가 아는 총소리를 들었다. 네 젊은이들은 결코 돌아오지 못하리라!\"\n\n이 대담한 단언에 짧고 시무룩한 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 〈모히칸〉이 척후의 치명적인 소총을 가리키는 것임을 알아챈 〈덩컨〉은, 그것이 정복자들에게 어떤 영향을 미칠지 골똘히 살피며 몸을 앞으로 내밀었다. 그러나 추장은 그저 이렇게 되받는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n\"〈레나페〉가 그토록 솜씨가 좋다면, 어찌하여 그들의 가장 용맹한 전사 가운데 하나가 여기 있는가?\"\n\n\"그는 달아나는 겁쟁이의 발자취를 좇다가 덫에 걸린 것이다. 교활한 비버도 잡힐 수 있는 법.\"\n\n이렇게 대답하며 〈엉커스〉는 손가락으로 그 외로운 〈휴런〉을 가리켰으나, 그토록 하찮은 대상에게 그 밖의 다른 눈길을 베푸는 것은 마다했다. 그 대답의 말과 말하는 자의 기색이 그 듣는 자들 사이에 강한 동요를 일으켰다. 모든 눈이 그 단순한 몸짓으로 가리켜진 인물을 향해 시무룩하게 굴러갔고, 낮고 위협적인 웅성거림이 무리 사이를 지나갔다. 그 불길한 소리가 바깥문에까지 닿았고, 여자와 아이들이 무리 속으로 밀고 들어와, 이제 어깨와 어깨 사이에는, 간절하고 호기심 어린 어떤 사람 얼굴의 거뭇한 윤곽으로 채워지지 않은 틈이라곤 하나도 남지 않았다.\n\n그러는 동안, 한가운데의 더 나이 든 추장들은 짧고 끊긴 문장으로 서로 의논했다. 말하는 자의 뜻을 가장 단순하고 가장 힘찬 형태로 전하지 않는 말은 한마디도 발해지지 않았다. 다시, 길고 깊이 엄숙한 침묵이 자리를 잡았다. 그 자리에 있는 모두가, 그것이 무겁고 중대한 판결의 근엄한 전조임을 알고 있었다. 바깥쪽 얼굴들의 둘레를 이룬 자들은 보려고 발끝으로 섰고, 죄인조차 한순간 더 깊은 감정 속에 제 수치를 잊은 채, 그 거뭇한 추장들의 무리를 향해 근심스럽고 괴로운 눈길을 던지고자 제 비굴한 얼굴을 드러냈다. 마침내 그토록 자주 거명된 그 나이 든 전사가 침묵을 깼다. 그는 땅에서 일어나, 미동 없는 〈엉커스〉의 형상을 지나, 위엄 있는 자세로 그 죄인 앞에 섰다. 그 순간, 앞서 말한 그 시든 노파가, 횃불을 든 채, 일종의 주문이었을 법한 알아들을 수 없는 말을 중얼거리며, 느릿하게 옆걸음 치는 듯한 춤으로 그 둘레 안으로 들어왔다. 그의 존재는 전혀 주제넘은 끼어듦이었으나, 누구도 개의치 않았다.\n\n〈엉커스〉에게 다가간 그는, 그 붉은 빛을 그의 몸에 던져 그 표정의 가장 작은 동요까지 드러내도록 활활 타는 나뭇가지를 들이댔다. 〈모히칸〉은 굳건하고 오만한 자세를 지켰다. 그의 눈은, 그 캐묻는 눈길을 마주해 주기는커녕, 마치 시야를 가로막는 장애물을 꿰뚫어 미래를 들여다보기라도 하듯, 한결같이 먼 곳에 머물러 있었다. 그 살핌에 만족한 그는, 가벼운 기쁨의 기색과 함께 그에게서 물러나, 죄지은 제 동족에게 같은 가혹한 시험을 행하러 나아갔다.\n\n젊은 〈휴런〉은 전투용 칠을 한 차림이라, 곱게 빚어진 그 몸은 옷으로 가려진 데가 거의 없었다. 그 빛이 사지와 관절 하나하나를 분간할 수 있게 했고, 그것들이 억누를 수 없는 고통 속에 뒤틀리고 있는 것을 본 〈덩컨〉은 진저리치며 고개를 돌렸다. 여자가 그 슬프고 수치스러운 광경에 낮고 애처로운 울부짖음을 막 시작하려는데, 추장이 손을 내밀어 그를 가만히 옆으로 밀어냈다.\n\n\"〈휘는 갈대〉여,\" 그가 그 젊은 죄인을 이름으로, 그의 본래 언어로 부르며 말했다. \"위대한 정령께서 너를 눈에 보기 좋게 빚으셨으나, 차라리 네가 태어나지 않았더라면 나았으리라. 네 혀는 마을에서 우렁차나, 싸움터에서는 잠잠하다. 내 젊은이들 가운데 누구도 너만큼 전쟁 기둥에 토마호크를 깊이 박지 못하거니와, 누구도 너만큼 〈잉기즈〉에게 가벼이 내리치지 못한다. 적은 네 등의 모양은 알아도, 네 눈의 빛깔은 본 적이 없다. 세 번이나 그들이 너에게 나오라 불렀으되, 그때마다 너는 응하기를 잊었다. 네 이름은 다시는 네 부족에서 입에 오르지 않으리라, 이미 잊혔느니라.\"\n\n추장이 한 문장 한 문장 사이에 묵직하게 뜸을 들이며 이 말을 천천히 발하는 동안, 죄인은 상대의 지위와 연륜에 대한 경의로 그 얼굴을 들었다. 수치와 공포와 자부심이 그 이목구비 속에서 다투었다. 안으로의 고뇌로 오므라든 그의 눈이, 그 숨결이 곧 자신의 명예였던 자들의 몸 위에서 번득였고, 한순간 뒤엣것의 감정이 우위를 차지했다. 그는 일어서서, 제 가슴을 드러내며, 무자비한 그의 심판자가 이미 치켜든 그 예리하고 번쩍이는 칼을 한결같이 바라보았다. 그 무기가 천천히 그의 심장 속으로 들어갈 때 그는 미소마저 지었으니, 죽음이 예상했던 것보다 덜 끔찍함을 알게 된 것이 기쁘기라도 한 듯했고, 엄혹하고 굽힘 없는 〈엉커스〉의 형상의 발치에 얼굴부터 무겁게 고꾸라졌다.\n\n여자가 크고 애처로운 비명을 지르며, 횃불을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐, 모든 것을 어둠 속에 파묻어 버렸다. 몸서리치는 구경꾼 무리 전체가, 어수선한 망령들처럼 오두막에서 미끄러져 나갔다. 그리하여 〈덩컨〉은, 이제 자신과, 인디언의 판결에 희생되어 아직도 꿈틀거리는 그 몸뚱이만이 그곳의 유일한 거주자가 되었다고 생각했다.\n\n현자는 이렇게 말했고, 왕들은 지체 없이\n회의를 파하고 그 우두머리를 따랐다.\n\n포프의 『일리아드』\n\n한순간 만에 청년은 자신이 틀렸음을 깨달았다. 강한 압력으로 한 손이 그의 팔에 얹혔고, 〈언카스〉의 낮은 목소리가 그의 귓가에 속삭였다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉족은 개들입니다. 겁쟁이의 피를 보았다 하여 전사가 떨 수는 없는 법. '회색 머리'와 〈사가모어〉는 무사하고, 〈호크아이〉의 소총도 잠들지 않았습니다. 가십시오. 〈언카스〉와 '열린 손'은 이제 모르는 사이입니다. 그것으로 충분합니다.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 더 듣고 싶었으나, 친구의 부드러운 떠밀음이 그를 문 쪽으로 재촉하며, 그들의 교류가 발각될 경우 닥칠 위험을 일깨웠다. 그 불가피함에 더디고 마지못해 굴복하며, 그는 그곳을 떠나 가까이 어슬렁대던 무리 속으로 섞여 들었다. 빈터에서 사위어 가는 불빛이, 소리 없이 이리저리 거니는 거무스름한 형상들 위로 희미하고 어렴풋한 빛을 던졌다. 이따금 여느 때보다 밝은 섬광이 오두막 안으로 비쳐 들며, 죽은 〈휴런〉의 시신 곁에 여전히 꼿꼿한 자세를 지키고 선 〈언카스〉의 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n이윽고 한 무리의 전사들이 다시 그곳에 들어갔다가, 도로 나오며 그 의식 없는 유해를 인근 숲으로 옮겨 갔다. 이 장면이 끝나자, 〈덩컨〉은 아무 질문도, 아무 주목도 받지 않은 채 오두막들 사이를 거닐며, 그 위험을 무릅쓰게 한 여인의 흔적을 찾으려 애썼다. 부족이 지금 같은 심기인 터라, 그런 바람이 마음을 스쳤다면, 달아나 동료들과 다시 합류하기란 쉬운 일이었을 것이다. 그러나 〈앨리스〉를 향한 그칠 줄 모르는 근심에 더해, 더 미약하나 더 새로운, 〈언카스〉의 운명에 관한 관심이 그를 그 자리에 붙들어 매는 데 한몫했다. 그리하여 그는 오두막에서 오두막으로 헤매기를 거듭하며, 마을 전체를 한 바퀴 다 돌 때까지, 들여다보는 곳마다 거듭되는 실망과만 마주칠 뿐이었다. 그토록 헛된 것으로 드러난 이런 식의 탐색을 단념하고, 그는 의혹을 끝내고자 〈데이비드〉를 찾아 캐물으리라 마음먹고, 발길을 돌려 회의 오두막으로 되짚어 갔다.\n\n심판의 자리이자 처형의 장소이기도 했던 그 건물에 이르자, 청년은 흥분이 이미 가라앉은 것을 알았다. 전사들은 다시 모여, 이제 차분히 담배를 피우며 〈호리컨〉 어귀로 떠났던 근래의 원정에서 있었던 주요 사건들을 진중하게 이야기하고 있었다. 〈덩컨〉의 귀환이 그의 정체와 그가 방문한 수상쩍은 정황을 그들에게 상기시킬 법도 했으나, 그것은 눈에 띄는 동요를 일으키지 않았다. 이런 점에서, 방금 벌어진 끔찍한 장면은 도리어 그의 의도에 유리하게 작용했으니, 이토록 뜻밖의 이점을 활용하는 것이 상책임을 깨닫는 데에 그는 제 감정 외에 다른 부추김이 필요치 않았다.\n\n망설이는 기색 없이, 그는 오두막 안으로 걸어 들어가, 그를 맞은 이들의 거동에 더없이 잘 어울리는 진중함으로 자리에 앉았다. 황급하나 살피는 한 차례 시선으로, 그는 〈언카스〉가 자신이 두고 떠난 자리에 여전히 남아 있되 〈데이비드〉는 다시 나타나지 않았음을 알아챘다. 전자에게는 곁에 자리한 젊은 〈휴런〉의 경계 어린 눈길 말고는 다른 구속이 가해지지 않았으나, 무장한 한 전사가 좁은 출입구의 한쪽을 이룬 기둥에 기대어 있었다. 그 밖의 모든 점에서 포로는 자유로워 보였다. 그러나 그는 모든 대화에서 배제되어 있었고, 생명과 의지를 지닌 사람이라기보다는 정교하게 빚어진 어떤 조각상의 분위기를 훨씬 더 짙게 풍겼다.\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 자신이 손아귀에 떨어진 이 사람들의 즉각적인 처벌의 무서운 본보기를, 너무도 최근에 목격한 터라, 어떤 주제넘은 대담함으로 자신을 드러내는 위험을 무릅쓸 수 없었다. 자신의 진짜 처지가 드러나면 당장 치명적일 수 있는 마당에, 그는 말보다 침묵과 사색을 훨씬 더 바랐을 것이다. 이 신중한 결심에는 불행하게도, 그를 맞은 이들은 다른 마음을 품은 듯했다. 그가 슬기롭게 그늘에 조금 비켜 잡은 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 프랑스어를 할 줄 아는 또 다른 연장의 전사가 그에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"내 〈캐나다〉 아버지께서는 자식들을 잊지 않으시는구려.\" 추장이 말했다. \"그분께 감사드리오. 내 젊은이 가운데 하나의 아내에게 악령이 깃들었소. 영리한 이방인께서 그놈을 쫓아낼 수 있겠소?\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 인디언들 사이에서 그런 빙의로 여겨지는 경우에 행해지는 의식 흉내에 관해 어느 정도 알고 있었다. 그는 이 정황이 어쩌면 자신의 목적을 진척시키는 데 보탬이 될 수 있음을 한눈에 알아보았다. 그러니 바로 그때, 이보다 그를 더 흡족하게 할 제안을 입에 올리기란 어려웠을 것이다. 그러나 자신이 꾸며 낸 인물의 위엄을 지킬 필요를 의식하여, 그는 감정을 억누르고 알맞은 신비로움을 담아 대답했다.\n\n\"영(靈)들도 저마다 다르오. 더러는 지혜의 힘에 굴복하나, 더러는 너무 강하지요.\"\n\n\"내 형제는 위대한 주술사로구려.\" 그 교활한 야만인이 말했다. \"한번 해 보시겠소?\"\n\n대답은 동의의 몸짓이었다. 〈휴런〉은 그 확답에 만족하여, 다시 파이프를 입에 물고 자리를 옮길 적당한 때를 기다렸다. 겉치레에 그런 희생을 요구하는 야만인들의 냉랭한 관습을 속으로 저주하던 조급한 〈헤이워드〉는, 추장 못지않은 무심한 태도를 꾸며 내는 수밖에 없었으니, 그 추장은 사실 그 앓는 여인의 가까운 친척이었다. 시간이 더디게 흘렀고, 이 경험주의 모험가에게는 그 지체가 한 시간처럼 느껴졌을 무렵, 〈휴런〉이 파이프를 내려놓고, 마치 병자의 오두막으로 길을 안내하려는 듯 가슴을 가로질러 겉옷을 끌어당겼다. 바로 그때, 건장한 체구의 한 전사가 문간을 어둡게 가리더니, 주의를 모으고 있던 무리 사이를 말없이 성큼성큼 걸어와, 〈덩컨〉을 받치고 있던 낮은 나뭇가지 더미의 한쪽 끝에 앉았다. 후자는 곁에 앉은 이에게 조급한 눈길을 던졌고, 자신이 〈마구아〉와 실제로 맞닿아 있음을 깨닫고는 걷잡을 수 없는 공포에 살갗이 오싹해졌다.\n\n이 교활하고 두려운 추장의 갑작스러운 귀환으로 〈휴런〉의 출발이 늦춰졌다. 꺼졌던 여러 파이프에 다시 불이 붙었다. 그동안 새로 온 자는 말 한마디 없이 허리띠에서 토마호크를 빼 들고, 그 머리에 달린 대통에 담배를 채워, 속이 빈 자루를 통해 잎의 연기를 빨아들이기 시작했으니, 그 무심함이란 마치 길고 고된 사냥에 지친 이틀을 비웠던 일 따위 없었다는 듯했다. 〈덩컨〉에게는 무수한 세월처럼 여겨졌을 십 분이 이런 식으로 흘렀을 법했고, 누구 하나 입을 열기도 전에 전사들은 흰 연기구름 속에 온통 휩싸였다.\n\n\"잘 왔네!\" 마침내 누군가 입을 열었다. \"내 벗은 사슴을 찾았는가?\"\n\n\"젊은이들이 그 짐에 비틀거리고 있소.\" 〈마구아〉가 대꾸했다. \"'휘어지는 갈대'를 사냥길로 보내시오. 그가 그들과 마주칠 게요.\"\n\n금기된 이름이 입에 오르자 깊고 무시무시한 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 마치 모두가 같은 순간에 부정한 것을 들이마신 듯, 저마다의 입술에서 파이프가 떨어졌다. 연기가 그들의 머리 위에서 작은 소용돌이를 이루며 감돌다가, 나선을 그리며 오두막 지붕의 트인 구멍으로 빠르게 솟아올라, 그 아래 자리를 연기 없이 맑게 비우니, 거뭇한 얼굴 하나하나가 또렷이 보였다. 대다수 전사의 시선은 땅바닥에 못 박혀 있었으나, 무리 가운데 더 젊고 덜 영민한 몇몇은 그 사납고 번득이는 눈알을 굴려, 부족에서 가장 존경받는 두 추장 사이에 앉은 한 백발의 야만인 쪽으로 향하게 두었다. 이 인디언의 풍채나 차림에는 그런 영예를 누릴 만한 구석이라곤 없어 보였다. 전자는 원주민 특유의 당당함이라기보다는 오히려 풀이 죽어 있었고, 후자는 그 부족의 평범한 사내들이 으레 걸치는 것에 지나지 않았다. 그도 주위의 대다수처럼 일 분 남짓 시선을 땅에 두고 있었으나, 마침내 곁눈질로 슬쩍 둘러보매, 자신이 뭇사람의 주목 대상이 되어 가고 있음을 알아챘다. 그러자 그는 일어나, 그 모두의 침묵 속에 목소리를 높였다.\n\n\"그것은 거짓이었소.\" 그가 말했다. \"내게는 아들이 없었소. 그 이름으로 불리던 자는 잊혔소. 그의 피는 창백했고, 그것은 〈휴런〉의 혈관에서 나온 것이 아니오. 사악한 〈치페와〉족이 내 아내를 속였소. '위대한 영(靈)'께서 〈위센투시〉의 가문이 끊어지리라 말씀하셨소. 제 핏줄의 악이 저와 함께 죽음을 아는 자는 행복하오. 나는 다 말했소.\"\n\n겁쟁이 젊은 인디언의 아버지였던 이 발언자는, 마치 듣는 이들의 눈에서 자신의 극기에 대한 칭송을 구하기라도 하듯 사방을 둘러보았다. 그러나 그 백성의 준엄한 관습은 이 쇠약한 노인에게 너무도 가혹한 것을 요구했다. 그 눈빛은 그의 비유적이고 호언하는 말과 모순되었고, 주름진 얼굴의 근육 하나하나가 고뇌로 일그러지고 있었다. 자신의 쓰디쓴 승리를 누리려 한순간을 서 있던 그는, 마치 사람들의 시선에 진저리가 난 듯 몸을 돌려, 담요로 얼굴을 가린 채 인디언 특유의 소리 없는 걸음으로 오두막을 걸어 나갔으니, 제 거처의 은밀함 속에서 자기와 같은 처지의, 늙고 외롭고 자식 없는 이의 위안을 찾으려는 것이었다.\n\n성격의 미덕과 결함이 대대로 전해진다고 믿는 인디언들은, 그가 말없이 떠나도록 내버려 두었다. 그러자 한 추장이, 보다 문명화된 사회의 많은 이들이 본받아 이로움을 얻을 만한 고매한 교양으로, 가장 늦게 온 〈마구아〉에게 예의를 갖춰 말을 건네며, 방금 목격한 나약함에서 젊은이들의 주의를 거두려 쾌활한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉족이 꿀단지를 노리는 곰들처럼 내 마을 주위를 어슬렁대더군. 허나 〈휴런〉이 잠든 것을 본 자가 일찍이 있었던가?\"\n\n천둥이 터지기에 앞서 몰려드는 먹구름의 어둠도, 〈마구아〉가 이렇게 외칠 때의 그 이마보다 더 검지는 못했다.\n\n\"호수의 〈델라웨어〉들 말이오!\"\n\n\"아닐세. 제 강가에서 아낙네의 치마를 두른 자들 말이야. 그중 하나가 부족 곁을 지나가고 있었네.\"\n\n\"내 젊은이들이 그자의 머리 가죽을 벗겼소?\"\n\n\"다리는 좋더군. 허나 그 팔은 토마호크보다 괭이에 더 어울리지.\" 상대가 〈언카스〉의 미동도 없는 형상을 가리키며 대꾸했다.\n\n자신이 그토록 미워할 까닭이 많다고 알려진 부족에서 온 포로를 보며 눈요기하려는 아녀자 같은 호기심을 드러내기는커녕, 〈마구아〉는 자신의 교활함이나 웅변이 당장 필요치 않을 때면 으레 짓던 그 사색에 잠긴 태도로 계속 담배를 피웠다. 늙은 아버지의 말에서 전해진 사실에 속으로는 놀랐으나, 그는 더 적당한 때로 캐물음을 미루며 자신에게 아무런 질문도 허락하지 않았다. 충분한 시간이 흐른 뒤에야 그는 파이프에서 재를 털어 내고, 토마호크를 제자리에 꽂고, 허리띠를 조이고는 일어서며, 자신의 조금 뒤에 서 있던 죄수 쪽으로 처음으로 눈길을 던졌다. 멍하니 있는 듯하면서도 빈틈없던 〈언카스〉가 그 움직임을 언뜻 알아채고, 불빛 쪽으로 홱 돌아서니, 두 사람의 시선이 마주쳤다. 거의 일 분 동안, 이 대담하고 길들지 않은 두 영혼은 서로의 눈을 똑바로 응시하며 섰으니, 어느 쪽도 자신이 맞닥뜨린 사나운 시선 앞에서 조금도 움츠러들지 않았다. 〈언카스〉의 몸이 부풀어 오르고, 그 콧구멍이 궁지에 몰린 호랑이처럼 벌어졌으나, 그 자세가 어찌나 굳건하고 흔들림 없던지, 상상 속에서 그는 손쉽게 제 부족의 호전적인 신을 그린 정교하고 흠 없는 형상으로 탈바꿈할 만했다. 〈마구아〉의 떨리는 이목구비의 윤곽은 더 유연하게 변했다. 그의 표정은 차차 도전의 빛깔을 잃고 흉포한 환희의 표정으로 바뀌더니, 가슴 밑바닥에서부터 숨을 한 번 몰아쉬며, 그는 그 더없이 가공할 이름을 소리 내어 입에 올렸다.\n\n\"〈민첩한 사슴〉!\"\n\n이 익히 알려진 별칭이 입에 오르자 전사들은 저마다 벌떡 일어섰고, 잠시 동안 원주민들의 극기 어린 의연함이 놀라움에 완전히 무너졌다. 미움받으면서도 존경받는 그 이름이 한목소리인 양 되풀이되어, 그 소리는 오두막 경계 너머까지 실려 갔다. 입구 주위를 서성이던 여인과 아이들이 그 말을 받아 메아리로 되뇌었고, 그 뒤를 또 다른 날카롭고 구슬픈 울부짖음이 이었다. 후자가 채 끝나기도 전에, 사내들 사이의 동요는 완전히 가라앉았다. 그 자리의 저마다가 마치 제 성급함이 부끄러운 듯 도로 앉았으나, 그토록 자주 자기 부족의 가장 뛰어나고 가장 당당한 자들을 상대로 용맹을 입증해 온 한 전사를 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피느라, 그들의 의미심장한 눈길이 포로 쪽으로 굴러가기를 멈춘 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다. 〈언카스〉는 자신의 승리를 누렸으나, 그저 잔잔한 미소로 자신의 개가를 드러내는 것으로 만족했으니, 그것은 모든 시대와 모든 민족에 속한 경멸의 표상이었다.\n\n〈마구아〉가 그 표정을 알아채고는 팔을 들어 포로를 향해 흔드니, 팔뚝에 매달린 가벼운 은 장식이 떨리는 팔의 동요에 덜그럭거렸고, 그는 복수에 찬 어조로 영어로 외쳤다.\n\n\"〈모히칸〉, 너는 죽는다!\"\n\n\"치유의 물도 죽은 〈휴런〉들을 결코 되살리지는 못한다.\" 〈언카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉어의 가락으로 대꾸했다. \"굽이치는 강물이 그들의 뼈를 씻어 내리지. 그들의 사내는 아낙네요, 그들의 아낙네는 올빼미다. 가라! 〈휴런〉의 개들을 한데 불러 모아, 전사를 우러러보게 하라. 내 콧구멍이 노여워한다. 겁쟁이의 피 냄새를 맡으니.\"\n\n이 마지막 비아냥이 깊이 찔러, 그 상처가 사무쳤다. 〈휴런〉족의 다수가 포로가 쓰는 낯선 말을 알아들었고, 그 가운데에 〈마구아〉도 끼어 있었다. 이 교활한 야만인은 그것을 알아보고는 곧장 자신의 이점을 활용했다. 어깨에서 가벼운 가죽 겉옷을 늘어뜨려 떨구고, 그는 팔을 뻗어 위험하고도 교묘한 웅변을 터뜨리기 시작했다. 이따금 들러붙는 그의 고질적 약점뿐 아니라 부족을 저버린 일로 그의 백성 사이에서 영향력이 꽤 손상되었을지언정, 그의 용기와 웅변가로서의 명성은 부인할 수 없는 것이었다. 그는 듣는 이 없이 말한 적이 없었고, 자기 의견에 동조하는 자를 만들지 않고 말을 마치는 일이 드물었다. 이번에는 그의 타고난 역량이 복수의 갈증으로 한층 자극받았다.\n\n그는 〈글렌〉의 섬에서 있었던 습격과, 자기 동료들의 죽음, 그리고 더없이 가공할 적들이 달아난 일을 다시 들려주었다. 이어 그는 손아귀에 떨어진 포로들을 이끌고 갔던 그 산의 성질과 위치를 묘사했다. 처녀들을 향한 자신의 피비린내 나는 의도며, 좌절된 자신의 악의에 관해서는 일언반구도 없이, 그는 〈긴 소총〉에게 일행이 기습당한 일과 그 치명적인 결말로 재빨리 넘어갔다. 여기서 그는 말을 멈추고, 죽은 자들을 향한 짐짓 꾸민 경의를 띠고 사방을 둘러보았으나, 실은 자기 이야기의 서두가 미친 영향을 살피려는 것이었다. 여느 때처럼 모든 눈이 그의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다. 거뭇한 형상 하나하나가 숨 쉬는 조각상 같았으니, 그 자세가 그토록 미동도 없고, 저마다의 주의가 그토록 골똘했다.\n\n이어 〈마구아〉는, 여태 맑고 강하고 드높던 목소리를 낮추어, 죽은 자들의 공덕을 짚었다. 인디언의 공감을 불러일으킬 법한 자질이라면 어느 하나도 그의 주목을 벗어나지 못했다. 하나는 사냥에 나서 헛되이 돌아온 적이 없었고, 또 하나는 적의 자취를 쫓는 데 지칠 줄 몰랐다. 이자는 용감했고, 저자는 너그러웠다. 요컨대 그는, 그토록 적은 수의 가문으로 이루어진 한 부족에서, 차례로 어느 가슴엔가 울림을 찾아낼 모든 현(絃)을 건드리도록 자신의 언급을 교묘히 다루었다.\n\n\"내 젊은이들의 뼈가,\" 그가 끝맺었다. \"〈휴런〉족의 매장지에 있는가? 여러분은 그렇지 않음을 알고 있소. 그들의 영혼은 지는 해를 향해 떠나, 행복한 사냥터를 향해 이미 큰물을 건너고 있소. 허나 그들은 양식도, 총이나 칼도, 모카신도 없이, 태어났을 적처럼 헐벗고 가난한 채로 떠났소. 이래도 되겠소? 그들의 혼이 굶주린 〈이로쿼이〉나 사내답지 못한 〈델라웨어〉처럼 의인들의 땅에 들어가야 하겠소, 아니면 손에 무기를 쥐고 등에 겉옷을 두르고 제 벗들을 맞아야 하겠소? 우리 조상들이 〈와이언도트〉의 부족들이 무엇이 되었다 여기겠소? 그들은 어두운 눈으로 제 자식들을 바라보며, 가라! 〈치페와〉가 〈휴런〉의 이름을 달고 여기 왔구나, 하고 말할 것이오. 형제들이여, 우리는 죽은 자들을 잊어서는 아니 되오. 붉은 피부의 사람은 결코 기억을 그치지 않소. 우리는 이 〈모히칸〉의 등에 짐을 잔뜩 실어 우리의 후한 선물에 비틀거리게 한 다음, 그를 내 젊은이들의 뒤로 보낼 것이오. 우리 귀는 열려 있지 않으나, 그들은 우리에게 도움을 청하고 있소. 그들은 말하오, 우리를 잊지 말라고. 이 〈모히칸〉의 혼이 짐을 지고 자기들 뒤를 따라 애쓰는 것을 보면, 그들은 우리가 그런 마음임을 알 것이오. 그러면 그들은 행복하게 나아갈 것이고, 우리 자식들은 말할 것이오, '우리 조상들이 제 벗들에게 이리하였으니, 우리도 그들에게 마땅히 이리해야 한다'라고. 〈양키〉가 무엇이오? 우리가 많이도 죽였으나, 땅은 여전히 창백하오. 〈휴런〉의 이름에 묻은 오점은 오직 인디언의 혈관에서 나온 피로만 가릴 수 있소. 이 〈델라웨어〉를 죽게 합시다.\"\n\n〈휴런〉 웅변가의 격정적인 말씨와 힘 있는 태도로 전해진 이런 열변의 효과는, 좀처럼 잘못 짚을 수 없는 것이었다. 〈마구아〉가 타고난 공감을 듣는 이들의 종교적 미신과 어찌나 교묘히 뒤섞어 놓았던지, 동족의 망령에게 제물을 바치는 일에 관습으로 이미 길든 그들의 마음은, 복수의 열망 속에 인간다움의 자취를 모조리 잃고 말았다. 특히 한 전사가, 사납고 흉포한 인상의 사내가, 발언자의 말에 기울인 주의로 유독 눈에 띄었다. 그의 표정은 스쳐 가는 감정마다 따라 변하더니, 마침내 치명적인 악의의 표정으로 굳어졌다. 〈마구아〉가 말을 끝맺자 그는 일어나, 악마의 비명을 내지르며, 머리 위로 휘두르는 그의 윤이 나는 작은 도끼가 횃불 빛에 번쩍이는 것이 보였다. 그 동작과 외침은 너무도 갑작스러워 말로는 그 피비린내 나는 의도를 가로막을 수 없었다. 그의 손에서 밝은 섬광이 뻗어 나오는 듯했고, 같은 순간 거뭇하고 힘찬 선 하나가 그것을 가로질렀다. 전자는 날아가는 토마호크였고, 후자는 그 겨냥을 빗나가게 하려 〈마구아〉가 앞으로 내쏜 팔이었다. 추장의 재빠르고 민첩한 동작이 영 늦은 것은 아니었다. 그 날카로운 무기는 〈언카스〉의 머리 가죽 술에서 전투 깃털을 베어 내고, 무슨 가공할 기계에서 던져지기라도 한 듯 오두막의 허술한 벽을 뚫고 지나갔다.\n\n〈덩컨〉은 그 위협적인 동작을 보고 벌떡 일어섰으니, 그 가슴은 목구멍으로 튀어 오르는 한편, 친구를 위한 더없이 고결한 결의로 부풀어 올랐다. 한 번의 눈길로 그는 그 일격이 빗나갔음을 알았고, 공포는 감탄으로 바뀌었다. 〈언카스〉는 가만히 서서, 감정 따위는 초월한 듯한 얼굴로 적의 눈을 응시했다. 이 갑작스럽고 앙심 어린 공격에 그가 지은 얼굴보다 더 차갑고, 더 침착하고, 더 흔들림 없는 대리석은 없었을 것이다. 이어 마치 그 자신에게는 그토록 다행이었던 솜씨의 모자람을 가엾이 여기듯, 그는 미소 지으며 제 부족어로 경멸의 말 몇 마디를 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"안 돼!\" 포로의 무사함을 확인하고 나서 〈마구아〉가 말했다. \"그의 수치 위로 해가 비쳐야 한다. 아낙네들이 그의 살이 떠는 것을 보아야 한다. 그러지 않으면 우리 복수는 아이들 장난과 같아질 것이다. 가라! 고요한 곳으로 그를 데려가라. 〈델라웨어〉가 밤에 잠들 수 있는지, 그리고 아침에 죽을 수 있는지 보자.\"\n\n죄수를 지키는 임무를 맡은 젊은이들은 곧장 나무껍질 끈을 그의 팔에 걸쳐 묶고는, 깊고 불길한 침묵 속에 그를 오두막에서 끌고 나갔다. 〈언카스〉의 형상이 문간에 섰을 때, 그 굳건한 걸음이 망설인 것은 그때뿐이었다. 거기서 그는 돌아서, 제 적들의 무리를 휙 둘러보는 오만한 눈길을 던졌으니, 그 눈길에서 〈덩컨〉은, 그가 희망에 아주 버림받은 것은 아니라는 표현으로 기꺼이 풀이할 만한 빛 하나를 읽어 냈다.\n\n〈마구아〉는 자신의 성공에 만족했거나, 아니면 자신의 은밀한 목적에 너무 골몰한 나머지 캐물음을 더 밀고 나가지 않았다. 외투를 떨쳐 가슴께로 여미며, 그 역시, 제 팔꿈치 곁의 자에게 그토록 치명적일 수도 있었을 주제를 좇지 않은 채, 그곳을 떠났다. 치미는 분노와 타고난 굳건함, 그리고 〈언카스〉를 향한 근심에도 불구하고, 〈헤이워드〉는 그토록 위험하고 교활한 적이 없어지자 눈에 띄게 마음이 놓였다. 그 연설이 자아낸 흥분은 차차 가라앉았다. 전사들은 다시 자리에 앉았고, 연기구름이 또 한 번 오두막을 가득 채웠다. 거의 반 시간 동안 음절 하나 입에 오르지 않았고, 곁으로 던지는 눈길 하나 거의 없었으니, 그토록 격정적이면서도 또 그토록 자제력 있는 이 존재들 사이에서는, 폭력과 소란의 장면마다 진중하고 사색에 잠긴 침묵이 으레 그 뒤를 잇는 법이었다.\n\n〈덩컨〉의 도움을 청했던 추장이 파이프를 다 피우자, 그는 마침내 떠나려는 마지막 몸짓을 성공적으로 취했다. 손가락 하나의 움직임이 의사로 가장한 자에게 따라오라고 건넨 신호였다. 연기구름 사이를 지나며, 〈덩컨〉은 마침내 서늘하고 상쾌한 여름밤의 맑은 공기를 들이쉴 수 있게 된 것이, 한 가지 이상의 이유로 기뻤다.\n\n그의 동행은, 〈헤이워드〉가 이미 헛되이 탐색했던 그 오두막들 사이로 길을 잡는 대신, 옆으로 비켜, 임시 마을을 굽어보는 인근 산기슭으로 곧장 나아갔다. 덤불 수풀이 그 산자락을 두르고 있어, 구부러지고 좁은 길을 지나야만 했다. 사내아이들은 빈터에서 놀이를 다시 시작하여, 저희끼리 형틀까지 쫓아가는 흉내 놀이를 펼치고 있었다. 놀이를 되도록 실제와 비슷하게 하려고, 그중 가장 대담한 한 아이가 여태 불길을 면했던 나무 우듬지 더미 몇 군데에 불씨를 몇 개 옮겨 놓았다. 그 불 하나의 불꽃이 추장과 〈덩컨〉의 길을 밝혀, 거친 풍경에 한층 야성적인 빛깔을 더했다. 민둥바위에서 조금 떨어진, 바로 그 앞에서, 그들은 풀 무성한 빈터로 들어서 그곳을 가로지를 채비를 했다. 바로 그때 불에 새 땔감이 더해져, 그 멀리 떨어진 자리에까지 강한 빛이 스며들었다. 그 빛은 산의 흰 표면에 떨어졌다가, 아래쪽으로, 그들의 길에 뜻밖에 일어선 거뭇하고 알 수 없어 보이는 한 존재 위로 반사되었다.\n\n인디언은 마치 나아가야 할지 망설이는 듯 멈춰 서며, 동행이 자기 곁으로 다가오도록 두었다. 처음엔 멈춰 있는 듯하던 커다란 검은 덩어리가, 이제 후자로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 식으로 움직이기 시작했다. 다시 불이 밝아지며, 그 불빛이 그 물체에 한층 또렷이 떨어졌다. 그러자 〈덩컨〉마저, 윗부분을 끊임없이 움직이게 하면서도 짐승 자체는 앉아 있는 듯한 그 쉴 새 없고 비스듬한 자세로 보아, 그것이 곰임을 알아보았다. 그것은 크고 사납게 으르렁대었고, 번득이는 눈알이 보이는 순간들도 있었으나, 그 밖에 적의의 기색은 보이지 않았다. 적어도 〈휴런〉은 이 기이한 침입자의 의도가 평화로운 것이라 확신한 듯했으니, 그것을 주의 깊이 살펴본 뒤 조용히 가던 길을 계속했다.\n\n곰이 인디언들 사이에서 흔히 길들여지곤 한다는 것을 알던 〈덩컨〉은, 부족이 아끼는 어떤 짐승이 먹이를 찾아 수풀로 흘러들었으려니 여기며, 동행의 본을 따랐다. 그들은 아무 방해도 받지 않고 그것을 지나쳤다. 그 괴물과 거의 맞닿을 만큼 가까이 가야 했음에도, 처음엔 그토록 조심스레 이 기이한 방문객의 정체를 가늠하던 〈휴런〉은, 이제 더는 살피느라 한순간도 허비하지 않고 나아가는 데 만족했다. 그러나 〈헤이워드〉는, 후방의 공격을 경계하는 유익한 조심성에서, 뒤를 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다. 그 짐승이 그들의 길을 따라 굴러오며 발걸음을 좇는 것을 알아채자, 그의 불안은 조금도 줄지 않았다. 그가 막 입을 열려 했으나, 바로 그때 인디언이 나무껍질 문 하나를 밀쳐 내고 산속 품에 자리한 동굴로 들어섰다.\n\n이토록 손쉬운 후퇴 방법을 활용하여, 〈덩컨〉은 그를 뒤따라 발을 들이고, 그 입구의 허술한 덮개를 기꺼이 닫으려던 참이었는데, 그것이 제 손에서 곰에게 끌려가는 것을 느꼈으니, 그 텁수룩한 형체가 곧 통로를 어둡게 가렸다. 그들은 이제 바위틈에 난 곧고 긴 회랑 안에 있었으니, 그 짐승과 마주치지 않고 물러나기란 불가능했다. 형편이 형편인지라, 청년은 되도록 안내인에게 바짝 붙어 앞으로 나아갔다. 곰은 그의 발치에서 자주 으르렁댔고, 한두 번은 그 거대한 앞발을 그의 몸에 얹어, 마치 그가 굴속으로 더 나아가는 것을 막으려는 듯했다.\n\n이 기이한 처지에서 〈헤이워드〉의 신경이 그를 얼마나 버티게 했을지는 가늠하기 어려웠을 것이다. 다행히도 그는 곧 위안을 얻었기 때문이다. 어슴푸레한 빛 한 줄기가 줄곧 그들의 앞에 있었고, 그들은 이제 그 빛이 새어 나오는 곳에 다다랐다.\n\n바위에 난 커다란 구멍이 여러 방의 쓰임에 맞도록 거칠게 손질되어 있었다. 그 칸막이는 단순하나 독창적이어서, 돌과 막대와 나무껍질을 한데 엮어 만든 것이었다. 위쪽의 트인 구멍들이 낮에는 빛을 들여보냈고, 밤에는 불과 횃불이 해를 대신했다. 이곳으로 〈휴런〉족은 그들의 귀중품 대부분을, 특히 부족과 더 각별히 관련된 것들을 가져다 두었다. 그리고 이제 보아하니, 초자연적인 힘의 희생양으로 여겨지던 그 병든 여인 역시, 그녀를 괴롭히는 자가 오두막의 잎사귀 덮개를 뚫는 것보다 돌벽을 뚫고 공격을 가하기가 더 어려우리라는 생각에서, 이곳으로 옮겨져 있었다. 〈덩컨〉과 그의 안내인이 처음 들어선 방은 오로지 그녀를 들이는 데 쓰이고 있었다. 후자는 그녀의 침상 곁으로 다가갔으니, 그 침상은 여인들에게 둘러싸여 있었고, 그 한가운데에서 〈헤이워드〉는 사라졌던 친구 〈데이비드〉를 발견하고 놀랐다.\n\n한 번의 눈길로, 가짜 의원은 그 병자가 자신의 치유 능력으로는 도무지 어찌할 수 없을 만큼 위중함을 알아채기에 충분했다. 그녀는 일종의 마비 상태로 누워, 눈앞에 빽빽이 모여든 것들에 무심했고, 다행히도 고통을 의식하지 못했다. 〈헤이워드〉는 자신의 의식 흉내가, 그 실패나 성공에 관심을 두기에는 너무도 위중한 사람에게 행해진다는 것을, 결코 애석해하지 않았다. 꾸미려던 기만이 불러일으킨 가벼운 양심의 가책은 곧장 가라앉았고, 그는 알맞은 기백으로 제 배역을 연기하고자 생각을 가다듬기 시작했는데, 그때 자신의 솜씨가 음악의 힘을 입증하려는 한 시도에 선수를 빼앗기려 함을 알았다.\n\n방문객들이 들어섰을 때 노래로 제 영혼을 쏟아 낼 채비를 하고 서 있던 〈개멋〉이, 한순간 머뭇거린 뒤, 피리에서 한 가락을 끌어내어, 그 효험에 대한 믿음이 어지간히만 통했더라도 기적을 일으켰을 법한 찬송가를 시작했다. 그는 끝까지 마치도록 허락받았으니, 인디언들이 그의 짐작된 정신적 결함을 존중한 데다, 〈덩컨〉도 그 지체가 너무 반가워 털끝만 한 방해도 무릅쓰지 못했기 때문이다. 그 가락의 죽어 가는 여운이 후자의 귀에 떨어지던 차에, 그는 그것이 등 뒤에서 절반은 인간 같고 절반은 무덤 같은 목소리로 되풀이되는 것을 듣고 화들짝 비켜섰다. 둘러보니, 그 텁수룩한 괴물이 동굴의 그늘에 몸을 곧추세우고 앉아 있었으니, 그곳에서 그 안절부절못하는 몸뚱이가 그 짐승 특유의 불안한 모양새로 흔들리는 동안, 그것은 일종의 낮은 으르렁거림으로, 가사는 아닐지언정, 노래하는 이의 선율과 얼마간 희미하게 닮은 소리를 되풀이하고 있었다.\n\n이토록 기이한 메아리가 〈데이비드〉에게 미친 영향은 묘사하기보다 상상하는 편이 나을 것이다. 그의 눈은 마치 제 눈을 의심하기라도 하듯 휘둥그레졌고, 그의 목소리는 지나친 경이에 곧장 멎었다. 〈헤이워드〉에게 어떤 중대한 소식을 전하려던 깊이 꾸민 계획은, 두려움과 자못 흡사하나 그 자신은 감탄이라 기꺼이 믿으려 한 어떤 감정에 밀려 그의 기억에서 쫓겨났다. 그 감정에 사로잡혀 그는 소리 높여 외쳤다. \"그분이 당신을 기다리고 계시오, 가까이 있소이다.\" 그러고는 황급히 동굴을 떠났다.\n\n\"스너그. 사자 역의 대사를 받아 적으셨소? 만약 그렇다면, 부디 나에게 주시오. 나는 외는 게 더디니.\"\n\n\"퀸스. 즉흥으로 해도 되네. 그저 으르렁대는 것뿐이니까.\"\n\n한여름 밤의 꿈\n\n이 장면에는 엄숙한 것과 우스꽝스러운 것이 기묘하게 뒤섞여 있었다. 짐승은 여전히 좌우로 구르는, 보아하니 지칠 줄 모르는 동작을 이어 갔으나, 데이비드의 가락을 흉내 내려던 그 가소로운 시도는 후자가 그 자리를 떠난 순간 멎었다. 거멋의 말은, 앞서 본 바와 같이 그의 모국어였고, 던컨에게는 그 안에 어떤 숨은 뜻이 잔뜩 배어 있는 듯했으나, 지금 눈앞에 있는 그 무엇도 그 암시의 대상을 알아내는 데 도움이 되지 않았다. 그러나 이 문제를 둘러싼 온갖 짐작은 추장의 거동으로 곧 끝이 났으니, 그는 병자의 침상 곁으로 다가가, 이 낯선 자의 솜씨를 지켜보려고 그곳에 모여 있던 여인 시중꾼 무리를 죄다 손짓으로 물러가게 했다. 마지못해서이긴 했으나, 그들은 군말 없이 그의 명을 따랐고, 멀리서 닫히는 문이 그 텅 빈 천연의 회랑을 따라 울려 보낸 나직한 메아리가 잦아들었을 때, 그는 정신을 잃은 자기 딸을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 내 형제가 그 힘을 보여 주게.\"\n\n이렇게 자신이 떠맡은 배역의 직무를 수행하라고 분명하게 요청받자, 헤이워드는 아주 잠깐의 지체조차 위험을 초래할까 우려했다. 그리하여 생각을 가다듬으려 애쓰며, 그는 인디언 주술사들이 흔히 자신의 무지와 무능을 감추는 데 쓰는 그런 종류의 주문과 그 괴상한 의식을 행할 채비를 했다. 어수선한 그의 생각으로 미루어, 만약 그 어설픈 첫 시도가 그 네발짐승의 사나운 으르렁거림에 가로막히지 않았더라면, 그는 머지않아 치명적까지는 아니어도 어떤 의심을 살 만한 실수에 빠지고 말았을 공산이 컸다. 그는 세 차례나 거듭 시도를 이어 가려 했으나, 그때마다 똑같이 영문 모를 방해에 부딪혔으니, 그 방해는 매번 앞엣것보다 한층 더 사납고 위협적인 듯했다.\n\n\"교활한 자들이 시샘하는군.\" 휴런족 추장이 말했다. \"나는 가겠네. 형제여, 저 여인은 내 가장 용맹한 젊은이 가운데 하나의 아내일세. 정당하게 다뤄 주게. 조용히!\" 그는 못마땅해하는 짐승에게 잠잠하라고 손짓하며 덧붙였다. \"나는 가네.\"\n\n추장은 제 말을 지켰고, 던컨은 이제 이 거칠고 황량한 거처에 무력한 병자와 사납고 위험한 짐승과 함께 홀로 남게 되었다. 후자는 곰이 지녔다고 알려진 그 영리한 기색으로 인디언의 발걸음에 귀를 기울이고 있다가, 또 다른 메아리가 그 또한 동굴을 떠났음을 알리자, 몸을 돌려 던컨에게로 뒤뚱뒤뚱 다가와 그 앞에서 사람처럼 꼿꼿이 선 본래의 자세로 주저앉았다. 청년은 이제 진지하게 예상하고 있는 그 공격에 맞설 만한 무기가 없을까 하여 근심스레 주위를 살폈다.\n\n그러나 보아하니 그 짐승의 기분은 별안간 달라진 듯했다. 못마땅한 으르렁거림을 이어 가거나 노여움의 기색을 더 드러내는 대신, 그 텁수룩한 몸뚱이 전체가 무언가 기이한 속의 경련에라도 휘둘린 듯 격하게 떨렸다. 거대하고 둔중한 발톱이 씩 벌어진 주둥이 언저리를 멍청하게 더듬었고, 헤이워드가 미심쩍은 경계심으로 그 동작에서 눈을 떼지 못하고 있는 사이, 그 험상궂은 머리가 한쪽으로 툭 떨어지더니, 그 자리에 정찰꾼의 정직하고 다부진 얼굴이 나타났는데, 그는 마음 깊은 데서 우러나오는 자기만의 독특한 방식으로 유쾌함을 한껏 누리고 있었다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 빈틈없는 숲사람이 헤이워드의 놀란 외침을 가로막으며 말했다. \"그 악당들이 이 근처에 있소. 주술과 어울리지 않는 소리는 무엇이든 그놈들을 떼로 다시 우리에게 몰려오게 할 거요.\"\n\n\"이 가장극이 무슨 영문인지 말해 주시오. 그리고 어찌하여 이토록 무모한 모험을 시도했는지도.\"\n\n\"아! 이성과 계산은 우연에 능가당하는 일이 잦지요.\" 정찰꾼이 대답했다. \"허나 이야기란 늘 처음부터 시작해야 하는 법이니, 차례대로 죄다 말씀드리리다. 우리가 헤어진 뒤, 나는 지휘관과 사가모어를 묵은 비버 굴에 들여보냈소. 그곳이 에드워드 요새 수비대보다도 휴런족으로부터 더 안전하지요. 그 북서쪽 깊은 데 사는 인디언들은 아직 그들 사이에 장사꾼을 들이지 않은 터라 여태 비버를 떠받든다오. 그러고 나서 엉커스와 나는, 약조한 대로 다른 진영으로 밀고 나아갔소. 그 젊은이를 보셨소?\"\n\n\"보았으니, 그게 큰 슬픔이오! 그는 사로잡혀, 해가 뜨면 죽임을 당하도록 선고받았소.\"\n\n# THE MASQUERADER 268\n\n\"그런 운명이 그를 덮치지 않을까 미심쩍긴 했소.\" 정찰꾼이 조금 덜 자신만만하고 덜 즐거운 어조로 말을 이었다. 그러나 곧 본래의 굳건한 목소리를 되찾으며 그는 계속했다. \"그 젊은이의 불운이야말로 내가 여기 있는 진짜 까닭이오. 그런 아이를 휴런족에게 내버려 두는 일은 결코 있을 수 없으니. 그놈들이 '날뛰는 사슴'과, 그놈들이 나를 부르는 이름인 '긴 총'을 같은 화형 기둥에 묶을 수 있었다면, 그 악당들이 보기 드문 잔치를 벌였을 거요! 허나 그놈들이 어찌하여 내게 그런 이름을 붙였는지는 도무지 알 수가 없었소. '사슴잡이'의 재간과 진짜 캐나다 총 한 자루의 솜씨 사이의 닮은 데가, 곰방대 돌과 부싯돌의 본성 사이만큼이나 적은데 말이오!\"\n\n\"이야기나 계속하시오.\" 조바심이 난 헤이워드가 말했다. \"휴런족이 언제 돌아올지 알 수 없으니.\"\n\n\"그놈들은 걱정 마시오. 주술사에게도 제 시간이 필요한 법이니, 정착지를 떠도는 떠돌이 사제처럼 말이오. 우리는 두 시간짜리 설교의 첫머리에 든 선교사만큼이나 방해받을 일이 없소. 자, 엉커스와 나는 돌아오는 그 악당 무리와 마주쳤소. 그 젊은이가 정찰꾼치고는 너무 앞서 나갔던 거요. 아니, 그 점으로 말하자면, 피가 뜨거운 자이니 그리 탓할 일도 아니지요. 게다가 결국 휴런족 가운데 하나가 겁쟁이로 드러나, 달아나면서 그를 매복으로 끌고 들어갔소.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 약점의 대가를 톡톡히 치렀군!\"\n\n정찰꾼이 의미심장하게 제 목을 손으로 쓱 그으며, \"당신 뜻을 알아들었소\" 하고 말하듯 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러고는 좀 더 또렷하나 그다지 더 알아듣기 쉽지는 않은 말로 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그 젊은이를 잃은 뒤, 짐작하다시피, 나는 휴런족 쪽으로 돌아섰소. 그놈들의 외곽 보초 한둘과 나 사이에 작은 다툼이 있었으나, 그건 이러쿵저러쿵할 일이 아니오. 그래서 그 악귀들을 쏘아 넘긴 뒤, 나는 더 이상의 소동 없이 굴 가까이까지 꽤 다가갔소. 그런데 운이 내게 무얼 베풀어 주었느냐 하면, 바로 그 부족에서 가장 이름난 주술사 가운데 하나가 사탄과 벌일 어떤 큰 싸움에 대비해 — 내가 잘 알기로 — 차림을 갖추고 있던 바로 그 자리로 나를 이끌어 준 거요. 허나 이제 보니 분명 섭리의 특별한 안배였던 것을 어찌 운이라 부를 수 있겠소! 그래서 머리를 가늠 좋게 한 대 후려쳐 그 거짓 사기꾼을 한동안 뻣뻣하게 만들고, 소란을 막으려고 호두 한 조각을 저녁 삼아 물려 두 묘목 사이에 매달아 둔 뒤, 그자의 장식을 거리낌 없이 빌려 입고, 일이 진척되도록 곰 역을 내가 떠맡았던 거요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 배역을 참으로 훌륭히 해내셨소. 짐승 본인이라도 그 흉내에 무색했을 거요.\"\n\n\"이런, 소령.\" 우쭐해진 숲사람이 대꾸했다. \"그토록 오래 황야에서 배워 온 자가 그런 짐승의 동작과 본성을 펼쳐 보일 줄 모른다면, 나는 형편없는 학생일 뿐이겠지요. 만약 그게 살쾡이나, 아니 덩치 큰 표범이었더라면, 당신께 볼만한 솜씨로 멋을 부려 보였을 거요. 허나 그토록 굼뜬 짐승의 짓을 보여 주는 것은 그리 놀랄 만한 재주가 못 되오. 하긴 그 점으로 말하자면, 곰도 지나치게 연기될 수 있긴 하지요. 그렇소, 그렇소. 본성이란 흉내 내어 맞추기보다 능가하기가 더 쉽다는 것을 아는 흉내꾼은 흔치 않소. 허나 우리 할 일은 죄다 아직 앞에 남아 있소. 그 고운 아씨는 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"하늘이나 알겠소. 마을의 굴이란 굴은 죄다 살펴봤으나, 그 부족 안에 그분이 있다는 자취라곤 손톱만큼도 찾지 못했소.\"\n\n\"그 노래쟁이가 우리를 떠나며 한 말을 들으셨지요 — '그분이 가까이 계시고, 당신을 기다린다'고?\"\n\n\"나는 그가 이 가엾은 여인을 두고 한 말이라고 믿을 수밖에 없었소.\"\n\n\"그 얼간이가 겁에 질려 전갈을 어설프게 더듬은 거요. 허나 그에게는 더 깊은 뜻이 있었소. 여기엔 정착지 전체를 갈라놓을 만큼 벽이 충분하오. 곰이라면 기어오를 줄 알아야지. 그러니 내 그 벽들 위를 한번 살펴보리다. 이 바위틈에 꿀단지가 숨겨져 있을지도 모르오. 알다시피 나는 단것을 탐하는 짐승이니까.\"\n\n정찰꾼은 제 너스레에 웃으며 뒤를 돌아보더니, 가는 동안 자신이 흉내 내는 짐승의 굼뜬 동작을 본떠 가며 칸막이 벽을 기어올랐다. 그러나 꼭대기에 닿은 순간, 그는 조용히 하라는 몸짓을 하고는 더없이 황급하게 미끄러져 내려왔다.\n\n\"그분이 여기 계시오.\" 그가 속삭였다. \"저 문으로 가면 그분을 찾을 거요. 시름에 잠긴 그 영혼에 위로의 말 한마디 건네고 싶었으나, 이런 괴물을 보면 정신을 놓을지도 모르니. 하긴 그 점으로 말하자면, 소령, 당신도 그 칠갑을 한 꼴이 그리 반가운 모습은 못 되오만.\"\n\n이미 간절히 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔던 던컨은, 이 맥 빠지는 말을 듣고 곧장 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\n\"그럼 내가 그토록 흉측하단 말이오?\" 그는 분한 기색으로 물었다.\n\n\"늑대를 놀래키거나 로열 아메리칸 부대의 돌격을 돌려세울 정도는 아니오. 허나 당신이 더 나은 얼굴을 하고 있던 때를 나는 본 적이 있소. 그 줄무늬 칠갑한 얼굴이야 스쿼들에게는 그리 나쁘게 보이지 않으나, 백인 피를 가진 젊은 여인은 제 살빛을 더 좋아하는 법이오. 보시오.\" 그는 물이 바위에서 똑똑 떨어져 곁의 틈으로 빠져나가기 전 작은 수정 같은 샘을 이루는 자리를 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"사가모어의 그 칠갑은 손쉽게 지울 수 있을 거요. 돌아오면 내가 새로 멋을 한번 부려 보겠소. 주술사가 칠을 바꾸는 거야 정착지의 멋쟁이 사내가 차림을 바꾸는 것만큼이나 흔한 일이니까.\"\n\n이 신중한 숲사람은 제 충고를 밀어붙일 논거를 찾느라 애쓸 것도 없었다. 그가 아직 말하고 있을 적에 던컨은 벌써 그 물을 썼다. 삽시간에 흉측하거나 거슬리던 자국이 죄다 지워졌고, 청년은 자연이 그에게 베푼 본래의 이목구비로 다시 나타났다. 이렇게 연인과 만날 채비를 갖추고서, 그는 동료에게 황급히 작별을 고하고, 일러 준 통로로 사라졌다. 정찰꾼은 흐뭇하게 그의 떠남을 지켜보며 뒤에 대고 고개를 끄덕이고는 잘되기를 비는 말을 중얼거렸다. 그러고 나서 그는 더없이 침착하게 휴런족 사이의 식량 저장 형편을 살피기 시작했으니 — 그 동굴은 다른 여러 용도와 더불어 그들이 사냥한 것들을 갈무리하는 곳간으로도 쓰이고 있었다.\n\n던컨에게는 멀리서 어렴풋이 비치는 불빛 말고는 다른 안내랄 것이 없었으나, 그것이 이 연인에게는 북극성 노릇을 톡톡히 해 주었다. 그 빛의 도움으로 그는 자기 희망의 항구로 들어설 수 있었으니, 그곳은 그저 동굴의 또 다른 한 칸으로, 윌리엄 헨리 요새 지휘관의 딸이라는 그토록 중요한 포로를 안전히 가두기 위해 따로 마련된 데였다. 그 안에는 그 불운한 요새에서 약탈한 물건들이 잔뜩 어질러져 있었다. 이 어수선함 한가운데서 그는 자신이 찾던 이를 발견했으니, 창백하고 근심에 차 두려워하고 있었으나, 여전히 사랑스러웠다. 데이비드가 그런 방문이 있으리라고 그녀에게 미리 일러 두었던 것이다.\n\n\"던컨!\" 그녀는, 제 입에서 나온 그 소리에 스스로 떨리는 듯한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"앨리스.\" 그가 대답하며, 짐짝과 상자, 무기, 가구 사이를 거침없이 뛰어넘어 마침내 그녀 곁에 섰다.\n\n\"당신이 결코 나를 저버리지 않으리란 걸 알았어요.\" 그녀는, 그러잖아도 풀이 죽어 있던 얼굴에 한순간 화색을 띠며 그를 올려다보고 말했다. \"하지만 혼자시군요! 이렇게 기억해 주시니 고맙긴 하지만, 당신이 정말 혼자가 아니라고 생각할 수 있기를 바라요.\"\n\n그녀가 도무지 서 있을 수 없을 만큼 떨고 있는 것을 본 던컨은, 그녀를 부드럽게 이끌어 앉게 하고는, 우리가 기록해 온 그 주요한 사건들을 들려주었다. 앨리스는 숨죽인 채 빠져들어 귀를 기울였다. 청년이 비탄에 잠긴 그 아버지의 슬픔을 가볍게만 짚으며, 듣는 이의 자존심을 다치지 않으려 조심했음에도, 딸의 두 뺨에는 마치 한 번도 운 적이 없었던 양 눈물이 거침없이 흘러내렸다. 그러나 던컨의 어루만지는 다정함은 곧 그 격정의 첫 북받침을 가라앉혔고, 그리하여 그녀는, 마음이 차분해지지는 못했어도, 한눈팔지 않는 정성으로 그의 말을 끝까지 들었다.\n\n\"자, 앨리스.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"이제 당신에게 아직 얼마나 많은 것이 기대되고 있는지 알게 될 거요. 우리의 노련하고 더없이 소중한 벗, 저 정찰꾼의 도움으로, 우리는 이 미개한 무리에게서 빠져나갈 길을 찾을 수 있을 거요. 허나 당신은 가진 힘을 다해 굳세야만 하오. 당신이 그 존경스러운 부친의 품으로 달아나는 것임을, 그리고 당신 자신의 행복은 물론 그분의 행복까지도 얼마나 그 분발에 달려 있는지를 명심하시오.\"\n\n\"저를 위해 그토록 많은 일을 해 주신 아버지를 위해 제가 어찌 달리할 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"나를 위해서도.\" 청년이, 두 손으로 감싸 쥔 그 손을 가만히 누르며 말을 이었다.\n\n그 대답으로 받은 천진함과 놀라움의 표정에, 던컨은 좀 더 분명히 말할 필요가 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"이런 이기적인 바람으로 당신을 붙들어 둘 자리도 때도 아니오.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"허나 나처럼 짐을 진 마음이 어찌 그 짐을 부려 놓고 싶지 않겠소? 사람들이 말하기를, 고난이야말로 가장 가까운 끈이라 하오. 당신을 위해 함께 겪은 고통이, 당신 부친과 나 사이에 설명할 것을 거의 남기지 않았소.\"\n\n\"그럼 가장 사랑하는 코라는요, 던컨. 설마 코라를 잊은 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"잊다니! 아니오. 안타까워했소. 여인을 그토록 슬퍼한 일은 일찍이 드물었을 만큼. 당신의 존경스러운 부친은 두 자식을 차별하지 않으셨소. 허나 나는 — 앨리스, 내가 이런 말을 해도 노여워하지 마시오, 나에게는 그분의 가치가 어느 정도 가려져 있었소 —\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 제 언니의 진가를 모르셨던 거예요.\" 앨리스가 손을 거두며 말했다. \"언니는 늘 당신을 자신의 가장 가까운 벗처럼 이야기하는걸요.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그렇게 믿고 싶소.\" 던컨이 황급히 대꾸했다. \"그 이상이기를 바랄 수도 있을 거요. 허나 앨리스, 당신과는, 당신 부친의 허락을 얻어 그보다 한결 더 가깝고 더 소중한 끈을 바랄 수 있는 거요.\"\n\n앨리스는 격하게 떨었고, 한순간 얼굴을 옆으로 돌리며 제 성에 흔한 감정에 굴복했다. 그러나 그것은 곧 지나가, 그녀에게 — 그 애정은 못 다스렸을지언정 — 제 거동만은 다스릴 여유를 남겼다.\n\n\"헤이워드.\" 그녀는 천진함과 의지함이 어린 애틋한 표정으로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"저를 더 다그치시기 전에, 그 아버지의 거룩한 임석과 성스러운 허락을 제게 베풀어 주세요.\"\n\n\"더는 말하지 말아야겠으나, 이보다 덜 말할 수도 없소.\" 청년이 대답하려는 찰나, 그의 어깨를 가볍게 톡 두드리는 손길에 말이 끊겼다. 벌떡 일어선 그가 몸을 돌려 침입자와 맞서니, 그의 시선이 머구아의 거뭇한 형상과 악의에 찬 얼굴에 가닿았다. 이런 순간에 들려온 그 미개인의 깊고 목쉰 웃음은, 던컨에게는 마치 악마의 지옥 같은 조롱처럼 들렸다. 그 찰나의 갑작스럽고 사나운 충동을 좇았더라면, 그는 그 휴런족에게 몸을 던져 두 사람의 운명을 죽음을 건 싸움의 결판에 내맡겼으리라. 그러나 그 어떤 무기도 없고, 그 교활한 적이 어떤 도움을 불러들일 수 있는지도 모르며, 바로 그때 어느 때보다도 제 마음에 소중한 이의 안위를 짊어진 처지였기에, 그는 그 무모한 생각을 품기가 무섭게 곧 단념하고 말았다.\n\n\"무엇을 바라는 거죠?\" 앨리스가, 가슴 위로 다소곳이 팔짱을 끼고, 헤이워드를 위한 두려움의 고통을 감추려 애쓰며, 자신을 사로잡은 자의 방문을 받을 때 늘 짓던 그 차갑고 거리를 둔 태도로 말했다.\n\n의기양양해하던 인디언은 다시 준엄한 얼굴을 되찾았으나, 청년의 불타는 눈빛이 던지는 위협 앞에서는 조심스레 뒤로 물러섰다. 그는 잠시 두 포로를 한결같은 눈길로 바라보다가, 옆으로 비켜서서, 던컨이 들어온 문과는 다른 문을 가로질러 통나무 하나를 떨어뜨려 막았다. 던컨은 이제 자신이 기습당한 까닭을 깨달았고, 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장났다고 믿으며, 앨리스를 제 가슴에 끌어안고는, 이런 동무와 함께 겪을 일이기에 거의 한스럽지도 않은 운명을 맞을 채비를 하고 섰다. 그러나 머구아는 당장의 폭력을 꾀하지는 않았다. 그의 첫 조치는, 보아하니, 새 포로를 단단히 가두려는 것이 분명했다. 그는 자신이 써 온 그 은밀한 출구를 통한 도주의 희망을 완전히 끊어 놓기 전까지는, 동굴 한가운데 미동도 없는 두 형체에 두 번째 눈길조차 주지 않았다. 헤이워드는 그의 모든 동작을 지켜보았으나, 여전히 굳건히, 가녀린 앨리스의 몸을 제 가슴에 끌어안은 채 서 있었으니, 그토록 자주 좌절당한 적에게 자비를 구하기에는 너무 자존심이 강하고 또 너무 희망이 없었던 것이다. 머구아는 목적을 이루자 포로들에게 다가가 영어로 말했다.\n\n\"창백한 얼굴들은 교활한 비버를 덫으로 잡지. 허나 붉은 피부는 양키를 잡는 법을 안다.\"\n\n\"휴런, 마음껏 해 봐라!\" 흥분한 헤이워드가, 제 목숨에 두 사람의 운명이 걸려 있음을 잊은 채 외쳤다. \"너도 네 복수도 똑같이 경멸할 따름이다.\"\n\n\"백인은 화형 기둥에서도 그 말을 하겠나?\" 머구아가, 그 말에 곁들인 비웃음으로 상대의 결의를 얼마나 믿지 않는지 동시에 드러내며 물었다.\n\n\"여기서, 너와 단둘이 얼굴을 맞대고든, 네 부족이 다 보는 앞에서든.\"\n\n\"르나르 쉬브틸은 위대한 추장이다!\" 인디언이 대꾸했다. \"내 가서 젊은이들을 데려와, 창백한 얼굴이 고문을 얼마나 용감하게 비웃는지 보여 주겠다.\"\n\n그는 말하면서 돌아서서, 던컨이 다가왔던 그 통로로 자리를 떠나려 했으나, 으르렁대는 소리가 그의 귀를 붙잡아 그를 멈칫하게 했다. 곰의 형상이 문간에 나타나, 그곳에 앉아 늘 그러듯 안절부절 좌우로 몸을 구르고 있었다. 머구아는, 병든 여인의 아버지가 그랬듯, 그 정체를 가늠하려는 듯 잠시 날카롭게 그것을 살폈다. 그는 제 부족의 더 천박한 미신을 한참 넘어선 자였기에, 그 주술사의 낯익은 차림새를 알아보자마자 냉랭한 경멸로 그것을 지나치려 했다. 그러나 더 크고 더 위협적인 으르렁거림에 그는 다시 멈춰 섰다. 그러고는 별안간 더는 시간을 끌지 않기로 결심한 듯, 단호히 앞으로 나아갔다. 그 흉내 짐승은, 조금 앞으로 다가왔다가, 그의 앞에서 천천히 물러나 마침내 다시 그 통로에 이르자, 뒷다리로 일어서서 제 야수 본보기가 하는 식으로 앞발로 허공을 휘저었다.\n\n\"멍청한 것!\" 추장이 휴런 말로 외쳤다. \"가서 아이들과 스쿼들이나 데리고 놀아라. 사내들은 사내의 지혜에 맡겨 두고.\"\n\n그는, 허리띠에 매달린 칼이나 도끼를 쓰겠다는 위협의 시늉조차 깔보며, 그 돌팔이라 여긴 자를 다시 한번 지나치려 했다. 별안간 그 짐승이 두 팔을, 아니 두 다리를 뻗어, 저 이름난 \"곰의 포옹\"의 힘에 견줄 만한 그러쥠으로 그를 옭아맸다. 헤이워드는 매부리의 그 모든 거동을 숨죽인 채 지켜보고 있었다. 그는 먼저 앨리스를 놓고, 어떤 짐짝에 둘러쳤던 사슴가죽 끈 한 가닥을 집어 들었으니, 정찰꾼의 무쇠 같은 근육에 두 팔이 옆구리에 박힌 적을 보자, 그자에게 달려들어 그 팔을 거기에 단단히 옭아맸다. 우리가 이 정황을 적어 내려간 것보다 짧은 동안에, 팔과 다리와 발이 그 끈의 스무 겹에 휘감겼다. 그 가공할 휴런족이 완전히 결박되자, 정찰꾼은 손을 풀었고, 던컨은 적을 등으로 눕혀, 도무지 어쩌지 못하게 만들었다.\n\n이 갑작스럽고 기이한 일이 진행되는 내내, 머구아는, 제 신경보다 한결 더 단단히 죈 자의 손아귀에 들었음을 확신하기 전까지 격하게 몸부림쳤음에도, 외마디 소리조차 내지 않았다. 그러나 매부리가, 제 행동을 간추려 설명하려는 셈으로, 그 짐승의 텁수룩한 턱을 벗겨 내고 자신의 우락부락하고 진지한 얼굴을 휴런족의 눈앞에 드러내자, 후자의 철학이 그만 무너져, 그로 하여금 한 번도 빠지는 법 없는 그 소리를 내뱉게 했다.\n\n\"흐엉!\"\n\n\"아! 이제 혀를 찾았군.\" 흔들림 없는 그의 정복자가 말했다. \"자, 그 혀를 써서 우리를 망치지 못하게, 내 실례를 무릅쓰고 자네 입을 막아야겠네.\"\n\n지체할 틈이 없었으므로, 정찰꾼은 그토록 긴요한 조처를 곧장 행하기 시작했다. 그가 인디언에게 재갈을 물리고 나자, 그 적은 무사히 전투력을 잃었다고 여겨도 좋을 만했다.\n\n\"그 악귀가 어디로 들어왔소?\" 일을 마친 부지런한 정찰꾼이 물었다. \"당신이 나를 떠난 뒤로 내 쪽으로는 단 한 놈도 지나간 적이 없소.\"\n\n던컨은 머구아가 들어온 문을 가리켰으니, 그 문은 이제 황급히 빠져나가기에는 장애물이 너무 많았다.\n\n\"그럼 그 고운 아씨를 데려오시오.\" 그의 벗이 말을 이었다. \"다른 출구로 숲을 향해 달려야 하오.\"\n\n\"그건 불가능하오!\" 던컨이 말했다. \"두려움이 그분을 짓눌러, 도무지 몸을 가누지 못하오. 앨리스! 사랑스러운, 내 소중한 앨리스, 정신을 차려요. 지금이 달아날 때요. 헛되구나! 듣기는 하나 따를 수가 없으니. 가시오, 고결하고 갸륵한 벗이여. 당신이나 살아남고, 나는 내 운명에 맡겨 두시오!\"\n\n\"길마다 끝이 있고, 재앙마다 그 가르침을 가져오는 법이오!\" 정찰꾼이 대꾸했다. \"자, 저 인디언 천으로 그분을 감싸시오. 그 작은 몸뚱이를 죄다 가리시오. 아니, 그 발은 황야에 짝이 없으니, 그분의 정체를 들통낼 거요. 죄다, 한 군데도 빠짐없이. 자, 이제 그분을 팔에 안고 따라오시오. 나머지는 내게 맡기고.\"\n\n그 벗의 말에서 짐작할 수 있듯, 던컨은 간절히 따르고 있었다. 상대가 말을 마치자, 그는 가벼운 앨리스의 몸을 두 팔에 안고, 정찰꾼의 발자취를 좇았다. 그들은 떠나올 때 그대로, 여전히 홀로 있는 그 병든 여인을 보고는, 천연의 회랑을 따라 입구께로 잰걸음으로 나아갔다. 나무껍질로 된 작은 문에 다가서니, 밖에서 웅성대는 목소리들이, 병자의 벗들과 친지들이 그 자리에 모여 다시 들어오라는 부름을 참을성 있게 기다리고 있음을 알렸다.\n\n\"내가 입을 열어 말하면,\" 매부리가 속삭였다. \"백인의 진짜 혀인 내 영어가, 그 악당들에게 적이 그들 사이에 끼어 있다고 일러바칠 거요. 당신이 그놈들 말을 해야 하오, 소령. 우리가 악령을 동굴에 가두었으며, 기운을 돋울 뿌리를 찾으러 그 여인을 숲으로 데려가는 길이라고 말하시오. 가진 꾀를 죄다 부리시오, 이건 정당한 일이니.\"\n\n문이 조금 열렸으니, 마치 밖의 누군가가 안의 일을 엿듣기라도 하듯, 정찰꾼은 그 지시를 멈출 수밖에 없었다. 사나운 으르렁거림이 그 엿듣는 자를 물리쳤고, 그러자 정찰꾼은 대담하게 나무껍질 덮개를 활짝 젖히고는, 가는 동안 곰의 배역을 연기하며 그곳을 떠났다. 던컨은 그의 뒤를 바싹 따랐고, 그리하여 근심에 찬 스무 명의 친지와 벗들 한가운데 서게 되었다.\n\n무리가 조금 물러서며, 그 아버지와, 보아하니 그 여인의 남편인 듯한 자가 다가오게 길을 터 주었다.\n\n\"내 형제가 악령을 쫓아냈는가?\" 전자가 물었다. \"팔에 안은 것은 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"그대의 자식일세.\" 던컨이 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"병이 그녀에게서 빠져나갔네. 그것은 바위 속에 갇혔어. 내 이 여인을 먼 데로 데려가, 어떤 추가 발작에도 견디도록 그녀를 튼튼히 하겠네. 해가 다시 뜨면 그녀는 젊은이의 천막에 있을 걸세.\"\n\n그 아버지가 낯선 자의 말뜻을 휴런 말로 옮기자, 낮게 웅성대는 소리가 그 소식을 받아들이는 흡족함을 알렸다. 추장 자신이 던컨에게 가라고 손을 내저으며, 굳건한 목소리로, 드높은 거동으로 소리 높여 말했다.\n\n\"가게. 나는 사내이니, 내가 바위 속에 들어가 그 악한 자와 싸우겠네.\"\n\n헤이워드는 기꺼이 그 말을 따라, 이미 그 작은 무리를 지나쳤으나, 이 섬뜩한 말에 발이 묶였다.\n\n\"내 형제가 미쳤는가?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"잔혹한가! 그가 병과 마주치면 병이 그에게 들어갈 것이고, 아니면 그가 병을 몰아내면 병이 그의 딸을 숲으로 쫓아갈 걸세. 안 되네. 내 자식들을 밖에서 기다리게 하고, 그 영이 나타나거든 몽둥이로 후려쳐 쓰러뜨리게. 그것은 교활하여, 얼마나 많은 이가 저와 싸울 채비를 하고 있는지 보면 산속에 제 몸을 묻어 버릴 걸세.\"\n\n이 별난 경고는 바라던 대로 들어맞았다. 동굴에 들어가는 대신, 아버지와 남편은 도끼를 빼 들고, 그 병든 친지를 괴롭히는 가상의 자에게 복수를 퍼부을 채비를 하고 자리를 잡았으며, 여인들과 아이들은 같은 뜻으로 덤불에서 가지를 꺾거나 바위 조각을 움켜쥐었다. 이 좋은 틈을 타, 그 가짜 주술사들은 자취를 감췄다.\n\n매부리는 인디언 미신의 본성에 그토록 깊이 기대면서도, 그것이 가장 슬기로운 추장들에게는 의지되기보다 그저 묵인될 따름이라는 것을 모르지 않았다. 그는 지금 이 위급한 상황에서 시간의 값어치를 잘 알고 있었다. 그의 적들의 자기기만이 아무리 깊든, 그것이 그의 계략을 거드는 데 아무리 보탬이 되었든, 인디언의 그 교묘한 본성에 작용하는 의심의 사소한 씨앗 하나가 치명적인 것으로 드러나기 십상이었다. 그리하여 그는 남의 눈에 가장 띄지 않을 만한 길을 골라, 마을 안으로 들어가기보다 그 가장자리를 에둘렀다. 전사들은 여전히 멀리서, 사그라드는 모닥불빛에 의지해 천막에서 천막으로 어슬렁거리는 모습이 보였다. 그러나 아이들은 놀이를 거두고 가죽 잠자리로 들었고, 밤의 고요가 어느새 그토록 분주하고 중대한 저녁의 소란과 흥분을 누르고 자리 잡기 시작하고 있었다.\n\n앨리스는 탁 트인 공기의 되살리는 기운 아래 정신을 차렸으니, 약해진 것이 마음의 힘이라기보다 몸의 힘이었던지라, 그동안 벌어진 일에 대해 굳이 설명을 들을 필요가 없었다.\n\n\"이제 제가 걸어 보도록 할게요.\" 그녀가, 숲에 들어섰을 때, 보는 이는 없었으나 진작 던컨의 품을 벗어나지 못한 것을 부끄러워하며 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"정말이지 기운을 차렸어요.\"\n\n\"아니오, 앨리스, 당신은 아직 너무 약하오.\"\n\n처녀는 가만히 몸부림치며 벗어나려 했고, 헤이워드는 그 소중한 짐을 내려놓을 수밖에 없었다. 그 곰을 연기하던 자는, 두 팔로 연인을 끌어안고 있는 동안에도 사랑하는 이의 그 달콤한 감정과는 분명 아무 인연이 없었으리라. 또한 그는, 떨고 있는 앨리스를 짓누른 그 천진한 부끄러움의 본성과도 어쩌면 인연이 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 천막에서 알맞은 거리에 이르자 걸음을 멈추고, 자신이 환히 꿰고 있는 한 가지 주제를 이야기했다.\n\n\"이 길을 따라가면 시내가 나올 거요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 북쪽 기슭을 따라 폭포에 이를 때까지 가시오. 그리고 오른편 언덕을 오르면, 다른 부족의 모닥불이 보일 거요. 거기로 가서 보호를 청하시오. 그들이 진짜 델라웨어족이라면, 당신은 안전할 거요. 지금 그 고운 아씨를 데리고 멀리 달아나는 것은 불가능하오. 우리가 열 몇 마일을 가기도 전에 휴런족이 우리 자취를 좇아 우리 머릿가죽을 차지할 거요. 가시오, 그리고 섭리가 함께하기를.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은!\" 헤이워드가 놀라 물었다. \"설마 여기서 헤어지자는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"휴런족이 델라웨어족의 자랑을 붙들고 있소. 모히칸족의 그 고귀한 피의 마지막 후예가 그놈들의 손아귀에 있단 말이오.\" 정찰꾼이 대꾸했다. \"내 가서 그를 위해 무얼 할 수 있을지 보겠소. 그놈들이 당신의 머릿가죽을 차지했더라면, 소령, 내 약조한 대로 그 머리칼 한 올마다 악당 하나씩 쓰러졌을 거요. 허나 만약 그 젊은 사가모어가 화형 기둥으로 끌려가야 한다면, 인디언들은 십자가 없는 사내가 어떻게 죽는지도 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n이 다부진 숲사람이, 어느 정도 제 양자라 할 만한 이에게 그토록 분명한 편애를 보였음에도, 던컨은 조금도 노여워하지 않고, 그토록 무모한 시도에 맞설 만한 갖가지 이유를 여전히 들이댔다. 거기에는 앨리스도 힘을 보태, 헤이워드의 간청에 제 간청을 보태어, 그토록 큰 위험을 품고도 성공의 가망은 그토록 적은 그 결심을 버리라고 빌었다. 그들의 능변과 재간은 헛되이 흩어졌다. 정찰꾼은 정성껏, 그러나 못마땅하게 그들의 말을 듣고는, 마침내 앨리스를 단번에 잠잠하게 만들고 헤이워드에게는 더 이상의 만류가 얼마나 부질없는지를 일러 주는 어조로 대답하여 그 논의를 끝맺었다.\n\n\"내 듣기로는,\" 그가 말했다. \"젊은 시절에는 아버지가 아들에 매인 것보다 더 단단히 사내를 여인에게 묶는 어떤 정이 있다 하더이다. 그럴지도 모르지요. 나는 내 살빛의 여인들이 사는 데 가 본 적이 거의 없으니, 정착지에서야 그게 천성의 도리일지도 모르오. 당신은 이 고운 아씨를 빼내려고 목숨과, 당신에게 소중한 모든 것을 걸었으니, 그 밑바닥에는 그런 어떤 마음이 깔려 있으리라 짐작하오. 나로 말하자면, 나는 그 젊은이에게 소총의 참된 됨됨이를 가르쳤고, 그는 그 값을 톡톡히 치러 주었소. 나는 피 흘리는 숱한 싸움에서 그의 곁에서 싸웠고, 한쪽 귀로는 그의 총소리를, 다른 쪽 귀로는 사가모어의 총소리를 들을 수 있는 한, 내 등 뒤에 적이 없음을 알았소. 겨울이고 여름이고, 밤이고 낮이고, 우리는 함께 황야를 누볐고, 같은 그릇으로 먹었으며, 하나가 자면 다른 하나가 지켜보았소. 그러니 엉커스가 고문에 끌려갔는데 내가 곁에 있었으면서도 — 우리 모두에게는, 살빛이 어떻든, 단 한 분의 다스리는 분이 계실 따름이오. 그분을 증인으로 부르노니, 그 모히칸 젊은이가 벗이 없어 스러지기 전에, 신의가 이 땅에서 사라지고, '사슴잡이'가 저 노래쟁이의 빽빽대는 무기만큼이나 무용해질 거요!\"\n\n# THE LOVERS 278\n\n던컨은 정찰꾼의 팔을 잡았던 손을 놓았고, 정찰꾼은 돌아서서 흔들림 없이 천막을 향해 발길을 되짚어 갔다. 멀어지는 그 모습을 잠시 바라보고 나서, 성공했으나 여전히 슬픔에 잠긴 헤이워드와 앨리스는, 함께 멀리 델라웨어족의 마을을 향해 길을 잡았다.\n\n바텀. — 나도 사자 역을 맡게 해 주오.\n\n한여름 밤의 꿈\n\n〈호크아이〉의 결의가 아무리 드높았다 한들, 그는 자신이 무릅쓰려는 온갖 어려움과 위험을 똑똑히 헤아리고 있었다. 진지로 돌아가는 동안, 그의 예리하고 단련된 지력은 적들의 경계심과 의심을 따돌릴 방도를 궁리하는 데 골똘히 매달려 있었으니, 그 경계심과 의심이 결코 제 것에 못지않다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 〈마구아〉와 주술사의 목숨을 살린 것은 오직 그 피부색뿐이었으니, 그들이야말로 그 자신의 안전을 위해 가장 먼저 제물로 바쳐졌어야 마땅한 자들이었으되, 그런 짓이 인디언의 본성에는 아무리 어울린다 한들, 피의 섞임을 모르는 사람들의 후예임을 자랑하는 자에게는 도무지 어울리지 않는다고 척후가 믿지 않았더라면 그러했을 것이다. 그리하여 그는 포로들을 묶은 가는 가지와 끈을 믿고, 오두막들의 한복판을 향해 곧장 길을 잡아 나아갔다.\n\n건물들에 다가갈수록 그의 발걸음은 한층 신중해졌고, 그의 빈틈없는 눈은 우호적이든 적대적이든 어떤 기색도 놓치지 않았다. 버려진 오두막 한 채가 다른 것들보다 조금 앞쪽에 서 있었는데, 절반쯤 짓다가 버린 듯 보였으니, 아마도 식량이나 물 같은 더 중요한 필수 요소 가운데 무언가가 모자란 탓이었을 것이다. 그러나 그 틈새로 희미한 불빛이 새어 나와, 비록 그 구조가 미완성일지언정 그 안에 사람이 들어 있음을 알렸다. 그리하여 척후는 그쪽으로 나아갔으니, 마치 본격적인 공격을 감행하기에 앞서 적의 전초 진지를 먼저 살피려는 신중한 장군과도 같았다.\n\n자신이 흉내 내는 짐승에 알맞은 자세를 취하고서, 〈호크아이〉는 안이 들여다보이는 작은 틈으로 기어갔다. 그곳은 〈데이비드 거뮤트〉가 머무는 처소로 드러났다. 이 충직한 성가 교사는 이제 자신의 온갖 슬픔과 두려움, 그리고 섭리의 보호에 기대는 온순한 의탁심과 더불어 이곳에 와 있었다. 방금 말한 대로 그 볼품없는 몸이 척후의 관찰 아래 들어온 바로 그 순간, 정작 이 숲사람 자신은, 비록 가장한 모습이긴 하나, 그 외로운 존재가 골똘히 빠져 있던 사색의 대상이었다.\n\n〈데이비드〉가 옛 기적들의 실현에 아무리 절대적인 믿음을 품고 있다 한들, 현세의 도덕을 다스리는 데 어떤 직접적인 초자연의 작용이 개입한다는 믿음만은 멀리했다. 다시 말해, 그는 〈발람〉의 나귀가 말을 할 수 있다는 점은 절대적으로 믿었으되, 곰이 노래한다는 문제에는 다소 회의적이었던 것이다. 그럼에도 후자에 관해서는 자신의 더없이 섬세한 청각 기관의 증언에 따라 확신을 갖고 있었다. 그의 분위기와 거동에는 그 정신 상태가 완전히 혼란에 빠져 있음을 척후에게 드러내는 무언가가 있었다. 그는 잔가지를 쌓은 더미 위에 앉아, 거기서 이따금 잔가지 몇 개를 빼어 자신의 낮은 불을 지피며, 머리를 팔에 괸 채 우울한 명상의 자세를 취하고 있었다. 음악에 헌신하는 이 사람의 차림새는 방금 묘사한 것에서 별다른 변화가 없었으니, 다만 그 벗어진 머리에 삼각 비버 모자를 씌운 것만이 달랐는데, 그 모자는 포로로 잡은 자들 가운데 누구의 탐욕도 자극할 만큼 매력적이지 못했던 것이다.\n\n영리한 〈호크아이〉는 저 사람이 병든 여인의 침상 곁에서 황급히 제 자리를 버리고 떠났던 일을 떠올리며, 그토록 엄숙한 숙고의 대상이 무엇일지 의심을 품지 않을 수 없었다. 먼저 오두막을 한 바퀴 돌아, 그것이 외따로 서 있고 그 안에 든 자의 성격으로 보아 방문객의 발길이 닿지 않을 듯하다는 것을 확인한 뒤, 그는 그 낮은 문을 통해 다름 아닌 〈거뮤트〉의 면전으로 들어섰다. 후자의 위치 탓에 불이 두 사람 사이에 놓이게 되었고, 〈호크아이〉가 똑바로 자리를 잡고 앉았을 때, 거의 일 분 가까이가 흘렀는데, 그동안 둘은 아무 말 없이 서로를 바라보고만 있었다. 이 놀람의 갑작스러움과 그 성격은 — 〈데이비드〉의 철학이라 하지는 않겠으나 — 그의 신앙과 결의에 거의 감당할 수 없을 만큼 벅찼다. 그는 더듬더듬 음높이 피리를 찾았고, 음악으로 악령을 쫓으려는 어수선한 작정으로 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"어둡고 신비로운 괴물아!\" 그가 외쳤으니, 떨리는 두 손으로 보조 눈인 안경을 고쳐 쓰고, 곤경에 처할 때마다 결코 그를 저버린 적 없는 의지처, 곧 영감 어린 시편의 운문을 찾으면서였다. \"나는 네 본성도 네 의도도 알지 못하나, 만일 네가 성전의 가장 미천한 종 가운데 하나의 신변과 권리를 해치려 무엇이든 꾀하고 있다면, 〈이스라엘〉 젊은이의 영감 어린 말씀에 귀 기울이고, 회개하라.\"\n\n곰은 그 텁수룩한 옆구리를 흔들더니, 이윽고 익히 아는 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n\"그 삑삑대는 무기는 치우고, 그 목구멍에 겸손을 가르치시오. 알아들을 수 있는 평범한 〈영어〉 다섯 마디가, 바로 지금은, 한 시간의 빽빽거림보다 값지니까.\"\n\n\"그대는 무엇인가!\" 〈데이비드〉가 물었으니, 본래의 의도를 좇기에는 도무지 자격을 잃은 채, 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다.\n\n\"당신과 같은 사람이오. 그리고 그 피가 곰이나 인디언의 섞임으로 더럽혀진 정도가 당신만큼이나 덜한 사람이지. 당신이 손에 쥐고 있는 그 어리석은 도구를 누구에게서 받았는지 벌써 잊었소?\"\n\n\"이런 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가?\" 〈데이비드〉가 대답했으니, 진실이 그에게 동트기 시작하자 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었다. \"이교도들과 함께 머무는 동안 숱한 놀라운 일을 겪었으되, 분명 이보다 더한 것은 없었소!\"\n\n\"자, 자,\" 〈호크아이〉가 대꾸하며, 흔들리는 동료의 신뢰를 한결 굳혀 주려고 제 정직한 얼굴을 가렸던 것을 벗었다. \"당신이 보게 될 살갗은, 비록 저 곱상한 분들의 것만큼 희지는 않을지언정, 하늘의 바람과 햇볕이 안겨 준 것 외에는 붉은 기운이라곤 한 점 없는 것이오. 자, 이제 본론으로 들어갑시다.\"\n\n\"먼저 그 처녀와, 그토록 용감하게 그녀를 찾아 나선 젊은이의 소식부터 말해 주오.\" 〈데이비드〉가 가로막았다.\n\n\"그래요, 그들은 다행히 이 악당들의 도끼에서 풀려났소. 헌데 당신, 나를 〈언카스〉의 자취로 이끌어 줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그 젊은이는 결박되어 있고, 그 죽음이 이미 정해졌을까 몹시 두렵소. 그토록 마음씨 고운 이가 무지 속에 죽어야 한다니 크게 애통하여, 나는 어울리는 찬송가 한 곡을 찾아 두었소—\"\n\n\"그에게로 나를 데려갈 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그 일은 어렵지 않을 것이오.\" 〈데이비드〉가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"허나 당신이 나타나면 그의 불행한 운명을 덜어 주기는커녕 오히려 키울까 몹시 두렵소.\"\n\n\"더는 말 말고, 앞장이나 서시오.\" 〈호크아이〉가 대꾸하며 다시 얼굴을 가렸고, 곧장 오두막을 떠남으로써 몸소 본을 보였다.\n\n길을 가는 동안, 척후는 자기 동료가 〈언카스〉에게 접근할 수 있었던 것이 상상 속의 정신적 결함이라는 특권 덕분이며, 거기에 보초 가운데 한 사람에게서 얻은 호의가 보태진 덕임을 알게 되었다. 그 보초는 〈영어〉를 조금 할 줄 안다는 이유로 〈데이비드〉가 종교적 대화의 상대로 골랐던 자였다. 이 새 친구의 의도를 그 〈휴런〉이 얼마나 알아들었는지는 자못 의심스러우나, 오로지 자기에게만 쏟아지는 관심이란 더 문명한 자에게 그러하듯 미개인에게도 흐뭇한 법이어서, 앞서 말한 효과를 낳았던 것이다. 척후가 단순한 〈데이비드〉에게서 이 세세한 사정을 캐낸 그 영리한 솜씨를 거듭 옮길 필요는 없겠고, 필요한 사실을 모조리 손에 넣은 뒤 그가 일러 준 지시의 내용도 이 자리에서 자세히 다루지 않으련다. 그 전모는 이야기가 진행되는 동안 독자에게 충분히 밝혀질 것이기 때문이다.\n\n〈언카스〉가 갇혀 있는 오두막은 마을의 바로 한복판에 있었고, 눈에 띄지 않고 다가가거나 떠나기로는 다른 어느 곳보다도 어려운 자리였다. 그러나 조금이라도 숨으려 드는 것은 〈호크아이〉의 방책이 아니었다. 자신의 변장과, 떠맡은 배역을 끝까지 해낼 능력을 믿고, 그는 그곳으로 가장 단순하고 곧은 길을 택했다. 하지만 이 시각이, 그가 그토록 멸시하는 듯 보이던 보호를 약간이나마 그에게 베풀어 주었다. 사내아이들은 이미 잠에 빠져 있었고, 여인들은 모두, 또 전사들도 대부분 밤을 보내려 제 오두막으로 물러간 뒤였다. 후자 가운데 네댓 명만이 〈언카스〉의 감옥 문 어귀에 남아, 경계하면서도 그 포로의 거동을 가까이서 살피고 있었다.\n\n저들의 가장 이름난 주술사의 익히 아는 분장을 한 자를 거느린 〈거뮤트〉를 보자, 그들은 선뜻 둘 모두에게 길을 터 주었다. 그러면서도 떠날 기색은 전혀 보이지 않았다. 오히려 그들은 그런 방문에서 당연히 기대할 법한 신비로운 의식에 한층 더 흥미를 느껴 그 자리에 묶이고 싶어 하는 눈치가 역력했다.\n\n척후는 〈휴런〉의 말로 그들에게 도무지 말을 건넬 수가 없었던 까닭에, 대화를 온전히 〈데이비드〉에게 맡길 수밖에 없었다. 후자는 그 단순함에도 불구하고 받은 지시를 충실히 해냈으니, 그 스승의 가장 큰 기대마저 넘어설 정도였다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉 놈들은 계집이오!\" 그가 자기가 쓰는 말을 어렴풋이 알아듣는 미개인을 향해 외쳤다. \"어리석은 내 동포 〈양기〉들이 그들더러 도끼를 들고 〈캐나다〉의 제 아비들을 치라고 시켰더니, 그들은 제 성별을 잊어버렸소. 내 형제는 〈민첩한 사슴〉이 제 치마를 달라고 청하며, 화형 기둥에서 〈휴런〉 사람들 앞에 우는 꼴을 듣고 보고 싶지 않소?\"\n\n강한 동의의 어조로 내뱉은 \"흠!\" 하는 외침은, 그토록 오래 미워하고 그토록 두려워해 온 적에게서 그런 나약함의 표출을 목격하는 데서 미개인이 느낄 만족을 알렸다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그를 비키게 하시오. 그러면 영험한 자가 그 개에게 입김을 불 테니! 내 형제들에게 그리 전하시오.\"\n\n그 〈휴런〉은 〈데이비드〉의 뜻을 동료들에게 설명해 주었고, 동료들은 그들대로 그 잔혹함의 세련된 묘리에서 길들지 않은 그 정신이 으레 찾을 법한 만족감으로 그 계획에 귀 기울였다. 그들은 입구에서 조금 물러서며, 짐작컨대 그 주술사에게 들어가라는 손짓을 했다. 그러나 곰은 그 말을 따르기는커녕 차지하고 있던 자리를 지키며 으르렁거렸다.\n\n\"영험한 자는 제 입김이 형제들에게 불어 닿아 그들의 용기마저 앗아 갈까 두려워하오.\" 〈데이비드〉가 받은 암시를 활용해 말을 이었다. \"그러니 더 멀리 떨어져 서야 하오.\"\n\n그런 불행이야말로 자신들에게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 무거운 재앙이라 여길 〈휴런〉 사람들은, 한 무리가 되어 물러나, 소리가 들리지 않을 만큼은 떨어졌으되 동시에 오두막 입구는 살필 수 있는 자리를 잡았다. 그러자 자신들의 안전을 확신한 듯, 척후는 제자리를 떠나 천천히 그 안으로 들어섰다. 그곳은 고요하고 음울했으니, 오직 포로 하나만이 들어 있었고, 취사에 쓰였던 불이 사위어 가는 잉걸불이 그곳을 밝히고 있었다.\n\n〈언카스〉는 먼 구석을 차지한 채 비스듬히 기대어 있었으니, 두 손과 두 발이 모두 질기고 아픈 가는 가지로 단단히 묶여 있었다. 그 무시무시한 형상이 처음 이 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 눈앞에 나타났을 때, 그는 그 짐승에게 눈길 한 번 던지려 하지 않았다. 〈데이비드〉를 문가에 남겨 둬 자신들이 감시받지 않는지 확인하게 한 척후는, 두 사람만 있다는 것이 확실해지기 전까지는 변장을 유지하는 편이 신중하리라 여겼다. 그리하여 말을 하는 대신, 그는 자신이 흉내 내는 그 짐승의 익살 한 가지를 연기하려 애썼다. 처음에는 적들이 자신을 괴롭히고 그 담력을 시험하려 진짜 짐승을 들여보낸 줄로 믿었던 이 젊은 〈모히칸〉은, 〈헤이워드〉에게는 그토록 정확해 보였던 그 연기에서, 단번에 그 가짜임을 드러내는 몇 가지 흠을 알아챘다. 더 능란한 〈언카스〉가 자신의 연기를 얼마나 낮잡아 보는지를 〈호크아이〉가 알았더라면, 아마 부아가 나서 그 흥행을 조금 더 길게 끌었을 것이다. 그러나 젊은이의 눈에 어린 경멸의 표정은 어찌나 여러 갈래로 해석될 수 있었던지, 이 갸륵한 척후는 그런 발견의 굴욕을 면할 수 있었다. 그리하여 〈데이비드〉가 미리 약속한 신호를 보내자마자, 곰의 사나운 으르렁거림 대신 낮게 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 오두막 안에 들렸다.\n\n〈언카스〉는 그 몸을 오두막 벽에 도로 기대고 두 눈을 감았으니, 마치 그토록 경멸스럽고 불쾌한 대상을 제 시야에서 몰아내고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 그러나 그 뱀의 소리가 들리는 순간, 그는 일어나 머리를 낮게 숙인 채 사방을 두루 돌아보며, 캐묻듯 온 방향으로 고개를 돌리다가, 마침내 그 날카로운 눈이 그 텁수룩한 괴물에 가닿았고, 거기에 못 박힌 듯, 마법의 힘에 붙들린 듯 머물렀다. 다시 같은 소리가 되풀이되었으니, 분명 그 짐승의 입에서 나오는 것이었다. 또 한 번 젊은이의 눈은 오두막 안을 휘둘러보았고, 앞서 머물렀던 곳으로 되돌아오며, 깊고 억눌린 목소리로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"〈호크아이〉!\"\n\n\"저자의 결박을 끊으시오.\" 〈호크아이〉가, 마침 그때 다가온 〈데이비드〉에게 말했다.\n\n성가 교사는 명령받은 대로 했고, 〈언카스〉는 제 사지가 풀려난 것을 알았다. 바로 그 순간 그 짐승의 마른 가죽이 바스락거리더니, 이내 척후가 본래의 제 모습으로 자리에서 일어섰다. 〈모히칸〉은 제 벗이 시도한 일의 본질을 직관으로 헤아린 듯, 혀로도 표정으로도 더는 놀람의 기색을 한 가닥도 드러내지 않았다. 〈호크아이〉가 그 텁수룩한 겉옷을 벗어 던졌으니 — 가죽 끈 몇 가닥을 그저 풀기만 하면 되는 일이었다 — 그는 길게 번득이는 칼 한 자루를 뽑아 〈언카스〉의 손에 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"붉은 〈휴런〉 놈들이 바깥에 있다.\" 그가 말했다. \"채비를 갖추자.\"\n\n이와 동시에 그는 또 다른 비슷한 무기 하나에 의미심장하게 손가락을 얹었으니, 둘 다 그날 저녁 적들 가운데서 거둔 그의 용맹의 결실이었다.\n\n\"우리는 갈 것이오.\" 〈언카스〉가 말했다.\n\n\"어디로?\"\n\n\"〈거북〉족에게로. 그들은 내 할아버지들의 자손이오.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야,\" 척후가 〈영어〉로 말했으니 — 마음이 조금 딴 데 가 있을 때면 곧잘 쓰는 말이었다. \"같은 피가 네 핏줄에 흐르고 있는 게 분명하다만, 세월과 거리가 그 빛깔을 조금 바꿔 놓았지. 문간의 저 〈밍고〉 놈들은 어쩐다? 여섯이나 되는데, 이 성가쟁이는 없는 거나 마찬가지이니.\"\n\n\"〈휴런〉 놈들은 허풍쟁이요.\" 〈언카스〉가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그들의 '토템'은 큰사슴이건만, 그들은 달팽이처럼 기지. 〈델라웨어〉는 거북의 자손이로되, 그들은 사슴을 앞지르오.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 네 말에 진실이 있구나. 단숨에 내달리면 네가 그 종족 전부를 앞지를 테고, 이 마일을 곧장 내달리는 경주라면, 그 악당들 가운데 누구 하나가 저쪽 마을 소리가 들리는 데에 닿기도 전에 들어가서 숨을 다시 고르고 있으리라는 걸 의심치 않는다. 허나 백인의 재능은 다리보다는 팔에 더 깃들어 있지. 나로 말하자면, 〈휴런〉 놈의 머리통을 그 누구 못지않게 잘 부술 수 있다만, 경주가 되면 그 악당들이 내게는 벅찰 게야.\"\n\n이미 앞장설 채비로 문가에 다가가 있던 〈언카스〉가 이제 물러나, 다시 한번 오두막 안쪽에 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 제 생각에 너무 골몰한 나머지 그 움직임을 알아채지 못한 〈호크아이〉는, 동료에게라기보다 혼잣말에 가깝게 계속 지껄였다.\n\n\"결국,\" 그가 말했다. \"한 사람을 다른 이의 재능에 매여 결박해 두는 건 당치 않은 일이지. 그러니, 〈언카스〉, 너는 도약을 감행하는 편이 낫겠다. 나는 가죽을 다시 뒤집어쓰고, 부족한 속도 대신 꾀에 기댈 테니.\"\n\n젊은 〈모히칸〉은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 조용히 두 팔을 포갠 채, 오두막 벽을 떠받치는 수직 기둥 가운데 하나에 제 몸을 기댔다.\n\n\"이런,\" 척후가 그를 올려다보며 말했다. \"어찌 꾸물대느냐? 처음엔 그 악당들이 너를 뒤쫓을 테니, 내게는 시간이 넉넉할 게다.\"\n\n\"〈언카스〉는 남겠소.\" 차분한 대답이었다.\n\n\"무엇 때문에?\"\n\n\"제 아버지의 형제와 함께 싸우고, 〈델라웨어〉의 벗과 함께 죽기 위해서요.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야,\" 〈호크아이〉가 〈언카스〉의 손을 제 쇠 같은 손가락 사이에 꽉 쥐며 대꾸했다. \"네가 나를 버리고 갔더라면 〈모히칸〉보다는 〈밍고〉에 더 가까웠을 게다. 허나 젊음이란 으레 목숨을 아끼는 법이라, 그래도 한번 권해 보려 했던 거지. 자, 전쟁에서 순전한 용기로 안 되는 일은 꾀로 해내야 한다. 가죽을 뒤집어써라. 곰 노릇이라면 너도 나만큼은 거뜬히 해낼 테니까.\"\n\n이 점에서 둘의 능력에 관한 〈언카스〉의 속내가 어떠했든, 그 진중한 얼굴은 제가 더 낫다는 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않았다. 그는 말없이 재빨리 그 짐승의 거죽으로 제 몸을 감싸고는, 더 나이 많은 동료가 지시할 다른 움직임을 기다렸다.\n\n\"자, 친구,\" 〈호크아이〉가 〈데이비드〉에게 말했다. \"옷을 바꿔 입는 게 당신에게 큰 도움이 될 거요. 당신은 황야의 임시변통에 도통 익숙지 않으니 말이오. 자, 내 사냥 셔츠와 모자를 받고, 당신의 담요와 모자를 내게 주오. 그 책과 안경도, 그리고 그 피리도 내게 맡겨야 하오. 언젠가 형편이 나아져 다시 만나면, 거기에 넘치는 감사까지 얹어 모두 돌려 드리리다.\"\n\n〈데이비드〉는 거명된 여러 물건을 선뜻 내주었으니, 그가 이 교환에서 여러모로 분명한 이득을 보지 않았더라면 그 너그러움에 큰 공을 돌릴 만했다. 〈호크아이〉가 빌린 옷가지를 걸치는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그 잠시도 가만있지 않는 두 눈이 안경 뒤에 가려지고 머리에 삼각 비버 모자가 얹히자, 둘의 키가 별반 다르지 않았던 터라, 별빛 아래서라면 그는 능히 그 성가 교사로 통할 만했다. 이 채비가 끝나자마자, 척후는 〈데이비드〉를 돌아보며 작별의 지시를 일러 주었다.\n\n\"당신은 겁이 꽤 많은 편이오?\" 그가 어떤 처방을 감히 내리기 전에 사정 전체를 알맞게 파악하려는 셈으로 대뜸 물었다.\n\n\"내 일은 평화로운 것이고, 내 성품은, 외람되나 분명코 자비와 사랑에 크게 기울어 있소.\" 자신의 사내다움을 그토록 직설적으로 공격받자 다소 발끈하여 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"허나 내가 가장 큰 곤경에 처해서도 주님에 대한 믿음을 잊은 적 있다고 말할 수 있는 자는 아무도 없소.\"\n\n\"당신에게 가장 큰 위험은 그 미개인들이 자기들이 속았다는 것을 알아채는 바로 그 순간일 거요. 그때 머리통을 얻어맞지만 않는다면, 당신이 정신이 온전치 못한 자라는 점이 당신을 지켜 줄 테고, 그러면 잠자리에서 죽음을 맞으리라 넉넉히 기대할 만하지. 만일 남는다면, 여기 이 그늘에 앉아 〈언카스〉의 역을 떠맡고, 인디언들의 꾀가 그 속임수를 알아챌 때까지 견뎌야 하오. 그때가 되면, 이미 말했듯이, 당신의 시련의 때가 닥칠 거요. 그러니 당신이 골라 보시오 — 내달릴지, 아니면 여기 머물지.\"\n\n\"그렇다 해도,\" 〈데이비드〉가 단호히 말했다. \"나는 그 〈델라웨어〉의 자리에 남겠소. 그는 나를 위해 용감하고 너그럽게 싸워 주었으니, 그를 섬기는 일이라면 이것은 물론 이보다 더한 일도 감히 무릅쓰겠소.\"\n\n\"당신은 사내답게 말했고, 더 슬기로운 가르침을 받았더라면 더 나은 길로 인도되었을 사람처럼 말했소. 고개를 숙이고 다리를 오므리시오. 그 생김새가 너무 일찍 진실을 일러 줄지 모르니. 될 수 있는 한 오래 잠자코 있고, 입을 열어야 할 때면 갑작스레 그 외침 가운데 하나를 터뜨리는 게 현명할 거요. 그러면 인디언들에게 당신이 여느 사람만큼 온전히 책임질 수 있는 자가 아님을 일깨워 줄 테니. 하지만, 내가 바라고 또 믿건대 그렇지 않겠지만, 만일 그들이 당신의 머리 가죽을 벗긴다면, 〈언카스〉와 내가 그 일을 잊지 않고, 참된 전사이자 미더운 벗답게 반드시 복수하리라는 것을 믿어도 좋소.\"\n\n\"멈추시오!\" 그 약속과 함께 자신을 두고 떠나려는 것을 알아챈 〈데이비드〉가 말했다. \"나는 복수라는 가증스러운 원리를 가르치지 않으신 분의 보잘것없고 미천한 추종자요. 그러니 내가 쓰러지거든, 내 넋을 위해 제물을 찾지 말고, 도리어 나를 죽인 자들을 용서하시오. 그리고 그들을 조금이라도 기억하려거든, 그들의 마음이 깨이고 그들의 영원한 복락을 비는 기도 속에서나 기억하시오.\"\n\n척후는 머뭇거리며 깊이 생각에 잠긴 듯했다.\n\n\"거기엔 한 원리가 있소.\" 그가 말했다. \"숲의 법칙과는 다른 것이오. 허나 곰곰이 헤아려 보면 공정하고 고귀한 것이지.\" 그러고는, 아마도 그가 그토록 오래 떠나 있던 한 처지를 사무치게 그리며 내쉰 마지막 한숨에 가까웠을 무거운 한숨을 토하며, 그가 덧붙였다. \"피의 섞임이 없는 사람으로서 나 자신도 그리 실천하고 싶은 바요. 인디언을 동료 기독교도 대하듯 대하기란 늘 쉬운 일은 아니지만 말이오. 신의 가호가 있기를, 친구여. 영원을 눈앞에 두고 그 문제를 마땅히 헤아려 보면, 당신의 냄새 맡는 방향이 크게 그릇되지는 않았다고 진정 믿소. 비록 많은 것이 타고난 재능과 유혹의 힘에 달려 있긴 하지만.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 척후는 돌아와 〈데이비드〉의 손을 다정하게 잡고 흔들었다. 이 우정의 행위가 끝나자, 그는 곧장 오두막을 떠났으니, 그 짐승의 새로운 분신을 데리고서였다.\n\n〈호크아이〉가 〈휴런〉 사람들의 시야에 들어선 그 순간, 그는 〈데이비드〉의 그 뻣뻣한 자세로 제 큰 몸을 곧추세우고, 박자를 맞추는 시늉으로 팔을 쭉 내뻗고는, 그가 의도한 그 성가의 흉내를 시작했다. 이 미묘한 모험의 성공으로 보아 다행스럽게도, 그가 상대해야 할 자들의 귀는 감미로운 소리의 화음에 거의 단련되지 않은 것들이었으니, 그렇지 않았더라면 그 한심한 시도는 영락없이 들통났을 것이다. 미개인들의 어두운 무리 곁을 위험할 만큼 가까이 지나야 했고, 그들에게 다가갈수록 척후의 목소리는 점점 커졌다. 가장 가까운 지점에 이르렀을 때, 〈영어〉를 하는 그 〈휴런〉이 팔을 쑥 내밀어 그 성가 교사로 짐작되는 자를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉의 개!\" 그가 몸을 앞으로 기울이고 침침한 빛 속을 들여다보며 상대의 표정을 살피려 하면서 말했다. \"그자가 겁먹었나? 〈휴런〉 사람들이 그의 신음을 듣게 될까?\"\n\n어찌나 사납고 자연스러운 으르렁거림이 그 짐승에게서 흘러나왔던지, 그 젊은 인디언은 붙잡은 손을 놓고 옆으로 흠칫 물러섰으니, 마치 제 앞에서 뒹구는 것이 가짜가 아니라 진짜 곰이 아닌지 스스로 확인하려는 듯했다. 제 목소리가 그 교활한 적들에게 정체를 들킬까 두려워하던 〈호크아이〉는, 이 가로막힘을 반갑게 활용하여, 더 세련된 사회였더라면 아마 \"장엄한 절정\"이라 일컬어졌을 법한 음악적 표현을 새로이 한바탕 터뜨렸다. 그러나 실제 청중들 사이에서 그것은, 다만 정신이 온전치 못하다고 여겨지는 자들에게 그들이 결코 아끼지 않는 그 경의를 그에게 한층 더 받을 자격으로 작용했을 뿐이다. 그 작은 인디언 무리는 한 무리가 되어 물러나며, 자기들 짐작에 그 주술사와 그 영감 받은 조수를 그대로 지나가게 했다.\n\n오두막들을 지나며 떠맡은 그 위엄 있고 신중한 걸음걸이를 이어 가기란, 〈언카스〉와 척후에게 여간한 인내가 아니고는 어려운 일이었으니, 특히 호기심이 두려움을 이긴 나머지 그 감시자들이 주술의 효과를 목격하려 오두막으로 다가서는 것을 곧바로 알아챘기 때문이다. 〈데이비드〉가 조금이라도 분별없거나 조급하게 움직였다가는 그들의 정체가 드러날 판이었고, 척후의 안전을 확보하기 위해서는 시간이 반드시 필요했다. 후자가 영리한 처사라 여겨 계속 내지른 그 큰 소리는, 그들이 지나갈 때마다 여러 오두막 문가로 호기심 어린 구경꾼들을 잔뜩 끌어냈고, 한두 번은 거무스름한 전사 하나가 미신 때문인지 경계심 때문인지 그들의 앞길을 가로질러 나서기도 했다. 그러나 그들은 가로막히지 않았으니, 그 시각의 어둠과 그 시도의 대담함이 그들의 가장 든든한 벗임이 드러났다.\n\n이 모험가들은 마을을 벗어나, 이제 숲의 은신처를 향해 빠르게 다가가고 있었는데, 그때 〈언카스〉가 갇혀 있던 오두막에서 크고 긴 외침이 솟구쳐 올랐다. 〈모히칸〉은 벌떡 일어서며 그 텁수룩한 거죽을 흔들었으니, 마치 그가 흉내 내던 짐승이 어떤 필사적인 발버둥을 치려는 듯했다.\n\n\"멈춰!\" 척후가 제 벗의 어깨를 움켜잡으며 말했다. \"또 한 번 소리치게 두어라! 놀라움일 뿐이니까.\"\n\n그가 지체할 까닭은 없었으니, 다음 순간 한바탕 외침이 바깥 공기를 가득 메우며 마을 전역으로 번져 갔기 때문이다. 〈언카스〉는 그 가죽을 벗어 던지고, 본래의 그 아름다운 균형의 모습으로 걸어 나왔다. 〈호크아이〉가 그의 어깨를 가볍게 두드리고는 앞으로 미끄러져 나아갔다.\n\n\"이제 그 악귀들이 우리 냄새를 좇아 보라지!\" 척후가, 덤불 아래에서 두 자루 소총을 거기 딸린 온갖 장구와 함께 끌어내며, 또 〈언카스〉에게 그의 무기를 건네면서 〈사슴 잡이〉를 휘둘러 보이며 말했다. \"적어도 둘은 제 죽음을 그 냄새에서 찾게 될 게다.\"\n\n그러고는, 사냥감을 노릴 채비를 갖춘 사냥꾼들처럼 총을 낮게 겨눠 든 채, 그들은 앞으로 내달았고, 이내 숲의 음울한 어둠 속에 파묻혔다.\n\n카이사르께서 '이것을 행하라' 하시면, 그대로 이루어지나이다.\"\n\n율리우스 카이사르\n\n이미 본 바와 같이, 〈언카스〉의 감옥 주위를 어슬렁대던 미개인들의 조바심이 마침내 마술사의 입김에 대한 두려움을 이겨 냈다. 그들은 조심스레, 두근거리는 가슴으로, 모닥불의 희미한 빛이 새어 나오는 틈새로 살금살금 다가갔다. 몇 분 동안 그들은 〈데이비드〉의 형상을 자기네 포로의 것으로 잘못 알았으나, 〈호크아이〉가 예견했던 바로 그 우연한 사태가 벌어졌다. 그 긴 몸의 양 끝을 그토록 가까이 모은 채로 있기에 지친 가수는, 차츰 아랫다리를 뻗는 것을 허락하다가, 마침내 그 흉하게 생긴 발 한쪽이 정말로 모닥불의 잔불에 닿아 그것을 옆으로 밀어내고 말았다. 처음에 〈휴런족〉은 그 〈델라웨어족〉이 마법으로 이렇게 흉하게 변한 줄로만 알았다. 그러나 〈데이비드〉가 자신이 관찰당하는 줄도 모르고 고개를 돌려, 그 포로의 거만한 이목구비 대신 그 순박하고 온화한 얼굴을 드러냈을 때에는, 아무리 잘 속는 원주민이라 해도 더는 의심할 수 없을 지경이었다. 그들은 한꺼번에 오두막으로 달려들어, 별다른 예의도 차리지 않고 포로에게 손을 얹고는, 곧장 그 속임수를 알아차렸다. 그러자 도망자들이 처음에 들었던 그 외침이 터져 나왔다. 그 뒤를 가장 광적이고 분노에 찬 복수의 표시가 이었다. 〈데이비드〉는 벗들의 퇴로를 가려 주겠다는 결심에는 굳건했으나, 자신의 마지막 시각이 왔음을 믿지 않을 수 없었다. 책과 피리를 빼앗긴 그는, 이런 주제에서는 좀처럼 자신을 저버리지 않던 기억력에 의지할 수밖에 없었으니, 크고도 격정 어린 가락을 터뜨리며 장송 성가의 첫 절을 부름으로써 저세상으로 건너가는 길을 누그러뜨리고자 했다. 인디언들은 때마침 그의 결함을 떠올렸고, 트인 바깥으로 달려 나가 앞서 묘사한 방식으로 마을을 깨워 일으켰다.\n\n원주민 전사는 잠잘 때와 마찬가지로, 그 어떤 방어 장구의 보호도 없이 싸운다. 그러므로 경보 소리가 채 다 울리기도 전에, 이백 명이 일어나 전투든 추격이든 어느 쪽이 요구되든 채비를 갖췄다. 탈주는 곧 알려졌고, 부족 전체가 한 덩어리로 평의회 오두막 주위에 몰려들어, 추장들의 지시를 애타게 기다렸다. 그들의 지혜에 이토록 갑작스러운 요구가 닥친 마당에, 교활한 〈마구아〉의 존재가 필요해지지 않을 리 없었다. 그의 이름이 입에 올랐고, 모두가 그가 나타나지 않는 것을 의아해하며 주위를 둘러보았다. 그리하여 그의 오두막으로 전령이 보내져, 그의 출석을 요청했다.\n\n그러는 사이, 젊은이들 가운데 가장 날쌔고 가장 신중한 자 몇이 명을 받아, 숲의 엄폐를 빌려 빈터를 한 바퀴 돌며, 의심스러운 이웃인 〈델라웨어족〉이 어떤 해코지도 꾀하지 않는지를 확인했다. 여인들과 아이들은 이리저리 뛰어다녔으니, 요컨대 진영 전체가 또 한 차례 거칠고 야만적인 혼란의 장면을 연출했다. 그러나 차츰 이런 무질서의 징후는 잦아들었고, 몇 분도 채 못 되어 가장 나이 많고 가장 명망 높은 추장들이 오두막에 모여 엄숙한 상의에 들어갔다.\n\n여러 목소리의 떠들썩함이 곧 한 무리가 다가오고 있음을 알렸으니, 그들에게서 이 새로운 기습의 수수께끼를 풀어 줄 어떤 소식을 들을 수 있으리라 기대할 만했다. 바깥의 군중이 길을 비키자, 전사 몇이 그 자리로 들어서며, 척후가 그토록 오래 결박해 둔 그 가엾은 마술사를 데리고 왔다.\n\n이 사내가 〈휴런족〉 사이에서 매우 엇갈리는 평가를 받고 있었음에도 — 더러는 그의 능력을 굳게 믿었고, 더러는 그를 사기꾼으로 여겼다 — 이제 모두가 그의 말에 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 귀를 기울였다. 그의 짧은 이야기가 끝나자, 병든 여인의 아비가 앞으로 나서서, 몇 마디 간결한 표현으로, 이번에는 자기가 아는 바를 들려주었다. 이 두 이야기는 그 후의 심문에 올바른 방향을 잡아 주었으니, 그 심문은 이제 미개인 특유의 교활함으로 이루어졌다.\n\n뒤죽박죽 무질서한 무리를 이루어 동굴로 몰려가는 대신, 추장들 가운데 가장 지혜롭고 가장 굳센 열 명이 추려져 조사를 수행하게 되었다. 한시도 허비할 수 없었으므로, 그 선택이 이루어진 즉시 임명된 자들이 한 덩어리로 일어나, 한마디 말도 없이 그곳을 떠났다. 입구에 이르자 앞장선 젊은이들이 어른들에게 길을 양보했고, 일행 전체가 낮고 어두운 통로를 따라, 공공의 이익을 위해 한 몸을 바칠 각오가 된 전사다운 굳건함으로 나아갔으나, 그와 동시에 자신들이 맞서려는 그 힘의 정체를 속으로는 의심하고 있었다.\n\n동굴의 바깥 방은 고요하고 음산했다. 여인은 늘 있던 자리에, 늘 있던 자세로 누워 있었으니, 그 자리에 있던 자들 가운데는 그녀가 이른바 \"백인의 의술\"에 들려 숲으로 옮겨지는 것을 보았노라 단언하는 이들도 있었건만. 아비가 들려준 이야기와 이토록 직접적이고 명백히 어긋나는 사실에, 모든 눈이 그에게로 쏠렸다. 말없는 비난에 마음이 상하고, 이토록 설명할 수 없는 정황에 속으로 동요한 추장은, 침상 곁으로 나아가 몸을 굽혀, 그 얼굴이 실재하는지 의심하기라도 하듯, 못 믿겠다는 눈길을 던졌다. 그의 딸은 죽어 있었다.\n\n한순간 천성의 그르침 없는 감정이 우세하여, 늙은 전사는 슬픔에 잠겨 두 눈을 가렸다. 이윽고 평정을 되찾은 그는 동료들을 향해 돌아서서, 시신을 가리키며 제 부족의 말로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"내 젊은이의 아내가 우리를 떠났소! 위대한 정령께서 그 자녀들에게 노하셨소.\"\n\n이 애통한 소식은 엄숙한 침묵 속에 받아들여졌다. 잠시 멈춤이 있은 뒤, 나이 든 인디언 하나가 막 입을 떼려는데, 검게 보이는 물체 하나가 옆방에서 굴러 나와 그들이 서 있는 방 한복판으로 들어오는 것이 보였다. 자기들이 상대해야 할 존재의 정체를 알지 못한 일행 전체가 조금 물러나, 그 물체가 빛 쪽으로 정면을 향하고 몸을 곧추세워, 일그러졌으나 여전히 사납고 음울한 〈마구아〉의 이목구비를 드러낼 때까지, 경이로움에 차 바라보았다. 이 발견에 이어 모두가 일제히 놀라움의 탄성을 터뜨렸다.\n\n그러나 추장의 진짜 처지가 곧 이해되자, 잘 드는 칼 몇 자루가 나타나, 그의 사지와 혀가 이내 풀려났다. 〈휴런족〉은 일어서서, 굴을 나서는 사자처럼 제 몸을 떨쳐 흔들었다. 한마디 말도 새어 나오지 않았으나, 그의 손은 칼자루를 발작하듯 만지작거렸고, 그의 험상궂은 눈은 마치 복수의 첫 폭발에 어울리는 표적을 찾기라도 하듯 일행 전체를 훑었다.\n\n이런 순간에 〈언카스〉와 척후가, 심지어 〈데이비드〉마저도 모두 그의 팔이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었던 것은 다행이었다. 분명코, 그를 거의 숨 막히게 하던 그 사나운 성정의 충동을 거슬러, 그 어떤 정교한 잔혹함도 그들의 죽음을 그때 미루어 놓지 못했을 터이니. 어디를 둘러보아도 벗으로 아는 얼굴들과 마주친 미개인은 쇠줄을 가는 듯 이를 갈며, 분풀이할 제물이 없어 그 격정을 삼켰다. 이 분노의 표출은 그 자리에 있던 모두에게 포착되었으니, 이미 광기에 가깝도록 상해 버린 그 성미를 더 자극할까 우려하여, 또 한마디 말이 나오기까지 몇 분이 흐르도록 그냥 두었다. 그러나 적당한 시간이 지났을 때, 일행 가운데 가장 나이 많은 자가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"내 벗이 적을 찾아냈구려.\" 그가 말했다. \"그자가 가까이 있어, 〈휴런족〉이 복수할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그 〈델라웨어족〉을 죽게 하라!\" 〈마구아〉가 우레 같은 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n또 한 차례 길고 의미심장한 침묵이 지켜졌고, 앞서처럼 마땅한 신중함으로, 같은 자가 그것을 깨뜨렸다.\n\n\"그 〈모히칸족〉은 발이 빠르고, 멀리 뛰오.\" 그가 말했다. \"허나 내 젊은이들이 그자의 자취를 좇고 있소.\"\n\n\"그자가 갔단 말이오?\" 〈마구아〉가 어찌나 깊고 목구멍에서 울리는 어조로 물었던지, 그 소리는 가슴속 가장 깊은 데서 나오는 듯했다.\n\n\"악령 하나가 우리 가운데 있었고, 그 〈델라웨어족〉이 우리 눈을 멀게 했소.\"\n\n\"악령이라!\" 상대가 비웃듯 되뇌었다. \"그토록 많은 〈휴런족〉의 목숨을 앗은 그 정령이렷다. '굴러떨어지는 강'에서 내 젊은이들을 죽이고, '치유의 샘'에서 그들의 머리 가죽을 벗기고, 이제 〈여우 르나르 쉬브틸〉의 두 팔을 묶은 그 정령 말이오!\"\n\n\"내 벗은 누구를 두고 하는 말이오?\"\n\n\"창백한 살갗 아래 〈휴런족〉의 심장과 교활함을 품은 그 개를 두고 하는 말이오 — 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉.\"\n\n이토록 무시무시한 이름을 입에 올리자, 듣는 이들 사이에 여느 때와 같은 효과가 일었다. 그러나 곰곰이 새겨 볼 틈이 주어지고, 그 가공할 대담한 적이 자기네 진영 한복판에까지 들어와 해코지를 했다는 것을 전사들이 떠올리자, 경이로움 자리에 두려운 분노가 들어섰고, 〈마구아〉의 가슴속에서 방금 막 들끓던 그 모든 사나운 격정이 별안간 그의 동료들에게로 옮겨 갔다. 그들 가운데 더러는 분에 못 이겨 이를 갈았고, 더러는 비명으로 감정을 토했으며, 또 더러는 원한의 대상이 제 손찌검에 시달리기라도 하듯 미친 듯이 허공을 후려쳤다. 그러나 이 갑작스러운 성정의 폭발은, 그들이 정지의 순간에 가장 즐겨 부리던 그 고요하고 음울한 자제 속으로 이내 가라앉았다.\n\n이번에는 〈마구아〉가 곰곰이 새겨 볼 여유를 찾았으니, 이제 태도를 바꾸어, 이토록 엄중한 사안에 걸맞은 위엄으로 생각하고 행동할 줄 아는 자의 풍모를 갖추었다.\n\n\"내 백성에게로 갑시다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그들이 우리를 기다리고 있소.\"\n\n그의 동료들은 말없이 동의했고, 미개한 일행 전체가 동굴을 떠나 평의회 오두막으로 돌아왔다. 그들이 자리에 앉자, 모든 눈이 〈마구아〉에게로 쏠렸으니, 그는 그러한 표시에서, 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려줄 임무를 모두의 합의로 자신에게 맡겼음을 깨달았다. 그는 일어서서, 거짓도 숨김도 없이 자기 이야기를 들려주었다. 〈던컨〉과 〈호크아이〉 둘이 함께 부린 속임수 전부가 물론 낱낱이 드러났으니, 부족 가운데 가장 미신에 빠진 자라 해도 이제 더는 그 사건들의 성격에 의심을 붙일 여지가 없었다. 그들이 모욕적으로, 수치스럽게, 치욕스럽게 속아 넘어갔다는 것은 너무도 분명했다. 그가 이야기를 끝내고 다시 자리에 앉자, 모인 부족은 — 그의 청중은 실상 일행의 전투원 전부를 포함하고 있었으니 — 적들의 대담함과 그 성공에 똑같이 경악한 자들처럼 서로를 마주 보며 앉아 있었다. 그러나 다음으로 떠오른 고민은 복수의 수단과 기회였다.\n\n도망자들의 자취를 좇아 추격대가 더 보내졌고, 이어 추장들은 진지하게 상의에 몰두했다. 나이 든 전사들이 잇달아 갖가지 다른 방책을 내놓았으니, 그 모든 것에 〈마구아〉는 말없이 공손한 청자로 임했다. 이 교활한 미개인은 책략과 자제력을 되찾았고, 이제 여느 때의 신중함과 솜씨로 제 목적을 향해 나아갔다. 말하고자 하는 자가 저마다 제 의견을 다 내놓은 뒤에야, 비로소 그는 제 견해를 펼칠 채비를 했다. 그의 견해는 한층 무게를 더하여 주어졌으니, 척후 몇이 이미 돌아와, 적들의 자취가 이웃한 진영으로 — 의심스러운 동맹인 〈델라웨어족〉의 진영으로 — 안전을 구하러 들어갔음이 의심할 여지 없을 만큼 멀리까지 추적되었음을 보고했기 때문이다. 이 중요한 정보를 손에 쥔 이점을 안고, 추장은 신중하게 동료들 앞에 제 계획을 펼쳐 놓았으니, 그의 웅변과 교활함으로 미루어 짐작할 수 있듯, 반대하는 목소리 하나 없이 채택되었다. 그 계획은 견해로 보나 동기로 보나, 간추리면 다음과 같았다.\n\n좀처럼 어기는 법이 없는 관례에 따라, 자매가 〈휴런족〉 마을에 닿자마자 떼어 놓였다는 것은 이미 언급한 바 있다. 〈마구아〉는 일찍이 〈앨리스〉의 신병을 붙들고 있는 것이 〈코라〉에게 가장 효과적인 견제가 됨을 간파했다. 그리하여 그들이 갈라설 때, 그는 전자를 제 손이 닿는 데 두고, 자신이 가장 귀히 여기는 쪽은 동맹들의 관리에 맡겼다. 이 조처는 그저 일시적인 것으로 이해되었으며, 인디언 정책의 변치 않는 규칙을 따르는 동시에 이웃들의 비위를 맞추려는 뜻에서 이루어진 것이었다.\n\n미개인의 마음속에서 좀처럼 잠들지 않는 그 복수심의 충동에 끊임없이 내몰리면서도, 추장은 자신의 더 항구적인 개인적 이익에도 주의를 게을리하지 않았다. 젊은 시절에 저지른 어리석음과 불충은, 그가 옛 백성의 신뢰를 온전히 다시 누리게 되기까지 길고도 고통스러운 속죄로써 씻어야 할 것이었으니, 신뢰가 없이는 인디언 부족 안에서 그 어떤 권위도 있을 수 없었다. 이토록 미묘하고 험난한 처지에서, 이 교활한 원주민은 제 영향력을 키울 수단이라면 무엇 하나 소홀히 하지 않았으니, 그 가장 행복한 방책 가운데 하나는 강력하고 위험한 이웃들의 호의를 가꾸어 거둔 성공이었다. 그의 시도가 거둔 결과는 그 정책이 기대한 바를 죄다 채웠으니, 인간으로 하여금 제 재능을 꼭 남들이 인정하는 만큼만 귀히 여기게 하는 그 천성의 지배적 원리에서 〈휴런족〉 또한 조금도 예외가 아니었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 전체의 고려를 위해 이렇게 겉치레로 희생을 치르면서도, 〈마구아〉는 결코 제 개인적 동기를 놓치지 않았다. 후자는 그의 모든 포로를 그의 통제 밖에 놓아 버린 뜻밖의 사태로 좌절되었으니, 그는 이제 얼마 전까지만 해도 제 편에서 은혜를 베푸는 것이 정책이던 자들에게 도리어 호의를 간청해야 하는 처지로 내몰린 자신을 발견했다.\n\n추장들 가운데 몇은 〈델라웨어족〉을 기습할 깊고 음험한 계략을 내놓았으니, 그들의 진영을 손에 넣음으로써 한 번의 타격으로 포로들을 되찾자는 것이었다. 모두가 한목소리로, 자기네 명예와 이익, 그리고 죽은 동포들의 안식과 행복이, 복수의 제단에 어서 몇몇 제물을 바칠 것을 준엄하게 요구한다는 데 동의했기 때문이다. 그러나 시도하기에 이토록 위험하고 그 결말이 이토록 불확실한 계획들을, 〈마구아〉는 별 어려움 없이 무산시켰다. 그는 여느 때의 솜씨로 그것들의 위험성과 허점을 드러내 보였고, 반대 의견의 형태를 띤 온갖 장애물을 모조리 치워 버린 다음에야, 비로소 제 구상을 내놓는 모험에 나섰다.\n\n그는 청중의 자존심을 추켜세우는 것으로 말문을 열었으니, 이는 결코 실패하는 법이 없는 주의를 끄는 방법이었다. 〈휴런족〉이 모욕을 응징하면서 자신들의 용기와 무용을 떨쳐 보인 갖가지 경우를 일일이 열거한 뒤, 그는 곁가지로 빠져 지혜의 미덕을 드높이 찬양했다. 그는 그 자질이야말로 비버와 여느 짐승 사이의, 짐승과 인간 사이의, 그리고 마침내 특히 〈휴런족〉과 나머지 인류 사이의 큰 차이를 이루는 점이라 그려 보였다. 분별의 속성을 흡족하리만치 추켜올린 뒤, 그는 그 쓰임이 어떤 식으로 자기 부족의 지금 처지에 들어맞는지를 펼쳐 보이는 일에 나섰다. 한편으로는, 그가 말하기를, 그들의 위대한 창백한 아버지인 〈캐나다〉 총독이 있으니, 그는 자녀들의 토마호크가 그토록 붉어진 이래로 모진 눈으로 그 자녀들을 바라보아 왔다. 다른 한편으로는, 자기들만큼이나 수가 많고, 다른 말을 쓰며, 다른 이해관계를 지니고, 자기들을 사랑하지 않으며, 어떤 구실이든 잡아 자기들을 그 위대한 백인 추장의 눈 밖에 나게 하기를 기꺼워할 한 민족이 있다. 이어 그는 그들의 궁핍을, 지난날의 봉사에 대해 마땅히 기대할 권리가 있는 선물들을, 자기네 본래의 사냥터와 고향 마을에서 멀리 떨어져 있음을, 그리고 이토록 위태로운 정황에서 본능을 덜 따르고 신중함을 더 따라야 할 필요를 이야기했다. 노인들은 그의 절제를 칭송하는 반면, 가장 사납고 가장 명망 높은 전사들 가운데 많은 이가 이런 책략 어린 계획들을 험상궂은 낯빛으로 듣고 있음을 알아챘을 때, 그는 교활하게도 그들이 가장 사랑하는 화제로 그들을 다시 이끌었다. 그는 그들의 지혜가 맺을 열매를 거리낌 없이 말했으니, 그것이 적들에 대한 완전하고도 최종적인 승리가 되리라고 대담하게 단언했다. 그는 심지어, 마땅한 신중함을 곁들이면 그 성공이 자기들이 미워할 만한 까닭이 있는 모든 자의 파멸까지 아우르는 식으로 확장될 수 있으리라고 은근히 어둡게 내비쳤다. 요컨대, 그는 호전적인 것과 책략적인 것을, 명백한 것과 모호한 것을 어찌나 잘 뒤섞었던지, 양편의 성향을 모두 추켜세우고 저마다에게 희망의 거리를 남겨 주면서도, 어느 쪽도 그의 의도를 똑똑히 알아들었노라 말할 수 없게 했다.\n\n이런 상태를 빚어낼 수 있는 웅변가나 정치가는, 후세에서 어떤 대접을 받든, 당대인들 사이에서는 흔히 인기를 누린다. 모두가 입에 올린 것보다 더 많은 것이 뜻해졌음을 알아챘고, 저마다 그 숨은 뜻이 바로 제 능력으로 이해할 수 있는 것이거나 제 소망이 미리 점치게 한 그런 것이라고 믿었다.\n\n이토록 흡족한 상황에서 〈마구아〉의 수완이 이긴 것은 놀랄 일이 아니다. 부족은 신중하게 행동하기로 합의했고, 한목소리로 이 일 전체의 지휘를 그토록 지혜롭고 알아듣기 쉬운 방책을 내놓은 추장의 다스림에 맡겼다.\n\n이제 〈마구아〉는 그 모든 교활함과 진취성의 큰 목적 하나를 이루었다. 제 백성의 호의에서 잃었던 입지를 온전히 되찾았을 뿐 아니라, 심지어 사태의 수장 자리에까지 오른 자신을 발견했다. 그는 사실상 그들의 통치자였으니, 제 인기를 유지할 수 있는 한, 그 어떤 군주도 그보다 더 전제적일 수 없었으며, 더구나 부족이 적지에 머무는 동안에는 더욱 그러했다. 그리하여 상의하는 듯한 겉모습을 벗어 던지고, 그는 제 직위의 위엄을 떠받치는 데 필요한 엄중한 권위의 풍모를 갖추었다.\n\n여러 방향으로 소식을 알아 오라 전령이 보내졌고, 첩자들은 〈델라웨어족〉의 진영에 다가가 그 동태를 살피라는 명을 받았으며, 전사들은 곧 그 소임이 필요해지리라는 언질과 함께 제 오두막으로 물러가게 되었고, 여인들과 아이들은 침묵을 지키는 것이 그들의 본분이라는 경고와 함께 물러가라는 명을 받았다. 이런 여러 조처가 이루어지자, 〈마구아〉는 마을을 가로질러 다니며, 제 존재가 그 사람의 비위를 맞추리라 여겨지는 곳마다 여기저기 멈추어 방문을 했다. 그는 제 벗들의 신뢰를 굳혔고, 흔들리는 자들을 다잡았으며, 모두를 흡족하게 했다. 그런 다음 자기 오두막을 찾아갔다. 〈휴런족〉 추장이 제 백성 사이에서 쫓겨났을 때 버리고 떠난 아내는 죽고 없었다. 자식은 하나도 없었으니, 이제 그는 그 어떤 동무도 없이 한 오두막을 차지하고 있었다. 그것은 실은 〈데이비드〉가 발견되었던 바로 그 허물어지고 외딴 건물이었으니, 그는 둘이 마주치던 그 몇 안 되는 경우에, 거만한 우월감에서 비롯된 경멸 어린 무관심으로 〈데이비드〉를 제 앞에 두는 것을 너그러이 참아 주었다.\n\n그리하여 정책의 노고가 끝나자, 〈마구아〉는 이곳으로 물러났다. 그러나 다른 이들이 잠든 동안, 그는 휴식을 알지도, 구하지도 않았다. 만약 새로 선출된 이 추장의 거동을 지켜볼 만큼 호기심 많은 자가 하나라도 있었다면, 그가 물러난 그 시각부터 전사들을 다시 모으기로 정해 둔 때까지, 오두막 한구석에 앉아 제 앞날의 계획을 곰곰이 궁리하는 모습을 보았을 것이다. 이따금 바람이 오두막 틈새로 새어 들었고, 모닥불의 잔불 위에서 너울대는 낮은 불꽃이 그 음울한 은둔자의 몸 위로 흔들리는 빛을 던졌다. 그런 순간에는, 이 거무스름한 미개인을, 제 멋대로 그려 낸 억울함을 곱씹으며 악행을 꾸미는 어둠의 군주로 상상하기란 그리 어렵지 않았을 것이다.\n\n그러나 날이 밝기 한참 전에, 전사들이 하나둘 〈마구아〉의 외딴 오두막으로 들어와, 마침내 스무 명에 이르도록 모여들었다. 저마다 소총을 지녔고, 그 밖의 온갖 전쟁 장구를 갖췄으나, 칠한 채색은 한결같이 평화로운 것이었다. 이 사나운 몰골의 존재들이 들어오는 것은 주목받지 못했으니, 더러는 그 자리의 그늘 속에 자리를 잡고, 더러는 미동도 없는 조각상처럼 서서, 지정된 무리 전체가 다 모일 때까지 기다렸다.\n\n이윽고 〈마구아〉가 일어나 출발 신호를 보내며, 스스로 앞장서 행진했다. 그들은 외줄로, \"인디언 줄\"이라는 독특한 이름을 얻은 그 잘 알려진 대형으로 우두머리를 뒤따랐다. 마음을 들끓게 하는 전쟁의 일에 나선 여느 사내들과 달리, 그들은 거들먹거리지도 눈에 띄지도 않게 제 진영에서 몰래 빠져나갔으니, 무모한 만용의 위업으로 덧없는 명성을 좇는 전사들이라기보다는 미끄러지듯 나아가는 한 무리의 유령에 가까웠다.\n\n〈델라웨어족〉의 진영으로 곧장 이어지는 길을 잡는 대신, 〈마구아〉는 일행을 이끌고 한동안 시냇물의 굽이를 따라 내려가, 비버들의 작은 인공 호수를 끼고 나아갔다. 이 영리하고 부지런한 짐승들이 만들어 놓은 빈터로 그들이 들어설 무렵 날이 밝기 시작했다. 옛 차림을 다시 갖춘 〈마구아〉는 제 망토를 이루는 무두질한 가죽 위에 여우의 윤곽을 지니고 있었으나, 일행 가운데 한 추장은 비버를 제 특유의 상징, 곧 \"토템\"으로 지니고 있었다. 만약 이 사내가 자기와 친족이라 여기는 이토록 강력한 무리를 지나치면서 어떤 경의의 표시도 베풀지 않았다면, 그것은 일종의 불경이 되었을 터였다. 그리하여 그는 걸음을 멈추고, 마치 더 지각 있는 존재들에게 말을 건네듯 다정하고 정겨운 말을 건넸다. 그는 그 짐승들을 제 사촌이라 부르며, 그토록 많은 탐욕스러운 장사꾼들이 인디언들을 부추겨 그들의 목숨을 앗으려 하는 와중에도 그들이 무사히 남아 있는 것은 자신의 보호하는 영향력 덕분임을 일깨워 주었다. 그는 앞으로도 호의를 베풀기를 약속했고, 고마워할 것을 당부했다. 그런 다음, 자신이 몸소 나선 이번 원정을 이야기했으며, 충분한 조심성과 에두름을 곁들이긴 했으나, 그들이 그토록 이름난 그 지혜의 일부를 그들의 친척에게 나누어 주는 것이 마땅하다는 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n이 기이한 연설이 펼쳐지는 동안, 그 화자의 동료들은 마치 다들 똑같이 그 적절함에 감명받기라도 한 것처럼, 그의 말에 엄숙하고도 진지하게 귀를 기울였다. 한두 차례 검은 물체들이 수면으로 떠오르는 것이 보였고, 〈휴런족〉은 제 말이 헛되이 베풀어진 것이 아니라 여겨 흡족함을 드러냈다. 그가 막 연설을 끝냈을 때, 큰 비버 한 마리의 머리가 한 오두막의 문에서 쑥 내밀어졌으니, 그 흙벽은 몹시 상해 있었고, 일행은 그 위치로 미루어 그 안에 아무도 살지 않으리라 믿어 온 터였다. 이토록 비상한 신뢰의 표시를 그 웅변가는 더없이 길한 징조로 받아들였으니, 그 짐승이 다소 황급히 물러갔음에도, 그는 감사와 칭송을 아낌없이 쏟아 냈다.\n\n〈마구아〉가 그 전사의 가족애를 흡족하게 하느라 충분한 시간을 허비했다고 여겼을 때, 그는 다시 출발 신호를 보냈다. 인디언들이 한 덩어리로, 여느 보통 사람의 귀에는 들리지 않았을 걸음으로 멀어져 가자, 바로 그 점잖아 보이는 비버가 다시 한번 그 은신처에서 머리를 내미는 모험을 감행했다. 만약 〈휴런족〉 가운데 누구라도 뒤를 돌아보았다면, 그 짐승이 그들의 거동을 자칫 이성으로 오인하기 쉬운 관심과 영민함으로 지켜보고 있는 것을 보았을 것이다. 사실, 그 네발짐승의 행태가 어찌나 뚜렷하고 알아듣기 쉬웠던지, 아무리 노련한 관찰자라 해도 그 행동을 도무지 설명할 길이 없었을 터였다. 일행이 숲으로 들어선 그 순간까지는. 그때 그 짐승이 통째로 오두막에서 나와, 그 동작으로 털가죽 가면을 벗으며 〈칭가치국〉의 엄숙한 이목구비를 드러냄으로써, 모든 것이 비로소 설명되었을 것이다.\n* 인디언 줄: 한 사람씩 외줄로 늘어서서 나아가는 인디언 특유의 행군 대형을 가리킨다.\n\n잠시면 됩니다, 부탁이오. 보시다시피 나는 지금 분주한 때라.\n\n「헛소동」\n\n이미 여러 차례 언급된 〈델라웨어〉족, 아니 그 절반의 부족은, 지금 야영지가 〈휴런〉족의 임시 마을과 무척이나 가까운 곳에 자리하고 있었는데, 그 전사의 수가 후자의 부족과 거의 맞먹을 만했다. 이웃과 마찬가지로 그들 역시 〈몽칼름〉을 따라 〈영국〉 왕실의 영토로 들어와, 〈모호크〉족의 사냥터에 무겁고도 심각한 침략을 거듭하고 있었다. 다만 그들은, 원주민들 사이에 흔한 그 알 수 없는 신중함으로, 정작 가장 절실히 필요한 순간에 제 도움을 거두는 것이 옳다고 여겼다. 프랑스군은 동맹의 이 뜻밖의 이탈을 여러 가지로 해명했다. 그러나 가장 널리 퍼진 견해는, 한때 그들을 군사적 보호를 위해 〈여섯 부족〉에게 의존하게 만들었던 오랜 조약에 대한 외경심이 작용하여, 이제는 옛 주인과 맞서기를 꺼리게 했다는 것이었다. 부족 자체로 말하자면, 〈몽칼름〉에게 그의 사절을 통해 인디언다운 간결함으로, 자기네 손도끼가 무뎌졌으니 그것을 벼리려면 시간이 필요하다고 알리는 것으로 만족했다. 〈캐나다〉의 노련한 사령관은, 잘못 판단된 어떤 가혹한 처사로 그를 공공연한 적으로 돌리느니, 차라리 소극적인 벗을 너그러이 받아들이는 편이 더 현명하다고 여겼다.\n\n〈마구아〉가 앞서 묘사한 방식으로 그 말없는 무리를 비버들의 정착지에서 숲으로 이끌던 그날 아침, 해는 〈델라웨어〉 야영지 위로 떠올랐으니, 마치 한낮의 온갖 일상적 일에 분주히 종사하는 활기찬 사람들 위로 느닷없이 솟아오른 듯했다. 여인들은 오두막에서 오두막으로 뛰어다녔는데, 더러는 아침 식사를 준비하는 데 매달렸고, 몇몇은 제 습관에 필요한 안락을 구하는 데 골똘했으나, 더 많은 이들은 걸음을 멈추고 벗들과 황급한 귓속말을 주고받았다. 전사들은 무리 지어 빈둥거리며, 이야기를 나누기보다는 사색에 잠겼고, 몇 마디 말을 입에 올릴 때에는 제 의견을 깊이 저울질하는 사람들처럼 말했다. 사냥 도구들이 오두막들 사이에 넉넉히 보였으나, 떠나는 이는 아무도 없었다. 여기저기서 전사 한 명이 제 무기를 살피고 있었으니, 숲의 짐승 말고는 마주칠 적이 없으리라 예상될 때면 좀처럼 베풀지 않는 주의를 기울이고 있었다. 그리고 이따금, 무리 전체의 눈길이 마을 한복판에 자리한 크고 고요한 오두막을 향해 일제히 돌려졌으니, 마치 그 안에 그들 공통의 생각거리가 담겨 있는 듯했다.\n\n이 광경이 펼쳐지는 동안, 마을의 평지를 이루는 바위 단(壇)의 가장 먼 끝에 한 사내가 불쑥 나타났다. 그는 무장을 하지 않았고, 그 칠한 무늬는 준엄한 얼굴의 타고난 엄격함을 더하기보다는 누그러뜨리는 쪽이었다. 〈델라웨어〉족의 눈에 온전히 들어오자 그는 걸음을 멈추고, 한 팔을 하늘로 치켜올렸다가 인상적으로 가슴 위로 떨어뜨리며 우호의 몸짓을 지었다. 마을 사람들은 나직한 환영의 웅성거림으로 그 인사에 답했고, 비슷한 우정의 표시로 그에게 다가오라고 권했다. 이 확약에 힘을 얻어, 그 어두운 형상은 잠시 서 있던 천연 바위 단의 가장자리를 떠났으니, 그곳에서 그는 발그레한 아침 하늘을 배경으로 또렷한 윤곽을 그리고 서 있었으며, 오두막들의 한가운데로 위엄 있게 나아갔다. 그가 다가오는 동안 들리는 것이라곤, 그의 팔과 목을 잔뜩 두른 가벼운 은 장식이 짤랑이는 소리와, 사슴 가죽 모카신 가장자리를 두른 작은 방울들의 딸랑이는 소리뿐이었다. 그는 나아가며 지나치는 사내들에게 정중한 인사의 몸짓을 여러 번 보냈으나, 여인들에게는 눈길조차 주지 않았으니, 지금의 이 일에서 그들의 호의 따위는 조금도 중요치 않다고 여기는 사람 같았다. 그 공통의 거만한 태도로 보아 주요 추장들이 모인 것이 분명한 무리에 이르자, 낯선 이는 걸음을 멈추었고, 그제야 〈델라웨어〉족은 자기네 앞에 선 그 활기차고 꼿꼿한 형상이 바로 그 이름난 〈휴런〉의 추장, 〈르 르나르 쉬브틸〉임을 알아보았다.\n\n그를 맞이하는 태도는 진중하고, 말이 없으며, 조심스러웠다. 앞에 선 전사들이 옆으로 물러서며, 그 행동으로 가장 인정받는 자기네 변사(辯士)에게 길을 열어 주었으니, 그는 북방 원주민들 사이에서 갈고닦인 모든 언어를 구사하는 자였다.\n\n\"지혜로운 〈휴런〉을 환영하노라.\" 〈델라웨어〉인이 〈마쿠아〉족의 말로 말했다. \"그는 호수의 형제들과 함께 자기네 '서코태시'를 먹으러 왔도다.\"\n\n* 서코태시: 옥수수와 콩을 함께 끓여 만든 원주민의 요리.\n\n\"그는 왔노라.\" 〈마구아〉가 동방 군주의 위엄으로 고개를 숙이며 되풀이했다.\n추장이 한 팔을 내밀어 상대의 손목을 잡으니, 그들은 다시 한번 우호의 인사를 주고받았다. 그러자 〈델라웨어〉인은 손님에게 제 오두막으로 들어와 아침 식사를 함께 나누자고 청했다. 청은 받아들여졌고, 두 전사는 노인 서넛을 거느린 채 차분히 걸어갔으니, 나머지 부족 사람들은 이토록 이례적인 방문의 까닭을 알고 싶은 마음에 애가 탔으나, 몸짓으로도 말로도 조금의 조바심조차 드러내지 않았다.\n\n뒤따른 짧고 소박한 식사 동안, 대화는 더없이 신중했고, 오로지 〈마구아〉가 얼마 전에 나섰던 사냥의 일들에만 관련되었다. 자리에 있는 모든 이가 그 방문이 어떤 은밀한 목적과, 그것도 아마 자기네에게 중요한 목적과 결부되어 있음을 완벽히 알고 있었음에도, 이 손님 대접하는 이들이 그 방문을 당연한 일로 여기는 듯한 태도보다 더한 모습을 보이기란, 아무리 세련된 예법으로도 불가능했을 것이다. 모두의 식욕이 채워지자, 여인들이 나무 접시와 호리병박을 치웠고, 두 무리는 저마다 기지(機智)의 미묘한 겨룸을 위해 채비를 갖추기 시작했다.\n\n\"내 위대한 〈캐나다〉 아버지의 얼굴이 다시 그의 〈휴런〉 자식들을 향했는가?\" 〈델라웨어〉족의 변사가 물었다.\n\n\"언제 그렇지 않은 적이 있었는가?\" 〈마구아〉가 대꾸했다. \"그분은 내 백성을 '가장 사랑하는 이들'이라 부르신다.\"\n\n〈델라웨어〉인은 그것이 거짓임을 알면서도 진중하게 고개를 숙여 수긍하고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그대 젊은이들의 도끼가 무척 붉었더군.\"\n\n\"그렇다. 그러나 지금은 빛나면서도 무뎌졌으니, 〈양키〉들은 죽었고 〈델라웨어〉족은 우리의 이웃이기 때문이다.\"\n\n상대는 그 평화로운 인사치레를 손짓으로 받아들이고는 말이 없었다. 그러자 〈마구아〉는, 그 학살에 대한 언급으로 그런 기억이 떠오른 듯, 물었다.\n\n\"내 포로가 형제들에게 폐를 끼치는가?\"\n\n\"그 여인은 환영받고 있다.\"\n\n\"〈휴런〉족과 〈델라웨어〉족 사이의 길은 짧고, 또 열려 있다. 그 여인이 내 형제에게 폐를 끼친다면 내 여인들에게 보내도록 하라.\"\n\n\"그 여인은 환영받고 있다.\" 후자 부족의 추장이 한층 더 힘주어 대답했다.\n\n좌절한 〈마구아〉는 몇 분 동안 말이 없었으나, 〈코라〉를 손에 넣으려던 이 공공연한 노력에서 당한 거절에는 도무지 무심해 보였다.\n\n\"내 젊은이들이 사냥하라고 산에 〈델라웨어〉족의 자리를 남겨 두는가?\" 그가 마침내 말을 이었다.\n\n\"〈레나페〉는 제 언덕의 주인이다.\" 상대가 다소 거만하게 대답했다.\n\n\"좋다. 정의야말로 붉은 피부를 가진 자의 주인이다! 어찌하여 그들이 서로를 향해 도끼를 번뜩이고 칼을 갈아야 하겠는가? 창백한 얼굴들이 꽃피는 철의 제비 떼보다 빽빽하지 아니한가?\"\n\n\"좋다!\" 그 말을 듣던 두셋이 동시에 외쳤다.\n\n〈마구아〉는, 제 말이 〈델라웨어〉족의 마음을 누그러뜨리도록 잠시 기다린 뒤에 덧붙였다.\n\n\"숲에 낯선 모카신이 있지 아니했는가? 내 형제들이 백인의 발 냄새를 맡지 아니했는가?\"\n\n\"내 〈캐나다〉 아버지가 오시게 하라.\" 상대가 얼버무리며 대답했다. \"그의 자식들은 그분을 뵐 준비가 되어 있다.\"\n\n\"위대한 추장이 오시는 것은 인디언과 함께 그들의 천막에서 담배를 피우기 위함이다. 〈휴런〉족 또한 그분을 환영한다고 말한다. 그러나 〈양키〉들은 긴 팔과, 결코 지치지 않는 다리를 지녔다! 내 젊은이들은 〈델라웨어〉족의 마을 가까이에서 〈양키〉들의 자취를 보았다고 꿈을 꾸었다더군?\"\n\n\"그들은 〈레나페〉가 잠들어 있는 것을 보지 못할 것이다.\"\n\n\"좋다. 눈이 열려 있는 전사는 제 적을 볼 수 있다.\" 〈마구아〉가, 동료의 신중함을 꿰뚫지 못함을 깨닫고 다시 한번 방향을 바꾸며 말했다. \"나는 형제에게 줄 선물을 가져왔다. 그의 부족은 옳지 않다고 여겨 전쟁의 길에 나서지 않았으나, 그들의 벗들은 그들이 사는 곳을 잊지 않았다.\"\n\n이렇게 제 너그러운 뜻을 밝히고 나서, 교활한 추장은 일어서서, 손님들의 눈이 휘둥그레지도록 진중하게 제 선물들을 펼쳐 놓았다. 그것들은 대개 별 가치 없는 자질구레한 장신구로, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에서 살해된 여인들에게서 약탈한 것이었다. 그 장신구들을 나누어 줌에 있어, 그 교활한 〈휴런〉인은 그것들을 고른 솜씨에 못지않은 재간을 보였다. 더 값나가는 것들을 가장 뛰어난 두 전사에게 — 그중 하나는 주인이었다 — 베푸는 한편, 그보다 못한 자들에게 바치는 것들에는 더없이 시의적절하고 알맞은 찬사를 곁들여, 그들에게 불평할 거리를 조금도 남기지 않았다. 요컨대, 그 의식 전체에는 실리와 아첨이 어찌나 절묘하게 어우러져 있었던지, 베푸는 자가 칭찬과 그토록 알맞게 뒤섞인 너그러움의 효과를, 상대하는 이들의 눈에서 곧바로 읽어 내기란 어렵지 않았다.\n\n〈마구아〉 쪽의 이 잘 판단된 정략적 일격은 즉각적인 결과를 낳지 않을 수 없었다. 〈델라웨어〉족은 진중함을 잃고 한결 다정한 표정을 지었으며, 특히 주인은 자기 몫으로 떨어진 넉넉한 전리품을 잠시 각별한 흐뭇함으로 바라본 뒤, 힘주어 그 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"내 형제는 지혜로운 추장이다. 그를 환영한다!\"\n\n\"〈휴런〉족은 그들의 벗 〈델라웨어〉족을 사랑한다.\" 〈마구아〉가 대답했다. \"어찌 그러지 않겠는가? 그들은 같은 태양에 물들었고, 그들의 의로운 이들은 죽은 뒤에도 같은 사냥터에서 사냥할 것이다. 붉은 피부를 가진 이들은 마땅히 벗이 되어, 눈을 뜨고 백인들을 바라보아야 한다. 내 형제는 숲에서 첩자의 냄새를 맡지 아니했는가?\"\n\n영어로 그 이름이 \"굳은 마음\"을 뜻하고, 프랑스인들이 \"르 쾨르 뒤르\"로 옮긴 그 〈델라웨어〉인은, 아마 그토록 의미심장한 칭호를 그에게 안겨 주었을 그 굳센 의지를 잠시 잊었다. 그의 얼굴은 눈에 띄게 덜 준엄해졌고, 이제는 한결 직접적으로 대답하기를 마다하지 않았다.\n\n\"내 진영 주위에 낯선 모카신들이 있었다. 그 발자국이 내 오두막들 안까지 났더라.\"\n\n\"내 형제는 그 개들을 쫓아내었는가?\" 〈마구아〉가, 추장이 앞서 한 얼버무림에는 조금도 개의치 않고 물었다.\n\n\"그럴 수는 없었다. 낯선 이는 언제나 〈레나페〉의 자식들에게 환영받는다.\"\n\n\"낯선 이는 그렇겠으나, 첩자는 아닐 터.\"\n\n\"〈양키〉들이 제 여인들을 첩자로 보내겠는가? 〈휴런〉의 추장은 그 전투에서 여인들을 사로잡았다고 말하지 않았는가?\"\n\n\"그는 거짓을 말하지 않았다. 〈양키〉들은 척후를 내보냈다. 그들이 내 천막에 들어왔으나, 그곳에서 환영한다 말해 줄 이를 찾지 못했다. 그러자 그들은 〈델라웨어〉족에게로 달아났으니, 그들이 이르기를, 〈델라웨어〉족은 우리의 벗이요, 그들의 마음은 〈캐나다〉 아버지에게서 돌아섰다고 하기 때문이다!\"\n\n이 빈정거림은 정곡을 찌르는 것이었으니, 더 발달한 사회였더라면 〈마구아〉에게 능란한 외교관이라는 명성을 안겨 주었을 만한 것이었다. 부족의 최근 이탈은, 그들 스스로도 잘 알고 있듯이 프랑스 동맹들 사이에서 〈델라웨어〉족에게 적잖은 비난을 안겨 주었고, 이제 그들은 자기네 앞으로의 행동이 시샘과 불신의 눈으로 지켜보아지리라는 것을 느끼게 되었다. 그러한 사정이 그들의 앞으로의 움직임에 매우 해로우리라는 것을 내다보는 데에는, 원인과 결과를 깊이 통찰할 필요조차 없었다. 그들의 먼 마을들, 그들의 사냥터, 그리고 수백에 이르는 그들의 여인과 아이들은, 그들 물리적 병력의 상당 부분과 더불어, 실제로 프랑스 영토의 경계 안에 들어 있었다. 따라서 이 불안을 자아내는 통보는, 〈마구아〉가 의도한 대로, 경악까지는 아니더라도 명백한 못마땅함으로 받아들여졌다.\n\n\"내 아버지가 내 얼굴을 들여다보게 하라.\" 〈르 쾨르 뒤르〉가 말했다. \"그는 아무 변함도 보지 못할 것이다. 내 젊은이들이 전쟁의 길에 나서지 않은 것은 사실이다. 그들에겐 그러지 않을 꿈이 있었다. 그러나 그들은 위대한 백인 추장을 사랑하고 우러른다.\"\n\n\"그분이, 자신의 가장 큰 적이 자식들의 진영에서 대접받고 있다는 소식을 들으면 그리 생각하시겠는가? 피에 굶주린 〈양키〉가 그대들의 화롯가에서 담배를 피운다는 말을 들으면? 그분의 벗을 그토록 많이 죽인 그 창백한 얼굴이 〈델라웨어〉족 사이를 드나든다는 말을 들으면? 가라! 내 위대한 〈캐나다〉 아버지는 어리석은 분이 아니다!\"\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉족이 두려워한다는 그 〈양키〉가 어디에 있는가?\" 상대가 대꾸했다. \"누가 내 젊은이들을 죽였는가? 누가 내 위대한 아버지의 철천지원수란 말인가!\"\n\n\"〈라 롱그 카라빈〉이다.\"\n\n〈델라웨어〉의 전사들은 그 잘 알려진 이름에 깜짝 놀랐으니, 그 경악으로 보아, 프랑스의 인디언 동맹들 사이에서 그토록 이름난 자가 자기네 수중에 들어와 있다는 것을 이제야 처음으로 알게 되었음이 드러났다.\n\n\"내 형제는 무슨 말을 하는가?\" 〈르 쾨르 뒤르〉가, 그 놀라움이 자기 종족의 여느 무심함을 한참이나 뛰어넘는 어조로 물었다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉은 결코 거짓을 말하지 않는다!\" 〈마구아〉가 차갑게 대꾸하며, 오두막 벽에 머리를 기대고 얇은 겉옷을 거무스름한 가슴 위로 끌어당겼다. \"〈델라웨어〉족이 제 포로들을 세어 보게 하라. 그러면 피부가 붉지도 창백하지도 않은 하나를 찾아낼 것이다.\"\n\n길고 사색에 잠긴 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 추장은 한쪽에서 동료들과 의논했고, 부족의 가장 뛰어난 다른 몇몇 사람을 불러 모으도록 전령들이 파견되었다.\n\n전사가 하나둘 들어올 때마다, 그들은 차례로 〈마구아〉가 방금 전한 중대한 소식을 알게 되었다. 놀라는 기색과, 늘 내는 그 나직하고 깊은 목 안의 탄성이 그들 모두에게 공통되었다. 소식은 입에서 입으로 퍼져, 마침내 야영지 전체가 거세게 동요했다. 여인들은 의논하는 전사들의 입에서 무심코 새어 나오는 음절들을 들으려고 하던 일을 멈추었다. 사내아이들은 놀이를 내팽개치고, 두려움 없이 제 아비들 사이를 거닐며, 그토록 미워하는 적의 대담함에 그들이 거리낌 없이 내뱉는 그 짧은 경이의 탄성을 들으며, 궁금한 듯 우러러보았다. 요컨대, 모든 일이 잠시 내버려졌고, 다른 모든 추구가 한쪽으로 밀려난 듯했으니, 부족이 제 나름의 독특한 방식으로 감정의 공공연한 표출에 마음껏 빠져들 수 있도록 함이었다.\n\n흥분이 조금 가라앉자, 노인들은 이토록 미묘하고 곤혹스러운 상황에서 제 부족의 명예와 안전을 위해 마땅히 행해야 할 바를 진지하게 헤아리기 시작했다. 이 모든 움직임이 이는 동안, 그리고 그 전반의 소란 한가운데서, 〈마구아〉는 제 자리를 지켰을 뿐 아니라, 처음에 취했던 바로 그 자세, 곧 오두막 벽에 기댄 자세 그대로 머물러, 그 결과에 아무 이해관계도 없는 사람처럼 꿈쩍 않고, 또 겉보기에 무심하게 앉아 있었다. 그러나 손님 대접하는 이들의 앞으로의 의도를 알려 줄 단 하나의 기색도, 그의 빈틈없는 눈을 벗어나지 못했다. 자신이 상대해야 할 부족의 본성에 관한 더없이 완벽한 지식으로, 그는 그들이 결정하는 모든 조치를 미리 내다보았으니, 많은 경우 그들이 미처 제 의도를 깨닫기도 전에 그가 먼저 그것을 알고 있었다 해도 거의 틀린 말이 아니었다.\n\n〈델라웨어〉족의 평의는 짧았다. 그것이 끝나자, 곧이어 부족 전체의 엄숙하고 격식 갖춘 회합이 뒤따르리라는 것을 두루 이는 부산함이 알렸다. 그러한 회합은 드물고, 오로지 더없이 중대한 일이 있을 때에만 소집되는 것이었기에, 여전히 한쪽에 떨어져 앉아 그 진행을 교활하고 음험한 눈으로 살피던 그 음흉한 〈휴런〉인은, 이제 제 모든 계략이 끝내 결판날 때가 왔음을 알았다. 그리하여 그는 오두막을 떠나, 전사들이 이미 모여들기 시작한 야영지 앞 공터로 말없이 걸어 나갔다.\n\n여인과 아이들까지 포함하여 저마다 제자리를 잡기까지는 아마 반 시간쯤 걸렸을 것이다. 그 지체는, 이토록 엄숙하고 이례적인 회합에 필요하다 여겨진 진중한 채비에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그러나 해가, 〈델라웨어〉족이 그 품에 야영지를 세운 그 산의 봉우리 위로 솟아오르는 것이 보일 무렵에는 대다수가 자리에 앉았고, 그 밝은 햇살이 그 높은 곳을 두른 나무들의 윤곽 뒤에서 쏘아져 내릴 때, 그것은 아마 그 아침 햇살이 일찍이 비춘 적 없을 만큼 진중하고, 주의 깊으며, 깊이 마음 쓰는 무리 위로 내려앉았다. 그 수는 천 명의 영혼을 다소 웃돌았다.\n\n이토록 진지한 미개인들이 모인 자리에는, 제 명성을 얻으려 청중을 어떤 성급하고, 어쩌면 분별없는 논의로 몰아갈 채비를 한 채, 때 이른 두각을 좇는 조급한 야심가란 결코 찾아볼 수 없는 법이다. 그토록 경솔하고 주제넘은 짓은 조숙한 지력의 몰락을 영원히 봉인하고 말 것이었다. 회합의 주제를 사람들 앞에 내놓는 일은 오로지 가장 나이 많고 경험 많은 이에게만 달려 있었다. 그러한 이가 어떤 움직임을 보이기로 마음먹기까지는, 어떤 무공도, 어떤 타고난 재주도, 변사로서의 어떤 명성도 조금의 끼어듦조차 정당화하지 못했을 것이다. 이번 경우에, 말할 특권을 지닌 그 나이 든 전사는, 제 주제의 막중함에 짓눌린 듯 말이 없었다. 그 지체는 회합에 늘 앞서는 으레 있는 숙의의 멈춤을 이미 한참 넘어섰으나, 가장 어린 사내아이조차 조바심이나 놀라움의 기색을 내비치지 않았다. 이따금, 대다수의 시선이 못 박혀 있던 땅에서 눈 하나가 들려, 어떤 한 오두막을 향해 흘끗거렸으니, 그것은 풍우의 습격을 막기 위해 기울인 각별한 정성을 빼고는 주위의 것들과 조금도 다를 바 없는 오두막이었다.\n\n마침내, 군중을 으레 술렁이게 하는 그 나직한 웅성거림 가운데 하나가 들렸고, 부족 전체가 한 충동에 따라 발딱 일어섰다. 그러자 문제의 그 오두막 문이 열리고, 세 사람이 거기서 나와 천천히 회합 장소로 다가왔다. 그들은 모두, 자리에 있는 가장 나이 든 이가 이른 그 나이마저 넘어선 노인들이었으나, 동료들에게 몸을 기댄 한가운데의 한 사람은 인간이 좀처럼 다다르도록 허락받지 못하는 햇수를 헤아렸다. 한때 삼나무처럼 크고 꼿꼿했던 그의 골격은, 이제 한 세기가 넘는 세월의 짓누름 아래 굽어 있었다. 인디언의 그 탄력 있고 가벼운 걸음은 사라졌고, 그 대신 그는 한 치 한 치 더딘 걸음으로 땅을 힘겹게 옮겨야 했다. 그의 어둡고 주름진 얼굴은, 어찌나 두텁게 어깨 위로 흘러내렸던지 그것을 마지막으로 자른 이래 아마 여러 세대가 지났음을 알리는 그 길고 흰 머리채와 기이하고 거친 대조를 이루었다.\n\n이 가부장 — 그 까마득한 나이에 더해 제 백성과 맺은 친연과 영향력을 헤아릴 때, 그를 그렇게 부름은 더없이 마땅하리라 — 의 옷차림은 부족의 소박한 양식을 엄격히 따르면서도 풍성하고 위풍당당했다. 그의 겉옷은 더없이 고운 가죽으로 지어졌는데, 지난 시대에 이룬 갖가지 무공을 상형으로 그려 넣을 수 있도록 그 털을 벗겨 낸 것이었다. 그의 가슴에는 메달이 잔뜩 달려 있었으니, 더러는 묵직한 은으로, 한둘은 금으로까지 된 것으로, 그의 긴 생애 동안 여러 기독교 군주가 보낸 선물이었다. 그는 또한 팔찌와, 발목 위로 두른 띠를 후자의 그 귀한 금속으로 두르고 있었다. 그의 머리는 — 전쟁의 일을 손에서 놓은 지 오래여서 그 위에 머리가 온통 자라도록 내버려 둔 것이었는데 — 도금한 일종의 머리띠로 둘려 있었고, 그 머리띠는 다시 더 작고 더 반짝이는 장식들을 달고 있었으니, 깊은 검정으로 물들인 채 늘어진 타조 깃털 세 개의 윤기 도는 빛깔 사이에서 반짝였으며, 그것은 그의 눈처럼 흰 머리채와 가슴 저린 대조를 이루었다. 그의 손도끼는 거의 은에 파묻혀 있었고, 칼자루는 단단한 금으로 된 뿔처럼 빛났다.\n\n이 우러름받는 인물의 느닷없는 등장이 자아낸 첫 감격과 기쁨의 웅성거림이 조금 가라앉기가 무섭게, \"〈타메눈드〉\"라는 이름이 입에서 입으로 속삭여졌다. 〈마구아〉는 이 지혜롭고 의로운 〈델라웨어〉인의 명성을 자주 들어 왔으니, 그 명성은 〈위대한 영(靈)〉과 은밀히 교통하는 드문 능력을 그에게 부여하는 데까지 이를 정도였으며, 훗날 그의 옛 영토를 빼앗은 백인 찬탈자들에게 약간의 변형을 거쳐 그 이름을 전하여, 광막한 한 제국의 가상의 수호성인으로 삼게 한 것이었다. 그리하여 〈휴런〉의 추장은 무리에서 조금 앞으로 다급히 나아가, 그 결정이 자신의 운명에 그토록 깊은 영향을 미칠 듯한 그 사람의 이목구비를 한층 가까이서 흘끗 볼 수 있는 자리로 갔다.\n\n노인의 두 눈은, 인간의 격정이 빚어내는 그 이기적인 놀음을 너무도 오래 지켜본 탓에 그 기관이 지쳐 버린 듯 감겨 있었다. 그 피부의 빛깔은 주위 대다수와 달라, 더 짙고 더 어두웠으니, 그 어두운 빛은 복잡하면서도 아름다운 무늬의 어떤 섬세하고 미로 같은 선들이 문신의 수법으로 그의 몸 거의 전체에 새겨진 데서 비롯된 것이었다. 〈휴런〉인이 자리한 위치에도 불구하고, 그는 살피며 침묵하는 〈마구아〉를 거들떠보지 않고 지나쳐, 그 두 명의 존엄한 부축꾼에게 기댄 채 군중의 높은 자리로 나아갔으니, 그곳에서 그는 군왕의 위엄과 아비의 풍모로 제 부족의 한가운데에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n이 세상보다는 오히려 다른 세상에 속한 이의 이 뜻밖의 방문이 그의 백성에게 받아들여진 그 외경과 애정은 무엇으로도 넘어설 수 없는 것이었다. 알맞고 점잖은 멈춤이 지난 뒤, 주요 추장들이 일어섰고, 그 가부장에게 다가가 그의 두 손을 공손히 제 머리 위에 얹으며 축복을 비는 듯했다. 더 젊은 이들은 그의 겉옷을 만지거나, 그토록 나이 많고, 그토록 의롭고, 그토록 용맹한 이의 기운을 들이마시려 그저 그의 곁에 다가서는 것으로 만족했다. 젊은 전사들 가운데서도 가장 뛰어난 이들만이 감히 후자의 의식을 행하려 들었으니, 군중의 대다수는 그토록 깊이 우러르고 그토록 사랑하는 그 형상을 바라보는 것만으로도 족한 행복이라 여겼다. 이 애정과 존경의 행위가 다 이루어지자, 추장들은 다시 저마다의 자리로 물러났고, 야영지 전체에 정적이 깃들었다.\n\n잠시 지체가 지난 뒤, 〈타메눈드〉의 나이 든 시종 가운데 하나에게서 지시를 귓속말로 받은 젊은이 몇이 일어나 무리를 떠나, 그 아침 내내 그토록 큰 주목의 대상이었다고 이미 적은 그 오두막으로 들어갔다. 몇 분 뒤 그들은 다시 나타나, 이 모든 엄숙한 채비를 불러일으킨 그 인물들을 심판의 자리로 호송했다. 군중이 갈라져 길을 내었고, 그 무리가 다시 들어서자 군중은 다시 메워져, 트인 원형으로 늘어선 인간의 몸으로 크고 빽빽한 띠를 이루었다.\n\n\"회의가 자리를 잡자, 모두를 앞질러 일어선\n〈아킬레우스〉가 인간들의 왕에게 이렇게 고했노라.\"\n\n포프 역 「일리아드」\n\n〈코라〉는 포로들 가운데서 맨 앞에 서서, 누이를 향한 정겨운 애정으로 두 팔을 〈앨리스〉의 팔에 엮고 있었다. 사방에 둘러선 미개인들의 무시무시하고 위협적인 진용에도 불구하고, 제 신변에 대한 어떤 두려움도 이 고결한 처녀로 하여금 떨고 있는 〈앨리스〉의 창백하고 근심 어린 얼굴에서 눈을 떼게 하지 못했다. 그들의 바로 곁에는 〈헤이워드〉가 서서 두 사람 모두에게 마음을 쏟고 있었으니, 이토록 극도로 불확실한 순간에는, 그가 가장 사랑하는 이를 향한 쪽으로조차 좀처럼 무게가 기울지 않았다. 〈호크아이〉는 동료들의 우월한 신분을 존중하여 조금 뒤편에 자리를 잡았으니, 지금 그들의 운명이 처한 처지가 다 같다 한들 그 존중의 마음을 잊게 할 수는 없었다. 〈언커스〉는 그 자리에 없었다.\n\n다시 완전한 정적이 깃들고, 으레 그러하듯 길고 인상 깊은 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 족장 곁에 앉아 있던 두 늙은 추장 가운데 하나가 일어나, 무척 또렷한 영어로 큰 소리로 물었다.\n\n\"내 포로들 가운데 누가 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉인가?\"\n\n〈던컨〉도 척후병도 대답하지 않았다. 그러나 전자는 어둡고 고요한 회중을 둘러보다가, 그 시선이 〈마구아〉의 악의에 찬 얼굴에 가 닿자 한 걸음 물러섰다. 그는 이 교활한 미개인이 그들을 부족 앞에 끌어내 심문하게 한 데에 어떤 은밀한 책동을 부렸음을 단번에 알아차렸고, 그 음험한 계획의 실행을 가로막을 온갖 장애를 던져 놓기로 마음먹었다. 그는 인디언들의 즉결 처벌을 한 차례 목격한 바 있었기에, 이제 자기 동료가 두 번째 본보기로 뽑히지나 않을까 두려워했다. 이 진퇴양난 속에서, 생각할 겨를이라곤 거의 또는 전혀 없이, 그는 어떤 위험이든 제 한 몸에 걸고서라도 그 더없이 소중한 벗을 감싸 두기로 별안간 작정했다. 그러나 그가 미처 입을 떼기도 전에, 한층 큰 목소리로, 더 또렷한 발음으로 물음이 되풀이되었다.\n\n\"우리에게 무기를 주시오.\" 젊은이가 오만하게 대답했다. \"그리고 저 숲에 우리를 두시오. 우리의 행적이 우리를 대신해 말할 것이오!\"\n\n\"이 사람이 그 이름으로 우리 귀를 채운 전사로구나!\" 추장이 이렇게 대꾸하며, 공적이든 우연이든, 미덕이든 죄악이든 그것으로 악명을 얻은 동족 가운데 하나를 처음 마주할 때 사람에게서 좀처럼 떼어 놓을 수 없을 법한 그런 호기심 어린 관심으로 〈헤이워드〉를 살폈다. \"무엇이 백인을 〈델라웨어〉족의 진영으로 데려왔는가?\"\n\n\"내 곤궁함이오. 나는 먹을 것과 거처와 벗을 구하러 왔소.\"\n\n\"그럴 리 없다. 숲에는 사냥감이 가득하다. 전사의 머리에는 구름 한 점 없는 하늘 말고 다른 거처가 필요치 않다. 그리고 〈델라웨어〉족은 〈잉기즈〉의 적이지, 벗이 아니다. 가라! 입은 말하였으되, 마음은 아무것도 말하지 않았다.\"\n\n〈던컨〉은 어찌 처신해야 할지 다소 막막하여 잠자코 있었다. 그러나 오가는 말을 모조리 주의 깊게 듣고 있던 척후병이 이제 꿋꿋이 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"내가 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉을 부르는 소리에 답하지 않은 것은, 수치 때문도 두려움 때문도 아니오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 어느 것도 정직한 사람의 천품은 아니니까. 허나 나는, 내 벗들이 바로 이 점에서 내 천품을 헤아려 준 사람에게 이름을 붙일 권리가 〈밍고〉족에게 있다고는 인정하지 않소. 더구나 그들이 붙인 이름은 거짓이니, 〈킬디어〉는 강선을 판 총신이지 카라빈이 아니거든. 그렇더라도 나는, 일가붙이에게서 〈너새니얼〉이라는 이름을, 제 강가에 사는 〈델라웨어〉족에게서 〈호크아이〉라는 영예로운 이름을 얻은 그 사람이오. 그리고 이 일에 가장 깊이 관련된 자의 허락도 없이, 〈이로쿼이〉족이 주제넘게 '롱 라이플'이라 일컬어 온 바로 그 사람이오.\"\n\n이제껏 〈던컨〉의 풍모를 진지하게 살피던 모든 이의 눈이, 그 순간 그 빼어난 이름을 새로이 자처하고 나선 이 사내의 꼿꼿한 무쇠 같은 골격을 향했다. 그토록 큰 영예를 차지하려는 자가 둘이나 나타난 것은 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 사칭하는 자란 드물기는 해도 원주민들 사이에 없지는 않았던 것이다. 그러나 이 일에 추호의 착오도 없어야 한다는 것은, 공정하고 준엄한 의도를 품은 〈델라웨어〉족에게 무엇보다 긴요한 일이었다. 그들 가운데 몇몇 노인이 사사로이 의논을 나누더니, 이윽고 보아하니, 그 문제를 두고 손님에게 캐묻기로 작정한 듯했다.\n\n\"내 형제는 한 마리 뱀이 내 진영으로 기어들었다고 말했다.\" 추장이 〈마구아〉에게 말했다. \"어느 자인가?\"\n\n〈휴런〉족 사내가 척후병을 가리켰다.\n\n\"지혜로운 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 늑대의 짖는 소리를 믿으려오?\" 〈던컨〉이 오랜 원수의 사악한 의도를 한층 굳게 확신하며 외쳤다. \"개는 결코 거짓을 말하지 않으나, 늑대가 참을 말한다는 소리를 언제 들어 보았소?\"\n\n〈마구아〉의 눈에서 불꽃이 번뜩였다. 그러나 침착함을 유지해야 한다는 것을 별안간 떠올리고, 그는 인디언들의 명민함이 이 다툼의 핵심에서 참된 진상을 반드시 가려내리라 굳게 믿으며, 잠자코 경멸의 빛을 띤 채 돌아섰다. 그의 믿음은 어긋나지 않았다. 또 한 차례 짧은 의논을 거친 뒤, 신중한 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 그에게 다시 돌아서, 더없이 사려 깊은 말씨로나마 추장들의 결정을 전했다.\n\n\"내 형제는 거짓말쟁이라 불렸다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 그의 벗들은 노여워한다. 그들은 그가 참을 말했음을 보일 것이다. 내 포로들에게 총을 주어, 어느 쪽이 그 사람인지 가려 보게 하라.\"\n\n〈마구아〉는 그 방책이 제게 대한 불신에서 비롯한 것임을 잘 알면서도, 그것을 마치 자기에 대한 예우인 양 받아들이는 체하며, 척후병만큼 능란한 명사수가 자기 말의 진실됨을 뒷받침해 주리라는 데 흡족해하며 동의의 몸짓을 지었다. 이내 무기가 다정한 적수들의 손에 쥐어졌고, 그들이 선 자리에서 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 그루터기 위에 우연히 놓여 있던 흙 그릇을 향해, 앉아 있는 무리의 머리 너머로 쏘아 보라는 분부가 내려졌다.\n\n〈헤이워드〉는 척후병과 겨룬다는 생각에 속으로 웃음 지었으나, 〈마구아〉의 진짜 속셈을 알아낼 때까지는 그 속임수를 밀고 나가기로 작정했다. 더없이 정성스레 소총을 들어 올려, 세 차례나 거듭 겨눈 끝에 그는 방아쇠를 당겼다. 총알은 그릇에서 손가락 몇 마디 떨어진 나무를 깎았고, 모두가 한목소리로 흡족함을 토하며 그 사격이 무기를 다루는 빼어난 솜씨의 증거로 여겨진다고 알렸다. 〈호크아이〉조차, 제가 기대한 것보다는 낫다고 말하려는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러나 그는 그 성공한 명사수와 겨룰 뜻을 내비치기는커녕, 무언가에 온통 골몰한 사람처럼 일 분이 넘도록 소총에 기댄 채 서 있었다. 그러나 무기를 건넨 젊은 인디언 가운데 하나가 그의 어깨를 건드리며 몹시 서툰 영어로 말을 거는 바람에 그는 그 상념에서 깨어났다.\n\n\"백인이 저것을 이길 수 있는가?\"\n\n\"그렇다, 〈휴런〉!\" 척후병이 짧은 소총을 오른손에 들어 올리며, 마치 갈대인 양 손쉽게 〈마구아〉를 향해 흔들어 보이며 외쳤다. \"그렇다, 〈휴런〉, 나는 지금 당장 너를 칠 수 있고, 지상의 어떤 힘도 그 일을 막지 못하리라! 솟구치는 매가 비둘기를 노릴 때보다, 내 마음만 먹는다면 지금 이 순간 네 심장에 총알을 박아 넣는 것이 더 틀림없으리라! 어찌 그러지 않겠는가? 어찌!—내 살빛의 천품이 그것을 금하고, 또 내가 가냘프고 죄 없는 머리들 위에 화를 불러올지도 모르기 때문이지. 만일 네가 하느님이라 불리는 분을 안다면, 그러므로 네 마음속 깊이 그분께 감사하라. 너에게는 그럴 까닭이 있으니.\"\n\n상기된 얼굴, 성난 눈, 부풀어 오른 척후병의 몸이, 그 말을 듣는 모든 이에게 은밀한 외경의 느낌을 자아냈다. 〈델라웨어〉족은 기대 속에 숨을 죽였다. 그러나 〈마구아〉 자신은, 제 원수의 자제심을 미덥지 못하게 여기면서도, 군중에 끼여 그 자리에 뿌리내린 사람처럼, 꼼짝 않고 침착하게 서 있었다.\n\n\"저것을 이겨라.\" 척후병의 팔꿈치께에 선 젊은 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"무엇을 이긴단 말이냐, 멍청아!—무엇을!\" 〈호크아이〉가, 이제는 그 눈이 더는 〈마구아〉의 몸을 찾지 않으면서도, 여전히 머리 위로 무기를 성나게 휘두르며 외쳤다.\n\n\"백인이 제가 자처하는 그 전사라면,\" 늙은 추장이 말했다. \"표적에 더 가까이 맞혀 보아라.\"\n\n척후병이 큰 소리로 웃었으니—그 소리는 〈헤이워드〉에게 부자연스러운 음향이 주는 듯한 섬뜩한 효과를 자아냈다—그러고는 그 총을 뻗은 왼손에 묵직하게 떨어뜨리자, 보아하니 그 충격으로 총이 발사되어, 그릇의 파편이 공중으로 튀어 올라 사방으로 흩어졌다. 거의 같은 순간, 그가 그것을 경멸하듯 땅에 떨어뜨리도록 내버려 둠에 따라, 소총이 덜그럭대는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n이토록 기이한 광경에 대한 첫 인상은 넋을 빼앗는 감탄이었다. 이어 낮으나 점점 커지는 웅성거림이 무리 사이로 번지더니, 마침내 구경꾼들의 심사가 자못 엇갈리고 있음을 드러내는 소리로 부풀어 올랐다. 더러는 그토록 유례없는 솜씨에 대한 흡족함을 드러내 놓고 내비쳤으나, 부족의 훨씬 더 많은 이는 그 사격의 성공이 우연의 결과라 믿는 쪽으로 기울었다. 〈헤이워드〉는 제 자처에 그토록 유리한 그 견해를 뒷받침하는 데 굼뜨지 않았다.\n\n\"우연이었소!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"겨냥 없이 쏠 수 있는 자는 없소!\"\n\n\"우연이라고!\" 흥분한 숲의 사내가 받아 외쳤으니, 그는 이제 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 제 정체를 굳게 지키기로 고집스레 작정한 터라, 속임수에 순순히 따르라는 〈헤이워드〉의 은밀한 눈짓 따위는 그에게 깡그리 헛것이 되고 말았다. \"저 거짓말쟁이 〈휴런〉도 우연이라 생각하는가? 그에게 다른 총을 한 자루 주고, 엄폐도 회피도 없이 우리를 얼굴과 얼굴을 맞대게 한 다음, 섭리와 우리 자신의 눈으로 우리 사이의 일을 판가름하게 하라! 소령, 당신에게 이런 제안을 하는 건 아니오. 우리 피는 같은 빛깔이고, 우리는 같은 주인을 섬기니 말이오.\"\n\n\"저 〈휴런〉이 거짓말쟁이라는 것은 너무도 명백하오.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 냉정하게 대꾸했다. \"당신이 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉이라고 그가 단언하는 것을 당신 스스로 듣지 않았소.\"\n\n고집스러운 〈호크아이〉가 제 정체를 입증하려는 그 무모한 열망 속에서 다음으로 어떤 격한 주장을 펼쳤을지는, 늙은 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 다시 한 번 끼어들지 않았더라면 도무지 알 수 없을 노릇이었다.\n\n\"구름 속에서 내려오는 매는 제 마음대로 돌아갈 수 있다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그들에게 총을 주어라.\"\n\n이번에는 척후병이 게걸스레 소총을 거머쥐었고, 〈마구아〉 또한, 비록 시샘 어린 눈으로 그 명사수의 동작을 지켜보았으나, 더는 두려워할 까닭이 없었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 이 〈델라웨어〉 부족이 보는 앞에서, 어느 쪽이 더 나은 사내인지 가려 보자.\" 척후병이, 그토록 많은 목숨을 끊는 방아쇠를 당겨 온 그 손가락으로 제 총의 개머리판을 톡톡 두드리며 외쳤다. \"소령, 저 나무에 매달린 조롱박이 보이지? 당신이 변경에 어울리는 명사수라면, 그 껍질을 깨뜨리는 걸 어디 한번 보여 주시오!\"\n\n〈던컨〉은 그 표적을 눈여겨보며, 다시 시험에 나설 채비를 갖췄다. 그 조롱박은 인디언들이 흔히 쓰는 작은 그릇 가운데 하나로, 사슴 가죽끈에 매달려 작은 소나무의 마른 가지에, 꼬박 백 걸음 거리에 드리워져 있었다. 자기애의 감정이란 어찌나 묘하게 뒤섞여 있는지, 이 젊은 군인은 자신을 심판하는 미개인들의 평이 아무 값어치도 없음을 잘 알면서도, 이 겨룸의 별안간한 동기를 잊은 채 한껏 빼어나고 싶은 마음에 사로잡혔다. 그의 솜씨가 결코 업신여길 수준이 아님은 이미 본 바이거니와, 이제 그는 그 가장 정교한 기량을 펼치기로 마음먹었다. 제 목숨이 그 결과에 달려 있었던들, 〈던컨〉의 겨냥이 이보다 더 신중하거나 조심스러울 수는 없었으리라. 그가 쏘았다. 그러자 그 소리에 앞으로 뛰어나간 서너 명의 젊은 인디언이, 총알이 본래 표적에서 아주 조금 옆으로 비낀 나무에 박혔다고 함성으로 알렸다. 전사들은 한목소리로 기쁨의 탄성을 내질렀고, 이어 그의 적수의 거동에 캐묻듯 눈길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"〈로열 아메리칸〉 연대에야 통할 솜씨겠지!\" 〈호크아이〉가 다시 한 번 제 특유의 소리 없고 진심 어린 투로 웃으며 말했다. \"허나 내 총이 그렇게나 자주 곧은 선에서 빗나갔던들, 지금 어느 귀부인의 토시가 된 담비 가죽의 임자가 여태 숲에 살아 있을 테고, 이미 마지막 심판을 받으러 떠난 피비린내 나는 〈밍고〉 놈들 가운데 숱한 자가 바로 오늘날까지도 식민지 사이를 누비며 제 악행을 부리고 있을 거요. 저 조롱박 임자인 인디언 여인이 제 천막에 그런 박을 더 많이 가지고 있기를 바라오. 저놈은 다시는 물을 담지 못할 테니!\"\n\n척후병은 말하는 동안 점화약을 흔들어 놓고 공이치기를 당겨 두었다. 말을 마치자 그는 한 발을 뒤로 빼고, 천천히 총구를 땅에서 들어 올렸다. 그 동작은 한결같고 고르며 한 방향으로 향했다. 완벽히 수평을 이루자, 총구는 떨림도 흔들림도 없이 한순간 멈추었으니, 마치 사람도 소총도 돌로 새겨진 듯했다. 그 정지한 찰나에, 그것은 환하게 번득이는 불꽃의 장막을 이루며 제 내용물을 쏟아 냈다. 다시 젊은 인디언들이 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 그러나 그들의 다급한 수색과 실망한 표정은 총알의 자취라곤 어디서도 찾을 수 없음을 알렸다.\n\n\"가라!\" 늙은 추장이 짙은 혐오의 어조로 척후병에게 말했다. \"너는 개의 가죽을 쓴 늑대로구나. 나는 〈잉기즈〉의 '롱 라이플'과 이야기하겠다.\"\n\n\"아! 당신이 입에 올린 그 이름을 안겨 준 그 총만 있었던들, 나는 가죽끈을 끊어, 조롱박을 깨뜨리지 않고 떨어뜨리겠노라 약속했을 거요!\" 〈호크아이〉가 상대의 태도에 조금도 흔들림 없이 대꾸했다. \"멍청이들, 이 숲의 명사수가 쏜 총알을 찾으려거든, 표적 둘레가 아니라 표적 안을 들여다보아야 한다!\"\n\n이번에는 그가 〈델라웨어〉족 말로 말했던 까닭에, 인디언 젊은이들은 단번에 그 뜻을 알아차렸다. 그들이 나무에서 조롱박을 잡아채어 의기양양한 함성과 함께 높이 치켜드니, 그 바닥에 총알이 뚫어 놓은 구멍이 드러났는데, 총알은 윗면 한가운데에 으레 나 있는 구멍을 지나친 뒤 그 바닥을 뚫고 나간 것이었다. 이 뜻밖의 광경에, 그 자리에 있던 모든 전사의 입에서 크고 격렬한 환희의 외침이 터져 나왔다. 그것으로 문제는 판가름 났고, 〈호크아이〉는 그 위험한 명성의 임자임을 확고히 굳혔다. 다시 〈헤이워드〉를 향했던 그 호기심 어리고 감탄하는 눈들이, 마침내 풍상에 시달린 척후병의 모습으로 쏠렸고, 그는 이내 자신을 에워싼 순박하고 꾸밈없는 이들의 으뜸가는 관심의 대상이 되었다. 별안간 일어난 떠들썩한 소란이 조금 가라앉자, 늙은 추장이 다시 심문을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"너는 어찌하여 내 귀를 막으려 했는가?\" 그가 〈던컨〉에게 말했다. \"〈델라웨어〉족이 어린 표범과 살쾡이를 구별 못 할 만큼 어리석은 줄 알았느냐?\"\n\n\"머지않아 저들은 저 〈휴런〉이 노래하는 새임을 알게 될 것이오.\" 〈던컨〉이 원주민들의 비유적인 말투를 따르려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"좋다. 누가 사람들의 귀를 닫을 수 있는지 우리가 알아보리라. 형제여,\" 추장이 〈마구아〉에게 눈길을 돌리며 덧붙였다. \"〈델라웨어〉족이 듣겠다.\"\n\n이렇게 지목되어 곧장 제 목적을 밝히라는 청을 받자, 〈휴런〉족 사내가 일어났다. 그는 더없이 신중하고 위엄 있게 원진의 한복판으로 나아가, 포로들과 마주 선 그 자리에서 말할 자세를 취했다. 그러나 입을 열기에 앞서, 그는 청중의 헤아림에 맞추어 제 말을 가다듬으려는 듯, 진지한 얼굴들로 이루어진 살아 있는 경계선 전체를 따라 천천히 눈길을 던졌다. 〈호크아이〉에게는 정중한 적의의 눈길을, 〈던컨〉에게는 꺼지지 않는 증오의 눈빛을 보냈다. 움츠러든 〈앨리스〉의 모습은 거의 거들떠보지도 않았다. 그러나 그 눈길이 〈코라〉의 굳세고 위엄 있으면서도 사랑스러운 자태에 가 닿자, 그의 눈은 한순간 머물렀으니, 그 표정은 무어라 규정하기 어려웠으리라. 그러고는 제 어두운 속셈으로 가득 차서, 그는 〈캐나다〉 지방의 말로 입을 열었으니, 그 말이 청중 대다수에게 통하리라는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n\"사람을 지으신 〈영〉께서 그들에게 저마다 다른 빛깔을 입히셨다.\" 교활한 〈휴런〉족 사내가 말문을 열었다. \"더러는 굼뜬 곰보다도 더 검다. 이들에게 그분은 노예가 되리라 이르셨고, 비버처럼 영원히 일하라 명하셨다. 남풍이 불 때면, 큰 카누가 그들을 떼로 싣고 오가는 저 큰 소금 호수의 기슭을 따라, 들소들의 울부짖음보다 더 큰 그들의 신음이 들리리라. 더러는 숲의 담비보다 더 창백한 얼굴로 지으시어, 이들에게 장사치가 되라 명하셨다. 제 여인들에게는 개요, 제 노예들에게는 늑대로다. 그분은 이 족속에게 비둘기의 본성을 주셨으니, 지칠 줄 모르는 날개와, 나무의 잎사귀보다 더 무성한 자식과, 땅을 집어삼킬 식욕이 그것이다. 그분은 그들에게 살쾡이의 거짓 울음 같은 혀와, 토끼 같은 마음과, 돼지의 교활함(허나 여우의 교활함은 없는)과, 큰사슴의 다리보다 더 긴 팔을 주셨다. 그 혀로 그는 인디언의 귀를 막고, 그 마음으로 전사들을 돈으로 사 제 싸움을 시키며, 그 교활함으로 땅의 재물을 그러모으는 법을 알고, 그 팔로 소금물의 기슭에서 큰 호수의 섬들에 이르기까지 온 땅을 끌어안는다. 그 탐식이 그를 병들게 한다. 하느님이 그에게 넉넉히 주셨건만, 그는 모든 것을 탐낸다. 백인이란 그러한 자들이다.\n\n\"〈위대한 영〉께서 더러는 저 해보다 더 밝고 더 붉은 살갗으로 지으셨다.\" 〈마구아〉가, 지평선의 안개 낀 대기를 뚫고 솟아오르려 안간힘 쓰는 그 음산한 빛덩이를 향해 위쪽을 인상 깊게 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"그리고 이들을 그분은 당신 뜻대로 빚으셨다. 그분은 그들에게 이 섬을, 손수 지으신 그대로, 나무로 뒤덮이고 사냥감으로 가득 찬 채로 주셨다. 바람이 그들의 빈터를 만들었고, 해와 비가 그들의 열매를 익혔으며, 눈이 내려 감사하라 일렀다. 길을 따라 떠날 무슨 필요가 그들에게 있었으랴! 그들은 언덕을 꿰뚫어 보았다. 비버가 일할 때면, 그들은 그늘에 누워 바라보았다. 여름에는 바람이 그들을 식혀 주었고, 겨울에는 가죽이 그들을 따뜻하게 해 주었다. 그들이 저희끼리 싸웠다면, 그것은 자신들이 사내임을 입증하기 위함이었다. 그들은 용맹했고, 공정했으며, 행복했다.\"\n\n여기서 화자는 말을 멈추고, 제 이야기가 듣는 이들의 심금을 울렸는지 살피려 다시 한 번 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그가 마주친 것은, 어디서나 제 눈에 못 박힌 듯한 눈들, 곧추선 머리들, 그리고 벌름거리는 콧구멍이었으니, 마치 그 자리의 사람마다 홀로 제 종족의 부당함을 바로잡을 수 있고 또 기꺼이 그러리라 느끼는 듯했다.\n\n\"〈위대한 영〉께서 당신의 붉은 자식들에게 저마다 다른 혀를 주셨다면,\" 그가 낮고 잔잔하며 우수에 찬 목소리로 이었다. \"그것은 모든 짐승이 그들의 말을 알아듣게 하려 함이었다. 더러는 그들의 사촌인 곰과 함께 눈 속에 두셨다. 더러는 행복한 사냥터로 가는 길목, 지는 해 가까이에 두셨다. 더러는 큰 민물 둘레의 땅에 두셨다. 그러나 가장 위대하고 가장 사랑하는 자식들에게는, 소금 호수의 모래를 주셨다. 내 형제들은 이 사랑받은 백성의 이름을 아는가?\"\n\n\"〈레나페〉다!\" 스무 갈래의 열띤 목소리가 일제히 외쳤다.\n\n\"〈레니 레나페〉였다.\" 〈마구아〉가 그들의 지난날 위대함에 경의를 표하듯 머리를 숙이는 체하며 대꾸했다. \"그것은 〈레나페〉의 부족들이었다! 해는 짠물에서 떠올라 단물 속으로 졌고, 결코 그들의 눈에서 제 모습을 감추지 않았다. 그러나 숲의 한낱 〈휴런〉인 내가, 어찌하여 지혜로운 백성에게 그들 자신의 전승을 들려준단 말인가? 어찌하여 그들에게 그들의 상처를, 그들의 옛 위대함을, 그들의 행적을, 그들의 영광을, 그들의 행복을—그들의 상실을, 그들의 패배를, 그들의 비참을 일깨운단 말인가? 그 모든 것을 보았고 그것이 참임을 아는 이가, 그들 가운데 하나도 없겠는가? 나는 다 말했다. 내 혀는 잠잠하니, 내 마음이 납덩이 같음이라. 나는 듣겠다.\"\n\n화자의 목소리가 별안간 그치자, 모든 얼굴과 모든 눈이 한결같은 움직임으로 존엄한 〈태머넌드〉를 향했다. 자리에 앉은 그 순간부터 지금 이 찰나에 이르기까지, 족장의 입술은 벌어진 적이 없었고, 생명의 기색이라곤 거의 새어 나오지 않았다. 그는 척후병의 솜씨가 그토록 또렷이 입증된 그 첫 장면 내내, 쇠약함에 몸을 굽힌 채, 자신이 어떤 자리에 있는지조차 모르는 듯 앉아 있었다. 그러나 〈마구아〉의 절묘하게 조절된 목소리에는 어떤 의식의 기미를 내비쳤고, 귀를 기울이려는 듯 한두 차례 고개를 들기까지 했다. 그러나 그 교활한 〈휴런〉이 제 부족을 이름으로 일컫자, 노인의 눈꺼풀이 들려 올라갔고, 그는 망령의 얼굴에나 어울릴 법한 그런 멍하고 뜻 모를 표정으로 무리를 내다보았다. 이윽고 그가 일어서려 애쓰니, 부축하는 이들이 그를 떠받쳐 그는 두 발로 섰는데, 쇠약함에 비틀거리면서도 그 자세는 위엄으로 좌중을 압도했다.\n\n\"누가 〈레나페〉의 자식들을 부르는가!\" 그가, 무리의 숨죽인 정적으로 인해 끔찍하리만치 또렷이 들리는 깊고 목쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"누가 지나간 것들을 말하는가! 알이 벌레가 되고—벌레가 파리가 되어, 스러지지 않더냐? 어찌하여 〈델라웨어〉족에게 이미 가 버린 좋은 것을 들려주는가? 남아 있는 것에 대해 〈마니투〉께 감사함이 차라리 나으리라.\"\n\n\"〈와이언도트〉입니다.\" 〈마구아〉가, 노인이 선 그 거친 단상에 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다. \"〈태머넌드〉의 벗입니다.\"\n\n\"벗이라!\" 현자가 되뇌었으니, 그 이마에 어두운 찌푸림이 내려앉으며, 한창때 그의 눈을 그토록 무섭게 만들었던 그 준엄함의 일부가 깃들었다. \"〈밍고〉족이 이 땅의 지배자던가? 무엇이 〈휴런〉을 이리로 데려왔는가?\"\n\n\"정의입니다. 그의 포로들이 그의 형제들과 함께 있으니, 그는 제 것을 찾으러 왔습니다.\"\n\n〈태머넌드〉는 부축하는 이들 가운데 하나에게로 고개를 돌려, 그가 들려주는 짧은 설명에 귀를 기울였다. 그러고는 청원자를 마주하여, 한순간 깊은 주의를 기울여 그를 바라본 뒤, 낮고 마지못한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"정의는 위대한 〈마니투〉의 법이다. 내 자식들아, 저 이방인에게 먹을 것을 주어라. 그런 다음, 〈휴런〉이여, 네 것을 가지고 떠나라.\"\n\n이 엄숙한 판결이 내려지자, 족장은 다시 자리에 앉아 눈을 감았으니, 마치 눈에 보이는 세상의 사물보다 제 무르익은 경험의 영상이 더 흡족한 듯했다. 이런 판결에 감히 투덜댈, 더구나 맞설 만큼 대담한 〈델라웨어〉 사람은 없었다. 말이 채 떨어지기도 전에, 너덧 명의 젊은 전사가 〈헤이워드〉와 척후병의 뒤로 다가서더니, 어찌나 능란하고도 재빠르게 그들의 팔에 가죽끈을 둘렀던지, 두 사람 모두를 순식간에 결박했다. 전자는 제 소중하고 거의 정신을 놓은 짐에 너무 골몰해 있던 터라, 그들의 의도를 그 일이 벌어지기 전에는 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 적대하는 〈델라웨어〉 부족조차 한층 우월한 종족으로 여기던 후자는, 저항 없이 순순히 따랐다. 그러나 앞서 오간 대화가 무슨 말로 이루어졌는지 척후병이 온전히 알아들었던들, 그의 태도가 그토록 고분고분하지는 않았으리라.\n\n〈마구아〉는 제 목적을 실행에 옮기기에 앞서, 회중 전체에 의기양양한 눈길을 던졌다. 사내들이 어떤 저항도 할 수 없음을 알아채고, 그는 가장 귀히 여기는 여인에게로 눈길을 돌렸다. 〈코라〉는 어찌나 차분하고 굳센 눈으로 그의 응시를 맞받았던지, 그의 결심이 흔들렸다. 그러자 그는 앞서 쓰던 술책을 떠올려, 〈앨리스〉가 기대어 선 전사의 팔에서 그녀를 일으켜 세우고, 〈헤이워드〉에게 따라오라 손짓하며, 둘러선 무리에게 길을 트라고 신호했다. 그러나 〈코라〉는 그가 예상한 충동을 좇기는커녕, 족장의 발치로 달려가 목소리를 높여 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"공정하고 존경받는 〈델라웨어〉여, 우리는 자비를 구하여 당신의 지혜와 권능에 기댑니다! 피에 대한 갈증을 채우려 거짓으로 당신의 귀를 더럽히는 저 교활하고 무자비한 괴물에게는 귀를 닫으소서. 오래 사셨고 세상의 악을 보아 오신 당신이라면, 그 재앙을 비참한 자들에게 누그러뜨려 줄 줄 아셔야 합니다.\"\n\n# THE SUPPLICANT 320\n\n노인의 눈이 무겁게 열리며, 그는 다시 한 번 위쪽으로 무리를 바라보았다. 청원자의 꿰뚫는 듯한 목소리가 그의 귀에 부풀어 오르자, 그의 눈은 천천히 그녀의 모습 쪽으로 움직이더니, 마침내 그곳에 한결같은 응시로 머물렀다. 〈코라〉는 무릎을 꿇고, 두 손을 맞쥐어 가슴에 누른 채, 마치 제 성(性)의 아름답고 숨 쉬는 본보기처럼, 빛바랜 그러나 위엄 있는 그의 얼굴을 일종의 거룩한 외경으로 우러르고 있었다. 〈태머넌드〉의 얼굴 표정이 차츰 변하더니, 그 멍함이 감탄 속에 사라지고, 한 세기 전 〈델라웨어〉족의 드넓은 무리에게 그의 젊은 열정을 전하곤 했던 그 명민함의 일부로 환히 밝아 왔다. 부축도 없이, 보아하니 힘들이는 기색도 없이 일어서서, 그는 듣는 이들을 그 단호함으로 놀라게 하는 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"너는 무엇이냐?\"\n\n\"한 여인입니다. 당신이 그리 부르신다면, 미움받는 종족의 하나—〈잉기〉입니다. 그러나 결코 당신을 해친 적 없고, 설령 그러려 한들 당신의 백성을 해칠 수 없는 자, 도움을 청하는 자입니다.\"\n\n\"말해 다오, 내 자식들아.\" 족장이, 그 눈은 여전히 무릎 꿇은 〈코라〉의 모습에 머문 채, 둘레의 이들에게 손짓하며 목쉰 소리로 이었다. \"〈델라웨어〉족은 어디에 진을 쳤느냐?\"\n\n\"〈호리컨〉의 맑은 샘 너머, 〈이로쿼이〉의 산속입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 내 강의 물을 마신 지,\" 현자가 이었다. \"타는 듯한 여름이 숱하게 오고 갔구나. 〈밍쿠언〉의 자식들은 가장 공정한 백인이다. 그러나 그들도 목이 말랐고, 그것을 제 것으로 삼았다. 그들이 우리를 그토록 멀리까지 좇아오는가?\"\n* 〈밍쿠언〉: 〈델라웨어〉족이 펜실베이니아의 창설자 윌리엄 펜을 부르던 이름.\n\n\"우리는 아무도 좇지 않으며, 아무것도 탐하지 않습니다.\" 〈코라〉가 대답했다. \"우리는 우리 뜻에 거슬러 사로잡혀 당신들 가운데로 끌려왔으니, 다만 우리 사람들에게로 평화로이 떠날 허락을 구할 따름입니다. 당신은 이 백성의 아버지요, 재판관이요, 거의 예언자라 할 〈태머넌드〉가 아니십니까?\"\n\n\"나는 오랜 세월을 산 〈태머넌드〉다.\"\n\n\"이제 한 칠 년쯤 되었지요, 당신의 백성 가운데 하나가 이 식민지 변경에서 한 백인 우두머리의 처분에 맡겨졌던 것이. 그는 자신이 선량하고 공정한 〈태머넌드〉의 혈통이라 주장했습니다. '가라.' 백인이 말했지요. '네 조상을 봐서 너는 자유다.' 당신은 그 〈영국〉 전사의 이름을 기억하십니까?\"\n\n\"내가 기억하기로는, 웃음 많던 소년 시절에,\" 족장이, 아득한 노령 특유의 회상으로 대꾸했다. \"내가 바닷가 모래밭에 서서, 백조보다 더 희고 숱한 독수리보다 더 넓은 날개를 단 큰 카누 하나가 떠오르는 해로부터 다가오는 것을 보았느니라.\"\n\n\"아니, 아닙니다. 저는 그토록 까마득한 때를 말하는 것이 아니라, 당신의 가장 젊은 전사의 기억 안에 들어 있을, 제 사람 가운데 하나가 당신의 일족에게 베푼 호의를 말하는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그것은 〈잉기즈〉와 〈더치먼〉이 〈델라웨어〉족의 사냥터를 두고 싸우던 때였더냐? 그때 〈태머넌드〉는 추장이었고, 활을 처음으로 내려놓고 백인의 번개를 든 것이—\"\n\n# THE SUPPLICANT\n\n_코라는 무릎을 꿇고 있었다. 두 손을 맞잡아 가슴에 꼭 댄 채, 그녀는 자기 성(性)의 아름답고 살아 숨 쉬는 본보기처럼 그렇게 있었다_]\n\n\"그것은 다만 어제의 일이었구나.\" 노인이 가슴을 울리는 비애로 받았다. \"〈레나페〉의 자식들이 이 세상의 주인이던 것이. 소금 호수의 물고기들, 새들, 짐승들, 그리고 숲의 〈멩궤〉가 그들을 〈사가모어〉로 받들었느니라.\"\n\n〈코라〉는 실망에 고개를 떨구고, 쓰라린 한순간 제 낙담과 씨름했다. 이윽고 그 풍요로운 얼굴과 빛나는 눈을 들어 올리며, 그녀는 족장 자신의 이 세상 것 아닌 목소리에 못지않게 가슴을 파고드는 어조로 이었다.\n\n\"말씀해 주십시오, 〈태머넌드〉는 아버지입니까?\"\n\n노인은 제 높은 자리에서 그녀를 내려다보며, 야윈 얼굴에 인자한 미소를 띠더니, 이윽고 천천히 회중 전체에 눈길을 던지며 대답했다.\n\n\"한 민족의 아버지로다.\"\n\n\"저 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 구하지 않습니다. 당신과 당신의 일족처럼, 존경받는 추장이여,\" 그녀가 두 손을 발작하듯 제 가슴에 누르고, 타오르는 두 뺨이 어깨 위로 흐트러져 내린 짙고 윤기 나는 머릿단의 미로 속에 거의 가려질 만큼 고개를 떨구며 이었다. \"제 조상의 저주가 그 자식에게 무겁게 내려앉았습니다. 그러나 저기 저 아이는, 지금 이때까지 〈하늘〉의 노여움의 무게를 한 번도 알지 못했습니다. 그녀는, 그 날들이 끝에 가까운 늙고 쇠약한 분의 딸입니다. 그녀를 사랑하고 그녀로 기뻐하는 이가 많고, 아주 많습니다. 그리고 그녀는 너무도 선량하고, 너무도 귀하여, 저 악당의 제물이 되기에는 마땅치 않습니다.\"\n\n\"나는 백인이 오만하고 굶주린 종족임을 안다. 나는 그들이 이 땅을 차지하려 들 뿐 아니라, 제 빛깔의 가장 미천한 자조차 붉은 사람의 〈사쳄〉보다 낫다고 주장함을 안다. 그들 부족의 개와 까마귀들은,\" 진지한 늙은 추장이, 말이 이어질수록 수치에 그 머리가 거의 땅에 짓눌리다시피 한 청원자의 상한 마음에는 아랑곳없이 이었다. \"눈빛이 아닌 피를 가진 여인을 제 천막으로 들이느니 차라리 짖고 까악거릴 것이다. 그러나 그들로 하여금 〈마니투〉의 얼굴 앞에서 너무 큰 소리로 으스대지 못하게 하라. 그들은 해 뜨는 데서 이 땅에 들어왔으니, 해 지는 데서 떠나갈 수도 있으리라. 나는 메뚜기가 나무에서 잎을 모조리 갉아 먹는 것을 자주 보았으나, 꽃피는 철은 언제나 다시 돌아왔다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 〈코라〉가, 마치 황홀경에서 되살아나는 듯 긴 숨을 들이쉬며, 얼굴을 들어 올려 그 빛나는 너울을 뒤로 떨치며 말했으니, 타오르는 눈빛이 죽은 듯 창백한 그 얼굴빛과 어긋났다. \"그러나 어찌하여—그것을 묻는 것은 우리에게 허락되지 않았지요. 아직 당신 앞에 끌려오지 않은 당신의 백성 가운데 하나가 있습니다. 〈휴런〉이 의기양양하게 떠나도록 두시기 전에, 그가 하는 말을 들어 주십시오.\"\n\n〈태머넌드〉가 미심쩍은 듯 둘레를 살피는 것을 보고, 그의 동료 가운데 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"그자는 한 마리 뱀입니다—〈잉기즈〉에게 매수된 붉은 살갗이지요. 우리는 그를 고문하려 붙잡아 두고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그를 데려오라.\" 현자가 대꾸했다.\n\n이윽고 〈태머넌드〉가 다시 한 번 제 자리에 내려앉았고, 젊은이들이 그 단출한 분부를 따르려 채비하는 동안 어찌나 깊은 정적이 감돌았던지, 가벼운 아침 공기의 흐름에 살랑이는 잎새들이 둘레의 숲에서 바스락거리는 소리가 또렷이 들렸다.\n만일 그대들이 나를 거부한다면, 그대들의 법에 침을 뱉으리라!\n\n# CHAPTER XXX\n\n〈베네치아〉의 법령에는 아무런 효력도 없으니,\n나는 판결을 청하노라. 답하라, 내가 그것을 얻겠는가?\n\n베니스의 상인\n\n근심에 찬 여러 분(分) 동안, 사람의 소리라곤 들리지 않는 정적이 이어졌다. 이윽고 물결치던 무리가 갈라졌다가 다시 닫혔고, 〈운카스〉가 살아 있는 원 한가운데 섰다. 자신들의 지혜의 원천인 양 그 현인의 이목구비를 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피던 모든 눈길이 일순간에 돌아서, 이제는 그 포로의 꼿꼿하고 날렵하며 흠 하나 없는 모습에 은밀한 감탄을 보냈다. 그러나 자신이 처한 자리도, 자신에게 쏠린 독차지한 주목도, 이 젊은 모히칸의 침착함을 조금도 흩뜨리지 못했다. 그는 사방을 신중하고 관찰하는 눈길로 둘러보며, 추장들의 얼굴에 잔뜩 드리운 적의의 굳은 표정을, 주의 깊은 아이들의 호기심 어린 응시를 대하는 것과 똑같은 차분함으로 마주했다. 그러나 그 오만한 살핌의 마지막에 〈태머넌드〉의 모습이 그의 시야에 들어오자, 그의 눈은 다른 모든 것을 이미 잊어버린 듯 한곳에 고정되었다. 이윽고 그는 느리고 소리 없는 걸음으로 그 공터를 가로질러 나아가, 현인의 발판 바로 앞에 자리했다. 그는 그곳에 선 채, 자신은 예리하게 관찰하면서도 주목받지 못하다가, 마침내 추장들 중 하나가 현인에게 그의 존재를 알렸다.\n\n\"포로는 어떤 혀로 〈마니토〉에게 말하는가?\" 그 가부장이 두 눈을 감은 채 물었다.\n\n\"제 조상들처럼,\" 〈운카스〉가 대답했다. \"〈델라웨어〉의 혀로 말합니다.\"\n\n이 갑작스럽고 뜻밖의 선언에, 나직하고 사나운 함성이 무리 사이로 번졌으니, 그것은 처음 노여움이 깨어날 때 사자가 내는 으르렁거림에 비겨도 어색하지 않을 만했고, 장차 그 분노의 무게를 예고하는 무서운 전조였다. 그 효과는 현인에게도 똑같이 강렬했으나, 다르게 드러났다. 그는 더없이 수치스러운 광경의 가장 작은 흔적조차 떨쳐 내려는 듯 한 손을 두 눈 앞으로 가져가며, 방금 들은 말을 그 나직하고 목쉰 어조로 되뇌었다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉라니! 나는 〈레나페〉의 부족들이 자기네 평의회의 불가에서 쫓겨나, 무너진 사슴 떼처럼 〈이로쿼이〉의 언덕들 사이로 흩어지는 것을 보고도 살아남았다! 나는 낯선 백성의 도끼가, 하늘의 바람조차 살려 둔 골짜기의 숲을 쓸어버리는 것을 보았다! 산을 달리는 짐승과 나무 위를 나는 새가 사람의 천막 안에서 사는 것을 보았으나, 〈델라웨어〉가 독사처럼 제 민족의 진영으로 기어드는, 그토록 비천한 자를 본 적은 한 번도 없었다.\"\n\n\"노래하는 새들이 제 부리를 열었으니,\" 〈운카스〉가 그 음악 같은 목소리의 가장 부드러운 가락으로 응했다. \"〈태머넌드〉께서 그 노래를 들으셨군요.\"\n\n현인은 흠칫하더니, 스쳐 지나가는 어떤 선율의 덧없는 소리를 붙잡으려는 듯 고개를 한쪽으로 기울였다.\n\n\"〈태머넌드〉가 꿈을 꾸는가!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"내 귀에 닿는 저 목소리는 무엇인가! 겨울들이 뒤로 돌아갔단 말인가! 여름이 〈레나페〉의 자손들에게 다시 오려는가!\"\n\n〈델라웨어〉 예언자의 입에서 터져 나온 이 두서없는 말 뒤로 엄숙하고 공손한 침묵이 이어졌다. 그의 백성은 그 알아들을 수 없는 말을, 그가 그토록 자주 더 높은 지혜와 나눈다고 믿어지는 그 신비로운 교감 중 하나로 한결같이 받아들이며, 외경 속에 그 계시의 결말을 기다렸다. 그러나 인내심 있게 한참을 멈춰 있던 끝에, 노인들 중 하나가 현인이 눈앞에 놓인 문제의 기억을 잃었음을 알아채고, 다시 한번 포로의 존재를 일깨우려 나섰다.\n\n\"저 거짓된 〈델라웨어〉는 〈태머넌드〉의 말을 듣게 될까 두려워 떨고 있습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"〈잉기〉가 흔적을 보여 주면 짖어 대는 사냥개입니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 그대들은,\" 〈운카스〉가 자기 둘레를 준엄하게 둘러보며 응수했다. \"프랑스인이 사슴 내장을 던져 주면 낑낑대는 개들이다!\"\n\n이 신랄한, 그리고 어쩌면 마땅한 반박에, 스무 자루의 칼이 공중에서 번뜩였고 그만큼의 전사들이 벌떡 일어섰다. 그러나 추장들 중 하나의 손짓이 그들의 격분의 분출을 억눌렀고, 다시 고요한 외양을 되돌려 놓았다. 만일 〈태머넌드〉가 보인 어떤 움직임이 그가 다시 말하려 함을 알리지 않았더라면, 그 일은 아마 더 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉여!\" 현인이 말을 이었다. \"너는 네 이름에 값할 자격이 거의 없도다. 내 백성은 여러 겨울 동안 밝은 해를 보지 못했으며, 구름에 가려 있을 때 제 부족을 저버리는 전사는 갑절로 배신자다. 〈마니토〉의 법은 공정하다. 그러하니, 강이 흐르고 산이 서 있는 동안, 나무에 꽃이 피고 지는 동안, 반드시 그러해야 한다. 그는 너희 것이다, 내 자식들아. 그를 정의로이 다루어라.\"\n\n이 마지막 판결의 끝 음절이 〈태머넌드〉의 입술을 떠나기까지, 사지 하나 움직이지 않았고, 여느 때보다 더 크고 긴 숨소리 하나 새어 나오지 않았다. 그러더니 마치 온 민족의 입이 하나로 합친 듯, 복수의 외침이 단번에 터져 나왔으니, 그들의 무자비한 의도를 알리는 무서운 전조였다. 이 길게 이어지는 야만적 함성 한가운데서, 한 추장이 드높은 목소리로, 포로가 불에 의한 고문이라는 무시무시한 시련을 견디도록 선고받았음을 공표했다. 원이 그 질서를 무너뜨렸고, 환희의 비명이 준비의 부산함과 소란에 뒤섞였다. 〈헤이워드〉는 자기를 붙든 자들과 미친 듯이 몸부림쳤고, 〈호크아이〉의 근심 어린 눈은 유달리 진지한 표정으로 주위를 살피기 시작했으며, 〈코라〉는 다시 한번 자비를 구하는 탄원자가 되어 가부장의 발치에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n이 모든 시련의 순간 내내, 〈운카스〉만이 홀로 그 평정을 지켰다. 그는 흔들림 없는 눈으로 준비를 지켜보았고, 고문하는 자들이 그를 붙잡으러 왔을 때, 굳건하고 꼿꼿한 자세로 그들을 맞았다. 그중 하나가, 가능하다면, 제 동료들보다 더 사납고 야만스러웠는데, 젊은 전사의 사냥용 셔츠를 움켜쥐고는 단번에 그 몸에서 찢어 냈다. 그러더니 광적인 환희의 함성을 지르며, 저항하지 않는 제물을 향해 뛰어들어, 그를 화형 기둥으로 끌고 갈 채비를 했다. 그러나 그가 인간의 정(情)에 가장 낯선 자로 보이던 바로 그 순간, 마치 초자연적인 힘이 〈운카스〉를 위해 끼어들기라도 한 듯 그 야만인의 목적이 돌연히 멎었다. 그 〈델라웨어〉의 눈알이 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 입이 벌어졌으며, 온몸이 경악의 자세로 얼어붙었다. 그는 느리고 절제된 동작으로 손을 들어, 한 손가락으로 포로의 가슴을 가리켰다. 그의 동료들이 의아해하며 그의 주위로 몰려들었고, 모든 눈이 그의 눈과 마찬가지로, 포로의 가슴에 선명한 푸른 빛으로 아름답게 문신된 작은 거북의 형상에 골똘히 못 박혔다.\n\n한순간 〈운카스〉는 그 광경을 향해 차분히 미소 지으며 자신의 승리를 만끽했다. 그러더니 드높고 오만한 팔의 휘두름으로 무리를 물리치고, 한 왕의 기품으로 민족 앞에 나아가, 무리 사이로 번지는 감탄의 웅성거림보다 더 큰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"〈레니 레나페〉의 사람들이여!\" 그가 말했다. \"내 종족은 대지를 떠받친다! 너희의 미약한 부족은 내 등딱지 위에 서 있다! 〈델라웨어〉가 지필 수 있는 그 어떤 불이 내 조상들의 자식을 태우겠는가,\" 그가 제 살갗 위의 단순한 문장을 자랑스레 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"그런 혈통에서 나온 피는 너희의 불길을 꺼 버리리라! 내 종족은 뭇 민족의 할아버지다!\"\n\n\"너는 누구냐?\" 〈태머넌드〉가, 포로의 말이 전하는 어떤 뜻에서보다도 자신이 들은 그 놀라운 어조에 더, 몸을 일으키며 물었다.\n\n\"〈친가치국〉의 아들 〈운카스〉입니다,\" 포로가 겸손히 대답하며, 민족에게서 돌아서, 상대의 인품과 연륜에 경의를 표하며 고개를 숙였다. \"위대한 〈우나미스〉의 아들입니다.\"\n\n\"〈태머넌드〉의 시각이 가까웠다!\" 현인이 외쳤다. \"마침내 그 밤에 날이 밝았도다! 내 자리를 평의회의 불가에서 채울 자가 여기 있음을 〈마니토〉께 감사드린다. 〈운카스〉의 자식 〈운카스〉를 찾았다! 죽어 가는 독수리의 눈이 떠오르는 해를 바라보게 하라.\"\n\n그 젊은이는 가볍게, 그러나 자랑스럽게 단상으로 올라섰으니, 그곳에서 그는 동요하며 경이로워하는 온 무리에게 모습을 드러냈다. 〈태머넌드〉는 그를 한참 동안 제 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓고는, 행복했던 날들을 떠올리는 자의 지칠 줄 모르는 응시로, 그 고운 얼굴의 이목구비 하나하나를 읽었다.\n\n\"〈태머넌드〉가 소년인가?\" 마침내 어리둥절해진 예언자가 외쳤다. \"내가 그토록 많은 눈[雪]을 꿈꾸었던가 — 내 백성이 떠도는 모래처럼 흩어지고 — 〈잉기〉들이 나무의 잎사귀보다 더 많은 것을! 〈태머넌드〉의 화살은 새끼 사슴조차 놀라게 하지 못하고, 그의 팔은 죽은 참나무 가지처럼 시들었으며, 달팽이가 달음박질에서 더 빠를 것이다. 그런데도 〈운카스〉는, 창백한 얼굴들에 맞서 싸우러 가던 그때 그 모습 그대로 그의 앞에 있구나! 〈운카스〉, 제 부족의 표범, 〈레나페〉의 맏아들, 모히칸의 가장 지혜로운 〈세이가모어〉! 말하라, 〈델라웨어〉들이여, 〈태머넌드〉가 백 번의 겨울 동안 잠든 자였더란 말이냐?\"\n\n이 말 뒤에 이어진 차분하고 깊은 침묵은, 그의 백성이 가부장의 전언을 받아들인 그 외경의 두려움을 충분히 알리고도 남았다. 무슨 일이 뒤따를지 숨죽인 기대 속에 모두 귀를 기울였으나, 감히 답하는 자는 없었다. 그러나 〈운카스〉는, 총애받는 자식의 다정함과 흠모로 그의 얼굴을 바라보며, 자신의 드높고 인정받은 지위를 빌려, 답해도 좋으리라 여겼다.\n\n\"〈태머넌드〉의 벗이 전장에서 제 백성을 이끌던 이래로,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 종족의 전사 넷이 살다가 죽었습니다. 거북의 피가 여러 추장에게 흘렀으나, 〈친가치국〉과 그 아들을 빼고는 모두 그들이 나온 대지로 돌아갔습니다.\"\n\n\"옳다 — 옳다,\" 현인이 응했으니, 한 줄기 기억의 섬광이 그의 모든 흐뭇한 환상을 깨뜨리고, 그를 단번에 제 민족의 참된 내력에 대한 자각으로 되돌려 놓았다. \"우리 현자들은 변치 않은 종족의 전사 둘이 〈잉기〉들의 언덕에 있다고 자주 말했다. 어찌하여 〈델라웨어〉의 평의회 불가에 그들의 자리가 그토록 오래 비어 있었느냐?\"\n\n이 말에 그 젊은이는, 경의를 표하느라 아직 조금 숙이고 있던 고개를 들었다. 그러고는 마치 제 가문의 방책을 단숨에 그리고 영원히 설명하려는 듯, 무리에게 들리도록 목소리를 높여, 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"한때 우리는 짠 호수가 노여움에 차 말하는 소리를 들을 수 있는 곳에서 잠들었습니다. 그때 우리는 그 땅의 지배자요 〈세이가모어〉였습니다. 그러나 시내마다 창백한 얼굴이 보이게 되자, 우리는 사슴을 따라 우리 민족의 강으로 돌아갔습니다. 〈델라웨어〉들은 떠나고 없었습니다. 그들 모두 중에 자기네가 사랑하던 물줄기를 마시러 남은 전사는 몇 되지 않았습니다. 그때 제 조상들이 말씀하셨습니다. '여기서 우리는 사냥하리라. 이 강의 물은 짠 호수로 흘러든다. 우리가 지는 해를 향해 가면, 단물의 큰 호수들로 흘러드는 시내를 찾으리니, 거기서 모히칸은, 맑은 샘에서 바다의 물고기처럼 죽으리라. 〈마니토〉께서 준비되시어 \"오라\" 하시면, 우리는 강을 따라 바다로 가서, 우리 것을 되찾으리라.' 〈델라웨어〉들이여, 거북의 자손들이 믿는 바가 그러합니다. 우리의 눈은 떠오르는 해에 있지, 지는 해를 향하지 않습니다. 우리는 그가 어디서 오는지는 알아도, 어디로 가는지는 모릅니다. 그것으로 충분합니다.\"\n\n〈레나페〉의 사람들은 미신이 더할 수 있는 온갖 경의로 그의 말에 귀를 기울이며, 그 젊은 〈세이가모어〉가 제 생각을 전하는 비유적인 언어에서조차 은밀한 매혹을 느꼈다. 〈운카스〉 자신은 영민한 눈으로 자신의 간략한 설명이 미친 효과를 지켜보다가, 청중이 흡족해함을 알아채자 점차 자신이 취했던 권위의 기색을 내려놓았다. 그러더니 〈태머넌드〉의 높은 자리를 에워싼 말없는 군중 위로 시선을 거닐게 하다가, 비로소 결박당한 〈호크아이〉를 알아보았다. 그는 자기 자리에서 다급히 내려서서, 제 벗의 곁으로 길을 헤치고 나아가, 자신의 칼을 빠르고 성난 일격으로 휘둘러 그 가죽끈을 끊고는, 무리에게 갈라서라고 손짓했다. 인디언들은 말없이 따랐고, 그가 그들 사이에 나타나기 전처럼 다시 한번 원을 지어 늘어섰다. 〈운카스〉는 척후의 손을 잡고, 그를 가부장의 발치로 이끌었다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 창백한 얼굴을 보십시오. 정의로운 사람이요, 〈델라웨어〉의 벗입니다.\"\n\n\"그는 〈민쿠언〉의 아들인가?\"\n\n\"그렇지 않습니다. 〈잉기〉들에게 알려졌고, 〈마쿠아〉들이 두려워하는 전사입니다.\"\n\n\"그는 제 행적으로 어떤 이름을 얻었느냐?\"\n\n\"우리는 그를 〈호크아이〉라 부릅니다,\" 〈운카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉 말을 써서 대답했다. \"그의 시야는 결코 어긋나는 법이 없기 때문입니다. 〈밍고〉들은 그가 자기네 전사들에게 안기는 죽음으로 그를 더 잘 압니다. 그들에게 그는 '긴 소총'입니다.\"\n\n\"〈라 롱그 카라빈〉!\" 〈태머넌드〉가 눈을 뜨고 척후를 준엄하게 바라보며 외쳤다. \"내 아들이 그를 벗이라 부른 것은 잘한 일이 아니다.\"\n\n\"저는 스스로 그러함을 증명하는 자를 그렇게 부릅니다,\" 젊은 추장이 더없이 차분하나 굳건한 태도로 응했다. \"〈운카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉들 사이에서 환영받는다면, 〈호크아이〉 또한 제 벗들과 함께 있는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그 창백한 얼굴은 내 젊은이들을 죽였다. 그의 이름은 〈레나페〉에게 가한 일격들로 자자하다.\"\n\n\"〈밍고〉가 그만큼을 〈델라웨어〉의 귀에 속삭였다면, 그는 그저 노래하는 새임을 보였을 뿐입니다,\" 척후가 말했으니, 이제 그러한 모욕적인 비난에서 스스로를 변호할 때라 여긴 그는, 자신이 말 건네는 자의 혀로 말하되, 그 인디언식 비유를 자기 특유의 관념으로 손질해 가며 말했다. \"내가 〈마쿠아〉들을 죽였다는 것은, 그들 자신의 평의회 불가에서라도 부인할 내가 아니오. 그러나 알고서, 내 손이 단 한 번이라도 〈델라웨어〉를 해친 적이 있다는 것은, 그들과 그들 민족에 속한 모든 것에 우호적인, 내 천성의 이치에 어긋나는 일이오.\"\n\n전사들 사이로 나직한 갈채의 탄성이 번졌으니, 그들은 비로소 자기네 잘못을 깨닫기 시작한 자들처럼 서로 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n\"그 〈휴런〉은 어디 있느냐?\" 〈태머넌드〉가 물었다. \"그가 내 귀를 막았느냐?\"\n\n〈운카스〉가 승리를 거둔 그 장면 동안 〈머과〉가 느낀 심정이란, 묘사하기보다는 짐작하는 편이 훨씬 나을 것이거니와, 그는 가부장 앞으로 대담하게 걸어 나와 그 부름에 답했다.\n\n\"정의로운 〈태머넌드〉께서는,\" 그가 말했다. \"〈휴런〉이 빌려준 것을 차지하지 않으실 것입니다.\"\n\n\"말하라, 내 형제의 아들아,\" 현인이 〈르 쉬브틸〉의 어두운 얼굴을 피하고, 〈운카스〉의 더 솔직한 이목구비로 기꺼이 돌아서며 응했다. \"저 이방인이 네게 정복자의 권리를 지녔느냐?\"\n\n\"그에게는 아무 권리도 없습니다. 표범이 여인들이 놓은 덫에 걸려들 수는 있어도, 그는 강하여 그것을 뛰어넘어 빠져나갈 줄 압니다.\"\n\n\"〈라 롱그 카라빈〉은?\"\n\n\"〈밍고〉들을 비웃습니다. 가라, 〈휴런〉아, 곰의 빛깔이 어떤지 네 아낙들에게 물어보아라.\"\n\n\"내 진영으로 함께 들어온 그 이방인과 흰 처녀는?\"\n\n\"트인 길로 나아가야 합니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 〈휴런〉이 내 전사들에게 맡긴 그 여인은?\"\n\n〈운카스〉는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그러니까 〈밍고〉가 내 진영으로 데려온 그 여인 말이다,\" 〈태머넌드〉가 엄숙하게 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그녀는 내 것입니다,\" 〈머과〉가 〈운카스〉를 향해 의기양양하게 손을 흔들며 외쳤다. \"모히칸, 너는 그녀가 내 것임을 알 것이다.\"\n\n\"내 아들이 말이 없구나,\" 〈태머넌드〉가, 그 젊은이가 슬픔에 잠겨 제게서 돌린 얼굴의 표정을 읽으려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"그러합니다,\" 나직한 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n짧고도 묵직한 침묵이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 무리가 〈밍고〉의 주장의 정당함을 얼마나 마지못해 인정하는지가 똑똑히 드러났다. 마침내, 그 결정이 오로지 자신에게만 달린 현인이 굳은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉이여, 떠나라.\"\n\n\"제가 온 그대로입니까, 정의로운 〈태머넌드〉여,\" 교활한 〈머과〉가 물었다. \"아니면 〈델라웨어〉들의 신의로 두 손을 가득 채워서입니까? 〈르 르나르 쉬브틸〉의 천막은 비어 있습니다. 그를 그의 것으로 강하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n노인은 잠시 혼자 생각에 잠겼다. 그러더니 그 존엄한 동료들 중 하나에게 고개를 기울이며 물었다.\n\n\"내 귀가 열려 있는가?\"\n\n\"그러합니다.\"\n\n\"이 〈밍고〉가 추장인가?\"\n\n\"제 민족에서 으뜸가는 자입니다.\"\n\n\"처녀여, 너는 무엇을 바라느냐? 위대한 전사가 너를 아내로 맞으려 한다. 가라! 네 혈통은 끊기지 않으리라.\"\n\n\"그런 치욕을 당하느니,\" 공포에 사로잡힌 〈코라〉가 외쳤다. \"천 번이라도 끊기는 편이 낫습니다!\"\n\n\"〈휴런〉이여, 그녀의 마음은 제 조상들의 천막에 있다. 마음 없는 처녀는 불행한 천막을 만든다.\"\n\n\"그녀는 제 백성의 혀로 말합니다,\" 〈머과〉가 쓰디쓴 빈정거림의 눈길로 제 제물을 바라보며 응했다. \"그녀는 장사치 종족이라, 밝은 눈빛 하나를 두고도 흥정할 것입니다. 〈태머넌드〉께서 그 말씀을 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"〈웜펌〉과 우리의 사랑을 가져가라.\"\n\n\"〈머과〉가 여기 가져온 것 말고는 아무것도 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 네 것을 가지고 떠나라. 위대한 〈마니토〉께서는 〈델라웨어〉가 불의해지는 것을 금하신다.\"\n\n〈머과〉가 앞으로 나아가, 제 포로의 팔을 억세게 움켜쥐었다. 〈델라웨어〉들은 말없이 물러섰고, 〈코라〉는 항변이 헛되리라는 것을 자각한 듯, 저항 없이 제 운명에 따를 채비를 했다.\n\n\"멈추시오, 멈추시오!\" 〈덩컨〉이 앞으로 뛰어나오며 외쳤다. \"〈휴런〉, 자비를 베푸시오! 그녀의 몸값이 당신을, 당신네 백성 중 일찍이 알려진 그 누구보다도 부유하게 만들어 줄 것이오.\"\n\n\"〈머과〉는 붉은 살갗을 지닌 자다. 창백한 얼굴들의 구슬 따위는 바라지 않는다.\"\n\n\"금이든, 은이든, 화약이든, 납이든 — 전사에게 필요한 모든 것이 당신 천막에 있게 될 것이오. 가장 위대한 추장에게 걸맞은 모든 것이 말이오.\"\n\n\"〈르 쉬브틸〉은 매우 강하다,\" 〈머과〉가 〈코라〉의 저항 없는 팔을 움켜쥔 손을 거칠게 흔들며 외쳤다. \"그는 복수를 이루었다!\"\n\n\"섭리를 다스리시는 전능하신 분이여!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 고뇌 속에 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"이런 일을 어찌 용납하실 수 있단 말입니까! 당신께, 정의로운 〈태머넌드〉여, 저는 자비를 호소합니다.\"\n\n\"〈델라웨어〉의 말은 끝났다,\" 현인이 두 눈을 감고 제 자리로 다시 주저앉으며 응했으니, 정신의 노고와 육신의 노고에 똑같이 지쳐 있었다. \"사람은 두 번 말하지 않는다.\"\n\n\"추장이 한번 내뱉은 말을 거두느라 제 시간을 허비해서는 안 된다는 것은 지혜롭고 사리에 맞는 말이오,\" 〈호크아이〉가 〈덩컨〉에게 잠자코 있으라 손짓하며 말했다. \"그러나 어느 전사든 제 포로의 머리에 손도끼를 박기 전에 잘 헤아려 보는 것 또한 신중한 일이오. 〈휴런〉, 나는 당신을 좋아하지 않소. 또한 어떤 〈밍고〉가 내 손에서 큰 호의를 받은 적이 있다고도 말할 수 없소. 이 전쟁이 곧 끝나지 않는다면, 당신네 전사들이 숲에서 나를 더 많이 만나게 되리라 짐작하는 것이 온당하오. 그러니 당신의 판단에 맡겨 보시오. 저런 포로를 당신 진영으로 데려가는 편이 낫겠소, 아니면 나 같은 자를, 곧 당신네 민족이 맨손으로 보면 크게 기뻐할 그런 사내를 데려가는 편이 낫겠소?\"\n\n\"'긴 소총'이 그 여인을 위해 제 목숨을 내놓겠다는 말이냐?\" 〈머과〉가 머뭇거리며 물었으니, 그는 이미 제 제물을 데리고 그 자리를 뜨려는 몸짓을 한 터였다.\n\n\"아니, 아니. 그렇게까지 말한 건 아니오,\" 〈호크아이〉가, 〈머과〉가 자신의 제안에 귀 기울이는 그 열의를 알아채고는, 걸맞은 신중함으로 물러서며 응했다. \"한창때라 쓸모 많은 전사를, 변경에서 가장 빼어난 여인과 맞바꾼다면 공평치 못한 거래일 테니. 다만, 당신이 그 처녀를 풀어 준다는 조건이라면, 지금 — 적어도 잎이 물들기 여섯 주 전인 지금 — 겨울 막사로 들어가는 데는 동의할 수도 있겠소.\"\n\n〈머과〉는 고개를 가로젓고, 무리에게 길을 비키라고 초조한 신호를 보냈다.\n\n\"음, 그렇다면,\" 척후가 아직 마음을 반도 정하지 못한 자의 생각에 잠긴 기색으로 덧붙였다. \"흥정에 '킬디어'까지 얹겠소. 노련한 사냥꾼의 말을 믿으시오. 이 총은 이 지방들을 통틀어 견줄 것이 없소.\"\n\n〈머과〉는 여전히 답하기를 마다하며, 무리를 흩어지게 하려는 노력을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"어쩌면,\" 척후가, 상대가 그 교환에 무관심을 드러내는 그만큼에 정확히 비례해, 짐짓 꾸민 침착함을 잃어 가며 덧붙였다. \"당신네 젊은이들에게 그 무기의 참된 묘를 가르쳐 주겠다고 약조한다면, 우리 판단 사이의 그 사소한 차이가 매끄러워질지도 모르겠소.\"\n\n〈르 르나르〉는, 그가 그 우호적인 제안에 귀 기울이기를 바라며 여전히 꿰뚫을 수 없는 띠를 이루어 그를 둘러싼 채 머뭇거리는 〈델라웨어〉들에게, 제 길을 비키라고 사납게 명했으니, 눈빛 하나로 그들 '예언자'의 어김없는 정의에 다시 호소하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다.\n\n\"명해진 것은 늦든 빠르든 닥치고야 마는 법이지,\" 〈호크아이〉가 슬프고 풀죽은 얼굴로 〈운카스〉에게 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"저 악당은 제 잇속을 알고, 그것을 놓치지 않을 게다! 신의 가호가 있기를, 얘야. 너는 네 타고난 혈육 사이에서 벗들을 찾았으니, 인디언의 피라곤 섞이지 않았으면서도 네가 만난 어떤 이들만큼이나 그들이 참되기를 바란다. 나로 말하면, 늦든 빠르든 죽어야 할 몸. 그러니 내 죽음을 곡할 자가 몇 안 되는 것이 다행이지. 어쨌거나 저 악귀들은 어떻게든 내 머릿가죽을 차지하고 말았을 테니, 하루이틀 차이야 영원한 시간의 셈에서 그리 큰 차이도 아닐 게다. 신의 가호가 있기를,\" 그 거친 숲사람이 고개를 한쪽으로 숙였다가, 이내 다시 방향을 바꿔 그 젊은이를 향해 애틋한 눈길을 던지며 덧붙였다. \"나는 너와 네 아비를 둘 다 사랑했다, 〈운카스〉. 우리 살갗이 온전히 같은 빛깔은 아니고, 우리 천성이 다소 다르긴 해도 말이다. 〈세이가모어〉에게 전해라, 내 가장 큰 곤경 속에서도 그를 결코 시야에서 놓치지 않았다고. 그리고 너로 말하면, 운 좋은 흔적을 좇을 때면 가끔 나를 떠올려라. 그리고 명심해라, 얘야, 천국이 하나든 둘이든, 저세상에는 정직한 사람들이 다시 만날 수 있는 길이 있단다. 소총은 우리가 숨겨 둔 자리에서 찾게 될 게다. 그것을 가지거라, 그리고 나를 위해 간직해라. 그리고 들어라, 얘야, 네 타고난 천성이 복수를 마다하지 않으니, 〈밍고〉들에게 그것을 좀 거리낌 없이 쓰거라. 그것이 나를 잃은 슬픔을 덜고, 네 마음을 가볍게 해 줄지도 모른다. 〈휴런〉, 나는 당신의 제안을 받아들이겠소. 그 여인을 풀어 주시오. 내가 당신의 포로요!\"\n\n이 너그러운 제안에, 억눌렸으되 여전히 또렷한 찬동의 웅성거림이 무리 사이로 번졌으니, 〈델라웨어〉 전사들 중 가장 사나운 자들마저 그 의도된 희생의 사내다움에 흡족함을 드러냈다. 〈머과〉는 멈칫했고, 한순간 근심에 차, 그가 망설였다고 해도 좋을 듯했다. 그러더니 흉포함과 감탄이 기이하게 뒤섞인 표정으로 〈코라〉에게 눈길을 던지자, 그의 목적은 영원히 굳어졌다.\n\n그는 고개를 뒤로 젖히는 동작으로 그 제안에 대한 경멸을 내비치며, 굳건하고 확고한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"〈르 르나르 쉬브틸〉은 위대한 추장이다. 그에게는 오직 하나의 마음뿐. 가자,\" 그가 제 포로를 앞으로 재촉하느라 너무 허물없이 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다. \"〈휴런〉은 수다쟁이가 아니다. 우리는 가겠다.\"\n\n처녀는 드높은 여인의 자존심으로 몸을 뒤로 빼었고, 그 모욕에 검은 눈이 타올랐으며, 짙은 피가 스쳐 지나가는 햇살처럼 그녀의 관자놀이까지 솟구쳤다.\n\n\"나는 당신의 포로이며, 때가 되면 죽음까지라도 따를 준비가 되어 있소. 그러나 폭력은 필요치 않소,\" 그녀가 차갑게 말했다. 그러고는 곧장 〈호크아이〉에게 돌아서서 덧붙였다. \"너그러운 사냥꾼이여! 진심으로 당신께 감사드립니다. 당신의 제안은 헛되며, 받아들여질 수도 없을 것입니다. 그러나 당신은 그 고귀한 뜻에서보다도 더, 저를 도와주실 수 있습니다. 저 풀이 죽어 고개 숙인 아이를 보십시오! 그 아이를 문명한 사람들의 거처에 데려다 놓을 때까지 결코 저버리지 마십시오. 그 아이의 아버지가 당신께 보답하리라고는 — 당신 같은 분은 사람의 보답을 초월한 분이니 — 말하지 않겠습니다,\" 그녀가 척후의 거친 손을 부여잡으며 말했다. \"그러나 그분은 당신께 감사하고, 당신을 축복하실 것입니다. 그리고 믿어 주십시오, 정의로운 노인의 축복은 하늘이 보시기에 효험이 있습니다. 이 끔찍한 순간에, 그분의 입에서 그 한마디를 들을 수만 있다면!\" 그녀의 목소리가 메었고, 한순간 그녀는 말이 없었다. 그러더니 정신을 잃은 제 동생을 부축하고 있는 〈덩컨〉에게 한 걸음 더 가까이 다가서며, 한층 가라앉은 어조로, 그러나 그 안에서 감정과 여인다운 습성이 두려운 다툼을 벌이는 채로 말을 이었다. \"당신이 갖게 될 그 보물을 소중히 아끼라는 말은 굳이 할 필요도 없겠지요. 당신은 그 아이를 사랑하십니다, 〈헤이워드〉. 그것이, 설령 그 아이에게 결점이 있다 한들, 천 가지 허물을 가려 줄 것입니다. 그 아이는 인간으로서 그럴 수 있는 한 친절하고, 온화하고, 다정하고, 선합니다. 마음에도 몸에도, 당신네 가운데 가장 자존심 높은 이조차 역겨워할 흠이라곤 하나도 없습니다. 그 아이는 곱습니다 — 아! 더없이 빼어나게 곱지요!\" 그녀가 제 아름다우나 덜 눈부신 손을 〈앨리스〉의 설화석고 같은 이마에 우수에 찬 애정으로 얹고, 그 눈썹가에 엉긴 금빛 머리칼을 갈라 주며 말했다. \"그러면서도 그 아이의 영혼은 그 살갗만큼이나 순수하고 티가 없습니다! 저는 많은 말을 할 수도 있겠지요 — 어쩌면, 더 냉철한 이성이 마땅하다 여길 것보다 더 많은 말을. 그러나 당신과 저 자신을 아끼겠습니다 ─\" 그녀의 목소리는 들리지 않게 되었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 동생의 몸 위로 숙여졌다. 길고 뜨거운 입맞춤 뒤에, 그녀는 일어섰다. 그러고는 죽음의 빛을 띤 얼굴로, 그러나 열에 들뜬 눈에 눈물 한 방울 없이, 돌아서서, 예전의 그 드높은 태도를 고스란히 되찾고 그 야만인에게 덧붙였다. \"자, 그럼, 그러는 것이 당신 뜻이라면, 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 가라,\" 〈덩컨〉이 〈앨리스〉를 한 인디언 처녀의 품에 맡기며 외쳤다. \"가라, 〈머과〉, 가라. 이 〈델라웨어〉들에게는 그들의 법이 있어 너를 붙잡는 것을 금하지만, 나는 — 나는 그런 의무가 없다. 가라, 악독한 괴물아 — 어찌하여 꾸물대느냐?\"\n\n따라오겠다는 이 위협을 〈머과〉가 어떤 표정으로 들었는지는 묘사하기 어려울 것이다. 처음에는 사납고도 또렷한 환희의 빛이 있었으나, 이내 교활한 냉담의 눈빛 속으로 즉시 가라앉았다.\n\n\"숲은 열려 있다,\" 그는 이렇게 답하는 것으로 만족했다. \"'펼친 손'은 와도 좋다.\"\n\n\"멈추시오,\" 〈호크아이〉가 〈덩컨〉의 팔을 붙잡고 억지로 붙들어 두며 외쳤다. \"당신은 저 악귀의 간계를 모르오. 그는 당신을 매복지로, 당신의 죽음으로 이끌 것이오 ─\"\n\n\"〈휴런〉,\" 제 민족의 준엄한 관습에 순종하여, 오가는 모든 말에 주의 깊고 진중한 청자로 있던 〈운카스〉가 끼어들었다. \"〈휴런〉, 〈델라웨어〉들의 정의는 〈마니토〉에게서 온다. 해를 보아라. 그것은 지금 솔송나무 윗가지에 있다. 네 길은 짧고 트여 있다. 그것이 나무들 위로 보이게 될 때, 네 흔적을 좇는 자들이 있을 것이다.\"\n\n\"내게 까마귀 우는 소리가 들리는구나!\" 〈머과〉가 조롱하는 웃음과 함께 외쳤다. \"가라!\" 그가, 제 통행을 받아들이려 천천히 갈라진 무리를 향해 손을 흔들며 덧붙였다. \"〈델라웨어〉들의 치마는 어디 있느냐! 자기네 화살과 총일랑 〈와이언도트〉들에게 보내라지. 먹을 사슴 고기와 일굴 옥수수밭이나 얻을 테니. 개들아, 토끼들아, 도둑놈들아 — 너희에게 침을 뱉는다!\"\n\n그가 떠나며 던진 빈정거림은 죽은 듯한, 불길한 침묵 속에 받아들여졌고, 이 신랄한 말을 입에 문 채, 의기양양한 〈머과〉는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 숲속으로 들어섰으니, 무력해진 제 포로를 거느리고, 인디언 환대의 침범할 수 없는 법에 보호받으며.\n* 〈운카스〉가 말하는 \"내 등딱지\"는 거북을 뜻하는 토템 문장으로, 모히칸의 거북 씨족이 만물의 기원을 떠받친다는 부족 신화를 가리킨다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI\n\n플루.—애들이고 짐바리고 다 죽이다니! 이는 전쟁의 법도에 명백히 어긋나는 일이오. 똑똑히 보시오, 세상에 이보다 더 뻔뻔스러운 악당 짓은 없을 것이오.\n\n헨리 5세\n\n적과 그 희생자가 시야에 머무는 동안, 그 무리는 〈휴런〉족에게 우호적인 어떤 힘에 의해 그 자리에 묶인 듯 미동도 없이 서 있었다. 그러나 그가 사라지는 순간, 무리는 사납고 거센 격정에 들끓고 요동치기 시작했다. 〈언카스〉는 그 높은 자리를 지키며, 〈코라〉의 옷빛이 숲의 나뭇잎과 한데 섞일 때까지 그 모습에서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 이윽고 그는 내려와, 무리 사이를 소리 없이 지나, 조금 전 나왔던 바로 그 오두막 안으로 사라졌다. 지나가는 젊은 추장의 눈에서 번뜩이는 분노의 빛을 알아챈 진중하고 주의 깊은 몇몇 전사가, 그가 사색을 위해 택한 곳까지 그를 뒤따랐다. 그런 다음 〈터메넌드〉와 〈앨리스〉가 물러갔고, 여자와 아이들에게는 흩어지라는 명이 내렸다. 그 뒤를 이은 중대한 한 시간 동안, 진영은 어지러워진 벌집을 닮았으니, 우두머리가 나타나 본보기를 보여 멀고 중대한 비상을 떠날 그 순간만을 기다리는 벌떼와도 같았다.\n\n마침내 한 젊은 전사가 〈언카스〉의 오두막에서 나와, 일종의 엄숙한 행진과도 같은 신중한 걸음으로, 바위 단의 갈라진 틈에서 자란 키 작은 소나무를 향해 다가가, 그 몸통에서 껍질을 벗겨 내고는, 말 한마디 없이 왔던 곳으로 돌아갔다. 곧이어 또 다른 자가 뒤따라, 그 어린나무의 가지를 모조리 쳐 내어, 헐벗고 껍질이 벗겨진 줄기만을 남겼다. 셋째 자는 그 기둥을 짙은 붉은 칠로 줄무늬를 그어 물들였다. 부족의 우두머리들이 품은 적대적 의도를 알리는 이 모든 표시를, 바깥의 사내들은 음울하고 불길한 침묵 속에 받아들였다. 마침내 〈모히칸〉족 청년 자신이 다시 모습을 드러냈으니, 허리띠와 각반을 빼고는 모든 의복을 벗어 버렸고, 그 잘생긴 얼굴의 절반은 위협하는 검은 구름 아래 감추어져 있었다.\n\n〈언카스〉는 느리고 위엄 있는 걸음으로 그 기둥을 향해 나아갔고, 곧장 절도 있는 발걸음으로 그 둘레를 돌기 시작했으니, 그것은 고대의 춤과도 같았으며, 동시에 그 목소리를 높여 거칠고 불규칙한 전쟁 노래를 읊었다. 그 가락은 인간이 내는 소리의 양 극단에 걸쳐 있었다. 때로는 우수에 잠겨 더없이 애처로워, 새들의 선율에 견줄 만하다가도, 돌연하고 소스라치는 전환으로, 그 깊이와 기운에 듣는 이를 떨게 만들었다. 노랫말은 적고 자주 되풀이되었으니, 일종의 기원, 곧 신을 향한 찬가에서 점차 나아가, 전사의 목적을 내비치고, 시작과 같이 위대한 정령에 대한 자신의 의탁을 고백하는 것으로 끝맺었다. 그가 노래한 그 함축적이고 선율적인 말을 옮길 수만 있다면, 그 노래는 다음과 같이 읽힐 법했다.\n\n\"마니투! 마니투! 마니투! 당신은 위대하시고, 선하시고, 지혜로우시니. 마니투! 마니투! 당신은 의로우시도다.\n\n하늘에서, 구름 속에서, 오, 내가 보네 많은 점을—검은 것 많고, 붉은 것 많네. 하늘에서, 오, 내가 보네 많은 구름을.\n\n숲속에서, 공중에서, 오, 내가 듣네 함성을, 긴 외침을, 그리고 울부짖음을. 숲속에서, 오, 내가 듣네 요란한 함성을!\n\n마니투! 마니투! 마니투! 당신은 약하시고—당신은 강하시니, 나는 더디네. 마니투! 마니투! 내게 도움을 주소서.\"\n\n각 연이라 할 만한 것의 끝에서, 그는 한 음을 여느 때보다 크고 길게 끌어올려 잠시 멈추었으니, 그것은 방금 토해 낸 정서에 유달리 잘 어울렸다. 첫 마무리는 엄숙하여, 경외의 뜻을 전하려는 것이었다. 둘째는 묘사하는 것으로, 경각심을 일으키는 데까지 닿았다. 그리고 셋째는, 전투의 온갖 무서운 소리를 한데 합친 듯, 그 젊은 전사의 입술에서 터져 나온, 저 잘 알려지고 끔찍한 전쟁의 함성이었다. 마지막은 첫째와 같이 겸손하고 간청하는 것이었다. 그는 이 노래를 세 번 되풀이했고, 그 횟수만큼 춤추며 기둥의 둘레를 돌았다.\n\n첫 바퀴가 끝나자, 〈레나페〉족의 진중하고 크게 존경받는 한 추장이 그 본보기를 따랐으니, 다만 그 노랫말은 자신의 것이되 가락은 비슷한 성격이었다. 전사가 차례차례 춤에 가담하여, 마침내 명망과 권위가 있는 자는 모두 그 소용돌이 속에 헤아려졌다. 이제 그 광경은 사납고도 끔찍해졌다. 추장들의 사납고 위협적인 얼굴은, 그들이 목구멍에서 나오는 소리를 한데 섞어 넣는 그 소름 끼치는 가락으로부터 한층 더한 힘을 얻었다. 바로 그때 〈언카스〉가 도끼를 기둥에 깊이 내리찍고, 목소리를 높여 외침을 토하니, 그것은 그 자신의 전투 함성이라 할 만했다. 이 행동은 그가 계획된 원정에서 우두머리의 권한을 떠맡았음을 알리는 것이었다.\n\n그것은 한 부족의 잠든 격정을 모두 일깨우는 신호였다. 그때까지 어린 나이의 수줍음에 억눌려 있던 백 명의 젊은이가, 적을 본뜬 그 상징물을 향해 광란의 무리를 이루어 달려들어, 조각조각 쪼개어, 마침내 그 줄기에서 땅속의 뿌리 말고는 아무것도 남지 않을 때까지 산산이 갈라놓았다. 이 소란의 순간 동안, 전쟁의 가장 무자비한 행위들이 그 나무 조각들 위에서 행해졌으니, 그것이 마치 자신들의 잔혹함에 희생된 살아 있는 제물이기라도 한 듯한 흉포함이 엿보였다. 더러는 머릿가죽이 벗겨졌고, 더러는 날카롭고 떨리는 도끼를 받았으며, 또 더러는 치명적인 칼에 찔리는 고초를 겪었다. 요컨대, 열의와 사나운 환희의 표출이 어찌나 크고 명백했던지, 그 원정은 부족 전체의 전쟁으로 선포되기에 이르렀다.\n\n〈언카스〉가 일격을 가한 그 순간, 그는 원 밖으로 나와, 두 눈을 들어 해를 보았으니, 해는 〈매과〉와의 휴전이 끝나기로 된 바로 그 지점에 막 다다르고 있었다. 그 사실은 곧, 거기에 어울리는 외침을 곁들인 의미심장한 몸짓으로 알려졌다. 흥분한 무리 전체가 즐거움의 날카로운 함성과 함께 그 모의 전쟁을 그만두고, 더 위태로운 실전의 시험을 준비하기 시작했다.\n\n진영의 면면은 순식간에 바뀌었다. 이미 무장하고 칠을 한 전사들은, 어떤 비범한 감정의 폭발도 할 줄 모르는 듯 고요해졌다. 한편 여자들은 오두막에서 쏟아져 나오며, 기쁨의 노래와 비탄의 노래를 어찌나 기이하게 뒤섞었던지, 어느 격정이 더 우세한지 가려내기 어려울 지경이었다. 그러나 누구 하나 한가한 자는 없었다. 더러는 가장 값진 물건을, 더러는 어린것을, 또 더러는 늙고 병약한 이를, 산자락에 밝은 초록의 양탄자처럼 펼쳐진 숲속으로 날랐다. 그곳으로 〈터메넌드〉 또한, 〈언카스〉와 짧고도 가슴 저미는 만남을 가진 뒤, 차분한 평정으로 물러갔다. 이 현자가 그에게서 떨어질 때는, 오래 잃었다가 막 되찾은 자식과 헤어지는 어버이가 그러할 만한 미련이 어렸다. 그사이 〈던컨〉은 〈앨리스〉를 안전한 곳으로 데려다준 뒤, 정찰병을 찾아 나섰으니, 그 얼굴에는 그 역시 다가오는 싸움을 얼마나 간절히 갈망하는지가 드러나 있었다.\n\n그러나 〈호크아이〉는 원주민들의 전쟁 노래와 가담 의식에 너무도 익숙하여, 눈앞에 펼쳐지는 광경에 어떤 흥미도 내비치지 않았다. 그는 다만 이따금 〈언카스〉를 따라 출진할 채비가 되었음을 알리는 전사들의 수와 자질을 흘긋 살필 뿐이었다. 이 점에서 그는 곧 흡족해졌으니, 이미 보았듯이, 그 젊은 추장의 위력은 부족의 모든 싸울 만한 사내를 순식간에 아울렀던 것이다. 이 중요한 사안이 그토록 흡족하게 결정된 뒤, 그는 한 인디언 소년을 보내어, 〈델라웨어〉족 진영에 다가설 때 무기를 숨겨 둔 자리에서 \"킬디어\"와 〈언카스〉의 소총을 찾아오게 했다. 이는 이중의 책략이었으니, 포로로 붙들릴 경우 무기를 그들 자신의 운명에서 지켜 주는 한편, 낯선 이들 사이에서 그들이 방어와 생존의 수단을 갖춘 사내라기보다 차라리 고초를 겪는 자로 비치게 하는 이점을 주었다. 자신이 끔찍이 아끼는 소총을 되찾는 임무를 다른 자에게 맡기면서도, 정찰병은 평소의 신중함을 조금도 잃지 않았다. 그는 〈매과〉가 수행원 없이 오지 않았음을 알았고, 또한 〈휴런〉족 첩자들이 숲의 경계를 따라 새로운 적들의 움직임을 살피고 있음도 알았다. 그러므로 자신이 직접 그 시험에 나서는 것은 목숨을 잃는 일이었을 것이고, 전사라 한들 나을 게 없었을 터이나, 소년의 위험은 그 목적이 발각된 뒤에야 비로소 시작될 법했다. 〈헤이워드〉가 그에게 합류했을 때, 정찰병은 이 시험의 결과를 침착하게 기다리고 있었다.\n\n잘 일러둔 데다 충분히 영리하기까지 한 그 소년은, 그런 신임을 받은 자부심과 젊은 야심의 온갖 기대로 가슴이 부풀어, 빈터를 가로질러 무심한 듯 숲을 향해 나아갔고, 총들이 숨겨진 곳에서 조금 떨어진 지점으로 숲에 들어섰다. 그러나 덤불의 잎사귀에 몸이 가려지는 순간, 그 거무스름한 형상이 마치 뱀처럼, 바라던 보물을 향해 미끄러져 가는 것이 보였다. 그는 성공했다. 그리고 다음 순간, 그는 마을이 들어선 단의 발치를 두른 좁은 트인 공간을 가로질러, 화살 같은 빠르기로, 양손에 노획물을 하나씩 들고 날 듯이 달려 나왔다. 그가 실제로 바위에 다다라 믿기 힘든 날렵함으로 그 비탈을 뛰어오르고 있을 때, 숲에서 날아온 한 발의 총성이 정찰병의 판단이 얼마나 정확했는지를 보여 주었다. 소년은 약하나 경멸이 담긴 외침으로 그에 응수했다. 곧이어 은신처의 다른 쪽에서 둘째 탄환이 그의 뒤를 좇아 날아왔다. 다음 순간 그는 위쪽 평지에 모습을 드러내어, 의기양양하게 두 총을 치켜들고는, 그토록 영광스러운 임무로 자신에게 영예를 베푼 그 이름난 사냥꾼을 향해 정복자의 기개로 걸어갔다.\n\n〈호크아이〉는 자신의 심부름꾼의 운명에 생생한 관심을 두었음에도, \"킬디어\"를 건네받자, 그 순간만은 다른 모든 생각을 마음에서 몰아낼 만한 만족감으로 그것을 받았다. 그는 식견 있는 눈으로 총을 살피고, 약실 뚜껑을 열댓 번이나 여닫으며, 격발 장치에 그만큼이나 중요한 다른 여러 시험을 해 본 뒤, 소년을 향해 돌아서서, 더없는 친절을 드러내며 다쳤느냐고 물었다. 그 꼬마는 자랑스레 그의 얼굴을 올려다볼 뿐, 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"아! 알겠구나, 얘야. 그 악당들이 네 팔을 스쳐 놓았어!\" 정찰병이 덧붙이며, 묵묵히 참고 있는 그 소년의 팔을 들어 올렸으니, 그 팔에는 탄환 한 발이 살을 깊이 헤집은 상처가 나 있었다. \"허나 으깬 오리나무를 조금 붙이면 신통하게 들을 게다. 그동안은 〈왐펌〉 표지로 싸매 주마! 너는 어린 나이에 전사의 일을 시작했구나, 용감한 녀석아. 무덤까지 가져갈 명예로운 흉터를 잔뜩 짊어지게 생겼다. 머릿가죽을 벗겨 본 젊은이도 이런 자국 하나 보이지 못하는 자를 내 많이 안다. 가거라!\" 팔을 다 동여매고 그가 말했다. \"너는 추장이 될 게다!\"\n\n소년은, 가장 허영심 많은 신하가 제 발그레한 훈장 띠를 자랑하는 것보다 더 자랑스럽게 제 흐르는 피를 여기며 떠나, 또래 무리 사이를 으스대며 걸으니, 뭇사람의 부러움과 흠모의 대상이 되었다.\n\n그러나 그토록 진지하고 중대한 임무가 산적한 순간이라, 이 젊은이의 한 차례 용기 어린 행동은, 더 온화한 정세였다면 받았을 만한 두루 미친 주목과 칭송을 끌어내지는 못했다. 그래도 그것은 〈델라웨어〉족에게 적들의 위치와 의도를 알려 준 셈이 되었다. 그리하여, 기개는 있으나 약한 그 소년보다 그 임무에 더 어울리는 한 무리의 결사대가, 숨어 있는 자들을 몰아내라는 명을 받았다. 그 임무는 곧 완수되었으니, 〈휴런〉족 대부분이 자신들이 발각된 것을 알자 스스로 물러났던 까닭이다. 〈델라웨어〉족은 자기네 진영에서 알맞을 만큼 떨어진 거리까지 뒤쫓다가, 매복에 끌려들까 염려하여 멈춰 서서 명령을 기다렸다. 양편이 다 같이 몸을 숨기자, 숲은 다시, 온화한 여름 아침과 깊은 적막이 빚어낼 수 있는 만큼 고요하고 잠잠해졌다.\n\n차분하나 그래도 조바심을 누르지 못한 〈언카스〉는 이제 추장들을 모아 그 권한을 나누었다. 그는 〈호크아이〉를, 여러 차례 시험을 거쳐 언제나 신뢰받을 만한 자로 드러난 전사라 소개했다. 자신의 벗이 호의로 받아들여지는 것을 보자, 그는 그에게 스무 명의 지휘를 맡겼으니, 그들 또한 그처럼 날렵하고 능숙하며 단호한 자들이었다. 그는 〈델라웨어〉족에게 〈헤이워드〉가 〈양기즈〉의 군대에서 차지하는 지위를 알려 준 뒤, 그에게도 동등한 권한의 직책을 내주려 했다. 그러나 〈던컨〉은 그 직책을 사양하며, 정찰병의 곁에서 지원병으로 복무할 뜻을 밝혔다. 이러한 배치를 마친 뒤, 그 젊은 〈모히칸〉족은 여러 원주민 추장을 임명하여 책임이 따르는 갖가지 자리를 채웠고, 시간이 촉박한지라, 진군의 명을 내렸다. 이백이 넘는 사내가 흔쾌히, 그러나 묵묵히 그를 따랐다.\n\n그들이 숲으로 들어서는 데는 아무런 방해도 없었다. 그들 자신의 정찰병의 은신처에 다다를 때까지, 경보를 울리거나 그들이 필요로 하는 정보를 줄 만한 살아 있는 것이라곤 하나도 마주치지 못했다. 이곳에서 멈추라는 명이 내렸고, 추장들이 모여 \"속삭이는 평의회\"를 열었다.\n\n이 모임에서는 여러 가지 작전 계획이 제시되었으나, 그 어느 것도 열렬한 그들의 우두머리의 뜻에 맞는 성격은 아니었다. 〈언카스〉가 제 마음이 시키는 대로 따랐다면, 그는 한순간도 지체 없이 부하들을 이끌고 돌격하여, 그 싸움을 당장의 결판이라는 모험에 걸었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 길은 동족이 받아들이는 온갖 관행과 견해에 어긋나는 일이었다. 그러므로 그는, 지금의 심사로는 저주스럽기 짝이 없는 신중함을 어쩔 수 없이 택해야 했고, 〈코라〉의 위험과 〈매과〉의 오만이 생생하게 떠오르는 가운데 그 불같은 기개가 부글대는데도, 거슬리는 충고에 귀를 기울여야 했다.\n\n몇 분에 걸친 흡족잖은 논의 끝에, 적진 쪽에서 한 사람이 홀로 다가오는 것이 보였으니, 그 서두르는 기색이 어찌나 역력했던지 평화의 제안을 띤 전령이 아닌가 하는 생각이 들 정도였다. 그러나 〈델라웨어〉족 평의회가 모여 있는 은신처에서 백 야드쯤 되는 곳에 이르자, 그 낯선 자는 머뭇거리며, 어느 길을 택해야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 듯하다가, 끝내 멈춰 섰다. 이제 모든 눈이 〈언카스〉를 향했으니, 어찌 처신해야 할지 지시를 구하는 듯했다.\n\n\"〈호크아이〉,\" 젊은 추장이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"저자가 다시는 〈휴런〉족에게 말하지 못하게 해야 하오.\"\n\n\"저놈의 때가 왔구먼,\" 말수 적은 정찰병이 말하며, 소총의 긴 총신을 나뭇잎 사이로 내밀어, 신중하고도 치명적인 겨냥을 했다. 그러나 방아쇠를 당기는 대신, 그는 다시 총구를 내리더니, 그 특유의 웃음을 한바탕 터뜨렸다. \"이 못난 죄인 분명히 말하건대, 난 저 도깨비 같은 놈을 〈밍고〉인 줄 알았지!\" 그가 말했다. \"한데 탄환을 박아 넣을 자리를 찾느라 그놈의 갈빗대를 따라 눈을 훑다가—믿어지겠나, 〈언카스〉—그 악사 양반의 불대를 봤지 뭔가. 그러니 결국 저자는 〈개멋〉이라 불리는 사람이라, 죽여 봐야 누구한테도 득 될 게 없고, 살려 두면 그 혀가 노래 말고 다른 짓도 할 줄 안다면야 우리 목적에 쓸모 있게 만들 수도 있겠어. 소리에 아직 효험이 남아 있다면, 곧 저 정직한 친구와 한바탕 이야기를 나눠 보지. 그것도 저자가 '킬디어'의 말보다 한결 듣기 좋다고 여길 목소리로 말이야.\"\n\n그렇게 말하며 〈호크아이〉는 소총을 옆에 내려놓고, 〈데이비드〉의 귀에 들릴 만한 거리에 이를 때까지 덤불을 헤치고 기어가, 자신을 그토록 안전하고 빛나게 〈휴런〉족 진영을 통과시켜 준 그 음악적 시도를 되풀이해 보았다. 〈개멋〉의 정교한 귀는 그리 쉽게 속을 리 없었고(사실대로 말하자면, 〈호크아이〉 말고 다른 누가 그런 소리를 내기란 어려운 노릇이었을 것이다), 그리하여 일찍이 한 번 그 소리를 들어 본 적이 있는 터라, 그는 이제 그것이 어디서 나는지 알았다. 그 가엾은 친구는 큰 곤혹에서 벗어난 듯 보였으니, 그 목소리의 방향을 좇는 일은—그에게는 포대(砲臺)를 정면으로 거슬러 오르는 것에 못지않게 힘겨운 일이었으나—그는 곧 숨어 있는 그 가객을 찾아냈다.\n\n\"〈휴런〉족이 저걸 어찌 여길지 궁금하군!\" 정찰병이 웃으며 말하더니, 동행의 팔을 붙잡고 후방으로 재촉했다. \"그 악당들이 귀에 들리는 데 누워 있다면, 멍청이가 하나가 아니라 둘이라고 할 테지! 허나 여기선 우리가 안전하네,\" 그가 〈언카스〉와 그 동료들을 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"자, 이제 〈밍고〉의 술책을 알아듣기 쉬운 영어로, 목소리 오르내림 없이 들려주게나.\"\n\n〈데이비드〉는 사납고 거칠어 보이는 추장들을 둘러보며 말없이 놀랐으나, 아는 얼굴들이 있다는 데 안심하여, 곧 정신을 가다듬어 알아들을 만한 대답을 했다.\n\n\"이교도들이 상당한 수로 밖에 나와 있는데,\" 〈데이비드〉가 말했다. \"내 두렵건대, 흉악한 의도를 품고 있소. 지난 한 시간 동안 그들의 거처에서는 입에 담기조차 불경한 소리들과 더불어, 울부짖음과 신성치 못한 흥청거림이 그치질 않았소. 참으로 어찌나 심했던지, 나는 평안을 찾아 〈델라웨어〉족에게로 달아났소이다.\"\n\n\"발걸음이 더 빨랐다면, 그 자리를 바꾼들 자네 귀가 덕 본 게 많지 않았을 걸세,\" 정찰병이 다소 무미건조하게 받았다. \"허나 그건 그렇다 치고, 〈휴런〉족은 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"여기와 그들의 마을 사이 숲속에 숨어 있는데, 그 세력이 어찌나 큰지, 분별이 있다면 당장 돌아서라고 일러 줄 정도요.\"\n\n〈언카스〉는 제 무리를 가려 주는 나무들의 행렬을 따라 눈길을 던지고는, 한 이름을 입에 올렸다.\n\n\"〈매과〉는?\"\n\n\"그들 가운데 있소. 그가 〈델라웨어〉족과 함께 머물던 처녀를 데려와, 동굴에 두고는, 성난 늑대처럼 제 야만족들의 선두에 섰소. 무엇이 그의 마음을 그토록 크게 어지럽혔는지 나는 모르겠소!\"\n\n\"그녀를 동굴에 두었다 했소!\" 〈헤이워드〉가 끼어들었다. \"그곳의 위치를 우리가 안다니 다행이오! 그녀를 당장 구해 낼 무슨 수가 없겠소?\"\n\n〈언카스〉는 정찰병을 간절히 바라본 뒤에야 물었다.\n\n\"〈호크아이〉의 생각은 어떻소?\"\n\n\"내게 소총 스무 자루를 주게. 그러면 나는 오른편으로, 시냇물을 따라 돌아, 비버의 굴들을 지나, 〈사가모어〉와 대령께 합류하겠네. 그러면 자네는 그쪽에서 함성을 듣게 될 걸세. 이 바람이면 한 마일은 거뜬히 보낼 수 있지. 그때 〈언카스〉, 자네는 그들의 전면을 몰아붙이게. 그들이 우리 총의 사정거리 안에 들어오면, 우리가 일격을 가할 테니, 늙은 변경 사람의 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대, 그 일격이 그들의 전열을 물푸레나무 활처럼 휘게 할 걸세. 그런 다음 우리는 그들의 마을을 들이쳐, 동굴에서 그 여인을 데려오는 거지. 그러면 부족과의 일은, 백인의 싸움 방식대로 일격과 승리로 끝내든지, 인디언 방식대로 피하고 숨으며 마무리하면 되네. 소령, 이 계책에 대단한 학식이야 없겠지만, 용기와 인내만 있으면 모두 해낼 수 있어.\"\n\"내 마음에 쏙 드오,\" 〈코라〉의 구출이 정찰병의 마음속 첫째 목표임을 알아챈 〈던컨〉이 외쳤다. \"내 마음에 쏙 드오. 당장 시도합시다.\"\n\n\"내 마음에 쏙 드오.\" 코라를 구출하는 것이 정찰병의 마음속 첫째 목적임을 알아챈 던컨이 외쳤다. \"내 마음에 쏙 드오. 당장 시도합시다.\"\n\n짧은 협의 끝에 계획이 무르익었고, 여러 무리에게 한층 명확히 전달되었다. 갖가지 신호가 정해지자 추장들은 흩어져, 저마다 할당된 자리로 향했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII\n\n위대한 왕이 몸값도 받지 않고\n검은 눈의 처녀를 제 〈크리사〉로 돌려보낼 때까지.\n\n포프\n〈언카스〉가 이렇게 병력을 배치하는 동안, 숲은 고요하여, 평의회에서 만났던 자들을 제외하면 마치 전능하신 창조주의 손에서 갓 나왔을 때처럼 사람 하나 깃들지 않은 듯했다. 눈길은 어느 방향으로든 나무들의 길고 그늘진 사잇길을 따라 멀리 뻗어 나갈 수 있었으나, 그 어디에도 평화롭고 잠든 풍경에 어울리지 않는 것이라곤 보이지 않았다. 여기저기서 너도밤나무 가지 사이로 푸드덕대는 새 소리가 들렸고, 이따금 다람쥐가 떨군 도토리에 일행의 놀란 시선이 잠시 그곳으로 쏠리기도 했다. 그러나 이 우연한 방해가 멎는 순간, 지나가는 바람이 그들의 머리 위로, 시내나 호수가 아니고서는 그토록 광막한 고장 위에 끊김 없이 펼쳐진 그 푸르고 굽이치는 숲의 표면을 따라 속삭이는 소리가 들려왔다. 〈델라웨어족〉과 그 적들의 마을 사이에 놓인 황야 일대에는, 마치 사람의 발길이 한 번도 닿은 적 없는 듯, 깊고도 숨결 같은 정적이 깃들어 있었다. 그러나 이 모험에서 임무상 맨 앞에 선 〈호크아이〉는 곧 맞서 싸울 자들의 성정을 너무도 잘 알았기에, 그 미덥지 못한 고요를 믿지 않았다.\n\n자신의 작은 무리가 모인 것을 본 정찰병은 〈킬디어〉를 한쪽 팔 안쪽에 비스듬히 끼고, 자기를 따라오라는 말없는 신호를 보낸 뒤, 그들을 후미 쪽으로 여러 로드 이끌어, 전진하며 건넜던 작은 시냇물의 바닥으로 데려갔다. 여기서 그는 멈춰 섰고, 진중하고 주의 깊은 전사들이 모두 자기 둘레로 모여들기를 기다린 후, 〈델라웨어〉 말로 입을 열어 물었다.\n\n\"내 젊은이들 가운데 이 물줄기가 우리를 어디로 데려갈지 아는 자가 있는가?\"\n\n한 〈델라웨어〉인이 손을 내밀어 두 손가락을 벌리고, 그것이 뿌리에서 어떻게 맞붙어 있는지를 가리키며 대답했다.\n\n\"해가 제 길이만큼 가기도 전에, 작은 물이 큰 물에 닿을 것입니다.\" 그러더니 자기가 말한 곳의 방향을 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"그 둘이 합치면 비버들에게 넉넉하지요.\"\n\n\"나도 그쯤으로 짐작했네.\" 정찰병은 나무 우듬지 사이의 트인 곳으로 눈길을 위로 던지며 대답했다. \"물길이 흐르는 방향과 산의 방위로 보아서 말이야. 자, 〈휴런족〉 냄새를 맡을 때까지 이 둑의 엄폐 안에 머물도록 하지.\"\n\n그의 동료들은 으레 하던 짧은 동의의 탄성을 냈으나, 우두머리가 친히 앞장서려는 것을 알아챈 한둘이 무언가 잘못되었다는 신호를 보냈다. 그들의 눈짓이 뜻하는 바를 알아차린 〈호크아이〉는 돌아서서, 자기 일행을 여기까지 따라온 자가 성가대 선창자임을 알아보았다.\n\n\"여보게, 친구.\" 정찰병이 진중하게, 어쩌면 제 공로를 의식한 다소 자랑스러운 태도로 물었다. \"이 무리가 가장 절망적인 임무를 위해 뽑힌 척후대이고, 비록 남이 말하면 더 나은 낯으로 할 만한 소리지만, 부하들을 한가로이 놀려 두지 않을 자의 지휘 아래 있다는 것을 자네는 아는가. 우리가 살았든 죽었든 한 〈휴런족〉의 몸뚱이를 밟기까지는 오 분도 안 걸릴지 모르고, 길어야 삼십 분을 넘기지 못할 걸세.\"\n\n\"비록 당신의 의도를 말로 듣지는 못했으나,\" 얼굴이 약간 상기되고, 평소 잔잔하고 무심하던 두 눈이 보기 드문 불꽃을 띠고 번득이던 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"당신의 부하들을 보니, 주께서 어여삐 여기신 종족의 여인과 사악하게도 혼인을 탐한 〈세겜〉 사람들과 맞서 싸우러 나간 〈야곱〉의 자손들이 떠오르더이다. 자, 나는 멀리 길을 떠나, 당신들이 찾는 처녀와 더불어 좋은 일과 궂은 일 속에서 오래 머물렀소. 비록 전사는 아니나, 허리를 동이고 칼을 갈아, 기꺼이 그녀를 위해 한 방 휘두르고 싶소이다.\"\n\n정찰병은 대답하기에 앞서, 이 기이한 입대의 승산을 마음속으로 가늠하듯 망설였다.\n\n\"자네는 어떤 무기도 쓸 줄을 모르네. 소총도 지니지 않았고. 게다가 내 말 믿게, 〈밍고족〉이 빼앗은 것은 거리낌 없이 도로 돌려줄 거야.\"\n\n\"비록 허풍 떨고 피에 굶주린 〈골리앗〉은 아니나,\" 〈데이비드〉가 알록달록하고 볼품없는 옷자락 아래에서 무릿매를 꺼내며 대답했다. \"나는 그 〈유대〉 소년의 본보기를 잊지 않았소. 이 오래된 전쟁 도구로 젊은 시절 나는 숱하게 익혔고, 어쩌면 그 솜씨가 아주 떠나가지는 않았을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"허!\" 〈호크아이〉가 사슴 가죽 끈과 앞주머니를 차갑고 맥 빠지게 하는 눈길로 살피며 말했다. \"그 물건이 화살이나 칼 사이에서야 제 몫을 할지도 모르지. 허나 이 〈멩궤족〉은 프랑스 놈들한테 한 사람당 잘 깎인 강선 총열을 받았다네. 하지만 불길 속을 다치지 않고 다니는 게 자네 타고난 재주인 듯하고, 지금껏 보살핌을 받아 왔으니 — 소령, 자네 소총 공이를 당겨 놓았구먼. 때 이른 한 발은 스무 개의 머리 가죽을 까닭 없이 잃는 셈이 될 게야 — 선창자, 자네는 따라와도 좋네. 함성을 지를 때 자네가 쓸모 있을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 친구.\" 자기와 이름이 같은 그 왕처럼 시냇물 조약돌 가운데서 손수 채비를 갖추며 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"비록 살생의 욕심은 없으나, 당신이 나를 돌려보냈더라면 내 영혼이 괴로웠을 것이오.\"\n\n\"명심하게.\" 정찰병이 〈거멋〉의 아직 쓰린 그 자리를 가리키며 제 머리를 의미심장하게 톡톡 두드리고 덧붙였다. \"우리는 노래하러가 아니라 싸우러 온 걸세. 전면적인 함성이 떨어지기 전에는, 소총 말고 아무것도 입을 열지 않네.\"\n\n〈데이비드〉는 그 조건에 따르겠다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러자 〈호크아이〉는 부하들을 또 한 번 살피는 눈길로 둘러본 뒤, 전진하라는 신호를 보냈다.\n\n그들의 길은 일 마일 거리를 그 물줄기의 바닥을 따라 이어졌다. 깎아지른 둑과 시내를 따라 우거진 덤불 덕에 발각될 큰 위험은 면했으나, 인디언식 공격에 알려진 어떤 조심성도 소홀히 하지 않았다. 한 전사는 양 측면에서 걷는다기보다 기어서, 이따금 숲 안을 흘끔흘끔 살폈고, 몇 분마다 무리는 멈춰 서서, 보다 덜 자연적인 상태에 있는 사람으로서는 좀처럼 짐작도 못 할 만큼 예민한 감각으로 적의 기척에 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 그들의 행군은 방해받지 않았고, 자기들의 전진이 들켰다는 아주 작은 기색도 없이, 그들은 작은 물줄기가 큰 물줄기에 합쳐지는 지점에 다다랐다. 여기서 정찰병은 다시 멈춰 서서 숲의 징후들을 살폈다.\n\n\"싸우기에 좋은 날이 될 듯하군.\" 그가 영어로 〈헤이워드〉에게 말하며, 넓은 장막처럼 창공을 가로질러 움직이기 시작한 구름으로 눈길을 위로 던졌다. \"밝은 해와 번쩍이는 총열은 정확한 조준에 친구가 못 되지. 모든 게 우리한테 유리해. 저들은 바람을 안고 있으니, 바람이 저들의 소리와 연기까지 우리 쪽으로 다 실어 올 거야. 그것만 해도 적잖은 일이지. 반면에 우리 쪽은 먼저 한 발 쏘고, 그다음에 시야가 트일 걸세. 한데 여기서 우리 엄폐물은 끝이로군. 비버들이 수백 년 동안 이 시내를 차지해 왔고, 그놈들 먹이와 둑 때문에, 보다시피 껍질 벗겨진 그루터기는 많아도 살아 있는 나무는 몇 그루 없으니 말이야.\"\n\n사실 〈호크아이〉는 이 몇 마디로, 이제 그들 앞에 펼쳐진 광경을 제법 그럴듯하게 그려 낸 셈이었다. 시내는 폭이 들쭉날쭉하여, 어떤 데서는 바위의 좁은 틈새로 쏟아져 나오고, 어떤 데서는 몇 에이커의 저지대 위로 퍼져, 못이라 부를 만한 작은 구역들을 이루었다. 그 둑을 따라 곳곳에는 죽은 나무들의 썩어 가는 잔해가, 비틀거리는 둥치 위에서 신음하는 것에서부터 그 생명의 원리를 신비로이 품은 거친 껍질을 막 빼앗긴 것에 이르기까지, 온갖 부패의 단계로 널려 있었다. 그 사이에는 길고 낮으며 이끼 덮인 더미 몇이 흩어져, 이전의, 오래전에 떠나간 세대의 기념물처럼 보였다.\n\n이 모든 자잘한 세부를, 정찰병은 아마 이전에는 한 번도 끌어 본 적 없을 진지함과 관심으로 살폈다. 그는 〈휴런족〉의 야영지가 시내를 따라 반 마일 남짓 위쪽에 있음을 알았다. 그리고 숨은 위험을 두려워하는 자 특유의 불안을 안고, 적의 존재를 알리는 가장 작은 흔적조차 찾을 수 없다는 데 크게 마음이 어지러웠다. 한두 번 그는 돌격 명령을 내려 기습으로 마을을 들이칠 마음이 일었으나, 그의 경험이 그토록 헛된 시도의 위험을 곧 일깨워 주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 〈언카스〉를 남겨 둔 쪽에서 들려올 적대의 소리에 귀를 곤두세웠으나, 폭풍을 예고하듯 돌풍이 되어 숲의 품을 휩쓸기 시작한 바람의 한숨 소리 외에는 아무것도 들리지 않았다. 마침내, 제 지식에서 조언을 구하기보다 평소답지 않은 조바심에 굴복하여, 그는 병력을 드러내고, 조심스럽되 꾸준히 물줄기를 거슬러 올라감으로써 일을 결판내기로 작정했다.\n\n정찰병은 관찰하는 동안 덤불에 가려 서 있었고, 동료들은 작은 물줄기가 흘러나오는 그 골짜기 바닥에 여전히 엎드려 있었다. 그러나 낮으면서도 알아들을 수 있는 그의 신호를 듣자, 무리 전체가 검은 유령들처럼 둑을 살금살금 기어올라, 말없이 그의 둘레에 늘어섰다. 나아가려는 방향을 가리키며 〈호크아이〉가 앞으로 나아가자, 무리는 일렬종대로 갈라져, 〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉를 빼면 마치 단 한 사람의 발자취만 남기려는 듯, 그의 발자국을 어김없이 따라 디뎠다.\n\n그러나 일행이 몸을 드러내자마자, 그들의 후미에서 열두 자루 소총의 일제 사격 소리가 들렸고, 한 〈델라웨어〉인이 상처 입은 사슴처럼 공중으로 높이 솟구쳤다가 온몸을 쭉 뻗고 완전히 숨이 끊겨 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"아! 이런 흉계가 있을 줄 알았지!\" 정찰병이 영어로 외치고는, 생각만큼 빠르게 제가 익힌 그 말로 덧붙였다. \"엄폐하라, 모두, 돌격!\"\n\n그 말에 무리는 흩어졌고, 〈헤이워드〉가 놀라움에서 채 헤어나기도 전에, 그는 자신이 〈데이비드〉와 단둘이 서 있음을 깨달았다. 다행히 〈휴런족〉은 이미 물러난 뒤라, 그는 그들의 사격으로부터 안전했다. 그러나 이 상태가 오래갈 수 없음은 분명했으니, 정찰병이 소총을 쏘고 나무에서 나무로 내달리며, 적이 천천히 땅을 내주는 그 후퇴를 압박하는 본보기를 보였기 때문이다.\n\n이 공격은 아주 작은 무리의 〈휴런족〉이 벌인 것이었던 듯하나, 그 무리는 제 편들 쪽으로 물러나며 차츰 수가 불어, 마침내 그들의 응사가 전진하는 〈델라웨어족〉의 사격에 거의, 아니 어쩌면 완전히 맞먹게 되었다. 〈헤이워드〉는 전투원들 가운데 몸을 던져, 동료들에게 필요한 신중함을 본받아, 제 소총으로 재빠르게 사격을 거듭했다. 이제 다툼은 뜨거워지면서도 한자리에 멈췄다. 양편 모두 나무로 제 몸을 되도록 가렸기에, 다친 자는 적었으며, 실로 조준하는 순간 말고는 몸의 어느 부분도 결코 드러내지 않았다. 그러나 형세는 차츰 〈호크아이〉와 그의 무리에게 불리하게 돌아가고 있었다. 눈썰미 빠른 정찰병은 어찌 손쓸지는 모른 채 제 위험을 알아챘다. 그는 땅을 지키는 것보다 후퇴하는 편이 더 위험함을 알았고, 한편으로 적이 자기 측면으로 병력을 내보내, 〈델라웨어족〉이 몸을 가리기가 너무도 어려워져 그들의 사격이 거의 멎을 지경임을 깨달았다. 이 난처한 순간, 적 부족 전체가 차츰 자기들을 에워싸고 있다는 생각이 들기 시작했을 때, 그들은 〈언카스〉가 배치된 곳에서 전투원들의 함성과 병장기 부딪는 소리가 숲의 아치 아래 울려 퍼지는 것을 들었다. 그곳은 어떤 의미로 〈호크아이〉와 그의 무리가 싸우고 있던 땅보다 아래에 놓인 저지대였다.\n\n이 공격의 효과는 즉각적이었고, 정찰병과 그의 벗들에게는 크나큰 위안이었다. 자신의 기습이 미리 예상되어 결국 실패한 것처럼, 이번에는 적이 그 목표와 병력 수에 속아 넘어가, 젊은 〈모히칸족〉의 맹렬한 공격에 맞설 너무 적은 병력만을 남겨 둔 듯했다. 이 사실은 숲속의 전투가 마을 쪽으로 빠르게 위로 굴러 올라가는 것과, 그들을 공격하던 자들의 수가 순식간에 줄어드는 것으로 이중으로 분명해졌으니, 그들은 전면을, 이제 드러난 바로는 방어의 주요 지점을 지키는 데 보태려 달려간 것이었다.\n\n목소리로, 또 제 본보기로 부하들을 북돋우며, 그때 〈호크아이〉는 적을 향해 밀고 들어가라는 호령을 내렸다. 그 거친 방식의 전쟁에서 돌격이란 그저 엄폐물에서 엄폐물로 적에게 더 가까이 다가가는 것에 지나지 않았고, 이 작전에서 그는 즉각, 또 성공적으로 복종받았다. 〈휴런족〉은 물러설 수밖에 없었고, 다툼의 무대는 그것이 시작된 보다 트인 지대에서, 공격받은 자들이 의지할 덤불을 찾은 곳으로 빠르게 옮겨 갔다. 여기서 싸움은 길어지고 고되었으며, 결판이 의심스러운 듯 보였다. 〈델라웨어족〉은 비록 쓰러진 자는 없었으나, 그들이 놓인 불리함 탓에 피를 줄줄 흘리기 시작했다.\n\n이 위기에서, 〈호크아이〉는 〈헤이워드〉의 엄폐물 노릇을 하던 바로 그 나무 뒤로 갈 방도를 찾아냈다. 그 자신의 전투원 대다수는 약간 오른쪽, 부르면 닿을 거리에 있으면서, 가려진 적을 향해 빠르나 헛된 사격을 거듭하고 있었다.\n\n\"자네는 젊은이야, 소령.\" 정찰병이 〈킬디어〉의 개머리판을 땅에 내려놓고 총열에 기댄 채, 앞선 분주함에 약간 지친 듯 말했다. \"그리고 언젠가 훗날 이 〈밍고족〉 같은 도깨비들에 맞서 군대를 이끄는 것이 자네 타고난 재주일지도 모르지. 자네는 여기서 인디언 싸움의 이치를 볼 수 있네. 그건 주로 날랜 손, 빠른 눈, 그리고 좋은 엄폐물에 달려 있어. 자, 만약 자네한테 왕립 아메리카 부대 한 중대가 여기 있다면, 이 일에서 그들을 어떻게 부리겠나?\"\n\n\"총검이 길을 낼 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 자네 말에는 백인다운 도리가 있어. 허나 사람은 이 황야에서, 제가 목숨을 몇이나 내줄 수 있는지를 자문해야 하네. 아니야 — 말일세[29].\" 정찰병이 무언가 골똘히 생각하는 사람처럼 고개를 저으며 말을 이었다. \"말이라니, 부끄럽지만, 머잖아 결국 이런 격전을 결판내는 건 말일세. 그 짐승들이 사람보다 낫고, 결국 우리도 말에 의지해야 할 게야. 편자 박힌 발굽을 홍인종의 모카신 위에 올려 보게. 그자가 일단 소총을 비우고 나면, 결코 다시 장전하려고 멈추지 않을 테니.\"\n\n\"이건 다른 때에 의논하는 편이 나을 주제입니다.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 대답했다. \"돌격할까요?\"\n\n\"제 숨 돌릴 짬을 쓸모 있는 궁리로 보낸다고 해서, 누구의 타고난 재주에 어긋날 건 없다고 보네.\" 정찰병이 대답했다. \"돌진이라면, 나는 그런 방책이 그리 내키지 않아. 시도하다 보면 머리 가죽 한둘은 버려야 하니까. 하지만,\" 그는 멀리서 들려오는 전투의 소리를 잡으려 고개를 옆으로 기울이며 덧붙였다. \"우리가 〈언카스〉에게 쓸모가 되려면, 우리 앞의 이 악당들을 처치해야 하네!\"\n\n그러고는 재빠르고 단호한 기색으로 돌아서서, 그는 제 인디언들에게 그들의 말로 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 그의 말에 함성이 답했고, 정해진 신호에 맞춰 전사들은 저마다 제 나무를 빙 돌아 잽싸게 움직였다. 그토록 많은 검은 몸뚱이가 한순간에 눈앞으로 휙 스치는 광경에, 〈휴런족〉은 황급한, 따라서 효과 없는 사격을 했다. 숨 돌릴 틈도 없이, 〈델라웨어족〉은 먹잇감에 덮치는 표범 떼처럼 긴 도약으로 숲을 향해 뛰어들었다. 〈호크아이〉가 앞장서서, 그 무시무시한 소총을 휘두르며 제 본보기로 부하들을 북돋웠다. 사격을 끌어내려 부린 술책에 속지 않은 더 늙고 더 교활한 〈휴런족〉 몇이 이제 바짝 다가들어 치명적인 일제 사격을 가하여, 정찰병의 맨 앞 전사 셋을 쓰러뜨림으로써 그의 우려를 입증했다. 그러나 그 충격은 돌격의 기세를 물리치기에는 부족했다. 〈델라웨어족〉은 제 천성의 사나움으로 엄폐물 안으로 쳐들어가, 맹렬한 공격으로 저항의 흔적을 모조리 쓸어버렸다.\n\n전투는 손에서 손으로 맞붙은 채 한순간만 이어졌고, 그러고 나서 공격받은 자들은 빠르게 땅을 내주어, 마침내 덤불의 맞은편 가장자리에 다다랐다. 거기서 그들은 쫓기는 짐승에게서 그토록 흔히 보이는 그 일종의 고집스러움으로 엄폐물에 매달렸다. 이 위태로운 순간, 다툼의 승패가 다시 의심스러워지고 있을 때, 〈휴런족〉의 뒤에서 소총 소리가 났고, 그들 후방의 공터에 자리한 비버 집들 사이에서 한 발의 총알이 윙 날아왔으며, 그 뒤로 사납고 소름 끼치는 전투의 함성이 이어졌다.\n\n\"저건 〈사가모어〉의 소리다!\" 〈호크아이〉가 제 우렁찬 목소리로 그 함성에 답하며 외쳤다. \"이제 앞뒤로 저놈들을 잡았다!\"\n\n〈휴런족〉에게 미친 효과는 즉각적이었다. 몸을 가릴 기회조차 주지 않는 방향에서의 공격에 기가 꺾여, 그들의 전사들은 한결같은 실망의 함성을 내질렀고, 한 덩어리로 흩어져, 도망 말고는 아무것도 아랑곳없이 공터를 가로질러 퍼져 나갔다. 많은 자가 그렇게 시도하다가 쫓는 〈델라웨어족〉의 총알과 매질 아래 쓰러졌다.\n\n정찰병과 〈칭가치국〉의 만남이나, 〈던컨〉이 〈먼로〉와 나눈 한층 가슴 뭉클한 해후를 우리는 멈추어 자세히 그리지 않겠다. 짧고 다급한 몇 마디가 양편에 사정을 설명해 주었다. 그러고 나서 〈호크아이〉는 제 무리에게 〈사가모어〉를 가리켜 보이며, 최고 지휘권을 그 〈모히칸족〉 추장의 손에 넘겼다. 〈칭가치국〉은, 토착 전사의 명령에 늘 힘을 실어 주는 그 진중한 위엄으로, 제 혈통과 경험이 그토록 빼어난 자격을 부여한 그 자리를 떠맡았다. 정찰병의 발자취를 좇아, 그는 무리를 이끌고 덤불을 거슬러 돌아갔으니, 부하들은 나아가면서 쓰러진 〈휴런족〉의 머리 가죽을 벗기고 제 편 전사자들의 시신은 숨겼으며, 마침내 정찰병이 멈추어 흡족해할 만한 지점에 다다랐다.\n\n앞선 다툼에서 한껏 숨을 고른 전사들은, 이제 자기들을 가리기에 족할 만큼 나무가 점점이 흩어진 평평한 땅 한 자락에 자리를 잡았다. 땅은 앞쪽으로 꽤 가파르게 떨어졌고, 그들의 눈 아래로는 좁고 어둑하며 숲이 우거진 골짜기가 몇 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어 있었다. 〈언카스〉가 〈휴런족〉의 본대와 여전히 다투고 있는 곳이 바로 이 빽빽하고 어두운 숲을 뚫고서였다.\n\n〈모히칸족〉과 그의 벗들은 언덕마루로 나아가, 단련된 귀로 전투의 소리에 귀를 기울였다. 새 몇 마리가 외딴 보금자리에서 놀라 골짜기의 잎 무성한 품 위를 맴돌았고, 여기저기서 이미 대기에 섞여 드는 듯한 옅고 안개 같은 구름이 나무들 위로 피어올라, 다툼이 격렬하고 한자리에 멈춰 있던 어떤 지점을 가리켰다.\n\n\"싸움이 비탈을 올라오고 있군요.\" 새로운 총성이 터진 방향을 가리키며 〈던컨〉이 말했다. \"우리는 저들의 전선 한복판에 너무 들어와 있어 쓸모가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"저들은 엄폐물이 더 두꺼운 골짜기 쪽으로 기울 거야.\" 정찰병이 말했다. \"그러면 우리가 저들의 측면을 단단히 잡게 되지. 가게, 〈사가모어〉. 자네는 함성을 지르며 젊은이들을 이끌기에 시간이 빠듯할 걸세. 나는 내 살빛을 한 전사들과 이 격전을 치르겠네. 자네는 나를 알지, 〈모히칸〉. 저놈들 가운데 단 한 〈휴런족〉도 〈킬디어〉의 통보 없이는 둔덕을 넘어 자네 후방으로 들지 못할 게야.\"\n\n그 인디언 추장은, 이제 빠르게 비탈을 굴러 올라오는 — 〈델라웨어족〉이 이기고 있다는 확실한 증거인 — 다툼의 징후를 살피려 또 한순간 멈추었다. 그리고 그는 적뿐 아니라 제 벗들이 가까이 왔음을, 폭풍이 터지기 전에 떨어지는 자잘한 우박처럼 땅의 마른 잎 사이로 후드득 떨어지기 시작한 아군의 총알로 깨닫고 나서야 비로소 그곳을 떴다. 〈호크아이〉와 그의 세 동료는 엄폐물로 몇 발짝 물러나, 그런 장면에서는 오랜 단련이 아니고서는 결코 줄 수 없는 침착함으로 결말을 기다렸다.\n\n오래지 않아 소총의 발사음은 숲의 메아리를 잃고, 트인 데서 쏘는 무기 소리처럼 들리기 시작했다. 그러고는 한 전사가 여기저기 숲의 변두리로 내몰려, 마지막으로 버틸 곳인 양 공터에 들어서며 대오를 추스르는 모습이 나타났다. 이들에게 곧 다른 자들이 합류하여, 마침내 한 줄로 길게 늘어선 거뭇한 형상들이 절망의 고집으로 엄폐물에 매달린 것이 보였다. 〈헤이워드〉는 차츰 조바심이 일어, 〈칭가치국〉 쪽으로 불안한 눈길을 돌렸다. 추장은 바위에 앉아, 차분한 얼굴만 드러낸 채, 마치 그저 다툼을 구경하려 거기 자리한 양 신중한 눈으로 그 광경을 살피고 있었다.\n\n\"〈델라웨어족〉이 칠 때가 왔습니다!\" 〈던컨〉이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 정찰병이 대답했다. \"그는 제 벗들의 냄새를 맡으면, 자기가 여기 있다는 걸 저들에게 알릴 거야. 보게, 보라고. 저 악당들이 저 소나무 떨기 속으로 들어가는군. 마치 날아갔다가 다시 내려앉는 벌 떼처럼 말이야. 맙소사, 저런 거뭇한 살갗 매듭 한복판에는 여인네라도 총알 한 발 박아 넣겠네!\"\n\n바로 그 순간 함성이 떨어졌고, 〈칭가치국〉과 그의 무리의 일제 사격에 〈휴런족〉 열둘이 쓰러졌다. 그 뒤를 이은 외침에 숲에서 단 한 줄기 전투의 함성이 답했고, 마치 천 명의 목구멍이 한 가지 노력으로 한데 어우러진 듯한 고함이 허공을 갈랐다. 〈휴런족〉은 휘청이며 제 전선의 중앙을 버렸고, 그들이 남긴 틈을 뚫고 〈언카스〉가 백 명의 전사를 거느리고 숲에서 뛰쳐나왔다.\n\n두 손을 좌우로 내저으며, 젊은 추장은 부하들에게 적을 가리켜 보였고, 그들은 갈라져 추격에 나섰다. 이제 싸움이 갈라져, 무너진 〈휴런족〉의 양 날개가 다시 숲에서 몸을 숨기려 했으나, 승리한 〈레나페족〉의 전사들에게 거세게 쫓겼다. 일 분쯤 지났을까, 소리들은 이미 서로 다른 방향으로 멀어지며, 숲의 메아리치는 아치 아래 차츰 또렷함을 잃어 갔다. 그러나 한 작은 〈휴런족〉 무리는 몸을 숨기기를 마다하고, 궁지에 몰린 사자들처럼, 더 가까이 난전에 끼어들고자, 〈칭가치국〉과 그의 무리가 막 떠난 그 비탈을 천천히 음울하게 물러 올라가고 있었다. 〈머과〉는 그 사납고 야만스러운 낯빛으로도, 또 여태 지키고 있는 오만한 권위의 기색으로도, 이 무리에서 두드러졌다.\n\n추격을 서두르려는 열의에, 〈언카스〉는 거의 홀로 남게 되었다. 그러나 그의 눈이 〈르 쉬빌〉의 모습을 붙잡은 순간, 다른 모든 고려는 잊혔다. 예닐곱 전사를 불러 모은 그 전투의 함성을 지르며, 수의 차이도 아랑곳없이, 그는 적을 향해 돌진했다. 그 움직임을 지켜보던 〈르 르나르〉는, 남모를 기쁨으로 그를 맞으려 멈추었다. 그러나 제 성급한 젊은 공격자의 무모함이 그를 제 손아귀에 넣어 주었다고 여긴 그 순간, 또 한 번 함성이 일었고, 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉이 백인 동료들을 모두 거느리고 구하러 달려오는 것이 보였다. 그 〈휴런족〉은 즉시 돌아서서, 비탈을 빠르게 후퇴하기 시작했다.\n\n인사나 축하를 나눌 겨를은 없었으니, 〈언카스〉가 비록 제 벗들이 와 있음을 알지 못한 채로도, 바람 같은 속도로 추격을 이어 갔기 때문이다. 〈호크아이〉가 그에게 엄폐물을 존중하라고 외쳤으나 헛일이었다. 젊은 〈모히칸족〉은 적들의 위험한 사격에 맞서며, 이내 그들을 제 곤두박질 같은 속도만큼이나 빠른 도주로 몰아넣었다. 그 경주가 오래가지 않았던 것, 또 백인들이 제 위치 덕을 크게 본 것이 다행이었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그 〈델라웨어〉인은 곧 동료들을 모두 앞질러, 제 무모함의 제물이 되었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 참사가 일어나기 전에, 쫓는 자와 쫓기는 자는 서로 일격이 닿을 거리를 두고 〈와이언도트족〉 마을로 들어섰다.\n\n제 거처가 눈앞에 있다는 데 흥분하고, 또 추격에 지친 〈휴런족〉은 이제 멈춰 서서, 절망의 광기로 제 평의회소 둘레에서 싸웠다. 그 들이침과 결말은 회오리바람이 휩쓸고 지나가며 파괴하는 것과도 같았다. 〈언카스〉의 토마호크, 〈호크아이〉의 일격, 심지어 아직 힘이 남은 〈먼로〉의 팔까지, 그 스쳐 가는 순간 모두가 분주했고, 땅은 곧 그들의 적으로 어지러이 뒤덮였다. 그럼에도 〈머과〉는, 대담하게 몸을 한껏 드러내고도, 옛 시가의 전설에서 총애받는 영웅들의 운명을 보살피려 마련된 듯한 그 일종의 신화적 가호로, 제 목숨을 노린 온갖 공격에서 벗어났다. 제 동료들이 쓰러진 것을 본 그 교활한 추장은, 분노와 실망을 가득 토해 내는 함성을 지르며, 살아남은 단 둘뿐인 벗을 거느리고 그곳에서 잽싸게 달아났으니, 〈델라웨어족〉은 죽은 자들에게서 승리의 피 묻은 전리품을 벗겨 내는 데 여념이 없었다.\n\n그러나 난전 속에서 그를 헛되이 찾던 〈언카스〉는 추격에 나서 앞으로 튀어 나갔고, 〈호크아이〉와 〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉가 여전히 그의 발자취를 바짝 좇았다. 정찰병이 할 수 있는 최선이라곤, 제 소총의 총구를 벗보다 약간 앞서 두는 것뿐이었으나, 그것은 〈언카스〉에게 어떤 마법의 방패와 다름없는 구실을 했다. 한 번 〈머과〉는 또 한 번, 그것도 마지막으로, 제 손실을 갚으려는 시도를 할 듯이 보였다. 그러나 그 뜻을 드러내기가 무섭게 단념하고, 그는 덤불 속으로 뛰어들었고, 적들이 그 사이로 그를 좇았으며, 그는 별안간 독자에게 이미 알려진 그 동굴 입구로 들어섰다. 오직 〈언카스〉를 아끼는 마음에서 사격을 삼갔던 〈호크아이〉는 성공의 함성을 지르며, 이제 사냥감을 확실히 잡았노라고 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 추격자들은 그 길고 좁은 입구로 뛰어들어, 물러가는 〈휴런족〉의 모습을 흘끗 잡을 만큼 때맞춰 들어섰다. 동굴의 자연적인 회랑과 지하의 방들을 지나는 그들의 통행에 앞서, 수백 명의 여자와 아이의 비명과 울부짖음이 먼저 터져 나왔다. 그곳은 그 흐릿하고 어렴풋한 빛 속에서, 불행한 망령들과 사나운 악귀들이 떼 지어 스쳐 다니는 지옥의 그늘처럼 보였다.\n\n여전히 〈언카스〉는, 마치 그에게 목숨이란 단 하나의 목표만을 지닌 양, 〈머과〉에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 〈헤이워드〉와 정찰병은, 아마 정도는 덜할지언정 같은 감정에 떠밀려, 여전히 그 뒤를 바짝 좇았다. 그러나 그 어둡고 음울한 통로에서 그들의 길은 점점 갈피를 잡기 어려워졌고, 물러가는 전사들이 보이는 것은 한결 흐릿하고 뜸해졌다. 그리하여 한순간 자취를 놓쳤다고 여겼을 때, 산 위로 이어지는 듯한 통로의 저 먼 끝에서 흰옷 한 자락이 펄럭이는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"〈코라〉다!\" 공포와 환희가 사납게 뒤엉킨 목소리로 〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤다.\n\n\"〈코라〉! 〈코라〉!\" 〈언카스〉가 사슴처럼 몸을 앞으로 굽히며 메아리처럼 따라 외쳤다.\n\n\"저 처녀다!\" 정찰병이 소리쳤다. \"용기를 내시오, 아가씨. 우리가 갑니다! 우리가 가요!\"\n\n이 사로잡힌 이의 모습을 흘끗 본 덕에 열 배는 더 힘이 솟은 부지런함으로 추격이 새로 시작되었다. 그러나 길은 험하고 끊긴 데다, 군데군데 거의 지날 수 없었다. 〈언카스〉는 제 소총을 버리고, 곤두박질치는 황급함으로 앞으로 뛰어들었다. 〈헤이워드〉도 무모하게 그 본보기를 따랐으나, 곧 잠시 후 두 사람은 그 미친 짓을 일깨움받았으니, 〈휴런족〉이 짬을 내어 바위 통로 아래로 쏜 총포의 굉음을 들었고, 그 총알이 젊은 〈모히칸족〉에게 가벼운 상처마저 입혔기 때문이다.\n\n\"바짝 다가가야 하네!\" 정찰병이 필사적인 도약으로 벗들을 지나치며 말했다. \"이 거리에서는 저 악당들이 우리를 하나씩 다 쏘아 떨어뜨릴 거야. 그리고 보게, 저놈들이 제 몸을 가리려고 저 처녀를 붙들고 있어!\"\n\n그의 말은 무시되었거나, 아니 오히려 들리지 않았으나, 그의 본보기는 동료들에게 따라졌으니, 그들은 믿기 어려운 분투로 도망자들에게 충분히 가까이 다가가, 〈코라〉가 두 전사 사이에 끼어 떠메여 가고, 〈머과〉가 그 도주의 방향과 방식을 일러 주고 있음을 알아보았다. 이 순간 넷의 모습이 하늘의 트인 틈을 배경으로 또렷이 그려졌다가, 사라졌다. 실망으로 거의 미칠 지경이 된 〈언카스〉와 〈헤이워드〉는, 이미 인간을 넘어선 듯하던 분투를 더욱 더했고, 쫓기는 자들의 경로를 알아챌 만큼 때맞춰 산허리 쪽 동굴에서 빠져나왔다. 그 길은 비탈을 올랐고, 여전히 위태롭고 고되었다.\n\n소총에 거추장스러웠던 데다, 어쩌면 동료들만큼 그 사로잡힌 이에게 깊은 관심을 품지 않았던 정찰병은, 동료들이 자기보다 조금 앞서게 두었고, 〈언카스〉는 이번에는 〈헤이워드〉보다 앞장섰다. 이런 식으로, 다른 때 다른 상황이었다면 거의 넘을 수 없다 여겨졌을 바위와 벼랑과 난관이 믿기 어려울 만큼 짧은 사이에 극복되었다. 그러나 성급한 젊은이들은, 〈코라〉에 거추장스러운 〈휴런족〉이 경주에서 땅을 잃고 있음을 알아내는 것으로 보답받았다.\n\n\"멈춰라, 〈와이언도트족〉의 개야!\" 〈언카스〉가 〈머과〉를 향해 제 번뜩이는 토마호크를 흔들며 외쳤다. \"한 〈델라웨어〉 처녀가 멈추라 한다!\"\n\n\"나는 더 가지 않겠다.\" 〈코라〉가 산마루에서 그리 멀지 않은, 깊은 벼랑 위로 내민 바위 턱에 뜻밖에 발을 디디며 외쳤다. \"죽이고 싶거든 죽여라, 가증스러운 〈휴런족〉아. 나는 더 가지 않겠다.\"\n\n그 처녀를 떠받치던 자들이, 마귀들이 해악에서 느낀다고들 하는 그 불경한 기쁨으로 손에 든 토마호크를 치켜들었으나, 〈머과〉가 그 쳐든 팔을 멈추게 했다. 그 〈휴런족〉 추장은 제 동료들에게서 빼앗은 무기들을 바위 너머로 던져 버린 뒤, 칼을 빼 들고, 서로 다투는 격정들이 사납게 맞부딪는 표정으로 제 포로에게 돌아섰다.\n\n\"여자여,\" 그가 말했다. \"골라라. 천막이냐, 〈르 쉬빌〉의 칼이냐!\"\n\n〈코라〉는 그를 거들떠보지 않고, 무릎을 꿇으며 눈을 들어 하늘을 향해 두 팔을 뻗고, 순하면서도 믿음에 찬 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"저는 당신의 것입니다! 당신이 가장 좋다고 여기는 대로 하소서!\"\n\n\"여자여.\" 〈머과〉가 쉰 목소리로 되풀이하며, 그 고요하고 빛나는 눈에서 한 줄기 눈길을 붙잡으려 헛되이 애썼다. \"골라라!\"\n\n그러나 〈코라〉는 그의 요구를 듣지도, 아랑곳하지도 않았다. 그 〈휴런족〉의 몸은 온 마디가 떨렸고, 그는 팔을 높이 쳐들었으나, 의심하는 자처럼 어리둥절한 기색으로 다시 떨어뜨렸다. 한 번 더 그는 제 자신과 씨름하며 그 날카로운 무기를 다시 들었다. 그러나 바로 그때 그들 위에서 찢어지는 듯한 외침이 들렸고, 〈언카스〉가 까마득한 높이에서 그 바위 턱 위로 미친 듯이 뛰어내리며 나타났다. 〈머과〉는 한 걸음 물러섰다. 그리고 그의 조력자 하나가 그 틈을 타서, 제 칼을 〈코라〉의 가슴에 박았다.\n\n그 〈휴런족〉은 죄를 저지르고 이미 물러서는 동족에게 호랑이처럼 덮쳤으나, 떨어지는 〈언카스〉의 몸이 그 패륜의 싸움꾼들을 갈라놓았다. 이 방해로 제 목표에서 빗나가고, 방금 목격한 그 살인에 미쳐 버린 〈머과〉는, 엎어진 〈델라웨어〉인의 등에 제 무기를 묻으며, 그 비열한 짓을 저지를 적에 이 세상 것이 아닌 듯한 함성을 내질렀다. 그러나 〈언카스〉는, 상처 입은 표범이 제 적에게 돌아서듯 그 일격에서 일어나, 〈코라〉를 죽인 자를, 스러져 가는 마지막 힘을 다 쏟은 안간힘으로 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다. 그러고는 준엄하고 흔들림 없는 눈길로 〈르 쉬빌〉에게 돌아서서, 힘이 자기를 저버리지만 않았더라면 무엇을 하려 했는지를 그 눈빛으로 드러냈다. 〈르 쉬빌〉은 저항하지 않는 〈델라웨어〉인의 맥 풀린 팔을 붙잡고, 제 칼을 그의 가슴에 세 차례나 거듭 찔러 넣었으니, 그 제물은 꺼지지 않는 경멸의 눈빛으로 적에게서 눈길을 떼지 않은 채, 그의 발치에 쓰러져 숨졌다.\n\n\"자비를! 자비를, 〈휴런족〉아!\" 공포에 거의 목이 멘 목소리로 〈헤이워드〉가 위에서 외쳤다. \"자비를 베풀라, 그러면 너도 자비를 받으리라!\"\n\n피 묻은 칼을 그 애원하는 젊은이를 향해 휙 치켜들며, 승리한 〈머과〉는 어찌나 사납고, 어찌나 거칠며, 그러면서도 어찌나 기쁨에 찬 외침을 내질렀던지, 그것은 천 피트 아래 골짜기에서 싸우던 자들의 귀에까지 야만적 승리의 소리를 실어 보냈다. 그에게 정찰병의 입에서 터져 나온 한마디가 답했으니, 그의 큰 키의 몸이 바로 그때, 마치 공중을 디딜 힘이라도 지닌 양 대담하고 무모한 발걸음으로, 그 위험한 바위들을 따라 그를 향해 빠르게 움직이는 것이 보였다. 그러나 사냥꾼이 그 무자비한 학살의 현장에 다다랐을 때, 그 바위 턱에는 죽은 자들만이 있었다.\n\n그의 날카로운 눈은 희생자들을 단 한 번 보고 나서, 제 앞에 놓인 험한 오르막으로 눈길을 쏘았다. 한 형상이 산마루에, 그 아찔한 높이의 바로 가장자리에, 두 팔을 쳐든 채 위협의 무시무시한 자세로 서 있었다. 그가 누구인지 가늠하려 멈추지도 않고 〈호크아이〉의 소총이 들렸으나, 아래쪽 도망자 하나의 머리 위로 떨어진 바위 하나가 정직한 〈거멋〉의 성난, 달아오른 얼굴을 드러냈다. 그때 〈머과〉가 바위틈에서 나와, 제 동료들 가운데 마지막 자의 시신을 차분한 무심함으로 넘어, 넓은 갈라진 틈을 뛰어, 〈데이비드〉의 팔이 닿을 수 없는 지점에서 바위를 기어올랐다. 단 한 번의 도약이면 그를 벼랑마루로 데려가, 그의 안전을 보장해 줄 터였다. 그러나 그 도약을 하기에 앞서, 그 〈휴런족〉은 멈추어, 정찰병을 향해 손을 흔들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"창백한 얼굴들은 개다! 〈델라웨어족〉은 여자들이다! 〈머과〉가 까마귀 밥으로 저들을 바위 위에 남겨 두노라!\"\n\n쉰 소리로 웃으며, 그는 필사적으로 뛰었으나 제 목표에 미치지 못했다. 다만 그의 손이 그 높이의 가장자리에 난 관목을 움켜쥐었다. 〈호크아이〉의 몸은 막 덮치려는 짐승처럼 웅크렸고, 그의 몸은 열망으로 어찌나 격하게 떨렸던지, 반쯤 든 소총의 총구가 바람에 나부끼는 잎사귀처럼 흔들렸다. 헛된 안간힘으로 제 힘을 소진하지 않고, 교활한 〈머과〉는 제 몸을 두 팔 길이만큼 늘어뜨려, 발을 디딜 바위 조각을 찾아냈다. 그러고는 온 힘을 모아 다시 시도하여, 무릎을 산의 가장자리에 끌어 올릴 만큼 성공했다. 적의 몸이 가장 한데 모인 바로 그때, 정찰병의 떨리던 무기가 그의 어깨로 끌어 올려졌다. 그것이 그 안의 것을 쏟아 낸 단 한순간만큼은, 둘레의 바위들조차 그 총만큼 미동도 없지는 못했다. 그 〈휴런족〉의 팔에서 힘이 풀렸고, 무릎은 여전히 제 자리를 지킨 채 몸이 약간 뒤로 넘어갔다. 무자비한 눈길을 제 적에게 돌리며, 그는 음산한 항거로 한 손을 흔들었다. 그러나 그 움켜쥔 손이 풀렸고, 그의 거뭇한 몸이 머리를 아래로 한 채 허공을 가르는 것이, 산에 매달린 관목의 가장자리를 스쳐 지나 파멸을 향해 곤두박질치기까지, 스쳐 가는 한순간 보였다.\n[29] 말: 기병(騎兵)을 뜻한다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII\n\n그들은 용감한 사내들처럼 오래도록 훌륭하게 싸웠고,\n이슬람의 주검으로 그 땅을 뒤덮었다.\n\n그들은 정복했으나 — 보자리스는 쓰러졌으니,\n온몸의 핏줄에서 피를 흘리며.\n\n살아남은 몇 안 되는 전우들은\n자랑스러운 만세 소리가 울리고\n\n붉은 전장이 정복되었을 때 그의 미소를 보았고,\n이윽고 죽음 속에 그의 눈꺼풀이 감기는 것을 보았으니,\n\n해 질 녘 꽃처럼,\n밤의 안식으로 드는 듯 고요하게.\n\n핼럭\n이튿날, 해는 슬픔에 잠긴 한 민족이 된 〈레나페〉를 비추었다. 싸움의 소리는 끝났고, 그들은 한 부족 전체를 멸함으로써 오랜 원한을 흡족히 채웠으며, 최근에 〈멩궤〉와 벌인 분쟁을 갚았다. 〈휴런〉족이 진을 쳤던 자리를 떠도는 검고 음산한 기운만으로도 그 유랑 부족의 운명은 충분히 드러났다. 한편 황량한 산봉우리 위로 다투어 날거나 시끄러운 떼를 이루어 드넓은 숲의 능선들을 가로질러 휩쓸어 가는 수백 마리의 까마귀가, 그 전투의 현장으로 향하는 끔찍한 길잡이가 되었다. 요컨대, 변경 전쟁의 징후에 조금이라도 단련된 눈이라면, 인디언의 복수에 뒤따르는 무자비한 결과를 가리키는 그 모든 분명한 증거를 어렵잖게 읽어 낼 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n그럼에도 해는 슬픔에 잠긴 한 민족이 된 〈레나페〉 위로 떠올랐다. 승리를 기뻐하는 어떤 함성도, 어떤 개선의 노래도 들리지 않았다. 가장 늦은 낙오자조차 그 잔혹한 일을 마치고 돌아왔으나, 그것은 다만 제 피비린내 나는 소임의 무시무시한 표식을 벗어 버리고, 비탄에 빠진 한 민족으로서 동족의 애도에 함께하기 위함이었다. 자긍과 환희는 겸비로 대체되었고, 인간의 격정 가운데 가장 사나운 것은 이미 가장 깊고 분명한 슬픔의 표현에 자리를 내주었다.\n오두막들은 텅 비었으나, 진지에서 가까운 한 자리를 진지한 얼굴들의 너른 띠가 에워싸고 있었으니, 생명을 지닌 모든 것이 그리로 모여들어, 이제 모두가 깊고 엄숙한 침묵 속에 한데 모여 있었다. 온갖 신분과 나이, 남녀와 갖가지 생업을 지닌 자들이 하나로 어우러져 이 살아 숨 쉬는 인간의 벽을 이루었으나, 그들은 하나의 감정에 사로잡혀 있었다. 저마다의 눈은, 그토록 크고 그토록 공통된 관심의 대상을 품은 그 둥근 자리의 한가운데에 못 박혀 있었다.\n\n길고 검은 머리채를 가슴 위로 풀어헤친 여섯 〈델라웨어〉 처녀가 한쪽에 따로 서서, 이따금 향기로운 풀로 엮은 들것 위에 향내 나는 풀잎과 숲의 꽃들을 흩뿌릴 때만 그 존재를 드러냈으니, 인디언의 가운 천을 덮은 그 들것은 이제 열정에 차고 고결하며 너그러웠던 〈코라〉의 남은 전부를 받치고 있었다. 그녀의 몸은 똑같이 소박하게 지은 천 여러 겹에 가려졌고, 그 얼굴은 사람들의 시선으로부터 영원히 닫혀 버렸다. 그녀의 발치에는 비탄에 빠진 〈먼로〉가 앉아 있었다. 그의 늙은 머리는 섭리의 일격에 어쩔 수 없이 굴복하여 거의 땅에 닿도록 숙여졌으나, 주름진 이마에는 감춰진 고뇌가 일렁였으니, 그것은 무심히 관자놀이에 흘러내린 회색 머리타래로도 그저 일부만 가려질 뿐이었다. 그 곁에는 〈거뮤트〉가, 햇살에 온순한 머리를 드러낸 채 서 있었고, 그의 눈은 방황하며 근심에 차서, 기이하면서도 거룩한 잠언이 그토록 많이 담긴 그 작은 책과, 그의 영혼이 위로를 베풀기를 갈망하는 그 존재 사이에서 똑같이 갈라지는 듯했다. 〈헤이워드〉 역시 가까이에 있었으니, 나무에 몸을 기댄 채, 사내다움을 다해 억눌러야만 했던 슬픔의 갑작스러운 북받침을 가라앉히려 애쓰고 있었다.\n\n그러나 이 무리가 아무리 비통하고 우울하게 그려진다 한들, 같은 자리의 맞은편을 차지한 또 다른 무리에 비하면 한결 덜 가슴 아픈 것이었다. 부족의 부로 마련할 수 있는 가장 화려한 장식으로 단장한 채, 살아 있을 때처럼 그 몸과 사지를 엄숙하고 단정한 자세로 갖추어 앉은 〈언카스〉가 거기 있었다. 화려한 깃털이 그의 머리 위에서 흔들렸고, 조가비 구슬과 목가리개, 팔찌와 메달이 그의 몸을 넘치도록 꾸몄으나, 그 흐릿한 눈과 텅 빈 이목구비는 그것들이 전하려는 자긍의 헛된 이야기를 너무도 강하게 반박했다.\n주검 바로 앞에는 〈칭가치국〉이 자리하고 있었으니, 그의 벌거벗은 가슴에 지울 수 없이 새겨진 제 종족의 선명한 푸른 문장 말고는, 무기도, 칠도, 어떤 장식도 없었다. 부족이 이렇게 모여 있던 긴 시간 내내, 이 〈모히칸〉 전사는 차갑고 생기 없는 아들의 얼굴에 한결같이 근심 어린 눈길을 두고 있었다. 그 응시가 어찌나 못 박힌 듯 강렬했고 그 자세가 어찌나 변함없었던지, 낯선 이라면 산 자와 죽은 자를 분간하지 못했을 것이다. 다만 한쪽의 어두운 얼굴을 가로질러 이따금 번득이는 어지러운 영혼의 빛과, 다른 한쪽의 이목구비에 영원히 내려앉은 죽음 같은 고요만이 그 구분을 일러 주었다.\n\n척후는 가까이서, 제 운명적이고 복수에 찬 무기에 시름겨운 자세로 기대어 있었다. 한편 〈타메넌드〉는 부족의 원로들에게 부축을 받으며 곁의 높은 자리를 차지하고 있었으니, 그곳에서 그는 말없이 슬픔에 잠긴 제 백성의 무리를 내려다볼 수 있었다.\n\n그 둥근 자리의 안쪽 가장자리 바로 안에는 한 군인이, 낯선 나라의 군복 차림으로 서 있었다. 그 바깥에는 그의 군마가, 멀리 길을 떠날 채비를 갖춘 듯한 말 탄 시종들의 한가운데에 있었다. 이 낯선 자의 복장은, 그가 〈캐나다〉군 지휘관의 신변에서 막중한 직책을 맡은 자임을 드러냈으니, 이제 보아하니, 동맹군의 사나운 격정으로 평화의 임무가 좌절된 것을 알고는, 너무 늦게 도착해 미리 막지 못한 한 싸움의 결실을 말없이 슬프게 지켜보는 구경꾼이 되는 데 만족한 듯했다.\n\n날은 첫 사분(四分)의 끝으로 기울고 있었으나, 그래도 무리는 동틀 녘부터 숨죽인 정적을 지키고 있었다. 그들 가운데서는 억눌린 흐느낌보다 큰 소리는 들리지 않았고, 그 길고 고통스러운 시간 내내 그 누구도 사지 하나 움직이지 않았으니, 다만 죽은 자를 기리며 이따금 바치는 소박하고 가슴 저린 헌물을 올릴 때만은 예외였다. 이제 검고 미동도 없는 형상 하나하나를 돌로 바꾸어 놓은 듯한 이러한 망연한 모습을 지탱할 수 있는 것은, 오직 인디언의 굳센 인내와 자제뿐이었다.\n마침내 〈델라웨어〉의 현인이 한쪽 팔을 뻗어, 시종들의 어깨에 기댄 채 일어섰으니, 어제 제 민족을 만났던 그 사람과 지금 높은 단 위에서 비틀거리는 그 사람 사이에 이미 또 한 시대가 끼어든 듯이, 그토록 쇠약한 기색이었다.\n\n\"〈레나페〉의 사람들이여!\" 그는 어떤 예언의 사명을 짊어진 목소리처럼 울리는, 공허한 어조로 말했다. \"〈마니토〉의 얼굴이 구름 뒤에 있도다! 그의 눈은 너희에게서 돌아섰고, 그의 귀는 닫혔으며, 그의 혀는 답하지 않는다. 너희가 그를 보지 못하나, 그의 심판은 너희 앞에 있도다. 너희 마음을 열고, 너희 영혼이 거짓을 말하지 않게 하라. 〈레나페〉의 사람들이여! 〈마니토〉의 얼굴이 구름 뒤에 있도다.\"\n이 소박하면서도 무시무시한 선포가 무리의 귀에 스며들자, 그들이 섬기는 그 우러르는 신령이 인간의 입을 빌리지 않고 직접 그 말을 발한 듯이, 그만큼 깊고 엄숙한 정적이 뒤따랐다. 그리하여 생기 없는 〈언카스〉조차, 그를 에워싼 겸허하고 순종하는 무리에 비하면 살아 있는 존재처럼 보였다. 그러나 그 직접적인 효과가 차츰 가시자, 나직한 목소리들의 웅성거림이 죽은 자를 기리는 일종의 노래를 시작했다. 그 소리는 여인들의 것으로, 가슴을 저미도록 부드럽고 애절했다. 그 말들은 어떤 규칙적인 이음새로 연결되지는 않았으나, 한 사람이 그치면 다른 사람이 찬가든 애가든 — 무어라 불리든 간에 — 그것을 이어받아, 제 감정과 그 자리가 일러 주는 말로 격정을 토해 냈다. 이따금 말하는 이는 모두가 터뜨리는 큰 슬픔의 폭발에 끊겼는데, 그러는 동안 〈코라〉의 관 둘레에 선 처녀들은, 슬픔에 정신이 나간 듯, 그 몸에서 풀과 꽃을 무턱대고 뜯어냈다. 그러나 통곡이 잦아드는 순간에는, 이 순결함과 다정함의 표식들을, 갖은 애틋함과 회한의 기색으로 다시 제자리에 놓았다. 잦고 잦은 끊김과 폭발로 한결 짜임새가 흐트러지긴 했어도, 그들의 말을 옮긴다면 정연한 영창이 담겼을 것이며, 그 내용은 본래 일관된 생각의 줄기를 지니고 있었음이 드러났을 것이다.\n\n신분과 자질로 그 소임에 뽑힌 한 처녀가, 죽은 전사의 자질을 겸손히 빗대며 시작하여, 인디언들이 아마도 저 다른 대륙의 끝에서 가져왔을, 그리하여 그 자체로 두 세계의 옛 역사를 잇는 고리를 이루는 동방의 심상으로 그 표현을 꾸몄다. 그녀는 그를 \"제 부족의 표범\"이라 불렀고, 그의 모카신이 이슬 위에 자국을 남기지 않았으며, 그의 도약이 어린 새끼 사슴의 도약과 같았고, 그의 눈이 캄캄한 밤의 별보다 밝았으며, 싸움터에서 그의 목소리가 〈마니토〉의 천둥처럼 우렁찼다고 노래했다. 그녀는 그를 낳은 어머니를 그에게 일깨우며, 그런 아들을 둔 어머니가 느꼈을 행복을 힘주어 이야기했다. 그녀는 그에게, 영혼들의 세계에서 만나거든, 〈델라웨어〉 처녀들이 그 아이의 무덤 위에 눈물을 흘렸으며 그녀를 복되다 불렀노라고 어머니에게 전해 달라 청했다.\n\n이어서 뒤를 잇는 이들은, 그 음조를 한결 부드럽고 더욱 다정한 가락으로 바꾸어, 여인의 섬세함과 예민함으로, 그 자신의 떠남에 그토록 가까운 때에 이승을 떠나, 〈위대한 영〉의 뜻이 너무도 분명하여 거스를 수 없게 한 그 낯선 처녀를 언급했다. 그들은 그에게 그녀에게 다정하라고, 그와 같은 전사에게 그토록 긴요한 기예들에 그녀가 서툰 것을 헤아려 주라고 권했다. 그들은 그녀의 견줄 데 없는 아름다움과 그 고결한 결의를, 시샘의 기색 없이, 천사들이 더 높은 탁월함을 기뻐하리라 여겨지듯 이야기했고, 이러한 자질들이 그녀가 받은 가르침의 자그마한 미흡함을 넉넉히 메우고도 남으리라 덧붙였다.\n그런 다음, 다시 다른 이들이 차례로, 다정과 사랑이 담긴 낮고 부드러운 말로, 그 처녀 자신에게 이야기했다. 그들은 그녀에게 마음을 즐겁게 가지라고, 앞날의 안녕을 조금도 두려워 말라고 권면했다. 그녀의 가장 작은 필요까지 헤아릴 줄 아는 사냥꾼이 동무가 될 것이며, 온갖 위험으로부터 그녀를 지킬 수 있는 전사가 그 곁에 있다고 했다. 그들은 그녀의 길이 즐겁고 그 짐이 가벼우리라 약속했다. 그들은 그녀에게, 젊은 시절의 벗들과 조상이 살던 땅을 부질없이 그리워 말라고 일렀으며, 〈레나페〉의 \"복된 사냥터\"에는 \"창백한 얼굴들의 천국\"만큼이나 즐거운 골짜기와 맑은 시냇물과 향기로운 꽃이 있다고 다짐해 주었다. 그들은 그녀에게 동무의 필요를 살피라고, 그리고 〈마니토〉가 그 둘 사이에 그토록 지혜롭게 세워 둔 구별을 결코 잊지 말라고 일렀다. 그러고는 노래의 격렬한 폭발 속에서, 그들은 한목소리로 〈모히칸〉의 심성을 노래했다. 그들은 그를 고결하고 사내답고 너그럽다고, 전사다운 모든 것이며 처녀가 사랑할 만한 모든 것이라 일컬었다. 그들은 가장 멀고 미묘한 심상으로 제 생각을 감싸면서도, 그 짧은 교제의 기간 동안, 제 성(性) 특유의 직관적 통찰로, 그의 마음이 다른 데로 기울었음을 알아챘노라고 드러냈다. 〈델라웨어〉 처녀들은 그의 눈에 들지 못했던 것이다! 그는 한때 소금 호숫가의 주인이었던 종족이었으니, 그의 바람은 그를 조상들의 무덤 둘레에 사는 한 백성에게로 이끌었다. 어찌 그런 끌림을 북돋우지 않으랴! 그녀가 제 민족의 다른 이들보다 더 순수하고 고귀한 피를 지녔음은 누구의 눈에도 보였을 것이며, 그녀가 숲에서의 삶이 지닌 위험과 대담함에 걸맞은 이였음은 그녀의 행실이 증명했다. 그리고 이제, 그들은 덧붙이기를, \"이 땅의 현명한 이\"가 그녀를 마음 맞는 영혼들을 만날 곳으로 옮겨 심었으니, 그녀는 영원히 행복하리라고 했다.\n\n그러고는, 목소리와 주제를 또 한 번 바꾸어, 이웃한 오두막에서 울고 있는 처녀에게 빗대는 말이 나왔다. 그들은 그녀를 눈송이에 견주었으니, 순결하고 희고 빛나며, 여름의 사나운 더위에 녹기 쉽고 겨울의 서리에 얼어붙기 쉽다고 했다. 그들은 그 살결과 슬픔이 그토록 그녀를 닮은 젊은 추장의 눈에 그녀가 사랑스러웠으리라 의심치 않았으나, 그런 편애를 드러내기는커녕, 그들이 애도하는 처녀보다 그녀를 덜 빼어난 이로 여기는 것이 분명했다. 그래도 그들은 그 보기 드문 매력이 마땅히 받을 만한 어떤 찬사도 그녀에게서 아끼지 않았다. 그녀의 곱슬머리는 포도덩굴의 무성한 덩굴손에 비유되었고, 그 눈은 푸른 하늘의 궁창에, 그리고 햇살로 발갛게 물든 가장 티 없는 구름조차 그녀의 화사함보다는 덜 매혹적이라 여겨졌다.\n\n이와 비슷한 노래들이 이어지는 동안에는, 음악의 웅성거림 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않았으니, 그것은 이따금 터지는 슬픔의 폭발 — 그 노래의 합창이라 할 만한 것 — 로 누그러지거나, 차라리 더욱 무시무시해졌다. 〈델라웨어〉인들 자신도 흘린 듯 귀 기울였고, 그 말하는 듯한 얼굴들의 변화로 미루어, 그들의 연민이 얼마나 깊고 참된 것인지는 너무도 분명했다. 〈데이비드〉조차 그토록 감미로운 목소리의 가락에 귀를 기울이기를 마다하지 않았으니, 노래가 끝나기 한참 전에, 그의 시선은 이미 그 영혼이 사로잡혔음을 알리고 있었다.\n\n백인들 가운데 유일하게 그 말을 알아듣는 척후는, 사색에 잠긴 자세에서 조금 깨어나, 처녀들이 노래를 이어 가는 동안 그 뜻을 새기려 얼굴을 옆으로 기울였다. 그러나 그들이 〈코라〉와 〈언카스〉의 앞날을 이야기할 때면, 그는 그들의 소박한 믿음의 그릇됨을 아는 자처럼 고개를 가로저었고, 다시 기댄 자세로 돌아가, 그 의식이 — 감정이 그토록 깊이 배어든 것을 의식이라 부를 수 있다면 — 끝날 때까지 그 자세를 지켰다. 〈헤이워드〉와 〈먼로〉 두 사람의 자제를 위해서는 다행히도, 그들은 자신들이 듣는 그 격렬한 소리의 뜻을 알지 못했다.\n\n〈칭가치국〉은 청중 가운데 원주민들이 드러내는 관심에서 홀로 예외였다. 그 장면 내내 그의 표정은 결코 변하지 않았고, 애가의 가장 격렬하거나 가장 애절한 대목에서도 그 굳은 얼굴에서는 근육 하나 움직이지 않았다. 차갑고 생기 없는 아들의 유해가 그에게는 전부였으며, 시각 말고 다른 모든 감각은 얼어붙은 듯했으니, 이는 그토록 오래 사랑했고 이제 곧 그의 시야에서 영원히 닫혀 버릴 그 얼굴을, 그의 눈이 마지막으로 응시하게 하려 함이었다.\n\n장례 의식의 이 단계에서, 무공으로 명성이 높고 특히 최근의 전투에서 세운 공로로 이름난 한 전사가, 준엄하고 엄숙한 풍모를 띤 채, 무리에서 천천히 걸어 나와 죽은 자의 곁에 자리했다.\n\n\"어찌하여 그대는 우리를 떠났는가, 〈와파나치〉의 자랑이여?\" 그는 빈 흙덩이가 살아 있는 사람의 능력을 그대로 간직한 듯, 〈언카스〉의 흐릿한 귀에 대고 말했다. \"그대의 세월은 나무에 걸린 해와 같았고, 그대의 영광은 한낮의 그 빛보다 밝았다. 그대는 갔으나, 젊은 전사여, 일백의 〈와이언도트〉인이 영혼들의 세계로 가는 그대의 길에서 가시덤불을 치우고 있다. 싸움터에서 그대를 본 자라면 누가 그대가 죽을 수 있다고 믿었으랴? 그대 이전에 누가 〈우타와〉에게 싸움터로 드는 길을 보여 주었던가? 그대의 발은 독수리의 날개 같았고, 그대의 팔은 소나무에서 떨어지는 가지보다 무거웠으며, 그대의 목소리는 구름 속에서 말하는 〈마니토〉와 같았다. 〈우타와〉의 혀는 약하고,\" 그는 우울한 눈길로 사방을 둘러보며 덧붙였다. \"그의 마음은 더없이 무겁다. 〈와파나치〉의 자랑이여, 어찌하여 그대는 우리를 떠났는가?\"\n\n그의 뒤를 다른 이들이 차례로 이어, 마침내 부족의 높고 재능 있는 사람들이 거의 다, 죽은 추장의 넋 위로 찬사의 헌사를 노래하거나 읊었다. 저마다 끝마칠 때면, 그곳 전체에 또다시 깊고 숨죽인 침묵이 깃들었다.\n\n이윽고 나직하고 깊은 소리가 들렸으니, 멀리서 들려오는 음악의 억눌린 반주 같았고, 겨우 들릴 만큼만 공기 중에 떠올랐으되, 너무도 흐릿하여 그 성격도, 그것이 나오는 곳도 다 같이 짐작거리로 남았다. 그러나 그 소리에 이어 또 한 가락, 다시 한 가락이 잇따랐으니, 저마다 한 단계 높은 음으로, 마침내 귀에 자라났다. 처음에는 길게 끌며 자주 되풀이되는 감탄으로, 끝내는 말로. 〈칭가치국〉의 입술이 그만큼 벌어진 것이, 그것이 아비의 애가임을 알렸다. 그를 향해 돌아선 눈은 하나도 없었고, 조바심의 기색이라곤 조금도 보이지 않았으나, 무리가 귀 기울이려 머리를 치켜드는 모습으로 미루어, 그들이 〈타메넌드〉 자신 말고는 일찍이 그 누구도 누리지 못한 강렬한 주의로 그 소리를 들이마시고 있음이 분명했다. 그러나 그들은 헛되이 귀 기울였다. 그 가락은 겨우 알아들을 만큼 커졌다가, 다시 더 희미하고 더 떨리게 잦아들어, 끝내 지나가는 한 줄기 바람결에 실려 가듯 귀에서 가라앉고 말았다. 〈세이거모어〉의 입술이 닫혔고, 그는 자리에 말없이 앉은 채로, 그 못 박힌 눈과 미동 없는 형상으로, 사람의 형상은 갖추었으되 사람의 영혼은 없이 전능한 손에서 빚어져 나온 어떤 피조물처럼 보였다. 이러한 징후로 제 벗의 마음이 그토록 거대한 인내의 노력에 채비되지 못했음을 알아챈 〈델라웨어〉인들은 주의를 거두었고, 타고난 섬세함으로, 모든 생각을 그 낯선 처녀의 장례로 돌리는 듯했다.\n\n원로 추장 가운데 한 사람이, 〈코라〉의 시신이 놓인 자리 가까이 둥근 자리에 몰려 있던 여인들에게 신호를 보냈다. 그 신호에 따라, 처녀들은 들것을 머리 높이로 들어 올려, 느리고 절도 있는 걸음으로 나아가며, 죽은 이를 기리는 또 다른 애도의 노래를 부르며 걸었다. 더없이 이교적이라 여긴 그 의식을 가까이서 지켜보던 〈거뮤트〉가, 이제 정신 나간 아비의 어깨 너머로 고개를 숙여 속삭였다.\n\n\"저들이 그대의 아이의 유해를 옮기고 있습니다. 우리도 따라가, 그 아이가 기독교식 장례로 묻히는 것을 보아야 하지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n〈먼로〉는 마지막 나팔이 그의 귀에 울리기라도 한 듯 움찔하더니, 근심에 차서 황급히 한 번 사방을 둘러본 뒤, 일어나 그 소박한 행렬을 따랐다. 군인다운 풍모였으나, 아비의 고통이라는 짐을 온전히 짊어진 채였다. 그의 벗들이 너무도 강렬하여 연민이라 부를 수 없는 슬픔으로 그의 둘레로 모여들었으니 — 그 젊은 프랑스인조차, 그토록 사랑스러운 이의 이르고 애처로운 운명에 절실히 마음 아파하는 자의 기색으로 행렬에 끼었다. 그러나 부족의 마지막이자 가장 미천한 여인까지 그 격렬하면서도 정연한 대열에 합류하자, 〈레나페〉의 사내들은 그 둥근 자리를 좁혀, 다시 〈언카스〉의 곁으로 둘러섰으니, 이전처럼 말없고, 엄숙하며, 미동도 없었다.\n\n〈코라〉의 무덤으로 골라 둔 자리는, 젊고 싱싱한 소나무들이 무리지어 뿌리 내려, 그 자리 위에 절로 우울하고도 어울리는 그늘을 드리운 자그마한 둔덕이었다. 거기에 이르자 처녀들은 짐을 내려놓고, 여러 분 동안, 인디언 특유의 인내와 타고난 수줍음으로, 가장 깊이 마음 쓰는 이들이 그 마련에 흡족해한다는 어떤 기색을 기다렸다. 마침내 그들의 풍습을 홀로 아는 척후가, 그들의 말로 일렀다.\n\n\"내 딸들이 잘해 주었소. 백인들이 그대들에게 고마워하오.\"\n\n이 호의의 증언에 만족한 처녀들은, 자작나무 껍질로 솜씨 있게, 또 제법 우아하게 엮어 만든 함에 시신을 안치하기 시작했고, 그런 다음 그것을 어둡고 마지막 거처로 내려놓았다. 유해를 덮고, 잎사귀와 그 밖의 자연스럽고 관습적인 것들로 갓 파낸 흙의 자취를 가리는 의식은, 똑같이 소박하고 말 없는 절차로 치러졌다. 그러나 이 슬프고도 다정한 소임을 행한 이 친절한 이들의 일이 그만큼 끝나자, 그들은 어디까지 더 나아가야 할지 모른다는 듯 머뭇거렸다. 척후가 다시 그들에게 말을 건넨 것은 의식의 이 단계에서였다.\n\n\"내 젊은 여인들은 할 만큼 했소.\" 그가 말했다. \"창백한 얼굴의 영혼은 음식도 옷도 필요치 않으니, 그 베풂은 제 살빛의 천국을 따르는 법이오. 보아하니,\" 그는 거룩한 노래로 앞장설 뜻을 내비치며 제 책을 마련하던 〈데이비드〉에게 눈길을 던지며 덧붙였다. \"기독교의 방식을 더 잘 아는 이가 곧 말하려 하는구려.\"\n\n여인들은 조심스레 옆으로 물러나, 그 장면의 주된 행위자였다가 이제는 뒤따르는 일을 지켜보는 온순하고 주의 깊은 구경꾼이 되었다. 〈데이비드〉가 이러한 방식으로 제 영혼의 경건한 감정을 쏟아 내는 동안, 그들에게서는 놀라움의 기색도, 조바심의 표정도 새어 나오지 않았다. 그들은 그 낯선 말의 뜻을 아는 자들처럼 귀 기울였고, 그 말이 전하려는 슬픔과 희망과 체념의 뒤섞인 감정을 느끼는 듯했다.\n\n방금 목격한 장면에 마음이 동하고, 어쩌면 제 은밀한 감정에도 이끌려, 노래의 명수는 평소의 노력을 뛰어넘었다. 그의 가득 차고 풍부한 목소리는 처녀들의 부드러운 음조에 견주어도 손색이 없었고, 그의 한결 절제된 가락은, 적어도 그 노래가 유난히 향했던 이들의 귀에는, 알아들을 수 있다는 그 곁들인 힘을 지니고 있었다. 그는 시작했을 때와 마찬가지로, 엄숙하고 장중한 정적 한가운데서 찬가를 마쳤다.\n\n그러나 그 마무리의 가락이 청중의 귀에 내려앉자, 그 은밀하고 겁먹은 눈길과, 무리의 전반적이면서도 억눌린 움직임이, 죽은 이의 아비에게서 무언가를 기대하고 있음을 드러냈다. 〈먼로〉는, 어쩌면 인간 본성이 감당할 수 있는 가장 큰 노력일 그것을 발휘할 때가 왔음을 느끼는 듯했다. 그는 회색 머리타래를 드러내고, 자신을 에워싼 겁먹고 고요한 무리를 굳세고 침착한 얼굴로 둘러보았다. 그러고는 척후에게 손짓으로 들으라 이르며 말했다.\n\n\"이 친절하고 다정한 여인들에게 전하시오, 가슴이 무너지고 쇠해 가는 한 사내가 감사를 드린다고. 그들에게 전하시오, 우리 모두가 저마다 다른 이름으로 섬기는 그 존재께서 그들의 자비를 기억하실 것이며, 우리가 성(性)도 신분도 살빛도 구별 없이 그분의 보좌 둘레에 모이는 때가 머지않으리라고.\"\n\n척후는 노병이 이 말을 전하는 그 떨리는 목소리에 귀 기울이더니, 말이 끝나자 그 효험을 의심하는 자처럼 천천히 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"이걸 그들에게 전한다는 건,\" 그가 말했다. \"겨울에 눈이 오지 않는다거나, 나무에서 잎이 다 떨어졌을 때 해가 가장 사납게 빛난다고 말하는 것이나 다름없소.\"\n\n그러고는 여인들을 향해, 듣는 이들의 이해력에 가장 잘 맞으리라 여겨지는 대로, 노병의 감사를 전했다. 〈먼로〉의 머리는 이미 가슴에 떨궈졌고, 그가 다시 빠르게 우울에 잠겨 들 무렵, 앞서 말한 젊은 프랑스인이 조심스레 그의 팔꿈치를 가볍게 건드렸다. 슬픔에 잠긴 노인의 주의를 끌자마자, 그는 가볍지만 단단히 덮인 들것을 들고 다가오는 한 무리의 젊은 인디언들을 가리킨 다음, 위쪽 해를 가리켰다.\n\n\"무슨 말인지 알겠소, 선생.\" 〈먼로〉가 억지로 다잡은 목소리로 답했다. \"알겠소. 이는 하늘의 뜻이니, 나는 따르겠소. 〈코라〉, 내 딸아! 가슴 무너진 아비의 기도가 지금 네게 닿을 수 있다면, 너는 얼마나 복되랴! 갑시다, 여러분.\" 그는 고결한 침착의 기색으로 사방을 둘러보며 덧붙였으나, 그 빛바랜 얼굴에 떨리는 고뇌는 너무도 강하여 가릴 수가 없었다. \"이곳에서 우리의 소임은 끝났소. 떠납시다.\"\n\n〈헤이워드〉는, 순간순간 자제력이 곧 자신을 저버리려 함을 느끼던 그 자리에서 그들을 데려가 줄 그 부름에 기꺼이 따랐다. 그러나 동료들이 말에 오르는 동안, 그는 척후의 손을 마주 잡고, 영국군 진지 안에서 다시 만나기로 한 약속의 조건을 되풀이할 짬을 냈다. 그러고는 기꺼이 안장에 몸을 던져, 나직하고 억눌린 흐느낌만이 〈앨리스〉의 존재를 알리는 그 들것 곁으로 군마를 몰았다. 이리하여, 〈먼로〉의 머리가 다시 가슴에 떨궈진 채, 〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉가 슬픔에 잠긴 침묵 속에 뒤따르고, 〈몽칼름〉의 부관이 호위병을 거느리고 함께한 가운데, 〈호크아이〉를 제외한 모든 백인이 〈델라웨어〉인들의 눈앞에서 사라져, 이내 그 고장의 광막한 숲속에 파묻혔다.\n\n그러나 공통된 재난을 통해, 숲의 이 소박한 거주민들의 마음을 이렇게 잠시 그들을 찾아온 낯선 이들과 묶어 준 그 끈은, 그리 쉽게 끊어지지 않았다. 그 백인 처녀와 〈모히칸〉의 젊은 전사에 관한 전설이 긴 밤과 고된 행군의 무료함을 달래는 일도, 그들의 젊고 용감한 자들을 복수의 열망으로 들끓게 하는 일도 그칠 때까지는 여러 해가 흘렀다. 이 중대한 사건들의 부차적인 행위자들도 잊히지 않았다. 그 뒤로 여러 해 동안 그들과 문명 생활 사이의 고리 노릇을 한 척후를 통해, 그들은 자신들의 물음에 대한 답으로, \"회색 머리\"가 — 그릇되이 믿어졌듯 군사적 불운에 짓눌려 — 이내 조상들에게로 거두어졌다는 것과, \"열린 손\"이 살아남은 그의 딸을 \"창백한 얼굴들\"의 정착지 깊은 데로 데려갔으며, 그곳에서 그녀의 눈물이 마침내 그치고, 그 즐거운 천성에 한결 어울리는 환한 미소가 그 자리를 대신했다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n그러나 이는 우리 이야기와 관련된 때보다 후일의 일이다. 제 살빛의 모든 이에게 버려진 〈호크아이〉는, 어떤 관념적인 결연의 끈도 줄 수 없는 힘으로 제 마음이 이끄는 그 자리로 돌아갔다. 그는 마침 〈언카스〉의 얼굴을 마지막으로 한 번 보기에 알맞은 때에 닿았으니, 〈델라웨어〉인들이 이미 그를 가죽으로 된 마지막 수의로 감싸고 있던 참이었다. 그들은 이 굳센 숲 사람의 갈망에 찬 마지막 응시를 허락하려 멈추었고, 그것이 끝나자, 시신은 다시는 열리지 않도록 감싸였다. 그런 다음 앞서와 같은 행렬이 이어졌고, 부족 전체가 그 추장의 임시 무덤 둘레로 모였으니 — 임시라 함은, 훗날 어느 날 그의 뼈가 제 백성의 뼈 사이에 누이는 것이 마땅했기 때문이다.\n\n그 움직임은, 그 감정과 마찬가지로, 일제히 모두에게서 비롯되었다. 이미 그려진 것과 똑같은 엄숙한 슬픔의 표정, 똑같은 굳은 침묵, 그리고 으뜸가는 애도자에 대한 똑같은 예우가, 매장의 자리 둘레에서 지켜졌다. 시신은 떠오르는 해를 향하여 안식의 자세로 안치되었고, 마지막 여정에 대비하여 전쟁과 사냥의 도구들이 곁에 놓였다. 함에는 트인 자리를 남겨, 흙으로부터 보호받으면서도, 영혼이 필요할 때 제 이승의 처소와 소통할 수 있게 했다. 그리고 그 모든 것은 원주민 특유의 솜씨로, 맹수의 본능으로부터 감추어지고 그 약탈로부터 지켜졌다. 그러자 손으로 행하는 의식이 그쳤고, 그 자리에 있던 모두가 의식의 한결 영적인 부분으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n〈칭가치국〉이 다시 한번 모두의 주목의 대상이 되었다. 그는 아직 입을 열지 않았으니, 그토록 이름난 추장에게서, 그토록 절실한 자리에서, 무언가 위로가 되고 가르침이 될 말이 기대되었다. 백성의 바람을 알아챈 이 준엄하고 자제하는 전사는, 근래 제 가운에 파묻고 있던 얼굴을 들어, 한결같은 눈으로 사방을 둘러보았다. 그러자 굳게 다물려 있던 그 뜻 깊은 입술이 갈라졌고, 그 긴 의식 동안 처음으로 그의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다.\n\n\"어찌하여 나의 형제들은 애도하는가!\" 그는 자신을 에워싼 낙담한 전사들의 검은 무리를 바라보며 말했다. \"어찌하여 나의 딸들은 우는가! 한 젊은이가 행복한 사냥터로 갔고, 한 추장이 명예로이 제 세월을 채웠음을! 그는 선했고, 의로웠으며, 용감했다. 누가 그것을 부인하랴? 〈마니토〉께서 그러한 전사를 필요로 하시어, 그를 불러 가셨다. 나로 말하면, 〈언카스〉의 아들이자 아비인 나는, 창백한 얼굴들의 개간지에 선 껍질 벗긴 소나무다. 나의 종족은 소금 호숫가에서, 〈델라웨어〉의 언덕에서 사라져 갔다. 그러나 제 부족의 〈뱀〉이 그 지혜를 잊었다고 누가 말하랴? 나는 혼자다 —\"\n\n\"아니, 아니오.\" 제 벗의 굳은 얼굴을, 자신도 그만한 자제의 기색으로 갈망하듯 응시하고 있던, 그러나 그 철학이 더는 견딜 수 없게 된 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"아니오, 〈세이거모어〉, 혼자가 아니오. 우리 살빛의 천품은 다를지언정, 하느님께서는 우리를 같은 길을 가도록 두셨소. 나는 일가붙이도 없고, 그대처럼, 백성도 없다 할 수 있소. 그는 그대의 아들이었고, 천성으로 붉은 피부의 사람이었소. 어쩌면 그대의 핏줄이 더 가까웠을지 모르나 — 허나 전쟁에서 그토록 자주 내 곁에서 싸웠고 평화 속에서 내 곁에서 잠들었던 그 아이를 내가 언젠가 잊는다면, 우리 살빛이나 천품이 어떻든 우리 모두를 지으신 그분께서 나를 잊으시기를! 그 아이는 한동안 우리를 떠났소. 허나, 〈세이거모어〉, 그대는 혼자가 아니오.\"\n\n〈칭가치국〉은, 감정이 북받친 척후가 갓 파낸 흙 위로 뻗은 그 손을 움켜쥐었고, 그 우정의 자세로, 이 굳세고 두려움 없는 두 숲 사람은 함께 머리를 숙였으니, 뜨거운 눈물이 그들의 발치로 떨어져, 떨어지는 빗방울처럼 〈언카스〉의 무덤을 적셨다.\n\n그 고장의 가장 이름난 두 전사에게서, 그렇게 터져 나온 이 감정의 폭발이 받아들여진 그 엄숙한 정적 한가운데서, 〈타메넌드〉가 무리를 흩으려 목소리를 높였다.\n\n\"되었다.\" 그가 말했다. \"가라, 〈레나페〉의 아이들이여, 〈마니토〉의 분노는 아직 다하지 않았다. 어찌하여 〈타메넌드〉가 머물러야 하랴? 창백한 얼굴들이 이 땅의 주인이니, 붉은 사람들의 때는 아직 다시 오지 않았다. 나의 날은 너무도 길었다. 아침에 나는 〈우나미스〉의 아들들이 행복하고 강성한 것을 보았다. 그런데도, 밤이 오기도 전에, 나는 지혜로운 종족 〈모히칸〉의 마지막 전사를 보기까지 살아 있구나.\"\n각주\n\n[1] 인디언의 각 부족은 저마다 언어 또는 방언을 지녔으므로, 그들은 같은 장소에 흔히 서로 다른 이름을 붙였는데, 그 명칭들은 거의 모두 그 대상을 묘사하는 것이었다. 그리하여, 그 기슭에 살던 부족이 쓰던, 이 아름다운 호수의 이름을 그대로 옮기면 \"호수의 꼬리\"가 된다. 흔히, 그리고 이제는 실로 법적으로도 그렇게 불리는 〈조지 호수〉는, 지도에서 보면 〈섐플레인 호수〉에 일종의 꼬리를 이룬다. 그 이름은 여기서 비롯되었다.\n\n[2] 워싱턴. 그는 유럽인 장군에게 그가 무모하게 빠져들고 있던 위험을 헛되이 일깨운 끝에, 이 일에서 제 결단과 용기로 영국군의 잔병을 구해 냈다. 워싱턴이 이 전투에서 얻은 명성은, 훗날 그가 미국군의 지휘관으로 선발된 주된 이유가 되었다. 주목할 만한 사정은, 온 아메리카가 그의 마땅한 명성으로 떠들썩했던 반면, 그의 이름이 이 전투에 관한 어떤 유럽 측 기록에도 나오지 않는다는 점이다. 적어도 필자는 그것을 찾으려 했으나 성공하지 못했다. 그러한 통치 체제 아래서는, 본국이 명성마저 이렇게 흡수해 버린다.\n\n[3] 〈뉴욕〉 식민지의 북서부를 차지한 인디언 부족들 사이에는 오랫동안 한 연합이 존재했으니, 처음에는 \"다섯 부족\"으로 알려졌다. 후일 또 한 부족이 받아들여지면서, 그 명칭은 \"여섯 부족\"으로 바뀌었다. 본래의 연합은 〈모호크〉, 〈오나이다〉, 〈세니카〉, 〈카유가〉, 〈오논다가〉로 이루어졌다. 여섯째 부족은 〈터스커로라〉였다. 이 모든 종족의 잔류민이 아직도 주(州)가 그들에게 보장해 준 땅에서 살고 있으나, 그들은 죽음으로든, 제 습성에 더 맞는 곳으로의 이주로든, 나날이 사라져 가고 있다. 머지않아, 그들이 수백 년 살아온 그 고장에는, 그들의 이름 말고는 이 비범한 종족의 자취가 남지 않을 것이다. 〈뉴욕〉주에는 〈모호크〉와 〈터스커로라〉를 제외한 그들 모두의 이름을 딴 카운티가 있다. 그 주의 둘째 강은 〈모호크〉강이라 불린다.\n\n[4] 〈로드아일랜드〉주에는 〈내러갠셋〉이라 불리는 만이 있으니, 일찍이 그 기슭에 살던 강성한 인디언 부족의 이름을 딴 것이다. 우연이었는지, 자연이 동물계에서 이따금 부리는 그 설명할 수 없는 변덕 중 하나였는지, 한때 〈내러갠셋〉이라는 이름으로 아메리카에 널리 알려졌던 한 말의 품종이 생겨났다. 그것들은 몸집이 작고, 흔히 아메리카에서 적갈색이라 부르는 빛깔이었으며, 측대보로 걷는 습성으로 두드러졌다. 이 품종의 말은, 그 강건함과 움직임의 편안함 때문에, 안장말로 크게 요구되었고 지금도 그러하다. 또한 발걸음이 든든하였으므로, \"새 고장\"의 나무뿌리와 구덩이 위를 다녀야 했던 여인들이 〈내러갠셋〉종을 크게 찾았다.\n\n[5] 북아메리카 전사는 온몸의 털을 뽑게 했다. 다만 정수리에 작은 한 줌만 남겨 두었으니, 이는 자신이 쓰러질 경우 적이 머리 가죽을 비틀어 벗기는 데 쓰게 하기 위함이었다. 머리 가죽은 승리의 유일하게 인정되는 전리품이었다. 그리하여 사람을 죽이는 것보다 그 머리 가죽을 얻는 것이 더 중하게 여겨졌다. 어떤 부족들은 주검을 치는 명예를 크게 강조한다. 이러한 관습은 대서양 연안 주들의 인디언들 사이에서는 거의 사라졌다.\n\n[6] 사냥 셔츠는 그림처럼 멋진 작업용 겉옷으로, 길이가 더 짧고, 술과 장식 매듭으로 꾸며져 있다. 그 빛깔은 몸을 숨길 목적으로 숲의 색조를 흉내 내려는 것이다. 많은 미국인 소총수 부대가 이렇게 차려입었으며, 그 복장은 근래의 것 가운데 가장 눈길을 끄는 것 중 하나다. 사냥 셔츠는 흔히 흰색이다.\n\n[7] 군대의 소총은 짧고, 사냥꾼의 소총은 늘 길다.\n\n[8] 〈미시시피〉강. 척후는 대서양 연안 주들의 부족들 사이에서 매우 널리 퍼진 한 전설을 가리키고 있다. 그들의 아시아 기원의 증거가 그 정황에서 추론되나, 인디언의 역사 전체에는 큰 불확실성이 감돈다.\n\n[9] 이 이야기의 무대는 위도 42도로, 그곳에서는 황혼이 결코 오래 이어지지 않는다.\n\n[10] 독자는 〈뉴욕〉이 본래 네덜란드의 식민지였음을 기억할 것이다.\n\n[11] 인디언의 주요 마을들은 〈뉴욕〉의 백인들에게 아직도 \"성채\"라 불린다. \"〈오나이다〉 성채\"는 흩어진 작은 마을에 지나지 않으나, 그 이름이 일반적으로 쓰인다.\n\n[12] 흔한 말로, 미국인은 한 끼의 양념을 \"렐리시\"라 부르니, 사물 대신 그 효과를 가져다 쓴 것이다. 이러한 지방어들은 흔히, 화자들의 저마다 다른 처지에 따라 그들의 입에 놓인다. 그 대부분은 지역적으로 쓰이며, 더러는 그 인물이 속한 특정 부류의 사람들에게 아주 독특한 것이다. 여기서는 척후가, 제 일행이 다행히도 갖추고 있던 그 소금을 직접 가리키며 이 말을 쓴 것이다.\n\n[13] 〈글렌스 폭포〉는 〈허드슨〉강에 있으니, 조수가 닿는 데, 곧 그 강이 외돛배가 다닐 수 있게 되는 곳에서 사오십 마일 위쪽이다. 척후가 들려준 이 그림 같고 두드러진 작은 폭포의 묘사는 충분히 정확하나, 그 물을 문명 생활의 용도로 끌어 쓰면서 그 아름다움이 크게 훼손되었다. 그 바위섬과 두 동굴은 모든 여행자에게 잘 알려져 있으니, 그 섬이 이제 폭포 바로 위로 강을 가로질러 놓인 다리의 교각을 떠받치고 있기 때문이다. 〈호크아이〉의 취향을 설명하자면, 사람은 늘 가장 덜 누리는 것을 가장 귀히 여긴다는 점을 기억해야 한다. 그리하여, 새 고장에서는, 오래된 나라라면 큰 비용을 들여서라도 보존했을 숲과 그 밖의 것들이, 흔히 말하는 \"개량\"을 위한다는 까닭만으로 없어져 버린다.\n\n[14] 인디언 말의 뜻은 그 강세와 음조에 크게 좌우된다.\n\n[15] 〈밍고〉는 다섯 부족을 가리키는 〈델라웨어〉 말이었다. 〈머콰스〉는 네덜란드인이 그들에게 붙인 이름이었다. 프랑스인은 그들과 처음 교류할 때부터 그들을 〈이로쿼이〉라 불렀다.\n\n[16] 백인들은 오랫동안 인디언의 유력자들을 회유하기 위해 메달을 주는 관습이 있었으니, 그것은 그들 자신의 투박한 장식 대신 몸에 걸쳤다. 영국인이 준 것은 대개 통치 중인 왕의 인장을, 미국인이 준 것은 대통령의 인장을 새겼다.\n\n[17] 아메리카 숲의 많은 동물이 소금 샘이 있는 곳으로 찾아든다. 이런 곳은 그 고장 말로 \"릭\" 또는 \"솔트 릭\"이라 불리니, 네발짐승이 소금기 어린 알갱이를 얻으려 흔히 땅을 핥아야 하는 데서 비롯된 이름이다. 이 릭은 사냥꾼들이 즐겨 찾는 곳으로, 그들은 그리로 통하는 길목 근처에서 사냥감을 노린다.\n\n[18] 앞서 일어난 사건들의 무대는 지금 〈볼스턴〉 마을이 서 있는 자리이니, 아메리카의 두 주요 온천지 중 하나다.\n\n[19] 몇 해 전, 필자는 〈온타리오 호수〉 기슭에 서 있는 〈오스위고 요새〉의 폐허 부근에서 사냥을 하고 있었다. 그가 노린 사냥감은 사슴이었고, 그 사냥터는 거의 끊김 없이 오십 마일이나 내륙으로 뻗은 숲이었다. 뜻밖에도 그는 서로 가까운 거리에 숲속에 놓인 여섯 내지 여덟 개의 사다리를 발견했다. 그것들은 투박하게 만들어졌고, 많이 썩어 있었다. 어찌하여 이런 곳에 이토록 많은 도구가 모이게 되었는지 의아하여, 그는 가까이 사는 한 노인을 찾아 그 까닭을 물었다.\n\n1776년의 전쟁 동안 〈오스위고 요새〉는 영국군이 지키고 있었다. 그 요새를 기습하기 위해 황야를 가로질러 이백 마일의 원정대가 보내졌다. 보아하니 미국군은, 앞서 말한 그 자리, 곧 요새에서 일이 마일 안쪽에 이르러서야 처음으로, 자신들이 발각되어 끊겨 나갈 큰 위험에 처했음을 알았다. 그들은 공성용 사다리를 버리고 황급히 후퇴했다. 이 사다리들은, 그렇게 내던져진 그 자리에서 삼십 년 동안 손대지 않은 채 놓여 있었다.\n\n[20] 프랑스에 복무한 독일인 〈디스코〉 남작. 이 이야기의 시점보다 몇 해 앞서, 이 장교는 〈뉴욕〉주 〈존스타운〉의 윌리엄 존슨 경에게 〈조지 호수〉 기슭에서 패했다. 부록의 주(註) H를 보라.\n\n[21] 분명, 1828년에 〈뉴욕〉 주지사로 사망한 고(故) 디윗 클린턴이다.\n\n[22] 아메리카 흉내지빠귀의 능력은 널리 알려져 있다. 그러나 진짜 흉내지빠귀는 〈뉴욕〉주만큼 북쪽에서는 발견되지 않으니, 다만 그곳에는 그보다 못한 두 대용새가 있다. 척후가 그토록 자주 일컫는 고양이새와, 흔히 땅타작새라 불리는 새다. 이 둘 중 어느 것이든 나이팅게일이나 종다리보다 낫다. 다만 대체로 아메리카의 새들은 유럽의 새들보다 덜 곱게 운다.\n\n[23] 〈조지 호수〉의 아름다움은 모든 미국인 여행자에게 잘 알려져 있다. 그것을 에워싼 산들의 높이와 인공적인 부속물에서는 스위스와 이탈리아의 가장 빼어난 호수들에 못 미치나, 윤곽과 물의 맑음에서는 그것들과 충분히 대등하고, 그 섬과 작은 섬들의 수와 배치에서는 그것들을 다 합친 것보다 훨씬 낫다. 길이가 삼십 마일도 채 안 되는 수면에 수백 개의 섬이 있다고들 한다. 실로 두 개의 호수라 할 만한 것을 잇는 좁은 물목은 섬들로 빼곡하여, 그 사이의 통로가 흔히 폭이 몇 피트밖에 안 된다. 호수 자체의 너비는 일 마일에서 삼 마일까지 다양하다.\n\n〈뉴욕〉주는 그 호수의 수와 아름다움으로 두드러진다. 그 변경 하나는 광대한 〈온타리오〉의 수면에 닿아 있고, 〈섐플레인〉은 또 다른 변경을 따라 거의 백 마일이나 뻗어 있다. 〈오나이다〉, 〈카유가〉, 〈커낸다이과〉, 〈세니카〉, 〈조지〉는 모두 길이가 삼십 마일에 이르는 호수이며, 그보다 작은 것들은 헤아릴 수 없이 많다. 이 호수들 대부분에는 이제 아름다운 마을들이 들어섰고, 그 많은 호수에 증기선이 다닌다.\n\n[24] 짐승들에게 건네는 이러한 일장 연설은 인디언들 사이에서 흔하다. 그들은 흔히 제 사냥감에게 이런 식으로 말을 건네는데, 그것이 고통 속에서 의연함을 보이느냐 그 반대냐에 따라, 비겁함을 꾸짖거나 그 결의를 칭찬한다.\n\n[25] 빻은 옥수수와 콩으로 만든 음식. 백인들도 즐겨 쓴다. 옥수수란 메이즈를 이른다.\n\n[26] 윌리엄 펜은 〈델라웨어〉인들에게 〈민쿠온〉이라 불렸으니, 그는 그들과의 거래에서 결코 폭력이나 불의를 쓰지 않았으므로, 그 정직함에 대한 평판이 속담처럼 굳어졌다. 미국인은 어쩌면 세계 역사에서 유례없을 제 나라의 기원을 마땅히 자랑스러워하나, 펜실베이니아인과 저지인은 어느 주의 토박이보다 제 조상을 더 떳떳이 여길 까닭이 있으니, 땅의 본래 주인들에게 아무런 부당함도 행해지지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n[27] 거북.\n\n[28] 껍질이 일부 혹은 전부 벗겨진 나무를, 그 고장 말로 \"블레이즈드\"라 한다. 이 말은 엄밀히 영어다. 말(馬)이 흰 무늬가 있을 때도 \"블레이즈드\"라 하기 때문이다.\n\n[29] 아메리카의 숲은 말이 지날 수 있으니, 덤불이 적고 뒤엉킨 수풀이 드물기 때문이다. 〈호크아이〉의 방책은 백인과 인디언 사이의 싸움에서 늘 가장 효과적임이 입증된 것이다. 웨인은 〈마이애미〉에서의 그 유명한 원정에서, 횡대로 적의 사격을 받은 다음, 기병을 측면으로 돌아 들게 하여, 인디언들이 미처 장전할 틈도 없이 그들의 엄폐물에서 몰아냈다. 〈마이애미〉 전투에서 싸운 가장 이름난 추장 중 하나는 필자에게, 붉은 사람들은 \"긴 칼과 가죽 각반\"을 한 전사들과는 싸울 수 없다고 단언했으니, 곧 군도와 군화를 갖춘 기병을 두고 한 말이었다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-27681"]=[[["# THE FLIGHT ACROSS THE LAKE 214", "# THE FLIGHT ACROSS THE LAKE 214"]], [["As soon as this slight salutation had passed, Montcalm moved towards them with a quick but graceful step, baring his head to the veteran, and dropping his spotless plume nearly to the earth in courtesy\nThe scout having ascertained that the Mohicans were sufficient of themselves to maintain the requisite distance, deliberately laid aside his paddle, and raised the fatal rifle", "적대하는 양쪽 군대가 서로 맞붙기까지 황야의 노고와 위험을 먼저 견뎌야 한다는 것은 북아메리카 식민지 전쟁만의 독특한 특징이었다. 적대하는 프랑스령과 영국령의 영토는 넓고도 도무지 뚫을 수 없을 듯한 삼림의 경계로 갈라져 있었다. 강인한 개척민과 그 곁에서 싸우는 훈련된 유럽인은, 보다 무인다운 충돌에서 용기를 떨칠 기회를 찾고자, 급류와 싸우거나 험준한 산길을 넘느라 흔히 몇 달을 허비하곤 했다. 그러나 숙련된 원주민 전사들의 인내와 극기를 본받아, 그들은 온갖 난관을 이겨 내는 법을 익혔다. 그리하여 마침내, 복수의 갈증을 채우려 피로써 맹세한 자들이나, 멀리 유럽의 군주들이 펴는 냉혹하고 이기적인 정책을 떠받치려는 자들의 침입을 면할 만큼, 그토록 어두운 숲의 후미진 곳도, 그토록 사랑스러운 은밀한 장소도 없었던 듯하다."]], [["# THE TERMAGANT 250", "# THE TERMAGANT 250"]], [["Throwing back her light vestment, she stretched forth her long skinny arm, in derision\nThe grim head fell on one side, and in its place appeared the honest, sturdy countenance of the scout", "저 시대 미개한 전쟁의 잔혹함과 흉포함을 〈허드슨〉강의 상류와 인근 호수들 사이에 놓인 고장만큼 생생하게 그려 낼 수 있는 지역은, 광막하게 펼쳐진 중간 변경지대 어디에도 아마 없을 것이다."]], [["Heyward and Alice took their way together towards the distant village of the Delawares\nCora had cast herself to her knees; and, with hands clenched in each other and pressed upon her bosom, she remained like a beauteous and breathing model of her sex", "그곳에서 자연이 교전자들의 행군에 제공한 편의는 너무도 분명하여 그냥 지나칠 수 없는 것이었다. 길게 뻗은 〈섐플레인〉 호수는 〈캐나다〉의 변경에서부터 이웃한 〈뉴욕〉 영토 깊숙한 곳까지 이어져, 프랑스군이 적을 치기 위해 정복해야 했던 거리의 절반을 가로지르는 천연의 통로를 이루었다. 그 남단 가까이에서, 이 호수는 또 다른 호수의 물을 받아들였는데, 그 물이 어찌나 맑았던지 예수회 선교사들이 세례의 상징적 정화 의식을 행할 곳으로 따로 골라, 그곳에 〈성체호(湖)〉라는 이름을 붙였을 정도였다. 그보다 덜 열성적이던 영국인들은, 통치 중인 자국 군주, 곧 〈하노버〉 왕가의 둘째 왕의 이름을 그 티 없는 샘에 부여하는 것으로 충분한 영예를 주었다고 여겼다. 이 두 무리는 한데 어우러져, 그 숲의 풍경을 지녔던 무지한 소유자들에게서 본래의 명칭 〈호리컨〉을 영원히 이어 갈 토착의 권리를 빼앗았다."]], [["\"Mine ear is open, and my heart prepared: The worst is worldly loss thou canst unfold: Say, is my kingdom lost?\" It was a feature peculiar to the colonial wars of North America, that the toils and dangers of the wilderness were to be encountered before the adverse hosts could meet.", "이 \"거룩한 호수\"는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 섬들 사이를 굽이쳐, 산들 속에 안긴 채, 남쪽으로 다시 십여 리그를 더 뻗어 나갔다."], ["A wide and apparently an impervious boundary of forests severed the possessions of the hostile provinces of France and England. The hardy colonist, and the trained European who fought at his side, frequently expended months in struggling against the rapids of the streams, or in effecting the rugged passes of the mountains, in quest of an opportunity to exhibit their courage in a more martial conflict.", "그곳에서 물이 더 나아가는 것을 가로막은 높은 평원과 함께, 그만큼의 마일에 걸친 육로 운반로가 시작되어, 모험가를 〈허드슨〉강 기슭으로 인도했다."], ["But, emulating the patience and self-denial of the practised native warriors, they learned to overcome every difficulty; and it would seem that, in time, there was no recess of the woods so dark, nor any secret place so lovely, that it might claim exemption from the inroads of those who had pledged their blood to satiate their vengeance, or to uphold the cold and selfish policy of the distant monarchs of Europe.", "바로 그 지점에서, 당시 그 고장 말로 급류 혹은 여울이라 불리던 흔한 장애물을 지나면, 강은 조수가 닿는 데까지 배가 다닐 수 있게 되었다."]], [["Perhaps no district throughout the wide extent of the intermediate frontiers can furnish a livelier picture of the cruelty and fierceness of the savage warfare of those periods than the country which lies between the head waters of the Hudson and the adjacent lakes.", "프랑스군의 쉴 줄 모르는 진취성이 적을 괴롭히려는 대담한 계획을 좇아 멀고도 험난한 〈앨러게니〉의 협곡까지 넘보았던 만큼, 그들의 정평 난 예리함이 우리가 방금 묘사한 이 지역의 천연의 이점을 놓쳤을 리 없다는 것은 쉽게 짐작할 수 있다."], ["The facilities which nature had there offered to the march of the combatants were too obvious to be neglected.", "이곳은 단연코, 식민지의 지배권을 두고 벌어진 대부분의 전투가 다투어진 피의 무대가 되었다."], ["The lengthened sheet of the Champlain stretched from the frontiers of Canada, deep within the borders of the neighboring province of New York, forming a natural passage across half the distance that the French were compelled to master in order to strike their enemies.", "길의 요충을 제압하는 여러 지점마다 요새가 세워졌고, 승리가 어느 쪽 깃발에 내려앉느냐에 따라 빼앗기고 되찾기를, 허물어지고 다시 쌓이기를 거듭했다."], ["Near its southern termination, it received the contributions of another lake, whose waters were so limpid as to have been exclusively selected by the Jesuit missionaries to perform the typical purification of baptism, and to obtain for it the title of lake \"du Saint Sacrement.\"", "농부가 위험한 길목에서 물러나 더 오래된 정착지의 안전한 경계 안으로 움츠러드는 동안, 흔히 본국의 왕권을 좌우하곤 했던 군대보다 더 큰 군대가 이 숲속에 파묻히는 광경이 보였으니, 그곳에서 그들은 좀처럼 돌아오지 못했고, 돌아온대야 근심으로 초췌해지거나 패배에 낙담한 해골 같은 무리뿐이었다."], ["The less zealous English thought they conferred a sufficient honor on its unsullied fountains, when they bestowed the name of their reigning prince, the second of the house of Hanover. The two united to rob the untutored possessors of its wooded scenery of their native right to perpetuate its original appellation of \"Horican.\"[1]", "비록 이 숙명의 땅에 평화의 기예는 알려지지 않았으나, 그 숲은 사람들로 들끓었고, 그 그늘과 골짜기는 군악 소리로 울렸으며, 산의 메아리는 혈기 왕성한 무수한 젊은이가 의기 충천한 한낮에 그곳을 서둘러 지나며 터뜨린 웃음을 되돌려 보내거나 거리낌 없는 외침을 되풀이했으니, 그들은 곧 망각의 기나긴 밤 속에 잠들고자 가는 길이었다."]], [["Winding its way among countless islands, and imbedded in mountains, the \"holy lake\" extended a dozen leagues still farther to the south. With the high plain that there interposed itself to the further passage of the water, commenced a portage of as many miles, which conducted the adventurer to the banks of the Hudson, at a point where, with the usual obstructions of the rapids, or rifts, as they were then termed in the language of the country, the river became navigable to the tide. While, in the pursuit of their daring plans of annoyance, the restless enterprise of the French even attempted the distant and difficult gorges of the Alleghany, it may easily be imagined that their proverbial acuteness would not overlook the natural advantages of the district we have just described. It became, emphatically, the bloody arena, in which most of the battles for the mastery of the colonies were contested. Forts were erected at the different points that commanded the facilities of the route, and were taken and retaken, razed and rebuilt, as victory alighted on the hostile banners. While the husbandman shrank back from the dangerous passes, within the safer boundaries of the more ancient settlements, armies larger than those that had often disposed of the sceptres of the mother countries, were seen to bury themselves in these forests, whence they rarely returned but in skeleton bands, that were haggard with care, or dejected by defeat. Though the arts of peace were unknown to this fatal region, its forests were alive with men; its shades and glens rang with the sounds of martial music, and the echoes of its mountains threw back the laugh, or repeated the wanton cry, of many a gallant and reckless youth, as he hurried by them, in the noontide of his spirits, to slumber in a long night of forgetfulness.", "영국과 프랑스가 어느 쪽도 끝내 지킬 운명이 아니었던 한 땅의 소유권을 두고 마지막으로 벌인 전쟁, 그 셋째 해에, 우리가 이제 이야기하려는 사건들이 일어난 곳은 바로 이 다툼과 유혈의 무대였다."]], [["It was in this scene of strife and bloodshed that the incidents we shall attempt to relate occurred, during the third year of the war which England and France last waged for the possession of a country that neither was destined to retain.", "해외에 나간 군 지휘관들의 무능함과 본국 회의에서의 치명적인 활력 부족이, 한때 옛 전사들과 정치가들의 재능과 진취성으로 〈영국〉이 올라섰던 자랑스러운 높이에서 그 위상을 끌어내렸다."], ["The imbecility of her military leaders abroad, and the fatal want of energy in her councils at home, had lowered the character of Great Britain from the proud elevation on which it had been placed, by the talents and enterprise of her former warriors and statesmen.", "더는 적들에게 두려움을 주지 못하게 되자, 그 신하들은 자존감의 확신을 빠르게 잃어 가고 있었다."], ["No longer dreaded by her enemies, her servants were fast losing the confidence of self-respect. In this mortifying abasement, the colonists, though innocent of her imbecility, and too humble to be the agents of her blunders, were but the natural participators.", "이 굴욕적인 추락 속에서, 식민지 사람들은 본국의 무능에 죄가 없고 그 실책의 앞잡이가 되기에는 너무 미천했음에도, 그저 자연히 그 처지를 함께 나누는 자들일 뿐이었다."]], [["They had recently seen a chosen army from that country, which, reverencing as a mother, they had blindly believed invincible--an army led by a chief who had been selected from a crowd of trained warriors, for his rare military endowments, disgracefully routed by a handful of French and Indians, and only saved from annihilation by the coolness and spirit of a Virginian boy, whose riper fame has since diffused itself, with the steady influence of moral truth, to the uttermost confines of Christendom.[2] A wide frontier had been laid naked by this unexpected disaster, and more substantial evils were preceded by a thousand fanciful and imaginary dangers.", "그들은 얼마 전, 어머니로 우러르며 무적이라 맹목적으로 믿어 온 그 나라에서 보낸 정예 군대가, 곧 훈련된 전사들 가운데서 그 보기 드문 군사적 자질로 선발된 한 지휘관이 이끌던 군대가, 한 줌의 프랑스군과 인디언에게 치욕스럽게 궤멸당하고, 어느 〈버지니아〉 청년의 침착함과 기개 덕에 가까스로 전멸을 면하는 것을 목격했다. 그 청년의 무르익은 명성은 그 후 도덕적 진리의 꾸준한 영향력을 띠고 기독교 세계의 가장 먼 변경에까지 퍼져 나갔다."], ["The alarmed colonists believed that the yells of the savages mingled with every fitful gust of wind that issued from the interminable forests of the west.", "이 뜻밖의 재앙으로 광대한 변경이 벌거벗겨졌고, 더 실질적인 재난에 앞서 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 공상적이고 허황한 위험들이 먼저 닥쳤다."], ["The terrific character of their merciless enemies increased immeasurably the natural horrors of warfare.", "겁에 질린 식민지 사람들은, 서쪽의 끝없는 숲에서 불어오는 변덕스러운 바람 한 줄기마다 미개인들의 비명이 섞여 있다고 믿었다."], ["Numberless recent massacres were still vivid in their recollections; nor was there any ear in the provinces so deaf as not to have drunk in with avidity the narrative of some fearful tale of midnight murder, in which the natives of the forests were the principal and barbarous actors.", "무자비한 적들의 끔찍한 성격은 전쟁이 본래 지닌 공포를 헤아릴 수 없이 키웠다. 최근에 벌어진 수많은 학살이 아직도 그들의 기억 속에 생생했고, 숲의 원주민들이 주된 야만의 배역을 맡은 어느 한밤중 살인의 무시무시한 이야기를, 게걸스레 빨아들이지 않을 만큼 무딘 귀는 이 영토 안에 하나도 없었다."], ["As the credulous and excited traveller related the hazardous chances of the wilderness, the blood of the timid curdled with terror, and mothers cast anxious glances even at those children which slumbered within the security of the largest towns.", "잘 속고 흥분한 여행자가 황야의 위험천만한 일들을 늘어놓을 때면, 겁 많은 자들의 피가 공포로 얼어붙었고, 어머니들은 가장 큰 도회의 안전 속에 잠든 아이들에게조차 불안한 눈길을 던졌다."], ["In short, the magnifying influence of fear began to set at naught the calculations of reason, and to render those who should have remembered their manhood, the slaves of the basest of passions.", "요컨대, 공포가 부풀려 놓는 영향력이 이성의 계산을 무로 돌리기 시작했고, 사내다움을 기억해야 마땅할 자들을 가장 비천한 격정의 노예로 만들었다."], ["Even the most confident and the stoutest hearts began to think the issue of the contest was becoming doubtful; and that abject class was hourly increasing in numbers, who thought they foresaw all the possessions of the English crown in America subdued by their Christian foes, or laid waste by the inroads of their relentless allies.", "가장 자신만만하고 가장 굳센 마음들조차 이 싸움의 결말이 의심스러워지고 있다고 생각하기 시작했고, 〈영국〉 왕실이 아메리카에 가진 모든 영토가 기독교도인 적들에게 정복되거나 무자비한 동맹군의 침략에 황폐해지는 것을 미리 내다본다고 여기는 비굴한 무리가 시시각각 그 수를 늘려 갔다."]], [["When, therefore, intelligence was received at the fort, which covered the southern termination of the portage between the Hudson and the lakes, that Montcalm had been seen moving up the Champlain, with an army \"numerous as the leaves on the trees,\"", "그러므로, 〈몽칼름〉이 \"나무의 잎사귀처럼 수많은\" 군대를 이끌고 〈섐플레인〉을 거슬러 올라가는 것이 목격되었다는 소식이, 〈허드슨〉강과 호수들 사이 육로 운반로의 남단을 지키던 요새에 전해졌을 때, 그 진위는, 자신의 일격이 닿을 거리에 적이 들어왔음을 알게 된 전사가 느껴야 할 준엄한 기쁨보다는, 오히려 공포에서 비롯된 비겁한 망설임 속에 받아들여졌다."], ["its truth was admitted with more of the craven reluctance of fear than with the stern joy that a warrior should feel, in finding an enemy within reach of his blow.", "이 소식은 한여름 어느 날 해 질 무렵, 한 인디언 전령이 가져왔는데, 그는 또한 \"거룩한 호수\""], ["The news had been brought, towards the decline of a day in midsummer, by an Indian runner, who also bore an urgent request from Munro, the commander of a work on the shore of the \"holy lake,\" for a speedy and powerful reinforcement.", "기슭에 자리한 진지의 지휘관 〈먼로〉가 보낸, 신속하고 강력한 증원을 바라는 긴박한 요청도 지니고 있었다."], ["It has already been mentioned that the distance between these two posts was less than five leagues.", "이 두 진지 사이의 거리가 다섯 리그가 채 못 된다는 것은 이미 언급한 바 있다."], ["The rude path, which originally formed their line of communication, had been widened for the passage of wagons; so that the distance which had been travelled by the son of the forest in two hours, might easily be effected by a detachment of troops, with their necessary baggage, between the rising and setting of a summer sun.", "본래 그 둘을 잇는 통로를 이루던 거친 오솔길은 마차가 지날 수 있도록 넓혀져 있었으니, 숲의 아들이 두 시간 만에 주파한 거리는, 필요한 짐을 거느린 한 부대 병력이라도 여름 해가 뜨고 지는 사이에 어렵잖게 갈 수 있었다."], ["The loyal servants of the British crown had given to one of these forest fastnesses the name of William Henry, and to the other that of Fort Edward; calling each after a favorite prince of the reigning family.", "〈영국〉 왕실의 충직한 신하들은 이 숲의 요새 가운데 하나에 〈윌리엄 헨리〉라는 이름을, 다른 하나에 〈에드워드〉 요새라는 이름을 붙여, 저마다 통치 가문이 아끼는 왕자의 이름을 땄다."], ["The veteran Scotchman just named held the first, with a regiment of regulars and a few provincials; a force really by far too small to make head against the formidable power that Montcalm was leading to the foot of his earthen mounds.", "방금 말한 노련한 스코틀랜드인은 첫째 요새를 정규군 한 연대와 약간의 식민지 민병으로 지키고 있었으니, 〈몽칼름〉이 그의 흙벽 발치로 이끌고 오는 가공할 병력에 맞서기에는 사실 턱없이 적은 군세였다."], ["At the latter, however, lay General Webb, who commanded the armies of the king in the northern provinces, with a body of more than five thousand men.", "그러나 후자에는, 북부 영토의 왕의 군대를 지휘하는 〈웨브〉 장군이 오천이 넘는 병력과 함께 머물고 있었다."], ["By uniting the several detachments of his command, this officer might have arrayed nearly double that number of combatants against the enterprising Frenchman, who had ventured so far from his reinforcements, with an army but little superior in numbers.", "휘하의 여러 분견대를 한데 합쳤다면, 이 장교는 거의 그 갑절에 달하는 전투원을 거느리고, 증원 부대에서 그토록 멀리, 병력에서 거의 우위에 있지도 못한 군대로 나선 진취적인 프랑스인에게 맞설 수도 있었을 것이다."]], [["But under the influence of their degraded fortunes, both officers and men appeared better disposed to await the approach of their formidable antagonists, within their works, than to resist the progress of their march, by emulating the successful example of the French at Fort du Quesne, and striking a blow on their advance.", "그러나 추락한 운세의 영향 아래, 장교도 병사도, 〈뒤케스느〉 요새에서 거둔 프랑스군의 성공한 본보기를 본받아 적의 행군을 저지하고 진격하는 그들에게 일격을 가하기보다는, 자신들의 진지 안에서 가공할 적이 다가오기를 기다리는 쪽에 더 마음이 기운 듯했다."]], [["After the first surprise of the intelligence had a little abated, a rumor was spread through the entrenched camp, which stretched along the margin of the Hudson, forming a chain of outworks to the body of the fort itself, that a chosen detachment of fifteen hundred men was to depart, with the dawn, for William Henry, the post at the northern extremity of the portage.", "소식이 안긴 첫 놀라움이 조금 가라앉자, 〈허드슨〉강 기슭을 따라 길게 뻗어 요새 본체에 외곽 방어선의 사슬을 이루던 보루 진지 안에 한 가지 소문이 퍼졌으니, 정예 분견대 천오백 명이 새벽과 함께 〈윌리엄 헨리〉로, 곧 육로 운반로 북단의 진지로 떠난다는 것이었다."], ["That which at first was only rumor, soon became certainty, as orders passed from the quarters of the commander-in-chief to the several corps he had selected for this service, to prepare for their speedy departure.", "처음엔 소문에 지나지 않던 것이, 총사령관의 막사에서 이 임무를 위해 선발된 여러 부대로 속히 출발 준비를 하라는 명령이 내려오면서, 이내 확실한 사실이 되었다."], ["All doubt as to the intention of Webb now vanished, and an hour or two of hurried footsteps and anxious faces succeeded.", "이제 〈웨브〉의 의도에 관한 의심은 모두 사라졌고, 한두 시간에 걸쳐 분주한 발걸음과 근심 어린 얼굴들이 뒤따랐다."], ["The novice in the military art flew from point to point, retarding his own preparations by the excess of his violent and somewhat distempered zeal; while the more practised veteran made his arrangements with a deliberation that scorned every appearance of haste; though his sober lineaments and anxious eye sufficiently betrayed that he had no very strong professional relish for the as yet untried and dreaded warfare of the wilderness.", "군사에 풋내기인 자는 이 지점에서 저 지점으로 뛰어다니며, 격렬하고 다소 어수선한 열의가 지나친 나머지 도리어 제 준비를 더디게 했다. 반면에 보다 노련한 고참은 서두르는 기색이라곤 일절 경멸하는 듯한 차분함으로 채비를 갖췄으나, 그 침착한 표정과 근심 어린 눈은 아직 겪어 보지 못한, 두려운 황야의 전쟁에 직업적으로 그다지 강한 흥미를 느끼지 못한다는 것을 충분히 드러냈다."], ["At length the sun set in a flood of glory, behind the distant western hills, and as darkness drew its veil around the secluded spot the sounds of preparation diminished; the last light finally disappeared from the log cabin of some officer; the trees cast their deeper shadows over the mounds and the rippling stream, and a silence soon pervaded the camp, as deep as that which reigned in the vast forest by which it was environed.", "마침내 해가 먼 서쪽 언덕 너머로 영광의 홍수 속에 저물고, 어둠이 이 외딴곳에 장막을 드리우자 준비의 소리는 잦아들었다. 마지막 불빛이 어느 장교의 통나무 오두막에서 끝내 사라졌고, 나무들은 둔덕과 잔물결 이는 시냇물 위로 한층 짙은 그림자를 드리웠으며, 이내 진지에는 그것을 에워싼 광막한 숲을 지배하는 것만큼이나 깊은 정적이 깔렸다."]], [["According to the orders of the preceding night, the heavy sleep of the army was broken by the rolling of the warning drums, whose rattling echoes were heard issuing, on the damp morning air, out of every vista of the woods, just as day began to draw the shaggy outlines of some tall pines of the vicinity, on the opening brightness of a soft and cloudless eastern sky.", "전날 밤의 명령에 따라, 군대의 깊은 잠은 경고를 알리는 북소리의 울림에 깨졌으니, 그 우르릉대는 메아리가 축축한 아침 공기를 타고 숲의 모든 트인 길마다에서 새어 나오는 것이 들렸다. 바로 그때 동이 트며, 부드럽고 구름 한 점 없는 동쪽 하늘의 밝아 오는 빛 위로 인근 키 큰 소나무 몇 그루의 텁수룩한 윤곽이 그려지기 시작했다."], ["In an instant the whole camp was in motion; the meanest soldier arousing from his lair to witness the departure of his comrades, and to share in the excitement and incidents of the hour.", "순식간에 진지 전체가 움직였다. 가장 미천한 병사조차 제 잠자리에서 일어나, 전우들의 출발을 지켜보고 이 시각의 흥분과 사건에 한몫 끼려 했다."], ["The simple array of the chosen band was soon completed.", "선발된 부대의 단출한 정렬은 곧 마무리되었다."], ["While the regular and trained hirelings of the king marched with haughtiness to the right of the line, the less pretending colonists took their humbler position on its left, with a docility that long practice had rendered easy.", "왕의 정규 훈련된 용병들이 거만하게 대열의 오른편으로 행진하는 동안, 덜 잘난 체하는 식민지 민병들은 오랜 단련으로 몸에 밴 순순함으로 그 왼편의 보다 겸손한 자리를 차지했다."], ["The scouts departed; strong guards preceded and followed the lumbering vehicles that bore the baggage; and before the gray light of the morning was mellowed by the rays of the sun, the main body of the combatants wheeled into column, and left the encampment with a show of high military bearing, that served to drown the slumbering apprehensions of many a novice, who was now about to make his first essay in arms.", "척후가 떠났고, 강력한 호위대가 짐을 실은 덜컹대는 수레들을 앞뒤로 호위했으며, 아침의 잿빛 빛이 햇살로 부드러워지기도 전에, 전투원의 본대가 종대로 선회하여, 드높은 군인다운 위풍을 떨치며 진지를 떠났으니, 그 위풍은 이제 막 첫 출진을 앞둔 무수한 풋내기의 잠들어 있던 불안을 잠재우기에 족했다."], ["While in view of their admiring comrades, the same proud front and ordered array was observed, until the notes of their fifes growing fainter in distance, the forest at length appeared to swallow up the living mass which had slowly entered its bosom.", "감탄하는 전우들의 시야 안에 있는 동안에는, 똑같이 당당한 정면과 정연한 대열이 유지되었으나, 이윽고 그들의 피리 소리가 멀어지며 희미해지자, 마침내 숲은 그 품으로 천천히 들어선 살아 있는 무리를 삼켜 버리는 듯했다."]], [["The deepest sounds of the retiring and invisible column had ceased to be borne on the breeze to the listeners, and the latest straggler had already disappeared in pursuit; but there still remained the signs of another departure, before a log cabin of unusual size and accommodations, in front of which those sentinels paced their rounds, who were known to guard the person of the English general.", "물러가는 보이지 않는 종대의 가장 깊은 소리마저 더는 산들바람에 실려 듣는 이들에게 닿지 않게 되었고, 가장 늦은 낙오자마저 이미 그 뒤를 좇아 사라졌다. 그러나 평소와 달리 크고 안락하게 지은 한 통나무 오두막 앞에는, 또 다른 출발의 기색이 아직 남아 있었으니, 그 앞에서는 〈영국〉 장군의 신변을 지키는 것으로 알려진 보초들이 순찰을 돌고 있었다."], ["At this spot were gathered some half dozen horses, caparisoned in a manner which showed that two, at least, were destined to bear the persons of females, of a rank that it was not usual to meet so far in the wilds of the country.", "이곳에는 예닐곱 마리의 말이 모여 있었는데, 그 차림새로 보아 적어도 두 마리는, 이 고장 황야 깊숙한 데서는 좀처럼 만나기 힘든 신분의 여인들을 태우도록 마련된 것임을 알 수 있었다."], ["A third wore the trappings and arms of an officer of the staff; while the rest, from the plainness of the housings, and the travelling mails with which they were encumbered, were evidently fitted for the reception of as many menials, who were, seemingly, already awaiting the pleasure of those they served.", "셋째 마리는 참모 장교의 장구와 무장을 갖추고 있었고, 나머지는 그 수수한 안장과 그것들이 짊어진 여행용 행낭으로 보아, 명백히 그만큼의 하인들을 태우도록 채비된 것이었으니, 그들은 이미 섬기는 이들의 분부를 기다리고 있는 듯했다."], ["At a respectful distance from this unusual show were gathered divers groups of curious idlers; some admiring the blood and bone of the high-mettled military charger, and others gazing at the preparations, with dull wonder of vulgar curiosity.", "이 예사롭지 않은 광경에서 조심스레 떨어진 거리에는, 호기심 어린 한가한 구경꾼들이 여러 무리로 모여 있었다. 더러는 혈기 넘치는 군마의 혈통과 골격에 감탄했고, 더러는 천박한 호기심의 멍한 경이로 그 채비를 바라보았다."], ["There was one man, however, who, by his countenance and actions, formed a marked exception to those who composed the latter class of spectators, being neither idle, nor seemingly very ignorant.", "그러나 그 가운데 한 사내는, 그 표정과 행동으로 보아 후자에 속하는 구경꾼들과는 뚜렷이 구별되었으니, 한가하지도, 그다지 무지해 보이지도 않았다."]], [["The person of this individual was to the last degree ungainly, without being in any particular manner deformed.", "이 사람의 몸은 어느 한구석 특별히 기형이랄 데는 없으면서도, 더없이 볼품이 없었다."], ["He had all the bones and joints of other men, without any of their proportions.", "그는 다른 이들이 지닌 뼈와 관절을 다 갖추었으되, 그 비례라곤 조금도 갖추지 못했다."], ["Erect, his stature surpassed that of his fellows; seated, he appeared reduced within the ordinary limits of the race.", "똑바로 서면 그 키가 동료들을 능가했고, 앉으면 보통 사람의 한계 안으로 줄어든 듯 보였다."], ["The same contrariety in his members seemed to exist throughout the whole man.", "그 사지에 깃든 똑같은 어긋남이 온몸에 두루 존재하는 듯했다."], ["His head was large; his shoulders narrow; his arms long and dangling; while his hands were small, if not delicate.", "머리는 컸고, 어깨는 좁았으며, 팔은 길어 축 늘어졌으나, 손은 작다 못해 섬세하달 정도였다."], ["His legs and thighs were thin, nearly to emaciation, but of extraordinary length; and his knees would have been considered tremendous, had they not been outdone by the broader foundations on which this false superstructure of the blended human orders was so profanely reared.", "다리와 허벅지는 여위어 거의 수척할 지경이었으나 유달리 길었고, 무릎은 어마어마하다 여겨졌을 법했으나, 인간의 여러 부류가 뒤섞인 이 거짓된 상부 구조가 그토록 함부로 세워진 더 널찍한 토대에 그만 압도당하고 말았다."], ["The ill-assorted and injudicious attire of the individual only served to render his awkwardness more conspicuous.", "어울리지 않고 분별없는 이 사내의 옷차림은 그의 어색함을 한층 두드러지게 할 뿐이었다."], ["A sky-blue coat, with short and broad skirts and low cape, exposed a long thin neck, and longer and thinner legs, to the worst animadversions of the evil disposed.", "짧고 넓은 자락에 낮은 깃을 단 하늘색 상의는, 길고 가는 목과 그보다 더 길고 가는 다리를, 심술궂은 자들의 더없이 짓궂은 혹평에 고스란히 드러냈다."], ["His nether garment was of yellow nankeen, closely fitted to the shape, and tied at his bunches of knees by large knots of white ribbon, a good deal sullied by use.", "그 아래옷은 노란색 난징목면으로, 몸에 꼭 맞게 지어, 울퉁불퉁한 무릎께를 써서 꽤나 더러워진 흰 리본의 큰 매듭으로 묶었다."], ["Clouded cotton stockings, and shoes, on one of the latter of which was a plated spur, completed the costume of the lower extremity of this figure, no curve or angle of which was concealed, but, on the other hand, studiously exhibited, through the vanity or simplicity of its owner.", "얼룩덜룩한 무명 양말과 신발 — 그중 한 짝에는 도금한 박차가 달려 있었다 — 이 이 형상의 하반신 차림을 완성했으니, 그 어떤 곡선이나 각도도 가려지기는커녕, 도리어 임자의 허영심인지 단순함인지로 인해 일부러 드러나 있었다."], ["From beneath the flap of an enormous pocket of a soiled vest of embossed silk, heavily ornamented with tarnished silver lace, projected an instrument, which, from being seen in such martial company, might have been easily mistaken for some mischievous and unknown implement of war.", "빛바랜 은빛 레이스로 잔뜩 장식한 양각 비단 조끼의, 더러워진 커다란 주머니 덮개 아래로는 한 도구가 삐죽 나와 있었는데, 이런 무인다운 무리 속에서 보이는 까닭에, 자칫 어떤 고약하고 알 수 없는 전쟁 도구로 오인되기 십상이었다."], ["Small as it was, this uncommon engine had excited the curiosity of most of the Europeans in the camp, though several of the provincials were seen to handle it, not only without fear, but with the utmost familiarity.", "작기는 했어도, 이 흔치 않은 기물은 진지 안 유럽인 대다수의 호기심을 자아냈으나, 몇몇 식민지 민병은 그것을 두려움 없이 다루는 것은 물론, 더없이 친숙하게 다루는 모습도 보였다."], ["A large, civil cocked hat, like those worn by clergymen within the last thirty years, surmounted the whole, furnishing dignity to a good-natured and somewhat vacant countenance, that apparently needed such artificial aid, to support the gravity of some high and extraordinary trust.", "지난 삼십 년 사이 성직자들이 쓰던 것 같은, 크고 점잖은 삼각모가 그 모든 것 위에 얹혀, 그 인위적인 도움이 있어야 어떤 높고 비범한 임무의 무게를 떠받칠 수 있는 듯한, 사람 좋고 다소 멍한 얼굴에 위엄을 더해 주었다."]], [["While the common herd stood aloof, in deference to the quarters of Webb, the figure we have described stalked in the centre of the domestics, freely expressing his censures or commendations on the merits of the horses, as by chance they displeased or satisfied his judgment.", "여느 무리가 〈웨브〉의 막사를 어려워하여 멀찍이 떨어져 선 동안, 우리가 묘사한 형상은 하인들 한가운데를 활보하며, 말들이 우연히 제 판단에 거슬리거나 흡족하거나 함에 따라 그 우열에 관한 비난이나 칭찬을 거리낌 없이 늘어놓았다."]], [["\"This beast, I rather conclude, friend, is not of home raising, but is from foreign lands, or perhaps from the little island itself over the blue water?\"", "\"이 짐승은 말이오, 친구, 내 생각엔 토종이 아니라 외국 땅에서, 어쩌면 저 푸른 바다 건너 그 작은 섬 자체에서 온 것 같소만?\" 그가 말했으니, 그 목소리는 부드럽고 감미로운 음색으로, 그 몸이 보기 드문 비례로 유별난 것에 못지않게 유별났다."], ["he said, in a voice as remarkable for the softness and sweetness of its tones, as was his person for its rare proportions: \"I may speak of these things, and be no braggart; for I have been down at both havens; that which is situate at the mouth of Thames, and is named after the capital of Old England, and that which is called 'Haven,'", "\"내가 이런 것들을 말한다고 허풍쟁이는 아니오. 내 두 항구에 다 내려가 보았으니까."], ["with the addition of the word 'New'; and have seen the snows and brigantines collecting their droves, like the gathering to the ark, being outward bound to the Island of Jamaica, for the purpose of barter and traffic in four-footed animals; but never before have I beheld a beast which verified the true Scripture war-horse like this: 'He paweth in the valley, and rejoiceth in his strength: he goeth on to meet the armed men.", "〈템스〉강 어귀에 자리해 옛 〈영국〉의 수도를 따 이름 지은 곳과, '뉴'라는 말을 덧붙여 '헤이븐'이라 불리는 곳 말이오. 또 거기서 외돛배와 쌍돛배들이, 마치 방주로 모여드는 무리처럼, 네발짐승을 사고팔러 〈자메이카〉섬으로 떠나며 제 떼를 끌어모으는 것도 보았소."], ["He saith among the trumpets, Ha, ha; and he smelleth the battle afar off, the thunder of the captains, and the shouting.'", "허나 그전엔 결코, 이 짐승처럼 성경 속 참된 군마를 그대로 입증하는 짐승을 본 적이 없소. '그것이 골짜기에서 발굽을 구르며 제 힘을 기뻐하고, 나아가 무장한 자들을 맞이하는도다."], ["It would seem that the stock of the horse of Israel has descended to our own time; would it not, friend?\"", "나팔 소리 가운데서 히히 울며, 멀리서 싸움 냄새를 맡고, 장수들의 우렛소리와 함성을 듣는도다.' 〈이스라엘〉 말의 혈통이 우리 시대에까지 내려온 듯하지 않소, 친구?\""]], [["Receiving no reply to this extraordinary appeal, which in truth, as it was delivered with the vigor of full and sonorous tones, merited some sort of notice, he who had thus sung forth the language of the Holy Book turned to the silent figure to whom he had unwittingly addressed himself, and found a new and more powerful subject of admiration in the object that encountered his gaze.", "이 유별난 호소에 아무 대답도 받지 못하자 — 사실 그것은 충만하고 낭랑한 음색의 힘으로 전해진 만큼 어떤 식으로든 주의를 받을 만했다 — 이렇게 성서의 언어를 읊어 댄 자는, 자신이 무심코 말을 건넨 말없는 형상 쪽으로 돌아서, 제 시선에 마주친 대상에서 새롭고 한층 강력한 감탄의 소재를 발견했다."], ["His eyes fell on the still, upright, and rigid form of the \"Indian runner,\" who had borne to the camp the unwelcome tidings of the preceding evening.", "그의 눈길은, 전날 저녁 진지에 반갑잖은 소식을 전한 \"인디언 전령\"의 고요하고 곧고 뻣뻣한 형상에 가닿았다."], ["Although in a state of perfect repose, and apparently disregarding, with characteristic stoicism, the excitement and bustle around him, there was a sullen fierceness mingled with the quiet of the savage, that was likely to arrest the attention of much more experienced eyes than those which now scanned him, in unconcealed amazement.", "완전한 정지의 상태에 있고, 특유의 금욕으로 주위의 흥분과 소란을 무시하는 듯했으나, 미개인의 그 고요함에는 침울한 흉포함이 뒤섞여 있어, 지금 감추지 않은 경탄으로 그를 훑어보는 눈보다 훨씬 더 노련한 눈이라도 사로잡았음 직했다."], ["The native bore both the tomahawk and knife of his tribe; and yet his appearance was not altogether that of a warrior.", "이 원주민은 제 부족의 토마호크와 칼을 둘 다 지니고 있었으나, 그 모습이 온전히 전사의 것만은 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, there was an air of neglect about his person, like that which might have proceeded from great and recent exertion, which he had not yet found leisure to repair.", "도리어 그의 몸에는 무언가 방치된 기색이 감돌았으니, 마치 크고도 최근의 분투에서 비롯되었으되 아직 손볼 겨를을 찾지 못한 것 같았다."], ["The colors of the war-paint had blended in dark confusion about his fierce countenance, and rendered his swarthy lineaments still more savage and repulsive than if art had attempted an effect which had been thus produced by chance. His eye, alone, which glistened like a fiery star amid lowering clouds, was to be seen in its state of native wildness.", "전투용 칠의 색채는 그 흉포한 얼굴에 어둡게 뒤섞여 엉클어져, 그 거무스름한 이목구비를, 차라리 기교로써 이런 효과를 노렸을 때보다도 한층 더 야만스럽고 혐오스럽게 만들었으니, 마치 잔뜩 찌푸린 구름 사이로 솟은 것이 그 본래의 야성 그대로 드러나 보이는 듯했다."], ["For a single instant, his searching and yet wary glance met the wondering look of the other, and then changing its direction, partly in cunning, and partly in disdain, it remained fixed, as if penetrating the distant air.", "한순간, 그의 살피면서도 경계하는 눈길이 상대의 의아해하는 눈빛과 마주쳤다가, 이내 방향을 돌려, 반쯤은 교활함에서, 반쯤은 경멸에서, 마치 먼 허공을 꿰뚫는 듯 한 곳에 고정되었다."]], [["It is impossible to say what unlooked-for remark this short and silent communication, between two such singular men, might have elicited from the white man, had not his active curiosity been again drawn to other objects.", "이토록 유별난 두 사내 사이의 짧고 말 없는 이 교감이, 백인에게서 어떤 뜻밖의 말을 끌어냈을지는, 만약 그의 활발한 호기심이 다시 다른 대상으로 쏠리지 않았더라면, 알 도리가 없다."], ["A general movement among the domestics, and a low sound of gentle voices, announced the approach of those whose presence alone was wanted to enable the cavalcade to move.", "하인들 사이의 전반적인 움직임과 부드러운 목소리들의 나직한 소리가, 그들이 있어야만 비로소 이 일행이 출발할 수 있는 이들의 다가옴을 알렸다."], ["The simple admirer of the war-horse instantly fell back to a low, gaunt, switch-tailed mare, that was unconsciously gleaning the faded herbage of the camp nigh by; where, leaning with one elbow on the blanket that concealed an apology for a saddle, he became a spectator of the departure, while a foal was quietly making its morning repast, on the opposite side of the same animal.", "군마를 소박하게 흠모하던 자는 즉각, 근처에서 무심코 진지의 시든 풀을 뜯고 있던, 키 작고 깡마른 데다 꼬리가 회초리 같은 암말 쪽으로 물러났다. 그곳에서 그는, 안장이랍시고 둘러친 것을 가린 담요에 한쪽 팔꿈치를 괴고는, 출발을 지켜보는 구경꾼이 되었으니, 그동안 망아지 한 마리가 같은 짐승의 반대편에서 조용히 아침 식사를 하고 있었다."]], [["A young man, in the dress of an officer, conducted to their steeds two females, who, as it was apparent by their dresses, were prepared to encounter the fatigues of a journey in the woods.", "장교 복장을 한 젊은이가 두 여인을 그들의 말로 인도했는데, 그 옷차림으로 보아 그들이 숲속 여정의 고초를 겪을 채비를 한 것이 분명했다."], ["One, and she was the most juvenile in her appearance, though both were young, permitted glimpses of her dazzling complexion, fair golden hair, and bright blue eyes, to be caught, as she artlessly suffered the morning air to blow aside the green veil which descended low from her beaver.", "둘 다 젊었으나, 그중 가장 앳되어 보이는 한 여인은, 천진하게도 아침 공기가 비버 모자에서 낮게 드리운 초록 베일을 옆으로 날리게 두는 동안, 눈부신 살결과 고운 금빛 머리칼, 밝은 푸른 눈을 언뜻언뜻 내비쳤다."], ["The flush which still lingered above the pines in the western sky was not more bright nor delicate than the bloom on her cheek; nor was the opening day more cheering than the animated smile which she bestowed on the youth, as he assisted her into the saddle.", "서쪽 하늘 소나무 위에 아직 머물러 있던 노을빛도 그녀의 뺨에 핀 홍조보다 더 밝거나 곱지 않았고, 밝아 오는 날도, 젊은이가 그녀를 안장에 오르도록 거들 때 그녀가 그에게 보낸 생기 넘치는 미소보다 더 상쾌하지 않았다."], ["The other, who appeared to share equally in the attentions of the young officer, concealed her charms from the gaze of the soldiery, with a care that seemed better fitted to the experience of four or five additional years.", "젊은 장교의 보살핌을 똑같이 나누어 받는 듯 보이던 다른 여인은, 네댓 살은 더 든 경험에 더 어울릴 법한 조심성으로, 병사들의 시선에서 제 아리따움을 감추었다."], ["It could be seen, however, that her person, though moulded with the same exquisite proportions, of which none of the graces were lost by the travelling dress she wore, was rather fuller and more mature than that of her companion.", "그러나 그녀의 몸은, 똑같이 빼어난 비례로 빚어져, 입은 여행복으로도 그 우아함을 조금도 잃지 않았으되, 동행보다는 다소 더 풍만하고 무르익었음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["No sooner were these females seated, than their attendant sprang lightly into the saddle of the war-horse, when the whole three bowed to Webb, who, in courtesy, awaited their parting on the threshold of his cabin, and turning their horses'", "이 여인들이 자리에 앉기가 무섭게, 그들의 시중을 드는 이가 군마의 안장 위로 가뿐히 뛰어올랐고, 그러자 세 사람 모두 〈웨브〉에게 절을 했다. 그는 예의를 갖추어 제 오두막 문턱에서 그들의 작별을 기다리고 있었다."], ["heads, they proceeded at a slow amble, followed by their train, towards the northern entrance of the encampment.", "그들은 말 머리를 돌려, 수행원들을 거느린 채 천천히 느린 걸음으로 진지의 북쪽 입구를 향해 나아갔다."], ["As they traversed that short distance, not a voice was heard amongst them; but a slight exclamation proceeded from the younger of the females, as the Indian runner glided by her, unexpectedly, and led the way along the military road in her front.", "그 짧은 거리를 가로지르는 동안 그들 사이에서는 한마디도 들리지 않았으나, 인디언 전령이 뜻밖에 더 젊은 여인 곁을 미끄러지듯 스쳐 지나 그녀 앞 군사 도로를 따라 앞장서자, 그녀의 입에서 가벼운 탄성이 새어 나왔다."], ["Though this sudden and startling movement of the Indian produced no sound from the other, in the surprise her veil also was allowed to open its folds, and betrayed an indescribable look of pity, admiration, and horror, as her dark eye followed the easy motions of the savage.", "이 인디언의 돌연하고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 움직임은 다른 여인에게서는 아무 소리도 자아내지 못했으나, 그 놀라움 속에 그녀의 베일도 주름을 펼치며 열렸으니, 그녀의 검은 눈이 미개인의 유연한 동작을 좇는 동안, 형언할 수 없는 연민과 감탄과 공포의 표정이 드러났다."], ["The tresses of this lady were shining and black, like the plumage of the raven.", "이 여인의 머릿단은 갈까마귀의 깃털처럼 윤기 흐르는 검은빛이었다."], ["Her complexion was not brown, but it rather appeared charged with the color of the rich blood, that seemed ready to burst its bounds.", "그녀의 살결은 갈색이라기보다, 차라리 금방이라도 제 한계를 터뜨릴 듯한 진한 피의 빛깔로 가득 찬 듯 보였다."], ["And yet there was neither coarseness nor want of shadowing in a countenance that was exquisitely regular and dignified, and surpassingly beautiful.", "그러면서도 더없이 단정하고 기품 있으며 빼어나게 아름다운 그 얼굴에는, 거칠음도, 음영의 부족함도 없었다."], ["She smiled, as if in pity at her own momentary forgetfulness, discovering by the act a row of teeth that would have shamed the purest ivory; when, replacing the veil, she bowed her face, and rode in silence, like one whose thoughts were abstracted from the scene around her.", "그녀는 마치 한순간의 제 망각을 가엾이 여기는 듯 미소 지었는데, 그 동작으로 가장 순결한 상아조차 무색하게 할 가지런한 잇바디를 드러냈다. 그러고는 베일을 다시 매만져 얼굴을 숙이고, 마치 주위의 광경에서 마음이 멀리 떠난 사람처럼 말없이 말을 몰았다."]], [["\"Sola, sola, wo, ha, ho, sola!\"", "\"솔라, 솔라, 워, 하, 호, 솔라!\""]], [["While one of the lovely beings we have so cursorily presented to the reader was thus lost in thought, the other quickly recovered from the alarm which induced the exclamation, and, laughing at her own weakness, she inquired of the youth who rode by her side,--", "셰익스피어\n우리가 독자에게 그토록 황급히 소개한 사랑스러운 두 사람 가운데 하나가 이렇듯 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안, 다른 하나는 방금 그 외침을 자아낸 놀라움에서 빠르게 헤어나, 제 나약함을 스스로 비웃으며 곁에서 말을 탄 청년에게 물었다."]], [["\"Are such spectres frequent in the woods, Heyward; or is this sight an especial entertainment on our behalf?", "\"이런 유령들이 숲에서 자주 나타나나요, 〈헤이워드〉? 아니면 이 광경이 우리를 위해 특별히 마련된 볼거리인가요?"], ["If the latter, gratitude must close our mouths; but if the former, both Cora and I shall have need to draw largely on that stock of hereditary courage which we boast, even before we are made to encounter the redoubtable Montcalm.\"", "만일 후자라면 고마운 마음에 입을 다물어야겠지만, 전자라면 〈코라〉와 나 둘 다, 저 가공할 〈몽칼름〉과 맞서기도 전에, 우리가 자랑으로 삼는 그 물려받은 용기의 밑천을 잔뜩 끌어다 써야겠는걸요.\""]], [["\"Yon Indian is a 'runner'", "\"저기 저 인디언은 군의 '전령'이오."], ["of the army; and, after the fashion of his people, he may be accounted a hero,\"", "제 부족의 방식대로 보자면 그는 영웅으로 여겨질 만하지.\""], ["returned the officer.", "장교가 대답했다."], ["\"He has volunteered to guide us to the lake, by a path but little known, sooner than if we followed the tardy movements of the column: and, by consequence, more agreeably.\"", "\"그는 거의 알려지지 않은 길을 통해 우리를 호수까지 안내하겠다고 자청했소. 종대의 굼뜬 움직임을 따르는 것보다 더 빨리, 따라서 더 기분 좋게 갈 수 있도록 말이오.\""]], [["\"I like him not,\"", "\"저 사람이 마음에 들지 않아요.\""], ["said the lady, shuddering, partly in assumed, yet more in real terror.", "여인이 몸을 떨며 말했으니, 얼마쯤은 꾸민 것이었으나 그보다 더 많이는 진짜 두려움에서였다."], ["\"You know him, Duncan, or you would not trust yourself so freely to his keeping?\"", "\"당신은 저 사람을 아시지요, 〈덩컨〉? 그러지 않고서야 이토록 선뜻 그의 손에 자신을 맡기지는 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["\"Say, rather, Alice, that I would not trust you. I do know him, or he would not have my confidence, and least of all at this moment. He is said to be a Canadian, too; and yet he served with our friends the Mohawks, who, as you know, are one of the six allied nations.[3] He was brought among us, as I have heard, by some strange accident in which your father was interested, and in which the savage was rigidly dealt by--but I forget the idle tale; it is enough, that he is now our friend.\"", "\"차라리, 〈앨리스〉, 당신을 맡기지는 않으리라고 말해 주오. 나는 정말로 그를 잘 알고 있소. 그러지 않았다면 그를 신임하지 않았을 테고, 더구나 이런 때에는 더더욱 그랬을 거요. 그는 〈캐나다〉 사람이라고도 하오. 그러면서도 우리의 벗인 〈모호크〉족과 함께 복무했지. 당신도 알다시피 그들은 여섯 동맹 부족 가운데 하나요. 내가 듣기로 그는 어떤 기이한 사건을 계기로 우리 사이에 들어왔는데, 그 일에는 당신 부친도 관여하셨고, 그 미개인은 엄혹한 처분을 받았다 하오. 허나 그 시시한 이야기는 잊어버리겠소. 그가 지금 우리의 벗이라는 사실로 족하니까.\""]], [["\"If he has been my father's enemy, I like him still less!\"", "\"그가 내 아버지의 적이었다면, 더욱더 마음에 들지 않아요!\""], ["exclaimed the now really anxious girl.", "이제 정말로 불안해진 처녀가 외쳤다."], ["\"Will you not speak to him, Major Heyward, that I may hear his tones?", "\"〈헤이워드〉 소령, 그에게 말을 좀 걸어 주시겠어요? 그의 목소리를 들어 보고 싶으니까요."], ["Foolish though it may be, you have often heard me avow my faith in the tones of the human voice!\"", "어리석은 일일지 모르나, 내가 사람 목소리의 음색을 믿는다고 늘 말하는 것을 당신도 여러 번 들으셨잖아요!\""]], [["\"It would be in vain; and answered, most probably, by an ejaculation.", "\"소용없을 거요. 십중팔구 외마디 소리로나 답할 테니까."], ["Though he may understand it, he affects, like most of his people, to be ignorant of the English; and least of all will he condescend to speak it, now that war demands the utmost exercise of his dignity.", "그는 영어를 알아들으면서도, 제 부족 대다수가 그러하듯 모르는 체하오. 더구나 전쟁이 그 위엄을 최대한 발휘하기를 요구하는 지금은, 영어로 말하는 데 응하려 들지 않을 거요."], ["But he stops; the private path by which we are to journey is, doubtless, at hand.\"", "한데 그가 멈추는군. 우리가 가야 할 그 은밀한 오솔길이 분명 가까운가 보오.\""]], [["The conjecture of Major Heyward was true.", "〈헤이워드〉 소령의 짐작은 맞았다."], ["When they reached the spot where the Indian stood, pointing into the thicket that fringed the military road, a narrow and blind path, which might, with some little inconvenience, receive one person at a time, became visible.", "그 인디언이 서서 군용 도로 가장자리를 두른 덤불 속을 가리키고 있는 곳에 다다르니, 좁고 분간하기 어려운 오솔길이, 다소 불편을 감수하면 한 번에 한 사람씩은 지날 만한 길이 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["\"Here, then, lies our way,\"", "\"자, 그러면 우리 길은 이쪽으로 나 있소.\""], ["said the young man, in a low voice.", "청년이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["\"Manifest no distrust, or you may invite the danger you appear to apprehend.\"", "\"불신하는 기색을 조금도 내비치지 마시오. 그러지 않으면 그대들이 두려워하는 듯한 그 위험을 도리어 불러들일지 모르니.\""]], [["\"Cora, what think you?\"", "\"〈코라〉, 너는 어떻게 생각하니?\""], ["asked the reluctant fair one.", "내키지 않아 하는 가녀린 처녀가 물었다."], ["\"If we journey with the troops, though we may find their presence irksome, shall we not feel better assurance of our safety?\"", "\"군대와 함께 가면, 그들이 곁에 있는 것이 거추장스럽기야 하겠지만, 그래도 우리 안전에 대해 더 큰 확신을 느낄 수 있지 않을까?\""]], [["\"Being little accustomed to the practices of the savages, Alice, you mistake the place of real danger,\"", "\"미개인들의 관행에 익숙지 않아서 그렇소, 〈앨리스〉. 그대는 진짜 위험이 도사린 곳을 잘못 짚고 있소.\""], ["said Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 말했다."], ["\"If enemies have reached the portage at all, a thing by no means probable, as our scouts are abroad, they will surely be found skirting the column where scalps abound the most.", "\"만일 적이 그 육로 운반로까지 닿았다면 — 우리 척후들이 나가 있으니 결코 있음 직한 일은 아니지만 — 그들은 분명 머릿가죽이 가장 많이 모인 종대 언저리를 어슬렁거리고 있을 거요."], ["The route of the detachment is known, while ours, having been determined within the hour, must still be secret.\"", "그 분견대의 행로는 알려져 있으나, 우리 길은 한 시간 안에 정해진 것이니 아직 비밀로 남아 있을 게 틀림없소.\""]], [["\"Should we distrust the man because his manners are not our manners, and that his skin is dark?\"", "\"저 사람의 태도가 우리 태도와 다르다고, 또 그 피부가 검다고 해서 그를 불신해야 할까요?\""], ["coldly asked Cora.", "〈코라〉가 차갑게 물었다."]], [["Alice hesitated no longer; but giving her Narragansett[4] a smart cut of the whip, she was the first to dash aside the slight branches of the bushes, and to follow the runner along the dark and tangled pathway.", "〈앨리스〉는 더는 머뭇거리지 않았다. 도리어 자기 〈내러갠셋〉에게 채찍을 매섭게 한 차례 가하고는, 맨 먼저 덤불의 가는 가지들을 옆으로 헤치며 어둡고 얽힌 오솔길을 따라 그 전령의 뒤를 좇았다."], ["The young man regarded the last speaker in open admiration, and even permitted her fairer though certainly not more beautiful companion to proceed unattended, while he sedulously opened the way himself for the passage of her who has been called Cora.", "청년은 마지막으로 말한 처녀를 노골적인 감탄으로 바라보았고, 더 희되 결코 더 아름답지는 않은 그 동행이 아무도 거들지 않은 채 나아가도록 내버려 두면서까지, 〈코라〉라 불린 처녀가 지날 길은 손수 부지런히 터 주었다."], ["It would seem that the domestics had been previously instructed; for, instead of penetrating the thicket, they followed the route of the column; a measure which Heyward stated had been dictated by the sagacity of their guide, in order to diminish the marks of their trail, if, haply, the Canadian savages should be lurking so far in advance of their army.", "하인들은 미리 지시를 받은 모양이었다. 덤불 속으로 파고드는 대신 종대의 행로를 따랐으니, 〈헤이워드〉의 말로는, 혹여 〈캐나다〉의 미개인들이 제 군대보다 그토록 멀리 앞서 숨어 있을 경우에 대비해 일행의 자취를 줄이려는, 안내인의 지혜에서 나온 방책이라 했다."], ["For many minutes the intricacy of the route admitted of no further dialogue; after which they emerged from the broad border of underbrush which grew along the line of the highway, and entered under the high but dark arches of the forest.", "길이 워낙 뒤얽혀 있어 여러 분 동안은 더 이상 대화를 나눌 수 없었다. 그러고 나서 그들은 큰길을 따라 자란 넓은 잡목 띠에서 빠져나와, 높되 어두운 숲의 아치 아래로 들어섰다."], ["Here their progress was less interrupted, and the instant the guide perceived that the females could command their steeds, he moved on, at a pace between a trot and a walk, and at a rate which kept the sure-footed and peculiar animals they rode, at a fast yet easy amble. The youth had turned to speak to the dark-eyed Cora, when the distant sound of horses'", "이곳에서는 행보가 한결 덜 막혔고, 안내인은 여인들이 제 말을 능히 다룬다는 것을 알아채자마자, 빠른 걸음과 보통 걸음 사이의 속도로 나아갔으니, 그들이 탄 발 디딤이 든든하고 유별난 짐승들이 빠르되 편안한 측대보로 가도록 하는 속도였다."], ["hoofs, clattering over the roots of the broken way in his rear, caused him to check his charger; and, as his companions drew their reins at the same instant, the whole party came to a halt, in order to obtain an explanation of the unlooked-for interruption.", "청년이 검은 눈의 〈코라〉에게 말을 걸려고 몸을 돌리는 순간, 뒤편 부서진 길의 뿌리 위를 덜그럭거리며 달려오는 말발굽 소리가 멀리서 들려와 그는 군마를 멈춰 세웠다. 그의 동행들도 같은 순간 고삐를 당기니, 일행 모두가 멈춰 서서 이 뜻밖의 방해가 어찌 된 영문인지 알아보려 했다."]], [["In a few moments a colt was seen gliding, like a fallow-deer, among the straight trunks of the pines; and, in another instant, the person of the ungainly man described in the preceding chapter, came into view, with as much rapidity as he could excite his meagre beast to endure without coming to an open rupture.", "이내 망아지 한 마리가 노루처럼 곧게 뻗은 소나무 줄기들 사이를 미끄러지듯 달려오는 것이 보였다. 또 한순간 뒤에는, 앞 장에서 묘사한 볼품없는 사내의 모습이 시야에 들어왔으니, 그는 비쩍 마른 짐승을 노골적인 파탄에까지 몰지 않으려 애쓰면서도, 그 짐승이 견딜 만한 한도껏 다그쳐 달려오고 있었다."], ["Until now this personage had escaped the observation of the travellers.", "이때까지 이 인물은 여행자들의 눈길을 피해 있었다."], ["If he possessed the power to arrest any wandering eye when exhibiting the glories of his altitude on foot, his equestrian graces were still more likely to attract attention.", "두 발로 서서 제 키의 영광을 펼칠 때조차 떠도는 눈길을 붙들어 두는 힘이 있었다면, 그가 말 위에서 부리는 우아함은 더더욱 시선을 끌 만했다."], ["Notwithstanding a constant application of his one armed heel to the flanks of the mare, the most confirmed gait that he could establish was a Canterbury gallop with the hind legs, in which those more forward assisted for doubtful moments, though generally content to maintain a loping trot.", "외짝 박차 달린 발뒤꿈치를 끊임없이 암말의 옆구리에 들이대도, 그가 끌어낼 수 있는 가장 한결같은 걸음새라곤 뒷다리로 내딛는 캔터베리식 구보였고, 그 사이 앞다리는 미덥지 못한 순간순간 거들기는 했으나 대개는 껑충대는 속보를 유지하는 데 만족했다."], ["Perhaps the rapidity of the changes from one of these paces to the other created an optical illusion, which might thus magnify the powers of the beast; for it is certain that Heyward, who possessed a true eye for the merits of a horse, was unable, with his utmost ingenuity, to decide by what sort of movement his pursuer worked his sinuous way on his footsteps with such persevering hardihood.", "어쩌면 이 걸음새에서 저 걸음새로 바뀌는 그 빠르기가 착시를 일으켜 짐승의 능력을 부풀려 보이게 했는지도 모른다. 말의 됨됨이를 보는 눈이 정확한 〈헤이워드〉조차, 제 온갖 재간을 다 써도, 자기를 뒤쫓는 자가 어떤 종류의 움직임으로 그토록 끈질긴 담력으로 제 발자취를 따라 구불구불 길을 헤쳐 오는지 분간할 수 없었던 것이 분명하니까."]], [["The industry and movements of the rider were not less remarkable than those of the ridden.", "탄 사람의 부지런함과 동작도 탄 짐승의 그것 못지않게 볼만했다."], ["At each change in the evolutions of the latter, the former raised his tall person in the stirrups; producing, in this manner, by the undue elongation of his legs, such sudden growths and diminishings of the stature, as baffled every conjecture that might be made as to his dimensions.", "짐승의 걸음새가 바뀔 때마다, 사내는 등자를 디뎌 제 큰 몸을 일으켜 세웠다. 이런 식으로 다리를 지나치게 늘여, 키가 별안간 자라났다 줄어들었다 하니, 그 치수가 도대체 어떠한지 헤아릴 만한 온갖 짐작을 무색하게 했다."], ["If to this be added the fact that, in consequence of the ex parte application of the spur, one side of the mare appeared to journey faster than the other; and that the aggrieved flank was resolutely indicated by unremitted flourishes of a bushy tail, we finish the picture of both horse and man.", "여기에, 박차가 한쪽에만 가해진 탓에 암말의 한 옆구리가 다른 옆구리보다 더 빨리 가는 듯 보였다는 것, 그리고 그 억울한 옆구리가 텁수룩한 꼬리의 끊임없는 휘두름으로 단호히 표시되었다는 사실을 보태면, 말과 사람 양쪽의 그림이 마저 완성된다."]], [["The frown which had gathered around the handsome, open, and manly brow of Heyward, gradually relaxed, and his lips curled into a slight smile, as he regarded the stranger.", "〈헤이워드〉의 잘생기고 트이고 사내다운 이마에 잡혔던 찌푸림이 차차 풀렸고, 그가 이 낯선 사내를 바라보는 동안 입술은 옅은 미소로 휘었다."], ["Alice made no very powerful effort to control her merriment; and even the dark, thoughtful eye of Cora lighted with a humor that, it would seem, the habit, rather than the nature of its mistress repressed.", "〈앨리스〉는 제 웃음을 억누르려 그리 애쓰지도 않았다. 〈코라〉의 검고 사려 깊은 눈조차도, 그 주인의 천성이라기보다는 몸에 밴 습관이 억눌러 온 듯한 익살로 환해졌다."]], [["\"Seek you any here?\"", "\"여기서 누구를 찾으시오?\""], ["demanded Heyward, when the other had arrived sufficiently nigh to abate his speed; \"I trust you are no messenger of evil tidings?\"", "상대가 속도를 늦출 만큼 가까이 다가오자 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다. \"흉한 소식을 전하는 사자는 아니시기를 바라오만?\""]], [["\"Even so,\" replied the stranger, making diligent use of his triangular castor, to produce a circulation in the close air of the woods, and leaving his hearers in doubt to which of the young man's questions he responded; when, however, he had cooled his face, and recovered his breath, he continued, \"I hear you are riding to William Henry; as I am journeying thitherward myself, I concluded good company would seem consistent to the wishes of both parties.\"", "\"바로 그러하오.\" 낯선 사내가 대답했으니, 삼각모를 부지런히 부쳐 숲의 텁텁한 공기에 바람을 일으키면서, 청년의 두 물음 가운데 어느 쪽에 답하는지 듣는 이들이 알 수 없게 두었다. 그러나 얼굴을 식히고 숨을 고른 뒤에 그는 말을 이었다. \"여러분이 〈윌리엄 헨리〉로 가신다고 들었소. 나도 그쪽으로 가는 길이니, 좋은 동행이라면 양쪽의 바람에 두루 맞으리라 결론지었소이다.\""]], [["\"You appear to possess the privilege of a casting vote,\"", "\"보아하니 댁은 결정권을 쥔 한 표의 특권을 가진 모양이오.\""], ["returned Heyward; \"we are three, whilst you have consulted no one but yourself.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 받아쳤다. \"우리는 셋인데, 댁은 자기 자신 말고는 아무와도 의논하지 않았으니 말이오.\""]], [["\"Even so.", "\"바로 그렇소."], ["The first point to be obtained is to know one's own mind.", "가장 먼저 얻어야 할 것은 제 마음을 아는 일이오."], ["Once sure of that, and where women are concerned, it is not easy, the next is, to act up to the decision.", "일단 그것이 분명해지면 — 여인이 얽힌 일에서는 쉽지 않은 노릇이오만 — 그다음은 그 결정대로 행하는 것이지요."], ["I have endeavored to do both, and here I am.\"", "나는 그 둘을 다 해 보려 애썼고, 그리하여 여기 와 있소이다.\""]], [["\"If you journey to the lake, you have mistaken your route,\"", "\"호수로 가신다면, 길을 잘못 드셨소.\""], ["said Heyward, haughtily; \"the highway thither is at least half a mile behind you.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 거만하게 말했다. \"그리로 가는 큰길은 적어도 반 마일은 댁 뒤에 있소.\""]], [["\"Even so,\" returned the stranger, nothing daunted by this cold reception; \"I have tarried at 'Edward' a week, and I should be dumb not to have inquired the road I was to journey; and if dumb there would be an end to my calling.\" After simpering in a small way, like one whose modesty prohibited a more open expression of his admiration of a witticism that was perfectly unintelligible to his hearers, he continued: \"It is not prudent for any one of my profession to be too familiar with those he is to instruct; for which reason I follow not the line of the army; besides which, I conclude that a gentleman of your character has the best judgment in matters of wayfaring; I have therefore decided to join company, in order that the ride may be made agreeable, and partake of social communion.\"", "\"바로 그렇소.\" 이 쌀쌀한 대접에도 조금도 기죽지 않고 낯선 사내가 대답했다. \"나는 〈에드워드〉에 일주일을 머물렀소. 그러니 가야 할 길을 묻지 않았다면 벙어리나 다름없을 게요. 또 벙어리라면 내 소명도 끝장이지요.\" 듣는 이들로서는 도무지 알아들을 수 없는, 그러나 본인은 멋들어졌다 여기는 농담에 대한 감탄을 더 드러내 놓고 표하기를 겸양이 막기라도 한 듯, 그가 조그맣게 히죽이고 나서 말을 이었다. \"내 직업을 가진 자가 제 가르칠 사람들과 너무 허물없이 지내는 것은 현명치 못하오. 그런 까닭에 나는 군대의 행렬을 따르지 않소이다. 게다가 댁 같은 분이라면 길 떠나는 일에 가장 밝은 판단을 지녔으리라 결론지었으니, 그래서 일행에 합류하기로 마음먹었소. 그러면 길 가는 일도 즐거워질 테고, 사람 사이의 사귐도 나눌 수 있을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["\"A most arbitrary, if not a hasty decision!\"", "\"성급하다고는 못 해도, 더없이 제멋대로인 결정이로군!\""], ["exclaimed Heyward, undecided whether to give vent to his growing anger, or to laugh in the other's face.", "〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤으니, 차오르는 노여움을 터뜨릴지 아니면 상대의 면전에 대고 웃어 줄지 정하지 못한 채였다."], ["\"But you speak of instruction, and of a profession; are you an adjunct to the provincial corps, as a master of the noble science of defence and offence; or, perhaps, you are one who draws lines and angles, under the pretence of expounding the mathematics?\"", "\"한데 댁은 가르침과 직업을 말씀하시는데, 댁은 식민지 군단에 딸린, 공방의 고귀한 학문을 가르치는 사범이시오? 아니면 어쩌면, 수학을 풀이한다는 구실로 선과 각을 긋는 사람이시오?\""]], [["The stranger regarded his interrogator a moment, in wonder; and then, losing every mark of self-satisfaction in an expression of solemn humility, he answered:--", "낯선 사내는 묻는 이를 한순간 의아하게 바라보더니, 자기만족의 모든 기색을 엄숙한 겸손의 표정 속에 지우며 대답했다."]], [["\"Of offence, I hope there is none, to either party: of defence, I make none--by God's good mercy, having committed no palpable sin since last entreating his pardoning grace. I understand not your allusions about lines and angles; and I leave expounding to those who have been called and set apart for that holy office. I lay claim to no higher gift than a small insight into the glorious art of petitioning and thanksgiving, as practised in psalmody.\"", "\"공방이라 하면, 어느 쪽에도 그런 것은 없기를 바라오. 방어로 말하자면 나는 아무것도 짓지 않소. 하느님의 너그러운 자비로, 마지막으로 그 용서의 은총을 빈 이래로는 뚜렷한 죄를 짓지 않았으니 말이오. 선이며 각이며 하는 댁의 비유는 알아듣지 못하겠소. 풀이하는 일은 그 거룩한 직분에 부름받아 따로 세워진 이들에게 맡기겠소. 나는 시편 영창에서 행하는바, 간구와 감사라는 영광스러운 기예에 대한 작은 통찰 말고는 더 높은 재능을 내세우지 않소이다.\" * 공방(攻防): 〈헤이워드〉가 'offence'와 'defence'를 군사 용어로 썼으나, 낯선 사내는 이를 '공격(죄)'과 '변명(방어)'의 도덕적 의미로 받아 엇갈리게 답한다."]], [["\"The man is, most manifestly, a disciple of Apollo,\"", "\"이 사람은, 누가 봐도 〈아폴로〉의 제자로군요.\""], ["cried the amused Alice, \"and I take him under my own especial protection.", "흥겨워진 〈앨리스〉가 외쳤다. \"내가 직접 특별히 보호해 드려야겠어요."], ["Nay, throw aside that frown, Heyward, and in pity to my longing ears, suffer him to journey in our train.", "자, 그 찌푸린 얼굴은 거두세요, 〈헤이워드〉. 그리고 갈망하는 내 귀를 가엾이 여겨, 이분이 우리 일행에 끼어 가도록 해 주세요."], ["Besides,\"", "게다가,\""], ["she added, in a low and hurried voice, casting a glance at the distant Cora, who slowly followed the footsteps of their silent but sullen guide, \"it may be a friend added to our strength, in time of need.\"", "그녀는 낮고 다급한 목소리로, 말없으나 뚱한 안내인의 발자취를 천천히 좇는 저만치의 〈코라〉를 흘긋 보며 덧붙였다, \"어려운 때에 우리 힘에 보태질 한 사람의 벗이 될지도 모르잖아요.\""]], [["\"Think you, Alice, that I would trust those I love by this secret path, did I imagine such need could happen?\"", "\"〈앨리스〉, 그런 일이 일어날 수 있다고 여겼다면 내가 사랑하는 이들을 이 은밀한 길로 데려왔겠소?\""]], [["\"Nay, nay, I think not of it now; but this strange man amuses me; and if he 'hath music in his soul,'", "\"아니, 아니에요, 지금 그런 걸 두고 하는 말이 아니에요. 다만 이 별난 사람이 나를 즐겁게 하니까요."], ["let us not churlishly reject his company.\"", "그리고 그가 '영혼에 음악을 지녔다'면, 우리가 매정하게 그 동행을 물리치지는 말기로 해요.\""], ["She pointed persuasively along the path with her riding-whip, while their eyes met in a look which the young man lingered a moment to prolong; then yielding to her gentle influence, he clapped his spurs into his charger, and in a few bounds was again at the side of Cora.", "그녀는 말채찍으로 오솔길 쪽을 가리키며 권하듯 했고, 그러는 동안 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳐 청년은 그 눈맞춤을 한순간 길게 끌었다. 이윽고 그녀의 부드러운 권유에 따라, 그는 군마에 박차를 박아 몇 번 도약 끝에 다시 〈코라〉의 곁에 섰다."]], [["\"I am glad to encounter thee, friend,\"", "\"이렇게 만나게 되어 기뻐요, 벗이여.\""], ["continued the maiden, waving her hand to the stranger to proceed, as she urged her Narragansett to renew its amble.", "처녀는 〈내러갠셋〉을 다시 측대보로 몰면서, 낯선 사내에게 앞장서라고 손을 저으며 말을 이었다."], ["\"Partial relatives have almost persuaded me that I am not entirely worthless in a duet myself; and we may enliven our wayfaring by indulging in our favorite pursuit.", "\"팔이 안으로 굽는 친척들이, 나 자신도 이중창에서 아주 쓸모없지는 않다고 거의 믿게 만들었답니다. 그러니 우리가 좋아하는 일에 마음껏 빠져 길 가는 흥을 돋울 수 있겠어요."], ["It might be of signal advantage to one, ignorant as I, to hear the opinions and experience of a master in the art.\"", "나처럼 무지한 사람에게는, 이 기예의 대가가 들려주는 견해와 경험을 듣는 것이 큰 도움이 될 거예요.\""]], [["\"It is refreshing both to the spirits and to the body to indulge in psalmody, in befitting seasons,\"", "\"마땅한 철에 시편 영창에 빠지는 것은 정신에도 몸에도 새 기운을 주지요.\""], ["returned the master of song, unhesitatingly complying with her intimation to follow; \"and nothing would relieve the mind more than such a consoling communion.", "노래의 대가는 그녀가 따라오라 넌지시 이른 데에 망설임 없이 응하며 대답했다. \"그처럼 위안을 주는 사귐만큼 마음을 풀어 주는 것도 없소이다."], ["But four parts are altogether necessary to the perfection of melody.", "허나 가락이 온전해지려면 네 성부가 다 갖추어져야 하오."], ["You have all the manifestations of a soft and rich treble; I can, by especial aid, carry a full tenor to the highest letter; but we lack counter and bass!", "아가씨에게는 부드럽고 풍부한 트레블의 온갖 자질이 다 보이오. 나는 특별한 도움을 받으면 충만한 테너를 가장 높은 음까지 끌어올릴 수 있고."], ["Yon officer of the king, who hesitated to admit me to his company, might fill the latter, if one may judge from the intonations of his voice in common dialogue.\"", "헌데 우리에겐 카운터와 베이스가 없구려! 나를 일행에 끼워 주기를 망설이던 저기 저 왕의 장교라면, 예사로운 대화에서 들리는 그 음색으로 미루어 보건대, 베이스를 채울 만하겠소만.\""]], [["\"Judge not too rashly from hasty and deceptive appearances,\"", "\"성급하고 그릇되기 쉬운 겉모습으로 너무 섣불리 판단하지는 마세요.\""], ["said the lady, smiling; \"though Major Heyward can assume such deep notes on occasion, believe me, his natural tones are better fitted for a mellow tenor than the bass you heard.\"", "여인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"〈헤이워드〉 소령이 때로 그토록 낮은 음을 낼 수는 있어도, 정말이지 그의 본래 음색은 댁이 들으신 그 베이스보다는 부드러운 테너에 더 어울린답니다.\""]], [["\"Is he, then, much practised in the art of psalmody?\"", "\"그렇다면 그분은 시편 영창의 기예에 꽤 단련되었소이까?\""], ["demanded her simple companion.", "그녀의 순박한 동행이 물었다."]], [["Alice felt disposed to laugh, though she succeeded in suppressing her merriment, ere she answered,--", "〈앨리스〉는 웃고 싶은 마음이 들었으나, 대답하기 전에 가까스로 그 웃음을 억눌렀다."]], [["\"I apprehend that he is rather addicted to profane song.", "\"내 짐작에 그분은 차라리 속된 노래 쪽에 더 빠져 있는 편이에요."], ["The chances of a soldier's life are but little fitted for the encouragement of more sober inclinations.\"", "군인의 삶이 겪는 일들이란 더 경건한 취향을 북돋우기엔 그리 알맞지 않으니까요.\""]], [["\"Man's voice is given to him, like his other talents, to be used, and not to be abused.", "\"사람의 목소리는 그 밖의 재능과 마찬가지로, 쓰라고 주어진 것이지 함부로 굴리라고 주어진 것이 아니오."], ["None can say they have ever known me neglect my gifts!", "내가 내 재능을 소홀히 하는 것을 보았다고 말할 수 있는 이는 아무도 없소이다!"], ["I am thankful that, though my boyhood may be said to have been set apart, like the youth of the royal David, for the purposes of music, no syllable of rude verse has ever profaned my lips.\"", "내 비록 소년 시절이 저 어린 다윗 왕처럼 음악의 목적에 바쳐졌다 할 수 있으나, 거친 시구의 한 음절도 내 입술을 더럽힌 적이 없으니, 그 점이 감사할 따름이오.\""]], [["\"You have, then, limited your efforts to sacred song?\"", "\"그렇다면 댁은 거룩한 노래에만 애써 오셨군요?\""]], [["\"Even so.", "\"바로 그렇소."], ["As the psalms of David exceed all other language, so does the psalmody that has been fitted to them by the divines and sages of the land, surpass all vain poetry.", "다윗의 시편이 다른 모든 말을 능가하듯, 이 땅의 신학자와 현인들이 그 시편에 맞춘 영창 또한 온갖 헛된 시가를 능가하지요."], ["Happily, I may say that I utter nothing but the thoughts and the wishes of the King of Israel himself; for though the times may call for some slight changes, yet does this version which we use in the colonies of New England, so much exceed all other versions, that, by its richness, its exactness, and its spiritual simplicity, it approacheth, as near as may be, to the great work of the inspired writer.", "다행히도 나는 〈이스라엘〉 왕 그분 자신의 생각과 소망 말고는 아무것도 읊지 않는다 할 수 있소. 시류가 더러 약간의 변경을 요구할지는 몰라도, 우리가 〈뉴잉글랜드〉 식민지에서 쓰는 이 판본은 다른 모든 판본을 어찌나 능가하는지, 그 풍부함과 정확함과 영적인 단순함으로써, 영감받은 저자의 위대한 작품에 더없이 가까이 다가가 있소이다."], ["I never abide in any place, sleeping or waking, without an example of this gifted work.", "나는 자나 깨나 어디에 머물든, 이 재능 어린 작품 한 부를 곁에 두지 않은 적이 없소."], ["'Tis the six-and-twentieth edition, promulgated at Boston, Anno Domini 1744; and is entitled, _The Psalms, Hymns, and Spiritual Songs of the Old and New Testaments; faithfully translated into English Metre, for the Use, Edification, and Comfort of the Saints, in Public and Private, especially in New England_.\"", "이것이 그 스물여섯 번째 판이오. 주후 1744년 〈보스턴〉에서 공포되었고, 제목은 『구약과 신약의 시편, 찬송, 그리고 영가 — 성도들의 공적·사적 쓰임과 교화와 위안을 위하여, 특히 〈뉴잉글랜드〉에서, 영어 운율로 충실히 옮김』이라 하오.\""]], [["During this eulogium on the rare production of his native poets, the stranger had drawn the book from his pocket, and, fitting a pair of iron-rimmed spectacles to his nose, opened the volume with a care and veneration suited to its sacred purposes.", "제 고향 시인들의 이 진귀한 산물을 이렇게 기리는 동안, 낯선 사내는 주머니에서 책을 꺼내, 쇠테 안경을 코에 걸치고는, 그 거룩한 목적에 걸맞은 조심과 경의로 책장을 펼쳤다."], ["Then, without circumlocution or apology, first pronouncing the word \"Standish,\" and placing the unknown engine, already described, to his mouth, from which he drew a high, shrill sound, that was followed by an octave below, from his own voice, he commenced singing the following words, in full, sweet, and melodious tones, that set the music, the poetry, and even the uneasy motion of his ill-trained beast at defiance:--", "그러고는 에두르거나 양해를 구하지도 않고, 먼저 \"스탠디시\"라는 말을 발음한 뒤, 앞서 말한 그 알 수 없는 도구를 입에 대어 높고 날카로운 소리를 한 차례 끌어냈으며, 거기에 자기 목소리로 한 옥타브 아래 음을 이어 붙이고는, 다음의 가사를 충만하고 감미롭고 가락진 음색으로 부르기 시작했으니, 그 음색은 음악도, 시도, 게다가 변변히 길들지 않은 짐승의 거북한 움직임마저도 능히 무색하게 만들었다."]], [["\"How good it is, O see, And how it pleaseth well, Together, e'en in unity, For brethren so to dwell.", "오, 보라, 이 얼마나 좋은가, 또 이 얼마나 흐뭇한가, 형제가 한데 어우러져, 그토록 한마음으로 사는 것이."], ["It's like the choice ointment, From the head to the beard did go: Down Aaron's beard, that downward went, His garment's skirts unto.\"", "그것은 머리에서 수염으로 흘러내린 귀한 향유와 같으니, 아래로 흐른 아론의 수염을 타고, 그 옷자락에까지 이르렀도다."]], [["The delivery of these skilful rhymes was accompanied, on the part of the stranger, by a regular rise and fall of his right hand, which terminated at the descent, by suffering the fingers to dwell a moment on the leaves of the little volume; and on the ascent, by such a flourish of the member as none but the initiated may ever hope to imitate.", "이 능란한 운문을 토해 내는 동안, 낯선 사내는 오른손을 규칙적으로 올렸다 내렸다 했으니, 내릴 때는 손가락이 그 작은 책의 책장 위에 한순간 머무는 것으로 끝맺었고, 올릴 때는 그 손을 휘저어 보였는데, 그 솜씨란 입문한 자가 아니고서는 결코 흉내 낼 엄두조차 못 낼 것이었다."], ["It would seem that long practice had rendered this manual accompaniment necessary; for it did not cease until the preposition which the poet had selected for the close of his verse, had been duly delivered like a word of two syllables.", "오랜 단련이 이 손짓 반주를 필수로 만든 모양이었다. 시인이 제 시구의 끝맺음으로 고른 그 전치사가 두 음절짜리 낱말처럼 마땅히 다 전달되고 나서야 그 손짓이 멈추었으니까."]], [["Such an innovation on the silence and retirement of the forest could not fail to enlist the ears of those who journeyed at so short a distance in advance.", "숲의 정적과 외짐을 이렇듯 깨뜨리는 일이, 그토록 가까운 거리 앞에서 길을 가는 이들의 귀를 끌지 않을 리 없었다."], ["The Indian muttered a few words in broken English to Heyward, who, in his turn, spoke to the stranger; at once interrupting, and, for the time, closing his musical efforts.", "그 인디언이 서툰 영어로 〈헤이워드〉에게 몇 마디 중얼거렸고, 〈헤이워드〉는 다시 낯선 사내에게 말을 건네어, 그의 음악적 노고를 단번에 끊고 한동안 닫아 버렸다."]], [["\"Though we are not in danger, common prudence would teach us to journey through this wilderness in as quiet a manner as possible.", "\"우리가 위험에 처한 것은 아니나, 평범한 신중함을 따르더라도 이 황야를 지날 때는 되도록 조용히 가야 할 것이오."], ["You will, then, pardon me, Alice, should I diminish your enjoyments, by requesting this gentleman to postpone his chant until a safer opportunity.\"", "그러니 〈앨리스〉, 이분께 더 안전한 기회가 올 때까지 그 영창을 미뤄 달라 청하여 그대의 즐거움을 줄이게 되더라도 용서해 주오.\""]], [["\"You will diminish them, indeed,\"", "\"정말이지 줄이는 셈이 되겠어요.\" 짓궂은 처녀가 받았다."], ["returned the arch girl, \"for never did I hear a more unworthy conjunction of execution and language, than that to which I have been listening; and I was far gone in a learned inquiry into the causes of such an unfitness between sound and sense, when you broke the charm of my musings by that bass of yours, Duncan!\"", "\"내가 지금껏 들어 온 것만큼 솜씨와 가사가 어울리지 않게 짝지어진 것은 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없으니까요. 그래서 나는 소리와 뜻 사이의 그런 부조화가 어디서 비롯되는지 학구적으로 깊이 파고들던 참이었는데, 〈덩컨〉, 당신의 그 베이스가 내 사색의 마법을 깨뜨려 버렸지 뭐예요!\""]], [["\"I know not what you call my bass,\"", "\"그대가 내 베이스라 부르는 게 무엇인지는 모르겠소만,\""], ["said Heyward, piqued at her remark, \"but I know that your safety, and that of Cora, is far dearer to me than could be any orchestra of Handel's music.\"", "그녀의 말에 발끈한 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"그대와 〈코라〉의 안전이, 〈헨델〉의 어떤 관현악단보다도 나에겐 훨씬 더 소중하다는 것만은 아오.\""], ["He paused and turned his head quickly towards a thicket, and then bent his eyes suspiciously on their guide, who continued his steady pace, in undisturbed gravity.", "그는 말을 멈추고 재빨리 어느 덤불 쪽으로 고개를 돌리더니, 이윽고 의심스러운 눈길을 안내인에게 보냈으나, 안내인은 흐트러짐 없는 엄숙함으로 한결같은 걸음을 이어 가고 있었다."], ["The young man smiled to himself, for he believed he had mistaken some shining berry of the woods for the glistening eyeballs of a prowling savage, and he rode forward, continuing the conversation which had been interrupted by the passing thought.", "청년은 속으로 미소 지었으니, 숲의 어떤 빛나는 열매를 어슬렁대는 미개인의 번뜩이는 눈알로 잘못 본 것이라 믿었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그는 앞으로 말을 몰아, 스쳐 간 그 생각으로 끊겼던 대화를 이어 갔다."]], [["Major Heyward was mistaken only in suffering his youthful and generous pride to suppress his active watchfulness.", "〈헤이워드〉 소령의 잘못이라면, 그저 젊고 너그러운 자존심이 그의 민첩한 경계심을 억누르도록 내버려 둔 것뿐이었다."], ["The cavalcade had not long passed, before the branches of the bushes that formed the thicket were cautiously moved asunder, and a human visage, as fiercely wild as savage art and unbridled passions could make it, peered out on the retiring footsteps of the travellers.", "일행이 지나간 지 얼마 되지 않아, 덤불을 이룬 수풀의 가지들이 조심스레 양옆으로 갈라졌고, 미개의 기예와 고삐 풀린 격정이 만들어 낼 수 있는 한 사납고 거칠게 일그러진 사람의 얼굴 하나가, 멀어져 가는 여행자들의 발자취를 내다보았다."], ["A gleam of exultation shot across the darkly painted lineaments of the inhabitant of the forest, as he traced the route of his intended victims, who rode unconsciously onward; the light and graceful forms of the females waving among the trees, in the curvatures of their path, followed at each bend by the manly figure of Heyward, until, finally, the shapeless person of the singing-master was concealed behind the numberless trunks of trees, that rose, in dark lines, in the intermediate space.", "이 숲의 거주자의 검게 칠해진 이목구비 위로 의기양양한 빛이 번득였으니, 그는 아무것도 모른 채 앞으로 말을 몰아 가는 제 표적들의 행로를 좇고 있었다. 여인들의 가볍고 우아한 자태가 굽이진 길을 따라 나무들 사이에서 너울거렸고, 그 굽이마다 〈헤이워드〉의 사내다운 모습이 뒤따랐으며, 마침내 노래 사범의 볼품없는 몸뚱이가, 그 사이 공간에 어두운 줄을 이루며 솟은 무수한 나무줄기 뒤로 가려졌다."]], [["\"Before these fields were shorn and tilled, Full to the brim our rivers flowed; The melody of waters filled The fresh and boundless wood; And torrents dashed, and rivulets played, And fountains spouted in the shade.\"", "이 들판이 베이고 갈리기 전에는, 우리네 강물이 가장자리까지 가득 흘렀고, 물의 가락이 싱그럽고 끝없는 숲을 가득 채웠으며, 급류가 부서지고 시냇물이 노닐었고, 샘물이 그늘 속에서 솟구쳤도다. 브라이언트"]], [["Leaving the unsuspecting Heyward and his confiding companions to penetrate still deeper into a forest that contained such treacherous inmates, we must use an author's privilege, and shift the scene a few miles to the westward of the place where we have last seen them.", "아무것도 의심하지 않는 〈헤이워드〉와 그를 굳게 믿는 일행이, 그토록 음흉한 거주자를 품은 숲속으로 더욱 깊이 파고들도록 두고, 우리는 작가의 특권을 빌려, 마지막으로 그들을 보았던 곳에서 서쪽으로 몇 마일 떨어진 데로 장면을 옮겨야겠다."]], [["On that day, two men were lingering on the banks of a small but rapid stream, within an hour's journey of the encampment of Webb, like those who awaited the appearance of an absent person, or the approach of some expected event.", "그날, 두 사내가 작지만 물살 빠른 시냇가에서, 〈웨브〉의 진지에서 한 시간 거리쯤 되는 곳에서 서성이고 있었으니, 마치 자리를 비운 사람이 나타나기를, 혹은 어떤 예정된 일이 다가오기를 기다리는 자들 같았다."], ["The vast canopy of woods spread itself to the margin of the river overhanging the water, and shadowing its dark current with a deeper hue.", "광막한 숲의 천막이 강기슭까지 펼쳐져 물 위로 드리워졌고, 그 검은 물줄기를 한층 짙은 빛으로 그늘 지웠다."], ["The rays of the sun were beginning to grow less fierce, and the intense heat of the day was lessened, as the cooler vapors of the springs and fountains rose above their leafy beds, and rested in the atmosphere.", "햇살은 그 사나움을 누그러뜨리기 시작했고, 샘과 옹달샘에서 더 서늘한 김이 그 잎새 이불 위로 피어올라 대기 속에 머무르면서, 한낮의 강렬한 더위가 한풀 꺾였다."], ["Still that breathing silence, which marks the drowsy sultriness of an American landscape in July, pervaded the secluded spot, interrupted only by the low voices of the men, the occasional and lazy tap of a woodpecker, the discordant cry of some gaudy jay, or a swelling on the ear, from the dull roar of a distant waterfall.", "그래도, 칠월 아메리카 풍경의 나른한 무더위를 일러 주는 그 숨 막힐 듯한 정적이 이 외딴 자리에 두루 깔려, 다만 사내들의 낮은 목소리와, 이따금 게으르게 두드리는 딱따구리의 소리, 어느 화려한 어치의 거슬리는 울음, 혹은 멀리 폭포의 둔탁한 굉음이 귓전에 부풀어 오는 소리에만 깨어질 뿐이었다."]], [["These feeble and broken sounds were, however, too familiar to the foresters, to draw their attention from the more interesting matter of their dialogue.", "그러나 이 희미하고 끊어진 소리들은 숲의 사람들에게 너무도 익숙하여, 그들의 한층 흥미로운 대화에서 주의를 끌어내지는 못했다."], ["While one of these loiterers showed the red skin and wild accoutrements of a native of the woods, the other exhibited, through the mask of his rude and nearly savage equipments, the brighter, though sunburnt and long-faded complexion of one who might claim descent from a European parentage.", "이 서성이는 두 사람 가운데 하나는 숲에서 나고 자란 토박이의 붉은 살갗과 거친 차림새를 드러냈고, 다른 하나는 그 투박하고 거의 야만에 가까운 차림새의 가면 너머로, 햇볕에 그을리고 오랜 세월 빛바랜 것이긴 해도 한층 밝은 안색을 보여 주었으니, 유럽 혈통의 후손임을 내세울 만한 자였다."], ["The former was seated on the end of a mossy log, in a posture that permitted him to heighten the effect of his earnest language, by the calm but expressive gestures of an Indian engaged in debate.", "앞의 사내는 이끼 낀 통나무 끝에 걸터앉아, 논쟁에 임한 인디언의 차분하면서도 뜻을 담은 몸짓으로 제 진지한 말의 효과를 한층 돋울 수 있는 자세를 취하고 있었다."], ["His body, which was nearly naked, presented a terrific emblem of death, drawn in intermingled colors of white and black.", "거의 헐벗은 그 몸은 흰빛과 검은빛이 뒤섞인 색으로 그려진 죽음의 끔찍한 상징을 드러냈다."], ["His closely shaved head, on which no other hair than the well known and chivalrous scalping tuft[5] was preserved, was without ornament of any kind, with the exception of a solitary eagle's plume, that crossed his crown, and depended over the left shoulder.", "바싹 깎은 머리에는, 익히 알려진 호기로운 머리 가죽 상투 외에 다른 머리카락이라곤 남아 있지 않았으며, 정수리를 가로질러 왼쪽 어깨 위로 늘어진 한 가닥 외로운 독수리 깃털 말고는 어떤 장식도 없었다."], ["A tomahawk and scalping-knife, of English manufacture, were in his girdle; while a short military rifle, of that sort with which the policy of the whites armed their savage allies, lay carelessly across his bare and sinewy knee.", "〈영국〉제 토마호크와 머리 가죽 벗기는 칼이 허리띠에 꽂혀 있었고, 백인들이 정략으로 그 야만의 동맹군을 무장시킨 그런 종류의 짧은 군용 소총 한 자루가, 그 헐벗고 힘줄 돋은 무릎 위에 무심히 가로놓여 있었다."], ["The expanded chest, full formed limbs, and grave countenance of this warrior, would denote that he had reached the vigor of his days, though no symptoms of decay appeared to have yet weakened his manhood.", "떡 벌어진 가슴, 충실히 여문 사지, 그리고 이 전사의 근엄한 표정은 그가 한창때의 기운에 다다랐음을 일러 주었으나, 아직 그 사내다움을 약하게 만든 쇠락의 기미는 보이지 않았다."]], [["The frame of the white man, judging by such parts as were not concealed by his clothes, was like that of one who had known hardships and exertion from his earliest youth.", "옷에 가려지지 않은 부분으로 미루어, 백인의 골격은 가장 어린 시절부터 고난과 노역을 겪어 온 자의 그것 같았다."], ["His person, though muscular, was rather attenuated than full; but every nerve and muscle appeared strung and indurated by unremitted exposure and toil.", "그 몸은 근육질이긴 했어도 충실하기보다는 차라리 여위었으나, 모든 신경과 근육이 쉴 새 없는 풍찬노숙과 노고로 팽팽히 당겨지고 단단히 굳어진 듯했다."], ["He wore a hunting-shirt of forest green, fringed with faded yellow[6], and a summer cap of skins which had been shorn of their fur.", "그는 숲의 초록빛 사냥 셔츠를 입었는데, 빛바랜 노란색으로 술을 달았으며, 털을 깎아 낸 가죽으로 만든 여름 모자를 썼다."], ["He also bore a knife in a girdle of wampum, like that which confined the scanty garments of the Indian, but no tomahawk.", "그도 인디언의 단출한 옷을 여미던 것과 같은 조가비 구슬 허리띠에 칼을 차고 있었으나, 토마호크는 없었다."], ["His moccasins were ornamented after the gay fashion of the natives, while the only part of his under-dress which appeared below the hunting-frock, was a pair of buckskin leggings, that laced at the sides, and which were gartered above the knees with the sinews of a deer.", "그의 모카신은 토박이들의 화려한 양식을 따라 장식되어 있었고, 사냥 셔츠 아래로 드러난 속옷의 유일한 부분은 사슴 가죽 각반 한 쌍이었으니, 옆구리에서 끈으로 매고 무릎 위에서 사슴 힘줄로 동인 것이었다."], ["A pouch and horn completed his personal accoutrements, though a rifle of great length[7], which the theory of the more ingenious whites had taught them was the most dangerous of all fire-arms, leaned against a neighboring sapling.", "주머니 하나와 화약통 하나가 그의 차림새를 마무리했으며, 다만 더 머리 좋은 백인들의 이론이 모든 화기 가운데 가장 위험한 것이라 가르친 매우 긴 소총 한 자루는 곁의 어린 나무에 기대어 있었다."], ["The eye of the hunter, or scout, whichever he might be, was small, quick, keen, and restless, roving while he spoke, on every side of him, as if in quest of game, or distrusting the sudden approach of some lurking enemy.", "사냥꾼인지 척후병인지 알 수 없는 이 사내의 눈은 작고 재빠르며 날카롭고 잠시도 가만있지 않아, 그가 말하는 동안에도 사방을 두루 훑었으니, 마치 사냥감을 찾는 듯, 혹은 숨어 있는 적이 느닷없이 다가올까 의심하는 듯했다."], ["Notwithstanding the symptoms of habitual suspicion, his countenance was not only without guile, but at the moment at which he is introduced, it was charged with an expression of sturdy honesty.", "몸에 밴 경계의 기색에도, 그의 표정에는 거짓이 없었을 뿐 아니라, 그가 처음 등장하는 이 순간에는 한결같은 정직함의 기색마저 가득했다."]], [["\"Even your traditions make the case in my favor, Chingachgook,\"", "\"자네네 전설을 봐도 이 일은 내 편을 들어주는걸세, 〈칭가치국〉.\""], ["he said, speaking in the tongue which was known to all the natives who formerly inhabited the country between the Hudson and the Potomac, and of which we shall give a free translation for the benefit of the reader; endeavoring, at the same time, to preserve some of the peculiarities, both of the individual and of the language.", "그가 말했으니, 일찍이 〈허드슨〉강과 〈포토맥〉강 사이의 땅에 살던 모든 토박이가 알아듣던 말로 이야기한 것이라, 우리는 독자의 편의를 위해 이를 자유로이 옮기되, 동시에 그 개인의, 또 그 언어의 몇몇 특색을 살려 보고자 한다. \"자네 조상은 지는 해 쪽에서 와서, 큰 강을 건너고, 그 땅의 사람들과 싸워 땅을 차지했지."], ["\"Your fathers came from the setting sun, crossed the big river,[8] fought the people of the country, and took the land; and mine came from the red sky of the morning, over the salt lake, and did their work much after the fashion that had been set them by yours; then let God judge the matter between us, and friends spare their words!\"", "또 내 조상은 아침의 붉은 하늘 쪽에서, 짠 호수를 건너와, 자네 조상이 남긴 본보기를 거의 그대로 따라 제 할 일을 했고. 그러니 우리 사이의 일은 하느님께서 가리시게 두고, 친구끼리는 말을 아끼세!\""]], [["\"My fathers fought with the naked redmen!\"", "\"내 조상은 헐벗은 붉은 사람들과 싸웠다!\""], ["returned the Indian sternly, in the same language.", "인디언이 같은 말로 준엄하게 받았다."], ["\"Is there no difference, Hawkeye, between the stone-headed arrow of the warrior, and the leaden bullet with which you kill?\"", "\"전사의 돌촉 화살과, 자네가 사람을 죽이는 데 쓰는 납 탄알 사이에 차이가 없단 말인가, 〈호크아이〉?\""]], [["\"There is reason in an Indian, though nature has made him with a red skin!\"", "\"붉은 살갗을 타고났어도 인디언에겐 이치가 있단 말이야!\""], ["said the white man, shaking his head like one on whom such an appeal to his justice was not thrown away.", "백인이 말했으니, 그렇게 정의에 호소하는 말이 헛되이 흘려보내지지 않은 자처럼 고개를 가로저었다. 잠시 그는 논쟁에서 밀린다는 것을 의식한 듯하더니, 이내 다시 기운을 차려, 제 한정된 식견이 허락하는 한 최선을 다해 상대의 반박에 답했다."], ["For a moment he appeared to be conscious of having the worst of the argument, then, rallying again, he answered the objection of his antagonist in the best manner his limited information would allow: \"I am no scholar, and I care not who knows it; but judging from what I have seen, at deer chases and squirrel hunts, of the sparks below, I should think a rifle in the hands of their grandfathers was not so dangerous as a hickory bow and a good flint-head might be, if drawn with Indian judgment, and sent by an Indian eye.\"", "\"나야 학자도 아니고, 그걸 누가 알든 상관없네. 허나 사슴 사냥이며 다람쥐 사냥에서 저 아래 불꽃들을 두고 내가 본 바로 미루어 보건대, 그 조상들 손에 들린 소총은, 인디언의 분별로 당기고 인디언의 눈으로 쏜 히커리 활과 좋은 부싯돌 촉만큼 위험하진 않았을 거라 생각하네.\""]], [["\"You have the story told by your fathers,\"", "\"자넨 자네 조상이 전한 이야기를 가졌지.\""], ["returned the other, coldly waving his hand.", "상대가 차갑게 손을 내저으며 받았다."], ["\"What say your old men?", "\"자네네 노인들은 뭐라 하던가?"], ["do they tell the young warriors, that the pale-faces met the redmen, painted for war and armed with the stone hatchet and wooden gun?\"", "창백한 얼굴들이, 전쟁 채비로 칠을 하고 돌도끼와 나무총으로 무장한 붉은 사람들을 만났다고, 젊은 전사들에게 일러 주던가?\""]], [["\"I am not a prejudiced man, nor one who vaunts himself on his natural privileges, though the worst enemy I have on earth, and he is an Iroquois, daren't deny that I am genuine white,\"", "\"나는 편견에 사로잡힌 사람도, 타고난 특권을 떠벌리는 사람도 아닐세. 허나 이 세상에서 내 가장 고약한 원수가 — 그놈은 〈이로쿼이〉인데 — 내가 순수한 백인이라는 걸 감히 부정하진 못하지.\""], ["the scout replied, surveying, with secret satisfaction, the faded color of his bony and sinewy hand; \"and I am willing to own that my people have many ways, of which, as an honest man, I can't approve.", "척후병이, 뼈 굵고 힘줄 돋은 제 손의 빛바랜 살갗을 은근한 만족 속에 살피며 답했다. \"또 나는 내 동족에게, 정직한 사람으로서 도무지 받아들일 수 없는 구석이 많다는 걸 기꺼이 인정하네."], ["It is one of their customs to write in books what they have done and seen, instead of telling them in their villages, where the lie can be given to the face of a cowardly boaster, and the brave soldier can call on his comrades to witness for the truth of his words.", "마을에서 이야기로 전하는 대신, 제가 한 일과 본 일을 책에다 적어 두는 게 그들의 풍습 가운데 하나지. 마을에서 이야기로 전한다면야, 비겁한 허풍쟁이의 면전에 거짓말이라 들이댈 수도 있고, 용감한 병사가 제 말이 참됨을 증언해 달라 전우들을 부를 수도 있건만."], ["In consequence of this bad fashion, a man who is too conscientious to misspend his days among the women, in learning the names of black marks, may never hear of the deeds of his fathers, nor feel a pride in striving to outdo them.", "이 못된 풍습 탓에, 검은 부호의 이름이나 익히며 아낙들 틈에서 세월을 허비하기엔 너무 양심 바른 사람은, 제 조상의 공적을 영영 듣지도 못하고, 그들을 능가하려 애쓰는 자부심도 느끼지 못할 수가 있네. 나로 말하자면, 〈범포〉 집안이 사격엔 능했으리라 결론짓는데, 내게 소총을 다루는 타고난 재간이 있는 까닭이라, 우리 거룩한 계명이 일러 주듯 온갖 선하고 악한 재능이 그러하듯, 그 재간도 대를 이어 전해 내려온 게 틀림없거든."], ["For myself, I conclude the Bumppos could shoot, for I have a natural turn with a rifle, which must have been handed down from generation to generation, as, our holy commandments tell us, all good and evil gifts are bestowed; though I should be loth to answer for other people in such a matter.", "허나 이런 일에서 남을 두고 장담하긴 꺼려지는군. 하지만 어떤 이야기에든 양면이 있는 법."], ["But every story has its two sides; so I ask you, Chingachgook, what passed, according to the traditions of the redmen, when our fathers first met?\"", "그러니 자네에게 묻겠네, 〈칭가치국〉. 붉은 사람들의 전설에 따르면, 우리 조상들이 처음 만났을 때 무슨 일이 있었는가?\""]], [["A silence of a minute succeeded, during which the Indian sat mute; then, full of the dignity of his office, he commenced his brief tale, with a solemnity that served to heighten its appearance of truth.", "잠시 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그동안 인디언은 말없이 앉아 있었다. 이윽고 제 소임의 위엄을 가득 담아, 그는 짤막한 이야기를 시작했으니, 그 엄숙함이 이야기의 참된 기색을 한층 돋우는 데 보탬이 되었다."]], [["\"Listen, Hawkeye, and your ear shall drink no lie.", "\"들어 보게, 〈호크아이〉. 자네 귀가 거짓을 들이켜는 일은 없을 걸세."], ["'Tis what my fathers have said, and what the Mohicans have done.\"", "이건 내 조상들이 말한 바요, 〈모히칸〉족이 행한 바라네.\""], ["He hesitated a single instant, and bending a cautious glance toward his companion, he continued, in a manner that was divided between interrogation and assertion, \"Does not this stream at our feet run towards the summer, until its waters grow salt, and the current flows upward?\"", "그는 한순간 머뭇거리더니, 동행에게 조심스러운 눈길을 던지며, 묻는 것과 단언하는 것 사이를 오가는 투로 말을 이었다. \"우리 발치의 이 시내는 여름 쪽으로 흐르지 않는가, 그 물이 짜질 때까지, 그러다 물줄기가 거꾸로 흐를 때까지 말일세?\""]], [["\"It can't be denied that your traditions tell you true in both these matters,\"", "\"그 두 가지 다 자네 전설이 참된 걸 일러 준다는 건 부정할 수 없지.\" 백인이 말했다."], ["said the white man; \"for I have been there, and have seen them; though, why water, which is so sweet in the shade, should become bitter in the sun, is an alteration for which I have never been able to account.\"", "\"나도 거기 가 봤고, 두 눈으로 봤으니까. 허나 그늘에선 그토록 단 물이 어찌하여 햇볕 아래선 쓰게 변하는지, 그건 내가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 변화일세.\""]], [["\"And the current!\"", "\"그런데 그 물줄기는!\""], ["demanded the Indian, who expected his reply with that sort of interest that a man feels in the confirmation of testimony, at which he marvels even while he respects it; \"the fathers of Chingachgook have not lied!\"", "인디언이 캐물었으니, 경탄하면서도 경의를 표하는 증언을 확인할 때 느끼는 그런 흥미로 그의 답을 기다렸다. \"〈칭가치국〉의 조상들이 거짓을 말하진 않았어!\""]], [["\"The Holy Bible is not more true, and that is the truest thing in nature.", "\"거룩한 성경도 그보다 더 참되진 않네, 그건 자연에서 가장 참된 것이지."], ["They call this up-stream current the tide, which is a thing soon explained, and clear enough.", "그 거슬러 오르는 물줄기를 조수라 부르는데, 그건 금세 설명되는, 충분히 분명한 것일세. 여섯 시간은 물이 들어오고, 여섯 시간은 물이 나가지."], ["Six hours the waters run in, and six hours they run out, and the reason is this: when there is higher water in the sea than in the river, they run in, until the river gets to be highest, and then it runs out again.\"", "그 까닭은 이렇다네. 바다가 강보다 물이 높으면, 강이 가장 높아질 때까지 물이 들어오고, 그러다 다시 나가는 게야.\""]], [["\"The waters in the woods, and on the great lakes, run downward until they lie like my hand,\"", "\"숲과 큰 호수들의 물은, 내 손처럼 평평히 누울 때까지 아래로 흐르지.\""], ["said the Indian, stretching the limb horizontally before him, \"and then they run no more.\"", "인디언이 팔을 제 앞으로 수평으로 뻗으며 말했다. \"그러고는 더 흐르지 않아.\""]], [["\"No honest man will deny it,\"", "\"정직한 사람이라면 그건 부정 못 하지.\""], ["said the scout, a little nettled at the implied distrust of his explanation of the mystery of the tides; \"and I grant that it is true on the small scale, and where the land is level.", "척후병이, 조수의 신비를 풀어낸 제 설명에 은근히 깃든 불신에 다소 부아가 나 말했다. \"작은 규모에서, 땅이 평평한 곳에선 그게 참이란 걸 나도 인정하네."], ["But everything depends on what scale you look at things.", "허나 모든 건 무슨 규모로 보느냐에 달려 있어."], ["Now, on the small scale, the 'arth is level; but on the large scale it is round.", "자, 작은 규모에선 땅이 평평하지. 허나 큰 규모에선 둥글거든."], ["In this manner, pools and ponds, and even the great fresh-water lake, may be stagnant, as you and I both know they are, having seen them; but when you come to spread water over a great tract, like the sea, where the earth is round, how in reason can the water be quiet?", "이런 까닭에, 웅덩이며 못이며, 심지어 큰 민물 호수까지도 고여 있을 수 있는 게야, 자네나 나나 둘 다 보아서 알듯이 말일세. 허나 바다처럼 드넓은 땅에 물을 펼쳐 놓을 때, 거기선 땅이 둥근데, 어찌 이치상 물이 잠잠할 수 있겠나?"], ["You might as well expect the river to lie still on the brink of those black rocks a mile above us, though your own ears tell you that it is tumbling over them at this very moment!\"", "우리 위로 일 마일쯤 떨어진 저 검은 바위 언저리에서 강이 가만히 누워 있길 바라는 거나 마찬가지지, 바로 이 순간에도 그 위로 강물이 굴러떨어지고 있다고 자네 귀가 일러 주는데도 말일세!\""]], [["If unsatisfied by the philosophy of his companion, the Indian was far too dignified to betray his unbelief.", "설령 동행의 이치에 흡족하지 못했더라도, 인디언은 너무도 위엄 있는지라 제 불신을 드러내지 않았다."], ["He listened like one who was convinced, and resumed his narrative in his former solemn manner.", "그는 납득한 자처럼 귀 기울이고는, 앞서의 엄숙한 투로 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["\"We came from the place where the sun is hid at night, over great plains where the buffaloes live, until we reached the big river.", "\"우리는 밤이면 해가 숨는 곳에서 와서, 들소가 사는 큰 평원을 지나, 큰 강에 다다랐다."], ["There we fought the Alligewi, till the ground was red with their blood.", "거기서 우리는 〈앨리거위〉족과 싸웠으니, 땅이 그들의 피로 붉어질 때까지였지."], ["From the banks of the big river to the shores of the salt lake, there was none to meet us.", "큰 강 기슭에서 짠 호수의 물가에 이르기까지, 우리를 맞설 자는 아무도 없었다."], ["The Maquas followed at a distance.", "〈머콰〉족은 멀찍이서 뒤따랐고."], ["We said the country should be ours from the place where the water runs up no longer on this stream, to a river twenty suns' journey toward the summer.", "우리는 이 시냇물에서 물이 더는 거슬러 오르지 않는 곳에서부터, 여름 쪽으로 스무 날 거리에 있는 강까지 그 땅이 우리 것이라 선언했다."], ["The land we had taken like warriors, we kept like men.", "전사답게 차지한 땅을, 우리는 사내답게 지켰지."], ["We drove the Maquas into the woods with the bears.", "우리는 〈머콰〉족을 곰들과 함께 숲속으로 몰아냈다."], ["They only tasted salt at the licks; they drew no fish from the great lake; we threw them the bones.\"", "그들은 소금밭에서나 소금 맛을 보았고, 큰 호수에서 물고기 한 마리 건지지 못했으며, 우리는 그들에게 뼈다귀나 던져 주었지.\""]], [["\"All this I have heard and believe,\"", "\"이 모든 걸 나도 들었고, 또 믿네.\""], ["said the white man, observing that the Indian paused: \"but it was long before the English came into the country.\"", "인디언이 말을 멈추는 것을 보고 백인이 말했다. \"허나 그건 〈영국〉인이 이 땅에 들어오기 한참 전의 일이지.\""]], [["\"A pine grew then where this chestnut now stands.", "\"지금 이 밤나무가 선 자리에 그때는 소나무가 자랐지."], ["The first pale-faces who came among us spoke no English.", "우리 가운데 처음 온 창백한 얼굴들은 〈영국〉말을 쓰지 않았어."], ["They came in a large canoe, when my fathers had buried the tomahawk with the redmen around them.", "그들은 큰 카누를 타고 왔는데, 그때는 내 조상들이 둘레의 붉은 사람들과 토마호크를 땅에 묻은 뒤였지."], ["Then, Hawkeye,\"", "그때, 〈호크아이〉.\""], ["he continued, betraying his deep emotion only by permitting his voice to fall to those low, guttural tones, which rendered his language, as spoken at times, so very musical; \"then, Hawkeye, we were one people, and we were happy.", "그가 말을 이었으니, 그 깊은 감회를 다만 목소리를 저 낮고 그르렁대는 가락으로 떨어뜨리는 것으로만 드러내는 터라, 이따금 그렇게 발음할 때면 그의 언어가 그토록 음악적으로 들렸다. \"그때, 〈호크아이〉, 우리는 한 백성이었고, 우리는 행복했다."], ["The salt lake gave us its fish, the wood its deer, and the air its birds.", "짠 호수는 우리에게 물고기를 주었고, 숲은 사슴을, 하늘은 새를 주었지."], ["We took wives who bore us children; we worshipped the Great Spirit; and we kept the Maquas beyond the sound of our songs of triumph!\"", "우리는 아내를 맞아 자식을 낳았고, 위대한 정령을 섬겼으며, 〈머콰〉족을 우리 승리의 노랫소리가 닿지 않는 데까지 몰아냈다!\""]], [["\"Know you anything of your own family at that time?\"", "\"그 시절 자네 일가에 대해 아는 게 있나?\""], ["demanded the white.", "백인이 캐물었다."], ["\"But you are a just man, for an Indian!", "\"허나 자넨 인디언치고는 공정한 사람이야!"], ["and, as I suppose you hold their gifts, your fathers must have been brave warriors, and wise men at the council fire.\"", "또 자네가 그들의 자질을 물려받았다 치면, 자네 조상은 용감한 전사들이요, 평의회 불가에선 지혜로운 사람들이었겠군.\""]], [["\"My tribe is the grandfather of nations, but I am an unmixed man.", "\"내 부족은 여러 민족의 할아버지지만, 나는 피가 섞이지 않은 사람일세."], ["The blood of chiefs is in my veins, where it must stay forever.", "추장의 피가 내 핏줄에 흐르고, 그건 영영 거기 머물러야 하지."], ["The Dutch landed, and gave my people the fire-water; they drank until the heavens and the earth seemed to meet, and they foolishly thought they had found the Great Spirit.", "〈네덜란드〉인이 상륙해 우리 백성에게 불의 물을 주었어. 그들은 하늘과 땅이 맞닿는 듯 보일 때까지 마셔 댔고, 어리석게도 위대한 정령을 찾았다 여겼지."], ["Then they parted with their land.", "그러고는 제 땅을 내주었어."], ["Foot by foot, they were driven back from the shores, until I, that am a chief and a sagamore, have never seen the sun shine but through the trees, and have never visited the graves of, my fathers!\"", "한 발짝 한 발짝, 그들은 물가에서 밀려났으니, 추장이요 〈사가모어〉인 이 몸이, 나무 사이로 비치는 것 말고는 햇빛을 본 적이 없고, 내 조상들의 무덤을 한 번도 찾지 못할 지경에 이른 게야!\""]], [["\"Graves bring solemn feelings over the mind,\"", "\"무덤은 마음에 엄숙한 정을 불러오지.\""], ["returned the scout, a good deal touched at the calm suffering of his companion; \"and they often aid a man in his good intentions; though, for myself, I expect to leave my own bones unburied, to bleach in the woods, or to be torn asunder by the wolves.", "동행의 차분한 고통에 적잖이 마음이 움직인 척후병이 받았다. \"또 그건 흔히 사람이 좋은 뜻을 품도록 돕는 법이고."], ["But where are to be found those of your race who came to their kin in the Delaware country, so many summers since?\"", "허나 나로 말하면, 내 뼈는 묻히지 못한 채 숲에서 바래거나, 늑대들에게 찢기리라 여기네. 그건 그렇고, 그 옛날, 그토록 많은 여름 전에 〈델라웨어〉 땅에서 제 일족을 찾아왔다는 자네 종족 사람들은 지금 어디 있나?\""]], [["\"Where are the blossoms of those summers!--fallen, one by one: so all of my family departed, each in his turn, to the land of spirits. I am on the hill-top, and must go down into the valley; and when Uncas follows in my footsteps, there will no longer be any of the blood of the sagamores, for my boy is the last of the Mohicans.\"", "\"그 여름들의 꽃은 어디 있는가! 하나하나 떨어져 버렸지. 내 일가도 다 그렇게, 저마다 차례로 정령들의 땅으로 떠났다네. 나는 언덕 꼭대기에 있고, 이제 골짜기로 내려가야 하지. 〈언커스〉가 내 발자취를 따라온 다음엔, 〈사가모어〉의 피를 이은 자는 더 이상 없을 게야, 내 아들이 〈모히칸〉족의 최후이니.\""]], [["\"Uncas is here!\"", "\"〈언커스〉가 여기 있습니다!\""], ["said another voice, in the same soft, guttural tones, near his elbow; \"who speaks to Uncas?\"", "또 다른 목소리가, 그 곁에서 똑같이 부드럽고 그르렁대는 가락으로 말했다. \"누가 〈언커스〉를 부르십니까?\""]], [["The white man loosened his knife in his leathern sheath, and made an involuntary movement of the hand towards his rifle, at this sudden interruption; but the Indian sat composed, and without turning his head at the unexpected sounds.", "이 느닷없는 끼어듦에, 백인은 가죽집의 칼을 느슨히 풀고 저도 모르게 손을 소총 쪽으로 가져갔다. 그러나 인디언은 침착히 앉은 채, 뜻밖의 소리에도 고개조차 돌리지 않았다."]], [["At the next instant, a youthful warrior passed between them, with a noiseless step, and seated himself on the bank of the rapid stream.", "다음 순간, 한 젊은 전사가 소리 없는 발걸음으로 둘 사이를 지나, 물살 빠른 시냇가에 자리를 잡았다."], ["No exclamation of surprise escaped the father, nor was any question asked, or reply given, for several minutes; each appearing to await the moment when he might speak, without betraying womanish curiosity or childish impatience.", "아버지에게선 놀라움의 탄성 하나 새어 나오지 않았고, 몇 분 동안 묻는 말도, 답하는 말도 없었으니, 저마다 아낙네 같은 호기심이나 아이 같은 조급함을 드러내지 않고 말할 수 있는 때를 기다리는 듯했다."], ["The white man seemed to take counsel from their customs, and, relinquishing his grasp of the rifle, he also remained silent and reserved.", "백인은 그들의 풍습에서 본을 받은 듯, 소총 쥐었던 손을 놓고 그 역시 말없이 삼간 채 있었다."], ["At length Chingachgook turned his eyes slowly towards his son, and demanded,--", "마침내 〈칭가치국〉이 천천히 아들에게로 눈길을 돌리며 물었다."]], [["\"Do the Maquas dare to leave the print of their moccasins in these woods?\"", "\"〈머콰〉족이 감히 이 숲에 제 모카신 자국을 남긴단 말이냐?\""]], [["\"I have been on their trail,\"", "\"그들의 자취를 좇아 봤습니다.\" 젊은 인디언이 답했다."], ["replied the young Indian, \"and know that they number as many as the fingers of my two hands; but they lie hid, like cowards.\"", "\"그 수가 제 두 손 손가락만큼 된다는 걸 알았지요. 허나 비겁한 자들답게 숨어 있습니다.\""]], [["\"The thieves are outlying for scalps and plunder!\"", "\"그 도둑놈들이 머리 가죽과 노략질을 노리고 잠복해 있는 게로군!\""], ["said the white man, whom we shall call Hawkeye, after the manner of his companions.", "백인이 말했으니, 우리는 그 동행들이 부르던 대로 그를 〈호크아이〉라 부르기로 한다."], ["\"That bushy Frenchman, Montcalm, will send his spies into our very camp, but he will know what road we travel!\"", "\"그 텁수룩한 프랑스 놈, 〈몽칼름〉이 제 첩자들을 우리 진지 한복판에까지 들여보낼 테지. 허나 우리가 어느 길로 가는지는 알게 될 게야!\""]], [["\"Tis enough!\"", "\"그만하면 됐다!\""], ["returned the father, glancing his eye towards the setting sun; \"they shall be driven like deer from their bushes.", "아버지가 지는 해 쪽으로 눈길을 던지며 받았다. \"그들을 덤불에서 사슴처럼 몰아내리라."], ["Hawkeye, let us eat to-night, and show the Maquas that we are men to-morrow.\"", "〈호크아이〉, 오늘 밤은 먹고, 내일은 〈머콰〉족에게 우리가 사내임을 보여 주세.\""]], [["\"I am as ready to do the one as the other; but to fight the Iroquois 'tis necessary to find the skulkers; and to eat, 'tis necessary to get the game--talk of the devil and he will come; there is a pair of the biggest antlers I have seen this season, moving the bushes below the hill! Now, Uncas,\"", "\"한쪽이든 다른 쪽이든 나야 기꺼이 할 준비가 됐네."], ["he continued in a half whisper, and laughing with a kind of inward sound, like one who had learnt to be watchful, \"I will bet my charger three times full of powder, against a foot of wampum, that I take him atwixt the eyes, and nearer to the right than to the left.\"", "허나 〈이로쿼이〉와 싸우려면 그 숨은 놈들을 찾아내야 하고, 먹으려면 사냥감을 잡아야지. 호랑이도 제 말 하면 온다더니, 이 철에 내가 본 것 중 가장 큰 뿔 한 쌍이 언덕 아래 덤불을 흔들고 있군!"], ["\"It cannot be!\"", "자, 〈언커스〉.\""], ["said the young Indian, springing to his feet with youthful eagerness; \"all but the tips of his horns are hid!\"", "그가 반쯤 속삭이며, 경계하는 법을 익힌 자처럼 안으로 삼키는 듯한 소리로 웃으며 말을 이었다. \"내 화약통을 세 번 가득 채운 화약을, 한 자 길이의 조가비 구슬과 걸고 내기하지, 내가 저놈을 두 눈 사이에, 왼쪽보다 오른쪽에 더 가깝게 맞히겠다고 말일세.\""]], [["\"He's a boy!\"", "\"애송이로군!\""], ["said the white man, shaking his head while he spoke, and addressing the father.", "백인이 고개를 저으며 말했다. 그러면서 아버지에게 말을 건넸다."], ["\"Does he think when a hunter sees a part of the creatur', he can't tell where the rest of him should be!\"", "\"사냥꾼이 짐승의 일부만 봐도 나머지가 어디 있는지 알 수 없다고 생각하는 모양이지!\""]], [["[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n_Avoiding the horns of the infuriated animal, Uncas darted to his side, and passed his knife across the throat_]", "\"애로구먼!\" 백인이 그렇게 말하며 고개를 가로젓고는, 아버지를 향해 말했다. \"사냥꾼이 짐승의 한 부분만 봐도 나머지가 어디 있을지 모를 줄 아는 게야!\""]], [["Adjusting his rifle, he was about to make an exhibition of that skill, on which he so much valued himself, when the warrior struck up the piece with his hand, saying--", "소총을 매만지며, 그가 그토록 스스로 자랑하는 그 솜씨를 한번 보여 주려던 참에, 전사가 손으로 총을 쳐올리며 말했다."]], [["\"Hawkeye!", "\"〈호크아이〉!"], ["will you fight the Maquas?\"", "〈머콰〉족과 싸우겠나?\""]], [["\"These Indians know the nature of the woods, as it might be by instinct!\"", "\"이 인디언들은 마치 본능으로 그러하듯 숲의 본성을 안단 말이야!\""], ["returned the scout, dropping his rifle, and turning away like a man who was convinced of his error.", "척후병이 소총을 내리고, 제 잘못을 깨달은 자처럼 돌아서며 받았다. \"저 수사슴은 자네 화살에 맡겨야겠군, 〈언커스〉."], ["\"I must leave the buck to your arrow, Uncas, or we may kill a deer for them thieves, the Iroquois, to eat.\"", "안 그러면 우리가 저 도둑놈들, 〈이로쿼이〉가 먹으라고 사슴을 잡아 주는 꼴이 될지도 모르니.\""]], [["The instant the father seconded this intimation by an expressive gesture of the hand, Uncas threw himself on the ground, and approached the animal with wary movements.", "아버지가 뜻을 담은 손짓으로 이 말에 맞장구를 치자, 〈언커스〉는 곧장 땅에 몸을 던지고 조심스러운 동작으로 짐승에게 다가갔다."], ["When within a few yards of the cover, he fitted an arrow to his bow with the utmost care, while the antlers moved, as if their owner snuffed an enemy in the tainted air.", "은신처에서 몇 야드 안에 이르렀을 때, 그는 더없이 신중하게 활시위에 화살을 메겼으니, 그동안 뿔은, 그 임자가 더럽혀진 공기에서 적의 냄새라도 맡은 듯 움직였다."], ["In another moment the twang of the cord was heard, a white streak was seen glancing into the bushes, and the wounded buck plunged from the cover, to the very feet of his hidden enemy.", "다음 순간 시위 튕기는 소리가 들리고, 흰 줄기 하나가 덤불 속으로 번뜩이며 들어가는 것이 보이더니, 상처 입은 수사슴이 은신처에서 솟구쳐 나와, 바로 그 숨은 적의 발치로 뛰어들었다."], ["Avoiding the horns of the infuriated animal, Uncas darted to his side, and passed his knife across the throat, when bounding to the edge of the river it fell, dyeing the waters with its blood.", "성난 짐승의 뿔을 피해, 〈언커스〉는 그 옆구리로 쏜살같이 달려들어 칼로 목을 그었으니, 짐승은 강가로 펄쩍 뛰어가 쓰러지며 제 피로 물을 물들였다."]], [["\"'Twas done with Indian skill,\"", "\"인디언 솜씨로 해냈군.\""], ["said the scout, laughing inwardly, but with vast satisfaction; \"and 'twas a pretty sight to behold!", "척후병이 속으로 웃으며, 그러나 그지없는 만족 속에 말했다. \"그리고 보기에도 멋진 광경이었어!"], ["Though an arrow is a near shot, and needs a knife to finish the work.\"", "비록 화살은 가까이서 쏘는 거라, 일을 끝내려면 칼이 필요하지만 말일세.\""]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"휴!\""], ["ejaculated his companion, turning quickly, like a hound who scented game.", "그의 동행이, 사냥감 냄새를 맡은 사냥개처럼 휙 돌아서며 외마디 소리를 냈다."]], [["\"By the Lord, there is a drove of them!\"", "\"이런, 저놈들 떼거리로구먼!\""], ["exclaimed the scout, whose eyes began to glisten with the ardor of his usual occupation; \"if they come within range of a bullet I will drop one, though the whole Six Nations should be lurking within sound!", "척후병이 외쳤으니, 그 눈은 늘 하던 일의 열기로 빛나기 시작했다. \"저놈들이 탄알 사정거리 안에 들면, 〈여섯 부족 연합〉 전체가 그 소리 안에 숨어 있다 한들 내 한 놈 거꾸러뜨리겠네!"], ["What do you hear, Chingachgook?", "무슨 소리가 들리나, 〈칭가치국〉?"], ["for to my ears the woods are dumb.\"", "내 귀엔 숲이 벙어리 같은데.\""]], [["\"There is but one deer, and he is dead,\"", "\"사슴은 한 마리뿐이고, 그놈은 죽었다.\""], ["said the Indian, bending his body till his ear nearly touched the earth.", "인디언이 귀가 거의 땅에 닿도록 몸을 굽히며 말했다."], ["\"I hear the sounds of feet!\"", "\"발소리가 들린다!\""]], [["\"Perhaps the wolves have driven the buck to shelter, and are following on his trail.\"", "\"늑대들이 수사슴을 숨을 데로 몰면서, 그 자취를 따라오는 건지도 모르지.\""]], [["\"No. The horses of white men are coming!\"", "\"아니야."], ["returned the other, raising himself with dignity, and resuming his seat on the log with his former composure.", "백인들의 말이 오고 있어!\" 상대가 위엄 있게 몸을 일으키며 받고는, 앞서의 침착함으로 다시 통나무에 자리를 잡았다."], ["\"Hawkeye, they are your brothers; speak to them.\"", "\"〈호크아이〉, 자네 형제들일세. 자네가 말하게.\""]], [["\"That will I, and in English that the king needn't be ashamed to answer,\" returned the hunter, speaking in the language of which he boasted; \"but I see nothing, nor do I hear the sounds of man or beast; 'tis strange that an Indian should understand white sounds better than a man who, his very enemies will own, has no cross in his blood, although he may have lived with the redskins long enough to be suspected! Ha! there goes something like the cracking of a dry stick, too--now I hear the bushes move--yes, yes, there is a trampling that I mistook for the falls--and--but here they come themselves; God keep them from the Iroquois!\"", "\"그러지, 그것도 임금님이 답하기에 부끄럽지 않을 〈영국〉말로 말일세.\" 사냥꾼이 제가 자랑하던 그 언어로 말하며 받았다. \"허나 아무것도 안 보이고, 사람이건 짐승이건 그 소리도 안 들리는걸. 인디언이 백인 소리를, 그 피에 한 방울 섞임도 없다는 걸 적들조차 인정하는 사람보다 더 잘 알아듣는다니 이상한 노릇이지, 비록 붉은 사람들과 어울려 산 세월이 의심받을 만큼 길긴 해도 말이야! 하! 저기 마른 나뭇가지 꺾이는 것 같은 소리도 나는군. 이제 덤불이 움직이는 게 들리네. 그래, 그래, 내가 폭포로 잘못 들었던 발 구르는 소리가 있어. 그리고, 아니, 저기 그들이 직접 오는군. 부디 하느님께서 그들을 〈이로쿼이〉에게서 지켜 주시기를!\" * 〈여섯 부족 연합〉: 〈이로쿼이〉 연맹을 이루던 여섯 부족을 가리킨다."]], [["\"Well, go thy way: thou shalt not from this grove Till I torment thee for this injury.\"", "\"좋다, 네 갈 길을 가라. 허나 이 작은 숲에서\n내 너를 이 상처의 빚으로 괴롭히기 전에는 떠나지 못하리라.\""]], [["_Midsummer Night's Dream._", "한여름 밤의 꿈"]], [["The words were still in the mouth of the scout, when the leader of the party, whose approaching footsteps had caught the vigilant ear of the Indian, came openly into view.", "이 말이 아직 척후의 입가에 머물러 있을 때, 그 다가오는 발소리가 인디언의 예민한 귀를 사로잡았던 일행의 우두머리가 대놓고 모습을 드러냈다."], ["A beaten path, such as those made by the periodical passage of the deer, wound through a little glen at no great distance, and struck the river at the point where the white man and his red companions had posted themselves.", "사슴이 철 따라 오가며 낸 것 같은 다져진 오솔길 하나가 멀지 않은 곳에서 작은 골짜기를 가로질러 굽이지다가, 백인과 그 붉은 동료들이 자리 잡은 바로 그 지점에서 강에 가닿았다."], ["Along this track the travellers, who had produced a surprise so unusual in the depths of the forest, advanced slowly towards the hunter, who was in front of his associates, in readiness to receive them.", "이 자취를 따라, 숲속 깊은 곳에서는 그토록 보기 드문 놀라움을 자아낸 그 여행자들이, 동료들 앞에 서서 그들을 맞을 채비를 갖춘 사냥꾼을 향해 천천히 다가왔다."]], [["\"Who comes?\"", "\"누가 오는가?\""], ["demanded the scout, throwing his rifle carelessly across his left arm, and keeping the forefinger of his right hand on the trigger, though he avoided all appearance of menace in the act, \"Who comes hither, among the beasts and dangers of the wilderness?\"", "척후가 왼팔에 무심히 소총을 걸치고 오른손 검지를 방아쇠에 댄 채, 그러나 그 동작에서 위협의 기색이라곤 일절 피하면서 물었다. \"이 황야의 짐승들과 위험들 한가운데로, 누가 이리로 오는가?\""]], [["\"Believers in religion, and friends to the law and to the king,\"", "\"신앙을 믿는 자들이며, 법과 왕의 벗들이오.\""], ["returned he who rode foremost.", "맨 앞에서 말을 탄 자가 대답했다."], ["\"Men who have journeyed since the rising sun, in the shades of this forest, without nourishment, and are sadly tired of their wayfaring.\"", "\"해가 뜬 뒤로 이 숲의 그늘 속에서 먹을 것도 없이 길을 걸어온 자들이며, 그 여정에 몹시 지친 자들이오.\""]], [["\"You are, then, lost,\"", "\"그렇다면 당신들은 길을 잃은 게로군.\""], ["interrupted the hunter, \"and have found how helpless 'tis not to know whether to take the right hand or the left?\"", "사냥꾼이 말을 끊었다. \"오른쪽으로 가야 할지 왼쪽으로 가야 할지 모른다는 게 얼마나 막막한 일인지 알게 된 게요?\""]], [["\"Even so; sucking babes are not more dependent on those who guide them than we who are of larger growth, and who may now be said to possess the stature without the knowledge of men.", "\"바로 그렇소. 젖먹이 아기들이 저를 이끄는 이들에게 매달리는 것보다, 다 자란 우리가, 이제는 사람의 키만 갖추었을 뿐 사람의 지혜는 갖추지 못했다 할 우리가 더 매달리고 있구려."], ["Know you the distance to a post of the crown called William Henry?\"", "〈윌리엄 헨리〉라 불리는 왕실의 진지까지 거리가 얼마나 되는지 아시오?\""]], [["\"Hoot!\"", "\"허!\""], ["shouted the scout, who did not spare his open laughter, though, instantly checking the dangerous sounds, he indulged his merriment at less risk of being overheard by any lurking enemies.", "척후가 거리낌 없는 웃음을 아끼지 않고 외쳤으나, 곧 그 위험한 소리를 억누르고는, 어디 숨어 있을 적들에게 들킬 위험이 덜한 채로 그 흥겨움을 마음껏 누렸다."], ["\"You are as much off the scent as a hound would be, with Horican atwixt him and the deer!", "\"당신은 사냥개가 저와 사슴 사이에 〈호리컨〉을 둔 꼴만큼이나 냄새를 놓쳤구려!"], ["William Henry, man!", "이보시오, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉라니!"], ["if you are friends to the king, and have business with the army, your better way would be to follow the river down to Edward, and lay the matter before Webb; who tarries there, instead of pushing into the defiles, and driving this saucy Frenchman back across Champlain, into his den again.\"", "당신들이 왕의 벗이고 군대와 볼일이 있다면, 차라리 강을 따라 〈에드워드〉로 내려가 그 일을 〈웨브〉 앞에 아뢰는 편이 나을 거요. 그 양반은 협곡으로 밀고 들어가 이 건방진 프랑스 놈을 〈섐플레인〉 너머 제 굴속으로 도로 몰아내는 대신, 거기 머물러 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["Before the stranger could make any reply to this unexpected proposition, another horseman dashed the bushes aside, and leaped his charger into the pathway, in front of his companion.", "이 뜻밖의 제안에 낯선 이가 미처 답하기도 전에, 또 다른 기수가 덤불을 헤치며 군마를 몰아 동료 앞으로 오솔길에 뛰어들었다."]], [["\"What, then, may be our distance from Fort Edward?\"", "\"그렇다면, 〈에드워드〉 요새까지 우리 거리가 얼마나 되겠소?\""], ["demanded a new speaker; \"the place you advise us to seek we left this morning, and our destination is the head of the lake.\"", "새로운 화자가 물었다. \"당신이 우리더러 찾아가라는 그곳은 우리가 오늘 아침 떠나온 곳이고, 우리의 목적지는 호수의 머리요.\""]], [["\"Then you must have lost your eyesight afore losing your way, for the road across the portage is cut to a good two rods, and is as grand a path, I calculate, as any that runs into London, or even before the palace of the king himself.\"", "\"그렇다면 당신들은 길을 잃기 전에 먼저 시력을 잃었던 게로군. 육로 운반로를 가로지르는 길은 좋이 두 로드 너비로 닦여 있어, 내 셈으로는 〈런던〉으로 뻗은 어느 길에도, 아니 왕 자신의 궁전 앞에 난 길에도 못지않은 훌륭한 길이오.\""]], [["\"We will not dispute concerning the excellence of the passage,\"", "\"그 길이 얼마나 좋은지를 두고 다투지는 맙시다.\""], ["returned Heyward, smiling; for, as the reader has anticipated, it was he.", "헤이워드가 미소 지으며 대답했으니, 독자가 짐작했듯, 바로 그였다."], ["\"It is enough, for the present, that we trusted to an Indian guide to take us by a nearer, though blinder path, and that we are deceived in his knowledge.", "\"지금으로선, 우리가 한 인디언 안내인을 믿고 더 가깝되 더 어두운 길로 가려 했다가, 그자의 지식에 속고 말았다는 것으로 족하오."], ["In plain words, we know not where we are.\"", "쉽게 말해, 우리는 우리가 어디 있는지 모르겠소.\""]], [["\"An Indian lost in the woods!\"", "\"인디언이 숲에서 길을 잃다니!\""], ["said the scout, shaking his head doubtingly; \"when the sun is scorching the tree-tops, and the water-courses are full; when the moss on every beech he sees, will tell him in which quarter the north star will shine at night!", "척후가 의심스레 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"해가 나무 우듬지를 태우고 물길이 가득 차 있을 때, 눈에 들어오는 너도밤나무마다 그 이끼가 밤에 북극성이 어느 방위에 빛날지 일러 줄 텐데!"], ["The woods are full of deer paths which run to the streams and licks, places well known to everybody; nor have the geese done their flight to the Canada waters altogether!", "숲에는 시내와 소금 핥는 자리로 이어지는 사슴 길이 가득하고, 그건 누구나 잘 아는 데란 말이오. 게다가 기러기들이 아직 〈캐나다〉의 물로 다 날아가 버린 것도 아니고!"], ["'Tis strange that an Indian should be lost atwixt Horican and the bend in the river.", "인디언이 〈호리컨〉과 강의 굽이 사이에서 길을 잃는다는 건 괴이쩍은 일이오."], ["Is he a Mohawk?\"", "그자가 〈모호크〉요?\""]], [["\"Not by birth, though adopted in that tribe; I think his birthplace was farther north, and he is one of those you call a Huron.\"", "\"태생으로는 아니나, 그 부족에 받아들여졌소. 내 생각엔 그자의 태생지는 더 북쪽이고, 당신들이 〈휴런〉이라 부르는 자들 가운데 하나요.\""]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"휴!\""], ["exclaimed the two companions of the scout, who had continued, until this part of the dialogue, seated immovable, and apparently indifferent to what passed, but who now sprang to their feet with an activity and interest that had evidently got the better of their reserve, by surprise.", "척후의 두 동료가 외쳤으니, 그들은 이 대목까지 꼼짝 않고 앉아 오가는 말에 무심한 듯하다가, 이제 그 절제를 놀라움에 압도당한 듯한 활기와 관심으로 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["\"A Huron!\"", "\"〈휴런〉이라!\""], ["repeated the sturdy scout, once more shaking his head in open distrust; \"they are a thievish race, nor do I care by whom they are adopted; you can never make anything of them but skulks and vagabonds.", "다부진 척후가 다시 한번 드러내 놓고 못 미더운 듯 고개를 저으며 되뇌었다. \"그놈들은 도둑의 족속이라, 누가 받아들였든 나는 상관 안 하오."], ["Since you trusted yourself to the care of one of that nation, I only wonder that you have not fallen in with more.\"", "그자들로는 숨어 다니는 자나 떠돌이밖에 만들 수가 없소. 당신이 그 부족의 하나에게 몸을 맡겼다 하니, 더 많은 놈들과 마주치지 않은 게 그저 신기할 따름이오.\""]], [["\"Of that there is little danger, since William Henry is so many miles in our front.", "\"그럴 위험은 거의 없소. 〈윌리엄 헨리〉가 우리 앞으로 그토록 여러 마일 떨어져 있으니 말이오."], ["You forget that I have told you our guide is now a Mohawk, and that he serves with our forces as a friend.\"", "그 안내인이 이제는 〈모호크〉이며, 우리 군대와 함께 벗으로서 복무한다고 내가 말한 것을 잊으셨군.\""]], [["\"And I tell you that he who is born a Mingo will die a Mingo,\"", "\"그리고 〈밍고〉로 태어난 자는 〈밍고〉로 죽는다고 내가 말하지 않았소.\""], ["returned the other, positively.", "상대가 단호히 대꾸했다."], ["\"A Mohawk!", "\"〈모호크〉라!"], ["No, give me a Delaware or a Mohican for honesty; and when they will fight, which they won't all do, having suffered their cunning enemies, the Maquas, to make them women--but when they will fight at all, look to a Delaware, or a Mohican, for a warrior!\"", "아니지, 정직함을 말하자면 내게는 〈델라웨어〉나 〈모히칸〉을 주시오. 그리고 그들이 싸우려 들 때면 — 다는 그러지 않소만, 교활한 적인 〈마콰〉가 자기들을 계집으로 만들어 버리는 걸 그냥 두었으니 — 하나 그들이 정말 싸우려 들 때면, 전사를 보고 싶거든 〈델라웨어〉나 〈모히칸〉을 보시오!\""]], [["\"Enough of this,\"", "\"그 이야기는 그만하시오.\""], ["said Heyward, impatiently; \"I wish not to inquire into the character of a man that I know, and to whom you must be a stranger.", "헤이워드가 참다못해 말했다. \"나는 내가 아는 사람, 그리고 당신에게는 낯선 자임이 분명한 사람의 됨됨이를 캐물을 마음이 없소."], ["You have not yet answered my question: what is our distance from the main army at Edward?\"", "당신은 아직 내 물음에 답하지 않았소. 〈에드워드〉의 본대까지 우리 거리가 얼마나 되오?\""]], [["\"It seems that may depend on who is your guide.", "\"그건 당신의 안내인이 누구냐에 달린 모양이오."], ["One would think such a horse as that might get over a good deal of ground atwixt sun-up and sun-down.\"", "저런 말이라면 해 뜰 녘과 해 질 녘 사이에 꽤나 먼 길을 넘을 수 있다고들 여길 테니.\""]], [["\"I wish no contention of idle words with you, friend,\"", "\"당신과 부질없는 말다툼을 벌일 마음은 없소, 친구.\""], ["said Heyward, curbing his dissatisfied manner, and speaking in a more gentle voice; \"if you will tell me the distance to Fort Edward, and conduct me thither, your labor shall not go without its reward.\"", "헤이워드가 불만스러운 기색을 누르고 한결 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"〈에드워드〉 요새까지 거리를 일러 주고 나를 그리로 인도해 준다면, 당신의 수고가 보답 없이 끝나지는 않을 것이오.\""]], [["\"And in so doing, how know I that I don't guide an enemy, and a spy of Montcalm, to the works of the army?", "\"그리한들, 내가 적을, 〈몽칼름〉의 첩자를 군대의 진지로 안내하는 게 아니라는 걸 어찌 알겠소?"], ["It is not every man who can speak the English tongue that is an honest subject.\"", "영어를 할 줄 안다고 해서 다 정직한 신하는 아니오.\""]], [["\"If you serve with the troops, of whom I judge you to be a scout, you should know of such a regiment of the king as the 60th.\"", "\"당신이 군대와 함께 복무한다면, 내가 보기에 당신은 척후인 듯한데, 왕의 연대 가운데 60연대쯤은 알 거요.\""]], [["\"The 60th!", "\"60연대라!"], ["you can tell me little of the Royal Americans that I don't know, though I do wear a hunting-shirt instead of a scarlet jacket.\"", "내가 다홍색 군복 대신 사냥용 셔츠를 걸치고 있긴 하나, 〈로열 아메리칸〉에 관해서라면 내가 모르는 걸 당신이 일러 줄 게 별로 없소.\""]], [["\"Well, then, among the other things, you may know the name of its major?\"", "\"그렇다면, 다른 것들 중에서도, 그 연대 소령의 이름쯤은 알겠구려?\""]], [["\"Its major!\"", "\"그 소령이라!\""], ["interrupted the hunter, elevating his body like one who was proud of his trust.", "사냥꾼이 제 직임을 자랑스러워하는 자처럼 몸을 곧추세우며 말을 끊었다."], ["\"If there is a man in the country who knows Major Effingham, he stands before you.\"", "\"이 고장에 〈에핑엄〉 소령을 아는 사람이 있다면, 그가 바로 당신 앞에 서 있소.\""]], [["\"It is a corps which has many majors; the gentleman you name is the senior, but I speak of the junior of them all; he who commands the companies in garrison at William Henry.\"", "\"그것은 소령이 여럿인 부대요. 당신이 댄 분은 선임이지만, 나는 그 가운데 가장 후임을 말하는 것이오. 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 수비대 중대들을 지휘하는 자 말이오.\""]], [["\"Yes, yes, I have heard that a young gentleman of vast riches, from one of the provinces far south, has got the place.", "\"그렇소, 그렇소, 남쪽 먼 어느 식민지 출신의, 막대한 재산을 가진 젊은 양반이 그 자리를 맡았다고 들었소."], ["He is over young, too, to hold such rank, and to be put above men whose heads are beginning to bleach; and yet they say he is a soldier in his knowledge, and a gallant gentleman!\"", "그런 계급을 지니기에는, 머리가 희끗희끗해지기 시작한 사람들 위에 놓이기에는 너무 젊긴 하지만, 그래도 사람들 말로는 식견으로는 군인이요, 늠름한 신사라 합디다!\""]], [["\"Whatever he may be, or however he may be qualified for his rank, he now speaks to you, and of course can be no enemy to dread.\"", "\"그가 무엇이든, 또 그 계급에 얼마나 걸맞든, 그가 지금 당신에게 말하고 있으니, 두려워할 적일 리는 당연히 없겠소.\""]], [["The scout regarded Heyward in surprise, and then lifting his cap, he answered, in a tone less confident than before, though still expressing doubt,--", "척후가 놀라 헤이워드를 바라보더니, 모자를 들어 올리고는, 전보다 덜 자신만만한, 그러나 여전히 의심을 드러내는 어조로 대답했다."]], [["\"I have heard a party was to leave the encampment this morning, for the lake shore.\"", "\"오늘 아침 한 부대가 호숫가로 가려고 진지를 떠난다는 말은 들었소.\""]], [["\"You have heard the truth; but I preferred a nearer route, trusting to the knowledge of the Indian I mentioned.\"", "\"당신이 들은 것이 사실이오. 다만 나는 더 가까운 길을 택했으니, 내가 말한 그 인디언의 지식을 믿고서 말이오.\""]], [["\"And he deceived you, and then deserted?\"", "\"그래서 그자가 당신을 속이고는, 그러고 나서 달아났소?\""]], [["\"Neither, as I believe; certainly not the latter, for he is to be found in the rear.\"", "\"내가 믿기로는 둘 다 아니오. 적어도 후자는 분명 아니오. 그자는 뒤편에 있을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["\"I should like to look at the creatur'; if it is a true Iroquois I can tell him by his knavish look, and by his paint,\"", "\"내 그 짐승을 한번 봐야겠소. 만일 그자가 진짜 〈이로쿼이〉라면, 그 악당 같은 낯짝과 칠한 무늬로 알아볼 수 있을 거요.\""], ["said the scout, stepping past the charger of Heyward, and entering the path behind the mare of the singing-master, whose foal had taken advantage of the halt to exact the maternal contribution.", "척후가 헤이워드의 군마 곁을 지나, 그 망아지가 멈춘 틈을 타 어미의 젖을 받아 먹고 있던 노래 선생의 암말 뒤로 난 길로 들어서며 말했다."], ["After shoving aside the bushes, and proceeding a few paces, he encountered the females, who awaited the result of the conference with anxiety, and not entirely without apprehension.", "덤불을 옆으로 밀치고 몇 걸음 나아가자, 그는 회담의 결과를 근심 어린, 그러면서도 불안이 아주 없지는 않은 마음으로 기다리던 여인들과 마주쳤다."], ["Behind these, the runner leaned against a tree, where he stood the close examination of the scout with an air unmoved, though with a look so dark and savage, that it might in itself excite fear.", "그 뒤에는 전령이 나무에 기대 서 있었으니, 그는 척후의 면밀한 검사를 흔들림 없는 태도로 견뎌 냈으되, 그 표정이 어찌나 어둡고 사나웠던지, 그 자체로 두려움을 자아낼 만했다."], ["Satisfied with his scrutiny, the hunter soon left him.", "살핌에 만족한 사냥꾼은 곧 그를 떠났다."], ["As he repassed the females, he paused a moment to gaze upon their beauty, answering to the smile and nod of Alice with a look of open pleasure.", "여인들 곁을 다시 지나며 그는 잠시 걸음을 멈추고 그들의 아름다움을 바라보았으니, 〈앨리스〉의 미소와 끄덕임에는 거리낌 없는 기쁨의 눈길로 답했다."], ["Thence he went to the side of the motherly animal, and spending a minute in a fruitless inquiry into the character of her rider, he shook his head and returned to Heyward.", "거기서 그는 그 어미다운 짐승 곁으로 가, 그 임자의 됨됨이를 살피느라 일 분쯤 부질없이 보내고는, 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 헤이워드에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["\"A Mingo is a Mingo, and God having made him so, neither the Mohawks nor any other tribe can alter him,\"", "\"〈밍고〉는 〈밍고〉요, 신이 그를 그렇게 지으셨으니, 〈모호크〉도, 그 어떤 부족도 그를 바꿔 놓을 수 없소.\" 그가 본래 자리로 돌아와서 말했다."], ["he said, when he had regained his former position.", "\"우리뿐이고, 당신이 오늘 밤 저 고귀한 말을 늑대들의 처분에 맡겨 둔다면, 내가 한 시간 안에 손수 〈에드워드〉로 가는 길을 보여 드릴 수 있소."], ["\"If we were alone, and you would leave that noble horse at the mercy of the wolves to-night, I could show you the way to Edward, myself, within an hour, for it lies only about an hour's journey hence; but with such ladies in your company 'tis impossible!\"", "여기서 한 시간 남짓한 거리밖에 안 되니 말이오. 허나 당신 일행에 저런 숙녀들이 있으니 그건 불가능하오!\""]], [["\"And why?", "\"어째서요?"], ["they are fatigued, but they are quite equal to a ride of a few more miles.\"", "저들은 지쳤으나, 몇 마일쯤 더 말을 타기에는 넉넉히 견딜 수 있소.\""]], [["\"'Tis a natural impossibility!\"", "\"그건 자연의 이치로 불가능하오!\""], ["repeated the scout; \"I wouldn't walk a mile in these woods after night gets into them, in company with that runner, for the best rifle in the colonies.", "척후가 거듭 말했다. \"나는 식민지에서 제일 좋은 소총을 준대도, 밤이 든 뒤에 저 전령과 함께라면 이 숲에서 한 마일도 걷지 않겠소."], ["They are full of outlying Iroquois, and your mongrel Mohawk knows where to find them too well, to be my companion.\"", "숲에는 밖에 나와 있는 〈이로쿼이〉가 가득하고, 당신의 잡종 〈모호크〉는 그들을 어디서 찾을지 너무 잘 아니, 내 길벗으로는 안 되오.\""]], [["\"Think you so?\"", "\"그리 생각하시오?\""], ["said Heyward, leaning forward in the saddle, and dropping his voice nearly to a whisper; \"I confess I have not been without my own suspicions, though I have endeavored to conceal them, and affected a confidence I have not always felt, on account of my companions.", "헤이워드가 안장에서 몸을 앞으로 기울이고, 목소리를 거의 속삭임에 가깝게 낮추며 말했다. \"고백하건대 나도 나름의 의심이 없지 않았소."], ["It was because I suspected him that I would follow no longer; making him, as you see, follow me.\"", "다만 동행들을 생각해, 그 의심을 감추려 애썼고, 늘 느끼지는 못한 믿음을 꾸며 냈을 뿐이오. 그자를 의심했기에 더는 따르지 않으려 했고, 보다시피 그자를 내 뒤를 따르게 한 것이오.\""]], [["\"I knew he was one of the cheats as soon as I laid eyes on him!\"", "\"내 저놈을 보자마자 사기꾼 중 하나란 걸 알았소!\""], ["returned the scout, placing a finger on his nose, in sign of caution.", "척후가 조심하라는 표시로 손가락을 코에 대며 대꾸했다."], ["\"The thief is leaning against the foot of the sugar sapling, that you can see over them bushes; his right leg is in a line with the bark of the tree, and,\"", "\"저 도둑은, 당신이 저 덤불 너머로 볼 수 있는 단풍 묘목 밑동에 기대 있소. 놈의 오른쪽 다리가 나무껍질과 일직선을 이루고 있으니,\""], ["tapping his rifle, \"I can take him from where I stand, between the ankle and the knee, with a single shot, putting an end to his tramping through the woods, for at least a month to come.", "그가 제 소총을 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"내 선 자리에서 발목과 무릎 사이를 단 한 발로 맞혀, 적어도 앞으로 한 달은 놈이 숲을 헤집고 다니는 걸 끝장낼 수 있소."], ["If I should go back to him, the cunning varmint would suspect something, and be dodging through the trees like a frightened deer.\"", "만일 내가 놈에게로 도로 가면, 그 교활한 짐승은 무언가를 눈치채고 겁먹은 사슴처럼 나무 사이로 요리조리 달아날 거요.\""]], [["\"It will not do.", "\"그건 안 되오."], ["He may be innocent, and I dislike the act.", "그자가 죄 없을 수도 있고, 나는 그런 짓이 싫소."], ["Though, if I felt confident of his treachery--\"", "다만, 그자의 배신을 확신한다면야ㅡ\""]], [["\"'Tis a safe thing to calculate on the knavery of an Iroquois,\"", "\"〈이로쿼이〉의 간계를 셈에 넣는 건 안전한 일이오.\""], ["said the scout, throwing his rifle forward, by a sort of instinctive movement.", "척후가 일종의 본능적인 동작으로 제 소총을 앞으로 내밀며 말했다."]], [["\"Hold!\"", "\"멈추시오!\" 헤이워드가 가로막았다."], ["interrupted Heyward, \"it will not do--we must think of some other scheme; and yet, I have much reason to believe the rascal has deceived me.\"", "\"그건 안 되오ㅡ다른 방책을 궁리해야 하오. 그렇긴 해도, 그 악당이 나를 속였다고 믿을 만한 까닭은 충분하오.\""]], [["The hunter, who had already abandoned his intention of maiming the runner, mused a moment, and then made a gesture, which instantly brought his two red companions to his side.", "이미 전령에게 상해를 입히려던 뜻을 접은 사냥꾼은 잠시 골똘히 생각하더니, 손짓을 했고, 그 손짓에 그의 두 붉은 동료가 즉시 그의 곁으로 다가왔다."], ["They spoke together earnestly in the Delaware language, though in an undertone; and by the gestures of the white man, which were frequently directed towards the top of the sapling, it was evident he pointed out the situation of their hidden enemy.", "그들은 낮은 목소리로나마 〈델라웨어〉 말로 진지하게 함께 이야기를 나누었으니, 묘목 우듬지 쪽을 자주 가리키는 백인의 몸짓으로 보아, 그가 숨은 적의 위치를 일러 주고 있음이 분명했다."], ["His companions were not long in comprehending his wishes, and laying aside their fire-arms, they parted, taking opposite sides of the path, and burying themselves in the thicket, with such cautious movements, that their steps were inaudible.", "그의 동료들은 그 뜻을 알아채는 데 오래 걸리지 않았고, 총을 내려놓고는 길의 양편으로 갈라져, 발소리 하나 들리지 않을 만큼 조심스러운 움직임으로 덤불 속에 몸을 묻었다."]], [["\"Now, go you back,\"", "\"자, 당신은 도로 돌아가시오.\" 사냥꾼이 다시 헤이워드에게 말했다."], ["said the hunter, speaking again to Heyward, \"and hold the imp in talk; these Mohicans here will take him without breaking his paint.\"", "\"그러고는 저 악마 같은 놈을 말로 붙들어 두시오. 여기 이 〈모히칸〉들이 그놈의 칠한 무늬 하나 흩뜨리지 않고 사로잡을 테니.\""]], [["\"Nay,\"", "\"아니오.\""], ["said Heyward, proudly, \"I will seize him myself.\"", "헤이워드가 자랑스레 말했다. \"내 손수 그를 붙잡겠소.\""]], [["\"Hist!", "\"쉿!"], ["what could you do, mounted, against an Indian in the bushes?\"", "말을 탄 채로, 덤불 속의 인디언을 상대로 당신이 무얼 할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["\"I will dismount.\"", "\"내려서겠소.\""]], [["\"And, think you, when he saw one of your feet out of the stirrup, he would wait for the other to be free?", "\"그렇다면, 당신의 한쪽 발이 등자에서 빠지는 걸 보고도, 그놈이 나머지 발이 풀려나기를 기다려 줄 성싶소?"], ["Whoever comes into the woods to deal with the natives, must use Indian fashions, if he would wish to prosper in his undertakings.", "원주민들을 상대하러 숲에 드는 자라면 누구든, 제 일에서 잘되기를 바란다면 인디언의 방식을 써야 하오."], ["Go, then, talk openly to the miscreant, and seem to believe him the truest friend you have on 'arth.\"", "그러니 가서, 그 악당에게 거리낌 없이 말을 걸고, 그자가 이 땅에서 당신이 가진 가장 진실한 벗이라고 믿는 척하시오.\""]], [["Heyward prepared to comply, though with strong disgust at the nature of the office he was compelled to execute.", "헤이워드는 따를 채비를 했으나, 어쩔 수 없이 떠맡게 된 그 일의 성질에 강한 거부감을 느꼈다."], ["Each moment, however, pressed upon him a conviction of the critical situation in which he had suffered his invaluable trust to be involved through his own confidence.", "그러나 매 순간이, 제 믿음 탓에 그 더없이 소중한 책임을 위태로운 처지에 빠뜨렸다는 깨달음을 그에게 들이밀었다."], ["The sun had already disappeared, and the woods, suddenly deprived of his light,[9] were assuming a dusky hue, which keenly reminded him that the hour the savage usually chose for his most barbarous and remorseless acts of vengeance or hostility, was speedily drawing near.", "해는 이미 사라졌고, 그 빛을 갑자기 잃은 숲은 어둑한 빛깔을 띠어 가고 있었으니, 미개인이 으레 가장 야만스럽고 가차 없는 복수나 적의의 행위를 저지를 시각으로 고르는 그 시간이 빠르게 다가오고 있음을 그에게 날카롭게 일깨웠다."], ["Stimulated by apprehension, he left the scout, who immediately entered into a loud conversation with the stranger that had so unceremoniously enlisted himself in the party of travellers that morning.", "불안에 떠밀린 그는 척후를 떠났고, 척후는 곧장 그날 아침 그토록 격식 없이 여행자 일행에 끼어든 낯선 이와 큰 소리로 대화를 나누기 시작했다."], ["In passing his gentler companions Heyward uttered a few words of encouragement, and was pleased to find that, though fatigued with the exercise of the day, they appeared to entertain no suspicion that their present embarrassment was other than the result of accident.", "더 연약한 동행들 곁을 지나며 헤이워드는 격려의 말 몇 마디를 건넸고, 그들이 비록 하루의 행로에 지쳤으나, 지금의 곤경이 우연의 결과 이외의 것이라는 의심은 품지 않은 듯하여 마음이 놓였다."], ["Giving them reason to believe he was merely employed in a consultation concerning the future route, he spurred his charger, and drew the reins again, when the animal had carried him within a few yards of the place where the sullen runner still stood, leaning against the tree.", "그저 앞으로의 길에 관해 상의하고 있을 뿐이라고 그들이 믿게끔 만든 뒤, 그는 군마에 박차를 가했다가, 그 짐승이 그를 태우고 침울한 전령이 여전히 나무에 기대선 자리에서 몇 야드 안으로 들어섰을 때 다시 고삐를 당겼다."]], [["\"You may see, Magua,\"", "\"〈머과〉, 자네도 보다시피,\""], ["he said, endeavoring to assume an air of freedom and confidence, \"that the night is closing around us, and yet we are no nearer to William Henry than when we left the encampment of Webb with the rising sun.", "그가 자유롭고 자신만만한 기색을 꾸며 보이려 애쓰며 말했다. \"밤이 우리를 에워싸며 다가드는데, 우리는 해 뜰 녘에 〈웨브〉의 진지를 떠났을 때보다 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에 조금도 더 가까워지지 못했네."], ["You have missed the way, nor have I been more fortunate.", "자네가 길을 잘못 들었고, 나 또한 더 나을 것이 없었네."], ["But, happily we have fallen in with a hunter, he whom you hear talking to the singer, that is acquainted with the deer-paths and by-ways of the woods, and who promises to lead us to a place where we may rest securely till the morning.\"", "허나 다행히도 우리는 한 사냥꾼을 만났네. 자네가 저 노래꾼과 이야기하는 걸 듣고 있는 저자가, 숲의 사슴 길과 샛길에 밝아, 우리를 아침까지 안전히 쉴 수 있는 곳으로 인도해 주겠다고 약속했네.\""]], [["The Indian riveted his glowing eyes on Heyward as he asked, in his imperfect English, \"Is he alone?\"", "인디언이 그 이글거리는 눈을 헤이워드에게 못 박고는, 어설픈 영어로 물었다. \"그자 혼자요?\""]], [["\"Alone!\"", "\"혼자라!\""], ["hesitatingly answered Heyward to whom deception was too new to be assumed without embarrassment.", "속임수가 너무도 낯설어 거북함 없이는 꾸며 낼 수 없는 헤이워드가 머뭇대며 대답했다. \"오!"], ["\"O! not alone, surely, Magua, for you know that we are with him.\"", "〈머과〉, 분명 혼자는 아니지, 우리가 그와 함께 있다는 걸 자네도 알지 않나.\""]], [["\"Then Le Renard Subtil will go,\"", "\"그렇다면 〈교활한 여우〉는 가겠소.\""], ["returned the runner, coolly raising his little wallet from the place where it had lain at his feet; \"and the pale-faces will see none but their own color.\"", "전령이 발치에 놓여 있던 작은 자루를 차갑게 집어 들며 대꾸했다. \"그러면 창백한 얼굴들은 저들과 같은 빛깔밖에 보지 못할 거요.\""]], [["\"Go!", "\"가겠다니!"], ["Whom call you Le Renard?\"", "자네가 〈여우〉라 부르는 게 누구인가?\""]], [["\"'Tis the name his Canada fathers have given to Magua,\"", "\"그건 〈캐나다〉의 아버지들이 〈머과〉에게 준 이름이오.\""], ["returned the runner, with an air that manifested his pride at the distinction.", "전령이 그 명예를 자랑하는 듯한 기색으로 대꾸했다."], ["\"Night is the same as day to Le Subtil, when Munro waits for him.\"", "\"〈먼로〉가 기다릴 때면, 〈교활한 자〉에게는 밤도 낮과 다름없소.\""]], [["\"And what account will Le Renard give the chief of William Henry concerning his daughters?", "\"그러면 〈여우〉는 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 지휘관에게 그 따님들에 관해 무어라 고할 텐가?"], ["Will he dare to tell the hot-blooded Scotsman that his children are left without a guide, though Magua promised to be one?\"", "〈머과〉가 안내인이 되겠다 약속해 놓고도 그 아이들을 안내인 없이 남겨 두었노라고, 그 다혈질의 스코틀랜드인에게 감히 말할 수 있겠나?\""]], [["\"Though the gray head has a loud voice, and a long arm, Le Renard will not hear him, or feel him, in the woods.\"", "\"비록 백발의 노인이 큰 목소리와 긴 팔을 가졌어도, 〈여우〉는 숲에서 그를 듣지도, 느끼지도 않을 거요.\""]], [["\"But what will the Mohawks say?", "\"허나 〈모호크〉들은 무어라 하겠나?"], ["They will make him petticoats, and bid him stay in the wigwam with the women, for he is no longer to be trusted with the business of a man.\"", "그들은 그에게 치마를 입혀, 더는 사내의 일을 맡길 수 없는 자이니 천막 안에서 계집들과 함께 머물러 있으라 명할 걸세.\""]], [["\"Le Subtil knows the path to the great lakes, and he can find the bones of his fathers,\"", "\"〈교활한 자〉는 큰 호수들로 가는 길을 알며, 제 아버지들의 뼈를 찾을 수 있소.\""], ["was the answer of the unmoved runner.", "흔들림 없는 전령의 대답이었다."]], [["\"Enough, Magua,\"", "\"그만하게, 〈머과〉.\""], ["said Heyward; \"are we not friends?", "헤이워드가 말했다. \"우리는 벗이 아닌가?"], ["Why should there be bitter words between us?", "어찌하여 우리 사이에 모진 말이 오가야 하나?"], ["Munro has promised you a gift for your services when performed, and I shall be your debtor for another.", "〈먼로〉가 자네의 봉사가 마쳐지면 그 대가로 선물을 주겠다 약속했고, 나 또한 또 다른 선물의 빚을 지게 될 걸세."], ["Rest your weary limbs, then, and open your wallet to eat.", "그러니 지친 사지를 쉬게 하고, 자루를 열어 먹게나."], ["We have a few moments to spare; let us not waste them in talk like wrangling women.", "우리에게 잠시 여유가 있으니, 다투는 계집들처럼 말로 그 시간을 허비하지는 마세."], ["When the ladies are refreshed we will proceed.\"", "숙녀들이 기운을 차리면 우리는 길을 나설 걸세.\""]], [["\"The pale-faces make themselves dogs to their women,\"", "\"창백한 얼굴들은 제 계집들에게 개처럼 굴어,\""], ["muttered the Indian, in his native language, \"and when they want to eat, their warriors must lay aside the tomahawk to feed their laziness.\"", "인디언이 제 모국어로 중얼거렸다. \"그들이 먹고자 할 때면, 그 전사들은 그 게으름을 먹이느라 도끼를 내려놓아야 하는구나.\""]], [["\"What say you, Renard?\"", "\"무어라 했나, 〈여우〉?\""]], [["\"Le Subtil says it is good.\"", "\"〈교활한 자〉는 좋다고 했소.\""]], [["The Indian then fastened his eyes keenly on the open countenance of Heyward, but meeting his glance, he turned them quickly away, and seating himself deliberately on the ground, he drew forth the remnant of some former repast, and began to eat, though not without first bending his looks slowly and cautiously around him.", "그러고 나서 인디언은 헤이워드의 트인 얼굴에 날카롭게 눈을 박았으나, 그 눈길과 마주치자 얼른 눈을 돌리고는, 신중하게 땅바닥에 앉아, 앞서 끼니의 남은 것을 꺼내 먹기 시작했으니, 다만 먼저 천천히, 조심스레 둘레로 시선을 굽혀 살피는 일을 빼놓지 않았다."]], [["\"This is well,\" continued Heyward; \"and Le Renard will have strength and sight to find the path in the morning;\" he paused, for sounds like the snapping of a dried stick, and the rustling of leaves, rose from the adjacent bushes, but recollecting himself instantly, he continued,--\"we must be moving before the sun is seen, or Montcalm may lie in our path, and shut us out from the fortress.\"", "\"이거 좋군.\" 헤이워드가 말을 이었다. \"〈여우〉는 아침에 길을 찾을 기운과 눈을 갖추게 될 걸세.\" 그가 말을 멈추었으니, 마른 나뭇가지가 딱 꺾이는 소리, 나뭇잎이 바스락대는 소리 같은 것이 곁의 덤불에서 일었기 때문이다. 그러나 곧 정신을 가다듬고 말을 이었다. \"우리는 해가 보이기 전에 움직여야 하네. 안 그러면 〈몽칼름〉이 우리 길목에 도사려, 우리를 요새에서 가로막아 버릴지도 모르네.\""]], [["The hand of Magua dropped from his mouth to his side, and though his eyes were fastened on the ground, his head was turned aside, his nostrils expanded, and his ears seemed even to stand more erect than usual, giving to him the appearance of a statue that was made to represent intense attention.", "〈머과〉의 손이 입에서 옆구리로 떨어졌고, 그 눈은 땅에 못 박혀 있었으나, 머리는 옆으로 돌아갔고, 콧구멍은 벌어졌으며, 두 귀마저 평소보다 더 곧추선 듯하여, 그에게 마치 강렬한 주의를 나타내려 빚어진 조각상 같은 형색을 더했다."]], [["Heyward, who watched his movements with a vigilant eye, carelessly extricated one of his feet from the stirrup, while he passed a hand towards the bear-skin covering of his holsters.", "빈틈없는 눈으로 그의 움직임을 지켜보던 헤이워드는, 무심한 척 한쪽 발을 등자에서 빼내며, 한 손을 권총집을 덮은 곰 가죽 쪽으로 옮겼다."], ["Every effort to detect the point most regarded by the runner was completely frustrated by the tremulous glances of his organs, which seemed not to rest a single instant on any particular object, and which, at the same time, could be hardly said to move.", "전령이 가장 주시하는 지점을 알아내려는 온갖 노력은, 어느 한순간도 특정한 대상에 머무는 법이 없으면서도 동시에 거의 움직인다고도 할 수 없는, 그 떨리는 눈길에 의해 완전히 좌절되고 말았다."], ["While he hesitated how to proceed, Le Subtil cautiously raised himself to his feet, though with a motion so slow and guarded, that not the slightest noise was produced by the change.", "그가 어찌할지 망설이는 사이, 〈교활한 자〉가 조심스레 몸을 일으켜 발을 디뎠으니, 그 동작이 어찌나 더디고 조심스러웠던지 그 변화로 인한 자그마한 소리조차 나지 않았다."], ["Heyward felt it had now become incumbent on him to act.", "헤이워드는 이제 자신이 행동에 나서야 할 때라고 느꼈다."], ["Throwing his leg over the saddle, he dismounted, with a determination to advance and seize his treacherous companion, trusting the result to his own manhood.", "그는 다리를 안장 너머로 넘겨 말에서 내렸으니, 나아가 그 배신자 동행을 붙잡으리라 다짐하고는, 그 결과를 제 사내다움에 맡겼다."], ["In order, however, to prevent unnecessary alarm, he still preserved an air of calmness and friendship.", "그러나 불필요한 경각을 막고자, 그는 여전히 차분하고 다정한 기색을 지켰다."]], [["\"Le Renard Subtil does not eat,\"", "\"〈교활한 여우〉가 먹지를 않는군.\""], ["he said, using the appellation he had found most flattering to the vanity of the Indian.", "그가 그 인디언의 허영심에 가장 흡족하리라 여긴 호칭을 쓰며 말했다."], ["\"His corn is not well parched, and it seems dry.", "\"옥수수가 제대로 볶이지 않아 메마른 듯하네."], ["Let me examine; perhaps something may be found among my own provisions that will help his appetite.\"", "어디 내가 살펴보지. 어쩌면 내 식량 가운데 그 입맛을 돋울 무언가가 있을지도 모르니.\""]], [["Magua held out the wallet to the proffer of the other.", "〈머과〉가 상대의 권유에 자루를 내밀었다."], ["He even suffered their hands to meet, without betraying the least emotion, or varying his riveted attitude of attention.", "그는 두 사람의 손이 맞닿는 것까지도, 조금의 동요도 내비치지 않고, 못 박힌 듯한 주의의 자세를 흩뜨리지도 않은 채 내버려 두었다."], ["But when he felt the fingers of Heyward moving gently along his own naked arm, he struck up the limb of the young man, and uttering a piercing cry as he darted beneath it, plunged, at a single bound, into the opposite thicket.", "그러나 헤이워드의 손가락이 제 맨팔을 따라 부드럽게 움직이는 것을 느끼자, 그는 젊은이의 팔을 쳐올리고는, 그 아래로 몸을 날리며 날카로운 비명을 내지르더니, 단 한 번의 도약으로 맞은편 덤불 속에 뛰어들었다."], ["At the next instant the form of Chingachgook appeared from the bushes, looking like a spectre in its paint, and glided across the path in swift pursuit.", "다음 순간 〈칭가치국〉의 형상이 덤불에서 나타났으니, 칠한 무늬 속에서 유령처럼 보이는 그가 잽싸게 뒤쫓으며 길을 가로질러 미끄러져 갔다."], ["Next followed the shout of Uncas, when the woods were lighted by a sudden flash, that was accompanied by the sharp report of the hunter's rifle.", "뒤이어 〈언카스〉의 외침이 따랐고, 그때 숲은 별안간 번뜩이는 섬광으로 환해졌으니, 거기에는 사냥꾼의 소총이 내는 날카로운 총성이 함께했다. * 로드: 길이의 단위로, 한 로드는 약 5미터에 해당한다."]], [["\"In such a night Did Thisbe fearfully o'ertrip the dew; And saw the lion's shadow ere himself.\"", "그러한 밤에\n티스베는 두려움에 떨며 이슬을 밟고 건너갔으니,"]], [["_Merchant of Venice._", "사자를 보기도 전에 그 그림자를 먼저 보았더라."]], [["The suddenness of the flight of his guide, and the wild cries of the pursuers, caused Heyward to remain fixed, for a few moments, in inactive surprise.", "베니스의 상인\n길잡이가 느닷없이 달아나고 추격자들의 거친 함성이 터져 나온 탓에, 〈헤이워드〉는 어쩔 줄 모르는 놀라움 속에서 잠시 그 자리에 꼼짝 못 하고 서 있었다."], ["Then recollecting the importance of securing the fugitive, he dashed aside the surrounding bushes, and pressed eagerly forward to lend his aid in the chase.", "그러다 도망자를 붙드는 것이 중요함을 떠올린 그는, 사방의 덤불을 헤치고 추격에 힘을 보태려 다급히 앞으로 내달았다."], ["Before he had, however, proceeded a hundred yards, he met the three foresters already returning from their unsuccessful pursuit.", "그러나 채 백 야드도 나아가기 전에, 그는 헛된 추격을 마치고 이미 돌아오던 세 사냥꾼과 마주쳤다."]], [["\"Why so soon disheartened!\"", "\"어찌 이리 빨리 낙심하시오!\""], ["he exclaimed; \"the scoundrel must be concealed behind some of these trees, and may yet be secured.", "그가 소리쳤다. \"그 악당은 분명 이 나무들 어딘가에 숨어 있을 테니, 아직 붙잡을 수 있소."], ["We are not safe while he goes at large.\"", "그자가 풀려나 돌아다니는 한 우리는 안전하지 못하오.\""]], [["\"Would you set a cloud to chase the wind?\"", "\"구름더러 바람을 쫓으라 하시겠소?\""], ["returned the disappointed scout; \"I heard the imp, brushing over the dry leaves, like a black snake, and blinking a glimpse of him, just over ag'in yon big pine, I pulled as it might be on the scent; but 'twouldn't do!", "실망한 정찰꾼이 대꾸했다. \"그 악마 같은 놈이 검은 뱀처럼 마른 잎을 스치며 지나가는 소리를 들었고, 저기 저 큰 소나무 바로 너머로 언뜻 그 모습을 비춰 보고는, 냄새를 좇듯이 한 방 당겼소만, 안 됐소이다!"], ["and yet for a reasoning aim, if anybody but myself had touched the trigger, I should call it a quick sight; and I may be accounted to have experience in these matters, and one who ought to know.", "허나 따져 보면, 나 아닌 다른 누가 방아쇠를 당겼더라면 빠른 솜씨라 일컬었을 것이오. 또 나는 이런 일에 경험이 있다 할 만하고, 마땅히 알 만한 사람이라 여겨지오."], ["Look at this sumach; its leaves are red, though everybody knows the fruit is in the yellow blossom, in the month of July!\"", "이 옻나무를 보시오. 잎이 붉지 않소, 누구나 그 열매가 칠월에 노란 꽃으로 핀다는 걸 알 텐데 말이오!\""]], [["\"'Tis the blood of Le Subtil!", "\"저건 〈르 쉬브틸〉의 피요!"], ["he is hurt, and may yet fall!\"", "그자가 다쳤으니, 아직 쓰러질지도 모르오!\""]], [["\"No, no,\"", "\"아니오, 아니오.\""], ["returned the scout, in decided disapprobation of this opinion, \"I rubbed the bark off a limb, perhaps, but the creature leaped the longer for it.", "정찰꾼이 그 의견을 단호히 부정하며 대꾸했다. \"어쩌면 내가 가지의 껍질을 벗겨 냈는지는 모르나, 그 짐승은 그 때문에 도리어 더 멀리 뛰었소."], ["A rifle-bullet acts on a running animal, when it barks him, much the same as one of your spurs on a horse; that is, it quickens motion, and puts life into the flesh, instead of taking it away.", "소총 탄알이란 달리는 짐승의 살갗을 스칠 때면, 마치 당신의 박차가 말에게 하는 것과 흡사하게 작용하오. 다시 말해, 그 움직임을 빠르게 하고 살에 생기를 불어넣지, 생기를 앗아 가지는 않는다는 거요."], ["But when it cuts the ragged hole, after a bound or two, there is, commonly, a stagnation of further leaping, be it Indian or be it deer!\"", "허나 너덜너덜한 구멍을 뚫어 놓으면, 한두 번 뛴 뒤엔 대개 더는 뛰지 못하고 멎고 마니, 인디언이든 사슴이든 매한가지요!\""]], [["\"We are four able bodies, to one wounded man!\"", "\"우리는 멀쩡한 넷이고, 저쪽은 부상당한 하나뿐이오!\""]], [["\"Is life grievous to you?\"", "\"목숨이 그리 짐스럽소?\""], ["interrupted the scout.", "정찰꾼이 말을 가로막았다."], ["\"Yonder red devils would draw you within swing of the tomahawks of his comrades, before you were heated in the chase.", "\"저 붉은 악마들은 추격으로 몸이 달아오르기도 전에 당신을 제 동료들의 손도끼가 닿는 거리 안으로 끌어들일 거요."], ["It was an unthoughtful act in a man who has so often slept with the war-whoop ringing in the air, to let off his piece within sound of an ambushment!", "전쟁 함성이 허공에 울리는 가운데서 그토록 자주 잠들곤 한 사람이, 매복이 들리는 거리 안에서 총을 쏘아 버리다니 분별없는 짓이었소! 하긴 그건 자연스러운 유혹이긴 했소만!"], ["But then it was a natural temptation!", "참으로 자연스러웠소이다!"], ["'twas very natural!", "자, 친구들, 우리 자리를 옮깁시다."], ["Come, friends, let us move our station, and in such a fashion, too, as will throw the cunning of a Mingo on a wrong scent, or our scalps will be drying in the wind in front of Montcalm's marquee, ag'in this hour to-morrow.\"", "그것도 〈밍고〉의 교활함을 엉뚱한 냄새 자취로 끌어들일 만한 방식으로 말이오. 안 그러면 내일 이맘때면 우리 머리가죽이 〈몽칼름〉의 천막 앞에서 바람에 말려지고 있을 테니.\""]], [["This appalling declaration, which the scout uttered with the cool assurance of a man who fully comprehended, while he did not fear to face the danger, served to remind Heyward of the importance of the charge with which he himself had been intrusted.", "이 섬뜩한 선언은, 위험을 똑똑히 헤아리면서도 그것을 마주하기를 두려워하지 않는 사람의 냉철한 확신으로 정찰꾼이 내뱉은 것이었으니, 〈헤이워드〉에게 제 자신이 떠맡은 책무의 중대함을 일깨우는 구실을 했다."], ["Glancing his eyes around, with a vain effort to pierce the gloom that was thickening beneath the leafy arches of the forest, he felt as if, cut off from human aid, his unresisting companions would soon lie at the entire mercy of those barbarous enemies, who, like beasts of prey, only waited till the gathering darkness might render their blows more fatally certain.", "숲의 잎 우거진 아치 아래로 짙어 가는 어둠을 꿰뚫어 보려 헛되이 애쓰며 사방으로 눈길을 던지자, 그는 인간의 도움에서 끊긴 채, 저항조차 못 하는 동행들이 머지않아 저 야만적인 적들의 처분에 온전히 내맡겨지리라는 느낌이 들었으니, 그 적들은 맹수처럼, 짙어 가는 어둠이 제 일격을 더 치명적으로 확실하게 해 줄 때까지 그저 기다리고 있을 따름이었다."], ["His awakened imagination, deluded by the deceptive light, converted each waving bush, or the fragment of some fallen tree, into human forms, and twenty times he fancied he could distinguish the horrid visages of his lurking foes, peering from their hiding-places, in never-ceasing watchfulness of the movements of his party.", "깨어난 그의 상상력은 속임수 같은 빛에 홀려, 흔들리는 덤불이며 쓰러진 나무토막마다를 사람의 형상으로 바꾸어 놓았고, 그는 스무 번이고, 숨어 있는 적들의 끔찍한 얼굴이 은신처에서 내다보며 끊임없는 경계로 제 일행의 움직임을 살피는 것을 분간할 수 있다고 여겼다."], ["Looking upward, he found that the thin fleecy clouds, which evening had painted on the blue sky, were already losing their faintest tints of rose-color, while the imbedded stream, which glided past the spot where he stood, was to be traced only by the dark boundary of its wooded banks.", "위를 올려다보니, 저녁이 푸른 하늘에 그려 놓았던 얇고 양털 같은 구름은 이미 그 희미하던 장밋빛마저 잃어 가고 있었고, 그가 선 자리를 스쳐 흐르는 깊숙이 박힌 시냇물은 오직 그 숲 우거진 기슭의 어두운 경계로만 더듬을 수 있을 따름이었다."]], [["\"What is to be done?\"", "\"어찌하면 좋겠소?\""], ["he said, feeling the utter helplessness of doubt in such a pressing strait; \"desert me not, for God's sake!", "이토록 절박한 곤경에서 망설임이 얼마나 무력한지 절감하며 그가 말했다. \"제발, 나를 저버리지 마시오!"], ["remain to defend those I escort, and freely name your own reward!\"", "남아서 내가 호위하는 이들을 지켜 주오. 그리고 보답이라면 무엇이든 마음대로 말하시오!\""]], [["His companions, who conversed apart in the language of their tribe, heeded not this sudden and earnest appeal.", "제 부족의 말로 따로 떨어져 이야기를 나누던 동행들은 이 갑작스럽고 간절한 호소에 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["Though their dialogue was maintained in low and cautious sounds, but little above a whisper, Heyward, who now approached, could easily distinguish the earnest tones of the younger warrior from the more deliberate speeches of his seniors.", "그들의 대화는 속삭임보다 그리 높지 않은, 나직하고 조심스러운 소리로 이어졌으나, 이제 다가선 〈헤이워드〉는 젊은 전사의 간절한 어조를 손위 사람들의 보다 신중한 말투와 어렵잖게 분간할 수 있었다."], ["It was evident that they debated on the propriety of some measure that nearly concerned the welfare of the travellers.", "그들이 여행자들의 안위에 깊이 관계되는 어떤 방책의 타당성을 두고 논하고 있음은 분명했다."], ["Yielding to his powerful interest in the subject, and impatient of a delay that seemed fraught with so much additional danger, Heyward drew still nigher to the dusky group, with an intention of making his offers of compensation more definite, when the white man, motioning, with his hand, as if he conceded the disputed point, turned away, saying in a sort of soliloquy, and in the English tongue,--", "그 사안에 품은 강한 관심에 못 이기고, 또 그토록 큰 위험을 더 보태는 듯한 지체에 조바심이 난 〈헤이워드〉는, 보상의 제안을 더 분명히 하려는 뜻으로 그 어둑한 무리에게 한층 가까이 다가섰는데, 마침 그때 백인이, 다투던 쟁점을 양보하기라도 하듯 손짓을 하며 몸을 돌리더니, 혼잣말처럼, 그것도 영어로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"Uncas is right!", "\"〈언카스〉의 말이 옳소!"], ["it would not be the act of men to leave such harmless things to their fate, even though it breaks up the harboring place forever.", "이 은신처를 영영 못 쓰게 만드는 한이 있더라도, 이토록 무해한 것들을 운명에 내맡기는 것은 사내가 할 짓이 아니오."], ["If you would save these tender blossoms from the fangs of the worst of serpents, gentleman, you have neither time to lose nor resolution to throw away!\"", "이 여린 꽃들을 더없이 고약한 뱀의 송곳니에서 구하시려거든, 나리, 허비할 시간도, 내던질 결단도 없소이다!\""]], [["\"How can such a wish be doubted!", "\"어찌 그런 바람을 의심할 수 있겠소!"], ["have I not already offered--\"", "내가 이미 약속하지 않았소—\""]], [["\"Offer your prayers to Him who can give us wisdom to circumvent the cunning of the devils who fill these woods,\" calmly interrupted the scout, \"but spare your offers of money, which neither you may live to realize, nor I to profit by. These Mohicans and I will do what man's thoughts can invent, to keep such flowers, which, though so sweet, were never made for the wilderness, from harm, and that without hope of any other recompense but such as God always gives to upright dealings. First, you must promise two things, both in your own name and for your friends, or without serving you, we shall only injure ourselves!\"", "\"이 숲을 가득 메운 악마들의 교활함을 따돌릴 지혜를 주실 수 있는 분께 기도나 드리시오.\" 정찰꾼이 차분히 말을 가로막았다. \"허나 돈을 주겠다는 제안일랑 거두시오. 당신이 살아서 그걸 내줄 수 있을지도, 내가 살아서 그 덕을 볼지도 모르는 일이니. 이 〈모히칸〉들과 나는, 비록 향기로우나 결코 황야를 위해 빚어진 적이 없는 이런 꽃들을 화에서 지키고자 사람의 머리로 생각해 낼 수 있는 일은 다 할 것이오. 그것도, 올바른 처사에 늘 내리시는 하느님의 보답 말고는 다른 어떤 대가도 바라지 않고서 말이오. 우선, 당신은 두 가지를 약속해야 하오. 당신 자신의 이름으로, 또 당신의 벗들을 위하여. 안 그러면 당신을 돕기는커녕 우리 자신만 해치는 꼴이 될 테니!\""]], [["\"Name them.\"", "\"무엇인지 말하시오.\""]], [["\"The one is, to be still as these sleeping woods, let what will happen; and the other is, to keep the place where we shall take you, forever a secret from all mortal men.\"", "\"하나는, 무슨 일이 일어나든 이 잠든 숲처럼 고요히 있는 것이오. 다른 하나는, 우리가 당신을 데려갈 곳을 모든 사람에게 영원히 비밀로 지키는 것이오.\""]], [["\"I will do my utmost to see both these conditions fulfilled.\"", "\"그 두 조건이 다 지켜지도록 내 힘껏 애쓰리다.\""]], [["\"Then follow, for we are losing moments that are as precious as the heart's blood to a stricken deer!\"", "\"그럼 따라오시오. 화살 맞은 사슴의 심장 피만큼이나 귀한 순간들을 우리가 흘려보내고 있으니!\""]], [["Heyward could distinguish the impatient gesture of the scout, through the increasing shadows of the evening, and he moved in his footsteps, swiftly, towards the place where he had left the remainder of his party.", "〈헤이워드〉는 짙어 가는 저녁 그림자 사이로 정찰꾼의 조바심 어린 몸짓을 분간할 수 있었고, 그의 발자취를 좇아, 제 일행의 나머지를 두고 온 자리로 잰걸음을 옮겼다."], ["When they rejoined the expecting and anxious females, he briefly acquainted them with the conditions of their new guide, and with the necessity that existed for their hushing every apprehension, in instant and serious exertions.", "기다리며 애태우던 여인들과 다시 합류하자, 그는 새 길잡이가 내건 조건과, 더불어 모든 두려움을 가라앉히고 곧장 진지하게 힘을 모아야 할 필요를 그녀들에게 간략히 알렸다."], ["Although his alarming communication was not received without much secret terror by the listeners, his earnest and impressive manner, aided perhaps by the nature of the danger, succeeded in bracing their nerves to undergo some unlooked-for and unusual trial.", "그의 놀라운 전갈이 듣는 이들에게 적잖은 은밀한 공포 없이 받아들여지지는 않았으나, 그의 간절하고 절절한 태도는, 어쩌면 위험의 성격에 힘입어, 마침내 그녀들의 신경을 다잡아 예기치 못한 보기 드문 시련을 견디게 하는 데 성공했다."], ["Silently, and without a moment's delay, they permitted him to assist them from their saddles, when they descended quickly to the water's edge, where the scout had collected the rest of the party, more by the agency of expressive gestures than by any use of words.", "그녀들은 잠자코, 한순간도 지체 없이, 그가 안장에서 내려 주도록 몸을 맡겼고, 이내 물가로 재빨리 내려갔으니, 그곳에는 정찰꾼이, 말보다는 표정 풍부한 몸짓의 힘으로 나머지 일행을 모아 둔 터였다."]], [["\"What to do with these dumb creatures!\"", "\"이 말 못 하는 짐승들을 어찌한다?\""], ["muttered the white man, on whom the sole control of their future movements appeared to devolve; \"it would be time lost to cut their throats, and cast them into the river; and to leave them here, would be to tell the Mingos that they have not far to seek to find their owners!\"", "앞으로의 움직임에 대한 단독 지휘권이 떠맡겨진 듯한 백인이 중얼거렸다. \"그 목을 베어 강에 던지자니 시간만 허비할 노릇이요, 여기 그냥 두자니 〈밍고〉들에게 그 임자를 찾으려면 멀리 갈 것 없다고 일러 주는 꼴이 될 테고!\""]], [["\"Then give them their bridles, and let them range the woods,\"", "\"그럼 고삐를 풀어 숲을 마음껏 돌아다니게 둡시다.\""], ["Heyward ventured to suggest.", "〈헤이워드〉가 조심스레 제안해 보았다."]], [["\"No; it would be better to mislead the imps, and make them believe they must equal a horse's speed to run down their chase.", "\"아니오. 그 악마들을 헷갈리게 해서, 제 사냥감을 따라잡으려면 말의 속도에 견주어야 한다고 믿게 만드는 편이 낫소."], ["Ay, ay, that will blind their fire-balls of eyes!", "옳지, 옳지, 그러면 그 불덩이 같은 눈깔을 멀게 만들 거요!"], ["Chingach--Hist?", "〈칭가츠〉— 쉿!"], ["what stirs the bush?\"", "무엇이 덤불을 흔드는 거지?\""]], [["\"The colt.\"", "\"망아지요.\""]], [["\"That colt, at least, must die,\"", "\"적어도 저 망아지만은 죽여야겠소.\""], ["muttered the scout, grasping the mane of the nimble beast, which easily eluded his hand; \"Uncas, your arrows!\"", "정찰꾼이, 제 손길을 가뿐히 피하는 날랜 짐승의 갈기를 움켜쥐며 중얼거렸다. \"〈언카스〉, 자네 화살을!\""]], [["\"Hold!\"", "\"멈추시오!\""], ["exclaimed the proprietor of the condemned animal, aloud, without regard to the whispering tones used by the others; \"spare the foal of Miriam!", "사형 선고를 받은 짐승의 임자가, 다른 이들이 쓰던 속삭이는 어조 따위는 아랑곳없이 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"〈미리암〉의 새끼를 살려 주오!"], ["it is the comely offspring of a faithful dam, and would willingly injure naught.\"", "충직한 어미가 낳은 잘생긴 새끼로, 무엇 하나 기꺼이 해치려 들지 않는 놈이오.\""]], [["\"When men struggle for the single life God has given them,\"", "\"사람이 하느님께서 주신 단 하나의 목숨을 두고 발버둥 칠 때에는,\""], ["said the scout sternly, \"even their own kind seem no more than the beasts of the wood.", "정찰꾼이 준엄하게 말했다. \"제 동족조차도 숲의 짐승과 다를 바 없어 보이는 법이오."], ["If you speak again, I shall leave you to the mercy of the Maquas!", "한 번 더 입을 열면, 당신을 〈마콰〉의 처분에 맡기고 떠나겠소!"], ["Draw to your arrow's head, Uncas; we have no time for second blows.\"", "화살을 끝까지 당기게, 〈언카스〉. 두 번째 일격을 가할 틈이 없으니.\""]], [["The low, muttering sounds of his threatening voice were still audible, when the wounded foal, first rearing on its hinder legs, plunged forward to its knees.", "위협 어린 그의 목소리가 나직이 웅얼대는 소리로 아직 귀에 들리는 가운데, 부상당한 망아지가 먼저 뒷다리로 곧추서더니 앞으로 고꾸라지며 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["It was met by Chingachgook, whose knife passed across its throat quicker than thought, and then precipitating the motions of the struggling victim, he dashed it into the river, down whose stream it glided away, gasping audibly for breath with its ebbing life.", "그 짐승을 맞이한 것은 〈칭가츠국〉이었으니, 그의 칼이 생각보다 빠르게 그 목을 가로 그었고, 이어 발버둥 치는 제물의 움직임을 재촉하며, 그는 그것을 강물 속으로 처박았다. 망아지는 그 물줄기를 따라 떠내려가며, 꺼져 가는 목숨과 함께 가쁜 숨을 또렷이 헐떡였다."], ["This deed of apparent cruelty, but of real necessity, fell upon the spirits of the travellers like a terrific warning of the peril in which they stood, heightened as it was by the calm though steady resolution of the actors in the scene.", "겉으로는 잔혹하나 실은 부득이한 이 행위는, 그 장면 속 행위자들의 차분하면서도 흔들림 없는 결연함에 한층 더해져, 여행자들의 마음에 그들이 처한 위험을 알리는 무시무시한 경고처럼 내려앉았다."], ["The sisters shuddered and clung closer to each other, while Heyward instinctively laid his hand on one of the pistols he had just drawn from their holsters, as he placed himself between his charge and those dense shadows that seemed to draw an impenetrable veil before the bosom of the forest.", "자매는 몸서리치며 서로에게 더욱 바싹 매달렸고, 〈헤이워드〉는 본능적으로, 방금 권총집에서 뽑아 든 권총 한 자루에 손을 얹은 채, 제가 지키는 이들과, 숲의 가슴 앞에 뚫을 수 없는 장막을 드리운 듯한 저 짙은 그림자들 사이에 몸을 세웠다."]], [["The Indians, however, hesitated not a moment, but taking the bridles, they led the frightened and reluctant horses into the bed of the river.", "그러나 인디언들은 한순간도 머뭇거리지 않고, 고삐를 잡아 겁먹고 내키지 않아 하는 말들을 강바닥으로 이끌었다."]], [["At a short distance from the shore they turned, and were soon concealed by the projection of the bank, under the brow of which they moved, in a direction opposite to the course of the waters.", "물가에서 얼마 떨어지지 않은 곳에서 그들은 방향을 틀었고, 이내 기슭의 돌출부에 가려 보이지 않게 되었으니, 그 벼랑 끝 아래로 물길의 흐름과는 반대 방향으로 나아갔다."], ["In the meantime, the scout drew a canoe of bark from its place of concealment beneath some low bushes, whose branches were waving with the eddies of the current, into which he silently motioned for the females to enter.", "그사이 정찰꾼은, 가지가 물살의 소용돌이에 일렁이는 낮은 덤불 밑 은신처에서 자작나무 껍질로 만든 카누 한 척을 끌어내었고, 거기에 타라고 잠자코 여인들에게 손짓했다."], ["They complied without hesitation, though many a fearful and anxious glance was thrown behind them towards the thickening gloom which now lay like a dark barrier along the margin of the stream.", "그녀들은 망설임 없이 그 말을 따랐으나, 이제 시냇가를 따라 어두운 장벽처럼 누운 짙어 가는 어둠을 향해, 두려움과 근심에 찬 눈길을 여러 번 뒤로 던졌다."]], [["So soon as Cora and Alice were seated, the scout, without regarding the element, directed Heyward to support one side of the frail vessel, and posting himself at the other, they bore it up against the stream, followed by the dejected owner of the dead foal.", "〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉가 자리에 앉기가 무섭게, 정찰꾼은 물 따위는 아랑곳하지 않고 〈헤이워드〉에게 그 연약한 배의 한쪽을 받치라 이르고는, 제 자신은 다른 쪽에 자리를 잡아, 둘이서 물살을 거슬러 배를 떠받쳤으니, 죽은 망아지의 낙담한 임자가 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["In this manner they proceeded, for many rods, in a silence that was only interrupted by the rippling of the water, as its eddies played around them, or the low dash made by their own cautious footsteps.", "이런 식으로 그들은 여러 로드를, 소용돌이가 둘레에서 노닐며 내는 물결 소리나, 그들 자신의 조심스러운 발걸음이 내는 나직한 물장구 소리 말고는 아무것도 깨뜨리지 못하는 고요 속에서 나아갔다."], ["Heyward yielded the guidance of the canoe implicitly to the scout, who approached or receded from the shore, to avoid the fragments of rocks, or deeper parts of the river, with a readiness that showed his knowledge of the route they held.", "〈헤이워드〉는 카누의 길잡이 노릇을 정찰꾼에게 고스란히 내맡겼으니, 그는 바위 조각이나 강의 더 깊은 곳을 피하려 기슭에 다가서거나 물러서며, 자신들이 가는 길에 훤한 사람임을 드러내는 능숙함을 보였다."], ["Occasionally he would stop; and in the midst of a breathing stillness, that the dull but increasing roar of the waterfall only served to render more impressive, he would listen with painful intenseness, to catch any sounds that might arise from the slumbering forest.", "이따금 그는 멈추어 섰고, 폭포의 둔중하나 점점 커져 가는 굉음이 도리어 한층 사무치게 만드는 숨죽인 정적 한가운데서, 잠든 숲에서 새어 나올 법한 어떤 소리라도 잡아내려 사무치도록 긴장한 채 귀를 기울였다."], ["When assured that all was still, and unable to detect, even by the aid of his practised senses, any sign of his approaching foes, he would deliberately resume his slow and unguarded progress.", "모든 것이 고요하다고, 또 그 단련된 감각의 힘으로도 다가오는 적의 어떤 기척도 알아챌 수 없다고 확신하면, 그는 신중하게 그 느리고 무방비한 전진을 다시 이어 갔다."], ["At length they reached a point in the river, where the roving eye of Heyward became riveted on a cluster of black objects, collected at a spot where the high bank threw a deeper shadow than usual on the dark waters.", "마침내 그들은 강의 한 지점에 이르렀는데, 그곳에서 〈헤이워드〉의 두리번거리던 눈은 한 무리의 검은 물체에 못 박혔으니, 높은 기슭이 어두운 물 위로 여느 때보다 더 짙은 그림자를 드리운 자리에 한데 모여 있었다."], ["Hesitating to advance, he pointed out the place to the attention of his companion.", "나아가기를 망설이며, 그는 그곳을 동행의 주의로 가리켜 보였다."]], [["\"Ay,\"", "\"옳지,\""], ["returned the composed scout, \"the Indians have hid the beasts with the judgment of natives!", "침착한 정찰꾼이 대꾸했다. \"인디언들이 원주민다운 분별로 짐승들을 숨겨 놓았소!"], ["Water leaves no trail, and an owl's eyes would be blinded by the darkness of such a hole.\"", "물은 자취를 남기지 않으니, 부엉이의 눈깔이라도 저런 굴의 어둠에는 멀어 버릴 거요.\""]], [["The whole party was soon reunited, and another consultation was held between the scout and his new comrades, during which, they whose fates depended on the faith and ingenuity of these unknown foresters, had a little leisure to observe their situation more minutely.", "일행 전체가 곧 다시 한자리에 모였고, 정찰꾼과 그의 새 동료들 사이에 또 한 차례 의논이 오갔으니, 그동안, 이 알지 못하는 사냥꾼들의 신의와 기지에 운명이 달린 이들은, 제 처지를 좀 더 자세히 살필 짧은 여유를 얻었다."]], [["The river was confined between high and cragged rocks, one of which impended above the spot where the canoe rested.", "강은 높고 험준한 바위들 사이에 갇혀 있었는데, 그중 하나는 카누가 멎은 자리 위로 우뚝 드리워져 있었다."], ["As these, again, were surmounted by tall trees, which appeared to totter on the brows of the precipice, it gave the stream the appearance of running through a deep and narrow dell.", "이 바위들은 또다시, 벼랑 끝에서 흔들거리는 듯한 키 큰 나무들에 덮여 있었으니, 강물이 깊고 좁은 골짜기를 가로질러 흐르는 듯 보였다."], ["All beneath the fantastic limbs and ragged tree-tops, which were, here and there, dimly painted against the starry zenith, lay alike in shadowed obscurity.", "별 총총한 천정을 배경으로 여기저기 어슴푸레 그려진 그 기괴한 가지들과 너덜너덜한 우듬지 아래로는, 죄다 그림자에 잠긴 어둠 속에 똑같이 누워 있었다."], ["Behind them, the curvature of the banks soon bounded the view, by the same dark and wooded outline; but in front, and apparently at no great distance, the water seemed piled against the heavens, whence it tumbled into caverns, out of which issued those sullen sounds that had loaded the evening atmosphere.", "그들 뒤로는, 기슭의 굽이가 이내 똑같이 어둡고 숲 우거진 윤곽으로 시야를 가로막았으나, 앞으로는, 보아하니 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서, 물이 하늘을 향해 쌓여 올라간 듯하더니 거기서 동굴 속으로 곤두박질쳤고, 그 동굴에서 저녁 공기를 짓누르던 그 음울한 소리가 새어 나왔다."], ["It seemed, in truth, to be a spot devoted to seclusion, and the sisters imbibed a soothing impression of security, as they gazed upon its romantic, though not unappalling beauties.", "그곳은 참으로 은둔에 바쳐진 자리인 듯했고, 자매는 비록 섬뜩하지 않은 것은 아니나 낭만적인 그 아름다움을 바라보며 안도의 마음을 들이마셨다."], ["A general movement among their conductors, however, soon recalled them from a contemplation of the wild charms that night had assisted to lend the place, to a painful sense of their real peril.", "그러나 길잡이들 사이에 일어난 한 차례의 움직임이, 밤이 그곳에 더해 준 거친 매혹에 대한 사색에서 그녀들을 이내 불러내어, 제 실제 위험에 대한 쓰라린 자각으로 되돌려 놓았다."]], [["The horses had been secured to some scattered shrubs that grew in the fissures of the rocks, where, standing in the water, they were left to pass the night.", "말들은 바위 틈새에 자란 흩어진 떨기나무 몇 그루에 매여, 물속에 선 채로 밤을 나도록 남겨졌다."], ["The scout directed Heyward and his disconsolate fellow-travellers to seat themselves in the forward end of the canoe, and took possession of the other himself, as erect and steady as if he floated in a vessel of much firmer materials.", "정찰꾼은 〈헤이워드〉와 그 시름에 잠긴 길동무들에게 카누의 앞쪽 끝에 앉으라 이른 다음, 제 자신은 다른 쪽 끝을 차지했으니, 마치 훨씬 단단한 재료로 지은 배에 떠 있기라도 한 듯 꼿꼿하고 흔들림이 없었다."], ["The Indians warily retraced their steps towards the place they had left, when the scout, placing his pole against a rock, by a powerful shove, sent his frail bark directly into the centre of the turbulent stream.", "인디언들이 떠나왔던 자리로 조심스레 발걸음을 되짚어 갈 무렵, 정찰꾼은 삿대를 바위에 대고 힘껏 밀어, 그 연약한 배를 사나운 물살 한복판으로 곧장 내보냈다."], ["For many minutes the struggle between the light bubble in which they floated, and the swift current, was severe and doubtful.", "여러 분 동안, 그들이 떠 있는 가벼운 거품 같은 배와 빠른 물살 사이의 다툼은 격렬했고 또 승부를 가늠하기 어려웠다."], ["Forbidden to stir even a hand, and almost afraid to breathe, lest they should expose the frail fabric to the fury of the stream, the passengers watched the glancing waters in feverish suspense.", "손 하나 까딱하는 것조차 금지당하고, 행여 그 연약한 배를 물살의 노여움에 드러낼까 두려워 숨조차 거의 쉬지 못한 채, 승객들은 열병 같은 조마조마함 속에서 번득이는 물살을 지켜보았다."], ["Twenty times they thought the whirling eddies were sweeping them to destruction, when the master-hand of their pilot would bring the bows of the canoe to stem the rapid.", "그들은 스무 번이나 휘도는 소용돌이가 자신들을 파멸로 휩쓸어 가는 줄로만 여겼는데, 그때마다 사공의 능란한 솜씨가 카누의 뱃머리를 급류 쪽으로 돌려세워 거슬러 나아가게 했다."], ["A long, a vigorous, and, as it appeared to the females, a desperate effort, closed the struggle.", "길고, 힘차며, 여인들이 보기에는 필사적이었던 한 차례의 분투가 그 다툼에 종지부를 찍었다."], ["Just as Alice veiled her eyes in horror, under the impression that they were about to be swept within the vortex at the foot of the cataract, the canoe floated, stationary, at the side of a flat rock, that lay on a level with the water.", "〈앨리스〉가 폭포 발치의 소용돌이 속으로 자신들이 휩쓸려 들어가려는 줄로 여겨 공포에 눈을 가리던 바로 그 순간, 카누는 물과 같은 높이로 놓인 평평한 바위 곁에 멎은 채 떠 있었다."]], [["\"Where are we? and what is next to be done?\"", "\"우리가 어디에 있는 거요?"], ["demanded Heyward, perceiving that the exertions of the scout had ceased.", "또 이제 무엇을 해야 하오?\" 정찰꾼의 분투가 멎은 것을 알아챈 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"You are at the foot of Glenn's,\"", "\"〈글렌〉폭포 발치에 와 있소.\" 폭포의 굉음 속이라 결과를 두려워하는 기색 없이 큰 소리로 상대가 대꾸했다."], ["returned the other, speaking aloud, without fear of consequences, within the roar of the cataract; \"and the next thing is to make a steady landing, lest the canoe upset, and you should go down again the hard road we have travelled, faster than you came up; 'tis a hard rift to stem, when the river is a little swelled; and five is an unnatural number to keep dry, in the hurry-skurry, with a little birchen bark and gum.", "\"이제 할 일은 흔들림 없이 뭍에 오르는 것이오. 안 그러면 카누가 뒤집혀, 올라온 것보다 더 빠르게 우리가 거슬러 온 그 험한 길을 도로 떠내려가게 될 테니."], ["There, go you all on the rock, and I will bring up the Mohicans with the venison.", "강물이 조금 불었을 때는 거슬러 오르기 고약한 여울이고, 자작나무 껍질과 송진 약간으로 만든 이런 배 위에서, 그 허둥지둥하는 와중에 다섯이나 되는 머릿수가 젖지 않고 버티기란 부자연스러운 노릇이오. 자, 다들 바위로 오르시오."], ["A man had better sleep without his scalp, than famish in the midst of plenty.\"", "나는 〈모히칸〉들을 사슴 고기와 함께 데려오리다. 사람이란 풍족함 한가운데서 굶어 죽느니, 차라리 머리가죽 없이 잠드는 편이 나으니까.\""]], [["His passengers gladly complied with these directions.", "승객들은 기꺼이 이 지시를 따랐다."], ["As the last foot touched the rock, the canoe whirled from its station, when the tall form of the scout was seen, for an instant, gliding above the waters, before it disappeared in the impenetrable darkness that rested on the bed of the river.", "마지막 발이 바위에 닿자, 카누는 그 자리에서 빙그르르 돌아 떠나갔고, 그 순간 정찰꾼의 큰 형체가 강바닥에 내려앉은 뚫을 수 없는 어둠 속으로 사라지기 직전, 물 위를 미끄러져 가는 것이 잠시 보였다."], ["Left by their guide, the travellers remained a few minutes in helpless ignorance, afraid even to move along the broken rocks, lest a false step should precipitate them down some one of the many deep and roaring caverns, into which the water seemed to tumble, on every side of them.", "길잡이에게 남겨진 여행자들은 무력한 막막함 속에 몇 분을 머물렀으니, 그들 사방으로 물이 곤두박질치는 듯한 그 깊고 굉음 치는 숱한 동굴 가운데 하나로 한 발만 잘못 디뎌도 곤두박질칠까 두려워, 부서진 바위 위를 따라 움직이는 것조차 겁이 났다."], ["Their suspense, however, was soon relieved; for aided by the skill of the natives, the canoe shot back into the eddy, and floated again at the side of the low rock before they thought the scout had even time to rejoin his companions.", "그러나 그들의 조마조마함은 이내 가셨으니, 원주민들의 솜씨에 힘입어 카누가 다시 소용돌이 속으로 쏜살같이 돌아와, 정찰꾼이 제 동료들과 다시 합류할 틈조차 있었을까 싶게, 낮은 바위 곁에 다시 떠 있었던 것이다."]], [["\"We are now fortified, garrisoned, and provisioned,\"", "\"이제 우리는 요새화되었고, 수비대를 갖추었으며, 군량까지 마련했으니,\""], ["cried Heyward, cheerfully, \"and may set Montcalm and his allies at defiance.", "〈헤이워드〉가 명랑하게 외쳤다. \"〈몽칼름〉과 그 동맹군쯤이야 거뜬히 맞설 수 있겠소."], ["How, now, my vigilant sentinel, can you see anything of those you call the Iroquois, on the mainland?\"", "자, 그래, 빈틈없는 우리 보초 양반, 당신이 〈이로쿼이〉라 부르는 자들이 뭍에 있는 기색이라도 보이오?\""]], [["\"I call them Iroquois, because to me every native, who speaks a foreign tongue, is accounted an enemy, though he may pretend to serve the king!", "\"내가 그들을 〈이로쿼이〉라 부르는 건, 내게는 낯선 말을 쓰는 원주민이라면 누구나, 비록 왕을 섬기는 척한다 해도, 적으로 여겨지기 때문이오!"], ["If Webb wants faith and honesty in an Indian, let him bring out the tribes of the Delawares, and send these greedy and lying Mohawks and Oneidas, with their six nations of varlets, where in nature they belong, among the French!\"", "〈웨브〉가 인디언에게서 신의와 정직을 바란다면, 〈델라웨어〉 부족들을 데려오고, 이 탐욕스럽고 거짓말 잘하는 〈모호크〉와 〈오나이다〉들을, 그 여섯 부족의 악당 떼와 함께, 마땅히 있어야 할 자리로, 곧 프랑스인들 틈으로 보내 버리라 하시오!\""]], [["\"We should then exchange a warlike for a useless friend!", "\"그렇다면 우리는 호전적인 벗을 쓸모없는 벗과 맞바꾸는 셈이 되지 않겠소!"], ["I have heard that the Delawares have laid aside the hatchet, and are content to be called women!\"", "듣자 하니 〈델라웨어〉들은 도끼를 내려놓고, 여자라 불리는 것에 만족한다던데!\""]], [["\"Ay, shame on the Hollanders[10] and Iroquois, who circumvented them by their deviltries, into such a treaty!", "\"옳거니, 제 악랄한 술수로 그들을 그런 조약에 옭아 넣은 〈네덜란드인〉들과 〈이로쿼이〉들에게 부끄러운 줄 알라지!"], ["But I have known them for twenty years, and I call him liar, that says cowardly blood runs in the veins of a Delaware.", "허나 나는 그들을 이십 년이나 알아 왔으니, 〈델라웨어〉의 핏줄에 비겁한 피가 흐른다고 말하는 자는 거짓말쟁이라 부르겠소."], ["You have driven their tribes from the sea-shore, and would now believe what their enemies say, that you may sleep at night upon an easy pillow.", "당신네가 그들의 부족을 바닷가에서 몰아내 놓고, 이제는 밤마다 편한 베개를 베고 자려고, 그 적들이 하는 말을 믿으려 드는 거요."], ["No, no; to me, every Indian who speaks a foreign tongue is an Iroquois, whether the castle[11] of his tribe be in Canada, or be in New York.\"", "아니, 아니오. 내게는, 그 부족의 본거지가 〈캐나다〉에 있든 〈뉴욕〉에 있든, 낯선 말을 쓰는 인디언이라면 누구나 〈이로쿼이〉요.\""]], [["Heyward, perceiving that the stubborn adherence of the scout to the cause of his friends the Delawares or Mohicans, for they were branches of the same numerous people, was likely to prolong a useless discussion, changed the subject.", "〈헤이워드〉는 정찰꾼이 제 벗인 〈델라웨어〉, 곧 〈모히칸〉들 — 그들은 같은 큰 부족의 갈래였으니 — 의 편을 완강히 고집하는 것이 쓸데없는 논쟁을 길게 끌 듯하다 여기고, 화제를 바꾸었다. * 본거지: 원문은 부족이 거점으로 삼은 요새 같은 마을을 뜻한다."]], [["\"Treaty or no treaty, I know full well, that your two companions are brave and cautious warriors!", "\"조약이 있든 없든, 나는 당신의 두 동행이 용맹하고 신중한 전사임을 잘 알고 있소!"], ["have they heard or seen anything of our enemies?\"", "그들이 우리 적들에 관해 무언가 듣거나 본 것이 있소?\""]], [["\"An Indian is a mortal to be felt afore he is seen,\"", "\"인디언이란 보이기에 앞서 느껴지는 인간이오.\""], ["returned the scout, ascending the rock, and throwing the deer carelessly down.", "정찰꾼이 바위로 오르며 사슴을 아무렇게나 내던지며 대꾸했다."], ["\"I trust to other signs than such as come in at the eye, when I am outlying on the trail of the Mingos.\"", "\"내가 〈밍고〉들의 자취를 좇아 바깥에 매복해 있을 때면, 눈에 들어오는 것 말고 다른 기척을 믿는 법이오.\""]], [["\"Do your ears tell you that they have traced our retreat?\"", "\"당신 귀가, 그들이 우리의 퇴로를 알아챘다고 일러 주오?\""]], [["\"I should be sorry to think they had, though this is a spot that stout courage might hold for a smart skrimmage.", "\"그렇다고 생각하긴 싫소만, 비록 여기가 굳센 용기라면 한바탕 솜씨 좋은 접전을 벌여 볼 만한 자리이긴 하오. 그래도 부인하지는 않겠소."], ["I will not deny, however, but the horses cowered when I passed them, as though they scented the wolves; and a wolf is a beast that is apt to hover about an Indian ambushment, craving the offals of the deer the savages kill.\"", "내가 곁을 지날 때 말들이 마치 늑대 냄새라도 맡은 듯 움츠러들었으니. 늑대란 인디언 매복 언저리를 맴돌며, 야만인들이 잡은 사슴의 내장 찌꺼기를 탐내는 짐승이오.\""]], [["\"You forget the buck at your feet!", "\"발치의 사슴은 잊으셨소!"], ["or, may we not owe their visit to the dead colt?", "아니면, 그들이 찾아든 까닭을 죽은 망아지 탓으로 돌려야 하지 않겠소?"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["what noise is that?\"", "저 소리는 무엇이오?\""]], [["\"Poor Miriam!\"", "\"가엾은 〈미리암〉!\""], ["murmured the stranger; \"thy foal was foreordained to become a prey to ravenous beasts!\"", "나그네가 중얼거렸다. \"네 새끼는 굶주린 짐승들의 먹이가 되도록 미리 정해져 있었구나!\""], ["Then, suddenly lifting up his voice, amid the eternal din of the waters, he sang aloud,--", "그러더니 별안간 끝없는 물의 굉음 한가운데서 목청을 높여 큰 소리로 노래했다. 이집트의 맏이를 그분께서 치셨으니,"]], [["\"First born of Egypt, smite did He, Of mankind, and of beast also; O, Egypt!", "사람이며 짐승까지도 그러하셨도다. 오, 이집트여!"], ["wonders sent 'midst thee, On Pharaoh and his servants too!\"", "네 한가운데로 보내신 이적들이여,"]], [["\"The death of the colt sits heavy on the heart of its owner,\"", "파라오와 그 종들에게도 내리셨도다! \"망아지의 죽음이 그 임자의 마음에 무겁게 얹혀 있소.\""], ["said the scout; \"but it's a good sign to see a man account upon his dumb friends.", "정찰꾼이 말했다. \"허나 사람이 제 말 못 하는 벗들을 소중히 여기는 것을 보니 좋은 징조요."], ["He has the religion of the matter, in believing what is to happen will happen; and with such a consolation, it won't be long afore he submits to the rationality of killing a four-footed beast, to save the lives of human men.", "그는 일어날 일은 일어나리라 믿으니, 그 일에 신앙을 지닌 셈이오. 또 그런 위안을 지녔으니, 사람의 목숨을 구하고자 네발짐승을 죽이는 것이 이치에 맞음을 받아들이기까지 그리 오래 걸리지 않을 거요."], ["It may be as you say,\"", "당신 말대로일지도 모르오.\""], ["he continued, reverting to the purport of Heyward's last remark; \"and the greater the reason why we should cut our steaks, and let the carcase drive down the stream, or we shall have the pack howling along the cliffs, begrudging every mouthful we swallow.", "그가 〈헤이워드〉의 마지막 말의 취지로 되돌아가며 이어 갔다. \"그러니 더더욱, 우리는 살점을 베어 내고 시체는 물줄기를 따라 떠내려가게 두어야 하오."], ["Besides, though the Delaware tongue is the same as a book to the Iroquois, the cunning varlets are quick enough at understanding the reason of a wolf's howl.\"", "안 그러면 늑대 떼가 벼랑을 따라 울부짖으며, 우리가 삼키는 한 입 한 입을 죄다 시샘할 테니. 게다가, 〈델라웨어〉의 말이 〈이로쿼이〉에게는 책 한 권과 다름없다 한들, 그 교활한 악당들은 늑대 울음소리의 까닭쯤은 알아챌 만큼 눈치가 빠르니까.\""]], [["The scout, whilst making his remarks, was busied in collecting certain necessary implements; as he concluded, he moved silently by the group of travellers, accompanied by the Mohicans, who seemed to comprehend his intentions with instinctive readiness, when the whole three disappeared in succession, seeming to vanish against the dark face of a perpendicular rock, that rose to the height of a few yards within as many feet of the water's edge.", "정찰꾼은 그렇게 말하는 동안 필요한 도구 몇 가지를 챙기느라 분주했고, 말을 마치자 〈모히칸〉들을 거느린 채 여행자들의 무리 곁을 잠자코 지나쳤으니, 그들은 본능적인 민첩함으로 그의 뜻을 알아챈 듯 보였다. 이윽고 셋은 차례차례, 물가에서 몇 피트 안쪽에 몇 야드 높이로 솟아오른 깎아지른 바위의 어두운 면을 배경으로, 마치 사라져 버리는 듯 잇따라 자취를 감추었다. 시온에서 한때 감미로이 흐르던 그 가락들을,"]], [["\"Those strains that once did sweet in Zion glide; He wales a portion with judicious care; And 'Let us worship God,' he says, with solemn air.\"", "그는 분별 있는 정성으로 한 대목을 가려 뽑아,\n엄숙한 기색으로 말하노라, \"우리 하느님을 경배하세.\""]], [["Heyward, and his female companions, witnessed this mysterious movement with secret uneasiness; for, though the conduct of the white man had hitherto been above reproach, his rude equipments, blunt address, and strong antipathies, together with the character of his silent associates, were all causes for exciting distrust in minds that had been so recently alarmed by Indian treachery.", "번스\n〈헤이워드〉와 그의 여인 일행은 이 알 수 없는 움직임을 남모를 불안 속에 지켜보았다. 비록 그 백인의 처신이 지금껏 나무랄 데 없었으나, 그의 거친 차림새와 무뚝뚝한 말투와 강한 반감, 거기에 말없는 동료들의 인상까지 더해져, 인디언의 배신에 얼마 전 놀라 떨었던 마음에 불신을 자아낼 만한 빌미가 되었던 것이다."]], [["The stranger alone disregarded the passing incidents.", "오직 그 낯선 사내만이 눈앞에서 벌어지는 일들에 무심했다."], ["He seated himself on a projection of the rocks, whence he gave no other signs of consciousness than by the struggles of his spirit, as manifested in frequent and heavy sighs.", "그는 바위의 튀어나온 곳에 자리를 잡았는데, 잦고 무거운 한숨으로 드러나는 영혼의 몸부림 말고는 의식이 깨어 있다는 다른 기색을 보이지 않았다."], ["Smothered voices were next heard, as though men called to each other in the bowels of the earth, when a sudden light flashed upon those without, and laid bare the much-prized secret of the place.", "이어 무언가 짓눌린 목소리들이 들렸으니, 마치 사람들이 땅속 깊은 데서 서로를 부르는 듯했다. 그러다 별안간 한 줄기 빛이 바깥에 있는 이들 위로 번뜩이며, 그토록 소중히 감춰져 있던 이곳의 비밀을 환히 드러냈다."]], [["At the farther extremity of a narrow, deep cavern in the rock, whose length appeared much extended by the perspective and the nature of the light by which it was seen, was seated the scout, holding a blazing knot of pine.", "바위 속에 난 좁고 깊은 동굴의 가장 안쪽 끝에는, 원근과 그것을 비추는 빛의 성질 때문에 그 길이가 한껏 늘어나 보였는데, 척후가 활활 타는 솔가지 매듭을 든 채 앉아 있었다."], ["The strong glare of the fire fell full upon his sturdy, weather-beaten countenance and forest attire, lending an air of romantic wildness to the aspect of an individual, who, seen by the sober light of day, would have exhibited the peculiarities of a man remarkable for the strangeness of his dress, the iron-like inflexibility of his frame, and the singular compound of quick, vigilant sagacity, and of exquisite simplicity, that by turns usurped the possession of his muscular features.", "불의 강렬한 빛이 그의 단단하고 풍상에 시달린 얼굴과 숲의 옷차림 위로 가득 쏟아져, 어떤 낭만적 야성을 더해 주었다. 그 사람은 차분한 대낮의 빛 아래서 보았다면, 옷차림의 기이함과 무쇠처럼 굽힐 줄 모르는 체격, 그리고 기민하고 빈틈없는 명민함과 더없이 순박한 단순함이 번갈아 가며 그 근육진 이목구비를 차지하는 그 묘한 결합으로 두드러질 인물이었으리라."], ["At a little distance in advance stood Uncas, his whole person thrown powerfully into view.", "그보다 조금 앞쪽에는 〈언카스〉가 온몸을 또렷이 드러낸 채 서 있었다."], ["The travellers anxiously regarded the upright, flexible figure of the young Mohican, graceful and unrestrained in the attitudes and movements of nature.", "여행자들은 천성의 자세와 동작에 우아하고 거침이 없는, 그 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 곧고도 유연한 자태를 근심스레 바라보았다."], ["Though his person was more than usually screened by a green and fringed hunting-shirt, like that of the white man, there was no concealment to his dark, glancing, fearless eye, alike terrible and calm; the bold outline of his high, haughty features, pure in their native red; or to the dignified elevation of his receding forehead, together with all the finest proportions of a noble head, bared to the generous scalping tuft.", "그의 몸은 백인의 것과 같은 초록빛에 술이 달린 사냥용 윗옷으로 평소보다 더 많이 가려져 있었으나, 무섭기도 하고 차분하기도 한 그 검고 번뜩이는 두려움 없는 눈은 감출 길이 없었으며, 본래의 붉은빛으로 순수한, 높고 거만한 이목구비의 대담한 윤곽도, 위엄 있게 우뚝한 뒤로 물러난 이마와, 시원스레 머리 가죽 한 줌까지 드러낸 고귀한 머리의 더없이 빼어난 균형도 가릴 길이 없었다."], ["It was the first opportunity possessed by Duncan and his companions, to view the marked lineaments of either of their Indian attendants, and each individual of the party felt relieved from a burden of doubt, as the proud and determined, though wild expression of the features of the young warrior forced itself on their notice.", "〈던컨〉과 그 일행이 두 인디언 안내자 가운데 어느 쪽이든 그 뚜렷한 이목구비를 살펴볼 수 있었던 것은 이번이 처음이었으니, 그 젊은 전사의 야성적이면서도 자랑스럽고 결연한 표정이 절로 눈에 들어오자, 일행은 저마다 의혹의 짐에서 풀려나는 듯한 안도를 느꼈다."], ["They felt it might be a being partially benighted in the vale of ignorance, but it could not be one who would willingly devote his rich natural gifts to the purposes of wanton treachery.", "그들은 이 사람이 무지의 골짜기에 어느 정도 어둠에 잠긴 존재일지언정, 그 풍부한 천부의 자질을 거리낌 없는 배신의 목적에 기꺼이 바칠 자는 결코 아니리라 여겼다."], ["The ingenuous Alice gazed at his free air and proud carriage, as she would have looked upon some precious relic of the Grecian chisel, to which life had been imparted by the intervention of a miracle; while Heyward, though accustomed to see the perfection of form which abounds among the uncorrupted natives, openly expressed his admiration at such an unblemished specimen of the noblest proportions of man.", "천진한 〈앨리스〉는 그의 자유로운 기품과 자랑스러운 자세를 바라보았으니, 그것은 마치 기적의 손길로 생명을 받은, 그리스 조각가의 끌에서 나온 어떤 귀한 유물을 바라보는 듯했다. 한편 〈헤이워드〉는 타락하지 않은 원주민들 사이에 흔한 완벽한 체형을 익히 보아 왔으면서도, 인간의 가장 고귀한 비례를 이토록 흠 없이 보여 주는 표본 앞에서는 거리낌 없이 감탄을 드러냈다."]], [["\"I could sleep in peace,\"", "\"저토록 두려움 없고 시원스러운 젊은이가 내 보초가 되어 준다면,\""], ["whispered Alice, in reply, \"with such a fearless and generous looking youth for my sentinel.", "〈앨리스〉가 속삭이듯 대답했다. \"나는 마음 놓고 잠들 수 있겠어요."], ["Surely, Duncan, those cruel murders, those terrific scenes of torture, of which we read and hear so much, are never acted in the presence of such as he!\"", "분명, 〈던컨〉, 우리가 그토록 많이 읽고 들었던 저 잔혹한 살육이며, 끔찍한 고문의 광경이며 하는 것들이, 저 사람 같은 이가 있는 데서야 결코 벌어지지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["\"This, certainly, is a rare and brilliant instance of those natural qualities, in which these peculiar people are said to excel,\"", "\"이건 분명, 이 특이한 족속이 빼어나다고 일컬어지는 그 천성의 자질을 보여 주는 드물고도 눈부신 본보기요.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["\"I agree with you, Alice, in thinking that such a front and eye were formed rather to intimidate than to deceive; but let us not practise a deception upon ourselves, by expecting any other exhibition of what we esteem virtue than according to the fashion of a savage.", "\"나도 당신과 같은 생각이오, 〈앨리스〉. 저런 이마와 눈은 속이기보다는 위압하도록 빚어진 거라고."], ["As bright examples of great qualities are but too uncommon among Christians, so are they singular and solitary with the Indians; though, for the honor of our common nature, neither are incapable of producing them.", "하지만 우리가 미덕이라 여기는 것을, 미개인의 방식이 아닌 다른 식으로 드러내 보이기를 기대함으로써, 스스로를 속이는 일은 하지 맙시다. 큰 자질의 빛나는 본보기가 기독교도 사이에서도 너무나 드문 것처럼, 인디언들 사이에서도 그것은 유별나고 외로운 것이오."], ["Let us then hope that this Mohican may not disappoint our wishes, but prove, what his looks assert him to be, a brave and constant friend.\"", "비록 우리 공통된 인간 본성의 영예를 위해 말하자면, 어느 쪽도 그런 본보기를 낳지 못할 만큼 무능하지는 않지만 말이오. 그러니 이 〈모히칸〉이 우리의 바람을 저버리지 않고, 그 생김새가 말해 주는 그대로, 용감하고 한결같은 벗임을 입증해 주기를 바랍시다.\""]], [["\"Now Major Heyward speaks as Major Heyward should,\"", "\"이제야 〈헤이워드〉 소령께서 〈헤이워드〉 소령답게 말씀하시는군요.\""], ["said Cora; \"who, that looks at this creature of nature, remembers the shade of his skin!\"", "〈코라〉가 말했다. \"이 천성의 피조물을 보면서, 그 누가 피부색의 짙고 옅음을 떠올리겠어요!\""]], [["A short, and apparently an embarrassed silence succeeded this remark, which was interrupted by the scout calling to them, aloud, to enter.", "이 말에 짧고도 다소 어색한 침묵이 뒤따랐는데, 척후가 그들에게 큰 소리로 들어오라고 부르는 바람에 그것이 끊겼다."]], [["\"This fire begins to show too bright a flame,\"", "\"이 불이 너무 밝은 불꽃을 내기 시작했소.\""], ["he continued, as they complied, \"and might light the Mingos to our undoing.", "그들이 따르는 동안 그가 말을 이었다. \"잘못하면 〈밍고〉들을 우리 파멸로 인도할 빛이 될 게요."], ["Uncas, drop the blanket, and show the knaves its dark side.", "〈언카스〉, 담요를 내려서 그 악당들에게 어두운 쪽을 보여 주거라."], ["This is not such a supper as a major of the Royal Americans has a right to expect, but I've known stout detachments of the corps glad to eat their venison raw, and without a relish too.[12] Here, you see, we have plenty of salt, and can make a quick broil.", "이게 왕립 아메리카 연대의 소령께서 누릴 권리가 있는 그런 만찬은 아니오만, 나는 이 연대의 든든한 분견대들이 사슴 고기를 날로, 그것도 양념 하나 없이 기꺼이 먹는 것을 보아 온 사람이오. 여기 보다시피, 우리한텐 소금이 넉넉하니 후딱 구워 낼 수 있소."], ["There's fresh sassafras boughs for the ladies to sit on, which may not be as proud as their my-hog-guinea chairs, but which sends up a sweeter flavor than the skin of any hog can do, be it of Guinea, or be it of any other land.", "숙녀분들께는 앉으실 싱싱한 사사프라스 가지가 있는데, 그게 그분들의 마-호그-기니 의자만큼 으리으리하진 않아도, 어떤 돼지 가죽이 풍기는 것보다 더 향긋한 냄새를 낸다오, 그게 기니의 돼지든 다른 어느 땅의 돼지든 말이오. 자, 친구, 망아지 때문에 너무 상심 마시오."], ["Come, friend, don't be mournful for the colt; 'twas an innocent thing, and had not seen much hardship.", "그건 죄 없는 짐승이었고, 모진 고생이라곤 별로 겪어 보지도 못했소. 그 죽음 덕에 그 짐승은 등이 까지고 발이 부르트는 숱한 고초를 면하게 될 게요!\""], ["Its death will save the creature many a sore back and weary foot!\"", "* 마-호그-기니: 호카니가 마호가니(mahogany)를 잘못 발음한 것이다."]], [["Uncas did as the other had directed, and when the voice of Hawkeye ceased, the roar of the cataract sounded like the rumbling of distant thunder.", "〈언카스〉는 척후가 시킨 대로 했고, 〈호크아이〉의 목소리가 그치자, 폭포의 굉음이 멀리서 울리는 천둥의 우르릉거림처럼 들려왔다."]], [["\"Are we quite safe in this cavern?\"", "\"이 동굴 안이면 우리가 정말 안전한 거요?\""], ["demanded Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."], ["\"Is there no danger of surprise?", "\"기습당할 위험은 없소?"], ["A single armed man, at its entrance, would hold us at his mercy.\"", "무장한 사내 하나가 입구에 있으면, 우린 그자의 처분에 맡겨진 신세가 될 텐데.\""]], [["A spectral-looking figure stalked from out the darkness behind the scout, and seizing a blazing brand, held it towards the farther extremity of their place of retreat.", "유령 같은 형상 하나가 척후의 등 뒤 어둠 속에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나오더니, 활활 타는 횃불을 움켜쥐고 그들의 은신처 가장 안쪽 끝을 향해 들이댔다."], ["Alice uttered a faint shriek, and even Cora rose to her feet, as this appalling object moved into the light; but a single word from Heyward calmed them, with the assurance it was only their attendant, Chingachgook, who, lifting another blanket, discovered that the cavern had two outlets.", "이 섬뜩한 물체가 불빛 속으로 들어서자 〈앨리스〉가 가냘픈 비명을 질렀고, 심지어 〈코라〉마저 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다. 그러나 〈헤이워드〉의 한마디가 그것이 그들의 안내자 〈칭가치국〉에 지나지 않는다는 확신을 주어 그들을 진정시켰다."], ["Then, holding the brand, he crossed a deep, narrow chasm in the rocks, which ran at right angles with the passage they were in, but which, unlike that, was open to the heavens, and entered another cave, answering to the description of the first, in every essential particular.", "그는 또 다른 담요를 들어 올려, 이 동굴에 출구가 둘임을 드러내 보였다. 그러고는 횃불을 든 채, 그들이 있는 통로와 직각으로 난, 바위 속의 깊고 좁은 틈을 건넜는데, 그 틈은 통로와 달리 하늘을 향해 트여 있었으며, 그는 그것을 지나 또 다른 동굴로 들어갔으니, 그곳은 모든 중요한 점에서 첫째 동굴과 똑같은 모양이었다."]], [["\"Such old foxes as Chingachgook and myself are not often caught in a burrow with one hole,\"", "\"〈칭가치국〉이나 나 같은 늙은 여우들은 구멍 하나뿐인 굴에서는 좀처럼 붙잡히지 않는 법이오.\" 〈호크아이〉가 웃으며 말했다."], ["said Hawkeye, laughing; \"you can easily see the cunning of the place--the rock is black limestone, which everybody knows is soft; it makes no uncomfortable pillow, where brush and pine wood is scarce; well, the fall was once a few yards below us, and I dare to say was, in its time, as regular and as handsome a sheet of water as any along the Hudson.", "\"이곳의 교묘함은 쉽게 알아볼 수 있을 게요. 이 바위는 검은 석회암인데, 다들 알다시피 무른 돌이오."], ["But old age is a great injury to good looks, as these sweet young ladies have yet to l'arn!", "덤불이며 솔가지가 귀한 데서는 그리 불편치 않은 베개가 되어 주지. 그건 그렇고, 이 폭포는 한때 우리보다 몇 야드 아래에 있었는데, 단언컨대 그 시절엔 〈허드슨〉강 어디에 못지않게 반듯하고 보기 좋은 물줄기였소."], ["The place is sadly changed!", "허나 늙음은 고운 외모에 큰 해를 끼치는 법이니, 이 어여쁜 젊은 숙녀분들이야 아직 배워야 할 노릇이지!"], ["These rocks are full of cracks, and in some places they are softer than at othersome, and the water has worked out deep hollows for itself, until it has fallen back, ay, some hundred feet, breaking here and wearing there, until the falls have neither shape nor consistency.\"", "이곳은 슬프게도 변하고 말았소! 이 바위들은 온통 금이 가 있고, 어떤 데는 다른 데보다 더 물러서, 물이 제 갈 길을 깊은 웅덩이로 파내고 또 파내며, 그렇게 뒤로 물러나, 그래, 몇백 피트나 물러나며, 여기서 부수고 저기서 갉아 내어, 마침내 폭포는 모양도 한결같음도 다 잃고 만 게요.\""]], [["\"In what part of them are we?\"", "\"우리가 지금 그 폭포의 어느 어름에 있는 거요?\""], ["asked Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"Why, we are nigh the spot that Providence first placed them at, but where, it seems, they were too rebellious to stay.", "\"음, 우린 섭리가 폭포를 처음 두었던 자리 가까이에 있소만, 보아하니 폭포는 너무 거역하는 성미라 거기 머물지 못했던 모양이오."], ["The rock proved softer on each side of us, and so they left the centre of the river bare and dry, first working out these two little holes for us to hide in.\"", "우리 양쪽 바위가 더 무르다는 게 드러나니, 폭포가 강 한가운데를 텅 비고 메마르게 남겨 둔 채, 우리가 숨을 이 작은 구멍 둘을 먼저 파낸 게지.\""]], [["\"We are then on an island?\"", "\"그럼 우린 섬 위에 있는 거요?\""]], [["\"Ay!", "\"그렇소!"], ["there are the falls on two sides of us, and the river above and below.", "우리 양쪽으로 폭포가 있고, 위로도 아래로도 강이 있소."], ["If you had daylight, it would be worth the trouble to step up on the height of this rock, and look at the perversity of the water.", "만일 한낮의 빛이 있다면, 이 바위 꼭대기로 올라가 물의 심술궂음을 바라볼 만한 수고를 할 가치가 있을 게요. 물이 도무지 아무 규칙도 없이 떨어지거든."], ["It falls by no rule at all; sometimes it leaps, sometimes it tumbles; there, it skips; here, it shoots; in one place 'tis white as snow, and in another 'tis green as grass; hereabouts, it pitches into deep hollows, that rumble and quake the 'arth; and hereaway, it ripples and sings like a brook, fashioning whirlpools and gulleys in the old stone, as it 'twas no harder than trodden clay.", "어떤 데선 뛰어오르고, 어떤 데선 곤두박질치고, 저기선 깡충 튀고, 여기선 쏜살같이 쏘아 대고, 한 군데선 눈처럼 희고, 또 한 군데선 풀처럼 푸르오. 이 어름에선 땅이 우르릉 떨릴 만큼 깊은 웅덩이로 처박히고, 저 어름에선 마치 밟아 다진 진흙보다 더 단단할 것도 없다는 듯, 그 오래된 돌에 소용돌이며 도랑을 빚어내며, 시냇물처럼 잔물결 일으키고 노래하오."], ["The whole design of the river seems disconcerted.", "강의 설계 전체가 흐트러진 듯하단 말이오."], ["First it runs smoothly, as if meaning to go down the descent as things were ordered; then it angles about and faces the shores; nor are there places wanting where it looks backward, as if unwilling to leave the wilderness, to mingle with the salt!", "처음엔 만물이 정해진 대로 비탈을 내려가려는 듯 매끄럽게 흐르다가, 이내 비스듬히 꺾여 기슭을 마주하고, 또 어떤 데선 마치 황야를 떠나 소금기와 섞이기 싫다는 듯 뒤를 돌아보는 데도 없지 않소!"], ["Ay, lady, the fine cobweb-looking cloth you wear at your throat, is coarse, and like a fish-net, to little spots I can show you, where the river fabricates all sorts of images, as if, having broke loose from order, it would try its hand at everything.", "그래요, 아가씨, 아가씨가 목에 두른 그 거미줄 같은 고운 천도, 강이 온갖 형상을 빚어내는, 내가 보여 줄 수 있는 작은 자리들에 비하면, 거칠고 투박해서 마치 어망 같다오. 마치 질서에서 풀려난 김에 무엇이든 한번 해 보려는 듯이 말이오."], ["And yet what does it amount to!", "그런데 그게 다 무슨 소용이오!"], ["After the water has been suffered to have its will, for a time, like a headstrong man, it is gathered together by the hand that made it, and a few rods below you may see it all, flowing on steadily towards the sea, as was foreordained from the first foundation of the 'arth!\"", "물이 한동안, 고집 센 사람처럼 제멋대로 굴도록 내버려 둔 뒤엔, 그것을 빚은 손에 의해 한데 모이고, 몇 로드 아래에선 그 모든 물이, 땅이 처음 세워지던 때부터 미리 정해진 대로, 바다를 향해 한결같이 흘러가는 것을 볼 수 있으니!\""]], [["While his auditors received a cheering assurance of the security of their place of concealment, from this untutored description of Glenn's,[13] they were much inclined to judge differently from Hawkeye, of its wild beauties.", "듣는 이들은 〈글렌〉의 이 투박한 묘사에서 자기들 은신처의 안전함에 대한 든든한 확신을 얻으면서도, 그 야성의 아름다움에 관해서는 〈호크아이〉와는 사뭇 다르게 판단하고 싶어졌다."], ["But they were not in a situation to suffer their thoughts to dwell on the charms of natural objects; and, as the scout had not found it necessary to cease his culinary labors while he spoke, unless to point out, with a broken fork, the direction of some particularly obnoxious point in the rebellious stream, they now suffered their attention to be drawn to the necessary, though more vulgar consideration of their supper.", "그러나 그들은 천연의 사물이 지닌 매력에 마음을 둘 처지가 아니었다. 그리고 척후가 말하는 동안에도 부서진 포크로 저 거역하는 물줄기의 유난히 고약한 어느 지점의 방향을 가리킬 때 말고는 굳이 요리 일손을 멈출 필요를 느끼지 못했으므로, 그들은 이제 더 속되긴 해도 꼭 필요한 일, 곧 저녁 식사에 주의를 빼앗기고 말았다."]], [["The repast, which was greatly aided by the addition of a few delicacies that Heyward had the precaution to bring with him when they left their horses, was exceedingly refreshing to the wearied party.", "이 식사는 〈헤이워드〉가 말에서 내릴 때 미리 챙겨 온 몇 가지 진미가 더해져 한결 푸짐해졌으니, 지친 일행에게는 더없이 기운을 북돋아 주었다."], ["Uncas acted as attendant to the females, performing all the little offices within his power, with a mixture of dignity and anxious grace, that served to amuse Heyward, who well knew that it was an utter innovation on the Indian customs, which forbid their warriors to descend to any menial employment, especially in favor of their women.", "〈언카스〉는 여인들의 시중을 들며, 제 능력이 닿는 온갖 자잘한 수발을 위엄과 근심 어린 우아함이 뒤섞인 태도로 들었는데, 이것이 〈헤이워드〉를 자못 흥겹게 했으니, 그는 이것이 인디언의 관습에 대한 전적인 파격임을 잘 알고 있었던 것이다. 인디언의 관습은 그들의 전사가 어떤 하인 노릇으로든, 특히 여인들을 위해 몸을 낮추는 것을 금하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["As the rites of hospitality were, however, considered sacred among them, this little departure from the dignity of manhood excited no audible comment.", "그러나 손님 접대의 예법은 그들 사이에서 신성하게 여겨졌으므로, 사내다움의 위엄에서 벗어난 이 작은 일탈은 입 밖에 낼 만한 어떤 평도 불러일으키지 않았다."], ["Had there been one there sufficiently disengaged to become a close observer, he might have fancied that the services of the young chief were not entirely impartial.", "만약 그 자리에 면밀한 관찰자가 될 만큼 한가로운 이가 하나라도 있었다면, 그는 이 젊은 추장의 시중이 결코 한결같이 공평하지만은 않다고 여겼을지도 모른다."], ["That while he tendered to Alice the gourd of sweet water and the venison in a trencher, neatly carved from the knot of the pepperidge, with sufficient courtesy, in performing the same offices to her sister, his dark eye lingered on her rich, speaking countenance.", "그가 〈앨리스〉에게는 충분한 예를 갖춰 단물이 든 표주박과 후추나무 옹이를 깔끔하게 깎아 만든 나무 접시에 담긴 사슴 고기를 건넸으나, 그 언니에게 똑같은 수발을 들 때면 그의 검은 눈이 그녀의 풍부하고 표정이 살아 있는 얼굴 위에 머물렀으니 말이다."], ["Once or twice he was compelled to speak, to command the attention of those he served.", "한두 번은 그가 시중드는 이들의 주의를 끌고자 입을 열어야 했다."], ["In such cases, he made use of English, broken and imperfect, but sufficiently intelligible, and which he rendered so mild and musical, by his deep,[14] guttural voice, that it never failed to cause both ladies to look up in admiration and astonishment.", "그럴 때면 그는 영어를 썼는데, 띄엄띄엄 서툴렀으나 충분히 알아들을 만했고, 그 깊고 후음 섞인 목소리로 어찌나 부드럽고 가락 있게 들려주었던지, 두 숙녀가 감탄과 놀라움에 절로 고개를 들어 올려다보게 만들지 않은 적이 없었다."], ["In the course of these civilities, a few sentences were exchanged, that served to establish the appearance of an amicable intercourse between the parties.", "이런 정중한 수발이 오가는 사이, 몇 마디 말이 주고받아졌으니, 그것은 양편 사이에 우호적인 교제의 외양을 세우는 데 보탬이 되었다."]], [["In the meanwhile, the gravity of Chingachgook remained immovable.", "그러는 동안에도 〈칭가치국〉의 근엄함은 미동도 없이 그대로였다."], ["He had seated himself more within the circle of light, where the frequent uneasy glances of his guests were better enabled to separate the natural expression of his face from the artificial terrors of the war-paint.", "그는 좀 더 불빛이 닿는 안쪽으로 자리를 잡고 앉았는데, 그곳에서는 손님들이 자주 보내는 불안한 곁눈질로도 그의 얼굴의 천성 그대로의 표정을, 전쟁용 칠로 꾸며진 인위적인 공포에서 한결 수월하게 가려낼 수 있었다."], ["They found a strong resemblance between father and son, with the difference that might be expected from age and hardships.", "그들은 부자 사이에 짙은 닮음이 있음을 발견했으니, 다만 나이와 모진 고생에서 비롯되었으리라 짐작할 만한 차이가 있을 뿐이었다."], ["The fierceness of his countenance now seemed to slumber, and in its place was to be seen the quiet, vacant composure, which distinguishes an Indian warrior, when his faculties are not required for any of the greater purposes of his existence.", "그의 얼굴의 사나움은 이제 잠든 듯했고, 그 자리에는, 인디언 전사가 그 능력을 자기 존재의 더 큰 어떤 목적에도 쓰지 않을 때 두드러지는, 그 조용하고 텅 빈 평정이 자리해 있었다."], ["It was, however, easy to be seen, by the occasional gleams that shot across his swarthy visage, that it was only necessary to arouse his passions, in order to give full effect to the terrific device which he had adopted to intimidate his enemies.", "그러나 그 검은 얼굴 위로 이따금 스치는 번뜩임으로 미루어, 그의 격정을 일깨우기만 하면 그가 적을 위압하려고 골라 쓴 그 무시무시한 분장이 곧장 온전한 효력을 발휘하리라는 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었다."], ["On the other hand, the quick, roving eye of the scout seldom rested.", "반면에 척후의 기민하고 두리번거리는 눈은 좀처럼 한곳에 머물지 않았다."], ["He ate and drank with an appetite that no sense of danger could disturb, but his vigilance seemed never to desert him.", "그는 어떤 위험에 대한 감각도 흩뜨릴 수 없는 식욕으로 먹고 마셨으나, 그 경계심만은 결코 그를 떠나지 않는 듯했다."], ["Twenty times the gourd or the venison was suspended before his lips, while his head was turned aside, as though he listened to some distant and distrusted sounds--a movement that never failed to recall his guests from regarding the novelties of their situation, to a recollection of the alarming reasons that had driven them to seek it.", "스무 번이나 표주박이며 사슴 고기가 그의 입술 앞에서 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 그의 머리는 한쪽으로 돌아가, 마치 멀리서 들려오는 미덥지 못한 어떤 소리에 귀 기울이는 듯했으니, 이 동작은 손님들을 제 처지의 신기함을 들여다보던 데서 끌어내어, 그곳을 찾아 들게 만든 그 놀라운 까닭을 다시금 떠올리게 하지 않은 적이 없었다."], ["As these frequent pauses were never followed by any remark, the momentary uneasiness they created quickly passed away, and for a time was forgotten.", "이 잦은 멈춤 뒤에 어떤 말도 뒤따르지 않았으므로, 그것이 자아낸 순간의 불안은 이내 사라졌고, 한동안은 잊히고 말았다."]], [["\"Come, friend,\"", "\"자, 친구.\""], ["said Hawkeye, drawing out a keg from beneath a cover of leaves, towards the close of the repast, and addressing the stranger who sat at his elbow, doing great justice to his culinary skill, \"try a little spruce; 'twill wash away all thoughts of the colt, and quicken the life in your bosom.", "식사가 끝나 갈 무렵, 〈호크아이〉가 나뭇잎으로 덮인 작은 통을 끄집어내며, 자기 옆에 앉아 그 요리 솜씨에 한껏 정성을 다해 주던 그 낯선 사내에게 말했다. \"가문비주를 좀 들어 보시오."], ["I drink to our better friendship, hoping that a little horse-flesh may leave no heartburnings atween us.", "망아지 생각일랑 죄 씻어 내고, 가슴속 생기를 북돋워 줄 게요. 우리 더 깊은 우정을 위해 한잔하리다."], ["How do you name yourself?\"", "부디 말고기 한 점쯤이 우리 사이에 아무 앙금도 남기지 않기를 바라며. 자, 그래, 그쪽 이름은 어찌 되시오?\""]], [["\"Gamut--David Gamut,\"", "\"〈개멋〉, 〈데이비드 개멋〉이라 하오.\""], ["returned the singing-master, preparing to wash down his sorrows in a powerful draught of the woodman's high-flavored and well-laced compound.", "그 성가 교사가 대답하며, 슬픔을 그 숲 사내의 향이 짙고 독한 술 한 모금에 흠뻑 씻어 내릴 채비를 했다."]], [["\"A very good name, and, I dare say, handed down from honest forefathers.", "\"아주 좋은 이름이오, 단언컨대 정직한 조상들로부터 물려받은 게지. 나는 이름을 좋아하는 사람이오, 비록 이 점에서는 기독교도의 방식이 미개인의 관습에 한참 못 미치지만 말이오."], ["I'm an admirator of names, though the Christian fashions fall far below savage customs in this particular.", "내가 아는 가장 큰 겁쟁이는 이름이 〈라이언〉이었소. 그리고 그 마누라 〈페이션스〉라는 여자는, 쫓기는 사슴이 한 로드 달려갈 짬도 안 되어, 사람을 귀가 멀도록 닦아세우곤 했지."], ["The biggest coward I ever knew was called Lyon; and his wife, Patience, would scold you out of hearing in less time than a hunted deer would run a rod.", "인디언한테는 이게 양심의 문제요. 자기를 일컫는 이름이, 대개 자기 자신과 같단 말이오."], ["With an Indian 'tis a matter of conscience; what he calls himself, he generally is--not that Chingachgook, which signifies Big Sarpent, is really a snake, big or little; but that he understands the windings and turnings of human natur', and is silent, and strikes his enemies when they least expect him.", "〈칭가치국〉이라는 게 '큰 뱀'을 뜻한다 해서, 그가 정말로 크든 작든 뱀이라는 건 아니오. 다만 그가 인간 본성의 굽이굽이를 꿰뚫고 있으며, 입이 무겁고, 적이 가장 방심한 틈에 친다는 뜻이지."], ["What may be your calling?\"", "그래, 그쪽 직업은 무엇이오?\""]], [["\"I am an unworthy instructor in the art of psalmody.\"", "\"나는 시편창의 기예를 가르치는 변변찮은 교사요.\""]], [["\"Anan!\"", "\"뭐라고?\""]], [["\"I teach singing to the youths, of the Connecticut levy.\"", "\"나는 〈코네티컷〉 징모병의 젊은이들에게 노래를 가르치오.\""]], [["\"You might be better employed.", "\"더 나은 일에 쓰일 수도 있었을 텐데."], ["The young hounds go laughing and singing too much already through the woods, when they ought not to breathe louder than a fox in his cover.", "그 젊은 사냥개들은 이미 숲을 지나며 웃고 노래하는 게 지나치다오, 굴속의 여우보다 더 크게 숨을 쉬어서도 안 될 판에 말이오."], ["Can you use the smooth bore, or handle the rifle?\"", "그쪽은 활강총을 다룰 줄 아시오, 아니면 소총은 다룰 줄 아시오?\""]], [["\"Praised be God, I have never had occasion to meddle with murderous implements!\"", "\"하느님께 감사하게도, 나는 살인의 도구에 손댈 일이 한 번도 없었소!\""]], [["\"Perhaps you understand the compass, and lay down the water-courses and mountains of the wilderness on paper, in order that they who follow may find places by their given names?\"", "\"그럼 혹 나침반을 알아서, 황야의 물길과 산을 종이에 옮겨 그릴 줄 아시오? 뒤따라오는 이들이 정해진 이름으로 길을 찾을 수 있도록 말이오?\""]], [["\"I practise no such employment.\"", "\"나는 그런 일은 하지 않소.\""]], [["\"You have a pair of legs that might make a long path seem short!", "\"긴 길도 짧아 보이게 만들 다리 한 쌍은 갖췄구려!"], ["you journey sometimes, I fancy, with tidings for the general.\"", "그쪽은 이따금 장군께 전할 소식을 들고 다니기도 하는 모양이지?\""]], [["\"Never; I follow no other than my own high vocation, which is instruction in sacred music!\"", "\"천만에. 나는 내 고귀한 천직 말고는 다른 무엇도 따르지 않소. 그건 성스러운 음악을 가르치는 일이오!\""]], [["\"'Tis a strange calling!\"", "\"참 이상한 직업이로군!\""], ["muttered Hawkeye, with an inward laugh, \"to go through life, like a catbird, mocking all the ups and downs that may happen to come out of other men's throats.", "〈호크아이〉가 속으로 웃으며 중얼거렸다. \"다른 사람들 목구멍에서 우연히 나오는 온갖 오르내림을 흉내 내면서, 잿빛 개똥지빠귀처럼 한평생을 산다니."], ["Well, friend, I suppose it is your gift, and mustn't be denied any more than if 'twas shooting, or some other better inclination.", "글쎄, 친구, 내 짐작엔 그게 그쪽의 천부의 재능인 모양이니, 사격이든 무슨 더 나은 성향이든 그런 것이나 마찬가지로, 부정해서야 안 될 노릇이겠지."], ["Let us hear what you can do in that way; 'twill be a friendly manner of saying good-night, for 'tis time that these ladies should be getting strength for a hard and a long push, in the pride of the morning, afore the Maquas are stirring!\"", "그쪽이 그 방면에서 무엇을 할 수 있는지 한번 들어 봅시다. 그건 정겹게 밤 인사를 건네는 방편이 될 게요, 〈마쿠아〉들이 채 깨어나기도 전, 이른 아침의 자랑스러운 빛 속에 이 숙녀분들이 모질고 긴 행군을 위해 기운을 차려야 할 때가 되었으니 말이오!\""]], [["\"With joyful pleasure do I consent,\"", "\"기쁘고 즐거운 마음으로 응하리다.\""], ["said David, adjusting his iron-rimmed spectacles, and producing his beloved little volume, which he immediately tendered to Alice.", "〈데이비드〉가 무쇠 테 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 그 아끼는 작은 책을 꺼내며 말했으니, 그는 그것을 곧장 〈앨리스〉에게 내밀었다."], ["\"What can be more fitting and consolatory, than to offer up evening praise, after a day of such exceeding jeopardy!\"", "\"이토록 더없는 위태로움의 하루를 보낸 뒤에, 저녁 찬미를 올려 드리는 것보다 더 어울리고 더 위안이 되는 일이 어디 있겠소!\""]], [["Alice smiled; but regarding Heyward, she blushed and hesitated.", "〈앨리스〉가 미소 지었으나, 〈헤이워드〉를 바라보고는 얼굴을 붉히며 머뭇거렸다."]], [["\"Indulge yourself,\"", "\"마음껏 하시오.\""], ["he whispered: \"ought not the suggestion of the worthy namesake of the Psalmist to have its weight at such a moment?\"", "그가 속삭였다. \"이 시편 기자와 이름이 같은 이 가상한 분의 제안이, 이런 순간에 응당 무게를 지녀야 하지 않겠소?\""]], [["Encouraged by his opinion, Alice did what her pious inclinations and her keen relish for gentle sounds, had before so strongly urged.", "그의 의견에 용기를 얻어, 〈앨리스〉는 그녀의 경건한 성향과 부드러운 음률에 대한 예민한 애호가 진작부터 그토록 강하게 권해 온 일을 했다."], ["The book was open at a hymn not ill adapted to their situation, and in which the poet, no longer goaded by his desire to excel the inspired king of Israel, had discovered some chastened and respectable powers.", "책은 그들 처지에 그리 어울리지 않지 않은 어느 찬송가에 펼쳐져 있었는데, 거기서 그 시인은 더 이상 영감을 받은 〈이스라엘〉의 왕을 능가하려는 욕망에 떠밀리지 않은 채, 다소 절제되고 점잖은 솜씨를 보여 주고 있었다."], ["Cora betrayed a disposition to support her sister, and the sacred song proceeded, after the indispensable preliminaries of the pitch-pipe and the tune had been duly attended to by the methodical David.", "〈코라〉도 동생을 거들 뜻을 내비쳤고, 꼼꼼한 〈데이비드〉가 음을 잡는 피리와 가락의 빼놓을 수 없는 사전 준비를 정성껏 마치자, 성스러운 노래가 시작되었다."]], [["The air was solemn and slow.", "가락은 엄숙하고 느렸다."], ["At times it rose to the fullest compass of the rich voices of the females, who hung over their little book in holy excitement, and again it sank so low, that the rushing of the waters ran through their melody, like a hollow accompaniment.", "때로는 그것이 두 여인의 풍부한 목소리가 다다를 수 있는 가장 넓은 음역까지 솟구쳤으니, 그들은 거룩한 흥분 속에 그 작은 책 위로 몸을 숙이고 있었으며, 또 어떤 때는 너무도 낮게 가라앉아, 물의 내달리는 소리가 그들의 선율 사이로, 마치 텅 빈 반주처럼 흘러들었다."], ["The natural taste and true ear of David governed and modified the sounds to suit the confined cavern, every crevice, and cranny of which was filled with the thrilling notes of their flexible voices.", "〈데이비드〉의 타고난 안목과 정확한 귀가 그 소리를 다스리고 다듬어 이 좁다란 동굴에 맞추니, 그 동굴의 갈라진 틈과 구석마다 그들의 유연한 목소리에서 나온 떨리는 음들로 가득 찼다."], ["The Indians riveted their eyes on the rocks, and listened with an attention that seemed to turn them into stone.", "인디언들은 두 눈을 바위에 못 박은 채, 자신들을 돌로 바꿔 놓은 듯한 골똘함으로 귀를 기울였다."], ["But the scout, who had placed his chin in his hand, with an expression of cold indifference, gradually suffered his rigid features to relax, until, as verse succeeded verse, he felt his iron nature subdued, while his recollection was carried back to boyhood, when his ears had been accustomed to listen to similar sounds of praise, in the settlements of the colony.", "그러나 차가운 무심함의 표정으로 턱을 손에 괴고 있던 척후는, 그 굳은 이목구비가 차츰 풀려, 한 절 한 절 이어지는 사이에, 자신의 무쇠 같은 천성이 누그러지는 것을 느꼈고, 그 기억은 어린 시절로 거슬러 올라갔으니, 그 무렵 그의 귀는 식민지의 정착촌에서 이와 비슷한 찬미의 소리에 귀 기울이는 데 익숙했던 것이다."], ["His roving eyes began to moisten, and before the hymn was ended, scalding tears rolled out of fountains that had long seemed dry, and followed each other down those cheeks, that had oftener felt the storms of heaven than any testimonials of weakness.", "두리번거리던 그의 눈이 젖어 들기 시작했고, 찬송이 채 끝나기도 전에, 오랫동안 말라 있는 듯했던 샘에서 뜨거운 눈물이 솟아나, 어떤 나약함의 증표보다도 하늘의 폭풍을 더 자주 견뎌 온 그 두 뺨을 따라 줄지어 굴러내렸다."], ["The singers were dwelling on one of those low, dying chords, which the ear devours with such greedy rapture, as if conscious that it is about to lose them, when a cry, that seemed neither human nor earthly, rose in the outward air, penetrating not only the recesses of the cavern, but to the inmost hearts of all who heard it.", "노래하는 이들이, 마치 곧 그것을 잃으리란 것을 의식한 듯 귀가 그토록 탐욕스러운 황홀로 삼키는, 그 낮게 잦아드는 화음들 가운데 하나에 머물러 있을 때였다. 인간의 것도 이 세상의 것도 아닌 듯한 외침 하나가 바깥 공기 속에 솟아올라, 동굴 깊은 곳뿐 아니라 그것을 들은 모든 이의 가장 깊은 가슴속까지 꿰뚫었다."], ["It was followed by a stillness apparently as deep as if the waters had been checked in their furious progress, at such a horrid and unusual interruption.", "그 뒤로는, 마치 그토록 끔찍하고 예사롭지 않은 방해에 물조차 그 사나운 내달림을 멈춘 듯, 깊디깊은 정적이 뒤따랐다."]], [["\"What is it?\"", "\"무슨 소리죠?\""], ["murmured Alice, after a few moments of terrible suspense.", "끔찍한 긴장의 몇 순간이 지난 뒤, 〈앨리스〉가 나직이 물었다."]], [["\"What is it?\"", "\"무슨 소리요?\""], ["repeated Heyward aloud.", "〈헤이워드〉가 큰 소리로 되물었다."]], [["Neither Hawkeye nor the Indians made any reply.", "〈호크아이〉도 인디언들도 아무 대답이 없었다."], ["They listened, as if expecting the sound would be repeated, with a manner that expressed their own astonishment.", "그들은 그 소리가 되풀이되기를 기다리는 듯, 그들 자신의 놀라움을 드러내는 태도로 귀를 기울였다."], ["At length they spoke together earnestly, in the Delaware language, when Uncas, passing by the inner and most concealed aperture, cautiously left the cavern.", "마침내 그들은 〈델라웨어〉 말로 진지하게 함께 이야기를 나누었고, 그러자 〈언카스〉가 안쪽의 가장 깊이 감춰진 입구를 지나 조심스레 동굴을 빠져나갔다."], ["When he had gone, the scout first spoke in English.", "그가 떠나자, 척후가 먼저 영어로 입을 열었다."]], [["\"What it is, or what it is not, none here can tell; though two of us have ranged the woods for more than thirty years!", "\"이게 무엇인지, 무엇이 아닌지, 여기 있는 누구도 말할 수 없소. 우리 둘이 삼십 년 넘게 이 숲을 누비고 다녔는데도 말이오!"], ["I did believe there was no cry that Indians or beast could make, that my ears had not heard; but this has proved that I was only a vain and conceited mortal!\"", "나는 인디언이나 짐승이 낼 수 있는 외침 가운데 내 귀가 들어 보지 못한 것은 없다고 믿어 왔소만, 이건 내가 한낱 헛되고 우쭐한 인간에 지나지 않았음을 증명해 보인 게요!\""]], [["\"Was it not, then, the shout the warriors make when they wish to intimidate their enemies?\"", "\"그럼 그건, 전사들이 적을 위압하려 할 때 내지르는 그 함성이 아니었나요?\""], ["asked Cora, who stood drawing her veil about her person, with a calmness to which her agitated sister was a stranger.", "〈코라〉가 물었으니, 그녀는 베일을 제 몸에 둘러 모으며, 동요하는 동생으로서는 알지 못할 침착함으로 서 있었다."]], [["\"No, no; this was bad, and shocking, and had a sort of unhuman sound; but when you once hear the war-whoop, you will never mistake it for anything else!", "\"아니, 아니오. 이건 고약하고 소름 끼치는, 어딘가 사람의 것이 아닌 소리였소."], ["Well, Uncas!\"", "허나 일단 전쟁의 함성을 한번 들으면, 그건 결코 다른 무엇과도 혼동하지 않을 게요!"], ["speaking in Delaware to the young chief as he re-entered, \"what see you?", "그래, 〈언카스〉!\" 그가 다시 들어온 젊은 추장에게 〈델라웨어〉 말로 말했다."], ["do our lights shine through the blankets?\"", "\"무엇이 보이더냐? 우리 불빛이 담요 사이로 새어 나가더냐?\""]], [["The answer was short, and apparently decided, being given in the same tongue.", "대답은 짧았고, 같은 말로 주어졌는데, 보아하니 단호했다."]], [["\"There is nothing to be seen without,\"", "\"바깥엔 아무것도 보이지 않는다는군.\""], ["continued Hawkeye, shaking his head in discontent; \"and our hiding-place is still in darkness!", "〈호크아이〉가 못마땅한 듯 고개를 저으며 말을 이었다. \"우리 은신처는 아직 어둠 속에 있고!"], ["Pass into the other cave, you that need it, and seek for sleep; we must be afoot long before the sun, and make the most of our time to get to Edward, while the Mingos are taking their morning nap.\"", "자, 그래야 할 분들은 다른 동굴로 가서 잠을 청하시오. 우리는 해가 뜨기 한참 전에 일어나, 〈밍고〉들이 아침잠을 자는 동안 시간을 한껏 아껴 〈에드워드〉로 가야 하오.\""]], [["Cora set the example of compliance, with a steadiness that taught the more timid Alice the necessity of obedience.", "〈코라〉가 한결같은 태도로 본을 보여 따랐으니, 그것은 더 겁 많은 〈앨리스〉에게 순순히 따라야 할 필요를 일러 주었다."], ["Before leaving the place, however, she whispered a request to Duncan that he would follow.", "그러나 그곳을 떠나기에 앞서, 그녀는 〈던컨〉에게 따라와 달라고 속삭여 청했다."], ["Uncas raised the blanket for their passage, and as the sisters turned to thank him for this act of attention, they saw the scout seated again before the dying embers, with his face resting on his hands, in a manner which showed how deeply he brooded on the unaccountable interruption which had broken up their evening devotions.", "〈언카스〉가 그들이 지날 수 있도록 담요를 들어 올렸고, 자매가 이 살뜰한 배려에 고마움을 전하려 몸을 돌렸을 때, 그들은 척후가 다시 꺼져 가는 잉걸불 앞에 앉아, 얼굴을 두 손에 묻은 모습을 보았으니, 그것은 그가 저녁 기도를 깨뜨린 그 알 수 없는 방해를 얼마나 깊이 곱씹고 있는지를 드러내 보이는 모습이었다."]], [["Heyward took with him a blazing knot, which threw a dim light through the narrow vista of their new apartment.", "〈헤이워드〉는 활활 타는 솔가지 매듭 하나를 들고 갔는데, 그것은 그들의 새로운 거처의 좁은 통로 사이로 어슴푸레한 빛을 던졌다."], ["Placing it in a favorable position, he joined the females, who now found themselves alone with him for the first time since they had left the friendly ramparts of Fort Edward.", "그것을 알맞은 자리에 두고서, 그는 여인들에게로 갔으니, 그들은 〈에드워드〉 요새의 정다운 성벽을 떠난 이래 처음으로 그와 단둘이 있게 된 셈이었다."]], [["\"Leave us not, Duncan,\"", "\"우리를 떠나지 마세요, 〈던컨〉.\""], ["said Alice; \"we cannot sleep in such a place as this, with that horrid cry still ringing in our ears!\"", "〈앨리스〉가 말했다. \"그 끔찍한 외침이 아직도 귓가에 쟁쟁한데, 이런 데서야 우리가 어떻게 잠들 수 있겠어요!\""]], [["\"First let us examine into the security of your fortress,\"", "\"먼저 당신들의 요새가 얼마나 안전한지 살펴봅시다.\""], ["he answered, \"and then we will speak of rest.\"", "그가 대답했다. \"그런 다음에 쉬는 얘기를 하지요.\""]], [["He approached the farther end of the cavern, to an outlet, which, like the others, was concealed by blankets, and removing the thick screen, breathed the fresh and reviving air from the cataract.", "그는 동굴의 가장 안쪽 끝, 다른 출구들과 마찬가지로 담요로 가려진 한 출구로 다가가, 그 두꺼운 가리개를 걷어 내고는 폭포에서 불어오는 싱그럽고 생기를 되살려 주는 공기를 들이마셨다."], ["One arm of the river flowed through a deep, narrow ravine, which its current had worn in the soft rock, directly beneath his feet, forming an effectual defence, as he believed, against any danger from that quarter; the water, a few rods above them, plunging, glancing, and sweeping along, in its most violent and broken manner.", "강의 한 줄기가, 그 물살이 무른 바위에 깎아 낸 깊고 좁은 협곡 사이로, 바로 그의 발밑을 흘러, 그가 믿기로는 그 방면에서 닥칠 어떤 위험에 대해서도 든든한 방어가 되어 주었으니, 그들로부터 몇 로드 위쪽에서 그 물은 더없이 사납고 어지럽게 곤두박질치고 번뜩이며 휩쓸려 내려갔다."]], [["\"Nature has made an impenetrable barrier on this side,\"", "\"이쪽엔 자연이 뚫을 수 없는 방벽을 세워 놓았소.\""], ["he continued, pointing down the perpendicular declivity into the dark current, before he dropped the blanket; \"and as you know that good men and true are on guard in front, I see no reason why the advice of our honest host should be disregarded.", "그는 담요를 내려놓기 전에, 깎아지른 비탈 아래 어두운 물살을 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"그리고 앞쪽에선 선량하고 미더운 사람들이 경계를 서고 있음을 당신들도 알 테니, 우리의 정직한 주인장의 충고를 거스를 까닭이 없을 것 같소."], ["I am certain Cora will join me in saying that sleep is necessary to you both.\"", "두 분 다 잠이 필요하다는 말에는 〈코라〉도 분명 나와 뜻을 같이할 거요.\""]], [["\"Cora may submit to the justice of your opinion, though she cannot put it in practise,\"", "\"〈코라〉는 당신 의견의 옳음에 따를 수는 있어도, 그것을 실행에 옮기지는 못할 것 같군요.\""], ["returned the elder sister, who had placed herself by the side of Alice, on a couch of sassafras; \"there would be other causes to chase away sleep, though we had been spared the shock of this mysterious noise.", "사사프라스로 만든 자리 위에 〈앨리스〉의 곁에 앉아 있던 언니가 대답했다. \"이 알 수 없는 소리의 충격을 면했다 한들, 잠을 쫓아낼 다른 까닭들이 있었을 거예요."], ["Ask yourself, Heyward, can daughters forget the anxiety a father must endure, whose children lodge, he knows not where or how, in such a wilderness, and in the midst of so many perils?\"", "〈헤이워드〉, 스스로에게 물어보세요. 자기 자식들이 이런 황야 속, 그토록 많은 위험 한가운데서, 어디에 어떻게 머무는지조차 알지 못하는 아버지가 견뎌야 할 그 근심을, 딸들이 잊을 수 있을까요?\""]], [["\"He is a soldier, and knows how to estimate the chances of the woods.\"", "\"그분은 군인이오, 숲의 위험을 가늠할 줄 아시지.\""]], [["\"He is a father, and cannot deny his nature.\"", "\"그분은 아버지예요, 그 본성을 부정하실 수는 없어요.\""]], [["\"How kind has he ever been to all my follies!", "\"내 온갖 철없는 짓에도 그분은 늘 얼마나 다정하셨던지!"], ["how tender and indulgent to all my wishes!\"", "내 모든 바람에 얼마나 자상하고 너그러우셨던지!\""], ["sobbed Alice.", "〈앨리스〉가 흐느꼈다."], ["\"We have been selfish, sister, in urging our visit at such hazard!\"", "\"언니, 우린 이기적이었어, 이런 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 찾아뵙겠다고 졸랐으니!\""]], [["\"I may have been rash in pressing his consent in a moment of much embarrassment, but I would have proved to him, that however others might neglect him in his strait, his children at least were faithful!\"", "\"내가 그분이 무척 곤혹스러우신 순간에 허락을 졸라 댄 게 경솔했는지도 모르지. 하지만 나는 그분께 증명해 보이고 싶었어, 남들이 그 곤경에 처하신 그분을 저버린다 해도, 적어도 그분의 자식들만은 변치 않음을 말이야!\""]], [["\"When he heard of your arrival at Edward,\"", "\"그분께서 당신들이 〈에드워드〉에 닿았다는 소식을 들으셨을 때,\" 〈헤이워드〉가 다정하게 말했다."], ["said Heyward, kindly, \"there was a powerful struggle in his bosom between fear and love; though the latter, heightened, if possible, by so long a separation, quickly prevailed.", "\"그 가슴속에선 두려움과 사랑 사이에 거센 싸움이 일었소. 비록 그토록 오랜 헤어짐으로 어쩌면 한층 더 깊어졌을 그 사랑이 이내 이겼지만 말이오."], ["'It is the spirit of my noble-minded Cora that leads them, Duncan,'", "'저 아이들을 이끄는 건 내 고결한 〈코라〉의 기개일세, 〈던컨〉,'"], ["he said, 'and I will not balk it.", "그분께서 말씀하셨소. '나는 그걸 막지 않겠네."], ["Would to God, that he who holds the honor of our royal master in his guardianship, would show but half her firmness!'\"", "우리 왕가의 군주의 명예를 그 손에 지키고 있는 자가 그 아이 굳셈의 절반만이라도 보여 준다면 오죽 좋으련만!'\""]], [["\"And did he not speak of me, Heyward?\"", "\"그래서 그분께서는 제 얘긴 안 하셨나요, 〈헤이워드〉?\""], ["demanded Alice, with jealous affection.", "〈앨리스〉가 시샘 어린 애정으로 물었다."], ["\"Surely, he forgot not altogether his little Elsie?\"", "\"분명, 그분께서 당신의 어린 〈엘시〉를 아주 잊지는 않으셨겠지요?\""]], [["\"That was impossible,\"", "\"그건 있을 수 없는 일이오.\""], ["returned the young man; \"he called you by a thousand endearing epithets, that I may not presume to use, but to the justice of which I can warmly testify.", "젊은이가 대답했다. \"그분께서는 당신을 천 가지 다정한 별명으로 부르셨는데, 내가 감히 입에 올릴 수는 없으나, 그 마땅함이야 내가 진심으로 증언할 수 있소."], ["Once, indeed, he said--\"", "정말이지 한번은 이렇게 말씀하셨소——\""]], [["Duncan ceased speaking; for while his eyes were riveted on those of Alice, who had turned towards him with the eagerness of filial affection, to catch his words, the same strong horrid cry, as before, filled the air, and rendered him mute.", "〈던컨〉이 말을 멈췄다. 그의 두 눈이 〈앨리스〉의 눈에 못 박혀 있었는데, 그녀가 자식 된 애정의 간절함으로 그의 말을 받아들이려 그를 향해 몸을 돌리던 그때, 앞서와 똑같은 그 거세고 끔찍한 외침이 공기를 채워, 그를 벙어리로 만들어 버렸기 때문이다."], ["A long, breathless silence succeeded, during which each looked at the others in fearful expectation of hearing the sound repeated.", "길고 숨 막히는 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그동안 저마다 그 소리가 되풀이되어 들릴까 두려운 기다림 속에 서로를 바라보았다."], ["At length the blanket was slowly raised, and the scout stood in the aperture with a countenance whose firmness evidently began to give way, before a mystery that seemed to threaten some danger, against which all his cunning and experience might prove of no avail.", "마침내 담요가 천천히 들어 올려지더니, 입구에 척후가 서 있었으니, 그 얼굴의 굳셈은, 그의 온갖 꾀와 경험으로도 아무 소용이 없을 듯한 어떤 위험을 들이대는 그 수수께끼 앞에서, 분명 무너지기 시작하고 있었다. 그들은 잠들지 않는다."]], [["\"They do not sleep. On yonder cliffs, a grisly band, I see them sit.\"", "저 너머 벼랑 위에, 소름 끼치는 무리,\n나는 그들이 앉아 있는 것을 본다."]], [["\"'Twould be neglecting a warning that is given for our good, to lie hid any longer,\"", "그레이\n\"이런 소리가 숲에 울려 퍼질 때 더 이상 숨어 있는다면, 우리를 위해 주어진 경고를 저버리는 일이 될 거요.\""], ["said Hawkeye, \"when such sounds are raised in the forest!", "〈호크아이〉가 말했다."], ["The gentle ones may keep close, but the Mohicans and I will watch upon the rock, where I suppose a major of the 60th would wish to keep us company.\"", "\"여린 분들은 안에 계셔도 되나, 〈모히칸〉들과 나는 저 바위 위에서 망을 보겠소. 60연대의 소령쯤이면 우리와 함께하고자 하시리라 짐작하오만.\""]], [["\"Is then our danger so pressing?\"", "\"그렇다면 우리가 처한 위험이 그토록 임박한 겁니까?\""], ["asked Cora.", "〈코라〉가 물었다."]], [["\"He who makes strange sounds, and gives them out for man's information, alone knows our danger.", "\"기이한 소리를 내어 그것으로 사람에게 알리는 그분만이 우리의 위험을 아실 따름이오."], ["I should think myself wicked, unto rebellion against his will, was I to burrow with such warnings in the air!", "이런 경고가 공중에 가득한데도 굴을 파고 들어앉는다면, 나는 그분의 뜻을 거역하는 사악한 자라 스스로를 여길 것이오!"], ["Even the weak soul who passes his days in singing, is stirred by the cry, and, as he says, is 'ready to go forth to the battle.'", "날마다 노래로 세월을 보내는 저 연약한 영혼조차 그 외침에 마음이 흔들려, 제 말마따나 '싸움터로 나아갈 채비'가 된 듯하오."], ["If 'twere only a battle, it would be a thing understood by us all, and easily managed; but I have heard that when such shrieks are atween heaven and 'arth, it betokens another sort of warfare!\"", "그저 한 차례 싸움이라면야 우리 모두가 익히 아는 일이라 손쉽게 감당할 터이나, 이런 비명이 하늘과 땅 사이에 울릴 적엔 또 다른 종류의 전쟁을 알리는 거라고 들었소!\""]], [["\"If all our reasons for fear, my friend, are confined to such as proceed from supernatural causes, we have but little occasion to be alarmed,\"", "\"우리가 두려워할 이유가 죄다 초자연적인 원인에서 비롯된 것뿐이라면, 친구여, 놀랄 까닭이 거의 없겠지요.\""], ["continued the undisturbed Cora; \"are you certain that our enemies have not invented some new and ingenious method to strike us with terror, that their conquest may become more easy?\"", "〈코라〉가 흐트러짐 없이 말을 이었다. \"우리의 적이 정복을 한결 수월하게 하려고, 우리에게 공포를 안기려 새롭고 교묘한 수법을 꾸며 낸 것은 아니라고 확신하시나요?\""]], [["\"Lady,\"", "\"아가씨.\""], ["returned the scout, solemnly, \"I have listened to all the sounds of the woods for thirty years, as a man will listen, whose life and death depend on the quickness of his ears.", "척후가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"나는 삼십 년 동안 숲의 온갖 소리에 귀를 기울여 왔소."], ["There is no whine of the panther, no whistle of the catbird, nor any invention of the devilish Mingos, that can cheat me!", "제 목숨이 귀의 빠르기에 달린 사내가 그러하듯 말이오. 표범의 울부짖음도, 캣버드의 휘파람도, 사악한 〈밍고〉들의 그 어떤 술책도 나를 속일 수는 없소!"], ["I have heard the forest moan like mortal men in their affliction; often, and again, have I listened to the wind playing its music in the branches of the girdled trees; and I have heard the lightning cracking in the air, like the snapping of blazing brush, as it spitted forth sparks and forked flames; but never have I thought that I heard more than the pleasure of Him who sported with the things of his hand.", "숲이 고통에 빠진 인간처럼 신음하는 것도 들어 보았고, 둘레의 껍질을 벗긴 나무 가지에서 바람이 제 음악을 연주하는 것도 몇 번이고 다시 들어 보았으며, 번개가 공중에서 불붙은 덤불이 탁탁 터지듯 갈라지며 불꽃과 갈라진 화염을 토해 내는 것도 들어 보았소. 허나 그 어느 것도 제 손으로 빚은 것들을 가지고 노시는 그분의 즐거움 이상의 무엇이라 여긴 적은 없었소."], ["But neither the Mohicans, nor I, who am a white man without a cross, can explain the cry just heard.", "허나 〈모히칸〉들도, 혼혈 한 점 섞이지 않은 백인인 나도, 방금 들은 그 외침만은 설명할 길이 없소."], ["We, therefore, believe it a sign given for our good.\"", "그러므로 우리는 그것을 우리를 위해 주어진 징조라 믿는 거요.\""]], [["\"It is extraordinary!\"", "\"기이한 일이군요!\""], ["said Heyward, taking his pistols from the place where he had laid them on entering; \"be it a sign of peace or a signal of war, it must be looked to.", "〈헤이워드〉가 안으로 들어오며 내려놓았던 자리에서 권총을 집어 들며 말했다. \"평화의 징조든 전쟁의 신호든, 살펴야 합니다."], ["Lead the way, my friend; I follow.\"", "앞장서시오, 친구. 내 따르리다.\""]], [["On issuing from their place of confinement, the whole party instantly experienced a grateful renovation of spirits, by exchanging the pent air of the hiding-place for the cool and invigorating atmosphere, which played around the whirlpools and pitches of the cataract.", "갇혀 있던 자리에서 나오자, 일행 모두는 답답한 은신처의 공기를 폭포의 소용돌이와 낙수 둘레에 감도는 서늘하고 생기를 돋우는 대기와 맞바꿈으로써, 단번에 기운을 되찾는 고마움을 느꼈다."], ["A heavy evening breeze swept along the surface of the river, and seemed to drive the roar of the falls into the recesses of their own caverns, whence it issued heavily and constant, like thunder rumbling beyond the distant hills.", "묵직한 저녁 산들바람이 강물의 표면을 따라 휩쓸며, 폭포의 포효를 제 동굴의 후미진 데로 몰아넣는 듯했으니, 그 소리는 멀리 언덕 너머에서 우르릉대는 천둥처럼 묵직하고 끊임없이 새어 나왔다."], ["The moon had risen, and its light was already glancing here and there on the waters above them; but the extremity of the rock where they stood still lay in shadow.", "달이 떠올라, 그 빛이 이미 그들 위쪽 물 위로 여기저기 어른거렸으나, 그들이 선 바위 끝자락은 아직 그늘 속에 잠겨 있었다."], ["With the exception of the sounds produced by the rushing waters, and an occasional breathing of the air, as it murmured past them in fitful currents, the scene was as still as night and solitude could make it.", "쏟아지는 물이 내는 소리와, 변덕스러운 흐름을 타고 곁을 속삭이며 지나는 이따금의 숨결 같은 바람을 제외하면, 그 풍경은 밤과 적막이 빚어낼 수 있는 만큼 고요했다."], ["In vain were the eyes of each individual bent along the opposite shores, in quest of some signs of life, that might explain the nature of the interruption they had heard.", "그들이 들은 그 방해의 정체를 밝혀 줄 어떤 생명의 기척이라도 찾으려, 저마다의 눈길이 맞은편 기슭을 따라 뻗었으나 헛수고였다."], ["Their anxious and eager looks were baffled by the deceptive light, or rested only on naked rocks, and straight and immovable trees.", "근심에 차고 간절한 그들의 눈길은 속이는 빛에 농락당하거나, 헐벗은 바위와 곧고 미동도 없는 나무들에만 머물 뿐이었다."]], [["\"There is nothing to be seen but the gloom and quiet of a lovely evening,\"", "\"사랑스러운 저녁의 어둠과 고요 말고는 보이는 게 아무것도 없군요.\""], ["whispered Duncan: \"how much should we prize such a scene, and all this breathing solitude, at any other moment, Cora!", "〈덩컨〉이 속삭였다. \"다른 때였다면 우리가 이런 풍경을, 숨 쉬는 듯한 이 모든 적막을 얼마나 값지게 여겼겠습니까, 〈코라〉!"], ["Fancy yourselves in security and what now, perhaps, increases your terror, may be made conducive to enjoyment--\"", "안전하다고 상상해 보십시오. 그러면 지금은 당신의 공포를 키우는 것이, 어쩌면 즐거움으로 바뀔 수도 있을 텐데——\""]], [["\"Listen!\"", "\"들어 보세요!\""], ["interrupted Alice.", "〈앨리스〉가 말을 가로막았다."]], [["The caution was unnecessary.", "그 주의는 필요치 않았다."], ["Once more the same sound arose, as if from the bed of the river, and having broken out of the narrow bounds of the cliffs, was heard undulating through the forest, in distant and dying cadences.", "또 한 번 같은 소리가 강바닥에서 솟아나는 듯 일어나, 벼랑의 좁은 경계를 뚫고 나와, 멀리 잦아드는 가락으로 숲을 가로질러 굽이치며 들려왔다."]], [["\"Can any here give a name to such a cry?\"", "\"여기 이런 외침에 이름을 붙일 수 있는 이가 있소?\" 마지막 메아리가 숲속으로 사라지자 〈호크아이〉가 물었다."], ["demanded Hawkeye, when the last echo was lost in the woods; \"if so, let him speak; for myself, I judge it not to belong to 'arth!\"", "\"있다면, 말해 보시오. 나로서는 그것이 이 땅에 속한 것이라 보지 않소!\""]], [["\"Here, then, is one who can undeceive you,\"", "\"그렇다면, 여기 당신의 오해를 풀어 줄 사람이 있소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다."], ["said Duncan; \"I know the sound full well, for often have I heard it on the field of battle, and in situations which are frequent in a soldier's life.", "\"나는 그 소리를 아주 잘 압니다. 전쟁터에서, 그리고 군인의 삶에 흔한 여러 상황에서 자주 들어 보았으니까."], ["'Tis the horrid shriek that a horse will give in his agony; oftener drawn from him in pain, though sometimes in terror.", "그것은 말이 단말마의 고통 속에서 내지르는 끔찍한 비명이오. 흔히는 고통에서 비롯되나, 때로는 공포에서 나오기도 하지요."], ["My charger is either a prey to the beasts of the forest, or he sees his danger, without the power to avoid it.", "내 군마가 숲의 짐승들에게 먹잇감이 되었거나, 아니면 제 위험을 보면서도 피할 힘이 없는 거요."], ["The sound might deceive me in the cavern, but in the open air I know it too well to be wrong.\"", "동굴 안에서라면 그 소리에 속았을지 몰라도, 트인 바깥에서야 너무도 잘 알기에 틀릴 리 없소.\""]], [["The scout and his companions listened to this simple explanation with the interest of men who imbibe new ideas, at the same time that they get rid of old ones, which had proved disagreeable inmates.", "척후와 그의 동료들은 이 단순한 설명을, 새로운 생각을 받아들이는 동시에, 거슬리는 식객임이 드러난 묵은 생각을 떨쳐 내는 자들의 흥미로 들었다."], ["The two latter uttered their usual and expressive exclamation, \"Hugh!\" as the truth first glanced upon their minds, while the former, after a short musing pause, took upon himself to reply.", "진실이 처음 그들의 마음을 스치자, 뒤의 두 사람은 늘 하던 의미심장한 외마디 \"흥!\"을 내뱉었고, 앞의 사내는 잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤 스스로 대답을 맡았다."]], [["\"I cannot deny your words,\"", "\"그대의 말을 부인할 수는 없소.\""], ["he said; \"for I am little skilled in horses, though born where they abound.", "그가 말했다. \"말이 흔한 곳에서 태어났으되, 나는 말에 관해서는 별로 아는 게 없으니."], ["The wolves must be hovering above their heads on the bank, and the timorsome creatures are calling on man for help, in the best manner they are able.", "늑대들이 기슭에서 그것들의 머리 위를 맴돌고 있는 게 분명하고, 그 겁 많은 짐승들은 제가 할 수 있는 최선의 방식으로 사람에게 도움을 청하고 있는 거요."], ["Uncas,\"--he spoke in Delaware--\"Uncas, drop down in the canoe, and whirl a brand among the pack; or fear may do what the wolves can't get at to perform, and leave us without horses in the morning, when we shall have so much need to journey swiftly!\"", "〈웅카스〉,\"——그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 이야기했다——\"〈웅카스〉, 카누를 타고 내려가, 그 무리 한가운데로 횃불을 던져라. 안 그러면 늑대들이 못 해낼 짓을 공포가 해치워, 아침에 우리가 빠르게 길을 가야 할 마당에 말 없이 남겨 둘지도 모르니!\""]], [["The young native had already descended to the water, to comply, when a long howl was raised on the edge of the river, and was borne swiftly off into the depths of the forest, as though the beasts, of their own accord, were abandoning their prey in sudden terror.", "젊은 원주민은 명을 따르려 이미 물가로 내려가 있었는데, 그때 길고 긴 울부짖음이 강가에서 일어나, 마치 짐승들이 제 발로, 갑작스러운 공포에 사로잡혀 먹잇감을 버리는 듯, 숲 깊은 데로 빠르게 실려 갔다."], ["Uncas, with instinctive quickness, receded, and the three foresters held another of their low, earnest conferences.", "〈웅카스〉는 본능적인 민첩함으로 물러섰고, 세 삼림의 사내들은 또 한 번 나직하고 진지한 논의를 나누었다."]], [["\"We have been like hunters who have lost the points of the heavens, and from whom the sun has been hid for days,\"", "\"우리는 하늘의 방위를 잃고, 며칠 동안 해가 가려진 사냥꾼들 같았소.\""], ["said Hawkeye, turning away from his companions; \"now we begin again to know the signs of our course, and the paths are cleared from briers!", "〈호크아이〉가 동료들에게서 돌아서며 말했다. \"이제야 다시 우리가 갈 길의 징표를 알아보기 시작했고, 길에서 가시덤불이 걷혔구려!"], ["Seat yourselves in the shade which the moon throws from yonder beech--'tis thicker than that of the pines--and let us wait for that which the Lord may choose to send next.", "저 너머 너도밤나무가 달빛에 드리운 그늘에 자리를 잡으시오——소나무 그늘보다 더 짙소——그리고 주께서 다음으로 보내고자 하시는 것을 기다립시다."], ["Let all your conversation be in whispers; though it would be better, and perhaps, in the end, wiser, if each one held discourse with his own thoughts, for a time.\"", "모든 말은 속삭임으로 하시오. 허나 한동안은 저마다 제 생각과 이야기를 나누는 편이 더 낫고, 어쩌면 끝내 더 현명한 일일 거요.\""]], [["The manner of the scout was seriously impressive, though no longer distinguished by any signs of unmanly apprehension.", "척후의 태도는 진지하게 사람의 마음을 울렸으나, 더는 사내답지 못한 두려움의 기색이라곤 보이지 않았다."], ["It was evident that his momentary weakness had vanished with the explanation of a mystery which his own experience had not served to fathom; and though he now felt all the realities of their actual condition, that he was prepared to meet them with the energy of his hardy nature.", "제 경험으로도 헤아릴 수 없었던 수수께끼가 풀리자 그 한순간의 약함은 사라졌음이 분명했고, 이제 그들이 처한 실제 상황의 온갖 현실을 절감하면서도, 그 강인한 천성의 기개로 그것에 맞설 채비가 되어 있음이 또한 분명했다."], ["This feeling seemed also common to the natives, who placed themselves in positions which commanded a full view of both shores, while their own persons were effectually concealed from observation.", "이 마음가짐은 원주민들에게도 공통된 듯했으니, 그들은 양쪽 기슭을 두루 굽어볼 수 있으면서도 제 몸은 남의 눈에 효과적으로 가려지는 자리에 들어앉았다."], ["In such circumstances, common prudence dictated that Heyward and his companions should imitate a caution that proceeded from so intelligent a source.", "이런 처지에서, 그토록 명민한 데서 비롯된 신중함을 〈헤이워드〉와 그 일행이 본받는 것은 마땅한 분별의 명령이었다."], ["The young man drew a pile of the sassafras from the cave, and placing it in the chasm which separated the two caverns, it was occupied by the sisters, who were thus protected by the rocks from any missiles, while their anxiety was relieved by the assurance that no danger could approach without a warning.", "청년은 동굴에서 사사프라스 한 무더기를 끌어내, 두 동굴을 가르는 틈에 깔아 두니, 자매가 그 자리를 차지하여, 그리하여 어떤 날아오는 물체로부터도 바위의 보호를 받게 되었고, 한편 어떤 위험도 경고 없이는 다가올 수 없다는 확신으로 그들의 근심도 덜어졌다."], ["Heyward himself was posted at hand, so near that he might communicate with his companions without raising his voice to a dangerous elevation, while David, in imitation of the woodsmen, bestowed his person in such a manner among the fissures of the rocks, that his ungainly limbs were no longer offensive to the eye.", "〈헤이워드〉 자신은 가까이 자리를 잡았으니, 위험할 만큼 목소리를 높이지 않고도 동료들과 연락할 수 있을 만큼 바투였고, 한편 〈데이비드〉는 삼림의 사내들을 본떠, 그 볼품없는 사지가 더는 눈에 거슬리지 않도록 바위 틈새에 제 몸을 끼워 넣었다."]], [["In this manner, hours passed by without further interruption.", "이런 식으로 더 이상의 방해 없이 여러 시간이 흘렀다."], ["The moon reached the zenith, and shed its mild light perpendicularly on the lovely sight of the sisters slumbering peacefully in each other's arms.", "달이 천정에 이르러, 서로의 품에 안겨 평화로이 잠든 자매의 사랑스러운 모습 위로 그 부드러운 빛을 수직으로 쏟아 내렸다."], ["Duncan cast the wide shawl of Cora before a spectacle he so much loved to contemplate, and then suffered his own head to seek a pillow on the rock.", "〈덩컨〉은 그토록 바라보기 좋아하던 그 광경 앞에 〈코라〉의 넓은 숄을 드리우고는, 자신의 머리도 바위에 베개를 찾게 했다."], ["David began to utter sounds that would have shocked his delicate organs in more wakeful moments; in short, all but Hawkeye and the Mohicans lost every idea of consciousness, in uncontrollable drowsiness.", "〈데이비드〉는 좀 더 깨어 있는 순간이었다면 제 섬세한 감각에 충격을 주었을 법한 소리를 내기 시작했으니, 요컨대 〈호크아이〉와 〈모히칸〉들을 제외한 모두가 걷잡을 수 없는 졸음 속에 의식이라는 관념을 죄다 잃고 말았다."], ["But the watchfulness of these vigilant protectors neither tired nor slumbered.", "그러나 이 빈틈없는 보호자들의 경계심은 지치지도 졸지도 않았다."], ["Immovable as that rock, of which each appeared to form a part, they lay, with their eyes roving, without intermission, along the dark margin of trees that bounded the adjacent shores of the narrow stream.", "저마다 그 일부를 이루는 듯한 바위처럼 미동도 없이, 그들은 좁은 물줄기의 인접한 양 기슭을 두른 나무들의 어두운 가장자리를 따라, 쉼 없이 눈길을 굴리며 엎드려 있었다."], ["Not a sound escaped them; the most subtle examination could not have told they breathed.", "그들에게서는 소리 하나 새어 나오지 않았으니, 아무리 면밀히 살펴도 그들이 숨을 쉬는지조차 알 수 없었을 것이다."], ["It was evident that this excess of caution proceeded from an experience that no subtlety on the part of their enemies could deceive.", "이 지나친 신중함이, 적이 어떤 술책을 부려도 속을 수 없다는 경험에서 비롯되었음이 분명했다."], ["It was, however, continued without any apparent consequences, until the moon had set, and a pale streak above the tree-tops, at the bend of the river a little below, announced the approach of day.", "그러나 그것은 별다른 결과 없이 이어지다가, 마침내 달이 지고, 조금 아래 강이 굽이도는 데 나무 우듬지 위로 옅은 줄무늬가 떠올라 동이 가까워졌음을 알렸다."]], [["Then, for the first time, Hawkeye was seen to stir.", "그제야 비로소 〈호크아이〉가 움직이는 것이 보였다."], ["He crawled along the rock, and shook Duncan from his heavy slumbers.", "그는 바위를 따라 기어가, 깊은 잠에 빠진 〈덩컨〉을 흔들어 깨웠다."]], [["\"Now is the time to journey,\"", "\"이제 길을 떠날 때요.\""], ["he whispered; \"awake the gentle ones, and be ready to get into the canoe when I bring it to the landing-place.\"", "그가 속삭였다. \"여린 분들을 깨우고, 내가 카누를 나루로 가져오거든 곧장 탈 채비를 하시오.\""]], [["\"Have you had a quiet night?\"", "\"조용한 밤을 보내셨소?\""], ["said Heyward; \"for myself, I believe sleep has got the better of my vigilance.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"나로 말하면, 잠이 내 경계심을 이긴 듯하오.\""]], [["\"All is yet still as midnight.", "\"아직 한밤중처럼 모든 게 고요하오."], ["Be silent, but be quick.\"", "잠자코, 허나 서둘러야 하오.\""]], [["By this time Duncan was thoroughly awake, and he immediately lifted the shawl from the sleeping females.", "이 무렵 〈덩컨〉은 완전히 잠에서 깨어, 즉시 잠든 여인들에게서 숄을 들어 올렸다. 그 움직임에 〈코라〉는 그를 밀어내려는 듯 손을 들었고, 한편 〈앨리스〉는 부드럽고 온화한 목소리로 중얼거렸다."], ["The motion caused Cora to raise her hand as if to repulse him, while Alice murmured, in her soft, gentle voice, \"No, no, dear father, we were not deserted; Duncan was with us!\"", "\"아니에요, 아니에요, 아버지, 우리는 버림받지 않았어요. 〈덩컨〉이 우리와 함께 있었어요!\""]], [["\"Yes, sweet innocence,\"", "\"그렇소, 사랑스러운 순결함이여.\""], ["whispered the youth; \"Duncan is here, and while life continues or danger remains, he will never quit thee.", "청년이 속삭였다. \"〈덩컨〉이 여기 있고, 목숨이 붙어 있는 한, 위험이 남아 있는 한, 결코 그대 곁을 떠나지 않으리다."], ["Cora!", "〈코라〉!"], ["Alice! awake!", "〈앨리스〉!"], ["The hour has come to move!\"", "일어나시오! 움직여야 할 때가 왔소!\""]], [["A loud shriek from the younger of the sisters, and the form of the other standing upright before him, in bewildered horror, was the unexpected answer he received.", "자매 가운데 동생이 내지른 날카로운 비명과, 언니가 어리둥절한 공포 속에 그의 앞에 꼿꼿이 일어선 모습이, 그가 받은 뜻밖의 대답이었다."], ["While the words were still on the lips of Heyward, there had arisen such a tumult of yells and cries as served to drive the swift currents of his own blood back from its bounding course into the fountains of his heart.", "〈헤이워드〉의 입술에 아직 그 말이 남아 있을 때, 그토록 거센 함성과 외침의 소동이 일어나, 제 핏줄을 내달리던 빠른 핏물을 도로 심장의 샘으로 돌려보낼 지경이었다."], ["It seemed, for near a minute, as if demons of hell had possessed themselves of the air about them, and were venting their savage humors in barbarous sounds.", "거의 한 순간 동안, 지옥의 악귀들이 그들 둘레의 공기를 사로잡고, 야만스러운 소리로 제 흉포한 기분을 토해 내는 듯했다."], ["The cries came from no particular direction, though it was evident they filled the woods, and as the appalled listeners easily imagined, the caverns of the falls, the rocks, the bed of the river, and the upper air.", "그 외침은 어느 한 방향에서 오는 것이 아니었으나, 숲을 가득 메우고, 또 겁에 질린 청자들이 쉬이 상상했듯, 폭포의 동굴과 바위와 강바닥과 위쪽 공중까지 가득 채운 것은 분명했다."], ["David raised his tall person in the midst of the infernal din, with a hand on either ear, exclaiming--", "〈데이비드〉가 그 지옥 같은 아우성 한가운데서 제 큰 키를 일으켜, 양손으로 두 귀를 막으며 외쳤다——"]], [["\"Whence comes this discord!", "\"이 불협화음은 어디서 오는 건가!"], ["Has hell broke loose, that man should utter sounds like these!\"", "지옥이 풀려나기라도 했단 말인가, 사람이 이런 소리를 내다니!\""]], [["The bright flashes and the quick reports of a dozen rifles, from the opposite banks of the stream, followed this incautious exposure of his person, and left the unfortunate singing-master senseless on that rock where he had been so long slumbering.", "맞은편 기슭에서 십여 자루 소총의 환한 섬광과 빠른 발사음이, 그가 경솔하게 제 몸을 드러낸 데 뒤따라, 그 오래도록 잠들어 있던 바위 위에 가엾은 성가 선생을 정신없이 쓰러뜨려 놓았다."], ["The Mohicans boldly sent back the intimidating yell of their enemies, who raised a shout of savage triumph at the fall of Gamut.", "〈모히칸〉들은 적들의 위협하는 함성을 대담하게 되받아쳤고, 적들은 〈거뮤트〉가 쓰러지자 야만스러운 승리의 함성을 질렀다."], ["The flash of rifles was then quick and close between them, but either party was too well skilled to leave even a limb exposed to the hostile aim.", "그러고는 양편 사이에 소총의 섬광이 빠르고 가깝게 오갔으나, 어느 쪽도 적의 조준에 팔다리 하나조차 드러낼 만큼 미숙하지 않았다."], ["Duncan listened with intense anxiety for the strokes of the paddle, believing that flight was now their only refuge.", "〈덩컨〉은 이제 도망만이 유일한 피난처라 믿으며, 노 젓는 소리를 간절히 마음 졸이며 들으려 했다."], ["The river glanced by with its ordinary velocity, but the canoe was nowhere to be seen on its dark waters.", "강물은 여느 때의 속도로 미끄러져 갔으나, 그 어두운 물 위 어디에도 카누는 보이지 않았다."], ["He had just fancied they were cruelly deserted by the scout, as a stream of flame issued from the rock beneath him, and a fierce yell, blended with a shriek of agony, announced that the messenger of death, sent from the fatal weapon of Hawkeye, had found a victim.", "척후가 무정하게도 자신들을 버렸다고 막 생각하던 차에, 그의 발밑 바위에서 한 줄기 화염이 솟아나고, 격렬한 함성에 단말마의 비명이 뒤섞여, 〈호크아이〉의 치명적인 무기에서 보내진 죽음의 사자가 제물을 찾았음을 알렸다."], ["At this slight repulse the assailants instantly withdrew, and gradually the place became as still as before the sudden tumult.", "이 가벼운 격퇴에 습격자들은 곧장 물러갔고, 차츰 그 자리는 갑작스러운 소동 이전처럼 고요해졌다."]], [["Duncan seized the favorable moment to spring to the body of Gamut, which he bore within the shelter of the narrow chasm that protected the sisters.", "〈덩컨〉은 이 좋은 순간을 붙잡아 〈거뮤트〉의 몸으로 달려가, 그를 자매를 보호하던 좁은 틈의 은신처 안으로 옮겼다."], ["In another minute the whole party was collected in this spot of comparative safety.", "다시 한 순간 만에 일행 전부가 이 비교적 안전한 자리에 모여들었다."]], [["\"The poor fellow has saved his scalp,\"", "\"이 가엾은 친구가 머리 가죽은 지켰구려.\""], ["said Hawkeye, coolly passing his hand over the head of David; \"but he is a proof that a man may be born with too long a tongue!", "〈호크아이〉가 〈데이비드〉의 머리 위로 손을 차분히 쓸어 보며 말했다. \"허나 그는 혀가 너무 긴 채로 태어날 수 있다는 산 증거요!"], ["'Twas downright madness to show six feet of flesh and blood, on a naked rock, to the raging savages.", "헐벗은 바위 위에서 날뛰는 야만인들에게 6피트나 되는 살과 피를 내보이다니, 순전한 광기였소."], ["I only wonder he has escaped with life.\"", "그저 목숨을 부지한 게 놀라울 따름이오.\""]], [["\"Is he not dead!\"", "\"죽은 건 아니지요!\""], ["demanded Cora, in a voice whose husky tones showed how powerfully natural horror struggled with her assumed firmness.", "〈코라〉가 물었으니, 그 쉰 목소리는 타고난 공포가 짐짓 꾸민 의연함과 얼마나 거세게 다투는지를 드러냈다."], ["\"Can we do aught to assist the wretched man?\"", "\"이 가엾은 사람을 도울 길이 있을까요?\""]], [["\"No, no!", "\"아니오, 아니오!"], ["the life is in his heart yet, and after he has slept awhile he will come to himself, and be a wiser man for it, till the hour of his real time shall come,\"", "아직 심장에 목숨이 붙어 있소. 한숨 자고 나면 제정신이 들 것이고, 진짜 때가 올 그 시각까지는 도리어 이로써 더 현명한 사내가 될 거요.\""], ["returned Hawkeye, casting another oblique glance at the insensible body, while he filled his charger with admirable nicety.", "〈호크아이〉가 정신을 잃은 몸에 또 한 번 비스듬한 눈길을 던지며 대답했으니, 그러면서도 감탄스러운 정성으로 제 총에 화약을 채웠다."], ["\"Carry him in, Uncas, and lay him on the sassafras.", "\"안으로 옮기게, 〈웅카스〉, 사사프라스 위에 눕히게."], ["The longer his nap lasts the better it will be for him, as I doubt whether he can find a proper cover for such a shape on these rocks; and singing won't do any good with the Iroquois.\"", "잠이 길수록 그에게 좋을 거요. 이런 바위에서 저런 체격에 알맞은 가림막을 찾을 수 있을지 의심스럽고, 또 〈이로쿼이〉를 상대로는 노래도 아무 소용이 없으니.\""]], [["\"You believe, then, the attack will be renewed?\"", "\"그렇다면 공격이 다시 시작되리라 보시오?\""], ["asked Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"Do I expect a hungry wolf will satisfy his craving with a mouthful!", "\"굶주린 늑대가 한입으로 제 갈망을 채우리라 기대하겠소!"], ["They have lost a man, and 'tis their fashion, when they meet a loss, and fail in the surprise, to fall back; but we shall have them on again, with new expedients to circumvent us, and master our scalps.", "저들은 한 사람을 잃었고, 손실을 입거나 기습에 실패하면 물러서는 게 저들의 방식이오. 허나 우리를 속여 넘기고 우리 머리 가죽을 차지하려는 새로운 꾀를 가지고 다시 덤벼들 거요."], ["Our main hope,\"", "우리의 주된 희망은,\""], ["he continued, raising his rugged countenance, across which a shade of anxiety just then passed like a darkening cloud, \"will be to keep the rock until Munro can send a party to our help!", "그가 그 거친 얼굴을 들며 말을 이었으니, 그 순간 어두워지는 구름처럼 근심의 그늘이 그 위를 스쳤다. \"〈먼로〉가 우리를 도울 부대를 보낼 때까지 이 바위를 지키는 것이오!"], ["God send it may be soon, and under a leader that knows the Indian customs!\"", "부디 그것이 곧, 인디언의 관습을 아는 지휘관 아래 이루어지기를 신께 비오!\""]], [["\"You hear our probable fortunes, Cora,\"", "\"우리에게 닥칠 법한 운명을 들으셨겠지요, 〈코라〉.\""], ["said Duncan, \"and you know we have everything to hope from the anxiety and experience of your father.", "〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"그러니 당신 부친의 노심초사와 경험에 우리가 모든 것을 걸 수 있음을 아실 겁니다."], ["Come, then, with Alice, into this cavern, where you, at least, will be safe from the murderous rifles of our enemies and where you may bestow a care suited to your gentle natures on our unfortunate comrade.\"", "자, 그러면 〈앨리스〉와 함께 이 동굴로 드시오. 거기서 적어도 당신만은 우리 적들의 살기 어린 소총을 피해 안전할 것이고, 우리의 불행한 동료에게 당신들의 다정한 천성에 어울리는 보살핌을 베풀 수 있을 테니.\""]], [["The sisters followed him into the outer cave, where David was beginning, by his sighs, to give symptoms of returning consciousness; and then commending the wounded man to their attention, he immediately prepared to leave them.", "자매는 그를 따라 바깥 동굴로 들어갔으니, 그곳에서는 〈데이비드〉가 한숨으로 의식이 돌아오는 징후를 보이기 시작하고 있었다. 그러고 나서 부상한 사내를 그들의 보살핌에 맡긴 뒤, 그는 즉시 그곳을 떠날 채비를 했다."]], [["\"Duncan!\"", "\"〈덩컨〉!\""], ["said the tremulous voice of Cora, when he had reached the mouth of the cavern.", "그가 동굴 입구에 이르렀을 때 〈코라〉의 떨리는 목소리가 불렀다."], ["He turned, and beheld the speaker, whose color had changed to a deadly paleness, and whose lip quivered, gazing after him, with an expression of interest which immediately recalled him to her side.", "그가 돌아서서, 말을 건 이를 바라보니, 그 얼굴빛은 죽은 듯 창백하게 변했고, 입술은 떨리며, 그의 뒤를 좇는 그 눈길에는 곧장 그를 곁으로 다시 불러들이는 간절함이 어려 있었다."], ["\"Remember, Duncan, how necessary your safety is to our own--how you bear a father's sacred trust--how much depends on your discretion and care--in short,\"", "\"기억하세요, 〈덩컨〉, 당신의 안전이 우리 자신에게 얼마나 절실한지를——당신이 한 아버지의 신성한 부탁을 짊어지고 있음을——당신의 분별과 보살핌에 얼마나 많은 것이 달려 있는지를——요컨대,\""], ["she added, while the tell-tale blood stole over her features, crimsoning her very temples, \"how very deservedly dear you are to all of the name of Munro.\"", "그가 덧붙였으니, 속내를 드러내는 핏기가 그 이목구비 위로 번져 관자놀이까지 붉게 물들였다. \"당신이 〈먼로〉라는 이름을 지닌 모든 이에게 얼마나 마땅히 소중한 사람인지를요.\""]], [["\"If anything could add to my own base love of life,\"", "\"내 비천한 목숨에 대한 애착에 무엇이든 더해질 수 있다면,\" 〈헤이워드〉가 무심결에 제 눈길이 말없는 〈앨리스〉의 앳된 모습으로 흘러가게 두며 말했다."], ["said Heyward, suffering his unconscious eyes to wander to the youthful form of the silent Alice, \"it would be so kind an assurance.", "\"그토록 다정한 다짐이야말로 바로 그것이겠지요. 60연대의 소령으로서, 우리의 정직한 주인장이 말해 주겠지만 나는 이 싸움에서 내 몫을 맡아야 합니다."], ["As major of the 60th, our honest host will tell you I must take my share of the fray; but our task will be easy; it is merely to keep these blood-hounds at bay for a few hours.\"", "허나 우리의 임무는 수월할 거요. 그저 이 핏빛 사냥개들을 몇 시간 동안 막아 두기만 하면 되니까.\""]], [["Without waiting for reply, he tore himself from the presence of the sisters, and joined the scout and his companions, who still lay within the protection of the little chasm between the two caves.", "대답을 기다리지 않고, 그는 자매의 곁에서 몸을 떼어 내, 두 동굴 사이의 작은 틈의 보호 안에 아직 엎드려 있던 척후와 그 동료들에게로 합류했다."]], [["\"I tell you, Uncas,\"", "\"내가 말하잖나, 〈웅카스〉.\""], ["said the former, as Heyward joined them, \"you are wasteful of your powder, and the kick of the rifle disconcerts your aim!", "〈헤이워드〉가 합류하자 앞의 사내가 말했다. \"자네는 화약을 낭비하고 있고, 소총의 반동이 자네 조준을 흐트러뜨리고 있어!"], ["Little powder, light lead, and a long arm, seldom fail of bringing the death screech from a Mingo!", "화약은 적게, 납탄은 가볍게, 총신은 길게, 그러면 〈밍고〉에게서 죽음의 비명을 끌어내는 데 좀처럼 실패하는 법이 없네!"], ["At least, such has been my experience with the creatur's.", "적어도 그놈들에 관한 내 경험은 그러했지. 자, 친구들, 각자의 엄폐물로 갑시다."], ["Come, friends; let us to our covers, for no man can tell when or where a Maqua[15] will strike his blow.\"", "〈마콰〉가 언제 어디서 일격을 날릴지는 아무도 모르니.\" * 마콰: 〈이로쿼이〉를 가리키는 다른 이름."]], [["The Indians silently repaired to their appointed stations, which were fissures in the rocks, whence they could command the approaches to the foot of the falls.", "인디언들은 말없이 제게 정해진 자리로 갔으니, 그것은 폭포 발치로 다가오는 길목을 굽어볼 수 있는 바위 틈새였다."], ["In the centre of the little island, a few short and stunted pines had found root, forming a thicket, into which Hawkeye darted with the swiftness of a deer, followed by the active Duncan.", "작은 섬 한가운데에는 키 작고 발육이 시원찮은 소나무 몇 그루가 뿌리를 내려 덤불을 이루고 있었는데, 〈호크아이〉가 사슴 같은 날렵함으로 그 안으로 뛰어들었고, 민첩한 〈덩컨〉이 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Here they secured themselves, as well as circumstances would permit, among the shrubs and fragments of stone that were scattered about the place.", "이곳에서 그들은, 그 자리에 흩어진 관목과 돌 부스러기 사이에, 형편이 허락하는 한 몸을 숨겼다."], ["Above them was a bare, rounded rock, on each side of which the water played its gambols, and plunged into the abysses beneath, in the manner already described.", "그들 위로는 헐벗은 둥근 바위가 있었고, 그 양옆으로 물이 장난치며 놀다가 이미 묘사한 대로 아래쪽 심연으로 곤두박질쳤다."], ["As the day had now dawned, the opposite shores no longer presented a confused outline, but they were able to look into the woods, and distinguish objects beneath the canopy of gloomy pines.", "이제 날이 밝았으므로, 맞은편 기슭은 더 이상 어수선한 윤곽으로 보이지 않았고, 그들은 숲속을 들여다보며 음울한 소나무의 차양 아래 사물들을 분간할 수 있었다."]], [["A long and anxious watch succeeded, but without any further evidences of a renewed attack; and Duncan began to hope that their fire had proved more fatal than was supposed, and that their enemies had been effectually repulsed.", "길고 근심 어린 망보기가 이어졌으나, 공격이 다시 시작되리라는 어떤 기색도 더는 없었다. 그리하여 〈덩컨〉은 자신들의 사격이 짐작보다 더 치명적이었고, 적들이 효과적으로 격퇴되었기를 바라기 시작했다."], ["When he ventured to utter this impression to his companion, it was met by Hawkeye with an incredulous shake of the head.", "그가 이 인상을 동료에게 감히 입에 올리자, 〈호크아이〉는 못 믿겠다는 듯 고개를 가로저으며 그것을 받았다."]], [["\"You know not the nature of a Maqua, if you think he is so easily beaten back without a scalp!\"", "\"〈마콰〉가 머리 가죽도 없이 그토록 쉬이 쫓겨났다고 생각한다면, 그대는 그놈의 본성을 모르는 거요!\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["\"If there was one of the imps yelling this morning, there were forty!", "\"오늘 아침에 함성을 지른 놈이 하나라면, 마흔은 됐을 거요!"], ["and they know our number and quality too well to give up the chase so soon.", "게다가 놈들은 우리의 수와 됨됨이를 너무도 잘 알아, 추격을 그리 빨리 포기하지 않소."], ["Hist!", "쉿!"], ["look into the water above, just where it breaks over the rocks.", "위쪽 물을 보시오, 바로 물이 바위를 넘어 부서지는 데를."], ["I am no mortal, if the risky devils haven't swam down upon the very pitch, and, as bad luck would have it, they have hit the head of the island.", "저 무모한 악귀들이 바로 그 낙수 위로 헤엄쳐 내려와, 운수 사납게도 섬의 머리에 닿지 않았다면 내가 사람이 아니오. 쉿!"], ["Hist! man, keep close!", "이보시오, 바싹 엎드리시오!"], ["or the hair will be off your crown in the turning of a knife!\"", "안 그러면 칼 한 번 돌리는 사이에 그대 정수리에서 머리털이 벗겨질 테니!\""]], [["Heyward lifted his head from the cover, and beheld what he justly considered a prodigy of rashness and skill.", "〈헤이워드〉가 엄폐물에서 고개를 들어, 그가 마땅히 무모함과 솜씨의 경이라 여긴 것을 바라보았다."], ["The river had worn away the edge of the soft rock in such a manner, as to render its first pitch less abrupt and perpendicular than is usual at waterfalls.", "강물이 무른 바위의 가장자리를 깎아 내어, 그 첫 낙수가 폭포에서 흔히 보이는 것보다 덜 가파르고 덜 수직이 되어 있었다."], ["With no other guide than the ripple of the stream where it met the head of the island, a party of their insatiable foes had ventured into the current, and swam down upon this point, knowing the ready access it would give, if successful, to their intended victims.", "물줄기가 섬의 머리와 만나는 데서 일렁이는 잔물결 말고는 다른 길잡이도 없이, 그 만족할 줄 모르는 적들의 한 무리가 물살 속으로 뛰어들어, 성공하기만 하면 노리는 제물에게로 손쉽게 다가갈 수 있음을 알고서, 이 지점으로 헤엄쳐 내려왔던 것이다."], ["As Hawkeye ceased speaking, four human heads could be seen peering above a few logs of drift-wood that had lodged on these naked rocks, and which had probably suggested the idea of the practicability of the hazardous undertaking.", "〈호크아이〉가 말을 마치자, 이 헐벗은 바위에 걸린 떠내려온 통나무 몇 개 위로 네 개의 사람 머리가 빠끔히 내다보는 것이 보였으니, 그 통나무들이 아마도 그 위험천만한 거사가 해 볼 만하다는 생각을 일으켰을 것이다."], ["At the next moment, a fifth form was seen floating over the green edge of the fall, a little from the line of the island.", "다음 순간, 섬의 선에서 조금 벗어나, 다섯 번째 형체가 폭포의 푸른 가장자리 위로 떠내려오는 것이 보였다."], ["The savage struggled powerfully to gain the point of safety, and, favored by the glancing water, he was already stretching forth an arm to meet the grasp of his companions, when he shot away again with the whirling current, appeared to rise into the air, with uplifted arms and starting eyeballs, and fell, with a sullen plunge, into that deep and yawning abyss over which he hovered.", "그 야만인은 안전한 지점에 닿으려 힘껏 몸부림쳤고, 비스듬히 흐르는 물의 도움으로 이미 한 팔을 뻗어 동료들의 손을 잡으려던 차에, 소용돌이치는 물살에 다시 휩쓸려, 두 팔을 치켜들고 눈알이 튀어나온 채 공중으로 솟구치는 듯하더니, 둔탁한 첨벙 소리와 함께, 그 위를 맴돌던 깊고 입을 벌린 심연 속으로 떨어졌다."], ["A single, wild, despairing shriek rose from the cavern, and all was hushed again, as the grave.", "동굴에서 외마디의 거칠고 절망적인 비명이 솟구쳤고, 모든 것은 다시 무덤처럼 잠잠해졌다."]], [["The first generous impulse of Duncan was to rush to the rescue of the hapless wretch; but he felt himself bound to the spot by the iron grasp of the immovable scout.", "〈덩컨〉의 첫 의로운 충동은 그 가엾은 자를 구하러 달려가는 것이었으나, 미동도 없는 척후의 쇠 같은 손아귀에 그 자리에 붙들린 것을 느꼈다."]], [["\"Would ye bring certain death upon us, by telling the Mingos where we lie?\"", "\"우리가 어디 엎드려 있는지 〈밍고〉들에게 알려, 우리에게 확실한 죽음을 가져오려는 거요?\""], ["demanded Hawkeye, sternly; \"'tis a charge of powder saved, and ammunition is as precious now as breath to a worried deer!", "〈호크아이〉가 준엄하게 따져 물었다. \"그건 화약 한 발 아낀 셈이고, 지금 탄약은 쫓기는 사슴에게 숨결만큼이나 귀하오!"], ["Freshen the priming of your pistols--the mist of the falls is apt to dampen the brimstone--and stand firm for a close struggle, while I fire on their rush.\"", "권총의 점화약을 갈아 두시오——폭포의 물안개가 화약을 적시기 십상이니——그리고 내가 놈들의 돌격에 사격하는 동안, 백병전에 대비해 굳게 버티시오.\""]], [["He placed his finger in his mouth, and drew a long, shrill whistle, which was answered from the rocks that were guarded by the Mohicans.", "그는 손가락을 입에 넣어, 길고 날카로운 휘파람을 불었고, 그것은 〈모히칸〉들이 지키던 바위에서 화답이 왔다."], ["Duncan caught glimpses of heads above the scattered drift-wood, as this signal rose on the air, but they disappeared again as suddenly as they had glanced upon his sight.", "이 신호가 공중에 울려 퍼지자 〈덩컨〉은 흩어진 떠내려온 통나무 위로 머리들이 어른거리는 것을 얼핏 보았으나, 그것들은 그의 눈에 비친 것만큼이나 갑작스레 다시 사라졌다."], ["A low, rustling sound next drew his attention behind him, and turning his head, he beheld Uncas within a few feet, creeping to his side.", "다음으로 나직이 바스락거리는 소리가 등 뒤에서 그의 주의를 끌어, 고개를 돌리니, 몇 피트 안에서 〈웅카스〉가 제 곁으로 기어오는 것이 보였다."], ["Hawkeye spoke to him in Delaware, when the young chief took his position with singular caution and undisturbed coolness.", "〈호크아이〉가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 그에게 이야기하자, 젊은 추장은 유별난 신중함과 흐트러짐 없는 침착함으로 자리를 잡았다."], ["To Heyward this was a moment of feverish and impatient suspense; though the scout saw fit to select it as a fit occasion to read a lecture to his more youthful associates on the art of using fire-arms with discretion.", "〈헤이워드〉에게 이것은 열에 들뜬 안달의 순간이었으나, 척후는 이 순간을 골라 제 손아래 동료들에게 화기를 분별 있게 다루는 기술을 일러 주는 적절한 기회로 삼는 것이 마땅하다고 보았다."]], [["\"Of all we'pons,\"", "\"모든 무기 가운데,\""], ["he commenced, \"the long-barrelled, true-grooved, soft-metalled rifle is the most dangerous in skilful hands, though it wants a strong arm, a quick eye, and great judgment in charging, to put forth all its beauties.", "그가 입을 열었다. \"총신이 길고, 강선이 제대로 파였으며, 무른 금속으로 된 소총이 솜씨 좋은 손에서는 가장 무서운 것이오."], ["The gunsmiths can have but little insight into their trade, when they make their fowling-pieces and short horsemen's--\"", "허나 그 온갖 아름다움을 죄다 발휘하려면 강한 팔과 빠른 눈, 그리고 장전에 큰 판단력이 필요하지. 총공이 사냥총이나 짧은 기병총 따위를 만들 때면, 제 일에 대한 안목이 거의 없는 게——\""]], [["He was interrupted by the low but expressive \"Hugh!\"", "그는 〈웅카스〉의 나직하나 의미심장한 \"흥!\""], ["of Uncas.", "소리에 말이 끊겼다."]], [["_Each of the combatants threw all his energies into that effort, and the result was, that both tottered on the brink of the precipice_]\n\"I see them, boy, I see them!\"", "\"보인다, 얘야, 보여!\" 〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Hawkeye; \"they are gathering for the rush, or they would keep their dingy backs below the logs.", "\"놈들이 돌격하려 모이고 있어, 안 그러면 그 거무튀튀한 등을 통나무 아래 감춰 두었을 테니."], ["Well, let them,\"", "좋다, 덤비라지.\""], ["he added, examining his flint; \"the leading man certainly comes on to his death, though it should be Montcalm himself!\"", "그가 제 부싯돌을 살피며 덧붙였다. \"선두에 선 놈은, 설령 그게 〈몽칼름〉 본인이라 해도, 분명 제 죽음으로 다가오는 거야!\""]], [["At that moment the woods were filled with another burst of cries, and at the signal four savages sprang from the cover of the drift-wood.", "그 순간 숲은 또 한 번의 함성으로 가득 찼고, 그 신호에 네 야만인이 떠내려온 통나무의 엄폐물에서 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["Heyward felt a burning desire to rush forward to meet them, so intense was the delirious anxiety of the moment; but he was restrained by the deliberate examples of the scout and Uncas.", "〈헤이워드〉는 그들을 맞으러 앞으로 달려 나가고픈 불타는 욕망을 느꼈으니, 그 순간의 광란 같은 조바심이 그토록 강렬했다. 그러나 척후와 〈웅카스〉의 침착한 본보기에 제지되었다."], ["When their foes who leaped over the black rock that divided them, with long bounds, uttering the wildest yells, were within a few rods, the rifle of Hawkeye slowly rose among the shrubs, and poured out its fatal contents.", "자신들을 가르는 검은 바위를 길게 도약해 뛰어넘으며 더없이 사나운 함성을 내지르던 적들이 몇 로드 안에 들어왔을 때, 〈호크아이〉의 소총이 관목 사이에서 천천히 올라가, 그 치명적인 내용물을 쏟아 냈다."], ["The foremost Indian bounded like a stricken deer, and fell headlong among the clefts of the island.", "맨 앞의 인디언이 화살 맞은 사슴처럼 펄쩍 뛰어오르더니, 섬의 갈라진 틈 사이로 곤두박질쳐 쓰러졌다."]], [["\"Now, Uncas!\"", "\"자, 〈웅카스〉!\" 척후가 제 긴 칼을 뽑으며 외쳤으니, 그 빠른 두 눈은 열의로 번득이기 시작했다."], ["cried the scout, drawing his long knife, while his quick eyes began to flash with ardor, \"take the last of the screeching imps; of the other two we are sartain!\"", "\"저 비명 지르는 악귀들의 마지막 놈을 맡아라. 나머지 둘은 우리가 확실히 처리하마!\""]], [["He was obeyed; and but two enemies remained to be overcome.", "명대로 되었으니, 이제 물리쳐야 할 적은 둘뿐이었다."], ["Heyward had given one of his pistols to Hawkeye, and together they rushed down a little declivity towards their foes; they discharged their weapons at the same instant, and equally without success.", "〈헤이워드〉는 제 권총 한 자루를 〈호크아이〉에게 건넸고, 둘은 함께 적들을 향해 자그마한 비탈을 달려 내려가, 같은 순간에 무기를 발사했으나, 둘 다 똑같이 빗나갔다."]], [["\"I know'd it!", "\"내 알고 있었지!"], ["and I said it!\"", "그렇게 말했고!\""], ["muttered the scout, whirling the despised little implement over the falls with bitter disdain.", "척후가 그 멸시하던 작은 물건을 쓰디쓴 경멸로 폭포 너머로 내던지며 중얼거렸다."], ["\"Come on, ye bloody minded hell-hounds!", "\"덤벼라, 이 피에 굶주린 지옥의 사냥개들아!"], ["ye meet a man without a cross!\"", "너희는 혼혈 한 점 없는 사내를 만났다!\""]], [["The words were barely uttered, when he encountered a savage of gigantic stature, and of the fiercest mien.", "그 말이 채 떨어지기 무섭게, 그는 거대한 체구에 더없이 사나운 인상을 한 야만인과 맞붙었다."], ["At the same moment, Duncan found himself engaged with the other, in a similar contest of hand to hand.", "같은 순간, 〈덩컨〉은 자신이 다른 한 놈과 비슷한 백병전에 휘말려 있음을 알아챘다."], ["With ready skill, Hawkeye and his antagonist each grasped that uplifted arm of the other which held the dangerous knife.", "〈호크아이〉와 그 적수는 능숙한 솜씨로 저마다 위험한 칼을 쥔 상대의 치켜든 팔을 움켜잡았다."], ["For near a minute they stood looking one another in the eye, and gradually exerting the power of their muscles for the mastery.", "거의 한 순간 동안 그들은 서로의 눈을 들여다보며 서 있다가, 차츰 우위를 차지하려 제 근육의 힘을 끌어냈다."], ["At length, the toughened sinews of the white man prevailed over the less practised limbs of the native.", "마침내 백인의 단련된 힘줄이 덜 단련된 원주민의 팔다리를 이겨 냈다."], ["The arm of the latter slowly gave way before the increasing force of the scout, who suddenly wresting his armed hand from the grasp of the foe, drove the sharp weapon through his naked bosom to the heart.", "후자의 팔이 척후의 점점 커지는 힘 앞에 천천히 굴복했고, 척후는 별안간 무기를 쥔 손을 적의 손아귀에서 비틀어 빼내, 그 날카로운 무기를 헐벗은 가슴팍을 꿰뚫어 심장에 박았다."], ["In the meantime Heyward had been pressed in a more deadly struggle.", "그동안 〈헤이워드〉는 한층 죽음에 가까운 싸움에 몰려 있었다."], ["His slight sword was snapped in the first encounter.", "그의 가느다란 검은 첫 충돌에서 부러졌다."], ["As he was destitute of any other means of defence, his safety now depended entirely on bodily strength and resolution.", "다른 방어 수단이 전혀 없었으므로, 이제 그의 안전은 오로지 육체의 힘과 결의에 달려 있었다."], ["Though deficient in neither of these qualities, he had met an enemy every way his equal.", "그 두 자질 어느 하나도 모자라지 않았으나, 그는 모든 면에서 자신과 대등한 적을 만났다."], ["Happily, he soon succeeded in disarming his adversary, whose knife fell on the rock at their feet; and from this moment it became a fierce struggle, who should cast the other over the dizzy height into a neighboring cavern of the falls.", "다행히, 그는 곧 적수를 무장 해제하는 데 성공하여, 그 칼이 둘의 발치 바위 위로 떨어졌다. 그리고 이 순간부터 그것은 누가 상대를 그 아찔한 높이 너머 폭포의 이웃한 동굴 속으로 내던질 것인가를 두고 벌이는 사나운 다툼이 되었다."], ["Every successive struggle brought them nearer to the verge, where Duncan perceived the final and conquering effort must be made.", "거듭되는 몸싸움마다 둘은 그 가장자리로 점점 가까워졌으니, 〈덩컨〉은 거기서 최후의 결판이 나는 힘겨루기가 벌어지리라는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Each of the combatants threw all his energies into that effort, and the result was, that both tottered on the brink of the precipice.", "두 싸움꾼은 저마다 제 온 기력을 그 안간힘에 쏟아부었고, 그 결과 둘 다 벼랑 끝에서 비틀거렸다."], ["Heyward felt the grasp of the other at his throat, and saw the grim smile the savage gave, under the revengeful hope that he hurried his enemy to a fate similar to his own, as he felt his body slowly yielding to a resistless power, and the young man experienced the passing agony of such a moment in all its horrors.", "〈헤이워드〉는 제 목을 죄어 오는 상대의 손아귀를 느꼈고, 야만인이 짓는 험상궂은 미소를 보았으니, 그것은 제 몸이 거역할 수 없는 힘에 천천히 굴복하는 것을 느끼며, 제 적을 제 운명과 같은 곳으로 몰아넣으리라는 복수에 찬 희망에서 나온 것이었고, 청년은 그런 순간의 스쳐 가는 고뇌를 그 온갖 끔찍함 속에서 겪었다. 그 극도의 위험의 순간에, 검은 손 하나와 번득이는 칼 한 자루가 그의 앞에 나타났다."], ["At that instant of extreme danger, a dark hand and glancing knife appeared before him; the Indian released his hold, as the blood flowed freely from around the several tendons of the wrist; and while Duncan was drawn backward by the saving arm of Uncas, his charmed eyes were still riveted on the fierce and disappointed countenance of his foe, who fell sullenly and disappointed down the irrecoverable precipice.", "손목의 여러 힘줄 둘레에서 피가 콸콸 쏟아지자 인디언은 잡았던 손을 놓았다. 그리고 〈웅카스〉의 구해 주는 팔에 〈덩컨〉이 뒤로 끌려가는 동안, 그의 홀린 눈은 사납고 낭패한 적의 얼굴에 여전히 못 박혀 있었으니, 그 적은 침울하게, 낭패한 채로, 돌이킬 수 없는 벼랑 아래로 떨어졌다."]], [["\"To cover!", "\"엄폐물로!"], ["to cover!\"", "엄폐물로!\""], ["cried Hawkeye, who just then had despatched the enemy; \"to cover, for your lives!", "마침 그때 제 적을 처치한 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"엄폐물로, 목숨이 아깝거든!"], ["the work is but half ended!\"", "일은 이제 절반밖에 안 끝났소!\""]], [["The young Mohican gave a shout of triumph, and, followed by Duncan, he glided up the acclivity they had descended to the combat, and sought the friendly shelter of the rocks and shrubs.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉이 승리의 함성을 질렀고, 〈덩컨〉을 뒤따르게 하여, 싸우러 내려갔던 비탈을 미끄러지듯 올라가, 바위와 관목의 정다운 은신처를 찾아들었다."]], [["\"They linger yet, Avengers of their native land.\"", "그들은 아직 머뭇거리고 있다,\n제 고향 땅의 복수자들이."]], [["The warning call of the scout was not uttered without occasion.", "그레이\n척후의 경고하는 외침은 까닭 없이 나온 것이 아니었다."], ["During the occurrence of the deadly encounter just related, the roar of the falls was unbroken by any human sound whatever.", "방금 이야기한 치명적인 격투가 벌어지는 동안, 폭포의 포효는 그 어떤 인간의 소리에도 끊기지 않았다."], ["It would seem that interest in the result had kept the natives on the opposite shores in breathless suspense, while the quick evolutions and swift changes in the position of the combatants, effectually prevented a fire that might prove dangerous alike to friend and enemy.", "강 건너편 기슭의 원주민들은 그 결말에 쏠린 관심으로 숨죽인 채 마음을 졸였던 듯하고, 한편 싸우는 자들의 위치가 재빠르게 바뀌고 휙휙 옮겨 다니는 통에 자칫 아군에게도 적군에게도 위험할 수 있는 사격은 사실상 막혀 버렸다."], ["But the moment the struggle was decided, a yell arose as fierce and savage as wild and revengful passions could throw into the air.", "그러나 승부가 결판난 순간, 사납고 흉포하기가, 광포하고 복수에 찬 격정이 허공으로 내지를 수 있는 한껏의 함성이 솟구쳤다."], ["It was followed by the swift flashes of the rifles, which sent their leaden messengers across the rock in volleys, as though the assailants would pour out their impotent fury on the insensible scene of the fatal contest.", "뒤이어 소총들이 잽싸게 번쩍이며, 마치 공격자들이 이 치명적인 싸움이 벌어진 무감각한 현장에 부질없는 분노를 쏟아붓기라도 하려는 듯, 일제히 납빛 사자(使者)들을 바위 너머로 날려 보냈다."]], [["A steady, though deliberate return was made from the rifle of Chingachgook, who had maintained his post throughout the fray with unmoved resolution.", "이에 대해, 싸움 내내 흔들림 없는 결의로 제 자리를 지켜 온 〈칭가치국〉의 소총에서 꾸준하면서도 신중한 응사가 이루어졌다."], ["When the triumphant shout of Uncas was borne to his ears, the gratified father raised his voice in a single responsive cry, after which his busy piece alone proved that he still guarded his pass with unwearied diligence.", "〈언카스〉의 승리에 찬 환호가 그의 귀에 실려 오자, 흡족해진 아비는 한 번의 호응하는 외침으로 목소리를 높였고, 그 뒤로는 분주한 그의 총만이, 그가 여전히 지칠 줄 모르는 부지런함으로 제 길목을 지키고 있음을 증명했다."], ["In this manner many minutes flew by with the swiftness of thought: the rifles of the assailants speaking, at times, in rattling volleys, and at others, in occasional, scattering shots.", "이런 식으로 여러 분이 생각만큼이나 빠르게 흘러갔으니, 공격자들의 소총은 때로 우두둑 일제히 울리고, 때로는 띄엄띄엄 흩어진 총성으로 말했다."], ["Though the rock, the trees, and the shrubs, were cut and torn in a hundred places around the besieged, their cover was so close, and so rigidly maintained, that, as yet, David had been the only sufferer in their little band.", "바위와 나무와 덤불이 포위당한 자들 주위 백 군데에서 찢기고 갈라졌으나, 그들의 엄폐는 어찌나 빈틈없고 또 어찌나 철저히 유지되었던지, 아직까지는 이 작은 무리 가운데 〈데이비드〉만이 유일한 피해자였다."]], [["\"Let them burn their powder,\"", "\"녀석들이 화약을 실컷 태우게 두시오.\""], ["said the deliberate scout, while bullet after bullet whizzed by the place where he securely lay; \"there will be a fine gathering of lead when it is over, and I fancy the imps will tire of the sport, afore these old stones cry out for mercy!", "신중한 척후가, 그가 안전히 엎드린 자리 옆으로 총알이 잇따라 윙윙 스쳐 가는 동안 말했다. \"다 끝나고 나면 납을 한바탕 그러모으게 될 게요."], ["Uncas, boy, you waste the kernels by overcharging: and a kicking rifle never carries a true bullet.", "그리고 내 짐작엔, 이 오래된 돌들이 자비를 구하짖기도 전에 저 도깨비 같은 것들이 이 놀음에 먼저 지칠 거요! 〈언카스〉, 얘야, 넌 약을 너무 많이 재서 알맹이를 낭비하는구나."], ["I told you to take that loping miscreant under the line of white paint; now, if your bullet went a hair's breadth, it went two inches above it.", "그리고 반동이 큰 소총은 결코 총알을 곧게 보내지 못한다. 저 껑충대는 악당을 흰 칠한 선 아래로 겨누라 일렀거늘, 네 총알이 머리카락 한 올만큼 빗나갔다면, 그 선보다 두 치는 위로 갔을 게다."], ["The life lies low in a Mingo, and humanity teaches us to make a quick end of the sarpents.\"", "〈밍고〉는 목숨이 아래쪽에 깃들어 있으니, 인정(人情)이 우리에게 가르치는 바, 그 뱀들은 단숨에 끝장을 내야 하는 법이다.\""]], [["A quiet smile lighted the haughty features of the young Mohican, betraying his knowledge of the English language, as well as of the other's meaning; but he suffered it to pass away without vindication or reply.", "이 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 오만한 이목구비에 잔잔한 미소가 어렸으니, 그가 영어를 알아들었을 뿐 아니라 상대의 말뜻도 알아챘음을 드러냈다. 그러나 그는 변명도 대꾸도 없이 그 미소를 흘려보냈다."]], [["\"I cannot permit you to accuse Uncas of want of judgment or of skill,\"", "\"나는 당신이 〈언카스〉를 두고 판단력이 모자라다거나 솜씨가 없다고 탓하는 것을 그냥 둘 수 없소.\""], ["said Duncan; \"he saved my life in the coolest and readiest manner, and he has made a friend who never will require to be reminded of the debt he owes.\"", "〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"그는 더없이 침착하고 더없이 기민하게 내 목숨을 구해 주었고, 자신이 진 빚을 결코 다시 일깨워 줄 필요가 없을 벗을 하나 얻은 것이오.\""]], [["Uncas partly raised his body, and offered his hand to the grasp of Heyward.", "〈언카스〉가 몸을 반쯤 일으켜, 〈헤이워드〉가 마주 쥐도록 손을 내밀었다."], ["During this act of friendship, the two young men exchanged looks of intelligence which caused Duncan to forget the character and condition of his wild associate.", "이 우정의 행위가 오가는 동안, 두 젊은이는 서로 뜻이 통하는 눈빛을 주고받았으니, 그로 인해 〈덩컨〉은 이 야생의 동행이 지닌 본성과 처지를 잊고 말았다."], ["In the meanwhile, Hawkeye, who looked on this burst of youthful feeling with a cool but kind regard, made the following reply:--", "그사이, 이 젊은이다운 감정의 분출을 차분하면서도 다정한 눈길로 지켜보던 〈호크아이〉가 다음과 같이 응답했다."]], [["\"Life is an obligation which friends often owe each other in the wilderness.", "\"황야에서는 목숨이라는 것이 벗들끼리 흔히 서로 지는 빚이지. 내 감히 말하건대, 나도 전에 〈언카스〉에게 그런 신세를 한두 번쯤 갚아 준 적이 있을 거요."], ["I dare say I may have served Uncas some such turn myself before now; and I very well remember that he has stood between me and death five different times: three times from the Mingos, once in crossing Horican, and--\"", "또 저 친구가 다섯 번이나 나와 죽음 사이를 막아서 주었던 것을 똑똑히 기억하고 있소. 세 번은 〈밍고〉의 손에서, 한 번은 〈호리컨〉을 건너다가, 그리고—\""]], [["\"That bullet was better aimed than common!\"", "\"저 총알은 여느 때보다 잘 겨눈 것이군!\""], ["exclaimed Duncan, involuntarily shrinking from a shot which struck the rock at his side with a smart rebound.", "〈덩컨〉이, 그의 곁 바위에 날카로운 반동으로 부딪친 한 발에 저도 모르게 움츠리며 외쳤다."]], [["Hawkeye laid his hand on the shapeless metal, and shook his head, as he examined it, saying, \"Falling lead is never flattened!", "〈호크아이〉가 그 형체 잃은 쇳덩이에 손을 얹고, 그것을 살피며 고개를 저었다. \"떨어지는 납은 결코 납작해지지 않는 법이오!"], ["had it come from the clouds this might have happened!\"", "구름에서 내려왔다면 이렇게 됐을 수도 있겠지만!\""]], [["But the rifle of Uncas was deliberately raised towards the heavens, directing his companions to a point, where the mystery was immediately explained.", "그러나 〈언카스〉의 소총이 신중하게 하늘을 향해 들렸으니, 동료들을 한 지점으로 이끌었고, 거기서 그 수수께끼가 곧장 풀렸다."], ["A ragged oak grew on the right bank of the river, nearly opposite to their position, which, seeking the freedom of the open space, had inclined so far forward, that its upper branches overhung that arm of the stream which flowed nearest to its own shore.", "그들의 자리와 거의 마주 보는 강 오른쪽 기슭에 우람한 떡갈나무 한 그루가 자라 있었는데, 트인 공간의 자유를 좇아 너무 앞으로 기울어진 나머지, 그 위쪽 가지들이 제 기슭에 가장 가까이 흐르는 물줄기 위로 드리워져 있었다."], ["Among the topmost leaves, which scantily concealed the gnarled and stunted limbs, a savage was nestled, partly concealed by the trunk of the tree, and partly exposed, as though looking down upon them to ascertain the effect produced by his treacherous aim.", "옹이지고 발육이 멈춘 가지들을 듬성듬성 가린 가장 높은 잎새들 사이에, 한 야만인이 둥지를 틀듯 엎드려 있었으니, 일부는 나무줄기에 가려지고 일부는 드러난 채로, 마치 제 사격의 효과를 확인하려 그들을 내려다보는 듯했다."]], [["\"These devils will scale heaven to circumvent us to our ruin,\"", "\"이 악마들이 우리를 옴짝달싹 못 하게 파멸로 몰아넣으려고 하늘까지 기어오르겠군.\""], ["said Hawkeye; \"keep him in play, boy, until I can bring 'Killdeer'", "〈호크아이〉가 말했다. \"얘야, 내가 '킬디어'를 겨눌 수 있을 때까지 저놈을 붙들어 두어라."], ["to bear, when we will try his metal on each side of the tree at once.\"", "그러면 나무 양옆에서 동시에 저놈의 배짱을 시험해 보자꾸나.\""]], [["Uncas delayed his fire until the scout uttered the word.", "〈언카스〉는 척후가 말을 뱉을 때까지 사격을 늦추었다."], ["The rifles flashed, the leaves and the bark of the oak flew into the air, and were scattered by the wind, but the Indian answered their assault by a taunting laugh, sending down upon them another bullet in return, that struck the cap of Hawkeye from his head.", "두 소총이 번쩍였고, 떡갈나무의 잎새와 껍질이 허공으로 튀어 올라 바람에 흩어졌으나, 그 인디언은 그들의 공격에 비웃는 웃음으로 답하며, 답례로 또 한 발을 내리꽂아 〈호크아이〉의 머리에서 모자를 쳐냈다."], ["Once more the savage yells burst out of the woods, and the leaden hail whistled above the heads of the besieged, as if to confine them to a place where they might become easy victims to the enterprise of the warrior who had mounted the tree.", "또 한 번 야만의 함성이 숲에서 터져 나왔고, 납빛 우박이 포위당한 자들의 머리 위로 휘파람 소리를 내며 날았으니, 마치 나무에 오른 전사의 모험에 손쉬운 제물이 될 만한 곳에 그들을 가두어 두려는 듯했다."]], [["\"This must be looked to!\"", "\"이건 어떻게든 손을 써야겠소!\""], ["said the scout, glancing about him with an anxious eye.", "척후가 근심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["\"Uncas, call up your father; we have need of all our we'pons to bring the cunning varmint from his roost.\"", "\"〈언카스〉, 네 아버지를 불러라. 저 교활한 짐승을 횃대에서 끌어내리자면 우리 무기를 죄다 동원해야겠다.\""]], [["The signal was instantly given; and, before Hawkeye had reloaded his rifle, they were joined by Chingachgook.", "신호는 즉시 전해졌고, 〈호크아이〉가 소총을 미처 다시 장전하기도 전에 그들은 〈칭가치국〉과 합류했다."], ["When his son pointed out to the experienced warrior the situation of their dangerous enemy, the usual exclamatory \"Hugh!\"", "아들이 노련한 전사에게 위험한 적의 위치를 가리켜 보이자, 노련한 전사의 입술에서 여느 때처럼 감탄사 \"휴!\"가 터져 나왔다."], ["burst from his lips; after which, no further expression of surprise or alarm was suffered to escape him.", "그 뒤로는 놀람이나 경악의 기색을 그 어떤 것도 새어 나오게 하지 않았다."], ["Hawkeye and the Mohicans conversed earnestly together in Delaware for a few moments, when each quietly took his post, in order to execute the plan they had speedily devised.", "〈호크아이〉와 〈모히칸〉 부자는 잠시 〈델라웨어〉 말로 진지하게 이야기를 나누었고, 이윽고 저마다 황급히 짜낸 계획을 실행하고자 조용히 제 자리를 잡았다."]], [["The warrior in the oak had maintained a quick, though ineffectual fire, from the moment of his discovery.", "떡갈나무에 오른 전사는 발각된 순간부터, 효과는 없을지언정 잰 사격을 이어 갔다."], ["But his aim was interrupted by the vigilance of his enemies, whose rifles instantaneously bore on any part of his person that was left exposed.", "그러나 그의 조준은 적들의 경계심에 가로막혔으니, 적들의 소총은 그의 몸 어느 부분이든 드러나는 즉시 그리로 겨누어졌다."], ["Still his bullets fell in the centre of the crouching party.", "그래도 그의 총알은 웅크린 무리의 한복판에 떨어졌다."], ["The clothes of Heyward, which rendered him peculiarly conspicuous, were repeatedly cut, and once blood was drawn from a slight wound in his arm.", "〈헤이워드〉를 유난히 눈에 띄게 만드는 그 옷은 거듭 찢겼고, 한 번은 팔에 입은 가벼운 상처에서 피가 흘렀다."]], [["At length, emboldened by the long and patient watchfulness of his enemies, the Huron attempted a better and more fatal aim.", "마침내, 적들의 길고 끈질긴 경계에 대담해진 〈휴런〉은 더 정확하고 더 치명적인 조준을 시도했다."], ["The quick eye of the Mohicans caught the dark line of his lower limbs incautiously exposed through the thin foliage, a few inches from the trunk of the tree.", "〈모히칸〉 부자의 잰 눈이, 나무줄기에서 몇 치 떨어진 곳에서 성긴 잎새 사이로 부주의하게 드러난 그의 하반신의 검은 윤곽을 포착했다."], ["Their rifles made a common report, when, sinking on his wounded limb, part of the body of the savage came into view.", "그들의 소총이 한 발의 총성으로 울렸을 때, 그 야만인이 다친 다리에 무게가 실려 주저앉으며 몸의 일부가 시야에 들어왔다."], ["Swift as thought, Hawkeye seized the advantage and discharged his fatal weapon into the top of the oak.", "생각만큼이나 빠르게, 〈호크아이〉가 그 기회를 붙잡아 떡갈나무 꼭대기에 치명적인 무기를 발사했다."], ["The leaves were unusually agitated; the dangerous rifle fell from its commanding elevation, and after a few moments of vain struggling, the form of the savage was seen swinging in the wind, while he still grasped a ragged and naked branch of the tree, with hands clenched in desperation.", "잎새들이 유난히 요동쳤고, 위험한 소총이 그 위압적인 높이에서 떨어졌으며, 잠시 동안의 헛된 발버둥 끝에, 그 야만인의 몸이 바람에 흔들리는 것이 보였으니, 그는 여전히 절망 속에 두 손을 꽉 움켜쥐고 나무의 앙상하게 벗겨진 가지를 붙들고 있었다."]], [["\"Give him, in pity give him--the contents of another rifle!\"", "\"저자에게, 제발 저자에게—다른 소총의 한 발을 먹이시오!\""], ["cried Duncan, turning away his eyes in horror from the spectacle of a fellow-creature in such awful jeopardy.", "〈덩컨〉이, 동족 하나가 그토록 끔찍한 위기에 처한 광경에서 공포에 질려 눈을 돌리며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Not a karnel!\" exclaimed the obdurate Hawkeye; \"his death is certain, and we have no powder to spare, for Indian fights sometimes last for days; 'tis their scalps or ours!--and God, who made us, has put into our natures the craving to keep the skin on the head!\"", "\"한 알도 못 쓰오!\" 완강한 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"저자의 죽음은 정해진 일이고, 우리에겐 낭비할 화약이 없소. 인디언과의 싸움은 때로 며칠씩 가는 법이니까. 저들의 머리 가죽이냐, 우리 것이냐의 일이오!—그리고 우리를 지으신 하느님께서 우리 본성 속에, 제 머리에 가죽을 지켜 두려는 갈망을 심어 놓으셨소!\""]], [["Against this stern and unyielding morality, supported as it was by such visible policy, there was no appeal.", "이토록 눈에 보이는 타산(打算)이 뒷받침하는, 이 준엄하고 굽힐 줄 모르는 도덕률에는 항변할 여지가 없었다."], ["From that moment the yells in the forest once more ceased, the fire was suffered to decline, and all eyes, those of friends as well as enemies, became fixed on the hopeless condition of the wretch who was dangling between heaven and earth.", "그 순간부터 숲의 함성이 다시 한번 그쳤고, 사격은 잦아들도록 내버려졌으며, 아군이든 적군이든 모든 눈이 하늘과 땅 사이에 매달린 그 가련한 자의 가망 없는 처지에 못 박혔다."], ["The body yielded to the currents of air, and though no murmur or groan escaped the victim, there were instants when he grimly faced his foes, and the anguish of cold despair might be traced, through the intervening distance, in possession of his swarthy lineaments.", "몸은 바람의 흐름에 내맡겨졌고, 그 희생자에게서 어떤 신음이나 끙끙대는 소리도 새어 나오지 않았으나, 어떤 순간에는 그가 침울하게 적들을 마주 보았으니, 사이에 가로놓인 거리 너머로도, 차디찬 절망의 고뇌가 그 거무스레한 이목구비를 사로잡고 있음을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["Three several times the scout raised his piece in mercy, and as often prudence getting the better of his intention, it was again silently lowered.", "척후는 자비심에서 세 차례나 총을 들었고, 그때마다 신중함이 그 의도를 이겨, 총은 다시 말없이 내려졌다."], ["At length one hand of the Huron lost its hold, and dropped exhausted to his side.", "마침내 〈휴런〉의 한 손이 잡은 것을 놓치고, 기진하여 옆구리로 떨어졌다."], ["A desperate and fruitless struggle to recover the branch succeeded, and then the savage was seen for a fleeting instant, grasping wildly at the empty air.", "가지를 다시 붙들려는 필사적이면서도 헛된 몸부림이 뒤따랐고, 이윽고 그 야만인이, 허공을 미친 듯이 움켜쥐며 한순간 덧없이 보였다."], ["The lightning is not quicker than was the flame from the rifle of Hawkeye; the limbs of the victim trembled and contracted, the head fell to the bosom, and the body parted the foaming waters like lead, when the element closed above it, in its ceaseless velocity, and every vestige of the unhappy Huron was lost forever.", "〈호크아이〉의 소총에서 인 불꽃은 번개보다도 빨랐다. 희생자의 사지가 떨리며 오그라들었고, 머리가 가슴께로 떨어졌으며, 몸은 거품 이는 물살을 납덩이처럼 가르고 들었으니, 물은 그 끊임없는 빠르기로 그 위에서 다시 닫혔고, 그 불행한 〈휴런〉의 자취는 모조리 영영 사라지고 말았다."]], [["No shout of triumph succeeded this important advantage, but even the Mohicans gazed at each other in silent horror.", "이 중대한 우세에 어떤 승리의 함성도 뒤따르지 않았으니, 〈모히칸〉 부자조차 말없는 공포 속에 서로를 응시했다."], ["A single yell burst from the woods, and all was again still.", "외마디 함성이 숲에서 터져 나왔고, 모든 것이 다시 고요해졌다."], ["Hawkeye, who alone appeared to reason on the occasion, shook his head at his own momentary weakness, even uttering his self-disapprobation aloud.", "이 일에 홀로 따져 보는 듯하던 〈호크아이〉는 제 한순간의 나약함에 고개를 저었으며, 제 자책의 말을 소리 내어 뱉기까지 했다."]], [["\"'Twas the last charge in my horn, and the last bullet in my pouch, and 'twas the act of a boy!\" he said; \"what mattered it whether he struck the rock living or dead: feeling would soon be over. Uncas, lad, go down to the canoe, and bring up the big horn; it is all the powder we have left, and we shall need it to the last grain, or I am ignorant of the Mingo nature.\"", "\"내 화약통에 남은 마지막 분량이었고, 내 주머니에 남은 마지막 총알이었는데, 그게 풋내기나 할 짓이었어!\" 그가 말했다. \"저자가 산 채로 바위에 부딪치든 죽어서 부딪치든 그게 무슨 대수였겠나. 어차피 곧 감각도 사라질 텐데. 〈언카스〉, 얘야, 카누로 내려가서 큰 화약뿔을 가져오너라. 우리에게 남은 화약이라곤 그게 전부고, 마지막 한 알까지 다 써야 할 게다. 안 그러면 내가 〈밍고〉의 본성을 모르는 셈이지.\""]], [["The young Mohican complied, leaving the scout turning over the useless contents of his pouch, and shaking the empty horn with renewed discontent.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉은 그 말을 따랐고, 척후는 제 주머니의 쓸모없는 내용물을 뒤적이며 빈 화약뿔을 새삼스레 못마땅한 듯 흔들고 있었다."], ["From this unsatisfactory examination, however, he was soon called by a loud and piercing exclamation from Uncas, that sounded, even to the unpractised ears of Duncan, as the signal of some new and unexpected calamity.", "그러나 그는 이 만족스럽지 못한 점검에서 곧, 〈언카스〉가 지르는 크고 날카로운 외침에 불려 나왔으니, 그 소리는 군사에 서툰 〈덩컨〉의 귀에마저, 어떤 새롭고 뜻밖의 재앙을 알리는 신호로 들렸다."], ["Every thought filled with apprehension for the precious treasure he had concealed in the cavern, the young man started to his feet, totally regardless of the hazard he incurred by such an exposure.", "동굴 속에 감춰 둔 소중한 보물에 대한 염려로 온통 가득 찬 채, 젊은이는 그렇게 몸을 드러냄으로써 무릅쓰는 위험은 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고 벌떡 일어났다."], ["As if actuated by a common impulse, his movement was imitated by his companions, and, together, they rushed down the pass to the friendly chasm, with a rapidity that rendered the scattering fire of their enemies perfectly harmless.", "마치 한 가지 충동에 떠밀린 듯, 그의 동작은 동료들에게도 그대로 따라졌고, 그들은 함께, 적들의 흩어진 사격이 완전히 무용지물이 될 만큼 빠르게, 그 다정한 협곡을 향해 길목을 달려 내려갔다."], ["The unwonted cry had brought the sisters, together with the wounded David, from their place of refuge; and the whole party, at a single glance, was made acquainted with the nature of the disaster that had disturbed even the practised stoicism of their youthful Indian protector.", "이 예사롭지 않은 외침이 자매를, 부상당한 〈데이비드〉와 함께 그들의 피난처에서 불러냈다. 그리고 그 무리 전체가, 단 한 번의 눈길로, 자기네 젊은 인디언 보호자의 단련된 극기심마저 흔들어 놓은 그 참사의 정체를 알게 되었다."]], [["At a short distance from the rock, their little bark was to be seen floating across the eddy, towards the swift current of the river, in a manner which proved that its course was directed by some hidden agent.", "바위에서 얼마 떨어지지 않은 곳에, 그들의 작은 배가 소용돌이를 가로질러 강의 빠른 물살을 향해 떠가는 것이 보였으니, 그 진로가 어떤 보이지 않는 손에 이끌리고 있음을 증명하는 모양새였다."], ["The instant this unwelcome sight caught the eye of the scout, his rifle was levelled as by instinct, but the barrel gave no answer to the bright sparks of the flint.", "이 달갑잖은 광경이 척후의 눈에 들어온 순간, 그의 소총이 본능에 이끌리듯 겨누어졌으나, 총신은 부싯돌의 밝은 불꽃에 아무런 응답도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"'Tis too late, 'tis too late!\"", "\"너무 늦었어, 너무 늦었어!\""], ["Hawkeye exclaimed, dropping the useless piece in bitter disappointment; \"the miscreant has struck the rapid; and had we powder, it could hardly send the lead swifter than he now goes!\"", "〈호크아이〉가 쓰라린 실망 속에 쓸모없어진 총을 떨구며 외쳤다. \"저 악당이 급류를 탔으니, 우리에게 화약이 있다 한들 지금 저자가 가는 것보다 빠르게 납을 보내기도 어렵겠소!\""]], [["The adventurous Huron raised his head above the shelter of the canoe, and while it glided swiftly down the stream, he waved his hand, and gave forth the shout, which was the known signal of success.", "그 대담한 〈휴런〉이 카누의 가림막 위로 고개를 들었고, 배가 물줄기를 따라 쏜살같이 미끄러져 내려가는 동안, 그는 손을 흔들며, 알려진 성공의 신호인 함성을 내질렀다."], ["His cry was answered by a yell and a laugh from the woods, as tauntingly exulting as if fifty demons were uttering their blasphemies at the fall of some Christian soul.", "그의 외침에는 숲에서 일어난 함성과 웃음이 응답했으니, 마치 쉰 마리의 악마가 어느 기독교도 영혼의 추락에 신성모독의 말을 내뱉기라도 하듯, 약 올리며 의기양양했다."]], [["\"Well may you laugh, ye children of the devil!\"", "\"실컷 웃으려무나, 이 악마의 자식들아!\""], ["said the scout, seating himself on a projection of the rock, and suffering his gun to fall neglected at his feet, \"for the three quickest and surest rifles in these woods are no better than so many stalks of mullein, or the last year's horns of a buck!\"", "척후가 바위의 튀어나온 곳에 주저앉으며, 제 총이 발치에 내버려진 채 떨어지도록 두고 말했다. \"이 숲에서 가장 빠르고 가장 정확한 소총 세 자루가, 그저 우단담배풀 줄기 몇 가닥이나 작년에 떨어진 수사슴 뿔 나부랭이만도 못하게 됐으니!\""]], [["\"What is to be done?\"", "\"어떻게 해야 하오?\""], ["demanded Duncan, losing the first feeling of disappointment in a more manly desire for exertion; \"what will become of us?\"", "〈덩컨〉이, 처음의 실망감을 보다 사내다운 분발의 욕구로 떨쳐 내며 물었다. \"우리는 어찌 되는 거요?\""]], [["Hawkeye made no other reply than by passing his finger around the crown of his head, in a manner so significant, that none who witnessed the action could mistake its meaning.", "〈호크아이〉는 다른 대답 대신, 제 머리 정수리 둘레로 손가락을 빙 둘렀으니, 그 동작이 어찌나 의미심장했던지 그것을 본 사람이라면 누구도 그 뜻을 오해할 수 없었다."]], [["\"Surely, surely, our case is not so desperate!\"", "\"설마, 설마 우리 처지가 그토록 절망적이지는 않겠지!\" 젊은이가 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed the youth; \"the Hurons are not here; we may make good the caverns; we may oppose their landing.\"", "\"〈휴런〉들은 여기 없잖소. 우리가 동굴을 굳게 지킬 수도, 그들의 상륙을 막을 수도 있지 않소.\""]], [["\"With what?\"", "\"무엇으로?\""], ["coolly demanded the scout.", "척후가 차갑게 물었다."], ["\"The arrows of Uncas, or such tears as women shed!", "\"〈언카스〉의 화살이나, 여인들이 흘리는 그런 눈물로?"], ["No, no; you are young, and rich, and have friends, and at such an age I know it is hard to die!", "아니, 아니지. 당신은 젊고, 부유하고, 벗들도 있으니, 그런 나이에 죽기란 힘든 일임을 나도 잘 아오!"], ["But,\"", "하지만,\""], ["glancing his eyes at the Mohicans, \"let us remember we are men without a cross, and let us teach these natives of the forest that white blood can run as freely as red, when the appointed hour is come.\"", "〈모히칸〉 부자에게 눈길을 던지며 그가 말했다. \"우리가 한 점 섞임 없는 사내들임을 기억하고, 정해진 시각이 오면 흰 피도 붉은 피만큼이나 거침없이 흐를 수 있음을 이 숲의 토박이들에게 가르쳐 줍시다.\""]], [["Duncan turned quickly in the direction indicated by the other's eyes, and read a confirmation of his worst apprehensions in the conduct of the Indians.", "〈덩컨〉이 상대가 눈으로 가리킨 방향으로 재빨리 돌아섰다가, 그 인디언들의 거동에서 자신의 가장 나쁜 우려가 사실임을 읽었다."], ["Chingachgook, placing himself in a dignified posture on another fragment of the rock, had already laid aside his knife and tomahawk, and was in the act of taking the eagle's plume from his head, and smoothing the solitary tuft of hair in readiness to perform its last and revolting office.", "〈칭가치국〉은 바위의 또 다른 조각 위에 위엄 있는 자세로 자리 잡고 앉아, 이미 칼과 토마호크를 내려놓은 채, 제 머리에서 독수리 깃을 빼고 그 마지막의 끔찍한 소임을 치를 채비로 외따로 남은 한 줌의 머리채를 매만지고 있었다."], ["His countenance was composed, though thoughtful, while his dark gleaming eyes were gradually losing the fierceness of the combat in an expression better suited to the change he expected momentarily to undergo.", "그의 표정은 생각에 잠긴 듯하면서도 차분했으며, 그 검고 번득이는 두 눈은 전투의 사나움을 차츰 잃고, 그가 머지않아 겪으리라 예상하는 변화에 한결 어울리는 표정으로 옮아 가고 있었다."]], [["\"Our case is not, cannot be so hopeless!\"", "\"우리 처지는 그렇지 않소, 그토록 가망 없을 리 없소!\""], ["said Duncan; \"even at this moment succor may be at hand.", "〈덩컨〉이 말했다. \"바로 이 순간에도 구원이 가까이 있을지 모르오."], ["I see no enemies!", "적이라곤 하나도 보이지 않잖소!"], ["they have sickened of a struggle in which they risk so much with so little prospect of gain!\"", "그토록 얻을 것 적은 일에 그토록 많은 것을 걸어야 하는 싸움에 저들도 진력이 난 게요!\""]], [["\"It may be a minute, or it may be an hour, afore the wily sarpents steal upon us, and it is quite in natur'", "\"저 교활한 뱀들이 우리에게 살그머니 다가오기까지는 일 분이 걸릴 수도, 한 시간이 걸릴 수도 있소."], ["for them to be lying within hearing at this very moment,\"", "그리고 바로 이 순간에도 우리 말소리가 들리는 거리에 엎드려 있는 것이 저들에겐 지극히 당연한 일이지.\""], ["said Hawkeye; \"but come they will, and in such a fashion as will leave us nothing to hope!", "〈호크아이〉가 말했다. \"허나 저들은 올 게요, 그것도 우리에게 바랄 것이라곤 아무것도 남기지 않을 그런 식으로!"], ["Chingachgook\"--he spoke in Delaware--\"my brother, we have fought our last battle together, and the Maquas will triumph in the death of the sage man of the Mohicans, and of the pale-face, whose eyes can make night as day, and level the clouds to the mists of the springs!\"", "〈칭가치국〉\"—그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 말했다—\"형제여, 우리는 마지막 싸움을 함께 치렀고, 〈마쿠아〉들은 〈모히칸〉의 현명한 사내와, 밤을 낮처럼 만들고 구름을 샘의 안개만큼 낮게 끌어내릴 수 있는 눈을 가진 창백한 얼굴의 죽음에 의기양양하겠지!\""]], [["\"Let the Mingo women go weep over their slain!\"", "\"〈밍고〉의 여인들더러 제 죽은 자들이나 곡하게 두라지!\""], ["returned the Indian, with characteristic pride and unmoved firmness; \"the Great Snake of the Mohicans has coiled himself in their wigwams, and has poisoned their triumph with the wailings of children whose fathers have not returned!", "인디언이 특유의 긍지와 흔들림 없는 굳셈으로 응답했다. \"〈모히칸〉의 위대한 뱀이 그들의 천막 속에 똬리를 틀어, 아비가 돌아오지 않은 아이들의 통곡으로 그들의 승리에 독을 풀어 놓았다!"], ["Eleven warriors lie hid from the graves of their tribes since the snows have melted, and none will tell where to find them when the tongue of Chingachgook shall be silent!", "눈이 녹은 이래로 열한 명의 전사가 제 부족의 무덤에서 멀리 숨겨졌으니, 〈칭가치국〉의 혀가 잠잠해지면 그들을 어디서 찾을지 아무도 일러 주지 못하리라!"], ["Let them draw the sharpest knife, and whirl the swiftest tomahawk, for their bitterest enemy is in their hands.", "그들더러 가장 날카로운 칼을 뽑고, 가장 빠른 토마호크를 휘두르게 하라. 그들의 가장 지독한 원수가 그들 손안에 있으니."], ["Uncas, topmost branch of a noble trunk, call on the cowards to hasten or their hearts will soften, and they will change to women!\"", "〈언카스〉야, 고귀한 줄기의 가장 높은 가지야, 저 겁쟁이들에게 서두르라 외쳐라. 안 그러면 그들의 마음이 약해져, 여인으로 변해 버릴 테니!\""]], [["\"They look among the fishes for their dead!\"", "\"그들은 제 죽은 자들을 물고기들 틈에서 찾고 있다!\""], ["returned the low, soft voice of the youthful chieftain; \"the Hurons float with the slimy eels!", "젊은 족장의 낮고 부드러운 목소리가 응답했다. \"〈휴런〉들은 미끈거리는 뱀장어들과 함께 떠다닌다!"], ["They drop from the oaks like fruit that is ready to be eaten!", "그들은 먹기 알맞게 익은 열매처럼 떡갈나무에서 떨어진다!"], ["and the Delawares laugh!\"", "그리고 〈델라웨어〉들은 웃는다!\""]], [["\"Ay, ay,\"", "\"옳지, 옳아.\""], ["muttered the scout, who had listened to this peculiar burst of the natives with deep attention; \"they have warmed their Indian feelings, and they'll soon provoke the Maquas to give them a speedy end.", "이 원주민들의 유별난 토로를 깊은 주의를 기울여 듣던 척후가 중얼거렸다. \"저들이 인디언다운 감정을 달구어 놓았으니, 곧 〈마쿠아〉들을 들쑤셔 자기들에게 잽싼 최후를 안기게 하겠군."], ["As for me, who am of the whole blood of the whites, it is befitting that I should die as becomes my color, with no words of scoffing in my mouth, and without bitterness at the heart!\"", "나로 말하자면, 순전히 백인의 피를 받은 사람이니, 내 빛깔에 어울리게, 입에 비웃는 말 한마디 없이, 또 마음에 쓰라림 없이 죽는 것이 마땅하지!\""]], [["\"Why die at all!\"", "\"어찌하여 죽기까지 한단 말인가요!\" 〈코라〉가, 이 순간까지 본능적인 공포로 자신을 바위에 못 박아 두었던 자리에서 앞으로 나서며 말했다."], ["said Cora, advancing from the place where natural horror had, until this moment, held her riveted to the rock; \"the path is open on every side; fly, then, to the woods, and call on God for succor.", "\"사방으로 길이 열려 있어요. 그러니 숲으로 달아나 하느님께 구원을 청하세요."], ["Go, brave men, we owe you too much already; let us no longer involve you in our hapless fortunes!\"", "가세요, 용감한 분들이여, 우리는 이미 당신들에게 너무 많은 빚을 졌어요. 더는 우리의 불운한 운명에 당신들을 끌어들이지 말게 해 주세요!\""]], [["\"You but little know the craft of the Iroquois, lady, if you judge they have left the path open to the woods!\"", "\"〈이로쿼이〉의 간계를 너무 모르는 말씀이오, 아가씨, 저들이 숲으로 가는 길을 열어 두었으리라 여기신다면!\""], ["returned Hawkeye, who, however, immediately added in his simplicity, \"the down stream current, it is certain, might soon sweep us beyond the reach of their rifles or the sounds of their voices.\"", "〈호크아이〉가 응답했으나, 이내 그 단순한 성품대로 덧붙였다. \"물길을 따라 내려가는 물살이라면, 우리를 저들의 소총이나 목소리가 닿지 않는 데까지 곧 쓸어 가 줄 게 분명하지만.\""]], [["\"Then try the river.", "\"그렇다면 강을 시도해 보세요."], ["Why linger to add to the number of the victims of our merciless enemies?\"", "어찌하여 머뭇거리며 우리의 무자비한 적들의 제물 수를 늘리려 하시나요?\""]], [["\"Why,\"", "\"어찌하여라니,\""], ["repeated the scout, looking about him proudly, \"because it is better for a man to die at peace with himself than to live haunted by an evil conscience!", "척후가 자랑스레 주위를 둘러보며 되뇌었다. \"사람이란 사악한 양심에 시달리며 사느니 제 마음과 평화로이 죽는 편이 낫기 때문이오!"], ["What answer could we give Munro, when he asked us where and how we left his children?\"", "우리가 〈먼로〉에게, 그분이 어디서 또 어떻게 제 자식들을 두고 왔느냐 물으신다면, 무어라 답할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["\"Go to him, and say, that you left them with a message to hasten to their aid,\" returned Cora, advancing nigher to the scout, in her generous ardor; \"that the Hurons bear them into the northern wilds, but that by vigilance and speed they may yet be rescued; and if, after all, it should please heaven that his assistance come too late, bear to him,\" she continued, her voice gradually lowering, until it seemed nearly choked, \"the love, the blessings, the final prayers of his daughters, and bid him not mourn their early fate, but to look forward with humble confidence to the Christian's goal to meet his children.\"", "\"그분께 가서, 그들을 구하러 서둘러 와 달라는 전갈과 함께 두고 왔다고 전하세요.\" 〈코라〉가 너그러운 열의로 척후에게 더 가까이 다가서며 응답했다. \"〈휴런〉들이 그들을 북쪽 황야로 데려가지만, 빈틈없이 서두른다면 아직 구해 낼 수 있다고요. 그리고 만에 하나, 끝내 하늘의 뜻으로 그분의 도움이 너무 늦게 닿는다면, 그분께 전해 주세요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었으니, 그 목소리는 차츰 낮아져 거의 목이 메는 듯했다. \"딸들의 사랑과, 축복과, 마지막 기도를요. 그리고 그들의 이른 운명을 슬퍼하지 마시고, 겸손한 믿음으로 자식들을 만날 기독교도의 종착지를 바라보시라 일러 주세요.\""]], [["The hard, weather-beaten features of the scout began to work, and when she had ended, he dropped his chin to his hand, like a man musing profoundly on the nature of the proposal.", "척후의 모질고 풍상에 시달린 이목구비가 씰룩이기 시작했고, 그녀가 말을 마치자 그는 그 제안의 본질을 깊이 곱씹는 사람처럼 턱을 손에 떨구었다."]], [["\"There is reason in her words!\"", "\"저 여인의 말에는 일리가 있어!\" 마침내 그의 앙다물려 떨리는 입술에서 말이 터져 나왔다."], ["at length broke from his compressed and trembling lips; \"ay, and they bear the spirit of Christianity; what might be right and proper in a redskin, may be sinful in a man who has not even a cross in blood to plead for his ignorance.", "\"그래, 그리고 그 말에는 기독교의 정신이 담겨 있어. 붉은 피부에게는 옳고 마땅한 일일지라도, 제 무지를 변명할 핏줄의 한 점 섞임조차 없는 사내에게는 죄가 될 수 있지."], ["Chingachgook!", "〈칭가치국〉!"], ["Uncas!", "〈언카스〉!"], ["hear you the talk of the dark-eyed woman!\"", "검은 눈의 여인이 하는 말을 들었나!\""]], [["He now spoke in Delaware to his companions, and his address, though calm and deliberate, seemed very decided.", "이제 그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 동료들에게 이야기했으니, 그의 말투는 차분하고 신중했으나 매우 단호한 듯했다."], ["The elder Mohican heard him with deep gravity, and appeared to ponder on his words, as though he felt the importance of their import.", "나이 든 〈모히칸〉은 깊은 진지함으로 그 말을 들었고, 그 의미의 중대함을 느끼는 듯 그의 말을 곰곰이 따져 보는 듯했다."], ["After a moment of hesitation, he waved his hand in assent, and uttered the English word \"Good!\" with the peculiar emphasis of his people.", "잠시 망설인 끝에, 그는 동의의 표시로 손을 흔들며 제 종족 특유의 강세로 영어 단어 \"좋다!\"를 내뱉었다."], ["Then, replacing his knife and tomahawk in his girdle, the warrior moved silently to the edge of the rock which was most concealed from the banks of the river.", "그러고는 칼과 토마호크를 다시 허리띠에 꽂고, 그 전사는 강기슭에서 가장 잘 가려진 바위 가장자리로 말없이 옮겨 갔다."], ["Here he paused a moment, pointed significantly to the woods below, and saying a few words in his own language, as if indicating his intended route, he dropped into the water, and sank from before the eyes of the witnesses of his movements.", "여기서 그는 잠시 멈춰, 아래쪽 숲을 의미심장하게 가리키고, 마치 자신이 가려는 길을 일러 주듯 제 말로 몇 마디 한 다음, 물속으로 떨어져 들어가, 자기 움직임을 지켜보던 이들의 눈앞에서 가라앉아 사라졌다."]], [["The scout delayed his departure to speak to the generous girl, whose breathing became lighter as she saw the success of her remonstrance.", "척후는 떠남을 미루고 그 너그러운 처녀에게 말을 건넸으니, 처녀는 자신의 간언이 먹혀든 것을 보고 숨결이 한결 가벼워졌다."]], [["\"Wisdom is sometimes given to the young, as well as to the old,\"", "\"지혜라는 것은 때로 늙은이만이 아니라 젊은이에게도 주어지는 법이오.\""], ["he said; \"and what you have spoken is wise, not to call it by a better word.", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 당신이 한 말은 현명하오, 그보다 더 나은 말로 부르지 않더라도."], ["If you are led into the woods, that is such of you as may be spared for a while, break the twigs on the bushes as you pass, and make the marks of your trail as broad as you can, when, if mortal eyes can see them, depend on having a friend who will follow to the ends of 'arth afore he desarts you.\"", "만일 당신들이 숲으로 끌려간다면—그러니까 당신들 가운데 한동안 목숨을 부지하게 될 자가 말이오—지나가면서 덤불의 잔가지를 꺾어, 당신들 자취의 흔적을 되도록 넓게 남겨 두시오. 그러면, 사람의 눈으로 그것을 볼 수만 있다면, 당신들을 버리느니 차라리 땅끝까지라도 뒤따라갈 벗이 하나 있음을 믿어도 좋소.\""]], [["He gave Cora an affectionate shake of the hand, lifted his rifle, and after regarding it a moment with melancholy solicitude, laid it carefully aside, and descended to the place where Chingachgook had just disappeared.", "그는 〈코라〉의 손을 다정하게 한 번 쥐고 흔든 다음, 소총을 들어 잠시 침울한 근심으로 그것을 바라보다가, 조심스레 한쪽에 내려놓고, 〈칭가치국〉이 방금 사라진 자리로 내려갔다."], ["For an instant he hung suspended by the rock; and looking about him, with a countenance of peculiar care, he added, bitterly, \"Had the powder held out, this disgrace could never have befallen!\"", "한순간 그는 바위에 매달려 있었고, 유난히 근심 어린 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 쓰라리게 덧붙였다. \"화약만 떨어지지 않았더라면, 이런 치욕은 결코 닥치지 않았을 텐데!\""], ["then, loosening his hold, the water closed above his head, and he also became lost to view.", "그러고는 잡은 것을 놓자, 물이 그의 머리 위에서 닫혔고, 그 또한 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["All eyes were now turned on Uncas, who stood leaning against the ragged rock, in immovable composure.", "이제 모든 눈이 〈언카스〉에게로 향했으니, 그는 꿈쩍 않는 차분함으로 울퉁불퉁한 바위에 기대어 서 있었다."], ["After waiting a short time, Cora pointed down the river, and said:--", "잠시 기다린 끝에, 〈코라〉가 강 아래쪽을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["\"Your friends have not been seen, and are now, most probably, in safety; is it not time for you to follow?\"", "\"당신의 벗들은 보이지 않으니, 이제는 십중팔구 안전하겠지요. 당신도 뒤따라갈 때가 되지 않았나요?\""]], [["\"Uncas will stay,\"", "\"〈언카스〉는 남겠소.\""], ["the young Mohican calmly answered in English.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉이 영어로 차분히 답했다."]], [["\"To increase the horror of our capture, and to diminish the chances of our release!", "\"우리가 사로잡히는 끔찍함을 더하고, 우리가 풀려날 가망을 줄이려고요!"], ["Go, generous young man,\"", "가세요, 너그러운 젊은이여,\""], ["Cora continued, lowering her eyes under the gaze of the Mohican, and, perhaps, with an intuitive consciousness of her power; \"go to my father, as I have said, and be the most confidential of my messengers.", "〈코라〉가 그 〈모히칸〉의 응시 아래 눈길을 내리뜨며, 어쩌면 제가 지닌 힘에 대한 직감적인 자각과 함께 말을 이었다. \"내가 말한 대로, 내 아버지께 가서, 내 가장 믿음직한 전령이 되어 주세요."], ["Tell him to trust you with the means to buy the freedom of his daughters.", "딸들의 자유를 사들일 방도를 당신께 맡기시라 일러 주세요. 가세요!"], ["Go! 'tis my wish, 'tis my prayer, that you will go!\"", "당신이 가는 것, 그것이 내 바람이고, 내 기도예요!\""]], [["The settled, calm look of the young chief changed to an expression of gloom, but he no longer hesitated.", "젊은 족장의 차분히 자리 잡은 표정이 침울한 빛으로 바뀌었으나, 그는 더는 망설이지 않았다."], ["With a noiseless step he crossed the rock, and dropped into the troubled stream.", "소리 없는 걸음으로 그는 바위를 가로질러, 일렁이는 물줄기 속으로 떨어져 들어갔다."], ["Hardly a breath was drawn by those he left behind, until they caught a glimpse of his head emerging for air, far down the current, when he again sank, and was seen no more.", "뒤에 남은 이들은, 그의 머리가 숨을 쉬려 물길 저 아래로 솟아오르는 것을 언뜻 보기까지 거의 숨도 쉬지 못했으니, 그는 다시 가라앉아 더는 보이지 않았다."]], [["These sudden and apparently successful experiments had all taken place in a few minutes of that time which had now become so precious.", "이 갑작스럽고 겉보기엔 성공적인 시도들은 모두, 이제 그토록 소중해진 그 시간의 단 몇 분 사이에 이루어졌다."], ["After the last look at Uncas, Cora turned, and, with a quivering lip, addressed herself to Heyward:--", "〈언카스〉를 마지막으로 한 번 바라본 뒤, 〈코라〉가 돌아서서, 떨리는 입술로 〈헤이워드〉에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["\"I have heard of your boasted skill in the water, too, Duncan,\"", "\"나는 당신이 물에서도 솜씨를 자랑한다고 들었어요, 〈덩컨〉.\""], ["she said; \"follow, then, the wise example set you by these simple and faithful beings.\"", "그녀가 말했다. \"그러니, 이 순박하고 충직한 이들이 당신께 보여 준 현명한 본보기를 따르세요.\""]], [["\"Is such the faith that Cora Munro would exact from her protector?\"", "\"〈코라 먼로〉가 제 보호자에게 요구하는 신의가 그런 것이오?\""], ["said the young man, smiling mournfully, but with bitterness.", "젊은이가 슬프게, 그러나 쓰라림을 담아 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"This is not a time for idle subtleties and false opinions,\"", "\"지금은 부질없는 궤변이나 그릇된 견해를 늘어놓을 때가 아니에요.\""], ["she answered; \"but a moment when every duty should be equally considered.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"모든 의무를 똑같이 헤아려야 할 순간이지요."], ["To us you can be of no further service here, but your precious life may be saved for other and nearer friends.\"", "당신은 이곳에서 우리에게 더는 도움이 될 수 없지만, 당신의 소중한 목숨은 다른, 더 가까운 벗들을 위해 구할 수 있어요.\""]], [["He made no reply, though his eyes fell wistfully on the beautiful form of Alice, who was clinging to his arm with the dependency of an infant.", "그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았으나, 그의 눈은 〈앨리스〉의 아름다운 자태에 애틋하게 머물렀으니, 그녀는 어린아이가 매달리듯 의지하며 그의 팔에 매달려 있었다."]], [["\"Consider,\"", "\"생각해 보세요,\""], ["continued Cora, after a pause, during which she seemed to struggle with a pang even more acute than any that her fears had excited, \"that the worst to us can be but death; a tribute that all must pay at the good time of God's appointment.\"", "〈코라〉가 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤 말을 이었으니, 그동안 그녀는 제 두려움이 불러일으킨 그 어떤 것보다도 더 날카로운 고통과 씨름하는 듯했다. \"우리에게 닥칠 최악이라 해야 죽음뿐, 그것은 하느님께서 정하신 좋은 때에 누구나 치러야 할 대가일 뿐이에요.\""]], [["\"There are evils worse than death,\"", "\"죽음보다 더한 화(禍)도 있소.\""], ["said Duncan, speaking hoarsely, and as if fretful at her importunity, \"but which the presence of one who would die in your behalf may avert.\"", "〈덩컨〉이 쉰 목소리로, 그녀의 집요함에 짜증이 난 듯 말했다. \"허나 당신을 위해 기꺼이 죽을 한 사람이 곁에 있으면 막아 낼 수 있는 화 말이오.\""]], [["Cora ceased her entreaties; and, veiling her face in her shawl, drew the nearly insensible Alice after her into the deepest recess of the inner cavern.", "〈코라〉는 애원을 그쳤다. 그러고는 숄로 얼굴을 가리고, 거의 정신을 잃은 〈앨리스〉를 끌고 안쪽 동굴의 가장 깊은 후미진 곳으로 들어갔다. \"마음껏 즐거워하라."]], [["\"Be gay securely; Dispel, my fair, with smiles, the tim'rous clouds, That hang on thy clear brow.\"", "나의 어여쁜 이여, 미소로 흩어 버려라, 그대의 맑은 이마에\n드리운 그 겁먹은 구름들을.\""]], [["_Death of Agrippina._", "〈아그리피나의 죽음〉"]], [["The sudden and almost magical change, from the stirring incidents of the combat to the stillness that now reigned around him, acted on the heated imagination of Heyward like some exciting dream.", "전투의 격동하는 사건들에서 이제 그를 둘러싼 정적으로의, 그 갑작스럽고 거의 마법 같은 전환은, 〈헤이워드〉의 달아오른 상상에 무슨 흥분된 꿈처럼 작용했다."], ["While all the images and events he had witnessed remained deeply impressed on his memory, he felt a difficulty in persuading himself of their truth.", "그가 목격한 온갖 형상과 사건들이 그의 기억 속에 깊이 새겨진 채 남아 있었으나, 그는 그것들이 사실임을 스스로 납득하기가 어렵게 느껴졌다."], ["Still ignorant of the fate of those who had trusted to the aid of the swift current, he at first listened intently to any signal, or sounds of alarm, which might announce the good or evil fortune of their hazardous undertaking.", "빠른 물살의 도움에 몸을 맡긴 자들의 운명을 아직 모르는 채, 그는 처음에 그들의 위험천만한 시도가 다행한 결말인지 불행한 결말인지를 알릴 만한 어떤 신호나 경고의 소리에 귀를 곤두세웠다."], ["His attention was, however, bestowed in vain; for, with the disappearance of Uncas, every sign of the adventurers had been lost, leaving him in total uncertainty of their fate.", "그러나 그 주의는 헛되이 기울여졌으니, 〈언커스〉가 사라짐과 함께 그 모험가들의 흔적이라곤 죄다 사라져, 그를 그들의 운명에 관한 온전한 불확실 속에 남겨 둔 까닭이었다."]], [["In a moment of such painful doubt, Duncan did not hesitate to look about him, without consulting that protection from the rocks which just before had been so necessary to his safety.", "그토록 고통스러운 의심의 순간에, 〈덩컨〉은 바로 조금 전까지 제 안전에 그토록 긴요했던 바위의 보호를 더는 구하지 않고 망설임 없이 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Every effort, however, to detect the least evidence of the approach of their hidden enemies, was as fruitless as the inquiry after his late companions.", "그러나 숨은 적들이 다가오는 기미를 조금이라도 알아채려는 온갖 노력은 방금 전 동행들의 안부를 물었던 것과 마찬가지로 결실이 없었다."], ["The wooded banks of the rivers seemed again deserted by everything possessing animal life.", "강의 우거진 기슭은 동물적 생명을 지닌 모든 것에게서 다시금 버림받은 듯했다."], ["The uproar which had so lately echoed through the vaults of the forest was gone, leaving the rush of the waters to swell and sink on the currents of the air, in the unmingled sweetness of nature.", "바로 조금 전 숲의 둥근 천장을 울리던 소란은 사라지고, 자연의 섞임 없는 감미로움 속에서 물살이 대기의 흐름을 따라 부풀었다 잦아들었다 하게 남겨 두었다."], ["A fish-hawk, which, secure on the topmost branches of a dead pine, had been a distant spectator of the fray, now stooped from his high and ragged perch, and soared, in wide sweeps, above his prey; while a jay, whose noisy voice had been stilled by the hoarser cries of the savages, ventured again to open his discordant throat, as though once more in undisturbed possession of his wild domains.", "죽은 소나무의 가장 높은 가지에 안전히 앉아 그 싸움을 멀리서 지켜보던 물수리 한 마리가, 이제 그 높고 거친 횃대에서 몸을 굽혀 내려와 제 먹잇감 위로 넓게 선회하며 솟구쳤다. 한편 미개인들의 더 거친 외침에 그 시끄러운 목소리를 잠재웠던 어치 한 마리는, 마치 한 번 더 제 야생의 영역을 평온히 차지한 듯, 다시금 그 거슬리는 목청을 열 엄두를 냈다."], ["Duncan caught from these natural accompaniments of the solitary scene a glimmering of hope; and he began to rally his faculties to renewed exertions, with something like a reviving confidence of success.", "〈덩컨〉은 이 외딴 풍경에 깃든 자연의 이런 화답들에서 희미한 희망의 빛을 붙잡았고, 다시금 성공을 확신하는 듯한 마음을 되살려, 새로운 분발을 향해 제 기력을 추스르기 시작했다."]], [["\"The Hurons are not to be seen,\"", "\"〈휴런〉족은 보이지 않소.\" 그가 〈데이비드〉에게 말을 건네며 이르렀으니, 그는 자신이 받은 정신이 아득해지는 일격의 여파에서 조금도 회복하지 못한 터였다."], ["he said, addressing David, who had by no means recovered from the effects of the stunning blow he had received; \"let us conceal ourselves in the cavern, and trust the rest to Providence.\"", "\"동굴 속에 몸을 숨깁시다. 그리고 나머지는 섭리에 맡깁시다.\""]], [["\"I remember to have united with two comely maidens, in lifting up our voices in praise and thanksgiving,\"", "\"내 기억하건대 두 어여쁜 처녀와 더불어 찬미와 감사로 목소리를 드높였소만.\""], ["returned the bewildered singing-master; \"since which time I have been visited by a heavy judgment for my sins.", "어리둥절한 성가 선생이 대답했다. \"그 후로 내 죄에 대한 무거운 심판이 내게 임하였소."], ["I have been mocked with the likeness of sleep, while sounds of discord have rent my ears, such as might manifest the fulness of time, and that nature had forgotten her harmony.\"", "나는 잠의 형상으로 조롱당하였고, 그러는 동안 불협화의 소리들이 내 귀를 찢었으니, 마치 때가 다 찼음을, 그리고 자연이 제 조화를 잊었음을 드러내려는 듯하였소.\""]], [["\"Poor fellow!", "\"가엾은 사람!"], ["thine own period was, in truth, near its accomplishment!", "참으로 그대 자신의 시간이 거의 다 차 갔던 게요!"], ["But arouse, and come with me; I will lead you where all other sounds but those of your own psalmody shall be excluded.\"", "허나 정신을 차리고 나와 함께 갑시다. 그대 자신의 성가 외에 다른 모든 소리가 차단될 곳으로 내 그대를 데려가리다.\""]], [["\"There is melody in the fall of the cataract, and the rushing of many waters is sweet to the senses!\"", "\"폭포가 떨어지는 데에 선율이 있고, 큰 물들이 쏟아지는 소리는 감각에 감미롭소!\""], ["said David, pressing his hand confusedly on his brow.", "〈데이비드〉가 어수선하게 제 이마를 손으로 누르며 말했다."], ["\"Is not the air yet filled with shrieks and cries, as though the departed spirits of the damned--\"", "\"저주받은 자들의 떠난 혼령들이 그러하듯, 공중은 아직도 비명과 외침으로 가득하지 않소—\""]], [["\"Not now, not now,\"", "\"이젠 아니오, 이젠 아니오.\""], ["interrupted the impatient Heyward, \"they have ceased, and they who raised them, I trust in God, they are gone too!", "참다못한 〈헤이워드〉가 가로막았다. \"그것들은 그쳤고, 그것들을 내질렀던 자들도, 하느님께 바라건대, 그들 역시 떠났소!"], ["everything but the water is still and at peace; in, then, where you may create those sounds you love so well to hear.\"", "물 말고는 모든 것이 고요하고 평온하오. 그러니 들어가시오, 그대가 그토록 즐겨 듣는 그 소리들을 만들어 낼 수 있는 곳으로.\""]], [["David smiled sadly, though not without a momentary gleam of pleasure, at this allusion to his beloved vocation.", "이 사랑하는 천직에 대한 언급에, 〈데이비드〉는 한순간의 기쁨의 빛이 없지는 않았으나, 슬프게 미소 지었다."], ["He no longer hesitated to be led to a spot which promised such unalloyed gratification to his wearied senses; and, leaning on the arm of his companion, he entered the narrow mouth of the cave.", "그는 제 지친 감각에 그토록 순수한 만족을 약속하는 곳으로 이끌려 가기를 더는 망설이지 않았다. 그리고 동행의 팔에 기대어 동굴의 좁은 입구로 들어섰다."], ["Duncan seized a pile of the sassafras, which he drew before the passage, studiously concealing every appearance of an aperture.", "〈덩컨〉은 사사프라스 더미를 움켜잡아 통로 앞으로 끌어다 놓아, 구멍이 있다는 기색을 일절 보이지 않도록 세심히 가렸다."], ["Within this fragile barrier he arranged the blankets abandoned by the foresters, darkening the inner extremity of the cavern, while its outer received a chastened light from the narrow ravine, through which one arm of the river rushed, to form the junction with its sister branch, a few rods below.", "이 허술한 차단막 안쪽에 그는 숲사람들이 버리고 간 담요를 펼쳐, 동굴 안쪽 끝을 어둡게 했다. 한편 그 바깥쪽은 좁은 협곡에서 누그러진 빛을 받아들였으니, 그 협곡으로는 강의 한 줄기가 쏟아져 내려, 몇 로드 아래에서 제 자매 줄기와 합류를 이루었다."]], [["\"I like not that principle of the natives, which teaches them to submit without a struggle, in emergencies that appear desperate,\"", "\"절망적으로 보이는 위급한 상황에서 싸움 없이 굴복하라고 가르치는 원주민들의 그 원칙이 나는 마음에 들지 않소.\" 그가 이 일에 분주한 동안 말했다."], ["he said, while busied in this employment; \"our own maxim, which says, 'while life remains there is hope,'", "\"'목숨이 붙어 있는 한 희망은 있다'고 하는 우리 자신의 격언이 더 위로가 되고, 군인의 기질에 더 잘 맞소."], ["is more consoling, and better suited to a soldier's temperament.", "〈코라〉, 당신에게는 부질없는 격려의 말을 늘어놓지 않으리다."], ["To you, Cora, I will urge no words of idle encouragement; your own fortitude and undisturbed reason will teach you all that may become your sex; but cannot we dry the tears of that trembling weeper on your bosom?\"", "당신 자신의 굳센 마음과 흐트러지지 않은 이성이, 당신의 성에 어울릴 모든 것을 당신에게 가르쳐 줄 테니. 허나 당신 가슴에 안긴 저 떨고 있는 가엾은 이의 눈물은 우리가 닦아 줄 수 없겠소?\""]], [["\"I am calmer, Duncan,\"", "\"이젠 더 차분해요, 〈덩컨〉.\""], ["said Alice, raising herself from the arms of her sister, and forcing an appearance of composure through her tears; \"much calmer, now.", "〈앨리스〉가 언니의 품에서 몸을 일으키며, 눈물을 머금은 채 애써 침착한 모습을 지어 보이며 말했다. \"이젠 훨씬 차분해요."], ["Surely, in this hidden spot we are safe, we are secret, free from injury; we will hope everything from those generous men who have risked so much already in our behalf.\"", "분명, 이 숨은 곳에서 우리는 안전하고, 우리는 들키지 않았으며, 해를 입을 일도 없어요. 우리는 이미 우리를 위해 그토록 많은 위험을 무릅쓴 저 너그러운 분들에게서 모든 것을 바랄 거예요.\""]], [["\"Now does our gentle Alice speak like a daughter of Munro!\"", "\"이제 우리의 다정한 〈앨리스〉가 〈먼로〉의 딸답게 말하는군요!\""], ["said Heyward, pausing to press her hand as he passed towards the outer entrance of the cavern.", "〈헤이워드〉가 동굴의 바깥쪽 입구를 향해 지나가다 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐려 걸음을 멈추며 말했다."], ["\"With two such examples of courage before him, a man would be ashamed to prove other than a hero.\"", "\"그토록 용감한 본보기 둘을 눈앞에 두고서, 사내라면 영웅이 아닌 다른 무엇으로 드러나기를 부끄러워할 거요.\""], ["He then seated himself in the centre of the cavern, grasping his remaining pistol with a hand convulsively clenched, while his contracted and frowning eye announced the sullen desperation of his purpose.", "그러고 나서 그는 동굴 한가운데에 자리 잡고 앉아, 발작하듯 움켜쥔 손으로 남은 권총 한 자루를 쥐었으니, 한편 그 찌푸려 모은 눈은 제 결심의 침울한 결사를 알리고 있었다."], ["\"The Hurons, if they come, may not gain our position so easily as they think,\"", "\"〈휴런〉족이 온다 해도, 제 생각만큼 우리 위치를 그리 쉽게 얻지는 못할 거요.\""], ["he lowly muttered; and dropping his head back against the rock, he seemed to await the result in patience, though his gaze was unceasingly bent on the open avenue to their place of retreat.", "그가 낮게 중얼거렸다. 그리고 머리를 뒤로 젖혀 바위에 기댄 채, 그는 인내로 결과를 기다리는 듯했으나, 그 시선은 그들의 은신처로 트인 통로를 향해 쉼 없이 쏠려 있었다."]], [["With the last sound of his voice, a deep, a long, and almost breathless silence succeeded.", "그의 목소리의 마지막 울림과 함께, 깊고 긴, 거의 숨조차 멎은 듯한 정적이 뒤따랐다."], ["The fresh air of the morning had penetrated the recess, and its influence was gradually felt on the spirits of its inmates.", "아침의 신선한 공기가 그 후미진 곳으로 스며들었고, 그 영향이 그 안에 있는 이들의 마음에 차츰 느껴졌다."], ["As minute after minute passed by, leaving them in undisturbed security, the insinuating feeling of hope was gradually gaining possession of every bosom, though each one felt reluctant to give utterance to expectations that the next moment might so fearfully destroy.", "한 순간 한 순간이 지나가며 그들을 흔들림 없는 안전 속에 남겨 두자, 슬며시 파고드는 희망의 감정이 차츰 모든 가슴을 사로잡았으나, 저마다 다음 순간 그토록 무섭게 깨질 수도 있는 기대를 입 밖에 내기를 꺼렸다."]], [["David alone formed an exception to these varying emotions.", "이 변덕스러운 감정에 예외를 이룬 것은 〈데이비드〉뿐이었다."], ["A gleam of light from the opening crossed his wan countenance, and fell upon the pages of the little volume, whose leaves he was again occupied in turning, as if searching for some song more fitted to their condition than any that had yet met his eye.", "입구에서 들어온 한 줄기 빛이 그의 핏기 없는 얼굴을 가로질러, 그 책장이 다시 넘어가고 있던 작은 책의 면들 위에 떨어졌으니, 그는 마치 지금껏 눈에 든 어떤 노래보다도 그들의 처지에 더 어울리는 노래를 찾기라도 하듯 책장을 넘기고 있었다."], ["He was, most probably, acting all this time under a confused recollection of the promised consolation of Duncan.", "그는 십중팔구 이 내내 〈덩컨〉이 약속한 위안에 대한 어렴풋한 기억을 좇아 이 모든 일을 하고 있었으리라."], ["At length, it would seem, his patient industry found its reward; for, without explanation or apology, he pronounced aloud the words \"Isle of Wight,\" drew a long, sweet sound from his pitch-pipe, and then ran through the preliminary modulations of the air, whose name he had just mentioned with the sweeter tones of his own musical voice.", "마침내, 보아하니 그의 끈질긴 노고가 보답을 찾은 듯했으니, 아무런 설명이나 사과도 없이 그는 \"〈와이트섬〉\"이라는 말을 소리 내어 발음하고, 음정 피리에서 길고 감미로운 소리를 끌어낸 다음, 방금 그 이름을 말한 곡조의 도입 변조를, 제 자신의 음악적 목소리의 더 감미로운 음색으로 훑어 나갔다."]], [["\"May not this prove dangerous?\"", "\"이게 위험하지 않을까요?\""], ["asked Cora, glancing her dark eye at Major Heyward.", "〈코라〉가 〈헤이워드〉 소령에게 검은 눈길을 던지며 물었다."]], [["\"Poor fellow!", "\"가엾은 사람!"], ["his voice is too feeble to be heard amid the din of the falls,\"", "그의 목소리는 폭포의 소란 속에서 들리기엔 너무 약하오.\""], ["was the answer; \"besides, the cavern will prove his friend.", "대답이 돌아왔다. \"게다가 동굴이 그의 편이 되어 줄 거요."], ["Let him indulge his passion, since it may be done without hazard.\"", "위험 없이 할 수 있는 일이니, 그의 열정을 마음껏 누리게 둡시다.\""]], [["\"Isle of Wight!\"", "\"〈와이트섬〉!\""], ["repeated David, looking about him with that dignity with which he had long been wont to silence the whispering echoes of his school; \"'tis a brave tune, and set to solemn words; let it be sung with meet respect!\"", "〈데이비드〉가 오랫동안 제 학교의 소곤대는 메아리를 잠재울 때 익혀 온 그 위엄으로 주위를 둘러보며 되풀이했다. \"이것은 장한 곡조요, 엄숙한 가사에 붙은 것이니, 마땅한 경의로써 부를지어다!\""]], [["After allowing a moment of stillness, to enforce his discipline, the voice of the singer was heard, in low, murmuring syllables, gradually stealing on the ear, until it filled the narrow vault with sounds rendered trebly thrilling by the feeble and tremulous utterance produced by his debility.", "제 규율을 다지고자 한순간의 정적을 허락한 뒤, 그 가수의 목소리가 낮고 웅얼대는 음절로 들려와, 차츰 귓가에 스며들더니, 이윽고 그 좁은 둥근 천장을 가득 채웠으니, 그의 쇠약함이 빚어낸 미약하고 떨리는 발성으로 인해 세 곱으로 짜릿하게 울리는 소리였다."], ["The melody, which no weakness could destroy, gradually wrought its sweet influence on the senses of those who heard it.", "그 어떤 허약함으로도 무너뜨릴 수 없는 그 선율은, 듣는 이들의 감각에 차츰 그 감미로운 영향을 미쳐 갔다."], ["It even prevailed over the miserable travesty of the song of David which the singer had selected from a volume of similar effusions, and caused the sense to be forgotten in the insinuating harmony of the sounds.", "그것은 그 가수가 비슷한 토로들을 모은 한 권에서 골라낸 〈데이비드〉의 노래의 그 가련한 졸렬한 모방마저 압도하여, 그 슬며시 파고드는 소리의 조화 속에 가사의 의미를 잊게 만들었다."], ["Alice unconsciously dried her tears, and bent her melting eyes on the pallid features of Gamut with an expression of chastened delight that she neither affected nor wished to conceal.", "〈앨리스〉는 저도 모르게 눈물을 닦고, 녹아내리는 듯한 눈을 〈가뮤트〉의 핏기 없는 얼굴에 쏟았으니, 그 표정은 그녀가 꾸미지도 감추려 하지도 않은 정화된 기쁨이었다."], ["Cora bestowed an approving smile on the pious efforts of the namesake of the Jewish prince, and Heyward soon turned his steady, stern look from the outlet of the cavern, to fasten it, with a milder character, on the face of David, or to meet the wandering beams which at moments strayed from the humid eyes of Alice.", "〈코라〉는 그 유대 왕자와 이름이 같은 이의 경건한 노력에 인정하는 미소를 보냈고, 〈헤이워드〉는 곧 그 한결같고 준엄한 눈길을 동굴의 출구에서 거두어, 더 부드러운 빛깔로 〈데이비드〉의 얼굴에 못박거나, 때때로 〈앨리스〉의 촉촉한 눈에서 새어 나오는 흩어진 빛줄기와 마주했다."], ["The open sympathy of the listeners stirred the spirit of the votary of music, whose voice regained its richness and volume, without losing that touching softness which proved its secret charm.", "듣는 이들의 숨김없는 공감은 그 음악의 신봉자의 마음을 움직였으니, 그의 목소리는 그 은밀한 매력을 입증하던 가슴 저미는 부드러움을 잃지 않으면서도 그 풍부함과 성량을 되찾았다."], ["Exerting his renovated powers to their utmost, he was yet filling the arches of the cave with long and full tones, when a yell burst into the air without, that instantly stilled his pious strains, choking his voice suddenly, as though his heart had literally bounded into the passage of his throat.", "새로워진 기력을 한껏 쥐어짜며, 그가 여전히 동굴의 둥근 천장을 길고 충만한 음으로 채우고 있을 때, 바깥에서 한 비명이 공중으로 터져 나와, 그 즉시 그의 경건한 가락을 멎게 했으니, 마치 그의 심장이 정말로 목구멍의 통로로 뛰어든 듯, 그의 목소리를 별안간 틀어막아 버렸다."]], [["\"We are lost!\"", "\"우린 끝났어요!\""], ["exclaimed Alice, throwing herself into the arms of Cora.", "〈앨리스〉가 〈코라〉의 품에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다."]], [["\"Not yet, not yet,\"", "\"아직 아니오, 아직 아니오.\""], ["returned the agitated but undaunted Heyward; \"the sound came from the centre of the island, and it has been produced by the sight of their dead companions.", "동요했으나 굴하지 않은 〈헤이워드〉가 대답했다. \"그 소리는 섬 한가운데서 났고, 죽은 동료들을 본 것이 빚어낸 것이오."], ["We are not yet discovered, and there is still hope.\"", "우린 아직 들키지 않았고, 아직 희망이 있소.\""]], [["Faint and almost despairing as was the prospect of escape, the words of Duncan were not thrown away, for it awakened the powers of the sisters in such a manner that they awaited the result in silence.", "탈출의 가망은 희미하고 거의 절망적이었으나, 〈덩컨〉의 말은 헛되이 흘러가지 않았으니, 그것이 자매들의 기력을 일깨워 그들로 하여금 침묵 속에 결과를 기다리게 한 까닭이었다."], ["A second yell soon followed the first, when a rush of voices was heard pouring down the island, from its upper to its lower extremity, until they reached the naked rock above the caverns, where, after a shout of savage triumph, the air continued full of horrible cries and screams, such as man alone can utter, and he only when in a state of the fiercest barbarity.", "곧 첫 비명에 이어 둘째 비명이 뒤따랐고, 이윽고 한 무리의 목소리가 섬의 위쪽 끝에서 아래쪽 끝까지 쏟아져 내리는 것이 들렸으니, 그들이 동굴 위의 헐벗은 바위에 다다르자, 미개한 승리의 함성이 터진 뒤로, 공중은 오직 인간만이 내지를 수 있는, 그것도 더없이 사나운 야만의 상태에 있을 때라야 내지를 수 있는 그런 끔찍한 외침과 비명으로 가득 찼다."]], [["The sounds quickly spread around them in every direction.", "그 소리는 사방 모든 방향으로 그들 주위에 빠르게 퍼졌다."], ["Some called to their fellows from the water's edge, and were answered from the heights above.", "더러는 물가에서 제 동료들을 불렀고, 위쪽 높은 데서 화답이 돌아왔다."], ["Cries were heard in the startling vicinity of the chasm between the two caves, which mingled with hoarser yells that arose out of the abyss of the deep ravine.", "두 동굴 사이의 틈, 그 깜짝 놀랄 만치 가까운 데서 외침이 들려왔으니, 깊은 협곡의 심연에서 솟아오르는 더 거친 비명과 뒤섞였다."], ["In short, so rapidly had the savage sounds diffused themselves over the barren rock, that it was not difficult for the anxious listeners to imagine they could be heard beneath, as in truth they were above and on every side of them.", "요컨대, 그 미개한 소리가 헐벗은 바위 위로 어찌나 빠르게 퍼졌던지, 근심에 잠긴 청자들이 그것이 자기들 아래에서도 들린다고 상상하기란 어렵지 않았으니, 사실인즉 그것은 그들의 위에도 사방에도 있었다."]], [["In the midst of this tumult, a triumphant yell was raised within a few yards of the hidden entrance to the cave.", "이 소란의 한가운데서, 동굴의 숨은 입구로부터 몇 야드 안쪽에서 한 의기양양한 비명이 일어났다."], ["Heyward abandoned every hope, with the belief it was the signal that they were discovered.", "〈헤이워드〉는 그것이 자기들이 발각되었다는 신호라고 믿고 온갖 희망을 버렸다."], ["Again the impression passed away, as he heard the voices collect near the spot where the white man had so reluctantly abandoned his rifle.", "그러나 그 백인이 그토록 마지못해 자신의 소총을 버린 그 지점 가까이로 목소리들이 모여드는 것을 듣자, 그 인상은 다시 사라졌다."], ["Amid the jargon of the Indian dialects that he now plainly heard, it was easy to distinguish not only words, but sentences, in the _patois_ of the Canadas.", "이제 또렷이 들리는 인디언 방언들의 알아들을 수 없는 말 가운데, 단어뿐 아니라 문장까지도 〈캐나다〉 사투리로 구별해 내기란 쉬웠다."], ["A burst of voices had shouted simultaneously, \"La Longue Carabine!\"", "한 무리의 목소리가 일제히 \"라 롱그 카라빈!\""], ["causing the opposite woods to re-echo with a name which, Heyward well remembered, had been given by his enemies to a celebrated hunter and scout of the English camp, and who, he now learnt for the first time, had been his late companion.", "하고 외쳐, 맞은편 숲이 한 이름을 메아리치게 했으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 잘 기억하건대 그것은 적들이 〈영국〉 진영의 어느 이름난 사냥꾼이자 척후에게 붙인 이름이었고, 그가 이제 처음으로 알게 된 바, 그자가 바로 그의 방금 전 동행이었던 것이다."]], [["\"La Longue Carabine!", "\"라 롱그 카라빈!"], ["La Longue Carabine!\"", "라 롱그 카라빈!\""], ["passed from mouth to mouth, until the whole band appeared to be collected around a trophy which would seem to announce the death of its formidable owner.", "그 말이 입에서 입으로 옮겨 다녔으니, 이윽고 그 무리 전체가, 그 가공할 임자의 죽음을 알리는 듯한 전리품 하나를 둘러싸고 모여든 것처럼 보였다."], ["After a vociferous consultation, which was, at times, deafened by bursts of savage joy, they again separated, filling the air with the name of a foe, whose body, Heyward could collect from their expressions, they hoped to find concealed in some crevice of the island.", "떠들썩한 의논 끝에 — 그것은 때때로 미개한 환희의 폭발에 묻혀 들리지 않았다 — 그들은 다시 흩어지며, 그 적의 이름으로 공중을 채웠으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 그들의 말에서 알아낼 수 있었던바, 그들은 그 적의 시신이 섬의 어느 갈라진 틈에 숨겨져 있기를 바라며 찾고 있었다."]], [["\"Now,\"", "\"이제.\""], ["he whispered to the trembling sisters, \"now is the moment of uncertainty!", "그가 떠는 자매들에게 속삭였다. \"이제가 불확실한 순간이오!"], ["if our place of retreat escape this scrutiny, we are still safe!", "우리 은신처가 이 수색을 면한다면, 우린 아직 안전하오!"], ["In every event, we are assured, by what has fallen from our enemies, that our friends have escaped, and in two short hours we may look for succor from Webb.\"", "어떤 경우든, 우리 적들의 입에서 새어 나온 말로 미루어, 우리 친구들이 무사히 빠져나갔음은 분명하니, 두어 시간 안에 〈웨브〉에게서 구원을 기대할 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["There were now a few minutes of fearful stillness, during which Heyward well knew that the savages conducted their search with greater vigilance and method.", "이제 무서운 정적이 몇 분간 흘렀으니, 그동안 미개인들이 한층 더 빈틈없고 체계적으로 수색을 벌인다는 것을 〈헤이워드〉는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["More than once he could distinguish their footsteps, as they brushed the sassafras, causing the faded leaves to rustle, and the branches to snap.", "그는 한 번 이상, 그들이 사사프라스를 스쳐 시든 잎이 바스락거리고 가지가 우지끈하는 소리에서 그들의 발걸음을 구별해 낼 수 있었다."], ["At length, the pile yielded a little, a corner of the blanket fell, and a faint ray of light gleamed into the inner part of the cave.", "마침내 그 더미가 조금 무너지며, 담요 한 귀퉁이가 떨어졌고, 희미한 빛 한 줄기가 동굴 안쪽으로 새어 들었다."], ["Cora folded Alice to her bosom in agony, and Duncan sprang to his feet.", "〈코라〉는 비통함 속에 〈앨리스〉를 제 가슴에 끌어안았고, 〈덩컨〉은 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["A shout was at that moment heard, as if issuing from the centre of the rock, announcing that the neighboring cavern had at length been entered.", "바로 그 순간 한 함성이 들려왔으니, 마치 바위 한가운데서 솟아나오는 듯, 이웃한 동굴에 마침내 발을 들였음을 알리는 것이었다."], ["In a minute, the number and loudness of the voices indicated that the whole party was collected in and around that secret place.", "잠깐 사이에, 목소리의 수와 우렁참은 그 무리 전체가 그 비밀스러운 곳 안과 그 주위에 모였음을 일러 주었다."]], [["As the inner passages to the two caves were so close to each other, Duncan, believing that escape was no longer possible, passed David and the sisters, to place himself between the latter and the first onset of the terrible meeting.", "두 동굴로 통하는 안쪽 통로가 서로 그토록 가까운 까닭에, 〈덩컨〉은 이제 탈출이 더는 가능하지 않다고 믿고, 〈데이비드〉와 자매들을 지나, 그 자매들과 그 끔찍한 마주침의 첫 들이닥침 사이에 제 몸을 놓으려 했다."], ["Grown desperate by his situation, he drew nigh the slight barrier which separated him only by a few feet from his relentless pursuers, and placing his face to the casual opening, he even looked out, with a sort of desperate indifference, on their movements.", "제 처지에 절망한 나머지, 그는 자신을 그 무자비한 추격자들과 겨우 몇 피트로 갈라놓을 뿐인 그 허술한 차단막에 다가가, 우연히 난 틈에 얼굴을 대고, 일종의 절망적인 무심함으로 그들의 움직임을 내다보기까지 했다."]], [["Within reach of his arm was the brawny shoulder of a gigantic Indian, whose deep and authoritative voice appeared to give directions to the proceedings of his fellows.", "그의 팔이 닿을 거리에는 한 거대한 인디언의 우람한 어깨가 있었으니, 그 깊고 권위 있는 목소리는 동료들의 일 처리에 지시를 내리는 듯했다."], ["Beyond him again, Duncan could look into the vault opposite, which was filled with savages, upturning and rifling the humble furniture of the scout.", "그 너머로 다시, 〈덩컨〉은 맞은편 동굴 안을 들여다볼 수 있었는데, 그곳은 미개인들로 가득 차, 그 척후의 초라한 세간을 뒤집고 샅샅이 뒤지고 있었다."], ["The wound of David had dyed the leaves of sassafras with a color that the natives well knew was anticipating the season.", "〈데이비드〉의 상처가 사사프라스 잎을 한 빛깔로 물들여 놓았으니, 원주민들은 그것이 계절을 앞지른 것임을 잘 알았다."], ["Over this sign of their success, they set up a howl, like an opening from so many hounds who had recovered a lost trail.", "자신들의 성공을 드러내는 이 표시를 두고, 그들은 잃었던 자취를 되찾은 그 많은 사냥개들이 짖어 대듯 울부짖음을 일으켰다."], ["After this yell of victory, they tore up the fragrant bed of the cavern, and bore the branches into the chasm, scattering the boughs, as if they suspected them of concealing the person of the man they had so long hated and feared.", "이 승리의 비명 뒤에, 그들은 동굴의 그 향기로운 잠자리를 헤집어 그 가지들을 협곡으로 날라, 마치 그것이 그토록 오래 미워하고 두려워한 그 사람을 숨기고 있기라도 한 듯, 그 가지들을 흩뿌렸다."], ["One fierce and wild-looking warrior approached the chief bearing a load of the brush, and pointing, exultingly, to the deep red stains with which it was sprinkled, uttered his joy in Indian yells, whose meaning Heyward was only enabled to comprehend by the frequent repetition of the name of \"La Longue Carabine!\"", "사납고 야만스러운 행색의 한 전사가 덤불을 한 짐 짊어지고 그 우두머리에게 다가가, 거기 뿌려진 짙은 핏자국을 의기양양하게 가리키며, 인디언 비명으로 제 환희를 내질렀으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 그 뜻을 헤아릴 수 있었던 것은 오직 \"라 롱그 카라빈!\"이라는 이름의 잦은 되풀이 덕분이었다."], ["When his triumph had ceased, he cast the brush on the slight heap that Duncan had made before the entrance of the second cavern, and closed the view.", "그의 의기양양함이 그치자, 그는 그 덤불을 〈덩컨〉이 둘째 동굴의 입구 앞에 쌓아 둔 그 허술한 더미 위로 던져, 시야를 막아 버렸다."], ["His example was followed by others, who, as they drew the branches from the cave of the scout, threw them into one pile, adding, unconsciously, to the security of those they sought.", "그의 본보기를 다른 이들이 따랐으니, 그들은 척후의 동굴에서 가지를 끌어내며 그것을 한 더미에 던져, 저도 모르게 자기들이 찾는 자들의 안전을 더해 주었다."], ["The very slightness of the defence was its chief merit, for no one thought of disturbing a mass of brush, which all of them believed, in that moment of hurry and confusion, had been accidentally raised by the hands of their own party.", "그 방어막의 바로 그 허술함이 그 으뜸가는 장점이었으니, 아무도 그 덤불 더미를 들쑤셔 볼 생각을 하지 않은 까닭이었다. 그들 모두가 그 서두름과 혼란의 순간에, 그것이 자기네 무리의 손에 우연히 쌓인 것이라고 믿었기 때문이다."]], [["As the blankets yielded before the outward pressure, and the branches settled in the fissure of the rock by their own weight, forming a compact body, Duncan once more breathed freely.", "담요가 바깥의 압력에 밀려나고, 가지들이 제 무게로 바위 틈에 내려앉아 빽빽한 한 덩이를 이루자, 〈덩컨〉은 다시 한번 자유로이 숨 쉬었다."], ["With a light step, and lighter heart, he returned to the centre of the cave, and took the place he had left, where he could command a view of the opening next the river.", "가벼운 걸음으로, 그리고 더 가벼운 마음으로, 그는 동굴 한가운데로 돌아가 자신이 떠났던 자리, 강 쪽의 입구를 내다볼 수 있는 그 자리를 차지했다."], ["While he was in the act of making this movement, the Indians, as if changing their purpose by a common impulse, broke away from the cavern in a body, and were heard rushing up the island again, towards the point whence they had originally descended.", "그가 이 움직임을 행하고 있던 바로 그때, 인디언들이 마치 공통의 충동으로 그 목적을 바꾸기라도 한 듯, 한 무리로 동굴에서 떨어져 나와, 다시 섬을 거슬러 올라, 그들이 본래 내려왔던 지점을 향해 달려가는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Here another wailing cry betrayed that they were again collected around the bodies of their dead comrades.", "이곳에서 또 한 차례의 통곡하는 외침이, 그들이 다시 죽은 동료들의 시신을 둘러싸고 모였음을 일러 주었다."]], [["Duncan now ventured to look at his companions; for, during the most critical moments of their danger, he had been apprehensive that the anxiety of his countenance might communicate some additional alarm to those who were so little able to sustain it.", "이제 〈덩컨〉은 제 동행들을 바라볼 엄두를 냈으니, 위험의 가장 위태로운 순간들 동안에는, 제 얼굴의 근심이 그것을 견뎌 낼 힘이 그토록 없는 이들에게 어떤 더한 불안을 전할까 두려워했던 까닭이었다."]], [["\"They are gone, Cora!\"", "\"그들은 갔소, 〈코라〉!\""], ["he whispered; \"Alice, they are returned whence they came, and we are saved!", "그가 속삭였다. \"〈앨리스〉, 그들은 왔던 곳으로 돌아갔고, 우린 살았소!"], ["To Heaven, that has alone delivered us from the grasp of so merciless an enemy, be all the praise!\"", "그토록 무자비한 적의 손아귀에서 우리를 홀로 건져 주신 하늘에, 온 찬미가 있기를!\""]], [["\"Then to Heaven will I return my thanks!\"", "\"그럼 하늘에 제 감사를 돌려 드리겠어요!\""], ["exclaimed the younger sister, rising from the encircling arms of Cora, and casting herself with enthusiastic gratitude on the naked rock; \"to that Heaven who has spared the tears of a gray-headed father; has saved the lives of those I so much love--\"", "동생이 〈코라〉의 감싸 안은 팔에서 일어나, 벅찬 감사로 헐벗은 바위 위에 몸을 던지며 외쳤다. \"백발의 아버지의 눈물을 아껴 주신 그 하늘에, 제가 그토록 사랑하는 이들의 목숨을 구해 주신—\""]], [["Both Heyward, and the more tempered Cora, witnessed the act of involuntary emotion with powerful sympathy, the former secretly believing that piety had never worn a form so lovely as it had now assumed in the youthful person of Alice.", "〈헤이워드〉도, 그리고 더 차분한 〈코라〉도, 그 무심결의 격정의 행위를 강렬한 공감으로 지켜보았으니, 전자는 신앙이 지금 〈앨리스〉의 젊은 모습으로 띤 만큼 사랑스러운 형상을 띤 적이 일찍이 없었다고 남몰래 믿었다."], ["Her eyes radiant with the glow of grateful feelings; the flush of her beauty was again seated on her cheeks, and her whole soul seemed ready and anxious to pour out its thanksgivings, through the medium of her eloquent features.", "그녀의 눈은 감사의 감정의 빛으로 환히 빛났고, 그 아름다움의 홍조가 다시 그녀의 뺨에 자리 잡았으며, 그녀의 온 영혼은 그 말 잘하는 이목구비를 통해 감사의 기도를 쏟아 낼 준비가 되어 간절한 듯했다."], ["But when her lips moved, the words they should have uttered appeared frozen by some new and sudden chill.", "그러나 그녀의 입술이 움직였을 때, 그것이 발해야 했을 말들은 어떤 새롭고 갑작스러운 한기에 얼어붙은 듯했다."], ["Her bloom gave place to the paleness of death; her soft and melting eyes grew hard, and seemed contracting with horror; while those hands which she had raised, clasped in each other, towards heaven, dropped in horizontal lines before her, the fingers pointed forward in convulsed motion.", "그녀의 혈색은 죽음의 창백함에 자리를 내주었고, 부드럽게 녹아내리던 눈은 굳어져, 공포로 오그라드는 듯했다. 한편 그녀가 맞잡아 하늘을 향해 들어 올렸던 그 두 손은, 손가락을 발작하듯 앞으로 가리킨 채 그녀 앞에 수평으로 떨어졌다."], ["Heyward turned, the instant she gave a direction to his suspicions, and, peering just above the ledge which formed the threshold of the open outlet of the cavern, he beheld the malignant, fierce, and savage features of Le Renard Subtil.", "그녀가 그의 의심에 방향을 일러 주는 그 즉시 〈헤이워드〉는 몸을 돌렸고, 동굴의 트인 출구의 문지방을 이루는 그 바위턱 바로 위로 엿보니, 그는 〈교활한 여우〉의 그 악의에 차고 사납고 야만스러운 얼굴을 보았다."]], [["In that moment of surprise, the self-possession of Heyward did not desert him.", "그 놀라움의 순간에도, 〈헤이워드〉의 침착함은 그를 저버리지 않았다."], ["He observed by the vacant expression of the Indian's countenance, that his eye, accustomed to the open air, had not yet been able to penetrate the dusky light which pervaded the depth of the cavern.", "그는 그 인디언의 얼굴에 떠오른 멍한 표정으로 보아, 탁 트인 바깥 공기에 익은 그자의 눈이 동굴 깊은 데를 채운 그 어스레한 빛을 아직 꿰뚫어 보지 못했음을 알아차렸다."], ["He had even thought of retreating beyond a curvature in the natural wall, which might still conceal him and his companions, when, by the sudden gleam of intelligence that shot across the features of the savage, he saw it was too late, and that they were betrayed.", "그는 자연이 빚은 벽의 한 굽이 너머로 물러설까 하는 생각마저 했으니, 그 굽이가 아직 그와 그의 동행들을 가려 줄지도 모를 일이었다. 그러나 그 미개인의 얼굴을 가로질러 별안간 번뜩인 깨달음의 빛으로, 그는 이미 너무 늦었음을, 그리고 그들이 발각되었음을 알았다."]], [["The look of exultation and brutal triumph which announced this terrible truth was irresistibly irritating.", "이 끔찍한 진실을 알리는 그 의기양양함과 잔인한 승리의 표정은 어찌할 수 없이 사람을 격분케 하는 것이었다."], ["Forgetful of everything but the impulses of his hot blood, Duncan levelled his pistol and fired.", "제 뜨거운 피의 충동 말고는 모든 것을 잊은 채, 〈덩컨〉은 권총을 겨누어 쏘았다."], ["The report of the weapon made the cavern bellow like an eruption from a volcano; and when the smoke it vomited had been driven away before the current of air which issued from the ravine, the place so lately occupied by the features of his treacherous guide was vacant.", "그 무기의 발사음이 동굴을 화산이 분출하듯 우르릉 울리게 했다. 그리고 그것이 토해 낸 연기가 협곡에서 흘러나온 공기의 흐름에 밀려 흩어졌을 때, 바로 조금 전 그 배신한 길잡이의 얼굴이 차지했던 그 자리는 비어 있었다."], ["Rushing to the outlet, Heyward caught a glimpse of his dark figure, stealing around a low and narrow ledge, which soon hid him entirely from sight.", "출구로 달려간 〈헤이워드〉는 그의 검은 형체가 낮고 좁은 바위턱을 돌아 슬며시 사라지는 모습을 언뜻 붙잡았으니, 그것은 이내 그를 시야에서 온전히 가려 버렸다."]], [["Among the savages, a frightful stillness succeeded the explosion, which had just been heard bursting from the bowels of the rock.", "미개인들 사이에는, 바위의 깊은 속에서 터져 나오는 것으로 막 들렸던 그 폭음 뒤로 무시무시한 정적이 뒤따랐다."], ["But when Le Renard raised his voice in a long and intelligible whoop, it was answered by a spontaneous yell from the mouth of every Indian within hearing of the sound.", "그러나 〈교활한 여우〉가 길고 알아들을 만한 함성으로 목소리를 높이자, 그 소리가 들리는 데 있던 모든 인디언의 입에서 일제히 터진 비명이 그것에 화답했다."], ["The clamorous noises again rushed down the island; and before Duncan had time to recover from the shock, his feeble barrier of brush was scattered to the winds, the cavern was entered at both its extremities, and he and his companions were dragged from their shelter and borne into the day, where they stood surrounded by the whole band of the triumphant Hurons.", "그 떠들썩한 소음이 다시 섬을 따라 쏟아져 내렸다. 그리고 〈덩컨〉이 그 충격에서 미처 회복할 틈도 없이, 그의 허술한 덤불 차단막은 바람에 흩어졌고, 동굴은 그 양쪽 끝에서 침입당했으며, 그와 그의 동행들은 그 은신처에서 끌려 나와 환한 대낮으로 떠밀려 나가, 그곳에서 의기양양한 〈휴런〉족 무리 전체에게 에워싸인 채 섰다."]], [["\"I fear we shall outsleep the coming morn As much as we this night have overwatched!\"", "\"두렵구나, 우리가 다가오는 아침을 너무 늦도록 잠들어 보낼까\n지난밤 너무 오래 깨어 지켜본 것만큼이나!\""]], [["_Midsummer Night's Dream._", "〈한여름 밤의 꿈〉"]], [["The instant the shock of this sudden misfortune had abated, Duncan began to make his observations on the appearance and proceedings of their captors.", "이 갑작스러운 불행의 충격이 가시는 순간, 〈던컨〉은 자신들을 사로잡은 자들의 모습과 행동을 살피기 시작했다."], ["Contrary to the usages of the natives in the wantonness of their success, they had respected, not only the persons of the trembling sisters, but his own.", "그들은 승리에 도취해 방종을 부리는 원주민들의 관습과는 달리, 떨고 있는 자매들의 몸뿐 아니라 그 자신의 몸에도 손대지 않고 존중했다."], ["The rich ornaments of his military attire had indeed been repeatedly handled by different individuals of the tribe with eyes expressing a savage longing to possess the baubles; but before the customary violence could be resorted to, a mandate in the authoritative voice of the large warrior already mentioned, stayed the uplifted hand, and convinced Heyward that they were to be reserved for some object of particular moment.", "그의 화려한 군복 장식은 과연 그 종족의 여러 자가 거듭 손으로 만지작거렸으니, 그 눈에는 그 노리개를 갖고 싶어 하는 야만적인 갈망이 어려 있었다. 그러나 으레 따르던 폭력이 가해지기도 전에, 앞서 언급한 덩치 큰 전사의 위엄 있는 목소리로 내려진 명령이 치켜든 손을 멈춰 세웠고, 〈헤이워드〉로 하여금 자신들이 무언가 특별히 중요한 목적을 위해 남겨질 것임을 깨닫게 했다."]], [["While, however, these manifestations of weakness were exhibited by the young and vain of the party, the more experienced warriors continued their search throughout both caverns, with an activity that denoted they were far from being satisfied with those fruits of their conquest which had already been brought to light.", "그러나 일행 가운데 젊고 허영심 많은 자들이 이런 나약함의 기색을 드러내는 동안, 더 노련한 전사들은 두 동굴을 샅샅이 뒤지기를 멈추지 않았으니, 그 부지런함은 이미 드러난 정복의 열매에 결코 만족하지 못하고 있음을 말해 주었다."], ["Unable to discover any new victim, these diligent workers of vengeance soon approached their male prisoners, pronouncing the name of \"La Longue Carabine,\" with a fierceness that could not easily be mistaken.", "새로운 희생자를 찾아내지 못하자, 이 부지런한 복수의 일꾼들은 이내 남자 포로들에게 다가가, 〈긴 총〉이라는 이름을, 좀처럼 오해할 수 없을 만큼 사나운 어조로 외쳐 댔다."], ["Duncan affected not to comprehend the meaning of their repeated and violent interrogatories, while his companion was spared the effort of a similar deception by his ignorance of French.", "〈던컨〉은 거듭되는 폭력적인 심문의 뜻을 알아듣지 못하는 척했고, 그의 동행은 프랑스어를 모르는 덕분에 그와 같은 가장의 수고를 면했다."], ["Wearied, at length, by their importunities, and apprehensive of irritating his captors by too stubborn a silence, the former looked about him in quest of Magua; who might interpret his answers to questions which were at each moment becoming more earnest and threatening.", "마침내 그 끈질긴 추궁에 지치고, 너무 완강한 침묵으로 자신을 사로잡은 자들을 자극할까 두려워, 전자는 〈마구아〉를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았으니, 그라면 시시각각 더 다급하고 위협적으로 변해 가는 물음에 자신의 대답을 통역해 줄 수 있을 터였다."]], [["The conduct of this savage had formed a solitary exception to that of all his fellows.", "이 야만인의 행동은 그 모든 동료들과 홀로 동떨어진 예외를 이루었다."], ["While the others were busily occupied in seeking to gratify their childish passion for finery, by plundering even the miserable effects of the scout, or had been searching, with such bloodthirsty vengeance in their looks, for their absent owner, Le Renard had stood at a little distance from the prisoners, with a demeanor so quiet and satisfied, as to betray that he had already effected the grand purpose of this treachery.", "다른 자들이 척후의 보잘것없는 소지품마저 약탈하며 장식품에 대한 어린아이 같은 욕심을 채우기에 분주하거나, 눈에 피에 굶주린 복수심을 띤 채 그 자리에 없는 임자를 찾아 헤매는 동안, 〈르나르〉는 포로들에게서 조금 떨어진 곳에, 그토록 차분하고 흡족한 태도로 서 있었으니, 그것은 그가 이미 이 배신의 큰 목적을 이루었음을 드러내고 있었다."], ["When the eyes of Heyward first met those of his recent guide, he turned them away in horror at the sinister though calm look he encountered.", "〈헤이워드〉의 눈이 얼마 전 자신의 안내자였던 자의 눈과 처음 마주쳤을 때, 그는 자신이 맞닥뜨린 그 음험하면서도 차분한 눈길에 진저리치며 시선을 돌렸다."], ["Conquering his disgust, however, he was able, with an averted face, to address his successful enemy.", "그러나 혐오를 억누르고, 그는 얼굴을 외면한 채 자신의 의기양양한 적에게 말을 건넬 수 있었다."]], [["\"Le Renard Subtil is too much of a warrior,\"", "\"교활한 〈르나르〉는 무인다운 사내인지라,\""], ["said the reluctant Heyward, \"to refuse telling an unarmed man what his conquerors say.\"", "마지못해 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다, \"무장하지 않은 사람에게 정복자들이 무어라 하는지 말해 주기를 거절하지는 않으리라.\""]], [["\"They ask for the hunter who knows the paths through the woods,\"", "\"저들은 숲을 가로지르는 길을 아는 사냥꾼을 찾고 있다.\""], ["returned Magua, in his broken English, laying his hand, at the same time, with a ferocious smile, on the bundle of leaves with which a wound on his own shoulder was bandaged.", "〈마구아〉가 서툰 영어로 대꾸하며, 동시에 사나운 미소를 머금고, 제 어깨의 상처를 동여맨 잎사귀 뭉치 위에 손을 얹었다."], ["\"La Longue Carabine!", "\"〈긴 총〉!"], ["his rifle is good, and his eye never shut; but, like the short gun of the white chief, it is nothing against the life of Le Subtil!\"", "그자의 총은 훌륭하고, 그 눈은 결코 감기지 않는다. 허나 흰 추장의 짧은 총처럼, 〈교활한 자〉의 목숨 앞에서는 아무것도 아니다!\""]], [["\"Le Renard is too brave to remember the hurts received in war, or the hands that gave them!\"", "\"〈르나르〉는 너무 용맹하여 전쟁에서 입은 상처나, 그것을 입힌 손을 기억하지는 않으리라!\""]], [["\"Was it war, when the tired Indian rested at the sugar-tree to taste his corn!", "\"지친 인디언이 단풍나무 아래 쉬며 제 옥수수를 맛보았을 때, 그것이 전쟁이었던가!"], ["who filled the bushes with creeping enemies!", "누가 덤불을 기어드는 적들로 가득 채웠는가!"], ["who drew the knife!", "누가 칼을 빼 들었는가!"], ["whose tongue was peace, while his heart was colored with blood!", "누구의 혀는 평화를 말하면서, 그 마음은 피로 물들어 있었는가!"], ["Did Magua say that the hatchet was out of the ground, and that his hand had dug it up?\"", "〈마구아〉가 도끼를 땅에서 뽑았다고, 제 손으로 그것을 파냈다고 말했던가?\""]], [["As Duncan dared not retort upon his accuser by reminding him of his own premeditated treachery, and disdained to deprecate his resentment by any words of apology, he remained silent.", "〈던컨〉은 자신을 비난하는 자에게 그가 미리 꾸민 배신을 일깨워 받아칠 엄두가 나지 않았고, 어떤 변명의 말로 그의 원한을 누그러뜨리기를 떳떳지 않게 여겨, 잠자코 있었다."], ["Magua seemed also content to rest the controversy as well as all further communication there, for he resumed the leaning attitude against the rock, from which, in momentary energy, he had arisen.", "〈마구아〉도 이 논쟁을, 그리고 그 밖의 모든 이야기를 거기서 그치는 데 만족한 듯, 순간의 격정에 일어섰던 바위에 다시 몸을 기대었다."], ["But the cry of \"La Longue Carabine\" was renewed the instant the impatient savages perceived that the short dialogue was ended.", "그러나 짧은 대화가 끝났음을 참을성 없는 야만인들이 알아채는 순간, \"〈긴 총〉\"을 외치는 소리가 다시금 일었다."]], [["\"You hear,\"", "\"들리는가,\""], ["said Magua, with stubborn indifference; \"the red Hurons call for the life of 'The Long Rifle,'", "〈마구아〉가 완강한 무관심으로 말했다."], ["or they will have the blood of them that keep him hid!\"", "\"붉은 〈휴런〉들은 '〈긴 총〉'의 목숨을 원하거나, 아니면 그를 숨긴 자들의 피를 원한다!\""]], [["\"He is gone--escaped; he is far beyond their reach.\"", "\"그는 가버렸다 — 달아났다. 그는 너희 손이 미치지 못할 멀리 있다.\""]], [["Renard smiled with cold contempt, as he answered,--", "〈르나르〉는 차가운 경멸의 미소를 지으며 대답했다."]], [["\"When the white man dies, he thinks he is at peace; but the redmen know how to torture even the ghosts of their enemies.", "\"백인은 죽으면 평화에 들었다고 여긴다. 허나 붉은 사람들은 적의 혼령마저 괴롭히는 법을 안다."], ["Where is his body?", "그자의 몸은 어디 있는가?"], ["Let the Hurons see his scalp!\"", "〈휴런〉들에게 그자의 머릿가죽을 보여라!\""]], [["\"He is not dead, but escaped.\"", "\"그는 죽지 않았고, 달아났다.\""]], [["Magua shook his head incredulously.", "〈마구아〉는 믿지 못하겠다는 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["\"Is he a bird, to spread his wings; or is he a fish, to swim without air!", "\"그자가 날개를 펴는 새인가, 아니면 공기 없이 헤엄치는 물고기인가!"], ["The white chief reads in his books, and he believes the Hurons are fools!\"", "흰 추장은 제 책 속에서 글을 읽고, 〈휴런〉들을 어리석은 자들이라 여기는구나!\""]], [["\"Though no fish, The Long Rifle can swim.", "\"물고기는 아니나, 〈긴 총〉은 헤엄칠 줄 안다."], ["He floated down the stream when the powder was all burnt, and when the eyes of the Hurons were behind a cloud.\"", "화약이 다 타 버리고, 〈휴런〉들의 눈이 구름 뒤에 가려졌을 때, 그는 강물을 따라 떠내려갔다.\""]], [["\"And why did the white chief stay?\"", "\"그러면 흰 추장은 어찌하여 남았는가?\""], ["demanded the still incredulous Indian.", "여전히 믿지 못하는 인디언이 따져 물었다."], ["\"Is he a stone that goes to the bottom, or does the scalp burn his head?\"", "\"그자는 바닥으로 가라앉는 돌인가, 아니면 머릿가죽이 그 머리를 태우기라도 하는가?\""]], [["\"That I am not a stone, your dead comrade, who fell into the falls, might answer, were the life still in him,\"", "\"내가 돌이 아니라는 것은, 폭포에 떨어진 너의 죽은 동료가, 아직 그 안에 목숨이 남아 있었다면 대답해 줄 수 있었을 것이다.\""], ["said the provoked young man, using, in his anger, that boastful language which was most likely to excite the admiration of an Indian.", "격분한 젊은이가, 분노에 차서 인디언의 감탄을 가장 잘 자아낼 법한 그 호기로운 말투를 쓰며 말했다."], ["\"The white man thinks none but cowards desert their women.\"", "\"백인은 겁쟁이만이 제 여인들을 버린다고 여긴다.\""]], [["Magua muttered a few words, inaudibly, between his teeth, before he continued, aloud,--", "〈마구아〉는 이 사이로 몇 마디를 알아들을 수 없게 중얼거리고는, 소리 높여 말을 이었다."]], [["\"Can the Delawares swim, too, as well as crawl in the bushes?", "\"〈델라웨어〉들도 덤불 속을 기는 것만큼이나 헤엄도 잘 치는가?"], ["Where is Le Gros Serpent?\"", "〈르 그로 세르팡〉은 어디 있는가?\""]], [["Duncan, who perceived by the use of these Canadian appellations, that his late companions were much better known to his enemies than to himself, answered, reluctantly, \"He also is gone down with the water.\"", "이 〈캐나다〉식 호칭을 쓰는 것으로 미루어, 얼마 전까지 함께했던 동행들이 자기보다 적들에게 훨씬 더 잘 알려져 있음을 깨달은 〈던컨〉은 마지못해 대답했다. \"그도 물길을 따라 내려갔다.\""]], [["\"Le Cerf Agile is not here?\"", "\"〈르 세르 아질〉은 여기 없는가?\""]], [["\"I know not whom you call 'The Nimble Deer,'\"", "\"누구를 '〈날쌘 사슴〉'이라 부르는지 모르겠다.\""], ["said Duncan, gladly profiting by any excuse to create delay.", "〈던컨〉이 시간을 끌 어떤 핑계라도 반가워하며 말했다."]], [["\"Uncas,\"", "\"〈웅카스〉다.\""], ["returned Magua, pronouncing the Delaware name with even greater difficulty than he spoke his English words.", "〈마구아〉가 그 〈델라웨어〉식 이름을, 영어보다도 한층 더 힘겹게 발음하며 대꾸했다."], ["\"'Bounding Elk' is what the white man says, when he calls to the young Mohican.\"", "\"'〈뛰노는 큰사슴〉'이 흰 사람이 젊은 〈모히칸〉을 부를 때 쓰는 말이다.\""]], [["\"Here is some confusion in names between us, Le Renard,\"", "\"우리 사이에 이름에 약간 혼동이 있구나, 〈르나르〉.\""], ["said Duncan, hoping to provoke a discussion.", "〈던컨〉이 논쟁을 부추기려는 마음으로 말했다."], ["\"_Daim_ is the French for deer, and _cerf_ for stag; _elan_ is the true term, when one would speak of an elk.\"", "\"프랑스어로 사슴은 〈daim〉, 수사슴은 〈cerf〉다. 큰사슴을 말하려면 〈elan〉이 옳은 낱말이다.\""]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"그렇다.\""], ["muttered the Indian, in his native tongue; \"the pale-faces are prattling women!", "인디언이 제 모어로 중얼거렸다. \"창백한 얼굴들은 재잘대는 여인네들이다!"], ["they have two words for each thing, while a redskin will make the sound of his voice speak for him.\"", "그들은 무엇 하나에 두 낱말을 가지지만, 붉은 살갗은 제 목소리로 제 뜻을 말하게 한다.\" 그러고는 말을 바꾸어, 그를 가르친 변경 사람들의 불완전한 어법을 좇아 말을 이었다."], ["Then changing his language, he continued, adhering to the imperfect nomenclature of his provincial instructors, \"The deer is swift, but weak; the elk is swift, but strong; and the son of Le Serpent is Le Cerf Agile.", "\"사슴은 빠르나 약하다. 큰사슴은 빠르고도 강하다."], ["Has he leaped the river to the woods?\"", "그리고 〈르 세르팡〉의 아들은 〈르 세르 아질〉이다. 그가 강을 뛰어넘어 숲으로 갔는가?\""]], [["\"If you mean the younger Delaware, he too is gone down with the water.\"", "\"네가 더 젊은 〈델라웨어〉를 말하는 것이라면, 그 역시 물길을 따라 내려갔다.\""]], [["As there was nothing improbable to an Indian in the manner of the escape, Magua admitted the truth of what he had heard, with a readiness that afforded additional evidence how little he would prize such worthless captives.", "인디언으로서는 그런 탈출 방식에 도무지 있을 법하지 않은 점이 없었으므로, 〈마구아〉는 들은 바의 진실을 선뜻 받아들였으니, 그 선선함은 그가 이런 하잘것없는 포로들을 얼마나 대수롭지 않게 여기는지를 한층 더 잘 드러내는 증거였다."], ["With his companions, however, the feeling was manifestly different.", "그러나 그의 동료들에게는 그 감정이 분명 달랐다."]], [["The Hurons had awaited the result of this short dialogue with characteristic patience, and with a silence that increased until there was a general stillness in the band.", "〈휴런〉들은 이 짧은 대화의 결과를 그들 특유의 인내로, 그리고 마침내 무리 전체가 고요해질 때까지 짙어 가는 침묵 속에서 기다려 왔다."], ["When Heyward ceased to speak, they turned their eyes, as one man, on Magua, demanding, in this expressive manner, an explanation of what had been said.", "〈헤이워드〉가 말을 그치자, 그들은 한 사람처럼 〈마구아〉에게로 눈을 돌려, 이 뜻있는 몸짓으로, 무슨 이야기가 오갔는지 설명을 요구했다."], ["Their interpreter pointed to the river, and made them acquainted with the result, as much by the action as by the few words he uttered.", "통역을 맡은 자는 강을 가리키며, 몇 마디 말로보다는 그 동작으로 그 결과를 알려 주었다."], ["When the fact was generally understood, the savages raised a frightful yell, which declared the extent of their disappointment.", "그 사실이 두루 이해되자, 야만인들은 무시무시한 함성을 질렀으니, 그것은 그들의 실망이 얼마나 큰지를 드러내는 것이었다."], ["Some ran furiously to the water's edge, beating the air with frantic gestures, while others spat upon the element, to resent the supposed treason it had committed against their acknowledged rights as conquerors.", "더러는 물가로 사납게 달려가 미친 듯한 몸짓으로 허공을 후려쳤고, 더러는 정복자로서 인정받은 자신들의 권리에 그 강물이 저질렀다고 여기는 배신을 분풀이하듯, 물 위에 침을 뱉었다."], ["A few, and they not the least powerful and terrific of the band, threw lowering looks, in which the fiercest passion was only tempered by habitual self-command, at those captives who still remained in their power; while one or two even gave vent to their malignant feelings by the most menacing gestures, against which neither the sex nor the beauty of the sisters was any protection.", "몇몇은, 그리고 그들은 무리 가운데 가장 힘세고 가장 끔찍한 자들에 못지않았는데, 아직 제 손아귀에 남아 있는 포로들에게 음험한 눈길을 던졌으니, 그 안의 가장 사나운 격정은 오직 몸에 밴 자제력으로만 억눌려 있을 뿐이었다. 한둘은 더 나아가 더없이 위협적인 몸짓으로 그 악의를 터뜨렸으니, 그 앞에서는 자매의 여린 성별도 그 아름다움도 아무런 보호가 되지 못했다."], ["The young soldier made a desperate, but fruitless effort, to spring to the side of Alice, when he saw the dark hand of a savage twisted in the rich tresses which were flowing in volumes over her shoulders, while a knife was passed around the head from which they fell, as if to denote the horrid manner in which it was about to be robbed of its beautiful ornament.", "젊은 군인은 어느 야만인의 거무튀튀한 손이 〈앨리스〉의 어깨 위로 물결치며 흘러내린 풍성한 머릿단을 휘감고, 그 머리카락이 자라난 머리둘레로 칼이 둘러지는 것을 보았을 때 — 마치 그 아름다운 장식을 빼앗으려는 끔찍한 방식을 알리려는 듯 — 필사적이었으나 헛된 몸부림으로 그녀의 곁으로 뛰어들려 했다."], ["But his hands were bound; and at the first movement he made, he felt the grasp of the powerful Indian who directed the band, pressing his shoulder like a vise.", "그러나 그의 두 손은 묶여 있었고, 그가 처음 몸을 움직이자, 무리를 지휘하던 그 힘센 인디언의 손아귀가 만력처럼 그의 어깨를 짓누르는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Immediately conscious how unavailing any struggle against such an overwhelming force must prove, he submitted to his fate, encouraging his gentle companions by a few low and tender assurances that the natives seldom failed to threaten more than they performed.", "이토록 압도적인 힘에 맞선 어떤 발버둥도 부질없으리라는 것을 단박에 깨닫고, 그는 제 운명에 순응하며, 원주민들이 행하는 것보다 더 많이 위협하는 일이 좀처럼 드물지 않다는 몇 마디 낮고 다정한 다짐으로 그 온순한 동행들을 다독였다."]], [["But, while Duncan resorted to these words of consolation to quiet the apprehensions of the sisters, he was not so weak as to deceive himself.", "그러나 〈던컨〉은 자매들의 두려움을 가라앉히려 이런 위안의 말을 건넸으되, 스스로를 속일 만큼 어리석지는 않았다."], ["He well knew that the authority of an Indian chief was so little conventional, that it was oftener maintained by physical superiority than by any moral supremacy he might possess.", "그는 인디언 추장의 권위라는 것이 관례에 거의 기대지 않아, 그가 지녔을 어떤 도덕적 우위보다는 육체적 우월함으로 유지되는 경우가 더 많다는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["The danger was, therefore, magnified exactly in proportion to the number of the savage spirits by which they were surrounded.", "그러므로 위험은 자신들을 에워싼 야만의 혼들의 수에 정확히 비례하여 커지는 것이었다."], ["The most positive mandate from him who seemed the acknowledged leader, was liable to be violated at each moment, by any rash hand that might choose to sacrifice a victim to the _manes_ of some dead friend or relative.", "인정받은 우두머리로 보이는 자가 내린 더없이 단호한 명령조차, 죽은 벗이나 친지의 혼령에게 희생을 바치려는 어떤 무모한 손에 의해 시시각각 어겨질 수 있었다."], ["While, therefore, he sustained an outward appearance of calmness and fortitude, his heart leaped into his throat, whenever any of their fierce captors drew nearer than common to the helpless sisters, or fastened one of their sullen wandering looks on those fragile forms which were so little able to resist the slightest assault.", "그러므로 그는 겉으로는 차분함과 의연함을 유지하면서도, 그 사나운 포로 잡은 자들 가운데 누구라도 무력한 자매들에게 평소보다 가까이 다가서거나, 가장 작은 공격에도 거의 버티지 못할 그 연약한 몸들에 음울하게 떠도는 눈길 하나라도 박을 때면, 그의 심장은 목구멍까지 치솟았다."]], [["His apprehensions were, however, greatly relieved, when he saw that the leader had summoned his warriors to himself in council.", "그러나 우두머리가 평의를 위해 제 전사들을 불러 모으는 것을 보고는, 그의 불안은 크게 누그러졌다."], ["Their deliberations were short, and it would seem, by the silence of most of the party, the decision unanimous.", "그들의 논의는 짧았고, 대다수가 침묵한 것으로 미루어 그 결정은 만장일치인 듯했다."], ["By the frequency with which the few speakers pointed in the direction of the encampment of Webb, it was apparent they dreaded the approach of danger from that quarter.", "몇 안 되는 발언자들이 〈웨브〉의 진영 쪽을 거듭 가리키는 것으로 보아, 그들이 그쪽에서 다가올 위험을 두려워하고 있음이 분명했다."], ["This consideration probably hastened their determination, and quickened the subsequent movements.", "이러한 고려가 아마도 그들의 결정을 재촉했고, 뒤이은 움직임을 빠르게 했을 것이다."]], [["During this short conference, Heyward, finding a respite from his greatest fears, had leisure to admire the cautious manner in which the Hurons had made their approaches, even after hostilities had ceased.", "이 짧은 협의가 이어지는 동안, 가장 큰 두려움에서 잠시 벗어난 〈헤이워드〉는, 적대 행위가 그친 뒤에도 〈휴런〉들이 접근해 온 그 신중한 방식을 감탄하며 살필 여유가 있었다."]], [["It has already been stated, that the upper half of the island was a naked rock, and destitute of any other defences than a few scattered logs of drift-wood.", "섬의 위쪽 절반이 헐벗은 바위로, 흩어진 떠내려온 통나무 몇 개 말고는 어떤 방어물도 없다는 것은 이미 말한 바 있다."], ["They had selected this point to make their descent, having borne the canoe through the wood around the cataract for that purpose.", "그들은 그곳을 상륙 지점으로 골라, 그러기 위해 폭포를 빙 둘러 숲을 지나 카누를 메고 왔던 것이다."], ["Placing their arms in the little vessel, a dozen men clinging to its sides had trusted themselves to the direction of the canoe, which was controlled by two of the most skilful warriors, in attitudes that enabled them to command a view of the dangerous passage.", "무기를 그 작은 배에 실어 놓고, 열두 명의 사내가 그 옆구리에 매달려, 위험한 길목을 굽어볼 수 있는 자세를 취한 가장 솜씨 좋은 전사 둘이 다루는 카누의 인도에 제 몸을 맡겼다."], ["Favored by this arrangement, they touched the head of the island at that point which had proved so fatal to their first adventures, but with the advantages of superior numbers, and the possession of fire-arms.", "이런 배치 덕분에, 그들은 처음 모험에 그토록 치명적이었던 바로 그 지점에서 섬의 머리에 닿았으되, 이번엔 우월한 수와 총기를 갖춘 이점이 있었다."], ["That such had been the manner of their descent was rendered quite apparent to Duncan; for they now bore the light bark from the upper end of the rock, and placed it in the water, near the mouth of the outer cavern.", "그들의 상륙이 그러한 방식으로 이루어졌음은 〈던컨〉에게 자못 분명히 드러났으니, 그들이 이제 그 가벼운 자작나무 배를 바위의 위쪽 끝에서 들어다 바깥쪽 동굴 어귀 가까운 물 위에 내려놓았기 때문이다."], ["As soon as this change was made, the leader made signs to the prisoners to descend and enter.", "이 옮김이 끝나자마자, 우두머리는 포로들에게 내려와 배에 타라고 손짓했다."]], [["As resistance was impossible, and remonstrance useless, Heyward set the example of submission, by leading the way into the canoe, where he was soon seated with the sisters, and the still wondering David.", "저항은 불가능하고 항의는 무익했으므로, 〈헤이워드〉가 앞장서 카누에 오르며 순응의 본을 보였으니, 그곳에서 그는 곧 자매들과, 여전히 어리둥절해하는 〈데이비드〉와 함께 자리를 잡았다."], ["Notwithstanding the Hurons were necessarily ignorant of the little channels among the eddies and rapids of the stream, they knew the common signs of such a navigation too well to commit any material blunder.", "〈휴런〉들은 그 강의 소용돌이와 급류 사이로 난 작은 물길을 으레 알지 못했으나, 그런 항행에 흔한 신호들은 너무도 잘 알고 있어 큰 실수라곤 저지르지 않았다."], ["When the pilot chosen for the task of guiding the canoe had taken his station, the whole band plunged again into the river, the vessel glided down the current, and in a few moments the captives found themselves on the south bank of the stream, nearly opposite to the point where they had struck it the preceding evening.", "카누를 인도하는 임무로 뽑힌 키잡이가 제자리를 잡자, 무리 전체가 다시 강물 속으로 뛰어들었고, 배는 물살을 따라 미끄러져 내려갔으며, 몇 순간 만에 포로들은 전날 저녁 강에 닿았던 지점과 거의 마주한, 강의 남쪽 기슭에 자신들이 와 있음을 알게 되었다."]], [["Here was held another short but earnest consultation, during which the horses, to whose panic their owners ascribed their heaviest misfortune, were led from the cover of the woods, and brought to the sheltered spot.", "이곳에서 또 한 차례 짧되 진지한 협의가 열렸으니, 그동안 그 임자들이 자신들의 가장 큰 불행의 탓으로 돌렸던, 공포에 질린 말들이 숲의 가림막에서 끌려 나와 이 으슥한 곳으로 옮겨졌다."], ["The band now divided.", "이제 무리가 둘로 나뉘었다."], ["The great chief so often mentioned, mounting the charger of Heyward, led the way directly across the river, followed by most of his people, and disappeared in the woods, leaving the prisoners in charge of six savages, at whose head was Le Renard Subtil.", "그토록 자주 언급된 그 큰 추장은 〈헤이워드〉의 군마에 올라타, 곧장 강을 가로질러 앞장섰고, 부하 대부분이 그 뒤를 따라 숲속으로 사라지며, 포로들을 여섯 야만인의 손에 맡겼으니, 그 우두머리는 교활한 〈르나르〉였다."], ["Duncan witnessed all their movements with renewed uneasiness.", "〈던컨〉은 그들의 모든 움직임을 새삼스러운 불안 속에 지켜보았다."]], [["He had been fond of believing, from the uncommon forbearance of the savages, that he was reserved as a prisoner to be delivered to Montcalm.", "그는 야만인들의 보기 드문 자제로 미루어, 자신이 〈몽칼름〉에게 넘겨질 포로로 남겨진 것이라 즐겨 믿어 왔다."], ["As the thoughts of those who are in misery seldom slumber, and the invention is never more lively than when it is stimulated by hope, however feeble and remote, he had even imagined that the parental feelings of Munro were to be made instrumental in seducing him from his duty to the king.", "불행에 빠진 자들의 생각은 좀처럼 잠들지 않고, 아무리 희미하고 먼 것일지언정 희망에 자극받을 때만큼 그 상상이 활발한 적도 없는 법인지라, 그는 〈먼로〉의 부정(父情)이 자신을 왕에 대한 의무에서 꾀어내는 데 쓰일 도구가 되리라고까지 그려 보았다."], ["For though the French commander bore a high character for courage and enterprise, he was also thought to be expert in those political practices, which do not always respect the nicer obligations of morality, and which so generally disgraced the European diplomacy of that period.", "그 프랑스 지휘관은 용기와 진취성으로 높은 평판을 지녔으되, 도덕의 더 미묘한 의무를 늘 존중하지는 않으며 그 시대 유럽 외교를 그토록 두루 욕되게 했던 그런 정치적 술책에도 능하다고 여겨졌기 때문이다."]], [["All those busy and ingenious speculations were now annihilated by the conduct of his captors.", "이 모든 분주하고 기발한 추측은 이제 자신을 사로잡은 자들의 행동으로 산산이 부서졌다."], ["That portion of the band who had followed the huge warrior took the route towards the foot of the Horican, and no other expectation was left for himself and companions, than that they were to be retained as hopeless captives by their savage conquerors.", "그 덩치 큰 전사를 따라간 무리는 〈호리컨〉의 기슭 쪽으로 길을 잡았고, 그 자신과 동행들에게는 야만의 정복자들에게 가망 없는 포로로 붙들려 있으리라는 것 말고는 어떤 다른 기대도 남지 않았다."], ["Anxious to know the worst, and willing, in such an emergency, to try the potency of gold, he overcame his reluctance to speak to Magua.", "최악을 알고자 안달하고, 이런 위급한 상황에서 황금의 위력을 시험해 볼 마음에서, 그는 〈마구아〉에게 말 거는 데 대한 꺼림을 이겨 냈다."], ["Addressing himself to his former guide, who had now assumed the authority and manner of one who was to direct the future movements of the party, he said, in tones as friendly and confiding as he could assume,--", "이제 일행의 앞으로의 움직임을 지휘할 자로서의 권위와 태도를 띤, 얼마 전까지의 안내자에게, 그는 가능한 한 다정하고 미덥게 들리는 어조로 말했다."]], [["\"I would speak to Magua, what is fit only for so great a chief to hear.\"", "\"〈마구아〉에게, 그토록 위대한 추장만이 들을 만한 것을 말하고 싶다.\""]], [["The Indian turned his eyes on the young soldier scornfully, as he answered,--", "인디언은 경멸하듯 젊은 군인에게 눈길을 돌리며 대답했다."]], [["\"Speak; trees have no ears!\"", "\"말하라. 나무에는 귀가 없으니!\""]], [["\"But the red Hurons are not deaf; and counsel that is fit for the great men of a nation would make the young warriors drunk.", "\"허나 붉은 〈휴런〉들은 귀머거리가 아니다. 한 민족의 위대한 자들에게 어울리는 의논은 젊은 전사들을 취하게 만들 것이다."], ["If Magua will not listen, the officer of the king knows how to be silent.\"", "〈마구아〉가 듣지 않겠다면, 왕의 장교는 침묵하는 법을 안다.\""]], [["The savage spoke carelessly to his comrades, who were busied, after their awkward manner, in preparing the horses for the reception of the sisters, and moved a little to one side, whither, by a cautious gesture, he induced Heyward to follow.", "야만인은 동료들에게 무심히 몇 마디 건넸으니, 그들은 그 어설픈 솜씨로 자매들을 태울 채비로 말들을 손보기에 분주했다. 그러고는 한쪽으로 조금 비켜서며, 조심스러운 몸짓으로 〈헤이워드〉를 그리로 따라오게 했다."]], [["\"Now speak,\"", "\"이제 말하라.\""], ["he said; \"if the words are such as Magua should hear.\"", "그가 말했다. \"그 말이 〈마구아〉가 들을 만한 것이라면.\""]], [["\"Le Renard Subtil has proved himself worthy of the honorable name given to him by his Canada fathers,\"", "\"교활한 〈르나르〉는 자신의 〈캐나다〉 아버지들이 붙여 준 그 명예로운 이름에 걸맞은 자임을 입증했다.\""], ["commenced Heyward; \"I see his wisdom, and all that he has done for us, and shall remember it, when the hour to reward him arrives.", "〈헤이워드〉가 운을 뗐다. \"나는 그의 지혜를, 그리고 그가 우리를 위해 한 모든 일을 보았고, 그에게 보답할 때가 오면 그것을 기억할 것이다."], ["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["Renard has proved that he is not only a great chief in council, but one who knows how to deceive his enemies!\"", "〈르나르〉는 자신이 평의에서 위대한 추장일 뿐 아니라, 적을 속이는 법을 아는 자임을 입증했다!\""]], [["\"What has Renard done?\"", "\"〈르나르〉가 무엇을 했단 말인가?\""], ["coldly demanded the Indian.", "인디언이 차갑게 물었다."]], [["\"What!", "\"무엇을 했느냐고!"], ["has he not seen that the woods were filled with outlying parties of the enemies, and that the Serpent could not steal through them without being seen?", "숲이 적들의 매복한 무리로 가득 찼고, 〈세르팡〉이 들키지 않고는 그 사이로 빠져나갈 수 없다는 것을 그가 보지 못했단 말인가?"], ["Then, did he not lose his path to blind the eyes of the Hurons?", "그러고는 〈휴런〉들의 눈을 가리려 길을 잃은 척하지 않았던가?"], ["Did he not pretend to go back to his tribe, who had treated him ill, and driven him from their wigwams like a dog?", "자신을 박대하고 개처럼 천막에서 내쫓은 제 종족에게 돌아가는 척하지 않았던가?"], ["And, when we saw what he wished to do, did we not aid him, by making a false face, that the Hurons might think the white man believed that his friend was his enemy?", "그리고 그가 무엇을 바라는지 우리가 알아챘을 때, 〈휴런〉들이 백인이 제 친구를 적이라 믿는다고 여기도록, 거짓 얼굴을 지어 우리가 그를 돕지 않았던가?"], ["Is not all this true?", "이 모든 것이 사실이 아닌가?"], ["And when Le Subtil had shut the eyes and stopped the ears of his nation by his wisdom, did they not forget that they had once done him wrong, and forced him to flee to the Mohawks?", "그리고 〈교활한 자〉가 제 지혜로 제 민족의 눈을 가리고 귀를 막았을 때, 그들이 한때 그에게 부당하게 굴어 그를 〈모호크〉족에게로 달아나게 만들었던 일을 잊지 않았던가?"], ["And did they not leave him on the south side of the river, with their prisoners, while they have gone foolishly on the north?", "그리고 그들이 강의 남쪽에 그를 포로들과 함께 남겨 두고, 어리석게도 북쪽으로 가버리지 않았던가?"], ["Does not Renard mean to turn like a fox on his footsteps, and to carry to the rich and gray-headed Scotchman his daughters?", "〈르나르〉는 여우처럼 제 발자취를 되짚어 돌아서서, 부유하고 백발인 그 스코틀랜드인에게 그 딸들을 데려갈 작정이 아닌가?"], ["Yes, Magua, I see it all, and I have already been thinking how so much wisdom and honesty should be repaid.", "그렇다, 〈마구아〉여, 나는 그 모두를 알며, 그토록 큰 지혜와 정직에 어떻게 보답해야 할지 이미 헤아려 보았다."], ["First, the chief of William Henry will give as a great chief should for such a service.", "먼저, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 추장은 그런 봉사에 대해 위대한 추장이 마땅히 줄 만큼 줄 것이다."], ["The medal[16] of Magua will no longer be of tin, but of beaten gold; his horn will run over with powder; dollars will be as plenty in his pouch as pebbles on the shore of Horican; and the deer will lick his hand, for they will know it to be vain to fly from the rifle he will carry!", "〈마구아〉의 메달은 더 이상 양철이 아니라 두드린 금이 될 것이며, 그의 화약통은 화약으로 넘쳐흐를 것이고, 달러가 그의 주머니 속에 〈호리컨〉 기슭의 자갈만큼 흔할 것이며, 사슴들은 그의 손을 핥을 터이니, 그가 지닐 총에서 달아나 봤자 부질없음을 알 것이기 때문이다!"], ["As for myself, I know not how to exceed the gratitude of the Scotchman, but I--yes, I will--\"", "나로 말하면, 그 스코틀랜드인의 고마움을 어떻게 넘어설 수 있을지 모르겠으나, 나는 — 그래, 나는 —\""]], [["\"What will the young chief who comes from towards the sun, give?\"", "\"해 뜨는 쪽에서 온 젊은 추장은 무엇을 주겠는가?\""], ["demanded the Huron, observing that Heyward hesitated in his desire to end the enumeration of benefits with that which might form the climax of an Indian's wishes.", "〈휴런〉이, 인디언의 소망 가운데 그 절정을 이룰 만한 것으로 그 은혜의 나열을 끝맺으려다 〈헤이워드〉가 머뭇거리는 것을 알아채고 물었다."]], [["\"He will make the fire-water from the Islands in the salt lake flow before the wigwam of Magua, until the heart of the Indian shall be lighter than the feathers of the humming-bird, and his breath sweeter than the wild honeysuckle.\"", "\"그는 짠 호수의 섬들에서 나는 불의 물을, 인디언의 마음이 벌새의 깃털보다 가벼워지고 그 숨결이 야생 인동덩굴보다 달콤해질 때까지, 〈마구아〉의 천막 앞에 흐르게 할 것이다.\""]], [["Le Renard had listened gravely as Heyward slowly proceeded in his subtle speech.", "〈르나르〉는 〈헤이워드〉가 그 교묘한 말을 천천히 이어 가는 동안 진지하게 귀 기울였다."], ["When the young man mentioned the artifice he supposed the Indian to have practised on his own nation, the countenance of the listener was veiled in an expression of cautious gravity.", "젊은이가, 인디언이 제 민족에게 부렸으리라 짐작한 술수를 언급했을 때, 듣는 자의 얼굴은 조심스러운 진지함의 표정으로 가려졌다."], ["At the allusion to the injury which Duncan affected to believe had driven the Huron from his native tribe, a gleam of such ungovernable ferocity flashed from the other's eyes, as induced the adventurous speaker to believe he had struck the proper chord.", "〈휴런〉을 그 본래 종족에게서 달아나게 했다고 〈던컨〉이 믿는 척한 그 상처를 넌지시 비추자, 다른 자의 눈에서 걷잡을 수 없는 흉포함의 섬광이 번뜩였으니, 이 대담한 화자로 하여금 자신이 알맞은 가락을 짚었다고 믿게 했다."], ["And by the time he reached the part where he so artfully blended the thirst of vengeance with the desire of gain, he had, at least, obtained a command of the deepest attention of the savage.", "그리고 그가 복수의 갈증을 이득의 욕망과 그토록 교묘히 뒤섞은 대목에 이르렀을 무렵엔, 적어도 그 야만인의 가장 깊은 주의를 사로잡는 데는 성공해 있었다."], ["The question put by Le Renard had been calm, and with all the dignity of an Indian; but it was quite apparent, by the thoughtful expression of the listener's countenance, that the answer was most cunningly devised.", "〈르나르〉가 던진 물음은 차분했고, 인디언다운 온갖 위엄을 갖춘 것이었으나, 듣는 자의 생각에 잠긴 얼굴빛으로 미루어, 그 대답이 더없이 교활하게 궁리되었음은 자못 분명했다."], ["The Huron mused a few moments, and then laying his hand on the rude bandages of his wounded shoulder, he said, with some energy,--", "〈휴런〉은 몇 순간 골똘히 생각하더니, 부상당한 어깨의 거친 붕대 위에 손을 얹고, 다소 힘주어 말했다."]], [["\"Do friends make such remarks?\"", "\"벗들이 그런 자국을 남긴단 말인가?\""]], [["\"Would La Longue Carabine cut one so light on an enemy?\"", "\"〈긴 총〉이라면 적에게 그토록 가벼이 한 칼 베었겠는가?\""]], [["\"Do the Delawares crawl upon those they love, like snakes, twisting themselves to strike?\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉들은 제가 사랑하는 자들에게, 뱀처럼 몸을 비틀어 치려고 기어든단 말인가?\""]], [["\"Would Le Gros Serpent have been heard by the ears of one he wished to be deaf?\"", "\"〈르 그로 세르팡〉이라면, 귀머거리이기를 바라는 자의 귀에 들렸겠는가?\""]], [["\"Does the white chief burn his powder in the faces of his brothers?\"", "\"흰 추장은 제 형제들의 얼굴에 대고 화약을 태운단 말인가?\""]], [["\"Does he ever miss his aim, when seriously bent to kill?\"", "\"진정 죽일 작정으로 겨눌 때, 그가 과녁을 빗맞히기라도 한단 말인가?\""], ["returned Duncan, smiling with well acted sincerity.", "〈던컨〉이 그럴듯하게 꾸민 진정성으로 미소 지으며 대꾸했다."]], [["Another long and deliberate pause succeeded these sententious questions and ready replies.", "이 함축적인 물음과 막힘없는 대답에 또 한 차례의 길고 신중한 침묵이 이어졌다."], ["Duncan saw that the Indian hesitated.", "〈던컨〉은 인디언이 망설이고 있음을 보았다."], ["In order to complete his victory, he was in the act of recommencing the enumeration of the rewards, when Magua made an expressive gesture and said--", "자신의 승리를 완결 짓고자, 그가 막 그 보답의 나열을 다시 시작하려는 참에, 〈마구아〉가 뜻있는 몸짓을 하며 말했다."]], [["\"Enough; Le Renard is a wise chief, and what he does will be seen.", "\"되었다. 〈르나르〉는 지혜로운 추장이고, 그가 하는 일은 곧 보게 될 것이다."], ["Go, and keep the mouth shut.", "가서 입을 다물고 있으라."], ["When Magua speaks, it will be the time to answer.\"", "〈마구아〉가 말할 때가, 대답할 때이리라.\""]], [["Heyward, perceiving that the eyes of his companion were warily fastened on the rest of the band, fell back immediately, in order to avoid the appearance of any suspicious confederacy with their leader.", "〈헤이워드〉는 자신의 동행자의 눈이 무리의 나머지에게 경계하듯 박혀 있음을 알아채고는, 그 우두머리와 의심스러운 결탁을 한 듯한 기색을 피하고자 곧장 물러났다."], ["Magua approached the horses, and affected to be well pleased with the diligence and ingenuity of his comrades.", "〈마구아〉는 말들에게 다가가, 동료들의 부지런함과 솜씨에 흡족한 척했다."], ["He then signed to Heyward to assist the sisters into the saddles, for he seldom deigned to use the English tongue, unless urged by some motive of more than usual moment.", "그러고는 〈헤이워드〉에게 자매들을 안장에 태우는 것을 거들라고 손짓했으니, 그는 평소보다 더 중대한 어떤 동기에 떠밀리지 않는 한 영어를 입에 올리는 일이 좀처럼 없었기 때문이다."]], [["There was no longer any plausible pretext for delay; and Duncan was obliged, however reluctantly, to comply.", "이제는 미룰 그럴듯한 핑계가 더는 없었고, 〈던컨〉은 마지못해서나마 따를 수밖에 없었다."], ["As he performed this office, he whispered his reviving hopes in the ears of the trembling females, who, through dread of encountering the savage countenances of their captors, seldom raised their eyes from the ground.", "이 일을 하는 동안, 그는 되살아나는 희망을, 떨고 있는 여인들의 귀에 속삭였으니, 그들은 자신들을 사로잡은 자들의 야만스러운 얼굴과 마주치는 것이 두려워, 좀처럼 눈을 땅에서 들지 못했다."], ["The mare of David had been taken with the followers of the large chief; in consequence, its owner, as well as Duncan, was compelled to journey on foot.", "〈데이비드〉의 암말은 그 큰 추장의 부하들과 함께 끌려갔으므로, 그 결과 그 임자도 〈던컨〉처럼 걸어서 가야만 했다."], ["The latter did not, however, so much regret this circumstance, as it might enable him to retard the speed of the party; for he still turned his longing looks in the direction of Fort Edward, in the vain expectation of catching some sound from that quarter of the forest, which might denote the approach of succor.", "그러나 후자는 이 일을 그다지 한탄하지 않았으니, 그것이 일행의 속도를 늦출 수 있게 해 줄지도 몰랐기 때문이다. 그는 여전히 〈에드워드〉 요새 쪽으로 간절한 눈길을 돌리며, 구원의 접근을 알릴 만한 어떤 소리를 그 숲의 방향에서 붙잡으려는 헛된 기대를 품고 있었다."]], [["When all were prepared, Magua made the signal to proceed, advancing in front to lead the party in person.", "모두 채비를 마치자, 〈마구아〉가 출발하라는 신호를 하며, 앞으로 나아가 몸소 일행을 이끌었다."], ["Next followed David, who was gradually coming to a true sense of his condition, as the effects of the wound became less and less apparent.", "그다음에는 〈데이비드〉가 따랐으니, 상처의 영향이 점점 옅어져 감에 따라 자신의 처지에 대한 참된 분별이 차츰 돌아오고 있었다."], ["The sisters rode in his rear, with Heyward at their side, while the Indians flanked the party, and brought up the close of the march, with a caution that seemed never to tire.", "자매들은 그 뒤에서 〈헤이워드〉를 곁에 두고 말을 탔으며, 인디언들은 일행의 양옆을 에워싸고, 결코 지칠 줄 모르는 듯한 경계로 행렬의 끝을 맡았다."]], [["In this manner they proceeded in uninterrupted silence, except when Heyward addressed some solitary word of comfort to the females, or David gave vent to the moanings of his spirit in piteous exclamations, which he intended should express the humility of resignation.", "이런 식으로 그들은, 〈헤이워드〉가 여인들에게 이따금 위안의 말 한마디를 건네거나, 〈데이비드〉가 체념의 겸허함을 나타내려는 양 가련한 탄성으로 제 영혼의 신음을 토할 때를 빼고는, 끊임없는 침묵 속에 나아갔다."], ["Their direction lay towards the south, and in a course nearly opposite to the road to William Henry.", "그들이 잡은 방향은 남쪽으로, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉로 가는 길과는 거의 정반대인 진로였다."], ["Notwithstanding this apparent adherence in Magua to the original determination of his conquerors, Heyward could not believe his tempting bait was so soon forgotten; and he knew the windings of an Indian path too well, to suppose that its apparent course led directly to its object, when artifice was at all necessary.", "〈마구아〉가 겉으로는 이렇게 정복자들의 본래 결정을 따르고 있었음에도, 〈헤이워드〉는 자신이 던진 유혹의 미끼가 그토록 빨리 잊혔으리라고는 믿을 수 없었다. 게다가 그는 인디언이 다니는 길의 굽이를 너무도 잘 알았기에, 술수가 조금이라도 필요한 마당에 그 겉보기 진로가 곧장 목적지로 이어지리라고는 여기지 않았다."], ["Mile after mile was, however, passed through the boundless woods, in this painful manner, without any prospect of a termination to their journey.", "그러나 끝없는 숲을 지나, 여정의 끝이 보일 기미라곤 없이, 이 고통스러운 방식으로 몇 마일이고 또 몇 마일이 지나갔다."], ["Heyward watched the sun, as he darted his meridian rays through the branches of the trees, and pined for the moment when the policy of Magua should change their route to one more favorable to his hopes.", "〈헤이워드〉는 나뭇가지 사이로 한낮의 햇살을 쏘아 보내는 해를 지켜보며, 〈마구아〉의 계략이 그들의 진로를 자신의 희망에 더 유리한 쪽으로 바꿀 그 순간을 애타게 기다렸다."], ["Sometimes he fancied the wary savage, despairing of passing the arm of Montcalm in safety, was holding his way towards a well-known border settlement, where a distinguished officer of the crown, and a favored friend of the Six Nations, held his large possessions, as well as his usual residence.", "때로는, 그 빈틈없는 야만인이 〈몽칼름〉의 군세를 무사히 지나치기를 단념하고, 잘 알려진 변경 정착지로 향하는 길을 잡고 있는 것이리라 상상하기도 했으니, 그곳에는 왕실의 한 이름난 장교이자 〈여섯 부족〉의 총애받는 벗이 그 너른 영지와 더불어 평소의 거처를 두고 있었다."], ["To be delivered into the hands of Sir William Johnson was far preferable to being led into the wilds of Canada; but in order to effect even the former, it would be necessary to traverse the forest for many weary leagues, each step of which was carrying him farther from the scene of the war, and, consequently, from the post, not only of honor, but of duty.", "〈윌리엄 존슨〉 경의 손에 넘겨지는 편이 〈캐나다〉의 황야로 끌려가는 것보다는 훨씬 나았으나, 전자나마 이루려 해도, 그 한 걸음 한 걸음이 그를 전쟁의 무대에서, 따라서 명예의 자리에서뿐 아니라 의무의 자리에서 점점 더 멀리 데려가는, 숲을 가로지르는 지치도록 먼 여러 리그를 횡단해야만 할 터였다."]], [["Cora alone remembered the parting injunctions of the scout, and whenever an opportunity offered, she stretched forth her arm to bend aside the twigs that met her hands.", "오직 〈코라〉만이 척후가 헤어질 때 남긴 당부를 기억하고 있었으니, 기회가 있을 때마다 그녀는 손에 닿는 잔가지들을 꺾어 비틀려고 팔을 뻗었다."], ["But the vigilance of the Indians rendered this act of precaution both difficult and dangerous.", "그러나 인디언들의 경계는 이 조심스러운 행동을 어렵고도 위험하게 만들었다."], ["She was often defeated in her purpose, by encountering their watchful eyes, when it became necessary to feign an alarm she did not feel, and occupy the limb by some gesture of feminine apprehension.", "그녀는 그들의 빈틈없는 눈과 마주칠 때면 자주 그 뜻을 이루지 못했고, 그럴 때면 느끼지도 않은 놀람을 가장하여, 여인다운 두려움의 어떤 몸짓으로 그 손을 채워야 했다."], ["Once, and once only, was she completely successful; when she broke down the bough of a large sumach, and, by a sudden thought, let her glove fall at the same instant.", "단 한 번, 오직 한 번 그녀는 완전히 성공했으니, 큰 옻나무 가지 하나를 꺾어 내며, 불현듯 떠오른 생각으로 같은 순간에 제 장갑을 떨어뜨렸을 때였다."], ["This sign, intended for those that might follow, was observed by one of her conductors, who restored the glove, broke the remaining branches of the bush in such a manner that it appeared to proceed from the struggling of some beast in its branches, and then laid his hand on his tomahawk, with a look so significant, that it put an effectual end to these stolen memorials of their passage.", "뒤따라올 자들을 위한 이 표시는 그녀를 데려가던 자 중 하나에게 들켰으니, 그는 장갑을 되돌려주고, 그 덤불의 남은 가지들을, 마치 어떤 짐승이 그 가지 사이에서 발버둥 친 것처럼 보이게끔 꺾어 놓고는, 제 손도끼에 손을 얹으며 그토록 의미심장한 눈길을 던졌기에, 이 몰래 남기던 자취의 표시는 영영 끝나고 말았다."]], [["As there were horses, to leave the prints of their footsteps, in both bands of the Indians, this interruption cut off any probable hopes of assistance being conveyed through the means of their trail.", "두 인디언 무리 모두에 발자국을 남길 말들이 있었으므로, 이 방해로 그들의 자취를 통해 도움이 전해질 만한 어떤 그럴듯한 희망마저 끊기고 말았다."]], [["Heyward would have ventured a remonstrance, had there been anything encouraging in the gloomy reserve of Magua.", "〈마구아〉의 음울한 과묵함에 무언가 고무될 만한 구석이라도 있었다면, 〈헤이워드〉는 항의를 무릅썼을지도 모른다."], ["But the savage, during all this time, seldom turned to look at his followers, and never spoke.", "그러나 그 야만인은 이 모든 시간 동안 좀처럼 제 추종자들을 돌아보지 않았고, 결코 입을 열지 않았다."], ["With the sun for his only guide, or aided by such blind marks as are only known to the sagacity of a native, he held his way along the barrens of pine, through occasional little fertile vales, across brooks and rivulets, and over undulating hills, with the accuracy of instinct, and nearly with the directness of a bird.", "오직 해를 길잡이로 삼거나, 원주민의 명민함만이 아는 그런 어렴풋한 표지의 도움을 받아, 그는 소나무 황무지를 따라, 이따금 나타나는 작고 비옥한 골짜기를 지나, 시내와 개울을 건너, 굽이치는 언덕을 넘어, 본능의 정확함으로, 그리고 거의 새의 곧음으로 제 길을 나아갔다."], ["He never seemed to hesitate.", "그는 결코 망설이는 법이 없는 듯했다."], ["Whether the path was hardly distinguishable, whether it disappeared, or whether it lay beaten and plain before him, made no sensible difference in his speed or certainty.", "길이 거의 알아볼 수 없든, 사라져 버렸든, 아니면 그의 앞에 또렷이 다져진 채 놓여 있든, 그의 속도나 확신에는 이렇다 할 차이가 없었다."], ["It seemed as if fatigue could not affect him.", "마치 피로라는 것이 그에게는 미치지 못하는 듯했다."], ["Whenever the eyes of the wearied travellers rose from the decayed leaves over which they trod, his dark form was to be seen glancing among the stems of the trees in front, his head immovably fastened in a forward position, with the light plume on his crest fluttering in a current of air, made solely by the swiftness of his own motion.", "지친 길손들의 눈이 자신들이 밟고 가는 썩은 잎사귀에서 들릴 때마다, 그의 거무튀튀한 형상이 앞쪽 나무줄기들 사이로 어른거리는 것이 보였으니, 그 머리는 미동도 없이 앞을 향해 고정된 채였고, 그 볏 위의 가벼운 깃털은 오직 제 움직임의 날램만으로 일어난 바람결에 나부끼고 있었다."]], [["But all this diligence and speed were not without an object.", "그러나 이 모든 부지런함과 속도에는 까닭이 없지 않았다."], ["After crossing a low vale, through which a gushing brook meandered, he suddenly ascended a hill, so steep and difficult of ascent, that the sisters were compelled to alight, in order to follow.", "콸콸 흐르는 시내가 굽이치는 낮은 골짜기 하나를 건넌 뒤, 그는 자매들이 따라오려면 말에서 내릴 수밖에 없을 만큼 가파르고 오르기 힘든 언덕 하나를 별안간 올랐다. 꼭대기에 이르자, 그들은 나무가 듬성듬성 덮인 평평한 자리에 와 있음을 알게 되었으니, 그 한 그루 아래에 〈마구아〉가 자신의 거무튀튀한 몸을 내던지고 있었다."], ["When the summit was gained, they found themselves on a level spot, but thinly covered with trees, under one of which Magua had thrown his dark form, as if willing and ready to seek that rest which was so much needed by the whole party.", "마치 일행 모두가 그토록 절실히 바라던 그 휴식을, 기꺼이, 마다 않고 구하려는 듯이. * 메달: 인디언 추장에게 동맹의 표시로 수여하던 표장."]], [["\"Cursed by my tribe If I forgive him.\"", "第11장\n\"내 부족에게 저주받으리라"]], [["_Shylock._", "내가 그를 용서한다면.\""]], [["The Indian had selected, for this desirable purpose, one of those steep, pyramidal hills, which bear a strong resemblance to artificial mounds, and which so frequently occur in the valleys of America.", "샤일록\n인디언은 이 바람직한 목적을 위해, 인공으로 쌓아 올린 둔덕과 흡사하여 아메리카의 골짜기마다 흔히 마주치게 되는, 그 가파르고 피라미드처럼 솟은 언덕 가운데 하나를 골랐다."], ["The one in question was high and precipitous; its top flattened, as usual; but with one of its sides more than ordinarily irregular.", "지금 그 언덕은 높고 험준했으며, 꼭대기는 여느 것들처럼 평평했으나, 한쪽 면이 유달리 들쭉날쭉했다."], ["It possessed no other apparent advantage for a resting-place than in its elevation and form, which might render defence easy, and surprise nearly impossible.", "그것이 쉴 곳으로서 지닌 유일하게 뚜렷한 이점이라곤, 방어를 수월하게 하고 기습을 거의 불가능하게 만들 만한 그 높이와 형세뿐이었다."], ["As Heyward, however, no longer expected that rescue which time and distance now rendered so improbable, he regarded these little peculiarities with an eye devoid of interest, devoting himself entirely to the comfort and condolence of his feebler companions.", "그러나 〈헤이워드〉는 이제 시간과 거리가 그토록 가망 없게 만들어 버린 구조를 더는 기대하지 않았던 까닭에, 이 사소한 특징들을 아무 관심도 없는 눈으로 바라보며, 자신은 오로지 한층 연약한 동행들을 위로하고 편안하게 하는 일에만 몰두했다."], ["The Narragansetts were suffered to browse on the branches of the trees and shrubs that were thinly scattered over the summit of the hill, while the remains of their provisions were spread under the shade of a beech, that stretched its horizontal limbs like a canopy above them.", "〈내러갠셋〉종 말들은 언덕 꼭대기에 듬성듬성 흩어진 나무와 떨기나무 가지를 뜯도록 내버려 두었고, 그동안 남은 식량은, 마치 천막처럼 수평으로 가지를 뻗은 너도밤나무 그늘 아래 펼쳐졌다."]], [["Notwithstanding the swiftness of their flight, one of the Indians had found an opportunity to strike a straggling fawn with an arrow, and had borne the more preferable fragments of the victim patiently on his shoulders, to the stopping-place.", "도주가 그토록 황급했음에도, 인디언 가운데 하나는 무리에서 떨어진 새끼 사슴을 화살로 쏘아 맞힐 틈을 찾아냈고, 그 제물에서 가장 먹음직한 부위를 어깨에 인내심 있게 짊어지고 이 쉴 곳까지 옮겨 왔다."], ["Without any aid from the science of cookery, he was immediately employed, in common with his fellows, in gorging himself with this digestible sustenance.", "요리법의 도움이라곤 일절 없이, 그는 곧장 동료들과 더불어 이 소화 잘되는 양식을 게걸스레 먹어 치우는 일에 매달렸다."], ["Magua alone sat apart, without participation in the revolting meal, and apparently buried in the deepest thought.", "오직 〈마구아〉만이 떨어져 앉아, 이 역겨운 식사에 끼지 않은 채, 보기에 더없이 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["This abstinence, so remarkable in an Indian, when he possessed the means of satisfying hunger, at length attracted the notice of Heyward.", "배고픔을 채울 수단을 가졌을 때조차 인디언에게는 그토록 보기 드문 이 절식이, 마침내 〈헤이워드〉의 눈길을 끌었다."], ["The young man willingly believed that the Huron deliberated on the most eligible manner of eluding the vigilance of his associates.", "청년은 기꺼이, 그 〈휴런〉족이 동료들의 경계를 따돌릴 가장 적합한 방도를 궁리하는 중이라고 믿었다."], ["With a view to assist his plans, by any suggestion of his own, and to strengthen the temptation, he left the beech, and straggled as if without an object, to the spot where Le Renard was seated.", "자신의 어떤 제안으로든 그 계획을 돕고, 유혹을 한층 강화할 요량으로, 그는 너도밤나무를 떠나 아무 목적도 없는 듯 어슬렁거리며 〈르나르〉가 앉은 자리로 다가갔다."]], [["\"Has not Magua kept the sun in his face long enough to escape all danger from the Canadians?\"", "\"〈마구아〉가 〈캐나다인〉들의 위험에서 모두 벗어날 만큼, 충분히 오래 해를 등지고 걸어오지 않았소?\""], ["he asked, as though no longer doubtful of the good intelligence established between them; \"and will not the chief of William Henry be better pleased to see his daughters before another night may have hardened his heart to their loss, to make him less liberal in his reward?\"", "그는 그들 사이에 좋은 양해가 이미 이루어졌음을 더는 의심치 않는 듯이 물었다. \"또〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 지휘관은, 그가 딸들을 잃은 슬픔에 마음이 굳어져 보답에 인색해지기 전, 또 하룻밤이 지나기 전에 딸들을 보는 편을 더 기뻐하지 않겠소?\""]], [["\"Do the pale-faces love their children less in the morning than at night?\"", "\"창백한 얼굴들은 제 자식을 밤보다 아침에 덜 사랑하오?\""], ["asked the Indian, coldly.", "인디언이 차갑게 물었다."]], [["\"By no means,\" returned Heyward, anxious to recall his error, if he had made one; \"the white man may, and does often, forget the burial-place of his fathers; he sometimes ceases to remember those he should love and has promised to cherish; but the affection of a parent for his child is never permitted to die.\"", "\"결코 그렇지 않소.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 혹여 자신이 실언을 했다면 그것을 되돌리고자 애쓰며 대답했다. \"백인은 제 조상의 무덤 자리를 잊을 수도 있고, 또 흔히 잊소. 때로는 마땅히 사랑해야 하고 아끼겠노라 약속한 이들을 기억하지 못하기도 하오. 그러나 자식을 향한 부모의 정만큼은 결코 죽도록 내버려 두지 않는 법이오.\""]], [["\"And is the heart of the white-headed chief soft, and will he think of the babes that his squaws have given him?", "\"그래, 흰머리 지휘관의 마음은 부드러우며, 제 여인들이 낳아 준 어린것들을 생각하겠소?"], ["He is hard to his warriors, and his eyes are made of stone!\"", "그는 제 전사들에게는 모질고, 그 눈은 돌로 되어 있던데!\""]], [["\"He is severe to the idle and wicked, but to the sober and deserving he is a leader, but just and humane.", "\"그는 게으르고 사악한 자들에게는 엄하나, 진중하고 떳떳한 자들에게는 지휘관이로되, 공정하고 인자하오."], ["I have known many fond and tender parents, but never have I seen a man whose heart was softer towards his child.", "나는 자식을 끔찍이 아끼고 다정한 부모를 숱하게 보아 왔지만, 그토록 자식을 향한 마음이 부드러운 사내는 본 적이 없소."], ["You have seen the gray-head in front of his warriors, Magua; but I have seen his eyes swimming in water, when he spoke of those children who are now in your power!\"", "〈마구아〉, 그대는 그 흰머리가 전사들 앞에 선 모습을 보았겠지만, 나는 지금 그대의 손아귀에 든 그 자식들을 이야기할 때 그의 눈이 눈물에 젖는 것을 보았소!\""]], [["Heyward paused, for he knew not how to construe the remarkable expression that gleamed across the swarthy features of the attentive Indian.", "〈헤이워드〉는 말을 멈추었으니, 귀 기울이던 그 인디언의 가무잡잡한 얼굴을 스쳐 번뜩인 기이한 표정을 어찌 풀이해야 할지 알 수 없었던 까닭이다."], ["At first it seemed as if the remembrance of the promised reward grew vivid in his mind, while he listened to the sources of parental feeling which were to assure its possession; but as Duncan proceeded, the expression of joy became so fiercely malignant, that it was impossible not to apprehend it proceeded from some passion more sinister than avarice.", "처음엔, 약속된 보답에 대한 기억이 그의 마음속에서 또렷해지는 듯했으니, 그 보답을 손에 넣게 해 줄 부정(父情)의 근원에 대한 이야기에 귀를 기울이는 동안이 그러했다. 그러나 〈덩컨〉이 말을 이어 갈수록, 그 기쁨의 표정은 어찌나 사납고 표독스러워졌던지, 그것이 탐욕보다 한층 음험한 어떤 격정에서 비롯된 것임을 헤아리지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["\"Go,\"", "\"가라.\""], ["said the Huron, suppressing the alarming exhibition in an instant, in a death-like calmness of countenance; \"go to the dark-haired daughter, and say, Magua waits to speak.", "그 〈휴런〉족은 그 섬뜩한 기색을 순식간에 억누르고, 죽은 듯이 잔잔해진 낯빛으로 말했다. \"검은 머리 딸에게 가서, 〈마구아〉가 할 말이 있어 기다린다고 전하라."], ["The father will remember what the child promises.\"", "아비는 그 딸이 약속한 바를 기억할 것이다.\""]], [["Duncan, who interpreted this speech to express a wish for some additional pledge that the promised gifts should not be withheld, slowly and reluctantly repaired to the place where the sisters were now resting from their fatigue, to communicate its purport to Cora.", "〈덩컨〉은 이 말을, 약속된 선물이 보류되지 않으리라는 추가의 다짐을 바라는 뜻으로 풀이하고, 느릿하고 마지못한 걸음으로 두 자매가 피로를 풀며 쉬고 있는 곳으로 가, 그 취지를 〈코라〉에게 전했다."]], [["\"You understand the nature of an Indian's wishes,\"", "\"당신은 인디언이 무엇을 원하는지 그 본질을 아실 테니,\""], ["he concluded, as he led her towards the place where she was expected, \"and must be prodigal of your offers of powder and blankets.", "그는 그녀가 기다려지고 있는 자리로 그녀를 데려가며 말을 맺었다. \"화약이며 담요를 아낌없이 제안하셔야 합니다."], ["Ardent spirits are, however, the most prized by such as he; nor would it be amiss to add some boon from your own hand, with that grace you so well know how to practise.", "그러나 저런 자가 가장 귀히 여기는 것은 독한 술이지요. 또 당신이 그토록 능란하게 베풀 줄 아는 그 우아함으로, 손수 무언가 호의를 더하는 것도 나쁘지 않을 겁니다."], ["Remember, Cora, that on your presence of mind and ingenuity even your life, as well as that of Alice, may in some measure depend.\"", "명심하십시오, 〈코라〉, 당신의 침착함과 기지에 〈앨리스〉의 목숨은 물론, 어느 정도는 당신 자신의 목숨까지도 달려 있을지 모른다는 것을.\""]], [["\"Heyward, and yours!\"", "\"〈헤이워드〉, 당신의 목숨도요!\""]], [["\"Mine is of little moment; it is already sold to my king, and is a prize to be seized by any enemy who may possess the power.", "\"내 목숨은 별것 아닙니다. 이미 내 왕에게 팔아넘긴 것이요, 그만한 힘을 지닌 적이라면 누구든 거머쥘 수 있는 전리품일 뿐이지요."], ["I have no father to expect me, and but few friends to lament a fate which I have courted with the insatiable longings of youth after distinction.", "나를 기다릴 아비도 없고, 명성을 향한 젊음의 채울 길 없는 갈망으로 자초한 운명을 애도해 줄 벗도 몇 안 됩니다."], ["But hush!", "하지만 쉿!"], ["we approach the Indian.", "인디언이 가까워졌습니다."], ["Magua, the lady with whom you wish to speak is here.\"", "〈마구아〉, 그대가 이야기하고자 한 부인이 여기 계시오.\""]], [["The Indian rose slowly from his seat, and stood for near a minute silent and motionless.", "인디언은 천천히 자리에서 일어나, 일 분 가까이 말없이 꼼짝도 않고 서 있었다."], ["He then signed with his hand for Heyward to retire, saying coldly,--", "그러다 손짓으로 〈헤이워드〉에게 물러가라 이르며 차갑게 말했다."]], [["\"When the Huron talks to the women, his tribe shut their ears.\"", "\"〈휴런〉족이 여인에게 말할 때면, 그 부족은 귀를 닫는 법이오.\""]], [["Duncan, still lingering, as if refusing to comply, Cora said, with a calm smile--", "〈덩컨〉이 응하기를 거부하듯 여전히 머뭇거리자, 〈코라〉가 잔잔한 미소를 띠고 말했다."]], [["\"You hear, Heyward, and delicacy at least should urge you to retire.", "\"들으셨지요, 〈헤이워드〉. 적어도 예의를 생각해서라도 물러가셔야 합니다."], ["Go to Alice, and comfort her with our reviving prospects.\"", "〈앨리스〉에게 가서, 되살아난 우리의 희망으로 그 애를 위로해 주세요.\""]], [["She waited until he had departed, and then turning to the native, with the dignity of her sex in her voice and manner, she added, \"What would Le Renard say to the daughter of Munro?\"", "그녀는 그가 떠날 때까지 기다렸다가, 그 원주민을 향해 돌아서서, 여인의 위엄을 목소리와 몸가짐에 실어 덧붙였다. \"〈르나르〉가 〈먼로〉의 딸에게 무슨 말을 하려는 거지요?\""]], [["\"Listen,\"", "\"들으시오.\""], ["said the Indian, laying his hand firmly upon her arm, as if willing to draw her utmost attention to his words; a movement that Cora as firmly but quietly repulsed, by extricating the limb from his grasp: \"Magua was born a chief and a warrior among the red Hurons of the lakes; he saw the suns of twenty summers make the snows of twenty winters run off in the streams, before he saw a pale-face; and he was happy!", "인디언이 자신의 말에 그녀의 온 주의를 끌어모으려는 듯, 그녀의 팔을 단단히 붙잡으며 말했다. 〈코라〉는 그 팔을 그의 손아귀에서 빼내어, 단호하되 조용히 그 동작을 물리쳤다."], ["Then his Canada fathers came into the woods, and taught him to drink the fire-water, and he became a rascal.", "\"〈마구아〉는 호수의 붉은 〈휴런〉족 가운데서 추장이자 전사로 태어났소. 그는 창백한 얼굴을 처음 보기 전, 스무 번의 여름 해가 스무 번의 겨울 눈을 시냇물로 녹여 흘려보내는 것을 보았소."], ["The Hurons drove him from the graves of his fathers, as they would chase the hunted buffalo.", "그리고 행복했소! 그런데 그의 〈캐나다〉 아비들이 숲으로 들어와, 그에게 불물을 마시는 법을 가르쳤고, 그는 건달이 되고 말았소."], ["He ran down the shores of the lakes, and followed their outlet to the 'city of cannon.'", "〈휴런〉족은 쫓기는 들소를 몰아내듯, 그를 조상의 무덤에서 내쫓았소."], ["There he hunted and fished, till the people chased him again through the woods into the arms of his enemies.", "그는 호수의 기슭을 따라 달려 내려가, 그 물줄기가 흘러 나가는 곳을 좇아 '대포의 도시'에 이르렀소. 그곳에서 그는 사냥하고 고기를 잡았으나, 사람들이 또다시 숲을 지나 그를 적들의 품으로 내몰았소."], ["The chief, who was born a Huron, was at last a warrior among the Mohawks!\"", "〈휴런〉족으로 태어난 그 추장이, 끝내 〈모호크〉족 가운데서 전사가 된 것이오!\""]], [["\"Something like this I had heard before,\"", "\"그 비슷한 이야기를 전에 들은 적이 있어요.\""], ["said Cora, observing that he paused to suppress those passions which began to burn with too bright a flame, as he recalled the recollection of his supposed injuries.", "〈코라〉가 말했으니, 그가 자신이 입었다 여기는 상처의 기억을 떠올리며 너무도 환히 불타오르기 시작하는 격정을 억누르느라 말을 멈추는 것을 본 까닭이었다."]], [["\"Was it the fault of Le Renard that his head was not made of rock?", "\"〈르나르〉의 머리가 바위로 되어 있지 않은 것이 그의 잘못이오?"], ["Who gave him the fire-water?", "누가 그에게 불물을 주었소?"], ["who made him a villain?", "누가 그를 악당으로 만들었소?"], ["'Twas the pale-faces, the people of your own color.\"", "바로 창백한 얼굴들, 당신과 같은 빛깔의 족속이오.\""]], [["\"And am I answerable that thoughtless and unprincipled men exist, whose shades of countenance may resemble mine?\"", "\"그렇다면 나와 살빛이 비슷할지 모르는, 생각 없고 줏대 없는 자들이 존재하는 것에 대해 내가 책임이라도 져야 하나요?\""], ["Cora calmly demanded of the excited savage.", "〈코라〉가 흥분한 그 미개인에게 차분히 따져 물었다."]], [["\"No; Magua is a man, and not a fool; such as you never open their lips to the burning stream: the Great Spirit has given you wisdom!\"", "\"아니오. 〈마구아〉는 사내요, 바보가 아니오. 당신 같은 이는 결코 그 타오르는 물줄기에 입을 대지 않지. 〈위대한 영(靈)〉이 당신에게 지혜를 주었으니까!\""]], [["\"What then have I to do, or say, in the matter of your misfortunes, not to say of your errors?\"", "\"그렇다면 그대의 불운에 대해, 그대의 잘못은 말할 것도 없거니와, 내가 무엇을 하고, 무엇을 말해야 한다는 거지요?\""]], [["\"Listen,\"", "\"들으시오.\""], ["repeated the Indian, resuming his earnest attitude; \"when his English and French fathers dug up the hatchet, Le Renard struck the war-post of the Mohawks, and went out against his own nation.", "인디언이 다시 진지한 태도로 돌아가며 거듭 말했다. \"그의 〈영국〉 아비와 〈프랑스〉 아비가 도끼를 파냈을 때, 〈르나르〉는 〈모호크〉족의 전쟁 기둥을 치고, 제 동족을 향해 나아갔소."], ["The pale-faces have driven the redskins from their hunting-grounds, and now when they fight, a white man leads the way.", "창백한 얼굴들은 붉은 살갗을 사냥터에서 몰아냈고, 이제 그들이 싸울 때는 백인이 앞장을 서지."], ["The old chief at Horican, your father, was the great captain of our war-party.", "〈호리컨〉의 그 늙은 추장, 곧 당신의 아비가 우리 전투대의 대장이었소."], ["He said to the Mohawks do this, and do that, and he was minded.", "그는 〈모호크〉족에게 이것을 하라, 저것을 하라 일렀고, 그대로 따랐소."], ["He made a law, that if an Indian swallowed the fire-water, and came into the cloth wigwams of his warriors, it should not be forgotten.", "그는 한 가지 법을 만들었으니, 인디언이 불물을 삼키고 제 전사들의 천막으로 들어오면, 그것을 잊지 않겠다는 것이었소."], ["Magua foolishly opened his mouth, and the hot liquor led him into the cabin of Munro.", "〈마구아〉는 어리석게 입을 열었고, 그 뜨거운 술이 그를 〈먼로〉의 막사로 이끌었소."], ["What did the gray-head?", "그 흰머리가 어찌했소?"], ["let his daughter say.\"", "그의 딸이 말해 보시오.\""]], [["\"He forgot not his words, and did justice by punishing the offender,\"", "\"그분은 자신의 말씀을 잊지 않으셨고, 죄지은 자를 벌하여 정의를 행하셨어요.\""], ["said the undaunted daughter.", "의연한 딸이 말했다."]], [["\"Justice!\"", "\"정의라!\""], ["repeated the Indian, casting an oblique glance of the most ferocious expression at her unyielding countenance; \"is it justice to make evil, and then punish for it?", "인디언이 그녀의 굽힘 없는 얼굴에 더없이 흉포한 표정의 곁눈질을 던지며 되뇌었다. \"악을 만들어 놓고, 그것을 빌미로 벌하는 것이 정의요?"], ["Magua was not himself; it was the fire-water that spoke and acted for him!", "〈마구아〉는 제정신이 아니었소. 그를 대신해 말하고 행한 것은 불물이었소!"], ["but Munro did not believe it.", "그런데도 〈먼로〉는 그것을 믿지 않았소."], ["The Huron chief was tied up before all the pale-faced warriors, and whipped like a dog.\"", "〈휴런〉족 추장은 창백한 얼굴의 전사들이 모두 보는 앞에서 묶인 채, 개처럼 채찍질을 당했소.\""]], [["Cora remained silent, for she knew not how to palliate this imprudent severity on the part of her father, in a manner to suit the comprehension of an Indian.", "〈코라〉는 잠자코 있었으니, 아비 쪽의 이 경솔한 가혹함을, 인디언이 알아들을 만한 방식으로 어떻게 누그러뜨려 변명해야 할지 알 수 없었던 까닭이다."]], [["\"See!\" continued Magua, tearing aside the slight calico that very imperfectly concealed his painted breast; \"here are scars given by knives and bullets--of these a warrior may boast before his nation; but the gray-head has left marks on the back of the Huron chief, that he must hide, like a squaw, under this painted cloth of the whites.\"", "\"보시오!\" 〈마구아〉가 칠을 한 가슴을 형편없이 가리고 있던 얇은 옥양목을 잡아 헤치며 말을 이었다. \"여기 칼과 총알이 남긴 상처들이 있소. 이런 것이라면 전사가 제 부족 앞에서 자랑할 만하지. 허나 그 흰머리는 〈휴런〉족 추장의 등에, 여인네처럼 백인들의 이 칠한 천 아래 숨겨야만 하는 자국을 남겨 놓았소.\""]], [["\"I had thought,\"", "\"나는 인디언 전사란 참을성이 있고,\""], ["resumed Cora, \"that an Indian warrior was patient, and that his spirit felt not, and knew not, the pain his body suffered.\"", "〈코라〉가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그 혼은 제 몸이 겪는 고통을 느끼지도 알지도 못하는 줄로 알았어요.\""]], [["\"When the Chippewas tied Magua to the stake, and cut this gash,\"", "\"〈치페와〉족이 〈마구아〉를 말뚝에 묶고 이 상처를 냈을 때,\""], ["said the other, laying his finger on a deep scar, \"the Huron laughed in their faces, and told them, Women struck so light!", "상대가 깊은 흉터에 손가락을 얹으며 말했다. \"그 〈휴런〉족은 그들의 면전에서 웃으며, 여인네들이 이보다 세게 친다고 했소."], ["His spirit was then in the clouds!", "그때 그의 혼은 구름 속에 있었소!"], ["But when he felt the blows of Munro, his spirit lay under the birch.", "그러나 〈먼로〉의 매질을 느꼈을 때, 그의 혼은 자작나무 회초리 아래 깔려 있었소."], ["The spirit of a Huron is never drunk; it remembers forever!\"", "〈휴런〉족의 혼은 결코 취하는 법이 없으며, 영원히 기억하오!\""]], [["\"But it may be appeased.", "\"하지만 그 혼도 달랠 수 있어요."], ["If my father has done you this injustice, show him how an Indian can forgive an injury, and take back his daughters.", "만약 내 아버지가 그대에게 이런 부당한 짓을 했다면, 인디언이 어떻게 상처를 용서하는지 그분께 보여 주고, 그 딸들을 도로 데려가게 해 주세요."], ["You have heard from Major Heyward--\"", "〈헤이워드〉 소령에게서 이미 들으셨다시피——\""]], [["Magua shook his head, forbidding the repetition of offers he so much despised.", "〈마구아〉는 고개를 저어, 그토록 경멸해 마지않는 제안을 되풀이하지 못하게 했다."]], [["\"What would you have?\"", "\"그럼 그대가 바라는 게 무엇인가요?\""], ["continued Cora, after a most painful pause, while the conviction forced itself on her mind that the too sanguine and generous Duncan had been cruelly deceived by the cunning of the savage.", "〈코라〉가 더없이 괴로운 침묵 끝에 말을 이었으니, 그동안 너무도 낙관적이고 너그러운 〈덩컨〉이 그 미개인의 간계에 잔혹하게 속아 넘어갔다는 확신이 그녀의 마음을 짓눌렀다."]], [["\"What a Huron loves--good for good; bad for bad!\"", "\"〈휴런〉족이 사랑하는 것——선에는 선으로, 악에는 악으로!\""]], [["\"You would then revenge the injury inflicted by Munro on his helpless daughters.", "\"그렇다면 그대는 〈먼로〉가 끼친 상처를 그의 가엾은 딸들에게 되갚으려는 거군요."], ["Would it not be more like a man to go before his face, and take the satisfaction of a warrior?\"", "그분의 얼굴 앞으로 나아가, 전사로서 응징을 취하는 편이 더 사내답지 않을까요?\""]], [["\"The arms of the pale-faces are long, and their knives sharp!\"", "\"창백한 얼굴들의 팔은 길고, 그 칼은 날카롭소!\""], ["returned the savage, with a malignant laugh: \"why should Le Renard go among the muskets of his warriors, when he holds the spirit of the gray-head in his hand?\"", "미개인이 표독스레 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"이미 그 흰머리의 혼을 손아귀에 쥐고 있는데, 어찌 〈르나르〉가 그 전사들의 머스킷 총 가운데로 들어가겠소?\""]], [["\"Name your intention, Magua,\"", "\"그대의 속셈을 말하세요, 〈마구아〉.\""], ["said Cora, struggling with herself to speak with steady calmness.", "〈코라〉가 흔들림 없이 침착하게 말하려 스스로와 싸우며 말했다."], ["\"Is it to lead us prisoners to the woods, or do you contemplate even some greater evil?", "\"우리를 포로로 숲으로 끌고 가려는 건가요, 아니면 그보다 더 끔찍한 악행을 꾀하는 건가요?"], ["Is there no reward, no means of palliating the injury, and of softening your heart?", "그 상처를 누그러뜨리고 그대의 마음을 풀어 줄 보상도, 방도도 전혀 없나요?"], ["At least, release my gentle sister, and pour out all your malice on me.", "적어도 내 가여운 동생만은 풀어 주고, 그대의 모든 원한은 내게 쏟으세요."], ["Purchase wealth by her safety, and satisfy your revenge with a single victim.", "동생의 안전으로 재물을 사들이고, 단 한 사람의 제물로 복수를 채우세요."], ["The loss of both of his daughters might bring the aged man to his grave, and where would then be the satisfaction of Le Renard?\"", "두 딸을 다 잃으면 그 노인은 무덤에 들지도 모르니, 그러면 〈르나르〉의 만족은 어디에 있겠어요?\""]], [["\"Listen,\"", "\"들으시오.\""], ["said the Indian again.", "인디언이 다시 말했다."], ["\"The light eyes can go back to the Horican, and tell the old chief what has been done, if the dark-haired woman will swear by the Great Spirit of her fathers to tell no lie.\"", "\"검은 머리 여인이 제 조상의 〈위대한 영〉께 맹세코 거짓을 말하지 않겠다고 한다면, 밝은 눈은 〈호리컨〉으로 돌아가 늙은 추장에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 전할 수 있소.\""]], [["\"What must I promise?\"", "\"내가 무엇을 약속해야 하나요?\""], ["demanded Cora, still maintaining a secret ascendency over the fierce native, by the collected and feminine dignity of her presence.", "〈코라〉가, 차분하고 여성스러운 위엄으로 그 사나운 원주민을 은밀히 압도하며 물었다."]], [["\"When Magua left his people, his wife was given to another chief; he has now made friends with the Hurons, and will go back to the graves of his tribe, on the shores of the great lake.", "\"〈마구아〉가 제 부족을 떠났을 때, 그의 아내는 다른 추장에게 주어졌소. 이제 그는 〈휴런〉족과 화해했으니, 그 큰 호수의 기슭, 제 부족의 무덤으로 돌아갈 것이오."], ["Let the daughter of the English chief follow, and live in his wigwam forever.\"", "〈영국〉 추장의 딸이 그를 따라가, 그의 천막에서 영원히 살게 하시오.\""]], [["However revolting a proposal of such a character might prove to Cora, she retained, notwithstanding her powerful disgust, sufficient self-command to reply, without betraying the weakness.", "이런 부류의 제안이 〈코라〉에게 아무리 역겨웠을지언정, 그녀는 그 격렬한 혐오감에도 불구하고, 그 약함을 드러내지 않고 대답할 만큼의 자제력을 잃지 않았다."]], [["\"And what pleasure would Magua find in sharing his cabin with a wife he did not love; one who would be of a nation and color different from his own?", "\"사랑하지도 않는 아내, 게다가 제 것과는 다른 종족, 다른 살빛의 아내와 천막을 함께 쓰며 〈마구아〉가 무슨 기쁨을 얻겠어요?"], ["It would be better to take the gold of Munro, and buy the heart of some Huron maid with his gifts.\"", "차라리 〈먼로〉의 황금을 받아, 그 선물로 어느 〈휴런〉족 처녀의 마음을 사는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?\""]], [["The Indian made no reply for near a minute, but bent his fierce looks on the countenance of Cora, in such wavering glances, that her eyes sank with shame, under an impression that, for the first time, they had encountered an expression that no chaste female might endure.", "인디언은 일 분 가까이 아무런 대꾸도 없이, 그 사나운 눈길을 〈코라〉의 얼굴에 박아 넣었는데, 그 흔들리는 눈초리가 어찌나 음험했던지, 정숙한 여인이라면 도저히 견딜 수 없는 어떤 표정과 처음으로 마주쳤다는 느낌에, 그녀의 눈이 부끄러움으로 내리깔렸다."], ["While she was shrinking within herself, in dread of having her ears wounded by some proposal still more shocking than the last, the voice of Magua answered, in its tones of deepest malignancy--", "그녀가 지난번보다 더 충격적인 어떤 제안에 귀를 다칠까 두려워 제 안으로 움츠러드는 동안, 〈마구아〉의 목소리가 더없이 음험한 음색으로 대답했다."]], [["\"When the blows scorched the back of the Huron, he would know where to find a woman to feel the smart.", "\"매질이 그 〈휴런〉족의 등을 지져 댈 때, 그는 그 쓰라림을 함께 느낄 여인을 어디서 찾아야 할지 알게 될 것이오."], ["The daughter of Munro would draw his water, hoe his corn, and cook his venison.", "〈먼로〉의 딸이 그의 물을 긷고, 그의 옥수수밭을 매고, 그의 사슴고기를 지을 것이오."], ["The body of the gray-head would sleep among his cannon, but his heart would lie within reach of the knife of Le Subtil.\"", "그 흰머리의 몸뚱이는 제 대포들 가운데서 잠들겠지만, 그의 심장은 〈르 쉬블〉의 칼이 닿는 곳에 놓여 있게 될 것이오.\""]], [["\"Monster!", "\"악마!"], ["well dost thou deserve thy treacherous name!\"", "그 음흉한 이름이 네게 참으로 어울리는구나!\""], ["cried Cora, in an ungovernable burst of filial indignation.", "〈코라〉가 걷잡을 수 없는 효심의 분노로 부르짖었다."], ["\"None but a fiend could meditate such a vengeance!", "\"오직 마귀만이 그런 복수를 꾸밀 수 있을 것이다!"], ["But thou overratest thy power!", "그러나 너는 네 힘을 과대평가하고 있어!"], ["You shall find it is, in truth, the heart of Munro you hold, and that it will defy your utmost malice!\"", "네가 손아귀에 쥔 것은 참으로 〈먼로〉의 심장이며, 그것이 네 극악한 악의를 비웃으리라는 것을 알게 되리라!\""]], [["The Indian answered this bold defiance by a ghastly smile, that showed an unaltered purpose, while he motioned her away, as if to close the conference forever.", "인디언은 이 대담한 항거에, 변치 않는 결심을 드러내는 섬뜩한 미소로 답하면서, 마치 그 회담을 영영 끝맺으려는 듯 그녀에게 물러가라 손짓했다."], ["Cora, already regretting her precipitation, was obliged to comply, for Magua instantly left the spot, and approached his gluttonous comrades.", "이미 자신의 성급함을 후회하던 〈코라〉는 따를 수밖에 없었으니, 〈마구아〉가 곧장 그 자리를 떠나 게걸스러운 동료들에게로 다가갔던 까닭이다."], ["Heyward flew to the side of the agitated female, and demanded the result of a dialogue that he had watched at a distance with so much interest.", "〈헤이워드〉가 동요한 여인의 곁으로 달려와, 그토록 깊은 관심으로 멀리서 지켜보던 대화의 결말을 물었다."], ["But unwilling to alarm the fears of Alice, she evaded a direct reply, betraying only by her countenance her utter want of success, and keeping her anxious looks fastened on the slightest movements of their captors.", "그러나 그녀는 〈앨리스〉의 불안을 자극하지 않으려, 직접적인 대답을 피한 채, 오직 표정으로만 자신이 완전히 실패했음을 드러내며, 근심 어린 눈길을 그들을 사로잡은 자들의 사소한 움직임 하나하나에 붙박아 두었다."], ["To the reiterated and earnest questions of her sister, concerning their probable destination, she made no other answer than by pointing towards the dark group, with an agitation she could not control, and murmuring, as she folded Alice to her bosom--", "그들이 어디로 가게 될지 거듭 간곡히 묻는 동생에게, 그녀는 다스릴 수 없는 동요로 그 검은 무리를 가리키는 것 말고는 다른 답을 하지 못했고, 〈앨리스〉를 제 가슴에 끌어안으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"There, there; read our fortunes in their faces; we shall see; we shall see!\"", "\"저기, 저기. 저들의 얼굴에서 우리의 운명을 읽으렴. 곧 알게 될 거야. 곧 알게 될 거야!\""]], [["The action, and the choked utterance of Cora, spoke more impressively than any words, and quickly drew the attention of her companions on that spot where her own was riveted with an intenseness that nothing but the importance of the stake could create.", "〈코라〉의 그 동작과 목멘 말은 어떤 말보다도 더 절절하게 와닿았고, 동행들의 시선을 재빨리, 그녀 자신의 눈길이 박혀 있던 그곳으로 끌어당겼으니, 걸린 것의 막중함만이 자아낼 수 있는 그런 골똘함으로 응시한 것이었다."]], [["When Magua reached the cluster of lolling savages, who, gorged with their disgusting meal, lay stretched on the earth in brutal indulgence, he commenced speaking with the dignity of an Indian chief.", "〈마구아〉가, 그 역겨운 식사로 배를 잔뜩 채우고 짐승 같은 방종 속에 땅바닥에 길게 드러누운 미개인 무리에게 다다랐을 때, 그는 인디언 추장의 위엄으로 말하기 시작했다."], ["The first syllables he uttered had the effect to cause his listeners to raise themselves in attitudes of respectful attention.", "그가 내뱉은 첫 음절들은 듣는 이들로 하여금 공손히 귀 기울이는 자세로 몸을 일으키게 하는 효과를 냈다."], ["As the Huron used his native language, the prisoners, notwithstanding the caution of the natives had kept them within the swing of their tomahawks, could only conjecture the substance of his harrangue, from the nature of those significant gestures with which an Indian always illustrates his eloquence.", "그 〈휴런〉족은 제 모국어를 썼던 까닭에, 포로들은, 원주민들의 경계로 인해 그 토마호크가 닿는 거리 안에 붙들려 있긴 했어도, 인디언이 늘 제 웅변에 곁들이는 그 의미심장한 몸짓의 성격으로 그 일장연설의 요지를 그저 짐작할 수 있을 따름이었다."]], [["At first, the language, as well as the action of Magua, appeared calm and deliberate.", "처음엔 〈마구아〉의 말도, 몸짓도 차분하고 신중해 보였다."], ["When he had succeeded in sufficiently awakening the attention of his comrades, Heyward fancied, by his pointing so frequently towards the direction of the great lakes, that he spoke of the land of their fathers, and of their distant tribe.", "그가 동료들의 주의를 충분히 일깨우는 데 성공하자, 〈헤이워드〉는 그가 큰 호수들 쪽을 자주 가리키는 것으로 미루어, 그들 조상의 땅과 멀리 있는 부족을 이야기하는 것이라 짐작했다."], ["Frequent indications of applause escaped the listeners, who, as they uttered the expressive \"Hugh!\"", "듣는 이들에게서 갈채의 기색이 잦게 새어 나왔으니, 그들은 그 표현적인 \"허!\""], ["looked at each other in commendation of the speaker.", "소리를 내뱉으며, 연사를 칭찬하듯 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Le Renard was too skilful to neglect his advantage.", "〈르나르〉는 이 이점을 소홀히 하기에는 너무도 능란했다."], ["He now spoke of the long and painful route by which they had left those spacious grounds and happy villages, to come and battle against the enemies of their Canadian fathers.", "그는 이제, 그 광활한 땅과 행복한 마을을 떠나 〈캐나다〉 아비들의 적과 싸우러 오기까지 거쳐 온 길고 고통스러운 여정을 이야기했다."], ["He enumerated the warriors of the party; their several merits; their frequent services to the nation; their wounds, and the number of the scalps they had taken.", "그는 일행의 전사들을 하나하나 꼽으며, 저마다의 공적, 부족에 바친 잦은 봉사, 그들의 상처, 그리고 그들이 거둔 머릿가죽의 수효를 열거했다."], ["Whenever he alluded to any present (and the subtle Indian neglected none), the dark countenance of the flattered individual gleamed with exultation, nor did he even hesitate to assert the truth of the words, by gestures of applause and confirmation.", "그가 그 자리의 누군가를 언급할 때마다(그리고 그 교활한 인디언은 한 사람도 빠뜨리지 않았다), 우쭐해진 자의 가무잡잡한 얼굴이 의기양양하게 번뜩였고, 그는 갈채와 동의의 몸짓으로 그 말의 진실함을 단언하기를 조금도 망설이지 않았다."], ["Then the voice of the speaker fell, and lost the loud, animated tones of triumph with which he had enumerated their deeds of success and victory.", "그러다 연사의 목소리가 낮아지며, 그들의 성공과 승리의 무용담을 늘어놓을 때의 그 크고 활기찬 승리의 어조를 잃었다."], ["He described the cataract of Glenn's; the impregnable position of its rocky island, with its caverns, and its numerous rapids and whirlpools; he named the name of La Longue Carabine, and paused until the forest beneath them had sent up the last echo of a loud and long yell, with which the hated appellation was received.", "그는 〈글렌〉 폭포를, 동굴이며 무수한 급류와 소용돌이를 거느린 그 바위섬의 난공불락의 형세를 그려 냈다. 그는 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉의 이름을 입에 올렸고, 그 미운 이름을 맞이하는 길고 우렁찬 함성의 마지막 메아리를 발아래 숲이 되돌려 보낼 때까지 말을 멈추었다."], ["He pointed towards the youthful military captive, and described the death of a favorite warrior, who had been precipitated into the deep ravine by his hand.", "그는 그 젊은 군인 포로를 가리키며, 제 손에 의해 깊은 협곡으로 떨어진 한 아끼던 전사의 죽음을 묘사했다."], ["He not only mentioned the fate of him who, hanging between heaven and earth, had presented such a spectacle of horror to the whole band, but he acted anew the terrors of his situation, his resolution and his death, on the branches of a sapling; and, finally, he rapidly recounted the manner in which each of their friends had fallen, never failing to touch upon their courage, and their most acknowledged virtues.", "그는 하늘과 땅 사이에 매달려 무리 전체에게 그토록 끔찍한 광경을 보여 준 그 전사의 운명을 언급했을 뿐 아니라, 어린나무 가지 위에서 그 처지의 공포와, 그의 결의와, 그의 죽음을 새로이 연기해 보였다. 그러고는 마침내, 자신들의 벗 하나하나가 어떻게 쓰러졌는지를 빠르게 되짚으며, 그들의 용기와 가장 인정받은 미덕에 빠짐없이 손을 댔다."], ["When this recital of events was ended, his voice once more changed, and became plaintive, and even musical, in its low guttural sounds.", "이 사건들의 서술이 끝나자, 그의 목소리는 다시 한번 바뀌어, 그 낮고 목쉰 소리 속에서 애처롭고, 심지어 가락마저 띠었다."], ["He now spoke of the wives and children of the slain; their destitution; their misery, both physical and moral; their distance; and, at last, of their unavenged wrongs.", "그는 이제 죽은 자들의 아내와 자식들을, 그들의 궁핍을, 육체와 정신 양면의 비참함을, 그들의 멀리 떨어진 처지를, 그리고 마침내 아직 복수하지 못한 그들의 원한을 이야기했다."], ["Then suddenly lifting his voice to a pitch of terrific energy, he concluded, by demanding,--", "그러다 별안간 목소리를 무시무시한 기세의 높이로 끌어올려, 다그쳐 물으며 끝을 맺었다."]], [["\"Are the Hurons dogs to bear this?", "\"〈휴런〉족이 이것을 견디는 개들이란 말인가?"], ["Who shall say to the wife of Menowgua that the fishes have his scalp, and that his nation have not taken revenge!", "누가 〈메노과〉의 아내에게, 물고기들이 그의 머릿가죽을 가져갔으며, 그의 부족은 복수하지 못했다고 말할 텐가!"], ["Who will dare meet the mother of Wassawattimie, that scornful woman, with his hands clean!", "누가 감히 〈와사와티미〉의 어미, 그 도도한 여인을 빈손으로 마주할 텐가!"], ["What shall be said to the old men when they ask us for scalps, and we have not a hair from a white head to give them!", "노인들이 우리에게 머릿가죽을 내놓으라 물을 때, 그들에게 줄 백인의 머리털 한 올 없다면, 우리는 무어라 말할 텐가!"], ["The women will point their fingers at us.", "여인들이 우리에게 손가락질을 할 것이다."], ["There is a dark spot on the names of the Hurons, and it must be hid in blood!\"", "〈휴런〉족의 이름에 검은 얼룩이 졌으니, 그것은 피로 씻어 가려야만 하리라!\""]], [["His voice was no longer audible in the burst of rage which now broke into the air, as if the wood, instead of containing so small a band, was filled with the nation.", "이제 공중으로 터져 나온 분노의 폭발 속에 그의 목소리는 더는 들리지 않았으니, 마치 숲이 그토록 작은 무리가 아니라 부족 전체로 가득 찬 듯했다."], ["During the foregoing address the progress of the speaker was too plainly read by those most interested in his success, through the medium of the countenances of the men he addressed.", "앞선 연설 동안, 연사의 진행은 그의 성공에 가장 깊은 관심을 둔 이들에게, 그가 향해 말하는 자들의 얼굴을 매개로 너무도 환히 읽혔다."], ["They had answered his melancholy and mourning by sympathy and sorrow; his assertions, by gestures of confirmation; and his boastings, with the exultation of savages.", "그들은 그의 비탄과 애도에 공감과 슬픔으로 답했고, 그의 단언에는 동의의 몸짓으로, 그의 자랑에는 미개인의 의기양양함으로 답했다."], ["When he spoke of courage, their looks were firm and responsive; when he alluded to their injuries, their eyes kindled with fury; when he mentioned the taunts of the women, they dropped their heads in shame; but when he pointed out their means of vengeance, he struck a chord which never failed to thrill in the breast of an Indian.", "그가 용기를 이야기할 때 그들의 눈길은 굳건하고 호응하는 듯했고, 그가 그들의 상처를 들먹일 때 그들의 눈은 분노로 타올랐으며, 그가 여인들의 조롱을 언급할 때 그들은 부끄러움에 고개를 떨구었다. 그러나 그가 복수의 수단을 가리켜 보이자, 그는 인디언의 가슴속에서 결코 떨려 울리지 않는 법이 없는 한 가닥 현(絃)을 건드렸다."], ["With the first intimation that it was within their reach, the whole band sprang upon their feet as one man; giving utterance to their rage in the most frantic cries, they rushed upon their prisoners in a body with drawn knives and uplifted tomahawks.", "그것이 제 손이 닿는 곳에 있다는 첫 암시에, 무리 전체가 한 사람처럼 벌떡 일어섰다. 더없이 광란에 찬 함성으로 분노를 토해 내며, 그들은 칼을 빼 들고 토마호크를 치켜든 채 한 덩어리가 되어 포로들에게 달려들었다."], ["Heyward threw himself between the sisters and the foremost, whom he grappled with a desperate strength that for a moment checked his violence.", "〈헤이워드〉는 두 자매와 맨 앞의 자 사이로 몸을 던져, 한순간 그 난폭함을 막아 낸 필사적인 힘으로 그자와 맞붙었다."], ["This unexpected resistance gave Magua time to interpose, and with rapid enunciation and animated gesture, he drew the attention of the band again to himself.", "이 뜻밖의 저항이 〈마구아〉에게 끼어들 틈을 주었고, 그는 빠른 발성과 활기찬 몸짓으로 무리의 주의를 다시 자신에게로 끌었다."], ["In that language he knew so well how to assume, he diverted his comrades from their instant purpose, and invited them to prolong the misery of their victims.", "그토록 능란하게 구사할 줄 아는 그 언어로, 그는 동료들을 그 즉각적인 목적에서 돌려세우고, 그 제물들의 고통을 길게 늘이도록 청했다."], ["His proposal was received with acclamations, and executed with the swiftness of thought.", "그의 제안은 갈채로 받아들여졌고, 생각의 속도로 실행에 옮겨졌다."]], [["Two powerful warriors cast themselves on Heyward, while another was occupied in securing the less active singing-master.", "힘센 전사 둘이 〈헤이워드〉에게 달려들었고, 그동안 또 하나는 덜 날쌘 그 성가대 선창자를 결박하는 일에 매달렸다."], ["Neither of the captives, however, submitted without a desperate though fruitless struggle.", "그러나 두 포로 어느 쪽도, 비록 헛될지언정 필사적인 몸부림 없이 굴복하지는 않았다."], ["Even David hurled his assailant to the earth; nor was Heyward secured until the victory over his companion enabled the Indians to direct their united force to that object.", "〈데이비드〉조차 자신을 덮친 자를 땅바닥에 메다꽂았고, 〈헤이워드〉도 동료를 제압한 승리 덕에 인디언들이 그 합쳐진 힘을 그를 향해 돌릴 수 있게 되기까지는 붙들리지 않았다."], ["He was then bound and fastened to the body of the sapling, on whose branches Magua had acted the pantomime of the falling Huron.", "이윽고 그는 묶여, 〈마구아〉가 떨어지는 〈휴런〉족의 무언극을 펼쳤던 그 어린나무 몸통에 단단히 매였다."], ["When the young soldier regained his recollection, he had the painful certainty before his eyes that a common fate was intended for the whole party.", "젊은 군인이 정신을 되찾았을 때, 그는 무리 전체에게 한가지 운명이 예정되어 있다는 고통스러운 확신을 눈앞에 두게 되었다."], ["On his right was Cora, in a durance similar to his own, pale and agitated, but with an eye, whose steady look still read the proceedings of their enemies.", "그의 오른편에는 〈코라〉가, 자신과 비슷하게 결박된 채, 창백하고 동요한 모습이었으나, 그 눈만큼은 흔들림 없는 시선으로 여전히 적들의 거동을 읽고 있었다."], ["On his left, the withes which bound her to a pine, performed that office for Alice which her trembling limbs refused, and alone kept her fragile form from sinking.", "그의 왼편에서는, 〈앨리스〉를 소나무에 묶은 가지 끈이, 그녀의 떨리는 사지가 거부한 그 노릇을 대신해, 홀로 그 연약한 몸이 주저앉지 않도록 지탱하고 있었다."], ["Her hands were clasped before her in prayer, but instead of looking upwards towards that power which alone could rescue them, her unconscious looks wandered to the countenance of Duncan with infantile dependency.", "그녀의 두 손은 기도하듯 앞으로 모아져 있었으나, 자신들을 구할 수 있는 유일한 힘을 향해 위를 우러르는 대신, 그 무심한 눈길은 어린아이 같은 의지심으로 〈덩컨〉의 얼굴을 향해 헤매었다."], ["David had contended, and the novelty of the circumstance held him silent, in deliberation on the propriety of the unusual occurrence.", "〈데이비드〉는 항거했으나, 그 상황의 생경함이 그를 잠자코 있게 하여, 이 예사롭지 않은 사태가 과연 온당한지를 곰곰이 따져 보게 했다."]], [["The vengeance of the Hurons had now taken a new direction, and they prepared to execute it with that barbarous ingenuity with which they were familiarized by the practice of centuries.", "이제 〈휴런〉족의 복수는 새로운 방향을 잡았고, 그들은 수백 년의 관행으로 몸에 밴 그 야만적인 재간으로 그것을 실행할 채비를 했다."], ["Some sought knots, to raise the blazing pile; one was riving the splinters of pine, in order to pierce the flesh of their captives with the burning fragments; and others bent the tops of two saplings to the earth, in order to suspend Heyward by the arms between the recoiling branches.", "더러는 활활 타는 장작더미를 쌓을 옹이를 찾았고, 한 사람은 포로들의 살을 불붙은 조각으로 꿰찌르려 소나무 가시를 쪼개고 있었으며, 또 다른 자들은 〈헤이워드〉를 튕겨 오르는 두 가지 사이에 두 팔로 매달려 하고자 두 어린나무의 우듬지를 땅으로 휘어 당겼다."], ["But the vengeance of Magua sought a deeper and a more malignant enjoyment.", "그러나 〈마구아〉의 복수는 한층 깊고 한층 음험한 쾌락을 좇고 있었다."]], [["While the less refined monsters of the band prepared, before the eyes of those who were to suffer, these well known and vulgar means of torture, he approached Cora, and pointed out, with the most malign expression of countenance, the speedy fate that awaited her:--", "무리 가운데 덜 세련된 흉물들이, 고통받을 자들의 눈앞에서, 이 익히 알려진 천박한 고문 수단들을 준비하는 동안, 그는 〈코라〉에게 다가가, 더없이 음험한 낯빛으로, 그녀를 기다리는 임박한 운명을 가리켜 보였다."]], [["\"Ha!\"", "\"하!\""], ["he added, \"what says the daughter of Munro?", "그가 덧붙였다. \"〈먼로〉의 딸이 무어라 하는가?"], ["Her head is too good to find a pillow in the wigwam of Le Renard; will she like it better when it rolls about this hill a plaything for the wolves?", "그 머리가 〈르나르〉의 천막에서 베개를 베기에는 너무 고결하다지. 그것이 늑대들의 놀잇감으로 이 언덕을 굴러다닐 때면 더 마음에 들겠는가?"], ["Her bosom cannot nurse the children of a Huron; she will see it spit upon by Indians!\"", "그 가슴이 〈휴런〉족의 아이들에게 젖을 물릴 수 없다지. 그것이 인디언들에게 침 뱉음을 당하는 꼴을 보게 되리라!\""]], [["\"What means the monster!\"", "\"저 악마가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말입니까!\""], ["demanded the astonished Heyward.", "깜짝 놀란 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"Nothing!\"", "\"아무것도 아니에요!\""], ["was the firm reply.", "단호한 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["\"He is a savage, a barbarous and ignorant savage, and knows not what he does.", "\"그는 미개인이에요, 야만스럽고 무지한 미개인이라, 제가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 몰라요."], ["Let us find leisure, with our dying breath, to ask for him penitence and pardon.\"", "죽어 가는 숨결로나마, 그를 위해 참회와 용서를 구할 여유를 가지도록 해요.\""]], [["\"Pardon!\"", "\"용서라!\""], ["echoed the fierce Huron, mistaking, in his anger, the meaning of her words; \"the memory of an Indian is longer than the arm of the pale-faces; his mercy shorter than their justice!", "사나운 〈휴런〉족이, 분노에 차 그녀의 말뜻을 오해하고 되받았다. \"인디언의 기억은 창백한 얼굴들의 팔보다 길고, 그의 자비는 그들의 정의보다 짧다!"], ["Say; shall I send the yellow hair to her father, and will you follow Magua to the great lakes, to carry his water, and feed him with corn?\"", "말하라, 노란 머리를 그 아비에게 보내 줄 테니, 너는 〈마구아〉를 따라 큰 호수로 가, 그의 물을 긷고, 그에게 옥수수를 먹이겠는가?\""]], [["Cora beckoned him away, with an emotion of disgust she could not control.", "〈코라〉는 다스릴 수 없는 혐오의 감정으로 그에게 물러가라 손짓했다."]], [["\"Leave me,\"", "\"날 내버려 둬.\" 그녀가, 한순간 인디언의 야만을 멈칫하게 한 엄숙함으로 말했다."], ["she said, with a solemnity that for a moment checked the barbarity of the Indian; \"you mingle bitterness in my prayers; you stand between me and my God!\"", "\"넌 내 기도에 쓰라림을 뒤섞고 있어. 넌 나와 내 하느님 사이를 가로막고 있어!\""]], [["The slight impression produced on the savage was, however, soon forgotten, and he continued pointing, with taunting irony, towards Alice.", "그러나 미개인에게 새겨진 그 옅은 인상은 이내 잊혔고, 그는 조롱 섞인 비아냥으로 〈앨리스〉를 가리키기를 이어 갔다."]], [["\"Look!", "\"보아라!"], ["the child weeps!", "저 아이가 우는구나!"], ["She is young to die!", "죽기엔 어리지!"], ["Send her to Munro, to comb his gray hairs, and keep life in the heart of the old man.\"", "저 애를 〈먼로〉에게 보내, 그의 백발을 빗기고, 노인의 가슴에 목숨을 붙들어 두게 하라.\""]], [["Cora could not resist the desire to look upon her youthful sister, in whose eyes she met an imploring glance, that betrayed the longings of nature.", "〈코라〉는 어린 동생을 바라보고픈 욕망을 떨칠 수 없었으니, 그 눈에서 그녀는, 본성의 갈망을 드러내는 애원의 눈빛과 마주쳤다."]], [["\"What says he, dearest Cora?\"", "\"저자가 뭐라고 해, 사랑하는 언니?\""], ["asked the trembling voice of Alice.", "〈앨리스〉의 떨리는 목소리가 물었다."], ["\"Did he speak of sending me to our father?\"", "\"날 아버지께 보내 준다고 했어?\""]], [["For many moments the elder sister looked upon the younger, with a countenance that wavered with powerful and contending emotions.", "한참 동안 언니는, 강렬하고 상반된 감정으로 흔들리는 얼굴로 동생을 바라보았다."], ["At length she spoke, though her tones had lost their rich and calm fulness, in an expression of tenderness that seemed maternal.", "마침내 그녀가 입을 열었으니, 비록 그 어조는 그 풍요롭고 잔잔한 충만함을 잃었으되, 어머니의 것인 듯한 다정함의 표현을 띠고 있었다."]], [["\"Alice,\"", "\"〈앨리스〉,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, \"the Huron offers us both life, nay, more than both; he offers to restore Duncan, our invaluable Duncan, as well as you, to our friends--to our father--to our heart-stricken, childless father, if I will bow down this rebellious, stubborn pride of mine, and consent--\"", "\"이 〈휴런〉족은 우리 둘 다에게 목숨을, 아니, 둘 이상을 주겠다는구나. 〈덩컨〉을, 우리의 더없이 소중한 〈덩컨〉을, 그리고 너를 우리 벗들에게, 우리 아버지에게, 가슴 찢긴 채 자식 잃은 우리 아버지에게 돌려보내 주겠다는구나, 내가 이 반항적이고 고집스러운 자존심을 굽히고, 응한다면——\""]], [["Her voice became choked, and clasping her hands, she looked upward, as if seeking, in her agony, intelligence from a wisdom that was infinite.", "그녀의 목소리가 목메었고, 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 그녀는 그 고뇌 속에서 무한한 지혜로부터 깨달음을 구하려는 듯 위를 우러렀다."]], [["\"Say on,\"", "\"말해 줘.\""], ["cried Alice; \"to what, dearest Cora?", "〈앨리스〉가 부르짖었다. \"무엇에 응한다는 거야, 사랑하는 언니?"], ["O, that the proffer were made to me!", "오, 그 제안이 내게 주어진 것이라면!"], ["to save you, to cheer our aged father, to restore Duncan, how cheerfully could I die!\"", "언니를 구하고, 늙은 아버지를 기쁘게 해 드리고, 〈덩컨〉을 돌려보낼 수 있다면, 내 얼마나 기꺼이 죽겠어!\""]], [["\"Die!\"", "\"죽는 거라!\""], ["repeated Cora, with a calmer and a firmer voice, \"that were easy!", "〈코라〉가 한결 차분하고 굳건한 목소리로 되뇌었다. \"그거라면 쉬울 텐데!"], ["Perhaps the alternative may not be less so.", "어쩌면 그 다른 길도 그만큼 쉬울지 모르지."], ["He would have me,\"", "저자는 나더러,\""], ["she continued, her accents sinking under a deep consciousness of the degradation of the proposal, \"follow him to the wilderness; go to the habitations of the Hurons; to remain there: in short to become his wife!", "그녀가, 그 제안의 굴욕에 대한 깊은 자각으로 어조를 가라앉히며 말을 이었다. \"저자를 따라 황야로, 〈휴런〉족의 거처로 가서, 거기 머물라는 거야."], ["Speak, then, Alice; child of my affections!", "한마디로, 저자의 아내가 되라는 거야! 그러니 말해 줘, 〈앨리스〉, 내 사랑하는 아이야!"], ["sister of my love!", "내 사랑하는 동생아!"], ["And you, too, Major Heyward, aid my weak reason with your counsel.", "그리고 당신도, 〈헤이워드〉 소령, 당신의 조언으로 내 약한 분별을 도와주세요. 그런 희생으로 목숨을 사야 하나요?"], ["Is life to be purchased by such a sacrifice?", "〈앨리스〉, 너는 그런 대가로 내 손에서 목숨을 받겠니?"], ["Will you, Alice, receive it at my hands at such a price?", "그리고 당신, 〈덩컨〉, 나를 이끌어 주세요."], ["And _you_, Duncan, guide me; control me between you; for I am wholly yours.\"", "두 분이서 나를 다잡아 주세요. 나는 온전히 두 분의 것이니까요.\""]], [["\"Would I!\"", "\"내가요!\""], ["echoed the indignant and astonished youth.", "분개하고 깜짝 놀란 청년이 받아쳤다. \"〈코라〉!"], ["\"Cora!", "〈코라〉!"], ["Cora! you jest with our misery!", "우리의 비참함을 두고 농을 하시는 겁니까!"], ["Name not the horrid alternative again; the thought itself is worse than a thousand deaths.\"", "그 끔찍한 길은 다시 입에 올리지 마십시오. 그 생각만으로도 천 번 죽는 것보다 더합니다.\""]], [["\"That such would be _your_ answer, I well knew!\"", "\"바로 그것이 당신의 대답이리라는 것을, 나는 잘 알고 있었어요!\""], ["exclaimed Cora, her cheeks flushing, and her dark eyes once more sparkling with the lingering emotions of a woman.", "〈코라〉가, 두 뺨이 붉어지고 검은 눈이 다시 한번 여인의 남은 감정으로 반짝이며 외쳤다. \"내 〈앨리스〉는 무어라 하니?"], ["\"What says my Alice? for her will I submit without another murmur.\"", "그 애를 위해서라면, 나는 더는 한마디 불평 없이 따르겠어.\""]], [["Although both Heyward and Cora listened with painful suspense and the deepest attention, no sounds were heard in reply.", "비록 〈헤이워드〉와 〈코라〉 둘 다 고통스러운 마음 졸임과 더없이 깊은 주의로 귀 기울였으나, 답으로 들려오는 소리는 없었다."], ["It appeared as if the delicate and sensitive form of Alice would shrink into itself, as she listened to this proposal.", "〈앨리스〉의 가녀리고 예민한 몸은, 이 제안에 귀 기울이는 동안 제 안으로 움츠러드는 듯했다."], ["Her arms had fallen lengthwise before her, the fingers moving in slight convulsions; her head dropped upon her bosom, and her whole person seemed suspended against the tree, looking like some beautiful emblem of the wounded delicacy of her sex, devoid of animation, and yet keenly conscious.", "그녀의 두 팔은 앞으로 길게 늘어뜨려져, 손가락이 가벼운 경련으로 움직였고, 머리는 가슴 위로 떨구어졌으며, 온몸은 나무에 매달린 채, 생기를 잃었으되 날카롭게 의식하고 있는, 상처 입은 여성다움의 어떤 아름다운 표상 같았다."], ["In a few moments, however, her head began to move slowly, in a sign of deep, unconquerable disapprobation.", "그러나 잠시 후, 그녀의 머리가 깊고 굽힐 수 없는 거부의 표시로 천천히 움직이기 시작했다."]], [["\"No, no, no; better that we die as we have lived, together!\"", "\"안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 우리가 살아온 그대로, 함께 죽는 편이 차라리 나아!\""]], [["\"Then die!\"", "\"그렇다면 죽어라!\""], ["shouted Magua, hurling his tomahawk with violence at the unresisting speaker, and gnashing his teeth with a rage that could no longer be bridled, at this sudden exhibition of firmness in the one he believed the weakest of the party.", "〈마구아〉가, 무리 가운데 가장 약하다 믿었던 자가 별안간 드러낸 이 굳셈에, 더는 억누를 수 없는 분노로 이를 갈며, 항거하지 않는 그 화자를 향해 토마호크를 사납게 내던지며 외쳤다."], ["The axe cleaved the air in front of Heyward, and cutting some of the flowing ringlets of Alice, quivered in the tree above her head.", "도끼가 〈헤이워드〉의 앞 공중을 가르며, 〈앨리스〉의 흘러내린 곱슬머리 몇 가닥을 베고는, 그녀의 머리 위 나무에 박혀 부르르 떨었다."], ["The sight maddened Duncan to desperation.", "그 광경에 〈덩컨〉은 미친 듯 절박해졌다."], ["Collecting all his energies in one effort, he snapped the twigs which bound him and rushed upon another savage who was preparing with loud yells, and a more deliberate aim, to repeat the blow.", "온 힘을 한 번에 그러모아, 그는 자신을 묶은 잔가지들을 끊어 버리고, 우렁찬 함성을 지르며 한층 신중한 겨냥으로 그 일격을 되풀이하려 채비하던 또 다른 미개인에게 달려들었다."], ["They encountered, grappled, and fell to the earth together.", "그들은 맞붙어, 엉겨, 함께 땅바닥으로 쓰러졌다."], ["The naked body of his antagonist afforded Heyward no means of holding his adversary, who glided from his grasp, and rose again with one knee on his chest, pressing him down with the weight of a giant.", "상대의 벗은 몸은 〈헤이워드〉가 적수를 붙들 만한 단서를 주지 않았으니, 그자는 그의 손아귀에서 미끄러져 나가, 한쪽 무릎으로 그의 가슴을 짚고 다시 일어서서는, 거인 같은 무게로 그를 짓눌렀다."], ["Duncan already saw the knife gleaming in the air, when a whistling sound swept past him, and was rather accompanied, than followed, by the sharp crack of a rifle.", "〈덩컨〉은 이미 칼이 공중에서 번뜩이는 것을 보았는데, 그때 휘파람 같은 소리가 그를 스쳐 지나갔고, 그에 뒤이었다기보다는 함께한 것이, 라이플의 날카로운 총성이었다."], ["He felt his breast relieved from the load it had endured; he saw the savage expression of his adversary's countenance change to a look of vacant wildness, when the Indian fell dead on the faded leaves by his side.", "그는 자신의 가슴이 견디던 짐에서 풀려나는 것을 느꼈고, 그 인디언이 곁의 시든 낙엽 위로 죽어 쓰러질 때, 적수의 얼굴에 깃든 그 미개한 표정이 멍하니 사나운 빛으로 바뀌는 것을 보았다."]], [["\"_Clo._--I am gone, sir, And anon, sir, I'll be with you again.\"", "클로. — 갑니다, 나리,\n이내, 나리,"]], [["_Twelfth Night._", "다시 모시러 오리다."]], [["The Hurons stood aghast at this sudden visitation of death on one of their band.", "「십이야」 〈휴런〉족은 제 무리 가운데 하나에게 이토록 느닷없이 죽음이 들이닥치자 아연실색하여 우뚝 섰다."], ["But, as they regarded the fatal accuracy of an aim which had dared to immolate an enemy at so much hazard to a friend, the name of \"La Longue Carabine\" burst simultaneously from every lip, and was succeeded by a wild and a sort of plaintive howl.", "그러나 동지를 그토록 큰 위험에 빠뜨리면서까지 적을 거꾸러뜨릴 만큼 대담했던 조준의 치명적인 정확성에 눈을 돌리는 순간, \"라 롱그 카라빈\"이라는 이름이 모든 입에서 동시에 터져 나왔고, 뒤이어 거칠고도 어딘가 구슬픈 울부짖음이 따랐다."], ["The cry was answered by a loud shout from a little thicket, where the incautious party had piled their arms; and at the next moment, Hawkeye, too eager to load the rifle he had regained, was seen advancing upon them, brandishing the clubbed weapon, and cutting the air with wide and powerful sweeps.", "그 외침에, 경솔한 무리가 무기를 쌓아 두었던 작은 덤불에서 우렁찬 함성이 화답했다. 그리고 다음 순간, 되찾은 소총을 미처 장전할 겨를도 없을 만큼 다급했던 〈매부리눈〉이 곤봉처럼 그 무기를 휘두르며 넓고 힘찬 호를 그려 허공을 가르며 그들에게로 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["Bold and rapid as was the progress of the scout, it was exceeded by that of a light and vigorous form which, bounding past him, leaped, with incredible activity and daring, into the very centre of the Hurons, where it stood, whirling a tomahawk, and flourishing a glittering knife, with fearful menaces, in front of Cora.", "척후의 전진이 대담하고 날래기는 했으되, 그를 앞질러 뛰어든, 가볍고도 기운찬 한 형상의 그것에는 미치지 못했다. 그 형상은 믿기 어려운 민첩함과 담대함으로 〈휴런〉족의 한복판으로 뛰어들어, 도끼를 빙빙 돌리고 번쩍이는 칼을 휘두르며, 〈코라〉 앞에 서서 무시무시하게 위협했다."], ["Quicker than the thoughts could follow these unexpected and audacious movements, an image, armed in the emblematic panoply of death, glided before their eyes, and assumed a threatening attitude at the other's side.", "이 뜻밖의 대담한 동작들을 생각이 미처 좇기도 전에, 죽음의 상징적 갑주로 무장한 또 다른 형상이 그들의 눈앞을 미끄러지듯 지나, 앞선 자의 곁에서 위협적인 자세를 취했다."], ["The savage tormentors recoiled before these warlike intruders, and uttered as they appeared in such quick succession, the often repeated and peculiar exclamation of surprise, followed by the well known and dreaded appellations of--", "미개한 고문자들은 이 호전적인 침입자들 앞에서 뒷걸음질했고, 그토록 빠른 잇따름으로 그들이 나타나자, 자주 되풀이되던 그 독특한 놀라움의 외침을 토했으니, 곧 익히 알려져 두려움을 사던 이름들이 그 뒤를 이었다."]], [["\"Le Cerf Agile!", "\"르 세르 아질!"], ["Le Gros Serpent!\"", "르 그로 세르팡!\""]], [["But the wary and vigilant leader of the Hurons was not so easily disconcerted.", "그러나 〈휴런〉족의 빈틈없고 경계심 깊은 우두머리는 그리 쉽사리 당황하지 않았다."], ["Casting his keen eyes around the little plain, he comprehended the nature of the assault at a glance, and encouraging his followers by his voice as well as by his example, he unsheathed his long and dangerous knife, and rushed with a loud whoop upon the expecting Chingachgook.", "작은 평지를 날카로운 눈으로 휘둘러보며 단번에 이 습격의 본질을 꿰뚫은 그는, 목소리로도 몸소 본을 보임으로써도 부하들을 북돋우며, 길고 위험한 칼을 뽑아 들고 우렁찬 함성과 함께 기다리던 〈칭가치국〉에게 달려들었다."], ["It was the signal for a general combat.", "그것은 전면적 격투의 신호였다."], ["Neither party had fire-arms, and the contest was to be decided in the deadliest manner; hand to hand, with weapons of offence, and none of defence.", "어느 쪽도 총기를 지니지 못했으니, 이 싸움은 가장 살벌한 방식으로 결판날 참이었다. 맨손으로 맞붙어, 공격용 무기만 있을 뿐 방어용 무기라곤 하나 없이."]], [["Uncas answered the whoop, and leaping on an enemy, with a single, well directed blow of his tomahawk, cleft him to the brain.", "〈운카스〉가 그 함성에 응답하여 한 적에게 뛰어들더니, 도끼의 단 한 번 정확한 일격으로 그를 골수까지 쪼개 버렸다."], ["Heyward tore the weapon of Magua from the sapling, and rushed eagerly towards the fray.", "〈헤이워드〉는 〈마구아〉의 무기를 어린나무에서 뽑아 들고, 격렬히 난투 속으로 달려들었다."], ["As the combatants were now equal in number, each singled an opponent from the adverse band.", "이제 양편의 수가 같아졌으니, 저마다 적의 무리에서 상대 하나를 골랐다."], ["The rush and blows passed with the fury of a whirlwind, and the swiftness of lightning.", "돌진과 타격이 회오리바람의 광포함과 번개의 신속함으로 오갔다."], ["Hawkeye soon got another enemy within reach of his arm, and with one sweep of his formidable weapon he beat down the slight and inartificial defences of his antagonist, crushing him to the earth with the blow.", "〈매부리눈〉은 곧 또 다른 적을 제 팔이 닿는 거리 안에 두었고, 그 가공할 무기의 단 한 번 휘두름으로 적의 빈약하고 어설픈 방어를 쳐 내려, 그 일격으로 땅바닥에 짓이겨 버렸다."], ["Heyward ventured to hurl the tomahawk he had seized, too ardent to await the moment of closing.", "〈헤이워드〉는 맞붙는 순간을 기다리기에는 너무 혈기가 앞선 나머지, 손에 쥔 도끼를 던지는 모험을 감행했다."], ["It struck the Indian he had selected on the forehead, and checked for an instant his onward rush.", "그것은 그가 고른 인디언의 이마를 맞혀, 한순간 그 전진을 멈추게 했다."], ["Encouraged by this slight advantage, the impetuous young man continued his onset, and sprang upon his enemy with naked hands.", "이 사소한 우위에 고무된 성급한 젊은이는 공격을 이어 가, 맨손으로 적에게 달려들었다."], ["A single instant was enough to assure him of the rashness of the measure, for he immediately found himself fully engaged, with all his activity and courage, in endeavoring to ward the desperate thrusts made with the knife of the Huron.", "단 한순간으로도 이 처사의 무모함을 충분히 깨달았으니, 그는 곧 〈휴런〉의 칼이 가하는 필사적인 찌르기를 막아 내려 제 온 민첩함과 용기를 다해 완전히 매달려야 하는 처지에 놓였다."], ["Unable longer to foil an enemy so alert and vigilant, he threw his arms about him, and succeeded in pinning the limbs of the other to his side, with an iron grasp, but one that was far too exhausting to himself to continue long.", "그토록 기민하고 빈틈없는 적을 더는 당해 낼 수 없게 되자, 그는 두 팔로 적을 끌어안아 무쇠 같은 움켜쥠으로 상대의 사지를 제 몸에 붙들어 매는 데 성공했으나, 그것은 그 자신을 너무도 지치게 하여 오래 버티기 어려운 것이었다."], ["In this extremity he heard a voice near him, shouting--", "이 절박한 처지에서 그는 가까이서 한 목소리가 외치는 것을 들었다."]], [["\"Extarminate the varlets!", "\"악당들을 몰살하라!"], ["no quarter to an accursed Mingo!\"", "저주받은 〈밍고〉에게는 자비란 없다!\""]], [["At the next moment, the breech of Hawkeye's rifle fell on the naked head of his adversary, whose muscles appeared to wither under the shock, as he sank from the arms of Duncan, flexible and motionless.", "다음 순간, 〈매부리눈〉의 소총 개머리판이 상대의 맨머리에 떨어졌고, 그 충격에 근육이 시들어 버리는 듯하더니, 그는 〈덩컨〉의 두 팔에서 축 늘어진 채 꿈쩍도 않고 미끄러져 내렸다."]], [["When Uncas had brained his first antagonist, he turned, like a hungry lion, to seek another.", "〈운카스〉는 첫 상대의 골을 부순 뒤, 굶주린 사자처럼 몸을 돌려 또 다른 적을 찾았다."], ["The fifth and only Huron disengaged at the first onset had paused a moment, and then seeing that all around him were employed in the deadly strife, he sought, with hellish vengeance, to complete the baffled work of revenge.", "첫 돌진에서 빠져나온 다섯째이자 마지막 〈휴런〉은 한순간 멈칫했으나, 이내 주위가 온통 살벌한 격투에 매여 있는 것을 보고는, 지옥 같은 앙심으로 좌절된 복수의 과업을 마무리하려 했다."], ["Raising a shout of triumph, he sprang towards the defenceless Cora, sending his keen axe, as the dreadful precursor of his approach.", "승리의 함성을 지르며, 그는 무방비의 〈코라〉에게로 달려들어, 그 접근의 끔찍한 전조로서 날카로운 도끼를 날렸다."], ["The tomahawk grazed her shoulder, and cutting the withes which bound her to the tree, left the maiden at liberty to fly.", "그 도끼는 그녀의 어깨를 스쳐, 그녀를 나무에 묶었던 가지 끈을 끊어, 처녀가 자유로이 달아날 수 있게 했다."], ["She eluded the grasp of the savage, and reckless of her own safety, threw herself on the bosom of Alice, striving with convulsed and ill-directed fingers, to tear asunder the twigs which confined the person of her sister.", "그녀는 미개인의 손아귀를 피하고, 제 안위는 아랑곳없이 〈앨리스〉의 가슴에 몸을 던져, 경련하는 갈팡질팡한 손가락으로 동생을 옭아맨 잔가지들을 갈라 떼어 내려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["Any other than a monster would have relented at such an act of generous devotion to the best and purest affection; but the breast of the Huron was a stranger to sympathy.", "괴물이 아닌 그 누구라도 가장 선하고 가장 순결한 애정을 향한 이토록 갸륵한 헌신 앞에서는 마음이 누그러졌으련만, 〈휴런〉의 가슴은 연민이란 것을 알지 못했다."], ["Seizing Cora by the rich tresses which fell in confusion about her form, he tore her from her frantic hold, and bowed her down with brutal violence to her knees.", "그녀의 형체를 따라 흐트러져 내린 풍성한 머리채를 움켜잡고, 그는 그녀를 그 미친 듯한 매달림에서 떼어 내, 잔인한 폭력으로 무릎 꿇렸다."], ["The savage drew the flowing curls through his hand, and raising them on high with an outstretched arm, he passed the knife around the exquisitely moulded head of his victim, with a taunting and exulting laugh.", "미개인은 흘러내린 곱슬머리를 손으로 훑어, 팔을 쭉 뻗어 높이 치켜들고는, 조롱하며 의기양양한 웃음과 함께 제물의 그 빼어나게 빚어진 머리 둘레로 칼을 둘렀다."], ["But he purchased this moment of fierce gratification with the loss of the fatal opportunity.", "그러나 그는 이 사나운 만족의 한순간을, 그 치명적인 기회를 잃는 것으로 사들였다."], ["It was just then the sight caught the eye of Uncas.", "바로 그때 그 광경이 〈운카스〉의 눈에 들어왔다."], ["Bounding from his footsteps he appeared for an instant darting through the air, and descending in a ball he fell on the chest of his enemy, driving him many yards from the spot, headlong and prostrate.", "그는 제자리에서 솟구쳐, 한순간 허공을 가르며 날아오르는 듯하더니, 한 덩어리가 되어 내리꽂혀 적의 가슴팍에 떨어졌고, 그를 그 자리에서 여러 야드나 곤두박질쳐 엎어지게 했다."], ["The violence of the exertion cast the young Mohican at his side.", "그 격렬한 힘은 젊은 〈모히칸〉을 그 곁으로 내동댕이쳤다."], ["They arose together, fought, and bled, each in his turn.", "두 사람은 함께 일어나, 싸우고, 번갈아 피를 흘렸다."], ["But the conflict was soon decided; the tomahawk of Heyward and the rifle of Hawkeye descended on the skull of the Huron, at the same moment that the knife of Uncas reached his heart.", "그러나 싸움은 곧 결판났다. 〈헤이워드〉의 도끼와 〈매부리눈〉의 소총이 〈휴런〉의 두개골에 떨어지는 바로 그 순간, 〈운카스〉의 칼이 그의 심장에 닿았다."]], [["The battle was now entirely terminated, with the exception of the protracted struggle between Le Renard Subtil and Le Gros Serpent.", "이제 르 르나르 쉬브틸과 르 그로 세르팡 사이의 길게 끄는 격투를 제외하고는, 전투가 완전히 끝났다."], ["Well did these barbarous warriors prove that they deserved those significant names which had been bestowed for deeds in former wars.", "이 야만의 전사들은 지난 전쟁들의 무공으로 얻은 그 뜻깊은 이름에 걸맞은 자들임을 톡톡히 증명했다."], ["When they engaged, some little time was lost in eluding the quick and vigorous thrusts which had been aimed at their lives.", "맞붙고 나서, 서로의 목숨을 노린 빠르고 힘찬 찌르기를 피하느라 잠시 시간이 흘렀다."], ["Suddenly darting on each other, they closed, and came to the earth, twisted together like twining serpents, in pliant and subtle folds.", "별안간 서로에게 덤벼들어, 둘은 한데 엉겨 땅바닥에 쓰러졌으니, 휘감기는 뱀들처럼 유연하고 교묘한 사림으로 서로 뒤틀려 있었다."], ["At the moment when the victors found themselves unoccupied, the spot where these experienced and desperate combatants lay, could only be distinguished by a cloud of dust and leaves which moved from the centre of the little plain towards its boundary, as if raised by the passage of a whirlwind.", "승자들이 제 손이 비었음을 깨달은 순간, 이 노련하고 필사적인 격투자들이 누운 자리는, 작은 평지의 한복판에서 그 가장자리로 회오리바람이 지나간 듯 일어나 움직이는 흙먼지와 나뭇잎의 구름으로밖에는 분간할 수 없었다."], ["Urged by the different motives of filial affection, friendship, and gratitude, Heyward and his companions rushed with one accord to the place, encircling the little canopy of dust which hung above the warriors.", "효심과 우정과 감사라는 저마다 다른 동기에 떠밀려, 〈헤이워드〉와 그 일행은 한마음으로 그곳으로 달려가, 두 전사 위에 드리운 작은 흙먼지 차양을 에워쌌다."], ["In vain did Uncas dart around the cloud, with a wish to strike his knife into the heart of his father's foe; the threatening rifle of Hawkeye was raised and suspended in vain, while Duncan endeavored to seize the limbs of the Huron with hands that appeared to have lost their power.", "〈운카스〉가 제 아비의 적의 심장에 칼을 박으려는 마음으로 구름 주위를 헛되이 맴돌았고, 〈매부리눈〉의 위협적인 소총이 헛되이 들려 멈춰 있었으며, 그동안 〈덩컨〉은 그 힘을 잃어버린 듯한 두 손으로 〈휴런〉의 사지를 붙들려 애썼다."], ["Covered, as they were, with dust and blood, the swift evolutions of the combatants seemed to incorporate their bodies into one.", "흙먼지와 피로 뒤덮인 채, 격투자들의 날랜 동작은 그 두 몸을 하나로 합쳐 놓는 듯했다."], ["The death-like looking figure of the Mohican, and the dark form of the Huron, gleamed before their eyes in such quick and confused succession, that the friends of the former knew not where nor when to plant the succoring blow.", "〈모히칸〉의 죽은 듯한 형상과 〈휴런〉의 거무스름한 형체가 그들의 눈앞에서 그토록 빠르고 어지러운 잇따름으로 번뜩이는지라, 앞선 자의 벗들은 구원의 일격을 어디에 또 언제 꽂아야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["It is true there were short and fleeting moments, when the fiery eyes of Magua were seen glittering, like the fabled organs of the basilisk, through the dusty wreath by which he was enveloped, and he read by those short and deadly glances the fate of the combat in the presence of his enemies; ere, however, any hostile hand could descend on his devoted head, its place was filled by the scowling visage of Chingachgook.", "사실, 짧고 덧없는 순간들이 있어, 〈마구아〉의 불타는 눈이, 그를 감싼 흙먼지의 화관 사이로, 전설 속 바실리스크의 기관처럼 번득이는 것이 보였고, 그는 그 짧고도 치명적인 눈길로 적들 앞에서 펼쳐지는 싸움의 운명을 읽었다. 그러나 그 어떤 적의 손이 그 운명 지어진 머리 위로 내리치기도 전에, 그 자리는 〈칭가치국〉의 험상궂은 얼굴로 채워졌다."], ["In this manner the scene of the combat was removed from the centre of the little plain to its verge.", "이런 식으로 격투의 장면은 작은 평지의 한복판에서 그 가장자리로 옮겨 갔다."], ["The Mohican now found an opportunity to make a powerful thrust with his knife; Magua suddenly relinquished his grasp, and fell backward without motion, and seemingly without life.", "이제 〈모히칸〉은 칼로 힘찬 일격을 가할 기회를 잡았다. 〈마구아〉는 별안간 움켜쥠을 풀고, 꿈쩍도 않고, 보아하니 숨도 없이, 뒤로 넘어졌다."], ["His adversary leaped on his feet, making the arches of the forest ring with the sounds of triumph.", "그의 적수는 벌떡 일어서며, 승리의 함성으로 숲의 둥근 천장을 울렸다."]], [["\"Well done for the Delawares!", "\"〈델라웨어〉족, 잘했다!"], ["victory to the Mohican!\"", "〈모히칸〉에게 승리를!\""], ["cried Hawkeye, once more elevating the butt of the long and fatal rifle; \"a finishing blow from a man without a cross will never tell against his honor, nor rob him of his right to the scalp.\"", "〈매부리눈〉이 길고 치명적인 소총의 개머리판을 또다시 치켜들며 외쳤다. \"혈통이 섞이지 않은 사내가 가하는 마지막 일격은 결코 그 명예에 누가 되지 않을 것이며, 머리가죽에 대한 그의 권리를 앗아 가지도 않으리라.\""]], [["But, at the very moment when the dangerous weapon was in the act of descending, the subtle Huron rolled swiftly from beneath the danger, over the edge of the precipice, and falling on his feet, was seen leaping, with a single bound, into the centre of a thicket of low bushes, which clung along its sides.", "그러나 바로 그 위험한 무기가 내리꽂히려는 순간, 교활한 〈휴런〉은 그 위험 아래에서 잽싸게 굴러 빠져나와, 벼랑 가장자리를 넘어 두 발로 떨어지더니, 단 한 번의 도약으로 그 비탈을 따라 매달려 자란 키 낮은 덤불 숲 한복판으로 뛰어드는 것이 보였다."], ["The Delawares, who had believed their enemy dead, uttered their exclamation of surprise, and were following with speed and clamor, like hounds in open view of the deer, when a shrill and peculiar cry from the scout instantly changed their purpose, and recalled them to the summit of the hill.", "적이 죽은 줄로만 믿었던 〈델라웨어〉족은 놀라움의 외침을 토하며, 사슴을 빤히 본 사냥개들처럼 빠른 걸음과 소란으로 뒤쫓으려 했으나, 척후의 날카롭고 독특한 외침이 단번에 그 마음을 돌려, 그들을 언덕 꼭대기로 다시 불러들였다."]], [["\"'Twas like himself,\"", "\"그자다운 짓이지.\""], ["cried the inveterate forester, whose prejudices contributed so largely to veil his natural sense of justice in all matters which concerned the Mingos; \"a lying and deceitful varlet as he is.", "〈밍고〉족에 관한 모든 일에서 천성의 정의감을 가리는 데 그토록 크게 한몫하던 편견을 지닌, 이 골수 산림꾼이 외쳤다. \"거짓되고 속임수 능한 악당이니까."], ["An honest Delaware now, being fairly vanquished, would have lain still, and been knocked on the head, but these knavish Maquas cling to life like so many cats-o'-the-mountain.", "정직한 〈델라웨어〉라면 정정당당히 패한 마당에 가만히 누워 머리를 얻어맞았을 테지만, 이 교활한 〈매콰〉 놈들은 산고양이처럼 목숨에 매달리거든. 가게 둬, 가게 둬."], ["Let him go--let him go; 'tis but one man, and he without rifle or bow, many a long mile from his French commerades; and, like a rattler that has lost his fangs, he can do no further mischief, until such time as he, and we too, may leave the prints of our moccasins over a long reach of sandy plain.", "사내 하나일 뿐이고, 그것도 소총도 활도 없이, 제 프랑스 동무들에게서 까마득히 먼 곳에 있으니까. 게다가 독니를 잃은 방울뱀처럼, 그자가 — 그리고 우리도 — 모래벌판을 한참 가로질러 모카신 자국을 남길 그런 때가 오기 전까지는 더 못된 짓이라곤 할 수가 없지."], ["See, Uncas,\"", "보아라, 〈운카스〉.\""], ["he added, in Delaware, \"your father is flaying the scalps already.", "그가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 덧붙였다. \"네 아비가 벌써 머리가죽을 벗기고 있구나."], ["It may be well to go round and feel the vagabonds that are left, or we may have another of them loping through the woods, and screeching like a jay that has been winged.\"", "한 바퀴 돌며 남은 부랑자들을 만져 보는 게 좋겠다. 안 그러면 또 한 놈이 숲을 껑충껑충 가로질러, 날개 다친 어치처럼 꽥꽥대며 달아날지도 모르니까.\""]], [["[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_\n_The battle was now entirely terminated, with the exception of the protracted struggle between Le Renard Subtil and Le Gros Serpent_]", "이렇게 말하며, 이 정직하나 가차 없는 척후는 죽은 자들 사이를 한 바퀴 돌며, 마치 그들이 짐승의 시체이기라도 한 양 태연히 그 감각 없는 가슴팍에 긴 칼을 박았다. 그러나 손위 〈모히칸〉이 이미 그를 앞질러, 저항 없는 시체들의 머리에서 승리의 표지를 떼어 낸 뒤였다."]], [["So saying, the honest, but implacable scout, made the circuit of the dead, into whose senseless bosoms he thrust his long knife, with as much coolness as though they had been so many brute carcasses. He had, however, been anticipated by the elder Mohican, who had already torn the emblems of victory from the unresisting heads of the slain.", "그러나 〈운카스〉는 제 습성을, 거의 천성이라 해도 좋을 것을 거슬러, 본능적인 섬세함으로 〈헤이워드〉와 함께 여인들을 도우러 날듯이 달려가, 〈앨리스〉를 재빨리 풀어 〈코라〉의 품에 안겼다."], ["But Uncas, denying his habits, we had almost said his nature, flew with instinctive delicacy, accompanied by Heyward, to the assistance of the females, and quickly releasing Alice, placed her in the arms of Cora.", "이토록 뜻밖에 목숨을 되찾고 서로를 되찾은 두 자매의 가슴속에 타오른, 만사를 주관하시는 전능자를 향한 감사를 우리는 굳이 묘사하려 들지 않겠다."], ["We shall not attempt to describe the gratitude to the Almighty Disposer of events which glowed in the bosoms of the sisters, who were thus unexpectedly restored to life and to each other.", "그들의 감사는 깊고도 말이 없었다."], ["Their thanksgivings were deep and silent; the offerings of their gentle spirits, burning brightest and purest on the secret altars of their hearts; and their renovated and more earthly feelings exhibiting themselves in long and fervent, though speechless caresses.", "그 온유한 영혼의 봉헌은 마음의 은밀한 제단 위에서 가장 밝고 순결하게 타올랐고, 새로워진 그들의 한결 인간적인 감정은 길고 뜨겁되 말 없는 어루만짐으로 드러났다."], ["As Alice rose from her knees, where she had sunk by the side of Cora, she threw herself on the bosom of the latter; and sobbed aloud the name of their aged father, while her soft, dove-like eyes sparkled with the rays of hope.", "〈앨리스〉는 〈코라〉의 곁에 주저앉았던 무릎에서 일어서며, 언니의 가슴에 몸을 던지고 늙은 아버지의 이름을 소리 내어 흐느꼈으니, 그 부드럽고 비둘기 같은 두 눈은 희망의 빛으로 반짝였다."]], [["\"We are saved!", "\"우린 살았어요!"], ["we are saved!\"", "우린 살았어요!\""], ["she murmured; \"to return to the arms of our dear, dear father, and his heart will not be broken with grief.", "그녀가 속삭였다. \"우리의 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아버지의 품으로 돌아가는 거예요."], ["And you too, Cora, my sister; my more than sister, my mother; you too are spared.", "아버지의 마음은 슬픔으로 찢기지 않을 거예요. 그리고 언니도, 〈코라〉 언니, 언니보다 더한 언니, 내 어머니 같은 언니, 언니도 화를 면했어요."], ["And Duncan,\"", "그리고 〈덩컨〉도.\""], ["she added, looking round upon the youth with a smile of ineffable innocence, \"even our own brave and noble Duncan has escaped without a hurt.\"", "그녀는 형언할 수 없는 순진함의 미소를 띠고 청년을 둘러보며 덧붙였다. \"우리의 용감하고 고귀한 〈덩컨〉마저 상처 하나 없이 벗어났어요.\""]], [["To these ardent and nearly incoherent words Cora made no other answer than by straining the youthful speaker to her heart, as she bent over her, in melting tenderness.", "이 뜨겁고도 거의 두서없는 말들에, 〈코라〉는 어린 화자를 가슴에 꼭 끌어안으며, 녹아드는 다정함으로 그 위에 몸을 숙이는 것 외에 다른 대답을 하지 않았다."], ["The manhood of Heyward felt no shame in dropping tears over this spectacle of affectionate rapture; and Uncas stood, fresh and blood-stained from the combat, a calm, and, apparently, an unmoved looker-on, it is true, but with eyes that had already lost their fierceness, and were beaming with a sympathy that elevated him far above the intelligence, and advanced him probably centuries before the practices of his nation.", "〈헤이워드〉의 사내다움은 이 애정 어린 환희의 광경에 눈물 흘리는 것을 부끄러워하지 않았다. 그리고 〈운카스〉는 격투의 흔적이 채 가시지 않은 핏자국 묻은 모습으로, 차분하게, 또 보아하니 마음의 동요 없는 구경꾼으로 서 있었으니, 그것은 사실이었으되, 그 두 눈은 이미 사나움을 잃고, 그를 제 부족의 지성보다 한참 위로 끌어올리고, 어쩌면 제 부족의 관습보다 수백 년은 앞서게 한 어떤 연민으로 빛나고 있었다."]], [["During this display of emotions so natural in their situation, Hawkeye, whose vigilant distrust had satisfied itself that the Hurons, who disfigured the heavenly scene, no longer possessed the power to interrupt its harmony, approached David, and liberated him from the bonds he had, until that moment, endured with the most exemplary patience.", "이 처지에서 그토록 자연스러운 감정의 표출이 펼쳐지는 동안, 빈틈없는 의심으로 저 천국 같은 광경을 흉하게 망쳐 놓던 〈휴런〉족이 더는 그 조화를 깨뜨릴 힘이 없음을 확인하고 안심한 〈매부리눈〉은, 〈데이비드〉에게 다가가, 그가 그 순간까지 더없이 본보기다운 인내로 견뎌 온 결박을 풀어 주었다."]], [["\"There,\"", "\"자.\""], ["exclaimed the scout, casting the last withe behind him, \"you are once more master of your own limbs, though you seem not to use them with greater judgment than that in which they were first fashioned.", "척후가 마지막 가지 끈을 뒤로 내던지며 외쳤다. \"당신은 다시 한번 제 사지의 주인이 되었소만, 그것이 처음 빚어졌을 때보다 더 분별 있게 그것을 쓰는 것 같지는 않구려."], ["If advice from one who is not older than yourself, but who having lived most of his time in the wilderness, may be said to have experience beyond his years, will give no offence, you are welcome to my thoughts; and these are, to part with the little tooting instrument in your jacket to the first fool you meet with, and buy some useful we'pon with the money, if it be only the barrel of a horseman's pistol.", "당신보다 나이가 많지도 않으나 평생 거의를 황야에서 살아 그 나이를 넘는 경험을 쌓았다 할 만한 자의 조언이 거슬리지 않는다면, 내 생각을 기꺼이 들려주리다. 그건 이렇소."], ["By industry and care, you might thus come to some prefarment; for by this time, I should think, your eyes would plainly tell you that a carrion crow is a better bird than a mocking thresher.", "당신 윗옷에 든 그 자그마한 피리 도구를 처음 만나는 바보에게 넘겨주고, 그 돈으로 쓸 만한 무기를, 그것이 한낱 기병 권총의 총신뿐일지라도, 사는 게 좋겠소. 부지런함과 정성으로, 당신은 그렇게 어느 정도 출세할 수도 있을 거요."], ["The one will, at least, remove foul sights from before the face of man, while the other is only good to brew disturbances in the woods, by cheating the ears of all that hear them.\"", "이맘때쯤이면, 내 생각엔, 시체를 먹는 까마귀가 흉내쟁이 개똥지빠귀보다 나은 새라는 걸 당신 두 눈이 똑똑히 일러 줄 테니까. 적어도 까마귀는 사람의 눈앞에서 더러운 광경을 치워 주지만, 다른 놈은 그 소리를 듣는 모든 것의 귀를 속여 숲속에 소동만 일으키는 데나 좋을 뿐이니 말이오.\""]], [["\"Arms and the clarion for the battle, but the song of thanksgiving to the victory!\"", "\"전투에는 무기와 나팔이나, 승리에는 감사의 노래를!\""], ["answered the liberated David.", "풀려난 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다."], ["\"Friend,\"", "\"벗이여.\""], ["he added, thrusting forth his lean, delicate hand towards Hawkeye, in kindness, while his eyes twinkled and grew moist, \"I thank thee that the hairs of my head still grow where they were first rooted by Providence; for, though those of other men may be more glossy and curling, I have ever found mine own well suited to the brain they shelter.", "그는 야위고 섬세한 손을 정답게 〈매부리눈〉에게 내밀며 덧붙였으니, 그 두 눈은 반짝이며 촉촉이 젖어 들었다. \"내 머리카락이 섭리께서 처음 뿌리내려 주신 그 자리에 여태 자라고 있음을, 그대에게 감사하오."], ["That I did not join myself to the battle, was less owing to disinclination, than to the bonds of the heathen.", "다른 이들의 머리칼이 더 윤기 나고 곱슬거릴지언정, 나는 늘 내 것이 그것이 감싼 두뇌에 잘 어울린다고 여겨 왔으니 말이오. 내가 전투에 합세하지 않은 것은 내키지 않아서가 아니라 이교도들의 결박 탓이었소."], ["Valiant and skilful hast thou proved thyself in the conflict, and I hereby thank thee, before proceeding to discharge other and more important duties, because thou hast proved thyself well worthy of a Christian's praise.\"", "그대는 이 싸움에서 용맹하고 능란함을 입증했으니, 더 중하고 다른 본분을 다하러 가기에 앞서, 이로써 그대에게 감사하오. 그대가 기독교인의 칭송을 받아 마땅함을 스스로 증명했기 때문이오.\""]], [["\"The thing is but a trifle, and what you may often see, if you tarry long among us,\"", "\"그까짓 건 사소한 일이고, 우리와 오래 머물다 보면 자주 보게 될 거요.\""], ["returned the scout, a good deal softened towards the man of song, by this unequivocal expression of gratitude.", "척후가 이 분명한 감사의 표현에 노래하는 이를 향해 한결 누그러져 대답했다."], ["\"I have got back my old companion, 'Killdeer,'\"", "\"내 옛 동무 '킬디어'를 되찾았으니.\""], ["he added, striking his hand on the breech of his rifle; \"and that in itself is a victory.", "그는 소총 개머리판을 손으로 치며 덧붙였다. \"그것만으로도 하나의 승리지."], ["These Iroquois are cunning, but they outwitted themselves when they placed their fire-arms out of reach; and had Uncas or his father been gifted with only their common Indian patience, we should have come in upon the knaves with three bullets instead of one, and that would have made a finish of the whole pack; yon loping varlet, as well as his commerades.", "이 〈이로쿼이〉 놈들은 교활하지만, 제 총기를 손 닿지 않는 데 둔 건 제 꾀에 제가 넘어간 셈이오. 만일 〈운카스〉나 그 아비가 인디언의 흔한 인내심만이라도 타고났던들, 우린 총알 하나가 아니라 셋으로 그 악당들을 덮쳤을 테고, 그러면 저 껑충껑충 달아난 놈까지 그 동무들과 함께 무리 전체를 끝장냈을 거요."], ["But 'twas all foreordered, and for the best.\"", "허나 다 미리 정해진 일이고, 그게 최선이었던 게지.\""]], [["\"Thou sayest well,\"", "\"옳은 말씀이오.\""], ["returned David, \"and hast caught the true spirit of Christianity.", "〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"그대는 기독교의 참된 정신을 깨우쳤구려."], ["He that is to be saved will be saved, and he that is predestined to be damned will be damned.", "구원받을 자는 구원받을 것이요, 저주받도록 예정된 자는 저주받을 것이오."], ["This is the doctrine of truth, and most consoling and refreshing it is to the true believer.\"", "이것이 진리의 교리요, 참된 신자에게는 더없이 위안이 되고 마음을 새롭게 하는 것이오.\""]], [["The scout, who by this time was seated, examining into the state of his rifle with a species of parental assiduity, now looked up at the other in a displeasure that he did not affect to conceal, roughly interrupting further speech.", "이때쯤 자리에 앉아 어버이 같은 정성으로 소총의 상태를 살피던 척후는, 이제 감추는 척도 하지 않은 못마땅함으로 상대를 올려다보며, 거칠게 말을 가로막았다."]], [["\"Doctrine, or no doctrine,\"", "\"교리든 뭐든.\""], ["said the sturdy woodsman, \"'tis the belief of knaves, and the curse of an honest man.", "이 다부진 산림꾼이 말했다. \"그건 악당들의 믿음이요, 정직한 사람에게는 저주요."], ["I can credit that yonder Huron was to fall by my hand, for with my own eyes I have seen it; but nothing short of being a witness will cause me to think he had met with any reward, or that Chingachgook, there, will be condemned at the final day.\"", "저기 저 〈휴런〉이 내 손에 쓰러질 운명이었다는 건 믿을 수 있소, 내 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보았으니까. 허나 직접 목격하지 않고서는, 그자가 무슨 보상을 받았다거나, 저기 저 〈칭가치국〉이 최후의 심판 날에 정죄당하리라는 건 결코 믿을 수가 없소.\""]], [["\"You have no warranty for such an audacious doctrine, nor any covenant to support it,\"", "\"그대는 그토록 당돌한 교리를 뒷받침할 보증도, 그것을 떠받칠 어떤 언약도 갖지 못했소.\""], ["cried David, who was deeply tinctured with the subtle distinctions which, in his time, and more especially in his province, had been drawn around the beautiful simplicity of revelation, by endeavoring to penetrate the awful mystery of the divine nature, supplying faith by self-sufficiency, and by consequence, involving those who reasoned from such human dogmas in absurdities and doubt; \"your temple is reared on the sands, and the first tempest will wash away its foundation.", "〈데이비드〉가 외쳤으니, 그는 제 시대에, 더욱이 제 식민지에서, 신성한 본성의 무서운 신비를 꿰뚫으려는 시도로, 자족으로써 믿음을 메우고, 그 결과 그런 인간의 교의로 추론하는 자들을 부조리와 의심 속에 빠뜨리며, 계시의 아름다운 단순함 둘레에 그어진 그 미묘한 구별들에 깊이 물들어 있었다. \"그대의 신전은 모래 위에 세워졌으니, 첫 폭풍우가 그 기초를 쓸어 가 버릴 거요."], ["I demand your authorities for such an uncharitable assertion (like other advocates of a system, David was not always accurate in his use of terms).", "나는 그토록 박정한 주장에 대한 그대의 전거를 요구하는 바요(다른 어떤 교리 옹호자들과 마찬가지로, 〈데이비드〉도 용어를 늘 정확히 쓰지는 못했다)."], ["Name chapter and verse; in which of the holy books do you find language to support you?\"", "장과 절을 대 보시오. 어느 성서에서 그대를 뒷받침할 말씀을 찾았소?\""]], [["\"Book!\"", "\"성서라!\" 〈매부리눈〉이 유별나면서도 잘 감추지 못한 경멸로 되뇌었다."], ["repeated Hawkeye, with singular and ill-concealed disdain; \"do you take me for a whimpering boy at the apron-string of one of your old gals; and this good rifle on my knee for the feather of a goose's wing, my ox's horn for a bottle of ink, and my leathern pouch for a cross-barred handkercher to carry my dinner?", "\"당신은 나를 늙은 할멈의 앞치마 끈에 매달려 칭얼대는 사내아이쯤으로 보는 거요? 그리고 내 무릎 위의 이 좋은 소총을 거위 깃펜으로, 내 황소 뿔을 잉크병으로, 내 가죽 주머니를 점심을 싸 다니는 격자무늬 손수건쯤으로 보는 거요?"], ["Book!", "성서라!"], ["what have such as I, who am a warrior of the wilderness, though a man without a cross, to do with books?", "비록 혈통이 섞이지 않은 사내이긴 하나 황야의 전사인 나 같은 자가 성서와 무슨 상관이 있단 말이오?"], ["I never read but in one, and the words that are written there are too simple and too plain to need much schooling; though I may boast that of forty long and hard-working years.\"", "나는 단 하나의 책에서밖에 읽지 않았는데, 거기 쓰인 말씀들은 너무 단순하고 너무 명백해서 많은 가르침이 필요 없소. 비록 그것이 마흔 해의 길고 고된 세월의 가르침이었다고 자랑할 수는 있겠지만 말이오.\""]], [["\"What call you the volume?\"", "\"그 책을 무어라 부르시오?\""], ["said David, misconceiving the other's meaning.", "〈데이비드〉가 상대의 뜻을 잘못 알아듣고 물었다."]], [["\"Tis open before your eyes,\"", "\"당신 두 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있소.\""], ["returned the scout; \"and he who owns it is not a niggard of its use.", "척후가 대답했다. \"그리고 그 임자는 그것을 쓰는 데 인색하지 않소."], ["I have heard it said that there are men who read in books to convince themselves there is a God.", "듣자 하니, 신이 있다는 걸 제 스스로 납득하려고 책에서 읽는 사람들이 있다더군."], ["I know not but man may so deform his works in the settlement, as to leave that which is so clear in the wilderness a matter of doubt among traders and priests.", "사람이 한번 정착지에서 제 일들을 그토록 망쳐 놓아서, 황야에서는 그토록 명백한 것을 장사꾼과 사제들 사이에서는 의심거리로 남겨 두는 건지도 모르지."], ["If any such there be, and he will follow me from sun to sun, through the windings of the forest, he shall see enough to teach him that he is a fool, and that the greatest of his folly lies in striving to rise to the level of One he can never equal, be it in goodness, or be it in power.\"", "그런 자가 있어 해 뜰 때부터 해 질 때까지 숲의 굽이굽이를 따라 나를 좇는다면, 자기가 바보라는 것과, 그 어리석음 중 가장 큰 것이 선함에서든 권능에서든 결코 견줄 수 없는 한 분의 자리로 오르려 애쓰는 데 있다는 것을 깨우치기에 족할 만큼은 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["The instant David discovered that he battled with a disputant who imbibed his faith from the lights of nature, eschewing all subtleties of doctrine, he willingly abandoned a controversy from which he believed neither profit nor credit was to be derived.", "자기가 자연의 빛에서 믿음을 길어 올리고 교리의 온갖 정교함을 멀리하는 논객과 다투고 있음을 깨닫는 순간, 〈데이비드〉는 이득도 명예도 끌어낼 수 없으리라 여긴 그 논쟁을 기꺼이 단념했다."], ["While the scout was speaking, he had also seated himself, and producing the ready little volume and the iron-rimmed spectacles, he prepared to discharge a duty, which nothing but the unexpected assault he had received in his orthodoxy could have so long suspended.", "척후가 말하는 동안, 그 역시 자리에 앉아, 늘 지니던 작은 책과 쇠테 안경을 꺼내, 정통 교리를 향해 받은 뜻밖의 공격이 아니었던들 그토록 오래 미루어졌을 리 없는 본분을 다할 채비를 했다."], ["He was, in truth, a minstrel of the western continent--of a much later day, certainly, than those gifted bards, who formerly sang the profane renown of baron and prince, but after the spirit of his own age and country; and he was now prepared to exercise the cunning of his craft, in celebration of, or rather in thanksgiving for, the recent victory.", "사실 그는 서반구의 음유시인이었으니, 일찍이 남작과 군주의 세속적 명성을 노래하던 그 재능 있는 음유시인들보다는 분명 훨씬 후대의 사람이었으되, 제 시대와 제 나라의 정신에 따른 자였다. 그는 이제 최근의 승리를 기리고자, 아니 오히려 그에 감사하고자, 제 기예의 솜씨를 발휘할 채비를 갖추었다."], ["He waited patiently for Hawkeye to cease, then lifting his eyes, together with his voice, he said, aloud,--", "그는 〈매부리눈〉이 말을 그칠 때까지 참을성 있게 기다렸다가, 두 눈과 목소리를 함께 들어 올려, 소리 높여 말했다."]], [["\"I invite you, friends, to join in praise for this signal deliverance from the hands of barbarians and infidels, to the comfortable and solemn tones of the tune, called 'Northampton.'\"", "\"벗들이여, 야만인과 이교도의 손에서 벗어난 이 놀라운 구원을 두고, '노샘프턴'이라 불리는 곡조의 편안하고도 장엄한 가락으로, 함께 찬미할 것을 권하오.\""]], [["He next named the page and verse where the rhymes selected were to be found, and applied the pitch-pipe to his lips, with the decent gravity that he had been wont to use in the temple.", "이어 그는 골라 둔 운율이 실린 쪽과 절을 일러 주고, 신전에서 늘 쓰던 그 점잖은 엄숙함으로 음높이 피리를 입에 댔다."], ["This time he was, however, without any accompaniment, for the sisters were just then pouring out those tender effusions of affection which have been already alluded to.", "그러나 이번에는 어떤 동반 가창도 없었으니, 두 자매가 바로 그때 앞서 언급한 그 다정한 애정의 토로를 쏟아 내고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Nothing deterred by the smallness of his audience, which, in truth, consisted only of the discontented scout, he raised his voice, commencing and ending the sacred song without accident or interruption of any kind.", "청중이 적은 것에, 사실 못마땅해하는 척후 하나뿐인 청중에 조금도 굴하지 않고, 그는 목소리를 높여, 그 어떤 사고도 방해도 없이 성가를 시작하고 끝맺었다."]], [["Hawkeye listened, while he coolly adjusted his flint and reloaded his rifle; but the sounds, wanting the extraneous assistance of scene and sympathy, failed to awaken his slumbering emotions.", "〈매부리눈〉은 부싯돌을 태연히 손보고 소총을 재장전하면서 귀를 기울였으나, 그 소리는 정경과 공감이라는 외적인 도움을 결한 까닭에, 잠들어 있는 그의 감정을 일깨우지 못했다."], ["Never minstrel, or by whatever more suitable name David should be known, drew upon his talents in the presence of more insensible auditors; though considering the singleness and sincerity of his motive, it is probable that no bard of profane song ever uttered notes that ascended so near to that throne where all homage and praise is due.", "음유시인이든, 〈데이비드〉를 무어라 더 알맞게 부를 이름이 있든, 이보다 더 무감각한 청중 앞에서 제 재능을 펼친 자는 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그 동기의 한결같음과 진실함을 헤아린다면, 세속의 노래를 부르는 그 어떤 음유시인도, 모든 경배와 찬미가 마땅히 바쳐질 그 보좌에 이토록 가까이 올라간 가락을 토한 적은 없었으리라."], ["The scout shook his head, and muttering some unintelligible words, among which \"throat\" and \"Iroquois,\"", "척후는 고개를 가로젓고, 알아들을 수 없는 몇 마디를 중얼거렸는데, 그중 \"목구멍\"과 \"〈이로쿼이〉\"만이 들렸을 뿐이며, 노획한 〈휴런〉족의 무기고 상태를 그러모아 살펴보러 걸어갔다."], ["were alone audible, he walked away, to collect, and to examine into, the state of the captured arsenal of the Hurons.", "이 일에 이제 〈칭가치국〉이 합류했으니, 그는 그 무기들 사이에서 제 것과 아들의 소총을 찾아냈다."], ["In this office he was now joined by Chingachgook, who found his own, as well as the rifle of his son, among the arms. Even Heyward and David were furnished with weapons; nor was ammunition wanting to render them all effectual.", "〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉마저 무기를 갖추게 되었고, 그것들을 모두 쓸모 있게 만들 탄약도 모자라지 않았다."]], [["When the foresters had made their selection, and distributed their prizes, the scout announced that the hour had arrived when it was necessary to move.", "산림꾼들이 고를 것을 다 고르고 노획품을 나누자, 척후는 떠나야 할 시각이 왔음을 알렸다."], ["By this time the song of Gamut had ceased, and the sisters had learned to still the exhibition of their emotions.", "이때쯤 〈개멋〉의 노래도 그쳤고, 두 자매도 감정의 표출을 가라앉히는 법을 익힌 터였다."], ["Aided by Duncan and the younger Mohican, the two latter descended the precipitous sides of that hill which they had so lately ascended under so very different auspices, and whose summit had so nearly proved the scene of their massacre.", "〈덩컨〉과 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 도움을 받아, 두 자매는 얼마 전 그토록 다른 처지에서 올랐던, 그리고 그 꼭대기가 하마터면 자신들의 학살의 무대가 될 뻔했던 그 가파른 언덕 비탈을 내려왔다."], ["At the foot, they found the Narragansetts browsing the herbage of the bushes; and having mounted, they followed the movements of a guide, who, in the most deadly straits, had so often proved himself their friend.", "기슭에 이르러, 그들은 〈내러갠셋〉 말들이 덤불의 풀을 뜯고 있는 것을 보았고, 말에 올라, 더없이 살벌한 곤경에서 그토록 자주 제 벗임을 증명해 온 안내자의 움직임을 따랐다."], ["The journey was, however, short.", "그러나 여정은 짧았다."], ["Hawkeye, leaving the blind path that the Hurons had followed, turned short to his right, and entering the thicket, he crossed a babbling brook, and halted in a narrow dell, under the shade of a few water elms.", "〈매부리눈〉은 〈휴런〉족이 따라온 어렴풋한 오솔길을 벗어나, 오른편으로 바투 꺾어, 덤불 속으로 들어가, 졸졸대는 시내를 건너, 몇 그루 물느릅나무 그늘 아래 좁은 골짜기에서 멈춰 섰다."], ["Their distance from the base of the fatal hill was but a few rods, and the steeds had been serviceable only in crossing the shallow stream.", "그 운명의 언덕 기슭에서의 거리는 고작 몇 로드에 지나지 않았으니, 말들은 그저 얕은 개울을 건너는 데에나 쓸모가 있었을 뿐이었다."]], [["The scout and the Indians appeared to be familiar with the sequestered place where they now were; for, leaning their rifles against the trees, they commenced throwing aside the dried leaves, and opening the blue clay, out of which a clear and sparkling spring of bright, glancing water, quickly bubbled.", "척후와 인디언들은 지금 이른 이 외딴곳에 익숙한 듯했다. 소총을 나무에 기대 세워 두고, 그들은 마른 잎을 옆으로 헤치며 푸른 진흙을 파헤치기 시작했고, 거기서 맑고 반짝이는 밝은 샘물이 일렁이며 이내 보글보글 솟아났다."], ["The white man then looked about him, as though seeking for some object, which was not to be found as readily as he expected:--", "이윽고 백인은, 기대만큼 쉽사리 찾아지지 않는 어떤 것을 찾는 양 둘레를 둘러보았다."]], [["\"Them careless imps, the Mohawks, with their Tuscarora and Onondaga brethren, have been here slaking their thirst,\"", "\"저 부주의한 도깨비 같은 〈모호크〉 놈들이, 제 〈터스커로라〉, 〈오논다가〉 형제들과 함께 여기 와서 목을 축였구먼.\" 그가 중얼거렸다."], ["he muttered, \"and the vagabonds have thrown away the gourd!", "\"그러고는 부랑자 놈들이 바가지를 내던져 버렸어!"], ["This is the way with benefits, when they are bestowed on such disremembering hounds!", "이런 잘 잊어먹는 개 같은 것들한테 은혜를 베풀면 다 이 꼴이지!"], ["Here has the Lord laid his hand, in the midst of the howling wilderness, for their good, and raised a fountain of water from the bowels of the 'arth, that might laugh at the richest shop of apothecary's ware in all the colonies; and see!", "여기, 주께서 울부짖는 황야 한복판에서, 그들의 이익을 위해 손을 펴시어, 땅속에서 물의 샘을 솟구치게 하셨으니, 식민지 전역의 그 어떤 으리으리한 약방 가게도 비웃을 만한 것이건만. 그런데 보라지!"], ["the knaves have trodden in the clay, and deformed the cleanliness of the place, as though they were brute beasts, instead of human men.\"", "악당들이 진흙을 짓밟아, 이곳의 정결함을 망쳐 놓았어. 마치 사람이 아니라 짐승이기라도 한 것처럼.\""]], [["Uncas silently extended towards him the desired gourd, which the spleen of Hawkeye had hitherto prevented him from observing, on a branch of an elm.", "〈운카스〉가 말없이 그를 향해 바라던 바가지를 내밀었으니, 그것은 느릅나무 가지에 걸려 있었으나 〈매부리눈〉의 언짢음 때문에 여태 그의 눈에 띄지 않았던 것이었다."], ["Filling it with water, he retired a short distance, to a place where the ground was more firm and dry; here he coolly seated himself, and after taking a long, and, apparently, a grateful draught, he commenced a very strict examination of the fragments of food left by the Hurons, which had hung in a wallet on his arm.", "거기 물을 채워, 그는 땅이 한결 단단하고 마른 데로 잠시 물러갔다. 거기서 그는 태연히 자리를 잡고 앉아, 길게, 또 보아하니 흡족하게 한 모금 들이켠 뒤, 제 팔에 멘 자루에 걸려 있던, 〈휴런〉족이 남긴 음식 조각들을 매우 꼼꼼히 살피기 시작했다."]], [["\"Thank you, lad!\"", "\"고맙다, 얘야!\""], ["he continued, returning the empty gourd to Uncas; \"now we will see how these rampaging Hurons lived, when outlying in ambushments.", "그가 빈 바가지를 〈운카스〉에게 돌려주며 말을 이었다. \"이제 이 날뛰는 〈휴런〉 놈들이 매복하러 나가 있을 때 어떻게 살았는지 좀 보자꾸나."], ["Look at this!", "이걸 봐!"], ["The varlets know the better pieces of the deer; and one would think they might carve and roast a saddle, equal to the best cook in the land!", "악당들이 사슴의 좋은 부위는 알아본단 말이야. 이 등심 토막을 보면 이 땅 최고의 요리사 못지않게 발라내고 구울 줄 안다 싶을 정도구먼!"], ["But everything is raw, for the Iroquois are thorough savages.", "허나 죄다 날것이야, 〈이로쿼이〉는 철저한 미개인들이니까."], ["Uncas, take my steel, and kindle a fire; a mouthful of a tender broil will give natur'", "〈운카스〉, 내 부싯쇠를 받아 불을 지피거라."], ["a helping hand, after so long a trail.\"", "이토록 긴 행군 끝에는, 연한 구이 한 입이 천성에 보탬이 될 테니.\""]], [["Heyward, perceiving that their guides now set about their repast in sober earnest, assisted the ladies to alight, and placed himself at their side, not unwilling to enjoy a few moments of grateful rest, after the bloody scene he had just gone through.", "안내자들이 이제 진지하게 식사 채비에 나서는 것을 본 〈헤이워드〉는, 숙녀들이 말에서 내리는 것을 도와 그들 곁에 자리를 잡았으니, 방금 겪은 피의 장면을 지나온 뒤 잠시나마 고마운 휴식을 누리는 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["While the culinary process was in hand, curiosity induced him to inquire into the circumstances which had led to their timely and unexpected rescue:--", "요리가 진행되는 동안, 호기심에 끌려 그는 자신들의 때맞은 뜻밖의 구출로 이어진 정황을 캐물었다."]], [["\"How is it that we see you so soon, my generous friend,\"", "\"이토록 빨리 그대를 보게 되다니 어찌 된 일이오, 너그러운 벗이여.\""], ["he asked, \"and without aid from the garrison of Edward?\"", "그가 물었다. \"그것도 〈에드워드〉 요새의 수비대 도움도 없이?\""]], [["\"Had we gone to the bend in the river, we might have been in time to rake the leaves over your bodies, but too late to have saved your scalps,\"", "\"우리가 강굽이로 갔던들, 당신들 시신 위에 나뭇잎을 그러모아 덮어 줄 만큼은 제때 닿았을지 모르나, 머리가죽을 구하기엔 너무 늦었을 거요.\""], ["coolly answered the scout.", "척후가 태연히 대답했다."], ["\"No, no; instead of throwing away strength and opportunity by crossing to the fort, we lay by, under the bank of the Hudson, waiting to watch the movements of the Hurons.\"", "\"아니, 아니지. 요새로 건너가느라 힘과 기회를 허비하느니, 우린 〈허드슨〉 강둑 아래 숨어, 〈휴런〉족의 움직임을 지켜보려 엎드려 있었던 거요.\""]], [["\"You were, then, witnesses of all that passed?\"", "\"그렇다면 그대들은 일어난 모든 일의 목격자였구려?\""]], [["\"Not of all; for Indian sight is too keen to be easily cheated, and we kept close.", "\"전부는 아니오. 인디언의 시력은 너무 예민해서 쉽사리 속아 넘어가지 않으니, 우린 바짝 숨어 있었소."], ["A difficult matter it was, too, to keep this Mohican boy snug in the ambushment.", "게다가 이 〈모히칸〉 애송이를 매복 속에 얌전히 붙들어 두는 것도 어려운 일이었지."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Uncas, Uncas, your behavior was more like that of a curious woman than of a warrior on his scent.\"", "〈운카스〉, 〈운카스〉, 네 행동거지는 냄새를 좇는 전사라기보다 호기심 많은 아낙에 가까웠다.\""]], [["Uncas permitted his eyes to turn for an instant on the sturdy countenance of the speaker, but he neither spoke nor gave any indication of repentance.", "〈운카스〉는 한순간 화자의 다부진 얼굴로 눈을 돌렸으나, 입을 떼지도, 뉘우치는 어떤 기색도 보이지 않았다."], ["On the contrary, Heyward thought the manner of the young Mohican was disdainful, if not a little fierce, and that he suppressed passions that were ready to explode, as much in compliment to the listeners, as from the deference he usually paid to his white associate.", "도리어 〈헤이워드〉는 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 태도가 사뭇 사납지는 않더라도 경멸 어리며, 또 그가 터질 듯한 격정을, 평소 백인 동료에게 바치던 경의에서만큼이나 듣는 이들에 대한 예우에서, 억누르고 있다고 여겼다."]], [["\"You saw our capture?\"", "\"우리가 사로잡히는 걸 보았소?\""], ["Heyward next demanded.", "〈헤이워드〉가 다시 물었다."]], [["\"We heard it,\"", "\"들었소.\""], ["was the significant answer.", "의미심장한 대답이었다."], ["\"An Indian yell is plain language to men who have passed their days in the woods.", "\"인디언의 함성은 숲에서 평생을 보낸 자들에겐 명백한 언어요."], ["But when you landed, we were driven to crawl, like sarpents, beneath the leaves; and then we lost sight of you entirely, until we placed eyes on you again, trussed to the trees, and ready bound for an Indian massacre.\"", "허나 당신들이 뭍에 오르자, 우린 뱀처럼 나뭇잎 아래로 기어들어야 했고, 그러고는 당신들을 완전히 시야에서 놓쳤다가, 나무에 묶여 인디언의 학살을 위해 단단히 결박된 당신들을 다시 두 눈으로 보게 된 거요.\""]], [["\"Our rescue was the deed of Providence.", "\"우리의 구출은 섭리의 역사였소."], ["It was nearly a miracle that you did not mistake the path, for the Hurons divided, and each band had its horses.\"", "그대가 길을 잘못 들지 않은 게 거의 기적이었으니, 〈휴런〉족이 갈라졌고 무리마다 제 말들을 거느렸으니 말이오.\""]], [["\"Ay!", "\"그렇소! 거기서 우린 냄새를 놓쳤고, 〈운카스〉가 아니었던들 정말로 자취를 잃었을 거요."], ["there we were thrown off the scent, and might, indeed, have lost the trail, had it not been for Uncas; we took the path, however, that led into the wilderness; for we judged, and judged rightly, that the savages would hold that course with their prisoners.", "허나 우린 황야로 이어지는 길을 택했소. 미개인들이 포로를 데리고 그 길을 갈 거라고 판단했고, 그 판단이 옳았으니까."], ["But when we had followed it for many miles, without finding a single twig broken, as I had advised, my mind misgave me; especially as all the footsteps had the prints of moccasins.\"", "그러나 내가 일러둔 대로 부러진 잔가지 하나 찾지 못한 채 여러 마일을 따라가다 보니, 마음에 미심쩍은 데가 생겼소. 더욱이 모든 발자국이 모카신 자국이었으니 말이오.\""]], [["\"Our captors had the precaution to see us shod like themselves,\"", "\"우리를 사로잡은 자들이 우리에게도 자기들처럼 신을 신기는 조심성을 부렸소.\""], ["said Duncan, raising a foot, and exhibiting the buckskin he wore.", "〈덩컨〉이 한 발을 들어, 자기가 신은 사슴가죽 신을 내보이며 말했다."]], [["\"Ay, 'twas judgmatical, and like themselves; though we were too expart to be thrown from a trail by so common an invention.\"", "\"그렇군, 그건 분별 있고, 그들다운 짓이었소. 허나 우린 그런 흔한 술책에 자취를 놓칠 만큼 풋내기는 아니었지.\""]], [["\"To what, then, are we indebted for our safety?\"", "\"그렇다면 우리는 무엇에 우리의 안전을 빚진 거요?\""]], [["\"To what, as a white man who has no taint of Indian blood, I should be ashamed to own; to the judgment of the young Mohican, in matters which I should know better than he, but which I can now hardly believe to be true, though my own eyes tell me it is so.\"", "\"인디언의 피가 한 방울도 섞이지 않은 백인으로서 인정하기 부끄러운 것에 빚졌소. 내가 그 애보다 더 잘 알았어야 마땅한 일에서, 그 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 판단에 말이오. 비록 내 두 눈이 그렇다 일러 주는데도 지금도 도무지 참이라고 믿기 어려운 것이지만.\""]], [["\"'Tis extraordinary!", "\"참으로 기이하구려!"], ["will you not name the reason?\"", "그 까닭을 말해 주지 않겠소?\""]], [["\"Uncas was bold enough to say, that the beasts ridden by the gentle ones,\"", "\"〈운카스〉는 대담하게도, 저 숙녀분들이 탄 짐승들이,\""], ["continued Hawkeye, glancing his eyes, not without curious interest, on the fillies of the ladies, \"planted the legs of one side on the ground at the same time, which is contrary to the movements of all trotting four-footed animals of my knowledge, except the bear.", "〈매부리눈〉이 호기심 어린 흥미가 없지 않게 숙녀들의 어린 암말들로 눈길을 돌리며 말을 이었다. \"한쪽의 두 다리를 동시에 땅에 디딘다고 했는데, 그건 내가 아는 한 곰을 빼고는 모든 빠른 걸음의 네발짐승의 움직임에 어긋나는 것이오."], ["And yet here are horses that always journey in this manner, as my own eyes have seen, and as their trail has shown for twenty long miles.\"", "그런데 여기, 내 두 눈으로 보았고, 그 자취가 스무 마일 내내 보여 준 대로, 늘 이런 식으로 걷는 말들이 있단 말이지.\""]], [["\"'Tis the merit of the animal!", "\"그건 그 짐승의 장점이라오!"], ["They come from the shores of Narragansett Bay, in the small province of Providence Plantations, and are celebrated for their hardihood, and the ease of this peculiar movement; though other horses are not unfrequently trained to the same.\"", "그것들은 〈프로비던스 플랜테이션스〉라는 작은 식민지의 〈내러갠셋〉 만 기슭에서 왔는데, 그 강인함과 이 독특한 걸음걸이의 편안함으로 이름이 높소. 비록 다른 말들도 같은 걸음을 익히는 일이 드물지는 않지만 말이오.\""]], [["\"It may be--it may be,\"", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지, 그럴 수도 있겠어.\""], ["said Hawkeye, who had listened with singular attention to this explanation; \"though I am a man who has the full blood of the whites, my judgment in deer and beaver is greater than in beasts of burden.", "이 설명에 유달리 귀를 기울이던 〈매부리눈〉이 말했다. \"비록 내가 백인의 피를 온전히 지닌 사람이긴 하나, 사슴과 비버에 관한 내 판단이 짐 나르는 짐승에 관한 것보다는 나으니까."], ["Major Effingham has many noble chargers, but I have never seen one travel after such a sideling gait.\"", "〈에핑엄〉 소령께 고귀한 군마가 여럿 있으나, 나는 그런 옆으로 디디는 걸음으로 가는 놈은 한 번도 본 적이 없소.\""]], [["\"True; for he would value the animals for very different properties.", "\"옳소. 그분이라면 사뭇 다른 자질로 그 짐승을 높이 살 테니까."], ["Still is this a breed highly esteemed, and as you witness, much honored with the burdens it is often destined to bear.\"", "그래도 이건 높이 평가받는 품종이며, 그대가 보다시피, 자주 짊어지도록 운명 지어진 짐으로 큰 영예를 누리는 짐승이오.\""]], [["The Mohicans had suspended their operations about the glimmering fire, to listen; and when Duncan had done, they looked at each other significantly, the father uttering the never-failing exclamation of surprise.", "〈모히칸〉 부자는 가물거리는 불 곁에서 하던 일을 멈추고 귀를 기울였다. 〈덩컨〉의 말이 끝나자, 그들은 의미심장하게 서로를 바라보았고, 아비가 결코 빠지는 법 없는 놀라움의 외침을 토했다."], ["The scout ruminated, like a man digesting his newly acquired knowledge, and once more stole a curious glance at the horses.", "척후는 새로이 얻은 지식을 곱씹는 사람처럼 되새기더니, 또 한 번 호기심 어린 눈길을 슬그머니 말들에게 던졌다."]], [["\"I dare to say there are even stranger sights to be seen in the settlements!\"", "\"감히 말하건대 정착지에는 이보다 더 기이한 광경도 볼 수 있겠지!\""], ["he said, at length; \"natur'", "그가 마침내 말했다."], ["is sadly abused by man, when he once gets the mastery.", "\"사람이 일단 주도권을 쥐면, 천성이란 게 딱하게도 짓밟히고 마는 법이야."], ["But, go sideling or go straight, Uncas had seen the movement, and their trail led us on to the broken bush.", "허나 옆으로 디디든 곧장 디디든, 〈운카스〉가 그 움직임을 보았고, 그 자취가 우리를 부러진 덤불로 이끌었소."], ["The outer branch, near the prints of one of the horses, was bent upward, as a lady breaks a flower from its stem, but all the rest were ragged and broken down, as if the strong hand of a man had been tearing them!", "한 말의 발자국 가까이, 바깥쪽 가지가 위로 휘어 있었는데, 마치 숙녀가 줄기에서 꽃 한 송이를 꺾듯 한 것이었으나, 나머지는 죄다 사내의 억센 손이 잡아 뜯기라도 한 양 너덜너덜 부러져 있었소!"], ["So I concluded that the cunning varmints had seen the twig bent, and had torn the rest, to make us believe a buck had been feeling the boughs with his antlers.\"", "그래서 나는 교활한 놈들이 그 휜 잔가지를 보고, 수사슴이 뿔로 가지를 비벼 댔다고 우리가 믿게끔 나머지를 잡아 뜯었다고 결론지었소.\""]], [["\"I do believe your sagacity did not deceive you; for some such thing occurred!\"", "\"그대의 명민함이 그대를 속이지 않은 게 분명하오. 정말 그런 일이 있었으니!\""]], [["\"That was easy to see,\"", "\"그건 알아보기 쉬운 일이었소.\""], ["added the scout, in no degree conscious of having exhibited any extraordinary sagacity; \"and a very different matter it was from a waddling horse!", "척후가 어떤 비범한 명민함을 드러냈다는 의식이라곤 조금도 없이 덧붙였다. \"그건 뒤뚱대는 말과는 사뭇 다른 문제였지!"], ["It then struck me the Mingos would push for this spring, for the knaves well know the vartue of its waters!\"", "그때 나는 〈밍고〉 놈들이 이 샘으로 밀어붙이리라는 데 생각이 미쳤소. 악당들이 그 물의 효험을 잘 아니까!\""]], [["\"Is it, then, so famous?\"", "\"그렇다면 그토록 이름난 곳이오?\""], ["demanded Heyward, examining, with a more curious eye, the secluded dell, with its bubbling fountain, surrounded, as it was, by earth of a deep dingy brown.", "〈헤이워드〉가, 그 깊고 거무칙칙한 갈색 흙으로 둘러싸인, 보글대는 샘이 솟는 이 외딴 골짜기를 한층 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피며 물었다."]], [["\"Few redskins, who travel south and east of the great lakes, but have heard of its qualities.", "\"오대호의 남쪽과 동쪽을 다니는 홍인종치고, 그 효능을 들어 보지 못한 자는 드물 거요."], ["Will you taste for yourself?\"", "직접 한번 맛보겠소?\""]], [["Heyward took the gourd, and after swallowing a little of the water, threw it aside with grimaces of discontent.", "〈헤이워드〉는 바가지를 받아, 물을 조금 삼키더니, 못마땅한 얼굴을 찡그리며 옆으로 던져 버렸다."], ["The scout laughed in his silent, but heartfelt manner, and shook his head with vast satisfaction.", "척후는 그 소리 없으나 진정에서 우러난 투로 웃으며, 더없는 흡족함으로 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아! 당신에겐 습관으로 얻어지는 그 맛이 없는 거요."], ["you want the flavor that one gets by habit; the time was when I liked it as little as yourself; but I have come to my taste, and I now crave it, as a deer does the licks.[17] Your high spiced wines are not better liked than a redskin relishes this water; especially when his natur'", "나도 한때는 당신만큼이나 이 맛을 싫어했지. 허나 차차 내 입에 맞게 되어, 이젠 사슴이 소금기 있는 곳을 찾듯 이걸 갈망하게 됐소."], ["is ailing.", "당신네 진하게 양념한 포도주도 홍인종이 이 물을 즐기는 것만큼은 못한다오. 특히 그 천성이 병들었을 때는 말이지."], ["But Uncas has made his fire, and it is time we think of eating, for our journey is long, and all before us.\"", "허나 〈운카스〉가 불을 지폈으니, 이제 먹을 것을 생각할 때요. 우리 여정은 길고, 죄다 우리 앞에 놓여 있으니.\""]], [["Interrupting the dialogue by this abrupt transition, the scout had instant recourse to the fragments of food which had escaped the voracity of the Hurons.", "* 핥는 곳: 짐승들이 소금기를 핥으러 모이는 곳. 이 느닷없는 화제 전환으로 대화를 끊으며, 척후는 곧장 〈휴런〉족의 게걸스러운 식욕을 면한 음식 조각들에 손을 댔다."], ["A very summary process completed the simple cookery, when he and the Mohicans commenced their humble meal, with the silence and characteristic diligence of men who ate in order to enable themselves to endure great and unremitting toil.", "매우 간략한 과정으로 그 소박한 요리가 마무리되자, 그와 〈모히칸〉 부자는, 큰 쉼 없는 노고를 견뎌 내기 위해 먹는 사람들 특유의 침묵과 부지런함으로, 그 보잘것없는 식사를 시작했다."]], [["When this necessary, and, happily, grateful duty had been performed, each of the foresters stooped and took a long and parting draught at that solitary and silent spring,[18] around which and its sister fountains, within fifty years, the wealth, beauty, and talents of a hemisphere were to assemble in throngs, in pursuit of health and pleasure.", "이 필요한, 그리고 다행히도 고마운 본분을 다하고 나서, 산림꾼들은 저마다 몸을 굽혀 그 외롭고 고요한 샘에서 길고도 작별의 한 모금을 들이켰으니, 그 샘과 그 자매 샘들 둘레로 오십 년 안에 반구의 부와 미와 재능이, 건강과 즐거움을 좇아 무리 지어 모여들 참이었다."], ["Then Hawkeye announced his determination to proceed.", "이윽고 〈매부리눈〉은 떠나기로 마음먹었음을 알렸다."], ["The sisters resumed their saddles; Duncan and David grasped their rifles, and followed on their footsteps; the scout leading the advance, and the Mohicans bringing up the rear.", "자매는 다시 안장에 올랐고, 〈덩컨〉과 〈데이비드〉는 소총을 거머쥐고 그들의 뒤를 따랐다. 척후가 앞장을 서고, 〈모히칸〉 부자가 후미를 맡았다."], ["The whole party moved swiftly through the narrow path, towards the north, leaving the healing waters to mingle unheeded with the adjacent brook, and the bodies of the dead to fester on the neighboring mount, without the rites of sepulture; a fate but too common to the warriors of the woods to excite either commiseration or comment.", "일행 전부는 좁은 오솔길을 따라 북쪽으로 빠르게 나아가, 그 치유의 물은 곁의 시내와 아무도 모르게 뒤섞이도록, 또 죽은 자들의 시신은 매장의 예도 없이 이웃한 언덕 위에서 썩어 가도록 남겨 두었으니, 그것은 연민도 논평도 자아내기엔 숲의 전사들에게 너무도 흔한 운명이었다."]], [["\"I'll seek a readier path.\"", "「나는 더 빠른 길을 찾으리라.」"]], [["The route taken by Hawkeye lay across those sandy plains, relieved by occasional valleys and swells of land, which had been traversed by their party on the morning of the same day, with the baffled Magua for their guide.", "파넬\n〈호크아이〉가 잡은 경로는, 바로 그날 아침 좌절한 〈마구아〉를 길잡이로 삼아 일행이 지나왔던, 군데군데 골짜기와 둔덕이 들어선 그 모래 평원을 가로질러 뻗어 있었다."], ["The sun had now fallen low towards the distant mountains; and as their journey lay through the interminable forest, the heat was no longer oppressive.", "해는 이제 먼 산들을 향해 낮게 기울었고, 그들의 여정이 끝없는 숲을 통해 이어진 까닭에 더위는 더 이상 견디기 힘들지 않았다."], ["Their progress, in consequence, was proportionate; and long before the twilight gathered about them, they had made good many toilsome miles on their return.", "그리하여 그들의 걸음도 그만큼 빨라졌고, 황혼이 그들을 에워싸기 한참 전에 그들은 돌아오는 길에서 힘겨운 여러 마일을 부지런히 답파했다."]], [["The hunter, like the savage whose place he filled, seemed to select among the blind signs of their wild route, with a species of instinct, seldom abating his speed, and never pausing to deliberate.", "그 자리를 대신한 미개인과 마찬가지로, 사냥꾼은 그들의 거친 경로에 흩어진 희미한 표지들 사이에서 일종의 본능으로 길을 가려내는 듯했으니, 좀처럼 속도를 늦추지 않았고, 망설이느라 멈추는 일은 결코 없었다."], ["A rapid and oblique glance at the moss on the trees, with an occasional upward gaze towards the setting sun, or a steady but passing look at the direction of the numerous water-courses, through which he waded, were sufficient to determine his path, and remove his greatest difficulties.", "나무에 낀 이끼를 향한 빠르고 비스듬한 눈길, 이따금 지는 해를 향해 위로 던지는 시선, 또는 그가 건너 다니던 무수한 물길의 방향을 향해 차분하나 스쳐 지나가는 듯한 응시만으로도, 그의 길을 정하고 그가 맞닥뜨린 가장 큰 난관을 떨쳐 내기에 족했다."], ["In the meantime, the forest began to change its hues, losing that lively green which had embellished its arches, in the graver light which is the usual precursor of the close of day.", "그러는 동안, 숲은 빛깔을 바꾸기 시작하여, 그 아치를 수놓던 생기 띤 초록을 잃고, 흔히 하루의 끝을 앞서 알리는 한층 무거운 빛으로 잠겨 들었다."]], [["While the eyes of the sisters were endeavoring to catch glimpses through the trees, of the flood of golden glory which formed a glittering halo around the sun, tinging here and there with ruby streaks, or bordering with narrow edgings of shining yellow, a mass of clouds that lay piled at no great distance above the western hills, Hawkeye turned suddenly, and, pointing upwards towards the gorgeous heavens, he spoke:--", "자매의 눈이 나무들 사이로, 해를 에워싸 반짝이는 후광을 이루는 황금빛 영광의 홍수를 — 그것은 서쪽 언덕 위 멀지 않은 곳에 쌓여 있던 한 무리의 구름을, 군데군데 루비빛 줄무늬로 물들이거나 빛나는 노란빛의 가느다란 가장자리로 둘러 가며 — 어렴풋이라도 붙잡으려 애쓰는 동안, 〈호크아이〉가 갑자기 몸을 돌려, 화려한 하늘을 향해 위쪽을 가리키며 입을 열었다."]], [["\"Yonder is the signal given to a man to seek his food and natural rest,\"", "\"저것이 사람더러 양식과 마땅한 휴식을 찾으라고 주어진 신호요.\""], ["he said: \"better and wiser would it be, if he could understand the signs of nature, and take a lesson from the fowls of the air and the beasts of the fields!", "그가 말했다. \"사람이 자연의 표지를 읽을 줄 알고, 공중의 새와 들의 짐승에게서 한 수 배울 수만 있다면 더 낫고 더 지혜로울 것이오!"], ["Our night, however, will soon be over; for, with the moon, we must be up and moving again.", "허나 우리의 밤은 이내 끝날 거요. 달이 뜨면 우리는 다시 일어나 움직여야 하니."], ["I remember to have fou't the Maquas, hereaways, in the first war in which I ever drew blood from man; and we threw up a work of blocks, to keep the ravenous varmints from handling our scalps.", "내가 사람의 피를 처음 본 그 첫 전쟁에서, 이 근방에서 〈마쿠아〉족과 싸웠던 게 기억나오. 그때 우리는 그 게걸스러운 짐승 같은 놈들이 우리 머리 가죽에 손대지 못하게 통나무로 방벽을 쌓았소."], ["If my marks do not fail me, we shall find the place a few rods farther to our left.\"", "내 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 우리 왼쪽으로 몇 로드만 더 가면 그곳을 찾을 거요.\""]], [["Without waiting for an assent, or, indeed, for any reply, the sturdy hunter moved boldly into a dense thicket of young chestnuts, shoving aside the branches of the exuberant shoots which nearly covered the ground, like a man who expected, at each step, to discover some object he had formerly known.", "동의를 기다리지도, 사실 그 어떤 대답도 기다리지 않고, 다부진 사냥꾼은 어린 밤나무가 빽빽이 우거진 덤불 속으로 거침없이 들어섰으니, 땅을 거의 다 뒤덮은 무성한 어린 가지들을 헤쳐 가는 그 모습은, 걸음을 옮길 때마다 예전에 알던 어떤 것을 발견하리라 기대하는 사람 같았다."], ["The recollection of the scout did not deceive him.", "척후의 기억은 그를 속이지 않았다."], ["After penetrating through the brush, matted as it was with briers, for a few hundred feet he entered an open space, that surrounded a low, green hillock, which was crowned by the decayed block-house in question.", "가시덤불로 얽힌 그 잡목 숲을 몇백 피트쯤 뚫고 들어가자, 그는 나지막한 푸른 둔덕을 에워싼 트인 공간으로 들어섰고, 그 둔덕 꼭대기에는 바로 그 허물어진 방어용 통나무 진지가 얹혀 있었다."], ["This rude and neglected building was one of those deserted works, which, having been thrown up on an emergency, had been abandoned with the disappearance of danger, and was now quietly crumbling in the solitude of the forest, neglected, and nearly forgotten, like the circumstances which had caused it to be reared.", "거칠고 버려진 이 건물은, 위급할 때 급히 쌓아 올렸다가 위험이 사라지자 버려진 그런 진지들 가운데 하나로, 이제는 숲의 고독 속에서 조용히 무너져 가며, 그것을 세우게 한 사정과 마찬가지로 잊혀 가고 거의 망각되어 있었다."], ["Such memorials of the passage and struggles of man are yet frequent throughout the broad barrier of wilderness which once separated the hostile provinces, and form a species of ruins that are intimately associated with the recollections of colonial history, and which are in appropriate keeping with the gloomy character of the surrounding scenery.[19] The roof of bark had long since fallen, and mingled with the soil; but the huge logs of pine, which had been hastily thrown together, still preserved their relative positions, though one angle of the work had given way under the pressure, and threatened a speedy downfall to the remainder of the rustic edifice.", "인간의 통과와 투쟁을 일러 주는 이런 자취는, 한때 적대하는 두 영토를 갈라놓던 광막한 황야의 경계 곳곳에 아직도 흔하여, 식민지 역사의 기억과 긴밀히 얽힌 일종의 폐허를 이루었으니, 그것은 주위 풍경의 음울한 성격과 어울리게 들어맞았다.[19] 나무껍질로 인 지붕은 오래전에 무너져 흙에 섞여 들었으나, 황급히 쌓아 올린 거대한 소나무 통나무들은 여전히 서로의 자리를 지키고 있었다. 다만 그 진지의 한쪽 모서리가 압력에 못 이겨 무너져, 이 투박한 건물의 나머지 부분도 머잖아 폭삭 주저앉을 듯했다."], ["While Heyward and his companions hesitated to approach a building so decayed, Hawkeye and the Indians entered within the low walls, not only without fear, but with obvious interest.", "〈헤이워드〉와 그 일행이 그토록 썩어 가는 건물에 다가가기를 망설이는 동안, 〈호크아이〉와 인디언들은 두려움 없이, 오히려 뚜렷한 흥미를 띠고 그 낮은 벽 안으로 들어섰다."], ["While the former surveyed the ruins, both internally and externally, with the curiosity of one whose recollections were reviving at each moment, Chingachgook related to his son, in the language of the Delawares, and with the pride of a conqueror, the brief history of the skirmish which had been fought, in his youth, in that secluded spot.", "전자가 순간마다 되살아나는 기억을 지닌 자의 호기심으로 그 폐허의 안팎을 두루 살피는 동안, 〈칭가치국〉은 정복자의 긍지를 담아, 〈델라웨어〉족의 언어로 그의 아들에게, 젊은 시절 이 외딴 자리에서 벌어졌던 전투의 짧은 내력을 들려주었다."], ["A strain of melancholy, however, blended with his triumph, rendering his voice, as usual, soft and musical.", "그러나 그 승리에는 한 가닥 우수가 섞여 들어, 여느 때처럼 그의 목소리를 부드럽고 가락 있게 만들었다."]], [["In the meantime, the sisters gladly dismounted, and prepared to enjoy their halt in the coolness of the evening, and in a security which they believed nothing but the beasts of the forest could invade.", "그러는 동안, 자매는 기꺼이 말에서 내려, 저녁의 서늘함 속에서, 그리고 숲의 짐승 말고는 아무것도 침범할 수 없으리라 믿는 안전 속에서 이 휴식을 누릴 채비를 했다."]], [["\"Would not our resting-place have been more retired, my worthy friend,\"", "\"우리의 쉴 자리가 좀 더 외진 곳이었더라면 좋지 않았겠소, 나의 훌륭한 벗이여,\""], ["demanded the more vigilant Duncan, perceiving that the scout had already finished his short survey, \"had we chosen a spot less known, and one more rarely visited than this?\"", "보다 경계심 깊은 〈덩컨〉이, 척후가 이미 그 짧은 답사를 마친 것을 알아채고 물었다. \"이곳보다 덜 알려지고, 사람의 발길이 더 드문 자리를 골랐더라면 말이오.\""]], [["\"Few live who know the block-house was ever raised,\"", "\"이 진지가 세워진 적이 있다는 걸 아는 사람은 이제 몇 남지 않았소.\" 느릿하고 사색에 잠긴 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["was the slow and musing answer; \"'tis not often that books are made, and narratives written, of such a scrimmage as was here fou't atween the Mohicans and the Mohawks, in a war of their own waging.", "\"여기서 〈모히칸〉족과 〈모호크〉족 사이에, 그들끼리 벌인 전쟁에서 치러진 그런 작은 싸움을 두고 책이 만들어지고 이야기가 쓰이는 일은 흔치 않소. 그때 나는 풋내기였고, 〈델라웨어〉족과 함께 출진했는데, 그들이 모욕당하고 억울한 일을 당한 종족인 줄 알았기 때문이오."], ["I was then a younker, and went out with the Delawares, because I know'd they were a scandalized and wronged race.", "사십 일 낮 사십 일 밤을, 그 악귀 같은 놈들이 내가 설계하고 일부를 직접 쌓은 이 통나무 더미를 에워싸고 우리 피를 갈구했소."], ["Forty days and forty nights did the imps crave our blood around this pile of logs, which I designed and partly reared, being, as you'll remember, no Indian myself, but a man without a cross.", "당신도 기억하다시피, 나 자신은 인디언이 아니라, 피가 섞이지 않은 사람이었지만 말이오. 〈델라웨어〉족이 그 작업에 힘을 보탰고, 우리는 열 대 스물의 형세에서도 그것을 굳건히 지켜, 마침내 양쪽의 수가 거의 같아지자, 우리는 그 사냥개 같은 놈들에게 쳐 나갔고, 그놈들 가운데 단 한 명도 제 패거리의 운명을 전하러 돌아가지 못했소."], ["The Delawares lent themselves to the work, and we made it good, ten to twenty, until our numbers were nearly equal, and then we sallied out upon the hounds, and not a man of them ever got back to tell the fate of his party.", "그렇소, 그렇소. 그때 나는 젊었고, 피를 보는 데 익숙지 않았소."], ["Yes, yes; I was then young, and new to the sight of blood; and not relishing the thought that creatures who had spirits like myself should lay on the naked ground, to be torn asunder by beasts, or to bleach in the rains, I buried the dead with my own hands, under that very little hillock where you have placed yourselves; and no bad seat does it make neither, though it be raised by the bones of mortal men.\"", "그래서 나와 같은 영혼을 지닌 피조물들이 벌거벗은 땅에 누워 짐승들에게 갈가리 찢기거나 빗속에 바래도록 두는 게 영 마뜩잖아, 내 손으로 그 죽은 자들을, 바로 당신들이 앉아 있는 저 작은 둔덕 아래 묻었소. 비록 죽은 인간들의 뼈로 돋워진 것이라 해도, 영 나쁜 자리는 아니지 않소.\""]], [["Heyward and the sisters arose, on the instant, from the grassy sepulchre; nor could the two latter, notwithstanding the terrific scenes they had so recently passed through, entirely suppress an emotion of natural horror, when they found themselves in such familiar contact with the grave of the dead Mohawks.", "〈헤이워드〉와 자매는 그 순간 풀로 덮인 무덤에서 벌떡 일어났다. 그리고 후자의 두 사람은, 그토록 끔찍한 장면들을 바로 얼마 전에 겪고도, 죽은 〈모호크〉족의 무덤과 그토록 가까이 맞닿아 있음을 깨닫자, 본능적인 공포의 감정을 온전히 억누르지 못했다."], ["The gray light, the gloomy little area of dark grass, surrounded by its border of brush, beyond which the pines rose, in breathing silence, apparently, into the very clouds, and the death-like stillness of the vast forest, were all in unison to deepen such a sensation.", "잿빛 빛, 잡목 숲으로 테두리 진 음울하고 자그마한 어두운 풀밭, 그 너머로 숨 쉬는 듯한 정적 속에 솟아 보일 만큼 구름에까지 닿은 소나무들, 그리고 광막한 숲의 죽음 같은 고요 — 이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 그런 감정을 깊게 했다."]], [["\"They are gone, and they are harmless,\"", "\"그들은 갔고, 이제 해를 끼치지 못하오.\""], ["continued Hawkeye, waving his hand, with a melancholy smile, at their manifest alarm: \"they'll never shout the war-whoop nor strike a blow with the tomahawk again!", "그들의 뚜렷한 놀라움을 향해 우수 어린 미소로 손을 내저으며 〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다. \"그들은 다시는 전투의 함성을 지르지도, 토마호크로 일격을 가하지도 못할 거요!"], ["And of all those who aided in placing them where they lie, Chingachgook and I only are living!", "그리고 그들을 지금 누워 있는 그 자리에 눕히는 데 거든 모든 이들 가운데, 〈칭가치국〉과 나만이 살아 있소!"], ["The brothers and family of the Mohican formed our war-party; and you see before you all that are now left of his race.\"", "이 〈모히칸〉의 형제와 가족들이 우리 전투대를 이루었으니, 지금 당신들 앞에 있는 것이 그 종족의 남은 전부요.\""]], [["The eyes of the listeners involuntarily sought the forms of the Indians, with a compassionate interest in their desolate fortune.", "듣는 이들의 눈은 저도 모르게 인디언들의 모습을 좇았으니, 그 황량한 운명에 대한 연민 어린 관심에서였다."], ["The dark persons were still to be seen within the shadows of the block-house, the son listening to the relation of his father with that sort of intenseness which would be created by a narrative that redounded so much to the honor of those whose names he had long revered for their courage and savage virtues.", "그 검은 형상들은 여전히 통나무 진지의 그늘 안에 보였고, 아들은 아버지의 이야기에 귀 기울이고 있었으니, 오래도록 그 용기와 미개의 미덕으로 우러러 온 이들의 이름에 그토록 큰 영예를 더해 주는 이야기가 자아낼 법한 그런 골똘함이었다."]], [["\"I had thought the Delawares a pacific people,\"", "\"나는 〈델라웨어〉족이 평화로운 종족이라, 결코 몸소 전쟁을 벌이지 않고, 자기 땅의 방어를 바로 당신이 죽였다는 그 〈모호크〉족에게 맡긴다고 생각했소만!\""], ["said Duncan, \"and that they never waged war in person; trusting the defence of their lands to those very Mohawks that you slew!\"", "〈덩컨〉이 말했다."]], [["\"'Tis true in part,\"", "\"일부는 사실이오.\""], ["returned the scout, \"and yet, at the bottom, 'tis a wicked lie.", "척후가 답했다. \"허나 근본을 따지면 그건 사악한 거짓말이오."], ["Such a treaty was made in ages gone by, through the deviltries of the Dutchers, who wished to disarm the natives that had the best right to the country where they had settled themselves.", "그런 협정은 오래전, 자기들이 자리 잡은 땅에 가장 정당한 권리를 가진 원주민들의 무장을 해제시키려 한 네덜란드인들의 농간으로 맺어진 것이오."], ["The Mohicans, though a part of the same nation, having to deal with the English, never entered into the silly bargain, but kept to their manhood; as in truth did the Delawares, when their eyes were opened to their folly.", "〈모히칸〉족은, 같은 민족의 한 갈래였으되, 〈영국〉인을 상대해야 했던 까닭에, 그 어리석은 거래에 결코 발을 들이지 않고 자신들의 사내다움을 지켰소. 사실, 〈델라웨어〉족도 제 어리석음에 눈을 뜨고 나서는 그러했지."], ["You see before you a chief of the great Mohican Sagamores!", "당신들 앞에 있는 이가 바로 위대한 〈모히칸〉의 추장 가문 출신의 한 우두머리요!"], ["Once his family could chase their deer over tracts of country wider than that which belongs to the Albany Patteroon, without crossing brook or hill that was not their own; but what is left to their descendant!", "한때 그의 가족은 〈올버니〉 영주의 영지보다 더 넓은 땅을 가로질러, 제 것이 아닌 시내나 언덕을 건너는 일 없이 사슴을 몰 수 있었소. 헌데 그 후손에게 남은 것이 무엇이오!"], ["He may find his six feet of earth when God chooses, and keep it in peace, perhaps, if he has a friend who will take the pains to sink his head so low that the ploughshares cannot reach it!\"", "그는 하느님이 정하실 때 여섯 자의 흙을 얻을 수 있을 것이고, 어쩌면 평화로이 그것을 지킬 수도 있겠지. 보습이 닿지 못할 만큼 그의 머리를 깊이 묻어 줄 수고를 마다하지 않는 벗이 그에게 있다면 말이오!\""]], [["\"Enough!\"", "\"그만하시오!\""], ["said Heyward, apprehensive that the subject might lead to a discussion that would interrupt the harmony so necessary to the preservation of his fair companions: \"we have journeyed far, and few among us are blessed with forms like that of yours, which seems to know neither fatigue nor weakness.\"", "이 화제가 어여쁜 동행들의 안녕을 지키는 데 그토록 긴요한 화목을 깨뜨릴 논쟁으로 이어질까 염려한 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"우리는 멀리 왔고, 우리 가운데 피로도 약함도 모르는 듯한 당신 같은 체격을 타고난 이는 몇 안 되오.\""]], [["\"The sinews and bones of a man carry me through it all,\"", "\"사람의 힘줄과 뼈가 나를 이 모든 것을 헤쳐 나가게 해 주오.\""], ["said the hunter, surveying his muscular limbs with a simplicity that betrayed the honest pleasure the compliment afforded him: \"there are larger and heavier men to be found in the settlements, but you might travel many days in a city before you could meet one able to walk fifty miles without stopping to take breath, or who has kept the hounds within hearing during a chase of hours.", "그 칭찬이 안긴 정직한 기쁨을 드러내는 소박함으로 제 근육진 사지를 살피며 사냥꾼이 말했다. \"정착지에 가면 더 크고 더 무거운 사내들도 있겠소만, 한 도시를 여러 날 돌아다녀도 숨 한 번 고르려 멈추지 않고 오십 마일을 걸을 수 있는 자나, 몇 시간에 걸친 사냥에서 사냥개를 들리는 거리 안에 잡아 둔 자는 좀처럼 만나지 못할 거요."], ["However, as flesh and blood are not always the same, it is quite reasonable to suppose that the gentle ones are willing to rest, after all they have seen and done this day.", "허나 피와 살이 늘 한결같지는 않으니, 저 점잖은 분들이 오늘 하루 보고 겪은 그 모든 일 끝에 쉬고 싶어 하는 것도 지극히 당연하오. 〈언커스〉야, 샘을 치우거라."], ["Uncas, clear out the spring, while your father and I make a cover for their tender heads of these chestnut shoots, and a bed of grass and leaves.\"", "네 아비와 내가 이 밤나무 어린 가지로 저 연약한 머리들을 가릴 지붕을 만들고, 풀과 잎으로 잠자리를 마련하마.\""]], [["The dialogue ceased, while the hunter and his companions busied themselves in preparations for the comfort and protection of those they guided.", "사냥꾼과 그 일행이 자신들이 인도하는 이들의 안락과 보호를 위한 채비에 분주한 동안, 대화는 그쳤다."], ["A spring, which many long years before had induced the natives to select the place for their temporary fortification, was soon cleared of leaves, and a fountain of crystal gushed from the bed, diffusing its waters over the verdant hillock.", "오래전 원주민들이 임시 요새 자리로 이곳을 고르게 한 한 샘이 이내 잎사귀가 치워지자, 수정 같은 샘물이 바닥에서 솟아 나와, 그 물을 푸른 둔덕 위로 퍼뜨렸다."], ["A corner of the building was then roofed in such a manner as to exclude the heavy dew of the climate, and piles of sweet shrubs and dried leaves were laid beneath it for the sisters to repose on.", "이어 건물의 한 모퉁이가, 이 고장의 무거운 이슬을 막아 낼 수 있도록 지붕이 덮였고, 그 아래에는 자매가 쉴 수 있도록 향기로운 관목과 마른 잎이 수북이 깔렸다."]], [["While the diligent woodsmen were employed in this manner, Cora and Alice partook of that refreshment which duty required much more than inclination prompted them to accept.", "부지런한 숲의 사내들이 이처럼 일하는 동안, 〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉는 마음이 내켜서라기보다 의무가 한층 더 요구하는 그 음식을 들었다."], ["They then retired within the walls, and first offering up their thanksgivings for past mercies, and petitioning for a continuance of the divine favor throughout the coming night, they laid their tender forms on the fragrant couch, and in spite of recollections and forebodings, soon sank into those slumbers which nature so imperiously demanded, and which were sweetened by hopes for the morrow.", "그러고 나서 그들은 벽 안으로 물러나, 먼저 지난 자비에 대한 감사를 올리고 다가오는 밤 내내 신의 은총이 이어지기를 청한 다음, 그 연약한 몸을 향기로운 잠자리에 뉘었으니, 기억과 불길한 예감에도 불구하고, 자연이 그토록 준엄하게 요구하던, 그리고 내일에 대한 희망으로 달콤해진 그 잠 속으로 이내 빠져들었다."], ["Duncan had prepared himself to pass the night in watchfulness near them, just without the ruin, but the scout, perceiving his intention, pointed towards Chingachgook, as he coolly disposed his own person on the grass, and said--", "〈덩컨〉은 폐허 바로 바깥, 그들 가까이에서 밤을 새워 망을 볼 채비를 했으나, 그 뜻을 알아챈 척후는 풀밭에 제 몸을 태연히 누이며 〈칭가치국〉을 가리켰고, 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"The eyes of a white man are too heavy and too blind for such a watch as this!", "\"백인의 눈은 이런 망을 보기엔 너무 무겁고 너무 멀었소!"], ["The Mohican will be our sentinel, therefore let us sleep.\"", "〈모히칸〉이 우리의 보초가 될 테니, 그러니 우리는 잡시다.\""]], [["\"I proved myself a sluggard on my post during the past night,\"", "\"나는 지난밤 내 초소에서 게으름뱅이임을 스스로 입증했소.\""], ["said Heyward, \"and have less need of repose than you, who did more credit to the character of a soldier.", "〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"그리고 병사의 본분에 더 어울리게 처신한 당신보다 내가 쉴 필요가 덜하오."], ["Let all the party seek their rest, then, while I hold guard.\"", "그러니 일행 모두가 쉬도록 하고, 내가 망을 보겠소.\""]], [["\"If we lay among the white tents of the 60th, and in front of an enemy like the French, I could not ask for a better watchman,\"", "\"우리가 제60연대의 흰 천막들 사이, 그리고 〈프랑스〉군 같은 적 앞에 누워 있다면, 나도 더 나은 파수꾼을 바라지 않겠소.\""], ["returned the scout; \"but in the darkness and among the signs of the wilderness your judgment would be like the folly of a child, and your vigilance thrown away.", "척후가 답했다. \"허나 어둠 속, 황야의 표지들 사이에서 당신의 판단은 어린아이의 어리석음과 같을 것이고, 당신의 경계는 헛수고가 될 거요."], ["Do then, like Uncas and myself, sleep, and sleep in safety.\"", "그러니 〈언커스〉와 나처럼, 자시오. 그리고 안심하고 자시오.\""]], [["Heyward perceived, in truth, that the younger Indian had thrown his form on the side of the hillock while they were talking, like one who sought to make the most of the time allotted to rest, and that his example had been followed by David, whose voice literally \"clove to his jaws,\" with the fever of his wound, heightened, as it was, by their toilsome march.", "〈헤이워드〉는, 그들이 이야기하는 동안 젊은 인디언이 마치 쉬도록 주어진 시간을 최대한 누리려는 사람처럼 둔덕 비탈에 제 몸을 던져 누운 것을, 그리고 그 본보기를 〈데이비드〉가 따른 것을 사실로 알아챘으니, 〈데이비드〉의 목소리는 그 고된 행군으로 한층 심해진 상처의 열로 인해 말 그대로 \"그의 턱에 들러붙어\""], ["Unwilling to prolong a useless discussion, the young man affected to comply, by posting his back against the logs of the block-house, in a half-recumbent posture, though resolutely determined, in his own mind, not to close an eye until he had delivered his precious charge into the arms of Munro himself.", "있었다. 쓸데없는 논쟁을 끌고 싶지 않아, 젊은이는 통나무 진지의 통나무에 등을 기대고 반쯤 기댄 자세를 취하며 따르는 시늉을 했으나, 그 소중한 짐을 〈먼로〉 본인의 품에 넘겨주기 전에는 한쪽 눈도 감지 않으리라 마음속으로 단단히 결심하고 있었다."], ["Hawkeye, believing he had prevailed, soon fell asleep, and a silence as deep as the solitude in which they had found it, pervaded the retired spot.", "자신이 뜻을 관철했다고 믿은 〈호크아이〉는 이내 잠이 들었고, 그들이 처음 그것을 발견했을 때의 고독만큼이나 깊은 정적이 그 외딴 자리를 뒤덮었다."]], [["For many minutes Duncan succeeded in keeping his senses on the alert, and alive to every moaning sound that arose from the forest.", "여러 분 동안 〈덩컨〉은 제 감각을 곤두세운 채로, 숲에서 일어나는 신음 같은 온갖 소리에 깨어 있도록 용케 버텼다."], ["His vision became more acute as the shades of evening settled on the place; and even after the stars were glimmering above his head, he was able to distinguish the recumbent forms of his companions, as they lay stretched on the grass, and to note the person of Chingachgook, who sat upright and motionless as one of the trees which formed the dark barrier on every side.", "저녁 그늘이 그곳에 내려앉자 그의 시야는 한층 예민해졌고, 별들이 그의 머리 위에서 반짝이기 시작한 뒤에도, 그는 풀밭에 길게 누운 동행들의 모습을 분간할 수 있었고, 사방의 어두운 장벽을 이룬 나무들 가운데 하나처럼 꼿꼿이 미동도 없이 앉은 〈칭가치국〉의 모습도 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["He still heard the gentle breathings of the sisters, who lay within a few feet of him, and not a leaf was ruffled by the passing air, of which his ear did not detect the whispering sound.", "그는 여전히 몇 피트 떨어진 곳에 누운 자매의 잔잔한 숨소리를 들었고, 스치는 바람에 잎사귀 하나가 흔들려도 그의 귀가 그 속삭이는 소리를 놓치는 법이 없었다."], ["At length, however, the mournful notes of a whippoorwill became blended with the moanings of an owl; his heavy eyes occasionally sought the bright rays of the stars, and then he fancied he saw them through the fallen lids.", "그러나 마침내, 쏙독새의 구슬픈 가락이 올빼미의 신음과 뒤섞였다. 무거운 그의 눈은 이따금 별들의 밝은 빛줄기를 좇았고, 그러다 그는 감긴 눈꺼풀 너머로 그것들이 보이는 듯한 착각에 빠졌다."], ["At instants of momentary wakefulness he mistook a bush for his associate sentinel; his head next sank upon his shoulder, which, in its turn, sought the support of the ground; and, finally, his whole person become relaxed and pliant, and the young man sank into a deep sleep, dreaming that he was a knight of ancient chivalry, holding his midnight vigils before the tent of a recaptured princess, whose favor he did not despair of gaining, by such a proof of devotion and watchfulness.", "잠깐씩 깨어나는 순간이면 그는 덤불을 동료 보초로 착각했다. 이어 그의 머리는 어깨 위로 떨어졌고, 그 어깨는 또 땅의 받침을 찾았으며, 마침내 그의 온몸이 노곤하게 풀려, 젊은이는 깊은 잠 속으로 가라앉아, 자신이 고대 기사도의 한 기사가 되어, 되찾은 어느 공주의 천막 앞에서 한밤의 망을 보는 꿈을 꾸었으니, 그 헌신과 경계의 증표로써 그녀의 총애를 얻기를 단념하지 않은 기사였다."]], [["How long the tired Duncan lay in this insensible state he never knew himself, but his slumbering visions had been long lost in total forgetfulness, when he was awakened by a light tap on the shoulder.", "지친 〈덩컨〉이 이 무감각한 상태로 얼마나 오래 누워 있었는지 그 자신도 결코 알지 못했으나, 그의 잠든 환상이 완전한 망각 속으로 오래 사라졌을 즈음, 그는 어깨를 가볍게 두드리는 손길에 깨어났다."], ["Aroused by this signal, slight as it was, he sprang upon his feet with a confused recollection of the self-imposed duty he had assumed with the commencement of the night.", "아무리 미약했어도 이 신호에 깨어난 그는, 밤이 시작될 때 스스로 떠맡은 임무에 대한 어렴풋한 기억과 함께 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["\"Who comes?\"", "\"누구냐?\""], ["he demanded, feeling for his sword at the place where it was usually suspended, \"Speak!", "칼이 늘 매여 있던 자리를 더듬으며 그가 다그쳤다. \"말하라!"], ["friend or enemy?\"", "벗이냐, 적이냐?\""]], [["\"Friend,\" replied the low voice of Chingachgook; who, pointing upwards at the luminary which was shedding its mild light through the opening in the trees, directly in their bivouac, immediately added, in his rude English, \"moon comes, and white man's fort far--far off; time to move, when sleep shuts both eyes of the Frenchman!\"", "\"벗이오.\" 〈칭가치국〉의 나직한 목소리가 답했다. 그는 나무 사이의 트인 틈으로 그들의 야영지에 바로 부드러운 빛을 쏟아붓는 천체를 위로 가리키며, 곧바로 그의 투박한 〈영어〉로 덧붙였다. \"달이 떴고, 백인의 요새는 멀리—멀리 있소. 〈프랑스〉인의 두 눈이 잠으로 닫혔을 때가 움직일 때요!\""]], [["\"You say true!", "\"옳은 말이오!"], ["call up your friends, and bridle the horses, while I prepare my own companions for the march!\"", "당신의 벗들을 깨우고 말들에 굴레를 채우시오. 그동안 나는 내 동행들에게 행군 채비를 시키겠소!\""]], [["\"We are awake, Duncan,\"", "\"우리는 깨어 있어요, 〈덩컨〉.\" 건물 안에서 〈앨리스〉의 부드럽고 은빛 같은 목소리가 말했다."], ["said the soft, silvery tones of Alice within the building, \"and ready to travel very fast after so refreshing a sleep; but you have watched through the tedious night in our behalf, after having endured so much fatigue the live-long day!\"", "\"그토록 상쾌한 잠을 잤으니 아주 빨리 갈 채비가 되었답니다. 하지만 당신은 온종일 그렇게 큰 피로를 견디고도, 우리를 위해 그 지루한 밤을 새워 망을 보았군요!\""]], [["\"Say, rather, I would have watched, but my treacherous eyes betrayed me; twice have I proved myself unfit for the trust I bear.\"", "\"차라리 망을 보려 했다고 해 두시오. 헌데 내 못 믿을 눈이 나를 배신했소. 두 번이나 나는 내가 맡은 소임에 부적합한 자임을 입증하고 말았소.\""]], [["\"Nay, Duncan, deny it not,\"", "\"아니에요, 〈덩컨〉, 그것을 부인하지 마세요.\""], ["interrupted the smiling Alice, issuing from the shadows of the building into the light of the moon, in all the loveliness of her freshened beauty; \"I know you to be a heedless one, when self is the object of your care, and but too vigilant in favor of others.", "미소 띤 〈앨리스〉가, 새로워진 아름다움의 온갖 사랑스러움을 띠고 건물의 그늘에서 달빛 속으로 나서며 가로막았다. \"나는 당신이 자신을 돌보아야 할 때면 부주의한 사람이지만, 남을 위할 때면 지나치리만치 경계하는 사람임을 알아요."], ["Can we not tarry here a little longer, while you find the rest you need?", "당신이 필요한 휴식을 취하는 동안 우리가 여기 좀 더 머물 수는 없을까요?"], ["Cheerfully, most cheerfully, will Cora and I keep the vigils, while you, and all these brave men, endeavor to snatch a little sleep!\"", "〈코라〉와 내가 기꺼이, 더없이 기꺼이 망을 볼게요. 그동안 당신과 이 모든 용감한 분들이 잠깐이라도 잠을 청하시도록 말이에요!\""]], [["\"If shame could cure me of my drowsiness, I should never close an eye again,\"", "\"수치심이 내 졸음을 고쳐 줄 수 있다면, 나는 다시는 한쪽 눈도 감지 않을 거요.\""], ["said the uneasy youth, gazing at the ingenuous countenance of Alice, where, however, in its sweet solicitude, he read nothing to confirm his half awakened suspicion.", "불안한 젊은이가 〈앨리스〉의 진실한 얼굴을 바라보며 말했으나, 그 다정한 염려 속에서 그는 반쯤 깨어난 제 의심을 굳혀 줄 그 무엇도 읽어 내지 못했다."], ["\"It is but too true, that after leading you into danger by my heedlessness, I have not even the merit of guarding your pillows as should become a soldier.\"", "\"내 부주의로 당신들을 위험에 끌어들이고도, 병사답게 당신들의 베갯머리를 지키는 공조차 세우지 못했다는 것은 너무도 분명한 사실이오.\""]], [["\"No one but Duncan himself should accuse Duncan of such a weakness.", "\"〈덩컨〉을 그런 약함으로 탓할 수 있는 사람은 〈덩컨〉 자신밖에 없어요."], ["Go, then, and sleep; believe me, neither of us, weak girls as we are, will betray our watch.\"", "그러니 가서 주무세요. 믿어 주세요, 연약한 처녀들인 우리지만, 둘 중 누구도 우리의 망을 저버리지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["The young man was relieved from the awkwardness of making any further protestations of his own demerits, by an exclamation from Chingachgook, and the attitude of riveted attention assumed by his son.", "자기 결함을 더 늘어놓아야 하는 어색함에서 젊은이가 벗어난 것은, 〈칭가치국〉이 내뱉은 외침과, 그의 아들이 취한, 한 점에 못 박힌 듯한 주의 깊은 자세 덕분이었다."]], [["\"The Mohicans hear an enemy!\"", "\"〈모히칸〉족이 적의 기척을 듣소!\""], ["whispered Hawkeye, who, by this time, in common with the whole party, was awake and stirring.", "〈호크아이〉가 속삭였으니, 이때쯤이면 그는 일행 전체와 마찬가지로 깨어나 움직이고 있었다."], ["\"They scent danger in the wind!\"", "\"그들이 바람결에 위험을 맡소!\""]], [["\"God forbid!\"", "\"제발 그러지 않기를!\""], ["exclaimed Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤다."], ["\"Surely we have had enough of bloodshed!\"", "\"우리는 분명 유혈을 충분히 겪었소!\""]], [["While he spoke, however, the young soldier seized his rifle, and advancing towards the front, prepared to atone for his venial remissness, by freely exposing his life in defence of those he attended.", "그러나 그가 말하는 동안에도, 젊은 군인은 제 소총을 움켜쥐고 앞쪽으로 나아가며, 자신이 보살피는 이들을 지키고자 거리낌 없이 목숨을 내놓음으로써, 그 용서받을 만한 태만을 속죄할 채비를 했다."]], [["\"'Tis some creature of the forest prowling around us in quest of food,\"", "\"먹이를 찾아 우리 주위를 어슬렁대는 숲의 어떤 짐승이오.\""], ["he said, in a whisper, as soon as the low, and apparently distant sounds, which had startled the Mohicans, reached his own ears.", "〈모히칸〉족을 놀라게 한 그 낮고, 얼핏 멀어 보이는 소리가 그 자신의 귀에 닿자마자 그가 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["\"Hist!\"", "\"쉿!\""], ["returned the attentive scout; \"'tis man; even I can now tell his tread, poor as my senses are when compared to an Indian's!", "주의 깊은 척후가 답했다. \"사람이오."], ["That scampering Huron has fallen in with one of Montcalm's outlying parties, and they have struck upon our trail.", "인디언에 비하면 내 감각이 보잘것없다 해도, 이제 나조차 그 발걸음을 알아차릴 수 있소! 그 달아난 〈휴런〉족 놈이 〈몽칼름〉의 정찰대 하나와 마주쳐, 그들이 우리 자취를 짚어 낸 거요."], ["I shouldn't like, myself, to spill more human blood in this spot,\"", "나 자신도 이 자리에서 더 이상 사람의 피를 흘리고 싶지는 않소만,\""], ["he added, looking around with anxiety in his features, at the dim objects by which he was surrounded; \"but what must be, must!", "그가 제 주위를 에워싼 흐릿한 형체들을 근심 어린 표정으로 둘러보며 덧붙였다. \"허나 일어날 일은 일어나야 하는 법!"], ["Lead the horses into the block-house, Uncas; and, friends, do you follow to the same shelter.", "말들을 진지 안으로 끌어들여라, 〈언커스〉. 그리고 벗들이여, 당신들도 같은 은신처로 따라 들어오시오."], ["Poor and old as it is, it offers a cover, and has rung with the crack of a rifle afore to-night!\"", "비록 낡고 오래되었어도, 그것은 몸을 가릴 데가 되고, 오늘 밤 전에도 소총 소리에 울린 적이 있는 곳이오!\""]], [["He was instantly obeyed, the Mohicans leading the Narragansetts within the ruin, whither the whole party repaired with the most guarded silence.", "그 명은 곧바로 받들어졌으니, 〈모히칸〉족이 〈내러갠셋〉종 말들을 폐허 안으로 이끌었고, 일행 전체가 더없이 조심스러운 침묵 속에 그곳으로 들었다."]], [["The sounds of approaching footsteps were now too distinctly audible to leave any doubts as to the nature of the interruption.", "다가오는 발소리가 이제는 너무도 또렷이 들려, 이 방해가 어떤 성격의 것인지 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["They were soon mingled with voices calling to each other in an Indian dialect, which the hunter, in a whisper, affirmed to Heyward was the language of the Hurons.", "그 소리는 이내, 어떤 인디언 방언으로 서로를 부르는 목소리들과 뒤섞였으니, 사냥꾼이 〈헤이워드〉에게 속삭여 일러 준 바로는 그것이 〈휴런〉족의 언어였다."], ["When the party reached the point where the horses had entered the thicket which surrounded the block-house, they were evidently at fault, having lost those marks which, until that moment, had directed their pursuit.", "그 무리가, 진지를 에워싼 덤불 속으로 말들이 들어선 지점에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 분명 갈피를 잃은 듯했으니, 그 순간까지 그들의 추적을 이끌어 온 표지들을 놓쳐 버린 것이었다."]], [["It would seem by the voices that twenty men were soon collected at that one spot, mingling their different opinions and advice in noisy clamor.", "목소리로 미루어 이내 스무 명쯤 되는 사내들이 그 한 자리에 모여, 저마다 다른 의견과 조언을 시끄럽게 떠들어 대며 뒤섞는 듯했다."]], [["\"The knaves know our weakness,\"", "\"이 악당들은 우리가 약한 줄 아는 거요.\""], ["whispered Hawkeye, who stood by the side of Heyward, in deep shade, looking through an opening in the logs, \"or they wouldn't indulge their idleness in such a squaw's march.", "깊은 그늘 속 〈헤이워드〉의 곁에 서서, 통나무 사이의 트인 틈으로 내다보며 〈호크아이〉가 속삭였다. \"그렇지 않고서야 저런 아낙네 같은 굼뜬 행군에 게으름을 부릴 리가 없지."], ["Listen to the reptiles!", "저 파충류 같은 놈들 소리를 들어 보시오!"], ["each man among them seems to have two tongues, and but a single leg.\"", "놈들은 저마다 혀는 둘이고 다리는 하나뿐인 모양이오.\""]], [["Duncan, brave as he was in the combat, could not, in such a moment of painful suspense, make any reply to the cool and characteristic remark of the scout.", "전투에서는 그토록 용감하던 〈덩컨〉도, 그토록 괴로운 긴장의 순간에는, 척후의 그 태연하고 특유한 평에 아무 대꾸도 할 수 없었다."], ["He only grasped his rifle more firmly, and fastened his eyes upon the narrow opening, through which he gazed upon the moonlight view with increasing anxiety.", "그는 그저 제 소총을 더 단단히 움켜쥐고, 점점 커지는 불안 속에 달빛 어린 광경을 내다보던 그 좁은 틈에 눈을 박았다."], ["The deeper tones of one who spoke as having authority were next heard, amid a silence that denoted the respect with which his orders, or rather advice, was received.", "이어, 권위를 지닌 자처럼 말하는 누군가의 한층 묵직한 목소리가, 그의 명령이, 아니 차라리 조언이 받아들여지는 경의를 드러내는 침묵 가운데 들려왔다."], ["After which, by the rustling of leaves, and cracking of dried twigs, it was apparent the savages were separating in pursuit of the lost trail.", "그런 다음, 잎사귀가 바스락대고 마른 잔가지가 부러지는 소리로 보아, 그 미개인들이 잃어버린 자취를 좇아 흩어지고 있음이 분명했다."], ["Fortunately for the pursued, the light of the moon, while it shed a flood of mild lustre upon the little area around the ruin, was not sufficiently strong to penetrate the deep arches of the forest, where the objects still lay in deceptive shadow.", "쫓기는 이들에게 다행스럽게도, 달빛은 폐허 둘레의 자그마한 공터에 부드러운 광채의 홍수를 쏟아부으면서도, 형체들이 여전히 사람을 속이는 그림자 속에 누워 있는 숲의 깊은 아치까지 뚫고 들 만큼 강하지는 않았다."], ["The search proved fruitless; for so short and sudden had been the passage from the faint path the travellers had journeyed into the thicket, that every trace of their footsteps was lost in the obscurity of the woods.", "수색은 헛수고로 드러났으니, 여행자들이 지나온 희미한 오솔길에서 덤불 속으로 들어선 그 통과가 어찌나 짧고 갑작스러웠던지, 그들 발자취의 모든 흔적이 숲의 어둠 속에 사라져 버린 까닭이었다."]], [["It was not long, however, before the restless savages were heard beating the brush, and gradually approaching the inner edge of that dense border of young chestnuts which encircled the little area.", "그러나 그 쉴 줄 모르는 미개인들이 덤불을 두드리며, 그 자그마한 공터를 에워싼 빽빽한 어린 밤나무 테두리의 안쪽 가장자리로 차츰 다가오는 소리가 들리기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다."]], [["\"They are coming,\"", "\"놈들이 오고 있소.\""], ["muttered Heyward, endeavoring to thrust his rifle through the chink in the logs; \"let us fire on their approach.\"", "통나무 틈으로 제 소총을 들이밀려 애쓰며 〈헤이워드〉가 중얼거렸다. \"다가오면 쏩시다.\""]], [["\"Keep everything in the shade,\"", "\"모든 것을 그늘 속에 두시오.\""], ["returned the scout; \"the snapping of a flint, or even the smell of a single karnel of the brimstone, would bring the hungry varlets upon us in a body.", "척후가 답했다. \"부싯돌 튀는 소리, 아니 유황 알갱이 하나의 냄새만으로도, 굶주린 그 악당들이 떼로 우리에게 덮쳐들 거요."], ["Should it please God that we must give battle for the scalps, trust to the experience of men who know the ways of the savages, and who are not often backward when the war-whoop is howled.\"", "우리가 머리 가죽을 걸고 싸워야 하는 것이 하느님의 뜻이라면, 미개인의 방식을 아는, 그리고 전투의 함성이 울릴 때 좀처럼 뒤로 물러서지 않는 사내들의 경험에 맡기시오.\""]], [["Duncan cast his eyes behind him, and saw that the trembling sisters were cowering in the far corner of the building, while the Mohicans stood in the shadow, like two upright posts, ready, and apparently willing, to strike when the blow should be needed.", "〈덩컨〉이 등 뒤를 돌아보니, 떨고 있는 자매가 건물의 저 먼 구석에 웅크려 있는 것이 보였고, 〈모히칸〉족은 그늘 속에 마치 곧추선 두 개의 기둥처럼 서서, 일격이 필요할 때 칠 채비를 갖춘 채, 또 그러기를 분명 마다하지 않는 모습이었다."], ["Curbing his impatience, he again looked out upon the area, and awaited the result in silence.", "조바심을 억누르며 그는 다시 공터를 내다보았고, 침묵 속에 결과를 기다렸다."], ["At that instant the thicket opened, and a tall and armed Huron advanced a few paces into the open space.", "그 찰나, 덤불이 갈라지며 키 크고 무장한 〈휴런〉족 하나가 트인 공간으로 몇 걸음 나섰다."], ["As he gazed upon the silent block-house, the moon fell upon his swarthy countenance, and betrayed its surprise and curiosity.", "그가 고요한 진지를 바라볼 때, 달빛이 그의 거무스레한 얼굴 위로 떨어져, 그 놀라움과 호기심을 고스란히 드러냈다."], ["He made the exclamation which usually accompanies the former emotion in an Indian, and, calling in a low voice, soon drew a companion to his side.", "그는 인디언에게서 전자의 감정에 으레 따르는 외침을 내뱉고는, 나직한 목소리로 불러 이내 한 동료를 제 곁으로 끌어들였다."]], [["These children of the woods stood together for several moments pointing at the crumbling edifice, and conversing in the unintelligible language of their tribe.", "이 숲의 자식들은 여러 순간 함께 서서, 허물어져 가는 건물을 가리키며 그들 부족의 알 수 없는 언어로 이야기를 나누었다."], ["They then approached, though with slow and cautious steps, pausing every instant to look at the building, like startled deer, whose curiosity struggled powerfully with their awakened apprehensions for the mastery.", "그런 다음, 느리고 조심스러운 걸음으로나마 다가오며, 순간마다 멈춰 서서 그 건물을 바라보았으니, 그 모습은 호기심이 깨어난 두려움과 거세게 우열을 다투는, 놀란 사슴 같았다."], ["The foot of one of them suddenly rested on the mound, and he stooped to examine its nature.", "그중 하나의 발이 문득 둔덕 위에 닿자, 그는 그것이 무엇인지 살피려 몸을 굽혔다."], ["At this moment, Heyward observed that the scout loosened his knife in his sheath, and lowered the muzzle of his rifle.", "이 순간, 〈헤이워드〉는 척후가 칼집 속의 칼을 헐겁게 하고 제 소총 총구를 낮추는 것을 보았다."], ["Imitating these movements, the young man prepared himself for the struggle, which now seemed inevitable.", "이 동작들을 흉내 내며, 젊은이도 이제 피할 수 없어 보이는 그 싸움에 대비했다."]], [["The savages were so near, that the least motion in one of the horses, or even a breath louder than common, would have betrayed the fugitives.", "미개인들이 어찌나 가까웠던지, 말 한 마리의 조그만 움직임이나, 평소보다 약간 큰 숨소리 하나만으로도 도망자들의 정체가 드러났을 것이다."], ["But, in discovering the character of the mound, the attention of the Hurons appeared directed to a different object.", "그러나 그 둔덕의 정체를 알아채자, 〈휴런〉족의 주의는 다른 대상으로 쏠리는 듯했다."], ["They spoke together, and the sounds of their voices were low and solemn, as if influenced by a reverence that was deeply blended with awe.", "그들은 함께 이야기했고, 그 목소리는 깊은 외경이 짙게 어린 경의에 사로잡힌 듯 나직하고 엄숙했다."], ["Then they drew warily back, keeping their eyes riveted on the ruin, as if they expected to see the apparitions of the dead issue from its silent walls, until having reached the boundary of the area, they moved slowly into the thicket, and disappeared.", "그런 다음 그들은, 마치 그 고요한 벽에서 죽은 자들의 망령이 나오기라도 할 듯 두 눈을 폐허에 박은 채, 조심스레 뒷걸음질 쳤고, 공터의 경계에 이르자 천천히 덤불 속으로 들어가 사라졌다."]], [["Hawkeye dropped the breech of his rifle to the earth, and drawing a long, free breath, exclaimed, in an audible whisper,--", "〈호크아이〉가 소총의 개머리판을 땅에 떨구고, 길고 자유로운 숨을 내쉬며, 들릴 만한 속삭임으로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Ay!", "\"옳거니!"], ["they respect the dead, and it has this time saved their own lives, and, it may be, the lives of better men too.\"", "놈들이 죽은 자들을 경외하는구먼. 그게 이번엔 제 목숨을 구했고, 어쩌면 더 나은 사내들의 목숨까지 구한 셈이오.\""]], [["Heyward lent his attention for a single moment to his companion, but without replying, he again turned towards those who just then interested him more.", "〈헤이워드〉는 한순간 동행에게 주의를 기울였으나, 대꾸하지 않고 다시, 바로 그때 그의 관심을 더 끄는 이들에게로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["He heard the two Hurons leave the bushes, and it was soon plain that all the pursuers were gathered about them, in deep attention to their report.", "그는 두 〈휴런〉족이 덤불을 떠나는 소리를 들었고, 이내 추격자들 모두가 그들 둘레에 모여, 그 보고에 깊이 주의를 기울이고 있음이 분명해졌다."], ["After a few minutes of earnest and solemn dialogue, altogether different from the noisy clamor with which they had first collected about the spot, the sounds grew fainter and more distant, and finally were lost in the depths of the forest.", "처음 그 자리에 모여들 때의 시끄러운 떠들썩함과는 전혀 다른, 몇 분간의 진지하고 엄숙한 대화 끝에, 그 소리는 점점 희미해지고 멀어지더니, 마침내 숲의 깊은 곳에서 사라졌다."]], [["Hawkeye waited until a signal from the listening Chingachgook assured him that every sound from the retiring party was completely swallowed by the distance, when he motioned to Heyward to lead forth the horses, and to assist the sisters into their saddles.", "〈호크아이〉는, 귀 기울여 듣던 〈칭가치국〉이 보낸 신호가, 물러가는 무리에게서 나는 모든 소리가 거리 속에 완전히 삼켜졌음을 그에게 확인해 줄 때까지 기다렸다가, 〈헤이워드〉에게 말들을 끌어내고 자매가 안장에 오르도록 거들라고 손짓했다."], ["The instant this was done, they issued through the broken gateway, and stealing out by a direction opposite to the one by which they had entered, they quitted the spot, the sisters casting furtive glances at the silent grave and crumbling ruin, as they left the soft light of the moon, to bury themselves in the gloom of the woods.", "그 일이 끝나는 즉시, 그들은 부서진 출입구를 통해 나가, 들어왔던 방향과 반대 방향으로 몰래 빠져나가며 그 자리를 떠났으니, 자매는 부드러운 달빛을 뒤로하고 숲의 음울함 속에 몸을 묻으며, 그 고요한 무덤과 허물어져 가는 폐허를 향해 슬그머니 흘끔거렸다."]], [["\"_Guard._--Qui est la?", "\"보초.—누구냐?"]], [["_Puc._--Paisans, pauvres gens de France.\"", "퓌셀.—농부요, 프랑스의 가난한 백성이라오.\""]], [["_King Henry VI._", "「헨리 6세」"]], [["During the rapid movement from the block-house, and until the party was deeply buried in the forest, each individual was too much interested in the escape to hazard a word even in whispers.", "요새 건물에서 황급히 빠져나와 일행이 숲 깊숙이 묻힐 때까지, 모두가 탈출에 너무 골몰한 나머지 속삭임으로조차 말 한마디 함부로 내뱉지 못했다."], ["The scout resumed his post in the advance, though his steps, after he had thrown a safe distance between himself and his enemies, were more deliberate than in their previous march, in consequence of his utter ignorance of the localities of the surrounding woods.", "정찰병은 다시 선두의 자리로 돌아갔으나, 적들과 자신 사이에 안전한 거리를 벌려 놓은 뒤로는, 주위 숲의 지형에 전혀 깜깜한 탓에 그 걸음이 앞서의 행군 때보다 한결 신중했다."], ["More than once he halted to consult with his confederates, the Mohicans, pointing upwards at the moon, and examining the barks of the trees with care.", "그는 한 번 이상 멈춰 서서 동료들인 모히칸족과 의논하며 달을 가리키기도 하고 나무껍질을 조심스레 살피기도 했다."], ["In these brief pauses, Heyward and the sisters listened, with senses rendered doubly acute by the danger, to detect any symptoms which might announce the proximity of their foes.", "이 짧은 멈춤들 속에서, 〈헤이워드〉와 자매는 위험으로 두 배나 예민해진 감각으로 귀를 기울여, 적들이 가까이 있음을 알릴 만한 기척이라도 잡아내려 했다."], ["At such moments, it seemed as if a vast range of country lay buried in eternal sleep; not the least sound arising from the forest, unless it was the distant and scarcely audible rippling of a water-course.", "그런 순간이면, 광막한 들판이 영원한 잠 속에 파묻힌 듯했으니, 멀고도 거의 들리지 않는 시냇물의 잔물결 소리를 빼고는 숲에서 아무런 소리도 일지 않았다."], ["Birds, beasts, and man, appeared to slumber alike, if, indeed, any of the latter were to be found in that wide tract of wilderness.", "새도 짐승도 사람도 한결같이 잠든 듯했으니, 설령 이 드넓은 황야의 자락 어딘가에 사람이 있었다 한들 그러했다."], ["But the sounds of the rivulet, feeble and murmuring as they were, relieved the guides at once from no trifling embarrassment, and towards it they immediately held their way.", "그러나 가냘프게 속삭일 뿐인 그 시냇물 소리가, 안내자들을 적잖은 곤혹에서 단숨에 풀어 주었고, 그들은 곧장 그쪽으로 길을 잡았다."]], [["When the banks of the little stream were gained, Hawkeye made another halt; and, taking the moccasins from his feet, he invited Heyward and Gamut to follow his example.", "자그마한 시내의 둑에 다다르자, 〈호크아이〉는 다시 한 번 걸음을 멈추었다. 그러고는 발에서 모카신을 벗어 들며, 〈헤이워드〉와 〈거뭇〉에게도 자기를 따라 하라고 권했다."], ["He then entered the water, and for near an hour they travelled in the bed of the brook, leaving no trail.", "그런 다음 물속으로 들어갔고, 한 시간 가까이 그들은 흔적을 남기지 않으려 개울 바닥을 걸었다."], ["The moon had already sunk into an immense pile of black clouds, which lay impending above the western horizon, when they issued from the low and devious water-course to rise again to the light and level of the sandy but wooded plain.", "달은 이미 서쪽 지평선 위에 드리운 시커먼 구름 더미 속으로 가라앉아 있었으니, 그들이 낮고 구불구불한 물길에서 벗어나 모래밭이되 나무가 우거진 평지의 빛과 높이로 다시 올라설 무렵이었다."], ["Here the scout seemed to be once more at home, for he held on his way with the certainty and diligence of a man who moved in the security of his own knowledge.", "이곳에서 정찰병은 다시 제 집 안에 든 듯이 보였으니, 제 앎의 든든함 속에서 움직이는 사람의 확신과 부지런함으로 길을 헤쳐 나갔다."], ["The path soon became more uneven, and the travellers could plainly perceive that the mountains drew nigher to them on each hand, and that they were, in truth, about entering one of their gorges.", "길은 이내 더 험해졌고, 여행자들은 양편으로 산들이 점점 가까이 다가서고 있음을, 그리하여 자신들이 실로 그 협곡 가운데 하나로 막 들어서려 함을 또렷이 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["Suddenly, Hawkeye made a pause, and waiting until he was joined by the whole party, he spoke, though in tones so low and cautious, that they added to the solemnity of his words, in the quiet and darkness of the place.", "갑자기 〈호크아이〉가 걸음을 멈추었고, 일행 모두가 합류하기를 기다린 뒤, 입을 열었으니, 그 목소리는 너무나 낮고 조심스러워서, 그 장소의 고요와 어둠 속에서 그의 말에 한층 엄숙함을 더했다."]], [["\"It is easy to know the pathways, and to find the licks and water-courses of the wilderness,\"", "\"황야의 길을 알고, 짐승이 소금을 핥는 곳이나 물길을 찾아내기란 쉬운 일이오.\""], ["he said; \"but who that saw this spot could venture to say, that a mighty army was at rest among yonder silent trees and barren mountains?\"", "그가 말했다. \"허나 이곳을 본 누가, 저 말 없는 나무들과 헐벗은 산들 가운데 거대한 군대가 쉬고 있다고 감히 말할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["\"We are then at no great distance from William Henry?\"", "\"그렇다면 우리가 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에서 그리 멀지 않다는 말이오?\""], ["said Heyward, advancing nigher to the scout.", "〈헤이워드〉가 정찰병에게 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다."]], [["\"It is yet a long and weary path, and when and where to strike it, is now our greatest difficulty.", "\"아직은 길고 고된 길이오. 더구나 언제, 어디서 그 길로 들어서느냐가 지금 우리의 가장 큰 난제요."], ["See,\"", "보시오,\" 그가 나무들 사이로, 잔잔한 품에 별빛을 비추는 작은 웅덩이가 있는 곳을 가리키며 말했다."], ["he said, pointing through the trees towards a spot where a little basin of water reflected the stars from its placid bosom, \"here is the 'bloody pond'; and I am on the ground that I have not only often travelled, but over which I have fou't the enemy, from the rising to the setting sun.\"", "\"여기가 '피의 못'이오. 그리고 나는, 여러 번 지나다녔을 뿐 아니라, 해 뜰 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 적과 싸웠던 바로 그 땅 위에 서 있는 거요.\""]], [["\"Ha!", "\"하!"], ["that sheet of dull and dreary water, then, is the sepulchre of the brave men who fell in the contest.", "그렇다면 저 흐릿하고 음산한 물판이, 그 싸움에서 쓰러진 용사들의 무덤이란 말이로군."], ["I have heard it named, but never have I stood on its banks before.\"", "이름은 들어 보았으나, 그 둑 위에 서 본 적은 한 번도 없었소.\""]], [["\"Three battles did we make with the Dutch-Frenchman[20] in a day,\"", "\"우리는 하루에 그 네덜란드계 프랑스인과 세 차례 싸움을 벌였소.\""], ["continued Hawkeye, pursuing the train of his own thoughts, rather than replying to the remark of Duncan.", "〈호크아이〉는 〈덩컨〉의 말에 대답하기보다는 제 생각의 줄기를 좇아 말을 이었다."], ["\"He met us hard by, in our outward march to ambush his advance, and scattered us, like driven deer, through the defile, to the shores of Horican.", "\"그자가 우리를 바로 이 가까이에서, 우리가 그자의 진격을 매복하려 나아가던 행군 길에서 맞닥뜨려, 쫓기는 사슴처럼 우리를 협곡을 가로질러 〈호리컨〉의 기슭까지 흩어 놓았소."], ["Then we rallied behind our fallen trees, and made head against him, under Sir William--who was made Sir William for that very deed; and well did we pay him for the disgrace of the morning!", "그러자 우리는 쓰러진 나무들 뒤로 다시 모여, 〈윌리엄〉 경 — 바로 그 공으로 〈윌리엄〉 경이 되신 분 — 의 지휘 아래 그자에게 맞섰소. 그리고 우리는 아침의 치욕을 톡톡히 갚아 주었소!"], ["Hundreds of Frenchmen saw the sun that day for the last time; and even the leader, Dieskau himself, fell into our hands so cut and torn with the lead, that he has gone back to his own country, unfit for further acts in war.\"", "그날 수백 명의 프랑스인이 마지막으로 해를 보았고, 우두머리인 〈디스카우〉 자신마저 납탄에 베이고 찢겨 우리 손에 떨어졌으니, 그자는 더는 전쟁에 나설 수 없게 되어 제 나라로 돌아가고 말았소.\""]], [["\"'Twas a noble repulse!\"", "\"훌륭한 격퇴였소!\""], ["exclaimed Heyward, in the heat of his youthful ardor; \"the fame of it reached us early, in our southern army.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 젊은 혈기에 달아올라 외쳤다. \"그 명성은 우리 남부의 군대에까지 일찌감치 닿았소.\""]], [["\"Ay!", "\"그렇소!"], ["but it did not end there.", "허나 거기서 끝나지 않았소."], ["I was sent by Major Effingham, at Sir William's own bidding, to outflank the French, and carry the tidings of their disaster across the portage, to the fort on the Hudson.", "나는 〈윌리엄〉 경 친히 내리신 명에 따라 〈에핑엄〉 소령의 지시로, 프랑스군의 측면을 돌아 그들의 참패 소식을 육로 운반로 너머 〈허드슨〉강의 요새로 전하러 보내졌소."], ["Just hereaway, where you see the trees rise into a mountain swell, I met a party coming down to our aid, and I led them where the enemy were taking their meal, little dreaming that they had not finished the bloody work of the day.\"", "바로 이 근처, 나무들이 산등성이로 솟아오른 저기에서, 나는 우리를 도우러 내려오는 한 무리를 만났고, 그들을 적이 끼니를 들고 있던 곳으로 이끌었소. 그자들은 그날의 피비린 일을 아직 다 끝내지 못했음을 꿈에도 몰랐던 거요.\""]], [["\"And you surprised them?\"", "\"그래서 그들을 기습했소?\""]], [["\"If death can be a surprise to men who are thinking only of the cravings of their appetites.", "\"오로지 제 식욕의 갈망만 생각하던 자들에게 죽음이 기습이 될 수 있다면 그렇소."], ["We gave them but little breathing time, for they had borne hard upon us in the fight of the morning, and there were few in our party who had not lost friend or relative by their hands.", "우리는 그자들에게 숨 돌릴 틈조차 거의 주지 않았으니, 그들이 아침의 싸움에서 우리를 모질게 몰아붙였던 데다, 우리 무리에 그들의 손에 벗이나 혈육을 잃지 않은 자가 거의 없었던 까닭이오."], ["When all was over, the dead, and some say the dying, were cast into that little pond.", "다 끝났을 때, 죽은 자들은, 그리고 더러는 죽어 가는 자들마저, 저 작은 못 속에 던져졌소."], ["These eyes have seen its waters colored with blood, as natural water never yet flowed from the bowels of the 'arth.\"", "이 두 눈은 그 물이 피로 물든 것을 보았으니, 그런 자연의 물이 일찍이 땅의 속에서 흘러나온 적은 없었소.\""]], [["\"It was a convenient, and, I trust, will prove a peaceful grave for a soldier.", "\"군인에게는 알맞은, 그리고 부디 평온한 무덤이 되기를 바라오."], ["You have, then, seen much service on this frontier?\"", "그렇다면 이 변경에서 많은 전투를 겪었군요?\""]], [["\"I!\"", "\"내가!\""], ["said the scout, erecting his tall person with an air of military pride; \"there are not many echoes among these hills that haven't rung with the crack of my rifle, nor is there the space of a square mile atwixt Horican and the river, that 'Killdeer'", "정찰병이 군인다운 자긍심으로 그 큰 키를 곧추세우며 말했다. \"이 산들 가운데 내 소총의 총성으로 울리지 않은 메아리가 별로 없고, 〈호리컨〉과 강 사이 한 평방마일의 땅치고 '킬디어'가 산 몸뚱이 하나 떨어뜨리지 않은 데가 없소."], ["hasn't dropped a living body on, be it an enemy or be it a brute beast.", "적이든 짐승이든 말이오."], ["As for the grave, there, being as quiet as you mention, it is another matter.", "무덤으로 말하자면, 그것이 당신 말처럼 그토록 고요한가는 또 다른 문제요."], ["There are them in the camp who say and think, man, to lie still, should not be buried while the breath is in the body; and certain it is that in the hurry of that evening, the doctors had but little time to say who was living and who was dead.", "진지에는, 사람이 가만히 누우려면 몸에 숨이 붙어 있는 동안에는 묻혀서는 안 된다고 말하고 또 그리 믿는 자들이 있소. 그리고 그날 저녁의 황망함 속에서, 군의관들이 누가 살아 있고 누가 죽었는지 가릴 틈이 거의 없었던 것만은 분명하오."], ["Hist!", "쉿!"], ["see you nothing walking on the shore of the pond?\"", "못의 기슭에 무언가 걷고 있는 것이 보이지 않소?\""]], [["\"'Tis not probable that any are as houseless as ourselves, in this dreary forest.\"", "\"이 음산한 숲속에서, 우리만큼이나 거처 없는 자가 또 있을 성싶지는 않소.\""]], [["\"Such as he may care but little for house or shelter, and night dew can never wet a body that passes its days in the water,\"", "\"그런 자라면 집이나 거처 따위는 거의 개의치 않을 것이고, 밤이슬은 낮을 물속에서 보내는 몸뚱이를 결코 적시지 못하는 법이오.\""], ["returned the scout, grasping the shoulder of Heyward with such convulsive strength as to make the young soldier painfully sensible how much superstitious terror had got the mastery of a man usually so dauntless.", "정찰병이 그렇게 받으며, 〈헤이워드〉의 어깨를 어찌나 발작적인 힘으로 움켜쥐던지, 젊은 군인은 평소 그토록 겁 없던 사내가 미신적인 공포에 얼마나 사로잡혀 있는지를 아프도록 절감했다."]], [["\"By heaven!", "\"맙소사!"], ["there is a human form, and it approaches!", "사람의 형상이오, 그것이 다가오고 있소!"], ["Stand to your arms, my friends; for we know not whom we encounter.\"", "무기를 잡으시오, 동지들. 우리가 누구를 맞닥뜨리는지 알 수 없으니.\""]], [["\"Qui vive?\"", "\"누구냐?\""], ["demanded a stern, quick voice, which sounded like a challenge from another world, issuing out of that solitary and solemn place.", "준엄하고 날카로운 목소리가 다그쳤으니, 그 외롭고 엄숙한 곳에서 흘러나온 그것은 마치 다른 세상에서 던지는 도전처럼 들렸다."]], [["\"What says it?\"", "\"뭐라고 한 거요?\""], ["whispered the scout; \"it speaks neither Indian nor English!\"", "정찰병이 속삭였다. \"인디언 말도, 영어도 아니잖소!\""]], [["\"Qui vive?\"", "\"누구냐?\""], ["repeated the same voice, which was quickly followed by the rattling of arms, and a menacing attitude.", "같은 목소리가 되풀이했고, 곧이어 무기를 절거덕거리는 소리와 위협하는 자세가 뒤따랐다."]], [["\"France!\"", "\"프랑스다!\""], ["cried Heyward, advancing from the shadow of the trees to the shore of the pond, within a few yards of the sentinel.", "〈헤이워드〉가 외치며 나무 그늘에서 못의 기슭으로 나아가, 보초로부터 몇 야드 안쪽까지 다가섰다."]], [["\"D'ou venez-vous--ou allez-vous, d'aussi bonne heure?\"", "\"어디서 오는가—이런 이른 시각에 어디로 가는가?\""], ["demanded the grenadier, in the language and with the accent of a man from old France.", "척탄병이 다그쳤으니, 그 말과 억양은 본토 프랑스에서 온 사람의 것이었다."]], [["\"Je viens de la decouverte, et je vais me coucher.\"", "\"정찰에서 돌아와, 잠자리에 들러 가는 길이오.\""]], [["\"Etes-vous officier du roi?\"", "\"국왕의 장교인가?\""]], [["\"Sans doute, mon camarade; me prends-tu pour un provincial!", "\"물론이지, 전우. 나를 식민지 사람으로 보는가!"], ["Je suis capitaine de chasseurs (Heyward well knew that the other was of a regiment in the line); j'ai ici, avec moi, les filles du commandant de la fortification.", "나는 추격병 대위요.\" (〈헤이워드〉는 상대가 정규 보병 연대 소속임을 잘 알고 있었다.) \"여기 나와 함께 요새 사령관의 따님들을 데리고 있소."], ["Aha!", "아하!"], ["tu en as entendu parler!", "그 이야기를 들었던 모양이군!"], ["je les ai fait prisonnieres pres de l'autre fort, et je les conduis au general.\"", "다른 요새 근처에서 이들을 포로로 잡아, 장군께 데려가는 길이오.\""]], [["\"Ma foi!", "\"이런!"], ["mesdames; j'en suis fache pour vous,\"", "부인들, 참으로 안되셨습니다.\""], ["exclaimed the young soldier, touching his cap with grace; \"mais--fortune de guerre!", "젊은 병사가 모자에 손을 우아하게 갖다 대며 외쳤다. \"허나—전쟁의 운명이지요!"], ["vous trouverez notre general un brave homme, et bien poli avec les dames.\"", "우리 장군께서는 용감한 분이시고, 부인들께 무척 정중하시다는 걸 아시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["\"C'est le caractere des gens de guerre,\"", "\"그것이 군인의 본성이지요.\""], ["said Cora, with admirable self-possession.", "〈코라〉가 감탄스러운 침착함으로 말했다."], ["\"Adieu, mon ami; je vous souhaiterais un devoir plus agreable a remplir.\"", "\"잘 가요, 친구여. 그대에게 좀 더 즐거운 임무가 주어지기를 바라겠어요.\""]], [["The soldier made a low and humble acknowledgment for her civility; and Heyward adding a \"Bonne nuit, mon camarade,\"", "병사는 그녀의 정중함에 낮고 겸손하게 답례했고, 〈헤이워드〉가 \"잘 자게, 전우\"라고 덧붙이자, 그들은 신중하게 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["they moved deliberately forward, leaving the sentinel pacing the banks of the silent pond, little suspecting an enemy of so much effrontery, and humming to himself those words, which were recalled to his mind by the sight of women, and perhaps by recollections of his own distant and beautiful France--", "보초는 그토록 뻔뻔한 적을 거의 의심하지 않은 채 말 없는 못의 둑을 거닐며, 여인들을 본 까닭에, 그리고 어쩌면 멀고도 아름다운 제 조국 프랑스에 대한 추억으로 떠오른 그 노랫말을 홀로 흥얼거렸다."]], [["\"Vive le vin, l'amour,\"", "\"포도주여, 사랑이여, 만세.\""], ["etc., etc.", "운운."]], [["\"'Tis well you understood the knave!\"", "\"저 악당의 말을 알아들으니 다행이구려!\" 정찰병이, 그곳에서 조금 거리를 두고 나서, 소총을 다시 팔 안쪽에 끼며 속삭였다."], ["whispered the scout, when they had gained a little distance from the place, and letting his rifle fall into the hollow of his arm again; \"I soon saw that he was one of them uneasy Frenchers; and well for him it was that his speech was friendly and his wishes kind, or a place might have been found for his bones among those of his countrymen.\"", "\"나는 곧 그자가 저 불안해하는 프랑스인 중 하나임을 알아챘소. 그자에겐 다행이었지, 그 말이 다정하고 그 속내가 친절했기에 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 그자의 뼈가 제 동포들 뼈 틈에 자리를 얻었을 거요.\""]], [["He was interrupted by a long and heavy groan which arose from the little basin, as though, in truth, the spirits of the departed lingered about their watery sepulchre.", "작은 웅덩이에서 길고 무거운 신음이 일어나며 그의 말을 끊었으니, 마치 떠난 자들의 혼령이 정말로 그 물의 무덤가에 머물러 있기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["\"Surely it was of flesh!\"", "\"분명 살아 있는 것이었소!\""], ["continued the scout; \"no spirit could handle its arms so steadily!\"", "정찰병이 말을 이었다. \"어떤 혼령도 그토록 흔들림 없이 무기를 다룰 수는 없으니!\""]], [["\"It _was_ of flesh; but whether the poor fellow still belongs to this world may well be doubted,\"", "\"살아 있는 것이었소. 허나 그 가엾은 친구가 아직도 이 세상에 속해 있는지는 의심스럽소.\""], ["said Heyward, glancing his eyes around him, and missing Chingachgook from their little band.", "〈헤이워드〉가 주위를 둘러보며, 일행 가운데 〈칭가치국〉이 보이지 않음을 알아채고 말했다."], ["Another groan more faint than the former, was succeeded by a heavy and sullen plunge into the water, and all was as still again as if the borders of the dreary pool had never been awakened from the silence of creation.", "앞의 것보다 더 희미한 또 한 차례의 신음이 일더니, 이어 물속으로 무겁고 둔하게 풍덩 잠기는 소리가 났고, 그러자 모든 것이 다시 고요해져, 마치 이 음산한 못의 가장자리가 천지창조 이래의 정적에서 한 번도 깨어난 적이 없었던 듯했다."], ["While they yet hesitated in uncertainty, the form of the Indian was seen gliding out of the thicket.", "그들이 아직 불안 속에 머뭇거리는 동안, 인디언의 형상이 덤불에서 미끄러져 나오는 것이 보였다."], ["As the chief rejoined them, with one hand he attached the reeking scalp of the unfortunate young Frenchman to his girdle, and with the other he replaced the knife and tomahawk that had drunk his blood.", "우두머리가 그들에게 합류할 때, 한 손으로는 불운한 젊은 프랑스인의 김이 나는 머릿가죽을 제 허리띠에 매달았고, 다른 손으로는 그의 피를 마신 칼과 토마호크를 도로 꽂아 넣었다."], ["He then took his wonted station, with the air of a man who believed he had done a deed of merit.", "그러고는 공을 세웠다고 믿는 사람의 표정으로 늘 서던 자리에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["The scout dropped one end of his rifle to the earth, and leaning his hands on the other, he stood musing in profound silence.", "정찰병은 소총의 한쪽 끝을 땅에 내려놓고, 다른 쪽 끝에 두 손을 얹은 채, 깊은 침묵 속에 생각에 잠겨 섰다."], ["Then shaking his head in a mournful manner, he muttered,--", "그러고는 슬픈 듯이 고개를 가로저으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"'T would have been a cruel and an unhuman act for a white-skin; but 'tis the gift and natur'", "\"백인에게라면 잔인하고 비인간적인 짓이었을 거요."], ["of an Indian, and I suppose it should not be denied.", "허나 그것은 인디언의 천성이자 본성이니, 부정해서는 안 되겠지 싶소."], ["I could wish, though, it had befallen an accursed Mingo, rather than that gay young boy from the old countries.\"", "그래도, 저 본국에서 온 명랑한 젊은이가 아니라 저주받은 〈밍고〉족 하나에게 그 일이 닥쳤더라면 좋았을 것을.\""]], [["\"Enough!\"", "\"그만하면 됐소!\" 〈헤이워드〉가, 영문 모르는 자매가 이 지체의 까닭을 헤아릴까 염려하여, 그리고 사냥꾼의 것과 자못 비슷한 일련의 생각으로 제 혐오를 억누르며 말했다."], ["said Heyward, apprehensive the unconscious sisters might comprehend the nature of the detention, and conquering his disgust by a train of reflections very much like that of the hunter; \"'tis done; and though better it were left undone, cannot be amended.", "\"이미 끝난 일이오. 안 하느니만 못한 일이었다 한들, 돌이킬 수는 없소."], ["You see we are, too obviously, within the sentinels of the enemy; what course do you propose to follow?\"", "보다시피 우리는 너무도 빤히 적의 보초 안쪽에 들어와 있소. 어떤 길을 따르자고 제안하겠소?\""]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"그렇소,\""], ["said Hawkeye, rousing himself again, \"'tis as you say, too late to harbor further thoughts about it.", "〈호크아이〉가 다시 정신을 차리며 말했다. \"당신 말대로요, 그 일을 두고 더 골몰하기엔 너무 늦었소."], ["Ay, the French have gathered around the fort in good earnest, and we have a delicate needle to thread in passing them.\"", "그렇소, 프랑스군이 요새 둘레에 단단히 모여들었으니, 그들을 지나려면 우리는 섬세한 바늘귀에 실을 꿰어야 하오.\""]], [["\"And but little time to do it in,\"", "\"그것도 짧은 시간 안에 해야 하오.\""], ["added Heyward, glancing his eyes upward, toward the bank of vapor that concealed the setting moon.", "〈헤이워드〉가 지는 달을 가린 안개의 둑을 향해 위를 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다."]], [["\"And little time to do it in!\"", "\"그것도 짧은 시간 안에!\""], ["repeated the scout.", "정찰병이 되풀이했다."], ["\"The thing may be done in two fashions, by the help of Providence, without which it may not be done at all.\"", "\"그 일은 두 가지 방식으로 해낼 수 있소, 섭리의 도움으로 말이오. 그 도움이 없다면 아예 해낼 수 없소.\""]], [["\"Name them quickly, for time presses.\"", "\"어서 말해 보시오, 시간이 다그치니.\""]], [["\"One would be to dismount the gentle ones, and let their beasts range the plain; by sending the Mohicans in front, we might then cut a lane through their sentries, and enter the fort over the dead bodies.\"", "\"하나는, 그 고운 분들을 말에서 내리게 하고 짐승들은 들판을 떠돌게 두는 거요. 모히칸족을 앞세워, 그들의 보초들 사이로 길을 베어 내고, 시체들을 밟고 요새로 들어가는 거요.\""]], [["\"It will not do--it will not do!\"", "\"안 되오—안 되오!\""], ["interrupted the generous Heyward; \"a soldier might force his way in this manner, but never with such a convoy.\"", "인심 후한 〈헤이워드〉가 가로막았다. \"군인이라면 이런 식으로 길을 뚫을 수 있겠지만, 이런 일행을 거느리고는 결코 안 되오.\""]], [["\"'Twould be, indeed, a bloody path for tender feet to wade in,\" returned the equally reluctant scout; \"but I thought it befitting my manhood to name it. We must then turn on our trail and get without the line of their look-outs, when we will bend short to the west, and enter the mountains; where I can hide you, so that all the devil's hounds in Montcalm's pay would be thrown off the scent, for months to come.\"", "\"실로, 여린 발이 헤쳐 가기엔 피로 물든 길이 될 거요.\" 마찬가지로 내키지 않아 하던 정찰병이 받았다. \"허나 그것을 말하는 것이 사내다운 도리라 여겼소. 그렇다면 우리는 왔던 자취로 되돌아가 그들의 감시선 밖으로 나가야 하오. 그런 다음 짧게 서쪽으로 꺾어 산으로 들어가는 거요. 거기서 내가 당신들을 숨겨 주리다. 〈몽칼름〉의 돈을 받는 악마의 사냥개를 다 풀어 놓아도, 몇 달이 가도록 냄새를 못 맡게 말이오.\""]], [["\"Let it be done, and that instantly.\"", "\"그리하시오, 그것도 당장에.\""]], [["Further words were unnecessary; for Hawkeye, merely uttering the mandate to \"follow,\" moved along the route by which they had just entered their present critical and even dangerous situation.", "더는 말이 필요 없었으니, 〈호크아이〉는 그저 \"따라오시오\"라는 명만을 내뱉고, 그들이 방금 이 위태롭고 위험하기까지 한 처지로 들어선 그 길을 따라 움직였다."], ["Their progress, like their late dialogue, was guarded, and without noise; for none knew at what moment a passing patrol, or a crouching picket of the enemy, might rise upon their path.", "그들의 전진은 방금 전의 대화처럼 조심스럽고 소리가 없었으니, 어느 순간에 지나가는 순찰대나 웅크린 적의 보초가 그들의 길 위로 솟아오를지 아무도 몰랐던 까닭이다."], ["As they held their silent way along the margin of the pond, again Heyward and the scout stole furtive glances at its appalling dreariness.", "못의 가장자리를 따라 말없이 길을 잡는 동안, 〈헤이워드〉와 정찰병은 다시금 그 소름 끼치는 음산함에 은밀한 시선을 던졌다."], ["They looked in vain for the form they had so recently seen stalking along its silent shores, while a low and regular wash of the little waves, by announcing that the waters were not yet subsided, furnished a frightful memorial of the deed of blood they had just witnessed.", "그들은 방금 전 그 고요한 기슭을 거닐던 형상을 찾았으나 헛수고였고, 작은 물결이 낮고 규칙적으로 찰싹이며, 물이 아직 가라앉지 않았음을 알리는 것이, 그들이 방금 목격한 피의 행위를 끔찍하게 떠올리게 했다."], ["Like all that passing and gloomy scene, the low basin, however, quickly melted in the darkness, and became blended with the mass of black objects in the rear of the travellers.", "그러나 그 음울하게 지나가던 모든 광경과 마찬가지로, 낮은 웅덩이도 이내 어둠 속에 녹아들어, 여행자들의 뒤편에 늘어선 검은 형상들의 덩어리에 섞여 들었다."]], [["Hawkeye soon deviated from the line of their retreat, and striking off towards the mountains which form the western boundary of the narrow plain, he led his followers, with swift steps, deep within the shadows that were cast from their high and broken summits.", "〈호크아이〉는 곧 퇴각의 길에서 벗어나, 좁은 평지의 서쪽 경계를 이루는 산들을 향해 방향을 틀었고, 일행을 빠른 걸음으로 그 높고 험한 봉우리들이 드리운 그림자 깊숙이 이끌었다."], ["The route was now painful; lying over ground ragged with rocks, and intersected with ravines, and their progress proportionately slow.", "이제 길은 고되었으니, 바위로 울퉁불퉁하고 골짜기로 끊긴 땅 위에 놓여 있어, 그들의 전진도 그만큼 더뎠다."], ["Bleak and black hills lay on every side of them, compensating in some degree for the additional toil of the march, by the sense of security they imparted.", "황량하고 시커먼 언덕들이 사방으로 그들을 에워쌌으니, 행군의 가중된 노고를, 그것들이 안겨 주는 안도감으로 어느 정도 보상해 주었다."], ["At length the party began slowly to climb a steep and rugged ascent by a path that curiously wound among rocks and trees, avoiding the one, and supported by the other, in a manner that showed it had been devised by men long practised in the arts of the wilderness.", "마침내 일행은 바위와 나무 사이를 묘하게 굽이도는 길을 따라, 가파르고 험한 비탈을 천천히 오르기 시작했으니, 그 길은 바위는 피하고 나무에는 기대는 식으로, 황야의 기예에 오래 단련된 사람들이 고안했음을 보여 주었다."], ["As they gradually rose from the level of the valleys, the thick darkness which usually precedes the approach of day began to disperse, and objects were seen in the plain and palpable colors with which they had been gifted by nature.", "골짜기의 높이에서 차츰 솟아오르자, 날이 다가오기 직전에 으레 깔리던 짙은 어둠이 흩어지기 시작했고, 사물들은 자연이 부여한 그 또렷하고 손에 잡힐 듯한 빛깔로 보이게 되었다."], ["When they issued from the stunted woods which clung to the barren sides of the mountain, upon a flat and mossy rock that formed its summit, they met the morning, as it came blushing above the green pines of a hill that lay on the opposite side of the valley of the Horican.", "산의 헐벗은 비탈에 매달린 우거진 숲에서 벗어나, 그 정상을 이루는 평평하고 이끼 낀 바위 위로 나섰을 때, 그들은 아침을 맞았으니, 그 아침은 〈호리컨〉 골짜기 건너편에 놓인 언덕의 푸른 소나무들 위로 붉게 물들며 떠오르고 있었다."]], [["The scout now told the sisters to dismount; and taking the bridles from the mouths, and the saddles off the backs of the jaded beasts, he turned them loose, to glean a scanty subsistence among the shrubs and meagre herbage of that elevated region.", "이제 정찰병은 자매에게 말에서 내리라 일렀고, 지친 짐승들의 입에서 재갈을, 등에서 안장을 벗긴 뒤, 그것들을 풀어 놓아, 그 높은 고장의 관목과 빈약한 풀 사이에서 변변찮은 끼니나마 뜯게 했다."]], [["\"Go,\"", "\"가거라,\""], ["he said, \"and seek your food where natur'", "그가 말했다. \"자연이 너희에게 먹을 것을 주는 곳에서 그것을 찾아라."], ["gives it you; and beware that you become not food to ravenous wolves yourselves, among these hills.\"", "그리고 이 언덕들 사이에서 너희 자신이 굶주린 늑대의 먹이가 되지 않도록 조심하거라.\""]], [["\"Have we no further need of them?\"", "\"이제 저것들이 더는 필요 없소?\""], ["demanded Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"See, and judge with your own eyes,\"", "\"보시오, 그리고 당신 두 눈으로 판단하시오.\""], ["said the scout, advancing towards the eastern brow of the mountain, whither he beckoned for the whole party to follow; \"if it was as easy to look into the heart of man as it is to spy out the nakedness of Montcalm's camp from this spot, hypocrites would grow scarce, and the cunning of a Mingo might prove a losing game, compared to the honesty of a Delaware.\"", "정찰병이 산의 동쪽 마루를 향해 나아가며, 일행 모두에게 따라오라 손짓하며 말했다. \"이 자리에서 〈몽칼름〉의 진지가 벌거벗고 드러난 것을 엿보기가 쉬운 만큼, 사람의 마음속을 들여다보기도 쉽다면, 위선자가 드물어질 것이고, 〈밍고〉족의 교활함은 〈델라웨어〉족의 정직함에 견주면 지는 노름이 될 거요.\""]], [["When the travellers reached the verge of the precipice, they saw, at a glance, the truth of the scout's declaration, and the admirable foresight with which he had led them to their commanding station.", "여행자들이 벼랑 끝에 다다랐을 때, 그들은 정찰병의 말이 진실임을, 그리고 그가 자신들을 이 내려다보기 좋은 자리로 이끈 그 빼어난 선견지명을 한눈에 알아보았다."]], [["The mountain on which they stood, elevated, perhaps, a thousand feet in the air, was a high cone that rose a little in advance of that range which stretches for miles along the western shores of the lake, until meeting its sister piles, beyond the water, it ran off towards the Canadas, in confused and broken masses of rock, thinly sprinkled with evergreens.", "그들이 서 있는 산은, 어쩌면 천 피트쯤 공중으로 솟았을 높은 원뿔로, 호수의 서쪽 기슭을 따라 여러 마일에 걸쳐 뻗은 산맥보다 조금 앞으로 나와 솟아 있었으니, 그 산맥은 물 건너 자매 봉우리들과 만난 뒤, 어지럽고 깨진 바위 덩어리가 되어 상록수가 듬성듬성 뿌려진 채 〈캐나다〉 쪽으로 달려 나갔다."], ["Immediately at the feet of the party, the southern shore of the Horican swept in a broad semicircle, from mountain to mountain, marking a wide strand, that soon rose into an uneven and somewhat elevated plain.", "일행의 바로 발치에서, 〈호리컨〉의 남쪽 기슭이 산에서 산으로 넓은 반원을 그리며 휘돌아, 너른 모래톱을 이루었으니, 그것이 이내 울퉁불퉁하고 다소 솟아오른 평지로 올라섰다."], ["To the north stretched the limpid, and, as it appeared from that dizzy height, the narrow sheet of the \"holy lake,\" indented with numberless bays, embellished by fantastic headlands, and dotted with countless islands.", "북쪽으로는, 맑고, 그 아찔한 높이에서 보기로는 좁다란 \"거룩한 호수\"의 물판이 뻗어 있었으니, 헤아릴 수 없는 만(灣)으로 들쭉날쭉하고, 기이한 곶들로 꾸며졌으며, 수없는 섬들이 점점이 박혀 있었다."], ["At the distance of a few leagues, the bed of the waters became lost among mountains, or was wrapped in the masses of vapor that came slowly rolling along their bosom, before a light morning air.", "몇 리그쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 물의 바닥은 산들 사이로 자취를 감추거나, 가벼운 아침 바람결에 그 품을 따라 천천히 굴러오는 안개 덩어리에 휩싸였다."], ["But a narrow opening between the crests of the hills pointed out the passage by which they found their way still farther north, to spread their pure and ample sheets again, before pouring out their tribute into the distant Champlain.", "그러나 언덕 마루들 사이의 좁은 틈이, 그 물이 더 북쪽으로 길을 찾아 나가, 맑고 너른 물판을 다시 펼친 뒤 먼 〈섐플레인〉으로 그 물을 부어 넣는 그 통로를 가리켜 보였다."], ["To the south stretched the defile, or rather broken plain, so often mentioned.", "남쪽으로는, 그토록 자주 언급된 협곡, 아니 끊긴 평지가 뻗어 있었다."], ["For several miles in this direction, the mountains appeared reluctant to yield their dominion, but within reach of the eye they diverged, and finally melted into the level and sandy lands, across which we have accompanied our adventurers in their double journey.", "이 방향으로 여러 마일에 걸쳐, 산들은 그 지배권을 내어 주기를 꺼리는 듯했으나, 눈이 닿는 데에서 그것들은 갈라지더니, 마침내 우리가 모험가들을 따라 두 차례의 여정을 함께한 그 평평하고 모래 깔린 땅으로 녹아들었다."], ["Along both ranges of hills, which bounded the opposite sides of the lake and valley, clouds of light vapor were rising in spiral wreaths from the uninhabited woods, looking like the smokes of hidden cottages; or rolled lazily down the declivities, to mingle with the fogs of the lower land.", "호수와 골짜기의 마주한 양편을 가른 두 산맥을 따라, 가벼운 안개구름이 사람 없는 숲에서 나선의 화환을 그리며 피어올랐으니, 마치 숨은 오두막의 연기처럼 보였고, 더러는 비탈을 따라 나른하게 굴러 내려가 낮은 땅의 안개에 섞여 들었다."], ["A single, solitary, snow-white cloud floated above the valley, and marked the spot beneath which lay the silent pool of the \"bloody pond.\"", "외로이 홀로 떠 있는 눈처럼 흰 구름 하나가 골짜기 위에 떠서, 그 아래에 \"피의 못\"의 말 없는 웅덩이가 놓인 자리를 일러 주었다."]], [["Directly on the shore of the lake, and nearer to its western than to its eastern margin, lay the extensive earthen ramparts and low buildings of William Henry.", "호수의 기슭 바로 위, 동쪽 가장자리보다 서쪽 가장자리에 더 가까이, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 드넓은 흙벽과 낮은 건물들이 놓여 있었다."], ["Two of the sweeping bastions appeared to rest on the water which washed their bases, while a deep ditch and extensive morasses guarded its other sides and angles.", "휘돌아 나간 두 능보(稜堡)는 그 밑동을 씻는 물 위에 기댄 듯했고, 깊은 해자와 넓은 늪지가 다른 쪽 면과 모서리들을 지켰다."], ["The land had been cleared of wood for a reasonable distance around the work, but every other part of the scene lay in the green livery of nature, except where the limpid water mellowed the view, or the bold rocks thrust their black and naked heads above the undulating outline of the mountain ranges.", "요새 둘레로 적당한 거리만큼 나무가 베여 있었으나, 그 광경의 다른 모든 부분은 자연의 푸른 제복에 싸여 있었으니, 다만 맑은 물이 풍경을 부드럽게 한 곳이나, 험한 바위들이 산맥의 굽이치는 윤곽선 위로 검고 헐벗은 머리를 내민 곳만은 예외였다."], ["In its front might be seen the scattered sentinels, who held a weary watch against their numerous foes; and within the walls themselves, the travellers looked down upon men still drowsy with a night of vigilance.", "그 정면에는, 수많은 적에 맞서 고단한 경계를 서던 흩어진 보초들이 보였고, 벽 안쪽으로는 밤샘의 경계로 아직 졸음에 겨운 병사들을 여행자들이 내려다보았다."], ["Towards the southeast, but in immediate contact with the fort, was an entrenched camp, posted on a rocky eminence, that would have been far more eligible for the work itself, in which Hawkeye pointed out the presence of those auxiliary regiments that had so recently left the Hudson in their company.", "남동쪽으로, 그러나 요새와 바로 맞닿은 곳에는, 차라리 요새 그 자체를 짓기에 훨씬 알맞았을 바위 언덕 위에 참호로 둘러친 진지가 자리해 있었으니, 〈호크아이〉는 그곳에, 얼마 전 그들과 함께 〈허드슨〉강을 떠났던 그 지원 연대들이 있음을 가리켜 보였다."], ["From the woods, a little farther to the south, rose numerous dark and lurid smokes, that were easily to be distinguished from the purer exhalations of the springs, and which the scout also showed to Heyward, as evidences that the enemy lay in force in that direction.", "조금 더 남쪽의 숲에서는, 샘의 더 맑은 김과는 쉬이 구별되는 어둡고 음산한 연기가 무수히 피어올랐으니, 정찰병은 그것 또한 〈헤이워드〉에게, 적이 그 방향에 대군으로 진치고 있다는 증거로 보여 주었다."]], [["But the spectacle which most concerned the young soldier was on the western bank of the lake, though quite near to its southern termination.", "그러나 젊은 군인의 마음을 가장 사로잡은 광경은 호수의 서쪽 기슭에 있었으니, 다만 그 남쪽 끝에 꽤 가까운 곳이었다."], ["On a strip of land, which appeared, from his stand, too narrow to contain such an army, but which, in truth, extended many hundreds of yards from the shores of the Horican to the base of the mountain, were to be seen the white tents and military engines of an encampment of ten thousand men.", "그가 선 자리에서 보기에는 그런 군대를 담기엔 너무 좁아 보였으나, 실은 〈호리컨〉의 기슭에서 산의 밑동까지 수백 야드나 뻗은 한 줄기 땅 위에, 만 명에 이르는 진영의 흰 천막들과 군용 기계들이 보였다."], ["Batteries were already thrown up in their front, and even while the spectators above them were looking down, with such different emotions, on a scene which lay like a map beneath their feet, the roar of artillery rose from the valley, and passed off in thundering echoes, along the eastern hills.", "그들의 정면에는 이미 포대가 쌓여 있었고, 그 위쪽에서 구경꾼들이 발치에 지도처럼 펼쳐진 광경을 그토록 서로 다른 감정으로 내려다보는 바로 그 순간에도, 포성이 골짜기에서 솟아올라, 우레 같은 메아리가 되어 동쪽 언덕들을 따라 사라져 갔다."]], [["\"Morning is just touching them below,\"", "\"아침이 저 아래의 그들을 이제 막 어루만지고 있구려.\""], ["said the deliberate and musing scout, \"and the watchers have a mind to wake up the sleepers by the sound of cannon.", "신중하고 생각에 잠긴 정찰병이 말했다. \"그리고 보초들은 대포 소리로 잠든 자들을 깨우려는 모양이오."], ["We are a few hours too late?", "우리가 몇 시간 늦었군."], ["Montcalm has already filled the woods with his accursed Iroquois.\"", "〈몽칼름〉은 이미 저 저주받은 〈이로쿼이〉족으로 숲을 가득 채웠소.\""]], [["\"The place is, indeed, invested,\"", "\"이곳은 실로 포위되었군요.\""], ["returned Duncan, \"but is there no expedient by which we may enter?", "〈덩컨〉이 받았다. \"허나 우리가 들어갈 방도가 없겠소?"], ["capture in the works would be far preferable to falling again into the hands of roving Indians.\"", "요새 안에서 사로잡히는 편이, 떠도는 인디언들의 손에 다시 떨어지는 것보다 훨씬 나으니.\""]], [["\"See!\"", "\"보시오!\""], ["exclaimed the scout, unconsciously directing the attention of Cora to the quarters of her own father, \"how that shot has made the stones fly from the side of the commandant's house!", "정찰병이 무심코 〈코라〉의 주의를 그녀의 친아버지의 거처로 돌리며 외쳤다. \"저 포탄이 사령관 집 벽에서 돌을 튀겨 날린 꼴을 좀 보시오!"], ["Ay!", "그렇소!"], ["these Frenchers will pull it to pieces faster than it was put together, solid and thick though it be.\"", "이 프랑스인들은, 그것이 아무리 단단하고 두껍게 쌓였다 한들, 쌓인 것보다 더 빨리 그것을 산산조각 낼 거요.\""]], [["\"Heyward, I sicken at the sight of danger that I cannot share,\"", "\"〈헤이워드〉, 나는 함께 나눌 수 없는 위험을 보는 것이 메스껍군요.\""], ["said the undaunted, but anxious daughter.", "겁 없으나 근심에 찬 딸이 말했다."], ["\"Let us go to Montcalm, and demand admission: he dare not deny a child the boon.\"", "\"〈몽칼름〉에게로 가서 들여보내 달라 청합시다. 그는 한 자식의 청을 감히 거절하지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["\"You would scarce find the tent of the Frenchman with the hair on your head,\"", "\"머리에 머리카락을 붙인 채로는 그 프랑스인의 천막을 찾기 어려울 거요.\""], ["said the blunt scout.", "무뚝뚝한 정찰병이 말했다."], ["\"If I had but one of the thousand boats which lie empty along that shore, it might be done.", "\"저 기슭을 따라 텅 빈 채 놓인 그 천 척의 배 가운데 한 척만 내게 있다면, 해 볼 수도 있겠소만."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["here will soon be an end of the firing, for yonder comes a fog that will turn day to night, and make an Indian arrow more dangerous than a moulded cannon.", "저기 낮을 밤으로 바꿀, 그리고 인디언의 화살을 주조된 대포보다 더 위험하게 만들 안개가 오고 있으니, 곧 포격도 끝이 나겠소."], ["Now, if you are equal to the work, and will follow, I will make a push; for I long to get down into that camp, if it be only to scatter some Mingo dogs that I see lurking in the skirts of yonder thicket of birch.\"", "자, 만일 그 일을 감당할 수 있고 따라오겠다면, 내가 한번 밀고 나가 보겠소. 나는 저 자작나무 덤불 가장자리에 숨어 도사린 게 보이는 〈밍고〉족 개들 몇 마리를 흩어 놓기 위해서라도, 저 진영으로 내려가고 싶어 좀이 쑤시니까.\""]], [["\"We are equal,\"", "\"우리는 감당할 수 있어요.\""], ["said Cora, firmly: \"on such an errand we will follow to any danger.\"", "〈코라〉가 단호히 말했다. \"그런 임무라면 어떤 위험에라도 따르겠어요.\""]], [["The scout turned to her with a smile of honest and cordial approbation as he answered,--", "정찰병은 정직하고 따뜻한 칭찬의 미소를 지으며 그녀를 돌아보고는 대답했다."]], [["\"I would I had a thousand men, of brawny limbs and quick eyes, that feared death as little as you!", "\"당신만큼이나 죽음을 가벼이 여기는, 억센 팔다리와 빠른 눈을 지닌 사내가 내게 천 명만 있다면!"], ["I'd send them jabbering Frenchers back into their den again, afore the week was ended, howling like so many fettered hounds or hungry wolves.", "한 주가 가기 전에, 저 지껄여 대는 프랑스인들을, 사슬에 묶인 사냥개나 굶주린 늑대 떼처럼 울부짖게 하여 제 굴 속으로 도로 쫓아 보낼 텐데."], ["But stir,\"", "허나 어서 움직이시오.\""], ["he added, turning from her to the rest of the party, \"the fog comes rolling down so fast, we shall have but just the time to meet it on the plain, and use it as a cover.", "그가 그녀에게서 나머지 일행 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 덧붙였다. \"안개가 어찌나 빠르게 굴러 내려오는지, 평지에서 그것을 맞아 엄폐물로 쓸 시간이 겨우 있을 뿐이오."], ["Remember, if any accident should befall me, to keep the air blowing on your left cheeks--or rather, follow the Mohicans; they'd scent their way, be it in day or be it at night.\"", "명심하시오, 만일 내게 무슨 변고가 닥치거든, 바람이 당신들의 왼뺨에 불도록 하시오—아니, 차라리 모히칸족을 따르시오. 그들은 낮이든 밤이든 냄새로 제 길을 찾아낼 테니.\""]], [["He then waved his hand for them to follow, and threw himself down the steep declivity, with free, but careful footsteps.", "그러고는 따라오라 손을 저으며, 자유로우나 조심스러운 걸음으로 가파른 비탈 아래로 몸을 던졌다."], ["Heyward assisted the sisters to descend, and in a few minutes they were all far down a mountain whose sides they had climbed with so much toil and pain.", "〈헤이워드〉가 자매가 내려가는 것을 도왔고, 몇 분 만에 그들은 모두, 그토록 큰 노고와 고통으로 그 비탈을 올랐던 산 아래로 멀찍이 내려가 있었다."]], [["The direction taken by Hawkeye soon brought the travellers to the level of the plain, nearly opposite to a sally-port in the western curtain of the fort, which lay, itself, at the distance of about half a mile from the point where he halted to allow Duncan to come up with his charge.", "〈호크아이〉가 잡은 방향은 곧 여행자들을 평지의 높이로 데려왔으니, 요새 서쪽 성벽의 출격문과 거의 마주한 자리였다. 그 출격문 자체는, 그가 〈덩컨〉이 일행을 데리고 따라오기를 기다리려 멈춰 선 지점에서 반 마일쯤 떨어져 있었다."], ["In their eagerness, and favored by the nature of the ground, they had anticipated the fog, which was rolling heavily down the lake, and it became necessary to pause, until the mists had wrapped the camp of the enemy in their fleecy mantle.", "그들은 조급함에, 그리고 지형의 도움을 받아, 호수를 따라 묵직하게 굴러 내려오던 안개보다 먼저 와 버렸고, 그래서 안개가 적의 진영을 그 솜털 같은 외투로 감쌀 때까지 멈추어 있어야 했다."], ["The Mohicans profited by the delay, to steal out of the woods, and to make a survey of surrounding objects.", "모히칸족은 이 지체를 틈타 숲에서 살그머니 나가 주위의 사물들을 정찰했다."], ["They were followed at a little distance by the scout, with a view to profit early by their report, and to obtain some faint knowledge for himself of the more immediate localities.", "정찰병은, 그들의 보고를 일찌감치 활용하고, 자신도 더 가까운 지형을 어렴풋이나마 알아 두려는 뜻으로, 조금 떨어져 그들의 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["In a very few moments he returned, his face reddened with vexation, while he muttered his disappointment in words of no very gentle import.", "이내 그가 돌아왔으니, 얼굴이 분함으로 붉어진 채, 그다지 점잖지 못한 말로 제 낙담을 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Here has the cunning Frenchman been posting a picket directly in our path,\"", "\"저 교활한 프랑스인이 우리 길목 바로 위에 보초를 세워 놓았소.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"redskins and whites; and we shall be as likely to fall into their midst as to pass them in the fog!\"", "\"붉은 피부든 흰 피부든 말이오. 우리는 안개 속에서 그들을 지나치기는커녕, 그들 한가운데로 떨어질 공산이 크겠소!\""]], [["\"Cannot we make a circuit to avoid the danger,\"", "\"그 위험을 피하도록 돌아갔다가,\""], ["asked Heyward, \"and come into our path again when it is passed?\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다. \"지나친 뒤 다시 우리 길로 들어설 수는 없소?\""]], [["\"Who that once bends from the line of his march in a fog can tell when or how to turn to find it again!", "\"안개 속에서 일단 제 행군의 선에서 벗어나면, 언제 어떻게 돌아서야 그것을 다시 찾는지 누가 알겠소!"], ["The mists of Horican are not like the curls from a peace-pipe, or the smoke which settles above a mosquito fire.\"", "〈호리컨〉의 안개는 화친 담뱃대에서 피어오르는 연기 가닥이나, 모기 쫓는 불 위에 내려앉는 연기 같은 것이 아니오.\""]], [["He was yet speaking, when a crashing sound was heard, and a cannon-ball entered the thicket, striking the body of a sapling, and rebounding to the earth, its force being much expended by previous resistance.", "그가 아직 말하고 있을 때, 무언가 부서지는 소리가 들리더니, 포탄 하나가 덤불 속으로 날아들어 어린나무의 몸통을 때리고 땅으로 튕겨 나왔으니, 그 힘이 앞선 저항에 거의 다 소진된 뒤였다."], ["The Indians followed instantly like busy attendants on the terrible messenger, and Uncas commenced speaking earnestly and with much action, in the Delaware tongue.", "인디언들이 그 무시무시한 전령을 분주히 시중드는 종복들처럼 곧장 뒤따랐고, 〈언카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 열렬히, 그리고 많은 몸짓을 곁들여 말하기 시작했다."]], [["\"It may be so, lad,\"", "\"그럴 수도 있겠다, 얘야.\""], ["muttered the scout, when he had ended; \"for desperate fevers are not to be treated like a toothache.", "그가 말을 마치자 정찰병이 중얼거렸다. \"절박한 열병은 치통 다루듯 다룰 게 아니니."], ["Come, then, the fog is shutting in.\"", "자, 그러면, 안개가 닥쳐오고 있소.\""]], [["\"Stop!\"", "\"멈추시오!\""], ["cried Heyward; \"first explain your expectations.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤다. \"먼저 당신 속셈을 설명하시오.\""]], [["\"'Tis soon done, and a small hope it is; but it is better than nothing.", "\"금세 끝날 이야기요, 그리고 작은 희망이긴 하나, 없는 것보다는 낫소."], ["This shot that you see,\"", "당신이 보는 이 포탄이,\""], ["added the scout, kicking the harmless iron with his foot, \"has ploughed the 'arth in its road from the fort, and we shall hunt for the furrow it has made, when all other signs may fail.", "정찰병이 그 무해한 쇳덩이를 발로 차며 덧붙였다. \"요새에서 날아오는 길에 땅을 갈아 놓았으니, 다른 모든 표지가 다 사라져도, 우리는 그것이 낸 고랑을 좇으면 되오."], ["No more words, but follow, or the fog may leave us in the middle of our path, a mark for both armies to shoot at.\"", "더는 말 말고, 따라오시오. 안 그러면 안개가 우리를 길 한복판에, 양쪽 군대가 다 쏘아 대는 과녁으로 버려둘지도 모르오.\""]], [["Heyward perceiving that, in fact, a crisis had arrived when acts were more required than words, placed himself between the sisters, and drew them swiftly forward, keeping the dim figure of their leader in his eye.", "〈헤이워드〉는, 실로 말보다 행동이 더 요구되는 위기가 닥쳤음을 깨닫고, 자매 사이에 자리를 잡고 그들을 재빨리 앞으로 이끌며, 안내자의 흐릿한 모습을 눈에서 놓치지 않았다."], ["It was soon apparent that Hawkeye had not magnified the power of the fog, for before they had proceeded twenty yards, it was difficult for the different individuals of the party to distinguish each other, in the vapor.", "〈호크아이〉가 안개의 위력을 부풀려 말한 것이 아님은 곧 분명해졌으니, 그들이 스무 야드도 채 나아가기 전에, 일행의 서로 다른 사람들이 그 안개 속에서 서로를 분간하기조차 어려워졌다."]], [["They had made their little circuit to the left, and were already inclining again towards the right, having, as Heyward thought, got over nearly half the distance to the friendly works, when his ears were saluted with the fierce summons, apparently within twenty feet of them, of--", "그들은 왼쪽으로 작게 돌았다가, 이미 다시 오른쪽으로 기울고 있었으니, 〈헤이워드〉의 생각으로는 아군의 진지까지 거리의 거의 절반을 지나왔을 무렵, 그의 귀에 사나운 호령이 와 닿았으니, 그것은 그들에게서 스무 피트도 안 되는 곳에서 들려오는 듯한—"]], [["\"Qui va la?\"", "\"누가 가느냐?\""]], [["\"Push on!\"", "\"밀고 나가시오!\""], ["whispered the scout, once more bending to the left.", "정찰병이 다시 한 번 왼쪽으로 꺾으며 속삭였다."]], [["\"Push on!\"", "\"밀고 나가시오!\""], ["repeated Heyward; when the summons was renewed by a dozen voices, each of which seemed charged with menace.", "〈헤이워드〉가 되풀이했다. 그러자 그 호령이 열두 명의 목소리로 새로워졌으니, 저마다 위협이 가득 실린 듯했다."]], [["\"C'est moi,\"", "\"나다.\""], ["cried Duncan, dragging, rather than leading those he supported, swiftly onward.", "〈덩컨〉이 외치며, 자신이 부축하던 이들을 이끈다기보다 끌다시피 재빨리 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["\"Bete!--qui?--moi!\"", "\"멍청한 놈!—누구냐?—나라니!\""]], [["\"Ami de la France.\"", "\"프랑스의 벗이다.\""]], [["\"Tu m'as plus l'air d'un _ennemi_ de la France; arrete!", "\"넌 내게 영락없이 프랑스의 적으로 보인다. 멈춰라!"], ["ou pardieu je te ferai ami du diable.", "안 그러면 맹세코 네놈을 악마의 벗으로 만들어 주마."], ["Non!", "안 돼!"], ["feu, camarades, feu!\"", "쏴라, 전우들, 쏴!\""]], [["The order was instantly obeyed, and the fog was stirred by the explosion of fifty muskets.", "명령은 즉각 받들어졌고, 안개는 쉰 자루의 머스킷이 터지는 소리에 흔들렸다."], ["Happily, the aim was bad, and the bullets cut the air in a direction a little different from that taken by the fugitives; though still so nigh them, that to the unpractised ears of David and the two females, it appeared as if they whistled within a few inches of the organs.", "다행히 조준이 빗나가, 총알들은 도망자들이 잡은 방향과 조금 다른 쪽으로 공기를 가르고 지나갔으나, 그래도 그들에게 너무 가까워, 〈데이비드〉와 두 여인의 익숙지 못한 귀에는 마치 총알이 귀에서 몇 인치 안쪽을 휘파람 소리를 내며 지나가는 듯했다."], ["The outcry was renewed, and the order, not only to fire again, but to pursue, was too plainly audible.", "함성이 새로워졌고, 다시 쏘라는 것뿐 아니라 추격하라는 명령마저 너무도 또렷이 들렸다."], ["When Heyward briefly explained the meaning of the words they heard, Hawkeye halted, and spoke with quick decision and great firmness.", "〈헤이워드〉가 그들이 들은 말의 뜻을 짤막하게 설명하자, 〈호크아이〉가 멈춰 서서, 빠른 결단과 큰 단호함으로 말했다."]], [["\"Let us deliver our fire,\"", "\"우리도 한 차례 쏩시다.\""], ["he said; \"they will believe it a sortie, and give way, or they will wait for reinforcements.\"", "그가 말했다. \"그들은 그것을 출격으로 여겨 길을 비키거나, 아니면 증원을 기다릴 거요.\""]], [["The scheme was well conceived, but failed in its effect.", "그 책략은 잘 구상되었으나, 그 효과는 빗나갔다."], ["The instant the French heard the pieces, it seemed as if the plain was alive with men, muskets rattling along its whole extent, from the shores of the lake to the farthest boundary of the woods.", "프랑스군이 총소리를 들은 순간, 마치 평지가 사람들로 살아 움직이는 듯하더니, 머스킷 소리가 호수의 기슭에서 숲의 가장 먼 경계까지 그 너른 전역을 따라 절거덕거렸다."]], [["\"We shall draw their entire army upon us, and bring on a general assault,\"", "\"우리가 그들의 군대 전체를 끌어들여, 전면적인 공격을 불러오겠소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 말했다."], ["said Duncan: \"lead on, my friend, for your own life, and ours.\"", "\"앞장서시오, 친구여, 당신 목숨과 우리 목숨을 위해서.\""]], [["The scout seemed willing to comply; but, in the hurry of the moment, and in the change of position, he had lost the direction.", "정찰병은 따를 마음이 있어 보였으나, 그 황망한 순간에, 그리고 자리를 옮기는 통에, 방향을 잃어버렸다."], ["In vain he turned either cheek towards the light air; they felt equally cool.", "그가 어느 쪽 뺨이든 가벼운 바람을 향해 돌려 보았으나 헛수고였으니, 양쪽 다 똑같이 서늘하게 느껴졌다."], ["In this dilemma, Uncas lighted on the furrow of the cannon-ball, where it had cut the ground in three adjacent ant-hills.", "이 곤경 속에서, 〈언카스〉가 포탄이 인접한 세 개의 개미집을 가르며 땅을 파 놓은 고랑을 찾아냈다."]], [["\"Give me the range!\"", "\"방향을 일러 다오!\""], ["said Hawkeye, bending to catch a glimpse of the direction, and then instantly moving onward.", "〈호크아이〉가 그 방향을 흘끗 잡으려 몸을 굽히며 말하더니, 곧장 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["Cries, oaths, voices calling to each other, and the reports of muskets, were now quick and incessant, and, apparently, on every side of them.", "외침과 욕설, 서로를 부르는 목소리, 그리고 머스킷의 총성이 이제 빠르고 끊임없이, 그리고 보아하니 그들의 사방에서 일었다."], ["Suddenly, a strong glare of light flashed across the scene, the fog rolled upwards in thick wreaths, and several cannon belched across the plain, and the roar was thrown heavily back from the bellowing echoes of the mountain.", "갑자기 강한 빛이 광경을 번쩍 가로지르더니, 안개가 두꺼운 화환을 그리며 위로 솟구쳤고, 여러 문의 대포가 평지를 가로질러 토해 냈으며, 그 굉음이 산의 울부짖는 메아리로부터 묵직하게 되돌아왔다."]], [["\"'Tis from the fort!\"", "\"요새에서 쏘는 것이오!\""], ["exclaimed Hawkeye, turning short on his tracks; \"and we, like stricken fools, were rushing to the woods, under the very knives of the Maquas.\"", "〈호크아이〉가 제 자취 위에서 홱 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"그런데 우리는, 얻어맞은 바보들처럼, 〈마콰〉족의 칼날 바로 밑으로 숲을 향해 돌진하고 있었구려.\""]], [["The instant their mistake was rectified, the whole party retraced the error with the utmost diligence.", "그들의 실수가 바로잡히는 순간, 일행 모두가 더없는 부지런함으로 그 잘못을 되짚어 갔다."], ["Duncan willingly relinquished the support of Cora to the arm of Uncas, and Cora as readily accepted the welcome assistance.", "〈덩컨〉은 기꺼이 〈코라〉의 부축을 〈언카스〉의 팔에 넘겼고, 〈코라〉 또한 기꺼이 그 반가운 도움을 받아들였다."], ["Men, hot and angry in pursuit, were evidently on their footsteps, and each instant threatened their capture, if not their destruction.", "추격에 달아오르고 성난 자들이 분명 그들의 발자취에 바짝 붙어 있었으니, 매 순간 그들의 파멸은 아닐지라도 사로잡힘이 위협이 되었다."]], [["\"Point de quartier aux coquins!\"", "\"저 악당들에게 자비는 없다!\""], ["cried an eager pursuer, who seemed to direct the operations of the enemy.", "적의 작전을 지휘하는 듯한 한 열렬한 추격자가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Stand firm, and be ready, my gallant 60ths!\"", "\"굳게 서서, 준비하라, 나의 용감한 60연대여!\""], ["suddenly exclaimed a voice above them; \"wait to see the enemy,--fire low, and sweep the glacis.\"", "갑자기 그들 위쪽에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다. \"적이 보일 때까지 기다려라—낮게 쏘아, 비탈을 쓸어 버려라.\""]], [["\"Father!", "\"아버지!"], ["father!\"", "아버지!\""], ["exclaimed a piercing cry from out the mist; \"it is I!", "안개 속에서 가슴을 찌르는 외침이 터져 나왔다. \"저예요!"], ["Alice!", "〈앨리스〉!"], ["thy own Elsie!", "아버지의 〈엘시〉예요!"], ["spare, O!", "살려 주세요, 오!"], ["save your daughters!\"", "당신의 딸들을 구해 주세요!\""]], [["\"Hold!\"", "\"멈춰라!\""], ["shouted the former speaker, in the awful tones of parental agony, the sound reaching even to the woods, and rolling back in solemn echo.", "앞서의 그 목소리가 외쳤으니, 부모의 고뇌가 담긴 그 무시무시한 음성이 숲에까지 닿아 엄숙한 메아리로 되울려 왔다."], ["\"'Tis she!", "\"그 애로구나!"], ["God has restored me my children!", "신께서 내 아이들을 돌려주셨다! 출격문을 열어라."], ["Throw open the sally-port; to the field, 60ths, to the field; pull not a trigger, lest ye kill my lambs!", "들판으로, 60연대여, 들판으로. 방아쇠를 당기지 마라, 내 어린양들을 죽일라!"], ["Drive off these dogs of France with your steel.\"", "저 프랑스의 개들을 너희 강철로 몰아내라.\""]], [["Duncan heard the grating of the rusty hinges, and darting to the spot, directed by the sound, he met a long line of dark red warriors, passing swiftly towards the glacis.", "〈덩컨〉은 녹슨 돌쩌귀가 삐걱대는 소리를 들었고, 그 소리에 이끌려 그 자리로 내달려, 비탈을 향해 재빠르게 나아가는 검붉은 전사들의 긴 행렬을 만났다."], ["He knew them for his own battalion of the royal Americans, and flying to their head, soon swept every trace of his pursuers from before the works.", "그는 그들이 자신이 속한 왕립 아메리카 대대임을 알아보았고, 그 선두로 날아가, 곧 요새 앞에서 추격자들의 자취를 남김없이 쓸어 냈다."]], [["For an instant, Cora and Alice had stood trembling and bewildered by this unexpected desertion; but, before either had leisure for speech, or even thought, an officer of gigantic frame whose locks were bleached with years and service, but whose air of military grandeur had been rather softened than destroyed by time, rushed out of the body of the mist, and folded them to his bosom, while large scalding tears rolled down his pale and wrinkled cheeks, and he exclaimed, in the peculiar accent of Scotland,--", "한순간, 〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉는 이 뜻밖의 버려짐에 떨며 어쩔 줄 모른 채 서 있었다. 그러나 둘 중 누구도 말할, 아니 생각할 겨를조차 갖기 전에, 거구의 한 장교가—세월과 복무로 머리는 세었으나, 군인다운 위엄의 기풍은 시간에 의해 무너졌다기보다 오히려 부드러워진—안개의 덩어리에서 뛰쳐나와 그들을 제 품에 끌어안았으니, 굵고 뜨거운 눈물이 그의 창백하고 주름진 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 그는 스코틀랜드 특유의 억양으로 외쳤다. \"이를 위해 당신께 감사드립니다, 주여! 위험이 어떻게 닥쳐온다 한들, 당신의 종은 이제 준비가 되었나이다!\""]], [["\"For this I thank thee, Lord!", "* 60연대: 본문의 \"60ths\"는 영국군 제60보병연대, 곧 왕립 아메리카 연대를 가리킨다."], ["Let danger come as it will, thy servant is now prepared!\"", "\"그럼 우리 들어가, 그가 전할 말을 들어 보세."]], [["\"Then go we in, to know his embassy; Which I could, with ready guess, declare, Before the Frenchman speak a word of it.\"", "프랑스인이 그 일을 한마디라도 꺼내기 전에,\n나는 망설임 없이 알아맞혀 말할 수 있으리니.\""]], [["_King Henry V._", "『헨리 5세』"]], [["A few succeeding days were passed amid the privations, the uproar, and the dangers of the siege, which was vigorously pressed by a power against whose approaches Munro possessed no competent means of resistance.", "이어지는 며칠은 포위 공격의 궁핍과 소란, 위험 속에 지나갔으니, 그 공격은 〈먼로〉가 막아 낼 변변한 수단을 갖지 못한 한 세력에 의해 맹렬히 밀어붙여졌다."], ["It appeared as if Webb, with his army, which lay slumbering on the banks of the Hudson, had utterly forgotten the strait to which his countrymen were reduced.", "마치 〈웨브〉가, 〈허드슨〉강 기슭에 잠든 듯 머무는 그의 군대와 더불어, 동포들이 처한 궁지를 까맣게 잊은 듯했다."], ["Montcalm had filled the woods of the portage with his savages, every yell and whoop from whom rang through the British encampment, chilling the hearts of men who were already but too much disposed to magnify the danger.", "〈몽칼름〉은 육로 운반로의 숲을 미개인들로 가득 채웠고, 그들이 내지르는 비명과 함성 하나하나가 〈영국〉군 진지에 울려 퍼져, 이미 위험을 지나치게 부풀려 헤아리는 데 너무도 기울어 있던 자들의 마음을 얼어붙게 했다."]], [["Not so, however, with the besieged.", "그러나 포위된 자들은 그렇지 않았다."], ["Animated by the words, and stimulated by the examples, of their leaders, they had found their courage, and maintained their ancient reputation, with zeal that did justice to the stern character of their commander.", "지휘관들의 말에 고무되고 그 본보기에 자극받아, 그들은 용기를 되찾았고, 자기네 지휘관의 준엄한 성품에 부끄럽지 않은 열의로 옛 명성을 지켜 냈다."], ["As if satisfied with the toil of marching through the wilderness to encounter his enemy, the French general, though of approved skill, had neglected to seize the adjacent mountains; whence the besieged might have been exterminated with impunity, and which, in the more modern warfare of the country, would not have been neglected for a single hour.", "적을 맞으러 황야를 행군하는 노고로 만족했다는 듯이, 프랑스 장군은 정평 난 솜씨를 지녔으면서도 인접한 산들을 점령하기를 게을리했으니, 그곳에서라면 포위된 자들을 무사히 몰살할 수 있었을 것이요, 이 고장의 보다 근대적인 전쟁이었다면 단 한 시간도 그냥 두지 않았을 곳이었다."], ["This sort of contempt for eminences, or rather dread of the labor of ascending them, might have been termed the besetting weakness of the warfare of the period.", "고지에 대한 이런 종류의 경멸, 아니 오히려 그곳을 오르는 노고에 대한 두려움은, 그 시대 전쟁의 고질적인 약점이라 할 만했다."], ["It originated in the simplicity of the Indian contests, in which, from the nature of the combats, and the density of the forests, fortresses were rare, and artillery next to useless.", "그것은 인디언 전투의 단순함에서 비롯되었으니, 그 싸움의 성격과 삼림의 빽빽함 탓에 요새는 드물었고 대포는 거의 쓸모가 없었다."], ["The carelessness engendered by these usages descended even to the war of the Revolution, and lost the States the important fortress of Ticonderoga, opening a way for the army of Burgoyne into what was then the bosom of the country.", "이런 관습이 빚어낸 부주의는 독립 전쟁에까지 이어져, 〈합중국〉이 〈타이콘더로가〉라는 중요한 요새를 잃고, 〈버고인〉의 군대가 당시 이 나라의 한복판으로 들어설 길을 열어 주는 결과를 낳았다."], ["We look back at this ignorance, or infatuation, whichever it may be called, with wonder, knowing that the neglect of an eminence, whose difficulties, like those of Mount Defiance, have been so greatly exaggerated, would, at the present time, prove fatal to the reputation of the engineer who had planned the works at their base, or to that of the general whose lot it was to defend them.", "우리는 이 무지를, 혹은 그것을 어떻게 부르든 그 맹목을 놀라움으로 돌아보거니와, 〈디파이언스산〉의 험준함이 그토록 크게 과장된 것처럼 어느 고지를 소홀히 한다면 오늘날에는 그 발치에 진지를 설계한 공병의 명성에든, 그것을 지킬 운명을 진 장군의 명성에든 치명적임을 우리는 알고 있다."]], [["The tourist, the valetudinarian, or the amateur of the beauties of nature, who, in the train of his four-in-hand, now rolls through the scenes we have attempted to describe, in quest of information, health, or pleasure, or floats steadily towards his object on those artificial waters which have sprung up under the administration of a statesman[21] who has dared to stake his political character on the hazardous issue, is not to suppose that his ancestors traversed those hills, or struggled with the same currents with equal facility.", "이제 사두마차 행렬에 실려, 식견이나 건강이나 즐거움을 좇아, 우리가 묘사하려 애쓴 이 풍경 사이를 굴러가는 여행객이나 요양객이나 자연미의 애호가, 혹은 위태로운 결말에 제 정치 생명을 걸 만큼 대담했던 어느 정치가의 치세 아래 솟아난 그 인공의 수로를 따라 목적지로 유유히 떠가는 이는, 자기 선조들이 그 언덕들을 그만한 수월함으로 가로지르거나 같은 물살과 그만큼 쉽게 씨름했으리라고 짐작해서는 안 된다."], ["The transportation of a single heavy gun was often considered equal to a victory gained; if, happily, the difficulties of the passage had not so far separated it from its necessary concomitant, the ammunition, as to render it no more than an useless tube of unwieldy iron.", "무거운 대포 한 문을 옮기는 일은 흔히 거둔 승리 하나와 맞먹는 것으로 여겨졌으니, 다만 길의 험난함이 그것을 필수의 동반물인 탄약과 너무 멀리 갈라놓아, 결국 거추장스러운 무쇠 관 한 개로 만들어 버리지 않았을 경우에 한해서였다."]], [["The evils of this state of things pressed heavily on the fortunes of the resolute Scotsman who now defended William Henry.", "이런 형편의 폐해는 이제 〈윌리엄 헨리〉를 지키는 단호한 스코틀랜드인의 운명을 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["Though his adversary neglected the hills, he had planted his batteries with judgment on the plain, and caused them to be served with vigor and skill.", "그의 적수는 산들을 소홀히 했으되, 평지에는 분별 있게 포대를 배치했고, 그것을 활기차고 능란하게 부리도록 했다."], ["Against this assault, the besieged could only oppose the imperfect and hasty preparations of a fortress in the wilderness.", "이 공격에 맞서, 포위된 자들이 내놓을 수 있는 것이라곤 황야 속 한 요새의 불완전하고 서두른 채비뿐이었다."]], [["It was in the afternoon of the fifth day of the siege, and the fourth of his own service in it, that Major Heyward profited by a parley that had just been beaten, by repairing to the ramparts of one of the water bastions, to breathe the cool air from the lake, and to take a survey of the progress of the siege.", "포위 닷새째, 그가 직접 그 일에 가담한 지 나흘째 되던 오후, 〈헤이워드〉 소령은 방금 울린 휴전을 틈타, 호수에서 불어오는 서늘한 바람을 쐬고 포위전의 진척을 둘러보려고 물가 보루 가운데 하나의 흉벽으로 올라갔다."], ["He was alone, if the solitary sentinel who paced the mound be excepted; for the artillerists had hastened also to profit by the temporary suspension of their arduous duties.", "둔덕을 거니는 외로운 보초 하나를 빼면 그는 혼자였으니, 포병들 역시 힘든 임무가 잠시 멈춘 틈을 서둘러 누리고 있었기 때문이다."], ["The evening was delightfully calm, and the light air from the limpid water fresh and soothing.", "저녁은 더없이 고요했고, 맑은 물에서 불어오는 산들바람은 신선하고 마음을 달래 주었다."], ["It seemed as if, with the termination to the roar of artillery and the plunging of shot, nature had also seized the moment to assume her mildest and most captivating form.", "마치 포성과 포탄이 떨어지는 소리가 그친 것과 더불어, 자연도 그 가장 온화하고 매혹적인 모습을 띠기 위해 그 순간을 붙잡은 듯했다."], ["The sun poured down his parting glory on the scene, without the oppression of those fierce rays that belong to the climate and the season.", "해는 이 풍경 위로 작별의 영광을 쏟아 부었으니, 이 기후와 계절 특유의 사나운 햇살이 안기는 압박은 없었다."], ["The mountains looked green and fresh and lovely; tempered with the milder light, or softened in shadow, as thin vapors floated between them and the sun.", "산들은 푸르고 싱그럽고 사랑스러워 보였으니, 더 부드러운 빛으로 누그러지거나, 산과 해 사이를 떠도는 엷은 안개에 그늘로 부드러워졌다."], ["The numerous islands rested on the bosom of the Horican, some low and sunken, as if imbedded in the waters, and others appearing to hover above the element, in little hillocks of green velvet; among which the fishermen of the beleaguering army peacefully rowed their skiffs, or floated at rest on the glassy mirror, in quiet pursuit of their employment.", "무수한 섬들이 〈호리컨〉의 품에 쉬고 있었으니, 더러는 낮게 가라앉아 마치 물속에 묻힌 듯했고, 더러는 푸른 벨벳의 작은 둔덕이 되어 수면 위에 떠 있는 듯 보였다. 그 사이로 포위군의 어부들은 한가로이 작은 배를 저었고, 혹은 유리 같은 거울 위에 멈춰 떠서 조용히 제 일을 좇았다."]], [["The scene was at once animated and still.", "이 풍경은 생기에 차 있으면서도 고요했다."], ["All that pertained to nature was sweet, or simply grand; while those parts which depended on the temper and movements of man were lively and playful.", "자연에 속한 모든 것은 감미롭거나 단순히 장엄했고, 인간의 기분과 움직임에 달린 부분은 활기차고 흥겨웠다."]], [["Two little spotless flags were abroad, the one on a salient angle of the fort, and the other on the advanced battery of the besiegers; emblems of the truce which existed, not only to the acts, but it would seem, also, to the enmity of the combatants.", "티 없이 흰 작은 깃발 두 개가 펄럭이고 있었으니, 하나는 요새의 돌출한 모서리에, 다른 하나는 포위군의 전진 포대에 꽂혀, 교전자들의 행위뿐 아니라, 보아하니 그 적의까지도 멈추게 한 휴전의 표상이었다."]], [["Behind these, again, swung, heavily opening and closing in silken folds, the rival standards of England and France.", "그 뒤로는 다시, 〈영국〉과 〈프랑스〉의 맞선 군기가 비단 자락으로 묵직하게 펴졌다 접혔다 하며 나부꼈다."]], [["A hundred gay and thoughtless young Frenchmen were drawing a net to the pebbly beach, within dangerous proximity to the sullen but silent cannon of the fort, while the eastern mountain was sending back the loud shouts and gay merriment that attended their sport.", "밝고 분별없는 젊은 프랑스인 백여 명이 요새의 시무룩하나 잠잠한 대포에 위험천만하게 가까운 자갈밭 해변으로 그물을 끌어 올리는 동안, 동쪽 산이 그들의 놀이에 따른 우렁찬 함성과 흥겨운 떠들썩함을 되돌려 보냈다."], ["Some were rushing eagerly to enjoy the aquatic games of the lake, and others were already toiling their way up the neighboring hills, with the restless curiosity of their nation.", "더러는 호수의 물놀이를 즐기려 앞다투어 달려갔고, 더러는 자기 민족 특유의 가만있지 못하는 호기심으로 이웃한 언덕들을 힘겹게 오르고 있었다."], ["To all these sports and pursuits, those of the enemy who watched the besieged, and the besieged themselves, were, however, merely the idle, though sympathizing spectators.", "그러나 이 모든 놀이와 일에 대해, 포위된 자들을 지켜보던 적군이나 포위된 자들 자신이나, 그저 한가로이 공감하며 바라보는 구경꾼일 따름이었다."], ["Here and there a picket had, indeed, raised a song, or mingled in a dance, which had drawn the dusky savages around them, from their lairs in the forest.", "여기저기서 보초 하나가 노래를 부르거나 춤판에 끼어들기도 했으니, 그것이 숲속 굴에서 거뭇한 미개인들을 그들 주위로 끌어내었다."], ["In short, everything wore rather the appearance of a day of pleasure, than of an hour stolen from the dangers and toil of a bloody and vindictive warfare.", "요컨대, 모든 것이 피비린내 나고 보복에 찬 전쟁의 위험과 노고에서 훔쳐 낸 한때라기보다는, 오히려 어느 즐거운 하루의 모습을 띠고 있었다."]], [["Duncan had stood in a musing attitude, contemplating this scene a few minutes, when his eyes were directed to the glacis in front of the sally-port already mentioned, by the sounds of approaching footsteps.", "〈던컨〉은 생각에 잠긴 자세로 잠시 이 풍경을 바라보며 서 있었으니, 다가오는 발소리에 그의 눈이 앞서 말한 출격문 앞의 비탈로 향했다."], ["He walked to an angle of the bastion, and beheld the scout advancing, under the custody of a French officer, to the body of the fort.", "그는 보루의 한 모서리로 걸어가, 한 프랑스 장교의 감시 아래 척후가 요새 본체로 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["The countenance of Hawkeye was haggard and careworn, and his air dejected, as though he felt the deepest degradation at having fallen into the power of his enemies.", "〈호크아이〉의 얼굴은 초췌하고 근심에 절었으며, 그 태도는 풀이 죽어 있었으니, 마치 적의 손아귀에 떨어진 것에 더없이 깊은 치욕을 느끼는 듯했다."], ["He was without his favorite weapon, and his arms were even bound behind him with thongs, made of the skin of a deer.", "그는 그가 아끼는 무기도 없이, 두 팔마저 사슴 가죽으로 만든 가죽끈에 등 뒤로 묶여 있었다."], ["The arrival of flags, to cover the messengers of summons, had occurred so often of late, that when Heyward first threw his careless glance on this group, he expected to see another of the officers of the enemy, charged with a similar office; but the instant he recognized the tall person, and still sturdy, though downcast features of his friend the woodsman, he started with surprise, and turned to descend from the bastion into the bosom of the work.", "항복 권고의 사자를 호위하는 깃발의 도착이 근래 너무도 잦았던 터라, 〈헤이워드〉가 이 무리에 처음 무심한 눈길을 던졌을 때는 비슷한 임무를 띤 또 다른 적군 장교를 보리라 여겼다. 그러나 키 큰 풍채와, 풀 죽은 와중에도 여전히 다부진 그 숲사람 친구의 이목구비를 알아본 순간, 그는 놀라 움찔하더니, 보루에서 진지 안으로 내려가려고 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["The sounds of other voices, however, caught his attention, and for a moment caused him to forget his purpose.", "그러나 다른 목소리들이 그의 주의를 끌어, 잠시 그 의도를 잊게 했다."], ["At the inner angle of the mound he met the sisters, walking along the parapet in search, like himself, of air and relief from confinement.", "둔덕 안쪽 모서리에서 그는 자매를 만났으니, 그들 또한 그처럼 바람을 쐬고 갇힌 데서 벗어나려 흉벽을 따라 거닐던 참이었다."], ["They had not met from that painful moment when he deserted them on the plain, only to assure their safety.", "그가 그들의 안전을 확인하려고만 평원에서 그들을 버리고 떠났던 그 고통스러운 순간 이래로 그들은 만난 적이 없었다."], ["He had parted from them worn with care, and jaded with fatigue; he now saw them refreshed and blooming, though timid and anxious.", "그는 근심에 닳고 피로에 지친 채 그들과 헤어졌으나, 이제 그들은 새로워지고 활짝 피었음을 보았으니, 다만 겁먹고 불안해하는 기색이었다."], ["Under such an inducement, it will cause no surprise that the young man lost sight, for a time, of other objects in order to address them.", "이런 유인이 있는 터라, 젊은이가 그들에게 말을 건네려고 한동안 다른 일들을 잊었다 한들 놀랄 일은 아니다."], ["He was, however, anticipated by the voice of the ingenuous and youthful Alice.", "그러나 천진하고 앳된 〈앨리스〉의 목소리가 그를 앞질렀다."]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["thou truant!", "이 게으름뱅이!"], ["thou recreant knight!", "이 비겁한 기사님!"], ["he who abandons his damsels in the very lists!\"", "바로 결투장에서 제 아가씨들을 버리고 가신 분!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried; \"here have we been days, nay, ages, expecting you at our feet, imploring mercy and forgetfulness of your craven backsliding, or, I should rather say, back-running--for verily you fled in a manner that no stricken deer, as our worthy friend the scout would say, could equal!\"", "\"우리는 여기서 며칠을, 아니 몇 세월을, 당신이 우리 발치에 와서 그 겁쟁이 같은 배신을, 아니 차라리 줄행랑이라 해야겠네요 — 정말이지 당신은, 우리의 훌륭한 친구 척후 양반 말마따나, 그 어떤 화살 맞은 사슴도 따라잡지 못할 모양으로 달아나셨으니까요! — 그 줄행랑의 용서와 망각을 빌어 주기를 기다려 왔답니다!\""]], [["\"You know that Alice means our thanks and our blessings,\"", "\"〈앨리스〉가 말하려는 건 우리의 감사와 축복이라는 걸 아시지요.\""], ["added the graver and more thoughtful Cora.", "더 진중하고 사려 깊은 〈코라〉가 덧붙였다."], ["\"In truth, we have a little wondered why you should so rigidly absent yourself from a place where the gratitude of the daughters might receive the support of a parent's thanks.\"", "\"사실, 우리는 좀 의아했어요. 딸들의 감사에 아버지의 사례까지 보탤 수 있는 곳에서 어찌 그리도 한사코 모습을 보이지 않으셨는지 말이에요.\""]], [["\"Your father himself could tell you, that though absent from your presence, I have not been altogether forgetful of your safety,\"", "\"아버님께서 직접 말씀해 주실 수 있을 겁니다. 비록 두 분 곁을 떠나 있었으되, 제가 두 분의 안전을 아주 잊었던 것은 아니라는 걸요.\""], ["returned the young man; \"the mastery of yonder village of huts,\"", "젊은이가 대답했다. \"저쪽 오두막 마을을,\""], ["pointing to the neighboring entrenched camp, \"has been keenly disputed; and he who holds it is sure to be possessed of this fort, and that which it contains.", "그가 이웃한 참호 진지를 가리키며 말했다. \"차지하려고 치열하게 다투어 왔으니, 그곳을 쥔 자가 이 요새와 그 안의 것을 손에 넣게 마련입니다."], ["My days and my nights have all been passed there since we separated, because I thought that duty called me thither.", "우리가 헤어진 뒤로 제 낮과 밤은 모두 그곳에서 보냈지요. 의무가 저를 그리로 부른다고 여겼으니까요."], ["But,\"", "하지만,\""], ["he added with an air of chagrin, which he endeavored, though unsuccessfully, to conceal, \"had I been aware that what I then believed a soldier's conduct could so be construed, shame would have been added to the list of reasons.\"", "그가 숨기려 했으나 끝내 숨기지 못한 분한 기색으로 덧붙였다. \"그때 군인의 도리라 믿었던 것이 그렇게 해석될 수 있음을 알았더라면, 그 이유들의 목록에 부끄러움까지 더해졌을 겁니다.\""]], [["\"Heyward!--Duncan!\" exclaimed Alice, bending forward to read his half-averted countenance, until a lock of her golden hair rested on her flushed cheek, and nearly concealed the tear that had started to her eye; \"did I think this idle tongue of mine had pained you, I would silence it forever, Cora can say, if Cora would, how justly we have prized your services, and how deep--I had almost said, how fervent--is our gratitude.\"", "\"〈헤이워드〉! — 〈던컨〉!\" 〈앨리스〉가 외치며, 반쯤 돌린 그의 얼굴을 읽으려고 몸을 앞으로 기울이니, 황금빛 머리카락 한 가닥이 발그레한 뺨에 드리워, 눈에 솟은 눈물을 거의 가려 버렸다. \"이 부질없는 제 혀가 당신을 아프게 했다고 여겼다면, 영영 입을 다물었을 거예요. 〈코라〉가, 〈코라〉만 말하려 든다면, 우리가 당신의 노고를 얼마나 정당하게 귀히 여겼는지, 우리의 감사가 얼마나 깊은지 — 아니, 얼마나 뜨거운지라고 할 뻔했네요 — 말해 줄 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["\"And will Cora attest the truth of this?\"", "\"〈코라〉가 이 말의 진실을 증언해 주겠소?\""], ["cried Duncan, suffering the cloud to be chased from his countenance by a smile of open pleasure.", "〈던컨〉이, 거리낌 없는 기쁨의 미소가 그 얼굴에서 먹구름을 몰아내도록 두며 외쳤다."], ["\"What says our graver sister?", "\"우리의 진중한 누님은 뭐라 하시려는지?"], ["Will she find an excuse for the neglect of the knight in the duty of a soldier?\"", "군인의 의무에서 기사의 태만을 변명해 줄 핑계를 찾아 주시려나?\""]], [["Cora made no immediate answer, but turned her face towards the water, as if looking on the sheet of the Horican.", "〈코라〉는 곧장 대답하지 않고, 마치 〈호리컨〉의 수면을 바라보듯 얼굴을 물 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["When she did bend her dark eyes on the young man, they were yet filled with an expression of anguish that at once drove every thought but that of kind solicitude from his mind.", "그녀가 마침내 그 검은 눈을 젊은이에게 돌렸을 때, 그 눈은 여전히 고뇌의 빛으로 가득 차, 그의 마음에서 다정한 염려 말고는 모든 생각을 단번에 몰아내었다."]], [["\"You are not well, dearest Miss Munro!\"", "\"몸이 편치 않으시군요, 친애하는 〈먼로〉 양!\""], ["he exclaimed; \"we have trifled while you are in suffering.\"", "그가 외쳤다. \"우리가 농담을 주고받는 동안 당신은 괴로워하고 있었군요.\""]], [["\"'Tis nothing,\"", "\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 그녀가 여인다운 절제로 그가 내민 부축을 사양하며 대답했다."], ["she answered, refusing his offered support with feminine reserve.", "\"이 꾸밈없으나 열정에 찬 애호가처럼,\" 그녀가 누이의 팔에 가볍게, 그러나 다정하게 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다."], ["\"That I cannot see the sunny side of the picture of life, like this artless but ardent enthusiast,\"", "\"제가 인생이라는 그림의 햇살 비치는 면을 보지 못하는 것은, 경험이 치르는 대가요, 어쩌면 제 천성의 불행이겠지요. 보세요,\""], ["she added, laying her hand lightly, but affectionately, on the arm of her sister, \"is the penalty of experience, and, perhaps, the misfortune of my nature.", "그녀가 의무감으로 약함을 떨쳐 내기로 작정한 듯 말을 이었다. \"둘러보세요, 〈헤이워드〉 소령님, 그리고 말해 주세요."], ["See,\" she continued, as if determined to shake off infirmity, in a sense of duty; \"look around you, Major Heyward, and tell me what a prospect is this for the daughter of a soldier whose greatest happiness is his honor and his military renown.\"", "명예와 무공을 더없는 행복으로 여기는 군인의 딸에게, 이것이 어떤 전망인지를요.\""]], [["\"Neither ought nor shall be tarnished by circumstances over which he has had no control,\"", "\"그 무엇도 그분이 어쩔 수 없었던 상황으로 인해 더럽혀져서는 안 되며, 그러지도 않을 겁니다.\""], ["Duncan warmly replied.", "〈던컨〉이 따뜻이 대답했다."], ["\"But your words recall me to my own duty.", "\"하지만 당신 말씀이 제 임무를 일깨우는군요."], ["I go now to your gallant father, to hear his determination in matters of the last moment to the defence.", "저는 이제 당신의 용맹한 아버님께 가서, 방어에 마지막으로 중대한 문제들에 관한 그분의 결단을 들으렵니다."], ["God bless you in every fortune, noble--Cora--I may and must call you.\"", "어떤 운명 속에서도 신의 가호가 있기를, 고귀한 — 〈코라〉 — 그렇게 불러도 되고, 또 불러야겠지요.\""], ["She frankly gave him her hand, though her lip quivered, and her cheeks gradually became of an ashy paleness.", "그녀가 선뜻 손을 내밀었으나, 입술은 떨렸고 뺨은 차츰 잿빛으로 창백해졌다. \"어떤 운명 속에서도, 당신은 당신 성(性)의 자랑이요 명예가 되리라는 걸 압니다."], ["\"In every fortune, I know you will be an ornament and honor to your sex.", "〈앨리스〉, 안녕히\" — 그의 어조가 찬미에서 다정함으로 바뀌었다 — \"안녕히, 〈앨리스〉."], ["Alice, adieu\"--his tone changed from admiration to tenderness--\"adieu, Alice; we shall soon meet again; as conquerors, I trust, and amid rejoicings!\"", "우리 곧 다시 만납시다. 그것도 정복자로서, 기쁨 속에서, 그러리라 믿어요!\""]], [["Without waiting for an answer from either, the young man threw himself down the grassy steps of the bastion, and moving rapidly across the parade, he was quickly in the presence of their father.", "어느 쪽의 대답도 기다리지 않고, 젊은이는 보루의 풀 덮인 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 연병장을 가로질러 재빨리 움직이더니, 이내 그들의 아버지 앞에 섰다."], ["Munro was pacing his narrow apartment with a disturbed air and gigantic strides as Duncan entered.", "〈던컨〉이 들어섰을 때 〈먼로〉는 어지러운 기색으로 거대한 걸음을 내디디며 그 비좁은 방을 거닐고 있었다."]], [["\"You have anticipated my wishes, Major Heyward,\"", "\"내 바람을 미리 헤아렸군, 〈헤이워드〉 소령.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"I was about to request this favor.\"", "\"마침 자네에게 이 부탁을 청하려던 참이었네.\""]], [["\"I am sorry to see, sir, that the messenger I so warmly recommended has returned in custody of the French!", "\"각하, 제가 그토록 따뜻이 추천한 사자가 프랑스군의 감시 아래 돌아온 것을 보니 안타깝습니다!"], ["I hope there is no reason to distrust his fidelity?\"", "그자의 충성을 의심할 까닭은 없겠지요?\""]], [["\"The fidelity of 'The Long Rifle'", "\"〈긴 소총〉의 충성은 내가 익히 아는 바일세.\""], ["is well known to me,\"", "〈먼로〉가 대답했다."], ["returned Munro, \"and is above suspicion; though his usual good fortune seems, at last, to have failed.", "\"의심할 여지가 없지. 다만 그자의 평소 행운이 끝내 떨어진 모양이야."], ["Montcalm has got him, and with the accursed politeness of his nation, he has sent him in with a doleful tale, of 'knowing how I valued the fellow, he could not think of retaining him.'", "〈몽칼름〉이 그를 붙잡았고, 제 나라 특유의 저 빌어먹을 정중함으로, '내가 그자를 얼마나 아끼는지 알기에, 차마 붙들어 둘 생각을 할 수 없었노라'는 구슬픈 사연과 함께 그를 들여보냈네."], ["A Jesuitical way, that, Major Duncan Heyward, of telling a man of his misfortunes!\"", "한 사내에게 제 불행을 일러 주는, 참으로 예수회다운 방식이지, 〈던컨 헤이워드〉 소령!\""]], [["\"But the general and his succor?\"", "\"하지만 장군과 그 원군은요?\""]], [["\"Did ye look to the south as ye entered, and could ye not see them?\"", "\"들어올 때 남쪽을 보았는가, 그들이 보이지 않던가?\""], ["said the old soldier, laughing bitterly.", "노병이 쓰디쓰게 웃으며 말했다."], ["\"Hoot!", "\"허!"], ["hoot!", "허!"], ["you're an impatient boy, sir, and cannot give the gentlemen leisure for their march!\"", "자네는 성급한 애송이야, 소령, 그 양반들에게 행군할 여유도 못 주는군!\""]], [["\"They are coming, then?", "\"그럼 오고 있다는 말씀입니까?"], ["The scout has said as much?\"", "척후가 그렇게 말했습니까?\""]], [["\"When?", "\"언제?"], ["and by what path?", "또 어느 길로?"], ["for the dunce has omitted to tell me this.", "그 멍청이가 그건 말해 주지 않았으니."], ["There is a letter, it would seem, too; and that is the only agreeable part of the matter.", "편지가 한 통 있는 모양인데, 그게 이 일에서 유일하게 흡족한 부분이야."], ["For the customary attentions of your Marquis of Montcalm--I warrant me, Duncan, that he of Lothian would buy a dozen such marquisates--but, if the news of the letter were bad, the gentility of this French monsieur would certainly compel him to let us know it.\"", "자네가 말하는 〈몽칼름〉 후작의 그 으레 갖추는 예의로 말하면 — 내 장담하건대, 〈던컨〉, 〈로디언〉의 사내라면 그런 후작 자리 열두 개는 사고도 남을 걸세 — 하지만 편지의 소식이 나쁜 것이었다면, 이 프랑스 양반의 그 점잖음이 분명 그걸 우리에게 알리도록 했을 게야.\""]], [["\"He keeps the letter, then, while he releases the messenger!\"", "\"그럼 사자는 풀어 주면서 편지는 쥐고 있다는 말씀이군요!\""]], [["\"Ay, that does he, and all for the sake of what you call your '_bonhommie_,'", "\"그렇다네, 그가 바로 그러는데, 죄다 자네가 말하는 그 '봉오미' 때문이지."], ["I would venture, if the truth was known, the fellow's grandfather taught the noble science of dancing.\"", "사실이 밝혀진다면 내 감히 말하겠네만, 그자의 할아비가 그 고상한 춤의 기예를 가르쳤을 게야.\""]], [["\"But what says the scout?", "\"하지만 척후는 뭐라고 합니까?"], ["he has eyes and ears, and a tongue: what verbal report does he make?\"", "그에겐 눈도 귀도 혀도 있으니, 입으로는 무슨 보고를 합니까?\""]], [["\"O!", "\"오!"], ["sir, he is not wanting in natural organs, and he is free to tell all that he has seen and heard.", "소령, 그자는 타고난 기관이 부족하진 않으니, 보고 들은 바를 죄다 거리낌 없이 말하지. 그 전부가 이렇다네."], ["The whole amount is this: there is a fort of his majesty's on the banks of the Hudson, called Edward, in honor of his gracious highness of York, you'll know; and it is well filled with armed men, as such a work should be.\"", "〈허드슨〉강 기슭에 폐하의 요새가 하나 있는데, 〈요크〉의 자애로운 전하를 기려 〈에드워드〉라 불리지, 알겠나. 그리고 그런 진지가 응당 그래야 하듯, 무장한 자들로 잘 채워져 있다네.\""]], [["\"But was there no movement, no signs of any intention to advance to our relief?\"", "\"하지만 아무 움직임도, 우리를 구하러 진격할 어떤 기색도 없었답니까?\""]], [["\"There were the morning and evening parades; and when one of the provincial loons--you'll know, Duncan, you're half a Scotsman yourself--when one of them dropped his powder over his porretch, if it touched the coals, it just burnt!\"", "\"아침 사열과 저녁 사열은 있었지. 그리고 그 식민지 멍청이 하나가 — 알겠나, 〈던컨〉, 자네도 반은 스코틀랜드인 아닌가 — 그자들 중 하나가 죽 위에다 화약을 흘렸을 때, 그게 숯불에 닿으면 그냥 타 버리더라네!\""], ["Then suddenly changing his bitter, ironical manner, to one more grave and thoughtful, he continued; \"and yet there might, and must be, something in that letter which it would be well to know!\"", "그러더니 그 쓰디쓰고 빈정대는 태도를 별안간 더 진중하고 사려 깊은 것으로 바꾸며 그가 말을 이었다. \"그래도 그 편지 안엔 알아 두면 좋을 무언가가 있을 법하고, 또 분명 있을 게야!\""]], [["\"Our decision should be speedy,\"", "\"우리의 결정은 신속해야 합니다.\" 〈던컨〉이 이 기분의 변화를 반갑게 틈타, 이 만남의 더 중요한 목적을 밀어붙이며 말했다."], ["said Duncan, gladly availing himself of this change of humor, to press the more important objects of their interview; \"I cannot conceal from you, sir, that the camp will not be much longer tenable; and I am sorry to add, that things appear no better in the fort; more than half the guns are bursted.\"", "\"각하, 진지가 더 오래 버티지 못하리라는 것을 숨길 수 없습니다. 그리고 안타깝게도 덧붙이건대, 요새 안의 사정도 나을 게 없어, 대포의 절반 이상이 터져 버렸습니다.\""]], [["\"And how should it be otherwise?", "\"어찌 안 그렇겠나?"], ["Some were fished from the bottom of the lake; some have been rusting in the woods since the discovery of the country; and some were never guns at all--mere privateersmen's playthings!", "더러는 호수 바닥에서 건져 올린 것이고, 더러는 이 고장이 발견된 이래로 숲속에서 녹슬어 온 것이며, 더러는 애당초 대포도 아니었지 — 그저 사략선원의 장난감이었어!"], ["Do you think, sir, you can have Woolwich Warren in the midst of a wilderness, three thousand miles from Great Britain!\"", "자네는 생각하나, 〈영국〉에서 삼천 마일이나 떨어진 황야 한복판에 〈울위치〉 병기창을 둘 수 있다고!\""]], [["\"The walls are crumbling about our ears, and provisions begin to fail us,\"", "\"성벽은 우리 귀 언저리에서 허물어지고, 식량은 동나기 시작했습니다.\""], ["continued Heyward, without regarding this new burst of indignation; \"even the men show signs of discontent and alarm.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 이 새로운 분노의 폭발에 아랑곳하지 않고 말을 이었다. \"병사들마저 불만과 동요의 기색을 보입니다.\""]], [["\"Major Heyward,\"", "\"〈헤이워드〉 소령,\""], ["said Munro, turning to his youthful associate with the dignity of his years and superior rank; \"I should have served his majesty for half a century, and earned these gray hairs, in vain, were I ignorant of all you say, and of the pressing nature of our circumstances; still, there is everything due to the honor of the king's arms and something to ourselves.", "〈먼로〉가 제 나이와 더 높은 지위의 위엄을 갖추고 젊은 동료에게 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 반세기를 폐하께 봉직하며 이 백발을 얻고서도, 자네가 하는 말과 우리 처지의 절박함을 모른다면, 그 모두가 헛수고였을 게야."], ["While there is hope of succor, this fortress will I defend, though it be to be done with pebbles gathered on the lake shore.", "그래도 폐하의 군기의 명예에 다해야 할 도리가 있고, 우리 자신에게도 얼마간 다해야 할 바가 있지. 원군의 희망이 있는 한, 이 요새를 나는 지킬 테니, 비록 그 일을 호숫가에서 주워 모은 조약돌로 해야 한다 해도 말일세."], ["It is a sight of the letter, therefore, that we want, that we may know the intentions of the man the Earl of Loudon has left among us as his substitute.\"", "그러니 우리가 바라는 것은 바로 그 편지를 보는 것이야, 〈라우든〉 백작이 자기 대리로 우리 가운데 남겨 둔 그 사내의 의중을 알 수 있도록 말이지.\""]], [["\"And can I be of service in the matter?\"", "\"그 일에 제가 도움이 될 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["\"Sir, you can; the Marquis of Montcalm has, in addition to his other civilities, invited me to a personal interview between the works and his own camp; in order, as he says, to impart some additional information. Now, I think it would not be wise to show any undue solicitude to meet him, and I would employ you, an officer of rank, as my substitute; for it would but ill comport with the honor of Scotland to let it be said one of her gentlemen was outdone in civility by a native of any other country on earth.\"", "\"있고말고, 소령. 〈몽칼름〉 후작이 다른 호의에 더하여, 진지와 자기 진영 사이에서 나와 직접 회견하자고 청해 왔네. 그가 말하기로는, 얼마간 추가 정보를 전하기 위해서라더군. 한데 그를 만나려고 지나친 열의를 보이는 것은 현명치 못하다 싶으니, 자네를, 지위 있는 장교를 내 대리로 쓰려 하네. 〈스코틀랜드〉의 신사 하나가 이 세상 그 어느 나라 태생에게 예의에서 뒤졌다는 말을 듣게 하는 것은, 〈스코틀랜드〉의 명예에 어울리지 않을 테니 말일세.\""]], [["Without assuming the supererogatory task of entering into a discussion of the comparative merits of national courtesy, Duncan cheerfully assented to supply the place of the veteran in the approaching interview.", "각 나라의 예절을 견주어 따지는 군더더기 같은 일에 끼어들지 않은 채, 〈던컨〉은 다가오는 회견에서 노병의 자리를 대신하기로 흔쾌히 응낙했다."], ["A long and confidential communication now succeeded, during which the young man received some additional insight into his duty, from the experience and native acuteness of his commander, and then the former took his leave.", "이제 길고 은밀한 대화가 이어졌으니, 그동안 젊은이는 그의 지휘관의 경험과 타고난 예리함에서 제 임무에 대한 얼마간의 통찰을 더 얻었고, 그런 뒤 그는 작별을 고했다."]], [["As Duncan could only act as the representative of the commandant of the fort, the ceremonies which should have accompanied a meeting between the heads of the adverse forces were of course dispensed with.", "〈던컨〉은 요새 사령관의 대리로서만 행할 수 있었으므로, 맞선 두 군의 수장 사이의 회견에 따랐을 의례는 물론 생략되었다."], ["The truce still existed, and with a roll and beat of the drum, and covered by a little white flag, Duncan left the sally-port, within ten minutes after his instructions were ended.", "휴전은 아직 이어지고 있었고, 북소리의 울림과 두드림과 함께, 작은 흰 깃발의 호위를 받으며, 〈던컨〉은 지시가 끝난 지 십 분 만에 출격문을 나섰다."], ["He was received by the French officer in advance with the usual formalities, and immediately accompanied to a distant marquee of the renowned soldier who led the forces of France.", "그는 앞선 프랑스 장교에게 으레 갖추는 격식으로 맞아들여졌고, 곧장 〈프랑스〉 군을 이끄는 그 이름난 군인의 먼 군막으로 인도되었다."]], [["The general of the enemy received the youthful messenger, surrounded by his principal officers, and by a swarthy band of the native chiefs, who had followed him to the field, with the warriors of their several tribes.", "적의 장군은 휘하의 주요 장교들과, 그를 따라 전장에 온 거뭇한 토착 추장들의 무리에 둘러싸여 그 앳된 사자를 맞았으니, 추장들은 저마다 제 부족의 전사들을 거느리고 있었다."], ["Heyward paused short, when, in glancing his eyes rapidly over the dark group of the latter, he beheld the malignant countenance of Magua, regarding him with the calm but sullen attention which marked the expression of that subtle savage.", "〈헤이워드〉는 그 거뭇한 무리에 재빨리 눈길을 던지다가, 그 교활한 미개인의 표정을 특징짓는 차분하나 시무룩한 주의로 자기를 바라보는 〈마구아〉의 악의에 찬 얼굴을 보고는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["A slight exclamation of surprise even burst from the lips of the young man; but instantly recollecting his errand, and the presence in which he stood, he suppressed every appearance of emotion, and turned to the hostile leader, who had already advanced a step to receive him.", "놀라움의 작은 외마디가 젊은이의 입술에서 새어 나오기까지 했으나, 그는 곧 제 임무와 자기가 서 있는 자리를 떠올리고는, 온갖 감정의 기색을 억누른 채, 이미 그를 맞으려 한 걸음 나선 적장 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["The Marquis of Montcalm was, at the period of which we write, in the flower of his age, and, it may be added, in the zenith of his fortunes.", "우리가 글로 쓰는 이 시기에, 〈몽칼름〉 후작은 인생의 한창때에, 덧붙이자면 운세의 절정에 있었다."], ["But, even in that enviable situation, he was affable, and distinguished as much for his attention to the forms of courtesy, as for that chivalrous courage which, only two short years afterwards, induced him to throw away his life on the plains of Abraham.", "그러나 그 부러운 처지에서도 그는 상냥했으며, 불과 두 해 뒤 〈에이브러햄〉 평원에서 제 목숨을 던지게 한 그 기사도적 용기에 못지않게, 예법에 기울이는 정성으로도 두드러졌다."], ["Duncan, in turning his eyes from the malign expression of Magua, suffered them to rest with pleasure on the smiling and polished features, and the noble military air, of the French general.", "〈던컨〉은 〈마구아〉의 악의에 찬 표정에서 눈을 돌려, 프랑스 장군의 미소 짓는 세련된 이목구비와 고귀한 무인의 풍모에 흐뭇이 눈길을 머물게 했다."]], [["\"Monsieur,\"", "\"무슈,\" 후작이 말했다."], ["said the latter, \"j'ai beaucoup de plaisir a--bah!--ou est cet interprete?\"", "\"참으로 기쁩니다, 아 — 흠! — 그 통역은 어디 있는가?\""]], [["\"Je crois, monsieur, qu'il ne sera pas necessaire,\"", "\"무슈, 그것은 필요 없을 듯합니다.\""], ["Heyward modestly replied; \"je parle un peu Francais.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 겸손히 대답했다. \"제가 프랑스어를 조금 합니다.\""]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["j'en suis bien aise,\"", "그거 참 다행이오.\" 〈몽칼름〉이 〈던컨〉의 팔을 친근하게 붙들고 군막 깊숙이, 귀가 닿지 않을 만큼 떨어진 곳으로 이끌며 말했다."], ["said Montcalm, taking Duncan familiarly by the arm, and leading him deep into the marquee, a little out of ear-shot; \"je deteste ces fripons-la; on ne sait jamais sur quel pie on est avec eux.", "\"나는 저 사기꾼 같은 자들이 질색이오. 그들과 함께 있으면 도무지 제 발 디딘 데가 어딘지 알 수가 없으니."], ["Eh, bien!", "자, 그럼!"], ["monsieur,\"", "무슈,\""], ["he continued, still speaking in French; \"though I should have been proud of receiving your commandant, I am very happy that he has seen proper to employ an officer so distinguished, and who, I am sure, is so amiable, as yourself.\"", "그가 여전히 프랑스어로 말을 이었다. \"비록 당신네 사령관을 맞았다면 영광이었겠으나, 그가 당신처럼 이토록 출중하고, 또 분명 이토록 호감 가는 장교를 쓰기로 한 것이 나는 무척 기쁘오.\""]], [["Duncan bowed low, pleased with the compliment, in spite of a most heroic determination to suffer no artifice to allure him into forgetfulness of the interest of his prince; and Montcalm, after a pause of a moment, as if to recollect his thoughts, proceeded,--", "〈던컨〉은 그 어떤 술책에도 홀려 제 군주의 이익을 잊지 않으리라는 더없이 영웅적인 결심에도 불구하고, 그 찬사에 흐뭇해하며 깊이 머리를 숙였다. 그리고 〈몽칼름〉은, 마치 생각을 가다듬는 듯 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말을 이었다."]], [["\"Your commandant is a brave man, and well qualified to repel my assault.", "\"당신네 사령관은 용감한 사내요, 내 공격을 물리치기에 능히 알맞은 인물이지."], ["Mais, monsieur, is it not time to begin to take more counsel of humanity, and less of your courage?", "하지만 무슈, 이제는 용기보다 인도(人道)에 더 귀를 기울이기 시작할 때가 아니겠소?"], ["The one as strongly characterizes the hero as the other.\"", "어느 쪽이든 다른 쪽 못지않게 영웅의 증표가 되는 법이니.\""]], [["\"We consider the qualities as inseparable,\"", "\"우리는 그 두 자질을 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것으로 여깁니다.\""], ["returned Duncan, smiling; \"but while we find in the vigor of your excellency every motive to stimulate the one, we can, as yet, see no particular call for the exercise of the other.\"", "〈던컨〉이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"다만, 각하의 기세에서 그 하나를 자극할 온갖 동기를 보긴 합니다만, 아직은 다른 하나를 발휘할 특별한 이유를 보지 못하겠습니다.\""]], [["Montcalm, in his turn, slightly bowed, but it was with the air of a man too practised to remember the language of flattery.", "이번에는 〈몽칼름〉이 살짝 머리를 숙였으나, 그것은 아첨의 말을 기억하기에는 너무 노련한 사내의 태도였다."], ["After musing a moment, he added,--", "잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["\"It is possible my glasses have deceived me, and that your works resist our cannon better than I had supposed.", "\"내 망원경이 나를 속였을지도 모르고, 당신네 진지가 내 짐작보다 우리 대포를 더 잘 견뎌 내는 것일지도 모르오."], ["You know our force?\"", "우리 병력을 아시오?\""]], [["\"Our accounts vary,\"", "\"전해 듣는 바가 제각각입니다.\""], ["said Duncan, carelessly; \"the highest, however, has not exceeded twenty thousand men.\"", "〈던컨〉이 무심히 말했다. \"하지만 가장 많이 잡아도 이만을 넘지는 않더군요.\""]], [["The Frenchman bit his lip, and fastened his eyes keenly on the other as if to read his thoughts; then, with a readiness peculiar to himself, he continued, as if assenting to the truth of an enumeration which quite doubled his army,--", "프랑스인은 입술을 깨물고, 마치 그의 속을 읽으려는 듯 날카로운 눈길을 상대에게 고정했다. 그러더니 제 특유의 민첩함으로, 자기 군대를 곱절로 부풀린 그 셈을 사실로 인정하는 듯이 말을 이었다."]], [["\"It is a poor compliment to the vigilance of us soldiers, monsieur, that, do what we will, we never can conceal our numbers.", "\"우리 군인들의 경계심에 대한 어줍은 찬사로군요, 무슈. 우리가 무슨 짓을 해도 도무지 그 수를 감출 수 없다니 말이오."], ["If it were to be done at all, one would believe it might succeed in these woods.", "만일 어디서든 그게 가능하다면, 이 숲속에서라면 성공하리라 믿었을 텐데."], ["Though you think it too soon to listen to the calls of humanity,\"", "비록 당신은 인도의 부름에 귀 기울이기엔 너무 이르다 여기지만,\""], ["he added, smiling archly, \"I may be permitted to believe that gallantry is not forgotten by one so young as yourself.", "그가 짓궂게 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"당신처럼 젊은 이라면 정중함만은 잊지 않았으리라 믿어도 되겠지요."], ["The daughters of the commandant, I learn, have passed into the fort since it was invested?\"", "듣자 하니, 사령관의 따님들이 포위가 시작된 뒤 요새로 들어왔다지요?\""]], [["\"It is true, monsieur; but, so far from weakening our efforts, they set us an example of courage in their own fortitude.", "\"사실입니다, 무슈. 하지만 그분들은 우리의 노력을 약화시키기는커녕, 그 꿋꿋함으로 우리에게 용기의 본보기를 보여 주십니다."], ["Were nothing but resolution necessary to repel so accomplished a soldier as M. de Montcalm, I would gladly trust the defence of William Henry to the elder of those ladies.\"", "〈몽칼름〉 씨처럼 노련한 군인을 물리치는 데 결의만 있으면 된다면, 저는 기꺼이 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 방어를 그 두 숙녀 중 손위 분께 맡기겠습니다.\""]], [["\"We have a wise ordinance in our Salique laws, which says, 'The crown of France shall never degrade the lance to the distaff,'\"", "\"우리에게는 〈살리카〉 법에 현명한 율법이 하나 있소. '〈프랑스〉의 왕관은 결코 창을 실패로 떨어뜨리지 않으리라.'\""], ["said Montcalm, dryly, and with a little hauteur; but instantly adding, with his former frank and easy air, \"as all the nobler qualities are hereditary, I can easily credit you; though, as I said before, courage has its limits, and humanity must not be forgotten.", "〈몽칼름〉이 무뚝뚝하게, 그리고 약간의 오만을 띠고 말했으나, 곧 예전의 솔직하고 편안한 태도로 덧붙였다. \"모든 고귀한 자질이 핏줄로 이어지는 만큼, 당신 말을 쉽게 믿겠소."], ["I trust, monsieur, you come authorized to treat for the surrender of the place?\"", "다만 앞서 말했듯, 용기에도 한계가 있고, 인도를 잊어서는 안 되오. 무슈, 당신이 이곳의 항복을 교섭할 권한을 받고 왔으리라 믿소만?\""]], [["\"Has your excellency found our defence so feeble as to believe the measure necessary?\"", "\"각하께서는 우리의 방어가 그 조치가 필요하다고 여기실 만큼 그토록 허약하다 보셨습니까?\""]], [["\"I should be sorry to have the defence protracted in such a manner as to irritate my red friends there,\"", "\"방어가 저기 내 붉은 벗들을 노하게 할 만큼 질질 끄는 것을 나는 보고 싶지 않소.\""], ["continued Montcalm, glancing his eyes at the group of grave and attentive Indians, without attending to the other's question; \"I find it difficult, even now, to limit them to the usages of war.\"", "〈몽칼름〉이 상대의 물음에는 답하지 않고, 진중하고 주의 깊은 인디언 무리에게 눈길을 던지며 말을 이었다. \"지금도 그들을 전쟁의 관례 안에 묶어 두기가 어렵소.\""]], [["Heyward was silent; for a painful recollection of the dangers he had so recently escaped came over his mind, and recalled the images of those defenceless beings who had shared in all his sufferings.", "〈헤이워드〉는 잠자코 있었으니, 자기가 그토록 최근에 벗어난 위험들에 대한 고통스러운 기억이 마음에 밀려와, 자기의 온갖 고난을 함께 겪었던 그 무력한 이들의 모습을 떠올렸기 때문이다."]], [["\"Ces messieurs-la,\"", "\"저자들은,\" 〈몽칼름〉이 자기가 얻었다고 여긴 우위를 좇으며 말했다."], ["said Montcalm, following up the advantage which he conceived he had gained, \"are most formidable when baffled: and it is unnecessary to tell you with what difficulty they are restrained in their anger.", "\"뜻이 꺾이면 더없이 무서워지지. 그들의 분노를 억누르기가 얼마나 어려운지는 굳이 말할 필요도 없소."], ["Eh bien, monsieur!", "자, 그럼!"], ["shall we speak of the terms?\"", "무슈, 우리 조건을 이야기해 볼까요?\""]], [["\"I fear your excellency has been deceived as to the strength of William Henry, and the resources of its garrison!\"", "\"각하께서는 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 힘과 그 수비대의 자원에 대해 잘못 알고 계신 듯합니다!\""]], [["\"I have not sat down before Quebec, but an earthen work, that is defended by twenty-three hundred gallant men,\"", "\"나는 〈퀘벡〉 앞에 진을 친 게 아니라, 용맹한 사내 이천삼백이 지키는 흙벽 진지 앞에 진을 친 것이오.\""], ["was the laconic reply.", "간결한 대답이었다."]], [["\"Our mounds are earthen, certainly--nor are they seated on the rocks of Cape Diamond; but they stand on that shore which proved so destructive to Dieskau and his army.", "\"우리 둔덕이 흙으로 된 것은 분명하며, 〈케이프 다이아몬드〉의 바위 위에 앉아 있지도 않습니다. 그러나 그것은 〈디스코〉와 그의 군대에게 그토록 파멸적이었던 그 기슭 위에 서 있습니다."], ["There is also a powerful force within a few hours' march of us, which we account upon as part of our means.\"", "또한 우리에게서 몇 시간 행군 거리 안에 강력한 병력이 있어, 우리는 그것을 우리 수단의 일부로 셈하고 있습니다.\""]], [["\"Some six or eight thousand men,\"", "\"육칠천 명쯤 되는 군대지요.\""], ["returned Montcalm, with much apparent indifference, \"whom their leader wisely judges to be safer in their works than in the field.\"", "〈몽칼름〉이 자못 무심한 듯 대답했다. \"그 지휘관이 야전보다는 제 진지 안에 있는 편이 더 안전하다고 현명하게 판단한 자들 말이오.\""]], [["It was now Heyward's turn to bite his lip with vexation, as the other so coolly alluded to a force which the young man knew to be overrated.", "이번에는 〈헤이워드〉가 분해서 입술을 깨물 차례였으니, 상대가 젊은이도 부풀려진 줄 아는 그 병력을 그토록 태연히 언급한 까닭이었다."], ["Both mused a little while in silence, when Montcalm renewed the conversation, in a way that showed he believed the visit of his guest was solely to propose terms of capitulation.", "둘은 잠시 말없이 생각에 잠겼다가, 〈몽칼름〉이 대화를 다시 시작했으니, 자기 손님의 방문이 오로지 항복 조건을 제안하기 위함이라 믿는 기색이었다."], ["On the other hand, Heyward began to throw sundry inducements in the way of the French general, to betray the discoveries he had made through the intercepted letter.", "한편 〈헤이워드〉는 가로챈 편지를 통해 알아낸 사실을 그가 누설하도록, 프랑스 장군에게 갖가지 유인을 던지기 시작했다."], ["The artifice of neither, however, succeeded; and after a protracted and fruitless interview, Duncan took his leave, favorably impressed with an opinion of the courtesy and talents of the enemy's captain, but as ignorant of what he came to learn as when he arrived.", "그러나 어느 쪽의 술책도 통하지 않았고, 길고 헛된 회견 끝에 〈던컨〉은 적장의 정중함과 재능에 호의적인 인상을 받은 채 작별을 고했으나, 자기가 알아내러 온 것에 대해서는 도착했을 때와 다름없이 깜깜했다."], ["Montcalm followed him as far as the entrance of the marquee, renewing his invitations to the commandant of the fort to give him an immediate meeting in the open ground, between the two armies.", "〈몽칼름〉은 군막 입구까지 그를 배웅하며, 두 군 사이의 트인 땅에서 즉시 회견하자는 청을 요새 사령관에게 거듭 전했다."]], [["There they separated, and Duncan returned to the advanced post of the French, accompanied as before; whence he instantly proceeded to the fort, and to the quarters of his own commander.", "그곳에서 그들은 헤어졌고, 〈던컨〉은 전과 같은 호위를 받으며 프랑스군의 전진 초소로 돌아왔다. 그곳에서 그는 곧장 요새로, 그리고 자기 지휘관의 처소로 나아갔다. * 봉오미: 프랑스어 'bonhomie', 곧 사람 좋음, 호인다운 너그러움을 뜻한다."]], [["\"_Edg._--Before you fight the battle, ope this letter.\"", "\"에드거. 싸움을 시작하기 전에, 이 편지를 펼쳐 보십시오.\""]], [["_King Lear._", "리어 왕"]], [["Major Heyward found Munro attended only by his daughters.", "〈헤이워드〉 소령이 〈먼로〉를 찾아갔을 때, 그의 곁에는 두 딸만이 있었다."], ["Alice sat upon his knee, parting the gray hairs on the forehead of the old man with her delicate fingers; and, whenever he affected to frown on her trifling, appeasing his assumed anger by pressing her ruby lips fondly on his wrinkled brow.", "〈앨리스〉가 그의 무릎에 앉아 가녀린 손가락으로 노인의 이마에 드리운 백발을 가르고 있었고, 그가 그 장난을 짐짓 못마땅한 듯 찌푸려 보일 때마다, 그 꾸며 낸 노여움을, 주름진 이마에 앵두 같은 입술을 다정히 눌러 달래곤 했다."], ["Cora was seated nigh them, a calm and amused looker-on; regarding the wayward movements of her more youthful sister, with that species of maternal fondness which characterized her love for Alice.", "〈코라〉는 그들 가까이에 앉아, 차분하고 흐뭇한 구경꾼으로서, 더 어린 동생의 변덕스러운 몸짓을, 〈앨리스〉를 향한 그녀의 사랑을 특징짓는 그 모성 어린 다정함으로 바라보았다."], ["Not only the dangers through which they had passed, but those which still impended above them, appeared to be momentarily forgotten, in the soothing indulgence of such a family meeting.", "지나온 위험뿐 아니라 아직도 그들 머리 위에 드리운 위험마저, 이러한 가족의 만남이 주는 위안의 탐닉 속에서 한순간 잊힌 듯했다."], ["It seemed as if they had profited by the short truce, to devote an instant to the purest and best affections: the daughters forgetting their fears, and the veteran his cares, in the security of the moment.", "마치 그들은 이 짧은 휴전을 틈타, 가장 순수하고 가장 좋은 애정에 한순간을 바치기로 한 듯했으니, 딸들은 그 두려움을, 노전사는 그 근심을, 그 순간의 안온함 속에 잊고 있었다."], ["Of this scene, Duncan, who in his eagerness to report his arrival had entered unannounced, stood many moments an unobserved and a delighted spectator.", "이 광경을, 자신의 도착을 알리려는 조급함에 기별도 없이 들어선 〈던컨〉은, 여러 순간을, 눈에 띄지 않은 채 흐뭇한 구경꾼으로 서서 지켜보았다."], ["But the quick and dancing eyes of Alice soon caught a glimpse of his figure reflected from a glass, and she sprang blushing from her father's knee, exclaiming aloud,--", "그러나 〈앨리스〉의 재빠르고 춤추는 듯한 눈이 이내 거울에 비친 그의 모습을 흘끗 잡아냈으니, 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 아버지의 무릎에서 벌떡 일어나, 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Major Heyward!\"", "\"〈헤이워드〉 소령님!\""]], [["\"What of the lad?\"", "\"그 친구가 어쨌다는 거냐?\""], ["demanded the father; \"I have sent him to crack a little with the Frenchman.", "아버지가 물었다. \"프랑스인과 이야기를 좀 나누라고 내가 보냈다."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["sir, you are young, and you're nimble!", "이보게, 자네는 젊고 또 날렵하지!"], ["Away with you, ye baggage; as if there were not troubles enough for a soldier, without having his camp filled with such prattling hussies as yourself!\"", "저리 가거라, 이 말괄량이야. 군인에게는 근심거리가 이미 충분하거늘, 진지를 너 같은 재잘대는 말괄량이로 가득 채우기까지 해야 한단 말이냐!\""]], [["Alice laughingly followed her sister, who instantly led the way from an apartment where she perceived their presence was no longer desirable.", "〈앨리스〉는 웃으며 언니를 뒤따랐으니, 언니는 자신들의 존재가 더는 환영받지 못함을 알아채고 곧장 방을 나서는 길을 앞장섰다."], ["Munro, instead of demanding the result of the young man's mission, paced the room for a few moments, with his hands behind his back, and his head inclined towards the floor, like a man lost in thought.", "〈먼로〉는 젊은이의 임무 결과를 묻는 대신, 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌리고 머리를 바닥으로 숙인 채, 생각에 잠긴 사람처럼 몇 순간 방 안을 거닐었다."], ["At length he raised his eyes, glistening with a father's fondness, and exclaimed,--", "마침내 그는, 아비의 다정함으로 반짝이는 눈을 들어, 외쳤다."]], [["\"They are a pair of excellent girls, Heyward, and such as any one may boast of.\"", "\"저 애들은 더없이 훌륭한 한 쌍의 딸이지, 〈헤이워드〉, 누구라도 자랑삼을 만한 그런 딸들이야.\""]], [["\"You are not now to learn my opinion of your daughters, Colonel Munro.\"", "\"제가 따님들에 대해 어찌 생각하는지는 이제 와 새삼 아실 일이 아니지요, 〈먼로〉 대령님.\""]], [["\"True, lad, true,\"", "\"옳아, 이 사람아, 옳은 말일세.\" 조급한 노인이 말을 끊었다."], ["interrupted the impatient old man; \"you were about opening your mind more fully on that matter the day you got in; but I did not think it becoming in an old soldier to be talking of nuptial blessings and wedding jokes when the enemies of his king were likely to be unbidden guests at the feast!", "\"자네가 도착하던 날 그 일을 더 속 시원히 털어놓으려 하지 않았던가. 허나 임금의 적들이 잔칫상에 청하지도 않은 손님으로 들이닥칠 판국에, 늙은 군인이 혼례의 축복이며 결혼 농담이나 늘어놓는 것이 온당치 못하다 여겼던 게야!"], ["But I was wrong, Duncan, boy, I was wrong there; and I am now ready to hear what you have to say.\"", "허나 내가 틀렸네, 〈던컨〉, 이 사람아, 그 점에선 내가 틀렸어. 이제 자네가 하고 싶은 말을 들을 준비가 되었네.\""]], [["\"Notwithstanding the pleasure your assurance gives me, dear sir, I have just now a message from Montcalm--\"", "\"그리 말씀해 주시니 기쁘기 그지없습니다만, 대령님, 지금 막 〈몽칼름〉에게서 전갈이 왔습니다—\""]], [["\"Let the Frenchman and all his host go to the devil, sir?\"", "\"그 프랑스인과 그 무리는 죄다 악마에게나 가라지, 이 사람아!\""], ["exclaimed the hasty veteran.", "성마른 노전사가 외쳤다."], ["\"He is not yet master of William Henry, nor shall he ever be, provided Webb proves himself the man he should. No, sir!", "\"그자는 아직 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 주인이 아니며, 〈웨브〉가 마땅히 그래야 할 사내임을 입증하기만 한다면, 영영 그리되지도 못할 걸세."], ["thank Heaven, we are not yet in such a strait that it can be said Munro is too much pressed to discharge the little domestic duties of his own family.", "아니지, 이 사람아! 하늘에 감사하게도, 우리는 아직 〈먼로〉가 제 집안의 자그마한 대소사를 챙길 겨를도 없을 만큼 쫓기는 처지에 놓이지는 않았네."], ["Your mother was the only child of my bosom friend, Duncan; and I'll just give you a hearing, though all the knights of St.", "자네 어머니는 내 가슴속 벗의 외동딸이었지, 〈던컨〉. 그러니 자네 말을 들어 주겠네, 비록 성 루이의 기사들이 죄다 한 몸이 되어 그 프랑스 성인을 앞세우고 출격문에 몰려와, 황송하옵게도 한마디 아뢰겠다고 간청한다 한들 말일세."], ["Louis were in a body at the sally-port, with the French saint at their head, craving to speak a word under favor.", "설탕통으로 살 수 있는 기사 작위라니, 참으로 그럴듯한 기사 신분이지! 게다가 그 푼돈짜리 후작 작위들이라니!"], ["A pretty degree of knighthood, sir, is that which can be bought with sugar-hogsheads!", "위엄과 유서로 따지면 엉겅퀴 훈장이야말로 진정한 기사도의 '네모 메 임푸네 라케시트'이지!"], ["and then your two-penny marquisates! The thistle is the order for dignity and antiquity; the veritable _nemo me impune lacessit_ of chivalry!", "자네에게도 그 작위에 오른 선조들이 있었네, 〈던컨〉, 그들은 스코틀랜드 귀족들의 자랑이었지.\""], ["Ye had ancestors in that degree, Duncan, and they were an ornament to the nobles of Scotland.\"", "* 네모 메 임푸네 라케시트: \"누구도 나를 건드리고 무사하지 못하리라\"라는 뜻의 스코틀랜드 왕가 좌우명."]], [["Heyward, who perceived that his superior took a malicious pleasure in exhibiting his contempt for the message of the French general, was fain to humor a spleen that he knew would be short-lived; he therefore replied with as much indifference as he could assume on such a subject,--", "〈헤이워드〉는 자신의 상관이 프랑스 장군의 전갈을 향한 경멸을 드러내며 짓궂은 즐거움을 누리고 있음을 알아챘으니, 곧 사그라들 줄 아는 그 노여움을 맞춰 주는 편이 낫겠다 싶었다. 그리하여 그는 그런 주제에 대해 짐짓 꾸며 낼 수 있는 한껏의 무심함으로 대답했다."]], [["\"My request, as you know, sir, went so far as to presume to the honor of being your son.\"", "\"제 청은, 아시다시피 대령님, 외람되게도 대령님의 아들이 되는 영광에까지 미친 것이었습니다.\""]], [["\"Ay, boy, you found words to make yourself very plainly comprehended.", "\"그래, 이 사람아, 자네는 제 뜻을 아주 명확히 전할 만한 말을 찾아냈었지."], ["But, let me ask ye, sir, have you been as intelligible to the girl?\"", "허나 한 가지 묻겠네, 이 사람아, 그 애에게도 그만큼 분명히 했는가?\""]], [["\"On my honor, no,\"", "\"맹세코, 아닙니다.\""], ["exclaimed Duncan, warmly; \"there would have been an abuse of a confided trust, had I taken advantage of my situation for such a purpose.\"", "〈던컨〉이 뜨겁게 외쳤다. \"그런 목적으로 제 처지를 이용했다면, 위탁받은 신뢰를 저버린 셈이 되었겠지요.\""]], [["\"Your notions are those of a gentleman, Major Heyward, and well enough in their place.", "\"자네 생각은 신사다운 것일세, 〈헤이워드〉 소령, 그 자리에선 더없이 마땅한 것이지."], ["But Cora Munro is a maiden too discreet, and of a mind too elevated and improved, to need the guardianship even of a father.\"", "허나 〈코라 먼로〉는 너무도 분별 있고, 너무도 고매하고 닦인 마음을 지닌 처녀라, 아비의 보호조차 필요로 하지 않는 아이일세.\" \"〈코라〉라니요!\""]], [["\"Cora!\" \"Ay--Cora!", "\"그래—〈코라〉 말일세!"], ["we are talking of your pretensions to Miss Munro, are we not, sir?\"", "우리는 〈먼로〉 양에게 자네가 품은 뜻을 이야기하는 것 아니던가, 이 사람아?\""]], [["\"I--I--I was not conscious of having mentioned her name,\"", "\"저는—저는—제가 그분 이름을 언급한 적이 있다고는 미처 생각지 못했습니다.\""], ["said Duncan, stammering.", "〈던컨〉이 말을 더듬으며 말했다."]], [["\"And to marry whom, then, did you wish my consent, Major Heyward?\"", "\"그러면 대체 누구와 혼인하고자 내 승낙을 바란 것인가, 〈헤이워드〉 소령?\""], ["demanded the old soldier, erecting himself in the dignity of offended feeling.", "노전사가, 상한 감정의 위엄에 몸을 곧추세우며 물었다."]], [["\"You have another, and not less lovely child.\"", "\"대령님께는 또 한 분, 그에 못지않게 사랑스러운 따님이 계십니다.\""]], [["\"Alice!\"", "\"〈앨리스〉라고!\""], ["exclaimed the father, in an astonishment equal to that with which Duncan had just repeated the name of her sister.", "아버지가, 방금 〈던컨〉이 언니의 이름을 되뇌었을 때와 똑같은 놀라움에 휩싸여 외쳤다."]], [["\"Such was the direction of my wishes, sir.\"", "\"제 바람은 그쪽을 향해 있었습니다, 대령님.\""]], [["The young man awaited in silence the result of the extraordinary effect produced by a communication which, as it now appeared, was so unexpected.", "젊은이는 자신이 자아낸 그 뜻밖의 비범한 반응의 결말을 말없이 기다렸다."], ["For several minutes Munro paced the chamber with long and rapid strides, his rigid features working convulsively, and every faculty seemingly absorbed in the musings of his own mind.", "여러 순간 〈먼로〉는 길고 빠른 보폭으로 방 안을 거닐었으니, 그 굳은 얼굴이 경련하듯 일그러졌고, 모든 정신이 제 마음속 상념에 온통 사로잡힌 듯했다."], ["At length, he paused directly in front of Heyward, and riveting his eyes upon those of the other, he said, with a lip that quivered violently,--", "마침내 그는 〈헤이워드〉의 정면에 멈추어 서서, 자신의 두 눈을 상대의 두 눈에 못 박은 채, 격하게 떨리는 입술로 말했다."]], [["\"Duncan Heyward, I have loved you for the sake of him whose blood is in your veins; I have loved you for your own good qualities; and I have loved you, because I thought you would contribute to the happiness of my child.", "\"〈던컨 헤이워드〉, 나는 자네 핏줄에 그 피가 흐르는 사람을 생각하여 자네를 사랑해 왔네. 나는 자네 자신의 훌륭한 자질로 인해 자네를 사랑해 왔고, 또 자네가 내 자식의 행복에 보탬이 되리라 여겼기에 자네를 사랑해 왔네."], ["But all this love would turn to hatred, were I assured that what I so much apprehend is true.\"", "허나 내가 그토록 우려하는 바가 사실임을 알게 된다면, 이 모든 사랑은 증오로 변하고 말 걸세.\""]], [["\"God forbid that any act or thought of mine should lead to such a change!\"", "\"제 어떤 행동이나 생각도 그런 변화로 이어지지 않게 하소서, 하느님!\""], ["exclaimed the young man, whose eye never quailed under the penetrating look it encountered.", "젊은이가 외쳤으니, 그의 눈은 마주친 꿰뚫는 시선 아래서도 결코 움츠러들지 않았다."], ["Without adverting the impossibility of the other's comprehending those feelings which were hid in his own bosom, Munro suffered himself to be appeased by the unaltered countenance he met, and with a voice sensibly softened, he continued,--", "자신의 가슴속에 감추어진 그 감정을 상대가 이해할 수 없다는 점에는 마음 쓰지 않은 채, 〈먼로〉는 자신이 마주한 변함없는 얼굴에 마음이 누그러지도록 내버려 두었으니, 눈에 띄게 부드러워진 목소리로 말을 이었다."]], [["\"You would be my son, Duncan, and you're ignorant of the history of the man you wish to call your father.", "\"자네는 내 아들이 되겠다 하지만, 자네가 아버지라 부르고자 하는 사내의 내력은 알지 못하네."], ["Sit ye down, young man, and I will open to you the wounds of a seared heart, in as few words as may be suitable.\"", "앉게, 젊은이, 내 화상 입은 가슴의 상처를, 알맞을 만큼 짧은 말로 자네에게 펼쳐 보이겠네.\""]], [["By this time, the message of Montcalm was as much forgotten by him who bore it as by the man for whose ears it was intended.", "이때쯤이면 〈몽칼름〉의 전갈은, 그것을 받아야 할 귀의 임자에게나 그것을 가져온 자에게나 똑같이 잊혀 있었다."], ["Each drew a chair, and while the veteran communed a few moments with his own thoughts, apparently in sadness, the youth suppressed his impatience in a look and attitude of respectful attention.", "두 사람은 저마다 의자를 끌어다 앉았고, 노전사가 몇 순간 제 상념과, 보아하니 슬픔에 잠긴 채 마주하는 동안, 젊은이는 공손한 경청의 표정과 자세로 제 조바심을 억눌렀다."], ["At length the former spoke:--", "마침내 노전사가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"You'll know, already, Major Heyward, that my family was both ancient and honorable,\"", "\"〈헤이워드〉 소령, 자네는 이미 내 가문이 유서 깊고도 명예로운 집안임을 알고 있을 걸세.\""], ["commenced the Scotsman; \"though it might not altogether be endowed with that amount of wealth that should correspond with its degree.", "스코틀랜드인이 말을 시작했다. \"비록 그 신분에 걸맞을 만큼의 재산을 두루 갖추지는 못했을지라도 말이지."], ["I was, may be, such an one as yourself when I plighted my faith to Alice Graham, the only child of a neighboring laird of some estate.", "내가 〈앨리스 그레이엄〉에게 언약을 맹세했을 때, 나는 아마 지금의 자네와 같은 처지였을 걸세. 그녀는 얼마간 재산을 지닌 이웃 영주의 외동딸이었네."], ["But the connection was disagreeable to her father, on more accounts than my poverty.", "허나 이 혼사는 그녀 아비의 마음에 들지 않았으니, 내 가난뿐 아니라 여러 까닭에서였지."], ["I did therefore what an honest man should--restored the maiden her troth, and departed the country in the service of my king.", "그리하여 나는 정직한 사내가 마땅히 할 바를 했네—처녀에게 그 언약을 돌려주고, 임금을 섬기고자 그 고장을 떠난 게야."], ["I had seen many regions, and had shed much blood in different lands, before duty called me to the islands of the West Indies.", "나는 여러 고장을 보았고, 갖가지 땅에서 많은 피를 흘렸으니, 마침내 임무가 나를 서인도제도로 불렀을 때였지."], ["There it was my lot to form a connection with one who in time became my wife, and the mother of Cora.", "그곳에서 나는 한 여인과 인연을 맺을 운명이었고, 그 여인은 때가 되어 내 아내가, 그리고 〈코라〉의 어미가 되었네."], ["She was the daughter of a gentleman of those isles, by a lady whose misfortune it was, if you will,\"", "그녀는 그 섬들의 한 신사가, 한 부인에게서 얻은 딸이었으니, 그 부인의 불행이라면, 굳이 그리 말하자면,\""], ["said the old man, proudly, \"to be descended, remotely, from that unfortunate class who are so basely enslaved to administer to the wants of a luxurious people.", "노인이 자랑스레 말했다. \"사치를 누리는 자들의 필요에 부응하고자 그토록 비천하게 노예로 부려지는 저 불운한 부류에서, 먼 핏줄로 비롯되었다는 것이었네."], ["Ay, sir, that is a curse entailed on Scotland by her unnatural union with a foreign and trading people.", "그렇다네, 이 사람아, 그것은 스코틀랜드가 외래의 장사치 민족과 어울리지 않는 결합을 맺어 짊어진 저주일세."], ["But could I find a man among them who would dare to reflect on my child, he should feel the weight of a father's anger!", "허나 그자들 가운데 내 자식을 비방할 만큼 대담한 사내를 내가 찾아낸다면, 그자는 아비의 노여움의 무게를 느끼게 될 걸세!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Major Heyward, you are yourself born at the south, where these unfortunate beings are considered of a race inferior to your own.\"", "〈헤이워드〉 소령, 자네 자신이 남부에서 태어나지 않았는가, 그곳에선 이 불운한 사람들을 자네들보다 열등한 인종으로 여기지.\""]], [["\"'Tis most unfortunately true, sir,\"", "\"참으로 불운하게도 사실입니다, 대령님.\""], ["said Duncan, unable any longer to prevent his eyes from sinking to the floor in embarrassment.", "〈던컨〉이, 더는 두 눈이 당혹감에 바닥으로 가라앉는 것을 막지 못한 채 말했다."]], [["\"And you cast it on my child as a reproach!", "\"그래서 자네는 그것을 내 자식에게 흠으로 씌우는 게로군!"], ["You scorn to mingle the blood of the Heywards with one so degraded--lovely and virtuous though she be?\"", "자네는 〈헤이워드〉 가문의 피를, 그토록 천한 핏줄과—제아무리 사랑스럽고 정숙한들—섞기를 멸시하는 게야?\""], ["fiercely demanded the jealous parent.", "시샘에 찬 아비가 사납게 물었다."]], [["\"Heaven protect me from a prejudice so unworthy of my reason!\"", "\"제 이성에 그토록 어울리지 않는 편견으로부터 저를 지켜 주소서, 하늘이여!\""], ["returned Duncan, at the same time conscious of such a feeling, and that as deeply rooted as if it had been ingrafted in his nature.", "〈던컨〉이 대답했으니, 동시에 그러한 감정이, 마치 제 천성에 접붙여진 듯 깊이 뿌리내려 있음을 의식하고 있었다."], ["\"The sweetness, the beauty, the witchery of your younger daughter, Colonel Munro, might explain my motives, without imputing to me this injustice.\"", "\"대령님의 작은따님이 지닌 그 다정함과 아름다움, 그 마력이라면, 제게 이런 부당함을 씌우지 않고도 제 동기를 설명해 줄 수 있을 것입니다, 〈먼로〉 대령님.\""]], [["\"Ye are right, sir,\"", "\"옳은 말일세, 이 사람아.\""], ["returned the old man, again changing his tones to those of gentleness, or rather softness; \"the girl is the image of what her mother was at her years, and before she had become acquainted with grief.", "노인이 다시 그 어조를 부드러움으로, 아니 차라리 온화함으로 바꾸며 대답했다. \"그 애는 제 어미가 그 나이 때, 그러니까 슬픔을 알기 전의 모습 그대로일세."], ["When death deprived me of my wife I returned to Scotland, enriched by the marriage; and would you think it, Duncan!", "죽음이 내게서 아내를 앗아 갔을 때, 나는 그 혼인으로 부유해진 채 스코틀랜드로 돌아갔네. 그런데 자네, 이걸 믿겠는가, 〈던컨〉!"], ["The suffering angel had remained in the heartless state of celibacy twenty long years, and that for the sake of a man who could forget her!", "그 고통받던 천사는 매정한 독신의 처지로 스무 해를 그대로 머물러 있었으니, 그것도 자신을 잊을 수 있었던 사내를 위해서였네!"], ["She did more, sir; she over-looked my want of faith, and all difficulties being now removed, she took me for her husband.\"", "그녀는 그보다 더한 일도 했네, 이 사람아. 그녀는 내 신의 없음을 눈감아 주었고, 이제 모든 장애가 사라지자 나를 제 남편으로 맞아들였네.\""]], [["\"And became the mother of Alice?\"", "\"그리하여 〈앨리스〉의 어머니가 되셨군요?\""], ["exclaimed Duncan, with an eagerness that might have proved dangerous at a moment when the thoughts of Munro were less occupied than at present.", "〈던컨〉이, 〈먼로〉의 생각이 지금만큼 사로잡혀 있지 않은 순간이었다면 위태로웠을 법한 간절함으로 외쳤다."]], [["\"She did, indeed,\"", "\"참으로 그랬네.\""], ["said the old man, \"and dearly did she pay for the blessing she bestowed.", "노인이 말했다. \"그리고 그녀는 자신이 베푼 그 축복의 값을 비싸게도 치렀지."], ["But she is a saint in heaven, sir; and it ill becomes one whose foot rests on the grave to mourn a lot so blessed.", "허나 그녀는 하늘에 있는 성인일세, 이 사람아. 무덤에 발을 디딘 자가 그토록 복된 운명을 애도하는 것은 온당치 못한 일이지."], ["I had her but a single year, though; a short term of happiness for one who had seen her youth fade in hopeless pining.\"", "그래도 나는 그녀를 단 한 해밖에 곁에 두지 못했네. 부질없는 그리움 속에 제 젊음이 시드는 것을 지켜본 이에게는 너무도 짧은 행복의 기간이었지.\""]], [["There was something so commanding in the distress of the old man, that Heyward did not dare to venture a syllable of consolation.", "노인의 비탄에는 무언가 압도하는 데가 있어서, 〈헤이워드〉는 위로의 말 한마디조차 감히 건네지 못했다."], ["Munro sat utterly unconscious of the other's presence, his features exposed and working with the anguish of his regrets, while heavy tears fell from his eyes, and rolled unheeded from his cheeks to the floor.", "〈먼로〉는 상대의 존재마저 까맣게 잊은 채 앉아 있었으니, 그 얼굴은 회한의 고뇌로 드러난 채 일그러졌고, 무거운 눈물이 두 눈에서 흘러내려, 뺨을 타고 바닥으로 떨어져도 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["At length he moved, as if suddenly recovering his recollection; when he arose, and taking a single turn across the room, he approached his companion with an air of military grandeur, and demanded,--", "마침내 그는 불현듯 정신을 차린 듯 몸을 움직였으니, 일어나 방을 한 차례 가로질러 거닌 뒤, 군인다운 위엄을 띠고 동행자에게 다가가 물었다."]], [["\"Have you not, Major Heyward, some communication that I should hear from the Marquis de Montcalm?\"", "\"〈헤이워드〉 소령, 자네가 내게 전할, 〈몽칼름〉 후작이 보낸 무슨 전언이 있지 않은가?\""]], [["Duncan started, in his turn, and immediately commenced, in an embarrassed voice, the half-forgotten message.", "이번에는 〈던컨〉이 흠칫 놀라, 곧장 당황한 목소리로 반쯤 잊혔던 전갈을 전하기 시작했다."], ["It is unnecessary to dwell upon the evasive, though polite manner, with which the French general had eluded every attempt of Heyward to worm from him the purport of the communication he had proposed making, or on the decided, though still polished message, by which he now gave his enemy to understand, that unless he chose to receive it in person, he should not receive it at all.", "프랑스 장군이, 자신이 전하겠다고 제안한 그 전언의 요지를 캐내려는 〈헤이워드〉의 온갖 시도를 정중하면서도 교묘히 피해 간 그 회피적인 태도에 대해서나, 또 직접 받지 않겠다면 아예 받지 못하리라는 뜻을, 단호하면서도 여전히 세련되게 적에게 알린 그 결연한 전갈에 대해 굳이 늘어놓을 필요는 없으리라."], ["As Munro listened to the detail of Duncan, the excited feelings of the father gradually gave way before the obligations of his station, and when the other was done, he saw before him nothing but the veteran, swelling with the wounded feelings of a soldier.", "〈먼로〉가 〈던컨〉의 소상한 이야기에 귀 기울이는 동안, 아비로서 격앙되었던 감정은 차츰 제 직분의 의무 앞에 자리를 내주었으니, 〈던컨〉이 이야기를 마쳤을 때 그가 마주한 것은, 군인의 상한 감정으로 부풀어 오른 노전사뿐이었다."]], [["\"You have said enough, Major Heyward!\"", "\"말은 그만하면 충분하네, 〈헤이워드〉 소령!\""], ["exclaimed the angry old man: \"enough to make a volume of commentary on French civility.", "노여운 노인이 외쳤다. \"프랑스식 예절에 관한 주석을 한 권 엮어 낼 만큼 충분해."], ["Here has this gentleman invited me to a conference, and when I send him a capable substitute, for ye're all that, Duncan, though your years are but few, he answers me with a riddle.\"", "이 신사가 내게 회담을 청해 놓고, 내가 유능한 대리인을 보냈건만—자네야말로 그런 사람이지, 〈던컨〉, 비록 나이는 어릴지언정—그자는 내게 수수께끼로 답하다니.\""]], [["\"He may have thought less favorably of the substitute, my dear sir; and you will remember that the invitation, which he now repeats, was to the commandant of the works, and not to his second.\"", "\"그가 대리인을 그리 탐탁잖게 여겼는지도 모릅니다, 대령님. 게다가 그가 지금 거듭하는 그 초청은, 요새의 사령관에게 한 것이지 그 부관에게 한 것이 아님을 기억하셔야겠지요.\""]], [["\"Well, sir, is not a substitute clothed with all the power and dignity of him who grants the commission?", "\"흠, 이 사람아, 대리인이란 권한을 위임한 자의 모든 권력과 위엄을 두른 자가 아니던가?"], ["He wishes to confer with Munro!", "그가 〈먼로〉와 회담하길 바란다고!"], ["Faith, sir, I have much inclination to indulge the man, if it should only be to let him behold the firm countenance we maintain in spite of his numbers and his summons.", "정말이지, 이 사람아, 그자의 청을 들어주고픈 마음이 굴뚝같네, 설령 그것이 그자의 병력과 그 통첩에도 아랑곳없이 우리가 지키는 굳건한 얼굴을 그자에게 보여 주기 위해서일 뿐이라 해도 말이야."], ["There might be no bad policy in such a stroke, young man.\"", "그런 한 수에 나쁜 책략이랄 것도 없겠지, 젊은이.\""]], [["Duncan, who believed it of the last importance that they should speedily come at the contents of the letter borne by the scout, gladly encouraged this idea.", "척후가 지닌 편지의 내용을 신속히 알아내는 일이야말로 더없이 중대하다고 여기던 〈던컨〉은, 이 생각을 기꺼이 부추겼다."]], [["\"Without doubt, he could gather no confidence by witnessing our indifference,\"", "\"의심할 바 없이, 우리의 무심함을 목격한들 그가 자신감을 얻지는 못할 것입니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["\"You never said truer word.", "\"그보다 더 옳은 말은 없을 걸세."], ["I could wish, sir, that he would visit the works in open day, and in the form of a storming party: that is the least failing method of proving the countenance of an enemy, and would be far preferable to the battering system he has chosen.", "나로서는, 이 사람아, 그가 환한 대낮에, 그것도 돌격대의 형세로 이 요새를 찾아 주었으면 싶네. 그것이야말로 적의 얼굴을 가늠하는, 가장 어긋남이 적은 방법이며, 그가 택한 포격 방식보다는 훨씬 나으니까."], ["The beauty and manliness of warfare has been much deformed, Major Heyward, by the arts of your Monsieur Vauban.", "자네의 그 무슈 보방의 기술 탓에, 전쟁의 아름다움과 사내다움이 몹시도 일그러지고 말았네, 〈헤이워드〉 소령."], ["Our ancestors were far above such scientific cowardice!\"", "우리 선조들은 그런 학술적 비겁함 따위는 한참 위에 있었지!\""]], [["\"It may be very true, sir; but we are now obliged to repel art by art.", "\"지당하신 말씀일지도 모릅니다, 대령님. 허나 우리는 이제 기술을 기술로 물리쳐야 하는 형편입니다."], ["What is your pleasure in the matter of the interview?\"", "회견 문제에 관해서는 어찌하기를 바라십니까?\""]], [["\"I will meet the Frenchman, and that without fear or delay; promptly; sir, as becomes a servant of my royal master.", "\"그 프랑스인을 만나겠네, 그것도 두려움이나 지체 없이, 신속하게 말일세. 이 사람아, 내 임금을 섬기는 신하답게 말이야."], ["Go, Major Heyward, and give them a flourish of the music; and send out a messenger to let them know who is coming.", "가게, 〈헤이워드〉 소령, 그들에게 음악을 한 가락 울려 주고, 누가 가는지 알리도록 전령을 내보내게."], ["We will follow with a small guard, for such respect is due to one who holds the honor of his king in keeping; and harkee, Duncan,\"", "우리는 소수의 호위와 함께 뒤따르겠네, 임금의 명예를 맡아 지키는 자에게는 그만한 예우가 마땅하니. 그리고 잘 듣게, 〈던컨〉.\""], ["he added, in a half whisper, though they were alone, \"it may be prudent to have some aid at hand, in case there should be treachery at the bottom of it all.\"", "그는, 둘뿐이었음에도 반쯤 속삭이며 덧붙였다. \"이 모든 일의 바닥에 배신이 깔려 있을 경우를 대비해, 가까이에 약간의 지원을 두는 편이 신중하겠네.\""]], [["The young man availed himself of this order to quit the apartment; and, as the day was fast coming to a close, he hastened, without delay, to make the necessary arrangements.", "젊은이는 이 명령을 핑계 삼아 방을 나섰으니, 해가 빠르게 저물고 있던 터라, 지체 없이 서둘러 필요한 채비를 갖추었다."], ["A very few minutes only were necessary to parade a few files, and to despatch an orderly with a flag to announce the approach of the commandant of the fort.", "몇 줄의 병사를 정렬시키고, 깃발을 든 전령을 보내어 요새 사령관의 행차를 알리는 데에는 단 몇 분이면 족했다."], ["When Duncan had done both these, he led the guard to the sally-port, near which he found his superior ready, waiting his appearance.", "〈던컨〉은 이 두 가지를 마치자, 호위대를 이끌고 출격문으로 갔으니, 그 가까이에서 채비를 갖추고 그가 나타나기를 기다리는 상관을 발견했다."], ["As soon as the usual ceremonials of a military departure were observed, the veteran and his more youthful companion left the fortress, attended by the escort.", "군의 출진에 따르는 통상의 의식이 치러지자마자, 노전사와 그 젊은 동행자는 호위대를 거느리고 요새를 떠났다."]], [["They had proceeded only a hundred yards from the works, when the little array which attended the French general to the conference, was seen issuing from the hollow way, which formed the bed of a brook that ran between the batteries of the besiegers and the fort.", "요새에서 백 야드밖에 나아가지 않았을 때, 프랑스 장군을 회담으로 수행하는 작은 행렬이, 포위군의 포대와 요새 사이를 흐르는 시내의 바닥을 이룬 움푹한 길에서 나오는 것이 보였다."], ["From the moment that Munro left his own works to appear in front of his enemies, his air had been grand, and his step and countenance highly military.", "〈먼로〉가 제 진지를 떠나 적들 앞에 나서는 순간부터, 그의 거동은 당당했고, 그 걸음과 얼굴은 더없이 군인다웠다."], ["The instant he caught a glimpse of the white plume that waved in the hat of Montcalm, his eye lighted, and age no longer appeared to possess any influence over his vast and still muscular person.", "〈몽칼름〉의 모자에 나부끼는 흰 깃을 흘끗 잡아챈 순간, 그의 눈은 빛났고, 노쇠함은 더 이상 그 거대하고 여전히 다부진 몸에 어떤 영향도 미치지 못하는 듯했다."]], [["\"Speak to the boys to be watchful, sir,\"", "\"부하들에게 경계하라 일러 두게, 이 사람아.\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 〈던컨〉에게 말했다."], ["he said, in an undertone, to Duncan; \"and to look well to their flints and steel, for one is never safe with a servant of these Louises; at the same time, we will show them the front of men in deep security.", "\"부싯돌과 격철을 잘 살피라고 말이야, 이 루이의 신하들과는 결코 안심할 수 없으니. 그러는 한편, 우리는 그들에게 깊은 안심에 든 사내들의 얼굴을 보여 주세."], ["Ye'll understand me, Major Heyward!\"", "내 말 알아듣겠지, 〈헤이워드〉 소령!\""]], [["He was interrupted by the clamor of a drum from the approaching Frenchmen, which was immediately answered, when each party pushed an orderly in advance, bearing a white flag, and the wary Scotsman halted, with his guard close at his back.", "다가오는 프랑스인들 쪽에서 울린 요란한 북소리에 그의 말이 끊겼으니, 그것에 곧장 응답이 이어졌고, 그러자 양편은 저마다 흰 깃발을 든 전령을 앞세웠으며, 신중한 스코틀랜드인은 호위대를 바짝 등 뒤에 둔 채 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["As soon as this slight salutation had passed, Montcalm moved towards them with a quick but graceful step, baring his head to the veteran, and dropping his spotless plume nearly to the earth in courtesy.", "이 가벼운 인사가 오가자마자, 〈몽칼름〉은 빠르면서도 우아한 걸음으로 그들에게 다가왔으니, 노전사에게 모자를 벗고, 그 티 없는 깃을 예의로 거의 땅에까지 드리웠다."], ["If the air of Munro was more commanding and manly, it wanted both the ease and insinuating polish of that of the Frenchman.", "〈먼로〉의 거동이 더 위엄 있고 사내다웠다면, 그것에는 저 프랑스인의 거동이 지닌 편안함도 마음을 파고드는 세련됨도 결여되어 있었다."], ["Neither spoke for a few moments, each regarding the other with curious and interested eyes.", "두 사람은 몇 순간 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 저마다 호기심과 흥미에 찬 눈으로 상대를 바라보았다."], ["Then, as became his superior rank and the nature of the interview, Montcalm broke the silence.", "이윽고, 그의 더 높은 지위와 회견의 성격에 걸맞게, 〈몽칼름〉이 침묵을 깼다."], ["After uttering the usual words of greeting, he turned to Duncan, and continued with a smile of recognition, speaking always in French,--\n[Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_", "통상의 인사말을 건넨 뒤, 그는 〈던컨〉을 향해 돌아서서, 알아본다는 미소를 지으며, 시종 프랑스어로 말을 이었다."]], [["_As soon as this slight salutation had passed, Montcalm moved towards them with a quick but graceful step, baring his head to the veteran, and dropping his spotless plume nearly to the earth in courtesy_]", "\"무슈, 이번 자리에 귀공이 함께해 주시는 즐거움을 우리에게 베풀어 주시니 기쁩니다. 평범한 통역을 쓸 필요가 없겠군요. 귀공의 손에 맡기면, 내가 직접 귀공의 언어로 말하는 것과 다름없는 안심을 느낄 테니 말입니다.\""]], [["\"I am rejoiced, monsieur, that you have given us the pleasure of your company on this occasion. There will be no necessity to employ an ordinary interpreter; for, in your hands, I feel the same security as if I spoke your language myself.\" Duncan acknowledged the compliment, when Montcalm, turning to his guard, which, in imitation of that of their enemies, pressed close upon him, continued,--", "〈던컨〉이 그 찬사에 답례하자, 〈몽칼름〉은, 적의 호위대를 본떠 자신에게 바짝 다가선 제 호위대를 향해 돌아서며 말을 이었다."]], [["\"En arriere, mes enfans--il fait chaud; retirez-vous un peu.\"", "\"앙 아리에르, 메 장팡—일 페 쇼, 르티레부 욍 푸.\""]], [["Before Major Heyward would imitate this proof of confidence, he glanced his eyes around the plain, and beheld with uneasiness the numerous dusky groups of savages, who looked out from the margin of the surrounding woods, curious spectators of the interview.", "* 앙 아리에르…: \"물러서거라, 얘들아—덥구나. 조금 떨어지거라\"라는 뜻의 프랑스어. 〈헤이워드〉 소령은 이 신뢰의 증표를 본받기에 앞서, 두 눈으로 들판을 두루 살폈으니, 에워싼 숲의 가장자리에서 내다보며 회견을 호기심으로 구경하는 미개인들의 거뭇한 무리가 무수함을 불안한 마음으로 보았다."]], [["\"Monsieur de Montcalm will readily acknowledge the difference in our situation,\"", "\"〈몽칼름〉 후작께서는 우리 처지의 차이를 흔쾌히 인정하실 줄 압니다.\""], ["he said, with some embarrassment, pointing at the same time towards those dangerous foes, who were to be seen in almost every direction.", "그가 다소 당혹스러운 기색으로, 동시에 거의 사방에서 보이는 저 위험한 적들을 가리키며 말했다."], ["\"Were we to dismiss our guard, we should stand here at the mercy of our enemies.\"", "\"우리가 호위대를 물린다면, 우리는 이 자리에서 적들의 처분에 내맡겨지는 셈입니다.\""]], [["\"Monsieur, you have the plighted faith of _un gentilhomme Francais_; for your safety,\"", "\"무슈, 귀공에게는 한 프랑스 신사가 맹세한 신의가 있습니다, 귀공의 안전을 위해서 말이지요.\""], ["returned Montcalm, laying his hand impressively on his heart; \"it should suffice.\"", "〈몽칼름〉이 한 손을 인상적으로 제 가슴에 얹으며 대답했다. \"그것으로 족할 것입니다.\""]], [["\"It shall.", "\"족하겠지요."], ["Fall back,\"", "물러서게.\" 〈던컨〉이 호위대를 이끄는 장교에게 덧붙였다."], ["Duncan added to the officer who led the escort; \"fall back, sir, beyond hearing, and wait for orders.\"", "\"물러서게, 소리가 들리지 않는 곳까지. 그리고 명령을 기다리게.\""]], [["Munro witnessed this movement with manifest uneasiness; nor did he fail to demand an instant explanation.", "〈먼로〉는 이 움직임을 역력한 불안과 함께 지켜보았으니, 즉각 해명을 요구하기를 잊지 않았다."]], [["\"Is it not our interest, sir, to betray no distrust?\"", "\"불신을 조금도 내비치지 않는 것이 우리에게 이롭지 않겠습니까, 대령님?\""], ["retorted Duncan.", "〈던컨〉이 응수했다."], ["\"Monsieur de Montcalm pledges his word for our safety, and I have ordered the men to withdraw a little, in order to prove how much we depend on his assurance.\"", "\"〈몽칼름〉 후작이 우리의 안전을 두고 그 말을 걸었으니, 저는 우리가 그의 보증에 얼마나 의지하는지를 보이고자 병사들에게 조금 물러서라 명한 것입니다.\""]], [["\"It may be all right, sir, but I have no overweening reliance on the faith of these marquesses, or marquis, as they call themselves.", "\"다 옳은 일일지도 모르지, 이 사람아, 허나 나는 이 후작들, 아니 제놈들이 일컫는 대로 후작이라는 자들의 신의에 그리 지나친 믿음을 두지 않네."], ["Their patents of nobility are too common to be certain that they bear the seal of true honor.\"", "그들의 귀족 작위장은 너무도 흔해 빠져, 그것이 참된 명예의 인장을 지녔다고 장담할 수 없으니까.\""]], [["\"You forget, dear sir, that we confer with an officer distinguished alike in Europe and America for his deeds.", "\"잊지 마십시오, 대령님, 우리는 그 공적으로 유럽과 아메리카에서 똑같이 이름난 장교와 회담하고 있습니다."], ["From a soldier of his reputation we can have nothing to apprehend.\"", "그만한 명성을 지닌 군인에게서 우리가 두려워할 것은 아무것도 없습니다.\""]], [["The old man made a gesture of resignation, though his rigid features still betrayed his obstinate adherence to a distrust, which he derived from a sort of hereditary contempt of his enemy, rather than from any present signs which might warrant so uncharitable a feeling.", "노인은 체념하는 몸짓을 했으나, 그 굳은 얼굴은 여전히 그 불신을 완강히 고수함을 드러냈으니, 그것은 그토록 박정한 감정을 정당화할 만한 어떤 당장의 징후에서라기보다, 적을 향한 일종의 세습된 경멸에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["Montcalm waited patiently until this little dialogue in demi-voice was ended, when he drew nigher, and opened the subject of their conference.", "〈몽칼름〉은 반쯤 죽인 목소리로 오간 이 짤막한 대화가 끝나기를 참을성 있게 기다렸으니, 그것이 끝나자 더 가까이 다가서서, 회담의 본론을 꺼냈다."]], [["\"I have solicited this interview from your superior, monsieur,\"", "\"내가 귀공의 상관에게 이 회견을 청한 것은, 무슈,\""], ["he said, \"because I believe he will allow himself to be persuaded that he has already done everything which is necessary for the honor of his prince, and will not listen to the admonitions of humanity.", "그가 말했다. \"그가 이미 자기 군주의 명예를 위해 필요한 모든 일을 다했음을 스스로 납득하게 되리라 믿었기 때문이며, 또 인도(人道)의 권고에 귀 기울이지 않을까 해서였습니다."], ["I will forever bear testimony that his resistance has been gallant, and was continued as long as there was hope.\"", "나는 그의 저항이 용맹했고, 희망이 있는 한 이어졌음을 영원히 증언하겠습니다.\""]], [["When this opening was translated to Munro, he answered with dignity, but with sufficient courtesy,--", "이 서두가 〈먼로〉에게 옮겨지자, 그는 위엄을 갖추되 충분한 예의로 답했다."]], [["\"However I may prize such testimony from Monsieur Montcalm, it will be more valuable when it shall be better merited.\"", "\"〈몽칼름〉 후작에게서 그런 증언을 받음을 내 아무리 귀히 여긴다 한들, 그것이 더 마땅히 받을 만한 것이 될 때 더욱 값지겠지요.\""]], [["The French general smiled, as Duncan gave him the purport of this reply, and observed,--", "프랑스 장군은, 〈던컨〉이 이 대답의 요지를 전하자 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["\"What is now so freely accorded to approved courage, may be refused to useless obstinacy.", "\"입증된 용기에 지금 그토록 선뜻 베풀어지는 것이, 부질없는 고집에는 거절될 수도 있습니다."], ["Monsieur would wish to see my camp, and witness, for himself, our numbers, and the impossibility of his resisting them, with success?\"", "무슈께서는 내 진영을 보시고, 우리의 병력과, 그것에 성공적으로 맞서기란 불가능하다는 점을 친히 목격하고 싶으신지요?\""]], [["\"I know that the king of France is well served,\"", "\"나는 프랑스 임금이 잘 섬김을 받고 있음을 압니다.\""], ["returned the unmoved Scotsman, as soon as Duncan ended his translation; \"but my own royal master has as many and as faithful troops.\"", "〈던컨〉이 통역을 마치자마자 동요 없는 스코틀랜드인이 대답했다. \"허나 내 임금께도 그만큼 많고 그만큼 충직한 군대가 있소이다.\""]], [["\"Though not at hand, fortunately for us,\"", "\"다만, 우리에게는 다행스럽게도, 가까이에 있지 않지요.\""], ["said Montcalm, without waiting, in his ardor, for the interpreter.", "〈몽칼름〉이, 그 열의에 차 통역을 기다리지도 않고 말했다."], ["\"There is a destiny in war, to which a brave man knows how to submit, with the same courage that he faces his foes.\"", "\"전쟁에는 운명이라는 것이 있어, 용감한 사내라면 제 적을 마주하는 것과 똑같은 용기로 그것에 따를 줄 안답니다.\""]], [["\"Had I been conscious that Monsieur Montcalm was master of the English, I should have spared myself the trouble of so awkward a translation,\"", "\"〈몽칼름〉 후작께서 영어에 능하심을 진작 알았더라면, 이토록 서툰 통역의 수고를 덜었을 것을.\""], ["said the vexed Duncan, dryly; remembering instantly his recent by-play with Munro.", "분이 치민 〈던컨〉이 무뚝뚝하게 말했으니, 〈먼로〉와 막 주고받았던 짤막한 연기가 곧장 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["\"Your pardon, monsieur,\"", "\"용서하시오, 무슈.\""], ["rejoined the Frenchman, suffering a slight color to appear on his dark cheek.", "프랑스인이, 거뭇한 뺨에 옅은 홍조가 어리도록 내버려 둔 채 응수했다."], ["\"There is a vast difference between understanding and speaking a foreign tongue; you will, therefore, please to assist me still.\"", "\"외국 말을 알아듣는 것과 말하는 것 사이에는 어마어마한 차이가 있지요. 그러니 부디 계속 나를 도와주시오.\""], ["Then after a short pause, he added, \"These hills afford us every opportunity of reconnoitring your works, messieurs, and I am possibly as well acquainted with their weak condition as you can be yourselves.\"", "그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"이 언덕들은 우리에게 귀공들의 요새를 정찰할 온갖 기회를 주니, 무슈들, 나는 아마 귀공들 자신만큼이나 그 허약한 형편을 잘 알고 있을 것입니다.\""]], [["\"Ask the French general if his glasses can reach to the Hudson,\"", "\"프랑스 장군에게 묻게, 그의 망원경이 〈허드슨〉강에까지 미치는지 말일세.\""], ["said Munro, proudly; \"and if he knows when and where to expect the army of Webb.\"", "〈먼로〉가 자랑스레 말했다. \"그리고 〈웨브〉의 군대가 언제 어디서 올지를 그가 아는지도.\""]], [["\"Let General Webb be his own interpreter,\"", "\"〈웨브〉 장군이 직접 통역하게 하시지요.\""], ["returned the politic Montcalm, suddenly extending an open letter towards Munro, as he spoke; \"you will there learn, monsieur, that his movements are not likely to prove embarrassing to my army.\"", "책략에 능한 〈몽칼름〉이, 말하면서 별안간 펼쳐진 편지 한 장을 〈먼로〉를 향해 내밀며 대답했다. \"거기서 알게 되실 겁니다, 무슈, 그의 움직임이 내 군대에 곤란을 끼칠 성싶지 않다는 것을 말입니다.\""]], [["The veteran seized the offered paper, without waiting for Duncan to translate the speech, and with an eagerness that betrayed how important he deemed its contents.", "노전사는 〈던컨〉이 그 말을 통역하기를 기다리지도 않고, 그 내용을 얼마나 중대히 여기는지 드러내는 간절함으로 내밀어진 종이를 낚아챘다."], ["As his eye passed hastily over the words, his countenance changed from its look of military pride to one of deep chagrin: his lip began to quiver; and, suffering the paper to fall from his hand, his head dropped upon his chest, like that of a man whose hopes were withered at a single blow.", "그의 눈이 그 글자들 위를 황급히 훑어 가는 동안, 그의 얼굴은 군인다운 자긍의 표정에서 깊은 낭패의 표정으로 변해 갔다. 입술이 떨리기 시작했고, 손에서 종이가 떨어지도록 내버려 둔 채, 단 한 번의 타격에 희망이 시들어 버린 사람처럼 고개가 가슴께로 떨구어졌다."], ["Duncan caught the letter from the ground, and without apology for the liberty he took, he read at a glance its cruel purport.", "〈던컨〉이 바닥에서 편지를 집어 들었으니, 제멋대로 그리한 무례를 사과하지도 않은 채, 그 잔혹한 내용을 한눈에 읽어 냈다."], ["Their common superior, so far from encouraging them to resist, advised a speedy surrender, urging in the plainest language as a reason, the utter impossibility of his sending a single man to their rescue.", "그들 공동의 상관은, 그들에게 저항을 북돋기는커녕, 신속한 항복을 권하면서, 단 한 명의 병사도 그들의 구원에 보낼 수 없다는 절대적인 불가능을 가장 평이한 말로 그 이유랍시고 역설하고 있었다."]], [["\"Here is no deception!\"", "\"여기엔 속임수가 없습니다!\""], ["exclaimed Duncan, examining the billet both inside and out; \"this is the signature of Webb, and must be the captured letter.\"", "〈던컨〉이 그 짤막한 글을 안팎으로 살피며 외쳤다. \"이것은 〈웨브〉의 서명이며, 노획된 편지가 틀림없습니다.\""]], [["\"The man has betrayed me!\"", "\"그자가 나를 배신했네!\""], ["Munro at length bitterly exclaimed: \"he has brought dishonor to the door of one where disgrace was never before known to dwell, and shame has he heaped heavily on my gray hairs.\"", "마침내 〈먼로〉가 비통하게 외쳤다. \"그자는, 일찍이 치욕이 깃든 적 없던 집의 문간에 불명예를 끌어들였고, 내 백발 위에 부끄러움을 무겁게도 쌓아 올렸네.\""]], [["\"Say not so,\"", "\"그리 말씀 마십시오.\""], ["cried Duncan; \"we are yet masters of the fort, and of our honor.", "〈던컨〉이 외쳤다. \"우리는 아직 요새의, 그리고 우리 명예의 주인입니다."], ["Let us then sell our lives at such a rate as shall make our enemies believe the purchase too dear.\"", "그러니 우리 목숨을, 적들이 그 값을 너무 비싸다 여길 만한 값에 팝시다.\""]], [["\"Boy, I thank thee,\"", "\"이 사람아, 고맙네.\""], ["exclaimed the old man, rousing himself from his stupor; \"you have, for once, reminded Munro of his duty.", "노인이 그 망연함에서 정신을 추스르며 외쳤다. \"자네가 이번 한 번, 〈먼로〉에게 제 의무를 일깨워 주었군."], ["We will go back, and dig our graves behind those ramparts.\"", "우리는 돌아가, 저 성벽 뒤에 우리의 무덤을 파세.\""]], [["\"Messieurs,\"", "\"무슈들,\""], ["said Montcalm, advancing towards them a step, in generous interest, \"you little know Louis de St.", "〈몽칼름〉이 관대한 관심에서 그들에게 한 걸음 다가서며 말했다."], ["Veran, if you believe him capable of profiting by this letter to humble brave men, or to build up a dishonest reputation for himself.", "\"귀공들이 〈루이 드 생베랑〉을, 이 편지를 빌미로 용감한 사내들을 욕보이거나 자신을 위해 떳떳지 못한 명성을 쌓을 자라 여긴다면, 그를 너무도 모르는 것입니다."], ["Listen to my terms before you leave me.\"", "나를 떠나기 전에 내 조건을 들어 보시오.\""]], [["\"What says the Frenchman?\"", "\"프랑스인이 뭐라 하는가?\""], ["demanded the veteran, sternly; \"does he make a merit of having captured a scout, with a note from headquarters?", "노전사가 준엄하게 물었다. \"그자는 본부의 쪽지를 지닌 척후를 사로잡은 것을 공으로 내세우는 게냐?"], ["Sir, he had better raise this siege, to go and sit down before Edward if he wishes to frighten his enemy with words.\"", "이 사람아, 그자가 적을 말로 겁주고 싶거든, 차라리 이 포위를 풀고 〈에드워드〉 요새 앞에나 가서 진을 치는 편이 낫겠네.\""]], [["Duncan explained the other's meaning.", "〈던컨〉이 상대의 뜻을 설명했다."]], [["\"Monsieur de Montcalm, we will hear you,\"", "\"〈몽칼름〉 후작, 우리가 귀공의 말을 듣겠소이다.\""], ["the veteran added, more calmly, as Duncan ended.", "〈던컨〉이 말을 마치자 노전사가 한층 차분히 덧붙였다."]], [["\"To retain the fort is now impossible,\"", "\"이제 요새를 지키기란 불가능합니다.\" 그 너그러운 적이 말했다."], ["said his liberal enemy; \"it is necessary to the interests of my master that it should be destroyed; but, as for yourselves, and your brave comrades, there is no privilege dear to a soldier that shall be denied.\"", "\"이곳이 파괴되는 것이 내 군주의 이익에 필요하지요. 허나 귀공들과 귀공들의 용감한 동지들에 관해서라면, 군인에게 소중한 어떤 특권도 거절되지 않을 것입니다.\""]], [["\"Our colors?\"", "\"우리 군기는요?\""], ["demanded Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"Carry them to England, and show them to your king.\"", "\"〈영국〉으로 가져가, 귀공의 임금에게 보여 주시오.\""]], [["\"Our arms?\"", "\"우리 무기는요?\""]], [["\"Keep them; none can use them better.\"", "\"지니시오. 누구도 그것을 더 잘 쓰지 못할 테니.\""]], [["\"Our march; the surrender of the place?\"", "\"우리의 행군은, 그리고 이곳의 양도는요?\""]], [["\"Shall all be done in a way most honorable to yourselves.\"", "\"모두 귀공들에게 더없이 명예로운 방식으로 이루어질 것입니다.\""]], [["Duncan now turned to explain these proposals to his commander, who heard him with amazement, and a sensibility that was deeply touched by such unusual and unexpected generosity.", "이제 〈던컨〉이 이 제안들을 자신의 지휘관에게 설명하고자 돌아서니, 지휘관은 그것을 경이로움과 함께, 그토록 예사롭지 않고 뜻밖의 관대함에 깊이 동하는 감수성으로 들었다."]], [["\"Go you, Duncan,\"", "\"가게, 〈던컨〉.\""], ["he said; \"go with this marquess, as indeed marquess he should be; go to his marquee and arrange it all.", "그가 말했다. \"이 후작과 함께 가게, 정녕 후작이라 할 만한 사람이니."], ["I have lived to see two things in my old age, that never did I expect to behold.", "그의 막사로 가서 모든 것을 매듭짓게. 나는 이 늘그막에, 결코 보리라 기대치 않았던 두 가지를 살아서 보았네."], ["An Englishman afraid to support a friend, and a Frenchman too honest to profit by his advantage.\"", "벗을 돕기를 두려워하는 〈영국〉인과, 제 유리한 처지를 이용하기에는 너무도 정직한 프랑스인을 말일세.\""]], [["So saying, the veteran again dropped his head to his chest, and returned slowly towards the fort, exhibiting, by the dejection of his air, to the anxious garrison, a harbinger of evil tidings.", "그리 말하고서, 노전사는 다시 고개를 가슴께로 떨군 채, 천천히 요새를 향해 돌아갔으니, 그 풀 죽은 거동으로, 근심에 찬 수비대에게 흉한 소식의 전조를 내비쳤다."]], [["From the shock of this unexpected blow the haughty feelings of Munro never recovered; but from that moment there commenced a change in his determined character, which accompanied him to a speedy grave.", "이 뜻밖의 타격이 안긴 충격으로부터 〈먼로〉의 도도한 감정은 결코 회복되지 못했다. 다만 그 순간부터, 그의 결연한 성품에 한 변화가 일기 시작했으니, 그것은 그를 머지않은 무덤에까지 따라갔다."], ["Duncan remained to settle the terms of the capitulation.", "〈던컨〉은 항복의 조건을 마무리하고자 남았다."], ["He was seen to re-enter the works during the first watches of the night, and immediately after a private conference with the commandant, to leave them again, It was then openly announced, that hostilities must cease--Munro having signed a treaty, by which the place was to be yielded to the enemy, with the morning; the garrison to retain their arms, their colors, and their baggage, and consequently, according to military opinion, their honor.", "그가 밤의 초경(初更)에 요새로 다시 들어가는 것이 보였고, 사령관과 은밀한 회담을 가진 직후, 다시 그곳을 떠나는 것이 보였다. 그제야 적대 행위를 중지해야 한다는 것이 공공연히 알려졌으니—〈먼로〉가 한 조약에 서명했던바, 그에 따라 이곳은 아침과 더불어 적에게 양도되며, 수비대는 그 무기와 군기와 군장을, 따라서 군인의 통념에 따르자면 그 명예를 보전하기로 되었다."]], [["\"Weave we the woof.", "우리는 씨실을 짠다."], ["The thread is spun.", "날실은 다 자아졌다."], ["The web is wove. The work is done.\"", "그물은 짜였고, 일은 끝났다."]], [["The hostile armies, which lay in the wilds of the Horican, passed the night of the 9th of August, 1757, much in the manner they would had they encountered on the fairest fields of Europe.", "그레이\n〈호리컨〉의 황야에 진을 친 적대하는 양쪽 군대는, 1757년 8월 9일 밤을, 유럽의 가장 빼어난 전장에서 맞붙었더라도 그러했을 모양과 별반 다르지 않게 보냈다."], ["While the conquered were still, sullen, and dejected, the victors triumphed.", "패한 자들은 여전히 말이 없고, 침울하고, 풀이 죽어 있었으나, 승자들은 의기양양했다."], ["But there are limits alike to grief and joy; and long before the watches of the morning came, the stillness of those boundless woods was only broken by a gay call from some exulting young Frenchman of the advanced pickets, or a menacing challenge from the fort, which sternly forbade the approach of any hostile footsteps before the stipulated moment.", "그러나 슬픔에도 기쁨에도 한계가 있는 법이어서, 새벽의 야경이 돌아오기 한참 전에, 그 가없는 숲의 정적을 깨뜨린 것이라곤 전초선에 나선 어느 들뜬 젊은 프랑스 병사의 흥겨운 외침이나, 정해진 시각이 오기 전에는 어떤 적의 발걸음도 다가오는 것을 준엄하게 금하는 요새의 위협적인 수하(誰何)뿐이었다."], ["Even these occasional threatening sounds ceased to be heard in that dull hour which precedes the day, at which period a listener might have sought in vain any evidence of the presence of those armed powers that then slumbered on the shores of the \"holy lake.\"", "이 이따금 들리던 위협의 소리마저, 동트기 직전의 그 음울한 시각에는 더 들리지 않게 되었으니, 그 무렵에는 귀 기울이는 자라도 \"거룩한 호수\"의 기슭에 잠들어 있는 그 무장한 세력의 존재를 입증할 무엇 하나 찾으려 해도 헛수고였을 것이다."]], [["It was during these moments of deep silence, that the canvas which concealed the entrance to a spacious marquee in the French encampment was shoved aside, and a man issued from beneath the drapery into the open air.", "이렇게 깊은 정적이 깔린 순간에, 프랑스 진영의 어느 널찍한 천막 입구를 가리고 있던 천이 옆으로 젖혀지더니, 한 사내가 휘장 아래에서 트인 바깥 공기 속으로 나왔다."], ["He was enveloped in a cloak that might have been intended as a protection from the chilling damps of the woods, but which served equally well as a mantle, to conceal his person.", "그는 숲의 으슬으슬한 습기를 막으려는 것이었을 외투를 두르고 있었으나, 그것은 또한 그 몸을 감추는 망토 노릇도 똑같이 잘 해 주었다."], ["He was permitted to pass the grenadier, who watched over the slumbers of the French commander, without interruption, the man making the usual salute which betokens military deference, as the other passed swiftly through the little city of tents, in the direction of William Henry.", "그는 프랑스군 사령관의 잠을 지키는 척탄병을 거리낌 없이 지나치도록 허락받았으니, 그가 천막의 작은 도성 사이를 〈윌리엄 헨리〉 방면으로 빠르게 지나갈 때, 그 병사는 군인다운 경의를 나타내는 여느 거수경례를 올렸다."], ["Whenever this unknown individual encountered one of the numberless sentinels who crossed his path, his answer was prompt, and as it appeared satisfactory; for he was uniformly allowed to proceed, without further interrogation.", "이 알 수 없는 인물이 제 앞길을 가로지르는 무수한 보초 가운데 하나와 마주칠 때마다, 그의 대답은 신속했고 또 흡족한 듯했으니, 그는 한결같이 더 이상의 심문 없이 나아가도록 허락받았다."]], [["With the exception of such repeated, but brief interruptions, he had moved, silently, from the centre of the camp, to its most advanced outposts, when he drew nigh the soldier who held his watch nearest to the works of the enemy.", "이런 거듭된, 그러나 짧은 제지를 빼고는, 그는 진영의 중심에서 가장 앞으로 나선 초소까지 소리 없이 움직였으며, 이윽고 적의 진지에 가장 가까이 보초를 서는 병사에게 다가갔다."], ["As he approached he was received with the usual challenge,--", "그가 다가가자 여느 수하로 맞이되었다."]], [["\"Qui vive?\"", "\"누구냐?\""]], [["\"France,\"", "\"프랑스.\""], ["was the reply.", "대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["\"Le mot d'ordre?\"", "\"암호는?\""]], [["\"La victoire,\"", "\"승리.\""], ["said the other, drawing so nigh as to be heard in a loud whisper.", "다른 이가 큰 속삭임으로 들릴 만큼 가까이 다가서며 말했다."]], [["\"C'est bien,\"", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["returned the sentinel, throwing his musket from the charge to his shoulder; \"vous vous promenez bien matin, monsieur!\"", "보초가 견착 자세에서 총을 어깨로 돌려 메며 대꾸했다. \"꽤나 이른 아침에 산책을 하시는군요, 나리!\""]], [["\"Il est necessaire d'etre vigilant, mon enfant,\"", "\"방심하지 않는 것이 필요하지, 이 사람아.\""], ["the other observed, dropping a fold of his cloak, and looking the soldier close in the face, as he passed him, still continuing his way towards the British fortification.", "다른 이가 외투 자락 한 겹을 떨어뜨리며, 여전히 영국군 요새 쪽으로 길을 이어 가는 채로, 병사의 얼굴을 바싹 들여다보며 일렀다."], ["The man started; his arms rattled heavily, as he threw them forward, in the lowest and most respectful salute; and when he had again recovered his piece, he turned to walk his post, muttering between his teeth,--", "사내는 흠칫했고, 가장 낮고 가장 공손한 경례로 무기를 앞으로 내밀자 그 쇠붙이가 묵직하게 절그럭거렸다. 그리고 다시 제 총을 추스른 뒤, 초소를 거닐려고 돌아서며 이 사이로 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"Il faut etre vigilant, en verite!", "\"참말이지 방심하지 말아야겠어!"], ["je crois que nous avons la, un caporal qui ne dort jamais!\"", "우리한테 결코 잠들지 않는 상관이 한 분 계신가 봐!\""]], [["The officer proceeded, without affecting to hear the words which escaped the sentinel in his surprise; nor did he again pause until he had reached the low strand, and in a somewhat dangerous vicinity to the western water bastion of the fort.", "장교는 놀란 보초의 입에서 새어 나온 그 말을 듣는 척도 않고 나아갔으며, 낮은 모래톱에 이르러 요새의 서쪽 수상 보루와 다소 위험할 만큼 가까운 데에 다다를 때까지 다시 걸음을 멈추지 않았다."], ["The light of an obscure moon was just sufficient to render objects, though dim, perceptible in their outlines.", "어슴푸레한 달빛은 사물의 윤곽을, 비록 흐릿할망정, 알아볼 수 있게 할 만큼만 비추었다."], ["He, therefore, took the precaution to place himself against the trunk of a tree, where he leaned for many minutes, and seemed to contemplate the dark and silent mounds of the English works in profound attention.", "그리하여 그는 한 그루 나무의 둥치에 몸을 기대는 조심성을 부려, 거기에 여러 분 동안 기대선 채로, 영국군 진지의 어둡고 고요한 흙더미를 깊은 주의를 기울여 응시하는 듯했다."], ["His gaze at the ramparts was not that of a curious or idle spectator; but his looks wandered from point to point, denoting his knowledge of military usages, and betraying that his search was not unaccompanied by distrust.", "보루를 향한 그의 눈길은 호기심 많거나 한가한 구경꾼의 것이 아니었으니, 그 시선이 이 지점에서 저 지점으로 옮겨 다니며 군사적 관행에 밝다는 것을 드러내었고, 그 살핌이 의심을 동반하지 않은 것이 아님을 누설했다."], ["At length he appeared satisfied; and having cast his eyes impatiently upwards towards the summit of the eastern mountain, as if anticipating the approach of the morning, he was in the act of turning on his footsteps, when a light sound on the nearest angle of the bastion caught his ear, and induced him to remain.", "마침내 그는 흡족해진 듯했고, 새벽의 다가옴을 기다리기라도 하는 양 동쪽 산의 마루를 향해 초조하게 눈을 치떴다가, 막 발길을 되돌리려던 참에, 보루의 가장 가까운 모서리에서 가벼운 소리가 그 귀를 사로잡아 그를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["Just then a figure was seen to approach the edge of the rampart, where it stood, apparently contemplating in its turn the distant tents of the French encampment.", "바로 그때 한 형체가 흉벽의 가장자리로 다가오는 것이 보였으니, 그것은 거기에 서서, 이번에는 멀리 프랑스 진영의 천막들을 응시하는 듯했다."], ["Its head was then turned towards the east, as though equally anxious for the appearance of light, when the form leaned against the mound, and seemed to gaze upon the glassy expanse of the waters, which, like a submarine firmament, glittered with its thousand mimic stars.", "이윽고 그 머리가 동쪽으로 돌려졌으니, 마치 그 역시 빛이 나타나기를 똑같이 애타게 기다리는 듯했고, 그러다 그 형체는 흙더미에 기대어, 마치 해저의 창공처럼 수천 개의 흉내 별로 반짝이는 물의 거울 같은 너른 면을 응시하는 듯했다."], ["The melancholy air, the hour, together with the vast frame of the man who thus leaned, in musing, against the English ramparts, left no doubt as to his person, in the mind of his observant spectator.", "그 우수에 잠긴 분위기와, 시각과, 영국군 흉벽에 그렇게 골똘히 기대선 사내의 거대한 골격은, 이를 지켜보던 자의 마음속에 그가 누구인지에 관한 의심을 남기지 않았다."], ["Delicacy, no less than prudence, now urged him to retire; and he had moved cautiously round the body of the tree for that purpose, when another sound drew his attention, and once more arrested his footsteps.", "사려 못지않게 분별심이 이제 그에게 물러가기를 재촉했고, 그가 그러려고 나무 둥치를 돌아 조심스레 움직였을 때, 또 다른 소리가 그의 주의를 끌어 다시 한번 그 발걸음을 붙들어 세웠다."], ["It was a low and almost inaudible movement of the water, and was succeeded by a grating of pebbles one against the other.", "그것은 물의 나지막한, 거의 들리지 않는 움직임이었으며, 그 뒤를 조약돌이 서로 갈리는 소리가 이었다."], ["In a moment he saw a dark form rise, as it were out of the lake, and steal without further noise to the land, within a few feet of the place where he himself stood.", "순식간에 그는 어두운 형체 하나가 마치 호수에서 솟아오르듯 일어나, 더는 소리도 없이, 그가 서 있는 자리에서 불과 몇 자 안 되는 곳으로 뭍에 살그머니 올라서는 것을 보았다."], ["A rifle next slowly rose between his eyes and the watery mirror; but before it could be discharged his own hand was on the lock.", "이어 한 자루 소총이 그의 두 눈과 물의 거울 사이로 천천히 들렸으나, 그것이 발사되기 전에 그 자신의 손이 격발 장치를 누르고 있었다."]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"휴!\""], ["exclaimed the savage, whose treacherous aim was so singularly and so unexpectedly interrupted.", "그토록 유별나고 그토록 뜻밖에 그 음험한 겨냥이 가로막힌 야만인이 외쳤다."]], [["Without making any reply, the French officer laid his hand on the shoulder of the Indian, and led him in profound silence to a distance from the spot, where their subsequent dialogue might have proved dangerous, and where it seemed that one of them, at least, sought a victim.", "아무 대답도 하지 않고, 프랑스 장교는 그 인디언의 어깨에 손을 얹어, 뒤따를 그들의 대화가 위험을 초래할 수도 있었던, 그리고 적어도 둘 중 하나는 제물을 노리는 듯했던 그 자리에서 멀리 떨어진 데로, 깊은 침묵 속에 그를 이끌었다."], ["Then, throwing open his cloak, so as to expose his uniform and the cross of St. Louis which was suspended at his breast, Montcalm sternly demanded,--", "그런 다음, 제 군복과 가슴에 매달린 〈성루이〉 십자 훈장이 드러나도록 외투를 활짝 열어젖히며, 〈몽칼름〉이 준엄하게 캐물었다."]], [["\"What means this!", "\"이것이 무슨 짓이냐!"], ["Does not my son know that the hatchet is buried between the English and his Canadian Father?\"", "내 아들은 영국군과 그의 캐나다인 아버지 사이에 손도끼가 묻혔다는 것을 모르는가?\""]], [["\"What can the Hurons do?\"", "\"휴런족이 무엇을 할 수 있겠소?\""], ["returned the savage, speaking also, though imperfectly, in the French language.", "야만인이, 비록 서툴기는 했으나 역시 프랑스 말로 응수했다."], ["\"Not a warrior has a scalp, and the pale-faces make friends!\"", "\"전사 가운데 머리 가죽 하나 얻은 자가 없는데, 창백한 얼굴들은 친구가 되었구려!\""]], [["\"Ha!", "\"하!"], ["Le Renard Subtil!", "교활한 〈여우〉여!"], ["Methinks this is an excess of zeal for a friend who was so late an enemy!", "이건 얼마 전까지 적이던 벗에 대한 도가 지나친 열성이로구나!"], ["How many suns have set since Le Renard struck the war-post of the English?\"", "〈여우〉가 영국군의 전쟁 기둥을 친 뒤로 해가 몇 번이나 졌느냐?\""]], [["\"Where is that sun!\"", "\"그 해가 어디 있소!\""], ["demanded the sullen savage.", "침울한 야만인이 따져 물었다."], ["\"Behind the hill; and it is dark and cold.", "\"언덕 너머에 있으니, 어둡고 차갑소."], ["But when he comes again, it will be bright and warm.", "허나 그것이 다시 떠오르면, 밝고 따뜻할 것이오."], ["Le Subtil is the sun of his tribe.", "〈교활한 자〉는 제 부족의 해요."], ["There have been clouds, and many mountains between him and his nation; but now he shines, and it is a clear sky!\"", "그와 그의 민족 사이에 구름이 끼고, 많은 산이 가로놓였으나, 이제 그가 빛나니 하늘이 맑소!\""]], [["\"That Le Renard has power with his people, I well know,\"", "\"〈여우〉가 제 부족에게 힘이 있다는 것은 내 잘 안다.\""], ["said Montcalm; \"for yesterday he hunted for their scalps, and to-day they hear him at the council-fire.\"", "〈몽칼름〉이 말했다. \"어제는 그들의 머리 가죽을 노려 사냥하더니, 오늘은 평의회 불가에서 그의 말에 귀 기울이니 말이다.\""]], [["\"Magua is a great chief.\"", "\"〈마구아〉는 위대한 추장이오.\""]], [["\"Let him prove it, by teaching his nation how to conduct itself towards our new friends.\"", "\"그렇다면 그것을 증명해 보여라, 제 민족에게 우리의 새 벗들을 어떻게 대해야 하는지 가르침으로써.\""]], [["\"Why did the chief of the Canadas bring his young men into the woods, and fire his cannon at the earthen house?\"", "\"캐나다의 추장은 어찌하여 제 젊은이들을 숲으로 데려와, 흙집을 향해 대포를 쏘았소?\""], ["demanded the subtle Indian.", "교활한 인디언이 따져 물었다."]], [["\"To subdue it.", "\"그것을 굴복시키기 위함이다."], ["My master owns the land, and your father has been ordered to drive off these English squatters.", "내 주군께서 그 땅을 소유하시니, 너의 아버지는 이 영국인 무단 점거자들을 몰아내라는 명을 받았다."], ["They have consented to go, and now he calls them enemies no longer.\"", "그들이 떠나기로 동의했으니, 이제 그분은 그들을 더 이상 적이라 부르지 않으신다.\""]], [["\"'Tis well.", "\"좋소."], ["Magua took the hatchet to color it with blood.", "〈마구아〉는 손도끼를 들어 피로 물들이려 했소."], ["It is now bright; when it is red, it shall be buried.\"", "지금 그것이 번쩍이오. 그것이 붉어지면, 그때 묻힐 것이오.\""]], [["\"But Magua is pledged not to sully the lilies of France.", "\"그러나 〈마구아〉는 프랑스의 백합을 더럽히지 않겠다고 맹세하였다."], ["The enemies of the great king across the salt lake are his enemies; his friends, the friends of the Hurons.\"", "소금 호수 건너 위대한 왕의 적은 그의 적이요, 그의 벗은 휴런족의 벗이다.\""]], [["\"Friends!\"", "\"벗이라!\""], ["repeated the Indian, in scorn.", "인디언이 비웃으며 되뇌었다."], ["\"Let his father give Magua a hand.\"", "\"그의 아버지더러 〈마구아〉에게 손을 한번 내밀어 보라 하시오.\""]], [["Montcalm, who felt that his influence over the warlike tribes he had gathered was to be maintained by concession rather than by power, complied reluctantly with the other's request.", "자기가 끌어모은 호전적인 부족들에 대한 영향력이 권력보다는 양보로써 유지되어야 함을 느낀 〈몽칼름〉은, 마지못해 상대의 청에 응했다."], ["The savage placed the finger of the French commander on a deep scar in his bosom, and then exultingly demanded,--", "야만인은 프랑스군 사령관의 손가락을 제 가슴의 깊은 흉터에 갖다 댄 다음, 의기양양하게 물었다."]], [["\"Does my father know that?\"", "\"내 아버지는 저것을 아시오?\""]], [["\"What warrior does not?", "\"어느 전사인들 모르겠느냐?"], ["'tis where a leaden bullet has cut.\"", "납탄이 베어 낸 자국이로다.\""]], [["\"And this?\"", "\"그럼 이것은?\""], ["continued the Indian, who had turned his naked back to the other, his body being without its usual calico mantle.", "인디언이 이으며, 평소의 무명 망토를 걸치지 않은 맨등을 상대에게 돌렸다."]], [["\"This!--my son has been sadly injured, here; who has done this?\"", "\"이것은! 내 아들이 여기에 몹시 상하였구나. 누가 이런 짓을 하였느냐?\""]], [["\"Magua slept hard in the English wigwams, and the sticks have left their mark,\"", "\"〈마구아〉가 영국인의 천막에서 모진 잠을 잤더니, 막대기들이 그 자국을 남겼소.\""], ["returned the savage, with a hollow laugh, which did not conceal the fierce temper that nearly choked him.", "야만인이, 그를 거의 질식시킬 듯한 사나운 성정을 감추지 못하는 공허한 웃음과 함께 응수했다. 그러더니 정신을 차리고는, 돌연한, 타고난 위엄으로 덧붙였다."], ["Then recollecting himself, with sudden and native dignity, he added, \"Go; teach your young men, it is peace.", "\"가시오. 당신의 젊은이들에게 이제 평화라고 가르치시오."], ["Le Renard Subtil knows how to speak to a Huron warrior.\"", "교활한 〈여우〉는 휴런족 전사에게 말하는 법을 알고 있소.\""]], [["Without deigning to bestow further words, or to wait for any answer, the savage cast his rifle into the hollow of his arm, and moved silently through the encampment towards the woods where his own tribe was known to lie.", "더는 말을 건넬 것도, 어떤 대답을 기다릴 것도 없이, 야만인은 제 소총을 팔 안쪽에 걸치고, 제 부족이 자리 잡고 있다고 알려진 숲을 향해 진영을 가로질러 소리 없이 움직였다."], ["Every few yards as he proceeded he was challenged by the sentinels; but he stalked sullenly onward, utterly disregarding the summons of the soldiers, who only spared his life because they knew the air and tread no less than the obstinate daring of an Indian.", "나아가는 동안 몇 걸음마다 보초들에게 수하를 받았으나, 그는 병사들의 호령을 깡그리 무시한 채 침울하게 성큼성큼 나아갔으니, 병사들이 그의 목숨을 살려 둔 것은 오로지 그의 분위기와 걸음걸이가, 인디언 특유의 고집스러운 대담함 못지않게 분명했기 때문이었다."]], [["Montcalm lingered long and melancholy on the strand, where he had been left by his companion, brooding deeply on the temper which his ungovernable ally had just discovered.", "〈몽칼름〉은 제 동행이 떠난 모래톱에 오래도록 우울하게 머물러, 다스릴 길 없는 그 동맹자가 방금 드러낸 성정을 깊이 곱씹었다."], ["Already had his fair fame been tarnished by one horrid scene, and in circumstances fearfully resembling those under which he now found himself.", "이미 그의 빛나던 명성은 한 차례의 끔찍한 장면으로 더럽혀진 바 있었으니, 그것도 그가 지금 처한 것과 무섭도록 닮은 정황 속에서였다."], ["As he mused he became keenly sensible of the deep responsibility they assume who disregard the means to attain their end, and of all the danger of setting in motion an engine which it exceeds human power to control.", "그는 곰곰이 생각할수록, 목적을 이루는 수단을 도외시하는 자들이 떠맡는 깊은 책임과, 인간의 힘으로 다스릴 수 없는 기계를 가동시키는 온갖 위험을 예민하게 절감하게 되었다."], ["Then shaking off a train of reflections that he accounted a weakness in such a moment of triumph, he retraced his steps towards his tent, giving the order as he passed, to make the signal that should arouse the army from its slumbers.", "그러다 이런 승리의 순간에 나약함이라 여겨질 그 일련의 상념을 떨쳐 내고, 그는 천막을 향해 발길을 되돌렸으니, 지나는 길에 군대를 잠에서 깨울 신호를 보내라는 명을 내렸다."]], [["The first tap of the French drums was echoed from the bosom of the fort, and presently the valley was filled with the strains of martial music, rising long, thrilling, and lively above the rattling accompaniment.", "프랑스군 북의 첫 두드림은 요새의 품에서 메아리쳐 돌아왔고, 이내 골짜기는 길고, 짜릿하고, 경쾌하게, 우르릉대는 반주 위로 솟아오르는 군악의 가락으로 가득 찼다."], ["The horns of the victors sounded merry and cheerful flourishes, until the last laggard of the camp was at his post; but the instant the British fifes had blown their shrill signal, they became mute.", "승자들의 나팔은 진영의 마지막 낙오자까지 제자리에 설 때까지 흥겹고 명랑한 곡을 울렸으나, 영국군 피리가 그 날카로운 신호를 불어 댄 그 순간, 그것들은 잠잠해졌다."], ["In the meantime the day had dawned, and when the line of the French army was ready to receive its general, the rays of a brilliant sun were glancing along the glittering array.", "그러는 사이 날이 밝았고, 프랑스군의 대열이 그 장군을 맞이할 채비가 되었을 때, 찬란한 태양의 광선이 번쩍이는 행렬을 따라 비스듬히 비쳤다."], ["Then that success, which was already so well known, was officially announced; the favored band who were selected to guard the gates of the fort were detailed, and defiled before their chief; the signal of their approach was given, and all the usual preparations for a change of masters were ordered and executed directly under the guns of the contested works.", "그러자 이미 그토록 잘 알려진 그 승리가 공식적으로 선포되었고, 요새의 성문을 지키도록 선발된 영예로운 부대가 차출되어 그 사령관 앞을 행진해 지나갔으며, 그들의 접근을 알리는 신호가 떨어지고, 주인이 바뀌는 데 따르는 온갖 여느 채비가, 다투던 그 진지의 대포들 바로 아래에서 명해지고 또 이행되었다."]], [["A very different scene presented itself within the lines of the Anglo-American army.", "영미 연합군의 전선 안에는 사뭇 다른 광경이 펼쳐졌다."], ["As soon as the warning signal was given, it exhibited all the signs of a hurried and forced departure.", "경고 신호가 떨어지자마자, 그곳은 황급하고 마지못한 출발의 온갖 기색을 드러냈다."], ["The sullen soldiers shouldered their empty tubes and fell into their places, like men whose blood had been heated by the past contest, and who only desired the opportunity to revenge an indignity which was still wounding to their pride, concealed as it was under all the observances of military etiquette.", "침울한 병사들은 빈 총을 어깨에 메고 제자리에 들어섰으니, 지난 싸움에 피가 끓어오른 채로, 군대의 예법이라는 온갖 격식 아래 감추어졌을지언정 여전히 그들의 자존심을 찌르는 그 모욕을 갚을 기회만을 바라는 자들 같았다."], ["Women and children ran from place to place, some bearing the scanty remnants of their baggage, and others searching in the ranks for those countenances they looked up to for protection.", "여인들과 아이들은 이리저리 뛰어다녔으니, 더러는 얼마 안 되는 짐의 잔해를 이고, 더러는 자기들이 보호를 우러러 의지하던 그 얼굴들을 대열 속에서 찾아 헤맸다."]], [["Munro appeared among his silent troops firm but dejected.", "〈먼로〉는 말 없는 그의 군대 가운데 굳건하나 풀이 죽은 모습으로 나타났다."], ["It was evident that the unexpected blow had struck deep into his heart, though he struggled to sustain his misfortune with the port of man.", "뜻밖의 일격이 그의 가슴에 깊이 박혔음이 분명했으니, 비록 그가 사내다운 풍모로 그 불운을 견디려 안간힘을 썼으나 그러했다."]], [["Duncan was touched at the quiet and impressive exhibition of his grief.", "〈덩컨〉은 그 조용하고 가슴을 울리는 슬픔의 표출에 마음이 움직였다."], ["He had discharged his own duty, and he now pressed to the side of the old man, to know in what particular he might serve him.", "그는 제 임무를 다한 터라, 이제 그 노인의 곁으로 다가가, 어떤 점에서 그를 도울 수 있을지 물었다."]], [["\"My daughters,\"", "\"내 딸들일세.\""], ["was the brief but expressive reply.", "짧으나 절절한 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["\"Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["are not arrangements already made for their convenience?\"", "그들의 편의를 위한 채비가 아직 마련되지 않았단 말입니까?\""]], [["\"To-day I am only a soldier, Major Heyward,\"", "\"오늘 나는 한낱 군인일 뿐이라네, 〈헤이워드〉 소령.\""], ["said the veteran.", "노병이 말했다."], ["\"All that you see here, claim alike to be my children.\"", "\"여기 자네 눈에 보이는 모든 이가 다 똑같이 내 자식임을 주장하고 있네.\""]], [["Duncan had heard enough.", "〈덩컨〉은 더 들을 것도 없었다."], ["Without losing one of those moments which had now become so precious, he flew towards the quarters of Munro, in quest of the sisters.", "이제 그토록 소중해진 그 순간들을 한순간도 잃지 않고, 그는 자매를 찾아 〈먼로〉의 숙소를 향해 내달렸다."], ["He found them on the threshold of the low edifice, already prepared to depart, and surrounded by a clamorous and weeping assemblage of their own sex, that had gathered about the place, with a sort of instinctive consciousness that it was the point most likely to be protected.", "그는 그들을 그 나지막한 건물의 문턱에서, 이미 떠날 채비를 갖춘 채로, 자기와 같은 성(性)의 떠들썩하고 흐느끼는 무리에 둘러싸인 모습으로 찾아냈으니, 그 무리는 그곳이 가장 보호받기 쉬운 지점이라는 어떤 본능적 자각에서 그 자리로 모여든 터였다."], ["Though the cheeks of Cora were pale, and her countenance anxious, she had lost none of her firmness; but the eyes of Alice were inflamed, and betrayed how long and bitterly she had wept.", "〈코라〉의 두 뺨은 창백하고 그 얼굴은 근심에 잠겼으나, 그녀는 굳건함을 조금도 잃지 않았다. 그러나 〈앨리스〉의 두 눈은 충혈되어, 그녀가 얼마나 오래도록 비통하게 울었는지를 누설하고 있었다."], ["They both, however, received the young man with undisguised pleasure; the former, for a novelty, being the first to speak.", "그러나 두 사람 모두 그 청년을 숨김없는 기쁨으로 맞이했으니, 평소와 달리 먼저 입을 연 것은 언니 쪽이었다."]], [["\"The fort is lost,\"", "\"요새는 잃었어요.\""], ["she said, with a melancholy smile; \"though our good name, I trust, remains.\"", "그녀가 우수 어린 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"허나 우리의 좋은 이름만은 남아 있으리라 믿어요.\""]], [["\"'Tis brighter than ever.", "\"그 어느 때보다 빛나고 있습니다."], ["But, dearest Miss Munro, it is time to think less of others, and to make some provision for yourself.", "그러나, 더없이 소중한 〈먼로〉 양, 이제는 남을 덜 생각하고, 당신 자신을 위해 무언가 대비할 때입니다."], ["Military usage,--pride,--that pride on which you so much value yourself, demands that your father and I should for a little while continue with the troops.", "군대의 관례가, 자존심이, 당신이 그토록 소중히 여기는 그 자존심이, 당신의 아버님과 제가 잠시 군대와 함께 남아 있기를 요구합니다."], ["Then where to seek a proper protector for you against the confusion and chances of such a scene?\"", "그러니 이런 장면의 혼란과 우연으로부터 당신을 지킬 마땅한 보호자를 어디서 구한단 말입니까?\""]], [["\"None is necessary,\"", "\"보호자는 필요 없어요.\""], ["returned Cora; \"who will dare to injure or insult the daughter of such a father, at a time like this?\"", "〈코라〉가 응수했다. \"이런 때에 누가 감히 그런 아버지의 딸을 해치거나 모욕하겠어요?\""]], [["\"I would not leave you alone,\" continued the youth, looking about him in a hurried manner, \"for the command of the best regiment in the pay of the king.", "\"왕의 봉급을 받는 가장 훌륭한 연대의 지휘권을 준대도 당신들을 혼자 두지는 않겠습니다.\""], ["Remember, our Alice is not gifted with all your firmness, and God only knows the terror she might endure.\"", "청년이 황급히 주위를 둘러보며 이었다. \"기억하십시오, 우리 〈앨리스〉는 당신의 모든 굳건함을 타고나지 못했으니, 그녀가 어떤 공포를 견뎌야 할지는 오직 하느님만이 아십니다.\""]], [["\"You may be right,\"", "\"당신 말이 옳을지도 몰라요.\""], ["Cora replied, smiling again, but far more sadly than before.", "〈코라〉가 다시 미소 지었으나, 아까보다 훨씬 슬프게 대답했다."], ["\"Listen!", "\"들어 보세요!"], ["chance has already sent us a friend when he is most needed.\"", "우연이 가장 필요한 때에 벌써 우리에게 한 벗을 보내 주었군요.\""]], [["Duncan did listen, and on the instant comprehended her meaning.", "〈덩컨〉은 과연 귀를 기울였고, 그 순간 그녀의 뜻을 알아챘다."], ["The low and serious sounds of the sacred music, so well known to the eastern provinces, caught his ear, and instantly drew him to an apartment in an adjacent building, which had already been deserted by its customary tenants.", "동부의 여러 영토에 익히 알려진 그 성가(聖歌)의 나지막하고 엄숙한 소리가 그의 귀를 사로잡아, 그를 곧장 이웃한 건물의 한 방으로 이끌었으니, 그곳은 본래 머물던 이들이 이미 떠나 버린 곳이었다."], ["There he found David, pouring out his pious feelings, through the only medium in which he ever indulged.", "그곳에서 그는 〈데이비드〉가, 그가 평생 빠져들곤 했던 단 하나의 통로를 통해 제 경건한 심정을 쏟아 내고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Duncan waited, until, by the cessation of the movement of the hand, he believed the strain was ended, when, by touching his shoulder, he drew the attention of the other to himself, and in a few words explained his wishes.", "〈덩컨〉은 그 손의 움직임이 멎는 것으로 가락이 끝났다고 여겨질 때까지 기다렸다가, 그의 어깨를 건드려 상대의 주의를 제게로 끌고, 몇 마디로 제 바람을 설명했다."]], [["\"Even so,\"", "\"그렇게 하지요.\""], ["replied the single-minded disciple of the King of Israel, when the young man had ended; \"I have found much that is comely and melodious in the maidens, and it is fitting that we who have consorted in so much peril, should abide together in peace.", "청년이 말을 마치자, 〈이스라엘〉 왕의 한결같은 제자가 대답했다. \"그 처녀들에게서 단정하고 가락 고운 점을 많이 보았으니, 그토록 큰 위험 속에 함께한 우리가 평화 속에 함께 머무는 것이 마땅합니다."], ["I will attend them, when I have completed my morning praise, to which nothing is now wanting but the doxology.", "내 아침 찬미를 마치는 대로 그들을 모시지요. 이제 송영(頌詠)밖에 남지 않았소이다."], ["Wilt thou bear a part, friend?", "한 소절 맡아 주시겠소, 친구?"], ["The metre is common, and the tune, 'Southwell.'\"", "운율은 평이하고, 곡조는 〈사우스웰〉이오.\""]], [["Then, extending the little volume, and giving the pitch of the air anew with considerate attention, David recommenced and finished his strains, with a fixedness of manner that it was not easy to interrupt.", "그러고 나서, 그 작은 책을 내밀고 사려 깊은 정성으로 곡의 음을 다시 잡으며, 〈데이비드〉는 좀처럼 가로막기 어려운 한결같은 태도로 그 가락을 다시 시작하여 끝맺었다."], ["Heyward was fain to wait until the verse was ended; when, seeing David relieving himself from the spectacles, and replacing the book, he continued,--", "〈헤이워드〉는 그 절이 끝날 때까지 어쩔 수 없이 기다려야 했으니, 〈데이비드〉가 안경을 벗어 내려놓고 책을 다시 집어넣는 것을 보고 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["\"It will be your duty to see that none dare to approach the ladies with any rude intention, or to offer insult or taunt at the misfortune of their brave father.", "\"누구도 무례한 의도로 감히 그 부인들에게 다가가거나, 그분들의 용감한 아버님의 불운에 모욕이나 조롱을 던지지 못하도록 지키는 것이 당신의 임무가 될 것입니다."], ["In this task you will be seconded by the domestics of their household.\"", "이 일에서 당신은 그 댁의 하인들의 도움을 받게 될 것이오.\""]], [["\"Even so.\"", "\"그렇게 하지요.\""]], [["\"It is possible that the Indians and stragglers of the enemy may intrude, in which case you will remind them of the terms of the capitulation, and threaten to report their conduct to Montcalm.", "\"적의 인디언들과 낙오자들이 끼어들지도 모르는데, 그런 경우에는 그들에게 항복 협정의 조항을 일깨우고, 그들의 행실을 〈몽칼름〉에게 보고하겠다고 위협하시오."], ["A word will suffice.\"", "한마디면 족할 것입니다.\""]], [["\"If not, I have that here which shall,\"", "\"그것으로 안 되면, 여기 그것을 해낼 것이 있소이다.\""], ["returned David, exhibiting his book, with an air in which meekness and confidence were singularly blended.", "〈데이비드〉가 제 책을 내보이며 응수했으니, 그 태도에는 온순함과 자신감이 유별나게 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["\"Here are words which, uttered, or rather thundered, with proper emphasis, and in measured time, shall quiet the most unruly temper:--", "\"여기에, 적절한 강세로, 가락에 맞추어 입에 올리면, 아니 우레처럼 터뜨리면, 더없이 다스리기 힘든 성정도 잠재울 말씀이 있소이다."]], [["\"'Why rage the heathen furiously!'\"--", "이방인들이 어찌하여 사납게 분노하는가!\""]], [["\"Enough,\"", "\"됐습니다.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 그 가락 어린 기원의 분출을 가로막으며 말했다."], ["said Heyward, interrupting the burst of his musical invocation: \"we understand each other; it is time that we should now assume our respective duties.\"", "\"우리는 서로 통했습니다. 이제 우리가 저마다의 임무를 맡을 때입니다.\""]], [["Gamut cheerfully assented, and together they sought the females.", "〈거뮤트〉가 흔쾌히 동의했고, 그들은 함께 그 여인들을 찾아갔다."], ["Cora received her new, and somewhat extraordinary protector, courteously at least; and even the pallid features of Alice lighted again with some of their native archness as she thanked Heyward for his care.", "〈코라〉는 자기의 새롭고 다소 별난 보호자를 적어도 정중하게 맞이했으며, 〈앨리스〉의 창백한 이목구비조차 〈헤이워드〉에게 그 보살핌을 감사하면서 본래의 장난기 어린 빛을 얼마간 되찾았다."], ["Duncan took occasion to assure them he had done the best that circumstances permitted, and, as he believed, quite enough for the security of their feelings; of danger there was none.", "〈덩컨〉은 이 기회에, 정황이 허락하는 한 최선을 다했으며, 자기가 믿기로 그들의 마음을 안심시키기에 충분할 만큼은 했다고 그들에게 확언했다. 위험은 없었다."], ["He then spoke gladly of his intention to rejoin them the moment he had led the advance a few miles towards the Hudson, and immediately took his leave.", "그러고는 선두를 이끌고 〈허드슨〉강 쪽으로 몇 마일 나아가는 대로 곧장 그들에게 다시 합류하겠다는 뜻을 기쁘게 밝히고, 곧바로 작별했다."]], [["By this time the signal of departure had been given, and the head of the English column was in motion.", "이 무렵 출발 신호가 떨어졌고, 영국군 종대의 선두가 움직이고 있었다."], ["The sisters started at the sound, and glancing their eyes around, they saw the white uniforms of the French grenadiers, who had already taken possession of the gates of the fort.", "자매는 그 소리에 흠칫하며 주위로 눈길을 던지다가, 이미 요새의 성문을 차지한 프랑스 척탄병들의 흰 군복을 보았다."], ["At that moment, an enormous cloud seemed to pass suddenly above their heads, and looking upward, they discovered that they stood beneath the wide folds of the standard of France.", "그 순간, 거대한 구름 한 점이 그들의 머리 위를 돌연 지나가는 듯하여 위를 올려다보니, 자기들이 프랑스 군기의 너른 자락 아래에 서 있음을 알게 되었다."]], [["\"Let us go,\"", "\"가요.\""], ["said Cora; \"this is no longer a fit place for the children of an English officer.\"", "〈코라〉가 말했다. \"여기는 더 이상 영국 장교의 딸들에게 어울리는 곳이 아니에요.\""]], [["Alice clung to the arm of her sister, and together they left the parade, accompanied by the moving throng that surrounded them.", "〈앨리스〉가 언니의 팔에 매달렸고, 둘은 함께 연병장을 떠났으니, 그들을 에워싼 움직이는 무리가 동행했다."]], [["As they passed the gates, the French officers, who had learned their rank, bowed often and low, forbearing, however, to intrude those attentions which they saw, with peculiar tact, might not be agreeable.", "그들이 성문을 지날 때, 그들의 신분을 알게 된 프랑스 장교들은 자주 깊이 허리를 숙였으나, 유별난 눈치로, 그것이 달갑지 않을 수도 있음을 알아채고는 그런 관심을 강요하기를 삼갔다."], ["As every vehicle and each beast of burden was occupied by the sick and wounded, Cora had decided to endure the fatigues of a foot march, rather than interfere with their comforts.", "모든 수레와 모든 짐승이 병자와 부상자로 차 있었던 까닭에, 〈코라〉는 그들의 편의를 방해하느니 차라리 도보 행군의 고단함을 견디기로 작정한 터였다."], ["Indeed, many a maimed and feeble soldier was compelled to drag his exhausted limbs in the rear of the columns, for the want of the necessary means of conveyance, in that wilderness.", "실로, 적잖은 불구의, 쇠약한 병사들이, 그 황야에서 운반할 마땅한 수단이 없어, 그 지친 사지를 끌며 종대의 후미를 따라야 했다."], ["The whole, however, was in motion; the weak and wounded, groaning, and in suffering; their comrades, silent and sullen; and the women and children in terror, they knew not of what.", "그러나 전체가 움직이고 있었으니, 쇠약한 자와 부상자는 신음하며 고통받았고, 그들의 전우는 말없이 침울했으며, 여인들과 아이들은 무엇 때문인지도 모르는 공포에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["As the confused and timid throng left the protecting mounds of the fort, and issued on the open plain, the whole scene was at once presented to their eyes.", "혼란스럽고 겁에 질린 무리가 요새의 보호하는 흙더미를 떠나 트인 벌판으로 나서자, 그 광경 전체가 단번에 그들의 눈앞에 펼쳐졌다."], ["At a little distance on the right, and somewhat in the rear, the French army stood to their arms, Montcalm having collected his parties, so soon as his guards had possession of the works.", "오른쪽으로 조금 떨어진, 그리고 다소 뒤편에는 프랑스군이 무장을 갖추고 서 있었으니, 〈몽칼름〉이 그의 호위병이 진지를 차지하자마자 자기 부대들을 한데 모은 터였다."], ["They were attentive but silent observers of the proceedings of the vanquished, failing in none of the stipulated military honors, and offering no taunt or insult, in their success, to their less fortunate foes.", "그들은 패한 자들의 행렬을 주의 깊으나 말없이 지켜보는 자들로서, 약속된 군사적 예우를 하나도 빠뜨리지 않았고, 승리에 도취해 자기들보다 운 없는 적에게 어떤 조롱이나 모욕도 던지지 않았다."], ["Living masses of the English, to the amount in the whole of near three thousand, were moving slowly across the plain, towards the common centre, and gradually approached each other, as they converged to the point of their march, a vista cut through the lofty trees, where the road to the Hudson entered the forest.", "통틀어 거의 삼천에 이르는 영국군의 살아 있는 무리가 벌판을 가로질러 공동의 중심을 향해 천천히 움직였으며, 행군의 목표 지점, 곧 키 큰 나무들 사이로 베어 낸 통로, 〈허드슨〉강으로 가는 길이 숲으로 들어서는 그곳으로 수렴해 가면서 차츰 서로 가까워졌다."], ["Along the sweeping borders of the woods, hung a dark cloud of savages, eying the passage of their enemies, and hovering, at a distance, like vultures, who were only kept from swooping on their prey, by the presence and restraint of a superior army.", "숲의 굽이진 가장자리를 따라서는 야만인들의 어두운 구름이 드리워, 적들의 통행을 노려보며, 멀찍이서 독수리처럼 맴돌았으니, 그들은 오직 더 강한 군대의 존재와 제지에 막혀 제 먹이로 내리꽂지 못할 뿐이었다."], ["A few had straggled among the conquered columns, where they stalked in sullen discontent; attentive, though, as yet, passive observers of the moving multitude.", "몇몇은 정복된 종대들 사이로 떨어져 나와, 침울한 불만 속에 성큼성큼 거닐었으니, 움직이는 군중을 주의 깊게, 그러나 아직은 수동적으로 지켜보는 자들이었다."]], [["The advance, with Heyward at its head, had already reached the defile, and was slowly disappearing, when the attention of Cora was drawn to a collection of stragglers, by the sounds of contention.", "〈헤이워드〉를 선두로 한 선발대는 이미 협로에 다다라 천천히 사라져 가던 차에, 〈코라〉의 주의가 다툼의 소리에 이끌려 한 무리의 낙오자들에게로 쏠렸다."], ["A truant provincial was paying the forfeit of his disobedience, by being plundered of those very effects which had caused him to desert his place in the ranks.", "한 무단이탈한 식민지 병사가 제 불복종의 대가를 치르고 있었으니, 다름 아니라 자기더러 대열에서 제자리를 버리게 만든 바로 그 물건들을 빼앗기는 중이었다."], ["The man was of powerful frame, and too avaricious to part with his goods without a struggle.", "그 사내는 힘센 골격을 지녔고, 너무 탐욕스러워 싸움 없이는 제 물건을 내놓으려 하지 않았다."], ["Individuals from either party interfered; the one side to prevent, and the other to aid in the robbery.", "양쪽에서 사람들이 끼어들었으니, 한쪽은 그 약탈을 막으려, 다른 쪽은 거들려 했다."], ["Voices grew loud and angry, and a hundred savages appeared, as it were by magic, where a dozen only had been seen a minute before.", "목소리가 크고 성나게 높아졌고, 일 분 전만 해도 열두엇밖에 보이지 않던 곳에 마치 마법처럼 백여 명의 야만인이 나타났다."], ["It was then that Cora saw the form of Magua gliding among his countrymen, and speaking with his fatal and artful eloquence.", "바로 그때 〈코라〉는 〈마구아〉의 형체가 제 동족들 사이로 미끄러져 다니며, 그 치명적이고 교활한 능변으로 말하는 것을 보았다."], ["The mass of women and children stopped, and hovered together like alarmed and fluttering birds.", "여인들과 아이들의 무리가 멈추어, 놀라 푸드덕거리는 새들처럼 한데 옹송그렸다."], ["But the cupidity of the Indian was soon gratified, and the different bodies again moved slowly onward.", "그러나 그 인디언의 탐욕은 이내 채워졌고, 갈라졌던 무리는 다시 천천히 앞으로 움직였다."]], [["The savages now fell back, and seemed content to let their enemies advance without further molestation.", "이제 야만인들은 물러서서, 더 이상의 방해 없이 적들이 나아가도록 내버려 두는 데 만족하는 듯했다."], ["But as the female crowd approached them, the gaudy colors of a shawl attracted the eyes of a wild and untutored Huron.", "그러나 여인들의 무리가 그들에게 다가가자, 한 숄의 화려한 빛깔이 거칠고 배운 데 없는 어느 휴런족의 눈을 끌었다."], ["He advanced to seize it, without the least hesitation.", "그는 조금의 망설임도 없이 그것을 채려 다가갔다."], ["The woman, more in terror than through love of the ornament, wrapped her child in the coveted article, and folded both more closely to her bosom.", "그 장식품을 아껴서라기보다 공포에 사로잡힌 그 여인은, 탐나는 그 물건으로 제 아이를 감싸, 둘 다 제 가슴에 더 꼭 끌어안았다."], ["Cora was in the act of speaking, with an intent to advise the woman to abandon the trifle, when the savage relinquished his hold of the shawl, and tore the screaming infant from her arms.", "〈코라〉가 그 여인에게 그 하찮은 것을 버리라고 일러 주려는 마음에서 막 입을 떼려던 차에, 야만인이 숄에 대한 손아귀를 놓고는, 비명을 지르는 그 갓난아이를 여인의 품에서 잡아챘다."], ["Abandoning everything to the greedy grasp of those around her, the mother darted, with distraction in her mien, to reclaim her child.", "주위에 몰린 자들의 탐욕스러운 손아귀에 모든 것을 내맡긴 채, 그 어미는 광란이 어린 얼굴로 제 아이를 되찾으려 달려들었다."], ["The Indian smiled grimly, and extended one hand, in sign of a willingness to exchange, while with the other, he flourished the babe over his head, holding it by the feet as if to enhance the value of the ransom.", "인디언은 음산하게 웃으며, 기꺼이 맞바꾸겠다는 표시로 한 손을 내밀었고, 다른 손으로는 그 갓난것을 두 발을 잡아 머리 위로 흔들어, 마치 그 몸값을 부풀리려는 듯했다."]], [["\"Here--here--there--all--any--everything!\"", "\"여기, 여기, 거기, 전부, 무엇이든, 모조리!\""], ["exclaimed the breathless woman; tearing the lighter articles of dress from her person, with ill-directed and trembling fingers; \"take all, but give me my babe!\"", "숨이 턱에 찬 여인이, 잘못 겨눈 떨리는 손가락으로 제 몸에서 가벼운 옷가지들을 잡아 뜯으며 외쳤다. \"다 가져요, 허나 내 아기를 돌려줘요!\""]], [["The savage spurned the worthless rags, and perceiving that the shawl had already become a prize to another, his bantering but sullen smile changing to a gleam of ferocity, he dashed the head of the infant against a rock, and cast its quivering remains to her very feet.", "야만인은 그 쓸모없는 헝겊들을 걷어찼고, 숄이 이미 다른 자의 전리품이 되어 버린 것을 알아채자, 그의 빈정대지만 침울하던 미소가 흉포함의 번뜩임으로 바뀌더니, 그 갓난아이의 머리를 바위에 내리쳐, 그 부들부들 떠는 잔해를 바로 그녀의 발치에 내던졌다."], ["For an instant, the mother stood, like a statue of despair, looking wildly down at the unseemly object, which had so lately nestled in her bosom and smiled in her face; and then she raised her eyes and countenance towards heaven, as if calling on God to curse the perpetrator of the foul deed.", "한순간, 어미는, 절망의 석상처럼 서서, 그토록 얼마 전까지 제 가슴에 안겨 제 얼굴을 보며 미소 짓던 그 흉측해진 것을 미친 듯이 내려다보았다. 그러고는 두 눈과 얼굴을 하늘로 들어 올려, 마치 그 더러운 짓을 저지른 자를 저주해 달라고 하느님께 부르짖는 듯했다."], ["She was spared the sin of such a prayer; for, maddened at his disappointment, and excited at the sight of blood, the Huron mercifully drove his tomahawk into her own brain.", "그녀는 그런 기도의 죄를 짓는 일만은 면했으니, 제 뜻이 어긋난 데 미쳐 날뛰고 피를 본 데 흥분한 그 휴런족이, 자비롭게도 제 토마호크를 그녀 자신의 머릿골에 박아 넣었기 때문이다."], ["The mother sank under the blow, and fell, grasping at her child, in death, with the same engrossing love that had caused her to cherish it when living.", "어미는 그 일격에 쓰러져, 살아 있을 때 제 아이를 아끼게 만든 그 똑같은 골똘한 사랑으로, 죽음 속에서도 제 아이를 움켜쥐며 넘어갔다."]], [["At that dangerous moment Magua placed his hands to his mouth, and raised the fatal and appalling whoop.", "그 위태로운 순간에 〈마구아〉가 두 손을 입에 갖다 대고, 그 치명적이고 소름 끼치는 함성을 올렸다."], ["The scattered Indians started at the well-known cry, as coursers bound at the signal to quit the goal; and, directly, there arose such a yell along the plain, and through the arches of the wood, as seldom burst from human lips before.", "흩어져 있던 인디언들이 그 익히 아는 외침에 흠칫했으니, 마치 출발 신호에 군마가 도약하듯 했고, 곧장 벌판을 따라, 숲의 궁륭을 가로질러, 일찍이 인간의 입에서 좀처럼 터져 나온 적 없는 함성이 솟았다."], ["They who heard it listened with a curdling horror at the heart, little inferior to that dread which may be expected to attend the blasts of the final summons.", "그것을 들은 자들은 가슴이 얼어붙는 공포로 귀 기울였으니, 그것은 최후의 부름을 알리는 나팔 소리에 따르리라 예상되는 그 두려움에 못지않은 것이었다."]], [["More than two thousand raving savages broke from the forest at the signal, and threw themselves across the fatal plain with instinctive alacrity.", "이천이 넘는 광란하는 야만인들이 그 신호에 숲에서 쏟아져 나와, 본능적인 날쌤으로 그 숙명의 벌판을 가로질러 몸을 던졌다."], ["We shall not dwell on the revolting horrors that succeeded.", "우리는 그 뒤를 이은 역겨운 참상을 길게 늘어놓지 않으련다."], ["Death was everywhere, and in his most terrific and disgusting aspects.", "죽음이 어디에나, 그것도 가장 끔찍하고 가장 혐오스러운 모습으로 있었다."], ["Resistance only served to inflame the murderers, who inflicted their furious blows long after their victims were beyond the power of their resentment.", "저항은 다만 그 살인자들을 더욱 격분시킬 뿐이었으니, 그들은 제 희생자들이 그 원한이 미칠 범위를 한참 벗어난 뒤에도 오래도록 사나운 일격을 퍼부었다."], ["The flow of blood might be likened to the outbreaking of a torrent; and, as the natives became heated and maddened by the sight, many among them even kneeled to the earth, and drank freely, exultingly, hellishly, of the crimson tide.", "피의 흐름은 급류의 터짐에 비길 만했으니, 원주민들이 그 광경에 달아오르고 미쳐 가면서, 그 가운데 많은 자가 심지어 땅에 무릎을 꿇고, 그 진홍의 물결을 마음껏, 의기양양하게, 지옥처럼 들이켰다."]], [["The trained bodies of the troops threw themselves quickly into solid masses, endeavoring to awe their assailants by the imposing appearance of a military front.", "훈련된 군대의 본대는 재빨리 단단한 무리로 뭉쳐, 군사적 정면의 위압적인 외양으로 제 습격자들을 위협하려 애썼다."], ["The experiment in some measure succeeded, though far too many suffered their unloaded muskets to be torn from their hands, in the vain hope of appeasing the savages.", "그 시도는 어느 정도 성공했으나, 너무도 많은 자가 야만인들을 달래 보려는 헛된 희망에서 제 장전 안 된 머스킷총을 손에서 빼앗기고 말았다."]], [["In such a scene none had leisure to note the fleeting moments.", "이런 장면 속에서 그 덧없는 순간들을 헤아릴 겨를이 있는 자는 아무도 없었다."], ["It might have been ten minutes (it seemed an age), that the sisters had stood riveted to one spot, horror-stricken, and nearly helpless.", "자매가 한자리에 못 박힌 채, 공포에 질려, 거의 어쩔 도리 없이 서 있던 것이 십 분쯤이었을지도 모른다(한 세월처럼 느껴졌으나)."], ["When the first blow was struck, their screaming companions had pressed upon them in a body, rendering flight impossible; and now that fear or death had scattered most, if not all, from around them, they saw no avenue open, but such as conducted to the tomahawks of their foes.", "첫 일격이 가해졌을 때, 비명을 지르던 그들의 동행이 한 덩어리로 그들을 밀쳐 와 달아날 길을 막아 버렸고, 이제 공포나 죽음이 그들 주위에서 거의 모두를, 아니 전부를 흩어 놓은 지금, 적들의 토마호크로 이어지는 길 말고는 트인 통로라곤 보이지 않았다."], ["On every side arose shrieks, groans, exhortations, and curses.", "사방에서 비명과 신음, 호소와 저주가 솟았다."], ["At this moment Alice caught a glimpse of the vast form of her father, moving rapidly across the plain, in the direction of the French army.", "이 순간 〈앨리스〉는 제 아버지의 거대한 형체가 프랑스군 방향으로 벌판을 빠르게 가로질러 가는 것을 얼핏 보았다."], ["He was, in truth, proceeding to Montcalm, fearless of every danger, to claim the tardy escort for which he had before conditioned.", "그는 사실, 온갖 위험에도 아랑곳없이, 전에 자기가 조건으로 내걸었던 그 더딘 호위대를 요구하러 〈몽칼름〉에게 나아가는 중이었다."], ["Fifty glittering axes and barbed spears were offered unheeded at his life, but the savages respected his rank and calmness, even in their fury.", "번쩍이는 도끼와 미늘 달린 창 쉰 자루가 그의 목숨을 노려 들이밀어졌으나 거들떠보아지지 않았으니, 야만인들은 그 격분 속에서도 그의 신분과 침착함을 존중했다."], ["The dangerous weapons were brushed aside by the still nervous arm of the veteran, or fell of themselves, after menacing an act that it would seem no one had courage to perform.", "그 위험한 무기들은 노병의 여전히 힘찬 팔에 옆으로 쳐내어지거나, 아무도 감히 저지를 용기가 없는 듯한 행위를 위협하다가 저절로 떨어졌다."], ["Fortunately, the vindictive Magua was searching for his victim in the very band the veteran had just quitted.", "다행히도, 복수에 불타는 〈마구아〉는 노병이 막 떠나온 바로 그 무리 속에서 제 제물을 찾고 있었다."]], [["\"Father--father--we are here!\"", "\"아버지, 아버지, 우리 여기 있어요!\""], ["shrieked Alice, as he passed, at no great distance, without appearing to heed them.", "그가 그리 멀지 않은 거리를 그들을 알아채는 기색도 없이 지나갈 때, 〈앨리스〉가 비명을 질렀다."], ["\"Come to us, father, or we die!\"", "\"우리에게로 오세요, 아버지, 안 그러면 우리는 죽어요!\""]], [["The cry was repeated, and in terms and tones that might have melted a heart of stone, but it was unanswered.", "그 외침은 돌의 심장도 녹였을 말과 어조로 거듭되었으나, 응답이 없었다."], ["Once, indeed, the old man appeared to catch the sounds, for he paused and listened; but Alice had dropped senseless on the earth, and Cora had sunk at her side, hovering in untiring tenderness over her lifeless form.", "과연 한 번, 노인이 그 소리를 알아챈 듯도 했으니, 그가 멈추어 귀 기울였다. 그러나 〈앨리스〉는 이미 정신을 잃고 땅에 쓰러졌고, 〈코라〉는 그 곁에 주저앉아, 지칠 줄 모르는 다정함으로 그 생기 없는 몸 위로 몸을 기울이고 있었다."], ["Munro shook his head in disappointment, and proceeded, bent on the high duty of his station.", "〈먼로〉는 실망에 고개를 가로젓고는, 제 직분의 드높은 임무를 향한 일념으로 나아갔다."]], [["\"Lady,\"", "\"부인.\""], ["said Gamut, who, helpless and useless as he was, had not yet dreamed of deserting his trust, \"it is the jubilee of the devils, and this is not a meet place for Christians to tarry in.", "무력하고 쓸모없는 처지였으나 아직 제 소임을 저버릴 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않은 〈거뮤트〉가 말했다. \"지금은 악마들의 환희의 잔치요, 여기는 기독교도가 머무를 마땅한 곳이 아니외다."], ["Let us up and fly.\"", "일어나 달아납시다.\""]], [["\"Go,\"", "\"가세요.\""], ["said Cora, still gazing at her unconscious sister; \"save thyself.", "여전히 정신 잃은 동생을 응시하며 〈코라〉가 말했다. \"당신 자신을 구하세요."], ["To me thou canst not be of further use.\"", "내게는 당신이 더 이상 쓸모가 없어요.\""]], [["David comprehended the unyielding character of her resolution, by the simple but expressive gesture that accompanied her words.", "〈데이비드〉는 그녀의 말에 곁들여진, 소박하나 절절한 몸짓으로, 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 결심의 성격을 알아챘다."], ["He gazed, for a moment, at the dusky forms that were acting their hellish rites on every side of him, and his tall person grew more erect, while his chest heaved, and every feature swelled, and seemed to speak with the power of the feelings by which he was governed.", "그는 한순간, 제 사방에서 지옥의 의식을 펼치고 있는 거무스름한 형체들을 응시했고, 그 큰 키가 한층 곧추섰으며, 가슴이 부풀어 오르고, 온갖 이목구비가 솟구쳐, 자기를 지배하는 그 감정의 힘으로 말을 하는 듯했다."]], [["\"If the Jewish boy might tame the evil spirit of Saul by the sound of his harp, and the words of sacred song, it may not be amiss,\"", "\"〈유대〉 소년이 제 하프 소리와 성스러운 노래의 말씀으로 〈사울〉의 악령을 길들일 수 있었다면, 여기서 음악의 힘을 시험해 보는 것도 나쁘지 않으리라.\""], ["he said, \"to try the potency of music here.\"", "그가 말했다."]], [["Then raising his voice to its highest tones, he poured out a strain so powerful as to be heard even amid the din of that bloody field.", "그러고는 제 목소리를 가장 높은 음으로 끌어올려, 그 피비린내 나는 들판의 아우성 한가운데서도 들릴 만큼 힘찬 가락을 쏟아 냈다."], ["More than one savage rushed towards them, thinking to rifle the unprotected sisters of their attire, and bear away their scalps; but when they found this strange and unmoved figure riveted to his post, they paused to listen.", "한 사람 이상의 야만인이 보호받지 못하는 자매에게서 그 옷을 약탈하고 머리 가죽을 벗겨 가려는 생각으로 그들에게 달려들었으나, 이 기이하고 미동 없는 형체가 제자리에 못 박혀 있는 것을 보고는 멈추어 귀를 기울였다."], ["Astonishment soon changed to admiration, and they passed on to other and less courageous victims, openly expressing their satisfaction at the firmness with which the white warrior sang his death song.", "놀라움은 이내 감탄으로 바뀌었고, 그들은 이 백인 전사가 제 죽음의 노래를 부르는 그 굳건함에 흡족함을 드러내 놓고 표하며, 더 만만하고 덜 용감한 다른 제물들에게로 지나갔다."], ["Encouraged and deluded by his success, David exerted all his powers to extend what he believed so holy an influence.", "제 성공에 고무되고 또 현혹된 〈데이비드〉는, 자기가 그토록 성스럽다고 믿는 그 영향력을 넓히려 온 힘을 쏟았다."], ["The unwonted sounds caught the ears of a distant savage, who flew raging from group to group, like one who, scorning to touch the vulgar herd, hunted for some victim more worthy of his renown.", "그 흔치 않은 소리가 멀리 있던 한 야만인의 귀를 사로잡았으니, 그는 마치 천한 무리를 건드리기를 경멸하여 제 명성에 더 걸맞은 제물을 찾는 자처럼, 이 무리 저 무리로 격노하여 날아다녔다."], ["It was Magua, who uttered a yell of pleasure when he beheld his ancient prisoners again at his mercy.", "그것은 〈마구아〉였으니, 제 오랜 포로들이 다시 제 손아귀에 들어온 것을 보고 기쁨의 함성을 질렀다."]], [["\"Come,\"", "\"이리 와.\""], ["he said, laying his soiled hands on the dress of Cora, \"the wigwam of the Huron is still open.", "그가 〈코라〉의 옷에 제 더러운 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"휴런족의 천막은 아직 열려 있다."], ["Is it not better than this place?\"", "이곳보다 낫지 않으냐?\""]], [["\"Away!\"", "\"저리 가!\""], ["cried Cora, veiling her eyes from his revolting aspect.", "〈코라〉가 그 역겨운 몰골에서 눈을 가리며 외쳤다."]], [["The Indian laughed tauntingly, as he held up his reeking hand, and answered,--\"It is red, but it comes from white veins!\"", "인디언은 김이 오르는 제 손을 들어 보이며 조롱하듯 웃고는 대답했다. \"붉구나, 허나 이건 흰 핏줄에서 나온 것이지!\""]], [["\"Monster!", "\"괴물!"], ["there is blood, oceans of blood, upon thy soul; thy spirit has moved this scene.\"", "너의 영혼에는 피가, 피의 바다가 있구나. 너의 혼령이 이 장면을 일으켰어.\""]], [["\"Magua is a great chief!\"", "\"〈마구아〉는 위대한 추장이다!\""], ["returned the exulting savage; \"will the dark hair go to his tribe?\"", "의기양양한 야만인이 응수했다. \"검은 머리는 그의 부족에게 가겠느냐?\""]], [["\"Never!", "\"결코!"], ["strike, if thou wilt, and complete thy revenge.\"", "칠 테면 쳐서, 너의 복수를 끝마쳐라.\""]], [["He hesitated a moment; and then catching the light and senseless form of Alice in his arms, the subtle Indian moved swiftly across the plain towards the woods.", "그는 한순간 머뭇거렸다. 그러더니 가볍고 정신 잃은 〈앨리스〉의 몸을 두 팔에 안아 들고, 교활한 인디언은 숲을 향해 벌판을 가로질러 빠르게 움직였다."]], [["\"Hold!\"", "\"멈춰!\""], ["shrieked Cora, following wildly on his footsteps; \"release the child!", "〈코라〉가 미친 듯이 그 발자취를 좇으며 비명을 질렀다. \"그 아이를 놓아줘!"], ["wretch!", "비열한 놈!"], ["what is't you do?\"", "무슨 짓을 하는 거냐?\""]], [["But Magua was deaf to her voice; or rather he knew his power, and was determined to maintain it.", "그러나 〈마구아〉는 그녀의 목소리에 귀가 멀었다. 아니 그보다는, 제 힘을 알았기에, 그것을 지키기로 작정한 것이었다."]], [["\"Stay--lady--stay,\"", "\"멈춰요, 부인, 멈춰요.\""], ["called Gamut, after the unconscious Cora.", "〈거뮤트〉가 정신 나간 〈코라〉의 뒤를 향해 외쳤다."], ["\"The holy charm is beginning to be felt, and soon shalt thou see this horrid tumult stilled.\"", "\"성스러운 주문이 차츰 통하기 시작하니, 이내 이 끔찍한 소동이 잠잠해지는 것을 보게 되리다.\""]], [["Perceiving that, in his turn, he was unheeded, the faithful David followed the distracted sister, raising his voice again in sacred song, and sweeping the air to the measure, with his long arm, in diligent accompaniment.", "이번에는 제가 거들떠보아지지 않음을 알아챈 충직한 〈데이비드〉는, 다시 한번 성스러운 노래로 목소리를 높이고, 그 긴 팔로 가락에 맞추어 공기를 가르며 부지런히 반주하면서, 그 정신 나간 언니를 뒤따랐다."], ["In this manner they traversed the plain, through the flying, the wounded, and the dead.", "이런 식으로 그들은 달아나는 자, 다친 자, 죽은 자들 사이를 지나 벌판을 가로질렀다."], ["The fierce Huron was, at any time, sufficient for himself and the victim that he bore; though Cora would have fallen, more than once, under the blows of her savage enemies, but for the extraordinary being who stalked in her rear, and who now appeared to the astonished natives gifted with the protecting spirit of madness.", "그 사나운 휴런족은, 어느 때든 제 한 몸과 제가 떠멘 제물을 건사하기에 족했다. 다만 〈코라〉는 제 뒤를 성큼성큼 따르는 그 비범한 존재가 아니었다면 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 야만의 적들의 일격에 쓰러졌을 것이니, 그 존재는 이제 놀란 원주민들에게 광기의 수호령을 타고난 자처럼 보였다."]], [["Magua, who knew how to avoid the more pressing dangers, and also to elude pursuit, entered the woods through a low ravine, where he quickly found the Narragansetts, which the travellers had abandoned so shortly before, awaiting his appearance, in custody of a savage as fierce and as malign in his expression as himself.", "더 급박한 위험을 피할 줄도, 또 추격을 따돌릴 줄도 알았던 〈마구아〉는, 낮은 협곡을 지나 숲으로 들어섰으니, 그곳에서 그는 얼마 전 그 여행자들이 버리고 간 〈나라간세트〉 말들이, 제 모습만큼이나 사납고 악의에 찬 표정의 한 야만인의 손에 맡겨진 채 제가 나타나기를 기다리고 있는 것을 이내 발견했다."], ["Laying Alice on one of the horses, he made a sign to Cora to mount the other.", "〈앨리스〉를 말 한 마리에 누인 다음, 그는 〈코라〉에게 다른 한 마리에 오르라는 표시를 했다."]], [["Notwithstanding the horror excited by the presence of her captor, there was a present relief in escaping from the bloody scene enacting on the plain, to which Cora could not be altogether insensible.", "자기를 사로잡은 자의 존재가 자아낸 공포에도 불구하고, 벌판에서 벌어지는 그 피비린내 나는 장면에서 벗어나는 데에는 당장의 안도가 있었으니, 〈코라〉도 그것에 아주 무감할 수는 없었다."], ["She took her seat, and held forth her arms for her sister, with an air of entreaty and love that even the Huron could not deny.", "그녀는 제 자리에 앉아, 휴런족조차 거절할 수 없을 만큼 간청과 사랑이 어린 태도로, 제 동생을 향해 두 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Placing Alice, then, on the same animal with Cora, he seized the bridle, and commenced his route by plunging deeper into the forest.", "그리하여 〈앨리스〉를 〈코라〉와 같은 짐승에 태운 뒤, 그는 고삐를 잡고, 숲 속으로 더욱 깊이 뛰어들며 그 길을 나섰다."], ["David, perceiving that he was left alone, utterly disregarded, as a subject too worthless even to destroy, threw his long limb across the saddle of the beast they had deserted, and made such progress in the pursuit as the difficulties of the path permitted.", "〈데이비드〉는, 죽일 가치조차 없는 대상으로 철저히 무시당한 채 홀로 남겨진 것을 알아채고, 그들이 버려둔 그 짐승의 안장에 제 긴 다리를 걸치고는, 그 길의 어려움이 허락하는 만큼 추격에 나아갔다."]], [["They soon began to ascend, but as the motion had a tendency to revive the dormant faculties of her sister, the attention of Cora was too much divided between the tenderest solicitude in her behalf, and in listening to the cries which were still too audible on the plain, to note the direction in which they journeyed.", "그들은 곧 오르막을 오르기 시작했으나, 그 움직임이 동생의 잠든 의식을 되살리는 경향이 있었던 까닭에, 〈코라〉의 주의는 동생을 위한 더없이 애틋한 염려와, 벌판에서 아직도 너무나 또렷이 들려오는 비명에 귀 기울이는 일 사이로 너무 갈라져, 자기들이 어느 방향으로 가는지를 살필 겨를이 없었다."], ["When, however, they gained the flattened surface of the mountain-top, and approached the eastern precipice, she recognized the spot to which she had once before been led under the more friendly auspices of the scout.", "그러나 산마루의 평평한 면에 다다라 동쪽 벼랑에 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 일찍이 그 정찰병의 더 다정한 안내 아래 한 차례 인도되었던 그 장소를 알아보았다."], ["Here Magua suffered them to dismount; and, notwithstanding their own captivity, the curiosity which seems inseparable from horror, induced them to gaze at the sickening sight below.", "여기서 〈마구아〉는 그들을 말에서 내리게 했고, 자기들이 사로잡힌 처지였음에도, 공포와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 듯한 그 호기심이 그들을 부추겨, 아래편의 그 메스꺼운 광경을 응시하게 했다."]], [["The cruel work was still unchecked.", "그 잔혹한 짓은 여전히 멎지 않았다."], ["On every side the captured were flying before their relentless persecutors, while the armed columns of the Christian king stood fast in an apathy which has never been explained, and which has left an unmovable blot on the otherwise fair escutcheon of their leader.", "사방에서 사로잡힌 자들이 그 무자비한 박해자들 앞에서 달아나고 있었으나, 기독교도 왕의 무장한 종대들은 일찍이 설명된 적 없는, 그리고 그렇지 않았더라면 흠 없었을 그 지휘관의 문장(紋章)에 지울 수 없는 오점을 남긴 그 무관심 속에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["Nor was the sword of death stayed until cupidity got the mastery of revenge.", "죽음의 검은 탐욕이 복수를 압도하기 전까지는 멎지 않았다."], ["Then, indeed, the shrieks of the wounded and the yells of their murderers grew less frequent, until, finally, the cries of horror were lost to their ear, or were drowned in the loud, long, and piercing whoops of the triumphant savages.", "그제야 비로소 부상자들의 비명과 그 살인자들의 함성이 차츰 뜸해지더니, 마침내 그 공포의 외침이 그들의 귀에서 사라지거나, 의기양양한 야만인들의 크고, 길고, 째지는 함성에 묻혀 버렸다. \"이런들 어떠하리."]], [["\"Why, anything: An honorable murderer, if you will; For naught I did in hate, but all in honor.\"", "원한다면 명예로운 살인자라 불러도 좋소. 증오에서 한 일은 하나도 없고, 모두 명예에서 한 일이니.\""]], [["_Othello._", "〈오셀로〉"]], [["The bloody and inhuman scene rather incidentally mentioned than described in the preceding chapter, is conspicuous in the pages of colonial history, by the merited title of \"The Massacre of William Henry.\"", "앞 장에서 묘사라기보다 그저 스치듯 언급된 그 피비린내 나고 비인간적인 장면은, \"〈윌리엄 헨리〉 학살\"이라는 마땅한 이름으로 식민지 역사의 페이지에 또렷이 남아 있다."], ["It so far deepened the stain which a previous and very similar event had left upon the reputation of the French commander, that it was not entirely erased by his early and glorious death.", "그 일은, 앞서 일어난 매우 흡사한 사건이 프랑스 지휘관의 명성에 남긴 오점을 한층 짙게 했으니, 그의 이르고도 영광스러운 죽음으로도 그 얼룩은 온전히 지워지지 못했다."], ["It is now becoming obscured by time; and thousands, who know that Montcalm died like a hero on the plains of Abraham, have yet to learn how much he was deficient in that moral courage without which no man can be truly great.", "이제 그것은 세월에 가려져 가고 있으며, 〈몽칼름〉이 〈에이브러햄〉 평원에서 영웅처럼 죽었음을 아는 수천의 사람들도, 그가 그것 없이는 어느 누구도 진정 위대해질 수 없는 그 도덕적 용기를 얼마나 결여했는지는 아직 배워야 할 처지다."], ["Pages might be written to prove, from this illustrious example, the defects of human excellence; to show how easy it is for generous sentiments, high courtesy, and chivalrous courage, to lose their influence beneath the chilling blight of selfishness, and to exhibit to the world a man who was great in all the minor attributes of character, but who was found wanting when it became necessary to prove how much principle is superior to policy.", "이 빛나는 본보기로부터, 인간의 탁월함이 지닌 결함을 입증하기 위해, 그리하여 너그러운 정서와 높은 예의와 기사다운 용기가 이기심이라는 차디찬 마름병 아래서 그 영향력을 잃기가 얼마나 쉬운지를 보이기 위해, 또한 성품의 온갖 사소한 자질에서는 위대하였으나, 신념이 정략보다 얼마나 우월한지를 입증해야 할 때에 이르러서는 모자란 자로 드러난 한 인물을 세상에 내보이기 위해, 여러 페이지를 쓸 수도 있으리라."], ["But the task would exceed our prerogatives; and, as history, like love, is so apt to surround her heroes with an atmosphere of imaginary brightness, it is probable that Louis de Saint Veran will be viewed by posterity only as the gallant defender of his country, while his cruel apathy on the shores of the Oswego and of the Horican will be forgotten.", "그러나 그 일은 우리의 권한을 넘어서는 것이며, 또한 역사란, 사랑과 마찬가지로, 제 영웅들을 상상의 광휘로 에워싸기를 그토록 즐기니, 아마도 〈루이 드 생베랑〉은 후세에 의해 그저 제 조국의 용맹한 수호자로만 비칠 것이요, 〈오스위고〉와 〈호리컨〉의 기슭에서 보인 그 잔혹한 무관심은 잊히고 말 것이다."], ["Deeply regretting this weakness on the part of a sister muse, we shall at once retire from her sacred precincts, within the proper limits of our own humble vocation.", "자매 격인 한 뮤즈의 이러한 약점을 깊이 애석해하면서, 우리는 곧 그 신성한 경내에서 물러나, 우리 자신의 미천한 소임이 지닌 본래의 한계 안으로 돌아가려 한다."]], [["The third day from the capture of the fort was drawing to a close, but the business of the narrative must still detain the reader on the shores of the \"holy lake.\"", "요새가 함락된 지 사흘째 되는 날이 저물어 가고 있었으나, 이야기의 진행상 독자를 아직 \"거룩한 호수\"의 기슭에 붙들어 두어야겠다."], ["When last seen, the environs of the works were filled with violence and uproar.", "마지막으로 보았을 때, 진지의 주변은 폭력과 소란으로 가득했다."], ["They were now possessed by stillness and death.", "이제 그곳을 차지한 것은 정적과 죽음이었다."], ["The blood-stained conquerors had departed; and their camp, which had so lately rung with the merry rejoicings of a victorious army, lay a silent and deserted city of huts.", "피로 물든 정복자들은 떠났고, 얼마 전까지만 해도 승리한 군대의 흥겨운 환호로 울리던 그들의 진영은, 말없이 버려진 오두막들의 도시로 누워 있었다."], ["The fortress was a smouldering ruin; charred rafters, fragments of exploded artillery, and rent mason-work, covering its earthen mounds in confused disorder.", "요새는 연기 자욱한 폐허였으니, 새까맣게 탄 서까래와 터져 나간 대포의 파편과 갈라진 석조물이 그 흙무더기를 어수선한 무질서 속에 뒤덮고 있었다."]], [["A frightful change had also occurred in the season.", "계절에도 무서운 변화가 일어나 있었다."], ["The sun had hid its warmth behind an impenetrable mass of vapor, and hundreds of human forms, which had blackened beneath the fierce heats of August, were stiffening in their deformity, before the blasts of a premature November.", "해는 그 온기를 도무지 뚫을 수 없는 수증기 더미 뒤로 감추었고, 8월의 사나운 더위 아래 검게 그을렸던 수백의 사람 형상이, 때 이른 11월의 돌풍 앞에 그 일그러진 모습 그대로 뻣뻣이 굳어 가고 있었다."], ["The curling and spotless mists, which had been seen sailing above the hills towards the north, were now returning in an interminable dusky sheet, that was urged along by the fury of a tempest.", "한때 북녘 언덕 위로 떠가던, 둥글게 말려 오르며 한 점 티 없던 안개는, 이제 폭풍의 광포함에 떠밀려 끝없이 이어지는 어두운 휘장이 되어 되돌아오고 있었다."], ["The crowded mirror of the Horican was gone; and, in its place, the green and angry waters lashed the shores, as if indignantly casting back its impurities to the polluted strand.", "〈호리컨〉의 빽빽이 들어찼던 거울 면은 사라졌고, 그 자리에서 푸르고 성난 물결이, 마치 제 더러움을 더럽혀진 기슭에 분연히 되던지기라도 하듯, 강기슭을 후려치고 있었다."], ["Still the clear fountain retained a portion of its charmed influence, but it reflected only the sombre gloom that fell from the impending heavens.", "그래도 그 맑은 샘은 마법 같은 영향력을 조금은 간직하고 있었으나, 이제 그것이 비추는 것이라곤 머리 위로 짓눌러 오는 하늘에서 내려앉은 음울한 어둠뿐이었다."], ["That humid and congenial atmosphere which commonly adorned the view, veiling its harshness, and softening its asperities, had disappeared, and the northern air poured across the waste of water so harsh and unmingled, that nothing was left to be conjectured by the eye, or fashioned by the fancy.", "흔히 그 풍경을 꾸며, 그 거친 모습을 가리고 그 험한 윤곽을 부드럽게 해 주던 그 축축하고 정겨운 대기는 사라졌고, 북녘 바람이 물의 황무지를 가로질러 너무도 매섭고 거르지 않은 채 쏟아지니, 눈으로 헤아릴 것도, 상상으로 빚어낼 것도 하나 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["The fiercer element had cropped the verdure of the plain, which looked as though it were scathed by the consuming lightning.", "더 사나운 기운이 평원의 푸르름을 베어 버려, 그곳은 마치 모든 것을 태우는 번개에 그을린 듯이 보였다."], ["But, here and there, a dark green tuft rose in the midst of the desolation; the earliest fruits of a soil that had been fattened with human blood.", "그러나 군데군데, 그 황량함 한가운데서 짙푸른 풀 더미가 솟아 있었으니, 사람의 피로 기름지게 된 토양이 맺은 그 첫 열매였다."], ["The whole landscape, which, seen by a favoring light, and in a genial temperature, had been found so lovely, appeared now like some pictured allegory of life, in which objects were arrayed in their harshest but truest colors, and without the relief of any shadowing.", "너그러운 빛 아래, 정겨운 기온 속에서 보면 그토록 사랑스럽던 그 온 풍경이, 이제는 어떤 그림 같은 삶의 우화처럼 보였으니, 그 안에서 사물들은 가장 거칠지만 가장 참된 색채로, 그 어떤 그늘의 완화도 없이 늘어서 있었다."]], [["The solitary and arid blades of grass arose from the passing gusts fearfully perceptible; the bold and rocky mountains were too distinct in their barrenness, and the eye even sought relief, in vain, by attempting to pierce the illimitable void of heaven, which was shut to its gaze by the dusky sheet of ragged and driving vapor.", "외롭고 메마른 풀잎들은 스쳐 지나는 돌풍에 무섭도록 또렷이 일렁였고, 우뚝 솟은 바위투성이 산들은 그 헐벗음 속에 지나치게 뚜렷했으며, 눈은 헛되이나마 위안을 구하여 하늘의 끝없는 허공을 꿰뚫어 보려 했으나, 그 시선은 누덕누덕 휘몰아치는 안개의 어두운 휘장에 가로막혔다."]], [["The wind blew unequally; sometimes sweeping heavily along the ground, seeming to whisper its moanings in the cold ears of the dead, then rising in a shrill and mournful whistling, it entered the forest with a rush that filled the air with the leaves and branches it scattered in its path.", "바람은 고르지 않게 불었으니, 어떤 때는 땅을 따라 무겁게 휩쓸어 죽은 자들의 차가운 귀에 제 신음을 속삭이는 듯하다가, 또 어떤 때는 날카롭고 구슬픈 휘파람 소리로 높아지며, 그 길에 흩뿌린 잎과 가지로 허공을 가득 채우는 돌풍과 함께 숲으로 밀려들었다."], ["Amid the unnatural shower, a few hungry ravens struggled with the gale; but no sooner was the green ocean of woods, which stretched beneath them, passed, than they gladly stopped, at random, to their hideous banquet.", "그 부자연스러운 소나기 속에서, 굶주린 까마귀 몇 마리가 강풍과 싸우고 있었으나, 발아래 펼쳐진 숲의 푸른 바다를 지나자마자 곧 흡족하게 아무 데나 내려앉아 그 끔찍한 잔치를 벌였다."]], [["In short, it was the scene of wildness and desolation; and it appeared as if all who had profanely entered it had been stricken, at a blow, by the relentless arm of death.", "요컨대, 그것은 황량함과 폐허의 풍경이었으니, 그곳에 불경하게 발을 들인 모든 자가 죽음의 가차 없는 팔에 단번에 베인 듯 보였다."], ["But the prohibition had ceased; and for the first time since the perpetrators of those foul deeds which had assisted to disfigure the scene were gone, living human beings had now presumed to approach the place.", "그러나 그 금제는 이제 풀렸다. 그리하여 그 풍경을 흉하게 만드는 데 한몫한 그 추악한 짓들을 저지른 자들이 떠난 이래 처음으로, 살아 있는 사람들이 이제 감히 그곳에 다가서고 있었다."]], [["About an hour before the setting of the sun, on the day already mentioned, the forms of five men might have been seen issuing from the narrow vista of trees, where the path to the Hudson entered the forest, and advancing in the direction of the ruined works.", "이미 말한 그날, 해가 지기 한 시간쯤 전에, 다섯 사내의 모습이, 〈허드슨〉강으로 가는 길이 숲으로 들어서는 그 좁은 나무들의 통로에서 빠져나와, 허물어진 진지 쪽으로 나아가는 것이 보였을 법하다."], ["At first their progress was slow and guarded, as though they entered with reluctance amid the horrors of the spot, or dreaded the renewal of its frightful incidents.", "처음에 그들의 발걸음은 더디고 조심스러웠으니, 마치 그곳의 참상 속으로 들어서기를 꺼리거나, 그 무시무시한 사건들이 되풀이될까 두려워하는 듯했다."], ["A light figure preceded the rest of the party, with the caution and activity of a native; ascending every hillock to reconnoitre, and indicating, by gestures, to his companions, the route he deemed it most prudent to pursue.", "한 날렵한 형상이 일행의 앞장을 섰으니, 그 신중함과 민첩함이 토박이의 것이었다. 그는 정찰하려 둔덕마다 오르고, 가장 분별 있다 여겨지는 길을 손짓으로 동료들에게 일러 주었다."], ["Nor were those in the rear wanting in every caution and foresight known to forest warfare.", "뒤따르는 자들 또한 숲의 전투에서 알려진 온갖 조심성과 선견을 갖추고 있었다."], ["One among them, he also was an Indian, moved a little on one flank, and watched the margin of the woods, with eyes long accustomed to read the smallest sign of danger.", "그들 가운데 한 사람, 그 역시 인디언이었는데, 한쪽 측면으로 조금 떨어져 움직이며, 아주 작은 위험의 기미라도 읽어 내는 데 오래 익숙해진 눈으로 숲의 가장자리를 살폈다."], ["The remaining three were white, though clad in vestments adapted, both in quality and color, to their present hazardous pursuit,--that of hanging on the skirts of a retiring army in the wilderness.", "나머지 셋은 백인이었으나, 그 옷차림은 빛깔과 재질 모두에서 지금의 위험한 추격에 — 황야에서 물러가는 군대의 꽁무니에 매달리는 일에 — 알맞은 것이었다."]], [["The effects produced by the appalling sights that constantly arose in their path to the lake shore, were as different as the characters of the respective individuals who composed the party.", "호수 기슭으로 가는 그들의 길에 끊임없이 떠오르는 소름 끼치는 광경이 자아내는 영향은, 일행을 이룬 각 개인의 성품만큼이나 제각각이었다."], ["The youth in front threw serious but furtive glances at the mangled victims, as he stepped lightly across the plain, afraid to exhibit his feelings, and yet too inexperienced to quell entirely their sudden and powerful influence.", "앞장선 젊은이는 평원을 가볍게 건너며, 처참하게 난도질당한 희생자들에게 진지하면서도 은밀한 눈길을 던졌으니, 제 감정을 드러내기를 두려워하면서도, 그 갑작스럽고 강력한 영향을 온전히 억누르기에는 아직 너무 미숙했다."], ["His red associate, however, was superior to such a weakness.", "그러나 그의 붉은 살갗 동료는 그러한 약점을 넘어서 있었다."], ["He passed the groups of dead with a steadiness of purpose, and an eye so calm, that nothing but long and inveterate practice could enable him to maintain.", "그는 죽은 자들의 무리를 흔들림 없는 결의로, 그리고 오래고 뿌리 깊은 단련이 아니고서는 도무지 지킬 수 없을 만큼 고요한 눈으로 지나쳐 갔다."], ["The sensations produced in the minds of even the white men were different, though uniformly sorrowful.", "백인들의 마음에 일어난 감정마저 제각각이었으되, 한결같이 비통했다."], ["One, whose gray locks and furrowed lineaments, blending with a martial air and tread, betrayed, in spite of the disguise of a woodsman's dress, a man long experienced in scenes of war, was not ashamed to groan aloud, whenever a spectacle of more than usual horror came under his view.", "그중 하나는, 그 희끗한 머리칼과 주름진 이목구비가 군인다운 기품과 걸음걸이와 어우러져, 사냥꾼 차림으로 변장했음에도 전쟁의 장면들에 오래 단련된 사람임을 드러냈는데, 유달리 끔찍한 광경이 그의 눈에 들어올 때마다 큰 소리로 신음하기를 부끄러워하지 않았다."], ["The young man at his elbow shuddered, but seemed to suppress his feelings in tenderness to his companion.", "그 곁의 젊은이는 몸서리쳤으나, 동료를 배려하는 마음에서 제 감정을 억누르는 듯했다."], ["Of them all, the straggler who brought up the rear appeared alone to betray his real thoughts, without fear of observation or dread of consequences.", "그들 모두 가운데, 맨 뒤에서 따라오는 낙오자만이 홀로, 남의 눈도, 뒤탈도 두려워하지 않고 제 속내를 고스란히 드러내는 듯했다."], ["He gazed at the most appalling sight with eyes and muscles that knew not how to waver, but with execrations so bitter and deep as to denote how much he denounced the crime of his enemies.", "그는 더없이 소름 끼치는 광경을 흔들릴 줄 모르는 눈과 근육으로 응시하면서도, 너무나 쓰라리고 깊은 저주를 내뱉어, 적들의 죄악을 얼마나 규탄하는지를 여실히 드러냈다."]], [["The reader will perceive at once, in these respective characters, the Mohicans, and their white friend, the scout; together with Munro and Heyward.", "독자는 이 저마다의 인물들 속에서, 곧 〈모히칸〉 부자(父子)와 그들의 백인 친구인 정찰꾼, 그리고 〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉를 단번에 알아볼 것이다."], ["It was, in truth, the father in quest of his children, attended by the youth who felt so deep a stake in their happiness, and those brave and trusty foresters, who had already proved their skill and fidelity through the trying scenes related.", "그것은 실로, 제 아이들을 찾아 나선 아비와, 그 아이들의 안위에 그토록 깊은 이해가 걸린 젊은이, 그리고 이미 그 험난한 장면들을 통해 제 솜씨와 충직함을 증명해 보인 용감하고 믿음직한 숲 사람들이었다."]], [["When Uncas, who moved in front, had reached the centre of the plain, he raised a cry that drew his companions in a body to the spot.", "앞서 움직이던 〈언카스〉가 평원의 한가운데에 이르렀을 때, 그는 동료들을 한데 끌어모으는 외침을 질렀다."], ["The young warrior had halted over a group of females who lay in a cluster, a confused mass of dead.", "젊은 전사는 한 무더기로 누운 여인들의 무리, 곧 죽은 자들이 뒤엉킨 더미 위에 멈춰 서 있었다."], ["Notwithstanding the revolting horror of the exhibition, Munro and Heyward flew towards the festering heap, endeavoring, with a love that no unseemliness could extinguish, to discover whether any vestiges of those they sought were to be seen among the tattered and many-colored garments.", "그 광경의 역겨운 참혹함에도 아랑곳없이, 〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉는 그 썩어 가는 더미를 향해 달려가, 그 어떤 흉한 모습으로도 꺼뜨릴 수 없는 사랑으로, 그들이 찾는 이들의 흔적이 그 갈가리 찢긴 갖가지 빛깔의 옷가지 사이에 보이지 않는지 살피려 애썼다."], ["The father and lover found instant relief in the search; though each was condemned again to experience the misery of an uncertainty that was hardly less insupportable than the most revolting truth.", "아비와 연인은 그 수색에서 곧바로 위안을 얻었으나, 둘 모두 다시금, 더없이 역겨운 진실 못지않게 견디기 힘든 불확실함의 고통을 겪을 운명에 처해 있었다."], ["They were standing, silent and thoughtful, around the melancholy pile, when the scout approached.", "그들이 그 서글픈 더미를 둘러싸고 말없이 생각에 잠겨 서 있을 때, 정찰꾼이 다가왔다."], ["Eying the sad spectacle with an angry countenance, the sturdy woodsman, for the first time since his entering the plain, spoke intelligibly and aloud:--", "그 우람한 숲 사람은 성난 얼굴로 그 슬픈 광경을 노려보더니, 평원에 들어선 이래 처음으로, 또렷이 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["\"I have been on many a shocking field, and have followed a trail of blood for many miles,\"", "\"나는 끔찍한 싸움터를 숱하게 겪었고, 피의 자취를 여러 마일 따라가 보기도 했소.\""], ["he said, \"but never have I found the hand of the devil so plain as it is here to be seen!", "그가 말했다. \"허나 여기서 보이는 것처럼 악마의 손길이 그토록 또렷한 데는 본 적이 없소!"], ["Revenge is an Indian feeling, and all who know me know that there is no cross in my veins; but this much will I say--here, in the face of heaven, and with the power of the Lord so manifest in this howling wilderness,--that should these Frenchers ever trust themselves again within the range of a ragged bullet, there is one rifle shall play its part, so long as flint will fire or powder burn!", "복수는 인디언의 정서요, 나를 아는 이라면 누구나 내 핏줄엔 잡것이 섞이지 않았음을 아오. 허나 이것만은 말하겠소 — 여기, 하늘을 마주하고, 이 울부짖는 황야에 주님의 권능이 이토록 뚜렷이 드러난 가운데서, 만일 저 프랑스 놈들이 다시 한번 누더기 같은 총알 사정거리 안에 제 몸을 들이밀기만 한다면, 부싯돌이 불을 일으키고 화약이 타는 한 제 몫을 다할 소총이 한 자루 있다는 것을!"], ["I leave the tomahawk and knife to such as have a natural gift to use them.", "도끼와 칼은 그것을 쓸 타고난 재주를 지닌 자들에게 맡기겠소."], ["What say you, Chingachgook,\"", "어찌 생각하오, 〈칭가치국〉?\""], ["he added in Delaware; \"shall the Hurons boast of this to their women when the deep snows come?\"", "그가 〈델라웨어〉 말로 덧붙였다. \"깊은 눈이 내릴 적에, 〈휴런〉 놈들이 제 여인들에게 이 일을 떠벌리게 두겠소?\""]], [["A gleam of resentment flashed across the dark lineaments of the Mohican chief: he loosened his knife in its sheath; and then turning calmly from the sight, his countenance settled into a repose as deep as if he never knew the instigation of passion.", "원한의 빛이 〈모히칸〉 추장의 검은 이목구비를 스쳤다. 그는 칼집에 든 칼을 느슨하게 풀었다. 그러더니 그 광경에서 차분히 몸을 돌려, 격정의 충동이라곤 알지도 못하는 사람처럼 그 얼굴은 깊은 평정 속에 가라앉았다."]], [["\"Montcalm!", "\"〈몽칼름〉!"], ["Montcalm!\"", "〈몽칼름〉!\""], ["continued the deeply resentful and less self-restrained scout; \"they say a time must come, when all the deeds done in the flesh will be seen at a single look; and that by eyes cleared from mortal infirmities.", "깊은 원한에 찼으되 절제가 덜한 정찰꾼이 말을 이었다. \"사람들이 말하기를, 육신으로 행한 모든 짓이 단 한 번의 눈길에 드러날 때가 반드시 오고, 그것도 인간의 허약함에서 벗어난 눈으로 보게 된다고 하오."], ["Woe betide the wretch who is born to behold this plain, with the judgment hanging about his soul!", "영혼에 심판이 매달린 채 이 평원을 바라보도록 태어난 가엾은 자에게 화가 있으리라!"], ["Ha--as I am a man of white blood, yonder lies a redskin, without the hair of his head where nature rooted it!", "하 — 내가 백인의 피를 지닌 사람인 것이 분명하듯, 저기 한 붉은 살갗이 누워 있구려, 자연이 머리털을 박아 둔 자리에 그 한 올도 없이!"], ["Look to him, Delaware; it may be one of your missing people; and he should have burial like a stout warrior.", "저자를 살펴보오, 〈델라웨어〉. 어쩌면 그대의 잃어버린 사람들 중 하나일지도 모르니, 의젓한 전사답게 묻어 주어야 마땅하오."], ["I see it in your eye, Sagamore: a Huron pays for this, afore the fall winds have blown away the scent of the blood!\"", "그대 눈에서 보이는구려, 〈사가모어〉. 가을바람이 피 냄새를 날려 버리기 전에, 〈휴런〉 놈 하나가 이 값을 치를 것이오!\""]], [["Chingachgook approached the mutilated form, and turning it over, he found the distinguishing marks of one of those six allied tribes, or nations, as they were called, who, while they fought in the English ranks, were so deadly hostile to his own people.", "〈칭가치국〉이 그 난도질당한 시신에 다가가 뒤집어 보니, 거기서 그 여섯 동맹 부족, 곧 그들이 불리던 이름으로는 여섯 민족 가운데 하나임을 알리는 표지가 나왔는데, 그들은 〈영국〉 대열에서 싸우면서도 그 자신의 겨레에게는 그토록 지독히 적대적인 자들이었다."], ["Spurning the loathsome object with his foot, he turned from it with the same indifference he would have quitted a brute carcass.", "그는 그 역겨운 물체를 발로 걷어차더니, 짐승의 주검을 떠나기라도 하듯 똑같은 무관심으로 거기서 돌아섰다."], ["The scout comprehended the action, and very deliberately pursued his own way, continuing, however, his denunciations against the French commander in the same resentful strain.", "정찰꾼은 그 행동의 뜻을 알아챘으나, 그는 자못 신중하게 제 갈 길을 가면서도, 같은 원한 어린 어조로 프랑스 지휘관을 향한 규탄을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"Nothing but vast wisdom and unlimited power should dare to sweep off men in multitudes,\"", "\"오로지 무한한 지혜와 무한한 권능만이 감히 사람들을 무더기로 쓸어버릴 수 있는 법이오.\""], ["he added; \"for it is only the one that can know the necessity of the judgment; and what is there, short of the other, that can replace the creatures of the Lord?", "그가 덧붙였다. \"심판의 필요를 알 수 있는 것은 오직 그 하나뿐이며, 그 다른 하나가 아니고서야 주님의 피조물을 무엇으로 되돌려 놓을 수 있겠소?"], ["I hold it a sin to kill the second buck afore the first is eaten, unless a march in the front, or an ambushment, be contemplated.", "나는 행군이 앞에 있거나 매복이 예정된 게 아니라면, 첫째 사슴을 다 먹기도 전에 둘째 사슴을 잡는 것을 죄로 여기오."], ["It is a different matter with a few warriors in open and rugged fight, for 'tis their gift to die with the rifle or the tomahawk in hand; according as their natures may happen to be, white or red.", "트인 곳에서 거친 싸움을 벌이는 몇몇 전사들 사이의 일은 또 다른 문제니, 그들이 백인이든 붉은 살갗이든 제 천성이 어떠하냐에 따라 소총이나 도끼를 손에 쥔 채 죽는 것이 그들의 타고난 몫인 까닭이오."], ["Uncas, come this way, lad, and let the ravens settle upon the Mingo.", "〈언카스〉, 이리 오너라, 얘야, 까마귀가 저 〈밍고〉 위에 내려앉게 두어라."], ["I know, from often seeing it, that they have a craving for the flesh of an Oneida; and it is as well to let the bird follow the gift of its natural appetite.\"", "나는 자주 본 터라, 그놈들이 〈오나이다〉의 살을 탐낸다는 것을 알고 있으니, 그 새가 제 타고난 식욕이 시키는 대로 따르게 두는 것도 괜찮은 일이지.\""]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"흐!\""], ["exclaimed the young Mohican, rising on the extremities of his feet, and gazing intently in his front, frightening the raven to some other prey, by the sound and the action.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉이 발끝으로 곧추서며 외쳤으니, 제 앞을 뚫어지게 바라보면서, 그 소리와 동작으로 까마귀를 쫓아 다른 먹잇감으로 보내 버렸다."]], [["\"What is it, boy?\"", "\"무엇이냐, 얘야?\""], ["whispered the scout, lowering his tall form into a crouching attitude, like a panther about to take his leap; \"God send it be a tardy Frencher, skulking for plunder.", "정찰꾼이 막 도약하려는 표범처럼 그 큰 키를 웅크리며 속삭였다. \"노략질하러 어슬렁대는 굼뜬 프랑스 놈이기를 신이여 보내 주소서."], ["I do believe 'Killdeer' would take an oncommon range to-day!\"", "오늘은 '킬디어'가 흔치 않게 멀리까지 날아갈 듯하구나!\""]], [["Uncas, without making any reply, bounded away from the spot, and in the next instant he was seen tearing from a bush, and waving in triumph a fragment of the green riding-veil of Cora.", "〈언카스〉는 아무 대꾸도 않고 그 자리에서 뛰쳐나갔으니, 다음 순간 그는 한 덤불에서 무언가를 뜯어내, 〈코라〉의 푸른 승마용 베일 조각을 의기양양하게 흔드는 모습으로 보였다."], ["The movement, the exhibition, and the cry, which again burst from the lips of the young Mohican, instantly drew the whole party about him.", "그 동작과, 그 내보임과, 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 입에서 또다시 터져 나온 그 외침이, 곧 일행 전부를 그의 주위로 끌어모았다."]], [["\"My child!\"", "\"내 아이!\""], ["said Munro, speaking quick and wildly \"give me my child!\"", "〈먼로〉가 빠르고 격하게 말했다. \"내 아이를 다오!\""]], [["\"Uncas will try,\"", "\"〈언카스〉가 애써 보겠습니다.\""], ["was the short and touching answer.", "그 짧고도 가슴 저미는 대답이었다."]], [["The simple but meaning assurance was lost on the father, who seized the piece of gauze, and crushed it in his hand, while his eyes roamed fearfully among the bushes, as if he equally dreaded and hoped for the secrets they might reveal.", "그 소박하나 뜻 깊은 다짐은 아비에게 가닿지 못했으니, 그는 그 망사 조각을 움켜쥐어 손안에 구겨 넣으면서, 마치 덤불이 드러낼지 모를 비밀을 두려워하면서도 동시에 바라기라도 하듯, 그 눈은 겁먹은 채 덤불 사이를 헤맸다."]], [["\"Here are no dead,\"", "\"여기엔 죽은 자가 없소.\""], ["said Heyward; \"the storm seems not to have passed this way.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"폭풍은 이쪽으로는 지나지 않은 듯하오.\""]], [["\"That's manifest; and clearer than the heavens above our heads,\" returned the undisturbed scout; \"but either she, or they that have robbed her, have passed the bush; for I remember the rag she wore to hide a face that all did love to look upon. Uncas, you are right; the dark-hair has been here, and she has fled like a frightened fawn, to the wood; none who could fly would remain to be murdered. Let us search for the marks she left; for to Indian eyes, I sometimes think even a humming-bird leaves his trail in the air.\"", "\"그건 분명하오, 우리 머리 위 하늘보다도 더 분명하지.\" 동요 없는 정찰꾼이 대답했다. \"허나 그 아가씨든, 아니면 그녀를 빼앗아 간 자들이든, 이 덤불을 지난 거요. 모두가 바라보길 좋아하던 그 얼굴을 가리려고 그녀가 둘렀던 천이 기억나니까. 〈언카스〉, 네 말이 옳다. 검은 머리 아가씨가 여기 있었고, 겁먹은 새끼 사슴처럼 숲으로 달아난 것이다. 달아날 수 있는 자라면 누구도 죽임을 당하려고 남아 있진 않았을 테니까. 그녀가 남긴 자취를 찾아보자. 인디언의 눈에는, 벌새조차 허공에 제 자취를 남긴다고 나는 이따금 생각하니 말이다.\""]], [["The young Mohican darted away at the suggestion, and the scout had hardly done speaking, before the former raised a cry of success from the margin of the forest.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉은 그 말에 쏜살같이 달려 나갔고, 정찰꾼이 미처 말을 끝내기도 전에, 그가 숲 가장자리에서 성공을 알리는 외침을 질렀다."], ["On reaching the spot, the anxious party perceived another portion of the veil fluttering on the lower branch of a beech.", "그 자리에 이르자, 애타는 일행은 너도밤나무의 낮은 가지에서 펄럭이는 또 다른 베일 조각을 알아보았다."]], [["\"Softly, softly,\"", "\"가만, 가만.\""], ["said the scout, extending his long rifle in front of the eager Heyward; \"we now know our work, but the beauty of the trail must not be deformed.", "정찰꾼이 다급한 〈헤이워드〉 앞으로 제 긴 소총을 뻗으며 말했다. \"이제 우리가 할 일은 알았소만, 이 자취의 아름다움을 망쳐서는 안 되오."], ["A step too soon may give us hours of trouble.", "한 걸음만 너무 일러도 우리에게 몇 시간의 수고를 안길 수 있소."], ["We have them, though; that much is beyond denial.\"", "그래도 우린 놈들을 잡은 거요. 그것만은 부인할 수 없소.\""]], [["\"Bless ye, bless ye, worthy man!\"", "\"고맙소, 고맙소, 훌륭한 양반!\""], ["exclaimed Munro; \"whither, then, have they fled, and where are my babes?\"", "〈먼로〉가 외쳤다. \"그럼, 그 애들이 어디로 달아났소, 내 자식들은 어디 있소?\""]], [["\"The path they have taken depends on many chances.", "\"그들이 택한 길은 여러 우연에 달려 있소."], ["If they have gone alone, they are quite as likely to move in a circle as straight, and they may be within a dozen miles of us; but if the Hurons, or any of the French Indians, have laid hands on them, 'tis probable they are now near the borders of the Canadas.", "만일 그들끼리만 갔다면, 똑바로 가기보다 빙 둘러 돌았을 공산도 그만큼 크니, 우리에게서 십여 마일 안에 있을 수도 있소. 허나 〈휴런〉 놈들이나 프랑스 편 인디언들이 손을 댔다면, 지금쯤 〈캐나다〉 변경 가까이에 있을 게요."], ["But what matters that?\"", "허나 그게 무슨 상관이겠소?\""], ["continued the deliberate scout, observing the powerful anxiety and disappointment the listeners exhibited; \"here are the Mohicans and I on one end of the trail, and, rely on it, we find the other, though they should be a hundred leagues asunder!", "신중한 정찰꾼은 듣는 이들이 드러내는 강렬한 불안과 실망을 살피며 말을 이었다. \"여기 〈모히칸〉 부자와 내가 이 자취의 한쪽 끝에 있으니, 믿으시오, 비록 그들이 백 리그쯤 떨어져 있다 한들 우리는 그 다른 끝을 찾아낼 것이오!"], ["Gently, gently, Uncas, you are as impatient as a man in the settlements; you forget that light feet leave but faint marks!\"", "천천히, 천천히, 〈언카스〉, 너는 정착지 사람처럼 성마르구나. 가벼운 발은 희미한 자국밖에 남기지 않는다는 걸 잊었느냐!\""]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"흐!\""], ["exclaimed Chingachgook, who had been occupied in examining an opening that had been evidently made through the low underbrush, which skirted the forest; and who now stood erect, as he pointed downwards, in the attitude and with the air of a man who beheld a disgusting serpent.", "〈칭가치국〉이 외쳤으니, 그는 숲 가장자리를 두른 나지막한 덤불을 분명히 헤집고 난 틈을 살피는 데 골몰해 있다가, 이제 똑바로 일어서서, 역겨운 뱀이라도 본 사람의 자세와 기색으로 아래를 가리켰다."]], [["\"Here is the palpable impression of the footstep of a man,\"", "\"여기 사람 발자국이 또렷하게 찍혀 있소.\""], ["cried Heyward, bending over the indicated spot; \"he has trod in the margin of this pool, and the mark cannot be mistaken.", "〈헤이워드〉가 그 가리킨 자리 위로 몸을 굽히며 외쳤다. \"이 웅덩이 가장자리를 밟았으니, 자국을 잘못 볼 리 없소."], ["They are captives.\"", "그들은 포로가 된 거요.\""]], [["\"Better so than left to starve in the wilderness,\"", "\"황야에서 굶어 죽도록 버려진 것보다야 낫소.\""], ["returned the scout; \"and they will leave a wider trail.", "정찰꾼이 대답했다. \"게다가 더 넓은 자취를 남길 테니."], ["I would wager fifty beaver skins against as many flints, that the Mohicans and I enter their wigwams within the month!", "한 달 안에 〈모히칸〉 부자와 내가 그들의 천막에 들어선다는 데, 비버 가죽 쉰 장을 부싯돌 쉰 개에 걸어도 좋소!"], ["Stoop to it, Uncas, and try what you can make of the moccasin; for moccasin it plainly is, and no shoe.\"", "거기에 몸을 굽혀라, 〈언카스〉, 그 모카신으로 무엇을 알아낼 수 있는지 보아라. 그건 분명 모카신이지 신발이 아니니 말이다.\""]], [["The young Mohican bent over the track, and removing the scattered leaves from around the place, he examined it with much of that sort of scrutiny that a money-dealer, in these days of pecuniary doubts, would bestow on a suspected due-bill.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉이 그 자국 위로 몸을 굽혀, 그 둘레에 흩어진 잎들을 치우고는, 요즘처럼 금전이 의심스러운 시절에 환전상이 미심쩍은 어음을 들여다볼 법한 그 면밀한 눈길로 그것을 살폈다."], ["At length he arose from his knees, satisfied with the result of the examination.", "마침내 그는 검사 결과에 흡족해하며 무릎을 펴고 일어섰다."]], [["\"Well, boy,\"", "\"자, 얘야,\""], ["demanded the attentive scout, \"what does it say?", "주의 깊은 정찰꾼이 물었다. \"뭐라고 하느냐?"], ["can you make anything of the tell-tale?\"", "그 일러바치는 자국에서 무얼 알아냈느냐?\""]], [["\"Le Renard Subtil!\"", "\"〈르 르나르 스뷔틸〉!\""]], [["\"Ha!", "\"하!"], ["that rampaging devil again!", "그 날뛰는 악마가 또로구나!"], ["there never will be an end of his loping, till 'Killdeer' has said a friendly word to him.\"", "'킬디어'가 그놈에게 다정한 한마디를 건네기 전에는, 그놈의 어슬렁거림은 결코 끝나지 않을 게다.\""]], [["Heyward reluctantly admitted the truth of this intelligence, and now expressed rather his hopes than his doubts by saying,--", "〈헤이워드〉는 마지못해 그 소식이 사실임을 인정했으나, 이제 제 의심보다는 차라리 희망을 드러내며 말했다."]], [["\"One moccasin is so much like another, it is probable there is some mistake.\"", "\"모카신이란 게 어느 것이나 너무 비슷하니, 아마 무슨 착오가 있는 게요.\""]], [["\"One moccasin like another!", "\"모카신이 어느 것이나 비슷하다고!"], ["you may as well say that one foot is like another; though we all know that some are long, and others short; some broad, and others narrow; some with high, and some with low insteps; some in-toed, and some out.", "그럼 발이란 게 어느 것이나 비슷하다고 해도 되겠구려. 허나 우리 모두 알다시피, 어떤 것은 길고 어떤 것은 짧으며, 어떤 것은 넓고 어떤 것은 좁고, 어떤 것은 발등이 높고 어떤 것은 낮으며, 어떤 것은 안짱발이고 어떤 것은 밭장발이오."], ["One moccasin is no more like another than one book is like another; though they who can read in one are seldom able to tell the marks of the other.", "모카신이 다른 모카신과 닮은 정도는 책 한 권이 다른 책과 닮은 정도와 다를 바 없소. 허나 한 권을 읽을 줄 아는 자라도 다른 한 권의 자국은 좀처럼 읽어 내지 못하는 법이지."], ["Which is all ordered for the best, giving to every man his natural advantages.", "그게 다 가장 좋도록 마련된 것이니, 사람마다 제 타고난 이점을 주는 게요. 내가 거기에 몸을 굽혀 보겠다, 〈언카스〉."], ["Let me get down to it, Uncas; neither book nor moccasin is the worse for having two opinions, instead of one.\"", "책이든 모카신이든, 의견이 하나가 아니라 둘이라고 해서 나빠질 게 없으니.\" 정찰꾼은 그 일에 몸을 굽혔다가, 곧바로 덧붙였다."], ["The scout stooped to the task, and instantly added, \"You are right, boy; here is the patch we saw so often in the other chase.", "\"네 말이 옳다, 얘야. 지난 추격에서 그토록 자주 보았던 그 덧댄 자국이 여기 있구나."], ["And the fellow will drink when he can get an opportunity: your drinking Indian always learns to walk with a wider toe than the natural savage, it being the gift of a drunkard to straddle, whether of white or red skin.", "그리고 이놈은 기회만 있으면 술을 마실 게다. 술 마시는 인디언은 으레 타고난 야만인보다 발끝을 더 벌리고 걷는 법을 익히는데, 백인이든 붉은 살갗이든 가랑이를 벌리고 걷는 것은 술꾼의 천성이니까."], ["'Tis just the length and breadth too!", "길이며 너비도 딱 그대로다!"], ["look at it, Sagamore: you measured the prints more than once, when we hunted the varmints from Glenn's to the health-springs.\"", "보시오, 〈사가모어〉. 우리가 〈글렌〉의 폭포에서 약수터까지 그 해충들을 쫓을 때, 그대가 이 자국을 한두 번 잰 게 아니지 않소.\""]], [["Chingachgook complied; and after finishing his short examination, he arose, and with a quiet demeanor, he merely pronounced the word--", "〈칭가치국〉은 그대로 따랐다. 그리고 짧은 검사를 마치자 일어서서, 차분한 태도로 그저 한마디를 내뱉었다."]], [["\"Magua!\"", "\"〈마구아〉!\""]], [["\"Ay, 'tis a settled thing; here then have passed the dark-hair and Magua.\"", "\"그렇소, 이제 확실해졌소. 그렇다면 여기를 검은 머리 아가씨와 〈마구아〉가 지난 거요.\""]], [["\"And not Alice?\"", "\"그럼 〈앨리스〉는 아니란 말이오?\""], ["demanded Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"Of her we have not yet seen the signs,\"", "\"그 아가씨의 자취는 아직 보지 못했소.\""], ["returned the scout, looking closely around at the trees, the bushes, and the ground.", "정찰꾼이 나무며 덤불이며 땅을 면밀히 둘러보며 대답했다."], ["\"What have we there?", "\"저긴 또 뭐냐?"], ["Uncas, bring hither the thing you see dangling from yonder thorn-bush.\"", "〈언카스〉, 저쪽 가시덤불에 매달려 흔들리는 저것을 이리 가져오너라.\""]], [["When the Indian had complied, the scout received the prize, and holding it on high, he laughed in his silent but heartfelt manner.", "인디언이 그대로 따르자, 정찰꾼은 그 노획물을 받아 들어 높이 쳐들고는, 소리는 없으나 진심에서 우러난 그만의 방식으로 웃었다."]], [["\"'Tis the tooting we'pon of the singer!", "\"그 노래꾼의 나팔 부는 무기로구나!"], ["now we shall have a trail a priest might travel,\"", "이제 사제라도 따라갈 만한 자취를 얻겠소.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Uncas, look for the marks of a shoe that is long enough to uphold six feet two of tottering human flesh.", "\"〈언카스〉, 비틀거리는 6피트 2인치 인간의 몸뚱이를 떠받칠 만큼 긴 신발 자국을 찾아보아라."], ["I begin to have some hopes of the fellow, since he has given up squalling to follow some better trade.\"", "그자가 꽥꽥대기를 그만두고 무언가 더 나은 일을 좇았으니, 이제 그놈에게 좀 기대를 걸어 볼 만하구나.\""]], [["\"At least, he has been faithful to his trust,\"", "\"적어도 제 소임에는 충실했던 게요.\" 〈헤이워드〉가 말했다."], ["said Heyward; \"and Cora and Alice are not without a friend.\"", "\"〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉에게 벗이 없지는 않은 셈이오.\""]], [["\"Yes,\"", "\"그렇소,\""], ["said Hawkeye, dropping his rifle, and leaning on it with an air of visible contempt, \"he will do their singing.", "〈호크아이〉가 소총을 내려놓고, 눈에 띄는 경멸의 기색으로 거기에 몸을 기대며 말했다. \"그자가 그들을 위해 노래는 불러 줄 거요."], ["Can he slay a buck for their dinner; journey by the moss on the beeches, or cut the throat of a Huron?", "헌데 그들의 끼니로 사슴을 잡을 수 있겠소? 너도밤나무의 이끼를 보고 길을 짚거나, 〈휴런〉 놈의 목을 벨 수 있겠소?"], ["If not, the first catbird[22] he meets is the cleverest of the two.", "그러지 못한다면, 그자가 처음 만나는 개똥지빠귀가 둘 중 더 영리한 셈이오."], ["Well, boy, any signs of such a foundation?\"", "자, 얘야, 그런 발판의 흔적이라도 좀 보이느냐?\""]], [["\"Here is something like the footstep of one who has worn a shoe; can it be that of our friend?\"", "\"여기 신발을 신은 자의 발자국 같은 게 있는데, 우리 벗의 것일 수 있을까요?\""]], [["\"Touch the leaves lightly, or you'll disconsart the formation.", "\"잎을 가만히 건드려라, 안 그러면 형태를 망친다."], ["That!", "저거!"], ["that is the print of a foot, but 'tis the dark-hair's; and small it is, too, for one of such a noble height and grand appearance.", "저건 발자국이긴 하다만, 검은 머리 아가씨의 것이다. 게다가 그토록 고귀한 키와 당당한 풍모를 지닌 이치고는 작기도 하구나."], ["The singer would cover it with his heel.\"", "그 노래꾼이라면 제 발뒤꿈치 하나로 다 덮어 버릴 게다.\""]], [["\"Where!", "\"어디!"], ["let me look on the footsteps of my child,\"", "내 아이의 발자국을 보게 해 주오.\""], ["said Munro, shoving the bushes aside, and bending fondly over the nearly obliterated impression.", "〈먼로〉가 덤불을 옆으로 밀치며, 거의 지워진 그 자국 위로 다정히 몸을 굽히며 말했다."], ["Though the tread, which had left the mark, had been light and rapid, it was still plainly visible.", "자국을 남긴 그 걸음이 가볍고 빨랐음에도, 그것은 아직 또렷이 보였다."], ["The aged soldier examined it with eyes that grew dim as he gazed; nor did he rise from his stooping posture until Heyward saw that he had watered the trace of his daughter's passage with a scalding tear.", "늙은 군인은 바라볼수록 흐려지는 눈으로 그것을 살폈으니, 〈헤이워드〉가 보기에 그가 딸이 지나간 자취를 뜨거운 눈물로 적실 때까지도 그 굽힌 자세에서 일어서지 않았다."], ["Willing to divert a distress which threatened each moment to break through the restraint of appearances, by giving the veteran something to do, the young man said to the scout,--", "매 순간 겉치레의 자제를 뚫고 터져 나올 듯한 그 비통을 달래고자, 노병에게 무언가 할 일을 주려고, 젊은이는 정찰꾼에게 말했다."]], [["\"As we now possess these infallible signs, let us commence our march.", "\"이제 이 틀림없는 자취를 손에 넣었으니, 행군을 시작합시다."], ["A moment, at such a time, will appear an age to the captives.\"", "이런 때엔 한순간이 포로들에게 영겁처럼 느껴질 게요.\""]], [["\"It is not the swiftest leaping deer that gives the longest chase,\"", "\"가장 빨리 뛰는 사슴이 가장 긴 추격을 안기는 건 아니오.\""], ["returned Hawkeye, without moving his eyes from the different marks that had come under his view; \"we know that the rampaging Huron has passed,--and the dark hair,--and the singer,--but where is she of the yellow locks and blue eyes?", "〈호크아이〉가 제 눈에 들어온 갖가지 자국에서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 대답했다. \"우리는 그 날뛰는 〈휴런〉이 지났음을 알고, 검은 머리 아가씨도, 노래꾼도 그러함을 아오."], ["Though little, and far from being as bold as her sister, she is fair to the view, and pleasant in discourse.", "허나 노란 머리에 푸른 눈을 한 그 아가씨는 어디 있소? 비록 자그마하고, 제 언니만큼 담대하지는 못해도, 보기에 곱고 말씨도 정겨운 아가씨인데."], ["Has she no friend, that none care for her?\"", "아무도 돌보아 주지 않다니, 그 아가씨에겐 벗이 없단 말이오?\""]], [["\"God forbid she should ever want hundreds!", "\"하늘이 막아 주소서, 그 애에게 어찌 수백의 벗이 없겠소!"], ["Are we not now in her pursuit?", "우리가 지금 그 애를 찾아 나선 게 아니오?"], ["for one, I will never cease the search till she be found.\"", "나로 말하면, 그 애를 찾을 때까지 결코 수색을 그치지 않겠소.\""]], [["\"In that case we may have to journey by different paths; for here she has not passed, light and little as her footstep would be.\"", "\"그렇다면 우리는 서로 다른 길로 가야 할지도 모르겠소. 그 발걸음이 아무리 가볍고 작다 한들, 여기를 그 아가씨가 지나지는 않았으니.\""]], [["Heyward drew back, all his ardor to proceed seeming to vanish on the instant.", "〈헤이워드〉는 물러섰으니, 나아가려던 그 모든 열의가 그 순간 사라지는 듯했다."], ["Without attending to this sudden change in the other's humor, the scout, after musing a moment, continued,--", "다른 이의 기분에 일어난 그 갑작스러운 변화에는 아랑곳없이, 정찰꾼은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말을 이었다."]], [["\"There is no woman in this wilderness could leave such a print as that, but the dark-hair or her sister.", "\"이 황야에서 그런 자국을 남길 만한 여인은, 검은 머리 아가씨나 그 동생밖에 없소."], ["We know that the first has been here, but where are the signs of the other?", "첫째가 여기 있었음은 알겠는데, 다른 하나의 자취는 어디 있단 말이오?"], ["Let us push deeper on the trail, and if nothing offers, we must go back to the plain and strike another scent.", "이 자취를 더 깊이 좇아 보다가, 아무것도 나오지 않으면, 우리는 평원으로 되돌아가 다른 냄새를 짚어야 하오."], ["Move on, Uncas, and keep your eyes on the dried leaves.", "나아가라, 〈언카스〉, 마른 잎에서 눈을 떼지 마라."], ["I will watch the bushes, while your father shall run with a low nose to the ground.", "나는 덤불을 살필 테고, 네 아비는 코를 땅에 낮추고 달릴 것이다."], ["Move on, friends; the sun is getting behind the hills.\"", "나아갑시다, 벗들이여. 해가 언덕 뒤로 넘어가고 있소.\""]], [["\"Is there nothing that I can do?\"", "\"내가 할 수 있는 일은 없소?\""], ["demanded the anxious Heyward.", "애타는 〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"You!\"", "\"당신 말이오!\""], ["repeated the scout, who, with his red friends, was already advancing in the order he had prescribed; \"yes, you can keep in our rear, and be careful not to cross the trail.\"", "정찰꾼이 되받았으니, 그는 이미 제 붉은 살갗 친구들과 함께 일러 둔 순서대로 나아가는 중이었다. \"그렇소, 당신은 우리 뒤에 바싹 붙어, 자취를 가로지르지 않도록 조심하면 되오.\""]], [["Before they had proceeded many rods, the Indians stopped, and appeared to gaze at some signs on the earth, with more than their usual keenness.", "그들이 몇 로드도 채 나아가기 전에, 인디언들이 멈춰 서더니, 땅 위의 어떤 자국을 평소보다 더한 예리함으로 들여다보는 듯했다."], ["Both father and son spoke quick and loud, now looking at the object of their mutual admiration, and now regarding each other with the most unequivocal pleasure.", "아비와 아들 둘 다 빠르고 큰 소리로 말하며, 함께 감탄하는 그 대상을 보다가, 또 더없이 분명한 기쁨으로 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["\"They have found the little foot!\"", "\"그 작은 발을 찾아냈소!\""], ["exclaimed the scout, moving forward, without attending further to his own portion of the duty.", "정찰꾼이, 제 몫의 임무는 더 살피지도 않고 앞으로 나아가며 외쳤다."], ["\"What have we here?", "\"여기 또 뭐가 있나?"], ["An ambushment has been planted in the spot?", "이 자리에 매복이 펼쳐졌었나?"], ["No, by the truest rifle on the frontiers, here have been them one-sided horses again!", "아니, 변경에서 가장 정직한 소총을 두고 맹세컨대, 여기 또 그 한쪽으로 기운 말들이 있었구나!"], ["Now the whole secret is out, and all is plain as the north star at midnight.", "이제 비밀이 죄다 드러났으니, 한밤중의 북극성처럼 환하게 밝혀졌소."], ["Yes, here they have mounted.", "그렇소, 여기서 그들이 말에 올랐소."], ["There the beasts have been bound to a sapling, in waiting; and yonder runs the broad path away to the north, in full sweep for the Canadas.\"", "저기엔 짐승들이 어린나무에 매여 기다리고 있었고, 저쪽으로는 넓은 길이 북쪽으로, 〈캐나다〉를 향해 곧장 내달리고 있소.\""]], [["\"But still there are no signs of Alice--of the younger Miss Munro,\"--said Duncan.", "\"허나 여전히 〈앨리스〉의, 〈먼로〉 양 가운데 동생의 자취는 없소.\" 던컨이 말했다."]], [["\"Unless the shining bauble Uncas has just lifted from the ground should prove one.", "\"〈언카스〉가 방금 땅에서 집어 든 저 반짝이는 패물이 그 자취가 아니라면 말이오."], ["Pass it this way, lad, that we may look at it.\"", "이리 넘겨라, 얘야, 우리가 그걸 좀 보게.\""]], [["Heyward instantly knew it for a trinket that Alice was fond of wearing, and which he recollected, with the tenacious memory of a lover, to have seen, on the fatal morning of the massacre, dangling from the fair neck of his mistress.", "〈헤이워드〉는 그것이 〈앨리스〉가 즐겨 차던 장신구임을 단번에 알아보았으니, 그는 연인 특유의 끈질긴 기억으로, 그 학살의 운명적인 아침에 그것이 사랑하는 이의 고운 목에 매달려 있던 것을 보았음을 떠올렸다."], ["He seized the highly prized jewel; and as he proclaimed the fact, it vanished from the eyes of the wondering scout, who in vain looked for it on the ground, long after it was warmly pressed against the beating heart of Duncan.", "그가 그 더없이 소중한 보석을 움켜쥐었다. 그리고 그가 그 사실을 알릴 무렵, 그것은 어리둥절한 정찰꾼의 눈앞에서 사라져 버렸으니, 정찰꾼은 그것이 던컨의 두근거리는 가슴에 따뜻이 눌린 지 한참이 지나도록 헛되이 땅에서 그것을 찾았다."]], [["\"Pshaw!\"", "\"쳇!\""], ["said the disappointed Hawkeye, ceasing to rake the leaves with the breech of his rifle; \"'tis a certain sign of age, when the sight begins to weaken.", "실망한 〈호크아이〉가 소총 개머리판으로 잎을 헤집기를 그만두며 말했다. \"시력이 약해지기 시작하면 그게 다 나이 든 증거지."], ["Such a glittering gewgaw, and not to be seen!", "그토록 반짝이는 노리개를 못 보다니!"], ["Well, well, I can squint along a clouded barrel yet, and that is enough to settle all disputes between me and the Mingos.", "자, 그래도 나는 흐린 총신을 따라 곁눈질할 수는 있으니, 그것만으로도 나와 〈밍고〉 놈들 사이의 다툼은 죄다 가릴 수 있소."], ["I should like to find the thing too, if it were only to carry it to the right owner, and that would be bringing the two ends of what I call a long trail together,--for by this time the broad St.", "나도 그 물건을 찾았으면 했는데, 그건 그저 임자에게 돌려주려는 것뿐이오. 그렇게 하면 내가 긴 자취라 부르는 것의 양 끝을 한데 잇는 셈이 되니까."], ["Lawrence, or, perhaps, the Great Lakes themselves, are atwixt us.\"", "헌데 이쯤이면 넓은 〈세인트로렌스〉강이, 어쩌면 큰 호수들 자체가 우리 사이를 가로막고 있을 게요.\""]], [["\"So much the more reason why we should not delay our march,\"", "\"그러니 더더욱 우리가 행군을 미뤄선 안 될 까닭이오.\""], ["returned Heyward; \"let us proceed.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 대답했다. \"어서 갑시다.\""]], [["\"Young blood and hot blood, they say, are much the same thing.", "\"젊은 피와 뜨거운 피는 매한가지라고들 하지."], ["We are not about to start on a squirrel hunt, or to drive a deer into the Horican, but to outlie for days and nights, and to stretch across a wilderness where the feet of men seldom go, and where no bookish knowledge would carry you through harmless.", "우리가 다람쥐 사냥을 떠나거나 사슴 한 마리를 〈호리컨〉으로 몰려는 게 아니라, 여러 날 밤낮을 한데서 지새우고, 사람의 발이 좀처럼 닿지 않는, 책에서 얻은 지식 따위로는 무사히 헤쳐 갈 수 없는 황야를 가로질러야 하는 거요."], ["An Indian never starts on such an expedition without smoking over his council-fire; and though a man of white blood, I honor their customs in this particular, seeing that they are deliberate and wise.", "인디언은 결코 제 평의회의 불가에서 담배를 피우지 않고는 그런 원정에 나서지 않소. 비록 나는 백인의 피를 지녔지만, 이 점에서만은 그들의 관습을 존중하오."], ["We will, therefore, go back, and light our fire to-night in the ruins of the old fort, and in the morning we shall be fresh, and ready to undertake our work like men, and not like babbling women or eager boys.\"", "그것이 신중하고 지혜로운 것임을 알기 때문이오. 그러니 우리는 되돌아가, 오늘 밤은 옛 요새의 폐허에서 불을 지피고, 아침이 되면 기운을 차려, 사내답게 우리 일을 맡읍시다, 재잘대는 아낙네나 안달하는 사내아이들처럼이 아니라.\""]], [["Heyward saw, by the manner of the scout, that altercation would be useless.", "〈헤이워드〉는 정찰꾼의 태도로 보아, 옥신각신해 봐야 소용없음을 알았다."], ["Munro had again sunk into that sort of apathy which had beset him since his late overwhelming misfortunes, and from which he was apparently to be roused only by some new and powerful excitement.", "〈먼로〉는 다시금, 근래의 압도적인 불행 이래 그를 사로잡아 온 그 일종의 무기력에 빠져들었으니, 거기서 그를 일으킬 수 있는 것은 오직 어떤 새롭고 강력한 자극뿐인 듯했다. 젊은이는 부득이한 일을 도리어 미덕으로 삼아, 노병의 팔을 붙들고는, 이미 그들을 평원으로 이끌었던 길을 되짚어 가기 시작한 인디언들과 정찰꾼의 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Making a merit of necessity, the young man took the veteran by the arm, and followed in the footsteps of the Indians and the scout, who had already begun to retrace the path which conducted them to the plain.", "* 개똥지빠귀: 원문의 catbird. 북아메리카에 서식하는, 고양이 울음 비슷한 소리를 내는 새."]], [["\"_Salar._--Why, I am sure, if he forfeit, thou wilt not take his flesh; what's that good for?\"", "\"샐라리노. — 글쎄, 그가 약속을 어긴들 자네가 설마 그 살점을 받아 내겠는가? 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있다고?\""]], [["\"_Shy._--To bait fish withal: if it will feed nothing else, it will feed my revenge.\"", "\"샤일록. — 물고기 미끼로나 쓰지. 달리 아무것도 먹이지 못한대도, 내 복수만큼은 먹여 살릴 테니.\""]], [["_Merchant of Venice._", "「베니스의 상인」"]], [["The shades of evening had come to increase the dreariness of the place, when the party entered the ruins of William Henry.", "저녁 어스름이 내려앉아 이곳의 황량함을 한층 짙게 만들 무렵, 일행은 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 폐허로 들어섰다."], ["The scout and his companions immediately made their preparations to pass the night there; but with an earnestness and sobriety of demeanor, that betrayed how much the unusual horrors they had just witnessed worked on even their practised feelings.", "척후와 그 동료들은 곧 그곳에서 밤을 지낼 채비를 시작했으나, 그 진지하고 가라앉은 태도는, 방금 목격한 그 예사롭지 않은 참상이 단련된 그들의 감정에까지 얼마나 깊이 작용했는지를 드러냈다."], ["A few fragments of rafters were reared against a blackened wall; and when Uncas had covered them slightly with brush, the temporary accommodations were deemed sufficient.", "서까래의 부서진 조각 몇 개가 검게 그을린 벽에 기대어 세워졌고, 〈웅카스〉가 그 위를 나뭇가지로 엉성하게 덮자, 임시 거처로는 충분하다고 여겨졌다."], ["The young Indian pointed towards his rude hut, when his labor was ended; and Heyward, who understood the meaning of the silent gesture, gently urged Munro to enter.", "일을 마친 젊은 인디언은 자신이 지은 거친 오두막을 가리켰고, 그 말 없는 몸짓의 뜻을 헤아린 〈헤이워드〉는 〈먼로〉에게 안으로 들어가기를 부드럽게 권했다."], ["Leaving the bereaved old man alone with his sorrows, Duncan immediately returned to the open air, too much excited himself to seek the repose he had recommended to his veteran friend.", "슬픔에 잠긴 노인을 그 비탄과 함께 홀로 남겨 둔 채, 〈덩컨〉은 곧 바깥바람 속으로 돌아왔으니, 그 자신이 너무도 격앙되어, 노병인 벗에게 권한 그 휴식을 정작 스스로는 구할 수 없었다."]], [["While Hawkeye and the Indians lighted their fire, and took their evening's repast, a frugal meal of dried bear's meat, the young man paid a visit to that curtain of the dilapidated fort which looked out on the sheet of the Horican.", "〈호크아이〉와 인디언들이 불을 피우고, 말린 곰고기로 차린 검소한 저녁 끼니를 드는 동안, 젊은이는 〈호리컨〉의 수면을 굽어보는 허물어진 요새의 그 성벽 쪽을 찾아갔다."], ["The wind had fallen, and the waves were already rolling on the sandy beach beneath him, in a more regular and tempered succession.", "바람은 잦아들었고, 그의 발치 모래 해변으로 파도가 이미 한결 고르고 누그러진 가락으로 밀려오고 있었다."], ["The clouds, as if tired of their furious chase, were breaking asunder; the heavier volumes, gathering in black masses about the horizon, while the lighter scud still hurried above the water, or eddied among the tops of the mountains, like broken flights of birds, hovering around their roosts.", "구름은 사납던 쫓김에 지치기라도 한 듯 갈라지고 있었으니, 무거운 덩어리들은 지평선께에 검은 무리를 이루어 모여들고, 가벼운 조각구름은 여전히 물 위로 서둘러 흘러가거나, 보금자리를 맴도는 흩어진 새 떼처럼 산봉우리 사이를 휘돌았다."], ["Here and there, a red and fiery star struggled through the drifting vapor, furnishing a lurid gleam of brightness to the dull aspect of the heavens.", "여기저기, 붉고 불타는 별 하나가 떠도는 안개를 뚫고 안간힘을 쓰며, 침침한 하늘의 둔한 낯빛에 음산한 한 줄기 광채를 보탰다."], ["Within the bosom of the encircling hills, an impenetrable darkness had already settled; and the plain lay like a vast and deserted charnel-house, without omen or whisper to disturb the slumbers of its numerous and hapless tenants.", "에워싼 언덕들의 품속에는 이미 뚫을 수 없는 어둠이 내려앉았고, 평원은 광막하고 버려진 납골당처럼 누워 있었으니, 그 무수하고 가엾은 입주자들의 잠을 어지럽힐 그 어떤 조짐도, 속삭임도 없었다."]], [["Of this scene, so chillingly in accordance with the past, Duncan stood for many minutes a rapt observer.", "지나간 일과 이토록 으스스하게 어우러진 이 광경을, 〈덩컨〉은 여러 분 동안 넋을 잃고 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["His eyes wandered from the bosom of the mound, where the foresters were seated around their glimmering fire, to the fainter light which still lingered in the skies, and then rested long and anxiously on the embodied gloom, which lay like a dreary void on that side of him where the dead reposed.", "그의 두 눈은 삼림의 사내들이 가물거리는 불 둘레에 앉아 있는 둔덕의 품에서, 아직 하늘에 머문 한층 희미한 빛으로 옮겨 갔다가, 이내 죽은 자들이 잠든 그쪽에 음울한 공허처럼 깔린 그 짙은 어둠에, 길고도 불안하게 머물렀다."], ["He soon fancied that inexplicable sounds arose from the place, though so indistinct and stolen, as to render not only their nature but even their existence uncertain.", "그는 곧 그곳에서 설명할 수 없는 소리들이 일어난다고 여겼으나, 그것은 너무도 어렴풋하고 은밀하여, 그 성질은커녕 그 존재 자체마저 분간할 수 없었다."], ["Ashamed of his apprehensions, the young man turned towards the water, and strove to divert his attentions to the mimic stars that dimly glimmered on its moving surface.", "자신의 불안이 부끄러워, 젊은이는 물 쪽으로 돌아서서, 일렁이는 수면 위로 어슴푸레 반짝이는 그 흉내 낸 별들로 주의를 돌리려 애썼다."], ["Still, his too conscious ears performed their ungrateful duty, as if to warn him of some lurking danger.", "그래도, 너무 예민한 그의 귀는, 어떤 숨어든 위험을 일러 주기라도 하려는 듯, 달갑잖은 제 소임을 다했다."], ["At length a swift trampling seemed quite audibly to rush athwart the darkness.", "마침내 무언가가 잰걸음으로 짓밟으며 어둠을 가로질러 내닫는 소리가 또렷이 들리는 듯했다."], ["Unable any longer to quiet his uneasiness, Duncan spoke in a low voice to the scout, requesting him to ascend the mound to the place where he stood.", "더는 불안을 가라앉힐 수 없게 된 〈덩컨〉은 낮은 목소리로 척후에게 말을 건네, 자신이 선 자리로 둔덕을 올라와 달라고 청했다."], ["Hawkeye threw his rifle across an arm, and complied, but with an air so unmoved and calm, as to prove how much he counted on the security of their position.", "〈호크아이〉는 소총을 한쪽 팔에 걸치고 응했으나, 그 태도가 너무도 흔들림 없고 고요하여, 그가 그들의 위치가 안전하다는 데 얼마나 기대고 있는지를 잘 드러냈다."]], [["\"Listen!\"", "\"들어 보시오!\" 상대가 천천히 제 팔꿈치 곁에 자리를 잡자 〈덩컨〉이 말했다."], ["said Duncan, when the other placed himself deliberately at his elbow: \"there are suppressed noises on the plain which may show that Montcalm has not yet entirely deserted his conquest.\"", "\"평원에 죽인 소리들이 있소. 〈몽칼름〉이 아직 제가 정복한 땅을 완전히 떠나지 않았다는 표일지도 모르오.\""]], [["\"Then ears are better than eyes,\"", "\"그렇다면 귀가 눈보다 낫구려.\""], ["said the undisturbed scout, who, having just deposited a portion of bear between his grinders, spoke thick and slow, like one whose mouth was doubly occupied.", "흔들림 없는 척후가 말했으니, 그는 막 곰고기 한 점을 어금니 사이에 밀어 넣은 터라, 입이 두 가지로 차 있는 자처럼 둔하고 느릿하게 말했다."], ["\"I, myself, saw him caged in Ty, with all his host; for your Frenchers, when they have done a clever thing, like to get back, and have a dance, or a merry-making, with the women over their success.\"", "\"나는 그자가 제 군세를 몽땅 거느린 채 〈타이〉에 갇힌 걸 이 두 눈으로 봤소. 당신네 프랑스 친구들은 영리한 한 수를 두고 나면, 돌아가서 여자들과 더불어 제 성공을 두고 춤을 추거나 잔치를 벌이기를 좋아하니까.\""]], [["\"I know not.", "\"나는 모르겠소."], ["An Indian seldom sleeps in war, and plunder may keep a Huron here after his tribe has departed.", "인디언은 전시에 좀처럼 잠들지 않고, 약탈거리 때문에 〈휴런〉 하나쯤은 제 부족이 떠난 뒤에도 여기 남아 있을 수 있소."], ["It would be well to extinguish the fire, and have a watch--listen!", "불을 끄고 보초를 세우는 게 좋겠소 — 들어 보시오!"], ["you hear the noise I mean!\"", "내가 말하는 그 소리가 들리지 않소!\""]], [["\"An Indian more rarely lurks about the graves.", "\"인디언은 무덤가에 도사리는 일이 더욱 드물지요."], ["Though ready to slay, and not over-regardful of the means, he is commonly content with the scalp, unless when blood is hot, and temper up; but after the spirit is once fairly gone, he forgets his enmity, and is willing to let the dead find their natural rest.", "죽일 채비야 늘 되어 있고, 그 수단을 그리 가리지도 않지만, 피가 끓어오르고 성미가 곤두서 있을 때가 아니라면 대개 머리가죽 하나로 만족하오. 허나 일단 혼이 온전히 떠나고 나면, 그는 제 원한을 잊고 죽은 자들이 제 자연스러운 안식을 찾도록 기꺼이 내버려 두지요."], ["Speaking of spirits, Major, are you of opinion that the heaven of a redskin and of us whites will be one and the same?\"", "혼 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 소령, 붉은 살갗을 가진 자의 천국과 우리 백인의 천국이 하나요 같은 것이라 보시오?\""]], [["\"No doubt--no doubt. I thought I heard it again!", "\"의심할 바 없소 — 의심할 바 없소."], ["or was it the rustling of the leaves in the top of the beech?\"", "또 들린 것 같은데! 아니면 너도밤나무 꼭대기 잎새가 바스락거린 소리였소?\""]], [["\"For my own part,\"", "\"내 생각으로는 말이오,\""], ["continued Hawkeye, turning his face, for a moment, in the direction indicated by Heyward, but with a vacant and careless manner, \"I believe that paradise is ordained for happiness; and that men will be indulged in it according to their dispositions and gifts.", "〈호크아이〉가 잠시 〈헤이워드〉가 가리킨 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸으나, 멍하고 무심한 태도로 말을 이었다. \"낙원은 행복을 위해 마련된 곳이고, 사람은 제 기질과 타고난 재간에 따라 그 안에서 누림을 허락받으리라 믿소."], ["I therefore judge that a redskin is not far from the truth when he believes he is to find them glorious hunting-grounds of which his traditions tell; nor, for that matter, do I think it would be any disparagement to a man without a cross to pass his time--\"", "그러니 붉은 살갗을 가진 자가, 제 전설이 일러 주는 그 영광스러운 사냥터를 거기서 찾으리라 믿을 때, 그가 진실에서 그리 멀지 않다고 나는 판단하오. 또 그 점으로 말하자면, 혈통에 섞임 없는 자가 그 시간을 보내는 게 그에게 조금도 흠이 되리라곤 생각지 않소 —\""]], [["\"You hear it again?\"", "\"또 들리지 않소?\""], ["interrupted Duncan.", "〈덩컨〉이 가로막았다."]], [["\"Ay, ay; when food is scarce, and when food is plenty, a wolf grows bold,\"", "\"그렇소, 그렇소. 먹이가 귀할 때도, 먹이가 넘칠 때도, 늑대는 대담해지는 법이오.\""], ["said the unmoved scout.", "흔들림 없는 척후가 말했다."], ["\"There would be picking, too, among the skins of the devils, if there was light and time for the sport.", "\"빛과 그럴 짬만 있다면, 저 악마들 가죽 가운데서도 골라잡을 게 좀 있을 거요."], ["But, concerning the life that is to come, major: I have heard preachers say, in the settlements, that heaven was a place of rest.", "허나 앞으로 올 삶에 대해서 말이오, 소령. 나는 정착지에서 설교자들이, 천국은 안식의 자리라 말하는 걸 들었소."], ["Now men's minds differ as to their ideas of enjoyment.", "한데 누림에 관한 생각은 사람마다 다르오."], ["For myself, and I say it with reverence to the ordering of Providence, it would be no great indulgence to be kept shut up in those mansions of which they preach, having a natural longing for motion and the chase.\"", "나로 말하자면, 섭리의 정하심에 대한 외경을 담아 말하건대, 움직임과 사냥을 타고난 그리움으로 품은 자에게, 그들이 설교하는 그 저택에 갇혀 지내는 건 그리 큰 누림이 못 될 거요.\""]], [["Duncan, who was now made to understand the nature of the noises he had heard, answered with more attention to the subject which the humor of the scout had chosen for discussion, by saying,--", "이제 자신이 들은 소리들의 정체를 알게 된 〈덩컨〉은, 척후의 기분이 골라잡은 그 화제에 한층 주의를 기울여 대답하며 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"It is difficult to account for the feelings that may attend the last great change.\"", "\"마지막 큰 변화에 따를 그 느낌이란 헤아리기 어려운 일이오.\""]], [["\"It would be a change, indeed, for a man who has passed his days in the open air,\"", "\"바깥바람 속에서 평생을 보낸 사람에겐 정말이지 큰 변화일 거요.\""], ["returned the single-minded scout; \"and who has so often broken his fast on the head-waters of the Hudson, to sleep within sound of the roaring Mohawk.", "외곬인 척후가 대꾸했다. \"〈허드슨〉의 발원지에서 그토록 자주 아침 끼니를 들고, 우렁찬 〈모호크〉강의 물소리가 닿는 데서 잠들어 온 자에게는 말이오."], ["But it is a comfort to know we serve a merciful Master, though we do it each after his fashion, and with great tracts of wilderness atween us--what goes there?\"", "허나 우리가 자비로우신 주인을 섬긴다는 걸 아는 건 위안이 되오. 비록 저마다 제 방식대로, 우리 사이에 광막한 황야를 끼고 그분을 섬길지라도 — 저기 무엇이 지나가오?\""]], [["\"Is it not the rushing of the wolves you have mentioned?\"", "\"당신이 말한 그 내달리는 늑대들 아니오?\""]], [["Hawkeye slowly shook his head, and beckoned for Duncan to follow him to a spot, to which the glare from the fire did not extend.", "〈호크아이〉는 천천히 고개를 저으며, 불빛이 닿지 않는 자리로 따라오라고 〈덩컨〉에게 손짓했다."], ["When he had taken this precaution, the scout placed himself in an attitude of intense attention, and listened long and keenly for a repetition of the low sound that had so unexpectedly startled him.", "이 조심을 다한 뒤, 척후는 온 정신을 곤두세운 자세를 취하고, 그토록 뜻밖에 그를 놀라게 한 그 낮은 소리가 되풀이되기를 길고도 날카롭게 귀 기울였다."], ["His vigilance, however, seemed exercised in vain; for, after a fruitless pause, he whispered to Duncan,--", "그러나 그 경계는 헛수고로 돌아간 듯했으니, 보람 없는 한참의 멈춤 끝에 그는 〈덩컨〉에게 속삭였다."]], [["\"We must give a call to Uncas.", "\"〈웅카스〉를 불러야겠소."], ["The boy has Indian senses, and may hear what is hid from us; for being a white-skin, I will not deny my nature.\"", "그 애는 인디언의 감각을 지녔으니, 우리에게 가려진 걸 들을지도 모르오. 나는 흰 살갗을 지닌 몸이라, 내 본성을 부인하진 않겠소.\""]], [["The young Mohican, who was conversing in a low voice with his father, started as he heard the moaning of an owl, and springing on his feet he looked towards the black mounds, as if seeking the place whence the sounds proceeded.", "낮은 목소리로 아비와 이야기를 나누던 젊은 〈모히칸〉은, 올빼미가 우는 소리를 듣자 흠칫하더니, 벌떡 일어나 그 소리가 흘러나온 곳을 찾으려는 듯 검은 둔덕들 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["The scout repeated the call, and in a few moments, Duncan saw the figure of Uncas stealing cautiously along the rampart, to the spot where they stood.", "척후가 그 부름을 되풀이하자, 곧 〈덩컨〉은 〈웅카스〉의 형상이 성벽을 따라 조심스레 그들이 선 자리로 다가오는 것을 보았다."]], [["Hawkeye explained his wishes in a very few words, which were spoken in the Delaware tongue.", "〈호크아이〉는 자신의 뜻을 〈델라웨어〉 말로, 몇 마디 안 되는 말로 일러 주었다."], ["So soon as Uncas was in possession of the reason why he was summoned, he threw himself flat on the turf; where, to the eyes of Duncan, he appeared to lie quiet and motionless.", "〈웅카스〉는 자신이 불려 온 까닭을 알아차리자마자, 잔디 위에 납작 엎드렸고, 〈덩컨〉의 눈에는 그가 고요히 꼼짝 않고 누운 듯 보였다."], ["Surprised at the immovable attitude of the young warrior, and curious to observe the manner in which he employed his faculties to obtain the desired information, Heyward advanced a few steps, and bent over the dark object, on which he had kept his eyes riveted.", "젊은 전사의 미동도 없는 자세에 놀라고, 그가 바라는 정보를 얻고자 제 능력을 어떻게 쓰는지 궁금해진 〈헤이워드〉는, 몇 걸음 나아가, 눈을 박아 두었던 그 어두운 물체 위로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["Then it was he discovered that the form of Uncas had vanished, and that he beheld only the dark outline of an inequality in the embankment.", "바로 그때 그는 〈웅카스〉의 형상이 사라지고, 자신이 보고 있는 것이 둑의 울퉁불퉁한 굴곡의 검은 윤곽뿐임을 알아차렸다."]], [["\"What has become of the Mohican?\"", "\"그 〈모히칸〉이 어찌 된 거요?\""], ["he demanded of the scout, stepping back in amazement; \"it was here that I saw him fall, and I could have sworn that here he yet remained.\"", "그가 놀라 뒷걸음치며 척후에게 물었다. \"바로 여기서 그가 엎드리는 걸 봤고, 여기 아직 그대로 있다고 맹세라도 할 수 있었는데.\""]], [["\"Hist!", "\"쉿!"], ["speak lower; for we know not what ears are open, and the Mingos are a quick-witted breed.", "더 낮게 말하시오. 어떤 귀가 열려 있을지 우리는 모르고, 〈밍고〉는 머리 회전이 빠른 족속이니까."], ["As for Uncas, he is out on the plain, and the Maquas, if any such are about us, will find their equal.\"", "〈웅카스〉로 말하자면, 그 애는 평원에 나가 있고, 우리 둘레에 〈매콰〉가 있다면 제 맞수를 만나게 될 거요.\""]], [["\"You think that Montcalm has not called off all his Indians?", "\"〈몽칼름〉이 제 인디언들을 다 거두지 않았다고 보는 거요?"], ["Let us give the alarm to our companions, that we may stand to our arms.", "우리 동료들에게 경보를 알려, 무기를 들고 맞설 채비를 합시다."], ["Here are five of us, who are not unused to meet an enemy.\"", "우리는 다섯이고, 적을 맞는 데 낯설지 않은 자들이오.\""]], [["\"Not a word to either, as you value life.", "\"목숨이 아깝거든 누구에게도 한마디 마시오."], ["Look at the Sagamore, how like a grand Indian chief he sits by the fire.", "저 〈사가모어〉를 보시오, 얼마나 위풍당당한 인디언 추장처럼 불가에 앉아 있는지."], ["If there are any skulkers out in the darkness, they will never discover by his countenance that we suspect danger at hand.\"", "어둠 속에 도사린 자가 있다 한들, 우리가 곁에 닥친 위험을 눈치챘다는 걸 그 낯빛으로는 결코 알아내지 못할 거요.\""]], [["\"But they may discover him, and it will prove his death.", "\"하지만 저들이 그를 발견할 수 있고, 그러면 그건 그의 죽음이 될 거요."], ["His person can be too plainly seen by the light of that fire, and he will become the first and most certain victim.\"", "저 불빛에 그의 몸이 너무 또렷이 보이니, 가장 먼저, 가장 확실한 희생자가 될 거요.\""]], [["\"It is undeniable that now you speak the truth,\"", "\"이제 당신이 옳은 말을 한다는 건 부인할 수 없구려.\""], ["returned the scout, betraying more anxiety than was usual; \"yet what can be done?", "평소보다 더한 불안을 내비치며 척후가 대꾸했다. \"허나 어쩌겠소?"], ["A single suspicious look might bring on an attack before we are ready to receive it.", "의심스러운 눈길 한 번이 우리가 받을 채비를 갖추기도 전에 공격을 불러올지 모르오. 그는 내가 〈웅카스〉에게 보낸 부름으로, 우리가 냄새를 맡았다는 걸 알고 있소."], ["He knows, by the call I gave to Uncas, that we have struck a scent: I will tell him that we are on the trail of the Mingos; his Indian nature will teach him how to act.\"", "〈밍고〉의 자취를 좇고 있다고 그에게 일러 주리다. 그의 인디언 본성이 어떻게 처신할지를 가르쳐 줄 거요.\""]], [["The scout applied his fingers to his mouth, and raised a low hissing sound, that caused Duncan, at first, to start aside, believing that he heard a serpent.", "척후는 손가락을 입에 대고, 낮게 쉭쉭거리는 소리를 냈으니, 〈덩컨〉은 처음엔 뱀 소리를 들은 줄 알고 흠칫 비켜섰다."], ["The head of Chingachgook was resting on a hand, as he sat musing by himself; but the moment he heard the warning of the animal whose name he bore, it arose to an upright position and his dark eyes glanced swiftly and keenly on every side of him.", "〈칭가치국〉은 홀로 생각에 잠긴 채 손에 머리를 괴고 앉아 있었으나, 제가 이름을 따온 그 짐승의 경고를 듣는 순간, 똑바로 일어나 앉았고, 그의 검은 두 눈이 사방을 잽싸고 날카롭게 훑었다."], ["With this sudden and perhaps involuntary movement, every appearance of surprise or alarm ended.", "이 갑작스럽고, 어쩌면 저도 모르게 나온 움직임과 더불어, 놀람이나 경계의 기색은 죄다 사라졌다."], ["His rifle lay untouched, and apparently unnoticed, within reach of his hand.", "그의 소총은 손이 닿는 데에 손대지도, 눈길도 주지 않은 듯 놓여 있었다."], ["The tomahawk that he had loosened in his belt for the sake of ease, was even suffered to fall from its usual situation to the ground, and his form seemed to sink, like that of a man whose nerves and sinews were suffered to relax for the purpose of rest.", "편하라고 허리띠에서 느슨히 풀어 두었던 손도끼마저 제자리에서 땅으로 떨어지도록 내버려 두었고, 그의 몸은 휴식을 위해 신경과 힘줄을 다 풀어 놓은 자의 그것처럼 가라앉는 듯했다."], ["Cunningly resuming his former position, though with a change of hands, as if the movement had been made merely to relieve the limb, the native awaited the result with a calmness and fortitude that none but an Indian warrior would have known how to exercise.", "마치 그저 팔다리를 편히 하려 한 동작인 양 손만 바꿔, 영리하게 본래 자세로 돌아간 채, 그 원주민은 인디언 전사가 아니고는 누구도 부릴 줄 모를 그런 고요함과 의연함으로 결과를 기다렸다."]], [["But Heyward saw that while to a less instructed eye the Mohican chief appeared to slumber, his nostrils were expanded, his head was turned a little to one side, as if to assist the organs of hearing, and that his quick and rapid glances ran incessantly over every object, within the power of his vision.", "그러나 〈헤이워드〉는, 덜 숙련된 눈에는 〈모히칸〉 추장이 졸고 있는 듯 보일지언정, 그의 콧구멍이 벌어져 있고, 청각의 기관을 돕기라도 하려는 듯 머리가 한쪽으로 살짝 기울어 있으며, 그 빠르고 잰 눈길이 제 시야가 미치는 온갖 사물 위로 쉴 새 없이 달리고 있음을 보았다."]], [["\"See the noble fellow!\"", "\"저 고귀한 친구를 보시오!\""], ["whispered Hawkeye, pressing the arm of Heyward; \"he knows that a look or a motion might discansart our schemes, and put us at the mercy of them imps--\"", "〈호크아이〉가 〈헤이워드〉의 팔을 누르며 속삭였다. \"눈짓 한 번, 몸짓 하나가 우리 계책을 어그러뜨리고 우리를 저 악귀들의 처분에 맡길 수 있다는 걸 그는 알고 있소 —\""]], [["He was interrupted by the flash and report of a rifle.", "소총의 섬광과 발사음이 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["The air was filled with sparks of fire around that spot where the eyes of Heyward were still fastened with admiration and wonder.", "〈헤이워드〉의 두 눈이 감탄과 경이로 여전히 박혀 있던 그 자리 둘레로, 공기가 불꽃으로 가득 찼다."], ["A second look told him that Chingachgook had disappeared in the confusion.", "다시 한번 보니 〈칭가치국〉은 그 혼란 속에 사라지고 없었다."], ["In the meantime the scout had thrown forward his rifle, like one prepared for service, and awaited impatiently the moment when an enemy might rise to view.", "그동안 척후는 임무에 나설 채비를 갖춘 자처럼 소총을 앞으로 내밀고, 적이 모습을 드러낼 그 순간을 안달하며 기다렸다."], ["But with the solitary and fruitless attempt made on the life of Chingachgook, the attack appeared to have terminated.", "그러나 〈칭가치국〉의 목숨을 노린 그 외롭고 보람 없는 한 차례 시도와 함께, 공격은 끝난 듯했다."], ["Once or twice the listeners thought they could distinguish the distant rustling of bushes, as bodies of some unknown description rushed through them; nor was it long before Hawkeye pointed out the \"scampering of the wolves,\"", "한두 번, 정체 모를 무언가가 덤불을 헤치고 내달리며 멀리서 바스락거리는 소리를 듣는 이들은 분간할 수 있다고 여겼다."], ["as they fled precipitately before the passage of some intruder on their proper domains.", "오래지 않아 〈호크아이〉는, 제 영역을 침범한 누군가가 지나가는 앞에서 황급히 달아나는 \"늑대들의 내뺌\"을 가리켜 보였다."], ["After an impatient and breathless pause, a plunge was heard in the water, and it was immediately followed by the report of another rifle.", "안달과 숨죽임의 멈춤 끝에, 물에 무언가가 뛰어드는 소리가 들렸고, 곧이어 또 다른 소총의 발사음이 뒤따랐다."]], [["\"There goes Uncas!\"", "\"저기 〈웅카스〉가 가는군!\""], ["said the scout; \"the boy bears a smart piece!", "척후가 말했다. \"그 애는 야무진 총을 지녔지!"], ["I know its crack, as well as a father knows the language of his child, for I carried the gun myself until a better offered.\"", "아비가 제 자식의 말투를 알듯, 나는 그 총소리를 알아듣소. 더 나은 게 생기기 전까지 그 총은 내가 직접 지니고 다녔으니 말이오.\""]], [["\"What can this mean?\"", "\"이게 무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["demanded Duncan; \"we are watched, and, as it would seem, marked for destruction.\"", "〈덩컨〉이 물었다. \"우리는 감시받고 있고, 보아하니 죽임을 당하도록 점찍힌 듯하오.\""]], [["\"Yonder scattered brand can witness that no good was intended, and this Indian will testify that no harm has been done,\"", "\"저기 흩어진 불씨가, 선의 따윈 없었다는 걸 증언할 테고, 이 인디언이 아무 해도 입지 않았다는 걸 증언할 거요.\""], ["returned the scout, dropping his rifle across his arm again, and following Chingachgook, who just then reappeared within the circle of light, into the bosom of the works.", "척후가 소총을 다시 팔에 걸치며 대꾸하더니, 마침 빛의 둘레 안에 다시 모습을 드러낸 〈칭가치국〉을 따라 진지의 품으로 들어섰다. \"어찌 된 거요, 〈사가모어〉?"], ["\"How is it, Sagamore? Are the Mingos upon us in earnest, or is it only one of those rept_y_les who hang upon the skirts of a war party, to scalp the dead, go in, and make their boast among the squaws of the valiant deeds done on the pale-faces?\"", "〈밍고〉가 진심으로 우리를 노린 거요, 아니면 그저 전투대의 꽁무니에 매달려 죽은 자의 머리가죽을 벗기고 돌아가, 창백한 얼굴들에게 떨친 용맹한 무용담을 여인네들 사이에서 떠벌리는 그런 굼벵이 하나였소?\""]], [["Chingachgook very quietly resumed his seat; nor did he make any reply, until after he had examined the firebrand which had been struck by the bullet that had nearly proved fatal to himself.", "〈칭가치국〉은 아주 조용히 제자리로 돌아가 앉았으며, 하마터면 제 목숨을 앗을 뻔한 그 총알에 맞은 불붙은 장작을 살핀 뒤에야 비로소 대답했다."], ["After which, he was content to reply, holding a single finger up to view, with the English monosyllable,--", "그러고 나서, 그는 손가락 하나를 들어 보이며, 영어 한 음절로 대답하는 것으로 족했다."]], [["\"One.\"", "\"하나.\""]], [["\"I thought as much,\"", "\"나도 그쯤으로 짐작했소.\" 〈호크아이〉가 자리에 앉으며 대꾸했다."], ["returned Hawkeye, seating himself; \"and as he had got the cover of the lake afore Uncas pulled upon him, it is more than probable the knave will sing his lies about some great ambushment, in which he was outlying on the trail of two Mohicans and a white hunter--for the officers can be considered as little better than idlers in such a scrimmage.", "\"그리고 〈웅카스〉가 그자에게 방아쇠를 당기기 전에 호수의 엄폐물에 들었으니, 그 악당이 어떤 대단한 매복에 관해 거짓 노래를 불러 댈 게 거의 틀림없소. 〈모히칸〉 둘과 백인 사냥꾼 하나의 자취를 밟아 매복해 있었노라고 말이오 — 이런 옥신각신에서 장교들이야 노는 자들이나 다름없다고 칠 테니까."], ["Well, let him--let him.", "뭐, 떠벌리든 말든 — 떠벌리든 말든."], ["There are always some honest men in every nation, though heaven knows, too, that they are scarce among the Maquas, to look down an upstart when he brags ag'in the face of reason.", "어느 민족에나 정직한 자가 늘 몇은 있는 법이오. 하늘은 알겠지만 〈매콰〉 사이엔 그런 자가 드물긴 하나, 이치에 맞서 허풍을 떠는 풋내기를 한번 눌러 줄 자 말이오."], ["The varlet sent his lead within whistle of your ears, Sagamore.\"", "그 망나니가 당신 귓전을 스치도록 납탄을 보냈소, 〈사가모어〉.\""]], [["Chingachgook turned a calm and incurious eye towards the place where the ball had struck, and then resumed his former attitude, with a composure that could not be disturbed by so trifling an incident.", "〈칭가치국〉은 총알이 박힌 자리로 고요하고 호기심 없는 눈길을 돌렸다가, 이런 하찮은 일 따위로는 흔들릴 수 없는 평정으로 본래 자세로 돌아갔다."], ["Just then Uncas glided into the circle, and seated himself at the fire, with the same appearance of indifference as was maintained by his father.", "바로 그때 〈웅카스〉가 빛의 둘레 안으로 미끄러져 들어와, 제 아비가 지켜 온 것과 똑같은 무심한 기색으로 불가에 앉았다."]], [["Of these several movements Heyward was a deeply interested and wondering observer.", "이 여러 움직임을, 〈헤이워드〉는 깊이 흥미를 느끼며 경탄하는 관찰자로서 지켜보았다."], ["It appeared to him as though the foresters had some secret means of intelligence, which had escaped the vigilance of his own faculties.", "그에게는 삼림의 사내들이, 제 능력의 경계를 벗어난 어떤 은밀한 소식의 수단을 지닌 듯이 여겨졌다."], ["In place of that eager and garrulous narration with which a white youth would have endeavored to communicate, and perhaps exaggerate, that which had passed out in the darkness of the plain, the young warrior was seemingly content to let his deeds speak for themselves.", "백인 청년이라면 평원의 어둠 속에서 벌어진 일을 알리려, 어쩌면 부풀리려 들었을 그 열렬하고 수다스러운 늘어놓음 대신, 젊은 전사는 제 행적이 스스로 말하게 두는 것으로 만족하는 듯했다."], ["It was, in fact, neither the moment nor the occasion for an Indian to boast of his exploits; and it is probable, that had Heyward neglected to inquire, not another syllable would, just then, have been uttered on the subject.", "사실 인디언이 제 공적을 자랑할 만한 때도, 자리도 아니었으니, 만일 〈헤이워드〉가 묻기를 게을리했다면, 그 일에 관해서는 당장에 더는 한 음절도 새어 나오지 않았으리라 짐작된다."]], [["\"What has become of our enemy, Uncas?\"", "\"우리 적은 어찌 됐소, 〈웅카스〉?\""], ["demanded Duncan: \"we heard your rifle, and hoped you had not fired in vain.\"", "〈덩컨〉이 물었다. \"당신 소총 소리를 들었고, 헛되이 쏘지 않았기를 바랐소.\""]], [["The young chief removed a fold of his hunting-shirt, and quietly exposed the fatal tuft of hair, which he bore as the symbol of victory.", "젊은 추장은 사냥 셔츠의 한 자락을 젖혀, 승리의 표징으로 지닌 그 죽음의 머리채를 조용히 드러내 보였다."], ["Chingachgook laid his hand on the scalp, and considered it for a moment with deep attention.", "〈칭가치국〉은 그 머리가죽에 손을 얹고, 한순간 깊은 주의를 기울여 그것을 살폈다."], ["Then dropping it, with disgust depicted in his strong features, he ejaculated,--", "그러더니 강인한 이목구비에 혐오를 띤 채 그것을 떨어뜨리며 내뱉었다."]], [["\"Oneida!\"", "\"〈오나이다〉!\""]], [["\"Oneida!\"", "\"〈오나이다〉라!\""], ["repeated the scout, who was fast losing his interest in the scene, in an apathy nearly assimilated to that of his red associates, but who now advanced with uncommon earnestness to regard the bloody badge.", "척후가 되뇌었으니, 그는 붉은 동료들의 그것에 거의 맞먹는 무심함에 빠져 이 장면에 대한 흥미를 빠르게 잃어 가던 참이었으나, 이제 보기 드문 진지함으로 다가와 그 피 묻은 표지를 들여다보았다."], ["\"By the Lord, if the Oneidas are outlying upon the trail, we shall be flanked by devils on every side of us!", "\"맙소사, 〈오나이다〉가 자취 위에 매복해 있다면, 우리는 사방에서 악마들에게 측면을 에워싸이는 거요!"], ["Now, to white eyes there is no difference between this bit of skin and that of any other Indian, and yet the Sagamore declares it came from the poll of a Mingo; nay, he even names the tribe of the poor devil with as much ease as if the scalp was the leaf of a book, and each hair a letter.", "자, 백인의 눈에는 이 살가죽 한 조각과 다른 어느 인디언의 것 사이에 아무 차이도 없건만, 〈사가모어〉는 이게 〈밍고〉의 정수리에서 나온 거라 단언하고, 아니, 그 머리가죽이 책의 한 장이고 털 한 올 한 올이 글자이기라도 한 듯, 그 가엾은 악당의 부족까지 이름 대어 말하지 않소."], ["What right have Christian whites to boast of their learning, when a savage can read a language that would prove too much for the wisest of them all!", "미개인이 그들 가운데 가장 지혜로운 자도 당해 내지 못할 언어를 읽어 내는데, 기독교를 믿는 백인이 무슨 권리로 제 학식을 자랑하겠소!"], ["What say _you_, lad; of what people was the knave?\"", "자네는 뭐라 하겠나, 이 사람아. 그 망나니가 어느 종족이었나?\""]], [["Uncas raised his eyes to the face of the scout, and answered, in his soft voice,--", "〈웅카스〉는 척후의 얼굴로 눈을 들어, 부드러운 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["\"Oneida.\"", "\"〈오나이다〉.\""]], [["\"Oneida, again!", "\"또 〈오나이다〉라!"], ["when one Indian makes a declaration it is commonly true; but when he is supported by his people, set it down as gospel!\"", "인디언 하나가 단언하면 대개 참인데, 그가 제 동족의 뒷받침을 받으면, 복음으로 받아들여도 좋소!\""]], [["\"The poor fellow has mistaken us for French,\"", "\"그 가엾은 자가 우리를 프랑스 사람으로 잘못 본 거요.\""], ["said Heyward; \"or he would not have attempted the life of a friend.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"아니라면 벗의 목숨을 노렸을 리 없소.\""]], [["\"He mistake a Mohican in his paint for a Huron!", "\"단장을 한 〈모히칸〉을 〈휴런〉으로 잘못 봤다고!"], ["You would be as likely to mistake the white-coated grenadiers of Montcalm for the scarlet jackets of the 'Royal Americans',\"", "그건 〈몽칼름〉의 흰 외투 입은 척탄병을 〈로열 아메리칸〉의 진홍색 상의로 잘못 보는 거나 진배없소.\" 척후가 대꾸했다."], ["returned the scout.", "\"아니, 아니오."], ["\"No, no, the sarpent knew his errand; nor was there any great mistake in the matter, for there is but little love atween a Delaware and a Mingo, let their tribes go out to fight for whom they may, in a white quarrel.", "그 뱀은 제가 무슨 볼일로 왔는지 알고 있었소. 또 그 일에 큰 착오도 없었던 게, 백인의 다툼에서 제 부족이 누구를 위해 싸우러 나가든, 〈델라웨어〉와 〈밍고〉 사이엔 정이라곤 거의 없으니까."], ["For that matter, though the Oneidas do serve his sacred majesty, who is my own sovereign lord and master, I should not have deliberated long about letting off 'Killdeer' at the imp myself, had luck thrown him in my way.\"", "그 점으로 말하자면, 〈오나이다〉가 그 거룩하신 폐하, 곧 내 군주요 주인이신 분을 섬긴다 한들, 운이 그 악귀를 내 앞에 던져 줬다면, 나 자신도 '〈킬디어〉'를 그자에게 쏘아 대는 걸 그리 오래 망설이지 않았을 거요.\""]], [["\"That would have been an abuse of our treaties, and unworthy of your character.\"", "\"그건 우리 조약을 저버리는 짓이고, 당신 인품에 어울리지 않는 일이오.\""]], [["\"When a man consorts much with a people,\"", "\"사람이 어떤 족속과 오래 어울리다 보면,\""], ["continued Hawkeye, \"if they are honest and he no knave, love will grow up atwixt them.", "〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다. \"그들이 정직하고 그 자신이 악당이 아닐 경우엔, 그들 사이에 정이 자라나는 법이오."], ["It is true that white cunning has managed to throw the tribes into great confusion as respects friends and enemies; so that the Hurons and the Oneidas, who speak the same tongue, or what may be called the same, take each other's scalps, and the Delawares are divided among themselves; a few hanging about their great council-fire on their own river, and fighting on the same side with the Mingos, while the greater part are in the Canadas, out of natural enmity to the Maquas--thus throwing everything into disorder, and destroying all the harmony of warfare.", "백인의 교활함이 벗과 적을 두고 부족들을 큰 혼란에 빠뜨려 놓은 건 사실이오. 그래서 같은 말을, 같다고 할 만한 말을 쓰는 〈휴런〉과 〈오나이다〉가 서로 머리가죽을 벗기고, 〈델라웨어〉는 저희끼리 갈라져, 더러는 제 강가의 큰 평의회 불 둘레에 머물며 〈밍고〉와 한편이 되어 싸우고, 더 많은 수는 〈매콰〉에 대한 타고난 원한으로 〈캐나다〉에 가 있으니 — 이렇게 모든 걸 어지럽혀, 전쟁의 온갖 조화를 깨뜨리는 거요."], ["Yet a red natur' is not likely to alter with every shift of policy; so that the love atwixt a Mohican and a Mingo is much like the regard between a white man and a sarpent.\"", "그래도 붉은 본성이 정략이 바뀔 때마다 변하지는 않을 터, 〈모히칸〉과 〈밍고〉 사이의 정이란 백인과 뱀 사이의 정 같은 것이오.\""]], [["\"I regret to hear it; for I had believed those natives who dwelt within our boundaries had found us too just and liberal, not to identify themselves fully with our quarrels.\"", "\"그런 말을 들으니 안타깝소. 나는 우리 경계 안에 사는 그 원주민들이, 우리가 너무도 공정하고 너그럽다 여겨 우리의 다툼에 온전히 한 몸이 되었으리라 믿었기에 하는 말이오.\""]], [["\"Why, I believe it is natur'", "\"글쎄, 남의 다툼보다 제 다툼을 더 치는 게 본성 아니겠소."], ["to give a preference to one's own quarrels before those of strangers.", "자, 나로 말하자면, 나는 정의를 사랑하오."], ["Now, for myself, I do love justice; and therefore I will not say I hate a Mingo, for that may be unsuitable to my color and my religion, though I will just repeat, it may have been owing to the night that 'Killdeer'", "그러니 〈밍고〉를 미워한다고는 말하지 않겠소. 그건 내 피부색과 내 신앙에 어울리지 않을지 모르니까."], ["had no hand in the death of this skulking Oneida.\"", "다만 한 번 더 되풀이하건대, '〈킬디어〉'가 이 숨어든 〈오나이다〉의 죽음에 손대지 않은 건 밤 덕분이었는지도 모르오.\""]], [["Then, as if satisfied with the force of his own reasons, whatever might be their effect on the opinions of the other disputant, the honest but implacable woodsman turned from the fire, content to let the controversy slumber.", "그러고는, 제 논리가 상대편 논객의 견해에 어떤 영향을 미쳤든 간에, 제 논거의 힘에 흡족했다는 듯, 그 정직하나 누그러질 줄 모르는 숲의 사내는 논쟁을 잠재워 두기로 하고 불에서 돌아앉았다."], ["Heyward withdrew to the rampart, too uneasy and too little accustomed to the warfare of the woods to remain at ease under the possibility of such insidious attacks.", "〈헤이워드〉는 성벽으로 물러났으니, 너무 마음이 불편하고 숲의 전쟁에 너무 서툴러, 그런 음험한 공격이 있을 수 있다는 상황에서 도무지 마음을 놓을 수 없었다."], ["Not so, however, with the scout and the Mohicans.", "그러나 척후와 〈모히칸〉들은 그렇지 않았다."], ["Those acute and long practised senses, whose powers so often exceed the limits of all ordinary credulity, after having detected the danger, had enabled them to ascertain its magnitude and duration.", "그 예리하고 오래 단련된 감각은, 그 힘이 흔히 보통의 믿음으로는 가늠할 수 없는 한계를 넘어서는 것이라, 위험을 알아챈 뒤에는 그 규모와 지속을 헤아릴 수 있게 해 주었다."], ["Not one of the three appeared in the least to doubt their perfect security, as was indicated by the preparations that were soon made to sit in council over their future proceedings.", "셋 중 누구도 제 완벽한 안전을 조금도 의심하는 기색이 없었으니, 곧 앞으로의 행보를 두고 평의하려 갖춰진 채비가 그것을 일러 주었다."]], [["The confusion of nations, and even of tribes, to which Hawkeye alluded, existed at that period in the fullest force.", "〈호크아이〉가 넌지시 말한 그 민족들, 아니 부족들의 혼란은, 그 시절 가장 극심한 정도로 존재하고 있었다."], ["The great tie of language, and, of course, of a common origin, was severed in many places; and it was one of its consequences, that the Delaware and the Mingo (as the people of the Six Nations were called) were found fighting in the same ranks, while the latter sought the scalp of the Huron, though believed to be the root of his own stock.", "언어라는, 따라서 공통의 기원이라는 그 위대한 끈이 곳곳에서 끊겼고, 그 결과 가운데 하나로, 〈델라웨어〉와 〈밍고〉(〈6부족 연맹〉의 사람들을 그렇게 불렀다)가 같은 대열에서 싸우는 한편, 〈밍고〉는 제 혈통의 뿌리로 여겨지는 〈휴런〉의 머리가죽을 노리는 광경이 벌어졌다."], ["The Delawares were even divided among themselves.", "〈델라웨어〉마저 저희끼리 갈라져 있었다."], ["Though love for the soil which had belonged to his ancestors kept the Sagamore of the Mohicans with a small band of followers who were serving at Edward, under the banners of the English king, by far the largest portion of his nation were known to be in the field as allies of Montcalm.", "조상에게 속했던 그 땅에 대한 사랑이 〈모히칸〉의 〈사가모어〉를, 영국 왕의 깃발 아래 〈에드워드〉에서 복무하는 한 줌의 추종자들과 함께 붙들어 두었으나, 그 민족의 단연 가장 큰 무리는 〈몽칼름〉의 동맹군으로서 싸움터에 나가 있는 것으로 알려져 있었다."], ["The reader probably knows, if enough has not already been gleaned from this narrative, that the Delaware, or Lenape, claimed to be the progenitors of that numerous people, who once were masters of most of the Eastern and Northern States of America, of whom the community of the Mohicans was an ancient and highly honored member.", "독자는 아마 알고 있을 터이나 — 이 이야기에서 이미 충분히 헤아리지 못했다면 — 〈델라웨어〉, 곧 〈레나페〉는, 한때 아메리카의 동부와 북부 주(州) 대부분의 주인이었던 그 수많은 민족의 조상임을 자처했으며, 〈모히칸〉의 공동체는 그 오래되고 더없이 존경받는 일원이었다."]], [["It was, of course, with a perfect understanding of the minute and intricate interest which had armed friend against friend, and brought natural enemies to combat by each other's side, that the scout and his companions now disposed themselves to deliberate on the measures that were to govern their future movements, amid so many jarring and savage races of men.", "벗을 벗과 맞서 무장하게 하고, 타고난 적들을 나란히 곁에서 싸우게 만든 그 미세하고 뒤얽힌 이해관계를 더없이 환히 꿰뚫은 채, 척후와 그 동료들은 이제 이토록 많은 어긋나고 미개한 종족들 가운데서 앞으로의 거동을 다스릴 방책을 의논하고자 자리를 잡았다."], ["Duncan knew enough of Indian customs to understand the reason that the fire was replenished, and why the warriors, not excepting Hawkeye, took their seats within the curl of its smoke with so much gravity and decorum.", "〈덩컨〉은 인디언 풍습을 충분히 알고 있어, 어찌하여 불을 다시 돋우는지, 그리고 어찌하여 〈호크아이〉마저 포함한 전사들이 그토록 엄숙하고 예의 바르게 그 연기의 굽이 안에 자리를 잡는지를 헤아렸다."], ["Placing himself at an angle of the works, where he might be a spectator of the scene within, while he kept a watchful eye against any danger from without, he awaited the result with as much patience as he could summon.", "안에서 벌어지는 장면을 지켜보는 동시에 바깥으로부터의 어떤 위험에도 경계의 눈길을 늦추지 않을 수 있는 진지의 한 모퉁이에 자리를 잡고서, 그는 그러모을 수 있는 한껏의 인내로 결과를 기다렸다."]], [["After a short and impressive pause, Chingachgook lighted a pipe whose bowl was curiously carved in one of the soft stones of the country, and whose stem was a tube of wood, and commenced smoking.", "짧고도 인상 깊은 멈춤 끝에, 〈칭가치국〉은 이 고장의 무른 돌 하나에 정교히 새긴 대통에, 나무관으로 된 설대를 단 담뱃대에 불을 붙여 피우기 시작했다."], ["When he had inhaled enough of the fragrance of the soothing weed, he passed the instrument into the hands of the scout.", "그 달래는 풀의 향을 넉넉히 들이마신 뒤, 그는 그 도구를 척후의 손에 건넸다."], ["In this manner the pipe had made its rounds three several times, amid the most profound silence, before either of the party opened his lips.", "이렇게 담뱃대는 더없이 깊은 침묵 속에 세 차례를 따로따로 돌고 나서야, 비로소 일행 중 누군가가 입을 열었다."], ["Then the Sagamore, as the oldest and highest in rank, in a few calm and dignified words, proposed the subject for deliberation.", "이윽고 〈사가모어〉가, 가장 연장이요 지위가 가장 높은 자로서, 몇 마디 차분하고 위엄 있는 말로 평의할 안건을 내놓았다."], ["He was answered by the scout; and Chingachgook rejoined, when the other objected to his opinions.", "척후가 그에게 대답했고, 상대가 자신의 견해에 이의를 제기하자 〈칭가치국〉이 다시 응했다."], ["But the youthful Uncas continued a silent and respectful listener, until Hawkeye, in complaisance, demanded his opinion.", "그러나 젊은 〈웅카스〉는, 〈호크아이〉가 정중하게 그 의견을 물을 때까지, 말 없고 공손한 경청자로 머물렀다."], ["Heyward gathered from the manners of the different speakers, that the father and son espoused one side of a disputed question, while the white man maintained the other.", "〈헤이워드〉는 저마다 말하는 자들의 태도에서, 아비와 아들이 다투는 문제의 한쪽 편을 들고, 백인이 다른 쪽을 견지하고 있음을 헤아렸다."], ["The contest gradually grew warmer, until it was quite evident the feelings of the speakers began to be somewhat enlisted in the debate.", "다툼은 차츰 뜨거워져, 마침내 말하는 자들의 감정이 그 논의에 다소 실리기 시작한 것이 자못 분명해졌다."]], [["Notwithstanding the increasing warmth of the amicable contest, the most decorous Christian assembly, not even excepting those in which its reverend ministers are collected, might have learned a wholesome lesson of moderation from the forbearance and courtesy of the disputants.", "이 우호적인 다툼의 열기가 점점 더해 갔음에도, 더없이 예법을 갖춘 기독교도의 회합이, 그 존경할 만한 목사들이 모인 회합조차도, 이 논객들의 인내와 정중함에서 절제의 유익한 교훈을 배울 수 있었으리라."], ["The words of Uncas were received with the same deep attention as those which fell from the maturer wisdom of his father; and so far from manifesting any impatience, neither spoke in reply, until a few moments of silent meditation were, seemingly, bestowed in deliberating on what had already been said.", "〈웅카스〉의 말은 그 아비의 무르익은 지혜에서 나온 말과 똑같이 깊은 주의로 받아들여졌고, 어느 쪽도 조바심을 내기는커녕, 이미 한 말을 두고 몇 순간의 말 없는 사색을 곰곰이 들이는 듯한 시간을 보낸 뒤에야 비로소 대답했다."]], [["The language of the Mohicans was accompanied by gestures so direct and natural, that Heyward had but little difficulty in following the thread of their argument.", "〈모히칸〉의 말은 너무도 직접적이고 자연스러운 몸짓이 곁들여져, 〈헤이워드〉는 그 논의의 갈피를 좇는 데 큰 어려움이 없었다."], ["On the other hand, the scout was obscure; because, from the lingering pride of color, he rather affected the cold and artificial manner which characterizes all classes of Anglo-Americans, when unexcited.", "반면에 척후는 알아듣기 어려웠으니, 피부색에 대한 미련 어린 자긍심에서, 격앙되지 않았을 때의 영국계 아메리카인 모든 계층의 특징인 그 차고 꾸민 듯한 태도를 짐짓 부린 까닭이었다."], ["By the frequency with which the Indians described the marks of a forest trail, it was evident they urged a pursuit by land, while the repeated sweep of Hawkeye's arm towards the Horican denoted that he was for a passage across its waters.", "인디언들이 숲속 자취의 표를 묘사하는 빈도로 보아, 그들이 육로로의 추적을 주장하고 있음이 분명했고, 〈호리컨〉 쪽으로 거듭 내젓는 〈호크아이〉의 팔은 그가 그 물길을 가로지르는 편을 지지함을 드러냈다."]], [["The latter was, to every appearance, fast losing ground, and the point was about to be decided against him, when he arose to his feet, and shaking off his apathy, he suddenly assumed the manner of an Indian, and adopted all the arts of native eloquence.", "후자는, 누가 보아도 빠르게 처지고 있어, 자칫 그에게 불리하게 결판이 날 참이었으나, 그가 벌떡 일어서더니 제 무심함을 떨치고 불현듯 인디언의 태도를 취하며, 원주민 웅변의 온갖 기교를 다 펼쳐 보였다."], ["Elevating an arm, he pointed out the track of the sun, repeating the gesture for every day that was necessary to accomplish their object.", "한쪽 팔을 들어, 그는 해의 자취를 가리키며, 그들의 목적을 이루는 데 필요한 날수만큼 그 몸짓을 거듭했다."], ["Then he delineated a long and painful path, amid rocks and water-courses.", "그런 다음 바위와 물길 사이로 난 길고도 고된 길을 그려 보였다."], ["The age and weakness of the slumbering and unconscious Munro were indicated by signs too palpable to be mistaken.", "잠들어 의식 없는 〈먼로〉의 노쇠함과 약함은, 잘못 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 또렷한 손짓으로 일러졌다."], ["Duncan perceived that even his own powers were spoken lightly of, as the scout extended his palm, and mentioned him by appellation of the \"Open Hand,\"--a name his liberality had purchased of all the friendly tribes.", "〈덩컨〉은 자신의 능력마저 가벼이 일컬어짐을 알아챘으니, 척후가 손바닥을 펼치며 그를 \"펼친 손\"이라는 칭호로 불렀던 것이다 — 그의 너그러움이 모든 우호적인 부족에게서 사들인 이름이었다."], ["Then came a representation of the light and graceful movements of a canoe, set in forcible contrast to the tottering steps of one enfeebled and tired.", "이어, 카누의 가볍고 우아한 움직임을, 쇠약하고 지친 자의 비틀거리는 걸음과 강한 대조로 놓아 그려 보였다."], ["He concluded by pointing to the scalp of the Oneida, and apparently urging the necessity of their departing speedily, and in a manner that should leave no trail.", "그는 〈오나이다〉의 머리가죽을 가리키며, 자취를 남기지 않을 방식으로 속히 떠나야 할 필요를 역설하는 듯하더니 말을 맺었다."]], [["The Mohicans listened gravely, and with countenances that reflected the sentiments of the speaker.", "〈모히칸〉들은 엄숙하게, 그리고 말하는 자의 심정을 비추는 낯빛으로 귀 기울였다."], ["Conviction gradually wrought its influence, and towards the close of Hawkeye's speech, his sentences were accompanied by the customary exclamation of commendation.", "차츰 확신이 그 영향을 미쳤고, 〈호크아이〉의 연설이 끝나갈 무렵엔 그의 문장마다 으레 따르는 찬동의 감탄사가 곁들여졌다."], ["In short, Uncas and his father became converts to his way of thinking, abandoning their own previously expressed opinions with a liberality and candor that, had they been the representatives of some great and civilized people, would have infallibly worked their political ruin, by destroying, forever, their reputation for consistency.", "요컨대, 〈웅카스〉와 그 아비는 그의 사고방식에 귀의하여, 앞서 밝혔던 제 견해를 너그러움과 솔직함으로 버렸으니, 만일 그들이 어느 위대하고 문명한 민족의 대표였더라면, 일관성에 대한 명성을 영영 무너뜨려 그 정치적 파멸을 어김없이 불러왔을 그런 너그러움과 솔직함이었다."]], [["The instant the matter in discussion was decided, the debate, and everything connected with it, except the results, appeared to be forgotten.", "논의되던 문제가 결판나는 순간, 그 논쟁도, 그것에 얽힌 모든 것도, 결과만 빼고는 잊힌 듯했다."], ["Hawkeye, without looking round to read his triumph in applauding eyes, very composedly stretched his tall frame before the dying embers, and closed his own organs in sleep.", "〈호크아이〉는 칭찬하는 눈길 속에서 제 승리를 읽으려 둘러보지도 않은 채, 아주 침착하게 꺼져 가는 잉걸불 앞에 제 큰 키를 뻗고, 두 눈을 감아 잠을 청했다."]], [["Left now in a measure to themselves, the Mohicans, whose time had been so much devoted to the interests of others, seized the moment to devote some attention to themselves.", "이제 어느 정도 저희들끼리 남겨지자, 그토록 많은 시간을 남들의 이익에 바쳤던 〈모히칸〉들은, 그 짬을 타 자신들에게 얼마간 마음을 쓰기로 했다."], ["Casting off, at once, the grave and austere demeanor of an Indian chief, Chingachgook commenced speaking to his son in the soft and playful tones of affection.", "인디언 추장의 그 엄숙하고 준엄한 태도를 단번에 벗어 던지고, 〈칭가치국〉은 부드럽고 장난스러운 애정의 어조로 아들에게 말을 건네기 시작했다."], ["Uncas gladly met the familiar air of his father; and before the hard breathing of the scout announced that he slept, a complete change was effected in the manner of his two associates.", "〈웅카스〉는 아비의 그 다정한 기색을 기껍게 맞았다. 척후의 거친 숨소리가 그가 잠들었음을 알리기도 전에, 그 두 동무의 태도에는 완연한 변화가 일었다."]], [["It is impossible to describe the music of their language, while thus engaged in laughter and endearments, in such a way as to render it intelligible to those whose ears have never listened to its melody.", "이리하여 웃음과 다정한 말에 빠져든 그들의 언어가 지닌 가락을, 그 선율에 한 번도 귀 기울여 본 적 없는 이들에게 알아듣도록 옮긴다는 것은 도무지 그려 낼 길이 없다."], ["The compass of their voices, particularly that of the youth, was wonderful,--extending from the deepest bass to tones that were even feminine in softness.", "그들 목소리의 음역은, 특히 그 젊은이의 것은 놀라워, 가장 깊은 저음에서 여인의 것이라 할 만큼 부드러운 음조에까지 뻗쳤다."], ["The eyes of the father followed the plastic and ingenious movements of the son with open delight, and he never failed to smile in reply to the other's contagious, but low laughter.", "아비의 두 눈은 아들의 유연하고 재간 있는 움직임을 숨김없는 기쁨으로 좇았고, 상대의 옮아 오는, 그러나 낮은 웃음에 그는 어김없이 미소로 화답했다."], ["While under the influence of these gentle and natural feelings, no trace of ferocity was to be seen in the softened features of the Sagamore.", "이 온화하고 자연스러운 감정의 영향 아래 있는 동안, 누그러진 〈사가모어〉의 이목구비에는 흉포함의 자취라곤 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["His figured panoply of death looked more like a disguise assumed in mockery, than a fierce annunciation of a desire to carry destruction in his footsteps.", "그가 몸에 두른 그 무늬 새긴 죽음의 갑주는, 제 발자국마다 파멸을 실어 나르겠다는 사나운 선포라기보다는, 차라리 조롱으로 걸친 변장처럼 보였다."]], [["After an hour passed in the indulgence of their better feelings, Chingachgook abruptly announced his desire to sleep, by wrapping his head in his blanket, and stretching his form on the naked earth.", "한 시간을 이 한결 나은 감정에 빠져 보낸 뒤, 〈칭가치국〉은 담요로 머리를 감싸고 맨땅에 몸을 뻗으며, 잠들고 싶다는 뜻을 불쑥 알렸다."], ["The merriment of Uncas instantly ceased; and carefully raking the coals in such a manner that they should impart their warmth to his father's feet, the youth sought his own pillow among the ruins of the place.", "〈웅카스〉의 즐거움은 그 즉시 멎었고, 잉걸불을 그 온기가 아비의 발에 닿도록 정성껏 그러모은 뒤, 젊은이는 그곳의 폐허 사이에서 제가 벨 베개를 찾았다."]], [["Imbibing renewed confidence from the security of these experienced foresters, Heyward soon imitated their example; and long before the night had turned, they who lay in the bosom of the ruined work, seemed to slumber as heavily as the unconscious multitude whose bones were already beginning to bleach on the surrounding plain.", "이 노련한 삼림의 사내들이 안전하다는 데서 새로운 자신을 들이마신 〈헤이워드〉도, 곧 그들을 본받았다. 그리하여 밤이 깊어 가기 한참 전에, 허물어진 진지의 품에 누운 자들은, 둘레의 평원 위에서 벌써 그 뼈가 하얗게 바래기 시작한 그 의식 없는 무리만큼이나 깊이 잠든 듯했다."]], [["\"Land of Albania!", "앨버니아의 땅이여!"], ["let me bend mine eyes On thee, thou rugged nurse of savage men!\"", "그대, 야만의 사내들을 길러 낸 거친 유모여, 그대 위에 내 두 눈을 머물게 하라!"]], [["_Childe Harold._", "차일드 해럴드"]], [["The heavens were still studded with stars, when Hawkeye came to arouse the sleepers.", "〈호크아이〉가 잠든 이들을 깨우러 왔을 때, 하늘은 여전히 별들로 촘촘했다."], ["Casting aside their cloaks Munro and Heyward were on their feet while the woodsman was still making his low calls, at the entrance of the rude shelter where they had passed the night.", "〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉는 망토를 걷어 내며 자리에서 일어섰으니, 그들이 밤을 지낸 거친 은신처 입구에서 숲의 사내가 아직도 나직한 부름을 보내고 있을 때였다."], ["When they issued from beneath its concealment, they found the scout awaiting their appearance nigh by, and the only salutation between them was the significant gesture for silence, made by their sagacious leader.", "가려진 그늘 아래에서 빠져나오자, 그들은 척후가 바로 곁에서 모습을 드러내기를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았고, 그들 사이에 오간 유일한 인사라곤 그 슬기로운 인도자가 조용히 하라며 보낸 의미심장한 손짓뿐이었다."]], [["\"Think over your prayers,\"", "\"기도문을 마음속으로 새겨 두시오.\""], ["he whispered, as they approached him; \"for He to whom you make them knows all tongues; that of the heart as well as those of the mouth.", "그들이 다가가자 그가 속삭였다. \"그대들이 기도를 올리는 그분은 모든 말을 아시니, 입의 말은 물론 마음의 말까지 다 아시오."], ["But speak not a syllable; it is rare for a white voice to pitch itself properly in the woods, as we have seen by the example of that miserable devil, the singer.", "허나 한 마디도 소리 내지 마시오. 백인의 목소리가 숲속에서 제대로 가락을 잡는 일은 드무니, 우리는 저 가련한 가엾은 자, 그 노래꾼을 본보기 삼아 이미 겪지 않았소."], ["Come,\"", "자,\" 그가 방어 시설의 한 막벽 쪽으로 돌아서며 말을 이었다."], ["he continued, turning towards a curtain of the works; \"let us get into the ditch on this side, and be regardful to step on the stones and fragments of wood as you go.\"", "\"이쪽으로 해서 해자 안으로 내려갑시다. 가면서 돌이나 나무 조각만 밟도록 조심하시오.\""]], [["His companions complied, though to two of them the reasons of this extraordinary precaution were yet a mystery.", "그의 동행들은 따랐으나, 그중 두 사람에게는 이 유별난 조심성의 까닭이 아직 수수께끼였다."], ["When they were in the low cavity that surrounded the earthen fort on three sides, they found the passage nearly choked by the ruins.", "흙으로 쌓은 요새를 세 면으로 에워싼 그 낮은 구덩이 안에 들어서자, 그들은 통로가 잔해로 거의 막혀 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["With care and patience, however, they succeeded in clambering after the scout, until they reached the sandy shore of the Horican.", "그러나 신중함과 인내로 척후의 뒤를 기어오르는 데 성공하여, 마침내 〈호리컨〉의 모래 기슭에 다다랐다."]], [["\"That's a trail that nothing but a nose can follow,\"", "\"저건 코가 아니고서는 따라올 수 없는 자취요.\" 만족한 척후가 그들이 지나온 험한 길을 돌아보며 말했다."], ["said the satisfied scout, looking back along their difficult way; \"grass is a treacherous carpet for a flying party to tread on, but wood and stone take no print from a moccasin.", "\"풀밭은 달아나는 무리가 밟기에는 믿지 못할 양탄자지만, 나무와 돌은 모카신 자국을 남기지 않으니까. 그대들이 만약 쇠 박은 군화를 신었더라면, 정말이지 두려워할 게 있었을 거요."], ["Had you worn your armed boots, there might, indeed, have been something to fear; but with the deer-skin suitably prepared, a man may trust himself, generally, on rocks with safety.", "허나 사슴 가죽을 알맞게 손질해 신으면, 사람은 대체로 바위 위에서 제 몸을 안심하고 맡길 수 있소. 〈웅카스〉, 카누를 뭍에 좀 더 가까이 밀어라."], ["Shove in the canoe nigher to the land, Uncas; this sand will take a stamp as easily as the butter of the Jarmans on the Mohawk.", "이 모래는 〈모호크〉강의 〈독일〉인들 버터만큼이나 쉽게 자국이 박히니까. 살살, 얘야, 살살."], ["Softly, lad, softly; it must not touch the beach, or the knaves will know by what road we have left the place.\"", "카누가 모래톱에 닿아선 안 돼. 안 그러면 그 악당들이 우리가 어느 길로 떠났는지 알아챌 테니까.\""]], [["The young man observed the precaution; and the scout, laying a board from the ruins to the canoe, made a sign for the two officers to enter.", "젊은이는 그 주의를 지켰다. 그러자 척후는 잔해에서 널빤지 하나를 카누에 걸쳐 놓고, 두 장교에게 들어오라는 손짓을 보냈다."], ["When this was done, everything was studiously restored to its former disorder; and then Hawkeye succeeded in reaching his little birchen vessel, without leaving behind him any of those marks which he appeared so much to dread.", "그 일이 끝나자, 모든 것이 본래의 어수선함 그대로 세심하게 되돌려졌고, 그런 다음 〈호크아이〉는 자신이 그토록 꺼리던 자국을 하나도 남기지 않은 채 제 작은 자작나무 배에 다다르는 데 성공했다."], ["Heyward was silent, until the Indians had cautiously paddled the canoe some distance from the fort, and within the broad and dark shadow that fell from the eastern mountain on the glassy surface of the lake; then he demanded,--", "인디언들이 카누를 조심스레 저어 요새에서 어느 정도 멀어져, 동쪽 산에서 호수의 거울 같은 수면 위로 드리운 넓고 어두운 그림자 안에 들 때까지, 〈헤이워드〉는 입을 다물고 있었다. 그러다 이윽고 그가 물었다."]], [["\"What need have we for this stolen and hurried departure?\"", "\"이렇게 몰래, 서둘러 떠날 필요가 어디 있소?\""]], [["\"If the blood of an Oneida could stain such a sheet of pure water as this we float on,\"", "\"우리가 떠 있는 이런 맑디맑은 수면을 〈오나이다〉 사람의 피로 물들일 수 있다면,\""], ["returned the scout, \"your two eyes would answer your own question.", "척후가 대답했다. \"그대의 두 눈이 그대 물음에 스스로 답해 줄 거요."], ["Have you forgotten the skulking rept_y_le that Uncas slew?\"", "〈웅카스〉가 죽인 그 살금살금 기던 파충류를 잊었소?\""]], [["\"By no means.", "\"천만에."], ["But he was said to be alone, and dead men give no cause for fear.\"", "허나 그자는 혼자였다고 했고, 죽은 자는 두려움의 빌미가 되지 않잖소.\""]], [["\"Ay, he was alone in his deviltry!", "\"맞소, 그자는 제 악행에 있어선 혼자였지!"], ["but an Indian whose tribe counts so many warriors, need seldom fear his blood will run, without the death-shriek coming speedily from some of his enemies.\"", "허나 그 부족에 전사가 그토록 많은 인디언이라면, 제 피가 흐른다 해서 그 적들 가운데 누구의 죽음의 비명이 곧 뒤따르지 않을까 두려워할 일은 좀처럼 없는 법이오.\""]], [["\"But our presence--the authority of Colonel Munro--would prove a sufficient protection against the anger of our allies, especially in a case where a wretch so well merited his fate.", "\"하지만 우리가 그 자리에 있었다는 사실은, 〈먼로〉 대령의 권위는, 우리 동맹의 분노에 맞설 충분한 보호가 되어 줄 거요. 더구나 그토록 마땅히 제 운명을 받을 만한 비열한 자의 경우라면 말이오."], ["I trust in Heaven you have not deviated a single foot from the direct line of our course, with so slight a reason!\"", "부디 하늘에 비건대, 이토록 사소한 이유로 우리 노정의 곧은 길에서 단 한 발짝도 벗어나진 않았겠지요!\""]], [["\"Do you think the bullet of that varlet's rifle would have turned aside, though his majesty the king had stood in its path?\"", "\"그 악당의 소총알이, 설령 폐하이신 국왕께서 그 길목에 서 계셨던들 비켜 갔으리라 생각하시오?\""], ["returned the stubborn scout.", "고집스러운 척후가 되받았다."], ["\"Why did not the grand Frencher, he who is captain-general of the Canadas, bury the tomahawks of the Hurons, if a word from a white can work so strongly on the natur' of an Indian?\"", "\"백인의 말 한마디가 인디언의 본성에 그토록 강하게 작용할 수 있다면, 어찌하여 저 거룩한 프랑스인, 〈캐나다〉의 총사령관인 그자는 〈휴런〉족의 토마호크를 묻어 두지 않았겠소?\""]], [["The reply of Heyward was interrupted by a groan from Munro; but after he had paused a moment, in deference to the sorrow of his aged friend, he resumed the subject.", "〈헤이워드〉의 대답은 〈먼로〉의 신음 소리에 끊겼으나, 늙은 벗의 슬픔을 헤아려 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤, 그는 다시 그 화제를 이어 갔다."]], [["\"The Marquis of Montcalm can only settle that error with his God,\"", "\"〈몽칼름〉 후작은 그 잘못을 오직 자신의 신과 더불어 매듭지을 수 있을 뿐이오.\""], ["said the young man solemnly.", "젊은이가 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["\"Ay, ay; now there is reason in your words, for they are bottomed on religion and honesty.", "\"옳소, 옳소. 이제야 그대 말에 이치가 있구려."], ["There is a vast difference between throwing a regiment of white coats atwixt the tribes and the prisoners, and coaxing an angry savage to forget he carries a knife and a rifle, with words that must begin with calling him your son.", "그 말은 신앙과 정직함에 뿌리를 두고 있으니 말이오. 흰 군복을 입은 한 연대를 부족들과 포로들 사이에 던져 넣는 것과, 성난 미개인더러 칼과 소총을 지녔다는 사실을 잊으라고, 그것도 그를 그대의 아들이라 부르는 말로 시작해야 하는 그런 말로 구슬리는 것 사이엔 엄청난 차이가 있소."], ["No, no,\"", "아니, 아니지.\" 척후가 이제 빠르게 멀어져 가는 〈윌리엄 헨리〉의 희미한 기슭을 돌아보며, 제 나름의 소리 없되 진심 어린 방식으로 웃으며 말을 이었다."], ["continued the scout, looking back at the dim shore of William Henry, which was now fast receding, and laughing in his own silent but heartfelt manner; \"I have put a trail of water atween us; and unless the imps can make friends with the fishes, and hear who has paddled across their basin, this fine morning, we shall throw the length of the Horican behind us, before they have made up their minds which path to take.\"", "\"내가 우리 사이에 물의 자취를 깔아 놓았소. 그러니 그 악마 같은 놈들이 물고기와 친구가 되어, 오늘 이 멋진 아침에 누가 그 못을 가로질러 노 저어 갔는지 들을 수 있는 게 아닌 한, 우리는 그놈들이 어느 길을 택할지 마음을 정하기도 전에 〈호리컨〉의 길이만큼을 등 뒤에 따돌리게 될 거요.\""]], [["\"With foes in front, and foes in our rear, our journey is like to be one of danger.\"", "\"앞에도 적, 뒤에도 적이니, 우리 여정은 위험으로 가득할 듯하구려.\""]], [["\"Danger!\" repeated Hawkeye, calmly; \"no, not absolutely of danger; for, with vigilant ears and quick eyes, we can manage to keep a few hours ahead of the knaves; or, if we must try the rifle, there are three of us who understand its gifts as well as any you can name on the borders. No, not of danger; but that we shall have what you may call a brisk push of it is probable; and it may happen, a brush, a skrimmage, or some such divarsion, but always where covers are good, and ammunition abundant.\"", "\"위험이라!\" 〈호크아이〉가 차분히 되뇌었다. \"아니오, 딱히 위험은 아니오. 귀를 곤두세우고 눈을 재빨리 놀리면, 우리는 그 악당들보다 몇 시간쯤 앞서 갈 수 있소. 혹여 소총을 써야 한다면, 우리 셋은 변경에서 그대가 댈 수 있는 누구 못지않게 그 솜씨를 아는 사람들이고. 아니, 위험은 아니오. 다만 그대가 이를테면 한바탕 부산한 몰아치기라 부를 만한 일을 겪게 되긴 할 거요. 그리고 어쩌면 한 차례 충돌이나 작은 접전, 뭐 그런 여흥 같은 게 벌어질 수도 있겠지. 허나 늘 엄폐할 데가 좋고 탄약이 넉넉한 곳에서 말이오.\""]], [["It is possible that Heyward's estimate of danger differed in some degree from that of the scout, for, instead of replying, he now sat in silence, while the canoe glided over several miles of water.", "〈헤이워드〉가 위험을 헤아리는 잣대는 척후의 그것과 어느 정도 달랐던 모양이니, 그는 대답하는 대신 이제 말없이 앉아 있었고, 그동안 카누는 몇 마일의 물 위를 미끄러져 갔다."], ["Just as the day dawned, they entered the narrows of the lake,[23] and stole swiftly and cautiously among their numberless little islands.", "막 동이 트자, 그들은 호수의 좁은 길목으로 들어서, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 작은 섬들 사이를 재빠르고 조심스럽게 빠져나갔다."], ["It was by this road that Montcalm had retired with his army; and the adventurers knew not but he had left some of his Indians in ambush, to protect the rear of his forces, and collect the stragglers.", "〈몽칼름〉이 군대를 이끌고 물러간 길도 바로 이 길이었다. 모험가들은 그가 제 군대의 후미를 지키고 낙오자들을 거두려 인디언 몇을 매복시켜 두었을지도 모른다고 여겼다."], ["They, therefore, approached the passage with the customary silence of their guarded habits.", "그리하여 그들은 평소의 경계하는 습성다운 침묵 속에 그 길목으로 다가갔다."]], [["Chingachgook laid aside his paddle; while Uncas and the scout urged the light vessel through crooked and intricate channels, where every foot that they advanced exposed them to the danger of some sudden rising on their progress.", "〈칭가치국〉은 노를 내려놓았고, 그동안 〈웅카스〉와 척후는 굽이지고 뒤얽힌 물길로 가벼운 배를 몰았으니, 그곳에서는 한 발짝 나아갈 때마다 진로 위로 무언가가 불쑥 솟구칠 위험에 그들을 내맡기게 되었다."], ["The eyes of the Sagamore moved warily from islet to islet, and copse to copse, as the canoe proceeded; and when a clearer sheet of water permitted, his keen vision was bent along the bald rocks and impending forests, that frowned upon the narrow strait.", "카누가 나아가는 동안 〈사가모어〉의 두 눈은 섬에서 섬으로, 덤불에서 덤불로 빈틈없이 움직였다. 더 트인 수면이 허락할 때면, 그 날카로운 눈길은 그 좁은 해협을 험상궂게 내려다보는 헐벗은 바위와 우뚝 매달린 숲을 따라 뻗어 갔다."]], [["Heyward, who was a doubly interested spectator, as well from the beauties of the place as from the apprehension natural to his situation, was just believing that he had permitted the latter to be excited without sufficient reason, when the paddle ceased moving, in obedience to a signal from Chingachgook.", "〈헤이워드〉는 그곳의 아름다움 때문이기도 하고 제 처지에서 비롯된 자연스러운 우려 때문이기도 하여 갑절로 마음이 쓰이는 구경꾼이었던 터라, 자신이 충분한 까닭도 없이 후자의 감정을 일게 했던 것이라고 막 믿어 갈 무렵, 〈칭가치국〉의 신호에 따라 노가 움직임을 멈췄다."]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"우후!\""], ["exclaimed Uncas, nearly at the moment that the light tap his father had made on the side of the canoe notified them of the vicinity of danger.", "〈웅카스〉가 외쳤으니, 그 아비가 카누 옆구리를 가볍게 두드려 위험이 가까웠음을 알린 바로 그 순간이었다."]], [["\"What now?\"", "\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\" 척후가 물었다."], ["asked the scout; \"the lake is as smooth as if the winds had never blown, and I can see along its sheet for miles; there is not so much as the black head of a loon dotting the water.\"", "\"호수는 바람이 한 번도 분 적 없는 듯 잔잔하고, 나는 그 수면을 따라 몇 마일이나 내다볼 수 있는데. 수면을 점찍을 검은 아비새 머리 하나조차 없잖느냐.\""]], [["The Indian gravely raised his paddle, and pointed in the direction in which his own steady look was riveted.", "인디언은 엄숙하게 노를 들어, 제 한결같은 눈길이 못 박혀 있는 방향을 가리켰다."], ["Duncan's eyes followed the motion.", "〈던컨〉의 눈이 그 움직임을 좇았다."], ["A few rods in their front lay another of the low wooded islets, but it appeared as calm and peaceful as if its solitude had never been disturbed by the foot of man.", "그들 앞으로 몇 로드 떨어진 곳에 또 하나의 낮고 숲 우거진 섬이 놓여 있었으나, 그것은 마치 그 적막함이 인간의 발길에 한 번도 흐트러진 적 없는 듯 고요하고 평화로워 보였다."]], [["\"I see nothing,\"", "\"아무것도 안 보이오.\""], ["he said, \"but land and water; and a lovely scene it is.\"", "그가 말했다. \"뭍과 물뿐이고, 사랑스러운 경치로구려.\""]], [["\"Hist!\"", "\"쉿!\""], ["interrupted the scout.", "척후가 가로막았다."], ["\"Ay, Sagamore, there is always a reason for what you do.", "\"그렇지, 〈사가모어〉, 자네가 하는 일엔 늘 까닭이 있는 법이지."], ["'Tis but a shade, and yet it is not natural.", "그저 그늘에 지나지 않지만, 그래도 자연스럽지가 않아."], ["You see the mist, major, that is rising above the island; you can't call it a fog, for it is more like a streak of thin cloud--\"", "소령, 저 섬 위로 피어오르는 안개가 보이오? 그걸 안개라고는 부를 수 없소, 가느다란 구름 한 줄기에 더 가까우니까.\""]], [["\"It is vapor from the water.\"", "\"물에서 올라온 수증기겠지요.\""]], [["\"That a child could tell.", "\"그건 어린아이라도 알 수 있소."], ["But what is the edging of blacker smoke that hangs along its lower side, and which you may trace down into the thicket of hazel?", "허나 그 아래쪽에 걸려 있는, 개암나무 덤불까지 죽 따라 내려가 볼 수 있는 저 더 검은 연기의 가장자리는 뭐겠소?"], ["'Tis from a fire; but one that, in my judgment, has been suffered to burn low.\"", "저건 불에서 난 거요. 허나 내 판단으로는 일부러 약하게 사위어 가도록 둔 불이오.\""]], [["\"Let us then push for the place, and relieve our doubts,\"", "\"그렇다면 저곳으로 밀고 가서 의심을 떨쳐 버립시다.\""], ["said the impatient Duncan; \"the party must be small that can lie on such a bit of land.\"", "참을성 없는 〈던컨〉이 말했다. \"저만한 땅뙈기에 숨을 수 있는 무리라면 분명 적을 거요.\""]], [["\"If you judge of Indian cunning by the rules you find in books, or by white sagacity, they will lead you astray, if not to your death,\"", "\"인디언의 교활함을 책에서 찾은 규칙이나 백인의 분별로 헤아린다면, 그것은 그대를 그릇된 길로, 죽음으로까지는 아니더라도 어긋난 데로 이끌 거요.\""], ["returned Hawkeye, examining the signs of the place with that acuteness which distinguished him.", "그를 남다르게 하던 그 예리함으로 그곳의 징후를 살피며 〈호크아이〉가 대답했다."], ["\"If I may be permitted to speak in this matter, it will be to say, that we have but two things to choose between: the one is, to return, and give up all thoughts of following the Hurons--\"", "\"이 일에 대해 내가 말하는 것이 허락된다면, 우리에게 선택할 길이 둘뿐이라는 거요. 하나는, 되돌아가서 〈휴런〉족을 쫓을 생각을 모두 단념하는 것.\""]], [["\"Never!\"", "\"결코 안 되오!\""], ["exclaimed Heyward, in a voice far too loud for their circumstances.", "〈헤이워드〉가 그들의 처지에 비추어 너무도 큰 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Well, well,\"", "\"좋소, 좋아.\""], ["continued Hawkeye, making a hasty sign to repress his impatience; \"I am much of your mind myself; though I thought it becoming my experience to tell the whole.", "그의 조급함을 누르려 황급히 손짓을 보내며 〈호크아이〉가 말을 이었다. \"나 자신도 그대 마음과 크게 다르지 않소."], ["We must then make a push, and if the Indians or Frenchers are in the narrows, run the gauntlet through these toppling mountains.", "다만 다 말해 두는 것이 내 경험에 어울린다고 여겼을 뿐이오. 그러니 우리는 밀고 나아가야 하고, 만약 인디언이나 프랑스인이 그 길목에 있다면, 저 우뚝 솟은 산들 사이를 뚫고 빗발치는 매질을 견뎌 내야 하오."], ["Is there reason in my words, Sagamore?\"", "내 말에 이치가 있는가, 〈사가모어〉?\""]], [["The Indian made no other answer than by dropping his paddle into the water, and urging forward the canoe.", "인디언은 노를 물속에 떨구어 카누를 앞으로 밀어내는 것 말고는 다른 어떤 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["As he held the office of directing its course, his resolution was sufficiently indicated by the movement.", "진로를 정하는 소임을 그가 맡고 있었던 만큼, 그 움직임만으로 그의 결의는 충분히 드러났다."], ["The whole party now plied their paddles vigorously, and in a very few moments they had reached a point whence they might command an entire view of the northern shore of the island, the side that had hitherto been concealed.", "이제 온 일행이 힘차게 노를 저었고, 몇 순간이 채 못 되어 그들은 그때껏 가려져 있던 면, 곧 섬의 북쪽 기슭 전체를 한눈에 볼 수 있는 지점에 다다랐다."]], [["\"There they are, by all the truth of signs,\"", "\"저기 있군, 온갖 징후가 다 들어맞았어.\""], ["whispered the scout; \"two canoes and a smoke.", "척후가 속삭였다. \"카누 두 척에 연기 한 줄기."], ["The knaves haven't yet got their eyes out of the mist, or we should hear the accursed whoop.", "그 악당들이 아직 안개에서 눈을 떼지 못했군, 안 그랬으면 그 저주받을 함성이 들렸을 텐데."], ["Together, friend!", "함께, 여보게들!"], ["we are leaving them, and are already nearly out of whistle of a bullet.\"", "우리는 놈들을 따돌리고 있고, 벌써 총알 휘파람 소리가 닿을 거리에서 거의 벗어났소.\""]], [["The well known crack of a rifle, whose ball came skipping along the placid surface of the strait, and a shrill yell from the island, interrupted his speech, and announced that their passage was discovered.", "소총의 그 익숙한 발사음과 함께, 그 총알이 잔잔한 해협 수면을 따라 튀듯 날아왔고, 섬에서 날카로운 비명이 터져 나와 그의 말을 끊으며, 그들의 통과가 발각되었음을 알렸다."], ["In another instant several savages were seen rushing into the canoes, which were soon dancing over the water, in pursuit.", "다음 순간, 여러 미개인들이 카누로 달려드는 것이 보였고, 카누들은 곧 추격에 나서 물 위에서 너울너울 춤추었다."], ["These fearful precursors of a coming struggle produced no change in the countenances and movements of his three guides, so far as Duncan could discover, except that the strokes of their paddles were longer and more in unison, and caused the little bark to spring forward like a creature possessing life and volition.", "다가오는 싸움을 알리는 이 무시무시한 전조들도, 〈던컨〉이 알아챌 수 있는 한, 그의 세 인도자의 표정이나 움직임에는 아무런 변화도 일으키지 못했으니, 다만 그들의 노질이 더 길어지고 더 한 호흡으로 맞아 들어, 그 작은 배가 마치 생명과 의지를 지닌 생물처럼 앞으로 솟구치게 했을 뿐이었다."]], [["\"Hold them there, Sagamore,\"", "\"저놈들을 거기 잡아 두게, 〈사가모어〉.\""], ["said Hawkeye, looking coolly backward over his left shoulder, while he still plied his paddle; \"keep them just there.", "여전히 노를 저으며 왼쪽 어깨 너머로 침착하게 뒤를 돌아보며 〈호크아이〉가 말했다. \"딱 거기 붙들어 두라고."], ["Them Hurons have never a piece in their nation that will execute at this distance; but 'Killdeer'", "저 〈휴런〉족에겐 이 거리에서 명중시킬 만한 총이 그 부족 전체에 한 자루도 없네."], ["has a barrel on which a man may calculate.\"", "허나 '킬디어'에겐 사람이 믿고 셈할 수 있는 총신이 있지.\""]], [["The scout having ascertained that the Mohicans were sufficient of themselves to maintain the requisite distance, deliberately laid aside his paddle, and raised the fatal rifle.", "척후는 〈모히칸〉족만으로도 필요한 거리를 유지하기에 충분하다는 것을 확인하고는, 차분히 노를 내려놓고 그 치명적인 소총을 들어 올렸다."], ["Three several times he brought the piece to his shoulder, and when his companions were expecting its report, he as often lowered it to request the Indians would permit their enemies to approach a little nigher.", "세 차례나 그는 그 총을 어깨에 갖다 댔고, 동행들이 그 발사음을 기다릴 적마다, 그는 그만큼 다시 총을 내려, 인디언들에게 적이 좀 더 가까이 다가오도록 두라고 청했다."], ["At length his accurate and fastidious eye seemed satisfied, and throwing out his left arm on the barrel, he was slowly elevating the muzzle, when an exclamation from Uncas, who sat in the bow, once more caused him to suspend the shot.", "마침내 그의 정확하고 까다로운 눈이 만족한 듯했고, 왼팔을 총신 위에 쭉 뻗고 총구를 천천히 들어 올리던 차에, 뱃머리에 앉아 있던 〈웅카스〉의 외침이 다시 한번 그 발사를 멈추게 했다."]], [["\"What now, lad?\"", "\"이번엔 또 뭐냐, 얘야?\" 〈호크아이〉가 다그쳤다."], ["demanded Hawkeye; \"you saved a Huron from the death-shriek by that word; have you reason for what you do?\"", "\"그 한마디로 너는 〈휴런〉 한 놈을 죽음의 비명에서 구해 줬다. 네가 하는 일에 까닭이 있느냐?\""]], [["Uncas pointed towards the rocky shore a little in their front, whence another war canoe was darting directly across their course.", "〈웅카스〉는 그들 앞으로 조금 떨어진 바위 기슭을 가리켰으니, 그곳에서 또 다른 전투용 카누가 그들의 진로를 곧장 가로질러 쏜살같이 달려오고 있었다."], ["It was too obvious now that their situation was imminently perilous to need the aid of language to confirm it.", "그들의 처지가 절박하게 위태롭다는 것은 이제 너무도 분명하여, 말의 도움을 빌려 확인할 필요조차 없었다."], ["The scout laid aside his rifle, and resumed the paddle, while Chingachgook inclined the bows of the canoe a little towards the western shore, in order to increase the distance between them and this new enemy.", "척후는 소총을 내려놓고 다시 노를 잡았고, 그동안 〈칭가치국〉은 이 새로운 적과의 거리를 벌리려 카누의 뱃머리를 서쪽 기슭 쪽으로 조금 기울였다."], ["In the meantime they were reminded of the presence of those who pressed upon their rear, by wild and exulting shouts.", "그러는 사이 그들의 후미를 바짝 좇는 자들이 거칠고 의기양양한 함성으로 제 존재를 일깨웠다."], ["The stirring scene awakened even Munro from his apathy.", "이 들끓는 장면은 〈먼로〉마저 무기력에서 깨어나게 했다."]], [["\"Let us make for the rocks on the main,\"", "\"본토의 바위로 향합시다.\""], ["he said, with the mien of a tired soldier, \"and give battle to the savages.", "지친 군인의 표정으로 그가 말했다. \"그리고 저 미개인들과 한판 싸웁시다."], ["God forbid that I, or those attached to me and mine, should ever trust again to the faith of any servant of the Louis's!\"", "나든, 나와 내 핏줄에 매인 자들이든, 다시는 〈루이〉의 그 어떤 신하의 신의에도 의지하는 일이 없게 하소서, 신이여 막아 주소서!\""]], [["\"He who wishes to prosper in Indian warfare,\"", "\"인디언과의 전쟁에서 잘 헤쳐 나가고자 하는 자는,\""], ["returned the scout, \"must not be too proud to learn from the wit of a native.", "척후가 대답했다. \"원주민의 슬기에서 배우기를 마다할 만큼 거만해선 안 되오."], ["Lay her more along the land, Sagamore; we are doubling on the varlets, and perhaps they may try to strike our trail on the long calculation.\"", "배를 뭍에 더 붙이게, 〈사가모어〉. 우리는 그 악당들을 따돌리려 휘돌고 있으니, 어쩌면 놈들이 멀리 셈을 내고 우리 자취를 끊으려 들지도 모르네.\""]], [["Hawkeye was not mistaken; for when the Hurons found their course was likely to throw them behind their chase, they rendered it less direct, until, by gradually bearing more and more obliquely, the two canoes were ere long, gliding on parallel lines, within two hundred yards of each other.", "〈호크아이〉의 짐작은 틀리지 않았으니, 〈휴런〉족은 자신들의 항로가 추격 대상의 뒤로 처질 듯하다는 것을 알아채자, 차츰 더더욱 비스듬히 방향을 틀어 항로를 덜 곧게 만들었고, 이내 두 카누는 서로 이백 야드 안쪽에서 나란히 미끄러져 갔다."], ["It now became entirely a trial of speed.", "이제 그것은 전적으로 속도의 겨룸이 되었다."], ["So rapid was the progress of the light vessels, that the lake curled in their front, in miniature waves, and their motion became undulating by its own velocity.", "가벼운 배들이 어찌나 빠르게 나아갔던지, 그 앞에서 호수가 자잘한 물결을 일으키며 곱슬거렸고, 그 움직임 자체의 속도로 인해 배가 너울너울 출렁였다."], ["It was, perhaps, owing to this circumstance, in addition to the necessity of keeping every hand employed at the paddles, that the Hurons had not immediate recourse to their fire-arms.", "〈휴런〉족이 곧장 총기에 의지하지 않은 것은, 모든 손이 노에 매달려 있어야 했던 절박함에 더해, 아마도 이 사정 때문이었을 것이다."], ["The exertions of the fugitives were too severe to continue long, and the pursuers had the advantage of numbers.", "달아나는 자들의 분투는 너무 가혹하여 오래 이어 갈 수 없었고, 추격자들은 수의 우위를 지니고 있었다."], ["Duncan observed, with uneasiness, that the scout began to look anxiously about him, as if searching for some further means of assisting their flight.", "〈던컨〉은 척후가 마치 그들의 도주를 도울 또 다른 방도를 찾기라도 하듯 근심스레 주위를 둘러보기 시작하는 것을 불안하게 지켜보았다."]], [["\"Edge her a little more from the sun, Sagamore,\"", "\"배를 해를 향한 데서 좀 더 비키게, 〈사가모어〉.\""], ["said the stubborn woodsman; \"I see the knaves are sparing a man to the rifle.", "고집스러운 숲의 사내가 말했다. \"그 악당들이 한 놈을 소총에 떼어 놓는 게 보이네."], ["A single broken bone might lose us our scalps.", "뼈 하나만 부러져도 우리 머릿가죽을 잃을 수 있어."], ["Edge more from the sun and we will put the island between us.\"", "해에서 더 비키게, 그러면 우리가 섬을 사이에 두게 될 테니.\""]], [["The expedient was not without its use.", "그 방책은 쓸모가 없지 않았다."], ["A long, low island lay at a little distance before them, and as they closed with it, the chasing canoe was compelled to take a side opposite to that on which the pursued passed.", "길고 낮은 섬 하나가 그들 앞으로 조금 떨어진 곳에 놓여 있었고, 그들이 그 섬에 바짝 다가가자, 추격하는 카누는 쫓기는 자들이 지나간 쪽과 반대편으로 돌 수밖에 없었다."], ["The scout and his companions did not neglect this advantage, but the instant they were hid from observation by the bushes, they redoubled efforts that before had seemed prodigious.", "척후와 그의 동행들은 이 이점을 놓치지 않았으니, 덤불에 가려 적의 눈에서 벗어난 순간, 앞서도 어마어마해 보이던 분투를 곱절로 쏟아부었다."], ["The two canoes came round the last low point, like two coursers at the top of their speed, the fugitives taking the lead.", "두 카누는 그 마지막 낮은 끝머리를 돌아 나왔으니, 마치 전속력으로 내달리는 두 마리 준마와도 같았고, 달아나는 쪽이 앞장서 있었다."], ["This change had brought them nigher to each other, however, while it altered their relative positions.", "그러나 이 변화로 인해 그들은 서로 더 가까워졌고, 그러는 동안 그 상대적 위치는 뒤바뀌었다."]], [["\"You showed knowledge in the shaping of birchen bark, Uncas, when you chose this from among the Huron canoes,\"", "\"〈웅카스〉, 자작나무 껍질 배의 됨됨이를 알아보는 식견이 있었구나, 그 〈휴런〉 카누들 가운데서 이것을 골랐으니 말이다.\""], ["said the scout, smiling, apparently more in satisfaction at their superiority in the race, than from that prospect of final escape which now began to open a little upon them.", "척후가 미소 지으며 말했으니, 이제 막 조금씩 열리기 시작한 그 끝내 벗어날 가망보다는, 이 겨룸에서의 우위에 대한 흡족함에서 비롯된 미소인 듯했다."], ["\"The imps have put all their strength again at the paddles, and we are to struggle for our scalps with bits of flattened wood, instead of clouded barrels and true eyes.", "\"그 악마 같은 놈들이 다시 온 힘을 노에 쏟아붓고 있군, 그러니 우리는 흐릿한 총신과 정확한 눈 대신, 납작하게 깎은 나뭇조각으로 우리 머릿가죽을 위해 싸워야 하는 셈이야."], ["A long stroke, and together, friends.\"", "길게, 그리고 함께 젓게, 여보게들.\""]], [["\"They are preparing for a shot,\"", "\"저들이 사격을 준비하고 있소.\""], ["said Heyward; \"and as we are in a line with them, it can scarcely fail.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 말했다. \"그리고 우리가 저들과 일직선상에 있으니, 빗나가기 어려울 거요.\""]], [["\"Get you then into the bottom of the canoe,\"", "\"그러면 그대는 카누 바닥으로 들어가시오.\" 척후가 대답했다."], ["returned the scout; \"you and the colonel; it will be so much taken from the size of the mark.\"", "\"그대와 대령 말이오. 그만큼 표적의 크기가 줄어들 테니.\""]], [["Heyward smiled, as he answered,--", "〈헤이워드〉가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["\"It would be but an ill example for the highest in rank to dodge, while the warriors were under fire!\"", "\"전사들이 총격을 받고 있는데 최고 계급에 있는 자가 몸을 피한다면, 그건 못된 본보기가 될 뿐이오!\""]], [["\"Lord! Lord!", "\"맙소사!"], ["That is now a white man's courage!\"", "맙소사! 그게 바로 백인의 용기란 거지!\""], ["exclaimed the scout; \"and like too many of his notions, not to be maintained by reason.", "척후가 외쳤다. \"허나 그자의 다른 숱한 생각들이 그렇듯, 이치로 떠받칠 수 없는 거요."], ["Do you think the Sagamore, or Uncas, or even I, who am a man without a cross, would deliberate about finding a cover in the skrimmage, when an open body would do no good?", "〈사가모어〉나 〈웅카스〉, 아니 혼혈이 섞이지 않은 사내인 나조차도, 몸을 드러내 봐야 아무 득도 없는 접전에서 엄폐할 데를 찾기를 망설이리라 생각하시오?"], ["For what have the Frenchers reared up their Quebec, if fighting is always to be done in the clearings?\" [Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_", "싸움이 늘 트인 데서만 벌어질 거라면, 프랑스인들이 무엇하러 그 〈퀘벡〉을 쌓아 올렸겠소?\""]], [["_The scout having ascertained that the Mohicans were sufficient of themselves to maintain the requisite distance, deliberately laid aside his paddle, and raised the fatal rifle_]", "척후는 〈모히칸〉들만으로도 필요한 거리를 유지할 수 있음을 확인하고는, 천천히 노를 옆에 내려놓고 그 치명적인 소총을 들어 올렸다"]], [["\"All that you say is very true, my friend,\" replied Heyward; \"still, our customs must prevent us from doing as you wish.\"", "〈휴런〉족의 일제 사격이 그 말을 끊었고, 총알들이 그들 주위로 휘파람 소리를 내며 날아드는 가운데, 〈던컨〉은 〈웅카스〉의 머리가 돌아가 자기와 〈먼로〉를 뒤돌아보는 것을 보았다."], ["A volley from the Hurons interrupted the discourse, and as the bullets whistled about them, Duncan saw the head of Uncas turned, looking back at himself and Munro. Notwithstanding the nearness of the enemy, and his own great personal danger, the countenance of the young warrior expressed no other emotion, as the former was compelled to think, than amazement at finding men willing to encounter so useless an exposure.", "적이 가까이 있고 제 몸 또한 크나큰 위험에 처했음에도, 그 젊은 전사의 얼굴에는, 〈던컨〉이 그리 생각할 수밖에 없었듯, 그토록 쓸데없이 몸을 드러내려는 자들을 보고 느끼는 놀라움 말고는 다른 어떤 감정도 떠올라 있지 않았다."], ["Chingachgook was probably better acquainted with the notions of white men, for he did not even cast a glance aside from the riveted look his eye maintained on the object by which he governed their course.", "〈칭가치국〉은 아마도 백인의 사고방식에 더 밝았던 모양인지, 진로를 다스리는 그 대상에 못 박힌 눈길을 곁으로 한 번 돌리지조차 않았다."], ["A ball soon struck the light and polished paddle from the hands of the chief, and drove it through the air, far in the advance.", "곧 총알 한 발이 그 추장의 손에서 가볍고 매끄러운 노를 쳐 내, 멀찍이 앞쪽 허공으로 날려 버렸다."], ["A shout arose from the Hurons, who seized the opportunity to fire another volley.", "〈휴런〉족에게서 함성이 일었으니, 그들은 그 틈을 놓치지 않고 또 한 차례 일제 사격을 퍼부었다."], ["Uncas described an arc in the water with his own blade, and as the canoe passed swiftly on, Chingachgook recovered his paddle, and flourishing it on high, he gave the war-whoop of the Mohicans, and then lent his strength and skill again to the important task.", "〈웅카스〉가 제 노로 물에 호를 그렸고, 카누가 잽싸게 지나가는 사이, 〈칭가치국〉은 노를 되찾아 그것을 높이 휘두르며 〈모히칸〉족의 전투 함성을 내질렀고, 그런 다음 다시 그 중대한 일에 제 힘과 솜씨를 쏟아부었다."]], [["The clamorous sounds of \"Le Gros Serpent!\"", "\"르 그로 세르팡!\""], ["\"La Longue Carabine!\"", "\"라 롱그 카라빈!\""], ["\"Le Cerf Agile!\"", "\"르 세르 아질!\""], ["burst at once from the canoes behind, and seemed to give new zeal to the pursuers. The scout seized \"Killdeer\"", "하는 떠들썩한 외침이 뒤따르는 카누들에서 한꺼번에 터져 나와, 추격자들에게 새로운 열의를 불어넣는 듯했다."], ["in his left hand, and elevating it above his head, he shook it in triumph at his enemies.", "척후는 왼손으로 '킬디어'를 거머쥐고, 머리 위로 높이 들어 올려, 적들을 향해 그것을 의기양양하게 흔들었다."], ["The savages answered the insult with a yell, and immediately another volley succeeded.", "미개인들은 함성으로 그 모욕에 응답했고, 곧장 또 한 차례 일제 사격이 뒤따랐다. 총알들이 호수를 따라 후두두 떨어졌고, 그중 하나는 그들의 작은 배 껍질을 꿰뚫기까지 했다."], ["The bullets pattered along the lake, and one even pierced the bark of their little vessel.", "이 위태로운 순간에도 〈모히칸〉족에게서는 알아챌 만한 어떤 동요도 찾아볼 수 없었으니, 그 굳은 표정에는 희망도 경악도 떠올라 있지 않았다."], ["No perceptible emotion could be discovered in the Mohicans during this critical moment, their rigid features expressing neither hope nor alarm; but the scout again turned his head, and laughing in his own silent manner, he said to Heyward,--", "그러나 척후는 다시 고개를 돌려, 제 나름의 소리 없는 방식으로 웃으며 〈헤이워드〉에게 말했다. * 르 그로 세르팡(큰 뱀)은 〈칭가치국〉, 라 롱그 카라빈(긴 소총)은 〈호크아이〉, 르 세르 아질(날쌘 사슴)은 〈웅카스〉를 프랑스어로 일컫는 별칭이다."]], [["\"The knaves love to hear the sounds of their pieces; but the eye is not to be found among the Mingos that can calculate a true range in a dancing canoe!", "\"그 악당들은 제 총소리 듣는 걸 좋아하지. 허나 〈밍고〉족 가운데 춤추는 카누에서 참된 사거리를 셈할 수 있는 눈은 찾아볼 수 없소!"], ["You see the dumb devils have taken off a man to charge, and by the smallest measurement that can be allowed, we move three feet to their two!\"", "보시오, 그 멍청한 악마들이 한 놈을 장전하느라 떼어 놓았으니, 가장 인색하게 잡아도 우리는 놈들이 둘 갈 때 셋을 나아가는 셈이오!\""]], [["Duncan, who was not altogether as easy under this nice estimate of distances as his companions, was glad to find, however, that owing to their superior dexterity, and the diversion among their enemies, they were very sensibly obtaining the advantage.", "이 정교한 거리 셈을 동행들만큼 마음 편하게 받아들이지는 못한 〈던컨〉이었으나, 그래도 그들의 빼어난 솜씨와 적들 사이에 생긴 차질 덕에 그들이 눈에 띄게 우위를 얻어 가고 있다는 것을 알게 되어 기뻤다."], ["The Hurons soon fired again, and a bullet struck the blade of Hawkeye's paddle without injury.", "〈휴런〉족은 곧 다시 사격했고, 총알 한 발이 〈호크아이〉의 노 깃을 맞혔으나 상처를 입히지는 못했다."]], [["\"That will do,\"", "\"이 정도면 됐군.\""], ["said the scout, examining the slight indentation with a curious eye; \"it would not have cut the skin of an infant, much less of men, who, like us, have been blown upon by the heavens in their anger.", "그 얕은 자국을 신기한 눈으로 살피며 척후가 말했다. \"이건 갓난아이의 살갗도 베지 못했을 거요, 하물며 우리처럼 노한 하늘의 입김을 맞아 온 사내들의 살갗이야."], ["Now, major, if you will try to use this piece of flattened wood, I'll let 'Killdeer' take a part in the conversation.\"", "자, 소령, 그대가 이 납작하게 깎은 나뭇조각을 써 보겠다면, 나는 '킬디어'를 이 대화에 한몫 끼게 하겠소.\""]], [["Heyward seized the paddle, and applied himself to the work with an eagerness that supplied the place of skill, while Hawkeye was engaged in inspecting the priming of his rifle.", "〈헤이워드〉는 노를 거머쥐고, 솜씨를 메우고도 남을 열의로 그 일에 매달렸고, 그동안 〈호크아이〉는 제 소총의 점화약을 살피는 데 몰두했다."], ["The latter then took a swift aim, and fired.", "그런 다음 그는 잽싸게 겨누어 발사했다."], ["The Huron in the bows of the leading canoe had risen with a similar object, and he now fell backward, suffering his gun to escape from his hands into the water.", "앞장선 카누 뱃머리의 〈휴런〉이 같은 목적으로 몸을 일으켰던 차에, 이제 그는 뒤로 나동그라지며 총이 손에서 물속으로 미끄러져 떨어지게 두었다."], ["In an instant, however, he recovered his feet, though his gestures were wild and bewildered.", "그러나 순식간에 그는 다시 발을 딛고 일어섰으니, 그 몸짓은 거칠고 어지러웠다."], ["At the same moment his companions suspended their efforts, and the chasing canoes clustered together, and became stationary.", "같은 순간 그의 동료들은 분투를 멈췄고, 추격하던 카누들은 한데 모여 멈춰 섰다."], ["Chingachgook and Uncas profited by the interval to regain their wind, though Duncan continued to work with the most persevering industry.", "〈칭가치국〉과 〈웅카스〉는 그 짬을 타 숨을 골랐으나, 〈던컨〉은 더없이 끈질긴 부지런함으로 계속 노를 저었다."], ["The father and son now cast calm but inquiring glances at each other, to learn if either had sustained any injury by the fire; for both well knew that no cry or exclamation would, in such a moment of necessity, have been permitted to betray the accident.", "아비와 아들은 이제 사격으로 인해 둘 중 누구든 상처를 입지는 않았는지 알아보려 차분하되 캐묻는 눈길을 서로에게 던졌다. 그토록 절박한 순간에는 어떤 외침이나 비명도 그 사고를 드러내도록 허락되지 않으리라는 것을 둘 다 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["A few large drops of blood were trickling down the shoulder of the Sagamore, who, when he perceived that the eyes of Uncas dwelt too long on the sight, raised some water in the hollow of his hand, and washing off the stain, was content to manifest, in this simple manner, the slightness of the injury.", "굵직한 핏방울 몇이 〈사가모어〉의 어깨를 타고 흘러내리고 있었으니, 그는 〈웅카스〉의 눈이 그 광경에 너무 오래 머무는 것을 알아채자, 오목한 손바닥에 물을 떠 그 얼룩을 씻어 내고는, 이 단출한 방식으로 상처가 가볍다는 것을 드러내는 데 만족했다."]], [["\"Softly, softly, major,\"", "\"살살, 살살하시오, 소령.\""], ["said the scout, who by this time had reloaded his rifle; \"we are a little too far already for a rifle to put forth its beauties, and you see yonder imps are holding a council.", "이즈음 소총을 다시 장전한 척후가 말했다. \"우리는 이미 소총이 제 빼어남을 펼치기엔 조금 너무 멀리 와 있고, 보시다시피 저쪽 악마 같은 놈들이 회의를 열고 있소."], ["Let them come up within striking distance--my eye may well be trusted in such a matter--and I will trail the varlets the length of the Horican, guaranteeing that not a shot of theirs shall, at the worst, more than break the skin, while 'Killdeer'", "놈들을 명중시킬 거리 안으로 들어오게 둡시다. 이런 일이라면 내 눈은 믿어도 좋소."], ["shall touch the life twice in three times.\"", "그러면 내가 〈호리컨〉의 길이만큼 그 악당들을 따라가며 약속하리다, 놈들의 총알은 잘해야 살갗을 찢는 데 그칠 테고, '킬디어'는 세 번에 두 번은 목숨을 건드릴 거라고.\""]], [["\"We forget our errand,\" returned the diligent Duncan.", "\"우리는 우리 볼일을 잊고 있소.\""], ["\"For God's sake let us profit by this advantage, and increase our distance from the enemy.\"", "부지런한 〈던컨〉이 대꾸했다. \"제발 이 우위를 이용해, 적과의 거리를 벌립시다.\""]], [["\"Give me my children,\"", "\"내 아이들을 다오.\""], ["said Munro hoarsely; \"trifle no longer with a father's agony, but restore me my babes.\"", "〈먼로〉가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"아비의 고뇌를 더는 희롱하지 말고, 내 어린것들을 돌려다오.\""]], [["Long and habitual deference to the mandates of his superiors had taught the scout the virtue of obedience.", "상관의 명령에 오래도록 몸에 밴 복종은 척후에게 따름의 덕을 가르쳐 두었다."], ["Throwing a last and lingering glance at the distant canoes, he laid aside his rifle, and relieving the wearied Duncan, resumed the paddle, which he wielded with sinews that never tired.", "멀리 있는 카누들에 마지막으로 미련 어린 눈길을 던지고 나서, 그는 소총을 내려놓고, 지친 〈던컨〉과 자리를 바꿔, 결코 지치지 않는 근력으로 휘두르던 그 노를 다시 잡았다."], ["His efforts were seconded by those of the Mohicans, and a very few minutes served to place such a sheet of water between them and their enemies, that Heyward once more breathed freely.", "그의 분투에 〈모히칸〉족의 분투가 보태졌고, 몇 분이 채 못 되어 그들과 적들 사이에 그만한 수면이 펼쳐져, 〈헤이워드〉는 다시 한번 마음 놓고 숨을 쉬었다."]], [["The lake now began to expand, and their route lay along a wide reach, that was lined, as before, by high and ragged mountains.", "이제 호수가 넓어지기 시작했고, 그들의 항로는 앞서처럼 높고 험준한 산들이 늘어선 너른 물길을 따라 뻗어 있었다."], ["But the islands were few, and easily avoided.", "그러나 섬은 드물어 쉽게 피할 수 있었다."], ["The strokes of the paddles grew more measured and regular, while they who plied them continued their labor, after the close and deadly chase from which they had just relieved themselves, with as much coolness as though their speed had been tried in sport, rather than under such pressing, nay, almost desperate circumstances.", "노질은 한결 가락 맞고 고르게 되었고, 노를 젓는 자들은 방금 벗어난 그 바짝 좇기던 죽음의 추격이 끝난 뒤에도, 마치 그들의 속도가 그토록 절박한, 아니 거의 필사적인 상황에서가 아니라 놀이로 겨루어진 것이기라도 한 듯한 침착함으로 제 노고를 이어 갔다."]], [["Instead of following the western shore, whither their errand led them, the wary Mohican inclined his course more towards those hills behind which Montcalm was known to have led his army into the formidable fortress of Ticonderoga.", "볼일이 그들을 이끄는 서쪽 기슭을 따르는 대신, 신중한 〈모히칸〉은 〈몽칼름〉이 군대를 이끌고 가공할 〈타이콘데로가〉 요새로 들어갔다고 알려진 저 언덕들 쪽으로 항로를 더 기울였다."], ["As the Hurons, to every appearance, had abandoned the pursuit, there was no apparent reason for this excess of caution.", "〈휴런〉족이 겉보기로는 추격을 단념한 듯했으니, 이토록 지나친 조심성에는 뚜렷한 까닭이 없었다."], ["It was, however, maintained for hours, until they had reached a bay, nigh the northern termination of the lake.", "그러나 그것은 호수의 북쪽 끝머리 가까이 한 만에 다다를 때까지 몇 시간이나 이어졌다."], ["Here the canoe was driven upon the beach, and the whole party landed.", "여기서 카누를 모래톱에 끌어 올렸고, 온 일행이 뭍에 내렸다."], ["Hawkeye and Heyward ascended an adjacent bluff, where the former, after considering the expanse of water beneath him, pointed out to the latter a small black object, hovering under a headland, at the distance of several miles.", "〈호크아이〉와 〈헤이워드〉는 인근의 벼랑에 올랐으니, 그곳에서 전자는 발아래 펼쳐진 물의 너비를 살펴본 뒤, 후자에게 몇 마일 떨어진 곶 아래에 어른거리는 작고 검은 물체 하나를 가리켜 보였다."]], [["\"Do you see it?\"", "\"저게 보이오?\""], ["demanded the scout.", "척후가 물었다."], ["\"Now, what would you account that spot, were you left alone to white experience to find your way through this wilderness?\"", "\"자, 그대가 이 황야를 헤쳐 갈 길을 백인의 경험에만 맡겨진 채 혼자 찾아야 한다면, 저 점을 무엇이라 여기겠소?\""]], [["\"But for its distance and its magnitude, I should suppose it a bird.", "\"그 거리와 크기만 아니라면, 새라고 여겼을 거요."], ["Can it be a living object?\"", "살아 있는 것일 수 있소?\""]], [["\"'Tis a canoe of good birchen bark, and paddled by fierce and crafty Mingos.", "\"저건 질 좋은 자작나무 껍질 카누요, 사납고 교활한 〈밍고〉족이 노 젓는."], ["Though Providence has lent to those who inhabit the woods eyes that would be needless to men in the settlements, where there are inventions to assist the sight, yet no human organs can see all the dangers which at this moment circumvent us.", "섭리가 숲에 사는 자들에게는, 시력을 돕는 발명품들이 있는 정착지의 사람들에게라면 필요 없을 눈을 빌려주셨으나, 그래도 어떤 인간의 기관도 이 순간 우리를 에워싼 온갖 위험을 다 볼 수는 없소."], ["These varlets pretend to be bent chiefly on their sun-down meal, but the moment it is dark they will be on our trail, as true as hounds on the scent.", "저 악당들은 주로 해 질 녘 끼니에만 마음을 쏟는 척하지만, 날이 어두워지는 순간 우리 자취를 좇을 거요, 냄새 맡은 사냥개만큼이나 어김없이."], ["We must throw them off, or our pursuit of Le Renard Subtil may be given up.", "우리는 놈들을 따돌려야 하오, 안 그러면 르 르나르 쉬브틸을 쫓는 일은 단념해야 할지도 모르오."], ["These lakes are useful at times, especially when the game takes the water,\"", "이런 호수들은 때로는 쓸모가 있소, 특히 사냥감이 물로 들 때는.\""], ["continued the scout, gazing about him with a countenance of concern; \"but they give no cover, except it be to the fishes.", "근심 어린 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보며 척후가 말을 이었다. \"허나 물고기에게가 아니라면, 엄폐할 데를 내주지 않지."], ["God knows what the country would be, if the settlements should ever spread far from the two rivers.", "정착지가 저 두 강에서 멀리까지 뻗어 나가게 된다면, 이 고장이 어찌 될지 신만이 아실 거요."], ["Both hunting and war would lose their beauty.\"", "사냥도 전쟁도 그 아름다움을 잃겠지.\""]], [["\"Let us not delay a moment, without some good and obvious cause.\"", "\"마땅하고 분명한 까닭이 없는 한, 한 순간도 지체하지 맙시다.\""]], [["\"I little like that smoke, which you may see worming up along the rock above the canoe,\"", "\"카누 위로 바위를 따라 꿈틀거리며 올라가는 저 연기가 영 마음에 들지 않는단 말이오.\""], ["interrupted the abstracted scout.", "골똘히 생각에 잠긴 척후가 가로막았다."], ["\"My life on it, other eyes than ours see it, and know its meaning.", "\"내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 우리 말고 다른 눈들도 저걸 보고 그 뜻을 알고 있소."], ["Well, words will not mend the matter, and it is time that we were doing.\"", "그래, 말로는 이 일이 나아지지 않으니, 이제 우리가 움직일 때요.\""]], [["Hawkeye moved away from the look-out, and descended, musing profoundly, to the shore.", "〈호크아이〉는 망보던 자리에서 물러나, 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 기슭으로 내려갔다."], ["He communicated the result of his observations to his companions, in Delaware, and a short and earnest consultation succeeded.", "그는 〈델라웨어〉 말로 동행들에게 제 관찰의 결론을 전했고, 짧고 진지한 의논이 뒤따랐다."], ["When it terminated, the three instantly set about executing their new resolutions.", "그것이 끝나자, 셋은 즉시 그들의 새로운 결의를 실행하는 데 나섰다."]], [["The canoe was lifted from the water, and borne on the shoulders of the party.", "카누를 물에서 들어 올려, 일행의 어깨에 짊어졌다."], ["They proceeded into the wood, making as broad and obvious a trail as possible.", "그들은 숲속으로 나아가며, 되도록 넓고 또렷한 자취를 남겼다."], ["They soon reached a water-course, which they crossed, and continued onward, until they came to an extensive and naked rock.", "그들은 곧 물줄기 하나에 다다라 그것을 건넜고, 너르고 헐벗은 바위에 이를 때까지 계속 나아갔다."], ["At this point, where their footsteps might be expected to be no longer visible, they retraced their route to the brook, walking backwards, with the utmost care.", "발자국이 더는 보이지 않으리라 여겨질 만한 이 지점에서, 그들은 더없이 조심스럽게 뒷걸음질하며 시내까지 제 길을 되짚어 갔다."], ["They now followed the bed of the little stream to the lake, into which they immediately launched their canoe again.", "이제 그들은 그 작은 시내의 바닥을 따라 호수까지 갔고, 그곳에서 곧장 카누를 다시 물에 띄웠다."], ["A low point concealed them from the headland, and the margin of the lake was fringed for some distance with dense and overhanging bushes.", "낮은 끝머리 하나가 곶에서 그들을 가려 주었고, 호숫가는 어느 정도 거리에 걸쳐 빽빽하게 우거져 드리운 덤불로 술처럼 둘러쳐져 있었다."], ["Under the cover of these natural advantages, they toiled their way, with patient industry, until the scout pronounced that he believed it would be safe once more to land.", "이 자연의 이점들에 몸을 가린 채, 그들은 끈기 있는 부지런함으로 길을 헤쳐 나아갔으니, 마침내 척후가 다시 한번 뭍에 올라도 안전할 듯하다고 판가름했다."]], [["The halt continued until evening rendered objects indistinct and uncertain to the eye.", "저녁이 사물을 눈에 흐릿하고 어슴푸레하게 만들 때까지 멈춤은 이어졌다."], ["Then they resumed their route, and, favored by the darkness, pushed silently and vigorously towards the western shore.", "그러고 나서 그들은 다시 길에 올랐고, 어둠의 도움을 받아 서쪽 기슭을 향해 소리 없이 힘차게 밀고 나아갔다."], ["Although the rugged outline of mountain, to which they were steering, presented no distinctive marks to the eyes of Duncan, the Mohican entered the little haven he had selected with the confidence and accuracy of an experienced pilot.", "그들이 향하던 산의 험준한 윤곽이 〈던컨〉의 눈에는 아무런 뚜렷한 표지도 내보이지 않았으나, 〈모히칸〉은 노련한 키잡이의 확신과 정확함으로 자신이 골라 둔 작은 항구로 들어섰다."]], [["The boat was again lifted and borne into the woods where it was carefully concealed under a pile of brush.", "배를 다시 들어 올려 숲으로 옮겨, 덤불 더미 아래 조심스레 감추었다."], ["The adventurers assumed their arms and packs, and the scout announced to Munro and Heyward that he and the Indians were at last in readiness to proceed.", "모험가들은 무기와 짐을 챙겼고, 척후는 〈먼로〉와 〈헤이워드〉에게 자신과 인디언들이 마침내 나아갈 채비가 되었음을 알렸다."], ["\"If you find a man there, he shall die a flea's death.\"", "\"거기서 누군가를 발견하거든, 벼룩처럼 죽게 될 거요.\""]], [["_Merry Wives of Windsor._\nThe party had landed on the border of a region that is, even to this day, less known to the inhabitants of the States, than the deserts of Arabia, or the steppes of Tartary.", "그 일행은, 오늘날까지도 합중국 주민들에게는 〈아라비아〉의 사막이나 〈타타르〉의 초원만큼이나 알려지지 않은 한 지역의 경계에 발을 디뎠다."], ["It was the sterile and rugged district which separates the tributaries of Champlain from those of the Hudson, the Mohawk, and the St. Lawrence.", "그곳은 〈섐플레인〉의 지류들을 〈허드슨〉, 〈모호크〉, 〈세인트로렌스〉의 지류들로부터 갈라놓는, 메마르고 험준한 고장이었다."], ["Since the period of our tale, the active spirit of the country has surrounded it with a belt of rich and thriving settlements, though none but the hunter or the savage is ever known, even now, to penetrate its wild recesses.", "우리 이야기가 펼쳐진 시기 이후로, 이 나라의 진취적인 기상이 그 둘레를 풍요롭고 번성하는 정착지의 띠로 에워쌌으나, 지금까지도 그 거친 후미진 곳을 뚫고 들어가는 자는 사냥꾼이나 미개인뿐이라 알려져 있다."]], [["As Hawkeye and the Mohicans had, however, often traversed the mountains and valleys of this vast wilderness, they did not hesitate to plunge into its depths, with the freedom of men accustomed to its privations and difficulties.", "그러나 〈호크아이〉와 두 〈모히칸〉은 이 광막한 황야의 산과 골짜기를 여러 차례 가로질러 본 터라, 그 궁핍과 곤란에 익숙한 자들 특유의 거리낌 없는 태도로, 망설임 없이 그 깊은 속으로 뛰어들었다."], ["For many hours the travellers toiled on their laborious way, guided by a star, or following the direction of some water-course, until the scout called a halt, and holding a short consultation with the Indians, they lighted their fire, and made the usual preparations to pass the remainder of the night where they then were.", "여행자들은 여러 시간 동안 별 하나를 길잡이 삼거나 어느 물줄기의 방향을 좇으며 고된 길을 헤쳐 나아갔고, 이윽고 척후가 발걸음을 멈추라 외쳤으며, 인디언들과 짤막한 의논을 나눈 뒤, 불을 피우고 그날 밤의 남은 시간을 그 자리에서 보내려는 여느 준비를 갖추었다."]], [["Imitating the example, and emulating the confidence, of their more experienced associates, Munro and Duncan slept without fear, if not without uneasiness.", "보다 노련한 동료들의 본을 따르고 그 자신감을 본받아, 〈먼로〉와 〈던컨〉은 불안이 없지는 않았으나 두려움 없이 잠들었다."], ["The dews were suffered to exhale, and the sun dispersed the mists, and was shedding a strong and clear light in the forest, when the travellers resumed their journey.", "이슬이 다 마르고, 해가 안개를 흩어 버려 숲에 강하고 맑은 빛을 드리울 무렵에야 여행자들은 다시 길을 떠났다."]], [["After proceeding a few miles, the progress of Hawkeye, who led the advance, became more deliberate and watchful.", "몇 마일을 나아간 뒤, 앞장서 이끌던 〈호크아이〉의 걸음이 한층 신중하고 조심스러워졌다."], ["He often stopped to examine the trees; nor did he cross a rivulet, without attentively considering the quantity, the velocity, and the color of its waters.", "그는 자주 멈춰 서서 나무들을 살폈고, 시내를 건널 때마다 그 물의 양과 빠르기와 빛깔을 유심히 헤아리지 않고서는 건너지 않았다."], ["Distrusting his own judgment his appeals to the opinion of Chingachgook were frequent and earnest.", "제 판단을 미덥잖게 여기는 그는 〈칭가치국〉의 의견을 구하는 일이 잦고도 간곡했다."], ["During one of these conferences, Heyward observed that Uncas stood a patient and silent, though, as he imagined, an interested listener.", "이런 협의가 한창일 때, 〈헤이워드〉는 〈언카스〉가 끈기 있고 말없이 서 있되, 그가 짐작하기로는 관심을 가지고 귀 기울이는 자임을 알아차렸다."], ["He was strongly tempted to address the young chief, and demand his opinion of their progress; but the calm and dignified demeanor of the native induced him to believe that, like himself, the other was wholly dependent on the sagacity and intelligence of the seniors of the party.", "그는 그 젊은 추장에게 말을 걸어 진행에 관한 의견을 물어보고 싶은 마음이 간절했으나, 그 원주민의 침착하고 위엄 있는 태도를 보고는, 그도 자신과 마찬가지로 일행 중 손위 어른들의 명민함과 지혜에 전적으로 기대고 있으리라 여기게 되었다."], ["At last, the scout spoke in English, and at once explained the embarrassment of their situation.", "마침내 척후가 영어로 입을 열어, 그들이 처한 곤경을 단번에 설명했다."]], [["\"When I found that the home path of the Hurons run north,\"", "\"〈휴런〉들이 돌아가는 길이 북쪽으로 났다는 걸 알았을 때,\""], ["he said, \"it did not need the judgment of many long years to tell that they would follow the valleys, and keep atween the waters of the Hudson and the Horican, until they might strike the springs of the Canada streams, which would lead them into the heart of the country of the Frenchers.", "그가 말했다, \"그자들이 골짜기를 따라가며 〈허드슨〉과 〈호리컨〉의 물줄기 사이를 지키다가, 마침내 〈캐나다〉 개울들의 샘에 닿아 그걸 타고 프랑스 놈들 땅 한복판으로 들어가리라는 건, 오랜 세월의 판단이 없어도 알 만한 일이었소."], ["Yet here are we, within a short range of the Scaroon, and not a sign of a trail have we crossed!", "헌데 우리가 지금 〈스카룬〉에서 멀지 않은 데까지 왔는데도, 흔적 하나 가로질러 본 적이 없단 말이오!"], ["Human natur' is weak, and it is possible we may not have taken the proper scent.\"", "인간의 본성이란 약한 법이라, 어쩌면 우리가 제 냄새를 못 짚었는지도 모르오.\""]], [["\"Heaven protect us from such an error!\"", "\"하느님께서 그런 잘못으로부터 우릴 지켜 주시기를!\""], ["exclaimed Duncan.", "〈던컨〉이 외쳤다."], ["\"Let us retrace our steps, and examine as we go, with keener eyes.", "\"온 길을 되짚으며, 더 날카로운 눈으로 가면서 살펴봅시다."], ["Has Uncas no counsel to offer in such a strait?\"", "〈언카스〉는 이런 궁지에 내놓을 조언이 없소?\""]], [["The young Mohican cast a glance at his father, but maintaining his quiet and reserved mien, he continued silent.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉은 제 아비에게 눈길을 던졌으나, 조용하고 삼가는 태도를 지킨 채 잠자코 있었다."], ["Chingachgook had caught the look, and motioning with his hand, he bade him speak.", "〈칭가치국〉이 그 눈길을 알아채고는, 손짓으로 말하라 일렀다."], ["The moment this permission was accorded, the countenance of Uncas changed from its grave composure to a gleam of intelligence and joy.", "이 허락이 떨어진 순간, 〈언카스〉의 표정은 엄숙한 평정에서 명민함과 기쁨이 번득이는 얼굴로 바뀌었다."], ["Bounding forward like a deer, he sprang up the side of a little acclivity, a few rods in advance, and stood exultingly over a spot of fresh earth that looked as though it had been recently upturned by the passage of some heavy animal.", "사슴처럼 펄쩍 뛰어 앞으로 나아가, 그는 몇 로드 앞 작은 비탈을 단숨에 올라, 어떤 무거운 짐승이 지나가며 갓 파헤친 듯한 신선한 흙더미 위에 의기양양하게 섰다."], ["The eyes of the whole party followed the unexpected movement, and read their success in the air of triumph that the youth assumed.", "일행 모두의 눈이 이 뜻밖의 움직임을 좇았고, 그 젊은이가 짓는 의기양양한 표정에서 그들의 성공을 읽었다."]], [["\"'Tis the trail!\"", "\"흔적이다!\""], ["exclaimed the scout, advancing to the spot: \"the lad is quick of sight and keen of wit for his years.\"", "척후가 그 자리로 다가서며 외쳤다. \"이 녀석은 나이에 비해 눈이 빠르고 머리가 예리하군.\""]], [["\"'Tis extraordinary that he should have withheld his knowledge so long,\"", "\"이렇게 오래도록 제가 아는 걸 감춰 두다니 별나구려.\""], ["muttered Duncan, at his elbow.", "〈던컨〉이 그의 곁에서 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"It would have been more wonderful had he spoken without a bidding.", "\"분부도 없이 입을 열었다면 그게 더 놀라운 일이었을 게요. 아니지, 아니야."], ["No, no; your young white, who gathers his learning from books and can measure what he knows by the page, may conceit that his knowledge, like his legs, outruns that of his father; but where experience is the master, the scholar is made to know the value of years, and respects them accordingly.\"", "책에서 배움을 얻고 제가 아는 것을 책장 수로나 헤아리는 그대들 젊은 백인이야, 제 지식이 제 다리처럼 아비의 것을 앞지른다고 자만할 수도 있겠지. 허나 경험이 스승인 곳에서는, 배우는 자가 세월의 값어치를 알게 되고, 그에 걸맞게 그걸 존중하는 법이라오.\""]], [["\"See!\"", "\"보십시오!\""], ["said Uncas, pointing north and south, at the evident marks of the broad trail on either side of him: \"the dark-hair has gone towards the frost.\"", "〈언카스〉가 제 양쪽으로 또렷이 난 넓은 흔적의 자국을 가리키며 북쪽과 남쪽을 짚어 말했다. \"검은 머리는 서리 쪽으로 갔습니다.\""]], [["\"Hound never ran on a more beautiful scent,\"", "\"사냥개도 이보다 고운 냄새를 좇아 달린 적은 없을 게요.\""], ["responded the scout, dashing forward, at once, on the indicated route; \"we are favored, greatly favored, and can follow with high noses.", "척후가 곧장 그 가리킨 길로 내달으며 응답했다. \"우린 은혜를 입었소, 큰 은혜를 입었으니, 코를 높이 들고 좇을 수 있겠소."], ["Ay, here are both your waddling beasts: this Huron travels like a white general.", "그래, 여기 그대들의 뒤뚱대는 짐승 두 마리가 다 있군. 이 〈휴런〉은 꼭 백인 장군처럼 다니는구려."], ["The fellow is stricken with a judgment, and is mad!", "이놈은 천벌이 내려 미친 게요!"], ["Look sharp for wheels, Sagamore,\"", "바퀴 자국을 잘 살피게, 〈사가모어〉.\""], ["he continued, looking back, and laughing in his newly awakened satisfaction; \"we shall soon have the fool journeying in a coach, and that with three of the best pair of eyes on the borders, in his rear.\"", "그가 뒤를 돌아보며, 새로 깨어난 만족감에 웃음을 터뜨리며 이었다. \"조만간 저 멍청이가 마차를 타고 다니는 꼴을 보겠소, 그것도 변경에서 가장 뛰어난 눈 세 쌍을 등 뒤에 달고서 말이오.\""]], [["The spirits of the scout, and the astonishing success of the chase, in which a circuitous distance of more than forty miles had been passed, did not fail to impart a portion of hope to the whole party.", "척후의 들뜬 기분과, 사십 마일이 넘는 우회의 거리를 지나온 이 추적의 놀라운 성공은, 일행 전체에게도 어느 정도 희망을 나누어 주지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Their advance was rapid; and made with as much confidence as a traveller would proceed along a wide highway.", "그들의 전진은 빨랐고, 여행자가 넓은 한길을 따라 나아가듯 그만한 자신감으로 이루어졌다."], ["If a rock, or a rivulet, or a bit of earth harder than common, severed the links of the clue they followed, the true eye of the scout recovered them at a distance, and seldom rendered the delay of a single moment necessary.", "바위나 시내, 혹은 여느 것보다 단단한 흙뙈기가 그들이 좇는 단서의 고리를 끊어 놓으면, 척후의 정확한 눈이 멀찍이서 그것을 되찾았고, 단 한순간의 지체조차 좀처럼 필요로 하지 않았다."], ["Their progress was much facilitated by the certainty that Magua had found it necessary to journey through the valleys; a circumstance which rendered the general direction of the route sure.", "〈마구아〉가 골짜기를 통해 여행하기를 어쩔 수 없이 택했다는 확신은 그들의 진행을 크게 도왔으니, 이 사정이 노정의 전반적 방향을 확실하게 해 주었기 때문이다."], ["Nor had the Huron entirely neglected the arts uniformly practised by the natives when retiring in front of any enemy.", "그렇다고 그 〈휴런〉이 어떤 적 앞에서 물러설 때 원주민들이 한결같이 쓰는 술수를 아주 게을리한 것도 아니었다."], ["False trails, and sudden turnings, were frequent, wherever a brook, or the formation of the ground, rendered them feasible; but his pursuers were rarely deceived, and never failed to detect their error, before they had lost either time or distance on the deceptive track.", "개울이나 지형이 그럴 만한 곳이면 어디든 거짓 흔적과 느닷없는 방향 전환이 잦았으나, 추격자들은 좀처럼 속지 않았고, 그 기만의 자취에서 시간이나 거리를 잃기 전에 어김없이 잘못을 가려냈다."]], [["By the middle of the afternoon they had passed the Scaroon, and were following the route of the declining sun.", "오후가 한가운데에 이를 무렵 그들은 〈스카룬〉을 지났고, 기우는 해의 방향을 좇고 있었다."], ["After descending an eminence to a low bottom, through which a stream glided, they suddenly came to a place where the party of Le Renard had made a halt.", "어느 높은 언덕을 내려와 시내가 미끄러져 흐르는 나지막한 골짜기에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 느닷없이 〈르나르〉의 일행이 멈춰 쉬었던 자리에 닿았다."], ["Extinguished brands were lying around a spring, the offals of a deer were scattered about the place, and the trees bore evident marks of having been browsed by the horses.", "꺼진 장작이 샘 둘레에 흩어져 있었고, 사슴의 내장이 그 자리 여기저기에 널려 있었으며, 나무들은 말들이 뜯어 먹은 흔적을 또렷이 지니고 있었다."], ["At a little distance, Heyward discovered, and contemplated with tender emotion, the small bower under which he was fain to believe that Cora and Alice had reposed.", "조금 떨어진 곳에서 〈헤이워드〉는 〈코라〉와 〈앨리스〉가 쉬었으리라 믿고 싶은 작은 나뭇가지 그늘막을 발견하고는 애틋한 감정으로 바라보았다."], ["But while the earth was trodden, and the footsteps of both men and beasts were so plainly visible around the place, the trail appeared to have suddenly ended.", "그러나 땅은 밟혀 있었고 사람과 짐승의 발자국이 그 자리 둘레에 그토록 또렷이 보였음에도, 흔적은 느닷없이 끊긴 듯했다."]], [["It was easy to follow the track of the Narragansetts, but they seemed only to have wandered without guides, or any other object than the pursuit of food.", "〈내러갠셋〉 말들의 자취는 좇기 쉬웠으나, 그것들은 길잡이도 없이, 먹이를 찾는 것 말고는 다른 목적도 없이 그저 헤매 다닌 듯할 뿐이었다."], ["At length Uncas, who, with his father, had endeavored to trace the route of the horses, came upon a sign of their presence that was quite recent.", "마침내 제 아비와 더불어 말들의 노정을 더듬으려 애쓰던 〈언카스〉가, 자못 최근의 것인 그것들의 흔적을 찾아냈다."], ["Before following the clue, he communicated his success to his companions; and while the latter were consulting on the circumstance, the youth reappeared, leading the two fillies, with their saddles broken, and the housings soiled, as though they had been permitted to run at will for several days.", "그 단서를 좇기에 앞서, 그는 동료들에게 제 성과를 알렸고, 그들이 그 정황을 두고 의논하는 동안, 그 젊은이가 안장이 부서지고 안장깔개가 더럽혀진 두 어린 암말을 끌고 다시 나타났으니, 말들은 며칠 동안 멋대로 돌아다니도록 내버려진 듯한 꼴이었다."]], [["\"What should this mean?\"", "\"이게 무슨 뜻일까요?\""], ["said Duncan, turning pale, and glancing his eyes around him, as if he feared the brush and leaves were about to give up some horrid secret.", "〈던컨〉이 창백해진 얼굴로, 마치 덤불과 나뭇잎이 무슨 끔찍한 비밀을 토해 낼까 두려운 듯 사방으로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["\"That our march is come to a quick end, and that we are in an enemy's country,\"", "\"우리 행군이 곧 끝났다는 것, 그리고 우리가 적의 땅에 들어와 있다는 뜻이오.\""], ["returned the scout.", "척후가 대답했다."], ["\"Had the knaves been pressed, and the gentle ones wanted horses to keep up with the party, he might have taken their scalps; but without an enemy at his heels, and with such rugged beasts as these, he would not hurt a hair of their heads.", "\"그 악당들이 쫓기는 처지였고 그 가녀린 분들이 일행을 따라가는 데 말이 필요했다면, 그자는 머리 가죽을 벗겨 버렸을지도 모르오. 허나 뒤를 쫓는 적도 없는 데다 저런 거친 짐승들을 데리고서라면, 그자는 두 분 머리카락 한 올도 다치게 하지 않을 게요."], ["I know your thoughts, and shame be it to our color that you have reason for them; but he who thinks that even a Mingo would ill-treat a woman, unless it be to tomahawk her, knows nothing of Indian natur', or the laws of the woods.", "그대 속마음을 내가 아오, 그리고 그대에게 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다는 게 우리 피부빛에 부끄러운 일이긴 하오. 허나 〈밍고〉조차 도끼로 찍어 죽이려는 게 아닌 다음에야 여인을 함부로 대하리라 여기는 자는, 인디언의 본성도, 숲의 법도도 도무지 모르는 자요."], ["No, no; I have heard that the French Indians had come into these hills, to hunt the moose, and we are getting within scent of their camp.", "아니지, 아니야. 내 듣자 하니 프랑스 편 인디언들이 사슴을 사냥하러 이 산중에 들어왔다 하더군, 그리고 우린 그자들 진영의 냄새가 닿는 데까지 다가가고 있소."], ["Why should they not?", "그러지 말란 법이 있겠소?"], ["the morning and evening guns of Ty may be heard any day among these mountains; for the Frenchers are running a new line atween the provinces of the king and the Canadas.", "〈타이〉의 아침저녁 대포 소리가 이 산중에서 날마다 들리니 말이오. 프랑스 놈들이 왕의 영토와 〈캐나다〉 사이에 새 경계선을 긋고 있으니까."], ["It is true that the horses are here, but the Hurons are gone; let us then hunt for the path by which they departed.\"", "말들이 여기 있는 건 사실이나, 〈휴런〉들은 떠나 버렸소. 그러니 그자들이 떠난 길을 찾아봅시다.\""]], [["Hawkeye and the Mohicans now applied themselves to their task in good earnest.", "이제 〈호크아이〉와 두 〈모히칸〉은 본격적으로 그 일에 매달렸다."], ["A circle of a few hundred feet in circumference was drawn, and each of the party took a segment for his portion.", "둘레가 수백 피트쯤 되는 원이 그려졌고, 일행은 저마다 그 한 자락씩을 제 몫으로 맡았다."], ["The examination, however, resulted in no discovery.", "그러나 살핀 결과 아무것도 찾아내지 못했다."], ["The impressions of footsteps were numerous, but they all appeared like those of men who had wandered about the spot, without any design to quit it.", "발자국의 자취는 수없이 많았으나, 모두 그 자리를 떠날 뜻 없이 그 주위를 헤매 다닌 자들의 것처럼 보였다."], ["Again the scout and his companions made the circuit of the halting-place, each slowly following the other, until they assembled in the centre once more, no wiser than when they started.", "다시 척후와 그의 동료들은 멈춰 쉰 자리를 한 바퀴 돌았으니, 저마다 천천히 앞사람을 좇다가 다시 한가운데에 모였으나, 출발할 때보다 조금도 더 알아낸 바가 없었다."]], [["\"Such cunning is not without its deviltry,\"", "\"이런 교활함에는 악마의 솜씨가 깃들지 않았을 리 없지.\""], ["exclaimed Hawkeye, when he met the disappointed looks of his assistants.", "〈호크아이〉가 조력자들의 실망한 낯빛과 마주치자 외쳤다."]], [["\"We must get down to it, Sagamore, beginning at the spring, and going over the ground by inches.", "\"우린 이걸 끝까지 파고들어야 하오, 〈사가모어〉, 샘에서 시작해서 한 치 한 치 땅을 훑어 갑시다."], ["The Huron shall never brag in his tribe that he has a foot which leaves no print.\"", "그 〈휴런〉이 제 부족에게 자국을 남기지 않는 발을 가졌노라 떠벌리게 두진 않을 거요.\""]], [["Setting the example himself, the scout engaged in the scrutiny with renewed zeal.", "스스로 본을 보이며, 척후는 새로워진 열의로 정밀 조사에 나섰다."], ["Not a leaf was left unturned.", "뒤집히지 않은 잎 하나 없었다."], ["The sticks were removed, and the stones lifted; for Indian cunning was known frequently to adopt these objects as covers, laboring with the utmost patience and industry, to conceal each footstep as they proceeded.", "막대기를 치우고 돌을 들어 올렸으니, 인디언의 교활함이 흔히 이런 것들을 가림막으로 삼아, 더없는 인내와 부지런함으로 나아가며 발자국 하나하나를 감추곤 한다고 알려져 있었기 때문이다."], ["Still no discovery was made.", "그래도 아무것도 찾아내지 못했다."], ["At length Uncas, whose activity had enabled him to achieve his portion of the task the soonest, raked the earth across the turbid little rill which ran from the spring, and diverted its course into another channel.", "마침내 그 민첩함 덕에 제 몫의 일을 가장 먼저 끝낸 〈언카스〉가, 샘에서 흘러나오는 흐릿한 작은 도랑을 가로질러 흙을 긁어모아, 그 물길을 다른 골로 돌려놓았다."], ["So soon as its narrow bed below the dam was dry, he stooped over it with keen and curious eyes.", "둑 아래 좁은 바닥이 마르자마자, 그는 날카롭고 호기심 어린 눈으로 그 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["A cry of exultation immediately announced the success of the young warrior.", "곧이어 터진 환호성이 그 젊은 전사의 성공을 알렸다."], ["The whole party crowded to the spot where Uncas pointed out the impression of a moccasin in the moist alluvion.", "일행 모두가 〈언카스〉가 가리킨, 축축한 충적토에 찍힌 모카신 자국이 있는 자리로 몰려들었다."]], [["\"The lad will be an honor to his people,\"", "\"이 녀석은 제 겨레의 영예가 될 게요.\""], ["said Hawkeye, regarding the trail with as much admiration as a naturalist would expend on the tusk of a mammoth or the rib of a mastodon; \"ay, and a thorn in the sides of the Hurons.", "〈호크아이〉가, 박물학자가 매머드의 어금니나 마스토돈의 갈비뼈에나 쏟을 법한 감탄으로 그 흔적을 바라보며 말했다. \"그래, 그리고 〈휴런〉들의 옆구리에 박힌 가시가 될 게요."], ["Yet that is not the footstep of an Indian!", "헌데 이건 인디언의 발자국이 아니로군!"], ["the weight is too much on the heel, and the toes are squared, as though one of the French dancers had been in, pigeon-winging his tribe!", "무게가 발뒤꿈치에 지나치게 실렸고, 발끝이 네모졌으니, 마치 프랑스 무용수 하나가 끼어들어 제 부족 앞에서 비둘기 날갯짓 춤이라도 춘 것 같소!"], ["Run back, Uncas, and bring me the size of the singer's foot.", "〈언카스〉, 도로 뛰어가서 그 노래꾼 발 크기를 재 오너라."], ["You will find a beautiful print of it just opposite yon rock, agin the hillside.\"", "저기 바위 맞은편, 산비탈에 그 고운 자국이 찍혀 있을 게다.\""]], [["While the youth was engaged in this commission, the scout and Chingachgook were attentively considering the impressions.", "그 젊은이가 이 심부름에 나선 동안, 척후와 〈칭가치국〉은 그 자국들을 유심히 살폈다."], ["The measurements agreed, and the former unhesitatingly pronounced that the footstep was that of David, who had once more, been made to exchange his shoes for moccasins.", "치수가 맞아떨어졌고, 척후는 망설임 없이 그것이 〈데이비드〉의 발자국이라 단언했으니, 그가 또 한 번 신을 모카신으로 바꿔 신게 된 것이었다."]], [["\"I can now read the whole of it, as plainly as if I had seen the arts of Le Subtil,\"", "\"이제 마치 〈르 쉬빌〉의 술수를 직접 본 듯 그 전모를 똑똑히 읽을 수 있소.\""], ["he added; \"the singer, being a man whose gifts lay chiefly in his throat and feet, was made to go first, and the others have trod in his steps, imitating their formation.\"", "그가 덧붙였다. \"그 노래꾼은 그 재주가 주로 목구멍과 두 발에 있는 자라, 앞장세워졌고, 나머지는 그 발자국 모양을 흉내 내며 그의 자취를 밟은 것이오.\""]], [["\"But,\"", "\"하지만,\""], ["cried Duncan, \"I see no signs of--\"", "〈던컨〉이 외쳤다, \"두 분의 흔적은 도무지 보이지 않는데——\""]], [["\"The gentle ones,\"", "\"그 가녀린 분들 말이오.\""], ["interrupted the scout; \"the varlet has found a way to carry them, until he supposed he had thrown any followers off the scent.", "척후가 가로막았다. \"그 악당이 두 분을 나르는 수를 찾아낸 거요, 어떤 추격자든 냄새를 떨쳐 냈다 여겨질 때까지 말이오."], ["My life on it, we see their pretty little feet again, before many rods go by.\"", "내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 몇 로드 가기 전에 우린 두 분의 곱고 작은 발자국을 다시 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["The whole party now proceeded, following the course of the rill, keeping anxious eyes on the regular impressions.", "이제 일행 모두가 그 도랑의 흐름을 좇아, 규칙적인 자국들에 근심 어린 눈을 둔 채 나아갔다."], ["The water soon flowed into its bed again, but watching the ground on either side, the foresters pursued their way content with knowing that the trail lay beneath.", "물은 곧 다시 제 바닥으로 흘러들었으나, 양쪽 땅을 살피며 그 숲의 사내들은, 흔적이 그 아래에 있음을 아는 것으로 만족하며 길을 갔다."], ["More than half a mile was passed, before the rill rippled close around the base of an extensive and dry rock.", "반 마일이 넘게 지나서야, 그 도랑이 넓고 메마른 바위의 밑동을 바싹 끼고 잔물결을 일으켰다."], ["Here they paused to make sure that the Hurons had not quitted the water.", "여기서 그들은 〈휴런〉들이 물에서 벗어나지 않았음을 확실히 하려고 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["It was fortunate they did so.", "그리한 것은 다행이었다."], ["For the quick and active Uncas soon found the impression of a foot on a bunch of moss, where it would seem an Indian had inadvertently trodden.", "재빠르고 민첩한 〈언카스〉가 곧, 인디언 하나가 무심코 밟은 듯한 이끼 덤불 위의 발자국을 찾아냈기 때문이다."], ["Pursuing the direction given by this discovery, he entered the neighboring thicket, and struck the trail, as fresh and obvious as it had been before they reached the spring.", "이 발견이 일러 준 방향을 좇아, 그는 이웃한 덤불로 들어가, 샘에 닿기 전과 다름없이 신선하고 또렷한 흔적을 짚어 냈다."], ["Another shout announced the good fortune of the youth to his companions, and at once terminated the search.", "또 한 번의 외침이 그 젊은이의 행운을 동료들에게 알리며, 단번에 수색을 끝냈다."]], [["\"Ay, it has been planned with Indian judgment,\"", "\"그래, 인디언의 판단으로 짜낸 계책이었소.\""], ["said the scout, when the party was assembled around the place; \"and would have blinded white eyes.\"", "일행이 그 자리에 모여들자 척후가 말했다. \"백인의 눈쯤은 멀게 했을 거요.\""]], [["\"Shall we proceed?\"", "\"이제 가도 되겠소?\""], ["demanded Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 물었다."]], [["\"Softly, softly: we know our path; but it is good to examine the formation of things.", "\"천천히, 천천히. 우리 길은 알았소만, 사물의 짜임새를 살펴보는 게 좋소."], ["This is my schooling, major; and if one neglects the book, there is little chance of learning from the open hand of Providence.", "이게 내 배움이오, 소령. 그리고 그 책을 게을리하면, 섭리의 펼쳐진 손에서 배울 기회는 거의 없는 법이오."], ["All is plain but one thing, which is the manner that the knave contrived to get the gentle ones along the blind trail.", "다 분명한데 한 가지만 그렇지 않으니, 그 악당이 이 보이지 않는 흔적을 따라 그 가녀린 분들을 어떻게 데려갔는가 하는 거요."], ["Even a Huron would be too proud to let their tender feet touch the water.\"", "〈휴런〉이라도 그 여린 발을 물에 닿게 하기엔 자존심이 너무 셀 텐데 말이오.\""]], [["\"Will this assist in explaining the difficulty?\"", "\"이게 그 의문을 푸는 데 도움이 되겠소?\""], ["said Heyward, pointing towards the fragments of a sort of handbarrow, that had been rudely constructed of boughs, and bound together with withes, and which now seemed carelessly cast aside as useless.", "〈헤이워드〉가, 나뭇가지로 거칠게 엮고 가는 버들로 동여매어 만든 일종의 들것 조각을 가리키며 말했으니, 그것은 이제 쓸모없어진 듯 아무렇게나 내버려진 것처럼 보였다."]], [["\"'Tis explained!\"", "\"풀렸소!\""], ["cried the delighted Hawkeye.", "기뻐하며 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다."], ["\"If them varlets have passed a minute, they have spent hours in striving to fabricate a lying end to their trail!", "\"그 악당들이 일 분이라도 묵었다면, 제 흔적에 거짓 끝을 지어내려 몇 시간은 들였을 게요!"], ["Well, I've known them to waste a day in the same manner, to as little purpose.", "그래, 나도 그자들이 이런 짓으로 하루를 허비하고도 별 소득 없는 꼴을 본 적이 있소."], ["Here we have three pair of moccasins, and two of little feet.", "여기 모카신 자국이 세 쌍, 그중 둘은 작은 발이오."], ["It is amazing that any mortal beings can journey on limbs so small!", "인간이 이토록 작은 사지로 여행할 수 있다니 놀랍구려!"], ["Pass me the thong of buckskin, Uncas, and let me take the length of this foot.", "〈언카스〉, 사슴 가죽 끈을 다오, 이 발 길이를 좀 재 보게."], ["By the Lord, it is no longer than a child's and yet the maidens are tall and comely.", "맙소사, 아이의 것보다 길지도 않은데, 그 처녀들은 키도 크고 곱지 않은가."], ["That Providence is partial in its gifts, for its own wise reasons, the best and most contented of us must allow.\"", "섭리가 그 나름의 현명한 까닭으로 베푸는 데 편파적이라는 것은, 우리 가운데 가장 선하고 가장 자족하는 자라도 인정할 수밖에 없는 일이오.\""]], [["\"The tender limbs of my daughters are unequal to these hardships,\"", "\"내 딸들의 여린 사지가 이런 고초를 견딜 만하지 못하오.\""], ["said Munro, looking at the light footsteps of his children, with a parent's love: \"we shall find their fainting forms in this desert.\"", "〈먼로〉가 제 아이들의 가벼운 발자국을 아비의 사랑으로 바라보며 말했다. \"우린 이 황야에서 기진맥진한 그 애들의 모습을 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["\"Of that there is little cause of fear,\"", "\"그건 두려워할 까닭이 거의 없소.\""], ["returned the scout, slowly shaking his head; \"this is a firm and straight, though a light step, and not over long.", "척후가 천천히 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"이건 가볍긴 해도 단단하고 곧은 걸음이고, 보폭이 그리 길지도 않소."], ["See, the heel has hardly touched the ground; and there the dark-hair has made a little jump, from root to root.", "보시오, 발뒤꿈치가 땅에 거의 닿지도 않았소. 그리고 저기서 검은 머리가 뿌리에서 뿌리로 작게 폴짝 뛰었소."], ["No, no; my knowledge for it, neither of them was nigh fainting, hereaway.", "아니지, 아니야. 내 지식을 걸고 말하건대, 이 근처에서 둘 중 누구도 기절할 지경은 아니었소."], ["Now, the singer was beginning to be foot-sore and leg-weary as is plain by his trail.", "헌데 그 노래꾼은 발이 부르트고 다리가 지쳐 가고 있었으니, 그 자취를 보면 분명하오."], ["There, you see, he slipped; here he has travelled wide, and tottered; and there, again, it looks as though he journeyed on snow-shoes.", "저기, 보시오, 그가 미끄러졌소. 여기선 갈팡질팡 비틀거렸고, 또 저기선 마치 눈신을 신고 걸은 것 같소."], ["Ay, ay, a man who uses his throat altogether, can hardly give his legs a proper training.\"", "그래, 그래, 온통 제 목구멍만 쓰는 사내는 제 다리를 제대로 단련하기 어려운 법이라오.\""]], [["From such undeniable testimony did the practised woodsman arrive at the truth, with nearly as much certainty and precision as if he had been a witness of all those events which his ingenuity so easily elucidated.", "이렇듯 부인할 수 없는 증거로부터, 그 노련한 숲의 사내는, 마치 제 재간으로 그토록 쉽게 풀어낸 그 모든 사건을 직접 목격한 증인이기라도 한 양, 거의 그만한 확실성과 정확성으로 진상에 다다랐다."], ["Cheered by these assurances, and satisfied by a reasoning that was so obvious, while it was so simple, the party resumed its course, after making a short halt to take a hurried repast.", "이 단언들에 힘을 얻고, 그토록 단순하면서도 그토록 명백한 추론에 흡족해진 일행은, 서둘러 끼니를 들고자 잠시 멈춰 쉰 뒤 다시 길을 이었다."]], [["When the meal was ended, the scout cast a glance upwards at the setting sun, and pushed forward with a rapidity which compelled Heyward and the still vigorous Munro to exert all their muscles to equal.", "식사가 끝나자, 척후는 기우는 해를 흘긋 올려다보고는, 〈헤이워드〉와 여전히 정정한 〈먼로〉가 따라잡으려 온 근육을 다 써야 할 만큼 빠른 속도로 밀고 나아갔다."], ["Their route now lay along the bottom which had already been mentioned.", "이제 그들의 노정은 앞서 말한 골짜기를 따라 났다."], ["As the Hurons had made no further efforts to conceal their footsteps, the progress of the pursuers was no longer delayed by uncertainty.", "〈휴런〉들이 더는 제 발자국을 감추려 애쓰지 않았으므로, 추격자들의 진행은 더 이상 불확실함으로 지체되지 않았다."], ["Before an hour had elapsed, however, the speed of Hawkeye sensibly abated, and his head, instead of maintaining its former direct and forward look, began to turn suspiciously from side to side, as if he were conscious of approaching danger.", "그러나 한 시간이 채 지나기도 전에, 〈호크아이〉의 속도가 눈에 띄게 누그러졌고, 그의 머리는 전처럼 곧장 앞을 향하는 대신, 마치 다가오는 위험을 의식한 듯 이쪽저쪽으로 의심스레 돌기 시작했다."], ["He soon stopped again, and waited for the whole party to come up.", "그는 곧 다시 멈춰 서서, 일행 전부가 따라붙기를 기다렸다."]], [["\"I scent the Hurons,\"", "\"〈휴런〉들의 냄새가 나오.\""], ["he said, speaking to the Mohicans; \"yonder is open sky, through the tree-tops, and we are getting too nigh their encampment.", "그가 두 〈모히칸〉에게 말했다. \"저기 나무 우듬지 너머로 트인 하늘이 있고, 우린 그자들 진영에 너무 가까이 다가가고 있소."], ["Sagamore, you will take the hillside, to the right; Uncas will bend along the brook to the left, while I will try the trail.", "〈사가모어〉, 그대는 오른편 산비탈을 맡으시오. 〈언카스〉는 왼편 개울을 따라 굽이쳐 가고, 나는 흔적을 따라가 보겠소."], ["If anything should happen, the call will be three croaks of a crow.", "무슨 일이 생기면, 신호는 까마귀 울음 세 번이오."], ["I saw one of the birds fanning himself in the air, just beyond the dead oak--another sign that we are touching an encampment.\"", "방금 저 죽은 떡갈나무 너머 공중에서 그 새 한 마리가 제 깃을 부치는 걸 봤소——그것도 우리가 진영에 닿아 가고 있다는 또 하나의 징표요.\""]], [["The Indians departed their several ways without reply, while Hawkeye cautiously proceeded with the two gentlemen.", "인디언들은 대꾸 없이 저마다 제 길로 떠났고, 〈호크아이〉는 두 신사와 함께 조심스레 나아갔다."], ["Heyward soon pressed to the side of their guide, eager to catch an early glimpse of those enemies he had pursued with so much toil and anxiety.", "〈헤이워드〉는 그토록 큰 노고와 근심으로 좇아온 그 적들을 한시바삐 엿보고 싶은 마음에, 곧 길잡이의 곁으로 바싹 다가섰다."], ["His companion told him to steal to the edge of the wood, which, as usual, was fringed with a thicket, and wait his coming, for he wished to examine certain suspicious signs a little on one side.", "그의 동행은, 여느 때처럼 덤불로 가장자리가 둘린 숲의 끄트머리로 살금살금 가서 자신이 올 때까지 기다리라 일렀으니, 한쪽 옆에서 미심쩍은 어떤 징표들을 좀 살펴보고 싶었기 때문이다."], ["Duncan obeyed, and soon found himself in a situation to command a view which he found as extraordinary as it was novel.", "〈던컨〉은 시키는 대로 했고, 곧 새롭기도 하거니와 예사롭지 않기도 한 광경을 한눈에 굽어볼 수 있는 자리에 서게 되었다."]], [["The trees of many acres had been felled, and the glow of a mild summer's evening had fallen on the clearing, in beautiful contrast to the gray light of the forest.", "수 에이커에 걸쳐 나무가 베어 넘겨져 있었고, 온화한 여름 저녁의 노을빛이 그 개간지 위로 내려앉아, 숲의 잿빛과 아름다운 대조를 이루었다."], ["A short distance from the place where Duncan stood, the stream had seemingly expanded into a little lake, covering most of the low land, from mountain to mountain.", "〈던컨〉이 선 자리에서 얼마 떨어지지 않은 곳에서, 시내는 작은 호수로 넓어진 듯, 산에서 산까지 그 낮은 땅 대부분을 뒤덮고 있었다."], ["The water fell out of this wide basin, in a cataract so regular and gentle, that it appeared rather to be the work of human hands, than fashioned by nature.", "물은 이 너른 웅덩이에서 흘러나가 폭포를 이루었는데, 어찌나 고르고 부드럽던지, 자연이 빚었다기보다 차라리 사람의 손이 만든 것처럼 보였다."], ["A hundred earthen dwellings stood on the margin of the lake, and even in its water, as though the latter had overflowed its usual banks.", "흙으로 지은 백여 채의 집이 호숫가에, 심지어 그 물속에까지 서 있었으니, 마치 물이 여느 둑을 넘어 불어난 것 같았다."], ["Their rounded roofs, admirably moulded for defence against the weather, denoted more of industry and foresight than the natives were wont to bestow on their regular habitations, much less on those they occupied for the temporary purposes of hunting and war.", "비바람을 막기에 절묘하게 빚어진 그 둥근 지붕들은, 원주민들이 늘 머무는 거처에, 더구나 사냥과 전쟁이라는 일시적인 목적으로 차지하는 거처에 좀처럼 들이지 않던 부지런함과 선견을 드러냈다."], ["In short, the whole village or town, whichever it might be termed, possessed more of method and neatness of execution, than the white men had been accustomed to believe belonged, ordinarily, to the Indian habits.", "요컨대, 마을이라 부르든 읍이라 부르든, 그 전체는 백인들이 으레 인디언의 습속에 속한다고 믿어 온 것보다 더 많은 짜임새와 솜씨의 단정함을 지니고 있었다."], ["It appeared, however, to be deserted.", "그러나 그곳은 버려진 듯 보였다."], ["At least, so thought Duncan for many minutes; but, at length, he fancied he discovered several human forms advancing towards him on all fours, and apparently dragging in their train some heavy, and as he was quick to apprehend, some formidable engine.", "적어도 〈던컨〉은 여러 분 동안 그렇게 여겼다. 그러나 마침내, 여러 사람의 형체가 네 발로 그를 향해 다가오며, 보아하니 무언가 무거운——그리고 그가 재빨리 알아챈 바로는 무언가 가공할——기계를 끌고 오는 것을 발견한 듯했다."], ["Just then a few dark looking heads gleamed out of the dwellings, and the place seemed suddenly alive with beings, which, however, glided from cover to cover so swiftly, as to allow no opportunity of examining their humors or pursuits.", "바로 그때 거뭇한 머리 몇몇이 집들에서 번뜩였고, 그곳은 갑자기 어떤 존재들로 살아 움직이는 듯했으니, 다만 그것들이 너무도 잽싸게 이 가림막에서 저 가림막으로 미끄러져 다니는 탓에, 그 기질이나 행위를 살필 틈이 도무지 없었다."], ["Alarmed at these suspicious and inexplicable movements, he was about to attempt the signal of the crows, when the rustling of leaves at hand drew his eyes in another direction.", "이 미심쩍고 알 수 없는 움직임에 놀란 그는, 막 까마귀 신호를 보내려던 참이었는데, 가까이서 잎이 바스락거리는 소리에 그의 눈길이 다른 쪽으로 끌렸다."]], [["The young man started, and recoiled a few paces instinctively, when he found himself within a hundred yards of a stranger Indian.", "그 젊은이는 흠칫하며 본능적으로 몇 걸음 뒷걸음쳤으니, 자신이 낯선 인디언 하나와 백 야드도 안 되는 거리에 있음을 알아차렸기 때문이다."], ["Recovering his recollection on the instant, instead of sounding an alarm, which might prove fatal to himself, he remained stationary, an attentive observer of the other's motions.", "그는 곧 정신을 가다듬고, 자칫 제 목숨에 치명적일 수도 있는 경보를 울리는 대신, 그 자리에 멈춰 선 채 상대의 움직임을 주의 깊게 지켜보는 관찰자가 되었다."]], [["An instant of calm observation served to assure Duncan that he was undiscovered.", "잠시 차분히 살핀 끝에 〈던컨〉은 자신이 들키지 않았음을 확신했다."], ["The native, like himself, seemed occupied in considering the low dwellings of the village, and the stolen movements of its inhabitants.", "그 원주민도 그처럼 마을의 나지막한 집들과 주민들의 은밀한 움직임을 살피는 데 골몰한 듯했다."], ["It was impossible to discover the expression of his features, through the grotesque mask of paint under which they were concealed; though Duncan fancied it was rather melancholy than savage.", "그 얼굴을 가린 기괴한 칠 가면 너머로 그 표정을 읽어 내기란 불가능했으나, 〈던컨〉은 그것이 사납다기보다 차라리 우울하다고 여겼다."], ["His head was shaved, as usual, with the exception of the crown, from whose tuft three or four faded feathers from a hawk's wing were loosely dangling.", "그의 머리는 여느 때처럼 박박 밀려 있었고, 다만 정수리만 남아, 그 다발에서 매의 날개에서 뽑은 빛바랜 깃털 서너 개가 느슨히 흔들렸다."], ["A ragged calico mantle half-encircled his body, while his nether garment was composed of an ordinary shirt, the sleeves of which were made to perform the office that is usually executed by a much more commodious arrangement.", "너덜너덜한 옥양목 망토가 그 몸을 절반쯤 둘렀고, 아랫도리는 평범한 셔츠로 되어 있었으니, 그 소매는 으레 훨씬 더 편리한 차림이 맡는 구실을 하도록 쓰였다."], ["His legs were bare, and sadly cut and torn by briers.", "다리는 맨살이었고, 가시덤불에 베이고 찢겨 보기 딱했다."], ["The feet were, however, covered with a pair of good deer-skin moccasins.", "그러나 발에는 좋은 사슴 가죽 모카신 한 켤레가 신겨 있었다."], ["Altogether, the appearance of the individual was forlorn and miserable.", "통틀어, 그 사람의 행색은 처량하고 비참했다."]], [["Duncan was still curiously observing the person of his neighbor, when the scout stole silently and cautiously to his side.", "〈던컨〉이 그 이웃한 자의 풍채를 여전히 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피고 있을 때, 척후가 소리 없이 조심스레 그의 곁으로 다가왔다."]], [["\"You see we have reached their settlement or encampment,\"", "\"보다시피 우리가 그자들의 거처, 아니 진영에 닿았소.\""], ["whispered the young man; \"and here is one of the savages himself, in a very embarrassing position for our further movements.\"", "젊은이가 속삭였다. \"그리고 여기 미개인 하나가, 우리가 더 움직이기에 자못 거북한 자리에 있구려.\""]], [["Hawkeye started, and dropped his rifle, directed by the finger of his companion, the stranger came under his view.", "〈호크아이〉가 흠칫하며 소총을 떨어뜨렸으나, 동행의 손가락이 가리키는 대로 그 낯선 자가 그의 시야에 들어왔다."], ["Then lowering the dangerous muzzle, he stretched forward his long neck, as if to assist a scrutiny that was already intensely keen.", "그러자 그 위험한 총구를 내리고, 그는 마치 이미 더없이 날카로운 관찰을 거들려는 듯, 긴 목을 앞으로 뺐다."]], [["\"The imp is not a Huron,\"", "\"이 마귀 같은 놈은 〈휴런〉이 아니오.\""], ["he said, \"nor of any of the Canada tribes; and yet you see, by his clothes, the knave has been plundering a white.", "그가 말했다, \"〈캐나다〉 부족 중 어느 것도 아니지. 헌데 보다시피, 옷차림으로 보아 이 악당이 백인 하나를 약탈한 게요."], ["Ay, Montcalm has raked the woods for his inroad, and a whooping, murdering set of varlets has he gathered together.", "그래, 〈몽칼름〉이 제 침공을 위해 숲을 샅샅이 훑어, 함성을 질러 대며 살육하는 악당들을 한데 그러모은 게요."], ["Can you see where he has put his rifle or his bow?\"", "그자가 제 소총이나 활을 어디 두었는지 보이오?\""]], [["\"He appears to have no arms; nor does he seem to be viciously inclined.", "\"무기는 없는 듯하고, 사납게 굴 기색도 없어 보이오."], ["Unless he communicate the alarm to his fellows, who as you see are dodging about the water, we have but little to fear from him.\"", "보다시피 물가를 요리조리 피해 다니는 제 동료들에게 경보를 알리지만 않는다면, 우리가 저자에게서 두려워할 건 거의 없소.\""]], [["The scout turned to Heyward, and regarded him a moment with unconcealed amazement.", "척후가 〈헤이워드〉를 돌아보며, 한순간 감추지 못한 놀라움으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["Then opening wide his mouth, he indulged in unrestrained and heartfelt laughter, though in that silent and peculiar manner which danger had so long taught him to practise.", "그러더니 입을 크게 벌리고, 거리낌 없는 진심 어린 웃음에 빠져들었으니, 다만 위험이 오래도록 그에게 가르쳐 온 그 소리 없고 독특한 방식이었다."]], [["Repeating the words, \"fellows who are dodging about the water!\"", "\"물가를 요리조리 피해 다니는 동료들이라!\" 그 말을 되뇌며 그가 덧붙였다."], ["he added, \"so much for schooling and passing a boyhood in the settlements!", "\"정착지에서 학교 다니고 어린 시절을 보낸 게 고작 이 꼴이구려!"], ["The knave has long legs, though, and shall not be trusted.", "허나 그 악당은 다리가 길어서, 믿을 수 없소."], ["Do you keep him under your rifle while I creep in behind, through the bush, and take him alive.", "그대는 그자를 소총 겨냥 아래 두고 있으시오, 내가 덤불을 헤치고 뒤로 기어들어 산 채로 잡을 테니."], ["Fire on no account.\"", "무슨 일이 있어도 쏘지는 마시오.\""]], [["Heyward had already permitted his companion to bury part of his person in the thicket, when, stretching forth an arm, he arrested him, in order to ask,--", "〈헤이워드〉는 이미 동행이 제 몸 일부를 덤불에 묻도록 내버려 두었다가, 한 팔을 뻗어 그를 붙잡아, 이렇게 물었다——"]], [["\"If I see you in danger, may I not risk a shot?\"", "\"그대가 위험에 처한 게 보이면, 한 발쯤 쏴도 되지 않겠소?\""]], [["Hawkeye regarded him a moment, like one who knew not how to take the question; then nodding his head, he answered, still laughing, though inaudibly,--", "〈호크아이〉가 한순간, 그 물음을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 모르는 자처럼 그를 바라보았다. 그러더니 고개를 끄덕이며, 여전히 소리 없이 웃으면서 대답했다——"]], [["\"Fire a whole platoon, major.\"", "\"한 소대를 통째로 쏘시오, 소령.\""]], [["In the next moment he was concealed by the leaves.", "다음 순간 그는 잎새에 가려 보이지 않았다."], ["Duncan waited several minutes in feverish impatience, before he caught another glimpse of the scout.", "〈던컨〉은 척후를 다시 한 번 엿보기까지 몇 분을 애타는 초조함 속에 기다렸다."], ["Then he reappeared, creeping along the earth, from which his dress was hardly distinguishable, directly in the rear of his intended captive.", "이윽고 그가 다시 나타났으니, 옷이 거의 분간되지 않을 만큼 땅에 바싹 붙어, 잡으려는 그 포로의 바로 뒤로 기어가고 있었다."], ["Having reached within a few yards of the latter, he arose to his feet, silently and slowly.", "그 뒤로 몇 야드 안에 이르자, 그는 소리 없이 천천히 몸을 일으켜 섰다."], ["At that instant, several loud blows were struck on the water, and Duncan turned his eyes just in time to perceive that a hundred dark forms were plunging, in a body, into the troubled little sheet.", "바로 그 순간, 물 위로 요란한 타격음이 여러 번 울렸고, 〈던컨〉이 눈을 돌리니 마침맞게, 백여 개의 거뭇한 형체가 한 덩어리로 그 일렁이는 작은 수면 속으로 뛰어드는 것이 보였다."], ["Grasping his rifle, his looks were again bent on the Indian near him.", "소총을 움켜쥐고, 그의 눈길은 다시 가까이 있는 인디언에게로 향했다."], ["Instead of taking the alarm, the unconscious savage stretched forward his neck, as if he also watched the movements about the gloomy lake, with a sort of silly curiosity.", "그 영문 모르는 미개인은 놀라기는커녕, 그도 일종의 어리석은 호기심으로 그 음울한 호수 주위의 움직임을 지켜보기라도 하듯 목을 앞으로 뺐다."], ["In the meantime, the uplifted hand of Hawkeye was above him.", "그동안 〈호크아이〉의 치켜든 손이 그 위에 있었다."], ["But, without any apparent reason, it was withdrawn, and its owner indulged in another long, though still silent, fit of merriment.", "그러나 이렇다 할 까닭도 없이 그 손이 거두어졌고, 그 임자는 또 한 번, 여전히 소리 없는 긴 웃음에 빠져들었다."], ["When the peculiar and hearty laughter of Hawkeye was ended, instead of grasping his victim by the throat, he tapped him lightly on the shoulder, and exclaimed aloud,--", "〈호크아이〉의 그 독특하고 진심 어린 웃음이 끝나자, 그는 제 먹잇감의 목을 움켜쥐는 대신, 그 어깨를 가볍게 톡톡 치며 큰 소리로 외쳤다——"]], [["\"How now, friend!", "\"어떤가, 친구!"], ["have you a mind to teach the beavers to sing?\"", "비버들에게 노래를 가르칠 셈인가?\""]], [["\"Even so,\"", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["was the ready answer.", "거침없는 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["\"It would seem that the Being that gave them power to improve his gifts so well, would not deny them voices to proclaim his praise.\"", "\"그들에게 그분의 선물을 그토록 잘 갈고닦을 힘을 주신 분이, 그분을 찬양할 목소리야 마다하시지 않을 듯하오만.\""]], [["_\"Bot._--Are we all met?\"", "보텀: 우리 다 모였소?"]], [["_\"Qui._--Pat--pat; and here's a marvellous Convenient place for our rehearsal.\"", "퀸스: 그래, 그래, 그래. 그리고 여기야말로 우리 연습을 하기에 기막히게 알맞은 자리로구먼."]], [["_Midsummer Night's Dream._", "「한여름 밤의 꿈」"]], [["The reader may better imagine, than we describe, the surprise of Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉의 놀라움은 우리가 묘사하기보다 독자가 상상하는 편이 나으리라."], ["His lurking Indians were suddenly converted into four-footed beasts; his lake into a beaver pond; his cataract into a dam, constructed by those industrious and ingenious quadrupeds; and a suspected enemy into his tried friend, David Gamut, the master of psalmody.", "숨어 있던 인디언은 별안간 네발짐승으로 바뀌었고, 그가 본 호수는 비버 못으로, 폭포는 그 부지런하고 영리한 네발짐승들이 쌓아 올린 둑으로, 의심하던 적은 시험을 거친 벗인 성가의 명수 〈데이비드 거뮤트〉로 변했다."], ["The presence of the latter created so many unexpected hopes relative to the sisters that, without a moment's hesitation, the young man broke out of his ambush, and sprang forward to join the two principal actors in the scene.", "후자가 모습을 드러내자 자매들에 관한 뜻밖의 희망이 수없이 솟아올라, 젊은이는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 매복처를 박차고 나와, 그 자리의 두 주역에게 합류하려 앞으로 내달렸다."]], [["The merriment of Hawkeye was not easily appeased.", "〈호크아이〉의 흥겨움은 좀처럼 가라앉지 않았다."], ["Without ceremony, and with a rough hand, he twirled the supple Gamut around on his heel, and more than once affirmed that the Hurons had done themselves great credit in the fashion of his costume.", "그는 거리낌 없이, 거친 손으로 유연한 〈거뮤트〉를 한 발로 빙그르르 돌려 세우고는, 〈휴런〉족이 그의 차림새를 꾸민 솜씨로 보아 제 명성을 한껏 드높였다고 거듭 단언했다."], ["Then seizing the hand of the other, he squeezed it with a gripe that brought the tears into the eyes of the placid David, and wished him joy of his new condition.", "그러고는 상대의 손을 거머쥐어, 차분한 〈데이비드〉의 눈에 눈물이 핑 돌 만큼 꽉 쥐고 흔들며, 새로운 처지를 축하해 주었다."]], [["\"You were about opening your throat-practysings among the beavers, were ye?\"", "\"비버들 사이에서 막 목청을 가다듬을 참이었던 게요, 안 그렇소?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"The cunning devils know half the trade already, for they beat the time with their tails, as you heard just now; and in good time it was too, or 'Killdeer'", "\"저 교활한 녀석들은 벌써 그 기술의 절반은 알고 있다오. 방금 들었다시피 꼬리로 박자를 맞추니까."], ["might have sounded the first note among them.", "게다가 마침맞은 때였소. 안 그랬으면 〈킬디어〉가 녀석들 사이에서 첫 음을 울렸을지도 모르니."], ["I have known greater fools, who could read and write, than an experienced old beaver; but as for squalling, the animals are born dumb!", "나는 글을 읽고 쓸 줄 알면서도 경험 많은 늙은 비버보다 더한 바보들을 본 적이 있소. 허나 깩깩대는 짓이라면, 저 짐승들은 벙어리로 태어났단 말이오!"], ["What think you of such a song as this?\"", "이런 노래는 어떻게 생각하시오?\""]], [["David shut his sensitive ears, and even Heyward, apprised as he was of the nature of the cry, looked upwards in quest of the bird, as the cawing of a crow rang in the air about them.", "〈데이비드〉는 예민한 귀를 틀어막았고, 그 울음소리의 정체를 익히 알고 있던 〈헤이워드〉조차, 까마귀의 까악 소리가 주위 공기를 갈랐을 때 그 새를 찾아 위를 올려다보았다."]], [["\"See!\"", "\"보시오!\" 웃음 짓던 척후가 신호에 따라 이미 다가오고 있던 나머지 일행을 가리키며 말을 이었다."], ["continued the laughing scout, as he pointed towards the remainder of the party, who, in obedience to the signal, were already approaching: \"this is music which has its natural virtues; it brings two good rifles to my elbow, to say nothing of the knives and tomahawks.", "\"이건 본디 제 쓸모를 지닌 음악이오. 칼과 도끼는 말할 것도 없거니와, 좋은 소총 두 자루를 내 곁으로 불러들이니 말이오."], ["But we see that you are safe; now tell us what has become of the maidens.\"", "허나 그대가 무사하다는 건 알겠소. 자, 이제 그 처녀들이 어찌 되었는지 말해 주시오.\""]], [["\"They are captives to the heathen,\"", "\"그분들은 이교도들에게 사로잡힌 몸입니다.\""], ["said David; \"and though greatly troubled in spirit, enjoying comfort and safety in the body.\"", "〈데이비드〉가 말했다. \"허나 정신은 몹시 괴로울지언정, 몸은 평안과 안전을 누리고 계십니다.\""]], [["\"Both?\"", "\"두 사람 다요?\""], ["demanded the breathless Heyward.", "〈헤이워드〉가 숨죽이며 물었다."]], [["\"Even so.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["Though our wayfaring has been sore and our sustenance scanty, we have had little other cause for complaint, except the violence done our feelings, by being thus led in captivity into a far land.\"", "비록 우리 여정이 고되고 양식이 변변치 못했으나, 이렇게 사로잡힌 채 머나먼 땅으로 끌려가며 감정에 입은 상처를 빼고는 달리 탄식할 까닭이 거의 없었습니다.\""]], [["\"Bless ye for these very words!\"", "\"그 말 한마디로 그대에게 축복이 있기를!\""], ["exclaimed the trembling Munro; \"I shall then receive my babes spotless and angel-like, as I lost them!\"", "떨리는 목소리로 〈먼로〉가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 내 어린것들을, 잃었을 때와 똑같이 티 없고 천사 같은 모습으로 돌려받겠구나!\""]], [["\"I know not that their delivery is at hand,\"", "\"그분들의 구출이 가까웠는지는 모르겠습니다.\""], ["returned the doubting David; \"the leader of these savages is possessed of an evil spirit that no power short of Omnipotence can tame.", "미덥지 못한 듯 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"이 야만인들의 우두머리는 전능하신 분이 아니고서는 그 무엇으로도 길들일 수 없는 사악한 영을 지녔습니다."], ["I have tried him sleeping and waking, but neither sounds nor language seem to touch his soul.\"", "잠들었을 때나 깨어 있을 때나 그를 시험해 보았으나, 음률도 말도 그의 영혼에 닿는 듯하지 않았습니다.\""]], [["\"Where is the knave?\"", "\"그 악당은 어디 있소?\""], ["bluntly interrupted the scout.", "척후가 무뚝뚝하게 끼어들었다."]], [["\"He hunts the moose to-day, with his young men; and to-morrow, as I hear, they pass farther into these forests, and nigher to the borders of Canada.", "\"오늘은 젊은이들과 함께 큰사슴을 사냥하러 갔습니다. 또 듣자 하니 내일은 이 숲을 더 깊이, 〈캐나다〉 변경에 더 가까이 옮겨 간다고 합니다."], ["The elder maiden is conveyed to a neighboring people, whose lodges are situate beyond yonder black pinnacle of rock; while the younger is detained among the women of the Hurons, whose dwellings are but two short miles hence, on a table-land, where the fire has done the office of the axe, and prepared the place for their reception.\"", "손위 처녀는 이웃한 부족에게로 보내졌는데, 그들의 오두막은 저 검은 바위 봉우리 너머에 자리해 있습니다. 손아래 분은 〈휴런〉족 여인들 가운데 붙들려 있는데, 그들의 거처는 여기서 짧게 두 마일가량 떨어진 평평한 고지에 있으니, 그곳은 불이 도끼의 노릇을 대신해 그들을 맞이할 자리를 마련해 둔 곳입니다.\""]], [["\"Alice, my gentle Alice!\"", "\"〈앨리스〉, 내 가여운 〈앨리스〉!\""], ["murmured Heyward; \"she has lost the consolation of her sister's presence!\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 중얼거렸다. \"언니가 곁에 있어 주던 위안마저 잃었구나!\""]], [["\"Even so.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["But so far as praise and thanksgiving in psalmody can temper the spirit in affliction, she has not suffered.\"", "허나 성가로 찬미하고 감사드리는 것이 고난 속의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 수 있는 한에서는, 그분도 괴로움만 겪지는 않으셨습니다.\""]], [["\"Has she then a heart for music?\"", "\"그렇다면 그 처녀가 음악을 좋아하는 마음이 있단 말이오?\""]], [["\"Of the graver and more solemn character; though it must be acknowledged that, in spite of all my endeavors, the maiden weeps oftener than she smiles.", "\"좀 더 진중하고 엄숙한 성질의 음악을 말입니다. 허나 내 온갖 애씀에도 불구하고, 그 처녀가 웃는 일보다 우는 일이 더 잦다는 것은 인정해야겠지요."], ["At such moments I forbear to press the holy songs; but there are many sweet and comfortable periods of satisfactory communication, when the ears of the savages are astounded with the upliftings of our voices.\"", "그런 때에는 거룩한 노래를 강권하기를 삼갑니다. 그래도 만족스러운 교감의 감미롭고 위안이 되는 시간이 여러 차례 있어, 우리 목소리가 드높이 울리면 야만인들의 귀가 깜짝 놀라곤 합니다.\""]], [["\"And why are you permitted to go at large, unwatched?\"", "\"그런데 어찌하여 그대는 감시도 받지 않고 자유로이 돌아다니도록 허락받았소?\""]], [["David composed his features into what he intended should express an air of modest humility, before he meekly replied--", "〈데이비드〉는 겸손하고 점잖은 기색을 드러내려는 양 얼굴 표정을 가다듬고 나서, 온순하게 대답했다."]], [["\"Little be the praise to such a worm as I.", "\"나 같은 미천한 벌레에게 무슨 칭찬이 있겠습니까."], ["But, though the power of psalmody was suspended in the terrible business of that field of blood through which we passed, it has recovered its influence even over the souls of the heathen, and I am suffered to go and come at will.\"", "허나 우리가 지나온 그 피의 들판에서 벌어진 끔찍한 일 가운데 성가의 힘이 잠시 멎기는 했어도, 이제는 이교도들의 영혼에까지 그 영향력을 되찾았으니, 나는 마음대로 오가도록 허락받은 것입니다.\""]], [["The scout laughed, and tapping his own forehead significantly, he perhaps explained the singular indulgence more satisfactorily when he said--", "척후는 웃으며, 의미심장하게 제 이마를 톡톡 두드렸으니, 다음과 같이 말하면서 그 유별난 관용을 어쩌면 더 흡족하게 설명한 셈이었다."]], [["\"The Indians never harm a non-composser.", "\"인디언은 결코 머리가 성치 않은 자를 해치지 않소."], ["But why, when the path lay open before your eyes, did you not strike back on your own trail (it is not so blind as that which a squirrel would make), and bring in the tidings to Edward?\"", "그런데 어찌하여, 눈앞에 길이 훤히 열려 있었거늘, 그대는 제가 온 자취를 되짚어 돌아가(다람쥐가 남길 자국만큼 흐릿하지도 않은데) 〈에드워드〉에 그 소식을 전하지 않았소?\""]], [["The scout, remembering only his own sturdy and iron nature, had probably exacted a task that David, under no circumstances, could have performed.", "척후는 제 자신의 강건하고 무쇠 같은 천성만을 떠올리며, 〈데이비드〉가 어떤 사정에서도 해낼 수 없을 과업을 요구한 셈이었으리라."], ["But, without entirely losing the meekness of his air, the latter was content to answer--", "그러나 후자는 점잖은 기색을 온전히 잃지는 않은 채, 이렇게 대답하는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["\"Though my soul would rejoice to visit the habitations of Christendom once more, my feet would rather follow the tender spirits intrusted to my keeping, even into the idolatrous province of the Jesuits, than take one step backward, while they pined in captivity and sorrow.\"", "\"내 영혼은 다시 한번 기독교 세계의 거처를 찾아가기를 기뻐하겠지만, 내 두 발은, 사로잡힌 채 슬픔에 잠겨 시들어 가는 그분들을 두고 한 발짝이라도 물러서기보다는, 차라리 내게 맡겨진 그 가녀린 영혼들을, 저 예수회 우상 숭배자들의 영토까지라도 따라가는 편을 택하겠습니다.\""]], [["Though the figurative language of David was not very intelligible, the sincere and steady expression of his eye, and the glow on his honest countenance, were not easily mistaken.", "〈데이비드〉의 비유적인 말은 그리 알아듣기 쉽지 않았으나, 그 눈빛의 진실하고 한결같은 표정과 정직한 얼굴에 어린 홍조는 좀처럼 오해할 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Uncas pressed closer to his side, and regarded the speaker with a look of commendation, while his father expressed his satisfaction by the ordinary pithy exclamation of approbation.", "〈언카스〉가 그의 곁으로 바짝 다가서서 그 말하는 이를 칭찬하는 눈빛으로 바라보았고, 그의 아버지는 여느 때처럼 짤막한 동의의 감탄으로 흡족함을 드러냈다."], ["The scout shook his head as he rejoined--", "척후는 고개를 가로저으며 다시 말했다."]], [["\"The Lord never intended that the man should place all his endeavors in his throat, to the neglect of other and better gifts!", "\"주님께서는 결코, 사람이 다른 더 나은 재능을 저버린 채 제 온 힘을 목구멍에만 쏟으라 하신 적이 없소!"], ["But he has fallen into the hands of some silly woman, when he should have been gathering his education under a blue sky, among the beauties of the forest.", "허나 이 사람은, 푸른 하늘 아래 숲의 아름다움 속에서 가르침을 받았어야 마땅하거늘, 어느 어리석은 아낙의 손에 떨어지고 만 게요."], ["Here, friend; I did intend to kindle a fire with this tooting whistle of thine; but as you value the thing, take it, and blow your best on it!\"", "자, 친구. 나는 이 삑삑대는 그대의 피리로 불을 지필 작정이었소만, 그대가 그토록 아끼니, 받아서 한껏 불어 보시오!\""]], [["Gamut received his pitch-pipe with as strong an expression of pleasure as he believed compatible with the grave functions he exercised.", "〈거뮤트〉는 자신이 수행하는 진중한 소임에 어울린다고 여기는 만큼의 강한 기쁨을 드러내며 음정 피리를 받아 들었다."], ["After essaying its virtues repeatedly, in contrast with his own voice, and satisfying himself that none of its melody was lost, he made a very serious demonstration towards achieving a few stanzas of one of the longest effusions in the little volume so often mentioned.", "그것의 효능을 제 목소리와 견주어 거듭 시험해 보고, 그 가락이 조금도 상하지 않았음에 만족하고 나서, 그는 앞서 그토록 자주 언급된 작은 책 속의 가장 긴 시가 가운데 하나에서 몇 연을 읊어 보려는 자못 진지한 채비에 들어갔다."]], [["Heyward, however, hastily interrupted his pious purpose, by continuing questions concerning the past and present condition of his fellow-captives, and in a manner more methodical than had been permitted by his feelings in the opening of their interview.", "그러나 〈헤이워드〉가 그의 경건한 의도를 황급히 가로막고, 동료로 사로잡힌 이들의 과거와 현재 처지에 관한 물음을 이어 갔으니, 두 사람이 처음 만났을 때 그의 감정이 허락하던 것보다는 한결 조리 있는 방식이었다."], ["David, though he regarded his treasure with longing eyes, was constrained to answer: especially as the venerable father took a part in the interrogatories, with an interest too imposing to be denied.", "〈데이비드〉는 제 보물을 애틋한 눈길로 바라보면서도 어쩔 수 없이 대답해야 했으니, 특히 그 노존경할 아버지가, 차마 거절할 수 없을 만큼 압도적인 관심을 품고 물음에 가세한 까닭이었다."], ["Nor did the scout fail to throw in a pertinent inquiry, whenever a fitting occasion presented.", "척후 역시 적절한 기회가 닿을 때마다 핵심을 찌르는 질문을 던지기를 잊지 않았다."], ["In this manner, though with frequent interruptions, which were filled with certain threatening sounds from the recovered instrument, the pursuers were put in possession of such leading circumstances as were likely to prove useful in accomplishing their great and engrossing object--the recovery of the sisters.", "이런 식으로, 비록 되찾은 악기에서 흘러나오는 어떤 위협적인 소리들로 메워진 잦은 중단이 있기는 했으되, 추적자들은 그들의 크고도 마음을 온통 사로잡은 목적—자매들의 구출—을 이루는 데 쓸모가 있을 법한 주요한 정황들을 알아내게 되었다."], ["The narrative of David was simple, and the facts but few.", "〈데이비드〉의 이야기는 단순했고, 그 사실이라곤 몇 가지에 지나지 않았다."]], [["Magua had waited on the mountain until a safe moment to retire presented itself, when he had descended, and taken the route along the western side of the Horican, in the direction of the Canadas.", "〈마구아〉는 안전하게 물러날 순간이 올 때까지 산 위에서 기다리다가, 마침내 내려와 〈호리컨〉 서쪽 기슭을 따라 〈캐나다〉 쪽으로 길을 잡았다."], ["As the subtle Huron was familiar with the paths, and well knew there was no immediate danger of pursuit, their progress had been moderate, and far from fatiguing.", "교활한 〈휴런〉족은 길에 훤했고, 당장은 추격당할 위험이 없음을 잘 알았으므로, 그들의 행보는 더디지도 고되지도 않았다."], ["It appeared from the unembellished statement of David, that his own presence had been rather endured than desired; though even Magua had not been entirely exempt from that veneration with which the Indians regard those whom the Great Spirit has visited in their intellects.", "〈데이비드〉의 꾸밈없는 진술로 미루어 보건대, 그 자신의 존재는 환영받았다기보다 그저 용인된 셈이었다. 비록 〈마구아〉조차도, 위대한 정령이 그 지성을 찾아왔다고 여기는 자들을 인디언이 우러르는 그 경외심에서 전적으로 자유롭지는 않았으나 말이다."], ["At night, the utmost care had been taken of the captives, both to prevent injury from the damps of the woods, and to guard against an escape.", "밤이면 사로잡힌 이들에게 더없는 보살핌이 베풀어졌으니, 숲의 습기로 해를 입지 않게 하는 동시에 도망을 막기 위함이었다."], ["At the spring, the horses were turned loose, as has been seen; and notwithstanding the remoteness and length of their trail, the artifices already named were resorted to, in order to cut off every clue to their place of retreat.", "앞서 본 대로, 샘가에서 말들은 풀려났고, 그들의 자취가 멀고 길었음에도, 그들의 은신처로 이르는 모든 실마리를 끊고자 이미 말한 술책들이 동원되었다."], ["On their arrival at the encampment of his people, Magua, in obedience to a policy seldom departed from, separated his prisoners.", "제 부족의 야영지에 다다르자, 〈마구아〉는 좀처럼 어기지 않는 방침에 따라 포로들을 갈라놓았다."], ["Cora had been sent to a tribe that temporarily occupied an adjacent valley, though David was too ignorant of the customs and history of the natives to be able to declare anything satisfactory concerning their name or character.", "〈코라〉는 인접한 골짜기를 한동안 차지하고 있던 한 부족에게로 보내졌으나, 〈데이비드〉는 원주민의 관습과 내력에 너무도 어두워 그들의 이름이나 성격에 관해 흡족한 무언가를 단언할 수 없었다."], ["He only knew that they had not engaged in the late expedition against William Henry; that, like the Hurons themselves, they were allies of Montcalm; and that they maintained an amicable, though a watchful intercourse with the warlike and savage people, whom chance had, for a time, brought in such close and disagreeable contact with themselves.", "그는 다만 그들이 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에 맞선 지난 원정에 가담하지 않았다는 것, 〈휴런〉족 자신처럼 그들도 〈몽칼름〉의 동맹이라는 것, 그리고 우연히 한동안 그토록 가깝고 거북한 접촉으로 얽히게 된 그 호전적이고 야만적인 무리와, 비록 경계는 늦추지 않으면서도 우호적인 교류를 이어 가고 있다는 것만을 알 따름이었다."]], [["The Mohicans and the scout listened to his interrupted and imperfect narrative, with an interest that obviously increased as he proceeded; and it was while attempting to explain the pursuits of the community in which Cora was detained, that the latter abruptly demanded--", "〈모히칸〉족과 척후는 그의 끊기고 불완전한 이야기에, 그가 말을 이어 갈수록 눈에 띄게 짙어지는 관심을 품고 귀를 기울였다. 그리고 〈코라〉가 붙들려 있는 그 부족의 생업을 설명해 보려 할 때, 후자가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["\"Did you see the fashion of their knives?", "\"그들 칼의 생김새를 보았소?"], ["Were they of English or French formation?\"", "〈영국〉식이었소, 〈프랑스〉식이었소?\""]], [["\"My thoughts were bent on no such vanities, but rather mingled in consolation with those of the maidens.\"", "\"내 생각은 그런 헛된 것에 쏠려 있지 않았고, 오히려 처녀들의 마음을 위로하는 데 함께 어우러져 있었습니다.\""]], [["\"The time may come when you will not consider the knife of a savage such a despisable vanity,\"", "\"야만인의 칼을 그토록 하찮은 헛것이라 여기지 못할 날이 올지도 모르오.\""], ["returned the scout, with a strong expression of contempt for the other's dulness.", "척후가 상대의 둔함을 향한 짙은 경멸을 드러내며 받아쳤다."], ["\"Had they held their corn-feast--or can you say anything of the totems of the tribe?\"", "\"그들이 옥수수 잔치를 벌였소? 아니면 그 부족의 토템에 관해 무어라도 말할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["\"Of corn, we had many and plentiful feasts; for the grain, being in the milk, is both sweet to the mouth and comfortable to the stomach.", "\"옥수수라면, 우리는 풍성한 잔치를 여러 차례 누렸습니다. 그 곡식이 알이 차 즙이 들 무렵이면 입에도 달고 위장에도 편안하니까요."], ["Of totem, I know not the meaning; but if it appertaineth in any wise to the art of Indian music, it need not be inquired after at their hands.", "토템이라면, 그 뜻을 모르겠습니다만, 혹 그것이 인디언 음악의 기예에 어떤 식으로든 닿아 있는 것이라면, 그들에게 물어볼 것까지도 없습니다."], ["They never join their voices in praise, and it would seem that they are among the profanest of the idolatrous.\"", "그들은 결코 목소리를 모아 찬미하는 법이 없으니, 우상 숭배자들 가운데서도 가장 불경한 축에 드는 듯합니다.\""]], [["\"Therein you belie the nature of an Indian.", "\"그 점에서 그대는 인디언의 천성을 그릇 말하는 게요."], ["Even the Mingo adores but the true and living God.", "〈밍고〉족조차도 오직 참되고 살아 계신 하느님만을 경배하오."], ["'Tis a wicked fabrication of the whites, and I say it to the shame of my color, that would make the warrior bow down before images of his own creation.", "전사로 하여금 제 손으로 빚은 형상 앞에 엎드리게 한다는 것은, 백인들의 사악한 날조요. 내 살빛의 부끄러움을 무릅쓰고 말하건대, 그렇소."], ["It is true, they endeavor to make truces with the wicked one--as who would not with an enemy he cannot conquer!--but they look up for favor and assistance to the Great and Good Spirit only.\"", "그들이 사악한 자와 휴전을 맺으려 애쓰는 것은 사실이오—제가 이길 수 없는 적과라면 누군들 그러지 않겠소!—허나 그들이 은혜와 도움을 바라 우러르는 것은 오직 위대하고 선한 정령뿐이오.\""]], [["\"It may be so,\"", "\"그럴지도 모르지요.\" 〈데이비드〉가 말했다."], ["said David; \"but I have seen strange and fantastic images drawn in their paint, of which their admiration and care savored of spiritual pride; especially one, and that, too, a foul and loathsome object.\"", "\"허나 나는 그들의 그림 물감으로 그려진 기이하고 환상적인 형상들을 보았는데, 그것을 향한 그들의 흠모와 정성은 영적인 교만을 풍기더이다. 특히 하나가 그러했으니, 그것도 더럽고 역겨운 물건이었습니다.\""]], [["\"Was it a sarpent?\"", "\"뱀이었소?\""], ["quickly demanded the scout.", "척후가 재빨리 물었다."]], [["\"Much the same.", "\"그와 비슷했습니다."], ["It was in the likeness of an abject and creeping tortoise.\"", "비천하게 기어 다니는 거북의 모습을 하고 있었습니다.\""]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"흐읏!\""], ["exclaimed both the attentive Mohicans in a breath; while the scout shook his head with an air of one who had made an important, but by no means a pleasing discovery.", "귀 기울여 듣던 두 〈모히칸〉족이 한숨에 내뱉었다. 한편 척후는 중대하기는 하나 결코 반갑지는 않은 발견을 한 사람의 기색으로 고개를 가로저었다."], ["Then the father spoke, in the language of the Delawares, and with a calmness and dignity that instantly arrested the attention even of those to whom his words were unintelligible.", "그러자 아버지가 〈델라웨어〉족의 언어로, 그 말을 알아듣지 못하는 이들의 주의마저 단번에 사로잡는 침착함과 위엄을 띠고 입을 열었다."], ["His gestures were impressive, and at times energetic.", "그의 몸짓은 인상적이었고, 때로 격렬했다."], ["Once he lifted his arm on high; and as it descended, the action threw aside the folds of his light mantle, a finger resting on his breast, as if he would enforce his meaning by the attitude.", "한번은 팔을 높이 치켜들었는데, 그것을 내리는 동작에 가벼운 망토 자락이 젖혀지며, 마치 그 자세로 제 뜻을 강조하려는 듯 손가락 하나를 가슴에 얹었다."], ["Duncan's eyes followed the movement, and he perceived that the animal just mentioned was beautifully, though faintly, worked in a blue tint, on the swarthy breast of the chief.", "〈덩컨〉의 눈이 그 움직임을 좇았고, 그는 방금 말한 그 짐승이 추장의 거무스름한 가슴 위에 푸른빛으로, 희미하기는 하나 아름답게 새겨져 있음을 알아보았다."], ["All that he had ever heard of the violent separation of the vast tribes of the Delawares rushed across his mind, and he awaited the proper moment to speak, with a suspense that was rendered nearly intolerable, by his interest in the stake.", "〈델라웨어〉족의 광대한 부족들이 거센 분열을 겪었다고 일찍이 들어온 모든 이야기가 그의 마음속을 스쳤고, 그는 걸린 이해관계에 대한 관심으로 거의 견딜 수 없을 만큼 졸인 마음으로 말할 적절한 순간을 기다렸다."], ["His wish, however, was anticipated by the scout, who turned from his red friend, saying--", "그러나 그의 바람은 척후가 앞질러, 붉은 피부의 벗에게서 몸을 돌리며 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"We have found that which may be good or evil to us, as Heaven disposes.", "\"우리는 하늘이 정하기에 따라 우리에게 길흉이 될 수 있는 무언가를 알아냈소."], ["The Sagamore is of the high blood of the Delawares, and is the great chief of their Tortoises!", "〈사가모어〉는 〈델라웨어〉족의 고귀한 혈통으로, 그들 거북 일족의 위대한 추장이오!"], ["That some of this stock are among the people of whom the singer tells us, is plain, by his words; and had he but spent half the breath in prudent questions, that he has blown away in making a trumpet of his throat, we might have known how many warriors they numbered.", "이 일족 가운데 일부가, 저 노래꾼이 우리에게 말한 그 무리 가운데 끼어 있다는 것은 그의 말로 보아 분명하오. 그가 제 목구멍을 나팔 삼아 헛되이 날려 보낸 숨의 절반만이라도 신중한 물음에 썼더라면, 그들이 전사를 몇이나 헤아리는지 알 수 있었을 텐데."], ["It is, altogether, a dangerous path we move in; for a friend whose face is turned from you often bears a bloodier mind than the enemy who seeks your scalp.\"", "우리가 나아가는 길은 도무지 위험한 길이오. 그대에게서 얼굴을 돌린 벗이, 그대의 머릿가죽을 노리는 적보다 더 피에 굶주린 마음을 품는 일이 흔하니 말이오.\""]], [["\"Explain,\"", "\"설명해 주시오.\""], ["said Duncan.", "〈덩컨〉이 말했다."]], [["\"'Tis a long and melancholy tradition, and one I little like to think of; for it is not to be denied, that the evil has been mainly done by men with white skins.", "\"길고도 우울한 전승이오. 나로서는 떠올리기조차 마뜩잖은 이야기요."], ["But it has ended in turning the tomahawk of brother against brother, and brought the Mingo and the Delaware to travel in the same path.\"", "그 악행이 주로 흰 피부의 사람들 손으로 저질러졌음을 부인할 수 없으니 말이오. 허나 그것은 끝내 형제의 도끼를 형제에게 겨누게 했고, 〈밍고〉족과 〈델라웨어〉족이 같은 길을 걷게 만들었소.\""]], [["\"You then suspect it is a portion of that people among whom Cora resides?\"", "\"그렇다면 그대는 〈코라〉가 머무는 그 무리가 바로 그 부족의 일부라고 의심하는 게요?\""]], [["The scout nodded his head in assent, though he seemed anxious to waive the further discussion of a subject that appeared painful.", "척후는 고개를 끄덕여 동의를 표했으나, 괴로워 보이는 그 주제를 더 논하기를 피하고 싶어 하는 듯했다."], ["The impatient Duncan now made several hasty and desperate propositions to attempt the release of the sisters.", "조바심 난 〈덩컨〉은 이제 자매들을 구해 내려는 성급하고 무모한 제안을 여럿 내놓았다."], ["Munro seemed to shake off his apathy, and listened to the wild schemes of the young man with a deference that his gray hairs and reverend years should have denied.", "〈먼로〉는 무기력을 떨쳐 낸 듯, 그 백발과 노존경할 연치로 보아 마땅히 거절했어야 할 만큼의 정중함으로 젊은이의 거친 계략에 귀를 기울였다."], ["But the scout, after suffering the ardor of the lover to expend itself a little, found means to convince him of the folly of precipitation, in a matter that would require their coolest judgment and utmost fortitude.", "그러나 척후는 연인의 열정이 조금 분출되도록 내버려 둔 뒤, 더없이 냉철한 판단과 극도의 인내가 요구될 이런 일에서 성급함이 얼마나 어리석은지를 그에게 깨우칠 방도를 찾아냈다."]], [["\"It would be well,\"", "\"이렇게 하는 편이 좋겠소.\""], ["he added, \"to let this man go in again, as usual, and for him to tarry in the lodges, giving notice to the gentle ones of our approach, until we call him out, by signal, to consult.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"이 사람을 여느 때처럼 다시 들여보내, 오두막에 머물면서 그 가녀린 처녀들에게 우리가 다가왔음을 알리게 하고, 우리가 신호를 보내 불러내어 의논할 때까지 기다리게 하는 거요."], ["You know the cry of a crow, friend, from the whistle of the whippoorwill?\"", "친구, 그대는 쏙독새의 휘파람 소리와 까마귀의 울음을 구별할 줄 아시오?\""]], [["\"'Tis a pleasing bird,\"", "\"기분 좋은 새지요.\""], ["returned David, \"and has a soft and melancholy note!", "〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"부드럽고 구슬픈 음을 내니까요!"], ["though the time is rather quick and ill-measured.\"", "다만 박자가 다소 빠르고 가락이 고르지 못합니다만.\""]], [["\"He speaks of the wish-ton-wish,\"", "\"이 사람은 쏙독새를 말하는 게요.\""], ["said the scout; \"well, since you like his whistle, it shall be your signal.", "척후가 말했다. \"좋소, 그대가 그 휘파람을 좋아하니, 그것을 그대의 신호로 삼읍시다."], ["Remember, then, when you hear the whippoorwill's call three times repeated, you are to come into the bushes where the bird might be supposed----\"", "그러면 기억하시오. 쏙독새의 울음이 세 번 거듭되는 것을 들으면, 그 새가 있음 직한 덤불로 나오는 거요—\""]], [["\"Stop,\"", "\"멈추시오.\""], ["interrupted Heyward; \"I will accompany him.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 끼어들었다. \"내가 함께 가겠소.\""]], [["\"You!\"", "\"그대가!\""], ["exclaimed the astonished Hawkeye; \"are you tired of seeing the sun rise and set?\"", "깜짝 놀란 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"해가 뜨고 지는 것을 보는 데 싫증이라도 났소?\""]], [["\"David is a living proof that the Hurons can be merciful.\"", "\"〈데이비드〉가 살아 있다는 것이, 〈휴런〉족도 자비를 베풀 수 있다는 증거 아니오.\""]], [["\"Ay, but David can use his throat, as no man in his senses would pervert the gift.\"", "\"그렇소, 허나 〈데이비드〉는 제 목청을 쓸 줄 알지. 제정신인 사람이라면 누구도 그런 재능을 그리 함부로 쓰지는 않을 게요.\""]], [["\"I, too, can play the madman, the fool, the hero; in short, any or everything to rescue her I love.", "\"나도 미친 사람, 바보, 영웅 노릇을 할 수 있소. 요컨대, 내가 사랑하는 그녀를 구하기 위해서라면 무엇이든, 그 어떤 것이든 다 할 수 있소."], ["Name your objections no longer; I am resolved.\"", "더는 반대를 입에 올리지 마시오. 나는 결심했소.\""]], [["Hawkeye regarded the young man a moment in speechless amazement.", "〈호크아이〉는 한순간 말문이 막힌 경악 속에 젊은이를 바라보았다."], ["But Duncan, who, in deference to the other's skill and services, had hitherto submitted somewhat implicitly to his dictation, now assumed the superior, with a manner that was not easily resisted.", "그러나 상대의 솜씨와 공로를 존중하여 그때껏 다소 고분고분 그의 지시에 따르던 〈덩컨〉이, 이제는 좀처럼 거스를 수 없는 태도로 윗사람의 위엄을 내세웠다."], ["He waved his hand, in sign of his dislike to all remonstrance, and then, in more tempered language, he continued--", "그는 어떤 반박도 싫다는 표시로 손을 내저은 다음, 한결 누그러진 말투로 말을 이었다."]], [["\"You have the means of disguise; change me; paint me, too, if you will; in short, alter me to anything--a fool.\"", "\"그대에게는 변장의 수단이 있잖소. 나를 바꿔 주시오. 원한다면 물감도 칠해 주시오. 요컨대, 무엇이든—바보로든—나를 변모시켜 주시오.\""]], [["\"It is not for one like me to say that he who is already formed by so powerful a hand as Providence, stands in need of a change,\"", "\"섭리처럼 강력한 손으로 이미 빚어진 자가 변모할 필요가 있다고 말하는 것은, 나 같은 자가 할 노릇이 아니오.\""], ["muttered the discontented scout.", "못마땅해하던 척후가 중얼거렸다."], ["\"When you send your parties abroad in war, you find it prudent, at least, to arrange the marks and places of encampment, in order that they who fight on your side may know when and where to expect a friend.\"", "\"그대들이 전쟁에 부대를 내보낼 때면, 같은 편에서 싸우는 자들이 언제 어디서 벗을 만날지 알 수 있도록, 적어도 표지와 야영지를 정해 두는 것이 현명하다고 여기지 않소.\""]], [["\"Listen,\"", "\"들어 보시오.\""], ["interrupted Duncan; \"you have heard from this faithful follower of the captives, that the Indians are of two tribes, if not of different nations.", "〈덩컨〉이 끼어들었다. \"그대도 사로잡힌 이들의 이 충직한 종자에게 들었듯, 인디언은 두 부족, 어쩌면 서로 다른 두 민족에 속해 있소."], ["With one, whom you think to be a branch of the Delawares, is she you call the 'dark-hair'; the other, and younger of the ladies, is undeniably with our declared enemies, the Hurons.", "그대가 〈델라웨어〉족의 한 갈래라 여기는 쪽에는 그대가 '검은 머리'라 부르는 분이 있고, 다른 한 분, 더 어린 숙녀는 틀림없이 우리의 공공연한 적인 〈휴런〉족에게 있소."], ["It becomes my youth and rank to attempt the latter adventure.", "후자의 모험을 시도하는 것은 내 젊음과 신분에 마땅한 일이오."], ["While you, therefore, are negotiating with your friends for the release of one of the sisters, I will effect that of the other, or die.\"", "그러니 그대가 자매 가운데 한 분의 구출을 위해 벗들과 교섭하는 동안, 나는 다른 분의 구출을 이루거나, 아니면 죽겠소.\""]], [["The awakened spirit of the young soldier gleamed in his eyes, and his form became imposing under its influence.", "깨어난 젊은 군인의 기개가 그 눈에 번득였고, 그 몸은 그 영향 아래 위풍을 띠었다."], ["Hawkeye, though too much accustomed to Indian artifices not to foresee the danger of the experiment, knew not well how to combat this sudden resolution.", "〈호크아이〉는 인디언의 술책에 너무도 익숙한지라 이 시도의 위험을 내다보지 못할 리 없었으나, 이 돌연한 결심을 어찌 꺾어야 할지 좀처럼 가늠이 서지 않았다."]], [["Perhaps there was something in the proposal that suited his own hardy nature, and that secret love of desperate adventure, which had increased with his experience, until hazard and danger had become, in some measure, necessary to the enjoyment of his existence.", "어쩌면 그 제안에는 그 자신의 강인한 천성과, 경험과 더불어 자라나 마침내 위험과 모험이 어느 정도 그의 삶을 즐기는 데 없어서는 안 될 것이 되어 버린 그 은밀한 무모한 모험에의 애착에 들어맞는 무언가가 있었으리라."], ["Instead of continuing to oppose the scheme of Duncan, his humor suddenly altered, and he lent himself to its execution.", "그는 〈덩컨〉의 계획에 계속 맞서기는커녕, 별안간 기분이 바뀌어 그 실행에 손을 보탰다."]], [["\"Come,\"", "\"자.\""], ["he said, with a good-humored smile; \"the buck that will take to the water must be headed, and not followed.", "그가 너그러운 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"물에 뛰어들려는 수사슴은 뒤따를 게 아니라 앞을 막아야 하는 법이오."], ["Chingachgook has as many different paints as the engineer officer's wife, who takes down natur' on scraps of paper, making the mountains look like cocks of rusty hay, and placing the blue sky in reach of your hand.", "〈칭가치국〉에게는, 산을 녹슨 건초 더미처럼 보이게 하고 푸른 하늘을 손이 닿을 데까지 끌어다 놓으며 자연을 종잇조각에 옮겨 그리는 그 공병 장교의 아내만큼이나 갖가지 물감이 있다오."], ["The Sagamore can use them, too.", "〈사가모어〉도 그것을 쓸 줄 알지. 통나무에 앉으시오."], ["Seat yourself on the log; and my life on it, he can soon make a natural fool of you, and that well to your liking.\"", "내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대, 그가 곧 그대를 천연덕스러운 바보로 만들어 줄 거요. 그것도 그대 마음에 쏙 들도록 말이오.\""]], [["Duncan complied; and the Mohican, who had been an attentive listener to the discourse, readily undertook the office.", "〈덩컨〉이 응했다. 그리고 그 이야기를 귀 기울여 듣던 〈모히칸〉족이 선뜻 그 소임을 맡았다."], ["Long practised in all the subtle arts of his race, he drew, with great dexterity and quickness, the fantastic shadow that the natives were accustomed to consider as the evidence of a friendly and jocular disposition.", "제 종족의 온갖 정교한 기예에 오래 단련된 그는, 인디언이 우호적이고 익살스러운 성정의 증거로 여기곤 하는 그 환상적인 그림자를, 더없는 솜씨와 빠르기로 그려 냈다."], ["Every line that could possibly be interpreted into a secret inclination for war, was carefully avoided; while, on the other hand, he studied those conceits that might be construed into amity.", "자칫 전쟁을 향한 은밀한 마음으로 풀이될 만한 선이라곤 모두 조심스레 피하면서, 다른 한편으로는 우정으로 해석될 만한 도안들을 궁리해 넣었다."]], [["In short, he entirely sacrificed every appearance of the warrior to the masquerade of a buffoon.", "요컨대 그는 전사의 모든 외양을 어릿광대의 가장극에 온전히 내주었다."], ["Such exhibitions were not uncommon among the Indians; and as Duncan was already sufficiently disguised in his dress, there certainly did exist some reason for believing that, with his knowledge of French, he might pass for a juggler from Ticonderoga, straggling among the allied and friendly tribes.", "그런 차림새는 인디언들 사이에서 드물지 않았다. 〈덩컨〉은 이미 옷차림으로 충분히 변장한 데다, 〈프랑스〉어에 밝았으므로, 동맹을 맺은 우호적인 부족들 사이를 떠도는 〈타이컨더로가〉 출신의 요술쟁이로 통할 만하다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 분명 있었다."]], [["When he was thought to be sufficiently painted, the scout gave him much friendly advice; concerted signals, and appointed the place where they should meet, in the event of mutual success.", "물감을 넉넉히 칠했다고 여겨지자, 척후는 그에게 우정 어린 조언을 많이 건네고, 신호를 짜 맞추고, 서로 성공을 거둘 경우 만날 자리를 정해 주었다."], ["The parting between Munro and his young friend was more melancholy; still, the former submitted to the separation with an indifference that his warm and honest nature would never have permitted in a more healthful state of mind.", "〈먼로〉와 그 젊은 벗의 작별은 한결 우울했다. 그래도 노인은, 좀 더 건강한 정신 상태였다면 그 따스하고 정직한 천성이 결코 용납하지 않았을 무심함으로 그 이별을 받아들였다."], ["The scout led Heyward aside, and acquainted him with his intention to leave the veteran in some safe encampment, in charge of Chingachgook, while he and Uncas pursued their inquiries among the people they had reason to believe were Delawares.", "척후는 〈헤이워드〉를 한쪽으로 데려가, 그 노병을 〈칭가치국〉에게 맡겨 어딘가 안전한 야영지에 머물게 하고, 그동안 자신과 〈언카스〉는 〈델라웨어〉족이라 믿을 만한 까닭이 있는 그 무리 사이에서 탐문을 이어 갈 작정임을 알렸다."], ["Then renewing his cautions and advice, he concluded by saying, with a solemnity and warmth of feeling, with which Duncan was deeply touched:", "그러고는 다시 한번 주의와 조언을 거듭하며, 〈덩컨〉이 깊이 감동할 만한 엄숙함과 따뜻한 정으로 이렇게 끝맺었다."]], [["\"And now God bless you!", "\"자, 그러니 신의 가호가 있기를!"], ["You have shown a spirit that I like; for it is the gift of youth, more especially one of warm blood and a stout heart.", "그대는 내 마음에 드는 기개를 보여 주었소. 그것은 젊음의 선물이니, 더욱이 뜨거운 피와 굳센 심장을 지닌 자의 선물이오."], ["But believe the warning of a man who has reason to know all he says to be true.", "허나 제가 하는 말이 모두 참임을 알 만한 까닭이 있는 사내의 경고를 믿으시오."], ["You will have occasion for your best manhood, and for a sharper wit than what is to be gathered in books, afore you outdo the cunning, or get the better of the courage of a Mingo.", "〈밍고〉족의 교활함을 능가하거나 그 용기를 꺾으려면, 그대는 한껏 사내다워야 하고, 책에서 주워 모을 수 있는 것보다 더 날카로운 기지를 부려야 할 게요."], ["God bless you!", "신의 가호가 있기를!"], ["if the Hurons master your scalp, rely on the promise of one who has two stout warriors to back him.", "만일 〈휴런〉족이 그대의 머릿가죽을 차지하거든, 든든한 전사 둘을 거느린 사내의 약속을 믿으시오. 녀석들은 제 승리의 대가를, 머리카락 한 올마다 목숨 하나씩으로 치르게 될 게요."], ["They shall pay for their victory, with a life for every hair it holds.", "내 말하건대, 젊은 신사여, 섭리께서 그대의 거사를 축복하시기를. 그것은 오롯이 선한 일이니 말이오."], ["I say, young gentleman, may Providence bless your undertaking, which is altogether for good; and remember, that to outwit the knaves it is lawful to practise things that may not be naturally the gift of a white skin.\"", "그리고 기억하시오. 그 악당들의 허를 찌르기 위해서라면, 본디 흰 피부에게 타고난 재능이 아닐지라도 그것을 부리는 것이 정당하다는 것을.\""]], [["Duncan shook his worthy and reluctant associate warmly by the hand, once more recommended his aged friend to his care, and returning his good wishes, he motioned to David to proceed.", "〈덩컨〉은 그 갸륵하고 마지못해하던 동료의 손을 따뜻하게 흔들어 잡고, 다시 한번 늙은 벗을 그에게 부탁하고, 그 축원에 답한 다음, 〈데이비드〉에게 앞장서라고 손짓했다."], ["Hawkeye gazed after the high-spirited and adventurous young man for several moments, in open admiration; then shaking his head doubtingly, he turned, and led his own division of the party into the concealment of the forest.", "〈호크아이〉는 기개 넘치고 모험심 강한 젊은이의 뒷모습을 잠시 드러내 놓고 감탄하며 바라보았다. 그러고는 미심쩍은 듯 고개를 가로저으며 돌아서서, 제 쪽 일행을 숲의 은밀한 품속으로 이끌고 들어갔다."]], [["The route taken by Duncan and David lay directly across the clearing of the beavers, and along the margin of their pond.", "〈덩컨〉과 〈데이비드〉가 잡은 길은 비버들이 닦아 놓은 공터를 곧장 가로질러, 그 못의 가장자리를 따라 뻗어 있었다."]], [["When the former found himself alone with one so simple, and so little qualified to render any assistance in desperate emergencies, he first began to be sensible of the difficulties of the task he had undertaken.", "전자는, 절박한 위급 상황에서 도움이라곤 거의 줄 수 없을 만큼 그토록 단순하고 그토록 자격이 모자란 이와 단둘이 남게 되자, 비로소 제가 떠맡은 과업의 어려움을 절감하기 시작했다."], ["The fading light increased the gloominess of the bleak and savage wilderness that stretched so far on every side of him; and there was even a fearful character in the stillness of those little huts, that he knew were so abundantly peopled.", "스러져 가는 빛은 사방으로 그토록 멀리 뻗은, 황량하고 거친 황야의 음울함을 더했고, 그가 사람들로 그득하다는 것을 익히 아는 그 작은 오두막들의 정적에마저 무시무시한 기운이 어려 있었다."], ["It struck him, as he gazed at the admirable structures and the wonderful precautions of their sagacious inmates, that even the brutes of these vast wilds were possessed of an instinct nearly commensurate with his own reason; and he could not reflect, without anxiety, on the unequal contest that he had so rashly courted.", "그 감탄스러운 구조물들과 영리한 거주자들의 놀라운 대비책을 바라보고 있자니, 이 광막한 야생의 짐승들조차 제 이성에 거의 맞먹는 본능을 지녔다는 생각이 들었다. 그리고 그는 제가 그토록 무모하게 자초한 그 불공평한 다툼을 불안 없이는 곱씹을 수 없었다."], ["Then came the glowing image of Alice; her distress; her actual danger; and all the peril of his situation was forgotten.", "그때 〈앨리스〉의 환한 모습이, 그녀의 비탄이, 그녀가 실제로 처한 위험이 떠올랐고, 제 처지의 온갖 위험은 까맣게 잊혔다."], ["Cheering David, he moved on with the light and vigorous step of youth and enterprise.", "〈데이비드〉를 다독이며, 그는 젊음과 거사 특유의 가볍고 힘찬 걸음으로 나아갔다."]], [["After making nearly a semicircle around the pond, they diverged from the water-course, and began to ascend to the level of a slight elevation in that bottom land, over which they journeyed.", "못 둘레로 거의 반원을 그리고 나서, 그들은 물길에서 갈라져 나와, 그들이 지나온 그 낮은 땅에서 살짝 솟은 둔덕의 마루로 올라가기 시작했다."], ["Within half an hour they gained the margin of another opening that bore all the signs of having been also made by the beavers, and which those sagacious animals had probably been induced, by some accident, to abandon, for the more eligible position they now occupied.", "반 시간이 채 못 되어 그들은 또 다른 공터의 가장자리에 이르렀는데, 그곳도 비버들이 만든 온갖 흔적을 지니고 있었다. 그 영리한 짐승들은 아마 어떤 사고로 인해 그곳을 버리고, 지금 차지하고 있는 더 알맞은 자리로 옮기게 된 듯했다."], ["A very natural sensation caused Duncan to hesitate a moment, unwilling to leave the cover of their bushy path, as a man pauses to collect his energies before he essays any hazardous experiment, in which he is secretly conscious they will all be needed.", "〈덩컨〉은 더없이 자연스러운 감정에 사로잡혀 한순간 머뭇거렸으니, 어떤 위험한 시도를 앞두고 거기에 제 온 힘이 다 필요하리라는 것을 은밀히 의식하는 사람이 기운을 그러모으려 잠시 멈추듯, 덤불 우거진 길의 엄폐물을 떠나기가 꺼려졌던 것이다."], ["He profited by the halt, to gather such information as might be obtained from his short and hasty glances.", "그는 멈춘 김에, 짧고 황급한 눈길로 얻어 낼 수 있는 정보를 그러모았다."]], [["On the opposite side of the clearing, and near the point where the brook tumbled over some rocks, from a still higher level, some fifty or sixty lodges, rudely fabricated of logs, brush, and earth intermingled, were to be discovered.", "공터 맞은편, 그리고 시냇물이 한층 높은 데서 몇몇 바위를 넘어 쏟아져 내리는 지점 가까이에는, 통나무와 잡목과 흙을 뒤섞어 거칠게 지은 쉰에서 예순 채가량의 오두막이 보였다."], ["They were arranged without any order, and seemed to be constructed with very little attention to neatness or beauty.", "그것들은 아무 질서 없이 늘어서 있었고, 정연함이나 아름다움에는 거의 마음을 쓰지 않은 채 지어진 듯했다."], ["Indeed, so very inferior were they in the two latter particulars to the village Duncan had just seen, that he began to expect a second surprise, no less astonishing than the former.", "사실, 그 두 가지 점에서 〈덩컨〉이 방금 본 마을보다 너무도 못한지라, 그는 앞선 것에 못지않게 놀라운 두 번째 경악을 또 한 차례 겪게 되리라 예상하기 시작했다."], ["This expectation was in no degree diminished, when, by the doubtful twilight, he beheld twenty or thirty forms rising alternately from the cover of the tall, coarse grass, in front of the lodges, and then sinking again from the sight, as it were to burrow in the earth.", "이 예상은, 어스름한 황혼 속에서, 오두막들 앞의 키 크고 거친 풀숲에서 스무 명에서 서른 명가량의 형상이 번갈아 솟아올랐다가 다시 시야에서 가라앉으며, 마치 땅속으로 파고드는 듯한 광경을 보았을 때 조금도 수그러들지 않았다."], ["By the sudden and hasty glimpses that he caught of these figures, they seemed more like dark glancing spectres, or some other unearthly beings, than creatures fashioned with the ordinary and vulgar materials of flesh and blood.", "그 형상들을 별안간 황급히 흘끗거린 모양으로 보아, 그들은 살과 피라는 예사롭고 흔한 재료로 빚어진 피조물이라기보다는, 차라리 어둑하게 번득이는 망령이나 그 밖의 어떤 이승의 것이 아닌 존재처럼 보였다."], ["A gaunt, naked form was seen, for a single instant, tossing its arms wildly in the air, and then the spot it had filled was vacant; the figure appearing suddenly in some other and distant place, or being succeeded by another, possessing the same mysterious character.", "여위고 헐벗은 형상 하나가 한순간 두 팔을 허공에 미친 듯이 휘젓는 것이 보이는가 싶더니, 그것이 차지하고 있던 자리가 텅 비었고, 그 형상은 별안간 어딘가 멀리 떨어진 다른 곳에 나타나거나, 똑같이 알 수 없는 성질을 지닌 또 다른 형상으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["David, observing that his companion lingered, pursued the direction of his gaze, and in some measure recalled the recollection of Heyward, by speaking.", "동료가 머뭇거리는 것을 알아챈 〈데이비드〉가 그 시선의 방향을 좇다가, 입을 열어 어느 정도 〈헤이워드〉의 정신을 되돌려 놓았다."]], [["\"There is much fruitful soil uncultivated here,\"", "\"여기엔 갈지 않은 비옥한 땅이 많구려.\""], ["he said; \"and I may add, without the sinful leaven of self-commendation, that since my short sojourn in these heathenish abodes, much good seed has been scattered by the wayside.\"", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 자화자찬이라는 죄스러운 누룩 없이 덧붙이건대, 이 이교의 거처에 잠시 머무는 동안 나는 길가에 좋은 씨앗을 적잖이 흩뿌렸소이다.\""]], [["\"The tribes are fonder of the chase than of the arts of men of labor,\"", "\"부족들은 일하는 자들의 기예보다 사냥을 더 좋아하지요.\""], ["returned the unconscious Duncan, still gazing at the objects of his wonder.", "여전히 제 경이의 대상을 응시한 채, 무심한 〈덩컨〉이 대꾸했다."]], [["\"It is rather joy than labor to the spirit, to lift up the voice in praise; but sadly do these boys abuse their gifts.", "\"목소리를 드높여 찬미하는 것은 영혼에게 노역이라기보다 차라리 기쁨이건만, 슬프게도 이 아이들은 제 재능을 함부로 허비하는구려."], ["Rarely have I found any of their age, on whom nature has so freely bestowed the elements of psalmody; and surely, surely, there are none who neglect them more.", "그 또래로서 자연이 이토록 후하게 성가의 자질을 베푼 자들을 나는 좀처럼 본 적이 없소. 그러면서도 분명코, 분명코 그것을 이토록 소홀히 하는 자들 또한 없소이다."], ["Three nights have I now tarried here, and three several times have I assembled the urchins to join in sacred song; and as often have they responded to my efforts with whoopings and howlings that have chilled my soul!\"", "이제 사흘 밤을 여기 머무는 동안 세 차례나 저 개구쟁이들을 모아 거룩한 노래에 함께하게 하려 했으나, 그때마다 녀석들은 내 영혼을 얼어붙게 하는 고함과 울부짖음으로 내 애씀에 답하더이다!\""]], [["\"Of whom speak you?\"", "\"누구를 두고 하는 말이오?\""]], [["\"Of those children of the devil, who waste the precious moments in yonder idle antics.", "\"저기서 부질없는 익살로 귀한 순간을 허비하는 저 악마의 자식들 말이오."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["the wholesome restraint of discipline is but little known among this self-abandoned people.", "이 자포자기한 무리 사이에서는 규율의 유익한 단속이 거의 알려져 있지 않소. 자작나무가 지천인 고장에서 회초리란 도무지 구경할 수 없으니, 섭리의 가장 빼어난 축복이 저런 고함 속에 허비되는 것이 내 눈에 놀라울 것도 없는 일이외다.\""], ["In a country of birches, a rod is never seen; and it ought not to appear a marvel in my eyes, that the choicest blessings of Providence are wasted in such cries as these.\"", "〈데이비드〉가 그 어린 무리를 향해 귀를 틀어막았으니, 바로 그때 녀석들의 고함이 숲을 가르며 날카롭게 울려 퍼졌다. 그러자 〈덩컨〉은 마치 제 미신을 비웃듯 입술을 비죽이면서 단호하게 말했다."]], [["David closed his ears against the juvenile pack, whose yell just then rang shrilly through the forest; and Duncan, suffering his lip to curl, as in mockery of his own superstition, said firmly:", "\"갑시다.\" 귀를 막은 손은 떼지 않은 채, 노래의 명수가 따랐고, 두 사람은 함께 〈데이비드〉가 이따금 \"블레셋 사람들의 천막\"이라 부르곤 하던 곳을 향해 길을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"We will proceed.\"", "\"하나 들짐승이 사냥의"]], [["Without removing the safeguards from his ears, the master of song complied, and together they pursued their way towards what David was sometimes wont to call \"the tents of the Philistines.\"", "특권을 주장할 수 있을지라도,\n사슴에게는 공간과 법도를 베풀어"]], [["\"But though the beast of game The privilege of chase may claim; Though space and law the stag we lend Ere hound we slip, or bow we bend; Who ever recked, where, how, or when The prowling fox was trapped or slain?\"", "사냥개를 풀거나 활을 당기기 전에 여유를 주거니와,\n어슬렁대는 여우가 어디서, 어떻게, 언제"]], [["_Lady of the Lake._\nIt is unusual to find an encampment of the natives, like those of the more instructed whites, guarded by the presence of armed men. Well informed of the approach of every danger, while it is yet at a distance, the Indian generally rests secure under his knowledge of the signs of the forest, and the long and difficult paths that separate him from those he has most reason to dread. But the enemy who, by any lucky concurrence of accidents, has found means to elude the vigilance of the scouts, will seldom meet with sentinels nearer home to sound the alarm. In addition to this general usage, the tribes friendly to the French knew too well the weight of the blow that had just been struck, to apprehend any immediate danger from the hostile nations that were tributary to the crown of Britain.", "덫에 걸리거나 죽임을 당하는지를 누가 헤아린 적이 있던가?\""]], [["When Duncan and David, therefore, found themselves in the centre of the children, who played the antics already mentioned, it was with the least previous intimation of their approach.", "「호수의 여인」 배운 백인들의 야영지와 달리, 원주민들의 야영지가 무장한 사람들의 존재로 지켜지는 것은 드문 일이다."], ["But so soon as they were observed, the whole of the juvenile pack raised, by common consent, a shrill and warning whoop; and then sank, as it were, by magic, from before the sight of their visitors.", "온갖 위험이 다가오는 것을 아직 멀리 떨어져 있을 때 미리 잘 알아채는 까닭에, 인디언은 대개 숲의 징후를 읽는 자신의 지식과, 가장 두려워할 까닭이 있는 자들과 자신을 갈라놓는 길고도 험난한 길들을 믿고 안심한다."], ["The naked, tawny bodies of the crouching urchins blended so nicely, at that hour, with the withered herbage, that at first it seemed as if the earth had, in truth, swallowed up their forms; though when surprise permitted Duncan to bend his look more curiously about the spot, he found it everywhere met by dark, quick, and rolling eyeballs.", "그러나 어떤 운 좋은 우연의 겹침으로 척후의 경계를 따돌릴 방도를 찾아낸 적은, 더 가까운 곳에서 경보를 울려 줄 보초와 마주치는 일이 좀처럼 없다. 이 일반적인 관습에 더하여, 프랑스에 우호적인 부족들은 방금 가해진 일격의 무게를 너무도 잘 알고 있었으므로, 〈영국〉 왕실에 조공하는 적대 부족들로부터 어떤 임박한 위험이 닥치리라고는 염려하지 않았다."]], [["Gathering no encouragement from this startling presage of the nature of the scrutiny he was likely to undergo from the more mature judgments of the men, there was an instant when the young soldier would have retreated.", "그리하여 〈덩컨〉과 〈데이비드〉가, 앞서 말한 익살을 부리며 놀던 아이들 한가운데에 들어섰을 때, 그들이 다가오는 기척은 거의 미리 알려지지 않았다. 그러나 두 사람이 눈에 띄자마자, 어린 무리 전체가 약속이라도 한 듯 날카롭고 경고하는 함성을 질렀고, 이어 마치 마법에 걸린 듯 방문객들의 시야에서 사라져 버렸다."], ["It was, however, too late to appear to hesitate.", "웅크린 개구쟁이들의 벌거벗은 황갈색 몸은, 그 시각에 시든 풀과 어찌나 곱게 어우러졌던지, 처음엔 정말로 땅이 그 형체들을 삼켜 버린 듯했다."], ["The cry of the children had drawn a dozen warriors to the door of the nearest lodge, where they stood clustered in a dark and savage group, gravely awaiting the nearer approach of those who had unexpectedly come among them.", "다만 놀라움이 가신 뒤 〈덩컨〉이 그 자리 여기저기로 좀 더 호기심 어린 눈길을 돌려 보니, 사방에서 검고 재빠르게 굴러가는 눈동자들과 마주칠 뿐이었다."]], [["David, in some measure familiarized to the scene, led the way with a steadiness that no slight obstacle was likely to disconcert, into this very building.", "더 성숙한 어른들의 판단으로부터 그가 겪게 될 듯한 그 검열의 성격을 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 미리 일러 주는 이 조짐에서 아무런 격려도 얻지 못한 까닭에, 젊은 군인은 한순간 물러서고 싶었다. 그러나 망설이는 기색을 보이기에는 이미 늦었다."], ["It was the principal edifice of the village, though roughly constructed of the bark and branches of trees; being the lodge in which the tribe held its councils and public meetings during their temporary residence on the borders of the English province.", "아이들의 외침이 가장 가까운 오두막 문간으로 열두엇의 전사를 끌어냈으니, 그들은 거기에 어둡고 사나운 무리를 이루어 모여 서서, 뜻밖에 자기들 가운데로 찾아든 자들이 더 가까이 다가오기를 근엄하게 기다렸다. 어느 정도 이 광경에 익숙해진 〈데이비드〉가, 웬만한 장애물로는 흔들리지 않을 듯한 침착함으로 앞장서서 바로 그 건물 안으로 이끌었다."], ["Duncan found it difficult to assume the necessary appearance of unconcern, as he brushed the dark and powerful frames of the savages who thronged its threshold; but, conscious that his existence depended on his presence of mind, he trusted to the discretion of his companion, whose footsteps he closely followed, endeavoring, as he proceeded, to rally his thoughts for the occasion.", "그것은 마을의 으뜸가는 건물이었으니, 비록 나무껍질과 가지로 거칠게 지어졌으나, 그 부족이 〈영국〉 영토의 변경에 임시로 머무는 동안 평의회와 공적인 모임을 여는 오두막이었다. 〈덩컨〉은 문턱에 빽빽이 들어찬 미개인들의 검고 강인한 몸을 스치며 지날 때, 필요한 무관심의 외양을 갖추기가 어려웠다."], ["His blood curdled when he found himself in absolute contact with such fierce and implacable enemies; but he so far mastered his feelings as to pursue his way into the centre of the lodge, with an exterior that did not betray the weakness.", "그러나 자신의 목숨이 침착함에 달려 있음을 의식한 그는, 발걸음을 바짝 뒤쫓는 동행의 분별에 의지하며, 나아가는 동안 이 자리에 맞게 생각을 추스르려 애썼다. 그토록 사납고 무자비한 적들과 그야말로 맞닿아 있음을 깨달았을 때 그의 피가 얼어붙었다."], ["Imitating the example of the deliberate Gamut, he drew a bundle of fragrant brush from beneath a pile that filled a corner of the hut, and seated himself in silence.", "그러나 그는 약점을 드러내지 않는 외양으로 오두막 한가운데로 길을 잡아 나아갈 만큼 자신의 감정을 다스렸다. 신중한 〈거뭇〉의 본을 따라, 그는 오두막 한구석을 채운 더미 아래에서 향기로운 잡목 한 묶음을 끌어내어, 말없이 자리에 앉았다."]], [["So soon as their visitor had passed, the observant warriors fell back from the entrance, and arranging themselves about him, they seemed patiently to await the moment when it might comport with the dignity of the stranger to speak.", "방문객이 지나가자마자, 유심히 살피던 전사들은 입구에서 물러나 그를 둘러싸고 자리를 잡았으니, 이방인의 위엄에 걸맞게 입을 열 만한 순간이 오기를 참을성 있게 기다리는 듯했다."], ["By far the greater number stood leaning, in lazy, lounging attitudes, against the upright posts that supported the crazy building, while three or four of the oldest and most distinguished of the chiefs placed themselves on the earth a little more in advance.", "단연 더 많은 수가, 그 흔들거리는 건물을 떠받치는 곧추선 기둥에 게으르고 나른한 자세로 기댄 채 서 있었고, 그동안 가장 나이 많고 가장 이름난 추장 서너 명이 조금 더 앞으로 나와 땅바닥에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["A flaring torch was burning in the place, and sent its red glare from face to face and figure to figure, as it waved in the currents of air.", "타오르는 횃불 하나가 그곳에서 타고 있었으니, 공기의 흐름에 일렁이며 얼굴에서 얼굴로, 형체에서 형체로 그 붉은 빛을 던졌다."], ["Duncan profited by its light to read the probable character of his reception, in the countenances of his hosts.", "〈덩컨〉은 그 빛을 이용해 주인들의 표정에서 자신이 받을 대접의 짐작되는 성격을 읽어 내려 했다."], ["But his ingenuity availed him little, against the cold artifices of the people he had encountered.", "그러나 그의 재간은, 그가 마주한 사람들의 차디찬 술책 앞에서 거의 소용이 없었다."], ["The chiefs in front scarce cast a glance at his person, keeping their eyes on the ground, with an air that might have been intended for respect, but which it was quite easy to construe into distrust.", "앞쪽의 추장들은 그의 몸에 거의 눈길도 주지 않은 채, 존경의 뜻이었을 법도 하나 불신으로 풀이하기도 아주 쉬운 태도로, 눈을 땅에 두고 있었다."], ["The men in shadow were less reserved.", "그늘 속의 사내들은 덜 조심스러웠다."], ["Duncan soon detected their searching, but stolen looks, which, in truth, scanned his person and attire inch by inch; leaving no emotion of the countenance, no gesture, no line of the paint, nor even the fashion of a garment, unheeded, and without comment.", "〈덩컨〉은 곧 그들의 탐색하면서도 몰래 던지는 눈길을 알아챘으니, 그것은 사실 그의 몸과 옷차림을 한 치 한 치 훑었고, 표정의 어떤 동요도, 어떤 몸짓도, 칠해진 무늬 한 줄도, 심지어 옷의 모양새 하나조차 놓치지 않고 평하지 않는 법이 없었다."]], [["At length one whose hair was beginning to be sprinkled with gray, but whose sinewy limbs and firm tread announced that he was still equal to the duties of manhood, advanced out of the gloom of a corner, whither he had probably posted himself to make his observations unseen, and spoke.", "마침내, 머리에 희끗희끗 흰빛이 섞이기 시작했으나 그 강건한 사지와 굳건한 걸음걸이로 보아 아직 사내로서의 임무를 감당할 만함을 드러내는 한 사내가, 아마도 들키지 않고 살펴보려 자리를 잡았을 어느 구석의 어둠에서 걸어 나와 입을 열었다."], ["He used the language of the Wyandots, or Hurons; his words were, consequently, unintelligible to Heyward, though they seemed, by the gestures that accompanied them, to be uttered more in courtesy than anger.", "그는 〈와이언도트〉, 곧 〈휴런〉의 말을 썼으므로, 그의 말은 〈헤이워드〉에게는 알아들을 수 없는 것이었으나, 그에 곁든 몸짓으로 미루어 분노보다는 예의로 발해진 듯했다."], ["The latter shook his head, and made a gesture indicative of his inability to reply.", "〈헤이워드〉는 고개를 가로저으며, 대답할 수 없음을 나타내는 몸짓을 했다."]], [["\"Do none of my brothers speak the French or the English?\"", "\"내 형제들 가운데 프랑스 말이나 영국 말을 하는 이는 없소?\""], ["he said, in the former language, looking about him from countenance to countenance, in hopes of finding a nod of assent.", "그가 앞엣것으로 말하며, 동의하는 끄덕임을 찾기를 바라며 이 얼굴 저 얼굴로 둘러보았다."]], [["Though more than one had turned, as if to catch the meaning of his words, they remained unanswered.", "한 사람 이상이, 그 말뜻을 알아채려는 듯 돌아보기는 했으나, 대답은 없었다."]], [["\"I should be grieved to think,\" continued Duncan, speaking slowly, and using the simplest French of which he was the master, \"to believe, that none of this wise and brave nation understand the language that the 'Grand Monarque'", "\"이토록 지혜롭고 용맹한 부족 가운데 단 한 사람도, '위대한 군주'께서 그 자녀들에게 말씀하실 때 쓰는 언어를 알아듣지 못한다고 생각하면,\""], ["uses when he talks to his children.", "〈덩컨〉이 천천히, 자신이 다룰 수 있는 가장 단순한 프랑스 말을 써서 이었다."], ["His heart would be heavy did he believe his red warriors paid him so little respect!\"", "\"나는 마음이 아플 것이오. 자신의 붉은 전사들이 자신을 그토록 우습게 여긴다고 믿으신다면, 그분의 마음이 무거우실 것이오!\""]], [["A long and grave pause succeeded, during which no movement of a limb, nor any expression of an eye, betrayed the impression produced by his remark.", "길고 근엄한 침묵이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 사지의 어떤 움직임도, 눈의 어떤 표정도, 그의 말이 자아낸 인상을 드러내지 않았다."], ["Duncan, who knew that silence was a virtue among his hosts, gladly had recourse to the custom, in order to arrange his ideas.", "침묵이 주인들 사이의 미덕임을 알던 〈덩컨〉은, 생각을 정리하고자 기꺼이 그 관습에 의지했다."], ["At length the same warrior who had before addressed him replied, by dryly demanding, in the language of the Canadas--", "마침내 앞서 그에게 말을 건넨 바로 그 전사가, 〈캐나다〉의 언어로 무뚝뚝하게 물으며 대꾸했다."]], [["\"When our Great Father speaks to his people, is it with the tongue of a Huron?\"", "\"우리의 위대한 아버지께서 그 백성에게 말씀하실 때, 〈휴런〉의 혀로 하시오?\""]], [["\"He knows no difference in his children, whether the color of the skin be red, or black, or white,\"", "\"그분은 자녀들을 구별하지 않으시오, 피부 빛깔이 붉든, 검든, 희든 말이오.\""], ["returned Duncan, evasively; \"though chiefly is he satisfied with the brave Hurons.\"", "〈덩컨〉이 둘러대며 대답했다. \"허나 무엇보다 그분은 용맹한 〈휴런〉을 흡족히 여기시오.\""]], [["\"In what manner will he speak,\"", "\"전령들이, 닷새 전 〈잉기즈〉의 머리에서 자라던 머릿가죽을 그분께 헤아려 바칠 때,\""], ["demanded the wary chief, \"when the runners count to him the scalps which five nights ago grew on the heads of the Yengeese?\"", "빈틈없는 추장이 물었다. \"그분은 어떤 식으로 말씀하시겠소?\""]], [["\"They were his enemies,\"", "\"그들은 그분의 적이었소.\" 〈덩컨〉이 저도 모르게 몸서리치며 말했다."], ["said Duncan, shuddering involuntarily; \"and, doubtless, he will say, It is good; my Hurons are very gallant.\"", "\"하여, 틀림없이 그분은 이렇게 말씀하실 것이오. 좋도다, 나의 〈휴런〉은 매우 용맹하도다.\""]], [["\"Our Canada father does not think it.", "\"우리의 〈캐나다〉 아버지는 그리 생각지 않으시오."], ["Instead of looking forward to reward his Indians, his eyes are turned backward.", "자신의 인디언에게 상을 내릴 것을 내다보기는커녕, 그분의 눈은 뒤를 향해 있소."], ["He sees the dead Yengeese, but no Huron.", "죽은 〈잉기즈〉는 보면서도 〈휴런〉은 보지 못하시오."], ["What can this mean?\"", "이게 무슨 뜻이겠소?\""]], [["\"A great chief, like him, has more thoughts than tongues.", "\"그분 같은 위대한 추장은 혀보다 생각이 많은 법이오."], ["He looks to see that no enemies are on his trail.\"", "그분은 자신의 자취에 적이 없는지를 살피시는 것이오.\""]], [["\"The canoe of a dead warrior will not float on the Horican,\"", "\"죽은 전사의 카누는 〈호리컨〉 위를 떠다니지 못하오.\""], ["returned the savage, gloomily.", "미개인이 음울하게 대꾸했다."], ["\"His ears are open to the Delawares, who are not our friends, and they fill them with lies.\"", "\"그분의 귀는 우리의 벗이 아닌 〈델라웨어〉를 향해 열려 있고, 그들이 그 귀를 거짓으로 채우고 있소.\""]], [["\"It cannot be.", "\"그럴 리 없소."], ["See; he has bid me, who am a man that knows the art of healing, to go to his children, the red Hurons of the great lakes, and ask if any are sick!\"", "보시오, 그분은, 치유의 술법을 아는 사람인 내게, 그분의 자녀들, 곧 대호수의 붉은 〈휴런〉에게 가서, 병든 이가 있는지 물으라 명하셨소!\""]], [["Another silence succeeded this annunciation of the character Duncan had assumed.", "〈덩컨〉이 떠맡은 신분에 대한 이 선포에 또 한 번의 침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["Every eye was simultaneously bent on his person, as if to inquire into the truth or falsehood of the declaration, with an intelligence and keenness that caused the subject of their scrutiny to tremble for the result.", "모든 눈이 일제히 그의 몸으로 쏠렸으니, 마치 그 선언의 참됨이나 거짓됨을 캐묻기라도 하듯, 그 명민함과 예리함은 그들 검열의 대상으로 하여금 그 결과가 두려워 떨게 했다."], ["He was, however, relieved again by the former speaker.", "그러나 그는 앞서의 그 말하던 자에 의해 다시 안도하게 되었다."]], [["\"Do the cunning men of the Canadas paint their skins?\"", "\"〈캐나다〉의 술법 부리는 자들이 제 살갗에 칠을 하오?\""], ["the Huron coldly continued; \"we have heard them boast that their faces were pale.\"", "〈휴런〉이 차갑게 이었다. \"우리는 그들이 제 얼굴이 창백하다고 자랑하는 걸 들었소.\""]], [["\"When an Indian chief comes among his white fathers,\"", "\"인디언 추장이 백인 아버지들 가운데로 갈 때면,\""], ["returned Duncan, with great steadiness, \"he lays aside his buffalo robe, to carry the shirt that is offered him.", "〈덩컨〉이 더없이 침착하게 대답했다. \"그에게 내어 준 셔츠를 걸치고자 자신의 들소 가죽옷을 벗어 두는 법이오."], ["My brothers have given me paint, and I wear it.\"", "내 형제들이 내게 칠감을 주었기에, 나는 그것을 칠한 것이오.\""]], [["A low murmur of applause announced that the compliment to the tribe was favorably received.", "낮은 갈채의 웅성거림이, 부족을 향한 이 찬사가 호의로 받아들여졌음을 알렸다."], ["The elderly chief made a gesture of commendation, which was answered by most of his companions, who each threw forth a hand, and uttered a brief exclamation of pleasure.", "나이 든 추장이 칭찬의 몸짓을 했고, 동료 대부분이 이에 답하여 저마다 한 손을 내뻗으며 짧은 기쁨의 탄성을 내질렀다."], ["Duncan began to breathe more freely, believing that the weight of his examination was past; and as he had already prepared a simple and probable tale to support his pretended occupation, his hopes of ultimate success grew brighter.", "〈덩컨〉은 한결 자유로이 숨 쉬기 시작했으니, 검열의 무게가 지나갔다고 믿었기 때문이다. 게다가 자신이 꾸며 댄 직무를 뒷받침할 단순하고 그럴듯한 이야기를 이미 준비해 둔 터라, 마침내 성공하리라는 그의 희망은 한층 밝아졌다."]], [["After a silence of a few moments, as if adjusting his thoughts, in order to make a suitable answer to the declaration their guest had just given, another warrior arose, and placed himself in an attitude to speak.", "손님이 방금 내놓은 말에 알맞은 대답을 하고자 생각을 가다듬기라도 하듯 잠깐의 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 또 다른 전사가 일어서서 말할 자세를 취했다."], ["While his lips were yet in the act of parting, a low but fearful sound arose from the forest, and was immediately succeeded by a high, shrill yell, that was drawn out, until it equalled the longest and most plaintive howl of the wolf.", "그의 입술이 막 벌어지려는 찰나, 낮으나 무시무시한 소리가 숲에서 솟아올랐고, 곧이어 높고 날카로운 비명이 뒤따라, 그것이 늑대의 가장 길고 가장 애처로운 울부짖음에 맞먹을 만큼 길게 끌렸다."], ["The sudden and terrible interruption caused Duncan to start from his seat, unconscious of everything but the effect produced by so frightful a cry.", "갑작스럽고 무서운 그 방해에 〈덩컨〉은 자리에서 벌떡 일어났으니, 그토록 끔찍한 외침이 자아낸 효과 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못했다."], ["At the same moment, the warriors glided in a body from the lodge, and the outer air was filled with loud shouts, that nearly drowned those awful sounds, which were still ringing beneath the arches of the woods.", "바로 그 순간, 전사들이 한 무리가 되어 오두막에서 미끄러져 나갔고, 바깥 공기는 우렁찬 함성으로 가득 차, 아직도 숲의 둥근 천장 아래 울리던 그 무서운 소리들을 거의 삼켜 버렸다."], ["Unable to command himself any longer, the youth broke from the place, and presently stood in the centre of a disorderly throng, that included nearly everything having life, within the limits of the encampment.", "더는 자신을 다스릴 수 없게 된 젊은이는 그곳을 박차고 나가, 이내 야영지의 경계 안에서 살아 있는 거의 모든 것을 망라한 무질서한 무리 한가운데에 섰다."], ["Men, women, and children; the aged, the infirm, the active, and the strong, were alike abroad; some exclaiming aloud, others clapping their hands with a joy that seemed frantic, and all expressing their savage pleasure in some unexpected event.", "남자도, 여자도, 아이도, 늙은이도, 병약한 이도, 활동적인 이도, 강건한 이도 다 같이 밖에 나와 있었으니, 더러는 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 더러는 광적인 듯한 기쁨으로 손뼉을 쳤으며, 모두가 어떤 뜻밖의 사건에 대한 미개한 즐거움을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Though astounded, at first, by the uproar, Heyward was soon enabled to find its solution by the scene that followed.", "처음엔 그 소란에 어리둥절했던 〈헤이워드〉도, 뒤이은 광경으로 이내 그 까닭을 알아낼 수 있었다."]], [["There yet lingered sufficient light in the heavens to exhibit those bright openings among the tree-tops, where different paths left the clearing to enter the depths of the wilderness.", "하늘에는 아직, 갈래갈래 다른 길들이 빈터를 떠나 황야의 깊은 곳으로 들어가는 저 나무 우듬지 사이의 밝게 트인 곳들을 드러낼 만한 빛이 남아 있었다."], ["Beneath one of them, a line of warriors issued from the woods, and advanced slowly towards the dwellings.", "그 가운데 한 길 아래로, 한 줄로 늘어선 전사들이 숲에서 나와 천천히 거처들 쪽으로 다가왔다."], ["One in front bore a short pole, on which, as it afterwards appeared, were suspended several human scalps.", "맨 앞의 한 사람이 짧은 장대를 들었는데, 나중에 드러났듯이, 거기에는 사람의 머릿가죽 여럿이 매달려 있었다."], ["The startling sounds that Duncan had heard were what the whites have not inappropriately called the \"death-halloo;\" and each repetition of the cry was intended to announce to the tribe the fate of an enemy.", "〈덩컨〉이 들었던 그 소름 끼치는 소리는, 백인들이 그리 부적절하지 않게 \"죽음의 함성\"이라 일컫는 것이었으니, 외침이 거듭될 때마다 적 하나의 운명을 부족에게 알리려는 뜻이었다."], ["Thus far the knowledge of Heyward assisted him in the explanation; and as he now knew that the interruption was caused by the unlooked-for return of a successful war-party, every disagreeable sensation was quieted in inward congratulation, for the opportune relief and insignificance it conferred on himself.", "여기까지는 〈헤이워드〉의 지식이 그 설명을 도왔다. 그리고 그 방해가 성공을 거둔 전쟁 부대의 뜻밖의 귀환에서 비롯된 것임을 이제 알게 되자, 온갖 언짢은 느낌이, 그것이 자신에게 가져다준 때맞춘 위안과 무의미함에 대한 속으로의 자축 속에 가라앉았다."]], [["When at the distance of a few hundred feet from the lodges, the newly arrived warriors halted.", "새로 도착한 전사들은 오두막에서 몇백 피트 떨어진 곳에 이르자 멈춰 섰다."], ["Their plaintive and terrific cry, which was intended to represent equally the wailings of the dead and the triumph of the victors, had entirely ceased.", "죽은 자들의 애도와 승리자들의 개선을 다 같이 나타내려던 그들의 애처롭고 끔찍한 외침이 완전히 그쳤다."], ["One of their number now called aloud, in words that were far from appalling, though not more intelligible to those for whose ears they were intended, than their expressive yells.", "이제 그들 가운데 한 사람이, 그 표현 가득한 비명에 못지않게 듣는 이들에게는 알아들을 수 없으나 결코 소름 끼치지는 않는 말로 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["It would be difficult to convey a suitable idea of the savage ecstasy with which the news thus imparted was received.", "그리하여 전해진 소식이 받아들여진 그 미개한 황홀을 적절히 그려 내기란 어려운 일일 것이다."], ["The whole encampment, in a moment, became a scene of the most violent bustle and commotion.", "야영지 전체가 한순간에 더없이 격렬한 부산스러움과 소동의 무대가 되었다."], ["The warriors drew their knives, and flourishing them, they arranged themselves in two lines, forming a lane that extended from the war-party to the lodges.", "전사들은 칼을 빼어 휘두르며 두 줄로 늘어서서, 전쟁 부대로부터 오두막까지 이어지는 통로를 이루었다."], ["The squaws seized clubs, axes, or whatever weapon of offence first offered itself to their hands, and rushed eagerly to act their part in the cruel game that was at hand.", "여자들은 곤봉이며 도끼며, 손에 먼저 잡히는 공격 무기는 무엇이든 거머쥐고, 곧 닥칠 잔혹한 놀이에서 제 몫을 다하려 다투어 달려들었다."], ["Even the children would not be excluded; but boys, little able to wield the instruments, tore the tomahawks from the belts of their fathers, and stole into the ranks, apt imitators of the savage traits exhibited by their parents.", "아이들조차 끼지 않으려 하지 않았다. 그 도구를 다루기엔 너무 어린 사내아이들이 제 아비의 허리띠에서 토마호크를 잡아 빼어 대열 속으로 슬며시 끼어들었으니, 부모가 보여 준 미개한 천성을 곧잘 흉내 내는 자들이었다."]], [["Large piles of brush lay scattered about the clearing, and a wary and aged squaw was occupied in firing as many as might serve to light the coming exhibition.", "빈터 곳곳에는 잡목 더미가 큼직하게 흩어져 있었고, 빈틈없고 나이 든 여자 하나가 곧 펼쳐질 구경거리를 밝힐 만큼 그것들에 불을 붙이느라 분주했다."], ["As the flame arose, its power exceeded that of the parting day, and assisted to render objects at the same time more distinct and more hideous.", "불길이 솟아오르자, 그 기세는 저물어 가는 한낮의 빛을 능가하여, 사물을 한층 또렷하면서도 한층 흉측하게 드러내는 데 한몫했다."], ["The whole scene formed a striking picture, whose frame was composed of the dark and tall border of pines.", "그 모든 광경이 한 폭의 강렬한 그림을 이루었으니, 그 액자는 어둡고 우뚝한 소나무 가장자리로 짜여 있었다."], ["The warriors just arrived were the most distant figures.", "막 도착한 전사들이 가장 멀리 놓인 형상들이었다."], ["A little in advance stood two men, who were apparently selected from the rest, as the principal actors in what was to follow.", "조금 앞쪽에는 두 사내가 서 있었는데, 앞으로 벌어질 일의 주역으로 나머지 가운데서 뽑힌 듯했다."], ["The light was not strong enough to render their features distinct, though it was quite evident that they were governed by very different emotions.", "빛이 그들의 이목구비를 또렷이 드러낼 만큼 강하지는 않았으나, 그들이 사뭇 다른 감정에 사로잡혀 있다는 것만은 아주 분명했다."], ["While one stood erect and firm, prepared to meet his fate like a hero, the other bowed his head, as if palsied by terror or stricken with shame.", "한 사람이 영웅처럼 제 운명을 맞을 채비를 한 듯 꼿꼿하고 굳건히 선 동안, 다른 한 사람은 마치 공포에 마비되거나 수치에 짓눌린 듯 고개를 떨구었다."], ["The high-spirited Duncan felt a powerful impulse of admiration and pity towards the former, though no opportunity could offer to exhibit his generous emotions.", "혈기 왕성한 〈덩컨〉은 앞엣사람을 향해 감탄과 연민의 강한 충동을 느꼈으나, 그 너그러운 감정을 드러낼 기회는 도무지 있을 수 없었다."], ["He watched his slightest movement, however, with eager eyes; and as he traced the fine outline of his admirably proportioned and active frame, he endeavored to persuade himself, that if the powers of man, seconded by such noble resolution, could bear one harmless through so severe a trial, the youthful captive before him might hope for success in the hazardous race he was about to run.", "그래도 그는 그자의 가장 작은 움직임까지 간절한 눈으로 지켜보았다. 그리고 그자의 감탄스러우리만큼 균형 잡히고 날렵한 몸의 고운 윤곽을 더듬어 가며, 만약 그토록 고결한 결의에 떠받쳐진 사람의 힘이 그토록 혹독한 시련을 무사히 견뎌 낼 수 있다면, 눈앞의 이 젊은 포로도 이제 막 달려야 할 그 위태로운 경주에서 성공을 바랄 수 있으리라고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼다."], ["Insensibly the young man drew nigher to the swarthy lines of the Hurons, and scarcely breathed, so intense became his interest in the spectacle.", "어느새 젊은이는 〈휴런〉의 거뭇한 대열에 더 가까이 다가가 있었고, 그 구경거리에 대한 그의 관심이 너무도 깊어진 나머지 거의 숨도 쉬지 못했다."], ["Just then the signal yell was given, and the momentary quiet which had preceded it was broken by a burst of cries, that far exceeded any before heard.", "바로 그때 신호의 함성이 울렸고, 그에 앞섰던 잠깐의 정적이 한바탕 터져 나온 외침으로 깨졌으니, 그것은 앞서 들었던 그 어떤 소리도 한참 넘어서는 것이었다."], ["The most abject of the two victims continued motionless; but the other bounded from the place at the cry, with the activity and swiftness of a deer.", "두 제물 가운데 더없이 비굴한 자는 움직임 없이 그대로 있었으나, 다른 자는 그 외침에 사슴의 날쌤과 빠르기로 그 자리에서 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Instead of rushing through the hostile lines, as had been expected, he just entered the dangerous defile, and before time was given for a single blow, turned short, and leaping the heads of a row of children, he gained at once the exterior and safer side of the formidable array.", "예상대로 적대하는 대열을 가로질러 돌진하는 대신, 그는 위험한 그 좁은 길에 막 들어서더니, 단 한 번의 일격을 가할 틈도 주지 않고 홱 방향을 틀어, 한 줄로 늘어선 아이들의 머리를 뛰어넘어, 단숨에 그 가공할 대열의 바깥쪽, 더 안전한 편에 이르렀다."], ["The artifice was answered by a hundred voices raised in imprecations; and the whole of the excited multitude broke from their order, and spread themselves about the place in wild confusion.", "그 술책에 백 개의 목소리가 저주를 퍼부으며 응했고, 흥분한 무리 전체가 대열을 흩뜨려 사납게 어지러이 그 자리에 퍼져 나갔다."]], [["A dozen blazing piles now shed their lurid brightness on the place, which resembled some unhallowed and supernatural arena, in which malicious demons had assembled to act their bloody and lawless rites.", "이제 십여 개의 활활 타는 더미가 그곳에 음산한 밝음을 던졌으니, 그곳은 악의에 찬 마귀들이 피비린내 나고 무도한 의식을 행하려 모여든 어떤 저주받고 초자연적인 투기장을 닮아 있었다."], ["The forms in the background looked like unearthly beings, gliding before the eye, and cleaving the air with frantic and unmeaning gestures; while the savage passions of such as passed the flames, were rendered fearfully distinct by the gleams that shot athwart their inflamed visages.", "뒤편의 형상들은, 눈앞을 스치듯 미끄러지며 광적이고 영문 모를 몸짓으로 공기를 가르는, 이 세상 것이 아닌 존재들처럼 보였다. 한편 불길을 지나는 자들의 미개한 격정은, 그 달아오른 얼굴을 가로질러 쏘아 대는 번득임으로 무섭도록 또렷이 드러났다."]], [["It will easily be understood, that amid such a concourse of vindictive enemies, no breathing time was allowed the fugitive.", "복수에 불타는 그토록 많은 적이 모인 가운데서, 달아나는 자에게 숨 돌릴 틈이 허락되지 않았으리라는 것은 쉽게 헤아릴 수 있다."], ["There was a single moment when it seemed as if he would have reached the forest, but the whole body of his captors threw themselves before him, and drove him back into the centre of his relentless persecutors.", "그가 막 숲에 다다른 듯 보이던 한순간이 있었으나, 그를 사로잡은 자들의 무리 전체가 그 앞을 가로막아, 그를 다시 그 무자비한 박해자들 한가운데로 몰아넣었다."], ["Turning like a headed deer, he shot, with the swiftness of an arrow, through a pillar of forked flame, and passing the whole multitude harmless, he appeared on the opposite side of the clearing.", "길이 막힌 사슴처럼 방향을 튼 그는, 화살의 빠르기로 갈래진 불기둥 하나를 꿰뚫고, 무리 전체를 무사히 지나, 빈터의 반대편에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Here too he was met and turned by a few of the older and more subtle of the Hurons.", "여기서도 그는 더 나이 들고 더 교활한 몇몇 〈휴런〉과 마주쳐 다시 방향을 돌려야 했다."], ["Once more he tried the throng, as if seeking safety in its blindness, and then several moments succeeded, during which Duncan believed the active and courageous young stranger was lost.", "그는 한 번 더, 그 눈먼 혼란 속에서 안전을 찾으려는 듯 무리에 부딪쳐 보았고, 그러고 나서 여러 순간이 이어졌으니, 그동안 〈덩컨〉은 날쌔고 용맹한 이 젊은 이방인이 끝장났다고 믿었다."]], [["Nothing could be distinguished but a dark mass of human forms tossed and involved in inexplicable confusion.", "뒤엉켜 알 수 없는 혼란 속에 출렁이는 검은 사람 형상의 덩어리 말고는 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다."], ["Arms, gleaming knives, and formidable clubs, appeared above them, but the blows were evidently given at random.", "그들 위로 팔이며 번득이는 칼이며 가공할 곤봉이 보였으나, 그 타격은 분명 닥치는 대로 가해지는 것이었다."], ["The awful effect was heightened by the piercing shrieks of the women and the fierce yells of the warriors.", "그 무서운 효과는 여자들의 찢어지는 비명과 전사들의 사나운 함성으로 한층 고조되었다."], ["Now and then Duncan caught a glimpse of a light form cleaving the air in some desperate bound, and he rather hoped than believed that the captive yet retained the command of his astonishing powers of activity.", "이따금 〈덩컨〉은 어떤 필사적인 도약 속에 공기를 가르는 가벼운 형체를 언뜻언뜻 보았고, 그 포로가 아직도 그 놀라운 활동력을 마음껏 부리고 있기를, 믿었다기보다 차라리 바랐다."], ["Suddenly the multitude rolled backward, and approached the spot where he himself stood.", "갑자기 무리가 뒤로 굴러, 그 자신이 서 있던 자리께로 다가왔다."], ["The heavy body in the rear pressed upon the women and children in front, and bore them to the earth.", "뒤편의 묵직한 무리가 앞쪽의 여자와 아이들을 짓눌러 그들을 땅바닥에 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["The stranger reappeared in the confusion.", "그 혼란 속에 이방인이 다시 나타났다."], ["Human power could not, however, much longer endure so severe a trial.", "그러나 사람의 힘이 그토록 혹독한 시련을 그리 오래 견뎌 낼 수는 없었다."], ["Of this the captive seemed conscious.", "포로도 이를 의식하는 듯했다."], ["Profiting by the momentary opening, he darted from among the warriors, and made a desperate, and, what seemed to Duncan, a final effort to gain the wood.", "한순간 트인 틈을 이용해, 그는 전사들 사이에서 뛰쳐나가, 〈덩컨〉이 보기엔 마지막인 듯한 필사적인 안간힘으로 숲에 이르려 했다."], ["As if aware that no danger was to be apprehended from the young soldier, the fugitive nearly brushed his person in his flight.", "젊은 군인에게서는 아무런 위험도 염려할 게 없음을 아는 듯, 달아나는 자는 달려가는 길에 그의 몸을 거의 스칠 듯이 지났다."], ["A tall and powerful Huron, who had husbanded his forces, pressed close upon his heels, and with an uplifted arm menaced a fatal blow.", "힘을 아껴 두었던 키 크고 강건한 〈휴런〉 하나가 그 뒤꿈치에 바짝 붙어, 치켜든 팔로 치명적인 일격을 겨누었다."], ["Duncan thrust forth a foot, and the shock precipitated the eager savage headlong, many feet in advance of his intended victim.", "〈덩컨〉이 발을 쑥 내밀자, 그 충격에 다급하던 미개인은 노리던 제물보다 여러 피트 앞으로 곤두박질쳐 나동그라졌다."], ["Thought itself is not quicker than was the motion with which the latter profited by the advantage; he turned, gleamed like a meteor again before the eyes of Duncan, and at the next moment, when the latter recovered his recollection, and gazed around in quest of the captive, he saw him quietly leaning against a small painted post, which stood before the door of the principal lodge.", "그 이점을 이용한 포로의 동작은 생각 그 자체보다도 빨랐으니, 그는 방향을 틀어 다시 〈덩컨〉의 눈앞에서 유성처럼 번득였고, 다음 순간, 〈덩컨〉이 정신을 되찾아 포로를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 그는 으뜸가는 오두막 문 앞에 서 있는 작고 칠해진 기둥에 조용히 기대 서 있었다."]], [["Apprehensive that the part he had taken in the escape might prove fatal to himself, Duncan left the place without delay.", "그 탈주에서 자신이 한 몫이 도리어 제 목숨을 위태롭게 할까 염려하여, 〈덩컨〉은 지체 없이 그 자리를 떴다."], ["He followed the crowd, which drew nigh the lodges, gloomy and sullen, like any other multitude that had been disappointed in an execution.", "그는, 처형에 어그러진 여느 무리가 그러하듯, 음울하고 시무룩하게 오두막께로 다가가는 군중을 뒤따랐다."], ["Curiosity, or perhaps a better feeling, induced him to approach the stranger.", "호기심이, 어쩌면 그보다 나은 어떤 감정이, 그로 하여금 그 이방인에게 다가가게 했다."], ["He found him, standing with one arm cast about the protecting post, and breathing thick and hard, after his exertions, but disdaining to permit a single sign of suffering to escape.", "그는 그자가, 한 팔을 지켜 주는 기둥에 두른 채, 안간힘을 쓴 뒤라 거칠고 가쁘게 숨 쉬면서도, 고통의 기색 하나 새어 나오게 하기를 경멸하며 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["His person was now protected by immemorial and sacred usage, until the tribe in council had deliberated and determined on his fate.", "이제 그의 몸은, 부족이 평의회에서 그 운명을 숙고하여 결정하기까지, 아득한 옛날부터 내려온 신성한 관습으로 보호받았다."], ["It was not difficult, however, to foretell the result, if any presage could be drawn from the feelings of those who crowded the place.", "그러나 그 자리를 메운 자들의 심정에서 어떤 조짐이라도 끌어낼 수 있다면, 그 결과를 미리 점치기란 어렵지 않았다."]], [["There was no term of abuse known to the Huron vocabulary that the disappointed women did not lavishly expend on the successful stranger.", "뜻을 이루지 못한 여자들이 성공을 거둔 이방인에게 아낌없이 퍼붓지 않은 욕설이라곤, 〈휴런〉의 어휘에 알려진 것 가운데 하나도 없었다."], ["They flouted at his efforts, and told him, with bitter scoffs, that his feet were better than his hands; and that he merited wings, while he knew not the use of an arrow or a knife.", "그들은 그의 분투를 비웃으며, 쓰라린 조롱으로, 그의 발이 손보다 낫더라, 화살이나 칼 쓸 줄도 모르는 주제에 날개가 어울리겠다, 하고 일러 댔다."], ["To all this the captive made no reply; but was content to preserve an attitude in which dignity was singularly blended with disdain.", "이 모든 것에 포로는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 다만 위엄과 경멸이 유달리 뒤섞인 자세를 지키는 것으로 만족했다."], ["Exasperated as much by his composure as by his good-fortune, their words became unintelligible, and were succeeded by shrill, piercing yells.", "그의 행운에 못지않게 그의 침착함에 약이 오른 그들의 말은 알아들을 수 없게 되었고, 이어 날카롭게 찢어지는 비명으로 바뀌었다."], ["Just then the crafty squaw, who had taken the necessary precaution to fire the piles, made her way through the throng, and cleared a place for herself in front of the captive.", "바로 그때, 더미에 불을 붙이는 필요한 채비를 해 두었던 그 교활한 여자가 무리를 헤치고 나와, 포로 앞에 제 자리를 텄다."], ["The squalid and withered person of this hag might well have obtained for her the character of possessing more than human cunning.", "이 노파의 추레하고 시든 몸은, 그가 사람의 것 이상의 교활함을 지녔다는 평판을 얻기에 족했으리라."], ["Throwing back her light vestment, she stretched forth her long skinny arm, in derision, and using the language of the Lenape, as more intelligible to the subject of her gibes, she commenced aloud--", "가벼운 옷자락을 뒤로 젖히고, 길고 앙상한 팔을 조롱하듯 내뻗으며, 자기 비웃음의 대상에게 더 잘 통하는 〈레나페〉의 말을 써서, 그가 큰 소리로 시작했다."]], [["\"Look you, Delaware!\"", "\"보거라, 〈델라웨어〉여!\""], ["she said, snapping her fingers in his face; \"your nation is a race of women, and the hoe is better fitted to your hands than the gun.", "그가 그의 얼굴에 대고 손가락을 딱 튀기며 말했다. \"네 부족은 계집들의 족속이라, 총보다는 괭이가 네 손에 더 어울리느니라."], ["Your squaws are the mothers of deer; but if a bear, or a wild cat, or a serpent were born among you, ye would flee.", "네 계집들은 사슴의 어미들이거니와, 너희 가운데 곰이나 들고양이나 뱀이 태어난다면, 너희는 달아나고 말리라."], ["The Huron girls shall make you petticoats, and we will find you a husband.\"", "〈휴런〉 처녀들이 네게 치마를 지어 줄 것이요, 우리가 네게 남편을 찾아 주마.\""]], [["A burst of savage laughter succeeded this attack, during which the soft and musical merriment of the younger females strangely chimed with the cracked voice of their older and more malignant companion.", "이 공격에 한바탕 미개한 웃음이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 젊은 여자들의 부드럽고 가락 고운 웃음소리가, 더 나이 들고 더 악의에 찬 그 동료의 갈라진 목소리와 야릇하게 어우러졌다."], ["But the stranger was superior to all their efforts.", "그러나 이방인은 그들의 온갖 짓에 흔들리지 않았다."], ["His head was immovable; nor did he betray the slightest consciousness that any were present, except when his haughty eye rolled towards the dusky forms of the warriors, who stalked in the background, silent and sullen observers of the scene.", "그의 머리는 미동도 없었다. 다만 그의 오만한 눈이 뒤편에서 말없이 시무룩하게 그 광경을 지켜보며 어슬렁대던 전사들의 거뭇한 형상 쪽으로 굴러갈 때를 빼고는, 누구든 그 자리에 있음을 의식하는 기색조차 조금도 내비치지 않았다."]], [["Infuriated at the self-command of the captive, the woman placed her arms akimbo; and throwing herself into a posture of defiance, she broke out anew, in a torrent of words that no art of ours could commit successfully to paper.", "포로의 자제력에 격분한 여자는 두 손을 허리에 짚고, 도전하는 자세를 취하더니, 우리의 어떤 솜씨로도 종이에 옮겨 담아 낼 수 없는 말의 격류 속에 다시 터져 나왔다."], ["Her breath was, however, expended in vain; for, although distinguished in her own nation as a proficient in the art of abuse, she was permitted to work herself into such a fury as actually to foam at the mouth, without causing a muscle to vibrate in the motionless figure of the stranger.", "그러나 그의 숨결은 헛되이 소진되었으니, 비록 제 부족에서 욕설의 명수로 손꼽혔음에도, 그는 실로 입에 거품을 물 만큼 스스로를 분노로 몰아붙이도록 내버려 두었으되, 이방인의 미동 없는 형상에서는 근육 하나 떨리게 하지 못했다."], ["The effect of his indifference began to extend itself to the other spectators; and a youngster, who was just quitting the condition of a boy, to enter the state of manhood, attempted to assist the termagant, by flourishing his tomahawk before their victim, and adding his empty boasts to the taunts of the woman.", "그의 무관심이 자아내는 영향이 다른 구경꾼들에게로 번지기 시작했다. 그리하여 막 소년의 처지를 벗어나 사내의 상태로 들어서려는 한 젊은이가, 그들의 제물 앞에서 토마호크를 휘두르고 여자의 조롱에 제 허튼 허풍을 보태며, 그 표독한 노파를 거들려 했다."], ["Then, indeed, the captive turned his face towards the light, and looked down on the stripling with an expression that was superior to contempt.", "그러자 과연 포로가 빛 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려, 경멸을 넘어선 표정으로 그 풋내기를 내려다보았다."], ["At the next moment he resumed his quiet and reclining attitude against the post.", "다음 순간 그는 다시 기둥에 기댄 조용한 자세로 돌아갔다."], ["But the change of posture had permitted Duncan to exchange glances with the firm and piercing eyes of Uncas. [Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_", "그러나 그 자세의 변화로 〈덩컨〉은 〈엉커스〉의 굳건하고 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈과 눈빛을 주고받을 수 있었다."]], [["_Throwing back her light vestment, she stretched forth her long skinny arm, in derision_]\nBreathless with amazement, and heavily oppressed with the critical situation of his friend, Heyward recoiled before the look, trembling lest its meaning might, in some unknown manner, hasten the prisoner's fate.", "놀라움에 숨이 막히고, 벗이 처한 위태로운 처지에 무겁게 짓눌린 〈헤이워드〉는, 그 눈빛이 자아낸 뜻이 어떤 알 수 없는 방식으로 포로의 운명을 재촉할까 두려워 떨며, 그 눈빛 앞에서 움찔 물러섰다."], ["There was not, however, any instant cause for such an apprehension.", "그러나 그런 염려를 할 당장의 까닭은 없었다."], ["Just then a warrior forced his way into the exasperated crowd.", "바로 그때 한 전사가 격분한 무리를 헤치고 들어왔다."], ["Motioning the women and children aside with a stern gesture, he took Uncas by the arm, and led him towards the door of the council lodge.", "준엄한 몸짓으로 여자와 아이들을 옆으로 물러서게 한 그는, 〈엉커스〉의 팔을 잡아 평의회 오두막의 문 쪽으로 데려갔다."], ["Thither all the chiefs, and most of the distinguished warriors, followed; among whom the anxious Heyward found means to enter without attracting any dangerous attention to himself.", "그리로 모든 추장과 이름난 전사 대부분이 뒤따랐으니, 그 가운데로 근심에 잠긴 〈헤이워드〉도, 위험하게 자신에게 이목을 끌지 않고 들어갈 방도를 찾아냈다."]], [["A few minutes were consumed in disposing of those present in a manner suitable to their rank and influence in the tribe.", "그 자리에 있는 자들을, 부족 내의 신분과 영향력에 걸맞게 배치하는 데 몇 분이 들었다."], ["An order very similar to that adopted in the preceding interview was observed; the aged and superior chiefs occupying the area of the spacious apartment, within the powerful light of a glaring torch, while their juniors and inferiors were arranged in the background, presenting a dark outline of swarthy and marked visages.", "앞선 대면에서 따랐던 것과 매우 비슷한 차례가 지켜졌으니, 나이 들고 지위 높은 추장들이 타오르는 횃불의 강한 빛 안에서 그 널찍한 방의 한가운데를 차지했고, 그동안 더 젊고 더 낮은 자들은 뒤편에 늘어서서, 거뭇하고 윤곽 뚜렷한 얼굴들의 어두운 윤곽을 이루었다."], ["In the very centre of the lodge, immediately under an opening that admitted the twinkling light of one or two stars, stood Uncas, calm, elevated, and collected.", "오두막의 바로 한복판, 별 한두 개의 반짝이는 빛이 들어오는 트인 구멍 바로 아래에, 〈엉커스〉가 차분하고 의연하고 침착하게 서 있었다."], ["His high and haughty carriage was not lost on his captors, who often bent their looks on his person, with eyes which, while they lost none of their inflexibility of purpose, plainly betrayed their admiration of the stranger's daring.", "그의 드높고 오만한 거동은 그를 사로잡은 자들에게도 헛되지 않았으니, 그들은 자주 그 몸으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 그 눈은 목적의 단호함을 조금도 잃지 않으면서도, 이방인의 대담함에 대한 감탄을 또렷이 드러냈다."]], [["The case was different with the individual whom Duncan had observed to stand forth with his friend, previously to the desperate trial of speed; and who, instead of joining in the chase, had remained, throughout its turbulent uproar, like a cringing statue, expressive of shame and disgrace.", "필사적인 속도의 시련에 앞서 자신의 벗과 함께 앞으로 나섰던, 그리고 그 추격에 끼는 대신, 그 떠들썩한 소동 내내, 수치와 치욕을 나타내는 움츠러든 조각상처럼 남아 있던 것을 〈덩컨〉이 눈여겨본 그 인물의 경우는 사정이 달랐다."], ["Though not a hand had been extended to greet him, nor yet an eye had condescended to watch his movements, he had also entered the lodge, as though impelled by a fate to whose decrees he submitted, seemingly, without a struggle.", "그를 맞이하려 내민 손도 하나 없었고, 그의 움직임을 지켜볼 만큼 굽어보아 준 눈도 하나 없었음에도, 그 또한, 다투는 기색 없이 그 명령에 따른 듯한 운명에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯, 오두막으로 들어와 있었다."], ["Heyward profited by the first opportunity to gaze in his face, secretly apprehensive he might find the features of another acquaintance; but they proved to be those of a stranger, and, what was still more inexplicable, of one who bore all the distinctive marks of a Huron warrior.", "〈헤이워드〉는, 또 다른 아는 이의 얼굴을 발견하게 될까 남몰래 염려하며, 첫 기회를 틈타 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그러나 그것은 모르는 자의 얼굴로 드러났고, 더욱이 영문 모를 일은, 〈휴런〉 전사의 온갖 뚜렷한 표지를 다 지닌 자의 얼굴이라는 것이었다."], ["Instead of mingling with his tribe, however, he sat apart, a solitary being in a multitude, his form shrinking into a crouching and abject attitude, as if anxious to fill as little space as possible.", "그러나 그는 제 부족과 어울리는 대신, 한쪽에 따로 떨어져 앉았으니, 무리 가운데 외로운 존재로, 되도록 적은 자리를 차지하려 애쓰기라도 하듯, 그 몸을 웅크리고 비굴한 자세로 움츠리고 있었다."], ["When each individual had taken his proper station, and silence reigned in the place, the gray-haired chief already introduced to the reader, spoke aloud, in the language of the Lenni Lenape.", "저마다 제 자리를 잡고, 그곳에 침묵이 깃들자, 앞서 독자에게 소개한 그 백발의 추장이, 〈레니 레나페〉의 말로 큰 소리로 입을 열었다."]], [["\"Delaware,\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉여,\""], ["he said, \"though one of a nation of women, you have proved yourself a man.", "그가 말했다. \"비록 계집들의 부족 가운데 하나일지라도, 너는 네가 사내임을 입증하였다."], ["I would give you food; but he who eats with a Huron should become his friend.", "내 너에게 먹을 것을 주고 싶으나, 〈휴런〉과 함께 먹는 자는 그의 벗이 되어야 하느니라."], ["Rest in peace till the morning sun, when our last words shall be spoken.\"", "아침 해가 뜰 때까지 편히 쉬어라, 그때 우리의 마지막 말이 내려질 것이다.\""]], [["\"Seven nights, and as many summer days, have I fasted on the trail of the Hurons,\"", "\"이레 밤과 그만큼의 여름 낮을, 나는 〈휴런〉의 자취를 좇으며 굶었다.\""], ["Uncas coldly replied; \"the children of the Lenape know how to travel the path of the just without lingering to eat.\"", "〈엉커스〉가 차갑게 대답했다. \"〈레나페〉의 자손들은 먹으려 머뭇대지 않고도 의로운 자의 길을 가는 법을 안다.\""]], [["\"Two of my young men are in pursuit of your companion,\"", "\"내 젊은이 둘이 네 동행을 뒤쫓고 있다.\" 상대가, 제 포로의 호언을 아랑곳하지 않는 듯이 이었다."], ["resumed the other, without appearing to regard the boast of his captive; \"when they get back, then will our wise men say to you 'live'", "\"그들이 돌아오면, 그때 우리의 지혜로운 자들이 네게 '살라' 혹은 '죽으라'"], ["or 'die.'\"", "할 것이다.\""]], [["\"Has a Huron no ears?\"", "\"〈휴런〉에게는 귀가 없는가?\""], ["scornfully exclaimed Uncas; \"twice, since he has been your prisoner, has the Delaware heard a gun that he knows.", "〈엉커스〉가 경멸하며 외쳤다. \"네 포로가 된 뒤로 두 번, 〈델라웨어〉는 자기가 아는 총소리를 들었다."], ["Your young men will never come back!\"", "네 젊은이들은 결코 돌아오지 못하리라!\""]], [["A short and sullen pause succeeded this bold assertion.", "이 대담한 단언에 짧고 시무룩한 침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["Duncan, who understood the Mohican to allude to the fatal rifle of the scout, bent forward in earnest observation of the effect it might produce on the conquerors; but the chief was content with simply retorting,--", "〈모히칸〉이 척후의 치명적인 소총을 가리키는 것임을 알아챈 〈덩컨〉은, 그것이 정복자들에게 어떤 영향을 미칠지 골똘히 살피며 몸을 앞으로 내밀었다. 그러나 추장은 그저 이렇게 되받는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["\"If the Lenape are so skilful, why is one of their bravest warriors here?\"", "\"〈레나페〉가 그토록 솜씨가 좋다면, 어찌하여 그들의 가장 용맹한 전사 가운데 하나가 여기 있는가?\""]], [["\"He followed in the steps of a flying coward, and fell into a snare.", "\"그는 달아나는 겁쟁이의 발자취를 좇다가 덫에 걸린 것이다."], ["The cunning beaver may be caught.\"", "교활한 비버도 잡힐 수 있는 법.\""]], [["As Uncas thus replied, he pointed with his finger toward the solitary Huron, but without deigning to bestow any other notice on so unworthy an object.", "이렇게 대답하며 〈엉커스〉는 손가락으로 그 외로운 〈휴런〉을 가리켰으나, 그토록 하찮은 대상에게 그 밖의 다른 눈길을 베푸는 것은 마다했다."], ["The words of the answer and the air of the speaker produced a strong sensation among his auditors.", "그 대답의 말과 말하는 자의 기색이 그 듣는 자들 사이에 강한 동요를 일으켰다."], ["Every eye rolled sullenly towards the individual indicated by the simple gesture, and a low, threatening murmur passed through the crowd.", "모든 눈이 그 단순한 몸짓으로 가리켜진 인물을 향해 시무룩하게 굴러갔고, 낮고 위협적인 웅성거림이 무리 사이를 지나갔다."], ["The ominous sounds reached the outer door, and the women and children pressing into the throng, no gap had been left, between shoulder and shoulder, that was not now filled with the dark lineaments of some eager and curious human countenance.", "그 불길한 소리가 바깥문에까지 닿았고, 여자와 아이들이 무리 속으로 밀고 들어와, 이제 어깨와 어깨 사이에는, 간절하고 호기심 어린 어떤 사람 얼굴의 거뭇한 윤곽으로 채워지지 않은 틈이라곤 하나도 남지 않았다."]], [["In the meantime, the more aged chiefs, in the centre, communed with each other in short and broken sentences.", "그러는 동안, 한가운데의 더 나이 든 추장들은 짧고 끊긴 문장으로 서로 의논했다."], ["Not a word was uttered that did not convey the meaning of the speaker, in the simplest and most energetic form.", "말하는 자의 뜻을 가장 단순하고 가장 힘찬 형태로 전하지 않는 말은 한마디도 발해지지 않았다."], ["Again, a long and deeply solemn pause took place.", "다시, 길고 깊이 엄숙한 침묵이 자리를 잡았다."], ["It was known, by all present, to be the grave precursor of a weighty and important judgment.", "그 자리에 있는 모두가, 그것이 무겁고 중대한 판결의 근엄한 전조임을 알고 있었다."], ["They who composed the outer circle of faces were on tiptoe to gaze; and even the culprit for an instant forgot his shame in a deeper emotion, and exposed his abject features, in order to cast an anxious and troubled glance at the dark assemblage of chiefs.", "바깥쪽 얼굴들의 둘레를 이룬 자들은 보려고 발끝으로 섰고, 죄인조차 한순간 더 깊은 감정 속에 제 수치를 잊은 채, 그 거뭇한 추장들의 무리를 향해 근심스럽고 괴로운 눈길을 던지고자 제 비굴한 얼굴을 드러냈다."], ["The silence was finally broken by the aged warrior so often named.", "마침내 그토록 자주 거명된 그 나이 든 전사가 침묵을 깼다."], ["He arose from the earth, and moving past the immovable form of Uncas, placed himself in a dignified attitude before the offender.", "그는 땅에서 일어나, 미동 없는 〈엉커스〉의 형상을 지나, 위엄 있는 자세로 그 죄인 앞에 섰다."], ["At that moment, the withered squaw already mentioned moved into the circle, in a slow, sideling sort of a dance, holding the torch, and muttering the indistinct words of what might have been a species of incantation.", "그 순간, 앞서 말한 그 시든 노파가, 횃불을 든 채, 일종의 주문이었을 법한 알아들을 수 없는 말을 중얼거리며, 느릿하게 옆걸음 치는 듯한 춤으로 그 둘레 안으로 들어왔다."], ["Though her presence was altogether an intrusion, it was unheeded.", "그의 존재는 전혀 주제넘은 끼어듦이었으나, 누구도 개의치 않았다."]], [["Approaching Uncas, she held the blazing brand in such a manner as to cast its red glare on his person, and to expose the slightest emotion of his countenance.", "〈엉커스〉에게 다가간 그는, 그 붉은 빛을 그의 몸에 던져 그 표정의 가장 작은 동요까지 드러내도록 활활 타는 나뭇가지를 들이댔다."], ["The Mohican maintained his firm and haughty attitude; and his eye, so far from deigning to meet her inquisitive look, dwelt steadily on the distance, as though it penetrated the obstacles which impeded the view, and looked into futurity.", "〈모히칸〉은 굳건하고 오만한 자세를 지켰다. 그의 눈은, 그 캐묻는 눈길을 마주해 주기는커녕, 마치 시야를 가로막는 장애물을 꿰뚫어 미래를 들여다보기라도 하듯, 한결같이 먼 곳에 머물러 있었다."], ["Satisfied with her examination, she left him, with a slight expression of pleasure, and proceeded to practise the same trying experiment on her delinquent countryman.", "그 살핌에 만족한 그는, 가벼운 기쁨의 기색과 함께 그에게서 물러나, 죄지은 제 동족에게 같은 가혹한 시험을 행하러 나아갔다."]], [["The young Huron was in his war paint, and very little of a finely moulded form was concealed by his attire.", "젊은 〈휴런〉은 전투용 칠을 한 차림이라, 곱게 빚어진 그 몸은 옷으로 가려진 데가 거의 없었다."], ["The light rendered every limb and joint discernible, and Duncan turned away in horror when he saw they were writhing in irrepressible agony.", "그 빛이 사지와 관절 하나하나를 분간할 수 있게 했고, 그것들이 억누를 수 없는 고통 속에 뒤틀리고 있는 것을 본 〈덩컨〉은 진저리치며 고개를 돌렸다."], ["The woman was commencing a low and plaintive howl at the sad and shameful spectacle, when the chief put forth his hand and gently pushed her aside.", "여자가 그 슬프고 수치스러운 광경에 낮고 애처로운 울부짖음을 막 시작하려는데, 추장이 손을 내밀어 그를 가만히 옆으로 밀어냈다."]], [["\"Reed-that-bends,\"", "\"〈휘는 갈대〉여,\""], ["he said, addressing the young culprit by name, and in his proper language, \"though the Great Spirit has made you pleasant to the eyes, it would have been better that you had not been born.", "그가 그 젊은 죄인을 이름으로, 그의 본래 언어로 부르며 말했다. \"위대한 정령께서 너를 눈에 보기 좋게 빚으셨으나, 차라리 네가 태어나지 않았더라면 나았으리라."], ["Your tongue is loud in the village, but in battle it is still.", "네 혀는 마을에서 우렁차나, 싸움터에서는 잠잠하다."], ["None of my young men strike the tomahawk deeper into the war-post--none of them so lightly on the Yengeese.", "내 젊은이들 가운데 누구도 너만큼 전쟁 기둥에 토마호크를 깊이 박지 못하거니와, 누구도 너만큼 〈잉기즈〉에게 가벼이 내리치지 못한다."], ["The enemy know the shape of your back, but they have never seen the color of your eyes.", "적은 네 등의 모양은 알아도, 네 눈의 빛깔은 본 적이 없다."], ["Three times have they called on you to come, and as often did you forget to answer.", "세 번이나 그들이 너에게 나오라 불렀으되, 그때마다 너는 응하기를 잊었다."], ["Your name will never be mentioned again in your tribe--it is already forgotten.\"", "네 이름은 다시는 네 부족에서 입에 오르지 않으리라, 이미 잊혔느니라.\""]], [["As the chief slowly uttered these words, pausing impressively between each sentence, the culprit raised his face, in deference to the other's rank and years.", "추장이 한 문장 한 문장 사이에 묵직하게 뜸을 들이며 이 말을 천천히 발하는 동안, 죄인은 상대의 지위와 연륜에 대한 경의로 그 얼굴을 들었다."], ["Shame, horror, and pride struggled in its lineaments.", "수치와 공포와 자부심이 그 이목구비 속에서 다투었다."], ["His eye, which was contracted with inward anguish, gleamed on the persons of those whose breath was his fame; and the latter emotion for an instant predominated.", "안으로의 고뇌로 오므라든 그의 눈이, 그 숨결이 곧 자신의 명예였던 자들의 몸 위에서 번득였고, 한순간 뒤엣것의 감정이 우위를 차지했다."], ["He arose to his feet, and baring his bosom, looked steadily on the keen, glittering knife, that was already upheld by his inexorable judge.", "그는 일어서서, 제 가슴을 드러내며, 무자비한 그의 심판자가 이미 치켜든 그 예리하고 번쩍이는 칼을 한결같이 바라보았다."], ["As the weapon passed slowly into his heart he even smiled, as if in joy at having found death less dreadful than he had anticipated, and fell heavily on his face, at the feet of the rigid and unyielding form of Uncas.", "그 무기가 천천히 그의 심장 속으로 들어갈 때 그는 미소마저 지었으니, 죽음이 예상했던 것보다 덜 끔찍함을 알게 된 것이 기쁘기라도 한 듯했고, 엄혹하고 굽힘 없는 〈엉커스〉의 형상의 발치에 얼굴부터 무겁게 고꾸라졌다."]], [["The squaw gave a loud and plaintive yell, dashed the torch to the earth, and buried everything in darkness.", "여자가 크고 애처로운 비명을 지르며, 횃불을 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳐, 모든 것을 어둠 속에 파묻어 버렸다."], ["The whole shuddering group of spectators glided from the lodge, like troubled sprites; and Duncan thought that he and the yet throbbing body of the victim of an Indian judgment had now become its only tenants.", "몸서리치는 구경꾼 무리 전체가, 어수선한 망령들처럼 오두막에서 미끄러져 나갔다. 그리하여 〈덩컨〉은, 이제 자신과, 인디언의 판결에 희생되어 아직도 꿈틀거리는 그 몸뚱이만이 그곳의 유일한 거주자가 되었다고 생각했다."]], [["\"Thus spoke the sage: the kings without delay Dissolve the council, and their chief obey.\"", "현자는 이렇게 말했고, 왕들은 지체 없이\n회의를 파하고 그 우두머리를 따랐다."]], [["POPE'S _Iliad._", "포프의 『일리아드』"]], [["A single moment served to convince the youth that he was mistaken.", "한순간 만에 청년은 자신이 틀렸음을 깨달았다."], ["A hand was laid, with a powerful pressure, on his arm, and the low voice of Uncas muttered in his ears,--", "강한 압력으로 한 손이 그의 팔에 얹혔고, 〈언카스〉의 낮은 목소리가 그의 귓가에 속삭였다."]], [["\"The Hurons are dogs.", "\"〈휴런〉족은 개들입니다."], ["The sight of a coward's blood can never make a warrior tremble. The 'Gray Head'", "겁쟁이의 피를 보았다 하여 전사가 떨 수는 없는 법."], ["and the Sagamore are safe, and the rifle of Hawkeye is not asleep.", "'회색 머리'와 〈사가모어〉는 무사하고, 〈호크아이〉의 소총도 잠들지 않았습니다. 가십시오."], ["Go,--Uncas and the 'Open Hand' are now strangers.", "〈언카스〉와 '열린 손'은 이제 모르는 사이입니다."], ["It is enough.\"", "그것으로 충분합니다.\""]], [["Heyward would gladly have heard more, but a gentle push from his friend urged him towards the door, and admonished him of the danger that might attend the discovery of their intercourse.", "〈헤이워드〉는 더 듣고 싶었으나, 친구의 부드러운 떠밀음이 그를 문 쪽으로 재촉하며, 그들의 교류가 발각될 경우 닥칠 위험을 일깨웠다."], ["Slowly and reluctantly yielding to the necessity, he quitted the place, and mingled with the throng that hovered nigh.", "그 불가피함에 더디고 마지못해 굴복하며, 그는 그곳을 떠나 가까이 어슬렁대던 무리 속으로 섞여 들었다."], ["The dying fires in the clearing cast a dim and uncertain light on the dusky figures that were silently stalking to and fro; and occasionally a brighter gleam than common glanced into the lodge, and exhibited the figure of Uncas still maintaining its upright attitude near the dead body of the Huron.", "빈터에서 사위어 가는 불빛이, 소리 없이 이리저리 거니는 거무스름한 형상들 위로 희미하고 어렴풋한 빛을 던졌다. 이따금 여느 때보다 밝은 섬광이 오두막 안으로 비쳐 들며, 죽은 〈휴런〉의 시신 곁에 여전히 꼿꼿한 자세를 지키고 선 〈언카스〉의 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["A knot of warriors soon entered the place again, and reissuing, they bore the senseless remains into the adjacent woods.", "이윽고 한 무리의 전사들이 다시 그곳에 들어갔다가, 도로 나오며 그 의식 없는 유해를 인근 숲으로 옮겨 갔다."], ["After this termination of the scene, Duncan wandered among the lodges, unquestioned and unnoticed, endeavoring to find some trace of her in whose behalf he incurred the risk he ran.", "이 장면이 끝나자, 〈덩컨〉은 아무 질문도, 아무 주목도 받지 않은 채 오두막들 사이를 거닐며, 그 위험을 무릅쓰게 한 여인의 흔적을 찾으려 애썼다."], ["In the present temper of the tribe, it would have been easy to have fled and rejoined his companions, had such a wish crossed his mind.", "부족이 지금 같은 심기인 터라, 그런 바람이 마음을 스쳤다면, 달아나 동료들과 다시 합류하기란 쉬운 일이었을 것이다."], ["But, in addition to the never-ceasing anxiety on account of Alice, a fresher, though feebler interest in the fate of Uncas assisted to chain him to the spot.", "그러나 〈앨리스〉를 향한 그칠 줄 모르는 근심에 더해, 더 미약하나 더 새로운, 〈언카스〉의 운명에 관한 관심이 그를 그 자리에 붙들어 매는 데 한몫했다."], ["He continued, therefore, to stray from hut to hut, looking into each only to encounter additional disappointment, until he had made the entire circuit of the village.", "그리하여 그는 오두막에서 오두막으로 헤매기를 거듭하며, 마을 전체를 한 바퀴 다 돌 때까지, 들여다보는 곳마다 거듭되는 실망과만 마주칠 뿐이었다."], ["Abandoning a species of inquiry that proved so fruitless, he retraced his steps to the council lodge, resolved to seek and question David, in order to put an end to his doubts.", "그토록 헛된 것으로 드러난 이런 식의 탐색을 단념하고, 그는 의혹을 끝내고자 〈데이비드〉를 찾아 캐물으리라 마음먹고, 발길을 돌려 회의 오두막으로 되짚어 갔다."]], [["On reaching the building which had proved alike the seat of judgment and the place of execution, the young man found that the excitement had already subsided.", "심판의 자리이자 처형의 장소이기도 했던 그 건물에 이르자, 청년은 흥분이 이미 가라앉은 것을 알았다."], ["The warriors had reassembled, and were now calmly smoking, while they conversed gravely on the chief incidents of their recent expedition to the head of the Horican.", "전사들은 다시 모여, 이제 차분히 담배를 피우며 〈호리컨〉 어귀로 떠났던 근래의 원정에서 있었던 주요 사건들을 진중하게 이야기하고 있었다."], ["Though the return of Duncan was likely to remind them of his character, and the suspicious circumstances of his visit, it produced no visible sensation.", "〈덩컨〉의 귀환이 그의 정체와 그가 방문한 수상쩍은 정황을 그들에게 상기시킬 법도 했으나, 그것은 눈에 띄는 동요를 일으키지 않았다."], ["So far, the terrible scene that had just occurred proved favorable to his views, and he required no other prompter than his own feelings to convince him of the expediency of profiting by so unexpected an advantage.", "이런 점에서, 방금 벌어진 끔찍한 장면은 도리어 그의 의도에 유리하게 작용했으니, 이토록 뜻밖의 이점을 활용하는 것이 상책임을 깨닫는 데에 그는 제 감정 외에 다른 부추김이 필요치 않았다."]], [["Without seeming to hesitate, he walked into the lodge, and took his seat with a gravity that accorded admirably with the deportment of his hosts.", "망설이는 기색 없이, 그는 오두막 안으로 걸어 들어가, 그를 맞은 이들의 거동에 더없이 잘 어울리는 진중함으로 자리에 앉았다."], ["A hasty but searching glance sufficed to tell him that, though Uncas still remained where he had left him, David had not reappeared.", "황급하나 살피는 한 차례 시선으로, 그는 〈언카스〉가 자신이 두고 떠난 자리에 여전히 남아 있되 〈데이비드〉는 다시 나타나지 않았음을 알아챘다."], ["No other restraint was imposed on the former than the watchful looks of a young Huron, who had placed himself at hand; though an armed warrior leaned against the post that formed one side of the narrow door-way.", "전자에게는 곁에 자리한 젊은 〈휴런〉의 경계 어린 눈길 말고는 다른 구속이 가해지지 않았으나, 무장한 한 전사가 좁은 출입구의 한쪽을 이룬 기둥에 기대어 있었다."], ["In every other respect, the captive seemed at liberty; still he was excluded from all participation in the discourse, and possessed much more of the air of some finely moulded statue than a man having life and volition.", "그 밖의 모든 점에서 포로는 자유로워 보였다. 그러나 그는 모든 대화에서 배제되어 있었고, 생명과 의지를 지닌 사람이라기보다는 정교하게 빚어진 어떤 조각상의 분위기를 훨씬 더 짙게 풍겼다."]], [["Heyward had too recently witnessed a frightful instance of the prompt punishments of the people into whose hands he had fallen, to hazard an exposure by any officious boldness.", "〈헤이워드〉는 자신이 손아귀에 떨어진 이 사람들의 즉각적인 처벌의 무서운 본보기를, 너무도 최근에 목격한 터라, 어떤 주제넘은 대담함으로 자신을 드러내는 위험을 무릅쓸 수 없었다."], ["He would greatly have preferred silence and meditation to speech, when a discovery of his real condition might prove so instantly fatal.", "자신의 진짜 처지가 드러나면 당장 치명적일 수 있는 마당에, 그는 말보다 침묵과 사색을 훨씬 더 바랐을 것이다."], ["Unfortunately for this prudent resolution, his entertainers appeared otherwise disposed.", "이 신중한 결심에는 불행하게도, 그를 맞은 이들은 다른 마음을 품은 듯했다."], ["He had not long occupied the seat wisely taken a little in the shade, when another of the elder warriors, who spoke the French language, addressed him:--", "그가 슬기롭게 그늘에 조금 비켜 잡은 자리에 앉은 지 얼마 되지 않아, 프랑스어를 할 줄 아는 또 다른 연장의 전사가 그에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["\"My Canada father does not forget his children,\"", "\"내 〈캐나다〉 아버지께서는 자식들을 잊지 않으시는구려.\""], ["said the chief; \"I thank him.", "추장이 말했다. \"그분께 감사드리오."], ["An evil spirit lives in the wife of one of my young men.", "내 젊은이 가운데 하나의 아내에게 악령이 깃들었소."], ["Can the cunning stranger frighten him away?\"", "영리한 이방인께서 그놈을 쫓아낼 수 있겠소?\""]], [["Heyward possessed some knowledge of the mummery practised among the Indians, in the cases of such supposed visitations.", "〈헤이워드〉는 인디언들 사이에서 그런 빙의로 여겨지는 경우에 행해지는 의식 흉내에 관해 어느 정도 알고 있었다."], ["He saw, at a glance, that the circumstance might possibly be improved to further his own end.", "그는 이 정황이 어쩌면 자신의 목적을 진척시키는 데 보탬이 될 수 있음을 한눈에 알아보았다."], ["It would, therefore, have been difficult, just then, to have uttered a proposal that would have given him more satisfaction.", "그러니 바로 그때, 이보다 그를 더 흡족하게 할 제안을 입에 올리기란 어려웠을 것이다."], ["Aware of the necessity of preserving the dignity of his imaginary character, however, he repressed his feelings, and answered with suitable mystery,--", "그러나 자신이 꾸며 낸 인물의 위엄을 지킬 필요를 의식하여, 그는 감정을 억누르고 알맞은 신비로움을 담아 대답했다."]], [["\"Spirits differ; some yield to the power of wisdom, while others are too strong.\"", "\"영(靈)들도 저마다 다르오. 더러는 지혜의 힘에 굴복하나, 더러는 너무 강하지요.\""]], [["\"My brother is a great medicine,\"", "\"내 형제는 위대한 주술사로구려.\""], ["said the cunning savage; \"he will try?\"", "그 교활한 야만인이 말했다. \"한번 해 보시겠소?\""]], [["A gesture of assent was the answer.", "대답은 동의의 몸짓이었다."], ["The Huron was content with the assurance, and resuming his pipe, he awaited the proper moment to move.", "〈휴런〉은 그 확답에 만족하여, 다시 파이프를 입에 물고 자리를 옮길 적당한 때를 기다렸다."], ["The impatient Heyward, inwardly execrating the cold customs of the savages, which required such sacrifices to appearance, was fain to assume an air of indifference, equal to that maintained by the chief, who was, in truth, a near relative of the afflicted woman.", "겉치레에 그런 희생을 요구하는 야만인들의 냉랭한 관습을 속으로 저주하던 조급한 〈헤이워드〉는, 추장 못지않은 무심한 태도를 꾸며 내는 수밖에 없었으니, 그 추장은 사실 그 앓는 여인의 가까운 친척이었다."], ["The minutes lingered, and the delay had seemed an hour to the adventurer in empiricism, when the Huron laid aside his pipe, and drew his robe across his breast, as if about to lead the way to the lodge of the invalid.", "시간이 더디게 흘렀고, 이 경험주의 모험가에게는 그 지체가 한 시간처럼 느껴졌을 무렵, 〈휴런〉이 파이프를 내려놓고, 마치 병자의 오두막으로 길을 안내하려는 듯 가슴을 가로질러 겉옷을 끌어당겼다."], ["Just then, a warrior of powerful frame darkened the door, and stalking silently among the attentive group, he seated himself on one end of the low pile of brush which sustained Duncan.", "바로 그때, 건장한 체구의 한 전사가 문간을 어둡게 가리더니, 주의를 모으고 있던 무리 사이를 말없이 성큼성큼 걸어와, 〈덩컨〉을 받치고 있던 낮은 나뭇가지 더미의 한쪽 끝에 앉았다."], ["The latter cast an impatient look at his neighbor, and felt his flesh creep with uncontrollable horror when he found himself in actual contact with Magua.", "후자는 곁에 앉은 이에게 조급한 눈길을 던졌고, 자신이 〈마구아〉와 실제로 맞닿아 있음을 깨닫고는 걷잡을 수 없는 공포에 살갗이 오싹해졌다."]], [["The sudden return of this artful and dreaded chief caused a delay in the departure of the Huron.", "이 교활하고 두려운 추장의 갑작스러운 귀환으로 〈휴런〉의 출발이 늦춰졌다."], ["Several pipes, that had been extinguished, were lighted again; while the newcomer, without speaking a word, drew his tomahawk from his girdle, and filling the bowl on its head, began to inhale the vapors of the weed through the hollow handle, with as much indifference as if he had not been absent two weary days on a long and toilsome hunt.", "꺼졌던 여러 파이프에 다시 불이 붙었다. 그동안 새로 온 자는 말 한마디 없이 허리띠에서 토마호크를 빼 들고, 그 머리에 달린 대통에 담배를 채워, 속이 빈 자루를 통해 잎의 연기를 빨아들이기 시작했으니, 그 무심함이란 마치 길고 고된 사냥에 지친 이틀을 비웠던 일 따위 없었다는 듯했다."], ["Ten minutes, which appeared so many ages to Duncan, might have passed in this manner; and the warriors were fairly enveloped in a cloud of white smoke before any of them spoke.", "〈덩컨〉에게는 무수한 세월처럼 여겨졌을 십 분이 이런 식으로 흘렀을 법했고, 누구 하나 입을 열기도 전에 전사들은 흰 연기구름 속에 온통 휩싸였다."]], [["\"Welcome!\"", "\"잘 왔네!\""], ["one at length uttered; \"has my friend found the moose?\"", "마침내 누군가 입을 열었다. \"내 벗은 사슴을 찾았는가?\""]], [["\"The young men stagger under their burdens,\"", "\"젊은이들이 그 짐에 비틀거리고 있소.\" 〈마구아〉가 대꾸했다."], ["returned Magua. \"Let 'Reed-that-bends'", "\"'휘어지는 갈대'를 사냥길로 보내시오."], ["go on the hunting-path; he will meet them.\"", "그가 그들과 마주칠 게요.\""]], [["A deep and awful silence succeeded the utterance of the forbidden name.", "금기된 이름이 입에 오르자 깊고 무시무시한 침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["Each pipe dropped from the lips of its owner as though all had inhaled an impurity at the same instant.", "마치 모두가 같은 순간에 부정한 것을 들이마신 듯, 저마다의 입술에서 파이프가 떨어졌다."], ["The smoke wreathed above their heads in little eddies, and curling in a spiral form, it ascended swiftly through the opening in the roof of the lodge, leaving the place beneath clear of its fumes, and each dark visage distinctly visible.", "연기가 그들의 머리 위에서 작은 소용돌이를 이루며 감돌다가, 나선을 그리며 오두막 지붕의 트인 구멍으로 빠르게 솟아올라, 그 아래 자리를 연기 없이 맑게 비우니, 거뭇한 얼굴 하나하나가 또렷이 보였다."], ["The looks of most of the warriors were riveted on the earth; though a few of the younger and less gifted of the party suffered their wild and glaring eyeballs to roll in the direction of a white-headed savage, who sat between two of the most venerated chiefs of the tribe.", "대다수 전사의 시선은 땅바닥에 못 박혀 있었으나, 무리 가운데 더 젊고 덜 영민한 몇몇은 그 사납고 번득이는 눈알을 굴려, 부족에서 가장 존경받는 두 추장 사이에 앉은 한 백발의 야만인 쪽으로 향하게 두었다."], ["There was nothing in the air or attire of this Indian that would seem to entitle him to such a distinction.", "이 인디언의 풍채나 차림에는 그런 영예를 누릴 만한 구석이라곤 없어 보였다."], ["The former was rather depressed, than remarkable for the bearing of the natives; and the latter was such as was commonly worn by the ordinary men of the nation.", "전자는 원주민 특유의 당당함이라기보다는 오히려 풀이 죽어 있었고, 후자는 그 부족의 평범한 사내들이 으레 걸치는 것에 지나지 않았다."], ["Like most around him, for more than a minute his look too was on the ground; but, trusting his eyes at length to steal a glance aside, he perceived that he was becoming an object of general attention.", "그도 주위의 대다수처럼 일 분 남짓 시선을 땅에 두고 있었으나, 마침내 곁눈질로 슬쩍 둘러보매, 자신이 뭇사람의 주목 대상이 되어 가고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["Then he arose and lifted his voice in the general silence.", "그러자 그는 일어나, 그 모두의 침묵 속에 목소리를 높였다."]], [["\"It was a lie,\"", "\"그것은 거짓이었소.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"I had no son.", "\"내게는 아들이 없었소. 그 이름으로 불리던 자는 잊혔소."], ["He who was called by that name is forgotten; his blood was pale; and it came not from the veins of a Huron; the wicked Chippewas cheated my squaw.", "그의 피는 창백했고, 그것은 〈휴런〉의 혈관에서 나온 것이 아니오. 사악한 〈치페와〉족이 내 아내를 속였소."], ["The Great Spirit has said, that the family of Wiss-entush should end; he is happy who knows that the evil of his race dies with himself.", "'위대한 영(靈)'께서 〈위센투시〉의 가문이 끊어지리라 말씀하셨소. 제 핏줄의 악이 저와 함께 죽음을 아는 자는 행복하오."], ["I have done.\"", "나는 다 말했소.\""]], [["The speaker, who was the father of the recreant young Indian, looked round and about him, as if seeking commendation of his stoicism in the eyes of his auditors.", "겁쟁이 젊은 인디언의 아버지였던 이 발언자는, 마치 듣는 이들의 눈에서 자신의 극기에 대한 칭송을 구하기라도 하듯 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["But the stern customs of his people had made too severe an exaction of the feeble old man.", "그러나 그 백성의 준엄한 관습은 이 쇠약한 노인에게 너무도 가혹한 것을 요구했다."], ["The expression of his eye contradicted his figurative and boastful language, while every muscle in his wrinkled visage was working with anguish.", "그 눈빛은 그의 비유적이고 호언하는 말과 모순되었고, 주름진 얼굴의 근육 하나하나가 고뇌로 일그러지고 있었다."], ["Standing a single minute to enjoy his bitter triumph, he turned away, as if sickening at the gaze of men, and veiling his face in his blanket, he walked from the lodge with the noiseless step of an Indian, seeking, in the privacy of his own abode, the sympathy of one like himself, aged, forlorn, and childless.", "자신의 쓰디쓴 승리를 누리려 한순간을 서 있던 그는, 마치 사람들의 시선에 진저리가 난 듯 몸을 돌려, 담요로 얼굴을 가린 채 인디언 특유의 소리 없는 걸음으로 오두막을 걸어 나갔으니, 제 거처의 은밀함 속에서 자기와 같은 처지의, 늙고 외롭고 자식 없는 이의 위안을 찾으려는 것이었다."]], [["The Indians, who believe in the hereditary transmission of virtues and defects in character, suffered him to depart in silence.", "성격의 미덕과 결함이 대대로 전해진다고 믿는 인디언들은, 그가 말없이 떠나도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Then, with an elevation of breeding that many in a more cultivated state of society might profitably emulate, one of the chiefs drew the attention of the young men from the weakness they had just witnessed, by saying, in a cheerful voice, addressing himself in courtesy to Magua, as the newest comer,--", "그러자 한 추장이, 보다 문명화된 사회의 많은 이들이 본받아 이로움을 얻을 만한 고매한 교양으로, 가장 늦게 온 〈마구아〉에게 예의를 갖춰 말을 건네며, 방금 목격한 나약함에서 젊은이들의 주의를 거두려 쾌활한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"The Delawares have been like bears after the honey-pots, prowling around my village.", "\"〈델라웨어〉족이 꿀단지를 노리는 곰들처럼 내 마을 주위를 어슬렁대더군."], ["But who has ever found a Huron asleep?\"", "허나 〈휴런〉이 잠든 것을 본 자가 일찍이 있었던가?\""]], [["The darkness of the impending cloud which precedes a burst of thunder was not blacker than the brow of Magua as he exclaimed,--", "천둥이 터지기에 앞서 몰려드는 먹구름의 어둠도, 〈마구아〉가 이렇게 외칠 때의 그 이마보다 더 검지는 못했다."]], [["\"The Delawares of the Lakes!\"", "\"호수의 〈델라웨어〉들 말이오!\""]], [["\"Not so.", "\"아닐세."], ["They who wear the petticoats of squaws, on their own river.", "제 강가에서 아낙네의 치마를 두른 자들 말이야."], ["One of them has been passing the tribe.\"", "그중 하나가 부족 곁을 지나가고 있었네.\""]], [["\"Did my young men take his scalp?\"", "\"내 젊은이들이 그자의 머리 가죽을 벗겼소?\""]], [["\"His legs were good, though his arm is better for the hoe than the tomahawk,\"", "\"다리는 좋더군. 허나 그 팔은 토마호크보다 괭이에 더 어울리지.\""], ["returned the other, pointing to the immovable form of Uncas.", "상대가 〈언카스〉의 미동도 없는 형상을 가리키며 대꾸했다."]], [["Instead of manifesting any womanish curiosity to feast his eyes with the sight of a captive from a people he was known to have so much reason to hate, Magua continued to smoke, with the meditative air that he usually maintained, when there was no immediate call on his cunning or his eloquence.", "자신이 그토록 미워할 까닭이 많다고 알려진 부족에서 온 포로를 보며 눈요기하려는 아녀자 같은 호기심을 드러내기는커녕, 〈마구아〉는 자신의 교활함이나 웅변이 당장 필요치 않을 때면 으레 짓던 그 사색에 잠긴 태도로 계속 담배를 피웠다."], ["Although secretly amazed at the facts communicated by the speech of the aged father, he permitted himself to ask no questions, reserving his inquiries for a more suitable moment.", "늙은 아버지의 말에서 전해진 사실에 속으로는 놀랐으나, 그는 더 적당한 때로 캐물음을 미루며 자신에게 아무런 질문도 허락하지 않았다."], ["It was only after a sufficient interval that he shook the ashes from his pipe, replaced the tomahawk, tightened his girdle, and arose, casting for the first time a glance in the direction of the prisoner, who stood a little behind him.", "충분한 시간이 흐른 뒤에야 그는 파이프에서 재를 털어 내고, 토마호크를 제자리에 꽂고, 허리띠를 조이고는 일어서며, 자신의 조금 뒤에 서 있던 죄수 쪽으로 처음으로 눈길을 던졌다."], ["The wary, though seemingly abstracted Uncas, caught a glimpse of the movement, and turning suddenly to the light, their looks met.", "멍하니 있는 듯하면서도 빈틈없던 〈언카스〉가 그 움직임을 언뜻 알아채고, 불빛 쪽으로 홱 돌아서니, 두 사람의 시선이 마주쳤다."], ["Near a minute these two bold and untamed spirits stood regarding one another steadily in the eye, neither quailing in the least before the fierce gaze he encountered.", "거의 일 분 동안, 이 대담하고 길들지 않은 두 영혼은 서로의 눈을 똑바로 응시하며 섰으니, 어느 쪽도 자신이 맞닥뜨린 사나운 시선 앞에서 조금도 움츠러들지 않았다."], ["The form of Uncas dilated, and his nostrils opened like those of a tiger at bay; but so rigid and unyielding was his posture, that he might easily have been converted by the imagination into an exquisite and faultless representation of the warlike deity of his tribe.", "〈언카스〉의 몸이 부풀어 오르고, 그 콧구멍이 궁지에 몰린 호랑이처럼 벌어졌으나, 그 자세가 어찌나 굳건하고 흔들림 없던지, 상상 속에서 그는 손쉽게 제 부족의 호전적인 신을 그린 정교하고 흠 없는 형상으로 탈바꿈할 만했다."], ["The lineaments of the quivering features of Magua proved more ductile; his countenance gradually lost its character of defiance in an expression of ferocious joy, and heaving a breath from the very bottom of his chest, he pronounced aloud the very formidable name of--", "〈마구아〉의 떨리는 이목구비의 윤곽은 더 유연하게 변했다. 그의 표정은 차차 도전의 빛깔을 잃고 흉포한 환희의 표정으로 바뀌더니, 가슴 밑바닥에서부터 숨을 한 번 몰아쉬며, 그는 그 더없이 가공할 이름을 소리 내어 입에 올렸다."]], [["\"Le Cerf Agile!\"", "\"〈민첩한 사슴〉!\""]], [["Each warrior sprang upon his feet at the utterance of the well known appellation, and there was a short period during which the stoical constancy of the natives was completely conquered by surprise.", "이 익히 알려진 별칭이 입에 오르자 전사들은 저마다 벌떡 일어섰고, 잠시 동안 원주민들의 극기 어린 의연함이 놀라움에 완전히 무너졌다."], ["The hated and yet respected name was repeated as by one voice, carrying the sound even beyond the limits of the lodge.", "미움받으면서도 존경받는 그 이름이 한목소리인 양 되풀이되어, 그 소리는 오두막 경계 너머까지 실려 갔다."], ["The women and children, who lingered around the entrance, took up the words in an echo, which was succeeded by another shrill and plaintive howl.", "입구 주위를 서성이던 여인과 아이들이 그 말을 받아 메아리로 되뇌었고, 그 뒤를 또 다른 날카롭고 구슬픈 울부짖음이 이었다."], ["The latter was not yet ended, when the sensation among the men had entirely abated.", "후자가 채 끝나기도 전에, 사내들 사이의 동요는 완전히 가라앉았다."], ["Each one in presence seated himself, as though ashamed of his precipitation; but it was many minutes before their meaning eyes ceased to roll towards their captive, in curious examination of a warrior who had so often proved his prowess on the best and proudest of their nation.", "그 자리의 저마다가 마치 제 성급함이 부끄러운 듯 도로 앉았으나, 그토록 자주 자기 부족의 가장 뛰어나고 가장 당당한 자들을 상대로 용맹을 입증해 온 한 전사를 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피느라, 그들의 의미심장한 눈길이 포로 쪽으로 굴러가기를 멈춘 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다."], ["Uncas enjoyed his victory, but was content with merely exhibiting his triumph by a quiet smile--an emblem of scorn which belongs to all time and every nation.", "〈언카스〉는 자신의 승리를 누렸으나, 그저 잔잔한 미소로 자신의 개가를 드러내는 것으로 만족했으니, 그것은 모든 시대와 모든 민족에 속한 경멸의 표상이었다."]], [["Magua caught the expression, and raising his arm, he shook it at the captive, the light silver ornaments attached to his bracelet rattling with the trembling agitation of the limb, as, in a tone of vengeance, he exclaimed, in English,--", "〈마구아〉가 그 표정을 알아채고는 팔을 들어 포로를 향해 흔드니, 팔뚝에 매달린 가벼운 은 장식이 떨리는 팔의 동요에 덜그럭거렸고, 그는 복수에 찬 어조로 영어로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Mohican, you die!\"", "\"〈모히칸〉, 너는 죽는다!\""]], [["\"The healing waters will never bring the dead Hurons to life,\"", "\"치유의 물도 죽은 〈휴런〉들을 결코 되살리지는 못한다.\" 〈언카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉어의 가락으로 대꾸했다."], ["returned Uncas, in the music of the Delawares; \"the tumbling river washes their bones; their men are squaws; their women owls.", "\"굽이치는 강물이 그들의 뼈를 씻어 내리지. 그들의 사내는 아낙네요, 그들의 아낙네는 올빼미다."], ["Go!", "가라!"], ["call together the Huron dogs, that they may look upon a warrior.", "〈휴런〉의 개들을 한데 불러 모아, 전사를 우러러보게 하라."], ["My nostrils are offended; they scent the blood of a coward.\"", "내 콧구멍이 노여워한다. 겁쟁이의 피 냄새를 맡으니.\""]], [["The latter allusion struck deep, and the injury rankled.", "이 마지막 비아냥이 깊이 찔러, 그 상처가 사무쳤다."], ["Many of the Hurons understood the strange tongue in which the captive spoke, among which number was Magua.", "〈휴런〉족의 다수가 포로가 쓰는 낯선 말을 알아들었고, 그 가운데에 〈마구아〉도 끼어 있었다."], ["This cunning savage beheld, and instantly profited by his advantage.", "이 교활한 야만인은 그것을 알아보고는 곧장 자신의 이점을 활용했다."], ["Dropping the light robe of skin from his shoulder, he stretched forth his arm, and commenced a burst of his dangerous and artful eloquence.", "어깨에서 가벼운 가죽 겉옷을 늘어뜨려 떨구고, 그는 팔을 뻗어 위험하고도 교묘한 웅변을 터뜨리기 시작했다."], ["However much his influence among his people had been impaired by his occasional and besetting weakness, as well as by his desertion of the tribe, his courage and his fame as an orator were undeniable.", "이따금 들러붙는 그의 고질적 약점뿐 아니라 부족을 저버린 일로 그의 백성 사이에서 영향력이 꽤 손상되었을지언정, 그의 용기와 웅변가로서의 명성은 부인할 수 없는 것이었다."], ["He never spoke without auditors, and rarely without making converts to his opinions.", "그는 듣는 이 없이 말한 적이 없었고, 자기 의견에 동조하는 자를 만들지 않고 말을 마치는 일이 드물었다."], ["On the present occasion, his native powers were stimulated by the thirst of revenge.", "이번에는 그의 타고난 역량이 복수의 갈증으로 한층 자극받았다."]], [["He again recounted the events of the attack on the island at Glenn's, the death of his associates, and the escape of their most formidable enemies.", "그는 〈글렌〉의 섬에서 있었던 습격과, 자기 동료들의 죽음, 그리고 더없이 가공할 적들이 달아난 일을 다시 들려주었다."], ["Then he described the nature and position of the mount whither he had led such captives as had fallen into their hands.", "이어 그는 손아귀에 떨어진 포로들을 이끌고 갔던 그 산의 성질과 위치를 묘사했다."], ["Of his own bloody intentions towards the maidens, and of his baffled malice he made no mention, but passed rapidly on to the surprise of the party by La Longue Carabine, and its fatal termination.", "처녀들을 향한 자신의 피비린내 나는 의도며, 좌절된 자신의 악의에 관해서는 일언반구도 없이, 그는 〈긴 소총〉에게 일행이 기습당한 일과 그 치명적인 결말로 재빨리 넘어갔다."], ["Here he paused, and looked about him, in affected veneration for the departed, but, in truth, to note the effect of his opening narrative.", "여기서 그는 말을 멈추고, 죽은 자들을 향한 짐짓 꾸민 경의를 띠고 사방을 둘러보았으나, 실은 자기 이야기의 서두가 미친 영향을 살피려는 것이었다."], ["As usual, every eye was riveted on his face.", "여느 때처럼 모든 눈이 그의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["Each dusky figure seemed a breathing statue, so motionless was the posture, so intense the attention of the individual.", "거뭇한 형상 하나하나가 숨 쉬는 조각상 같았으니, 그 자세가 그토록 미동도 없고, 저마다의 주의가 그토록 골똘했다."]], [["Then Magua dropped his voice, which had hitherto been clear, strong, and elevated, and touched upon the merits of the dead.", "이어 〈마구아〉는, 여태 맑고 강하고 드높던 목소리를 낮추어, 죽은 자들의 공덕을 짚었다."], ["No quality that was likely to command the sympathy of an Indian escaped his notice.", "인디언의 공감을 불러일으킬 법한 자질이라면 어느 하나도 그의 주목을 벗어나지 못했다."], ["One had never been known to follow the chase in vain; another had been indefatigable on the trail of their enemies.", "하나는 사냥에 나서 헛되이 돌아온 적이 없었고, 또 하나는 적의 자취를 쫓는 데 지칠 줄 몰랐다."], ["This was brave, that generous.", "이자는 용감했고, 저자는 너그러웠다."], ["In short, he so managed his allusions, that in a nation, which was composed of so few families, he contrived to strike every chord that might find, in its turn, some breast in which to vibrate.", "요컨대 그는, 그토록 적은 수의 가문으로 이루어진 한 부족에서, 차례로 어느 가슴엔가 울림을 찾아낼 모든 현(絃)을 건드리도록 자신의 언급을 교묘히 다루었다."]], [["\"Are the bones of my young men,\"", "\"내 젊은이들의 뼈가,\""], ["he concluded, \"in the burial-place of the Hurons?", "그가 끝맺었다. \"〈휴런〉족의 매장지에 있는가?"], ["You know they are not.", "여러분은 그렇지 않음을 알고 있소."], ["Their spirits are gone towards the setting sun, and are already crossing the great waters, to the happy hunting-grounds.", "그들의 영혼은 지는 해를 향해 떠나, 행복한 사냥터를 향해 이미 큰물을 건너고 있소."], ["But they departed without food, without guns or knives, without moccasins, naked and poor as they were born.", "허나 그들은 양식도, 총이나 칼도, 모카신도 없이, 태어났을 적처럼 헐벗고 가난한 채로 떠났소."], ["Shall this be?", "이래도 되겠소?"], ["Are their souls to enter the land of the just like hungry Iroquois or unmanly Delawares; or shall they meet their friends with arms in their hands and robes on their backs?", "그들의 혼이 굶주린 〈이로쿼이〉나 사내답지 못한 〈델라웨어〉처럼 의인들의 땅에 들어가야 하겠소, 아니면 손에 무기를 쥐고 등에 겉옷을 두르고 제 벗들을 맞아야 하겠소?"], ["What will our fathers think the tribes of the Wyandots have become?", "우리 조상들이 〈와이언도트〉의 부족들이 무엇이 되었다 여기겠소?"], ["They will look on their children with a dark eye, and say, Go!", "그들은 어두운 눈으로 제 자식들을 바라보며, 가라!"], ["a Chippewa has come hither with the name of a Huron.", "〈치페와〉가 〈휴런〉의 이름을 달고 여기 왔구나, 하고 말할 것이오."], ["Brothers, we must not forget the dead; a redskin never ceases to remember.", "형제들이여, 우리는 죽은 자들을 잊어서는 아니 되오. 붉은 피부의 사람은 결코 기억을 그치지 않소."], ["We will load the back of this Mohican until he staggers under our bounty, and despatch him after my young men.", "우리는 이 〈모히칸〉의 등에 짐을 잔뜩 실어 우리의 후한 선물에 비틀거리게 한 다음, 그를 내 젊은이들의 뒤로 보낼 것이오."], ["They call to us for aid, though our ears are not open; they say, Forget us not.", "우리 귀는 열려 있지 않으나, 그들은 우리에게 도움을 청하고 있소. 그들은 말하오, 우리를 잊지 말라고."], ["When they see the spirit of this Mohican toiling after them with his burden, they will know we are of that mind.", "이 〈모히칸〉의 혼이 짐을 지고 자기들 뒤를 따라 애쓰는 것을 보면, 그들은 우리가 그런 마음임을 알 것이오."], ["Then will they go on happy; and our children will say, 'So did our fathers to their friends, so must we do to them.'", "그러면 그들은 행복하게 나아갈 것이고, 우리 자식들은 말할 것이오, '우리 조상들이 제 벗들에게 이리하였으니, 우리도 그들에게 마땅히 이리해야 한다'라고."], ["What is a Yengee?", "〈양키〉가 무엇이오?"], ["we have slain many, but the earth is still pale.", "우리가 많이도 죽였으나, 땅은 여전히 창백하오."], ["A stain on the name of a Huron can only be hid by blood that comes from the veins of an Indian.", "〈휴런〉의 이름에 묻은 오점은 오직 인디언의 혈관에서 나온 피로만 가릴 수 있소."], ["Let this Delaware die.\"", "이 〈델라웨어〉를 죽게 합시다.\""]], [["The effect of such an harangue, delivered in the nervous language and with the emphatic manner of a Huron orator, could scarcely be mistaken.", "〈휴런〉 웅변가의 격정적인 말씨와 힘 있는 태도로 전해진 이런 열변의 효과는, 좀처럼 잘못 짚을 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Magua had so artfully blended the natural sympathies with the religious superstition of his auditors, that their minds, already prepared by custom to sacrifice a victim to the _manes_ of their countrymen, lost every vestige of humanity in a wish for revenge.", "〈마구아〉가 타고난 공감을 듣는 이들의 종교적 미신과 어찌나 교묘히 뒤섞어 놓았던지, 동족의 망령에게 제물을 바치는 일에 관습으로 이미 길든 그들의 마음은, 복수의 열망 속에 인간다움의 자취를 모조리 잃고 말았다."], ["One warrior in particular, a man of wild and ferocious mien, had been conspicuous for the attention he had given to the words of the speaker.", "특히 한 전사가, 사납고 흉포한 인상의 사내가, 발언자의 말에 기울인 주의로 유독 눈에 띄었다."], ["His countenance had changed with each passing emotion, until it settled into a look of deadly malice.", "그의 표정은 스쳐 가는 감정마다 따라 변하더니, 마침내 치명적인 악의의 표정으로 굳어졌다."], ["As Magua ended he arose, and uttering the yell of a demon, his polished little axe was seen glancing in the torch-light as he whirled it above his head.", "〈마구아〉가 말을 끝맺자 그는 일어나, 악마의 비명을 내지르며, 머리 위로 휘두르는 그의 윤이 나는 작은 도끼가 횃불 빛에 번쩍이는 것이 보였다."], ["The motion and the cry were too sudden for words to interrupt his bloody intention.", "그 동작과 외침은 너무도 갑작스러워 말로는 그 피비린내 나는 의도를 가로막을 수 없었다."], ["It appeared as if a bright gleam shot from his hand, which was crossed at the same moment by a dark and powerful line.", "그의 손에서 밝은 섬광이 뻗어 나오는 듯했고, 같은 순간 거뭇하고 힘찬 선 하나가 그것을 가로질렀다."], ["The former was the tomahawk in its passage; the latter the arm that Magua darted forward to divert its aim.", "전자는 날아가는 토마호크였고, 후자는 그 겨냥을 빗나가게 하려 〈마구아〉가 앞으로 내쏜 팔이었다."], ["The quick and ready motion of the chief was not entirely too late.", "추장의 재빠르고 민첩한 동작이 영 늦은 것은 아니었다."], ["The keen weapon cut the war-plume from the scalping-tuft of Uncas, and passed through the frail wall of the lodge, as though it were hurled from some formidable engine.", "그 날카로운 무기는 〈언카스〉의 머리 가죽 술에서 전투 깃털을 베어 내고, 무슨 가공할 기계에서 던져지기라도 한 듯 오두막의 허술한 벽을 뚫고 지나갔다."]], [["Duncan had seen the threatening action, and sprang upon his feet, with a heart which while it leaped into his throat, swelled with the most generous resolution in behalf of his friend.", "〈덩컨〉은 그 위협적인 동작을 보고 벌떡 일어섰으니, 그 가슴은 목구멍으로 튀어 오르는 한편, 친구를 위한 더없이 고결한 결의로 부풀어 올랐다."], ["A glance told him that the blow had failed, and terror changed to admiration.", "한 번의 눈길로 그는 그 일격이 빗나갔음을 알았고, 공포는 감탄으로 바뀌었다."], ["Uncas stood still, looking his enemy in the eye with features that seemed superior to emotion.", "〈언카스〉는 가만히 서서, 감정 따위는 초월한 듯한 얼굴로 적의 눈을 응시했다."], ["Marble could not be colder, calmer, or steadier than the countenance he put upon this sudden and vindictive attack.", "이 갑작스럽고 앙심 어린 공격에 그가 지은 얼굴보다 더 차갑고, 더 침착하고, 더 흔들림 없는 대리석은 없었을 것이다."], ["Then, as if pitying a want of skill which had proved so fortunate to himself, he smiled, and muttered a few words of contempt in his own tongue.", "이어 마치 그 자신에게는 그토록 다행이었던 솜씨의 모자람을 가엾이 여기듯, 그는 미소 지으며 제 부족어로 경멸의 말 몇 마디를 중얼거렸다."]], [["\"No!\"", "\"안 돼!\" 포로의 무사함을 확인하고 나서 〈마구아〉가 말했다."], ["said Magua, after satisfying himself of the safety of the captive; \"the sun must shine on his shame; the squaws must see his flesh tremble, or our revenge will be like the play of boys.", "\"그의 수치 위로 해가 비쳐야 한다. 아낙네들이 그의 살이 떠는 것을 보아야 한다."], ["Go!", "그러지 않으면 우리 복수는 아이들 장난과 같아질 것이다. 가라!"], ["take him where there is silence; let us see if a Delaware can sleep at night, and in the morning die.\"", "고요한 곳으로 그를 데려가라. 〈델라웨어〉가 밤에 잠들 수 있는지, 그리고 아침에 죽을 수 있는지 보자.\""]], [["The young men whose duty it was to guard the prisoner instantly passed their ligaments of bark across his arms, and led him from the lodge, amid a profound and ominous silence.", "죄수를 지키는 임무를 맡은 젊은이들은 곧장 나무껍질 끈을 그의 팔에 걸쳐 묶고는, 깊고 불길한 침묵 속에 그를 오두막에서 끌고 나갔다."], ["It was only as the figure of Uncas stood in the opening of the door that his firm step hesitated.", "〈언카스〉의 형상이 문간에 섰을 때, 그 굳건한 걸음이 망설인 것은 그때뿐이었다."], ["There he turned, and, in the sweeping and haughty glance that he threw around the circle of his enemies, Duncan caught a look which he was glad to construe into an expression that he was not entirely deserted by hope.", "거기서 그는 돌아서, 제 적들의 무리를 휙 둘러보는 오만한 눈길을 던졌으니, 그 눈길에서 〈덩컨〉은, 그가 희망에 아주 버림받은 것은 아니라는 표현으로 기꺼이 풀이할 만한 빛 하나를 읽어 냈다."]], [["Magua was content with his success, or too much occupied with his secret purposes to push his inquiries any further.", "〈마구아〉는 자신의 성공에 만족했거나, 아니면 자신의 은밀한 목적에 너무 골몰한 나머지 캐물음을 더 밀고 나가지 않았다."], ["Shaking his mantle, and folding it on his bosom, he also quitted the place, without pursuing a subject which might have proved so fatal to the individual at his elbow.", "외투를 떨쳐 가슴께로 여미며, 그 역시, 제 팔꿈치 곁의 자에게 그토록 치명적일 수도 있었을 주제를 좇지 않은 채, 그곳을 떠났다."], ["Notwithstanding his rising resentment, his natural firmness, and his anxiety in behalf of Uncas, Heyward felt sensibly relieved by the absence of so dangerous and so subtle a foe.", "치미는 분노와 타고난 굳건함, 그리고 〈언카스〉를 향한 근심에도 불구하고, 〈헤이워드〉는 그토록 위험하고 교활한 적이 없어지자 눈에 띄게 마음이 놓였다."], ["The excitement produced by the speech gradually subsided.", "그 연설이 자아낸 흥분은 차차 가라앉았다."], ["The warriors resumed their seats, and clouds of smoke once more filled the lodge.", "전사들은 다시 자리에 앉았고, 연기구름이 또 한 번 오두막을 가득 채웠다."], ["For near half an hour, not a syllable was uttered, or scarcely a look cast aside; a grave and meditative silence being the ordinary succession to every scene of violence and commotion among those beings, who were alike so impetuous and yet so self-restrained.", "거의 반 시간 동안 음절 하나 입에 오르지 않았고, 곁으로 던지는 눈길 하나 거의 없었으니, 그토록 격정적이면서도 또 그토록 자제력 있는 이 존재들 사이에서는, 폭력과 소란의 장면마다 진중하고 사색에 잠긴 침묵이 으레 그 뒤를 잇는 법이었다."]], [["When the chief who had solicited the aid of Duncan finished his pipe, he made a final and successful movement towards departing.", "〈덩컨〉의 도움을 청했던 추장이 파이프를 다 피우자, 그는 마침내 떠나려는 마지막 몸짓을 성공적으로 취했다."], ["A motion of a finger was the intimation he gave the supposed physician to follow; and passing through the clouds of smoke, Duncan was glad, on more accounts than one, to be able, at last, to breathe the pure air of a cool and refreshing summer evening.", "손가락 하나의 움직임이 의사로 가장한 자에게 따라오라고 건넨 신호였다. 연기구름 사이를 지나며, 〈덩컨〉은 마침내 서늘하고 상쾌한 여름밤의 맑은 공기를 들이쉴 수 있게 된 것이, 한 가지 이상의 이유로 기뻤다."]], [["Instead of pursuing his way among those lodges where Heyward had already made his unsuccessful search, his companion turned aside, and proceeded directly towards the base of an adjacent mountain, which overhung the temporary village.", "그의 동행은, 〈헤이워드〉가 이미 헛되이 탐색했던 그 오두막들 사이로 길을 잡는 대신, 옆으로 비켜, 임시 마을을 굽어보는 인근 산기슭으로 곧장 나아갔다."], ["A thicket of brush skirted its foot, and it became necessary to proceed through a crooked and narrow path.", "덤불 수풀이 그 산자락을 두르고 있어, 구부러지고 좁은 길을 지나야만 했다."], ["The boys had resumed their sports in the clearing, and were enacting a mimic chase to the post among themselves.", "사내아이들은 빈터에서 놀이를 다시 시작하여, 저희끼리 형틀까지 쫓아가는 흉내 놀이를 펼치고 있었다."], ["In order to render their games as like the reality as possible, one of the boldest of their number had conveyed a few brands into some piles of tree-tops that had hitherto escaped the burning.", "놀이를 되도록 실제와 비슷하게 하려고, 그중 가장 대담한 한 아이가 여태 불길을 면했던 나무 우듬지 더미 몇 군데에 불씨를 몇 개 옮겨 놓았다."], ["The blaze of one of these fires lighted the way of the chief and Duncan, and gave a character of additional wildness to the rude scenery.", "그 불 하나의 불꽃이 추장과 〈덩컨〉의 길을 밝혀, 거친 풍경에 한층 야성적인 빛깔을 더했다."], ["At a little distance from a bald rock, and directly in its front, they entered a grassy opening, which they prepared to cross.", "민둥바위에서 조금 떨어진, 바로 그 앞에서, 그들은 풀 무성한 빈터로 들어서 그곳을 가로지를 채비를 했다."], ["Just then fresh fuel was added to the fire, and a powerful light penetrated even to that distant spot.", "바로 그때 불에 새 땔감이 더해져, 그 멀리 떨어진 자리에까지 강한 빛이 스며들었다."], ["It fell upon the white surface of the mountain, and was reflected downwards upon a dark and mysterious-looking being that arose, unexpectedly, in their path.", "그 빛은 산의 흰 표면에 떨어졌다가, 아래쪽으로, 그들의 길에 뜻밖에 일어선 거뭇하고 알 수 없어 보이는 한 존재 위로 반사되었다."]], [["The Indian paused, as if doubtful whether to proceed, and permitted his companion to approach his side.", "인디언은 마치 나아가야 할지 망설이는 듯 멈춰 서며, 동행이 자기 곁으로 다가오도록 두었다."], ["A large black ball, which at first seemed stationary, now began to move in a manner that to the latter was inexplicable.", "처음엔 멈춰 있는 듯하던 커다란 검은 덩어리가, 이제 후자로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 식으로 움직이기 시작했다."], ["Again the fire brightened, and its glare fell more distinctly on the object.", "다시 불이 밝아지며, 그 불빛이 그 물체에 한층 또렷이 떨어졌다."], ["Then even Duncan knew it, by its restless and sideling attitudes, which kept the upper part of its form in constant motion, while the animal itself appeared seated, to be a bear.", "그러자 〈덩컨〉마저, 윗부분을 끊임없이 움직이게 하면서도 짐승 자체는 앉아 있는 듯한 그 쉴 새 없고 비스듬한 자세로 보아, 그것이 곰임을 알아보았다."], ["Though it growled loudly and fiercely, and there were instants when its glistening eyeballs might be seen, it gave no other indications of hostility.", "그것은 크고 사납게 으르렁대었고, 번득이는 눈알이 보이는 순간들도 있었으나, 그 밖에 적의의 기색은 보이지 않았다."], ["The Huron, at least, seemed assured that the intentions of this singular intruder were peaceable, for after giving it an attentive examination, he quietly pursued his course.", "적어도 〈휴런〉은 이 기이한 침입자의 의도가 평화로운 것이라 확신한 듯했으니, 그것을 주의 깊이 살펴본 뒤 조용히 가던 길을 계속했다."]], [["Duncan, who knew that the animal was often domesticated among the Indians, followed the example of his companion, believing that some favorite of the tribe had found its way into the thicket, in search of food.", "곰이 인디언들 사이에서 흔히 길들여지곤 한다는 것을 알던 〈덩컨〉은, 부족이 아끼는 어떤 짐승이 먹이를 찾아 수풀로 흘러들었으려니 여기며, 동행의 본을 따랐다."], ["They passed it unmolested.", "그들은 아무 방해도 받지 않고 그것을 지나쳤다."], ["Though obliged to come nearly in contact with the monster, the Huron, who had at first so warily determined the character of his strange visitor, was now content with proceeding without wasting a moment in further examination; but Heyward was unable to prevent his eyes from looking backward, in salutary watchfulness against attacks in the rear.", "그 괴물과 거의 맞닿을 만큼 가까이 가야 했음에도, 처음엔 그토록 조심스레 이 기이한 방문객의 정체를 가늠하던 〈휴런〉은, 이제 더는 살피느라 한순간도 허비하지 않고 나아가는 데 만족했다. 그러나 〈헤이워드〉는, 후방의 공격을 경계하는 유익한 조심성에서, 뒤를 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["His uneasiness was in no degree diminished when he perceived the beast rolling along their path, and following their footsteps.", "그 짐승이 그들의 길을 따라 굴러오며 발걸음을 좇는 것을 알아채자, 그의 불안은 조금도 줄지 않았다."], ["He would have spoken, but the Indian at that moment shoved aside a door of bark, and entered a cavern in the bosom of the mountain.", "그가 막 입을 열려 했으나, 바로 그때 인디언이 나무껍질 문 하나를 밀쳐 내고 산속 품에 자리한 동굴로 들어섰다."]], [["Profiting by so easy a method of retreat, Duncan stepped after him, and was gladly closing the slight cover to the opening, when he felt it drawn from his hand by the beast, whose shaggy form immediately darkened the passage.", "이토록 손쉬운 후퇴 방법을 활용하여, 〈덩컨〉은 그를 뒤따라 발을 들이고, 그 입구의 허술한 덮개를 기꺼이 닫으려던 참이었는데, 그것이 제 손에서 곰에게 끌려가는 것을 느꼈으니, 그 텁수룩한 형체가 곧 통로를 어둡게 가렸다."], ["They were now in a straight and long gallery, in a chasm of the rocks, where retreat without encountering the animal was impossible.", "그들은 이제 바위틈에 난 곧고 긴 회랑 안에 있었으니, 그 짐승과 마주치지 않고 물러나기란 불가능했다."], ["Making the best of the circumstances, the young man pressed forward, keeping as close as possible to his conductor.", "형편이 형편인지라, 청년은 되도록 안내인에게 바짝 붙어 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["The bear growled frequently at his heels, and once or twice its enormous paws were laid on his person, as if disposed to prevent his further passage into the den.", "곰은 그의 발치에서 자주 으르렁댔고, 한두 번은 그 거대한 앞발을 그의 몸에 얹어, 마치 그가 굴속으로 더 나아가는 것을 막으려는 듯했다."]], [["How long the nerves of Heyward would have sustained him in this extraordinary situation, it might be difficult to decide; for, happily, he soon found relief.", "이 기이한 처지에서 〈헤이워드〉의 신경이 그를 얼마나 버티게 했을지는 가늠하기 어려웠을 것이다. 다행히도 그는 곧 위안을 얻었기 때문이다."], ["A glimmer of light had constantly been in their front, and they now arrived at the place whence it proceeded.", "어슴푸레한 빛 한 줄기가 줄곧 그들의 앞에 있었고, 그들은 이제 그 빛이 새어 나오는 곳에 다다랐다."]], [["A large cavity in the rock had been rudely fitted to answer the purposes of many apartments.", "바위에 난 커다란 구멍이 여러 방의 쓰임에 맞도록 거칠게 손질되어 있었다."], ["The subdivisions were simple but ingenious, being composed of stone, sticks, and bark, intermingled.", "그 칸막이는 단순하나 독창적이어서, 돌과 막대와 나무껍질을 한데 엮어 만든 것이었다."], ["Openings above admitted the light by day, and at night fires and torches supplied the place of the sun.", "위쪽의 트인 구멍들이 낮에는 빛을 들여보냈고, 밤에는 불과 횃불이 해를 대신했다."], ["Hither the Hurons had brought most of their valuables, especially those which more particularly pertained to the nation; and hither, as it now appeared, the sick woman, who was believed to be the victim of supernatural power, had been transported also, under an impression that her tormentor would find more difficulty in making his assaults through walls of stone than through the leafy coverings of the lodges.", "이곳으로 〈휴런〉족은 그들의 귀중품 대부분을, 특히 부족과 더 각별히 관련된 것들을 가져다 두었다. 그리고 이제 보아하니, 초자연적인 힘의 희생양으로 여겨지던 그 병든 여인 역시, 그녀를 괴롭히는 자가 오두막의 잎사귀 덮개를 뚫는 것보다 돌벽을 뚫고 공격을 가하기가 더 어려우리라는 생각에서, 이곳으로 옮겨져 있었다."], ["The apartment into which Duncan and his guide first entered, had been exclusively devoted to her accommodation.", "〈덩컨〉과 그의 안내인이 처음 들어선 방은 오로지 그녀를 들이는 데 쓰이고 있었다."], ["The latter approached her bedside, which was surrounded by females, in the centre of whom Heyward was surprised to find his missing friend David.", "후자는 그녀의 침상 곁으로 다가갔으니, 그 침상은 여인들에게 둘러싸여 있었고, 그 한가운데에서 〈헤이워드〉는 사라졌던 친구 〈데이비드〉를 발견하고 놀랐다."]], [["A single look was sufficient to apprise the pretended leech that the invalid was far beyond his powers of healing.", "한 번의 눈길로, 가짜 의원은 그 병자가 자신의 치유 능력으로는 도무지 어찌할 수 없을 만큼 위중함을 알아채기에 충분했다."], ["She lay in a sort of paralysis, indifferent to the objects which crowded before her sight, and happily unconscious of suffering.", "그녀는 일종의 마비 상태로 누워, 눈앞에 빽빽이 모여든 것들에 무심했고, 다행히도 고통을 의식하지 못했다."], ["Heyward was far from regretting that his mummeries were to be performed on one who was much too ill to take an interest in their failure or success.", "〈헤이워드〉는 자신의 의식 흉내가, 그 실패나 성공에 관심을 두기에는 너무도 위중한 사람에게 행해진다는 것을, 결코 애석해하지 않았다."], ["The slight qualm of conscience which had been excited by the intended deception was instantly appeased, and he began to collect his thoughts, in order to enact his part with suitable spirit, when he found he was about to be anticipated in his skill by an attempt to prove the power of music.", "꾸미려던 기만이 불러일으킨 가벼운 양심의 가책은 곧장 가라앉았고, 그는 알맞은 기백으로 제 배역을 연기하고자 생각을 가다듬기 시작했는데, 그때 자신의 솜씨가 음악의 힘을 입증하려는 한 시도에 선수를 빼앗기려 함을 알았다."]], [["Gamut, who had stood prepared to pour forth his spirit in song when the visitors entered, after delaying a moment, drew a strain from his pipe, and commenced a hymn that might have worked a miracle, had faith in its efficacy been of much avail.", "방문객들이 들어섰을 때 노래로 제 영혼을 쏟아 낼 채비를 하고 서 있던 〈개멋〉이, 한순간 머뭇거린 뒤, 피리에서 한 가락을 끌어내어, 그 효험에 대한 믿음이 어지간히만 통했더라도 기적을 일으켰을 법한 찬송가를 시작했다."], ["He was allowed to proceed to the close, the Indians respecting his imaginary infirmity, and Duncan too glad of the delay to hazard the slightest interruption.", "그는 끝까지 마치도록 허락받았으니, 인디언들이 그의 짐작된 정신적 결함을 존중한 데다, 〈덩컨〉도 그 지체가 너무 반가워 털끝만 한 방해도 무릅쓰지 못했기 때문이다."], ["As the dying cadence of his strains was falling on the ears of the latter, he started aside at hearing them repeated behind him in a voice half human, half sepulchral.", "그 가락의 죽어 가는 여운이 후자의 귀에 떨어지던 차에, 그는 그것이 등 뒤에서 절반은 인간 같고 절반은 무덤 같은 목소리로 되풀이되는 것을 듣고 화들짝 비켜섰다."], ["Looking around, he beheld the shaggy monster seated on end in a shadow of the cavern, where, while his restless body swung in the uneasy manner of the animal, it repeated, in a sort of low growl, sound, if not words, which bore some slight resemblance to the melody of the singer.", "둘러보니, 그 텁수룩한 괴물이 동굴의 그늘에 몸을 곧추세우고 앉아 있었으니, 그곳에서 그 안절부절못하는 몸뚱이가 그 짐승 특유의 불안한 모양새로 흔들리는 동안, 그것은 일종의 낮은 으르렁거림으로, 가사는 아닐지언정, 노래하는 이의 선율과 얼마간 희미하게 닮은 소리를 되풀이하고 있었다."]], [["The effect of so strange an echo on David may better be imagined than described.", "이토록 기이한 메아리가 〈데이비드〉에게 미친 영향은 묘사하기보다 상상하는 편이 나을 것이다."], ["His eyes opened as if he doubted their truth; and his voice became instantly mute in excess of wonder.", "그의 눈은 마치 제 눈을 의심하기라도 하듯 휘둥그레졌고, 그의 목소리는 지나친 경이에 곧장 멎었다."], ["A deep-laid scheme, of communicating some important intelligence to Heyward, was driven from his recollection by an emotion which very nearly resembled fear, but which he was fain to believe was admiration.", "〈헤이워드〉에게 어떤 중대한 소식을 전하려던 깊이 꾸민 계획은, 두려움과 자못 흡사하나 그 자신은 감탄이라 기꺼이 믿으려 한 어떤 감정에 밀려 그의 기억에서 쫓겨났다. 그 감정에 사로잡혀 그는 소리 높여 외쳤다."], ["Under its influence, he exclaimed aloud--\"She expects you, and is at hand;\"", "\"그분이 당신을 기다리고 계시오, 가까이 있소이다.\""], ["and precipitately left the cavern.", "그러고는 황급히 동굴을 떠났다."]], [["_\"Snug._--Have you the lion's part written?", "\"스너그. 사자 역의 대사를 받아 적으셨소?"], ["Pray you, if it be, give it me, for I am slow of study.\"", "만약 그렇다면, 부디 나에게 주시오. 나는 외는 게 더디니.\""]], [["_\"Quince_.--You may do it extempore, for it is nothing but roaring.\"", "\"퀸스. 즉흥으로 해도 되네. 그저 으르렁대는 것뿐이니까.\""]], [["_Midsummer Night's Dream._", "한여름 밤의 꿈"]], [["There was a strange blending of the ridiculous with that which was solemn in this scene.", "이 장면에는 엄숙한 것과 우스꽝스러운 것이 기묘하게 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["The beast still continued its rolling, and apparently untiring movements, though its ludicrous attempt to imitate the melody of David ceased the instant the latter abandoned the field.", "짐승은 여전히 좌우로 구르는, 보아하니 지칠 줄 모르는 동작을 이어 갔으나, 데이비드의 가락을 흉내 내려던 그 가소로운 시도는 후자가 그 자리를 떠난 순간 멎었다."], ["The words of Gamut were, as has been seen, in his native tongue; and to Duncan they seemed pregnant with some hidden meaning, though nothing present assisted him in discovering the object of their illusion.", "거멋의 말은, 앞서 본 바와 같이 그의 모국어였고, 던컨에게는 그 안에 어떤 숨은 뜻이 잔뜩 배어 있는 듯했으나, 지금 눈앞에 있는 그 무엇도 그 암시의 대상을 알아내는 데 도움이 되지 않았다."], ["A speedy end was, however, put to every conjecture on the subject, by the manner of the chief, who advanced to the bedside of the invalid, and beckoned away the whole group of female attendants that had clustered there to witness the skill of the stranger.", "그러나 이 문제를 둘러싼 온갖 짐작은 추장의 거동으로 곧 끝이 났으니, 그는 병자의 침상 곁으로 다가가, 이 낯선 자의 솜씨를 지켜보려고 그곳에 모여 있던 여인 시중꾼 무리를 죄다 손짓으로 물러가게 했다."], ["He was implicitly, though reluctantly, obeyed; and when the low echo which rang along the hollow natural gallery from the distant closing door had ceased, pointing towards his insensible daughter, he said,--", "마지못해서이긴 했으나, 그들은 군말 없이 그의 명을 따랐고, 멀리서 닫히는 문이 그 텅 빈 천연의 회랑을 따라 울려 보낸 나직한 메아리가 잦아들었을 때, 그는 정신을 잃은 자기 딸을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["\"Now let my brother show his power.\"", "\"이제 내 형제가 그 힘을 보여 주게.\""]], [["Thus unequivocally called on to exercise the functions of his assumed character, Heyward was apprehensive that the smallest delay might prove dangerous.", "이렇게 자신이 떠맡은 배역의 직무를 수행하라고 분명하게 요청받자, 헤이워드는 아주 잠깐의 지체조차 위험을 초래할까 우려했다."], ["Endeavoring then to collect his ideas, he prepared to perform that species of incantation, and those uncouth rites, under which the Indian conjurers are accustomed to conceal their ignorance and impotency.", "그리하여 생각을 가다듬으려 애쓰며, 그는 인디언 주술사들이 흔히 자신의 무지와 무능을 감추는 데 쓰는 그런 종류의 주문과 그 괴상한 의식을 행할 채비를 했다."], ["It is more than probable that, in the disordered state of his thoughts, he would soon have fallen into some suspicious, if not fatal error, had not his incipient attempts been interrupted by a fierce growl from the quadruped.", "어수선한 그의 생각으로 미루어, 만약 그 어설픈 첫 시도가 그 네발짐승의 사나운 으르렁거림에 가로막히지 않았더라면, 그는 머지않아 치명적까지는 아니어도 어떤 의심을 살 만한 실수에 빠지고 말았을 공산이 컸다."], ["Three several times did he renew his efforts to proceed, and as often was he met by the same unaccountable opposition, each interruption seeming more savage and threatening than the preceding.", "그는 세 차례나 거듭 시도를 이어 가려 했으나, 그때마다 똑같이 영문 모를 방해에 부딪혔으니, 그 방해는 매번 앞엣것보다 한층 더 사납고 위협적인 듯했다."]], [["\"The cunning ones are jealous,\"", "\"교활한 자들이 시샘하는군.\""], ["said the Huron; \"I go.", "휴런족 추장이 말했다. \"나는 가겠네."], ["Brother, the woman is the wife of one of my bravest young men; deal justly by her.", "형제여, 저 여인은 내 가장 용맹한 젊은이 가운데 하나의 아내일세. 정당하게 다뤄 주게."], ["Peace!\"", "조용히!\""], ["he added, beckoning to the discontented beast to be quiet; \"I go.\"", "그는 못마땅해하는 짐승에게 잠잠하라고 손짓하며 덧붙였다. \"나는 가네.\""]], [["The chief was as good as his word, and Duncan now found himself alone in that wild and desolate abode, with the helpless invalid, and the fierce and dangerous brute.", "추장은 제 말을 지켰고, 던컨은 이제 이 거칠고 황량한 거처에 무력한 병자와 사납고 위험한 짐승과 함께 홀로 남게 되었다."], ["The latter listened to the movements of the Indian with that air of sagacity that a bear is known to possess, until another echo announced that he had also left the cavern, when it turned and came waddling up to Duncan, before whom it seated itself, in its natural attitude, erect like a man.", "후자는 곰이 지녔다고 알려진 그 영리한 기색으로 인디언의 발걸음에 귀를 기울이고 있다가, 또 다른 메아리가 그 또한 동굴을 떠났음을 알리자, 몸을 돌려 던컨에게로 뒤뚱뒤뚱 다가와 그 앞에서 사람처럼 꼿꼿이 선 본래의 자세로 주저앉았다."], ["The youth looked anxiously about him for some weapon, with which he might make a resistance against the attack he now seriously expected.", "청년은 이제 진지하게 예상하고 있는 그 공격에 맞설 만한 무기가 없을까 하여 근심스레 주위를 살폈다."]], [["It seemed, however, as if the humor of the animal had suddenly changed.", "그러나 보아하니 그 짐승의 기분은 별안간 달라진 듯했다."], ["Instead of continuing its discontented growls, or manifesting any further signs of anger, the whole of its shaggy body shook violently, as if agitated by some strange internal convulsion.", "못마땅한 으르렁거림을 이어 가거나 노여움의 기색을 더 드러내는 대신, 그 텁수룩한 몸뚱이 전체가 무언가 기이한 속의 경련에라도 휘둘린 듯 격하게 떨렸다."], ["The huge and unwieldy talons pawed stupidly about the grinning muzzle, and while Heyward kept his eyes riveted on its movements with jealous watchfulness, the grim head fell on one side, and in its place appeared the honest, sturdy countenance of the scout, who was indulging from the bottom of his soul, in his own peculiar expression of merriment.", "거대하고 둔중한 발톱이 씩 벌어진 주둥이 언저리를 멍청하게 더듬었고, 헤이워드가 미심쩍은 경계심으로 그 동작에서 눈을 떼지 못하고 있는 사이, 그 험상궂은 머리가 한쪽으로 툭 떨어지더니, 그 자리에 정찰꾼의 정직하고 다부진 얼굴이 나타났는데, 그는 마음 깊은 데서 우러나오는 자기만의 독특한 방식으로 유쾌함을 한껏 누리고 있었다."]], [["\"Hist!\"", "\"쉿!\" 빈틈없는 숲사람이 헤이워드의 놀란 외침을 가로막으며 말했다."], ["said the wary woodsman, interrupting Heyward's exclamation of surprise; \"the varlets are about the place, and any sounds that are not natural to witchcraft would bring them back upon us in a body.\"", "\"그 악당들이 이 근처에 있소. 주술과 어울리지 않는 소리는 무엇이든 그놈들을 떼로 다시 우리에게 몰려오게 할 거요.\""]], [["\"Tell me the meaning of this masquerade; and why you have attempted so desperate an adventure.\"", "\"이 가장극이 무슨 영문인지 말해 주시오. 그리고 어찌하여 이토록 무모한 모험을 시도했는지도.\""]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["reason and calculation are often outdone by accident,\"", "이성과 계산은 우연에 능가당하는 일이 잦지요.\""], ["returned the scout.", "정찰꾼이 대답했다."], ["\"But as a story should always commence at the beginning, I will tell you the whole in order.", "\"허나 이야기란 늘 처음부터 시작해야 하는 법이니, 차례대로 죄다 말씀드리리다. 우리가 헤어진 뒤, 나는 지휘관과 사가모어를 묵은 비버 굴에 들여보냈소."], ["After we parted I placed the commandant and the Sagamore in an old beaver lodge, where they are safer from the Hurons than they would be in the garrison of Edward, for your high northwest Indians, not having as yet got the traders among them, continue to venerate the beaver.", "그곳이 에드워드 요새 수비대보다도 휴런족으로부터 더 안전하지요. 그 북서쪽 깊은 데 사는 인디언들은 아직 그들 사이에 장사꾼을 들이지 않은 터라 여태 비버를 떠받든다오."], ["After which Uncas and I pushed for the other encampment, as was agreed; have you seen the lad?\"", "그러고 나서 엉커스와 나는, 약조한 대로 다른 진영으로 밀고 나아갔소. 그 젊은이를 보셨소?\""]], [["\"To my great grief!", "\"보았으니, 그게 큰 슬픔이오!"], ["he is captive, and condemned to die at the rising of the sun.\"", "그는 사로잡혀, 해가 뜨면 죽임을 당하도록 선고받았소.\""]], [["# THE MASQUERADER 268", "# THE MASQUERADER 268"]], [["_The grim head fell on one side, and in its place appeared the honest, sturdy countenance of the scout_]\n\"I had misgivings that such would be his fate,\"", "\"그런 운명이 그를 덮치지 않을까 미심쩍긴 했소.\" 정찰꾼이 조금 덜 자신만만하고 덜 즐거운 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["resumed the scout, in a less confident and joyous tone.", "그러나 곧 본래의 굳건한 목소리를 되찾으며 그는 계속했다."], ["But soon regaining his naturally firm voice, he continued: \"His bad fortune is the true reason of my being here, for it would never do to abandon such a boy to the Hurons.", "\"그 젊은이의 불운이야말로 내가 여기 있는 진짜 까닭이오. 그런 아이를 휴런족에게 내버려 두는 일은 결코 있을 수 없으니."], ["A rare time the knaves would have of it, could they tie The Bounding Elk and The Long Carabine, as they call me, to the same stake!", "그놈들이 '날뛰는 사슴'과, 그놈들이 나를 부르는 이름인 '긴 총'을 같은 화형 기둥에 묶을 수 있었다면, 그 악당들이 보기 드문 잔치를 벌였을 거요!"], ["Though why they have given me such a name I never knew, there being as little likeness between the gifts of 'Killdeer,'", "허나 그놈들이 어찌하여 내게 그런 이름을 붙였는지는 도무지 알 수가 없었소."], ["and the performance of one of your real Canada carabynes, as there is between the natur' of a pipe-stone and a flint!\"", "'사슴잡이'의 재간과 진짜 캐나다 총 한 자루의 솜씨 사이의 닮은 데가, 곰방대 돌과 부싯돌의 본성 사이만큼이나 적은데 말이오!\""]], [["\"Keep to your tale,\"", "\"이야기나 계속하시오.\""], ["said the impatient Heyward; \"we know not at what moment the Hurons may return.\"", "조바심이 난 헤이워드가 말했다. \"휴런족이 언제 돌아올지 알 수 없으니.\""]], [["\"No fear of them.", "\"그놈들은 걱정 마시오."], ["A conjurer must have his time, like a straggling priest in the settlements.", "주술사에게도 제 시간이 필요한 법이니, 정착지를 떠도는 떠돌이 사제처럼 말이오."], ["We are as safe from interruption as a missionary would be at the beginning of a two hours'", "우리는 두 시간짜리 설교의 첫머리에 든 선교사만큼이나 방해받을 일이 없소. 자, 엉커스와 나는 돌아오는 그 악당 무리와 마주쳤소."], ["discourse.", "그 젊은이가 정찰꾼치고는 너무 앞서 나갔던 거요."], ["Well, Uncas and I fell in with a return party of the varlets; the lad was much too forward for a scout; nay, for that matter, being of hot blood, he was not so much to blame; and, after all, one of the Hurons proved a coward, and in fleeing led him into an ambushment.\"", "아니, 그 점으로 말하자면, 피가 뜨거운 자이니 그리 탓할 일도 아니지요. 게다가 결국 휴런족 가운데 하나가 겁쟁이로 드러나, 달아나면서 그를 매복으로 끌고 들어갔소.\""]], [["\"And dearly has he paid for the weakness!\"", "\"그리고 그 약점의 대가를 톡톡히 치렀군!\""]], [["The scout significantly passed his hand across his own throat, and nodded, as if he said, \"I comprehend your meaning.\"", "정찰꾼이 의미심장하게 제 목을 손으로 쓱 그으며, \"당신 뜻을 알아들었소\" 하고 말하듯 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["After which he continued, in a more audible though scarcely more intelligible language,--", "그러고는 좀 더 또렷하나 그다지 더 알아듣기 쉽지는 않은 말로 이어 갔다."]], [["\"After the loss of the boy I turned upon the Hurons, as you may judge.", "\"그 젊은이를 잃은 뒤, 짐작하다시피, 나는 휴런족 쪽으로 돌아섰소."], ["There have been scrimmages atween one or two of their outlyers and myself; but that is neither here nor there.", "그놈들의 외곽 보초 한둘과 나 사이에 작은 다툼이 있었으나, 그건 이러쿵저러쿵할 일이 아니오."], ["So, after I had shot the imps, I got in pretty nigh to the lodges without further commotion.", "그래서 그 악귀들을 쏘아 넘긴 뒤, 나는 더 이상의 소동 없이 굴 가까이까지 꽤 다가갔소."], ["Then what should luck do in my favor, but lead me to the very spot where one of the most famous conjurers of the tribe was dressing himself, as I well knew, for some great battle with Satan--though why should I call that luck, which it now seems was an especial ordering of Providence!", "그런데 운이 내게 무얼 베풀어 주었느냐 하면, 바로 그 부족에서 가장 이름난 주술사 가운데 하나가 사탄과 벌일 어떤 큰 싸움에 대비해 — 내가 잘 알기로 — 차림을 갖추고 있던 바로 그 자리로 나를 이끌어 준 거요. 허나 이제 보니 분명 섭리의 특별한 안배였던 것을 어찌 운이라 부를 수 있겠소!"], ["So a judgmatical rap over the head stiffened the lying impostor for a time, and leaving him a bit of walnut for his supper, to prevent an uproar, and stringing him up atween two sapplings, I made free with his finery, and took the part of the bear on myself, in order that the operations might proceed.\"", "그래서 머리를 가늠 좋게 한 대 후려쳐 그 거짓 사기꾼을 한동안 뻣뻣하게 만들고, 소란을 막으려고 호두 한 조각을 저녁 삼아 물려 두 묘목 사이에 매달아 둔 뒤, 그자의 장식을 거리낌 없이 빌려 입고, 일이 진척되도록 곰 역을 내가 떠맡았던 거요.\""]], [["\"And admirably did you enact the character; the animal itself might have been shamed by the representation.\"", "\"그리고 그 배역을 참으로 훌륭히 해내셨소. 짐승 본인이라도 그 흉내에 무색했을 거요.\""]], [["\"Lord, major,\"", "\"이런, 소령.\""], ["returned the flattered woodsman, \"I should be but a poor scholar for one who has studied so long in the wilderness, did I not know how to set forth the movements and natur'", "우쭐해진 숲사람이 대꾸했다. \"그토록 오래 황야에서 배워 온 자가 그런 짐승의 동작과 본성을 펼쳐 보일 줄 모른다면, 나는 형편없는 학생일 뿐이겠지요."], ["of such a beast. Had it been now a catamount, or even a full-sized panther, I would have embellished a performance for you worth regarding.", "만약 그게 살쾡이나, 아니 덩치 큰 표범이었더라면, 당신께 볼만한 솜씨로 멋을 부려 보였을 거요."], ["But it is no such marvellous feat to exhibit the feats of so dull a beast; though, for that matter too, a bear may be overacted.", "허나 그토록 굼뜬 짐승의 짓을 보여 주는 것은 그리 놀랄 만한 재주가 못 되오. 하긴 그 점으로 말하자면, 곰도 지나치게 연기될 수 있긴 하지요."], ["Yes, yes; it is not every imitator that knows natur'", "그렇소, 그렇소. 본성이란 흉내 내어 맞추기보다 능가하기가 더 쉽다는 것을 아는 흉내꾼은 흔치 않소."], ["may be outdone easier than she is equalled.", "허나 우리 할 일은 죄다 아직 앞에 남아 있소."], ["But all our work is yet before us: where is the gentle one?\"", "그 고운 아씨는 어디 있소?\""]], [["\"Heaven knows; I have examined every lodge in the village, without discovering the slightest trace of her presence in the tribe.\"", "\"하늘이나 알겠소. 마을의 굴이란 굴은 죄다 살펴봤으나, 그 부족 안에 그분이 있다는 자취라곤 손톱만큼도 찾지 못했소.\""]], [["\"You heard what the singer said, as he left us,--'She is at hand, and expects you'?\"", "\"그 노래쟁이가 우리를 떠나며 한 말을 들으셨지요 — '그분이 가까이 계시고, 당신을 기다린다'고?\""]], [["\"I have been compelled to believe he alluded to this unhappy woman.\"", "\"나는 그가 이 가엾은 여인을 두고 한 말이라고 믿을 수밖에 없었소.\""]], [["\"The simpleton was frightened, and blundered through his message; but he had a deeper meaning.", "\"그 얼간이가 겁에 질려 전갈을 어설프게 더듬은 거요. 허나 그에게는 더 깊은 뜻이 있었소."], ["Here are walls enough to separate the whole settlement.", "여기엔 정착지 전체를 갈라놓을 만큼 벽이 충분하오."], ["A bear ought to climb; therefore will I take a look above them.", "곰이라면 기어오를 줄 알아야지. 그러니 내 그 벽들 위를 한번 살펴보리다."], ["There may be honey-pots hid in these rocks, and I am a beast you know, that has a hankering for the sweets.\"", "이 바위틈에 꿀단지가 숨겨져 있을지도 모르오. 알다시피 나는 단것을 탐하는 짐승이니까.\""]], [["The scout looked behind him, laughing at his own conceit, while he clambered up the partition, imitating, as he went, the clumsy motions of the beast he represented; but the instant the summit was gained he made a gesture for silence, and slid down with the utmost precipitation.", "정찰꾼은 제 너스레에 웃으며 뒤를 돌아보더니, 가는 동안 자신이 흉내 내는 짐승의 굼뜬 동작을 본떠 가며 칸막이 벽을 기어올랐다. 그러나 꼭대기에 닿은 순간, 그는 조용히 하라는 몸짓을 하고는 더없이 황급하게 미끄러져 내려왔다."]], [["\"She is here,\"", "\"그분이 여기 계시오.\""], ["he whispered, \"and by that door you will find her.", "그가 속삭였다. \"저 문으로 가면 그분을 찾을 거요."], ["I would have spoken a word of comfort to the afflicted soul; but the sight of such a monster might upset her reason.", "시름에 잠긴 그 영혼에 위로의 말 한마디 건네고 싶었으나, 이런 괴물을 보면 정신을 놓을지도 모르니."], ["Though for that matter, major, you are none of the most inviting yourself in your paint.\"", "하긴 그 점으로 말하자면, 소령, 당신도 그 칠갑을 한 꼴이 그리 반가운 모습은 못 되오만.\""]], [["Duncan, who had already sprung eagerly forward, drew instantly back on hearing these discouraging words.", "이미 간절히 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔던 던컨은, 이 맥 빠지는 말을 듣고 곧장 뒤로 물러섰다."]], [["\"Am I, then, so very revolting?\"", "\"그럼 내가 그토록 흉측하단 말이오?\""], ["he demanded, with an air of chagrin.", "그는 분한 기색으로 물었다."]], [["\"You might not startle a wolf, or turn the Royal Americans from a charge; but I have seen the time when you had a better-favored look; your streaked countenances are not ill-judged of by the squaws, but young women of white blood give the preference to their own color.", "\"늑대를 놀래키거나 로열 아메리칸 부대의 돌격을 돌려세울 정도는 아니오. 허나 당신이 더 나은 얼굴을 하고 있던 때를 나는 본 적이 있소."], ["See,\"", "그 줄무늬 칠갑한 얼굴이야 스쿼들에게는 그리 나쁘게 보이지 않으나, 백인 피를 가진 젊은 여인은 제 살빛을 더 좋아하는 법이오. 보시오.\""], ["he added, pointing to a place where the water trickled from a rock, forming a little crystal spring before it found an issue through the adjacent crevices; \"you may easily get rid of the Sagamore's daub, and when you come back I will try my hand at a new embellishment.", "그는 물이 바위에서 똑똑 떨어져 곁의 틈으로 빠져나가기 전 작은 수정 같은 샘을 이루는 자리를 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"사가모어의 그 칠갑은 손쉽게 지울 수 있을 거요."], ["It's as common for a conjurer to alter his paint as for a buck in the settlements to change his finery.\"", "돌아오면 내가 새로 멋을 한번 부려 보겠소. 주술사가 칠을 바꾸는 거야 정착지의 멋쟁이 사내가 차림을 바꾸는 것만큼이나 흔한 일이니까.\""]], [["The deliberate woodsman had little occasion to hunt for arguments to enforce his advice.", "이 신중한 숲사람은 제 충고를 밀어붙일 논거를 찾느라 애쓸 것도 없었다."], ["He was yet speaking when Duncan availed himself of the water.", "그가 아직 말하고 있을 적에 던컨은 벌써 그 물을 썼다."], ["In a moment every frightful or offensive mark was obliterated, and the youth appeared again in the lineaments with which he had been gifted by nature.", "삽시간에 흉측하거나 거슬리던 자국이 죄다 지워졌고, 청년은 자연이 그에게 베푼 본래의 이목구비로 다시 나타났다."], ["Thus prepared for an interview with his mistress, he took a hasty leave of his companion, and disappeared through the indicated passage.", "이렇게 연인과 만날 채비를 갖추고서, 그는 동료에게 황급히 작별을 고하고, 일러 준 통로로 사라졌다."], ["The scout witnessed his departure with complacency, nodding his head after him, and muttering his good wishes; after which he very coolly set about an examination of the state of the larder, among the Hurons--the cavern, among other purposes, being used as a receptacle for the fruits of their hunts.", "정찰꾼은 흐뭇하게 그의 떠남을 지켜보며 뒤에 대고 고개를 끄덕이고는 잘되기를 비는 말을 중얼거렸다. 그러고 나서 그는 더없이 침착하게 휴런족 사이의 식량 저장 형편을 살피기 시작했으니 — 그 동굴은 다른 여러 용도와 더불어 그들이 사냥한 것들을 갈무리하는 곳간으로도 쓰이고 있었다."]], [["Duncan had no other guide than a distant glimmering light, which served, however, the office of a polar star to the lover.", "던컨에게는 멀리서 어렴풋이 비치는 불빛 말고는 다른 안내랄 것이 없었으나, 그것이 이 연인에게는 북극성 노릇을 톡톡히 해 주었다."], ["By its aid he was enabled to enter the haven of his hopes, which was merely another apartment of the cavern, that had been solely appropriated to the safe-keeping of so important a prisoner as a daughter of the commandant of William Henry.", "그 빛의 도움으로 그는 자기 희망의 항구로 들어설 수 있었으니, 그곳은 그저 동굴의 또 다른 한 칸으로, 윌리엄 헨리 요새 지휘관의 딸이라는 그토록 중요한 포로를 안전히 가두기 위해 따로 마련된 데였다."], ["It was profusely strewed with the plunder of that unlucky fortress.", "그 안에는 그 불운한 요새에서 약탈한 물건들이 잔뜩 어질러져 있었다."], ["In the midst of this confusion he found her he sought, pale, anxious, and terrified, but lovely.", "이 어수선함 한가운데서 그는 자신이 찾던 이를 발견했으니, 창백하고 근심에 차 두려워하고 있었으나, 여전히 사랑스러웠다."], ["David had prepared her for such a visit.", "데이비드가 그런 방문이 있으리라고 그녀에게 미리 일러 두었던 것이다."]], [["\"Duncan!\"", "\"던컨!\""], ["she exclaimed, in a voice that seemed to tremble at the sounds created by itself.", "그녀는, 제 입에서 나온 그 소리에 스스로 떨리는 듯한 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Alice\"", "\"앨리스.\""], ["he answered, leaping carelessly among trunks, boxes, arms, and furniture, until he stood at her side.", "그가 대답하며, 짐짝과 상자, 무기, 가구 사이를 거침없이 뛰어넘어 마침내 그녀 곁에 섰다."]], [["\"I knew that you would never desert me,\"", "\"당신이 결코 나를 저버리지 않으리란 걸 알았어요.\""], ["she said, looking up with a momentary glow on her otherwise dejected countenance.", "그녀는, 그러잖아도 풀이 죽어 있던 얼굴에 한순간 화색을 띠며 그를 올려다보고 말했다."], ["\"But you are alone!", "\"하지만 혼자시군요!"], ["grateful as it is to be thus remembered, I could wish to think you are not entirely alone.\"", "이렇게 기억해 주시니 고맙긴 하지만, 당신이 정말 혼자가 아니라고 생각할 수 있기를 바라요.\""]], [["Duncan, observing that she trembled in a manner which betrayed her inability to stand, gently induced her to be seated, while he recounted those leading incidents which it has been our task to record.", "그녀가 도무지 서 있을 수 없을 만큼 떨고 있는 것을 본 던컨은, 그녀를 부드럽게 이끌어 앉게 하고는, 우리가 기록해 온 그 주요한 사건들을 들려주었다."], ["Alice listened with breathless interest; and though the young man touched lightly on the sorrows of the stricken father, taking care, however, not to wound the self-love of his auditor, the tears ran as freely down the cheeks of the daughter as though she had never wept before.", "앨리스는 숨죽인 채 빠져들어 귀를 기울였다. 청년이 비탄에 잠긴 그 아버지의 슬픔을 가볍게만 짚으며, 듣는 이의 자존심을 다치지 않으려 조심했음에도, 딸의 두 뺨에는 마치 한 번도 운 적이 없었던 양 눈물이 거침없이 흘러내렸다."], ["The soothing tenderness of Duncan, however, soon quieted the first burst of her emotions, and she then heard him to the close with undivided attention, if not with composure.", "그러나 던컨의 어루만지는 다정함은 곧 그 격정의 첫 북받침을 가라앉혔고, 그리하여 그녀는, 마음이 차분해지지는 못했어도, 한눈팔지 않는 정성으로 그의 말을 끝까지 들었다."]], [["\"And now, Alice,\"", "\"자, 앨리스.\""], ["he added, \"you will see how much is still expected of you.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"이제 당신에게 아직 얼마나 많은 것이 기대되고 있는지 알게 될 거요."], ["By the assistance of our experienced and invaluable friend, the scout, we may find our way from this savage people, but you will have to exert your utmost fortitude.", "우리의 노련하고 더없이 소중한 벗, 저 정찰꾼의 도움으로, 우리는 이 미개한 무리에게서 빠져나갈 길을 찾을 수 있을 거요. 허나 당신은 가진 힘을 다해 굳세야만 하오."], ["Remember that you fly to the arms of your venerable parent, and how much his happiness, as well as your own, depends on those exertions.\"", "당신이 그 존경스러운 부친의 품으로 달아나는 것임을, 그리고 당신 자신의 행복은 물론 그분의 행복까지도 얼마나 그 분발에 달려 있는지를 명심하시오.\""]], [["\"Can I do otherwise for a father who has done so much for me?\"", "\"저를 위해 그토록 많은 일을 해 주신 아버지를 위해 제가 어찌 달리할 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["\"And for me too,\"", "\"나를 위해서도.\""], ["continued the youth, gently pressing the hand he held in both his own.", "청년이, 두 손으로 감싸 쥔 그 손을 가만히 누르며 말을 이었다."]], [["The look of innocence and surprise which he received in return convinced Duncan of the necessity of being more explicit.", "그 대답으로 받은 천진함과 놀라움의 표정에, 던컨은 좀 더 분명히 말할 필요가 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["\"This is neither the place nor the occasion to detain you with selfish wishes,\"", "\"이런 이기적인 바람으로 당신을 붙들어 둘 자리도 때도 아니오.\" 그가 덧붙였다."], ["he added; \"but what heart loaded like mine would not wish to cast its burden?", "\"허나 나처럼 짐을 진 마음이 어찌 그 짐을 부려 놓고 싶지 않겠소?"], ["They say misery is the closest of all ties; our common suffering in your behalf left but little to be explained between your father and myself.\"", "사람들이 말하기를, 고난이야말로 가장 가까운 끈이라 하오. 당신을 위해 함께 겪은 고통이, 당신 부친과 나 사이에 설명할 것을 거의 남기지 않았소.\""]], [["\"And dearest Cora, Duncan; surely Cora was not forgotten?\"", "\"그럼 가장 사랑하는 코라는요, 던컨. 설마 코라를 잊은 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["\"Not forgotten!", "\"잊다니! 아니오."], ["no; regretted, as woman was seldom mourned before.", "안타까워했소. 여인을 그토록 슬퍼한 일은 일찍이 드물었을 만큼."], ["Your venerable father knew no difference between his children; but I--Alice, you will not be offended when I say, that to me her worth was in a degree obscured--\"", "당신의 존경스러운 부친은 두 자식을 차별하지 않으셨소. 허나 나는 — 앨리스, 내가 이런 말을 해도 노여워하지 마시오, 나에게는 그분의 가치가 어느 정도 가려져 있었소 —\""]], [["\"Then you knew not the merit of my sister,\"", "\"그렇다면 당신은 제 언니의 진가를 모르셨던 거예요.\""], ["said Alice, withdrawing her hand; \"of you she ever speaks as of one who is her nearest friend.\"", "앨리스가 손을 거두며 말했다. \"언니는 늘 당신을 자신의 가장 가까운 벗처럼 이야기하는걸요.\""]], [["\"I would gladly believe her such,\"", "\"기꺼이 그렇게 믿고 싶소.\" 던컨이 황급히 대꾸했다."], ["returned Duncan, hastily; \"I could wish her to be even more; but with you, Alice, I have the permission of your father to aspire to a still nearer and dearer tie.\"", "\"그 이상이기를 바랄 수도 있을 거요. 허나 앨리스, 당신과는, 당신 부친의 허락을 얻어 그보다 한결 더 가깝고 더 소중한 끈을 바랄 수 있는 거요.\""]], [["Alice trembled violently, and there was an instant during which she bent her face aside, yielding to the emotions common to her sex; but they quickly passed away, leaving her mistress of her deportment, if not of her affections.", "앨리스는 격하게 떨었고, 한순간 얼굴을 옆으로 돌리며 제 성에 흔한 감정에 굴복했다. 그러나 그것은 곧 지나가, 그녀에게 — 그 애정은 못 다스렸을지언정 — 제 거동만은 다스릴 여유를 남겼다."]], [["\"Heyward,\"", "\"헤이워드.\""], ["she said, looking him full in the face with a touching expression of innocence and dependency, \"give me the sacred presence and the holy sanction of that parent before you urge me further.\"", "그녀는 천진함과 의지함이 어린 애틋한 표정으로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"저를 더 다그치시기 전에, 그 아버지의 거룩한 임석과 성스러운 허락을 제게 베풀어 주세요.\""]], [["\"Though more I should not, less I could not say,\"", "\"더는 말하지 말아야겠으나, 이보다 덜 말할 수도 없소.\""], ["the youth was about to answer, when he was interrupted by a light tap on his shoulder.", "청년이 대답하려는 찰나, 그의 어깨를 가볍게 톡 두드리는 손길에 말이 끊겼다."], ["Starting to his feet, he turned, and, confronting the intruder, his looks fell on the dark form and malignant visage of Magua.", "벌떡 일어선 그가 몸을 돌려 침입자와 맞서니, 그의 시선이 머구아의 거뭇한 형상과 악의에 찬 얼굴에 가닿았다."], ["The deep guttural laugh of the savage sounded, at such a moment, to Duncan like the hellish taunt of a demon.", "이런 순간에 들려온 그 미개인의 깊고 목쉰 웃음은, 던컨에게는 마치 악마의 지옥 같은 조롱처럼 들렸다."], ["Had he pursued the sudden and fierce impulse of the instant, he would have cast himself on the Huron, and committed their fortunes to the issue of a deadly struggle.", "그 찰나의 갑작스럽고 사나운 충동을 좇았더라면, 그는 그 휴런족에게 몸을 던져 두 사람의 운명을 죽음을 건 싸움의 결판에 내맡겼으리라."], ["But, without arms of any description, ignorant of what succor his subtle enemy could command, and charged with the safety of one who was just then dearer than ever to his heart, he no sooner entertained than he abandoned the desperate intention.", "그러나 그 어떤 무기도 없고, 그 교활한 적이 어떤 도움을 불러들일 수 있는지도 모르며, 바로 그때 어느 때보다도 제 마음에 소중한 이의 안위를 짊어진 처지였기에, 그는 그 무모한 생각을 품기가 무섭게 곧 단념하고 말았다."]], [["\"What is your purpose?\"", "\"무엇을 바라는 거죠?\""], ["said Alice, meekly folding her arms on her bosom, and struggling to conceal an agony of apprehension in behalf of Heyward, in the usual cold and distant manner with which she received the visits of her captor.", "앨리스가, 가슴 위로 다소곳이 팔짱을 끼고, 헤이워드를 위한 두려움의 고통을 감추려 애쓰며, 자신을 사로잡은 자의 방문을 받을 때 늘 짓던 그 차갑고 거리를 둔 태도로 말했다."]], [["The exulting Indian had resumed his austere countenance, though he drew warily back before the menacing glance of the young man's fiery eye.", "의기양양해하던 인디언은 다시 준엄한 얼굴을 되찾았으나, 청년의 불타는 눈빛이 던지는 위협 앞에서는 조심스레 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["He regarded both his captives for a moment with a steady look, and then stepping aside, he dropped a log of wood across a door different from that by which Duncan had entered.", "그는 잠시 두 포로를 한결같은 눈길로 바라보다가, 옆으로 비켜서서, 던컨이 들어온 문과는 다른 문을 가로질러 통나무 하나를 떨어뜨려 막았다."], ["The latter now comprehended the manner of his surprise, and believing himself irretrievably lost, he drew Alice to his bosom, and stood prepared to meet a fate which he hardly regretted, since it was to be suffered in such company.", "던컨은 이제 자신이 기습당한 까닭을 깨달았고, 돌이킬 수 없이 끝장났다고 믿으며, 앨리스를 제 가슴에 끌어안고는, 이런 동무와 함께 겪을 일이기에 거의 한스럽지도 않은 운명을 맞을 채비를 하고 섰다."], ["But Magua meditated no immediate violence.", "그러나 머구아는 당장의 폭력을 꾀하지는 않았다."], ["His first measures were very evidently taken to secure his new captive; nor did he even bestow a second glance at the motionless forms in the centre of the cavern, until he had completely cut off every hope of retreat through the private outlet he had himself used.", "그의 첫 조치는, 보아하니, 새 포로를 단단히 가두려는 것이 분명했다. 그는 자신이 써 온 그 은밀한 출구를 통한 도주의 희망을 완전히 끊어 놓기 전까지는, 동굴 한가운데 미동도 없는 두 형체에 두 번째 눈길조차 주지 않았다."], ["He was watched in all his movements by Heyward, who, however, remained firm, still folding the fragile form of Alice to his heart, at once too proud and too hopeless to ask favor of an enemy so often foiled.", "헤이워드는 그의 모든 동작을 지켜보았으나, 여전히 굳건히, 가녀린 앨리스의 몸을 제 가슴에 끌어안은 채 서 있었으니, 그토록 자주 좌절당한 적에게 자비를 구하기에는 너무 자존심이 강하고 또 너무 희망이 없었던 것이다."], ["When Magua had effected his object he approached his prisoners, and said in English,--", "머구아는 목적을 이루자 포로들에게 다가가 영어로 말했다."]], [["\"The pale-faces trap the cunning beavers; but the redskins know how to take the Yengeese.\"", "\"창백한 얼굴들은 교활한 비버를 덫으로 잡지. 허나 붉은 피부는 양키를 잡는 법을 안다.\""]], [["\"Huron, do your worst!\"", "\"휴런, 마음껏 해 봐라!\""], ["exclaimed the excited Heyward, forgetful that a double stake was involved in his life; \"you and your vengeance are alike despised.\"", "흥분한 헤이워드가, 제 목숨에 두 사람의 운명이 걸려 있음을 잊은 채 외쳤다. \"너도 네 복수도 똑같이 경멸할 따름이다.\""]], [["\"Will the white man speak these words at the stake?\"", "\"백인은 화형 기둥에서도 그 말을 하겠나?\""], ["asked Magua; manifesting, at the same time, how little faith he had in the other's resolution by the sneer that accompanied his words.", "머구아가, 그 말에 곁들인 비웃음으로 상대의 결의를 얼마나 믿지 않는지 동시에 드러내며 물었다."]], [["\"Here; singly to your face, or in the presence of your nation.\"", "\"여기서, 너와 단둘이 얼굴을 맞대고든, 네 부족이 다 보는 앞에서든.\""]], [["\"Le Renard Subtil is a great chief!\"", "\"르나르 쉬브틸은 위대한 추장이다!\""], ["returned the Indian; \"he will go and bring his young men to see how bravely a pale-face can laugh at the tortures.\"", "인디언이 대꾸했다. \"내 가서 젊은이들을 데려와, 창백한 얼굴이 고문을 얼마나 용감하게 비웃는지 보여 주겠다.\""]], [["He turned away while speaking, and was about to leave the place through the avenue by which Duncan had approached, when a growl caught his ear, and caused him to hesitate.", "그는 말하면서 돌아서서, 던컨이 다가왔던 그 통로로 자리를 떠나려 했으나, 으르렁대는 소리가 그의 귀를 붙잡아 그를 멈칫하게 했다."], ["The figure of the bear appeared in the door, where it sat, rolling from side to side in its customary restlessness.", "곰의 형상이 문간에 나타나, 그곳에 앉아 늘 그러듯 안절부절 좌우로 몸을 구르고 있었다."], ["Magua, like the father of the sick woman, eyed it keenly for a moment, as if to ascertain its character.", "머구아는, 병든 여인의 아버지가 그랬듯, 그 정체를 가늠하려는 듯 잠시 날카롭게 그것을 살폈다."], ["He was far above the more vulgar superstitions of his tribe, and so soon as he recognized the well-known attire of the conjurer, he prepared to pass it in cool contempt.", "그는 제 부족의 더 천박한 미신을 한참 넘어선 자였기에, 그 주술사의 낯익은 차림새를 알아보자마자 냉랭한 경멸로 그것을 지나치려 했다."], ["But a louder and more threatening growl caused him again to pause.", "그러나 더 크고 더 위협적인 으르렁거림에 그는 다시 멈춰 섰다."], ["Then he seemed as if suddenly resolved to trifle no longer, and moved resolutely forward.", "그러고는 별안간 더는 시간을 끌지 않기로 결심한 듯, 단호히 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["The mimic animal, which had advanced a little, retired slowly in his front, until it arrived again at the pass, when rearing on its hinder legs it beat the air with its paws, in the manner practised by its brutal prototype.", "그 흉내 짐승은, 조금 앞으로 다가왔다가, 그의 앞에서 천천히 물러나 마침내 다시 그 통로에 이르자, 뒷다리로 일어서서 제 야수 본보기가 하는 식으로 앞발로 허공을 휘저었다."]], [["\"Fool!\"", "\"멍청한 것!\" 추장이 휴런 말로 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed the chief, in Huron, \"go play with the children and squaws; leave men to their wisdom.\"", "\"가서 아이들과 스쿼들이나 데리고 놀아라. 사내들은 사내의 지혜에 맡겨 두고.\""]], [["He once more endeavored to pass the supposed empiric, scorning even the parade of threatening to use the knife, or tomahawk, that was pendent from his belt.", "그는, 허리띠에 매달린 칼이나 도끼를 쓰겠다는 위협의 시늉조차 깔보며, 그 돌팔이라 여긴 자를 다시 한번 지나치려 했다."], ["Suddenly the beast extended its arms, or rather legs, and inclosed him in a grasp that might have vied with the far-famed power of the \"bear's hug\" itself.", "별안간 그 짐승이 두 팔을, 아니 두 다리를 뻗어, 저 이름난 \"곰의 포옹\"의 힘에 견줄 만한 그러쥠으로 그를 옭아맸다."], ["Heyward had watched the whole procedure, on the part of Hawkeye, with breathless interest.", "헤이워드는 매부리의 그 모든 거동을 숨죽인 채 지켜보고 있었다."], ["At first he relinquished his hold of Alice; then he caught up a thong of buckskin, which had been used around some bundle, and when he beheld his enemy with his two arms pinned to his side by the iron muscles of the scout, he rushed upon him, and effectually secured them there.", "그는 먼저 앨리스를 놓고, 어떤 짐짝에 둘러쳤던 사슴가죽 끈 한 가닥을 집어 들었으니, 정찰꾼의 무쇠 같은 근육에 두 팔이 옆구리에 박힌 적을 보자, 그자에게 달려들어 그 팔을 거기에 단단히 옭아맸다."], ["Arms, legs, and feet were encircled in twenty folds of the thong, in less time than we have taken to record the circumstance.", "우리가 이 정황을 적어 내려간 것보다 짧은 동안에, 팔과 다리와 발이 그 끈의 스무 겹에 휘감겼다."], ["When the formidable Huron was completely pinioned, the scout released his hold, and Duncan laid his enemy on his back, utterly helpless.", "그 가공할 휴런족이 완전히 결박되자, 정찰꾼은 손을 풀었고, 던컨은 적을 등으로 눕혀, 도무지 어쩌지 못하게 만들었다."]], [["Throughout the whole of this sudden and extraordinary operation, Magua, though he had struggled violently, until assured he was in the hands of one whose nerves were far better strung than his own, had not uttered the slightest exclamation.", "이 갑작스럽고 기이한 일이 진행되는 내내, 머구아는, 제 신경보다 한결 더 단단히 죈 자의 손아귀에 들었음을 확신하기 전까지 격하게 몸부림쳤음에도, 외마디 소리조차 내지 않았다."], ["But when Hawkeye, by way of making a summary explanation of his conduct, removed the shaggy jaws of the beast, and exposed his own rugged and earnest countenance to the gaze of the Huron, the philosophy of the latter was so far mastered as to permit him to utter the never-failing,--", "그러나 매부리가, 제 행동을 간추려 설명하려는 셈으로, 그 짐승의 텁수룩한 턱을 벗겨 내고 자신의 우락부락하고 진지한 얼굴을 휴런족의 눈앞에 드러내자, 후자의 철학이 그만 무너져, 그로 하여금 한 번도 빠지는 법 없는 그 소리를 내뱉게 했다."]], [["\"Hugh!\"", "\"흐엉!\""]], [["\"Ay!", "\"아!"], ["you've found your tongue,\"", "이제 혀를 찾았군.\""], ["said his undisturbed conqueror; \"now, in order that you shall not use it to our ruin, I must make free to stop your mouth.\"", "흔들림 없는 그의 정복자가 말했다. \"자, 그 혀를 써서 우리를 망치지 못하게, 내 실례를 무릅쓰고 자네 입을 막아야겠네.\""]], [["As there was no time to be lost, the scout immediately set about effecting so necessary a precaution; and when he had gagged the Indian, his enemy might safely have been considered as _hors de combat_.", "지체할 틈이 없었으므로, 정찰꾼은 그토록 긴요한 조처를 곧장 행하기 시작했다. 그가 인디언에게 재갈을 물리고 나자, 그 적은 무사히 전투력을 잃었다고 여겨도 좋을 만했다."]], [["\"By what place did the imp enter?\"", "\"그 악귀가 어디로 들어왔소?\""], ["asked the industrious scout, when his work was ended.", "일을 마친 부지런한 정찰꾼이 물었다."], ["\"Not a soul has passed my way since you left me.\"", "\"당신이 나를 떠난 뒤로 내 쪽으로는 단 한 놈도 지나간 적이 없소.\""]], [["Duncan pointed out the door by which Magua had come, and which now presented too many obstacles to a quick retreat.", "던컨은 머구아가 들어온 문을 가리켰으니, 그 문은 이제 황급히 빠져나가기에는 장애물이 너무 많았다."]], [["\"Bring on the gentle one, then,\"", "\"그럼 그 고운 아씨를 데려오시오.\""], ["continued his friend; \"we must make a push for the woods by the other outlet.\"", "그의 벗이 말을 이었다. \"다른 출구로 숲을 향해 달려야 하오.\""]], [["\"'Tis impossible!\"", "\"그건 불가능하오!\""], ["said Duncan; \"fear has overcome her, and she is helpless.", "던컨이 말했다. \"두려움이 그분을 짓눌러, 도무지 몸을 가누지 못하오."], ["Alice!", "앨리스!"], ["my sweet, my own Alice, arouse yourself; now is the moment to fly.", "사랑스러운, 내 소중한 앨리스, 정신을 차려요. 지금이 달아날 때요."], ["'Tis in vain!", "헛되구나!"], ["she hears, but is unable to follow.", "듣기는 하나 따를 수가 없으니."], ["Go, noble and worthy friend; save yourself, and leave me to my fate!\"", "가시오, 고결하고 갸륵한 벗이여. 당신이나 살아남고, 나는 내 운명에 맡겨 두시오!\""]], [["\"Every trail has its end, and every calamity brings its lesson!\"", "\"길마다 끝이 있고, 재앙마다 그 가르침을 가져오는 법이오!\""], ["returned the scout.", "정찰꾼이 대꾸했다."], ["\"There, wrap her in them Indian cloths.", "\"자, 저 인디언 천으로 그분을 감싸시오."], ["Conceal all of her little form.", "그 작은 몸뚱이를 죄다 가리시오."], ["Nay, that foot has no fellow in the wilderness; it will betray her.", "아니, 그 발은 황야에 짝이 없으니, 그분의 정체를 들통낼 거요."], ["All, every part.", "죄다, 한 군데도 빠짐없이."], ["Now take her in your arms, and follow.", "자, 이제 그분을 팔에 안고 따라오시오."], ["Leave the rest to me.\"", "나머지는 내게 맡기고.\""]], [["Duncan, as may be gathered from the words of his companion, was eagerly obeying; and as the other finished speaking, he took the light person of Alice in his arms, and followed on the footsteps of the scout.", "그 벗의 말에서 짐작할 수 있듯, 던컨은 간절히 따르고 있었다. 상대가 말을 마치자, 그는 가벼운 앨리스의 몸을 두 팔에 안고, 정찰꾼의 발자취를 좇았다."], ["They found the sick woman as they had left her, still alone, and passed swiftly on, by the natural gallery, to the place of entrance.", "그들은 떠나올 때 그대로, 여전히 홀로 있는 그 병든 여인을 보고는, 천연의 회랑을 따라 입구께로 잰걸음으로 나아갔다."], ["As they approached the little door of bark, a murmur of voices without announced that the friends and relatives of the invalid were gathered about the place, patiently awaiting a summons to re-enter.", "나무껍질로 된 작은 문에 다가서니, 밖에서 웅성대는 목소리들이, 병자의 벗들과 친지들이 그 자리에 모여 다시 들어오라는 부름을 참을성 있게 기다리고 있음을 알렸다."]], [["\"If I open my lips to speak,\"", "\"내가 입을 열어 말하면,\""], ["Hawkeye whispered, \"my English, which is the genuine tongue of a white-skin, will tell the varlets that an enemy is among them.", "매부리가 속삭였다. \"백인의 진짜 혀인 내 영어가, 그 악당들에게 적이 그들 사이에 끼어 있다고 일러바칠 거요."], ["You must give 'em your jargon, major; and say that we have shut the evil spirit in the cave, and are taking the woman to the woods in order to find strengthening roots.", "당신이 그놈들 말을 해야 하오, 소령. 우리가 악령을 동굴에 가두었으며, 기운을 돋울 뿌리를 찾으러 그 여인을 숲으로 데려가는 길이라고 말하시오."], ["Practyse all your cunning, for it is a lawful undertaking.\"", "가진 꾀를 죄다 부리시오, 이건 정당한 일이니.\""]], [["The door opened a little, as if one without was listening to the proceedings within, and compelled the scout to cease his directions.", "문이 조금 열렸으니, 마치 밖의 누군가가 안의 일을 엿듣기라도 하듯, 정찰꾼은 그 지시를 멈출 수밖에 없었다."], ["A fierce growl repelled the eavesdropper, and then the scout boldly threw open the covering of bark, and left the place, enacting the character of the bear as he proceeded.", "사나운 으르렁거림이 그 엿듣는 자를 물리쳤고, 그러자 정찰꾼은 대담하게 나무껍질 덮개를 활짝 젖히고는, 가는 동안 곰의 배역을 연기하며 그곳을 떠났다."], ["Duncan kept close at his heels, and so found himself in the centre of a cluster of twenty anxious relatives and friends.", "던컨은 그의 뒤를 바싹 따랐고, 그리하여 근심에 찬 스무 명의 친지와 벗들 한가운데 서게 되었다."]], [["The crowd fell back a little, and permitted the father, and one who appeared to be the husband of the woman, to approach.", "무리가 조금 물러서며, 그 아버지와, 보아하니 그 여인의 남편인 듯한 자가 다가오게 길을 터 주었다."]], [["\"Has my brother driven away the evil spirit?\"", "\"내 형제가 악령을 쫓아냈는가?\""], ["demanded the former.", "전자가 물었다."], ["\"What has he in his arms?\"", "\"팔에 안은 것은 무엇인가?\""]], [["\"Thy child,\"", "\"그대의 자식일세.\""], ["returned Duncan, gravely; \"the disease has gone out of her; it is shut up in the rocks.", "던컨이 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"병이 그녀에게서 빠져나갔네."], ["I take the woman to a distance, where I will strengthen her against any further attacks.", "그것은 바위 속에 갇혔어. 내 이 여인을 먼 데로 데려가, 어떤 추가 발작에도 견디도록 그녀를 튼튼히 하겠네."], ["She shall be in the wigwam of the young man when the sun comes again.\"", "해가 다시 뜨면 그녀는 젊은이의 천막에 있을 걸세.\""]], [["When the father had translated the meaning of the stranger's words into the Huron language, a suppressed murmur announced the satisfaction with which the intelligence was received.", "그 아버지가 낯선 자의 말뜻을 휴런 말로 옮기자, 낮게 웅성대는 소리가 그 소식을 받아들이는 흡족함을 알렸다."], ["The chief himself waved his hand for Duncan to proceed, saying aloud, in a firm voice, and with a lofty manner,--", "추장 자신이 던컨에게 가라고 손을 내저으며, 굳건한 목소리로, 드높은 거동으로 소리 높여 말했다."]], [["\"Go; I am a man, and I will enter the rock and fight the wicked one.\"", "\"가게. 나는 사내이니, 내가 바위 속에 들어가 그 악한 자와 싸우겠네.\""]], [["Heyward had gladly obeyed, and was already past the little group, when these startling words arrested him.", "헤이워드는 기꺼이 그 말을 따라, 이미 그 작은 무리를 지나쳤으나, 이 섬뜩한 말에 발이 묶였다."]], [["\"Is my brother mad?\"", "\"내 형제가 미쳤는가?\""], ["he exclaimed; \"is he cruel!", "그가 외쳤다. \"잔혹한가!"], ["He will meet the disease, and it will enter him; or he will drive out the disease, and it will chase his daughter into the woods.", "그가 병과 마주치면 병이 그에게 들어갈 것이고, 아니면 그가 병을 몰아내면 병이 그의 딸을 숲으로 쫓아갈 걸세. 안 되네."], ["No; let my children wait without, and if the spirit appears beat him down with clubs.", "내 자식들을 밖에서 기다리게 하고, 그 영이 나타나거든 몽둥이로 후려쳐 쓰러뜨리게."], ["He is cunning, and will bury himself in the mountain, when he sees how many are ready to fight him.\"", "그것은 교활하여, 얼마나 많은 이가 저와 싸울 채비를 하고 있는지 보면 산속에 제 몸을 묻어 버릴 걸세.\""]], [["This singular warning had the desired effect.", "이 별난 경고는 바라던 대로 들어맞았다."], ["Instead of entering the cavern, the father and husband drew their tomahawks, and posted themselves in readiness to deal their vengeance on the imaginary tormentor of their sick relative, while the women and children broke branches from the bushes, or seized fragments of the rock, with a similar intention.", "동굴에 들어가는 대신, 아버지와 남편은 도끼를 빼 들고, 그 병든 친지를 괴롭히는 가상의 자에게 복수를 퍼부을 채비를 하고 자리를 잡았으며, 여인들과 아이들은 같은 뜻으로 덤불에서 가지를 꺾거나 바위 조각을 움켜쥐었다."], ["At this favorable moment the counterfeit conjurers disappeared.", "이 좋은 틈을 타, 그 가짜 주술사들은 자취를 감췄다."]], [["Hawkeye, at the same time that he had presumed so far on the nature of the Indian superstitions, was not ignorant that they were rather tolerated than relied on by the wisest of the chiefs.", "매부리는 인디언 미신의 본성에 그토록 깊이 기대면서도, 그것이 가장 슬기로운 추장들에게는 의지되기보다 그저 묵인될 따름이라는 것을 모르지 않았다."], ["He well knew the value of time in the present emergency.", "그는 지금 이 위급한 상황에서 시간의 값어치를 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Whatever might be the extent of the self-delusion of his enemies, and however it had tended to assist his schemes, the slightest cause of suspicion, acting on the subtle nature of an Indian, would be likely to prove fatal.", "그의 적들의 자기기만이 아무리 깊든, 그것이 그의 계략을 거드는 데 아무리 보탬이 되었든, 인디언의 그 교묘한 본성에 작용하는 의심의 사소한 씨앗 하나가 치명적인 것으로 드러나기 십상이었다."], ["Taking the path, therefore, that was most likely to avoid observation, he rather skirted than entered the village.", "그리하여 그는 남의 눈에 가장 띄지 않을 만한 길을 골라, 마을 안으로 들어가기보다 그 가장자리를 에둘렀다."], ["The warriors were still to be seen in the distance, by the fading light of the fires, stalking from lodge to lodge.", "전사들은 여전히 멀리서, 사그라드는 모닥불빛에 의지해 천막에서 천막으로 어슬렁거리는 모습이 보였다."], ["But the children had abandoned their sports for their beds of skins, and the quiet of night was already beginning to prevail over the turbulence and excitement of so busy and important an evening.", "그러나 아이들은 놀이를 거두고 가죽 잠자리로 들었고, 밤의 고요가 어느새 그토록 분주하고 중대한 저녁의 소란과 흥분을 누르고 자리 잡기 시작하고 있었다."]], [["Alice revived under the renovating influence of the open air, and as her physical rather than her mental powers had been the subject of weakness, she stood in no need of any explanation of that which had occurred.", "앨리스는 탁 트인 공기의 되살리는 기운 아래 정신을 차렸으니, 약해진 것이 마음의 힘이라기보다 몸의 힘이었던지라, 그동안 벌어진 일에 대해 굳이 설명을 들을 필요가 없었다."]], [["\"Now let me make an effort to walk,\"", "\"이제 제가 걸어 보도록 할게요.\""], ["she said, when they had entered the forest, blushing, though unseen, that she had not been sooner able to quit the arms of Duncan; \"I am indeed restored.\"", "그녀가, 숲에 들어섰을 때, 보는 이는 없었으나 진작 던컨의 품을 벗어나지 못한 것을 부끄러워하며 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"정말이지 기운을 차렸어요.\""]], [["\"Nay, Alice, you are yet too weak.\"", "\"아니오, 앨리스, 당신은 아직 너무 약하오.\""]], [["The maiden struggled gently to release herself, and Heyward was compelled to part with his precious burden.", "처녀는 가만히 몸부림치며 벗어나려 했고, 헤이워드는 그 소중한 짐을 내려놓을 수밖에 없었다."], ["The representative of the bear had certainly been an entire stranger to the delicious emotions of the lover while his arms encircled his mistress; and he was, perhaps, a stranger also to the nature of that feeling of ingenuous shame that oppressed the trembling Alice.", "그 곰을 연기하던 자는, 두 팔로 연인을 끌어안고 있는 동안에도 사랑하는 이의 그 달콤한 감정과는 분명 아무 인연이 없었으리라. 또한 그는, 떨고 있는 앨리스를 짓누른 그 천진한 부끄러움의 본성과도 어쩌면 인연이 없었을 것이다."], ["But when he found himself at a suitable distance from the lodges he made a halt, and spoke on a subject of which he was thoroughly the master.", "그러나 그는 천막에서 알맞은 거리에 이르자 걸음을 멈추고, 자신이 환히 꿰고 있는 한 가지 주제를 이야기했다."]], [["\"This path will lead you to the brook,\"", "\"이 길을 따라가면 시내가 나올 거요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"follow its northern bank until you come to a fall; and mount the hill on your right, and you will see the fires of the other people.", "\"그 북쪽 기슭을 따라 폭포에 이를 때까지 가시오. 그리고 오른편 언덕을 오르면, 다른 부족의 모닥불이 보일 거요."], ["There you must go and demand protection; if they are true Delawares, you will be safe.", "거기로 가서 보호를 청하시오. 그들이 진짜 델라웨어족이라면, 당신은 안전할 거요."], ["A distant flight with that gentle one, just now, is impossible.", "지금 그 고운 아씨를 데리고 멀리 달아나는 것은 불가능하오."], ["The Hurons would follow up our trail, and master our scalps, before we had got a dozen miles.", "우리가 열 몇 마일을 가기도 전에 휴런족이 우리 자취를 좇아 우리 머릿가죽을 차지할 거요."], ["Go, and Providence be with you.\"", "가시오, 그리고 섭리가 함께하기를.\""]], [["\"And you!\"", "\"그럼 당신은!\""], ["demanded Heyward, in surprise; \"surely we part not here?\"", "헤이워드가 놀라 물었다. \"설마 여기서 헤어지자는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["\"The Hurons hold the pride of the Delawares; the last of the high blood of the Mohicans is in their power,\"", "\"휴런족이 델라웨어족의 자랑을 붙들고 있소. 모히칸족의 그 고귀한 피의 마지막 후예가 그놈들의 손아귀에 있단 말이오.\""], ["returned the scout; \"I go to see what can be done in his favor.", "정찰꾼이 대꾸했다. \"내 가서 그를 위해 무얼 할 수 있을지 보겠소."], ["Had they mastered your scalp, major, a knave should have fallen for every hair it held, as I promised; but if the young Sagamore is to be led to the stake, the Indians shall see also how a man without a cross can die.\"", "그놈들이 당신의 머릿가죽을 차지했더라면, 소령, 내 약조한 대로 그 머리칼 한 올마다 악당 하나씩 쓰러졌을 거요. 허나 만약 그 젊은 사가모어가 화형 기둥으로 끌려가야 한다면, 인디언들은 십자가 없는 사내가 어떻게 죽는지도 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["Not in the least offended with the decided preference that the sturdy woodsman gave to one who might, in some degree, be called the child of his adoption, Duncan still continued to urge such reasons against so desperate an effort as presented themselves.", "이 다부진 숲사람이, 어느 정도 제 양자라 할 만한 이에게 그토록 분명한 편애를 보였음에도, 던컨은 조금도 노여워하지 않고, 그토록 무모한 시도에 맞설 만한 갖가지 이유를 여전히 들이댔다."], ["He was aided by Alice, who mingled her entreaties with those of Heyward that he would abandon a resolution that promised so much danger, with so little hope of success.", "거기에는 앨리스도 힘을 보태, 헤이워드의 간청에 제 간청을 보태어, 그토록 큰 위험을 품고도 성공의 가망은 그토록 적은 그 결심을 버리라고 빌었다."], ["Their eloquence and ingenuity were expended in vain.", "그들의 능변과 재간은 헛되이 흩어졌다."], ["The scout heard them attentively, but impatiently, and finally closed the discussion, by answering, in a tone that instantly silenced Alice, while it told Heyward how fruitless any further remonstrances would be,--", "정찰꾼은 정성껏, 그러나 못마땅하게 그들의 말을 듣고는, 마침내 앨리스를 단번에 잠잠하게 만들고 헤이워드에게는 더 이상의 만류가 얼마나 부질없는지를 일러 주는 어조로 대답하여 그 논의를 끝맺었다."]], [["\"I have heard,\"", "\"내 듣기로는,\""], ["he said, \"that there is a feeling in youth which binds man to woman closer than the father is tied to the son.", "그가 말했다. \"젊은 시절에는 아버지가 아들에 매인 것보다 더 단단히 사내를 여인에게 묶는 어떤 정이 있다 하더이다."], ["It may be so.", "그럴지도 모르지요."], ["I have seldom been where women of my color dwell; but such may be the gifts of nature in the settlements.", "나는 내 살빛의 여인들이 사는 데 가 본 적이 거의 없으니, 정착지에서야 그게 천성의 도리일지도 모르오."], ["You have risked life, and all that is dear to you, to bring off this gentle one, and I suppose that some such disposition is at the bottom of it all.", "당신은 이 고운 아씨를 빼내려고 목숨과, 당신에게 소중한 모든 것을 걸었으니, 그 밑바닥에는 그런 어떤 마음이 깔려 있으리라 짐작하오."], ["As for me, I taught the lad the real character of a rifle; and well has he paid me for it.", "나로 말하자면, 나는 그 젊은이에게 소총의 참된 됨됨이를 가르쳤고, 그는 그 값을 톡톡히 치러 주었소."], ["I have fou't at his side in many a bloody scrimmage; and so long as I could hear the crack of his piece in one ear, and that of the Sagamore in the other, I knew no enemy was on my back.", "나는 피 흘리는 숱한 싸움에서 그의 곁에서 싸웠고, 한쪽 귀로는 그의 총소리를, 다른 쪽 귀로는 사가모어의 총소리를 들을 수 있는 한, 내 등 뒤에 적이 없음을 알았소."], ["Winters and summers, nights and days, have we roved the wilderness in company, eating of the same dish, one sleeping while the other watched; and afore it shall be said that Uncas was taken to the torment, and I at hand--There is but a single Ruler of us all, whatever may be the color of the skin; and Him I call to witness, that before the Mohican boy shall perish for the want of a friend, good faith shall depart the 'arth, and 'Killdeer'", "겨울이고 여름이고, 밤이고 낮이고, 우리는 함께 황야를 누볐고, 같은 그릇으로 먹었으며, 하나가 자면 다른 하나가 지켜보았소. 그러니 엉커스가 고문에 끌려갔는데 내가 곁에 있었으면서도 — 우리 모두에게는, 살빛이 어떻든, 단 한 분의 다스리는 분이 계실 따름이오."], ["become as harmless as the tooting we'pon of the singer!\" [Illustration: _Copyright by Charles Scribner's Sons_", "그분을 증인으로 부르노니, 그 모히칸 젊은이가 벗이 없어 스러지기 전에, 신의가 이 땅에서 사라지고, '사슴잡이'가 저 노래쟁이의 빽빽대는 무기만큼이나 무용해질 거요!\""]], [["# THE LOVERS 278", "# THE LOVERS 278"]], [["_Heyward and Alice took their way together towards the distant village of the Delawares_]\nDuncan released his hold on the arm of the scout, who turned, and steadily retraced his steps towards the lodges.", "던컨은 정찰꾼의 팔을 잡았던 손을 놓았고, 정찰꾼은 돌아서서 흔들림 없이 천막을 향해 발길을 되짚어 갔다."], ["After pausing a moment to gaze at his retiring form, the successful and yet sorrowful Heyward, and Alice, took their way together towards the distant village of the Delawares.", "멀어지는 그 모습을 잠시 바라보고 나서, 성공했으나 여전히 슬픔에 잠긴 헤이워드와 앨리스는, 함께 멀리 델라웨어족의 마을을 향해 길을 잡았다."]], [["\"_Bot._--Let me play the lion too.\"", "바텀. — 나도 사자 역을 맡게 해 주오."]], [["_Midsummer Night's Dream._", "한여름 밤의 꿈"]], [["Notwithstanding the high resolution of Hawkeye, he fully comprehended all the difficulties and dangers he was about to incur.", "〈호크아이〉의 결의가 아무리 드높았다 한들, 그는 자신이 무릅쓰려는 온갖 어려움과 위험을 똑똑히 헤아리고 있었다."], ["In his return to the camp, his acute and practised intellects were intently engaged in devising means to counteract a watchfulness and suspicion on the part of his enemies, that he knew were, in no degree, inferior to his own.", "진지로 돌아가는 동안, 그의 예리하고 단련된 지력은 적들의 경계심과 의심을 따돌릴 방도를 궁리하는 데 골똘히 매달려 있었으니, 그 경계심과 의심이 결코 제 것에 못지않다는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["Nothing but the color of his skin had saved the lives of Magua and the conjurer, who would have been the first victims sacrificed to his own security, had not the scout believed such an act, however congenial it might be to the nature of an Indian, utterly unworthy of one who boasted a descent from men that knew no cross of blood.", "〈마구아〉와 주술사의 목숨을 살린 것은 오직 그 피부색뿐이었으니, 그들이야말로 그 자신의 안전을 위해 가장 먼저 제물로 바쳐졌어야 마땅한 자들이었으되, 그런 짓이 인디언의 본성에는 아무리 어울린다 한들, 피의 섞임을 모르는 사람들의 후예임을 자랑하는 자에게는 도무지 어울리지 않는다고 척후가 믿지 않았더라면 그러했을 것이다."], ["Accordingly, he trusted to the withes and ligaments with which he had bound his captives, and pursued his way directly towards the centre of the lodges.", "그리하여 그는 포로들을 묶은 가는 가지와 끈을 믿고, 오두막들의 한복판을 향해 곧장 길을 잡아 나아갔다."]], [["As he approached the buildings, his steps became more deliberate, and his vigilant eye suffered no sign, whether friendly or hostile, to escape him.", "건물들에 다가갈수록 그의 발걸음은 한층 신중해졌고, 그의 빈틈없는 눈은 우호적이든 적대적이든 어떤 기색도 놓치지 않았다."], ["A neglected hut was a little in advance of the others, and appeared as if it had been deserted when half completed--most probably on account of failing in some of the more important requisites; such as food or water.", "버려진 오두막 한 채가 다른 것들보다 조금 앞쪽에 서 있었는데, 절반쯤 짓다가 버린 듯 보였으니, 아마도 식량이나 물 같은 더 중요한 필수 요소 가운데 무언가가 모자란 탓이었을 것이다."], ["A faint light glimmered through its cracks, however, and announced that, notwithstanding its imperfect structure, it was not without a tenant.", "그러나 그 틈새로 희미한 불빛이 새어 나와, 비록 그 구조가 미완성일지언정 그 안에 사람이 들어 있음을 알렸다."], ["Thither, then, the scout proceeded, like a prudent general, who was about to feel the advanced positions of his enemy, before he hazarded the main attack.", "그리하여 척후는 그쪽으로 나아갔으니, 마치 본격적인 공격을 감행하기에 앞서 적의 전초 진지를 먼저 살피려는 신중한 장군과도 같았다."]], [["Throwing himself into a suitable posture for the beast he represented, Hawkeye crawled to a little opening, where he might command a view of the interior.", "자신이 흉내 내는 짐승에 알맞은 자세를 취하고서, 〈호크아이〉는 안이 들여다보이는 작은 틈으로 기어갔다."], ["It proved to be the abiding-place of David Gamut.", "그곳은 〈데이비드 거뮤트〉가 머무는 처소로 드러났다."], ["Hither the faithful singing-master had now brought himself, together with all his sorrows, his apprehensions, and his meek dependence on the protection of Providence.", "이 충직한 성가 교사는 이제 자신의 온갖 슬픔과 두려움, 그리고 섭리의 보호에 기대는 온순한 의탁심과 더불어 이곳에 와 있었다."], ["At the precise moment when his ungainly person came under the observation of the scout, in the manner just mentioned, the woodsman himself, though in his assumed character, was the subject of the solitary being's profoundest reflections.", "방금 말한 대로 그 볼품없는 몸이 척후의 관찰 아래 들어온 바로 그 순간, 정작 이 숲사람 자신은, 비록 가장한 모습이긴 하나, 그 외로운 존재가 골똘히 빠져 있던 사색의 대상이었다."]], [["However implicit the faith of David was in the performance of ancient miracles, he eschewed the belief of any direct supernatural agency in the management of modern morality.", "〈데이비드〉가 옛 기적들의 실현에 아무리 절대적인 믿음을 품고 있다 한들, 현세의 도덕을 다스리는 데 어떤 직접적인 초자연의 작용이 개입한다는 믿음만은 멀리했다."], ["In other words, while he had implicit faith in the ability of Balsam's ass to speak, he was somewhat skeptical on the subject of a bear's singing; and yet he had been assured of the latter, on the testimony of his own exquisite organs.", "다시 말해, 그는 〈발람〉의 나귀가 말을 할 수 있다는 점은 절대적으로 믿었으되, 곰이 노래한다는 문제에는 다소 회의적이었던 것이다. 그럼에도 후자에 관해서는 자신의 더없이 섬세한 청각 기관의 증언에 따라 확신을 갖고 있었다."], ["There was something in his air and manner that betrayed to the scout the utter confusion of the state of his mind.", "그의 분위기와 거동에는 그 정신 상태가 완전히 혼란에 빠져 있음을 척후에게 드러내는 무언가가 있었다."], ["He was seated on a pile of brush, a few twigs from which occasionally fed his low fire, with his head leaning on his arm, in a posture of melancholy musing.", "그는 잔가지를 쌓은 더미 위에 앉아, 거기서 이따금 잔가지 몇 개를 빼어 자신의 낮은 불을 지피며, 머리를 팔에 괸 채 우울한 명상의 자세를 취하고 있었다."], ["The costume of the votary of music had undergone no other alteration from that so lately described, except that he had covered his bald head with the triangular beaver, which had not proved sufficiently alluring to excite the cupidity of any of his captors.", "음악에 헌신하는 이 사람의 차림새는 방금 묘사한 것에서 별다른 변화가 없었으니, 다만 그 벗어진 머리에 삼각 비버 모자를 씌운 것만이 달랐는데, 그 모자는 포로로 잡은 자들 가운데 누구의 탐욕도 자극할 만큼 매력적이지 못했던 것이다."]], [["The ingenious Hawkeye, who recalled the hasty manner in which the other had abandoned his post at the bedside of the sick woman, was not without his suspicions concerning the subject of so much solemn deliberation.", "영리한 〈호크아이〉는 저 사람이 병든 여인의 침상 곁에서 황급히 제 자리를 버리고 떠났던 일을 떠올리며, 그토록 엄숙한 숙고의 대상이 무엇일지 의심을 품지 않을 수 없었다."], ["First making the circuit of the hut, and ascertaining that it stood quite alone, and that the character of its inmate was likely to protect it from visitors, he ventured through its low door, into the very presence of Gamut.", "먼저 오두막을 한 바퀴 돌아, 그것이 외따로 서 있고 그 안에 든 자의 성격으로 보아 방문객의 발길이 닿지 않을 듯하다는 것을 확인한 뒤, 그는 그 낮은 문을 통해 다름 아닌 〈거뮤트〉의 면전으로 들어섰다."], ["The position of the latter brought the fire between them; and when Hawkeye had seated himself on end, near a minute elapsed, during which the two remained regarding each other without speaking.", "후자의 위치 탓에 불이 두 사람 사이에 놓이게 되었고, 〈호크아이〉가 똑바로 자리를 잡고 앉았을 때, 거의 일 분 가까이가 흘렀는데, 그동안 둘은 아무 말 없이 서로를 바라보고만 있었다."], ["The suddenness and the nature of the surprise had nearly proved too much for--we will not say the philosophy--but for the faith and resolution of David.", "이 놀람의 갑작스러움과 그 성격은 — 〈데이비드〉의 철학이라 하지는 않겠으나 — 그의 신앙과 결의에 거의 감당할 수 없을 만큼 벅찼다."], ["He fumbled for his pitch-pipe, and arose with a confused intention of attempting a musical exorcism.", "그는 더듬더듬 음높이 피리를 찾았고, 음악으로 악령을 쫓으려는 어수선한 작정으로 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["\"Dark and mysterious monster!\"", "\"어둡고 신비로운 괴물아!\""], ["he exclaimed, while with trembling hands he disposed of his auxiliary eyes, and sought his never-failing resource in trouble, the gifted version of the Psalms: \"I know not your nature nor intents; but if aught you meditate against the person and rights of one of the humblest servants of the temple, listen to the inspired language of the youth of Israel, and repent.\"", "그가 외쳤으니, 떨리는 두 손으로 보조 눈인 안경을 고쳐 쓰고, 곤경에 처할 때마다 결코 그를 저버린 적 없는 의지처, 곧 영감 어린 시편의 운문을 찾으면서였다. \"나는 네 본성도 네 의도도 알지 못하나, 만일 네가 성전의 가장 미천한 종 가운데 하나의 신변과 권리를 해치려 무엇이든 꾀하고 있다면, 〈이스라엘〉 젊은이의 영감 어린 말씀에 귀 기울이고, 회개하라.\""]], [["The bear shook his shaggy sides, and then a well-known voice replied,--", "곰은 그 텁수룩한 옆구리를 흔들더니, 이윽고 익히 아는 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["\"Put up the tooting we'pon, and teach your throat modesty.", "\"그 삑삑대는 무기는 치우고, 그 목구멍에 겸손을 가르치시오."], ["Five words of plain and comprehensible English are worth, just now, an hour of squalling.\"", "알아들을 수 있는 평범한 〈영어〉 다섯 마디가, 바로 지금은, 한 시간의 빽빽거림보다 값지니까.\""]], [["\"What art thou!\"", "\"그대는 무엇인가!\""], ["demanded David, utterly disqualified to pursue his original intention, and nearly gasping for breath.", "〈데이비드〉가 물었으니, 본래의 의도를 좇기에는 도무지 자격을 잃은 채, 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."]], [["\"A man like yourself; and one whose blood is as little tainted by the cross of a bear, or an Indian, as your own.", "\"당신과 같은 사람이오. 그리고 그 피가 곰이나 인디언의 섞임으로 더럽혀진 정도가 당신만큼이나 덜한 사람이지."], ["Have you so soon forgotten from whom you received the foolish instrument you hold in your hand?\"", "당신이 손에 쥐고 있는 그 어리석은 도구를 누구에게서 받았는지 벌써 잊었소?\""]], [["\"Can these things be?\"", "\"이런 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["returned David, breathing more freely, as the truth began to dawn upon him.", "〈데이비드〉가 대답했으니, 진실이 그에게 동트기 시작하자 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었다."], ["\"I have found many marvels during my sojourn with the heathen, but surely nothing to excel this!\"", "\"이교도들과 함께 머무는 동안 숱한 놀라운 일을 겪었으되, 분명 이보다 더한 것은 없었소!\""]], [["\"Come, come,\"", "\"자, 자,\""], ["returned Hawkeye, uncasing his honest countenance, the better to assure the wavering confidence of his companion; \"you may see a skin, which, if it be not as white as one of the gentle ones, has no tinge of red to it that the winds of heaven and the sun have not bestowed.", "〈호크아이〉가 대꾸하며, 흔들리는 동료의 신뢰를 한결 굳혀 주려고 제 정직한 얼굴을 가렸던 것을 벗었다. \"당신이 보게 될 살갗은, 비록 저 곱상한 분들의 것만큼 희지는 않을지언정, 하늘의 바람과 햇볕이 안겨 준 것 외에는 붉은 기운이라곤 한 점 없는 것이오."], ["Now let us to business.\"", "자, 이제 본론으로 들어갑시다.\""]], [["\"First tell me of the maiden, and of the youth who so bravely sought her,\"", "\"먼저 그 처녀와, 그토록 용감하게 그녀를 찾아 나선 젊은이의 소식부터 말해 주오.\""], ["interrupted David.", "〈데이비드〉가 가로막았다."]], [["\"Ay, they are happily freed from the tomahawks of these varlets.", "\"그래요, 그들은 다행히 이 악당들의 도끼에서 풀려났소."], ["But can you put me on the scent of Uncas?\"", "헌데 당신, 나를 〈언카스〉의 자취로 이끌어 줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["\"The young man is in bondage, and much I fear his death is decreed.", "\"그 젊은이는 결박되어 있고, 그 죽음이 이미 정해졌을까 몹시 두렵소."], ["I greatly mourn that one so well disposed should die in his ignorance, and I have sought a goodly hymn--\"", "그토록 마음씨 고운 이가 무지 속에 죽어야 한다니 크게 애통하여, 나는 어울리는 찬송가 한 곡을 찾아 두었소—\""]], [["\"Can you lead me to him?\"", "\"그에게로 나를 데려갈 수 있겠소?\""]], [["\"The task will not be difficult,\"", "\"그 일은 어렵지 않을 것이오.\""], ["returned David, hesitating; \"though I greatly fear your presence would rather increase than mitigate his unhappy fortunes.\"", "〈데이비드〉가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"허나 당신이 나타나면 그의 불행한 운명을 덜어 주기는커녕 오히려 키울까 몹시 두렵소.\""]], [["\"No more words, but lead on,\"", "\"더는 말 말고, 앞장이나 서시오.\""], ["returned Hawkeye, concealing his face again, and setting the example in his own person, by instantly quitting the lodge.", "〈호크아이〉가 대꾸하며 다시 얼굴을 가렸고, 곧장 오두막을 떠남으로써 몸소 본을 보였다."]], [["As they proceeded, the scout ascertained that his companion found access to Uncas, under privilege of his imaginary infirmity, aided by the favor he had acquired with one of the guards, who, in consequence of speaking a little English, had been selected by David as the subject of a religious conversation.", "길을 가는 동안, 척후는 자기 동료가 〈언카스〉에게 접근할 수 있었던 것이 상상 속의 정신적 결함이라는 특권 덕분이며, 거기에 보초 가운데 한 사람에게서 얻은 호의가 보태진 덕임을 알게 되었다. 그 보초는 〈영어〉를 조금 할 줄 안다는 이유로 〈데이비드〉가 종교적 대화의 상대로 골랐던 자였다."], ["How far the Huron comprehended the intentions of his new friend, may well be doubted; but as exclusive attention is as flattering to a savage as to a more civilized individual, it had produced the effect we have mentioned.", "이 새 친구의 의도를 그 〈휴런〉이 얼마나 알아들었는지는 자못 의심스러우나, 오로지 자기에게만 쏟아지는 관심이란 더 문명한 자에게 그러하듯 미개인에게도 흐뭇한 법이어서, 앞서 말한 효과를 낳았던 것이다."], ["It is unnecessary to repeat the shrewd manner with which the scout extracted these particulars from the simple David; neither shall we dwell in this place on the nature of the instructions he delivered, when completely master of all the necessary facts; as the whole will be sufficiently explained to the reader in the course of the narrative.", "척후가 단순한 〈데이비드〉에게서 이 세세한 사정을 캐낸 그 영리한 솜씨를 거듭 옮길 필요는 없겠고, 필요한 사실을 모조리 손에 넣은 뒤 그가 일러 준 지시의 내용도 이 자리에서 자세히 다루지 않으련다. 그 전모는 이야기가 진행되는 동안 독자에게 충분히 밝혀질 것이기 때문이다."]], [["The lodge in which Uncas was confined was in the very centre of the village, and in a situation, perhaps, more difficult than any other to approach, or leave, without observation.", "〈언카스〉가 갇혀 있는 오두막은 마을의 바로 한복판에 있었고, 눈에 띄지 않고 다가가거나 떠나기로는 다른 어느 곳보다도 어려운 자리였다."], ["But it was not the policy of Hawkeye to affect the least concealment.", "그러나 조금이라도 숨으려 드는 것은 〈호크아이〉의 방책이 아니었다."], ["Presuming on his disguise, and his ability to sustain the character he had assumed, he took the most plain and direct route to the place.", "자신의 변장과, 떠맡은 배역을 끝까지 해낼 능력을 믿고, 그는 그곳으로 가장 단순하고 곧은 길을 택했다."], ["The hour, however, afforded him some little of that protection which he appeared so much to despise.", "하지만 이 시각이, 그가 그토록 멸시하는 듯 보이던 보호를 약간이나마 그에게 베풀어 주었다."], ["The boys were already buried in sleep, and all the women, and most of the warriors, had retired to their lodges for the night.", "사내아이들은 이미 잠에 빠져 있었고, 여인들은 모두, 또 전사들도 대부분 밤을 보내려 제 오두막으로 물러간 뒤였다."], ["Four or five of the latter only lingered about the door of the prison of Uncas, wary but close observers of the manner of their captive.", "후자 가운데 네댓 명만이 〈언카스〉의 감옥 문 어귀에 남아, 경계하면서도 그 포로의 거동을 가까이서 살피고 있었다."]], [["At the sight of Gamut, accompanied by one in the well known masquerade of their most distinguished conjurer, they readily made way for them both.", "저들의 가장 이름난 주술사의 익히 아는 분장을 한 자를 거느린 〈거뮤트〉를 보자, 그들은 선뜻 둘 모두에게 길을 터 주었다."], ["Still they betrayed no intention to depart.", "그러면서도 떠날 기색은 전혀 보이지 않았다."], ["On the other hand, they were evidently disposed to remain bound to the place by an additional interest in the mysterious mummeries that they of course expected from such a visit.", "오히려 그들은 그런 방문에서 당연히 기대할 법한 신비로운 의식에 한층 더 흥미를 느껴 그 자리에 묶이고 싶어 하는 눈치가 역력했다."]], [["From the total inability of the scout to address the Hurons in their own language, he was compelled to trust the conversation entirely to David.", "척후는 〈휴런〉의 말로 그들에게 도무지 말을 건넬 수가 없었던 까닭에, 대화를 온전히 〈데이비드〉에게 맡길 수밖에 없었다."], ["Notwithstanding the simplicity of the latter, he did ample justice to the instructions he had received, more than fulfilling the strongest hopes of his teacher.", "후자는 그 단순함에도 불구하고 받은 지시를 충실히 해냈으니, 그 스승의 가장 큰 기대마저 넘어설 정도였다."]], [["\"The Delawares are women!\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉 놈들은 계집이오!\""], ["he exclaimed, addressing himself to the savage who had a slight understanding of the language in which he spoke; \"the Yengeese, my foolish countrymen, have told them to take up the tomahawk, and strike their fathers in the Canadas, and they have forgotten their sex.", "그가 자기가 쓰는 말을 어렴풋이 알아듣는 미개인을 향해 외쳤다. \"어리석은 내 동포 〈양기〉들이 그들더러 도끼를 들고 〈캐나다〉의 제 아비들을 치라고 시켰더니, 그들은 제 성별을 잊어버렸소."], ["Does my brother wish to hear Le Cerf Agile ask for his petticoats, and see him weep before the Hurons, at the stake?\"", "내 형제는 〈민첩한 사슴〉이 제 치마를 달라고 청하며, 화형 기둥에서 〈휴런〉 사람들 앞에 우는 꼴을 듣고 보고 싶지 않소?\""]], [["The exclamation \"Hugh!\"", "강한 동의의 어조로 내뱉은 \"흠!\""], ["delivered in a strong tone of assent, announced the gratification the savage would receive in witnessing such an exhibition of weakness in an enemy so long hated and so much feared.", "하는 외침은, 그토록 오래 미워하고 그토록 두려워해 온 적에게서 그런 나약함의 표출을 목격하는 데서 미개인이 느낄 만족을 알렸다."]], [["\"Then let him step aside, and the cunning man will blow upon the dog!", "\"그렇다면 그를 비키게 하시오. 그러면 영험한 자가 그 개에게 입김을 불 테니!"], ["Tell it to my brothers.\"", "내 형제들에게 그리 전하시오.\""]], [["The Huron explained the meaning of David to his fellows, who, in their turn, listened to the project with that sort of satisfaction that their untamed spirits might be expected to find in such a refinement in cruelty.", "그 〈휴런〉은 〈데이비드〉의 뜻을 동료들에게 설명해 주었고, 동료들은 그들대로 그 잔혹함의 세련된 묘리에서 길들지 않은 그 정신이 으레 찾을 법한 만족감으로 그 계획에 귀 기울였다."], ["They drew back a little from the entrance, and motioned to the supposed conjurer to enter.", "그들은 입구에서 조금 물러서며, 짐작컨대 그 주술사에게 들어가라는 손짓을 했다."], ["But the bear, instead of obeying, maintained the seat it had taken, and growled.", "그러나 곰은 그 말을 따르기는커녕 차지하고 있던 자리를 지키며 으르렁거렸다."]], [["\"The cunning man is afraid that his breath will blow upon his brothers, and take away their courage too,\"", "\"영험한 자는 제 입김이 형제들에게 불어 닿아 그들의 용기마저 앗아 갈까 두려워하오.\""], ["continued David, improving the hint he received; \"they must stand farther off.\"", "〈데이비드〉가 받은 암시를 활용해 말을 이었다. \"그러니 더 멀리 떨어져 서야 하오.\""]], [["The Hurons, who would have deemed such a misfortune the heaviest calamity that could befall them, fell back in a body, taking a position where they were out of ear-shot, though at the same time they could command a view of the entrance to the lodge.", "그런 불행이야말로 자신들에게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 무거운 재앙이라 여길 〈휴런〉 사람들은, 한 무리가 되어 물러나, 소리가 들리지 않을 만큼은 떨어졌으되 동시에 오두막 입구는 살필 수 있는 자리를 잡았다."], ["Then, as if satisfied of their safety, the scout left his position, and slowly entered the place.", "그러자 자신들의 안전을 확신한 듯, 척후는 제자리를 떠나 천천히 그 안으로 들어섰다."], ["It was silent and gloomy, being tenanted solely by the captive, and lighted by the dying embers of a fire, which had been used for the purposes of cookery.", "그곳은 고요하고 음울했으니, 오직 포로 하나만이 들어 있었고, 취사에 쓰였던 불이 사위어 가는 잉걸불이 그곳을 밝히고 있었다."]], [["Uncas occupied a distant corner, in a reclining attitude, being rigidly bound, both hands and feet, by strong and painful withes.", "〈언카스〉는 먼 구석을 차지한 채 비스듬히 기대어 있었으니, 두 손과 두 발이 모두 질기고 아픈 가는 가지로 단단히 묶여 있었다."], ["When the frightful object first presented itself to the young Mohican, he did not deign to bestow a single glance on the animal.", "그 무시무시한 형상이 처음 이 젊은 〈모히칸〉의 눈앞에 나타났을 때, 그는 그 짐승에게 눈길 한 번 던지려 하지 않았다."], ["The scout, who had left David at the door, to ascertain they were not observed, thought it prudent to preserve his disguise until assured of their privacy.", "〈데이비드〉를 문가에 남겨 둬 자신들이 감시받지 않는지 확인하게 한 척후는, 두 사람만 있다는 것이 확실해지기 전까지는 변장을 유지하는 편이 신중하리라 여겼다."], ["Instead of speaking, therefore, he exerted himself to enact one of the antics of the animal he represented.", "그리하여 말을 하는 대신, 그는 자신이 흉내 내는 그 짐승의 익살 한 가지를 연기하려 애썼다."], ["The young Mohican, who at first believed his enemies had sent in a real beast to torment him, and try his nerves, detected, in those performances that to Heyward had appeared so accurate, certain blemishes, that at once betrayed the counterfeit.", "처음에는 적들이 자신을 괴롭히고 그 담력을 시험하려 진짜 짐승을 들여보낸 줄로 믿었던 이 젊은 〈모히칸〉은, 〈헤이워드〉에게는 그토록 정확해 보였던 그 연기에서, 단번에 그 가짜임을 드러내는 몇 가지 흠을 알아챘다."], ["Had Hawkeye been aware of the low estimation in which the more skilful Uncas held his representations, he would probably have prolonged the entertainment a little in pique.", "더 능란한 〈언카스〉가 자신의 연기를 얼마나 낮잡아 보는지를 〈호크아이〉가 알았더라면, 아마 부아가 나서 그 흥행을 조금 더 길게 끌었을 것이다."], ["But the scornful expression of the young man's eye admitted of so many constructions, that the worthy scout was spared the mortification of such a discovery.", "그러나 젊은이의 눈에 어린 경멸의 표정은 어찌나 여러 갈래로 해석될 수 있었던지, 이 갸륵한 척후는 그런 발견의 굴욕을 면할 수 있었다."], ["As soon, therefore, as David gave the pre-concerted signal, a low hissing sound was heard in the lodge, in place of the fierce growlings of the bear.", "그리하여 〈데이비드〉가 미리 약속한 신호를 보내자마자, 곰의 사나운 으르렁거림 대신 낮게 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 오두막 안에 들렸다."]], [["Uncas had cast his body back against the wall of the hut, and closed his eyes, as if willing to exclude so contemptible and disagreeable an object from his sight.", "〈언카스〉는 그 몸을 오두막 벽에 도로 기대고 두 눈을 감았으니, 마치 그토록 경멸스럽고 불쾌한 대상을 제 시야에서 몰아내고 싶어 하는 듯했다."], ["But the moment the noise of the serpent was heard, he arose, and cast his looks on each side of him, bending his head low, and turning it inquiringly in every direction, until his keen eye rested on the shaggy monster, where it remained riveted, as though fixed by the power of a charm.", "그러나 그 뱀의 소리가 들리는 순간, 그는 일어나 머리를 낮게 숙인 채 사방을 두루 돌아보며, 캐묻듯 온 방향으로 고개를 돌리다가, 마침내 그 날카로운 눈이 그 텁수룩한 괴물에 가닿았고, 거기에 못 박힌 듯, 마법의 힘에 붙들린 듯 머물렀다."], ["Again the same sounds were repeated, evidently proceeding from the mouth of the beast.", "다시 같은 소리가 되풀이되었으니, 분명 그 짐승의 입에서 나오는 것이었다."], ["Once more the eyes of the youth roamed over the interior of the lodge, and returning to their former resting place, he uttered, in a deep, suppressed voice,--", "또 한 번 젊은이의 눈은 오두막 안을 휘둘러보았고, 앞서 머물렀던 곳으로 되돌아오며, 깊고 억눌린 목소리로 입을 열었다."]], [["\"Hawkeye!\"", "\"〈호크아이〉!\""]], [["\"Cut his bands,\"", "\"저자의 결박을 끊으시오.\""], ["said Hawkeye to David, who just then approached them.", "〈호크아이〉가, 마침 그때 다가온 〈데이비드〉에게 말했다."]], [["The singer did as he was ordered, and Uncas found his limbs released.", "성가 교사는 명령받은 대로 했고, 〈언카스〉는 제 사지가 풀려난 것을 알았다."], ["At the same moment the dried skin of the animal rattled, and presently the scout arose to his feet, in proper person.", "바로 그 순간 그 짐승의 마른 가죽이 바스락거리더니, 이내 척후가 본래의 제 모습으로 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["The Mohican appeared to comprehend the nature of the attempt his friend had made, intuitively; neither tongue nor feature betraying another symptom of surprise.", "〈모히칸〉은 제 벗이 시도한 일의 본질을 직관으로 헤아린 듯, 혀로도 표정으로도 더는 놀람의 기색을 한 가닥도 드러내지 않았다."], ["When Hawkeye had cast his shaggy vestment, which was done by simply loosing certain thongs of skin, he drew a long glittering knife, and put it in the hands of Uncas.", "〈호크아이〉가 그 텁수룩한 겉옷을 벗어 던졌으니 — 가죽 끈 몇 가닥을 그저 풀기만 하면 되는 일이었다 — 그는 길게 번득이는 칼 한 자루를 뽑아 〈언카스〉의 손에 쥐여 주었다."]], [["\"The red Hurons are without,\"", "\"붉은 〈휴런〉 놈들이 바깥에 있다.\""], ["he said; \"let us be ready.\"", "그가 말했다. \"채비를 갖추자.\""]], [["At the same time he laid his finger significantly on another similar weapon, both being the fruits of his prowess among their enemies during the evening.", "이와 동시에 그는 또 다른 비슷한 무기 하나에 의미심장하게 손가락을 얹었으니, 둘 다 그날 저녁 적들 가운데서 거둔 그의 용맹의 결실이었다."]], [["\"We will go,\"", "\"우리는 갈 것이오.\""], ["said Uncas.", "〈언카스〉가 말했다."]], [["\"Whither?\"", "\"어디로?\""]], [["\"To the Tortoises; they are the children of my grandfathers.\"", "\"〈거북〉족에게로. 그들은 내 할아버지들의 자손이오.\""]], [["\"Ay, lad,\"", "\"그래, 얘야,\""], ["said the scout in English--a language he was apt to use when a little abstracted in mind; \"the same blood runs in your veins, I believe; but time and distance have a little changed its color.", "척후가 〈영어〉로 말했으니 — 마음이 조금 딴 데 가 있을 때면 곧잘 쓰는 말이었다. \"같은 피가 네 핏줄에 흐르고 있는 게 분명하다만, 세월과 거리가 그 빛깔을 조금 바꿔 놓았지."], ["What shall we do with the Mingos at the door?", "문간의 저 〈밍고〉 놈들은 어쩐다?"], ["They count six, and this singer is as good as nothing.\"", "여섯이나 되는데, 이 성가쟁이는 없는 거나 마찬가지이니.\""]], [["\"The Hurons are boasters,\"", "\"〈휴런〉 놈들은 허풍쟁이요.\""], ["said Uncas scornfully; \"their 'totem'", "〈언카스〉가 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["is a moose, and they run like snails.", "\"그들의 '토템'은 큰사슴이건만, 그들은 달팽이처럼 기지."], ["The Delawares are children of the tortoise, and they outstrip the deer.\"", "〈델라웨어〉는 거북의 자손이로되, 그들은 사슴을 앞지르오.\""]], [["\"Ay, lad, there is truth in what you say; and I doubt not, on a rush, you would pass the whole nation; and, in a straight race of two miles, would be in, and get your breath again, afore a knave of them all was within hearing of the other village.", "\"그래, 얘야, 네 말에 진실이 있구나. 단숨에 내달리면 네가 그 종족 전부를 앞지를 테고, 이 마일을 곧장 내달리는 경주라면, 그 악당들 가운데 누구 하나가 저쪽 마을 소리가 들리는 데에 닿기도 전에 들어가서 숨을 다시 고르고 있으리라는 걸 의심치 않는다."], ["But the gift of a white man lies more in his arms than in his legs.", "허나 백인의 재능은 다리보다는 팔에 더 깃들어 있지."], ["As for myself, I can brain a Huron as well as a better man; but when it comes to a race, the knaves would prove too much for me.\"", "나로 말하자면, 〈휴런〉 놈의 머리통을 그 누구 못지않게 잘 부술 수 있다만, 경주가 되면 그 악당들이 내게는 벅찰 게야.\""]], [["Uncas, who had already approached the door, in readiness to lead the way, now recoiled; and placed himself, once more, in the bottom of the lodge.", "이미 앞장설 채비로 문가에 다가가 있던 〈언카스〉가 이제 물러나, 다시 한번 오두막 안쪽에 자리를 잡았다."], ["But Hawkeye, who was too much occupied with his own thoughts to note the movement, continued speaking more to himself than to his companion.", "그러나 제 생각에 너무 골몰한 나머지 그 움직임을 알아채지 못한 〈호크아이〉는, 동료에게라기보다 혼잣말에 가깝게 계속 지껄였다."]], [["\"After all,\"", "\"결국,\""], ["he said, \"it is unreasonable to keep one man in bondage to the gifts of another.", "그가 말했다. \"한 사람을 다른 이의 재능에 매여 결박해 두는 건 당치 않은 일이지."], ["So, Uncas, you had better take the leap, while I put on the skin again, and trust to cunning for want of speed.\"", "그러니, 〈언카스〉, 너는 도약을 감행하는 편이 낫겠다. 나는 가죽을 다시 뒤집어쓰고, 부족한 속도 대신 꾀에 기댈 테니.\""]], [["The young Mohican made no reply, but quietly folded his arms, and leaned his body against one of the upright posts that supported the wall of the hut.", "젊은 〈모히칸〉은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 조용히 두 팔을 포갠 채, 오두막 벽을 떠받치는 수직 기둥 가운데 하나에 제 몸을 기댔다."]], [["\"Well,\"", "\"이런,\""], ["said the scout, looking up at him, \"why do you tarry?", "척후가 그를 올려다보며 말했다. \"어찌 꾸물대느냐?"], ["There will be time enough for me, as the knaves will give chase to you at first.\"", "처음엔 그 악당들이 너를 뒤쫓을 테니, 내게는 시간이 넉넉할 게다.\""]], [["\"Uncas will stay,\"", "\"〈언카스〉는 남겠소.\""], ["was the calm reply.", "차분한 대답이었다."]], [["\"For what?\"", "\"무엇 때문에?\""]], [["\"To fight with his father's brother, and die with the friend of the Delawares.\"", "\"제 아버지의 형제와 함께 싸우고, 〈델라웨어〉의 벗과 함께 죽기 위해서요.\""]], [["\"Ay, lad,\"", "\"그래, 얘야,\""], ["returned Hawkeye, squeezing the hand of Uncas between his own iron fingers; \"'twould have been more like a Mingo than a Mohican had you left me.", "〈호크아이〉가 〈언카스〉의 손을 제 쇠 같은 손가락 사이에 꽉 쥐며 대꾸했다. \"네가 나를 버리고 갔더라면 〈모히칸〉보다는 〈밍고〉에 더 가까웠을 게다."], ["But I thought I would make the offer, seeing that youth commonly loves life.", "허나 젊음이란 으레 목숨을 아끼는 법이라, 그래도 한번 권해 보려 했던 거지."], ["Well, what can't be done by main courage, in war, must be done by circumvention.", "자, 전쟁에서 순전한 용기로 안 되는 일은 꾀로 해내야 한다."], ["Put on the skin; I doubt not you can play the bear nearly as well as myself.\"", "가죽을 뒤집어써라. 곰 노릇이라면 너도 나만큼은 거뜬히 해낼 테니까.\""]], [["Whatever might have been the private opinion of Uncas of their respective abilities in this particular, his grave countenance manifested no opinion of his own superiority.", "이 점에서 둘의 능력에 관한 〈언카스〉의 속내가 어떠했든, 그 진중한 얼굴은 제가 더 낫다는 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않았다."], ["He silently and expeditiously encased himself in the covering of the beast, and then awaited such other movements as his more aged companion saw fit to dictate.", "그는 말없이 재빨리 그 짐승의 거죽으로 제 몸을 감싸고는, 더 나이 많은 동료가 지시할 다른 움직임을 기다렸다."]], [["\"Now, friend,\"", "\"자, 친구,\""], ["said Hawkeye, addressing David, \"an exchange of garments will be a great convenience to you, inasmuch as you are but little accustomed to the make-shifts of the wilderness.", "〈호크아이〉가 〈데이비드〉에게 말했다. \"옷을 바꿔 입는 게 당신에게 큰 도움이 될 거요."], ["Here, take my hunting shirt and cap, and give me your blanket and hat.", "당신은 황야의 임시변통에 도통 익숙지 않으니 말이오. 자, 내 사냥 셔츠와 모자를 받고, 당신의 담요와 모자를 내게 주오."], ["You must trust me with the book and spectacles, as well as the tooter, too; if we ever meet again, in better times, you shall have all back again, with many thanks into the bargain.\"", "그 책과 안경도, 그리고 그 피리도 내게 맡겨야 하오. 언젠가 형편이 나아져 다시 만나면, 거기에 넘치는 감사까지 얹어 모두 돌려 드리리다.\""]], [["David parted with the several articles named with a readiness that would have done great credit to his liberality, had he not certainly profited, in many particulars, by the exchange.", "〈데이비드〉는 거명된 여러 물건을 선뜻 내주었으니, 그가 이 교환에서 여러모로 분명한 이득을 보지 않았더라면 그 너그러움에 큰 공을 돌릴 만했다."], ["Hawkeye was not long in assuming his borrowed garments; and when his restless eyes were hid behind the glasses, and his head was surmounted by the triangular beaver, as their statures were not dissimilar, he might readily have passed for the singer by star-light.", "〈호크아이〉가 빌린 옷가지를 걸치는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그 잠시도 가만있지 않는 두 눈이 안경 뒤에 가려지고 머리에 삼각 비버 모자가 얹히자, 둘의 키가 별반 다르지 않았던 터라, 별빛 아래서라면 그는 능히 그 성가 교사로 통할 만했다."], ["As soon as these dispositions were made, the scout turned to David, and gave him his parting instructions.", "이 채비가 끝나자마자, 척후는 〈데이비드〉를 돌아보며 작별의 지시를 일러 주었다."]], [["\"Are you much given to cowardice?\"", "\"당신은 겁이 꽤 많은 편이오?\""], ["he bluntly asked, by way of obtaining a suitable understanding of the whole case before he ventured a prescription.", "그가 어떤 처방을 감히 내리기 전에 사정 전체를 알맞게 파악하려는 셈으로 대뜸 물었다."]], [["\"My pursuits are peaceful, and my temper, I humbly trust, is greatly given to mercy and love,\"", "\"내 일은 평화로운 것이고, 내 성품은, 외람되나 분명코 자비와 사랑에 크게 기울어 있소.\""], ["returned David, a little nettled at so direct an attack on his manhood; \"but there are none who can say that I have ever forgotten my faith in the Lord, even in the greatest straits.\"", "자신의 사내다움을 그토록 직설적으로 공격받자 다소 발끈하여 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"허나 내가 가장 큰 곤경에 처해서도 주님에 대한 믿음을 잊은 적 있다고 말할 수 있는 자는 아무도 없소.\""]], [["\"Your chiefest danger will be at the moment when the savages find out that they have been deceived.", "\"당신에게 가장 큰 위험은 그 미개인들이 자기들이 속았다는 것을 알아채는 바로 그 순간일 거요."], ["If you are not then knocked in the head, your being a non-composser will protect you; and you'll then have good reason to expect to die in your bed.", "그때 머리통을 얻어맞지만 않는다면, 당신이 정신이 온전치 못한 자라는 점이 당신을 지켜 줄 테고, 그러면 잠자리에서 죽음을 맞으리라 넉넉히 기대할 만하지."], ["If you stay, it must be to sit down here in the shadow, and take the part of Uncas, until such times as the cunning of the Indians discover the cheat, when, as I have already said, your time of trial will come.", "만일 남는다면, 여기 이 그늘에 앉아 〈언카스〉의 역을 떠맡고, 인디언들의 꾀가 그 속임수를 알아챌 때까지 견뎌야 하오. 그때가 되면, 이미 말했듯이, 당신의 시련의 때가 닥칠 거요."], ["So choose for yourself,--to make a rush or tarry here.\"", "그러니 당신이 골라 보시오 — 내달릴지, 아니면 여기 머물지.\""]], [["\"Even so,\"", "\"그렇다 해도,\""], ["said David, firmly; \"I will abide in the place of the Delaware.", "〈데이비드〉가 단호히 말했다. \"나는 그 〈델라웨어〉의 자리에 남겠소."], ["Bravely and generously has he battled in my behalf; and this, and more, will I dare in his service.\"", "그는 나를 위해 용감하고 너그럽게 싸워 주었으니, 그를 섬기는 일이라면 이것은 물론 이보다 더한 일도 감히 무릅쓰겠소.\""]], [["\"You have spoken as a man, and like one who, under wiser schooling, would have been brought to better things.", "\"당신은 사내답게 말했고, 더 슬기로운 가르침을 받았더라면 더 나은 길로 인도되었을 사람처럼 말했소."], ["Hold your head down, and draw in your legs; their formation might tell the truth too early.", "고개를 숙이고 다리를 오므리시오. 그 생김새가 너무 일찍 진실을 일러 줄지 모르니."], ["Keep silent as long as may be; and it would be wise, when you do speak, to break out suddenly in one of your shoutings, which will serve to remind the Indians that you are not altogether as responsible as men should be.", "될 수 있는 한 오래 잠자코 있고, 입을 열어야 할 때면 갑작스레 그 외침 가운데 하나를 터뜨리는 게 현명할 거요. 그러면 인디언들에게 당신이 여느 사람만큼 온전히 책임질 수 있는 자가 아님을 일깨워 줄 테니."], ["If, however, they take your scalp, as I trust and believe they will not, depend on it, Uncas and I will not forget the deed, but revenge it as becomes true warriors and trusty friends.\"", "하지만, 내가 바라고 또 믿건대 그렇지 않겠지만, 만일 그들이 당신의 머리 가죽을 벗긴다면, 〈언카스〉와 내가 그 일을 잊지 않고, 참된 전사이자 미더운 벗답게 반드시 복수하리라는 것을 믿어도 좋소.\""]], [["\"Hold!\"", "\"멈추시오!\""], ["said David, perceiving that with this assurance they were about to leave him; \"I am an unworthy and humble follower of One who taught not the damnable principle of revenge.", "그 약속과 함께 자신을 두고 떠나려는 것을 알아챈 〈데이비드〉가 말했다. \"나는 복수라는 가증스러운 원리를 가르치지 않으신 분의 보잘것없고 미천한 추종자요."], ["Should I fall, therefore, seek no victims to my manes, but rather forgive my destroyers; and if you remember them at all, let it be in prayers for the enlightening of their minds, and for their eternal welfare.\"", "그러니 내가 쓰러지거든, 내 넋을 위해 제물을 찾지 말고, 도리어 나를 죽인 자들을 용서하시오. 그리고 그들을 조금이라도 기억하려거든, 그들의 마음이 깨이고 그들의 영원한 복락을 비는 기도 속에서나 기억하시오.\""]], [["The scout hesitated, and appeared to muse.", "척후는 머뭇거리며 깊이 생각에 잠긴 듯했다."]], [["\"There is a principle in that,\" he said, \"different from the law of the woods; and yet it is fair and noble to reflect upon.\" Then, heaving a heavy sigh, probably among the last he ever drew in pining for a condition he had so long abandoned, he added, \"It is what I would wish to practise, myself, as one without a cross of blood, though it is not always easy to deal with an Indian as you would with a fellow Christian. God bless you, friend; I do believe your scent is not greatly wrong, when the matter is duly considered, and keeping eternity before the eyes, though much depends on the natural gifts, and the force of temptation.\"", "\"거기엔 한 원리가 있소.\" 그가 말했다. \"숲의 법칙과는 다른 것이오. 허나 곰곰이 헤아려 보면 공정하고 고귀한 것이지.\" 그러고는, 아마도 그가 그토록 오래 떠나 있던 한 처지를 사무치게 그리며 내쉰 마지막 한숨에 가까웠을 무거운 한숨을 토하며, 그가 덧붙였다. \"피의 섞임이 없는 사람으로서 나 자신도 그리 실천하고 싶은 바요. 인디언을 동료 기독교도 대하듯 대하기란 늘 쉬운 일은 아니지만 말이오. 신의 가호가 있기를, 친구여. 영원을 눈앞에 두고 그 문제를 마땅히 헤아려 보면, 당신의 냄새 맡는 방향이 크게 그릇되지는 않았다고 진정 믿소. 비록 많은 것이 타고난 재능과 유혹의 힘에 달려 있긴 하지만.\""]], [["So saying, the scout returned and shook David cordially by the hand; after which act of friendship he immediately left the lodge, attended by the new representative of the beast.", "이렇게 말하며 척후는 돌아와 〈데이비드〉의 손을 다정하게 잡고 흔들었다. 이 우정의 행위가 끝나자, 그는 곧장 오두막을 떠났으니, 그 짐승의 새로운 분신을 데리고서였다."]], [["The instant Hawkeye found himself under the observation of the Hurons, he drew up his tall form in the rigid manner of David, threw out his arm in the act of keeping time, and commenced what he intended for an imitation of his psalmody.", "〈호크아이〉가 〈휴런〉 사람들의 시야에 들어선 그 순간, 그는 〈데이비드〉의 그 뻣뻣한 자세로 제 큰 몸을 곧추세우고, 박자를 맞추는 시늉으로 팔을 쭉 내뻗고는, 그가 의도한 그 성가의 흉내를 시작했다."], ["Happily for the success of this delicate adventure, he had to deal with ears but little practised in the concord of sweet sounds, or the miserable effort would infallibly have been detected.", "이 미묘한 모험의 성공으로 보아 다행스럽게도, 그가 상대해야 할 자들의 귀는 감미로운 소리의 화음에 거의 단련되지 않은 것들이었으니, 그렇지 않았더라면 그 한심한 시도는 영락없이 들통났을 것이다."], ["It was necessary to pass within a dangerous proximity of the dark group of the savages, and the voice of the scout grew louder as they drew nigher.", "미개인들의 어두운 무리 곁을 위험할 만큼 가까이 지나야 했고, 그들에게 다가갈수록 척후의 목소리는 점점 커졌다."], ["When at the nearest point, the Huron who spoke the English thrust out an arm, and stopped the supposed singing-master.", "가장 가까운 지점에 이르렀을 때, 〈영어〉를 하는 그 〈휴런〉이 팔을 쑥 내밀어 그 성가 교사로 짐작되는 자를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["\"The Delaware dog!\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉의 개!\""], ["he said, leaning forward, and peering through the dim light to catch the expression of the other's features; \"is he afraid?", "그가 몸을 앞으로 기울이고 침침한 빛 속을 들여다보며 상대의 표정을 살피려 하면서 말했다. \"그자가 겁먹었나?"], ["will the Hurons hear his groans?\"", "〈휴런〉 사람들이 그의 신음을 듣게 될까?\""]], [["A growl so exceedingly fierce and natural proceeded from the beast, that the young Indian released his hold and started aside, as if to assure himself that it was not a veritable bear, and no counterfeit, that was rolling before him.", "어찌나 사납고 자연스러운 으르렁거림이 그 짐승에게서 흘러나왔던지, 그 젊은 인디언은 붙잡은 손을 놓고 옆으로 흠칫 물러섰으니, 마치 제 앞에서 뒹구는 것이 가짜가 아니라 진짜 곰이 아닌지 스스로 확인하려는 듯했다."], ["Hawkeye, who feared his voice would betray him to his subtle enemies, gladly profited by the interruption, to break out anew in such a burst of musical expression as would, probably, in a more refined state of society have been termed \"a grand crash.\"", "제 목소리가 그 교활한 적들에게 정체를 들킬까 두려워하던 〈호크아이〉는, 이 가로막힘을 반갑게 활용하여, 더 세련된 사회였더라면 아마 \"장엄한 절정\"이라 일컬어졌을 법한 음악적 표현을 새로이 한바탕 터뜨렸다."], ["Among his actual auditors, however, it merely gave him an additional claim to that respect which they never withhold from such as are believed to be the subjects of mental alienation.", "그러나 실제 청중들 사이에서 그것은, 다만 정신이 온전치 못하다고 여겨지는 자들에게 그들이 결코 아끼지 않는 그 경의를 그에게 한층 더 받을 자격으로 작용했을 뿐이다."], ["The little knot of Indians drew back in a body, and suffered, as they thought, the conjurer and his inspired assistant to proceed.", "그 작은 인디언 무리는 한 무리가 되어 물러나며, 자기들 짐작에 그 주술사와 그 영감 받은 조수를 그대로 지나가게 했다."]], [["It required no common exercise of fortitude in Uncas and the scout, to continue the dignified and deliberate pace they had assumed in passing the lodges; especially as they immediately perceived that curiosity had so far mastered fear, as to induce the watchers to approach the hut, in order to witness the effect of the incantations.", "오두막들을 지나며 떠맡은 그 위엄 있고 신중한 걸음걸이를 이어 가기란, 〈언카스〉와 척후에게 여간한 인내가 아니고는 어려운 일이었으니, 특히 호기심이 두려움을 이긴 나머지 그 감시자들이 주술의 효과를 목격하려 오두막으로 다가서는 것을 곧바로 알아챘기 때문이다."], ["The least injudicious or impatient movement on the part of David might betray them, and time was absolutely necessary to insure the safety of the scout.", "〈데이비드〉가 조금이라도 분별없거나 조급하게 움직였다가는 그들의 정체가 드러날 판이었고, 척후의 안전을 확보하기 위해서는 시간이 반드시 필요했다."], ["The loud noise the latter conceived it politic to continue, drew many curious gazers to the doors of the different huts as they passed; and once or twice a dark-looking warrior stepped across their path, led to the act by superstition or watchfulness.", "후자가 영리한 처사라 여겨 계속 내지른 그 큰 소리는, 그들이 지나갈 때마다 여러 오두막 문가로 호기심 어린 구경꾼들을 잔뜩 끌어냈고, 한두 번은 거무스름한 전사 하나가 미신 때문인지 경계심 때문인지 그들의 앞길을 가로질러 나서기도 했다."], ["They were not, however, interrupted; the darkness of the hour, and the coldness of the attempt, proving their principal friends.", "그러나 그들은 가로막히지 않았으니, 그 시각의 어둠과 그 시도의 대담함이 그들의 가장 든든한 벗임이 드러났다."]], [["The adventurers had got clear of the village, and were now swiftly approaching the shelter of the woods, when a loud and long cry arose from the lodge where Uncas had been confined.", "이 모험가들은 마을을 벗어나, 이제 숲의 은신처를 향해 빠르게 다가가고 있었는데, 그때 〈언카스〉가 갇혀 있던 오두막에서 크고 긴 외침이 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["The Mohican started on his feet, and shook his shaggy covering, as though the animal he counterfeited was about to make some desperate effort.", "〈모히칸〉은 벌떡 일어서며 그 텁수룩한 거죽을 흔들었으니, 마치 그가 흉내 내던 짐승이 어떤 필사적인 발버둥을 치려는 듯했다."]], [["\"Hold!\"", "\"멈춰!\""], ["said the scout, grasping his friend by the shoulder, \"let them yell again!", "척후가 제 벗의 어깨를 움켜잡으며 말했다. \"또 한 번 소리치게 두어라!"], ["'Twas nothing but wonderment.\"", "놀라움일 뿐이니까.\""]], [["He had no occasion to delay, for the next instant a burst of cries filled the outer air, and ran along the whole extent of the village.", "그가 지체할 까닭은 없었으니, 다음 순간 한바탕 외침이 바깥 공기를 가득 메우며 마을 전역으로 번져 갔기 때문이다."], ["Uncas cast his skin, and stepped forth in his own beautiful proportions.", "〈언카스〉는 그 가죽을 벗어 던지고, 본래의 그 아름다운 균형의 모습으로 걸어 나왔다."], ["Hawkeye tapped him lightly on the shoulder, and glided ahead.", "〈호크아이〉가 그의 어깨를 가볍게 두드리고는 앞으로 미끄러져 나아갔다."]], [["\"Now let the devils strike our scent!\"", "\"이제 그 악귀들이 우리 냄새를 좇아 보라지!\""], ["said the scout, tearing two rifles, with all their attendant accoutrements, from beneath a bush, and flourishing \"Killdeer\"", "척후가, 덤불 아래에서 두 자루 소총을 거기 딸린 온갖 장구와 함께 끌어내며, 또 〈언카스〉에게 그의 무기를 건네면서 〈사슴 잡이〉를 휘둘러 보이며 말했다."], ["as he handed Uncas his weapon; \"two, at least, will find it to their deaths.\"", "\"적어도 둘은 제 죽음을 그 냄새에서 찾게 될 게다.\""]], [["Then throwing their pieces to a low trail, like sportsmen in readiness for their game, they dashed forward, and were soon buried in the sombre darkness of the forest.", "그러고는, 사냥감을 노릴 채비를 갖춘 사냥꾼들처럼 총을 낮게 겨눠 든 채, 그들은 앞으로 내달았고, 이내 숲의 음울한 어둠 속에 파묻혔다."]], [["\"_Ant._ I shall remember: When Caesar says _Do this_, it is performed.\"", "카이사르께서 '이것을 행하라' 하시면, 그대로 이루어지나이다.\""]], [["_Julius Caesar._", "율리우스 카이사르"]], [["The impatience of the savages who lingered about the prison of Uncas, as has been seen, had overcome their dread of the conjurer's breath.", "이미 본 바와 같이, 〈언카스〉의 감옥 주위를 어슬렁대던 미개인들의 조바심이 마침내 마술사의 입김에 대한 두려움을 이겨 냈다."], ["They stole cautiously, and with beating hearts, to a crevice, through which the faint light of the fire was glimmering.", "그들은 조심스레, 두근거리는 가슴으로, 모닥불의 희미한 빛이 새어 나오는 틈새로 살금살금 다가갔다."], ["For several minutes they mistook the form of David for that of their prisoner; but the very accident which Hawkeye had foreseen occurred.", "몇 분 동안 그들은 〈데이비드〉의 형상을 자기네 포로의 것으로 잘못 알았으나, 〈호크아이〉가 예견했던 바로 그 우연한 사태가 벌어졌다."], ["Tired of keeping the extremities of his long person so near together, the singer gradually suffered the lower limbs to extend themselves, until one of his misshapen feet actually came in contact with and shoved aside the embers of the fire.", "그 긴 몸의 양 끝을 그토록 가까이 모은 채로 있기에 지친 가수는, 차츰 아랫다리를 뻗는 것을 허락하다가, 마침내 그 흉하게 생긴 발 한쪽이 정말로 모닥불의 잔불에 닿아 그것을 옆으로 밀어내고 말았다."], ["At first the Hurons believed the Delaware had been thus deformed by witchcraft.", "처음에 〈휴런족〉은 그 〈델라웨어족〉이 마법으로 이렇게 흉하게 변한 줄로만 알았다."], ["But when David, unconscious of being observed, turned his head, and exposed his simple, mild countenance, in place of the haughty lineaments of their prisoner, it would have exceeded the credulity of even a native to have doubted any longer.", "그러나 〈데이비드〉가 자신이 관찰당하는 줄도 모르고 고개를 돌려, 그 포로의 거만한 이목구비 대신 그 순박하고 온화한 얼굴을 드러냈을 때에는, 아무리 잘 속는 원주민이라 해도 더는 의심할 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["They rushed together into the lodge, and laying their hands, with but little ceremony, on their captive, immediately detected the imposition.", "그들은 한꺼번에 오두막으로 달려들어, 별다른 예의도 차리지 않고 포로에게 손을 얹고는, 곧장 그 속임수를 알아차렸다."], ["Then arose the cry first heard by the fugitives.", "그러자 도망자들이 처음에 들었던 그 외침이 터져 나왔다."], ["It was succeeded by the most frantic and angry demonstrations of vengeance.", "그 뒤를 가장 광적이고 분노에 찬 복수의 표시가 이었다."], ["David, however firm in his determination to cover the retreat of his friends, was compelled to believe that his own final hour had come.", "〈데이비드〉는 벗들의 퇴로를 가려 주겠다는 결심에는 굳건했으나, 자신의 마지막 시각이 왔음을 믿지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Deprived of his book and his pipe, he was fain to trust to a memory that rarely failed him on such subjects; and breaking forth in a loud and impassioned strain, he endeavored to soothe his passage into the other world, by singing the opening verse of a funeral anthem.", "책과 피리를 빼앗긴 그는, 이런 주제에서는 좀처럼 자신을 저버리지 않던 기억력에 의지할 수밖에 없었으니, 크고도 격정 어린 가락을 터뜨리며 장송 성가의 첫 절을 부름으로써 저세상으로 건너가는 길을 누그러뜨리고자 했다."], ["The Indians were seasonably reminded of his infirmity, and rushing into the open air, they aroused the village in the manner described.", "인디언들은 때마침 그의 결함을 떠올렸고, 트인 바깥으로 달려 나가 앞서 묘사한 방식으로 마을을 깨워 일으켰다."]], [["A native warrior fights as he sleeps, without the protection of anything defensive.", "원주민 전사는 잠잘 때와 마찬가지로, 그 어떤 방어 장구의 보호도 없이 싸운다."], ["The sounds of the alarm were, therefore, hardly uttered, before two hundred men were afoot, and ready for the battle or the chase, as either might be required.", "그러므로 경보 소리가 채 다 울리기도 전에, 이백 명이 일어나 전투든 추격이든 어느 쪽이 요구되든 채비를 갖췄다."], ["The escape was soon known; and the whole tribe crowded, in a body, around the council-lodge, impatiently awaiting the instruction of their chiefs.", "탈주는 곧 알려졌고, 부족 전체가 한 덩어리로 평의회 오두막 주위에 몰려들어, 추장들의 지시를 애타게 기다렸다."], ["In such a sudden demand on their wisdom, the presence of the cunning Magua could scarcely fail of being needed.", "그들의 지혜에 이토록 갑작스러운 요구가 닥친 마당에, 교활한 〈마구아〉의 존재가 필요해지지 않을 리 없었다."], ["His name was mentioned, and all looked round in wonder that he did not appear.", "그의 이름이 입에 올랐고, 모두가 그가 나타나지 않는 것을 의아해하며 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Messengers were then despatched to his lodge, requiring his presence.", "그리하여 그의 오두막으로 전령이 보내져, 그의 출석을 요청했다."]], [["In the meantime, some of the swiftest and most discreet of the young men were ordered to make the circuit of the clearing, under cover of the woods, in order to ascertain that their suspected neighbors, the Delawares, designed no mischief.", "그러는 사이, 젊은이들 가운데 가장 날쌔고 가장 신중한 자 몇이 명을 받아, 숲의 엄폐를 빌려 빈터를 한 바퀴 돌며, 의심스러운 이웃인 〈델라웨어족〉이 어떤 해코지도 꾀하지 않는지를 확인했다."], ["Women and children ran to and fro; and in short, the whole encampment exhibited another scene of wild and savage confusion.", "여인들과 아이들은 이리저리 뛰어다녔으니, 요컨대 진영 전체가 또 한 차례 거칠고 야만적인 혼란의 장면을 연출했다."], ["Gradually, however, these symptoms of disorder diminished; and in a few minutes the oldest and most distinguished chiefs were assembled in the lodge, in grave consultation.", "그러나 차츰 이런 무질서의 징후는 잦아들었고, 몇 분도 채 못 되어 가장 나이 많고 가장 명망 높은 추장들이 오두막에 모여 엄숙한 상의에 들어갔다."]], [["The clamor of many voices soon announced that a party approached, who might be expected to communicate some intelligence that would explain the mystery of the novel surprise.", "여러 목소리의 떠들썩함이 곧 한 무리가 다가오고 있음을 알렸으니, 그들에게서 이 새로운 기습의 수수께끼를 풀어 줄 어떤 소식을 들을 수 있으리라 기대할 만했다."], ["The crowd without gave way, and several warriors entered the place, bringing with them the hapless conjurer, who had been left so long by the scout in duress.", "바깥의 군중이 길을 비키자, 전사 몇이 그 자리로 들어서며, 척후가 그토록 오래 결박해 둔 그 가엾은 마술사를 데리고 왔다."]], [["Notwithstanding this man was held in very unequal estimation among the Hurons, some believing implicitly in his power, and others deeming him an impostor, he was now listened to by all with the deepest attention.", "이 사내가 〈휴런족〉 사이에서 매우 엇갈리는 평가를 받고 있었음에도 — 더러는 그의 능력을 굳게 믿었고, 더러는 그를 사기꾼으로 여겼다 — 이제 모두가 그의 말에 더없이 깊은 주의를 기울여 귀를 기울였다."], ["When his brief story was ended, the father of the sick woman stepped forth, and, in a few pithy expressions, related, in his turn, what he knew.", "그의 짧은 이야기가 끝나자, 병든 여인의 아비가 앞으로 나서서, 몇 마디 간결한 표현으로, 이번에는 자기가 아는 바를 들려주었다."], ["These two narratives gave a proper direction to the subsequent inquiries, which were now made with the characteristic cunning of savages.", "이 두 이야기는 그 후의 심문에 올바른 방향을 잡아 주었으니, 그 심문은 이제 미개인 특유의 교활함으로 이루어졌다."]], [["Instead of rushing in a confused and disorderly throng to the cavern, ten of the wisest and firmest among the chiefs were selected to prosecute the investigation.", "뒤죽박죽 무질서한 무리를 이루어 동굴로 몰려가는 대신, 추장들 가운데 가장 지혜롭고 가장 굳센 열 명이 추려져 조사를 수행하게 되었다."], ["As no time was to be lost, the instant the choice was made the individuals appointed rose in a body, and left the place without speaking.", "한시도 허비할 수 없었으므로, 그 선택이 이루어진 즉시 임명된 자들이 한 덩어리로 일어나, 한마디 말도 없이 그곳을 떠났다."], ["On reaching the entrance, the younger men in advance made way for their seniors; and the whole proceeded along the low, dark gallery, with the firmness of warriors ready to devote themselves to the public good, though, at the same time, secretly doubting the nature of the power with which they were about to contend.", "입구에 이르자 앞장선 젊은이들이 어른들에게 길을 양보했고, 일행 전체가 낮고 어두운 통로를 따라, 공공의 이익을 위해 한 몸을 바칠 각오가 된 전사다운 굳건함으로 나아갔으나, 그와 동시에 자신들이 맞서려는 그 힘의 정체를 속으로는 의심하고 있었다."]], [["The outer apartment of the cavern was silent and gloomy.", "동굴의 바깥 방은 고요하고 음산했다."], ["The woman lay in her usual place and posture, though there were those present who affirmed they had seen her borne to the woods, by the supposed \"medicine of the white men.\"", "여인은 늘 있던 자리에, 늘 있던 자세로 누워 있었으니, 그 자리에 있던 자들 가운데는 그녀가 이른바 \"백인의 의술\"에 들려 숲으로 옮겨지는 것을 보았노라 단언하는 이들도 있었건만."], ["Such a direct and palpable contradiction of the tale related by the father, caused all eyes to be turned on him.", "아비가 들려준 이야기와 이토록 직접적이고 명백히 어긋나는 사실에, 모든 눈이 그에게로 쏠렸다."], ["Chafed by the silent imputation, and inwardly troubled by so unaccountable a circumstance, the chief advanced to the side of the bed, and stooping, cast an incredulous look at the features, as if distrusting their reality.", "말없는 비난에 마음이 상하고, 이토록 설명할 수 없는 정황에 속으로 동요한 추장은, 침상 곁으로 나아가 몸을 굽혀, 그 얼굴이 실재하는지 의심하기라도 하듯, 못 믿겠다는 눈길을 던졌다."], ["His daughter was dead.", "그의 딸은 죽어 있었다."]], [["The unerring feeling of nature, for a moment prevailed, and the old warrior hid his eyes in sorrow.", "한순간 천성의 그르침 없는 감정이 우세하여, 늙은 전사는 슬픔에 잠겨 두 눈을 가렸다."], ["Then recovering his self-possession, he faced his companions, and pointing towards the corpse, he said, in the language of his people,--", "이윽고 평정을 되찾은 그는 동료들을 향해 돌아서서, 시신을 가리키며 제 부족의 말로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["\"The wife of my young man has left us!", "\"내 젊은이의 아내가 우리를 떠났소!"], ["the Great Spirit is angry with his children.\"", "위대한 정령께서 그 자녀들에게 노하셨소.\""]], [["The mournful intelligence was received in solemn silence.", "이 애통한 소식은 엄숙한 침묵 속에 받아들여졌다."], ["After a short pause, one of the elder Indians was about to speak, when a dark-looking object was seen rolling out of an adjoining apartment, into the very centre of the room where they stood.", "잠시 멈춤이 있은 뒤, 나이 든 인디언 하나가 막 입을 떼려는데, 검게 보이는 물체 하나가 옆방에서 굴러 나와 그들이 서 있는 방 한복판으로 들어오는 것이 보였다."], ["Ignorant of the nature of the beings they had to deal with, the whole party drew back a little, and gazed in admiration, until the object fronted the light, and rising on end, exhibited the distorted, but still fierce and sullen features of Magua.", "자기들이 상대해야 할 존재의 정체를 알지 못한 일행 전체가 조금 물러나, 그 물체가 빛 쪽으로 정면을 향하고 몸을 곧추세워, 일그러졌으나 여전히 사납고 음울한 〈마구아〉의 이목구비를 드러낼 때까지, 경이로움에 차 바라보았다."], ["The discovery was succeeded by a general exclamation of amazement.", "이 발견에 이어 모두가 일제히 놀라움의 탄성을 터뜨렸다."]], [["As soon, however, as the true situation of the chief was understood, several ready knives appeared, and his limbs and tongue were quickly released.", "그러나 추장의 진짜 처지가 곧 이해되자, 잘 드는 칼 몇 자루가 나타나, 그의 사지와 혀가 이내 풀려났다."], ["The Huron arose, and shook himself like a lion quitting his lair.", "〈휴런족〉은 일어서서, 굴을 나서는 사자처럼 제 몸을 떨쳐 흔들었다."], ["Not a word escaped him, though his hand played convulsively with the handle of his knife, while his lowering eyes scanned the whole party, as if they sought an object suited to the first burst of his vengeance.", "한마디 말도 새어 나오지 않았으나, 그의 손은 칼자루를 발작하듯 만지작거렸고, 그의 험상궂은 눈은 마치 복수의 첫 폭발에 어울리는 표적을 찾기라도 하듯 일행 전체를 훑었다."]], [["It was happy for Uncas and the scout, and even David, that they were all beyond the reach of his arm at such a moment; for, assuredly, no refinement in cruelty would then have deferred their deaths, in opposition to the promptings of the fierce temper that nearly choked him.", "이런 순간에 〈언카스〉와 척후가, 심지어 〈데이비드〉마저도 모두 그의 팔이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었던 것은 다행이었다. 분명코, 그를 거의 숨 막히게 하던 그 사나운 성정의 충동을 거슬러, 그 어떤 정교한 잔혹함도 그들의 죽음을 그때 미루어 놓지 못했을 터이니."], ["Meeting everywhere faces that he knew as friends, the savage grated his teeth together like rasps of iron, and swallowed his passion for want of a victim on whom to vent it.", "어디를 둘러보아도 벗으로 아는 얼굴들과 마주친 미개인은 쇠줄을 가는 듯 이를 갈며, 분풀이할 제물이 없어 그 격정을 삼켰다."], ["This exhibition of anger was noted by all present; and, from an apprehension of exasperating a temper that was already chafed nearly to madness, several minutes were suffered to pass before another word was uttered.", "이 분노의 표출은 그 자리에 있던 모두에게 포착되었으니, 이미 광기에 가깝도록 상해 버린 그 성미를 더 자극할까 우려하여, 또 한마디 말이 나오기까지 몇 분이 흐르도록 그냥 두었다."], ["When, however, suitable time had elapsed, the oldest of the party spoke.", "그러나 적당한 시간이 지났을 때, 일행 가운데 가장 나이 많은 자가 입을 열었다."]], [["\"My friend has found an enemy,\"", "\"내 벗이 적을 찾아냈구려.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Is he nigh, that the Hurons may take revenge?\"", "\"그자가 가까이 있어, 〈휴런족〉이 복수할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["\"Let the Delaware die!\"", "\"그 〈델라웨어족〉을 죽게 하라!\""], ["exclaimed Magua, in a voice of thunder.", "〈마구아〉가 우레 같은 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["Another long and expressive silence was observed, and was broken, as before, with due precaution, by the same individual.", "또 한 차례 길고 의미심장한 침묵이 지켜졌고, 앞서처럼 마땅한 신중함으로, 같은 자가 그것을 깨뜨렸다."]], [["\"The Mohican is swift of foot, and leaps far,\"", "\"그 〈모히칸족〉은 발이 빠르고, 멀리 뛰오.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"but my young men are on his trail.\"", "\"허나 내 젊은이들이 그자의 자취를 좇고 있소.\""]], [["\"Is he gone?\"", "\"그자가 갔단 말이오?\""], ["demanded Magua, in tones so deep and guttural, that they seemed to proceed from his inmost chest.", "〈마구아〉가 어찌나 깊고 목구멍에서 울리는 어조로 물었던지, 그 소리는 가슴속 가장 깊은 데서 나오는 듯했다."]], [["\"An evil spirit has been among us, and the Delaware has blinded our eyes.\"", "\"악령 하나가 우리 가운데 있었고, 그 〈델라웨어족〉이 우리 눈을 멀게 했소.\""]], [["\"An evil spirit!\"", "\"악령이라!\" 상대가 비웃듯 되뇌었다."], ["repeated the other, mockingly; \"'tis the spirit that has taken the lives of so many Hurons; the spirit that slew my young men at 'the tumbling river'; that took their scalps at the 'healing spring'; and who has now bound the arms of Le Renard Subtil!\"", "\"그토록 많은 〈휴런족〉의 목숨을 앗은 그 정령이렷다. '굴러떨어지는 강'에서 내 젊은이들을 죽이고, '치유의 샘'에서 그들의 머리 가죽을 벗기고, 이제 〈여우 르나르 쉬브틸〉의 두 팔을 묶은 그 정령 말이오!\""]], [["\"Of whom does my friend speak?\"", "\"내 벗은 누구를 두고 하는 말이오?\""]], [["\"Of the dog who carries the heart and cunning of a Huron under a pale skin--La Longue Carabine.\"", "\"창백한 살갗 아래 〈휴런족〉의 심장과 교활함을 품은 그 개를 두고 하는 말이오 — 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉.\""]], [["The pronunciation of so terrible a name produced the usual effect among his auditors.", "이토록 무시무시한 이름을 입에 올리자, 듣는 이들 사이에 여느 때와 같은 효과가 일었다."], ["But when time was given for reflection, and the warriors remembered that their formidable and daring enemy had even been in the bosom of their encampment, working injury, fearful rage took the place of wonder, and all those fierce passions with which the bosom of Magua had just been struggling were suddenly transferred to his companions.", "그러나 곰곰이 새겨 볼 틈이 주어지고, 그 가공할 대담한 적이 자기네 진영 한복판에까지 들어와 해코지를 했다는 것을 전사들이 떠올리자, 경이로움 자리에 두려운 분노가 들어섰고, 〈마구아〉의 가슴속에서 방금 막 들끓던 그 모든 사나운 격정이 별안간 그의 동료들에게로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Some among them gnashed their teeth in anger, others vented their feelings in yells, and some, again beat the air as frantically as if the object of their resentment were suffering under their blows.", "그들 가운데 더러는 분에 못 이겨 이를 갈았고, 더러는 비명으로 감정을 토했으며, 또 더러는 원한의 대상이 제 손찌검에 시달리기라도 하듯 미친 듯이 허공을 후려쳤다."], ["But this sudden outbreaking of temper as quickly subsided in the still and sullen restraint they most affected, in their moments of inaction.", "그러나 이 갑작스러운 성정의 폭발은, 그들이 정지의 순간에 가장 즐겨 부리던 그 고요하고 음울한 자제 속으로 이내 가라앉았다."]], [["Magua who had in his turn found leisure for reflection, now changed his manner, and assumed the air of one who knew how to think and act with a dignity worthy of so grave a subject.", "이번에는 〈마구아〉가 곰곰이 새겨 볼 여유를 찾았으니, 이제 태도를 바꾸어, 이토록 엄중한 사안에 걸맞은 위엄으로 생각하고 행동할 줄 아는 자의 풍모를 갖추었다."]], [["\"Let us go to my people,\"", "\"내 백성에게로 갑시다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"they wait for us.\"", "\"그들이 우리를 기다리고 있소.\""]], [["His companions consented in silence, and the whole of the savage party left the cavern and returned to the council-lodge.", "그의 동료들은 말없이 동의했고, 미개한 일행 전체가 동굴을 떠나 평의회 오두막으로 돌아왔다."], ["When they were seated, all eyes turned on Magua, who understood, from such an indication, that, by common consent, they had devolved the duty of relating what had passed on him.", "그들이 자리에 앉자, 모든 눈이 〈마구아〉에게로 쏠렸으니, 그는 그러한 표시에서, 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려줄 임무를 모두의 합의로 자신에게 맡겼음을 깨달았다."], ["He arose, and told his tale without duplicity or reservation.", "그는 일어서서, 거짓도 숨김도 없이 자기 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["The whole deception practised by both Duncan and Hawkeye was, of course, laid naked; and no room was found, even for the most superstitious of the tribe, any longer to affix a doubt on the character of the occurrences.", "〈던컨〉과 〈호크아이〉 둘이 함께 부린 속임수 전부가 물론 낱낱이 드러났으니, 부족 가운데 가장 미신에 빠진 자라 해도 이제 더는 그 사건들의 성격에 의심을 붙일 여지가 없었다."], ["It was but too apparent that they had been insultingly, shamefully, disgracefully deceived.", "그들이 모욕적으로, 수치스럽게, 치욕스럽게 속아 넘어갔다는 것은 너무도 분명했다."], ["When he had ended, and resumed his seat, the collected tribe--for his auditors, in substance, included all the fighting men of the party--sat regarding each other like men astonished equally at the audacity and the success of their enemies.", "그가 이야기를 끝내고 다시 자리에 앉자, 모인 부족은 — 그의 청중은 실상 일행의 전투원 전부를 포함하고 있었으니 — 적들의 대담함과 그 성공에 똑같이 경악한 자들처럼 서로를 마주 보며 앉아 있었다."], ["The next consideration, however, was the means and opportunities for revenge.", "그러나 다음으로 떠오른 고민은 복수의 수단과 기회였다."]], [["Additional pursuers were sent on the trail of the fugitives; and then the chiefs applied themselves, in earnest, to the business of consultation.", "도망자들의 자취를 좇아 추격대가 더 보내졌고, 이어 추장들은 진지하게 상의에 몰두했다."], ["Many different expedients were proposed by the elder warriors, in succession, to all of which Magua was a silent and respectful listener.", "나이 든 전사들이 잇달아 갖가지 다른 방책을 내놓았으니, 그 모든 것에 〈마구아〉는 말없이 공손한 청자로 임했다."], ["That subtle savage had recovered his artifice and self-command, and now proceeded towards his object with his customary caution and skill.", "이 교활한 미개인은 책략과 자제력을 되찾았고, 이제 여느 때의 신중함과 솜씨로 제 목적을 향해 나아갔다."], ["It was only when each one disposed to speak had uttered his sentiments, that he prepared to advance his own opinions.", "말하고자 하는 자가 저마다 제 의견을 다 내놓은 뒤에야, 비로소 그는 제 견해를 펼칠 채비를 했다."], ["They were given with additional weight from the circumstance that some of the runners had already returned, and reported that their enemies had been traced so far as to leave no doubt of their having sought safety in the neighboring camp of their suspected allies, the Delawares.", "그의 견해는 한층 무게를 더하여 주어졌으니, 척후 몇이 이미 돌아와, 적들의 자취가 이웃한 진영으로 — 의심스러운 동맹인 〈델라웨어족〉의 진영으로 — 안전을 구하러 들어갔음이 의심할 여지 없을 만큼 멀리까지 추적되었음을 보고했기 때문이다."], ["With the advantage of possessing this important intelligence, the chief warily laid his plans before his fellows, and, as might have been anticipated from his eloquence and cunning, they were adopted without a dissenting voice.", "이 중요한 정보를 손에 쥔 이점을 안고, 추장은 신중하게 동료들 앞에 제 계획을 펼쳐 놓았으니, 그의 웅변과 교활함으로 미루어 짐작할 수 있듯, 반대하는 목소리 하나 없이 채택되었다."], ["They were, briefly, as follows, both in opinions and in motives.", "그 계획은 견해로 보나 동기로 보나, 간추리면 다음과 같았다."]], [["It has been already stated that, in obedience to a policy rarely departed from, the sisters were separated so soon as they reached the Huron village.", "좀처럼 어기는 법이 없는 관례에 따라, 자매가 〈휴런족〉 마을에 닿자마자 떼어 놓였다는 것은 이미 언급한 바 있다."], ["Magua had early discovered that in retaining the person of Alice, he possessed the most effectual check on Cora.", "〈마구아〉는 일찍이 〈앨리스〉의 신병을 붙들고 있는 것이 〈코라〉에게 가장 효과적인 견제가 됨을 간파했다."], ["When they parted, therefore, he kept the former within reach of his hand, consigning the one he most valued to the keeping of their allies.", "그리하여 그들이 갈라설 때, 그는 전자를 제 손이 닿는 데 두고, 자신이 가장 귀히 여기는 쪽은 동맹들의 관리에 맡겼다."], ["The arrangement was understood to be merely temporary, and was made as much with a view to flatter his neighbors as in obedience to the invariable rule of Indian policy.", "이 조처는 그저 일시적인 것으로 이해되었으며, 인디언 정책의 변치 않는 규칙을 따르는 동시에 이웃들의 비위를 맞추려는 뜻에서 이루어진 것이었다."]], [["While goaded incessantly by those revengeful impulses that in a savage seldom slumber, the chief was still attentive to his more permanent personal interests.", "미개인의 마음속에서 좀처럼 잠들지 않는 그 복수심의 충동에 끊임없이 내몰리면서도, 추장은 자신의 더 항구적인 개인적 이익에도 주의를 게을리하지 않았다."], ["The follies and disloyalty committed in his youth were to be expiated by a long and painful penance, ere he could be restored to the full enjoyment of the confidence of his ancient people; and without confidence, there could be no authority in an Indian tribe.", "젊은 시절에 저지른 어리석음과 불충은, 그가 옛 백성의 신뢰를 온전히 다시 누리게 되기까지 길고도 고통스러운 속죄로써 씻어야 할 것이었으니, 신뢰가 없이는 인디언 부족 안에서 그 어떤 권위도 있을 수 없었다."], ["In this delicate and arduous situation, the crafty native had neglected no means of increasing his influence; and one of the happiest of his expedients had been the success with which he had cultivated the favor of their powerful and dangerous neighbors.", "이토록 미묘하고 험난한 처지에서, 이 교활한 원주민은 제 영향력을 키울 수단이라면 무엇 하나 소홀히 하지 않았으니, 그 가장 행복한 방책 가운데 하나는 강력하고 위험한 이웃들의 호의를 가꾸어 거둔 성공이었다."], ["The result of his experiment had answered all the expectations of his policy; for the Hurons were in no degree exempt from that governing principle of nature, which induces man to value his gifts precisely in the degree that they are appreciated by others.", "그의 시도가 거둔 결과는 그 정책이 기대한 바를 죄다 채웠으니, 인간으로 하여금 제 재능을 꼭 남들이 인정하는 만큼만 귀히 여기게 하는 그 천성의 지배적 원리에서 〈휴런족〉 또한 조금도 예외가 아니었기 때문이다."]], [["But, while he was making this ostensible sacrifice to general considerations, Magua never lost sight of his individual motives.", "그러나 전체의 고려를 위해 이렇게 겉치레로 희생을 치르면서도, 〈마구아〉는 결코 제 개인적 동기를 놓치지 않았다."], ["The latter had been frustrated by the unlooked-for events which had placed all his prisoners beyond his control; and he now found himself reduced to the necessity of suing for favors to those whom it had so lately been his policy to oblige.", "후자는 그의 모든 포로를 그의 통제 밖에 놓아 버린 뜻밖의 사태로 좌절되었으니, 그는 이제 얼마 전까지만 해도 제 편에서 은혜를 베푸는 것이 정책이던 자들에게 도리어 호의를 간청해야 하는 처지로 내몰린 자신을 발견했다."]], [["Several of the chiefs had proposed deep and treacherous schemes to surprise the Delawares, and, by gaining possession of their camp, to recover their prisoners by the same blow; for all agreed that their honor, their interests, and the peace and happiness of their dead countrymen, imperiously required them speedily to immolate some victims to their revenge.", "추장들 가운데 몇은 〈델라웨어족〉을 기습할 깊고 음험한 계략을 내놓았으니, 그들의 진영을 손에 넣음으로써 한 번의 타격으로 포로들을 되찾자는 것이었다. 모두가 한목소리로, 자기네 명예와 이익, 그리고 죽은 동포들의 안식과 행복이, 복수의 제단에 어서 몇몇 제물을 바칠 것을 준엄하게 요구한다는 데 동의했기 때문이다."], ["But plans so dangerous to attempt, and of such doubtful issue, Magua found little difficulty in defeating.", "그러나 시도하기에 이토록 위험하고 그 결말이 이토록 불확실한 계획들을, 〈마구아〉는 별 어려움 없이 무산시켰다."], ["He exposed their risk and fallacy with his usual skill; and it was only after he had removed every impediment, in the shape of opposing advice, that he ventured to propose his own projects.", "그는 여느 때의 솜씨로 그것들의 위험성과 허점을 드러내 보였고, 반대 의견의 형태를 띤 온갖 장애물을 모조리 치워 버린 다음에야, 비로소 제 구상을 내놓는 모험에 나섰다."]], [["He commenced by flattering the self-love of his auditors; a never-failing method of commanding attention.", "그는 청중의 자존심을 추켜세우는 것으로 말문을 열었으니, 이는 결코 실패하는 법이 없는 주의를 끄는 방법이었다."], ["When he had enumerated the many different occasions on which the Hurons had exhibited their courage and prowess, in the punishment of insults, he digressed in a high encomium on the virtue of wisdom.", "〈휴런족〉이 모욕을 응징하면서 자신들의 용기와 무용을 떨쳐 보인 갖가지 경우를 일일이 열거한 뒤, 그는 곁가지로 빠져 지혜의 미덕을 드높이 찬양했다."], ["He painted the quality, as forming the great point of difference between the beaver and other brutes; between brutes and men; and, finally, between the Hurons, in particular, and the rest of the human race.", "그는 그 자질이야말로 비버와 여느 짐승 사이의, 짐승과 인간 사이의, 그리고 마침내 특히 〈휴런족〉과 나머지 인류 사이의 큰 차이를 이루는 점이라 그려 보였다."], ["After he had sufficiently extolled the property of discretion, he undertook to exhibit in what manner its use was applicable to the present situation of their tribe.", "분별의 속성을 흡족하리만치 추켜올린 뒤, 그는 그 쓰임이 어떤 식으로 자기 부족의 지금 처지에 들어맞는지를 펼쳐 보이는 일에 나섰다."], ["On the one hand, he said, was their great pale father, the governor of the Canadas, who had looked upon his children with a hard eye since their tomahawks had been so red; on the other, a people as numerous as themselves, who spoke a different language, possessed different interests, and loved them not, and who would be glad of any pretence to bring them in disgrace with the great white chief.", "한편으로는, 그가 말하기를, 그들의 위대한 창백한 아버지인 〈캐나다〉 총독이 있으니, 그는 자녀들의 토마호크가 그토록 붉어진 이래로 모진 눈으로 그 자녀들을 바라보아 왔다. 다른 한편으로는, 자기들만큼이나 수가 많고, 다른 말을 쓰며, 다른 이해관계를 지니고, 자기들을 사랑하지 않으며, 어떤 구실이든 잡아 자기들을 그 위대한 백인 추장의 눈 밖에 나게 하기를 기꺼워할 한 민족이 있다."], ["Then he spoke of their necessities; of the gifts they had a right to expect for their past services; of their distance from their proper hunting-grounds and native villages; and of the necessity of consulting prudence more, and inclination less, in so critical circumstances.", "이어 그는 그들의 궁핍을, 지난날의 봉사에 대해 마땅히 기대할 권리가 있는 선물들을, 자기네 본래의 사냥터와 고향 마을에서 멀리 떨어져 있음을, 그리고 이토록 위태로운 정황에서 본능을 덜 따르고 신중함을 더 따라야 할 필요를 이야기했다."], ["When he perceived that, while the old men applauded his moderation, many of the fiercest and most distinguished of the warriors listened to these politic plans with lowering looks, he cunningly led them back to the subject which they most loved.", "노인들은 그의 절제를 칭송하는 반면, 가장 사납고 가장 명망 높은 전사들 가운데 많은 이가 이런 책략 어린 계획들을 험상궂은 낯빛으로 듣고 있음을 알아챘을 때, 그는 교활하게도 그들이 가장 사랑하는 화제로 그들을 다시 이끌었다."], ["He spoke openly of the fruits of their wisdom, which he boldly pronounced would be a complete and final triumph over their enemies.", "그는 그들의 지혜가 맺을 열매를 거리낌 없이 말했으니, 그것이 적들에 대한 완전하고도 최종적인 승리가 되리라고 대담하게 단언했다."], ["He even darkly hinted that their success might be extended, with proper caution, in such a manner as to include the destruction of all whom they had reason to hate.", "그는 심지어, 마땅한 신중함을 곁들이면 그 성공이 자기들이 미워할 만한 까닭이 있는 모든 자의 파멸까지 아우르는 식으로 확장될 수 있으리라고 은근히 어둡게 내비쳤다."], ["In short, he so blended the warlike with the artful, the obvious with the obscure, as to flatter the propensities of both parties, and to leave to each subject of hope, while neither could say it clearly comprehended his intentions.", "요컨대, 그는 호전적인 것과 책략적인 것을, 명백한 것과 모호한 것을 어찌나 잘 뒤섞었던지, 양편의 성향을 모두 추켜세우고 저마다에게 희망의 거리를 남겨 주면서도, 어느 쪽도 그의 의도를 똑똑히 알아들었노라 말할 수 없게 했다."]], [["The orator, or the politician, who can produce such a state of things, is commonly popular with his contemporaries, however he may be treated by posterity.", "이런 상태를 빚어낼 수 있는 웅변가나 정치가는, 후세에서 어떤 대접을 받든, 당대인들 사이에서는 흔히 인기를 누린다."], ["All perceived that more was meant than was uttered, and each one believed that the hidden meaning was precisely such as his own faculties enabled him to understand, or his own wishes led him to anticipate.", "모두가 입에 올린 것보다 더 많은 것이 뜻해졌음을 알아챘고, 저마다 그 숨은 뜻이 바로 제 능력으로 이해할 수 있는 것이거나 제 소망이 미리 점치게 한 그런 것이라고 믿었다."]], [["In this happy state of things, it is not surprising that the management of Magua prevailed.", "이토록 흡족한 상황에서 〈마구아〉의 수완이 이긴 것은 놀랄 일이 아니다."], ["The tribe consented to act with deliberation, and with one voice they committed the direction of the whole affair to the government of the chief who had suggested such wise and intelligible expedients.", "부족은 신중하게 행동하기로 합의했고, 한목소리로 이 일 전체의 지휘를 그토록 지혜롭고 알아듣기 쉬운 방책을 내놓은 추장의 다스림에 맡겼다."]], [["Magua had now attained one great object of all his cunning and enterprise.", "이제 〈마구아〉는 그 모든 교활함과 진취성의 큰 목적 하나를 이루었다."], ["The ground he had lost in the favor of his people was completely regained, and he found himself even placed at the head of affairs.", "제 백성의 호의에서 잃었던 입지를 온전히 되찾았을 뿐 아니라, 심지어 사태의 수장 자리에까지 오른 자신을 발견했다."], ["He was, in truth, their ruler; and, so long as he could maintain his popularity, no monarch could be more despotic, especially while the tribe continued in a hostile country.", "그는 사실상 그들의 통치자였으니, 제 인기를 유지할 수 있는 한, 그 어떤 군주도 그보다 더 전제적일 수 없었으며, 더구나 부족이 적지에 머무는 동안에는 더욱 그러했다."], ["Throwing off, therefore, the appearance of consultation, he assumed the grave air of authority necessary to support the dignity of his office.", "그리하여 상의하는 듯한 겉모습을 벗어 던지고, 그는 제 직위의 위엄을 떠받치는 데 필요한 엄중한 권위의 풍모를 갖추었다."]], [["Runners were despatched for intelligence in different directions; spies were ordered to approach and feel the encampment of the Delawares; the warriors were dismissed to their lodges, with an intimation that their services would soon be needed; and the women and children were ordered to retire, with a warning that it was their province to be silent.", "여러 방향으로 소식을 알아 오라 전령이 보내졌고, 첩자들은 〈델라웨어족〉의 진영에 다가가 그 동태를 살피라는 명을 받았으며, 전사들은 곧 그 소임이 필요해지리라는 언질과 함께 제 오두막으로 물러가게 되었고, 여인들과 아이들은 침묵을 지키는 것이 그들의 본분이라는 경고와 함께 물러가라는 명을 받았다."], ["When these several arrangements were made, Magua passed through the village, stopping here and there to pay a visit where he thought his presence might be flattering to the individual.", "이런 여러 조처가 이루어지자, 〈마구아〉는 마을을 가로질러 다니며, 제 존재가 그 사람의 비위를 맞추리라 여겨지는 곳마다 여기저기 멈추어 방문을 했다."], ["He confirmed his friends in their confidence, fixed the wavering, and gratified all.", "그는 제 벗들의 신뢰를 굳혔고, 흔들리는 자들을 다잡았으며, 모두를 흡족하게 했다."], ["Then he sought his own lodge.", "그런 다음 자기 오두막을 찾아갔다."], ["The wife the Huron chief had abandoned, when he was chased from among his people, was dead.", "〈휴런족〉 추장이 제 백성 사이에서 쫓겨났을 때 버리고 떠난 아내는 죽고 없었다."], ["Children he had none; and he now occupied a hut, without companion of any sort.", "자식은 하나도 없었으니, 이제 그는 그 어떤 동무도 없이 한 오두막을 차지하고 있었다."], ["It was, in fact, the dilapidated and solitary structure in which David had been discovered, and whom he had tolerated in his presence, on those few occasions when they met, with the contemptuous indifference of a haughty superiority.", "그것은 실은 〈데이비드〉가 발견되었던 바로 그 허물어지고 외딴 건물이었으니, 그는 둘이 마주치던 그 몇 안 되는 경우에, 거만한 우월감에서 비롯된 경멸 어린 무관심으로 〈데이비드〉를 제 앞에 두는 것을 너그러이 참아 주었다."]], [["Hither, then, Magua retired, when his labors of policy were ended.", "그리하여 정책의 노고가 끝나자, 〈마구아〉는 이곳으로 물러났다."], ["While others slept, however, he neither knew nor sought repose.", "그러나 다른 이들이 잠든 동안, 그는 휴식을 알지도, 구하지도 않았다."], ["Had there been one sufficiently curious to have watched the movements of the newly elected chief, he would have seen him seated in a corner of his lodge, musing on the subject of his future plans, from the hour of his retirement to the time he had appointed for the warriors to assemble again.", "만약 새로 선출된 이 추장의 거동을 지켜볼 만큼 호기심 많은 자가 하나라도 있었다면, 그가 물러난 그 시각부터 전사들을 다시 모으기로 정해 둔 때까지, 오두막 한구석에 앉아 제 앞날의 계획을 곰곰이 궁리하는 모습을 보았을 것이다."], ["Occasionally the air breathed through the crevices of the hut, and the low flames that fluttered about the embers of the fire threw their wavering light on the person of the sullen recluse.", "이따금 바람이 오두막 틈새로 새어 들었고, 모닥불의 잔불 위에서 너울대는 낮은 불꽃이 그 음울한 은둔자의 몸 위로 흔들리는 빛을 던졌다."], ["At such moments it would not have been difficult to have fancied the dusky savage the Prince of Darkness, brooding on his own fancied wrongs, and plotting evil.", "그런 순간에는, 이 거무스름한 미개인을, 제 멋대로 그려 낸 억울함을 곱씹으며 악행을 꾸미는 어둠의 군주로 상상하기란 그리 어렵지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Long before the day dawned, however, warrior after warrior entered the solitary hut of Magua, until they had collected to the number of twenty.", "그러나 날이 밝기 한참 전에, 전사들이 하나둘 〈마구아〉의 외딴 오두막으로 들어와, 마침내 스무 명에 이르도록 모여들었다."], ["Each bore his rifle, and all the other accoutrements of war, though the paint was uniformly peaceful.", "저마다 소총을 지녔고, 그 밖의 온갖 전쟁 장구를 갖췄으나, 칠한 채색은 한결같이 평화로운 것이었다."], ["The entrance of these fierce-looking beings was unnoticed; some seating themselves in the shadows of the place, and others standing like motionless statues, until the whole of the designated band was collected.", "이 사나운 몰골의 존재들이 들어오는 것은 주목받지 못했으니, 더러는 그 자리의 그늘 속에 자리를 잡고, 더러는 미동도 없는 조각상처럼 서서, 지정된 무리 전체가 다 모일 때까지 기다렸다."]], [["Then Magua arose and gave the signal to proceed, marching himself in advance.", "이윽고 〈마구아〉가 일어나 출발 신호를 보내며, 스스로 앞장서 행진했다."], ["They followed their leader singly, and in that well-known order which has obtained the distinguishing appellation of \"Indian file.\"", "그들은 외줄로, \"인디언 줄\"이라는 독특한 이름을 얻은 그 잘 알려진 대형으로 우두머리를 뒤따랐다."], ["Unlike other men engaged in the spirit-stirring business of war, they stole from their camp unostentatiously and unobserved, resembling a band of gliding spectres, more than warriors seeking the bubble reputation by deeds of desperate daring.", "마음을 들끓게 하는 전쟁의 일에 나선 여느 사내들과 달리, 그들은 거들먹거리지도 눈에 띄지도 않게 제 진영에서 몰래 빠져나갔으니, 무모한 만용의 위업으로 덧없는 명성을 좇는 전사들이라기보다는 미끄러지듯 나아가는 한 무리의 유령에 가까웠다."]], [["Instead of taking the path which led directly towards the camp of the Delawares, Magua led his party for some distance down the windings of the stream, and along the little artificial lake of the beavers.", "〈델라웨어족〉의 진영으로 곧장 이어지는 길을 잡는 대신, 〈마구아〉는 일행을 이끌고 한동안 시냇물의 굽이를 따라 내려가, 비버들의 작은 인공 호수를 끼고 나아갔다."], ["The day began to dawn as they entered the clearing which had been formed by those sagacious and industrious animals.", "이 영리하고 부지런한 짐승들이 만들어 놓은 빈터로 그들이 들어설 무렵 날이 밝기 시작했다."], ["Though Magua, who had resumed his ancient garb, bore the outline of a fox on the dressed skin which formed his robe, there was one chief of his party who carried the beaver as his peculiar symbol, or \"totem.\"", "옛 차림을 다시 갖춘 〈마구아〉는 제 망토를 이루는 무두질한 가죽 위에 여우의 윤곽을 지니고 있었으나, 일행 가운데 한 추장은 비버를 제 특유의 상징, 곧 \"토템\"으로 지니고 있었다."], ["There would have been a species of profanity in the omission, had this man passed so powerful a community of his fancied kindred, without bestowing some evidence of his regard.", "만약 이 사내가 자기와 친족이라 여기는 이토록 강력한 무리를 지나치면서 어떤 경의의 표시도 베풀지 않았다면, 그것은 일종의 불경이 되었을 터였다."], ["Accordingly, he paused, and spoke in words as kind and friendly as if he were addressing more intelligent beings.", "그리하여 그는 걸음을 멈추고, 마치 더 지각 있는 존재들에게 말을 건네듯 다정하고 정겨운 말을 건넸다."], ["He called the animals his cousins, and reminded them that his protecting influence was the reason they remained unharmed, while so many avaricious traders were prompting the Indians to take their lives.", "그는 그 짐승들을 제 사촌이라 부르며, 그토록 많은 탐욕스러운 장사꾼들이 인디언들을 부추겨 그들의 목숨을 앗으려 하는 와중에도 그들이 무사히 남아 있는 것은 자신의 보호하는 영향력 덕분임을 일깨워 주었다."], ["He promised a continuance of his favors, and admonished them to be grateful.", "그는 앞으로도 호의를 베풀기를 약속했고, 고마워할 것을 당부했다."], ["After which, he spoke of the expedition in which he was himself engaged, and intimated, though with sufficient delicacy and circumlocution, the expediency of bestowing on their relative a portion of that wisdom for which they were so renowned.[24]", "그런 다음, 자신이 몸소 나선 이번 원정을 이야기했으며, 충분한 조심성과 에두름을 곁들이긴 했으나, 그들이 그토록 이름난 그 지혜의 일부를 그들의 친척에게 나누어 주는 것이 마땅하다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["During the utterance of this extraordinary address, the companions of the speaker were as grave and as attentive to his language as though they were all equally impressed with its propriety.", "이 기이한 연설이 펼쳐지는 동안, 그 화자의 동료들은 마치 다들 똑같이 그 적절함에 감명받기라도 한 것처럼, 그의 말에 엄숙하고도 진지하게 귀를 기울였다."], ["Once or twice black objects were seen rising to the surface of the water, and the Huron expressed pleasure, conceiving that his words were not bestowed in vain.", "한두 차례 검은 물체들이 수면으로 떠오르는 것이 보였고, 〈휴런족〉은 제 말이 헛되이 베풀어진 것이 아니라 여겨 흡족함을 드러냈다."], ["Just as he had ended his address, the head of a large beaver was thrust from the door of a lodge, whose earthen walls had been much injured, and which the party had believed, from its situation, to be uninhabited.", "그가 막 연설을 끝냈을 때, 큰 비버 한 마리의 머리가 한 오두막의 문에서 쑥 내밀어졌으니, 그 흙벽은 몹시 상해 있었고, 일행은 그 위치로 미루어 그 안에 아무도 살지 않으리라 믿어 온 터였다."], ["Such an extraordinary sign of confidence was received by the orator as a highly favorable omen; and though the animal retreated a little precipitately, he was lavish of his thanks and commendations.", "이토록 비상한 신뢰의 표시를 그 웅변가는 더없이 길한 징조로 받아들였으니, 그 짐승이 다소 황급히 물러갔음에도, 그는 감사와 칭송을 아낌없이 쏟아 냈다."]], [["When Magua thought sufficient time had been lost in gratifying the family affection of the warrior, he again made the signal to proceed.", "〈마구아〉가 그 전사의 가족애를 흡족하게 하느라 충분한 시간을 허비했다고 여겼을 때, 그는 다시 출발 신호를 보냈다."], ["As the Indians moved away in a body, and with a step that would have been inaudible to the ears of any common man, the same venerable-looking beaver once more ventured his head from its cover.", "인디언들이 한 덩어리로, 여느 보통 사람의 귀에는 들리지 않았을 걸음으로 멀어져 가자, 바로 그 점잖아 보이는 비버가 다시 한번 그 은신처에서 머리를 내미는 모험을 감행했다. 만약 〈휴런족〉 가운데 누구라도 뒤를 돌아보았다면, 그 짐승이 그들의 거동을 자칫 이성으로 오인하기 쉬운 관심과 영민함으로 지켜보고 있는 것을 보았을 것이다."], ["Had any of the Hurons turned to look behind them, they would have seen the animal watching their movements with an interest and sagacity that might easily have been mistaken for reason.", "사실, 그 네발짐승의 행태가 어찌나 뚜렷하고 알아듣기 쉬웠던지, 아무리 노련한 관찰자라 해도 그 행동을 도무지 설명할 길이 없었을 터였다. 일행이 숲으로 들어선 그 순간까지는."], ["Indeed, so very distinct and intelligible were the devices of the quadruped, that even the most experienced observer would have been at a loss to account for its actions, until the moment when the party entered the forest, when the whole would have been explained, by seeing the entire animal issue from the lodge, uncasing, by the act, the grave features of Chingachgook from his mask of fur.", "그때 그 짐승이 통째로 오두막에서 나와, 그 동작으로 털가죽 가면을 벗으며 〈칭가치국〉의 엄숙한 이목구비를 드러냄으로써, 모든 것이 비로소 설명되었을 것이다. * 인디언 줄: 한 사람씩 외줄로 늘어서서 나아가는 인디언 특유의 행군 대형을 가리킨다."]], [["\"Brief, I pray you; for you see, 'tis a busy time with me.\"", "잠시면 됩니다, 부탁이오. 보시다시피 나는 지금 분주한 때라."]], [["_Much Ado About Nothing._", "「헛소동」"]], [["The tribe, or rather half tribe, of Delawares, which has been so often mentioned, and whose present place of encampment was so nigh the temporary village of the Hurons, could assemble about an equal number of warriors with the latter people.", "이미 여러 차례 언급된 〈델라웨어〉족, 아니 그 절반의 부족은, 지금 야영지가 〈휴런〉족의 임시 마을과 무척이나 가까운 곳에 자리하고 있었는데, 그 전사의 수가 후자의 부족과 거의 맞먹을 만했다."], ["Like their neighbors, they had followed Montcalm into the territories of the English crown, and were making heavy and serious inroads on the hunting-grounds of the Mohawks; though they had seen fit, with the mysterious reserve so common among the natives, to withhold their assistance at the moment when it was most required.", "이웃과 마찬가지로 그들 역시 〈몽칼름〉을 따라 〈영국〉 왕실의 영토로 들어와, 〈모호크〉족의 사냥터에 무겁고도 심각한 침략을 거듭하고 있었다. 다만 그들은, 원주민들 사이에 흔한 그 알 수 없는 신중함으로, 정작 가장 절실히 필요한 순간에 제 도움을 거두는 것이 옳다고 여겼다."], ["The French had accounted for this unexpected defection on the part of their ally in various ways.", "프랑스군은 동맹의 이 뜻밖의 이탈을 여러 가지로 해명했다."], ["It was the prevalent opinion, however, that they had been influenced by veneration for the ancient treaty, that had once made them dependent on the Six Nations for military protection, and now rendered them reluctant to encounter their former masters.", "그러나 가장 널리 퍼진 견해는, 한때 그들을 군사적 보호를 위해 〈여섯 부족〉에게 의존하게 만들었던 오랜 조약에 대한 외경심이 작용하여, 이제는 옛 주인과 맞서기를 꺼리게 했다는 것이었다."], ["As for the tribe itself, it had been content to announce to Montcalm, through his emissaries, with Indian brevity, that their hatchets were dull, and time was necessary to sharpen them.", "부족 자체로 말하자면, 〈몽칼름〉에게 그의 사절을 통해 인디언다운 간결함으로, 자기네 손도끼가 무뎌졌으니 그것을 벼리려면 시간이 필요하다고 알리는 것으로 만족했다."], ["The politic captain of the Canadas had deemed it wiser to submit to entertain a passive friend, than by any acts of ill-judged severity to convert him into an open enemy.", "〈캐나다〉의 노련한 사령관은, 잘못 판단된 어떤 가혹한 처사로 그를 공공연한 적으로 돌리느니, 차라리 소극적인 벗을 너그러이 받아들이는 편이 더 현명하다고 여겼다."]], [["On that morning when Magua led his silent party from the settlement of the beavers into the forest, in the manner described, the sun rose upon the Delaware encampment as if it had suddenly burst upon a busy people, actively employed in all the customary avocations of high noon.", "〈마구아〉가 앞서 묘사한 방식으로 그 말없는 무리를 비버들의 정착지에서 숲으로 이끌던 그날 아침, 해는 〈델라웨어〉 야영지 위로 떠올랐으니, 마치 한낮의 온갖 일상적 일에 분주히 종사하는 활기찬 사람들 위로 느닷없이 솟아오른 듯했다."], ["The women ran from lodge to lodge, some engaged in preparing their morning's meal, a few earnestly bent on seeking the comforts necessary to their habits, but more pausing to exchange hasty and whispered sentences with their friends.", "여인들은 오두막에서 오두막으로 뛰어다녔는데, 더러는 아침 식사를 준비하는 데 매달렸고, 몇몇은 제 습관에 필요한 안락을 구하는 데 골똘했으나, 더 많은 이들은 걸음을 멈추고 벗들과 황급한 귓속말을 주고받았다."], ["The warriors were lounging in groups, musing more than they conversed; and when a few words were uttered, speaking like men who deeply weighed their opinions.", "전사들은 무리 지어 빈둥거리며, 이야기를 나누기보다는 사색에 잠겼고, 몇 마디 말을 입에 올릴 때에는 제 의견을 깊이 저울질하는 사람들처럼 말했다."], ["The instruments of the chase were to be seen in abundance among the lodges; but none departed.", "사냥 도구들이 오두막들 사이에 넉넉히 보였으나, 떠나는 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["Here and there a warrior was examining his arms, with an attention that is rarely bestowed on the implements, when no other enemy than the beasts of the forest is expected to be encountered.", "여기저기서 전사 한 명이 제 무기를 살피고 있었으니, 숲의 짐승 말고는 마주칠 적이 없으리라 예상될 때면 좀처럼 베풀지 않는 주의를 기울이고 있었다."], ["And, occasionally, the eyes of a whole group were turned simultaneously towards a large and silent lodge in the centre of the village, as if it contained the subject of their common thoughts.", "그리고 이따금, 무리 전체의 눈길이 마을 한복판에 자리한 크고 고요한 오두막을 향해 일제히 돌려졌으니, 마치 그 안에 그들 공통의 생각거리가 담겨 있는 듯했다."]], [["During the existence of this scene, a man suddenly appeared at the farthest extremity of a platform of rock which formed the level of the village.", "이 광경이 펼쳐지는 동안, 마을의 평지를 이루는 바위 단(壇)의 가장 먼 끝에 한 사내가 불쑥 나타났다."], ["He was without arms, and his paint tended rather to soften than increase the natural sternness of his austere countenance.", "그는 무장을 하지 않았고, 그 칠한 무늬는 준엄한 얼굴의 타고난 엄격함을 더하기보다는 누그러뜨리는 쪽이었다."], ["When in full view of the Delawares he stopped, and made a gesture of amity, by throwing his arm upward towards heaven, and then letting it fall impressively on his breast.", "〈델라웨어〉족의 눈에 온전히 들어오자 그는 걸음을 멈추고, 한 팔을 하늘로 치켜올렸다가 인상적으로 가슴 위로 떨어뜨리며 우호의 몸짓을 지었다."], ["The inhabitants of the village answered his salute by a low murmur of welcome, and encouraged him to advance by similar indications of friendship.", "마을 사람들은 나직한 환영의 웅성거림으로 그 인사에 답했고, 비슷한 우정의 표시로 그에게 다가오라고 권했다."], ["Fortified by these assurances, the dark figure left the brow of the natural rocky terrace, where it had stood a moment, drawn in a strong outline against the blushing morning sky, and moved with dignity into the very centre of the huts.", "이 확약에 힘을 얻어, 그 어두운 형상은 잠시 서 있던 천연 바위 단의 가장자리를 떠났으니, 그곳에서 그는 발그레한 아침 하늘을 배경으로 또렷한 윤곽을 그리고 서 있었으며, 오두막들의 한가운데로 위엄 있게 나아갔다."], ["As he approached, nothing was audible but the rattling of the light silver ornaments that loaded his arms and neck, and the tinkling of the little bells that fringed his deer-skin moccasins.", "그가 다가오는 동안 들리는 것이라곤, 그의 팔과 목을 잔뜩 두른 가벼운 은 장식이 짤랑이는 소리와, 사슴 가죽 모카신 가장자리를 두른 작은 방울들의 딸랑이는 소리뿐이었다."], ["He made, as he advanced, many courteous signs of greeting to the men he passed, neglecting to notice the women, however, like one who deemed their favor, in the present enterprise, of no importance.", "그는 나아가며 지나치는 사내들에게 정중한 인사의 몸짓을 여러 번 보냈으나, 여인들에게는 눈길조차 주지 않았으니, 지금의 이 일에서 그들의 호의 따위는 조금도 중요치 않다고 여기는 사람 같았다."], ["When he had reached the group in which it was evident, by the haughtiness of their common mien, that the principal chiefs were collected, the stranger paused, and then the Delawares saw that the active and erect form that stood before them was that of the well-known Huron chief, Le Renard Subtil.", "그 공통의 거만한 태도로 보아 주요 추장들이 모인 것이 분명한 무리에 이르자, 낯선 이는 걸음을 멈추었고, 그제야 〈델라웨어〉족은 자기네 앞에 선 그 활기차고 꼿꼿한 형상이 바로 그 이름난 〈휴런〉의 추장, 〈르 르나르 쉬브틸〉임을 알아보았다."]], [["His reception was grave, silent, and wary.", "그를 맞이하는 태도는 진중하고, 말이 없으며, 조심스러웠다."], ["The warriors in front stepped aside, opening the way to their most approved orator by the action; one who spoke all those languages that were cultivated among the northern aborigines.", "앞에 선 전사들이 옆으로 물러서며, 그 행동으로 가장 인정받는 자기네 변사(辯士)에게 길을 열어 주었으니, 그는 북방 원주민들 사이에서 갈고닦인 모든 언어를 구사하는 자였다."]], [["\"The wise Huron is welcome,\"", "\"지혜로운 〈휴런〉을 환영하노라.\""], ["said the Delaware, in the language of the Maquas; \"he is come to eat his 'succotash,'[25] with his brothers of the lakes.\"", "〈델라웨어〉인이 〈마쿠아〉족의 말로 말했다. \"그는 호수의 형제들과 함께 자기네 '서코태시'를 먹으러 왔도다.\""]], [["\"He is come,\" repeated Magua, bending his head with the dignity of an Eastern prince.", "* 서코태시: 옥수수와 콩을 함께 끓여 만든 원주민의 요리."]], [["The chief extended his arm, and taking the other by the wrist, they once more exchanged friendly salutations.", "\"그는 왔노라.\" 〈마구아〉가 동방 군주의 위엄으로 고개를 숙이며 되풀이했다."], ["Then the Delaware invited his guest to enter his own lodge, and share his morning meal.", "추장이 한 팔을 내밀어 상대의 손목을 잡으니, 그들은 다시 한번 우호의 인사를 주고받았다."], ["The invitation was accepted; and the two warriors, attended by three or four of the old men, walked calmly away, leaving the rest of the tribe devoured by a desire to understand the reasons of so unusual a visit, and yet not betraying the least impatience by sign or word.", "그러자 〈델라웨어〉인은 손님에게 제 오두막으로 들어와 아침 식사를 함께 나누자고 청했다. 청은 받아들여졌고, 두 전사는 노인 서넛을 거느린 채 차분히 걸어갔으니, 나머지 부족 사람들은 이토록 이례적인 방문의 까닭을 알고 싶은 마음에 애가 탔으나, 몸짓으로도 말로도 조금의 조바심조차 드러내지 않았다."]], [["During the short and frugal repast that followed, the conversation was extremely circumspect, and related entirely to the events of the hunt in which Magua had so lately been engaged.", "뒤따른 짧고 소박한 식사 동안, 대화는 더없이 신중했고, 오로지 〈마구아〉가 얼마 전에 나섰던 사냥의 일들에만 관련되었다."], ["It would have been impossible for the most finished breeding to wear more of the appearance of considering the visit as a matter of course, than did his hosts, notwithstanding every individual present was perfectly aware that it must be connected with some secret object, and that probably of importance to themselves.", "자리에 있는 모든 이가 그 방문이 어떤 은밀한 목적과, 그것도 아마 자기네에게 중요한 목적과 결부되어 있음을 완벽히 알고 있었음에도, 이 손님 대접하는 이들이 그 방문을 당연한 일로 여기는 듯한 태도보다 더한 모습을 보이기란, 아무리 세련된 예법으로도 불가능했을 것이다."], ["When the appetites of the whole were appeased, the squaws removed the trenchers and gourd, and the two parties began to prepare themselves for a subtle trial of their wits.", "모두의 식욕이 채워지자, 여인들이 나무 접시와 호리병박을 치웠고, 두 무리는 저마다 기지(機智)의 미묘한 겨룸을 위해 채비를 갖추기 시작했다."]], [["\"Is the face of my great Canada father turned again towards his Huron children?\"", "\"내 위대한 〈캐나다〉 아버지의 얼굴이 다시 그의 〈휴런〉 자식들을 향했는가?\""], ["demanded the orator of the Delawares.", "〈델라웨어〉족의 변사가 물었다."]], [["\"When was it ever otherwise?\"", "\"언제 그렇지 않은 적이 있었는가?\""], ["returned Magua.", "〈마구아〉가 대꾸했다."], ["\"He calls my people 'most beloved.'\"", "\"그분은 내 백성을 '가장 사랑하는 이들'이라 부르신다.\""]], [["The Delaware gravely bowed his acquiescence to what he knew to be false, and continued,--", "〈델라웨어〉인은 그것이 거짓임을 알면서도 진중하게 고개를 숙여 수긍하고는 말을 이었다."]], [["\"The tomahawks of your young men have been very red.\"", "\"그대 젊은이들의 도끼가 무척 붉었더군.\""]], [["\"It is so; but they are now bright and dull; for the Yengeese are dead, and the Delawares are our neighbors.\"", "\"그렇다. 그러나 지금은 빛나면서도 무뎌졌으니, 〈양키〉들은 죽었고 〈델라웨어〉족은 우리의 이웃이기 때문이다.\""]], [["The other acknowledged the pacific compliment by a gesture of the hand, and remained silent.", "상대는 그 평화로운 인사치레를 손짓으로 받아들이고는 말이 없었다."], ["Then Magua, as if recalled to such a recollection, by the allusion to the massacre, demanded,--", "그러자 〈마구아〉는, 그 학살에 대한 언급으로 그런 기억이 떠오른 듯, 물었다."]], [["\"Does my prisoner give trouble to my brothers?\"", "\"내 포로가 형제들에게 폐를 끼치는가?\""]], [["\"She is welcome.\"", "\"그 여인은 환영받고 있다.\""]], [["\"The path between the Hurons and the Delawares is short, and it is open; let her be sent to my squaws, if she gives trouble to my brother.\"", "\"〈휴런〉족과 〈델라웨어〉족 사이의 길은 짧고, 또 열려 있다. 그 여인이 내 형제에게 폐를 끼친다면 내 여인들에게 보내도록 하라.\""]], [["\"She is welcome,\"", "\"그 여인은 환영받고 있다.\""], ["returned the chief of the latter nation, still more emphatically.", "후자 부족의 추장이 한층 더 힘주어 대답했다."]], [["The baffled Magua continued silent several minutes, apparently indifferent, however, to the repulse he had received in this his open effort to gain possession of Cora.", "좌절한 〈마구아〉는 몇 분 동안 말이 없었으나, 〈코라〉를 손에 넣으려던 이 공공연한 노력에서 당한 거절에는 도무지 무심해 보였다."]], [["\"Do my young men leave the Delawares room on the mountains for their hunts?\"", "\"내 젊은이들이 사냥하라고 산에 〈델라웨어〉족의 자리를 남겨 두는가?\""], ["he at length continued.", "그가 마침내 말을 이었다."]], [["\"The Lenape are rulers of their own hills,\"", "\"〈레나페〉는 제 언덕의 주인이다.\""], ["returned the other, a little haughtily.", "상대가 다소 거만하게 대답했다."]], [["\"It is well.", "\"좋다."], ["Justice is the master of a redskin!", "정의야말로 붉은 피부를 가진 자의 주인이다!"], ["Why should they brighten their tomahawks, and sharpen their knives against each other?", "어찌하여 그들이 서로를 향해 도끼를 번뜩이고 칼을 갈아야 하겠는가?"], ["Are not the pale-faces thicker than the swallows in the season of flowers?\"", "창백한 얼굴들이 꽃피는 철의 제비 떼보다 빽빽하지 아니한가?\""]], [["\"Good!\"", "\"좋다!\""], ["exclaimed two or three of his auditors at the same time.", "그 말을 듣던 두셋이 동시에 외쳤다."]], [["Magua waited a little, to permit his words to soften the feelings of the Delawares, before he added,--", "〈마구아〉는, 제 말이 〈델라웨어〉족의 마음을 누그러뜨리도록 잠시 기다린 뒤에 덧붙였다."]], [["\"Have there not been strange moccasins in the woods?", "\"숲에 낯선 모카신이 있지 아니했는가?"], ["Have not my brothers scented the feet of white men?\"", "내 형제들이 백인의 발 냄새를 맡지 아니했는가?\""]], [["\"Let my Canada father come,\"", "\"내 〈캐나다〉 아버지가 오시게 하라.\""], ["returned the other evasively; \"his children are ready to see him.\"", "상대가 얼버무리며 대답했다. \"그의 자식들은 그분을 뵐 준비가 되어 있다.\""]], [["\"When the great chief comes, it is to smoke with the Indians in their wigwams.", "\"위대한 추장이 오시는 것은 인디언과 함께 그들의 천막에서 담배를 피우기 위함이다."], ["The Hurons say, too, he is welcome.", "〈휴런〉족 또한 그분을 환영한다고 말한다."], ["But the Yengeese have long arms, and legs that never tire!", "그러나 〈양키〉들은 긴 팔과, 결코 지치지 않는 다리를 지녔다!"], ["My young men dreamed they had seen the trail of the Yengeese nigh the village of the Delawares?\"", "내 젊은이들은 〈델라웨어〉족의 마을 가까이에서 〈양키〉들의 자취를 보았다고 꿈을 꾸었다더군?\""]], [["\"They will not find the Lenape asleep.\"", "\"그들은 〈레나페〉가 잠들어 있는 것을 보지 못할 것이다.\""]], [["\"It is well.", "\"좋다."], ["The warrior whose eye is open can see his enemy,\"", "눈이 열려 있는 전사는 제 적을 볼 수 있다.\""], ["said Magua, once more shifting his ground, when he found himself unable to penetrate the caution of his companion.", "〈마구아〉가, 동료의 신중함을 꿰뚫지 못함을 깨닫고 다시 한번 방향을 바꾸며 말했다."], ["\"I have brought gifts to my brother.", "\"나는 형제에게 줄 선물을 가져왔다."], ["His nation would not go on the war-path because they did not think it well; but their friends have remembered where they lived.\"", "그의 부족은 옳지 않다고 여겨 전쟁의 길에 나서지 않았으나, 그들의 벗들은 그들이 사는 곳을 잊지 않았다.\""]], [["When he had thus announced his liberal intention, the crafty chief arose, and gravely spread his presents before the dazzled eyes of his hosts.", "이렇게 제 너그러운 뜻을 밝히고 나서, 교활한 추장은 일어서서, 손님들의 눈이 휘둥그레지도록 진중하게 제 선물들을 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["They consisted principally of trinkets of little value, plundered from the slaughtered females of William Henry.", "그것들은 대개 별 가치 없는 자질구레한 장신구로, 〈윌리엄 헨리〉에서 살해된 여인들에게서 약탈한 것이었다."], ["In the division of the baubles the cunning Huron discovered no less art than in their selection.", "그 장신구들을 나누어 줌에 있어, 그 교활한 〈휴런〉인은 그것들을 고른 솜씨에 못지않은 재간을 보였다."], ["While he bestowed those of greater value on the two most distinguished warriors, one of whom was his host, he seasoned his offerings to their inferiors with such well-timed and apposite compliments, as left them no grounds of complaint.", "더 값나가는 것들을 가장 뛰어난 두 전사에게 — 그중 하나는 주인이었다 — 베푸는 한편, 그보다 못한 자들에게 바치는 것들에는 더없이 시의적절하고 알맞은 찬사를 곁들여, 그들에게 불평할 거리를 조금도 남기지 않았다."], ["In short, the whole ceremony contained such a happy blending of the profitable with the flattering, that it was not difficult for the donor immediately to read the effect of a generosity so aptly mingled with praise, in the eyes of those he addressed.", "요컨대, 그 의식 전체에는 실리와 아첨이 어찌나 절묘하게 어우러져 있었던지, 베푸는 자가 칭찬과 그토록 알맞게 뒤섞인 너그러움의 효과를, 상대하는 이들의 눈에서 곧바로 읽어 내기란 어렵지 않았다."]], [["This well-judged and politic stroke on the part of Magua was not without instantaneous results.", "〈마구아〉 쪽의 이 잘 판단된 정략적 일격은 즉각적인 결과를 낳지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The Delawares lost their gravity in a much more cordial expression; and the host, in particular, after contemplating his own liberal share of the spoil for some moments with peculiar gratification, repeated with strong emphasis, the words,--", "〈델라웨어〉족은 진중함을 잃고 한결 다정한 표정을 지었으며, 특히 주인은 자기 몫으로 떨어진 넉넉한 전리품을 잠시 각별한 흐뭇함으로 바라본 뒤, 힘주어 그 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["\"My brother is a wise chief.", "\"내 형제는 지혜로운 추장이다."], ["He is welcome!\"", "그를 환영한다!\""]], [["\"The Hurons love their friends the Delawares,\"", "\"〈휴런〉족은 그들의 벗 〈델라웨어〉족을 사랑한다.\""], ["returned Magua.", "〈마구아〉가 대답했다."], ["\"Why should they not?", "\"어찌 그러지 않겠는가?"], ["they are colored by the same sun, and their just men will hunt in the same grounds after death.", "그들은 같은 태양에 물들었고, 그들의 의로운 이들은 죽은 뒤에도 같은 사냥터에서 사냥할 것이다."], ["The redskins should be friends, and look with open eyes on the white men.", "붉은 피부를 가진 이들은 마땅히 벗이 되어, 눈을 뜨고 백인들을 바라보아야 한다."], ["Has not my brother scented spies in the woods?\"", "내 형제는 숲에서 첩자의 냄새를 맡지 아니했는가?\""]], [["The Delaware, whose name in English signified \"Hard Heart,\" an appellation that the French had translated into \"Le Coeur-dur,\"", "영어로 그 이름이 \"굳은 마음\"을 뜻하고, 프랑스인들이 \"르 쾨르 뒤르\"로 옮긴 그 〈델라웨어〉인은, 아마 그토록 의미심장한 칭호를 그에게 안겨 주었을 그 굳센 의지를 잠시 잊었다."], ["forgot the obduracy of purpose, which had probably obtained him so significant a title. His countenance grew very sensibly less stern, and now deigned to answer more directly.", "그의 얼굴은 눈에 띄게 덜 준엄해졌고, 이제는 한결 직접적으로 대답하기를 마다하지 않았다."]], [["\"There have been strange moccasins about my camp.", "\"내 진영 주위에 낯선 모카신들이 있었다."], ["They have been tracked into my lodges.\"", "그 발자국이 내 오두막들 안까지 났더라.\""]], [["\"Did my brother beat out the dogs?\"", "\"내 형제는 그 개들을 쫓아내었는가?\""], ["asked Magua, without adverting in any manner to the former equivocation of the chief.", "〈마구아〉가, 추장이 앞서 한 얼버무림에는 조금도 개의치 않고 물었다."]], [["\"It would not do.", "\"그럴 수는 없었다."], ["The stranger is always welcome to the children of the Lenape.\"", "낯선 이는 언제나 〈레나페〉의 자식들에게 환영받는다.\""]], [["\"The stranger, but not the spy.\"", "\"낯선 이는 그렇겠으나, 첩자는 아닐 터.\""]], [["\"Would the Yengeese send their women as spies?", "\"〈양키〉들이 제 여인들을 첩자로 보내겠는가?"], ["Did not the Huron chief say he took women in the battle?\"", "〈휴런〉의 추장은 그 전투에서 여인들을 사로잡았다고 말하지 않았는가?\""]], [["\"He told no lie.", "\"그는 거짓을 말하지 않았다."], ["The Yengeese have sent out their scouts.", "〈양키〉들은 척후를 내보냈다."], ["They have been in my wigwams, but they found there no one to say welcome.", "그들이 내 천막에 들어왔으나, 그곳에서 환영한다 말해 줄 이를 찾지 못했다."], ["Then they fled to the Delawares--for, say they, the Delawares are our friends; their minds are turned from their Canada father!\"", "그러자 그들은 〈델라웨어〉족에게로 달아났으니, 그들이 이르기를, 〈델라웨어〉족은 우리의 벗이요, 그들의 마음은 〈캐나다〉 아버지에게서 돌아섰다고 하기 때문이다!\""]], [["This insinuation was a home thrust, and one that in a more advanced state of society, would have entitled Magua to the reputation of a skilful diplomatist.", "이 빈정거림은 정곡을 찌르는 것이었으니, 더 발달한 사회였더라면 〈마구아〉에게 능란한 외교관이라는 명성을 안겨 주었을 만한 것이었다."], ["The recent defection of the tribe had, as they well knew themselves, subjected the Delawares to much reproach among their French allies; and they were now made to feel that their future actions were to be regarded with jealousy and distrust.", "부족의 최근 이탈은, 그들 스스로도 잘 알고 있듯이 프랑스 동맹들 사이에서 〈델라웨어〉족에게 적잖은 비난을 안겨 주었고, 이제 그들은 자기네 앞으로의 행동이 시샘과 불신의 눈으로 지켜보아지리라는 것을 느끼게 되었다."], ["There was no deep insight into causes and effects necessary to foresee that such a situation of things was likely to prove highly prejudicial to their future movements.", "그러한 사정이 그들의 앞으로의 움직임에 매우 해로우리라는 것을 내다보는 데에는, 원인과 결과를 깊이 통찰할 필요조차 없었다."], ["Their distant villages, their hunting-grounds, and hundreds of their women and children, together with a material part of their physical force, were actually within the limits of the French territory.", "그들의 먼 마을들, 그들의 사냥터, 그리고 수백에 이르는 그들의 여인과 아이들은, 그들 물리적 병력의 상당 부분과 더불어, 실제로 프랑스 영토의 경계 안에 들어 있었다."], ["Accordingly, this alarming annunciation was received, as Magua intended, with manifest disapprobation, if not with alarm.", "따라서 이 불안을 자아내는 통보는, 〈마구아〉가 의도한 대로, 경악까지는 아니더라도 명백한 못마땅함으로 받아들여졌다."]], [["\"Let my father look in my face,\"", "\"내 아버지가 내 얼굴을 들여다보게 하라.\""], ["said Le Coeur-dur; \"he will see no change.", "〈르 쾨르 뒤르〉가 말했다. \"그는 아무 변함도 보지 못할 것이다."], ["It is true, my young men did not go out on the war-path; they had dreams for not doing so.", "내 젊은이들이 전쟁의 길에 나서지 않은 것은 사실이다. 그들에겐 그러지 않을 꿈이 있었다."], ["But they love and venerate the great white chief.\"", "그러나 그들은 위대한 백인 추장을 사랑하고 우러른다.\""]], [["\"Will he think so when he hears that his greatest enemy is fed in the camp of his children?", "\"그분이, 자신의 가장 큰 적이 자식들의 진영에서 대접받고 있다는 소식을 들으면 그리 생각하시겠는가?"], ["When he is told a bloody Yengee smokes at your fire?", "피에 굶주린 〈양키〉가 그대들의 화롯가에서 담배를 피운다는 말을 들으면?"], ["That the pale-face who has slain so many of his friends goes in and out among the Delawares?", "그분의 벗을 그토록 많이 죽인 그 창백한 얼굴이 〈델라웨어〉족 사이를 드나든다는 말을 들으면? 가라!"], ["Go! my great Canada father is not a fool!\"", "내 위대한 〈캐나다〉 아버지는 어리석은 분이 아니다!\""]], [["\"Where is the Yengee that the Delawares fear?\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉족이 두려워한다는 그 〈양키〉가 어디에 있는가?\""], ["returned the other; \"who has slain my young men?", "상대가 대꾸했다. \"누가 내 젊은이들을 죽였는가?"], ["who is the mortal enemy of my Great Father!\"", "누가 내 위대한 아버지의 철천지원수란 말인가!\""]], [["\"La Longue Carabine.\"", "\"〈라 롱그 카라빈〉이다.\""]], [["The Delaware warriors started at the well-known name, betraying, by their amazement, that they now learnt, for the first time, one so famous among the Indian allies of France was within their power.", "〈델라웨어〉의 전사들은 그 잘 알려진 이름에 깜짝 놀랐으니, 그 경악으로 보아, 프랑스의 인디언 동맹들 사이에서 그토록 이름난 자가 자기네 수중에 들어와 있다는 것을 이제야 처음으로 알게 되었음이 드러났다."]], [["\"What does my brother mean?\"", "\"내 형제는 무슨 말을 하는가?\""], ["demanded Le Coeur-dur, in a tone that, by its wonder, far exceeded the usual apathy of his race.", "〈르 쾨르 뒤르〉가, 그 놀라움이 자기 종족의 여느 무심함을 한참이나 뛰어넘는 어조로 물었다."]], [["\"A Huron never lies!\"", "\"〈휴런〉은 결코 거짓을 말하지 않는다!\""], ["returned Magua coldly, leaning his head against the side of the lodge, and drawing his slight robe across his tawny breast.", "〈마구아〉가 차갑게 대꾸하며, 오두막 벽에 머리를 기대고 얇은 겉옷을 거무스름한 가슴 위로 끌어당겼다."], ["\"Let the Delawares count their prisoners; they will find one whose skin is neither red nor pale.\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉족이 제 포로들을 세어 보게 하라. 그러면 피부가 붉지도 창백하지도 않은 하나를 찾아낼 것이다.\""]], [["A long and musing pause succeeded.", "길고 사색에 잠긴 침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["The chief consulted apart with his companions, and messengers were despatched to collect certain others of the most distinguished men of the tribe.", "추장은 한쪽에서 동료들과 의논했고, 부족의 가장 뛰어난 다른 몇몇 사람을 불러 모으도록 전령들이 파견되었다."]], [["As warrior after warrior dropped in, they were each made acquainted, in turn, with the important intelligence that Magua had just communicated.", "전사가 하나둘 들어올 때마다, 그들은 차례로 〈마구아〉가 방금 전한 중대한 소식을 알게 되었다."], ["The air of surprise, and the usual low, deep, guttural exclamation, were common to them all.", "놀라는 기색과, 늘 내는 그 나직하고 깊은 목 안의 탄성이 그들 모두에게 공통되었다."], ["The news spread from mouth to mouth, until the whole encampment became powerfully agitated.", "소식은 입에서 입으로 퍼져, 마침내 야영지 전체가 거세게 동요했다."], ["The women suspended their labors, to catch such syllables as unguardedly fell from the lips of the consulting warriors.", "여인들은 의논하는 전사들의 입에서 무심코 새어 나오는 음절들을 들으려고 하던 일을 멈추었다."], ["The boys deserted their sports, and walking fearlessly among their fathers, looked up in curious admiration, as they heard the brief exclamations of wonder they so freely expressed at the temerity of their hated foe.", "사내아이들은 놀이를 내팽개치고, 두려움 없이 제 아비들 사이를 거닐며, 그토록 미워하는 적의 대담함에 그들이 거리낌 없이 내뱉는 그 짧은 경이의 탄성을 들으며, 궁금한 듯 우러러보았다."], ["In short, every occupation was abandoned for the time, and all other pursuits seemed discarded, in order that the tribe might freely indulge, after their own peculiar manner, in an open expression of feeling.", "요컨대, 모든 일이 잠시 내버려졌고, 다른 모든 추구가 한쪽으로 밀려난 듯했으니, 부족이 제 나름의 독특한 방식으로 감정의 공공연한 표출에 마음껏 빠져들 수 있도록 함이었다."]], [["When the excitement had a little abated, the old men disposed themselves seriously to consider that which it became the honor and safety of their tribe to perform, under circumstances of so much delicacy and embarrassment.", "흥분이 조금 가라앉자, 노인들은 이토록 미묘하고 곤혹스러운 상황에서 제 부족의 명예와 안전을 위해 마땅히 행해야 할 바를 진지하게 헤아리기 시작했다."], ["During all these movements, and in the midst of the general commotion, Magua had not only maintained his seat, but the very attitude he had originally taken, against the side of the lodge, where he continued as immovable, and, apparently, as unconcerned, as if he had no interest in the result.", "이 모든 움직임이 이는 동안, 그리고 그 전반의 소란 한가운데서, 〈마구아〉는 제 자리를 지켰을 뿐 아니라, 처음에 취했던 바로 그 자세, 곧 오두막 벽에 기댄 자세 그대로 머물러, 그 결과에 아무 이해관계도 없는 사람처럼 꿈쩍 않고, 또 겉보기에 무심하게 앉아 있었다."], ["Not a single indication of the future intentions of his hosts, however, escaped his vigilant eyes.", "그러나 손님 대접하는 이들의 앞으로의 의도를 알려 줄 단 하나의 기색도, 그의 빈틈없는 눈을 벗어나지 못했다."], ["With his consummate knowledge of the nature of the people with whom he had to deal, he anticipated every measure on which they decided; and it might almost be said, that, in many instances, he knew their intentions, even before they became known to themselves.", "자신이 상대해야 할 부족의 본성에 관한 더없이 완벽한 지식으로, 그는 그들이 결정하는 모든 조치를 미리 내다보았으니, 많은 경우 그들이 미처 제 의도를 깨닫기도 전에 그가 먼저 그것을 알고 있었다 해도 거의 틀린 말이 아니었다."]], [["The council of the Delawares was short.", "〈델라웨어〉족의 평의는 짧았다."], ["When it was ended, a general bustle announced that it was to be immediately succeeded by a solemn and formal assemblage of the nation.", "그것이 끝나자, 곧이어 부족 전체의 엄숙하고 격식 갖춘 회합이 뒤따르리라는 것을 두루 이는 부산함이 알렸다."], ["As such meetings were rare, and only called on occasions of the last importance, the subtle Huron, who still sat apart, a wily and dark observer of the proceedings, now knew that all his projects must be brought to their final issue.", "그러한 회합은 드물고, 오로지 더없이 중대한 일이 있을 때에만 소집되는 것이었기에, 여전히 한쪽에 떨어져 앉아 그 진행을 교활하고 음험한 눈으로 살피던 그 음흉한 〈휴런〉인은, 이제 제 모든 계략이 끝내 결판날 때가 왔음을 알았다."], ["He therefore left the lodge, and walked silently forth to the place in front of the encampment whither the warriors were already beginning to collect.", "그리하여 그는 오두막을 떠나, 전사들이 이미 모여들기 시작한 야영지 앞 공터로 말없이 걸어 나갔다."]], [["It might have been half an hour before each individual, including even the women and children, was in his place.", "여인과 아이들까지 포함하여 저마다 제자리를 잡기까지는 아마 반 시간쯤 걸렸을 것이다."], ["The delay had been created by the grave preparations that were deemed necessary to so solemn and unusual a conference.", "그 지체는, 이토록 엄숙하고 이례적인 회합에 필요하다 여겨진 진중한 채비에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["But when the sun was seen climbing above the tops of that mountain against whose bosom the Delawares had constructed their encampment, most were seated; and as his bright rays darted from behind the outline of trees that fringed the eminence, they fell upon as grave, as attentive, and as deeply interested a multitude, as was probably ever before lighted by his morning beams.", "그러나 해가, 〈델라웨어〉족이 그 품에 야영지를 세운 그 산의 봉우리 위로 솟아오르는 것이 보일 무렵에는 대다수가 자리에 앉았고, 그 밝은 햇살이 그 높은 곳을 두른 나무들의 윤곽 뒤에서 쏘아져 내릴 때, 그것은 아마 그 아침 햇살이 일찍이 비춘 적 없을 만큼 진중하고, 주의 깊으며, 깊이 마음 쓰는 무리 위로 내려앉았다."], ["Its number somewhat exceeded a thousand souls.", "그 수는 천 명의 영혼을 다소 웃돌았다."]], [["In a collection of such serious savages, there is never to be found any impatient aspirant after premature distinction, standing ready to move his auditors to some hasty, and, perhaps, injudicious discussion, in order that his own reputation may be the gainer.", "이토록 진지한 미개인들이 모인 자리에는, 제 명성을 얻으려 청중을 어떤 성급하고, 어쩌면 분별없는 논의로 몰아갈 채비를 한 채, 때 이른 두각을 좇는 조급한 야심가란 결코 찾아볼 수 없는 법이다."], ["An act of so much precipitancy and presumption would seal the downfall of precocious intellect forever.", "그토록 경솔하고 주제넘은 짓은 조숙한 지력의 몰락을 영원히 봉인하고 말 것이었다."], ["It rested solely with the oldest and most experienced of the men to lay the subject of the conference before the people.", "회합의 주제를 사람들 앞에 내놓는 일은 오로지 가장 나이 많고 경험 많은 이에게만 달려 있었다."], ["Until such a one chose to make some movement, no deeds in arms, no natural gifts, nor any renown as an orator, would have justified the slightest interruption.", "그러한 이가 어떤 움직임을 보이기로 마음먹기까지는, 어떤 무공도, 어떤 타고난 재주도, 변사로서의 어떤 명성도 조금의 끼어듦조차 정당화하지 못했을 것이다."], ["On the present occasion, the aged warrior whose privilege it was to speak, was silent, seemingly oppressed with the magnitude of his subject.", "이번 경우에, 말할 특권을 지닌 그 나이 든 전사는, 제 주제의 막중함에 짓눌린 듯 말이 없었다."], ["The delay had already continued long beyond the usual deliberative pause that always precedes a conference; but no sign of impatience or surprise escaped even the youngest boy.", "그 지체는 회합에 늘 앞서는 으레 있는 숙의의 멈춤을 이미 한참 넘어섰으나, 가장 어린 사내아이조차 조바심이나 놀라움의 기색을 내비치지 않았다."], ["Occasionally, an eye was raised from the earth, where the looks of most were riveted, and strayed towards a particular lodge, that was, however, in no manner distinguished from those around it, except in the peculiar care that had been taken to protect it against the assaults of the weather.", "이따금, 대다수의 시선이 못 박혀 있던 땅에서 눈 하나가 들려, 어떤 한 오두막을 향해 흘끗거렸으니, 그것은 풍우의 습격을 막기 위해 기울인 각별한 정성을 빼고는 주위의 것들과 조금도 다를 바 없는 오두막이었다."]], [["At length, one of those low murmurs that are so apt to disturb a multitude, was heard, and the whole nation arose to their feet by a common impulse.", "마침내, 군중을 으레 술렁이게 하는 그 나직한 웅성거림 가운데 하나가 들렸고, 부족 전체가 한 충동에 따라 발딱 일어섰다."], ["At that the door of the lodge in question opened, and three men, issuing from it, slowly approached the place of consultation.", "그러자 문제의 그 오두막 문이 열리고, 세 사람이 거기서 나와 천천히 회합 장소로 다가왔다."], ["They were all aged, even beyond that period to which the oldest present had reached; but one in the centre, who leaned on his companions for support, had numbered an amount of years to which the human race is seldom permitted to attain.", "그들은 모두, 자리에 있는 가장 나이 든 이가 이른 그 나이마저 넘어선 노인들이었으나, 동료들에게 몸을 기댄 한가운데의 한 사람은 인간이 좀처럼 다다르도록 허락받지 못하는 햇수를 헤아렸다."], ["His frame, which had once been tall and erect, like the cedar, was now bending under the pressure of more than a century.", "한때 삼나무처럼 크고 꼿꼿했던 그의 골격은, 이제 한 세기가 넘는 세월의 짓누름 아래 굽어 있었다."], ["The elastic, light step of an Indian was gone, and in its place he was compelled to toil his tardy way over the ground, inch by inch.", "인디언의 그 탄력 있고 가벼운 걸음은 사라졌고, 그 대신 그는 한 치 한 치 더딘 걸음으로 땅을 힘겹게 옮겨야 했다."], ["His dark, wrinkled countenance was in singular and wild contrast with the long white locks which floated on his shoulders in such thickness as to announce that generations had probably passed away since they had last been shorn.", "그의 어둡고 주름진 얼굴은, 어찌나 두텁게 어깨 위로 흘러내렸던지 그것을 마지막으로 자른 이래 아마 여러 세대가 지났음을 알리는 그 길고 흰 머리채와 기이하고 거친 대조를 이루었다."]], [["The dress of this patriarch--for such, considering his vast age, in conjunction with his affinity and influence with his people, he might very properly be termed--was rich and imposing, though strictly after the simple fashions of the tribe.", "이 가부장 — 그 까마득한 나이에 더해 제 백성과 맺은 친연과 영향력을 헤아릴 때, 그를 그렇게 부름은 더없이 마땅하리라 — 의 옷차림은 부족의 소박한 양식을 엄격히 따르면서도 풍성하고 위풍당당했다."], ["His robe was of the finest skins, which had been deprived of their fur, in order to admit of a hieroglyphical representation of various deeds in arms, done in former ages.", "그의 겉옷은 더없이 고운 가죽으로 지어졌는데, 지난 시대에 이룬 갖가지 무공을 상형으로 그려 넣을 수 있도록 그 털을 벗겨 낸 것이었다."], ["His bosom was loaded with medals, some in massive silver, and one or two even in gold, the gifts of various Christian potentates during the long period of his life.", "그의 가슴에는 메달이 잔뜩 달려 있었으니, 더러는 묵직한 은으로, 한둘은 금으로까지 된 것으로, 그의 긴 생애 동안 여러 기독교 군주가 보낸 선물이었다."], ["He also wore armlets, and cinctures above the ankles, of the latter precious metal.", "그는 또한 팔찌와, 발목 위로 두른 띠를 후자의 그 귀한 금속으로 두르고 있었다."], ["His head, on the whole of which the hair had been permitted to grow, the pursuits of war having so long been abandoned, was encircled by a sort of plated diadem, which, in its turn, bore lesser and more glittering ornaments, that sparkled amid the glossy hues of three drooping ostrich feathers, dyed a deep black, in touching contrast to the color of his snow-white locks.", "그의 머리는 — 전쟁의 일을 손에서 놓은 지 오래여서 그 위에 머리가 온통 자라도록 내버려 둔 것이었는데 — 도금한 일종의 머리띠로 둘려 있었고, 그 머리띠는 다시 더 작고 더 반짝이는 장식들을 달고 있었으니, 깊은 검정으로 물들인 채 늘어진 타조 깃털 세 개의 윤기 도는 빛깔 사이에서 반짝였으며, 그것은 그의 눈처럼 흰 머리채와 가슴 저린 대조를 이루었다."], ["His tomahawk was nearly hid in silver, and the handle of his knife shone like a horn of solid gold.", "그의 손도끼는 거의 은에 파묻혀 있었고, 칼자루는 단단한 금으로 된 뿔처럼 빛났다."]], [["So soon as the first hum of emotion and pleasure, which the sudden appearance of this venerated individual created, had a little subsided, the name of \"Tamenund\"", "이 우러름받는 인물의 느닷없는 등장이 자아낸 첫 감격과 기쁨의 웅성거림이 조금 가라앉기가 무섭게, \"〈타메눈드〉\"라는 이름이 입에서 입으로 속삭여졌다."], ["was whispered from mouth to mouth. Magua had often heard the fame of this wise and just Delaware; a reputation that even proceeded so far as to bestow on him the rare gift of holding secret communion with the Great Spirit, and which has since transmitted his name, with some slight alteration, to the white usurpers of his ancient territory, as the imaginary tutelar saint of a vast empire.", "〈마구아〉는 이 지혜롭고 의로운 〈델라웨어〉인의 명성을 자주 들어 왔으니, 그 명성은 〈위대한 영(靈)〉과 은밀히 교통하는 드문 능력을 그에게 부여하는 데까지 이를 정도였으며, 훗날 그의 옛 영토를 빼앗은 백인 찬탈자들에게 약간의 변형을 거쳐 그 이름을 전하여, 광막한 한 제국의 가상의 수호성인으로 삼게 한 것이었다."], ["The Huron chief, therefore, stepped eagerly out a little from the throng, to a spot whence he might catch a nearer glimpse of the features of the man, whose decision was likely to produce so deep an influence on his own fortunes.", "그리하여 〈휴런〉의 추장은 무리에서 조금 앞으로 다급히 나아가, 그 결정이 자신의 운명에 그토록 깊은 영향을 미칠 듯한 그 사람의 이목구비를 한층 가까이서 흘끗 볼 수 있는 자리로 갔다."]], [["The eyes of the old man were closed, as though the organs were wearied with having so long witnessed the selfish workings of the human passions.", "노인의 두 눈은, 인간의 격정이 빚어내는 그 이기적인 놀음을 너무도 오래 지켜본 탓에 그 기관이 지쳐 버린 듯 감겨 있었다."], ["The color of his skin differed from that of most around him, being richer and darker, the latter hue having been produced by certain delicate and mazy lines of complicated and yet beautiful figures, which had been traced over most of his person by the operation of tattooing.", "그 피부의 빛깔은 주위 대다수와 달라, 더 짙고 더 어두웠으니, 그 어두운 빛은 복잡하면서도 아름다운 무늬의 어떤 섬세하고 미로 같은 선들이 문신의 수법으로 그의 몸 거의 전체에 새겨진 데서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["Notwithstanding the position of the Huron, he passed the observant and silent Magua without notice, and leaning on his two venerable supporters proceeded to the high place of the multitude, where he seated himself in the centre of his nation, with the dignity of a monarch and the air of a father.", "〈휴런〉인이 자리한 위치에도 불구하고, 그는 살피며 침묵하는 〈마구아〉를 거들떠보지 않고 지나쳐, 그 두 명의 존엄한 부축꾼에게 기댄 채 군중의 높은 자리로 나아갔으니, 그곳에서 그는 군왕의 위엄과 아비의 풍모로 제 부족의 한가운데에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Nothing could surpass the reverence and affection with which this unexpected visit from one who belonged rather to another world than to this, was received by his people.", "이 세상보다는 오히려 다른 세상에 속한 이의 이 뜻밖의 방문이 그의 백성에게 받아들여진 그 외경과 애정은 무엇으로도 넘어설 수 없는 것이었다."], ["After a suitable and decent pause, the principal chiefs arose; and approaching the patriarch, they placed his hands reverently on their heads, seeming to entreat a blessing.", "알맞고 점잖은 멈춤이 지난 뒤, 주요 추장들이 일어섰고, 그 가부장에게 다가가 그의 두 손을 공손히 제 머리 위에 얹으며 축복을 비는 듯했다."], ["The younger men were content with touching his robe, or even drawing nigh his person, in order to breathe in the atmosphere of one so aged, so just, and so valiant.", "더 젊은 이들은 그의 겉옷을 만지거나, 그토록 나이 많고, 그토록 의롭고, 그토록 용맹한 이의 기운을 들이마시려 그저 그의 곁에 다가서는 것으로 만족했다."], ["None but the most distinguished among the youthful warriors even presumed so far as to perform the latter ceremony; the great mass of the multitude deeming it a sufficient happiness to look upon a form so deeply venerated, and so well beloved.", "젊은 전사들 가운데서도 가장 뛰어난 이들만이 감히 후자의 의식을 행하려 들었으니, 군중의 대다수는 그토록 깊이 우러르고 그토록 사랑하는 그 형상을 바라보는 것만으로도 족한 행복이라 여겼다."], ["When these acts of affection and respect were performed, the chiefs drew back again to their several places, and silence reigned in the whole encampment.", "이 애정과 존경의 행위가 다 이루어지자, 추장들은 다시 저마다의 자리로 물러났고, 야영지 전체에 정적이 깃들었다."]], [["After a short delay, a few of the young men, to whom instructions had been whispered by one of the aged attendants of Tamenund, arose, left the crowd, and entered the lodge which has already been noted as the object of so much attention throughout that morning.", "잠시 지체가 지난 뒤, 〈타메눈드〉의 나이 든 시종 가운데 하나에게서 지시를 귓속말로 받은 젊은이 몇이 일어나 무리를 떠나, 그 아침 내내 그토록 큰 주목의 대상이었다고 이미 적은 그 오두막으로 들어갔다."], ["In a few minutes they reappeared, escorting the individuals who had caused all these solemn preparations towards the seat of judgment.", "몇 분 뒤 그들은 다시 나타나, 이 모든 엄숙한 채비를 불러일으킨 그 인물들을 심판의 자리로 호송했다."], ["The crowd opened in a lane; and when the party had re-entered, it closed in again, forming a large and dense belt of human bodies, arranged in an open circle.", "군중이 갈라져 길을 내었고, 그 무리가 다시 들어서자 군중은 다시 메워져, 트인 원형으로 늘어선 인간의 몸으로 크고 빽빽한 띠를 이루었다."]], [["\"The assembly seated, rising o'er the rest, Achilles thus the king of men addressed.\"", "\"회의가 자리를 잡자, 모두를 앞질러 일어선\n〈아킬레우스〉가 인간들의 왕에게 이렇게 고했노라.\""]], [["POPE'S _Iliad._", "포프 역 「일리아드」"]], [["Cora stood foremost among the prisoners, entwining her arms in those of Alice, in the tenderness of sisterly love.", "〈코라〉는 포로들 가운데서 맨 앞에 서서, 누이를 향한 정겨운 애정으로 두 팔을 〈앨리스〉의 팔에 엮고 있었다."], ["Notwithstanding the fearful and menacing array of savages on every side of her, no apprehension on her own account could prevent the noble-minded maiden from keeping her eyes fastened on the pale and anxious features of the trembling Alice.", "사방에 둘러선 미개인들의 무시무시하고 위협적인 진용에도 불구하고, 제 신변에 대한 어떤 두려움도 이 고결한 처녀로 하여금 떨고 있는 〈앨리스〉의 창백하고 근심 어린 얼굴에서 눈을 떼게 하지 못했다."], ["Close at their side stood Heyward, with an interest in both, that, at such a moment of intense uncertainty, scarcely knew a preponderance in favor of her whom he most loved.", "그들의 바로 곁에는 〈헤이워드〉가 서서 두 사람 모두에게 마음을 쏟고 있었으니, 이토록 극도로 불확실한 순간에는, 그가 가장 사랑하는 이를 향한 쪽으로조차 좀처럼 무게가 기울지 않았다."], ["Hawkeye had placed himself a little in the rear, with a deference to the superior rank of his companions, that no similarity in the state of their present fortunes could induce him to forget.", "〈호크아이〉는 동료들의 우월한 신분을 존중하여 조금 뒤편에 자리를 잡았으니, 지금 그들의 운명이 처한 처지가 다 같다 한들 그 존중의 마음을 잊게 할 수는 없었다."], ["Uncas was not there.", "〈언커스〉는 그 자리에 없었다."]], [["When perfect silence was again restored, and after the usual long, impressive pause, one of the two aged chiefs who sat at the side of the patriarch arose, and demanded aloud, in very intelligible English,--", "다시 완전한 정적이 깃들고, 으레 그러하듯 길고 인상 깊은 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 족장 곁에 앉아 있던 두 늙은 추장 가운데 하나가 일어나, 무척 또렷한 영어로 큰 소리로 물었다."]], [["\"Which of my prisoners is La Longue Carabine?\"", "\"내 포로들 가운데 누가 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉인가?\""]], [["Neither Duncan nor the scout answered.", "〈던컨〉도 척후병도 대답하지 않았다."], ["The former, however, glanced his eyes around the dark and silent assembly, and recoiled a pace, when they fell on the malignant visage of Magua.", "그러나 전자는 어둡고 고요한 회중을 둘러보다가, 그 시선이 〈마구아〉의 악의에 찬 얼굴에 가 닿자 한 걸음 물러섰다."], ["He saw, at once, that this wily savage had some secret agency in their present arraignment before the nation, and determined to throw every possible impediment in the way of the execution of his sinister plans.", "그는 이 교활한 미개인이 그들을 부족 앞에 끌어내 심문하게 한 데에 어떤 은밀한 책동을 부렸음을 단번에 알아차렸고, 그 음험한 계획의 실행을 가로막을 온갖 장애를 던져 놓기로 마음먹었다."], ["He had witnessed one instance of the summary punishments of the Indians, and now dreaded that his companion was to be selected for a second.", "그는 인디언들의 즉결 처벌을 한 차례 목격한 바 있었기에, 이제 자기 동료가 두 번째 본보기로 뽑히지나 않을까 두려워했다."], ["In this dilemma, with little or no time for reflection, he suddenly determined to cloak his invaluable friend, at any or every hazard to himself.", "이 진퇴양난 속에서, 생각할 겨를이라곤 거의 또는 전혀 없이, 그는 어떤 위험이든 제 한 몸에 걸고서라도 그 더없이 소중한 벗을 감싸 두기로 별안간 작정했다."], ["Before he had time, however, to speak, the question was repeated in a louder voice, and with a clearer utterance.", "그러나 그가 미처 입을 떼기도 전에, 한층 큰 목소리로, 더 또렷한 발음으로 물음이 되풀이되었다."]], [["\"Give us arms,\"", "\"우리에게 무기를 주시오.\""], ["the young man haughtily replied, \"and place us in yonder woods.", "젊은이가 오만하게 대답했다. \"그리고 저 숲에 우리를 두시오."], ["Our deeds shall speak for us!\"", "우리의 행적이 우리를 대신해 말할 것이오!\""]], [["\"This is the warrior whose name has filled our ears!\"", "\"이 사람이 그 이름으로 우리 귀를 채운 전사로구나!\""], ["returned the chief, regarding Heyward with that sort of curious interest which seems inseparable from man, when first beholding one of his fellows to whom merit or accident, virtue or crime, has given notoriety.", "추장이 이렇게 대꾸하며, 공적이든 우연이든, 미덕이든 죄악이든 그것으로 악명을 얻은 동족 가운데 하나를 처음 마주할 때 사람에게서 좀처럼 떼어 놓을 수 없을 법한 그런 호기심 어린 관심으로 〈헤이워드〉를 살폈다."], ["\"What has brought the white man into the camp of the Delawares?\"", "\"무엇이 백인을 〈델라웨어〉족의 진영으로 데려왔는가?\""]], [["\"My necessities.", "\"내 곤궁함이오."], ["I come for food, shelter and friends.\"", "나는 먹을 것과 거처와 벗을 구하러 왔소.\""]], [["\"It cannot be.", "\"그럴 리 없다."], ["The woods are full of game.", "숲에는 사냥감이 가득하다."], ["The head of a warrior needs no other shelter than a sky without clouds; and the Delawares are the enemies, and not the friends, of the Yengeese.", "전사의 머리에는 구름 한 점 없는 하늘 말고 다른 거처가 필요치 않다. 그리고 〈델라웨어〉족은 〈잉기즈〉의 적이지, 벗이 아니다."], ["Go!", "가라!"], ["the mouth has spoken, while the heart said nothing.\"", "입은 말하였으되, 마음은 아무것도 말하지 않았다.\""]], [["Duncan, a little at a loss in what manner to proceed, remained silent; but the scout, who had listened attentively to all that passed, now advanced steadily to the front.", "〈던컨〉은 어찌 처신해야 할지 다소 막막하여 잠자코 있었다. 그러나 오가는 말을 모조리 주의 깊게 듣고 있던 척후병이 이제 꿋꿋이 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["\"That I did not answer to the call for La Longue Carabine, was not owing either to shame or fear,\"", "\"내가 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉을 부르는 소리에 답하지 않은 것은, 수치 때문도 두려움 때문도 아니오.\""], ["he said; \"for neither one nor the other is the gift of an honest man.", "그가 말했다. \"그 어느 것도 정직한 사람의 천품은 아니니까."], ["But I do not admit the right of the Mingos to bestow a name on one whose friends have been mindful of his gifts, in this particular; especially as their title is a lie, 'Killdeer' being a grooved barrel and no carabyne.", "허나 나는, 내 벗들이 바로 이 점에서 내 천품을 헤아려 준 사람에게 이름을 붙일 권리가 〈밍고〉족에게 있다고는 인정하지 않소."], ["I am the man, however, that got the name of Nathaniel from my kin; the compliment of Hawkeye from the Delawares, who live on their own river; and whom the Iroquois have presumed to style the 'Long Rifle,'", "더구나 그들이 붙인 이름은 거짓이니, 〈킬디어〉는 강선을 판 총신이지 카라빈이 아니거든. 그렇더라도 나는, 일가붙이에게서 〈너새니얼〉이라는 이름을, 제 강가에 사는 〈델라웨어〉족에게서 〈호크아이〉라는 영예로운 이름을 얻은 그 사람이오."], ["without any warranty from him who is most concerned in the matter.\"", "그리고 이 일에 가장 깊이 관련된 자의 허락도 없이, 〈이로쿼이〉족이 주제넘게 '롱 라이플'이라 일컬어 온 바로 그 사람이오.\""]], [["The eyes of all present, which had hitherto been gravely scanning the person of Duncan, were now turned, on the instant, towards the upright iron frame of this new pretender to the distinguished appellation.", "이제껏 〈던컨〉의 풍모를 진지하게 살피던 모든 이의 눈이, 그 순간 그 빼어난 이름을 새로이 자처하고 나선 이 사내의 꼿꼿한 무쇠 같은 골격을 향했다."], ["It was in no degree remarkable that there should be found two who were willing to claim so great an honor, for impostors, though rare, were not unknown amongst the natives; but it was altogether material to the just and severe intentions of the Delawares, that there should be no mistake in the matter.", "그토록 큰 영예를 차지하려는 자가 둘이나 나타난 것은 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 사칭하는 자란 드물기는 해도 원주민들 사이에 없지는 않았던 것이다. 그러나 이 일에 추호의 착오도 없어야 한다는 것은, 공정하고 준엄한 의도를 품은 〈델라웨어〉족에게 무엇보다 긴요한 일이었다."], ["Some of their old men consulted together in private, and then, as it would seem, they determined to interrogate their visitor on the subject.", "그들 가운데 몇몇 노인이 사사로이 의논을 나누더니, 이윽고 보아하니, 그 문제를 두고 손님에게 캐묻기로 작정한 듯했다."]], [["\"My brother has said that a snake crept into my camp,\"", "\"내 형제는 한 마리 뱀이 내 진영으로 기어들었다고 말했다.\""], ["said the chief to Magua; \"which is he?\"", "추장이 〈마구아〉에게 말했다. \"어느 자인가?\""]], [["The Huron pointed to the scout.", "〈휴런〉족 사내가 척후병을 가리켰다."]], [["\"Will a wise Delaware believe the barking of a wolf?\"", "\"지혜로운 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 늑대의 짖는 소리를 믿으려오?\""], ["exclaimed Duncan, still more confirmed in the evil intentions of his ancient enemy: \"a dog never lies, but when was a wolf known to speak the truth?\"", "〈던컨〉이 오랜 원수의 사악한 의도를 한층 굳게 확신하며 외쳤다. \"개는 결코 거짓을 말하지 않으나, 늑대가 참을 말한다는 소리를 언제 들어 보았소?\""]], [["The eyes of Magua flashed fire; but, suddenly recollecting the necessity of maintaining his presence of mind, he turned away in silent disdain, well assured that the sagacity of the Indians would not fail to extract the real merits of the point in controversy.", "〈마구아〉의 눈에서 불꽃이 번뜩였다. 그러나 침착함을 유지해야 한다는 것을 별안간 떠올리고, 그는 인디언들의 명민함이 이 다툼의 핵심에서 참된 진상을 반드시 가려내리라 굳게 믿으며, 잠자코 경멸의 빛을 띤 채 돌아섰다."], ["He was not deceived; for, after another short consultation, the wary Delaware turned to him again, and expressed the determination of the chiefs, though in the most considerate language.", "그의 믿음은 어긋나지 않았다. 또 한 차례 짧은 의논을 거친 뒤, 신중한 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 그에게 다시 돌아서, 더없이 사려 깊은 말씨로나마 추장들의 결정을 전했다."]], [["\"My brother has been called a liar,\"", "\"내 형제는 거짓말쟁이라 불렸다.\""], ["he said, \"and his friends are angry.", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 그의 벗들은 노여워한다."], ["They will show that he has spoken the truth.", "그들은 그가 참을 말했음을 보일 것이다."], ["Give my prisoners guns, and let them prove which is the man.\"", "내 포로들에게 총을 주어, 어느 쪽이 그 사람인지 가려 보게 하라.\""]], [["Magua affected to consider the expedient, which he well knew proceeded from distrust of himself, as a compliment, and made a gesture of acquiescence, well content that his veracity should be supported by so skilful a marksman as the scout.", "〈마구아〉는 그 방책이 제게 대한 불신에서 비롯한 것임을 잘 알면서도, 그것을 마치 자기에 대한 예우인 양 받아들이는 체하며, 척후병만큼 능란한 명사수가 자기 말의 진실됨을 뒷받침해 주리라는 데 흡족해하며 동의의 몸짓을 지었다."], ["The weapons were instantly placed in the hands of the friendly opponents, and they were bid to fire over the heads of the seated multitude at an earthen vessel, which lay, by accident, on a stump some fifty yards from the place where they stood.", "이내 무기가 다정한 적수들의 손에 쥐어졌고, 그들이 선 자리에서 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 그루터기 위에 우연히 놓여 있던 흙 그릇을 향해, 앉아 있는 무리의 머리 너머로 쏘아 보라는 분부가 내려졌다."]], [["Heyward smiled to himself at the idea of a competition with the scout, though he determined to persevere in the deception, until apprised of the real designs of Magua.", "〈헤이워드〉는 척후병과 겨룬다는 생각에 속으로 웃음 지었으나, 〈마구아〉의 진짜 속셈을 알아낼 때까지는 그 속임수를 밀고 나가기로 작정했다."], ["Raising his rifle with the utmost care, and renewing his aim three several times, he fired.", "더없이 정성스레 소총을 들어 올려, 세 차례나 거듭 겨눈 끝에 그는 방아쇠를 당겼다."], ["The bullet cut the wood within a few inches of the vessel; and a general exclamation of satisfaction announced that the shot was considered a proof of great skill in the use of the weapon.", "총알은 그릇에서 손가락 몇 마디 떨어진 나무를 깎았고, 모두가 한목소리로 흡족함을 토하며 그 사격이 무기를 다루는 빼어난 솜씨의 증거로 여겨진다고 알렸다."], ["Even Hawkeye nodded his head, as if he would say, it was better than he had expected.", "〈호크아이〉조차, 제가 기대한 것보다는 낫다고 말하려는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["But, instead of manifesting an intention to contend with the successful marksman, he stood leaning on his rifle for more than a minute, like a man who was completely buried in thought.", "그러나 그는 그 성공한 명사수와 겨룰 뜻을 내비치기는커녕, 무언가에 온통 골몰한 사람처럼 일 분이 넘도록 소총에 기댄 채 서 있었다."], ["From this reverie he was, however, awakened by one of the young Indians who had furnished the arms, and who now touched his shoulder, saying, in exceedingly broken English,--", "그러나 무기를 건넨 젊은 인디언 가운데 하나가 그의 어깨를 건드리며 몹시 서툰 영어로 말을 거는 바람에 그는 그 상념에서 깨어났다."]], [["\"Can the pale-face beat it?\"", "\"백인이 저것을 이길 수 있는가?\""]], [["\"Yes, Huron!\"", "\"그렇다, 〈휴런〉!\""], ["exclaimed the scout, raising the short rifle in his right hand, and shaking it at Magua, with as much apparent ease as if it were a reed; \"yes, Huron, I could strike you now, and no power of earth could prevent the deed!", "척후병이 짧은 소총을 오른손에 들어 올리며, 마치 갈대인 양 손쉽게 〈마구아〉를 향해 흔들어 보이며 외쳤다. \"그렇다, 〈휴런〉, 나는 지금 당장 너를 칠 수 있고, 지상의 어떤 힘도 그 일을 막지 못하리라!"], ["The soaring hawk is not more certain of the dove than I am this moment of you, did I choose to send a bullet to your heart!", "솟구치는 매가 비둘기를 노릴 때보다, 내 마음만 먹는다면 지금 이 순간 네 심장에 총알을 박아 넣는 것이 더 틀림없으리라!"], ["Why should I not?", "어찌 그러지 않겠는가?"], ["Why!--because the gifts of my color forbid it, and I might draw down evil on tender and innocent heads.", "어찌!—내 살빛의 천품이 그것을 금하고, 또 내가 가냘프고 죄 없는 머리들 위에 화를 불러올지도 모르기 때문이지."], ["If you know such a being as God, thank Him, therefore, in your inward soul; for you have reason.\"", "만일 네가 하느님이라 불리는 분을 안다면, 그러므로 네 마음속 깊이 그분께 감사하라. 너에게는 그럴 까닭이 있으니.\""]], [["The flushed countenance, angry eye, and swelling figure of the scout, produced a sensation of secret awe in all that heard him.", "상기된 얼굴, 성난 눈, 부풀어 오른 척후병의 몸이, 그 말을 듣는 모든 이에게 은밀한 외경의 느낌을 자아냈다."], ["The Delawares held their breath in expectation; but Magua himself, even while he distrusted the forbearance of his enemy, remained immovable and calm, where he stood wedged in by the crowd, as one who grew to the spot.", "〈델라웨어〉족은 기대 속에 숨을 죽였다. 그러나 〈마구아〉 자신은, 제 원수의 자제심을 미덥지 못하게 여기면서도, 군중에 끼여 그 자리에 뿌리내린 사람처럼, 꼼짝 않고 침착하게 서 있었다."]], [["\"Beat it,\"", "\"저것을 이겨라.\""], ["replied the young Delaware at the elbow of the scout.", "척후병의 팔꿈치께에 선 젊은 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 대꾸했다."]], [["\"Beat what, fool!--what!\"", "\"무엇을 이긴단 말이냐, 멍청아!—무엇을!\""], ["exclaimed Hawkeye, still flourishing the weapon angrily above his head, though his eye no longer sought the person of Magua.", "〈호크아이〉가, 이제는 그 눈이 더는 〈마구아〉의 몸을 찾지 않으면서도, 여전히 머리 위로 무기를 성나게 휘두르며 외쳤다."]], [["\"If the white man is the warrior he pretends,\"", "\"백인이 제가 자처하는 그 전사라면,\""], ["said the aged chief, \"let him strike nigher to the mark.\"", "늙은 추장이 말했다. \"표적에 더 가까이 맞혀 보아라.\""]], [["The scout laughed aloud--a noise that produced the startling effect of an unnatural sound on Heyward; then dropping the piece heavily into his extended left hand, it was discharged, apparently by the shock, driving the fragments of the vessel into the air, and scattering them on every side.", "척후병이 큰 소리로 웃었으니—그 소리는 〈헤이워드〉에게 부자연스러운 음향이 주는 듯한 섬뜩한 효과를 자아냈다—그러고는 그 총을 뻗은 왼손에 묵직하게 떨어뜨리자, 보아하니 그 충격으로 총이 발사되어, 그릇의 파편이 공중으로 튀어 올라 사방으로 흩어졌다."], ["Almost at the same instant, the rattling sound of the rifle was heard, as he suffered it to fall, contemptuously, to the earth.", "거의 같은 순간, 그가 그것을 경멸하듯 땅에 떨어뜨리도록 내버려 둠에 따라, 소총이 덜그럭대는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["The first impression of so strange a scene was engrossing admiration.", "이토록 기이한 광경에 대한 첫 인상은 넋을 빼앗는 감탄이었다."], ["Then a low, but increasing murmur, ran through the multitude, and finally swelled into sounds that denoted a lively opposition in the sentiments of the spectators.", "이어 낮으나 점점 커지는 웅성거림이 무리 사이로 번지더니, 마침내 구경꾼들의 심사가 자못 엇갈리고 있음을 드러내는 소리로 부풀어 올랐다."], ["While some openly testified their satisfaction at so unexampled dexterity, by far the larger portion of the tribe were inclined to believe the success of the shot was the result of accident.", "더러는 그토록 유례없는 솜씨에 대한 흡족함을 드러내 놓고 내비쳤으나, 부족의 훨씬 더 많은 이는 그 사격의 성공이 우연의 결과라 믿는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["Heyward was not slow to confirm an opinion that was so favorable to his own pretentions.", "〈헤이워드〉는 제 자처에 그토록 유리한 그 견해를 뒷받침하는 데 굼뜨지 않았다."]], [["\"It was chance!\"", "\"우연이었소!\""], ["he exclaimed; \"none can shoot without an aim!\"", "그가 외쳤다. \"겨냥 없이 쏠 수 있는 자는 없소!\""]], [["\"Chance!\"", "\"우연이라고!\""], ["echoed the excited woodsman, who was now stubbornly bent on maintaining his identity at every hazard, and on whom the secret hints of Heyward to acquiesce in the deception were entirely lost.", "흥분한 숲의 사내가 받아 외쳤으니, 그는 이제 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 제 정체를 굳게 지키기로 고집스레 작정한 터라, 속임수에 순순히 따르라는 〈헤이워드〉의 은밀한 눈짓 따위는 그에게 깡그리 헛것이 되고 말았다."], ["\"Does yonder lying Huron, too, think it chance?", "\"저 거짓말쟁이 〈휴런〉도 우연이라 생각하는가?"], ["Give him another gun, and place us face to face, without cover or dodge, and let Providence, and our own eyes, decide the matter atween us!", "그에게 다른 총을 한 자루 주고, 엄폐도 회피도 없이 우리를 얼굴과 얼굴을 맞대게 한 다음, 섭리와 우리 자신의 눈으로 우리 사이의 일을 판가름하게 하라!"], ["I do not make the offer to you, major; for our blood is of a color, and we serve the same master.\"", "소령, 당신에게 이런 제안을 하는 건 아니오. 우리 피는 같은 빛깔이고, 우리는 같은 주인을 섬기니 말이오.\""]], [["\"That the Huron is a liar, is very evident,\"", "\"저 〈휴런〉이 거짓말쟁이라는 것은 너무도 명백하오.\""], ["returned Heyward, coolly; \"you have yourself heard him assert you to be La Longue Carabine.\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 냉정하게 대꾸했다. \"당신이 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉이라고 그가 단언하는 것을 당신 스스로 듣지 않았소.\""]], [["It were impossible to say what violent assertion the stubborn Hawkeye would have next made, in his headlong wish to vindicate his identity, had not the aged Delaware once more interposed.", "고집스러운 〈호크아이〉가 제 정체를 입증하려는 그 무모한 열망 속에서 다음으로 어떤 격한 주장을 펼쳤을지는, 늙은 〈델라웨어〉 사람이 다시 한 번 끼어들지 않았더라면 도무지 알 수 없을 노릇이었다."]], [["\"The hawk which comes from the clouds can return when he will,\"", "\"구름 속에서 내려오는 매는 제 마음대로 돌아갈 수 있다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"give them the guns.\"", "\"그들에게 총을 주어라.\""]], [["This time the scout seized the rifle with avidity; nor had Magua, though he watched the movement of the marksman with jealous eyes, any further cause for apprehension.", "이번에는 척후병이 게걸스레 소총을 거머쥐었고, 〈마구아〉 또한, 비록 시샘 어린 눈으로 그 명사수의 동작을 지켜보았으나, 더는 두려워할 까닭이 없었다."]], [["\"Now let it be proved, in the face of this tribe of Delawares, which is the better man,\"", "\"자, 이제 이 〈델라웨어〉 부족이 보는 앞에서, 어느 쪽이 더 나은 사내인지 가려 보자.\""], ["cried the scout, tapping the butt of his piece with that finger which had pulled so many fatal triggers.", "척후병이, 그토록 많은 목숨을 끊는 방아쇠를 당겨 온 그 손가락으로 제 총의 개머리판을 톡톡 두드리며 외쳤다."], ["\"You see the gourd hanging against yonder tree, major; if you are a marksman fit for the borders, let me see you break its shell!\"", "\"소령, 저 나무에 매달린 조롱박이 보이지? 당신이 변경에 어울리는 명사수라면, 그 껍질을 깨뜨리는 걸 어디 한번 보여 주시오!\""]], [["Duncan noted the object, and prepared himself to renew the trial.", "〈던컨〉은 그 표적을 눈여겨보며, 다시 시험에 나설 채비를 갖췄다."], ["The gourd was one of the usual little vessels used by the Indians, and it was suspended from a dead branch of a small pine, by a thong of deer-skin, at the full distance of a hundred yards.", "그 조롱박은 인디언들이 흔히 쓰는 작은 그릇 가운데 하나로, 사슴 가죽끈에 매달려 작은 소나무의 마른 가지에, 꼬박 백 걸음 거리에 드리워져 있었다."], ["So strangely compounded is the feeling of self-love, that the young soldier, while he knew the utter worthlessness of the suffrages of his savage umpires, forgot the sudden motives of the contest in a wish to excel.", "자기애의 감정이란 어찌나 묘하게 뒤섞여 있는지, 이 젊은 군인은 자신을 심판하는 미개인들의 평이 아무 값어치도 없음을 잘 알면서도, 이 겨룸의 별안간한 동기를 잊은 채 한껏 빼어나고 싶은 마음에 사로잡혔다."], ["It has been seen, already, that his skill was far from being contemptible, and he now resolved to put forth its nicest qualities.", "그의 솜씨가 결코 업신여길 수준이 아님은 이미 본 바이거니와, 이제 그는 그 가장 정교한 기량을 펼치기로 마음먹었다."], ["Had his life depended on the issue, the aim of Duncan could not have been more deliberate or guarded.", "제 목숨이 그 결과에 달려 있었던들, 〈던컨〉의 겨냥이 이보다 더 신중하거나 조심스러울 수는 없었으리라."], ["He fired; and three or four young Indians, who sprang forward at the report, announced with a shout, that the ball was in the tree, a very little on one side of the proper object.", "그가 쏘았다. 그러자 그 소리에 앞으로 뛰어나간 서너 명의 젊은 인디언이, 총알이 본래 표적에서 아주 조금 옆으로 비낀 나무에 박혔다고 함성으로 알렸다."], ["The warriors uttered a common ejaculation of pleasure, and then turned their eyes inquiringly on the movements of his rival.", "전사들은 한목소리로 기쁨의 탄성을 내질렀고, 이어 그의 적수의 거동에 캐묻듯 눈길을 돌렸다."]], [["\"It may do for the Royal Americans!\"", "\"〈로열 아메리칸〉 연대에야 통할 솜씨겠지!\""], ["said Hawkeye, laughing once more in his own silent, heartfelt manner; \"but had my gun often turned so much from the true line, many a marten, whose skin is now in a lady's muff, would still be in the woods; ay, and many a bloody Mingo, who has departed to his final account, would be acting his deviltries at this very day, atween the provinces.", "〈호크아이〉가 다시 한 번 제 특유의 소리 없고 진심 어린 투로 웃으며 말했다. \"허나 내 총이 그렇게나 자주 곧은 선에서 빗나갔던들, 지금 어느 귀부인의 토시가 된 담비 가죽의 임자가 여태 숲에 살아 있을 테고, 이미 마지막 심판을 받으러 떠난 피비린내 나는 〈밍고〉 놈들 가운데 숱한 자가 바로 오늘날까지도 식민지 사이를 누비며 제 악행을 부리고 있을 거요."], ["I hope the squaw who owns the gourd has more of them in her wigwam, for this will never hold water again!\"", "저 조롱박 임자인 인디언 여인이 제 천막에 그런 박을 더 많이 가지고 있기를 바라오. 저놈은 다시는 물을 담지 못할 테니!\""]], [["The scout had shook his priming, and cocked his piece, while speaking; and, as he ended, he threw back a foot, and slowly raised the muzzle from the earth: the motion was steady, uniform, and in one direction.", "척후병은 말하는 동안 점화약을 흔들어 놓고 공이치기를 당겨 두었다. 말을 마치자 그는 한 발을 뒤로 빼고, 천천히 총구를 땅에서 들어 올렸다."], ["When on a perfect level, it remained for a single moment, without tremor or variation, as though both man and rifle were carved in stone.", "그 동작은 한결같고 고르며 한 방향으로 향했다. 완벽히 수평을 이루자, 총구는 떨림도 흔들림도 없이 한순간 멈추었으니, 마치 사람도 소총도 돌로 새겨진 듯했다."], ["During that stationary instant, it poured forth its contents, in a bright, glancing sheet of flame.", "그 정지한 찰나에, 그것은 환하게 번득이는 불꽃의 장막을 이루며 제 내용물을 쏟아 냈다."], ["Again the young Indians bounded forward; but their hurried search and disappointed looks announced that no traces of the bullet were to be seen.", "다시 젊은 인디언들이 앞으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 그러나 그들의 다급한 수색과 실망한 표정은 총알의 자취라곤 어디서도 찾을 수 없음을 알렸다."]], [["\"Go!\"", "\"가라!\""], ["said the old chief to the scout, in a tone of strong disgust; \"thou art a wolf in the skin of a dog.", "늙은 추장이 짙은 혐오의 어조로 척후병에게 말했다. \"너는 개의 가죽을 쓴 늑대로구나."], ["I will talk to the 'Long Rifle' of the Yengeese.\"", "나는 〈잉기즈〉의 '롱 라이플'과 이야기하겠다.\""]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["had I that piece which furnished the name you use, I would obligate myself to cut the thong, and drop the gourd without breaking it!\"", "당신이 입에 올린 그 이름을 안겨 준 그 총만 있었던들, 나는 가죽끈을 끊어, 조롱박을 깨뜨리지 않고 떨어뜨리겠노라 약속했을 거요!\""], ["returned Hawkeye, perfectly undisturbed by the other's manner, \"Fools, if you would find the bullet of a sharpshooter of these woods, you must look in the object and not around it!\"", "〈호크아이〉가 상대의 태도에 조금도 흔들림 없이 대꾸했다. \"멍청이들, 이 숲의 명사수가 쏜 총알을 찾으려거든, 표적 둘레가 아니라 표적 안을 들여다보아야 한다!\""]], [["The Indian youths instantly comprehended his meaning--for this time he spoke in the Delaware tongue--and tearing the gourd from the tree, they held it on high with an exulting shout, displaying a hole in its bottom, which had been cut by the bullet, after passing through the usual orifice in the centre of its upper side.", "이번에는 그가 〈델라웨어〉족 말로 말했던 까닭에, 인디언 젊은이들은 단번에 그 뜻을 알아차렸다. 그들이 나무에서 조롱박을 잡아채어 의기양양한 함성과 함께 높이 치켜드니, 그 바닥에 총알이 뚫어 놓은 구멍이 드러났는데, 총알은 윗면 한가운데에 으레 나 있는 구멍을 지나친 뒤 그 바닥을 뚫고 나간 것이었다."], ["At this unexpected exhibition, a loud and vehement expression of pleasure burst from the mouth of every warrior present.", "이 뜻밖의 광경에, 그 자리에 있던 모든 전사의 입에서 크고 격렬한 환희의 외침이 터져 나왔다."], ["It decided the question, and effectually established Hawkeye in the possession of his dangerous reputation.", "그것으로 문제는 판가름 났고, 〈호크아이〉는 그 위험한 명성의 임자임을 확고히 굳혔다."], ["Those curious and admiring eyes which had been turned again on Heyward, were finally directed to the weather-beaten form of the scout, who immediately became the principal object of attention to the simple and unsophisticated beings by whom he was surrounded.", "다시 〈헤이워드〉를 향했던 그 호기심 어리고 감탄하는 눈들이, 마침내 풍상에 시달린 척후병의 모습으로 쏠렸고, 그는 이내 자신을 에워싼 순박하고 꾸밈없는 이들의 으뜸가는 관심의 대상이 되었다."], ["When the sudden and noisy commotion had a little subsided, the aged chief resumed his examination.", "별안간 일어난 떠들썩한 소란이 조금 가라앉자, 늙은 추장이 다시 심문을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"Why did you wish to stop my ears?\"", "\"너는 어찌하여 내 귀를 막으려 했는가?\""], ["he said, addressing Duncan; \"are the Delawares fools, that they could not know the young panther from the cat?\"", "그가 〈던컨〉에게 말했다. \"〈델라웨어〉족이 어린 표범과 살쾡이를 구별 못 할 만큼 어리석은 줄 알았느냐?\""]], [["\"They will yet find the Huron a singing-bird,\"", "\"머지않아 저들은 저 〈휴런〉이 노래하는 새임을 알게 될 것이오.\""], ["said Duncan, endeavoring to adopt the figurative language of the natives.", "〈던컨〉이 원주민들의 비유적인 말투를 따르려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"It is good.", "\"좋다."], ["We will know who can shut the ears of men.", "누가 사람들의 귀를 닫을 수 있는지 우리가 알아보리라."], ["Brother,\"", "형제여,\""], ["added the chief, turning his eyes on Magua, \"the Delawares listen.\"", "추장이 〈마구아〉에게 눈길을 돌리며 덧붙였다. \"〈델라웨어〉족이 듣겠다.\""]], [["Thus singled, and directly called on to declare his object, the Huron arose; and advancing with great deliberation and dignity into the very centre of the circle, where he stood confronted to the prisoners, he placed himself in an attitude to speak.", "이렇게 지목되어 곧장 제 목적을 밝히라는 청을 받자, 〈휴런〉족 사내가 일어났다. 그는 더없이 신중하고 위엄 있게 원진의 한복판으로 나아가, 포로들과 마주 선 그 자리에서 말할 자세를 취했다."], ["Before opening his mouth, however, he bent his eyes slowly along the whole living boundary of earnest faces as if to temper his expressions to the capacities of his audience.", "그러나 입을 열기에 앞서, 그는 청중의 헤아림에 맞추어 제 말을 가다듬으려는 듯, 진지한 얼굴들로 이루어진 살아 있는 경계선 전체를 따라 천천히 눈길을 던졌다. 〈호크아이〉에게는 정중한 적의의 눈길을, 〈던컨〉에게는 꺼지지 않는 증오의 눈빛을 보냈다."], ["On Hawkeye he cast a glance of respectful enmity; on Duncan, a look of inextinguishable hatred; the shrinking figure of Alice he scarcely deigned to notice; but when his glance met the firm, commanding, and yet lovely form of Cora, his eye lingered a moment, with an expression that it might have been difficult to define.", "움츠러든 〈앨리스〉의 모습은 거의 거들떠보지도 않았다. 그러나 그 눈길이 〈코라〉의 굳세고 위엄 있으면서도 사랑스러운 자태에 가 닿자, 그의 눈은 한순간 머물렀으니, 그 표정은 무어라 규정하기 어려웠으리라."], ["Then, filled with his own dark intentions, he spoke in the language of the Canadas, a tongue that he well knew was comprehended by most of his auditors.", "그러고는 제 어두운 속셈으로 가득 차서, 그는 〈캐나다〉 지방의 말로 입을 열었으니, 그 말이 청중 대다수에게 통하리라는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["\"The Spirit that made men colored them differently,\" commenced the subtle Huron.", "\"사람을 지으신 〈영〉께서 그들에게 저마다 다른 빛깔을 입히셨다.\""], ["\"Some are blacker than the sluggish bear.", "교활한 〈휴런〉족 사내가 말문을 열었다."], ["These He said would be slaves; and He ordered them to work forever, like the beaver.", "\"더러는 굼뜬 곰보다도 더 검다. 이들에게 그분은 노예가 되리라 이르셨고, 비버처럼 영원히 일하라 명하셨다."], ["You may hear them groan, when the south wind blows, louder than the lowing buffaloes, along the shores of the great salt lake, where the big canoes come and go with them in droves.", "남풍이 불 때면, 큰 카누가 그들을 떼로 싣고 오가는 저 큰 소금 호수의 기슭을 따라, 들소들의 울부짖음보다 더 큰 그들의 신음이 들리리라."], ["Some He made with faces paler than the ermine of the forests: and these He ordered to be traders; dogs to their women, and wolves to their slaves.", "더러는 숲의 담비보다 더 창백한 얼굴로 지으시어, 이들에게 장사치가 되라 명하셨다. 제 여인들에게는 개요, 제 노예들에게는 늑대로다."], ["He gave this people the nature of the pigeon: wings that never tire; young, more plentiful than the leaves on the trees, and appetites to devour the earth.", "그분은 이 족속에게 비둘기의 본성을 주셨으니, 지칠 줄 모르는 날개와, 나무의 잎사귀보다 더 무성한 자식과, 땅을 집어삼킬 식욕이 그것이다."], ["He gave them tongues like the false call of the wild-cat; hearts like rabbits; the cunning of the hog (but none of the fox), and arms longer than the legs of the moose.", "그분은 그들에게 살쾡이의 거짓 울음 같은 혀와, 토끼 같은 마음과, 돼지의 교활함(허나 여우의 교활함은 없는)과, 큰사슴의 다리보다 더 긴 팔을 주셨다."], ["With his tongue, he stops the ears of the Indians; his heart teaches him to pay warriors to fight his battles; his cunning tells him how to get together the goods of the earth; and his arms inclose the land from the shores of the salt-water to the islands of the great lake.", "그 혀로 그는 인디언의 귀를 막고, 그 마음으로 전사들을 돈으로 사 제 싸움을 시키며, 그 교활함으로 땅의 재물을 그러모으는 법을 알고, 그 팔로 소금물의 기슭에서 큰 호수의 섬들에 이르기까지 온 땅을 끌어안는다. 그 탐식이 그를 병들게 한다."], ["His gluttony makes him sick. God gave him enough, and yet he wants all.", "하느님이 그에게 넉넉히 주셨건만, 그는 모든 것을 탐낸다."], ["Such are the pale-faces.", "백인이란 그러한 자들이다."]], [["\"Some the Great Spirit made with skins brighter and redder than yonder sun,\"", "\"〈위대한 영〉께서 더러는 저 해보다 더 밝고 더 붉은 살갗으로 지으셨다.\""], ["continued Magua, pointing impressively upwards to the lurid luminary, which was struggling through the misty atmosphere of the horizon; \"and these did He fashion to His own mind.", "〈마구아〉가, 지평선의 안개 낀 대기를 뚫고 솟아오르려 안간힘 쓰는 그 음산한 빛덩이를 향해 위쪽을 인상 깊게 가리키며 말을 이었다. \"그리고 이들을 그분은 당신 뜻대로 빚으셨다."], ["He gave them this island as He had made it, covered with trees, and filled with game.", "그분은 그들에게 이 섬을, 손수 지으신 그대로, 나무로 뒤덮이고 사냥감으로 가득 찬 채로 주셨다."], ["The wind made their clearings; the sun and rains ripened their fruits; and the snows came to tell them to be thankful.", "바람이 그들의 빈터를 만들었고, 해와 비가 그들의 열매를 익혔으며, 눈이 내려 감사하라 일렀다."], ["What need had they of roads to journey by!", "길을 따라 떠날 무슨 필요가 그들에게 있었으랴!"], ["They saw through the hills.", "그들은 언덕을 꿰뚫어 보았다."], ["When the beavers worked, they lay in the shade, and looked on.", "비버가 일할 때면, 그들은 그늘에 누워 바라보았다."], ["The winds cooled them in summer; in winter, skins kept them warm.", "여름에는 바람이 그들을 식혀 주었고, 겨울에는 가죽이 그들을 따뜻하게 해 주었다."], ["If they fought among themselves, it was to prove that they were men.", "그들이 저희끼리 싸웠다면, 그것은 자신들이 사내임을 입증하기 위함이었다."], ["They were brave; they were just; they were happy.\"", "그들은 용맹했고, 공정했으며, 행복했다.\""]], [["Here the speaker paused, and again looked around him, to discover if his legend had touched the sympathies of his listeners.", "여기서 화자는 말을 멈추고, 제 이야기가 듣는 이들의 심금을 울렸는지 살피려 다시 한 번 둘레를 둘러보았다."], ["He met everywhere with eyes riveted on his own, heads erect, and nostrils expanded, as if each individual present felt himself able and willing, singly, to redress the wrongs of his race.", "그가 마주친 것은, 어디서나 제 눈에 못 박힌 듯한 눈들, 곧추선 머리들, 그리고 벌름거리는 콧구멍이었으니, 마치 그 자리의 사람마다 홀로 제 종족의 부당함을 바로잡을 수 있고 또 기꺼이 그러리라 느끼는 듯했다."]], [["\"If the Great Spirit gave different tongues to his red children,\"", "\"〈위대한 영〉께서 당신의 붉은 자식들에게 저마다 다른 혀를 주셨다면,\""], ["he continued, in a low, still, melancholy voice, \"it was that all animals might understand them.", "그가 낮고 잔잔하며 우수에 찬 목소리로 이었다. \"그것은 모든 짐승이 그들의 말을 알아듣게 하려 함이었다."], ["Some He placed among the snows, with their cousin the bear.", "더러는 그들의 사촌인 곰과 함께 눈 속에 두셨다."], ["Some he placed near the setting sun, on the road to the happy hunting-grounds.", "더러는 행복한 사냥터로 가는 길목, 지는 해 가까이에 두셨다."], ["Some on the lands around the great fresh waters; but to his greatest, and most beloved, He gave the sands of the salt lake.", "더러는 큰 민물 둘레의 땅에 두셨다. 그러나 가장 위대하고 가장 사랑하는 자식들에게는, 소금 호수의 모래를 주셨다."], ["Do my brothers know the name of this favored people?\"", "내 형제들은 이 사랑받은 백성의 이름을 아는가?\""]], [["\"It was the Lenape!\"", "\"〈레나페〉다!\""], ["exclaimed twenty eager voices, in a breath.", "스무 갈래의 열띤 목소리가 일제히 외쳤다."]], [["\"It was the Lenni Lenape,\"", "\"〈레니 레나페〉였다.\""], ["returned Magua, affecting to bend his head in reverence to their former greatness.", "〈마구아〉가 그들의 지난날 위대함에 경의를 표하듯 머리를 숙이는 체하며 대꾸했다."], ["\"It was the tribes of the Lenape!", "\"그것은 〈레나페〉의 부족들이었다!"], ["The sun rose from water that was salt, and set in water that was sweet, and never hid himself from their eyes.", "해는 짠물에서 떠올라 단물 속으로 졌고, 결코 그들의 눈에서 제 모습을 감추지 않았다."], ["But why should I, a Huron of the woods, tell a wise people their own traditions?", "그러나 숲의 한낱 〈휴런〉인 내가, 어찌하여 지혜로운 백성에게 그들 자신의 전승을 들려준단 말인가?"], ["Why remind them of their injuries; their ancient greatness; their deeds; their glory; their happiness,--their losses; their defeats; their misery?", "어찌하여 그들에게 그들의 상처를, 그들의 옛 위대함을, 그들의 행적을, 그들의 영광을, 그들의 행복을—그들의 상실을, 그들의 패배를, 그들의 비참을 일깨운단 말인가?"], ["Is there not one among them who has seen it all, and who knows it to be true?", "그 모든 것을 보았고 그것이 참임을 아는 이가, 그들 가운데 하나도 없겠는가?"], ["I have done.", "나는 다 말했다."], ["My tongue is still, for my heart is of lead.", "내 혀는 잠잠하니, 내 마음이 납덩이 같음이라."], ["I listen.\"", "나는 듣겠다.\""]], [["As the voice of the speaker suddenly ceased, every face and all eyes turned, by a common movement, towards the venerable Tamenund.", "화자의 목소리가 별안간 그치자, 모든 얼굴과 모든 눈이 한결같은 움직임으로 존엄한 〈태머넌드〉를 향했다."], ["From the moment that he took his seat, until the present instant, the lips of the patriarch had not severed, and scarcely a sign of life had escaped him.", "자리에 앉은 그 순간부터 지금 이 찰나에 이르기까지, 족장의 입술은 벌어진 적이 없었고, 생명의 기색이라곤 거의 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["He sat bent in feebleness, and apparently unconscious of the presence he was in, during the whole of that opening scene, in which the skill of the scout had been so clearly established.", "그는 척후병의 솜씨가 그토록 또렷이 입증된 그 첫 장면 내내, 쇠약함에 몸을 굽힌 채, 자신이 어떤 자리에 있는지조차 모르는 듯 앉아 있었다."], ["At the nicely graduated sound of Magua's voice, however, he betrayed some evidence of consciousness, and once or twice he even raised his head, as if to listen.", "그러나 〈마구아〉의 절묘하게 조절된 목소리에는 어떤 의식의 기미를 내비쳤고, 귀를 기울이려는 듯 한두 차례 고개를 들기까지 했다."], ["But when the crafty Huron spoke of his nation by name, the eyelids of the old man raised themselves, and he looked out upon the multitude with that sort of dull unmeaning expression which might be supposed to belong to the countenance of a spectre.", "그러나 그 교활한 〈휴런〉이 제 부족을 이름으로 일컫자, 노인의 눈꺼풀이 들려 올라갔고, 그는 망령의 얼굴에나 어울릴 법한 그런 멍하고 뜻 모를 표정으로 무리를 내다보았다."], ["Then he made an effort to rise, and being upheld by his supporters, he gained his feet, in a posture commanding by its dignity, while he tottered with weakness.", "이윽고 그가 일어서려 애쓰니, 부축하는 이들이 그를 떠받쳐 그는 두 발로 섰는데, 쇠약함에 비틀거리면서도 그 자세는 위엄으로 좌중을 압도했다."]], [["\"Who calls upon the children of the Lenape!\"", "\"누가 〈레나페〉의 자식들을 부르는가!\""], ["he said, in a deep, guttural voice, that was rendered awfully audible by the breathless silence of the multitude: \"who speaks of things gone!", "그가, 무리의 숨죽인 정적으로 인해 끔찍하리만치 또렷이 들리는 깊고 목쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"누가 지나간 것들을 말하는가!"], ["Does not the egg become a worm--the worm a fly, and perish?", "알이 벌레가 되고—벌레가 파리가 되어, 스러지지 않더냐?"], ["Why tell the Delawares of good that is past?", "어찌하여 〈델라웨어〉족에게 이미 가 버린 좋은 것을 들려주는가?"], ["Better thank the Manitou for that which remains.\"", "남아 있는 것에 대해 〈마니투〉께 감사함이 차라리 나으리라.\""]], [["\"It is a Wyandot,\"", "\"〈와이언도트〉입니다.\""], ["said Magua, stepping nigher to the rude platform on which the other stood; \"a friend of Tamenund.\"", "〈마구아〉가, 노인이 선 그 거친 단상에 더 가까이 다가서며 말했다. \"〈태머넌드〉의 벗입니다.\""]], [["\"A friend!\"", "\"벗이라!\""], ["repeated the sage, on whose brow a dark frown settled, imparting a portion of that severity which had rendered his eye so terrible in middle age.", "현자가 되뇌었으니, 그 이마에 어두운 찌푸림이 내려앉으며, 한창때 그의 눈을 그토록 무섭게 만들었던 그 준엄함의 일부가 깃들었다."], ["\"Are the Mingos rulers of the earth?", "\"〈밍고〉족이 이 땅의 지배자던가?"], ["What brings a Huron here?\"", "무엇이 〈휴런〉을 이리로 데려왔는가?\""]], [["\"Justice.", "\"정의입니다."], ["His prisoners are with his brothers, and he comes for his own.\"", "그의 포로들이 그의 형제들과 함께 있으니, 그는 제 것을 찾으러 왔습니다.\""]], [["Tamenund turned his head towards one of his supporters, and listened to the short explanation the man gave.", "〈태머넌드〉는 부축하는 이들 가운데 하나에게로 고개를 돌려, 그가 들려주는 짧은 설명에 귀를 기울였다."], ["Then facing the applicant, he regarded him a moment with deep attention; after which he said, in a low and reluctant voice,--", "그러고는 청원자를 마주하여, 한순간 깊은 주의를 기울여 그를 바라본 뒤, 낮고 마지못한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Justice is the law of the great Manitou.", "\"정의는 위대한 〈마니투〉의 법이다."], ["My children, give the stranger food.", "내 자식들아, 저 이방인에게 먹을 것을 주어라."], ["Then, Huron, take thine own and depart.\"", "그런 다음, 〈휴런〉이여, 네 것을 가지고 떠나라.\""]], [["On the delivery of this solemn judgment, the patriarch seated himself, and closed his eyes again, as if better pleased with the images of his own ripened experience than with the visible objects of the world.", "이 엄숙한 판결이 내려지자, 족장은 다시 자리에 앉아 눈을 감았으니, 마치 눈에 보이는 세상의 사물보다 제 무르익은 경험의 영상이 더 흡족한 듯했다."], ["Against such a decree there was no Delaware sufficiently hardy to murmur, much less oppose himself.", "이런 판결에 감히 투덜댈, 더구나 맞설 만큼 대담한 〈델라웨어〉 사람은 없었다."], ["The words were barely uttered when four or five of the younger warriors, stepping behind Heyward and the scout, passed thongs so dexterously and rapidly around their arms, as to hold them both in instant bondage.", "말이 채 떨어지기도 전에, 너덧 명의 젊은 전사가 〈헤이워드〉와 척후병의 뒤로 다가서더니, 어찌나 능란하고도 재빠르게 그들의 팔에 가죽끈을 둘렀던지, 두 사람 모두를 순식간에 결박했다."], ["The former was too much engrossed with his precious and nearly insensible burden, to be aware of their intentions before they were executed; and the latter, who considered even the hostile tribes of the Delawares a superior race of beings, submitted without resistance.", "전자는 제 소중하고 거의 정신을 놓은 짐에 너무 골몰해 있던 터라, 그들의 의도를 그 일이 벌어지기 전에는 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 적대하는 〈델라웨어〉 부족조차 한층 우월한 종족으로 여기던 후자는, 저항 없이 순순히 따랐다."], ["Perhaps, however, the manner of the scout would not have been so passive, had he fully comprehended the language in which the preceding dialogue had been conducted.", "그러나 앞서 오간 대화가 무슨 말로 이루어졌는지 척후병이 온전히 알아들었던들, 그의 태도가 그토록 고분고분하지는 않았으리라."]], [["Magua cast a look of triumph around the whole assembly before he proceeded to the execution of his purpose.", "〈마구아〉는 제 목적을 실행에 옮기기에 앞서, 회중 전체에 의기양양한 눈길을 던졌다."], ["Perceiving that the men were unable to offer any resistance, he turned his looks on her he valued most.", "사내들이 어떤 저항도 할 수 없음을 알아채고, 그는 가장 귀히 여기는 여인에게로 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["Cora met his gaze with an eye so calm and firm, that his resolution wavered.", "〈코라〉는 어찌나 차분하고 굳센 눈으로 그의 응시를 맞받았던지, 그의 결심이 흔들렸다."], ["Then recollecting his former artifice, he raised Alice from the arms of the warrior against whom she leaned, and beckoning Heyward to follow, he motioned for the encircling crowd to open.", "그러자 그는 앞서 쓰던 술책을 떠올려, 〈앨리스〉가 기대어 선 전사의 팔에서 그녀를 일으켜 세우고, 〈헤이워드〉에게 따라오라 손짓하며, 둘러선 무리에게 길을 트라고 신호했다."], ["But Cora, instead of obeying the impulse he had expected, rushed to the feet of the patriarch, and raising her voice, exclaimed aloud,--", "그러나 〈코라〉는 그가 예상한 충동을 좇기는커녕, 족장의 발치로 달려가 목소리를 높여 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["\"Just and venerable Delaware, on thy wisdom and power we lean for mercy!", "\"공정하고 존경받는 〈델라웨어〉여, 우리는 자비를 구하여 당신의 지혜와 권능에 기댑니다!"], ["Be deaf to yonder artful and remorseless monster, who poisons thy ears with falsehoods to feed his thirst for blood.", "피에 대한 갈증을 채우려 거짓으로 당신의 귀를 더럽히는 저 교활하고 무자비한 괴물에게는 귀를 닫으소서."], ["Thou that hast lived long, and that hast seen the evil of the world, should know how to temper its calamities to the miserable.\"", "오래 사셨고 세상의 악을 보아 오신 당신이라면, 그 재앙을 비참한 자들에게 누그러뜨려 줄 줄 아셔야 합니다.\""]], [["# THE SUPPLICANT 320", "# THE SUPPLICANT 320"]], [["The eyes of the old man opened heavily, and he once more looked upwards at the multitude.", "노인의 눈이 무겁게 열리며, 그는 다시 한 번 위쪽으로 무리를 바라보았다."], ["As the piercing tones of the supplicant swelled on his ears, they moved slowly in the direction of her person, and finally settled there in a steady gaze.", "청원자의 꿰뚫는 듯한 목소리가 그의 귀에 부풀어 오르자, 그의 눈은 천천히 그녀의 모습 쪽으로 움직이더니, 마침내 그곳에 한결같은 응시로 머물렀다."], ["Cora had cast herself to her knees; and, with hands clenched in each other and pressed upon her bosom, she remained like a beauteous and breathing model of her sex, looking up in his faded, but majestic countenance, with a species of holy reverence.", "〈코라〉는 무릎을 꿇고, 두 손을 맞쥐어 가슴에 누른 채, 마치 제 성(性)의 아름답고 숨 쉬는 본보기처럼, 빛바랜 그러나 위엄 있는 그의 얼굴을 일종의 거룩한 외경으로 우러르고 있었다."], ["Gradually the expression of Tamenund's features changed, and losing their vacancy in admiration, they lighted with a portion of that intelligence which a century before had been wont to communicate his youthful fire to the extensive bands of the Delawares.", "〈태머넌드〉의 얼굴 표정이 차츰 변하더니, 그 멍함이 감탄 속에 사라지고, 한 세기 전 〈델라웨어〉족의 드넓은 무리에게 그의 젊은 열정을 전하곤 했던 그 명민함의 일부로 환히 밝아 왔다."], ["Rising without assistance, and seemingly without an effort, he demanded, in a voice that startled its auditors by its firmness,--", "부축도 없이, 보아하니 힘들이는 기색도 없이 일어서서, 그는 듣는 이들을 그 단호함으로 놀라게 하는 목소리로 물었다."]], [["\"What art thou?\"", "\"너는 무엇이냐?\""]], [["\"A woman.", "\"한 여인입니다."], ["One of a hated race, if thou wilt--a Yengee.", "당신이 그리 부르신다면, 미움받는 종족의 하나—〈잉기〉입니다."], ["But one who has never harmed thee, and who cannot harm thy people, if she would; who asks for succor.\"", "그러나 결코 당신을 해친 적 없고, 설령 그러려 한들 당신의 백성을 해칠 수 없는 자, 도움을 청하는 자입니다.\""]], [["\"Tell me, my children,\"", "\"말해 다오, 내 자식들아.\""], ["continued the patriarch, hoarsely, motioning to those around him, though his eyes still dwelt upon the kneeling form of Cora, \"where have the Delawares camped?\"", "족장이, 그 눈은 여전히 무릎 꿇은 〈코라〉의 모습에 머문 채, 둘레의 이들에게 손짓하며 목쉰 소리로 이었다. \"〈델라웨어〉족은 어디에 진을 쳤느냐?\""]], [["\"In the mountains of the Iroquois, beyond the clear springs of the Horican.\"", "\"〈호리컨〉의 맑은 샘 너머, 〈이로쿼이〉의 산속입니다.\""]], [["\"Many parching summers are come and gone,\"", "\"내가 내 강의 물을 마신 지,\" 현자가 이었다."], ["continued the sage, \"since I drank of the water of my own rivers.", "\"타는 듯한 여름이 숱하게 오고 갔구나. 〈밍쿠언〉의 자식들은 가장 공정한 백인이다."], ["The children of Minquon[26] are the justest white men; but they were thirsty, and they took it to themselves.", "그러나 그들도 목이 말랐고, 그것을 제 것으로 삼았다. 그들이 우리를 그토록 멀리까지 좇아오는가?\""], ["Do they follow us so far?\"", "* 〈밍쿠언〉: 〈델라웨어〉족이 펜실베이니아의 창설자 윌리엄 펜을 부르던 이름."]], [["\"We follow none; we covet nothing,\" answered Cora.", "\"우리는 아무도 좇지 않으며, 아무것도 탐하지 않습니다.\""], ["\"Captives against our wills, have we been brought among you; and we ask but permission to depart to our own in peace.", "〈코라〉가 대답했다. \"우리는 우리 뜻에 거슬러 사로잡혀 당신들 가운데로 끌려왔으니, 다만 우리 사람들에게로 평화로이 떠날 허락을 구할 따름입니다."], ["Art thou not Tamenund--the father, the judge, I had almost said, the prophet--of this people?\"", "당신은 이 백성의 아버지요, 재판관이요, 거의 예언자라 할 〈태머넌드〉가 아니십니까?\""]], [["\"I am Tamenund of many days.\"", "\"나는 오랜 세월을 산 〈태머넌드〉다.\""]], [["\"'Tis now some seven years that one of thy people was at the mercy of a white chief on the borders of this province.", "\"이제 한 칠 년쯤 되었지요, 당신의 백성 가운데 하나가 이 식민지 변경에서 한 백인 우두머리의 처분에 맡겨졌던 것이."], ["He claimed to be of the blood of the good and just Tamenund.", "그는 자신이 선량하고 공정한 〈태머넌드〉의 혈통이라 주장했습니다."], ["'Go,'", "'가라.'"], ["said the white man, 'for thy parent's sake thou art free.'", "백인이 말했지요. '네 조상을 봐서 너는 자유다.'"], ["Dost thou remember the name of that English warrior?\"", "당신은 그 〈영국〉 전사의 이름을 기억하십니까?\""]], [["\"I remember, that when a laughing boy,\"", "\"내가 기억하기로는, 웃음 많던 소년 시절에,\""], ["returned the patriarch, with the peculiar recollection of vast age, \"I stood upon the sands of the sea-shore, and saw a big canoe, with wings whiter than the swan's, and wider than many eagles, come from the rising sun.\"", "족장이, 아득한 노령 특유의 회상으로 대꾸했다. \"내가 바닷가 모래밭에 서서, 백조보다 더 희고 숱한 독수리보다 더 넓은 날개를 단 큰 카누 하나가 떠오르는 해로부터 다가오는 것을 보았느니라.\""]], [["\"Nay, nay; I speak not of a time so very distant, but of favor shown to thy kindred by one of mine, within the memory of thy youngest warrior.\"", "\"아니, 아닙니다. 저는 그토록 까마득한 때를 말하는 것이 아니라, 당신의 가장 젊은 전사의 기억 안에 들어 있을, 제 사람 가운데 하나가 당신의 일족에게 베푼 호의를 말하는 것입니다.\""]], [["\"Was it when the Yengeese and the Dutchmanne fought for the hunting-grounds of the Delawares?", "\"그것은 〈잉기즈〉와 〈더치먼〉이 〈델라웨어〉족의 사냥터를 두고 싸우던 때였더냐?"], ["Then Tamenund was a chief, and first laid aside the bow for the lightning of the pale faces--\"", "그때 〈태머넌드〉는 추장이었고, 활을 처음으로 내려놓고 백인의 번개를 든 것이—\""]], [["# THE SUPPLICANT", "# THE SUPPLICANT"]], [["_Cora had cast herself to her knees; and, with hands clenched in each other and pressed upon her bosom, she remained like a beauteous and breathing model of her sex_]", "_코라는 무릎을 꿇고 있었다. 두 손을 맞잡아 가슴에 꼭 댄 채, 그녀는 자기 성(性)의 아름답고 살아 숨 쉬는 본보기처럼 그렇게 있었다_]"]], [["\"Nor yet then,\" interrupted Cora, \"by many ages; I speak of a thing of yesterday.", "\"그것은 다만 어제의 일이었구나.\""], ["Surely, surely, you forget it not.\" \"It was but yesterday,\"", "노인이 가슴을 울리는 비애로 받았다."], ["rejoined the aged man, with touching pathos, \"that the children of the Lenape were masters of the world.", "\"〈레나페〉의 자식들이 이 세상의 주인이던 것이."], ["The fishes of the salt lake, the birds, the beasts, and the Mengwe of the woods, owned them for sagamores.\"", "소금 호수의 물고기들, 새들, 짐승들, 그리고 숲의 〈멩궤〉가 그들을 〈사가모어〉로 받들었느니라.\""]], [["Cora bowed her head in disappointment, and, for a bitter moment, struggled with her chagrin.", "〈코라〉는 실망에 고개를 떨구고, 쓰라린 한순간 제 낙담과 씨름했다."], ["Then elevating her rich features and beaming eyes, she continued, in tones scarcely less penetrating than the unearthly voice of the patriarch himself,--", "이윽고 그 풍요로운 얼굴과 빛나는 눈을 들어 올리며, 그녀는 족장 자신의 이 세상 것 아닌 목소리에 못지않게 가슴을 파고드는 어조로 이었다."]], [["\"Tell me, is Tamenund a father?\"", "\"말씀해 주십시오, 〈태머넌드〉는 아버지입니까?\""]], [["The old man looked down upon her from his elevated stand, with a benignant smile on his wasted countenance, and then casting his eyes slowly over the whole assemblage, he answered,--", "노인은 제 높은 자리에서 그녀를 내려다보며, 야윈 얼굴에 인자한 미소를 띠더니, 이윽고 천천히 회중 전체에 눈길을 던지며 대답했다."]], [["\"Of a nation.\"", "\"한 민족의 아버지로다.\""]], [["\"For myself I ask nothing.", "\"저 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 구하지 않습니다."], ["Like thee and thine, venerable chief,\"", "당신과 당신의 일족처럼, 존경받는 추장이여,\""], ["she continued, pressing her hands convulsively on her heart, and suffering her head to droop until her burning cheeks were nearly concealed in the maze of dark glossy tresses that fell in disorder upon her shoulders, \"the curse of my ancestors has fallen heavily on their child.", "그녀가 두 손을 발작하듯 제 가슴에 누르고, 타오르는 두 뺨이 어깨 위로 흐트러져 내린 짙고 윤기 나는 머릿단의 미로 속에 거의 가려질 만큼 고개를 떨구며 이었다. \"제 조상의 저주가 그 자식에게 무겁게 내려앉았습니다."], ["But yonder is one who has never known the weight of Heaven's displeasure until now.", "그러나 저기 저 아이는, 지금 이때까지 〈하늘〉의 노여움의 무게를 한 번도 알지 못했습니다."], ["She is the daughter of an old and failing man, whose days are near their close.", "그녀는, 그 날들이 끝에 가까운 늙고 쇠약한 분의 딸입니다."], ["She has many, very many, to love her, and delight in her; and she is too good, much too precious, to become the victim of that villain.\"", "그녀를 사랑하고 그녀로 기뻐하는 이가 많고, 아주 많습니다. 그리고 그녀는 너무도 선량하고, 너무도 귀하여, 저 악당의 제물이 되기에는 마땅치 않습니다.\""]], [["\"I know that the pale-faces are a proud and hungry race.", "\"나는 백인이 오만하고 굶주린 종족임을 안다."], ["I know that they claim not only to have the earth, but that the meanest of their color is better than the sachems of the redman.", "나는 그들이 이 땅을 차지하려 들 뿐 아니라, 제 빛깔의 가장 미천한 자조차 붉은 사람의 〈사쳄〉보다 낫다고 주장함을 안다."], ["The dogs and crows of their tribes,\"", "그들 부족의 개와 까마귀들은,\""], ["continued the earnest old chieftain, without heeding the wounded spirit of his listener, whose head was nearly crushed to the earth in shame, as he proceeded, \"would bark and caw before they would take a woman to their wigwams whose blood was not of the color of snow.", "진지한 늙은 추장이, 말이 이어질수록 수치에 그 머리가 거의 땅에 짓눌리다시피 한 청원자의 상한 마음에는 아랑곳없이 이었다. \"눈빛이 아닌 피를 가진 여인을 제 천막으로 들이느니 차라리 짖고 까악거릴 것이다."], ["But let them not boast before the face of the Manitou too loud.", "그러나 그들로 하여금 〈마니투〉의 얼굴 앞에서 너무 큰 소리로 으스대지 못하게 하라."], ["They entered the land at the rising, and may yet go off at the setting sun.", "그들은 해 뜨는 데서 이 땅에 들어왔으니, 해 지는 데서 떠나갈 수도 있으리라."], ["I have often seen the locusts strip the leaves from the trees, but the season of blossoms has always come again.\"", "나는 메뚜기가 나무에서 잎을 모조리 갉아 먹는 것을 자주 보았으나, 꽃피는 철은 언제나 다시 돌아왔다.\""]], [["\"It is so,\"", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Cora, drawing a long breath, as if reviving from a trance, raising her face, and shaking back her shining veil, with a kindling eye, that contradicted the death-like paleness of her countenance; \"but why--it is not permitted us to inquire.", "〈코라〉가, 마치 황홀경에서 되살아나는 듯 긴 숨을 들이쉬며, 얼굴을 들어 올려 그 빛나는 너울을 뒤로 떨치며 말했으니, 타오르는 눈빛이 죽은 듯 창백한 그 얼굴빛과 어긋났다. \"그러나 어찌하여—그것을 묻는 것은 우리에게 허락되지 않았지요."], ["There is yet one of thine own people who has not been brought before thee; before thou lettest the Huron depart in triumph, hear him speak.\"", "아직 당신 앞에 끌려오지 않은 당신의 백성 가운데 하나가 있습니다. 〈휴런〉이 의기양양하게 떠나도록 두시기 전에, 그가 하는 말을 들어 주십시오.\""]], [["Observing Tamenund to look about him doubtingly, one of his companions said,--", "〈태머넌드〉가 미심쩍은 듯 둘레를 살피는 것을 보고, 그의 동료 가운데 하나가 말했다."]], [["\"It is a snake--a redskin in the pay of the Yengeese.", "\"그자는 한 마리 뱀입니다—〈잉기즈〉에게 매수된 붉은 살갗이지요."], ["We keep him for the torture.\"", "우리는 그를 고문하려 붙잡아 두고 있습니다.\""]], [["\"Let him come,\"", "\"그를 데려오라.\""], ["returned the sage.", "현자가 대꾸했다."]], [["Then Tamenund once more sank into his seat, and a silence so deep prevailed, while the young men prepared to obey his simple mandate, that the leaves, which fluttered in the draught of the light morning air, were distinctly heard rustling in the surrounding forest.", "이윽고 〈태머넌드〉가 다시 한 번 제 자리에 내려앉았고, 젊은이들이 그 단출한 분부를 따르려 채비하는 동안 어찌나 깊은 정적이 감돌았던지, 가벼운 아침 공기의 흐름에 살랑이는 잎새들이 둘레의 숲에서 바스락거리는 소리가 또렷이 들렸다. 만일 그대들이 나를 거부한다면, 그대들의 법에 침을 뱉으리라!"]], [["# CHAPTER XXX", "# CHAPTER XXX"]], [["\"If you deny me, fie upon your law!", "〈베네치아〉의 법령에는 아무런 효력도 없으니,\n나는 판결을 청하노라."], ["There is no force in the decrees of Venice: I stand for judgment; answer, shall I have it?\"", "답하라, 내가 그것을 얻겠는가?"]], [["_Merchant of Venice._", "베니스의 상인"]], [["The silence continued unbroken by human sounds for many anxious minutes.", "근심에 찬 여러 분(分) 동안, 사람의 소리라곤 들리지 않는 정적이 이어졌다."], ["Then the waving multitude opened and shut again, and Uncas stood in the living circle.", "이윽고 물결치던 무리가 갈라졌다가 다시 닫혔고, 〈운카스〉가 살아 있는 원 한가운데 섰다."], ["All those eyes, which had been curiously studying the lineaments of the sage, as the source of their own intelligence, turned on the instant, and were now bent in secret admiration on the erect, agile, and faultless person of the captive.", "자신들의 지혜의 원천인 양 그 현인의 이목구비를 호기심 어린 눈으로 살피던 모든 눈길이 일순간에 돌아서, 이제는 그 포로의 꼿꼿하고 날렵하며 흠 하나 없는 모습에 은밀한 감탄을 보냈다."], ["But neither the presence in which he found himself, nor the exclusive attention that he attracted, in any manner disturbed the self-possession of the young Mohican.", "그러나 자신이 처한 자리도, 자신에게 쏠린 독차지한 주목도, 이 젊은 모히칸의 침착함을 조금도 흩뜨리지 못했다."], ["He cast a deliberate and observing look on every side of him, meeting the settled expression of hostility that lowered in the visages of the chiefs, with the same calmness as the curious gaze of the attentive children.", "그는 사방을 신중하고 관찰하는 눈길로 둘러보며, 추장들의 얼굴에 잔뜩 드리운 적의의 굳은 표정을, 주의 깊은 아이들의 호기심 어린 응시를 대하는 것과 똑같은 차분함으로 마주했다."], ["But when, last in his haughty scrutiny, the person of Tamenund came under his glance, his eye became fixed, as though all other objects were already forgotten.", "그러나 그 오만한 살핌의 마지막에 〈태머넌드〉의 모습이 그의 시야에 들어오자, 그의 눈은 다른 모든 것을 이미 잊어버린 듯 한곳에 고정되었다."], ["Then advancing with a slow and noiseless step up the area, he placed himself immediately before the footstool of the sage.", "이윽고 그는 느리고 소리 없는 걸음으로 그 공터를 가로질러 나아가, 현인의 발판 바로 앞에 자리했다."], ["Here he stood unnoted, though keenly observant himself, until one of the chiefs apprised the latter of his presence.", "그는 그곳에 선 채, 자신은 예리하게 관찰하면서도 주목받지 못하다가, 마침내 추장들 중 하나가 현인에게 그의 존재를 알렸다."]], [["\"With what tongue does the prisoner speak to the Manitou?\"", "\"포로는 어떤 혀로 〈마니토〉에게 말하는가?\""], ["demanded the patriarch, without unclosing his eyes.", "그 가부장이 두 눈을 감은 채 물었다."]], [["\"Like his fathers,\"", "\"제 조상들처럼,\""], ["Uncas replied; \"with the tongue of a Delaware.\"", "〈운카스〉가 대답했다. \"〈델라웨어〉의 혀로 말합니다.\""]], [["At this sudden and unexpected annunciation, a low, fierce yell ran through the multitude, that might not inaptly be compared to the growl of the lion, as his choler is first awakened--a fearful omen of the weight of his future anger.", "이 갑작스럽고 뜻밖의 선언에, 나직하고 사나운 함성이 무리 사이로 번졌으니, 그것은 처음 노여움이 깨어날 때 사자가 내는 으르렁거림에 비겨도 어색하지 않을 만했고, 장차 그 분노의 무게를 예고하는 무서운 전조였다."], ["The effect was equally strong on the sage, though differently exhibited.", "그 효과는 현인에게도 똑같이 강렬했으나, 다르게 드러났다."], ["He passed a hand before his eyes, as if to exclude the least evidence of so shameful a spectacle, while he repeated, in his low, guttural tones, the words he had just heard.", "그는 더없이 수치스러운 광경의 가장 작은 흔적조차 떨쳐 내려는 듯 한 손을 두 눈 앞으로 가져가며, 방금 들은 말을 그 나직하고 목쉰 어조로 되뇌었다."]], [["\"A Delaware!", "\"〈델라웨어〉라니!"], ["I have lived to see the tribes of the Lenape driven from their council-fires, and scattered, like broken herds of deer, among the hills of the Iroquois!", "나는 〈레나페〉의 부족들이 자기네 평의회의 불가에서 쫓겨나, 무너진 사슴 떼처럼 〈이로쿼이〉의 언덕들 사이로 흩어지는 것을 보고도 살아남았다!"], ["I have seen the hatchets of a strange people sweep woods from the valleys, that the winds of heaven had spared!", "나는 낯선 백성의 도끼가, 하늘의 바람조차 살려 둔 골짜기의 숲을 쓸어버리는 것을 보았다!"], ["The beasts that run on the mountains, and the birds that fly above the trees, have I seen living in the wigwams of men; but never before have I found a Delaware so base as to creep, like a poisonous serpent, into the camps of his nation.\"", "산을 달리는 짐승과 나무 위를 나는 새가 사람의 천막 안에서 사는 것을 보았으나, 〈델라웨어〉가 독사처럼 제 민족의 진영으로 기어드는, 그토록 비천한 자를 본 적은 한 번도 없었다.\""]], [["\"The singing-birds have opened their bills,\"", "\"노래하는 새들이 제 부리를 열었으니,\""], ["returned Uncas, in the softest notes of his own musical voice; \"and Tamenund has heard their song.\"", "〈운카스〉가 그 음악 같은 목소리의 가장 부드러운 가락으로 응했다. \"〈태머넌드〉께서 그 노래를 들으셨군요.\""]], [["The sage started, and bent his head aside, as if to catch the fleeting sounds of some passing melody.", "현인은 흠칫하더니, 스쳐 지나가는 어떤 선율의 덧없는 소리를 붙잡으려는 듯 고개를 한쪽으로 기울였다."]], [["\"Does Tamenund dream!\"", "\"〈태머넌드〉가 꿈을 꾸는가!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["\"What voice is at his ear!", "\"내 귀에 닿는 저 목소리는 무엇인가!"], ["Have the winters gone backward!", "겨울들이 뒤로 돌아갔단 말인가!"], ["Will summer come again to the children of the Lenape!\"", "여름이 〈레나페〉의 자손들에게 다시 오려는가!\""]], [["A solemn and respectful silence succeeded this incoherent burst from the lips of the Delaware prophet.", "〈델라웨어〉 예언자의 입에서 터져 나온 이 두서없는 말 뒤로 엄숙하고 공손한 침묵이 이어졌다."], ["His people steadily construed his unintelligible language into one of those mysterious conferences he was believed to hold so frequently with a superior intelligence, and they awaited the issue of the revelation in awe.", "그의 백성은 그 알아들을 수 없는 말을, 그가 그토록 자주 더 높은 지혜와 나눈다고 믿어지는 그 신비로운 교감 중 하나로 한결같이 받아들이며, 외경 속에 그 계시의 결말을 기다렸다."], ["After a patient pause, however, one of the aged men, perceiving that the sage had lost the recollection of the subject before them, ventured to remind him again of the presence of the prisoner.", "그러나 인내심 있게 한참을 멈춰 있던 끝에, 노인들 중 하나가 현인이 눈앞에 놓인 문제의 기억을 잃었음을 알아채고, 다시 한번 포로의 존재를 일깨우려 나섰다."]], [["\"The false Delaware trembles lest he should hear the words of Tamenund,\" he said.", "\"저 거짓된 〈델라웨어〉는 〈태머넌드〉의 말을 듣게 될까 두려워 떨고 있습니다,\""], ["\"'Tis a hound that howls, when the Yengeese show him a trail.\"", "그가 말했다. \"〈잉기〉가 흔적을 보여 주면 짖어 대는 사냥개입니다.\""]], [["\"And ye,\"", "\"그리고 그대들은,\""], ["returned Uncas, looking sternly around him, \"are dogs that whine, when the Frenchman casts ye the offals of his deer!\"", "〈운카스〉가 자기 둘레를 준엄하게 둘러보며 응수했다. \"프랑스인이 사슴 내장을 던져 주면 낑낑대는 개들이다!\""]], [["Twenty knives gleamed in the air, and as many warriors sprang to their feet, at this biting, and perhaps merited retort; but a motion from one of the chiefs suppressed the outbreaking of their tempers, and restored the appearance of quiet.", "이 신랄한, 그리고 어쩌면 마땅한 반박에, 스무 자루의 칼이 공중에서 번뜩였고 그만큼의 전사들이 벌떡 일어섰다. 그러나 추장들 중 하나의 손짓이 그들의 격분의 분출을 억눌렀고, 다시 고요한 외양을 되돌려 놓았다."], ["The task might probably have been more difficult, had not a movement made by Tamenund indicated that he was again about to speak.", "만일 〈태머넌드〉가 보인 어떤 움직임이 그가 다시 말하려 함을 알리지 않았더라면, 그 일은 아마 더 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["\"Delaware!\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉여!\""], ["resumed the sage, \"little art thou worthy of thy name.", "현인이 말을 이었다. \"너는 네 이름에 값할 자격이 거의 없도다."], ["My people have not seen a bright sun in many winters; and the warrior who deserts his tribe when hid in clouds is doubly a traitor.", "내 백성은 여러 겨울 동안 밝은 해를 보지 못했으며, 구름에 가려 있을 때 제 부족을 저버리는 전사는 갑절로 배신자다."], ["The law of the Manitou is just.", "〈마니토〉의 법은 공정하다."], ["It is so; while the rivers run and the mountains stand, while the blossoms come and go on the trees, it must be so.", "그러하니, 강이 흐르고 산이 서 있는 동안, 나무에 꽃이 피고 지는 동안, 반드시 그러해야 한다."], ["He is thine, my children; deal justly by him.\"", "그는 너희 것이다, 내 자식들아. 그를 정의로이 다루어라.\""]], [["Not a limb was moved, nor was a breath drawn louder and longer than common, until the closing syllable of this final decree had passed the lips of Tamenund.", "이 마지막 판결의 끝 음절이 〈태머넌드〉의 입술을 떠나기까지, 사지 하나 움직이지 않았고, 여느 때보다 더 크고 긴 숨소리 하나 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["Then a cry of vengeance burst at once, as it might be, from the united lips of the nation; a frightful augury of their ruthless intentions.", "그러더니 마치 온 민족의 입이 하나로 합친 듯, 복수의 외침이 단번에 터져 나왔으니, 그들의 무자비한 의도를 알리는 무서운 전조였다."], ["In the midst of these prolonged and savage yells, a chief proclaimed, in a high voice, that the captive was condemned to endure the dreadful trial of torture by fire.", "이 길게 이어지는 야만적 함성 한가운데서, 한 추장이 드높은 목소리로, 포로가 불에 의한 고문이라는 무시무시한 시련을 견디도록 선고받았음을 공표했다."], ["The circle broke its order, and screams of delight mingled with the bustle and tumult of preparation.", "원이 그 질서를 무너뜨렸고, 환희의 비명이 준비의 부산함과 소란에 뒤섞였다."], ["Heyward struggled madly with his captors; the anxious eyes of Hawkeye began to look around him, with an expression of peculiar earnestness; and Cora again threw herself at the feet of the patriarch, once more a suppliant for mercy.", "〈헤이워드〉는 자기를 붙든 자들과 미친 듯이 몸부림쳤고, 〈호크아이〉의 근심 어린 눈은 유달리 진지한 표정으로 주위를 살피기 시작했으며, 〈코라〉는 다시 한번 자비를 구하는 탄원자가 되어 가부장의 발치에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["Throughout the whole of these trying moments, Uncas had alone preserved his serenity.", "이 모든 시련의 순간 내내, 〈운카스〉만이 홀로 그 평정을 지켰다."], ["He looked on the preparations with a steady eye, and when the tormentors came to seize him, he met them with a firm and upright attitude.", "그는 흔들림 없는 눈으로 준비를 지켜보았고, 고문하는 자들이 그를 붙잡으러 왔을 때, 굳건하고 꼿꼿한 자세로 그들을 맞았다."], ["One among them, if possible, more fierce and savage than his fellows, seized the hunting-shirt of the young warrior, and at a single effort tore it from his body.", "그중 하나가, 가능하다면, 제 동료들보다 더 사납고 야만스러웠는데, 젊은 전사의 사냥용 셔츠를 움켜쥐고는 단번에 그 몸에서 찢어 냈다."], ["Then, with a yell of frantic pleasure, he leaped towards his unresisting victim, and prepared to lead him to the stake.", "그러더니 광적인 환희의 함성을 지르며, 저항하지 않는 제물을 향해 뛰어들어, 그를 화형 기둥으로 끌고 갈 채비를 했다."], ["But, at that moment, when he appeared most a stranger to the feelings of humanity, the purpose of the savage was arrested as suddenly as if a supernatural agency had interposed in the behalf of Uncas.", "그러나 그가 인간의 정(情)에 가장 낯선 자로 보이던 바로 그 순간, 마치 초자연적인 힘이 〈운카스〉를 위해 끼어들기라도 한 듯 그 야만인의 목적이 돌연히 멎었다."], ["The eyeballs of the Delaware seemed to start from their sockets; his mouth opened, and his whole form became frozen in an attitude of amazement.", "그 〈델라웨어〉의 눈알이 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 입이 벌어졌으며, 온몸이 경악의 자세로 얼어붙었다."], ["Raising his hand with a slow and regulated motion, he pointed with a finger to the bosom of the captive.", "그는 느리고 절제된 동작으로 손을 들어, 한 손가락으로 포로의 가슴을 가리켰다."], ["His companions crowded about him in wonder, and every eye was, like his own, fastened intently on the figure of a small tortoise, beautifully tattooed on the breast of the prisoner, in a bright blue tint.", "그의 동료들이 의아해하며 그의 주위로 몰려들었고, 모든 눈이 그의 눈과 마찬가지로, 포로의 가슴에 선명한 푸른 빛으로 아름답게 문신된 작은 거북의 형상에 골똘히 못 박혔다."]], [["For a single instant Uncas enjoyed his triumph, smiling calmly on the scene.", "한순간 〈운카스〉는 그 광경을 향해 차분히 미소 지으며 자신의 승리를 만끽했다."], ["Then motioning the crowd away with a high and haughty sweep of his arm, he advanced in front of the nation with the air of a king, and spoke in a voice louder than the murmur of admiration that ran through the multitude.", "그러더니 드높고 오만한 팔의 휘두름으로 무리를 물리치고, 한 왕의 기품으로 민족 앞에 나아가, 무리 사이로 번지는 감탄의 웅성거림보다 더 큰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Men of the Lenni Lenape!\"", "\"〈레니 레나페〉의 사람들이여!\""], ["he said, \"my race upholds the earth!", "그가 말했다. \"내 종족은 대지를 떠받친다!"], ["Your feeble tribe stands on my shell![27] What fire that a Delaware can light would burn the child of my fathers,\"", "너희의 미약한 부족은 내 등딱지 위에 서 있다! 〈델라웨어〉가 지필 수 있는 그 어떤 불이 내 조상들의 자식을 태우겠는가,\""], ["he added, pointing proudly to the simple blazonry on his skin; \"the blood that came from such a stock would smother your flames!", "그가 제 살갗 위의 단순한 문장을 자랑스레 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"그런 혈통에서 나온 피는 너희의 불길을 꺼 버리리라!"], ["My race is the grandfather of nations!\"", "내 종족은 뭇 민족의 할아버지다!\""]], [["\"Who art thou?\"", "\"너는 누구냐?\""], ["demanded Tamenund, rising at the startling tones he heard, more than at any meaning conveyed by the language of the prisoner.", "〈태머넌드〉가, 포로의 말이 전하는 어떤 뜻에서보다도 자신이 들은 그 놀라운 어조에 더, 몸을 일으키며 물었다."]], [["\"Uncas, the son of Chingachgook,\"", "\"〈친가치국〉의 아들 〈운카스〉입니다,\""], ["answered the captive modestly, turning from the nation, and bending his head in reverence to the other's character and years; \"a son of the great Unamis.\"", "포로가 겸손히 대답하며, 민족에게서 돌아서, 상대의 인품과 연륜에 경의를 표하며 고개를 숙였다. \"위대한 〈우나미스〉의 아들입니다.\""]], [["\"The hour of Tamenund is nigh!\"", "\"〈태머넌드〉의 시각이 가까웠다!\""], ["exclaimed the sage; \"the day is come, at last, to the night!", "현인이 외쳤다. \"마침내 그 밤에 날이 밝았도다!"], ["I thank the Manitou, that one is here to fill my place at the council-fire.", "내 자리를 평의회의 불가에서 채울 자가 여기 있음을 〈마니토〉께 감사드린다."], ["Uncas, the child of Uncas, is found!", "〈운카스〉의 자식 〈운카스〉를 찾았다!"], ["Let the eyes of a dying eagle gaze on the rising sun.\"", "죽어 가는 독수리의 눈이 떠오르는 해를 바라보게 하라.\""]], [["The youth stepped lightly, but proudly, on the platform, where he became visible to the whole agitated and wondering multitude.", "그 젊은이는 가볍게, 그러나 자랑스럽게 단상으로 올라섰으니, 그곳에서 그는 동요하며 경이로워하는 온 무리에게 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Tamenund held him long at the length of his arm, and read every turn in the fine lineaments of his countenance, with the untiring gaze of one who recalled days of happiness.", "〈태머넌드〉는 그를 한참 동안 제 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓고는, 행복했던 날들을 떠올리는 자의 지칠 줄 모르는 응시로, 그 고운 얼굴의 이목구비 하나하나를 읽었다."]], [["\"Is Tamenund a boy?\"", "\"〈태머넌드〉가 소년인가?\""], ["at length the bewildered prophet exclaimed.", "마침내 어리둥절해진 예언자가 외쳤다."], ["\"Have I dreamt of so many snows--that my people were scattered like floating sands--of Yengeese, more plenty than the leaves on the trees!", "\"내가 그토록 많은 눈[雪]을 꿈꾸었던가 — 내 백성이 떠도는 모래처럼 흩어지고 — 〈잉기〉들이 나무의 잎사귀보다 더 많은 것을!"], ["The arrow of Tamenund would not frighten the fawn; his arm is withered like the branch of a dead oak; the snail would be swifter in the race; yet is Uncas before him as they went to battle against the pale-faces!", "〈태머넌드〉의 화살은 새끼 사슴조차 놀라게 하지 못하고, 그의 팔은 죽은 참나무 가지처럼 시들었으며, 달팽이가 달음박질에서 더 빠를 것이다. 그런데도 〈운카스〉는, 창백한 얼굴들에 맞서 싸우러 가던 그때 그 모습 그대로 그의 앞에 있구나!"], ["Uncas, the panther of his tribe, the eldest son of the Lenape, the wisest Sagamore of the Mohicans!", "〈운카스〉, 제 부족의 표범, 〈레나페〉의 맏아들, 모히칸의 가장 지혜로운 〈세이가모어〉!"], ["Tell me, ye Delawares, has Tamenund been a sleeper for a hundred winters?\"", "말하라, 〈델라웨어〉들이여, 〈태머넌드〉가 백 번의 겨울 동안 잠든 자였더란 말이냐?\""]], [["The calm and deep silence which succeeded these words, sufficiently announced the awful reverence with which his people received the communication of the patriarch.", "이 말 뒤에 이어진 차분하고 깊은 침묵은, 그의 백성이 가부장의 전언을 받아들인 그 외경의 두려움을 충분히 알리고도 남았다."], ["None dared to answer, though all listened in breathless expectation of what might follow.", "무슨 일이 뒤따를지 숨죽인 기대 속에 모두 귀를 기울였으나, 감히 답하는 자는 없었다."], ["Uncas, however, looking in his face with the fondness and veneration of a favored child, presumed on his own high and acknowledged rank, to reply.", "그러나 〈운카스〉는, 총애받는 자식의 다정함과 흠모로 그의 얼굴을 바라보며, 자신의 드높고 인정받은 지위를 빌려, 답해도 좋으리라 여겼다."]], [["\"Four warriors of his race have lived, and died,\"", "\"〈태머넌드〉의 벗이 전장에서 제 백성을 이끌던 이래로,\""], ["he said, \"since the friend of Tamenund led his people in battle.", "그가 말했다. \"그 종족의 전사 넷이 살다가 죽었습니다."], ["The blood of the turtle has been in many chiefs, but all have gone back into the earth from whence they came except Chingachgook and his son.\"", "거북의 피가 여러 추장에게 흘렀으나, 〈친가치국〉과 그 아들을 빼고는 모두 그들이 나온 대지로 돌아갔습니다.\""]], [["\"It is true--it is true,\"", "\"옳다 — 옳다,\""], ["returned the sage; a flash of recollection destroying all his pleasing fancies, and restoring him at once to a consciousness of the true history of his nation.", "현인이 응했으니, 한 줄기 기억의 섬광이 그의 모든 흐뭇한 환상을 깨뜨리고, 그를 단번에 제 민족의 참된 내력에 대한 자각으로 되돌려 놓았다."], ["\"Our wise men have often said that two warriors of the unchanged race were in the hills of the Yengeese; why have their seats at the council-fires of the Delawares been so long empty?\"", "\"우리 현자들은 변치 않은 종족의 전사 둘이 〈잉기〉들의 언덕에 있다고 자주 말했다. 어찌하여 〈델라웨어〉의 평의회 불가에 그들의 자리가 그토록 오래 비어 있었느냐?\""]], [["At these words the young man raised his head, which he had still kept bowed a little, in reverence; and lifting his voice so as to be heard by the multitude, as if to explain at once and forever the policy of his family, he said aloud,--", "이 말에 그 젊은이는, 경의를 표하느라 아직 조금 숙이고 있던 고개를 들었다. 그러고는 마치 제 가문의 방책을 단숨에 그리고 영원히 설명하려는 듯, 무리에게 들리도록 목소리를 높여, 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Once we slept where we could hear the salt lake speak in its anger.", "\"한때 우리는 짠 호수가 노여움에 차 말하는 소리를 들을 수 있는 곳에서 잠들었습니다."], ["Then we were rulers and sagamores over the land.", "그때 우리는 그 땅의 지배자요 〈세이가모어〉였습니다."], ["But when a pale-face was seen on every brook, we followed the deer back to the river of our nation.", "그러나 시내마다 창백한 얼굴이 보이게 되자, 우리는 사슴을 따라 우리 민족의 강으로 돌아갔습니다."], ["The Delawares were gone.", "〈델라웨어〉들은 떠나고 없었습니다."], ["Few warriors of them all stayed to drink of the stream they loved.", "그들 모두 중에 자기네가 사랑하던 물줄기를 마시러 남은 전사는 몇 되지 않았습니다."], ["Then said my fathers, 'Here will we hunt.", "그때 제 조상들이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["The waters of the river go into the salt lake.", "'여기서 우리는 사냥하리라."], ["If we go towards the setting sun, we shall find streams that run into the great lakes of sweet water; there would a Mohican die, like fishes of the sea, in the clear springs.", "이 강의 물은 짠 호수로 흘러든다. 우리가 지는 해를 향해 가면, 단물의 큰 호수들로 흘러드는 시내를 찾으리니, 거기서 모히칸은, 맑은 샘에서 바다의 물고기처럼 죽으리라."], ["When the Manitou is ready, and shall say \"Come,\"", "〈마니토〉께서 준비되시어 \"오라\""], ["we will follow the river to the sea, and take our own again.'", "하시면, 우리는 강을 따라 바다로 가서, 우리 것을 되찾으리라.'"], ["Such, Delawares, is the belief of the children of the Turtle.", "〈델라웨어〉들이여, 거북의 자손들이 믿는 바가 그러합니다."], ["Our eyes are on the rising, and not towards the setting sun.", "우리의 눈은 떠오르는 해에 있지, 지는 해를 향하지 않습니다."], ["We know whence he comes, but we know not whither he goes.", "우리는 그가 어디서 오는지는 알아도, 어디로 가는지는 모릅니다."], ["It is enough.\"", "그것으로 충분합니다.\""]], [["The men of the Lenape listened to his words with all the respect that superstition could lend, finding a secret charm even in the figurative language with which the young Sagamore imparted his ideas.", "〈레나페〉의 사람들은 미신이 더할 수 있는 온갖 경의로 그의 말에 귀를 기울이며, 그 젊은 〈세이가모어〉가 제 생각을 전하는 비유적인 언어에서조차 은밀한 매혹을 느꼈다."], ["Uncas himself watched the effect of his brief explanation with intelligent eyes, and gradually dropped the air of authority he had assumed, as he perceived that his auditors were content.", "〈운카스〉 자신은 영민한 눈으로 자신의 간략한 설명이 미친 효과를 지켜보다가, 청중이 흡족해함을 알아채자 점차 자신이 취했던 권위의 기색을 내려놓았다."], ["Then permitting his looks to wander over the silent throng that crowded around the elevated seat of Tamenund, he first perceived Hawkeye in his bonds.", "그러더니 〈태머넌드〉의 높은 자리를 에워싼 말없는 군중 위로 시선을 거닐게 하다가, 비로소 결박당한 〈호크아이〉를 알아보았다."], ["Stepping eagerly from his stand, he made way for himself to the side of his friend; and cutting his thongs with a quick and angry stroke of his own knife, he motioned to the crowd to divide.", "그는 자기 자리에서 다급히 내려서서, 제 벗의 곁으로 길을 헤치고 나아가, 자신의 칼을 빠르고 성난 일격으로 휘둘러 그 가죽끈을 끊고는, 무리에게 갈라서라고 손짓했다."], ["The Indians silently obeyed, and once more they stood ranged in their circle, as before his appearance among them.", "인디언들은 말없이 따랐고, 그가 그들 사이에 나타나기 전처럼 다시 한번 원을 지어 늘어섰다."], ["Uncas took the scout by the hand, and led him to the feet of the patriarch.", "〈운카스〉는 척후의 손을 잡고, 그를 가부장의 발치로 이끌었다."]], [["\"Father,\"", "\"아버지,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"look at this pale-face; a just man, and the friend of the Delawares.\"", "\"이 창백한 얼굴을 보십시오. 정의로운 사람이요, 〈델라웨어〉의 벗입니다.\""]], [["\"Is he a son of Minquon?\"", "\"그는 〈민쿠언〉의 아들인가?\""]], [["\"Not so; a warrior known to the Yengeese, and feared by the Maquas.\"", "\"그렇지 않습니다. 〈잉기〉들에게 알려졌고, 〈마쿠아〉들이 두려워하는 전사입니다.\""]], [["\"What name has he gained by his deeds?\"", "\"그는 제 행적으로 어떤 이름을 얻었느냐?\""]], [["\"We call him Hawkeye,\"", "\"우리는 그를 〈호크아이〉라 부릅니다,\""], ["Uncas replied, using the Delaware phrase; \"for his sight never fails.", "〈운카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉 말을 써서 대답했다. \"그의 시야는 결코 어긋나는 법이 없기 때문입니다."], ["The Mingos know him better by the death he gives their warriors; with them he is 'The Long Rifle.'\"", "〈밍고〉들은 그가 자기네 전사들에게 안기는 죽음으로 그를 더 잘 압니다. 그들에게 그는 '긴 소총'입니다.\""]], [["\"La Longue Carabine!\"", "\"〈라 롱그 카라빈〉!\""], ["exclaimed Tamenund, opening his eyes, and regarding the scout sternly.", "〈태머넌드〉가 눈을 뜨고 척후를 준엄하게 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["\"My son has not done well to call him friend.\"", "\"내 아들이 그를 벗이라 부른 것은 잘한 일이 아니다.\""]], [["\"I call him so who proves himself such,\"", "\"저는 스스로 그러함을 증명하는 자를 그렇게 부릅니다,\""], ["returned the young chief, with great calmness, but with a steady mien.", "젊은 추장이 더없이 차분하나 굳건한 태도로 응했다."], ["\"If Uncas is welcome among the Delawares, then is Hawkeye with his friends.\"", "\"〈운카스〉가 〈델라웨어〉들 사이에서 환영받는다면, 〈호크아이〉 또한 제 벗들과 함께 있는 것입니다.\""]], [["\"The pale-face has slain my young men; his name is great for the blows he has struck the Lenape.\"", "\"그 창백한 얼굴은 내 젊은이들을 죽였다. 그의 이름은 〈레나페〉에게 가한 일격들로 자자하다.\""]], [["\"If a Mingo has whispered that much in the ear of the Delaware, he has only shown that he is a singing-bird,\"", "\"〈밍고〉가 그만큼을 〈델라웨어〉의 귀에 속삭였다면, 그는 그저 노래하는 새임을 보였을 뿐입니다,\""], ["said the scout, who now believed that it was time to vindicate himself from such offensive charges, and who spoke in the tongue of the man he addressed, modifying his Indian figures, however, with his own peculiar notions.", "척후가 말했으니, 이제 그러한 모욕적인 비난에서 스스로를 변호할 때라 여긴 그는, 자신이 말 건네는 자의 혀로 말하되, 그 인디언식 비유를 자기 특유의 관념으로 손질해 가며 말했다."], ["\"That I have slain the Maquas I am not the man to deny, even at their own council-fires; but that, knowingly, my hand has ever harmed a Delaware, is opposed to the reason of my gifts, which is friendly to them, and all that belongs to their nation.\"", "\"내가 〈마쿠아〉들을 죽였다는 것은, 그들 자신의 평의회 불가에서라도 부인할 내가 아니오. 그러나 알고서, 내 손이 단 한 번이라도 〈델라웨어〉를 해친 적이 있다는 것은, 그들과 그들 민족에 속한 모든 것에 우호적인, 내 천성의 이치에 어긋나는 일이오.\""]], [["A low exclamation of applause passed among the warriors, who exchanged looks with each other like men that first began to perceive their error.", "전사들 사이로 나직한 갈채의 탄성이 번졌으니, 그들은 비로소 자기네 잘못을 깨닫기 시작한 자들처럼 서로 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["\"Where is the Huron?\"", "\"그 〈휴런〉은 어디 있느냐?\""], ["demanded Tamenund.", "〈태머넌드〉가 물었다."], ["\"Has he stopped my ears?\"", "\"그가 내 귀를 막았느냐?\""]], [["Magua, whose feelings during that scene in which Uncas had triumphed may be much better imagined than described, answered to the call by stepping boldly in front of the patriarch.", "〈운카스〉가 승리를 거둔 그 장면 동안 〈머과〉가 느낀 심정이란, 묘사하기보다는 짐작하는 편이 훨씬 나을 것이거니와, 그는 가부장 앞으로 대담하게 걸어 나와 그 부름에 답했다."]], [["\"The just Tamenund,\"", "\"정의로운 〈태머넌드〉께서는,\""], ["he said, \"will not keep what a Huron has lent.\"", "그가 말했다. \"〈휴런〉이 빌려준 것을 차지하지 않으실 것입니다.\""]], [["\"Tell me, son of my brother,\"", "\"말하라, 내 형제의 아들아,\""], ["returned the sage, avoiding the dark countenance of Le Subtil, and turning gladly to the more ingenuous features of Uncas, \"has the stranger a conqueror's right over you?\"", "현인이 〈르 쉬브틸〉의 어두운 얼굴을 피하고, 〈운카스〉의 더 솔직한 이목구비로 기꺼이 돌아서며 응했다. \"저 이방인이 네게 정복자의 권리를 지녔느냐?\""]], [["\"He has none.", "\"그에게는 아무 권리도 없습니다."], ["The panther may get into snares set by the women; but he is strong, and knows how to leap through them.\"", "표범이 여인들이 놓은 덫에 걸려들 수는 있어도, 그는 강하여 그것을 뛰어넘어 빠져나갈 줄 압니다.\""]], [["\"La Longue Carabine?\"", "\"〈라 롱그 카라빈〉은?\""]], [["\"Laughs at the Mingoes.", "\"〈밍고〉들을 비웃습니다."], ["Go, Huron, ask your squaws the color of a bear.\"", "가라, 〈휴런〉아, 곰의 빛깔이 어떤지 네 아낙들에게 물어보아라.\""]], [["\"The stranger and the white maiden that came into my camp together?\"", "\"내 진영으로 함께 들어온 그 이방인과 흰 처녀는?\""]], [["\"Should journey on an open path.\"", "\"트인 길로 나아가야 합니다.\""]], [["\"And the woman that Huron left with my warriors?\"", "\"그러면 〈휴런〉이 내 전사들에게 맡긴 그 여인은?\""]], [["Uncas made no reply.", "〈운카스〉는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"And the woman that the Mingo has brought into my camp,\"", "\"그러니까 〈밍고〉가 내 진영으로 데려온 그 여인 말이다,\""], ["repeated Tamenund, gravely.", "〈태머넌드〉가 엄숙하게 되풀이했다."]], [["\"She is mine,\"", "\"그녀는 내 것입니다,\""], ["cried Magua, shaking his hand in triumph at Uncas.", "〈머과〉가 〈운카스〉를 향해 의기양양하게 손을 흔들며 외쳤다."], ["\"Mohican, you know that she is mine.\"", "\"모히칸, 너는 그녀가 내 것임을 알 것이다.\""]], [["\"My son is silent,\"", "\"내 아들이 말이 없구나,\""], ["said Tamenund, endeavoring to read the expression of the face that the youth turned from him in sorrow.", "〈태머넌드〉가, 그 젊은이가 슬픔에 잠겨 제게서 돌린 얼굴의 표정을 읽으려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["\"It is so,\"", "\"그러합니다,\""], ["was the low answer.", "나직한 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["A short and impressive pause succeeded, during which it was very apparent with what reluctance the multitude admitted the justice of the Mingo's claim.", "짧고도 묵직한 침묵이 뒤따랐으니, 그동안 무리가 〈밍고〉의 주장의 정당함을 얼마나 마지못해 인정하는지가 똑똑히 드러났다."], ["At length the sage, in whom alone the decision depended, said, in a firm voice,--", "마침내, 그 결정이 오로지 자신에게만 달린 현인이 굳은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Huron, depart.\"", "\"〈휴런〉이여, 떠나라.\""]], [["\"As he came, just Tamenund,\"", "\"제가 온 그대로입니까, 정의로운 〈태머넌드〉여,\""], ["demanded the wily Magua; \"or with hands filled with the faith of the Delawares?", "교활한 〈머과〉가 물었다. \"아니면 〈델라웨어〉들의 신의로 두 손을 가득 채워서입니까?"], ["The wigwam of Le Renard Subtil is empty.", "〈르 르나르 쉬브틸〉의 천막은 비어 있습니다."], ["Make him strong with his own.\"", "그를 그의 것으로 강하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["The aged man mused with himself for a time; and then bending his head towards one of his venerable companions, he asked,--", "노인은 잠시 혼자 생각에 잠겼다. 그러더니 그 존엄한 동료들 중 하나에게 고개를 기울이며 물었다."]], [["\"Are my ears open?\"", "\"내 귀가 열려 있는가?\""]], [["\"It is true.\"", "\"그러합니다.\""]], [["\"Is this Mingo a chief?\"", "\"이 〈밍고〉가 추장인가?\""]], [["\"The first in his nation.\"", "\"제 민족에서 으뜸가는 자입니다.\""]], [["\"Girl, what wouldst thou?", "\"처녀여, 너는 무엇을 바라느냐?"], ["A great warrior takes thee to wife.", "위대한 전사가 너를 아내로 맞으려 한다."], ["Go!", "가라!"], ["thy race will not end.\"", "네 혈통은 끊기지 않으리라.\""]], [["\"Better, a thousand times, it should,\"", "\"그런 치욕을 당하느니,\""], ["exclaimed the horror-struck Cora, \"than meet with such a degradation!\"", "공포에 사로잡힌 〈코라〉가 외쳤다. \"천 번이라도 끊기는 편이 낫습니다!\""]], [["\"Huron, her mind is in the tents of her fathers.", "\"〈휴런〉이여, 그녀의 마음은 제 조상들의 천막에 있다."], ["An unwilling maiden makes an unhappy wigwam.\"", "마음 없는 처녀는 불행한 천막을 만든다.\""]], [["\"She speaks with the tongue of her people,\"", "\"그녀는 제 백성의 혀로 말합니다,\""], ["returned Magua, regarding his victim with a look of bitter irony.", "〈머과〉가 쓰디쓴 빈정거림의 눈길로 제 제물을 바라보며 응했다."], ["\"She is of a race of traders, and will bargain for a bright look.", "\"그녀는 장사치 종족이라, 밝은 눈빛 하나를 두고도 흥정할 것입니다."], ["Let Tamenund speak the words.\"", "〈태머넌드〉께서 그 말씀을 해 주십시오.\""]], [["\"Take you the wampum, and our love.\"", "\"〈웜펌〉과 우리의 사랑을 가져가라.\""]], [["\"Nothing hence but what Magua brought hither.\"", "\"〈머과〉가 여기 가져온 것 말고는 아무것도 안 됩니다.\""]], [["\"Then depart with thine own.", "\"그렇다면 네 것을 가지고 떠나라."], ["The great Manitou forbids that a Delaware should be unjust.\"", "위대한 〈마니토〉께서는 〈델라웨어〉가 불의해지는 것을 금하신다.\""]], [["Magua advanced, and seized his captive strongly by the arm; the Delawares fell back, in silence; and Cora, as if conscious that remonstrance would be useless, prepared to submit to her fate without resistance.", "〈머과〉가 앞으로 나아가, 제 포로의 팔을 억세게 움켜쥐었다. 〈델라웨어〉들은 말없이 물러섰고, 〈코라〉는 항변이 헛되리라는 것을 자각한 듯, 저항 없이 제 운명에 따를 채비를 했다."]], [["\"Hold, hold!\"", "\"멈추시오, 멈추시오!\""], ["cried Duncan, springing forward; \"Huron, have mercy!", "〈덩컨〉이 앞으로 뛰어나오며 외쳤다. \"〈휴런〉, 자비를 베푸시오!"], ["her ransom shall make thee richer than any of thy people were ever yet known to be.\"", "그녀의 몸값이 당신을, 당신네 백성 중 일찍이 알려진 그 누구보다도 부유하게 만들어 줄 것이오.\""]], [["\"Magua is a redskin; he wants not the beads of the pale-faces.\"", "\"〈머과〉는 붉은 살갗을 지닌 자다. 창백한 얼굴들의 구슬 따위는 바라지 않는다.\""]], [["\"Gold, silver, powder, lead--all that a warrior needs shall be in thy wigwam; all that becomes the greatest chief.\"", "\"금이든, 은이든, 화약이든, 납이든 — 전사에게 필요한 모든 것이 당신 천막에 있게 될 것이오. 가장 위대한 추장에게 걸맞은 모든 것이 말이오.\""]], [["\"Le Subtil is very strong,\"", "\"〈르 쉬브틸〉은 매우 강하다,\""], ["cried Magua, violently shaking the hand which grasped the unresisting arm of Cora; \"he has his revenge!\"", "〈머과〉가 〈코라〉의 저항 없는 팔을 움켜쥔 손을 거칠게 흔들며 외쳤다. \"그는 복수를 이루었다!\""]], [["\"Mighty ruler of providence!\"", "\"섭리를 다스리시는 전능하신 분이여!\""], ["exclaimed Heyward, clasping his hands together in agony, \"can this be suffered!", "〈헤이워드〉가 고뇌 속에 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"이런 일을 어찌 용납하실 수 있단 말입니까!"], ["To you, just Tamenund, I appeal for mercy.\"", "당신께, 정의로운 〈태머넌드〉여, 저는 자비를 호소합니다.\""]], [["\"The words of the Delaware are said,\"", "\"〈델라웨어〉의 말은 끝났다,\""], ["returned the sage, closing his eyes, and dropping back into his seat, alike wearied with his mental and his bodily exertion.", "현인이 두 눈을 감고 제 자리로 다시 주저앉으며 응했으니, 정신의 노고와 육신의 노고에 똑같이 지쳐 있었다."], ["\"Men speak not twice.\"", "\"사람은 두 번 말하지 않는다.\""]], [["\"That a chief should not misspend his time in unsaying what had once been spoken, is wise and reasonable,\"", "\"추장이 한번 내뱉은 말을 거두느라 제 시간을 허비해서는 안 된다는 것은 지혜롭고 사리에 맞는 말이오,\""], ["said Hawkeye, motioning to Duncan to be silent; \"but it is also prudent in every warrior to consider well before he strikes his tomahawk into the head of his prisoner.", "〈호크아이〉가 〈덩컨〉에게 잠자코 있으라 손짓하며 말했다. \"그러나 어느 전사든 제 포로의 머리에 손도끼를 박기 전에 잘 헤아려 보는 것 또한 신중한 일이오."], ["Huron, I love you not; nor can I say that any Mingo has ever received much favor at my hands.", "〈휴런〉, 나는 당신을 좋아하지 않소. 또한 어떤 〈밍고〉가 내 손에서 큰 호의를 받은 적이 있다고도 말할 수 없소."], ["It is fair to conclude that, if this war does not soon end, many more of your warriors will meet me in the woods.", "이 전쟁이 곧 끝나지 않는다면, 당신네 전사들이 숲에서 나를 더 많이 만나게 되리라 짐작하는 것이 온당하오."], ["Put it to your judgment, then, whether you would prefer taking such a prisoner as that into your encampment, or one like myself, who am a man that it would greatly rejoice your nation to see with naked hands.\"", "그러니 당신의 판단에 맡겨 보시오. 저런 포로를 당신 진영으로 데려가는 편이 낫겠소, 아니면 나 같은 자를, 곧 당신네 민족이 맨손으로 보면 크게 기뻐할 그런 사내를 데려가는 편이 낫겠소?\""]], [["\"Will 'The Long Rifle' give his life for the woman?\"", "\"'긴 소총'이 그 여인을 위해 제 목숨을 내놓겠다는 말이냐?\""], ["demanded Magua, hesitatingly; for he had already made a motion towards quitting the place with his victim.", "〈머과〉가 머뭇거리며 물었으니, 그는 이미 제 제물을 데리고 그 자리를 뜨려는 몸짓을 한 터였다."]], [["\"No, no; I have not said so much as that,\"", "\"아니, 아니. 그렇게까지 말한 건 아니오,\""], ["returned Hawkeye, drawing back with suitable discretion, when he noted the eagerness with which Magua listened to his proposal.", "〈호크아이〉가, 〈머과〉가 자신의 제안에 귀 기울이는 그 열의를 알아채고는, 걸맞은 신중함으로 물러서며 응했다."], ["\"It would be an unequal exchange, to give a warrior, in the prime of his age and usefulness, for the best woman on the frontiers.", "\"한창때라 쓸모 많은 전사를, 변경에서 가장 빼어난 여인과 맞바꾼다면 공평치 못한 거래일 테니."], ["I might consent to go into winter-quarters, now--at least six weeks afore the leaves will turn--on condition you will release the maiden.\"", "다만, 당신이 그 처녀를 풀어 준다는 조건이라면, 지금 — 적어도 잎이 물들기 여섯 주 전인 지금 — 겨울 막사로 들어가는 데는 동의할 수도 있겠소.\""]], [["Magua shook his head, and made an impatient sign for the crowd to open.", "〈머과〉는 고개를 가로젓고, 무리에게 길을 비키라고 초조한 신호를 보냈다."]], [["\"Well, then,\"", "\"음, 그렇다면,\""], ["added the scout, with the musing air of a man who had not half made up his mind, \"I will throw 'Killdeer'", "척후가 아직 마음을 반도 정하지 못한 자의 생각에 잠긴 기색으로 덧붙였다."], ["into the bargain.", "\"흥정에 '킬디어'까지 얹겠소."], ["Take the word of an experienced hunter, the piece has not its equal atween the provinces.\"", "노련한 사냥꾼의 말을 믿으시오. 이 총은 이 지방들을 통틀어 견줄 것이 없소.\""]], [["Magua still disdained to reply, continuing his efforts to disperse the crowd.", "〈머과〉는 여전히 답하기를 마다하며, 무리를 흩어지게 하려는 노력을 이어 갔다."]], [["\"Perhaps,\"", "\"어쩌면,\""], ["added the scout, losing his dissembled coolness, exactly in proportion as the other manifested an indifference to the exchange, \"if I should condition to teach your young men the real virtue of the we'pon, it would smooth the little differences in our judgments.\"", "척후가, 상대가 그 교환에 무관심을 드러내는 그만큼에 정확히 비례해, 짐짓 꾸민 침착함을 잃어 가며 덧붙였다. \"당신네 젊은이들에게 그 무기의 참된 묘를 가르쳐 주겠다고 약조한다면, 우리 판단 사이의 그 사소한 차이가 매끄러워질지도 모르겠소.\""]], [["Le Renard fiercely ordered the Delawares, who still lingered in an impenetrable belt around him, in hopes he would listen to the amicable proposal, to open his path, threatening, by the glance of his eye, another appeal to the infallible justice of their \"prophet.\"", "〈르 르나르〉는, 그가 그 우호적인 제안에 귀 기울이기를 바라며 여전히 꿰뚫을 수 없는 띠를 이루어 그를 둘러싼 채 머뭇거리는 〈델라웨어〉들에게, 제 길을 비키라고 사납게 명했으니, 눈빛 하나로 그들 '예언자'의 어김없는 정의에 다시 호소하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."]], [["\"What is ordered must sooner or later arrive,\"", "\"명해진 것은 늦든 빠르든 닥치고야 마는 법이지,\" 〈호크아이〉가 슬프고 풀죽은 얼굴로 〈운카스〉에게 돌아서며 말을 이었다."], ["continued Hawkeye, turning with a sad and humbled look to Uncas.", "\"저 악당은 제 잇속을 알고, 그것을 놓치지 않을 게다! 신의 가호가 있기를, 얘야."], ["\"The varlet knows his advantage, and will keep it!", "너는 네 타고난 혈육 사이에서 벗들을 찾았으니, 인디언의 피라곤 섞이지 않았으면서도 네가 만난 어떤 이들만큼이나 그들이 참되기를 바란다. 나로 말하면, 늦든 빠르든 죽어야 할 몸."], ["God bless you, boy; you have found friends among your natural kin and I hope they will prove as true as some you have met who had no Indian cross.", "그러니 내 죽음을 곡할 자가 몇 안 되는 것이 다행이지. 어쨌거나 저 악귀들은 어떻게든 내 머릿가죽을 차지하고 말았을 테니, 하루이틀 차이야 영원한 시간의 셈에서 그리 큰 차이도 아닐 게다."], ["As for me, sooner or later, I must die; it is therefore fortunate there are but few to make my death-howl.", "신의 가호가 있기를,\" 그 거친 숲사람이 고개를 한쪽으로 숙였다가, 이내 다시 방향을 바꿔 그 젊은이를 향해 애틋한 눈길을 던지며 덧붙였다."], ["After all, it is likely the imps would have managed to master my scalp, so a day or two will make no great difference in the everlasting reckoning of time.", "\"나는 너와 네 아비를 둘 다 사랑했다, 〈운카스〉. 우리 살갗이 온전히 같은 빛깔은 아니고, 우리 천성이 다소 다르긴 해도 말이다."], ["God bless you,\"", "〈세이가모어〉에게 전해라, 내 가장 큰 곤경 속에서도 그를 결코 시야에서 놓치지 않았다고."], ["added the rugged woodsman, bending his head aside, and then instantly changing its direction again, with a wistful look towards the youth; \"I loved both you and your father, Uncas, though our skins are not altogether of a color, and our gifts are somewhat different.", "그리고 너로 말하면, 운 좋은 흔적을 좇을 때면 가끔 나를 떠올려라. 그리고 명심해라, 얘야, 천국이 하나든 둘이든, 저세상에는 정직한 사람들이 다시 만날 수 있는 길이 있단다."], ["Tell the Sagamore I never lost sight of him in my greatest trouble; and, as for you, think of me sometimes when on a lucky trail; and depend on it, boy, whether there be one heaven or two, there is a path in the other world by which honest men may come together again.", "소총은 우리가 숨겨 둔 자리에서 찾게 될 게다. 그것을 가지거라, 그리고 나를 위해 간직해라."], ["You'll find the rifle in the place we hid it; take it, and keep it for my sake; and harkee, lad, as your natural gifts don't deny you the use of vengeance, use it a little freely on the Mingos; it may unburden grief at my loss, and ease your mind.", "그리고 들어라, 얘야, 네 타고난 천성이 복수를 마다하지 않으니, 〈밍고〉들에게 그것을 좀 거리낌 없이 쓰거라. 그것이 나를 잃은 슬픔을 덜고, 네 마음을 가볍게 해 줄지도 모른다."], ["Huron, I accept your offer; release the woman.", "〈휴런〉, 나는 당신의 제안을 받아들이겠소. 그 여인을 풀어 주시오."], ["I am your prisoner!\"", "내가 당신의 포로요!\""]], [["A suppressed, but still distinct murmur of approbation, ran through the crowd at this generous proposition; even the fiercest among the Delaware warriors manifesting pleasure at the manliness of the intended sacrifice.", "이 너그러운 제안에, 억눌렸으되 여전히 또렷한 찬동의 웅성거림이 무리 사이로 번졌으니, 〈델라웨어〉 전사들 중 가장 사나운 자들마저 그 의도된 희생의 사내다움에 흡족함을 드러냈다."], ["Magua paused, and for an anxious moment, it might be said, he doubted; then casting his eyes on Cora, with an expression in which ferocity and admiration were strangely mingled, his purpose became fixed forever.", "〈머과〉는 멈칫했고, 한순간 근심에 차, 그가 망설였다고 해도 좋을 듯했다. 그러더니 흉포함과 감탄이 기이하게 뒤섞인 표정으로 〈코라〉에게 눈길을 던지자, 그의 목적은 영원히 굳어졌다."]], [["He intimated his contempt of the offer with a backward motion of his head, and said, in a steady and settled voice,--", "그는 고개를 뒤로 젖히는 동작으로 그 제안에 대한 경멸을 내비치며, 굳건하고 확고한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"Le Renard Subtil is a great chief; he has but one mind.", "\"〈르 르나르 쉬브틸〉은 위대한 추장이다. 그에게는 오직 하나의 마음뿐."], ["Come,\"", "가자,\" 그가 제 포로를 앞으로 재촉하느라 너무 허물없이 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 덧붙였다."], ["he added, laying his hand too familiarly on the shoulder of his captive to urge her onward; \"a Huron is no tattler; we will go.\"", "\"〈휴런〉은 수다쟁이가 아니다. 우리는 가겠다.\""]], [["The maiden drew back in lofty womanly reserve, and her dark eye kindled, while the rich blood shot, like the passing brightness of the sun, into her very temples, at the indignity.", "처녀는 드높은 여인의 자존심으로 몸을 뒤로 빼었고, 그 모욕에 검은 눈이 타올랐으며, 짙은 피가 스쳐 지나가는 햇살처럼 그녀의 관자놀이까지 솟구쳤다."]], [["\"I am your prisoner, and at a fitting time shall be ready to follow, even to my death.", "\"나는 당신의 포로이며, 때가 되면 죽음까지라도 따를 준비가 되어 있소. 그러나 폭력은 필요치 않소,\""], ["But violence is unnecessary,\"", "그녀가 차갑게 말했다."], ["she coldly said; and immediately turning to Hawkeye, added, \"Generous hunter!", "그러고는 곧장 〈호크아이〉에게 돌아서서 덧붙였다. \"너그러운 사냥꾼이여!"], ["from my soul I thank you.", "진심으로 당신께 감사드립니다."], ["Your offer is in vain, neither could it be accepted; but still you may serve me, even more than in your own noble intention.", "당신의 제안은 헛되며, 받아들여질 수도 없을 것입니다. 그러나 당신은 그 고귀한 뜻에서보다도 더, 저를 도와주실 수 있습니다."], ["Look at that drooping, humbled child!", "저 풀이 죽어 고개 숙인 아이를 보십시오!"], ["Abandon her not until you leave her in the habitation of civilized men.", "그 아이를 문명한 사람들의 거처에 데려다 놓을 때까지 결코 저버리지 마십시오. 그 아이의 아버지가 당신께 보답하리라고는 — 당신 같은 분은 사람의 보답을 초월한 분이니 — 말하지 않겠습니다,\""], ["I will not say,\"", "그녀가 척후의 거친 손을 부여잡으며 말했다."], ["wringing the hard hand of the scout, \"that her father will reward you--for such as you are above the rewards of men--but he will thank you, and bless you.", "\"그러나 그분은 당신께 감사하고, 당신을 축복하실 것입니다. 그리고 믿어 주십시오, 정의로운 노인의 축복은 하늘이 보시기에 효험이 있습니다."], ["And, believe me, the blessing of a just and aged man has virtue in the sight of Heaven.", "이 끔찍한 순간에, 그분의 입에서 그 한마디를 들을 수만 있다면!\" 그녀의 목소리가 메었고, 한순간 그녀는 말이 없었다."], ["Would to God, I could hear one from his lips at this awful moment!\"", "그러더니 정신을 잃은 제 동생을 부축하고 있는 〈덩컨〉에게 한 걸음 더 가까이 다가서며, 한층 가라앉은 어조로, 그러나 그 안에서 감정과 여인다운 습성이 두려운 다툼을 벌이는 채로 말을 이었다. \"당신이 갖게 될 그 보물을 소중히 아끼라는 말은 굳이 할 필요도 없겠지요."], ["Her voice became choked, and, for an instant, she was silent; then advancing a step nigher to Duncan, who was supporting her unconscious sister, she continued, in more subdued tones, but in which feeling and the habits of her sex maintained a fearful struggle,--\"I need not tell you to cherish the treasure you will possess.", "당신은 그 아이를 사랑하십니다, 〈헤이워드〉. 그것이, 설령 그 아이에게 결점이 있다 한들, 천 가지 허물을 가려 줄 것입니다."], ["You love her, Heyward; that would conceal a thousand faults, though she had them.", "그 아이는 인간으로서 그럴 수 있는 한 친절하고, 온화하고, 다정하고, 선합니다. 마음에도 몸에도, 당신네 가운데 가장 자존심 높은 이조차 역겨워할 흠이라곤 하나도 없습니다."], ["She is kind, gentle, sweet, good, as mortal may be.", "그 아이는 곱습니다 — 아! 더없이 빼어나게 곱지요!\""], ["There is not a blemish in mind or person at which the proudest of you all would sicken.", "그녀가 제 아름다우나 덜 눈부신 손을 〈앨리스〉의 설화석고 같은 이마에 우수에 찬 애정으로 얹고, 그 눈썹가에 엉긴 금빛 머리칼을 갈라 주며 말했다."], ["She is fair--O! how surpassingly fair!\"", "\"그러면서도 그 아이의 영혼은 그 살갗만큼이나 순수하고 티가 없습니다!"], ["laying her own beautiful, but less brilliant hand, in melancholy affection on the alabaster forehead of Alice, and parting the golden hair which clustered about her brows; \"and yet her soul is pure and spotless as her skin!", "저는 많은 말을 할 수도 있겠지요 — 어쩌면, 더 냉철한 이성이 마땅하다 여길 것보다 더 많은 말을. 그러나 당신과 저 자신을 아끼겠습니다 ─\""], ["I could say much--more, perhaps, than cooler reason would approve; but I will spare you and myself\"--Her voice became inaudible, and her face was bent over the form of her sister.", "그녀의 목소리는 들리지 않게 되었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 동생의 몸 위로 숙여졌다. 길고 뜨거운 입맞춤 뒤에, 그녀는 일어섰다."], ["After a long and burning kiss, she arose, and with features of the hue of death, but without even a tear in her feverish eye, she turned away, and added, to the savage, with all her former elevation of manner,--\"Now, sir, if it be your pleasure, I will follow.\"", "그러고는 죽음의 빛을 띤 얼굴로, 그러나 열에 들뜬 눈에 눈물 한 방울 없이, 돌아서서, 예전의 그 드높은 태도를 고스란히 되찾고 그 야만인에게 덧붙였다. \"자, 그럼, 그러는 것이 당신 뜻이라면, 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["\"Ay, go,\"", "\"그래, 가라,\""], ["cried Duncan, placing Alice in the arms of an Indian girl; \"go, Magua, go.", "〈덩컨〉이 〈앨리스〉를 한 인디언 처녀의 품에 맡기며 외쳤다. \"가라, 〈머과〉, 가라."], ["These Delawares have their laws, which forbid them to detain you; but I--I have no such obligation.", "이 〈델라웨어〉들에게는 그들의 법이 있어 너를 붙잡는 것을 금하지만, 나는 — 나는 그런 의무가 없다."], ["Go, malignant monster--why do you delay?\"", "가라, 악독한 괴물아 — 어찌하여 꾸물대느냐?\""]], [["It would be difficult to describe the expression with which Magua listened to this threat to follow.", "따라오겠다는 이 위협을 〈머과〉가 어떤 표정으로 들었는지는 묘사하기 어려울 것이다."], ["There was at first a fierce and manifest display of joy, and then it was instantly subdued in a look of cunning coldness.", "처음에는 사납고도 또렷한 환희의 빛이 있었으나, 이내 교활한 냉담의 눈빛 속으로 즉시 가라앉았다."]], [["\"The woods are open,\"", "\"숲은 열려 있다,\""], ["he was content with answering.", "그는 이렇게 답하는 것으로 만족했다."], ["\"'The Open Hand' can come.\"", "\"'펼친 손'은 와도 좋다.\""]], [["\"Hold,\"", "\"멈추시오,\""], ["cried Hawkeye, seizing Duncan by the arm, and detaining him by violence; \"you know not the craft of the imp.", "〈호크아이〉가 〈덩컨〉의 팔을 붙잡고 억지로 붙들어 두며 외쳤다. \"당신은 저 악귀의 간계를 모르오."], ["He would lead you to an ambushment, and your death--\"", "그는 당신을 매복지로, 당신의 죽음으로 이끌 것이오 ─\""]], [["\"Huron,\"", "\"〈휴런〉,\""], ["interrupted Uncas, who, submissive to the stern customs of his people, had been an attentive and grave listener to all that passed; \"Huron, the justice of the Delawares comes from the Manitou.", "제 민족의 준엄한 관습에 순종하여, 오가는 모든 말에 주의 깊고 진중한 청자로 있던 〈운카스〉가 끼어들었다. \"〈휴런〉, 〈델라웨어〉들의 정의는 〈마니토〉에게서 온다."], ["Look at the sun.", "해를 보아라."], ["He is now in the upper branches of the hemlock.", "그것은 지금 솔송나무 윗가지에 있다."], ["Your path is short and open.", "네 길은 짧고 트여 있다."], ["When he is seen above the trees, there will be men on your trail.\"", "그것이 나무들 위로 보이게 될 때, 네 흔적을 좇는 자들이 있을 것이다.\""]], [["\"I hear a crow!\"", "\"내게 까마귀 우는 소리가 들리는구나!\""], ["exclaimed Magua, with a taunting laugh.", "〈머과〉가 조롱하는 웃음과 함께 외쳤다."], ["\"Go!\"", "\"가라!\""], ["he added, shaking his hand at the crowd, which had slowly opened to admit his passage,--\"Where are the petticoats of the Delawares!", "그가, 제 통행을 받아들이려 천천히 갈라진 무리를 향해 손을 흔들며 덧붙였다. \"〈델라웨어〉들의 치마는 어디 있느냐!"], ["Let them send their arrows and their guns to the Wyandots; they shall have venison to eat, and corn to hoe.", "자기네 화살과 총일랑 〈와이언도트〉들에게 보내라지. 먹을 사슴 고기와 일굴 옥수수밭이나 얻을 테니."], ["Dogs, rabbits, thieves--I spit on you!\"", "개들아, 토끼들아, 도둑놈들아 — 너희에게 침을 뱉는다!\""]], [["His parting gibes were listened to in a dead, boding silence, and, with these biting words in his mouth, the triumphant Magua passed unmolested into the forest, followed by his passive captive, and protected by the inviolable laws of Indian hospitality.", "그가 떠나며 던진 빈정거림은 죽은 듯한, 불길한 침묵 속에 받아들여졌고, 이 신랄한 말을 입에 문 채, 의기양양한 〈머과〉는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 숲속으로 들어섰으니, 무력해진 제 포로를 거느리고, 인디언 환대의 침범할 수 없는 법에 보호받으며. * 〈운카스〉가 말하는 \"내 등딱지\"는 거북을 뜻하는 토템 문장으로, 모히칸의 거북 씨족이 만물의 기원을 떠받친다는 부족 신화를 가리킨다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI", "# CHAPTER XXXI"]], [["_\"Flue._--Kill the poys and the luggage!", "플루.—애들이고 짐바리고 다 죽이다니!"], ["'Tis expressly against the law of arms; 'tis as arrant a piece of knavery, mark you now, as can be offered in the world.\"", "이는 전쟁의 법도에 명백히 어긋나는 일이오. 똑똑히 보시오, 세상에 이보다 더 뻔뻔스러운 악당 짓은 없을 것이오."]], [["_King Henry V._", "헨리 5세"]], [["So long as their enemy and his victim continued in sight, the multitude remained motionless as beings charmed to the place by some power that was friendly to the Huron; but the instant he disappeared, it became tossed and agitated by fierce and powerful passion.", "적과 그 희생자가 시야에 머무는 동안, 그 무리는 〈휴런〉족에게 우호적인 어떤 힘에 의해 그 자리에 묶인 듯 미동도 없이 서 있었다. 그러나 그가 사라지는 순간, 무리는 사납고 거센 격정에 들끓고 요동치기 시작했다."], ["Uncas maintained his elevated stand, keeping his eyes on the form of Cora, until the colors of her dress were blended with the foliage of the forest; when he descended, and moving silently through the throng, he disappeared in that lodge from which he had so recently issued.", "〈언카스〉는 그 높은 자리를 지키며, 〈코라〉의 옷빛이 숲의 나뭇잎과 한데 섞일 때까지 그 모습에서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 이윽고 그는 내려와, 무리 사이를 소리 없이 지나, 조금 전 나왔던 바로 그 오두막 안으로 사라졌다."], ["A few of the graver and more attentive warriors, who caught the gleams of anger that shot from the eyes of the young chief in passing, followed him to the place he had selected for his meditations.", "지나가는 젊은 추장의 눈에서 번뜩이는 분노의 빛을 알아챈 진중하고 주의 깊은 몇몇 전사가, 그가 사색을 위해 택한 곳까지 그를 뒤따랐다."], ["After which, Tamenund and Alice were removed, and the women and children were ordered to disperse.", "그런 다음 〈터메넌드〉와 〈앨리스〉가 물러갔고, 여자와 아이들에게는 흩어지라는 명이 내렸다."], ["During the momentous hour that succeeded, the encampment resembled a hive of troubled bees, who only awaited the appearance and example of their leader to take some distant and momentous flight.", "그 뒤를 이은 중대한 한 시간 동안, 진영은 어지러워진 벌집을 닮았으니, 우두머리가 나타나 본보기를 보여 멀고 중대한 비상을 떠날 그 순간만을 기다리는 벌떼와도 같았다."]], [["A young warrior at length issued from the lodge of Uncas; and moving deliberately, with a sort of grave march, towards a dwarf pine that grew in the crevices of the rocky terrace, he tore the bark from its body, and then returned whence he came without speaking.", "마침내 한 젊은 전사가 〈언카스〉의 오두막에서 나와, 일종의 엄숙한 행진과도 같은 신중한 걸음으로, 바위 단의 갈라진 틈에서 자란 키 작은 소나무를 향해 다가가, 그 몸통에서 껍질을 벗겨 내고는, 말 한마디 없이 왔던 곳으로 돌아갔다."], ["He was soon followed by another, who stripped the sapling of its branches, leaving it a naked and blazed[28] trunk.", "곧이어 또 다른 자가 뒤따라, 그 어린나무의 가지를 모조리 쳐 내어, 헐벗고 껍질이 벗겨진 줄기만을 남겼다."], ["A third colored the posts with stripes of a dark red paint; all which indications of a hostile design in the leaders of the nation were received by the men without in a gloomy and ominous silence.", "셋째 자는 그 기둥을 짙은 붉은 칠로 줄무늬를 그어 물들였다. 부족의 우두머리들이 품은 적대적 의도를 알리는 이 모든 표시를, 바깥의 사내들은 음울하고 불길한 침묵 속에 받아들였다."], ["Finally, the Mohican himself reappeared, divested of all his attire except his girdle and leggings, and with one-half of his fine features hid under a cloud of threatening black.", "마침내 〈모히칸〉족 청년 자신이 다시 모습을 드러냈으니, 허리띠와 각반을 빼고는 모든 의복을 벗어 버렸고, 그 잘생긴 얼굴의 절반은 위협하는 검은 구름 아래 감추어져 있었다."]], [["Uncas moved with a slow and dignified tread towards the post, which he immediately commenced encircling with a measured step, not unlike an ancient dance, raising his voice, at the same time, in the wild and irregular chant of his war-song.", "〈언카스〉는 느리고 위엄 있는 걸음으로 그 기둥을 향해 나아갔고, 곧장 절도 있는 발걸음으로 그 둘레를 돌기 시작했으니, 그것은 고대의 춤과도 같았으며, 동시에 그 목소리를 높여 거칠고 불규칙한 전쟁 노래를 읊었다."], ["The notes were in the extremes of human sounds; being sometimes melancholy and exquisitely plaintive, even rivalling the melody of birds--and then, by sudden and startling transitions, causing the auditors to tremble by their depth and energy.", "그 가락은 인간이 내는 소리의 양 극단에 걸쳐 있었다. 때로는 우수에 잠겨 더없이 애처로워, 새들의 선율에 견줄 만하다가도, 돌연하고 소스라치는 전환으로, 그 깊이와 기운에 듣는 이를 떨게 만들었다."], ["The words were few and often repeated, proceeding gradually from a sort of invocation, or hymn to the Deity, to an intimation of the warrior's object, and terminating as they commenced with an acknowledgment of his own dependence on the Great Spirit.", "노랫말은 적고 자주 되풀이되었으니, 일종의 기원, 곧 신을 향한 찬가에서 점차 나아가, 전사의 목적을 내비치고, 시작과 같이 위대한 정령에 대한 자신의 의탁을 고백하는 것으로 끝맺었다."], ["If it were possible to translate the comprehensive and melodious language in which he spoke, the ode might read something like the following:", "그가 노래한 그 함축적이고 선율적인 말을 옮길 수만 있다면, 그 노래는 다음과 같이 읽힐 법했다."]], [["\"Manitou!", "\"마니투!"], ["Manitou!", "마니투!"], ["Manitou!", "마니투!"], ["Thou art great, thou art good, thou art wise: Manitou!", "당신은 위대하시고, 선하시고, 지혜로우시니. 마니투!"], ["Manitou!", "마니투!"], ["Thou art just.", "당신은 의로우시도다."]], [["\"In the heavens, in the clouds, O, I see Many spots--many dark, many red: In the heavens, O, I see Many clouds.", "하늘에서, 구름 속에서, 오, 내가 보네 많은 점을—검은 것 많고, 붉은 것 많네. 하늘에서, 오, 내가 보네 많은 구름을."]], [["\"In the woods, in the air, O, I hear The whoop, the long yell, and the cry: In the woods, O, I hear The loud whoop!", "숲속에서, 공중에서, 오, 내가 듣네 함성을, 긴 외침을, 그리고 울부짖음을. 숲속에서, 오, 내가 듣네 요란한 함성을!"]], [["\"Manitou!", "마니투!"], ["Manitou!", "마니투!"], ["Manitou!", "마니투!"], ["Thou art weak--thou art strong; I am slow: Manitou!", "당신은 약하시고—당신은 강하시니, 나는 더디네. 마니투!"], ["Manitou!", "마니투!"], ["Give me aid.\"", "내게 도움을 주소서.\""]], [["At the end of what might be called each verse he made a pause, by raising a note louder and longer than common, that was peculiarly suited to the sentiment just expressed.", "각 연이라 할 만한 것의 끝에서, 그는 한 음을 여느 때보다 크고 길게 끌어올려 잠시 멈추었으니, 그것은 방금 토해 낸 정서에 유달리 잘 어울렸다. 첫 마무리는 엄숙하여, 경외의 뜻을 전하려는 것이었다."], ["The first close was solemn, and intended to convey the idea of veneration; the second descriptive, bordering on the alarming; and the third was the well known and terrific war-whoop, which burst from the lips of the young warrior, like a combination of all the frightful sounds of battle.", "둘째는 묘사하는 것으로, 경각심을 일으키는 데까지 닿았다. 그리고 셋째는, 전투의 온갖 무서운 소리를 한데 합친 듯, 그 젊은 전사의 입술에서 터져 나온, 저 잘 알려지고 끔찍한 전쟁의 함성이었다."], ["The last was like the first, humble and imploring.", "마지막은 첫째와 같이 겸손하고 간청하는 것이었다."], ["Three times did he repeat this song, and as often did he encircle the post in his dance.", "그는 이 노래를 세 번 되풀이했고, 그 횟수만큼 춤추며 기둥의 둘레를 돌았다."]], [["At the close of the first turn, a grave and highly esteemed chief of the Lenape followed his example, singing words of his own, however, to music of a similar character.", "첫 바퀴가 끝나자, 〈레나페〉족의 진중하고 크게 존경받는 한 추장이 그 본보기를 따랐으니, 다만 그 노랫말은 자신의 것이되 가락은 비슷한 성격이었다."], ["Warrior after warrior enlisted in the dance, until all of any renown and authority were numbered in its mazes.", "전사가 차례차례 춤에 가담하여, 마침내 명망과 권위가 있는 자는 모두 그 소용돌이 속에 헤아려졌다."], ["The spectacle now became wildly terrific; the fierce-looking and menacing visages of the chiefs receiving additional power from the appalling strains in which they mingled their guttural tones.", "이제 그 광경은 사납고도 끔찍해졌다. 추장들의 사납고 위협적인 얼굴은, 그들이 목구멍에서 나오는 소리를 한데 섞어 넣는 그 소름 끼치는 가락으로부터 한층 더한 힘을 얻었다."], ["Just then Uncas struck his tomahawk deep into the post, and raised his voice in a shout, which might be termed his own battle-cry.", "바로 그때 〈언카스〉가 도끼를 기둥에 깊이 내리찍고, 목소리를 높여 외침을 토하니, 그것은 그 자신의 전투 함성이라 할 만했다."], ["The act announced that he had assumed the chief authority in the intended expedition.", "이 행동은 그가 계획된 원정에서 우두머리의 권한을 떠맡았음을 알리는 것이었다."]], [["It was a signal that awakened all the slumbering passions of a nation.", "그것은 한 부족의 잠든 격정을 모두 일깨우는 신호였다."], ["A hundred youths, who had hitherto been restrained by the diffidence of their years, rushed in a frantic body on the fancied emblem of their enemy, and severed it asunder, splinter by splinter, until nothing remained of the trunk but its roots in the earth.", "그때까지 어린 나이의 수줍음에 억눌려 있던 백 명의 젊은이가, 적을 본뜬 그 상징물을 향해 광란의 무리를 이루어 달려들어, 조각조각 쪼개어, 마침내 그 줄기에서 땅속의 뿌리 말고는 아무것도 남지 않을 때까지 산산이 갈라놓았다."], ["During this moment of tumult, the most ruthless deeds of war were performed on the fragments of the tree, with as much apparent ferocity as if they were the living victims of their cruelty.", "이 소란의 순간 동안, 전쟁의 가장 무자비한 행위들이 그 나무 조각들 위에서 행해졌으니, 그것이 마치 자신들의 잔혹함에 희생된 살아 있는 제물이기라도 한 듯한 흉포함이 엿보였다."], ["Some were scalped; some received the keen and trembling axe; and others suffered by thrusts from the fatal knife.", "더러는 머릿가죽이 벗겨졌고, 더러는 날카롭고 떨리는 도끼를 받았으며, 또 더러는 치명적인 칼에 찔리는 고초를 겪었다."], ["In short, the manifestations of zeal and fierce delight were so great and unequivocal, that the expedition was declared to be a war of the nation.", "요컨대, 열의와 사나운 환희의 표출이 어찌나 크고 명백했던지, 그 원정은 부족 전체의 전쟁으로 선포되기에 이르렀다."]], [["The instant Uncas had struck the blow, he moved out of the circle, and cast his eyes up to the sun, which was just gaining the point, when the truce with Magua was to end.", "〈언카스〉가 일격을 가한 그 순간, 그는 원 밖으로 나와, 두 눈을 들어 해를 보았으니, 해는 〈매과〉와의 휴전이 끝나기로 된 바로 그 지점에 막 다다르고 있었다."], ["The fact was soon announced by a significant gesture, accompanied by a corresponding cry; and the whole of the excited multitude abandoned their mimic warfare, with shrill yells of pleasure, to prepare for the more hazardous experiment of the reality.", "그 사실은 곧, 거기에 어울리는 외침을 곁들인 의미심장한 몸짓으로 알려졌다. 흥분한 무리 전체가 즐거움의 날카로운 함성과 함께 그 모의 전쟁을 그만두고, 더 위태로운 실전의 시험을 준비하기 시작했다."]], [["The whole face of the encampment was instantly changed.", "진영의 면면은 순식간에 바뀌었다."], ["The warriors, who were already armed and painted, became as still as if they were incapable of any uncommon burst of emotion.", "이미 무장하고 칠을 한 전사들은, 어떤 비범한 감정의 폭발도 할 줄 모르는 듯 고요해졌다."], ["On the other hand, the women broke out of the lodges, with the songs of joy and those of lamentation, so strangely mingled, that it might have been difficult to have said which passion preponderated. None, however, were idle.", "한편 여자들은 오두막에서 쏟아져 나오며, 기쁨의 노래와 비탄의 노래를 어찌나 기이하게 뒤섞었던지, 어느 격정이 더 우세한지 가려내기 어려울 지경이었다."], ["Some bore their choicest articles, others their young, and some their aged and infirm, into the forest, which spread itself like a verdant carpet of bright green against the side of the mountain.", "그러나 누구 하나 한가한 자는 없었다. 더러는 가장 값진 물건을, 더러는 어린것을, 또 더러는 늙고 병약한 이를, 산자락에 밝은 초록의 양탄자처럼 펼쳐진 숲속으로 날랐다."], ["Thither Tamenund also retired, with calm composure, after a short and touching interview with Uncas; from whom the sage separated with the reluctance that a parent would quit a long lost and just recovered child.", "그곳으로 〈터메넌드〉 또한, 〈언카스〉와 짧고도 가슴 저미는 만남을 가진 뒤, 차분한 평정으로 물러갔다. 이 현자가 그에게서 떨어질 때는, 오래 잃었다가 막 되찾은 자식과 헤어지는 어버이가 그러할 만한 미련이 어렸다."], ["In the meantime, Duncan saw Alice to a place of safety, and then sought the scout, with a countenance that denoted how eagerly he also panted for the approaching contest.", "그사이 〈던컨〉은 〈앨리스〉를 안전한 곳으로 데려다준 뒤, 정찰병을 찾아 나섰으니, 그 얼굴에는 그 역시 다가오는 싸움을 얼마나 간절히 갈망하는지가 드러나 있었다."]], [["But Hawkeye was too much accustomed to the war-song and the enlistments of the natives, to betray any interest in the passing scene.", "그러나 〈호크아이〉는 원주민들의 전쟁 노래와 가담 의식에 너무도 익숙하여, 눈앞에 펼쳐지는 광경에 어떤 흥미도 내비치지 않았다."], ["He merely cast an occasional look at the number and quality of the warriors, who, from time to time, signified their readiness to accompany Uncas to the field.", "그는 다만 이따금 〈언카스〉를 따라 출진할 채비가 되었음을 알리는 전사들의 수와 자질을 흘긋 살필 뿐이었다."], ["In this particular he was soon satisfied; for, as has been already seen, the power of the young chief quickly embraced every fighting man in the nation. After this material point was so satisfactorily decided, he despatched an Indian boy in quest of \"Killdeer\"", "이 점에서 그는 곧 흡족해졌으니, 이미 보았듯이, 그 젊은 추장의 위력은 부족의 모든 싸울 만한 사내를 순식간에 아울렀던 것이다."], ["and the rifle of Uncas, to the place where they had deposited the weapons on approaching the camp of the Delawares; a measure of double policy, inasmuch as it protected the arms from their own fate, if detained as prisoners, and gave them the advantage of appearing among the strangers rather as sufferers than as men provided with the means of defence and subsistence.", "이 중요한 사안이 그토록 흡족하게 결정된 뒤, 그는 한 인디언 소년을 보내어, 〈델라웨어〉족 진영에 다가설 때 무기를 숨겨 둔 자리에서 \"킬디어\"와 〈언카스〉의 소총을 찾아오게 했다. 이는 이중의 책략이었으니, 포로로 붙들릴 경우 무기를 그들 자신의 운명에서 지켜 주는 한편, 낯선 이들 사이에서 그들이 방어와 생존의 수단을 갖춘 사내라기보다 차라리 고초를 겪는 자로 비치게 하는 이점을 주었다."], ["In selecting another to perform the office of reclaiming his highly prized rifle, the scout had lost sight of none of his habitual caution.", "자신이 끔찍이 아끼는 소총을 되찾는 임무를 다른 자에게 맡기면서도, 정찰병은 평소의 신중함을 조금도 잃지 않았다."], ["He knew that Magua had not come unattended, and he also knew that Huron spies watched the movements of their new enemies, along the whole boundary of the woods.", "그는 〈매과〉가 수행원 없이 오지 않았음을 알았고, 또한 〈휴런〉족 첩자들이 숲의 경계를 따라 새로운 적들의 움직임을 살피고 있음도 알았다."], ["It would, therefore, have been fatal to himself to have attempted the experiment; a warrior would have fared no better; but the danger of a boy would not be likely to commence until after his object was discovered.", "그러므로 자신이 직접 그 시험에 나서는 것은 목숨을 잃는 일이었을 것이고, 전사라 한들 나을 게 없었을 터이나, 소년의 위험은 그 목적이 발각된 뒤에야 비로소 시작될 법했다."], ["When Heyward joined him, the scout was coolly awaiting the result of this experiment.", "〈헤이워드〉가 그에게 합류했을 때, 정찰병은 이 시험의 결과를 침착하게 기다리고 있었다."]], [["The boy, who had been well instructed, and was sufficiently crafty, proceeded, with a bosom that was swelling with the pride of such a confidence, and all the hopes of young ambition, carelessly across the clearing to the wood, which he entered at a point at some little distance from the place where the guns were secreted.", "잘 일러둔 데다 충분히 영리하기까지 한 그 소년은, 그런 신임을 받은 자부심과 젊은 야심의 온갖 기대로 가슴이 부풀어, 빈터를 가로질러 무심한 듯 숲을 향해 나아갔고, 총들이 숨겨진 곳에서 조금 떨어진 지점으로 숲에 들어섰다."], ["The instant, however, he was concealed by the foliage of the bushes, his dusky form was to be seen gliding, like that of a serpent, towards the desired treasure.", "그러나 덤불의 잎사귀에 몸이 가려지는 순간, 그 거무스름한 형상이 마치 뱀처럼, 바라던 보물을 향해 미끄러져 가는 것이 보였다."], ["He was successful; and in another moment he appeared flying across the narrow opening that skirted the base of the terrace on which the village stood, with the velocity of an arrow, and bearing a prize in each hand.", "그는 성공했다. 그리고 다음 순간, 그는 마을이 들어선 단의 발치를 두른 좁은 트인 공간을 가로질러, 화살 같은 빠르기로, 양손에 노획물을 하나씩 들고 날 듯이 달려 나왔다."], ["He had actually gained the crags, and was leaping up their sides with incredible activity, when a shot from the woods showed how accurate had been the judgment of the scout.", "그가 실제로 바위에 다다라 믿기 힘든 날렵함으로 그 비탈을 뛰어오르고 있을 때, 숲에서 날아온 한 발의 총성이 정찰병의 판단이 얼마나 정확했는지를 보여 주었다."], ["The boy answered it with a feeble but contemptuous shout; and immediately a second bullet was sent after him from another part of the cover.", "소년은 약하나 경멸이 담긴 외침으로 그에 응수했다. 곧이어 은신처의 다른 쪽에서 둘째 탄환이 그의 뒤를 좇아 날아왔다."], ["At the next instant he appeared on the level above, elevating his guns in triumph, while he moved with the air of a conqueror towards the renowned hunter who had honored him by so glorious a commission.", "다음 순간 그는 위쪽 평지에 모습을 드러내어, 의기양양하게 두 총을 치켜들고는, 그토록 영광스러운 임무로 자신에게 영예를 베푼 그 이름난 사냥꾼을 향해 정복자의 기개로 걸어갔다."]], [["Notwithstanding the lively interest Hawkeye had taken in the fate of his messenger, he received \"Killdeer\" with a satisfaction that, momentarily, drove all other recollections from his mind.", "〈호크아이〉는 자신의 심부름꾼의 운명에 생생한 관심을 두었음에도, \"킬디어\"를 건네받자, 그 순간만은 다른 모든 생각을 마음에서 몰아낼 만한 만족감으로 그것을 받았다."], ["After examining the piece with an intelligent eye, and opening and shutting the pan some ten or fifteen times, and trying sundry other equally important experiments on the lock, he turned to the boy, and demanded with great manifestations of kindness, if he was hurt.", "그는 식견 있는 눈으로 총을 살피고, 약실 뚜껑을 열댓 번이나 여닫으며, 격발 장치에 그만큼이나 중요한 다른 여러 시험을 해 본 뒤, 소년을 향해 돌아서서, 더없는 친절을 드러내며 다쳤느냐고 물었다."], ["The urchin looked proudly up in his face, but made no reply.", "그 꼬마는 자랑스레 그의 얼굴을 올려다볼 뿐, 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I see, lad, the knaves have barked your arm!\"", "알겠구나, 얘야. 그 악당들이 네 팔을 스쳐 놓았어!\""], ["added the scout, taking up the limb of the patient sufferer, across which a deep flesh wound had been made by one of the bullets; \"but a little bruised alder will act like a charm.", "정찰병이 덧붙이며, 묵묵히 참고 있는 그 소년의 팔을 들어 올렸으니, 그 팔에는 탄환 한 발이 살을 깊이 헤집은 상처가 나 있었다. \"허나 으깬 오리나무를 조금 붙이면 신통하게 들을 게다."], ["In the meantime I will wrap it in a badge of wampum!", "그동안은 〈왐펌〉 표지로 싸매 주마!"], ["You have commenced the business of a warrior early, my brave boy, and are likely to bear a plenty of honorable scars to your grave.", "너는 어린 나이에 전사의 일을 시작했구나, 용감한 녀석아. 무덤까지 가져갈 명예로운 흉터를 잔뜩 짊어지게 생겼다."], ["I know many young men that have taken scalps who cannot show such a mark as this.", "머릿가죽을 벗겨 본 젊은이도 이런 자국 하나 보이지 못하는 자를 내 많이 안다."], ["Go!\"", "가거라!\""], ["having bound up the arm; \"you will be a chief!\"", "팔을 다 동여매고 그가 말했다. \"너는 추장이 될 게다!\""]], [["The lad departed, prouder of his flowing blood than the vainest courtier could be of his blushing ribbon; and stalked among the fellows of his age, an object of general admiration and envy.", "소년은, 가장 허영심 많은 신하가 제 발그레한 훈장 띠를 자랑하는 것보다 더 자랑스럽게 제 흐르는 피를 여기며 떠나, 또래 무리 사이를 으스대며 걸으니, 뭇사람의 부러움과 흠모의 대상이 되었다."]], [["But in a moment of so many serious and important duties, this single act of juvenile fortitude did not attract the general notice and commendation it would have received under milder auspices.", "그러나 그토록 진지하고 중대한 임무가 산적한 순간이라, 이 젊은이의 한 차례 용기 어린 행동은, 더 온화한 정세였다면 받았을 만한 두루 미친 주목과 칭송을 끌어내지는 못했다."], ["It had, however, served to apprise the Delawares of the position and the intentions of their enemies.", "그래도 그것은 〈델라웨어〉족에게 적들의 위치와 의도를 알려 준 셈이 되었다."], ["Accordingly a party of adventurers, better suited to the task than the weak though spirited boy, was ordered to dislodge the skulkers.", "그리하여, 기개는 있으나 약한 그 소년보다 그 임무에 더 어울리는 한 무리의 결사대가, 숨어 있는 자들을 몰아내라는 명을 받았다."], ["The duty was soon performed; for most of the Hurons retired of themselves when they found they had been discovered.", "그 임무는 곧 완수되었으니, 〈휴런〉족 대부분이 자신들이 발각된 것을 알자 스스로 물러났던 까닭이다."], ["The Delawares followed to a sufficient distance from their own encampment, and then halted for orders, apprehensive of being led into an ambush.", "〈델라웨어〉족은 자기네 진영에서 알맞을 만큼 떨어진 거리까지 뒤쫓다가, 매복에 끌려들까 염려하여 멈춰 서서 명령을 기다렸다."], ["As both parties secreted themselves, the woods were again as still and quiet as a mild summer morning and deep solitude could render them.", "양편이 다 같이 몸을 숨기자, 숲은 다시, 온화한 여름 아침과 깊은 적막이 빚어낼 수 있는 만큼 고요하고 잠잠해졌다."]], [["The calm but still impatient Uncas now collected his chiefs, and divided his power.", "차분하나 그래도 조바심을 누르지 못한 〈언카스〉는 이제 추장들을 모아 그 권한을 나누었다."], ["He presented Hawkeye as a warrior, often tried, and always found deserving of confidence.", "그는 〈호크아이〉를, 여러 차례 시험을 거쳐 언제나 신뢰받을 만한 자로 드러난 전사라 소개했다."], ["When he found his friend met with a favorable reception, he bestowed on him the command of twenty men, like himself, active, skilful, and resolute.", "자신의 벗이 호의로 받아들여지는 것을 보자, 그는 그에게 스무 명의 지휘를 맡겼으니, 그들 또한 그처럼 날렵하고 능숙하며 단호한 자들이었다."], ["He gave the Delawares to understand the rank of Heyward among the troops of the Yengeese, and then tendered to him a trust of equal authority.", "그는 〈델라웨어〉족에게 〈헤이워드〉가 〈양기즈〉의 군대에서 차지하는 지위를 알려 준 뒤, 그에게도 동등한 권한의 직책을 내주려 했다."], ["But Duncan declined the charge, professing his readiness to serve as a volunteer by the side of the scout.", "그러나 〈던컨〉은 그 직책을 사양하며, 정찰병의 곁에서 지원병으로 복무할 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["After this disposition, the young Mohican appointed various native chiefs to fill the different situations of responsibility, and the time pressing, he gave forth the word to march.", "이러한 배치를 마친 뒤, 그 젊은 〈모히칸〉족은 여러 원주민 추장을 임명하여 책임이 따르는 갖가지 자리를 채웠고, 시간이 촉박한지라, 진군의 명을 내렸다."], ["He was cheerfully, but silently, obeyed by more than two hundred men.", "이백이 넘는 사내가 흔쾌히, 그러나 묵묵히 그를 따랐다."]], [["Their entrance into the forest was perfectly unmolested; nor did they encounter any living objects, that could either give the alarm, or furnish the intelligence they needed, until they came upon the lairs of their own scouts.", "그들이 숲으로 들어서는 데는 아무런 방해도 없었다. 그들 자신의 정찰병의 은신처에 다다를 때까지, 경보를 울리거나 그들이 필요로 하는 정보를 줄 만한 살아 있는 것이라곤 하나도 마주치지 못했다."], ["Here a halt was ordered, and the chiefs were assembled to hold a \"whispering council.\"", "이곳에서 멈추라는 명이 내렸고, 추장들이 모여 \"속삭이는 평의회\"를 열었다."]], [["At this meeting divers plans of operation were suggested, though none of a character to meet the wishes of their ardent leader.", "이 모임에서는 여러 가지 작전 계획이 제시되었으나, 그 어느 것도 열렬한 그들의 우두머리의 뜻에 맞는 성격은 아니었다."], ["Had Uncas followed the promptings of his own inclinations, he would have led his followers to the charge without a moment's delay, and put the conflict to the hazard of an instant issue; but such a course would have been in opposition to all the received practices and opinions of his countrymen.", "〈언카스〉가 제 마음이 시키는 대로 따랐다면, 그는 한순간도 지체 없이 부하들을 이끌고 돌격하여, 그 싸움을 당장의 결판이라는 모험에 걸었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 길은 동족이 받아들이는 온갖 관행과 견해에 어긋나는 일이었다."], ["He was, therefore, fain to adopt a caution that in the present temper of his mind he execrated, and to listen to advice at which his fiery spirit chafed, under the vivid recollection of Cora's danger and Magua's insolence.", "그러므로 그는, 지금의 심사로는 저주스럽기 짝이 없는 신중함을 어쩔 수 없이 택해야 했고, 〈코라〉의 위험과 〈매과〉의 오만이 생생하게 떠오르는 가운데 그 불같은 기개가 부글대는데도, 거슬리는 충고에 귀를 기울여야 했다."]], [["After an unsatisfactory conference of many minutes, a solitary individual was seen advancing from the side of the enemy, with such apparent haste, as to induce the belief he might be a messenger charged with pacific overtures.", "몇 분에 걸친 흡족잖은 논의 끝에, 적진 쪽에서 한 사람이 홀로 다가오는 것이 보였으니, 그 서두르는 기색이 어찌나 역력했던지 평화의 제안을 띤 전령이 아닌가 하는 생각이 들 정도였다."], ["When within a hundred yards, however, of the cover behind which the Delaware council had assembled, the stranger hesitated, appeared uncertain what course to take, and finally halted.", "그러나 〈델라웨어〉족 평의회가 모여 있는 은신처에서 백 야드쯤 되는 곳에 이르자, 그 낯선 자는 머뭇거리며, 어느 길을 택해야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 듯하다가, 끝내 멈춰 섰다."], ["All eyes were now turned on Uncas, as if seeking directions how to proceed.", "이제 모든 눈이 〈언카스〉를 향했으니, 어찌 처신해야 할지 지시를 구하는 듯했다."]], [["\"Hawkeye,\"", "\"〈호크아이〉,\""], ["said the young chief, in a low voice, \"he must never speak to the Hurons again.\"", "젊은 추장이 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"저자가 다시는 〈휴런〉족에게 말하지 못하게 해야 하오.\""]], [["\"His time has come,\"", "\"저놈의 때가 왔구먼,\""], ["said the laconic scout, thrusting the long barrel of his rifle through the leaves, and taking his deliberate and fatal aim.", "말수 적은 정찰병이 말하며, 소총의 긴 총신을 나뭇잎 사이로 내밀어, 신중하고도 치명적인 겨냥을 했다."], ["But, instead of pulling the trigger he lowered the muzzle again, and indulged himself in a fit of his peculiar mirth.", "그러나 방아쇠를 당기는 대신, 그는 다시 총구를 내리더니, 그 특유의 웃음을 한바탕 터뜨렸다."], ["\"I took the imp for a Mingo, as I'm a miserable sinner!\"", "\"이 못난 죄인 분명히 말하건대, 난 저 도깨비 같은 놈을 〈밍고〉인 줄 알았지!\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; \"but when my eye ranged along his ribs for a place to get the bullet in--would you think it, Uncas--I saw the musicianer's blower; and so, after all, it is the man they call Gamut, whose death can profit no one, and whose life, if his tongue can do anything but sing, may be made serviceable to our own ends.", "\"한데 탄환을 박아 넣을 자리를 찾느라 그놈의 갈빗대를 따라 눈을 훑다가—믿어지겠나, 〈언카스〉—그 악사 양반의 불대를 봤지 뭔가. 그러니 결국 저자는 〈개멋〉이라 불리는 사람이라, 죽여 봐야 누구한테도 득 될 게 없고, 살려 두면 그 혀가 노래 말고 다른 짓도 할 줄 안다면야 우리 목적에 쓸모 있게 만들 수도 있겠어."], ["If sounds have not lost their virtue, I'll soon have a discourse with the honest fellow, and that in a voice he'll find more agreeable than the speech of 'Killdeer.'\"", "소리에 아직 효험이 남아 있다면, 곧 저 정직한 친구와 한바탕 이야기를 나눠 보지. 그것도 저자가 '킬디어'의 말보다 한결 듣기 좋다고 여길 목소리로 말이야.\""]], [["So saying, Hawkeye laid aside his rifle; and crawling through the bushes until within hearing of David, he attempted to repeat the musical effort, which had conducted himself, with so much safety and _eclat_, through the Huron encampment.", "그렇게 말하며 〈호크아이〉는 소총을 옆에 내려놓고, 〈데이비드〉의 귀에 들릴 만한 거리에 이를 때까지 덤불을 헤치고 기어가, 자신을 그토록 안전하고 빛나게 〈휴런〉족 진영을 통과시켜 준 그 음악적 시도를 되풀이해 보았다."], ["The exquisite organs of Gamut could not readily be deceived (and, to say the truth, it would have been difficult for any other than Hawkeye to produce a similar noise), and consequently, having once before heard the sounds, he now knew whence they proceeded.", "〈개멋〉의 정교한 귀는 그리 쉽게 속을 리 없었고(사실대로 말하자면, 〈호크아이〉 말고 다른 누가 그런 소리를 내기란 어려운 노릇이었을 것이다), 그리하여 일찍이 한 번 그 소리를 들어 본 적이 있는 터라, 그는 이제 그것이 어디서 나는지 알았다."], ["The poor fellow appeared relieved from a state of great embarrassment; for pursuing the direction of the voice--a task that to him was not much less arduous than it would have been to have gone up in the face of a battery--he soon discovered the hidden songster.", "그 가엾은 친구는 큰 곤혹에서 벗어난 듯 보였으니, 그 목소리의 방향을 좇는 일은—그에게는 포대(砲臺)를 정면으로 거슬러 오르는 것에 못지않게 힘겨운 일이었으나—그는 곧 숨어 있는 그 가객을 찾아냈다."]], [["\"I wonder what the Hurons will think of that!\"", "\"〈휴런〉족이 저걸 어찌 여길지 궁금하군!\""], ["said the scout, laughing, as he took his companion by the arm, and urged him towards the rear.", "정찰병이 웃으며 말하더니, 동행의 팔을 붙잡고 후방으로 재촉했다."], ["\"If the knaves lie within ear-shot, they will say there are two non-compossers instead of one!", "\"그 악당들이 귀에 들리는 데 누워 있다면, 멍청이가 하나가 아니라 둘이라고 할 테지!"], ["But here we are safe,\"", "허나 여기선 우리가 안전하네,\""], ["he added, pointing to Uncas and his associates.", "그가 〈언카스〉와 그 동료들을 가리키며 덧붙였다."], ["\"Now give us the history of the Mingo inventions in natural English, and without any ups and downs of voice.\"", "\"자, 이제 〈밍고〉의 술책을 알아듣기 쉬운 영어로, 목소리 오르내림 없이 들려주게나.\""]], [["David gazed about him, at the fierce and wild-looking chiefs, in mute wonder; but assured by the presence of faces that he knew, he soon rallied his faculties so far as to make an intelligent reply.", "〈데이비드〉는 사납고 거칠어 보이는 추장들을 둘러보며 말없이 놀랐으나, 아는 얼굴들이 있다는 데 안심하여, 곧 정신을 가다듬어 알아들을 만한 대답을 했다."]], [["\"The heathen are abroad in goodly numbers,\"", "\"이교도들이 상당한 수로 밖에 나와 있는데,\""], ["said David, \"and, I fear, with evil intent.", "〈데이비드〉가 말했다. \"내 두렵건대, 흉악한 의도를 품고 있소."], ["There has been much howling and ungodly revelry, together with such sounds as it is profanity to utter, in their habitations within the past hour; so much so, in truth, that I have fled to the Delawares in search of peace.\"", "지난 한 시간 동안 그들의 거처에서는 입에 담기조차 불경한 소리들과 더불어, 울부짖음과 신성치 못한 흥청거림이 그치질 않았소. 참으로 어찌나 심했던지, 나는 평안을 찾아 〈델라웨어〉족에게로 달아났소이다.\""]], [["\"Your ears might not have profited much by the exchange, had you been quicker of foot,\"", "\"발걸음이 더 빨랐다면, 그 자리를 바꾼들 자네 귀가 덕 본 게 많지 않았을 걸세,\""], ["returned the scout, a little dryly.", "정찰병이 다소 무미건조하게 받았다."], ["\"But let that be as it may; where are the Hurons?\"", "\"허나 그건 그렇다 치고, 〈휴런〉족은 어디 있나?\""]], [["\"They lie hid in the forest, between this spot and their village, in such force, that prudence would teach you instantly to return.\"", "\"여기와 그들의 마을 사이 숲속에 숨어 있는데, 그 세력이 어찌나 큰지, 분별이 있다면 당장 돌아서라고 일러 줄 정도요.\""]], [["Uncas cast a glance along the range of trees which concealed his own band and mentioned the name of--", "〈언카스〉는 제 무리를 가려 주는 나무들의 행렬을 따라 눈길을 던지고는, 한 이름을 입에 올렸다."]], [["\"Magua?\"", "\"〈매과〉는?\""]], [["\"Is among them.", "\"그들 가운데 있소."], ["He brought in the maiden that had sojourned with the Delawares, and leaving her in the cave, has put himself, like a raging wolf, at the head of his savages.", "그가 〈델라웨어〉족과 함께 머물던 처녀를 데려와, 동굴에 두고는, 성난 늑대처럼 제 야만족들의 선두에 섰소."], ["I know not what has troubled his spirit so greatly!\"", "무엇이 그의 마음을 그토록 크게 어지럽혔는지 나는 모르겠소!\""]], [["\"He has left her, you say, in the cave!\"", "\"그녀를 동굴에 두었다 했소!\""], ["interrupted Heyward; \"'tis well that we know its situation!", "〈헤이워드〉가 끼어들었다. \"그곳의 위치를 우리가 안다니 다행이오!"], ["May not something be done for her instant relief?\"", "그녀를 당장 구해 낼 무슨 수가 없겠소?\""]], [["Uncas looked earnestly at the scout, before he asked,--", "〈언카스〉는 정찰병을 간절히 바라본 뒤에야 물었다."]], [["\"What says Hawkeye?\"", "\"〈호크아이〉의 생각은 어떻소?\""]], [["\"Give me twenty rifles, and I will turn to the right, along the stream; and passing by the huts of the beaver, will join the Sagamore and the colonel. You shall then hear the whoop from that quarter; with this wind one may easily send it a mile. Then, Uncas, do you drive in their front; when they come within range of our pieces, we will give them a blow that, I pledge the good name of an old frontiersman, shall make their line bend like an ashen bow. After which, we will carry their village, and take the woman from the cave; when the affair may be finished with the tribe, according to a white man's battle, by a blow and a victory; or, in the Indian fashion, with dodge and cover. There may be no great learning, major, in this plan, but with courage and patience it can all be done.\"", "\"내게 소총 스무 자루를 주게. 그러면 나는 오른편으로, 시냇물을 따라 돌아, 비버의 굴들을 지나, 〈사가모어〉와 대령께 합류하겠네. 그러면 자네는 그쪽에서 함성을 듣게 될 걸세. 이 바람이면 한 마일은 거뜬히 보낼 수 있지. 그때 〈언카스〉, 자네는 그들의 전면을 몰아붙이게. 그들이 우리 총의 사정거리 안에 들어오면, 우리가 일격을 가할 테니, 늙은 변경 사람의 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대, 그 일격이 그들의 전열을 물푸레나무 활처럼 휘게 할 걸세. 그런 다음 우리는 그들의 마을을 들이쳐, 동굴에서 그 여인을 데려오는 거지. 그러면 부족과의 일은, 백인의 싸움 방식대로 일격과 승리로 끝내든지, 인디언 방식대로 피하고 숨으며 마무리하면 되네. 소령, 이 계책에 대단한 학식이야 없겠지만, 용기와 인내만 있으면 모두 해낼 수 있어.\" \"내 마음에 쏙 드오,\" 〈코라〉의 구출이 정찰병의 마음속 첫째 목표임을 알아챈 〈던컨〉이 외쳤다. \"내 마음에 쏙 드오. 당장 시도합시다.\""]], [["\"I like it much,\"", "\"내 마음에 쏙 드오.\""], ["cried Duncan, who saw the release of Cora was the primary object in the mind of the scout; \"I like it much.", "코라를 구출하는 것이 정찰병의 마음속 첫째 목적임을 알아챈 던컨이 외쳤다. \"내 마음에 쏙 드오."], ["Let it be instantly attempted.\"", "당장 시도합시다.\""]], [["After a short conference, the plan was matured, and rendered more intelligible to the several parties; the different signals were appointed, and the chiefs separated, each to his allotted station.", "짧은 협의 끝에 계획이 무르익었고, 여러 무리에게 한층 명확히 전달되었다. 갖가지 신호가 정해지자 추장들은 흩어져, 저마다 할당된 자리로 향했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII", "# CHAPTER XXXII"]], [["\"But plagues shall spread, and funeral fires increase, Till the great king, without a ransom paid, To her own Chrysa send the black-eyed maid.\"", "위대한 왕이 몸값도 받지 않고\n검은 눈의 처녀를 제 〈크리사〉로 돌려보낼 때까지."]], [["During the time Uncas was making this disposition of his forces, the woods were still, and, with the exception of those who had met in council, apparently as much untenanted, as when they came fresh from the hands of their Almighty Creator.", "포프\n〈언카스〉가 이렇게 병력을 배치하는 동안, 숲은 고요하여, 평의회에서 만났던 자들을 제외하면 마치 전능하신 창조주의 손에서 갓 나왔을 때처럼 사람 하나 깃들지 않은 듯했다."], ["The eye could range, in every direction, through the long and shadowed vistas of the trees; but nowhere was any object to be seen that did not properly belong to the peaceful and slumbering scenery.", "눈길은 어느 방향으로든 나무들의 길고 그늘진 사잇길을 따라 멀리 뻗어 나갈 수 있었으나, 그 어디에도 평화롭고 잠든 풍경에 어울리지 않는 것이라곤 보이지 않았다."], ["Here and there a bird was heard fluttering among the branches of the beeches, and occasionally a squirrel dropped a nut, drawing the startled looks of the party, for a moment, to the place; but the instant the casual interruption ceased, the passing air was heard murmuring above their heads, along that verdant and undulating surface of forest, which spread itself unbroken, unless by stream or lake, over such a vast region of country.", "여기저기서 너도밤나무 가지 사이로 푸드덕대는 새 소리가 들렸고, 이따금 다람쥐가 떨군 도토리에 일행의 놀란 시선이 잠시 그곳으로 쏠리기도 했다. 그러나 이 우연한 방해가 멎는 순간, 지나가는 바람이 그들의 머리 위로, 시내나 호수가 아니고서는 그토록 광막한 고장 위에 끊김 없이 펼쳐진 그 푸르고 굽이치는 숲의 표면을 따라 속삭이는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Across the tract of wilderness, which lay between the Delawares and the village of their enemies, it seemed as if the foot of man had never trodden, so breathing and deep was the silence in which it lay.", "〈델라웨어족〉과 그 적들의 마을 사이에 놓인 황야 일대에는, 마치 사람의 발길이 한 번도 닿은 적 없는 듯, 깊고도 숨결 같은 정적이 깃들어 있었다."], ["But Hawkeye, whose duty led him foremost in the adventure, knew the character of those with whom he was about to contend too well to trust the treacherous quiet.", "그러나 이 모험에서 임무상 맨 앞에 선 〈호크아이〉는 곧 맞서 싸울 자들의 성정을 너무도 잘 알았기에, 그 미덥지 못한 고요를 믿지 않았다."]], [["When he saw his little band collected, the scout threw \"Killdeer\" into the hollow of his arm, and making a silent signal that he would be followed, he led them many rods towards the rear, into the bed of a little brook which they had crossed in advancing.", "자신의 작은 무리가 모인 것을 본 정찰병은 〈킬디어〉를 한쪽 팔 안쪽에 비스듬히 끼고, 자기를 따라오라는 말없는 신호를 보낸 뒤, 그들을 후미 쪽으로 여러 로드 이끌어, 전진하며 건넜던 작은 시냇물의 바닥으로 데려갔다."], ["Here he halted; and after waiting for the whole of his grave and attentive warriors to close about him, he spoke in Delaware, demanding--", "여기서 그는 멈춰 섰고, 진중하고 주의 깊은 전사들이 모두 자기 둘레로 모여들기를 기다린 후, 〈델라웨어〉 말로 입을 열어 물었다."]], [["\"Do any of my young men know whither this run will lead us?\"", "\"내 젊은이들 가운데 이 물줄기가 우리를 어디로 데려갈지 아는 자가 있는가?\""]], [["A Delaware stretched forth a hand, with the two fingers separated, and indicating the manner in which they were joined at the root, he answered,--", "한 〈델라웨어〉인이 손을 내밀어 두 손가락을 벌리고, 그것이 뿌리에서 어떻게 맞붙어 있는지를 가리키며 대답했다."]], [["\"Before the sun could go his own length, the little water will be in the big.\"", "\"해가 제 길이만큼 가기도 전에, 작은 물이 큰 물에 닿을 것입니다.\""], ["Then he added, pointing in the direction of the place he mentioned, \"the two make enough for the beavers.\"", "그러더니 자기가 말한 곳의 방향을 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"그 둘이 합치면 비버들에게 넉넉하지요.\""]], [["\"I thought as much,\"", "\"나도 그쯤으로 짐작했네.\""], ["returned the scout, glancing his eye upwards at the opening in the tree-tops, \"from the course it takes, and the bearings of the mountains.", "정찰병은 나무 우듬지 사이의 트인 곳으로 눈길을 위로 던지며 대답했다. \"물길이 흐르는 방향과 산의 방위로 보아서 말이야."], ["Men, we will keep within the cover of its banks till we scent the Hurons.\"", "자, 〈휴런족〉 냄새를 맡을 때까지 이 둑의 엄폐 안에 머물도록 하지.\""]], [["His companions gave the usual brief exclamation of assent, but perceiving that their leader was about to lead the way in person, one or two made signs that all was not as it should be.", "그의 동료들은 으레 하던 짧은 동의의 탄성을 냈으나, 우두머리가 친히 앞장서려는 것을 알아챈 한둘이 무언가 잘못되었다는 신호를 보냈다."], ["Hawkeye, who comprehended their meaning glances, turned, and perceived that his party had been followed thus far by the singing-master.", "그들의 눈짓이 뜻하는 바를 알아차린 〈호크아이〉는 돌아서서, 자기 일행을 여기까지 따라온 자가 성가대 선창자임을 알아보았다."]], [["\"Do you know, friend,\"", "\"여보게, 친구.\""], ["asked the scout gravely, and perhaps with a little of the pride of conscious deserving in his manner, \"that this is a band of rangers chosen for the most desperate service, and put under the command of one who, though another might say it with a better face, will not be apt to leave them idle.", "정찰병이 진중하게, 어쩌면 제 공로를 의식한 다소 자랑스러운 태도로 물었다. \"이 무리가 가장 절망적인 임무를 위해 뽑힌 척후대이고, 비록 남이 말하면 더 나은 낯으로 할 만한 소리지만, 부하들을 한가로이 놀려 두지 않을 자의 지휘 아래 있다는 것을 자네는 아는가."], ["It may not be five, it cannot be thirty minutes before we tread on the body of a Huron, living or dead.\"", "우리가 살았든 죽었든 한 〈휴런족〉의 몸뚱이를 밟기까지는 오 분도 안 걸릴지 모르고, 길어야 삼십 분을 넘기지 못할 걸세.\""]], [["\"Though not admonished of your intentions in words,\"", "\"비록 당신의 의도를 말로 듣지는 못했으나,\""], ["returned David, whose face was a little flushed, and whose ordinarily quiet and unmeaning eyes glimmered with an expression of unusual fire, \"your men have reminded me of the children of Jacob going out to battle against the Shechemites, for wickedly aspiring to wedlock with a woman of a race that was favored of the Lord.", "얼굴이 약간 상기되고, 평소 잔잔하고 무심하던 두 눈이 보기 드문 불꽃을 띠고 번득이던 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"당신의 부하들을 보니, 주께서 어여삐 여기신 종족의 여인과 사악하게도 혼인을 탐한 〈세겜〉 사람들과 맞서 싸우러 나간 〈야곱〉의 자손들이 떠오르더이다."], ["Now, I have journeyed far, and sojourned much in good and evil with the maiden ye seek; and though not a man of war, with my loins girded and my sword sharpened, yet would I gladly strike a blow in her behalf.\"", "자, 나는 멀리 길을 떠나, 당신들이 찾는 처녀와 더불어 좋은 일과 궂은 일 속에서 오래 머물렀소. 비록 전사는 아니나, 허리를 동이고 칼을 갈아, 기꺼이 그녀를 위해 한 방 휘두르고 싶소이다.\""]], [["The scout hesitated, as if weighing the chances of such a strange enlistment in his mind before he answered,--", "정찰병은 대답하기에 앞서, 이 기이한 입대의 승산을 마음속으로 가늠하듯 망설였다."]], [["\"You know not the use of any we'pon.", "\"자네는 어떤 무기도 쓸 줄을 모르네."], ["You carry no rifle; and believe me, what the Mingos take they will freely give again.\"", "소총도 지니지 않았고. 게다가 내 말 믿게, 〈밍고족〉이 빼앗은 것은 거리낌 없이 도로 돌려줄 거야.\""]], [["\"Though not a vaunting and bloodily disposed Goliath,\"", "\"비록 허풍 떨고 피에 굶주린 〈골리앗〉은 아니나,\""], ["returned David, drawing a sling from beneath his parti-colored and uncouth attire, \"I have not forgotten the example of the Jewish boy.", "〈데이비드〉가 알록달록하고 볼품없는 옷자락 아래에서 무릿매를 꺼내며 대답했다. \"나는 그 〈유대〉 소년의 본보기를 잊지 않았소."], ["With this ancient instrument of war have I practised much in my youth, and peradventure the skill has not entirely departed from me.\"", "이 오래된 전쟁 도구로 젊은 시절 나는 숱하게 익혔고, 어쩌면 그 솜씨가 아주 떠나가지는 않았을지도 모르오.\""]], [["\"Ay!\" said Hawkeye, considering the deer-skin thong and apron, with a cold and discouraging eye; \"the thing might do its work among arrows, or even knives; but these Mengwe have been furnished by the Frenchers with a good grooved barrel a man. However, it seems to be your gift to go unharmed amid fire; and as you have hitherto been favored--major, you have left your rifle at a cock; a single shot before the time would be just twenty scalps lost to no purpose--singer, you can follow; we may find use for you in the shoutings.\"", "\"허!\" 〈호크아이〉가 사슴 가죽 끈과 앞주머니를 차갑고 맥 빠지게 하는 눈길로 살피며 말했다. \"그 물건이 화살이나 칼 사이에서야 제 몫을 할지도 모르지. 허나 이 〈멩궤족〉은 프랑스 놈들한테 한 사람당 잘 깎인 강선 총열을 받았다네. 하지만 불길 속을 다치지 않고 다니는 게 자네 타고난 재주인 듯하고, 지금껏 보살핌을 받아 왔으니 — 소령, 자네 소총 공이를 당겨 놓았구먼. 때 이른 한 발은 스무 개의 머리 가죽을 까닭 없이 잃는 셈이 될 게야 — 선창자, 자네는 따라와도 좋네. 함성을 지를 때 자네가 쓸모 있을지도 모르지.\""]], [["\"I thank you, friend,\"", "\"고맙소, 친구.\""], ["returned David, supplying himself, like his royal namesake, from among the pebbles of the brook; \"though not given to the desire to kill, had you sent me away my spirit would have been troubled.\"", "자기와 이름이 같은 그 왕처럼 시냇물 조약돌 가운데서 손수 채비를 갖추며 〈데이비드〉가 대답했다. \"비록 살생의 욕심은 없으나, 당신이 나를 돌려보냈더라면 내 영혼이 괴로웠을 것이오.\""]], [["\"Remember,\"", "\"명심하게.\""], ["added the scout, tapping his own head significantly on that spot where Gamut was yet sore, \"we come to fight, and not to musickate.", "정찰병이 〈거멋〉의 아직 쓰린 그 자리를 가리키며 제 머리를 의미심장하게 톡톡 두드리고 덧붙였다. \"우리는 노래하러가 아니라 싸우러 온 걸세."], ["Until the general whoop is given, nothing speaks but the rifle.\"", "전면적인 함성이 떨어지기 전에는, 소총 말고 아무것도 입을 열지 않네.\""]], [["David nodded, as much as to signify his acquiescence with the terms; and then Hawkeye, casting another observant glance over his followers, made the signal to proceed.", "〈데이비드〉는 그 조건에 따르겠다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였다. 그러자 〈호크아이〉는 부하들을 또 한 번 살피는 눈길로 둘러본 뒤, 전진하라는 신호를 보냈다."]], [["Their route lay, for the distance of a mile, along the bed of the water-course.", "그들의 길은 일 마일 거리를 그 물줄기의 바닥을 따라 이어졌다."], ["Though protected from any great danger of observation by the precipitous banks, and the thick shrubbery which skirted the stream, no precaution known to an Indian attack was neglected.", "깎아지른 둑과 시내를 따라 우거진 덤불 덕에 발각될 큰 위험은 면했으나, 인디언식 공격에 알려진 어떤 조심성도 소홀히 하지 않았다."], ["A warrior rather crawled than walked on each flank, so as to catch occasional glimpses into the forest; and every few minutes the band came to a halt, and listened for hostile sounds, with an acuteness of organs that would be scarcely conceivable to a man in a less natural state.", "한 전사는 양 측면에서 걷는다기보다 기어서, 이따금 숲 안을 흘끔흘끔 살폈고, 몇 분마다 무리는 멈춰 서서, 보다 덜 자연적인 상태에 있는 사람으로서는 좀처럼 짐작도 못 할 만큼 예민한 감각으로 적의 기척에 귀를 기울였다."], ["Their march was, however, unmolested, and they reached the point where the lesser stream was lost in the greater, without the smallest evidence that their progress had been noted.", "그러나 그들의 행군은 방해받지 않았고, 자기들의 전진이 들켰다는 아주 작은 기색도 없이, 그들은 작은 물줄기가 큰 물줄기에 합쳐지는 지점에 다다랐다."], ["Here the scout again halted, to consult the signs of the forest.", "여기서 정찰병은 다시 멈춰 서서 숲의 징후들을 살폈다."]], [["\"We are likely to have a good day for a fight,\" he said, in English, addressing Heyward, and glancing his eye upwards at the clouds, which began to move in broad sheets across the firmament; \"a bright sun and a glittering barrel are no friends to true sight. Everything is favorable; they have the wind, which will bring down their noises and their smoke too, no little matter in itself; whereas, with us it will be first a shot, and then a clear view. But here is an end of our cover; the beavers have had the range of this stream for hundreds of years, and what atween their food and their dams, there is, as you see, many a girdled stub, but few living trees.\"", "\"싸우기에 좋은 날이 될 듯하군.\" 그가 영어로 〈헤이워드〉에게 말하며, 넓은 장막처럼 창공을 가로질러 움직이기 시작한 구름으로 눈길을 위로 던졌다. \"밝은 해와 번쩍이는 총열은 정확한 조준에 친구가 못 되지. 모든 게 우리한테 유리해. 저들은 바람을 안고 있으니, 바람이 저들의 소리와 연기까지 우리 쪽으로 다 실어 올 거야. 그것만 해도 적잖은 일이지. 반면에 우리 쪽은 먼저 한 발 쏘고, 그다음에 시야가 트일 걸세. 한데 여기서 우리 엄폐물은 끝이로군. 비버들이 수백 년 동안 이 시내를 차지해 왔고, 그놈들 먹이와 둑 때문에, 보다시피 껍질 벗겨진 그루터기는 많아도 살아 있는 나무는 몇 그루 없으니 말이야.\""]], [["Hawkeye had, in truth, in these few words, given no bad description of the prospect that now lay in their front.", "사실 〈호크아이〉는 이 몇 마디로, 이제 그들 앞에 펼쳐진 광경을 제법 그럴듯하게 그려 낸 셈이었다."], ["The brook was irregular in its width, sometimes shooting through narrow fissures in the rocks, and at others spreading over acres of bottom land, forming little areas that might be termed ponds.", "시내는 폭이 들쭉날쭉하여, 어떤 데서는 바위의 좁은 틈새로 쏟아져 나오고, 어떤 데서는 몇 에이커의 저지대 위로 퍼져, 못이라 부를 만한 작은 구역들을 이루었다."], ["Everywhere along its banks were the mouldering relics of dead trees, in all the stages of decay, from those that groaned on their tottering trunks to such as had recently been robbed of those rugged coats that so mysteriously contain their principle of life.", "그 둑을 따라 곳곳에는 죽은 나무들의 썩어 가는 잔해가, 비틀거리는 둥치 위에서 신음하는 것에서부터 그 생명의 원리를 신비로이 품은 거친 껍질을 막 빼앗긴 것에 이르기까지, 온갖 부패의 단계로 널려 있었다."], ["A few long, low, and moss-covered piles were scattered among them, like the memorials of a former and long-departed generation.", "그 사이에는 길고 낮으며 이끼 덮인 더미 몇이 흩어져, 이전의, 오래전에 떠나간 세대의 기념물처럼 보였다."]], [["All these minute particulars were noted by the scout, with a gravity and interest that they probably had never before attracted.", "이 모든 자잘한 세부를, 정찰병은 아마 이전에는 한 번도 끌어 본 적 없을 진지함과 관심으로 살폈다."], ["He knew that the Huron encampment lay a short half mile up the brook; and, with the characteristic anxiety of one who dreaded a hidden danger, he was greatly troubled at not finding the smallest trace of the presence of his enemy.", "그는 〈휴런족〉의 야영지가 시내를 따라 반 마일 남짓 위쪽에 있음을 알았다. 그리고 숨은 위험을 두려워하는 자 특유의 불안을 안고, 적의 존재를 알리는 가장 작은 흔적조차 찾을 수 없다는 데 크게 마음이 어지러웠다."], ["Once or twice he felt induced to give the order for a rush, and to attempt the village by surprise; but his experience quickly admonished him of the danger of so useless an experiment.", "한두 번 그는 돌격 명령을 내려 기습으로 마을을 들이칠 마음이 일었으나, 그의 경험이 그토록 헛된 시도의 위험을 곧 일깨워 주었다."], ["Then he listened intently, and with painful uncertainty, for the sounds of hostility in the quarter where Uncas was left; but nothing was audible except the sighing of the wind, that began to sweep over the bosom of the forest in gusts which threatened a tempest.", "그러고 나서 그는 〈언카스〉를 남겨 둔 쪽에서 들려올 적대의 소리에 귀를 곤두세웠으나, 폭풍을 예고하듯 돌풍이 되어 숲의 품을 휩쓸기 시작한 바람의 한숨 소리 외에는 아무것도 들리지 않았다."], ["At length, yielding rather to his unusual impatience than taking counsel from his knowledge, he determined to bring matters to an issue, by unmasking his force, and proceeding cautiously, but steadily, up the stream.", "마침내, 제 지식에서 조언을 구하기보다 평소답지 않은 조바심에 굴복하여, 그는 병력을 드러내고, 조심스럽되 꾸준히 물줄기를 거슬러 올라감으로써 일을 결판내기로 작정했다."]], [["The scout had stood, while making his observations, sheltered by a brake, and his companions still lay in the bed of the ravine, through which the smaller stream debouched; but on hearing his low, though intelligible signal, the whole party stole up the bank, like so many dark spectres, and silently arranged themselves around him.", "정찰병은 관찰하는 동안 덤불에 가려 서 있었고, 동료들은 작은 물줄기가 흘러나오는 그 골짜기 바닥에 여전히 엎드려 있었다. 그러나 낮으면서도 알아들을 수 있는 그의 신호를 듣자, 무리 전체가 검은 유령들처럼 둑을 살금살금 기어올라, 말없이 그의 둘레에 늘어섰다."], ["Pointing in the direction he wished to proceed, Hawkeye advanced, the band breaking off in single files, and following so accurately in his footsteps, as if to leave it, if we except Heyward and David, the trail of but a single man.", "나아가려는 방향을 가리키며 〈호크아이〉가 앞으로 나아가자, 무리는 일렬종대로 갈라져, 〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉를 빼면 마치 단 한 사람의 발자취만 남기려는 듯, 그의 발자국을 어김없이 따라 디뎠다."]], [["The party was, however, scarcely uncovered before a volley from a dozen rifles was heard in their rear; and a Delaware leaping high into the air, like a wounded deer, fell at his whole length, perfectly dead.", "그러나 일행이 몸을 드러내자마자, 그들의 후미에서 열두 자루 소총의 일제 사격 소리가 들렸고, 한 〈델라웨어〉인이 상처 입은 사슴처럼 공중으로 높이 솟구쳤다가 온몸을 쭉 뻗고 완전히 숨이 끊겨 쓰러졌다."]], [["\"Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I feared some deviltry like this!\"", "이런 흉계가 있을 줄 알았지!\""], ["exclaimed the scout, in English; adding, with the quickness of thought, in his adopted tongue, \"To cover, men, and charge!\"", "정찰병이 영어로 외치고는, 생각만큼 빠르게 제가 익힌 그 말로 덧붙였다. \"엄폐하라, 모두, 돌격!\""]], [["The band dispersed at the word, and before Heyward had well recovered from his surprise, he found himself standing alone with David.", "그 말에 무리는 흩어졌고, 〈헤이워드〉가 놀라움에서 채 헤어나기도 전에, 그는 자신이 〈데이비드〉와 단둘이 서 있음을 깨달았다."], ["Luckily, the Hurons had already fallen back, and he was safe from their fire.", "다행히 〈휴런족〉은 이미 물러난 뒤라, 그는 그들의 사격으로부터 안전했다."], ["But this state of things was evidently to be of short continuance; for the scout set the example of pressing on their retreat, by discharging his rifle, and darting from tree to tree, as his enemy slowly yielded ground.", "그러나 이 상태가 오래갈 수 없음은 분명했으니, 정찰병이 소총을 쏘고 나무에서 나무로 내달리며, 적이 천천히 땅을 내주는 그 후퇴를 압박하는 본보기를 보였기 때문이다."]], [["It would seem that the assault had been made by a very small party of the Hurons, which, however, continued to increase in numbers, as it retired on its friends, until the return fire was very nearly, if not quite, equal to that maintained by the advancing Delawares.", "이 공격은 아주 작은 무리의 〈휴런족〉이 벌인 것이었던 듯하나, 그 무리는 제 편들 쪽으로 물러나며 차츰 수가 불어, 마침내 그들의 응사가 전진하는 〈델라웨어족〉의 사격에 거의, 아니 어쩌면 완전히 맞먹게 되었다."], ["Heyward threw himself among the combatants, and imitating the necessary caution of his companions, he made quick discharges with his own rifle.", "〈헤이워드〉는 전투원들 가운데 몸을 던져, 동료들에게 필요한 신중함을 본받아, 제 소총으로 재빠르게 사격을 거듭했다."], ["The contest now grew warm and stationary.", "이제 다툼은 뜨거워지면서도 한자리에 멈췄다."], ["Few were injured, as both parties kept their bodies as much protected as possible by the trees; never, indeed, exposing any part of their persons except in the act of taking aim.", "양편 모두 나무로 제 몸을 되도록 가렸기에, 다친 자는 적었으며, 실로 조준하는 순간 말고는 몸의 어느 부분도 결코 드러내지 않았다."], ["But the chances were gradually growing unfavorable to Hawkeye and his band.", "그러나 형세는 차츰 〈호크아이〉와 그의 무리에게 불리하게 돌아가고 있었다."], ["The quick-sighted scout perceived his danger, without knowing how to remedy it.", "눈썰미 빠른 정찰병은 어찌 손쓸지는 모른 채 제 위험을 알아챘다."], ["He saw it was more dangerous to retreat than to maintain his ground; while he found his enemy throwing out men on his flank, which rendered the task of keeping themselves covered so very difficult to the Delawares, as nearly to silence their fire.", "그는 땅을 지키는 것보다 후퇴하는 편이 더 위험함을 알았고, 한편으로 적이 자기 측면으로 병력을 내보내, 〈델라웨어족〉이 몸을 가리기가 너무도 어려워져 그들의 사격이 거의 멎을 지경임을 깨달았다."], ["At this embarrassing moment, when they began to think the whole of the hostile tribe was gradually encircling them, they heard the yell of combatants, and the rattling of arms, echoing under the arches of the wood, at the place where Uncas was posted; a bottom which, in a manner, lay beneath the ground on which Hawkeye and his party were contending.", "이 난처한 순간, 적 부족 전체가 차츰 자기들을 에워싸고 있다는 생각이 들기 시작했을 때, 그들은 〈언카스〉가 배치된 곳에서 전투원들의 함성과 병장기 부딪는 소리가 숲의 아치 아래 울려 퍼지는 것을 들었다. 그곳은 어떤 의미로 〈호크아이〉와 그의 무리가 싸우고 있던 땅보다 아래에 놓인 저지대였다."]], [["The effects of this attack were instantaneous, and to the scout and his friends greatly relieving.", "이 공격의 효과는 즉각적이었고, 정찰병과 그의 벗들에게는 크나큰 위안이었다."], ["It would seem that, while his own surprise had been anticipated, and had consequently failed, the enemy, in their turn, having been deceived in its object and in his numbers, had left too small a force to resist the impetuous onset of the young Mohican.", "자신의 기습이 미리 예상되어 결국 실패한 것처럼, 이번에는 적이 그 목표와 병력 수에 속아 넘어가, 젊은 〈모히칸족〉의 맹렬한 공격에 맞설 너무 적은 병력만을 남겨 둔 듯했다."], ["This fact was doubly apparent, by the rapid manner in which the battle in the forest rolled upwards towards the village, and by an instant falling off in the number of their assailants, who rushed to assist in maintaining the front, and, as it now proved to be, the principal point of defence.", "이 사실은 숲속의 전투가 마을 쪽으로 빠르게 위로 굴러 올라가는 것과, 그들을 공격하던 자들의 수가 순식간에 줄어드는 것으로 이중으로 분명해졌으니, 그들은 전면을, 이제 드러난 바로는 방어의 주요 지점을 지키는 데 보태려 달려간 것이었다."]], [["Animating his followers by his voice, and his own example, Hawkeye then gave the word to bear down upon their foes.", "목소리로, 또 제 본보기로 부하들을 북돋우며, 그때 〈호크아이〉는 적을 향해 밀고 들어가라는 호령을 내렸다."], ["The charge, in that rude species of warfare, consisted merely in pushing from cover to cover, nigher to the enemy; and in this manoeuvre he was instantly and successfully obeyed.", "그 거친 방식의 전쟁에서 돌격이란 그저 엄폐물에서 엄폐물로 적에게 더 가까이 다가가는 것에 지나지 않았고, 이 작전에서 그는 즉각, 또 성공적으로 복종받았다."], ["The Hurons were compelled to withdraw, and the scene of the contest rapidly changed from the more open ground on which it had commenced, to a spot where the assailed found a thicket to rest upon.", "〈휴런족〉은 물러설 수밖에 없었고, 다툼의 무대는 그것이 시작된 보다 트인 지대에서, 공격받은 자들이 의지할 덤불을 찾은 곳으로 빠르게 옮겨 갔다."], ["Here the struggle was protracted, arduous, and seemingly of doubtful issue; the Delawares, though none of them fell, beginning to bleed freely, in consequence of the disadvantage at which they were held.", "여기서 싸움은 길어지고 고되었으며, 결판이 의심스러운 듯 보였다. 〈델라웨어족〉은 비록 쓰러진 자는 없었으나, 그들이 놓인 불리함 탓에 피를 줄줄 흘리기 시작했다."]], [["In this crisis, Hawkeye found means to get behind the same tree as that which served for a cover to Heyward; most of his own combatants being within call, a little on his right, where they maintained rapid, though fruitless, discharges on their sheltered enemies.", "이 위기에서, 〈호크아이〉는 〈헤이워드〉의 엄폐물 노릇을 하던 바로 그 나무 뒤로 갈 방도를 찾아냈다. 그 자신의 전투원 대다수는 약간 오른쪽, 부르면 닿을 거리에 있으면서, 가려진 적을 향해 빠르나 헛된 사격을 거듭하고 있었다."]], [["\"You are a young man, major,\"", "\"자네는 젊은이야, 소령.\""], ["said the scout, dropping the butt of \"Killdeer\"", "정찰병이 〈킬디어〉의 개머리판을 땅에 내려놓고 총열에 기댄 채, 앞선 분주함에 약간 지친 듯 말했다."], ["to the earth, and leaning on the barrel, a little fatigued with his previous industry; \"and it may be your gift to lead armies at some future day ag'in these imps the Mingos, You may here see the philosophy of an Indian fight.", "\"그리고 언젠가 훗날 이 〈밍고족〉 같은 도깨비들에 맞서 군대를 이끄는 것이 자네 타고난 재주일지도 모르지. 자네는 여기서 인디언 싸움의 이치를 볼 수 있네."], ["It consists mainly in a ready hand, a quick eye, and a good cover.", "그건 주로 날랜 손, 빠른 눈, 그리고 좋은 엄폐물에 달려 있어."], ["Now, if you had a company of the Royal Americans here, in what manner would you set them to work in this business?\"", "자, 만약 자네한테 왕립 아메리카 부대 한 중대가 여기 있다면, 이 일에서 그들을 어떻게 부리겠나?\""]], [["\"The bayonet would make a road.\"", "\"총검이 길을 낼 것입니다.\""]], [["\"Ay, there is white reason in what you say; but a man must ask himself, in this wilderness, how many lives he can spare.", "\"그렇지, 자네 말에는 백인다운 도리가 있어. 허나 사람은 이 황야에서, 제가 목숨을 몇이나 내줄 수 있는지를 자문해야 하네."], ["No--horse,\"[29] continued the scout, shaking his head, like one who mused; \"horse, I am ashamed to say, must, sooner or later, decide these scrimmages.", "아니야 — 말일세[29].\" 정찰병이 무언가 골똘히 생각하는 사람처럼 고개를 저으며 말을 이었다."], ["The brutes are better than men, and to horse must we come at last.", "\"말이라니, 부끄럽지만, 머잖아 결국 이런 격전을 결판내는 건 말일세. 그 짐승들이 사람보다 낫고, 결국 우리도 말에 의지해야 할 게야."], ["Put a shodden hoof on the moccasin of a redskin; and if his rifle be once emptied, he will never stop to load it again.\"", "편자 박힌 발굽을 홍인종의 모카신 위에 올려 보게. 그자가 일단 소총을 비우고 나면, 결코 다시 장전하려고 멈추지 않을 테니.\""]], [["\"This is a subject that might better be discussed at another time,\"", "\"이건 다른 때에 의논하는 편이 나을 주제입니다.\""], ["returned Heyward; \"shall we charge?\"", "〈헤이워드〉가 대답했다. \"돌격할까요?\""]], [["\"I see no contradiction to the gifts of any man, in passing his breathing spells in useful reflections,\"", "\"제 숨 돌릴 짬을 쓸모 있는 궁리로 보낸다고 해서, 누구의 타고난 재주에 어긋날 건 없다고 보네.\""], ["the scout replied.", "정찰병이 대답했다."], ["\"As to a rush, I little relish such a measure; for a scalp or two must be thrown away in the attempt.", "\"돌진이라면, 나는 그런 방책이 그리 내키지 않아. 시도하다 보면 머리 가죽 한둘은 버려야 하니까."], ["And yet,\"", "하지만,\""], ["he added, bending his head aside, to catch the sounds of the distant combat, \"if we are to be of use to Uncas, these knaves in our front must be got rid of!\"", "그는 멀리서 들려오는 전투의 소리를 잡으려 고개를 옆으로 기울이며 덧붙였다. \"우리가 〈언카스〉에게 쓸모가 되려면, 우리 앞의 이 악당들을 처치해야 하네!\""]], [["Then turning, with a prompt and decided air, he called aloud to his Indians, in their own language.", "그러고는 재빠르고 단호한 기색으로 돌아서서, 그는 제 인디언들에게 그들의 말로 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["His words were answered by a shout; and, at a given signal, each warrior made a swift movement around his particular tree.", "그의 말에 함성이 답했고, 정해진 신호에 맞춰 전사들은 저마다 제 나무를 빙 돌아 잽싸게 움직였다."], ["The sight of so many dark bodies, glancing before their eyes at the same instant, drew a hasty, and consequently an ineffectual fire from the Hurons.", "그토록 많은 검은 몸뚱이가 한순간에 눈앞으로 휙 스치는 광경에, 〈휴런족〉은 황급한, 따라서 효과 없는 사격을 했다."], ["Without stopping to breathe, the Delawares leaped, in long bounds, towards the wood, like so many panthers springing upon their prey.", "숨 돌릴 틈도 없이, 〈델라웨어족〉은 먹잇감에 덮치는 표범 떼처럼 긴 도약으로 숲을 향해 뛰어들었다."], ["Hawkeye was in front, brandishing his terrible rifle, and animating his followers by his example.", "〈호크아이〉가 앞장서서, 그 무시무시한 소총을 휘두르며 제 본보기로 부하들을 북돋웠다."], ["A few of the older and more cunning Hurons, who had not been deceived by the artifice which had been practised to draw their fire, now made a close and deadly discharge of their pieces, and justified the apprehensions of the scout, by felling three of his foremost warriors.", "사격을 끌어내려 부린 술책에 속지 않은 더 늙고 더 교활한 〈휴런족〉 몇이 이제 바짝 다가들어 치명적인 일제 사격을 가하여, 정찰병의 맨 앞 전사 셋을 쓰러뜨림으로써 그의 우려를 입증했다."], ["But the shock was insufficient to repel the impetus of the charge.", "그러나 그 충격은 돌격의 기세를 물리치기에는 부족했다."], ["The Delawares broke into the cover with the ferocity of their natures, and swept away every trace of resistance by the fury of the onset.", "〈델라웨어족〉은 제 천성의 사나움으로 엄폐물 안으로 쳐들어가, 맹렬한 공격으로 저항의 흔적을 모조리 쓸어버렸다."]], [["The combat endured only for an instant, hand to hand, and then the assailed yielded ground rapidly, until they reached the opposite margin of the thicket, where they clung to the cover, with the sort of obstinacy that is so often witnessed in hunted brutes.", "전투는 손에서 손으로 맞붙은 채 한순간만 이어졌고, 그러고 나서 공격받은 자들은 빠르게 땅을 내주어, 마침내 덤불의 맞은편 가장자리에 다다랐다. 거기서 그들은 쫓기는 짐승에게서 그토록 흔히 보이는 그 일종의 고집스러움으로 엄폐물에 매달렸다."], ["At this critical moment, when the success of the struggle was again becoming doubtful, the crack of the rifle was heard behind the Hurons, and a bullet came whizzing from among some beaver lodges, which were situated in the clearing, in their rear, and was followed by the fierce and appalling yell of the war-whoop.", "이 위태로운 순간, 다툼의 승패가 다시 의심스러워지고 있을 때, 〈휴런족〉의 뒤에서 소총 소리가 났고, 그들 후방의 공터에 자리한 비버 집들 사이에서 한 발의 총알이 윙 날아왔으며, 그 뒤로 사납고 소름 끼치는 전투의 함성이 이어졌다."]], [["\"There speaks the Sagamore!\"", "\"저건 〈사가모어〉의 소리다!\""], ["shouted Hawkeye, answering the cry with his own stentorian voice; \"we have them now in face and back!\"", "〈호크아이〉가 제 우렁찬 목소리로 그 함성에 답하며 외쳤다. \"이제 앞뒤로 저놈들을 잡았다!\""]], [["The effect on the Hurons was instantaneous.", "〈휴런족〉에게 미친 효과는 즉각적이었다."], ["Discouraged by an assault from a quarter that left them no opportunity for cover, their warriors uttered a common yell of disappointment, and breaking off in a body, they spread themselves across the opening, heedless of every consideration but flight.", "몸을 가릴 기회조차 주지 않는 방향에서의 공격에 기가 꺾여, 그들의 전사들은 한결같은 실망의 함성을 내질렀고, 한 덩어리로 흩어져, 도망 말고는 아무것도 아랑곳없이 공터를 가로질러 퍼져 나갔다."], ["Many fell, in making the experiment, under the bullets and the blows of the pursuing Delawares.", "많은 자가 그렇게 시도하다가 쫓는 〈델라웨어족〉의 총알과 매질 아래 쓰러졌다."]], [["We shall not pause to detail the meeting between the scout and Chingachgook, or the more touching interview that Duncan held with Munro.", "정찰병과 〈칭가치국〉의 만남이나, 〈던컨〉이 〈먼로〉와 나눈 한층 가슴 뭉클한 해후를 우리는 멈추어 자세히 그리지 않겠다."], ["A few brief and hurried words served to explain the state of things to both parties; and then Hawkeye pointing out the Sagamore to his band, resigned the chief authority into the hands of the Mohican chief.", "짧고 다급한 몇 마디가 양편에 사정을 설명해 주었다. 그러고 나서 〈호크아이〉는 제 무리에게 〈사가모어〉를 가리켜 보이며, 최고 지휘권을 그 〈모히칸족〉 추장의 손에 넘겼다."], ["Chingachgook assumed the station to which his birth and experience gave him so distinguished a claim, with the grave dignity that always gives force to the mandates of a native warrior.", "〈칭가치국〉은, 토착 전사의 명령에 늘 힘을 실어 주는 그 진중한 위엄으로, 제 혈통과 경험이 그토록 빼어난 자격을 부여한 그 자리를 떠맡았다."], ["Following the footsteps of the scout, he led the party back through the thicket, his men scalping the fallen Hurons, and secreting the bodies of their own dead as they proceeded, until they gained a point where the former was content to make a halt.", "정찰병의 발자취를 좇아, 그는 무리를 이끌고 덤불을 거슬러 돌아갔으니, 부하들은 나아가면서 쓰러진 〈휴런족〉의 머리 가죽을 벗기고 제 편 전사자들의 시신은 숨겼으며, 마침내 정찰병이 멈추어 흡족해할 만한 지점에 다다랐다."]], [["The warriors, who had breathed themselves freely in the preceding struggle, were now posted on a bit of level ground, sprinkled with trees in sufficient numbers to conceal them.", "앞선 다툼에서 한껏 숨을 고른 전사들은, 이제 자기들을 가리기에 족할 만큼 나무가 점점이 흩어진 평평한 땅 한 자락에 자리를 잡았다."], ["The land fell away rather precipitately in front, and beneath their eyes stretched, for several miles, a narrow, dark, and wooded vale.", "땅은 앞쪽으로 꽤 가파르게 떨어졌고, 그들의 눈 아래로는 좁고 어둑하며 숲이 우거진 골짜기가 몇 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어 있었다."], ["It was through this dense and dark forest that Uncas was still contending with the main body of the Hurons.", "〈언카스〉가 〈휴런족〉의 본대와 여전히 다투고 있는 곳이 바로 이 빽빽하고 어두운 숲을 뚫고서였다."]], [["The Mohican and his friends advanced to the brow of the hill, and listened, with practised ears, to the sounds of the combat.", "〈모히칸족〉과 그의 벗들은 언덕마루로 나아가, 단련된 귀로 전투의 소리에 귀를 기울였다."], ["A few birds hovered over the leafy bosom of the valley, frightened from their secluded nests; and here and there a light vapory cloud, which seemed already blending with the atmosphere, arose above the trees, and indicated some spot where the struggle had been fierce and stationary.", "새 몇 마리가 외딴 보금자리에서 놀라 골짜기의 잎 무성한 품 위를 맴돌았고, 여기저기서 이미 대기에 섞여 드는 듯한 옅고 안개 같은 구름이 나무들 위로 피어올라, 다툼이 격렬하고 한자리에 멈춰 있던 어떤 지점을 가리켰다."]], [["\"The fight is coming up the ascent,\"", "\"싸움이 비탈을 올라오고 있군요.\""], ["said Duncan, pointing in the direction of a new explosion of fire-arms; \"we are too much in the centre of their line to be effective.\"", "새로운 총성이 터진 방향을 가리키며 〈던컨〉이 말했다. \"우리는 저들의 전선 한복판에 너무 들어와 있어 쓸모가 없습니다.\""]], [["\"They will incline into the hollow, where the cover is thicker,\"", "\"저들은 엄폐물이 더 두꺼운 골짜기 쪽으로 기울 거야.\""], ["said the scout, \"and that will leave us well on their flank.", "정찰병이 말했다. \"그러면 우리가 저들의 측면을 단단히 잡게 되지."], ["Go, Sagamore; you will hardly be in time to give the whoop, and lead on the young men.", "가게, 〈사가모어〉. 자네는 함성을 지르며 젊은이들을 이끌기에 시간이 빠듯할 걸세."], ["I will fight this scrimmage with warriors of my own color.", "나는 내 살빛을 한 전사들과 이 격전을 치르겠네."], ["You know me, Mohican; not a Huron of them all shall cross the swell, into your rear, without the notice of 'Killdeer.'\"", "자네는 나를 알지, 〈모히칸〉. 저놈들 가운데 단 한 〈휴런족〉도 〈킬디어〉의 통보 없이는 둔덕을 넘어 자네 후방으로 들지 못할 게야.\""]], [["The Indian chief paused another moment to consider the signs of the contest, which was now rolling rapidly up the ascent, a certain evidence that the Delawares triumphed; nor did he actually quit the place until admonished of the proximity of his friends, as well as enemies, by the bullets of the former, which began to patter among the dried leaves on the ground, like the bits of falling hail which precede the bursting of the tempest.", "그 인디언 추장은, 이제 빠르게 비탈을 굴러 올라오는 — 〈델라웨어족〉이 이기고 있다는 확실한 증거인 — 다툼의 징후를 살피려 또 한순간 멈추었다. 그리고 그는 적뿐 아니라 제 벗들이 가까이 왔음을, 폭풍이 터지기 전에 떨어지는 자잘한 우박처럼 땅의 마른 잎 사이로 후드득 떨어지기 시작한 아군의 총알로 깨닫고 나서야 비로소 그곳을 떴다."], ["Hawkeye and his three companions withdrew a few paces to a shelter, and awaited the issue with calmness, that nothing but great practice could impart in such a scene.", "〈호크아이〉와 그의 세 동료는 엄폐물로 몇 발짝 물러나, 그런 장면에서는 오랜 단련이 아니고서는 결코 줄 수 없는 침착함으로 결말을 기다렸다."]], [["It was not long before the reports of the rifles began to lose the echoes of the woods, and to sound like weapons discharged in the open air.", "오래지 않아 소총의 발사음은 숲의 메아리를 잃고, 트인 데서 쏘는 무기 소리처럼 들리기 시작했다."], ["Then a warrior appeared, here and there, driven to the skirts of the forest, and rallying as he entered the clearing, as at the place where the final stand was to be made.", "그러고는 한 전사가 여기저기 숲의 변두리로 내몰려, 마지막으로 버틸 곳인 양 공터에 들어서며 대오를 추스르는 모습이 나타났다."], ["These were soon joined by the others, until a long line of swarthy figures was to be seen clinging to the cover with the obstinacy of desperation.", "이들에게 곧 다른 자들이 합류하여, 마침내 한 줄로 길게 늘어선 거뭇한 형상들이 절망의 고집으로 엄폐물에 매달린 것이 보였다."], ["Heyward began to grow impatient, and turned his eyes anxiously in the direction of Chingachgook.", "〈헤이워드〉는 차츰 조바심이 일어, 〈칭가치국〉 쪽으로 불안한 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["The chief was seated on a rock, with nothing visible but his calm visage, considering the spectacle with an eye as deliberate as if he were posted there merely to view the struggle.", "추장은 바위에 앉아, 차분한 얼굴만 드러낸 채, 마치 그저 다툼을 구경하려 거기 자리한 양 신중한 눈으로 그 광경을 살피고 있었다."]], [["\"The time is come for the Delawares to strike!\"", "\"〈델라웨어족〉이 칠 때가 왔습니다!\""], ["said Duncan.", "〈던컨〉이 말했다."]], [["\"Not so, not so,\"", "\"아니, 아니야.\""], ["returned the scout; \"when he scents his friends, he will let them know that he is here.", "정찰병이 대답했다. \"그는 제 벗들의 냄새를 맡으면, 자기가 여기 있다는 걸 저들에게 알릴 거야."], ["See, see; the knaves are getting in that clump of pines, like bees settling after their flight.", "보게, 보라고. 저 악당들이 저 소나무 떨기 속으로 들어가는군."], ["By the Lord, a squaw might put a bullet into the centre of such a knot of dark skins!\"", "마치 날아갔다가 다시 내려앉는 벌 떼처럼 말이야. 맙소사, 저런 거뭇한 살갗 매듭 한복판에는 여인네라도 총알 한 발 박아 넣겠네!\""]], [["At that instant the whoop was given, and a dozen Hurons fell by a discharge from Chingachgook and his band.", "바로 그 순간 함성이 떨어졌고, 〈칭가치국〉과 그의 무리의 일제 사격에 〈휴런족〉 열둘이 쓰러졌다."], ["The shout that followed was answered by a single war-cry from the forest, and a yell passed through the air that sounded as if a thousand throats were united in a common effort.", "그 뒤를 이은 외침에 숲에서 단 한 줄기 전투의 함성이 답했고, 마치 천 명의 목구멍이 한 가지 노력으로 한데 어우러진 듯한 고함이 허공을 갈랐다."], ["The Hurons staggered, deserting the centre of their line, and Uncas issued from the forest through the opening they left, at the head of a hundred warriors.", "〈휴런족〉은 휘청이며 제 전선의 중앙을 버렸고, 그들이 남긴 틈을 뚫고 〈언카스〉가 백 명의 전사를 거느리고 숲에서 뛰쳐나왔다."]], [["Waving his hands right and left, the young chief pointed out the enemy to his followers, who separated in pursuit.", "두 손을 좌우로 내저으며, 젊은 추장은 부하들에게 적을 가리켜 보였고, 그들은 갈라져 추격에 나섰다."], ["The war now divided, both wings of the broken Hurons seeking protection in the woods again, hotly pressed by the victorious warriors of the Lenape.", "이제 싸움이 갈라져, 무너진 〈휴런족〉의 양 날개가 다시 숲에서 몸을 숨기려 했으나, 승리한 〈레나페족〉의 전사들에게 거세게 쫓겼다."], ["A minute might have passed, but the sounds were already receding in different directions, and gradually losing their distinctness beneath the echoing arches of the woods.", "일 분쯤 지났을까, 소리들은 이미 서로 다른 방향으로 멀어지며, 숲의 메아리치는 아치 아래 차츰 또렷함을 잃어 갔다."], ["One little knot of Hurons, however, had disdained to seek a cover, and were retiring, like lions at bay, slowly and sullenly up the acclivity which Chingachgook and his band had just deserted, to mingle more closely in the fray.", "그러나 한 작은 〈휴런족〉 무리는 몸을 숨기기를 마다하고, 궁지에 몰린 사자들처럼, 더 가까이 난전에 끼어들고자, 〈칭가치국〉과 그의 무리가 막 떠난 그 비탈을 천천히 음울하게 물러 올라가고 있었다."], ["Magua was conspicuous in this party, both by his fierce and savage mien, and by the air of haughty authority he yet maintained.", "〈머과〉는 그 사납고 야만스러운 낯빛으로도, 또 여태 지키고 있는 오만한 권위의 기색으로도, 이 무리에서 두드러졌다."]], [["In his eagerness to expedite the pursuit, Uncas had left himself nearly alone; but the moment his eyes caught the figure of Le Subtil, every other consideration was forgotten.", "추격을 서두르려는 열의에, 〈언카스〉는 거의 홀로 남게 되었다. 그러나 그의 눈이 〈르 쉬빌〉의 모습을 붙잡은 순간, 다른 모든 고려는 잊혔다."], ["Raising his cry of battle, which recalled some six or seven warriors, and reckless of the disparity of their numbers, he rushed upon his enemy.", "예닐곱 전사를 불러 모은 그 전투의 함성을 지르며, 수의 차이도 아랑곳없이, 그는 적을 향해 돌진했다."], ["Le Renard, who watched the movement, paused to receive him with secret joy.", "그 움직임을 지켜보던 〈르 르나르〉는, 남모를 기쁨으로 그를 맞으려 멈추었다."], ["But at the moment when he thought the rashness of his impetuous young assailant had left him at his mercy, another shout was given, and La Longue Carabine was seen rushing to the rescue, attended by all his white associates.", "그러나 제 성급한 젊은 공격자의 무모함이 그를 제 손아귀에 넣어 주었다고 여긴 그 순간, 또 한 번 함성이 일었고, 〈라 롱그 카라빈〉이 백인 동료들을 모두 거느리고 구하러 달려오는 것이 보였다."], ["The Huron instantly turned, and commenced a rapid retreat up the ascent.", "그 〈휴런족〉은 즉시 돌아서서, 비탈을 빠르게 후퇴하기 시작했다."]], [["There was no time for greetings or congratulations; for Uncas, though unconscious of the presence of his friends, continued the pursuit with the velocity of the wind.", "인사나 축하를 나눌 겨를은 없었으니, 〈언카스〉가 비록 제 벗들이 와 있음을 알지 못한 채로도, 바람 같은 속도로 추격을 이어 갔기 때문이다."], ["In vain Hawkeye called to him to respect the covers; the young Mohican braved the dangerous fire of his enemies, and soon compelled them to a flight as swift as his own headlong speed.", "〈호크아이〉가 그에게 엄폐물을 존중하라고 외쳤으나 헛일이었다. 젊은 〈모히칸족〉은 적들의 위험한 사격에 맞서며, 이내 그들을 제 곤두박질 같은 속도만큼이나 빠른 도주로 몰아넣었다."], ["It was fortunate that the race was of short continuance, and that the white men were much favored by their position, or the Delaware would soon have outstripped all his companions, and fallen a victim to his own temerity.", "그 경주가 오래가지 않았던 것, 또 백인들이 제 위치 덕을 크게 본 것이 다행이었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그 〈델라웨어〉인은 곧 동료들을 모두 앞질러, 제 무모함의 제물이 되었을 것이다."], ["But ere such a calamity could happen, the pursuers and pursued entered the Wyandot village, within striking distance of each other.", "그러나 그런 참사가 일어나기 전에, 쫓는 자와 쫓기는 자는 서로 일격이 닿을 거리를 두고 〈와이언도트족〉 마을로 들어섰다."]], [["Excited by the presence of their dwellings, and tired of the chase, the Hurons now made a stand, and fought around their council-lodge with the fury of despair.", "제 거처가 눈앞에 있다는 데 흥분하고, 또 추격에 지친 〈휴런족〉은 이제 멈춰 서서, 절망의 광기로 제 평의회소 둘레에서 싸웠다."], ["The onset and the issue were like the passage and destruction of a whirlwind.", "그 들이침과 결말은 회오리바람이 휩쓸고 지나가며 파괴하는 것과도 같았다."], ["The tomahawk of Uncas, the blows of Hawkeye, and even the still nervous arm of Munro, were all busy for that passing moment, and the ground was quickly strewed with their enemies.", "〈언카스〉의 토마호크, 〈호크아이〉의 일격, 심지어 아직 힘이 남은 〈먼로〉의 팔까지, 그 스쳐 가는 순간 모두가 분주했고, 땅은 곧 그들의 적으로 어지러이 뒤덮였다."], ["Still Magua, though daring and much exposed, escaped from every effort against his life, with that sort of fabled protection that was made to overlook the fortunes of favored heroes in the legends of ancient poetry.", "그럼에도 〈머과〉는, 대담하게 몸을 한껏 드러내고도, 옛 시가의 전설에서 총애받는 영웅들의 운명을 보살피려 마련된 듯한 그 일종의 신화적 가호로, 제 목숨을 노린 온갖 공격에서 벗어났다."], ["Raising a yell that spoke volumes of anger and disappointment, the subtle chief, when he saw his comrades fallen, darted away from the place, attended by his two only surviving friends, leaving the Delawares engaged in stripping the dead of the bloody trophies of their victory.", "제 동료들이 쓰러진 것을 본 그 교활한 추장은, 분노와 실망을 가득 토해 내는 함성을 지르며, 살아남은 단 둘뿐인 벗을 거느리고 그곳에서 잽싸게 달아났으니, 〈델라웨어족〉은 죽은 자들에게서 승리의 피 묻은 전리품을 벗겨 내는 데 여념이 없었다."]], [["But Uncas, who had vainly sought him in the _melee_ bounded forward in pursuit; Hawkeye, Heyward, and David still pressing on his footsteps.", "그러나 난전 속에서 그를 헛되이 찾던 〈언카스〉는 추격에 나서 앞으로 튀어 나갔고, 〈호크아이〉와 〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉가 여전히 그의 발자취를 바짝 좇았다."], ["The utmost that the scout could effect, was to keep the muzzle of his rifle a little in advance of his friend, to whom, however, it answered every purpose of a charmed shield.", "정찰병이 할 수 있는 최선이라곤, 제 소총의 총구를 벗보다 약간 앞서 두는 것뿐이었으나, 그것은 〈언카스〉에게 어떤 마법의 방패와 다름없는 구실을 했다."], ["Once Magua appeared disposed to make another and a final effort to revenge his losses; but, abandoning his intention as soon as demonstrated, he leaped into a thicket of bushes, through which he was followed by his enemies, and suddenly entered the mouth of the cave already known to the reader.", "한 번 〈머과〉는 또 한 번, 그것도 마지막으로, 제 손실을 갚으려는 시도를 할 듯이 보였다. 그러나 그 뜻을 드러내기가 무섭게 단념하고, 그는 덤불 속으로 뛰어들었고, 적들이 그 사이로 그를 좇았으며, 그는 별안간 독자에게 이미 알려진 그 동굴 입구로 들어섰다."], ["Hawkeye, who had only forborne to fire in tenderness to Uncas, raised a shout of success, and proclaimed aloud, that now they were certain of their game.", "오직 〈언카스〉를 아끼는 마음에서 사격을 삼갔던 〈호크아이〉는 성공의 함성을 지르며, 이제 사냥감을 확실히 잡았노라고 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["The pursuers dashed into the long and narrow entrance, in time to catch a glimpse of the retreating forms of the Hurons.", "추격자들은 그 길고 좁은 입구로 뛰어들어, 물러가는 〈휴런족〉의 모습을 흘끗 잡을 만큼 때맞춰 들어섰다."], ["Their passage through the natural galleries and subterraneous apartments of the cavern was preceded by the shrieks and cries of hundreds of women and children.", "동굴의 자연적인 회랑과 지하의 방들을 지나는 그들의 통행에 앞서, 수백 명의 여자와 아이의 비명과 울부짖음이 먼저 터져 나왔다."], ["The place, seen by its dim and uncertain light, appeared like the shades of the infernal regions, across which unhappy ghosts and savage demons were flitting in multitudes.", "그곳은 그 흐릿하고 어렴풋한 빛 속에서, 불행한 망령들과 사나운 악귀들이 떼 지어 스쳐 다니는 지옥의 그늘처럼 보였다."]], [["Still Uncas kept his eye on Magua, as if life to him possessed but a single object.", "여전히 〈언카스〉는, 마치 그에게 목숨이란 단 하나의 목표만을 지닌 양, 〈머과〉에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["Heyward and the scout still pressed on his rear, actuated, though possibly in a less degree, by a common feeling.", "〈헤이워드〉와 정찰병은, 아마 정도는 덜할지언정 같은 감정에 떠밀려, 여전히 그 뒤를 바짝 좇았다."], ["But their way was becoming intricate, in those dark and gloomy passages, and the glimpses of the retiring warriors less distinct and frequent; and for a moment the trace was believed to be lost, when a white robe was seen fluttering in the farther extremity of a passage that seemed to lead up the mountain.", "그러나 그 어둡고 음울한 통로에서 그들의 길은 점점 갈피를 잡기 어려워졌고, 물러가는 전사들이 보이는 것은 한결 흐릿하고 뜸해졌다. 그리하여 한순간 자취를 놓쳤다고 여겼을 때, 산 위로 이어지는 듯한 통로의 저 먼 끝에서 흰옷 한 자락이 펄럭이는 것이 보였다."]], [["\"'Tis Cora!\"", "\"〈코라〉다!\""], ["exclaimed Heyward, in a voice in which horror and delight were wildly mingled.", "공포와 환희가 사납게 뒤엉킨 목소리로 〈헤이워드〉가 외쳤다."]], [["\"Cora!", "\"〈코라〉!"], ["Cora!\"", "〈코라〉!\""], ["echoed Uncas, bending forward like a deer.", "〈언카스〉가 사슴처럼 몸을 앞으로 굽히며 메아리처럼 따라 외쳤다."]], [["\"'Tis the maiden!\" shouted the scout, \"Courage, lady; we come!--we come!\"", "\"저 처녀다!\" 정찰병이 소리쳤다. \"용기를 내시오, 아가씨. 우리가 갑니다! 우리가 가요!\""]], [["The chase was renewed with a diligence rendered tenfold encouraging by this glimpse of the captive.", "이 사로잡힌 이의 모습을 흘끗 본 덕에 열 배는 더 힘이 솟은 부지런함으로 추격이 새로 시작되었다."], ["But the way was rugged, broken, and in spots nearly impassable.", "그러나 길은 험하고 끊긴 데다, 군데군데 거의 지날 수 없었다."], ["Uncas abandoned his rifle, and leaped forward with headlong precipitation.", "〈언카스〉는 제 소총을 버리고, 곤두박질치는 황급함으로 앞으로 뛰어들었다."], ["Heyward rashly imitated his example, though both were, a moment afterwards, admonished of its madness, by hearing the bellowing of a piece, that the Hurons found time to discharge down the passage in the rocks, the bullet from which even gave the young Mohican a slight wound.", "〈헤이워드〉도 무모하게 그 본보기를 따랐으나, 곧 잠시 후 두 사람은 그 미친 짓을 일깨움받았으니, 〈휴런족〉이 짬을 내어 바위 통로 아래로 쏜 총포의 굉음을 들었고, 그 총알이 젊은 〈모히칸족〉에게 가벼운 상처마저 입혔기 때문이다."]], [["\"We must close!\"", "\"바짝 다가가야 하네!\" 정찰병이 필사적인 도약으로 벗들을 지나치며 말했다."], ["said the scout, passing his friends by a desperate leap; \"the knaves will pick us all off at this distance; and see, they hold the maiden so as to shield themselves!\"", "\"이 거리에서는 저 악당들이 우리를 하나씩 다 쏘아 떨어뜨릴 거야. 그리고 보게, 저놈들이 제 몸을 가리려고 저 처녀를 붙들고 있어!\""]], [["Though his words were unheeded, or rather unheard, his example was followed by his companions, who, by incredible exertions, got near enough to the fugitives to perceive that Cora was borne along between the two warriors, while Magua prescribed the direction and manner of their flight.", "그의 말은 무시되었거나, 아니 오히려 들리지 않았으나, 그의 본보기는 동료들에게 따라졌으니, 그들은 믿기 어려운 분투로 도망자들에게 충분히 가까이 다가가, 〈코라〉가 두 전사 사이에 끼어 떠메여 가고, 〈머과〉가 그 도주의 방향과 방식을 일러 주고 있음을 알아보았다."], ["At this moment the forms of all four were strongly drawn against an opening in the sky, and they disappeared.", "이 순간 넷의 모습이 하늘의 트인 틈을 배경으로 또렷이 그려졌다가, 사라졌다."], ["Nearly frantic with disappointment, Uncas and Heyward increased efforts that already seemed super-human, and they issued from the cavern on the side of the mountain, in time to note the route of the pursued.", "실망으로 거의 미칠 지경이 된 〈언카스〉와 〈헤이워드〉는, 이미 인간을 넘어선 듯하던 분투를 더욱 더했고, 쫓기는 자들의 경로를 알아챌 만큼 때맞춰 산허리 쪽 동굴에서 빠져나왔다."], ["The course lay up the ascent, and still continued hazardous and laborious.", "그 길은 비탈을 올랐고, 여전히 위태롭고 고되었다."]], [["Encumbered by his rifle, and, perhaps, not sustained by so deep an interest in the captive as his companions, the scout suffered the latter to precede him a little, Uncas, in his turn, taking the lead of Heyward.", "소총에 거추장스러웠던 데다, 어쩌면 동료들만큼 그 사로잡힌 이에게 깊은 관심을 품지 않았던 정찰병은, 동료들이 자기보다 조금 앞서게 두었고, 〈언카스〉는 이번에는 〈헤이워드〉보다 앞장섰다."], ["In this manner, rocks, precipices, and difficulties were surmounted in an incredibly short space, that at another time, and under other circumstances, would have been deemed almost insuperable.", "이런 식으로, 다른 때 다른 상황이었다면 거의 넘을 수 없다 여겨졌을 바위와 벼랑과 난관이 믿기 어려울 만큼 짧은 사이에 극복되었다."], ["But the impetuous young men were rewarded, by finding that, encumbered with Cora, the Hurons were losing ground in the race.", "그러나 성급한 젊은이들은, 〈코라〉에 거추장스러운 〈휴런족〉이 경주에서 땅을 잃고 있음을 알아내는 것으로 보답받았다."]], [["\"Stay, dog of the Wyandots!\"", "\"멈춰라, 〈와이언도트족〉의 개야!\""], ["exclaimed Uncas, shaking his bright tomahawk at Magua; \"a Delaware girl calls stay!\"", "〈언카스〉가 〈머과〉를 향해 제 번뜩이는 토마호크를 흔들며 외쳤다. \"한 〈델라웨어〉 처녀가 멈추라 한다!\""]], [["\"I will go no farther,\"", "\"나는 더 가지 않겠다.\""], ["cried Cora, stepping unexpectedly on a ledge of rocks, that overhung a deep precipice, at no great distance from the summit of the mountain.", "〈코라〉가 산마루에서 그리 멀지 않은, 깊은 벼랑 위로 내민 바위 턱에 뜻밖에 발을 디디며 외쳤다."], ["\"Kill me if thou wilt, detestable Huron; I will go no farther.\"", "\"죽이고 싶거든 죽여라, 가증스러운 〈휴런족〉아. 나는 더 가지 않겠다.\""]], [["The supporters of the maiden raised their ready tomahawks with the impious joy that fiends are thought to take in mischief, but Magua stayed the uplifted arms.", "그 처녀를 떠받치던 자들이, 마귀들이 해악에서 느낀다고들 하는 그 불경한 기쁨으로 손에 든 토마호크를 치켜들었으나, 〈머과〉가 그 쳐든 팔을 멈추게 했다."], ["The Huron chief, after casting the weapons he had wrested from his companions over the rock, drew his knife, and turned to his captive, with a look in which conflicting passions fiercely contended.", "그 〈휴런족〉 추장은 제 동료들에게서 빼앗은 무기들을 바위 너머로 던져 버린 뒤, 칼을 빼 들고, 서로 다투는 격정들이 사납게 맞부딪는 표정으로 제 포로에게 돌아섰다."]], [["\"Woman,\"", "\"여자여,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, \"choose; the wigwam or the knife of Le Subtil!\"", "\"골라라. 천막이냐, 〈르 쉬빌〉의 칼이냐!\""]], [["Cora regarded him not, but dropping on her knees, she raised her eyes and stretched her arms towards heaven, saying, in a meek and yet confiding voice,--", "〈코라〉는 그를 거들떠보지 않고, 무릎을 꿇으며 눈을 들어 하늘을 향해 두 팔을 뻗고, 순하면서도 믿음에 찬 목소리로 말했다."]], [["\"I am thine!", "\"저는 당신의 것입니다!"], ["do with me as thou seest best!\"", "당신이 가장 좋다고 여기는 대로 하소서!\""]], [["\"Woman,\"", "\"여자여.\""], ["repeated Magua, hoarsely, and endeavoring in vain to catch a glance from her serene and beaming eye, \"choose!\"", "〈머과〉가 쉰 목소리로 되풀이하며, 그 고요하고 빛나는 눈에서 한 줄기 눈길을 붙잡으려 헛되이 애썼다. \"골라라!\""]], [["But Cora neither heard nor heeded his demand.", "그러나 〈코라〉는 그의 요구를 듣지도, 아랑곳하지도 않았다."], ["The form of the Huron trembled in every fibre, and he raised his arm on high, but dropped it again with a bewildered air, like one who doubted.", "그 〈휴런족〉의 몸은 온 마디가 떨렸고, 그는 팔을 높이 쳐들었으나, 의심하는 자처럼 어리둥절한 기색으로 다시 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Once more he struggled with himself and lifted the keen weapon again; but just then a piercing cry was heard above them, and Uncas appeared, leaping frantically, from a fearful height, upon the ledge.", "한 번 더 그는 제 자신과 씨름하며 그 날카로운 무기를 다시 들었다. 그러나 바로 그때 그들 위에서 찢어지는 듯한 외침이 들렸고, 〈언카스〉가 까마득한 높이에서 그 바위 턱 위로 미친 듯이 뛰어내리며 나타났다."], ["Magua recoiled a step; and one of his assistants, profiting by the chance, sheathed his own knife in the bosom of Cora.", "〈머과〉는 한 걸음 물러섰다. 그리고 그의 조력자 하나가 그 틈을 타서, 제 칼을 〈코라〉의 가슴에 박았다."]], [["The Huron sprang like a tiger on his offending and already retreating countryman, but the falling form of Uncas separated the unnatural combatants.", "그 〈휴런족〉은 죄를 저지르고 이미 물러서는 동족에게 호랑이처럼 덮쳤으나, 떨어지는 〈언카스〉의 몸이 그 패륜의 싸움꾼들을 갈라놓았다."], ["Diverted from his object by this interruption, and maddened by the murder he had just witnessed, Magua buried his weapon in the back of the prostrate Delaware, uttering an unearthly shout as he committed the dastardly deed.", "이 방해로 제 목표에서 빗나가고, 방금 목격한 그 살인에 미쳐 버린 〈머과〉는, 엎어진 〈델라웨어〉인의 등에 제 무기를 묻으며, 그 비열한 짓을 저지를 적에 이 세상 것이 아닌 듯한 함성을 내질렀다."], ["But Uncas arose from the blow, as the wounded panther turns upon his foe, and struck the murderer of Cora to his feet, by an effort in which the last of his failing strength was expended.", "그러나 〈언카스〉는, 상처 입은 표범이 제 적에게 돌아서듯 그 일격에서 일어나, 〈코라〉를 죽인 자를, 스러져 가는 마지막 힘을 다 쏟은 안간힘으로 쳐서 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["Then, with a stern and steady look, he turned to Le Subtil, and indicated by the expression of his eye, all that he would do, had not the power deserted him.", "그러고는 준엄하고 흔들림 없는 눈길로 〈르 쉬빌〉에게 돌아서서, 힘이 자기를 저버리지만 않았더라면 무엇을 하려 했는지를 그 눈빛으로 드러냈다."], ["The latter seized the nerveless arm of the unresisting Delaware, and passed his knife into his bosom three several times, before his victim, still keeping his gaze riveted on his enemy with a look of inextinguishable scorn, fell dead at his feet.", "〈르 쉬빌〉은 저항하지 않는 〈델라웨어〉인의 맥 풀린 팔을 붙잡고, 제 칼을 그의 가슴에 세 차례나 거듭 찔러 넣었으니, 그 제물은 꺼지지 않는 경멸의 눈빛으로 적에게서 눈길을 떼지 않은 채, 그의 발치에 쓰러져 숨졌다."]], [["\"Mercy!", "\"자비를!"], ["mercy! Huron,\"", "자비를, 〈휴런족〉아!\""], ["cried Heyward, from above, in tones nearly choked by horror; \"give mercy, and thou shalt receive it!\"", "공포에 거의 목이 멘 목소리로 〈헤이워드〉가 위에서 외쳤다. \"자비를 베풀라, 그러면 너도 자비를 받으리라!\""]], [["Whirling the bloody knife up at the imploring youth, the victorious Magua uttered a cry so fierce, so wild, and yet so joyous, that it conveyed the sounds of savage triumph to the ears of those who fought in the valley, a thousand feet below.", "피 묻은 칼을 그 애원하는 젊은이를 향해 휙 치켜들며, 승리한 〈머과〉는 어찌나 사납고, 어찌나 거칠며, 그러면서도 어찌나 기쁨에 찬 외침을 내질렀던지, 그것은 천 피트 아래 골짜기에서 싸우던 자들의 귀에까지 야만적 승리의 소리를 실어 보냈다."], ["He was answered by a burst from the lips of the scout, whose tall person was just then seen moving swiftly towards him, along those dangerous crags, with steps as bold and reckless as if he possessed the power to move in air.", "그에게 정찰병의 입에서 터져 나온 한마디가 답했으니, 그의 큰 키의 몸이 바로 그때, 마치 공중을 디딜 힘이라도 지닌 양 대담하고 무모한 발걸음으로, 그 위험한 바위들을 따라 그를 향해 빠르게 움직이는 것이 보였다."], ["But when the hunter reached the scene of the ruthless massacre, the ledge was tenanted only by the dead.", "그러나 사냥꾼이 그 무자비한 학살의 현장에 다다랐을 때, 그 바위 턱에는 죽은 자들만이 있었다."]], [["His keen eye took a single look at the victims, and then shot its glances over the difficulties of the ascent in his front.", "그의 날카로운 눈은 희생자들을 단 한 번 보고 나서, 제 앞에 놓인 험한 오르막으로 눈길을 쏘았다."], ["A form stood at the brow of the mountain, on the very edge of the giddy height, with uplifted arms, in an awful attitude of menace.", "한 형상이 산마루에, 그 아찔한 높이의 바로 가장자리에, 두 팔을 쳐든 채 위협의 무시무시한 자세로 서 있었다."], ["Without stopping to consider his person, the rifle of Hawkeye was raised; but a rock, which fell on the head of one of the fugitives below exposed the indignant and glowing countenance of the honest Gamut.", "그가 누구인지 가늠하려 멈추지도 않고 〈호크아이〉의 소총이 들렸으나, 아래쪽 도망자 하나의 머리 위로 떨어진 바위 하나가 정직한 〈거멋〉의 성난, 달아오른 얼굴을 드러냈다."], ["Then Magua issued from a crevice, and stepping with calm indifference over the body of the last of his associates, he leaped a wide fissure, and ascended the rocks at a point where the arm of David could not reach him.", "그때 〈머과〉가 바위틈에서 나와, 제 동료들 가운데 마지막 자의 시신을 차분한 무심함으로 넘어, 넓은 갈라진 틈을 뛰어, 〈데이비드〉의 팔이 닿을 수 없는 지점에서 바위를 기어올랐다."], ["A single bound would carry him to the brow of the precipice, and assure his safety.", "단 한 번의 도약이면 그를 벼랑마루로 데려가, 그의 안전을 보장해 줄 터였다."], ["Before taking the leap, however, the Huron paused, and shaking his hand at the scout, he shouted,--", "그러나 그 도약을 하기에 앞서, 그 〈휴런족〉은 멈추어, 정찰병을 향해 손을 흔들며 외쳤다."]], [["\"The pale-faces are dogs!", "\"창백한 얼굴들은 개다!"], ["the Delawares women!", "〈델라웨어족〉은 여자들이다!"], ["Magua leaves them on the rocks, for the crows!\"", "〈머과〉가 까마귀 밥으로 저들을 바위 위에 남겨 두노라!\""]], [["Laughing hoarsely, he made a desperate leap, and fell short of his mark; though his hand grasped a shrub on the verge of the height.", "쉰 소리로 웃으며, 그는 필사적으로 뛰었으나 제 목표에 미치지 못했다. 다만 그의 손이 그 높이의 가장자리에 난 관목을 움켜쥐었다."], ["The form of Hawkeye had crouched like a beast about to take its spring, and his frame trembled so violently with eagerness, that the muzzle of the half-raised rifle played like a leaf fluttering in the wind.", "〈호크아이〉의 몸은 막 덮치려는 짐승처럼 웅크렸고, 그의 몸은 열망으로 어찌나 격하게 떨렸던지, 반쯤 든 소총의 총구가 바람에 나부끼는 잎사귀처럼 흔들렸다."], ["Without exhausting himself with fruitless efforts, the cunning Magua suffered his body to drop to the length of his arms, and found a fragment for his feet to rest on.", "헛된 안간힘으로 제 힘을 소진하지 않고, 교활한 〈머과〉는 제 몸을 두 팔 길이만큼 늘어뜨려, 발을 디딜 바위 조각을 찾아냈다."], ["Then summoning all his powers, he renewed the attempt, and so far succeeded, as to draw his knees on the edge of the mountain.", "그러고는 온 힘을 모아 다시 시도하여, 무릎을 산의 가장자리에 끌어 올릴 만큼 성공했다."], ["It was now, when the body of his enemy was most collected together, that the agitated weapon of the scout was drawn to his shoulder.", "적의 몸이 가장 한데 모인 바로 그때, 정찰병의 떨리던 무기가 그의 어깨로 끌어 올려졌다."], ["The surrounding rocks themselves were not steadier than the piece became, for the single instant that it poured out its contents.", "그것이 그 안의 것을 쏟아 낸 단 한순간만큼은, 둘레의 바위들조차 그 총만큼 미동도 없지는 못했다."], ["The arms of the Huron relaxed, and his body fell back a little, while his knees still kept their position.", "그 〈휴런족〉의 팔에서 힘이 풀렸고, 무릎은 여전히 제 자리를 지킨 채 몸이 약간 뒤로 넘어갔다."], ["Turning a relentless look on his enemy, he shook a hand in grim defiance.", "무자비한 눈길을 제 적에게 돌리며, 그는 음산한 항거로 한 손을 흔들었다."], ["But his hold loosened, and his dark person was seen cutting the air with its head downwards, for a fleeting instant, until it glided past the fringe of shrubbery which clung to the mountain, in its rapid flight to destruction.", "그러나 그 움켜쥔 손이 풀렸고, 그의 거뭇한 몸이 머리를 아래로 한 채 허공을 가르는 것이, 산에 매달린 관목의 가장자리를 스쳐 지나 파멸을 향해 곤두박질치기까지, 스쳐 가는 한순간 보였다. [29] 말: 기병(騎兵)을 뜻한다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII", "# CHAPTER XXXIII"]], [["\"They fought, like brave men, long and well, They piled that ground with Moslem slain, They conquered--but Bozzaris fell, Bleeding at every vein. His few surviving comrades saw His smile when rang their proud hurrah, And the red field was won; Then saw in death his eyelids close Calmly, as to a night's repose, Like flowers at set of sun.\"", "그들은 용감한 사내들처럼 오래도록 훌륭하게 싸웠고,\n이슬람의 주검으로 그 땅을 뒤덮었다."]], [["The sun found the Lenape, on the succeeding day, a nation of mourners. The sounds of the battle were over, and they had fed fat their ancient grudge, and had avenged their recent quarrel with the Mengwe, by the destruction of a whole community. The black and murky atmosphere that floated around the spot where the Hurons had encamped, sufficiently announced, of itself, the fate of that wandering tribe; while hundreds of ravens, that struggled above the bleak summits of the mountains, or swept, in noisy flocks, across the wide ranges of the woods, furnished a frightful direction to the scene of the combat. In short, any eye, at all practised in the signs of a frontier warfare, might easily have traced all those unerring evidences of the ruthless results which attend an Indian vengeance.", "그들은 정복했으나 — 보자리스는 쓰러졌으니,\n온몸의 핏줄에서 피를 흘리며."]], [["Still, the sun rose on the Lenape a nation of mourners. No shouts of success, no songs of triumph, were heard, in rejoicings for their victory. The latest straggler had returned from his fell employment, only to strip himself of the terrific emblems of his bloody calling, and to join in the lamentations of his countrymen, as a stricken people. Pride and exultation were supplanted by humility, and the fiercest of human passions was already succeeded by the most profound and unequivocal demonstrations of grief.", "살아남은 몇 안 되는 전우들은\n자랑스러운 만세 소리가 울리고"]], [["The lodges were deserted; but a broad belt of earnest faces encircled a spot in their vicinity, whither everything possessing life had repaired, and where all were now collected, in deep and awful silence. Though beings of every rank and age, of both sexes, and of all pursuits, had united to form this breathing wall of bodies, they were influenced by a single emotion. Each eye was riveted on the centre of that ring, which contained the objects of so much, and of so common, an interest.", "붉은 전장이 정복되었을 때 그의 미소를 보았고,\n이윽고 죽음 속에 그의 눈꺼풀이 감기는 것을 보았으니,"]], [["Six Delaware girls, with their long, dark, flowing tresses falling loosely across their bosoms, stood apart, and only gave proofs of their existence as they occasionally strewed sweet-scented herbs and forest flowers on a litter of fragrant plants, that, under a pall of Indian robes, supported all that now remained of the ardent, high-souled, and generous Cora. Her form was concealed in many wrappers of the same simple manufacture, and her face was shut forever from the gaze of men. At her feet was seated the desolate Munro. His aged head was bowed nearly to the earth, in compelled submission to the stroke of Providence; but a hidden anguish struggled about his furrowed brow, that was only partially concealed by the careless locks of gray that had fallen, neglected, on his temples. Gamut stood at his side, his meek head bared to the rays of the sun, while his eyes, wandering and concerned, seemed to be equally divided between that little volume, which contained so many quaint but holy maxims, and the being in whose behalf his soul yearned to administer consolation. Heyward was also nigh, supporting himself against a tree, and endeavoring to keep down those sudden risings of sorrow that it required his utmost manhood to subdue.", "해 질 녘 꽃처럼,\n밤의 안식으로 드는 듯 고요하게."]], [["But sad and melancholy as this group may easily be imagined, it was far less touching than another, that occupied the opposite space of the same area.", "핼럭\n이튿날, 해는 슬픔에 잠긴 한 민족이 된 〈레나페〉를 비추었다. 싸움의 소리는 끝났고, 그들은 한 부족 전체를 멸함으로써 오랜 원한을 흡족히 채웠으며, 최근에 〈멩궤〉와 벌인 분쟁을 갚았다."], ["Seated, as in life, with his form and limbs arranged in grave and decent composure, Uncas appeared, arrayed in the most gorgeous ornaments that the wealth of the tribe could furnish.", "〈휴런〉족이 진을 쳤던 자리를 떠도는 검고 음산한 기운만으로도 그 유랑 부족의 운명은 충분히 드러났다. 한편 황량한 산봉우리 위로 다투어 날거나 시끄러운 떼를 이루어 드넓은 숲의 능선들을 가로질러 휩쓸어 가는 수백 마리의 까마귀가, 그 전투의 현장으로 향하는 끔찍한 길잡이가 되었다."], ["Rich plumes nodded above his head; wampum, gorgets, bracelets, and medals, adorned his person in profusion; though his dull eye and vacant lineaments too strongly contradicted the idle tale of pride they would convey.", "요컨대, 변경 전쟁의 징후에 조금이라도 단련된 눈이라면, 인디언의 복수에 뒤따르는 무자비한 결과를 가리키는 그 모든 분명한 증거를 어렵잖게 읽어 낼 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["Directly in front of the corpse Chingachgook was placed, without arms, paint, or adornment of any sort, except the bright blue blazonry of his race, that was indelibly impressed on his naked bosom. During the long period that the tribe had been thus collected, the Mohican warrior had kept a steady, anxious look on the cold and senseless countenance of his son. So riveted and intense had been that gaze, and so changeless his attitude, that a stranger might not have told the living from the dead, but for the occasional gleamings of a troubled spirit that shot athwart the dark visage of one, and the death-like calm that had forever settled on the lineaments of the other.", "그럼에도 해는 슬픔에 잠긴 한 민족이 된 〈레나페〉 위로 떠올랐다. 승리를 기뻐하는 어떤 함성도, 어떤 개선의 노래도 들리지 않았다. 가장 늦은 낙오자조차 그 잔혹한 일을 마치고 돌아왔으나, 그것은 다만 제 피비린내 나는 소임의 무시무시한 표식을 벗어 버리고, 비탄에 빠진 한 민족으로서 동족의 애도에 함께하기 위함이었다. 자긍과 환희는 겸비로 대체되었고, 인간의 격정 가운데 가장 사나운 것은 이미 가장 깊고 분명한 슬픔의 표현에 자리를 내주었다. 오두막들은 텅 비었으나, 진지에서 가까운 한 자리를 진지한 얼굴들의 너른 띠가 에워싸고 있었으니, 생명을 지닌 모든 것이 그리로 모여들어, 이제 모두가 깊고 엄숙한 침묵 속에 한데 모여 있었다. 온갖 신분과 나이, 남녀와 갖가지 생업을 지닌 자들이 하나로 어우러져 이 살아 숨 쉬는 인간의 벽을 이루었으나, 그들은 하나의 감정에 사로잡혀 있었다. 저마다의 눈은, 그토록 크고 그토록 공통된 관심의 대상을 품은 그 둥근 자리의 한가운데에 못 박혀 있었다."]], [["The scout was hard by, leaning in a pensive posture on his own fatal and avenging weapon; while Tamenund, supported by the elders of his nation, occupied a high place at hand, whence he might look down on the mute and sorrowful assemblage of his people.", "길고 검은 머리채를 가슴 위로 풀어헤친 여섯 〈델라웨어〉 처녀가 한쪽에 따로 서서, 이따금 향기로운 풀로 엮은 들것 위에 향내 나는 풀잎과 숲의 꽃들을 흩뿌릴 때만 그 존재를 드러냈으니, 인디언의 가운 천을 덮은 그 들것은 이제 열정에 차고 고결하며 너그러웠던 〈코라〉의 남은 전부를 받치고 있었다. 그녀의 몸은 똑같이 소박하게 지은 천 여러 겹에 가려졌고, 그 얼굴은 사람들의 시선으로부터 영원히 닫혀 버렸다."], ["Just within the inner edge of the circle stood a soldier, in the military attire of a strange nation; and without it was his war-horse, in the centre of a collection of mounted domestics, seemingly in readiness to undertake some distant journey.", "그녀의 발치에는 비탄에 빠진 〈먼로〉가 앉아 있었다. 그의 늙은 머리는 섭리의 일격에 어쩔 수 없이 굴복하여 거의 땅에 닿도록 숙여졌으나, 주름진 이마에는 감춰진 고뇌가 일렁였으니, 그것은 무심히 관자놀이에 흘러내린 회색 머리타래로도 그저 일부만 가려질 뿐이었다."], ["The vestments of the stranger announced him to be one who held a responsible situation near the person of the captain of the Canadas; and who, as it would now seem, finding his errand of peace frustrated by the fierce impetuosity of his allies, was content to become a silent and sad spectator of the fruits of a contest that he had arrived too late to anticipate.", "그 곁에는 〈거뮤트〉가, 햇살에 온순한 머리를 드러낸 채 서 있었고, 그의 눈은 방황하며 근심에 차서, 기이하면서도 거룩한 잠언이 그토록 많이 담긴 그 작은 책과, 그의 영혼이 위로를 베풀기를 갈망하는 그 존재 사이에서 똑같이 갈라지는 듯했다. 〈헤이워드〉 역시 가까이에 있었으니, 나무에 몸을 기댄 채, 사내다움을 다해 억눌러야만 했던 슬픔의 갑작스러운 북받침을 가라앉히려 애쓰고 있었다."]], [["The day was drawing to the close of its first quarter, and yet had the multitude maintained its breathing stillness since its dawn. No sound louder than a stifled sob had been heard among them, nor had even a limb been moved throughout that long and painful period, except to perform the simple and touching offerings that were made, from time to time, in commemoration of the dead. The patience and forbearance of Indian fortitude could alone support such an appearance of abstraction, as seemed now to have turned each dark and motionless figure into stone.", "그러나 이 무리가 아무리 비통하고 우울하게 그려진다 한들, 같은 자리의 맞은편을 차지한 또 다른 무리에 비하면 한결 덜 가슴 아픈 것이었다. 부족의 부로 마련할 수 있는 가장 화려한 장식으로 단장한 채, 살아 있을 때처럼 그 몸과 사지를 엄숙하고 단정한 자세로 갖추어 앉은 〈언카스〉가 거기 있었다. 화려한 깃털이 그의 머리 위에서 흔들렸고, 조가비 구슬과 목가리개, 팔찌와 메달이 그의 몸을 넘치도록 꾸몄으나, 그 흐릿한 눈과 텅 빈 이목구비는 그것들이 전하려는 자긍의 헛된 이야기를 너무도 강하게 반박했다. 주검 바로 앞에는 〈칭가치국〉이 자리하고 있었으니, 그의 벌거벗은 가슴에 지울 수 없이 새겨진 제 종족의 선명한 푸른 문장 말고는, 무기도, 칠도, 어떤 장식도 없었다. 부족이 이렇게 모여 있던 긴 시간 내내, 이 〈모히칸〉 전사는 차갑고 생기 없는 아들의 얼굴에 한결같이 근심 어린 눈길을 두고 있었다. 그 응시가 어찌나 못 박힌 듯 강렬했고 그 자세가 어찌나 변함없었던지, 낯선 이라면 산 자와 죽은 자를 분간하지 못했을 것이다. 다만 한쪽의 어두운 얼굴을 가로질러 이따금 번득이는 어지러운 영혼의 빛과, 다른 한쪽의 이목구비에 영원히 내려앉은 죽음 같은 고요만이 그 구분을 일러 주었다."]], [["At length, the sage of the Delawares stretched forth an arm, and leaning on the shoulders of his attendants, he arose with an air as feeble as if another age had already intervened between the man who had met his nation the preceding day, and him who now tottered on his elevated stand.", "척후는 가까이서, 제 운명적이고 복수에 찬 무기에 시름겨운 자세로 기대어 있었다. 한편 〈타메넌드〉는 부족의 원로들에게 부축을 받으며 곁의 높은 자리를 차지하고 있었으니, 그곳에서 그는 말없이 슬픔에 잠긴 제 백성의 무리를 내려다볼 수 있었다."]], [["\"Men of the Lenape!\" he said, in hollow tones that sounded like a voice charged with some prophetic mission; \"the face of the Manitou is behind a cloud! His eye is turned from you; His ears are shut; His tongue gives no answer. You see Him not; yet His judgments are before you. Let your hearts be open and your spirits tell no lie. Men of the Lenape! the face of the Manitou is behind a cloud.\"", "그 둥근 자리의 안쪽 가장자리 바로 안에는 한 군인이, 낯선 나라의 군복 차림으로 서 있었다. 그 바깥에는 그의 군마가, 멀리 길을 떠날 채비를 갖춘 듯한 말 탄 시종들의 한가운데에 있었다. 이 낯선 자의 복장은, 그가 〈캐나다〉군 지휘관의 신변에서 막중한 직책을 맡은 자임을 드러냈으니, 이제 보아하니, 동맹군의 사나운 격정으로 평화의 임무가 좌절된 것을 알고는, 너무 늦게 도착해 미리 막지 못한 한 싸움의 결실을 말없이 슬프게 지켜보는 구경꾼이 되는 데 만족한 듯했다."]], [["As this simple and yet terrible annunciation stole on the ears of the multitude, a stillness as deep and awful succeeded as if the venerated spirit they worshipped had uttered the words without the aid of human organs; and even the inanimate Uncas appeared a being of life, compared with the humbled and submissive throng by whom he was surrounded. As the immediate effect, however, gradually passed away, a low murmur of voices commenced a sort of chant in honor of the dead.", "날은 첫 사분(四分)의 끝으로 기울고 있었으나, 그래도 무리는 동틀 녘부터 숨죽인 정적을 지키고 있었다."], ["The sounds were those of females, and were thrillingly soft and wailing. The words were connected by no regular continuation, but as one ceased another took up the eulogy, or lamentation, whichever it might be called, and gave vent to her emotions in such language as was suggested by her feelings and the occasion.", "그들 가운데서는 억눌린 흐느낌보다 큰 소리는 들리지 않았고, 그 길고 고통스러운 시간 내내 그 누구도 사지 하나 움직이지 않았으니, 다만 죽은 자를 기리며 이따금 바치는 소박하고 가슴 저린 헌물을 올릴 때만은 예외였다."], ["At intervals the speaker was interrupted by general and loud bursts of sorrow, during which the girls around the bier of Cora plucked the plants and flowers blindly from her body, as if bewildered with grief.", "이제 검고 미동도 없는 형상 하나하나를 돌로 바꾸어 놓은 듯한 이러한 망연한 모습을 지탱할 수 있는 것은, 오직 인디언의 굳센 인내와 자제뿐이었다."], ["But, in the milder moments of their plaint, these emblems of purity and sweetness were cast back to their places, with every sign of tenderness and regret. Though rendered less connected by many and general interruptions and outbreakings, a translation of their language would have contained a regular descant, which, in substance, might have proved to possess a train of consecutive ideas.", "마침내 〈델라웨어〉의 현인이 한쪽 팔을 뻗어, 시종들의 어깨에 기댄 채 일어섰으니, 어제 제 민족을 만났던 그 사람과 지금 높은 단 위에서 비틀거리는 그 사람 사이에 이미 또 한 시대가 끼어든 듯이, 그토록 쇠약한 기색이었다."]], [["A girl, selected for the task by her rank and qualifications, commenced by modest allusions to the qualities of the deceased warrior, embellishing her expressions with those oriental images that the Indians have probably brought with them from the extremes of the other continent, and which form of themselves a link to connect the ancient histories of the two worlds. She called him the \"panther of his tribe\"; and described him as one whose moccasin left no trail on the dews; whose bound was like the leap of the young fawn; whose eye was brighter than a star in the dark night; and whose voice, in battle, was loud as the thunder of the Manitou. She reminded him of the mother who bore him, and dwelt forcibly on the happiness she must feel in possessing such a son. She bade him tell her, when they met in the world of spirits, that the Delaware girls had shed tears above the grave of her child, and had called her blessed.", "\"〈레나페〉의 사람들이여!\" 그는 어떤 예언의 사명을 짊어진 목소리처럼 울리는, 공허한 어조로 말했다. \"〈마니토〉의 얼굴이 구름 뒤에 있도다! 그의 눈은 너희에게서 돌아섰고, 그의 귀는 닫혔으며, 그의 혀는 답하지 않는다. 너희가 그를 보지 못하나, 그의 심판은 너희 앞에 있도다. 너희 마음을 열고, 너희 영혼이 거짓을 말하지 않게 하라. 〈레나페〉의 사람들이여! 〈마니토〉의 얼굴이 구름 뒤에 있도다.\" 이 소박하면서도 무시무시한 선포가 무리의 귀에 스며들자, 그들이 섬기는 그 우러르는 신령이 인간의 입을 빌리지 않고 직접 그 말을 발한 듯이, 그만큼 깊고 엄숙한 정적이 뒤따랐다. 그리하여 생기 없는 〈언카스〉조차, 그를 에워싼 겸허하고 순종하는 무리에 비하면 살아 있는 존재처럼 보였다. 그러나 그 직접적인 효과가 차츰 가시자, 나직한 목소리들의 웅성거림이 죽은 자를 기리는 일종의 노래를 시작했다. 그 소리는 여인들의 것으로, 가슴을 저미도록 부드럽고 애절했다. 그 말들은 어떤 규칙적인 이음새로 연결되지는 않았으나, 한 사람이 그치면 다른 사람이 찬가든 애가든 — 무어라 불리든 간에 — 그것을 이어받아, 제 감정과 그 자리가 일러 주는 말로 격정을 토해 냈다. 이따금 말하는 이는 모두가 터뜨리는 큰 슬픔의 폭발에 끊겼는데, 그러는 동안 〈코라〉의 관 둘레에 선 처녀들은, 슬픔에 정신이 나간 듯, 그 몸에서 풀과 꽃을 무턱대고 뜯어냈다. 그러나 통곡이 잦아드는 순간에는, 이 순결함과 다정함의 표식들을, 갖은 애틋함과 회한의 기색으로 다시 제자리에 놓았다. 잦고 잦은 끊김과 폭발로 한결 짜임새가 흐트러지긴 했어도, 그들의 말을 옮긴다면 정연한 영창이 담겼을 것이며, 그 내용은 본래 일관된 생각의 줄기를 지니고 있었음이 드러났을 것이다."]], [["Then, they who succeeded, changing their tones to a milder and still more tender strain, alluded, with the delicacy and sensitiveness of woman, to the stranger maiden, who had left the upper earth at a time so near his own departure, as to render the will of the Great Spirit too manifest to be disregarded.", "신분과 자질로 그 소임에 뽑힌 한 처녀가, 죽은 전사의 자질을 겸손히 빗대며 시작하여, 인디언들이 아마도 저 다른 대륙의 끝에서 가져왔을, 그리하여 그 자체로 두 세계의 옛 역사를 잇는 고리를 이루는 동방의 심상으로 그 표현을 꾸몄다."], ["They admonished him to be kind to her, and to have consideration for her ignorance of those arts which were so necessary to the comfort of a warrior like himself.", "그녀는 그를 \"제 부족의 표범\"이라 불렀고, 그의 모카신이 이슬 위에 자국을 남기지 않았으며, 그의 도약이 어린 새끼 사슴의 도약과 같았고, 그의 눈이 캄캄한 밤의 별보다 밝았으며, 싸움터에서 그의 목소리가 〈마니토〉의 천둥처럼 우렁찼다고 노래했다."], ["They dwelt upon her matchless beauty, and on her noble resolution, without the taint of envy, and as angels may be thought to delight in a superior excellence; adding, that these endowments should prove more than equivalent for any little imperfections in her education.", "그녀는 그를 낳은 어머니를 그에게 일깨우며, 그런 아들을 둔 어머니가 느꼈을 행복을 힘주어 이야기했다. 그녀는 그에게, 영혼들의 세계에서 만나거든, 〈델라웨어〉 처녀들이 그 아이의 무덤 위에 눈물을 흘렸으며 그녀를 복되다 불렀노라고 어머니에게 전해 달라 청했다."]], [["After which, others again, in due succession, spoke to the maiden herself, in the low, soft language of tenderness and love.", "이어서 뒤를 잇는 이들은, 그 음조를 한결 부드럽고 더욱 다정한 가락으로 바꾸어, 여인의 섬세함과 예민함으로, 그 자신의 떠남에 그토록 가까운 때에 이승을 떠나, 〈위대한 영〉의 뜻이 너무도 분명하여 거스를 수 없게 한 그 낯선 처녀를 언급했다."], ["They exhorted her to be of cheerful mind, and to fear nothing for future welfare.", "그들은 그에게 그녀에게 다정하라고, 그와 같은 전사에게 그토록 긴요한 기예들에 그녀가 서툰 것을 헤아려 주라고 권했다."], ["A hunter would be her companion, who knew how to provide for her smallest wants; and a warrior was at her side who was able to protect her against every danger.", "그들은 그녀의 견줄 데 없는 아름다움과 그 고결한 결의를, 시샘의 기색 없이, 천사들이 더 높은 탁월함을 기뻐하리라 여겨지듯 이야기했고, 이러한 자질들이 그녀가 받은 가르침의 자그마한 미흡함을 넉넉히 메우고도 남으리라 덧붙였다."], ["They promised that her path should be pleasant, and her burden light.", "그런 다음, 다시 다른 이들이 차례로, 다정과 사랑이 담긴 낮고 부드러운 말로, 그 처녀 자신에게 이야기했다."], ["They cautioned her against unavailing regrets for the friends of her youth, and the scenes where her fathers had dwelt; assuring her that the \"blessed hunting-grounds of the Lenape\"", "그들은 그녀에게 마음을 즐겁게 가지라고, 앞날의 안녕을 조금도 두려워 말라고 권면했다. 그녀의 가장 작은 필요까지 헤아릴 줄 아는 사냥꾼이 동무가 될 것이며, 온갖 위험으로부터 그녀를 지킬 수 있는 전사가 그 곁에 있다고 했다."], ["contained vales as pleasant, streams as pure, and flowers as sweet, as the \"heaven of the pale-faces.\"", "그들은 그녀의 길이 즐겁고 그 짐이 가벼우리라 약속했다."], ["They advised her to be attentive to the wants of her companion, and never to forget the distinction which the Manitou had so wisely established between them.", "그들은 그녀에게, 젊은 시절의 벗들과 조상이 살던 땅을 부질없이 그리워 말라고 일렀으며, 〈레나페〉의 \"복된 사냥터\"에는 \"창백한 얼굴들의 천국\"만큼이나 즐거운 골짜기와 맑은 시냇물과 향기로운 꽃이 있다고 다짐해 주었다."], ["Then, in a wild burst of their chant, they sang with united voices the temper of the Mohican's mind.", "그들은 그녀에게 동무의 필요를 살피라고, 그리고 〈마니토〉가 그 둘 사이에 그토록 지혜롭게 세워 둔 구별을 결코 잊지 말라고 일렀다."], ["They pronounced him noble, manly and generous; all that became a warrior, and all that a maid might love.", "그러고는 노래의 격렬한 폭발 속에서, 그들은 한목소리로 〈모히칸〉의 심성을 노래했다."], ["Clothing their ideas in the most remote and subtle images, they betrayed, that, in the short period of their intercourse, they had discovered, with the intuitive perception of their sex, the truant disposition of his inclinations.", "그들은 그를 고결하고 사내답고 너그럽다고, 전사다운 모든 것이며 처녀가 사랑할 만한 모든 것이라 일컬었다. 그들은 가장 멀고 미묘한 심상으로 제 생각을 감싸면서도, 그 짧은 교제의 기간 동안, 제 성(性) 특유의 직관적 통찰로, 그의 마음이 다른 데로 기울었음을 알아챘노라고 드러냈다."], ["The Delaware girls had found no favor in his eyes!", "〈델라웨어〉 처녀들은 그의 눈에 들지 못했던 것이다!"], ["He was of a race that had once been lords on the shores of the salt lake, and his wishes had led him back to a people who dwelt about the graves of his fathers.", "그는 한때 소금 호숫가의 주인이었던 종족이었으니, 그의 바람은 그를 조상들의 무덤 둘레에 사는 한 백성에게로 이끌었다."], ["Why should not such a predilection be encouraged!", "어찌 그런 끌림을 북돋우지 않으랴!"], ["That she was of a blood purer and richer than the rest of her nation, any eye might have seen; that she was equal to the dangers and daring of a life in the woods, her conduct had proved; and now, they added, the \"wise one of the earth\"", "그녀가 제 민족의 다른 이들보다 더 순수하고 고귀한 피를 지녔음은 누구의 눈에도 보였을 것이며, 그녀가 숲에서의 삶이 지닌 위험과 대담함에 걸맞은 이였음은 그녀의 행실이 증명했다."], ["had transplanted her to a place where she would find congenial spirits, and might be forever happy.", "그리고 이제, 그들은 덧붙이기를, \"이 땅의 현명한 이\"가 그녀를 마음 맞는 영혼들을 만날 곳으로 옮겨 심었으니, 그녀는 영원히 행복하리라고 했다."]], [["Then, with another transition in voice and subject, allusions were made to the virgin who wept in the adjacent lodge.", "그러고는, 목소리와 주제를 또 한 번 바꾸어, 이웃한 오두막에서 울고 있는 처녀에게 빗대는 말이 나왔다."], ["They compared her to flakes of snow; as pure, as white, as brilliant, and as liable to melt in the fierce heats of summer, or congeal in the frosts of winter.", "그들은 그녀를 눈송이에 견주었으니, 순결하고 희고 빛나며, 여름의 사나운 더위에 녹기 쉽고 겨울의 서리에 얼어붙기 쉽다고 했다."], ["They doubted not that she was lovely in the eyes of the young chief, whose skin and whose sorrow seemed so like her own; but, though far from expressing such a preference, it was evident they deemed her less excellent than the maid they mourned.", "그들은 그 살결과 슬픔이 그토록 그녀를 닮은 젊은 추장의 눈에 그녀가 사랑스러웠으리라 의심치 않았으나, 그런 편애를 드러내기는커녕, 그들이 애도하는 처녀보다 그녀를 덜 빼어난 이로 여기는 것이 분명했다."], ["Still they denied her no meed her rare charms might properly claim.", "그래도 그들은 그 보기 드문 매력이 마땅히 받을 만한 어떤 찬사도 그녀에게서 아끼지 않았다."], ["Her ringlets were compared to the exuberant tendrils of the vine, her eye to the blue vault of the heavens, and the most spotless cloud, with its glowing flush of the sun, was admitted to be less attractive than her bloom.", "그녀의 곱슬머리는 포도덩굴의 무성한 덩굴손에 비유되었고, 그 눈은 푸른 하늘의 궁창에, 그리고 햇살로 발갛게 물든 가장 티 없는 구름조차 그녀의 화사함보다는 덜 매혹적이라 여겨졌다."]], [["During these and similar songs nothing was audible but the murmurs of the music; relieved, as it was, or rather rendered terrible, by those occasional bursts of grief which might be called its choruses.", "이와 비슷한 노래들이 이어지는 동안에는, 음악의 웅성거림 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않았으니, 그것은 이따금 터지는 슬픔의 폭발 — 그 노래의 합창이라 할 만한 것 — 로 누그러지거나, 차라리 더욱 무시무시해졌다."], ["The Delawares themselves listened like charmed men; and it was very apparent, by the variations of their speaking countenances, how deep and true was their sympathy.", "〈델라웨어〉인들 자신도 흘린 듯 귀 기울였고, 그 말하는 듯한 얼굴들의 변화로 미루어, 그들의 연민이 얼마나 깊고 참된 것인지는 너무도 분명했다."], ["Even David was not reluctant to lend his ears to tones of voices so sweet; and long ere the chant was ended, his gaze announced that his soul was enthralled.", "〈데이비드〉조차 그토록 감미로운 목소리의 가락에 귀를 기울이기를 마다하지 않았으니, 노래가 끝나기 한참 전에, 그의 시선은 이미 그 영혼이 사로잡혔음을 알리고 있었다."]], [["The scout, to whom alone, of all the white men, the words were intelligible, suffered himself to be a little aroused from his meditative posture, and bent his face aside, to catch their meaning, as the girls proceeded.", "백인들 가운데 유일하게 그 말을 알아듣는 척후는, 사색에 잠긴 자세에서 조금 깨어나, 처녀들이 노래를 이어 가는 동안 그 뜻을 새기려 얼굴을 옆으로 기울였다."], ["But when they spoke of the future prospects of Cora and Uncas, he shook his head, like one who knew the error of their simple creed, and resuming his reclining attitude, he maintained it until the ceremony--if that might be called a ceremony, in which feeling was so deeply imbued--was finished.", "그러나 그들이 〈코라〉와 〈언카스〉의 앞날을 이야기할 때면, 그는 그들의 소박한 믿음의 그릇됨을 아는 자처럼 고개를 가로저었고, 다시 기댄 자세로 돌아가, 그 의식이 — 감정이 그토록 깊이 배어든 것을 의식이라 부를 수 있다면 — 끝날 때까지 그 자세를 지켰다."], ["Happily for the self-command of both Heyward and Munro, they knew not the meaning of the wild sounds they heard.", "〈헤이워드〉와 〈먼로〉 두 사람의 자제를 위해서는 다행히도, 그들은 자신들이 듣는 그 격렬한 소리의 뜻을 알지 못했다."]], [["Chingachgook was a solitary exception to the interest manifested by the native part of the audience.", "〈칭가치국〉은 청중 가운데 원주민들이 드러내는 관심에서 홀로 예외였다."], ["His look never changed throughout the whole of the scene, nor did a muscle move in his rigid countenance, even at the wildest or the most pathetic parts of the lamentation.", "그 장면 내내 그의 표정은 결코 변하지 않았고, 애가의 가장 격렬하거나 가장 애절한 대목에서도 그 굳은 얼굴에서는 근육 하나 움직이지 않았다."], ["The cold and senseless remains of his son was all to him, and every other sense but that of sight seemed frozen, in order that his eyes might take their final gaze at those lineaments he had so long loved, and which were now about to be closed forever from his view.", "차갑고 생기 없는 아들의 유해가 그에게는 전부였으며, 시각 말고 다른 모든 감각은 얼어붙은 듯했으니, 이는 그토록 오래 사랑했고 이제 곧 그의 시야에서 영원히 닫혀 버릴 그 얼굴을, 그의 눈이 마지막으로 응시하게 하려 함이었다."]], [["In this stage of the funeral obsequies, a warrior much renowned for deeds in arms, and more especially for services in the recent combat, a man of stern and grave demeanor, advanced slowly from the crowd, and placed himself nigh the person of the dead.", "장례 의식의 이 단계에서, 무공으로 명성이 높고 특히 최근의 전투에서 세운 공로로 이름난 한 전사가, 준엄하고 엄숙한 풍모를 띤 채, 무리에서 천천히 걸어 나와 죽은 자의 곁에 자리했다."]], [["\"Why hast thou left us, pride of the Wapanachki?\"", "\"어찌하여 그대는 우리를 떠났는가, 〈와파나치〉의 자랑이여?\""], ["he said, addressing himself to the dull ears of Uncas, as if the empty clay retained the faculties of the animated man; \"thy time has been like that of the sun when in the trees; thy glory brighter than his light at noonday.", "그는 빈 흙덩이가 살아 있는 사람의 능력을 그대로 간직한 듯, 〈언카스〉의 흐릿한 귀에 대고 말했다. \"그대의 세월은 나무에 걸린 해와 같았고, 그대의 영광은 한낮의 그 빛보다 밝았다."], ["Thou art gone, youthful warrior, but a hundred Wyandots are clearing the briers from thy path to the world of spirits.", "그대는 갔으나, 젊은 전사여, 일백의 〈와이언도트〉인이 영혼들의 세계로 가는 그대의 길에서 가시덤불을 치우고 있다."], ["Who that saw thee in battle would believe that thou couldst die?", "싸움터에서 그대를 본 자라면 누가 그대가 죽을 수 있다고 믿었으랴?"], ["Who before thee has ever shown Uttawa the way into the fight?", "그대 이전에 누가 〈우타와〉에게 싸움터로 드는 길을 보여 주었던가?"], ["Thy feet were like the wings of eagles; thine arm heavier than falling branches from the pine; and thy voice like the Manitou when he speaks in the clouds.", "그대의 발은 독수리의 날개 같았고, 그대의 팔은 소나무에서 떨어지는 가지보다 무거웠으며, 그대의 목소리는 구름 속에서 말하는 〈마니토〉와 같았다."], ["The tongue of Uttawa is weak,\"", "〈우타와〉의 혀는 약하고,\""], ["he added, looking about him with a melancholy gaze, \"and his heart exceeding heavy.", "그는 우울한 눈길로 사방을 둘러보며 덧붙였다. \"그의 마음은 더없이 무겁다."], ["Pride of the Wapanachki, why hast thou left us?\"", "〈와파나치〉의 자랑이여, 어찌하여 그대는 우리를 떠났는가?\""]], [["He was succeeded by others, in due order, until most of the high and gifted men of the nation had sung or spoken their tribute of praise over the _manes_ of the deceased chief.", "그의 뒤를 다른 이들이 차례로 이어, 마침내 부족의 높고 재능 있는 사람들이 거의 다, 죽은 추장의 넋 위로 찬사의 헌사를 노래하거나 읊었다."], ["When each had ended, another deep and breathing silence reigned in all the place.", "저마다 끝마칠 때면, 그곳 전체에 또다시 깊고 숨죽인 침묵이 깃들었다."]], [["Then a low, deep sound was heard, like the suppressed accompaniment of distant music, rising just high enough on the air to be audible, and yet so indistinctly, as to leave its character, and the place whence it proceeded, alike matters of conjecture.", "이윽고 나직하고 깊은 소리가 들렸으니, 멀리서 들려오는 음악의 억눌린 반주 같았고, 겨우 들릴 만큼만 공기 중에 떠올랐으되, 너무도 흐릿하여 그 성격도, 그것이 나오는 곳도 다 같이 짐작거리로 남았다."], ["It was, however, succeeded by another and another strain, each in a higher key, until they grew on the ear, first in long drawn and often repeated interjections, and finally in words.", "그러나 그 소리에 이어 또 한 가락, 다시 한 가락이 잇따랐으니, 저마다 한 단계 높은 음으로, 마침내 귀에 자라났다. 처음에는 길게 끌며 자주 되풀이되는 감탄으로, 끝내는 말로."], ["The lips of Chingachgook had so far parted, as to announce that it was the monody of the father.", "〈칭가치국〉의 입술이 그만큼 벌어진 것이, 그것이 아비의 애가임을 알렸다."], ["Though not an eye was turned towards him, nor the smallest sign of impatience exhibited, it was apparent, by the manner in which the multitude elevated their heads to listen, that they drank in the sounds with an intenseness of attention, that none but Tamenund himself had ever before commanded.", "그를 향해 돌아선 눈은 하나도 없었고, 조바심의 기색이라곤 조금도 보이지 않았으나, 무리가 귀 기울이려 머리를 치켜드는 모습으로 미루어, 그들이 〈타메넌드〉 자신 말고는 일찍이 그 누구도 누리지 못한 강렬한 주의로 그 소리를 들이마시고 있음이 분명했다."], ["But they listened in vain.", "그러나 그들은 헛되이 귀 기울였다."], ["The strains rose just so loud as to become intelligible, and then grew fainter and more trembling, until they finally sank on the ear, as if borne away by a passing breath of wind.", "그 가락은 겨우 알아들을 만큼 커졌다가, 다시 더 희미하고 더 떨리게 잦아들어, 끝내 지나가는 한 줄기 바람결에 실려 가듯 귀에서 가라앉고 말았다."], ["The lips of the Sagamore closed, and he remained silent in his seat, looking, with his riveted eye and motionless form, like some creature that had been turned from the Almighty hand with the form but without the spirit of a man.", "〈세이거모어〉의 입술이 닫혔고, 그는 자리에 말없이 앉은 채로, 그 못 박힌 눈과 미동 없는 형상으로, 사람의 형상은 갖추었으되 사람의 영혼은 없이 전능한 손에서 빚어져 나온 어떤 피조물처럼 보였다."], ["The Delawares, who knew by these symptoms that the mind of their friend was not prepared for so mighty an effort of fortitude, relaxed in their attention; and, with an innate delicacy, seemed to bestow all their thoughts on the obsequies of the stranger maiden.", "이러한 징후로 제 벗의 마음이 그토록 거대한 인내의 노력에 채비되지 못했음을 알아챈 〈델라웨어〉인들은 주의를 거두었고, 타고난 섬세함으로, 모든 생각을 그 낯선 처녀의 장례로 돌리는 듯했다."]], [["A signal was given, by one of the elder chiefs, to the women who crowded that part of the circle near which the body of Cora lay.", "원로 추장 가운데 한 사람이, 〈코라〉의 시신이 놓인 자리 가까이 둥근 자리에 몰려 있던 여인들에게 신호를 보냈다."], ["Obedient to the sign, the girls raised the bier to the elevation of their heads, and advanced with slow and regulated steps, chanting, as they proceeded, another wailing song in praise of the deceased.", "그 신호에 따라, 처녀들은 들것을 머리 높이로 들어 올려, 느리고 절도 있는 걸음으로 나아가며, 죽은 이를 기리는 또 다른 애도의 노래를 부르며 걸었다."], ["Gamut, who had been a close observer of rites he deemed so heathenish, now bent his head over the shoulder of the unconscious father, whispering,--", "더없이 이교적이라 여긴 그 의식을 가까이서 지켜보던 〈거뮤트〉가, 이제 정신 나간 아비의 어깨 너머로 고개를 숙여 속삭였다."]], [["\"They move with the remains of thy child; shall we not follow, and see them interred with Christian burial?\"", "\"저들이 그대의 아이의 유해를 옮기고 있습니다. 우리도 따라가, 그 아이가 기독교식 장례로 묻히는 것을 보아야 하지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["Munro started, as if the last trumpet had sounded in his ear, and bestowing one anxious and hurried glance around him, he arose and followed in the simple train, with the mien of a soldier, but bearing the full burden of a parent's suffering.", "〈먼로〉는 마지막 나팔이 그의 귀에 울리기라도 한 듯 움찔하더니, 근심에 차서 황급히 한 번 사방을 둘러본 뒤, 일어나 그 소박한 행렬을 따랐다. 군인다운 풍모였으나, 아비의 고통이라는 짐을 온전히 짊어진 채였다."], ["His friends pressed around him with a sorrow that was too strong to be termed sympathy--even the young Frenchman joining in the procession, with the air of a man who was sensibly touched at the early and melancholy fate of one so lovely.", "그의 벗들이 너무도 강렬하여 연민이라 부를 수 없는 슬픔으로 그의 둘레로 모여들었으니 — 그 젊은 프랑스인조차, 그토록 사랑스러운 이의 이르고 애처로운 운명에 절실히 마음 아파하는 자의 기색으로 행렬에 끼었다."], ["But when the last and humblest female of the tribe had joined in the wild, and yet ordered array, the men of the Lenape contracted their circle, and formed again around the person of Uncas, as silent, as grave, and as motionless as before.", "그러나 부족의 마지막이자 가장 미천한 여인까지 그 격렬하면서도 정연한 대열에 합류하자, 〈레나페〉의 사내들은 그 둥근 자리를 좁혀, 다시 〈언카스〉의 곁으로 둘러섰으니, 이전처럼 말없고, 엄숙하며, 미동도 없었다."]], [["The place which had been chosen for the grave of Cora was a little knoll, where a cluster of young and healthful pines had taken root, forming of themselves a melancholy and appropriate shade over the spot.", "〈코라〉의 무덤으로 골라 둔 자리는, 젊고 싱싱한 소나무들이 무리지어 뿌리 내려, 그 자리 위에 절로 우울하고도 어울리는 그늘을 드리운 자그마한 둔덕이었다."], ["On reaching it the girls deposited their burden, and continued for many minutes waiting, with characteristic patience, and native timidity, for some evidence that they whose feelings were most concerned were content with the arrangement.", "거기에 이르자 처녀들은 짐을 내려놓고, 여러 분 동안, 인디언 특유의 인내와 타고난 수줍음으로, 가장 깊이 마음 쓰는 이들이 그 마련에 흡족해한다는 어떤 기색을 기다렸다."], ["At length the scout, who alone understood their habits, said, in their own language,--", "마침내 그들의 풍습을 홀로 아는 척후가, 그들의 말로 일렀다."]], [["\"My daughters have done well; the white men thank them.\"", "\"내 딸들이 잘해 주었소. 백인들이 그대들에게 고마워하오.\""]], [["Satisfied with this testimony in their favor, the girls proceeded to deposit the body in a shell, ingeniously, and not inelegantly, fabricated of the bark of the birch; after which they lowered it into its dark and final abode.", "이 호의의 증언에 만족한 처녀들은, 자작나무 껍질로 솜씨 있게, 또 제법 우아하게 엮어 만든 함에 시신을 안치하기 시작했고, 그런 다음 그것을 어둡고 마지막 거처로 내려놓았다."], ["The ceremony of covering the remains, and concealing the marks of the fresh earth, by leaves and other natural and customary objects, was conducted with the same simple and silent forms.", "유해를 덮고, 잎사귀와 그 밖의 자연스럽고 관습적인 것들로 갓 파낸 흙의 자취를 가리는 의식은, 똑같이 소박하고 말 없는 절차로 치러졌다."], ["But when the labors of the kind beings who had performed these sad and friendly offices were so far completed, they hesitated, in a way to show that they knew not how much further they might proceed.", "그러나 이 슬프고도 다정한 소임을 행한 이 친절한 이들의 일이 그만큼 끝나자, 그들은 어디까지 더 나아가야 할지 모른다는 듯 머뭇거렸다."], ["It was in this stage of the rites that the scout again addressed them:--", "척후가 다시 그들에게 말을 건넨 것은 의식의 이 단계에서였다."]], [["\"My young women have done enough,\"", "\"내 젊은 여인들은 할 만큼 했소.\""], ["he said; \"the spirit of a pale-face has no need of food or raiment, their gifts being according to the heaven of their color.", "그가 말했다. \"창백한 얼굴의 영혼은 음식도 옷도 필요치 않으니, 그 베풂은 제 살빛의 천국을 따르는 법이오."], ["I see,\"", "보아하니,\""], ["he added, glancing an eye at David, who was preparing his book in a manner that indicated an intention to lead the way in sacred song, \"that one who better knows the Christian fashions is about to speak.\"", "그는 거룩한 노래로 앞장설 뜻을 내비치며 제 책을 마련하던 〈데이비드〉에게 눈길을 던지며 덧붙였다. \"기독교의 방식을 더 잘 아는 이가 곧 말하려 하는구려.\""]], [["The females stood modestly aside, and, from having been the principal actors in the scene, they now became the meek and attentive observers of that which followed.", "여인들은 조심스레 옆으로 물러나, 그 장면의 주된 행위자였다가 이제는 뒤따르는 일을 지켜보는 온순하고 주의 깊은 구경꾼이 되었다."], ["During the time David was occupied in pouring out the pious feelings of his spirit in this manner, not a sign of surprise, nor a look of impatience, escaped them.", "〈데이비드〉가 이러한 방식으로 제 영혼의 경건한 감정을 쏟아 내는 동안, 그들에게서는 놀라움의 기색도, 조바심의 표정도 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["They listened like those who knew the meaning of the strange words, and appeared as if they felt the mingled emotions of sorrow, hope, and resignation, they were intended to convey.", "그들은 그 낯선 말의 뜻을 아는 자들처럼 귀 기울였고, 그 말이 전하려는 슬픔과 희망과 체념의 뒤섞인 감정을 느끼는 듯했다."]], [["Excited by the scene he had just witnessed, and perhaps influenced by his own secret emotions, the master of song exceeded his usual efforts.", "방금 목격한 장면에 마음이 동하고, 어쩌면 제 은밀한 감정에도 이끌려, 노래의 명수는 평소의 노력을 뛰어넘었다."], ["His full, rich voice was not found to suffer by a comparison with the soft tones of the girls; and his more modulated strains possessed, at least for the ears of those to whom they were peculiarly addressed, the additional power of intelligence.", "그의 가득 차고 풍부한 목소리는 처녀들의 부드러운 음조에 견주어도 손색이 없었고, 그의 한결 절제된 가락은, 적어도 그 노래가 유난히 향했던 이들의 귀에는, 알아들을 수 있다는 그 곁들인 힘을 지니고 있었다."], ["He ended the anthem, as he had commenced it, in the midst of a grave and solemn stillness.", "그는 시작했을 때와 마찬가지로, 엄숙하고 장중한 정적 한가운데서 찬가를 마쳤다."]], [["When, however, the closing cadence had fallen on the ears of his auditors, the secret, timorous glances of the eyes, and the general, and yet subdued movement of the assemblage, betrayed that something was expected from the father of the deceased.", "그러나 그 마무리의 가락이 청중의 귀에 내려앉자, 그 은밀하고 겁먹은 눈길과, 무리의 전반적이면서도 억눌린 움직임이, 죽은 이의 아비에게서 무언가를 기대하고 있음을 드러냈다."], ["Munro seemed sensible that the time was come for him to exert what is, perhaps, the greatest effort of which human nature is capable.", "〈먼로〉는, 어쩌면 인간 본성이 감당할 수 있는 가장 큰 노력일 그것을 발휘할 때가 왔음을 느끼는 듯했다."], ["He bared his gray locks, and looked around the timid and quiet throng by which he was encircled with a firm and collected countenance.", "그는 회색 머리타래를 드러내고, 자신을 에워싼 겁먹고 고요한 무리를 굳세고 침착한 얼굴로 둘러보았다."], ["Then motioning with his hand for the scout to listen, he said,--", "그러고는 척후에게 손짓으로 들으라 이르며 말했다."]], [["\"Say to these kind and gentle females, that a heartbroken and failing man returns them his thanks.", "\"이 친절하고 다정한 여인들에게 전하시오, 가슴이 무너지고 쇠해 가는 한 사내가 감사를 드린다고."], ["Tell them, that the Being we all worship, under different names, will be mindful of their charity; and that the time shall not be distant when we may assemble around his throne without distinction of sex, or rank, or color.\"", "그들에게 전하시오, 우리 모두가 저마다 다른 이름으로 섬기는 그 존재께서 그들의 자비를 기억하실 것이며, 우리가 성(性)도 신분도 살빛도 구별 없이 그분의 보좌 둘레에 모이는 때가 머지않으리라고.\""]], [["The scout listened to the tremulous voice in which the veteran delivered these words, and shook his head slowly when they were ended, as one who doubted their efficacy.", "척후는 노병이 이 말을 전하는 그 떨리는 목소리에 귀 기울이더니, 말이 끝나자 그 효험을 의심하는 자처럼 천천히 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["\"To tell them this,\"", "\"이걸 그들에게 전한다는 건,\""], ["he said, \"would be to tell them that the snows come not in the winter, or that the sun shines fiercest when the trees are stripped of their leaves.\"", "그가 말했다. \"겨울에 눈이 오지 않는다거나, 나무에서 잎이 다 떨어졌을 때 해가 가장 사납게 빛난다고 말하는 것이나 다름없소.\""]], [["Then turning to the women, he made such a communication of the other's gratitude as he deemed most suited to the capacities of his listeners.", "그러고는 여인들을 향해, 듣는 이들의 이해력에 가장 잘 맞으리라 여겨지는 대로, 노병의 감사를 전했다."], ["The head of Munro had already sunk upon his chest, and he was again fast relapsing into melancholy, when the young Frenchman before named ventured to touch him lightly on the elbow.", "〈먼로〉의 머리는 이미 가슴에 떨궈졌고, 그가 다시 빠르게 우울에 잠겨 들 무렵, 앞서 말한 젊은 프랑스인이 조심스레 그의 팔꿈치를 가볍게 건드렸다."], ["As soon as he had gained the attention of the mourning old man, he pointed towards a group of young Indians, who approached with a light but closely covered litter, and then pointed upward towards the sun.", "슬픔에 잠긴 노인의 주의를 끌자마자, 그는 가볍지만 단단히 덮인 들것을 들고 다가오는 한 무리의 젊은 인디언들을 가리킨 다음, 위쪽 해를 가리켰다."]], [["\"I understand you, sir,\"", "\"무슨 말인지 알겠소, 선생.\" 〈먼로〉가 억지로 다잡은 목소리로 답했다."], ["returned Munro, with a voice of forced firmness; \"I understand you.", "\"알겠소. 이는 하늘의 뜻이니, 나는 따르겠소."], ["It is the will of Heaven, and I submit.", "〈코라〉, 내 딸아! 가슴 무너진 아비의 기도가 지금 네게 닿을 수 있다면, 너는 얼마나 복되랴!"], ["Cora, my child!", "갑시다, 여러분.\""], ["if the prayers of a heartbroken father could avail thee now, how blessed shouldst thou be! Come, gentlemen,\"", "그는 고결한 침착의 기색으로 사방을 둘러보며 덧붙였으나, 그 빛바랜 얼굴에 떨리는 고뇌는 너무도 강하여 가릴 수가 없었다."], ["he added, looking about him with an air of lofty composure, though the anguish that quivered in his faded countenance was far too powerful to be concealed, \"our duty here is ended; let us depart.\"", "\"이곳에서 우리의 소임은 끝났소. 떠납시다.\""]], [["Heyward gladly obeyed a summons that took them from a spot where, each instant, he felt his self-control was about to desert him.", "〈헤이워드〉는, 순간순간 자제력이 곧 자신을 저버리려 함을 느끼던 그 자리에서 그들을 데려가 줄 그 부름에 기꺼이 따랐다."], ["While his companions were mounting, however, he found time to press the hand of the scout, and to repeat the terms of an engagement they had made, to meet again within the posts of the British army.", "그러나 동료들이 말에 오르는 동안, 그는 척후의 손을 마주 잡고, 영국군 진지 안에서 다시 만나기로 한 약속의 조건을 되풀이할 짬을 냈다."], ["Then gladly throwing himself into the saddle, he spurred his charger to the side of the litter, whence low and stifled sobs alone announced the presence of Alice.", "그러고는 기꺼이 안장에 몸을 던져, 나직하고 억눌린 흐느낌만이 〈앨리스〉의 존재를 알리는 그 들것 곁으로 군마를 몰았다."], ["In this manner, the head of Munro again dropping on his bosom, with Heyward and David following in sorrowing silence, and attended by the aide of Montcalm with his guard, all the white men, with the exception of Hawkeye, passed from before the eyes of the Delawares, and were soon buried in the vast forests of that region.", "이리하여, 〈먼로〉의 머리가 다시 가슴에 떨궈진 채, 〈헤이워드〉와 〈데이비드〉가 슬픔에 잠긴 침묵 속에 뒤따르고, 〈몽칼름〉의 부관이 호위병을 거느리고 함께한 가운데, 〈호크아이〉를 제외한 모든 백인이 〈델라웨어〉인들의 눈앞에서 사라져, 이내 그 고장의 광막한 숲속에 파묻혔다."]], [["But the tie which, through their common calamity, had united the feelings of these simple dwellers in the woods with the strangers who had thus transiently visited them, was not so easily broken.", "그러나 공통된 재난을 통해, 숲의 이 소박한 거주민들의 마음을 이렇게 잠시 그들을 찾아온 낯선 이들과 묶어 준 그 끈은, 그리 쉽게 끊어지지 않았다."], ["Years passed away before the traditionary tale of the white maiden, and of the young warrior of the Mohicans, ceased to beguile the long nights and tedious marches, or to animate their youthful and brave with a desire for vengeance. Neither were the secondary actors in these momentous incidents forgotten.", "그 백인 처녀와 〈모히칸〉의 젊은 전사에 관한 전설이 긴 밤과 고된 행군의 무료함을 달래는 일도, 그들의 젊고 용감한 자들을 복수의 열망으로 들끓게 하는 일도 그칠 때까지는 여러 해가 흘렀다."], ["Through the medium of the scout, who served for years afterwards as a link between them and civilized life, they learned, in answer to their inquiries, that the \"Gray Head\" was speedily gathered to his fathers--borne down, as was erroneously believed, by his military misfortunes; and that the \"Open Hand\"", "이 중대한 사건들의 부차적인 행위자들도 잊히지 않았다."], ["had conveyed his surviving daughter far into the settlements of the \"pale-faces,\" where her tears had at last ceased to flow, and had been succeeded by the bright smiles which were better suited to her joyous nature.", "그 뒤로 여러 해 동안 그들과 문명 생활 사이의 고리 노릇을 한 척후를 통해, 그들은 자신들의 물음에 대한 답으로, \"회색 머리\"가 — 그릇되이 믿어졌듯 군사적 불운에 짓눌려 — 이내 조상들에게로 거두어졌다는 것과, \"열린 손\"이 살아남은 그의 딸을 \"창백한 얼굴들\"의 정착지 깊은 데로 데려갔으며, 그곳에서 그녀의 눈물이 마침내 그치고, 그 즐거운 천성에 한결 어울리는 환한 미소가 그 자리를 대신했다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["But these were events of a time later than that which concerns our tale.", "그러나 이는 우리 이야기와 관련된 때보다 후일의 일이다."], ["Deserted by all of his color, Hawkeye returned to the spot where his own sympathies led him, with a force that no ideal bond of union could bestow.", "제 살빛의 모든 이에게 버려진 〈호크아이〉는, 어떤 관념적인 결연의 끈도 줄 수 없는 힘으로 제 마음이 이끄는 그 자리로 돌아갔다."], ["He was just in time to catch a parting look of the features of Uncas, whom the Delawares were already inclosing in his last vestments of skins.", "그는 마침 〈언카스〉의 얼굴을 마지막으로 한 번 보기에 알맞은 때에 닿았으니, 〈델라웨어〉인들이 이미 그를 가죽으로 된 마지막 수의로 감싸고 있던 참이었다."], ["They paused to permit the longing and lingering gaze of the sturdy woodsman, and when it was ended, the body was enveloped, never to be unclosed again.", "그들은 이 굳센 숲 사람의 갈망에 찬 마지막 응시를 허락하려 멈추었고, 그것이 끝나자, 시신은 다시는 열리지 않도록 감싸였다."], ["Then came a procession like the other, and the whole nation was collected about the temporary grave of the chief--temporary, because it was proper that, at some future day, his bones should rest among those of his own people.", "그런 다음 앞서와 같은 행렬이 이어졌고, 부족 전체가 그 추장의 임시 무덤 둘레로 모였으니 — 임시라 함은, 훗날 어느 날 그의 뼈가 제 백성의 뼈 사이에 누이는 것이 마땅했기 때문이다."]], [["The movement, like the feeling, had been simultaneous and general.", "그 움직임은, 그 감정과 마찬가지로, 일제히 모두에게서 비롯되었다."], ["The same grave expression of grief, the same rigid silence, and the same deference to the principal mourner, were observed around the place of interment as have been already described.", "이미 그려진 것과 똑같은 엄숙한 슬픔의 표정, 똑같은 굳은 침묵, 그리고 으뜸가는 애도자에 대한 똑같은 예우가, 매장의 자리 둘레에서 지켜졌다."], ["The body was deposited in an attitude of repose, facing the rising sun, with the implements of war and of the chase at hand, in readiness for the final journey.", "시신은 떠오르는 해를 향하여 안식의 자세로 안치되었고, 마지막 여정에 대비하여 전쟁과 사냥의 도구들이 곁에 놓였다."], ["An opening was left in the shell, by which it was protected from the soil, for the spirit to communicate with its earthly tenement, when necessary; and the whole was concealed from the instinct, and protected from the ravages of the beasts of prey, with an ingenuity peculiar to the natives.", "함에는 트인 자리를 남겨, 흙으로부터 보호받으면서도, 영혼이 필요할 때 제 이승의 처소와 소통할 수 있게 했다. 그리고 그 모든 것은 원주민 특유의 솜씨로, 맹수의 본능으로부터 감추어지고 그 약탈로부터 지켜졌다."], ["The manual rites then ceased, and all present reverted to the more spiritual part of the ceremonies.", "그러자 손으로 행하는 의식이 그쳤고, 그 자리에 있던 모두가 의식의 한결 영적인 부분으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["Chingachgook became once more the object of the common attention.", "〈칭가치국〉이 다시 한번 모두의 주목의 대상이 되었다."], ["He had not yet spoken, and something consolatory and instructive was expected from so renowned a chief on an occasion of such interest.", "그는 아직 입을 열지 않았으니, 그토록 이름난 추장에게서, 그토록 절실한 자리에서, 무언가 위로가 되고 가르침이 될 말이 기대되었다."], ["Conscious of the wishes of the people, the stern and self-restrained warrior raised his face, which had latterly been buried in his robe, and looked about him with a steady eye.", "백성의 바람을 알아챈 이 준엄하고 자제하는 전사는, 근래 제 가운에 파묻고 있던 얼굴을 들어, 한결같은 눈으로 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["His firmly compressed and expressive lips then severed, and for the first time during the long ceremonies his voice was distinctly audible.", "그러자 굳게 다물려 있던 그 뜻 깊은 입술이 갈라졌고, 그 긴 의식 동안 처음으로 그의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다."]], [["\"Why do my brothers mourn!\"", "\"어찌하여 나의 형제들은 애도하는가!\""], ["he said, regarding the dark race of dejected warriors by whom he was environed; \"why do my daughters weep!", "그는 자신을 에워싼 낙담한 전사들의 검은 무리를 바라보며 말했다. \"어찌하여 나의 딸들은 우는가!"], ["that a young man has gone to the happy hunting-grounds; that a chief has filled his time with honor!", "한 젊은이가 행복한 사냥터로 갔고, 한 추장이 명예로이 제 세월을 채웠음을!"], ["He was good; he was dutiful; he was brave.", "그는 선했고, 의로웠으며, 용감했다."], ["Who can deny it?", "누가 그것을 부인하랴?"], ["The Manitou had need of such a warrior, and He has called him away.", "〈마니토〉께서 그러한 전사를 필요로 하시어, 그를 불러 가셨다."], ["As for me, the son and the father of Uncas, I am a blazed pine, in a clearing of the pale-faces.", "나로 말하면, 〈언카스〉의 아들이자 아비인 나는, 창백한 얼굴들의 개간지에 선 껍질 벗긴 소나무다."], ["My race has gone from the shores of the salt lake, and the hills of the Delawares.", "나의 종족은 소금 호숫가에서, 〈델라웨어〉의 언덕에서 사라져 갔다."], ["But who can say that the Serpent of his tribe has forgotten his wisdom?", "그러나 제 부족의 〈뱀〉이 그 지혜를 잊었다고 누가 말하랴?"], ["I am alone--\"", "나는 혼자다 —\""]], [["\"No, no,\"", "\"아니, 아니오.\""], ["cried Hawkeye, who had been gazing with a yearning look at the rigid features of his friend, with something like his own self-command, but whose philosophy could endure no longer; \"no, Sagamore, not alone.", "제 벗의 굳은 얼굴을, 자신도 그만한 자제의 기색으로 갈망하듯 응시하고 있던, 그러나 그 철학이 더는 견딜 수 없게 된 〈호크아이〉가 외쳤다. \"아니오, 〈세이거모어〉, 혼자가 아니오."], ["The gifts of our colors may be different, but God has so placed us as to journey in the same path.", "우리 살빛의 천품은 다를지언정, 하느님께서는 우리를 같은 길을 가도록 두셨소."], ["I have no kin, and I may also say, like you, no people.", "나는 일가붙이도 없고, 그대처럼, 백성도 없다 할 수 있소."], ["He was your son, and a redskin by nature; and it may be that your blood was nearer--but if ever I forget the lad who has so often fou't at my side in war, and slept at my side in peace, may He who made us all, whatever may be our color or our gifts, forget me!", "그는 그대의 아들이었고, 천성으로 붉은 피부의 사람이었소. 어쩌면 그대의 핏줄이 더 가까웠을지 모르나 — 허나 전쟁에서 그토록 자주 내 곁에서 싸웠고 평화 속에서 내 곁에서 잠들었던 그 아이를 내가 언젠가 잊는다면, 우리 살빛이나 천품이 어떻든 우리 모두를 지으신 그분께서 나를 잊으시기를!"], ["The boy has left us for a time; but, Sagamore, you are not alone.\"", "그 아이는 한동안 우리를 떠났소. 허나, 〈세이거모어〉, 그대는 혼자가 아니오.\""]], [["Chingachgook grasped the hand that, in the warmth of feeling, the scout had stretched across the fresh earth, and in that attitude of friendship these two sturdy and intrepid woodsmen bowed their heads together, while scalding tears fell to their feet, watering the grave of Uncas like drops of falling rain.", "〈칭가치국〉은, 감정이 북받친 척후가 갓 파낸 흙 위로 뻗은 그 손을 움켜쥐었고, 그 우정의 자세로, 이 굳세고 두려움 없는 두 숲 사람은 함께 머리를 숙였으니, 뜨거운 눈물이 그들의 발치로 떨어져, 떨어지는 빗방울처럼 〈언카스〉의 무덤을 적셨다."]], [["In the midst of the awful stillness with which such a burst of feeling, coming, as it did, from the two most renowned warriors of that region, was received, Tamenund lifted his voice to disperse the multitude.", "그 고장의 가장 이름난 두 전사에게서, 그렇게 터져 나온 이 감정의 폭발이 받아들여진 그 엄숙한 정적 한가운데서, 〈타메넌드〉가 무리를 흩으려 목소리를 높였다."]], [["\"It is enough,\"", "\"되었다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["\"Go, children of the Lenape, the anger of the Manitou is not done.", "\"가라, 〈레나페〉의 아이들이여, 〈마니토〉의 분노는 아직 다하지 않았다."], ["Why should Tamenund stay?", "어찌하여 〈타메넌드〉가 머물러야 하랴?"], ["The pale-faces are masters of the earth, and the time of the redmen has not yet come again.", "창백한 얼굴들이 이 땅의 주인이니, 붉은 사람들의 때는 아직 다시 오지 않았다. 나의 날은 너무도 길었다."], ["My day has been too long.", "아침에 나는 〈우나미스〉의 아들들이 행복하고 강성한 것을 보았다."], ["In the morning I saw the sons of Unamis happy and strong; and yet, before the night has come, have I lived to see the last warrior of the wise race of the Mohicans.\"", "그런데도, 밤이 오기도 전에, 나는 지혜로운 종족 〈모히칸〉의 마지막 전사를 보기까지 살아 있구나.\" 각주"]], [["[1] As each nation of the Indians had either its language or its dialect, they usually gave different names to the same places, though nearly all of their appellations were descriptive of the object.", "[1] 인디언의 각 부족은 저마다 언어 또는 방언을 지녔으므로, 그들은 같은 장소에 흔히 서로 다른 이름을 붙였는데, 그 명칭들은 거의 모두 그 대상을 묘사하는 것이었다."], ["Thus, a literal translation of the name of this beautiful sheet of water, used by the tribe that dwelt on its banks would be \"The Tail of the Lake.\"", "그리하여, 그 기슭에 살던 부족이 쓰던, 이 아름다운 호수의 이름을 그대로 옮기면 \"호수의 꼬리\"가 된다."], ["Lake George, as it is vulgarly, and now indeed legally called, forms a sort of tail to Lake Champlain, when viewed on the map.", "흔히, 그리고 이제는 실로 법적으로도 그렇게 불리는 〈조지 호수〉는, 지도에서 보면 〈섐플레인 호수〉에 일종의 꼬리를 이룬다."], ["Hence the name.", "그 이름은 여기서 비롯되었다."]], [["[2] Washington: who, after uselessly admonishing the European general of the danger into which he was heedlessly running, saved the remnants of the British army, on this occasion, by his decision and courage.", "[2] 워싱턴. 그는 유럽인 장군에게 그가 무모하게 빠져들고 있던 위험을 헛되이 일깨운 끝에, 이 일에서 제 결단과 용기로 영국군의 잔병을 구해 냈다."], ["The reputation earned by Washington in this battle was the principal cause of his being selected to command the American armies at a later day.", "워싱턴이 이 전투에서 얻은 명성은, 훗날 그가 미국군의 지휘관으로 선발된 주된 이유가 되었다."], ["It is a circumstance worthy of observation, that, while all America rang with his well-merited reputation, his name does not occur in any European account of the battle; at least, the author has searched for it without success.", "주목할 만한 사정은, 온 아메리카가 그의 마땅한 명성으로 떠들썩했던 반면, 그의 이름이 이 전투에 관한 어떤 유럽 측 기록에도 나오지 않는다는 점이다. 적어도 필자는 그것을 찾으려 했으나 성공하지 못했다."], ["In this manner does the mother country absorb even the fame, under that system of rule.", "그러한 통치 체제 아래서는, 본국이 명성마저 이렇게 흡수해 버린다."]], [["[3] There existed for a long time a confederation among the Indian tribes which occupied the northwestern part of the colony of New York, which was at first known as the \"Five Nations.\"", "[3] 〈뉴욕〉 식민지의 북서부를 차지한 인디언 부족들 사이에는 오랫동안 한 연합이 존재했으니, 처음에는 \"다섯 부족\"으로 알려졌다."], ["At a later day it admitted another tribe, when the appellation was changed to that of the \"Six Nations.\"", "후일 또 한 부족이 받아들여지면서, 그 명칭은 \"여섯 부족\"으로 바뀌었다."], ["The original confederation consisted of the Mohawks, the Oneidas, the Senecas, the Cayugas, and the Onondagas.", "본래의 연합은 〈모호크〉, 〈오나이다〉, 〈세니카〉, 〈카유가〉, 〈오논다가〉로 이루어졌다."], ["The sixth tribe was the Tuscaroras.", "여섯째 부족은 〈터스커로라〉였다."], ["There are remnants of all these people still living on lands secured to them by the State; but they are daily disappearing, either by deaths or by removals to scenes more congenial to their habits.", "이 모든 종족의 잔류민이 아직도 주(州)가 그들에게 보장해 준 땅에서 살고 있으나, 그들은 죽음으로든, 제 습성에 더 맞는 곳으로의 이주로든, 나날이 사라져 가고 있다."], ["In a short time there will be no remains of these extraordinary people, in those regions in which they dwelt for centuries, but their names.", "머지않아, 그들이 수백 년 살아온 그 고장에는, 그들의 이름 말고는 이 비범한 종족의 자취가 남지 않을 것이다."], ["The State of New York has counties named after all of them but the Mohawks and the Tuscaroras.", "〈뉴욕〉주에는 〈모호크〉와 〈터스커로라〉를 제외한 그들 모두의 이름을 딴 카운티가 있다."], ["The second river of that State is called the Mohawk.", "그 주의 둘째 강은 〈모호크〉강이라 불린다."]], [["[4] In the State of Rhode Island there is a bay called Narragansett, so named after a powerful tribe of Indians, which formerly dwelt on its banks.", "[4] 〈로드아일랜드〉주에는 〈내러갠셋〉이라 불리는 만이 있으니, 일찍이 그 기슭에 살던 강성한 인디언 부족의 이름을 딴 것이다."], ["Accident, or one of those unaccountable freaks which nature sometimes plays in the animal world, gave rise to a breed of horses which were once well known in America by the name of the Narragansetts.", "우연이었는지, 자연이 동물계에서 이따금 부리는 그 설명할 수 없는 변덕 중 하나였는지, 한때 〈내러갠셋〉이라는 이름으로 아메리카에 널리 알려졌던 한 말의 품종이 생겨났다."], ["They were small, commonly of the color called sorrel in America, and distinguished by their habit of pacing.", "그것들은 몸집이 작고, 흔히 아메리카에서 적갈색이라 부르는 빛깔이었으며, 측대보로 걷는 습성으로 두드러졌다."], ["Horses of this race were, and are still, in much request as saddle-horses, on account of their hardiness and the ease of their movements.", "이 품종의 말은, 그 강건함과 움직임의 편안함 때문에, 안장말로 크게 요구되었고 지금도 그러하다."], ["As they were also sure of foot, the Narragansetts were greatly sought for by females who were obliged to travel over the roots and holes in the \"new countries.\"", "또한 발걸음이 든든하였으므로, \"새 고장\"의 나무뿌리와 구덩이 위를 다녀야 했던 여인들이 〈내러갠셋〉종을 크게 찾았다."]], [["[5] The North American warrior caused the hair to be plucked from his whole body; a small tuft, only, was left on the crown of his head in order that his enemy might avail himself of it, in wrenching off the scalp in the event of his fall.", "[5] 북아메리카 전사는 온몸의 털을 뽑게 했다. 다만 정수리에 작은 한 줌만 남겨 두었으니, 이는 자신이 쓰러질 경우 적이 머리 가죽을 비틀어 벗기는 데 쓰게 하기 위함이었다."], ["The scalp was the only admissible trophy of victory.", "머리 가죽은 승리의 유일하게 인정되는 전리품이었다."], ["Thus, it was deemed more important to obtain the scalp than to kill the man.", "그리하여 사람을 죽이는 것보다 그 머리 가죽을 얻는 것이 더 중하게 여겨졌다."], ["Some tribes lay great stress on the honor of striking a dead body.", "어떤 부족들은 주검을 치는 명예를 크게 강조한다."], ["These practices have nearly disappeared among the Indians of the Atlantic States.", "이러한 관습은 대서양 연안 주들의 인디언들 사이에서는 거의 사라졌다."]], [["[6] The hunting-shirt is a picturesque smock frock, being shorter, and ornamented with fringes and tassels.", "[6] 사냥 셔츠는 그림처럼 멋진 작업용 겉옷으로, 길이가 더 짧고, 술과 장식 매듭으로 꾸며져 있다."], ["The colors are intended to imitate the hues of the wood with a view to concealment.", "그 빛깔은 몸을 숨길 목적으로 숲의 색조를 흉내 내려는 것이다."], ["Many corps of American riflemen have been thus attired; and the dress is one of the most striking of modern times.", "많은 미국인 소총수 부대가 이렇게 차려입었으며, 그 복장은 근래의 것 가운데 가장 눈길을 끄는 것 중 하나다."], ["The hunting-shirt is frequently white.", "사냥 셔츠는 흔히 흰색이다."]], [["[7] The rifle of the army is short; that of the hunter is always long.", "[7] 군대의 소총은 짧고, 사냥꾼의 소총은 늘 길다."]], [["[8] The Mississippi.", "[8] 〈미시시피〉강."], ["The scout alludes to a tradition which is very popular among the tribes of the Atlantic States.", "척후는 대서양 연안 주들의 부족들 사이에서 매우 널리 퍼진 한 전설을 가리키고 있다."], ["Evidence of their Asiatic origin is deduced from the circumstances, though great uncertainty hangs over the whole history of the Indians.", "그들의 아시아 기원의 증거가 그 정황에서 추론되나, 인디언의 역사 전체에는 큰 불확실성이 감돈다."]], [["[9] The scene of this tale was in the 42d degree of latitude, where the twilight is never of long continuance.", "[9] 이 이야기의 무대는 위도 42도로, 그곳에서는 황혼이 결코 오래 이어지지 않는다."]], [["[10] The reader will remember that New York was originally a colony of the Dutch.", "[10] 독자는 〈뉴욕〉이 본래 네덜란드의 식민지였음을 기억할 것이다."]], [["[11] The principal villages of the Indians are still called \"castles\" by the whites of New York.", "[11] 인디언의 주요 마을들은 〈뉴욕〉의 백인들에게 아직도 \"성채\"라 불린다."], ["\"Oneida castle\" is no more than a scattered hamlet; but the name is in general use.", "\"〈오나이다〉 성채\"는 흩어진 작은 마을에 지나지 않으나, 그 이름이 일반적으로 쓰인다."]], [["[12] In vulgar parlance the condiments of a repast are called by the American \"a relish,\" substituting the thing for its effect.", "[12] 흔한 말로, 미국인은 한 끼의 양념을 \"렐리시\"라 부르니, 사물 대신 그 효과를 가져다 쓴 것이다."], ["These provincial terms are frequently put in the mouths of the speakers, according to their several conditions in life.", "이러한 지방어들은 흔히, 화자들의 저마다 다른 처지에 따라 그들의 입에 놓인다."], ["Most of them are of local use, and others quite peculiar to the particular class of men to which the character belongs.", "그 대부분은 지역적으로 쓰이며, 더러는 그 인물이 속한 특정 부류의 사람들에게 아주 독특한 것이다."], ["In the present instance, the scout uses the word with immediate reference to the salt, with which his own party was so fortunate as to be provided.", "여기서는 척후가, 제 일행이 다행히도 갖추고 있던 그 소금을 직접 가리키며 이 말을 쓴 것이다."]], [["[13] Glenn's Falls are on the Hudson, some forty or fifty miles above the head of tide, or the place where that river becomes navigable for sloops.", "[13] 〈글렌스 폭포〉는 〈허드슨〉강에 있으니, 조수가 닿는 데, 곧 그 강이 외돛배가 다닐 수 있게 되는 곳에서 사오십 마일 위쪽이다."], ["The description of this picturesque and remarkable little cataract, as given by the scout, is sufficiently correct, though the application of the water to the uses of civilized life has materially injured its beauties.", "척후가 들려준 이 그림 같고 두드러진 작은 폭포의 묘사는 충분히 정확하나, 그 물을 문명 생활의 용도로 끌어 쓰면서 그 아름다움이 크게 훼손되었다."], ["The rocky island and the two caverns are well known to every traveller, since the former sustains a pier of a bridge, which is now thrown across the river, immediately above the fall.", "그 바위섬과 두 동굴은 모든 여행자에게 잘 알려져 있으니, 그 섬이 이제 폭포 바로 위로 강을 가로질러 놓인 다리의 교각을 떠받치고 있기 때문이다."], ["In explanation of the taste of Hawkeye, it should be remembered that men always prize that most which is least enjoyed.", "〈호크아이〉의 취향을 설명하자면, 사람은 늘 가장 덜 누리는 것을 가장 귀히 여긴다는 점을 기억해야 한다."], ["Thus, in a new country, the woods and other objects, which in an old country would be maintained at great cost, are got rid of, simply with a view of \"improving,\" as it is called.", "그리하여, 새 고장에서는, 오래된 나라라면 큰 비용을 들여서라도 보존했을 숲과 그 밖의 것들이, 흔히 말하는 \"개량\"을 위한다는 까닭만으로 없어져 버린다."]], [["[14] The meaning of Indian words is much governed by the emphasis and tones.", "[14] 인디언 말의 뜻은 그 강세와 음조에 크게 좌우된다."]], [["[15] Mingo was the Delaware term for the Five Nations.", "[15] 〈밍고〉는 다섯 부족을 가리키는 〈델라웨어〉 말이었다."], ["Maquas was the name given them by the Dutch.", "〈머콰스〉는 네덜란드인이 그들에게 붙인 이름이었다."], ["The French, from their first intercourse with them, called them Iroquois.", "프랑스인은 그들과 처음 교류할 때부터 그들을 〈이로쿼이〉라 불렀다."]], [["[16] It has long been a practice with the whites to conciliate the important men of the Indians, by presenting medals, which are worn in the place of their own rude ornaments.", "[16] 백인들은 오랫동안 인디언의 유력자들을 회유하기 위해 메달을 주는 관습이 있었으니, 그것은 그들 자신의 투박한 장식 대신 몸에 걸쳤다."], ["Those given by the English generally bear the impression of the reigning king, and those given by the Americans that of the president.", "영국인이 준 것은 대개 통치 중인 왕의 인장을, 미국인이 준 것은 대통령의 인장을 새겼다."]], [["[17] Many of the animals of the American forests resort to those spots where salt springs are found.", "[17] 아메리카 숲의 많은 동물이 소금 샘이 있는 곳으로 찾아든다."], ["These are called \"licks\" or \"salt licks,\"", "이런 곳은 그 고장 말로 \"릭\""], ["in the language of the country, from the circumstance that the quadruped is often obliged to lick the earth, in order to obtain the saline particles.", "또는 \"솔트 릭\"이라 불리니, 네발짐승이 소금기 어린 알갱이를 얻으려 흔히 땅을 핥아야 하는 데서 비롯된 이름이다."], ["These licks are great places of resort with the hunters, who waylay their game near the paths that lead to them.", "이 릭은 사냥꾼들이 즐겨 찾는 곳으로, 그들은 그리로 통하는 길목 근처에서 사냥감을 노린다."]], [["[18] The scene of the foregoing incidents is on the spot where the village of Ballston now stands; one of the two principal watering-places of America.", "[18] 앞서 일어난 사건들의 무대는 지금 〈볼스턴〉 마을이 서 있는 자리이니, 아메리카의 두 주요 온천지 중 하나다."]], [["[19] Some years since, the writer was shooting in the vicinity of the ruins of Fort Oswego, which stands on the shores of Lake Ontario.", "[19] 몇 해 전, 필자는 〈온타리오 호수〉 기슭에 서 있는 〈오스위고 요새〉의 폐허 부근에서 사냥을 하고 있었다."], ["His game was deer, and his chase a forest that stretched with little interruption, fifty miles inland.", "그가 노린 사냥감은 사슴이었고, 그 사냥터는 거의 끊김 없이 오십 마일이나 내륙으로 뻗은 숲이었다."], ["Unexpectedly he came upon six or eight ladders lying in the woods within a short distance of each other.", "뜻밖에도 그는 서로 가까운 거리에 숲속에 놓인 여섯 내지 여덟 개의 사다리를 발견했다."], ["They were rudely made, and much decayed.", "그것들은 투박하게 만들어졌고, 많이 썩어 있었다."], ["Wondering what could have assembled so many of these instruments in such a place, he sought an old man who resided near for the explanation.", "어찌하여 이런 곳에 이토록 많은 도구가 모이게 되었는지 의아하여, 그는 가까이 사는 한 노인을 찾아 그 까닭을 물었다."]], [["During the war of 1776 Fort Oswego was held by the British.", "1776년의 전쟁 동안 〈오스위고 요새〉는 영국군이 지키고 있었다."], ["An expedition had been sent two hundred miles through the wilderness to surprise the fort.", "그 요새를 기습하기 위해 황야를 가로질러 이백 마일의 원정대가 보내졌다."], ["It appears that the Americans, on reaching the spot named, which was within a mile or two of the fort, first learned that they were expected, and in great danger of being cut off.", "보아하니 미국군은, 앞서 말한 그 자리, 곧 요새에서 일이 마일 안쪽에 이르러서야 처음으로, 자신들이 발각되어 끊겨 나갈 큰 위험에 처했음을 알았다."], ["They threw away their scaling-ladders, and made a rapid retreat.", "그들은 공성용 사다리를 버리고 황급히 후퇴했다."], ["These ladders had lain unmolested thirty years, in the spot where they had thus been cast.", "이 사다리들은, 그렇게 내던져진 그 자리에서 삼십 년 동안 손대지 않은 채 놓여 있었다."]], [["[20] Baron Dieskau, a German, in the service of France.", "[20] 프랑스에 복무한 독일인 〈디스코〉 남작."], ["A few years previous to the period of the tale, this officer was defeated by Sir William Johnson of Johnstown, New York, on the shores of Lake George.", "이 이야기의 시점보다 몇 해 앞서, 이 장교는 〈뉴욕〉주 〈존스타운〉의 윌리엄 존슨 경에게 〈조지 호수〉 기슭에서 패했다."], ["See Appendix, Note H.", "부록의 주(註) H를 보라."]], [["[21] Evidently the late De Witt Clinton, who died governor of New York in 1828.", "[21] 분명, 1828년에 〈뉴욕〉 주지사로 사망한 고(故) 디윗 클린턴이다."]], [["[22] The powers of the American mocking-bird are generally known.", "[22] 아메리카 흉내지빠귀의 능력은 널리 알려져 있다."], ["But the true mocking-bird is not found so far north as the State of New York, where it has, however, two substitutes of inferior excellence; the catbird, so often named by the scout, and the bird vulgarly called ground-thresher.", "그러나 진짜 흉내지빠귀는 〈뉴욕〉주만큼 북쪽에서는 발견되지 않으니, 다만 그곳에는 그보다 못한 두 대용새가 있다. 척후가 그토록 자주 일컫는 고양이새와, 흔히 땅타작새라 불리는 새다."], ["Either of these two last birds is superior to the nightingale, or the lark, though, in general, the American birds are less musical than those of Europe.", "이 둘 중 어느 것이든 나이팅게일이나 종다리보다 낫다. 다만 대체로 아메리카의 새들은 유럽의 새들보다 덜 곱게 운다."]], [["[23] The beauties of Lake George are well known to every American tourist.", "[23] 〈조지 호수〉의 아름다움은 모든 미국인 여행자에게 잘 알려져 있다."], ["In the height of the mountains which surround it, and in artificial accessories, it is inferior to the finest of the Swiss and Italian lakes, while in outline and purity of water it is fully their equal; and in the number and disposition of its isles and islets much superior to them all together.", "그것을 에워싼 산들의 높이와 인공적인 부속물에서는 스위스와 이탈리아의 가장 빼어난 호수들에 못 미치나, 윤곽과 물의 맑음에서는 그것들과 충분히 대등하고, 그 섬과 작은 섬들의 수와 배치에서는 그것들을 다 합친 것보다 훨씬 낫다."], ["There are said to be some hundreds of islands in a sheet of water less than thirty miles long.", "길이가 삼십 마일도 채 안 되는 수면에 수백 개의 섬이 있다고들 한다."], ["The narrows which connect what may be called, in truth, two lakes, are crowded with islands to such a degree as to leave passages between them frequently of only a few feet in width.", "실로 두 개의 호수라 할 만한 것을 잇는 좁은 물목은 섬들로 빼곡하여, 그 사이의 통로가 흔히 폭이 몇 피트밖에 안 된다."], ["The lake itself varies in breadth from one to three miles.", "호수 자체의 너비는 일 마일에서 삼 마일까지 다양하다."]], [["The State of New York is remarkable for the number and beauty of its lakes.", "〈뉴욕〉주는 그 호수의 수와 아름다움으로 두드러진다."], ["One of its frontiers lies on the vast sheet of Ontario, while Champlain stretches nearly a hundred miles along another.", "그 변경 하나는 광대한 〈온타리오〉의 수면에 닿아 있고, 〈섐플레인〉은 또 다른 변경을 따라 거의 백 마일이나 뻗어 있다."], ["Oneida, Cayuga, Canandaigua, Seneca, and George, are all lakes of thirty miles in length, while those of a size smaller are without number.", "〈오나이다〉, 〈카유가〉, 〈커낸다이과〉, 〈세니카〉, 〈조지〉는 모두 길이가 삼십 마일에 이르는 호수이며, 그보다 작은 것들은 헤아릴 수 없이 많다."], ["On most of these lakes there are now beautiful villages, and on many of them steamboats.", "이 호수들 대부분에는 이제 아름다운 마을들이 들어섰고, 그 많은 호수에 증기선이 다닌다."]], [["[24] These harangues of the beasts are frequent among the Indians.", "[24] 짐승들에게 건네는 이러한 일장 연설은 인디언들 사이에서 흔하다."], ["They often address their victims in this way, reproaching them for cowardice, or commending their resolution, as they may happen to exhibit fortitude, or the reverse in suffering.", "그들은 흔히 제 사냥감에게 이런 식으로 말을 건네는데, 그것이 고통 속에서 의연함을 보이느냐 그 반대냐에 따라, 비겁함을 꾸짖거나 그 결의를 칭찬한다."]], [["[25] A dish composed of cracked corn and beans.", "[25] 빻은 옥수수와 콩으로 만든 음식."], ["It is much used also by the whites.", "백인들도 즐겨 쓴다."], ["By corn is meant maize.", "옥수수란 메이즈를 이른다."]], [["[26] William Penn was termed Minquon by the Delawares, and, as he never used violence or injustice in his dealings with them, his reputation for probity passed into a proverb.", "[26] 윌리엄 펜은 〈델라웨어〉인들에게 〈민쿠온〉이라 불렸으니, 그는 그들과의 거래에서 결코 폭력이나 불의를 쓰지 않았으므로, 그 정직함에 대한 평판이 속담처럼 굳어졌다."], ["The American is justly proud of the origin of his nation, which is perhaps unequalled in the history of the world; but the Pennsylvanian and Jerseyman have more reason to value themselves in their ancestors than the natives of any other State, since no wrong was done the original owners of the soil.", "미국인은 어쩌면 세계 역사에서 유례없을 제 나라의 기원을 마땅히 자랑스러워하나, 펜실베이니아인과 저지인은 어느 주의 토박이보다 제 조상을 더 떳떳이 여길 까닭이 있으니, 땅의 본래 주인들에게 아무런 부당함도 행해지지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["[27] Turtle.", "[27] 거북."]], [["[28] A tree which has been partially or entirely stripped of its bark is said, in the language of the country, to be \"blazed.\"", "[28] 껍질이 일부 혹은 전부 벗겨진 나무를, 그 고장 말로 \"블레이즈드\"라 한다. 이 말은 엄밀히 영어다."], ["The term is strictly English; for a horse is said to be blazed when it has a white mark.", "말(馬)이 흰 무늬가 있을 때도 \"블레이즈드\"라 하기 때문이다."]], [["[29] The American forest admits of the passage of horse, there being little underbush, and few tangled brakes.", "[29] 아메리카의 숲은 말이 지날 수 있으니, 덤불이 적고 뒤엉킨 수풀이 드물기 때문이다."], ["The plan of Hawkeye is the one which has always proved the most successful in the battles between the whites and the Indians.", "〈호크아이〉의 방책은 백인과 인디언 사이의 싸움에서 늘 가장 효과적임이 입증된 것이다."], ["Wayne, in his celebrated campaign on the Miami, received the fire of his enemies in line; and then causing his dragoons to wheel round his flanks, the Indians were driven from their covers before they had time to load.", "웨인은 〈마이애미〉에서의 그 유명한 원정에서, 횡대로 적의 사격을 받은 다음, 기병을 측면으로 돌아 들게 하여, 인디언들이 미처 장전할 틈도 없이 그들의 엄폐물에서 몰아냈다."], ["One of the most conspicuous of the chiefs who fought in the battle of Miami assured the writer, that the redmen could not fight the warriors with \"long knives and leather-stockings\"; meaning the dragoons with their sabres and boots. End of Project Gutenberg's The Last of the Mohicans, by James Fenimore Cooper", "〈마이애미〉 전투에서 싸운 가장 이름난 추장 중 하나는 필자에게, 붉은 사람들은 \"긴 칼과 가죽 각반\"을 한 전사들과는 싸울 수 없다고 단언했으니, 곧 군도와 군화를 갖춘 기병을 두고 한 말이었다."]]];
